if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-158"]="# Volume I — Chapter I\n\nEmma Woodhouse, handsome, clever, and rich, with a comfortable home and happy disposition, seemed to unite some of the best blessings of existence; and had lived nearly twenty-one years in the world with very little to distress or vex her.\n\nShe was the youngest of the two daughters of a most affectionate, indulgent father; and had, in consequence of her sister’s marriage, been mistress of his house from a very early period. Her mother had died too long ago for her to have more than an indistinct remembrance of her caresses; and her place had been supplied by an excellent woman as governess, who had fallen little short of a mother in affection.\n\nSixteen years had Miss Taylor been in Mr. Woodhouse’s family, less as a governess than a friend, very fond of both daughters, but particularly of Emma. Between them it was more the intimacy of sisters. Even before Miss Taylor had ceased to hold the nominal office of governess, the mildness of her temper had hardly allowed her to impose any restraint; and the shadow of authority being now long passed away, they had been living together as friend and friend very mutually attached, and Emma doing just what she liked; highly esteeming Miss Taylor’s judgment, but directed chiefly by her own.\n\nThe real evils, indeed, of Emma’s situation were the power of having rather too much her own way, and a disposition to think a little too well of herself; these were the disadvantages which threatened alloy to her many enjoyments. The danger, however, was at present so unperceived, that they did not by any means rank as misfortunes with her.\n\nSorrow came—a gentle sorrow—but not at all in the shape of any disagreeable consciousness.—Miss Taylor married. It was Miss Taylor’s loss which first brought grief. It was on the wedding-day of this beloved friend that Emma first sat in mournful thought of any continuance. The wedding over, and the bride-people gone, her father and herself were left to dine together, with no prospect of a third to cheer a long evening. Her father composed himself to sleep after dinner, as usual, and she had then only to sit and think of what she had lost.\n\nThe event had every promise of happiness for her friend. Mr. Weston was a man of unexceptionable character, easy fortune, suitable age, and pleasant manners; and there was some satisfaction in considering with what self-denying, generous friendship she had always wished and promoted the match; but it was a black morning’s work for her. The want of Miss Taylor would be felt every hour of every day. She recalled her past kindness—the kindness, the affection of sixteen years—how she had taught and how she had played with her from five years old—how she had devoted all her powers to attach and amuse her in health—and how nursed her through the various illnesses of childhood. A large debt of gratitude was owing here; but the intercourse of the last seven years, the equal footing and perfect unreserve which had soon followed Isabella’s marriage, on their being left to each other, was yet a dearer, tenderer recollection. She had been a friend and companion such as few possessed: intelligent, well-informed, useful, gentle, knowing all the ways of the family, interested in all its concerns, and peculiarly interested in herself, in every pleasure, every scheme of hers—one to whom she could speak every thought as it arose, and who had such an affection for her as could never find fault.\n\nHow was she to bear the change?—It was true that her friend was going only half a mile from them; but Emma was aware that great must be the difference between a Mrs. Weston, only half a mile from them, and a Miss Taylor in the house; and with all her advantages, natural and domestic, she was now in great danger of suffering from intellectual solitude. She dearly loved her father, but he was no companion for her. He could not meet her in conversation, rational or playful.\n\nThe evil of the actual disparity in their ages (and Mr. Woodhouse had not married early) was much increased by his constitution and habits; for having been a valetudinarian all his life, without activity of mind or body, he was a much older man in ways than in years; and though everywhere beloved for the friendliness of his heart and his amiable temper, his talents could not have recommended him at any time.\n\nHer sister, though comparatively but little removed by matrimony, being settled in London, only sixteen miles off, was much beyond her daily reach; and many a long October and November evening must be struggled through at Hartfield, before Christmas brought the next visit from Isabella and her husband, and their little children, to fill the house, and give her pleasant society again.\n\nHighbury, the large and populous village, almost amounting to a town, to which Hartfield, in spite of its separate lawn, and shrubberies, and name, did really belong, afforded her no equals. The Woodhouses were first in consequence there. All looked up to them. She had many acquaintance in the place, for her father was universally civil, but not one among them who could be accepted in lieu of Miss Taylor for even half a day. It was a melancholy change; and Emma could not but sigh over it, and wish for impossible things, till her father awoke, and made it necessary to be cheerful. His spirits required support. He was a nervous man, easily depressed; fond of every body that he was used to, and hating to part with them; hating change of every kind. Matrimony, as the origin of change, was always disagreeable; and he was by no means yet reconciled to his own daughter’s marrying, nor could ever speak of her but with compassion, though it had been entirely a match of affection, when he was now obliged to part with Miss Taylor too; and from his habits of gentle selfishness, and of being never able to suppose that other people could feel differently from himself, he was very much disposed to think Miss Taylor had done as sad a thing for herself as for them, and would have been a great deal happier if she had spent all the rest of her life at Hartfield. Emma smiled and chatted as cheerfully as she could, to keep him from such thoughts; but when tea came, it was impossible for him not to say exactly as he had said at dinner,\n\n“Poor Miss Taylor!—I wish she were here again. What a pity it is that Mr. Weston ever thought of her!”\n\n“I cannot agree with you, papa; you know I cannot. Mr. Weston is such a good-humoured, pleasant, excellent man, that he thoroughly deserves a good wife;—and you would not have had Miss Taylor live with us for ever, and bear all my odd humours, when she might have a house of her own?”\n\n“A house of her own!—But where is the advantage of a house of her own? This is three times as large.—And you have never any odd humours, my dear.”\n\n“How often we shall be going to see them, and they coming to see us!—We shall be always meeting! We must begin; we must go and pay wedding visit very soon.”\n\n“My dear, how am I to get so far? Randalls is such a distance. I could not walk half so far.”\n\n“No, papa, nobody thought of your walking. We must go in the carriage, to be sure.”\n\n“The carriage! But James will not like to put the horses to for such a little way;—and where are the poor horses to be while we are paying our visit?”\n\n“They are to be put into Mr. Weston’s stable, papa. You know we have settled all that already. We talked it all over with Mr. Weston last night. And as for James, you may be very sure he will always like going to Randalls, because of his daughter’s being housemaid there. I only doubt whether he will ever take us anywhere else. That was your doing, papa. You got Hannah that good place. Nobody thought of Hannah till you mentioned her—James is so obliged to you!”\n\n“I am very glad I did think of her. It was very lucky, for I would not have had poor James think himself slighted upon any account; and I am sure she will make a very good servant: she is a civil, pretty-spoken girl; I have a great opinion of her. Whenever I see her, she always curtseys and asks me how I do, in a very pretty manner; and when you have had her here to do needlework, I observe she always turns the lock of the door the right way and never bangs it. I am sure she will be an excellent servant; and it will be a great comfort to poor Miss Taylor to have somebody about her that she is used to see. Whenever James goes over to see his daughter, you know, she will be hearing of us. He will be able to tell her how we all are.”\n\nEmma spared no exertions to maintain this happier flow of ideas, and hoped, by the help of backgammon, to get her father tolerably through the evening, and be attacked by no regrets but her own. The backgammon-table was placed; but a visitor immediately afterwards walked in and made it unnecessary.\n\nMr. Knightley, a sensible man about seven or eight-and-thirty, was not only a very old and intimate friend of the family, but particularly connected with it, as the elder brother of Isabella’s husband. He lived about a mile from Highbury, was a frequent visitor, and always welcome, and at this time more welcome than usual, as coming directly from their mutual connexions in London. He had returned to a late dinner, after some days’ absence, and now walked up to Hartfield to say that all were well in Brunswick Square. It was a happy circumstance, and animated Mr. Woodhouse for some time. Mr. Knightley had a cheerful manner, which always did him good; and his many inquiries after “poor Isabella” and her children were answered most satisfactorily. When this was over, Mr. Woodhouse gratefully observed, “It is very kind of you, Mr. Knightley, to come out at this late hour to call upon us. I am afraid you must have had a shocking walk.”\n\n“Not at all, sir. It is a beautiful moonlight night; and so mild that I must draw back from your great fire.”\n\n“But you must have found it very damp and dirty. I wish you may not catch cold.”\n\n“Dirty, sir! Look at my shoes. Not a speck on them.”\n\n“Well! that is quite surprising, for we have had a vast deal of rain here. It rained dreadfully hard for half an hour while we were at breakfast. I wanted them to put off the wedding.”\n\n“By the bye—I have not wished you joy. Being pretty well aware of what sort of joy you must both be feeling, I have been in no hurry with my congratulations; but I hope it all went off tolerably well. How did you all behave? Who cried most?”\n\n“Ah! poor Miss Taylor! ’Tis a sad business.”\n\n“Poor Mr. and Miss Woodhouse, if you please; but I cannot possibly say ‘poor Miss Taylor.’ I have a great regard for you and Emma; but when it comes to the question of dependence or independence!—At any rate, it must be better to have only one to please than two.”\n\n“Especially when one of those two is such a fanciful, troublesome creature!” said Emma playfully. “That is what you have in your head, I know—and what you would certainly say if my father were not by.”\n\n“I believe it is very true, my dear, indeed,” said Mr. Woodhouse, with a sigh. “I am afraid I am sometimes very fanciful and troublesome.”\n\n“My dearest papa! You do not think I could mean you, or suppose Mr. Knightley to mean you. What a horrible idea! Oh no! I meant only myself. Mr. Knightley loves to find fault with me, you know—in a joke—it is all a joke. We always say what we like to one another.”\n\nMr. Knightley, in fact, was one of the few people who could see faults in Emma Woodhouse, and the only one who ever told her of them: and though this was not particularly agreeable to Emma herself, she knew it would be so much less so to her father, that she would not have him really suspect such a circumstance as her not being thought perfect by every body.\n\n“Emma knows I never flatter her,” said Mr. Knightley, “but I meant no reflection on any body. Miss Taylor has been used to have two persons to please; she will now have but one. The chances are that she must be a gainer.”\n\n“Well,” said Emma, willing to let it pass—“you want to hear about the wedding; and I shall be happy to tell you, for we all behaved charmingly. Every body was punctual, every body in their best looks: not a tear, and hardly a long face to be seen. Oh no; we all felt that we were going to be only half a mile apart, and were sure of meeting every day.”\n\n“Dear Emma bears every thing so well,” said her father. “But, Mr. Knightley, she is really very sorry to lose poor Miss Taylor, and I am sure she will miss her more than she thinks for.”\n\nEmma turned away her head, divided between tears and smiles. “It is impossible that Emma should not miss such a companion,” said Mr. Knightley. “We should not like her so well as we do, sir, if we could suppose it; but she knows how much the marriage is to Miss Taylor’s advantage; she knows how very acceptable it must be, at Miss Taylor’s time of life, to be settled in a home of her own, and how important to her to be secure of a comfortable provision, and therefore cannot allow herself to feel so much pain as pleasure. Every friend of Miss Taylor must be glad to have her so happily married.”\n\n“And you have forgotten one matter of joy to me,” said Emma, “and a very considerable one—that I made the match myself. I made the match, you know, four years ago; and to have it take place, and be proved in the right, when so many people said Mr. Weston would never marry again, may comfort me for any thing.”\n\nMr. Knightley shook his head at her. Her father fondly replied, “Ah! my dear, I wish you would not make matches and foretell things, for whatever you say always comes to pass. Pray do not make any more matches.”\n\n“I promise you to make none for myself, papa; but I must, indeed, for other people. It is the greatest amusement in the world! And after such success, you know!—Every body said that Mr. Weston would never marry again. Oh dear, no! Mr. Weston, who had been a widower so long, and who seemed so perfectly comfortable without a wife, so constantly occupied either in his business in town or among his friends here, always acceptable wherever he went, always cheerful—Mr. Weston need not spend a single evening in the year alone if he did not like it. Oh no! Mr. Weston certainly would never marry again. Some people even talked of a promise to his wife on her deathbed, and others of the son and the uncle not letting him. All manner of solemn nonsense was talked on the subject, but I believed none of it.\n\n“Ever since the day—about four years ago—that Miss Taylor and I met with him in Broadway Lane, when, because it began to drizzle, he darted away with so much gallantry, and borrowed two umbrellas for us from Farmer Mitchell’s, I made up my mind on the subject. I planned the match from that hour; and when such success has blessed me in this instance, dear papa, you cannot think that I shall leave off match-making.”\n\n“I do not understand what you mean by ‘success,’” said Mr. Knightley. “Success supposes endeavour. Your time has been properly and delicately spent, if you have been endeavouring for the last four years to bring about this marriage. A worthy employment for a young lady’s mind! But if, which I rather imagine, your making the match, as you call it, means only your planning it, your saying to yourself one idle day, ‘I think it would be a very good thing for Miss Taylor if Mr. Weston were to marry her,’ and saying it again to yourself every now and then afterwards, why do you talk of success? Where is your merit? What are you proud of? You made a lucky guess; and that is all that can be said.”\n\n“And have you never known the pleasure and triumph of a lucky guess?—I pity you.—I thought you cleverer—for, depend upon it a lucky guess is never merely luck. There is always some talent in it. And as to my poor word ‘success,’ which you quarrel with, I do not know that I am so entirely without any claim to it. You have drawn two pretty pictures; but I think there may be a third—a something between the do-nothing and the do-all. If I had not promoted Mr. Weston’s visits here, and given many little encouragements, and smoothed many little matters, it might not have come to any thing after all. I think you must know Hartfield enough to comprehend that.”\n\n“A straightforward, open-hearted man like Weston, and a rational, unaffected woman like Miss Taylor, may be safely left to manage their own concerns. You are more likely to have done harm to yourself, than good to them, by interference.”\n\n“Emma never thinks of herself, if she can do good to others,” rejoined Mr. Woodhouse, understanding but in part. “But, my dear, pray do not make any more matches; they are silly things, and break up one’s family circle grievously.”\n\n“Only one more, papa; only for Mr. Elton. Poor Mr. Elton! You like Mr. Elton, papa,—I must look about for a wife for him. There is nobody in Highbury who deserves him—and he has been here a whole year, and has fitted up his house so comfortably, that it would be a shame to have him single any longer—and I thought when he was joining their hands to-day, he looked so very much as if he would like to have the same kind office done for him! I think very well of Mr. Elton, and this is the only way I have of doing him a service.”\n\n“Mr. Elton is a very pretty young man, to be sure, and a very good young man, and I have a great regard for him. But if you want to shew him any attention, my dear, ask him to come and dine with us some day. That will be a much better thing. I dare say Mr. Knightley will be so kind as to meet him.”\n\n“With a great deal of pleasure, sir, at any time,” said Mr. Knightley, laughing, “and I agree with you entirely, that it will be a much better thing. Invite him to dinner, Emma, and help him to the best of the fish and the chicken, but leave him to chuse his own wife. Depend upon it, a man of six or seven-and-twenty can take care of himself.”\n\n# Volume I — Chapter II\n\nMr. Weston was a native of Highbury, and born of a respectable family, which for the last two or three generations had been rising into gentility and property. He had received a good education, but, on succeeding early in life to a small independence, had become indisposed for any of the more homely pursuits in which his brothers were engaged, and had satisfied an active, cheerful mind and social temper by entering into the militia of his county, then embodied.\n\nCaptain Weston was a general favourite; and when the chances of his military life had introduced him to Miss Churchill, of a great Yorkshire family, and Miss Churchill fell in love with him, nobody was surprized, except her brother and his wife, who had never seen him, and who were full of pride and importance, which the connexion would offend.\n\nMiss Churchill, however, being of age, and with the full command of her fortune—though her fortune bore no proportion to the family-estate—was not to be dissuaded from the marriage, and it took place, to the infinite mortification of Mr. and Mrs. Churchill, who threw her off with due decorum. It was an unsuitable connexion, and did not produce much happiness. Mrs. Weston ought to have found more in it, for she had a husband whose warm heart and sweet temper made him think every thing due to her in return for the great goodness of being in love with him; but though she had one sort of spirit, she had not the best. She had resolution enough to pursue her own will in spite of her brother, but not enough to refrain from unreasonable regrets at that brother’s unreasonable anger, nor from missing the luxuries of her former home. They lived beyond their income, but still it was nothing in comparison of Enscombe: she did not cease to love her husband, but she wanted at once to be the wife of Captain Weston, and Miss Churchill of Enscombe.\n\nCaptain Weston, who had been considered, especially by the Churchills, as making such an amazing match, was proved to have much the worst of the bargain; for when his wife died, after a three years’ marriage, he was rather a poorer man than at first, and with a child to maintain. From the expense of the child, however, he was soon relieved. The boy had, with the additional softening claim of a lingering illness of his mother’s, been the means of a sort of reconciliation; and Mr. and Mrs. Churchill, having no children of their own, nor any other young creature of equal kindred to care for, offered to take the whole charge of the little Frank soon after her decease. Some scruples and some reluctance the widower-father may be supposed to have felt; but as they were overcome by other considerations, the child was given up to the care and the wealth of the Churchills, and he had only his own comfort to seek, and his own situation to improve as he could.\n\nA complete change of life became desirable. He quitted the militia and engaged in trade, having brothers already established in a good way in London, which afforded him a favourable opening. It was a concern which brought just employment enough. He had still a small house in Highbury, where most of his leisure days were spent; and between useful occupation and the pleasures of society, the next eighteen or twenty years of his life passed cheerfully away. He had, by that time, realised an easy competence—enough to secure the purchase of a little estate adjoining Highbury, which he had always longed for—enough to marry a woman as portionless even as Miss Taylor, and to live according to the wishes of his own friendly and social disposition.\n\nIt was now some time since Miss Taylor had begun to influence his schemes; but as it was not the tyrannic influence of youth on youth, it had not shaken his determination of never settling till he could purchase Randalls, and the sale of Randalls was long looked forward to; but he had gone steadily on, with these objects in view, till they were accomplished. He had made his fortune, bought his house, and obtained his wife; and was beginning a new period of existence, with every probability of greater happiness than in any yet passed through. He had never been an unhappy man; his own temper had secured him from that, even in his first marriage; but his second must shew him how delightful a well-judging and truly amiable woman could be, and must give him the pleasantest proof of its being a great deal better to choose than to be chosen, to excite gratitude than to feel it.\n\nHe had only himself to please in his choice: his fortune was his own; for as to Frank, it was more than being tacitly brought up as his uncle’s heir, it had become so avowed an adoption as to have him assume the name of Churchill on coming of age. It was most unlikely, therefore, that he should ever want his father’s assistance. His father had no apprehension of it. The aunt was a capricious woman, and governed her husband entirely; but it was not in Mr. Weston’s nature to imagine that any caprice could be strong enough to affect one so dear, and, as he believed, so deservedly dear. He saw his son every year in London, and was proud of him; and his fond report of him as a very fine young man had made Highbury feel a sort of pride in him too. He was looked on as sufficiently belonging to the place to make his merits and prospects a kind of common concern.\n\nMr. Frank Churchill was one of the boasts of Highbury, and a lively curiosity to see him prevailed, though the compliment was so little returned that he had never been there in his life. His coming to visit his father had been often talked of but never achieved.\n\nNow, upon his father’s marriage, it was very generally proposed, as a most proper attention, that the visit should take place. There was not a dissentient voice on the subject, either when Mrs. Perry drank tea with Mrs. and Miss Bates, or when Mrs. and Miss Bates returned the visit. Now was the time for Mr. Frank Churchill to come among them; and the hope strengthened when it was understood that he had written to his new mother on the occasion. For a few days, every morning visit in Highbury included some mention of the handsome letter Mrs. Weston had received. “I suppose you have heard of the handsome letter Mr. Frank Churchill has written to Mrs. Weston? I understand it was a very handsome letter, indeed. Mr. Woodhouse told me of it. Mr. Woodhouse saw the letter, and he says he never saw such a handsome letter in his life.”\n\nIt was, indeed, a highly prized letter. Mrs. Weston had, of course, formed a very favourable idea of the young man; and such a pleasing attention was an irresistible proof of his great good sense, and a most welcome addition to every source and every expression of congratulation which her marriage had already secured. She felt herself a most fortunate woman; and she had lived long enough to know how fortunate she might well be thought, where the only regret was for a partial separation from friends whose friendship for her had never cooled, and who could ill bear to part with her.\n\nShe knew that at times she must be missed; and could not think, without pain, of Emma’s losing a single pleasure, or suffering an hour’s ennui, from the want of her companionableness: but dear Emma was of no feeble character; she was more equal to her situation than most girls would have been, and had sense, and energy, and spirits that might be hoped would bear her well and happily through its little difficulties and privations. And then there was such comfort in the very easy distance of Randalls from Hartfield, so convenient for even solitary female walking, and in Mr. Weston’s disposition and circumstances, which would make the approaching season no hindrance to their spending half the evenings in the week together.\n\nHer situation was altogether the subject of hours of gratitude to Mrs. Weston, and of moments only of regret; and her satisfaction—her more than satisfaction—her cheerful enjoyment, was so just and so apparent, that Emma, well as she knew her father, was sometimes taken by surprize at his being still able to pity ‘poor Miss Taylor,’ when they left her at Randalls in the centre of every domestic comfort, or saw her go away in the evening attended by her pleasant husband to a carriage of her own. But never did she go without Mr. Woodhouse’s giving a gentle sigh, and saying, “Ah, poor Miss Taylor! She would be very glad to stay.”\n\nThere was no recovering Miss Taylor—nor much likelihood of ceasing to pity her; but a few weeks brought some alleviation to Mr. Woodhouse. The compliments of his neighbours were over; he was no longer teased by being wished joy of so sorrowful an event; and the wedding-cake, which had been a great distress to him, was all eat up. His own stomach could bear nothing rich, and he could never believe other people to be different from himself. What was unwholesome to him he regarded as unfit for any body; and he had, therefore, earnestly tried to dissuade them from having any wedding-cake at all, and when that proved vain, as earnestly tried to prevent any body’s eating it. He had been at the pains of consulting Mr. Perry, the apothecary, on the subject. Mr. Perry was an intelligent, gentlemanlike man, whose frequent visits were one of the comforts of Mr. Woodhouse’s life; and upon being applied to, he could not but acknowledge (though it seemed rather against the bias of inclination) that wedding-cake might certainly disagree with many—perhaps with most people, unless taken moderately. With such an opinion, in confirmation of his own, Mr. Woodhouse hoped to influence every visitor of the newly married pair; but still the cake was eaten; and there was no rest for his benevolent nerves till it was all gone.\n\nThere was a strange rumour in Highbury of all the little Perrys being seen with a slice of Mrs. Weston’s wedding-cake in their hands: but Mr. Woodhouse would never believe it.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter III\n\nMr. Woodhouse was fond of society in his own way. He liked very much to have his friends come and see him; and from various united causes, from his long residence at Hartfield, and his good nature, from his fortune, his house, and his daughter, he could command the visits of his own little circle, in a great measure, as he liked. He had not much intercourse with any families beyond that circle; his horror of late hours, and large dinner-parties, made him unfit for any acquaintance but such as would visit him on his own terms. Fortunately for him, Highbury, including Randalls in the same parish, and Donwell Abbey in the parish adjoining, the seat of Mr. Knightley, comprehended many such. Not unfrequently, through Emma’s persuasion, he had some of the chosen and the best to dine with him: but evening parties were what he preferred; and, unless he fancied himself at any time unequal to company, there was scarcely an evening in the week in which Emma could not make up a card-table for him.\n\nReal, long-standing regard brought the Westons and Mr. Knightley; and by Mr. Elton, a young man living alone without liking it, the privilege of exchanging any vacant evening of his own blank solitude for the elegancies and society of Mr. Woodhouse’s drawing-room, and the smiles of his lovely daughter, was in no danger of being thrown away.\n\nAfter these came a second set; among the most come-at-able of whom were Mrs. and Miss Bates, and Mrs. Goddard, three ladies almost always at the service of an invitation from Hartfield, and who were fetched and carried home so often, that Mr. Woodhouse thought it no hardship for either James or the horses. Had it taken place only once a year, it would have been a grievance.\n\nMrs. Bates, the widow of a former vicar of Highbury, was a very old lady, almost past every thing but tea and quadrille. She lived with her single daughter in a very small way, and was considered with all the regard and respect which a harmless old lady, under such untoward circumstances, can excite. Her daughter enjoyed a most uncommon degree of popularity for a woman neither young, handsome, rich, nor married. Miss Bates stood in the very worst predicament in the world for having much of the public favour; and she had no intellectual superiority to make atonement to herself, or frighten those who might hate her into outward respect. She had never boasted either beauty or cleverness. Her youth had passed without distinction, and her middle of life was devoted to the care of a failing mother, and the endeavour to make a small income go as far as possible. And yet she was a happy woman, and a woman whom no one named without good-will. It was her own universal good-will and contented temper which worked such wonders. She loved every body, was interested in every body’s happiness, quicksighted to every body’s merits; thought herself a most fortunate creature, and surrounded with blessings in such an excellent mother, and so many good neighbours and friends, and a home that wanted for nothing. The simplicity and cheerfulness of her nature, her contented and grateful spirit, were a recommendation to every body, and a mine of felicity to herself. She was a great talker upon little matters, which exactly suited Mr. Woodhouse, full of trivial communications and harmless gossip.\n\nMrs. Goddard was the mistress of a School—not of a seminary, or an establishment, or any thing which professed, in long sentences of refined nonsense, to combine liberal acquirements with elegant morality, upon new principles and new systems—and where young ladies for enormous pay might be screwed out of health and into vanity—but a real, honest, old-fashioned Boarding-school, where a reasonable quantity of accomplishments were sold at a reasonable price, and where girls might be sent to be out of the way, and scramble themselves into a little education, without any danger of coming back prodigies. Mrs. Goddard’s school was in high repute—and very deservedly; for Highbury was reckoned a particularly healthy spot: she had an ample house and garden, gave the children plenty of wholesome food, let them run about a great deal in the summer, and in winter dressed their chilblains with her own hands. It was no wonder that a train of twenty young couple now walked after her to church. She was a plain, motherly kind of woman, who had worked hard in her youth, and now thought herself entitled to the occasional holiday of a tea-visit; and having formerly owed much to Mr. Woodhouse’s kindness, felt his particular claim on her to leave her neat parlour, hung round with fancy-work, whenever she could, and win or lose a few sixpences by his fireside.\n\nThese were the ladies whom Emma found herself very frequently able to collect; and happy was she, for her father’s sake, in the power; though, as far as she was herself concerned, it was no remedy for the absence of Mrs. Weston. She was delighted to see her father look comfortable, and very much pleased with herself for contriving things so well; but the quiet prosings of three such women made her feel that every evening so spent was indeed one of the long evenings she had fearfully anticipated.\n\nAs she sat one morning, looking forward to exactly such a close of the present day, a note was brought from Mrs. Goddard, requesting, in most respectful terms, to be allowed to bring Miss Smith with her; a most welcome request: for Miss Smith was a girl of seventeen, whom Emma knew very well by sight, and had long felt an interest in, on account of her beauty. A very gracious invitation was returned, and the evening no longer dreaded by the fair mistress of the mansion.\n\nHarriet Smith was the natural daughter of somebody. Somebody had placed her, several years back, at Mrs. Goddard’s school, and somebody had lately raised her from the condition of scholar to that of parlour-boarder. This was all that was generally known of her history. She had no visible friends but what had been acquired at Highbury, and was now just returned from a long visit in the country to some young ladies who had been at school there with her.\n\nShe was a very pretty girl, and her beauty happened to be of a sort which Emma particularly admired. She was short, plump, and fair, with a fine bloom, blue eyes, light hair, regular features, and a look of great sweetness, and, before the end of the evening, Emma was as much pleased with her manners as her person, and quite determined to continue the acquaintance.\n\nShe was not struck by any thing remarkably clever in Miss Smith’s conversation, but she found her altogether very engaging—not inconveniently shy, not unwilling to talk—and yet so far from pushing, shewing so proper and becoming a deference, seeming so pleasantly grateful for being admitted to Hartfield, and so artlessly impressed by the appearance of every thing in so superior a style to what she had been used to, that she must have good sense, and deserve encouragement. Encouragement should be given. Those soft blue eyes, and all those natural graces, should not be wasted on the inferior society of Highbury and its connexions. The acquaintance she had already formed were unworthy of her. The friends from whom she had just parted, though very good sort of people, must be doing her harm. They were a family of the name of Martin, whom Emma well knew by character, as renting a large farm of Mr. Knightley, and residing in the parish of Donwell—very creditably, she believed—she knew Mr. Knightley thought highly of them—but they must be coarse and unpolished, and very unfit to be the intimates of a girl who wanted only a little more knowledge and elegance to be quite perfect. She would notice her; she would improve her; she would detach her from her bad acquaintance, and introduce her into good society; she would form her opinions and her manners. It would be an interesting, and certainly a very kind undertaking; highly becoming her own situation in life, her leisure, and powers.\n\nShe was so busy in admiring those soft blue eyes, in talking and listening, and forming all these schemes in the in-betweens, that the evening flew away at a very unusual rate; and the supper-table, which always closed such parties, and for which she had been used to sit and watch the due time, was all set out and ready, and moved forwards to the fire, before she was aware. With an alacrity beyond the common impulse of a spirit which yet was never indifferent to the credit of doing every thing well and attentively, with the real good-will of a mind delighted with its own ideas, did she then do all the honours of the meal, and help and recommend the minced chicken and scalloped oysters, with an urgency which she knew would be acceptable to the early hours and civil scruples of their guests.\n\nUpon such occasions poor Mr. Woodhouse’s feelings were in sad warfare. He loved to have the cloth laid, because it had been the fashion of his youth, but his conviction of suppers being very unwholesome made him rather sorry to see any thing put on it; and while his hospitality would have welcomed his visitors to every thing, his care for their health made him grieve that they would eat.\n\nSuch another small basin of thin gruel as his own was all that he could, with thorough self-approbation, recommend; though he might constrain himself, while the ladies were comfortably clearing the nicer things, to say:\n\n“Mrs. Bates, let me propose your venturing on one of these eggs. An egg boiled very soft is not unwholesome. Serle understands boiling an egg better than any body. I would not recommend an egg boiled by any body else; but you need not be afraid, they are very small, you see—one of our small eggs will not hurt you. Miss Bates, let Emma help you to a little bit of tart—a very little bit. Ours are all apple-tarts. You need not be afraid of unwholesome preserves here. I do not advise the custard. Mrs. Goddard, what say you to half a glass of wine? A small half-glass, put into a tumbler of water? I do not think it could disagree with you.”\n\nEmma allowed her father to talk—but supplied her visitors in a much more satisfactory style, and on the present evening had particular pleasure in sending them away happy. The happiness of Miss Smith was quite equal to her intentions. Miss Woodhouse was so great a personage in Highbury, that the prospect of the introduction had given as much panic as pleasure; but the humble, grateful little girl went off with highly gratified feelings, delighted with the affability with which Miss Woodhouse had treated her all the evening, and actually shaken hands with her at last!\n\n# Volume I — Chapter IV\n\nHarriet Smith’s intimacy at Hartfield was soon a settled thing. Quick and decided in her ways, Emma lost no time in inviting, encouraging, and telling her to come very often; and as their acquaintance increased, so did their satisfaction in each other. As a walking companion, Emma had very early foreseen how useful she might find her. In that respect Mrs. Weston’s loss had been important. Her father never went beyond the shrubbery, where two divisions of the ground sufficed him for his long walk, or his short, as the year varied; and since Mrs. Weston’s marriage her exercise had been too much confined. She had ventured once alone to Randalls, but it was not pleasant; and a Harriet Smith, therefore, one whom she could summon at any time to a walk, would be a valuable addition to her privileges. But in every respect, as she saw more of her, she approved her, and was confirmed in all her kind designs.\n\nHarriet certainly was not clever, but she had a sweet, docile, grateful disposition, was totally free from conceit, and only desiring to be guided by any one she looked up to. Her early attachment to herself was very amiable; and her inclination for good company, and power of appreciating what was elegant and clever, shewed that there was no want of taste, though strength of understanding must not be expected. Altogether she was quite convinced of Harriet Smith’s being exactly the young friend she wanted—exactly the something which her home required. Such a friend as Mrs. Weston was out of the question. Two such could never be granted. Two such she did not want. It was quite a different sort of thing, a sentiment distinct and independent. Mrs. Weston was the object of a regard which had its basis in gratitude and esteem. Harriet would be loved as one to whom she could be useful. For Mrs. Weston there was nothing to be done; for Harriet every thing.\n\nHer first attempts at usefulness were in an endeavour to find out who were the parents, but Harriet could not tell. She was ready to tell every thing in her power, but on this subject questions were vain. Emma was obliged to fancy what she liked—but she could never believe that in the same situation she should not have discovered the truth. Harriet had no penetration. She had been satisfied to hear and believe just what Mrs. Goddard chose to tell her; and looked no farther.\n\nMrs. Goddard, and the teachers, and the girls and the affairs of the school in general, formed naturally a great part of the conversation—and but for her acquaintance with the Martins of Abbey-Mill Farm, it must have been the whole. But the Martins occupied her thoughts a good deal; she had spent two very happy months with them, and now loved to talk of the pleasures of her visit, and describe the many comforts and wonders of the place. Emma encouraged her talkativeness—amused by such a picture of another set of beings, and enjoying the youthful simplicity which could speak with so much exultation of Mrs. Martin’s having “two parlours, two very good parlours, indeed; one of them quite as large as Mrs. Goddard’s drawing-room; and of her having an upper maid who had lived five-and-twenty years with her; and of their having eight cows, two of them Alderneys, and one a little Welch cow, a very pretty little Welch cow indeed; and of Mrs. Martin’s saying as she was so fond of it, it should be called her cow; and of their having a very handsome summer-house in their garden, where some day next year they were all to drink tea:—a very handsome summer-house, large enough to hold a dozen people.”\n\nFor some time she was amused, without thinking beyond the immediate cause; but as she came to understand the family better, other feelings arose. She had taken up a wrong idea, fancying it was a mother and daughter, a son and son’s wife, who all lived together; but when it appeared that the Mr. Martin, who bore a part in the narrative, and was always mentioned with approbation for his great good-nature in doing something or other, was a single man; that there was no young Mrs. Martin, no wife in the case; she did suspect danger to her poor little friend from all this hospitality and kindness, and that, if she were not taken care of, she might be required to sink herself forever.\n\nWith this inspiriting notion, her questions increased in number and meaning; and she particularly led Harriet to talk more of Mr. Martin, and there was evidently no dislike to it. Harriet was very ready to speak of the share he had had in their moonlight walks and merry evening games; and dwelt a good deal upon his being so very good-humoured and obliging. He had gone three miles round one day in order to bring her some walnuts, because she had said how fond she was of them, and in every thing else he was so very obliging. He had his shepherd’s son into the parlour one night on purpose to sing to her. She was very fond of singing. He could sing a little himself. She believed he was very clever, and understood every thing. He had a very fine flock, and, while she was with them, he had been bid more for his wool than any body in the country. She believed every body spoke well of him. His mother and sisters were very fond of him. Mrs. Martin had told her one day (and there was a blush as she said it,) that it was impossible for any body to be a better son, and therefore she was sure, whenever he married, he would make a good husband. Not that she wanted him to marry. She was in no hurry at all.\n\n“Well done, Mrs. Martin!” thought Emma. “You know what you are about.”\n\n“And when she had come away, Mrs. Martin was so very kind as to send Mrs. Goddard a beautiful goose—the finest goose Mrs. Goddard had ever seen. Mrs. Goddard had dressed it on a Sunday, and asked all the three teachers, Miss Nash, and Miss Prince, and Miss Richardson, to sup with her.”\n\n“Mr. Martin, I suppose, is not a man of information beyond the line of his own business? He does not read?”\n\n“Oh yes!—that is, no—I do not know—but I believe he has read a good deal—but not what you would think any thing of. He reads the Agricultural Reports, and some other books that lay in one of the window seats—but he reads all them to himself. But sometimes of an evening, before we went to cards, he would read something aloud out of the Elegant Extracts, very entertaining. And I know he has read the Vicar of Wakefield. He never read the Romance of the Forest, nor The Children of the Abbey. He had never heard of such books before I mentioned them, but he is determined to get them now as soon as ever he can.”\n\nThe next question was—\n\n“What sort of looking man is Mr. Martin?”\n\n“Oh! not handsome—not at all handsome. I thought him very plain at first, but I do not think him so plain now. One does not, you know, after a time. But did you never see him? He is in Highbury every now and then, and he is sure to ride through every week in his way to Kingston. He has passed you very often.”\n\n“That may be, and I may have seen him fifty times, but without having any idea of his name. A young farmer, whether on horseback or on foot, is the very last sort of person to raise my curiosity. The yeomanry are precisely the order of people with whom I feel I can have nothing to do. A degree or two lower, and a creditable appearance might interest me; I might hope to be useful to their families in some way or other. But a farmer can need none of my help, and is, therefore, in one sense, as much above my notice as in every other he is below it.”\n\n“To be sure. Oh yes! It is not likely you should ever have observed him; but he knows you very well indeed—I mean by sight.”\n\n“I have no doubt of his being a very respectable young man. I know, indeed, that he is so, and, as such, wish him well. What do you imagine his age to be?”\n\n“He was four-and-twenty the 8th of last June, and my birthday is the 23rd just a fortnight and a day’s difference—which is very odd.”\n\n“Only four-and-twenty. That is too young to settle. His mother is perfectly right not to be in a hurry. They seem very comfortable as they are, and if she were to take any pains to marry him, she would probably repent it. Six years hence, if he could meet with a good sort of young woman in the same rank as his own, with a little money, it might be very desirable.”\n\n“Six years hence! Dear Miss Woodhouse, he would be thirty years old!”\n\n“Well, and that is as early as most men can afford to marry, who are not born to an independence. Mr. Martin, I imagine, has his fortune entirely to make—cannot be at all beforehand with the world. Whatever money he might come into when his father died, whatever his share of the family property, it is, I dare say, all afloat, all employed in his stock, and so forth; and though, with diligence and good luck, he may be rich in time, it is next to impossible that he should have realised any thing yet.”\n\n“To be sure, so it is. But they live very comfortably. They have no indoors man, else they do not want for any thing; and Mrs. Martin talks of taking a boy another year.”\n\n“I wish you may not get into a scrape, Harriet, whenever he does marry;—I mean, as to being acquainted with his wife—for though his sisters, from a superior education, are not to be altogether objected to, it does not follow that he might marry any body at all fit for you to notice. The misfortune of your birth ought to make you particularly careful as to your associates. There can be no doubt of your being a gentleman’s daughter, and you must support your claim to that station by every thing within your own power, or there will be plenty of people who would take pleasure in degrading you.”\n\n“Yes, to be sure, I suppose there are. But while I visit at Hartfield, and you are so kind to me, Miss Woodhouse, I am not afraid of what any body can do.”\n\n“You understand the force of influence pretty well, Harriet; but I would have you so firmly established in good society, as to be independent even of Hartfield and Miss Woodhouse. I want to see you permanently well connected, and to that end it will be advisable to have as few odd acquaintance as may be; and, therefore, I say that if you should still be in this country when Mr. Martin marries, I wish you may not be drawn in by your intimacy with the sisters, to be acquainted with the wife, who will probably be some mere farmer’s daughter, without education.”\n\n“To be sure. Yes. Not that I think Mr. Martin would ever marry any body but what had had some education—and been very well brought up. However, I do not mean to set up my opinion against yours—and I am sure I shall not wish for the acquaintance of his wife. I shall always have a great regard for the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and should be very sorry to give them up, for they are quite as well educated as me. But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, certainly I had better not visit her, if I can help it.”\n\nEmma watched her through the fluctuations of this speech, and saw no alarming symptoms of love. The young man had been the first admirer, but she trusted there was no other hold, and that there would be no serious difficulty, on Harriet’s side, to oppose any friendly arrangement of her own.\n\nThey met Mr. Martin the very next day, as they were walking on the Donwell road. He was on foot, and after looking very respectfully at her, looked with most unfeigned satisfaction at her companion. Emma was not sorry to have such an opportunity of survey; and walking a few yards forward, while they talked together, soon made her quick eye sufficiently acquainted with Mr. Robert Martin. His appearance was very neat, and he looked like a sensible young man, but his person had no other advantage; and when he came to be contrasted with gentlemen, she thought he must lose all the ground he had gained in Harriet’s inclination. Harriet was not insensible of manner; she had voluntarily noticed her father’s gentleness with admiration as well as wonder. Mr. Martin looked as if he did not know what manner was.\n\nThey remained but a few minutes together, as Miss Woodhouse must not be kept waiting; and Harriet then came running to her with a smiling face, and in a flutter of spirits, which Miss Woodhouse hoped very soon to compose.\n\n“Only think of our happening to meet him!—How very odd! It was quite a chance, he said, that he had not gone round by Randalls. He did not think we ever walked this road. He thought we walked towards Randalls most days. He has not been able to get the Romance of the Forest yet. He was so busy the last time he was at Kingston that he quite forgot it, but he goes again to-morrow. So very odd we should happen to meet! Well, Miss Woodhouse, is he like what you expected? What do you think of him? Do you think him so very plain?”\n\n“He is very plain, undoubtedly—remarkably plain:—but that is nothing compared with his entire want of gentility. I had no right to expect much, and I did not expect much; but I had no idea that he could be so very clownish, so totally without air. I had imagined him, I confess, a degree or two nearer gentility.”\n\n“To be sure,” said Harriet, in a mortified voice, “he is not so genteel as real gentlemen.”\n\n“I think, Harriet, since your acquaintance with us, you have been repeatedly in the company of some such very real gentlemen, that you must yourself be struck with the difference in Mr. Martin. At Hartfield, you have had very good specimens of well educated, well bred men. I should be surprized if, after seeing them, you could be in company with Mr. Martin again without perceiving him to be a very inferior creature—and rather wondering at yourself for having ever thought him at all agreeable before. Do not you begin to feel that now? Were not you struck? I am sure you must have been struck by his awkward look and abrupt manner, and the uncouthness of a voice which I heard to be wholly unmodulated as I stood here.”\n\n“Certainly, he is not like Mr. Knightley. He has not such a fine air and way of walking as Mr. Knightley. I see the difference plain enough. But Mr. Knightley is so very fine a man!”\n\n“Mr. Knightley’s air is so remarkably good that it is not fair to compare Mr. Martin with him. You might not see one in a hundred with gentleman so plainly written as in Mr. Knightley. But he is not the only gentleman you have been lately used to. What say you to Mr. Weston and Mr. Elton? Compare Mr. Martin with either of them. Compare their manner of carrying themselves; of walking; of speaking; of being silent. You must see the difference.”\n\n“Oh yes!—there is a great difference. But Mr. Weston is almost an old man. Mr. Weston must be between forty and fifty.”\n\n“Which makes his good manners the more valuable. The older a person grows, Harriet, the more important it is that their manners should not be bad; the more glaring and disgusting any loudness, or coarseness, or awkwardness becomes. What is passable in youth is detestable in later age. Mr. Martin is now awkward and abrupt; what will he be at Mr. Weston’s time of life?”\n\n“There is no saying, indeed,” replied Harriet rather solemnly.\n\n“But there may be pretty good guessing. He will be a completely gross, vulgar farmer, totally inattentive to appearances, and thinking of nothing but profit and loss.”\n\n“Will he, indeed? That will be very bad.”\n\n“How much his business engrosses him already is very plain from the circumstance of his forgetting to inquire for the book you recommended. He was a great deal too full of the market to think of any thing else—which is just as it should be, for a thriving man. What has he to do with books? And I have no doubt that he will thrive, and be a very rich man in time—and his being illiterate and coarse need not disturb us.”\n\n“I wonder he did not remember the book”—was all Harriet’s answer, and spoken with a degree of grave displeasure which Emma thought might be safely left to itself. She, therefore, said no more for some time. Her next beginning was,\n\n“In one respect, perhaps, Mr. Elton’s manners are superior to Mr. Knightley’s or Mr. Weston’s. They have more gentleness. They might be more safely held up as a pattern. There is an openness, a quickness, almost a bluntness in Mr. Weston, which every body likes in him, because there is so much good-humour with it—but that would not do to be copied. Neither would Mr. Knightley’s downright, decided, commanding sort of manner, though it suits him very well; his figure, and look, and situation in life seem to allow it; but if any young man were to set about copying him, he would not be sufferable. On the contrary, I think a young man might be very safely recommended to take Mr. Elton as a model. Mr. Elton is good-humoured, cheerful, obliging, and gentle. He seems to me to be grown particularly gentle of late. I do not know whether he has any design of ingratiating himself with either of us, Harriet, by additional softness, but it strikes me that his manners are softer than they used to be. If he means any thing, it must be to please you. Did not I tell you what he said of you the other day?”\n\nShe then repeated some warm personal praise which she had drawn from Mr. Elton, and now did full justice to; and Harriet blushed and smiled, and said she had always thought Mr. Elton very agreeable.\n\nMr. Elton was the very person fixed on by Emma for driving the young farmer out of Harriet’s head. She thought it would be an excellent match; and only too palpably desirable, natural, and probable, for her to have much merit in planning it. She feared it was what every body else must think of and predict. It was not likely, however, that any body should have equalled her in the date of the plan, as it had entered her brain during the very first evening of Harriet’s coming to Hartfield. The longer she considered it, the greater was her sense of its expediency. Mr. Elton’s situation was most suitable, quite the gentleman himself, and without low connexions; at the same time, not of any family that could fairly object to the doubtful birth of Harriet. He had a comfortable home for her, and Emma imagined a very sufficient income; for though the vicarage of Highbury was not large, he was known to have some independent property; and she thought very highly of him as a good-humoured, well-meaning, respectable young man, without any deficiency of useful understanding or knowledge of the world.\n\nShe had already satisfied herself that he thought Harriet a beautiful girl, which she trusted, with such frequent meetings at Hartfield, was foundation enough on his side; and on Harriet’s there could be little doubt that the idea of being preferred by him would have all the usual weight and efficacy. And he was really a very pleasing young man, a young man whom any woman not fastidious might like. He was reckoned very handsome; his person much admired in general, though not by her, there being a want of elegance of feature which she could not dispense with:—but the girl who could be gratified by a Robert Martin’s riding about the country to get walnuts for her might very well be conquered by Mr. Elton’s admiration.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter V\n\n“I do not know what your opinion may be, Mrs. Weston,” said Mr. Knightley, “of this great intimacy between Emma and Harriet Smith, but I think it a bad thing.”\n\n“A bad thing! Do you really think it a bad thing?—why so?”\n\n“I think they will neither of them do the other any good.”\n\n“You surprize me! Emma must do Harriet good: and by supplying her with a new object of interest, Harriet may be said to do Emma good. I have been seeing their intimacy with the greatest pleasure. How very differently we feel!—Not think they will do each other any good! This will certainly be the beginning of one of our quarrels about Emma, Mr. Knightley.”\n\n“Perhaps you think I am come on purpose to quarrel with you, knowing Weston to be out, and that you must still fight your own battle.”\n\n“Mr. Weston would undoubtedly support me, if he were here, for he thinks exactly as I do on the subject. We were speaking of it only yesterday, and agreeing how fortunate it was for Emma, that there should be such a girl in Highbury for her to associate with. Mr. Knightley, I shall not allow you to be a fair judge in this case. You are so much used to live alone, that you do not know the value of a companion; and, perhaps no man can be a good judge of the comfort a woman feels in the society of one of her own sex, after being used to it all her life. I can imagine your objection to Harriet Smith. She is not the superior young woman which Emma’s friend ought to be. But on the other hand, as Emma wants to see her better informed, it will be an inducement to her to read more herself. They will read together. She means it, I know.”\n\n“Emma has been meaning to read more ever since she was twelve years old. I have seen a great many lists of her drawing-up at various times of books that she meant to read regularly through—and very good lists they were—very well chosen, and very neatly arranged—sometimes alphabetically, and sometimes by some other rule. The list she drew up when only fourteen—I remember thinking it did her judgment so much credit, that I preserved it some time; and I dare say she may have made out a very good list now. But I have done with expecting any course of steady reading from Emma. She will never submit to any thing requiring industry and patience, and a subjection of the fancy to the understanding. Where Miss Taylor failed to stimulate, I may safely affirm that Harriet Smith will do nothing.—You never could persuade her to read half so much as you wished.—You know you could not.”\n\n“I dare say,” replied Mrs. Weston, smiling, “that I thought so then;—but since we have parted, I can never remember Emma’s omitting to do any thing I wished.”\n\n“There is hardly any desiring to refresh such a memory as that,”—said Mr. Knightley, feelingly; and for a moment or two he had done. “But I,” he soon added, “who have had no such charm thrown over my senses, must still see, hear, and remember. Emma is spoiled by being the cleverest of her family. At ten years old, she had the misfortune of being able to answer questions which puzzled her sister at seventeen. She was always quick and assured: Isabella slow and diffident. And ever since she was twelve, Emma has been mistress of the house and of you all. In her mother she lost the only person able to cope with her. She inherits her mother’s talents, and must have been under subjection to her.”\n\n“I should have been sorry, Mr. Knightley, to be dependent on your recommendation, had I quitted Mr. Woodhouse’s family and wanted another situation; I do not think you would have spoken a good word for me to any body. I am sure you always thought me unfit for the office I held.”\n\n“Yes,” said he, smiling. “You are better placed here; very fit for a wife, but not at all for a governess. But you were preparing yourself to be an excellent wife all the time you were at Hartfield. You might not give Emma such a complete education as your powers would seem to promise; but you were receiving a very good education from her, on the very material matrimonial point of submitting your own will, and doing as you were bid; and if Weston had asked me to recommend him a wife, I should certainly have named Miss Taylor.”\n\n“Thank you. There will be very little merit in making a good wife to such a man as Mr. Weston.”\n\n“Why, to own the truth, I am afraid you are rather thrown away, and that with every disposition to bear, there will be nothing to be borne. We will not despair, however. Weston may grow cross from the wantonness of comfort, or his son may plague him.”\n\n“I hope not that.—It is not likely. No, Mr. Knightley, do not foretell vexation from that quarter.”\n\n“Not I, indeed. I only name possibilities. I do not pretend to Emma’s genius for foretelling and guessing. I hope, with all my heart, the young man may be a Weston in merit, and a Churchill in fortune.—But Harriet Smith—I have not half done about Harriet Smith. I think her the very worst sort of companion that Emma could possibly have. She knows nothing herself, and looks upon Emma as knowing every thing. She is a flatterer in all her ways; and so much the worse, because undesigned. Her ignorance is hourly flattery. How can Emma imagine she has any thing to learn herself, while Harriet is presenting such a delightful inferiority? And as for Harriet, I will venture to say that she cannot gain by the acquaintance. Hartfield will only put her out of conceit with all the other places she belongs to. She will grow just refined enough to be uncomfortable with those among whom birth and circumstances have placed her home. I am much mistaken if Emma’s doctrines give any strength of mind, or tend at all to make a girl adapt herself rationally to the varieties of her situation in life.—They only give a little polish.”\n\n“I either depend more upon Emma’s good sense than you do, or am more anxious for her present comfort; for I cannot lament the acquaintance. How well she looked last night!”\n\n“Oh! you would rather talk of her person than her mind, would you? Very well; I shall not attempt to deny Emma’s being pretty.”\n\n“Pretty! say beautiful rather. Can you imagine any thing nearer perfect beauty than Emma altogether—face and figure?”\n\n“I do not know what I could imagine, but I confess that I have seldom seen a face or figure more pleasing to me than hers. But I am a partial old friend.”\n\n“Such an eye!—the true hazle eye—and so brilliant! regular features, open countenance, with a complexion! oh! what a bloom of full health, and such a pretty height and size; such a firm and upright figure! There is health, not merely in her bloom, but in her air, her head, her glance. One hears sometimes of a child being ‘the picture of health;’ now, Emma always gives me the idea of being the complete picture of grown-up health. She is loveliness itself. Mr. Knightley, is not she?”\n\n“I have not a fault to find with her person,” he replied. “I think her all you describe. I love to look at her; and I will add this praise, that I do not think her personally vain. Considering how very handsome she is, she appears to be little occupied with it; her vanity lies another way. Mrs. Weston, I am not to be talked out of my dislike of Harriet Smith, or my dread of its doing them both harm.”\n\n“And I, Mr. Knightley, am equally stout in my confidence of its not doing them any harm. With all dear Emma’s little faults, she is an excellent creature. Where shall we see a better daughter, or a kinder sister, or a truer friend? No, no; she has qualities which may be trusted; she will never lead any one really wrong; she will make no lasting blunder; where Emma errs once, she is in the right a hundred times.”\n\n“Very well; I will not plague you any more. Emma shall be an angel, and I will keep my spleen to myself till Christmas brings John and Isabella. John loves Emma with a reasonable and therefore not a blind affection, and Isabella always thinks as he does; except when he is not quite frightened enough about the children. I am sure of having their opinions with me.”\n\n“I know that you all love her really too well to be unjust or unkind; but excuse me, Mr. Knightley, if I take the liberty (I consider myself, you know, as having somewhat of the privilege of speech that Emma’s mother might have had) the liberty of hinting that I do not think any possible good can arise from Harriet Smith’s intimacy being made a matter of much discussion among you. Pray excuse me; but supposing any little inconvenience may be apprehended from the intimacy, it cannot be expected that Emma, accountable to nobody but her father, who perfectly approves the acquaintance, should put an end to it, so long as it is a source of pleasure to herself. It has been so many years my province to give advice, that you cannot be surprized, Mr. Knightley, at this little remains of office.”\n\n“Not at all,” cried he; “I am much obliged to you for it. It is very good advice, and it shall have a better fate than your advice has often found; for it shall be attended to.”\n\n“Mrs. John Knightley is easily alarmed, and might be made unhappy about her sister.”\n\n“Be satisfied,” said he, “I will not raise any outcry. I will keep my ill-humour to myself. I have a very sincere interest in Emma. Isabella does not seem more my sister; has never excited a greater interest; perhaps hardly so great. There is an anxiety, a curiosity in what one feels for Emma. I wonder what will become of her!”\n\n“So do I,” said Mrs. Weston gently, “very much.”\n\n“She always declares she will never marry, which, of course, means just nothing at all. But I have no idea that she has yet ever seen a man she cared for. It would not be a bad thing for her to be very much in love with a proper object. I should like to see Emma in love, and in some doubt of a return; it would do her good. But there is nobody hereabouts to attach her; and she goes so seldom from home.”\n\n“There does, indeed, seem as little to tempt her to break her resolution at present,” said Mrs. Weston, “as can well be; and while she is so happy at Hartfield, I cannot wish her to be forming any attachment which would be creating such difficulties on poor Mr. Woodhouse’s account. I do not recommend matrimony at present to Emma, though I mean no slight to the state, I assure you.”\n\nPart of her meaning was to conceal some favourite thoughts of her own and Mr. Weston’s on the subject, as much as possible. There were wishes at Randalls respecting Emma’s destiny, but it was not desirable to have them suspected; and the quiet transition which Mr. Knightley soon afterwards made to “What does Weston think of the weather; shall we have rain?” convinced her that he had nothing more to say or surmise about Hartfield.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter VI\n\nEmma could not feel a doubt of having given Harriet’s fancy a proper direction and raised the gratitude of her young vanity to a very good purpose, for she found her decidedly more sensible than before of Mr. Elton’s being a remarkably handsome man, with most agreeable manners; and as she had no hesitation in following up the assurance of his admiration by agreeable hints, she was soon pretty confident of creating as much liking on Harriet’s side, as there could be any occasion for. She was quite convinced of Mr. Elton’s being in the fairest way of falling in love, if not in love already. She had no scruple with regard to him. He talked of Harriet, and praised her so warmly, that she could not suppose any thing wanting which a little time would not add. His perception of the striking improvement of Harriet’s manner, since her introduction at Hartfield, was not one of the least agreeable proofs of his growing attachment.\n\n“You have given Miss Smith all that she required,” said he; “you have made her graceful and easy. She was a beautiful creature when she came to you, but, in my opinion, the attractions you have added are infinitely superior to what she received from nature.”\n\n“I am glad you think I have been useful to her; but Harriet only wanted drawing out, and receiving a few, very few hints. She had all the natural grace of sweetness of temper and artlessness in herself. I have done very little.”\n\n“If it were admissible to contradict a lady,” said the gallant Mr. Elton—\n\n“I have perhaps given her a little more decision of character, have taught her to think on points which had not fallen in her way before.”\n\n“Exactly so; that is what principally strikes me. So much superadded decision of character! Skilful has been the hand!”\n\n“Great has been the pleasure, I am sure. I never met with a disposition more truly amiable.”\n\n“I have no doubt of it.” And it was spoken with a sort of sighing animation, which had a vast deal of the lover. She was not less pleased another day with the manner in which he seconded a sudden wish of hers, to have Harriet’s picture.\n\n“Did you ever have your likeness taken, Harriet?” said she: “did you ever sit for your picture?”\n\nHarriet was on the point of leaving the room, and only stopt to say, with a very interesting naïveté,\n\n“Oh! dear, no, never.”\n\nNo sooner was she out of sight, than Emma exclaimed,\n\n“What an exquisite possession a good picture of her would be! I would give any money for it. I almost long to attempt her likeness myself. You do not know it I dare say, but two or three years ago I had a great passion for taking likenesses, and attempted several of my friends, and was thought to have a tolerable eye in general. But from one cause or another, I gave it up in disgust. But really, I could almost venture, if Harriet would sit to me. It would be such a delight to have her picture!”\n\n“Let me entreat you,” cried Mr. Elton; “it would indeed be a delight! Let me entreat you, Miss Woodhouse, to exercise so charming a talent in favour of your friend. I know what your drawings are. How could you suppose me ignorant? Is not this room rich in specimens of your landscapes and flowers; and has not Mrs. Weston some inimitable figure-pieces in her drawing-room, at Randalls?”\n\nYes, good man!—thought Emma—but what has all that to do with taking likenesses? You know nothing of drawing. Don’t pretend to be in raptures about mine. Keep your raptures for Harriet’s face. “Well, if you give me such kind encouragement, Mr. Elton, I believe I shall try what I can do. Harriet’s features are very delicate, which makes a likeness difficult; and yet there is a peculiarity in the shape of the eye and the lines about the mouth which one ought to catch.”\n\n“Exactly so—The shape of the eye and the lines about the mouth—I have not a doubt of your success. Pray, pray attempt it. As you will do it, it will indeed, to use your own words, be an exquisite possession.”\n\n“But I am afraid, Mr. Elton, Harriet will not like to sit. She thinks so little of her own beauty. Did not you observe her manner of answering me? How completely it meant, ‘why should my picture be drawn?’”\n\n“Oh! yes, I observed it, I assure you. It was not lost on me. But still I cannot imagine she would not be persuaded.”\n\nHarriet was soon back again, and the proposal almost immediately made; and she had no scruples which could stand many minutes against the earnest pressing of both the others. Emma wished to go to work directly, and therefore produced the portfolio containing her various attempts at portraits, for not one of them had ever been finished, that they might decide together on the best size for Harriet. Her many beginnings were displayed. Miniatures, half-lengths, whole-lengths, pencil, crayon, and water-colours had been all tried in turn. She had always wanted to do every thing, and had made more progress both in drawing and music than many might have done with so little labour as she would ever submit to. She played and sang;—and drew in almost every style; but steadiness had always been wanting; and in nothing had she approached the degree of excellence which she would have been glad to command, and ought not to have failed of. She was not much deceived as to her own skill either as an artist or a musician, but she was not unwilling to have others deceived, or sorry to know her reputation for accomplishment often higher than it deserved.\n\nThere was merit in every drawing—in the least finished, perhaps the most; her style was spirited; but had there been much less, or had there been ten times more, the delight and admiration of her two companions would have been the same. They were both in ecstasies. A likeness pleases every body; and Miss Woodhouse’s performances must be capital.\n\n“No great variety of faces for you,” said Emma. “I had only my own family to study from. There is my father—another of my father—but the idea of sitting for his picture made him so nervous, that I could only take him by stealth; neither of them very like therefore. Mrs. Weston again, and again, and again, you see. Dear Mrs. Weston! always my kindest friend on every occasion. She would sit whenever I asked her. There is my sister; and really quite her own little elegant figure!—and the face not unlike. I should have made a good likeness of her, if she would have sat longer, but she was in such a hurry to have me draw her four children that she would not be quiet. Then, here come all my attempts at three of those four children;—there they are, Henry and John and Bella, from one end of the sheet to the other, and any one of them might do for any one of the rest. She was so eager to have them drawn that I could not refuse; but there is no making children of three or four years old stand still you know; nor can it be very easy to take any likeness of them, beyond the air and complexion, unless they are coarser featured than any of mama’s children ever were. Here is my sketch of the fourth, who was a baby. I took him as he was sleeping on the sofa, and it is as strong a likeness of his cockade as you would wish to see. He had nestled down his head most conveniently. That’s very like. I am rather proud of little George. The corner of the sofa is very good. Then here is my last,”—unclosing a pretty sketch of a gentleman in small size, whole-length—“my last and my best—my brother, Mr. John Knightley.—This did not want much of being finished, when I put it away in a pet, and vowed I would never take another likeness. I could not help being provoked; for after all my pains, and when I had really made a very good likeness of it—(Mrs. Weston and I were quite agreed in thinking it very like)—only too handsome—too flattering—but that was a fault on the right side”—after all this, came poor dear Isabella’s cold approbation of—“Yes, it was a little like—but to be sure it did not do him justice. We had had a great deal of trouble in persuading him to sit at all. It was made a great favour of; and altogether it was more than I could bear; and so I never would finish it, to have it apologised over as an unfavourable likeness, to every morning visitor in Brunswick Square;—and, as I said, I did then forswear ever drawing any body again. But for Harriet’s sake, or rather for my own, and as there are no husbands and wives in the case at present, I will break my resolution now.”\n\nMr. Elton seemed very properly struck and delighted by the idea, and was repeating, “No husbands and wives in the case at present indeed, as you observe. Exactly so. No husbands and wives,” with so interesting a consciousness, that Emma began to consider whether she had not better leave them together at once. But as she wanted to be drawing, the declaration must wait a little longer.\n\nShe had soon fixed on the size and sort of portrait. It was to be a whole-length in water-colours, like Mr. John Knightley’s, and was destined, if she could please herself, to hold a very honourable station over the mantelpiece.\n\nThe sitting began; and Harriet, smiling and blushing, and afraid of not keeping her attitude and countenance, presented a very sweet mixture of youthful expression to the steady eyes of the artist. But there was no doing any thing, with Mr. Elton fidgeting behind her and watching every touch. She gave him credit for stationing himself where he might gaze and gaze again without offence; but was really obliged to put an end to it, and request him to place himself elsewhere. It then occurred to her to employ him in reading.\n\n“If he would be so good as to read to them, it would be a kindness indeed! It would amuse away the difficulties of her part, and lessen the irksomeness of Miss Smith’s.”\n\nMr. Elton was only too happy. Harriet listened, and Emma drew in peace. She must allow him to be still frequently coming to look; any thing less would certainly have been too little in a lover; and he was ready at the smallest intermission of the pencil, to jump up and see the progress, and be charmed.—There was no being displeased with such an encourager, for his admiration made him discern a likeness almost before it was possible. She could not respect his eye, but his love and his complaisance were unexceptionable.\n\nThe sitting was altogether very satisfactory; she was quite enough pleased with the first day’s sketch to wish to go on. There was no want of likeness, she had been fortunate in the attitude, and as she meant to throw in a little improvement to the figure, to give a little more height, and considerably more elegance, she had great confidence of its being in every way a pretty drawing at last, and of its filling its destined place with credit to them both—a standing memorial of the beauty of one, the skill of the other, and the friendship of both; with as many other agreeable associations as Mr. Elton’s very promising attachment was likely to add.\n\nHarriet was to sit again the next day; and Mr. Elton, just as he ought, entreated for the permission of attending and reading to them again.\n\n“By all means. We shall be most happy to consider you as one of the party.”\n\nThe same civilities and courtesies, the same success and satisfaction, took place on the morrow, and accompanied the whole progress of the picture, which was rapid and happy. Every body who saw it was pleased, but Mr. Elton was in continual raptures, and defended it through every criticism.\n\n“Miss Woodhouse has given her friend the only beauty she wanted,”—observed Mrs. Weston to him—not in the least suspecting that she was addressing a lover.—“The expression of the eye is most correct, but Miss Smith has not those eyebrows and eyelashes. It is the fault of her face that she has them not.”\n\n“Do you think so?” replied he. “I cannot agree with you. It appears to me a most perfect resemblance in every feature. I never saw such a likeness in my life. We must allow for the effect of shade, you know.”\n\n“You have made her too tall, Emma,” said Mr. Knightley.\n\nEmma knew that she had, but would not own it; and Mr. Elton warmly added,\n\n“Oh no! certainly not too tall; not in the least too tall. Consider, she is sitting down—which naturally presents a different—which in short gives exactly the idea—and the proportions must be preserved, you know. Proportions, fore-shortening.—Oh no! it gives one exactly the idea of such a height as Miss Smith’s. Exactly so indeed!”\n\n“It is very pretty,” said Mr. Woodhouse. “So prettily done! Just as your drawings always are, my dear. I do not know any body who draws so well as you do. The only thing I do not thoroughly like is, that she seems to be sitting out of doors, with only a little shawl over her shoulders—and it makes one think she must catch cold.”\n\n“But, my dear papa, it is supposed to be summer; a warm day in summer. Look at the tree.”\n\n“But it is never safe to sit out of doors, my dear.”\n\n“You, sir, may say any thing,” cried Mr. Elton, “but I must confess that I regard it as a most happy thought, the placing of Miss Smith out of doors; and the tree is touched with such inimitable spirit! Any other situation would have been much less in character. The naïveté of Miss Smith’s manners—and altogether—Oh, it is most admirable! I cannot keep my eyes from it. I never saw such a likeness.”\n\nThe next thing wanted was to get the picture framed; and here were a few difficulties. It must be done directly; it must be done in London; the order must go through the hands of some intelligent person whose taste could be depended on; and Isabella, the usual doer of all commissions, must not be applied to, because it was December, and Mr. Woodhouse could not bear the idea of her stirring out of her house in the fogs of December. But no sooner was the distress known to Mr. Elton, than it was removed. His gallantry was always on the alert. “Might he be trusted with the commission, what infinite pleasure should he have in executing it! he could ride to London at any time. It was impossible to say how much he should be gratified by being employed on such an errand.”\n\n“He was too good!—she could not endure the thought!—she would not give him such a troublesome office for the world,”—brought on the desired repetition of entreaties and assurances,—and a very few minutes settled the business.\n\nMr. Elton was to take the drawing to London, chuse the frame, and give the directions; and Emma thought she could so pack it as to ensure its safety without much incommoding him, while he seemed mostly fearful of not being incommoded enough.\n\n“What a precious deposit!” said he with a tender sigh, as he received it.\n\n“This man is almost too gallant to be in love,” thought Emma. “I should say so, but that I suppose there may be a hundred different ways of being in love. He is an excellent young man, and will suit Harriet exactly; it will be an ‘Exactly so,’ as he says himself; but he does sigh and languish, and study for compliments rather more than I could endure as a principal. I come in for a pretty good share as a second. But it is his gratitude on Harriet’s account.”\n\n# Volume I — Chapter VII\n\nThe very day of Mr. Elton’s going to London produced a fresh occasion for Emma’s services towards her friend. Harriet had been at Hartfield, as usual, soon after breakfast; and, after a time, had gone home to return again to dinner: she returned, and sooner than had been talked of, and with an agitated, hurried look, announcing something extraordinary to have happened which she was longing to tell. Half a minute brought it all out. She had heard, as soon as she got back to Mrs. Goddard’s, that Mr. Martin had been there an hour before, and finding she was not at home, nor particularly expected, had left a little parcel for her from one of his sisters, and gone away; and on opening this parcel, she had actually found, besides the two songs which she had lent Elizabeth to copy, a letter to herself; and this letter was from him, from Mr. Martin, and contained a direct proposal of marriage. “Who could have thought it? She was so surprized she did not know what to do. Yes, quite a proposal of marriage; and a very good letter, at least she thought so. And he wrote as if he really loved her very much—but she did not know—and so, she was come as fast as she could to ask Miss Woodhouse what she should do.—” Emma was half-ashamed of her friend for seeming so pleased and so doubtful.\n\n“Upon my word,” she cried, “the young man is determined not to lose any thing for want of asking. He will connect himself well if he can.”\n\n“Will you read the letter?” cried Harriet. “Pray do. I’d rather you would.”\n\nEmma was not sorry to be pressed. She read, and was surprized. The style of the letter was much above her expectation. There were not merely no grammatical errors, but as a composition it would not have disgraced a gentleman; the language, though plain, was strong and unaffected, and the sentiments it conveyed very much to the credit of the writer. It was short, but expressed good sense, warm attachment, liberality, propriety, even delicacy of feeling. She paused over it, while Harriet stood anxiously watching for her opinion, with a “Well, well,” and was at last forced to add, “Is it a good letter? or is it too short?”\n\n“Yes, indeed, a very good letter,” replied Emma rather slowly—“so good a letter, Harriet, that every thing considered, I think one of his sisters must have helped him. I can hardly imagine the young man whom I saw talking with you the other day could express himself so well, if left quite to his own powers, and yet it is not the style of a woman; no, certainly, it is too strong and concise; not diffuse enough for a woman. No doubt he is a sensible man, and I suppose may have a natural talent for—thinks strongly and clearly—and when he takes a pen in hand, his thoughts naturally find proper words. It is so with some men. Yes, I understand the sort of mind. Vigorous, decided, with sentiments to a certain point, not coarse. A better written letter, Harriet (returning it,) than I had expected.”\n\n“Well,” said the still waiting Harriet;—“well—and—and what shall I do?”\n\n“What shall you do! In what respect? Do you mean with regard to this letter?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But what are you in doubt of? You must answer it of course—and speedily.”\n\n“Yes. But what shall I say? Dear Miss Woodhouse, do advise me.”\n\n“Oh no, no! the letter had much better be all your own. You will express yourself very properly, I am sure. There is no danger of your not being intelligible, which is the first thing. Your meaning must be unequivocal; no doubts or demurs: and such expressions of gratitude and concern for the pain you are inflicting as propriety requires, will present themselves unbidden to your mind, I am persuaded. You need not be prompted to write with the appearance of sorrow for his disappointment.”\n\n“You think I ought to refuse him then,” said Harriet, looking down.\n\n“Ought to refuse him! My dear Harriet, what do you mean? Are you in any doubt as to that? I thought—but I beg your pardon, perhaps I have been under a mistake. I certainly have been misunderstanding you, if you feel in doubt as to the purport of your answer. I had imagined you were consulting me only as to the wording of it.”\n\nHarriet was silent. With a little reserve of manner, Emma continued:\n\n“You mean to return a favourable answer, I collect.”\n\n“No, I do not; that is, I do not mean—What shall I do? What would you advise me to do? Pray, dear Miss Woodhouse, tell me what I ought to do.”\n\n“I shall not give you any advice, Harriet. I will have nothing to do with it. This is a point which you must settle with your feelings.”\n\n“I had no notion that he liked me so very much,” said Harriet, contemplating the letter. For a little while Emma persevered in her silence; but beginning to apprehend the bewitching flattery of that letter might be too powerful, she thought it best to say,\n\n“I lay it down as a general rule, Harriet, that if a woman doubts as to whether she should accept a man or not, she certainly ought to refuse him. If she can hesitate as to ‘Yes,’ she ought to say ‘No’ directly. It is not a state to be safely entered into with doubtful feelings, with half a heart. I thought it my duty as a friend, and older than yourself, to say thus much to you. But do not imagine that I want to influence you.”\n\n“Oh! no, I am sure you are a great deal too kind to—but if you would just advise me what I had best do—No, no, I do not mean that—As you say, one’s mind ought to be quite made up—One should not be hesitating—It is a very serious thing.—It will be safer to say ‘No,’ perhaps.—Do you think I had better say ‘No?’”\n\n“Not for the world,” said Emma, smiling graciously, “would I advise you either way. You must be the best judge of your own happiness. If you prefer Mr. Martin to every other person; if you think him the most agreeable man you have ever been in company with, why should you hesitate? You blush, Harriet.—Does any body else occur to you at this moment under such a definition? Harriet, Harriet, do not deceive yourself; do not be run away with by gratitude and compassion. At this moment whom are you thinking of?”\n\nThe symptoms were favourable.—Instead of answering, Harriet turned away confused, and stood thoughtfully by the fire; and though the letter was still in her hand, it was now mechanically twisted about without regard. Emma waited the result with impatience, but not without strong hopes. At last, with some hesitation, Harriet said—\n\n“Miss Woodhouse, as you will not give me your opinion, I must do as well as I can by myself; and I have now quite determined, and really almost made up my mind—to refuse Mr. Martin. Do you think I am right?”\n\n“Perfectly, perfectly right, my dearest Harriet; you are doing just what you ought. While you were at all in suspense I kept my feelings to myself, but now that you are so completely decided I have no hesitation in approving. Dear Harriet, I give myself joy of this. It would have grieved me to lose your acquaintance, which must have been the consequence of your marrying Mr. Martin. While you were in the smallest degree wavering, I said nothing about it, because I would not influence; but it would have been the loss of a friend to me. I could not have visited Mrs. Robert Martin, of Abbey-Mill Farm. Now I am secure of you for ever.”\n\nHarriet had not surmised her own danger, but the idea of it struck her forcibly.\n\n“You could not have visited me!” she cried, looking aghast. “No, to be sure you could not; but I never thought of that before. That would have been too dreadful!—What an escape!—Dear Miss Woodhouse, I would not give up the pleasure and honour of being intimate with you for any thing in the world.”\n\n“Indeed, Harriet, it would have been a severe pang to lose you; but it must have been. You would have thrown yourself out of all good society. I must have given you up.”\n\n“Dear me!—How should I ever have borne it! It would have killed me never to come to Hartfield any more!”\n\n“Dear affectionate creature!—You banished to Abbey-Mill Farm!—You confined to the society of the illiterate and vulgar all your life! I wonder how the young man could have the assurance to ask it. He must have a pretty good opinion of himself.”\n\n“I do not think he is conceited either, in general,” said Harriet, her conscience opposing such censure; “at least, he is very good natured, and I shall always feel much obliged to him, and have a great regard for—but that is quite a different thing from—and you know, though he may like me, it does not follow that I should—and certainly I must confess that since my visiting here I have seen people—and if one comes to compare them, person and manners, there is no comparison at all, one is so very handsome and agreeable. However, I do really think Mr. Martin a very amiable young man, and have a great opinion of him; and his being so much attached to me—and his writing such a letter—but as to leaving you, it is what I would not do upon any consideration.”\n\n“Thank you, thank you, my own sweet little friend. We will not be parted. A woman is not to marry a man merely because she is asked, or because he is attached to her, and can write a tolerable letter.”\n\n“Oh no;—and it is but a short letter too.”\n\nEmma felt the bad taste of her friend, but let it pass with a “very true; and it would be a small consolation to her, for the clownish manner which might be offending her every hour of the day, to know that her husband could write a good letter.”\n\n“Oh! yes, very. Nobody cares for a letter; the thing is, to be always happy with pleasant companions. I am quite determined to refuse him. But how shall I do? What shall I say?”\n\nEmma assured her there would be no difficulty in the answer, and advised its being written directly, which was agreed to, in the hope of her assistance; and though Emma continued to protest against any assistance being wanted, it was in fact given in the formation of every sentence. The looking over his letter again, in replying to it, had such a softening tendency, that it was particularly necessary to brace her up with a few decisive expressions; and she was so very much concerned at the idea of making him unhappy, and thought so much of what his mother and sisters would think and say, and was so anxious that they should not fancy her ungrateful, that Emma believed if the young man had come in her way at that moment, he would have been accepted after all.\n\nThis letter, however, was written, and sealed, and sent. The business was finished, and Harriet safe. She was rather low all the evening, but Emma could allow for her amiable regrets, and sometimes relieved them by speaking of her own affection, sometimes by bringing forward the idea of Mr. Elton.\n\n“I shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again,” was said in rather a sorrowful tone.\n\n“Nor, if you were, could I ever bear to part with you, my Harriet. You are a great deal too necessary at Hartfield to be spared to Abbey-Mill.”\n\n“And I am sure I should never want to go there; for I am never happy but at Hartfield.”\n\nSome time afterwards it was, “I think Mrs. Goddard would be very much surprized if she knew what had happened. I am sure Miss Nash would—for Miss Nash thinks her own sister very well married, and it is only a linen-draper.”\n\n“One should be sorry to see greater pride or refinement in the teacher of a school, Harriet. I dare say Miss Nash would envy you such an opportunity as this of being married. Even this conquest would appear valuable in her eyes. As to any thing superior for you, I suppose she is quite in the dark. The attentions of a certain person can hardly be among the tittle-tattle of Highbury yet. Hitherto I fancy you and I are the only people to whom his looks and manners have explained themselves.”\n\nHarriet blushed and smiled, and said something about wondering that people should like her so much. The idea of Mr. Elton was certainly cheering; but still, after a time, she was tender-hearted again towards the rejected Mr. Martin.\n\n“Now he has got my letter,” said she softly. “I wonder what they are all doing—whether his sisters know—if he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too. I hope he will not mind it so very much.”\n\n“Let us think of those among our absent friends who are more cheerfully employed,” cried Emma. “At this moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is shewing your picture to his mother and sisters, telling how much more beautiful is the original, and after being asked for it five or six times, allowing them to hear your name, your own dear name.”\n\n“My picture!—But he has left my picture in Bond-street.”\n\n“Has he so!—Then I know nothing of Mr. Elton. No, my dear little modest Harriet, depend upon it the picture will not be in Bond-street till just before he mounts his horse to-morrow. It is his companion all this evening, his solace, his delight. It opens his designs to his family, it introduces you among them, it diffuses through the party those pleasantest feelings of our nature, eager curiosity and warm prepossession. How cheerful, how animated, how suspicious, how busy their imaginations all are!”\n\nHarriet smiled again, and her smiles grew stronger.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter VIII\n\nHarriet slept at Hartfield that night. For some weeks past she had been spending more than half her time there, and gradually getting to have a bed-room appropriated to herself; and Emma judged it best in every respect, safest and kindest, to keep her with them as much as possible just at present. She was obliged to go the next morning for an hour or two to Mrs. Goddard’s, but it was then to be settled that she should return to Hartfield, to make a regular visit of some days.\n\nWhile she was gone, Mr. Knightley called, and sat some time with Mr. Woodhouse and Emma, till Mr. Woodhouse, who had previously made up his mind to walk out, was persuaded by his daughter not to defer it, and was induced by the entreaties of both, though against the scruples of his own civility, to leave Mr. Knightley for that purpose. Mr. Knightley, who had nothing of ceremony about him, was offering by his short, decided answers, an amusing contrast to the protracted apologies and civil hesitations of the other.\n\n“Well, I believe, if you will excuse me, Mr. Knightley, if you will not consider me as doing a very rude thing, I shall take Emma’s advice and go out for a quarter of an hour. As the sun is out, I believe I had better take my three turns while I can. I treat you without ceremony, Mr. Knightley. We invalids think we are privileged people.”\n\n“My dear sir, do not make a stranger of me.”\n\n“I leave an excellent substitute in my daughter. Emma will be happy to entertain you. And therefore I think I will beg your excuse and take my three turns—my winter walk.”\n\n“You cannot do better, sir.”\n\n“I would ask for the pleasure of your company, Mr. Knightley, but I am a very slow walker, and my pace would be tedious to you; and, besides, you have another long walk before you, to Donwell Abbey.”\n\n“Thank you, sir, thank you; I am going this moment myself; and I think the sooner you go the better. I will fetch your greatcoat and open the garden door for you.”\n\nMr. Woodhouse at last was off; but Mr. Knightley, instead of being immediately off likewise, sat down again, seemingly inclined for more chat. He began speaking of Harriet, and speaking of her with more voluntary praise than Emma had ever heard before.\n\n“I cannot rate her beauty as you do,” said he; “but she is a pretty little creature, and I am inclined to think very well of her disposition. Her character depends upon those she is with; but in good hands she will turn out a valuable woman.”\n\n“I am glad you think so; and the good hands, I hope, may not be wanting.”\n\n“Come,” said he, “you are anxious for a compliment, so I will tell you that you have improved her. You have cured her of her school-girl’s giggle; she really does you credit.”\n\n“Thank you. I should be mortified indeed if I did not believe I had been of some use; but it is not every body who will bestow praise where they may. You do not often overpower me with it.”\n\n“You are expecting her again, you say, this morning?”\n\n“Almost every moment. She has been gone longer already than she intended.”\n\n“Something has happened to delay her; some visitors perhaps.”\n\n“Highbury gossips!—Tiresome wretches!”\n\n“Harriet may not consider every body tiresome that you would.”\n\nEmma knew this was too true for contradiction, and therefore said nothing. He presently added, with a smile,\n\n“I do not pretend to fix on times or places, but I must tell you that I have good reason to believe your little friend will soon hear of something to her advantage.”\n\n“Indeed! how so? of what sort?”\n\n“A very serious sort, I assure you;” still smiling.\n\n“Very serious! I can think of but one thing—Who is in love with her? Who makes you their confidant?”\n\nEmma was more than half in hopes of Mr. Elton’s having dropt a hint. Mr. Knightley was a sort of general friend and adviser, and she knew Mr. Elton looked up to him.\n\n“I have reason to think,” he replied, “that Harriet Smith will soon have an offer of marriage, and from a most unexceptionable quarter:—Robert Martin is the man. Her visit to Abbey-Mill, this summer, seems to have done his business. He is desperately in love and means to marry her.”\n\n“He is very obliging,” said Emma; “but is he sure that Harriet means to marry him?”\n\n“Well, well, means to make her an offer then. Will that do? He came to the Abbey two evenings ago, on purpose to consult me about it. He knows I have a thorough regard for him and all his family, and, I believe, considers me as one of his best friends. He came to ask me whether I thought it would be imprudent in him to settle so early; whether I thought her too young: in short, whether I approved his choice altogether; having some apprehension perhaps of her being considered (especially since your making so much of her) as in a line of society above him. I was very much pleased with all that he said. I never hear better sense from any one than Robert Martin. He always speaks to the purpose; open, straightforward, and very well judging. He told me every thing; his circumstances and plans, and what they all proposed doing in the event of his marriage. He is an excellent young man, both as son and brother. I had no hesitation in advising him to marry. He proved to me that he could afford it; and that being the case, I was convinced he could not do better. I praised the fair lady too, and altogether sent him away very happy. If he had never esteemed my opinion before, he would have thought highly of me then; and, I dare say, left the house thinking me the best friend and counsellor man ever had. This happened the night before last. Now, as we may fairly suppose, he would not allow much time to pass before he spoke to the lady, and as he does not appear to have spoken yesterday, it is not unlikely that he should be at Mrs. Goddard’s to-day; and she may be detained by a visitor, without thinking him at all a tiresome wretch.”\n\n“Pray, Mr. Knightley,” said Emma, who had been smiling to herself through a great part of this speech, “how do you know that Mr. Martin did not speak yesterday?”\n\n“Certainly,” replied he, surprized, “I do not absolutely know it; but it may be inferred. Was not she the whole day with you?”\n\n“Come,” said she, “I will tell you something, in return for what you have told me. He did speak yesterday—that is, he wrote, and was refused.”\n\nThis was obliged to be repeated before it could be believed; and Mr. Knightley actually looked red with surprize and displeasure, as he stood up, in tall indignation, and said,\n\n“Then she is a greater simpleton than I ever believed her. What is the foolish girl about?”\n\n“Oh! to be sure,” cried Emma, “it is always incomprehensible to a man that a woman should ever refuse an offer of marriage. A man always imagines a woman to be ready for any body who asks her.”\n\n“Nonsense! a man does not imagine any such thing. But what is the meaning of this? Harriet Smith refuse Robert Martin? madness, if it is so; but I hope you are mistaken.”\n\n“I saw her answer!—nothing could be clearer.”\n\n“You saw her answer!—you wrote her answer too. Emma, this is your doing. You persuaded her to refuse him.”\n\n“And if I did, (which, however, I am far from allowing) I should not feel that I had done wrong. Mr. Martin is a very respectable young man, but I cannot admit him to be Harriet’s equal; and am rather surprized indeed that he should have ventured to address her. By your account, he does seem to have had some scruples. It is a pity that they were ever got over.”\n\n“Not Harriet’s equal!” exclaimed Mr. Knightley loudly and warmly; and with calmer asperity, added, a few moments afterwards, “No, he is not her equal indeed, for he is as much her superior in sense as in situation. Emma, your infatuation about that girl blinds you. What are Harriet Smith’s claims, either of birth, nature or education, to any connexion higher than Robert Martin? She is the natural daughter of nobody knows whom, with probably no settled provision at all, and certainly no respectable relations. She is known only as parlour-boarder at a common school. She is not a sensible girl, nor a girl of any information. She has been taught nothing useful, and is too young and too simple to have acquired any thing herself. At her age she can have no experience, and with her little wit, is not very likely ever to have any that can avail her. She is pretty, and she is good tempered, and that is all. My only scruple in advising the match was on his account, as being beneath his deserts, and a bad connexion for him. I felt that, as to fortune, in all probability he might do much better; and that as to a rational companion or useful helpmate, he could not do worse. But I could not reason so to a man in love, and was willing to trust to there being no harm in her, to her having that sort of disposition, which, in good hands, like his, might be easily led aright and turn out very well. The advantage of the match I felt to be all on her side; and had not the smallest doubt (nor have I now) that there would be a general cry-out upon her extreme good luck. Even your satisfaction I made sure of. It crossed my mind immediately that you would not regret your friend’s leaving Highbury, for the sake of her being settled so well. I remember saying to myself, ‘Even Emma, with all her partiality for Harriet, will think this a good match.’”\n\n“I cannot help wondering at your knowing so little of Emma as to say any such thing. What! think a farmer, (and with all his sense and all his merit Mr. Martin is nothing more,) a good match for my intimate friend! Not regret her leaving Highbury for the sake of marrying a man whom I could never admit as an acquaintance of my own! I wonder you should think it possible for me to have such feelings. I assure you mine are very different. I must think your statement by no means fair. You are not just to Harriet’s claims. They would be estimated very differently by others as well as myself; Mr. Martin may be the richest of the two, but he is undoubtedly her inferior as to rank in society.—The sphere in which she moves is much above his.—It would be a degradation.”\n\n“A degradation to illegitimacy and ignorance, to be married to a respectable, intelligent gentleman-farmer!”\n\n“As to the circumstances of her birth, though in a legal sense she may be called Nobody, it will not hold in common sense. She is not to pay for the offence of others, by being held below the level of those with whom she is brought up.—There can scarcely be a doubt that her father is a gentleman—and a gentleman of fortune.—Her allowance is very liberal; nothing has ever been grudged for her improvement or comfort.—That she is a gentleman’s daughter, is indubitable to me; that she associates with gentlemen’s daughters, no one, I apprehend, will deny.—She is superior to Mr. Robert Martin.”\n\n“Whoever might be her parents,” said Mr. Knightley, “whoever may have had the charge of her, it does not appear to have been any part of their plan to introduce her into what you would call good society. After receiving a very indifferent education she is left in Mrs. Goddard’s hands to shift as she can;—to move, in short, in Mrs. Goddard’s line, to have Mrs. Goddard’s acquaintance. Her friends evidently thought this good enough for her; and it was good enough. She desired nothing better herself. Till you chose to turn her into a friend, her mind had no distaste for her own set, nor any ambition beyond it. She was as happy as possible with the Martins in the summer. She had no sense of superiority then. If she has it now, you have given it. You have been no friend to Harriet Smith, Emma. Robert Martin would never have proceeded so far, if he had not felt persuaded of her not being disinclined to him. I know him well. He has too much real feeling to address any woman on the haphazard of selfish passion. And as to conceit, he is the farthest from it of any man I know. Depend upon it he had encouragement.”\n\nIt was most convenient to Emma not to make a direct reply to this assertion; she chose rather to take up her own line of the subject again.\n\n“You are a very warm friend to Mr. Martin; but, as I said before, are unjust to Harriet. Harriet’s claims to marry well are not so contemptible as you represent them. She is not a clever girl, but she has better sense than you are aware of, and does not deserve to have her understanding spoken of so slightingly. Waiving that point, however, and supposing her to be, as you describe her, only pretty and good-natured, let me tell you, that in the degree she possesses them, they are not trivial recommendations to the world in general, for she is, in fact, a beautiful girl, and must be thought so by ninety-nine people out of an hundred; and till it appears that men are much more philosophic on the subject of beauty than they are generally supposed; till they do fall in love with well-informed minds instead of handsome faces, a girl, with such loveliness as Harriet, has a certainty of being admired and sought after, of having the power of chusing from among many, consequently a claim to be nice. Her good-nature, too, is not so very slight a claim, comprehending, as it does, real, thorough sweetness of temper and manner, a very humble opinion of herself, and a great readiness to be pleased with other people. I am very much mistaken if your sex in general would not think such beauty, and such temper, the highest claims a woman could possess.”\n\n“Upon my word, Emma, to hear you abusing the reason you have, is almost enough to make me think so too. Better be without sense, than misapply it as you do.”\n\n“To be sure!” cried she playfully. “I know that is the feeling of you all. I know that such a girl as Harriet is exactly what every man delights in—what at once bewitches his senses and satisfies his judgment. Oh! Harriet may pick and chuse. Were you, yourself, ever to marry, she is the very woman for you. And is she, at seventeen, just entering into life, just beginning to be known, to be wondered at because she does not accept the first offer she receives? No—pray let her have time to look about her.”\n\n“I have always thought it a very foolish intimacy,” said Mr. Knightley presently, “though I have kept my thoughts to myself; but I now perceive that it will be a very unfortunate one for Harriet. You will puff her up with such ideas of her own beauty, and of what she has a claim to, that, in a little while, nobody within her reach will be good enough for her. Vanity working on a weak head, produces every sort of mischief. Nothing so easy as for a young lady to raise her expectations too high. Miss Harriet Smith may not find offers of marriage flow in so fast, though she is a very pretty girl. Men of sense, whatever you may chuse to say, do not want silly wives. Men of family would not be very fond of connecting themselves with a girl of such obscurity—and most prudent men would be afraid of the inconvenience and disgrace they might be involved in, when the mystery of her parentage came to be revealed. Let her marry Robert Martin, and she is safe, respectable, and happy for ever; but if you encourage her to expect to marry greatly, and teach her to be satisfied with nothing less than a man of consequence and large fortune, she may be a parlour-boarder at Mrs. Goddard’s all the rest of her life—or, at least, (for Harriet Smith is a girl who will marry somebody or other,) till she grow desperate, and is glad to catch at the old writing-master’s son.”\n\n“We think so very differently on this point, Mr. Knightley, that there can be no use in canvassing it. We shall only be making each other more angry. But as to my letting her marry Robert Martin, it is impossible; she has refused him, and so decidedly, I think, as must prevent any second application. She must abide by the evil of having refused him, whatever it may be; and as to the refusal itself, I will not pretend to say that I might not influence her a little; but I assure you there was very little for me or for any body to do. His appearance is so much against him, and his manner so bad, that if she ever were disposed to favour him, she is not now. I can imagine, that before she had seen any body superior, she might tolerate him. He was the brother of her friends, and he took pains to please her; and altogether, having seen nobody better (that must have been his great assistant) she might not, while she was at Abbey-Mill, find him disagreeable. But the case is altered now. She knows now what gentlemen are; and nothing but a gentleman in education and manner has any chance with Harriet.”\n\n“Nonsense, errant nonsense, as ever was talked!” cried Mr. Knightley.—“Robert Martin’s manners have sense, sincerity, and good-humour to recommend them; and his mind has more true gentility than Harriet Smith could understand.”\n\nEmma made no answer, and tried to look cheerfully unconcerned, but was really feeling uncomfortable and wanting him very much to be gone. She did not repent what she had done; she still thought herself a better judge of such a point of female right and refinement than he could be; but yet she had a sort of habitual respect for his judgment in general, which made her dislike having it so loudly against her; and to have him sitting just opposite to her in angry state, was very disagreeable. Some minutes passed in this unpleasant silence, with only one attempt on Emma’s side to talk of the weather, but he made no answer. He was thinking. The result of his thoughts appeared at last in these words.\n\n“Robert Martin has no great loss—if he can but think so; and I hope it will not be long before he does. Your views for Harriet are best known to yourself; but as you make no secret of your love of match-making, it is fair to suppose that views, and plans, and projects you have;—and as a friend I shall just hint to you that if Elton is the man, I think it will be all labour in vain.”\n\nEmma laughed and disclaimed. He continued,\n\n“Depend upon it, Elton will not do. Elton is a very good sort of man, and a very respectable vicar of Highbury, but not at all likely to make an imprudent match. He knows the value of a good income as well as any body. Elton may talk sentimentally, but he will act rationally. He is as well acquainted with his own claims, as you can be with Harriet’s. He knows that he is a very handsome young man, and a great favourite wherever he goes; and from his general way of talking in unreserved moments, when there are only men present, I am convinced that he does not mean to throw himself away. I have heard him speak with great animation of a large family of young ladies that his sisters are intimate with, who have all twenty thousand pounds apiece.”\n\n“I am very much obliged to you,” said Emma, laughing again. “If I had set my heart on Mr. Elton’s marrying Harriet, it would have been very kind to open my eyes; but at present I only want to keep Harriet to myself. I have done with match-making indeed. I could never hope to equal my own doings at Randalls. I shall leave off while I am well.”\n\n“Good morning to you,”—said he, rising and walking off abruptly. He was very much vexed. He felt the disappointment of the young man, and was mortified to have been the means of promoting it, by the sanction he had given; and the part which he was persuaded Emma had taken in the affair, was provoking him exceedingly.\n\nEmma remained in a state of vexation too; but there was more indistinctness in the causes of her’s, than in his. She did not always feel so absolutely satisfied with herself, so entirely convinced that her opinions were right and her adversary’s wrong, as Mr. Knightley. He walked off in more complete self-approbation than he left for her. She was not so materially cast down, however, but that a little time and the return of Harriet were very adequate restoratives. Harriet’s staying away so long was beginning to make her uneasy. The possibility of the young man’s coming to Mrs. Goddard’s that morning, and meeting with Harriet and pleading his own cause, gave alarming ideas. The dread of such a failure after all became the prominent uneasiness; and when Harriet appeared, and in very good spirits, and without having any such reason to give for her long absence, she felt a satisfaction which settled her with her own mind, and convinced her, that let Mr. Knightley think or say what he would, she had done nothing which woman’s friendship and woman’s feelings would not justify.\n\nHe had frightened her a little about Mr. Elton; but when she considered that Mr. Knightley could not have observed him as she had done, neither with the interest, nor (she must be allowed to tell herself, in spite of Mr. Knightley’s pretensions) with the skill of such an observer on such a question as herself, that he had spoken it hastily and in anger, she was able to believe, that he had rather said what he wished resentfully to be true, than what he knew any thing about. He certainly might have heard Mr. Elton speak with more unreserve than she had ever done, and Mr. Elton might not be of an imprudent, inconsiderate disposition as to money matters; he might naturally be rather attentive than otherwise to them; but then, Mr. Knightley did not make due allowance for the influence of a strong passion at war with all interested motives. Mr. Knightley saw no such passion, and of course thought nothing of its effects; but she saw too much of it to feel a doubt of its overcoming any hesitations that a reasonable prudence might originally suggest; and more than a reasonable, becoming degree of prudence, she was very sure did not belong to Mr. Elton.\n\nHarriet’s cheerful look and manner established hers: she came back, not to think of Mr. Martin, but to talk of Mr. Elton. Miss Nash had been telling her something, which she repeated immediately with great delight. Mr. Perry had been to Mrs. Goddard’s to attend a sick child, and Miss Nash had seen him, and he had told Miss Nash, that as he was coming back yesterday from Clayton Park, he had met Mr. Elton, and found to his great surprize, that Mr. Elton was actually on his road to London, and not meaning to return till the morrow, though it was the whist-club night, which he had been never known to miss before; and Mr. Perry had remonstrated with him about it, and told him how shabby it was in him, their best player, to absent himself, and tried very much to persuade him to put off his journey only one day; but it would not do; Mr. Elton had been determined to go on, and had said in a very particular way indeed, that he was going on business which he would not put off for any inducement in the world; and something about a very enviable commission, and being the bearer of something exceedingly precious. Mr. Perry could not quite understand him, but he was very sure there must be a lady in the case, and he told him so; and Mr. Elton only looked very conscious and smiling, and rode off in great spirits. Miss Nash had told her all this, and had talked a great deal more about Mr. Elton; and said, looking so very significantly at her, “that she did not pretend to understand what his business might be, but she only knew that any woman whom Mr. Elton could prefer, she should think the luckiest woman in the world; for, beyond a doubt, Mr. Elton had not his equal for beauty or agreeableness.”\n\n# Volume I — Chapter IX\n\nMr. Knightley might quarrel with her, but Emma could not quarrel with herself. He was so much displeased, that it was longer than usual before he came to Hartfield again; and when they did meet, his grave looks shewed that she was not forgiven. She was sorry, but could not repent. On the contrary, her plans and proceedings were more and more justified and endeared to her by the general appearances of the next few days.\n\nThe Picture, elegantly framed, came safely to hand soon after Mr. Elton’s return, and being hung over the mantelpiece of the common sitting-room, he got up to look at it, and sighed out his half sentences of admiration just as he ought; and as for Harriet’s feelings, they were visibly forming themselves into as strong and steady an attachment as her youth and sort of mind admitted. Emma was soon perfectly satisfied of Mr. Martin’s being no otherwise remembered, than as he furnished a contrast with Mr. Elton, of the utmost advantage to the latter.\n\nHer views of improving her little friend’s mind, by a great deal of useful reading and conversation, had never yet led to more than a few first chapters, and the intention of going on to-morrow. It was much easier to chat than to study; much pleasanter to let her imagination range and work at Harriet’s fortune, than to be labouring to enlarge her comprehension or exercise it on sober facts; and the only literary pursuit which engaged Harriet at present, the only mental provision she was making for the evening of life, was the collecting and transcribing all the riddles of every sort that she could meet with, into a thin quarto of hot-pressed paper, made up by her friend, and ornamented with ciphers and trophies.\n\nIn this age of literature, such collections on a very grand scale are not uncommon. Miss Nash, head-teacher at Mrs. Goddard’s, had written out at least three hundred; and Harriet, who had taken the first hint of it from her, hoped, with Miss Woodhouse’s help, to get a great many more. Emma assisted with her invention, memory and taste; and as Harriet wrote a very pretty hand, it was likely to be an arrangement of the first order, in form as well as quantity.\n\nMr. Woodhouse was almost as much interested in the business as the girls, and tried very often to recollect something worth their putting in. “So many clever riddles as there used to be when he was young—he wondered he could not remember them! but he hoped he should in time.” And it always ended in “Kitty, a fair but frozen maid.”\n\nHis good friend Perry, too, whom he had spoken to on the subject, did not at present recollect any thing of the riddle kind; but he had desired Perry to be upon the watch, and as he went about so much, something, he thought, might come from that quarter.\n\nIt was by no means his daughter’s wish that the intellects of Highbury in general should be put under requisition. Mr. Elton was the only one whose assistance she asked. He was invited to contribute any really good enigmas, charades, or conundrums that he might recollect; and she had the pleasure of seeing him most intently at work with his recollections; and at the same time, as she could perceive, most earnestly careful that nothing ungallant, nothing that did not breathe a compliment to the sex should pass his lips. They owed to him their two or three politest puzzles; and the joy and exultation with which at last he recalled, and rather sentimentally recited, that well-known charade,\n\nMy first doth affliction denote, Which my second is destin’d to feel And my whole is the best antidote That affliction to soften and heal.—\n\nmade her quite sorry to acknowledge that they had transcribed it some pages ago already.\n\n“Why will not you write one yourself for us, Mr. Elton?” said she; “that is the only security for its freshness; and nothing could be easier to you.”\n\n“Oh no! he had never written, hardly ever, any thing of the kind in his life. The stupidest fellow! He was afraid not even Miss Woodhouse”—he stopt a moment—“or Miss Smith could inspire him.”\n\nThe very next day however produced some proof of inspiration. He called for a few moments, just to leave a piece of paper on the table containing, as he said, a charade, which a friend of his had addressed to a young lady, the object of his admiration, but which, from his manner, Emma was immediately convinced must be his own.\n\n“I do not offer it for Miss Smith’s collection,” said he. “Being my friend’s, I have no right to expose it in any degree to the public eye, but perhaps you may not dislike looking at it.”\n\nThe speech was more to Emma than to Harriet, which Emma could understand. There was deep consciousness about him, and he found it easier to meet her eye than her friend’s. He was gone the next moment:—after another moment’s pause,\n\n“Take it,” said Emma, smiling, and pushing the paper towards Harriet—“it is for you. Take your own.”\n\nBut Harriet was in a tremor, and could not touch it; and Emma, never loth to be first, was obliged to examine it herself.\n\nTo Miss——\n\nCHARADE.\n\nMy first displays the wealth and pomp of kings, Lords of the earth! their luxury and ease. Another view of man, my second brings, Behold him there, the monarch of the seas!\n\nBut ah! united, what reverse we have! Man’s boasted power and freedom, all are flown; Lord of the earth and sea, he bends a slave, And woman, lovely woman, reigns alone.\n\nThy ready wit the word will soon supply, May its approval beam in that soft eye!\n\nShe cast her eye over it, pondered, caught the meaning, read it through again to be quite certain, and quite mistress of the lines, and then passing it to Harriet, sat happily smiling, and saying to herself, while Harriet was puzzling over the paper in all the confusion of hope and dulness, “Very well, Mr. Elton, very well indeed. I have read worse charades. Courtship—a very good hint. I give you credit for it. This is feeling your way. This is saying very plainly—‘Pray, Miss Smith, give me leave to pay my addresses to you. Approve my charade and my intentions in the same glance.’\n\nMay its approval beam in that soft eye!\n\nHarriet exactly. Soft is the very word for her eye—of all epithets, the justest that could be given.\n\nThy ready wit the word will soon supply.\n\nHumph—Harriet’s ready wit! All the better. A man must be very much in love, indeed, to describe her so. Ah! Mr. Knightley, I wish you had the benefit of this; I think this would convince you. For once in your life you would be obliged to own yourself mistaken. An excellent charade indeed! and very much to the purpose. Things must come to a crisis soon now.”\n\nShe was obliged to break off from these very pleasant observations, which were otherwise of a sort to run into great length, by the eagerness of Harriet’s wondering questions.\n\n“What can it be, Miss Woodhouse?—what can it be? I have not an idea—I cannot guess it in the least. What can it possibly be? Do try to find it out, Miss Woodhouse. Do help me. I never saw any thing so hard. Is it kingdom? I wonder who the friend was—and who could be the young lady. Do you think it is a good one? Can it be woman?\n\nAnd woman, lovely woman, reigns alone.\n\nCan it be Neptune?\n\nBehold him there, the monarch of the seas!\n\nOr a trident? or a mermaid? or a shark? Oh, no! shark is only one syllable. It must be very clever, or he would not have brought it. Oh! Miss Woodhouse, do you think we shall ever find it out?”\n\n“Mermaids and sharks! Nonsense! My dear Harriet, what are you thinking of? Where would be the use of his bringing us a charade made by a friend upon a mermaid or a shark? Give me the paper and listen.\n\nFor Miss ———, read Miss Smith.\n\nMy first displays the wealth and pomp of kings, Lords of the earth! their luxury and ease.\n\nThat is court.\n\nAnother view of man, my second brings; Behold him there, the monarch of the seas!\n\nThat is ship;—plain as it can be.—Now for the cream.\n\nBut ah! united, (courtship, you know,) what reverse we have! Man’s boasted power and freedom, all are flown. Lord of the earth and sea, he bends a slave, And woman, lovely woman, reigns alone.\n\nA very proper compliment!—and then follows the application, which I think, my dear Harriet, you cannot find much difficulty in comprehending. Read it in comfort to yourself. There can be no doubt of its being written for you and to you.”\n\nHarriet could not long resist so delightful a persuasion. She read the concluding lines, and was all flutter and happiness. She could not speak. But she was not wanted to speak. It was enough for her to feel. Emma spoke for her.\n\n“There is so pointed, and so particular a meaning in this compliment,” said she, “that I cannot have a doubt as to Mr. Elton’s intentions. You are his object—and you will soon receive the completest proof of it. I thought it must be so. I thought I could not be so deceived; but now, it is clear; the state of his mind is as clear and decided, as my wishes on the subject have been ever since I knew you. Yes, Harriet, just so long have I been wanting the very circumstance to happen that has happened. I could never tell whether an attachment between you and Mr. Elton were most desirable or most natural. Its probability and its eligibility have really so equalled each other! I am very happy. I congratulate you, my dear Harriet, with all my heart. This is an attachment which a woman may well feel pride in creating. This is a connexion which offers nothing but good. It will give you every thing that you want—consideration, independence, a proper home—it will fix you in the centre of all your real friends, close to Hartfield and to me, and confirm our intimacy for ever. This, Harriet, is an alliance which can never raise a blush in either of us.”\n\n“Dear Miss Woodhouse!”—and “Dear Miss Woodhouse,” was all that Harriet, with many tender embraces could articulate at first; but when they did arrive at something more like conversation, it was sufficiently clear to her friend that she saw, felt, anticipated, and remembered just as she ought. Mr. Elton’s superiority had very ample acknowledgment.\n\n“Whatever you say is always right,” cried Harriet, “and therefore I suppose, and believe, and hope it must be so; but otherwise I could not have imagined it. It is so much beyond any thing I deserve. Mr. Elton, who might marry any body! There cannot be two opinions about him. He is so very superior. Only think of those sweet verses—‘To Miss ———.’ Dear me, how clever!—Could it really be meant for me?”\n\n“I cannot make a question, or listen to a question about that. It is a certainty. Receive it on my judgment. It is a sort of prologue to the play, a motto to the chapter; and will be soon followed by matter-of-fact prose.”\n\n“It is a sort of thing which nobody could have expected. I am sure, a month ago, I had no more idea myself!—The strangest things do take place!”\n\n“When Miss Smiths and Mr. Eltons get acquainted—they do indeed—and really it is strange; it is out of the common course that what is so evidently, so palpably desirable—what courts the pre-arrangement of other people, should so immediately shape itself into the proper form. You and Mr. Elton are by situation called together; you belong to one another by every circumstance of your respective homes. Your marrying will be equal to the match at Randalls. There does seem to be a something in the air of Hartfield which gives love exactly the right direction, and sends it into the very channel where it ought to flow.\n\nThe course of true love never did run smooth—\n\nA Hartfield edition of Shakespeare would have a long note on that passage.”\n\n“That Mr. Elton should really be in love with me,—me, of all people, who did not know him, to speak to him, at Michaelmas! And he, the very handsomest man that ever was, and a man that every body looks up to, quite like Mr. Knightley! His company so sought after, that every body says he need not eat a single meal by himself if he does not chuse it; that he has more invitations than there are days in the week. And so excellent in the Church! Miss Nash has put down all the texts he has ever preached from since he came to Highbury. Dear me! When I look back to the first time I saw him! How little did I think!—The two Abbots and I ran into the front room and peeped through the blind when we heard he was going by, and Miss Nash came and scolded us away, and staid to look through herself; however, she called me back presently, and let me look too, which was very good-natured. And how beautiful we thought he looked! He was arm-in-arm with Mr. Cole.”\n\n“This is an alliance which, whoever—whatever your friends may be, must be agreeable to them, provided at least they have common sense; and we are not to be addressing our conduct to fools. If they are anxious to see you happily married, here is a man whose amiable character gives every assurance of it;—if they wish to have you settled in the same country and circle which they have chosen to place you in, here it will be accomplished; and if their only object is that you should, in the common phrase, be well married, here is the comfortable fortune, the respectable establishment, the rise in the world which must satisfy them.”\n\n“Yes, very true. How nicely you talk; I love to hear you. You understand every thing. You and Mr. Elton are one as clever as the other. This charade!—If I had studied a twelvemonth, I could never have made any thing like it.”\n\n“I thought he meant to try his skill, by his manner of declining it yesterday.”\n\n“I do think it is, without exception, the best charade I ever read.”\n\n“I never read one more to the purpose, certainly.”\n\n“It is as long again as almost all we have had before.”\n\n“I do not consider its length as particularly in its favour. Such things in general cannot be too short.”\n\nHarriet was too intent on the lines to hear. The most satisfactory comparisons were rising in her mind.\n\n“It is one thing,” said she, presently—her cheeks in a glow—“to have very good sense in a common way, like every body else, and if there is any thing to say, to sit down and write a letter, and say just what you must, in a short way; and another, to write verses and charades like this.”\n\nEmma could not have desired a more spirited rejection of Mr. Martin’s prose.\n\n“Such sweet lines!” continued Harriet—“these two last!—But how shall I ever be able to return the paper, or say I have found it out?—Oh! Miss Woodhouse, what can we do about that?”\n\n“Leave it to me. You do nothing. He will be here this evening, I dare say, and then I will give it him back, and some nonsense or other will pass between us, and you shall not be committed.—Your soft eyes shall chuse their own time for beaming. Trust to me.”\n\n“Oh! Miss Woodhouse, what a pity that I must not write this beautiful charade into my book! I am sure I have not got one half so good.”\n\n“Leave out the two last lines, and there is no reason why you should not write it into your book.”\n\n“Oh! but those two lines are”—\n\n—“The best of all. Granted;—for private enjoyment; and for private enjoyment keep them. They are not at all the less written you know, because you divide them. The couplet does not cease to be, nor does its meaning change. But take it away, and all appropriation ceases, and a very pretty gallant charade remains, fit for any collection. Depend upon it, he would not like to have his charade slighted, much better than his passion. A poet in love must be encouraged in both capacities, or neither. Give me the book, I will write it down, and then there can be no possible reflection on you.”\n\nHarriet submitted, though her mind could hardly separate the parts, so as to feel quite sure that her friend were not writing down a declaration of love. It seemed too precious an offering for any degree of publicity.\n\n“I shall never let that book go out of my own hands,” said she.\n\n“Very well,” replied Emma; “a most natural feeling; and the longer it lasts, the better I shall be pleased. But here is my father coming: you will not object to my reading the charade to him. It will be giving him so much pleasure! He loves any thing of the sort, and especially any thing that pays woman a compliment. He has the tenderest spirit of gallantry towards us all!—You must let me read it to him.”\n\nHarriet looked grave.\n\n“My dear Harriet, you must not refine too much upon this charade.—You will betray your feelings improperly, if you are too conscious and too quick, and appear to affix more meaning, or even quite all the meaning which may be affixed to it. Do not be overpowered by such a little tribute of admiration. If he had been anxious for secrecy, he would not have left the paper while I was by; but he rather pushed it towards me than towards you. Do not let us be too solemn on the business. He has encouragement enough to proceed, without our sighing out our souls over this charade.”\n\n“Oh! no—I hope I shall not be ridiculous about it. Do as you please.”\n\nMr. Woodhouse came in, and very soon led to the subject again, by the recurrence of his very frequent inquiry of “Well, my dears, how does your book go on?—Have you got any thing fresh?”\n\n“Yes, papa; we have something to read you, something quite fresh. A piece of paper was found on the table this morning—(dropt, we suppose, by a fairy)—containing a very pretty charade, and we have just copied it in.”\n\nShe read it to him, just as he liked to have any thing read, slowly and distinctly, and two or three times over, with explanations of every part as she proceeded—and he was very much pleased, and, as she had foreseen, especially struck with the complimentary conclusion.\n\n“Aye, that’s very just, indeed, that’s very properly said. Very true. ‘Woman, lovely woman.’ It is such a pretty charade, my dear, that I can easily guess what fairy brought it.—Nobody could have written so prettily, but you, Emma.”\n\nEmma only nodded, and smiled.—After a little thinking, and a very tender sigh, he added,\n\n“Ah! it is no difficulty to see who you take after! Your dear mother was so clever at all those things! If I had but her memory! But I can remember nothing;—not even that particular riddle which you have heard me mention; I can only recollect the first stanza; and there are several.\n\nKitty, a fair but frozen maid, Kindled a flame I yet deplore, The hood-wink’d boy I called to aid, Though of his near approach afraid, So fatal to my suit before.\n\nAnd that is all that I can recollect of it—but it is very clever all the way through. But I think, my dear, you said you had got it.”\n\n“Yes, papa, it is written out in our second page. We copied it from the Elegant Extracts. It was Garrick’s, you know.”\n\n“Aye, very true.—I wish I could recollect more of it.\n\nKitty, a fair but frozen maid.\n\nThe name makes me think of poor Isabella; for she was very near being christened Catherine after her grandmama. I hope we shall have her here next week. Have you thought, my dear, where you shall put her—and what room there will be for the children?”\n\n“Oh! yes—she will have her own room, of course; the room she always has;—and there is the nursery for the children,—just as usual, you know. Why should there be any change?”\n\n“I do not know, my dear—but it is so long since she was here!—not since last Easter, and then only for a few days.—Mr. John Knightley’s being a lawyer is very inconvenient.—Poor Isabella!—she is sadly taken away from us all!—and how sorry she will be when she comes, not to see Miss Taylor here!”\n\n“She will not be surprized, papa, at least.”\n\n“I do not know, my dear. I am sure I was very much surprized when I first heard she was going to be married.”\n\n“We must ask Mr. and Mrs. Weston to dine with us, while Isabella is here.”\n\n“Yes, my dear, if there is time.—But—(in a very depressed tone)—she is coming for only one week. There will not be time for any thing.”\n\n“It is unfortunate that they cannot stay longer—but it seems a case of necessity. Mr. John Knightley must be in town again on the 28th, and we ought to be thankful, papa, that we are to have the whole of the time they can give to the country, that two or three days are not to be taken out for the Abbey. Mr. Knightley promises to give up his claim this Christmas—though you know it is longer since they were with him, than with us.”\n\n“It would be very hard, indeed, my dear, if poor Isabella were to be anywhere but at Hartfield.”\n\nMr. Woodhouse could never allow for Mr. Knightley’s claims on his brother, or any body’s claims on Isabella, except his own. He sat musing a little while, and then said,\n\n“But I do not see why poor Isabella should be obliged to go back so soon, though he does. I think, Emma, I shall try and persuade her to stay longer with us. She and the children might stay very well.”\n\n“Ah! papa—that is what you never have been able to accomplish, and I do not think you ever will. Isabella cannot bear to stay behind her husband.”\n\nThis was too true for contradiction. Unwelcome as it was, Mr. Woodhouse could only give a submissive sigh; and as Emma saw his spirits affected by the idea of his daughter’s attachment to her husband, she immediately led to such a branch of the subject as must raise them.\n\n“Harriet must give us as much of her company as she can while my brother and sister are here. I am sure she will be pleased with the children. We are very proud of the children, are not we, papa? I wonder which she will think the handsomest, Henry or John?”\n\n“Aye, I wonder which she will. Poor little dears, how glad they will be to come. They are very fond of being at Hartfield, Harriet.”\n\n“I dare say they are, sir. I am sure I do not know who is not.”\n\n“Henry is a fine boy, but John is very like his mama. Henry is the eldest, he was named after me, not after his father. John, the second, is named after his father. Some people are surprized, I believe, that the eldest was not, but Isabella would have him called Henry, which I thought very pretty of her. And he is a very clever boy, indeed. They are all remarkably clever; and they have so many pretty ways. They will come and stand by my chair, and say, ‘Grandpapa, can you give me a bit of string?’ and once Henry asked me for a knife, but I told him knives were only made for grandpapas. I think their father is too rough with them very often.”\n\n“He appears rough to you,” said Emma, “because you are so very gentle yourself; but if you could compare him with other papas, you would not think him rough. He wishes his boys to be active and hardy; and if they misbehave, can give them a sharp word now and then; but he is an affectionate father—certainly Mr. John Knightley is an affectionate father. The children are all fond of him.”\n\n“And then their uncle comes in, and tosses them up to the ceiling in a very frightful way!”\n\n“But they like it, papa; there is nothing they like so much. It is such enjoyment to them, that if their uncle did not lay down the rule of their taking turns, whichever began would never give way to the other.”\n\n“Well, I cannot understand it.”\n\n“That is the case with us all, papa. One half of the world cannot understand the pleasures of the other.”\n\nLater in the morning, and just as the girls were going to separate in preparation for the regular four o’clock dinner, the hero of this inimitable charade walked in again. Harriet turned away; but Emma could receive him with the usual smile, and her quick eye soon discerned in his the consciousness of having made a push—of having thrown a die; and she imagined he was come to see how it might turn up. His ostensible reason, however, was to ask whether Mr. Woodhouse’s party could be made up in the evening without him, or whether he should be in the smallest degree necessary at Hartfield. If he were, every thing else must give way; but otherwise his friend Cole had been saying so much about his dining with him—had made such a point of it, that he had promised him conditionally to come.\n\nEmma thanked him, but could not allow of his disappointing his friend on their account; her father was sure of his rubber. He re-urged—she re-declined; and he seemed then about to make his bow, when taking the paper from the table, she returned it—\n\n“Oh! here is the charade you were so obliging as to leave with us; thank you for the sight of it. We admired it so much, that I have ventured to write it into Miss Smith’s collection. Your friend will not take it amiss I hope. Of course I have not transcribed beyond the first eight lines.”\n\nMr. Elton certainly did not very well know what to say. He looked rather doubtingly—rather confused; said something about “honour,”—glanced at Emma and at Harriet, and then seeing the book open on the table, took it up, and examined it very attentively. With the view of passing off an awkward moment, Emma smilingly said,\n\n“You must make my apologies to your friend; but so good a charade must not be confined to one or two. He may be sure of every woman’s approbation while he writes with such gallantry.”\n\n“I have no hesitation in saying,” replied Mr. Elton, though hesitating a good deal while he spoke; “I have no hesitation in saying—at least if my friend feels at all as I do—I have not the smallest doubt that, could he see his little effusion honoured as I see it, (looking at the book again, and replacing it on the table), he would consider it as the proudest moment of his life.”\n\nAfter this speech he was gone as soon as possible. Emma could not think it too soon; for with all his good and agreeable qualities, there was a sort of parade in his speeches which was very apt to incline her to laugh. She ran away to indulge the inclination, leaving the tender and the sublime of pleasure to Harriet’s share.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter X\n\nThough now the middle of December, there had yet been no weather to prevent the young ladies from tolerably regular exercise; and on the morrow, Emma had a charitable visit to pay to a poor sick family, who lived a little way out of Highbury.\n\nTheir road to this detached cottage was down Vicarage Lane, a lane leading at right angles from the broad, though irregular, main street of the place; and, as may be inferred, containing the blessed abode of Mr. Elton. A few inferior dwellings were first to be passed, and then, about a quarter of a mile down the lane rose the Vicarage, an old and not very good house, almost as close to the road as it could be. It had no advantage of situation; but had been very much smartened up by the present proprietor; and, such as it was, there could be no possibility of the two friends passing it without a slackened pace and observing eyes.—Emma’s remark was—\n\n“There it is. There go you and your riddle-book one of these days.”—Harriet’s was—\n\n“Oh, what a sweet house!—How very beautiful!—There are the yellow curtains that Miss Nash admires so much.”\n\n“I do not often walk this way now,” said Emma, as they proceeded, “but then there will be an inducement, and I shall gradually get intimately acquainted with all the hedges, gates, pools and pollards of this part of Highbury.”\n\nHarriet, she found, had never in her life been inside the Vicarage, and her curiosity to see it was so extreme, that, considering exteriors and probabilities, Emma could only class it, as a proof of love, with Mr. Elton’s seeing ready wit in her.\n\n“I wish we could contrive it,” said she; “but I cannot think of any tolerable pretence for going in;—no servant that I want to inquire about of his housekeeper—no message from my father.”\n\nShe pondered, but could think of nothing. After a mutual silence of some minutes, Harriet thus began again—\n\n“I do so wonder, Miss Woodhouse, that you should not be married, or going to be married! so charming as you are!”—\n\nEmma laughed, and replied,\n\n“My being charming, Harriet, is not quite enough to induce me to marry; I must find other people charming—one other person at least. And I am not only, not going to be married, at present, but have very little intention of ever marrying at all.”\n\n“Ah!—so you say; but I cannot believe it.”\n\n“I must see somebody very superior to any one I have seen yet, to be tempted; Mr. Elton, you know, (recollecting herself,) is out of the question: and I do not wish to see any such person. I would rather not be tempted. I cannot really change for the better. If I were to marry, I must expect to repent it.”\n\n“Dear me!—it is so odd to hear a woman talk so!”—\n\n“I have none of the usual inducements of women to marry. Were I to fall in love, indeed, it would be a different thing! but I never have been in love; it is not my way, or my nature; and I do not think I ever shall. And, without love, I am sure I should be a fool to change such a situation as mine. Fortune I do not want; employment I do not want; consequence I do not want: I believe few married women are half as much mistress of their husband’s house as I am of Hartfield; and never, never could I expect to be so truly beloved and important; so always first and always right in any man’s eyes as I am in my father’s.”\n\n“But then, to be an old maid at last, like Miss Bates!”\n\n“That is as formidable an image as you could present, Harriet; and if I thought I should ever be like Miss Bates! so silly—so satisfied—so smiling—so prosing—so undistinguishing and unfastidious—and so apt to tell every thing relative to every body about me, I would marry to-morrow. But between us, I am convinced there never can be any likeness, except in being unmarried.”\n\n“But still, you will be an old maid! and that’s so dreadful!”\n\n“Never mind, Harriet, I shall not be a poor old maid; and it is poverty only which makes celibacy contemptible to a generous public! A single woman, with a very narrow income, must be a ridiculous, disagreeable old maid! the proper sport of boys and girls, but a single woman, of good fortune, is always respectable, and may be as sensible and pleasant as any body else. And the distinction is not quite so much against the candour and common sense of the world as appears at first; for a very narrow income has a tendency to contract the mind, and sour the temper. Those who can barely live, and who live perforce in a very small, and generally very inferior, society, may well be illiberal and cross. This does not apply, however, to Miss Bates; she is only too good natured and too silly to suit me; but, in general, she is very much to the taste of every body, though single and though poor. Poverty certainly has not contracted her mind: I really believe, if she had only a shilling in the world, she would be very likely to give away sixpence of it; and nobody is afraid of her: that is a great charm.”\n\n“Dear me! but what shall you do? how shall you employ yourself when you grow old?”\n\n“If I know myself, Harriet, mine is an active, busy mind, with a great many independent resources; and I do not perceive why I should be more in want of employment at forty or fifty than one-and-twenty. Woman’s usual occupations of hand and mind will be as open to me then as they are now; or with no important variation. If I draw less, I shall read more; if I give up music, I shall take to carpet-work. And as for objects of interest, objects for the affections, which is in truth the great point of inferiority, the want of which is really the great evil to be avoided in not marrying, I shall be very well off, with all the children of a sister I love so much, to care about. There will be enough of them, in all probability, to supply every sort of sensation that declining life can need. There will be enough for every hope and every fear; and though my attachment to none can equal that of a parent, it suits my ideas of comfort better than what is warmer and blinder. My nephews and nieces!—I shall often have a niece with me.”\n\n“Do you know Miss Bates’s niece? That is, I know you must have seen her a hundred times—but are you acquainted?”\n\n“Oh! yes; we are always forced to be acquainted whenever she comes to Highbury. By the bye, that is almost enough to put one out of conceit with a niece. Heaven forbid! at least, that I should ever bore people half so much about all the Knightleys together, as she does about Jane Fairfax. One is sick of the very name of Jane Fairfax. Every letter from her is read forty times over; her compliments to all friends go round and round again; and if she does but send her aunt the pattern of a stomacher, or knit a pair of garters for her grandmother, one hears of nothing else for a month. I wish Jane Fairfax very well; but she tires me to death.”\n\nThey were now approaching the cottage, and all idle topics were superseded. Emma was very compassionate; and the distresses of the poor were as sure of relief from her personal attention and kindness, her counsel and her patience, as from her purse. She understood their ways, could allow for their ignorance and their temptations, had no romantic expectations of extraordinary virtue from those for whom education had done so little; entered into their troubles with ready sympathy, and always gave her assistance with as much intelligence as good-will. In the present instance, it was sickness and poverty together which she came to visit; and after remaining there as long as she could give comfort or advice, she quitted the cottage with such an impression of the scene as made her say to Harriet, as they walked away,\n\n“These are the sights, Harriet, to do one good. How trifling they make every thing else appear!—I feel now as if I could think of nothing but these poor creatures all the rest of the day; and yet, who can say how soon it may all vanish from my mind?”\n\n“Very true,” said Harriet. “Poor creatures! one can think of nothing else.”\n\n“And really, I do not think the impression will soon be over,” said Emma, as she crossed the low hedge, and tottering footstep which ended the narrow, slippery path through the cottage garden, and brought them into the lane again. “I do not think it will,” stopping to look once more at all the outward wretchedness of the place, and recall the still greater within.\n\n“Oh! dear, no,” said her companion.\n\nThey walked on. The lane made a slight bend; and when that bend was passed, Mr. Elton was immediately in sight; and so near as to give Emma time only to say farther,\n\n“Ah! Harriet, here comes a very sudden trial of our stability in good thoughts. Well, (smiling,) I hope it may be allowed that if compassion has produced exertion and relief to the sufferers, it has done all that is truly important. If we feel for the wretched, enough to do all we can for them, the rest is empty sympathy, only distressing to ourselves.”\n\nHarriet could just answer, “Oh! dear, yes,” before the gentleman joined them. The wants and sufferings of the poor family, however, were the first subject on meeting. He had been going to call on them. His visit he would now defer; but they had a very interesting parley about what could be done and should be done. Mr. Elton then turned back to accompany them.\n\n“To fall in with each other on such an errand as this,” thought Emma; “to meet in a charitable scheme; this will bring a great increase of love on each side. I should not wonder if it were to bring on the declaration. It must, if I were not here. I wish I were anywhere else.”\n\nAnxious to separate herself from them as far as she could, she soon afterwards took possession of a narrow footpath, a little raised on one side of the lane, leaving them together in the main road. But she had not been there two minutes when she found that Harriet’s habits of dependence and imitation were bringing her up too, and that, in short, they would both be soon after her. This would not do; she immediately stopped, under pretence of having some alteration to make in the lacing of her half-boot, and stooping down in complete occupation of the footpath, begged them to have the goodness to walk on, and she would follow in half a minute. They did as they were desired; and by the time she judged it reasonable to have done with her boot, she had the comfort of farther delay in her power, being overtaken by a child from the cottage, setting out, according to orders, with her pitcher, to fetch broth from Hartfield. To walk by the side of this child, and talk to and question her, was the most natural thing in the world, or would have been the most natural, had she been acting just then without design; and by this means the others were still able to keep ahead, without any obligation of waiting for her. She gained on them, however, involuntarily: the child’s pace was quick, and theirs rather slow; and she was the more concerned at it, from their being evidently in a conversation which interested them. Mr. Elton was speaking with animation, Harriet listening with a very pleased attention; and Emma, having sent the child on, was beginning to think how she might draw back a little more, when they both looked around, and she was obliged to join them.\n\nMr. Elton was still talking, still engaged in some interesting detail; and Emma experienced some disappointment when she found that he was only giving his fair companion an account of the yesterday’s party at his friend Cole’s, and that she was come in herself for the Stilton cheese, the north Wiltshire, the butter, the celery, the beet-root, and all the dessert.\n\n“This would soon have led to something better, of course,” was her consoling reflection; “any thing interests between those who love; and any thing will serve as introduction to what is near the heart. If I could but have kept longer away!”\n\nThey now walked on together quietly, till within view of the vicarage pales, when a sudden resolution, of at least getting Harriet into the house, made her again find something very much amiss about her boot, and fall behind to arrange it once more. She then broke the lace off short, and dexterously throwing it into a ditch, was presently obliged to entreat them to stop, and acknowledged her inability to put herself to rights so as to be able to walk home in tolerable comfort.\n\n“Part of my lace is gone,” said she, “and I do not know how I am to contrive. I really am a most troublesome companion to you both, but I hope I am not often so ill-equipped. Mr. Elton, I must beg leave to stop at your house, and ask your housekeeper for a bit of ribband or string, or any thing just to keep my boot on.”\n\nMr. Elton looked all happiness at this proposition; and nothing could exceed his alertness and attention in conducting them into his house and endeavouring to make every thing appear to advantage. The room they were taken into was the one he chiefly occupied, and looking forwards; behind it was another with which it immediately communicated; the door between them was open, and Emma passed into it with the housekeeper to receive her assistance in the most comfortable manner. She was obliged to leave the door ajar as she found it; but she fully intended that Mr. Elton should close it. It was not closed, however, it still remained ajar; but by engaging the housekeeper in incessant conversation, she hoped to make it practicable for him to chuse his own subject in the adjoining room. For ten minutes she could hear nothing but herself. It could be protracted no longer. She was then obliged to be finished, and make her appearance.\n\nThe lovers were standing together at one of the windows. It had a most favourable aspect; and, for half a minute, Emma felt the glory of having schemed successfully. But it would not do; he had not come to the point. He had been most agreeable, most delightful; he had told Harriet that he had seen them go by, and had purposely followed them; other little gallantries and allusions had been dropt, but nothing serious.\n\n“Cautious, very cautious,” thought Emma; “he advances inch by inch, and will hazard nothing till he believes himself secure.”\n\nStill, however, though every thing had not been accomplished by her ingenious device, she could not but flatter herself that it had been the occasion of much present enjoyment to both, and must be leading them forward to the great event.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter XI\n\nMr. Elton must now be left to himself. It was no longer in Emma’s power to superintend his happiness or quicken his measures. The coming of her sister’s family was so very near at hand, that first in anticipation, and then in reality, it became henceforth her prime object of interest; and during the ten days of their stay at Hartfield it was not to be expected—she did not herself expect—that any thing beyond occasional, fortuitous assistance could be afforded by her to the lovers. They might advance rapidly if they would, however; they must advance somehow or other whether they would or no. She hardly wished to have more leisure for them. There are people, who the more you do for them, the less they will do for themselves.\n\nMr. and Mrs. John Knightley, from having been longer than usual absent from Surry, were exciting of course rather more than the usual interest. Till this year, every long vacation since their marriage had been divided between Hartfield and Donwell Abbey; but all the holidays of this autumn had been given to sea-bathing for the children, and it was therefore many months since they had been seen in a regular way by their Surry connexions, or seen at all by Mr. Woodhouse, who could not be induced to get so far as London, even for poor Isabella’s sake; and who consequently was now most nervously and apprehensively happy in forestalling this too short visit.\n\nHe thought much of the evils of the journey for her, and not a little of the fatigues of his own horses and coachman who were to bring some of the party the last half of the way; but his alarms were needless; the sixteen miles being happily accomplished, and Mr. and Mrs. John Knightley, their five children, and a competent number of nursery-maids, all reaching Hartfield in safety. The bustle and joy of such an arrival, the many to be talked to, welcomed, encouraged, and variously dispersed and disposed of, produced a noise and confusion which his nerves could not have borne under any other cause, nor have endured much longer even for this; but the ways of Hartfield and the feelings of her father were so respected by Mrs. John Knightley, that in spite of maternal solicitude for the immediate enjoyment of her little ones, and for their having instantly all the liberty and attendance, all the eating and drinking, and sleeping and playing, which they could possibly wish for, without the smallest delay, the children were never allowed to be long a disturbance to him, either in themselves or in any restless attendance on them.\n\nMrs. John Knightley was a pretty, elegant little woman, of gentle, quiet manners, and a disposition remarkably amiable and affectionate; wrapt up in her family; a devoted wife, a doating mother, and so tenderly attached to her father and sister that, but for these higher ties, a warmer love might have seemed impossible. She could never see a fault in any of them. She was not a woman of strong understanding or any quickness; and with this resemblance of her father, she inherited also much of his constitution; was delicate in her own health, over-careful of that of her children, had many fears and many nerves, and was as fond of her own Mr. Wingfield in town as her father could be of Mr. Perry. They were alike too, in a general benevolence of temper, and a strong habit of regard for every old acquaintance.\n\nMr. John Knightley was a tall, gentleman-like, and very clever man; rising in his profession, domestic, and respectable in his private character; but with reserved manners which prevented his being generally pleasing; and capable of being sometimes out of humour. He was not an ill-tempered man, not so often unreasonably cross as to deserve such a reproach; but his temper was not his great perfection; and, indeed, with such a worshipping wife, it was hardly possible that any natural defects in it should not be increased. The extreme sweetness of her temper must hurt his. He had all the clearness and quickness of mind which she wanted, and he could sometimes act an ungracious, or say a severe thing.\n\nHe was not a great favourite with his fair sister-in-law. Nothing wrong in him escaped her. She was quick in feeling the little injuries to Isabella, which Isabella never felt herself. Perhaps she might have passed over more had his manners been flattering to Isabella’s sister, but they were only those of a calmly kind brother and friend, without praise and without blindness; but hardly any degree of personal compliment could have made her regardless of that greatest fault of all in her eyes which he sometimes fell into, the want of respectful forbearance towards her father. There he had not always the patience that could have been wished. Mr. Woodhouse’s peculiarities and fidgetiness were sometimes provoking him to a rational remonstrance or sharp retort equally ill-bestowed. It did not often happen; for Mr. John Knightley had really a great regard for his father-in-law, and generally a strong sense of what was due to him; but it was too often for Emma’s charity, especially as there was all the pain of apprehension frequently to be endured, though the offence came not. The beginning, however, of every visit displayed none but the properest feelings, and this being of necessity so short might be hoped to pass away in unsullied cordiality. They had not been long seated and composed when Mr. Woodhouse, with a melancholy shake of the head and a sigh, called his daughter’s attention to the sad change at Hartfield since she had been there last.\n\n“Ah, my dear,” said he, “poor Miss Taylor—It is a grievous business.”\n\n“Oh yes, sir,” cried she with ready sympathy, “how you must miss her! And dear Emma, too!—What a dreadful loss to you both!—I have been so grieved for you.—I could not imagine how you could possibly do without her.—It is a sad change indeed.—But I hope she is pretty well, sir.”\n\n“Pretty well, my dear—I hope—pretty well.—I do not know but that the place agrees with her tolerably.”\n\nMr. John Knightley here asked Emma quietly whether there were any doubts of the air of Randalls.\n\n“Oh! no—none in the least. I never saw Mrs. Weston better in my life—never looking so well. Papa is only speaking his own regret.”\n\n“Very much to the honour of both,” was the handsome reply.\n\n“And do you see her, sir, tolerably often?” asked Isabella in the plaintive tone which just suited her father.\n\nMr. Woodhouse hesitated.—“Not near so often, my dear, as I could wish.”\n\n“Oh! papa, we have missed seeing them but one entire day since they married. Either in the morning or evening of every day, excepting one, have we seen either Mr. Weston or Mrs. Weston, and generally both, either at Randalls or here—and as you may suppose, Isabella, most frequently here. They are very, very kind in their visits. Mr. Weston is really as kind as herself. Papa, if you speak in that melancholy way, you will be giving Isabella a false idea of us all. Every body must be aware that Miss Taylor must be missed, but every body ought also to be assured that Mr. and Mrs. Weston do really prevent our missing her by any means to the extent we ourselves anticipated—which is the exact truth.”\n\n“Just as it should be,” said Mr. John Knightley, “and just as I hoped it was from your letters. Her wish of shewing you attention could not be doubted, and his being a disengaged and social man makes it all easy. I have been always telling you, my love, that I had no idea of the change being so very material to Hartfield as you apprehended; and now you have Emma’s account, I hope you will be satisfied.”\n\n“Why, to be sure,” said Mr. Woodhouse—“yes, certainly—I cannot deny that Mrs. Weston, poor Mrs. Weston, does come and see us pretty often—but then—she is always obliged to go away again.”\n\n“It would be very hard upon Mr. Weston if she did not, papa.—You quite forget poor Mr. Weston.”\n\n“I think, indeed,” said John Knightley pleasantly, “that Mr. Weston has some little claim. You and I, Emma, will venture to take the part of the poor husband. I, being a husband, and you not being a wife, the claims of the man may very likely strike us with equal force. As for Isabella, she has been married long enough to see the convenience of putting all the Mr. Westons aside as much as she can.”\n\n“Me, my love,” cried his wife, hearing and understanding only in part.— “Are you talking about me?—I am sure nobody ought to be, or can be, a greater advocate for matrimony than I am; and if it had not been for the misery of her leaving Hartfield, I should never have thought of Miss Taylor but as the most fortunate woman in the world; and as to slighting Mr. Weston, that excellent Mr. Weston, I think there is nothing he does not deserve. I believe he is one of the very best-tempered men that ever existed. Excepting yourself and your brother, I do not know his equal for temper. I shall never forget his flying Henry’s kite for him that very windy day last Easter—and ever since his particular kindness last September twelvemonth in writing that note, at twelve o’clock at night, on purpose to assure me that there was no scarlet fever at Cobham, I have been convinced there could not be a more feeling heart nor a better man in existence.—If any body can deserve him, it must be Miss Taylor.”\n\n“Where is the young man?” said John Knightley. “Has he been here on this occasion—or has he not?”\n\n“He has not been here yet,” replied Emma. “There was a strong expectation of his coming soon after the marriage, but it ended in nothing; and I have not heard him mentioned lately.”\n\n“But you should tell them of the letter, my dear,” said her father. “He wrote a letter to poor Mrs. Weston, to congratulate her, and a very proper, handsome letter it was. She shewed it to me. I thought it very well done of him indeed. Whether it was his own idea you know, one cannot tell. He is but young, and his uncle, perhaps—”\n\n“My dear papa, he is three-and-twenty. You forget how time passes.”\n\n“Three-and-twenty!—is he indeed?—Well, I could not have thought it—and he was but two years old when he lost his poor mother! Well, time does fly indeed!—and my memory is very bad. However, it was an exceeding good, pretty letter, and gave Mr. and Mrs. Weston a great deal of pleasure. I remember it was written from Weymouth, and dated Sept. 28th—and began, ‘My dear Madam,’ but I forget how it went on; and it was signed ‘F. C. Weston Churchill.’—I remember that perfectly.”\n\n“How very pleasing and proper of him!” cried the good-hearted Mrs. John Knightley. “I have no doubt of his being a most amiable young man. But how sad it is that he should not live at home with his father! There is something so shocking in a child’s being taken away from his parents and natural home! I never could comprehend how Mr. Weston could part with him. To give up one’s child! I really never could think well of any body who proposed such a thing to any body else.”\n\n“Nobody ever did think well of the Churchills, I fancy,” observed Mr. John Knightley coolly. “But you need not imagine Mr. Weston to have felt what you would feel in giving up Henry or John. Mr. Weston is rather an easy, cheerful-tempered man, than a man of strong feelings; he takes things as he finds them, and makes enjoyment of them somehow or other, depending, I suspect, much more upon what is called society for his comforts, that is, upon the power of eating and drinking, and playing whist with his neighbours five times a week, than upon family affection, or any thing that home affords.”\n\nEmma could not like what bordered on a reflection on Mr. Weston, and had half a mind to take it up; but she struggled, and let it pass. She would keep the peace if possible; and there was something honourable and valuable in the strong domestic habits, the all-sufficiency of home to himself, whence resulted her brother’s disposition to look down on the common rate of social intercourse, and those to whom it was important.—It had a high claim to forbearance.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter XII\n\nMr. Knightley was to dine with them—rather against the inclination of Mr. Woodhouse, who did not like that any one should share with him in Isabella’s first day. Emma’s sense of right however had decided it; and besides the consideration of what was due to each brother, she had particular pleasure, from the circumstance of the late disagreement between Mr. Knightley and herself, in procuring him the proper invitation.\n\nShe hoped they might now become friends again. She thought it was time to make up. Making-up indeed would not do. She certainly had not been in the wrong, and he would never own that he had. Concession must be out of the question; but it was time to appear to forget that they had ever quarrelled; and she hoped it might rather assist the restoration of friendship, that when he came into the room she had one of the children with her—the youngest, a nice little girl about eight months old, who was now making her first visit to Hartfield, and very happy to be danced about in her aunt’s arms. It did assist; for though he began with grave looks and short questions, he was soon led on to talk of them all in the usual way, and to take the child out of her arms with all the unceremoniousness of perfect amity. Emma felt they were friends again; and the conviction giving her at first great satisfaction, and then a little sauciness, she could not help saying, as he was admiring the baby,\n\n“What a comfort it is, that we think alike about our nephews and nieces. As to men and women, our opinions are sometimes very different; but with regard to these children, I observe we never disagree.”\n\n“If you were as much guided by nature in your estimate of men and women, and as little under the power of fancy and whim in your dealings with them, as you are where these children are concerned, we might always think alike.”\n\n“To be sure—our discordancies must always arise from my being in the wrong.”\n\n“Yes,” said he, smiling—“and reason good. I was sixteen years old when you were born.”\n\n“A material difference then,” she replied—“and no doubt you were much my superior in judgment at that period of our lives; but does not the lapse of one-and-twenty years bring our understandings a good deal nearer?”\n\n“Yes—a good deal nearer.”\n\n“But still, not near enough to give me a chance of being right, if we think differently.”\n\n“I have still the advantage of you by sixteen years’ experience, and by not being a pretty young woman and a spoiled child. Come, my dear Emma, let us be friends, and say no more about it. Tell your aunt, little Emma, that she ought to set you a better example than to be renewing old grievances, and that if she were not wrong before, she is now.”\n\n“That’s true,” she cried—“very true. Little Emma, grow up a better woman than your aunt. Be infinitely cleverer and not half so conceited. Now, Mr. Knightley, a word or two more, and I have done. As far as good intentions went, we were both right, and I must say that no effects on my side of the argument have yet proved wrong. I only want to know that Mr. Martin is not very, very bitterly disappointed.”\n\n“A man cannot be more so,” was his short, full answer.\n\n“Ah!—Indeed I am very sorry.—Come, shake hands with me.”\n\nThis had just taken place and with great cordiality, when John Knightley made his appearance, and “How d’ye do, George?” and “John, how are you?” succeeded in the true English style, burying under a calmness that seemed all but indifference, the real attachment which would have led either of them, if requisite, to do every thing for the good of the other.\n\nThe evening was quiet and conversable, as Mr. Woodhouse declined cards entirely for the sake of comfortable talk with his dear Isabella, and the little party made two natural divisions; on one side he and his daughter; on the other the two Mr. Knightleys; their subjects totally distinct, or very rarely mixing—and Emma only occasionally joining in one or the other.\n\nThe brothers talked of their own concerns and pursuits, but principally of those of the elder, whose temper was by much the most communicative, and who was always the greater talker. As a magistrate, he had generally some point of law to consult John about, or, at least, some curious anecdote to give; and as a farmer, as keeping in hand the home-farm at Donwell, he had to tell what every field was to bear next year, and to give all such local information as could not fail of being interesting to a brother whose home it had equally been the longest part of his life, and whose attachments were strong. The plan of a drain, the change of a fence, the felling of a tree, and the destination of every acre for wheat, turnips, or spring corn, was entered into with as much equality of interest by John, as his cooler manners rendered possible; and if his willing brother ever left him any thing to inquire about, his inquiries even approached a tone of eagerness.\n\nWhile they were thus comfortably occupied, Mr. Woodhouse was enjoying a full flow of happy regrets and fearful affection with his daughter.\n\n“My poor dear Isabella,” said he, fondly taking her hand, and interrupting, for a few moments, her busy labours for some one of her five children—“How long it is, how terribly long since you were here! And how tired you must be after your journey! You must go to bed early, my dear—and I recommend a little gruel to you before you go.—You and I will have a nice basin of gruel together. My dear Emma, suppose we all have a little gruel.”\n\nEmma could not suppose any such thing, knowing as she did, that both the Mr. Knightleys were as unpersuadable on that article as herself;—and two basins only were ordered. After a little more discourse in praise of gruel, with some wondering at its not being taken every evening by every body, he proceeded to say, with an air of grave reflection,\n\n“It was an awkward business, my dear, your spending the autumn at South End instead of coming here. I never had much opinion of the sea air.”\n\n“Mr. Wingfield most strenuously recommended it, sir—or we should not have gone. He recommended it for all the children, but particularly for the weakness in little Bella’s throat,—both sea air and bathing.”\n\n“Ah! my dear, but Perry had many doubts about the sea doing her any good; and as to myself, I have been long perfectly convinced, though perhaps I never told you so before, that the sea is very rarely of use to any body. I am sure it almost killed me once.”\n\n“Come, come,” cried Emma, feeling this to be an unsafe subject, “I must beg you not to talk of the sea. It makes me envious and miserable;—I who have never seen it! South End is prohibited, if you please. My dear Isabella, I have not heard you make one inquiry about Mr. Perry yet; and he never forgets you.”\n\n“Oh! good Mr. Perry—how is he, sir?”\n\n“Why, pretty well; but not quite well. Poor Perry is bilious, and he has not time to take care of himself—he tells me he has not time to take care of himself—which is very sad—but he is always wanted all round the country. I suppose there is not a man in such practice anywhere. But then there is not so clever a man any where.”\n\n“And Mrs. Perry and the children, how are they? do the children grow? I have a great regard for Mr. Perry. I hope he will be calling soon. He will be so pleased to see my little ones.”\n\n“I hope he will be here to-morrow, for I have a question or two to ask him about myself of some consequence. And, my dear, whenever he comes, you had better let him look at little Bella’s throat.”\n\n“Oh! my dear sir, her throat is so much better that I have hardly any uneasiness about it. Either bathing has been of the greatest service to her, or else it is to be attributed to an excellent embrocation of Mr. Wingfield’s, which we have been applying at times ever since August.”\n\n“It is not very likely, my dear, that bathing should have been of use to her—and if I had known you were wanting an embrocation, I would have spoken to—\n\n“You seem to me to have forgotten Mrs. and Miss Bates,” said Emma, “I have not heard one inquiry after them.”\n\n“Oh! the good Bateses—I am quite ashamed of myself—but you mention them in most of your letters. I hope they are quite well. Good old Mrs. Bates—I will call upon her to-morrow, and take my children.—They are always so pleased to see my children.—And that excellent Miss Bates!—such thorough worthy people!—How are they, sir?”\n\n“Why, pretty well, my dear, upon the whole. But poor Mrs. Bates had a bad cold about a month ago.”\n\n“How sorry I am! But colds were never so prevalent as they have been this autumn. Mr. Wingfield told me that he has never known them more general or heavy—except when it has been quite an influenza.”\n\n“That has been a good deal the case, my dear; but not to the degree you mention. Perry says that colds have been very general, but not so heavy as he has very often known them in November. Perry does not call it altogether a sickly season.”\n\n“No, I do not know that Mr. Wingfield considers it very sickly except—\n\n“Ah! my poor dear child, the truth is, that in London it is always a sickly season. Nobody is healthy in London, nobody can be. It is a dreadful thing to have you forced to live there! so far off!—and the air so bad!”\n\n“No, indeed—we are not at all in a bad air. Our part of London is very superior to most others!—You must not confound us with London in general, my dear sir. The neighbourhood of Brunswick Square is very different from almost all the rest. We are so very airy! I should be unwilling, I own, to live in any other part of the town;—there is hardly any other that I could be satisfied to have my children in: but we are so remarkably airy!—Mr. Wingfield thinks the vicinity of Brunswick Square decidedly the most favourable as to air.”\n\n“Ah! my dear, it is not like Hartfield. You make the best of it—but after you have been a week at Hartfield, you are all of you different creatures; you do not look like the same. Now I cannot say, that I think you are any of you looking well at present.”\n\n“I am sorry to hear you say so, sir; but I assure you, excepting those little nervous head-aches and palpitations which I am never entirely free from anywhere, I am quite well myself; and if the children were rather pale before they went to bed, it was only because they were a little more tired than usual, from their journey and the happiness of coming. I hope you will think better of their looks to-morrow; for I assure you Mr. Wingfield told me, that he did not believe he had ever sent us off altogether, in such good case. I trust, at least, that you do not think Mr. Knightley looking ill,” turning her eyes with affectionate anxiety towards her husband.\n\n“Middling, my dear; I cannot compliment you. I think Mr. John Knightley very far from looking well.”\n\n“What is the matter, sir?—Did you speak to me?” cried Mr. John Knightley, hearing his own name.\n\n“I am sorry to find, my love, that my father does not think you looking well—but I hope it is only from being a little fatigued. I could have wished, however, as you know, that you had seen Mr. Wingfield before you left home.”\n\n“My dear Isabella,”—exclaimed he hastily—“pray do not concern yourself about my looks. Be satisfied with doctoring and coddling yourself and the children, and let me look as I chuse.”\n\n“I did not thoroughly understand what you were telling your brother,” cried Emma, “about your friend Mr. Graham’s intending to have a bailiff from Scotland, to look after his new estate. What will it answer? Will not the old prejudice be too strong?”\n\nAnd she talked in this way so long and successfully that, when forced to give her attention again to her father and sister, she had nothing worse to hear than Isabella’s kind inquiry after Jane Fairfax; and Jane Fairfax, though no great favourite with her in general, she was at that moment very happy to assist in praising.\n\n“That sweet, amiable Jane Fairfax!” said Mrs. John Knightley.—“It is so long since I have seen her, except now and then for a moment accidentally in town! What happiness it must be to her good old grandmother and excellent aunt, when she comes to visit them! I always regret excessively on dear Emma’s account that she cannot be more at Highbury; but now their daughter is married, I suppose Colonel and Mrs. Campbell will not be able to part with her at all. She would be such a delightful companion for Emma.”\n\nMr. Woodhouse agreed to it all, but added,\n\n“Our little friend Harriet Smith, however, is just such another pretty kind of young person. You will like Harriet. Emma could not have a better companion than Harriet.”\n\n“I am most happy to hear it—but only Jane Fairfax one knows to be so very accomplished and superior!—and exactly Emma’s age.”\n\nThis topic was discussed very happily, and others succeeded of similar moment, and passed away with similar harmony; but the evening did not close without a little return of agitation. The gruel came and supplied a great deal to be said—much praise and many comments—undoubting decision of its wholesomeness for every constitution, and pretty severe Philippics upon the many houses where it was never met with tolerably;—but, unfortunately, among the failures which the daughter had to instance, the most recent, and therefore most prominent, was in her own cook at South End, a young woman hired for the time, who never had been able to understand what she meant by a basin of nice smooth gruel, thin, but not too thin. Often as she had wished for and ordered it, she had never been able to get any thing tolerable. Here was a dangerous opening.\n\n“Ah!” said Mr. Woodhouse, shaking his head and fixing his eyes on her with tender concern.—The ejaculation in Emma’s ear expressed, “Ah! there is no end of the sad consequences of your going to South End. It does not bear talking of.” And for a little while she hoped he would not talk of it, and that a silent rumination might suffice to restore him to the relish of his own smooth gruel. After an interval of some minutes, however, he began with,\n\n“I shall always be very sorry that you went to the sea this autumn, instead of coming here.”\n\n“But why should you be sorry, sir?—I assure you, it did the children a great deal of good.”\n\n“And, moreover, if you must go to the sea, it had better not have been to South End. South End is an unhealthy place. Perry was surprized to hear you had fixed upon South End.”\n\n“I know there is such an idea with many people, but indeed it is quite a mistake, sir.—We all had our health perfectly well there, never found the least inconvenience from the mud; and Mr. Wingfield says it is entirely a mistake to suppose the place unhealthy; and I am sure he may be depended on, for he thoroughly understands the nature of the air, and his own brother and family have been there repeatedly.”\n\n“You should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere.—Perry was a week at Cromer once, and he holds it to be the best of all the sea-bathing places. A fine open sea, he says, and very pure air. And, by what I understand, you might have had lodgings there quite away from the sea—a quarter of a mile off—very comfortable. You should have consulted Perry.”\n\n“But, my dear sir, the difference of the journey;—only consider how great it would have been.—An hundred miles, perhaps, instead of forty.”\n\n“Ah! my dear, as Perry says, where health is at stake, nothing else should be considered; and if one is to travel, there is not much to chuse between forty miles and an hundred.—Better not move at all, better stay in London altogether than travel forty miles to get into a worse air. This is just what Perry said. It seemed to him a very ill-judged measure.”\n\nEmma’s attempts to stop her father had been vain; and when he had reached such a point as this, she could not wonder at her brother-in-law’s breaking out.\n\n“Mr. Perry,” said he, in a voice of very strong displeasure, “would do as well to keep his opinion till it is asked for. Why does he make it any business of his, to wonder at what I do?—at my taking my family to one part of the coast or another?—I may be allowed, I hope, the use of my judgment as well as Mr. Perry.—I want his directions no more than his drugs.” He paused—and growing cooler in a moment, added, with only sarcastic dryness, “If Mr. Perry can tell me how to convey a wife and five children a distance of an hundred and thirty miles with no greater expense or inconvenience than a distance of forty, I should be as willing to prefer Cromer to South End as he could himself.”\n\n“True, true,” cried Mr. Knightley, with most ready interposition—“very true. That’s a consideration indeed.—But John, as to what I was telling you of my idea of moving the path to Langham, of turning it more to the right that it may not cut through the home meadows, I cannot conceive any difficulty. I should not attempt it, if it were to be the means of inconvenience to the Highbury people, but if you call to mind exactly the present line of the path.... The only way of proving it, however, will be to turn to our maps. I shall see you at the Abbey to-morrow morning I hope, and then we will look them over, and you shall give me your opinion.”\n\nMr. Woodhouse was rather agitated by such harsh reflections on his friend Perry, to whom he had, in fact, though unconsciously, been attributing many of his own feelings and expressions;—but the soothing attentions of his daughters gradually removed the present evil, and the immediate alertness of one brother, and better recollections of the other, prevented any renewal of it.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter XIII\n\nThere could hardly be a happier creature in the world than Mrs. John Knightley, in this short visit to Hartfield, going about every morning among her old acquaintance with her five children, and talking over what she had done every evening with her father and sister. She had nothing to wish otherwise, but that the days did not pass so swiftly. It was a delightful visit;—perfect, in being much too short.\n\nIn general their evenings were less engaged with friends than their mornings; but one complete dinner engagement, and out of the house too, there was no avoiding, though at Christmas. Mr. Weston would take no denial; they must all dine at Randalls one day;—even Mr. Woodhouse was persuaded to think it a possible thing in preference to a division of the party.\n\nHow they were all to be conveyed, he would have made a difficulty if he could, but as his son and daughter’s carriage and horses were actually at Hartfield, he was not able to make more than a simple question on that head; it hardly amounted to a doubt; nor did it occupy Emma long to convince him that they might in one of the carriages find room for Harriet also.\n\nHarriet, Mr. Elton, and Mr. Knightley, their own especial set, were the only persons invited to meet them;—the hours were to be early, as well as the numbers few; Mr. Woodhouse’s habits and inclination being consulted in every thing.\n\nThe evening before this great event (for it was a very great event that Mr. Woodhouse should dine out, on the 24th of December) had been spent by Harriet at Hartfield, and she had gone home so much indisposed with a cold, that, but for her own earnest wish of being nursed by Mrs. Goddard, Emma could not have allowed her to leave the house. Emma called on her the next day, and found her doom already signed with regard to Randalls. She was very feverish and had a bad sore throat: Mrs. Goddard was full of care and affection, Mr. Perry was talked of, and Harriet herself was too ill and low to resist the authority which excluded her from this delightful engagement, though she could not speak of her loss without many tears.\n\nEmma sat with her as long as she could, to attend her in Mrs. Goddard’s unavoidable absences, and raise her spirits by representing how much Mr. Elton’s would be depressed when he knew her state; and left her at last tolerably comfortable, in the sweet dependence of his having a most comfortless visit, and of their all missing her very much. She had not advanced many yards from Mrs. Goddard’s door, when she was met by Mr. Elton himself, evidently coming towards it, and as they walked on slowly together in conversation about the invalid—of whom he, on the rumour of considerable illness, had been going to inquire, that he might carry some report of her to Hartfield—they were overtaken by Mr. John Knightley returning from the daily visit to Donwell, with his two eldest boys, whose healthy, glowing faces shewed all the benefit of a country run, and seemed to ensure a quick despatch of the roast mutton and rice pudding they were hastening home for. They joined company and proceeded together. Emma was just describing the nature of her friend’s complaint;—“a throat very much inflamed, with a great deal of heat about her, a quick, low pulse, &c. and she was sorry to find from Mrs. Goddard that Harriet was liable to very bad sore-throats, and had often alarmed her with them.” Mr. Elton looked all alarm on the occasion, as he exclaimed,\n\n“A sore-throat!—I hope not infectious. I hope not of a putrid infectious sort. Has Perry seen her? Indeed you should take care of yourself as well as of your friend. Let me entreat you to run no risks. Why does not Perry see her?”\n\nEmma, who was not really at all frightened herself, tranquillised this excess of apprehension by assurances of Mrs. Goddard’s experience and care; but as there must still remain a degree of uneasiness which she could not wish to reason away, which she would rather feed and assist than not, she added soon afterwards—as if quite another subject,\n\n“It is so cold, so very cold—and looks and feels so very much like snow, that if it were to any other place or with any other party, I should really try not to go out to-day—and dissuade my father from venturing; but as he has made up his mind, and does not seem to feel the cold himself, I do not like to interfere, as I know it would be so great a disappointment to Mr. and Mrs. Weston. But, upon my word, Mr. Elton, in your case, I should certainly excuse myself. You appear to me a little hoarse already, and when you consider what demand of voice and what fatigues to-morrow will bring, I think it would be no more than common prudence to stay at home and take care of yourself to-night.”\n\nMr. Elton looked as if he did not very well know what answer to make; which was exactly the case; for though very much gratified by the kind care of such a fair lady, and not liking to resist any advice of her’s, he had not really the least inclination to give up the visit;—but Emma, too eager and busy in her own previous conceptions and views to hear him impartially, or see him with clear vision, was very well satisfied with his muttering acknowledgment of its being “very cold, certainly very cold,” and walked on, rejoicing in having extricated him from Randalls, and secured him the power of sending to inquire after Harriet every hour of the evening.\n\n“You do quite right,” said she;—“we will make your apologies to Mr. and Mrs. Weston.”\n\nBut hardly had she so spoken, when she found her brother was civilly offering a seat in his carriage, if the weather were Mr. Elton’s only objection, and Mr. Elton actually accepting the offer with much prompt satisfaction. It was a done thing; Mr. Elton was to go, and never had his broad handsome face expressed more pleasure than at this moment; never had his smile been stronger, nor his eyes more exulting than when he next looked at her.\n\n“Well,” said she to herself, “this is most strange!—After I had got him off so well, to chuse to go into company, and leave Harriet ill behind!—Most strange indeed!—But there is, I believe, in many men, especially single men, such an inclination—such a passion for dining out—a dinner engagement is so high in the class of their pleasures, their employments, their dignities, almost their duties, that any thing gives way to it—and this must be the case with Mr. Elton; a most valuable, amiable, pleasing young man undoubtedly, and very much in love with Harriet; but still, he cannot refuse an invitation, he must dine out wherever he is asked. What a strange thing love is! he can see ready wit in Harriet, but will not dine alone for her.”\n\nSoon afterwards Mr. Elton quitted them, and she could not but do him the justice of feeling that there was a great deal of sentiment in his manner of naming Harriet at parting; in the tone of his voice while assuring her that he should call at Mrs. Goddard’s for news of her fair friend, the last thing before he prepared for the happiness of meeting her again, when he hoped to be able to give a better report; and he sighed and smiled himself off in a way that left the balance of approbation much in his favour.\n\nAfter a few minutes of entire silence between them, John Knightley began with—\n\n“I never in my life saw a man more intent on being agreeable than Mr. Elton. It is downright labour to him where ladies are concerned. With men he can be rational and unaffected, but when he has ladies to please, every feature works.”\n\n“Mr. Elton’s manners are not perfect,” replied Emma; “but where there is a wish to please, one ought to overlook, and one does overlook a great deal. Where a man does his best with only moderate powers, he will have the advantage over negligent superiority. There is such perfect good-temper and good-will in Mr. Elton as one cannot but value.”\n\n“Yes,” said Mr. John Knightley presently, with some slyness, “he seems to have a great deal of good-will towards you.”\n\n“Me!” she replied with a smile of astonishment, “are you imagining me to be Mr. Elton’s object?”\n\n“Such an imagination has crossed me, I own, Emma; and if it never occurred to you before, you may as well take it into consideration now.”\n\n“Mr. Elton in love with me!—What an idea!”\n\n“I do not say it is so; but you will do well to consider whether it is so or not, and to regulate your behaviour accordingly. I think your manners to him encouraging. I speak as a friend, Emma. You had better look about you, and ascertain what you do, and what you mean to do.”\n\n“I thank you; but I assure you you are quite mistaken. Mr. Elton and I are very good friends, and nothing more;” and she walked on, amusing herself in the consideration of the blunders which often arise from a partial knowledge of circumstances, of the mistakes which people of high pretensions to judgment are for ever falling into; and not very well pleased with her brother for imagining her blind and ignorant, and in want of counsel. He said no more.\n\nMr. Woodhouse had so completely made up his mind to the visit, that in spite of the increasing coldness, he seemed to have no idea of shrinking from it, and set forward at last most punctually with his eldest daughter in his own carriage, with less apparent consciousness of the weather than either of the others; too full of the wonder of his own going, and the pleasure it was to afford at Randalls to see that it was cold, and too well wrapt up to feel it. The cold, however, was severe; and by the time the second carriage was in motion, a few flakes of snow were finding their way down, and the sky had the appearance of being so overcharged as to want only a milder air to produce a very white world in a very short time.\n\nEmma soon saw that her companion was not in the happiest humour. The preparing and the going abroad in such weather, with the sacrifice of his children after dinner, were evils, were disagreeables at least, which Mr. John Knightley did not by any means like; he anticipated nothing in the visit that could be at all worth the purchase; and the whole of their drive to the vicarage was spent by him in expressing his discontent.\n\n“A man,” said he, “must have a very good opinion of himself when he asks people to leave their own fireside, and encounter such a day as this, for the sake of coming to see him. He must think himself a most agreeable fellow; I could not do such a thing. It is the greatest absurdity—Actually snowing at this moment!—The folly of not allowing people to be comfortable at home—and the folly of people’s not staying comfortably at home when they can! If we were obliged to go out such an evening as this, by any call of duty or business, what a hardship we should deem it;—and here are we, probably with rather thinner clothing than usual, setting forward voluntarily, without excuse, in defiance of the voice of nature, which tells man, in every thing given to his view or his feelings, to stay at home himself, and keep all under shelter that he can;—here are we setting forward to spend five dull hours in another man’s house, with nothing to say or to hear that was not said and heard yesterday, and may not be said and heard again to-morrow. Going in dismal weather, to return probably in worse;—four horses and four servants taken out for nothing but to convey five idle, shivering creatures into colder rooms and worse company than they might have had at home.”\n\nEmma did not find herself equal to give the pleased assent, which no doubt he was in the habit of receiving, to emulate the “Very true, my love,” which must have been usually administered by his travelling companion; but she had resolution enough to refrain from making any answer at all. She could not be complying, she dreaded being quarrelsome; her heroism reached only to silence. She allowed him to talk, and arranged the glasses, and wrapped herself up, without opening her lips.\n\nThey arrived, the carriage turned, the step was let down, and Mr. Elton, spruce, black, and smiling, was with them instantly. Emma thought with pleasure of some change of subject. Mr. Elton was all obligation and cheerfulness; he was so very cheerful in his civilities indeed, that she began to think he must have received a different account of Harriet from what had reached her. She had sent while dressing, and the answer had been, “Much the same—not better.”\n\n“My report from Mrs. Goddard’s,” said she presently, “was not so pleasant as I had hoped—‘Not better’ was my answer.”\n\nHis face lengthened immediately; and his voice was the voice of sentiment as he answered.\n\n“Oh! no—I am grieved to find—I was on the point of telling you that when I called at Mrs. Goddard’s door, which I did the very last thing before I returned to dress, I was told that Miss Smith was not better, by no means better, rather worse. Very much grieved and concerned—I had flattered myself that she must be better after such a cordial as I knew had been given her in the morning.”\n\nEmma smiled and answered—“My visit was of use to the nervous part of her complaint, I hope; but not even I can charm away a sore throat; it is a most severe cold indeed. Mr. Perry has been with her, as you probably heard.”\n\n“Yes—I imagined—that is—I did not—”\n\n“He has been used to her in these complaints, and I hope to-morrow morning will bring us both a more comfortable report. But it is impossible not to feel uneasiness. Such a sad loss to our party to-day!”\n\n“Dreadful!—Exactly so, indeed.—She will be missed every moment.”\n\nThis was very proper; the sigh which accompanied it was really estimable; but it should have lasted longer. Emma was rather in dismay when only half a minute afterwards he began to speak of other things, and in a voice of the greatest alacrity and enjoyment.\n\n“What an excellent device,” said he, “the use of a sheepskin for carriages. How very comfortable they make it;—impossible to feel cold with such precautions. The contrivances of modern days indeed have rendered a gentleman’s carriage perfectly complete. One is so fenced and guarded from the weather, that not a breath of air can find its way unpermitted. Weather becomes absolutely of no consequence. It is a very cold afternoon—but in this carriage we know nothing of the matter.—Ha! snows a little I see.”\n\n“Yes,” said John Knightley, “and I think we shall have a good deal of it.”\n\n“Christmas weather,” observed Mr. Elton. “Quite seasonable; and extremely fortunate we may think ourselves that it did not begin yesterday, and prevent this day’s party, which it might very possibly have done, for Mr. Woodhouse would hardly have ventured had there been much snow on the ground; but now it is of no consequence. This is quite the season indeed for friendly meetings. At Christmas every body invites their friends about them, and people think little of even the worst weather. I was snowed up at a friend’s house once for a week. Nothing could be pleasanter. I went for only one night, and could not get away till that very day se’nnight.”\n\nMr. John Knightley looked as if he did not comprehend the pleasure, but said only, coolly,\n\n“I cannot wish to be snowed up a week at Randalls.”\n\nAt another time Emma might have been amused, but she was too much astonished now at Mr. Elton’s spirits for other feelings. Harriet seemed quite forgotten in the expectation of a pleasant party.\n\n“We are sure of excellent fires,” continued he, “and every thing in the greatest comfort. Charming people, Mr. and Mrs. Weston;—Mrs. Weston indeed is much beyond praise, and he is exactly what one values, so hospitable, and so fond of society;—it will be a small party, but where small parties are select, they are perhaps the most agreeable of any. Mr. Weston’s dining-room does not accommodate more than ten comfortably; and for my part, I would rather, under such circumstances, fall short by two than exceed by two. I think you will agree with me, (turning with a soft air to Emma,) I think I shall certainly have your approbation, though Mr. Knightley perhaps, from being used to the large parties of London, may not quite enter into our feelings.”\n\n“I know nothing of the large parties of London, sir—I never dine with any body.”\n\n“Indeed! (in a tone of wonder and pity,) I had no idea that the law had been so great a slavery. Well, sir, the time must come when you will be paid for all this, when you will have little labour and great enjoyment.”\n\n“My first enjoyment,” replied John Knightley, as they passed through the sweep-gate, “will be to find myself safe at Hartfield again.”\n\n# Volume I — Chapter XIV\n\nSome change of countenance was necessary for each gentleman as they walked into Mrs. Weston’s drawing-room;—Mr. Elton must compose his joyous looks, and Mr. John Knightley disperse his ill-humour. Mr. Elton must smile less, and Mr. John Knightley more, to fit them for the place.—Emma only might be as nature prompted, and shew herself just as happy as she was. To her it was real enjoyment to be with the Westons. Mr. Weston was a great favourite, and there was not a creature in the world to whom she spoke with such unreserve, as to his wife; not any one, to whom she related with such conviction of being listened to and understood, of being always interesting and always intelligible, the little affairs, arrangements, perplexities, and pleasures of her father and herself. She could tell nothing of Hartfield, in which Mrs. Weston had not a lively concern; and half an hour’s uninterrupted communication of all those little matters on which the daily happiness of private life depends, was one of the first gratifications of each.\n\nThis was a pleasure which perhaps the whole day’s visit might not afford, which certainly did not belong to the present half-hour; but the very sight of Mrs. Weston, her smile, her touch, her voice was grateful to Emma, and she determined to think as little as possible of Mr. Elton’s oddities, or of any thing else unpleasant, and enjoy all that was enjoyable to the utmost.\n\nThe misfortune of Harriet’s cold had been pretty well gone through before her arrival. Mr. Woodhouse had been safely seated long enough to give the history of it, besides all the history of his own and Isabella’s coming, and of Emma’s being to follow, and had indeed just got to the end of his satisfaction that James should come and see his daughter, when the others appeared, and Mrs. Weston, who had been almost wholly engrossed by her attentions to him, was able to turn away and welcome her dear Emma.\n\nEmma’s project of forgetting Mr. Elton for a while made her rather sorry to find, when they had all taken their places, that he was close to her. The difficulty was great of driving his strange insensibility towards Harriet, from her mind, while he not only sat at her elbow, but was continually obtruding his happy countenance on her notice, and solicitously addressing her upon every occasion. Instead of forgetting him, his behaviour was such that she could not avoid the internal suggestion of “Can it really be as my brother imagined? can it be possible for this man to be beginning to transfer his affections from Harriet to me?—Absurd and insufferable!”—Yet he would be so anxious for her being perfectly warm, would be so interested about her father, and so delighted with Mrs. Weston; and at last would begin admiring her drawings with so much zeal and so little knowledge as seemed terribly like a would-be lover, and made it some effort with her to preserve her good manners. For her own sake she could not be rude; and for Harriet’s, in the hope that all would yet turn out right, she was even positively civil; but it was an effort; especially as something was going on amongst the others, in the most overpowering period of Mr. Elton’s nonsense, which she particularly wished to listen to. She heard enough to know that Mr. Weston was giving some information about his son; she heard the words “my son,” and “Frank,” and “my son,” repeated several times over; and, from a few other half-syllables very much suspected that he was announcing an early visit from his son; but before she could quiet Mr. Elton, the subject was so completely past that any reviving question from her would have been awkward.\n\nNow, it so happened that in spite of Emma’s resolution of never marrying, there was something in the name, in the idea of Mr. Frank Churchill, which always interested her. She had frequently thought—especially since his father’s marriage with Miss Taylor—that if she were to marry, he was the very person to suit her in age, character and condition. He seemed by this connexion between the families, quite to belong to her. She could not but suppose it to be a match that every body who knew them must think of. That Mr. and Mrs. Weston did think of it, she was very strongly persuaded; and though not meaning to be induced by him, or by any body else, to give up a situation which she believed more replete with good than any she could change it for, she had a great curiosity to see him, a decided intention of finding him pleasant, of being liked by him to a certain degree, and a sort of pleasure in the idea of their being coupled in their friends’ imaginations.\n\nWith such sensations, Mr. Elton’s civilities were dreadfully ill-timed; but she had the comfort of appearing very polite, while feeling very cross—and of thinking that the rest of the visit could not possibly pass without bringing forward the same information again, or the substance of it, from the open-hearted Mr. Weston.—So it proved;—for when happily released from Mr. Elton, and seated by Mr. Weston, at dinner, he made use of the very first interval in the cares of hospitality, the very first leisure from the saddle of mutton, to say to her,\n\n“We want only two more to be just the right number. I should like to see two more here,—your pretty little friend, Miss Smith, and my son—and then I should say we were quite complete. I believe you did not hear me telling the others in the drawing-room that we are expecting Frank. I had a letter from him this morning, and he will be with us within a fortnight.”\n\nEmma spoke with a very proper degree of pleasure; and fully assented to his proposition of Mr. Frank Churchill and Miss Smith making their party quite complete.\n\n“He has been wanting to come to us,” continued Mr. Weston, “ever since September: every letter has been full of it; but he cannot command his own time. He has those to please who must be pleased, and who (between ourselves) are sometimes to be pleased only by a good many sacrifices. But now I have no doubt of seeing him here about the second week in January.”\n\n“What a very great pleasure it will be to you! and Mrs. Weston is so anxious to be acquainted with him, that she must be almost as happy as yourself.”\n\n“Yes, she would be, but that she thinks there will be another put-off. She does not depend upon his coming so much as I do: but she does not know the parties so well as I do. The case, you see, is—(but this is quite between ourselves: I did not mention a syllable of it in the other room. There are secrets in all families, you know)—The case is, that a party of friends are invited to pay a visit at Enscombe in January; and that Frank’s coming depends upon their being put off. If they are not put off, he cannot stir. But I know they will, because it is a family that a certain lady, of some consequence, at Enscombe, has a particular dislike to: and though it is thought necessary to invite them once in two or three years, they always are put off when it comes to the point. I have not the smallest doubt of the issue. I am as confident of seeing Frank here before the middle of January, as I am of being here myself: but your good friend there (nodding towards the upper end of the table) has so few vagaries herself, and has been so little used to them at Hartfield, that she cannot calculate on their effects, as I have been long in the practice of doing.”\n\n“I am sorry there should be any thing like doubt in the case,” replied Emma; “but am disposed to side with you, Mr. Weston. If you think he will come, I shall think so too; for you know Enscombe.”\n\n“Yes—I have some right to that knowledge; though I have never been at the place in my life.—She is an odd woman!—But I never allow myself to speak ill of her, on Frank’s account; for I do believe her to be very fond of him. I used to think she was not capable of being fond of any body, except herself: but she has always been kind to him (in her way—allowing for little whims and caprices, and expecting every thing to be as she likes). And it is no small credit, in my opinion, to him, that he should excite such an affection; for, though I would not say it to any body else, she has no more heart than a stone to people in general; and the devil of a temper.”\n\nEmma liked the subject so well, that she began upon it, to Mrs. Weston, very soon after their moving into the drawing-room: wishing her joy—yet observing, that she knew the first meeting must be rather alarming.— Mrs. Weston agreed to it; but added, that she should be very glad to be secure of undergoing the anxiety of a first meeting at the time talked of: “for I cannot depend upon his coming. I cannot be so sanguine as Mr. Weston. I am very much afraid that it will all end in nothing. Mr. Weston, I dare say, has been telling you exactly how the matter stands?”\n\n“Yes—it seems to depend upon nothing but the ill-humour of Mrs. Churchill, which I imagine to be the most certain thing in the world.”\n\n“My Emma!” replied Mrs. Weston, smiling, “what is the certainty of caprice?” Then turning to Isabella, who had not been attending before—“You must know, my dear Mrs. Knightley, that we are by no means so sure of seeing Mr. Frank Churchill, in my opinion, as his father thinks. It depends entirely upon his aunt’s spirits and pleasure; in short, upon her temper. To you—to my two daughters—I may venture on the truth. Mrs. Churchill rules at Enscombe, and is a very odd-tempered woman; and his coming now, depends upon her being willing to spare him.”\n\n“Oh, Mrs. Churchill; every body knows Mrs. Churchill,” replied Isabella: “and I am sure I never think of that poor young man without the greatest compassion. To be constantly living with an ill-tempered person, must be dreadful. It is what we happily have never known any thing of; but it must be a life of misery. What a blessing, that she never had any children! Poor little creatures, how unhappy she would have made them!”\n\nEmma wished she had been alone with Mrs. Weston. She should then have heard more: Mrs. Weston would speak to her, with a degree of unreserve which she would not hazard with Isabella; and, she really believed, would scarcely try to conceal any thing relative to the Churchills from her, excepting those views on the young man, of which her own imagination had already given her such instinctive knowledge. But at present there was nothing more to be said. Mr. Woodhouse very soon followed them into the drawing-room. To be sitting long after dinner, was a confinement that he could not endure. Neither wine nor conversation was any thing to him; and gladly did he move to those with whom he was always comfortable.\n\nWhile he talked to Isabella, however, Emma found an opportunity of saying,\n\n“And so you do not consider this visit from your son as by any means certain. I am sorry for it. The introduction must be unpleasant, whenever it takes place; and the sooner it could be over, the better.”\n\n“Yes; and every delay makes one more apprehensive of other delays. Even if this family, the Braithwaites, are put off, I am still afraid that some excuse may be found for disappointing us. I cannot bear to imagine any reluctance on his side; but I am sure there is a great wish on the Churchills’ to keep him to themselves. There is jealousy. They are jealous even of his regard for his father. In short, I can feel no dependence on his coming, and I wish Mr. Weston were less sanguine.”\n\n“He ought to come,” said Emma. “If he could stay only a couple of days, he ought to come; and one can hardly conceive a young man’s not having it in his power to do as much as that. A young woman, if she fall into bad hands, may be teased, and kept at a distance from those she wants to be with; but one cannot comprehend a young man’s being under such restraint, as not to be able to spend a week with his father, if he likes it.”\n\n“One ought to be at Enscombe, and know the ways of the family, before one decides upon what he can do,” replied Mrs. Weston. “One ought to use the same caution, perhaps, in judging of the conduct of any one individual of any one family; but Enscombe, I believe, certainly must not be judged by general rules: she is so very unreasonable; and every thing gives way to her.”\n\n“But she is so fond of the nephew: he is so very great a favourite. Now, according to my idea of Mrs. Churchill, it would be most natural, that while she makes no sacrifice for the comfort of the husband, to whom she owes every thing, while she exercises incessant caprice towards him, she should frequently be governed by the nephew, to whom she owes nothing at all.”\n\n“My dearest Emma, do not pretend, with your sweet temper, to understand a bad one, or to lay down rules for it: you must let it go its own way. I have no doubt of his having, at times, considerable influence; but it may be perfectly impossible for him to know beforehand when it will be.”\n\nEmma listened, and then coolly said, “I shall not be satisfied, unless he comes.”\n\n“He may have a great deal of influence on some points,” continued Mrs. Weston, “and on others, very little: and among those, on which she is beyond his reach, it is but too likely, may be this very circumstance of his coming away from them to visit us.”\n\n# Volume I — Chapter XV\n\nMr. Woodhouse was soon ready for his tea; and when he had drank his tea he was quite ready to go home; and it was as much as his three companions could do, to entertain away his notice of the lateness of the hour, before the other gentlemen appeared. Mr. Weston was chatty and convivial, and no friend to early separations of any sort; but at last the drawing-room party did receive an augmentation. Mr. Elton, in very good spirits, was one of the first to walk in. Mrs. Weston and Emma were sitting together on a sofa. He joined them immediately, and, with scarcely an invitation, seated himself between them.\n\nEmma, in good spirits too, from the amusement afforded her mind by the expectation of Mr. Frank Churchill, was willing to forget his late improprieties, and be as well satisfied with him as before, and on his making Harriet his very first subject, was ready to listen with most friendly smiles.\n\nHe professed himself extremely anxious about her fair friend—her fair, lovely, amiable friend. “Did she know?—had she heard any thing about her, since their being at Randalls?—he felt much anxiety—he must confess that the nature of her complaint alarmed him considerably.” And in this style he talked on for some time very properly, not much attending to any answer, but altogether sufficiently awake to the terror of a bad sore throat; and Emma was quite in charity with him.\n\nBut at last there seemed a perverse turn; it seemed all at once as if he were more afraid of its being a bad sore throat on her account, than on Harriet’s—more anxious that she should escape the infection, than that there should be no infection in the complaint. He began with great earnestness to entreat her to refrain from visiting the sick-chamber again, for the present—to entreat her to promise him not to venture into such hazard till he had seen Mr. Perry and learnt his opinion; and though she tried to laugh it off and bring the subject back into its proper course, there was no putting an end to his extreme solicitude about her. She was vexed. It did appear—there was no concealing it—exactly like the pretence of being in love with her, instead of Harriet; an inconstancy, if real, the most contemptible and abominable! and she had difficulty in behaving with temper. He turned to Mrs. Weston to implore her assistance, “Would not she give him her support?—would not she add her persuasions to his, to induce Miss Woodhouse not to go to Mrs. Goddard’s till it were certain that Miss Smith’s disorder had no infection? He could not be satisfied without a promise—would not she give him her influence in procuring it?”\n\n“So scrupulous for others,” he continued, “and yet so careless for herself! She wanted me to nurse my cold by staying at home to-day, and yet will not promise to avoid the danger of catching an ulcerated sore throat herself. Is this fair, Mrs. Weston?—Judge between us. Have not I some right to complain? I am sure of your kind support and aid.”\n\nEmma saw Mrs. Weston’s surprize, and felt that it must be great, at an address which, in words and manner, was assuming to himself the right of first interest in her; and as for herself, she was too much provoked and offended to have the power of directly saying any thing to the purpose. She could only give him a look; but it was such a look as she thought must restore him to his senses, and then left the sofa, removing to a seat by her sister, and giving her all her attention.\n\nShe had not time to know how Mr. Elton took the reproof, so rapidly did another subject succeed; for Mr. John Knightley now came into the room from examining the weather, and opened on them all with the information of the ground being covered with snow, and of its still snowing fast, with a strong drifting wind; concluding with these words to Mr. Woodhouse:\n\n“This will prove a spirited beginning of your winter engagements, sir. Something new for your coachman and horses to be making their way through a storm of snow.”\n\nPoor Mr. Woodhouse was silent from consternation; but every body else had something to say; every body was either surprized or not surprized, and had some question to ask, or some comfort to offer. Mrs. Weston and Emma tried earnestly to cheer him and turn his attention from his son-in-law, who was pursuing his triumph rather unfeelingly.\n\n“I admired your resolution very much, sir,” said he, “in venturing out in such weather, for of course you saw there would be snow very soon. Every body must have seen the snow coming on. I admired your spirit; and I dare say we shall get home very well. Another hour or two’s snow can hardly make the road impassable; and we are two carriages; if one is blown over in the bleak part of the common field there will be the other at hand. I dare say we shall be all safe at Hartfield before midnight.”\n\nMr. Weston, with triumph of a different sort, was confessing that he had known it to be snowing some time, but had not said a word, lest it should make Mr. Woodhouse uncomfortable, and be an excuse for his hurrying away. As to there being any quantity of snow fallen or likely to fall to impede their return, that was a mere joke; he was afraid they would find no difficulty. He wished the road might be impassable, that he might be able to keep them all at Randalls; and with the utmost good-will was sure that accommodation might be found for every body, calling on his wife to agree with him, that with a little contrivance, every body might be lodged, which she hardly knew how to do, from the consciousness of there being but two spare rooms in the house.\n\n“What is to be done, my dear Emma?—what is to be done?” was Mr. Woodhouse’s first exclamation, and all that he could say for some time. To her he looked for comfort; and her assurances of safety, her representation of the excellence of the horses, and of James, and of their having so many friends about them, revived him a little.\n\nHis eldest daughter’s alarm was equal to his own. The horror of being blocked up at Randalls, while her children were at Hartfield, was full in her imagination; and fancying the road to be now just passable for adventurous people, but in a state that admitted no delay, she was eager to have it settled, that her father and Emma should remain at Randalls, while she and her husband set forward instantly through all the possible accumulations of drifted snow that might impede them.\n\n“You had better order the carriage directly, my love,” said she; “I dare say we shall be able to get along, if we set off directly; and if we do come to any thing very bad, I can get out and walk. I am not at all afraid. I should not mind walking half the way. I could change my shoes, you know, the moment I got home; and it is not the sort of thing that gives me cold.”\n\n“Indeed!” replied he. “Then, my dear Isabella, it is the most extraordinary sort of thing in the world, for in general every thing does give you cold. Walk home!—you are prettily shod for walking home, I dare say. It will be bad enough for the horses.”\n\nIsabella turned to Mrs. Weston for her approbation of the plan. Mrs. Weston could only approve. Isabella then went to Emma; but Emma could not so entirely give up the hope of their being all able to get away; and they were still discussing the point, when Mr. Knightley, who had left the room immediately after his brother’s first report of the snow, came back again, and told them that he had been out of doors to examine, and could answer for there not being the smallest difficulty in their getting home, whenever they liked it, either now or an hour hence. He had gone beyond the sweep—some way along the Highbury road—the snow was nowhere above half an inch deep—in many places hardly enough to whiten the ground; a very few flakes were falling at present, but the clouds were parting, and there was every appearance of its being soon over. He had seen the coachmen, and they both agreed with him in there being nothing to apprehend.\n\nTo Isabella, the relief of such tidings was very great, and they were scarcely less acceptable to Emma on her father’s account, who was immediately set as much at ease on the subject as his nervous constitution allowed; but the alarm that had been raised could not be appeased so as to admit of any comfort for him while he continued at Randalls. He was satisfied of there being no present danger in returning home, but no assurances could convince him that it was safe to stay; and while the others were variously urging and recommending, Mr. Knightley and Emma settled it in a few brief sentences: thus—\n\n“Your father will not be easy; why do not you go?”\n\n“I am ready, if the others are.”\n\n“Shall I ring the bell?”\n\n“Yes, do.”\n\nAnd the bell was rung, and the carriages spoken for. A few minutes more, and Emma hoped to see one troublesome companion deposited in his own house, to get sober and cool, and the other recover his temper and happiness when this visit of hardship were over.\n\nThe carriage came: and Mr. Woodhouse, always the first object on such occasions, was carefully attended to his own by Mr. Knightley and Mr. Weston; but not all that either could say could prevent some renewal of alarm at the sight of the snow which had actually fallen, and the discovery of a much darker night than he had been prepared for. “He was afraid they should have a very bad drive. He was afraid poor Isabella would not like it. And there would be poor Emma in the carriage behind. He did not know what they had best do. They must keep as much together as they could;” and James was talked to, and given a charge to go very slow and wait for the other carriage.\n\nIsabella stept in after her father; John Knightley, forgetting that he did not belong to their party, stept in after his wife very naturally; so that Emma found, on being escorted and followed into the second carriage by Mr. Elton, that the door was to be lawfully shut on them, and that they were to have a tête-à-tête drive. It would not have been the awkwardness of a moment, it would have been rather a pleasure, previous to the suspicions of this very day; she could have talked to him of Harriet, and the three-quarters of a mile would have seemed but one. But now, she would rather it had not happened. She believed he had been drinking too much of Mr. Weston’s good wine, and felt sure that he would want to be talking nonsense.\n\nTo restrain him as much as might be, by her own manners, she was immediately preparing to speak with exquisite calmness and gravity of the weather and the night; but scarcely had she begun, scarcely had they passed the sweep-gate and joined the other carriage, than she found her subject cut up—her hand seized—her attention demanded, and Mr. Elton actually making violent love to her: availing himself of the precious opportunity, declaring sentiments which must be already well known, hoping—fearing—adoring—ready to die if she refused him; but flattering himself that his ardent attachment and unequalled love and unexampled passion could not fail of having some effect, and in short, very much resolved on being seriously accepted as soon as possible. It really was so. Without scruple—without apology—without much apparent diffidence, Mr. Elton, the lover of Harriet, was professing himself her lover. She tried to stop him; but vainly; he would go on, and say it all. Angry as she was, the thought of the moment made her resolve to restrain herself when she did speak. She felt that half this folly must be drunkenness, and therefore could hope that it might belong only to the passing hour. Accordingly, with a mixture of the serious and the playful, which she hoped would best suit his half and half state, she replied,\n\n“I am very much astonished, Mr. Elton. This to me! you forget yourself—you take me for my friend—any message to Miss Smith I shall be happy to deliver; but no more of this to me, if you please.”\n\n“Miss Smith!—message to Miss Smith!—What could she possibly mean!”—And he repeated her words with such assurance of accent, such boastful pretence of amazement, that she could not help replying with quickness,\n\n“Mr. Elton, this is the most extraordinary conduct! and I can account for it only in one way; you are not yourself, or you could not speak either to me, or of Harriet, in such a manner. Command yourself enough to say no more, and I will endeavour to forget it.”\n\nBut Mr. Elton had only drunk wine enough to elevate his spirits, not at all to confuse his intellects. He perfectly knew his own meaning; and having warmly protested against her suspicion as most injurious, and slightly touched upon his respect for Miss Smith as her friend,—but acknowledging his wonder that Miss Smith should be mentioned at all,—he resumed the subject of his own passion, and was very urgent for a favourable answer.\n\nAs she thought less of his inebriety, she thought more of his inconstancy and presumption; and with fewer struggles for politeness, replied,\n\n“It is impossible for me to doubt any longer. You have made yourself too clear. Mr. Elton, my astonishment is much beyond any thing I can express. After such behaviour, as I have witnessed during the last month, to Miss Smith—such attentions as I have been in the daily habit of observing—to be addressing me in this manner—this is an unsteadiness of character, indeed, which I had not supposed possible! Believe me, sir, I am far, very far, from gratified in being the object of such professions.”\n\n“Good Heaven!” cried Mr. Elton, “what can be the meaning of this?—Miss Smith!—I never thought of Miss Smith in the whole course of my existence—never paid her any attentions, but as your friend: never cared whether she were dead or alive, but as your friend. If she has fancied otherwise, her own wishes have misled her, and I am very sorry—extremely sorry—But, Miss Smith, indeed!—Oh! Miss Woodhouse! who can think of Miss Smith, when Miss Woodhouse is near! No, upon my honour, there is no unsteadiness of character. I have thought only of you. I protest against having paid the smallest attention to any one else. Every thing that I have said or done, for many weeks past, has been with the sole view of marking my adoration of yourself. You cannot really, seriously, doubt it. No!—(in an accent meant to be insinuating)—I am sure you have seen and understood me.”\n\nIt would be impossible to say what Emma felt, on hearing this—which of all her unpleasant sensations was uppermost. She was too completely overpowered to be immediately able to reply: and two moments of silence being ample encouragement for Mr. Elton’s sanguine state of mind, he tried to take her hand again, as he joyously exclaimed—\n\n“Charming Miss Woodhouse! allow me to interpret this interesting silence. It confesses that you have long understood me.”\n\n“No, sir,” cried Emma, “it confesses no such thing. So far from having long understood you, I have been in a most complete error with respect to your views, till this moment. As to myself, I am very sorry that you should have been giving way to any feelings—Nothing could be farther from my wishes—your attachment to my friend Harriet—your pursuit of her, (pursuit, it appeared,) gave me great pleasure, and I have been very earnestly wishing you success: but had I supposed that she were not your attraction to Hartfield, I should certainly have thought you judged ill in making your visits so frequent. Am I to believe that you have never sought to recommend yourself particularly to Miss Smith?—that you have never thought seriously of her?”\n\n“Never, madam,” cried he, affronted in his turn: “never, I assure you. I think seriously of Miss Smith!—Miss Smith is a very good sort of girl; and I should be happy to see her respectably settled. I wish her extremely well: and, no doubt, there are men who might not object to—Every body has their level: but as for myself, I am not, I think, quite so much at a loss. I need not so totally despair of an equal alliance, as to be addressing myself to Miss Smith!—No, madam, my visits to Hartfield have been for yourself only; and the encouragement I received—”\n\n“Encouragement!—I give you encouragement!—Sir, you have been entirely mistaken in supposing it. I have seen you only as the admirer of my friend. In no other light could you have been more to me than a common acquaintance. I am exceedingly sorry: but it is well that the mistake ends where it does. Had the same behaviour continued, Miss Smith might have been led into a misconception of your views; not being aware, probably, any more than myself, of the very great inequality which you are so sensible of. But, as it is, the disappointment is single, and, I trust, will not be lasting. I have no thoughts of matrimony at present.”\n\nHe was too angry to say another word; her manner too decided to invite supplication; and in this state of swelling resentment, and mutually deep mortification, they had to continue together a few minutes longer, for the fears of Mr. Woodhouse had confined them to a foot-pace. If there had not been so much anger, there would have been desperate awkwardness; but their straightforward emotions left no room for the little zigzags of embarrassment. Without knowing when the carriage turned into Vicarage Lane, or when it stopped, they found themselves, all at once, at the door of his house; and he was out before another syllable passed.—Emma then felt it indispensable to wish him a good night. The compliment was just returned, coldly and proudly; and, under indescribable irritation of spirits, she was then conveyed to Hartfield.\n\nThere she was welcomed, with the utmost delight, by her father, who had been trembling for the dangers of a solitary drive from Vicarage Lane—turning a corner which he could never bear to think of—and in strange hands—a mere common coachman—no James; and there it seemed as if her return only were wanted to make every thing go well: for Mr. John Knightley, ashamed of his ill-humour, was now all kindness and attention; and so particularly solicitous for the comfort of her father, as to seem—if not quite ready to join him in a basin of gruel—perfectly sensible of its being exceedingly wholesome; and the day was concluding in peace and comfort to all their little party, except herself.—But her mind had never been in such perturbation; and it needed a very strong effort to appear attentive and cheerful till the usual hour of separating allowed her the relief of quiet reflection.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter XVI\n\nThe hair was curled, and the maid sent away, and Emma sat down to think and be miserable.—It was a wretched business indeed!—Such an overthrow of every thing she had been wishing for!—Such a development of every thing most unwelcome!—Such a blow for Harriet!—that was the worst of all. Every part of it brought pain and humiliation, of some sort or other; but, compared with the evil to Harriet, all was light; and she would gladly have submitted to feel yet more mistaken—more in error—more disgraced by mis-judgment, than she actually was, could the effects of her blunders have been confined to herself.\n\n“If I had not persuaded Harriet into liking the man, I could have borne any thing. He might have doubled his presumption to me—but poor Harriet!”\n\nHow she could have been so deceived!—He protested that he had never thought seriously of Harriet—never! She looked back as well as she could; but it was all confusion. She had taken up the idea, she supposed, and made every thing bend to it. His manners, however, must have been unmarked, wavering, dubious, or she could not have been so misled.\n\nThe picture!—How eager he had been about the picture!—and the charade!—and an hundred other circumstances;—how clearly they had seemed to point at Harriet. To be sure, the charade, with its “ready wit”—but then the “soft eyes”—in fact it suited neither; it was a jumble without taste or truth. Who could have seen through such thick-headed nonsense?\n\nCertainly she had often, especially of late, thought his manners to herself unnecessarily gallant; but it had passed as his way, as a mere error of judgment, of knowledge, of taste, as one proof among others that he had not always lived in the best society, that with all the gentleness of his address, true elegance was sometimes wanting; but, till this very day, she had never, for an instant, suspected it to mean any thing but grateful respect to her as Harriet’s friend.\n\nTo Mr. John Knightley was she indebted for her first idea on the subject, for the first start of its possibility. There was no denying that those brothers had penetration. She remembered what Mr. Knightley had once said to her about Mr. Elton, the caution he had given, the conviction he had professed that Mr. Elton would never marry indiscreetly; and blushed to think how much truer a knowledge of his character had been there shewn than any she had reached herself. It was dreadfully mortifying; but Mr. Elton was proving himself, in many respects, the very reverse of what she had meant and believed him; proud, assuming, conceited; very full of his own claims, and little concerned about the feelings of others.\n\nContrary to the usual course of things, Mr. Elton’s wanting to pay his addresses to her had sunk him in her opinion. His professions and his proposals did him no service. She thought nothing of his attachment, and was insulted by his hopes. He wanted to marry well, and having the arrogance to raise his eyes to her, pretended to be in love; but she was perfectly easy as to his not suffering any disappointment that need be cared for. There had been no real affection either in his language or manners. Sighs and fine words had been given in abundance; but she could hardly devise any set of expressions, or fancy any tone of voice, less allied with real love. She need not trouble herself to pity him. He only wanted to aggrandise and enrich himself; and if Miss Woodhouse of Hartfield, the heiress of thirty thousand pounds, were not quite so easily obtained as he had fancied, he would soon try for Miss Somebody else with twenty, or with ten.\n\nBut—that he should talk of encouragement, should consider her as aware of his views, accepting his attentions, meaning (in short), to marry him!—should suppose himself her equal in connexion or mind!—look down upon her friend, so well understanding the gradations of rank below him, and be so blind to what rose above, as to fancy himself shewing no presumption in addressing her!—It was most provoking.\n\nPerhaps it was not fair to expect him to feel how very much he was her inferior in talent, and all the elegancies of mind. The very want of such equality might prevent his perception of it; but he must know that in fortune and consequence she was greatly his superior. He must know that the Woodhouses had been settled for several generations at Hartfield, the younger branch of a very ancient family—and that the Eltons were nobody. The landed property of Hartfield certainly was inconsiderable, being but a sort of notch in the Donwell Abbey estate, to which all the rest of Highbury belonged; but their fortune, from other sources, was such as to make them scarcely secondary to Donwell Abbey itself, in every other kind of consequence; and the Woodhouses had long held a high place in the consideration of the neighbourhood which Mr. Elton had first entered not two years ago, to make his way as he could, without any alliances but in trade, or any thing to recommend him to notice but his situation and his civility.—But he had fancied her in love with him; that evidently must have been his dependence; and after raving a little about the seeming incongruity of gentle manners and a conceited head, Emma was obliged in common honesty to stop and admit that her own behaviour to him had been so complaisant and obliging, so full of courtesy and attention, as (supposing her real motive unperceived) might warrant a man of ordinary observation and delicacy, like Mr. Elton, in fancying himself a very decided favourite. If she had so misinterpreted his feelings, she had little right to wonder that he, with self-interest to blind him, should have mistaken hers.\n\nThe first error and the worst lay at her door. It was foolish, it was wrong, to take so active a part in bringing any two people together. It was adventuring too far, assuming too much, making light of what ought to be serious, a trick of what ought to be simple. She was quite concerned and ashamed, and resolved to do such things no more.\n\n“Here have I,” said she, “actually talked poor Harriet into being very much attached to this man. She might never have thought of him but for me; and certainly never would have thought of him with hope, if I had not assured her of his attachment, for she is as modest and humble as I used to think him. Oh! that I had been satisfied with persuading her not to accept young Martin. There I was quite right. That was well done of me; but there I should have stopped, and left the rest to time and chance. I was introducing her into good company, and giving her the opportunity of pleasing some one worth having; I ought not to have attempted more. But now, poor girl, her peace is cut up for some time. I have been but half a friend to her; and if she were not to feel this disappointment so very much, I am sure I have not an idea of any body else who would be at all desirable for her;—William Coxe—Oh! no, I could not endure William Coxe—a pert young lawyer.”\n\nShe stopt to blush and laugh at her own relapse, and then resumed a more serious, more dispiriting cogitation upon what had been, and might be, and must be. The distressing explanation she had to make to Harriet, and all that poor Harriet would be suffering, with the awkwardness of future meetings, the difficulties of continuing or discontinuing the acquaintance, of subduing feelings, concealing resentment, and avoiding eclat, were enough to occupy her in most unmirthful reflections some time longer, and she went to bed at last with nothing settled but the conviction of her having blundered most dreadfully.\n\nTo youth and natural cheerfulness like Emma’s, though under temporary gloom at night, the return of day will hardly fail to bring return of spirits. The youth and cheerfulness of morning are in happy analogy, and of powerful operation; and if the distress be not poignant enough to keep the eyes unclosed, they will be sure to open to sensations of softened pain and brighter hope.\n\nEmma got up on the morrow more disposed for comfort than she had gone to bed, more ready to see alleviations of the evil before her, and to depend on getting tolerably out of it.\n\nIt was a great consolation that Mr. Elton should not be really in love with her, or so particularly amiable as to make it shocking to disappoint him—that Harriet’s nature should not be of that superior sort in which the feelings are most acute and retentive—and that there could be no necessity for any body’s knowing what had passed except the three principals, and especially for her father’s being given a moment’s uneasiness about it.\n\nThese were very cheering thoughts; and the sight of a great deal of snow on the ground did her further service, for any thing was welcome that might justify their all three being quite asunder at present.\n\nThe weather was most favourable for her; though Christmas Day, she could not go to church. Mr. Woodhouse would have been miserable had his daughter attempted it, and she was therefore safe from either exciting or receiving unpleasant and most unsuitable ideas. The ground covered with snow, and the atmosphere in that unsettled state between frost and thaw, which is of all others the most unfriendly for exercise, every morning beginning in rain or snow, and every evening setting in to freeze, she was for many days a most honourable prisoner. No intercourse with Harriet possible but by note; no church for her on Sunday any more than on Christmas Day; and no need to find excuses for Mr. Elton’s absenting himself.\n\nIt was weather which might fairly confine every body at home; and though she hoped and believed him to be really taking comfort in some society or other, it was very pleasant to have her father so well satisfied with his being all alone in his own house, too wise to stir out; and to hear him say to Mr. Knightley, whom no weather could keep entirely from them,—\n\n“Ah! Mr. Knightley, why do not you stay at home like poor Mr. Elton?”\n\nThese days of confinement would have been, but for her private perplexities, remarkably comfortable, as such seclusion exactly suited her brother, whose feelings must always be of great importance to his companions; and he had, besides, so thoroughly cleared off his ill-humour at Randalls, that his amiableness never failed him during the rest of his stay at Hartfield. He was always agreeable and obliging, and speaking pleasantly of every body. But with all the hopes of cheerfulness, and all the present comfort of delay, there was still such an evil hanging over her in the hour of explanation with Harriet, as made it impossible for Emma to be ever perfectly at ease.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter XVII\n\nMr. and Mrs. John Knightley were not detained long at Hartfield. The weather soon improved enough for those to move who must move; and Mr. Woodhouse having, as usual, tried to persuade his daughter to stay behind with all her children, was obliged to see the whole party set off, and return to his lamentations over the destiny of poor Isabella;—which poor Isabella, passing her life with those she doated on, full of their merits, blind to their faults, and always innocently busy, might have been a model of right feminine happiness.\n\nThe evening of the very day on which they went brought a note from Mr. Elton to Mr. Woodhouse, a long, civil, ceremonious note, to say, with Mr. Elton’s best compliments, “that he was proposing to leave Highbury the following morning in his way to Bath; where, in compliance with the pressing entreaties of some friends, he had engaged to spend a few weeks, and very much regretted the impossibility he was under, from various circumstances of weather and business, of taking a personal leave of Mr. Woodhouse, of whose friendly civilities he should ever retain a grateful sense—and had Mr. Woodhouse any commands, should be happy to attend to them.”\n\nEmma was most agreeably surprized.—Mr. Elton’s absence just at this time was the very thing to be desired. She admired him for contriving it, though not able to give him much credit for the manner in which it was announced. Resentment could not have been more plainly spoken than in a civility to her father, from which she was so pointedly excluded. She had not even a share in his opening compliments.—Her name was not mentioned;—and there was so striking a change in all this, and such an ill-judged solemnity of leave-taking in his graceful acknowledgments, as she thought, at first, could not escape her father’s suspicion.\n\nIt did, however.—Her father was quite taken up with the surprize of so sudden a journey, and his fears that Mr. Elton might never get safely to the end of it, and saw nothing extraordinary in his language. It was a very useful note, for it supplied them with fresh matter for thought and conversation during the rest of their lonely evening. Mr. Woodhouse talked over his alarms, and Emma was in spirits to persuade them away with all her usual promptitude.\n\nShe now resolved to keep Harriet no longer in the dark. She had reason to believe her nearly recovered from her cold, and it was desirable that she should have as much time as possible for getting the better of her other complaint before the gentleman’s return. She went to Mrs. Goddard’s accordingly the very next day, to undergo the necessary penance of communication; and a severe one it was.—She had to destroy all the hopes which she had been so industriously feeding—to appear in the ungracious character of the one preferred—and acknowledge herself grossly mistaken and mis-judging in all her ideas on one subject, all her observations, all her convictions, all her prophecies for the last six weeks.\n\nThe confession completely renewed her first shame—and the sight of Harriet’s tears made her think that she should never be in charity with herself again.\n\nHarriet bore the intelligence very well—blaming nobody—and in every thing testifying such an ingenuousness of disposition and lowly opinion of herself, as must appear with particular advantage at that moment to her friend.\n\nEmma was in the humour to value simplicity and modesty to the utmost; and all that was amiable, all that ought to be attaching, seemed on Harriet’s side, not her own. Harriet did not consider herself as having any thing to complain of. The affection of such a man as Mr. Elton would have been too great a distinction.—She never could have deserved him—and nobody but so partial and kind a friend as Miss Woodhouse would have thought it possible.\n\nHer tears fell abundantly—but her grief was so truly artless, that no dignity could have made it more respectable in Emma’s eyes—and she listened to her and tried to console her with all her heart and understanding—really for the time convinced that Harriet was the superior creature of the two—and that to resemble her would be more for her own welfare and happiness than all that genius or intelligence could do.\n\nIt was rather too late in the day to set about being simple-minded and ignorant; but she left her with every previous resolution confirmed of being humble and discreet, and repressing imagination all the rest of her life. Her second duty now, inferior only to her father’s claims, was to promote Harriet’s comfort, and endeavour to prove her own affection in some better method than by match-making. She got her to Hartfield, and shewed her the most unvarying kindness, striving to occupy and amuse her, and by books and conversation, to drive Mr. Elton from her thoughts.\n\nTime, she knew, must be allowed for this being thoroughly done; and she could suppose herself but an indifferent judge of such matters in general, and very inadequate to sympathise in an attachment to Mr. Elton in particular; but it seemed to her reasonable that at Harriet’s age, and with the entire extinction of all hope, such a progress might be made towards a state of composure by the time of Mr. Elton’s return, as to allow them all to meet again in the common routine of acquaintance, without any danger of betraying sentiments or increasing them.\n\nHarriet did think him all perfection, and maintained the non-existence of any body equal to him in person or goodness—and did, in truth, prove herself more resolutely in love than Emma had foreseen; but yet it appeared to her so natural, so inevitable to strive against an inclination of that sort unrequited, that she could not comprehend its continuing very long in equal force.\n\nIf Mr. Elton, on his return, made his own indifference as evident and indubitable as she could not doubt he would anxiously do, she could not imagine Harriet’s persisting to place her happiness in the sight or the recollection of him.\n\nTheir being fixed, so absolutely fixed, in the same place, was bad for each, for all three. Not one of them had the power of removal, or of effecting any material change of society. They must encounter each other, and make the best of it.\n\nHarriet was farther unfortunate in the tone of her companions at Mrs. Goddard’s; Mr. Elton being the adoration of all the teachers and great girls in the school; and it must be at Hartfield only that she could have any chance of hearing him spoken of with cooling moderation or repellent truth. Where the wound had been given, there must the cure be found if anywhere; and Emma felt that, till she saw her in the way of cure, there could be no true peace for herself.\n\n# Volume I — Chapter XVIII\n\nMr. Frank Churchill did not come. When the time proposed drew near, Mrs. Weston’s fears were justified in the arrival of a letter of excuse. For the present, he could not be spared, to his “very great mortification and regret; but still he looked forward with the hope of coming to Randalls at no distant period.”\n\nMrs. Weston was exceedingly disappointed—much more disappointed, in fact, than her husband, though her dependence on seeing the young man had been so much more sober: but a sanguine temper, though for ever expecting more good than occurs, does not always pay for its hopes by any proportionate depression. It soon flies over the present failure, and begins to hope again. For half an hour Mr. Weston was surprized and sorry; but then he began to perceive that Frank’s coming two or three months later would be a much better plan; better time of year; better weather; and that he would be able, without any doubt, to stay considerably longer with them than if he had come sooner.\n\nThese feelings rapidly restored his comfort, while Mrs. Weston, of a more apprehensive disposition, foresaw nothing but a repetition of excuses and delays; and after all her concern for what her husband was to suffer, suffered a great deal more herself.\n\nEmma was not at this time in a state of spirits to care really about Mr. Frank Churchill’s not coming, except as a disappointment at Randalls. The acquaintance at present had no charm for her. She wanted, rather, to be quiet, and out of temptation; but still, as it was desirable that she should appear, in general, like her usual self, she took care to express as much interest in the circumstance, and enter as warmly into Mr. and Mrs. Weston’s disappointment, as might naturally belong to their friendship.\n\nShe was the first to announce it to Mr. Knightley; and exclaimed quite as much as was necessary, (or, being acting a part, perhaps rather more,) at the conduct of the Churchills, in keeping him away. She then proceeded to say a good deal more than she felt, of the advantage of such an addition to their confined society in Surry; the pleasure of looking at somebody new; the gala-day to Highbury entire, which the sight of him would have made; and ending with reflections on the Churchills again, found herself directly involved in a disagreement with Mr. Knightley; and, to her great amusement, perceived that she was taking the other side of the question from her real opinion, and making use of Mrs. Weston’s arguments against herself.\n\n“The Churchills are very likely in fault,” said Mr. Knightley, coolly; “but I dare say he might come if he would.”\n\n“I do not know why you should say so. He wishes exceedingly to come; but his uncle and aunt will not spare him.”\n\n“I cannot believe that he has not the power of coming, if he made a point of it. It is too unlikely, for me to believe it without proof.”\n\n“How odd you are! What has Mr. Frank Churchill done, to make you suppose him such an unnatural creature?”\n\n“I am not supposing him at all an unnatural creature, in suspecting that he may have learnt to be above his connexions, and to care very little for any thing but his own pleasure, from living with those who have always set him the example of it. It is a great deal more natural than one could wish, that a young man, brought up by those who are proud, luxurious, and selfish, should be proud, luxurious, and selfish too. If Frank Churchill had wanted to see his father, he would have contrived it between September and January. A man at his age—what is he?—three or four-and-twenty—cannot be without the means of doing as much as that. It is impossible.”\n\n“That’s easily said, and easily felt by you, who have always been your own master. You are the worst judge in the world, Mr. Knightley, of the difficulties of dependence. You do not know what it is to have tempers to manage.”\n\n“It is not to be conceived that a man of three or four-and-twenty should not have liberty of mind or limb to that amount. He cannot want money—he cannot want leisure. We know, on the contrary, that he has so much of both, that he is glad to get rid of them at the idlest haunts in the kingdom. We hear of him for ever at some watering-place or other. A little while ago, he was at Weymouth. This proves that he can leave the Churchills.”\n\n“Yes, sometimes he can.”\n\n“And those times are whenever he thinks it worth his while; whenever there is any temptation of pleasure.”\n\n“It is very unfair to judge of any body’s conduct, without an intimate knowledge of their situation. Nobody, who has not been in the interior of a family, can say what the difficulties of any individual of that family may be. We ought to be acquainted with Enscombe, and with Mrs. Churchill’s temper, before we pretend to decide upon what her nephew can do. He may, at times, be able to do a great deal more than he can at others.”\n\n“There is one thing, Emma, which a man can always do, if he chuses, and that is, his duty; not by manoeuvring and finessing, but by vigour and resolution. It is Frank Churchill’s duty to pay this attention to his father. He knows it to be so, by his promises and messages; but if he wished to do it, it might be done. A man who felt rightly would say at once, simply and resolutely, to Mrs. Churchill—‘Every sacrifice of mere pleasure you will always find me ready to make to your convenience; but I must go and see my father immediately. I know he would be hurt by my failing in such a mark of respect to him on the present occasion. I shall, therefore, set off to-morrow.’—If he would say so to her at once, in the tone of decision becoming a man, there would be no opposition made to his going.”\n\n“No,” said Emma, laughing; “but perhaps there might be some made to his coming back again. Such language for a young man entirely dependent, to use!—Nobody but you, Mr. Knightley, would imagine it possible. But you have not an idea of what is requisite in situations directly opposite to your own. Mr. Frank Churchill to be making such a speech as that to the uncle and aunt, who have brought him up, and are to provide for him!—Standing up in the middle of the room, I suppose, and speaking as loud as he could!—How can you imagine such conduct practicable?”\n\n“Depend upon it, Emma, a sensible man would find no difficulty in it. He would feel himself in the right; and the declaration—made, of course, as a man of sense would make it, in a proper manner—would do him more good, raise him higher, fix his interest stronger with the people he depended on, than all that a line of shifts and expedients can ever do. Respect would be added to affection. They would feel that they could trust him; that the nephew who had done rightly by his father, would do rightly by them; for they know, as well as he does, as well as all the world must know, that he ought to pay this visit to his father; and while meanly exerting their power to delay it, are in their hearts not thinking the better of him for submitting to their whims. Respect for right conduct is felt by every body. If he would act in this sort of manner, on principle, consistently, regularly, their little minds would bend to his.”\n\n“I rather doubt that. You are very fond of bending little minds; but where little minds belong to rich people in authority, I think they have a knack of swelling out, till they are quite as unmanageable as great ones. I can imagine, that if you, as you are, Mr. Knightley, were to be transported and placed all at once in Mr. Frank Churchill’s situation, you would be able to say and do just what you have been recommending for him; and it might have a very good effect. The Churchills might not have a word to say in return; but then, you would have no habits of early obedience and long observance to break through. To him who has, it might not be so easy to burst forth at once into perfect independence, and set all their claims on his gratitude and regard at nought. He may have as strong a sense of what would be right, as you can have, without being so equal, under particular circumstances, to act up to it.”\n\n“Then it would not be so strong a sense. If it failed to produce equal exertion, it could not be an equal conviction.”\n\n“Oh, the difference of situation and habit! I wish you would try to understand what an amiable young man may be likely to feel in directly opposing those, whom as child and boy he has been looking up to all his life.”\n\n“Our amiable young man is a very weak young man, if this be the first occasion of his carrying through a resolution to do right against the will of others. It ought to have been a habit with him by this time, of following his duty, instead of consulting expediency. I can allow for the fears of the child, but not of the man. As he became rational, he ought to have roused himself and shaken off all that was unworthy in their authority. He ought to have opposed the first attempt on their side to make him slight his father. Had he begun as he ought, there would have been no difficulty now.”\n\n“We shall never agree about him,” cried Emma; “but that is nothing extraordinary. I have not the least idea of his being a weak young man: I feel sure that he is not. Mr. Weston would not be blind to folly, though in his own son; but he is very likely to have a more yielding, complying, mild disposition than would suit your notions of man’s perfection. I dare say he has; and though it may cut him off from some advantages, it will secure him many others.”\n\n“Yes; all the advantages of sitting still when he ought to move, and of leading a life of mere idle pleasure, and fancying himself extremely expert in finding excuses for it. He can sit down and write a fine flourishing letter, full of professions and falsehoods, and persuade himself that he has hit upon the very best method in the world of preserving peace at home and preventing his father’s having any right to complain. His letters disgust me.”\n\n“Your feelings are singular. They seem to satisfy every body else.”\n\n“I suspect they do not satisfy Mrs. Weston. They hardly can satisfy a woman of her good sense and quick feelings: standing in a mother’s place, but without a mother’s affection to blind her. It is on her account that attention to Randalls is doubly due, and she must doubly feel the omission. Had she been a person of consequence herself, he would have come I dare say; and it would not have signified whether he did or no. Can you think your friend behindhand in these sort of considerations? Do you suppose she does not often say all this to herself? No, Emma, your amiable young man can be amiable only in French, not in English. He may be very ‘amiable,’ have very good manners, and be very agreeable; but he can have no English delicacy towards the feelings of other people: nothing really amiable about him.”\n\n“You seem determined to think ill of him.”\n\n“Me!—not at all,” replied Mr. Knightley, rather displeased; “I do not want to think ill of him. I should be as ready to acknowledge his merits as any other man; but I hear of none, except what are merely personal; that he is well-grown and good-looking, with smooth, plausible manners.”\n\n“Well, if he have nothing else to recommend him, he will be a treasure at Highbury. We do not often look upon fine young men, well-bred and agreeable. We must not be nice and ask for all the virtues into the bargain. Cannot you imagine, Mr. Knightley, what a sensation his coming will produce? There will be but one subject throughout the parishes of Donwell and Highbury; but one interest—one object of curiosity; it will be all Mr. Frank Churchill; we shall think and speak of nobody else.”\n\n“You will excuse my being so much over-powered. If I find him conversable, I shall be glad of his acquaintance; but if he is only a chattering coxcomb, he will not occupy much of my time or thoughts.”\n\n“My idea of him is, that he can adapt his conversation to the taste of every body, and has the power as well as the wish of being universally agreeable. To you, he will talk of farming; to me, of drawing or music; and so on to every body, having that general information on all subjects which will enable him to follow the lead, or take the lead, just as propriety may require, and to speak extremely well on each; that is my idea of him.”\n\n“And mine,” said Mr. Knightley warmly, “is, that if he turn out any thing like it, he will be the most insufferable fellow breathing! What! at three-and-twenty to be the king of his company—the great man—the practised politician, who is to read every body’s character, and make every body’s talents conduce to the display of his own superiority; to be dispensing his flatteries around, that he may make all appear like fools compared with himself! My dear Emma, your own good sense could not endure such a puppy when it came to the point.”\n\n“I will say no more about him,” cried Emma, “you turn every thing to evil. We are both prejudiced; you against, I for him; and we have no chance of agreeing till he is really here.”\n\n“Prejudiced! I am not prejudiced.”\n\n“But I am very much, and without being at all ashamed of it. My love for Mr. and Mrs. Weston gives me a decided prejudice in his favour.”\n\n“He is a person I never think of from one month’s end to another,” said Mr. Knightley, with a degree of vexation, which made Emma immediately talk of something else, though she could not comprehend why he should be angry.\n\nTo take a dislike to a young man, only because he appeared to be of a different disposition from himself, was unworthy the real liberality of mind which she was always used to acknowledge in him; for with all the high opinion of himself, which she had often laid to his charge, she had never before for a moment supposed it could make him unjust to the merit of another.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter I\n\nEmma and Harriet had been walking together one morning, and, in Emma’s opinion, had been talking enough of Mr. Elton for that day. She could not think that Harriet’s solace or her own sins required more; and she was therefore industriously getting rid of the subject as they returned;—but it burst out again when she thought she had succeeded, and after speaking some time of what the poor must suffer in winter, and receiving no other answer than a very plaintive—“Mr. Elton is so good to the poor!” she found something else must be done.\n\nThey were just approaching the house where lived Mrs. and Miss Bates. She determined to call upon them and seek safety in numbers. There was always sufficient reason for such an attention; Mrs. and Miss Bates loved to be called on, and she knew she was considered by the very few who presumed ever to see imperfection in her, as rather negligent in that respect, and as not contributing what she ought to the stock of their scanty comforts.\n\nShe had had many a hint from Mr. Knightley and some from her own heart, as to her deficiency—but none were equal to counteract the persuasion of its being very disagreeable,—a waste of time—tiresome women—and all the horror of being in danger of falling in with the second-rate and third-rate of Highbury, who were calling on them for ever, and therefore she seldom went near them. But now she made the sudden resolution of not passing their door without going in—observing, as she proposed it to Harriet, that, as well as she could calculate, they were just now quite safe from any letter from Jane Fairfax.\n\nThe house belonged to people in business. Mrs. and Miss Bates occupied the drawing-room floor; and there, in the very moderate-sized apartment, which was every thing to them, the visitors were most cordially and even gratefully welcomed; the quiet neat old lady, who with her knitting was seated in the warmest corner, wanting even to give up her place to Miss Woodhouse, and her more active, talking daughter, almost ready to overpower them with care and kindness, thanks for their visit, solicitude for their shoes, anxious inquiries after Mr. Woodhouse’s health, cheerful communications about her mother’s, and sweet-cake from the beaufet—“Mrs. Cole had just been there, just called in for ten minutes, and had been so good as to sit an hour with them, and she had taken a piece of cake and been so kind as to say she liked it very much; and, therefore, she hoped Miss Woodhouse and Miss Smith would do them the favour to eat a piece too.”\n\nThe mention of the Coles was sure to be followed by that of Mr. Elton. There was intimacy between them, and Mr. Cole had heard from Mr. Elton since his going away. Emma knew what was coming; they must have the letter over again, and settle how long he had been gone, and how much he was engaged in company, and what a favourite he was wherever he went, and how full the Master of the Ceremonies’ ball had been; and she went through it very well, with all the interest and all the commendation that could be requisite, and always putting forward to prevent Harriet’s being obliged to say a word.\n\nThis she had been prepared for when she entered the house; but meant, having once talked him handsomely over, to be no farther incommoded by any troublesome topic, and to wander at large amongst all the Mistresses and Misses of Highbury, and their card-parties. She had not been prepared to have Jane Fairfax succeed Mr. Elton; but he was actually hurried off by Miss Bates, she jumped away from him at last abruptly to the Coles, to usher in a letter from her niece.\n\n“Oh! yes—Mr. Elton, I understand—certainly as to dancing—Mrs. Cole was telling me that dancing at the rooms at Bath was—Mrs. Cole was so kind as to sit some time with us, talking of Jane; for as soon as she came in, she began inquiring after her, Jane is so very great a favourite there. Whenever she is with us, Mrs. Cole does not know how to shew her kindness enough; and I must say that Jane deserves it as much as any body can. And so she began inquiring after her directly, saying, ‘I know you cannot have heard from Jane lately, because it is not her time for writing;’ and when I immediately said, ‘But indeed we have, we had a letter this very morning,’ I do not know that I ever saw any body more surprized. ‘Have you, upon your honour?’ said she; ‘well, that is quite unexpected. Do let me hear what she says.’”\n\nEmma’s politeness was at hand directly, to say, with smiling interest—\n\n“Have you heard from Miss Fairfax so lately? I am extremely happy. I hope she is well?”\n\n“Thank you. You are so kind!” replied the happily deceived aunt, while eagerly hunting for the letter.—“Oh! here it is. I was sure it could not be far off; but I had put my huswife upon it, you see, without being aware, and so it was quite hid, but I had it in my hand so very lately that I was almost sure it must be on the table. I was reading it to Mrs. Cole, and since she went away, I was reading it again to my mother, for it is such a pleasure to her—a letter from Jane—that she can never hear it often enough; so I knew it could not be far off, and here it is, only just under my huswife—and since you are so kind as to wish to hear what she says;—but, first of all, I really must, in justice to Jane, apologise for her writing so short a letter—only two pages you see—hardly two—and in general she fills the whole paper and crosses half. My mother often wonders that I can make it out so well. She often says, when the letter is first opened, ‘Well, Hetty, now I think you will be put to it to make out all that checker-work’—don’t you, ma’am?—And then I tell her, I am sure she would contrive to make it out herself, if she had nobody to do it for her—every word of it—I am sure she would pore over it till she had made out every word. And, indeed, though my mother’s eyes are not so good as they were, she can see amazingly well still, thank God! with the help of spectacles. It is such a blessing! My mother’s are really very good indeed. Jane often says, when she is here, ‘I am sure, grandmama, you must have had very strong eyes to see as you do—and so much fine work as you have done too!—I only wish my eyes may last me as well.’”\n\nAll this spoken extremely fast obliged Miss Bates to stop for breath; and Emma said something very civil about the excellence of Miss Fairfax’s handwriting.\n\n“You are extremely kind,” replied Miss Bates, highly gratified; “you who are such a judge, and write so beautifully yourself. I am sure there is nobody’s praise that could give us so much pleasure as Miss Woodhouse’s. My mother does not hear; she is a little deaf you know. Ma’am,” addressing her, “do you hear what Miss Woodhouse is so obliging to say about Jane’s handwriting?”\n\nAnd Emma had the advantage of hearing her own silly compliment repeated twice over before the good old lady could comprehend it. She was pondering, in the meanwhile, upon the possibility, without seeming very rude, of making her escape from Jane Fairfax’s letter, and had almost resolved on hurrying away directly under some slight excuse, when Miss Bates turned to her again and seized her attention.\n\n“My mother’s deafness is very trifling you see—just nothing at all. By only raising my voice, and saying any thing two or three times over, she is sure to hear; but then she is used to my voice. But it is very remarkable that she should always hear Jane better than she does me. Jane speaks so distinct! However, she will not find her grandmama at all deafer than she was two years ago; which is saying a great deal at my mother’s time of life—and it really is full two years, you know, since she was here. We never were so long without seeing her before, and as I was telling Mrs. Cole, we shall hardly know how to make enough of her now.”\n\n“Are you expecting Miss Fairfax here soon?”\n\n“Oh yes; next week.”\n\n“Indeed!—that must be a very great pleasure.”\n\n“Thank you. You are very kind. Yes, next week. Every body is so surprized; and every body says the same obliging things. I am sure she will be as happy to see her friends at Highbury, as they can be to see her. Yes, Friday or Saturday; she cannot say which, because Colonel Campbell will be wanting the carriage himself one of those days. So very good of them to send her the whole way! But they always do, you know. Oh yes, Friday or Saturday next. That is what she writes about. That is the reason of her writing out of rule, as we call it; for, in the common course, we should not have heard from her before next Tuesday or Wednesday.”\n\n“Yes, so I imagined. I was afraid there could be little chance of my hearing any thing of Miss Fairfax to-day.”\n\n“So obliging of you! No, we should not have heard, if it had not been for this particular circumstance, of her being to come here so soon. My mother is so delighted!—for she is to be three months with us at least. Three months, she says so, positively, as I am going to have the pleasure of reading to you. The case is, you see, that the Campbells are going to Ireland. Mrs. Dixon has persuaded her father and mother to come over and see her directly. They had not intended to go over till the summer, but she is so impatient to see them again—for till she married, last October, she was never away from them so much as a week, which must make it very strange to be in different kingdoms, I was going to say, but however different countries, and so she wrote a very urgent letter to her mother—or her father, I declare I do not know which it was, but we shall see presently in Jane’s letter—wrote in Mr. Dixon’s name as well as her own, to press their coming over directly, and they would give them the meeting in Dublin, and take them back to their country seat, Baly-craig, a beautiful place, I fancy. Jane has heard a great deal of its beauty; from Mr. Dixon, I mean—I do not know that she ever heard about it from any body else; but it was very natural, you know, that he should like to speak of his own place while he was paying his addresses—and as Jane used to be very often walking out with them—for Colonel and Mrs. Campbell were very particular about their daughter’s not walking out often with only Mr. Dixon, for which I do not at all blame them; of course she heard every thing he might be telling Miss Campbell about his own home in Ireland; and I think she wrote us word that he had shewn them some drawings of the place, views that he had taken himself. He is a most amiable, charming young man, I believe. Jane was quite longing to go to Ireland, from his account of things.”\n\nAt this moment, an ingenious and animating suspicion entering Emma’s brain with regard to Jane Fairfax, this charming Mr. Dixon, and the not going to Ireland, she said, with the insidious design of farther discovery,\n\n“You must feel it very fortunate that Miss Fairfax should be allowed to come to you at such a time. Considering the very particular friendship between her and Mrs. Dixon, you could hardly have expected her to be excused from accompanying Colonel and Mrs. Campbell.”\n\n“Very true, very true, indeed. The very thing that we have always been rather afraid of; for we should not have liked to have her at such a distance from us, for months together—not able to come if any thing was to happen. But you see, every thing turns out for the best. They want her (Mr. and Mrs. Dixon) excessively to come over with Colonel and Mrs. Campbell; quite depend upon it; nothing can be more kind or pressing than their joint invitation, Jane says, as you will hear presently; Mr. Dixon does not seem in the least backward in any attention. He is a most charming young man. Ever since the service he rendered Jane at Weymouth, when they were out in that party on the water, and she, by the sudden whirling round of something or other among the sails, would have been dashed into the sea at once, and actually was all but gone, if he had not, with the greatest presence of mind, caught hold of her habit— (I can never think of it without trembling!)—But ever since we had the history of that day, I have been so fond of Mr. Dixon!”\n\n“But, in spite of all her friends’ urgency, and her own wish of seeing Ireland, Miss Fairfax prefers devoting the time to you and Mrs. Bates?”\n\n“Yes—entirely her own doing, entirely her own choice; and Colonel and Mrs. Campbell think she does quite right, just what they should recommend; and indeed they particularly wish her to try her native air, as she has not been quite so well as usual lately.”\n\n“I am concerned to hear of it. I think they judge wisely. But Mrs. Dixon must be very much disappointed. Mrs. Dixon, I understand, has no remarkable degree of personal beauty; is not, by any means, to be compared with Miss Fairfax.”\n\n“Oh! no. You are very obliging to say such things—but certainly not. There is no comparison between them. Miss Campbell always was absolutely plain—but extremely elegant and amiable.”\n\n“Yes, that of course.”\n\n“Jane caught a bad cold, poor thing! so long ago as the 7th of November, (as I am going to read to you,) and has never been well since. A long time, is not it, for a cold to hang upon her? She never mentioned it before, because she would not alarm us. Just like her! so considerate!—But however, she is so far from well, that her kind friends the Campbells think she had better come home, and try an air that always agrees with her; and they have no doubt that three or four months at Highbury will entirely cure her—and it is certainly a great deal better that she should come here, than go to Ireland, if she is unwell. Nobody could nurse her, as we should do.”\n\n“It appears to me the most desirable arrangement in the world.”\n\n“And so she is to come to us next Friday or Saturday, and the Campbells leave town in their way to Holyhead the Monday following—as you will find from Jane’s letter. So sudden!—You may guess, dear Miss Woodhouse, what a flurry it has thrown me in! If it was not for the drawback of her illness—but I am afraid we must expect to see her grown thin, and looking very poorly. I must tell you what an unlucky thing happened to me, as to that. I always make a point of reading Jane’s letters through to myself first, before I read them aloud to my mother, you know, for fear of there being any thing in them to distress her. Jane desired me to do it, so I always do: and so I began to-day with my usual caution; but no sooner did I come to the mention of her being unwell, than I burst out, quite frightened, with ‘Bless me! poor Jane is ill!’—which my mother, being on the watch, heard distinctly, and was sadly alarmed at. However, when I read on, I found it was not near so bad as I had fancied at first; and I make so light of it now to her, that she does not think much about it. But I cannot imagine how I could be so off my guard. If Jane does not get well soon, we will call in Mr. Perry. The expense shall not be thought of; and though he is so liberal, and so fond of Jane that I dare say he would not mean to charge any thing for attendance, we could not suffer it to be so, you know. He has a wife and family to maintain, and is not to be giving away his time. Well, now I have just given you a hint of what Jane writes about, we will turn to her letter, and I am sure she tells her own story a great deal better than I can tell it for her.”\n\n“I am afraid we must be running away,” said Emma, glancing at Harriet, and beginning to rise—“My father will be expecting us. I had no intention, I thought I had no power of staying more than five minutes, when I first entered the house. I merely called, because I would not pass the door without inquiring after Mrs. Bates; but I have been so pleasantly detained! Now, however, we must wish you and Mrs. Bates good morning.”\n\nAnd not all that could be urged to detain her succeeded. She regained the street—happy in this, that though much had been forced on her against her will, though she had in fact heard the whole substance of Jane Fairfax’s letter, she had been able to escape the letter itself.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter II\n\nJane Fairfax was an orphan, the only child of Mrs. Bates’s youngest daughter.\n\nThe marriage of Lieut. Fairfax of the ——regiment of infantry, and Miss Jane Bates, had had its day of fame and pleasure, hope and interest; but nothing now remained of it, save the melancholy remembrance of him dying in action abroad—of his widow sinking under consumption and grief soon afterwards—and this girl.\n\nBy birth she belonged to Highbury: and when at three years old, on losing her mother, she became the property, the charge, the consolation, the foundling of her grandmother and aunt, there had seemed every probability of her being permanently fixed there; of her being taught only what very limited means could command, and growing up with no advantages of connexion or improvement, to be engrafted on what nature had given her in a pleasing person, good understanding, and warm-hearted, well-meaning relations.\n\nBut the compassionate feelings of a friend of her father gave a change to her destiny. This was Colonel Campbell, who had very highly regarded Fairfax, as an excellent officer and most deserving young man; and farther, had been indebted to him for such attentions, during a severe camp-fever, as he believed had saved his life. These were claims which he did not learn to overlook, though some years passed away from the death of poor Fairfax, before his own return to England put any thing in his power. When he did return, he sought out the child and took notice of her. He was a married man, with only one living child, a girl, about Jane’s age: and Jane became their guest, paying them long visits and growing a favourite with all; and before she was nine years old, his daughter’s great fondness for her, and his own wish of being a real friend, united to produce an offer from Colonel Campbell of undertaking the whole charge of her education. It was accepted; and from that period Jane had belonged to Colonel Campbell’s family, and had lived with them entirely, only visiting her grandmother from time to time.\n\nThe plan was that she should be brought up for educating others; the very few hundred pounds which she inherited from her father making independence impossible. To provide for her otherwise was out of Colonel Campbell’s power; for though his income, by pay and appointments, was handsome, his fortune was moderate and must be all his daughter’s; but, by giving her an education, he hoped to be supplying the means of respectable subsistence hereafter.\n\nSuch was Jane Fairfax’s history. She had fallen into good hands, known nothing but kindness from the Campbells, and been given an excellent education. Living constantly with right-minded and well-informed people, her heart and understanding had received every advantage of discipline and culture; and Colonel Campbell’s residence being in London, every lighter talent had been done full justice to, by the attendance of first-rate masters. Her disposition and abilities were equally worthy of all that friendship could do; and at eighteen or nineteen she was, as far as such an early age can be qualified for the care of children, fully competent to the office of instruction herself; but she was too much beloved to be parted with. Neither father nor mother could promote, and the daughter could not endure it. The evil day was put off. It was easy to decide that she was still too young; and Jane remained with them, sharing, as another daughter, in all the rational pleasures of an elegant society, and a judicious mixture of home and amusement, with only the drawback of the future, the sobering suggestions of her own good understanding to remind her that all this might soon be over.\n\nThe affection of the whole family, the warm attachment of Miss Campbell in particular, was the more honourable to each party from the circumstance of Jane’s decided superiority both in beauty and acquirements. That nature had given it in feature could not be unseen by the young woman, nor could her higher powers of mind be unfelt by the parents. They continued together with unabated regard however, till the marriage of Miss Campbell, who by that chance, that luck which so often defies anticipation in matrimonial affairs, giving attraction to what is moderate rather than to what is superior, engaged the affections of Mr. Dixon, a young man, rich and agreeable, almost as soon as they were acquainted; and was eligibly and happily settled, while Jane Fairfax had yet her bread to earn.\n\nThis event had very lately taken place; too lately for any thing to be yet attempted by her less fortunate friend towards entering on her path of duty; though she had now reached the age which her own judgment had fixed on for beginning. She had long resolved that one-and-twenty should be the period. With the fortitude of a devoted novitiate, she had resolved at one-and-twenty to complete the sacrifice, and retire from all the pleasures of life, of rational intercourse, equal society, peace and hope, to penance and mortification for ever.\n\nThe good sense of Colonel and Mrs. Campbell could not oppose such a resolution, though their feelings did. As long as they lived, no exertions would be necessary, their home might be hers for ever; and for their own comfort they would have retained her wholly; but this would be selfishness:—what must be at last, had better be soon. Perhaps they began to feel it might have been kinder and wiser to have resisted the temptation of any delay, and spared her from a taste of such enjoyments of ease and leisure as must now be relinquished. Still, however, affection was glad to catch at any reasonable excuse for not hurrying on the wretched moment. She had never been quite well since the time of their daughter’s marriage; and till she should have completely recovered her usual strength, they must forbid her engaging in duties, which, so far from being compatible with a weakened frame and varying spirits, seemed, under the most favourable circumstances, to require something more than human perfection of body and mind to be discharged with tolerable comfort.\n\nWith regard to her not accompanying them to Ireland, her account to her aunt contained nothing but truth, though there might be some truths not told. It was her own choice to give the time of their absence to Highbury; to spend, perhaps, her last months of perfect liberty with those kind relations to whom she was so very dear: and the Campbells, whatever might be their motive or motives, whether single, or double, or treble, gave the arrangement their ready sanction, and said, that they depended more on a few months spent in her native air, for the recovery of her health, than on any thing else. Certain it was that she was to come; and that Highbury, instead of welcoming that perfect novelty which had been so long promised it—Mr. Frank Churchill—must put up for the present with Jane Fairfax, who could bring only the freshness of a two years’ absence.\n\nEmma was sorry;—to have to pay civilities to a person she did not like through three long months!—to be always doing more than she wished, and less than she ought! Why she did not like Jane Fairfax might be a difficult question to answer; Mr. Knightley had once told her it was because she saw in her the really accomplished young woman, which she wanted to be thought herself; and though the accusation had been eagerly refuted at the time, there were moments of self-examination in which her conscience could not quite acquit her. But “she could never get acquainted with her: she did not know how it was, but there was such coldness and reserve—such apparent indifference whether she pleased or not—and then, her aunt was such an eternal talker!—and she was made such a fuss with by every body!—and it had been always imagined that they were to be so intimate—because their ages were the same, every body had supposed they must be so fond of each other.” These were her reasons—she had no better.\n\nIt was a dislike so little just—every imputed fault was so magnified by fancy, that she never saw Jane Fairfax the first time after any considerable absence, without feeling that she had injured her; and now, when the due visit was paid, on her arrival, after a two years’ interval, she was particularly struck with the very appearance and manners, which for those two whole years she had been depreciating. Jane Fairfax was very elegant, remarkably elegant; and she had herself the highest value for elegance. Her height was pretty, just such as almost every body would think tall, and nobody could think very tall; her figure particularly graceful; her size a most becoming medium, between fat and thin, though a slight appearance of ill-health seemed to point out the likeliest evil of the two. Emma could not but feel all this; and then, her face—her features—there was more beauty in them altogether than she had remembered; it was not regular, but it was very pleasing beauty. Her eyes, a deep grey, with dark eye-lashes and eyebrows, had never been denied their praise; but the skin, which she had been used to cavil at, as wanting colour, had a clearness and delicacy which really needed no fuller bloom. It was a style of beauty, of which elegance was the reigning character, and as such, she must, in honour, by all her principles, admire it:—elegance, which, whether of person or of mind, she saw so little in Highbury. There, not to be vulgar, was distinction, and merit.\n\nIn short, she sat, during the first visit, looking at Jane Fairfax with twofold complacency; the sense of pleasure and the sense of rendering justice, and was determining that she would dislike her no longer. When she took in her history, indeed, her situation, as well as her beauty; when she considered what all this elegance was destined to, what she was going to sink from, how she was going to live, it seemed impossible to feel any thing but compassion and respect; especially, if to every well-known particular entitling her to interest, were added the highly probable circumstance of an attachment to Mr. Dixon, which she had so naturally started to herself. In that case, nothing could be more pitiable or more honourable than the sacrifices she had resolved on. Emma was very willing now to acquit her of having seduced Mr. Dixon’s affections from his wife, or of any thing mischievous which her imagination had suggested at first. If it were love, it might be simple, single, successless love on her side alone. She might have been unconsciously sucking in the sad poison, while a sharer of his conversation with her friend; and from the best, the purest of motives, might now be denying herself this visit to Ireland, and resolving to divide herself effectually from him and his connexions by soon beginning her career of laborious duty.\n\nUpon the whole, Emma left her with such softened, charitable feelings, as made her look around in walking home, and lament that Highbury afforded no young man worthy of giving her independence; nobody that she could wish to scheme about for her.\n\nThese were charming feelings—but not lasting. Before she had committed herself by any public profession of eternal friendship for Jane Fairfax, or done more towards a recantation of past prejudices and errors, than saying to Mr. Knightley, “She certainly is handsome; she is better than handsome!” Jane had spent an evening at Hartfield with her grandmother and aunt, and every thing was relapsing much into its usual state. Former provocations reappeared. The aunt was as tiresome as ever; more tiresome, because anxiety for her health was now added to admiration of her powers; and they had to listen to the description of exactly how little bread and butter she ate for breakfast, and how small a slice of mutton for dinner, as well as to see exhibitions of new caps and new workbags for her mother and herself; and Jane’s offences rose again. They had music; Emma was obliged to play; and the thanks and praise which necessarily followed appeared to her an affectation of candour, an air of greatness, meaning only to shew off in higher style her own very superior performance. She was, besides, which was the worst of all, so cold, so cautious! There was no getting at her real opinion. Wrapt up in a cloak of politeness, she seemed determined to hazard nothing. She was disgustingly, was suspiciously reserved.\n\nIf any thing could be more, where all was most, she was more reserved on the subject of Weymouth and the Dixons than any thing. She seemed bent on giving no real insight into Mr. Dixon’s character, or her own value for his company, or opinion of the suitableness of the match. It was all general approbation and smoothness; nothing delineated or distinguished. It did her no service however. Her caution was thrown away. Emma saw its artifice, and returned to her first surmises. There probably was something more to conceal than her own preference; Mr. Dixon, perhaps, had been very near changing one friend for the other, or been fixed only to Miss Campbell, for the sake of the future twelve thousand pounds.\n\nThe like reserve prevailed on other topics. She and Mr. Frank Churchill had been at Weymouth at the same time. It was known that they were a little acquainted; but not a syllable of real information could Emma procure as to what he truly was. “Was he handsome?”—“She believed he was reckoned a very fine young man.” “Was he agreeable?”—“He was generally thought so.” “Did he appear a sensible young man; a young man of information?”—“At a watering-place, or in a common London acquaintance, it was difficult to decide on such points. Manners were all that could be safely judged of, under a much longer knowledge than they had yet had of Mr. Churchill. She believed every body found his manners pleasing.” Emma could not forgive her.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter III\n\nEmma could not forgive her;—but as neither provocation nor resentment were discerned by Mr. Knightley, who had been of the party, and had seen only proper attention and pleasing behaviour on each side, he was expressing the next morning, being at Hartfield again on business with Mr. Woodhouse, his approbation of the whole; not so openly as he might have done had her father been out of the room, but speaking plain enough to be very intelligible to Emma. He had been used to think her unjust to Jane, and had now great pleasure in marking an improvement.\n\n“A very pleasant evening,” he began, as soon as Mr. Woodhouse had been talked into what was necessary, told that he understood, and the papers swept away;—“particularly pleasant. You and Miss Fairfax gave us some very good music. I do not know a more luxurious state, sir, than sitting at one’s ease to be entertained a whole evening by two such young women; sometimes with music and sometimes with conversation. I am sure Miss Fairfax must have found the evening pleasant, Emma. You left nothing undone. I was glad you made her play so much, for having no instrument at her grandmother’s, it must have been a real indulgence.”\n\n“I am happy you approved,” said Emma, smiling; “but I hope I am not often deficient in what is due to guests at Hartfield.”\n\n“No, my dear,” said her father instantly; “that I am sure you are not. There is nobody half so attentive and civil as you are. If any thing, you are too attentive. The muffin last night—if it had been handed round once, I think it would have been enough.”\n\n“No,” said Mr. Knightley, nearly at the same time; “you are not often deficient; not often deficient either in manner or comprehension. I think you understand me, therefore.”\n\nAn arch look expressed—“I understand you well enough;” but she said only, “Miss Fairfax is reserved.”\n\n“I always told you she was—a little; but you will soon overcome all that part of her reserve which ought to be overcome, all that has its foundation in diffidence. What arises from discretion must be honoured.”\n\n“You think her diffident. I do not see it.”\n\n“My dear Emma,” said he, moving from his chair into one close by her, “you are not going to tell me, I hope, that you had not a pleasant evening.”\n\n“Oh! no; I was pleased with my own perseverance in asking questions; and amused to think how little information I obtained.”\n\n“I am disappointed,” was his only answer.\n\n“I hope every body had a pleasant evening,” said Mr. Woodhouse, in his quiet way. “I had. Once, I felt the fire rather too much; but then I moved back my chair a little, a very little, and it did not disturb me. Miss Bates was very chatty and good-humoured, as she always is, though she speaks rather too quick. However, she is very agreeable, and Mrs. Bates too, in a different way. I like old friends; and Miss Jane Fairfax is a very pretty sort of young lady, a very pretty and a very well-behaved young lady indeed. She must have found the evening agreeable, Mr. Knightley, because she had Emma.”\n\n“True, sir; and Emma, because she had Miss Fairfax.”\n\nEmma saw his anxiety, and wishing to appease it, at least for the present, said, and with a sincerity which no one could question—\n\n“She is a sort of elegant creature that one cannot keep one’s eyes from. I am always watching her to admire; and I do pity her from my heart.”\n\nMr. Knightley looked as if he were more gratified than he cared to express; and before he could make any reply, Mr. Woodhouse, whose thoughts were on the Bates’s, said—\n\n“It is a great pity that their circumstances should be so confined! a great pity indeed! and I have often wished—but it is so little one can venture to do—small, trifling presents, of any thing uncommon—Now we have killed a porker, and Emma thinks of sending them a loin or a leg; it is very small and delicate—Hartfield pork is not like any other pork—but still it is pork—and, my dear Emma, unless one could be sure of their making it into steaks, nicely fried, as ours are fried, without the smallest grease, and not roast it, for no stomach can bear roast pork—I think we had better send the leg—do not you think so, my dear?”\n\n“My dear papa, I sent the whole hind-quarter. I knew you would wish it. There will be the leg to be salted, you know, which is so very nice, and the loin to be dressed directly in any manner they like.”\n\n“That’s right, my dear, very right. I had not thought of it before, but that is the best way. They must not over-salt the leg; and then, if it is not over-salted, and if it is very thoroughly boiled, just as Serle boils ours, and eaten very moderately of, with a boiled turnip, and a little carrot or parsnip, I do not consider it unwholesome.”\n\n“Emma,” said Mr. Knightley presently, “I have a piece of news for you. You like news—and I heard an article in my way hither that I think will interest you.”\n\n“News! Oh! yes, I always like news. What is it?—why do you smile so?—where did you hear it?—at Randalls?”\n\nHe had time only to say,\n\n“No, not at Randalls; I have not been near Randalls,” when the door was thrown open, and Miss Bates and Miss Fairfax walked into the room. Full of thanks, and full of news, Miss Bates knew not which to give quickest. Mr. Knightley soon saw that he had lost his moment, and that not another syllable of communication could rest with him.\n\n“Oh! my dear sir, how are you this morning? My dear Miss Woodhouse—I come quite over-powered. Such a beautiful hind-quarter of pork! You are too bountiful! Have you heard the news? Mr. Elton is going to be married.”\n\nEmma had not had time even to think of Mr. Elton, and she was so completely surprized that she could not avoid a little start, and a little blush, at the sound.\n\n“There is my news:—I thought it would interest you,” said Mr. Knightley, with a smile which implied a conviction of some part of what had passed between them.\n\n“But where could you hear it?” cried Miss Bates. “Where could you possibly hear it, Mr. Knightley? For it is not five minutes since I received Mrs. Cole’s note—no, it cannot be more than five—or at least ten—for I had got my bonnet and spencer on, just ready to come out—I was only gone down to speak to Patty again about the pork—Jane was standing in the passage—were not you, Jane?—for my mother was so afraid that we had not any salting-pan large enough. So I said I would go down and see, and Jane said, ‘Shall I go down instead? for I think you have a little cold, and Patty has been washing the kitchen.’—‘Oh! my dear,’ said I—well, and just then came the note. A Miss Hawkins—that’s all I know. A Miss Hawkins of Bath. But, Mr. Knightley, how could you possibly have heard it? for the very moment Mr. Cole told Mrs. Cole of it, she sat down and wrote to me. A Miss Hawkins—”\n\n“I was with Mr. Cole on business an hour and a half ago. He had just read Elton’s letter as I was shewn in, and handed it to me directly.”\n\n“Well! that is quite—I suppose there never was a piece of news more generally interesting. My dear sir, you really are too bountiful. My mother desires her very best compliments and regards, and a thousand thanks, and says you really quite oppress her.”\n\n“We consider our Hartfield pork,” replied Mr. Woodhouse—“indeed it certainly is, so very superior to all other pork, that Emma and I cannot have a greater pleasure than—”\n\n“Oh! my dear sir, as my mother says, our friends are only too good to us. If ever there were people who, without having great wealth themselves, had every thing they could wish for, I am sure it is us. We may well say that ‘our lot is cast in a goodly heritage.’ Well, Mr. Knightley, and so you actually saw the letter; well—”\n\n“It was short—merely to announce—but cheerful, exulting, of course.”— Here was a sly glance at Emma. “He had been so fortunate as to—I forget the precise words—one has no business to remember them. The information was, as you state, that he was going to be married to a Miss Hawkins. By his style, I should imagine it just settled.”\n\n“Mr. Elton going to be married!” said Emma, as soon as she could speak. “He will have every body’s wishes for his happiness.”\n\n“He is very young to settle,” was Mr. Woodhouse’s observation. “He had better not be in a hurry. He seemed to me very well off as he was. We were always glad to see him at Hartfield.”\n\n“A new neighbour for us all, Miss Woodhouse!” said Miss Bates, joyfully; “my mother is so pleased!—she says she cannot bear to have the poor old Vicarage without a mistress. This is great news, indeed. Jane, you have never seen Mr. Elton!—no wonder that you have such a curiosity to see him.”\n\nJane’s curiosity did not appear of that absorbing nature as wholly to occupy her.\n\n“No—I have never seen Mr. Elton,” she replied, starting on this appeal; “is he—is he a tall man?”\n\n“Who shall answer that question?” cried Emma. “My father would say ‘yes,’ Mr. Knightley ‘no;’ and Miss Bates and I that he is just the happy medium. When you have been here a little longer, Miss Fairfax, you will understand that Mr. Elton is the standard of perfection in Highbury, both in person and mind.”\n\n“Very true, Miss Woodhouse, so she will. He is the very best young man—But, my dear Jane, if you remember, I told you yesterday he was precisely the height of Mr. Perry. Miss Hawkins,—I dare say, an excellent young woman. His extreme attention to my mother—wanting her to sit in the vicarage pew, that she might hear the better, for my mother is a little deaf, you know—it is not much, but she does not hear quite quick. Jane says that Colonel Campbell is a little deaf. He fancied bathing might be good for it—the warm bath—but she says it did him no lasting benefit. Colonel Campbell, you know, is quite our angel. And Mr. Dixon seems a very charming young man, quite worthy of him. It is such a happiness when good people get together—and they always do. Now, here will be Mr. Elton and Miss Hawkins; and there are the Coles, such very good people; and the Perrys—I suppose there never was a happier or a better couple than Mr. and Mrs. Perry. I say, sir,” turning to Mr. Woodhouse, “I think there are few places with such society as Highbury. I always say, we are quite blessed in our neighbours.—My dear sir, if there is one thing my mother loves better than another, it is pork—a roast loin of pork—”\n\n“As to who, or what Miss Hawkins is, or how long he has been acquainted with her,” said Emma, “nothing I suppose can be known. One feels that it cannot be a very long acquaintance. He has been gone only four weeks.”\n\nNobody had any information to give; and, after a few more wonderings, Emma said,\n\n“You are silent, Miss Fairfax—but I hope you mean to take an interest in this news. You, who have been hearing and seeing so much of late on these subjects, who must have been so deep in the business on Miss Campbell’s account—we shall not excuse your being indifferent about Mr. Elton and Miss Hawkins.”\n\n“When I have seen Mr. Elton,” replied Jane, “I dare say I shall be interested—but I believe it requires that with me. And as it is some months since Miss Campbell married, the impression may be a little worn off.”\n\n“Yes, he has been gone just four weeks, as you observe, Miss Woodhouse,” said Miss Bates, “four weeks yesterday.—A Miss Hawkins!—Well, I had always rather fancied it would be some young lady hereabouts; not that I ever—Mrs. Cole once whispered to me—but I immediately said, ‘No, Mr. Elton is a most worthy young man—but’—In short, I do not think I am particularly quick at those sort of discoveries. I do not pretend to it. What is before me, I see. At the same time, nobody could wonder if Mr. Elton should have aspired—Miss Woodhouse lets me chatter on, so good-humouredly. She knows I would not offend for the world. How does Miss Smith do? She seems quite recovered now. Have you heard from Mrs. John Knightley lately? Oh! those dear little children. Jane, do you know I always fancy Mr. Dixon like Mr. John Knightley. I mean in person—tall, and with that sort of look—and not very talkative.”\n\n“Quite wrong, my dear aunt; there is no likeness at all.”\n\n“Very odd! but one never does form a just idea of any body beforehand. One takes up a notion, and runs away with it. Mr. Dixon, you say, is not, strictly speaking, handsome?”\n\n“Handsome! Oh! no—far from it—certainly plain. I told you he was plain.”\n\n“My dear, you said that Miss Campbell would not allow him to be plain, and that you yourself—”\n\n“Oh! as for me, my judgment is worth nothing. Where I have a regard, I always think a person well-looking. But I gave what I believed the general opinion, when I called him plain.”\n\n“Well, my dear Jane, I believe we must be running away. The weather does not look well, and grandmama will be uneasy. You are too obliging, my dear Miss Woodhouse; but we really must take leave. This has been a most agreeable piece of news indeed. I shall just go round by Mrs. Cole’s; but I shall not stop three minutes: and, Jane, you had better go home directly—I would not have you out in a shower!—We think she is the better for Highbury already. Thank you, we do indeed. I shall not attempt calling on Mrs. Goddard, for I really do not think she cares for any thing but boiled pork: when we dress the leg it will be another thing. Good morning to you, my dear sir. Oh! Mr. Knightley is coming too. Well, that is so very!—I am sure if Jane is tired, you will be so kind as to give her your arm.—Mr. Elton, and Miss Hawkins!—Good morning to you.”\n\nEmma, alone with her father, had half her attention wanted by him while he lamented that young people would be in such a hurry to marry—and to marry strangers too—and the other half she could give to her own view of the subject. It was to herself an amusing and a very welcome piece of news, as proving that Mr. Elton could not have suffered long; but she was sorry for Harriet: Harriet must feel it—and all that she could hope was, by giving the first information herself, to save her from hearing it abruptly from others. It was now about the time that she was likely to call. If she were to meet Miss Bates in her way!—and upon its beginning to rain, Emma was obliged to expect that the weather would be detaining her at Mrs. Goddard’s, and that the intelligence would undoubtedly rush upon her without preparation.\n\nThe shower was heavy, but short; and it had not been over five minutes, when in came Harriet, with just the heated, agitated look which hurrying thither with a full heart was likely to give; and the “Oh! Miss Woodhouse, what do you think has happened!” which instantly burst forth, had all the evidence of corresponding perturbation. As the blow was given, Emma felt that she could not now shew greater kindness than in listening; and Harriet, unchecked, ran eagerly through what she had to tell. “She had set out from Mrs. Goddard’s half an hour ago—she had been afraid it would rain—she had been afraid it would pour down every moment—but she thought she might get to Hartfield first—she had hurried on as fast as possible; but then, as she was passing by the house where a young woman was making up a gown for her, she thought she would just step in and see how it went on; and though she did not seem to stay half a moment there, soon after she came out it began to rain, and she did not know what to do; so she ran on directly, as fast as she could, and took shelter at Ford’s.”—Ford’s was the principal woollen-draper, linen-draper, and haberdasher’s shop united; the shop first in size and fashion in the place.—“And so, there she had set, without an idea of any thing in the world, full ten minutes, perhaps—when, all of a sudden, who should come in—to be sure it was so very odd!—but they always dealt at Ford’s—who should come in, but Elizabeth Martin and her brother!—Dear Miss Woodhouse! only think. I thought I should have fainted. I did not know what to do. I was sitting near the door—Elizabeth saw me directly; but he did not; he was busy with the umbrella. I am sure she saw me, but she looked away directly, and took no notice; and they both went to quite the farther end of the shop; and I kept sitting near the door!—Oh! dear; I was so miserable! I am sure I must have been as white as my gown. I could not go away you know, because of the rain; but I did so wish myself anywhere in the world but there.—Oh! dear, Miss Woodhouse—well, at last, I fancy, he looked round and saw me; for instead of going on with her buyings, they began whispering to one another. I am sure they were talking of me; and I could not help thinking that he was persuading her to speak to me—(do you think he was, Miss Woodhouse?)—for presently she came forward—came quite up to me, and asked me how I did, and seemed ready to shake hands, if I would. She did not do any of it in the same way that she used; I could see she was altered; but, however, she seemed to try to be very friendly, and we shook hands, and stood talking some time; but I know no more what I said—I was in such a tremble!—I remember she said she was sorry we never met now; which I thought almost too kind! Dear, Miss Woodhouse, I was absolutely miserable! By that time, it was beginning to hold up, and I was determined that nothing should stop me from getting away—and then—only think!—I found he was coming up towards me too—slowly you know, and as if he did not quite know what to do; and so he came and spoke, and I answered—and I stood for a minute, feeling dreadfully, you know, one can’t tell how; and then I took courage, and said it did not rain, and I must go; and so off I set; and I had not got three yards from the door, when he came after me, only to say, if I was going to Hartfield, he thought I had much better go round by Mr. Cole’s stables, for I should find the near way quite floated by this rain. Oh! dear, I thought it would have been the death of me! So I said, I was very much obliged to him: you know I could not do less; and then he went back to Elizabeth, and I came round by the stables—I believe I did—but I hardly knew where I was, or any thing about it. Oh! Miss Woodhouse, I would rather done any thing than have it happen: and yet, you know, there was a sort of satisfaction in seeing him behave so pleasantly and so kindly. And Elizabeth, too. Oh! Miss Woodhouse, do talk to me and make me comfortable again.”\n\nVery sincerely did Emma wish to do so; but it was not immediately in her power. She was obliged to stop and think. She was not thoroughly comfortable herself. The young man’s conduct, and his sister’s, seemed the result of real feeling, and she could not but pity them. As Harriet described it, there had been an interesting mixture of wounded affection and genuine delicacy in their behaviour. But she had believed them to be well-meaning, worthy people before; and what difference did this make in the evils of the connexion? It was folly to be disturbed by it. Of course, he must be sorry to lose her—they must be all sorry. Ambition, as well as love, had probably been mortified. They might all have hoped to rise by Harriet’s acquaintance: and besides, what was the value of Harriet’s description?—So easily pleased—so little discerning;—what signified her praise?\n\nShe exerted herself, and did try to make her comfortable, by considering all that had passed as a mere trifle, and quite unworthy of being dwelt on,\n\n“It might be distressing, for the moment,” said she; “but you seem to have behaved extremely well; and it is over—and may never—can never, as a first meeting, occur again, and therefore you need not think about it.”\n\nHarriet said, “very true,” and she “would not think about it;” but still she talked of it—still she could talk of nothing else; and Emma, at last, in order to put the Martins out of her head, was obliged to hurry on the news, which she had meant to give with so much tender caution; hardly knowing herself whether to rejoice or be angry, ashamed or only amused, at such a state of mind in poor Harriet—such a conclusion of Mr. Elton’s importance with her!\n\nMr. Elton’s rights, however, gradually revived. Though she did not feel the first intelligence as she might have done the day before, or an hour before, its interest soon increased; and before their first conversation was over, she had talked herself into all the sensations of curiosity, wonder and regret, pain and pleasure, as to this fortunate Miss Hawkins, which could conduce to place the Martins under proper subordination in her fancy.\n\nEmma learned to be rather glad that there had been such a meeting. It had been serviceable in deadening the first shock, without retaining any influence to alarm. As Harriet now lived, the Martins could not get at her, without seeking her, where hitherto they had wanted either the courage or the condescension to seek her; for since her refusal of the brother, the sisters never had been at Mrs. Goddard’s; and a twelvemonth might pass without their being thrown together again, with any necessity, or even any power of speech.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter IV\n\nHuman nature is so well disposed towards those who are in interesting situations, that a young person, who either marries or dies, is sure of being kindly spoken of.\n\nA week had not passed since Miss Hawkins’s name was first mentioned in Highbury, before she was, by some means or other, discovered to have every recommendation of person and mind; to be handsome, elegant, highly accomplished, and perfectly amiable: and when Mr. Elton himself arrived to triumph in his happy prospects, and circulate the fame of her merits, there was very little more for him to do, than to tell her Christian name, and say whose music she principally played.\n\nMr. Elton returned, a very happy man. He had gone away rejected and mortified—disappointed in a very sanguine hope, after a series of what appeared to him strong encouragement; and not only losing the right lady, but finding himself debased to the level of a very wrong one. He had gone away deeply offended—he came back engaged to another—and to another as superior, of course, to the first, as under such circumstances what is gained always is to what is lost. He came back gay and self-satisfied, eager and busy, caring nothing for Miss Woodhouse, and defying Miss Smith.\n\nThe charming Augusta Hawkins, in addition to all the usual advantages of perfect beauty and merit, was in possession of an independent fortune, of so many thousands as would always be called ten; a point of some dignity, as well as some convenience: the story told well; he had not thrown himself away—he had gained a woman of 10,000 l. or thereabouts; and he had gained her with such delightful rapidity—the first hour of introduction had been so very soon followed by distinguishing notice; the history which he had to give Mrs. Cole of the rise and progress of the affair was so glorious—the steps so quick, from the accidental rencontre, to the dinner at Mr. Green’s, and the party at Mrs. Brown’s—smiles and blushes rising in importance—with consciousness and agitation richly scattered—the lady had been so easily impressed—so sweetly disposed—had in short, to use a most intelligible phrase, been so very ready to have him, that vanity and prudence were equally contented.\n\nHe had caught both substance and shadow—both fortune and affection, and was just the happy man he ought to be; talking only of himself and his own concerns—expecting to be congratulated—ready to be laughed at—and, with cordial, fearless smiles, now addressing all the young ladies of the place, to whom, a few weeks ago, he would have been more cautiously gallant.\n\nThe wedding was no distant event, as the parties had only themselves to please, and nothing but the necessary preparations to wait for; and when he set out for Bath again, there was a general expectation, which a certain glance of Mrs. Cole’s did not seem to contradict, that when he next entered Highbury he would bring his bride.\n\nDuring his present short stay, Emma had barely seen him; but just enough to feel that the first meeting was over, and to give her the impression of his not being improved by the mixture of pique and pretension, now spread over his air. She was, in fact, beginning very much to wonder that she had ever thought him pleasing at all; and his sight was so inseparably connected with some very disagreeable feelings, that, except in a moral light, as a penance, a lesson, a source of profitable humiliation to her own mind, she would have been thankful to be assured of never seeing him again. She wished him very well; but he gave her pain, and his welfare twenty miles off would administer most satisfaction.\n\nThe pain of his continued residence in Highbury, however, must certainly be lessened by his marriage. Many vain solicitudes would be prevented—many awkwardnesses smoothed by it. A Mrs. Elton would be an excuse for any change of intercourse; former intimacy might sink without remark. It would be almost beginning their life of civility again.\n\nOf the lady, individually, Emma thought very little. She was good enough for Mr. Elton, no doubt; accomplished enough for Highbury—handsome enough—to look plain, probably, by Harriet’s side. As to connexion, there Emma was perfectly easy; persuaded, that after all his own vaunted claims and disdain of Harriet, he had done nothing. On that article, truth seemed attainable. What she was, must be uncertain; but who she was, might be found out; and setting aside the 10,000 l., it did not appear that she was at all Harriet’s superior. She brought no name, no blood, no alliance. Miss Hawkins was the youngest of the two daughters of a Bristol—merchant, of course, he must be called; but, as the whole of the profits of his mercantile life appeared so very moderate, it was not unfair to guess the dignity of his line of trade had been very moderate also. Part of every winter she had been used to spend in Bath; but Bristol was her home, the very heart of Bristol; for though the father and mother had died some years ago, an uncle remained—in the law line—nothing more distinctly honourable was hazarded of him, than that he was in the law line; and with him the daughter had lived. Emma guessed him to be the drudge of some attorney, and too stupid to rise. And all the grandeur of the connexion seemed dependent on the elder sister, who was very well married, to a gentleman in a great way, near Bristol, who kept two carriages! That was the wind-up of the history; that was the glory of Miss Hawkins.\n\nCould she but have given Harriet her feelings about it all! She had talked her into love; but, alas! she was not so easily to be talked out of it. The charm of an object to occupy the many vacancies of Harriet’s mind was not to be talked away. He might be superseded by another; he certainly would indeed; nothing could be clearer; even a Robert Martin would have been sufficient; but nothing else, she feared, would cure her. Harriet was one of those, who, having once begun, would be always in love. And now, poor girl! she was considerably worse from this reappearance of Mr. Elton. She was always having a glimpse of him somewhere or other. Emma saw him only once; but two or three times every day Harriet was sure just to meet with him, or just to miss him, just to hear his voice, or see his shoulder, just to have something occur to preserve him in her fancy, in all the favouring warmth of surprize and conjecture. She was, moreover, perpetually hearing about him; for, excepting when at Hartfield, she was always among those who saw no fault in Mr. Elton, and found nothing so interesting as the discussion of his concerns; and every report, therefore, every guess—all that had already occurred, all that might occur in the arrangement of his affairs, comprehending income, servants, and furniture, was continually in agitation around her. Her regard was receiving strength by invariable praise of him, and her regrets kept alive, and feelings irritated by ceaseless repetitions of Miss Hawkins’s happiness, and continual observation of, how much he seemed attached!—his air as he walked by the house—the very sitting of his hat, being all in proof of how much he was in love!\n\nHad it been allowable entertainment, had there been no pain to her friend, or reproach to herself, in the waverings of Harriet’s mind, Emma would have been amused by its variations. Sometimes Mr. Elton predominated, sometimes the Martins; and each was occasionally useful as a check to the other. Mr. Elton’s engagement had been the cure of the agitation of meeting Mr. Martin. The unhappiness produced by the knowledge of that engagement had been a little put aside by Elizabeth Martin’s calling at Mrs. Goddard’s a few days afterwards. Harriet had not been at home; but a note had been prepared and left for her, written in the very style to touch; a small mixture of reproach, with a great deal of kindness; and till Mr. Elton himself appeared, she had been much occupied by it, continually pondering over what could be done in return, and wishing to do more than she dared to confess. But Mr. Elton, in person, had driven away all such cares. While he staid, the Martins were forgotten; and on the very morning of his setting off for Bath again, Emma, to dissipate some of the distress it occasioned, judged it best for her to return Elizabeth Martin’s visit.\n\nHow that visit was to be acknowledged—what would be necessary—and what might be safest, had been a point of some doubtful consideration. Absolute neglect of the mother and sisters, when invited to come, would be ingratitude. It must not be: and yet the danger of a renewal of the acquaintance—!\n\nAfter much thinking, she could determine on nothing better, than Harriet’s returning the visit; but in a way that, if they had understanding, should convince them that it was to be only a formal acquaintance. She meant to take her in the carriage, leave her at the Abbey Mill, while she drove a little farther, and call for her again so soon, as to allow no time for insidious applications or dangerous recurrences to the past, and give the most decided proof of what degree of intimacy was chosen for the future.\n\nShe could think of nothing better: and though there was something in it which her own heart could not approve—something of ingratitude, merely glossed over—it must be done, or what would become of Harriet?\n\n# Volume II — Chapter V\n\nSmall heart had Harriet for visiting. Only half an hour before her friend called for her at Mrs. Goddard’s, her evil stars had led her to the very spot where, at that moment, a trunk, directed to The Rev. Philip Elton, White-Hart, Bath, was to be seen under the operation of being lifted into the butcher’s cart, which was to convey it to where the coaches past; and every thing in this world, excepting that trunk and the direction, was consequently a blank.\n\nShe went, however; and when they reached the farm, and she was to be put down, at the end of the broad, neat gravel walk, which led between espalier apple-trees to the front door, the sight of every thing which had given her so much pleasure the autumn before, was beginning to revive a little local agitation; and when they parted, Emma observed her to be looking around with a sort of fearful curiosity, which determined her not to allow the visit to exceed the proposed quarter of an hour. She went on herself, to give that portion of time to an old servant who was married, and settled in Donwell.\n\nThe quarter of an hour brought her punctually to the white gate again; and Miss Smith receiving her summons, was with her without delay, and unattended by any alarming young man. She came solitarily down the gravel walk—a Miss Martin just appearing at the door, and parting with her seemingly with ceremonious civility.\n\nHarriet could not very soon give an intelligible account. She was feeling too much; but at last Emma collected from her enough to understand the sort of meeting, and the sort of pain it was creating. She had seen only Mrs. Martin and the two girls. They had received her doubtingly, if not coolly; and nothing beyond the merest commonplace had been talked almost all the time—till just at last, when Mrs. Martin’s saying, all of a sudden, that she thought Miss Smith was grown, had brought on a more interesting subject, and a warmer manner. In that very room she had been measured last September, with her two friends. There were the pencilled marks and memorandums on the wainscot by the window. He had done it. They all seemed to remember the day, the hour, the party, the occasion—to feel the same consciousness, the same regrets—to be ready to return to the same good understanding; and they were just growing again like themselves, (Harriet, as Emma must suspect, as ready as the best of them to be cordial and happy,) when the carriage reappeared, and all was over. The style of the visit, and the shortness of it, were then felt to be decisive. Fourteen minutes to be given to those with whom she had thankfully passed six weeks not six months ago!—Emma could not but picture it all, and feel how justly they might resent, how naturally Harriet must suffer. It was a bad business. She would have given a great deal, or endured a great deal, to have had the Martins in a higher rank of life. They were so deserving, that a little higher should have been enough: but as it was, how could she have done otherwise?—Impossible!—She could not repent. They must be separated; but there was a great deal of pain in the process—so much to herself at this time, that she soon felt the necessity of a little consolation, and resolved on going home by way of Randalls to procure it. Her mind was quite sick of Mr. Elton and the Martins. The refreshment of Randalls was absolutely necessary.\n\nIt was a good scheme; but on driving to the door they heard that neither “master nor mistress was at home;” they had both been out some time; the man believed they were gone to Hartfield.\n\n“This is too bad,” cried Emma, as they turned away. “And now we shall just miss them; too provoking!—I do not know when I have been so disappointed.” And she leaned back in the corner, to indulge her murmurs, or to reason them away; probably a little of both—such being the commonest process of a not ill-disposed mind. Presently the carriage stopt; she looked up; it was stopt by Mr. and Mrs. Weston, who were standing to speak to her. There was instant pleasure in the sight of them, and still greater pleasure was conveyed in sound—for Mr. Weston immediately accosted her with,\n\n“How d’ye do?—how d’ye do?—We have been sitting with your father—glad to see him so well. Frank comes to-morrow—I had a letter this morning—we see him to-morrow by dinner-time to a certainty—he is at Oxford to-day, and he comes for a whole fortnight; I knew it would be so. If he had come at Christmas he could not have staid three days; I was always glad he did not come at Christmas; now we are going to have just the right weather for him, fine, dry, settled weather. We shall enjoy him completely; every thing has turned out exactly as we could wish.”\n\nThere was no resisting such news, no possibility of avoiding the influence of such a happy face as Mr. Weston’s, confirmed as it all was by the words and the countenance of his wife, fewer and quieter, but not less to the purpose. To know that she thought his coming certain was enough to make Emma consider it so, and sincerely did she rejoice in their joy. It was a most delightful reanimation of exhausted spirits. The worn-out past was sunk in the freshness of what was coming; and in the rapidity of half a moment’s thought, she hoped Mr. Elton would now be talked of no more.\n\nMr. Weston gave her the history of the engagements at Enscombe, which allowed his son to answer for having an entire fortnight at his command, as well as the route and the method of his journey; and she listened, and smiled, and congratulated.\n\n“I shall soon bring him over to Hartfield,” said he, at the conclusion.\n\nEmma could imagine she saw a touch of the arm at this speech, from his wife.\n\n“We had better move on, Mr. Weston,” said she, “we are detaining the girls.”\n\n“Well, well, I am ready;”—and turning again to Emma, “but you must not be expecting such a very fine young man; you have only had my account you know; I dare say he is really nothing extraordinary:”—though his own sparkling eyes at the moment were speaking a very different conviction.\n\nEmma could look perfectly unconscious and innocent, and answer in a manner that appropriated nothing.\n\n“Think of me to-morrow, my dear Emma, about four o’clock,” was Mrs. Weston’s parting injunction; spoken with some anxiety, and meant only for her.\n\n“Four o’clock!—depend upon it he will be here by three,” was Mr. Weston’s quick amendment; and so ended a most satisfactory meeting. Emma’s spirits were mounted quite up to happiness; every thing wore a different air; James and his horses seemed not half so sluggish as before. When she looked at the hedges, she thought the elder at least must soon be coming out; and when she turned round to Harriet, she saw something like a look of spring, a tender smile even there.\n\n“Will Mr. Frank Churchill pass through Bath as well as Oxford?”—was a question, however, which did not augur much.\n\nBut neither geography nor tranquillity could come all at once, and Emma was now in a humour to resolve that they should both come in time.\n\nThe morning of the interesting day arrived, and Mrs. Weston’s faithful pupil did not forget either at ten, or eleven, or twelve o’clock, that she was to think of her at four.\n\n“My dear, dear anxious friend,”—said she, in mental soliloquy, while walking downstairs from her own room, “always overcareful for every body’s comfort but your own; I see you now in all your little fidgets, going again and again into his room, to be sure that all is right.” The clock struck twelve as she passed through the hall. “’Tis twelve; I shall not forget to think of you four hours hence; and by this time to-morrow, perhaps, or a little later, I may be thinking of the possibility of their all calling here. I am sure they will bring him soon.”\n\nShe opened the parlour door, and saw two gentlemen sitting with her father—Mr. Weston and his son. They had been arrived only a few minutes, and Mr. Weston had scarcely finished his explanation of Frank’s being a day before his time, and her father was yet in the midst of his very civil welcome and congratulations, when she appeared, to have her share of surprize, introduction, and pleasure.\n\nThe Frank Churchill so long talked of, so high in interest, was actually before her—he was presented to her, and she did not think too much had been said in his praise; he was a very good looking young man; height, air, address, all were unexceptionable, and his countenance had a great deal of the spirit and liveliness of his father’s; he looked quick and sensible. She felt immediately that she should like him; and there was a well-bred ease of manner, and a readiness to talk, which convinced her that he came intending to be acquainted with her, and that acquainted they soon must be.\n\nHe had reached Randalls the evening before. She was pleased with the eagerness to arrive which had made him alter his plan, and travel earlier, later, and quicker, that he might gain half a day.\n\n“I told you yesterday,” cried Mr. Weston with exultation, “I told you all that he would be here before the time named. I remembered what I used to do myself. One cannot creep upon a journey; one cannot help getting on faster than one has planned; and the pleasure of coming in upon one’s friends before the look-out begins, is worth a great deal more than any little exertion it needs.”\n\n“It is a great pleasure where one can indulge in it,” said the young man, “though there are not many houses that I should presume on so far; but in coming home I felt I might do any thing.”\n\nThe word home made his father look on him with fresh complacency. Emma was directly sure that he knew how to make himself agreeable; the conviction was strengthened by what followed. He was very much pleased with Randalls, thought it a most admirably arranged house, would hardly allow it even to be very small, admired the situation, the walk to Highbury, Highbury itself, Hartfield still more, and professed himself to have always felt the sort of interest in the country which none but one’s own country gives, and the greatest curiosity to visit it. That he should never have been able to indulge so amiable a feeling before, passed suspiciously through Emma’s brain; but still, if it were a falsehood, it was a pleasant one, and pleasantly handled. His manner had no air of study or exaggeration. He did really look and speak as if in a state of no common enjoyment.\n\nTheir subjects in general were such as belong to an opening acquaintance. On his side were the inquiries,—“Was she a horsewoman?—Pleasant rides?—Pleasant walks?—Had they a large neighbourhood?—Highbury, perhaps, afforded society enough?—There were several very pretty houses in and about it.—Balls—had they balls?—Was it a musical society?”\n\nBut when satisfied on all these points, and their acquaintance proportionably advanced, he contrived to find an opportunity, while their two fathers were engaged with each other, of introducing his mother-in-law, and speaking of her with so much handsome praise, so much warm admiration, so much gratitude for the happiness she secured to his father, and her very kind reception of himself, as was an additional proof of his knowing how to please—and of his certainly thinking it worth while to try to please her. He did not advance a word of praise beyond what she knew to be thoroughly deserved by Mrs. Weston; but, undoubtedly he could know very little of the matter. He understood what would be welcome; he could be sure of little else. “His father’s marriage,” he said, “had been the wisest measure, every friend must rejoice in it; and the family from whom he had received such a blessing must be ever considered as having conferred the highest obligation on him.”\n\nHe got as near as he could to thanking her for Miss Taylor’s merits, without seeming quite to forget that in the common course of things it was to be rather supposed that Miss Taylor had formed Miss Woodhouse’s character, than Miss Woodhouse Miss Taylor’s. And at last, as if resolved to qualify his opinion completely for travelling round to its object, he wound it all up with astonishment at the youth and beauty of her person.\n\n“Elegant, agreeable manners, I was prepared for,” said he; “but I confess that, considering every thing, I had not expected more than a very tolerably well-looking woman of a certain age; I did not know that I was to find a pretty young woman in Mrs. Weston.”\n\n“You cannot see too much perfection in Mrs. Weston for my feelings,” said Emma; “were you to guess her to be eighteen, I should listen with pleasure; but she would be ready to quarrel with you for using such words. Don’t let her imagine that you have spoken of her as a pretty young woman.”\n\n“I hope I should know better,” he replied; “no, depend upon it, (with a gallant bow,) that in addressing Mrs. Weston I should understand whom I might praise without any danger of being thought extravagant in my terms.”\n\nEmma wondered whether the same suspicion of what might be expected from their knowing each other, which had taken strong possession of her mind, had ever crossed his; and whether his compliments were to be considered as marks of acquiescence, or proofs of defiance. She must see more of him to understand his ways; at present she only felt they were agreeable.\n\nShe had no doubt of what Mr. Weston was often thinking about. His quick eye she detected again and again glancing towards them with a happy expression; and even, when he might have determined not to look, she was confident that he was often listening.\n\nHer own father’s perfect exemption from any thought of the kind, the entire deficiency in him of all such sort of penetration or suspicion, was a most comfortable circumstance. Happily he was not farther from approving matrimony than from foreseeing it.—Though always objecting to every marriage that was arranged, he never suffered beforehand from the apprehension of any; it seemed as if he could not think so ill of any two persons’ understanding as to suppose they meant to marry till it were proved against them. She blessed the favouring blindness. He could now, without the drawback of a single unpleasant surmise, without a glance forward at any possible treachery in his guest, give way to all his natural kind-hearted civility in solicitous inquiries after Mr. Frank Churchill’s accommodation on his journey, through the sad evils of sleeping two nights on the road, and express very genuine unmixed anxiety to know that he had certainly escaped catching cold—which, however, he could not allow him to feel quite assured of himself till after another night.\n\nA reasonable visit paid, Mr. Weston began to move.—“He must be going. He had business at the Crown about his hay, and a great many errands for Mrs. Weston at Ford’s, but he need not hurry any body else.” His son, too well bred to hear the hint, rose immediately also, saying,\n\n“As you are going farther on business, sir, I will take the opportunity of paying a visit, which must be paid some day or other, and therefore may as well be paid now. I have the honour of being acquainted with a neighbour of yours, (turning to Emma,) a lady residing in or near Highbury; a family of the name of Fairfax. I shall have no difficulty, I suppose, in finding the house; though Fairfax, I believe, is not the proper name—I should rather say Barnes, or Bates. Do you know any family of that name?”\n\n“To be sure we do,” cried his father; “Mrs. Bates—we passed her house—I saw Miss Bates at the window. True, true, you are acquainted with Miss Fairfax; I remember you knew her at Weymouth, and a fine girl she is. Call upon her, by all means.”\n\n“There is no necessity for my calling this morning,” said the young man; “another day would do as well; but there was that degree of acquaintance at Weymouth which—”\n\n“Oh! go to-day, go to-day. Do not defer it. What is right to be done cannot be done too soon. And, besides, I must give you a hint, Frank; any want of attention to her here should be carefully avoided. You saw her with the Campbells, when she was the equal of every body she mixed with, but here she is with a poor old grandmother, who has barely enough to live on. If you do not call early it will be a slight.”\n\nThe son looked convinced.\n\n“I have heard her speak of the acquaintance,” said Emma; “she is a very elegant young woman.”\n\nHe agreed to it, but with so quiet a “Yes,” as inclined her almost to doubt his real concurrence; and yet there must be a very distinct sort of elegance for the fashionable world, if Jane Fairfax could be thought only ordinarily gifted with it.\n\n“If you were never particularly struck by her manners before,” said she, “I think you will to-day. You will see her to advantage; see her and hear her—no, I am afraid you will not hear her at all, for she has an aunt who never holds her tongue.”\n\n“You are acquainted with Miss Jane Fairfax, sir, are you?” said Mr. Woodhouse, always the last to make his way in conversation; “then give me leave to assure you that you will find her a very agreeable young lady. She is staying here on a visit to her grandmama and aunt, very worthy people; I have known them all my life. They will be extremely glad to see you, I am sure; and one of my servants shall go with you to shew you the way.”\n\n“My dear sir, upon no account in the world; my father can direct me.”\n\n“But your father is not going so far; he is only going to the Crown, quite on the other side of the street, and there are a great many houses; you might be very much at a loss, and it is a very dirty walk, unless you keep on the footpath; but my coachman can tell you where you had best cross the street.”\n\nMr. Frank Churchill still declined it, looking as serious as he could, and his father gave his hearty support by calling out, “My good friend, this is quite unnecessary; Frank knows a puddle of water when he sees it, and as to Mrs. Bates’s, he may get there from the Crown in a hop, step, and jump.”\n\nThey were permitted to go alone; and with a cordial nod from one, and a graceful bow from the other, the two gentlemen took leave. Emma remained very well pleased with this beginning of the acquaintance, and could now engage to think of them all at Randalls any hour of the day, with full confidence in their comfort.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter VI\n\nThe next morning brought Mr. Frank Churchill again. He came with Mrs. Weston, to whom and to Highbury he seemed to take very cordially. He had been sitting with her, it appeared, most companionably at home, till her usual hour of exercise; and on being desired to chuse their walk, immediately fixed on Highbury.—“He did not doubt there being very pleasant walks in every direction, but if left to him, he should always chuse the same. Highbury, that airy, cheerful, happy-looking Highbury, would be his constant attraction.”—Highbury, with Mrs. Weston, stood for Hartfield; and she trusted to its bearing the same construction with him. They walked thither directly.\n\nEmma had hardly expected them: for Mr. Weston, who had called in for half a minute, in order to hear that his son was very handsome, knew nothing of their plans; and it was an agreeable surprize to her, therefore, to perceive them walking up to the house together, arm in arm. She was wanting to see him again, and especially to see him in company with Mrs. Weston, upon his behaviour to whom her opinion of him was to depend. If he were deficient there, nothing should make amends for it. But on seeing them together, she became perfectly satisfied. It was not merely in fine words or hyperbolical compliment that he paid his duty; nothing could be more proper or pleasing than his whole manner to her—nothing could more agreeably denote his wish of considering her as a friend and securing her affection. And there was time enough for Emma to form a reasonable judgment, as their visit included all the rest of the morning. They were all three walking about together for an hour or two—first round the shrubberies of Hartfield, and afterwards in Highbury. He was delighted with every thing; admired Hartfield sufficiently for Mr. Woodhouse’s ear; and when their going farther was resolved on, confessed his wish to be made acquainted with the whole village, and found matter of commendation and interest much oftener than Emma could have supposed.\n\nSome of the objects of his curiosity spoke very amiable feelings. He begged to be shewn the house which his father had lived in so long, and which had been the home of his father’s father; and on recollecting that an old woman who had nursed him was still living, walked in quest of her cottage from one end of the street to the other; and though in some points of pursuit or observation there was no positive merit, they shewed, altogether, a good-will towards Highbury in general, which must be very like a merit to those he was with.\n\nEmma watched and decided, that with such feelings as were now shewn, it could not be fairly supposed that he had been ever voluntarily absenting himself; that he had not been acting a part, or making a parade of insincere professions; and that Mr. Knightley certainly had not done him justice.\n\nTheir first pause was at the Crown Inn, an inconsiderable house, though the principal one of the sort, where a couple of pair of post-horses were kept, more for the convenience of the neighbourhood than from any run on the road; and his companions had not expected to be detained by any interest excited there; but in passing it they gave the history of the large room visibly added; it had been built many years ago for a ball-room, and while the neighbourhood had been in a particularly populous, dancing state, had been occasionally used as such;—but such brilliant days had long passed away, and now the highest purpose for which it was ever wanted was to accommodate a whist club established among the gentlemen and half-gentlemen of the place. He was immediately interested. Its character as a ball-room caught him; and instead of passing on, he stopt for several minutes at the two superior sashed windows which were open, to look in and contemplate its capabilities, and lament that its original purpose should have ceased. He saw no fault in the room, he would acknowledge none which they suggested. No, it was long enough, broad enough, handsome enough. It would hold the very number for comfort. They ought to have balls there at least every fortnight through the winter. Why had not Miss Woodhouse revived the former good old days of the room?—She who could do any thing in Highbury! The want of proper families in the place, and the conviction that none beyond the place and its immediate environs could be tempted to attend, were mentioned; but he was not satisfied. He could not be persuaded that so many good-looking houses as he saw around him, could not furnish numbers enough for such a meeting; and even when particulars were given and families described, he was still unwilling to admit that the inconvenience of such a mixture would be any thing, or that there would be the smallest difficulty in every body’s returning into their proper place the next morning. He argued like a young man very much bent on dancing; and Emma was rather surprized to see the constitution of the Weston prevail so decidedly against the habits of the Churchills. He seemed to have all the life and spirit, cheerful feelings, and social inclinations of his father, and nothing of the pride or reserve of Enscombe. Of pride, indeed, there was, perhaps, scarcely enough; his indifference to a confusion of rank, bordered too much on inelegance of mind. He could be no judge, however, of the evil he was holding cheap. It was but an effusion of lively spirits.\n\nAt last he was persuaded to move on from the front of the Crown; and being now almost facing the house where the Bateses lodged, Emma recollected his intended visit the day before, and asked him if he had paid it.\n\n“Yes, oh! yes”—he replied; “I was just going to mention it. A very successful visit:—I saw all the three ladies; and felt very much obliged to you for your preparatory hint. If the talking aunt had taken me quite by surprize, it must have been the death of me. As it was, I was only betrayed into paying a most unreasonable visit. Ten minutes would have been all that was necessary, perhaps all that was proper; and I had told my father I should certainly be at home before him—but there was no getting away, no pause; and, to my utter astonishment, I found, when he (finding me nowhere else) joined me there at last, that I had been actually sitting with them very nearly three-quarters of an hour. The good lady had not given me the possibility of escape before.”\n\n“And how did you think Miss Fairfax looking?”\n\n“Ill, very ill—that is, if a young lady can ever be allowed to look ill. But the expression is hardly admissible, Mrs. Weston, is it? Ladies can never look ill. And, seriously, Miss Fairfax is naturally so pale, as almost always to give the appearance of ill health.—A most deplorable want of complexion.”\n\nEmma would not agree to this, and began a warm defence of Miss Fairfax’s complexion. “It was certainly never brilliant, but she would not allow it to have a sickly hue in general; and there was a softness and delicacy in her skin which gave peculiar elegance to the character of her face.” He listened with all due deference; acknowledged that he had heard many people say the same—but yet he must confess, that to him nothing could make amends for the want of the fine glow of health. Where features were indifferent, a fine complexion gave beauty to them all; and where they were good, the effect was—fortunately he need not attempt to describe what the effect was.\n\n“Well,” said Emma, “there is no disputing about taste.—At least you admire her except her complexion.”\n\nHe shook his head and laughed.—“I cannot separate Miss Fairfax and her complexion.”\n\n“Did you see her often at Weymouth? Were you often in the same society?”\n\nAt this moment they were approaching Ford’s, and he hastily exclaimed, “Ha! this must be the very shop that every body attends every day of their lives, as my father informs me. He comes to Highbury himself, he says, six days out of the seven, and has always business at Ford’s. If it be not inconvenient to you, pray let us go in, that I may prove myself to belong to the place, to be a true citizen of Highbury. I must buy something at Ford’s. It will be taking out my freedom.—I dare say they sell gloves.”\n\n“Oh! yes, gloves and every thing. I do admire your patriotism. You will be adored in Highbury. You were very popular before you came, because you were Mr. Weston’s son—but lay out half a guinea at Ford’s, and your popularity will stand upon your own virtues.”\n\nThey went in; and while the sleek, well-tied parcels of “Men’s Beavers” and “York Tan” were bringing down and displaying on the counter, he said—“But I beg your pardon, Miss Woodhouse, you were speaking to me, you were saying something at the very moment of this burst of my amor patriae. Do not let me lose it. I assure you the utmost stretch of public fame would not make me amends for the loss of any happiness in private life.”\n\n“I merely asked, whether you had known much of Miss Fairfax and her party at Weymouth.”\n\n“And now that I understand your question, I must pronounce it to be a very unfair one. It is always the lady’s right to decide on the degree of acquaintance. Miss Fairfax must already have given her account.—I shall not commit myself by claiming more than she may chuse to allow.”\n\n“Upon my word! you answer as discreetly as she could do herself. But her account of every thing leaves so much to be guessed, she is so very reserved, so very unwilling to give the least information about any body, that I really think you may say what you like of your acquaintance with her.”\n\n“May I, indeed?—Then I will speak the truth, and nothing suits me so well. I met her frequently at Weymouth. I had known the Campbells a little in town; and at Weymouth we were very much in the same set. Colonel Campbell is a very agreeable man, and Mrs. Campbell a friendly, warm-hearted woman. I like them all.”\n\n“You know Miss Fairfax’s situation in life, I conclude; what she is destined to be?”\n\n“Yes—(rather hesitatingly)—I believe I do.”\n\n“You get upon delicate subjects, Emma,” said Mrs. Weston smiling; “remember that I am here.—Mr. Frank Churchill hardly knows what to say when you speak of Miss Fairfax’s situation in life. I will move a little farther off.”\n\n“I certainly do forget to think of her,” said Emma, “as having ever been any thing but my friend and my dearest friend.”\n\nHe looked as if he fully understood and honoured such a sentiment.\n\nWhen the gloves were bought, and they had quitted the shop again, “Did you ever hear the young lady we were speaking of, play?” said Frank Churchill.\n\n“Ever hear her!” repeated Emma. “You forget how much she belongs to Highbury. I have heard her every year of our lives since we both began. She plays charmingly.”\n\n“You think so, do you?—I wanted the opinion of some one who could really judge. She appeared to me to play well, that is, with considerable taste, but I know nothing of the matter myself.—I am excessively fond of music, but without the smallest skill or right of judging of any body’s performance.—I have been used to hear her’s admired; and I remember one proof of her being thought to play well:—a man, a very musical man, and in love with another woman—engaged to her—on the point of marriage—would yet never ask that other woman to sit down to the instrument, if the lady in question could sit down instead—never seemed to like to hear one if he could hear the other. That, I thought, in a man of known musical talent, was some proof.”\n\n“Proof indeed!” said Emma, highly amused.—“Mr. Dixon is very musical, is he? We shall know more about them all, in half an hour, from you, than Miss Fairfax would have vouchsafed in half a year.”\n\n“Yes, Mr. Dixon and Miss Campbell were the persons; and I thought it a very strong proof.”\n\n“Certainly—very strong it was; to own the truth, a great deal stronger than, if I had been Miss Campbell, would have been at all agreeable to me. I could not excuse a man’s having more music than love—more ear than eye—a more acute sensibility to fine sounds than to my feelings. How did Miss Campbell appear to like it?”\n\n“It was her very particular friend, you know.”\n\n“Poor comfort!” said Emma, laughing. “One would rather have a stranger preferred than one’s very particular friend—with a stranger it might not recur again—but the misery of having a very particular friend always at hand, to do every thing better than one does oneself!—Poor Mrs. Dixon! Well, I am glad she is gone to settle in Ireland.”\n\n“You are right. It was not very flattering to Miss Campbell; but she really did not seem to feel it.”\n\n“So much the better—or so much the worse:—I do not know which. But be it sweetness or be it stupidity in her—quickness of friendship, or dulness of feeling—there was one person, I think, who must have felt it: Miss Fairfax herself. She must have felt the improper and dangerous distinction.”\n\n“As to that—I do not—”\n\n“Oh! do not imagine that I expect an account of Miss Fairfax’s sensations from you, or from any body else. They are known to no human being, I guess, but herself. But if she continued to play whenever she was asked by Mr. Dixon, one may guess what one chuses.”\n\n“There appeared such a perfectly good understanding among them all—” he began rather quickly, but checking himself, added, “however, it is impossible for me to say on what terms they really were—how it might all be behind the scenes. I can only say that there was smoothness outwardly. But you, who have known Miss Fairfax from a child, must be a better judge of her character, and of how she is likely to conduct herself in critical situations, than I can be.”\n\n“I have known her from a child, undoubtedly; we have been children and women together; and it is natural to suppose that we should be intimate,—that we should have taken to each other whenever she visited her friends. But we never did. I hardly know how it has happened; a little, perhaps, from that wickedness on my side which was prone to take disgust towards a girl so idolized and so cried up as she always was, by her aunt and grandmother, and all their set. And then, her reserve—I never could attach myself to any one so completely reserved.”\n\n“It is a most repulsive quality, indeed,” said he. “Oftentimes very convenient, no doubt, but never pleasing. There is safety in reserve, but no attraction. One cannot love a reserved person.”\n\n“Not till the reserve ceases towards oneself; and then the attraction may be the greater. But I must be more in want of a friend, or an agreeable companion, than I have yet been, to take the trouble of conquering any body’s reserve to procure one. Intimacy between Miss Fairfax and me is quite out of the question. I have no reason to think ill of her—not the least—except that such extreme and perpetual cautiousness of word and manner, such a dread of giving a distinct idea about any body, is apt to suggest suspicions of there being something to conceal.”\n\nHe perfectly agreed with her: and after walking together so long, and thinking so much alike, Emma felt herself so well acquainted with him, that she could hardly believe it to be only their second meeting. He was not exactly what she had expected; less of the man of the world in some of his notions, less of the spoiled child of fortune, therefore better than she had expected. His ideas seemed more moderate—his feelings warmer. She was particularly struck by his manner of considering Mr. Elton’s house, which, as well as the church, he would go and look at, and would not join them in finding much fault with. No, he could not believe it a bad house; not such a house as a man was to be pitied for having. If it were to be shared with the woman he loved, he could not think any man to be pitied for having that house. There must be ample room in it for every real comfort. The man must be a blockhead who wanted more.\n\nMrs. Weston laughed, and said he did not know what he was talking about. Used only to a large house himself, and without ever thinking how many advantages and accommodations were attached to its size, he could be no judge of the privations inevitably belonging to a small one. But Emma, in her own mind, determined that he did know what he was talking about, and that he shewed a very amiable inclination to settle early in life, and to marry, from worthy motives. He might not be aware of the inroads on domestic peace to be occasioned by no housekeeper’s room, or a bad butler’s pantry, but no doubt he did perfectly feel that Enscombe could not make him happy, and that whenever he were attached, he would willingly give up much of wealth to be allowed an early establishment.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter VII\n\nEmma’s very good opinion of Frank Churchill was a little shaken the following day, by hearing that he was gone off to London, merely to have his hair cut. A sudden freak seemed to have seized him at breakfast, and he had sent for a chaise and set off, intending to return to dinner, but with no more important view that appeared than having his hair cut. There was certainly no harm in his travelling sixteen miles twice over on such an errand; but there was an air of foppery and nonsense in it which she could not approve. It did not accord with the rationality of plan, the moderation in expense, or even the unselfish warmth of heart, which she had believed herself to discern in him yesterday. Vanity, extravagance, love of change, restlessness of temper, which must be doing something, good or bad; heedlessness as to the pleasure of his father and Mrs. Weston, indifferent as to how his conduct might appear in general; he became liable to all these charges. His father only called him a coxcomb, and thought it a very good story; but that Mrs. Weston did not like it, was clear enough, by her passing it over as quickly as possible, and making no other comment than that “all young people would have their little whims.”\n\nWith the exception of this little blot, Emma found that his visit hitherto had given her friend only good ideas of him. Mrs. Weston was very ready to say how attentive and pleasant a companion he made himself—how much she saw to like in his disposition altogether. He appeared to have a very open temper—certainly a very cheerful and lively one; she could observe nothing wrong in his notions, a great deal decidedly right; he spoke of his uncle with warm regard, was fond of talking of him—said he would be the best man in the world if he were left to himself; and though there was no being attached to the aunt, he acknowledged her kindness with gratitude, and seemed to mean always to speak of her with respect. This was all very promising; and, but for such an unfortunate fancy for having his hair cut, there was nothing to denote him unworthy of the distinguished honour which her imagination had given him; the honour, if not of being really in love with her, of being at least very near it, and saved only by her own indifference—(for still her resolution held of never marrying)—the honour, in short, of being marked out for her by all their joint acquaintance.\n\nMr. Weston, on his side, added a virtue to the account which must have some weight. He gave her to understand that Frank admired her extremely—thought her very beautiful and very charming; and with so much to be said for him altogether, she found she must not judge him harshly. As Mrs. Weston observed, “all young people would have their little whims.”\n\nThere was one person among his new acquaintance in Surry, not so leniently disposed. In general he was judged, throughout the parishes of Donwell and Highbury, with great candour; liberal allowances were made for the little excesses of such a handsome young man—one who smiled so often and bowed so well; but there was one spirit among them not to be softened, from its power of censure, by bows or smiles—Mr. Knightley. The circumstance was told him at Hartfield; for the moment, he was silent; but Emma heard him almost immediately afterwards say to himself, over a newspaper he held in his hand, “Hum! just the trifling, silly fellow I took him for.” She had half a mind to resent; but an instant’s observation convinced her that it was really said only to relieve his own feelings, and not meant to provoke; and therefore she let it pass.\n\nAlthough in one instance the bearers of not good tidings, Mr. and Mrs. Weston’s visit this morning was in another respect particularly opportune. Something occurred while they were at Hartfield, to make Emma want their advice; and, which was still more lucky, she wanted exactly the advice they gave.\n\nThis was the occurrence:—The Coles had been settled some years in Highbury, and were very good sort of people—friendly, liberal, and unpretending; but, on the other hand, they were of low origin, in trade, and only moderately genteel. On their first coming into the country, they had lived in proportion to their income, quietly, keeping little company, and that little unexpensively; but the last year or two had brought them a considerable increase of means—the house in town had yielded greater profits, and fortune in general had smiled on them. With their wealth, their views increased; their want of a larger house, their inclination for more company. They added to their house, to their number of servants, to their expenses of every sort; and by this time were, in fortune and style of living, second only to the family at Hartfield. Their love of society, and their new dining-room, prepared every body for their keeping dinner-company; and a few parties, chiefly among the single men, had already taken place. The regular and best families Emma could hardly suppose they would presume to invite—neither Donwell, nor Hartfield, nor Randalls. Nothing should tempt her to go, if they did; and she regretted that her father’s known habits would be giving her refusal less meaning than she could wish. The Coles were very respectable in their way, but they ought to be taught that it was not for them to arrange the terms on which the superior families would visit them. This lesson, she very much feared, they would receive only from herself; she had little hope of Mr. Knightley, none of Mr. Weston.\n\nBut she had made up her mind how to meet this presumption so many weeks before it appeared, that when the insult came at last, it found her very differently affected. Donwell and Randalls had received their invitation, and none had come for her father and herself; and Mrs. Weston’s accounting for it with “I suppose they will not take the liberty with you; they know you do not dine out,” was not quite sufficient. She felt that she should like to have had the power of refusal; and afterwards, as the idea of the party to be assembled there, consisting precisely of those whose society was dearest to her, occurred again and again, she did not know that she might not have been tempted to accept. Harriet was to be there in the evening, and the Bateses. They had been speaking of it as they walked about Highbury the day before, and Frank Churchill had most earnestly lamented her absence. Might not the evening end in a dance? had been a question of his. The bare possibility of it acted as a farther irritation on her spirits; and her being left in solitary grandeur, even supposing the omission to be intended as a compliment, was but poor comfort.\n\nIt was the arrival of this very invitation while the Westons were at Hartfield, which made their presence so acceptable; for though her first remark, on reading it, was that “of course it must be declined,” she so very soon proceeded to ask them what they advised her to do, that their advice for her going was most prompt and successful.\n\nShe owned that, considering every thing, she was not absolutely without inclination for the party. The Coles expressed themselves so properly—there was so much real attention in the manner of it—so much consideration for her father. “They would have solicited the honour earlier, but had been waiting the arrival of a folding-screen from London, which they hoped might keep Mr. Woodhouse from any draught of air, and therefore induce him the more readily to give them the honour of his company.” Upon the whole, she was very persuadable; and it being briefly settled among themselves how it might be done without neglecting his comfort—how certainly Mrs. Goddard, if not Mrs. Bates, might be depended on for bearing him company—Mr. Woodhouse was to be talked into an acquiescence of his daughter’s going out to dinner on a day now near at hand, and spending the whole evening away from him. As for his going, Emma did not wish him to think it possible, the hours would be too late, and the party too numerous. He was soon pretty well resigned.\n\n“I am not fond of dinner-visiting,” said he—“I never was. No more is Emma. Late hours do not agree with us. I am sorry Mr. and Mrs. Cole should have done it. I think it would be much better if they would come in one afternoon next summer, and take their tea with us—take us in their afternoon walk; which they might do, as our hours are so reasonable, and yet get home without being out in the damp of the evening. The dews of a summer evening are what I would not expose any body to. However, as they are so very desirous to have dear Emma dine with them, and as you will both be there, and Mr. Knightley too, to take care of her, I cannot wish to prevent it, provided the weather be what it ought, neither damp, nor cold, nor windy.” Then turning to Mrs. Weston, with a look of gentle reproach—“Ah! Miss Taylor, if you had not married, you would have staid at home with me.”\n\n“Well, sir,” cried Mr. Weston, “as I took Miss Taylor away, it is incumbent on me to supply her place, if I can; and I will step to Mrs. Goddard in a moment, if you wish it.”\n\nBut the idea of any thing to be done in a moment, was increasing, not lessening, Mr. Woodhouse’s agitation. The ladies knew better how to allay it. Mr. Weston must be quiet, and every thing deliberately arranged.\n\nWith this treatment, Mr. Woodhouse was soon composed enough for talking as usual. “He should be happy to see Mrs. Goddard. He had a great regard for Mrs. Goddard; and Emma should write a line, and invite her. James could take the note. But first of all, there must be an answer written to Mrs. Cole.”\n\n“You will make my excuses, my dear, as civilly as possible. You will say that I am quite an invalid, and go no where, and therefore must decline their obliging invitation; beginning with my compliments, of course. But you will do every thing right. I need not tell you what is to be done. We must remember to let James know that the carriage will be wanted on Tuesday. I shall have no fears for you with him. We have never been there above once since the new approach was made; but still I have no doubt that James will take you very safely. And when you get there, you must tell him at what time you would have him come for you again; and you had better name an early hour. You will not like staying late. You will get very tired when tea is over.”\n\n“But you would not wish me to come away before I am tired, papa?”\n\n“Oh! no, my love; but you will soon be tired. There will be a great many people talking at once. You will not like the noise.”\n\n“But, my dear sir,” cried Mr. Weston, “if Emma comes away early, it will be breaking up the party.”\n\n“And no great harm if it does,” said Mr. Woodhouse. “The sooner every party breaks up, the better.”\n\n“But you do not consider how it may appear to the Coles. Emma’s going away directly after tea might be giving offence. They are good-natured people, and think little of their own claims; but still they must feel that any body’s hurrying away is no great compliment; and Miss Woodhouse’s doing it would be more thought of than any other person’s in the room. You would not wish to disappoint and mortify the Coles, I am sure, sir; friendly, good sort of people as ever lived, and who have been your neighbours these ten years.”\n\n“No, upon no account in the world, Mr. Weston; I am much obliged to you for reminding me. I should be extremely sorry to be giving them any pain. I know what worthy people they are. Perry tells me that Mr. Cole never touches malt liquor. You would not think it to look at him, but he is bilious—Mr. Cole is very bilious. No, I would not be the means of giving them any pain. My dear Emma, we must consider this. I am sure, rather than run the risk of hurting Mr. and Mrs. Cole, you would stay a little longer than you might wish. You will not regard being tired. You will be perfectly safe, you know, among your friends.”\n\n“Oh yes, papa. I have no fears at all for myself; and I should have no scruples of staying as late as Mrs. Weston, but on your account. I am only afraid of your sitting up for me. I am not afraid of your not being exceedingly comfortable with Mrs. Goddard. She loves piquet, you know; but when she is gone home, I am afraid you will be sitting up by yourself, instead of going to bed at your usual time—and the idea of that would entirely destroy my comfort. You must promise me not to sit up.”\n\nHe did, on the condition of some promises on her side: such as that, if she came home cold, she would be sure to warm herself thoroughly; if hungry, that she would take something to eat; that her own maid should sit up for her; and that Serle and the butler should see that every thing were safe in the house, as usual.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter VIII\n\nFrank Churchill came back again; and if he kept his father’s dinner waiting, it was not known at Hartfield; for Mrs. Weston was too anxious for his being a favourite with Mr. Woodhouse, to betray any imperfection which could be concealed.\n\nHe came back, had had his hair cut, and laughed at himself with a very good grace, but without seeming really at all ashamed of what he had done. He had no reason to wish his hair longer, to conceal any confusion of face; no reason to wish the money unspent, to improve his spirits. He was quite as undaunted and as lively as ever; and, after seeing him, Emma thus moralised to herself:—\n\n“I do not know whether it ought to be so, but certainly silly things do cease to be silly if they are done by sensible people in an impudent way. Wickedness is always wickedness, but folly is not always folly.—It depends upon the character of those who handle it. Mr. Knightley, he is not a trifling, silly young man. If he were, he would have done this differently. He would either have gloried in the achievement, or been ashamed of it. There would have been either the ostentation of a coxcomb, or the evasions of a mind too weak to defend its own vanities.—No, I am perfectly sure that he is not trifling or silly.”\n\nWith Tuesday came the agreeable prospect of seeing him again, and for a longer time than hitherto; of judging of his general manners, and by inference, of the meaning of his manners towards herself; of guessing how soon it might be necessary for her to throw coldness into her air; and of fancying what the observations of all those might be, who were now seeing them together for the first time.\n\nShe meant to be very happy, in spite of the scene being laid at Mr. Cole’s; and without being able to forget that among the failings of Mr. Elton, even in the days of his favour, none had disturbed her more than his propensity to dine with Mr. Cole.\n\nHer father’s comfort was amply secured, Mrs. Bates as well as Mrs. Goddard being able to come; and her last pleasing duty, before she left the house, was to pay her respects to them as they sat together after dinner; and while her father was fondly noticing the beauty of her dress, to make the two ladies all the amends in her power, by helping them to large slices of cake and full glasses of wine, for whatever unwilling self-denial his care of their constitution might have obliged them to practise during the meal.—She had provided a plentiful dinner for them; she wished she could know that they had been allowed to eat it.\n\nShe followed another carriage to Mr. Cole’s door; and was pleased to see that it was Mr. Knightley’s; for Mr. Knightley keeping no horses, having little spare money and a great deal of health, activity, and independence, was too apt, in Emma’s opinion, to get about as he could, and not use his carriage so often as became the owner of Donwell Abbey. She had an opportunity now of speaking her approbation while warm from her heart, for he stopped to hand her out.\n\n“This is coming as you should do,” said she; “like a gentleman.—I am quite glad to see you.”\n\nHe thanked her, observing, “How lucky that we should arrive at the same moment! for, if we had met first in the drawing-room, I doubt whether you would have discerned me to be more of a gentleman than usual.—You might not have distinguished how I came, by my look or manner.”\n\n“Yes I should, I am sure I should. There is always a look of consciousness or bustle when people come in a way which they know to be beneath them. You think you carry it off very well, I dare say, but with you it is a sort of bravado, an air of affected unconcern; I always observe it whenever I meet you under those circumstances. Now you have nothing to try for. You are not afraid of being supposed ashamed. You are not striving to look taller than any body else. Now I shall really be very happy to walk into the same room with you.”\n\n“Nonsensical girl!” was his reply, but not at all in anger.\n\nEmma had as much reason to be satisfied with the rest of the party as with Mr. Knightley. She was received with a cordial respect which could not but please, and given all the consequence she could wish for. When the Westons arrived, the kindest looks of love, the strongest of admiration were for her, from both husband and wife; the son approached her with a cheerful eagerness which marked her as his peculiar object, and at dinner she found him seated by her—and, as she firmly believed, not without some dexterity on his side.\n\nThe party was rather large, as it included one other family, a proper unobjectionable country family, whom the Coles had the advantage of naming among their acquaintance, and the male part of Mr. Cox’s family, the lawyer of Highbury. The less worthy females were to come in the evening, with Miss Bates, Miss Fairfax, and Miss Smith; but already, at dinner, they were too numerous for any subject of conversation to be general; and, while politics and Mr. Elton were talked over, Emma could fairly surrender all her attention to the pleasantness of her neighbour. The first remote sound to which she felt herself obliged to attend, was the name of Jane Fairfax. Mrs. Cole seemed to be relating something of her that was expected to be very interesting. She listened, and found it well worth listening to. That very dear part of Emma, her fancy, received an amusing supply. Mrs. Cole was telling that she had been calling on Miss Bates, and as soon as she entered the room had been struck by the sight of a pianoforte—a very elegant looking instrument—not a grand, but a large-sized square pianoforte; and the substance of the story, the end of all the dialogue which ensued of surprize, and inquiry, and congratulations on her side, and explanations on Miss Bates’s, was, that this pianoforte had arrived from Broadwood’s the day before, to the great astonishment of both aunt and niece—entirely unexpected; that at first, by Miss Bates’s account, Jane herself was quite at a loss, quite bewildered to think who could possibly have ordered it—but now, they were both perfectly satisfied that it could be from only one quarter;—of course it must be from Colonel Campbell.\n\n“One can suppose nothing else,” added Mrs. Cole, “and I was only surprized that there could ever have been a doubt. But Jane, it seems, had a letter from them very lately, and not a word was said about it. She knows their ways best; but I should not consider their silence as any reason for their not meaning to make the present. They might chuse to surprize her.”\n\nMrs. Cole had many to agree with her; every body who spoke on the subject was equally convinced that it must come from Colonel Campbell, and equally rejoiced that such a present had been made; and there were enough ready to speak to allow Emma to think her own way, and still listen to Mrs. Cole.\n\n“I declare, I do not know when I have heard any thing that has given me more satisfaction!—It always has quite hurt me that Jane Fairfax, who plays so delightfully, should not have an instrument. It seemed quite a shame, especially considering how many houses there are where fine instruments are absolutely thrown away. This is like giving ourselves a slap, to be sure! and it was but yesterday I was telling Mr. Cole, I really was ashamed to look at our new grand pianoforte in the drawing-room, while I do not know one note from another, and our little girls, who are but just beginning, perhaps may never make any thing of it; and there is poor Jane Fairfax, who is mistress of music, has not any thing of the nature of an instrument, not even the pitifullest old spinet in the world, to amuse herself with.—I was saying this to Mr. Cole but yesterday, and he quite agreed with me; only he is so particularly fond of music that he could not help indulging himself in the purchase, hoping that some of our good neighbours might be so obliging occasionally to put it to a better use than we can; and that really is the reason why the instrument was bought—or else I am sure we ought to be ashamed of it.—We are in great hopes that Miss Woodhouse may be prevailed with to try it this evening.”\n\nMiss Woodhouse made the proper acquiescence; and finding that nothing more was to be entrapped from any communication of Mrs. Cole’s, turned to Frank Churchill.\n\n“Why do you smile?” said she.\n\n“Nay, why do you?”\n\n“Me!—I suppose I smile for pleasure at Colonel Campbell’s being so rich and so liberal.—It is a handsome present.”\n\n“Very.”\n\n“I rather wonder that it was never made before.”\n\n“Perhaps Miss Fairfax has never been staying here so long before.”\n\n“Or that he did not give her the use of their own instrument—which must now be shut up in London, untouched by any body.”\n\n“That is a grand pianoforte, and he might think it too large for Mrs. Bates’s house.”\n\n“You may say what you chuse—but your countenance testifies that your thoughts on this subject are very much like mine.”\n\n“I do not know. I rather believe you are giving me more credit for acuteness than I deserve. I smile because you smile, and shall probably suspect whatever I find you suspect; but at present I do not see what there is to question. If Colonel Campbell is not the person, who can be?”\n\n“What do you say to Mrs. Dixon?”\n\n“Mrs. Dixon! very true indeed. I had not thought of Mrs. Dixon. She must know as well as her father, how acceptable an instrument would be; and perhaps the mode of it, the mystery, the surprize, is more like a young woman’s scheme than an elderly man’s. It is Mrs. Dixon, I dare say. I told you that your suspicions would guide mine.”\n\n“If so, you must extend your suspicions and comprehend Mr. Dixon in them.”\n\n“Mr. Dixon.—Very well. Yes, I immediately perceive that it must be the joint present of Mr. and Mrs. Dixon. We were speaking the other day, you know, of his being so warm an admirer of her performance.”\n\n“Yes, and what you told me on that head, confirmed an idea which I had entertained before.—I do not mean to reflect upon the good intentions of either Mr. Dixon or Miss Fairfax, but I cannot help suspecting either that, after making his proposals to her friend, he had the misfortune to fall in love with her, or that he became conscious of a little attachment on her side. One might guess twenty things without guessing exactly the right; but I am sure there must be a particular cause for her chusing to come to Highbury instead of going with the Campbells to Ireland. Here, she must be leading a life of privation and penance; there it would have been all enjoyment. As to the pretence of trying her native air, I look upon that as a mere excuse.—In the summer it might have passed; but what can any body’s native air do for them in the months of January, February, and March? Good fires and carriages would be much more to the purpose in most cases of delicate health, and I dare say in her’s. I do not require you to adopt all my suspicions, though you make so noble a profession of doing it, but I honestly tell you what they are.”\n\n“And, upon my word, they have an air of great probability. Mr. Dixon’s preference of her music to her friend’s, I can answer for being very decided.”\n\n“And then, he saved her life. Did you ever hear of that?—A water party; and by some accident she was falling overboard. He caught her.”\n\n“He did. I was there—one of the party.”\n\n“Were you really?—Well!—But you observed nothing of course, for it seems to be a new idea to you.—If I had been there, I think I should have made some discoveries.”\n\n“I dare say you would; but I, simple I, saw nothing but the fact, that Miss Fairfax was nearly dashed from the vessel and that Mr. Dixon caught her.—It was the work of a moment. And though the consequent shock and alarm was very great and much more durable—indeed I believe it was half an hour before any of us were comfortable again—yet that was too general a sensation for any thing of peculiar anxiety to be observable. I do not mean to say, however, that you might not have made discoveries.”\n\nThe conversation was here interrupted. They were called on to share in the awkwardness of a rather long interval between the courses, and obliged to be as formal and as orderly as the others; but when the table was again safely covered, when every corner dish was placed exactly right, and occupation and ease were generally restored, Emma said,\n\n“The arrival of this pianoforte is decisive with me. I wanted to know a little more, and this tells me quite enough. Depend upon it, we shall soon hear that it is a present from Mr. and Mrs. Dixon.”\n\n“And if the Dixons should absolutely deny all knowledge of it we must conclude it to come from the Campbells.”\n\n“No, I am sure it is not from the Campbells. Miss Fairfax knows it is not from the Campbells, or they would have been guessed at first. She would not have been puzzled, had she dared fix on them. I may not have convinced you perhaps, but I am perfectly convinced myself that Mr. Dixon is a principal in the business.”\n\n“Indeed you injure me if you suppose me unconvinced. Your reasonings carry my judgment along with them entirely. At first, while I supposed you satisfied that Colonel Campbell was the giver, I saw it only as paternal kindness, and thought it the most natural thing in the world. But when you mentioned Mrs. Dixon, I felt how much more probable that it should be the tribute of warm female friendship. And now I can see it in no other light than as an offering of love.”\n\nThere was no occasion to press the matter farther. The conviction seemed real; he looked as if he felt it. She said no more, other subjects took their turn; and the rest of the dinner passed away; the dessert succeeded, the children came in, and were talked to and admired amid the usual rate of conversation; a few clever things said, a few downright silly, but by much the larger proportion neither the one nor the other—nothing worse than everyday remarks, dull repetitions, old news, and heavy jokes.\n\nThe ladies had not been long in the drawing-room, before the other ladies, in their different divisions, arrived. Emma watched the entree of her own particular little friend; and if she could not exult in her dignity and grace, she could not only love the blooming sweetness and the artless manner, but could most heartily rejoice in that light, cheerful, unsentimental disposition which allowed her so many alleviations of pleasure, in the midst of the pangs of disappointed affection. There she sat—and who would have guessed how many tears she had been lately shedding? To be in company, nicely dressed herself and seeing others nicely dressed, to sit and smile and look pretty, and say nothing, was enough for the happiness of the present hour. Jane Fairfax did look and move superior; but Emma suspected she might have been glad to change feelings with Harriet, very glad to have purchased the mortification of having loved—yes, of having loved even Mr. Elton in vain—by the surrender of all the dangerous pleasure of knowing herself beloved by the husband of her friend.\n\nIn so large a party it was not necessary that Emma should approach her. She did not wish to speak of the pianoforte, she felt too much in the secret herself, to think the appearance of curiosity or interest fair, and therefore purposely kept at a distance; but by the others, the subject was almost immediately introduced, and she saw the blush of consciousness with which congratulations were received, the blush of guilt which accompanied the name of “my excellent friend Colonel Campbell.”\n\nMrs. Weston, kind-hearted and musical, was particularly interested by the circumstance, and Emma could not help being amused at her perseverance in dwelling on the subject; and having so much to ask and to say as to tone, touch, and pedal, totally unsuspicious of that wish of saying as little about it as possible, which she plainly read in the fair heroine’s countenance.\n\nThey were soon joined by some of the gentlemen; and the very first of the early was Frank Churchill. In he walked, the first and the handsomest; and after paying his compliments en passant to Miss Bates and her niece, made his way directly to the opposite side of the circle, where sat Miss Woodhouse; and till he could find a seat by her, would not sit at all. Emma divined what every body present must be thinking. She was his object, and every body must perceive it. She introduced him to her friend, Miss Smith, and, at convenient moments afterwards, heard what each thought of the other. “He had never seen so lovely a face, and was delighted with her naïveté.” And she, “Only to be sure it was paying him too great a compliment, but she did think there were some looks a little like Mr. Elton.” Emma restrained her indignation, and only turned from her in silence.\n\nSmiles of intelligence passed between her and the gentleman on first glancing towards Miss Fairfax; but it was most prudent to avoid speech. He told her that he had been impatient to leave the dining-room—hated sitting long—was always the first to move when he could—that his father, Mr. Knightley, Mr. Cox, and Mr. Cole, were left very busy over parish business—that as long as he had staid, however, it had been pleasant enough, as he had found them in general a set of gentlemanlike, sensible men; and spoke so handsomely of Highbury altogether—thought it so abundant in agreeable families—that Emma began to feel she had been used to despise the place rather too much. She questioned him as to the society in Yorkshire—the extent of the neighbourhood about Enscombe, and the sort; and could make out from his answers that, as far as Enscombe was concerned, there was very little going on, that their visitings were among a range of great families, none very near; and that even when days were fixed, and invitations accepted, it was an even chance that Mrs. Churchill were not in health and spirits for going; that they made a point of visiting no fresh person; and that, though he had his separate engagements, it was not without difficulty, without considerable address at times, that he could get away, or introduce an acquaintance for a night.\n\nShe saw that Enscombe could not satisfy, and that Highbury, taken at its best, might reasonably please a young man who had more retirement at home than he liked. His importance at Enscombe was very evident. He did not boast, but it naturally betrayed itself, that he had persuaded his aunt where his uncle could do nothing, and on her laughing and noticing it, he owned that he believed (excepting one or two points) he could with time persuade her to any thing. One of those points on which his influence failed, he then mentioned. He had wanted very much to go abroad—had been very eager indeed to be allowed to travel—but she would not hear of it. This had happened the year before. Now, he said, he was beginning to have no longer the same wish.\n\nThe unpersuadable point, which he did not mention, Emma guessed to be good behaviour to his father.\n\n“I have made a most wretched discovery,” said he, after a short pause.— “I have been here a week to-morrow—half my time. I never knew days fly so fast. A week to-morrow!—And I have hardly begun to enjoy myself. But just got acquainted with Mrs. Weston, and others!—I hate the recollection.”\n\n“Perhaps you may now begin to regret that you spent one whole day, out of so few, in having your hair cut.”\n\n“No,” said he, smiling, “that is no subject of regret at all. I have no pleasure in seeing my friends, unless I can believe myself fit to be seen.”\n\nThe rest of the gentlemen being now in the room, Emma found herself obliged to turn from him for a few minutes, and listen to Mr. Cole. When Mr. Cole had moved away, and her attention could be restored as before, she saw Frank Churchill looking intently across the room at Miss Fairfax, who was sitting exactly opposite.\n\n“What is the matter?” said she.\n\nHe started. “Thank you for rousing me,” he replied. “I believe I have been very rude; but really Miss Fairfax has done her hair in so odd a way—so very odd a way—that I cannot keep my eyes from her. I never saw any thing so outrée!—Those curls!—This must be a fancy of her own. I see nobody else looking like her!—I must go and ask her whether it is an Irish fashion. Shall I?—Yes, I will—I declare I will—and you shall see how she takes it;—whether she colours.”\n\nHe was gone immediately; and Emma soon saw him standing before Miss Fairfax, and talking to her; but as to its effect on the young lady, as he had improvidently placed himself exactly between them, exactly in front of Miss Fairfax, she could absolutely distinguish nothing.\n\nBefore he could return to his chair, it was taken by Mrs. Weston.\n\n“This is the luxury of a large party,” said she:—“one can get near every body, and say every thing. My dear Emma, I am longing to talk to you. I have been making discoveries and forming plans, just like yourself, and I must tell them while the idea is fresh. Do you know how Miss Bates and her niece came here?”\n\n“How?—They were invited, were not they?”\n\n“Oh! yes—but how they were conveyed hither?—the manner of their coming?”\n\n“They walked, I conclude. How else could they come?”\n\n“Very true.—Well, a little while ago it occurred to me how very sad it would be to have Jane Fairfax walking home again, late at night, and cold as the nights are now. And as I looked at her, though I never saw her appear to more advantage, it struck me that she was heated, and would therefore be particularly liable to take cold. Poor girl! I could not bear the idea of it; so, as soon as Mr. Weston came into the room, and I could get at him, I spoke to him about the carriage. You may guess how readily he came into my wishes; and having his approbation, I made my way directly to Miss Bates, to assure her that the carriage would be at her service before it took us home; for I thought it would be making her comfortable at once. Good soul! she was as grateful as possible, you may be sure. ‘Nobody was ever so fortunate as herself!’—but with many, many thanks—‘there was no occasion to trouble us, for Mr. Knightley’s carriage had brought, and was to take them home again.’ I was quite surprized;—very glad, I am sure; but really quite surprized. Such a very kind attention—and so thoughtful an attention!—the sort of thing that so few men would think of. And, in short, from knowing his usual ways, I am very much inclined to think that it was for their accommodation the carriage was used at all. I do suspect he would not have had a pair of horses for himself, and that it was only as an excuse for assisting them.”\n\n“Very likely,” said Emma—“nothing more likely. I know no man more likely than Mr. Knightley to do the sort of thing—to do any thing really good-natured, useful, considerate, or benevolent. He is not a gallant man, but he is a very humane one; and this, considering Jane Fairfax’s ill-health, would appear a case of humanity to him;—and for an act of unostentatious kindness, there is nobody whom I would fix on more than on Mr. Knightley. I know he had horses to-day—for we arrived together; and I laughed at him about it, but he said not a word that could betray.”\n\n“Well,” said Mrs. Weston, smiling, “you give him credit for more simple, disinterested benevolence in this instance than I do; for while Miss Bates was speaking, a suspicion darted into my head, and I have never been able to get it out again. The more I think of it, the more probable it appears. In short, I have made a match between Mr. Knightley and Jane Fairfax. See the consequence of keeping you company!—What do you say to it?”\n\n“Mr. Knightley and Jane Fairfax!” exclaimed Emma. “Dear Mrs. Weston, how could you think of such a thing?—Mr. Knightley!—Mr. Knightley must not marry!—You would not have little Henry cut out from Donwell?—Oh! no, no, Henry must have Donwell. I cannot at all consent to Mr. Knightley’s marrying; and I am sure it is not at all likely. I am amazed that you should think of such a thing.”\n\n“My dear Emma, I have told you what led me to think of it. I do not want the match—I do not want to injure dear little Henry—but the idea has been given me by circumstances; and if Mr. Knightley really wished to marry, you would not have him refrain on Henry’s account, a boy of six years old, who knows nothing of the matter?”\n\n“Yes, I would. I could not bear to have Henry supplanted.—Mr. Knightley marry!—No, I have never had such an idea, and I cannot adopt it now. And Jane Fairfax, too, of all women!”\n\n“Nay, she has always been a first favourite with him, as you very well know.”\n\n“But the imprudence of such a match!”\n\n“I am not speaking of its prudence; merely its probability.”\n\n“I see no probability in it, unless you have any better foundation than what you mention. His good-nature, his humanity, as I tell you, would be quite enough to account for the horses. He has a great regard for the Bateses, you know, independent of Jane Fairfax—and is always glad to shew them attention. My dear Mrs. Weston, do not take to match-making. You do it very ill. Jane Fairfax mistress of the Abbey!—Oh! no, no;—every feeling revolts. For his own sake, I would not have him do so mad a thing.”\n\n“Imprudent, if you please—but not mad. Excepting inequality of fortune, and perhaps a little disparity of age, I can see nothing unsuitable.”\n\n“But Mr. Knightley does not want to marry. I am sure he has not the least idea of it. Do not put it into his head. Why should he marry?—He is as happy as possible by himself; with his farm, and his sheep, and his library, and all the parish to manage; and he is extremely fond of his brother’s children. He has no occasion to marry, either to fill up his time or his heart.”\n\n“My dear Emma, as long as he thinks so, it is so; but if he really loves Jane Fairfax—”\n\n“Nonsense! He does not care about Jane Fairfax. In the way of love, I am sure he does not. He would do any good to her, or her family; but—”\n\n“Well,” said Mrs. Weston, laughing, “perhaps the greatest good he could do them, would be to give Jane such a respectable home.”\n\n“If it would be good to her, I am sure it would be evil to himself; a very shameful and degrading connexion. How would he bear to have Miss Bates belonging to him?—To have her haunting the Abbey, and thanking him all day long for his great kindness in marrying Jane?—‘So very kind and obliging!—But he always had been such a very kind neighbour!’ And then fly off, through half a sentence, to her mother’s old petticoat. ‘Not that it was such a very old petticoat either—for still it would last a great while—and, indeed, she must thankfully say that their petticoats were all very strong.’”\n\n“For shame, Emma! Do not mimic her. You divert me against my conscience. And, upon my word, I do not think Mr. Knightley would be much disturbed by Miss Bates. Little things do not irritate him. She might talk on; and if he wanted to say any thing himself, he would only talk louder, and drown her voice. But the question is not, whether it would be a bad connexion for him, but whether he wishes it; and I think he does. I have heard him speak, and so must you, so very highly of Jane Fairfax! The interest he takes in her—his anxiety about her health—his concern that she should have no happier prospect! I have heard him express himself so warmly on those points!—Such an admirer of her performance on the pianoforte, and of her voice! I have heard him say that he could listen to her for ever. Oh! and I had almost forgotten one idea that occurred to me—this pianoforte that has been sent here by somebody—though we have all been so well satisfied to consider it a present from the Campbells, may it not be from Mr. Knightley? I cannot help suspecting him. I think he is just the person to do it, even without being in love.”\n\n“Then it can be no argument to prove that he is in love. But I do not think it is at all a likely thing for him to do. Mr. Knightley does nothing mysteriously.”\n\n“I have heard him lamenting her having no instrument repeatedly; oftener than I should suppose such a circumstance would, in the common course of things, occur to him.”\n\n“Very well; and if he had intended to give her one, he would have told her so.”\n\n“There might be scruples of delicacy, my dear Emma. I have a very strong notion that it comes from him. I am sure he was particularly silent when Mrs. Cole told us of it at dinner.”\n\n“You take up an idea, Mrs. Weston, and run away with it; as you have many a time reproached me with doing. I see no sign of attachment—I believe nothing of the pianoforte—and proof only shall convince me that Mr. Knightley has any thought of marrying Jane Fairfax.”\n\nThey combated the point some time longer in the same way; Emma rather gaining ground over the mind of her friend; for Mrs. Weston was the most used of the two to yield; till a little bustle in the room shewed them that tea was over, and the instrument in preparation;—and at the same moment Mr. Cole approaching to entreat Miss Woodhouse would do them the honour of trying it. Frank Churchill, of whom, in the eagerness of her conversation with Mrs. Weston, she had been seeing nothing, except that he had found a seat by Miss Fairfax, followed Mr. Cole, to add his very pressing entreaties; and as, in every respect, it suited Emma best to lead, she gave a very proper compliance.\n\nShe knew the limitations of her own powers too well to attempt more than she could perform with credit; she wanted neither taste nor spirit in the little things which are generally acceptable, and could accompany her own voice well. One accompaniment to her song took her agreeably by surprize—a second, slightly but correctly taken by Frank Churchill. Her pardon was duly begged at the close of the song, and every thing usual followed. He was accused of having a delightful voice, and a perfect knowledge of music; which was properly denied; and that he knew nothing of the matter, and had no voice at all, roundly asserted. They sang together once more; and Emma would then resign her place to Miss Fairfax, whose performance, both vocal and instrumental, she never could attempt to conceal from herself, was infinitely superior to her own.\n\nWith mixed feelings, she seated herself at a little distance from the numbers round the instrument, to listen. Frank Churchill sang again. They had sung together once or twice, it appeared, at Weymouth. But the sight of Mr. Knightley among the most attentive, soon drew away half Emma’s mind; and she fell into a train of thinking on the subject of Mrs. Weston’s suspicions, to which the sweet sounds of the united voices gave only momentary interruptions. Her objections to Mr. Knightley’s marrying did not in the least subside. She could see nothing but evil in it. It would be a great disappointment to Mr. John Knightley; consequently to Isabella. A real injury to the children—a most mortifying change, and material loss to them all;—a very great deduction from her father’s daily comfort—and, as to herself, she could not at all endure the idea of Jane Fairfax at Donwell Abbey. A Mrs. Knightley for them all to give way to!—No—Mr. Knightley must never marry. Little Henry must remain the heir of Donwell.\n\nPresently Mr. Knightley looked back, and came and sat down by her. They talked at first only of the performance. His admiration was certainly very warm; yet she thought, but for Mrs. Weston, it would not have struck her. As a sort of touchstone, however, she began to speak of his kindness in conveying the aunt and niece; and though his answer was in the spirit of cutting the matter short, she believed it to indicate only his disinclination to dwell on any kindness of his own.\n\n“I often feel concern,” said she, “that I dare not make our carriage more useful on such occasions. It is not that I am without the wish; but you know how impossible my father would deem it that James should put-to for such a purpose.”\n\n“Quite out of the question, quite out of the question,” he replied;—“but you must often wish it, I am sure.” And he smiled with such seeming pleasure at the conviction, that she must proceed another step.\n\n“This present from the Campbells,” said she—“this pianoforte is very kindly given.”\n\n“Yes,” he replied, and without the smallest apparent embarrassment.—“But they would have done better had they given her notice of it. Surprizes are foolish things. The pleasure is not enhanced, and the inconvenience is often considerable. I should have expected better judgment in Colonel Campbell.”\n\nFrom that moment, Emma could have taken her oath that Mr. Knightley had had no concern in giving the instrument. But whether he were entirely free from peculiar attachment—whether there were no actual preference—remained a little longer doubtful. Towards the end of Jane’s second song, her voice grew thick.\n\n“That will do,” said he, when it was finished, thinking aloud—“you have sung quite enough for one evening—now be quiet.”\n\nAnother song, however, was soon begged for. “One more;—they would not fatigue Miss Fairfax on any account, and would only ask for one more.” And Frank Churchill was heard to say, “I think you could manage this without effort; the first part is so very trifling. The strength of the song falls on the second.”\n\nMr. Knightley grew angry.\n\n“That fellow,” said he, indignantly, “thinks of nothing but shewing off his own voice. This must not be.” And touching Miss Bates, who at that moment passed near—“Miss Bates, are you mad, to let your niece sing herself hoarse in this manner? Go, and interfere. They have no mercy on her.”\n\nMiss Bates, in her real anxiety for Jane, could hardly stay even to be grateful, before she stept forward and put an end to all farther singing. Here ceased the concert part of the evening, for Miss Woodhouse and Miss Fairfax were the only young lady performers; but soon (within five minutes) the proposal of dancing—originating nobody exactly knew where—was so effectually promoted by Mr. and Mrs. Cole, that every thing was rapidly clearing away, to give proper space. Mrs. Weston, capital in her country-dances, was seated, and beginning an irresistible waltz; and Frank Churchill, coming up with most becoming gallantry to Emma, had secured her hand, and led her up to the top.\n\nWhile waiting till the other young people could pair themselves off, Emma found time, in spite of the compliments she was receiving on her voice and her taste, to look about, and see what became of Mr. Knightley. This would be a trial. He was no dancer in general. If he were to be very alert in engaging Jane Fairfax now, it might augur something. There was no immediate appearance. No; he was talking to Mrs. Cole—he was looking on unconcerned; Jane was asked by somebody else, and he was still talking to Mrs. Cole.\n\nEmma had no longer an alarm for Henry; his interest was yet safe; and she led off the dance with genuine spirit and enjoyment. Not more than five couple could be mustered; but the rarity and the suddenness of it made it very delightful, and she found herself well matched in a partner. They were a couple worth looking at.\n\nTwo dances, unfortunately, were all that could be allowed. It was growing late, and Miss Bates became anxious to get home, on her mother’s account. After some attempts, therefore, to be permitted to begin again, they were obliged to thank Mrs. Weston, look sorrowful, and have done.\n\n“Perhaps it is as well,” said Frank Churchill, as he attended Emma to her carriage. “I must have asked Miss Fairfax, and her languid dancing would not have agreed with me, after yours.”\n\n# Volume II — Chapter IX\n\nEmma did not repent her condescension in going to the Coles. The visit afforded her many pleasant recollections the next day; and all that she might be supposed to have lost on the side of dignified seclusion, must be amply repaid in the splendour of popularity. She must have delighted the Coles—worthy people, who deserved to be made happy!—And left a name behind her that would not soon die away.\n\nPerfect happiness, even in memory, is not common; and there were two points on which she was not quite easy. She doubted whether she had not transgressed the duty of woman by woman, in betraying her suspicions of Jane Fairfax’s feelings to Frank Churchill. It was hardly right; but it had been so strong an idea, that it would escape her, and his submission to all that she told, was a compliment to her penetration, which made it difficult for her to be quite certain that she ought to have held her tongue.\n\nThe other circumstance of regret related also to Jane Fairfax; and there she had no doubt. She did unfeignedly and unequivocally regret the inferiority of her own playing and singing. She did most heartily grieve over the idleness of her childhood—and sat down and practised vigorously an hour and a half.\n\nShe was then interrupted by Harriet’s coming in; and if Harriet’s praise could have satisfied her, she might soon have been comforted.\n\n“Oh! if I could but play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!”\n\n“Don’t class us together, Harriet. My playing is no more like her’s, than a lamp is like sunshine.”\n\n“Oh! dear—I think you play the best of the two. I think you play quite as well as she does. I am sure I had much rather hear you. Every body last night said how well you played.”\n\n“Those who knew any thing about it, must have felt the difference. The truth is, Harriet, that my playing is just good enough to be praised, but Jane Fairfax’s is much beyond it.”\n\n“Well, I always shall think that you play quite as well as she does, or that if there is any difference nobody would ever find it out. Mr. Cole said how much taste you had; and Mr. Frank Churchill talked a great deal about your taste, and that he valued taste much more than execution.”\n\n“Ah! but Jane Fairfax has them both, Harriet.”\n\n“Are you sure? I saw she had execution, but I did not know she had any taste. Nobody talked about it. And I hate Italian singing.—There is no understanding a word of it. Besides, if she does play so very well, you know, it is no more than she is obliged to do, because she will have to teach. The Coxes were wondering last night whether she would get into any great family. How did you think the Coxes looked?”\n\n“Just as they always do—very vulgar.”\n\n“They told me something,” said Harriet rather hesitatingly; “but it is nothing of any consequence.”\n\nEmma was obliged to ask what they had told her, though fearful of its producing Mr. Elton.\n\n“They told me—that Mr. Martin dined with them last Saturday.”\n\n“Oh!”\n\n“He came to their father upon some business, and he asked him to stay to dinner.”\n\n“Oh!”\n\n“They talked a great deal about him, especially Anne Cox. I do not know what she meant, but she asked me if I thought I should go and stay there again next summer.”\n\n“She meant to be impertinently curious, just as such an Anne Cox should be.”\n\n“She said he was very agreeable the day he dined there. He sat by her at dinner. Miss Nash thinks either of the Coxes would be very glad to marry him.”\n\n“Very likely.—I think they are, without exception, the most vulgar girls in Highbury.”\n\nHarriet had business at Ford’s.—Emma thought it most prudent to go with her. Another accidental meeting with the Martins was possible, and in her present state, would be dangerous.\n\nHarriet, tempted by every thing and swayed by half a word, was always very long at a purchase; and while she was still hanging over muslins and changing her mind, Emma went to the door for amusement.—Much could not be hoped from the traffic of even the busiest part of Highbury;—Mr. Perry walking hastily by, Mr. William Cox letting himself in at the office-door, Mr. Cole’s carriage-horses returning from exercise, or a stray letter-boy on an obstinate mule, were the liveliest objects she could presume to expect; and when her eyes fell only on the butcher with his tray, a tidy old woman travelling homewards from shop with her full basket, two curs quarrelling over a dirty bone, and a string of dawdling children round the baker’s little bow-window eyeing the gingerbread, she knew she had no reason to complain, and was amused enough; quite enough still to stand at the door. A mind lively and at ease, can do with seeing nothing, and can see nothing that does not answer.\n\nShe looked down the Randalls road. The scene enlarged; two persons appeared; Mrs. Weston and her son-in-law; they were walking into Highbury;—to Hartfield of course. They were stopping, however, in the first place at Mrs. Bates’s; whose house was a little nearer Randalls than Ford’s; and had all but knocked, when Emma caught their eye.—Immediately they crossed the road and came forward to her; and the agreeableness of yesterday’s engagement seemed to give fresh pleasure to the present meeting. Mrs. Weston informed her that she was going to call on the Bateses, in order to hear the new instrument.\n\n“For my companion tells me,” said she, “that I absolutely promised Miss Bates last night, that I would come this morning. I was not aware of it myself. I did not know that I had fixed a day, but as he says I did, I am going now.”\n\n“And while Mrs. Weston pays her visit, I may be allowed, I hope,” said Frank Churchill, “to join your party and wait for her at Hartfield—if you are going home.”\n\nMrs. Weston was disappointed.\n\n“I thought you meant to go with me. They would be very much pleased.”\n\n“Me! I should be quite in the way. But, perhaps—I may be equally in the way here. Miss Woodhouse looks as if she did not want me. My aunt always sends me off when she is shopping. She says I fidget her to death; and Miss Woodhouse looks as if she could almost say the same. What am I to do?”\n\n“I am here on no business of my own,” said Emma; “I am only waiting for my friend. She will probably have soon done, and then we shall go home. But you had better go with Mrs. Weston and hear the instrument.”\n\n“Well—if you advise it.—But (with a smile) if Colonel Campbell should have employed a careless friend, and if it should prove to have an indifferent tone—what shall I say? I shall be no support to Mrs. Weston. She might do very well by herself. A disagreeable truth would be palatable through her lips, but I am the wretchedest being in the world at a civil falsehood.”\n\n“I do not believe any such thing,” replied Emma.—“I am persuaded that you can be as insincere as your neighbours, when it is necessary; but there is no reason to suppose the instrument is indifferent. Quite otherwise indeed, if I understood Miss Fairfax’s opinion last night.”\n\n“Do come with me,” said Mrs. Weston, “if it be not very disagreeable to you. It need not detain us long. We will go to Hartfield afterwards. We will follow them to Hartfield. I really wish you to call with me. It will be felt so great an attention! and I always thought you meant it.”\n\nHe could say no more; and with the hope of Hartfield to reward him, returned with Mrs. Weston to Mrs. Bates’s door. Emma watched them in, and then joined Harriet at the interesting counter,—trying, with all the force of her own mind, to convince her that if she wanted plain muslin it was of no use to look at figured; and that a blue ribbon, be it ever so beautiful, would still never match her yellow pattern. At last it was all settled, even to the destination of the parcel.\n\n“Should I send it to Mrs. Goddard’s, ma’am?” asked Mrs. Ford.—“Yes—no—yes, to Mrs. Goddard’s. Only my pattern gown is at Hartfield. No, you shall send it to Hartfield, if you please. But then, Mrs. Goddard will want to see it.—And I could take the pattern gown home any day. But I shall want the ribbon directly—so it had better go to Hartfield—at least the ribbon. You could make it into two parcels, Mrs. Ford, could not you?”\n\n“It is not worth while, Harriet, to give Mrs. Ford the trouble of two parcels.”\n\n“No more it is.”\n\n“No trouble in the world, ma’am,” said the obliging Mrs. Ford.\n\n“Oh! but indeed I would much rather have it only in one. Then, if you please, you shall send it all to Mrs. Goddard’s—I do not know—No, I think, Miss Woodhouse, I may just as well have it sent to Hartfield, and take it home with me at night. What do you advise?”\n\n“That you do not give another half-second to the subject. To Hartfield, if you please, Mrs. Ford.”\n\n“Aye, that will be much best,” said Harriet, quite satisfied, “I should not at all like to have it sent to Mrs. Goddard’s.”\n\nVoices approached the shop—or rather one voice and two ladies: Mrs. Weston and Miss Bates met them at the door.\n\n“My dear Miss Woodhouse,” said the latter, “I am just run across to entreat the favour of you to come and sit down with us a little while, and give us your opinion of our new instrument; you and Miss Smith. How do you do, Miss Smith?—Very well I thank you.—And I begged Mrs. Weston to come with me, that I might be sure of succeeding.”\n\n“I hope Mrs. Bates and Miss Fairfax are—”\n\n“Very well, I am much obliged to you. My mother is delightfully well; and Jane caught no cold last night. How is Mr. Woodhouse?—I am so glad to hear such a good account. Mrs. Weston told me you were here.—Oh! then, said I, I must run across, I am sure Miss Woodhouse will allow me just to run across and entreat her to come in; my mother will be so very happy to see her—and now we are such a nice party, she cannot refuse.—‘Aye, pray do,’ said Mr. Frank Churchill, ‘Miss Woodhouse’s opinion of the instrument will be worth having.’—But, said I, I shall be more sure of succeeding if one of you will go with me.—‘Oh,’ said he, ‘wait half a minute, till I have finished my job;’—For, would you believe it, Miss Woodhouse, there he is, in the most obliging manner in the world, fastening in the rivet of my mother’s spectacles.—The rivet came out, you know, this morning.—So very obliging!—For my mother had no use of her spectacles—could not put them on. And, by the bye, every body ought to have two pair of spectacles; they should indeed. Jane said so. I meant to take them over to John Saunders the first thing I did, but something or other hindered me all the morning; first one thing, then another, there is no saying what, you know. At one time Patty came to say she thought the kitchen chimney wanted sweeping. Oh, said I, Patty do not come with your bad news to me. Here is the rivet of your mistress’s spectacles out. Then the baked apples came home, Mrs. Wallis sent them by her boy; they are extremely civil and obliging to us, the Wallises, always—I have heard some people say that Mrs. Wallis can be uncivil and give a very rude answer, but we have never known any thing but the greatest attention from them. And it cannot be for the value of our custom now, for what is our consumption of bread, you know? Only three of us.—besides dear Jane at present—and she really eats nothing—makes such a shocking breakfast, you would be quite frightened if you saw it. I dare not let my mother know how little she eats—so I say one thing and then I say another, and it passes off. But about the middle of the day she gets hungry, and there is nothing she likes so well as these baked apples, and they are extremely wholesome, for I took the opportunity the other day of asking Mr. Perry; I happened to meet him in the street. Not that I had any doubt before—I have so often heard Mr. Woodhouse recommend a baked apple. I believe it is the only way that Mr. Woodhouse thinks the fruit thoroughly wholesome. We have apple-dumplings, however, very often. Patty makes an excellent apple-dumpling. Well, Mrs. Weston, you have prevailed, I hope, and these ladies will oblige us.”\n\nEmma would be “very happy to wait on Mrs. Bates, &c.,” and they did at last move out of the shop, with no farther delay from Miss Bates than,\n\n“How do you do, Mrs. Ford? I beg your pardon. I did not see you before. I hear you have a charming collection of new ribbons from town. Jane came back delighted yesterday. Thank ye, the gloves do very well—only a little too large about the wrist; but Jane is taking them in.”\n\n“What was I talking of?” said she, beginning again when they were all in the street.\n\nEmma wondered on what, of all the medley, she would fix.\n\n“I declare I cannot recollect what I was talking of.—Oh! my mother’s spectacles. So very obliging of Mr. Frank Churchill! ‘Oh!’ said he, ‘I do think I can fasten the rivet; I like a job of this kind excessively.’—Which you know shewed him to be so very.... Indeed I must say that, much as I had heard of him before and much as I had expected, he very far exceeds any thing.... I do congratulate you, Mrs. Weston, most warmly. He seems every thing the fondest parent could.... ‘Oh!’ said he, ‘I can fasten the rivet. I like a job of that sort excessively.’ I never shall forget his manner. And when I brought out the baked apples from the closet, and hoped our friends would be so very obliging as to take some, ‘Oh!’ said he directly, ‘there is nothing in the way of fruit half so good, and these are the finest-looking home-baked apples I ever saw in my life.’ That, you know, was so very.... And I am sure, by his manner, it was no compliment. Indeed they are very delightful apples, and Mrs. Wallis does them full justice—only we do not have them baked more than twice, and Mr. Woodhouse made us promise to have them done three times—but Miss Woodhouse will be so good as not to mention it. The apples themselves are the very finest sort for baking, beyond a doubt; all from Donwell—some of Mr. Knightley’s most liberal supply. He sends us a sack every year; and certainly there never was such a keeping apple anywhere as one of his trees—I believe there is two of them. My mother says the orchard was always famous in her younger days. But I was really quite shocked the other day—for Mr. Knightley called one morning, and Jane was eating these apples, and we talked about them and said how much she enjoyed them, and he asked whether we were not got to the end of our stock. ‘I am sure you must be,’ said he, ‘and I will send you another supply; for I have a great many more than I can ever use. William Larkins let me keep a larger quantity than usual this year. I will send you some more, before they get good for nothing.’ So I begged he would not—for really as to ours being gone, I could not absolutely say that we had a great many left—it was but half a dozen indeed; but they should be all kept for Jane; and I could not at all bear that he should be sending us more, so liberal as he had been already; and Jane said the same. And when he was gone, she almost quarrelled with me—No, I should not say quarrelled, for we never had a quarrel in our lives; but she was quite distressed that I had owned the apples were so nearly gone; she wished I had made him believe we had a great many left. Oh, said I, my dear, I did say as much as I could. However, the very same evening William Larkins came over with a large basket of apples, the same sort of apples, a bushel at least, and I was very much obliged, and went down and spoke to William Larkins and said every thing, as you may suppose. William Larkins is such an old acquaintance! I am always glad to see him. But, however, I found afterwards from Patty, that William said it was all the apples of that sort his master had; he had brought them all—and now his master had not one left to bake or boil. William did not seem to mind it himself, he was so pleased to think his master had sold so many; for William, you know, thinks more of his master’s profit than any thing; but Mrs. Hodges, he said, was quite displeased at their being all sent away. She could not bear that her master should not be able to have another apple-tart this spring. He told Patty this, but bid her not mind it, and be sure not to say any thing to us about it, for Mrs. Hodges would be cross sometimes, and as long as so many sacks were sold, it did not signify who ate the remainder. And so Patty told me, and I was excessively shocked indeed! I would not have Mr. Knightley know any thing about it for the world! He would be so very.... I wanted to keep it from Jane’s knowledge; but, unluckily, I had mentioned it before I was aware.”\n\nMiss Bates had just done as Patty opened the door; and her visitors walked upstairs without having any regular narration to attend to, pursued only by the sounds of her desultory good-will.\n\n“Pray take care, Mrs. Weston, there is a step at the turning. Pray take care, Miss Woodhouse, ours is rather a dark staircase—rather darker and narrower than one could wish. Miss Smith, pray take care. Miss Woodhouse, I am quite concerned, I am sure you hit your foot. Miss Smith, the step at the turning.”\n\n# Volume II — Chapter X\n\nThe appearance of the little sitting-room as they entered, was tranquillity itself; Mrs. Bates, deprived of her usual employment, slumbering on one side of the fire, Frank Churchill, at a table near her, most deedily occupied about her spectacles, and Jane Fairfax, standing with her back to them, intent on her pianoforte.\n\nBusy as he was, however, the young man was yet able to shew a most happy countenance on seeing Emma again.\n\n“This is a pleasure,” said he, in rather a low voice, “coming at least ten minutes earlier than I had calculated. You find me trying to be useful; tell me if you think I shall succeed.”\n\n“What!” said Mrs. Weston, “have not you finished it yet? you would not earn a very good livelihood as a working silversmith at this rate.”\n\n“I have not been working uninterruptedly,” he replied, “I have been assisting Miss Fairfax in trying to make her instrument stand steadily, it was not quite firm; an unevenness in the floor, I believe. You see we have been wedging one leg with paper. This was very kind of you to be persuaded to come. I was almost afraid you would be hurrying home.”\n\nHe contrived that she should be seated by him; and was sufficiently employed in looking out the best baked apple for her, and trying to make her help or advise him in his work, till Jane Fairfax was quite ready to sit down to the pianoforte again. That she was not immediately ready, Emma did suspect to arise from the state of her nerves; she had not yet possessed the instrument long enough to touch it without emotion; she must reason herself into the power of performance; and Emma could not but pity such feelings, whatever their origin, and could not but resolve never to expose them to her neighbour again.\n\nAt last Jane began, and though the first bars were feebly given, the powers of the instrument were gradually done full justice to. Mrs. Weston had been delighted before, and was delighted again; Emma joined her in all her praise; and the pianoforte, with every proper discrimination, was pronounced to be altogether of the highest promise.\n\n“Whoever Colonel Campbell might employ,” said Frank Churchill, with a smile at Emma, “the person has not chosen ill. I heard a good deal of Colonel Campbell’s taste at Weymouth; and the softness of the upper notes I am sure is exactly what he and all that party would particularly prize. I dare say, Miss Fairfax, that he either gave his friend very minute directions, or wrote to Broadwood himself. Do not you think so?”\n\nJane did not look round. She was not obliged to hear. Mrs. Weston had been speaking to her at the same moment.\n\n“It is not fair,” said Emma, in a whisper; “mine was a random guess. Do not distress her.”\n\nHe shook his head with a smile, and looked as if he had very little doubt and very little mercy. Soon afterwards he began again,\n\n“How much your friends in Ireland must be enjoying your pleasure on this occasion, Miss Fairfax. I dare say they often think of you, and wonder which will be the day, the precise day of the instrument’s coming to hand. Do you imagine Colonel Campbell knows the business to be going forward just at this time?—Do you imagine it to be the consequence of an immediate commission from him, or that he may have sent only a general direction, an order indefinite as to time, to depend upon contingencies and conveniences?”\n\nHe paused. She could not but hear; she could not avoid answering,\n\n“Till I have a letter from Colonel Campbell,” said she, in a voice of forced calmness, “I can imagine nothing with any confidence. It must be all conjecture.”\n\n“Conjecture—aye, sometimes one conjectures right, and sometimes one conjectures wrong. I wish I could conjecture how soon I shall make this rivet quite firm. What nonsense one talks, Miss Woodhouse, when hard at work, if one talks at all;—your real workmen, I suppose, hold their tongues; but we gentlemen labourers if we get hold of a word—Miss Fairfax said something about conjecturing. There, it is done. I have the pleasure, madam, (to Mrs. Bates,) of restoring your spectacles, healed for the present.”\n\nHe was very warmly thanked both by mother and daughter; to escape a little from the latter, he went to the pianoforte, and begged Miss Fairfax, who was still sitting at it, to play something more.\n\n“If you are very kind,” said he, “it will be one of the waltzes we danced last night;—let me live them over again. You did not enjoy them as I did; you appeared tired the whole time. I believe you were glad we danced no longer; but I would have given worlds—all the worlds one ever has to give—for another half-hour.”\n\nShe played.\n\n“What felicity it is to hear a tune again which has made one happy!—If I mistake not that was danced at Weymouth.”\n\nShe looked up at him for a moment, coloured deeply, and played something else. He took some music from a chair near the pianoforte, and turning to Emma, said,\n\n“Here is something quite new to me. Do you know it?—Cramer.—And here are a new set of Irish melodies. That, from such a quarter, one might expect. This was all sent with the instrument. Very thoughtful of Colonel Campbell, was not it?—He knew Miss Fairfax could have no music here. I honour that part of the attention particularly; it shews it to have been so thoroughly from the heart. Nothing hastily done; nothing incomplete. True affection only could have prompted it.”\n\nEmma wished he would be less pointed, yet could not help being amused; and when on glancing her eye towards Jane Fairfax she caught the remains of a smile, when she saw that with all the deep blush of consciousness, there had been a smile of secret delight, she had less scruple in the amusement, and much less compunction with respect to her.—This amiable, upright, perfect Jane Fairfax was apparently cherishing very reprehensible feelings.\n\nHe brought all the music to her, and they looked it over together.—Emma took the opportunity of whispering,\n\n“You speak too plain. She must understand you.”\n\n“I hope she does. I would have her understand me. I am not in the least ashamed of my meaning.”\n\n“But really, I am half ashamed, and wish I had never taken up the idea.”\n\n“I am very glad you did, and that you communicated it to me. I have now a key to all her odd looks and ways. Leave shame to her. If she does wrong, she ought to feel it.”\n\n“She is not entirely without it, I think.”\n\n“I do not see much sign of it. She is playing Robin Adair at this moment—his favourite.”\n\nShortly afterwards Miss Bates, passing near the window, descried Mr. Knightley on horse-back not far off.\n\n“Mr. Knightley I declare!—I must speak to him if possible, just to thank him. I will not open the window here; it would give you all cold; but I can go into my mother’s room you know. I dare say he will come in when he knows who is here. Quite delightful to have you all meet so!—Our little room so honoured!”\n\nShe was in the adjoining chamber while she still spoke, and opening the casement there, immediately called Mr. Knightley’s attention, and every syllable of their conversation was as distinctly heard by the others, as if it had passed within the same apartment.\n\n“How d’ ye do?—how d’ye do?—Very well, I thank you. So obliged to you for the carriage last night. We were just in time; my mother just ready for us. Pray come in; do come in. You will find some friends here.”\n\nSo began Miss Bates; and Mr. Knightley seemed determined to be heard in his turn, for most resolutely and commandingly did he say,\n\n“How is your niece, Miss Bates?—I want to inquire after you all, but particularly your niece. How is Miss Fairfax?—I hope she caught no cold last night. How is she to-day? Tell me how Miss Fairfax is.”\n\nAnd Miss Bates was obliged to give a direct answer before he would hear her in any thing else. The listeners were amused; and Mrs. Weston gave Emma a look of particular meaning. But Emma still shook her head in steady scepticism.\n\n“So obliged to you!—so very much obliged to you for the carriage,” resumed Miss Bates.\n\nHe cut her short with,\n\n“I am going to Kingston. Can I do any thing for you?”\n\n“Oh! dear, Kingston—are you?—Mrs. Cole was saying the other day she wanted something from Kingston.”\n\n“Mrs. Cole has servants to send. Can I do any thing for you?”\n\n“No, I thank you. But do come in. Who do you think is here?—Miss Woodhouse and Miss Smith; so kind as to call to hear the new pianoforte. Do put up your horse at the Crown, and come in.”\n\n“Well,” said he, in a deliberating manner, “for five minutes, perhaps.”\n\n“And here is Mrs. Weston and Mr. Frank Churchill too!—Quite delightful; so many friends!”\n\n“No, not now, I thank you. I could not stay two minutes. I must get on to Kingston as fast as I can.”\n\n“Oh! do come in. They will be so very happy to see you.”\n\n“No, no; your room is full enough. I will call another day, and hear the pianoforte.”\n\n“Well, I am so sorry!—Oh! Mr. Knightley, what a delightful party last night; how extremely pleasant.—Did you ever see such dancing?—Was not it delightful?—Miss Woodhouse and Mr. Frank Churchill; I never saw any thing equal to it.”\n\n“Oh! very delightful indeed; I can say nothing less, for I suppose Miss Woodhouse and Mr. Frank Churchill are hearing every thing that passes. And (raising his voice still more) I do not see why Miss Fairfax should not be mentioned too. I think Miss Fairfax dances very well; and Mrs. Weston is the very best country-dance player, without exception, in England. Now, if your friends have any gratitude, they will say something pretty loud about you and me in return; but I cannot stay to hear it.”\n\n“Oh! Mr. Knightley, one moment more; something of consequence—so shocked!—Jane and I are both so shocked about the apples!”\n\n“What is the matter now?”\n\n“To think of your sending us all your store apples. You said you had a great many, and now you have not one left. We really are so shocked! Mrs. Hodges may well be angry. William Larkins mentioned it here. You should not have done it, indeed you should not. Ah! he is off. He never can bear to be thanked. But I thought he would have staid now, and it would have been a pity not to have mentioned.... Well, (returning to the room,) I have not been able to succeed. Mr. Knightley cannot stop. He is going to Kingston. He asked me if he could do any thing....”\n\n“Yes,” said Jane, “we heard his kind offers, we heard every thing.”\n\n“Oh! yes, my dear, I dare say you might, because you know, the door was open, and the window was open, and Mr. Knightley spoke loud. You must have heard every thing to be sure. ‘Can I do any thing for you at Kingston?’ said he; so I just mentioned.... Oh! Miss Woodhouse, must you be going?—You seem but just come—so very obliging of you.”\n\nEmma found it really time to be at home; the visit had already lasted long; and on examining watches, so much of the morning was perceived to be gone, that Mrs. Weston and her companion taking leave also, could allow themselves only to walk with the two young ladies to Hartfield gates, before they set off for Randalls.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter XI\n\nIt may be possible to do without dancing entirely. Instances have been known of young people passing many, many months successively, without being at any ball of any description, and no material injury accrue either to body or mind;—but when a beginning is made—when the felicities of rapid motion have once been, though slightly, felt—it must be a very heavy set that does not ask for more.\n\nFrank Churchill had danced once at Highbury, and longed to dance again; and the last half-hour of an evening which Mr. Woodhouse was persuaded to spend with his daughter at Randalls, was passed by the two young people in schemes on the subject. Frank’s was the first idea; and his the greatest zeal in pursuing it; for the lady was the best judge of the difficulties, and the most solicitous for accommodation and appearance. But still she had inclination enough for shewing people again how delightfully Mr. Frank Churchill and Miss Woodhouse danced—for doing that in which she need not blush to compare herself with Jane Fairfax—and even for simple dancing itself, without any of the wicked aids of vanity—to assist him first in pacing out the room they were in to see what it could be made to hold—and then in taking the dimensions of the other parlour, in the hope of discovering, in spite of all that Mr. Weston could say of their exactly equal size, that it was a little the largest.\n\nHis first proposition and request, that the dance begun at Mr. Cole’s should be finished there—that the same party should be collected, and the same musician engaged, met with the readiest acquiescence. Mr. Weston entered into the idea with thorough enjoyment, and Mrs. Weston most willingly undertook to play as long as they could wish to dance; and the interesting employment had followed, of reckoning up exactly who there would be, and portioning out the indispensable division of space to every couple.\n\n“You and Miss Smith, and Miss Fairfax, will be three, and the two Miss Coxes five,” had been repeated many times over. “And there will be the two Gilberts, young Cox, my father, and myself, besides Mr. Knightley. Yes, that will be quite enough for pleasure. You and Miss Smith, and Miss Fairfax, will be three, and the two Miss Coxes five; and for five couple there will be plenty of room.”\n\nBut soon it came to be on one side,\n\n“But will there be good room for five couple?—I really do not think there will.”\n\nOn another,\n\n“And after all, five couple are not enough to make it worth while to stand up. Five couple are nothing, when one thinks seriously about it. It will not do to invite five couple. It can be allowable only as the thought of the moment.”\n\nSomebody said that Miss Gilbert was expected at her brother’s, and must be invited with the rest. Somebody else believed Mrs. Gilbert would have danced the other evening, if she had been asked. A word was put in for a second young Cox; and at last, Mr. Weston naming one family of cousins who must be included, and another of very old acquaintance who could not be left out, it became a certainty that the five couple would be at least ten, and a very interesting speculation in what possible manner they could be disposed of.\n\nThe doors of the two rooms were just opposite each other. “Might not they use both rooms, and dance across the passage?” It seemed the best scheme; and yet it was not so good but that many of them wanted a better. Emma said it would be awkward; Mrs. Weston was in distress about the supper; and Mr. Woodhouse opposed it earnestly, on the score of health. It made him so very unhappy, indeed, that it could not be persevered in.\n\n“Oh! no,” said he; “it would be the extreme of imprudence. I could not bear it for Emma!—Emma is not strong. She would catch a dreadful cold. So would poor little Harriet. So you would all. Mrs. Weston, you would be quite laid up; do not let them talk of such a wild thing. Pray do not let them talk of it. That young man (speaking lower) is very thoughtless. Do not tell his father, but that young man is not quite the thing. He has been opening the doors very often this evening, and keeping them open very inconsiderately. He does not think of the draught. I do not mean to set you against him, but indeed he is not quite the thing!”\n\nMrs. Weston was sorry for such a charge. She knew the importance of it, and said every thing in her power to do it away. Every door was now closed, the passage plan given up, and the first scheme of dancing only in the room they were in resorted to again; and with such good-will on Frank Churchill’s part, that the space which a quarter of an hour before had been deemed barely sufficient for five couple, was now endeavoured to be made out quite enough for ten.\n\n“We were too magnificent,” said he. “We allowed unnecessary room. Ten couple may stand here very well.”\n\nEmma demurred. “It would be a crowd—a sad crowd; and what could be worse than dancing without space to turn in?”\n\n“Very true,” he gravely replied; “it was very bad.” But still he went on measuring, and still he ended with,\n\n“I think there will be very tolerable room for ten couple.”\n\n“No, no,” said she, “you are quite unreasonable. It would be dreadful to be standing so close! Nothing can be farther from pleasure than to be dancing in a crowd—and a crowd in a little room!”\n\n“There is no denying it,” he replied. “I agree with you exactly. A crowd in a little room—Miss Woodhouse, you have the art of giving pictures in a few words. Exquisite, quite exquisite!—Still, however, having proceeded so far, one is unwilling to give the matter up. It would be a disappointment to my father—and altogether—I do not know that—I am rather of opinion that ten couple might stand here very well.”\n\nEmma perceived that the nature of his gallantry was a little self-willed, and that he would rather oppose than lose the pleasure of dancing with her; but she took the compliment, and forgave the rest. Had she intended ever to marry him, it might have been worth while to pause and consider, and try to understand the value of his preference, and the character of his temper; but for all the purposes of their acquaintance, he was quite amiable enough.\n\nBefore the middle of the next day, he was at Hartfield; and he entered the room with such an agreeable smile as certified the continuance of the scheme. It soon appeared that he came to announce an improvement.\n\n“Well, Miss Woodhouse,” he almost immediately began, “your inclination for dancing has not been quite frightened away, I hope, by the terrors of my father’s little rooms. I bring a new proposal on the subject:—a thought of my father’s, which waits only your approbation to be acted upon. May I hope for the honour of your hand for the two first dances of this little projected ball, to be given, not at Randalls, but at the Crown Inn?”\n\n“The Crown!”\n\n“Yes; if you and Mr. Woodhouse see no objection, and I trust you cannot, my father hopes his friends will be so kind as to visit him there. Better accommodations, he can promise them, and not a less grateful welcome than at Randalls. It is his own idea. Mrs. Weston sees no objection to it, provided you are satisfied. This is what we all feel. Oh! you were perfectly right! Ten couple, in either of the Randalls rooms, would have been insufferable!—Dreadful!—I felt how right you were the whole time, but was too anxious for securing any thing to like to yield. Is not it a good exchange?—You consent—I hope you consent?”\n\n“It appears to me a plan that nobody can object to, if Mr. and Mrs. Weston do not. I think it admirable; and, as far as I can answer for myself, shall be most happy—It seems the only improvement that could be. Papa, do you not think it an excellent improvement?”\n\nShe was obliged to repeat and explain it, before it was fully comprehended; and then, being quite new, farther representations were necessary to make it acceptable.\n\n“No; he thought it very far from an improvement—a very bad plan—much worse than the other. A room at an inn was always damp and dangerous; never properly aired, or fit to be inhabited. If they must dance, they had better dance at Randalls. He had never been in the room at the Crown in his life—did not know the people who kept it by sight.—Oh! no—a very bad plan. They would catch worse colds at the Crown than anywhere.”\n\n“I was going to observe, sir,” said Frank Churchill, “that one of the great recommendations of this change would be the very little danger of any body’s catching cold—so much less danger at the Crown than at Randalls! Mr. Perry might have reason to regret the alteration, but nobody else could.”\n\n“Sir,” said Mr. Woodhouse, rather warmly, “you are very much mistaken if you suppose Mr. Perry to be that sort of character. Mr. Perry is extremely concerned when any of us are ill. But I do not understand how the room at the Crown can be safer for you than your father’s house.”\n\n“From the very circumstance of its being larger, sir. We shall have no occasion to open the windows at all—not once the whole evening; and it is that dreadful habit of opening the windows, letting in cold air upon heated bodies, which (as you well know, sir) does the mischief.”\n\n“Open the windows!—but surely, Mr. Churchill, nobody would think of opening the windows at Randalls. Nobody could be so imprudent! I never heard of such a thing. Dancing with open windows!—I am sure, neither your father nor Mrs. Weston (poor Miss Taylor that was) would suffer it.”\n\n“Ah! sir—but a thoughtless young person will sometimes step behind a window-curtain, and throw up a sash, without its being suspected. I have often known it done myself.”\n\n“Have you indeed, sir?—Bless me! I never could have supposed it. But I live out of the world, and am often astonished at what I hear. However, this does make a difference; and, perhaps, when we come to talk it over—but these sort of things require a good deal of consideration. One cannot resolve upon them in a hurry. If Mr. and Mrs. Weston will be so obliging as to call here one morning, we may talk it over, and see what can be done.”\n\n“But, unfortunately, sir, my time is so limited—”\n\n“Oh!” interrupted Emma, “there will be plenty of time for talking every thing over. There is no hurry at all. If it can be contrived to be at the Crown, papa, it will be very convenient for the horses. They will be so near their own stable.”\n\n“So they will, my dear. That is a great thing. Not that James ever complains; but it is right to spare our horses when we can. If I could be sure of the rooms being thoroughly aired—but is Mrs. Stokes to be trusted? I doubt it. I do not know her, even by sight.”\n\n“I can answer for every thing of that nature, sir, because it will be under Mrs. Weston’s care. Mrs. Weston undertakes to direct the whole.”\n\n“There, papa!—Now you must be satisfied—Our own dear Mrs. Weston, who is carefulness itself. Do not you remember what Mr. Perry said, so many years ago, when I had the measles? ‘If Miss Taylor undertakes to wrap Miss Emma up, you need not have any fears, sir.’ How often have I heard you speak of it as such a compliment to her!”\n\n“Aye, very true. Mr. Perry did say so. I shall never forget it. Poor little Emma! You were very bad with the measles; that is, you would have been very bad, but for Perry’s great attention. He came four times a day for a week. He said, from the first, it was a very good sort—which was our great comfort; but the measles are a dreadful complaint. I hope whenever poor Isabella’s little ones have the measles, she will send for Perry.”\n\n“My father and Mrs. Weston are at the Crown at this moment,” said Frank Churchill, “examining the capabilities of the house. I left them there and came on to Hartfield, impatient for your opinion, and hoping you might be persuaded to join them and give your advice on the spot. I was desired to say so from both. It would be the greatest pleasure to them, if you could allow me to attend you there. They can do nothing satisfactorily without you.”\n\nEmma was most happy to be called to such a council; and her father, engaging to think it all over while she was gone, the two young people set off together without delay for the Crown. There were Mr. and Mrs. Weston; delighted to see her and receive her approbation, very busy and very happy in their different way; she, in some little distress; and he, finding every thing perfect.\n\n“Emma,” said she, “this paper is worse than I expected. Look! in places you see it is dreadfully dirty; and the wainscot is more yellow and forlorn than any thing I could have imagined.”\n\n“My dear, you are too particular,” said her husband. “What does all that signify? You will see nothing of it by candlelight. It will be as clean as Randalls by candlelight. We never see any thing of it on our club-nights.”\n\nThe ladies here probably exchanged looks which meant, “Men never know when things are dirty or not;” and the gentlemen perhaps thought each to himself, “Women will have their little nonsenses and needless cares.”\n\nOne perplexity, however, arose, which the gentlemen did not disdain. It regarded a supper-room. At the time of the ballroom’s being built, suppers had not been in question; and a small card-room adjoining, was the only addition. What was to be done? This card-room would be wanted as a card-room now; or, if cards were conveniently voted unnecessary by their four selves, still was it not too small for any comfortable supper? Another room of much better size might be secured for the purpose; but it was at the other end of the house, and a long awkward passage must be gone through to get at it. This made a difficulty. Mrs. Weston was afraid of draughts for the young people in that passage; and neither Emma nor the gentlemen could tolerate the prospect of being miserably crowded at supper.\n\nMrs. Weston proposed having no regular supper; merely sandwiches, &c., set out in the little room; but that was scouted as a wretched suggestion. A private dance, without sitting down to supper, was pronounced an infamous fraud upon the rights of men and women; and Mrs. Weston must not speak of it again. She then took another line of expediency, and looking into the doubtful room, observed,\n\n“I do not think it is so very small. We shall not be many, you know.”\n\nAnd Mr. Weston at the same time, walking briskly with long steps through the passage, was calling out,\n\n“You talk a great deal of the length of this passage, my dear. It is a mere nothing after all; and not the least draught from the stairs.”\n\n“I wish,” said Mrs. Weston, “one could know which arrangement our guests in general would like best. To do what would be most generally pleasing must be our object—if one could but tell what that would be.”\n\n“Yes, very true,” cried Frank, “very true. You want your neighbours’ opinions. I do not wonder at you. If one could ascertain what the chief of them—the Coles, for instance. They are not far off. Shall I call upon them? Or Miss Bates? She is still nearer.—And I do not know whether Miss Bates is not as likely to understand the inclinations of the rest of the people as any body. I think we do want a larger council. Suppose I go and invite Miss Bates to join us?”\n\n“Well—if you please,” said Mrs. Weston rather hesitating, “if you think she will be of any use.”\n\n“You will get nothing to the purpose from Miss Bates,” said Emma. “She will be all delight and gratitude, but she will tell you nothing. She will not even listen to your questions. I see no advantage in consulting Miss Bates.”\n\n“But she is so amusing, so extremely amusing! I am very fond of hearing Miss Bates talk. And I need not bring the whole family, you know.”\n\nHere Mr. Weston joined them, and on hearing what was proposed, gave it his decided approbation.\n\n“Aye, do, Frank.—Go and fetch Miss Bates, and let us end the matter at once. She will enjoy the scheme, I am sure; and I do not know a properer person for shewing us how to do away difficulties. Fetch Miss Bates. We are growing a little too nice. She is a standing lesson of how to be happy. But fetch them both. Invite them both.”\n\n“Both sir! Can the old lady?”...\n\n“The old lady! No, the young lady, to be sure. I shall think you a great blockhead, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.”\n\n“Oh! I beg your pardon, sir. I did not immediately recollect. Undoubtedly if you wish it, I will endeavour to persuade them both.” And away he ran.\n\nLong before he reappeared, attending the short, neat, brisk-moving aunt, and her elegant niece,—Mrs. Weston, like a sweet-tempered woman and a good wife, had examined the passage again, and found the evils of it much less than she had supposed before—indeed very trifling; and here ended the difficulties of decision. All the rest, in speculation at least, was perfectly smooth. All the minor arrangements of table and chair, lights and music, tea and supper, made themselves; or were left as mere trifles to be settled at any time between Mrs. Weston and Mrs. Stokes.—Every body invited, was certainly to come; Frank had already written to Enscombe to propose staying a few days beyond his fortnight, which could not possibly be refused. And a delightful dance it was to be.\n\nMost cordially, when Miss Bates arrived, did she agree that it must. As a counsellor she was not wanted; but as an approver, (a much safer character,) she was truly welcome. Her approbation, at once general and minute, warm and incessant, could not but please; and for another half-hour they were all walking to and fro, between the different rooms, some suggesting, some attending, and all in happy enjoyment of the future. The party did not break up without Emma’s being positively secured for the two first dances by the hero of the evening, nor without her overhearing Mr. Weston whisper to his wife, “He has asked her, my dear. That’s right. I knew he would!”\n\n# Volume II — Chapter XII\n\nOne thing only was wanting to make the prospect of the ball completely satisfactory to Emma—its being fixed for a day within the granted term of Frank Churchill’s stay in Surry; for, in spite of Mr. Weston’s confidence, she could not think it so very impossible that the Churchills might not allow their nephew to remain a day beyond his fortnight. But this was not judged feasible. The preparations must take their time, nothing could be properly ready till the third week were entered on, and for a few days they must be planning, proceeding and hoping in uncertainty—at the risk—in her opinion, the great risk, of its being all in vain.\n\nEnscombe however was gracious, gracious in fact, if not in word. His wish of staying longer evidently did not please; but it was not opposed. All was safe and prosperous; and as the removal of one solicitude generally makes way for another, Emma, being now certain of her ball, began to adopt as the next vexation Mr. Knightley’s provoking indifference about it. Either because he did not dance himself, or because the plan had been formed without his being consulted, he seemed resolved that it should not interest him, determined against its exciting any present curiosity, or affording him any future amusement. To her voluntary communications Emma could get no more approving reply, than,\n\n“Very well. If the Westons think it worth while to be at all this trouble for a few hours of noisy entertainment, I have nothing to say against it, but that they shall not chuse pleasures for me.—Oh! yes, I must be there; I could not refuse; and I will keep as much awake as I can; but I would rather be at home, looking over William Larkins’s week’s account; much rather, I confess.—Pleasure in seeing dancing!—not I, indeed—I never look at it—I do not know who does.—Fine dancing, I believe, like virtue, must be its own reward. Those who are standing by are usually thinking of something very different.”\n\nThis Emma felt was aimed at her; and it made her quite angry. It was not in compliment to Jane Fairfax however that he was so indifferent, or so indignant; he was not guided by her feelings in reprobating the ball, for she enjoyed the thought of it to an extraordinary degree. It made her animated—open hearted—she voluntarily said;—\n\n“Oh! Miss Woodhouse, I hope nothing may happen to prevent the ball. What a disappointment it would be! I do look forward to it, I own, with very great pleasure.”\n\nIt was not to oblige Jane Fairfax therefore that he would have preferred the society of William Larkins. No!—she was more and more convinced that Mrs. Weston was quite mistaken in that surmise. There was a great deal of friendly and of compassionate attachment on his side—but no love.\n\nAlas! there was soon no leisure for quarrelling with Mr. Knightley. Two days of joyful security were immediately followed by the over-throw of every thing. A letter arrived from Mr. Churchill to urge his nephew’s instant return. Mrs. Churchill was unwell—far too unwell to do without him; she had been in a very suffering state (so said her husband) when writing to her nephew two days before, though from her usual unwillingness to give pain, and constant habit of never thinking of herself, she had not mentioned it; but now she was too ill to trifle, and must entreat him to set off for Enscombe without delay.\n\nThe substance of this letter was forwarded to Emma, in a note from Mrs. Weston, instantly. As to his going, it was inevitable. He must be gone within a few hours, though without feeling any real alarm for his aunt, to lessen his repugnance. He knew her illnesses; they never occurred but for her own convenience.\n\nMrs. Weston added, “that he could only allow himself time to hurry to Highbury, after breakfast, and take leave of the few friends there whom he could suppose to feel any interest in him; and that he might be expected at Hartfield very soon.”\n\nThis wretched note was the finale of Emma’s breakfast. When once it had been read, there was no doing any thing, but lament and exclaim. The loss of the ball—the loss of the young man—and all that the young man might be feeling!—It was too wretched!—Such a delightful evening as it would have been!—Every body so happy! and she and her partner the happiest!—“I said it would be so,” was the only consolation.\n\nHer father’s feelings were quite distinct. He thought principally of Mrs. Churchill’s illness, and wanted to know how she was treated; and as for the ball, it was shocking to have dear Emma disappointed; but they would all be safer at home.\n\nEmma was ready for her visitor some time before he appeared; but if this reflected at all upon his impatience, his sorrowful look and total want of spirits when he did come might redeem him. He felt the going away almost too much to speak of it. His dejection was most evident. He sat really lost in thought for the first few minutes; and when rousing himself, it was only to say,\n\n“Of all horrid things, leave-taking is the worst.”\n\n“But you will come again,” said Emma. “This will not be your only visit to Randalls.”\n\n“Ah!—(shaking his head)—the uncertainty of when I may be able to return!—I shall try for it with a zeal!—It will be the object of all my thoughts and cares!—and if my uncle and aunt go to town this spring—but I am afraid—they did not stir last spring—I am afraid it is a custom gone for ever.”\n\n“Our poor ball must be quite given up.”\n\n“Ah! that ball!—why did we wait for any thing?—why not seize the pleasure at once?—How often is happiness destroyed by preparation, foolish preparation!—You told us it would be so.—Oh! Miss Woodhouse, why are you always so right?”\n\n“Indeed, I am very sorry to be right in this instance. I would much rather have been merry than wise.”\n\n“If I can come again, we are still to have our ball. My father depends on it. Do not forget your engagement.”\n\nEmma looked graciously.\n\n“Such a fortnight as it has been!” he continued; “every day more precious and more delightful than the day before!—every day making me less fit to bear any other place. Happy those, who can remain at Highbury!”\n\n“As you do us such ample justice now,” said Emma, laughing, “I will venture to ask, whether you did not come a little doubtfully at first? Do not we rather surpass your expectations? I am sure we do. I am sure you did not much expect to like us. You would not have been so long in coming, if you had had a pleasant idea of Highbury.”\n\nHe laughed rather consciously; and though denying the sentiment, Emma was convinced that it had been so.\n\n“And you must be off this very morning?”\n\n“Yes; my father is to join me here: we shall walk back together, and I must be off immediately. I am almost afraid that every moment will bring him.”\n\n“Not five minutes to spare even for your friends Miss Fairfax and Miss Bates? How unlucky! Miss Bates’s powerful, argumentative mind might have strengthened yours.”\n\n“Yes—I have called there; passing the door, I thought it better. It was a right thing to do. I went in for three minutes, and was detained by Miss Bates’s being absent. She was out; and I felt it impossible not to wait till she came in. She is a woman that one may, that one must laugh at; but that one would not wish to slight. It was better to pay my visit, then”—\n\nHe hesitated, got up, walked to a window.\n\n“In short,” said he, “perhaps, Miss Woodhouse—I think you can hardly be quite without suspicion”—\n\nHe looked at her, as if wanting to read her thoughts. She hardly knew what to say. It seemed like the forerunner of something absolutely serious, which she did not wish. Forcing herself to speak, therefore, in the hope of putting it by, she calmly said,\n\n“You are quite in the right; it was most natural to pay your visit, then”—\n\nHe was silent. She believed he was looking at her; probably reflecting on what she had said, and trying to understand the manner. She heard him sigh. It was natural for him to feel that he had cause to sigh. He could not believe her to be encouraging him. A few awkward moments passed, and he sat down again; and in a more determined manner said,\n\n“It was something to feel that all the rest of my time might be given to Hartfield. My regard for Hartfield is most warm”—\n\nHe stopt again, rose again, and seemed quite embarrassed.—He was more in love with her than Emma had supposed; and who can say how it might have ended, if his father had not made his appearance? Mr. Woodhouse soon followed; and the necessity of exertion made him composed.\n\nA very few minutes more, however, completed the present trial. Mr. Weston, always alert when business was to be done, and as incapable of procrastinating any evil that was inevitable, as of foreseeing any that was doubtful, said, “It was time to go;” and the young man, though he might and did sigh, could not but agree, to take leave.\n\n“I shall hear about you all,” said he; “that is my chief consolation. I shall hear of every thing that is going on among you. I have engaged Mrs. Weston to correspond with me. She has been so kind as to promise it. Oh! the blessing of a female correspondent, when one is really interested in the absent!—she will tell me every thing. In her letters I shall be at dear Highbury again.”\n\nA very friendly shake of the hand, a very earnest “Good-bye,” closed the speech, and the door had soon shut out Frank Churchill. Short had been the notice—short their meeting; he was gone; and Emma felt so sorry to part, and foresaw so great a loss to their little society from his absence as to begin to be afraid of being too sorry, and feeling it too much.\n\nIt was a sad change. They had been meeting almost every day since his arrival. Certainly his being at Randalls had given great spirit to the last two weeks—indescribable spirit; the idea, the expectation of seeing him which every morning had brought, the assurance of his attentions, his liveliness, his manners! It had been a very happy fortnight, and forlorn must be the sinking from it into the common course of Hartfield days. To complete every other recommendation, he had almost told her that he loved her. What strength, or what constancy of affection he might be subject to, was another point; but at present she could not doubt his having a decidedly warm admiration, a conscious preference of herself; and this persuasion, joined to all the rest, made her think that she must be a little in love with him, in spite of every previous determination against it.\n\n“I certainly must,” said she. “This sensation of listlessness, weariness, stupidity, this disinclination to sit down and employ myself, this feeling of every thing’s being dull and insipid about the house!— I must be in love; I should be the oddest creature in the world if I were not—for a few weeks at least. Well! evil to some is always good to others. I shall have many fellow-mourners for the ball, if not for Frank Churchill; but Mr. Knightley will be happy. He may spend the evening with his dear William Larkins now if he likes.”\n\nMr. Knightley, however, shewed no triumphant happiness. He could not say that he was sorry on his own account; his very cheerful look would have contradicted him if he had; but he said, and very steadily, that he was sorry for the disappointment of the others, and with considerable kindness added,\n\n“You, Emma, who have so few opportunities of dancing, you are really out of luck; you are very much out of luck!”\n\nIt was some days before she saw Jane Fairfax, to judge of her honest regret in this woeful change; but when they did meet, her composure was odious. She had been particularly unwell, however, suffering from headache to a degree, which made her aunt declare, that had the ball taken place, she did not think Jane could have attended it; and it was charity to impute some of her unbecoming indifference to the languor of ill-health.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter XIII\n\nEmma continued to entertain no doubt of her being in love. Her ideas only varied as to the how much. At first, she thought it was a good deal; and afterwards, but little. She had great pleasure in hearing Frank Churchill talked of; and, for his sake, greater pleasure than ever in seeing Mr. and Mrs. Weston; she was very often thinking of him, and quite impatient for a letter, that she might know how he was, how were his spirits, how was his aunt, and what was the chance of his coming to Randalls again this spring. But, on the other hand, she could not admit herself to be unhappy, nor, after the first morning, to be less disposed for employment than usual; she was still busy and cheerful; and, pleasing as he was, she could yet imagine him to have faults; and farther, though thinking of him so much, and, as she sat drawing or working, forming a thousand amusing schemes for the progress and close of their attachment, fancying interesting dialogues, and inventing elegant letters; the conclusion of every imaginary declaration on his side was that she refused him. Their affection was always to subside into friendship. Every thing tender and charming was to mark their parting; but still they were to part. When she became sensible of this, it struck her that she could not be very much in love; for in spite of her previous and fixed determination never to quit her father, never to marry, a strong attachment certainly must produce more of a struggle than she could foresee in her own feelings.\n\n“I do not find myself making any use of the word sacrifice,” said she.—“In not one of all my clever replies, my delicate negatives, is there any allusion to making a sacrifice. I do suspect that he is not really necessary to my happiness. So much the better. I certainly will not persuade myself to feel more than I do. I am quite enough in love. I should be sorry to be more.”\n\nUpon the whole, she was equally contented with her view of his feelings.\n\n“He is undoubtedly very much in love—every thing denotes it—very much in love indeed!—and when he comes again, if his affection continue, I must be on my guard not to encourage it.—It would be most inexcusable to do otherwise, as my own mind is quite made up. Not that I imagine he can think I have been encouraging him hitherto. No, if he had believed me at all to share his feelings, he would not have been so wretched. Could he have thought himself encouraged, his looks and language at parting would have been different.—Still, however, I must be on my guard. This is in the supposition of his attachment continuing what it now is; but I do not know that I expect it will; I do not look upon him to be quite the sort of man—I do not altogether build upon his steadiness or constancy.—His feelings are warm, but I can imagine them rather changeable.—Every consideration of the subject, in short, makes me thankful that my happiness is not more deeply involved.—I shall do very well again after a little while—and then, it will be a good thing over; for they say every body is in love once in their lives, and I shall have been let off easily.”\n\nWhen his letter to Mrs. Weston arrived, Emma had the perusal of it; and she read it with a degree of pleasure and admiration which made her at first shake her head over her own sensations, and think she had undervalued their strength. It was a long, well-written letter, giving the particulars of his journey and of his feelings, expressing all the affection, gratitude, and respect which was natural and honourable, and describing every thing exterior and local that could be supposed attractive, with spirit and precision. No suspicious flourishes now of apology or concern; it was the language of real feeling towards Mrs. Weston; and the transition from Highbury to Enscombe, the contrast between the places in some of the first blessings of social life was just enough touched on to shew how keenly it was felt, and how much more might have been said but for the restraints of propriety.—The charm of her own name was not wanting. Miss Woodhouse appeared more than once, and never without a something of pleasing connexion, either a compliment to her taste, or a remembrance of what she had said; and in the very last time of its meeting her eye, unadorned as it was by any such broad wreath of gallantry, she yet could discern the effect of her influence and acknowledge the greatest compliment perhaps of all conveyed. Compressed into the very lowest vacant corner were these words—“I had not a spare moment on Tuesday, as you know, for Miss Woodhouse’s beautiful little friend. Pray make my excuses and adieus to her.” This, Emma could not doubt, was all for herself. Harriet was remembered only from being her friend. His information and prospects as to Enscombe were neither worse nor better than had been anticipated; Mrs. Churchill was recovering, and he dared not yet, even in his own imagination, fix a time for coming to Randalls again.\n\nGratifying, however, and stimulative as was the letter in the material part, its sentiments, she yet found, when it was folded up and returned to Mrs. Weston, that it had not added any lasting warmth, that she could still do without the writer, and that he must learn to do without her. Her intentions were unchanged. Her resolution of refusal only grew more interesting by the addition of a scheme for his subsequent consolation and happiness. His recollection of Harriet, and the words which clothed it, the “beautiful little friend,” suggested to her the idea of Harriet’s succeeding her in his affections. Was it impossible?—No.—Harriet undoubtedly was greatly his inferior in understanding; but he had been very much struck with the loveliness of her face and the warm simplicity of her manner; and all the probabilities of circumstance and connexion were in her favour.—For Harriet, it would be advantageous and delightful indeed.\n\n“I must not dwell upon it,” said she.—“I must not think of it. I know the danger of indulging such speculations. But stranger things have happened; and when we cease to care for each other as we do now, it will be the means of confirming us in that sort of true disinterested friendship which I can already look forward to with pleasure.”\n\nIt was well to have a comfort in store on Harriet’s behalf, though it might be wise to let the fancy touch it seldom; for evil in that quarter was at hand. As Frank Churchill’s arrival had succeeded Mr. Elton’s engagement in the conversation of Highbury, as the latest interest had entirely borne down the first, so now upon Frank Churchill’s disappearance, Mr. Elton’s concerns were assuming the most irresistible form.—His wedding-day was named. He would soon be among them again; Mr. Elton and his bride. There was hardly time to talk over the first letter from Enscombe before “Mr. Elton and his bride” was in every body’s mouth, and Frank Churchill was forgotten. Emma grew sick at the sound. She had had three weeks of happy exemption from Mr. Elton; and Harriet’s mind, she had been willing to hope, had been lately gaining strength. With Mr. Weston’s ball in view at least, there had been a great deal of insensibility to other things; but it was now too evident that she had not attained such a state of composure as could stand against the actual approach—new carriage, bell-ringing, and all.\n\nPoor Harriet was in a flutter of spirits which required all the reasonings and soothings and attentions of every kind that Emma could give. Emma felt that she could not do too much for her, that Harriet had a right to all her ingenuity and all her patience; but it was heavy work to be for ever convincing without producing any effect, for ever agreed to, without being able to make their opinions the same. Harriet listened submissively, and said “it was very true—it was just as Miss Woodhouse described—it was not worth while to think about them—and she would not think about them any longer” but no change of subject could avail, and the next half-hour saw her as anxious and restless about the Eltons as before. At last Emma attacked her on another ground.\n\n“Your allowing yourself to be so occupied and so unhappy about Mr. Elton’s marrying, Harriet, is the strongest reproach you can make me. You could not give me a greater reproof for the mistake I fell into. It was all my doing, I know. I have not forgotten it, I assure you.—Deceived myself, I did very miserably deceive you—and it will be a painful reflection to me for ever. Do not imagine me in danger of forgetting it.”\n\nHarriet felt this too much to utter more than a few words of eager exclamation. Emma continued,\n\n“I have not said, exert yourself Harriet for my sake; think less, talk less of Mr. Elton for my sake; because for your own sake rather, I would wish it to be done, for the sake of what is more important than my comfort, a habit of self-command in you, a consideration of what is your duty, an attention to propriety, an endeavour to avoid the suspicions of others, to save your health and credit, and restore your tranquillity. These are the motives which I have been pressing on you. They are very important—and sorry I am that you cannot feel them sufficiently to act upon them. My being saved from pain is a very secondary consideration. I want you to save yourself from greater pain. Perhaps I may sometimes have felt that Harriet would not forget what was due—or rather what would be kind by me.”\n\nThis appeal to her affections did more than all the rest. The idea of wanting gratitude and consideration for Miss Woodhouse, whom she really loved extremely, made her wretched for a while, and when the violence of grief was comforted away, still remained powerful enough to prompt to what was right and support her in it very tolerably.\n\n“You, who have been the best friend I ever had in my life—Want gratitude to you!—Nobody is equal to you!—I care for nobody as I do for you!—Oh! Miss Woodhouse, how ungrateful I have been!”\n\nSuch expressions, assisted as they were by every thing that look and manner could do, made Emma feel that she had never loved Harriet so well, nor valued her affection so highly before.\n\n“There is no charm equal to tenderness of heart,” said she afterwards to herself. “There is nothing to be compared to it. Warmth and tenderness of heart, with an affectionate, open manner, will beat all the clearness of head in the world, for attraction, I am sure it will. It is tenderness of heart which makes my dear father so generally beloved—which gives Isabella all her popularity.—I have it not—but I know how to prize and respect it.—Harriet is my superior in all the charm and all the felicity it gives. Dear Harriet!—I would not change you for the clearest-headed, longest-sighted, best-judging female breathing. Oh! the coldness of a Jane Fairfax!—Harriet is worth a hundred such—And for a wife—a sensible man’s wife—it is invaluable. I mention no names; but happy the man who changes Emma for Harriet!”\n\n# Volume II — Chapter XIV\n\nMrs. Elton was first seen at church: but though devotion might be interrupted, curiosity could not be satisfied by a bride in a pew, and it must be left for the visits in form which were then to be paid, to settle whether she were very pretty indeed, or only rather pretty, or not pretty at all.\n\nEmma had feelings, less of curiosity than of pride or propriety, to make her resolve on not being the last to pay her respects; and she made a point of Harriet’s going with her, that the worst of the business might be gone through as soon as possible.\n\nShe could not enter the house again, could not be in the same room to which she had with such vain artifice retreated three months ago, to lace up her boot, without recollecting. A thousand vexatious thoughts would recur. Compliments, charades, and horrible blunders; and it was not to be supposed that poor Harriet should not be recollecting too; but she behaved very well, and was only rather pale and silent. The visit was of course short; and there was so much embarrassment and occupation of mind to shorten it, that Emma would not allow herself entirely to form an opinion of the lady, and on no account to give one, beyond the nothing-meaning terms of being “elegantly dressed, and very pleasing.”\n\nShe did not really like her. She would not be in a hurry to find fault, but she suspected that there was no elegance;—ease, but not elegance.— She was almost sure that for a young woman, a stranger, a bride, there was too much ease. Her person was rather good; her face not unpretty; but neither feature, nor air, nor voice, nor manner, were elegant. Emma thought at least it would turn out so.\n\nAs for Mr. Elton, his manners did not appear—but no, she would not permit a hasty or a witty word from herself about his manners. It was an awkward ceremony at any time to be receiving wedding visits, and a man had need be all grace to acquit himself well through it. The woman was better off; she might have the assistance of fine clothes, and the privilege of bashfulness, but the man had only his own good sense to depend on; and when she considered how peculiarly unlucky poor Mr. Elton was in being in the same room at once with the woman he had just married, the woman he had wanted to marry, and the woman whom he had been expected to marry, she must allow him to have the right to look as little wise, and to be as much affectedly, and as little really easy as could be.\n\n“Well, Miss Woodhouse,” said Harriet, when they had quitted the house, and after waiting in vain for her friend to begin; “Well, Miss Woodhouse, (with a gentle sigh,) what do you think of her?—Is not she very charming?”\n\nThere was a little hesitation in Emma’s answer.\n\n“Oh! yes—very—a very pleasing young woman.”\n\n“I think her beautiful, quite beautiful.”\n\n“Very nicely dressed, indeed; a remarkably elegant gown.”\n\n“I am not at all surprized that he should have fallen in love.”\n\n“Oh! no—there is nothing to surprize one at all.—A pretty fortune; and she came in his way.”\n\n“I dare say,” returned Harriet, sighing again, “I dare say she was very much attached to him.”\n\n“Perhaps she might; but it is not every man’s fate to marry the woman who loves him best. Miss Hawkins perhaps wanted a home, and thought this the best offer she was likely to have.”\n\n“Yes,” said Harriet earnestly, “and well she might, nobody could ever have a better. Well, I wish them happy with all my heart. And now, Miss Woodhouse, I do not think I shall mind seeing them again. He is just as superior as ever;—but being married, you know, it is quite a different thing. No, indeed, Miss Woodhouse, you need not be afraid; I can sit and admire him now without any great misery. To know that he has not thrown himself away, is such a comfort!—She does seem a charming young woman, just what he deserves. Happy creature! He called her ‘Augusta.’ How delightful!”\n\nWhen the visit was returned, Emma made up her mind. She could then see more and judge better. From Harriet’s happening not to be at Hartfield, and her father’s being present to engage Mr. Elton, she had a quarter of an hour of the lady’s conversation to herself, and could composedly attend to her; and the quarter of an hour quite convinced her that Mrs. Elton was a vain woman, extremely well satisfied with herself, and thinking much of her own importance; that she meant to shine and be very superior, but with manners which had been formed in a bad school, pert and familiar; that all her notions were drawn from one set of people, and one style of living; that if not foolish she was ignorant, and that her society would certainly do Mr. Elton no good.\n\nHarriet would have been a better match. If not wise or refined herself, she would have connected him with those who were; but Miss Hawkins, it might be fairly supposed from her easy conceit, had been the best of her own set. The rich brother-in-law near Bristol was the pride of the alliance, and his place and his carriages were the pride of him.\n\nThe very first subject after being seated was Maple Grove, “My brother Mr. Suckling’s seat;”—a comparison of Hartfield to Maple Grove. The grounds of Hartfield were small, but neat and pretty; and the house was modern and well-built. Mrs. Elton seemed most favourably impressed by the size of the room, the entrance, and all that she could see or imagine. “Very like Maple Grove indeed!—She was quite struck by the likeness!—That room was the very shape and size of the morning-room at Maple Grove; her sister’s favourite room.”—Mr. Elton was appealed to.—“Was not it astonishingly like?—She could really almost fancy herself at Maple Grove.”\n\n“And the staircase—You know, as I came in, I observed how very like the staircase was; placed exactly in the same part of the house. I really could not help exclaiming! I assure you, Miss Woodhouse, it is very delightful to me, to be reminded of a place I am so extremely partial to as Maple Grove. I have spent so many happy months there! (with a little sigh of sentiment). A charming place, undoubtedly. Every body who sees it is struck by its beauty; but to me, it has been quite a home. Whenever you are transplanted, like me, Miss Woodhouse, you will understand how very delightful it is to meet with any thing at all like what one has left behind. I always say this is quite one of the evils of matrimony.”\n\nEmma made as slight a reply as she could; but it was fully sufficient for Mrs. Elton, who only wanted to be talking herself.\n\n“So extremely like Maple Grove! And it is not merely the house—the grounds, I assure you, as far as I could observe, are strikingly like. The laurels at Maple Grove are in the same profusion as here, and stand very much in the same way—just across the lawn; and I had a glimpse of a fine large tree, with a bench round it, which put me so exactly in mind! My brother and sister will be enchanted with this place. People who have extensive grounds themselves are always pleased with any thing in the same style.”\n\nEmma doubted the truth of this sentiment. She had a great idea that people who had extensive grounds themselves cared very little for the extensive grounds of any body else; but it was not worth while to attack an error so double-dyed, and therefore only said in reply,\n\n“When you have seen more of this country, I am afraid you will think you have overrated Hartfield. Surry is full of beauties.”\n\n“Oh! yes, I am quite aware of that. It is the garden of England, you know. Surry is the garden of England.”\n\n“Yes; but we must not rest our claims on that distinction. Many counties, I believe, are called the garden of England, as well as Surry.”\n\n“No, I fancy not,” replied Mrs. Elton, with a most satisfied smile. “I never heard any county but Surry called so.”\n\nEmma was silenced.\n\n“My brother and sister have promised us a visit in the spring, or summer at farthest,” continued Mrs. Elton; “and that will be our time for exploring. While they are with us, we shall explore a great deal, I dare say. They will have their barouche-landau, of course, which holds four perfectly; and therefore, without saying any thing of our carriage, we should be able to explore the different beauties extremely well. They would hardly come in their chaise, I think, at that season of the year. Indeed, when the time draws on, I shall decidedly recommend their bringing the barouche-landau; it will be so very much preferable. When people come into a beautiful country of this sort, you know, Miss Woodhouse, one naturally wishes them to see as much as possible; and Mr. Suckling is extremely fond of exploring. We explored to King’s-Weston twice last summer, in that way, most delightfully, just after their first having the barouche-landau. You have many parties of that kind here, I suppose, Miss Woodhouse, every summer?”\n\n“No; not immediately here. We are rather out of distance of the very striking beauties which attract the sort of parties you speak of; and we are a very quiet set of people, I believe; more disposed to stay at home than engage in schemes of pleasure.”\n\n“Ah! there is nothing like staying at home for real comfort. Nobody can be more devoted to home than I am. I was quite a proverb for it at Maple Grove. Many a time has Selina said, when she has been going to Bristol, ‘I really cannot get this girl to move from the house. I absolutely must go in by myself, though I hate being stuck up in the barouche-landau without a companion; but Augusta, I believe, with her own good-will, would never stir beyond the park paling.’ Many a time has she said so; and yet I am no advocate for entire seclusion. I think, on the contrary, when people shut themselves up entirely from society, it is a very bad thing; and that it is much more advisable to mix in the world in a proper degree, without living in it either too much or too little. I perfectly understand your situation, however, Miss Woodhouse—(looking towards Mr. Woodhouse), Your father’s state of health must be a great drawback. Why does not he try Bath?—Indeed he should. Let me recommend Bath to you. I assure you I have no doubt of its doing Mr. Woodhouse good.”\n\n“My father tried it more than once, formerly; but without receiving any benefit; and Mr. Perry, whose name, I dare say, is not unknown to you, does not conceive it would be at all more likely to be useful now.”\n\n“Ah! that’s a great pity; for I assure you, Miss Woodhouse, where the waters do agree, it is quite wonderful the relief they give. In my Bath life, I have seen such instances of it! And it is so cheerful a place, that it could not fail of being of use to Mr. Woodhouse’s spirits, which, I understand, are sometimes much depressed. And as to its recommendations to you, I fancy I need not take much pains to dwell on them. The advantages of Bath to the young are pretty generally understood. It would be a charming introduction for you, who have lived so secluded a life; and I could immediately secure you some of the best society in the place. A line from me would bring you a little host of acquaintance; and my particular friend, Mrs. Partridge, the lady I have always resided with when in Bath, would be most happy to shew you any attentions, and would be the very person for you to go into public with.”\n\nIt was as much as Emma could bear, without being impolite. The idea of her being indebted to Mrs. Elton for what was called an introduction—of her going into public under the auspices of a friend of Mrs. Elton’s—probably some vulgar, dashing widow, who, with the help of a boarder, just made a shift to live!—The dignity of Miss Woodhouse, of Hartfield, was sunk indeed!\n\nShe restrained herself, however, from any of the reproofs she could have given, and only thanked Mrs. Elton coolly; “but their going to Bath was quite out of the question; and she was not perfectly convinced that the place might suit her better than her father.” And then, to prevent farther outrage and indignation, changed the subject directly.\n\n“I do not ask whether you are musical, Mrs. Elton. Upon these occasions, a lady’s character generally precedes her; and Highbury has long known that you are a superior performer.”\n\n“Oh! no, indeed; I must protest against any such idea. A superior performer!—very far from it, I assure you. Consider from how partial a quarter your information came. I am doatingly fond of music—passionately fond;—and my friends say I am not entirely devoid of taste; but as to any thing else, upon my honour my performance is mediocre to the last degree. You, Miss Woodhouse, I well know, play delightfully. I assure you it has been the greatest satisfaction, comfort, and delight to me, to hear what a musical society I am got into. I absolutely cannot do without music. It is a necessary of life to me; and having always been used to a very musical society, both at Maple Grove and in Bath, it would have been a most serious sacrifice. I honestly said as much to Mr. E. when he was speaking of my future home, and expressing his fears lest the retirement of it should be disagreeable; and the inferiority of the house too—knowing what I had been accustomed to—of course he was not wholly without apprehension. When he was speaking of it in that way, I honestly said that the world I could give up—parties, balls, plays—for I had no fear of retirement. Blessed with so many resources within myself, the world was not necessary to me. I could do very well without it. To those who had no resources it was a different thing; but my resources made me quite independent. And as to smaller-sized rooms than I had been used to, I really could not give it a thought. I hoped I was perfectly equal to any sacrifice of that description. Certainly I had been accustomed to every luxury at Maple Grove; but I did assure him that two carriages were not necessary to my happiness, nor were spacious apartments. ‘But,’ said I, ‘to be quite honest, I do not think I can live without something of a musical society. I condition for nothing else; but without music, life would be a blank to me.’”\n\n“We cannot suppose,” said Emma, smiling, “that Mr. Elton would hesitate to assure you of there being a very musical society in Highbury; and I hope you will not find he has outstepped the truth more than may be pardoned, in consideration of the motive.”\n\n“No, indeed, I have no doubts at all on that head. I am delighted to find myself in such a circle. I hope we shall have many sweet little concerts together. I think, Miss Woodhouse, you and I must establish a musical club, and have regular weekly meetings at your house, or ours. Will not it be a good plan? If we exert ourselves, I think we shall not be long in want of allies. Something of that nature would be particularly desirable for me, as an inducement to keep me in practice; for married women, you know—there is a sad story against them, in general. They are but too apt to give up music.”\n\n“But you, who are so extremely fond of it—there can be no danger, surely?”\n\n“I should hope not; but really when I look around among my acquaintance, I tremble. Selina has entirely given up music—never touches the instrument—though she played sweetly. And the same may be said of Mrs. Jeffereys—Clara Partridge, that was—and of the two Milmans, now Mrs. Bird and Mrs. James Cooper; and of more than I can enumerate. Upon my word it is enough to put one in a fright. I used to be quite angry with Selina; but really I begin now to comprehend that a married woman has many things to call her attention. I believe I was half an hour this morning shut up with my housekeeper.”\n\n“But every thing of that kind,” said Emma, “will soon be in so regular a train—”\n\n“Well,” said Mrs. Elton, laughing, “we shall see.”\n\nEmma, finding her so determined upon neglecting her music, had nothing more to say; and, after a moment’s pause, Mrs. Elton chose another subject.\n\n“We have been calling at Randalls,” said she, “and found them both at home; and very pleasant people they seem to be. I like them extremely. Mr. Weston seems an excellent creature—quite a first-rate favourite with me already, I assure you. And she appears so truly good—there is something so motherly and kind-hearted about her, that it wins upon one directly. She was your governess, I think?”\n\nEmma was almost too much astonished to answer; but Mrs. Elton hardly waited for the affirmative before she went on.\n\n“Having understood as much, I was rather astonished to find her so very lady-like! But she is really quite the gentlewoman.”\n\n“Mrs. Weston’s manners,” said Emma, “were always particularly good. Their propriety, simplicity, and elegance, would make them the safest model for any young woman.”\n\n“And who do you think came in while we were there?”\n\nEmma was quite at a loss. The tone implied some old acquaintance—and how could she possibly guess?\n\n“Knightley!” continued Mrs. Elton; “Knightley himself!—Was not it lucky?—for, not being within when he called the other day, I had never seen him before; and of course, as so particular a friend of Mr. E.’s, I had a great curiosity. ‘My friend Knightley’ had been so often mentioned, that I was really impatient to see him; and I must do my cara sposo the justice to say that he need not be ashamed of his friend. Knightley is quite the gentleman. I like him very much. Decidedly, I think, a very gentleman-like man.”\n\nHappily, it was now time to be gone. They were off; and Emma could breathe.\n\n“Insufferable woman!” was her immediate exclamation. “Worse than I had supposed. Absolutely insufferable! Knightley!—I could not have believed it. Knightley!—never seen him in her life before, and call him Knightley!—and discover that he is a gentleman! A little upstart, vulgar being, with her Mr. E., and her caro sposo, and her resources, and all her airs of pert pretension and underbred finery. Actually to discover that Mr. Knightley is a gentleman! I doubt whether he will return the compliment, and discover her to be a lady. I could not have believed it! And to propose that she and I should unite to form a musical club! One would fancy we were bosom friends! And Mrs. Weston!—Astonished that the person who had brought me up should be a gentlewoman! Worse and worse. I never met with her equal. Much beyond my hopes. Harriet is disgraced by any comparison. Oh! what would Frank Churchill say to her, if he were here? How angry and how diverted he would be! Ah! there I am—thinking of him directly. Always the first person to be thought of! How I catch myself out! Frank Churchill comes as regularly into my mind!”—\n\nAll this ran so glibly through her thoughts, that by the time her father had arranged himself, after the bustle of the Eltons’ departure, and was ready to speak, she was very tolerably capable of attending.\n\n“Well, my dear,” he deliberately began, “considering we never saw her before, she seems a very pretty sort of young lady; and I dare say she was very much pleased with you. She speaks a little too quick. A little quickness of voice there is which rather hurts the ear. But I believe I am nice; I do not like strange voices; and nobody speaks like you and poor Miss Taylor. However, she seems a very obliging, pretty-behaved young lady, and no doubt will make him a very good wife. Though I think he had better not have married. I made the best excuses I could for not having been able to wait on him and Mrs. Elton on this happy occasion; I said that I hoped I should in the course of the summer. But I ought to have gone before. Not to wait upon a bride is very remiss. Ah! it shews what a sad invalid I am! But I do not like the corner into Vicarage Lane.”\n\n“I dare say your apologies were accepted, sir. Mr. Elton knows you.”\n\n“Yes: but a young lady—a bride—I ought to have paid my respects to her if possible. It was being very deficient.”\n\n“But, my dear papa, you are no friend to matrimony; and therefore why should you be so anxious to pay your respects to a bride? It ought to be no recommendation to you. It is encouraging people to marry if you make so much of them.”\n\n“No, my dear, I never encouraged any body to marry, but I would always wish to pay every proper attention to a lady—and a bride, especially, is never to be neglected. More is avowedly due to her. A bride, you know, my dear, is always the first in company, let the others be who they may.”\n\n“Well, papa, if this is not encouragement to marry, I do not know what is. And I should never have expected you to be lending your sanction to such vanity-baits for poor young ladies.”\n\n“My dear, you do not understand me. This is a matter of mere common politeness and good-breeding, and has nothing to do with any encouragement to people to marry.”\n\nEmma had done. Her father was growing nervous, and could not understand her. Her mind returned to Mrs. Elton’s offences, and long, very long, did they occupy her.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter XV\n\nEmma was not required, by any subsequent discovery, to retract her ill opinion of Mrs. Elton. Her observation had been pretty correct. Such as Mrs. Elton appeared to her on this second interview, such she appeared whenever they met again,—self-important, presuming, familiar, ignorant, and ill-bred. She had a little beauty and a little accomplishment, but so little judgment that she thought herself coming with superior knowledge of the world, to enliven and improve a country neighbourhood; and conceived Miss Hawkins to have held such a place in society as Mrs. Elton’s consequence only could surpass.\n\nThere was no reason to suppose Mr. Elton thought at all differently from his wife. He seemed not merely happy with her, but proud. He had the air of congratulating himself on having brought such a woman to Highbury, as not even Miss Woodhouse could equal; and the greater part of her new acquaintance, disposed to commend, or not in the habit of judging, following the lead of Miss Bates’s good-will, or taking it for granted that the bride must be as clever and as agreeable as she professed herself, were very well satisfied; so that Mrs. Elton’s praise passed from one mouth to another as it ought to do, unimpeded by Miss Woodhouse, who readily continued her first contribution and talked with a good grace of her being “very pleasant and very elegantly dressed.”\n\nIn one respect Mrs. Elton grew even worse than she had appeared at first. Her feelings altered towards Emma.—Offended, probably, by the little encouragement which her proposals of intimacy met with, she drew back in her turn and gradually became much more cold and distant; and though the effect was agreeable, the ill-will which produced it was necessarily increasing Emma’s dislike. Her manners, too—and Mr. Elton’s, were unpleasant towards Harriet. They were sneering and negligent. Emma hoped it must rapidly work Harriet’s cure; but the sensations which could prompt such behaviour sunk them both very much.—It was not to be doubted that poor Harriet’s attachment had been an offering to conjugal unreserve, and her own share in the story, under a colouring the least favourable to her and the most soothing to him, had in all likelihood been given also. She was, of course, the object of their joint dislike.—When they had nothing else to say, it must be always easy to begin abusing Miss Woodhouse; and the enmity which they dared not shew in open disrespect to her, found a broader vent in contemptuous treatment of Harriet.\n\nMrs. Elton took a great fancy to Jane Fairfax; and from the first. Not merely when a state of warfare with one young lady might be supposed to recommend the other, but from the very first; and she was not satisfied with expressing a natural and reasonable admiration—but without solicitation, or plea, or privilege, she must be wanting to assist and befriend her.—Before Emma had forfeited her confidence, and about the third time of their meeting, she heard all Mrs. Elton’s knight-errantry on the subject.—\n\n“Jane Fairfax is absolutely charming, Miss Woodhouse.—I quite rave about Jane Fairfax.—A sweet, interesting creature. So mild and ladylike—and with such talents!—I assure you I think she has very extraordinary talents. I do not scruple to say that she plays extremely well. I know enough of music to speak decidedly on that point. Oh! she is absolutely charming! You will laugh at my warmth—but, upon my word, I talk of nothing but Jane Fairfax.—And her situation is so calculated to affect one!—Miss Woodhouse, we must exert ourselves and endeavour to do something for her. We must bring her forward. Such talent as hers must not be suffered to remain unknown.—I dare say you have heard those charming lines of the poet,\n\n‘Full many a flower is born to blush unseen, ‘And waste its fragrance on the desert air.’\n\nWe must not allow them to be verified in sweet Jane Fairfax.”\n\n“I cannot think there is any danger of it,” was Emma’s calm answer—“and when you are better acquainted with Miss Fairfax’s situation and understand what her home has been, with Colonel and Mrs. Campbell, I have no idea that you will suppose her talents can be unknown.”\n\n“Oh! but dear Miss Woodhouse, she is now in such retirement, such obscurity, so thrown away.—Whatever advantages she may have enjoyed with the Campbells are so palpably at an end! And I think she feels it. I am sure she does. She is very timid and silent. One can see that she feels the want of encouragement. I like her the better for it. I must confess it is a recommendation to me. I am a great advocate for timidity—and I am sure one does not often meet with it.—But in those who are at all inferior, it is extremely prepossessing. Oh! I assure you, Jane Fairfax is a very delightful character, and interests me more than I can express.”\n\n“You appear to feel a great deal—but I am not aware how you or any of Miss Fairfax’s acquaintance here, any of those who have known her longer than yourself, can shew her any other attention than”—\n\n“My dear Miss Woodhouse, a vast deal may be done by those who dare to act. You and I need not be afraid. If we set the example, many will follow it as far as they can; though all have not our situations. We have carriages to fetch and convey her home, and we live in a style which could not make the addition of Jane Fairfax, at any time, the least inconvenient.—I should be extremely displeased if Wright were to send us up such a dinner, as could make me regret having asked more than Jane Fairfax to partake of it. I have no idea of that sort of thing. It is not likely that I should, considering what I have been used to. My greatest danger, perhaps, in housekeeping, may be quite the other way, in doing too much, and being too careless of expense. Maple Grove will probably be my model more than it ought to be—for we do not at all affect to equal my brother, Mr. Suckling, in income.—However, my resolution is taken as to noticing Jane Fairfax.—I shall certainly have her very often at my house, shall introduce her wherever I can, shall have musical parties to draw out her talents, and shall be constantly on the watch for an eligible situation. My acquaintance is so very extensive, that I have little doubt of hearing of something to suit her shortly.—I shall introduce her, of course, very particularly to my brother and sister when they come to us. I am sure they will like her extremely; and when she gets a little acquainted with them, her fears will completely wear off, for there really is nothing in the manners of either but what is highly conciliating.—I shall have her very often indeed while they are with me, and I dare say we shall sometimes find a seat for her in the barouche-landau in some of our exploring parties.”\n\n“Poor Jane Fairfax!”—thought Emma.—“You have not deserved this. You may have done wrong with regard to Mr. Dixon, but this is a punishment beyond what you can have merited!—The kindness and protection of Mrs. Elton!—‘Jane Fairfax and Jane Fairfax.’ Heavens! Let me not suppose that she dares go about, Emma Woodhouse-ing me!—But upon my honour, there seems no limits to the licentiousness of that woman’s tongue!”\n\nEmma had not to listen to such paradings again—to any so exclusively addressed to herself—so disgustingly decorated with a “dear Miss Woodhouse.” The change on Mrs. Elton’s side soon afterwards appeared, and she was left in peace—neither forced to be the very particular friend of Mrs. Elton, nor, under Mrs. Elton’s guidance, the very active patroness of Jane Fairfax, and only sharing with others in a general way, in knowing what was felt, what was meditated, what was done.\n\nShe looked on with some amusement.—Miss Bates’s gratitude for Mrs. Elton’s attentions to Jane was in the first style of guileless simplicity and warmth. She was quite one of her worthies—the most amiable, affable, delightful woman—just as accomplished and condescending as Mrs. Elton meant to be considered. Emma’s only surprize was that Jane Fairfax should accept those attentions and tolerate Mrs. Elton as she seemed to do. She heard of her walking with the Eltons, sitting with the Eltons, spending a day with the Eltons! This was astonishing!—She could not have believed it possible that the taste or the pride of Miss Fairfax could endure such society and friendship as the Vicarage had to offer.\n\n“She is a riddle, quite a riddle!” said she.—“To chuse to remain here month after month, under privations of every sort! And now to chuse the mortification of Mrs. Elton’s notice and the penury of her conversation, rather than return to the superior companions who have always loved her with such real, generous affection.”\n\nJane had come to Highbury professedly for three months; the Campbells were gone to Ireland for three months; but now the Campbells had promised their daughter to stay at least till Midsummer, and fresh invitations had arrived for her to join them there. According to Miss Bates—it all came from her—Mrs. Dixon had written most pressingly. Would Jane but go, means were to be found, servants sent, friends contrived—no travelling difficulty allowed to exist; but still she had declined it!\n\n“She must have some motive, more powerful than appears, for refusing this invitation,” was Emma’s conclusion. “She must be under some sort of penance, inflicted either by the Campbells or herself. There is great fear, great caution, great resolution somewhere.—She is not to be with the Dixons. The decree is issued by somebody. But why must she consent to be with the Eltons?—Here is quite a separate puzzle.”\n\nUpon her speaking her wonder aloud on that part of the subject, before the few who knew her opinion of Mrs. Elton, Mrs. Weston ventured this apology for Jane.\n\n“We cannot suppose that she has any great enjoyment at the Vicarage, my dear Emma—but it is better than being always at home. Her aunt is a good creature, but, as a constant companion, must be very tiresome. We must consider what Miss Fairfax quits, before we condemn her taste for what she goes to.”\n\n“You are right, Mrs. Weston,” said Mr. Knightley warmly, “Miss Fairfax is as capable as any of us of forming a just opinion of Mrs. Elton. Could she have chosen with whom to associate, she would not have chosen her. But (with a reproachful smile at Emma) she receives attentions from Mrs. Elton, which nobody else pays her.”\n\nEmma felt that Mrs. Weston was giving her a momentary glance; and she was herself struck by his warmth. With a faint blush, she presently replied,\n\n“Such attentions as Mrs. Elton’s, I should have imagined, would rather disgust than gratify Miss Fairfax. Mrs. Elton’s invitations I should have imagined any thing but inviting.”\n\n“I should not wonder,” said Mrs. Weston, “if Miss Fairfax were to have been drawn on beyond her own inclination, by her aunt’s eagerness in accepting Mrs. Elton’s civilities for her. Poor Miss Bates may very likely have committed her niece and hurried her into a greater appearance of intimacy than her own good sense would have dictated, in spite of the very natural wish of a little change.”\n\nBoth felt rather anxious to hear him speak again; and after a few minutes silence, he said,\n\n“Another thing must be taken into consideration too—Mrs. Elton does not talk to Miss Fairfax as she speaks of her. We all know the difference between the pronouns he or she and thou, the plainest spoken amongst us; we all feel the influence of a something beyond common civility in our personal intercourse with each other—a something more early implanted. We cannot give any body the disagreeable hints that we may have been very full of the hour before. We feel things differently. And besides the operation of this, as a general principle, you may be sure that Miss Fairfax awes Mrs. Elton by her superiority both of mind and manner; and that, face to face, Mrs. Elton treats her with all the respect which she has a claim to. Such a woman as Jane Fairfax probably never fell in Mrs. Elton’s way before—and no degree of vanity can prevent her acknowledging her own comparative littleness in action, if not in consciousness.”\n\n“I know how highly you think of Jane Fairfax,” said Emma. Little Henry was in her thoughts, and a mixture of alarm and delicacy made her irresolute what else to say.\n\n“Yes,” he replied, “any body may know how highly I think of her.”\n\n“And yet,” said Emma, beginning hastily and with an arch look, but soon stopping—it was better, however, to know the worst at once—she hurried on—“And yet, perhaps, you may hardly be aware yourself how highly it is. The extent of your admiration may take you by surprize some day or other.”\n\nMr. Knightley was hard at work upon the lower buttons of his thick leather gaiters, and either the exertion of getting them together, or some other cause, brought the colour into his face, as he answered,\n\n“Oh! are you there?—But you are miserably behindhand. Mr. Cole gave me a hint of it six weeks ago.”\n\nHe stopped.—Emma felt her foot pressed by Mrs. Weston, and did not herself know what to think. In a moment he went on—\n\n“That will never be, however, I can assure you. Miss Fairfax, I dare say, would not have me if I were to ask her—and I am very sure I shall never ask her.”\n\nEmma returned her friend’s pressure with interest; and was pleased enough to exclaim,\n\n“You are not vain, Mr. Knightley. I will say that for you.”\n\nHe seemed hardly to hear her; he was thoughtful—and in a manner which shewed him not pleased, soon afterwards said,\n\n“So you have been settling that I should marry Jane Fairfax?”\n\n“No indeed I have not. You have scolded me too much for match-making, for me to presume to take such a liberty with you. What I said just now, meant nothing. One says those sort of things, of course, without any idea of a serious meaning. Oh! no, upon my word I have not the smallest wish for your marrying Jane Fairfax or Jane any body. You would not come in and sit with us in this comfortable way, if you were married.”\n\nMr. Knightley was thoughtful again. The result of his reverie was, “No, Emma, I do not think the extent of my admiration for her will ever take me by surprize.—I never had a thought of her in that way, I assure you.” And soon afterwards, “Jane Fairfax is a very charming young woman—but not even Jane Fairfax is perfect. She has a fault. She has not the open temper which a man would wish for in a wife.”\n\nEmma could not but rejoice to hear that she had a fault. “Well,” said she, “and you soon silenced Mr. Cole, I suppose?”\n\n“Yes, very soon. He gave me a quiet hint; I told him he was mistaken; he asked my pardon and said no more. Cole does not want to be wiser or wittier than his neighbours.”\n\n“In that respect how unlike dear Mrs. Elton, who wants to be wiser and wittier than all the world! I wonder how she speaks of the Coles—what she calls them! How can she find any appellation for them, deep enough in familiar vulgarity? She calls you, Knightley—what can she do for Mr. Cole? And so I am not to be surprized that Jane Fairfax accepts her civilities and consents to be with her. Mrs. Weston, your argument weighs most with me. I can much more readily enter into the temptation of getting away from Miss Bates, than I can believe in the triumph of Miss Fairfax’s mind over Mrs. Elton. I have no faith in Mrs. Elton’s acknowledging herself the inferior in thought, word, or deed; or in her being under any restraint beyond her own scanty rule of good-breeding. I cannot imagine that she will not be continually insulting her visitor with praise, encouragement, and offers of service; that she will not be continually detailing her magnificent intentions, from the procuring her a permanent situation to the including her in those delightful exploring parties which are to take place in the barouche-landau.”\n\n“Jane Fairfax has feeling,” said Mr. Knightley—“I do not accuse her of want of feeling. Her sensibilities, I suspect, are strong—and her temper excellent in its power of forbearance, patience, self-control; but it wants openness. She is reserved, more reserved, I think, than she used to be—And I love an open temper. No—till Cole alluded to my supposed attachment, it had never entered my head. I saw Jane Fairfax and conversed with her, with admiration and pleasure always—but with no thought beyond.”\n\n“Well, Mrs. Weston,” said Emma triumphantly when he left them, “what do you say now to Mr. Knightley’s marrying Jane Fairfax?”\n\n“Why, really, dear Emma, I say that he is so very much occupied by the idea of not being in love with her, that I should not wonder if it were to end in his being so at last. Do not beat me.”\n\n# Volume II — Chapter XVI\n\nEvery body in and about Highbury who had ever visited Mr. Elton, was disposed to pay him attention on his marriage. Dinner-parties and evening-parties were made for him and his lady; and invitations flowed in so fast that she had soon the pleasure of apprehending they were never to have a disengaged day.\n\n“I see how it is,” said she. “I see what a life I am to lead among you. Upon my word we shall be absolutely dissipated. We really seem quite the fashion. If this is living in the country, it is nothing very formidable. From Monday next to Saturday, I assure you we have not a disengaged day!—A woman with fewer resources than I have, need not have been at a loss.”\n\nNo invitation came amiss to her. Her Bath habits made evening-parties perfectly natural to her, and Maple Grove had given her a taste for dinners. She was a little shocked at the want of two drawing rooms, at the poor attempt at rout-cakes, and there being no ice in the Highbury card-parties. Mrs. Bates, Mrs. Perry, Mrs. Goddard and others, were a good deal behind-hand in knowledge of the world, but she would soon shew them how every thing ought to be arranged. In the course of the spring she must return their civilities by one very superior party—in which her card-tables should be set out with their separate candles and unbroken packs in the true style—and more waiters engaged for the evening than their own establishment could furnish, to carry round the refreshments at exactly the proper hour, and in the proper order.\n\nEmma, in the meanwhile, could not be satisfied without a dinner at Hartfield for the Eltons. They must not do less than others, or she should be exposed to odious suspicions, and imagined capable of pitiful resentment. A dinner there must be. After Emma had talked about it for ten minutes, Mr. Woodhouse felt no unwillingness, and only made the usual stipulation of not sitting at the bottom of the table himself, with the usual regular difficulty of deciding who should do it for him.\n\nThe persons to be invited, required little thought. Besides the Eltons, it must be the Westons and Mr. Knightley; so far it was all of course—and it was hardly less inevitable that poor little Harriet must be asked to make the eighth:—but this invitation was not given with equal satisfaction, and on many accounts Emma was particularly pleased by Harriet’s begging to be allowed to decline it. “She would rather not be in his company more than she could help. She was not yet quite able to see him and his charming happy wife together, without feeling uncomfortable. If Miss Woodhouse would not be displeased, she would rather stay at home.” It was precisely what Emma would have wished, had she deemed it possible enough for wishing. She was delighted with the fortitude of her little friend—for fortitude she knew it was in her to give up being in company and stay at home; and she could now invite the very person whom she really wanted to make the eighth, Jane Fairfax.— Since her last conversation with Mrs. Weston and Mr. Knightley, she was more conscience-stricken about Jane Fairfax than she had often been.—Mr. Knightley’s words dwelt with her. He had said that Jane Fairfax received attentions from Mrs. Elton which nobody else paid her.\n\n“This is very true,” said she, “at least as far as relates to me, which was all that was meant—and it is very shameful.—Of the same age—and always knowing her—I ought to have been more her friend.—She will never like me now. I have neglected her too long. But I will shew her greater attention than I have done.”\n\nEvery invitation was successful. They were all disengaged and all happy.—The preparatory interest of this dinner, however, was not yet over. A circumstance rather unlucky occurred. The two eldest little Knightleys were engaged to pay their grandpapa and aunt a visit of some weeks in the spring, and their papa now proposed bringing them, and staying one whole day at Hartfield—which one day would be the very day of this party.—His professional engagements did not allow of his being put off, but both father and daughter were disturbed by its happening so. Mr. Woodhouse considered eight persons at dinner together as the utmost that his nerves could bear—and here would be a ninth—and Emma apprehended that it would be a ninth very much out of humour at not being able to come even to Hartfield for forty-eight hours without falling in with a dinner-party.\n\nShe comforted her father better than she could comfort herself, by representing that though he certainly would make them nine, yet he always said so little, that the increase of noise would be very immaterial. She thought it in reality a sad exchange for herself, to have him with his grave looks and reluctant conversation opposed to her instead of his brother.\n\nThe event was more favourable to Mr. Woodhouse than to Emma. John Knightley came; but Mr. Weston was unexpectedly summoned to town and must be absent on the very day. He might be able to join them in the evening, but certainly not to dinner. Mr. Woodhouse was quite at ease; and the seeing him so, with the arrival of the little boys and the philosophic composure of her brother on hearing his fate, removed the chief of even Emma’s vexation.\n\nThe day came, the party were punctually assembled, and Mr. John Knightley seemed early to devote himself to the business of being agreeable. Instead of drawing his brother off to a window while they waited for dinner, he was talking to Miss Fairfax. Mrs. Elton, as elegant as lace and pearls could make her, he looked at in silence—wanting only to observe enough for Isabella’s information—but Miss Fairfax was an old acquaintance and a quiet girl, and he could talk to her. He had met her before breakfast as he was returning from a walk with his little boys, when it had been just beginning to rain. It was natural to have some civil hopes on the subject, and he said,\n\n“I hope you did not venture far, Miss Fairfax, this morning, or I am sure you must have been wet.—We scarcely got home in time. I hope you turned directly.”\n\n“I went only to the post-office,” said she, “and reached home before the rain was much. It is my daily errand. I always fetch the letters when I am here. It saves trouble, and is a something to get me out. A walk before breakfast does me good.”\n\n“Not a walk in the rain, I should imagine.”\n\n“No, but it did not absolutely rain when I set out.”\n\nMr. John Knightley smiled, and replied,\n\n“That is to say, you chose to have your walk, for you were not six yards from your own door when I had the pleasure of meeting you; and Henry and John had seen more drops than they could count long before. The post-office has a great charm at one period of our lives. When you have lived to my age, you will begin to think letters are never worth going through the rain for.”\n\nThere was a little blush, and then this answer,\n\n“I must not hope to be ever situated as you are, in the midst of every dearest connexion, and therefore I cannot expect that simply growing older should make me indifferent about letters.”\n\n“Indifferent! Oh! no—I never conceived you could become indifferent. Letters are no matter of indifference; they are generally a very positive curse.”\n\n“You are speaking of letters of business; mine are letters of friendship.”\n\n“I have often thought them the worst of the two,” replied he coolly. “Business, you know, may bring money, but friendship hardly ever does.”\n\n“Ah! you are not serious now. I know Mr. John Knightley too well—I am very sure he understands the value of friendship as well as any body. I can easily believe that letters are very little to you, much less than to me, but it is not your being ten years older than myself which makes the difference, it is not age, but situation. You have every body dearest to you always at hand, I, probably, never shall again; and therefore till I have outlived all my affections, a post-office, I think, must always have power to draw me out, in worse weather than to-day.”\n\n“When I talked of your being altered by time, by the progress of years,” said John Knightley, “I meant to imply the change of situation which time usually brings. I consider one as including the other. Time will generally lessen the interest of every attachment not within the daily circle—but that is not the change I had in view for you. As an old friend, you will allow me to hope, Miss Fairfax, that ten years hence you may have as many concentrated objects as I have.”\n\nIt was kindly said, and very far from giving offence. A pleasant “thank you” seemed meant to laugh it off, but a blush, a quivering lip, a tear in the eye, shewed that it was felt beyond a laugh. Her attention was now claimed by Mr. Woodhouse, who being, according to his custom on such occasions, making the circle of his guests, and paying his particular compliments to the ladies, was ending with her—and with all his mildest urbanity, said,\n\n“I am very sorry to hear, Miss Fairfax, of your being out this morning in the rain. Young ladies should take care of themselves.—Young ladies are delicate plants. They should take care of their health and their complexion. My dear, did you change your stockings?”\n\n“Yes, sir, I did indeed; and I am very much obliged by your kind solicitude about me.”\n\n“My dear Miss Fairfax, young ladies are very sure to be cared for.—I hope your good grand-mama and aunt are well. They are some of my very old friends. I wish my health allowed me to be a better neighbour. You do us a great deal of honour to-day, I am sure. My daughter and I are both highly sensible of your goodness, and have the greatest satisfaction in seeing you at Hartfield.”\n\nThe kind-hearted, polite old man might then sit down and feel that he had done his duty, and made every fair lady welcome and easy.\n\nBy this time, the walk in the rain had reached Mrs. Elton, and her remonstrances now opened upon Jane.\n\n“My dear Jane, what is this I hear?—Going to the post-office in the rain!—This must not be, I assure you.—You sad girl, how could you do such a thing?—It is a sign I was not there to take care of you.”\n\nJane very patiently assured her that she had not caught any cold.\n\n“Oh! do not tell me. You really are a very sad girl, and do not know how to take care of yourself.—To the post-office indeed! Mrs. Weston, did you ever hear the like? You and I must positively exert our authority.”\n\n“My advice,” said Mrs. Weston kindly and persuasively, “I certainly do feel tempted to give. Miss Fairfax, you must not run such risks.—Liable as you have been to severe colds, indeed you ought to be particularly careful, especially at this time of year. The spring I always think requires more than common care. Better wait an hour or two, or even half a day for your letters, than run the risk of bringing on your cough again. Now do not you feel that you had? Yes, I am sure you are much too reasonable. You look as if you would not do such a thing again.”\n\n“Oh! she shall not do such a thing again,” eagerly rejoined Mrs. Elton. “We will not allow her to do such a thing again:”—and nodding significantly—“there must be some arrangement made, there must indeed. I shall speak to Mr. E. The man who fetches our letters every morning (one of our men, I forget his name) shall inquire for yours too and bring them to you. That will obviate all difficulties you know; and from us I really think, my dear Jane, you can have no scruple to accept such an accommodation.”\n\n“You are extremely kind,” said Jane; “but I cannot give up my early walk. I am advised to be out of doors as much as I can, I must walk somewhere, and the post-office is an object; and upon my word, I have scarcely ever had a bad morning before.”\n\n“My dear Jane, say no more about it. The thing is determined, that is (laughing affectedly) as far as I can presume to determine any thing without the concurrence of my lord and master. You know, Mrs. Weston, you and I must be cautious how we express ourselves. But I do flatter myself, my dear Jane, that my influence is not entirely worn out. If I meet with no insuperable difficulties therefore, consider that point as settled.”\n\n“Excuse me,” said Jane earnestly, “I cannot by any means consent to such an arrangement, so needlessly troublesome to your servant. If the errand were not a pleasure to me, it could be done, as it always is when I am not here, by my grandmama’s.”\n\n“Oh! my dear; but so much as Patty has to do!—And it is a kindness to employ our men.”\n\nJane looked as if she did not mean to be conquered; but instead of answering, she began speaking again to Mr. John Knightley.\n\n“The post-office is a wonderful establishment!” said she.—“The regularity and despatch of it! If one thinks of all that it has to do, and all that it does so well, it is really astonishing!”\n\n“It is certainly very well regulated.”\n\n“So seldom that any negligence or blunder appears! So seldom that a letter, among the thousands that are constantly passing about the kingdom, is even carried wrong—and not one in a million, I suppose, actually lost! And when one considers the variety of hands, and of bad hands too, that are to be deciphered, it increases the wonder.”\n\n“The clerks grow expert from habit.—They must begin with some quickness of sight and hand, and exercise improves them. If you want any farther explanation,” continued he, smiling, “they are paid for it. That is the key to a great deal of capacity. The public pays and must be served well.”\n\nThe varieties of handwriting were farther talked of, and the usual observations made.\n\n“I have heard it asserted,” said John Knightley, “that the same sort of handwriting often prevails in a family; and where the same master teaches, it is natural enough. But for that reason, I should imagine the likeness must be chiefly confined to the females, for boys have very little teaching after an early age, and scramble into any hand they can get. Isabella and Emma, I think, do write very much alike. I have not always known their writing apart.”\n\n“Yes,” said his brother hesitatingly, “there is a likeness. I know what you mean—but Emma’s hand is the strongest.”\n\n“Isabella and Emma both write beautifully,” said Mr. Woodhouse; “and always did. And so does poor Mrs. Weston”—with half a sigh and half a smile at her.\n\n“I never saw any gentleman’s handwriting”—Emma began, looking also at Mrs. Weston; but stopped, on perceiving that Mrs. Weston was attending to some one else—and the pause gave her time to reflect, “Now, how am I going to introduce him?—Am I unequal to speaking his name at once before all these people? Is it necessary for me to use any roundabout phrase?—Your Yorkshire friend—your correspondent in Yorkshire;—that would be the way, I suppose, if I were very bad.—No, I can pronounce his name without the smallest distress. I certainly get better and better.—Now for it.”\n\nMrs. Weston was disengaged and Emma began again—“Mr. Frank Churchill writes one of the best gentleman’s hands I ever saw.”\n\n“I do not admire it,” said Mr. Knightley. “It is too small—wants strength. It is like a woman’s writing.”\n\nThis was not submitted to by either lady. They vindicated him against the base aspersion. “No, it by no means wanted strength—it was not a large hand, but very clear and certainly strong. Had not Mrs. Weston any letter about her to produce?” No, she had heard from him very lately, but having answered the letter, had put it away.\n\n“If we were in the other room,” said Emma, “if I had my writing-desk, I am sure I could produce a specimen. I have a note of his.—Do not you remember, Mrs. Weston, employing him to write for you one day?”\n\n“He chose to say he was employed”—\n\n“Well, well, I have that note; and can shew it after dinner to convince Mr. Knightley.”\n\n“Oh! when a gallant young man, like Mr. Frank Churchill,” said Mr. Knightley dryly, “writes to a fair lady like Miss Woodhouse, he will, of course, put forth his best.”\n\nDinner was on table.—Mrs. Elton, before she could be spoken to, was ready; and before Mr. Woodhouse had reached her with his request to be allowed to hand her into the dining-parlour, was saying—\n\n“Must I go first? I really am ashamed of always leading the way.”\n\nJane’s solicitude about fetching her own letters had not escaped Emma. She had heard and seen it all; and felt some curiosity to know whether the wet walk of this morning had produced any. She suspected that it had; that it would not have been so resolutely encountered but in full expectation of hearing from some one very dear, and that it had not been in vain. She thought there was an air of greater happiness than usual—a glow both of complexion and spirits.\n\nShe could have made an inquiry or two, as to the expedition and the expense of the Irish mails;—it was at her tongue’s end—but she abstained. She was quite determined not to utter a word that should hurt Jane Fairfax’s feelings; and they followed the other ladies out of the room, arm in arm, with an appearance of good-will highly becoming to the beauty and grace of each.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter XVII\n\nWhen the ladies returned to the drawing-room after dinner, Emma found it hardly possible to prevent their making two distinct parties;—with so much perseverance in judging and behaving ill did Mrs. Elton engross Jane Fairfax and slight herself. She and Mrs. Weston were obliged to be almost always either talking together or silent together. Mrs. Elton left them no choice. If Jane repressed her for a little time, she soon began again; and though much that passed between them was in a half-whisper, especially on Mrs. Elton’s side, there was no avoiding a knowledge of their principal subjects: The post-office—catching cold—fetching letters—and friendship, were long under discussion; and to them succeeded one, which must be at least equally unpleasant to Jane—inquiries whether she had yet heard of any situation likely to suit her, and professions of Mrs. Elton’s meditated activity.\n\n“Here is April come!” said she, “I get quite anxious about you. June will soon be here.”\n\n“But I have never fixed on June or any other month—merely looked forward to the summer in general.”\n\n“But have you really heard of nothing?”\n\n“I have not even made any inquiry; I do not wish to make any yet.”\n\n“Oh! my dear, we cannot begin too early; you are not aware of the difficulty of procuring exactly the desirable thing.”\n\n“I not aware!” said Jane, shaking her head; “dear Mrs. Elton, who can have thought of it as I have done?”\n\n“But you have not seen so much of the world as I have. You do not know how many candidates there always are for the first situations. I saw a vast deal of that in the neighbourhood round Maple Grove. A cousin of Mr. Suckling, Mrs. Bragge, had such an infinity of applications; every body was anxious to be in her family, for she moves in the first circle. Wax-candles in the schoolroom! You may imagine how desirable! Of all houses in the kingdom Mrs. Bragge’s is the one I would most wish to see you in.”\n\n“Colonel and Mrs. Campbell are to be in town again by midsummer,” said Jane. “I must spend some time with them; I am sure they will want it;—afterwards I may probably be glad to dispose of myself. But I would not wish you to take the trouble of making any inquiries at present.”\n\n“Trouble! aye, I know your scruples. You are afraid of giving me trouble; but I assure you, my dear Jane, the Campbells can hardly be more interested about you than I am. I shall write to Mrs. Partridge in a day or two, and shall give her a strict charge to be on the look-out for any thing eligible.”\n\n“Thank you, but I would rather you did not mention the subject to her; till the time draws nearer, I do not wish to be giving any body trouble.”\n\n“But, my dear child, the time is drawing near; here is April, and June, or say even July, is very near, with such business to accomplish before us. Your inexperience really amuses me! A situation such as you deserve, and your friends would require for you, is no everyday occurrence, is not obtained at a moment’s notice; indeed, indeed, we must begin inquiring directly.”\n\n“Excuse me, ma’am, but this is by no means my intention; I make no inquiry myself, and should be sorry to have any made by my friends. When I am quite determined as to the time, I am not at all afraid of being long unemployed. There are places in town, offices, where inquiry would soon produce something—Offices for the sale—not quite of human flesh—but of human intellect.”\n\n“Oh! my dear, human flesh! You quite shock me; if you mean a fling at the slave-trade, I assure you Mr. Suckling was always rather a friend to the abolition.”\n\n“I did not mean, I was not thinking of the slave-trade,” replied Jane; “governess-trade, I assure you, was all that I had in view; widely different certainly as to the guilt of those who carry it on; but as to the greater misery of the victims, I do not know where it lies. But I only mean to say that there are advertising offices, and that by applying to them I should have no doubt of very soon meeting with something that would do.”\n\n“Something that would do!” repeated Mrs. Elton. “Aye, that may suit your humble ideas of yourself;—I know what a modest creature you are; but it will not satisfy your friends to have you taking up with any thing that may offer, any inferior, commonplace situation, in a family not moving in a certain circle, or able to command the elegancies of life.”\n\n“You are very obliging; but as to all that, I am very indifferent; it would be no object to me to be with the rich; my mortifications, I think, would only be the greater; I should suffer more from comparison. A gentleman’s family is all that I should condition for.”\n\n“I know you, I know you; you would take up with any thing; but I shall be a little more nice, and I am sure the good Campbells will be quite on my side; with your superior talents, you have a right to move in the first circle. Your musical knowledge alone would entitle you to name your own terms, have as many rooms as you like, and mix in the family as much as you chose;—that is—I do not know—if you knew the harp, you might do all that, I am very sure; but you sing as well as play;—yes, I really believe you might, even without the harp, stipulate for what you chose;—and you must and shall be delightfully, honourably and comfortably settled before the Campbells or I have any rest.”\n\n“You may well class the delight, the honour, and the comfort of such a situation together,” said Jane, “they are pretty sure to be equal; however, I am very serious in not wishing any thing to be attempted at present for me. I am exceedingly obliged to you, Mrs. Elton, I am obliged to any body who feels for me, but I am quite serious in wishing nothing to be done till the summer. For two or three months longer I shall remain where I am, and as I am.”\n\n“And I am quite serious too, I assure you,” replied Mrs. Elton gaily, “in resolving to be always on the watch, and employing my friends to watch also, that nothing really unexceptionable may pass us.”\n\nIn this style she ran on; never thoroughly stopped by any thing till Mr. Woodhouse came into the room; her vanity had then a change of object, and Emma heard her saying in the same half-whisper to Jane,\n\n“Here comes this dear old beau of mine, I protest!—Only think of his gallantry in coming away before the other men!—what a dear creature he is;—I assure you I like him excessively. I admire all that quaint, old-fashioned politeness; it is much more to my taste than modern ease; modern ease often disgusts me. But this good old Mr. Woodhouse, I wish you had heard his gallant speeches to me at dinner. Oh! I assure you I began to think my cara sposo would be absolutely jealous. I fancy I am rather a favourite; he took notice of my gown. How do you like it?—Selina’s choice—handsome, I think, but I do not know whether it is not over-trimmed; I have the greatest dislike to the idea of being over-trimmed—quite a horror of finery. I must put on a few ornaments now, because it is expected of me. A bride, you know, must appear like a bride, but my natural taste is all for simplicity; a simple style of dress is so infinitely preferable to finery. But I am quite in the minority, I believe; few people seem to value simplicity of dress,—show and finery are every thing. I have some notion of putting such a trimming as this to my white and silver poplin. Do you think it will look well?”\n\nThe whole party were but just reassembled in the drawing-room when Mr. Weston made his appearance among them. He had returned to a late dinner, and walked to Hartfield as soon as it was over. He had been too much expected by the best judges, for surprize—but there was great joy. Mr. Woodhouse was almost as glad to see him now, as he would have been sorry to see him before. John Knightley only was in mute astonishment.—That a man who might have spent his evening quietly at home after a day of business in London, should set off again, and walk half a mile to another man’s house, for the sake of being in mixed company till bed-time, of finishing his day in the efforts of civility and the noise of numbers, was a circumstance to strike him deeply. A man who had been in motion since eight o’clock in the morning, and might now have been still, who had been long talking, and might have been silent, who had been in more than one crowd, and might have been alone!—Such a man, to quit the tranquillity and independence of his own fireside, and on the evening of a cold sleety April day rush out again into the world!—Could he by a touch of his finger have instantly taken back his wife, there would have been a motive; but his coming would probably prolong rather than break up the party. John Knightley looked at him with amazement, then shrugged his shoulders, and said, “I could not have believed it even of him.”\n\nMr. Weston meanwhile, perfectly unsuspicious of the indignation he was exciting, happy and cheerful as usual, and with all the right of being principal talker, which a day spent anywhere from home confers, was making himself agreeable among the rest; and having satisfied the inquiries of his wife as to his dinner, convincing her that none of all her careful directions to the servants had been forgotten, and spread abroad what public news he had heard, was proceeding to a family communication, which, though principally addressed to Mrs. Weston, he had not the smallest doubt of being highly interesting to every body in the room. He gave her a letter, it was from Frank, and to herself; he had met with it in his way, and had taken the liberty of opening it.\n\n“Read it, read it,” said he, “it will give you pleasure; only a few lines—will not take you long; read it to Emma.”\n\nThe two ladies looked over it together; and he sat smiling and talking to them the whole time, in a voice a little subdued, but very audible to every body.\n\n“Well, he is coming, you see; good news, I think. Well, what do you say to it?—I always told you he would be here again soon, did not I?—Anne, my dear, did not I always tell you so, and you would not believe me?—In town next week, you see—at the latest, I dare say; for she is as impatient as the black gentleman when any thing is to be done; most likely they will be there to-morrow or Saturday. As to her illness, all nothing of course. But it is an excellent thing to have Frank among us again, so near as town. They will stay a good while when they do come, and he will be half his time with us. This is precisely what I wanted. Well, pretty good news, is not it? Have you finished it? Has Emma read it all? Put it up, put it up; we will have a good talk about it some other time, but it will not do now. I shall only just mention the circumstance to the others in a common way.”\n\nMrs. Weston was most comfortably pleased on the occasion. Her looks and words had nothing to restrain them. She was happy, she knew she was happy, and knew she ought to be happy. Her congratulations were warm and open; but Emma could not speak so fluently. She was a little occupied in weighing her own feelings, and trying to understand the degree of her agitation, which she rather thought was considerable.\n\nMr. Weston, however, too eager to be very observant, too communicative to want others to talk, was very well satisfied with what she did say, and soon moved away to make the rest of his friends happy by a partial communication of what the whole room must have overheard already.\n\nIt was well that he took every body’s joy for granted, or he might not have thought either Mr. Woodhouse or Mr. Knightley particularly delighted. They were the first entitled, after Mrs. Weston and Emma, to be made happy;—from them he would have proceeded to Miss Fairfax, but she was so deep in conversation with John Knightley, that it would have been too positive an interruption; and finding himself close to Mrs. Elton, and her attention disengaged, he necessarily began on the subject with her.\n\n# Volume II — Chapter XVIII\n\n“I hope I shall soon have the pleasure of introducing my son to you,” said Mr. Weston.\n\nMrs. Elton, very willing to suppose a particular compliment intended her by such a hope, smiled most graciously.\n\n“You have heard of a certain Frank Churchill, I presume,” he continued—“and know him to be my son, though he does not bear my name.”\n\n“Oh! yes, and I shall be very happy in his acquaintance. I am sure Mr. Elton will lose no time in calling on him; and we shall both have great pleasure in seeing him at the Vicarage.”\n\n“You are very obliging.—Frank will be extremely happy, I am sure.— He is to be in town next week, if not sooner. We have notice of it in a letter to-day. I met the letters in my way this morning, and seeing my son’s hand, presumed to open it—though it was not directed to me—it was to Mrs. Weston. She is his principal correspondent, I assure you. I hardly ever get a letter.”\n\n“And so you absolutely opened what was directed to her! Oh! Mr. Weston—(laughing affectedly) I must protest against that.—A most dangerous precedent indeed!—I beg you will not let your neighbours follow your example.—Upon my word, if this is what I am to expect, we married women must begin to exert ourselves!—Oh! Mr. Weston, I could not have believed it of you!”\n\n“Aye, we men are sad fellows. You must take care of yourself, Mrs. Elton.—This letter tells us—it is a short letter—written in a hurry, merely to give us notice—it tells us that they are all coming up to town directly, on Mrs. Churchill’s account—she has not been well the whole winter, and thinks Enscombe too cold for her—so they are all to move southward without loss of time.”\n\n“Indeed!—from Yorkshire, I think. Enscombe is in Yorkshire?”\n\n“Yes, they are about one hundred and ninety miles from London, a considerable journey.”\n\n“Yes, upon my word, very considerable. Sixty-five miles farther than from Maple Grove to London. But what is distance, Mr. Weston, to people of large fortune?—You would be amazed to hear how my brother, Mr. Suckling, sometimes flies about. You will hardly believe me—but twice in one week he and Mr. Bragge went to London and back again with four horses.”\n\n“The evil of the distance from Enscombe,” said Mr. Weston, “is, that Mrs. Churchill, as we understand, has not been able to leave the sofa for a week together. In Frank’s last letter she complained, he said, of being too weak to get into her conservatory without having both his arm and his uncle’s! This, you know, speaks a great degree of weakness—but now she is so impatient to be in town, that she means to sleep only two nights on the road.—So Frank writes word. Certainly, delicate ladies have very extraordinary constitutions, Mrs. Elton. You must grant me that.”\n\n“No, indeed, I shall grant you nothing. I always take the part of my own sex. I do indeed. I give you notice—You will find me a formidable antagonist on that point. I always stand up for women—and I assure you, if you knew how Selina feels with respect to sleeping at an inn, you would not wonder at Mrs. Churchill’s making incredible exertions to avoid it. Selina says it is quite horror to her—and I believe I have caught a little of her nicety. She always travels with her own sheets; an excellent precaution. Does Mrs. Churchill do the same?”\n\n“Depend upon it, Mrs. Churchill does every thing that any other fine lady ever did. Mrs. Churchill will not be second to any lady in the land for”—\n\nMrs. Elton eagerly interposed with,\n\n“Oh! Mr. Weston, do not mistake me. Selina is no fine lady, I assure you. Do not run away with such an idea.”\n\n“Is not she? Then she is no rule for Mrs. Churchill, who is as thorough a fine lady as any body ever beheld.”\n\nMrs. Elton began to think she had been wrong in disclaiming so warmly. It was by no means her object to have it believed that her sister was not a fine lady; perhaps there was want of spirit in the pretence of it;—and she was considering in what way she had best retract, when Mr. Weston went on.\n\n“Mrs. Churchill is not much in my good graces, as you may suspect—but this is quite between ourselves. She is very fond of Frank, and therefore I would not speak ill of her. Besides, she is out of health now; but that indeed, by her own account, she has always been. I would not say so to every body, Mrs. Elton, but I have not much faith in Mrs. Churchill’s illness.”\n\n“If she is really ill, why not go to Bath, Mr. Weston?—To Bath, or to Clifton?” “She has taken it into her head that Enscombe is too cold for her. The fact is, I suppose, that she is tired of Enscombe. She has now been a longer time stationary there, than she ever was before, and she begins to want change. It is a retired place. A fine place, but very retired.”\n\n“Aye—like Maple Grove, I dare say. Nothing can stand more retired from the road than Maple Grove. Such an immense plantation all round it! You seem shut out from every thing—in the most complete retirement.—And Mrs. Churchill probably has not health or spirits like Selina to enjoy that sort of seclusion. Or, perhaps she may not have resources enough in herself to be qualified for a country life. I always say a woman cannot have too many resources—and I feel very thankful that I have so many myself as to be quite independent of society.”\n\n“Frank was here in February for a fortnight.”\n\n“So I remember to have heard. He will find an addition to the society of Highbury when he comes again; that is, if I may presume to call myself an addition. But perhaps he may never have heard of there being such a creature in the world.”\n\nThis was too loud a call for a compliment to be passed by, and Mr. Weston, with a very good grace, immediately exclaimed,\n\n“My dear madam! Nobody but yourself could imagine such a thing possible. Not heard of you!—I believe Mrs. Weston’s letters lately have been full of very little else than Mrs. Elton.”\n\nHe had done his duty and could return to his son.\n\n“When Frank left us,” continued he, “it was quite uncertain when we might see him again, which makes this day’s news doubly welcome. It has been completely unexpected. That is, I always had a strong persuasion he would be here again soon, I was sure something favourable would turn up—but nobody believed me. He and Mrs. Weston were both dreadfully desponding. ‘How could he contrive to come? And how could it be supposed that his uncle and aunt would spare him again?’ and so forth—I always felt that something would happen in our favour; and so it has, you see. I have observed, Mrs. Elton, in the course of my life, that if things are going untowardly one month, they are sure to mend the next.”\n\n“Very true, Mr. Weston, perfectly true. It is just what I used to say to a certain gentleman in company in the days of courtship, when, because things did not go quite right, did not proceed with all the rapidity which suited his feelings, he was apt to be in despair, and exclaim that he was sure at this rate it would be May before Hymen’s saffron robe would be put on for us. Oh! the pains I have been at to dispel those gloomy ideas and give him cheerfuller views! The carriage—we had disappointments about the carriage;—one morning, I remember, he came to me quite in despair.”\n\nShe was stopped by a slight fit of coughing, and Mr. Weston instantly seized the opportunity of going on.\n\n“You were mentioning May. May is the very month which Mrs. Churchill is ordered, or has ordered herself, to spend in some warmer place than Enscombe—in short, to spend in London; so that we have the agreeable prospect of frequent visits from Frank the whole spring—precisely the season of the year which one should have chosen for it: days almost at the longest; weather genial and pleasant, always inviting one out, and never too hot for exercise. When he was here before, we made the best of it; but there was a good deal of wet, damp, cheerless weather; there always is in February, you know, and we could not do half that we intended. Now will be the time. This will be complete enjoyment; and I do not know, Mrs. Elton, whether the uncertainty of our meetings, the sort of constant expectation there will be of his coming in to-day or to-morrow, and at any hour, may not be more friendly to happiness than having him actually in the house. I think it is so. I think it is the state of mind which gives most spirit and delight. I hope you will be pleased with my son; but you must not expect a prodigy. He is generally thought a fine young man, but do not expect a prodigy. Mrs. Weston’s partiality for him is very great, and, as you may suppose, most gratifying to me. She thinks nobody equal to him.”\n\n“And I assure you, Mr. Weston, I have very little doubt that my opinion will be decidedly in his favour. I have heard so much in praise of Mr. Frank Churchill.—At the same time it is fair to observe, that I am one of those who always judge for themselves, and are by no means implicitly guided by others. I give you notice that as I find your son, so I shall judge of him.—I am no flatterer.”\n\nMr. Weston was musing.\n\n“I hope,” said he presently, “I have not been severe upon poor Mrs. Churchill. If she is ill I should be sorry to do her injustice; but there are some traits in her character which make it difficult for me to speak of her with the forbearance I could wish. You cannot be ignorant, Mrs. Elton, of my connexion with the family, nor of the treatment I have met with; and, between ourselves, the whole blame of it is to be laid to her. She was the instigator. Frank’s mother would never have been slighted as she was but for her. Mr. Churchill has pride; but his pride is nothing to his wife’s: his is a quiet, indolent, gentlemanlike sort of pride that would harm nobody, and only make himself a little helpless and tiresome; but her pride is arrogance and insolence! And what inclines one less to bear, she has no fair pretence of family or blood. She was nobody when he married her, barely the daughter of a gentleman; but ever since her being turned into a Churchill she has out-Churchill’d them all in high and mighty claims: but in herself, I assure you, she is an upstart.”\n\n“Only think! well, that must be infinitely provoking! I have quite a horror of upstarts. Maple Grove has given me a thorough disgust to people of that sort; for there is a family in that neighbourhood who are such an annoyance to my brother and sister from the airs they give themselves! Your description of Mrs. Churchill made me think of them directly. People of the name of Tupman, very lately settled there, and encumbered with many low connexions, but giving themselves immense airs, and expecting to be on a footing with the old established families. A year and a half is the very utmost that they can have lived at West Hall; and how they got their fortune nobody knows. They came from Birmingham, which is not a place to promise much, you know, Mr. Weston. One has not great hopes from Birmingham. I always say there is something direful in the sound: but nothing more is positively known of the Tupmans, though a good many things I assure you are suspected; and yet by their manners they evidently think themselves equal even to my brother, Mr. Suckling, who happens to be one of their nearest neighbours. It is infinitely too bad. Mr. Suckling, who has been eleven years a resident at Maple Grove, and whose father had it before him—I believe, at least—I am almost sure that old Mr. Suckling had completed the purchase before his death.”\n\nThey were interrupted. Tea was carrying round, and Mr. Weston, having said all that he wanted, soon took the opportunity of walking away.\n\nAfter tea, Mr. and Mrs. Weston, and Mr. Elton sat down with Mr. Woodhouse to cards. The remaining five were left to their own powers, and Emma doubted their getting on very well; for Mr. Knightley seemed little disposed for conversation; Mrs. Elton was wanting notice, which nobody had inclination to pay, and she was herself in a worry of spirits which would have made her prefer being silent.\n\nMr. John Knightley proved more talkative than his brother. He was to leave them early the next day; and he soon began with—\n\n“Well, Emma, I do not believe I have any thing more to say about the boys; but you have your sister’s letter, and every thing is down at full length there we may be sure. My charge would be much more concise than her’s, and probably not much in the same spirit; all that I have to recommend being comprised in, do not spoil them, and do not physic them.”\n\n“I rather hope to satisfy you both,” said Emma, “for I shall do all in my power to make them happy, which will be enough for Isabella; and happiness must preclude false indulgence and physic.”\n\n“And if you find them troublesome, you must send them home again.”\n\n“That is very likely. You think so, do not you?”\n\n“I hope I am aware that they may be too noisy for your father—or even may be some encumbrance to you, if your visiting engagements continue to increase as much as they have done lately.”\n\n“Increase!”\n\n“Certainly; you must be sensible that the last half-year has made a great difference in your way of life.”\n\n“Difference! No indeed I am not.”\n\n“There can be no doubt of your being much more engaged with company than you used to be. Witness this very time. Here am I come down for only one day, and you are engaged with a dinner-party!—When did it happen before, or any thing like it? Your neighbourhood is increasing, and you mix more with it. A little while ago, every letter to Isabella brought an account of fresh gaieties; dinners at Mr. Cole’s, or balls at the Crown. The difference which Randalls, Randalls alone makes in your goings-on, is very great.”\n\n“Yes,” said his brother quickly, “it is Randalls that does it all.”\n\n“Very well—and as Randalls, I suppose, is not likely to have less influence than heretofore, it strikes me as a possible thing, Emma, that Henry and John may be sometimes in the way. And if they are, I only beg you to send them home.”\n\n“No,” cried Mr. Knightley, “that need not be the consequence. Let them be sent to Donwell. I shall certainly be at leisure.”\n\n“Upon my word,” exclaimed Emma, “you amuse me! I should like to know how many of all my numerous engagements take place without your being of the party; and why I am to be supposed in danger of wanting leisure to attend to the little boys. These amazing engagements of mine—what have they been? Dining once with the Coles—and having a ball talked of, which never took place. I can understand you—(nodding at Mr. John Knightley)—your good fortune in meeting with so many of your friends at once here, delights you too much to pass unnoticed. But you, (turning to Mr. Knightley,) who know how very, very seldom I am ever two hours from Hartfield, why you should foresee such a series of dissipation for me, I cannot imagine. And as to my dear little boys, I must say, that if Aunt Emma has not time for them, I do not think they would fare much better with Uncle Knightley, who is absent from home about five hours where she is absent one—and who, when he is at home, is either reading to himself or settling his accounts.”\n\nMr. Knightley seemed to be trying not to smile; and succeeded without difficulty, upon Mrs. Elton’s beginning to talk to him.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter I\n\nA very little quiet reflection was enough to satisfy Emma as to the nature of her agitation on hearing this news of Frank Churchill. She was soon convinced that it was not for herself she was feeling at all apprehensive or embarrassed; it was for him. Her own attachment had really subsided into a mere nothing; it was not worth thinking of;—but if he, who had undoubtedly been always so much the most in love of the two, were to be returning with the same warmth of sentiment which he had taken away, it would be very distressing. If a separation of two months should not have cooled him, there were dangers and evils before her:—caution for him and for herself would be necessary. She did not mean to have her own affections entangled again, and it would be incumbent on her to avoid any encouragement of his.\n\nShe wished she might be able to keep him from an absolute declaration. That would be so very painful a conclusion of their present acquaintance! and yet, she could not help rather anticipating something decisive. She felt as if the spring would not pass without bringing a crisis, an event, a something to alter her present composed and tranquil state.\n\nIt was not very long, though rather longer than Mr. Weston had foreseen, before she had the power of forming some opinion of Frank Churchill’s feelings. The Enscombe family were not in town quite so soon as had been imagined, but he was at Highbury very soon afterwards. He rode down for a couple of hours; he could not yet do more; but as he came from Randalls immediately to Hartfield, she could then exercise all her quick observation, and speedily determine how he was influenced, and how she must act. They met with the utmost friendliness. There could be no doubt of his great pleasure in seeing her. But she had an almost instant doubt of his caring for her as he had done, of his feeling the same tenderness in the same degree. She watched him well. It was a clear thing he was less in love than he had been. Absence, with the conviction probably of her indifference, had produced this very natural and very desirable effect.\n\nHe was in high spirits; as ready to talk and laugh as ever, and seemed delighted to speak of his former visit, and recur to old stories: and he was not without agitation. It was not in his calmness that she read his comparative indifference. He was not calm; his spirits were evidently fluttered; there was restlessness about him. Lively as he was, it seemed a liveliness that did not satisfy himself; but what decided her belief on the subject, was his staying only a quarter of an hour, and hurrying away to make other calls in Highbury. “He had seen a group of old acquaintance in the street as he passed—he had not stopped, he would not stop for more than a word—but he had the vanity to think they would be disappointed if he did not call, and much as he wished to stay longer at Hartfield, he must hurry off.” She had no doubt as to his being less in love—but neither his agitated spirits, nor his hurrying away, seemed like a perfect cure; and she was rather inclined to think it implied a dread of her returning power, and a discreet resolution of not trusting himself with her long.\n\nThis was the only visit from Frank Churchill in the course of ten days. He was often hoping, intending to come—but was always prevented. His aunt could not bear to have him leave her. Such was his own account at Randall’s. If he were quite sincere, if he really tried to come, it was to be inferred that Mrs. Churchill’s removal to London had been of no service to the wilful or nervous part of her disorder. That she was really ill was very certain; he had declared himself convinced of it, at Randalls. Though much might be fancy, he could not doubt, when he looked back, that she was in a weaker state of health than she had been half a year ago. He did not believe it to proceed from any thing that care and medicine might not remove, or at least that she might not have many years of existence before her; but he could not be prevailed on, by all his father’s doubts, to say that her complaints were merely imaginary, or that she was as strong as ever.\n\nIt soon appeared that London was not the place for her. She could not endure its noise. Her nerves were under continual irritation and suffering; and by the ten days’ end, her nephew’s letter to Randalls communicated a change of plan. They were going to remove immediately to Richmond. Mrs. Churchill had been recommended to the medical skill of an eminent person there, and had otherwise a fancy for the place. A ready-furnished house in a favourite spot was engaged, and much benefit expected from the change.\n\nEmma heard that Frank wrote in the highest spirits of this arrangement, and seemed most fully to appreciate the blessing of having two months before him of such near neighbourhood to many dear friends—for the house was taken for May and June. She was told that now he wrote with the greatest confidence of being often with them, almost as often as he could even wish.\n\nEmma saw how Mr. Weston understood these joyous prospects. He was considering her as the source of all the happiness they offered. She hoped it was not so. Two months must bring it to the proof.\n\nMr. Weston’s own happiness was indisputable. He was quite delighted. It was the very circumstance he could have wished for. Now, it would be really having Frank in their neighbourhood. What were nine miles to a young man?—An hour’s ride. He would be always coming over. The difference in that respect of Richmond and London was enough to make the whole difference of seeing him always and seeing him never. Sixteen miles—nay, eighteen—it must be full eighteen to Manchester-street—was a serious obstacle. Were he ever able to get away, the day would be spent in coming and returning. There was no comfort in having him in London; he might as well be at Enscombe; but Richmond was the very distance for easy intercourse. Better than nearer!\n\nOne good thing was immediately brought to a certainty by this removal,—the ball at the Crown. It had not been forgotten before, but it had been soon acknowledged vain to attempt to fix a day. Now, however, it was absolutely to be; every preparation was resumed, and very soon after the Churchills had removed to Richmond, a few lines from Frank, to say that his aunt felt already much better for the change, and that he had no doubt of being able to join them for twenty-four hours at any given time, induced them to name as early a day as possible.\n\nMr. Weston’s ball was to be a real thing. A very few to-morrows stood between the young people of Highbury and happiness.\n\nMr. Woodhouse was resigned. The time of year lightened the evil to him. May was better for every thing than February. Mrs. Bates was engaged to spend the evening at Hartfield, James had due notice, and he sanguinely hoped that neither dear little Henry nor dear little John would have any thing the matter with them, while dear Emma were gone.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter II\n\nNo misfortune occurred, again to prevent the ball. The day approached, the day arrived; and after a morning of some anxious watching, Frank Churchill, in all the certainty of his own self, reached Randalls before dinner, and every thing was safe.\n\nNo second meeting had there yet been between him and Emma. The room at the Crown was to witness it;—but it would be better than a common meeting in a crowd. Mr. Weston had been so very earnest in his entreaties for her arriving there as soon as possible after themselves, for the purpose of taking her opinion as to the propriety and comfort of the rooms before any other persons came, that she could not refuse him, and must therefore spend some quiet interval in the young man’s company. She was to convey Harriet, and they drove to the Crown in good time, the Randalls party just sufficiently before them.\n\nFrank Churchill seemed to have been on the watch; and though he did not say much, his eyes declared that he meant to have a delightful evening. They all walked about together, to see that every thing was as it should be; and within a few minutes were joined by the contents of another carriage, which Emma could not hear the sound of at first, without great surprize. “So unreasonably early!” she was going to exclaim; but she presently found that it was a family of old friends, who were coming, like herself, by particular desire, to help Mr. Weston’s judgment; and they were so very closely followed by another carriage of cousins, who had been entreated to come early with the same distinguishing earnestness, on the same errand, that it seemed as if half the company might soon be collected together for the purpose of preparatory inspection.\n\nEmma perceived that her taste was not the only taste on which Mr. Weston depended, and felt, that to be the favourite and intimate of a man who had so many intimates and confidantes, was not the very first distinction in the scale of vanity. She liked his open manners, but a little less of open-heartedness would have made him a higher character.—General benevolence, but not general friendship, made a man what he ought to be.—She could fancy such a man. The whole party walked about, and looked, and praised again; and then, having nothing else to do, formed a sort of half-circle round the fire, to observe in their various modes, till other subjects were started, that, though May, a fire in the evening was still very pleasant.\n\nEmma found that it was not Mr. Weston’s fault that the number of privy councillors was not yet larger. They had stopped at Mrs. Bates’s door to offer the use of their carriage, but the aunt and niece were to be brought by the Eltons.\n\nFrank was standing by her, but not steadily; there was a restlessness, which shewed a mind not at ease. He was looking about, he was going to the door, he was watching for the sound of other carriages,—impatient to begin, or afraid of being always near her.\n\nMrs. Elton was spoken of. “I think she must be here soon,” said he. “I have a great curiosity to see Mrs. Elton, I have heard so much of her. It cannot be long, I think, before she comes.”\n\nA carriage was heard. He was on the move immediately; but coming back, said,\n\n“I am forgetting that I am not acquainted with her. I have never seen either Mr. or Mrs. Elton. I have no business to put myself forward.”\n\nMr. and Mrs. Elton appeared; and all the smiles and the proprieties passed.\n\n“But Miss Bates and Miss Fairfax!” said Mr. Weston, looking about. “We thought you were to bring them.”\n\nThe mistake had been slight. The carriage was sent for them now. Emma longed to know what Frank’s first opinion of Mrs. Elton might be; how he was affected by the studied elegance of her dress, and her smiles of graciousness. He was immediately qualifying himself to form an opinion, by giving her very proper attention, after the introduction had passed.\n\nIn a few minutes the carriage returned.—Somebody talked of rain.—“I will see that there are umbrellas, sir,” said Frank to his father: “Miss Bates must not be forgotten:” and away he went. Mr. Weston was following; but Mrs. Elton detained him, to gratify him by her opinion of his son; and so briskly did she begin, that the young man himself, though by no means moving slowly, could hardly be out of hearing.\n\n“A very fine young man indeed, Mr. Weston. You know I candidly told you I should form my own opinion; and I am happy to say that I am extremely pleased with him.—You may believe me. I never compliment. I think him a very handsome young man, and his manners are precisely what I like and approve—so truly the gentleman, without the least conceit or puppyism. You must know I have a vast dislike to puppies—quite a horror of them. They were never tolerated at Maple Grove. Neither Mr. Suckling nor me had ever any patience with them; and we used sometimes to say very cutting things! Selina, who is mild almost to a fault, bore with them much better.”\n\nWhile she talked of his son, Mr. Weston’s attention was chained; but when she got to Maple Grove, he could recollect that there were ladies just arriving to be attended to, and with happy smiles must hurry away.\n\nMrs. Elton turned to Mrs. Weston. “I have no doubt of its being our carriage with Miss Bates and Jane. Our coachman and horses are so extremely expeditious!—I believe we drive faster than any body.—What a pleasure it is to send one’s carriage for a friend!—I understand you were so kind as to offer, but another time it will be quite unnecessary. You may be very sure I shall always take care of them.”\n\nMiss Bates and Miss Fairfax, escorted by the two gentlemen, walked into the room; and Mrs. Elton seemed to think it as much her duty as Mrs. Weston’s to receive them. Her gestures and movements might be understood by any one who looked on like Emma; but her words, every body’s words, were soon lost under the incessant flow of Miss Bates, who came in talking, and had not finished her speech under many minutes after her being admitted into the circle at the fire. As the door opened she was heard,\n\n“So very obliging of you!—No rain at all. Nothing to signify. I do not care for myself. Quite thick shoes. And Jane declares—Well!—(as soon as she was within the door) Well! This is brilliant indeed!—This is admirable!—Excellently contrived, upon my word. Nothing wanting. Could not have imagined it.—So well lighted up!—Jane, Jane, look!—did you ever see any thing? Oh! Mr. Weston, you must really have had Aladdin’s lamp. Good Mrs. Stokes would not know her own room again. I saw her as I came in; she was standing in the entrance. ‘Oh! Mrs. Stokes,’ said I—but I had not time for more.” She was now met by Mrs. Weston.—“Very well, I thank you, ma’am. I hope you are quite well. Very happy to hear it. So afraid you might have a headache!—seeing you pass by so often, and knowing how much trouble you must have. Delighted to hear it indeed. Ah! dear Mrs. Elton, so obliged to you for the carriage!—excellent time. Jane and I quite ready. Did not keep the horses a moment. Most comfortable carriage.—Oh! and I am sure our thanks are due to you, Mrs. Weston, on that score. Mrs. Elton had most kindly sent Jane a note, or we should have been.—But two such offers in one day!—Never were such neighbours. I said to my mother, ‘Upon my word, ma’am—.’ Thank you, my mother is remarkably well. Gone to Mr. Woodhouse’s. I made her take her shawl—for the evenings are not warm—her large new shawl— Mrs. Dixon’s wedding-present.—So kind of her to think of my mother! Bought at Weymouth, you know—Mr. Dixon’s choice. There were three others, Jane says, which they hesitated about some time. Colonel Campbell rather preferred an olive. My dear Jane, are you sure you did not wet your feet?—It was but a drop or two, but I am so afraid:—but Mr. Frank Churchill was so extremely—and there was a mat to step upon—I shall never forget his extreme politeness.—Oh! Mr. Frank Churchill, I must tell you my mother’s spectacles have never been in fault since; the rivet never came out again. My mother often talks of your good-nature. Does not she, Jane?—Do not we often talk of Mr. Frank Churchill?—Ah! here’s Miss Woodhouse.—Dear Miss Woodhouse, how do you do?—Very well I thank you, quite well. This is meeting quite in fairy-land!—Such a transformation!—Must not compliment, I know (eyeing Emma most complacently)—that would be rude—but upon my word, Miss Woodhouse, you do look—how do you like Jane’s hair?—You are a judge.—She did it all herself. Quite wonderful how she does her hair!—No hairdresser from London I think could.—Ah! Dr. Hughes I declare—and Mrs. Hughes. Must go and speak to Dr. and Mrs. Hughes for a moment.—How do you do? How do you do?—Very well, I thank you. This is delightful, is not it?—Where’s dear Mr. Richard?—Oh! there he is. Don’t disturb him. Much better employed talking to the young ladies. How do you do, Mr. Richard?—I saw you the other day as you rode through the town—Mrs. Otway, I protest!—and good Mr. Otway, and Miss Otway and Miss Caroline.—Such a host of friends!—and Mr. George and Mr. Arthur!—How do you do? How do you all do?—Quite well, I am much obliged to you. Never better.—Don’t I hear another carriage?—Who can this be?—very likely the worthy Coles.—Upon my word, this is charming to be standing about among such friends! And such a noble fire!—I am quite roasted. No coffee, I thank you, for me—never take coffee.—A little tea if you please, sir, by and bye,—no hurry—Oh! here it comes. Every thing so good!”\n\nFrank Churchill returned to his station by Emma; and as soon as Miss Bates was quiet, she found herself necessarily overhearing the discourse of Mrs. Elton and Miss Fairfax, who were standing a little way behind her.—He was thoughtful. Whether he were overhearing too, she could not determine. After a good many compliments to Jane on her dress and look, compliments very quietly and properly taken, Mrs. Elton was evidently wanting to be complimented herself—and it was, “How do you like my gown?—How do you like my trimming?—How has Wright done my hair?”—with many other relative questions, all answered with patient politeness. Mrs. Elton then said, “Nobody can think less of dress in general than I do—but upon such an occasion as this, when every body’s eyes are so much upon me, and in compliment to the Westons—who I have no doubt are giving this ball chiefly to do me honour—I would not wish to be inferior to others. And I see very few pearls in the room except mine.—So Frank Churchill is a capital dancer, I understand.—We shall see if our styles suit.—A fine young man certainly is Frank Churchill. I like him very well.”\n\nAt this moment Frank began talking so vigorously, that Emma could not but imagine he had overheard his own praises, and did not want to hear more;—and the voices of the ladies were drowned for a while, till another suspension brought Mrs. Elton’s tones again distinctly forward.—Mr. Elton had just joined them, and his wife was exclaiming,\n\n“Oh! you have found us out at last, have you, in our seclusion?—I was this moment telling Jane, I thought you would begin to be impatient for tidings of us.”\n\n“Jane!”—repeated Frank Churchill, with a look of surprize and displeasure.—“That is easy—but Miss Fairfax does not disapprove it, I suppose.”\n\n“How do you like Mrs. Elton?” said Emma in a whisper.\n\n“Not at all.”\n\n“You are ungrateful.”\n\n“Ungrateful!—What do you mean?” Then changing from a frown to a smile—“No, do not tell me—I do not want to know what you mean.—Where is my father?—When are we to begin dancing?”\n\nEmma could hardly understand him; he seemed in an odd humour. He walked off to find his father, but was quickly back again with both Mr. and Mrs. Weston. He had met with them in a little perplexity, which must be laid before Emma. It had just occurred to Mrs. Weston that Mrs. Elton must be asked to begin the ball; that she would expect it; which interfered with all their wishes of giving Emma that distinction.—Emma heard the sad truth with fortitude.\n\n“And what are we to do for a proper partner for her?” said Mr. Weston. “She will think Frank ought to ask her.”\n\nFrank turned instantly to Emma, to claim her former promise; and boasted himself an engaged man, which his father looked his most perfect approbation of—and it then appeared that Mrs. Weston was wanting him to dance with Mrs. Elton himself, and that their business was to help to persuade him into it, which was done pretty soon.—Mr. Weston and Mrs. Elton led the way, Mr. Frank Churchill and Miss Woodhouse followed. Emma must submit to stand second to Mrs. Elton, though she had always considered the ball as peculiarly for her. It was almost enough to make her think of marrying. Mrs. Elton had undoubtedly the advantage, at this time, in vanity completely gratified; for though she had intended to begin with Frank Churchill, she could not lose by the change. Mr. Weston might be his son’s superior.—In spite of this little rub, however, Emma was smiling with enjoyment, delighted to see the respectable length of the set as it was forming, and to feel that she had so many hours of unusual festivity before her.—She was more disturbed by Mr. Knightley’s not dancing than by any thing else.—There he was, among the standers-by, where he ought not to be; he ought to be dancing,—not classing himself with the husbands, and fathers, and whist-players, who were pretending to feel an interest in the dance till their rubbers were made up,—so young as he looked!—He could not have appeared to greater advantage perhaps anywhere, than where he had placed himself. His tall, firm, upright figure, among the bulky forms and stooping shoulders of the elderly men, was such as Emma felt must draw every body’s eyes; and, excepting her own partner, there was not one among the whole row of young men who could be compared with him.—He moved a few steps nearer, and those few steps were enough to prove in how gentlemanlike a manner, with what natural grace, he must have danced, would he but take the trouble.—Whenever she caught his eye, she forced him to smile; but in general he was looking grave. She wished he could love a ballroom better, and could like Frank Churchill better.—He seemed often observing her. She must not flatter herself that he thought of her dancing, but if he were criticising her behaviour, she did not feel afraid. There was nothing like flirtation between her and her partner. They seemed more like cheerful, easy friends, than lovers. That Frank Churchill thought less of her than he had done, was indubitable.\n\nThe ball proceeded pleasantly. The anxious cares, the incessant attentions of Mrs. Weston, were not thrown away. Every body seemed happy; and the praise of being a delightful ball, which is seldom bestowed till after a ball has ceased to be, was repeatedly given in the very beginning of the existence of this. Of very important, very recordable events, it was not more productive than such meetings usually are. There was one, however, which Emma thought something of.—The two last dances before supper were begun, and Harriet had no partner;—the only young lady sitting down;—and so equal had been hitherto the number of dancers, that how there could be any one disengaged was the wonder!—But Emma’s wonder lessened soon afterwards, on seeing Mr. Elton sauntering about. He would not ask Harriet to dance if it were possible to be avoided: she was sure he would not—and she was expecting him every moment to escape into the card-room.\n\nEscape, however, was not his plan. He came to the part of the room where the sitters-by were collected, spoke to some, and walked about in front of them, as if to shew his liberty, and his resolution of maintaining it. He did not omit being sometimes directly before Miss Smith, or speaking to those who were close to her.—Emma saw it. She was not yet dancing; she was working her way up from the bottom, and had therefore leisure to look around, and by only turning her head a little she saw it all. When she was half-way up the set, the whole group were exactly behind her, and she would no longer allow her eyes to watch; but Mr. Elton was so near, that she heard every syllable of a dialogue which just then took place between him and Mrs. Weston; and she perceived that his wife, who was standing immediately above her, was not only listening also, but even encouraging him by significant glances.—The kind-hearted, gentle Mrs. Weston had left her seat to join him and say, “Do not you dance, Mr. Elton?” to which his prompt reply was, “Most readily, Mrs. Weston, if you will dance with me.”\n\n“Me!—oh! no—I would get you a better partner than myself. I am no dancer.”\n\n“If Mrs. Gilbert wishes to dance,” said he, “I shall have great pleasure, I am sure—for, though beginning to feel myself rather an old married man, and that my dancing days are over, it would give me very great pleasure at any time to stand up with an old friend like Mrs. Gilbert.”\n\n“Mrs. Gilbert does not mean to dance, but there is a young lady disengaged whom I should be very glad to see dancing—Miss Smith.” “Miss Smith!—oh!—I had not observed.—You are extremely obliging—and if I were not an old married man.—But my dancing days are over, Mrs. Weston. You will excuse me. Any thing else I should be most happy to do, at your command—but my dancing days are over.”\n\nMrs. Weston said no more; and Emma could imagine with what surprize and mortification she must be returning to her seat. This was Mr. Elton! the amiable, obliging, gentle Mr. Elton.—She looked round for a moment; he had joined Mr. Knightley at a little distance, and was arranging himself for settled conversation, while smiles of high glee passed between him and his wife.\n\nShe would not look again. Her heart was in a glow, and she feared her face might be as hot.\n\nIn another moment a happier sight caught her;—Mr. Knightley leading Harriet to the set!—Never had she been more surprized, seldom more delighted, than at that instant. She was all pleasure and gratitude, both for Harriet and herself, and longed to be thanking him; and though too distant for speech, her countenance said much, as soon as she could catch his eye again.\n\nHis dancing proved to be just what she had believed it, extremely good; and Harriet would have seemed almost too lucky, if it had not been for the cruel state of things before, and for the very complete enjoyment and very high sense of the distinction which her happy features announced. It was not thrown away on her, she bounded higher than ever, flew farther down the middle, and was in a continual course of smiles.\n\nMr. Elton had retreated into the card-room, looking (Emma trusted) very foolish. She did not think he was quite so hardened as his wife, though growing very like her;—she spoke some of her feelings, by observing audibly to her partner,\n\n“Knightley has taken pity on poor little Miss Smith!—Very good-natured, I declare.”\n\nSupper was announced. The move began; and Miss Bates might be heard from that moment, without interruption, till her being seated at table and taking up her spoon.\n\n“Jane, Jane, my dear Jane, where are you?—Here is your tippet. Mrs. Weston begs you to put on your tippet. She says she is afraid there will be draughts in the passage, though every thing has been done—One door nailed up—Quantities of matting—My dear Jane, indeed you must. Mr. Churchill, oh! you are too obliging! How well you put it on!—so gratified! Excellent dancing indeed!—Yes, my dear, I ran home, as I said I should, to help grandmama to bed, and got back again, and nobody missed me.—I set off without saying a word, just as I told you. Grandmama was quite well, had a charming evening with Mr. Woodhouse, a vast deal of chat, and backgammon.—Tea was made downstairs, biscuits and baked apples and wine before she came away: amazing luck in some of her throws: and she inquired a great deal about you, how you were amused, and who were your partners. ‘Oh!’ said I, ‘I shall not forestall Jane; I left her dancing with Mr. George Otway; she will love to tell you all about it herself to-morrow: her first partner was Mr. Elton, I do not know who will ask her next, perhaps Mr. William Cox.’ My dear sir, you are too obliging.—Is there nobody you would not rather?—I am not helpless. Sir, you are most kind. Upon my word, Jane on one arm, and me on the other!—Stop, stop, let us stand a little back, Mrs. Elton is going; dear Mrs. Elton, how elegant she looks!—Beautiful lace!—Now we all follow in her train. Quite the queen of the evening!—Well, here we are at the passage. Two steps, Jane, take care of the two steps. Oh! no, there is but one. Well, I was persuaded there were two. How very odd! I was convinced there were two, and there is but one. I never saw any thing equal to the comfort and style—Candles everywhere.—I was telling you of your grandmama, Jane,—There was a little disappointment.—The baked apples and biscuits, excellent in their way, you know; but there was a delicate fricassee of sweetbread and some asparagus brought in at first, and good Mr. Woodhouse, not thinking the asparagus quite boiled enough, sent it all out again. Now there is nothing grandmama loves better than sweetbread and asparagus—so she was rather disappointed, but we agreed we would not speak of it to any body, for fear of its getting round to dear Miss Woodhouse, who would be so very much concerned!—Well, this is brilliant! I am all amazement! could not have supposed any thing!—Such elegance and profusion!—I have seen nothing like it since—Well, where shall we sit? where shall we sit? Anywhere, so that Jane is not in a draught. Where I sit is of no consequence. Oh! do you recommend this side?—Well, I am sure, Mr. Churchill—only it seems too good—but just as you please. What you direct in this house cannot be wrong. Dear Jane, how shall we ever recollect half the dishes for grandmama? Soup too! Bless me! I should not be helped so soon, but it smells most excellent, and I cannot help beginning.”\n\nEmma had no opportunity of speaking to Mr. Knightley till after supper; but, when they were all in the ballroom again, her eyes invited him irresistibly to come to her and be thanked. He was warm in his reprobation of Mr. Elton’s conduct; it had been unpardonable rudeness; and Mrs. Elton’s looks also received the due share of censure.\n\n“They aimed at wounding more than Harriet,” said he. “Emma, why is it that they are your enemies?”\n\nHe looked with smiling penetration; and, on receiving no answer, added, “She ought not to be angry with you, I suspect, whatever he may be.—To that surmise, you say nothing, of course; but confess, Emma, that you did want him to marry Harriet.”\n\n“I did,” replied Emma, “and they cannot forgive me.”\n\nHe shook his head; but there was a smile of indulgence with it, and he only said,\n\n“I shall not scold you. I leave you to your own reflections.”\n\n“Can you trust me with such flatterers?—Does my vain spirit ever tell me I am wrong?”\n\n“Not your vain spirit, but your serious spirit.—If one leads you wrong, I am sure the other tells you of it.”\n\n“I do own myself to have been completely mistaken in Mr. Elton. There is a littleness about him which you discovered, and which I did not: and I was fully convinced of his being in love with Harriet. It was through a series of strange blunders!”\n\n“And, in return for your acknowledging so much, I will do you the justice to say, that you would have chosen for him better than he has chosen for himself.—Harriet Smith has some first-rate qualities, which Mrs. Elton is totally without. An unpretending, single-minded, artless girl—infinitely to be preferred by any man of sense and taste to such a woman as Mrs. Elton. I found Harriet more conversable than I expected.”\n\nEmma was extremely gratified.—They were interrupted by the bustle of Mr. Weston calling on every body to begin dancing again.\n\n“Come Miss Woodhouse, Miss Otway, Miss Fairfax, what are you all doing?—Come Emma, set your companions the example. Every body is lazy! Every body is asleep!”\n\n“I am ready,” said Emma, “whenever I am wanted.”\n\n“Whom are you going to dance with?” asked Mr. Knightley.\n\nShe hesitated a moment, and then replied, “With you, if you will ask me.”\n\n“Will you?” said he, offering his hand.\n\n“Indeed I will. You have shewn that you can dance, and you know we are not really so much brother and sister as to make it at all improper.”\n\n“Brother and sister! no, indeed.”\n\n# Volume III — Chapter III\n\nThis little explanation with Mr. Knightley gave Emma considerable pleasure. It was one of the agreeable recollections of the ball, which she walked about the lawn the next morning to enjoy.—She was extremely glad that they had come to so good an understanding respecting the Eltons, and that their opinions of both husband and wife were so much alike; and his praise of Harriet, his concession in her favour, was peculiarly gratifying. The impertinence of the Eltons, which for a few minutes had threatened to ruin the rest of her evening, had been the occasion of some of its highest satisfactions; and she looked forward to another happy result—the cure of Harriet’s infatuation.—From Harriet’s manner of speaking of the circumstance before they quitted the ballroom, she had strong hopes. It seemed as if her eyes were suddenly opened, and she were enabled to see that Mr. Elton was not the superior creature she had believed him. The fever was over, and Emma could harbour little fear of the pulse being quickened again by injurious courtesy. She depended on the evil feelings of the Eltons for supplying all the discipline of pointed neglect that could be farther requisite.—Harriet rational, Frank Churchill not too much in love, and Mr. Knightley not wanting to quarrel with her, how very happy a summer must be before her!\n\nShe was not to see Frank Churchill this morning. He had told her that he could not allow himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, as he was to be at home by the middle of the day. She did not regret it.\n\nHaving arranged all these matters, looked them through, and put them all to rights, she was just turning to the house with spirits freshened up for the demands of the two little boys, as well as of their grandpapa, when the great iron sweep-gate opened, and two persons entered whom she had never less expected to see together—Frank Churchill, with Harriet leaning on his arm—actually Harriet!—A moment sufficed to convince her that something extraordinary had happened. Harriet looked white and frightened, and he was trying to cheer her.—The iron gates and the front-door were not twenty yards asunder;—they were all three soon in the hall, and Harriet immediately sinking into a chair fainted away.\n\nA young lady who faints, must be recovered; questions must be answered, and surprizes be explained. Such events are very interesting, but the suspense of them cannot last long. A few minutes made Emma acquainted with the whole.\n\nMiss Smith, and Miss Bickerton, another parlour boarder at Mrs. Goddard’s, who had been also at the ball, had walked out together, and taken a road, the Richmond road, which, though apparently public enough for safety, had led them into alarm.—About half a mile beyond Highbury, making a sudden turn, and deeply shaded by elms on each side, it became for a considerable stretch very retired; and when the young ladies had advanced some way into it, they had suddenly perceived at a small distance before them, on a broader patch of greensward by the side, a party of gipsies. A child on the watch, came towards them to beg; and Miss Bickerton, excessively frightened, gave a great scream, and calling on Harriet to follow her, ran up a steep bank, cleared a slight hedge at the top, and made the best of her way by a short cut back to Highbury. But poor Harriet could not follow. She had suffered very much from cramp after dancing, and her first attempt to mount the bank brought on such a return of it as made her absolutely powerless—and in this state, and exceedingly terrified, she had been obliged to remain.\n\nHow the trampers might have behaved, had the young ladies been more courageous, must be doubtful; but such an invitation for attack could not be resisted; and Harriet was soon assailed by half a dozen children, headed by a stout woman and a great boy, all clamorous, and impertinent in look, though not absolutely in word.—More and more frightened, she immediately promised them money, and taking out her purse, gave them a shilling, and begged them not to want more, or to use her ill.—She was then able to walk, though but slowly, and was moving away—but her terror and her purse were too tempting, and she was followed, or rather surrounded, by the whole gang, demanding more.\n\nIn this state Frank Churchill had found her, she trembling and conditioning, they loud and insolent. By a most fortunate chance his leaving Highbury had been delayed so as to bring him to her assistance at this critical moment. The pleasantness of the morning had induced him to walk forward, and leave his horses to meet him by another road, a mile or two beyond Highbury—and happening to have borrowed a pair of scissors the night before of Miss Bates, and to have forgotten to restore them, he had been obliged to stop at her door, and go in for a few minutes: he was therefore later than he had intended; and being on foot, was unseen by the whole party till almost close to them. The terror which the woman and boy had been creating in Harriet was then their own portion. He had left them completely frightened; and Harriet eagerly clinging to him, and hardly able to speak, had just strength enough to reach Hartfield, before her spirits were quite overcome. It was his idea to bring her to Hartfield: he had thought of no other place.\n\nThis was the amount of the whole story,—of his communication and of Harriet’s as soon as she had recovered her senses and speech.—He dared not stay longer than to see her well; these several delays left him not another minute to lose; and Emma engaging to give assurance of her safety to Mrs. Goddard, and notice of there being such a set of people in the neighbourhood to Mr. Knightley, he set off, with all the grateful blessings that she could utter for her friend and herself.\n\nSuch an adventure as this,—a fine young man and a lovely young woman thrown together in such a way, could hardly fail of suggesting certain ideas to the coldest heart and the steadiest brain. So Emma thought, at least. Could a linguist, could a grammarian, could even a mathematician have seen what she did, have witnessed their appearance together, and heard their history of it, without feeling that circumstances had been at work to make them peculiarly interesting to each other?—How much more must an imaginist, like herself, be on fire with speculation and foresight!—especially with such a groundwork of anticipation as her mind had already made.\n\nIt was a very extraordinary thing! Nothing of the sort had ever occurred before to any young ladies in the place, within her memory; no rencontre, no alarm of the kind;—and now it had happened to the very person, and at the very hour, when the other very person was chancing to pass by to rescue her!—It certainly was very extraordinary!—And knowing, as she did, the favourable state of mind of each at this period, it struck her the more. He was wishing to get the better of his attachment to herself, she just recovering from her mania for Mr. Elton. It seemed as if every thing united to promise the most interesting consequences. It was not possible that the occurrence should not be strongly recommending each to the other.\n\nIn the few minutes’ conversation which she had yet had with him, while Harriet had been partially insensible, he had spoken of her terror, her naïveté, her fervour as she seized and clung to his arm, with a sensibility amused and delighted; and just at last, after Harriet’s own account had been given, he had expressed his indignation at the abominable folly of Miss Bickerton in the warmest terms. Every thing was to take its natural course, however, neither impelled nor assisted. She would not stir a step, nor drop a hint. No, she had had enough of interference. There could be no harm in a scheme, a mere passive scheme. It was no more than a wish. Beyond it she would on no account proceed.\n\nEmma’s first resolution was to keep her father from the knowledge of what had passed,—aware of the anxiety and alarm it would occasion: but she soon felt that concealment must be impossible. Within half an hour it was known all over Highbury. It was the very event to engage those who talk most, the young and the low; and all the youth and servants in the place were soon in the happiness of frightful news. The last night’s ball seemed lost in the gipsies. Poor Mr. Woodhouse trembled as he sat, and, as Emma had foreseen, would scarcely be satisfied without their promising never to go beyond the shrubbery again. It was some comfort to him that many inquiries after himself and Miss Woodhouse (for his neighbours knew that he loved to be inquired after), as well as Miss Smith, were coming in during the rest of the day; and he had the pleasure of returning for answer, that they were all very indifferent—which, though not exactly true, for she was perfectly well, and Harriet not much otherwise, Emma would not interfere with. She had an unhappy state of health in general for the child of such a man, for she hardly knew what indisposition was; and if he did not invent illnesses for her, she could make no figure in a message.\n\nThe gipsies did not wait for the operations of justice; they took themselves off in a hurry. The young ladies of Highbury might have walked again in safety before their panic began, and the whole history dwindled soon into a matter of little importance but to Emma and her nephews:—in her imagination it maintained its ground, and Henry and John were still asking every day for the story of Harriet and the gipsies, and still tenaciously setting her right if she varied in the slightest particular from the original recital.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter IV\n\nA very few days had passed after this adventure, when Harriet came one morning to Emma with a small parcel in her hand, and after sitting down and hesitating, thus began:\n\n“Miss Woodhouse—if you are at leisure—I have something that I should like to tell you—a sort of confession to make—and then, you know, it will be over.”\n\nEmma was a good deal surprized; but begged her to speak. There was a seriousness in Harriet’s manner which prepared her, quite as much as her words, for something more than ordinary.\n\n“It is my duty, and I am sure it is my wish,” she continued, “to have no reserves with you on this subject. As I am happily quite an altered creature in one respect, it is very fit that you should have the satisfaction of knowing it. I do not want to say more than is necessary—I am too much ashamed of having given way as I have done, and I dare say you understand me.”\n\n“Yes,” said Emma, “I hope I do.”\n\n“How I could so long a time be fancying myself!...” cried Harriet, warmly. “It seems like madness! I can see nothing at all extraordinary in him now.—I do not care whether I meet him or not—except that of the two I had rather not see him—and indeed I would go any distance round to avoid him—but I do not envy his wife in the least; I neither admire her nor envy her, as I have done: she is very charming, I dare say, and all that, but I think her very ill-tempered and disagreeable—I shall never forget her look the other night!—However, I assure you, Miss Woodhouse, I wish her no evil.—No, let them be ever so happy together, it will not give me another moment’s pang: and to convince you that I have been speaking truth, I am now going to destroy—what I ought to have destroyed long ago—what I ought never to have kept—I know that very well (blushing as she spoke).—However, now I will destroy it all—and it is my particular wish to do it in your presence, that you may see how rational I am grown. Cannot you guess what this parcel holds?” said she, with a conscious look.\n\n“Not the least in the world.—Did he ever give you any thing?”\n\n“No—I cannot call them gifts; but they are things that I have valued very much.”\n\nShe held the parcel towards her, and Emma read the words Most precious treasures on the top. Her curiosity was greatly excited. Harriet unfolded the parcel, and she looked on with impatience. Within abundance of silver paper was a pretty little Tunbridge-ware box, which Harriet opened: it was well lined with the softest cotton; but, excepting the cotton, Emma saw only a small piece of court-plaister.\n\n“Now,” said Harriet, “you must recollect.”\n\n“No, indeed I do not.”\n\n“Dear me! I should not have thought it possible you could forget what passed in this very room about court-plaister, one of the very last times we ever met in it!—It was but a very few days before I had my sore throat—just before Mr. and Mrs. John Knightley came—I think the very evening.—Do not you remember his cutting his finger with your new penknife, and your recommending court-plaister?—But, as you had none about you, and knew I had, you desired me to supply him; and so I took mine out and cut him a piece; but it was a great deal too large, and he cut it smaller, and kept playing some time with what was left, before he gave it back to me. And so then, in my nonsense, I could not help making a treasure of it—so I put it by never to be used, and looked at it now and then as a great treat.”\n\n“My dearest Harriet!” cried Emma, putting her hand before her face, and jumping up, “you make me more ashamed of myself than I can bear. Remember it? Aye, I remember it all now; all, except your saving this relic—I knew nothing of that till this moment—but the cutting the finger, and my recommending court-plaister, and saying I had none about me!—Oh! my sins, my sins!—And I had plenty all the while in my pocket!—One of my senseless tricks!—I deserve to be under a continual blush all the rest of my life.—Well—(sitting down again)—go on—what else?”\n\n“And had you really some at hand yourself? I am sure I never suspected it, you did it so naturally.”\n\n“And so you actually put this piece of court-plaister by for his sake!” said Emma, recovering from her state of shame and feeling divided between wonder and amusement. And secretly she added to herself, “Lord bless me! when should I ever have thought of putting by in cotton a piece of court-plaister that Frank Churchill had been pulling about! I never was equal to this.”\n\n“Here,” resumed Harriet, turning to her box again, “here is something still more valuable, I mean that has been more valuable, because this is what did really once belong to him, which the court-plaister never did.”\n\nEmma was quite eager to see this superior treasure. It was the end of an old pencil,—the part without any lead.\n\n“This was really his,” said Harriet.—“Do not you remember one morning?—no, I dare say you do not. But one morning—I forget exactly the day—but perhaps it was the Tuesday or Wednesday before that evening, he wanted to make a memorandum in his pocket-book; it was about spruce-beer. Mr. Knightley had been telling him something about brewing spruce-beer, and he wanted to put it down; but when he took out his pencil, there was so little lead that he soon cut it all away, and it would not do, so you lent him another, and this was left upon the table as good for nothing. But I kept my eye on it; and, as soon as I dared, caught it up, and never parted with it again from that moment.”\n\n“I do remember it,” cried Emma; “I perfectly remember it.—Talking about spruce-beer.—Oh! yes—Mr. Knightley and I both saying we liked it, and Mr. Elton’s seeming resolved to learn to like it too. I perfectly remember it.—Stop; Mr. Knightley was standing just here, was not he? I have an idea he was standing just here.”\n\n“Ah! I do not know. I cannot recollect.—It is very odd, but I cannot recollect.—Mr. Elton was sitting here, I remember, much about where I am now.”—\n\n“Well, go on.”\n\n“Oh! that’s all. I have nothing more to shew you, or to say—except that I am now going to throw them both behind the fire, and I wish you to see me do it.”\n\n“My poor dear Harriet! and have you actually found happiness in treasuring up these things?”\n\n“Yes, simpleton as I was!—but I am quite ashamed of it now, and wish I could forget as easily as I can burn them. It was very wrong of me, you know, to keep any remembrances, after he was married. I knew it was—but had not resolution enough to part with them.”\n\n“But, Harriet, is it necessary to burn the court-plaister?—I have not a word to say for the bit of old pencil, but the court-plaister might be useful.”\n\n“I shall be happier to burn it,” replied Harriet. “It has a disagreeable look to me. I must get rid of every thing.—There it goes, and there is an end, thank Heaven! of Mr. Elton.”\n\n“And when,” thought Emma, “will there be a beginning of Mr. Churchill?”\n\nShe had soon afterwards reason to believe that the beginning was already made, and could not but hope that the gipsy, though she had told no fortune, might be proved to have made Harriet’s.—About a fortnight after the alarm, they came to a sufficient explanation, and quite undesignedly. Emma was not thinking of it at the moment, which made the information she received more valuable. She merely said, in the course of some trivial chat, “Well, Harriet, whenever you marry I would advise you to do so and so”—and thought no more of it, till after a minute’s silence she heard Harriet say in a very serious tone, “I shall never marry.”\n\nEmma then looked up, and immediately saw how it was; and after a moment’s debate, as to whether it should pass unnoticed or not, replied,\n\n“Never marry!—This is a new resolution.”\n\n“It is one that I shall never change, however.”\n\nAfter another short hesitation, “I hope it does not proceed from—I hope it is not in compliment to Mr. Elton?”\n\n“Mr. Elton indeed!” cried Harriet indignantly.—“Oh! no”—and Emma could just catch the words, “so superior to Mr. Elton!”\n\nShe then took a longer time for consideration. Should she proceed no farther?—should she let it pass, and seem to suspect nothing?—Perhaps Harriet might think her cold or angry if she did; or perhaps if she were totally silent, it might only drive Harriet into asking her to hear too much; and against any thing like such an unreserve as had been, such an open and frequent discussion of hopes and chances, she was perfectly resolved.—She believed it would be wiser for her to say and know at once, all that she meant to say and know. Plain dealing was always best. She had previously determined how far she would proceed, on any application of the sort; and it would be safer for both, to have the judicious law of her own brain laid down with speed.—She was decided, and thus spoke—\n\n“Harriet, I will not affect to be in doubt of your meaning. Your resolution, or rather your expectation of never marrying, results from an idea that the person whom you might prefer, would be too greatly your superior in situation to think of you. Is not it so?”\n\n“Oh! Miss Woodhouse, believe me I have not the presumption to suppose— Indeed I am not so mad.—But it is a pleasure to me to admire him at a distance—and to think of his infinite superiority to all the rest of the world, with the gratitude, wonder, and veneration, which are so proper, in me especially.”\n\n“I am not at all surprized at you, Harriet. The service he rendered you was enough to warm your heart.”\n\n“Service! oh! it was such an inexpressible obligation!—The very recollection of it, and all that I felt at the time—when I saw him coming—his noble look—and my wretchedness before. Such a change! In one moment such a change! From perfect misery to perfect happiness!”\n\n“It is very natural. It is natural, and it is honourable.—Yes, honourable, I think, to chuse so well and so gratefully.—But that it will be a fortunate preference is more than I can promise. I do not advise you to give way to it, Harriet. I do not by any means engage for its being returned. Consider what you are about. Perhaps it will be wisest in you to check your feelings while you can: at any rate do not let them carry you far, unless you are persuaded of his liking you. Be observant of him. Let his behaviour be the guide of your sensations. I give you this caution now, because I shall never speak to you again on the subject. I am determined against all interference. Henceforward I know nothing of the matter. Let no name ever pass our lips. We were very wrong before; we will be cautious now.—He is your superior, no doubt, and there do seem objections and obstacles of a very serious nature; but yet, Harriet, more wonderful things have taken place, there have been matches of greater disparity. But take care of yourself. I would not have you too sanguine; though, however it may end, be assured your raising your thoughts to him, is a mark of good taste which I shall always know how to value.”\n\nHarriet kissed her hand in silent and submissive gratitude. Emma was very decided in thinking such an attachment no bad thing for her friend. Its tendency would be to raise and refine her mind—and it must be saving her from the danger of degradation.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter V\n\nIn this state of schemes, and hopes, and connivance, June opened upon Hartfield. To Highbury in general it brought no material change. The Eltons were still talking of a visit from the Sucklings, and of the use to be made of their barouche-landau; and Jane Fairfax was still at her grandmother’s; and as the return of the Campbells from Ireland was again delayed, and August, instead of Midsummer, fixed for it, she was likely to remain there full two months longer, provided at least she were able to defeat Mrs. Elton’s activity in her service, and save herself from being hurried into a delightful situation against her will.\n\nMr. Knightley, who, for some reason best known to himself, had certainly taken an early dislike to Frank Churchill, was only growing to dislike him more. He began to suspect him of some double dealing in his pursuit of Emma. That Emma was his object appeared indisputable. Every thing declared it; his own attentions, his father’s hints, his mother-in-law’s guarded silence; it was all in unison; words, conduct, discretion, and indiscretion, told the same story. But while so many were devoting him to Emma, and Emma herself making him over to Harriet, Mr. Knightley began to suspect him of some inclination to trifle with Jane Fairfax. He could not understand it; but there were symptoms of intelligence between them—he thought so at least—symptoms of admiration on his side, which, having once observed, he could not persuade himself to think entirely void of meaning, however he might wish to escape any of Emma’s errors of imagination. She was not present when the suspicion first arose. He was dining with the Randalls family, and Jane, at the Eltons’; and he had seen a look, more than a single look, at Miss Fairfax, which, from the admirer of Miss Woodhouse, seemed somewhat out of place. When he was again in their company, he could not help remembering what he had seen; nor could he avoid observations which, unless it were like Cowper and his fire at twilight,\n\n“Myself creating what I saw,”\n\nbrought him yet stronger suspicion of there being a something of private liking, of private understanding even, between Frank Churchill and Jane.\n\nHe had walked up one day after dinner, as he very often did, to spend his evening at Hartfield. Emma and Harriet were going to walk; he joined them; and, on returning, they fell in with a larger party, who, like themselves, judged it wisest to take their exercise early, as the weather threatened rain; Mr. and Mrs. Weston and their son, Miss Bates and her niece, who had accidentally met. They all united; and, on reaching Hartfield gates, Emma, who knew it was exactly the sort of visiting that would be welcome to her father, pressed them all to go in and drink tea with him. The Randalls party agreed to it immediately; and after a pretty long speech from Miss Bates, which few persons listened to, she also found it possible to accept dear Miss Woodhouse’s most obliging invitation.\n\nAs they were turning into the grounds, Mr. Perry passed by on horseback. The gentlemen spoke of his horse.\n\n“By the bye,” said Frank Churchill to Mrs. Weston presently, “what became of Mr. Perry’s plan of setting up his carriage?”\n\nMrs. Weston looked surprized, and said, “I did not know that he ever had any such plan.”\n\n“Nay, I had it from you. You wrote me word of it three months ago.”\n\n“Me! impossible!”\n\n“Indeed you did. I remember it perfectly. You mentioned it as what was certainly to be very soon. Mrs. Perry had told somebody, and was extremely happy about it. It was owing to her persuasion, as she thought his being out in bad weather did him a great deal of harm. You must remember it now?”\n\n“Upon my word I never heard of it till this moment.”\n\n“Never! really, never!—Bless me! how could it be?—Then I must have dreamt it—but I was completely persuaded—Miss Smith, you walk as if you were tired. You will not be sorry to find yourself at home.”\n\n“What is this?—What is this?” cried Mr. Weston, “about Perry and a carriage? Is Perry going to set up his carriage, Frank? I am glad he can afford it. You had it from himself, had you?”\n\n“No, sir,” replied his son, laughing, “I seem to have had it from nobody.—Very odd!—I really was persuaded of Mrs. Weston’s having mentioned it in one of her letters to Enscombe, many weeks ago, with all these particulars—but as she declares she never heard a syllable of it before, of course it must have been a dream. I am a great dreamer. I dream of every body at Highbury when I am away—and when I have gone through my particular friends, then I begin dreaming of Mr. and Mrs. Perry.”\n\n“It is odd though,” observed his father, “that you should have had such a regular connected dream about people whom it was not very likely you should be thinking of at Enscombe. Perry’s setting up his carriage! and his wife’s persuading him to it, out of care for his health—just what will happen, I have no doubt, some time or other; only a little premature. What an air of probability sometimes runs through a dream! And at others, what a heap of absurdities it is! Well, Frank, your dream certainly shews that Highbury is in your thoughts when you are absent. Emma, you are a great dreamer, I think?”\n\nEmma was out of hearing. She had hurried on before her guests to prepare her father for their appearance, and was beyond the reach of Mr. Weston’s hint.\n\n“Why, to own the truth,” cried Miss Bates, who had been trying in vain to be heard the last two minutes, “if I must speak on this subject, there is no denying that Mr. Frank Churchill might have—I do not mean to say that he did not dream it—I am sure I have sometimes the oddest dreams in the world—but if I am questioned about it, I must acknowledge that there was such an idea last spring; for Mrs. Perry herself mentioned it to my mother, and the Coles knew of it as well as ourselves—but it was quite a secret, known to nobody else, and only thought of about three days. Mrs. Perry was very anxious that he should have a carriage, and came to my mother in great spirits one morning because she thought she had prevailed. Jane, don’t you remember grandmama’s telling us of it when we got home? I forget where we had been walking to—very likely to Randalls; yes, I think it was to Randalls. Mrs. Perry was always particularly fond of my mother—indeed I do not know who is not—and she had mentioned it to her in confidence; she had no objection to her telling us, of course, but it was not to go beyond: and, from that day to this, I never mentioned it to a soul that I know of. At the same time, I will not positively answer for my having never dropt a hint, because I know I do sometimes pop out a thing before I am aware. I am a talker, you know; I am rather a talker; and now and then I have let a thing escape me which I should not. I am not like Jane; I wish I were. I will answer for it she never betrayed the least thing in the world. Where is she?—Oh! just behind. Perfectly remember Mrs. Perry’s coming.—Extraordinary dream, indeed!”\n\nThey were entering the hall. Mr. Knightley’s eyes had preceded Miss Bates’s in a glance at Jane. From Frank Churchill’s face, where he thought he saw confusion suppressed or laughed away, he had involuntarily turned to hers; but she was indeed behind, and too busy with her shawl. Mr. Weston had walked in. The two other gentlemen waited at the door to let her pass. Mr. Knightley suspected in Frank Churchill the determination of catching her eye—he seemed watching her intently—in vain, however, if it were so—Jane passed between them into the hall, and looked at neither.\n\nThere was no time for farther remark or explanation. The dream must be borne with, and Mr. Knightley must take his seat with the rest round the large modern circular table which Emma had introduced at Hartfield, and which none but Emma could have had power to place there and persuade her father to use, instead of the small-sized Pembroke, on which two of his daily meals had, for forty years been crowded. Tea passed pleasantly, and nobody seemed in a hurry to move.\n\n“Miss Woodhouse,” said Frank Churchill, after examining a table behind him, which he could reach as he sat, “have your nephews taken away their alphabets—their box of letters? It used to stand here. Where is it? This is a sort of dull-looking evening, that ought to be treated rather as winter than summer. We had great amusement with those letters one morning. I want to puzzle you again.”\n\nEmma was pleased with the thought; and producing the box, the table was quickly scattered over with alphabets, which no one seemed so much disposed to employ as their two selves. They were rapidly forming words for each other, or for any body else who would be puzzled. The quietness of the game made it particularly eligible for Mr. Woodhouse, who had often been distressed by the more animated sort, which Mr. Weston had occasionally introduced, and who now sat happily occupied in lamenting, with tender melancholy, over the departure of the “poor little boys,” or in fondly pointing out, as he took up any stray letter near him, how beautifully Emma had written it.\n\nFrank Churchill placed a word before Miss Fairfax. She gave a slight glance round the table, and applied herself to it. Frank was next to Emma, Jane opposite to them—and Mr. Knightley so placed as to see them all; and it was his object to see as much as he could, with as little apparent observation. The word was discovered, and with a faint smile pushed away. If meant to be immediately mixed with the others, and buried from sight, she should have looked on the table instead of looking just across, for it was not mixed; and Harriet, eager after every fresh word, and finding out none, directly took it up, and fell to work. She was sitting by Mr. Knightley, and turned to him for help. The word was blunder; and as Harriet exultingly proclaimed it, there was a blush on Jane’s cheek which gave it a meaning not otherwise ostensible. Mr. Knightley connected it with the dream; but how it could all be, was beyond his comprehension. How the delicacy, the discretion of his favourite could have been so lain asleep! He feared there must be some decided involvement. Disingenuousness and double dealing seemed to meet him at every turn. These letters were but the vehicle for gallantry and trick. It was a child’s play, chosen to conceal a deeper game on Frank Churchill’s part.\n\nWith great indignation did he continue to observe him; with great alarm and distrust, to observe also his two blinded companions. He saw a short word prepared for Emma, and given to her with a look sly and demure. He saw that Emma had soon made it out, and found it highly entertaining, though it was something which she judged it proper to appear to censure; for she said, “Nonsense! for shame!” He heard Frank Churchill next say, with a glance towards Jane, “I will give it to her—shall I?”—and as clearly heard Emma opposing it with eager laughing warmth. “No, no, you must not; you shall not, indeed.”\n\nIt was done however. This gallant young man, who seemed to love without feeling, and to recommend himself without complaisance, directly handed over the word to Miss Fairfax, and with a particular degree of sedate civility entreated her to study it. Mr. Knightley’s excessive curiosity to know what this word might be, made him seize every possible moment for darting his eye towards it, and it was not long before he saw it to be Dixon. Jane Fairfax’s perception seemed to accompany his; her comprehension was certainly more equal to the covert meaning, the superior intelligence, of those five letters so arranged. She was evidently displeased; looked up, and seeing herself watched, blushed more deeply than he had ever perceived her, and saying only, “I did not know that proper names were allowed,” pushed away the letters with even an angry spirit, and looked resolved to be engaged by no other word that could be offered. Her face was averted from those who had made the attack, and turned towards her aunt.\n\n“Aye, very true, my dear,” cried the latter, though Jane had not spoken a word—“I was just going to say the same thing. It is time for us to be going indeed. The evening is closing in, and grandmama will be looking for us. My dear sir, you are too obliging. We really must wish you good night.”\n\nJane’s alertness in moving, proved her as ready as her aunt had preconceived. She was immediately up, and wanting to quit the table; but so many were also moving, that she could not get away; and Mr. Knightley thought he saw another collection of letters anxiously pushed towards her, and resolutely swept away by her unexamined. She was afterwards looking for her shawl—Frank Churchill was looking also—it was growing dusk, and the room was in confusion; and how they parted, Mr. Knightley could not tell.\n\nHe remained at Hartfield after all the rest, his thoughts full of what he had seen; so full, that when the candles came to assist his observations, he must—yes, he certainly must, as a friend—an anxious friend—give Emma some hint, ask her some question. He could not see her in a situation of such danger, without trying to preserve her. It was his duty.\n\n“Pray, Emma,” said he, “may I ask in what lay the great amusement, the poignant sting of the last word given to you and Miss Fairfax? I saw the word, and am curious to know how it could be so very entertaining to the one, and so very distressing to the other.”\n\nEmma was extremely confused. She could not endure to give him the true explanation; for though her suspicions were by no means removed, she was really ashamed of having ever imparted them.\n\n“Oh!” she cried in evident embarrassment, “it all meant nothing; a mere joke among ourselves.”\n\n“The joke,” he replied gravely, “seemed confined to you and Mr. Churchill.”\n\nHe had hoped she would speak again, but she did not. She would rather busy herself about any thing than speak. He sat a little while in doubt. A variety of evils crossed his mind. Interference—fruitless interference. Emma’s confusion, and the acknowledged intimacy, seemed to declare her affection engaged. Yet he would speak. He owed it to her, to risk any thing that might be involved in an unwelcome interference, rather than her welfare; to encounter any thing, rather than the remembrance of neglect in such a cause.\n\n“My dear Emma,” said he at last, with earnest kindness, “do you think you perfectly understand the degree of acquaintance between the gentleman and lady we have been speaking of?”\n\n“Between Mr. Frank Churchill and Miss Fairfax? Oh! yes, perfectly.—Why do you make a doubt of it?”\n\n“Have you never at any time had reason to think that he admired her, or that she admired him?”\n\n“Never, never!” she cried with a most open eagerness—“Never, for the twentieth part of a moment, did such an idea occur to me. And how could it possibly come into your head?”\n\n“I have lately imagined that I saw symptoms of attachment between them—certain expressive looks, which I did not believe meant to be public.”\n\n“Oh! you amuse me excessively. I am delighted to find that you can vouchsafe to let your imagination wander—but it will not do—very sorry to check you in your first essay—but indeed it will not do. There is no admiration between them, I do assure you; and the appearances which have caught you, have arisen from some peculiar circumstances—feelings rather of a totally different nature—it is impossible exactly to explain:—there is a good deal of nonsense in it—but the part which is capable of being communicated, which is sense, is, that they are as far from any attachment or admiration for one another, as any two beings in the world can be. That is, I presume it to be so on her side, and I can answer for its being so on his. I will answer for the gentleman’s indifference.”\n\nShe spoke with a confidence which staggered, with a satisfaction which silenced, Mr. Knightley. She was in gay spirits, and would have prolonged the conversation, wanting to hear the particulars of his suspicions, every look described, and all the wheres and hows of a circumstance which highly entertained her: but his gaiety did not meet hers. He found he could not be useful, and his feelings were too much irritated for talking. That he might not be irritated into an absolute fever, by the fire which Mr. Woodhouse’s tender habits required almost every evening throughout the year, he soon afterwards took a hasty leave, and walked home to the coolness and solitude of Donwell Abbey.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter VI\n\nAfter being long fed with hopes of a speedy visit from Mr. and Mrs. Suckling, the Highbury world were obliged to endure the mortification of hearing that they could not possibly come till the autumn. No such importation of novelties could enrich their intellectual stores at present. In the daily interchange of news, they must be again restricted to the other topics with which for a while the Sucklings’ coming had been united, such as the last accounts of Mrs. Churchill, whose health seemed every day to supply a different report, and the situation of Mrs. Weston, whose happiness it was to be hoped might eventually be as much increased by the arrival of a child, as that of all her neighbours was by the approach of it.\n\nMrs. Elton was very much disappointed. It was the delay of a great deal of pleasure and parade. Her introductions and recommendations must all wait, and every projected party be still only talked of. So she thought at first;—but a little consideration convinced her that every thing need not be put off. Why should not they explore to Box Hill though the Sucklings did not come? They could go there again with them in the autumn. It was settled that they should go to Box Hill. That there was to be such a party had been long generally known: it had even given the idea of another. Emma had never been to Box Hill; she wished to see what every body found so well worth seeing, and she and Mr. Weston had agreed to chuse some fine morning and drive thither. Two or three more of the chosen only were to be admitted to join them, and it was to be done in a quiet, unpretending, elegant way, infinitely superior to the bustle and preparation, the regular eating and drinking, and picnic parade of the Eltons and the Sucklings.\n\nThis was so very well understood between them, that Emma could not but feel some surprise, and a little displeasure, on hearing from Mr. Weston that he had been proposing to Mrs. Elton, as her brother and sister had failed her, that the two parties should unite, and go together; and that as Mrs. Elton had very readily acceded to it, so it was to be, if she had no objection. Now, as her objection was nothing but her very great dislike of Mrs. Elton, of which Mr. Weston must already be perfectly aware, it was not worth bringing forward again:—it could not be done without a reproof to him, which would be giving pain to his wife; and she found herself therefore obliged to consent to an arrangement which she would have done a great deal to avoid; an arrangement which would probably expose her even to the degradation of being said to be of Mrs. Elton’s party! Every feeling was offended; and the forbearance of her outward submission left a heavy arrear due of secret severity in her reflections on the unmanageable goodwill of Mr. Weston’s temper.\n\n“I am glad you approve of what I have done,” said he very comfortably. “But I thought you would. Such schemes as these are nothing without numbers. One cannot have too large a party. A large party secures its own amusement. And she is a good-natured woman after all. One could not leave her out.”\n\nEmma denied none of it aloud, and agreed to none of it in private.\n\nIt was now the middle of June, and the weather fine; and Mrs. Elton was growing impatient to name the day, and settle with Mr. Weston as to pigeon-pies and cold lamb, when a lame carriage-horse threw every thing into sad uncertainty. It might be weeks, it might be only a few days, before the horse were useable; but no preparations could be ventured on, and it was all melancholy stagnation. Mrs. Elton’s resources were inadequate to such an attack.\n\n“Is not this most vexatious, Knightley?” she cried.—“And such weather for exploring!—These delays and disappointments are quite odious. What are we to do?—The year will wear away at this rate, and nothing done. Before this time last year I assure you we had had a delightful exploring party from Maple Grove to Kings Weston.”\n\n“You had better explore to Donwell,” replied Mr. Knightley. “That may be done without horses. Come, and eat my strawberries. They are ripening fast.”\n\nIf Mr. Knightley did not begin seriously, he was obliged to proceed so, for his proposal was caught at with delight; and the “Oh! I should like it of all things,” was not plainer in words than manner. Donwell was famous for its strawberry-beds, which seemed a plea for the invitation: but no plea was necessary; cabbage-beds would have been enough to tempt the lady, who only wanted to be going somewhere. She promised him again and again to come—much oftener than he doubted—and was extremely gratified by such a proof of intimacy, such a distinguishing compliment as she chose to consider it.\n\n“You may depend upon me,” said she. “I certainly will come. Name your day, and I will come. You will allow me to bring Jane Fairfax?”\n\n“I cannot name a day,” said he, “till I have spoken to some others whom I would wish to meet you.”\n\n“Oh! leave all that to me. Only give me a carte-blanche.—I am Lady Patroness, you know. It is my party. I will bring friends with me.”\n\n“I hope you will bring Elton,” said he: “but I will not trouble you to give any other invitations.”\n\n“Oh! now you are looking very sly. But consider—you need not be afraid of delegating power to me. I am no young lady on her preferment. Married women, you know, may be safely authorised. It is my party. Leave it all to me. I will invite your guests.”\n\n“No,”—he calmly replied,—“there is but one married woman in the world whom I can ever allow to invite what guests she pleases to Donwell, and that one is—”\n\n“—Mrs. Weston, I suppose,” interrupted Mrs. Elton, rather mortified.\n\n“No—Mrs. Knightley;—and till she is in being, I will manage such matters myself.”\n\n“Ah! you are an odd creature!” she cried, satisfied to have no one preferred to herself.—“You are a humourist, and may say what you like. Quite a humourist. Well, I shall bring Jane with me—Jane and her aunt.—The rest I leave to you. I have no objections at all to meeting the Hartfield family. Don’t scruple. I know you are attached to them.”\n\n“You certainly will meet them if I can prevail; and I shall call on Miss Bates in my way home.”\n\n“That’s quite unnecessary; I see Jane every day:—but as you like. It is to be a morning scheme, you know, Knightley; quite a simple thing. I shall wear a large bonnet, and bring one of my little baskets hanging on my arm. Here,—probably this basket with pink ribbon. Nothing can be more simple, you see. And Jane will have such another. There is to be no form or parade—a sort of gipsy party. We are to walk about your gardens, and gather the strawberries ourselves, and sit under trees;—and whatever else you may like to provide, it is to be all out of doors—a table spread in the shade, you know. Every thing as natural and simple as possible. Is not that your idea?”\n\n“Not quite. My idea of the simple and the natural will be to have the table spread in the dining-room. The nature and the simplicity of gentlemen and ladies, with their servants and furniture, I think is best observed by meals within doors. When you are tired of eating strawberries in the garden, there shall be cold meat in the house.”\n\n“Well—as you please; only don’t have a great set out. And, by the bye, can I or my housekeeper be of any use to you with our opinion?—Pray be sincere, Knightley. If you wish me to talk to Mrs. Hodges, or to inspect anything—”\n\n“I have not the least wish for it, I thank you.”\n\n“Well—but if any difficulties should arise, my housekeeper is extremely clever.”\n\n“I will answer for it, that mine thinks herself full as clever, and would spurn any body’s assistance.”\n\n“I wish we had a donkey. The thing would be for us all to come on donkeys, Jane, Miss Bates, and me—and my caro sposo walking by. I really must talk to him about purchasing a donkey. In a country life I conceive it to be a sort of necessary; for, let a woman have ever so many resources, it is not possible for her to be always shut up at home;—and very long walks, you know—in summer there is dust, and in winter there is dirt.”\n\n“You will not find either, between Donwell and Highbury. Donwell Lane is never dusty, and now it is perfectly dry. Come on a donkey, however, if you prefer it. You can borrow Mrs. Cole’s. I would wish every thing to be as much to your taste as possible.”\n\n“That I am sure you would. Indeed I do you justice, my good friend. Under that peculiar sort of dry, blunt manner, I know you have the warmest heart. As I tell Mr. E., you are a thorough humourist.—Yes, believe me, Knightley, I am fully sensible of your attention to me in the whole of this scheme. You have hit upon the very thing to please me.”\n\nMr. Knightley had another reason for avoiding a table in the shade. He wished to persuade Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to join the party; and he knew that to have any of them sitting down out of doors to eat would inevitably make him ill. Mr. Woodhouse must not, under the specious pretence of a morning drive, and an hour or two spent at Donwell, be tempted away to his misery.\n\nHe was invited on good faith. No lurking horrors were to upbraid him for his easy credulity. He did consent. He had not been at Donwell for two years. “Some very fine morning, he, and Emma, and Harriet, could go very well; and he could sit still with Mrs. Weston, while the dear girls walked about the gardens. He did not suppose they could be damp now, in the middle of the day. He should like to see the old house again exceedingly, and should be very happy to meet Mr. and Mrs. Elton, and any other of his neighbours.—He could not see any objection at all to his, and Emma’s, and Harriet’s going there some very fine morning. He thought it very well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them—very kind and sensible—much cleverer than dining out.—He was not fond of dining out.”\n\nMr. Knightley was fortunate in every body’s most ready concurrence. The invitation was everywhere so well received, that it seemed as if, like Mrs. Elton, they were all taking the scheme as a particular compliment to themselves.—Emma and Harriet professed very high expectations of pleasure from it; and Mr. Weston, unasked, promised to get Frank over to join them, if possible; a proof of approbation and gratitude which could have been dispensed with.—Mr. Knightley was then obliged to say that he should be glad to see him; and Mr. Weston engaged to lose no time in writing, and spare no arguments to induce him to come.\n\nIn the meanwhile the lame horse recovered so fast, that the party to Box Hill was again under happy consideration; and at last Donwell was settled for one day, and Box Hill for the next,—the weather appearing exactly right.\n\nUnder a bright mid-day sun, at almost Midsummer, Mr. Woodhouse was safely conveyed in his carriage, with one window down, to partake of this al-fresco party; and in one of the most comfortable rooms in the Abbey, especially prepared for him by a fire all the morning, he was happily placed, quite at his ease, ready to talk with pleasure of what had been achieved, and advise every body to come and sit down, and not to heat themselves.—Mrs. Weston, who seemed to have walked there on purpose to be tired, and sit all the time with him, remained, when all the others were invited or persuaded out, his patient listener and sympathiser.\n\nIt was so long since Emma had been at the Abbey, that as soon as she was satisfied of her father’s comfort, she was glad to leave him, and look around her; eager to refresh and correct her memory with more particular observation, more exact understanding of a house and grounds which must ever be so interesting to her and all her family.\n\nShe felt all the honest pride and complacency which her alliance with the present and future proprietor could fairly warrant, as she viewed the respectable size and style of the building, its suitable, becoming, characteristic situation, low and sheltered—its ample gardens stretching down to meadows washed by a stream, of which the Abbey, with all the old neglect of prospect, had scarcely a sight—and its abundance of timber in rows and avenues, which neither fashion nor extravagance had rooted up.—The house was larger than Hartfield, and totally unlike it, covering a good deal of ground, rambling and irregular, with many comfortable, and one or two handsome rooms.—It was just what it ought to be, and it looked what it was—and Emma felt an increasing respect for it, as the residence of a family of such true gentility, untainted in blood and understanding.—Some faults of temper John Knightley had; but Isabella had connected herself unexceptionably. She had given them neither men, nor names, nor places, that could raise a blush. These were pleasant feelings, and she walked about and indulged them till it was necessary to do as the others did, and collect round the strawberry-beds.—The whole party were assembled, excepting Frank Churchill, who was expected every moment from Richmond; and Mrs. Elton, in all her apparatus of happiness, her large bonnet and her basket, was very ready to lead the way in gathering, accepting, or talking—strawberries, and only strawberries, could now be thought or spoken of.—“The best fruit in England—every body’s favourite—always wholesome.—These the finest beds and finest sorts.—Delightful to gather for one’s self—the only way of really enjoying them.—Morning decidedly the best time—never tired—every sort good—hautboy infinitely superior—no comparison—the others hardly eatable—hautboys very scarce—Chili preferred—white wood finest flavour of all—price of strawberries in London—abundance about Bristol—Maple Grove—cultivation—beds when to be renewed—gardeners thinking exactly different—no general rule—gardeners never to be put out of their way—delicious fruit—only too rich to be eaten much of—inferior to cherries—currants more refreshing—only objection to gathering strawberries the stooping—glaring sun—tired to death—could bear it no longer—must go and sit in the shade.”\n\nSuch, for half an hour, was the conversation—interrupted only once by Mrs. Weston, who came out, in her solicitude after her son-in-law, to inquire if he were come—and she was a little uneasy.—She had some fears of his horse.\n\nSeats tolerably in the shade were found; and now Emma was obliged to overhear what Mrs. Elton and Jane Fairfax were talking of.—A situation, a most desirable situation, was in question. Mrs. Elton had received notice of it that morning, and was in raptures. It was not with Mrs. Suckling, it was not with Mrs. Bragge, but in felicity and splendour it fell short only of them: it was with a cousin of Mrs. Bragge, an acquaintance of Mrs. Suckling, a lady known at Maple Grove. Delightful, charming, superior, first circles, spheres, lines, ranks, every thing—and Mrs. Elton was wild to have the offer closed with immediately.—On her side, all was warmth, energy, and triumph—and she positively refused to take her friend’s negative, though Miss Fairfax continued to assure her that she would not at present engage in any thing, repeating the same motives which she had been heard to urge before.—Still Mrs. Elton insisted on being authorised to write an acquiescence by the morrow’s post.—How Jane could bear it at all, was astonishing to Emma.—She did look vexed, she did speak pointedly—and at last, with a decision of action unusual to her, proposed a removal.—“Should not they walk? Would not Mr. Knightley shew them the gardens—all the gardens?—She wished to see the whole extent.”—The pertinacity of her friend seemed more than she could bear.\n\nIt was hot; and after walking some time over the gardens in a scattered, dispersed way, scarcely any three together, they insensibly followed one another to the delicious shade of a broad short avenue of limes, which stretching beyond the garden at an equal distance from the river, seemed the finish of the pleasure grounds.—It led to nothing; nothing but a view at the end over a low stone wall with high pillars, which seemed intended, in their erection, to give the appearance of an approach to the house, which never had been there. Disputable, however, as might be the taste of such a termination, it was in itself a charming walk, and the view which closed it extremely pretty.—The considerable slope, at nearly the foot of which the Abbey stood, gradually acquired a steeper form beyond its grounds; and at half a mile distant was a bank of considerable abruptness and grandeur, well clothed with wood;—and at the bottom of this bank, favourably placed and sheltered, rose the Abbey Mill Farm, with meadows in front, and the river making a close and handsome curve around it.\n\nIt was a sweet view—sweet to the eye and the mind. English verdure, English culture, English comfort, seen under a sun bright, without being oppressive.\n\nIn this walk Emma and Mr. Weston found all the others assembled; and towards this view she immediately perceived Mr. Knightley and Harriet distinct from the rest, quietly leading the way. Mr. Knightley and Harriet!—It was an odd tête-à-tête; but she was glad to see it.—There had been a time when he would have scorned her as a companion, and turned from her with little ceremony. Now they seemed in pleasant conversation. There had been a time also when Emma would have been sorry to see Harriet in a spot so favourable for the Abbey Mill Farm; but now she feared it not. It might be safely viewed with all its appendages of prosperity and beauty, its rich pastures, spreading flocks, orchard in blossom, and light column of smoke ascending.—She joined them at the wall, and found them more engaged in talking than in looking around. He was giving Harriet information as to modes of agriculture, etc. and Emma received a smile which seemed to say, “These are my own concerns. I have a right to talk on such subjects, without being suspected of introducing Robert Martin.”—She did not suspect him. It was too old a story.—Robert Martin had probably ceased to think of Harriet.—They took a few turns together along the walk.—The shade was most refreshing, and Emma found it the pleasantest part of the day.\n\nThe next remove was to the house; they must all go in and eat;—and they were all seated and busy, and still Frank Churchill did not come. Mrs. Weston looked, and looked in vain. His father would not own himself uneasy, and laughed at her fears; but she could not be cured of wishing that he would part with his black mare. He had expressed himself as to coming, with more than common certainty. “His aunt was so much better, that he had not a doubt of getting over to them.”—Mrs. Churchill’s state, however, as many were ready to remind her, was liable to such sudden variation as might disappoint her nephew in the most reasonable dependence—and Mrs. Weston was at last persuaded to believe, or to say, that it must be by some attack of Mrs. Churchill that he was prevented coming.—Emma looked at Harriet while the point was under consideration; she behaved very well, and betrayed no emotion.\n\nThe cold repast was over, and the party were to go out once more to see what had not yet been seen, the old Abbey fish-ponds; perhaps get as far as the clover, which was to be begun cutting on the morrow, or, at any rate, have the pleasure of being hot, and growing cool again.—Mr. Woodhouse, who had already taken his little round in the highest part of the gardens, where no damps from the river were imagined even by him, stirred no more; and his daughter resolved to remain with him, that Mrs. Weston might be persuaded away by her husband to the exercise and variety which her spirits seemed to need.\n\nMr. Knightley had done all in his power for Mr. Woodhouse’s entertainment. Books of engravings, drawers of medals, cameos, corals, shells, and every other family collection within his cabinets, had been prepared for his old friend, to while away the morning; and the kindness had perfectly answered. Mr. Woodhouse had been exceedingly well amused. Mrs. Weston had been shewing them all to him, and now he would shew them all to Emma;—fortunate in having no other resemblance to a child, than in a total want of taste for what he saw, for he was slow, constant, and methodical.—Before this second looking over was begun, however, Emma walked into the hall for the sake of a few moments’ free observation of the entrance and ground-plot of the house—and was hardly there, when Jane Fairfax appeared, coming quickly in from the garden, and with a look of escape.—Little expecting to meet Miss Woodhouse so soon, there was a start at first; but Miss Woodhouse was the very person she was in quest of.\n\n“Will you be so kind,” said she, “when I am missed, as to say that I am gone home?—I am going this moment.—My aunt is not aware how late it is, nor how long we have been absent—but I am sure we shall be wanted, and I am determined to go directly.—I have said nothing about it to any body. It would only be giving trouble and distress. Some are gone to the ponds, and some to the lime walk. Till they all come in I shall not be missed; and when they do, will you have the goodness to say that I am gone?”\n\n“Certainly, if you wish it;—but you are not going to walk to Highbury alone?”\n\n“Yes—what should hurt me?—I walk fast. I shall be at home in twenty minutes.”\n\n“But it is too far, indeed it is, to be walking quite alone. Let my father’s servant go with you.—Let me order the carriage. It can be round in five minutes.”\n\n“Thank you, thank you—but on no account.—I would rather walk.—And for me to be afraid of walking alone!—I, who may so soon have to guard others!”\n\nShe spoke with great agitation; and Emma very feelingly replied, “That can be no reason for your being exposed to danger now. I must order the carriage. The heat even would be danger.—You are fatigued already.”\n\n“I am,”—she answered—“I am fatigued; but it is not the sort of fatigue—quick walking will refresh me.—Miss Woodhouse, we all know at times what it is to be wearied in spirits. Mine, I confess, are exhausted. The greatest kindness you can shew me, will be to let me have my own way, and only say that I am gone when it is necessary.”\n\nEmma had not another word to oppose. She saw it all; and entering into her feelings, promoted her quitting the house immediately, and watched her safely off with the zeal of a friend. Her parting look was grateful—and her parting words, “Oh! Miss Woodhouse, the comfort of being sometimes alone!”—seemed to burst from an overcharged heart, and to describe somewhat of the continual endurance to be practised by her, even towards some of those who loved her best.\n\n“Such a home, indeed! such an aunt!” said Emma, as she turned back into the hall again. “I do pity you. And the more sensibility you betray of their just horrors, the more I shall like you.”\n\nJane had not been gone a quarter of an hour, and they had only accomplished some views of St. Mark’s Place, Venice, when Frank Churchill entered the room. Emma had not been thinking of him, she had forgotten to think of him—but she was very glad to see him. Mrs. Weston would be at ease. The black mare was blameless; they were right who had named Mrs. Churchill as the cause. He had been detained by a temporary increase of illness in her; a nervous seizure, which had lasted some hours—and he had quite given up every thought of coming, till very late;—and had he known how hot a ride he should have, and how late, with all his hurry, he must be, he believed he should not have come at all. The heat was excessive; he had never suffered any thing like it—almost wished he had staid at home—nothing killed him like heat—he could bear any degree of cold, etc., but heat was intolerable—and he sat down, at the greatest possible distance from the slight remains of Mr. Woodhouse’s fire, looking very deplorable.\n\n“You will soon be cooler, if you sit still,” said Emma.\n\n“As soon as I am cooler I shall go back again. I could very ill be spared—but such a point had been made of my coming! You will all be going soon I suppose; the whole party breaking up. I met one as I came—Madness in such weather!—absolute madness!”\n\nEmma listened, and looked, and soon perceived that Frank Churchill’s state might be best defined by the expressive phrase of being out of humour. Some people were always cross when they were hot. Such might be his constitution; and as she knew that eating and drinking were often the cure of such incidental complaints, she recommended his taking some refreshment; he would find abundance of every thing in the dining-room—and she humanely pointed out the door.\n\n“No—he should not eat. He was not hungry; it would only make him hotter.” In two minutes, however, he relented in his own favour; and muttering something about spruce-beer, walked off. Emma returned all her attention to her father, saying in secret—\n\n“I am glad I have done being in love with him. I should not like a man who is so soon discomposed by a hot morning. Harriet’s sweet easy temper will not mind it.”\n\nHe was gone long enough to have had a very comfortable meal, and came back all the better—grown quite cool—and, with good manners, like himself—able to draw a chair close to them, take an interest in their employment; and regret, in a reasonable way, that he should be so late. He was not in his best spirits, but seemed trying to improve them; and, at last, made himself talk nonsense very agreeably. They were looking over views in Swisserland.\n\n“As soon as my aunt gets well, I shall go abroad,” said he. “I shall never be easy till I have seen some of these places. You will have my sketches, some time or other, to look at—or my tour to read—or my poem. I shall do something to expose myself.”\n\n“That may be—but not by sketches in Swisserland. You will never go to Swisserland. Your uncle and aunt will never allow you to leave England.”\n\n“They may be induced to go too. A warm climate may be prescribed for her. I have more than half an expectation of our all going abroad. I assure you I have. I feel a strong persuasion, this morning, that I shall soon be abroad. I ought to travel. I am tired of doing nothing. I want a change. I am serious, Miss Woodhouse, whatever your penetrating eyes may fancy—I am sick of England—and would leave it to-morrow, if I could.”\n\n“You are sick of prosperity and indulgence. Cannot you invent a few hardships for yourself, and be contented to stay?”\n\n“I sick of prosperity and indulgence! You are quite mistaken. I do not look upon myself as either prosperous or indulged. I am thwarted in every thing material. I do not consider myself at all a fortunate person.”\n\n“You are not quite so miserable, though, as when you first came. Go and eat and drink a little more, and you will do very well. Another slice of cold meat, another draught of Madeira and water, will make you nearly on a par with the rest of us.”\n\n“No—I shall not stir. I shall sit by you. You are my best cure.”\n\n“We are going to Box Hill to-morrow;—you will join us. It is not Swisserland, but it will be something for a young man so much in want of a change. You will stay, and go with us?”\n\n“No, certainly not; I shall go home in the cool of the evening.”\n\n“But you may come again in the cool of to-morrow morning.”\n\n“No—It will not be worth while. If I come, I shall be cross.”\n\n“Then pray stay at Richmond.”\n\n“But if I do, I shall be crosser still. I can never bear to think of you all there without me.”\n\n“These are difficulties which you must settle for yourself. Chuse your own degree of crossness. I shall press you no more.”\n\nThe rest of the party were now returning, and all were soon collected. With some there was great joy at the sight of Frank Churchill; others took it very composedly; but there was a very general distress and disturbance on Miss Fairfax’s disappearance being explained. That it was time for every body to go, concluded the subject; and with a short final arrangement for the next day’s scheme, they parted. Frank Churchill’s little inclination to exclude himself increased so much, that his last words to Emma were,\n\n“Well;—if you wish me to stay and join the party, I will.”\n\nShe smiled her acceptance; and nothing less than a summons from Richmond was to take him back before the following evening.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter VII\n\nThey had a very fine day for Box Hill; and all the other outward circumstances of arrangement, accommodation, and punctuality, were in favour of a pleasant party. Mr. Weston directed the whole, officiating safely between Hartfield and the Vicarage, and every body was in good time. Emma and Harriet went together; Miss Bates and her niece, with the Eltons; the gentlemen on horseback. Mrs. Weston remained with Mr. Woodhouse. Nothing was wanting but to be happy when they got there. Seven miles were travelled in expectation of enjoyment, and every body had a burst of admiration on first arriving; but in the general amount of the day there was deficiency. There was a languor, a want of spirits, a want of union, which could not be got over. They separated too much into parties. The Eltons walked together; Mr. Knightley took charge of Miss Bates and Jane; and Emma and Harriet belonged to Frank Churchill. And Mr. Weston tried, in vain, to make them harmonise better. It seemed at first an accidental division, but it never materially varied. Mr. and Mrs. Elton, indeed, shewed no unwillingness to mix, and be as agreeable as they could; but during the two whole hours that were spent on the hill, there seemed a principle of separation, between the other parties, too strong for any fine prospects, or any cold collation, or any cheerful Mr. Weston, to remove.\n\nAt first it was downright dulness to Emma. She had never seen Frank Churchill so silent and stupid. He said nothing worth hearing—looked without seeing—admired without intelligence—listened without knowing what she said. While he was so dull, it was no wonder that Harriet should be dull likewise; and they were both insufferable.\n\nWhen they all sat down it was better; to her taste a great deal better, for Frank Churchill grew talkative and gay, making her his first object. Every distinguishing attention that could be paid, was paid to her. To amuse her, and be agreeable in her eyes, seemed all that he cared for—and Emma, glad to be enlivened, not sorry to be flattered, was gay and easy too, and gave him all the friendly encouragement, the admission to be gallant, which she had ever given in the first and most animating period of their acquaintance; but which now, in her own estimation, meant nothing, though in the judgment of most people looking on it must have had such an appearance as no English word but flirtation could very well describe. “Mr. Frank Churchill and Miss Woodhouse flirted together excessively.” They were laying themselves open to that very phrase—and to having it sent off in a letter to Maple Grove by one lady, to Ireland by another. Not that Emma was gay and thoughtless from any real felicity; it was rather because she felt less happy than she had expected. She laughed because she was disappointed; and though she liked him for his attentions, and thought them all, whether in friendship, admiration, or playfulness, extremely judicious, they were not winning back her heart. She still intended him for her friend.\n\n“How much I am obliged to you,” said he, “for telling me to come to-day!—If it had not been for you, I should certainly have lost all the happiness of this party. I had quite determined to go away again.”\n\n“Yes, you were very cross; and I do not know what about, except that you were too late for the best strawberries. I was a kinder friend than you deserved. But you were humble. You begged hard to be commanded to come.”\n\n“Don’t say I was cross. I was fatigued. The heat overcame me.”\n\n“It is hotter to-day.”\n\n“Not to my feelings. I am perfectly comfortable to-day.”\n\n“You are comfortable because you are under command.”\n\n“Your command?—Yes.”\n\n“Perhaps I intended you to say so, but I meant self-command. You had, somehow or other, broken bounds yesterday, and run away from your own management; but to-day you are got back again—and as I cannot be always with you, it is best to believe your temper under your own command rather than mine.”\n\n“It comes to the same thing. I can have no self-command without a motive. You order me, whether you speak or not. And you can be always with me. You are always with me.”\n\n“Dating from three o’clock yesterday. My perpetual influence could not begin earlier, or you would not have been so much out of humour before.”\n\n“Three o’clock yesterday! That is your date. I thought I had seen you first in February.”\n\n“Your gallantry is really unanswerable. But (lowering her voice)—nobody speaks except ourselves, and it is rather too much to be talking nonsense for the entertainment of seven silent people.”\n\n“I say nothing of which I am ashamed,” replied he, with lively impudence. “I saw you first in February. Let every body on the Hill hear me if they can. Let my accents swell to Mickleham on one side, and Dorking on the other. I saw you first in February.” And then whispering—“Our companions are excessively stupid. What shall we do to rouse them? Any nonsense will serve. They shall talk. Ladies and gentlemen, I am ordered by Miss Woodhouse (who, wherever she is, presides) to say, that she desires to know what you are all thinking of?”\n\nSome laughed, and answered good-humouredly. Miss Bates said a great deal; Mrs. Elton swelled at the idea of Miss Woodhouse’s presiding; Mr. Knightley’s answer was the most distinct.\n\n“Is Miss Woodhouse sure that she would like to hear what we are all thinking of?”\n\n“Oh! no, no”—cried Emma, laughing as carelessly as she could—“Upon no account in the world. It is the very last thing I would stand the brunt of just now. Let me hear any thing rather than what you are all thinking of. I will not say quite all. There are one or two, perhaps, (glancing at Mr. Weston and Harriet,) whose thoughts I might not be afraid of knowing.”\n\n“It is a sort of thing,” cried Mrs. Elton emphatically, “which I should not have thought myself privileged to inquire into. Though, perhaps, as the Chaperon of the party—I never was in any circle—exploring parties—young ladies—married women—”\n\nHer mutterings were chiefly to her husband; and he murmured, in reply,\n\n“Very true, my love, very true. Exactly so, indeed—quite unheard of—but some ladies say any thing. Better pass it off as a joke. Every body knows what is due to you.”\n\n“It will not do,” whispered Frank to Emma; “they are most of them affronted. I will attack them with more address. Ladies and gentlemen—I am ordered by Miss Woodhouse to say, that she waives her right of knowing exactly what you may all be thinking of, and only requires something very entertaining from each of you, in a general way. Here are seven of you, besides myself, (who, she is pleased to say, am very entertaining already,) and she only demands from each of you either one thing very clever, be it prose or verse, original or repeated—or two things moderately clever—or three things very dull indeed, and she engages to laugh heartily at them all.”\n\n“Oh! very well,” exclaimed Miss Bates, “then I need not be uneasy. ‘Three things very dull indeed.’ That will just do for me, you know. I shall be sure to say three dull things as soon as ever I open my mouth, shan’t I? (looking round with the most good-humoured dependence on every body’s assent)—Do not you all think I shall?”\n\nEmma could not resist.\n\n“Ah! ma’am, but there may be a difficulty. Pardon me—but you will be limited as to number—only three at once.”\n\nMiss Bates, deceived by the mock ceremony of her manner, did not immediately catch her meaning; but, when it burst on her, it could not anger, though a slight blush shewed that it could pain her.\n\n“Ah!—well—to be sure. Yes, I see what she means, (turning to Mr. Knightley,) and I will try to hold my tongue. I must make myself very disagreeable, or she would not have said such a thing to an old friend.”\n\n“I like your plan,” cried Mr. Weston. “Agreed, agreed. I will do my best. I am making a conundrum. How will a conundrum reckon?”\n\n“Low, I am afraid, sir, very low,” answered his son;—“but we shall be indulgent—especially to any one who leads the way.”\n\n“No, no,” said Emma, “it will not reckon low. A conundrum of Mr. Weston’s shall clear him and his next neighbour. Come, sir, pray let me hear it.”\n\n“I doubt its being very clever myself,” said Mr. Weston. “It is too much a matter of fact, but here it is.—What two letters of the alphabet are there, that express perfection?”\n\n“What two letters!—express perfection! I am sure I do not know.”\n\n“Ah! you will never guess. You, (to Emma), I am certain, will never guess.—I will tell you.—M. and A.—Em-ma.—Do you understand?”\n\nUnderstanding and gratification came together. It might be a very indifferent piece of wit, but Emma found a great deal to laugh at and enjoy in it—and so did Frank and Harriet.—It did not seem to touch the rest of the party equally; some looked very stupid about it, and Mr. Knightley gravely said,\n\n“This explains the sort of clever thing that is wanted, and Mr. Weston has done very well for himself; but he must have knocked up every body else. Perfection should not have come quite so soon.”\n\n“Oh! for myself, I protest I must be excused,” said Mrs. Elton; “I really cannot attempt—I am not at all fond of the sort of thing. I had an acrostic once sent to me upon my own name, which I was not at all pleased with. I knew who it came from. An abominable puppy!—You know who I mean (nodding to her husband). These kind of things are very well at Christmas, when one is sitting round the fire; but quite out of place, in my opinion, when one is exploring about the country in summer. Miss Woodhouse must excuse me. I am not one of those who have witty things at every body’s service. I do not pretend to be a wit. I have a great deal of vivacity in my own way, but I really must be allowed to judge when to speak and when to hold my tongue. Pass us, if you please, Mr. Churchill. Pass Mr. E., Knightley, Jane, and myself. We have nothing clever to say—not one of us.\n\n“Yes, yes, pray pass me,” added her husband, with a sort of sneering consciousness; “I have nothing to say that can entertain Miss Woodhouse, or any other young lady. An old married man—quite good for nothing. Shall we walk, Augusta?”\n\n“With all my heart. I am really tired of exploring so long on one spot. Come, Jane, take my other arm.”\n\nJane declined it, however, and the husband and wife walked off. “Happy couple!” said Frank Churchill, as soon as they were out of hearing:—“How well they suit one another!—Very lucky—marrying as they did, upon an acquaintance formed only in a public place!—They only knew each other, I think, a few weeks in Bath! Peculiarly lucky!—for as to any real knowledge of a person’s disposition that Bath, or any public place, can give—it is all nothing; there can be no knowledge. It is only by seeing women in their own homes, among their own set, just as they always are, that you can form any just judgment. Short of that, it is all guess and luck—and will generally be ill-luck. How many a man has committed himself on a short acquaintance, and rued it all the rest of his life!”\n\nMiss Fairfax, who had seldom spoken before, except among her own confederates, spoke now.\n\n“Such things do occur, undoubtedly.”—She was stopped by a cough. Frank Churchill turned towards her to listen.\n\n“You were speaking,” said he, gravely. She recovered her voice.\n\n“I was only going to observe, that though such unfortunate circumstances do sometimes occur both to men and women, I cannot imagine them to be very frequent. A hasty and imprudent attachment may arise—but there is generally time to recover from it afterwards. I would be understood to mean, that it can be only weak, irresolute characters, (whose happiness must be always at the mercy of chance,) who will suffer an unfortunate acquaintance to be an inconvenience, an oppression for ever.”\n\nHe made no answer; merely looked, and bowed in submission; and soon afterwards said, in a lively tone,\n\n“Well, I have so little confidence in my own judgment, that whenever I marry, I hope some body will chuse my wife for me. Will you? (turning to Emma.) Will you chuse a wife for me?—I am sure I should like any body fixed on by you. You provide for the family, you know, (with a smile at his father). Find some body for me. I am in no hurry. Adopt her, educate her.”\n\n“And make her like myself.”\n\n“By all means, if you can.”\n\n“Very well. I undertake the commission. You shall have a charming wife.”\n\n“She must be very lively, and have hazle eyes. I care for nothing else. I shall go abroad for a couple of years—and when I return, I shall come to you for my wife. Remember.”\n\nEmma was in no danger of forgetting. It was a commission to touch every favourite feeling. Would not Harriet be the very creature described? Hazle eyes excepted, two years more might make her all that he wished. He might even have Harriet in his thoughts at the moment; who could say? Referring the education to her seemed to imply it.\n\n“Now, ma’am,” said Jane to her aunt, “shall we join Mrs. Elton?”\n\n“If you please, my dear. With all my heart. I am quite ready. I was ready to have gone with her, but this will do just as well. We shall soon overtake her. There she is—no, that’s somebody else. That’s one of the ladies in the Irish car party, not at all like her.—Well, I declare—”\n\nThey walked off, followed in half a minute by Mr. Knightley. Mr. Weston, his son, Emma, and Harriet, only remained; and the young man’s spirits now rose to a pitch almost unpleasant. Even Emma grew tired at last of flattery and merriment, and wished herself rather walking quietly about with any of the others, or sitting almost alone, and quite unattended to, in tranquil observation of the beautiful views beneath her. The appearance of the servants looking out for them to give notice of the carriages was a joyful sight; and even the bustle of collecting and preparing to depart, and the solicitude of Mrs. Elton to have her carriage first, were gladly endured, in the prospect of the quiet drive home which was to close the very questionable enjoyments of this day of pleasure. Such another scheme, composed of so many ill-assorted people, she hoped never to be betrayed into again.\n\nWhile waiting for the carriage, she found Mr. Knightley by her side. He looked around, as if to see that no one were near, and then said,\n\n“Emma, I must once more speak to you as I have been used to do: a privilege rather endured than allowed, perhaps, but I must still use it. I cannot see you acting wrong, without a remonstrance. How could you be so unfeeling to Miss Bates? How could you be so insolent in your wit to a woman of her character, age, and situation?—Emma, I had not thought it possible.”\n\nEmma recollected, blushed, was sorry, but tried to laugh it off.\n\n“Nay, how could I help saying what I did?—Nobody could have helped it. It was not so very bad. I dare say she did not understand me.”\n\n“I assure you she did. She felt your full meaning. She has talked of it since. I wish you could have heard how she talked of it—with what candour and generosity. I wish you could have heard her honouring your forbearance, in being able to pay her such attentions, as she was for ever receiving from yourself and your father, when her society must be so irksome.”\n\n“Oh!” cried Emma, “I know there is not a better creature in the world: but you must allow, that what is good and what is ridiculous are most unfortunately blended in her.”\n\n“They are blended,” said he, “I acknowledge; and, were she prosperous, I could allow much for the occasional prevalence of the ridiculous over the good. Were she a woman of fortune, I would leave every harmless absurdity to take its chance, I would not quarrel with you for any liberties of manner. Were she your equal in situation—but, Emma, consider how far this is from being the case. She is poor; she has sunk from the comforts she was born to; and, if she live to old age, must probably sink more. Her situation should secure your compassion. It was badly done, indeed! You, whom she had known from an infant, whom she had seen grow up from a period when her notice was an honour, to have you now, in thoughtless spirits, and the pride of the moment, laugh at her, humble her—and before her niece, too—and before others, many of whom (certainly some,) would be entirely guided by your treatment of her.—This is not pleasant to you, Emma—and it is very far from pleasant to me; but I must, I will,—I will tell you truths while I can; satisfied with proving myself your friend by very faithful counsel, and trusting that you will some time or other do me greater justice than you can do now.”\n\nWhile they talked, they were advancing towards the carriage; it was ready; and, before she could speak again, he had handed her in. He had misinterpreted the feelings which had kept her face averted, and her tongue motionless. They were combined only of anger against herself, mortification, and deep concern. She had not been able to speak; and, on entering the carriage, sunk back for a moment overcome—then reproaching herself for having taken no leave, making no acknowledgment, parting in apparent sullenness, she looked out with voice and hand eager to shew a difference; but it was just too late. He had turned away, and the horses were in motion. She continued to look back, but in vain; and soon, with what appeared unusual speed, they were half way down the hill, and every thing left far behind. She was vexed beyond what could have been expressed—almost beyond what she could conceal. Never had she felt so agitated, mortified, grieved, at any circumstance in her life. She was most forcibly struck. The truth of this representation there was no denying. She felt it at her heart. How could she have been so brutal, so cruel to Miss Bates! How could she have exposed herself to such ill opinion in any one she valued! And how suffer him to leave her without saying one word of gratitude, of concurrence, of common kindness!\n\nTime did not compose her. As she reflected more, she seemed but to feel it more. She never had been so depressed. Happily it was not necessary to speak. There was only Harriet, who seemed not in spirits herself, fagged, and very willing to be silent; and Emma felt the tears running down her cheeks almost all the way home, without being at any trouble to check them, extraordinary as they were.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter VIII\n\nThe wretchedness of a scheme to Box Hill was in Emma’s thoughts all the evening. How it might be considered by the rest of the party, she could not tell. They, in their different homes, and their different ways, might be looking back on it with pleasure; but in her view it was a morning more completely misspent, more totally bare of rational satisfaction at the time, and more to be abhorred in recollection, than any she had ever passed. A whole evening of back-gammon with her father, was felicity to it. There, indeed, lay real pleasure, for there she was giving up the sweetest hours of the twenty-four to his comfort; and feeling that, unmerited as might be the degree of his fond affection and confiding esteem, she could not, in her general conduct, be open to any severe reproach. As a daughter, she hoped she was not without a heart. She hoped no one could have said to her, “How could you be so unfeeling to your father?—I must, I will tell you truths while I can.” Miss Bates should never again—no, never! If attention, in future, could do away the past, she might hope to be forgiven. She had been often remiss, her conscience told her so; remiss, perhaps, more in thought than fact; scornful, ungracious. But it should be so no more. In the warmth of true contrition, she would call upon her the very next morning, and it should be the beginning, on her side, of a regular, equal, kindly intercourse.\n\nShe was just as determined when the morrow came, and went early, that nothing might prevent her. It was not unlikely, she thought, that she might see Mr. Knightley in her way; or, perhaps, he might come in while she were paying her visit. She had no objection. She would not be ashamed of the appearance of the penitence, so justly and truly hers. Her eyes were towards Donwell as she walked, but she saw him not.\n\n“The ladies were all at home.” She had never rejoiced at the sound before, nor ever before entered the passage, nor walked up the stairs, with any wish of giving pleasure, but in conferring obligation, or of deriving it, except in subsequent ridicule.\n\nThere was a bustle on her approach; a good deal of moving and talking. She heard Miss Bates’s voice, something was to be done in a hurry; the maid looked frightened and awkward; hoped she would be pleased to wait a moment, and then ushered her in too soon. The aunt and niece seemed both escaping into the adjoining room. Jane she had a distinct glimpse of, looking extremely ill; and, before the door had shut them out, she heard Miss Bates saying, “Well, my dear, I shall say you are laid down upon the bed, and I am sure you are ill enough.”\n\nPoor old Mrs. Bates, civil and humble as usual, looked as if she did not quite understand what was going on.\n\n“I am afraid Jane is not very well,” said she, “but I do not know; they tell me she is well. I dare say my daughter will be here presently, Miss Woodhouse. I hope you find a chair. I wish Hetty had not gone. I am very little able—Have you a chair, ma’am? Do you sit where you like? I am sure she will be here presently.”\n\nEmma seriously hoped she would. She had a moment’s fear of Miss Bates keeping away from her. But Miss Bates soon came—“Very happy and obliged”—but Emma’s conscience told her that there was not the same cheerful volubility as before—less ease of look and manner. A very friendly inquiry after Miss Fairfax, she hoped, might lead the way to a return of old feelings. The touch seemed immediate.\n\n“Ah! Miss Woodhouse, how kind you are!—I suppose you have heard—and are come to give us joy. This does not seem much like joy, indeed, in me—(twinkling away a tear or two)—but it will be very trying for us to part with her, after having had her so long, and she has a dreadful headache just now, writing all the morning:—such long letters, you know, to be written to Colonel Campbell, and Mrs. Dixon. ‘My dear,’ said I, ‘you will blind yourself’—for tears were in her eyes perpetually. One cannot wonder, one cannot wonder. It is a great change; and though she is amazingly fortunate—such a situation, I suppose, as no young woman before ever met with on first going out—do not think us ungrateful, Miss Woodhouse, for such surprising good fortune—(again dispersing her tears)—but, poor dear soul! if you were to see what a headache she has. When one is in great pain, you know one cannot feel any blessing quite as it may deserve. She is as low as possible. To look at her, nobody would think how delighted and happy she is to have secured such a situation. You will excuse her not coming to you—she is not able—she is gone into her own room—I want her to lie down upon the bed. ‘My dear,’ said I, ‘I shall say you are laid down upon the bed:’ but, however, she is not; she is walking about the room. But, now that she has written her letters, she says she shall soon be well. She will be extremely sorry to miss seeing you, Miss Woodhouse, but your kindness will excuse her. You were kept waiting at the door—I was quite ashamed—but somehow there was a little bustle—for it so happened that we had not heard the knock, and till you were on the stairs, we did not know any body was coming. ‘It is only Mrs. Cole,’ said I, ‘depend upon it. Nobody else would come so early.’ ‘Well,’ said she, ‘it must be borne some time or other, and it may as well be now.’ But then Patty came in, and said it was you. ‘Oh!’ said I, ‘it is Miss Woodhouse: I am sure you will like to see her.’—‘I can see nobody,’ said she; and up she got, and would go away; and that was what made us keep you waiting—and extremely sorry and ashamed we were. ‘If you must go, my dear,’ said I, ‘you must, and I will say you are laid down upon the bed.’”\n\nEmma was most sincerely interested. Her heart had been long growing kinder towards Jane; and this picture of her present sufferings acted as a cure of every former ungenerous suspicion, and left her nothing but pity; and the remembrance of the less just and less gentle sensations of the past, obliged her to admit that Jane might very naturally resolve on seeing Mrs. Cole or any other steady friend, when she might not bear to see herself. She spoke as she felt, with earnest regret and solicitude—sincerely wishing that the circumstances which she collected from Miss Bates to be now actually determined on, might be as much for Miss Fairfax’s advantage and comfort as possible. “It must be a severe trial to them all. She had understood it was to be delayed till Colonel Campbell’s return.”\n\n“So very kind!” replied Miss Bates. “But you are always kind.”\n\nThere was no bearing such an “always;” and to break through her dreadful gratitude, Emma made the direct inquiry of—\n\n“Where—may I ask?—is Miss Fairfax going?”\n\n“To a Mrs. Smallridge—charming woman—most superior—to have the charge of her three little girls—delightful children. Impossible that any situation could be more replete with comfort; if we except, perhaps, Mrs. Suckling’s own family, and Mrs. Bragge’s; but Mrs. Smallridge is intimate with both, and in the very same neighbourhood:—lives only four miles from Maple Grove. Jane will be only four miles from Maple Grove.”\n\n“Mrs. Elton, I suppose, has been the person to whom Miss Fairfax owes—”\n\n“Yes, our good Mrs. Elton. The most indefatigable, true friend. She would not take a denial. She would not let Jane say, ‘No;’ for when Jane first heard of it, (it was the day before yesterday, the very morning we were at Donwell,) when Jane first heard of it, she was quite decided against accepting the offer, and for the reasons you mention; exactly as you say, she had made up her mind to close with nothing till Colonel Campbell’s return, and nothing should induce her to enter into any engagement at present—and so she told Mrs. Elton over and over again—and I am sure I had no more idea that she would change her mind!—but that good Mrs. Elton, whose judgment never fails her, saw farther than I did. It is not every body that would have stood out in such a kind way as she did, and refuse to take Jane’s answer; but she positively declared she would not write any such denial yesterday, as Jane wished her; she would wait—and, sure enough, yesterday evening it was all settled that Jane should go. Quite a surprize to me! I had not the least idea!—Jane took Mrs. Elton aside, and told her at once, that upon thinking over the advantages of Mrs. Smallridge’s situation, she had come to the resolution of accepting it.—I did not know a word of it till it was all settled.”\n\n“You spent the evening with Mrs. Elton?”\n\n“Yes, all of us; Mrs. Elton would have us come. It was settled so, upon the hill, while we were walking about with Mr. Knightley. ‘You must all spend your evening with us,’ said she—‘I positively must have you all come.’”\n\n“Mr. Knightley was there too, was he?”\n\n“No, not Mr. Knightley; he declined it from the first; and though I thought he would come, because Mrs. Elton declared she would not let him off, he did not;—but my mother, and Jane, and I, were all there, and a very agreeable evening we had. Such kind friends, you know, Miss Woodhouse, one must always find agreeable, though every body seemed rather fagged after the morning’s party. Even pleasure, you know, is fatiguing—and I cannot say that any of them seemed very much to have enjoyed it. However, I shall always think it a very pleasant party, and feel extremely obliged to the kind friends who included me in it.”\n\n“Miss Fairfax, I suppose, though you were not aware of it, had been making up her mind the whole day?”\n\n“I dare say she had.”\n\n“Whenever the time may come, it must be unwelcome to her and all her friends—but I hope her engagement will have every alleviation that is possible—I mean, as to the character and manners of the family.”\n\n“Thank you, dear Miss Woodhouse. Yes, indeed, there is every thing in the world that can make her happy in it. Except the Sucklings and Bragges, there is not such another nursery establishment, so liberal and elegant, in all Mrs. Elton’s acquaintance. Mrs. Smallridge, a most delightful woman!—A style of living almost equal to Maple Grove—and as to the children, except the little Sucklings and little Bragges, there are not such elegant sweet children anywhere. Jane will be treated with such regard and kindness!—It will be nothing but pleasure, a life of pleasure.—And her salary!—I really cannot venture to name her salary to you, Miss Woodhouse. Even you, used as you are to great sums, would hardly believe that so much could be given to a young person like Jane.”\n\n“Ah! madam,” cried Emma, “if other children are at all like what I remember to have been myself, I should think five times the amount of what I have ever yet heard named as a salary on such occasions, dearly earned.”\n\n“You are so noble in your ideas!”\n\n“And when is Miss Fairfax to leave you?”\n\n“Very soon, very soon, indeed; that’s the worst of it. Within a fortnight. Mrs. Smallridge is in a great hurry. My poor mother does not know how to bear it. So then, I try to put it out of her thoughts, and say, Come ma’am, do not let us think about it any more.”\n\n“Her friends must all be sorry to lose her; and will not Colonel and Mrs. Campbell be sorry to find that she has engaged herself before their return?”\n\n“Yes; Jane says she is sure they will; but yet, this is such a situation as she cannot feel herself justified in declining. I was so astonished when she first told me what she had been saying to Mrs. Elton, and when Mrs. Elton at the same moment came congratulating me upon it! It was before tea—stay—no, it could not be before tea, because we were just going to cards—and yet it was before tea, because I remember thinking—Oh! no, now I recollect, now I have it; something happened before tea, but not that. Mr. Elton was called out of the room before tea, old John Abdy’s son wanted to speak with him. Poor old John, I have a great regard for him; he was clerk to my poor father twenty-seven years; and now, poor old man, he is bed-ridden, and very poorly with the rheumatic gout in his joints—I must go and see him to-day; and so will Jane, I am sure, if she gets out at all. And poor John’s son came to talk to Mr. Elton about relief from the parish; he is very well to do himself, you know, being head man at the Crown, ostler, and every thing of that sort, but still he cannot keep his father without some help; and so, when Mr. Elton came back, he told us what John ostler had been telling him, and then it came out about the chaise having been sent to Randalls to take Mr. Frank Churchill to Richmond. That was what happened before tea. It was after tea that Jane spoke to Mrs. Elton.”\n\nMiss Bates would hardly give Emma time to say how perfectly new this circumstance was to her; but as without supposing it possible that she could be ignorant of any of the particulars of Mr. Frank Churchill’s going, she proceeded to give them all, it was of no consequence.\n\nWhat Mr. Elton had learned from the ostler on the subject, being the accumulation of the ostler’s own knowledge, and the knowledge of the servants at Randalls, was, that a messenger had come over from Richmond soon after the return of the party from Box Hill—which messenger, however, had been no more than was expected; and that Mr. Churchill had sent his nephew a few lines, containing, upon the whole, a tolerable account of Mrs. Churchill, and only wishing him not to delay coming back beyond the next morning early; but that Mr. Frank Churchill having resolved to go home directly, without waiting at all, and his horse seeming to have got a cold, Tom had been sent off immediately for the Crown chaise, and the ostler had stood out and seen it pass by, the boy going a good pace, and driving very steady.\n\nThere was nothing in all this either to astonish or interest, and it caught Emma’s attention only as it united with the subject which already engaged her mind. The contrast between Mrs. Churchill’s importance in the world, and Jane Fairfax’s, struck her; one was every thing, the other nothing—and she sat musing on the difference of woman’s destiny, and quite unconscious on what her eyes were fixed, till roused by Miss Bates’s saying,\n\n“Aye, I see what you are thinking of, the pianoforte. What is to become of that?—Very true. Poor dear Jane was talking of it just now.—‘You must go,’ said she. ‘You and I must part. You will have no business here.—Let it stay, however,’ said she; ‘give it houseroom till Colonel Campbell comes back. I shall talk about it to him; he will settle for me; he will help me out of all my difficulties.’—And to this day, I do believe, she knows not whether it was his present or his daughter’s.”\n\nNow Emma was obliged to think of the pianoforte; and the remembrance of all her former fanciful and unfair conjectures was so little pleasing, that she soon allowed herself to believe her visit had been long enough; and, with a repetition of every thing that she could venture to say of the good wishes which she really felt, took leave.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter IX\n\nEmma’s pensive meditations, as she walked home, were not interrupted; but on entering the parlour, she found those who must rouse her. Mr. Knightley and Harriet had arrived during her absence, and were sitting with her father.—Mr. Knightley immediately got up, and in a manner decidedly graver than usual, said,\n\n“I would not go away without seeing you, but I have no time to spare, and therefore must now be gone directly. I am going to London, to spend a few days with John and Isabella. Have you any thing to send or say, besides the ‘love,’ which nobody carries?”\n\n“Nothing at all. But is not this a sudden scheme?”\n\n“Yes—rather—I have been thinking of it some little time.”\n\nEmma was sure he had not forgiven her; he looked unlike himself. Time, however, she thought, would tell him that they ought to be friends again. While he stood, as if meaning to go, but not going—her father began his inquiries.\n\n“Well, my dear, and did you get there safely?—And how did you find my worthy old friend and her daughter?—I dare say they must have been very much obliged to you for coming. Dear Emma has been to call on Mrs. and Miss Bates, Mr. Knightley, as I told you before. She is always so attentive to them!”\n\nEmma’s colour was heightened by this unjust praise; and with a smile, and shake of the head, which spoke much, she looked at Mr. Knightley.—It seemed as if there were an instantaneous impression in her favour, as if his eyes received the truth from hers, and all that had passed of good in her feelings were at once caught and honoured.— He looked at her with a glow of regard. She was warmly gratified—and in another moment still more so, by a little movement of more than common friendliness on his part.—He took her hand;—whether she had not herself made the first motion, she could not say—she might, perhaps, have rather offered it—but he took her hand, pressed it, and certainly was on the point of carrying it to his lips—when, from some fancy or other, he suddenly let it go.—Why he should feel such a scruple, why he should change his mind when it was all but done, she could not perceive.—He would have judged better, she thought, if he had not stopped.—The intention, however, was indubitable; and whether it was that his manners had in general so little gallantry, or however else it happened, but she thought nothing became him more.—It was with him, of so simple, yet so dignified a nature.—She could not but recall the attempt with great satisfaction. It spoke such perfect amity.—He left them immediately afterwards—gone in a moment. He always moved with the alertness of a mind which could neither be undecided nor dilatory, but now he seemed more sudden than usual in his disappearance.\n\nEmma could not regret her having gone to Miss Bates, but she wished she had left her ten minutes earlier;—it would have been a great pleasure to talk over Jane Fairfax’s situation with Mr. Knightley.—Neither would she regret that he should be going to Brunswick Square, for she knew how much his visit would be enjoyed—but it might have happened at a better time—and to have had longer notice of it, would have been pleasanter.—They parted thorough friends, however; she could not be deceived as to the meaning of his countenance, and his unfinished gallantry;—it was all done to assure her that she had fully recovered his good opinion.—He had been sitting with them half an hour, she found. It was a pity that she had not come back earlier!\n\nIn the hope of diverting her father’s thoughts from the disagreeableness of Mr. Knightley’s going to London; and going so suddenly; and going on horseback, which she knew would be all very bad; Emma communicated her news of Jane Fairfax, and her dependence on the effect was justified; it supplied a very useful check,—interested, without disturbing him. He had long made up his mind to Jane Fairfax’s going out as governess, and could talk of it cheerfully, but Mr. Knightley’s going to London had been an unexpected blow.\n\n“I am very glad, indeed, my dear, to hear she is to be so comfortably settled. Mrs. Elton is very good-natured and agreeable, and I dare say her acquaintance are just what they ought to be. I hope it is a dry situation, and that her health will be taken good care of. It ought to be a first object, as I am sure poor Miss Taylor’s always was with me. You know, my dear, she is going to be to this new lady what Miss Taylor was to us. And I hope she will be better off in one respect, and not be induced to go away after it has been her home so long.”\n\nThe following day brought news from Richmond to throw every thing else into the background. An express arrived at Randalls to announce the death of Mrs. Churchill! Though her nephew had had no particular reason to hasten back on her account, she had not lived above six-and-thirty hours after his return. A sudden seizure of a different nature from any thing foreboded by her general state, had carried her off after a short struggle. The great Mrs. Churchill was no more.\n\nIt was felt as such things must be felt. Every body had a degree of gravity and sorrow; tenderness towards the departed, solicitude for the surviving friends; and, in a reasonable time, curiosity to know where she would be buried. Goldsmith tells us, that when lovely woman stoops to folly, she has nothing to do but to die; and when she stoops to be disagreeable, it is equally to be recommended as a clearer of ill-fame. Mrs. Churchill, after being disliked at least twenty-five years, was now spoken of with compassionate allowances. In one point she was fully justified. She had never been admitted before to be seriously ill. The event acquitted her of all the fancifulness, and all the selfishness of imaginary complaints.\n\n“Poor Mrs. Churchill! no doubt she had been suffering a great deal: more than any body had ever supposed—and continual pain would try the temper. It was a sad event—a great shock—with all her faults, what would Mr. Churchill do without her? Mr. Churchill’s loss would be dreadful indeed. Mr. Churchill would never get over it.”—Even Mr. Weston shook his head, and looked solemn, and said, “Ah! poor woman, who would have thought it!” and resolved, that his mourning should be as handsome as possible; and his wife sat sighing and moralising over her broad hems with a commiseration and good sense, true and steady. How it would affect Frank was among the earliest thoughts of both. It was also a very early speculation with Emma. The character of Mrs. Churchill, the grief of her husband—her mind glanced over them both with awe and compassion—and then rested with lightened feelings on how Frank might be affected by the event, how benefited, how freed. She saw in a moment all the possible good. Now, an attachment to Harriet Smith would have nothing to encounter. Mr. Churchill, independent of his wife, was feared by nobody; an easy, guidable man, to be persuaded into any thing by his nephew. All that remained to be wished was, that the nephew should form the attachment, as, with all her goodwill in the cause, Emma could feel no certainty of its being already formed.\n\nHarriet behaved extremely well on the occasion, with great self-command. What ever she might feel of brighter hope, she betrayed nothing. Emma was gratified, to observe such a proof in her of strengthened character, and refrained from any allusion that might endanger its maintenance. They spoke, therefore, of Mrs. Churchill’s death with mutual forbearance.\n\nShort letters from Frank were received at Randalls, communicating all that was immediately important of their state and plans. Mr. Churchill was better than could be expected; and their first removal, on the departure of the funeral for Yorkshire, was to be to the house of a very old friend in Windsor, to whom Mr. Churchill had been promising a visit the last ten years. At present, there was nothing to be done for Harriet; good wishes for the future were all that could yet be possible on Emma’s side.\n\nIt was a more pressing concern to shew attention to Jane Fairfax, whose prospects were closing, while Harriet’s opened, and whose engagements now allowed of no delay in any one at Highbury, who wished to shew her kindness—and with Emma it was grown into a first wish. She had scarcely a stronger regret than for her past coldness; and the person, whom she had been so many months neglecting, was now the very one on whom she would have lavished every distinction of regard or sympathy. She wanted to be of use to her; wanted to shew a value for her society, and testify respect and consideration. She resolved to prevail on her to spend a day at Hartfield. A note was written to urge it. The invitation was refused, and by a verbal message. “Miss Fairfax was not well enough to write;” and when Mr. Perry called at Hartfield, the same morning, it appeared that she was so much indisposed as to have been visited, though against her own consent, by himself, and that she was suffering under severe headaches, and a nervous fever to a degree, which made him doubt the possibility of her going to Mrs. Smallridge’s at the time proposed. Her health seemed for the moment completely deranged—appetite quite gone—and though there were no absolutely alarming symptoms, nothing touching the pulmonary complaint, which was the standing apprehension of the family, Mr. Perry was uneasy about her. He thought she had undertaken more than she was equal to, and that she felt it so herself, though she would not own it. Her spirits seemed overcome. Her present home, he could not but observe, was unfavourable to a nervous disorder:—confined always to one room;—he could have wished it otherwise—and her good aunt, though his very old friend, he must acknowledge to be not the best companion for an invalid of that description. Her care and attention could not be questioned; they were, in fact, only too great. He very much feared that Miss Fairfax derived more evil than good from them. Emma listened with the warmest concern; grieved for her more and more, and looked around eager to discover some way of being useful. To take her—be it only an hour or two—from her aunt, to give her change of air and scene, and quiet rational conversation, even for an hour or two, might do her good; and the following morning she wrote again to say, in the most feeling language she could command, that she would call for her in the carriage at any hour that Jane would name—mentioning that she had Mr. Perry’s decided opinion, in favour of such exercise for his patient. The answer was only in this short note:\n\n“Miss Fairfax’s compliments and thanks, but is quite unequal to any exercise.”\n\nEmma felt that her own note had deserved something better; but it was impossible to quarrel with words, whose tremulous inequality shewed indisposition so plainly, and she thought only of how she might best counteract this unwillingness to be seen or assisted. In spite of the answer, therefore, she ordered the carriage, and drove to Mrs. Bates’s, in the hope that Jane would be induced to join her—but it would not do;—Miss Bates came to the carriage door, all gratitude, and agreeing with her most earnestly in thinking an airing might be of the greatest service—and every thing that message could do was tried—but all in vain. Miss Bates was obliged to return without success; Jane was quite unpersuadable; the mere proposal of going out seemed to make her worse.—Emma wished she could have seen her, and tried her own powers; but, almost before she could hint the wish, Miss Bates made it appear that she had promised her niece on no account to let Miss Woodhouse in. “Indeed, the truth was, that poor dear Jane could not bear to see any body—any body at all—Mrs. Elton, indeed, could not be denied—and Mrs. Cole had made such a point—and Mrs. Perry had said so much—but, except them, Jane would really see nobody.”\n\nEmma did not want to be classed with the Mrs. Eltons, the Mrs. Perrys, and the Mrs. Coles, who would force themselves anywhere; neither could she feel any right of preference herself—she submitted, therefore, and only questioned Miss Bates farther as to her niece’s appetite and diet, which she longed to be able to assist. On that subject poor Miss Bates was very unhappy, and very communicative; Jane would hardly eat any thing:—Mr. Perry recommended nourishing food; but every thing they could command (and never had any body such good neighbours) was distasteful.\n\nEmma, on reaching home, called the housekeeper directly, to an examination of her stores; and some arrowroot of very superior quality was speedily despatched to Miss Bates with a most friendly note. In half an hour the arrowroot was returned, with a thousand thanks from Miss Bates, but “dear Jane would not be satisfied without its being sent back; it was a thing she could not take—and, moreover, she insisted on her saying, that she was not at all in want of any thing.”\n\nWhen Emma afterwards heard that Jane Fairfax had been seen wandering about the meadows, at some distance from Highbury, on the afternoon of the very day on which she had, under the plea of being unequal to any exercise, so peremptorily refused to go out with her in the carriage, she could have no doubt—putting every thing together—that Jane was resolved to receive no kindness from her. She was sorry, very sorry. Her heart was grieved for a state which seemed but the more pitiable from this sort of irritation of spirits, inconsistency of action, and inequality of powers; and it mortified her that she was given so little credit for proper feeling, or esteemed so little worthy as a friend: but she had the consolation of knowing that her intentions were good, and of being able to say to herself, that could Mr. Knightley have been privy to all her attempts of assisting Jane Fairfax, could he even have seen into her heart, he would not, on this occasion, have found any thing to reprove.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter X\n\nOne morning, about ten days after Mrs. Churchill’s decease, Emma was called downstairs to Mr. Weston, who “could not stay five minutes, and wanted particularly to speak with her.”—He met her at the parlour-door, and hardly asking her how she did, in the natural key of his voice, sunk it immediately, to say, unheard by her father,\n\n“Can you come to Randalls at any time this morning?—Do, if it be possible. Mrs. Weston wants to see you. She must see you.”\n\n“Is she unwell?”\n\n“No, no, not at all—only a little agitated. She would have ordered the carriage, and come to you, but she must see you alone, and that you know—(nodding towards her father)—Humph!—Can you come?”\n\n“Certainly. This moment, if you please. It is impossible to refuse what you ask in such a way. But what can be the matter?—Is she really not ill?”\n\n“Depend upon me—but ask no more questions. You will know it all in time. The most unaccountable business! But hush, hush!”\n\nTo guess what all this meant, was impossible even for Emma. Something really important seemed announced by his looks; but, as her friend was well, she endeavoured not to be uneasy, and settling it with her father, that she would take her walk now, she and Mr. Weston were soon out of the house together and on their way at a quick pace for Randalls.\n\n“Now,”—said Emma, when they were fairly beyond the sweep gates,—“now Mr. Weston, do let me know what has happened.”\n\n“No, no,”—he gravely replied.—“Don’t ask me. I promised my wife to leave it all to her. She will break it to you better than I can. Do not be impatient, Emma; it will all come out too soon.”\n\n“Break it to me,” cried Emma, standing still with terror.—“Good God!—Mr. Weston, tell me at once.—Something has happened in Brunswick Square. I know it has. Tell me, I charge you tell me this moment what it is.”\n\n“No, indeed you are mistaken.”—\n\n“Mr. Weston do not trifle with me.—Consider how many of my dearest friends are now in Brunswick Square. Which of them is it?—I charge you by all that is sacred, not to attempt concealment.”\n\n“Upon my word, Emma.”—\n\n“Your word!—why not your honour!—why not say upon your honour, that it has nothing to do with any of them? Good Heavens!—What can be to be broke to me, that does not relate to one of that family?”\n\n“Upon my honour,” said he very seriously, “it does not. It is not in the smallest degree connected with any human being of the name of Knightley.”\n\nEmma’s courage returned, and she walked on.\n\n“I was wrong,” he continued, “in talking of its being broke to you. I should not have used the expression. In fact, it does not concern you—it concerns only myself,—that is, we hope.—Humph!—In short, my dear Emma, there is no occasion to be so uneasy about it. I don’t say that it is not a disagreeable business—but things might be much worse.—If we walk fast, we shall soon be at Randalls.”\n\nEmma found that she must wait; and now it required little effort. She asked no more questions therefore, merely employed her own fancy, and that soon pointed out to her the probability of its being some money concern—something just come to light, of a disagreeable nature in the circumstances of the family,—something which the late event at Richmond had brought forward. Her fancy was very active. Half a dozen natural children, perhaps—and poor Frank cut off!—This, though very undesirable, would be no matter of agony to her. It inspired little more than an animating curiosity.\n\n“Who is that gentleman on horseback?” said she, as they proceeded—speaking more to assist Mr. Weston in keeping his secret, than with any other view.\n\n“I do not know.—One of the Otways.—Not Frank;—it is not Frank, I assure you. You will not see him. He is half way to Windsor by this time.”\n\n“Has your son been with you, then?”\n\n“Oh! yes—did not you know?—Well, well, never mind.”\n\nFor a moment he was silent; and then added, in a tone much more guarded and demure,\n\n“Yes, Frank came over this morning, just to ask us how we did.”\n\nThey hurried on, and were speedily at Randalls.—“Well, my dear,” said he, as they entered the room—“I have brought her, and now I hope you will soon be better. I shall leave you together. There is no use in delay. I shall not be far off, if you want me.”—And Emma distinctly heard him add, in a lower tone, before he quitted the room,—“I have been as good as my word. She has not the least idea.”\n\nMrs. Weston was looking so ill, and had an air of so much perturbation, that Emma’s uneasiness increased; and the moment they were alone, she eagerly said,\n\n“What is it my dear friend? Something of a very unpleasant nature, I find, has occurred;—do let me know directly what it is. I have been walking all this way in complete suspense. We both abhor suspense. Do not let mine continue longer. It will do you good to speak of your distress, whatever it may be.”\n\n“Have you indeed no idea?” said Mrs. Weston in a trembling voice. “Cannot you, my dear Emma—cannot you form a guess as to what you are to hear?”\n\n“So far as that it relates to Mr. Frank Churchill, I do guess.”\n\n“You are right. It does relate to him, and I will tell you directly;” (resuming her work, and seeming resolved against looking up.) “He has been here this very morning, on a most extraordinary errand. It is impossible to express our surprize. He came to speak to his father on a subject,—to announce an attachment—”\n\nShe stopped to breathe. Emma thought first of herself, and then of Harriet.\n\n“More than an attachment, indeed,” resumed Mrs. Weston; “an engagement—a positive engagement.—What will you say, Emma—what will any body say, when it is known that Frank Churchill and Miss Fairfax are engaged;—nay, that they have been long engaged!”\n\nEmma even jumped with surprize;—and, horror-struck, exclaimed,\n\n“Jane Fairfax!—Good God! You are not serious? You do not mean it?”\n\n“You may well be amazed,” returned Mrs. Weston, still averting her eyes, and talking on with eagerness, that Emma might have time to recover— “You may well be amazed. But it is even so. There has been a solemn engagement between them ever since October—formed at Weymouth, and kept a secret from every body. Not a creature knowing it but themselves—neither the Campbells, nor her family, nor his.—It is so wonderful, that though perfectly convinced of the fact, it is yet almost incredible to myself. I can hardly believe it.—I thought I knew him.”\n\nEmma scarcely heard what was said.—Her mind was divided between two ideas—her own former conversations with him about Miss Fairfax; and poor Harriet;—and for some time she could only exclaim, and require confirmation, repeated confirmation.\n\n“Well,” said she at last, trying to recover herself; “this is a circumstance which I must think of at least half a day, before I can at all comprehend it. What!—engaged to her all the winter—before either of them came to Highbury?”\n\n“Engaged since October,—secretly engaged.—It has hurt me, Emma, very much. It has hurt his father equally. Some part of his conduct we cannot excuse.”\n\nEmma pondered a moment, and then replied, “I will not pretend not to understand you; and to give you all the relief in my power, be assured that no such effect has followed his attentions to me, as you are apprehensive of.”\n\nMrs. Weston looked up, afraid to believe; but Emma’s countenance was as steady as her words.\n\n“That you may have less difficulty in believing this boast, of my present perfect indifference,” she continued, “I will farther tell you, that there was a period in the early part of our acquaintance, when I did like him, when I was very much disposed to be attached to him—nay, was attached—and how it came to cease, is perhaps the wonder. Fortunately, however, it did cease. I have really for some time past, for at least these three months, cared nothing about him. You may believe me, Mrs. Weston. This is the simple truth.”\n\nMrs. Weston kissed her with tears of joy; and when she could find utterance, assured her, that this protestation had done her more good than any thing else in the world could do.\n\n“Mr. Weston will be almost as much relieved as myself,” said she. “On this point we have been wretched. It was our darling wish that you might be attached to each other—and we were persuaded that it was so.— Imagine what we have been feeling on your account.”\n\n“I have escaped; and that I should escape, may be a matter of grateful wonder to you and myself. But this does not acquit him, Mrs. Weston; and I must say, that I think him greatly to blame. What right had he to come among us with affection and faith engaged, and with manners so very disengaged? What right had he to endeavour to please, as he certainly did—to distinguish any one young woman with persevering attention, as he certainly did—while he really belonged to another?—How could he tell what mischief he might be doing?—How could he tell that he might not be making me in love with him?—very wrong, very wrong indeed.”\n\n“From something that he said, my dear Emma, I rather imagine—”\n\n“And how could she bear such behaviour! Composure with a witness! to look on, while repeated attentions were offering to another woman, before her face, and not resent it.—That is a degree of placidity, which I can neither comprehend nor respect.”\n\n“There were misunderstandings between them, Emma; he said so expressly. He had not time to enter into much explanation. He was here only a quarter of an hour, and in a state of agitation which did not allow the full use even of the time he could stay—but that there had been misunderstandings he decidedly said. The present crisis, indeed, seemed to be brought on by them; and those misunderstandings might very possibly arise from the impropriety of his conduct.”\n\n“Impropriety! Oh! Mrs. Weston—it is too calm a censure. Much, much beyond impropriety!—It has sunk him, I cannot say how it has sunk him in my opinion. So unlike what a man should be!—None of that upright integrity, that strict adherence to truth and principle, that disdain of trick and littleness, which a man should display in every transaction of his life.”\n\n“Nay, dear Emma, now I must take his part; for though he has been wrong in this instance, I have known him long enough to answer for his having many, very many, good qualities; and—”\n\n“Good God!” cried Emma, not attending to her.—“Mrs. Smallridge, too! Jane actually on the point of going as governess! What could he mean by such horrible indelicacy? To suffer her to engage herself—to suffer her even to think of such a measure!”\n\n“He knew nothing about it, Emma. On this article I can fully acquit him. It was a private resolution of hers, not communicated to him—or at least not communicated in a way to carry conviction.—Till yesterday, I know he said he was in the dark as to her plans. They burst on him, I do not know how, but by some letter or message—and it was the discovery of what she was doing, of this very project of hers, which determined him to come forward at once, own it all to his uncle, throw himself on his kindness, and, in short, put an end to the miserable state of concealment that had been carrying on so long.”\n\nEmma began to listen better.\n\n“I am to hear from him soon,” continued Mrs. Weston. “He told me at parting, that he should soon write; and he spoke in a manner which seemed to promise me many particulars that could not be given now. Let us wait, therefore, for this letter. It may bring many extenuations. It may make many things intelligible and excusable which now are not to be understood. Don’t let us be severe, don’t let us be in a hurry to condemn him. Let us have patience. I must love him; and now that I am satisfied on one point, the one material point, I am sincerely anxious for its all turning out well, and ready to hope that it may. They must both have suffered a great deal under such a system of secresy and concealment.”\n\n“His sufferings,” replied Emma dryly, “do not appear to have done him much harm. Well, and how did Mr. Churchill take it?”\n\n“Most favourably for his nephew—gave his consent with scarcely a difficulty. Conceive what the events of a week have done in that family! While poor Mrs. Churchill lived, I suppose there could not have been a hope, a chance, a possibility;—but scarcely are her remains at rest in the family vault, than her husband is persuaded to act exactly opposite to what she would have required. What a blessing it is, when undue influence does not survive the grave!—He gave his consent with very little persuasion.”\n\n“Ah!” thought Emma, “he would have done as much for Harriet.”\n\n“This was settled last night, and Frank was off with the light this morning. He stopped at Highbury, at the Bates’s, I fancy, some time—and then came on hither; but was in such a hurry to get back to his uncle, to whom he is just now more necessary than ever, that, as I tell you, he could stay with us but a quarter of an hour.—He was very much agitated—very much, indeed—to a degree that made him appear quite a different creature from any thing I had ever seen him before.—In addition to all the rest, there had been the shock of finding her so very unwell, which he had had no previous suspicion of—and there was every appearance of his having been feeling a great deal.”\n\n“And do you really believe the affair to have been carrying on with such perfect secresy?—The Campbells, the Dixons, did none of them know of the engagement?”\n\nEmma could not speak the name of Dixon without a little blush.\n\n“None; not one. He positively said that it had been known to no being in the world but their two selves.”\n\n“Well,” said Emma, “I suppose we shall gradually grow reconciled to the idea, and I wish them very happy. But I shall always think it a very abominable sort of proceeding. What has it been but a system of hypocrisy and deceit,—espionage, and treachery?—To come among us with professions of openness and simplicity; and such a league in secret to judge us all!—Here have we been, the whole winter and spring, completely duped, fancying ourselves all on an equal footing of truth and honour, with two people in the midst of us who may have been carrying round, comparing and sitting in judgment on sentiments and words that were never meant for both to hear.—They must take the consequence, if they have heard each other spoken of in a way not perfectly agreeable!”\n\n“I am quite easy on that head,” replied Mrs. Weston. “I am very sure that I never said any thing of either to the other, which both might not have heard.”\n\n“You are in luck.—Your only blunder was confined to my ear, when you imagined a certain friend of ours in love with the lady.”\n\n“True. But as I have always had a thoroughly good opinion of Miss Fairfax, I never could, under any blunder, have spoken ill of her; and as to speaking ill of him, there I must have been safe.”\n\nAt this moment Mr. Weston appeared at a little distance from the window, evidently on the watch. His wife gave him a look which invited him in; and, while he was coming round, added, “Now, dearest Emma, let me intreat you to say and look every thing that may set his heart at ease, and incline him to be satisfied with the match. Let us make the best of it—and, indeed, almost every thing may be fairly said in her favour. It is not a connexion to gratify; but if Mr. Churchill does not feel that, why should we? and it may be a very fortunate circumstance for him, for Frank, I mean, that he should have attached himself to a girl of such steadiness of character and good judgment as I have always given her credit for—and still am disposed to give her credit for, in spite of this one great deviation from the strict rule of right. And how much may be said in her situation for even that error!”\n\n“Much, indeed!” cried Emma feelingly. “If a woman can ever be excused for thinking only of herself, it is in a situation like Jane Fairfax’s.—Of such, one may almost say, that ‘the world is not their’s, nor the world’s law.’”\n\nShe met Mr. Weston on his entrance, with a smiling countenance, exclaiming,\n\n“A very pretty trick you have been playing me, upon my word! This was a device, I suppose, to sport with my curiosity, and exercise my talent of guessing. But you really frightened me. I thought you had lost half your property, at least. And here, instead of its being a matter of condolence, it turns out to be one of congratulation.—I congratulate you, Mr. Weston, with all my heart, on the prospect of having one of the most lovely and accomplished young women in England for your daughter.”\n\nA glance or two between him and his wife, convinced him that all was as right as this speech proclaimed; and its happy effect on his spirits was immediate. His air and voice recovered their usual briskness: he shook her heartily and gratefully by the hand, and entered on the subject in a manner to prove, that he now only wanted time and persuasion to think the engagement no very bad thing. His companions suggested only what could palliate imprudence, or smooth objections; and by the time they had talked it all over together, and he had talked it all over again with Emma, in their walk back to Hartfield, he was become perfectly reconciled, and not far from thinking it the very best thing that Frank could possibly have done.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter XI\n\n“Harriet, poor Harriet!”—Those were the words; in them lay the tormenting ideas which Emma could not get rid of, and which constituted the real misery of the business to her. Frank Churchill had behaved very ill by herself—very ill in many ways,—but it was not so much his behaviour as her own, which made her so angry with him. It was the scrape which he had drawn her into on Harriet’s account, that gave the deepest hue to his offence.—Poor Harriet! to be a second time the dupe of her misconceptions and flattery. Mr. Knightley had spoken prophetically, when he once said, “Emma, you have been no friend to Harriet Smith.”—She was afraid she had done her nothing but disservice.—It was true that she had not to charge herself, in this instance as in the former, with being the sole and original author of the mischief; with having suggested such feelings as might otherwise never have entered Harriet’s imagination; for Harriet had acknowledged her admiration and preference of Frank Churchill before she had ever given her a hint on the subject; but she felt completely guilty of having encouraged what she might have repressed. She might have prevented the indulgence and increase of such sentiments. Her influence would have been enough. And now she was very conscious that she ought to have prevented them.—She felt that she had been risking her friend’s happiness on most insufficient grounds. Common sense would have directed her to tell Harriet, that she must not allow herself to think of him, and that there were five hundred chances to one against his ever caring for her.—“But, with common sense,” she added, “I am afraid I have had little to do.”\n\nShe was extremely angry with herself. If she could not have been angry with Frank Churchill too, it would have been dreadful.—As for Jane Fairfax, she might at least relieve her feelings from any present solicitude on her account. Harriet would be anxiety enough; she need no longer be unhappy about Jane, whose troubles and whose ill-health having, of course, the same origin, must be equally under cure.—Her days of insignificance and evil were over.—She would soon be well, and happy, and prosperous.—Emma could now imagine why her own attentions had been slighted. This discovery laid many smaller matters open. No doubt it had been from jealousy.—In Jane’s eyes she had been a rival; and well might any thing she could offer of assistance or regard be repulsed. An airing in the Hartfield carriage would have been the rack, and arrowroot from the Hartfield storeroom must have been poison. She understood it all; and as far as her mind could disengage itself from the injustice and selfishness of angry feelings, she acknowledged that Jane Fairfax would have neither elevation nor happiness beyond her desert. But poor Harriet was such an engrossing charge! There was little sympathy to be spared for any body else. Emma was sadly fearful that this second disappointment would be more severe than the first. Considering the very superior claims of the object, it ought; and judging by its apparently stronger effect on Harriet’s mind, producing reserve and self-command, it would.—She must communicate the painful truth, however, and as soon as possible. An injunction of secresy had been among Mr. Weston’s parting words. “For the present, the whole affair was to be completely a secret. Mr. Churchill had made a point of it, as a token of respect to the wife he had so very recently lost; and every body admitted it to be no more than due decorum.”—Emma had promised; but still Harriet must be excepted. It was her superior duty.\n\nIn spite of her vexation, she could not help feeling it almost ridiculous, that she should have the very same distressing and delicate office to perform by Harriet, which Mrs. Weston had just gone through by herself. The intelligence, which had been so anxiously announced to her, she was now to be anxiously announcing to another. Her heart beat quick on hearing Harriet’s footstep and voice; so, she supposed, had poor Mrs. Weston felt when she was approaching Randalls. Could the event of the disclosure bear an equal resemblance!—But of that, unfortunately, there could be no chance.\n\n“Well, Miss Woodhouse!” cried Harriet, coming eagerly into the room—“is not this the oddest news that ever was?”\n\n“What news do you mean?” replied Emma, unable to guess, by look or voice, whether Harriet could indeed have received any hint.\n\n“About Jane Fairfax. Did you ever hear any thing so strange? Oh!—you need not be afraid of owning it to me, for Mr. Weston has told me himself. I met him just now. He told me it was to be a great secret; and, therefore, I should not think of mentioning it to any body but you, but he said you knew it.”\n\n“What did Mr. Weston tell you?”—said Emma, still perplexed.\n\n“Oh! he told me all about it; that Jane Fairfax and Mr. Frank Churchill are to be married, and that they have been privately engaged to one another this long while. How very odd!”\n\nIt was, indeed, so odd; Harriet’s behaviour was so extremely odd, that Emma did not know how to understand it. Her character appeared absolutely changed. She seemed to propose shewing no agitation, or disappointment, or peculiar concern in the discovery. Emma looked at her, quite unable to speak.\n\n“Had you any idea,” cried Harriet, “of his being in love with her?—You, perhaps, might.—You (blushing as she spoke) who can see into every body’s heart; but nobody else—”\n\n“Upon my word,” said Emma, “I begin to doubt my having any such talent. Can you seriously ask me, Harriet, whether I imagined him attached to another woman at the very time that I was—tacitly, if not openly—encouraging you to give way to your own feelings?—I never had the slightest suspicion, till within the last hour, of Mr. Frank Churchill’s having the least regard for Jane Fairfax. You may be very sure that if I had, I should have cautioned you accordingly.”\n\n“Me!” cried Harriet, colouring, and astonished. “Why should you caution me?—You do not think I care about Mr. Frank Churchill.”\n\n“I am delighted to hear you speak so stoutly on the subject,” replied Emma, smiling; “but you do not mean to deny that there was a time—and not very distant either—when you gave me reason to understand that you did care about him?”\n\n“Him!—never, never. Dear Miss Woodhouse, how could you so mistake me?” turning away distressed.\n\n“Harriet!” cried Emma, after a moment’s pause—“What do you mean?—Good Heaven! what do you mean?—Mistake you!—Am I to suppose then?—”\n\nShe could not speak another word.—Her voice was lost; and she sat down, waiting in great terror till Harriet should answer.\n\nHarriet, who was standing at some distance, and with face turned from her, did not immediately say any thing; and when she did speak, it was in a voice nearly as agitated as Emma’s.\n\n“I should not have thought it possible,” she began, “that you could have misunderstood me! I know we agreed never to name him—but considering how infinitely superior he is to every body else, I should not have thought it possible that I could be supposed to mean any other person. Mr. Frank Churchill, indeed! I do not know who would ever look at him in the company of the other. I hope I have a better taste than to think of Mr. Frank Churchill, who is like nobody by his side. And that you should have been so mistaken, is amazing!—I am sure, but for believing that you entirely approved and meant to encourage me in my attachment, I should have considered it at first too great a presumption almost, to dare to think of him. At first, if you had not told me that more wonderful things had happened; that there had been matches of greater disparity (those were your very words);—I should not have dared to give way to—I should not have thought it possible—But if you, who had been always acquainted with him—”\n\n“Harriet!” cried Emma, collecting herself resolutely—“Let us understand each other now, without the possibility of farther mistake. Are you speaking of—Mr. Knightley?”\n\n“To be sure I am. I never could have an idea of any body else—and so I thought you knew. When we talked about him, it was as clear as possible.”\n\n“Not quite,” returned Emma, with forced calmness, “for all that you then said, appeared to me to relate to a different person. I could almost assert that you had named Mr. Frank Churchill. I am sure the service Mr. Frank Churchill had rendered you, in protecting you from the gipsies, was spoken of.”\n\n“Oh! Miss Woodhouse, how you do forget!”\n\n“My dear Harriet, I perfectly remember the substance of what I said on the occasion. I told you that I did not wonder at your attachment; that considering the service he had rendered you, it was extremely natural:—and you agreed to it, expressing yourself very warmly as to your sense of that service, and mentioning even what your sensations had been in seeing him come forward to your rescue.—The impression of it is strong on my memory.”\n\n“Oh, dear,” cried Harriet, “now I recollect what you mean; but I was thinking of something very different at the time. It was not the gipsies—it was not Mr. Frank Churchill that I meant. No! (with some elevation) I was thinking of a much more precious circumstance—of Mr. Knightley’s coming and asking me to dance, when Mr. Elton would not stand up with me; and when there was no other partner in the room. That was the kind action; that was the noble benevolence and generosity; that was the service which made me begin to feel how superior he was to every other being upon earth.”\n\n“Good God!” cried Emma, “this has been a most unfortunate—most deplorable mistake!—What is to be done?”\n\n“You would not have encouraged me, then, if you had understood me? At least, however, I cannot be worse off than I should have been, if the other had been the person; and now—it is possible—”\n\nShe paused a few moments. Emma could not speak.\n\n“I do not wonder, Miss Woodhouse,” she resumed, “that you should feel a great difference between the two, as to me or as to any body. You must think one five hundred million times more above me than the other. But I hope, Miss Woodhouse, that supposing—that if—strange as it may appear—. But you know they were your own words, that more wonderful things had happened, matches of greater disparity had taken place than between Mr. Frank Churchill and me; and, therefore, it seems as if such a thing even as this, may have occurred before—and if I should be so fortunate, beyond expression, as to—if Mr. Knightley should really—if he does not mind the disparity, I hope, dear Miss Woodhouse, you will not set yourself against it, and try to put difficulties in the way. But you are too good for that, I am sure.”\n\nHarriet was standing at one of the windows. Emma turned round to look at her in consternation, and hastily said,\n\n“Have you any idea of Mr. Knightley’s returning your affection?”\n\n“Yes,” replied Harriet modestly, but not fearfully—“I must say that I have.”\n\nEmma’s eyes were instantly withdrawn; and she sat silently meditating, in a fixed attitude, for a few minutes. A few minutes were sufficient for making her acquainted with her own heart. A mind like hers, once opening to suspicion, made rapid progress. She touched—she admitted—she acknowledged the whole truth. Why was it so much worse that Harriet should be in love with Mr. Knightley, than with Frank Churchill? Why was the evil so dreadfully increased by Harriet’s having some hope of a return? It darted through her, with the speed of an arrow, that Mr. Knightley must marry no one but herself!\n\nHer own conduct, as well as her own heart, was before her in the same few minutes. She saw it all with a clearness which had never blessed her before. How improperly had she been acting by Harriet! How inconsiderate, how indelicate, how irrational, how unfeeling had been her conduct! What blindness, what madness, had led her on! It struck her with dreadful force, and she was ready to give it every bad name in the world. Some portion of respect for herself, however, in spite of all these demerits—some concern for her own appearance, and a strong sense of justice by Harriet—(there would be no need of compassion to the girl who believed herself loved by Mr. Knightley—but justice required that she should not be made unhappy by any coldness now,) gave Emma the resolution to sit and endure farther with calmness, with even apparent kindness.—For her own advantage indeed, it was fit that the utmost extent of Harriet’s hopes should be enquired into; and Harriet had done nothing to forfeit the regard and interest which had been so voluntarily formed and maintained—or to deserve to be slighted by the person, whose counsels had never led her right.—Rousing from reflection, therefore, and subduing her emotion, she turned to Harriet again, and, in a more inviting accent, renewed the conversation; for as to the subject which had first introduced it, the wonderful story of Jane Fairfax, that was quite sunk and lost.—Neither of them thought but of Mr. Knightley and themselves.\n\nHarriet, who had been standing in no unhappy reverie, was yet very glad to be called from it, by the now encouraging manner of such a judge, and such a friend as Miss Woodhouse, and only wanted invitation, to give the history of her hopes with great, though trembling delight.—Emma’s tremblings as she asked, and as she listened, were better concealed than Harriet’s, but they were not less. Her voice was not unsteady; but her mind was in all the perturbation that such a development of self, such a burst of threatening evil, such a confusion of sudden and perplexing emotions, must create.—She listened with much inward suffering, but with great outward patience, to Harriet’s detail.—Methodical, or well arranged, or very well delivered, it could not be expected to be; but it contained, when separated from all the feebleness and tautology of the narration, a substance to sink her spirit—especially with the corroborating circumstances, which her own memory brought in favour of Mr. Knightley’s most improved opinion of Harriet.\n\nHarriet had been conscious of a difference in his behaviour ever since those two decisive dances.—Emma knew that he had, on that occasion, found her much superior to his expectation. From that evening, or at least from the time of Miss Woodhouse’s encouraging her to think of him, Harriet had begun to be sensible of his talking to her much more than he had been used to do, and of his having indeed quite a different manner towards her; a manner of kindness and sweetness!—Latterly she had been more and more aware of it. When they had been all walking together, he had so often come and walked by her, and talked so very delightfully!—He seemed to want to be acquainted with her. Emma knew it to have been very much the case. She had often observed the change, to almost the same extent.—Harriet repeated expressions of approbation and praise from him—and Emma felt them to be in the closest agreement with what she had known of his opinion of Harriet. He praised her for being without art or affectation, for having simple, honest, generous, feelings.—She knew that he saw such recommendations in Harriet; he had dwelt on them to her more than once.—Much that lived in Harriet’s memory, many little particulars of the notice she had received from him, a look, a speech, a removal from one chair to another, a compliment implied, a preference inferred, had been unnoticed, because unsuspected, by Emma. Circumstances that might swell to half an hour’s relation, and contained multiplied proofs to her who had seen them, had passed undiscerned by her who now heard them; but the two latest occurrences to be mentioned, the two of strongest promise to Harriet, were not without some degree of witness from Emma herself.—The first, was his walking with her apart from the others, in the lime-walk at Donwell, where they had been walking some time before Emma came, and he had taken pains (as she was convinced) to draw her from the rest to himself—and at first, he had talked to her in a more particular way than he had ever done before, in a very particular way indeed!—(Harriet could not recall it without a blush.) He seemed to be almost asking her, whether her affections were engaged.—But as soon as she (Miss Woodhouse) appeared likely to join them, he changed the subject, and began talking about farming:—The second, was his having sat talking with her nearly half an hour before Emma came back from her visit, the very last morning of his being at Hartfield—though, when he first came in, he had said that he could not stay five minutes—and his having told her, during their conversation, that though he must go to London, it was very much against his inclination that he left home at all, which was much more (as Emma felt) than he had acknowledged to her. The superior degree of confidence towards Harriet, which this one article marked, gave her severe pain.\n\nOn the subject of the first of the two circumstances, she did, after a little reflection, venture the following question. “Might he not?—Is not it possible, that when enquiring, as you thought, into the state of your affections, he might be alluding to Mr. Martin—he might have Mr. Martin’s interest in view? But Harriet rejected the suspicion with spirit.\n\n“Mr. Martin! No indeed!—There was not a hint of Mr. Martin. I hope I know better now, than to care for Mr. Martin, or to be suspected of it.”\n\nWhen Harriet had closed her evidence, she appealed to her dear Miss Woodhouse, to say whether she had not good ground for hope.\n\n“I never should have presumed to think of it at first,” said she, “but for you. You told me to observe him carefully, and let his behaviour be the rule of mine—and so I have. But now I seem to feel that I may deserve him; and that if he does chuse me, it will not be any thing so very wonderful.”\n\nThe bitter feelings occasioned by this speech, the many bitter feelings, made the utmost exertion necessary on Emma’s side, to enable her to say on reply,\n\n“Harriet, I will only venture to declare, that Mr. Knightley is the last man in the world, who would intentionally give any woman the idea of his feeling for her more than he really does.”\n\nHarriet seemed ready to worship her friend for a sentence so satisfactory; and Emma was only saved from raptures and fondness, which at that moment would have been dreadful penance, by the sound of her father’s footsteps. He was coming through the hall. Harriet was too much agitated to encounter him. “She could not compose herself— Mr. Woodhouse would be alarmed—she had better go;”—with most ready encouragement from her friend, therefore, she passed off through another door—and the moment she was gone, this was the spontaneous burst of Emma’s feelings: “Oh God! that I had never seen her!”\n\nThe rest of the day, the following night, were hardly enough for her thoughts.—She was bewildered amidst the confusion of all that had rushed on her within the last few hours. Every moment had brought a fresh surprize; and every surprize must be matter of humiliation to her.—How to understand it all! How to understand the deceptions she had been thus practising on herself, and living under!—The blunders, the blindness of her own head and heart!—she sat still, she walked about, she tried her own room, she tried the shrubbery—in every place, every posture, she perceived that she had acted most weakly; that she had been imposed on by others in a most mortifying degree; that she had been imposing on herself in a degree yet more mortifying; that she was wretched, and should probably find this day but the beginning of wretchedness.\n\nTo understand, thoroughly understand her own heart, was the first endeavour. To that point went every leisure moment which her father’s claims on her allowed, and every moment of involuntary absence of mind.\n\nHow long had Mr. Knightley been so dear to her, as every feeling declared him now to be? When had his influence, such influence begun?— When had he succeeded to that place in her affection, which Frank Churchill had once, for a short period, occupied?—She looked back; she compared the two—compared them, as they had always stood in her estimation, from the time of the latter’s becoming known to her—and as they must at any time have been compared by her, had it—oh! had it, by any blessed felicity, occurred to her, to institute the comparison.—She saw that there never had been a time when she did not consider Mr. Knightley as infinitely the superior, or when his regard for her had not been infinitely the most dear. She saw, that in persuading herself, in fancying, in acting to the contrary, she had been entirely under a delusion, totally ignorant of her own heart—and, in short, that she had never really cared for Frank Churchill at all!\n\nThis was the conclusion of the first series of reflection. This was the knowledge of herself, on the first question of inquiry, which she reached; and without being long in reaching it.—She was most sorrowfully indignant; ashamed of every sensation but the one revealed to her—her affection for Mr. Knightley.—Every other part of her mind was disgusting.\n\nWith insufferable vanity had she believed herself in the secret of every body’s feelings; with unpardonable arrogance proposed to arrange every body’s destiny. She was proved to have been universally mistaken; and she had not quite done nothing—for she had done mischief. She had brought evil on Harriet, on herself, and she too much feared, on Mr. Knightley.—Were this most unequal of all connexions to take place, on her must rest all the reproach of having given it a beginning; for his attachment, she must believe to be produced only by a consciousness of Harriet’s;—and even were this not the case, he would never have known Harriet at all but for her folly.\n\nMr. Knightley and Harriet Smith!—It was a union to distance every wonder of the kind.—The attachment of Frank Churchill and Jane Fairfax became commonplace, threadbare, stale in the comparison, exciting no surprize, presenting no disparity, affording nothing to be said or thought.—Mr. Knightley and Harriet Smith!—Such an elevation on her side! Such a debasement on his! It was horrible to Emma to think how it must sink him in the general opinion, to foresee the smiles, the sneers, the merriment it would prompt at his expense; the mortification and disdain of his brother, the thousand inconveniences to himself.—Could it be?—No; it was impossible. And yet it was far, very far, from impossible.—Was it a new circumstance for a man of first-rate abilities to be captivated by very inferior powers? Was it new for one, perhaps too busy to seek, to be the prize of a girl who would seek him?—Was it new for any thing in this world to be unequal, inconsistent, incongruous—or for chance and circumstance (as second causes) to direct the human fate?\n\nOh! had she never brought Harriet forward! Had she left her where she ought, and where he had told her she ought!—Had she not, with a folly which no tongue could express, prevented her marrying the unexceptionable young man who would have made her happy and respectable in the line of life to which she ought to belong—all would have been safe; none of this dreadful sequel would have been.\n\nHow Harriet could ever have had the presumption to raise her thoughts to Mr. Knightley!—How she could dare to fancy herself the chosen of such a man till actually assured of it!—But Harriet was less humble, had fewer scruples than formerly.—Her inferiority, whether of mind or situation, seemed little felt.—She had seemed more sensible of Mr. Elton’s being to stoop in marrying her, than she now seemed of Mr. Knightley’s.—Alas! was not that her own doing too? Who had been at pains to give Harriet notions of self-consequence but herself?—Who but herself had taught her, that she was to elevate herself if possible, and that her claims were great to a high worldly establishment?—If Harriet, from being humble, were grown vain, it was her doing too.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter XII\n\nTill now that she was threatened with its loss, Emma had never known how much of her happiness depended on being first with Mr. Knightley, first in interest and affection.—Satisfied that it was so, and feeling it her due, she had enjoyed it without reflection; and only in the dread of being supplanted, found how inexpressibly important it had been.—Long, very long, she felt she had been first; for, having no female connexions of his own, there had been only Isabella whose claims could be compared with hers, and she had always known exactly how far he loved and esteemed Isabella. She had herself been first with him for many years past. She had not deserved it; she had often been negligent or perverse, slighting his advice, or even wilfully opposing him, insensible of half his merits, and quarrelling with him because he would not acknowledge her false and insolent estimate of her own—but still, from family attachment and habit, and thorough excellence of mind, he had loved her, and watched over her from a girl, with an endeavour to improve her, and an anxiety for her doing right, which no other creature had at all shared. In spite of all her faults, she knew she was dear to him; might she not say, very dear?—When the suggestions of hope, however, which must follow here, presented themselves, she could not presume to indulge them. Harriet Smith might think herself not unworthy of being peculiarly, exclusively, passionately loved by Mr. Knightley. She could not. She could not flatter herself with any idea of blindness in his attachment to her. She had received a very recent proof of its impartiality.—How shocked had he been by her behaviour to Miss Bates! How directly, how strongly had he expressed himself to her on the subject!—Not too strongly for the offence—but far, far too strongly to issue from any feeling softer than upright justice and clear-sighted goodwill.—She had no hope, nothing to deserve the name of hope, that he could have that sort of affection for herself which was now in question; but there was a hope (at times a slight one, at times much stronger,) that Harriet might have deceived herself, and be overrating his regard for her.—Wish it she must, for his sake—be the consequence nothing to herself, but his remaining single all his life. Could she be secure of that, indeed, of his never marrying at all, she believed she should be perfectly satisfied.—Let him but continue the same Mr. Knightley to her and her father, the same Mr. Knightley to all the world; let Donwell and Hartfield lose none of their precious intercourse of friendship and confidence, and her peace would be fully secured.—Marriage, in fact, would not do for her. It would be incompatible with what she owed to her father, and with what she felt for him. Nothing should separate her from her father. She would not marry, even if she were asked by Mr. Knightley.\n\nIt must be her ardent wish that Harriet might be disappointed; and she hoped, that when able to see them together again, she might at least be able to ascertain what the chances for it were.—She should see them henceforward with the closest observance; and wretchedly as she had hitherto misunderstood even those she was watching, she did not know how to admit that she could be blinded here.—He was expected back every day. The power of observation would be soon given—frightfully soon it appeared when her thoughts were in one course. In the meanwhile, she resolved against seeing Harriet.—It would do neither of them good, it would do the subject no good, to be talking of it farther.—She was resolved not to be convinced, as long as she could doubt, and yet had no authority for opposing Harriet’s confidence. To talk would be only to irritate.—She wrote to her, therefore, kindly, but decisively, to beg that she would not, at present, come to Hartfield; acknowledging it to be her conviction, that all farther confidential discussion of one topic had better be avoided; and hoping, that if a few days were allowed to pass before they met again, except in the company of others—she objected only to a tête-à-tête—they might be able to act as if they had forgotten the conversation of yesterday.—Harriet submitted, and approved, and was grateful.\n\nThis point was just arranged, when a visitor arrived to tear Emma’s thoughts a little from the one subject which had engrossed them, sleeping or waking, the last twenty-four hours—Mrs. Weston, who had been calling on her daughter-in-law elect, and took Hartfield in her way home, almost as much in duty to Emma as in pleasure to herself, to relate all the particulars of so interesting an interview.\n\nMr. Weston had accompanied her to Mrs. Bates’s, and gone through his share of this essential attention most handsomely; but she having then induced Miss Fairfax to join her in an airing, was now returned with much more to say, and much more to say with satisfaction, than a quarter of an hour spent in Mrs. Bates’s parlour, with all the encumbrance of awkward feelings, could have afforded.\n\nA little curiosity Emma had; and she made the most of it while her friend related. Mrs. Weston had set off to pay the visit in a good deal of agitation herself; and in the first place had wished not to go at all at present, to be allowed merely to write to Miss Fairfax instead, and to defer this ceremonious call till a little time had passed, and Mr. Churchill could be reconciled to the engagement’s becoming known; as, considering every thing, she thought such a visit could not be paid without leading to reports:—but Mr. Weston had thought differently; he was extremely anxious to shew his approbation to Miss Fairfax and her family, and did not conceive that any suspicion could be excited by it; or if it were, that it would be of any consequence; for “such things,” he observed, “always got about.” Emma smiled, and felt that Mr. Weston had very good reason for saying so. They had gone, in short—and very great had been the evident distress and confusion of the lady. She had hardly been able to speak a word, and every look and action had shewn how deeply she was suffering from consciousness. The quiet, heart-felt satisfaction of the old lady, and the rapturous delight of her daughter—who proved even too joyous to talk as usual, had been a gratifying, yet almost an affecting, scene. They were both so truly respectable in their happiness, so disinterested in every sensation; thought so much of Jane; so much of every body, and so little of themselves, that every kindly feeling was at work for them. Miss Fairfax’s recent illness had offered a fair plea for Mrs. Weston to invite her to an airing; she had drawn back and declined at first, but, on being pressed had yielded; and, in the course of their drive, Mrs. Weston had, by gentle encouragement, overcome so much of her embarrassment, as to bring her to converse on the important subject. Apologies for her seemingly ungracious silence in their first reception, and the warmest expressions of the gratitude she was always feeling towards herself and Mr. Weston, must necessarily open the cause; but when these effusions were put by, they had talked a good deal of the present and of the future state of the engagement. Mrs. Weston was convinced that such conversation must be the greatest relief to her companion, pent up within her own mind as every thing had so long been, and was very much pleased with all that she had said on the subject.\n\n“On the misery of what she had suffered, during the concealment of so many months,” continued Mrs. Weston, “she was energetic. This was one of her expressions. ‘I will not say, that since I entered into the engagement I have not had some happy moments; but I can say, that I have never known the blessing of one tranquil hour:’—and the quivering lip, Emma, which uttered it, was an attestation that I felt at my heart.”\n\n“Poor girl!” said Emma. “She thinks herself wrong, then, for having consented to a private engagement?”\n\n“Wrong! No one, I believe, can blame her more than she is disposed to blame herself. ‘The consequence,’ said she, ‘has been a state of perpetual suffering to me; and so it ought. But after all the punishment that misconduct can bring, it is still not less misconduct. Pain is no expiation. I never can be blameless. I have been acting contrary to all my sense of right; and the fortunate turn that every thing has taken, and the kindness I am now receiving, is what my conscience tells me ought not to be.’ ‘Do not imagine, madam,’ she continued, ‘that I was taught wrong. Do not let any reflection fall on the principles or the care of the friends who brought me up. The error has been all my own; and I do assure you that, with all the excuse that present circumstances may appear to give, I shall yet dread making the story known to Colonel Campbell.’”\n\n“Poor girl!” said Emma again. “She loves him then excessively, I suppose. It must have been from attachment only, that she could be led to form the engagement. Her affection must have overpowered her judgment.”\n\n“Yes, I have no doubt of her being extremely attached to him.”\n\n“I am afraid,” returned Emma, sighing, “that I must often have contributed to make her unhappy.”\n\n“On your side, my love, it was very innocently done. But she probably had something of that in her thoughts, when alluding to the misunderstandings which he had given us hints of before. One natural consequence of the evil she had involved herself in,” she said, “was that of making her unreasonable. The consciousness of having done amiss, had exposed her to a thousand inquietudes, and made her captious and irritable to a degree that must have been—that had been—hard for him to bear. ‘I did not make the allowances,’ said she, ‘which I ought to have done, for his temper and spirits—his delightful spirits, and that gaiety, that playfulness of disposition, which, under any other circumstances, would, I am sure, have been as constantly bewitching to me, as they were at first.’ She then began to speak of you, and of the great kindness you had shewn her during her illness; and with a blush which shewed me how it was all connected, desired me, whenever I had an opportunity, to thank you—I could not thank you too much—for every wish and every endeavour to do her good. She was sensible that you had never received any proper acknowledgment from herself.”\n\n“If I did not know her to be happy now,” said Emma, seriously, “which, in spite of every little drawback from her scrupulous conscience, she must be, I could not bear these thanks;—for, oh! Mrs. Weston, if there were an account drawn up of the evil and the good I have done Miss Fairfax!—Well (checking herself, and trying to be more lively), this is all to be forgotten. You are very kind to bring me these interesting particulars. They shew her to the greatest advantage. I am sure she is very good—I hope she will be very happy. It is fit that the fortune should be on his side, for I think the merit will be all on hers.”\n\nSuch a conclusion could not pass unanswered by Mrs. Weston. She thought well of Frank in almost every respect; and, what was more, she loved him very much, and her defence was, therefore, earnest. She talked with a great deal of reason, and at least equal affection—but she had too much to urge for Emma’s attention; it was soon gone to Brunswick Square or to Donwell; she forgot to attempt to listen; and when Mrs. Weston ended with, “We have not yet had the letter we are so anxious for, you know, but I hope it will soon come,” she was obliged to pause before she answered, and at last obliged to answer at random, before she could at all recollect what letter it was which they were so anxious for.\n\n“Are you well, my Emma?” was Mrs. Weston’s parting question.\n\n“Oh! perfectly. I am always well, you know. Be sure to give me intelligence of the letter as soon as possible.”\n\nMrs. Weston’s communications furnished Emma with more food for unpleasant reflection, by increasing her esteem and compassion, and her sense of past injustice towards Miss Fairfax. She bitterly regretted not having sought a closer acquaintance with her, and blushed for the envious feelings which had certainly been, in some measure, the cause. Had she followed Mr. Knightley’s known wishes, in paying that attention to Miss Fairfax, which was every way her due; had she tried to know her better; had she done her part towards intimacy; had she endeavoured to find a friend there instead of in Harriet Smith; she must, in all probability, have been spared from every pain which pressed on her now.—Birth, abilities, and education, had been equally marking one as an associate for her, to be received with gratitude; and the other—what was she?—Supposing even that they had never become intimate friends; that she had never been admitted into Miss Fairfax’s confidence on this important matter—which was most probable—still, in knowing her as she ought, and as she might, she must have been preserved from the abominable suspicions of an improper attachment to Mr. Dixon, which she had not only so foolishly fashioned and harboured herself, but had so unpardonably imparted; an idea which she greatly feared had been made a subject of material distress to the delicacy of Jane’s feelings, by the levity or carelessness of Frank Churchill’s. Of all the sources of evil surrounding the former, since her coming to Highbury, she was persuaded that she must herself have been the worst. She must have been a perpetual enemy. They never could have been all three together, without her having stabbed Jane Fairfax’s peace in a thousand instances; and on Box Hill, perhaps, it had been the agony of a mind that would bear no more.\n\nThe evening of this day was very long, and melancholy, at Hartfield. The weather added what it could of gloom. A cold stormy rain set in, and nothing of July appeared but in the trees and shrubs, which the wind was despoiling, and the length of the day, which only made such cruel sights the longer visible.\n\nThe weather affected Mr. Woodhouse, and he could only be kept tolerably comfortable by almost ceaseless attention on his daughter’s side, and by exertions which had never cost her half so much before. It reminded her of their first forlorn tête-à-tête, on the evening of Mrs. Weston’s wedding-day; but Mr. Knightley had walked in then, soon after tea, and dissipated every melancholy fancy. Alas! such delightful proofs of Hartfield’s attraction, as those sort of visits conveyed, might shortly be over. The picture which she had then drawn of the privations of the approaching winter, had proved erroneous; no friends had deserted them, no pleasures had been lost.—But her present forebodings she feared would experience no similar contradiction. The prospect before her now, was threatening to a degree that could not be entirely dispelled—that might not be even partially brightened. If all took place that might take place among the circle of her friends, Hartfield must be comparatively deserted; and she left to cheer her father with the spirits only of ruined happiness.\n\nThe child to be born at Randalls must be a tie there even dearer than herself; and Mrs. Weston’s heart and time would be occupied by it. They should lose her; and, probably, in great measure, her husband also.—Frank Churchill would return among them no more; and Miss Fairfax, it was reasonable to suppose, would soon cease to belong to Highbury. They would be married, and settled either at or near Enscombe. All that were good would be withdrawn; and if to these losses, the loss of Donwell were to be added, what would remain of cheerful or of rational society within their reach? Mr. Knightley to be no longer coming there for his evening comfort!—No longer walking in at all hours, as if ever willing to change his own home for their’s!—How was it to be endured? And if he were to be lost to them for Harriet’s sake; if he were to be thought of hereafter, as finding in Harriet’s society all that he wanted; if Harriet were to be the chosen, the first, the dearest, the friend, the wife to whom he looked for all the best blessings of existence; what could be increasing Emma’s wretchedness but the reflection never far distant from her mind, that it had been all her own work?\n\nWhen it came to such a pitch as this, she was not able to refrain from a start, or a heavy sigh, or even from walking about the room for a few seconds—and the only source whence any thing like consolation or composure could be drawn, was in the resolution of her own better conduct, and the hope that, however inferior in spirit and gaiety might be the following and every future winter of her life to the past, it would yet find her more rational, more acquainted with herself, and leave her less to regret when it were gone.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter XIII\n\nThe weather continued much the same all the following morning; and the same loneliness, and the same melancholy, seemed to reign at Hartfield—but in the afternoon it cleared; the wind changed into a softer quarter; the clouds were carried off; the sun appeared; it was summer again. With all the eagerness which such a transition gives, Emma resolved to be out of doors as soon as possible. Never had the exquisite sight, smell, sensation of nature, tranquil, warm, and brilliant after a storm, been more attractive to her. She longed for the serenity they might gradually introduce; and on Mr. Perry’s coming in soon after dinner, with a disengaged hour to give her father, she lost no time in hurrying into the shrubbery.—There, with spirits freshened, and thoughts a little relieved, she had taken a few turns, when she saw Mr. Knightley passing through the garden door, and coming towards her.—It was the first intimation of his being returned from London. She had been thinking of him the moment before, as unquestionably sixteen miles distant.—There was time only for the quickest arrangement of mind. She must be collected and calm. In half a minute they were together. The “How d’ye do’s” were quiet and constrained on each side. She asked after their mutual friends; they were all well.—When had he left them?—Only that morning. He must have had a wet ride.—Yes.—He meant to walk with her, she found. “He had just looked into the dining-room, and as he was not wanted there, preferred being out of doors.”—She thought he neither looked nor spoke cheerfully; and the first possible cause for it, suggested by her fears, was, that he had perhaps been communicating his plans to his brother, and was pained by the manner in which they had been received.\n\nThey walked together. He was silent. She thought he was often looking at her, and trying for a fuller view of her face than it suited her to give. And this belief produced another dread. Perhaps he wanted to speak to her, of his attachment to Harriet; he might be watching for encouragement to begin.—She did not, could not, feel equal to lead the way to any such subject. He must do it all himself. Yet she could not bear this silence. With him it was most unnatural. She considered—resolved—and, trying to smile, began—\n\n“You have some news to hear, now you are come back, that will rather surprize you.”\n\n“Have I?” said he quietly, and looking at her; “of what nature?”\n\n“Oh! the best nature in the world—a wedding.”\n\nAfter waiting a moment, as if to be sure she intended to say no more, he replied,\n\n“If you mean Miss Fairfax and Frank Churchill, I have heard that already.”\n\n“How is it possible?” cried Emma, turning her glowing cheeks towards him; for, while she spoke, it occurred to her that he might have called at Mrs. Goddard’s in his way.\n\n“I had a few lines on parish business from Mr. Weston this morning, and at the end of them he gave me a brief account of what had happened.”\n\nEmma was quite relieved, and could presently say, with a little more composure,\n\n“You probably have been less surprized than any of us, for you have had your suspicions.—I have not forgotten that you once tried to give me a caution.—I wish I had attended to it—but—(with a sinking voice and a heavy sigh) I seem to have been doomed to blindness.”\n\nFor a moment or two nothing was said, and she was unsuspicious of having excited any particular interest, till she found her arm drawn within his, and pressed against his heart, and heard him thus saying, in a tone of great sensibility, speaking low,\n\n“Time, my dearest Emma, time will heal the wound.—Your own excellent sense—your exertions for your father’s sake—I know you will not allow yourself—.” Her arm was pressed again, as he added, in a more broken and subdued accent, “The feelings of the warmest friendship—Indignation—Abominable scoundrel!”—And in a louder, steadier tone, he concluded with, “He will soon be gone. They will soon be in Yorkshire. I am sorry for her. She deserves a better fate.”\n\nEmma understood him; and as soon as she could recover from the flutter of pleasure, excited by such tender consideration, replied,\n\n“You are very kind—but you are mistaken—and I must set you right.— I am not in want of that sort of compassion. My blindness to what was going on, led me to act by them in a way that I must always be ashamed of, and I was very foolishly tempted to say and do many things which may well lay me open to unpleasant conjectures, but I have no other reason to regret that I was not in the secret earlier.”\n\n“Emma!” cried he, looking eagerly at her, “are you, indeed?”—but checking himself—“No, no, I understand you—forgive me—I am pleased that you can say even so much.—He is no object of regret, indeed! and it will not be very long, I hope, before that becomes the acknowledgment of more than your reason.—Fortunate that your affections were not farther entangled!—I could never, I confess, from your manners, assure myself as to the degree of what you felt—I could only be certain that there was a preference—and a preference which I never believed him to deserve.—He is a disgrace to the name of man.—And is he to be rewarded with that sweet young woman?—Jane, Jane, you will be a miserable creature.”\n\n“Mr. Knightley,” said Emma, trying to be lively, but really confused—“I am in a very extraordinary situation. I cannot let you continue in your error; and yet, perhaps, since my manners gave such an impression, I have as much reason to be ashamed of confessing that I never have been at all attached to the person we are speaking of, as it might be natural for a woman to feel in confessing exactly the reverse.—But I never have.”\n\nHe listened in perfect silence. She wished him to speak, but he would not. She supposed she must say more before she were entitled to his clemency; but it was a hard case to be obliged still to lower herself in his opinion. She went on, however.\n\n“I have very little to say for my own conduct.—I was tempted by his attentions, and allowed myself to appear pleased.—An old story, probably—a common case—and no more than has happened to hundreds of my sex before; and yet it may not be the more excusable in one who sets up as I do for Understanding. Many circumstances assisted the temptation. He was the son of Mr. Weston—he was continually here—I always found him very pleasant—and, in short, for (with a sigh) let me swell out the causes ever so ingeniously, they all centre in this at last—my vanity was flattered, and I allowed his attentions. Latterly, however—for some time, indeed—I have had no idea of their meaning any thing.—I thought them a habit, a trick, nothing that called for seriousness on my side. He has imposed on me, but he has not injured me. I have never been attached to him. And now I can tolerably comprehend his behaviour. He never wished to attach me. It was merely a blind to conceal his real situation with another.—It was his object to blind all about him; and no one, I am sure, could be more effectually blinded than myself—except that I was not blinded—that it was my good fortune—that, in short, I was somehow or other safe from him.”\n\nShe had hoped for an answer here—for a few words to say that her conduct was at least intelligible; but he was silent; and, as far as she could judge, deep in thought. At last, and tolerably in his usual tone, he said,\n\n“I have never had a high opinion of Frank Churchill.—I can suppose, however, that I may have underrated him. My acquaintance with him has been but trifling.—And even if I have not underrated him hitherto, he may yet turn out well.—With such a woman he has a chance.—I have no motive for wishing him ill—and for her sake, whose happiness will be involved in his good character and conduct, I shall certainly wish him well.”\n\n“I have no doubt of their being happy together,” said Emma; “I believe them to be very mutually and very sincerely attached.”\n\n“He is a most fortunate man!” returned Mr. Knightley, with energy. “So early in life—at three-and-twenty—a period when, if a man chuses a wife, he generally chuses ill. At three-and-twenty to have drawn such a prize! What years of felicity that man, in all human calculation, has before him!—Assured of the love of such a woman—the disinterested love, for Jane Fairfax’s character vouches for her disinterestedness; every thing in his favour,—equality of situation—I mean, as far as regards society, and all the habits and manners that are important; equality in every point but one—and that one, since the purity of her heart is not to be doubted, such as must increase his felicity, for it will be his to bestow the only advantages she wants.—A man would always wish to give a woman a better home than the one he takes her from; and he who can do it, where there is no doubt of her regard, must, I think, be the happiest of mortals.—Frank Churchill is, indeed, the favourite of fortune. Every thing turns out for his good.—He meets with a young woman at a watering-place, gains her affection, cannot even weary her by negligent treatment—and had he and all his family sought round the world for a perfect wife for him, they could not have found her superior.—His aunt is in the way.—His aunt dies.—He has only to speak.—His friends are eager to promote his happiness.—He had used every body ill—and they are all delighted to forgive him.—He is a fortunate man indeed!”\n\n“You speak as if you envied him.”\n\n“And I do envy him, Emma. In one respect he is the object of my envy.”\n\nEmma could say no more. They seemed to be within half a sentence of Harriet, and her immediate feeling was to avert the subject, if possible. She made her plan; she would speak of something totally different—the children in Brunswick Square; and she only waited for breath to begin, when Mr. Knightley startled her, by saying,\n\n“You will not ask me what is the point of envy.—You are determined, I see, to have no curiosity.—You are wise—but I cannot be wise. Emma, I must tell you what you will not ask, though I may wish it unsaid the next moment.”\n\n“Oh! then, don’t speak it, don’t speak it,” she eagerly cried. “Take a little time, consider, do not commit yourself.”\n\n“Thank you,” said he, in an accent of deep mortification, and not another syllable followed.\n\nEmma could not bear to give him pain. He was wishing to confide in her—perhaps to consult her;—cost her what it would, she would listen. She might assist his resolution, or reconcile him to it; she might give just praise to Harriet, or, by representing to him his own independence, relieve him from that state of indecision, which must be more intolerable than any alternative to such a mind as his.—They had reached the house.\n\n“You are going in, I suppose?” said he.\n\n“No,”—replied Emma—quite confirmed by the depressed manner in which he still spoke—“I should like to take another turn. Mr. Perry is not gone.” And, after proceeding a few steps, she added—“I stopped you ungraciously, just now, Mr. Knightley, and, I am afraid, gave you pain.—But if you have any wish to speak openly to me as a friend, or to ask my opinion of any thing that you may have in contemplation—as a friend, indeed, you may command me.—I will hear whatever you like. I will tell you exactly what I think.”\n\n“As a friend!”—repeated Mr. Knightley.—“Emma, that I fear is a word—No, I have no wish—Stay, yes, why should I hesitate?—I have gone too far already for concealment.—Emma, I accept your offer—Extraordinary as it may seem, I accept it, and refer myself to you as a friend.—Tell me, then, have I no chance of ever succeeding?”\n\nHe stopped in his earnestness to look the question, and the expression of his eyes overpowered her.\n\n“My dearest Emma,” said he, “for dearest you will always be, whatever the event of this hour’s conversation, my dearest, most beloved Emma—tell me at once. Say ‘No,’ if it is to be said.”—She could really say nothing.—“You are silent,” he cried, with great animation; “absolutely silent! at present I ask no more.”\n\nEmma was almost ready to sink under the agitation of this moment. The dread of being awakened from the happiest dream, was perhaps the most prominent feeling.\n\n“I cannot make speeches, Emma:” he soon resumed; and in a tone of such sincere, decided, intelligible tenderness as was tolerably convincing.—“If I loved you less, I might be able to talk about it more. But you know what I am.—You hear nothing but truth from me.—I have blamed you, and lectured you, and you have borne it as no other woman in England would have borne it.—Bear with the truths I would tell you now, dearest Emma, as well as you have borne with them. The manner, perhaps, may have as little to recommend them. God knows, I have been a very indifferent lover.—But you understand me.—Yes, you see, you understand my feelings—and will return them if you can. At present, I ask only to hear, once to hear your voice.”\n\nWhile he spoke, Emma’s mind was most busy, and, with all the wonderful velocity of thought, had been able—and yet without losing a word—to catch and comprehend the exact truth of the whole; to see that Harriet’s hopes had been entirely groundless, a mistake, a delusion, as complete a delusion as any of her own—that Harriet was nothing; that she was every thing herself; that what she had been saying relative to Harriet had been all taken as the language of her own feelings; and that her agitation, her doubts, her reluctance, her discouragement, had been all received as discouragement from herself.—And not only was there time for these convictions, with all their glow of attendant happiness; there was time also to rejoice that Harriet’s secret had not escaped her, and to resolve that it need not, and should not.—It was all the service she could now render her poor friend; for as to any of that heroism of sentiment which might have prompted her to entreat him to transfer his affection from herself to Harriet, as infinitely the most worthy of the two—or even the more simple sublimity of resolving to refuse him at once and for ever, without vouchsafing any motive, because he could not marry them both, Emma had it not. She felt for Harriet, with pain and with contrition; but no flight of generosity run mad, opposing all that could be probable or reasonable, entered her brain. She had led her friend astray, and it would be a reproach to her for ever; but her judgment was as strong as her feelings, and as strong as it had ever been before, in reprobating any such alliance for him, as most unequal and degrading. Her way was clear, though not quite smooth.—She spoke then, on being so entreated.—What did she say?—Just what she ought, of course. A lady always does.—She said enough to shew there need not be despair—and to invite him to say more himself. He had despaired at one period; he had received such an injunction to caution and silence, as for the time crushed every hope;—she had begun by refusing to hear him.—The change had perhaps been somewhat sudden;—her proposal of taking another turn, her renewing the conversation which she had just put an end to, might be a little extraordinary!—She felt its inconsistency; but Mr. Knightley was so obliging as to put up with it, and seek no farther explanation.\n\nSeldom, very seldom, does complete truth belong to any human disclosure; seldom can it happen that something is not a little disguised, or a little mistaken; but where, as in this case, though the conduct is mistaken, the feelings are not, it may not be very material.—Mr. Knightley could not impute to Emma a more relenting heart than she possessed, or a heart more disposed to accept of his.\n\nHe had, in fact, been wholly unsuspicious of his own influence. He had followed her into the shrubbery with no idea of trying it. He had come, in his anxiety to see how she bore Frank Churchill’s engagement, with no selfish view, no view at all, but of endeavouring, if she allowed him an opening, to soothe or to counsel her.—The rest had been the work of the moment, the immediate effect of what he heard, on his feelings. The delightful assurance of her total indifference towards Frank Churchill, of her having a heart completely disengaged from him, had given birth to the hope, that, in time, he might gain her affection himself;—but it had been no present hope—he had only, in the momentary conquest of eagerness over judgment, aspired to be told that she did not forbid his attempt to attach her.—The superior hopes which gradually opened were so much the more enchanting.—The affection, which he had been asking to be allowed to create, if he could, was already his!—Within half an hour, he had passed from a thoroughly distressed state of mind, to something so like perfect happiness, that it could bear no other name.\n\nHer change was equal.—This one half-hour had given to each the same precious certainty of being beloved, had cleared from each the same degree of ignorance, jealousy, or distrust.—On his side, there had been a long-standing jealousy, old as the arrival, or even the expectation, of Frank Churchill.—He had been in love with Emma, and jealous of Frank Churchill, from about the same period, one sentiment having probably enlightened him as to the other. It was his jealousy of Frank Churchill that had taken him from the country.—The Box Hill party had decided him on going away. He would save himself from witnessing again such permitted, encouraged attentions.—He had gone to learn to be indifferent.—But he had gone to a wrong place. There was too much domestic happiness in his brother’s house; woman wore too amiable a form in it; Isabella was too much like Emma—differing only in those striking inferiorities, which always brought the other in brilliancy before him, for much to have been done, even had his time been longer.—He had stayed on, however, vigorously, day after day—till this very morning’s post had conveyed the history of Jane Fairfax.—Then, with the gladness which must be felt, nay, which he did not scruple to feel, having never believed Frank Churchill to be at all deserving Emma, was there so much fond solicitude, so much keen anxiety for her, that he could stay no longer. He had ridden home through the rain; and had walked up directly after dinner, to see how this sweetest and best of all creatures, faultless in spite of all her faults, bore the discovery.\n\nHe had found her agitated and low.—Frank Churchill was a villain.— He heard her declare that she had never loved him. Frank Churchill’s character was not desperate.—She was his own Emma, by hand and word, when they returned into the house; and if he could have thought of Frank Churchill then, he might have deemed him a very good sort of fellow.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter XIV\n\nWhat totally different feelings did Emma take back into the house from what she had brought out!—she had then been only daring to hope for a little respite of suffering;—she was now in an exquisite flutter of happiness, and such happiness moreover as she believed must still be greater when the flutter should have passed away.\n\nThey sat down to tea—the same party round the same table—how often it had been collected!—and how often had her eyes fallen on the same shrubs in the lawn, and observed the same beautiful effect of the western sun!—But never in such a state of spirits, never in any thing like it; and it was with difficulty that she could summon enough of her usual self to be the attentive lady of the house, or even the attentive daughter.\n\nPoor Mr. Woodhouse little suspected what was plotting against him in the breast of that man whom he was so cordially welcoming, and so anxiously hoping might not have taken cold from his ride.—Could he have seen the heart, he would have cared very little for the lungs; but without the most distant imagination of the impending evil, without the slightest perception of any thing extraordinary in the looks or ways of either, he repeated to them very comfortably all the articles of news he had received from Mr. Perry, and talked on with much self-contentment, totally unsuspicious of what they could have told him in return.\n\nAs long as Mr. Knightley remained with them, Emma’s fever continued; but when he was gone, she began to be a little tranquillised and subdued—and in the course of the sleepless night, which was the tax for such an evening, she found one or two such very serious points to consider, as made her feel, that even her happiness must have some alloy. Her father—and Harriet. She could not be alone without feeling the full weight of their separate claims; and how to guard the comfort of both to the utmost, was the question. With respect to her father, it was a question soon answered. She hardly knew yet what Mr. Knightley would ask; but a very short parley with her own heart produced the most solemn resolution of never quitting her father.—She even wept over the idea of it, as a sin of thought. While he lived, it must be only an engagement; but she flattered herself, that if divested of the danger of drawing her away, it might become an increase of comfort to him.—How to do her best by Harriet, was of more difficult decision;—how to spare her from any unnecessary pain; how to make her any possible atonement; how to appear least her enemy?—On these subjects, her perplexity and distress were very great—and her mind had to pass again and again through every bitter reproach and sorrowful regret that had ever surrounded it.—She could only resolve at last, that she would still avoid a meeting with her, and communicate all that need be told by letter; that it would be inexpressibly desirable to have her removed just now for a time from Highbury, and—indulging in one scheme more—nearly resolve, that it might be practicable to get an invitation for her to Brunswick Square.—Isabella had been pleased with Harriet; and a few weeks spent in London must give her some amusement.—She did not think it in Harriet’s nature to escape being benefited by novelty and variety, by the streets, the shops, and the children.—At any rate, it would be a proof of attention and kindness in herself, from whom every thing was due; a separation for the present; an averting of the evil day, when they must all be together again.\n\nShe rose early, and wrote her letter to Harriet; an employment which left her so very serious, so nearly sad, that Mr. Knightley, in walking up to Hartfield to breakfast, did not arrive at all too soon; and half an hour stolen afterwards to go over the same ground again with him, literally and figuratively, was quite necessary to reinstate her in a proper share of the happiness of the evening before.\n\nHe had not left her long, by no means long enough for her to have the slightest inclination for thinking of any body else, when a letter was brought her from Randalls—a very thick letter;—she guessed what it must contain, and deprecated the necessity of reading it.—She was now in perfect charity with Frank Churchill; she wanted no explanations, she wanted only to have her thoughts to herself—and as for understanding any thing he wrote, she was sure she was incapable of it.—It must be waded through, however. She opened the packet; it was too surely so;—a note from Mrs. Weston to herself, ushered in the letter from Frank to Mrs. Weston.\n\n“I have the greatest pleasure, my dear Emma, in forwarding to you the enclosed. I know what thorough justice you will do it, and have scarcely a doubt of its happy effect.—I think we shall never materially disagree about the writer again; but I will not delay you by a long preface.—We are quite well.—This letter has been the cure of all the little nervousness I have been feeling lately.—I did not quite like your looks on Tuesday, but it was an ungenial morning; and though you will never own being affected by weather, I think every body feels a north-east wind.—I felt for your dear father very much in the storm of Tuesday afternoon and yesterday morning, but had the comfort of hearing last night, by Mr. Perry, that it had not made him ill.\n\n“Yours ever, “A. W.”\n\n[To Mrs. Weston.]\n\nWindsor—July.\n\nMY DEAR MADAM,\n\n“If I made myself intelligible yesterday, this letter will be expected; but expected or not, I know it will be read with candour and indulgence.—You are all goodness, and I believe there will be need of even all your goodness to allow for some parts of my past conduct.—But I have been forgiven by one who had still more to resent. My courage rises while I write. It is very difficult for the prosperous to be humble. I have already met with such success in two applications for pardon, that I may be in danger of thinking myself too sure of yours, and of those among your friends who have had any ground of offence.—You must all endeavour to comprehend the exact nature of my situation when I first arrived at Randalls; you must consider me as having a secret which was to be kept at all hazards. This was the fact. My right to place myself in a situation requiring such concealment, is another question. I shall not discuss it here. For my temptation to think it a right, I refer every caviller to a brick house, sashed windows below, and casements above, in Highbury. I dared not address her openly; my difficulties in the then state of Enscombe must be too well known to require definition; and I was fortunate enough to prevail, before we parted at Weymouth, and to induce the most upright female mind in the creation to stoop in charity to a secret engagement.—Had she refused, I should have gone mad.—But you will be ready to say, what was your hope in doing this?—What did you look forward to?—To any thing, every thing—to time, chance, circumstance, slow effects, sudden bursts, perseverance and weariness, health and sickness. Every possibility of good was before me, and the first of blessings secured, in obtaining her promises of faith and correspondence. If you need farther explanation, I have the honour, my dear madam, of being your husband’s son, and the advantage of inheriting a disposition to hope for good, which no inheritance of houses or lands can ever equal the value of.—See me, then, under these circumstances, arriving on my first visit to Randalls;—and here I am conscious of wrong, for that visit might have been sooner paid. You will look back and see that I did not come till Miss Fairfax was in Highbury; and as you were the person slighted, you will forgive me instantly; but I must work on my father’s compassion, by reminding him, that so long as I absented myself from his house, so long I lost the blessing of knowing you. My behaviour, during the very happy fortnight which I spent with you, did not, I hope, lay me open to reprehension, excepting on one point. And now I come to the principal, the only important part of my conduct while belonging to you, which excites my own anxiety, or requires very solicitous explanation. With the greatest respect, and the warmest friendship, do I mention Miss Woodhouse; my father perhaps will think I ought to add, with the deepest humiliation.—A few words which dropped from him yesterday spoke his opinion, and some censure I acknowledge myself liable to.—My behaviour to Miss Woodhouse indicated, I believe, more than it ought.—In order to assist a concealment so essential to me, I was led on to make more than an allowable use of the sort of intimacy into which we were immediately thrown.—I cannot deny that Miss Woodhouse was my ostensible object—but I am sure you will believe the declaration, that had I not been convinced of her indifference, I would not have been induced by any selfish views to go on.—Amiable and delightful as Miss Woodhouse is, she never gave me the idea of a young woman likely to be attached; and that she was perfectly free from any tendency to being attached to me, was as much my conviction as my wish.—She received my attentions with an easy, friendly, goodhumoured playfulness, which exactly suited me. We seemed to understand each other. From our relative situation, those attentions were her due, and were felt to be so.—Whether Miss Woodhouse began really to understand me before the expiration of that fortnight, I cannot say;—when I called to take leave of her, I remember that I was within a moment of confessing the truth, and I then fancied she was not without suspicion; but I have no doubt of her having since detected me, at least in some degree.—She may not have surmised the whole, but her quickness must have penetrated a part. I cannot doubt it. You will find, whenever the subject becomes freed from its present restraints, that it did not take her wholly by surprize. She frequently gave me hints of it. I remember her telling me at the ball, that I owed Mrs. Elton gratitude for her attentions to Miss Fairfax.—I hope this history of my conduct towards her will be admitted by you and my father as great extenuation of what you saw amiss. While you considered me as having sinned against Emma Woodhouse, I could deserve nothing from either. Acquit me here, and procure for me, when it is allowable, the acquittal and good wishes of that said Emma Woodhouse, whom I regard with so much brotherly affection, as to long to have her as deeply and as happily in love as myself.—Whatever strange things I said or did during that fortnight, you have now a key to. My heart was in Highbury, and my business was to get my body thither as often as might be, and with the least suspicion. If you remember any queernesses, set them all to the right account.—Of the pianoforte so much talked of, I feel it only necessary to say, that its being ordered was absolutely unknown to Miss F—, who would never have allowed me to send it, had any choice been given her.—The delicacy of her mind throughout the whole engagement, my dear madam, is much beyond my power of doing justice to. You will soon, I earnestly hope, know her thoroughly yourself.—No description can describe her. She must tell you herself what she is—yet not by word, for never was there a human creature who would so designedly suppress her own merit.—Since I began this letter, which will be longer than I foresaw, I have heard from her.—She gives a good account of her own health; but as she never complains, I dare not depend. I want to have your opinion of her looks. I know you will soon call on her; she is living in dread of the visit. Perhaps it is paid already. Let me hear from you without delay; I am impatient for a thousand particulars. Remember how few minutes I was at Randalls, and in how bewildered, how mad a state: and I am not much better yet; still insane either from happiness or misery. When I think of the kindness and favour I have met with, of her excellence and patience, and my uncle’s generosity, I am mad with joy: but when I recollect all the uneasiness I occasioned her, and how little I deserve to be forgiven, I am mad with anger. If I could but see her again!—But I must not propose it yet. My uncle has been too good for me to encroach.—I must still add to this long letter. You have not heard all that you ought to hear. I could not give any connected detail yesterday; but the suddenness, and, in one light, the unseasonableness with which the affair burst out, needs explanation; for though the event of the 26th ult., as you will conclude, immediately opened to me the happiest prospects, I should not have presumed on such early measures, but from the very particular circumstances, which left me not an hour to lose. I should myself have shrunk from any thing so hasty, and she would have felt every scruple of mine with multiplied strength and refinement.—But I had no choice. The hasty engagement she had entered into with that woman—Here, my dear madam, I was obliged to leave off abruptly, to recollect and compose myself.—I have been walking over the country, and am now, I hope, rational enough to make the rest of my letter what it ought to be.—It is, in fact, a most mortifying retrospect for me. I behaved shamefully. And here I can admit, that my manners to Miss W., in being unpleasant to Miss F., were highly blameable. She disapproved them, which ought to have been enough.—My plea of concealing the truth she did not think sufficient.—She was displeased; I thought unreasonably so: I thought her, on a thousand occasions, unnecessarily scrupulous and cautious: I thought her even cold. But she was always right. If I had followed her judgment, and subdued my spirits to the level of what she deemed proper, I should have escaped the greatest unhappiness I have ever known.—We quarrelled.— Do you remember the morning spent at Donwell?—There every little dissatisfaction that had occurred before came to a crisis. I was late; I met her walking home by herself, and wanted to walk with her, but she would not suffer it. She absolutely refused to allow me, which I then thought most unreasonable. Now, however, I see nothing in it but a very natural and consistent degree of discretion. While I, to blind the world to our engagement, was behaving one hour with objectionable particularity to another woman, was she to be consenting the next to a proposal which might have made every previous caution useless?—Had we been met walking together between Donwell and Highbury, the truth must have been suspected.—I was mad enough, however, to resent.—I doubted her affection. I doubted it more the next day on Box Hill; when, provoked by such conduct on my side, such shameful, insolent neglect of her, and such apparent devotion to Miss W., as it would have been impossible for any woman of sense to endure, she spoke her resentment in a form of words perfectly intelligible to me.—In short, my dear madam, it was a quarrel blameless on her side, abominable on mine; and I returned the same evening to Richmond, though I might have staid with you till the next morning, merely because I would be as angry with her as possible. Even then, I was not such a fool as not to mean to be reconciled in time; but I was the injured person, injured by her coldness, and I went away determined that she should make the first advances.—I shall always congratulate myself that you were not of the Box Hill party. Had you witnessed my behaviour there, I can hardly suppose you would ever have thought well of me again. Its effect upon her appears in the immediate resolution it produced: as soon as she found I was really gone from Randalls, she closed with the offer of that officious Mrs. Elton; the whole system of whose treatment of her, by the bye, has ever filled me with indignation and hatred. I must not quarrel with a spirit of forbearance which has been so richly extended towards myself; but, otherwise, I should loudly protest against the share of it which that woman has known.—‘Jane,’ indeed!—You will observe that I have not yet indulged myself in calling her by that name, even to you. Think, then, what I must have endured in hearing it bandied between the Eltons with all the vulgarity of needless repetition, and all the insolence of imaginary superiority. Have patience with me, I shall soon have done.—She closed with this offer, resolving to break with me entirely, and wrote the next day to tell me that we never were to meet again.—She felt the engagement to be a source of repentance and misery to each: she dissolved it.—This letter reached me on the very morning of my poor aunt’s death. I answered it within an hour; but from the confusion of my mind, and the multiplicity of business falling on me at once, my answer, instead of being sent with all the many other letters of that day, was locked up in my writing-desk; and I, trusting that I had written enough, though but a few lines, to satisfy her, remained without any uneasiness.—I was rather disappointed that I did not hear from her again speedily; but I made excuses for her, and was too busy, and—may I add?—too cheerful in my views to be captious.—We removed to Windsor; and two days afterwards I received a parcel from her, my own letters all returned!—and a few lines at the same time by the post, stating her extreme surprize at not having had the smallest reply to her last; and adding, that as silence on such a point could not be misconstrued, and as it must be equally desirable to both to have every subordinate arrangement concluded as soon as possible, she now sent me, by a safe conveyance, all my letters, and requested, that if I could not directly command hers, so as to send them to Highbury within a week, I would forward them after that period to her at—: in short, the full direction to Mr. Smallridge’s, near Bristol, stared me in the face. I knew the name, the place, I knew all about it, and instantly saw what she had been doing. It was perfectly accordant with that resolution of character which I knew her to possess; and the secrecy she had maintained, as to any such design in her former letter, was equally descriptive of its anxious delicacy. For the world would not she have seemed to threaten me.—Imagine the shock; imagine how, till I had actually detected my own blunder, I raved at the blunders of the post.—What was to be done?—One thing only.—I must speak to my uncle. Without his sanction I could not hope to be listened to again.—I spoke; circumstances were in my favour; the late event had softened away his pride, and he was, earlier than I could have anticipated, wholly reconciled and complying; and could say at last, poor man! with a deep sigh, that he wished I might find as much happiness in the marriage state as he had done.—I felt that it would be of a different sort.—Are you disposed to pity me for what I must have suffered in opening the cause to him, for my suspense while all was at stake?—No; do not pity me till I reached Highbury, and saw how ill I had made her. Do not pity me till I saw her wan, sick looks.—I reached Highbury at the time of day when, from my knowledge of their late breakfast hour, I was certain of a good chance of finding her alone.—I was not disappointed; and at last I was not disappointed either in the object of my journey. A great deal of very reasonable, very just displeasure I had to persuade away. But it is done; we are reconciled, dearer, much dearer, than ever, and no moment’s uneasiness can ever occur between us again. Now, my dear madam, I will release you; but I could not conclude before. A thousand and a thousand thanks for all the kindness you have ever shewn me, and ten thousand for the attentions your heart will dictate towards her.—If you think me in a way to be happier than I deserve, I am quite of your opinion.—Miss W. calls me the child of good fortune. I hope she is right.—In one respect, my good fortune is undoubted, that of being able to subscribe myself,\n\nYour obliged and affectionate Son,\n\nF. C. WESTON CHURCHILL.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter XV\n\nThis letter must make its way to Emma’s feelings. She was obliged, in spite of her previous determination to the contrary, to do it all the justice that Mrs. Weston foretold. As soon as she came to her own name, it was irresistible; every line relating to herself was interesting, and almost every line agreeable; and when this charm ceased, the subject could still maintain itself, by the natural return of her former regard for the writer, and the very strong attraction which any picture of love must have for her at that moment. She never stopt till she had gone through the whole; and though it was impossible not to feel that he had been wrong, yet he had been less wrong than she had supposed—and he had suffered, and was very sorry—and he was so grateful to Mrs. Weston, and so much in love with Miss Fairfax, and she was so happy herself, that there was no being severe; and could he have entered the room, she must have shaken hands with him as heartily as ever.\n\nShe thought so well of the letter, that when Mr. Knightley came again, she desired him to read it. She was sure of Mrs. Weston’s wishing it to be communicated; especially to one, who, like Mr. Knightley, had seen so much to blame in his conduct.\n\n“I shall be very glad to look it over,” said he; “but it seems long. I will take it home with me at night.”\n\nBut that would not do. Mr. Weston was to call in the evening, and she must return it by him.\n\n“I would rather be talking to you,” he replied; “but as it seems a matter of justice, it shall be done.”\n\nHe began—stopping, however, almost directly to say, “Had I been offered the sight of one of this gentleman’s letters to his mother-in-law a few months ago, Emma, it would not have been taken with such indifference.”\n\nHe proceeded a little farther, reading to himself; and then, with a smile, observed, “Humph! a fine complimentary opening: But it is his way. One man’s style must not be the rule of another’s. We will not be severe.”\n\n“It will be natural for me,” he added shortly afterwards, “to speak my opinion aloud as I read. By doing it, I shall feel that I am near you. It will not be so great a loss of time: but if you dislike it—”\n\n“Not at all. I should wish it.”\n\nMr. Knightley returned to his reading with greater alacrity.\n\n“He trifles here,” said he, “as to the temptation. He knows he is wrong, and has nothing rational to urge.—Bad.—He ought not to have formed the engagement.—‘His father’s disposition:’—he is unjust, however, to his father. Mr. Weston’s sanguine temper was a blessing on all his upright and honourable exertions; but Mr. Weston earned every present comfort before he endeavoured to gain it.—Very true; he did not come till Miss Fairfax was here.”\n\n“And I have not forgotten,” said Emma, “how sure you were that he might have come sooner if he would. You pass it over very handsomely—but you were perfectly right.”\n\n“I was not quite impartial in my judgment, Emma:—but yet, I think—had you not been in the case—I should still have distrusted him.”\n\nWhen he came to Miss Woodhouse, he was obliged to read the whole of it aloud—all that related to her, with a smile; a look; a shake of the head; a word or two of assent, or disapprobation; or merely of love, as the subject required; concluding, however, seriously, and, after steady reflection, thus—\n\n“Very bad—though it might have been worse.—Playing a most dangerous game. Too much indebted to the event for his acquittal.—No judge of his own manners by you.—Always deceived in fact by his own wishes, and regardless of little besides his own convenience.—Fancying you to have fathomed his secret. Natural enough!—his own mind full of intrigue, that he should suspect it in others.—Mystery; Finesse—how they pervert the understanding! My Emma, does not every thing serve to prove more and more the beauty of truth and sincerity in all our dealings with each other?”\n\nEmma agreed to it, and with a blush of sensibility on Harriet’s account, which she could not give any sincere explanation of.\n\n“You had better go on,” said she.\n\nHe did so, but very soon stopt again to say, “the pianoforte! Ah! That was the act of a very, very young man, one too young to consider whether the inconvenience of it might not very much exceed the pleasure. A boyish scheme, indeed!—I cannot comprehend a man’s wishing to give a woman any proof of affection which he knows she would rather dispense with; and he did know that she would have prevented the instrument’s coming if she could.”\n\nAfter this, he made some progress without any pause. Frank Churchill’s confession of having behaved shamefully was the first thing to call for more than a word in passing.\n\n“I perfectly agree with you, sir,”—was then his remark. “You did behave very shamefully. You never wrote a truer line.” And having gone through what immediately followed of the basis of their disagreement, and his persisting to act in direct opposition to Jane Fairfax’s sense of right, he made a fuller pause to say, “This is very bad.—He had induced her to place herself, for his sake, in a situation of extreme difficulty and uneasiness, and it should have been his first object to prevent her from suffering unnecessarily.—She must have had much more to contend with, in carrying on the correspondence, than he could. He should have respected even unreasonable scruples, had there been such; but hers were all reasonable. We must look to her one fault, and remember that she had done a wrong thing in consenting to the engagement, to bear that she should have been in such a state of punishment.”\n\nEmma knew that he was now getting to the Box Hill party, and grew uncomfortable. Her own behaviour had been so very improper! She was deeply ashamed, and a little afraid of his next look. It was all read, however, steadily, attentively, and without the smallest remark; and, excepting one momentary glance at her, instantly withdrawn, in the fear of giving pain—no remembrance of Box Hill seemed to exist.\n\n“There is no saying much for the delicacy of our good friends, the Eltons,” was his next observation.—“His feelings are natural.—What! actually resolve to break with him entirely!—She felt the engagement to be a source of repentance and misery to each—she dissolved it.—What a view this gives of her sense of his behaviour!—Well, he must be a most extraordinary—”\n\n“Nay, nay, read on.—You will find how very much he suffers.”\n\n“I hope he does,” replied Mr. Knightley coolly, and resuming the letter. “‘Smallridge!’—What does this mean? What is all this?”\n\n“She had engaged to go as governess to Mrs. Smallridge’s children—a dear friend of Mrs. Elton’s—a neighbour of Maple Grove; and, by the bye, I wonder how Mrs. Elton bears the disappointment?”\n\n“Say nothing, my dear Emma, while you oblige me to read—not even of Mrs. Elton. Only one page more. I shall soon have done. What a letter the man writes!”\n\n“I wish you would read it with a kinder spirit towards him.”\n\n“Well, there is feeling here.—He does seem to have suffered in finding her ill.—Certainly, I can have no doubt of his being fond of her. ‘Dearer, much dearer than ever.’ I hope he may long continue to feel all the value of such a reconciliation.—He is a very liberal thanker, with his thousands and tens of thousands.—‘Happier than I deserve.’ Come, he knows himself there. ‘Miss Woodhouse calls me the child of good fortune.’—Those were Miss Woodhouse’s words, were they?— And a fine ending—and there is the letter. The child of good fortune! That was your name for him, was it?”\n\n“You do not appear so well satisfied with his letter as I am; but still you must, at least I hope you must, think the better of him for it. I hope it does him some service with you.”\n\n“Yes, certainly it does. He has had great faults, faults of inconsideration and thoughtlessness; and I am very much of his opinion in thinking him likely to be happier than he deserves: but still as he is, beyond a doubt, really attached to Miss Fairfax, and will soon, it may be hoped, have the advantage of being constantly with her, I am very ready to believe his character will improve, and acquire from hers the steadiness and delicacy of principle that it wants. And now, let me talk to you of something else. I have another person’s interest at present so much at heart, that I cannot think any longer about Frank Churchill. Ever since I left you this morning, Emma, my mind has been hard at work on one subject.”\n\nThe subject followed; it was in plain, unaffected, gentlemanlike English, such as Mr. Knightley used even to the woman he was in love with, how to be able to ask her to marry him, without attacking the happiness of her father. Emma’s answer was ready at the first word. “While her dear father lived, any change of condition must be impossible for her. She could never quit him.” Part only of this answer, however, was admitted. The impossibility of her quitting her father, Mr. Knightley felt as strongly as herself; but the inadmissibility of any other change, he could not agree to. He had been thinking it over most deeply, most intently; he had at first hoped to induce Mr. Woodhouse to remove with her to Donwell; he had wanted to believe it feasible, but his knowledge of Mr. Woodhouse would not suffer him to deceive himself long; and now he confessed his persuasion, that such a transplantation would be a risk of her father’s comfort, perhaps even of his life, which must not be hazarded. Mr. Woodhouse taken from Hartfield!—No, he felt that it ought not to be attempted. But the plan which had arisen on the sacrifice of this, he trusted his dearest Emma would not find in any respect objectionable; it was, that he should be received at Hartfield; that so long as her father’s happiness—in other words, his life—required Hartfield to continue her home, it should be his likewise.\n\nOf their all removing to Donwell, Emma had already had her own passing thoughts. Like him, she had tried the scheme and rejected it; but such an alternative as this had not occurred to her. She was sensible of all the affection it evinced. She felt that, in quitting Donwell, he must be sacrificing a great deal of independence of hours and habits; that in living constantly with her father, and in no house of his own, there would be much, very much, to be borne with. She promised to think of it, and advised him to think of it more; but he was fully convinced, that no reflection could alter his wishes or his opinion on the subject. He had given it, he could assure her, very long and calm consideration; he had been walking away from William Larkins the whole morning, to have his thoughts to himself.\n\n“Ah! there is one difficulty unprovided for,” cried Emma. “I am sure William Larkins will not like it. You must get his consent before you ask mine.”\n\nShe promised, however, to think of it; and pretty nearly promised, moreover, to think of it, with the intention of finding it a very good scheme.\n\nIt is remarkable, that Emma, in the many, very many, points of view in which she was now beginning to consider Donwell Abbey, was never struck with any sense of injury to her nephew Henry, whose rights as heir-expectant had formerly been so tenaciously regarded. Think she must of the possible difference to the poor little boy; and yet she only gave herself a saucy conscious smile about it, and found amusement in detecting the real cause of that violent dislike of Mr. Knightley’s marrying Jane Fairfax, or any body else, which at the time she had wholly imputed to the amiable solicitude of the sister and the aunt.\n\nThis proposal of his, this plan of marrying and continuing at Hartfield—the more she contemplated it, the more pleasing it became. His evils seemed to lessen, her own advantages to increase, their mutual good to outweigh every drawback. Such a companion for herself in the periods of anxiety and cheerlessness before her!—Such a partner in all those duties and cares to which time must be giving increase of melancholy!\n\nShe would have been too happy but for poor Harriet; but every blessing of her own seemed to involve and advance the sufferings of her friend, who must now be even excluded from Hartfield. The delightful family party which Emma was securing for herself, poor Harriet must, in mere charitable caution, be kept at a distance from. She would be a loser in every way. Emma could not deplore her future absence as any deduction from her own enjoyment. In such a party, Harriet would be rather a dead weight than otherwise; but for the poor girl herself, it seemed a peculiarly cruel necessity that was to be placing her in such a state of unmerited punishment.\n\nIn time, of course, Mr. Knightley would be forgotten, that is, supplanted; but this could not be expected to happen very early. Mr. Knightley himself would be doing nothing to assist the cure;—not like Mr. Elton. Mr. Knightley, always so kind, so feeling, so truly considerate for every body, would never deserve to be less worshipped than now; and it really was too much to hope even of Harriet, that she could be in love with more than three men in one year.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter XVI\n\nIt was a very great relief to Emma to find Harriet as desirous as herself to avoid a meeting. Their intercourse was painful enough by letter. How much worse, had they been obliged to meet!\n\nHarriet expressed herself very much as might be supposed, without reproaches, or apparent sense of ill-usage; and yet Emma fancied there was a something of resentment, a something bordering on it in her style, which increased the desirableness of their being separate.—It might be only her own consciousness; but it seemed as if an angel only could have been quite without resentment under such a stroke.\n\nShe had no difficulty in procuring Isabella’s invitation; and she was fortunate in having a sufficient reason for asking it, without resorting to invention.—There was a tooth amiss. Harriet really wished, and had wished some time, to consult a dentist. Mrs. John Knightley was delighted to be of use; any thing of ill health was a recommendation to her—and though not so fond of a dentist as of a Mr. Wingfield, she was quite eager to have Harriet under her care.—When it was thus settled on her sister’s side, Emma proposed it to her friend, and found her very persuadable.—Harriet was to go; she was invited for at least a fortnight; she was to be conveyed in Mr. Woodhouse’s carriage.—It was all arranged, it was all completed, and Harriet was safe in Brunswick Square.\n\nNow Emma could, indeed, enjoy Mr. Knightley’s visits; now she could talk, and she could listen with true happiness, unchecked by that sense of injustice, of guilt, of something most painful, which had haunted her when remembering how disappointed a heart was near her, how much might at that moment, and at a little distance, be enduring by the feelings which she had led astray herself.\n\nThe difference of Harriet at Mrs. Goddard’s, or in London, made perhaps an unreasonable difference in Emma’s sensations; but she could not think of her in London without objects of curiosity and employment, which must be averting the past, and carrying her out of herself.\n\nShe would not allow any other anxiety to succeed directly to the place in her mind which Harriet had occupied. There was a communication before her, one which she only could be competent to make—the confession of her engagement to her father; but she would have nothing to do with it at present.—She had resolved to defer the disclosure till Mrs. Weston were safe and well. No additional agitation should be thrown at this period among those she loved—and the evil should not act on herself by anticipation before the appointed time.—A fortnight, at least, of leisure and peace of mind, to crown every warmer, but more agitating, delight, should be hers.\n\nShe soon resolved, equally as a duty and a pleasure, to employ half an hour of this holiday of spirits in calling on Miss Fairfax.—She ought to go—and she was longing to see her; the resemblance of their present situations increasing every other motive of goodwill. It would be a secret satisfaction; but the consciousness of a similarity of prospect would certainly add to the interest with which she should attend to any thing Jane might communicate.\n\nShe went—she had driven once unsuccessfully to the door, but had not been into the house since the morning after Box Hill, when poor Jane had been in such distress as had filled her with compassion, though all the worst of her sufferings had been unsuspected.—The fear of being still unwelcome, determined her, though assured of their being at home, to wait in the passage, and send up her name.—She heard Patty announcing it; but no such bustle succeeded as poor Miss Bates had before made so happily intelligible.—No; she heard nothing but the instant reply of, “Beg her to walk up;”—and a moment afterwards she was met on the stairs by Jane herself, coming eagerly forward, as if no other reception of her were felt sufficient.—Emma had never seen her look so well, so lovely, so engaging. There was consciousness, animation, and warmth; there was every thing which her countenance or manner could ever have wanted.— She came forward with an offered hand; and said, in a low, but very feeling tone,\n\n“This is most kind, indeed!—Miss Woodhouse, it is impossible for me to express—I hope you will believe—Excuse me for being so entirely without words.”\n\nEmma was gratified, and would soon have shewn no want of words, if the sound of Mrs. Elton’s voice from the sitting-room had not checked her, and made it expedient to compress all her friendly and all her congratulatory sensations into a very, very earnest shake of the hand.\n\nMrs. Bates and Mrs. Elton were together. Miss Bates was out, which accounted for the previous tranquillity. Emma could have wished Mrs. Elton elsewhere; but she was in a humour to have patience with every body; and as Mrs. Elton met her with unusual graciousness, she hoped the rencontre would do them no harm.\n\nShe soon believed herself to penetrate Mrs. Elton’s thoughts, and understand why she was, like herself, in happy spirits; it was being in Miss Fairfax’s confidence, and fancying herself acquainted with what was still a secret to other people. Emma saw symptoms of it immediately in the expression of her face; and while paying her own compliments to Mrs. Bates, and appearing to attend to the good old lady’s replies, she saw her with a sort of anxious parade of mystery fold up a letter which she had apparently been reading aloud to Miss Fairfax, and return it into the purple and gold reticule by her side, saying, with significant nods,\n\n“We can finish this some other time, you know. You and I shall not want opportunities. And, in fact, you have heard all the essential already. I only wanted to prove to you that Mrs. S. admits our apology, and is not offended. You see how delightfully she writes. Oh! she is a sweet creature! You would have doated on her, had you gone.—But not a word more. Let us be discreet—quite on our good behaviour.—Hush!—You remember those lines—I forget the poem at this moment:\n\n“For when a lady’s in the case, “You know all other things give place.”\n\nNow I say, my dear, in our case, for lady, read——mum! a word to the wise.—I am in a fine flow of spirits, an’t I? But I want to set your heart at ease as to Mrs. S.—My representation, you see, has quite appeased her.”\n\nAnd again, on Emma’s merely turning her head to look at Mrs. Bates’s knitting, she added, in a half whisper,\n\n“I mentioned no names, you will observe.—Oh! no; cautious as a minister of state. I managed it extremely well.”\n\nEmma could not doubt. It was a palpable display, repeated on every possible occasion. When they had all talked a little while in harmony of the weather and Mrs. Weston, she found herself abruptly addressed with,\n\n“Do not you think, Miss Woodhouse, our saucy little friend here is charmingly recovered?—Do not you think her cure does Perry the highest credit?—(here was a side-glance of great meaning at Jane.) Upon my word, Perry has restored her in a wonderful short time!—Oh! if you had seen her, as I did, when she was at the worst!”—And when Mrs. Bates was saying something to Emma, whispered farther, “We do not say a word of any assistance that Perry might have; not a word of a certain young physician from Windsor.—Oh! no; Perry shall have all the credit.”\n\n“I have scarce had the pleasure of seeing you, Miss Woodhouse,” she shortly afterwards began, “since the party to Box Hill. Very pleasant party. But yet I think there was something wanting. Things did not seem—that is, there seemed a little cloud upon the spirits of some.—So it appeared to me at least, but I might be mistaken. However, I think it answered so far as to tempt one to go again. What say you both to our collecting the same party, and exploring to Box Hill again, while the fine weather lasts?—It must be the same party, you know, quite the same party, not one exception.”\n\nSoon after this Miss Bates came in, and Emma could not help being diverted by the perplexity of her first answer to herself, resulting, she supposed, from doubt of what might be said, and impatience to say every thing.\n\n“Thank you, dear Miss Woodhouse, you are all kindness.—It is impossible to say—Yes, indeed, I quite understand—dearest Jane’s prospects—that is, I do not mean.—But she is charmingly recovered.—How is Mr. Woodhouse?—I am so glad.—Quite out of my power.—Such a happy little circle as you find us here.—Yes, indeed.—Charming young man!—that is—so very friendly; I mean good Mr. Perry!—such attention to Jane!”—And from her great, her more than commonly thankful delight towards Mrs. Elton for being there, Emma guessed that there had been a little show of resentment towards Jane, from the vicarage quarter, which was now graciously overcome.—After a few whispers, indeed, which placed it beyond a guess, Mrs. Elton, speaking louder, said,\n\n“Yes, here I am, my good friend; and here I have been so long, that anywhere else I should think it necessary to apologise; but, the truth is, that I am waiting for my lord and master. He promised to join me here, and pay his respects to you.”\n\n“What! are we to have the pleasure of a call from Mr. Elton?—That will be a favour indeed! for I know gentlemen do not like morning visits, and Mr. Elton’s time is so engaged.”\n\n“Upon my word it is, Miss Bates.—He really is engaged from morning to night.—There is no end of people’s coming to him, on some pretence or other.—The magistrates, and overseers, and churchwardens, are always wanting his opinion. They seem not able to do any thing without him.—‘Upon my word, Mr. E.,’ I often say, ‘rather you than I.—I do not know what would become of my crayons and my instrument, if I had half so many applicants.’—Bad enough as it is, for I absolutely neglect them both to an unpardonable degree.—I believe I have not played a bar this fortnight.—However, he is coming, I assure you: yes, indeed, on purpose to wait on you all.” And putting up her hand to screen her words from Emma—“A congratulatory visit, you know.—Oh! yes, quite indispensable.”\n\nMiss Bates looked about her, so happily—!\n\n“He promised to come to me as soon as he could disengage himself from Knightley; but he and Knightley are shut up together in deep consultation.—Mr. E. is Knightley’s right hand.”\n\nEmma would not have smiled for the world, and only said, “Is Mr. Elton gone on foot to Donwell?—He will have a hot walk.”\n\n“Oh! no, it is a meeting at the Crown, a regular meeting. Weston and Cole will be there too; but one is apt to speak only of those who lead.—I fancy Mr. E. and Knightley have every thing their own way.”\n\n“Have not you mistaken the day?” said Emma. “I am almost certain that the meeting at the Crown is not till to-morrow.—Mr. Knightley was at Hartfield yesterday, and spoke of it as for Saturday.”\n\n“Oh! no, the meeting is certainly to-day,” was the abrupt answer, which denoted the impossibility of any blunder on Mrs. Elton’s side.—“I do believe,” she continued, “this is the most troublesome parish that ever was. We never heard of such things at Maple Grove.”\n\n“Your parish there was small,” said Jane.\n\n“Upon my word, my dear, I do not know, for I never heard the subject talked of.”\n\n“But it is proved by the smallness of the school, which I have heard you speak of, as under the patronage of your sister and Mrs. Bragge; the only school, and not more than five-and-twenty children.”\n\n“Ah! you clever creature, that’s very true. What a thinking brain you have! I say, Jane, what a perfect character you and I should make, if we could be shaken together. My liveliness and your solidity would produce perfection.—Not that I presume to insinuate, however, that some people may not think you perfection already.—But hush!—not a word, if you please.”\n\nIt seemed an unnecessary caution; Jane was wanting to give her words, not to Mrs. Elton, but to Miss Woodhouse, as the latter plainly saw. The wish of distinguishing her, as far as civility permitted, was very evident, though it could not often proceed beyond a look.\n\nMr. Elton made his appearance. His lady greeted him with some of her sparkling vivacity.\n\n“Very pretty, sir, upon my word; to send me on here, to be an encumbrance to my friends, so long before you vouchsafe to come!—But you knew what a dutiful creature you had to deal with. You knew I should not stir till my lord and master appeared.—Here have I been sitting this hour, giving these young ladies a sample of true conjugal obedience—for who can say, you know, how soon it may be wanted?”\n\nMr. Elton was so hot and tired, that all this wit seemed thrown away. His civilities to the other ladies must be paid; but his subsequent object was to lament over himself for the heat he was suffering, and the walk he had had for nothing.\n\n“When I got to Donwell,” said he, “Knightley could not be found. Very odd! very unaccountable! after the note I sent him this morning, and the message he returned, that he should certainly be at home till one.”\n\n“Donwell!” cried his wife.—“My dear Mr. E., you have not been to Donwell!—You mean the Crown; you come from the meeting at the Crown.”\n\n“No, no, that’s to-morrow; and I particularly wanted to see Knightley to-day on that very account.—Such a dreadful broiling morning!—I went over the fields too—(speaking in a tone of great ill-usage,) which made it so much the worse. And then not to find him at home! I assure you I am not at all pleased. And no apology left, no message for me. The housekeeper declared she knew nothing of my being expected.—Very extraordinary!—And nobody knew at all which way he was gone. Perhaps to Hartfield, perhaps to the Abbey Mill, perhaps into his woods.—Miss Woodhouse, this is not like our friend Knightley!—Can you explain it?”\n\nEmma amused herself by protesting that it was very extraordinary, indeed, and that she had not a syllable to say for him.\n\n“I cannot imagine,” said Mrs. Elton, (feeling the indignity as a wife ought to do,) “I cannot imagine how he could do such a thing by you, of all people in the world! The very last person whom one should expect to be forgotten!—My dear Mr. E., he must have left a message for you, I am sure he must.—Not even Knightley could be so very eccentric;—and his servants forgot it. Depend upon it, that was the case: and very likely to happen with the Donwell servants, who are all, I have often observed, extremely awkward and remiss.—I am sure I would not have such a creature as his Harry stand at our sideboard for any consideration. And as for Mrs. Hodges, Wright holds her very cheap indeed.—She promised Wright a receipt, and never sent it.”\n\n“I met William Larkins,” continued Mr. Elton, “as I got near the house, and he told me I should not find his master at home, but I did not believe him.—William seemed rather out of humour. He did not know what was come to his master lately, he said, but he could hardly ever get the speech of him. I have nothing to do with William’s wants, but it really is of very great importance that I should see Knightley to-day; and it becomes a matter, therefore, of very serious inconvenience that I should have had this hot walk to no purpose.”\n\nEmma felt that she could not do better than go home directly. In all probability she was at this very time waited for there; and Mr. Knightley might be preserved from sinking deeper in aggression towards Mr. Elton, if not towards William Larkins.\n\nShe was pleased, on taking leave, to find Miss Fairfax determined to attend her out of the room, to go with her even downstairs; it gave her an opportunity which she immediately made use of, to say,\n\n“It is as well, perhaps, that I have not had the possibility. Had you not been surrounded by other friends, I might have been tempted to introduce a subject, to ask questions, to speak more openly than might have been strictly correct.—I feel that I should certainly have been impertinent.”\n\n“Oh!” cried Jane, with a blush and an hesitation which Emma thought infinitely more becoming to her than all the elegance of all her usual composure—“there would have been no danger. The danger would have been of my wearying you. You could not have gratified me more than by expressing an interest—. Indeed, Miss Woodhouse, (speaking more collectedly,) with the consciousness which I have of misconduct, very great misconduct, it is particularly consoling to me to know that those of my friends, whose good opinion is most worth preserving, are not disgusted to such a degree as to—I have not time for half that I could wish to say. I long to make apologies, excuses, to urge something for myself. I feel it so very due. But, unfortunately—in short, if your compassion does not stand my friend—”\n\n“Oh! you are too scrupulous, indeed you are,” cried Emma warmly, and taking her hand. “You owe me no apologies; and every body to whom you might be supposed to owe them, is so perfectly satisfied, so delighted even—”\n\n“You are very kind, but I know what my manners were to you.—So cold and artificial!—I had always a part to act.—It was a life of deceit!—I know that I must have disgusted you.”\n\n“Pray say no more. I feel that all the apologies should be on my side. Let us forgive each other at once. We must do whatever is to be done quickest, and I think our feelings will lose no time there. I hope you have pleasant accounts from Windsor?”\n\n“Very.”\n\n“And the next news, I suppose, will be, that we are to lose you—just as I begin to know you.”\n\n“Oh! as to all that, of course nothing can be thought of yet. I am here till claimed by Colonel and Mrs. Campbell.”\n\n“Nothing can be actually settled yet, perhaps,” replied Emma, smiling—“but, excuse me, it must be thought of.”\n\nThe smile was returned as Jane answered,\n\n“You are very right; it has been thought of. And I will own to you, (I am sure it will be safe), that so far as our living with Mr. Churchill at Enscombe, it is settled. There must be three months, at least, of deep mourning; but when they are over, I imagine there will be nothing more to wait for.”\n\n“Thank you, thank you.—This is just what I wanted to be assured of.—Oh! if you knew how much I love every thing that is decided and open!—Good-bye, good-bye.”\n\n# Volume III — Chapter XVII\n\nMrs. Weston’s friends were all made happy by her safety; and if the satisfaction of her well-doing could be increased to Emma, it was by knowing her to be the mother of a little girl. She had been decided in wishing for a Miss Weston. She would not acknowledge that it was with any view of making a match for her, hereafter, with either of Isabella’s sons; but she was convinced that a daughter would suit both father and mother best. It would be a great comfort to Mr. Weston, as he grew older—and even Mr. Weston might be growing older ten years hence—to have his fireside enlivened by the sports and the nonsense, the freaks and the fancies of a child never banished from home; and Mrs. Weston—no one could doubt that a daughter would be most to her; and it would be quite a pity that any one who so well knew how to teach, should not have their powers in exercise again.\n\n“She has had the advantage, you know, of practising on me,” she continued—“like La Baronne d’Almane on La Comtesse d’Ostalis, in Madame de Genlis’ Adelaide and Theodore, and we shall now see her own little Adelaide educated on a more perfect plan.”\n\n“That is,” replied Mr. Knightley, “she will indulge her even more than she did you, and believe that she does not indulge her at all. It will be the only difference.”\n\n“Poor child!” cried Emma; “at that rate, what will become of her?”\n\n“Nothing very bad.—The fate of thousands. She will be disagreeable in infancy, and correct herself as she grows older. I am losing all my bitterness against spoilt children, my dearest Emma. I, who am owing all my happiness to you, would not it be horrible ingratitude in me to be severe on them?”\n\nEmma laughed, and replied: “But I had the assistance of all your endeavours to counteract the indulgence of other people. I doubt whether my own sense would have corrected me without it.”\n\n“Do you?—I have no doubt. Nature gave you understanding:—Miss Taylor gave you principles. You must have done well. My interference was quite as likely to do harm as good. It was very natural for you to say, what right has he to lecture me?—and I am afraid very natural for you to feel that it was done in a disagreeable manner. I do not believe I did you any good. The good was all to myself, by making you an object of the tenderest affection to me. I could not think about you so much without doating on you, faults and all; and by dint of fancying so many errors, have been in love with you ever since you were thirteen at least.”\n\n“I am sure you were of use to me,” cried Emma. “I was very often influenced rightly by you—oftener than I would own at the time. I am very sure you did me good. And if poor little Anna Weston is to be spoiled, it will be the greatest humanity in you to do as much for her as you have done for me, except falling in love with her when she is thirteen.”\n\n“How often, when you were a girl, have you said to me, with one of your saucy looks—‘Mr. Knightley, I am going to do so-and-so; papa says I may, or I have Miss Taylor’s leave’—something which, you knew, I did not approve. In such cases my interference was giving you two bad feelings instead of one.”\n\n“What an amiable creature I was!—No wonder you should hold my speeches in such affectionate remembrance.”\n\n“‘Mr. Knightley.’—You always called me, ‘Mr. Knightley;’ and, from habit, it has not so very formal a sound.—And yet it is formal. I want you to call me something else, but I do not know what.”\n\n“I remember once calling you ‘George,’ in one of my amiable fits, about ten years ago. I did it because I thought it would offend you; but, as you made no objection, I never did it again.”\n\n“And cannot you call me ‘George’ now?”\n\n“Impossible!—I never can call you any thing but ‘Mr. Knightley.’ I will not promise even to equal the elegant terseness of Mrs. Elton, by calling you Mr. K.—But I will promise,” she added presently, laughing and blushing—“I will promise to call you once by your Christian name. I do not say when, but perhaps you may guess where;—in the building in which N. takes M. for better, for worse.”\n\nEmma grieved that she could not be more openly just to one important service which his better sense would have rendered her, to the advice which would have saved her from the worst of all her womanly follies—her wilful intimacy with Harriet Smith; but it was too tender a subject.—She could not enter on it.—Harriet was very seldom mentioned between them. This, on his side, might merely proceed from her not being thought of; but Emma was rather inclined to attribute it to delicacy, and a suspicion, from some appearances, that their friendship were declining. She was aware herself, that, parting under any other circumstances, they certainly should have corresponded more, and that her intelligence would not have rested, as it now almost wholly did, on Isabella’s letters. He might observe that it was so. The pain of being obliged to practise concealment towards him, was very little inferior to the pain of having made Harriet unhappy.\n\nIsabella sent quite as good an account of her visitor as could be expected; on her first arrival she had thought her out of spirits, which appeared perfectly natural, as there was a dentist to be consulted; but, since that business had been over, she did not appear to find Harriet different from what she had known her before.—Isabella, to be sure, was no very quick observer; yet if Harriet had not been equal to playing with the children, it would not have escaped her. Emma’s comforts and hopes were most agreeably carried on, by Harriet’s being to stay longer; her fortnight was likely to be a month at least. Mr. and Mrs. John Knightley were to come down in August, and she was invited to remain till they could bring her back.\n\n“John does not even mention your friend,” said Mr. Knightley. “Here is his answer, if you like to see it.”\n\nIt was the answer to the communication of his intended marriage. Emma accepted it with a very eager hand, with an impatience all alive to know what he would say about it, and not at all checked by hearing that her friend was unmentioned.\n\n“John enters like a brother into my happiness,” continued Mr. Knightley, “but he is no complimenter; and though I well know him to have, likewise, a most brotherly affection for you, he is so far from making flourishes, that any other young woman might think him rather cool in her praise. But I am not afraid of your seeing what he writes.”\n\n“He writes like a sensible man,” replied Emma, when she had read the letter. “I honour his sincerity. It is very plain that he considers the good fortune of the engagement as all on my side, but that he is not without hope of my growing, in time, as worthy of your affection, as you think me already. Had he said any thing to bear a different construction, I should not have believed him.”\n\n“My Emma, he means no such thing. He only means—”\n\n“He and I should differ very little in our estimation of the two,” interrupted she, with a sort of serious smile—“much less, perhaps, than he is aware of, if we could enter without ceremony or reserve on the subject.”\n\n“Emma, my dear Emma—”\n\n“Oh!” she cried with more thorough gaiety, “if you fancy your brother does not do me justice, only wait till my dear father is in the secret, and hear his opinion. Depend upon it, he will be much farther from doing you justice. He will think all the happiness, all the advantage, on your side of the question; all the merit on mine. I wish I may not sink into ‘poor Emma’ with him at once.—His tender compassion towards oppressed worth can go no farther.”\n\n“Ah!” he cried, “I wish your father might be half as easily convinced as John will be, of our having every right that equal worth can give, to be happy together. I am amused by one part of John’s letter—did you notice it?—where he says, that my information did not take him wholly by surprize, that he was rather in expectation of hearing something of the kind.”\n\n“If I understand your brother, he only means so far as your having some thoughts of marrying. He had no idea of me. He seems perfectly unprepared for that.”\n\n“Yes, yes—but I am amused that he should have seen so far into my feelings. What has he been judging by?—I am not conscious of any difference in my spirits or conversation that could prepare him at this time for my marrying any more than at another.—But it was so, I suppose. I dare say there was a difference when I was staying with them the other day. I believe I did not play with the children quite so much as usual. I remember one evening the poor boys saying, ‘Uncle seems always tired now.’”\n\nThe time was coming when the news must spread farther, and other persons’ reception of it tried. As soon as Mrs. Weston was sufficiently recovered to admit Mr. Woodhouse’s visits, Emma having it in view that her gentle reasonings should be employed in the cause, resolved first to announce it at home, and then at Randalls.—But how to break it to her father at last!—She had bound herself to do it, in such an hour of Mr. Knightley’s absence, or when it came to the point her heart would have failed her, and she must have put it off; but Mr. Knightley was to come at such a time, and follow up the beginning she was to make.—She was forced to speak, and to speak cheerfully too. She must not make it a more decided subject of misery to him, by a melancholy tone herself. She must not appear to think it a misfortune.—With all the spirits she could command, she prepared him first for something strange, and then, in a few words, said, that if his consent and approbation could be obtained—which, she trusted, would be attended with no difficulty, since it was a plan to promote the happiness of all—she and Mr. Knightley meant to marry; by which means Hartfield would receive the constant addition of that person’s company whom she knew he loved, next to his daughters and Mrs. Weston, best in the world.\n\nPoor man!—it was at first a considerable shock to him, and he tried earnestly to dissuade her from it. She was reminded, more than once, of having always said she would never marry, and assured that it would be a great deal better for her to remain single; and told of poor Isabella, and poor Miss Taylor.—But it would not do. Emma hung about him affectionately, and smiled, and said it must be so; and that he must not class her with Isabella and Mrs. Weston, whose marriages taking them from Hartfield, had, indeed, made a melancholy change: but she was not going from Hartfield; she should be always there; she was introducing no change in their numbers or their comforts but for the better; and she was very sure that he would be a great deal the happier for having Mr. Knightley always at hand, when he were once got used to the idea.—Did he not love Mr. Knightley very much?—He would not deny that he did, she was sure.—Whom did he ever want to consult on business but Mr. Knightley?—Who was so useful to him, who so ready to write his letters, who so glad to assist him?—Who so cheerful, so attentive, so attached to him?—Would not he like to have him always on the spot?—Yes. That was all very true. Mr. Knightley could not be there too often; he should be glad to see him every day;—but they did see him every day as it was.—Why could not they go on as they had done?\n\nMr. Woodhouse could not be soon reconciled; but the worst was overcome, the idea was given; time and continual repetition must do the rest.—To Emma’s entreaties and assurances succeeded Mr. Knightley’s, whose fond praise of her gave the subject even a kind of welcome; and he was soon used to be talked to by each, on every fair occasion.—They had all the assistance which Isabella could give, by letters of the strongest approbation; and Mrs. Weston was ready, on the first meeting, to consider the subject in the most serviceable light—first, as a settled, and, secondly, as a good one—well aware of the nearly equal importance of the two recommendations to Mr. Woodhouse’s mind.—It was agreed upon, as what was to be; and every body by whom he was used to be guided assuring him that it would be for his happiness; and having some feelings himself which almost admitted it, he began to think that some time or other—in another year or two, perhaps—it might not be so very bad if the marriage did take place.\n\nMrs. Weston was acting no part, feigning no feelings in all that she said to him in favour of the event.—She had been extremely surprized, never more so, than when Emma first opened the affair to her; but she saw in it only increase of happiness to all, and had no scruple in urging him to the utmost.—She had such a regard for Mr. Knightley, as to think he deserved even her dearest Emma; and it was in every respect so proper, suitable, and unexceptionable a connexion, and in one respect, one point of the highest importance, so peculiarly eligible, so singularly fortunate, that now it seemed as if Emma could not safely have attached herself to any other creature, and that she had herself been the stupidest of beings in not having thought of it, and wished it long ago.—How very few of those men in a rank of life to address Emma would have renounced their own home for Hartfield! And who but Mr. Knightley could know and bear with Mr. Woodhouse, so as to make such an arrangement desirable!—The difficulty of disposing of poor Mr. Woodhouse had been always felt in her husband’s plans and her own, for a marriage between Frank and Emma. How to settle the claims of Enscombe and Hartfield had been a continual impediment—less acknowledged by Mr. Weston than by herself—but even he had never been able to finish the subject better than by saying—“Those matters will take care of themselves; the young people will find a way.” But here there was nothing to be shifted off in a wild speculation on the future. It was all right, all open, all equal. No sacrifice on any side worth the name. It was a union of the highest promise of felicity in itself, and without one real, rational difficulty to oppose or delay it.\n\nMrs. Weston, with her baby on her knee, indulging in such reflections as these, was one of the happiest women in the world. If any thing could increase her delight, it was perceiving that the baby would soon have outgrown its first set of caps.\n\nThe news was universally a surprize wherever it spread; and Mr. Weston had his five minutes share of it; but five minutes were enough to familiarise the idea to his quickness of mind.—He saw the advantages of the match, and rejoiced in them with all the constancy of his wife; but the wonder of it was very soon nothing; and by the end of an hour he was not far from believing that he had always foreseen it.\n\n“It is to be a secret, I conclude,” said he. “These matters are always a secret, till it is found out that every body knows them. Only let me be told when I may speak out.—I wonder whether Jane has any suspicion.”\n\nHe went to Highbury the next morning, and satisfied himself on that point. He told her the news. Was not she like a daughter, his eldest daughter?—he must tell her; and Miss Bates being present, it passed, of course, to Mrs. Cole, Mrs. Perry, and Mrs. Elton, immediately afterwards. It was no more than the principals were prepared for; they had calculated from the time of its being known at Randalls, how soon it would be over Highbury; and were thinking of themselves, as the evening wonder in many a family circle, with great sagacity.\n\nIn general, it was a very well approved match. Some might think him, and others might think her, the most in luck. One set might recommend their all removing to Donwell, and leaving Hartfield for the John Knightleys; and another might predict disagreements among their servants; but yet, upon the whole, there was no serious objection raised, except in one habitation, the Vicarage.—There, the surprize was not softened by any satisfaction. Mr. Elton cared little about it, compared with his wife; he only hoped “the young lady’s pride would now be contented;” and supposed “she had always meant to catch Knightley if she could;” and, on the point of living at Hartfield, could daringly exclaim, “Rather he than I!”—But Mrs. Elton was very much discomposed indeed.—“Poor Knightley! poor fellow!—sad business for him.”—She was extremely concerned; for, though very eccentric, he had a thousand good qualities.—How could he be so taken in?—Did not think him at all in love—not in the least.—Poor Knightley!—There would be an end of all pleasant intercourse with him.—How happy he had been to come and dine with them whenever they asked him! But that would be all over now.—Poor fellow!—No more exploring parties to Donwell made for her. Oh! no; there would be a Mrs. Knightley to throw cold water on every thing.—Extremely disagreeable! But she was not at all sorry that she had abused the housekeeper the other day.—Shocking plan, living together. It would never do. She knew a family near Maple Grove who had tried it, and been obliged to separate before the end of the first quarter.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter XVIII\n\nTime passed on. A few more to-morrows, and the party from London would be arriving. It was an alarming change; and Emma was thinking of it one morning, as what must bring a great deal to agitate and grieve her, when Mr. Knightley came in, and distressing thoughts were put by. After the first chat of pleasure he was silent; and then, in a graver tone, began with,\n\n“I have something to tell you, Emma; some news.”\n\n“Good or bad?” said she, quickly, looking up in his face.\n\n“I do not know which it ought to be called.”\n\n“Oh! good I am sure.—I see it in your countenance. You are trying not to smile.”\n\n“I am afraid,” said he, composing his features, “I am very much afraid, my dear Emma, that you will not smile when you hear it.”\n\n“Indeed! but why so?—I can hardly imagine that any thing which pleases or amuses you, should not please and amuse me too.”\n\n“There is one subject,” he replied, “I hope but one, on which we do not think alike.” He paused a moment, again smiling, with his eyes fixed on her face. “Does nothing occur to you?—Do not you recollect?—Harriet Smith.”\n\nHer cheeks flushed at the name, and she felt afraid of something, though she knew not what.\n\n“Have you heard from her yourself this morning?” cried he. “You have, I believe, and know the whole.”\n\n“No, I have not; I know nothing; pray tell me.”\n\n“You are prepared for the worst, I see—and very bad it is. Harriet Smith marries Robert Martin.”\n\nEmma gave a start, which did not seem like being prepared—and her eyes, in eager gaze, said, “No, this is impossible!” but her lips were closed.\n\n“It is so, indeed,” continued Mr. Knightley; “I have it from Robert Martin himself. He left me not half an hour ago.”\n\nShe was still looking at him with the most speaking amazement.\n\n“You like it, my Emma, as little as I feared.—I wish our opinions were the same. But in time they will. Time, you may be sure, will make one or the other of us think differently; and, in the meanwhile, we need not talk much on the subject.”\n\n“You mistake me, you quite mistake me,” she replied, exerting herself. “It is not that such a circumstance would now make me unhappy, but I cannot believe it. It seems an impossibility!—You cannot mean to say, that Harriet Smith has accepted Robert Martin. You cannot mean that he has even proposed to her again—yet. You only mean, that he intends it.”\n\n“I mean that he has done it,” answered Mr. Knightley, with smiling but determined decision, “and been accepted.”\n\n“Good God!” she cried.—“Well!”—Then having recourse to her workbasket, in excuse for leaning down her face, and concealing all the exquisite feelings of delight and entertainment which she knew she must be expressing, she added, “Well, now tell me every thing; make this intelligible to me. How, where, when?—Let me know it all. I never was more surprized—but it does not make me unhappy, I assure you.—How—how has it been possible?”\n\n“It is a very simple story. He went to town on business three days ago, and I got him to take charge of some papers which I was wanting to send to John.—He delivered these papers to John, at his chambers, and was asked by him to join their party the same evening to Astley’s. They were going to take the two eldest boys to Astley’s. The party was to be our brother and sister, Henry, John—and Miss Smith. My friend Robert could not resist. They called for him in their way; were all extremely amused; and my brother asked him to dine with them the next day—which he did—and in the course of that visit (as I understand) he found an opportunity of speaking to Harriet; and certainly did not speak in vain.—She made him, by her acceptance, as happy even as he is deserving. He came down by yesterday’s coach, and was with me this morning immediately after breakfast, to report his proceedings, first on my affairs, and then on his own. This is all that I can relate of the how, where, and when. Your friend Harriet will make a much longer history when you see her.—She will give you all the minute particulars, which only woman’s language can make interesting.—In our communications we deal only in the great.—However, I must say, that Robert Martin’s heart seemed for him, and to me, very overflowing; and that he did mention, without its being much to the purpose, that on quitting their box at Astley’s, my brother took charge of Mrs. John Knightley and little John, and he followed with Miss Smith and Henry; and that at one time they were in such a crowd, as to make Miss Smith rather uneasy.”\n\nHe stopped.—Emma dared not attempt any immediate reply. To speak, she was sure would be to betray a most unreasonable degree of happiness. She must wait a moment, or he would think her mad. Her silence disturbed him; and after observing her a little while, he added,\n\n“Emma, my love, you said that this circumstance would not now make you unhappy; but I am afraid it gives you more pain than you expected. His situation is an evil—but you must consider it as what satisfies your friend; and I will answer for your thinking better and better of him as you know him more. His good sense and good principles would delight you.—As far as the man is concerned, you could not wish your friend in better hands. His rank in society I would alter if I could, which is saying a great deal I assure you, Emma.—You laugh at me about William Larkins; but I could quite as ill spare Robert Martin.”\n\nHe wanted her to look up and smile; and having now brought herself not to smile too broadly—she did—cheerfully answering,\n\n“You need not be at any pains to reconcile me to the match. I think Harriet is doing extremely well. Her connexions may be worse than his. In respectability of character, there can be no doubt that they are. I have been silent from surprize merely, excessive surprize. You cannot imagine how suddenly it has come on me! how peculiarly unprepared I was!—for I had reason to believe her very lately more determined against him, much more, than she was before.”\n\n“You ought to know your friend best,” replied Mr. Knightley; “but I should say she was a good-tempered, soft-hearted girl, not likely to be very, very determined against any young man who told her he loved her.”\n\nEmma could not help laughing as she answered, “Upon my word, I believe you know her quite as well as I do.—But, Mr. Knightley, are you perfectly sure that she has absolutely and downright accepted him. I could suppose she might in time—but can she already?—Did not you misunderstand him?—You were both talking of other things; of business, shows of cattle, or new drills—and might not you, in the confusion of so many subjects, mistake him?—It was not Harriet’s hand that he was certain of—it was the dimensions of some famous ox.”\n\nThe contrast between the countenance and air of Mr. Knightley and Robert Martin was, at this moment, so strong to Emma’s feelings, and so strong was the recollection of all that had so recently passed on Harriet’s side, so fresh the sound of those words, spoken with such emphasis, “No, I hope I know better than to think of Robert Martin,” that she was really expecting the intelligence to prove, in some measure, premature. It could not be otherwise.\n\n“Do you dare say this?” cried Mr. Knightley. “Do you dare to suppose me so great a blockhead, as not to know what a man is talking of?—What do you deserve?”\n\n“Oh! I always deserve the best treatment, because I never put up with any other; and, therefore, you must give me a plain, direct answer. Are you quite sure that you understand the terms on which Mr. Martin and Harriet now are?”\n\n“I am quite sure,” he replied, speaking very distinctly, “that he told me she had accepted him; and that there was no obscurity, nothing doubtful, in the words he used; and I think I can give you a proof that it must be so. He asked my opinion as to what he was now to do. He knew of no one but Mrs. Goddard to whom he could apply for information of her relations or friends. Could I mention any thing more fit to be done, than to go to Mrs. Goddard? I assured him that I could not. Then, he said, he would endeavour to see her in the course of this day.”\n\n“I am perfectly satisfied,” replied Emma, with the brightest smiles, “and most sincerely wish them happy.”\n\n“You are materially changed since we talked on this subject before.”\n\n“I hope so—for at that time I was a fool.”\n\n“And I am changed also; for I am now very willing to grant you all Harriet’s good qualities. I have taken some pains for your sake, and for Robert Martin’s sake, (whom I have always had reason to believe as much in love with her as ever,) to get acquainted with her. I have often talked to her a good deal. You must have seen that I did. Sometimes, indeed, I have thought you were half suspecting me of pleading poor Martin’s cause, which was never the case; but, from all my observations, I am convinced of her being an artless, amiable girl, with very good notions, very seriously good principles, and placing her happiness in the affections and utility of domestic life.—Much of this, I have no doubt, she may thank you for.”\n\n“Me!” cried Emma, shaking her head.—“Ah! poor Harriet!”\n\nShe checked herself, however, and submitted quietly to a little more praise than she deserved.\n\nTheir conversation was soon afterwards closed by the entrance of her father. She was not sorry. She wanted to be alone. Her mind was in a state of flutter and wonder, which made it impossible for her to be collected. She was in dancing, singing, exclaiming spirits; and till she had moved about, and talked to herself, and laughed and reflected, she could be fit for nothing rational.\n\nHer father’s business was to announce James’s being gone out to put the horses to, preparatory to their now daily drive to Randalls; and she had, therefore, an immediate excuse for disappearing.\n\nThe joy, the gratitude, the exquisite delight of her sensations may be imagined. The sole grievance and alloy thus removed in the prospect of Harriet’s welfare, she was really in danger of becoming too happy for security.—What had she to wish for? Nothing, but to grow more worthy of him, whose intentions and judgment had been ever so superior to her own. Nothing, but that the lessons of her past folly might teach her humility and circumspection in future.\n\nSerious she was, very serious in her thankfulness, and in her resolutions; and yet there was no preventing a laugh, sometimes in the very midst of them. She must laugh at such a close! Such an end of the doleful disappointment of five weeks back! Such a heart—such a Harriet!\n\nNow there would be pleasure in her returning—Every thing would be a pleasure. It would be a great pleasure to know Robert Martin.\n\nHigh in the rank of her most serious and heartfelt felicities, was the reflection that all necessity of concealment from Mr. Knightley would soon be over. The disguise, equivocation, mystery, so hateful to her to practise, might soon be over. She could now look forward to giving him that full and perfect confidence which her disposition was most ready to welcome as a duty.\n\nIn the gayest and happiest spirits she set forward with her father; not always listening, but always agreeing to what he said; and, whether in speech or silence, conniving at the comfortable persuasion of his being obliged to go to Randalls every day, or poor Mrs. Weston would be disappointed.\n\nThey arrived.—Mrs. Weston was alone in the drawing-room:—but hardly had they been told of the baby, and Mr. Woodhouse received the thanks for coming, which he asked for, when a glimpse was caught through the blind, of two figures passing near the window.\n\n“It is Frank and Miss Fairfax,” said Mrs. Weston. “I was just going to tell you of our agreeable surprize in seeing him arrive this morning. He stays till to-morrow, and Miss Fairfax has been persuaded to spend the day with us.—They are coming in, I hope.”\n\nIn half a minute they were in the room. Emma was extremely glad to see him—but there was a degree of confusion—a number of embarrassing recollections on each side. They met readily and smiling, but with a consciousness which at first allowed little to be said; and having all sat down again, there was for some time such a blank in the circle, that Emma began to doubt whether the wish now indulged, which she had long felt, of seeing Frank Churchill once more, and of seeing him with Jane, would yield its proportion of pleasure. When Mr. Weston joined the party, however, and when the baby was fetched, there was no longer a want of subject or animation—or of courage and opportunity for Frank Churchill to draw near her and say,\n\n“I have to thank you, Miss Woodhouse, for a very kind forgiving message in one of Mrs. Weston’s letters. I hope time has not made you less willing to pardon. I hope you do not retract what you then said.”\n\n“No, indeed,” cried Emma, most happy to begin, “not in the least. I am particularly glad to see and shake hands with you—and to give you joy in person.”\n\nHe thanked her with all his heart, and continued some time to speak with serious feeling of his gratitude and happiness.\n\n“Is not she looking well?” said he, turning his eyes towards Jane. “Better than she ever used to do?—You see how my father and Mrs. Weston doat upon her.”\n\nBut his spirits were soon rising again, and with laughing eyes, after mentioning the expected return of the Campbells, he named the name of Dixon.—Emma blushed, and forbade its being pronounced in her hearing.\n\n“I can never think of it,” she cried, “without extreme shame.”\n\n“The shame,” he answered, “is all mine, or ought to be. But is it possible that you had no suspicion?—I mean of late. Early, I know, you had none.”\n\n“I never had the smallest, I assure you.”\n\n“That appears quite wonderful. I was once very near—and I wish I had—it would have been better. But though I was always doing wrong things, they were very bad wrong things, and such as did me no service.—It would have been a much better transgression had I broken the bond of secrecy and told you every thing.”\n\n“It is not now worth a regret,” said Emma.\n\n“I have some hope,” resumed he, “of my uncle’s being persuaded to pay a visit at Randalls; he wants to be introduced to her. When the Campbells are returned, we shall meet them in London, and continue there, I trust, till we may carry her northward.—But now, I am at such a distance from her—is not it hard, Miss Woodhouse?—Till this morning, we have not once met since the day of reconciliation. Do not you pity me?”\n\nEmma spoke her pity so very kindly, that with a sudden accession of gay thought, he cried,\n\n“Ah! by the bye,” then sinking his voice, and looking demure for the moment—“I hope Mr. Knightley is well?” He paused.—She coloured and laughed.—“I know you saw my letter, and think you may remember my wish in your favour. Let me return your congratulations.—I assure you that I have heard the news with the warmest interest and satisfaction.—He is a man whom I cannot presume to praise.”\n\nEmma was delighted, and only wanted him to go on in the same style; but his mind was the next moment in his own concerns and with his own Jane, and his next words were,\n\n“Did you ever see such a skin?—such smoothness! such delicacy!—and yet without being actually fair.—One cannot call her fair. It is a most uncommon complexion, with her dark eye-lashes and hair—a most distinguishing complexion! So peculiarly the lady in it.—Just colour enough for beauty.”\n\n“I have always admired her complexion,” replied Emma, archly; “but do not I remember the time when you found fault with her for being so pale?—When we first began to talk of her.—Have you quite forgotten?”\n\n“Oh! no—what an impudent dog I was!—How could I dare—”\n\nBut he laughed so heartily at the recollection, that Emma could not help saying,\n\n“I do suspect that in the midst of your perplexities at that time, you had very great amusement in tricking us all.—I am sure you had.—I am sure it was a consolation to you.”\n\n“Oh! no, no, no—how can you suspect me of such a thing? I was the most miserable wretch!”\n\n“Not quite so miserable as to be insensible to mirth. I am sure it was a source of high entertainment to you, to feel that you were taking us all in.—Perhaps I am the readier to suspect, because, to tell you the truth, I think it might have been some amusement to myself in the same situation. I think there is a little likeness between us.”\n\nHe bowed.\n\n“If not in our dispositions,” she presently added, with a look of true sensibility, “there is a likeness in our destiny; the destiny which bids fair to connect us with two characters so much superior to our own.”\n\n“True, true,” he answered, warmly. “No, not true on your side. You can have no superior, but most true on mine.—She is a complete angel. Look at her. Is not she an angel in every gesture? Observe the turn of her throat. Observe her eyes, as she is looking up at my father.—You will be glad to hear (inclining his head, and whispering seriously) that my uncle means to give her all my aunt’s jewels. They are to be new set. I am resolved to have some in an ornament for the head. Will not it be beautiful in her dark hair?”\n\n“Very beautiful, indeed,” replied Emma; and she spoke so kindly, that he gratefully burst out,\n\n“How delighted I am to see you again! and to see you in such excellent looks!—I would not have missed this meeting for the world. I should certainly have called at Hartfield, had you failed to come.”\n\nThe others had been talking of the child, Mrs. Weston giving an account of a little alarm she had been under, the evening before, from the infant’s appearing not quite well. She believed she had been foolish, but it had alarmed her, and she had been within half a minute of sending for Mr. Perry. Perhaps she ought to be ashamed, but Mr. Weston had been almost as uneasy as herself.—In ten minutes, however, the child had been perfectly well again. This was her history; and particularly interesting it was to Mr. Woodhouse, who commended her very much for thinking of sending for Perry, and only regretted that she had not done it. “She should always send for Perry, if the child appeared in the slightest degree disordered, were it only for a moment. She could not be too soon alarmed, nor send for Perry too often. It was a pity, perhaps, that he had not come last night; for, though the child seemed well now, very well considering, it would probably have been better if Perry had seen it.”\n\nFrank Churchill caught the name.\n\n“Perry!” said he to Emma, and trying, as he spoke, to catch Miss Fairfax’s eye. “My friend Mr. Perry! What are they saying about Mr. Perry?—Has he been here this morning?—And how does he travel now?—Has he set up his carriage?”\n\nEmma soon recollected, and understood him; and while she joined in the laugh, it was evident from Jane’s countenance that she too was really hearing him, though trying to seem deaf.\n\n“Such an extraordinary dream of mine!” he cried. “I can never think of it without laughing.—She hears us, she hears us, Miss Woodhouse. I see it in her cheek, her smile, her vain attempt to frown. Look at her. Do not you see that, at this instant, the very passage of her own letter, which sent me the report, is passing under her eye—that the whole blunder is spread before her—that she can attend to nothing else, though pretending to listen to the others?”\n\nJane was forced to smile completely, for a moment; and the smile partly remained as she turned towards him, and said in a conscious, low, yet steady voice,\n\n“How you can bear such recollections, is astonishing to me!—They will sometimes obtrude—but how you can court them!”\n\nHe had a great deal to say in return, and very entertainingly; but Emma’s feelings were chiefly with Jane, in the argument; and on leaving Randalls, and falling naturally into a comparison of the two men, she felt, that pleased as she had been to see Frank Churchill, and really regarding him as she did with friendship, she had never been more sensible of Mr. Knightley’s high superiority of character. The happiness of this most happy day, received its completion, in the animated contemplation of his worth which this comparison produced.\n\n# Volume III — Chapter XIX\n\nIf Emma had still, at intervals, an anxious feeling for Harriet, a momentary doubt of its being possible for her to be really cured of her attachment to Mr. Knightley, and really able to accept another man from unbiased inclination, it was not long that she had to suffer from the recurrence of any such uncertainty. A very few days brought the party from London, and she had no sooner an opportunity of being one hour alone with Harriet, than she became perfectly satisfied—unaccountable as it was!—that Robert Martin had thoroughly supplanted Mr. Knightley, and was now forming all her views of happiness.\n\nHarriet was a little distressed—did look a little foolish at first: but having once owned that she had been presumptuous and silly, and self-deceived, before, her pain and confusion seemed to die away with the words, and leave her without a care for the past, and with the fullest exultation in the present and future; for, as to her friend’s approbation, Emma had instantly removed every fear of that nature, by meeting her with the most unqualified congratulations.—Harriet was most happy to give every particular of the evening at Astley’s, and the dinner the next day; she could dwell on it all with the utmost delight. But what did such particulars explain?—The fact was, as Emma could now acknowledge, that Harriet had always liked Robert Martin; and that his continuing to love her had been irresistible.—Beyond this, it must ever be unintelligible to Emma.\n\nThe event, however, was most joyful; and every day was giving her fresh reason for thinking so.—Harriet’s parentage became known. She proved to be the daughter of a tradesman, rich enough to afford her the comfortable maintenance which had ever been hers, and decent enough to have always wished for concealment.—Such was the blood of gentility which Emma had formerly been so ready to vouch for!—It was likely to be as untainted, perhaps, as the blood of many a gentleman: but what a connexion had she been preparing for Mr. Knightley—or for the Churchills—or even for Mr. Elton!—The stain of illegitimacy, unbleached by nobility or wealth, would have been a stain indeed.\n\nNo objection was raised on the father’s side; the young man was treated liberally; it was all as it should be: and as Emma became acquainted with Robert Martin, who was now introduced at Hartfield, she fully acknowledged in him all the appearance of sense and worth which could bid fairest for her little friend. She had no doubt of Harriet’s happiness with any good-tempered man; but with him, and in the home he offered, there would be the hope of more, of security, stability, and improvement. She would be placed in the midst of those who loved her, and who had better sense than herself; retired enough for safety, and occupied enough for cheerfulness. She would be never led into temptation, nor left for it to find her out. She would be respectable and happy; and Emma admitted her to be the luckiest creature in the world, to have created so steady and persevering an affection in such a man;—or, if not quite the luckiest, to yield only to herself.\n\nHarriet, necessarily drawn away by her engagements with the Martins, was less and less at Hartfield; which was not to be regretted.—The intimacy between her and Emma must sink; their friendship must change into a calmer sort of goodwill; and, fortunately, what ought to be, and must be, seemed already beginning, and in the most gradual, natural manner.\n\nBefore the end of September, Emma attended Harriet to church, and saw her hand bestowed on Robert Martin with so complete a satisfaction, as no remembrances, even connected with Mr. Elton as he stood before them, could impair.—Perhaps, indeed, at that time she scarcely saw Mr. Elton, but as the clergyman whose blessing at the altar might next fall on herself.—Robert Martin and Harriet Smith, the latest couple engaged of the three, were the first to be married.\n\nJane Fairfax had already quitted Highbury, and was restored to the comforts of her beloved home with the Campbells.—The Mr. Churchills were also in town; and they were only waiting for November.\n\nThe intermediate month was the one fixed on, as far as they dared, by Emma and Mr. Knightley.—They had determined that their marriage ought to be concluded while John and Isabella were still at Hartfield, to allow them the fortnight’s absence in a tour to the seaside, which was the plan.—John and Isabella, and every other friend, were agreed in approving it. But Mr. Woodhouse—how was Mr. Woodhouse to be induced to consent?—he, who had never yet alluded to their marriage but as a distant event.\n\nWhen first sounded on the subject, he was so miserable, that they were almost hopeless.—A second allusion, indeed, gave less pain.—He began to think it was to be, and that he could not prevent it—a very promising step of the mind on its way to resignation. Still, however, he was not happy. Nay, he appeared so much otherwise, that his daughter’s courage failed. She could not bear to see him suffering, to know him fancying himself neglected; and though her understanding almost acquiesced in the assurance of both the Mr. Knightleys, that when once the event were over, his distress would be soon over too, she hesitated—she could not proceed.\n\nIn this state of suspense they were befriended, not by any sudden illumination of Mr. Woodhouse’s mind, or any wonderful change of his nervous system, but by the operation of the same system in another way.—Mrs. Weston’s poultry-house was robbed one night of all her turkeys—evidently by the ingenuity of man. Other poultry-yards in the neighbourhood also suffered.—Pilfering was housebreaking to Mr. Woodhouse’s fears.—He was very uneasy; and but for the sense of his son-in-law’s protection, would have been under wretched alarm every night of his life. The strength, resolution, and presence of mind of the Mr. Knightleys, commanded his fullest dependence. While either of them protected him and his, Hartfield was safe.—But Mr. John Knightley must be in London again by the end of the first week in November.\n\nThe result of this distress was, that, with a much more voluntary, cheerful consent than his daughter had ever presumed to hope for at the moment, she was able to fix her wedding-day—and Mr. Elton was called on, within a month from the marriage of Mr. and Mrs. Robert Martin, to join the hands of Mr. Knightley and Miss Woodhouse.\n\nThe wedding was very much like other weddings, where the parties have no taste for finery or parade; and Mrs. Elton, from the particulars detailed by her husband, thought it all extremely shabby, and very inferior to her own.—“Very little white satin, very few lace veils; a most pitiful business!—Selina would stare when she heard of it.”—But, in spite of these deficiencies, the wishes, the hopes, the confidence, the predictions of the small band of true friends who witnessed the ceremony, were fully answered in the perfect happiness of the union.\n\nFINIS";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-158"]="# 제1권 1장\n\n엠마 우드하우스는 아름답고 영리하며 부유한 데다, 안락한 집과 쾌활한 천성까지 갖추어, 삶이 줄 수 있는 최상의 축복 몇 가지를 한 몸에 모은 듯한 사람이었다. 그리하여 이 세상에 태어나 스물한 해가 다 되도록, 그녀를 괴롭히거나 성가시게 한 일은 거의 없었다.\n\n그녀는 더없이 자애롭고 너그러운 아버지의 두 딸 가운데 막내였고, 언니가 결혼한 덕에 아주 일찍부터 집안의 안주인 노릇을 해 왔다. 어머니는 너무 오래전에 돌아가셔서, 그 손길에 대한 어렴풋한 기억밖에 남아 있지 않았다. 그리고 그 빈자리는 가정교사로 들어온 한 훌륭한 여인이 채워 주었으니, 애정에 있어서는 어머니에 거의 못지않은 사람이었다.\n\n미스 테일러가 우드하우스 집안에 머문 지 열여섯 해 — 가정교사라기보다는 벗에 가까웠고, 두 딸을 모두 아꼈으나 그중에서도 엠마를 각별히 사랑했다. 두 사람 사이는 차라리 자매의 친밀함이었다. 미스 테일러가 가정교사라는 이름뿐인 직책을 내려놓기 전부터도, 그 온화한 성품은 무엇 하나 억누르는 법이 없었다. 그리고 권위의 그림자마저 걷힌 지 오래인 지금, 두 사람은 서로 깊이 아끼는 벗과 벗으로 함께 살아왔고, 엠마는 그저 제 하고 싶은 대로 했다 — 미스 테일러의 판단을 높이 사면서도, 따르는 것은 주로 자기 자신의 판단이었지만.\n\n실로 엠마의 처지에서 진짜 화근이라 할 것은, 매사를 제 뜻대로 너무 많이 할 수 있는 권한과, 자기 자신을 조금 지나치게 좋게 여기는 기질이었다. 이 둘이야말로 그녀의 숱한 즐거움에 흠집을 낼 우려가 있는 불리한 조건이었다. 그러나 그 위험은 지금으로서는 워낙 감지되지 않아서, 그녀에게는 도무지 불행 축에 끼지 못했다.\n\n슬픔이 찾아왔다 — 잔잔한 슬픔이 — 그러나 결코 불쾌한 자각의 모습은 아니었다. 미스 테일러가 결혼한 것이다. 처음으로 비탄을 안겨 준 것은 미스 테일러를 잃는 일이었다. 이 사랑하는 벗의 혼인날에야 엠마는 처음으로, 이 상실이 이어지리라는 침울한 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있었다. 식이 끝나고 신부 일행이 떠나자, 긴 저녁을 밝혀 줄 세 번째 사람도 기약 없이, 아버지와 단둘이 저녁을 들게 되었다. 아버지는 여느 때처럼 식사 뒤 잠자리에 들 채비를 했고, 그러고 나니 그녀에게는 잃어버린 것을 곱씹으며 앉아 있는 일밖에 남지 않았다.\n\n그 혼사는 벗에게 온갖 행복을 약속하는 일이었다. 미스터 웨스턴은 나무랄 데 없는 인품에 넉넉한 재산, 어울리는 나이와 유쾌한 몸가짐을 갖춘 사람이었다. 자신이 늘 얼마나 헌신적이고 너그러운 우정으로 이 혼사를 바라고 밀어 왔는가를 떠올리면 흐뭇한 구석도 없지 않았다. 그러나 그녀에게는 암담한 아침나절이었다. 미스 테일러의 빈자리는 날마다, 시간마다 사무칠 터였다. 그녀는 지난날의 다정함을 떠올렸다 — 열여섯 해의 다정함과 애정을 — 다섯 살 적부터 어떻게 가르쳐 주고 어떻게 함께 놀아 주었는지를 — 건강할 때는 곁에 붙어 즐겁게 해 주려고 온 힘을 쏟고, 어린 시절의 갖은 병치레 때는 어떻게 간호해 주었는지를. 여기에 진 고마움의 빚이 컸다. 그러나 이저벨라가 결혼하고 둘만 남게 되면서 곧 자리 잡은 지난 칠 년의 교제, 그 대등한 사이와 흉허물 없는 격의 없음은 그보다 더 귀하고 애틋한 기억이었다. 그녀는 흔치 않은 벗이자 말동무였다. 총명하고 박식하고 쓸모 있고 온화하며, 집안의 모든 사정에 밝고 집안의 모든 관심사에 마음을 쏟았으며, 그중에서도 유독 그녀 자신에게, 그녀의 모든 기쁨과 모든 계획에 마음을 쏟았다 — 떠오르는 생각은 무엇이든 그대로 말할 수 있고, 결코 흠을 잡는 법이 없을 만큼 그녀를 사랑해 주는 사람이었다.\n\n이 변화를 어찌 견딘단 말인가? 벗이 가는 곳이 겨우 반 마일 밖인 것은 사실이었다. 그러나 반 마일 밖의 웨스턴 부인과 한집안의 미스 테일러 사이에는 크나큰 차이가 있으리라는 것을 엠마는 알고 있었다. 타고난 조건과 집안 형편이 그토록 유리한데도, 그녀는 이제 정신의 고독에 시달릴 큰 위험에 놓여 있었다. 아버지를 끔찍이 사랑했지만, 아버지는 그녀의 말동무가 되지 못했다. 진지한 대화든 장난스러운 대화든, 아버지는 그녀를 받아 주지 못했다.\n\n부녀간의 실제 나이 차이에서 오는 곤란은 (미스터 우드하우스는 일찍 결혼한 편이 아니었다) 그의 체질과 습성 탓에 한층 더했다. 평생을 병약가*로 살아 몸도 마음도 움직이는 일이 없던 그는 나이보다 훨씬 늙은이처럼 굴었다. 따뜻한 마음씨와 상냥한 성품으로 어딜 가나 사랑받았으나, 그 재간이 그를 돋보이게 한 적은 한 번도 없었다.\n* 병약가(valetudinarian): 실제 병보다 건강 걱정에 사로잡혀 사는 사람.\n\n언니는 결혼으로 그리 멀리 떠난 것은 아니어서 겨우 십육 마일 밖 런던에 자리 잡았지만, 날마다 오갈 수 있는 거리는 결코 아니었다. 성탄절이 되어 이저벨라 부부와 어린아이들이 다시 찾아와 집을 채우고 즐거운 어울림을 돌려주기까지, 시월과 십일월의 기나긴 저녁들을 하트필드에서 견뎌 내야만 했다.\n\n하이베리는 읍내라 해도 좋을 만큼 크고 사람 많은 마을이었고, 하트필드는 잔디밭과 관목 숲과 이름이 따로 있다 한들 실은 그 마을에 속해 있었지만, 그곳에 그녀와 어깨를 나란히 할 사람은 없었다. 우드하우스 집안은 그곳에서 첫째가는 가문이었다. 모두가 그들을 우러러보았다. 아버지가 누구에게나 깍듯한 덕에 그녀도 아는 사람이야 많았으나, 그 가운데 미스 테일러를 한나절만이라도 대신할 만한 이는 하나도 없었다. 쓸쓸한 변화였다. 엠마는 한숨을 쉬며 이룰 수 없는 일들을 바라는 수밖에 없었다. 이윽고 아버지가 잠에서 깨어, 명랑한 얼굴을 지어야 할 때까지. 아버지의 기운은 떠받쳐 드려야 했다. 그는 신경이 예민하고 쉬이 의기소침해지는 사람이었다. 정든 사람이라면 누구든 좋아했고, 헤어지기를 싫어했으며, 어떤 종류의 변화든 질색이었다. 변화의 근원인 혼인은 언제나 마뜩잖은 것이었다. 제 딸의 결혼조차 — 그것이 온전히 애정으로 맺어진 혼사였는데도 — 아직 도무지 받아들이지 못해 딸 이야기가 나오면 으레 측은해하는 투로만 말하던 차에, 이제 미스 테일러와도 헤어져야 했던 것이다. 게다가 악의 없는 이기심이 몸에 배어, 남들이 자기와 다르게 느낄 수 있다는 것을 도무지 상상하지 못하는 사람인지라, 미스 테일러가 자기들에게만이 아니라 그녀 자신에게도 못할 짓을 했으며, 남은 평생을 하트필드에서 보냈더라면 훨씬 행복했으리라고 굳게 여기는 쪽이었다. 엠마는 아버지가 그런 생각에 빠지지 않도록 힘닿는 데까지 명랑하게 웃고 떠들었다. 그러나 차가 나오자, 아버지는 저녁상에서 했던 말을 토씨 하나 다르지 않게 또 하지 않고는 배기지 못했다.\n\n\"가엾은 미스 테일러! 다시 여기 있어 주면 좋으련만. 미스터 웨스턴이 그 사람한테 마음을 두다니, 이렇게 애석한 일이 있나!\"\n\n\"아버지, 저는 그 말씀엔 동의할 수 없어요. 아시잖아요. 미스터 웨스턴은 그렇게 쾌활하고 유쾌하고 훌륭한 분이니, 좋은 아내를 맞을 자격이 충분하고말고요. 그리고 미스 테일러가 자기 집을 가질 수 있는데, 평생 우리 집에 살면서 저의 별난 변덕이나 다 받아 주기를 바라실 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"자기 집이라니! 자기 집이 뭐가 좋단 말이냐? 여기가 세 배는 넓은데. 그리고 얘야, 너는 별난 변덕 같은 건 부린 적이 없단다.\"\n\n\"우리가 얼마나 자주 보러 가고, 그분들이 얼마나 자주 보러 오겠어요! 늘 만나게 될 거예요! 시작은 우리가 해야죠. 조만간 신혼 인사를 하러 가야 해요.\"\n\n\"얘야, 내가 그 먼 데를 어떻게 간단 말이냐? 랜덜스가 어디 가까운 거리냐. 나는 그 절반도 못 걷는다.\"\n\n\"아유, 아버지, 걸어가시라는 사람이 어디 있어요. 당연히 마차로 가야죠.\"\n\n\"마차라니! 그 정도 거리에 말을 매자면 제임스가 싫어할 텐데. 그리고 우리가 인사를 드리는 동안 그 가엾은 말들은 어디에 둔단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"미스터 웨스턴네 마구간에 두면 되죠, 아버지. 그건 벌써 다 정해 둔 일이잖아요. 어젯밤에 미스터 웨스턴하고 다 이야기해 뒀어요. 그리고 제임스라면 염려 마세요. 딸이 랜덜스에서 하녀로 일하고 있으니, 랜덜스 가는 길이라면 언제든 좋아할 게 분명해요. 오히려 다른 데는 데려다주려 할지 그게 의심스러울 지경이에요. 그게 다 아버지가 하신 일이에요. 해나한테 그 좋은 자리를 구해 주셨잖아요. 아버지가 말을 꺼내기 전엔 아무도 해나를 생각하지 못했는걸요 — 제임스가 아버지께 얼마나 고마워하는데요!\"\n\n\"그 아이를 생각해 내길 참 잘했지. 운이 참 좋았어. 가엾은 제임스가 어떤 일로든 푸대접받았다고 여기게 하고 싶진 않았으니까. 그리고 그 애는 분명 아주 좋은 하녀가 될 게다. 예의 바르고 말씨가 고운 아이야. 나는 그 애를 아주 높이 산단다. 그 애는 나를 볼 때마다 어찌나 곱게 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 안부를 묻는지. 또 네가 바느질을 시키려고 이리 불렀을 때 보니, 문고리를 꼭 바르게 돌려 닫지 쾅 닫는 법이 없더구나. 분명 훌륭한 하녀가 될 게야. 그리고 가엾은 미스 테일러한테도, 낯익은 사람이 곁에 있다는 게 큰 위안이 되겠지. 제임스가 딸을 보러 건너갈 때마다 우리 소식을 듣게 될 테니 말이다. 우리가 다들 어찌 지내는지 전해 줄 수 있을 게야.\"\n\n엠마는 이 한결 밝아진 생각의 물줄기가 끊기지 않도록 온갖 애를 다 썼고, 주사위 놀이의 힘을 빌려 아버지가 저녁나절을 그럭저럭 넘기고, 아쉬움이라면 오직 저 혼자만의 것이 되기를 바랐다. 주사위 놀이판이 차려졌다. 그러나 곧바로 손님이 들어서면서 그것은 필요 없게 되었다.\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 서른일고여덟쯤 된 분별 있는 남자로, 이 집안의 아주 오랜 절친한 벗일 뿐 아니라 이저벨라 남편의 형으로서 각별한 인연까지 있는 사람이었다. 하이베리에서 일 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 살며 자주 드나드는, 언제나 반가운 손님이었는데, 이번에는 런던의 그 사돈집에서 곧장 오는 길이라 여느 때보다 더욱 반가웠다. 며칠 집을 비웠다가 돌아와 늦은 저녁을 들고는, 브런즈윅 스퀘어의 모두가 잘 지낸다는 소식을 전하러 하트필드까지 걸어 올라온 것이었다. 반가운 소식이라 미스터 우드하우스도 한동안 생기가 돌았다. 미스터 나이틀리의 쾌활한 태도는 언제나 그에게 약이 되었다. \"가엾은 이저벨라\"와 아이들의 안부를 묻는 갖은 물음에 더없이 흡족한 대답이 돌아왔다. 그것이 끝나자 미스터 우드하우스가 고마워하며 말했다. \"이 늦은 시간에 우리를 찾아 주시다니 참으로 고맙소, 미스터 나이틀리. 오시는 길이 끔찍하지 않았을까 걱정이오.\"\n\n\"천만에요. 달빛이 아름다운 밤입니다. 어찌나 포근한지, 이 훌륭한 난롯불에서 오히려 물러앉아야겠는걸요.\"\n\n\"하지만 길이 몹시 질척하고 더러웠을 텐데. 감기나 들지 않으시면 좋으련만.\"\n\n\"더럽다니요! 제 신을 보십시오. 흙 한 점 안 묻었습니다.\"\n\n\"거참! 그것 놀랍군. 여긴 비가 어지간히 왔는데. 아침을 드는 동안 반 시간을 어찌나 무섭게 퍼붓던지. 나는 혼례를 미루자고까지 했다오.\"\n\n\"그러고 보니 — 아직 축하 인사를 못 드렸군요. 두 분이 어떤 기쁨을 느끼고 계실지 잘 알기에, 서둘러 축하드리지 않았습니다. 그래도 일이 그럭저럭 잘 치러졌기를 바랍니다. 다들 어떠셨습니까? 누가 제일 많이 울던가요?\"\n\n\"아! 가엾은 미스 테일러! 참 딱한 일이지.\"\n\n\"가엾은 건 우드하우스 부녀라고 해 주시지요. '가엾은 미스 테일러'라고는 도저히 못 하겠습니다. 어르신과 엠마를 높이 사기는 합니다만, 매인 몸이냐 제 몸의 주인이냐 하는 문제가 되면야! 어쨌든 비위를 맞출 사람이 둘에서 하나로 주는 편이 나은 게 분명하지요.\"\n\n\"그 둘 중 하나가 이렇게 변덕스럽고 성가신 사람이라면 더더욱요!\" 엠마가 장난스럽게 말했다. \"그게 지금 머릿속에 든 생각이죠, 다 알아요 — 아버지만 안 계셨으면 분명 그렇게 말했을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 말이 정말 맞는 것 같구나, 얘야.\" 미스터 우드하우스가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"나는 아무래도 가끔 무척 변덕스럽고 성가신 사람인가 보다.\"\n\n\"아버지도 참! 제가 아버지를 두고 한 말이라고 생각하시는 건 아니죠? 미스터 나이틀리가 아버지를 두고 한 말이라고요? 세상에 그런 끔찍한 생각이! 아니에요, 제 얘기였어요. 미스터 나이틀리는 저한테 흠잡기를 좋아하잖아요 — 농담으로요 — 다 농담이에요. 우리는 늘 서로 하고 싶은 말을 다 하고 사는걸요.\"\n\n사실 미스터 나이틀리는 엠마 우드하우스에게서 결점을 볼 줄 아는 몇 안 되는 사람 가운데 하나였고, 그것을 그녀에게 말해 주는 유일한 사람이었다. 엠마 자신에게도 그리 달가운 일은 아니었지만, 아버지에게는 훨씬 더 달갑지 않으리라는 것을 알기에, 세상 모두가 딸을 완벽하다고 여기지는 않는다는 사실을 아버지가 정말로 눈치채게 하고 싶지는 않았다.\n\n\"엠마는 내가 빈말로 추어주는 법이 없다는 걸 압니다.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"하지만 누구를 두고 한 말은 아니었습니다. 미스 테일러는 비위 맞출 사람 둘에 익숙했는데, 이제는 하나만 맞추면 되니까요. 십중팔구 그녀가 득을 보는 셈이지요.\"\n\n\"자아,\" 엠마가 그 말은 그냥 넘기기로 하고 말했다. \"혼례 이야기가 듣고 싶으신 거죠. 기꺼이 들려 드릴게요. 우리 모두 더없이 의젓하게 굴었으니까요. 한 사람도 늦지 않았고, 다들 더없이 고운 차림이었어요. 눈물 한 방울 없었고, 시무룩한 얼굴도 거의 안 보였죠. 그럼요. 겨우 반 마일 떨어져 사는 것뿐이니 날마다 만나게 되리라는 걸 모두 알고 있었는걸요.\"\n\n\"우리 엠마는 무엇이든 참 잘 견딘다오.\" 아버지가 말했다. \"하지만 미스터 나이틀리, 이 애는 가엾은 미스 테일러를 잃어서 정말로 몹시 서운해하고 있소. 분명 저 생각보다 훨씬 더 그리워하게 될 게요.\"\n\n엠마는 눈물과 미소 사이에서 고개를 돌렸다. \"엠마가 그런 벗을 그리워하지 않을 리야 없지요.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"그럴 수 있는 사람이라면 우리가 이렇게까지 좋아하지 않을 겁니다. 하지만 엠마는 이 결혼이 미스 테일러에게 얼마나 득이 되는지 알고, 미스 테일러 나이에 제 집을 갖고 자리 잡는 일이 얼마나 반가운 일인지, 안락한 살림이 보장된다는 게 얼마나 중요한지도 압니다. 그러니 기쁨보다 아픔이 크도록 제 마음을 내버려 둘 수 없는 거지요. 미스 테일러의 벗이라면 누구나 그녀가 이토록 행복하게 결혼한 것을 기뻐해야 마땅합니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 제게 또 하나의 기쁨이 있다는 걸 잊으셨네요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"그것도 아주 큰 기쁨을요 — 이 혼사를 제가 맺어 줬다는 것 말이에요. 사 년 전에 제가 맺어 준 거예요. 그 많은 사람이 미스터 웨스턴은 절대 재혼하지 않을 거라고들 했는데, 이 혼사가 이루어져서 제가 옳았다는 게 증명됐으니, 그거면 어떤 일이든 위로가 되고도 남죠.\"\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 그녀를 향해 고개를 가로저었다. 아버지는 애정 어린 투로 답했다. \"아이고, 얘야, 제발 혼사를 맺어 주고 앞일을 점치는 일은 그만두려무나. 네가 말하는 건 죄다 들어맞으니 말이다. 부디 더는 중매를 서지 말아 다오.\"\n\n\"제 혼사는 안 맺겠다고 약속드릴게요, 아버지. 하지만 남들 혼사는 정말이지 그만둘 수 없어요. 세상에 이만한 재미가 또 없는걸요! 게다가 이런 성공을 거둔 다음인데요! 미스터 웨스턴은 절대 재혼하지 않을 거라고 다들 말했어요. 어림도 없죠! 그렇게 오래 홀아비로 지내면서 아내 없이도 더없이 편안해 보이고, 읍내 일이다 이곳 친구들이다 늘 바쁘게 지내고, 어딜 가나 환영받고 늘 쾌활한 미스터 웨스턴 — 마음만 먹으면 일 년에 단 하루 저녁도 혼자 보낼 일이 없는 분인데. 아유, 그럼요! 미스터 웨스턴은 절대로 재혼하지 않을 거라고요. 죽은 부인에게 임종 자리에서 약속했다느니, 아들과 외삼촌이 허락하지 않는다느니 하는 말까지 있었죠. 이 일을 두고 별별 진지한 헛소리가 다 돌았지만, 저는 하나도 믿지 않았어요.\n\n\"사 년쯤 전 그날 — 미스 테일러와 제가 브로드웨이 골목에서 그분과 마주친 날, 이슬비가 듣기 시작하니까 그분이 어찌나 늠름하게 내달려 미첼 농장에서 우산을 두 개나 빌려다 주시던지 — 그날부터 저는 마음을 정했어요. 그 시간부터 이 혼사를 계획한 거예요. 이번 일에 이런 성공이 따라 줬는데, 아버지, 설마 제가 중매를 그만두리라고 생각하시는 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"그 '성공'이라는 말을 무슨 뜻으로 쓰는지 모르겠군.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"성공이란 노력을 전제로 하는 말이야. 지난 사 년을 이 결혼을 성사시키려 애쓰며 보냈다면야 시간을 알맞고 곱게 쓴 셈이지. 젊은 아가씨의 머리에 썩 어울리는 소일거리니까! 하지만 내 짐작대로, 자네가 말하는 중매라는 게 그저 계획해 본 것뿐이라면 — 한가한 어느 날 '미스터 웨스턴이 미스 테일러와 결혼하면 그녀한테 참 좋을 텐데' 하고 혼잣말을 하고, 그 뒤로 이따금 같은 말을 되뇌었을 뿐이라면 — 무슨 성공을 말하는 건가? 자네 공이 어디 있지? 뭐가 자랑스러운 건가? 운 좋게 알아맞힌 것뿐이야. 그 이상은 말할 게 없네.\"\n\n\"운 좋게 알아맞히는 기쁨과 통쾌함을 한 번도 못 느껴 보셨어요? 가엾기도 하셔라. 더 영리하신 줄 알았는데요 — 정말이지, 운 좋은 짐작은 결코 그저 운만은 아니거든요. 거기엔 언제나 얼마간의 재능이 들어 있어요. 그리고 트집을 잡으시는 제 가련한 '성공'이라는 말로 말하자면, 제가 그 말을 쓸 자격이 아주 없지는 않은 것 같은데요. 두 폭의 그럴듯한 그림을 그려 주셨지만, 세 번째 그림도 있을 수 있다고 생각해요 — 아무것도 안 하는 쪽과 다 하는 쪽, 그 중간 어디쯤요. 제가 미스터 웨스턴이 우리 집에 드나들도록 부추기지 않았다면, 자잘한 격려를 숱하게 보태고 자잘한 일들을 숱하게 매만지지 않았다면, 결국 아무 일도 안 됐을지 몰라요. 하트필드를 아실 만큼 아시는 분이면 그쯤은 아실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"웨스턴처럼 시원시원하고 흉금 없는 남자와 미스 테일러처럼 분별 있고 꾸밈없는 여자라면, 제 일은 제게 맡겨 두어도 안심이야. 끼어들어 봐야 그들에게 득이 되기보다 자네 자신에게 해가 되기 십상이지.\"\n\n\"엠마는 남에게 좋은 일을 할 수 있다면 제 생각은 도통 안 하는 아이라오.\" 미스터 우드하우스가 절반쯤만 알아듣고 거들었다. \"하지만 얘야, 부디 더는 중매를 서지 말아 다오. 그건 어리석은 짓이야. 한 집안의 단란함을 무참히 깨뜨리는 일이란다.\"\n\n\"한 번만 더요, 아버지. 미스터 엘턴 것만요. 가엾은 미스터 엘턴! 아버지도 미스터 엘턴을 좋아하시잖아요 — 그분 아내가 될 사람을 제가 찾아봐야 해요. 하이베리에는 그분에게 어울리는 사람이 없어요. 여기 온 지 벌써 만 일 년에, 집도 그렇게 아늑하게 꾸며 놓으셨는데, 더 오래 혼자 두는 건 면목 없는 일이죠. 오늘 두 사람의 손을 맞잡게 해 주실 때 보니, 당신도 누가 똑같이 해 드리면 좋겠다는 얼굴이 역력하시던걸요! 저는 미스터 엘턴을 아주 좋게 생각해요. 그리고 이게 제가 그분께 보답할 수 있는 유일한 길이고요.\"\n\n\"미스터 엘턴이야 아주 훤한 젊은이고말고. 아주 착실한 젊은이지. 나도 그 사람을 무척 아낀단다. 하지만 얘야, 그 사람한테 마음을 써 주고 싶거든 언제 한번 저녁이나 들러 오라고 하려무나. 그게 훨씬 나은 일이야. 미스터 나이틀리도 고맙게 자리를 함께해 주시겠지.\"\n\n\"언제든 기꺼이 그러지요.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 웃으며 말했다. \"그리고 그게 훨씬 나은 일이라는 데 전적으로 동감입니다. 엠마, 저녁에 초대해서 생선과 닭고기 가장 좋은 부위를 덜어 주게. 다만 아내는 본인이 고르게 두고. 알아 두게, 스물예닐곱 먹은 남자는 제 앞가림은 할 줄 안다네.\"\n\n# 제1권 2장\n\n미스터 웨스턴은 하이베리 토박이로, 지난 두세 대에 걸쳐 신사 계급의 지위와 재산을 점차 쌓아 온 점잖은 집안에서 태어났다. 그는 좋은 교육을 받았으나, 젊어서 일찌감치 얼마 안 되는 독립적 재산을 물려받게 되자, 형제들이 종사하던 보다 소박한 생업 따위에는 흥미를 잃고 말았다. 그리하여 활달하고 명랑한 마음과 사교적인 기질을 만족시키려고, 당시 소집되어 있던 고장의 민병대에 들어갔다.\n\n웨스턴 대위는 누구에게나 사랑받는 사람이었다. 군 생활의 우연이 그를 요크셔의 명문가 출신인 미스 처칠과 만나게 했고, 미스 처칠이 그에게 반했을 때, 그를 한 번도 본 적 없으면서 자긍심과 위세가 대단하여 이런 인연이 그 자존심을 건드릴 그녀의 오라버니와 올케를 제외하고는 아무도 놀라지 않았다.\n\n그러나 미스 처칠은 성년인 데다 제 재산을 마음대로 다룰 수 있었던지라 — 그 재산이 집안의 영지에 견줄 만한 것은 결코 아니었지만 — 이 혼인을 단념시킬 수 없었고, 마침내 식이 치러졌으니, 미스터 처칠과 처칠 부인으로서는 이만저만 분한 일이 아니어서 격식에 맞추어 그녀와 의절했다. 어울리지 않는 인연이었고, 그리 많은 행복을 가져다주지도 못했다. 웨스턴 부인은 그 안에서 더 많은 행복을 찾았어야 마땅했으니, 따뜻한 마음과 다정한 성품으로 그녀가 자기를 사랑해 주는 그 크나큰 은혜에 보답하느라 무엇이든 다 해 주어야 한다고 여기는 남편을 두었기 때문이다. 그러나 그녀에게는 어떤 종류의 기개는 있었으되, 가장 훌륭한 기개는 없었다. 오라버니의 반대를 무릅쓰고 제 뜻을 밀고 나갈 결단력은 있었으나, 그 오라버니의 부당한 노여움에 부당한 회한을 품지 않을 만큼은 못 되었고, 옛집의 호사를 그리워하지 않을 만큼도 못 되었다. 두 사람은 수입 이상으로 살았으나, 그래도 엔스컴에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀는 남편을 사랑하기를 그치지 않았으나, 웨스턴 대위의 아내이면서 동시에 엔스컴의 미스 처칠이고도 싶어 했다.\n\n웨스턴 대위는, 특히 처칠 집안 사람들에게는 놀라운 혼처를 잡은 사람으로 여겨졌으나, 정작 거래에서 훨씬 손해를 본 쪽으로 드러났다. 결혼한 지 삼 년 만에 아내가 죽었을 때, 그는 처음보다 오히려 가난해진 데다 부양해야 할 아이까지 딸린 처지였기 때문이다. 그렇지만 아이를 키우는 비용에서는 곧 벗어났다. 사내아이는, 어머니의 길고 더딘 병환이라는 마음 누그러뜨리는 명분까지 더해져, 일종의 화해의 매개가 되었다. 자기 자식이 없고, 같은 핏줄로 보살필 만한 다른 어린것도 없던 미스터 처칠과 처칠 부인은, 그녀가 세상을 뜬 지 얼마 되지 않아 어린 프랭크를 통째로 떠맡겠다고 나섰다. 홀아비가 된 아버지로서는 얼마간의 거리낌과 망설임을 느꼈으리라 짐작할 만하다. 그러나 다른 사정들에 밀려 그 마음이 누그러지자, 아이는 처칠 집안의 보살핌과 재산에 맡겨졌고, 그에게는 오직 제 한 몸의 안락을 도모하고 제 처지를 형편 닿는 대로 나아지게 하는 일만 남았다.\n\n삶을 송두리째 바꾸는 것이 바람직해졌다. 그는 민병대를 떠나 장사에 뛰어들었는데, 형제들이 이미 런던에서 자리를 잘 잡아 두어 그에게 유리한 길을 열어 주었다. 적당한 일거리를 가져다주는 정도의 사업이었다. 그는 여전히 하이베리에 작은 집 한 채를 두고, 한가한 날들을 대개 그곳에서 보냈다. 그렇게 쓸모 있는 일과 사교의 즐거움 사이에서, 그 뒤 십팔 년인가 이십 년의 세월이 명랑하게 흘러갔다. 그 무렵이 되자 그는 어지간한 재산을 일구어 두었으니 — 늘 마음에 두고 있던 하이베리 인근의 작은 영지를 사들이기에 넉넉하고 — 미스 테일러만큼이나 지참금 없는 여인을 아내로 맞고도, 다정하고 사교적인 제 성향에 맞추어 살아가기에 넉넉했다.\n\n미스 테일러가 그의 계획에 영향을 미치기 시작한 지도 어느덧 얼마간 되었다. 그러나 그것이 젊은이가 젊은이에게 휘두르는 횡포 같은 영향은 아니었으므로, 랜덜스를 사들이기 전에는 절대로 살림을 차리지 않겠다는 그의 결심을 흔들어 놓지는 못했고, 랜덜스가 매물로 나오기를 오래도록 기다려야 했다. 그러나 그는 이 목표들을 눈앞에 두고 꾸준히 나아가 마침내 그것들을 이루었다. 재산을 일구었고, 집을 사들였으며, 아내를 얻었다. 그리하여 그는 인생의 새로운 시기를 시작하려는 참이었으니, 지금껏 지나온 어느 때보다도 더 큰 행복을 누릴 공산이 다분했다. 그는 한 번도 불행한 사람이었던 적이 없었다. 첫 결혼 때조차 그의 천성이 그를 그런 데서 지켜 주었다. 그러나 두 번째 결혼은 분별 있고 진실로 사랑스러운 여인이 얼마나 즐거운 존재인지 그에게 보여 줄 터였고, 선택받기보다 선택하는 편이, 고마움을 느끼기보다 고마움을 불러일으키는 편이 훨씬 더 낫다는 가장 흐뭇한 증거를 안겨 줄 터였다.\n\n그가 자기 선택에서 만족시켜야 할 사람은 오직 자기 자신뿐이었다. 재산도 온전히 그의 것이었다. 프랭크로 말하자면, 그저 외삼촌의 상속자로 암묵리에 길러지는 정도를 넘어, 성년이 되면 처칠이라는 성을 따르게 할 만큼 공공연한 양자로 인정된 처지였기 때문이다. 그러니 그가 아버지의 도움을 필요로 할 일은 거의 없을 듯했다. 아버지도 그런 걱정은 없었다. 외숙모는 변덕스러운 여자로 남편을 완전히 휘어잡고 있었으나, 그토록 사랑스럽고, 또 그가 믿기로 마땅히 사랑받을 만한 아이에게 그 어떤 변덕인들 영향을 미칠 만큼 강할 수 있다고는, 미스터 웨스턴의 천성으로는 도무지 상상이 되지 않았다. 그는 해마다 런던에서 아들을 만났고 그를 자랑스러워했다. 그가 아주 훌륭한 청년이라고 흐뭇하게 전하는 통에, 하이베리 사람들도 그를 두고 일종의 자랑스러움을 느끼게 되었다. 그는 이 고장에 속한 사람으로 충분히 여겨졌으므로, 그의 장점과 앞날은 일종의 공동의 관심사가 되었다.\n\n미스터 프랭크 처칠은 하이베리의 자랑거리 가운데 하나였고, 그를 한번 보고 싶다는 활기찬 호기심이 널리 퍼져 있었다. 정작 그 호의에 대한 보답은 거의 없어서, 그는 평생 단 한 번도 이곳에 온 적이 없었지만 말이다. 아버지를 보러 온다는 이야기는 자주 오갔으나, 한 번도 실현되지 않았다.\n\n이제 아버지가 결혼한 마당이니, 그 방문이 마땅히 행해져야 할 더없이 적절한 도리라는 의견이 두루 나왔다. 페리 부인이 베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠와 차를 마실 때든, 베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠가 그 답방을 갔을 때든, 이 문제에 대해 이견을 내는 목소리는 하나도 없었다. 이제야말로 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 그들 사이에 와야 할 때였다. 그리고 그가 이 일을 맞아 새어머니에게 편지를 썼다는 사실이 알려지자 그 기대는 한층 굳어졌다. 며칠 동안 하이베리에서 오가는 아침 방문마다, 웨스턴 부인이 받은 그 훌륭한 편지 이야기가 빠지지 않았다. \"미스터 프랭크 처칠이 웨스턴 부인에게 보낸 그 훌륭한 편지 이야기는 들으셨겠지요? 정말이지 아주 훌륭한 편지였다더군요. 미스터 우드하우스가 제게 말씀해 주셨어요. 미스터 우드하우스가 그 편지를 직접 보셨는데, 평생 그렇게 훌륭한 편지는 본 적이 없다고 하시던걸요.\"\n\n그것은 과연 대단히 귀하게 여겨진 편지였다. 웨스턴 부인은 물론 그 청년에 대해 매우 호의적인 인상을 품게 되었다. 그토록 흐뭇한 배려야말로 그의 뛰어난 분별을 보여 주는 거역할 수 없는 증거였고, 그녀의 결혼이 이미 확보해 둔 온갖 축하의 원천과 표현에 더없이 반가운 보탬이 되었다. 그녀는 스스로를 더없이 운 좋은 여인이라 느꼈다. 그리고 자기가 어찌 그토록 운 좋게 여겨질 만한지를 알 만큼 살아온 터였으니, 유일한 아쉬움이라면 자신에 대한 우정이 결코 식은 적 없고 자신과 헤어지기를 못내 아쉬워하는 벗들과 얼마간 떨어지게 된 것뿐이었기 때문이다.\n\n그녀는 때때로 사람들이 자기를 그리워하리라는 것을 알았다. 그리고 자기가 곁에 있어 주지 못해 엠마가 단 하나의 즐거움이라도 잃거나 한 시간이라도 따분함에 시달릴 일을 생각하면 마음이 아팠다. 그러나 사랑스러운 엠마는 결코 나약한 성품이 아니었다. 그녀는 여느 아가씨들보다 제 처지를 잘 감당할 만한 아이였고, 분별과 활력과 기운을 갖추어, 그 자잘한 어려움과 결핍을 무던하고 행복하게 헤쳐 나가리라 기대할 만했다. 게다가 랜덜스가 하트필드에서 아주 만만한 거리에 있어, 여자 혼자 걷기에도 무리가 없다는 점에는 그렇게 위안이 되었고, 미스터 웨스턴의 성품과 형편 또한 그러하여, 다가오는 계절에도 일주일에 절반의 저녁은 함께 보내는 데 아무런 지장이 없을 터였다.\n\n그녀의 처지는 통틀어 웨스턴 부인에게 몇 시간씩 이어지는 감사의 까닭이 되었고, 아쉬움이라야 잠깐잠깐의 순간뿐이었다. 그녀의 만족 — 만족 이상의 것 — 그 명랑한 즐거움이 어찌나 온당하고 또렷한지, 엠마는 아버지를 잘 알면서도, 온갖 안락의 한복판인 랜덜스에 그녀를 두고 올 때나, 저녁이면 유쾌한 남편의 시중을 받으며 제 마차에 오르는 그녀를 볼 때, 아버지가 여전히 \"가엾은 미스 테일러\"라며 가여워할 수 있다는 데에 이따금 놀라곤 했다. 그러나 그녀가 떠날 때면 미스터 우드하우스는 으레 나직이 한숨을 쉬며 이렇게 말하지 않는 법이 없었다. \"아아, 가엾은 미스 테일러! 저 사람은 여기 머물고 싶어 할 텐데.\"\n\n미스 테일러를 되찾을 길은 없었고, 그녀를 가여워하기를 그칠 가망도 별로 없었다. 그러나 몇 주가 지나자 미스터 우드하우스에게도 얼마간 위안이 찾아왔다. 이웃들의 인사치레도 끝났고, 그토록 슬픈 일을 두고 기뻐하라는 덕담에 시달릴 일도 더는 없었으며, 그를 큰 고통에 빠뜨렸던 그 혼례 케이크도 죄다 먹어 치워졌다. 그 자신의 위장은 기름진 것을 하나도 받아들이지 못했고, 그는 남들이 자기와 다를 수 있다고는 도무지 믿지 못했다. 자기에게 해로운 것이면 그 누구에게도 마땅찮은 것이라 여겼으므로, 그는 혼례 케이크 따위는 아예 만들지 말라고 간곡히 만류하려 애썼고, 그것이 헛수고로 돌아가자, 이번에는 아무도 그것을 먹지 못하게 하려고 한사코 애를 썼다. 그는 이 문제를 두고 약제사 미스터 페리에게 의견을 구하는 수고까지 마다하지 않았다. 미스터 페리는 총명하고 신사다운 사람으로, 그가 자주 드나드는 것이 미스터 우드하우스의 삶의 위안 가운데 하나였는데, 막상 물음을 받자 (자기 뜻과는 다소 어긋나는 듯 보였음에도) 인정하지 않을 수 없었으니, 혼례 케이크란 적당히 먹지 않는 한 많은 사람에게 — 어쩌면 대다수에게 — 분명 탈을 낼 수 있다는 것이었다. 자기 견해를 뒷받침하는 이런 소견을 손에 넣은 미스터 우드하우스는, 그 신혼부부를 찾는 손님마다 이를 일러 영향을 미치고 싶어 했다. 그러나 케이크는 그래도 다 먹혔고, 그것이 죄다 없어지기까지 그의 어진 신경에는 도무지 안식이 없었다.\n\n하이베리에는 페리네 어린것들이 저마다 웨스턴 부인의 혼례 케이크를 한 조각씩 손에 들고 있는 것을 보았다는 이상한 소문이 돌았다. 그러나 미스터 우드하우스는 결코 그 말을 믿으려 들지 않았다.\n\n# 제1권 3장\n\n미스터 우드하우스는 그 나름의 방식으로 사람들과 어울리기를 좋아했다. 벗들이 찾아와 만나는 것을 무척 즐겼는데, 여러 가지가 한데 어우러진 까닭에 — 하트필드에서 오래 살아온 데다 천성이 너그러웠고, 거기에 재산과 저택과 딸까지 더해진 덕에 — 자신의 작은 교우 모임의 방문만큼은 대개 제 뜻대로 청할 수 있었다. 그 모임 너머의 어느 집안과도 그는 그리 왕래가 없었다. 늦은 시간과 큰 만찬을 질색하는 탓에, 그가 정한 조건대로 찾아와 줄 사람이 아니고서는 누구와도 사귈 수 없었던 것이다. 다행히도 하이베리는 — 같은 교구에 속한 랜덜스와, 미스터 나이틀리의 영지인 이웃 교구 돈웰 애비까지 아울러 — 그런 사람을 여럿 품고 있었다. 엠마가 권하는 덕에 그는 골라 뽑은 가장 좋은 손님 몇을 만찬에 부르는 일도 드물지 않았으나, 그가 더 좋아한 것은 저녁 모임이었다. 그리고 그가 어쩌다 사람 만날 기운이 없다 싶지 않는 한, 일주일 가운데 엠마가 그를 위해 카드판 하나 짜 주지 못할 저녁은 거의 없었다.\n\n웨스턴 부부와 미스터 나이틀리는 진실하고 오래된 정으로 찾아왔다. 그리고 혼자 사는 것을 달가워하지 않는 청년 미스터 엘턴으로 말하자면, 텅 빈 고독으로 비어 있는 제 저녁 한때를 미스터 우드하우스의 응접실의 우아함과 사교, 그리고 그 사랑스러운 딸의 미소와 맞바꾸는 특권을 허투루 흘려보낼 까닭이 없었다.\n\n이들 다음으로는 두 번째 부류가 있었으니, 그중에서도 가장 부르기 좋은 이들이 베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠, 그리고 고더드 부인 — 하트필드의 초대라면 거의 언제든 응할 채비가 되어 있는 세 여인이었다. 어찌나 자주 마차로 모셔 오고 모셔 가는지, 미스터 우드하우스는 그것이 제임스에게도 말들에게도 고된 일이 아니라고 여겼다. 그것이 일 년에 단 한 번뿐인 일이었다면 짐스럽게 느꼈을 테지만.\n\n베이츠 부인은 하이베리의 전임 교구 목사를 여읜 미망인으로, 차와 카드리유*를 빼면 거의 무엇도 할 수 없는 매우 늙은 부인이었다. 그녀는 시집 못 간 딸과 함께 아주 검소하게 살았고, 그처럼 딱한 처지에 놓인 무해한 노부인이 불러일으킬 만한 온갖 호의와 존경을 한 몸에 받았다. 그 딸은 젊지도, 곱지도, 부유하지도, 결혼도 하지 않은 여자치고는 더없이 드물 만큼 인기를 누렸다. 미스 베이츠는 세상의 호의를 듬뿍 받기에는 이보다 더 나쁠 수 없는 처지에 놓여 있었다. 게다가 그 부족함을 스스로 메워 주거나, 그녀를 미워할지 모를 이들을 겉으로나마 굽히게 할 만한 지적 우월함도 없었다. 그녀는 미모도 영민함도 자랑해 본 적이 없었다. 젊은 날은 이렇다 할 두각 없이 흘러갔고, 중년은 쇠약해 가는 어머니를 돌보는 일과, 변변찮은 수입을 한 푼이라도 더 늘려 쓰려는 안간힘에 바쳐졌다. 그런데도 그녀는 행복한 여자였고, 누구도 호의 없이는 그 이름을 입에 올리지 않는 여자였다. 그처럼 놀라운 일을 빚어낸 것은 다름 아닌 그녀 자신의 두루 미치는 선의와 만족할 줄 아는 마음이었다. 그녀는 모든 사람을 사랑했고, 모든 사람의 행복에 마음을 썼으며, 모든 사람의 장점을 재빨리 알아보았다. 자신을 더없이 운 좋은 사람으로 여겼고, 그토록 훌륭한 어머니와 그 많은 좋은 이웃과 벗, 그리고 무엇 하나 부족함 없는 보금자리로 둘러싸인 복받은 사람이라 생각했다. 그 천성의 꾸밈없음과 쾌활함, 만족할 줄 알고 감사할 줄 아는 마음은 누구에게나 그녀를 추천할 만한 것이었으며, 그녀 자신에게는 행복의 금광이었다. 그녀는 사소한 일을 두고 끝없이 떠드는 수다쟁이였는데, 별것 아닌 전언과 무해한 잡담으로 가득 찬 그 말솜씨가 미스터 우드하우스에게는 더할 나위 없이 잘 맞았다.\n* 카드리유: 네 사람이 하는 옛 카드놀이.\n\n고더드 부인은 어느 학교의 교장이었다 — 그러나 그것은 신식 원리니 신식 제도니 하며, 세련된 헛소리를 늘어놓는 긴 문장으로 교양과 우아한 도덕을 결합한다고 떠벌리는, 그리고 어린 아가씨들이 엄청난 수업료를 내고 건강을 쥐어짜내 허영심만 부풀려 가는 그런 학원이나 양성소 따위가 아니었다 — 그저 진짜배기의 정직하고 구식인 기숙학교로, 거기서는 적당한 양의 교양을 적당한 값에 팔았고, 거기로 보내진 여자아이들은 거추장스럽지 않게 떨어져 있으면서 약간의 교육을 그럭저럭 주워 담을 뿐, 무슨 신동이 되어 돌아올 위험 따위는 결코 없었다. 고더드 부인의 학교는 평판이 아주 좋았는데 — 마땅히 그럴 만했다. 하이베리는 유난히 건강에 좋은 곳으로 꼽혔으니 말이다. 그녀에게는 널찍한 집과 정원이 있었고, 아이들에게 건강에 좋은 음식을 넉넉히 먹였으며, 여름에는 마음껏 뛰놀게 했고, 겨울에는 손수 아이들의 동상을 싸매 주었다. 지금 스무 쌍의 처녀들이 줄지어 그녀를 뒤따라 교회에 가는 것도 놀랄 일이 아니었다. 그녀는 수수하고 어머니 같은 여인으로, 젊어서 모질게 일했고 이제는 가끔 차 모임에 나가 쉬어도 좋을 자격이 있다고 여겼다. 그리고 지난날 미스터 우드하우스의 친절에 많은 신세를 진 터라, 자수품으로 빙 둘러 꾸민 그 정갈한 거실을 형편 닿는 대로 마다하고 나와, 그의 난롯가에서 몇 닢의 동전을 따거나 잃어 주는 것을 그에 대한 각별한 도리로 여겼다.\n\n이들이 바로 엠마가 매우 자주 불러 모을 수 있었던 부인들이었다. 그녀는 아버지를 위해 그럴 힘이 있다는 것이 기뻤다. 다만 그녀 자신으로 말하자면, 그것이 웨스턴 부인의 부재를 달래 주는 약은 못 되었다. 아버지가 편안해 보이는 것이 반가웠고, 일을 그토록 잘 꾸려 낸 자신이 무척 대견하기도 했다. 그러나 그런 세 여인의 잔잔한 푸념과 늘어놓는 이야기를 듣고 있노라면, 그렇게 보낸 저녁 하나하나가 그녀가 두려워하며 내다보았던 바로 그 기나긴 저녁임을 새삼 절감하게 되었다.\n\n어느 날 아침 그녀가 앉아서 오늘 하루도 꼭 그렇게 저물겠거니 내다보고 있을 때, 고더드 부인에게서 쪽지 한 장이 왔다. 더없이 정중한 말로 미스 스미스를 데려와도 좋겠느냐는 청이었다 — 더없이 반가운 청이었다. 미스 스미스는 열일곱 살 난 아가씨로, 엠마는 그 얼굴은 익히 알고 있었고, 그 미모 덕에 오래전부터 관심을 두어 온 터였다. 아주 흔쾌한 초대의 답이 갔고, 이 저택의 아름다운 안주인은 더 이상 그 저녁을 두려워하지 않게 되었다.\n\n해리엇 스미스는 누군가의 사생아였다. 몇 해 전 누군가가 그녀를 고더드 부인의 학교에 들여보냈고, 근래에 누군가가 그녀를 일반 학생의 신분에서 거실 기숙생의 자리로 올려 주었다. 그녀의 내력에 관해 세상에 알려진 것은 그게 전부였다. 하이베리에서 사귄 사람들 말고는 이렇다 할 친구도 없었으며, 지금은 그 학교에서 함께 지냈던 몇몇 아가씨를 만나러 시골에 오래 다녀온 길이었다.\n\n그녀는 무척 예쁜 아가씨였고, 그 아름다움이 마침 엠마가 특히 좋아하는 부류의 것이었다. 키가 자그마하고 통통하며 살결이 희고 발그레한 혈색에, 푸른 눈과 옅은 빛깔의 머리칼, 단정한 이목구비, 그리고 더없이 사랑스러운 표정을 지니고 있었다. 저녁이 다 가기도 전에 엠마는 그 용모만큼이나 몸가짐에도 흡족해져, 이 교제를 이어 가기로 단단히 마음먹었다.\n\n미스 스미스의 대화에서 무슨 눈에 띄게 영리한 구석에 감탄한 것은 아니었으나, 엠마는 그녀가 통틀어 무척 마음을 끄는 사람임을 알았다 — 거북할 만큼 수줍어하지도 않고, 말하기를 꺼리지도 않으면서, 그러면서도 결코 나서는 법이 없고, 더없이 적절하고 어울리는 공손함을 보이며, 하트필드에 받아들여진 것을 그토록 사랑스럽게 고마워하고, 평소에 익숙했던 것보다 한층 격조 높은 만사의 풍경에 그토록 꾸밈없이 감탄하는 모습이었다. 그러니 분별이 있는 게 분명하고, 북돋아 줄 만한 사람이었다. 마땅히 북돋아 주어야 했다. 저 부드러운 푸른 눈과 저 모든 타고난 우아함을, 하이베리와 그 언저리의 변변찮은 사교에 헛되이 흘려보내서는 안 될 일이었다. 그녀가 이미 맺은 교제는 그녀에게 어울리지 않았다. 그녀가 방금 헤어지고 온 그 친구들은, 비록 퍽 괜찮은 사람들이라 해도, 그녀에게 해가 되고 있는 게 분명했다. 그들은 마틴이라는 집안 사람들로, 엠마는 그 평판을 익히 알고 있었으니 — 미스터 나이틀리의 큰 농장을 빌려 돈웰 교구에서 사는 이들이었고, 그녀가 보기에 꽤 떳떳한 살림이었으며, 미스터 나이틀리가 그들을 높이 친다는 것도 알고 있었다 — 그래도 그들은 거칠고 다듬어지지 않은 사람들임이 분명했고, 조금만 더 지식과 우아함을 갖추면 흠잡을 데 없이 완벽해질 아가씨와 가깝게 지내기에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 이들이었다. 자기가 그녀를 보살펴 주리라. 자기가 그녀를 다듬어 주리라. 그 못된 교제에서 떼어 내어 좋은 사교계로 이끌어 들이리라. 그녀의 견해와 몸가짐을 자기가 빚어 주리라. 흥미로운 일이요, 분명히 더없이 친절한 사업이 될 터였다. 그녀 자신의 생활 형편과 여유와 능력에 썩 잘 어울리는 일이었다.\n\n그녀는 그 부드러운 푸른 눈에 감탄하랴, 이야기하고 또 들으랴, 그 사이사이로 이 모든 계획을 짜내랴 어찌나 분주했던지, 저녁나절이 여느 때와 사뭇 다른 속도로 휙 지나가 버렸다. 늘 그런 모임의 끝을 알리며 차려지던 야식상은 — 평소 같으면 적당한 때를 가늠하며 지켜보고 앉아 있던 그 상은 — 어느새 다 차려져 난로 쪽으로 옮겨질 때까지도 그녀는 미처 알아채지 못했다. 매사를 잘, 빈틈없이 해내는 평판에는 결코 무심하지 않으면서도, 평소의 충동을 넘어서는 민첩함으로, 제 생각에 스스로 흐뭇해하는 마음에서 우러난 진정한 선의로, 그녀는 그제야 식사 자리의 온갖 시중을 도맡았다. 다진 닭고기와 굴 그라탕을 손님들에게 권하며 덜어 주었는데, 그 권하는 정성이 손님들의 이른 잠자리 습관과 예의 바른 사양심에도 기꺼이 받아들여지리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다.\n\n이런 자리에서 가엾은 미스터 우드하우스의 심사는 안타까운 싸움을 벌였다. 그는 식탁보를 까는 것을 좋아했으니, 그것이 그의 젊은 시절 풍습이었기 때문이다. 그러나 야식이 건강에 매우 해롭다는 그의 신념 탓에, 그 위에 무엇이 차려지는 것을 보면 도리어 마음이 언짢았다. 손님 대접하는 마음으로야 무엇이든 다 내놓고 싶었지만, 그들의 건강을 염려하는 마음에 그들이 먹는다는 것 자체가 안타까웠던 것이다.\n\n자기가 드는 것과 똑같은, 그런 묽은 죽 한 그릇 정도라야 그가 떳떳한 마음으로 권할 수 있는 전부였다. 그래도 부인들이 더 좋은 음식들을 흐뭇하게 비워 내는 동안, 그는 애써 자신을 다잡으며 이렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"베이츠 부인, 이 달걀을 하나 드셔 보시지요. 아주 살짝 삶은 달걀은 해롭지 않답니다. 설이 달걀 삶는 솜씨는 누구보다 낫지요. 다른 사람이 삶은 달걀이라면 권하지 않겠습니다만, 염려 마세요, 보시다시피 아주 작은 것들이라 — 우리 집 작은 달걀 하나쯤이야 해롭지 않을 겁니다. 미스 베이츠, 엠마가 타르트를 조금만 덜어 드리게 할게요 — 아주 조금만요. 우리 건 다 사과 타르트랍니다. 여기서는 몸에 해로운 절임 따위는 걱정 안 하셔도 돼요. 커스터드는 권하지 않겠어요. 고더드 부인, 포도주 반 잔쯤은 어떠십니까? 작은 잔으로 반 잔을, 물 채운 컵에 타서요? 그 정도면 부인께 거슬리지 않을 것 같은데요.\"\n\n엠마는 아버지가 떠들게 내버려 두었다 — 그러면서도 손님들에게는 훨씬 만족스러운 방식으로 음식을 차려 냈고, 오늘 저녁에는 그들을 행복하게 돌려보내는 데서 각별한 기쁨을 느꼈다. 미스 스미스의 행복은 엠마가 의도한 그대로였다. 미스 우드하우스는 하이베리에서 워낙 대단한 인물인지라, 그녀에게 소개된다는 전망은 기쁨만큼이나 큰 공포를 안겨 주었던 것이다. 그러나 겸손하고 감사할 줄 아는 이 어린 아가씨는 더없이 흐뭇한 마음을 안고 떠나갔으니, 미스 우드하우스가 저녁 내내 보여 준 다정함에 감격했고, 마지막에는 정말로 그녀와 손까지 맞잡았던 것이다!\n\n# 제1권 4장\n\n해리엇 스미스가 하트필드에 드나드는 일은 곧 기정사실이 되었다. 매사에 빠르고 결단력 있는 엠마는 지체 없이 그녀를 초대하고 부추기고 자주 오라고 일렀으며, 친분이 깊어질수록 서로에 대한 흡족함도 깊어 갔다. 산책 동무로서 엠마는 일찍부터 그녀가 얼마나 쓸모 있을지를 내다보고 있었다. 그 점에서 웨스턴 부인의 빈자리는 컸다. 아버지는 관목 숲 밖으로는 결코 나가지 않았고, 철에 따라 긴 산책이든 짧은 산책이든 그곳을 두 갈래로 나눈 길로 족했다. 그리고 웨스턴 부인이 결혼한 뒤로 그녀의 운동은 너무 좁은 데 갇혀 있었다. 한번은 혼자 랜덜스까지 가 보았으나 즐겁지 않았다. 그러니 언제든 불러내어 산책을 청할 수 있는 해리엇 스미스 같은 사람은 그녀의 특권에 더해질 값진 보탬이었다. 그러나 어느 모로 보나, 그녀를 알아 갈수록 엠마는 그녀를 마음에 들어 했고, 자신의 온갖 자애로운 계획에 더욱 확신을 굳혔다.\n\n해리엇은 분명 영리하지는 않았으나, 상냥하고 고분고분하며 고마움을 아는 천성에, 우쭐대는 구석이라곤 전혀 없었고, 그저 자신이 우러러보는 누군가에게 이끌리기만을 바랐다. 일찌감치 엠마에게 보인 애착은 더없이 사랑스러웠다. 그리고 좋은 사람들과 어울리려는 성향과, 우아하고 영리한 것을 알아보는 능력은, 이해력의 깊이까지는 바랄 수 없을지언정 취향이 모자라지는 않음을 보여 주었다. 무엇보다도 엠마는, 해리엇 스미스야말로 자신이 바라던 바로 그 어린 벗이라고 — 자기 집이 필요로 하던 바로 그 무엇이라고 확신했다. 웨스턴 부인 같은 벗은 애초에 말이 안 되었다. 그런 사람이 둘이나 주어질 리 없었다. 그런 사람이 둘이나 필요하지도 않았다. 이것은 전혀 다른 종류의 것, 별개의 독립된 감정이었다. 웨스턴 부인은 고마움과 존경에 뿌리를 둔 애정의 대상이었다. 해리엇은 자신이 도움을 줄 수 있는 사람으로서 사랑받을 터였다. 웨스턴 부인에게는 해 줄 일이 없었으나, 해리엇에게는 무엇이든 해 줄 일이 있었다.\n\n쓸모 있는 사람이 되어 보려는 그녀의 첫 시도는 부모가 누구인지 알아내려는 노력이었으나, 해리엇은 말해 줄 수가 없었다. 자기가 할 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 다 말할 채비가 되어 있었지만, 이 문제에서는 물어봐야 헛일이었다. 엠마는 좋을 대로 상상하는 수밖에 없었다 — 그러나 같은 처지였다면 자기라면 진실을 캐내지 못했을 리 없다는 생각만은 결코 버릴 수 없었다. 해리엇에게는 통찰이 없었다. 그녀는 고더드 부인이 골라 들려주는 말만 듣고 믿는 데 만족했고, 그 너머는 들여다보지 않았다.\n\n고더드 부인, 그리고 선생들과 여학생들과 학교의 갖가지 일들이 자연히 대화의 큰 부분을 차지했다 — 그리고 애비 밀 농장의 마틴 댁과 친분이 없었다면, 그것이 대화의 전부였을 것이다. 그러나 마틴 댁은 그녀의 마음을 적잖이 차지하고 있었다. 그녀는 그 집에서 무척 행복한 두 달을 보냈고, 이제는 그 방문의 즐거움을 이야기하고 그곳의 갖은 안락함과 신기함을 늘어놓기를 좋아했다. 엠마는 그녀의 수다를 부추겼다 — 또 다른 부류의 인간 군상을 그린 그런 그림이 재미있었고, 마틴 부인에게 \"거실이 둘이나, 그것도 아주 훌륭한 거실이 둘이나 있는데, 그중 하나는 고더드 부인의 응접실만큼이나 넓다는 것, 마틴 부인 곁에서 스물다섯 해를 산 상급 하녀가 있다는 것, 소가 여덟 마리인데 그중 둘은 올더니종이고 한 마리는 작은 웨일스 소로 정말이지 아주 예쁘장한 작은 웨일스 소라는 것, 해리엇이 그 소를 어찌나 아끼는지 마틴 부인이 그건 해리엇의 소라고 불러야겠다고 말했다는 것, 정원에 아주 근사한 정자가 있어서 내년 어느 날 다들 그곳에서 차를 마실 거라는 것 — 열두 사람은 너끈히 들어갈 만큼 큰, 아주 근사한 정자라는 것\"을 이토록 의기양양하게 떠벌릴 수 있는 그 앳된 천진함이 즐거웠던 것이다.\n\n한동안 엠마는 그 직접적인 원인 너머는 생각하지 않은 채 재미있어했다. 그러나 그 집안을 더 잘 알게 되면서 다른 감정이 일었다. 그녀는 잘못된 생각을 품고 있었으니, 어머니와 딸, 아들과 며느리가 모두 함께 사는 줄로 짐작했던 것이다. 그러나 그 이야기에서 한몫을 차지하며 늘 무언가에 베푼 그 큰 호의를 칭찬받곤 하던 그 미스터 마틴이 독신 남자라는 것 — 젊은 마틴 부인이라곤 없고, 이 경우에 아내라곤 없다는 것이 드러나자, 그녀는 이 온갖 환대와 친절이 가엾은 어린 벗에게 위험이 될까 봐 의심했고, 잘 지켜 주지 않으면 해리엇이 영영 제 신분을 떨어뜨리게 될지도 모른다고 생각했다.\n\n이 가슴 부푸는 생각에 힘입어, 그녀의 질문은 수와 의도가 부쩍 늘었다. 그녀는 특히 해리엇이 미스터 마틴 이야기를 더 하도록 이끌었고, 해리엇은 그것을 싫어하는 기색이 전혀 없었다. 해리엇은 달빛 산책과 흥겨운 저녁놀이에서 그가 한몫했던 일을 기꺼이 이야기했고, 그가 어찌나 쾌활하고 친절한지를 한참이나 늘어놓았다. 한번은 그녀가 호두를 좋아한다고 말했다는 이유로, 그가 호두를 가져다주려고 삼 마일이나 빙 돌아 다녀왔으며, 다른 모든 일에서도 그토록 친절했다는 것이다. 어느 날 밤에는 그녀에게 노래를 들려주려고 일부러 목동의 아들을 거실로 불러들였다고 했다. 그녀는 노래를 무척 좋아했다. 그도 노래를 조금 부를 줄 알았다. 그녀가 보기에 그는 아주 영리하고 무엇이든 다 아는 사람이었다. 그는 아주 훌륭한 양 떼를 거느렸고, 그녀가 그 집에 머무는 동안 그의 양털값으로 이 고장 누구보다도 높은 값이 매겨졌다. 그녀가 보기에 모두가 그를 좋게 말했다. 어머니와 누이들도 그를 무척 아꼈다. 어느 날 마틴 부인은 그녀에게 (이 말을 할 때 해리엇은 얼굴을 붉혔다) 누구도 그보다 더 좋은 아들일 수는 없으니, 그가 언제 결혼하든 분명 좋은 남편이 될 거라고 말했다고 했다. 그렇다고 마틴 부인이 그를 결혼시키고 싶어 한 건 아니라고 했다. 전혀 서두를 게 없다고 했다는 것이다.\n\n\"잘하셨어요, 마틴 부인!\" 엠마는 속으로 생각했다. \"댁이 무슨 속셈인지 다 알겠네요.\"\n\n\"그리고 제가 떠나올 때, 마틴 부인은 어찌나 친절하신지 고더드 부인께 멋진 거위를 한 마리 보내 주셨어요 — 고더드 부인이 여태 본 중에 가장 좋은 거위였어요. 고더드 부인은 그걸 일요일에 요리해서 선생님 세 분을, 미스 내시랑 미스 프린스랑 미스 리처드슨을 모두 저녁 식사에 부르셨어요.\"\n\n\"미스터 마틴은 아마 제 일 분야 말고는 별로 아는 게 없는 사람이겠지? 책은 안 읽고?\"\n\n\"아, 읽어요! — 그러니까, 아니 — 모르겠어요 — 하지만 제 생각엔 꽤 많이 읽은 것 같아요 — 미스 우드하우스가 대단하게 여길 만한 건 아니지만요. 농업 보고서를 읽고, 창가 자리에 놓여 있던 다른 책들도 몇 권 읽어요 — 그래도 그건 다 혼자 읽는 거예요. 하지만 가끔 저녁에, 우리가 카드놀이를 하기 전에 그분이 『우아한 발췌집』에서 무언가를 소리 내어 읽어 주곤 했는데, 아주 재미있었어요. 그리고 그분이 『웨이크필드의 목사』를 읽은 것도 알아요. 『숲속의 로맨스』나 『수도원의 아이들』은 한 번도 안 읽었고요. 제가 말하기 전엔 그런 책이 있는 줄도 몰랐지만, 이제는 할 수 있는 대로 빨리 그 책들을 구해 보겠다고 단단히 마음먹었어요.\"\n\n다음 질문은 이러했다 —\n\n\"미스터 마틴은 어떻게 생긴 사람이야?\"\n\n\"아! 잘생기진 않았어요 — 전혀 잘생기진 않았죠. 처음엔 아주 못생겼다고 생각했는데, 지금은 그렇게 못생겼다고 생각하지 않아요. 아시다시피 시간이 좀 지나면 그렇게 안 되잖아요. 그런데 한 번도 그분을 못 보셨어요? 그분은 이따금 하이베리에 오고, 매주 킹스턴에 가는 길에 꼭 말을 타고 지나가요. 미스 우드하우스 곁을 아주 여러 번 지나갔는걸요.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠지. 쉰 번쯤 봤을지도 모르고. 하지만 이름은 도통 알 길이 없었어. 젊은 농부란, 말을 탔든 걸어가든, 내 호기심을 끌 만한 사람으론 가장 거리가 먼 부류거든. 자영농이야말로 내가 도무지 상관할 일이 없다고 느끼는 바로 그 계층이고. 한두 단계만 더 낮으면, 또 행색이 그럴듯하면 내 관심을 끌지도 모르지. 그런 사람들 집안에는 어떤 식으로든 내가 도움이 되어 줄 수 있기를 바라기도 할 테고. 하지만 농부는 내 도움이 필요할 게 없으니, 어떤 의미에서는 내 눈길이 미칠 데 너머에 있는 셈이야 — 다른 모든 면에서는 내 눈길 아래에 있는 만큼이나 말이지.\"\n\n\"그럼요. 아, 네! 미스 우드하우스가 그분을 눈여겨봤을 리 없겠지요. 하지만 그분은 미스 우드하우스를 정말 잘 알아요 — 그러니까 얼굴은요.\"\n\n\"그가 아주 점잖은 청년이라는 건 의심하지 않아. 정말이지 그렇다는 걸 알고, 그러니 그가 잘되기를 바라지. 그 사람 나이는 몇이나 됐을 것 같아?\"\n\n\"지난 유월 팔 일에 스물네 살이 됐고, 제 생일은 그달 이십삼 일이니 딱 보름하고 하루 차이예요 — 참 신기하죠.\"\n\n\"겨우 스물넷. 자리 잡기엔 너무 어리네. 그분 어머니가 서두르지 않는 건 더없이 옳은 일이야. 지금 그대로도 아주 편안해 보이는데, 어머니가 굳이 그를 결혼시키려고 애쓰면 아마 후회하게 될걸. 한 육 년쯤 뒤에, 자기와 같은 신분에 돈도 조금 있는 괜찮은 색싯감을 만난다면 모를까, 그땐 아주 바람직하겠지.\"\n\n\"육 년 뒤요! 어머나, 미스 우드하우스, 그럼 그분은 서른 살이 되잖아요!\"\n\n\"그래, 그게 독립적인 재산을 타고나지 못한 남자 대다수가 결혼할 형편이 되는 가장 이른 나이야. 내 짐작에 미스터 마틴은 제 재산을 온전히 처음부터 일궈야 하는 처지일 테고 — 세상에서 한 발도 앞서 있지 못할 테지. 아버지가 돌아가실 때 얼마간 물려받든, 집안 재산에서 제 몫이 얼마든, 그건 분명 죄다 묶여 있고, 죄다 가축이며 그런 데 들어가 있을 거야. 그러니 부지런함과 운이 따른다면 언젠가는 부자가 될지 몰라도, 아직 무언가를 실하게 모아 두었을 리는 거의 없지.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 정말 그래요. 하지만 그분들은 아주 편안하게 살아요. 집안 남자 일꾼은 없지만, 그것 말고는 부족한 게 없거든요. 그리고 마틴 부인은 내년에 사내아이를 하나 들일까 한다고 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"해리엇, 그가 언제 결혼하든 네가 곤란한 처지에 빠지지 않기를 바라 — 그러니까 그의 아내와 사귀게 되는 일에서 말이야. 그의 누이들이야 교육을 더 받았으니 아주 못 사귈 사람은 아니라 해도, 그렇다고 그가 네가 알 만한 사람을 아내로 맞으리란 보장은 없으니까. 네 출생의 불운을 생각하면 너는 사귀는 사람을 각별히 가려야 마땅해. 네가 신사의 딸이라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없으니, 너는 네 힘이 닿는 온갖 방법으로 그 신분에 대한 권리를 지켜야 해. 안 그러면 너를 깎아내리며 고소해할 사람이 수두룩할 테니까.\"\n\n\"네, 그럼요, 그런 사람들이 있겠지요. 하지만 제가 하트필드에 드나들고, 미스 우드하우스가 이렇게 잘해 주시는 한, 누가 무슨 짓을 한들 저는 두렵지 않아요.\"\n\n\"영향력의 힘을 제법 잘 아는구나, 해리엇. 하지만 나는 네가 좋은 사람들 틈에 단단히 자리를 잡아서, 하트필드와 미스 우드하우스에게마저 기대지 않아도 될 만큼 되었으면 좋겠어. 나는 네가 줄곧 좋은 인연을 맺고 사는 걸 보고 싶고, 그러자면 별난 친지는 되도록 적게 두는 게 좋아. 그래서 하는 말인데, 미스터 마틴이 결혼할 때 네가 여전히 이 고장에 있다면, 그의 누이들과 친하다는 이유로 그 아내와 사귀게 되는 일에 끌려들지 않았으면 해. 그 아내라는 사람은 십중팔구 교육이라곤 못 받은 그저 그런 농부의 딸일 테니까.\"\n\n\"그럼요. 네. 그렇다고 제가 미스터 마틴이 교육도 좀 받고 아주 잘 자란 사람 말고 다른 누구와 결혼하리라 생각하는 건 아니에요 — 하지만 제 의견을 미스 우드하우스 의견에 맞세울 생각은 없어요 — 그리고 그분 아내와 사귀고 싶어 하지 않을 게 분명하고요. 저는 늘 마틴 자매를, 특히 엘리자베스를 무척 좋아할 거고, 그분들과 멀어진다면 정말 슬플 거예요. 그분들은 저만큼이나 교육을 잘 받았으니까요. 하지만 그분이 아주 무지하고 천박한 여자와 결혼한다면, 가능한 한 그 여자네는 안 찾아가는 게 분명 나을 거예요.\"\n\n엠마는 이 말의 갈피갈피를 헤아리며 해리엇을 살폈으나, 사랑에 빠진 위태로운 징후는 보이지 않았다. 그 청년이 첫 흠모자이긴 했어도, 그녀가 보기에 달리 마음을 붙들린 데는 없었고, 자신이 마련해 줄 어떤 다정한 혼처에 맞설 만한 심각한 걸림돌이 해리엇 쪽에 있지도 않을 듯했다.\n\n바로 다음 날, 돈웰 길을 걷다가 두 사람은 미스터 마틴과 마주쳤다. 그는 걸어오고 있었는데, 엠마를 아주 공손하게 바라본 뒤, 그 동행을 더없이 꾸밈없는 흡족함으로 바라보았다. 엠마는 이렇게 살펴볼 기회가 생긴 것이 싫지 않았다. 두 사람이 이야기를 나누는 동안 그녀는 몇 야드쯤 앞서 걸으면서, 곧 그 빠른 눈으로 로버트 마틴이라는 인물을 충분히 파악했다. 그의 행색은 아주 깔끔했고, 분별 있는 청년처럼 보였으나, 그 사람됨에는 그 밖의 장점이라곤 없었다. 그리고 신사들과 견주게 되면, 그가 해리엇의 호감 속에서 얻어 둔 입지를 죄다 잃고 말리라고 그녀는 생각했다. 해리엇은 태도에 둔감하지 않았다. 그녀는 엠마 아버지의 부드러움을 누가 시키지도 않았는데 감탄과 함께 신기해하며 알아본 적이 있었다. 그런데 미스터 마틴은 태도라는 게 무엇인지 도무지 모르는 사람처럼 보였다.\n\n미스 우드하우스를 기다리게 해서는 안 되었기에 두 사람은 잠깐만 함께 있었다. 그러고 나서 해리엇은 미소 띤 얼굴로, 들뜬 마음에 설레며 엠마에게 달려왔다 — 미스 우드하우스가 머잖아 가라앉혀 주기를 바라는 그 설렘으로.\n\n\"우리가 그분과 마주치다니, 생각 좀 해 보세요! — 정말 신기하죠! 그분 말로는 랜덜스 쪽으로 돌아가지 않은 게 순전히 우연이었대요. 우리가 이 길로 걸으리라곤 생각도 못 했대요. 우리가 거의 매일 랜덜스 쪽으로 걷는 줄 알았다는 거예요. 그분은 아직 『숲속의 로맨스』를 못 구하셨대요. 지난번 킹스턴에 갔을 때 너무 바빠서 깜빡 잊으셨다는데, 내일 또 가신대요. 우리가 마주치다니 정말 신기하죠! 그런데 미스 우드하우스, 그분이 생각하셨던 그대로인가요? 어떻게 보세요? 정말 그렇게 못생겼다고 보세요?\"\n\n\"못생긴 건 분명해 — 두드러지게 못생겼지. 하지만 그건 그 사람한테 품위라곤 전혀 없다는 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니야. 내가 많은 걸 기대할 권리야 없었고, 많이 기대하지도 않았어. 하지만 그가 그렇게까지 촌스러울 줄은, 그렇게까지 풍채라곤 없을 줄은 미처 몰랐어. 솔직히 나는 그가 한두 단계는 품위에 더 가까운 사람일 줄 알았거든.\"\n\n\"그렇긴 해요.\" 해리엇이 풀 죽은 목소리로 말했다. \"진짜 신사들만큼 품위 있진 않죠.\"\n\n\"해리엇, 너는 우리와 알고 지내면서 그런 진짜 신사들과 거듭 자리를 함께해 왔으니, 미스터 마틴의 차이가 너 스스로도 눈에 띌 거야. 하트필드에서 너는 잘 배우고 잘 자란 남자들의 아주 훌륭한 본보기를 봐 왔잖아. 그런 사람들을 보고 난 다음에 다시 미스터 마틴과 자리를 함께하면서도 그가 한참 못한 사람이라는 걸 못 알아본다면, 또 전에는 그를 어떻게 그래도 괜찮은 사람이라 여겼었나 스스로 의아해하지 않는다면, 나는 깜짝 놀랄 거야. 이제 그런 게 느껴지기 시작하지 않니? 눈에 확 들어오지 않았어? 그 어색한 표정과 퉁명스러운 태도, 그리고 내가 여기 서서 들으니 가락이라곤 도무지 없던 그 거친 목소리에 분명 너도 깜짝 놀랐을 텐데.\"\n\n\"확실히 그분은 미스터 나이틀리 같진 않아요. 미스터 나이틀리처럼 그렇게 멋진 풍채와 걸음걸이는 못 가졌죠. 그 차이는 저도 똑똑히 알겠어요. 하지만 미스터 나이틀리는 정말이지 아주 훌륭한 분이잖아요!\"\n\n\"미스터 나이틀리의 풍채야 워낙 두드러지게 좋으니, 미스터 마틴을 그분과 견주는 건 공평하지 않아. 미스터 나이틀리만큼 '신사'라고 또렷이 적혀 있는 사람은 백에 하나 볼까 말까일걸. 하지만 네가 요즘 만난 신사가 그분만 있는 건 아니잖아. 미스터 웨스턴이랑 미스터 엘턴은 어떻게 생각해? 미스터 마틴을 그 두 분 가운데 어느 쪽과든 견줘 봐. 몸가짐을, 걸음걸이를, 말투를, 또 입을 다물고 있는 모습을 견줘 보라고. 그 차이가 분명 보일 거야.\"\n\n\"아, 네! — 큰 차이가 있죠. 하지만 미스터 웨스턴은 거의 노인이잖아요. 미스터 웨스턴은 마흔에서 쉰 사이쯤 됐을 거예요.\"\n\n\"바로 그래서 그분의 훌륭한 태도가 더 값진 거야. 사람은 나이가 들수록, 해리엇, 그 태도가 나쁘지 않은 게 더 중요해지거든. 그럴수록 어떤 시끄러움이나 거칠음이나 어색함이든 더 도드라지고 역겨워지는 법이야. 젊을 때 봐 넘길 만한 게 늙어서는 진저리 나는 게 되지. 미스터 마틴은 지금도 어색하고 퉁명스러운데, 미스터 웨스턴 나이가 되면 어떻게 될까?\"\n\n\"정말 뭐라 말할 수가 없네요.\" 해리엇이 자못 진지하게 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 꽤 그럴듯하게 짐작은 할 수 있지. 그는 행색 따위는 도무지 신경도 안 쓰고, 이익이니 손해니 하는 것밖에 모르는, 영락없이 천하고 상스러운 농부가 되어 있을 거야.\"\n\n\"정말 그럴까요? 그럼 아주 안 좋겠네요.\"\n\n\"제 일에 벌써 얼마나 골몰해 있는지는, 네가 권한 그 책을 물어보기를 잊어버린 것만 봐도 빤하지. 그는 장터 생각에 너무 푹 빠져서 다른 건 생각할 겨를이 없었던 거야 — 하긴 잘나가는 사람한테는 마땅히 그래야 할 일이지. 그가 책하고 무슨 상관이 있겠어? 그리고 그가 분명 잘되어서 언젠가는 아주 부자가 될 거라는 것도 의심하지 않아 — 그러니 그가 무식하고 거칠다는 건 우리가 신경 쓸 일이 아니야.\"\n\n\"그분이 그 책을 기억 못 한 게 이상하네요\" — 이것이 해리엇의 대답 전부였고, 자못 진지하게 못마땅해하는 투로 한 말이라, 엠마는 그쯤은 그냥 내버려 두어도 무방하리라 여겼다. 그래서 한동안 더 말하지 않았다. 그녀가 다음으로 꺼낸 말은 이러했다.\n\n\"한 가지 점에서는, 아마, 미스터 엘턴의 태도가 미스터 나이틀리나 미스터 웨스턴보다 나을 거야. 더 부드럽거든. 본보기로 삼기에 더 안전하지. 미스터 웨스턴에게는 솔직함과 시원시원함, 거의 무뚝뚝하달 만한 데가 있는데, 사람들은 다들 그런 점을 그분에게서는 좋아해. 거기에 쾌활함이 워낙 많이 배어 있으니까 — 하지만 그건 따라 해서는 안 될 거야. 미스터 나이틀리의 그 거침없고 단호하고 위엄 있는 태도도 마찬가지지. 그분에게는 아주 잘 어울리지만 말이야. 그분의 풍채며 인상이며 사회적 처지가 그걸 허락하는 것 같거든. 하지만 어떤 젊은 남자가 그분을 따라 하려 든다면, 봐줄 수가 없을 거야. 반대로, 젊은 남자한테는 미스터 엘턴을 본보기로 삼으라고 아주 안심하고 권할 만하다고 생각해. 미스터 엘턴은 쾌활하고 밝고 친절하고 부드럽거든. 내가 보기에 요즘 들어 특히 더 부드러워진 것 같아. 미스터 엘턴이 한층 더 다정하게 굴어서 우리 둘 중 누구한테든 환심을 사려는 속셈이 있는 건지는 모르겠지만, 해리엇, 그분 태도가 전보다 부드러워졌다는 건 분명 눈에 띄어. 무슨 뜻이 있다면, 그건 너를 기쁘게 하려는 걸 거야. 요전 날 그분이 너를 두고 한 말, 내가 안 들려줬던가?\"\n\n그러고서 그녀는 미스터 엘턴에게서 끌어낸 따뜻한 인물 칭찬을 몇 마디 옮겼는데, 이제는 그 말에 온전히 무게를 실어 주었다. 해리엇은 얼굴을 붉히며 미소 짓고는, 자기도 늘 미스터 엘턴을 아주 괜찮은 분이라 생각했다고 말했다.\n\n미스터 엘턴이야말로 해리엇의 머릿속에서 그 젊은 농부를 몰아내려고 엠마가 점찍어 둔 바로 그 사람이었다. 그녀는 그것이 더없이 훌륭한 혼처가 되리라 생각했다. 다만 너무도 빤히 바람직하고 자연스럽고 그럼 직한 일이라, 그것을 계획한 자기 공을 크게 내세울 수가 없을 정도였다. 다른 사람들도 누구나 떠올리고 점쳐 볼 게 분명한 일이라 못내 마음에 걸렸다. 그러나 계획을 떠올린 시점에서는 누구도 자기를 따라올 수 없으리라 — 그 생각은 해리엇이 하트필드에 온 바로 그 첫날 저녁에 이미 그녀의 머릿속에 들어왔으니 말이다. 곱씹어 볼수록 그 합당함에 대한 확신은 더욱 커졌다. 미스터 엘턴의 처지는 더없이 알맞았다. 그 자신 어엿한 신사인 데다 천한 연줄이 없었고, 동시에 해리엇의 미심쩍은 출생을 정당하게 트집 잡을 만한 집안 출신도 아니었다. 그는 해리엇에게 안락한 보금자리를 줄 수 있었고, 엠마는 수입도 아주 넉넉하리라 짐작했다. 하이베리의 목사관이 큰 편은 아니어도, 그가 얼마간 독립적인 재산을 가졌다고 알려져 있었기 때문이다. 게다가 그녀는 그를, 쾌활하고 선의가 넘치는 점잖은 청년으로, 쓸모 있는 분별이나 세상 물정에 대한 식견에 모자람이 없는 사람으로 아주 높이 보고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 그가 해리엇을 아름다운 아가씨로 여긴다는 것을 이미 확인해 둔 터였고, 하트필드에서 그토록 자주 만나다 보면 그 점이 그의 편에서는 충분한 토대가 되리라 믿었다. 그리고 해리엇 편에서는, 그에게서 마음을 받고 있다는 생각이 으레 그렇듯 충분한 무게와 효력을 발휘하리라는 데 거의 의심이 없었다. 게다가 그는 정말이지 아주 호감 가는 청년이었고, 까다롭지 않은 여자라면 누구든 좋아할 만한 청년이었다. 그는 아주 잘생겼다고들 했다. 그 사람됨은 대체로 많은 이의 찬탄을 받았다 — 그녀 자신은 아니었지만, 그녀로서는 도무지 양보할 수 없는 이목구비의 우아함이 그에게는 모자랐던 것이다. 그러나 로버트 마틴이 호두를 구해다 주려고 들판을 누비고 다닌 것에 흐뭇해할 수 있는 아가씨라면, 미스터 엘턴의 흠모쯤에는 너끈히 마음을 빼앗길 만했다.\n\n# 제1권 5장\n\n\"엠마와 해리엇 스미스 사이의 그 대단한 친밀함을 두고 부인께서 어떻게 보시는지는 모르겠습니다만,\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"저는 그것을 나쁜 일이라고 봅니다.\"\n\n\"나쁜 일이라뇨! 정말로 나쁜 일이라고 보세요? 어째서요?\"\n\n\"두 사람 다 서로에게 아무런 득이 되지 못할 거라고 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"놀랍군요! 엠마는 분명 해리엇에게 득이 될 거예요. 그리고 새로운 관심거리를 줌으로써, 해리엇도 엠마에게 득이 된다고 할 수 있고요. 저는 두 사람의 친밀함을 더없이 흐뭇하게 지켜봐 왔는걸요. 우리가 어쩜 이리 다르게 느낄까요! 서로에게 아무 득이 안 되리라니요! 미스터 나이틀리, 이건 분명 엠마를 두고 벌이는 우리 말다툼의 시작이 되겠네요.\"\n\n\"제가 일부러 부인과 다투려고 찾아온 거라 여기실지도 모르겠군요. 웨스턴이 출타 중이니 부인 혼자서 싸움을 감당해야 한다는 걸 알고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"미스터 웨스턴이 여기 계셨다면 분명 제 편을 들었을 거예요. 이 문제에 관해서라면 그분도 저와 똑같이 생각하시니까요. 바로 어제도 우리가 그 이야기를 나누며, 엠마가 어울릴 만한 그런 아가씨가 하이베리에 있다는 게 얼마나 다행한 일인지 의견을 모았는걸요. 미스터 나이틀리, 저는 이 일에서 당신을 공정한 심판으로 인정해 드릴 수 없어요. 당신은 워낙 혼자 사는 데 익숙해서 말동무의 가치를 모르세요. 그리고 평생을 그렇게 지내 온 여자가 같은 여자의 벗에게서 느끼는 위안이 어떤 것인지, 그건 아마 어떤 남자도 제대로 가늠하지 못할 거예요. 해리엇 스미스를 두고 당신이 어떤 반론을 펴실지는 짐작이 가요. 엠마의 벗이라면 마땅히 갖추어야 할, 그런 빼어난 아가씨는 못 되지요. 하지만 다른 한편으로, 엠마가 해리엇을 더 식견 있는 사람으로 만들고 싶어 하니, 그게 엠마 자신도 더 많이 읽도록 이끄는 자극이 될 거예요. 두 사람이 함께 책을 읽겠지요. 엠마가 그럴 작정인 걸 저는 알아요.\"\n\n\"엠마는 열두 살 적부터 책을 더 많이 읽을 작정이었답니다. 여러 시기에 걸쳐 엠마가 작성해 둔, 차근차근 끝까지 읽으려던 책 목록을 저는 한두 번 본 게 아니에요. 아주 훌륭한 목록이었지요. 더없이 잘 골랐고, 더없이 정갈하게 정리돼 있었어요. 어떤 때는 알파벳순으로, 또 어떤 때는 다른 규칙대로요. 겨우 열네 살에 작성한 그 목록은, 제가 보기에 엠마의 판단력을 어찌나 잘 보여 주던지, 한동안 간직해 두었을 정도였습니다. 지금쯤은 아마 또 아주 훌륭한 목록을 하나 만들어 두었겠지요. 하지만 엠마에게서 꾸준한 독서가 이어지기를 기대하는 일은 저는 진작에 접었습니다. 그 아이는 근면과 인내를, 그리고 공상을 이해력에 복종시키는 일을 결코 받아들이지 못해요. 미스 테일러도 자극하지 못한 일을, 해리엇 스미스가 해낼 리 없다고 저는 자신 있게 단언합니다. 부인께서도 바라시는 만큼 엠마를 책 읽게 만들지 못하셨잖아요. 못 하셨다는 걸 잘 아시면서.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 웨스턴 부인이 미소 지으며 답했다. \"그때는 저도 그렇게 여겼던 것 같아요. 하지만 우리가 헤어진 뒤로는, 제가 바라는 일을 엠마가 하지 않은 적이 한 번이라도 있었는지 도무지 기억나지 않는걸요.\"\n\n\"그런 종류의 기억이라면 굳이 되살릴 마음이 좀처럼 들지 않겠지요.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 감회 어린 투로 말했다. 그러고는 한두 순간 입을 다물었다. \"하지만 저는,\" 그가 곧 덧붙였다. \"그런 마법에 감각을 사로잡힌 적이 없으니, 여전히 보고 듣고 기억할 수밖에 없습니다. 엠마는 제 식구 중에서 가장 영리하다는 탓에 버릇이 나빠졌어요. 열 살 적에 이미, 열일곱 살 언니를 쩔쩔매게 한 물음에 척척 답할 줄 아는 불운을 타고났지요. 늘 영리하고 자신만만했고, 이저벨라는 굼뜨고 수줍었습니다. 그리고 열두 살 이래로, 엠마는 그 집과 부인을 포함한 모두의 안주인 노릇을 해 왔어요. 어머니를 잃으면서, 엠마는 자기를 감당할 수 있는 유일한 사람을 잃은 셈입니다. 엠마는 어머니의 재능을 물려받았으니, 어머니 밑에서라면 분명 복종하며 자랐을 테지요.\"\n\n\"미스터 나이틀리, 제가 만일 우드하우스 댁을 떠나 다른 자리를 구해야 했다면, 당신의 추천에 기대야 하는 처지가 안 됐기를 바랄 따름이에요. 당신이라면 누구에게도 저를 좋게 말해 주지 않았을 것 같으니까요. 당신은 분명 제가 맡은 그 직책에 어울리지 않는 사람이라고 늘 여기셨을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"부인은 여기에 계신 편이 더 좋아요. 아내로는 더없이 적격이지만, 가정교사로는 도무지 아니었습니다. 그래도 부인은 하트필드에 계신 내내 훌륭한 아내가 될 준비를 하고 계셨던 거지요. 부인의 역량이 약속하는 만큼 엠마에게 온전한 교육을 베풀지는 못했을지 몰라도, 정작 부인은 엠마에게서 아주 훌륭한 교육을 받고 계셨던 겁니다. 다름 아닌 혼인에서 가장 긴요한 그 대목 — 자기 뜻을 굽히고 시키는 대로 하는 법 말입니다. 그러니 웨스턴이 제게 아내를 추천해 달라고 했다면, 저는 분명 미스 테일러를 꼽았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"고마워요. 미스터 웨스턴 같은 분에게 좋은 아내가 되는 데야 무슨 큰 공이 있겠어요.\"\n\n\"실은 말입니다, 저는 부인이 차라리 그 재능이 아깝게 쓰이는 게 아닐까, 견디려는 온갖 마음가짐을 갖춰 두었건만 정작 견딜 거리가 하나도 없는 게 아닐까 걱정입니다. 그렇다고 절망할 일은 아니지요. 웨스턴이 안락함에 겨워 까다로워질 수도 있고, 아들이 그를 속 썩일 수도 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그것만은 아니길 바라요. 그럴 것 같지도 않고요. 안 돼요, 미스터 나이틀리, 그쪽에서 골칫거리가 생기리라고 점치지 마세요.\"\n\n\"점치는 거야 제가 아니지요. 저는 그저 있을 법한 일들을 들어 본 것뿐입니다. 점치고 알아맞히는 엠마의 천재성을 제가 흉내 낼 리야 있겠습니까. 진심으로 바라건대, 그 젊은이가 인품으로는 웨스턴 같고 재산으로는 처칠 같기를요. 하지만 해리엇 스미스 — 해리엇 스미스에 관해서는 아직 절반도 못 했습니다. 저는 그 아이가 엠마에게 있을 수 있는 가장 나쁜 부류의 말동무라고 봅니다. 자기는 아무것도 모르면서, 엠마는 모든 걸 다 안다고 우러러보지요. 하는 짓마다 아첨이에요. 게다가 그게 의도된 게 아니라서 더 나쁩니다. 그 무지가 매시간 아첨인 셈이지요. 해리엇이 그토록 흐뭇한 열등함을 떡하니 내보이는 마당에, 엠마가 자기도 배울 게 있다고 어찌 생각이나 하겠습니까? 또 해리엇으로 말하자면, 그 아이가 이 사귐으로 얻을 게 없다고 저는 감히 말하겠습니다. 하트필드는 그 아이가 본디 속한 다른 모든 곳을 못마땅하게 여기도록 만들 뿐이에요. 그 아이는 딱 불편할 만큼만 세련되어져서, 출생과 형편이 정해 준 제 보금자리의 사람들과 어울리기 거북해질 겁니다. 엠마의 가르침이 정신에 어떤 힘을 길러 준다거나, 아가씨가 제 삶의 갖가지 처지에 분별 있게 적응하도록 이끈다고 한다면 — 제가 크게 틀린 거겠지요. 그건 그저 약간의 광택만 입혀 줄 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"제가 당신보다 엠마의 양식을 더 믿거나, 아니면 엠마의 지금 행복을 더 염려하는 거겠지요. 저로서는 그 사귐을 한탄할 수가 없으니까요. 엠마가 어젯밤에 얼마나 고와 보였는지 몰라요!\"\n\n\"오! 부인은 엠마의 마음보다 외모를 이야기하고 싶으신 게로군요? 좋습니다. 엠마가 예쁘다는 것까지 부정할 생각은 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"예쁘다니요! 차라리 아름답다고 하셔야죠. 얼굴이며 자태며, 엠마만큼 완벽한 아름다움에 가까운 걸 상상이나 하실 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"무얼 상상할 수 있을지는 모르겠지만, 솔직히 말해 엠마만큼 제 눈에 흡족한 얼굴이나 자태는 좀처럼 본 적이 없다는 건 인정하겠습니다. 하지만 저야 한쪽으로 기운 오랜 벗이니까요.\"\n\n\"그 눈매! 진짜 개암빛 눈동자가 어찌나 빛나는지! 반듯한 이목구비에, 환히 트인 표정에, 그 혈색이라니! 오! 무르익은 건강이 피워 낸 그 화사함, 그리고 그 어여쁜 키와 몸매, 그 단단하고 꼿꼿한 자태! 화사함만 건강한 게 아니라, 분위기며 머리를 두는 모양새며 눈길까지 건강이 배어 있어요. 어떤 아이를 두고 '건강의 화신'이라고들 하잖아요. 그런데 엠마는 늘 제게 다 자란 건강의 완벽한 화신 같다는 느낌을 줘요. 사랑스러움 그 자체예요. 미스터 나이틀리, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"엠마의 외모에는 트집 잡을 데가 없습니다.\" 그가 답했다. \"부인이 묘사하신 그대로라고 생각해요. 저도 엠마를 바라보는 게 좋습니다. 그리고 이 칭찬도 보태지요. 엠마가 제 외모를 두고 허영을 부리지는 않는다고 봅니다. 그토록 빼어나게 아름다운데도, 정작 그쪽에는 별로 마음을 쏟지 않는 것 같아요. 엠마의 허영은 다른 데 있습니다. 웨스턴 부인, 해리엇 스미스에 대한 제 못마땅함이나, 그 사귐이 두 사람 모두에게 해가 되리라는 제 우려는, 말로 거두어지지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 저는, 미스터 나이틀리, 그 사귐이 두 사람에게 아무런 해도 끼치지 않으리라는 확신에서 똑같이 굳건해요. 우리 사랑스러운 엠마에게 자잘한 결점이 다 있다 해도, 그 아이는 더없이 훌륭한 사람이에요. 더 나은 딸을, 더 다정한 동생을, 더 진실한 벗을 어디서 보겠어요? 아니에요, 아니에요. 그 아이에게는 믿어도 좋을 자질이 있어요. 누구를 정말로 그릇된 길로 이끄는 일은 결코 없을 거고, 두고두고 후회할 실수도 저지르지 않을 거예요. 엠마가 한 번 그르치는 동안, 백 번은 옳을 사람이니까요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다. 더는 부인을 성가시게 하지 않겠습니다. 엠마는 천사라 칩시다. 저는 성탄절에 존과 이저벨라가 올 때까지 제 울화는 혼자 삭이지요. 존은 엠마를 합당한, 그래서 맹목적이지 않은 애정으로 사랑하고, 이저벨라는 늘 남편과 똑같이 생각하니까요. 다만 남편이 아이들 일로 충분히 겁먹지 않을 때만 빼고요. 두 사람의 의견은 제 편이리라 확신합니다.\"\n\n\"당신들 모두 그 아이를 정말이지 너무 깊이 사랑하니, 불공정하거나 매정하게 굴 리야 없겠지요. 하지만 미스터 나이틀리, 제가 한마디 무람없이 거들어도 양해해 주세요. (아시다시피 저는 엠마의 어머니가 가졌을 법한 발언권을 얼마쯤은 가졌다고 자처하니까요.) 무람없이 한 가지 일러두자면, 해리엇 스미스와의 친밀함을 당신들 사이에서 이러쿵저러쿵 많이 따지는 일에서 무슨 좋은 결과가 나올 수 있을 것 같지가 않아요. 부디 양해해 주세요. 그러나 설령 그 친밀함에서 자잘한 불편이 우려된다 한들, 그 사귐을 흔쾌히 인정하는 아버지 말고는 누구에게도 책임질 일이 없는 엠마가, 그게 그 아이 자신에게 기쁨의 원천인 한, 그 사귐을 끝내리라고는 기대할 수 없는 노릇이지요. 조언하는 일이 제 소관이었던 게 워낙 여러 해라서요. 미스터 나이틀리, 직분의 이런 자투리가 남아 있다 한들 놀라지는 마세요.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그 점, 부인께 무척 감사드립니다. 아주 좋은 조언이에요. 그리고 이 조언은 부인의 조언들이 흔히 맞닥뜨린 운명보다 더 나은 운명을 누리게 될 겁니다. 받들어질 테니까요.\"\n\n\"미시즈 존 나이틀리는 쉬이 놀라는 사람이라, 동생 일로 마음이 상할 수도 있어요.\"\n\n\"안심하십시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 어떤 소동도 일으키지 않을 겁니다. 제 언짢음은 혼자 간직하지요. 저는 엠마에게 아주 진실한 관심을 두고 있습니다. 제게 이저벨라가 엠마보다 더 누이 같지도 않고, 더 큰 관심을 불러일으킨 적도 없어요. 어쩌면 그만큼도 못 되지요. 엠마를 향해 느끼는 마음에는 어떤 염려, 어떤 궁금함이 있습니다. 저 아이가 앞으로 어찌 될지 궁금하단 말입니다!\"\n\n\"저도 그래요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 부드럽게 말했다. \"아주 많이요.\"\n\n\"엠마는 늘 자기는 결코 결혼하지 않을 거라고 공언하지요. 물론 그건 아무 뜻도 없는 소리지만요. 하지만 그 아이가 지금껏 마음에 둘 만한 남자를 본 적이 있을 것 같지는 않습니다. 합당한 상대에게 흠뻑 빠져 보는 것도 엠마에게 나쁘지 않을 텐데요. 저는 엠마가 사랑에 빠지되, 그 사랑이 되돌아올지 어떨지 얼마쯤 마음 졸이는 모습을 보고 싶습니다. 그게 그 아이에게 약이 될 테니까요. 하지만 이 근방에는 엠마의 마음을 붙들 사람이 없고, 그 아이는 좀처럼 집을 떠나는 일이 없으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"정말이지 지금으로서는 그 아이가 제 결심을 깰 만한 유혹거리가 거의 없는 것 같아요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"그럴 만한 게 도무지 없다시피 하지요. 그리고 그 아이가 하트필드에서 그토록 행복한 마당에, 가엾은 미스터 우드하우스 때문에 그런 곤란을 빚을 연정을 품게 되기를 저로서는 바랄 수가 없어요. 지금 엠마에게 혼인을 권하지는 않아요. 그렇다고 혼인이라는 것을 깔보는 건 결코 아니에요, 정말이에요.\"\n\n그녀가 그렇게 말한 데에는, 이 문제를 두고 자신과 미스터 웨스턴이 은근히 품어 온 어떤 속내를 되도록 감추려는 뜻도 얼마쯤 있었다. 랜덜스에는 엠마의 앞날에 관한 어떤 바람이 있었으나, 그것이 의심받는 일은 바람직하지 않았다. 그리고 곧이어 미스터 나이틀리가 \"웨스턴은 날씨를 어찌 보던가요? 비가 오려나요?\"라고 조용히 화제를 돌리는 것을 보고, 그녀는 그가 하트필드에 관해 더는 할 말도, 짐작할 일도 없다는 것을 확신했다.\n\n# 제1권 6장\n\n엠마는 해리엇의 공상을 올바른 방향으로 이끌었으며, 어린 허영심에서 우러난 고마움을 아주 좋은 목적에 쏟게 했다는 데 의심을 품을 수 없었다. 해리엇이 미스터 엘턴을 놀랄 만큼 잘생긴 남자에 더없이 호감 가는 태도를 지닌 사람으로 여기는 정도가, 전보다 한결 또렷해진 것을 알아차렸기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 흠모하고 있다는 확신에 호감 가는 암시를 슬쩍슬쩍 덧붙이는 데 조금도 망설임이 없었으므로, 엠마는 해리엇 쪽에서도 필요한 만큼의 호감이 자라나리라고 곧 꽤 자신하게 되었다. 미스터 엘턴이 아직 사랑에 빠진 것은 아닐지언정, 사랑에 빠지기에 더없이 좋은 길에 들어섰다는 것을 그녀는 확신했다. 그에 대해서는 거리낄 것이 없었다. 그는 해리엇 이야기를 했고, 어찌나 따뜻하게 칭찬을 하는지, 시간이 조금만 더해 주면 모자랄 것이 하나도 없으리라 여겨졌다. 해리엇의 태도가 하트필드에 드나든 뒤로 눈에 띄게 나아졌음을 알아본 것도, 그의 연정이 자라나고 있다는 적잖이 흐뭇한 증거 가운데 하나였다.\n\n\"미스 스미스에게 필요한 것을 모두 갖추어 주셨군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"기품 있고 편안한 사람으로 만들어 주셨어요. 미스 우드하우스에게 처음 왔을 때도 아름다운 사람이었지만, 제 생각으로는 보태 주신 매력이 타고난 것보다 한없이 윗길입니다.\"\n\n\"제가 그 애에게 도움이 되었다고 생각해 주시니 기쁘네요. 하지만 해리엇은 그저 끌어내 주고, 아주 약간의 암시만 받으면 될 아이였어요. 다정한 성품과 꾸밈없음에서 우러나는 타고난 기품을 본래 다 지니고 있었거든요. 제가 한 일은 별것 없어요.\"\n\n\"숙녀의 말씀에 반박하는 것이 허락된다면야.\" 점잖은 미스터 엘턴이 말했다.\n\n\"제가 어쩌면 그 애에게 성격에 결단력을 조금 더해 주었는지는 모르겠네요. 전에는 미처 생각해 보지 못했던 문제들을 생각하도록 가르쳐 주었으니까요.\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다. 제 눈에 가장 두드러진 것이 바로 그 점이에요. 그토록 더해진 성격의 결단력이라니! 솜씨 좋은 손길이었군요!\"\n\n\"정말 더없는 기쁨이었답니다. 그토록 진실로 사랑스러운 천성을 가진 사람은 처음 만나 봤어요.\"\n\n\"의심할 여지가 없지요.\" 그렇게 말하는 그의 어조에는 한숨 섞인 활기가 깃들어 있어, 연인다운 구석이 다분했다. 다른 날에는 해리엇의 초상화를 갖고 싶다는 그녀의 갑작스러운 바람을 그가 거드는 태도에도 그녀는 못지않게 흐뭇했다.\n\n\"해리엇, 초상화를 그려 본 적이 있나요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"초상화 모델로 앉아 본 적은요?\"\n\n해리엇은 막 방을 나서려던 참이었는데, 멈춰 서서 무척 흥미로운 천진함으로 이렇게만 말했다.\n\n\"어머! 아니에요, 한 번도요.\"\n\n그녀가 시야에서 사라지자마자 엠마가 외쳤다.\n\n\"저 애를 잘 그린 초상화가 있다면 얼마나 더없이 귀한 보물이겠어요! 얼마를 주고서라도 갖고 싶을 정도예요. 제가 직접 저 애를 그려 보고 싶을 지경이라니까요. 모르고 계시겠지만, 두어 해 전에 저는 초상화에 무척 빠져서 친구들 몇몇을 그려 보았는데, 대체로 눈썰미가 그럭저럭 괜찮다는 평을 들었답니다. 그런데 이런저런 까닭으로 진력이 나서 손을 놔 버렸지요. 하지만 정말이지, 해리엇이 모델로 앉아만 준다면 한번 해 볼 만도 해요. 저 애의 초상화를 갖는다면 얼마나 기쁠까요!\"\n\n\"제발 부탁드립니다.\" 미스터 엘턴이 외쳤다. \"정말이지 더없는 기쁨일 거예요! 미스 우드하우스, 부디 그 매력 넘치는 재능을 친구분을 위해 발휘해 주십시오. 저는 미스 우드하우스의 그림이 어떤지 압니다. 제가 모를 거라고 생각하셨나요? 이 방만 해도 미스 우드하우스의 풍경화와 꽃 그림이 그득하지 않습니까. 랜덜스의 웨스턴 부인 응접실에도 누구도 흉내 못 낼 인물화가 몇 점 걸려 있지 않습니까?\"\n\n그래요, 착한 양반!—엠마는 생각했다—하지만 그게 다 초상화 그리는 것과 무슨 상관이람? 당신은 그림에 대해 아무것도 모르잖아요. 내 그림에 황홀해하는 척하지 말아요. 그 황홀함은 해리엇의 얼굴을 위해 아껴 두세요. \"그래요, 그렇게 친절히 격려해 주신다면, 미스터 엘턴, 제가 할 수 있는 데까지 한번 해 봐야겠네요. 해리엇의 이목구비는 무척 섬세해서 닮게 그리기가 어렵답니다. 그래도 눈매와 입가의 선에 독특한 데가 있으니, 그걸 꼭 잡아내야겠지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다. 눈매와 입가의 선이라—성공하시리라는 걸 추호도 의심치 않습니다. 부디, 부디 한번 해 보십시오. 미스 우드하우스가 그리신다면, 정말이지 당신 말씀 그대로, 더없이 귀한 보물이 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 미스터 엘턴, 해리엇이 모델로 앉기를 싫어할까 봐 걱정이에요. 자기 미모를 워낙 대수롭잖게 여기거든요. 제 물음에 답하던 태도를 못 보셨어요? '내 초상화를 왜 그려요?' 하는 뜻이 어찌나 역력하던지요.\"\n\n\"아! 그럼요, 저도 봤습니다, 정말이에요. 놓치지 않았어요. 하지만 그래도 설득이 안 될 거라고는 도무지 생각되지 않는걸요.\"\n\n해리엇은 곧 돌아왔고, 제안은 거의 곧바로 꺼내졌다. 그녀의 거리낌은 두 사람의 진지한 채근 앞에서 몇 분도 버티지 못했다. 엠마는 곧장 작업에 들어가고 싶었으므로, 해리엇에게 맞는 가장 좋은 크기를 함께 정해 보려고, 자신의 갖가지 초상화 시도를 담은 화첩을 꺼내 왔다—그 가운데 완성된 것은 단 한 점도 없었다. 그녀가 시작만 해 둔 많은 그림이 펼쳐졌다. 세밀화, 반신상, 전신상, 연필화, 크레용화, 수채화—차례로 다 시도해 본 터였다. 그녀는 늘 무엇이든 다 해 보고 싶어 했고, 그림에서나 음악에서나, 어지간히 손에 익혔을 만한 노력보다 훨씬 적게 들이고도 어지간한 사람들보다 많이 나아간 편이었다. 그녀는 연주도 하고 노래도 불렀으며, 거의 모든 화풍으로 그림도 그렸다. 그러나 늘 끈기가 모자랐고, 어느 것 하나도, 마음만 먹었다면 능히 이르렀을 법한—그리고 마땅히 실패하지 말았어야 할—그 탁월함의 경지에는 다가가지 못했다. 화가로서든 음악가로서든 제 솜씨에 대해 크게 속고 있는 것은 아니었으나, 남들이 속는 것을 마다하지도 않았고, 제 재주에 대한 평판이 받을 만한 것보다 흔히 높다는 것을 알고도 서운해하지 않았다.\n\n어느 그림에나 나름의 좋은 점이 있었다—가장 덜 완성된 것에 어쩌면 가장 많이 있었는지도 모른다. 그녀의 화풍은 생기가 넘쳤다. 그러나 그것이 훨씬 못했다 한들, 혹은 열 곱절 더 뛰어났다 한들, 두 동행의 기쁨과 감탄은 마찬가지였으리라. 둘 다 황홀경에 빠져 있었다. 닮게 그린 초상화란 누구에게나 기쁨을 주는 법이고, 미스 우드하우스의 솜씨라면 당연히 일품일 터였다.\n\n\"두 분께 보여 드릴 얼굴이 그리 다양하지는 못해요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"본보기로 삼을 게 제 가족뿐이었거든요. 이건 우리 아버지—이건 또 우리 아버지인데—초상화 모델로 앉으신다는 생각만 해도 어찌나 안절부절못하시던지, 몰래 그리는 수밖에 없었지 뭐예요. 그래서 둘 다 그리 닮지는 않았어요. 이건 또 웨스턴 부인이에요, 보세요, 또 웨스턴 부인이고, 또 웨스턴 부인이죠. 사랑스러운 웨스턴 부인! 무슨 일에든 언제나 제게 가장 다정한 벗이에요. 부탁만 하면 언제든 앉아 주셨거든요. 이건 우리 언니예요. 정말이지 언니 특유의 그 자그맣고 우아한 자태 그대로죠!—얼굴도 그리 안 닮은 건 아니고요. 좀 더 오래 앉아 줬더라면 아주 닮게 그렸을 텐데, 어찌나 제게 네 아이를 그리라고 다그치던지 가만히 있지를 않았어요. 그래서 여기, 그 네 아이 가운데 셋을 그려 본 것들이 죄다 나오네요. 자, 보세요, 헨리와 존과 벨라가 종이 이쪽 끝에서 저쪽 끝까지 있죠. 누구를 골라 봐도 그 누구라 해도 통할 만하답니다. 언니가 어찌나 애들을 그려 달라고 안달이던지 거절할 수가 없었어요. 하지만 세 살 네 살 먹은 아이들을 가만히 세워 둘 수는 없는 노릇이잖아요. 게다가 분위기와 혈색을 넘어 닮게 그리기란—우리 언니 자식들이 어느 누구보다 이목구비가 거칠기라도 했다면 또 모를까—여간 어려운 일이 아니에요. 이건 넷째를 그린 거예요, 그땐 갓난아기였죠. 소파에서 잠든 모습 그대로 그렸는데, 머리쓰개만큼은 더 바랄 게 없을 만큼 닮게 그렸답니다. 머리를 어찌나 알맞게 파묻고 있던지요. 그건 정말 닮았어요. 어린 조지는 좀 자랑스럽다니까요. 소파 모서리도 아주 잘 됐고요. 그리고 이게 마지막 거예요.\"—신사를 작은 크기로 그린 어여쁜 전신상 스케치를 펼치며—\"마지막이자 가장 잘된 거죠—우리 형부, 미스터 존 나이틀리예요.—이건 거의 완성될 뻔하다가 제가 토라져서 치워 버리고는, 다시는 누구의 초상화도 그리지 않겠다고 맹세해 버렸지 뭐예요. 약이 오를 수밖에 없었어요. 그 온갖 공을 들여서 정말 아주 닮게 그려 놓았는데—(웨스턴 부인도 저도 정말 빼다 박았다는 데 의견이 딱 맞았어요)—그저 너무 잘생기게, 너무 후하게 그렸을 뿐이었지만, 그래 봐야 그건 좋은 쪽으로 난 흠이잖아요—이 모든 게 다 끝난 다음에, 가엾고 사랑스러운 이저벨라가 무덤덤하게 하는 칭찬이라는 게 고작—'그래, 좀 닮긴 했네—그래도 정말이지 그이를 제대로 살리진 못했어'였어요. 우리는 형부가 모델로 앉도록 설득하느라 어지간히 애를 먹었거든요. 큰 선심이라도 쓰는 양 굴어서, 도무지 더는 못 견디겠더라고요. 그래서 끝내 완성하지 않기로 했어요. 안 그러면 브런즈윅 스퀘어로 아침마다 찾아오는 손님들한테 일일이, 잘 안 닮은 초상화라고 변명을 늘어놓아야 할 테니까요.—그래서, 말씀드렸듯이, 그때 다시는 누구도 그리지 않겠다고 작정했던 거예요. 하지만 해리엇을 위해서, 아니 차라리 저 자신을 위해서, 게다가 지금으로서는 부부가 얽힌 경우도 아니니, 이제 그 결심을 깨려고요.\"\n\n미스터 엘턴은 그 말에 적잖이 마음이 동하고 흐뭇해진 듯이, \"지금으로서는 부부가 얽힌 경우도 아니지요, 정말이지 말씀하신 대로요. 바로 그렇습니다. 부부가 얽힌 경우가 아니지요.\" 하고 어찌나 의미심장하게 곱씹던지, 엠마는 차라리 지금 당장 둘만 남겨 두는 편이 낫지 않을까 생각하기 시작했다. 그러나 그림을 그리고 싶었으므로, 그 선언은 좀 더 미루어 두는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그녀는 곧 초상화의 크기와 종류를 정했다. 미스터 존 나이틀리의 것처럼 수채 전신상으로 하기로 했고, 마음에 흡족하게만 그려진다면 벽난로 선반 위 아주 영예로운 자리를 차지할 운명이었다.\n\n모델로 앉히는 일이 시작되었다. 해리엇은 미소 짓고 얼굴을 붉히며, 자세와 표정을 제대로 지키지 못할까 봐 안절부절못했으니, 화가의 차분한 눈에는 더없이 사랑스러운 앳된 표정의 어우러짐이 비쳤다. 그러나 미스터 엘턴이 뒤에서 안절부절 서성이며 붓질 하나하나를 지켜보는 통에 도무지 아무것도 할 수가 없었다. 그가 흠 잡힐 일 없이 마음껏 보고 또 볼 수 있는 자리를 잡은 것은 가상한 일이었으나, 결국 그녀는 그것을 그만두게 하고 다른 데로 가 달라고 청할 수밖에 없었다. 그러다 문득 그에게 책을 읽혀야겠다는 생각이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"그가 친절을 베풀어 책을 읽어 준다면 그야말로 고마운 일일 텐데! 그러면 그녀 쪽의 어려움을 잊게 해 주고, 미스 스미스 쪽의 따분함도 덜어 줄 테니까.\"\n\n미스터 엘턴은 더없이 기뻐했다. 해리엇은 귀를 기울였고, 엠마는 평온히 그림을 그렸다. 그가 여전히 자주 와서 들여다보는 것은 너그러이 봐줄 수밖에 없었다. 그보다 덜했다면 연인으로서 너무 미적지근했을 테니까. 그는 붓이 잠깐만 멈춰도 벌떡 일어나 진척을 보고 황홀해할 채비가 되어 있었다.—이런 격려자에게 언짢아할 수는 없는 노릇이었으니, 그의 감탄은 닮은 점이 채 드러나기도 전에 그것을 알아보게 만들 정도였다. 그의 눈썰미는 존경할 수 없었으나, 그의 사랑과 살가움만큼은 흠잡을 데가 없었다.\n\n모델로 앉히는 일은 전체적으로 무척 만족스러웠다. 첫날의 스케치에 그녀는 계속 이어 갈 마음이 들 만큼 흡족했다. 닮은 점이 모자라지 않았고, 자세도 운 좋게 잘 잡혔다. 게다가 자태에 약간 손을 보태 키를 조금 더 키우고 우아함을 한결 더 보탤 작정이었으므로, 끝내는 모든 면에서 어여쁜 그림이 되어, 정해진 자리를 두 사람 모두에게 영예로이 채워 주리라 크게 자신했다—한쪽의 미모와 다른 한쪽의 솜씨, 그리고 둘의 우정을 길이 기리는 기념물이 되리라고, 거기에 미스터 엘턴의 더없이 유망한 연정이 보태 줄 법한 온갖 흐뭇한 연상까지 곁들여서.\n\n해리엇은 이튿날 다시 앉기로 했고, 미스터 엘턴은 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로, 다시 와서 책을 읽어 주게 해 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"그럼요. 함께해 주시면 저희야 더없이 기쁘죠.\"\n\n이튿날에도 똑같은 정중함과 예의, 똑같은 성공과 만족이 펼쳐졌고, 그것은 빠르고 흐뭇하게 진척되는 그림의 전 과정에 함께했다. 그 그림을 본 사람은 누구나 흡족해했으나, 미스터 엘턴은 끊임없이 황홀경에 빠져, 어떤 비평에 맞서서도 그것을 옹호했다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스가 친구분에게 부족했던 단 하나의 아름다움을 더해 주셨네요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 그에게 말했다—자신이 연인에게 건네는 말이라고는 추호도 의심치 못한 채.—\"눈매의 표현이 더없이 정확해요. 하지만 미스 스미스는 저런 눈썹과 속눈썹을 갖고 있지 않잖아요. 그게 없는 건 그 애 얼굴의 흠이지만요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 보십니까?\" 그가 대답했다. \"저는 동의할 수 없는걸요. 제게는 어느 이목구비 하나 더없이 완벽하게 닮아 보입니다. 평생 이렇게 닮은 그림은 본 적이 없어요. 음영의 효과도 감안해야지요, 아시잖습니까.\"\n\n\"엠마, 키를 너무 크게 그렸군.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다.\n\n엠마도 그런 줄 알았으나 인정하려 들지 않았고, 미스터 엘턴이 열을 올리며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요! 결코 너무 크지 않습니다. 조금도 너무 크지 않아요. 생각해 보십시오, 미스 스미스는 앉아 있잖습니까—그러면 자연히 다른—요컨대 딱 그런 느낌을 주는 거지요—그리고 비례는 지켜져야 하는 법이잖습니까, 아시다시피. 비례 말입니다, 단축법요.—아, 아니에요! 이건 딱 미스 스미스만 한 키의 느낌을 줍니다. 정말이지 바로 그래요!\"\n\n\"아주 예쁘구나.\" 미스터 우드하우스가 말했다. \"참 예쁘게도 그렸어! 네 그림은 늘 그렇듯이 말이다, 얘야. 너만큼 그림을 잘 그리는 사람은 본 적이 없단다. 다만 한 가지 영 마음에 안 드는 건, 어깨에 숄만 살짝 걸친 채 바깥에 앉아 있는 것처럼 보인다는 거야—그러니 자꾸 감기에 걸릴까 봐 걱정이 되는구나.\"\n\n\"하지만 아버지, 이건 여름이라는 설정이에요. 여름의 따뜻한 날이요. 저 나무를 보세요.\"\n\n\"그래도 바깥에 앉아 있는 건 도통 안전하지가 않단다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"어르신께서야 무슨 말씀이든 하셔도 좋습니다.\" 미스터 엘턴이 외쳤다. \"하지만 저는 고백건대, 미스 스미스를 바깥에 둔 것이야말로 더없이 멋진 착상이라고 봅니다. 그리고 저 나무는 누구도 흉내 못 낼 생기로 그려졌고요! 다른 어떤 배경이었다면 훨씬 덜 어울렸을 거예요. 미스 스미스의 천진한 태도와—그리고 전체적으로—아, 정말 감탄스럽습니다! 도무지 눈을 뗄 수가 없어요. 평생 이렇게 닮은 그림은 본 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n다음으로 필요한 것은 그림을 액자에 넣는 일이었는데, 여기에 자잘한 어려움이 좀 있었다. 당장 해야 하고, 런던에서 해야 하며, 그 주문은 안목을 믿을 만한 분별 있는 누군가의 손을 거쳐야 했다. 그리고 늘 온갖 심부름을 도맡던 이저벨라에게는 부탁할 수 없었으니, 때가 십이월이라 미스터 우드하우스가 딸이 십이월의 안개 속에 집 밖으로 나선다는 생각을 견디지 못했기 때문이다. 그러나 이 곤란이 미스터 엘턴의 귀에 들어가자마자 곧 해소되었다. 그의 친절은 언제나 빈틈없이 대기하고 있었다. \"그 심부름을 제게 맡겨 주신다면, 그걸 해내는 데 얼마나 더없는 기쁨을 느끼겠습니까! 저야 언제든 런던에 다녀올 수 있고요. 그런 심부름에 쓰임받는다면 얼마나 기쁠지 이루 말할 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"너무 친절하셔라!—차마 그런 생각은 할 수 없어요!—세상없어도 그런 번거로운 일을 맡길 수는 없고말고요.\"—이런 말이 바라던 대로 거듭되는 간청과 다짐을 불러왔고, 불과 몇 분 만에 일이 매듭지어졌다.\n\n미스터 엘턴이 그림을 런던으로 가져가 액자를 고르고 지시를 내리기로 했다. 그리고 엠마는 그가 그리 불편하지 않게 안전을 보장할 수 있도록 잘 포장할 수 있겠다고 생각했으나, 정작 그는 충분히 불편을 떠맡지 못할까 봐 도리어 걱정하는 듯했다.\n\n\"이 얼마나 소중한 맡김인지요!\" 그는 그것을 받아 들며 애틋한 한숨과 함께 말했다.\n\n이 사람은 사랑에 빠지기에는 너무 점잖은 게 아닐까—엠마는 생각했다—그렇게 말하고 싶지만, 사랑에 빠지는 데에도 백 가지 다른 방식이 있을 테지. 그는 훌륭한 청년이고, 해리엇에게 딱 맞을 거야. 본인 말마따나 '바로 그렇습니다'가 될 테고. 하지만 한숨을 쉬고 시름에 잠기고 또 그렇게 듣기 좋은 말을 짜내는 모양새는, 그게 상대가 나라면 도저히 못 견딜 정도지. 둘째 가는 상대로서야 어지간히 한몫 받는 셈이지만. 하지만 이건 다 해리엇을 위한 그의 고마움 때문이야.\n\n# 제1권 7장\n\n미스터 엘턴이 런던으로 떠난 바로 그날, 엠마가 벗을 위해 솜씨를 발휘할 새로운 기회가 찾아왔다. 해리엇은 여느 때처럼 아침을 먹은 지 얼마 되지 않아 하트필드에 와 있었고, 한동안 머물다가 저녁상에 맞추어 다시 오기로 하고 집으로 돌아갔다. 그런데 이야기했던 것보다 일찍, 들떠서 황급한 얼굴로 돌아와, 무언가 예사롭지 않은 일이 일어났다며 어서 털어놓고 싶어 안달이었다. 반 분이 채 못 되어 모든 사연이 쏟아져 나왔다. 고더드 부인 댁으로 돌아가자마자 들으니, 미스터 마틴이 한 시간 전에 다녀갔는데, 해리엇이 집에 없고 곧 돌아올 기미도 없자 누이 가운데 하나가 보낸 작은 꾸러미를 두고 갔다는 것이었다. 그런데 그 꾸러미를 풀어 보니, 엘리자베스에게 베껴 쓰라고 빌려준 노래 두 곡 말고도 자기 앞으로 온 편지가 한 통 들어 있었고, 그 편지가 바로 그에게서, 미스터 마틴에게서 온 것으로, 정식 청혼이 담겨 있었다는 것이다. \"누가 그런 걸 생각이나 했겠어요? 어찌나 놀랐는지 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠더라고요. 네, 정말 청혼이에요. 그리고 아주 좋은 편지였어요, 적어도 저는 그렇게 생각했어요. 정말로 저를 무척 사랑하는 것처럼 썼더라고요 — 그런데 저는 잘 모르겠어서 — 그래서 미스 우드하우스께 어떻게 해야 좋을지 여쭤보려고 한달음에 달려온 거예요.\" 엠마는 그토록 기뻐하면서도 그토록 망설이는 벗의 모습이 절반쯤은 부끄럽게 여겨졌다.\n\n\"어머나,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"이 청년, 물어보지 않아서 놓치는 일은 없게 하겠다고 단단히 작정했나 봐요. 할 수만 있다면 좋은 데로 연을 맺어 두자는 거죠.\"\n\n\"읽어 보시겠어요?\" 해리엇이 외쳤다. \"제발요. 미스 우드하우스께서 읽어 주시면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n엠마는 권하는 것이 싫지 않았다. 편지를 읽고는 놀랐다. 편지의 문체가 기대보다 훨씬 윗길이었다. 문법에 틀린 데가 없는 것은 물론이요, 글로서도 신사의 체면을 깎을 만한 데가 없었다. 표현은 꾸밈없이 평이했으나 힘 있고 가식이 없었으며, 거기 담긴 마음은 쓴 사람을 크게 돋보이게 했다. 짧았지만, 분별과 따뜻한 연정과 너그러움과 예의, 심지어 섬세한 감정까지 잘 드러나 있었다. 엠마가 편지를 두고 머뭇거리는 동안, 해리엇은 그 의견을 애타게 기다리며 곁에 서서 \"그래서, 그래서요\" 하고 보채다가, 마침내 한마디 보태지 않을 수 없었다. \"좋은 편지인가요? 아니면 너무 짧은가요?\"\n\n\"네, 정말이지 아주 좋은 편지예요.\" 엠마가 다소 천천히 대답했다. \"해리엇, 이렇게 좋은 편지라면, 모든 걸 미루어 볼 때 누이 가운데 하나가 거들어 준 게 분명해요. 요전 날 당신과 이야기 나누던 그 청년이, 온전히 제 힘으로 이만큼 표현할 수 있으리라고는 도무지 상상하기 어려운걸요. 그렇다고 여자의 문체도 아니에요. 아니, 분명 그래요. 너무 힘 있고 간결하거든요. 여자가 쓰기엔 충분히 늘어지지 않았어요. 분명 분별 있는 사람이고, 타고난 재능이 있는 모양이에요 — 생각이 힘 있고 명료한 거죠 — 그런 사람은 펜을 들면 생각이 절로 알맞은 말을 찾아내거든요. 그런 남자들이 더러 있어요. 네, 어떤 부류의 머리인지 알겠어요. 활기차고, 단호하고, 어느 선까지는 감정도 있되, 거칠지는 않은 사람. 해리엇, 내가 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 잘 쓴 편지예요.\" 그러면서 편지를 돌려주었다.\n\n\"그래서요.\" 여전히 기다리던 해리엇이 말했다. \"그래서 — 그럼 — 그럼 저는 어떻게 해야 하죠?\"\n\n\"어떻게 해야 하냐니요! 무엇에 대해서요? 이 편지에 대해서 말하는 거예요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그런데 뭘 망설이는 거예요? 당연히 답장을 해야죠 — 그것도 빨리요.\"\n\n\"네. 그런데 뭐라고 써야 하죠? 미스 우드하우스, 제발 좀 일러 주세요.\"\n\n\"아니, 안 돼요! 편지는 온전히 당신 손으로 쓰는 게 훨씬 나아요. 분명 아주 적절하게 표현할 수 있을 거예요. 당신 뜻이 안 통할 염려는 없어요. 그게 무엇보다 중요한 거고요. 뜻이 분명해야 해요. 흐리멍덩하거나 머뭇거리는 데가 없이요. 그리고 당신이 상대에게 안기는 아픔에 대한 감사와 염려의 표현은, 예의가 요구하는 대로, 청하지 않아도 당신 마음속에 절로 떠오를 거예요. 틀림없어요. 그 사람의 실망을 안타까워하는 기색으로 쓰라고 일러 줄 필요도 없죠.\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 그분을 거절해야 한다고 보시는 거네요.\" 해리엇이 고개를 떨구며 말했다.\n\n\"거절해야 하느냐니요! 해리엇, 그게 무슨 말이에요? 그 점을 두고 망설이는 거예요? 나는 — 아니, 미안해요. 어쩌면 내가 잘못 생각하고 있었나 봐요. 당신이 답장의 취지를 두고 망설이는 거라면, 내가 분명 당신을 오해하고 있었어요. 나는 그저 답장을 어떤 말로 쓸지만 의논하는 줄 알았거든요.\"\n\n해리엇은 잠자코 있었다. 엠마는 다소 거리를 둔 태도로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"받아들이는 쪽으로 답장할 생각이군요. 내가 보기엔요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그렇지 않아요. 그러니까, 그럴 생각이 — 저는 어떻게 해야 하죠? 어떻게 하라고 일러 주시겠어요? 제발, 미스 우드하우스, 제가 어떻게 해야 하는지 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"나는 아무 조언도 하지 않겠어요, 해리엇. 이 일엔 관여하지 않을래요. 이건 당신이 당신 마음과 의논해서 정해야 할 문제예요.\"\n\n\"그분이 저를 그렇게까지 좋아하는 줄은 미처 몰랐어요.\" 해리엇이 편지를 들여다보며 말했다. 엠마는 한동안 입을 다물고 버텼다. 그러나 그 편지의 홀리는 듯한 달콤한 말이 너무 큰 힘을 발휘할까 염려되기 시작하자, 이렇게 말하는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다.\n\n\"해리엇, 나는 이걸 하나의 일반 원칙으로 삼아요. 여자가 어떤 남자를 받아들여야 할지 말지 망설인다면, 그건 분명 거절해야 한다는 뜻이라고요. '네' 하기를 머뭇거릴 정도라면 곧장 '아니요'라고 해야 해요. 미심쩍은 마음, 마음의 절반만 가지고 무사히 들어설 수 있는 처지가 아니거든요. 당신보다 나이도 위인 벗으로서, 이만큼은 말해 주는 게 도리라고 생각했어요. 하지만 내가 당신을 좌지우지하려 한다고는 생각하지 말아요.\"\n\n\"어머! 아니에요, 미스 우드하우스는 너무 다정한 분이라 그러실 리가 — 하지만 제가 어떻게 하는 게 제일 좋을지 그것만 일러 주시면 — 아니, 아니에요, 그런 뜻이 아니라 — 말씀하신 대로, 마음이란 단단히 정해야 하는 거죠 — 머뭇거려선 안 되고요 — 아주 중대한 일이니까요. 어쩌면 '아니요'라고 하는 편이 더 안전할지도 몰라요. '아니요'라고 하는 게 낫다고 보세요?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 있어도,\" 엠마가 너그러이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"나는 이렇게도 저렇게도 권하지 않을 거예요. 당신 자신의 행복은 당신이 가장 잘 판단할 수 있어요. 만약 다른 누구보다 미스터 마틴이 좋다면, 지금껏 함께해 본 사람 중 그분이 가장 마음에 드는 사람이라고 생각한다면, 망설일 까닭이 어디 있겠어요? 얼굴이 붉어지는군요, 해리엇. 지금 이 순간, 그런 조건에 들어맞는 다른 누군가가 떠오르는 건 아니에요? 해리엇, 해리엇, 자신을 속이지 말아요. 고마움과 동정에 휩쓸리지 말고요. 지금 이 순간, 누구를 생각하고 있죠?\"\n\n징조가 좋았다. 해리엇은 대답 대신 당황하여 고개를 돌리고, 생각에 잠긴 채 난롯가에 서 있었다. 편지는 아직 손에 쥐고 있었지만, 이제는 무심결에 만지작거리며 비틀고 있을 뿐이었다. 엠마는 그 결과를 안달하며 기다렸으나, 강한 희망이 없지 않았다. 마침내 해리엇이 다소 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스, 의견을 안 주시겠다니, 제 힘으로 할 수 있는 데까지 해 봐야겠지요. 그래서 이제 완전히 결심했어요. 정말이지 거의 마음을 굳혔어요 — 미스터 마틴을 거절하기로요. 제가 옳다고 보세요?\"\n\n\"더없이 옳아요, 더없이 옳고말고요, 사랑하는 해리엇. 당신은 마땅히 해야 할 일을 하고 있는 거예요. 당신이 조금이라도 갈피를 못 잡는 동안엔 내 마음을 드러내지 않았지만, 이제 이렇게 완전히 결심했으니 나도 망설임 없이 찬성해요. 사랑하는 해리엇, 이 일이 나는 기뻐요. 당신과의 교제를 잃었다면 나는 슬펐을 거예요. 미스터 마틴과 결혼했다면 분명 그렇게 됐을 테니까요. 당신이 조금이라도 흔들리는 동안엔 좌지우지하고 싶지 않아서 아무 말도 안 했지만, 그건 내게 벗 하나를 잃는 일이었을 거예요. 나는 애비 밀 농장의 미시즈 로버트 마틴을 찾아갈 수는 없었을 테니까요. 이제 당신을 영원히 내 곁에 둘 수 있게 됐어요.\"\n\n해리엇은 자기가 처했던 위험을 짐작도 못 하고 있었으나, 그 생각이 떠오르자 큰 충격으로 다가왔다.\n\n\"저를 찾아오실 수 없었을 거라고요!\" 그녀가 질겁한 얼굴로 외쳤다. \"네, 정말 그러셨겠죠. 그런데 저는 그런 건 미처 생각도 못 했어요. 그건 정말 너무 끔찍했을 거예요! 큰일 날 뻔했네요! 미스 우드하우스, 저는 세상 그 무엇과도 당신과 가깝게 지내는 기쁨과 영광을 바꾸지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 해리엇, 당신을 잃는 건 내게 모진 아픔이었을 거예요. 하지만 어쩔 수 없는 일이었겠죠. 당신은 스스로를 모든 좋은 사교계 바깥으로 내던지는 셈이 됐을 테고요. 나는 당신을 포기할 수밖에 없었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"어머나! 그걸 제가 어떻게 견뎠겠어요! 다시는 하트필드에 못 온다면 저는 죽고 말았을 거예요!\"\n\n\"이 다정하고 사랑스러운 사람! 당신이 애비 밀 농장으로 쫓겨 가다니! 당신이 평생토록 무식하고 천한 사람들 틈에 갇혀 지내다니! 그 청년이 어떻게 그런 걸 청할 배짱이 났는지 모르겠어요. 자기를 어지간히 대단하게 여기는 모양이에요.\"\n\n\"그분이 대체로 우쭐대는 사람이라곤 생각하지 않아요.\" 해리엇이 그런 비난에 양심이 걸려 말했다. \"적어도 마음씨가 아주 착한 분이고, 저는 늘 그분께 무척 고마운 마음일 거예요. 또 아주 좋게 생각하고 — 하지만 그건 완전히 다른 얘기죠 — 게다가 아시다시피, 그분이 저를 좋아한다고 해서 제가 꼭 — 그리고 솔직히 고백하자면, 여기 드나들면서부터 저도 여러 사람을 보았는걸요 — 그래서 그분들을 견주어 본다면, 인물이며 몸가짐이며 도무지 비교가 안 돼요. 어떤 분은 정말이지 그렇게 잘생기고 유쾌한걸요. 그래도 저는 정말로 미스터 마틴을 아주 호감 가는 청년이라 생각하고, 아주 좋게 보고 있어요. 그리고 저를 그토록 마음에 두고 — 그런 편지까지 써 주시고 — 하지만 당신을 떠나는 일이라면, 그건 어떤 경우에도 하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 고마워요, 내 사랑스러운 작은 벗. 우리는 헤어지지 않을 거예요. 여자가 그저 청혼을 받았다고 해서, 또는 상대가 자기를 마음에 두고 그럭저럭 봐줄 만한 편지를 쓸 줄 안다고 해서 결혼해야 하는 건 아니에요.\"\n\n\"어머, 그럼요. 게다가 편지도 짧기만 한걸요.\"\n\n엠마는 벗의 형편없는 취향이 느껴졌으나, 그냥 넘기며 말했다. \"정말 그래요. 그리고 하루 종일 시도 때도 없이 거슬릴 상스러운 태도를 두고, 남편이 편지 하나는 잘 쓴다는 게 위안이 된다 한들 그게 얼마나 되겠어요.\"\n\n\"아! 네, 정말요. 편지 같은 건 아무도 신경 안 쓰죠. 중요한 건 마음에 드는 사람들과 늘 행복하게 지내는 거고요. 저는 그분을 거절하기로 완전히 마음먹었어요. 그런데 어떻게 하죠? 뭐라고 써야 하죠?\"\n\n엠마는 답장에 어려울 게 없으리라고 안심시키며 곧장 쓰라고 권했고, 그러면 도와주리라는 기대에 해리엇은 이에 따랐다. 엠마는 계속 도움 같은 건 필요 없다고 우겼지만, 실은 문장 하나하나를 짓는 데 손을 보탰다. 답장을 쓰느라 그의 편지를 다시 들여다보는 일이 마음을 자꾸 누그러뜨리는 통에, 단호한 표현 몇 마디로 그녀를 다잡아 주는 것이 특히 필요했다. 게다가 해리엇은 그를 불행하게 만든다는 생각에 어찌나 마음을 졸이고, 그의 어머니와 누이들이 어떻게 생각하고 무어라 말할지에 어찌나 마음을 쓰며, 그들이 자기를 배은망덕한 사람으로 여기지 않을까 어찌나 노심초사하던지, 엠마는 만약 그 청년이 그 순간 눈앞에 나타나기라도 했다면 결국 받아들여지고 말았으리라고 생각했다.\n\n그래도 이 편지는 다 쓰여 봉인되어 부쳐졌다. 일은 마무리되었고, 해리엇은 무사했다. 그녀는 저녁 내내 다소 기운이 없었으나, 엠마는 그 갸륵한 아쉬움을 너그러이 헤아려 주었고, 때로는 자기 애정을 말하며, 때로는 미스터 엘턴의 생각을 끄집어내며 그 마음을 달래 주었다.\n\n\"다시는 애비 밀에 초대받지 못하겠죠.\" 다소 서글픈 어조로 한 말이었다.\n\n\"설사 초대받는다 해도, 나는 당신과 헤어지는 걸 도무지 견딜 수 없을 거예요, 내 해리엇. 당신은 하트필드에 너무나 꼭 필요한 사람이라, 애비 밀에 내줄 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 저도 거기 가고 싶을 일은 결코 없을 거예요. 저는 하트필드에서만 행복하니까요.\"\n\n얼마쯤 지나서는 이런 말이었다. \"고더드 부인이 이번 일을 아시면 무척 놀라실 것 같아요. 미스 내시도 분명 그럴 거고요 — 미스 내시는 자기 언니가 아주 잘 결혼했다고 여기는데, 그래 봐야 포목상인걸요.\"\n\n\"학교 선생이 그보다 더한 자만이나 까다로움을 보인다면 안타까운 일이겠죠, 해리엇. 장담컨대 미스 내시는 당신이 이렇게 결혼할 기회를 얻은 걸 부러워할 거예요. 이런 정도의 정복마저 그분 눈에는 값져 보일 테니까요. 당신에게 더 나은 무언가가 있다는 건, 짐작건대 그분은 전혀 모르고 있을 거고요. 어떤 분의 관심이 아직 하이베리의 잡담거리에 올랐을 리는 없으니까요. 지금까지 그분의 눈길과 몸가짐이 무얼 말하는지 알아챈 건, 당신과 나뿐일 거예요.\"\n\n해리엇은 얼굴을 붉히며 미소 지었고, 사람들이 자기를 그토록 좋아하는 게 이상하다는 식의 말을 했다. 미스터 엘턴 생각은 분명 마음을 들뜨게 했다. 그러나 여전히, 얼마 지나자 그녀는 거절당한 미스터 마틴 쪽으로 다시 마음이 여려졌다.\n\n\"이제 그분이 제 편지를 받았겠죠.\" 그녀가 나직이 말했다. \"다들 어떻게 하고 있을까요 — 누이들도 알게 됐을까요 — 그분이 괴로워하면 누이들도 같이 괴로워할 텐데. 그분이 너무 마음 아파하지 않으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"멀리 있는 우리 벗들 중에 좀 더 즐겁게 지내는 사람을 생각하자고요.\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"어쩌면 지금 이 순간, 미스터 엘턴은 자기 어머니와 누이들에게 당신 그림을 보여 주면서, 실물은 이보다 얼마나 더 아름다운지 늘어놓고, 대여섯 번이나 졸린 끝에야 마침내 당신 이름을, 당신의 그 사랑스러운 이름을 들려주고 있을지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"제 그림이요! 하지만 그분은 그림을 본드 가에 두고 가셨는걸요.\"\n\n\"그랬어요? 그렇다면 내가 미스터 엘턴을 도통 잘못 본 거겠죠. 아니에요, 내 다정하고 겸손한 해리엇, 믿어도 좋아요. 그 그림은 내일 그분이 말에 오르기 직전까지는 본드 가에 들어가지 않을 거예요. 오늘 저녁 내내 그건 그분의 동무요, 위안이요, 기쁨이에요. 그게 그분의 속내를 가족에게 열어 보이고, 당신을 그들 사이에 소개하며, 그 자리에 우리 본성에서 가장 즐거운 감정들 — 간절한 호기심과 따뜻한 호의 — 을 퍼뜨리고 있는 거죠. 그들의 상상이 지금 얼마나 들뜨고, 얼마나 활기차고, 얼마나 미심쩍어하고, 얼마나 분주할까요!\"\n\n해리엇은 다시 미소 지었고, 그 미소는 점점 짙어졌다.\n\n# 제1권 8장\n\n해리엇은 그날 밤 하트필드에서 묵었다. 지난 몇 주 동안 그녀는 그곳에서 보내는 시간이 절반을 넘었고, 차츰 제 방 하나를 따로 갖다시피 하게 되었다. 엠마는 모든 면에서, 가장 안전하고 가장 다정한 길은 지금 같은 때 되도록 그녀를 곁에 두는 것이라 판단했다. 해리엇은 이튿날 아침 한두 시간 고더드 부인 댁에 다녀와야 했으나, 그러고 나서 하트필드로 돌아와 며칠간 정식으로 머물기로 정해 두었다.\n\n해리엇이 자리를 비운 사이 미스터 나이틀리가 찾아와 미스터 우드하우스와 엠마와 함께 한동안 앉아 있었다. 미스터 우드하우스는 본디 산책을 나갈 작정이었던지라, 딸의 권유에 미루지 않기로 했고, 두 사람의 청에 못 이겨 — 본인의 예의범절로는 마음에 걸려 하면서도 — 그 일을 위해 미스터 나이틀리를 두고 자리를 떴다. 격식 따위는 도무지 모르는 미스터 나이틀리는 짧고 단호한 대답으로, 상대편의 장황한 사과와 정중한 망설임과 우스운 대조를 이루었다.\n\n\"그럼, 미스터 나이틀리, 양해해 주신다면, 제가 무척 무례한 짓을 한다고 여기지 않으신다면, 엠마의 권유대로 십오 분쯤 나갔다 오겠소. 해가 났으니 가능할 때 세 바퀴를 돌아 두는 편이 좋겠지. 격식 없이 대하는 것이오, 미스터 나이틀리. 우리 같은 병약한 사람은 특권이 있다고 여긴다오.\"\n\n\"어르신, 저를 손님 취급하지 마십시오.\"\n\n\"내 딸이라는 훌륭한 대리인을 남겨 두고 가는 것이니. 엠마가 기꺼이 상대해 드릴 게요. 그러니 부디 양해해 주시면 세 바퀴를 — 겨울 산책을 다녀오리다.\"\n\n\"더없이 좋은 일이지요, 어르신.\"\n\n\"동행을 청하고 싶소만, 미스터 나이틀리, 나는 걸음이 몹시 느려서 내 보폭이 지루하실 게요. 게다가 당신은 돈웰 애비까지 또 먼 길을 걸어가야 하지 않소.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 어르신, 고맙습니다. 저도 지금 막 일어서려던 참입니다. 그리고 어르신께서 어서 다녀오실수록 좋겠습니다. 외투를 가져다 드리고 정원 문을 열어 드리지요.\"\n\n마침내 미스터 우드하우스가 나갔다. 그러나 미스터 나이틀리는 곧바로 따라 일어서는 대신 다시 앉았으니, 좀 더 이야기를 나누고 싶은 눈치였다. 그는 해리엇 이야기를 꺼냈는데, 엠마가 일찍이 들어 본 적 없을 만큼 자청해서 칭찬하는 투였다.\n\n\"나는 자네만큼 그 아이의 미모를 높이 치지는 않네.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 예쁘장한 아이인 건 분명하고, 성품도 아주 좋게 보아도 되겠다 싶어. 그 아이의 성격은 어울리는 상대에 따라 달라지겠지만, 좋은 손에 맡겨지면 쓸 만한 여인으로 자라날 거야.\"\n\n\"그렇게 봐 주시니 기뻐요. 그리고 그 좋은 손이라는 것도, 부족하지 않기를 바라고요.\"\n\n\"이런,\" 그가 말했다. \"칭찬을 듣고 싶어 안달이군. 그래, 말해 주지. 자네가 그 아이를 나아지게 한 건 맞아. 여학생티 나던 키득거림을 고쳐 놓았으니, 정말 자네 덕을 톡톡히 본 셈이야.\"\n\n\"고마워요. 제가 조금이나마 도움이 됐다고 믿지 못한다면 정말 면목이 없겠죠. 하지만 칭찬할 만한 데서 칭찬해 주는 사람이 다들 그런 건 아니에요. 미스터 나이틀리는 저한테 칭찬을 퍼부어 기를 죽이는 일이 흔치 않잖아요.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침에 그 아이가 다시 온다고 했지?\"\n\n\"이제 곧 올 거예요. 본인이 작정한 시간보다 벌써 늦었는걸요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일로 지체되는 모양이군. 손님이 왔는지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"하이베리의 수다쟁이들이겠죠! 진절머리 나는 사람들 같으니!\"\n\n\"해리엇은 자네라면 진절머리 친다고 할 사람들을 다 그렇게 여기지는 않을지도 몰라.\"\n\n엠마는 이것이 반박할 수 없을 만큼 옳은 말임을 알았기에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 잠시 후 그가 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"시간이나 장소까지 콕 짚을 생각은 없네만, 자네의 그 어린 친구가 머지않아 자기에게 이로운 소식을 듣게 되리라고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 있다는 건 말해 둬야겠어.\"\n\n\"어머나! 어째서요? 어떤 종류의 소식인데요?\"\n\n\"아주 진지한 종류의 일이야, 분명히.\" 그는 여전히 미소를 띤 채 말했다.\n\n\"아주 진지한 일이라니! 짚이는 건 한 가지뿐인데요 — 누가 그 애를 사랑하고 있죠? 누가 미스터 나이틀리한테 속내를 털어놓던가요?\"\n\n엠마는 미스터 엘턴이 무슨 언질이라도 흘렸기를 절반 넘게 기대했다. 미스터 나이틀리는 누구에게나 벗이자 조언자 같은 사람이었고, 미스터 엘턴이 그를 우러러본다는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다.\n\n\"내가 보기엔,\" 그가 대답했다. \"해리엇 스미스가 머지않아 청혼을 받게 될 거야. 그것도 나무랄 데 하나 없는 쪽에서 말이지. 로버트 마틴이 바로 그 사람이네. 올여름 애비 밀에 다녀온 게 일을 단단히 매듭지은 모양이야. 그 친구는 사랑에 흠뻑 빠져 그 아이와 결혼하려고 마음먹었어.\"\n\n\"참 고마운 분이네요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"하지만 해리엇이 그분과 결혼할 마음이 있다고 확신하시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 그럼 청혼을 하려 든다고 해 두지. 그러면 됐나? 그 친구가 이틀 전 저녁에 일부러 그 일을 의논하려고 애비로 찾아왔어. 내가 자기와 자기 식구 모두를 깊이 아낀다는 걸 알고, 나를 자기의 가장 좋은 벗 가운데 하나로 여기는 모양이야. 이렇게 일찍 살림을 차리는 게 경솔한 일이 아닐지, 그 아이가 너무 어리다고 봐야 할지 — 한마디로 자기 선택을 내가 전적으로 찬성하는지 물으러 온 거지. 어쩌면 (특히 자네가 그 아이를 그토록 떠받든 뒤로) 그 아이가 자기보다 윗줄 사람으로 여겨지지 않을까 하는 걱정도 좀 있었던 게야. 나는 그가 한 말이 죄다 무척 마음에 들었어. 로버트 마틴만큼 분별 있는 소리를 하는 사람을 나는 본 적이 없네. 그는 늘 요점을 짚어 말하지. 솔직하고, 거침없고, 판단력이 아주 뛰어나. 자기 사정과 계획, 결혼하게 되면 식구들이 어떻게 할 작정인지까지 전부 다 이야기해 주더군. 그는 아들로 보나 형제로 보나 훌륭한 청년이야. 나는 망설임 없이 결혼하라고 일러 줬어. 자기가 결혼을 감당할 형편이 된다는 걸 내게 입증해 보였고, 그렇다면 이보다 더 나은 선택은 없으리라고 나도 확신했으니까. 그 처자도 함께 칭찬해 주었고, 결국 그를 아주 흐뭇하게 돌려보냈지. 그가 전에는 내 의견을 대수롭잖게 여겼더라도, 그때만큼은 나를 높이 보았을 거야. 그리고 분명 세상에 둘도 없는 좋은 벗이자 조언자라고 여기며 우리 집을 나섰을 게야. 이게 그저께 밤의 일이지. 자, 우리가 무리 없이 짐작하건대, 그가 그 아이에게 말을 꺼내기까지 오래 시간을 끌었을 리는 없어. 그런데 어제는 말을 꺼낸 것 같지 않으니, 오늘쯤 고더드 부인 댁에 가 있을 법도 하지. 그러면 그 아이는 손님 때문에 발이 묶인 거고, 그 손님을 진절머리 나는 사람이라고는 전혀 여기지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"그런데 미스터 나이틀리,\" 이 이야기의 상당 부분을 혼자 미소 지으며 듣고 있던 엠마가 말했다. \"미스터 마틴이 어제 말을 꺼내지 않았다는 걸 어떻게 아세요?\"\n\n\"물론,\" 그가 놀라며 대답했다. \"딱 잘라 안다는 건 아니지. 하지만 미루어 짐작할 수 있잖나. 그 아이가 온종일 자네와 함께 있지 않았나?\"\n\n\"이런,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"들려주신 이야기에 대한 답례로 한 가지 알려 드릴게요. 그분은 어제 말을 꺼냈어요 — 그러니까, 편지를 썼고, 거절당했죠.\"\n\n이 말은 곧이들리기까지 몇 번을 되풀이해야 했다. 미스터 나이틀리는 실제로 놀라움과 언짢음으로 얼굴이 붉어진 채 분연히 자리에서 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그 아이는 내가 여태 여긴 것보다 더한 멍청이로군. 그 어리석은 처자가 대체 무슨 짓인가?\"\n\n\"어머! 그럼요.\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"여자가 청혼을 거절할 수 있다는 건 남자한테 늘 도무지 이해 못 할 일이죠. 남자는 여자란 자기에게 청하는 사람이라면 누구든 받아들일 준비가 돼 있다고 늘 상상하니까요.\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼! 남자는 그런 상상 따위 안 해. 한데 이게 대체 어찌 된 영문인가? 해리엇 스미스가 로버트 마틴을 거절했다고? 그게 사실이라면 미친 짓이야. 하지만 자네가 잘못 안 거였으면 좋겠군.\"\n\n\"제가 그 애의 답장을 봤어요! 그보다 더 분명할 순 없죠.\"\n\n\"자네가 그 답장을 봤다 — 자네가 그 답장을 쓰기도 했겠지. 엠마, 이건 자네 짓이야. 자네가 그 아이를 부추겨 거절하게 한 거야.\"\n\n\"설령 그랬다 해도 (그렇다고 인정하는 건 결코 아니지만요) 잘못했다는 느낌은 안 들 거예요. 미스터 마틴은 아주 점잖은 청년이지만, 그분을 해리엇과 동등하다고는 인정할 수 없거든요. 오히려 그분이 감히 그 애한테 말을 건넨 게 좀 놀라울 정도예요. 미스터 나이틀리 말씀으로는 그분도 얼마간 망설임이 있었던 모양인데. 그 망설임을 끝내 떨쳐 버린 게 안타까운 일이죠.\"\n\n\"해리엇과 동등하지 않다니!\" 미스터 나이틀리가 큰 소리로 격하게 외쳤다. 그러고는 잠시 후 한결 가라앉은, 그러나 날 선 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그래, 그는 정말 그 아이와 동등하지 않아. 처지로 보나 분별로 보나 그가 그 아이보다 훨씬 윗길이니까. 엠마, 그 처자에게 홀딱 빠져서 자네 눈이 멀었어. 해리엇 스미스가 출생으로 보나, 천성으로 보나, 교육으로 보나, 로버트 마틴보다 윗줄과 인연을 맺을 무슨 자격이 있나? 누구의 자식인지도 모르는 사생아에, 정해진 재산이라곤 십중팔구 한 푼도 없고, 변변한 친척이라곤 분명히 하나도 없어. 그저 평범한 학교의 기숙 하숙생으로나 알려졌을 뿐이지. 분별 있는 처자도 아니고, 무얼 아는 처자도 아니야. 쓸모 있는 건 하나도 배운 게 없고, 스스로 익히기엔 너무 어리고 너무 단순하지. 그 나이에 무슨 경험이 있을 리 없고, 그 변변찮은 머리로는 앞으로도 쓸 만한 경험을 얻을 가망이 별로 없어. 예쁘고 성격이 좋다 — 그게 다야. 내가 그 혼사를 일러 주면서 유일하게 마음에 걸렸던 건 그 친구 쪽이었어. 그 아이가 그의 자격에는 못 미치고, 그에게는 안 좋은 인연이라는 점에서 말이지. 재산으로 따지면 그가 십중팔구 훨씬 나은 짝을 만날 수 있을 거고, 이치를 아는 반려나 쓸모 있는 조력자로 따지면 이보다 못한 선택은 없으리라 여겼으니까. 하지만 사랑에 빠진 남자에게 그렇게 따져 말할 수는 없었고, 그 아이가 나쁜 데 없는 아이라는 것, 그의 손 같은 좋은 손에 맡겨지면 쉽사리 바로 이끌려 아주 잘될 그런 성품을 지녔다는 것을 믿어 보기로 한 거야. 이 혼사의 이득은 죄다 그 아이 쪽에 있다고 느꼈고, 그 아이의 더없는 행운을 두고 다들 한목소리로 떠들어 댈 거라는 데 추호의 의심도 없었어 (지금도 없고). 자네의 만족까지도 나는 확신했지. 자네 친구가 그토록 잘 자리 잡는 것이니, 그 아이가 하이베리를 떠나는 걸 자네가 아쉬워하지 않으리라는 생각이 곧장 들었거든. '아무리 해리엇을 편애하는 엠마라도 이건 좋은 혼사라고 여길 거야.' 이렇게 혼잣말한 게 기억나는군.\"\n\n\"그런 말씀을 하실 만큼 엠마를 그토록 모르신다는 데 놀라지 않을 수가 없네요. 뭐라고요! 농장주를 (그리고 미스터 마틴은 아무리 분별 있고 미덕이 넘쳐도 그 이상은 아니죠) 제 친한 친구의 좋은 혼처라 여긴다고요! 제가 결코 제 지인으로 인정할 수 없는 남자와 결혼하려고 하이베리를 떠나는데 아쉬워하지 않는다고요! 제게 그런 마음이 있을 수 있다고 여기시다니 의아하네요. 분명히 말씀드리지만, 제 마음은 전혀 달라요. 미스터 나이틀리의 말씀은 결코 공정하지 못하다고 봐요. 해리엇의 자격에 대해 정당하지 않으세요. 그건 저뿐 아니라 다른 사람들도 아주 다르게 헤아릴 거예요. 미스터 마틴이 둘 중 더 부유할지는 몰라도, 사교계의 지위로는 의심할 바 없이 그 애보다 아래예요. 그 애가 움직이는 영역은 그분보다 훨씬 위고요. 그건 격을 떨어뜨리는 일이에요.\"\n\n\"점잖고 지각 있는 신사 농장주와 결혼하는 게, 사생아 신분에 무지한 처자에게 격을 떨어뜨리는 일이라니!\"\n\n\"출생의 사정으로 말하자면, 법적인 뜻으로는 그 애를 '아무것도 아닌 사람'이라 부를 수 있을지 몰라도, 상식으로는 통하지 않아요. 함께 자란 사람들보다 아래로 내몰려 남의 잘못 값을 그 애가 치를 까닭은 없죠. 그 애 아버지가 신사라는 건 — 그것도 재산 있는 신사라는 건 거의 의심할 여지가 없어요. 그 애한테 주는 돈도 아주 넉넉하고요. 그 애의 교양이나 안락을 위해 인색하게 군 적이 한 번도 없어요. 그 애가 신사의 딸이라는 건 제겐 명백한 사실이에요. 그 애가 신사의 딸들과 어울린다는 것도, 제 생각엔 아무도 부정 못 할 거고요. 그 애는 미스터 로버트 마틴보다 윗길이에요.\"\n\n\"그 아이의 부모가 누구든,\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"그 아이를 맡아 키운 이가 누구든, 자네가 좋은 사교계라 부를 곳에 그 아이를 들여보내는 건 애당초 그들 계획에 들어 있지 않았던 모양이야. 변변찮은 교육을 받은 뒤 그 아이는 고더드 부인 손에 맡겨져 제 알아서 살아가게 되었지. 한마디로 고더드 부인의 부류 안에서 움직이고, 고더드 부인의 지인들과 어울리도록 말이야. 그 아이의 후견인들은 분명 그 정도면 그 아이에게 충분하다고 여겼고, 실제로도 충분했어. 그 아이 자신도 그 이상을 바라지 않았고. 자네가 그 아이를 친구로 삼기로 마음먹기 전까지, 그 아이는 제 부류를 싫어하는 마음도 없었고 그 너머를 넘보는 야심도 없었어. 여름 내내 마틴네와 더없이 행복하게 지냈잖나. 그때는 우월감 따위 없었어. 지금 그게 있다면, 자네가 심어 준 거지. 엠마, 자네는 해리엇 스미스에게 좋은 친구가 못 됐어. 로버트 마틴은 그 아이가 자기를 싫어하지 않는다는 확신이 없었다면 이렇게까지 나아가지 않았을 거야. 나는 그를 잘 알아. 그는 진정한 감정이 너무 깊은 사람이라, 제멋대로의 욕심에 기대 되는대로 여자에게 말을 건넬 사람이 아니야. 그리고 자만으로 말하자면, 내가 아는 누구보다도 그것과 거리가 먼 사람이지. 분명히 말해 두는데, 그는 격려를 받았던 거야.\"\n\n이 단정에 곧장 대꾸하지 않는 편이 엠마에게는 가장 편했다. 그녀는 차라리 제가 펴던 논점으로 다시 돌아가기를 택했다.\n\n\"미스터 마틴한테는 아주 열렬한 벗이시군요. 하지만 앞서 말씀드렸듯, 해리엇한테는 부당하세요. 해리엇이 좋은 혼처를 만날 자격은 말씀하시는 것만큼 하찮지 않아요. 영리한 처자는 아니지만, 미스터 나이틀리가 아시는 것보다 분별이 있고, 그 지각을 그렇게 깔보듯 말씀하실 만큼은 아니에요. 하지만 그 점은 접어 두고, 미스터 나이틀리 말씀대로 그저 예쁘고 성격 좋은 게 다라고 쳐도, 한 가지만 말씀드릴게요. 그 애가 그것들을 지닌 정도로 보면, 세상 사람들 일반에게는 결코 사소한 장점이 아니에요. 사실 그 애는 아름다운 처자고, 백 사람 중 아흔아홉은 그렇게 여길 게 틀림없으니까요. 그리고 남자들이 미모라는 문제에서 흔히 여겨지는 것보다 훨씬 철학적인 존재임이 드러나기 전까지는 — 그러니까 잘생긴 얼굴 대신 박식한 정신과 사랑에 빠지기 전까지는 — 해리엇만 한 사랑스러움을 지닌 처자는 흠모받고 구애받을 게 확실하고, 여럿 가운데서 고를 힘이 있으며, 따라서 까다롭게 굴 자격이 있어요. 그 애의 좋은 성품 또한 그리 가벼운 자격이 아니에요. 거기엔 더없이 진실하고 온전한 성격과 태도의 다정함, 자기 자신에 대한 아주 겸손한 평가, 남을 기꺼이 좋아하는 한결같은 마음이 다 들어 있으니까요. 그런 미모와 그런 성품이야말로 여자가 지닐 수 있는 최고의 자격이라고 남자들이 대체로 여기지 않는다면, 제가 크게 잘못 안 거겠죠.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 엠마, 자네가 그 멀쩡한 분별을 그렇게 함부로 써 대는 걸 듣고 있자니, 나마저 거의 그 말이 맞다고 여길 지경이군. 그렇게 잘못 쓸 바엔 차라리 분별이 없는 편이 낫겠어.\"\n\n\"그럼요!\" 그녀가 장난스럽게 외쳤다. \"그게 남자분들 모두의 속마음인 줄 다 알아요. 해리엇 같은 처자야말로 모든 남자가 좋아하는 바로 그런 여자라는 걸요 — 단번에 감각을 홀리는 동시에 분별까지 만족시키는 여자죠. 어머! 해리엇은 골라잡으면 돼요. 미스터 나이틀리, 당신이 만약 결혼이라도 하신다면, 그 애야말로 당신께 꼭 맞는 여자예요. 그런데 그 애가 이제 열일곱에, 막 세상에 발을 들이고, 이제 막 사람들 입에 오르내리기 시작한 마당에, 첫 청혼을 받아들이지 않았다고 의아해할 일인가요? 아니죠 — 부디 그 애한테 둘러볼 시간을 좀 주세요.\"\n\n\"나는 늘 그게 아주 어리석은 사귐이라 여겨 왔어.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 잠시 후 말했다. \"생각을 내색하지 않았을 뿐이지. 하지만 이제 보니 해리엇한테는 아주 불행한 사귐이 되겠군. 자네는 그 아이한테 제 미모며 자기가 누릴 자격이 있다는 것에 관해 그런 생각을 잔뜩 불어넣어, 머지않아 그 아이 닿는 범위 안엔 그 아이에게 어울릴 만한 사람이 하나도 없게 될 거야. 약한 머리에 작용하는 허영은 온갖 화근을 빚어내지. 젊은 아가씨가 제 기대를 지나치게 높이 끌어올리기만큼 쉬운 일도 없어. 미스 해리엇 스미스가 아무리 예쁜 처자라 해도, 청혼이 그렇게 줄줄이 밀려들지는 않을걸. 자네가 무어라 말하든, 분별 있는 남자들은 어리석은 아내를 원하지 않아. 좋은 집안 남자들은 그런 출신 불분명한 처자와 인연 맺기를 그리 좋아하지 않을 거고, 가장 신중한 남자들은 그 아이 부모의 비밀이 드러날 때 휘말릴 불편과 망신을 두려워할 거야. 그 아이를 로버트 마틴과 결혼시켜. 그러면 그 아이는 평생 안전하고 점잖고 행복할 거야. 하지만 자네가 그 아이더러 대단한 혼처를 바라도록 부추기고, 지체 높고 재산 많은 남자가 아니면 안 된다고 여기도록 가르친다면, 그 아이는 평생 고더드 부인 댁 기숙 하숙생으로 남거나 — 아니면 적어도 (해리엇 스미스는 어떻게든 누군가와 결혼할 처자니까) 자포자기할 만큼 절박해져서야 그 늙은 글쓰기 선생 아들이라도 덥석 붙잡고 기뻐하게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"이 점에 관해선 우리 생각이 너무 달라서, 미스터 나이틀리, 따져 봐야 아무 소용 없겠어요. 서로 더 화만 돋울 뿐이죠. 하지만 제가 그 애를 로버트 마틴과 결혼하게 내버려 둔다는 건 불가능해요. 그 애는 그분을 거절했고, 제가 보기엔 다시 청할 엄두를 내지 못할 만큼 단호하게 거절했어요. 그분을 거절한 데서 비롯될 손해가 무엇이든 그 애는 감수해야겠죠. 거절 자체로 말하자면, 제가 그 애에게 조금 영향을 미쳤을 수도 있다는 건 부정하지 않겠어요. 하지만 분명히 말씀드리는데, 저나 누구나 할 일이 별로 없었어요. 그분 외모는 그 애 마음에 영 거슬리고 태도도 너무 나빠서, 설령 한때 그 애가 그분에게 호의를 품을 마음이 있었다 해도, 지금은 아니에요. 더 나은 사람을 본 적 없을 때라면 그 애가 그분을 견뎌 냈을 수도 있겠다 싶긴 해요. 그분은 그 애 친구들의 오라비였고, 그 애 마음에 들려고 애를 썼으니까요. 그리고 무엇보다 더 나은 사람을 본 적이 없었으니 (그게 그분한테 큰 도움이 됐을 거예요) 애비 밀에 있는 동안엔 그 애도 그분을 그리 싫어하지 않았을지 몰라요. 하지만 이제는 사정이 달라졌어요. 그 애는 이제 신사가 어떤 사람인지 알거든요. 교육과 태도가 신사다운 사람이 아니고서는 해리엇한테 가망이 없어요.\"\n\n\"허튼소리, 들어 본 중에 가장 새빨간 허튼소리야!\" 미스터 나이틀리가 외쳤다. \"로버트 마틴의 태도엔 분별과 진실함과 쾌활함이 받쳐 주고 있고, 그의 마음엔 해리엇 스미스가 이해할 수 있는 것보다 더 참된 기품이 깃들어 있어.\"\n\n엠마는 아무 대꾸도 않고 짐짓 명랑하게 무심한 척하려 했으나, 실은 마음이 불편했고 그가 어서 가 주기를 몹시 바랐다. 그녀는 제가 한 일을 뉘우치지는 않았다. 여자의 권리와 품위에 관한 그런 문제라면 그보다 자기가 더 나은 판단자라고 여전히 여겼다. 그러면서도 그의 판단 일반에 대해서는 몸에 밴 일종의 존중이 있었던지라, 그것이 그토록 큰 소리로 자기를 거스르는 것이 싫었다. 게다가 그가 화난 채로 바로 맞은편에 앉아 있는 것이 무척 거북했다. 이 불편한 침묵 속에 몇 분이 흘렀고, 엠마 쪽에서 날씨 이야기로 한 번 운을 떼 보았으나 그는 대꾸하지 않았다. 그는 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그 생각의 결과가 마침내 이런 말로 나타났다.\n\n\"로버트 마틴이 잃은 건 별로 크지 않아 — 그렇게 여길 수만 있다면 말이지. 그가 오래지 않아 그렇게 여기게 되기를 바라네. 자네가 해리엇을 두고 무슨 속셈인지는 자네만 알겠지. 하지만 중매하기를 좋아하는 걸 자네가 숨기지 않으니, 무슨 속셈이며 계획이며 꿍꿍이가 있으리라 보는 게 마땅하겠지. 벗으로서 한마디만 귀띔하겠는데, 만약 엘턴이 그 상대라면, 그건 죄다 헛수고가 될 거라고 보네.\"\n\n엠마는 웃으며 아니라고 발뺌했다. 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"분명히 말하지만, 엘턴은 안 돼. 엘턴은 아주 괜찮은 사람이고, 하이베리의 아주 점잖은 부목사야. 하지만 경솔한 혼사를 맺을 사람은 결코 아니지. 그는 누구 못지않게 좋은 수입의 가치를 잘 알아. 엘턴이 감상적으로 말할지는 몰라도, 행동은 분별 있게 할 거야. 자네가 해리엇의 자격을 잘 아는 만큼이나 그는 제 자격을 잘 알고 있어. 자기가 아주 잘생긴 청년이고 어딜 가나 큰 인기를 누린다는 걸 알지. 그리고 남자들만 있는 자리에서 거리낌 없이 말할 때 그가 늘 하는 이야기로 미루어 보면, 나는 그가 자기 자신을 함부로 내던질 생각이 없다고 확신하네. 자기 누이들과 친한 어느 처녀들 일가 이야기를 그가 아주 신나게 하는 걸 들은 적이 있는데, 그 처녀들은 하나같이 이만 파운드씩 가졌다더군.\"\n\n\"정말 고마운 말씀이네요.\" 엠마가 다시 웃으며 말했다. \"제가 만약 미스터 엘턴이 해리엇과 결혼하도록 마음먹고 있었다면, 제 눈을 뜨게 해 주신 게 참 친절한 일이었겠죠. 하지만 지금 저는 그저 해리엇을 제 곁에 두고 싶을 뿐이에요. 중매는 정말이지 그만뒀어요. 랜덜스에서 제가 해낸 일에 견줄 만한 건 다시는 바랄 수 없을 테니까요. 한창 잘나갈 때 손을 떼려고요.\"\n\n\"그럼 안녕히.\" 그가 일어서며 불쑥 걸어 나가며 말했다. 그는 몹시 언짢았다. 그 청년의 실망이 느껴졌고, 자기가 찬성해 준 탓에 그 일을 부추긴 빌미가 되었다는 게 분했다. 게다가 이 일에 엠마가 손을 댔다고 확신하는 바, 그것이 그의 부아를 한껏 돋우고 있었다.\n\n엠마 역시 언짢은 상태로 남았다. 그러나 그녀가 언짢은 까닭은 그의 경우보다 한층 흐릿했다. 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리만큼 늘 자기 자신에 그토록 완전히 만족하거나, 제 의견은 옳고 상대의 의견은 그르다고 그토록 온전히 확신하지는 못했다. 그는 그녀가 남긴 것보다 훨씬 더 흡족한 자기만족 속에 걸어 나갔다. 그렇다고 그녀가 그리 심하게 풀이 죽은 것은 아니었으니, 약간의 시간과 해리엇의 귀가만으로도 충분히 회복될 만했다. 해리엇이 그토록 오래 안 돌아오는 것이 슬슬 불안해지던 참이었다. 그 청년이 그날 아침 고더드 부인 댁에 찾아와 해리엇을 만나 제 사정을 호소할지도 모른다는 가능성이 불안한 상상을 불러일으켰다. 결국 그런 낭패에 대한 두려움이 가장 큰 불안으로 떠올랐다. 그런데 해리엇이 나타났고, 그것도 아주 기분이 좋은 채로, 오래 자리를 비운 데 그런 까닭이라곤 댈 것 하나 없이 돌아오자, 엠마는 마음이 가라앉는 만족을 느꼈다. 그리고 미스터 나이틀리가 무어라 생각하든 말하든, 자신은 여자의 우정과 여자의 마음이 정당하다 인정할 만한 일밖에 하지 않았다고 확신하게 되었다.\n\n그는 미스터 엘턴에 관해 그녀를 조금 겁먹게 했었다. 그러나 미스터 나이틀리가 자기만큼 그를 눈여겨봤을 리 없다는 것 — 그런 관심을 가지고 본 것도 아니거니와, (미스터 나이틀리가 어떻게 자처하든, 이 정도는 스스로 말해도 되겠지만) 이런 문제에서 자기 같은 관찰자만 한 안목으로 본 것도 아니라는 것, 게다가 그가 그 말을 성급하게, 화난 김에 내뱉었다는 것을 헤아리고 나니, 그녀는 그가 무얼 알고 한 말이라기보다 분에 못 이겨 그렇게 되기를 바라며 한 말이라고 믿을 수 있었다. 그가 자기보다 미스터 엘턴이 거리낌 없이 말하는 것을 더 많이 들었을 수는 있고, 미스터 엘턴이 돈 문제에 관해 경솔하거나 무분별한 성품이 아닐 수도 있다. 오히려 그런 일에 자연스레 신경을 좀 쓰는 편일 수도 있겠지. 하지만 미스터 나이틀리는 온갖 타산적 동기와 맞서 싸우는 강렬한 연정의 힘을 충분히 헤아리지 못한 것이다. 미스터 나이틀리는 그런 연정을 보지 못했으니, 당연히 그 위력 따위 안중에도 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 그것을 너무 많이 보아 온 터라, 그 연정이 본디 합당한 신중함이 일으킬 법한 어떤 망설임도 다 이겨 내리라는 데 한 점 의심이 없었다. 그리고 미스터 엘턴에게는 합당하고 적절한 정도를 넘는 신중함 따위는 없다고 그녀는 아주 확신했다.\n\n해리엇의 명랑한 낯빛과 태도가 엠마의 마음을 안정시켰다. 그녀는 미스터 마틴을 떠올리려고가 아니라 미스터 엘턴 이야기를 하려고 돌아온 것이었다. 미스 내시가 그녀에게 무슨 이야기를 해 줬는데, 그것을 그녀는 곧장 아주 신이 나서 되풀이했다. 미스터 페리가 아픈 아이를 보러 고더드 부인 댁에 왔었고, 미스 내시가 그를 보았는데, 그가 미스 내시에게 이렇게 말했다는 것이다. 어제 클레이턴 파크에서 돌아오는 길에 미스터 엘턴과 마주쳤는데, 놀랍게도 미스터 엘턴이 실제로 런던으로 가는 길이며, 이튿날까지는 돌아오지 않을 작정이더라는 것이었다. 그날이 한 번도 빠진 적 없다고 알려진 휘스트 클럽이 있는 밤인데도 말이다. 미스터 페리가 그 점을 두고 그에게 핀잔을 주며, 가장 잘 치는 사람이 자리를 비우다니 너무하지 않느냐 하고는, 여행을 하루만 미루라고 한참을 설득했지만 소용없었다. 미스터 엘턴은 그대로 가기로 단단히 마음먹은 채, 세상 어떤 유혹에도 미룰 수 없는 볼일이 있어 간다고 아주 의미심장한 투로 말했다는 것이다. 그러면서 무척 부러움 살 만한 임무니, 더없이 소중한 무언가를 전하러 가는 길이니 하는 이야기를 했다고 한다. 미스터 페리는 그 말을 잘 이해할 수 없었으나, 분명 거기엔 어떤 숙녀가 얽혀 있는 게 틀림없다고 여겨 그렇게 말했더니, 미스터 엘턴은 그저 무척 의미심장하게 미소 지을 뿐, 잔뜩 들뜬 채로 말을 달려 가 버렸다는 것이다. 미스 내시가 그녀에게 이 이야기를 죄다 들려주고는 미스터 엘턴에 관해 한참 더 떠들었으며, 그녀를 아주 의미심장하게 바라보면서 이렇게 말했다고 한다. 그의 볼일이 무엇인지 안다고 하는 건 아니지만, 미스터 엘턴이 마음에 둘 만한 여자라면 누구든 세상에서 가장 운 좋은 여자라고 생각한다고. 미모로 보나 상냥함으로 보나 미스터 엘턴에게 견줄 사람은 분명 없으니까.\n* 휘스트: 네 사람이 둘씩 짝을 지어 겨루는 18~19세기 영국 카드놀이로, 브리지의 전신.\n\n# 제1권 9장\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 그녀와 다툴 수 있었을지 몰라도, 엠마는 자기 자신과 다툴 수는 없었다. 그가 어찌나 못마땅해했던지, 다시 하트필드를 찾기까지는 여느 때보다 오래 걸렸다. 그리고 마침내 두 사람이 마주쳤을 때, 그의 굳은 표정은 그녀가 아직 용서받지 못했음을 말해 주었다. 그녀는 미안했지만 뉘우칠 수는 없었다. 오히려 그 뒤 며칠간 두루 펼쳐지는 정황은, 자신의 계획과 처사를 점점 더 정당해 보이게 하고 점점 더 흐뭇하게 만들어 주었다.\n\n미스터 엘턴이 돌아오고 얼마 지나지 않아, 우아하게 액자에 끼운 그 그림이 무사히 당도했다. 그것을 평소 쓰는 거실 벽난로 위에 걸어 두자, 그는 일어서서 그것을 바라보며 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 감탄의 토막말을 한숨처럼 내뱉었다. 해리엇의 마음으로 말하자면, 그녀의 나이와 천성이 허락하는 한도에서 가장 강하고 한결같은 연정으로 또렷이 영글어 가는 것이 눈에 보였다. 엠마는 곧, 미스터 마틴이 미스터 엘턴을 더없이 돋보이게 해 주는 대조 인물로서가 아니라면 달리 기억되는 일은 없으리라는 데에 완전히 만족했다.\n\n유익한 독서와 대화를 잔뜩 시켜 어린 벗의 지성을 길러 주려던 그녀의 포부는, 여태껏 첫 몇 장(章)과 내일은 더 나아가자는 다짐 이상으로 진척된 적이 없었다. 공부하기보다 수다 떠는 편이 훨씬 쉬웠고, 차분한 사실들로 해리엇의 이해력을 넓히거나 단련시키려 애쓰는 것보다, 상상의 나래를 펴 해리엇의 앞날을 그려 보는 편이 훨씬 즐거웠다. 그리고 지금 해리엇을 사로잡은 유일한 문학적 소일거리, 인생의 황혼을 위해 그녀가 마련해 두는 유일한 정신의 양식이라고는, 마주치는 온갖 종류의 수수께끼를 죄다 모아, 벗이 만들어 주고 암호와 트로피 무늬로 꾸며 준 얄팍한 4절판 광택지 공책에 베껴 옮기는 일이었다.\n\n이 문학의 시대에, 그런 수집은 아주 대규모라 해도 드물지 않았다. 고더드 부인 학교의 수석 교사인 미스 내시는 적어도 삼백 편은 적어 두었고, 그녀에게서 처음 그 착상을 얻은 해리엇은 미스 우드하우스의 도움으로 그보다 훨씬 많이 모으기를 바랐다. 엠마는 자신의 창의력과 기억력과 안목으로 거들었다. 게다가 해리엇이 글씨를 아주 예쁘게 쓰는 터라, 그 공책은 양으로 보나 모양으로 보나 일류의 작품이 될 듯했다.\n\n미스터 우드하우스도 두 아가씨 못지않게 이 일에 관심을 두어, 공책에 넣을 만한 것을 떠올려 보려고 자주 애를 썼다. \"내가 젊었을 적엔 영리한 수수께끼가 그렇게나 많았는데 — 어째서 그게 기억이 안 나는지 모르겠구나! 하지만 차차 떠오르겠지.\" 그리고 그것은 언제나 \"키티, 곱지만 얼어붙은 처녀\"로 끝나곤 했다.\n\n이 일에 관해 의논한 그의 좋은 벗 미스터 페리 역시 지금으로서는 수수께끼류라곤 아무것도 떠올리지 못했다. 그러나 그는 페리에게 늘 눈여겨봐 달라고 부탁해 두었고, 페리는 워낙 여기저기 많이 돌아다니는 사람이니 그쪽에서 뭔가 나오지 않을까 생각했다.\n\n하이베리 사람들의 지력을 두루 차출하는 것은 결코 그의 딸이 바라는 바가 아니었다. 엠마가 도움을 청한 사람은 미스터 엘턴 하나뿐이었다. 정말 좋은 수수께끼나 셔레이드, 또는 재담이 생각나거든 좀 보태 달라고 그를 청했다. 그리고 그녀는, 그가 기억을 더듬으며 더없이 골몰하는 모습을 흐뭇하게 지켜보았다. 동시에, 그녀가 알아챈 바로는, 신사답지 못한 것이나 여성에 대한 찬사가 깃들지 않은 것은 한마디도 입에 올리지 않으려고 그가 무척이나 정성스레 조심하는 것도 보였다. 두세 편의 더없이 점잖은 수수께끼는 그의 덕이었다. 그리고 그가 마침내 그 유명한 셔레이드를 떠올려, 자못 감상적으로 읊었을 때의 그 기쁨과 의기양양함이란—\n\n내 첫째는 괴로움을 가리키니,\n    둘째가 그것을 느끼게 마련이라.\n허나 내 전체는 더없이 좋은 약,\n    그 괴로움을 누그러뜨려 낫게 하리라.—\n\n— 그 시를 두고, 우리가 이미 몇 장 앞에 베껴 두었노라고 인정하기가 그녀로서는 자못 미안할 지경이었다.\n* 셔레이드(수수께끼 시): 한 단어를 음절별로 나누어 각 음절과 전체를 시구로 묘사해 알아맞히게 하는 말놀이.\n\n\"미스터 엘턴, 직접 한 편 지어 주시지 그러세요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래야 새것임이 보장되는 유일한 길이잖아요. 그리고 당신께야 그보다 쉬운 일도 없을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다! 저는 평생 그런 걸 지어 본 적이 없습니다, 거의 한 번도요. 이렇게 둔한 사람이라니! 미스 우드하우스조차도—\" 그가 잠시 말을 멈추었다. \"—아니, 미스 스미스조차도 제게 영감을 불어넣지 못할까 두렵습니다.\"\n\n그러나 바로 이튿날, 영감의 증거 비슷한 것이 나타났다. 그가 잠깐 들러, 탁자 위에 종이 한 장을 놓고 갔던 것이다. 그의 말로는 어떤 벗이 자신이 흠모하는 한 젊은 아가씨에게 보낸 셔레이드라고 했지만, 그 태도로 보아 엠마는 그것이 분명 그 자신의 작품이리라고 곧장 확신했다.\n\n\"미스 스미스의 수집첩에 넣으시라고 드리는 건 아닙니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"제 벗의 것이니, 제가 이를 조금이라도 세상에 내보일 권리는 없지요. 하지만 한번 들여다보시는 거야 싫지 않으실 듯해서요.\"\n\n이 말은 해리엇보다 엠마를 향한 것이었고, 엠마는 그 까닭을 알 만했다. 그에게는 깊은 자의식이 서려 있었고, 그는 벗의 눈보다 그녀의 눈을 마주하는 편이 더 수월하다고 여기는 듯했다. 그는 곧 떠나갔다. 다시 잠깐의 침묵이 흐른 뒤,\n\n\"받아요.\" 엠마가 미소 지으며, 종이를 해리엇 쪽으로 밀어 주며 말했다. \"당신 거예요. 당신 것이니 가지세요.\"\n\n그러나 해리엇은 떨고 있어 그것에 손도 대지 못했다. 그리고 무슨 일이든 앞장서기를 마다하지 않는 엠마는, 별수 없이 자기가 직접 들여다보아야 했다.\n\n미스 ㅇㅇㅇ에게\n\n셔레이드\n\n내 첫째는 임금들의 부귀와 영화를 펼쳐 보이니,\n    땅의 주인들이로다! 그들의 사치와 안일이여.\n인간의 또 다른 면모를 내 둘째가 가져오니,\n    저기 보라, 바다의 군주를!\n\n허나 아아! 둘이 하나로 합치면 어떤 역전이 펼쳐지는가!\n    인간이 뽐내던 권세도 자유도 죄다 사라지고,\n땅과 바다의 주인은 노예로 굽실거리니,\n    여인이, 사랑스러운 여인이 홀로 군림하도다.\n\n그대의 재치가 그 낱말을 곧 일러 주리니,\n    그 부드러운 눈에 허락의 빛이 비치기를!\n\n그녀는 그 위로 눈길을 죽 훑고, 곰곰 생각하다 뜻을 알아채고는, 확실히 해 두고 시구를 완전히 제 것으로 삼기 위해 한 번 더 통독했다. 그러고는 그것을 해리엇에게 건네주고, 흐뭇하게 미소 지으며 앉아 있었다. 해리엇이 희망과 둔함이 뒤섞인 혼란 속에 그 종이를 들여다보며 쩔쩔매는 동안, 엠마는 속으로 이렇게 되뇌었다. \"아주 좋아요, 미스터 엘턴, 정말 훌륭해요. 이보다 못한 셔레이드도 읽어 봤는걸요. 구애(courtship) — 아주 좋은 암시예요. 그 점은 인정해 드리죠. 이건 길을 더듬어 보는 거예요. 이건 아주 분명하게 이렇게 말하는 거죠 — '미스 스미스, 부디 제가 당신께 구애할 수 있도록 허락해 주십시오. 제 셔레이드와 제 마음을 같은 눈길로 받아 주십시오.'\n\n그 부드러운 눈에 허락의 빛이 비치기를!\n\n해리엇 그대로구나. 부드럽다는 건 그 애 눈에 꼭 맞는 말이야 — 온갖 수식어 가운데 더없이 알맞은 표현이지.\n\n그대의 재치가 그 낱말을 곧 일러 주리니.\n\n흠 — 해리엇의 재치라! 차라리 잘됐어. 남자가 그 애를 그렇게 묘사할 정도라면, 정말이지 단단히 사랑에 빠진 게 분명하지. 아아! 미스터 나이틀리, 당신께서 이걸 보셨으면 좋으련만. 이거라면 당신도 수긍하실걸요. 평생 한 번쯤은 당신이 틀렸다고 인정하지 않을 수 없으실 거예요. 정말이지 훌륭한 셔레이드예요! 그리고 더없이 딱 들어맞고요. 이제 일이 곧 고비를 맞겠어.\"\n\n이 더없이 흐뭇한 사색은 그러지 않았다면 한참을 이어졌을 법한 것이었으나, 영문을 모르겠다는 해리엇의 다급한 물음에 끊겼다.\n\n\"이게 뭘까요, 미스 우드하우스? 뭘까요? 저는 도통 모르겠어요 — 조금도 짐작이 안 가요. 대체 뭘 수 있을까요? 좀 알아내 주세요, 미스 우드하우스. 좀 도와주세요. 이렇게 어려운 건 처음 봐요. 왕국(kingdom)일까요? 그 벗이 누군지, 그 젊은 아가씨가 누군지 궁금해요. 잘 지은 것 같아요? 여인(woman)일까요?\n\n여인이, 사랑스러운 여인이 홀로 군림하도다.\n\n넵튠(Neptune)일까요?\n\n저기 보라, 바다의 군주를!\n\n아니면 삼지창(trident)? 인어(mermaid)? 상어(shark)? 아유, 아니에요! 상어는 한 음절뿐인걸요. 분명 아주 영리한 거겠죠, 안 그러면 가져오지도 않으셨을 테니까요. 아아! 미스 우드하우스, 우리가 정말 알아낼 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"인어니 상어니! 말도 안 돼요! 해리엇, 무슨 생각을 하는 거예요? 그분이 인어나 상어를 두고 벗이 지은 셔레이드를 가져다줄 까닭이 어디 있겠어요? 그 종이를 이리 줘 보고, 들어 봐요.\n\n미스 ㅇㅇㅇ에게, 라는 건 미스 스미스에게, 라고 읽으세요.\n\n내 첫째는 임금들의 부귀와 영화를 펼쳐 보이니,\n    땅의 주인들이로다! 그들의 사치와 안일이여.\n\n이건 궁정(court)이에요.\n\n인간의 또 다른 면모를 내 둘째가 가져오니,\n    저기 보라, 바다의 군주를!\n\n이건 배(ship) — 이보다 더 명백할 수가 없죠. 자, 이제 알맹이예요.\n\n허나 아아! 둘이 하나로 합치면, (구애courtship 말이에요, 알겠죠,) 어떤 역전이 펼쳐지는가!\n    인간이 뽐내던 권세도 자유도 죄다 사라지고,\n땅과 바다의 주인은 노예로 굽실거리니,\n    여인이, 사랑스러운 여인이 홀로 군림하도다.\n\n아주 적절한 찬사로군요! 그리고 그 뒤에 적용 구절이 따르는데, 해리엇, 이건 알아듣기에 그리 어렵지 않을 거예요. 마음 편히 혼자 읽어 봐요. 이게 당신을 위해, 당신에게 쓰였다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없어요.\"\n\n해리엇은 그토록 기쁜 설득을 오래 거스를 수가 없었다. 마무리 시구를 읽고는, 온통 설렘과 행복에 휩싸였다. 그녀는 말을 잇지 못했다. 그러나 말할 필요는 없었다. 그녀로서는 그저 느끼는 것만으로 충분했다. 엠마가 대신 말해 주었다.\n\n\"이 찬사에는 너무도 콕 집어 가리키는, 너무도 각별한 뜻이 담겨 있어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래서 저는 미스터 엘턴의 마음에 대해 추호의 의심도 품을 수가 없어요. 당신이 그분의 마음이 향한 사람이에요 — 머잖아 그 가장 완벽한 증거를 받게 될 거예요. 저는 분명 그러리라 생각했어요. 제가 그렇게까지 잘못 볼 리 없다고 생각했고요. 그런데 이제 분명해졌어요. 그분의 마음 상태는, 당신을 알게 된 뒤로 줄곧 이 일에 대해 제가 품어 온 바람만큼이나 분명하고 확고해요. 그래요, 해리엇, 바로 그만큼 오래 저는 지금 일어난 바로 이 일이 일어나기를 바라 왔어요. 당신과 미스터 엘턴 사이의 연정이 가장 바람직한 것인지 가장 자연스러운 것인지, 저는 도무지 가릴 수가 없었어요. 그 개연성과 그 합당함이 정말이지 그토록 엇비슷했거든요! 저는 정말 기뻐요. 진심으로 축하해요, 해리엇. 이건 여자가 맺어 주었다는 데에 능히 자부심을 느낄 만한 연정이에요. 이건 좋은 것밖에 가져다주지 않는 인연이고요. 당신이 바라는 모든 것을 줄 거예요 — 존중, 자립, 번듯한 가정을요 — 당신을 진정한 벗들 한가운데, 하트필드와 제 곁에 붙박아 두고, 우리 사이의 친밀함을 영원히 굳혀 줄 거예요. 이건, 해리엇, 우리 둘 중 누구의 얼굴도 결코 붉히게 하지 않을 결합이에요.\"\n\n\"미스 우드하우스, 정말 고마워요!\" — 그리고 \"미스 우드하우스, 고마워요\"라는 말만이, 다정하게 몇 번이고 끌어안으며 해리엇이 처음에 겨우 입에 올릴 수 있는 전부였다. 그러나 두 사람이 대화다운 대화에 이르렀을 때, 해리엇이 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 보고, 느끼고, 기대하고, 기억하고 있음이 벗에게 충분히 분명해졌다. 미스터 엘턴의 출중함은 더없이 충분한 인정을 받았다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스가 하시는 말씀은 언제나 옳아요.\" 해리엇이 외쳤다. \"그러니 저도 그러려니 짐작하고, 믿고, 바라요 — 그렇지 않고서야 저로서는 도무지 상상도 못 했을 거예요. 이건 제가 누릴 자격을 한참 넘어서는 일이거든요. 누구하고든 결혼할 수 있는 미스터 엘턴인데! 그분에 대해선 두말할 게 없죠. 그렇게 출중한 분이니까요. 그 어여쁜 시구를 좀 보세요 — '미스 ㅇㅇㅇ에게.' 어머나, 어쩜 이리 영리하실까! 정말로 저를 두고 쓰신 걸까요?\"\n\n\"그 점에 대해선 저는 의문을 제기할 수도, 의문을 들을 수도 없어요. 그건 확실한 일이에요. 제 판단을 믿고 받아들여요. 이건 연극의 서막 같은 것, 한 장(章)의 머리에 붙는 제사(題詞) 같은 거예요. 머잖아 사실 그대로의 산문이 뒤따를 거고요.\"\n\n\"이건 아무도 예상 못 했을 일이에요. 정말이지, 한 달 전만 해도 저 자신도 생각조차 못 했는걸요! — 세상에 별일이 다 일어나네요!\"\n\n\"미스 스미스 같은 사람들과 미스터 엘턴 같은 사람들이 서로 알게 되면 — 정말 그렇게 되는 법이고요 — 또 정말이지 신기한 일이에요. 이렇게 너무도 뚜렷이, 너무도 분명히 바람직한 것 — 남들이 미리 주선하고 싶어 할 만한 것이, 이토록 당장에 제대로 된 모양새로 빚어진다는 건 흔한 일이 아니거든요. 당신과 미스터 엘턴은 처지로 보아 서로 부름받은 사이예요. 두 분은 각자의 가정 형편 모든 면에서 서로에게 속해 있고요. 두 분의 결혼은 랜덜스의 혼사에 맞먹는 일이 될 거예요. 하트필드의 공기에는 정말이지 무언가가 있는 것 같아요 — 사랑에 꼭 알맞은 방향을 잡아 주고, 마땅히 흘러야 할 바로 그 물길로 흘려보내 주는 무언가가요.\n\n진정한 사랑의 길은 결코 순탄히 흐른 적이 없으니 —\n\n하트필드판 셰익스피어라면 그 구절에 긴 주석을 달아야 할 거예요.\"\n* 인용은 셰익스피어 「한여름 밤의 꿈」의 한 구절.\n\n\"미스터 엘턴이 정말로 저를 사랑하다니 — 하고많은 사람 중에 저를, 미카엘 축일* 무렵만 해도 말을 나누기는커녕 알지도 못했던 저를! 게다가 그분은 여태껏 본 중에 가장 잘생긴 남자고, 모두가 우러러보는 분, 꼭 미스터 나이틀리 같은 분인데! 그분을 찾는 데가 어찌나 많은지, 본인만 마음먹지 않으면 끼니 한 번 혼자 들 일이 없다고들, 일주일 날수보다 초대받는 자리가 더 많다고들 다들 말하잖아요. 그리고 교회에서 또 얼마나 훌륭하신데요! 미스 내시는 그분이 하이베리에 오신 뒤로 설교하신 성경 구절을 죄다 적어 두었대요. 어머나! 그분을 처음 뵈었던 때를 돌이켜 보면! 제가 어찌 짐작이나 했겠어요! — 그분이 지나가신다는 말을 듣고 애벗 자매하고 제가 앞방으로 달려가 블라인드 틈으로 엿보았는데, 미스 내시가 와서 우리를 야단쳐 쫓아 놓고는 본인이 남아서 들여다보았어요. 그래도 곧 저를 다시 불러서 저도 보게 해 주었으니, 참 마음씨가 고우셨죠. 그리고 그분이 어찌나 멋져 보이던지! 미스터 콜과 팔짱을 끼고 계셨어요.\"\n* 미카엘 축일(Michaelmas): 9월 29일. 영국에서 분기·임차 기한의 기준이 되던 날.\n\n\"이건, 당신의 벗들이 누구든 — 무엇이든 간에, 적어도 그들에게 분별이 있다면야 흡족해할 결합이에요. 우리야 어리석은 자들에게 처신을 맞출 까닭이 없으니까요. 그들이 당신을 행복하게 결혼시키고 싶어 한다면, 여기 그 어진 성품으로 그것을 온전히 보장해 줄 남자가 있어요. 그들이 당신을, 자기들이 골라 자리 잡게 해 준 바로 그 고장과 그 사교 범위 안에 정착시키고 싶어 한다면, 여기서 그게 이루어질 거고요. 그리고 그들의 유일한 바람이 흔히 말하듯 당신이 좋은 데 시집가는 것뿐이라면, 여기 그들을 만족시킬 만한 넉넉한 재산, 번듯한 가정, 출세가 있어요.\"\n\n\"네, 정말 그래요. 어쩜 그리 멋지게 말씀하세요. 듣고 있으면 참 좋아요. 미스 우드하우스는 뭐든 다 아시네요. 미스 우드하우스와 미스터 엘턴은 두 분 다 똑같이 영리하시고요. 이 셔레이드 좀 봐요! — 제가 일 년을 공부했어도 이런 건 결코 못 지었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"어제 사양하시던 그 태도로 보아, 솜씨를 한번 시험해 보실 작정이구나 싶었어요.\"\n\n\"이건 정말이지, 두말할 것 없이 제가 읽어 본 중에 가장 훌륭한 셔레이드예요.\"\n\n\"이보다 더 딱 들어맞는 건 분명 한 번도 못 봤어요.\"\n\n\"이건 전에 우리가 봤던 거의 모든 것보다 두 배는 길어요.\"\n\n\"길이가 특별히 그것의 장점이라고는 생각지 않아요. 이런 건 대체로 짧으면 짧을수록 좋거든요.\"\n\n해리엇은 그 시구에 너무 골몰해 있어 듣지 못했다. 더없이 흡족한 비교들이 그녀의 머릿속에 떠오르고 있었다.\n\n\"이건 별개의 일이에요.\" 이윽고 그녀가, 두 뺨을 발갛게 물들이며 말했다. \"남들처럼 평범하게 분별이 좀 있어서, 할 말이 있으면 앉아서 편지를 써, 꼭 해야 할 말을 짤막하게 적는 거하고요 — 이렇게 시구나 셔레이드를 짓는 거하고는요.\"\n\n미스터 마틴의 산문에 대한 이보다 더 기운찬 거부를 엠마는 바랄 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"이렇게 어여쁜 시구라니!\" 해리엇이 말을 이었다. \"이 마지막 두 줄 좀 봐요! — 그런데 이 종이를 대체 어떻게 돌려드리죠, 아니면 제가 답을 알아냈다고 어떻게 말하고요? — 아아! 미스 우드하우스, 그 일을 어떻게 하면 좋을까요?\"\n\n\"제게 맡겨요. 당신은 아무것도 하지 말고요. 오늘 저녁이면 그분이 분명 여기 오실 거예요. 그때 제가 돌려드리고, 우리끼리 이런저런 실없는 말이 오갈 테니, 당신이 나설 일은 없을 거예요. — 당신의 부드러운 눈은 빛을 발할 때를 스스로 고르면 돼요. 저만 믿어요.\"\n\n\"아아! 미스 우드하우스, 이 아름다운 셔레이드를 제 공책에 적어 넣을 수 없다니 정말 아쉬워요! 분명 이 절반만큼 좋은 것도 저는 한 편도 못 가졌는걸요.\"\n\n\"마지막 두 줄만 빼면, 공책에 적어 넣지 못할 까닭이 없어요.\"\n\n\"아아! 하지만 그 두 줄은 —\"\n\n\"가장 좋은 부분이죠. 인정해요. — 혼자만의 즐거움을 위해서요. 그러니 혼자 간직할 몫으로 두세요. 알다시피, 둘로 나눈다고 해서 그 줄이 쓰이지 않은 게 되는 건 아니에요. 그 대구가 사라지는 것도, 그 뜻이 달라지는 것도 아니고요. 하지만 그 부분을 떼어 내면, 모든 임자 표시가 사라지고, 어느 수집첩에든 어울릴 만한 아주 어여쁜 점잖은 셔레이드만 남게 되죠. 정말이에요, 그분은 자기 셔레이드가 푸대접받는 걸, 자기 연정이 푸대접받는 것보다 더 싫어하실 거예요. 사랑에 빠진 시인이라면 양쪽 자격 모두에서 북돋아 주든가, 아니면 둘 다 그만두든가 해야 하는 법이거든요. 공책을 이리 줘 봐요, 제가 적어 넣을게요. 그러면 당신한테 돌아올 책망 같은 건 전혀 없을 거예요.\"\n\n해리엇은 따랐다. 다만 그녀의 머릿속에서는 그 부분들을 잘라 내기가 좀처럼 어려워, 벗이 적어 넣는 것이 사랑의 고백이 아니라고는 영 확신할 수가 없었다. 그것은 조금이라도 세상에 내놓기엔 너무도 소중한 선물처럼 여겨졌다.\n\n\"이 공책은 결코 제 손에서 내놓지 않을 거예요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아주 좋아요.\" 엠마가 대답했다. \"더없이 자연스러운 마음이죠. 그리고 그 마음이 오래갈수록 저도 더 기쁠 거예요. 그런데 마침 아버지가 오시네요. 이 셔레이드를 아버지께 읽어 드려도 싫지 않겠죠? 그러면 아버지가 무척 기뻐하실 거예요! 아버지는 이런 종류를 다 좋아하시거든요. 특히 여성에게 찬사를 바치는 거라면 더더욱요. 우리 모두를 향한 다정한 기사도 정신이 그득한 분이세요! — 제가 아버지께 읽어 드리게 해 줘요.\"\n\n해리엇의 표정이 굳어졌다.\n\n\"해리엇, 이 셔레이드를 두고 너무 깊이 파고들면 안 돼요. — 너무 의식하고 너무 성급하게 굴어서, 거기 담겼을 법한 의미보다 더 많은 의미를, 아니 거기 담길 수 있는 의미 전부를 갖다 붙이는 듯이 보이면, 당신 마음을 엉뚱하게 들켜 버리고 말 거예요. 이런 자그마한 찬사 하나에 짓눌리지 말아요. 그분이 정말 비밀로 하고 싶었다면, 제가 곁에 있는데 그 종이를 두고 가지 않았을 거예요. 게다가 그분은 그걸 당신보다는 오히려 제 쪽으로 밀어 놓으셨고요. 이 일을 두고 우리가 너무 엄숙해지지는 말기로 해요. 우리가 이 셔레이드를 두고 영혼까지 한숨으로 쏟아 내지 않더라도, 그분이 다음 단계로 나아갈 격려는 충분하니까요.\"\n\n\"아아! 그래요 — 이 일로 제가 우스워지는 일은 없었으면 좋겠어요. 좋으실 대로 하세요.\"\n\n미스터 우드하우스가 들어와, 그가 아주 자주 던지는 물음을 또 꺼내며 곧장 그 화제로 이끌었다. \"그래, 얘들아, 너희 공책은 잘되어 가니? — 뭐 새것이라도 좀 생겼고?\"\n\n\"네, 아버지. 읽어 드릴 게 있어요, 아주 새것이에요. 오늘 아침에 탁자 위에서 종이 한 장이 발견됐는데 — (요정이 떨어뜨리고 간 거겠죠) — 아주 어여쁜 셔레이드가 적혀 있어서, 방금 베껴 넣었어요.\"\n\n그녀는 아버지가 무엇이든 읽어 주는 걸 좋아하는 그대로, 천천히 또박또박, 두세 번씩 거듭 읽어 드렸고, 읽어 나가면서 구절마다 풀이도 곁들였다. 그러자 아버지는 무척 기뻐했고, 그녀가 미리 내다본 대로, 특히 그 찬사로 맺는 마무리에 깊이 감동했다.\n\n\"옳거니, 정말 지당하구나, 정말 제대로 한 말이야. 참으로 옳은 말이지. '여인이, 사랑스러운 여인이'라. 얘야, 어찌나 어여쁜 셔레이드인지, 어떤 요정이 가져왔는지 쉬이 짐작이 가는구나. — 너 말고는 누구도 이렇게 어여쁘게 쓸 수 없을 게다, 엠마.\"\n\n엠마는 그저 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었다. 잠깐 생각에 잠겼다가, 자못 다정한 한숨을 내쉬고는 아버지가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아아! 네가 누구를 닮았는지는 보면 알지! 네 어머니는 그런 일에 어찌나 영리했는지! 나한테도 그이만 한 기억력이 있다면 좋으련만! 하지만 나는 도통 아무것도 기억나질 않아 — 너한테 말한 적 있는 그 수수께끼조차도 말이다. 첫 연밖에 기억나지 않는구나, 여러 연이나 되는데도.\n\n키티, 곱지만 얼어붙은 처녀,\n    아직도 한스러운 불길을 지폈네.\n눈 가린 그 소년을 나는 도움 청해 불렀으나,\n가까이 다가오는 그가 두려웠으니,\n    전엔 내 구애에 그토록 치명적이었던 까닭에.\n\n내가 기억하는 건 그게 다란다 — 하지만 처음부터 끝까지 아주 영리한 거야. 그런데 얘야, 네가 그걸 가졌다고 했던 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"네, 아버지. 우리 공책 두 번째 쪽에 적혀 있어요. 『우아한 발췌집』에서 베껴 왔거든요. 개릭의 작품이잖아요.\"\n\n\"옳지, 정말 그렇지. — 더 기억해 낼 수 있으면 좋으련만.\n\n키티, 곱지만 얼어붙은 처녀.\n\n그 이름을 들으니 가엾은 이저벨라가 생각나는구나. 그 애가 하마터면 외할머니를 따라 캐서린이라고 세례명을 받을 뻔했으니 말이다. 다음 주에는 그 애가 여기 와 주면 좋겠는데. 얘야, 그 애를 어디에 들일지 생각해 두었니 — 아이들이 쓸 방은 어떻게 되고?\"\n\n\"아유! 그럼요 — 언니야 물론 자기 방을 쓰겠죠. 늘 쓰던 그 방요. 그리고 아이들을 위해선 육아실이 있고요 — 늘 그래 왔듯이요, 아시잖아요. 뭘 바꿀 까닭이 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 얘야 — 하지만 그 애가 여기 안 온 지 하도 오래라서! — 지난 부활절 이후로 한 번도, 그것도 겨우 며칠뿐이었잖니. — 미스터 존 나이틀리가 변호사라는 게 참 불편한 노릇이야. — 가엾은 이저벨라! — 우리 모두에게서 그렇게 떨어져 살다니! — 그리고 와서 미스 테일러가 여기 없는 걸 보면 얼마나 서운해할꼬!\"\n\n\"적어도 놀라지는 않을 거예요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 얘야. 나야 그 애가 결혼한다는 말을 처음 들었을 때 정말이지 무척 놀랐단다.\"\n\n\"언니가 여기 있는 동안, 미스터 웨스턴 부부를 초대해서 함께 식사하면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 시간이 되거든 그러려무나. — 하지만 — (자못 풀 죽은 목소리로) — 그 애는 겨우 한 주만 오는 거잖니. 뭘 할 시간이 없겠구나.\"\n\n\"더 오래 머물 수 없다니 안타까운 일이에요 — 하지만 어쩔 수 없는 사정인가 봐요. 미스터 존 나이틀리가 28일에는 다시 시내에 가 있어야 하니까요. 그래도 아버지, 우리는 고맙게 여겨야 해요. 그 부부가 시골에 내어 줄 수 있는 시간 전부를 우리가 차지하게 됐고, 그중 이삼 일을 애비에 떼어 주지 않아도 되니까요. 미스터 나이틀리가 이번 성탄절엔 자기 몫을 양보하겠다고 약속했거든요 — 따지고 보면 그 부부가 우리 집보다 그분 집에 안 간 지가 더 오래됐는데도 말이에요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 이저벨라가 하트필드 아닌 다른 데 있어야 한다면, 그건 정말이지 너무 가혹한 일이지, 얘야.\"\n\n미스터 우드하우스는 형으로서 동생에게 가지는 미스터 나이틀리의 권리도, 자기 말고 다른 누가 이저벨라에게 가지는 권리도, 도무지 인정하지 못했다. 그는 잠시 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있다가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그 사람이 그래야 한다고 해서, 가엾은 이저벨라까지 그렇게 빨리 돌아가야 할 까닭은 모르겠구나. 엠마야, 내가 한번 그 애더러 우리하고 좀 더 머물자고 설득해 봐야겠다. 그 애하고 아이들이야 얼마든지 더 있어도 될 텐데.\"\n\n\"아아! 아버지 — 그건 한 번도 해내신 적 없는 일이고, 앞으로도 못 하시리라고 봐요. 언니는 남편을 두고 뒤처져 머무르는 걸 견디지 못하거든요.\"\n\n이는 반박하기엔 너무도 맞는 말이었다. 달갑지 않은 말이긴 했어도, 미스터 우드하우스는 그저 체념의 한숨을 내쉴 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 딸이 남편에게 그토록 매여 있다는 생각에 아버지의 기운이 꺾이는 것을 본 엠마는, 곧장 그 기운을 북돋울 만한 화제의 갈래로 이끌었다.\n\n\"오라버니 부부가 여기 와 있는 동안, 해리엇이 되도록 자주 함께해 주어야 해요. 분명 아이들을 좋아하게 될 거예요. 우리는 그 아이들이 무척 자랑스럽잖아요, 그렇죠, 아버지? 헨리하고 존 중에 누가 더 잘생겼다고 할지 궁금해요.\"\n\n\"옳지, 누구라고 할지 나도 궁금하구나. 가여운 것들, 오게 되면 얼마나 좋아할꼬. 그 애들은 하트필드에 오는 걸 무척 좋아한단다, 해리엇.\"\n\n\"분명 그러실 거예요, 선생님. 하트필드를 안 좋아하는 사람이 누가 있겠어요.\"\n\n\"헨리는 잘생긴 사내아이지만, 존은 제 엄마를 꼭 닮았지. 헨리가 맏이인데, 제 아버지가 아니라 나를 따라 이름을 지었단다. 둘째인 존이 제 아버지 이름을 땄고. 맏이가 아버지 이름을 안 따른 걸 의아해하는 사람도 있는가 본데, 이저벨라가 굳이 헨리라고 부르고 싶어 했으니, 나는 그게 참 어여쁜 마음이라고 생각했지. 게다가 정말이지 아주 영리한 아이야. 다들 놀랍도록 영리하단다. 그리고 어찌나 어여쁜 짓을 많이 하는지. 내 의자 옆에 와 서서는 '할아버지, 끈 한 가닥만 주실래요?' 하고 묻는단다. 한번은 헨리가 칼을 달라고 해서, 칼은 할아버지들이나 쓰라고 만든 거라고 일러 주었지. 그 애들 아비는 너무 거칠게 대할 때가 많은 것 같아.\"\n\n\"아버지께 거칠어 보이는 거예요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"아버지가 워낙 더없이 다정하신 분이니까요. 하지만 다른 아버지들하고 견주어 보시면, 거칠다고는 생각지 않으실 거예요. 그분은 아들들이 활달하고 다부지게 자라기를 바라시는 거예요. 그래서 애들이 잘못하면 이따금 따끔하게 한마디 하시는 거고요. 하지만 다정한 아버지세요 — 미스터 존 나이틀리는 분명 다정한 아버지예요. 아이들도 다 그분을 따르고요.\"\n\n\"그러고 나면 또 외삼촌이 들어와서, 그 애들을 천장까지 막 던져 올린다니까, 어찌나 무시무시한지!\"\n\n\"하지만 애들은 그걸 좋아해요, 아버지. 그보다 좋아하는 게 없는걸요. 그게 어찌나 신나는지, 외삼촌이 번갈아 하라는 규칙을 정해 두지 않으면, 먼저 시작한 애가 다른 애한테 절대 양보하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 나는 도무지 모르겠구나.\"\n\n\"우리 모두가 다 그런 거예요, 아버지. 세상의 절반은 나머지 절반의 즐거움을 이해하지 못하는 법이니까요.\"\n\n아침나절도 한참 지나, 두 아가씨가 으레 네 시에 드는 식사를 채비하러 막 헤어지려던 참에, 이 견줄 데 없는 셔레이드의 주인공이 다시 들어섰다. 해리엇은 고개를 돌렸다. 그러나 엠마는 여느 때의 미소로 그를 맞을 수 있었고, 그녀의 날카로운 눈은 곧 그의 눈에서 한 수를 던졌다는 — 주사위를 굴렸다는 자의식을 알아챘다. 그리고 그가 그것이 어떻게 나오는지 보러 왔으리라고 짐작했다. 그러나 그의 표면적인 명분은, 오늘 저녁 미스터 우드하우스의 카드놀이 모임이 자기 없이도 꾸려질 수 있을지, 아니면 자기가 하트필드에 조금이라도 꼭 있어야 할지를 묻는 것이었다. 만일 그래야 한다면 다른 일은 다 제쳐 두겠지만, 그렇지 않다면 그의 벗 미스터 콜이 함께 식사하자고 어찌나 거듭 청하던지 — 어찌나 그것에 못을 박던지, 사정이 되면 가겠노라고 그가 조건부로 약속해 둔 터였다.\n\n엠마는 고마워하면서도, 자기들 때문에 그가 벗을 실망시키는 일은 허락할 수 없다고 했다. 아버지의 휘스트 한판은 틀림없이 꾸려질 거라고 했다. 그가 다시 청했고 — 그녀가 다시 사양했다. 그러고 나서 그가 막 인사하고 물러가려는 듯하자, 그녀는 탁자에서 그 종이를 집어 그에게 돌려주었다.\n* 휘스트(whist): 네 사람이 둘씩 짝을 지어 하는 카드놀이. 한 판을 러버(rubber)라 한다.\n\n\"아, 여기 친절하게도 우리에게 남겨 주신 그 셔레이드요. 보여 주셔서 고마워요. 우리가 어찌나 감탄했던지, 제가 외람되게도 미스 스미스의 수집첩에 적어 넣었어요. 당신 벗께서 언짢게 여기지 않으셨으면 좋겠네요. 물론 앞의 여덟 줄까지밖에 베끼지 않았고요.\"\n\n미스터 엘턴은 정녕 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐다. 그는 자못 미심쩍은 듯, 자못 당황한 얼굴로 \"영광\"이라는 둥 무어라 중얼거리더니, 엠마와 해리엇을 번갈아 흘긋 보았다. 그러다 탁자 위에 공책이 펼쳐져 있는 것을 보고는, 그것을 집어 들어 아주 유심히 살펴보았다. 어색한 순간을 넘기려는 뜻으로, 엠마가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"당신 벗께 제 대신 사과를 전해 주세요. 하지만 이렇게 훌륭한 셔레이드를 한두 사람한테만 가두어 둘 수야 없잖아요. 그분이 이토록 정중하게 쓰시는 한, 어느 여성에게서든 칭찬받으리라는 건 분명한 일이에요.\"\n\n\"저는 서슴없이 말씀드릴 수 있습니다.\" 미스터 엘턴이, 말하는 내내 꽤나 머뭇거리면서도 대답했다. \"서슴없이 말씀드리건대 — 적어도 제 벗이 저와 조금이라도 같은 심정이라면 말이지요 — 그가 만일 자신의 그 자그마한 시구가 제가 보는 것처럼 이렇게 영예롭게 대접받는 걸 본다면 (다시 공책을 보고는, 그것을 탁자에 도로 내려놓으며), 분명 자기 생애 가장 자랑스러운 순간이라 여길 거라는 데에 추호의 의심도 없습니다.\"\n\n이 말을 마치고 그는 되도록 서둘러 떠났다. 엠마로서는 너무 이르다고 여길 수가 없었다. 그의 온갖 어질고 호감 가는 자질에도 불구하고, 그의 말투에는 일종의 과장된 허세가 있어 그녀를 자꾸만 웃음 짓게 만들기 십상이었던 것이다. 그녀는 그 충동을 마음껏 풀어내려고 달아났고, 다정하고 숭고한 기쁨의 몫일랑 해리엇에게 남겨 두었다.\n\n# 제1권 10장\n\n이제 십이월 중순이었지만, 아직 두 아가씨가 제법 규칙적인 산책을 못 할 만한 날씨는 없었다. 다음 날, 엠마는 하이베리에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 사는 가난한 병자 가족에게 자선의 방문을 가야 할 일이 있었다.\n\n이 외딴 오두막으로 가는 길은 비커리지 골목을 따라 나 있었으니, 이 골목은 들쭉날쭉하긴 해도 넓은 마을 큰길에서 직각으로 갈라져 뻗은 길이었고, 짐작할 수 있듯 미스터 엘턴의 복된 거처가 자리한 곳이었다. 먼저 변변찮은 집 몇 채를 지나고 나면, 골목을 사분의 일 마일쯤 내려간 곳에 목사관이 솟아 있었다. 낡고 그리 좋다 할 수 없는 집으로, 더는 가까울 수 없을 만큼 길에 바싹 붙어 있었다. 위치의 이점이라곤 없었지만, 지금 주인이 무척 말끔하게 단장해 놓은 집이었다. 그런 집인지라, 두 벗이 걸음을 늦추고 눈여겨보지 않은 채 그 앞을 지나칠 도리는 없었다. 엠마의 말은 이러했다.\n\n\"저기예요. 언젠가는 당신도 그 수수께끼 시집을 들고 저리로 들어가게 될 거예요.\" 해리엇의 말은 이러했다.\n\n\"어머, 정말 아름다운 집이에요! 어쩜 저렇게 예쁠까요! 미스 내시가 그렇게 칭찬하던 노란 커튼이 저기 있네요.\"\n\n\"요즘은 이 길로 잘 안 다녀요.\" 두 사람이 걸음을 옮기며 엠마가 말했다. \"하지만 머잖아 끌릴 만한 핑계가 생기겠죠. 그러면 하이베리 이 일대의 울타리며 대문이며 못이며 가지치기한 나무까지 죄다 차차 속속들이 알게 될 거예요.\"\n\n알고 보니 해리엇은 평생 한 번도 목사관 안에 들어가 본 적이 없었고, 그 안을 보고 싶어 하는 호기심이 어찌나 대단한지, 외관과 여러 가능성을 따져 보건대 엠마로서는 그것을 사랑의 증거로밖에 분류할 수 없었으니, 미스터 엘턴이 자기에게서 빈틈없는 기지를 보아낸 것과 같은 부류였다.\n\n\"어떻게든 들어갈 수 있으면 좋으련만.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"그런데 들어갈 만한 그럴듯한 핑계가 도무지 떠오르질 않네요. 그 집 가정부에게 물어볼 하인 자리도 없고, 아버지가 전하라는 말씀도 없으니.\"\n\n엠마는 곰곰이 궁리했지만 아무것도 떠오르지 않았다. 둘 다 몇 분쯤 말이 없다가, 해리엇이 다시 이렇게 입을 열었다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스, 저는 정말 이상해요. 어떻게 미스 우드하우스가 아직 결혼도 안 하고, 결혼할 생각도 없으신지! 그렇게 사랑스러우신데!\"\n\n엠마는 웃으며 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 사랑스럽다는 것만으로는, 해리엇, 결혼할 마음이 들기에 영 부족해요. 나도 다른 사람을 사랑스럽게 여겨야죠 — 적어도 단 한 사람은요. 그런데 나는 지금 결혼할 생각이 없을 뿐 아니라, 평생 결혼할 마음이 거의 없답니다.\"\n\n\"어머! 그렇게 말씀하셔도, 저는 믿을 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"마음이 동하려면, 지금껏 본 누구보다 훨씬 뛰어난 사람을 만나야 할 거예요. 미스터 엘턴은, 알다시피,\" (제정신을 차리며) \"논외고요. 그리고 나는 그런 사람을 만나고 싶지도 않아요. 차라리 마음이 동하지 않는 편이 나아요. 나는 정말이지 지금보다 나아질 수가 없거든요. 결혼이라도 했다간 후회할 게 뻔하죠.\"\n\n\"세상에! 여자가 그런 말을 하는 걸 들으니 어찌나 별난지!\"\n\n\"나에겐 여자들이 흔히 결혼하는 까닭이 하나도 없어요. 사랑에 빠진다면야, 정말이지 이야기가 다르겠죠! 하지만 나는 한 번도 사랑에 빠져 본 적이 없어요. 그건 내 방식도, 내 천성도 아니에요. 앞으로도 그럴 일은 없을 것 같고요. 그리고 사랑도 없이 지금 같은 처지를 바꾼다면, 분명 나는 바보일 거예요. 재산도 아쉽지 않고, 할 일도 아쉽지 않고, 지위도 아쉽지 않아요. 내가 하트필드를 다스리는 것의 절반만큼이라도 제 남편 집의 안주인 노릇을 하는 결혼한 여자는 드물 거예요. 그리고 우리 아버지 눈에 비치는 만큼 이토록 진심으로 사랑받고 소중히 여겨지는 일, 어떤 남자 눈에든 늘 첫째이고 늘 옳은 사람으로 비치는 일은, 결코, 결코 다시 바랄 수 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그러다 결국 미스 베이츠처럼 노처녀로 늙으시면요!\"\n\n\"그건 당신이 들이밀 수 있는 가장 무시무시한 그림이네요, 해리엇. 내가 미스 베이츠처럼 될 것 같다는 생각이 든다면! 그렇게 어리석고 — 그렇게 만족하고 — 그렇게 헤프게 웃고 — 그렇게 지루하게 늘어놓고 — 그렇게 가릴 줄도 까다로울 줄도 모르고 — 주변 사람 누구에 관한 일이든 죄다 떠벌리는 사람이 될 것 같다면, 나는 당장 내일이라도 결혼할 거예요. 하지만 우리끼리 말인데, 결혼 안 한 것 말고는 닮을 구석이라곤 절대 있을 수 없다고 확신해요.\"\n\n\"그래도 어쨌든 노처녀가 되시는 거잖아요! 그건 너무 끔찍해요!\"\n\n\"걱정 말아요, 해리엇, 나는 가난한 노처녀는 되지 않을 테니까요. 너그러운 세상 사람들 눈에 독신을 경멸스럽게 만드는 건 오직 가난뿐이거든요! 수입이 아주 빠듯한 독신 여자는 우스꽝스럽고 못마땅한 노처녀가 되기 마련이죠! 사내아이 계집아이 들이 놀려 먹기 딱 좋은 감이고요. 하지만 재산이 넉넉한 독신 여자라면 언제나 떳떳하고, 다른 누구 못지않게 분별 있고 유쾌할 수 있어요. 그리고 이 차이가 세상의 공정함과 양식을 거스르는 것이 처음 보이는 것만큼 심하지는 않아요. 수입이 아주 빠듯하면 마음이 옹졸해지고 성미가 비뚤어지기 십상이거든요. 겨우 입에 풀칠하는 사람들, 그것도 어쩔 수 없이 아주 좁고 대개 아주 못한 사람들 틈에서 사는 이들이라면, 인색하고 까다로워지는 게 당연하죠. 하지만 이건 미스 베이츠에겐 해당이 안 돼요. 그분은 그저 너무 사람이 좋고 너무 어수룩해서 내 취향에 안 맞을 뿐이에요. 그래도 결혼도 안 하고 가난한데도, 대체로 누구에게나 무척 사랑받는 분이죠. 가난이 그분 마음을 옹졸하게 만들지는 분명 못했어요. 정말이지, 그분은 세상에 가진 게 일 실링뿐이라도, 그중 육 펜스는 선뜻 남에게 내줄 분이거든요. 그리고 아무도 그분을 무서워하지 않죠. 그게 큰 매력이에요.\"\n\n\"세상에! 그렇지만 그럼 어떻게 하시려고요? 나이 들면 무얼 하며 지내시려고요?\"\n\n\"내가 나를 안다면, 해리엇, 나는 활동적이고 분주한 사람이라 혼자서도 즐길 거리가 무척 많아요. 그러니 마흔이나 쉰에, 스물한 살 때보다 더 할 일이 아쉬울 까닭이 없을 것 같아요. 여자가 손과 머리로 흔히 하는 일들은 그때도 지금과 다름없이 내게 열려 있을 테니까요. 큰 변화 없이요. 그림을 덜 그리면 책을 더 읽고, 음악을 그만두면 양탄자 짜기를 시작하겠죠. 그리고 관심을 쏟을 대상, 애정을 쏟을 대상으로 말하자면 — 사실 그게 가장 부족한 점이고, 결혼하지 않을 때 정말로 피해야 할 큰 화근이 바로 그것의 결핍이지만 — 그 점에서도 나는 아주 넉넉할 거예요. 내가 그토록 사랑하는 언니의 아이들이 다 있고, 그 아이들을 보살필 수 있으니까요. 십중팔구 그 아이들만으로도 기우는 인생이 필요로 하는 온갖 감정을 채우고 남을 거예요. 온갖 희망과 온갖 두려움을 채우기에 충분할 테고요. 그 아이들 누구에게 쏟는 애정도 부모의 그것에는 못 미치겠지만, 그래도 더 뜨겁고 더 맹목적인 것보다는 내 안락에 대한 생각에 한결 잘 맞아요. 내 조카들이요! 나는 곧잘 조카딸을 곁에 두게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"미스 베이츠의 조카딸을 아세요? 그러니까, 분명 백 번은 보셨겠지만 — 아는 사이세요?\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요. 그 애가 하이베리에 올 때마다 우리는 늘 어쩔 수 없이 아는 사이가 되죠. 그러고 보니, 조카딸이라면 정나미가 떨어질 만도 한 게 바로 그 일이에요. 천만에! 적어도 나는, 그 사람이 제인 페어팩스 이야기를 늘어놓는 만큼이라도, 나이틀리네 사람들 모두를 두고 사람들을 지겹게 만드는 일은 절대 없을 거예요. 제인 페어팩스라는 이름만 들어도 진절머리가 나요. 그 애한테서 온 편지는 한 통 한 통 마흔 번씩 읽어 대고, 친구들 모두에게 안부 전한 것은 돌고 돌아 되풀이되죠. 그 애가 고모한테 가슴받이 본 하나를 보내거나 할머니한테 양말대님 한 켤레를 떠 보내기라도 하면, 한 달 내내 그 이야기밖에 안 들려요. 나는 제인 페어팩스가 잘되길 진심으로 바라지만, 그 애 이야기는 정말이지 사람을 진저리 나게 해요.\"\n\n이제 둘은 그 오두막에 다가서고 있었고, 한가한 화제는 죄다 밀려났다. 엠마는 인정이 무척 많았다. 가난한 이들의 곤경은 그녀의 지갑에서뿐 아니라, 그녀가 손수 기울이는 관심과 친절, 조언과 인내에서 어김없이 구제를 받았다. 그녀는 그들의 생활 방식을 이해했고, 그들의 무지와 유혹을 너그러이 헤아렸으며, 교육이 거의 베풀어지지 못한 이들에게서 비범한 미덕을 바라는 낭만적 기대 따위는 없었다. 그녀는 선뜻 동정하며 그들의 고난 속으로 들어갔고, 늘 선의 못지않은 지혜로써 도움을 주었다. 이번 경우는 병과 가난이 겹친 집을 찾은 것이었다. 위안이나 조언을 줄 수 있는 한 그곳에 머문 뒤, 엠마는 그 정경에서 받은 인상을 가슴에 품고 오두막을 나섰으니, 멀어져 가며 해리엇에게 이렇게 말할 정도였다.\n\n\"이런 광경이야말로, 해리엇, 사람에게 약이 되는 거예요. 이런 걸 보고 나면 다른 모든 게 얼마나 하찮아 보이는지! 지금은 하루 종일 이 가엾은 사람들 생각밖에 못 할 것 같아요. 그런데도, 이 모든 게 머릿속에서 얼마나 빨리 사라져 버릴지 누가 알겠어요?\"\n\n\"정말 맞아요.\" 해리엇이 말했다. \"가엾은 사람들! 다른 건 아무것도 생각할 수가 없네요.\"\n\n\"그리고 정말이지, 이 인상이 그리 쉬이 가시지는 않을 것 같아요.\" 엠마가 오두막 정원을 가로지르는 좁고 미끄러운 길의 끝에 놓인 낮은 울타리와 비틀거릴 만한 디딤돌을 넘어, 다시 골목으로 들어서며 말했다. \"가시지 않을 거예요.\" 그러고는 걸음을 멈추고, 그곳의 온갖 겉으로 드러난 비참함을 한 번 더 바라보며, 안쪽의 한층 더한 비참함을 되새겼다.\n\n\"어머! 그럼요, 절대로요.\" 길동무가 말했다.\n\n둘은 계속 걸었다. 골목은 살짝 굽이졌고, 그 굽이를 지나자마자 미스터 엘턴이 곧장 눈에 들어왔으니, 어찌나 가까운지 엠마는 겨우 이 말을 덧붙일 틈밖에 없었다.\n\n\"아! 해리엇, 우리의 갸륵한 마음이 얼마나 단단한지 시험할 일이 아주 갑작스레 닥쳐오네요. 자,\" (미소 지으며) \"이렇게 보면 되지 않을까요. 동정심이 고통받는 이들에게 수고와 구제를 베풀게 했다면, 그것은 정말로 중요한 일을 다 한 거라고요. 우리가 비참한 이들을 가엾이 여기고, 그들을 위해 할 수 있는 일을 다할 만큼 마음을 쓴다면, 그 나머지는 공허한 동정일 뿐, 우리 자신만 괴롭힐 따름이죠.\"\n\n해리엇이 겨우 \"어머! 그럼요, 맞아요.\" 하고 대답하자마자 그 신사가 두 사람과 합류했다. 그렇긴 해도, 만나서 처음 꺼낸 화제는 가난한 가족의 궁핍과 고통이었다. 그도 마침 그 집에 들르려던 참이었다. 이제 그 방문은 미루기로 했다. 그러나 무엇을 할 수 있고 무엇을 해야 할지를 두고 자못 흥미로운 의논이 오갔다. 그러고서 미스터 엘턴은 발길을 돌려 두 사람을 따라나섰다.\n\n\"이런 일을 하다 마주치다니.\" 엠마는 생각했다. \"자선의 계획에서 만나다니. 이건 양쪽 모두의 연정을 크게 더해 줄 거야. 이러다 고백이 터져 나온다 해도 놀랍지 않겠어. 내가 여기 없다면 분명 그리될 텐데. 차라리 내가 딴 데 있었으면.\"\n\n두 사람에게서 되도록 멀찍이 떨어지고 싶은 마음에, 엠마는 곧 골목 한쪽으로 조금 돋워진 좁은 오솔길로 올라서고, 둘은 큰길에 함께 남겨 두었다. 그러나 그곳에 오른 지 채 이 분도 안 되어, 의지하고 흉내 내는 해리엇의 습성 탓에 그녀까지 따라 올라오고 있음을, 요컨대 곧 둘 다 자기 뒤를 따르게 되리라는 것을 깨달았다. 이래선 안 될 일이었다. 엠마는 즉시 발을 멈추고, 반장화의 끈을 좀 고쳐 매야겠다는 핑계로 오솔길을 가득 차지하고 몸을 굽힌 채, 두 사람더러 부디 먼저 가 주면 자기는 곧 반 분 안에 따라가겠다고 청했다. 둘은 시키는 대로 했다. 그리고 장화 손질을 마칠 만하다고 여겨질 무렵, 엠마는 더 지체할 수 있게 된 위안을 손에 넣었으니, 오두막에서 나온 한 아이가 분부받은 대로 단지를 들고 하트필드에서 묽은 죽을 받아 가려고 길을 나서다가 엠마를 따라잡은 것이었다. 이 아이 곁에서 함께 걸으며 말을 걸고 이것저것 묻는 일은 세상 더없이 자연스러운 일이었고, 만약 그때 무슨 꿍꿍이 없이 행동하고 있었다면 더없이 자연스러운 일이 되었을 터였다. 이 덕분에 다른 두 사람은 여전히 앞서갈 수 있었고, 그녀를 기다려야 할 의무도 지지 않았다. 그렇지만 엠마는 본의 아니게 그들에게 다가가고 말았다. 아이의 걸음은 빨랐고 그들의 걸음은 다소 느렸던 것이다. 게다가 두 사람이 보아하니 자기들끼리 흥미로운 대화에 빠져 있던 터라, 그녀로서는 더더욱 마음이 쓰였다. 미스터 엘턴은 활기차게 말하고 있었고, 해리엇은 무척 흐뭇한 표정으로 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 엠마는 아이를 먼저 보내 놓고, 어떻게 하면 좀 더 뒤로 물러설 수 있을까 궁리하기 시작하던 참이었는데, 그때 두 사람이 동시에 돌아보는 바람에 어쩔 수 없이 그들과 합류해야 했다.\n\n미스터 엘턴은 여전히 이야기를 이어 가며, 무언가 흥미로운 시시콜콜한 대목에 빠져 있었다. 그런데 알고 보니 그는 그저 자신의 어여쁜 길동무에게 어제 친구 콜의 집에서 있었던 모임 이야기를 들려주고 있을 뿐이었고, 화제에 오른 것이라곤 스틸턴 치즈, 노스 윌트셔 치즈, 버터, 셀러리, 비트, 그리고 온갖 후식이었으니, 엠마는 적잖이 실망했다.\n\n\"이게 곧 더 나은 이야기로 이어졌을 게 분명해.\" 그녀는 이렇게 자신을 달랬다. \"서로 사랑하는 사이라면 무엇이든 흥미로운 법이고, 무엇이든 마음 깊은 곳의 화제로 들어가는 실마리가 되는 법이니까. 내가 좀 더 오래 떨어져 있을 수만 있었더라면!\"\n\n이제 셋은 조용히 함께 걸어, 목사관 울타리가 보이는 데까지 이르렀다. 그때 적어도 해리엇만이라도 그 집 안으로 들여보내겠다는 결심이 불현듯 떠오른 엠마는, 또다시 장화에 무언가 몹시 잘못된 데가 있는 척하며 뒤로 처져 다시 한번 그것을 손보았다. 이번에는 끈을 짧게 뚝 끊어, 솜씨 좋게 도랑에 던져 버리고는, 곧 두 사람에게 멈춰 달라고 청하지 않을 수 없게 되어, 도저히 매무새를 바로잡아 집까지 그럭저럭 편히 걸어갈 수가 없겠다고 실토했다.\n\n\"끈이 한쪽 떨어져 나가 버렸어요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠네요. 정말 두 분께 더없이 성가신 길동무지만, 제가 이렇게 차림새가 엉망인 일이 그리 자주는 없으면 좋겠어요. 미스터 엘턴, 댁에 좀 들러서, 가정부에게 리본이나 끈, 아니 장화를 붙들어 매 둘 만한 것이면 무엇이든 한 조각 청해도 될까요?\"\n\n미스터 엘턴은 이 제안에 더없이 흡족한 기색이었고, 두 사람을 자기 집으로 안내하며 무엇 하나라도 돋보이게 하려고 보이는 그 민첩함과 정성은 따를 자가 없었다. 두 사람이 안내받아 든 방은 그가 주로 쓰는 앞쪽 방이었고, 그 뒤로 곧장 통하는 또 다른 방이 있었다. 두 방 사이의 문은 열려 있었고, 엠마는 가정부와 함께 그 방으로 들어가 더없이 편안하게 도움을 받았다. 엠마는 문을 발견했을 때 그대로 빠끔히 열어 둘 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 미스터 엘턴이 그 문을 닫아 주기를 단단히 기대하고 있었다. 그렇지만 문은 닫히지 않았고, 여전히 빠끔히 열린 채였다. 다만 엠마는 가정부를 쉴 새 없는 대화에 붙들어 둠으로써, 그가 옆방에서 마음껏 제 화제를 고를 수 있게 해 주기를 바랐다. 십 분 동안 엠마의 귀에는 제 목소리밖에 들리지 않았다. 더는 시간을 끌 수 없었다. 그제야 엠마는 손질을 마치고 모습을 드러내야만 했다.\n\n두 연인은 창가에 함께 서 있었다. 더없이 좋은 광경이었다. 그리고 반 분 동안 엠마는 계략을 멋지게 성공시킨 영광을 만끽했다. 그러나 안 될 일이었다. 그는 아직 본론에 이르지 못했던 것이다. 그는 더없이 상냥하고 더없이 유쾌했다. 그는 해리엇에게, 두 사람이 지나가는 것을 보고 일부러 뒤따라왔노라 말했다. 그 밖에도 자잘한 정중함과 넌지시 던지는 말들이 오갔으나, 진지한 것은 하나도 없었다.\n\n\"조심스럽기도 해라, 정말 조심스러워.\" 엠마는 생각했다. \"한 치 한 치 다가가면서, 확실하다 싶기 전엔 아무것도 걸지 않으려는 거야.\"\n\n그렇지만 자신의 기발한 꾀로 모든 것이 이루어진 것은 아니었어도, 엠마는 그것이 적어도 지금 두 사람에게 큰 즐거움을 안겨 준 계기였으며, 분명 두 사람을 그 위대한 사건으로 이끌어 가고 있으리라고 스스로를 추어올리지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n# 제1권 11장\n\n이제 미스터 엘턴은 그 자신에게 내맡겨 둘 수밖에 없었다. 그의 행복을 살피거나 그의 발걸음을 재촉하는 일은 더 이상 엠마의 능력 밖이었다. 언니네 식구가 오는 날이 워낙 코앞으로 다가와, 처음에는 기대 속에서, 이윽고 실제로, 그것이 그녀의 가장 큰 관심사가 되었다. 그리고 그들이 하트필드에 머무는 열흘 동안, 그 두 연인에게 엠마가 이따금 우연히 거드는 것 이상의 도움을 줄 수 있으리라고는 기대할 수 없었다 — 그녀 자신도 그것을 기대하지 않았다. 그러나 그들도 마음만 먹으면 빠르게 나아갈 수 있으리라. 좋든 싫든 어떻게든 나아가야만 하는 일이었다. 엠마는 그들을 위해 더 많은 여유를 갖고 싶은 마음도 별로 없었다. 세상에는 해 주면 해 줄수록 스스로는 점점 덜 하려 드는 사람들이 있는 법이니까.\n\n미스터 존 나이틀리 부부는 여느 때보다 오래 서리를 떠나 있었던 터라, 당연히 평소보다 한층 큰 관심을 불러일으켰다. 올해까지, 결혼 이후 긴 휴가는 매번 하트필드와 돈웰 애비로 나누어 보내 왔다. 그러나 올가을 휴가는 모두 아이들의 해수욕에 바쳐졌으므로, 서리의 친척들이 그들을 제대로 본 지도 여러 달이 지났고, 미스터 우드하우스는 아예 한 번도 보지 못한 터였다 — 가엾은 이저벨라를 위해서라도 런던까지 그 먼 길을 가도록 도무지 그를 설득할 수 없었으니 말이다. 그리하여 그는 이 너무 짧은 방문을 앞두고 더없이 초조하고 조마조마한 행복에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n그는 딸에게 여정이 안겨 줄 갖은 고생을 골똘히 걱정했고, 일행의 마지막 절반을 태워 와야 할 자기 집 말들과 마부의 고단함도 적잖이 염려했다. 그러나 그 근심은 부질없었다. 십육 마일은 다행히 무사히 지나갔고, 미스터 존 나이틀리 부부와 다섯 아이, 그리고 충분한 수의 유모들이 모두 무탈히 하트필드에 닿았다. 그런 도착에 따르는 부산함과 기쁨, 말을 건네고 맞이하고 다독이고 이리저리 흩어 놓고 자리를 잡아 주어야 할 그 많은 사람들이 빚어내는 소란과 혼잡은, 다른 어떤 이유로 일어났다면 그의 신경이 도무지 견디지 못했을 것이고, 이 일을 위해서라도 그리 오래 버티지는 못했을 것이다. 그러나 미시즈 존 나이틀리는 하트필드의 법도와 아버지의 마음을 워낙 깍듯이 헤아렸기에, 제 어린것들이 한시바삐 즐거움을 누리고, 그 아이들이 바랄 법한 온갖 자유와 시중, 먹고 마시고 자고 노는 모든 것을 조금의 지체도 없이 곧장 누리기를 바라는 어미의 애틋한 마음에도 불구하고, 아이들이 그 자체로든 아니면 그 아이들을 부산스레 돌보는 일로든 오래도록 그를 성가시게 하는 일은 결코 없도록 했다.\n\n미시즈 존 나이틀리는 예쁘장하고 우아한 자그마한 여인으로, 부드럽고 조용한 몸가짐에 천성이 유난히 상냥하고 다정했다. 가족에게 푹 빠져 헌신적인 아내요, 자식에게 애지중지하는 어미였으며, 아버지와 동생에게도 어찌나 살뜰히 매여 있던지, 이 더 높은 인연들이 아니었더라면 그보다 더 따뜻한 사랑이란 있을 수 없을 듯싶을 정도였다. 그녀는 그들 가운데 누구에게서도 결코 흠을 보지 못했다. 그녀는 명민한 분별력이나 어떤 재빠름을 갖춘 여인은 아니었다. 그리고 아버지를 그렇게 닮은 까닭에 그의 체질도 많이 물려받아, 제 건강은 허약하면서 아이들 건강에는 지나치게 노심초사했으며, 두려움도 많고 신경도 많아, 아버지가 미스터 페리를 아끼는 만큼이나 런던의 미스터 윙필드를 따랐다. 두 사람은 또한 너그러운 천성과, 옛 지인이라면 누구든 한결같이 살뜰히 여기는 강한 습성에서도 닮은꼴이었다.\n\n미스터 존 나이틀리는 키가 크고 신사다우며 아주 영리한 사람으로, 자기 직업에서 두각을 나타내고 있었고, 가정적이며 사사로운 됨됨이도 존경받을 만했다. 그러나 무뚝뚝한 몸가짐 탓에 두루 호감을 사지는 못했고, 때로 언짢은 기색을 드러낼 줄도 아는 사람이었다. 성미가 고약한 사람은 아니었고, 그런 비난을 들을 만큼 까닭 없이 자주 신경질을 부리지도 않았다. 그러나 그 성미가 그의 가장 빼어난 덕은 아니었다. 더구나 그토록 숭배하는 아내를 두고서, 그 천성의 어떤 결점이 더 자라나지 않기란 거의 불가능한 일이었다. 그녀의 더없이 다정한 성품이 도리어 그의 성미를 거스르는 셈이었다. 그에게는 그녀에게 없는 명석함과 재빠름이 두루 있었고, 그래서 가끔은 무례하게 굴거나 매정한 말을 내뱉을 줄도 알았다.\n\n그는 그 어여쁜 처제에게 그리 사랑받는 사람은 아니었다. 그의 어떤 잘못도 그녀의 눈을 비껴가지 못했다. 그녀는 이저벨라가 정작 자신은 한 번도 느끼지 못하는 자잘한 상처들을 재빨리 알아챘다. 어쩌면 그가 이저벨라의 동생에게 듣기 좋은 말이라도 곧잘 해 주는 사람이었다면 더 많은 것을 눈감아 주었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그의 태도란 그저 차분하고 다정한 오빠이자 벗의 것일 뿐, 칭찬도 없고 그렇다고 눈멀어 감싸 주는 법도 없었다. 그렇지만 어떤 개인적인 찬사로도, 그녀의 눈에 가장 큰 잘못으로 비치는 것 — 그가 이따금 저지르고 마는, 아버지에 대한 공손한 인내의 결여 — 만큼은 그녀가 못 본 척할 수 없었다. 그 대목에서 그는 바랄 만한 만큼의 참을성을 늘 갖추고 있지는 못했다. 미스터 우드하우스의 별난 버릇과 안달이 때로 그를 자극해, 분별 있는 항변이든 날카로운 응수든 똑같이 어울리지 않는 말을 내뱉게 했던 것이다. 자주 있는 일은 아니었다. 미스터 존 나이틀리는 사실 장인을 무척 존경했고, 대체로 장인에 대한 도리를 깊이 헤아렸으니까. 그러나 엠마의 너그러움이 감당하기에는 그 횟수가 너무 잦았다. 더구나 실제로 거슬리는 일이 일어나지 않을 때조차, 그런 일이 벌어질까 봐 마음 졸여야 하는 고통을 줄곧 견뎌야 했으니 더욱 그러했다. 그래도 방문이 시작될 무렵에는 언제나 더없이 마땅한 마음만이 드러나는 법이었고, 이번 방문은 으레 그렇듯 워낙 짧을 수밖에 없으니 흠 없는 화기애애함 속에 지나가기를 바랄 만했다. 자리에 앉아 미처 마음을 가라앉히기도 전에, 미스터 우드하우스가 침울하게 고개를 가로젓고 한숨을 쉬며, 지난번 딸이 다녀간 이후 하트필드에 닥친 슬픈 변화에 대해 딸의 주의를 끌었다.\n\n\"아이고, 얘야,\" 그가 말했다. \"가엾은 미스 테일러 말이다 — 참으로 딱한 일이지.\"\n\n\"아유, 그러게요, 아버지.\" 그녀가 곧장 동정 어린 목소리로 외쳤다. \"얼마나 그분이 그리우시겠어요! 사랑하는 엠마도요! 두 분께 이 얼마나 끔찍한 상실인지요! 저는 두 분 생각에 어찌나 마음이 아팠는지 몰라요. 그분 없이 어떻게 지내실까 도무지 상상이 안 갔어요. 정말이지 슬픈 변화예요. 그래도 그분은 꽤 잘 지내시는 거죠, 아버지?\"\n\n\"꽤 잘 지내는 듯하구나, 얘야 — 그러기를 바란다 — 꽤 잘 지내는 게지. 그래도 그곳 물이 그 사람한테 그럭저럭 맞는 모양이긴 하다만.\"\n\n여기서 미스터 존 나이틀리가 엠마에게 조용히, 랜덜스의 공기에 무슨 미심쩍은 데라도 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아유! 아니에요 — 조금도요. 저는 평생 웨스턴 부인이 그렇게 좋아 보이는 걸 본 적이 없어요 — 그렇게 안색이 좋은 적이 없었죠. 아버지는 그저 당신 자신의 서운함을 말씀하시는 것뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"두 분 모두에게 참으로 명예로운 일이군.\" 점잖은 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그래서 아버지, 그분을 그럭저럭 자주 만나시나요?\" 이저벨라가 제 아버지에게 꼭 어울리는 가련한 목소리로 물었다.\n\n미스터 우드하우스가 머뭇거렸다. \"내가 바라는 만큼 그리 자주는 못 본다, 얘야.\"\n\n\"아유! 아버지, 우리가 그분들을 못 본 날이라곤 결혼한 뒤로 통틀어 하루밖에 없는걸요. 그 하루를 빼고는 날마다 아침이든 저녁이든 미스터 웨스턴이나 웨스턴 부인을, 대개는 두 분 다 뵈었어요 — 랜덜스에서든 여기서든요. 그리고 언니도 짐작하겠지만, 거의 다 여기서였죠. 그분들은 정말이지 더없이 친절하게 와 주세요. 미스터 웨스턴은 정말 부인 못지않게 다정한 분이고요. 아버지, 그렇게 침울하게 말씀하시면 언니가 우리 모두에 대해 잘못된 생각을 갖게 될 거예요. 미스 테일러가 그립다는 건 누구나 알 만한 일이지만, 미스터 웨스턴 부부가 우리가 미리 짐작했던 만큼은 결코 그분을 그리워하지 않도록 정말로 막아 주고 있다는 것도 모두가 똑똑히 알아 둬야 해요 — 이게 한 치도 틀림없는 사실이거든요.\"\n\n\"바로 그래야 마땅하지요.\" 미스터 존 나이틀리가 말했다. \"그리고 처제 편지를 보고 내가 짐작했던 그대로네요. 그분이 처제에게 마음을 써 주고 싶어 하리라는 건 의심할 여지가 없고, 그분 남편이 매인 데 없이 사교적인 사람이니 만사가 수월한 거지요. 여보, 내가 늘 말하지 않았소. 그 변화가 당신이 걱정하던 것만큼 하트필드에 대단한 일은 아닐 거라고 말이오. 이제 엠마 얘기까지 들었으니, 부디 마음이 놓이기를 바라오.\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 그야 물론,\" 미스터 우드하우스가 말했다. \"그래, 분명히 — 웨스턴 부인이, 그 가엾은 웨스턴 부인이 우리를 꽤 자주 보러 오는 거야 나도 부인할 수 없지 — 하지만 그 사람은 — 늘 다시 돌아가야만 하잖니.\"\n\n\"만약 그분이 안 돌아가면 미스터 웨스턴이 무척 곤란하실 거예요, 아버지. 가엾은 미스터 웨스턴은 아주 잊으셨군요.\"\n\n\"제 생각에도 정말,\" 존 나이틀리가 유쾌하게 말했다. \"미스터 웨스턴에게도 얼마간 권리가 있지요. 엠마, 자네와 나는 그 가엾은 남편 편을 한번 들어 봅시다. 나는 남편이고 자네는 아내가 아니니, 남자의 권리라는 게 우리 둘에게는 똑같은 무게로 와닿을 법하지요. 이저벨라로 말하자면, 결혼한 지 오래라 미스터 웨스턴이고 뭐고 죄다 한껏 제쳐 두는 편이 편하다는 걸 알 만큼은 됐고요.\"\n\n\"저요, 여보?\" 그 아내가 일부만 듣고 일부만 알아듣고는 외쳤다. \"제 얘기를 하시는 거예요? 결혼을 두고 저보다 더 든든한 옹호자가 있을 리도, 있을 수도 없다고요. 미스 테일러가 하트필드를 떠나는 그 비참함만 아니었다면, 저는 그분을 세상에서 가장 운 좋은 여인으로밖에 생각하지 않았을 거예요. 그리고 미스터 웨스턴을 가볍게 여기다니요, 그 훌륭한 미스터 웨스턴을 — 그분이 받을 자격이 없는 게 하나도 없다고 생각하는걸요. 그분은 세상에 둘도 없이 성품이 좋은 분 가운데 하나일 거예요. 당신과 시아주버님만 빼면, 성미로 따져 그분에게 견줄 사람을 저는 모르겠어요. 지난 부활절 그 바람 몹시 불던 날, 헨리의 연을 대신 날려 주시던 그 모습을 저는 영영 못 잊을 거예요 — 그리고 재작년 구월에, 콥햄에 성홍열이 돌지 않는다고 일부러 안심시켜 주려고 밤 열두 시에 그 쪽지를 써 보내 주신 그 각별한 친절 이후로, 그분보다 더 마음 따뜻하고 더 훌륭한 사람은 세상에 없으리라고 저는 확신하게 됐어요. 누구든 그분에게 어울릴 자격이 있는 사람이라면, 그건 분명 미스 테일러일 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 젊은이는 어디 있소?\" 존 나이틀리가 말했다. \"이번에 여기 왔었소 — 안 왔었소?\"\n\n\"아직 안 왔어요.\" 엠마가 대답했다. \"결혼하고 곧 오리라는 기대가 자못 컸지만, 결국 아무 일도 없이 끝났어요. 그리고 요즘은 그분 얘기가 나오는 걸 통 듣지 못했고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 얘야, 너 그 편지 얘기를 해 드려야지.\" 그녀의 아버지가 말했다. \"그 사람이 가엾은 웨스턴 부인에게 축하 편지를 써 보냈는데, 아주 격식 있고 훌륭한 편지였단다. 부인이 그걸 내게 보여 줬지. 나는 그 사람이 참 잘했다고 여겼다. 그게 자기 생각에서 나온 건지는 모를 일이지만 말이다. 그 사람은 아직 젊고, 외삼촌이 어쩌면 —\"\n\n\"아버지도 참, 그분은 스물셋이에요. 세월 가는 걸 잊으셨군요.\"\n\n\"스물셋이라! 정말 그러냐? 거참, 도무지 그럴 줄은 몰랐구나 — 가엾은 어미를 여의었을 때 그 애가 겨우 두 살이었는데! 거참, 세월이 정말 쏜살같구나! 게다가 내 기억력은 영 형편없고. 어쨌든 그건 더없이 훌륭하고 멋진 편지였고, 미스터 웨스턴 부부에게 큰 기쁨을 안겨 줬단다. 내 기억에 그건 웨이머스에서 쓴 거였고, 날짜가 구월 이십팔일이었지 — 그리고 '친애하는 부인께'로 시작했는데, 그다음은 어떻게 이어졌는지 잊었구나. 그리고 'F. C. 웨스턴 처칠'이라고 서명했지 — 그건 똑똑히 기억한단다.\"\n\n\"그 사람 참 마음씨 곱고 반듯하기도 하지!\" 마음씨 착한 미시즈 존 나이틀리가 외쳤다. \"더없이 사랑스러운 젊은이임에 틀림없어요. 그렇지만 아버지와 함께 집에서 살지 못한다니 얼마나 슬픈 일인지요! 자식이 제 부모와 타고난 보금자리에서 떼어내진다니, 거기엔 어쩐지 너무도 충격적인 데가 있어요! 미스터 웨스턴이 어떻게 그 애를 보낼 수 있었는지 저는 도무지 이해할 수가 없어요. 제 자식을 내준다니요! 정말이지 남에게 그런 일을 권하는 사람이라면 누구든 저는 좋게 볼 수가 없을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"처칠 집안을 좋게 본 사람은 애당초 아무도 없을걸.\" 미스터 존 나이틀리가 냉랭하게 말했다. \"하지만 당신이 헨리나 존을 내줄 때 느낄 법한 그 심정을 미스터 웨스턴도 느꼈으리라고는 생각지 마시오. 미스터 웨스턴은 감정이 깊은 사람이라기보다는 오히려 느긋하고 쾌활한 사람이오. 매사를 있는 그대로 받아들이고 어떻게든 거기서 즐거움을 만들어 내는 사람이지. 짐작건대 그의 위안이란 가정의 정 같은 것보다는, 이른바 사교라는 데에, 그러니까 이웃들과 일주일에 다섯 번씩 먹고 마시고 휘스트를 칠 수 있는 그 능력에 훨씬 더 기대고 있을 거요. 집안이 주는 것 따위보다 말이오.\"\n* 휘스트: 네 사람이 둘씩 짝을 지어 하는 카드놀이로, 19세기 영국 상류층의 대표적인 사교 오락.\n\n엠마는 미스터 웨스턴을 흠잡는 듯한 말이 영 마음에 들지 않아 한마디 받아칠까 하는 마음이 반쯤 일었으나, 꾹 참고 그냥 흘려보냈다. 될 수 있으면 평화를 지키고 싶었다. 게다가 그 강한 가정적 습성, 제 집이 제게는 부족함 없이 족하다는 그 마음에는 어딘가 떳떳하고 값진 데가 있었으니 — 형부가 흔한 수준의 사교를, 또 그런 사교를 소중히 여기는 사람들을 내려다보는 기질도 거기서 비롯된 것이었다. 그것은 너그러이 봐줄 만한 충분한 자격이 있었다.\n\n# 제1권 12장\n\n미스터 나이틀리가 그들과 함께 저녁을 들기로 되어 있었다. 미스터 우드하우스로서는 썩 내키지 않는 일이었으니, 이저벨라가 돌아와 함께 보내는 첫날을 누구와도 나누고 싶지 않았던 까닭이다. 그러나 엠마의 옳고 그름에 대한 분별이 이를 정해 두었다. 두 형제에게 마땅히 갖추어야 할 도리를 헤아린 것은 물론이거니와, 얼마 전 미스터 나이틀리와 자기 사이에 있었던 불화 탓에, 그에게 합당한 초대를 보내는 일이 그녀에게는 각별한 즐거움이기도 했다.\n\n이제 두 사람이 다시 벗이 될 수 있기를 그녀는 바랐다. 화해할 때가 되었다고 여긴 것이다. 사실 화해라 할 것까지는 없었다. 자기가 그릇된 것은 결코 아니었고, 그 또한 자기가 잘못했다고 인정할 리 없었다. 어느 한쪽의 양보란 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 그러나 그들이 다툰 적이 있었다는 사실 자체를 잊은 척할 때는 되었다. 그가 방으로 들어섰을 때 그녀가 아이 하나를 안고 있었다는 점이 우정의 회복에 도움이 되기를 그녀는 바랐다 — 가장 어린아이, 생후 여덟 달쯤 된 예쁘장한 여자아이로, 이제 처음으로 하트필드를 찾아온 참이었고, 이모 품에 안겨 둥기둥기 어르는 손길이 무척이나 즐거운 듯했다. 과연 도움이 되었다. 그가 처음에는 굳은 표정과 짤막한 물음으로 시작했으나, 이내 여느 때처럼 그 아이들 모두에 관한 이야기로 이끌려 들어갔고, 더없이 다정한 사이의 격의 없는 태도로 아이를 그녀의 품에서 받아 안았던 것이다. 엠마는 두 사람이 다시 벗이 되었음을 느꼈다. 그 확신이 처음에는 큰 만족을 주더니, 이윽고 약간의 장난기를 부추겨, 그가 아기를 어르며 들여다보고 있을 때 그녀는 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"우리가 조카들에 대해서는 생각이 똑같다는 게 얼마나 마음 든든한지 몰라요. 남자와 여자 이야기로 가면 우리 의견이 영 갈릴 때가 더러 있지만, 이 아이들에 관해서라면 한 번도 어긋난 적이 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"자네가 남자와 여자를 가늠할 때에도 이 아이들을 대할 때만큼이나 천성을 길잡이로 삼고, 공상이나 변덕에 휘둘리지 않는다면, 우리 생각은 늘 같을 수 있을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그럼요 — 우리 사이의 불화는 언제나 제가 그릇된 데서 비롯되는 거겠죠.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그럴 만한 이유가 있지. 자네가 태어났을 때 나는 열여섯 살이었으니까.\"\n\n\"그땐 대단한 차이였죠.\" 그녀가 받았다. \"그 시절 우리 둘의 처지로 보면, 분별에서 당신이 저보다 훨씬 위였던 게 틀림없고요. 하지만 스물한 해가 흘렀으니, 우리 견식도 어지간히 가까워지지 않았을까요?\"\n\n\"그래 — 어지간히 가까워졌지.\"\n\n\"하지만 그래도, 우리 생각이 갈릴 때 제가 옳을 가능성을 줄 만큼은 아니라는 거죠.\"\n\n\"나는 여전히 십육 년의 경험만큼 자네보다 앞서 있고, 게다가 예쁘장한 젊은 아가씨도 아니고 응석받이 어린애도 아니라는 이점까지 있지. 자, 엠마, 우리 그만 화해하고 이 일은 더 들추지 마세. 꼬마 엠마야, 네 이모더러 일러 주려무나 — 묵은 불평을 되살리는 것보다 너한테 더 나은 본을 보여야 하는 거라고. 그리고 전에는 틀리지 않았다 쳐도, 지금은 틀린 거라고 말이다.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"정말 맞는 말이에요. 꼬마 엠마야, 너는 자라서 네 이모보다 나은 여자가 되렴. 한없이 더 영리하고, 그 절반만큼도 우쭐대지 말고. 자, 미스터 나이틀리, 한두 마디만 더 하고 끝낼게요. 좋은 뜻으로 한 일이라는 점에서는 우리 둘 다 옳았고, 제 쪽 주장으로 인한 결과 가운데 잘못된 것으로 드러난 건 아직 하나도 없다고 말씀드려야겠어요. 저는 그저 미스터 마틴이 정말이지 몹시, 쓰라리게 낙심한 건 아니라는 걸 알고 싶을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"남자가 그보다 더 낙심할 수는 없을 정도일세.\" 짤막하나 빈틈없는 대답이었다.\n\n\"아! 정말이지 마음이 아프네요. 자, 저와 손을 맞잡아요.\"\n\n그렇게 더없이 따뜻하게 두 사람이 막 손을 맞잡았을 때, 존 나이틀리가 모습을 드러냈다. \"잘 지냈나, 조지?\" \"존, 자네는 어떤가?\" 하는 인사가 진짜 영국식으로 오갔으니, 거의 무심해 보일 만큼 잔잔한 겉모습 아래에, 필요하다면 어느 쪽이든 상대를 위해 무엇이든 해 줄 진정한 정을 묻어 두는 식이었다.\n\n저녁나절은 조용하고 도란도란 이야기 나누기 좋은 시간이었으니, 미스터 우드하우스가 사랑하는 이저벨라와 정겹게 이야기를 나누고자 카드놀이를 아예 마다한 까닭이었다. 자그마한 일행은 자연스레 두 패로 나뉘었다. 한쪽에는 그와 딸이, 다른 쪽에는 두 미스터 나이틀리가 있었다. 화제는 완전히 갈려 거의 섞이는 법이 없었고 — 엠마만이 이따금 이쪽저쪽에 끼어들 따름이었다.\n\n형제는 저마다의 일과 관심사를 이야기했으나, 주로는 형 쪽의 것이었으니, 기질이 훨씬 더 말이 많은 편이었고 늘 더 많이 떠드는 쪽이었다. 치안 판사로서 그는 대개 법률상의 어떤 점을 존에게 물을 거리가 있거나, 적어도 들려줄 만한 흥미로운 일화 하나쯤은 있었다. 또 농부로서, 돈웰의 직영 농장을 손수 건사하는 처지였으니, 어느 밭에 내년 무엇을 심을지를 일러 주고, 동생에게 흥미롭지 않을 수 없는 갖가지 고장 소식을 전해야 했다 — 그곳은 동생에게도 인생의 가장 긴 세월 동안 똑같이 고향이었고, 그 애착이 깊은 까닭이었다. 배수로를 놓을 계획이며, 울타리를 바꾸는 일이며, 나무 한 그루를 베는 일이며, 한 에이커 한 에이커가 밀밭이 될지 순무밭이 될지 봄보리밭이 될지 하는 이야기까지, 존은 그의 차분한 성미가 허락하는 한도껏 똑같은 관심으로 함께 파고들었다. 그리고 기꺼이 떠드는 형이 물어볼 거리를 조금이라도 남겨 두는 날이면, 그 물음에는 열의에 가까운 어조까지 묻어났다.\n\n이렇게 그들이 흐뭇하게 이야기에 빠져 있는 동안, 미스터 우드하우스는 딸을 붙들고 행복한 아쉬움과 근심 어린 애정을 한껏 쏟아 내며 즐기고 있었다.\n\n\"가엾은 우리 이저벨라.\" 그가 다정하게 딸의 손을 잡으며, 다섯 아이 가운데 누군가를 위해 분주히 손을 놀리던 그녀를 잠시 멈춰 세우고 말했다. \"어쩌면 이렇게 오랜만이냐, 끔찍이도 오랜만이로구나, 네가 여기 온 게! 그 먼 길을 오느라 얼마나 고단하겠니! 오늘은 일찍 자거라, 얘야 — 그리고 자기 전에 묽은 죽을 좀 들기를 권하마. 너랑 나랑 같이 죽 한 사발씩 들자꾸나. 엠마야, 우리 다 같이 죽을 좀 드는 게 어떻겠니.\"\n\n엠마는 도무지 그럴 마음이 들지 않았으니, 두 미스터 나이틀리가 그 죽이라는 물건에 대해서는 자기만큼이나 끄떡없다는 걸 잘 아는 까닭이었다. 그리하여 죽은 딱 두 사발만 시켰다. 죽을 기리는 이야기가 조금 더 오가고, 어찌하여 사람마다 저녁마다 그것을 들지 않는지 의아해하는 말까지 곁들인 끝에, 그는 자못 진지하게 생각에 잠긴 표정으로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"얘야, 그건 좀 잘못된 일이었어. 가을을 여기 오지 않고 사우스엔드에서 보내다니 말이다. 나는 바닷바람을 그리 대단하게 여긴 적이 없단다.\"\n\n\"미스터 윙필드가 한사코 권한 거예요, 아버지 — 안 그랬으면 가지도 않았어요. 아이들 모두한테 좋다고 했지만, 특히 우리 벨라의 목이 약한 데 좋다고요 — 바닷바람도 해수욕도요.\"\n\n\"아! 얘야, 하지만 페리는 바다가 그 애한테 좋을지 못내 미심쩍어했단다. 그리고 나로 말하자면, 전에 너한테 말한 적은 없는 것 같다만, 바다가 누구한테든 이로운 경우란 아주 드물다고 오래전부터 확실히 믿어 왔어. 한번은 바다가 나를 죽일 뻔했다니까.\"\n\n\"자, 자.\" 엠마가 위험한 화제임을 느끼고 끼어들었다. \"제발 바다 이야기는 그만하셨으면 해요. 저는 부러워서 속상하단 말이에요 — 한 번도 본 적이 없는 제가요! 사우스엔드는 금지예요, 부탁이에요. 그런데 이저벨라 언니, 언니가 미스터 페리 안부 묻는 걸 아직 한 번도 못 들었네. 그분은 늘 언니를 잊지 않는데.\"\n\n\"아! 그 좋으신 미스터 페리 — 그분은 어떠세요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그럭저럭 괜찮긴 한데, 아주 좋지는 않단다. 가엾은 페리는 담즙이 안 좋은데, 제 몸 돌볼 틈이 없어 — 제 몸 돌볼 틈이 없다고 나한테 그러더구나 — 참 딱한 노릇이지 — 하지만 온 고장에서 늘 그 사람을 찾으니 말이다. 그만큼 환자가 몰리는 의원은 어디에도 없을 게다. 하기야 그만큼 솜씨 좋은 사람도 어디에도 없으니.\"\n\n\"그럼 페리 부인하고 아이들은요? 다들 잘 있어요? 아이들은 많이 컸고요? 저는 미스터 페리를 아주 좋게 여겨요. 곧 한번 들러 주시면 좋겠어요. 우리 어린것들을 보면 무척 반가워하실 텐데.\"\n\n\"내일 와 줬으면 한단다. 나도 내 몸에 관해 좀 중요한 걸 한두 가지 물어볼 게 있거든. 그리고 얘야, 그 사람이 오거든 우리 벨라의 목을 한번 봐 달라고 하는 게 좋겠다.\"\n\n\"아! 아버지, 그 애 목은 한결 좋아져서 이젠 거의 걱정이 없을 정도예요. 해수욕이 그 애한테 더없이 도움이 됐거나, 아니면 팔월부터 줄곧 이따금 발라 온 미스터 윙필드의 그 훌륭한 도포약 덕분이거나일 거예요.\"\n\n\"얘야, 해수욕이 그 애한테 도움이 됐을 성싶지는 않구나 — 그리고 네가 도포약이 필요했던 줄 알았더라면, 내가 말을 넣었을 텐데 —\"\n\n\"아버지, 아무래도 베이츠 부인하고 미스 베이츠는 까맣게 잊으신 모양이에요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"그분들 안부를 한마디도 안 물으시잖아요.\"\n\n\"아! 그 착한 베이츠네 — 내가 다 부끄럽구나 — 하지만 너는 편지마다 거의 그분들 이야기를 하더구나. 다들 잘 계시면 좋겠다. 그 마음씨 좋으신 베이츠 노부인 — 내일 찾아뵙고 아이들도 데려가야겠어. 그분들은 늘 우리 아이들을 보면 그렇게 반가워하시니까. 그리고 그 더없이 훌륭하신 미스 베이츠! 어쩌면 그렇게들 한결같이 갸륵한 분들인지! 그분들은 어떠세요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 대체로는 그럭저럭 잘 지내신단다, 얘야. 하지만 가엾은 베이츠 부인이 한 달쯤 전에 고약한 감기에 걸리셨지.\"\n\n\"저런, 어쩌나! 하지만 감기가 올가을처럼 흔했던 적이 없었어요. 미스터 윙필드 말로는, 거의 독감이 돌 때 말고는 이렇게 두루두루, 이렇게 심하게 걸리는 건 본 적이 없다고 하던걸요.\"\n\n\"그런 면이 적잖아 있긴 했지, 얘야. 하지만 네가 말하는 정도까지는 아니란다. 페리 말로는 감기가 두루 돌긴 했어도, 십일월에 흔히 보던 만큼 심하지는 않다더구나. 페리는 통틀어 병치레가 많은 철이라고는 하지 않더라.\"\n\n\"네, 미스터 윙필드도 딱히 그렇게까지 병치레가 많다고 여기는 것 같지는 않아요, 다만 —\"\n\n\"아! 가엾은 내 새끼, 사실은 런던이란 데가 늘 병치레가 많은 철이란 말이다. 런던에서는 누구도 건강하지 못하고, 건강할 수가 없어. 너를 거기서 살게 하다니, 끔찍한 일이지! 그렇게 멀리서! 게다가 공기는 또 그렇게 나쁘고!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 정말이지 우리는 결코 나쁜 공기 속에 사는 게 아니에요. 우리 동네는 런던에서도 다른 데보다 훨씬 좋은 곳인걸요! 아버지, 우리를 런던 전체랑 한데 묶어 생각하시면 안 돼요. 브런즈윅 스퀘어 일대는 다른 데 거의 전부와는 아주 다르거든요. 우리 동네는 정말이지 바람이 잘 통해요! 솔직히 저는 런던의 다른 어디에서도 살고 싶지 않아요 — 우리 아이들을 두고 만족할 만한 곳이 그 밖엔 거의 없거든요. 하지만 우리 동네는 유난히 바람이 잘 통한답니다! 미스터 윙필드도 공기로 치면 브런즈윅 스퀘어 부근이 단연 가장 좋다고 여기는걸요.\"\n\n\"아! 얘야, 그래도 하트필드만은 못하지. 너는 좋게만 말하지만 — 너희가 하트필드에서 한 주만 지내고 나면 다들 딴사람이 되거든. 도무지 같은 사람 같지가 않아. 그런데 지금은, 너희 중 누구도 안색이 좋아 보인다고는 못 하겠구나.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말씀하시니 마음이 안 좋네요, 아버지. 하지만 정말이지, 어딜 가든 영 떨쳐 내지 못하는 그 자잘한 신경성 두통과 가슴 두근거림만 빼면, 저는 아주 건강해요. 그리고 아이들이 잠들기 전에 좀 창백해 보였다면, 그건 그저 여행에다 여기 온 기쁨까지 더해 평소보다 좀 더 고단했던 탓일 뿐이에요. 내일은 그 애들 안색을 더 좋게 봐 주시면 좋겠어요. 정말이지 미스터 윙필드가 그러는데, 우리를 이렇게 다들 좋은 상태로 떠나보낸 적은 없는 것 같다고 했거든요. 적어도 미스터 나이틀리만큼은 안색이 나쁘다고 여기지 않으시겠죠.\" 그러면서 그녀는 애정 어린 근심을 담아 남편 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸다.\n\n\"그저 그렇구나, 얘야. 빈말로 추어줄 수가 없는걸. 미스터 존 나이틀리는 안색이 좋은 것과는 한참 멀어 보인다.\"\n\n\"무슨 일입니까, 어르신? 저더러 하신 말씀인가요?\" 자기 이름이 들리자 미스터 존 나이틀리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"여보, 아버지께서 당신 안색이 좋아 보이지 않는다고 하셔서 마음이 안 좋네요 — 하지만 그저 좀 피곤한 탓이길 바라요. 그래도 알다시피, 집을 떠나기 전에 미스터 윙필드를 한번 봤더라면 싶었어요.\"\n\n\"이저벨라.\" 그가 황급히 외쳤다. \"제발 내 안색 따위는 걱정하지 마시오. 당신은 당신하고 아이들이나 약 먹이고 싸고도느라 만족하시고, 나는 내키는 대로 보이게 내버려 두구려.\"\n\n\"형부가 미스터 나이틀리께 하시던 얘기를 제가 제대로 못 알아들었어요.\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"형부 친구분 미스터 그레이엄이 새로 산 영지를 관리하려고 스코틀랜드에서 집사를 데려올 작정이라는 거 말이에요. 그게 잘될까요? 그 해묵은 편견이 너무 세지 않을까요?\"\n\n그러고는 이런 식으로 어찌나 길게, 그리고 솜씨 좋게 이야기를 끌어갔던지, 다시 아버지와 언니 쪽으로 주의를 돌려야 했을 무렵에는, 이저벨라가 제인 페어팩스의 안부를 다정하게 묻는 것 말고는 더 고약한 이야기를 들을 일이 없었다. 그리고 제인 페어팩스로 말하자면, 평소에는 엠마가 그리 마음에 들어 하는 상대는 아니었으나, 그 순간만큼은 그녀를 함께 칭찬해 주는 일이 무척이나 반가웠다.\n\n\"그 사랑스럽고 다정한 제인 페어팩스!\" 미시즈 존 나이틀리가 말했다. \"어쩌다 시내에서 잠깐 우연히 마주친 것 말고는, 그 애를 본 지 정말 오래됐어! 그 애가 찾아가면 그 착하신 노할머니랑 훌륭하신 이모님은 얼마나 좋으실까! 나는 우리 엠마를 봐서라도 그 애가 하이베리에 더 자주 못 있는 게 늘 너무너무 아쉬워. 하지만 이제 딸이 결혼했으니, 캠벨 대령 부부도 그 애를 도무지 떼어 놓지 못하실 거야. 엠마한테 정말 더없이 즐거운 말동무가 될 텐데.\"\n\n미스터 우드하우스는 그 모든 말에 동의하면서도 한마디 덧붙였다.\n\n\"하지만 우리 어린 벗 해리엇 스미스도 꼭 그만큼이나 예쁘장하고 참한 아가씨란다. 너도 해리엇을 좋아하게 될 게다. 엠마한테 해리엇보다 더 나은 말동무는 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 정말 기뻐요 — 하지만 그렇게 더없이 교양 있고 빼어난 사람으로 알려진 건 제인 페어팩스뿐이잖아요! 게다가 나이도 엠마하고 꼭 같고요.\"\n\n이 화제는 무척 화기애애하게 논의되었고, 비슷한 정도의 다른 화제들이 뒤를 이어 비슷한 화목함 속에 흘러갔다. 그러나 그날 저녁은 작은 소동이 한 차례 되살아나는 일 없이는 끝나지 않았다. 죽이 나오자 할 말거리가 잔뜩 생겼던 것이다 — 갖은 찬사와 숱한 품평, 어떤 체질에도 이롭다는 일말의 의심 없는 단언, 그리고 그것을 변변하게 내놓는 법이 없는 여러 집에 대한 자못 매서운 성토 — 그러나 공교롭게도, 딸이 들 수 있는 실패 사례들 가운데 가장 최근의 것이라 그만큼 두드러진 것이, 다름 아닌 사우스엔드에서 둔 자기 집 요리사 이야기였다. 그 철에 잠깐 고용한 젊은 여자였는데, 묽되 너무 묽지는 않은, 곱고 매끈한 죽 한 사발이 무슨 말인지 도무지 알아듣질 못했다는 것이다. 아무리 들고 싶어 청해도, 변변한 것을 한 번도 얻어 본 적이 없다고 했다. 여기서 위험한 틈이 열렸다.\n\n\"아!\" 미스터 우드하우스가 고개를 가로저으며 딱한 근심을 담아 딸에게 눈길을 고정한 채 내뱉었다. 엠마의 귀에는 그 탄식이 이렇게 들렸다. \"아! 사우스엔드에 간 일로 인한 딱한 결과는 끝이 없구나. 입에 올리기조차 못할 일이야.\" 그래서 그녀는 잠시나마 아버지가 그 일을 입에 올리지 않기를, 잠자코 곱씹는 것만으로 그가 제 매끈한 죽 맛을 다시 즐길 기분이 돌아오기를 바랐다. 그러나 몇 분쯤 사이를 두고 나서, 그는 이렇게 운을 뗐다.\n\n\"올가을에 여기 오지 않고 바다로 간 일은, 두고두고 못내 안타까울 게다.\"\n\n\"하지만 왜 안타까워하세요, 아버지? 정말이지 그게 아이들한테 큰 도움이 됐는걸요.\"\n\n\"게다가 말이다, 꼭 바다로 가야 했다면, 사우스엔드만은 아니었어야 했어. 사우스엔드는 건강에 안 좋은 곳이란다. 페리도 네가 사우스엔드로 정했다는 말에 놀라더구나.\"\n\n\"많은 사람이 그런 생각을 갖고 있다는 건 저도 알아요, 하지만 정말이지 그건 잘못된 생각이에요, 아버지. 우리는 거기서 다들 더없이 건강하게 지냈고, 진흙 때문에 조금도 불편한 적이 없었어요. 그리고 미스터 윙필드 말로는 그곳을 건강에 안 좋다고 여기는 건 완전히 잘못이라고 했어요. 그 사람 말은 믿을 만하다고 확신해요, 공기의 성질을 속속들이 아는 사람인 데다, 자기 친형 식구도 거기를 여러 번 다녀왔으니까요.\"\n\n\"어딜 가려거든, 크로머로 갔어야지, 얘야. 페리도 한번 크로머에서 한 주를 보냈는데, 해수욕장 중에 거기가 단연 최고라고 한단다. 탁 트인 멋진 바다에, 공기도 아주 맑다더구나. 그리고 내가 듣기로는, 거기서는 바다랑 아주 떨어진 데에 — 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 데에 — 아주 편안한 숙소를 잡을 수 있었다더라. 페리한테 의논했어야 했어.\"\n\n\"하지만 아버지, 길의 차이가 있잖아요. 그게 얼마나 컸을지 한번 생각해 보세요. 사십 마일이 아니라, 어쩌면 백 마일이었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"아! 얘야, 페리 말마따나, 건강이 걸린 일이라면 다른 건 따질 게 못 된단다. 그리고 기왕 길을 나서야 한다면, 사십 마일이나 백 마일이나 별 차이가 없는 거야. 차라리 아예 움직이지 않는 게 낫지, 사십 마일을 가서 더 나쁜 공기 속에 들어가느니 그냥 런던에 죽 머무는 게 낫고말고. 이게 바로 페리가 한 말이란다. 그 사람 보기엔 영 잘못 판단한 처사였던 게지.\"\n\n아버지를 멈추려는 엠마의 시도는 헛수고였다. 그리고 그가 이런 지경에까지 이르고 보니, 형부가 발끈하고 나선 것도 그녀로서는 이상할 게 없었다.\n\n\"미스터 페리는 말이지요.\" 그가 자못 못마땅한 목소리로 말했다. \"물어볼 때까지 제 의견을 잠자코 간직하는 편이 나을 겁니다. 어째서 그 사람이 내가 하는 일을 두고 의아해하는 걸 제 일거리로 삼는 겁니까? 내가 식구를 해안의 이쪽으로 데려가든 저쪽으로 데려가든 말이지요. 미스터 페리한테 그럴 자격이 있다면, 나한테도 내 판단을 쓸 자격쯤은 있을 줄 압니다. 나는 그 사람 약이 필요 없는 만큼이나 그 사람 지시도 필요 없어요.\" 그는 말을 멈추었고 — 이내 좀 누그러져, 다만 비꼬는 듯한 무미건조함만을 담아 덧붙였다. \"미스터 페리가, 아내와 다섯 아이를 백삼십 마일이나 되는 거리로 사십 마일짜리 거리만큼의 비용과 수고만으로 옮기는 방도를 알려 줄 수 있다면야, 나도 그 사람 못지않게 기꺼이 사우스엔드보다 크로머를 택하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"맞아, 맞아.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 더없이 잽싸게 끼어들며 외쳤다. \"정말 맞는 말일세. 그건 정말 따져 볼 만한 점이지. 그런데 존, 내가 자네한테 들려주던 그 생각 말일세, 랭엄으로 가는 길을 옮기는 것 말이야 — 직영 목초지를 가로지르지 않도록 좀 더 오른쪽으로 트는 것 말일세, 나는 무슨 어려움이 있을지 도무지 모르겠어. 하이베리 사람들한테 불편이 되는 일이라면 시도조차 안 하겠지만, 자네가 지금 그 길이 난 자리를 정확히 떠올려 보면…… 하지만 그걸 따져 볼 유일한 길은 지도를 들여다보는 거겠지. 내일 아침 애비에서 자네를 볼 수 있으면 좋겠네. 그때 함께 지도를 살펴보고, 자네 의견을 들려주게나.\"\n\n미스터 우드하우스는 벗 페리를 향한 그토록 모진 비난에 적잖이 동요했으니, 사실 그는 자기도 모르는 사이에 자신의 숱한 감정과 표현을 그 페리의 입에 갖다 붙여 온 터였다. 그러나 두 딸의 어르고 다독이는 보살핌이 차츰 눈앞의 화근을 걷어 냈고, 한 형제의 그 즉각적인 기민함과 다른 형제의 누그러진 마음이 그 일이 되살아나는 것을 막아 주었다.\n\n# 제1권 13장\n\n미시즈 존 나이틀리는 이번 하트필드 방문 동안만큼은 세상에 둘도 없이 행복한 사람이었을 것이다. 아침마다 다섯 아이를 데리고 옛 친지들을 두루 찾아다니고, 저녁마다 그날 한 일을 아버지며 동생과 두루 이야기하며 지냈으니 말이다. 그녀에게는 달리 바랄 것이 하나도 없었다 — 다만 날들이 이토록 빨리 흘러가지만 않았으면 했을 뿐. 더없이 즐거운 방문이었다. 너무 짧다는 점에서 완벽한 방문이었다.\n\n대체로 그들의 저녁나절은 아침보다는 벗들과 어울리는 일이 적었다. 그러나 단 한 차례의 정식 만찬 약속만은 — 그것도 집을 떠나 가야 하는 약속이었는데 — 비록 성탄절이라 해도 피할 도리가 없었다. 미스터 웨스턴은 거절을 용납하지 않았다. 다들 하루 날을 잡아 랜덜스에서 만찬을 들어야 했다. 미스터 우드하우스조차도, 일행이 둘로 갈라지는 것보다는 그쪽이 차라리 가능한 일이라고 여기도록 설득당하고 말았다.\n\n다들 어떻게 가야 하느냐 하는 문제에서, 그는 트집을 잡을 수 있었다면 잡으려 들었을 것이다. 그러나 아들 내외의 마차와 말이 실제로 하트필드에 와 있었으니, 그 점에 관해서는 그저 간단한 물음 하나밖에 던지지 못했다. 그것은 의심이라고 할 것도 못 되었다. 게다가 마차 한 대에 해리엇이 탈 자리도 충분히 나리라는 것을 아버지에게 납득시키는 데에도 엠마는 오래 걸리지 않았다.\n\n해리엇과 미스터 엘턴, 그리고 미스터 나이틀리 — 그들만의 각별한 동아리 — 가 그들과 자리를 함께하도록 초대받은 사람의 전부였다. 인원이 적은 만큼 시간도 일렀다. 매사에 미스터 우드하우스의 습관과 취향이 고려되었던 것이다.\n\n이 큰 행사 전날 저녁(미스터 우드하우스가 십이월 이십사일에 밖에서 만찬을 든다는 것은 실로 대단한 행사였으니까)을 해리엇은 하트필드에서 보냈고, 감기 기운이 어찌나 심해진 채 집으로 돌아갔던지, 고더드 부인의 간호를 받고 싶다는 본인의 간곡한 소망만 아니었던들 엠마는 그녀를 집으로 보내지 못했을 것이다. 이튿날 엠마가 그녀를 찾아갔을 때, 랜덜스 일에 관한 한 그녀의 운명은 이미 결정 나 있었다. 그녀는 열이 몹시 높았고 목이 심하게 부어 있었다. 고더드 부인은 근심과 애정으로 가득했고, 미스터 페리 이야기가 오갔으며, 해리엇 자신도 그 즐거운 약속에서 자기를 빼놓는 권위에 맞서기에는 너무 아프고 기운이 없었다 — 비록 그 상실을 이야기하면서 눈물을 한참 쏟지 않을 수 없었지만.\n\n엠마는 고더드 부인이 자리를 비울 수밖에 없는 동안 해리엇을 돌봐 주려고 되도록 오래 곁에 앉아 있었고, 그녀의 상태를 알게 되면 미스터 엘턴이 얼마나 풀이 죽을지를 그려 보여 주며 기운을 북돋아 주었다. 그러고는 마침내, 미스터 엘턴이 더없이 위안 없는 방문을 하게 되리라는, 그리고 모두가 그녀를 몹시 그리워하리라는 달콤한 기대에 기대게 하여, 그녀를 그럭저럭 편안한 상태로 두고 떠났다. 고더드 부인의 집 문에서 몇 걸음 채 나아가기도 전에, 그녀는 다름 아닌 미스터 엘턴과 마주쳤다. 그는 분명 그 문을 향해 오고 있던 차였다. 두 사람이 환자에 대한 이야기를 나누며 천천히 함께 걷는 동안 — 그는 그녀가 꽤 심하게 앓는다는 소문을 듣고, 하트필드에 그 소식을 전할 수 있도록 안부를 물으러 가던 길이었다 — 미스터 존 나이틀리가 두 큰아들을 데리고 돈웰에서의 일과 같은 방문을 마치고 돌아오다 그들을 따라잡았다. 시골을 한바탕 뛰어다닌 덕에 두 아이의 건강하고 발그레한 얼굴은 그 효험을 고스란히 드러내고 있었고, 부랴부랴 집으로 향하는 양고기 구이며 쌀 푸딩을 단숨에 해치우리라는 것을 예고하는 듯했다. 그들은 한 무리가 되어 함께 나아갔다. 엠마는 마침 자기 벗이 앓는 병의 성질을 설명하고 있었다 — \"목이 몹시 부었고, 온몸에 열이 대단하며, 맥은 빠르고 약하고,\" 등등. \"그리고 해리엇이 목이 심하게 붓는 병에 잘 걸리는 체질이라 여러 번 자기를 놀라게 했다는 것을 고더드 부인에게서 듣고 마음이 안 좋았다\"는 것이었다. 이 대목에서 미스터 엘턴은 더없이 놀란 얼굴로 외쳤다.\n\n\"목이 부었다고요! 옮는 병이 아니어야 할 텐데요. 부패성 전염병 같은 게 아니어야 할 텐데요. 페리가 진찰했습니까? 정말이지 벗뿐 아니라 본인 몸도 챙기셔야 합니다. 부디 조금이라도 위험을 무릅쓰지 마시기를 간청드립니다. 왜 페리가 진찰하지 않습니까?\"\n\n사실 조금도 겁이 나지 않았던 엠마는, 고더드 부인의 경험과 보살핌을 들어 안심시킴으로써 이 지나친 염려를 가라앉혔다. 그러나 여전히 어느 정도의 불안은 남아 있을 수밖에 없었고, 그것을 그녀는 굳이 따져서 없애 주고 싶지 않았으며 — 차라리 부추기고 거들어 주고 싶었으므로 — 조금 뒤에 마치 전혀 다른 화제인 양 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"날이 어찌나 추운지, 정말이지 추워요 — 보기에도 느낌에도 꼭 눈이 올 것만 같으니, 다른 곳에 가는 거라거나 다른 일행과 가는 거라면 저는 정말 오늘 외출하지 않으려고 애쓸 거예요 — 아버지께도 무리하지 마시라고 말리고요. 하지만 아버지께서 이미 마음을 정하셨고, 정작 본인은 추위를 느끼지도 않으시는 것 같으니, 끼어들고 싶지가 않네요. 그랬다간 미스터 웨스턴 내외분께 얼마나 큰 실망을 안겨 드릴지 잘 아니까요. 하지만 정말이지, 미스터 엘턴, 당신 같은 경우라면 저는 분명히 사양하겠어요. 제가 보기에 벌써 목이 조금 잠기신 것 같은데, 내일 어떤 목소리가 필요하고 또 얼마나 고단할지를 생각하면, 오늘 밤은 집에 머물며 몸을 돌보시는 게 그저 평범한 분별이 아닐까 싶어요.\"\n\n미스터 엘턴은 무어라 대답해야 좋을지 잘 모르겠다는 얼굴이었다. 실제로도 꼭 그러했다. 이토록 어여쁜 숙녀의 친절한 보살핌에 무척 흐뭇하고, 그녀의 어떤 조언도 거스르고 싶지 않았지만, 그렇다고 그 방문을 포기할 마음은 정말이지 조금도 없었던 것이다. 그러나 엠마는 자기가 미리 품어 둔 생각과 견해에 너무 골똘하고 분주한 나머지, 그를 공정하게 듣거나 또렷한 눈으로 보지 못했으므로, \"춥군요, 분명히 추워요\" 하고 우물거리는 그의 대답에 아주 흡족해하며, 그를 랜덜스에서 빼냈고 그가 저녁 내내 시간마다 해리엇의 안부를 물으러 사람을 보낼 여유를 확보해 주었다는 데 기뻐하며 걸음을 계속했다.\n\n\"아주 잘 생각하셨어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"미스터 웨스턴 내외분께는 우리가 대신 사과를 전해 드릴게요.\"\n\n그러나 그렇게 말하기가 무섭게, 그녀는 자기 형부가 정중하게도, 날씨만이 미스터 엘턴의 유일한 거리낌이라면 자기 마차에 한 자리를 내주겠다고 제안하는 것을, 그리고 미스터 엘턴이 실제로 흔쾌한 만족을 드러내며 그 제안을 덥석 받아들이는 것을 보게 되었다. 일은 그렇게 결정 나 버렸다. 미스터 엘턴은 가게 되었고, 그 넓적하고 잘생긴 얼굴이 이 순간만큼 기쁨을 드러낸 적은 일찍이 없었으며, 다음 순간 그녀를 바라볼 때만큼 그 미소가 짙고 그 눈이 의기양양했던 적도 없었다.\n\n\"이런,\" 그녀는 속으로 생각했다. \"참 별일도 다 있네! 애써 그렇게 잘 빼내 줬더니, 도리어 사람들 틈에 끼겠다고 골라잡고는 아픈 해리엇을 두고 가다니! 정말이지 별일도 다 있어! 하긴 많은 남자들에게는, 특히 독신 남자들에게는 그런 성향이 — 밖에서 만찬을 드는 일에 대한 그런 열정이 있는가 봐. 만찬 약속이라는 게 그들의 즐거움, 그들의 일과, 그들의 체면, 거의 그들의 의무 가운데서도 워낙 높은 자리를 차지하는지라, 그 앞에서는 무엇이든 양보하게 되는 거지 — 미스터 엘턴도 분명 그런 경우일 거야. 두말할 것 없이 더없이 귀하고 상냥하고 호감 가는 청년이고, 해리엇을 무척 사랑하지만 — 그래도 초대를 거절하지는 못하는 거고, 청을 받으면 어디든 가서 만찬을 들어야 하는 거지. 사랑이란 참 이상한 것이로구나! 해리엇의 영민한 재치는 알아보면서도, 정작 그녀를 위해서는 혼자 식사하는 일조차 못 하니 말이야.\"\n\n곧 미스터 엘턴이 그들 곁을 떠났는데, 헤어질 무렵 해리엇을 입에 올리던 그의 태도에 적지 않은 진심이 담겨 있었음을 그녀는 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 그가 어여쁜 벗의 소식을 알아보러 고더드 부인의 집에 들르겠노라고 — 그녀를 다시 만나는 행복을 준비하기 직전에 가장 마지막으로 할 일로 그러겠노라고, 그때는 더 나은 소식을 전할 수 있기를 바란다고 — 그녀에게 다짐할 때의 그 목소리의 어조에 말이다. 그러고서 그는 한숨을 쉬고 미소를 지으며 물러갔는데, 그 모양새가 호감의 저울추를 그에게 한껏 기울게 했다.\n\n두 사람 사이에 몇 분간 완전한 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 존 나이틀리가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"내 평생 미스터 엘턴만큼 호감을 사려고 애쓰는 사람은 본 적이 없습니다. 숙녀들이 얽힌 자리에서는 그게 아예 노역 수준이지요. 남자들끼리 있을 때는 사리에 밝고 꾸밈도 없는데, 비위를 맞춰야 할 숙녀가 있으면 얼굴의 근육 하나하나가 다 움직인다니까요.\"\n\n\"미스터 엘턴의 태도가 완벽하지는 않죠.\" 엠마가 대답했다. \"하지만 호감을 사려는 마음이 있는 데서는, 누구든 많은 것을 너그러이 보아 넘겨야 마땅하고, 또 실제로 보아 넘기게 되는 법이에요. 어떤 사람이 그저 그만한 재간으로 최선을 다한다면, 무심한 우월함보다는 나은 평가를 받게 마련이죠. 미스터 엘턴에게는 누구든 높이 사지 않을 수 없는 더없이 온화한 성품과 선의가 있어요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 미스터 존 나이틀리가 이윽고 짐짓 능청스럽게 말했다. \"처제한테 선의가 무척 넘쳐 보이긴 합디다.\"\n\n\"저요!\" 그녀가 놀란 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다. \"지금 저를 미스터 엘턴의 목표로 상상하고 계신 거예요?\"\n\n\"그런 상상이 머리를 스친 건 사실입니다, 엠마. 그리고 지금껏 처제 머릿속엔 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없다면, 이참에 한번 고려해 보는 것도 좋을 거예요.\"\n\n\"미스터 엘턴이 저를 사랑한다니! 별 생각을 다 하시네요!\"\n\n\"그렇다고 단정하는 건 아닙니다. 다만 그런지 아닌지를 잘 헤아려 보고, 거기에 맞게 처신하는 게 좋을 거란 말이지요. 내가 보기엔 처제가 그 사람한테 하는 태도가 그를 부추기는 데가 있어요. 벗으로서 하는 말입니다, 엠마. 주위를 잘 살펴서, 처제가 무얼 하고 있고 무얼 하려는지 똑똑히 알아 두는 게 좋을 거예요.\"\n\n\"고맙긴 한데요, 정말이지 형부가 완전히 잘못 짚으셨어요. 미스터 엘턴과 저는 아주 좋은 친구일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니에요.\" 그러고서 그녀는, 사정을 어설피 아는 데서 흔히 빚어지는 헛다리짚음을, 판단력에 자부심이 높은 사람들이 언제나 빠지고 마는 오류를 곰곰 따져 보며 혼자 즐거워하면서 걸음을 계속했다. 그리고 자기를 눈멀고 무지하여 충고가 필요한 사람으로 여기는 형부에게 그리 흡족하지 않았다. 그는 더는 말하지 않았다.\n\n미스터 우드하우스는 이 방문에 어찌나 단단히 마음을 굳혀 두었던지, 점점 심해지는 추위에도 거기서 물러설 생각이 도무지 없는 듯했고, 마침내 더없이 정확한 시각에 큰딸과 함께 자기 마차로 길을 나섰다. 날씨에 대한 의식도 다른 두 사람보다 겉으로는 덜해 보였다. 자기가 외출한다는 사실의 경이로움과, 랜덜스에 가져다줄 기쁨에 너무 들떠 추운 줄도 몰랐고, 또 워낙 단단히 싸맨 터라 추위를 느끼지도 못했던 것이다. 그러나 추위는 매서웠다. 두 번째 마차가 움직일 무렵에는 눈송이 몇 점이 길을 찾아 내려오고 있었고, 하늘은 잔뜩 무겁게 내려앉아, 공기가 조금만 누그러지면 순식간에 온 세상을 새하얗게 만들 태세였다.\n\n엠마는 곧 자기 동행이 그리 흐뭇한 기분이 아님을 알아챘다. 이런 날씨에 채비를 하고 밖으로 나서야 한다는 것은, 게다가 만찬 뒤에는 아이들을 두고 와야 한다는 희생까지 더해져, 미스터 존 나이틀리가 결코 좋아할 수 없는 고역이요, 적어도 못마땅한 일이었다. 그는 이 방문에서 그 대가를 치를 만한 가치가 있을 일이라곤 조금도 기대하지 않았다. 그리하여 목사관까지 가는 마차 길 내내 그는 자기 불만을 늘어놓는 데 시간을 다 썼다.\n\n\"사람이,\" 그가 말했다. \"남더러 제 집 난롯가를 떠나서 이런 날을 무릅쓰고 자기를 보러 오라고 청할 때는, 자기 자신을 어지간히도 대단하게 여기는 게 분명합니다. 자기가 더없이 호감 가는 사람이라고 생각하는 거지요. 나라면 그런 짓은 못 합니다. 이런 어처구니없는 일이 또 어디 있어요 — 바로 지금 이 순간에도 눈이 오고 있잖습니까! 사람들이 집에서 편히 지내도록 두지 않는 어리석음 — 그리고 편히 집에 있을 수 있는데도 그러지 못하는 사람들의 어리석음이라니! 만약 우리가 어떤 의무나 볼일 때문에 이런 저녁에 나가야만 한다면, 우리는 그걸 얼마나 큰 고역으로 여기겠습니까. 그런데 보세요, 평소보다 도리어 얇은 옷을 걸친 채로, 자청해서, 변명거리도 없이, 자연의 목소리를 거스르며 길을 나서고 있지 않습니까. 자연은 사람의 눈과 마음에 비치는 온갖 것을 통해, 제 집에 머물고 거둘 수 있는 모든 것을 지붕 아래 거두라고 일러 주는데 말입니다. 그런데 우리는 이렇게 길을 나서서, 남의 집에서 따분한 다섯 시간을 보내려 하고 있어요. 어제 주고받은 말, 또 내일도 주고받을 말 말고는 할 말도 들을 말도 없는 그런 자리를요. 음울한 날씨에 갔다가, 아마도 더 고약한 날씨에 돌아오겠지요 — 말 네 마리와 하인 넷을 끌어내는 게, 한가하고 추위에 떠는 다섯 사람을, 집에 있었으면 누렸을 것보다 더 추운 방과 더 못한 자리로 실어 나르는 일밖에 더 됩니까.\"\n\n엠마는, 그가 으레 받아 왔을 흐뭇한 맞장구를 — 늘 그의 길동무가 베풀어 주었을 \"정말 맞아요, 여보\" 같은 말을 — 흉내 낼 마음이 도무지 들지 않았다. 그러나 아예 아무 대답도 하지 않을 만큼의 결단력은 있었다. 맞장구를 칠 수도 없었고, 그렇다고 다투는 것은 무서웠으니, 그녀의 영웅적 기개는 침묵에까지밖에 미치지 못했다. 그녀는 그가 떠들도록 내버려 두고, 유리창을 매만지고 몸을 단단히 싸맸을 뿐, 입술 한 번 떼지 않았다.\n\n마차가 닿고, 방향을 틀고, 발판이 내려지자, 말쑥하고 검은 차림에 미소 띤 미스터 엘턴이 곧장 그들 앞에 와 있었다. 엠마는 화제가 좀 바뀌겠구나 싶어 반가웠다. 미스터 엘턴은 온통 정중함과 쾌활함 그 자체였다. 어찌나 쾌활하게 예를 차리던지, 그녀는 그가 자기에게 전해진 것과는 다른 해리엇의 소식을 들은 게 아닐까 생각하기 시작했다. 그녀는 옷을 갈아입는 동안 사람을 보내 물었는데, 돌아온 대답은 \"별 차도 없음 — 나아지지 않음\"이었던 것이다.\n\n\"제가 고더드 부인 댁에서 받은 소식은,\" 그녀가 이윽고 말했다. \"바라던 것만큼 좋진 않았어요 — '나아지지 않았다'는 게 제가 받은 대답이었죠.\"\n\n그의 얼굴은 즉시 길쭉해졌고, 대답하는 그의 목소리는 진정 어린 목소리였다.\n\n\"오! 아니 — 그 소식을 들으니 마음이 아픕니다 — 마침 말씀드리려던 참이었어요. 제가 옷을 갈아입으러 돌아가기 직전 가장 마지막 일로 고더드 부인 댁 문에 들렀더니, 미스 스미스가 나아지지 않았다고, 도무지 차도가 없을뿐더러 오히려 더 나빠졌다고 하더군요. 정말이지 슬프고 걱정스러워요 — 아침에 들었다시피 그렇게 좋은 강심제를 들었으니 분명 좀 나아졌으리라고 혼자 기대하고 있었거든요.\"\n\n엠마는 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"제 방문이 그 병의 신경 쪽에는 도움이 됐기를 바라요. 하지만 저라 한들 부은 목을 마법으로 낫게 해 줄 수는 없죠. 정말 지독한 감기예요. 미스터 페리가 다녀갔어요, 들으셨겠지만.\"\n\n\"네 — 그런 줄 알았습니다 — 그러니까 — 사실은 못 들었지만 —\"\n\n\"미스터 페리는 해리엇의 그런 증상에 익숙하니까, 내일 아침이면 우리 둘 다 더 마음 놓을 소식을 받게 되길 바라요. 하지만 불안하지 않을 수가 없네요. 오늘 우리 모임에 이렇게 안타까운 빈자리라니!\"\n\n\"끔찍하지요! 정말이지 꼭 그렇습니다. 매 순간 그녀가 아쉬울 거예요.\"\n\n여기까지는 아주 온당했다. 거기 곁들인 한숨도 진정 가상한 것이었다. 그러나 그것은 좀 더 오래 갔어야 했다. 겨우 삼십 초 뒤에 그가 다른 이야기를, 그것도 더없이 활기차고 흥겨운 목소리로 꺼내기 시작했을 때, 엠마는 어지간히 당황하고 말았다.\n\n\"마차에 양가죽을 쓰다니,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 얼마나 기막힌 고안입니까. 얼마나 편안하게 해 주는지요. 이렇게 대비를 해 두면 추위라곤 느낄 수가 없어요. 정말이지 요즘의 고안들은 신사의 마차를 더없이 완벽하게 만들어 놓았어요. 날씨로부터 어찌나 단단히 막아 주고 지켜 주는지, 허락 없이는 바람 한 줄기도 비집고 들어올 수가 없습니다. 날씨가 아예 아무 상관도 없어지는 거지요. 무척 추운 오후입니다만 — 이 마차 안에서는 그런 걸 도통 모르고 있잖아요. 허! 눈이 좀 오는군요.\"\n\n\"네.\" 존 나이틀리가 말했다. \"꽤 많이 올 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"성탄절 날씨답군요.\" 미스터 엘턴이 말했다. \"딱 제철에 어울리지요. 그리고 어제 시작되어서 오늘 모임을 막아 버리지 않은 게 우리로서는 더없는 행운이라고 여길 만합니다. 어제 시작됐더라면 충분히 그럴 수도 있었지요. 땅에 눈이 많이 쌓였더라면 미스터 우드하우스가 좀처럼 길을 나서려 하지 않으셨을 테니까요. 하지만 이제는 아무 상관 없습니다. 지금이야말로 정녕 정다운 모임의 계절이지요. 성탄절이면 누구나 벗들을 곁으로 불러들이고, 아무리 고약한 날씨라도 대수롭잖게 여기는 법입니다. 저는 언젠가 한 친구 집에서 일주일이나 눈에 갇힌 적이 있었지요. 그보다 더 즐거운 일이 없었습니다. 하룻밤만 묵을 작정으로 갔는데, 꼭 그날로부터 이레째가 되어서야 빠져나올 수 있었어요.\"\n\n미스터 존 나이틀리는 그 즐거움을 도무지 이해하지 못하겠다는 얼굴이었지만, 그저 쌀쌀맞게 한마디만 했다.\n\n\"나는 랜덜스에서 일주일씩 눈에 갇히고 싶은 마음은 없군요.\"\n\n다른 때 같았으면 엠마도 재미있어했겠지만, 지금은 미스터 엘턴의 들뜬 기분에 너무 놀란 나머지 다른 감정을 느낄 겨를이 없었다. 즐거운 모임에 대한 기대 속에서 해리엇은 까맣게 잊힌 듯했다.\n\n\"틀림없이 훌륭한 난롯불이 있을 거고,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"무엇 하나 더없이 안락할 겁니다. 매력적인 분들이지요, 미스터 웨스턴 내외분은. 웨스턴 부인은 정말이지 칭찬으로는 모자란 분이고, 그분은 또 누구나 귀히 여길 만한 그런 분이에요. 그렇게 손님 대접에 후하고, 또 그렇게 사람들과 어울리기를 좋아하시니까요. 작은 모임이 되겠지만, 작은 모임이 엄선된 사람들로 이루어졌을 때는 아마 어떤 모임보다도 더 즐거운 법이지요. 미스터 웨스턴의 식당은 열 명을 넘기면 편안하게 들이지 못하는데, 저로서는 이런 경우에 둘이 넘치느니 차라리 둘이 모자라는 편이 낫습니다. 제 생각에 동의하시겠지요.\" (엠마를 향해 부드러운 태도로 돌아서며) \"분명 제 편을 들어 주시리라 믿어요. 미스터 나이틀리는 런던의 큰 모임들에 익숙하신지라 우리 마음을 썩 잘 이해하지 못하실지도 모르지만요.\"\n\n\"저는 런던의 큰 모임 같은 건 전혀 모릅니다, 미스터 엘턴 — 누구와도 만찬을 들지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"정말입니까!\" (놀라움과 동정이 섞인 어조로) \"법조계 일이 그렇게나 큰 굴레인 줄은 몰랐습니다. 그래도 미스터 나이틀리, 이 모든 수고에 보답을 받으실 때가 분명 올 겁니다. 일은 적고 즐거움은 많아질 날이 말이지요.\"\n\n\"제 첫 번째 즐거움은,\" 마차가 진입로 문을 지날 때 존 나이틀리가 대답했다. \"하트필드에 무사히 다시 돌아와 있는 걸 보는 일이 될 겁니다.\"\n\n# 제1권 14장\n\n웨스턴 부인의 응접실로 들어서면서 두 신사는 각기 얼굴빛을 조금씩 고쳐야 했다 — 미스터 엘턴은 들뜬 표정을 가라앉혀야 했고, 미스터 존 나이틀리는 언짢은 기색을 흩어 버려야 했다. 미스터 엘턴은 웃음을 줄여야 했고, 미스터 존 나이틀리는 웃음을 늘려야 그 자리에 어울릴 수 있었다. 엠마만이 천성이 이끄는 대로 굴 수 있었고, 자신이 행복한 그대로 행복해 보일 수 있었다. 그녀에게 웨스턴 부부와 함께 있는 것은 진정한 즐거움이었다. 미스터 웨스턴은 더없이 좋아하는 사람이었고, 그의 아내만큼 거리낌 없이 이야기를 나눌 수 있는 상대는 이 세상 어디에도 없었다. 자신과 아버지의 자잘한 일들, 살림의 짜임새, 고민거리며 즐거움을 — 늘 귀 기울여 들어 주고 이해해 주며, 언제나 흥미로워하고 언제나 알아들어 주리라는 확신을 가지고 털어놓을 수 있는 사람은 그녀 말고는 없었다. 하트필드의 어떤 이야기를 꺼내든 웨스턴 부인이 진심으로 마음 써 주지 않는 것이 없었으니, 사사로운 삶의 나날의 행복이 달려 있는 그 온갖 자잘한 일들을 반 시간 동안 끊김 없이 주고받는 것이야말로 두 사람 모두에게 으뜸가는 기쁨 가운데 하나였다.\n\n이는 어쩌면 하루 종일의 방문으로도 누리지 못할 즐거움이었고, 지금 이 반 시간에는 분명 허락되지 않을 즐거움이었다. 그러나 웨스턴 부인을 보는 것만으로도, 그 미소와 손길과 목소리만으로도 엠마는 흐뭇했고, 미스터 엘턴의 별난 행동이나 그 밖의 거슬리는 일은 되도록 적게 생각하며 즐길 수 있는 것을 한껏 즐기리라 마음먹었다.\n\n해리엇이 감기에 걸렸다는 불운에 대해서는 그녀가 도착하기 전에 이미 꽤 충분히 다루어진 뒤였다. 미스터 우드하우스는 진작 자리를 잡고 앉아 그 내력을 다 풀어 놓았고, 그뿐 아니라 자신과 이저벨라가 오게 된 사연, 엠마가 뒤따라오기로 한 일까지 죄다 늘어놓았으며, 제임스가 건너와 제 딸을 보게 된다는 사실에 흐뭇해하던 이야기를 막 끝맺던 참에 다른 사람들이 들어섰다. 그동안 거의 온통 그를 살피는 일에만 매달려 있던 웨스턴 부인은 그제야 몸을 돌려 사랑하는 엠마를 맞이할 수 있었다.\n\n한동안 미스터 엘턴을 잊어 보자던 엠마의 계획은, 모두가 자리를 잡고 나서 보니 그가 바로 옆에 앉아 있다는 것을 알게 되어 적잖이 언짢았다. 그가 옆자리에 앉아 있을 뿐 아니라, 행복한 얼굴을 끊임없이 그녀의 눈앞에 들이밀고, 무슨 일이 있을 때마다 살뜰하게 말을 걸어 오니, 해리엇을 향한 그 기이한 무심함을 머릿속에서 몰아내기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니었다. 그를 잊기는커녕, 그의 행동거지가 어찌나 그러한지 그녀는 속으로 이런 생각이 드는 것을 어쩌지 못했다. \"정말로 형부가 짐작한 그대로일까? 이 사람이 해리엇에게 두었던 마음을 나에게로 옮기기 시작했단 말인가? — 터무니없고 견딜 수 없는 일이야!\" 그런데도 그는 그녀가 조금이라도 추울세라 안달하고, 그녀의 아버지에 대해 그토록 관심을 보이며, 웨스턴 부인에게 그토록 기뻐하더니, 급기야는 그녀의 그림을 어찌나 열을 올려 — 그러면서도 어찌나 아는 것 없이 — 칭찬하기 시작하던지, 영락없이 구애하려 드는 사람 같아서, 그녀로서는 예의를 지키느라 적잖이 애를 써야 했다. 자기 체면을 위해서라도 무례하게 굴 수는 없었고, 해리엇을 위해서는 그래도 모든 일이 잘 풀리리라는 바람에서 도리어 분명하게 정중히 굴기까지 했다. 그러나 그것은 안간힘이었다. 더구나 미스터 엘턴의 부질없는 수작이 가장 견디기 힘들게 이어지던 바로 그 순간에, 다른 사람들 사이에서 그녀가 유독 귀 기울이고 싶은 무언가가 오가고 있었으니 말이다. 미스터 웨스턴이 자기 아들에 대한 무슨 소식을 전하고 있다는 것은 알아들을 만큼은 들었다. \"우리 아들\"이며 \"프랭크\"며 \"우리 아들\"이라는 말이 몇 번이고 되풀이되는 것을 들었고, 띄엄띄엄 들린 다른 몇 마디 토막으로 미루어, 아들이 머지않아 찾아오리라는 것을 알리는 중이라고 강하게 짐작했다. 그러나 미스터 엘턴을 잠재우기도 전에 그 화제는 어찌나 말끔히 지나가 버렸는지, 그녀가 다시 끄집어내려고 무슨 질문을 던진다면 어색하기 짝이 없을 노릇이었다.\n\n그런데 마침 이런 일이 있었다. 결코 결혼하지 않으리라는 엠마의 결심에도 불구하고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠이라는 이름에는, 그를 떠올린다는 것 자체에는 늘 그녀의 마음을 끄는 무언가가 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 — 특히 그의 아버지가 미스 테일러와 결혼한 뒤로 — 자주 이런 생각을 하곤 했다. 만일 자신이 결혼이란 걸 하게 된다면, 나이며 성품이며 처지로 보아 그야말로 자기에게 딱 맞는 사람이 바로 그 사람이라고. 두 집안 사이의 이 인연으로 보아, 그는 어쩐지 온전히 그녀에게 속한 사람 같았다. 두 사람을 아는 이라면 누구나 이 혼사를 떠올릴 수밖에 없으리라고 그녀는 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 웨스턴 부부가 그 생각을 하고 있다는 것은 아주 강하게 확신하던 바였다. 그러면서도 그에게든 다른 누구에게든 이끌려, 지금의 처지 — 바꿀 수 있는 그 어떤 처지보다 좋은 것이 그득하다고 믿는 이 처지 — 를 내놓을 마음은 없으면서도, 그녀는 그를 만나 보고 싶은 큰 호기심을 품었고, 그를 유쾌한 사람이라고 여기기로, 또 그에게 어느 정도는 사랑받기로 단단히 작정해 두었으며, 두 사람이 벗들의 상상 속에서 짝지어진다는 생각에 일종의 즐거움마저 느끼고 있었다.\n\n이런 심사인지라 미스터 엘턴의 친절은 끔찍하게 때를 잘못 만난 셈이었다. 그래도 속으로는 몹시 짜증을 내면서 겉으로는 더없이 정중해 보일 수 있다는 위안이 있었고, 또 이 방문이 끝나기 전에는 흉금을 터놓는 미스터 웨스턴에게서 같은 소식을, 적어도 그 알맹이를 반드시 다시 듣게 되리라는 생각도 위안이 되었다. 과연 그렇게 되었다. 미스터 엘턴에게서 다행히 풀려나 식탁에서 미스터 웨스턴 곁에 앉게 되자, 그는 손님 접대의 분주함에서 처음으로 한숨 돌릴 틈이 나자마자, 양 다리 고기에서 처음으로 손을 놓을 여유가 생기자마자 그녀에게 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"꼭 알맞은 수가 되려면 딱 두 사람만 더 있으면 됩니다. 이 자리에 두 사람을 더 보았으면 싶군요 — 아가씨의 어여쁜 친구 미스 스미스와 우리 아들 말입니다 — 그러면 더할 나위 없이 완벽하다고 하겠지요. 우리가 프랭크를 기다리고 있다는 걸 응접실에서 다른 분들께 이야기할 때 아가씨는 못 들었을 겁니다. 오늘 아침에 그 애한테서 편지가 왔는데, 보름 안으로 우리한테 오겠다는군요.\"\n\n엠마는 더없이 마땅한 정도의 기쁨을 담아 말했고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠과 미스 스미스가 있어야 이 모임이 완벽해진다는 그의 말에 흔쾌히 동의했다.\n\n\"그 애는 9월부터 줄곧 우리한테 오고 싶어 했지요.\" 미스터 웨스턴이 말을 이었다. \"편지마다 그 이야기로 가득했어요. 하지만 제 시간을 제 마음대로 쓰지 못하는 처지라서요. 비위를 맞춰야만 하는 사람들이 있는데, 그 사람들이라는 게 (우리끼리 이야기지만) 더러는 적잖은 희생을 치러야만 비위가 맞춰지거든요. 하지만 이번에는 1월 둘째 주쯤이면 틀림없이 여기서 그 애를 보게 되리라 봅니다.\"\n\n\"얼마나 큰 기쁨이시겠어요! 게다가 웨스턴 부인도 그분과 알고 지내기를 그토록 고대하고 계시니, 거의 미스터 웨스턴만큼이나 행복하시겠네요.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 그럴 테지요. 다만 또 한 번 미뤄지지 않을까 걱정하고 있어서 말입니다. 그 사람은 나만큼 그 애가 올 거라고 믿질 못해요. 하지만 그 사람은 나만큼 그쪽 사람들을 잘 알지 못하니까요. 사정인즉슨 이렇습니다 — (그렇지만 이건 어디까지나 우리끼리 이야기입니다. 저 방에서는 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았어요. 어느 집안에나 비밀은 있는 법이니까요) — 사정인즉, 1월에 친구 한 무리가 엔스컴으로 방문 초대를 받았는데, 프랭크가 오느냐 마느냐가 그 사람들의 방문이 미뤄지느냐에 달려 있다는 겁니다. 그쪽이 미뤄지지 않으면 그 애는 꼼짝도 못 해요. 하지만 나는 미뤄질 줄로 압니다. 엔스컴의 어떤 지체 높으신 부인이 유독 질색하는 집안이거든요. 그래서 두세 해에 한 번쯤은 초대해야 한다고들 여기면서도, 막상 때가 되면 늘 미뤄지고 말지요. 그 결말에 대해서는 나는 손톱만큼도 의심하지 않아요. 1월 중순 전에 여기서 프랭크를 보리라는 것은, 내가 지금 여기 있다는 것만큼이나 확실하다고 봅니다. 하지만 저기 저 댁의 좋은 친구분은\" — 그는 식탁 윗자리 쪽으로 고개를 끄덕여 보였다 — \"본인이 워낙 변덕이 없는 사람인 데다 하트필드에서도 그런 일을 거의 겪어 본 적이 없으니, 변덕이라는 게 어떤 결과를 빚는지를 나처럼 오래 겪어 온 사람만큼 가늠하지 못하는 거지요.\"\n\n\"이 일에 의심할 구석이 있다니 안타깝네요.\" 엠마가 대답했다. \"하지만 저는 미스터 웨스턴 편을 들고 싶어요. 그분이 올 거라고 생각하신다면, 저도 그렇게 생각하겠어요. 엔스컴을 잘 아시는 분이니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요 — 그걸 안다고 할 만한 자격은 좀 있는 셈입니다. 정작 그곳에는 평생 한 번도 가 본 적이 없지만요. 참 별난 여자예요! 그렇지만 나는 프랭크를 봐서라도 그 사람을 함부로 헐뜯지 않으려 합니다. 그 사람이 그 애를 무척 아끼는 것만은 정말이라고 믿거든요. 예전엔 그 사람이 자기 자신 말고는 누구도 아낄 줄 모르는 사람이라고 여겼는데, 그 애한테만은 늘 다정했어요 (그 사람 나름의 방식으로 말이지요 — 자잘한 변덕과 까탈을 눈감아 주고, 무엇이든 제 뜻대로 되기를 바라는 걸 참아 준다면요). 그리고 내 생각에, 그런 애정을 불러일으킨다는 건 그 애한테 적잖이 자랑스러운 일입니다. 다른 사람한테는 못할 말이지만, 그 사람은 사람들 전반에게는 돌멩이만큼도 정이 없고, 성미는 악마 같으니 말입니다.\"\n\n엠마는 이 화제가 어찌나 마음에 들었던지, 응접실로 자리를 옮긴 지 얼마 되지 않아 웨스턴 부인에게 그 이야기를 꺼냈다 — 기쁘시겠다고 하면서도, 첫 만남이라는 게 꽤나 마음 졸이는 일임은 안다고 덧붙였다. 웨스턴 부인은 거기에 동의했지만, 이렇게 보탰다. 말이 나온 그 시기에 첫 만남의 조마조마함을 겪게 되는 것이 차라리 확실하다면 더없이 기쁘겠다고. \"그 애가 올 거라고 확신할 수가 없어서요. 나는 미스터 웨스턴만큼 낙관할 수가 없네요. 결국 모두 흐지부지되고 말까 봐 정말 걱정이에요. 미스터 웨스턴이 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 그쪽 사정을 그대로 말씀드렸겠지요?\"\n\n\"네 — 모든 게 그저 처칠 부인의 언짢은 기분에 달려 있는 듯하던데, 제 생각엔 그게 세상에서 가장 확실한 것 같던걸요.\"\n\n\"엠마!\" 웨스턴 부인이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"변덕에 무슨 확실함이 있겠어요?\" 그러고는 그때까지 듣고 있지 않던 이저벨라에게로 몸을 돌렸다 — \"이걸 아셔야 해요, 미시즈 나이틀리. 내 생각에 우리는 그 애 아버지가 여기는 것처럼 미스터 프랭크 처칠을 보게 되리라고 결코 그렇게 확신할 수가 없답니다. 모든 게 오로지 그 애 외숙모의 기분과 흥에 달려 있어요. 한마디로 그분 성미에 달린 거지요. 부인께는 — 내 두 딸에게는 — 사실대로 털어놓아도 되겠지요. 처칠 부인이 엔스컴을 다스리는데, 성미가 아주 별난 여자예요. 그러니 그 애가 지금 오느냐 마느냐는 그분이 그 애를 기꺼이 내어 주려는지에 달려 있답니다.\"\n\n\"아, 처칠 부인이요. 처칠 부인이야 누구나 알지요.\" 이저벨라가 대답했다. \"그리고 저는 정말이지 그 가엾은 청년을 생각할 때마다 한없는 측은함이 들어요. 성미 고약한 사람과 끊임없이 함께 산다는 건 끔찍한 일임에 틀림없어요. 우리야 다행히 그런 걸 한 번도 겪어 보지 못했지만, 그건 분명 비참한 삶일 거예요. 그래도 그분에게 아이가 없었다는 게 얼마나 다행인지요! 가엾은 어린것들, 그분이 얼마나 불행하게 만들었겠어요!\"\n\n엠마는 웨스턴 부인과 단둘이 있었더라면 좋았을 것을, 하고 생각했다. 그랬더라면 더 많은 이야기를 들었을 텐데. 웨스턴 부인은 이저벨라에게는 차마 못 할 만큼의 거리낌 없는 정도로 그녀에게 이야기해 주었을 테고, 처칠 집안에 관한 일이라면 무엇 하나 그녀에게 감추려 들지 않으리라고 그녀는 정말로 믿었다 — 그 청년에 관한 속내, 그러니까 그녀 자신의 상상이 이미 본능처럼 일러 준 그 생각만은 빼고 말이다. 그러나 지금으로서는 더 할 이야기가 없었다. 미스터 우드하우스가 이내 그들을 따라 응접실로 들어왔던 것이다. 식사 뒤에 오래 앉아 있는 것은 그가 견딜 수 없는 속박이었다. 그에게는 포도주도 대화도 아무 의미가 없었으니, 늘 마음 편한 사람들 곁으로 기꺼이 옮겨 온 것이다.\n\n그가 이저벨라와 이야기를 나누는 동안, 그래도 엠마는 이렇게 말할 기회를 잡았다.\n\n\"그러니까 아드님의 이번 방문을 결코 확실하다고는 여기지 않으시는군요. 안타까워요. 그 인사라는 게 언제 이루어지든 마음 편치 않은 일일 테니, 빨리 끝날수록 좋을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그래요. 게다가 미뤄질 때마다 다른 일도 또 미뤄지지 않을까 더 조마조마해지지요. 설령 그 집안, 그러니까 브레이스웨이트 집안의 방문이 미뤄진다 해도, 우리를 실망시킬 또 다른 핑계가 어디선가 나올까 봐 여전히 걱정이에요. 그 애 쪽에 무슨 내키지 않는 마음이 있다고는 차마 상상하기도 싫지만, 처칠 집안 쪽에는 그 애를 제 사람으로만 붙들어 두려는 큰 바람이 있는 게 분명해요. 시샘하는 거지요. 그 애가 제 아버지를 위하는 마음까지도 시샘한다니까요. 한마디로 나는 그 애가 오리라고 도무지 믿을 수가 없어서, 미스터 웨스턴이 좀 덜 낙관하셨으면 싶어요.\"\n\n\"와야지요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"단 이틀만 머물 수 있다 해도 와야 마땅해요. 청년이 그 정도 일조차 제 뜻대로 못 한다니, 도무지 상상하기 어려운 일인걸요. 젊은 여자라면 나쁜 사람들 손에 떨어질 경우 시달림을 당하고, 함께 있고 싶은 사람들과 멀찍이 떨어져 지내야 할 수도 있지요. 하지만 젊은 남자가, 제가 원한다면 아버지와 일주일도 함께 보내지 못할 만큼 그런 속박 아래 놓인다는 건 도무지 이해할 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"그 애가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 단정하려면, 직접 엔스컴에 가 보고 그 집안의 방식을 알아야 해요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 대답했다. \"어쩌면 어느 집안이든 그 한 사람의 행동을 판단할 때는 똑같이 조심해야 할 테지요. 하지만 엔스컴만은, 정말이지 일반적인 잣대로 판단해서는 안 될 거예요. 그 사람이 어찌나 막무가내인지, 모든 게 그 사람 앞에 무릎을 꿇으니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그분이 그 조카를 그토록 아끼시잖아요. 그 조카가 그렇게나 끔찍이 사랑받는 사람이고요. 자, 처칠 부인에 대한 제 생각으로는 이러는 게 가장 자연스러울 텐데요. 그분이 모든 것을 빚진 남편의 편안함을 위해서는 아무런 희생도 치르지 않고, 그 남편에게는 끊임없이 변덕을 부리면서, 정작 아무것도 빚진 게 없는 조카에게는 자주 휘둘린다는 거 말이에요.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 엠마, 그 고운 성미를 가지고 못된 성미를 이해한다고, 거기에 무슨 법칙을 세운다고 우기지 말아요. 그런 건 제멋대로 가게 내버려 둬야 한답니다. 그 애한테 때로 상당한 영향력이 있다는 건 의심하지 않아요. 하지만 그 영향력이 언제 통할지를 그 애가 미리 안다는 건 도무지 불가능한 일일지도 모르지요.\"\n\n엠마는 듣고 있다가, 이윽고 차분하게 말했다. \"그분이 오지 않으면 저는 만족하지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 애가 어떤 일에는 큰 영향을 미칠 수도 있겠지요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그러나 또 어떤 일에는 거의 미치지 못할 거예요. 그리고 그 애의 힘이 미치지 못하는 일들 가운데에는, 다름 아닌 바로 이번 일 — 그 사람들을 떠나 우리를 보러 온다는 이 일이 들어 있을 가능성이 너무도 크답니다.\"\n\n# 제1권 15장\n\n미스터 우드하우스는 곧 차를 들 채비를 마쳤다. 그리고 차를 마시고 나니 이내 집으로 돌아갈 채비도 다 된 셈이었다. 다른 신사들이 나타나기 전까지 시간이 늦었다는 사실에 그가 주의를 돌리지 않도록 막는 것은 세 동무가 할 수 있는 일의 전부였다. 미스터 웨스턴은 수다스럽고 흥겨운 사람이라 어떤 종류의 이른 작별도 좋아하지 않았다. 그러나 마침내 응접실 무리에 인원이 보태졌다. 미스터 엘턴이 무척 기분이 좋은 얼굴로 가장 먼저 걸어 들어온 사람 가운데 하나였다. 웨스턴 부인과 엠마는 소파에 나란히 앉아 있었다. 그는 곧장 두 사람에게 다가오더니, 청하지도 않았는데 둘 사이에 자리를 잡고 앉았다.\n\n엠마 또한 프랭크 처칠을 기대하는 데서 오는 즐거움으로 기분이 좋았던 터라, 그의 최근의 무례함을 기꺼이 잊고 예전처럼 그를 흡족하게 대할 마음이 들었다. 그리고 그가 해리엇을 맨 첫머리 화제로 꺼내자, 더없이 다정한 미소로 귀를 기울일 채비가 되어 있었다.\n\n그는 그녀의 어여쁜 벗을 — 그 어여쁘고, 사랑스럽고, 상냥한 벗을 — 몹시 염려하는 기색을 내비쳤다. \"혹 소식을 들으셨는지요? 랜덜스에 오신 뒤로 그녀에 관해 무어라도 들으신 게 있는지요? 저는 무척 마음이 쓰입니다. 솔직히 말씀드려, 그 병세의 성질이 저를 적잖이 놀라게 했다는 것을 고백하지 않을 수 없군요.\" 이런 투로 그는 한동안 아주 적절하게 말을 이어 갔다. 무슨 대답이 돌아오든 그리 귀담아듣지는 않았으나, 고약한 인후염의 무서움에는 충분히 깨어 있는 사람처럼 굴었다. 그리하여 엠마는 그에게 꽤 너그러운 마음이 되었다.\n\n그러나 마침내 어딘가 비뚤어진 전환이 일어나는 듯했다. 별안간, 그가 해리엇을 두고 고약한 인후염을 걱정하기보다 그녀를 두고 더 두려워하는 것처럼 보이기 시작한 것이다. 그 병에 전염성이 없기를 바라기보다, 그녀가 전염을 피하기를 더 안달하는 듯했다. 그는 더없이 진지하게, 당분간은 다시 그 병실을 찾지 말아 달라고 간청하기 시작했다. 자기가 미스터 페리를 만나 그 의견을 듣기 전까지는 그런 위험을 무릅쓰지 않겠다고 약속해 달라고 그녀에게 청했다. 그녀가 웃어넘기며 화제를 본래의 줄기로 되돌리려 애썼지만, 그녀를 향한 그의 지나친 노심초사를 그칠 길이 없었다. 그녀는 짜증이 났다. 그것은 — 감출 길도 없이 — 영락없이 해리엇이 아니라 그녀 자신에게 연정을 품은 척하는 꼴이었다. 변심이라니, 그것이 진심이라면 더없이 경멸스럽고 가증스러운 일이었다! 그녀는 평정을 지키며 처신하기가 힘들었다. 그는 웨스턴 부인 쪽으로 돌아서서 그 도움을 호소했다. \"부인께서 제 편을 들어 주시지 않겠습니까? 미스 스미스의 병에 전염성이 없다는 것이 확실해지기 전까지는 미스 우드하우스가 고더드 부인 댁에 가지 못하도록, 제 설득에 부인의 설득을 보태 주시지 않겠습니까? 저는 약속을 받지 않고는 마음이 놓이질 않습니다. 부인께서 그 약속을 받아 내는 데 영향력을 좀 보태 주시지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"남들 일에는 이토록 세심하면서,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"정작 자신에게는 이토록 무심하시다니요! 미스 우드하우스는 오늘 제가 집에 머물며 감기를 다스리기를 바라셨으면서, 정작 본인은 곪은 인후염에 걸릴 위험을 피하겠다는 약속은 하려 들지 않으십니다. 이게 공평합니까, 웨스턴 부인? 우리 둘 사이를 판가름해 주십시오. 제가 불평할 권리가 좀 있지 않습니까? 부인의 자상한 지지와 도움을 믿어 의심치 않습니다.\"\n\n엠마는 웨스턴 부인의 놀라는 기색을 보았고, 그 놀라움이 클 수밖에 없으리라고 느꼈다. 말로 보나 태도로 보나, 그녀에 대해 가장 먼저 관심을 기울일 권리를 제 것으로 떠맡는 듯한 수작이었으니 말이다. 정작 그녀 자신은 너무도 화가 나고 모욕을 느낀 나머지 곧장 무어라 똑 부러지게 말할 힘조차 없었다. 그저 눈길 하나를 던지는 것이 고작이었지만, 그것은 그를 제정신으로 돌려놓고도 남으리라 여겨질 만한 눈길이었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 소파를 떠나 언니 곁의 자리로 옮겨 가, 온통 그쪽에만 마음을 쏟았다.\n\n미스터 엘턴이 그 질책을 어떻게 받아들였는지 알아볼 겨를도 없이, 어찌나 빠르게 또 다른 화제가 뒤를 이었는지 모른다. 날씨를 살피고 온 미스터 존 나이틀리가 마침 방으로 들어서더니, 땅이 온통 눈에 덮였고 지금도 거센 바람에 눈발이 휘몰아치며 여전히 펑펑 쏟아지고 있다는 소식으로 좌중을 향해 입을 열었던 것이다. 그러고는 미스터 우드하우스를 향해 이런 말로 끝을 맺었다.\n\n\"이번 겨울 나들이를 아주 패기 넘치게 여시게 됐군요, 어르신. 마부와 말들로서는 눈보라를 헤치고 길을 내야 하니, 자못 새로운 일이 되겠습니다.\"\n\n가엾은 미스터 우드하우스는 너무 놀라 입을 다물고 말았다. 그러나 다른 사람들은 저마다 할 말이 있었다. 모두가 놀라거나 놀라지 않거나 했고, 던질 물음이나 건넬 위로를 한마디씩 가지고 있었다. 웨스턴 부인과 엠마는 그의 기운을 북돋우고, 사뭇 매정하게 제 승리를 몰아붙이는 사위에게서 그의 주의를 돌리려 진지하게 애썼다.\n\n\"어르신의 결단이 참으로 감탄스러웠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"이런 날씨에 길을 나서시다니요. 물론 어르신께서도 곧 눈이 오리라는 걸 보셨겠지요. 누구라도 눈이 오는 기미를 알아챘을 테니까요. 어르신의 그 패기에 감탄했습니다. 무사히 잘 돌아가게 되리라고 믿습니다. 한두 시간 더 눈이 온다 한들 길이 통 막혀 버리지는 않을 테고, 게다가 우리는 마차가 둘이지 않습니까. 하나가 저 휑한 공유지 벌판에서 뒤집힌다 해도, 다른 하나가 곁에 있을 테니까요. 자정 전에는 모두 무사히 하트필드에 닿으리라 봅니다.\"\n\n미스터 웨스턴은 또 다른 종류의 의기양양함으로, 자기는 얼마 전부터 눈이 오는 줄 알고 있었으나 미스터 우드하우스를 불편하게 하여 서둘러 떠날 핑계가 될까 봐 한마디도 하지 않았노라 털어놓았다. 귀로를 막을 만큼 눈이 쌓였다거나 쌓일 성싶다는 이야기는 그저 우스개일 뿐, 다들 아무 어려움 없이 가게 되리라고 그는 보았다. 그는 오히려 길이 막혀 모두를 랜덜스에 붙들어 둘 수 있으면 좋겠다고 했다. 그러고는 더없는 호의로, 조금만 머리를 쓰면 누구든 다 묵을 자리를 마련할 수 있다고 장담하며, 아내에게 동의를 구했다. 그러나 집에 손님방이 둘밖에 없음을 알고 있던 아내로서는 어떻게 그리할 수 있을지 막막하기만 했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 좋으냐, 우리 엠마야? 어쩌면 좋으냐?\" 이것이 미스터 우드하우스의 첫 탄식이었고, 한동안은 그가 할 수 있는 말의 전부였다. 그는 위로를 구하며 그녀를 바라보았다. 무사하리라는 그녀의 다짐, 말들과 제임스가 얼마나 훌륭한지, 또 곁에 든든한 벗들이 얼마나 많은지에 대한 그녀의 설명이 그를 조금이나마 되살려 놓았다.\n\n큰딸의 불안은 아버지에 못지않았다. 제 아이들이 하트필드에 있는데 자기가 랜덜스에 발이 묶이는 끔찍한 상상이 머릿속에 가득했던 것이다. 그녀는 길이 지금이라면 모험심 있는 사람에게는 그럭저럭 다닐 만하되 한시도 지체해서는 안 될 형편이라 여기고, 아버지와 엠마는 랜덜스에 남고 자기 부부는 길을 막을지도 모를 온갖 눈더미를 무릅쓰고 당장 떠나는 것으로 일을 매듭짓고 싶어 안달했다.\n\n\"여보, 어서 마차를 부르라 이르세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당장 출발하면 그럭저럭 갈 수 있을 거예요. 정 못 갈 만큼 사나운 데를 만나면 내려서 걸으면 되고요. 저는 조금도 무섭지 않아요. 절반쯤은 걷는 것도 괜찮아요. 집에 닿는 즉시 신을 갈아 신으면 되잖아요. 그 정도로 감기 드는 사람도 아니고요.\"\n\n\"저런!\" 그가 대답했다. \"그렇다면, 우리 이저벨라야, 그건 세상에서 둘도 없이 별난 일이로구나. 너는 대개 무슨 일에나 감기가 드는 아이니 말이다. 걸어서 집에 간다니! 걸어가기에 참 어여쁘게도 신을 차려 신었겠구나. 말들에게도 충분히 험한 길이 될 텐데.\"\n\n이저벨라는 그 계획에 동의를 구하고자 웨스턴 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 웨스턴 부인으로서는 찬성하는 수밖에 없었다. 이저벨라는 다시 엠마에게로 갔다. 그러나 엠마는 모두가 무사히 떠날 수 있으리라는 희망을 그렇게까지 완전히 접을 수가 없었다. 두 사람이 여전히 그 문제를 놓고 의논하고 있는데, 동생이 처음 눈 소식을 전하자마자 곧장 방을 나섰던 미스터 나이틀리가 돌아와, 자기가 밖에 나가 살펴보고 왔노라고, 지금이든 한 시간 뒤든 원하는 때 언제고 모두 집에 돌아가는 데 조금의 어려움도 없으리라 장담한다고 말했다. 그는 진입로를 지나 하이베리 길을 얼마쯤 걸어 보았는데, 눈은 어디고 반 인치를 넘는 데가 없고 여러 곳은 땅을 희끗하게 덮을 만큼도 못 되더라고 했다. 지금은 눈송이 몇 점이 떨어질 뿐이며, 구름이 갈라지고 있어 곧 그칠 기색이 완연하더라는 것이었다. 마부들도 만나 보았는데, 둘 다 염려할 것이 없다는 그의 말에 동의하더라고 했다.\n\n이저벨라에게는 그런 소식이 주는 안도가 무척 컸다. 엠마에게도 아버지를 생각하면 그에 못지않게 반가운 소식이었다. 아버지는 이 일에 관해서만큼은 그 예민한 체질이 허락하는 한도 내에서 당장 마음을 놓았다. 그러나 일단 일어난 불안은 그가 랜덜스에 머무는 동안에는 어떤 위로도 받아들일 수 없을 만큼 좀처럼 가라앉지 않았다. 그는 지금 집으로 돌아가는 데 위험이 없다는 점에는 만족했으나, 머물러 있는 편이 안전하다는 것은 어떤 다짐으로도 납득시킬 수 없었다. 다른 사람들이 저마다 이러쿵저러쿵 권하고 채근하는 동안, 미스터 나이틀리와 엠마는 짤막한 몇 마디로 일을 매듭지었다. 이런 식으로.\n\n\"아버님이 마음을 놓지 못하실 거야. 어서 가지 그래?\"\n\n\"다른 사람들만 준비되면 저는 됐어요.\"\n\n\"종을 울릴까?\"\n\n\"네, 그래 주세요.\"\n\n그리하여 종이 울리고, 마차를 대기시키라 일렀다. 몇 분만 더 지나면, 골치 아픈 동무 하나는 제 집에 내려놓아 술도 깨고 머리도 식히게 되고, 다른 하나는 이 고역스러운 방문이 끝나면 본래의 기분과 행복을 되찾으리라고 엠마는 바랐다.\n\n마차가 왔다. 그리고 이런 자리에서는 늘 첫째로 챙겨야 할 미스터 우드하우스가 미스터 나이틀리와 미스터 웨스턴의 정성스러운 부축을 받으며 제 마차로 안내되었다. 그러나 둘이 무어라 말한들, 실제로 내린 눈을 보고 또 예상보다 훨씬 어두운 밤이라는 것을 알게 되자 다시금 일어나는 불안을 막을 길은 없었다. \"길이 몹시 험할까 봐 걱정이라고. 가엾은 이저벨라가 싫어할까 봐 걱정이라고. 게다가 가엾은 엠마는 뒤따르는 마차에 타게 되니. 어찌해야 가장 좋을지 모르겠다고. 되도록 바짝 붙어서 가야 한다고.\" 그리하여 제임스에게 일러, 아주 천천히 가며 다른 마차를 기다리라는 분부가 내려졌다.\n\n이저벨라는 아버지를 따라 마차에 올랐다. 존 나이틀리는 자기가 그쪽 일행이 아니라는 것을 깜빡한 채 아주 자연스럽게 아내를 따라 올라탔다. 그리하여 엠마는 미스터 엘턴의 호위와 뒤따름 속에 두 번째 마차에 오르고 보니, 문이 둘만 남긴 채 떳떳이 닫히게 되었고, 단둘이 마주 앉아 가게 되었음을 알게 되었다. 바로 오늘 그런 의심이 일기 전이었다면 한순간의 어색함은커녕 오히려 즐거운 일이었을 것이다. 해리엇 이야기를 나눌 수 있었을 테고, 사분의 삼 마일 길도 그저 한순간처럼 여겨졌을 것이다. 그러나 지금은, 차라리 이런 일이 없었더라면 싶었다. 그가 미스터 웨스턴의 좋은 포도주를 너무 많이 마신 것 같았고, 분명 또 허튼소리를 늘어놓고 싶어 하리라는 확신이 들었다.\n\n되도록 그를 자제시키고자 제 몸가짐으로라도 그리하려고, 그녀는 날씨와 밤에 대해 더없이 차분하고 진지한 어조로 말을 꺼낼 채비를 곧장 했다. 그러나 채 말을 시작하기도 전에, 진입로 문을 지나 다른 마차와 합류하기 무섭게, 그녀는 제 화제가 잘려 나가고 — 손이 붙잡히고 — 주의를 요구당하고, 미스터 엘턴이 실제로 격렬하게 사랑을 고백하고 있음을 알게 되었다. 그는 이 귀한 기회를 틈타, 이미 빤히 알고 계실 것이 분명한 마음을 토로하고 — 바라고 — 두려워하고 — 흠모하며 — 거절하시면 죽겠노라 했다. 그러면서도 자신의 뜨거운 연정과 견줄 데 없는 사랑과 전례 없는 열정이 효험을 보지 못할 리 없으리라 스스로 흐뭇해했고, 한마디로 되도록 빨리 진지하게 받아들여지기로 단단히 작정한 모양이었다. 정말로 그러했다. 거리낌도 없이 — 사과도 없이 — 별로 머뭇거리는 기색도 없이, 해리엇을 사모하던 미스터 엘턴이 엠마 자신을 사모한다고 고백하고 있었다. 그녀는 그를 막아 보려 했으나 헛일이었다. 그는 기어이 말을 이어, 할 말을 다 쏟아 내려 했다. 화가 치밀었지만, 그 순간의 생각으로 그녀는 정작 입을 열 때는 자제하기로 마음먹었다. 이 어리석음의 절반은 취기 탓이리라 여겨졌고, 그러니 그것이 한때의 일로 그치기를 바랄 수 있었다. 그리하여, 반쯤 취하고 반쯤 깬 그의 상태에 가장 잘 맞으리라 싶은, 진지함과 장난기가 뒤섞인 어조로 그녀는 대답했다.\n\n\"무척 놀랍군요, 미스터 엘턴. 저한테 이런 말씀을요! 정신을 어디 두셨어요. 제 벗과 저를 혼동하고 계시잖아요. 미스 스미스에게 전할 말씀이 있으시면 기꺼이 전해 드리겠지만, 저한테는 부디 더 이상 이러지 마세요.\"\n\n\"미스 스미스라니요! 미스 스미스에게 전할 말씀이라니요! 도대체 무슨 뜻으로 하시는 말씀입니까!\" 그러고는 어찌나 자신만만한 어조로, 어찌나 과장된 놀라움을 가장하며 그녀의 말을 되받던지, 그녀는 얼른 이렇게 응수하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"미스터 엘턴, 이거야말로 둘도 없이 해괴한 처사예요! 제가 이걸 이해할 길은 한 가지뿐이군요. 당신은 지금 제정신이 아니에요. 그렇지 않고서야 저한테든 해리엇에 대해서든 이런 식으로 말씀하실 리 없죠. 더는 아무 말씀 마실 만큼만 자제하세요. 그러면 저도 잊으려 애써 보겠어요.\"\n\n그러나 미스터 엘턴은 기분이 들뜰 만큼만 포도주를 마셨을 뿐, 정신이 흐려질 정도는 결코 아니었다. 그는 제 뜻을 또렷이 알고 있었다. 그는 그녀의 그런 의심이야말로 더없이 모욕적이라며 열을 올려 항변하고는, 미스 스미스의 벗으로서 그녀를 존중한다는 뜻을 슬쩍 비치면서도 — 미스 스미스가 거론되었다는 것 자체가 의아하다고 시인하면서 — 다시 제 열정의 화제로 돌아가, 호의적인 대답을 들려 달라고 몹시 다그쳤다.\n\n그의 취기를 덜 생각하게 되니, 그의 변심과 주제넘음이 더 크게 다가왔다. 그리하여 예의를 차리려는 안간힘을 한결 덜고 그녀는 대답했다.\n\n\"이제 더는 의심할 여지가 없군요. 너무도 분명하게 드러내셨으니까요. 미스터 엘턴, 제 놀라움은 무어라 표현할 수 있는 정도를 한참 넘어섰어요. 지난 한 달 동안 미스 스미스에게 보여 온 그런 행동을 제 눈으로 똑똑히 보았고 — 날마다 지켜보는 게 일과였던 그런 관심을 보여 놓고 — 저한테 이런 식으로 말씀하시다니. 이거야말로 제가 도무지 있을 법하다고 여기지 못했던 성품의 변덕이군요! 믿어 주세요, 선생님. 저는 그런 고백의 대상이 된 것이 조금도, 정말이지 조금도 기쁘지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 미스터 엘턴이 외쳤다. \"도대체 이게 무슨 말씀입니까? 미스 스미스라니요! 저는 살아오는 내내 미스 스미스를 단 한 번도 마음에 둔 적이 없습니다. 그녀에게 관심을 기울인 것이 있다면 오로지 당신의 벗이기 때문이었지요. 그녀가 죽었는지 살았는지조차 당신의 벗으로서가 아니면 마음 쓴 적이 없습니다. 그녀가 달리 짐작했다면, 그건 제 바람이 아니라 그녀 자신의 바람이 그녀를 그르친 것이니, 참으로 안됐습니다 — 더없이 안된 일이지요. 하지만, 미스 스미스라니요, 정말이지! 아! 미스 우드하우스! 미스 우드하우스가 곁에 계신데 누가 미스 스미스 따위를 생각하겠습니까! 아니요, 제 명예를 걸고, 성품의 변덕 같은 건 없습니다. 저는 오직 당신만을 생각해 왔어요. 다른 누구에게도 손톱만큼의 관심을 기울인 적이 없다고 단언합니다. 지난 여러 주 동안 제가 한 말이나 행동은 죄다 당신을 향한 흠모를 드러내려는 단 하나의 뜻에서 나온 것입니다. 설마 정말로, 진지하게, 그걸 의심하시는 건 아니겠지요. 아니에요! (애써 은근한 척하는 어조로) 당신은 분명 저를 알아보고 헤아리셨을 겁니다.\"\n\n이 말을 들은 엠마의 심정이 어떠했는지, 그 온갖 언짢은 감정 가운데 무엇이 가장 위에 있었는지는 도무지 말로 다 할 수 없었다. 그녀는 너무도 완전히 압도된 나머지 곧장 대답할 수가 없었다. 그리고 두 순간의 침묵이 미스터 엘턴의 들뜬 심사에는 더없이 넉넉한 격려가 되었으니, 그는 다시 그녀의 손을 잡으려 하며 기쁨에 겨워 외쳤다.\n\n\"매혹적인 미스 우드하우스! 이 의미심장한 침묵을 제가 풀이하도록 허락해 주십시오. 이건 당신이 진작부터 저를 헤아리고 계셨음을 고백하는 거지요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 선생님.\" 엠마가 소리쳤다. \"그건 그런 걸 고백하는 게 결코 아니에요. 진작부터 헤아리기는커녕, 저는 이 순간까지 당신의 속내에 관해 더없이 완벽한 착각에 빠져 있었어요. 저로 말하자면, 당신이 무슨 감정에든 빠져들고 계셨다면 참으로 유감입니다 — 그건 제 바람과 더없이 거리가 먼 일이니까요. 당신이 제 벗 해리엇에게 품은 연정이 — 그녀를 향한 당신의 구애가, (구애로 보였으니까요) 저를 무척 기쁘게 했고, 저는 당신의 성공을 진심으로 간절히 바라 왔어요. 하지만 그녀가 당신이 하트필드에 오시는 이유가 아니었다는 걸 알았더라면, 그렇게 잦은 방문이야말로 당신의 판단이 잘못되었다고 분명 여겼을 거예요. 그래, 당신이 미스 스미스에게 각별히 잘 보이려 한 적이 한 번도 없었다는 말씀이세요? 그녀를 진지하게 마음에 둔 적이 한 번도 없었다고요?\"\n\n\"한 번도 없습니다, 부인.\" 이번에는 그가 발끈해서 외쳤다. \"단 한 번도, 분명히 말씀드립니다. 제가 미스 스미스를 진지하게 마음에 둔다니요! 미스 스미스는 꽤 괜찮은 처녀이긴 하지요. 그녀가 번듯하게 자리 잡는 것을 본다면 저도 기쁠 겁니다. 그녀가 더없이 잘되기를 바라고요. 그리고 물론, 그녀에게라면 마다하지 않을 남자들도 있겠지요 — 사람마다 제 분수가 있는 법이니까요. 하지만 저로 말할 것 같으면, 제가 그렇게까지 막막한 처지는 아니라고 봅니다. 대등한 혼처를 그토록 단념하여 미스 스미스 따위에게 말을 건넬 만큼은 아니란 말입니다! 아니요, 부인, 제가 하트필드를 찾은 것은 오로지 당신 때문이었습니다. 그리고 제가 받은 격려로 말하자면 —\"\n\n\"격려라니요! 제가 당신에게 격려를 했다고요! 선생님, 그렇게 짐작하셨다면 완전히 잘못 아신 거예요. 저는 당신을 그저 제 벗을 흠모하는 분으로만 보았어요. 그 외에 다른 의미로는, 당신은 저한테 그저 흔한 지인 이상은 결코 아니었습니다. 참으로 유감이에요. 하지만 그 오해가 여기서 끝나는 게 다행이고요. 같은 행동이 계속되었더라면, 미스 스미스가 당신의 속내를 오해하게 되었을지도 모르죠. 당신이 그토록 절감하시는 그 엄청난 신분의 격차를, 그녀도 저처럼 미처 몰랐을 테니까요. 하지만 이렇게 된 이상, 실망하는 사람은 하나뿐이고, 또 오래가지도 않으리라 믿어요. 저는 지금으로선 결혼할 생각이 전혀 없습니다.\"\n\n그는 너무 화가 나 더는 한마디도 못 했다. 그녀의 태도 또한 너무 단호하여 애원을 청할 여지가 없었다. 이렇게 부풀어 오르는 원망과 서로의 깊은 굴욕 속에서, 두 사람은 몇 분을 더 함께 있어야 했다. 미스터 우드하우스의 염려 탓에 두 마차가 걸음걸이만 한 속도로 묶여 있었던 것이다. 그토록 분노가 크지 않았더라면 절망스러운 어색함이 감돌았겠지만, 두 사람의 거침없는 감정은 자잘하게 우물쭈물할 여지를 남기지 않았다. 마차가 어느 결에 비커리지 골목으로 접어들었는지, 어디서 멈춰 섰는지도 모른 채, 두 사람은 별안간 그의 집 문 앞에 닿아 있었다. 그는 또 한마디 말이 오가기도 전에 마차에서 내려 버렸다. 그제야 엠마는 잘 자라는 인사를 건네지 않을 수 없다고 느꼈다. 그 인사는 차갑고 거만하게, 꼭 그만큼만 돌아왔다. 그리고 형언할 수 없는 심사의 동요 속에서, 그녀는 하트필드로 실려 갔다.\n\n그곳에서 그녀는 아버지의 더없이 기쁜 환대를 받았다. 아버지는 비커리지 골목에서 혼자 마차를 타고 오는 위험에 — 그가 도무지 떠올리기조차 견딜 수 없는 모퉁이를 돌아오는 길에 — 그것도 낯선 손에, 그저 흔해 빠진 마부에게, 제임스도 없이 맡겨진다는 생각에 떨고 있었던 것이다. 그곳에서는 마치 그녀가 돌아오기만 하면 만사가 잘 풀릴 듯싶었다. 미스터 존 나이틀리도 제 심술을 부끄러워하며 이제는 온통 다정함과 정성으로 굴었고, 장인의 안락을 어찌나 각별히 챙기던지, 함께 묽은 죽 한 그릇을 들 채비까지는 아니어도 그것이 더없이 몸에 좋다는 것만큼은 충분히 헤아리는 듯 보였다. 그리하여 그날 하루는 그 작은 일행 모두에게 — 그녀 한 사람만 빼고 — 평온하고 안락하게 저물어 갔다. 그러나 그녀의 마음은 일찍이 그토록 어지러웠던 적이 없었다. 그래서 헤어질 때가 되어 비로소 그녀에게 조용한 사색의 안식을 허락해 줄 때까지, 줄곧 주의 깊고 명랑한 척하려면 무던히도 힘써야 했다.\n\n# 제1권 16장\n\n머리 손질이 끝나고 하녀를 물리자, 엠마는 앉아서 생각에 잠겼고 또 비참해졌다. 참으로 끔찍한 일이었다! 그녀가 바라 온 모든 것이 이토록 뒤집히다니! 더없이 달갑잖은 일들이 이토록 한꺼번에 드러나다니! 해리엇에게 이런 타격이라니! 그것이 무엇보다도 최악이었다. 이 일의 어느 구석을 보아도 이런저런 종류의 아픔과 굴욕이 따라왔다. 그러나 해리엇이 겪을 화에 견주면 다른 것은 죄다 가벼웠다. 자신의 실수가 빚은 결과가 오직 제게만 미칠 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 실제보다 더 많이 틀리고, 더 그르치고, 그릇된 판단으로 더 망신당하는 일조차 기꺼이 감수했으리라.\n\n\"내가 해리엇을 부추겨 그 사람을 좋아하게 만들지만 않았더라면, 무엇이든 견딜 수 있었을 텐데. 그 사람이 나한테 그런 주제넘은 짓을 두 배로 했어도 좋았어. 하지만 가엾은 해리엇은!\"\n\n어쩌면 자신이 그렇게까지 속을 수 있었을까! 그는 해리엇을 진지하게 생각한 적이 한 번도 없다고, 단 한 번도 없다고 단언했다. 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 지난 일을 되짚어 보았으나, 모든 것이 뒤죽박죽이었다. 짐작건대 자신이 어떤 생각을 한번 품고는 모든 것을 거기에 끼워 맞춘 모양이었다. 그러나 그의 태도도 분명 어딘가 모호하고, 흔들리고, 알쏭달쏭한 데가 있었을 것이다. 그렇지 않고서야 그녀가 그토록 잘못 헤아릴 리 없었다.\n\n그 초상화! 그가 그 초상화에 얼마나 열을 올렸던가! 그리고 그 수수께끼 시! 게다가 그 밖에도 수백 가지 정황이 있었다. 그 모든 것이 해리엇을 가리키는 것이 어쩌면 그렇게도 또렷해 보였던지. 하기야 그 수수께끼 시의 \"민첩한 재치\"는 — 그렇지만 또 \"부드러운 눈동자\"는 — 사실 어느 쪽에도 맞지 않았다. 그것은 운치도 진실도 없는 뒤죽박죽이었다. 그런 멍청한 헛소리를 누가 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있었겠는가?\n\n물론 그녀도 자주, 특히 요즈음 들어서는, 그가 자신을 대하는 태도가 쓸데없이 정중하다고 여기긴 했다. 그러나 그것을 그저 그의 버릇으로, 판단력이나 식견이나 안목의 단순한 흠으로 넘겼던 것이다 — 그가 늘 최상류 사회에서만 살아온 것은 아니어서, 그토록 부드러운 몸가짐에도 불구하고 더러 참된 우아함이 결여되곤 한다는 여러 증거 가운데 하나로. 그러나 바로 오늘에 이르기까지, 그녀는 그것이 해리엇의 벗인 자신에게 보내는 고마운 경의 외에 다른 무엇을 뜻한다고는 한순간도 의심해 본 적이 없었다.\n\n이 문제에 관한 첫 생각, 그 가능성을 처음 떠올리게 해 준 사람은 미스터 존 나이틀리였다. 그 형제에게 통찰력이 있다는 것은 부정할 수 없었다. 그녀는 언젠가 미스터 나이틀리가 미스터 엘턴을 두고 했던 말, 그가 일러 준 경계의 말, 미스터 엘턴이 결코 분별없이 결혼하지는 않으리라던 그 확신을 떠올렸다. 그리고 거기 드러난 그의 인품에 대한 식견이 자신이 다다른 그 어떤 것보다 얼마나 더 진실에 가까웠는지를 생각하니 얼굴이 화끈거렸다. 끔찍하게 면목 없는 일이었다. 그러나 미스터 엘턴은 여러모로 그녀가 의도하고 믿어 온 것과는 정반대의 사람임을 스스로 입증하고 있었다 — 거만하고, 주제넘고, 우쭐대고, 제 권리 주장으로 가득 차서 남의 감정에는 거의 무심한 사람이었다.\n\n여느 경우의 통례와는 반대로, 미스터 엘턴이 자신에게 구애하려 했다는 사실은 도리어 그녀의 평가에서 그를 끌어내렸다. 그의 고백도 청혼도 그에게 아무런 득이 되지 못했다. 그녀는 그의 연정을 대수롭지 않게 여겼고, 그의 기대에 모욕감을 느꼈다. 그는 잘 결혼하고 싶었던 것이고, 자신에게까지 눈을 들이대는 오만을 부려 사랑에 빠진 척한 것이었다. 그러나 그녀로서는 그가 마음 써 줄 만한 실망 따위는 조금도 겪지 않으리라는 점에 대해 완전히 마음이 놓였다. 그의 말에도 태도에도 참된 애정이라곤 없었다. 한숨과 고운 말은 차고 넘치게 쏟아 냈으나, 참된 사랑과 이만큼 동떨어진 표현의 묶음이나 목소리의 가락은 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없을 정도였다. 그를 가엾이 여기느라 마음 쓸 필요도 없었다. 그는 그저 제 몸을 키우고 살림을 불리고 싶었을 뿐이다. 그러니 하트필드의 미스 우드하우스, 삼만 파운드의 상속녀가 제 짐작만큼 그리 호락호락 손에 들어오지 않는다면, 그는 곧 이만 파운드짜리, 아니 만 파운드짜리 미스 아무개를 노릴 사람이었다.\n\n그러나 — 그가 자신이 부추김을 받았다는 듯이 말하다니, 자신을 그의 속셈을 알고 그의 관심을 받아들이며 (한마디로) 그와 결혼할 작정인 여자로 여기다니! 자신을 가문에서나 식견에서나 그와 대등한 사람이라 생각하다니! 자기보다 아래인 신분의 등급은 그토록 잘 가려내면서도, 위로 솟은 것에 대해서는 그토록 눈이 어두워, 자신에게 구애하는 것이 조금도 주제넘은 짓이 아니라고 여기다니! 그것은 더없이 분통 터지는 일이었다.\n\n어쩌면 그가 재능에서나, 정신의 온갖 우아함에서나 자신보다 한참 못하다는 것을 느끼기를 바라는 것은 공정치 않은 일인지도 몰랐다. 그런 대등함이 없다는 바로 그 사실이 그가 그것을 알아채지 못하게 막았을 수도 있으니까. 그러나 재산과 지체에서는 그녀가 그보다 훨씬 위라는 것쯤은 그도 분명 알았을 것이다. 우드하우스 집안이 여러 대에 걸쳐 하트필드에 자리 잡아 온, 아주 유서 깊은 가문의 방계라는 것 — 그리고 엘턴 집안은 이름 없는 가문이라는 것을 그도 분명 알았을 것이다. 하트필드의 토지 재산이야 보잘것없어서, 하이베리의 나머지 땅이 죄다 속해 있는 돈웰 애비 영지에 박힌 일종의 작은 틈에 지나지 않았다. 그러나 다른 출처에서 나오는 그들의 재산은, 그 밖의 모든 지체에 있어서 그들을 돈웰 애비 그 자체에도 거의 뒤지지 않게 해 줄 만한 것이었다. 게다가 우드하우스 집안은 오래도록 이웃의 높은 존경을 받아 왔으니, 미스터 엘턴이 이 고장에 처음 발을 들인 것은 채 이태도 안 되는 일로, 장사로 맺은 인연 말고는 아무 연줄도 없고, 제 처지와 깍듯함 외에는 눈에 띌 만한 아무것도 없이 그저 스스로 길을 헤쳐 나가려던 처지였다. 그런데도 그는 그녀가 자신에게 반했다고 믿었던 것이다. 그것이 분명 그의 믿는 구석이었으리라. 그리고 부드러운 몸가짐과 우쭐한 머릿속이 어울리지 않는 듯하다며 한동안 분개해 보던 끝에, 엠마는 양심상 멈추고 인정하지 않을 수 없었다 — 그를 대한 자신의 행동이 어찌나 살갑고 고분고분하며, 어찌나 예의와 관심으로 가득했던지 (자신의 진짜 속내를 그가 알아채지 못했다고 친다면), 미스터 엘턴 정도의 평범한 관찰력과 섬세함을 지닌 사내라면 자신이 단연 사랑받고 있다고 믿을 만도 했다는 것을. 만약 자신이 그토록 그의 감정을 잘못 읽었다면, 사욕에 눈이 먼 그가 자신의 감정을 오해했다 한들 놀랄 자격이 거의 없는 셈이었다.\n\n첫 번째 잘못, 그것도 가장 큰 잘못은 그녀 자신에게 있었다. 어떤 두 사람을 맺어 주려고 그렇게 적극적으로 끼어든 것은 어리석고도 그릇된 일이었다. 그것은 너무 멀리 나아간 모험이요, 너무 많은 것을 함부로 떠맡은 일이며, 진지하게 다루어야 할 일을 가벼이 여기고, 단순해야 할 일을 술수로 만든 짓이었다. 그녀는 못내 걱정스럽고 부끄러웠으며, 다시는 그런 일을 하지 않으리라 결심했다.\n\n\"이렇게 됐어.\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. \"내가 기어이 가엾은 해리엇을 구슬려 이 남자한테 깊이 빠지게 만들어 놓았어. 나만 없었으면 그 애는 그 사람을 떠올리지도 않았을 거야. 그리고 내가 그 사람의 연정을 장담하지만 않았어도, 결코 희망을 품고 그 사람을 생각하진 않았을 거야 — 그 애는 내가 전에 그 사람을 그리 여겼듯이, 그만큼 겸손하고 자신을 낮추는 아이니까. 아! 그 젊은 마틴을 받아들이지 말라고 설득한 데서 만족했어야 했는데. 거기까진 내가 아주 옳았어. 그건 잘한 일이었어. 하지만 거기서 멈추고, 나머지는 세월과 운에 맡겼어야 했어. 나는 그 애를 좋은 사람들 사이로 끌어 주고, 가질 만한 누군가의 마음에 들 기회를 주고 있었던 거야. 그 이상을 시도해선 안 되는 거였어. 그런데 이제 가엾은 그 애는 한동안 마음의 평화를 잃게 됐어. 나는 그 애한테 반쪽짜리 벗밖에 못 됐어. 그리고 그 애가 이번 실망을 그렇게 심하게 느끼지 않는다 쳐도, 그 애한테 조금이라도 어울릴 만한 다른 사람은 정말이지 도무지 떠오르질 않아. 윌리엄 콕스 — 아! 안 돼, 윌리엄 콕스는 못 참아 — 건방진 젊은 변호사라니.\"\n\n그녀는 자신이 또 옛 버릇으로 되돌아간 것에 얼굴을 붉히며 웃다가 멈추었고, 이내 지나간 일, 있을 법한 일, 있을 수밖에 없는 일에 대해 한층 진지하고 한층 풀 죽은 사색으로 돌아갔다. 해리엇에게 해야 할 그 괴로운 해명, 그리고 가엾은 해리엇이 겪을 온갖 일들 — 앞으로의 만남이 어색해지리라는 것, 교제를 이어 갈지 끊을지 정하기가 곤란하리라는 것, 감정을 억누르고 분한 마음을 감추며 소동이 나지 않게 피해야 하는 일들 — 이런 것만으로도 한동안 더 그녀를 더없이 우울한 상념에 잠기게 하기 충분했다. 그리하여 마침내 잠자리에 들었으나, 정해진 것이라곤 자신이 더없이 끔찍하게 일을 그르쳤다는 확신뿐이었다.\n\n엠마처럼 젊고 천성이 쾌활한 사람에게는, 비록 밤에는 잠시 우울에 잠긴다 해도, 날이 다시 밝으면 좀처럼 기운이 돌아오지 않는 법이 없다. 아침의 젊음과 쾌활함은 행복한 조응을 이루며 강한 힘으로 작용한다. 그리고 그 괴로움이 두 눈을 감기지 못할 만큼 사무치는 것이 아니라면, 그 눈은 틀림없이 한결 누그러진 아픔과 한층 밝아진 희망의 감각으로 떠지게 마련이다.\n\n이튿날 엠마는 잠자리에 들 때보다 한결 마음 편한 기분으로 일어났다. 눈앞에 놓인 화를 누그러뜨릴 방도가 보이고, 그럭저럭 거기서 벗어나리라는 기대를 걸 수 있었다.\n\n미스터 엘턴이 자신을 정말로 사랑하는 것이 아니라는 것, 그를 실망시키는 것이 끔찍하게 여겨질 만큼 그가 그리 각별히 사랑스러운 사람도 아니라는 것 — 해리엇의 천성이 감정이 가장 예민하고 오래 가는 그런 우월한 부류는 아니라는 것 — 그리고 일어난 일을 굳이 세 당사자 말고는 아무도 알 필요가 없으며, 특히 아버지에게는 단 한순간의 불안도 안겨 드릴 까닭이 없다는 것, 이 모두가 큰 위안이었다.\n\n이런 생각들은 무척 마음을 북돋아 주었다. 게다가 땅에 두껍게 쌓인 눈이 보이는 것도 한층 도움이 되었으니, 지금으로서는 셋이 서로 완전히 떨어져 지내는 것을 정당화해 줄 만한 일이라면 무엇이든 반가웠던 것이다.\n\n날씨는 그녀에게 더없이 유리했다. 성탄절이었으나 교회에 갈 수 없었다. 딸이 그러려 들었다면 미스터 우드하우스는 비탄에 빠졌을 터, 그래서 그녀는 달갑잖고 도무지 어울리지도 않는 생각을 불러일으키거나 받게 될 염려에서 안전했다. 땅은 눈으로 덮였고, 대기는 얼음과 해빙 사이의 그 어수선한 상태에 있었으니 — 운동에는 무엇보다도 고약한 날씨로, 아침마다 비나 눈으로 시작하고 저녁마다 얼어붙으니 — 그녀는 여러 날 동안 더없이 떳떳한 갇힌 몸이 되었다. 해리엇과는 쪽지 말고는 어떤 왕래도 불가능했고, 일요일에도 성탄절과 마찬가지로 교회에 갈 수 없었으며, 미스터 엘턴이 자리를 비우는 데 대한 구실을 찾을 필요도 없었다.\n\n누구든 집 안에 묶어 둘 만한 날씨였다. 그리고 그가 어딘가 누군가의 모임에서 정말로 위안을 얻고 있으리라 바라고 또 믿기는 했지만, 그가 사려 깊게도 바깥출입을 삼간 채 제 집에 홀로 있다는 데 아버지가 그토록 흡족해하시는 모습을 보는 것은 무척 흐뭇한 일이었다. 게다가 어떤 날씨로도 그들에게서 완전히 떼어 놓을 수 없는 미스터 나이틀리에게 아버지가 이렇게 말하는 것을 듣는 것도 그러했다.\n\n\"아, 미스터 나이틀리, 어째서 가엾은 미스터 엘턴처럼 집에 가만히 계시질 않소?\"\n\n이 갇혀 지낸 날들은, 그녀 혼자만의 골칫거리만 아니었다면 유난히 편안했을 것이다. 이런 칩거가 형부의 취향에 딱 맞았기 때문이다 — 형부의 기분이란 늘 함께 지내는 이들에게 큰 영향을 미치기 마련이었는데, 게다가 그는 랜덜스에서 묻혀 온 언짢음을 어찌나 말끔히 털어 버렸던지, 하트필드에 머무는 내내 그 상냥함이 한 번도 흐트러지지 않았다. 그는 늘 유쾌하고 다정했으며, 누구에 대해서든 좋게 말했다. 그러나 쾌활함을 향한 온갖 기대에도, 또 미루어 둔 데서 오는 지금의 온갖 편안함에도, 해리엇과 해명을 나눌 그 시간에는 여전히 그토록 큰 화가 그녀의 머리 위에 드리워 있어, 엠마로서는 도무지 마음을 온전히 놓을 수가 없었다.\n\n# 제1권 17장\n\n미스터 존 나이틀리 부부가 하트필드에 오래 머물지는 않았다. 떠나야 할 사람들이 떠날 수 있을 만큼 날씨가 곧 풀렸고, 미스터 우드하우스는 여느 때처럼 딸에게 아이들을 모두 데리고 남으라고 설득해 보았으나, 결국 일행이 모두 길을 나서는 것을 지켜볼 수밖에 없었으며, 가엾은 이저벨라의 운명을 두고 탄식하는 자리로 되돌아오고 말았다. 그런데 그 가엾은 이저벨라로 말하자면, 끔찍이 사랑하는 사람들과 더불어 살며, 그들의 장점으로 가득 차고 그들의 결점에는 눈이 멀어, 늘 천진하게 바쁘게 지내니, 올바른 여성적 행복의 본보기라 할 만한 사람이었다.\n\n그들이 떠난 바로 그날 저녁, 미스터 엘턴이 미스터 우드하우스에게 보낸 쪽지가 왔으니, 길고도 정중하며 격식을 갖춘 쪽지였다. 미스터 엘턴의 깍듯한 인사와 더불어, 다음과 같은 내용이었다. \"이튿날 아침 하이베리를 떠나 바스로 향할 작정이며, 그곳에서 몇몇 벗들의 간곡한 청에 따라 몇 주를 머물기로 약속하였으나, 날씨며 용무며 여러 사정으로 미스터 우드하우스를 직접 찾아뵙고 작별 인사를 드릴 수 없게 된 것을 몹시 애석히 여긴다. 그가 베풀어 주신 다정한 호의에 대해서는 언제까지나 고마운 마음을 간직할 것이며, 미스터 우드하우스께서 분부하실 일이 있으시면 기꺼이 받들겠노라\"는 것이었다.\n\n엠마는 더없이 흐뭇하게 놀랐다. 하필 이 시점에 미스터 엘턴이 자리를 비운다는 것은 바라 마지않던 바로 그 일이었다. 그가 이런 일을 꾸며 낸 것이 기특했으나, 그것을 알려 온 방식만큼은 그리 높이 사 줄 수 없었다. 그토록 노골적으로 자신을 빼놓고 아버지에게만 보낸 정중함 속에, 원망이 이보다 더 분명하게 드러날 수는 없었으리라. 그녀는 그 첫머리의 인사말에서조차 한자리를 얻지 못했다. 그녀의 이름은 거론되지 않았다. 이 모든 데에서 어찌나 두드러진 변화가 있고, 그 우아한 사례의 말 속에 작별 인사의 분별없는 엄숙함이 어찌나 짙던지, 처음에 그녀는 이것이 아버지의 의심을 피하지 못하리라 여겼다.\n\n그러나 의심을 피했다. 아버지는 그토록 갑작스러운 여행에 놀라고, 미스터 엘턴이 무사히 그 끝에 가닿지 못할까 걱정하는 데 온통 마음을 빼앗긴 터라, 그 글에서 이상한 점이라곤 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그 쪽지는 무척 쓸모가 있었으니, 두 사람만의 적적한 저녁 나머지를 채울 새로운 생각거리와 이야깃거리를 제공해 주었기 때문이다. 미스터 우드하우스는 자기 걱정들을 늘어놓았고, 엠마는 여느 때처럼 재빠른 솜씨로 그 걱정들을 달래어 물리칠 만큼 기운이 좋았다.\n\n이제 그녀는 해리엇을 더는 모르는 채로 두지 않기로 마음먹었다. 해리엇이 감기에서 거의 나았다고 믿을 만한 근거가 있었고, 그 신사가 돌아오기 전에 또 다른 병을 떨쳐 낼 시간을 되도록 많이 갖는 편이 바람직했다. 그리하여 바로 이튿날 그녀는 고더드 부인의 집을 찾아가, 사실을 털어놓는 필요한 속죄의 절차를 치렀으니, 그것은 혹독한 속죄였다. 그토록 부지런히 키워 온 모든 희망을 부숴 버려야 했고, 자기가 그 선택받은 사람이라는 야박한 처지로 나서야 했으며, 지난 여섯 주 동안 한 가지 문제에 관한 자신의 모든 생각, 모든 관찰, 모든 확신, 모든 예언이 죄다 어처구니없이 틀렸고 잘못된 판단이었음을 인정해야 했던 것이다.\n\n이 고백은 처음의 부끄러움을 송두리째 되살려 놓았다. 그리고 해리엇의 눈물을 보고 있자니, 다시는 스스로에게 떳떳할 수 없으리라는 생각마저 들었다.\n\n해리엇은 그 소식을 아주 의젓하게 받아들였다. 누구도 탓하지 않았고, 매사에 어찌나 천진한 성품과 자신을 낮추는 겸손함을 드러내던지, 바로 그 순간 그 벗의 눈에는 그것이 유난히 도드라져 보일 수밖에 없었다.\n\n엠마는 그 순간 소박함과 겸손함을 더없이 높이 사고 싶은 심정이었다. 사랑스러운 모든 것, 마음을 끌 만한 모든 것이 자기 쪽이 아니라 해리엇 쪽에 있는 듯했다. 해리엇은 자기에게 불평할 거리가 조금이라도 있다고 여기지 않았다. 미스터 엘턴 같은 남자의 애정이라면 자기에게는 과분한 영광이었을 것이고, 결코 그를 누릴 자격이 없었으며, 미스 우드하우스처럼 편애 어린 다정한 벗이 아니고서야 누구도 그것이 가능하리라 생각하지 못했으리라는 것이었다.\n\n해리엇의 눈물은 그칠 줄 모르고 흘렀다. 그러나 그 슬픔이 어찌나 진실로 꾸밈없던지, 어떤 위엄을 갖추었다 한들 엠마의 눈에 그보다 더 존중스러워 보일 수는 없었으리라. 엠마는 온 마음과 온 지혜를 다해 해리엇의 말에 귀 기울이고 위로하려 애썼다. 그러는 동안만큼은, 둘 중에 더 나은 사람은 해리엇이며, 그녀를 닮는 것이야말로 천재성이나 영민함이 할 수 있는 그 무엇보다 자신의 안녕과 행복에 보탬이 되리라고 정말로 믿었던 것이다.\n\n이제 와서 소박하고 무지한 사람이 되어 보겠다고 마음먹기에는 너무 늦은 때였다. 그러나 그녀는 겸손하고 신중해지자고, 그리고 남은 평생 상상의 날개를 억누르자고 미리 다진 결심을 모조리 굳힌 채 해리엇을 떠났다. 이제 그녀의 두 번째 의무는, 오직 아버지에 대한 의무에만 자리를 양보하는 그 의무는, 해리엇을 편안하게 해 주고, 중매보다 더 나은 방법으로 자신의 애정을 증명해 보이려 애쓰는 것이었다. 그녀는 해리엇을 하트필드로 데려와 한결같은 친절을 베풀었고, 책과 대화로 그녀의 마음을 붙들고 즐겁게 해 주며 미스터 엘턴을 그 생각에서 몰아내려 힘썼다.\n\n이 일이 온전히 이루어지려면 시간이 필요하다는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그리고 그녀로서는 이런 일에 대해 대체로 변변찮은 판단자에 지나지 않으며, 더구나 미스터 엘턴을 향한 연정에 공감하기에는 턱없이 부족하다고 스스로 인정할 수 있었다. 그러나 해리엇의 나이로 보아, 그리고 모든 희망이 완전히 사그라든 마당이니, 미스터 엘턴이 돌아올 무렵에는 마음의 평정을 향해 어지간히 나아가 있어서, 감정을 들키거나 더 키울 위험 없이 다 함께 예사로운 교제의 일상 속에서 다시 만날 수 있으리라고 보는 것이 사리에 맞을 듯했다.\n\n해리엇은 정말로 그를 완벽 그 자체로 여겼고, 인물에서나 인품에서나 그에 견줄 사람은 세상에 없노라 우겼다. 그리고 실제로 엠마가 내다본 것보다 훨씬 더 단단히 사랑에 빠진 것으로 드러났다. 그러나 보답 없는 그런 마음에 맞서 싸우는 것은 너무도 당연하고 불가피한 일로 여겨졌으므로, 그 마음이 그토록 오래 같은 힘으로 이어지리라고는 도무지 짐작할 수 없었다.\n\n미스터 엘턴이 돌아와서, 의심할 바 없이 애써 그러하리라 그녀가 믿어 마지않듯이, 자신의 무관심을 분명하고 의심할 여지없이 드러내 보인다면, 해리엇이 그를 보거나 떠올리는 데에 끝끝내 자기 행복을 걸어 두리라고는 상상할 수 없었다.\n\n세 사람이 같은 곳에 붙박여, 그토록 꼼짝없이 붙박여 있다는 것은 저마다에게, 셋 모두에게 나쁜 일이었다. 누구 하나 그곳을 떠날 수도, 교제의 형편을 이렇다 할 만큼 바꿔 놓을 수도 없었다. 그들은 서로 마주칠 수밖에 없었고, 그 안에서 최선을 다하는 수밖에 없었다.\n\n해리엇은 고더드 부인 집에 있는 동무들의 말투 탓에 한층 더 불운했다. 미스터 엘턴은 그 학교의 모든 선생과 큰 여학생들이 떠받드는 우상이었기 때문이다. 그에 관한 이야기를 차분히 가라앉히는 절도나 마음을 식히는 진실과 더불어 들을 가망이 있는 곳은 오직 하트필드뿐이었다. 상처가 난 곳, 거기에서 치유도 찾아야 하는 법이니, 어디에서든 찾을 수 있다면 그러했다. 그리고 엠마는, 해리엇이 치유의 길로 들어선 것을 보기 전에는 자기에게도 참된 평안은 있을 수 없으리라고 느꼈다.\n\n# 제1권 18장\n\n미스터 프랭크 처칠은 오지 않았다. 약속한 날이 가까워지자, 핑계를 적은 편지가 도착함으로써 웨스턴 부인의 염려가 들어맞았다. 당분간은 도저히 시간을 낼 수 없다는 것이었다. \"더할 나위 없는 낙심과 유감\"을 안고서. \"그래도 머지않은 때에 랜덜스로 갈 수 있으리라는 희망만은 잃지 않고 있습니다.\" 하고 그는 적었다.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 몹시 실망했다 — 실은 남편보다 훨씬 더 실망했는데, 그 젊은이를 만나리라는 기대는 그녀 쪽이 훨씬 차분했는데도 그러했다. 그러나 낙천적인 기질이란, 일어나는 것보다 늘 더 많은 좋은 일을 기대하면서도, 그 기대에 걸맞은 침울함으로 늘 그 값을 치르는 것은 아니다. 그것은 이내 눈앞의 차질을 훌쩍 뛰어넘어, 다시 희망을 품기 시작한다. 미스터 웨스턴은 반 시간쯤 놀라고 서운해했다. 그러나 곧 프랭크가 두세 달 뒤에 오는 편이 훨씬 나은 계획이라는 것을, 더 좋은 철에, 더 좋은 날씨에 오는 셈이고, 게다가 일찍 왔을 때보다 의심할 바 없이 훨씬 더 오래 함께 머물 수 있으리라는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다.\n\n이런 느낌들이 그의 마음을 금세 도로 편안하게 해 준 반면, 좀 더 걱정 많은 성미인 웨스턴 부인은 핑계와 미룸이 되풀이되는 일밖에 내다보지 못했다. 그리하여 남편이 겪을 마음고생을 그토록 염려하고도, 정작 더 큰 고생을 한 것은 그녀 자신이었다.\n\n이 무렵 엠마는, 랜덜스 쪽의 실망이라는 점을 빼고는, 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 오지 않는 일을 진심으로 마음에 둘 만한 심경이 아니었다. 그와 알고 지내는 일이 지금으로서는 아무런 매력도 없었다. 그녀는 차라리 조용히, 유혹에서 벗어나 있고 싶었다. 그래도 겉으로는 대체로 평소의 자기다워 보이는 것이 바람직했으므로, 그들의 우정으로 보아 마땅할 만큼의 관심을 그 일에 드러내고, 미스터·미시즈 웨스턴의 실망에 그만큼 따뜻하게 동조하려고 신경을 썼다.\n\n그녀는 그 소식을 미스터 나이틀리에게 가장 먼저 알린 사람이었다. 그리고 프랭크를 못 오게 붙들어 둔 처칠 집안의 처사를 두고, 꼭 필요한 만큼은 (어쩌면 연기를 하느라 다소 지나치게) 탄식해 보였다. 이어서 그녀는, 답답한 서리 지방의 한정된 사교계에 그런 새 인물이 보태지는 이점을, 새 얼굴을 바라보는 즐거움을, 그를 보았더라면 하이베리 전체에 베풀어졌을 축제 같은 하루를, 실제로 느끼는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이 늘어놓았다. 그러다 다시 처칠 집안에 대한 비난으로 말을 맺으려는 참에, 자신이 미스터 나이틀리와 곧장 의견 다툼에 빠져 있음을 알아차렸다. 그리고 무척 우습게도, 자신이 본심과는 정반대 편을 들면서, 웨스턴 부인의 논리를 끌어다 도리어 제 주장에 맞세우고 있음을 깨달았다.\n\n\"처칠 집안 잘못일 공산이 크지.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 냉정하게 말했다. \"하지만 마음만 먹으면 그 사람도 올 수 있었을 게 분명해.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 말씀하시는지 모르겠네요. 그분은 오고 싶어 못 견뎌 하는데, 외삼촌과 외숙모가 놓아주질 않는다잖아요.\"\n\n\"마음만 단단히 먹었다면 올 힘이 없었으리라곤 믿기지 않아. 증거 없이 그대로 믿기엔 너무 그럴듯하지 않은 얘기야.\"\n\n\"별스러우셔라! 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 대체 무슨 짓을 했기에, 그렇게 인정머리 없는 사람으로 단정하시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"인정머리 없는 사람이라고 단정하는 게 결코 아니야. 다만, 늘 그런 본을 보여 온 사람들과 함께 살다 보니, 제 인척들을 우습게 여기고 제 즐거움 말고는 무엇에도 마음 쓰지 않는 버릇이 들었으리라고 의심하는 것뿐이지. 거만하고 사치스럽고 이기적인 자들 손에 자란 젊은이가 저 또한 거만하고 사치스럽고 이기적인 사람이 되는 것은, 차라리 바라는 것보다 훨씬 자연스러운 일이거든. 프랭크 처칠이 정말로 제 아버지를 보고 싶었다면, 구월에서 일월 사이에 어떻게든 짬을 내었을 거야. 그 나이의 남자가 — 대체 몇이지? 스물서넛? — 그만한 일을 해낼 방도가 없을 리 없어. 그건 불가능한 얘기야.\"\n\n\"그건 늘 자기 몸의 주인으로 살아오신 분이니 쉽게 하시는 말씀이고, 쉽게 드는 생각이죠. 미스터 나이틀리, 매인 몸의 고충에 대해서라면 당신은 세상에서 가장 형편없는 판관이세요. 남의 비위를 맞춰 가며 사는 게 어떤 건지 모르시잖아요.\"\n\n\"스물서넛 먹은 남자가 그만한 정도의 정신적·신체적 자유조차 없다는 건 도무지 생각할 수 없는 일이야. 돈이 부족할 리 없고 — 한가한 시간이 부족할 리도 없지. 오히려 우리가 아는 바로는, 그가 그 둘을 어찌나 넉넉히 가졌는지, 이 나라에서 제일 한량 같은 곳들에서 그것들을 기꺼이 탕진하고 다닐 정도잖아. 어느 휴양지에서든 늘 그의 소식이 들려오니까. 얼마 전에도 웨이머스에 있었지. 이건 그가 처칠 집안을 떠날 수 있다는 증거야.\"\n\n\"네, 가끔은요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 '가끔'이란, 제가 그럴 만한 가치가 있다고 여길 때마다, 즐거움의 유혹이 있을 때마다겠지.\"\n\n\"누군가의 처지를 속속들이 알지도 못하면서 그 행실을 판단하는 건 몹시 부당한 일이에요. 한 집안의 속사정에 들어가 보지 않은 사람은, 그 집안 어느 한 사람의 고충이 어떠한지 함부로 말할 수 없어요. 그 조카가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 단정하기 전에, 우리는 먼저 엔스컴이 어떤 곳인지, 처칠 부인의 성미가 어떤지부터 알아야 마땅하죠. 그 사람은 어떤 때는 다른 때보다 훨씬 많은 일을 할 수 있을지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"엠마, 한 가지만은 사람이 마음만 먹으면 언제든 할 수 있어. 바로 제 도리야 — 술수를 부리거나 잔재주를 피워서가 아니라, 기개와 결단으로 말이지. 아버지에게 이만한 관심을 표하는 것은 프랭크 처칠의 도리야. 본인도 그렇다는 걸 알고 있어, 제가 한 약속과 전갈로 보아 말이지. 그러니 정말로 하고 싶었다면 해낼 수 있었을 거야. 도리를 옳게 느끼는 남자라면, 처칠 부인에게 즉시 이렇게 말했겠지 — 간단하고 단호하게. '한낱 즐거움을 희생하는 일이라면 외숙모님 편하시도록 언제든 기꺼이 응하겠습니다. 하지만 지금 당장 가서 아버지를 뵈어야겠습니다. 이런 때에 그분께 마땅히 표해야 할 예의를 저버린다면 아버지께서 상심하시리라는 걸 압니다. 그러니 내일 떠나겠습니다.' — 이렇게 남자다운 결단의 어조로 단번에 말했더라면, 그가 가는 일에 아무 반대도 없었을 거야.\"\n\n\"없겠죠.\" 엠마가 웃으며 말했다. \"하지만 다시 돌아오는 일에는 어쩌면 반대가 좀 있었을지도 몰라요. 전적으로 남에게 매여 사는 젊은이가 그런 말을 입에 올리다니요! 미스터 나이틀리, 당신 말고는 아무도 그게 가능하다고 상상하지 못할 거예요. 하지만 당신은, 자기 처지와 정반대인 처지에서는 무엇이 필요한지 도무지 짐작도 못 하시는걸요. 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 자기를 길러 주고 또 앞날까지 책임져 줄 외삼촌·외숙모에게 그런 연설을 하다니요! 방 한가운데 떡 버티고 서서, 목청껏 소리 높여 말하는 모습이라도 그리시는 거예요? 그런 행동을 어떻게 실행할 수 있다고 상상하시죠?\"\n\n\"엠마, 분별 있는 남자라면 그쯤은 조금도 어렵지 않게 해낸다고 믿어도 좋아. 제가 옳다는 걸 느낄 테고, 그 선언은 — 물론 분별 있는 사람이 으레 하듯 온당한 태도로 한 것이겠지 — 온갖 둘러대기와 임시방편을 다 동원하는 것보다 그에게 훨씬 이로울 거야. 그의 입지를 높여 주고, 그가 의지하는 사람들 사이에서 그의 신망을 더 단단히 굳혀 줄 테니까. 애정에 존경까지 더해질 거란 말이지. 그들은 그를 믿어도 되겠다고 느끼게 될 거야. 아버지에게 도리를 다한 조카라면 자기들에게도 도리를 다하리라고 말이지. 그도 알고, 그들도 알고, 온 세상이 알 수밖에 없는 사실이거든 — 그가 마땅히 아버지를 찾아뵈어야 한다는 것을. 한편으로는 옹졸하게 권력을 휘둘러 그 걸음을 미루게 하면서도, 그가 제 변덕에 순순히 따르는 것을 두고 마음속으로는 결코 그를 더 높이 보지 않는단 말이지. 옳은 행실에 대한 존경은 누구나 느끼는 거야. 그가 이런 식으로, 원칙에 따라 한결같이, 흐트러짐 없이 처신한다면, 그들의 옹졸한 마음이 도리어 그에게 굽혀 들 거야.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 그건 좀 의심스러운데요. 당신은 옹졸한 마음을 굽히게 하는 일을 무척 좋아하시지만, 옹졸한 마음이 권세 쥔 부자에게 붙어 있으면, 그건 한껏 부풀어 올라서 결국 큰 마음 못지않게 다루기 힘들어지는 재주가 있거든요. 상상은 할 수 있어요 — 지금 그대로의 당신, 미스터 나이틀리가 어느 날 갑자기 옮겨져 미스터 프랭크 처칠의 처지에 놓인다면, 당신은 방금 그분에게 권하신 말과 행동을 그대로 해낼 수 있겠죠. 그리고 효과도 아주 좋을 거예요. 처칠 집안은 한마디 대꾸도 못 할지 몰라요. 하지만 그건 당신에게는 떨쳐 내야 할, 어릴 적부터 몸에 밴 복종의 습관도, 오랜 순종의 관습도 없기 때문이죠. 그런 게 몸에 밴 사람으로서는, 단번에 완전한 독립으로 박차고 일어나 그들이 제 고마움과 존경에 거는 온갖 권리를 깡그리 무시한다는 게 그리 쉽지 않을지도 몰라요. 무엇이 옳은지에 대한 분별이야 그분도 당신 못지않게 강할지 모르지만, 특정한 상황에서 그것을 실행에 옮기는 일에서는 그만큼 대등하지 못할 수도 있는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그건 그만큼 강한 분별이 아니야. 똑같은 노력을 끌어내지 못한다면, 그건 똑같은 신념일 수 없으니까.\"\n\n\"아, 처지와 습관의 차이라니까요! 어릴 적부터 줄곧 우러러보며 자라 온 사람들을 정면으로 거스를 때, 마음씨 고운 젊은이가 어떤 심정일지를 좀 헤아려 보려고 애써 주셨으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"그 '마음씨 고운 젊은이'가 옳은 일을 하려는 결심을 남들의 뜻을 거스르며 끝까지 밀어붙이는 게 이번이 처음이라면, 그건 아주 나약한 젊은이야. 이쯤 됐으면 편의를 따지는 대신 제 도리를 좇는 일이 벌써 그에게 습관이 되었어야 마땅하지. 아이의 두려움이야 봐줄 수 있어도, 어른의 두려움은 봐줄 수 없어. 철이 들면서 그는 마땅히 분발하여, 그들 권위 속의 떳떳지 못한 것을 다 떨쳐 냈어야 해. 제 아버지를 가벼이 여기게 하려는 그들의 첫 시도에 맞섰어야 한다고. 마땅히 해야 할 대로 시작했더라면, 지금 와서 무슨 어려움이 있을 리 없지.\"\n\n\"그분에 대해서라면 우리는 영영 의견이 안 맞겠네요.\" 엠마가 소리쳤다. \"하지만 그건 별로 이상한 일도 아니죠. 저는 그분이 나약한 젊은이라곤 조금도 생각하지 않아요. 절대 아니라고 확신해요. 미스터 웨스턴이라면 제 아들이라 해도 어리석음에 눈멀지 않으실 거예요. 다만 그분은, 당신이 생각하시는 완벽한 남자상에 들어맞기보다는, 좀 더 무르고 순응적이고 온순한 성품일 공산이 크죠. 분명 그러리라 싶어요. 그리고 그런 성품 탓에 어떤 이점은 잃을지 몰라도, 그 대신 다른 많은 이점을 확실히 얻게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지. 움직여야 할 때 가만 앉아 있는 온갖 이점이며, 한낱 게으른 즐거움의 삶을 누리면서, 거기에 변명거리를 찾아내는 데 제가 더없이 능하다고 우쭐대는 이점까지 말이지. 그는 앉아서, 다짐과 거짓말로 가득한 번지르르한 편지나 한 통 써 내려가고는, 집안의 평화를 지키면서 아버지에게 불평할 빌미조차 주지 않을 세상에서 가장 좋은 방법을 자기가 찾아냈다고 스스로를 설득하겠지. 그자의 편지들은 정말 역겨워.\"\n\n\"느끼시는 바가 참 별나시네요. 다른 사람들은 다들 그 편지에 흡족해하는 것 같던데요.\"\n\n\"웨스턴 부인은 흡족하지 않으리라 짐작해. 그만한 양식과 예민한 감성을 지닌 여인이 흡족할 리 없지. 어머니 자리에 서 있으되, 눈을 멀게 할 어머니의 애정은 없는 사람이니까. 랜덜스에 대한 관심이 갑절로 마땅한 것은 바로 그녀 때문이고, 그녀로서는 그 소홀함을 갑절로 느낄 수밖에 없어. 그녀가 만약 대단한 신분의 사람이었다면, 그는 분명 왔을 거야. 그리고 그랬다면 그가 오든 말든 별 상관도 없었겠지. 자네 친구가 이런 종류의 생각에 뒤처져 있을 것 같나? 그녀가 이런 말을 마음속으로 수도 없이 되뇌지 않으리라 보나? 아니야, 엠마, 자네의 그 마음씨 고운 젊은이는 프랑스어로나 고울 뿐, 영어로는 곱지가 못해. 그는 무척 '상냥'하고, 예의범절도 아주 훌륭하고, 더없이 붙임성 있는 사람일지 몰라. 하지만 남의 감정을 헤아리는 영국식 섬세함이라곤 없어. 진정으로 마음씨 고운 구석이라곤 하나도 없는 사람이지.\"\n\n\"기어이 그분을 나쁘게 보기로 작정하셨군요.\"\n\n\"내가? 천만에.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 다소 언짢은 듯 대꾸했다. \"그를 나쁘게 보고 싶은 마음은 없어. 나도 누구 못지않게 기꺼이 그의 장점을 인정할 거야. 하지만 들리는 거라곤 한낱 외모에 관한 것뿐이지 — 체격 좋고 인물 훤하며, 매끄럽고 그럴듯한 몸가짐을 지녔다는 것 말고는.\"\n\n\"뭐, 달리 내세울 게 없다 해도, 그분은 하이베리에서 보배가 될 거예요. 우리가 잘 자라고 붙임성 있는 훤칠한 젊은이를 자주 보는 것도 아니잖아요. 그러니 까다롭게 굴면서 거기에 온갖 미덕까지 덤으로 갖추라고 요구해서는 안 되죠. 미스터 나이틀리, 그분이 오면 어떤 소동이 벌어질지 상상도 안 되세요? 돈웰과 하이베리 두 교구를 통틀어 화제는 오직 하나뿐일 거예요. 오직 하나의 관심사, 하나의 호기심의 대상 — 온통 미스터 프랭크 처칠 얘기뿐이겠죠. 다들 다른 사람은 생각도 입에 올리지도 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 그토록 압도당하지 못하는 건 양해해 주게. 말이 통하는 사람이라면 알고 지내게 되어 기쁘겠지만, 그저 재잘대는 멋쟁이 허풍선이에 불과하다면, 내 시간이나 생각을 그리 많이 차지하진 못할 거야.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 그분은 누구의 취향에든 맞춰 대화할 줄 알고, 또 모든 사람에게 두루 호감을 살 만한 능력과 더불어 그러고자 하는 마음까지 지닌 분이에요. 당신에게는 농사 얘기를, 저에게는 그림이나 음악 얘기를, 그런 식으로 누구에게든 맞춰 가면서요. 온갖 주제에 대한 두루뭉술한 지식을 갖춘 덕에, 자리에 어울리는 대로 남의 말을 따라가기도 하고 직접 이끌기도 하면서, 어느 화제든 아주 능란하게 풀어낼 수 있을 거예요. 그게 제가 그분을 그리는 모습이에요.\"\n\n\"내가 그리는 모습은 말이지,\" 미스터 나이틀리가 발끈하여 말했다. \"만약 그가 그 비슷한 사람으로 드러난다면, 살아 숨 쉬는 인간 가운데 가장 못 견딜 작자가 되리라는 거야! 아니, 스물셋에 좌중의 왕 노릇이라니 — 거물 행세에, 모두의 성품을 읽어 내고 모두의 재주를 끌어다 제 잘난 점을 돋보이게 하는 데 써먹는, 닳고 닳은 정객이라니 — 사방에 아첨을 뿌려 대며, 저 자신에 비하면 다들 바보로 보이게 만들다니! 엠마, 정작 그 자리에 닥치면 자네의 그 분별력으로도 그런 멋쟁이 풋내기를 결코 견디지 못할걸.\"\n\n\"그분 얘긴 더는 안 할래요.\" 엠마가 소리쳤다. \"당신은 뭐든 다 나쁘게만 돌리시니까요. 우리 둘 다 편견에 사로잡혀 있어요 — 당신은 그분을 반대하는 쪽으로, 저는 두둔하는 쪽으로요. 그러니 그분이 정말로 여기 오기 전까지는 의견이 맞을 가망이 없네요.\"\n\n\"편견이라니! 나는 편견 같은 거 없어.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 잔뜩 있어요. 그리고 조금도 부끄럽지 않고요. 미스터·미시즈 웨스턴을 사랑하는 마음 때문에, 저는 그분을 두둔하는 쪽으로 확실히 편견을 갖고 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 내가 한 달이 가고 또 한 달이 가도 도통 생각조차 안 하는 사람이야.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 어느 정도 짜증 섞인 투로 말했다. 그 바람에 엠마는 곧장 다른 화제로 말을 돌렸으나, 그가 왜 화를 내는지는 도무지 알 수 없었다.\n\n한 젊은이가 그저 자기와 기질이 다른 듯 보인다는 이유만으로 그를 못마땅해하는 것은, 그녀가 늘 그에게서 인정해 온 진정 너그러운 마음에 어울리지 않는 일이었다. 그녀가 종종 그를 두고 자부심이 지나치다고 탓해 오기는 했어도, 그 자부심이 그로 하여금 남의 장점에 부당해지게 할 수 있으리라고는 이제껏 단 한 순간도 생각해 본 적이 없었기 때문이다.\n\n# 제2권 1장\n\n엠마와 해리엇은 어느 날 아침 함께 산책을 했는데, 엠마가 보기에 그날 하루치로는 미스터 엘턴 이야기를 충분히 한 셈이었다. 해리엇의 위안에도 자신의 죄에도 그 이상은 필요치 않으리라 여긴 엠마는, 돌아오는 길 내내 부지런히 그 화제를 떨쳐 내려 애썼다. 그러나 성공했다 싶을 무렵 그것은 다시 불쑥 솟아올랐고, 겨울에 가난한 사람들이 얼마나 고생할지를 한참 이야기한 끝에 돌아온 대답이라고는 더없이 구슬픈 — \"미스터 엘턴은 가난한 사람들한테 어찌나 잘해 주는지!\" 한마디뿐이라, 엠마는 무언가 다른 수를 써야겠다고 깨달았다.\n\n마침 두 사람은 베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠가 사는 집 가까이에 이르러 있었다. 엠마는 그 집에 들러 머릿수로 안전을 도모하기로 마음먹었다. 그런 친절을 베풀 만한 명분이야 언제나 충분했다. 베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠는 방문받기를 좋아했고, 또 엠마는 자기에게서 결점을 볼 줄 아는 극소수의 사람들 사이에서 자기가 이 점에 다소 소홀하다는 평을 듣는다는 것을, 그들의 빈약한 위안거리에 마땅히 보태야 할 몫을 보태지 않는다는 평을 듣는다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n이 부족함에 대해서는 미스터 나이틀리에게서 여러 번 귀띔을 들었고 제 마음으로부터도 더러 들었지만 — 그 어느 것도, 그것이 몹시 거북한 일이라는 생각을, 시간 낭비요, 따분한 여자들이요, 그들을 끊임없이 찾아드는 하이베리의 이류 삼류 인사들과 어울릴 위험이라는 온갖 끔찍함을 이겨 낼 만큼은 못 되었다. 그래서 엠마는 좀처럼 그들 근처에 가지 않았다. 그러나 이번에는 문 앞을 그냥 지나치지 않고 들어가 보리라 불쑥 결심했고 — 해리엇에게 그 뜻을 밝히면서, 자기가 셈해 본 바로는 지금쯤이면 제인 페어팩스의 편지 따위는 안전하게 피할 수 있는 때라고 일렀다.\n\n그 집은 장사하는 사람들의 소유였다. 베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠는 응접실 층을 쓰고 있었다. 그곳, 그들에게는 전부나 다름없는 그 아주 아담한 방에서 두 손님은 더없이 다정하게, 심지어 고마워하는 마음으로까지 환대받았다. 가장 따뜻한 구석에 뜨개질감을 들고 앉아 있던 조용하고 단정한 노부인은 미스 우드하우스에게 자리까지 내주려 했고, 더 활동적이고 말 많은 딸은 미스 우드하우스 일행을 보살핌과 친절로 거의 압도할 지경이었다 — 방문해 주어 고맙다는 인사, 신발 걱정, 미스터 우드하우스의 건강을 묻는 근심 어린 물음, 어머니의 건강에 대한 명랑한 소식, 그리고 찬장에서 꺼낸 달콤한 케이크까지 — \"콜 부인이 방금 다녀가셨어요, 잠깐 십 분만 들르신다더니 어찌나 친절하신지 한 시간이나 함께 앉아 계셨지 뭐예요, 게다가 케이크도 한 조각 드시고는 어찌나 고맙게도 아주 맛있다고 말씀해 주시던지요, 그래서 미스 우드하우스랑 미스 스미스도 한 조각씩 들어 주시면 정말 고맙겠어요.\"\n\n콜 부부 이야기가 나오면 으레 미스터 엘턴 이야기가 뒤따르기 마련이었다. 두 집은 가깝게 지내는 사이였고, 미스터 콜은 미스터 엘턴이 떠난 뒤로 그에게서 소식을 들은 터였다. 엠마는 무슨 이야기가 나올지 알았다. 그 편지를 다시 한번 들어야 할 테고, 그가 떠난 지 얼마나 됐는지, 사교 모임에 얼마나 자주 어울리는지, 가는 곳마다 얼마나 인기인지, 무도회 주관자가 연 무도회가 얼마나 성황이었는지를 두루 짚어야 할 터였다. 엠마는 필요한 만큼의 관심과 칭찬을 다 갖추어 그 일을 아주 무난히 치러 냈고, 해리엇이 한마디라도 해야 하는 처지에 놓이지 않도록 늘 앞장서서 나섰다.\n\n이 정도는 집에 들어설 때부터 각오한 바였다. 그러나 일단 그를 한바탕 그럴듯하게 이야기하고 나면 그다음부터는 어떤 성가신 화제에도 더 시달리지 않고, 하이베리의 온갖 부인네들과 아가씨들과 그들의 카드 모임 사이를 마음껏 누비고 다닐 작정이었다. 미스터 엘턴의 뒤를 제인 페어팩스가 이어받으리라고는 미처 대비하지 못했다. 그런데 미스 베이츠가 정말로 그를 서둘러 밀어내고는, 마침내 그에게서 콜 부부 쪽으로 불쑥 건너뛰더니, 조카에게서 온 편지 이야기로 끌고 가는 것이었다.\n\n\"아! 그럼요 — 미스터 엘턴, 알겠어요 — 춤 이야기야 물론이고요 — 콜 부인이 그러시는데 바스의 무도회장에서 춤이 — 콜 부인이 어찌나 친절하신지 한참을 함께 앉아 제인 이야기를 나눠 주셨어요. 들어오시자마자 제인 안부부터 물으시던걸요, 거기서 제인이 어찌나 사랑받는지요. 제인이 우리랑 함께 있을 때면 콜 부인은 친절을 어떻게 다 베풀어야 할지 모르실 정도예요. 정말이지 제인은 누구 못지않게 그런 대접을 받을 자격이 있고말고요. 그래서 곧장 안부를 물으시면서 이러시지 뭐예요. '요즘 제인한테서 소식 못 들으셨겠죠, 편지 쓸 때가 아직 안 됐으니까요.' 그래서 제가 얼른 '아니에요, 정말 들었어요, 바로 오늘 아침에 편지가 왔답니다.' 했더니, 그렇게 놀라는 사람을 제가 본 적이 있나 싶어요. '정말로요? 맹세코요?' 하시면서 '어머, 그거 정말 뜻밖이네요. 어디 뭐라고 썼는지 좀 들려주세요.' 하시는 거예요.\"\n\n엠마는 곧바로 예의를 차려, 미소 띤 관심으로 말했다 —\n\n\"미스 페어팩스한테서 그렇게 최근에 소식을 들으셨어요? 정말 기쁘네요. 그분이 건강하시면 좋겠어요?\"\n\n\"고마워요. 어쩜 그렇게 친절하신지!\" 행복하게 속아 넘어간 이모는 부지런히 편지를 찾으면서 대답했다. \"아! 여기 있네요. 멀리 갈 리가 없다 했지요, 그런데 글쎄 나도 모르게 반짇고리를 그 위에 올려놓는 바람에 그만 가려져 버렸지 뭐예요, 하지만 조금 전까지 손에 들고 있었으니 분명 탁자 위 어디 있겠거니 했지요. 콜 부인한테 읽어 드리고 있었고, 그분이 가신 뒤로는 어머니께 다시 읽어 드리던 참이었어요, 어머니는 제인 편지를 어찌나 좋아하시는지 — 아무리 들어도 또 듣고 싶어 하시거든요. 그러니 멀리 갈 리 없다 했죠, 자 여기, 바로 반짇고리 밑에 있었네요 — 그리고 이렇게 친절하게도 뭐라고 썼는지 듣고 싶어 하시니까요 — 하지만 무엇보다 먼저, 제인을 위해 공정을 기하자면, 이렇게 짧은 편지를 쓴 것을 정말 사과드려야겠어요 — 보세요, 겨우 두 장이에요 — 두 장도 안 되네요 — 평소엔 종이를 가득 채우고 가로질러 또 절반을 채우는 아이거든요. 어머니는 내가 그걸 어쩜 그렇게 잘 읽어 내는지 자주 신기해하세요. 편지를 막 펼치면 으레 이러시지요. '얘, 헤티야, 이 격자무늬 글씨를 다 읽어 내려면 이번엔 좀 애먹겠구나.' — 그렇죠, 어머니? — 그러면 제가 그러죠, 대신 읽어 줄 사람이 아무도 없으면 어머니 혼자서도 분명 어떻게든 읽어 내실 거라고요 — 한 자도 빠짐없이요 — 한 자 한 자 다 읽어 낼 때까지 들여다보고 또 들여다보실 게 분명해요. 그리고 정말이지, 어머니 눈이 예전만은 못해도 아직 신기할 만큼 잘 보세요, 다행스럽게도요! 안경의 도움을 받으면요. 이런 복이 어디 있어요! 우리 어머니 눈은 정말 아주 좋으세요. 제인이 여기 있을 때면 자주 이래요. '할머니, 그렇게 잘 보시는 걸 보면 분명 아주 좋은 눈을 타고나셨나 봐요 — 게다가 그 고운 바느질은 또 얼마나 많이 하셨고요! — 제 눈도 그렇게 오래 버텨 주기만 바랄 뿐이에요.'\"\n\n이 모든 말을 어찌나 빠르게 쏟아냈는지 미스 베이츠는 숨을 돌리려고 멈춰야 했다. 엠마는 미스 페어팩스의 빼어난 필체에 대해 무척 정중한 말을 한마디 건넸다.\n\n\"정말 친절하시네요.\" 미스 베이츠가 몹시 흐뭇해하며 대답했다. \"그런 걸 보실 줄 아는 분이, 본인도 그렇게 아름답게 쓰시는 분이 말이에요. 정말이지 미스 우드하우스의 칭찬만큼 우리를 기쁘게 해 줄 칭찬은 어디에도 없을 거예요. 어머니는 못 들으세요, 아시다시피 귀가 좀 어두우셔서요. 어머니,\" 노부인을 향해 돌아서며, \"미스 우드하우스가 제인 글씨에 대해서 이렇게 고맙게 해 주시는 말씀, 들리세요?\"\n\n그리하여 엠마는 그 착한 노부인이 알아듣기까지 자신의 어리석은 칭찬이 두 번이나 거듭되는 것을 들어야 하는 처지에 놓였다. 그러는 동안 엠마는, 너무 무례해 보이지 않으면서 제인 페어팩스의 편지에서 빠져나갈 방법이 없을까 궁리하다가, 적당한 핑계를 대고 당장 서둘러 자리를 뜨기로 거의 마음을 굳히던 참이었는데, 미스 베이츠가 다시 그녀를 향해 돌아서며 주의를 사로잡았다.\n\n\"우리 어머니 귀 어두운 건 정말 별것 아니에요, 보시다시피요 — 거의 아무것도 아닌걸요. 목소리만 좀 높이고 무슨 말이든 두세 번만 되풀이하면 분명 들으세요. 하긴 제 목소리에 익으셨으니까요. 그런데 참 신기한 게, 늘 저보다 제인 말을 더 잘 들으신다는 거예요. 제인은 발음이 어찌나 또렷한지요! 어쨌든 제인은 할머니가 이 년 전보다 조금도 더 귀가 어두워지지 않았다는 걸 알게 될 거예요. 어머니 연세에 그건 정말 대단한 거지요 — 그러고 보니 제인이 여기 다녀간 지가 정말 꼬박 이 년이 됐네요. 전에는 이렇게 오래 못 본 적이 없었는데, 콜 부인한테도 말했지만, 이제 그 애를 어떻게 대접해야 충분할지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"미스 페어팩스가 곧 여기로 오나요?\"\n\n\"아 그럼요, 다음 주에요.\"\n\n\"정말요! 그거 참 큰 기쁨이시겠어요.\"\n\n\"고마워요. 정말 친절하시네요. 그래요, 다음 주예요. 다들 어찌나 놀라는지요, 그리고 다들 똑같이 고마운 말씀들을 해 주세요. 분명 그 애도 하이베리의 친구들을 보고 그들이 그 애를 보는 만큼이나 기뻐할 거예요. 그래요, 금요일이나 토요일이에요. 그 애도 어느 쪽인지는 못 정한대요, 그 며칠 중 하루는 캠벨 대령이 마차를 직접 쓰셔야 하거든요. 그 먼 길을 줄곧 마차로 보내 주시다니 어찌나 고마운지요! 하지만 늘 그렇게 해 주신답니다, 아시잖아요. 아 그럼요, 다음 주 금요일이나 토요일이에요. 그 애가 그 이야기를 쓴 거예요. 그게 바로, 우리가 흔히 말하듯, 그 애가 정해진 때를 벗어나서 편지를 쓴 까닭이지요. 평소대로라면 다음 주 화요일이나 수요일이나 돼야 소식을 들었을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 저도 그렇게 짐작했어요. 오늘 미스 페어팩스 소식을 들을 가망은 거의 없겠거니 했거든요.\"\n\n\"어쩜 그렇게 다정하신지! 맞아요, 이렇게 곧 온다는 특별한 사정만 아니었으면 우리도 못 들었을 거예요. 어머니가 어찌나 좋아하시는지요! — 적어도 석 달은 우리랑 함께 지낼 거니까요. 석 달이요, 그 애가 분명히 그렇게 썼어요, 제가 이제 그 기쁜 편지를 읽어 드릴 참인걸요. 사정인즉슨 이래요, 캠벨 대령 식구가 아일랜드로 가게 됐거든요. 딕슨 부인이 부모님더러 당장 건너와서 보고 가시라고 졸랐대요. 본래는 여름이나 돼야 건너갈 작정이셨는데, 그 애가 부모님을 다시 보고 싶어서 어찌나 안달인지요 — 작년 시월에 결혼하기 전까지는 단 일주일도 부모님과 떨어져 본 적이 없는 애라, 서로 다른 왕국에, 라고 할 뻔했네요, 하여튼 서로 다른 나라에 있다는 게 무척 낯설 거예요. 그래서 어머니께 — 아니 아버지께였나, 정말이지 어느 쪽이었는지 모르겠지만 곧 제인 편지에서 알게 되겠지요 — 아주 다급한 편지를 써 보냈대요, 미스터 딕슨 이름까지 함께 넣어서, 당장 건너오시라고요, 그러면 더블린에서 마중을 나가 자기들 시골 저택인 발리크레이그로 모셔 가겠다고요, 아주 아름다운 곳인가 봐요. 제인이 그 아름다움에 대해 많이 들었거든요. 미스터 딕슨한테서 말이에요 — 다른 누구한테서 들었다는 얘기는 못 들었어요. 하지만 당연한 일이죠, 구애하는 동안 자기 고향 이야기를 하고 싶었을 테니까요 — 그리고 제인이 그들과 자주 함께 산책을 다니곤 했거든요 — 캠벨 대령 내외분이 따님이 미스터 딕슨하고 단둘이서만 자주 산책 나가는 걸 무척 조심하셨으니까요, 그건 제가 조금도 탓할 일이 아니지요. 그러니 미스터 딕슨이 미스 캠벨한테 아일랜드의 제 고향 이야기를 들려줄 때면 제인도 죄다 듣게 마련이었던 거예요. 그러고 보니 제인이 우리한테 써 보냈던 것 같아요, 그분이 그곳 그림을 몇 점 보여 줬다고요, 손수 그린 경치들을요. 정말 더없이 상냥하고 매력적인 청년인가 봐요. 제인은 그분 이야기를 듣고 아일랜드에 어찌나 가고 싶어 했는지 몰라요.\"\n\n이 순간, 제인 페어팩스와 이 매력적인 미스터 딕슨과 아일랜드에 가지 않는 일을 두고 묘하게 사람을 들뜨게 하는 의심이 엠마의 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 그래서 그녀는 좀 더 캐내려는 음흉한 속셈으로 말했다.\n\n\"미스 페어팩스가 하필 이런 때에 여기 오게 됐으니 정말 다행이라고 여기시겠어요. 그분과 딕슨 부인 사이가 그토록 각별한 우정인데, 캠벨 대령 내외분을 따라가지 않아도 된다는 건 좀처럼 기대하기 어려웠을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"정말 맞아요, 정말 맞는 말씀이에요. 바로 그게 우리가 늘 좀 겁내던 일이거든요. 몇 달씩이나 그렇게 먼 데 그 애를 두고 싶지 않았으니까요 — 무슨 일이 생겨도 올 수 없을 테니 말이에요. 그런데 보세요, 모든 게 다 잘 풀리는걸요. 그쪽에서도 (미스터 딕슨 내외분 말이에요) 캠벨 대령 내외분과 함께 건너오라고 그 애한테 어찌나 간곡히 청했는지요, 그건 정말 틀림없어요. 그 양쪽이 함께 보낸 초대만큼 친절하고 간곡한 건 없을 거라고 제인이 그러는데, 이제 곧 들으시게 될 거예요. 미스터 딕슨은 어떤 마음 씀씀이에도 조금도 인색한 분이 아닌가 봐요. 정말 더없이 매력적인 청년이에요. 웨이머스에서 제인한테 베푼 그 일 이후로 줄곧요, 그 뱃놀이 모임에 나갔을 때 말이에요, 돛대 사이에서 뭔가가 갑자기 휙 돌아오는 바람에 제인이 하마터면 그대로 바다에 처박힐 뻔했거든요, 정말 거의 빠질 뻔했는데, 그분이 더없이 침착하게 그 애 옷자락을 붙잡지 않았더라면요 — (생각만 해도 늘 몸서리가 쳐져요!) — 그날 이야기를 들은 뒤로 저는 미스터 딕슨이 어찌나 좋은지 몰라요!\"\n\n\"하지만 친구들이 그토록 권하고 본인도 아일랜드를 보고 싶어 했는데도, 미스 페어팩스는 그 시간을 댁과 베이츠 부인께 바치는 쪽을 택한 거군요?\"\n\n\"네 — 온전히 그 애 뜻이고, 온전히 그 애 선택이에요. 캠벨 대령 내외분도 그 애가 아주 잘하는 거라고, 바로 당신들이 권하고 싶었던 거라고 여기세요. 게다가 두 분이 그 애더러 고향 공기를 꼭 좀 쐬어 보라고 특별히 바라셨거든요, 요즘 그 애가 평소만큼 건강하지가 못해서요.\"\n\n\"그 말씀을 들으니 걱정이 되네요. 두 분이 현명하게 판단하신 것 같아요. 하지만 딕슨 부인은 무척 서운하시겠어요. 제가 알기로 딕슨 부인은 특별히 빼어난 미모를 가진 분은 아니지요. 도저히 미스 페어팩스에 견줄 바가 못 되고요.\"\n\n\"아! 그럼요. 그런 말씀까지 해 주시다니 정말 다정도 하셔라 — 하지만 분명 아니지요. 두 사람을 견준다는 건 말이 안 돼요. 미스 캠벨은 늘 정말이지 평범했지요 — 그래도 더없이 우아하고 상냥했고요.\"\n\n\"네, 그야 물론이죠.\"\n\n\"제인이 그만 지독한 감기에 걸렸지 뭐예요, 가엾어라! 벌써 십일월 칠일에요 (이제 읽어 드릴게요), 그 뒤로 영 낫질 않았대요. 감기가 그렇게 오래 붙어 있다니 너무하지 않아요? 우리를 걱정시키지 않으려고 그동안 한 번도 말을 안 했던 거예요. 꼭 그 애답죠! 어쩜 그렇게 사려 깊은지요! — 하지만 어쨌든 영 낫질 않으니까, 그 친절한 친구분들 캠벨 대령 내외분이 그 애더러 집에 돌아와 늘 잘 맞던 공기를 쐬는 게 좋겠다고 여기신 거예요. 하이베리에서 서너 달 지내면 말끔히 나으리라고 두 분도 믿어 의심치 않으세요 — 몸이 안 좋다면야 아일랜드로 가는 것보다 이리로 오는 편이 확실히 훨씬 나은 거고요. 우리만큼 그 애를 간호해 줄 사람은 아무 데도 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"제가 보기에도 이만큼 더 바랄 나위 없는 일이 없네요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 애가 다음 주 금요일이나 토요일에 우리한테 오고, 캠벨 대령 내외분은 그다음 월요일에 홀리헤드로 가시는 길에 런던을 떠나신대요 — 제인 편지를 보시면 다 나와 있어요. 어찌나 갑작스러운지요! 미스 우드하우스, 제가 얼마나 부산스러워졌는지 짐작이 가시지요! 그 애 병만 아니라면야 — 하지만 아무래도 그 애가 야위고 안색이 무척 안 좋아진 걸 보게 될 각오를 해야 할 것 같아요. 그것 때문에 제가 얼마나 운 나쁜 일을 겪었는지 말씀드려야겠어요. 제가 늘 어머니께 큰 소리로 읽어 드리기 전에 먼저 혼자서 제인 편지를 끝까지 읽어 보거든요, 아시다시피요, 혹시 어머니를 속상하게 할 만한 게 있을까 봐서요. 제인이 그렇게 해 달라고 부탁해서 늘 그렇게 한답니다. 그래서 오늘도 평소대로 조심해서 읽기 시작했는데, 그 애가 몸이 안 좋다는 대목에 이르자마자 그만 깜짝 놀라서 '어머나! 가엾은 제인이 아프구나!' 하고 불쑥 소리치고 만 거예요 — 그걸 마침 귀를 기울이고 계시던 어머니가 또렷이 들으시고는 그만 몹시 놀라셨지 뭐예요. 그래도 계속 읽어 보니 처음 생각만큼 그렇게 나쁘지가 않더라고요. 그래서 지금은 어머니께 아주 가볍게 말씀드려서 별로 마음에 두지 않으세요. 그런데 제가 어쩜 그렇게 방심할 수 있었는지 모르겠어요. 제인이 곧 낫지 않으면 미스터 페리를 부를 거예요. 비용은 따질 게 아니지요. 그분이 워낙 후하시고 제인을 어찌나 아끼시는지 진찰비 같은 건 한 푼도 안 받으려 하시겠지만, 그래도 우리가 그렇게 둘 수야 없지요, 아시잖아요. 그분도 부양할 처자식이 있으니, 시간을 그저 거저 내주실 수는 없는 노릇이에요. 자, 이제 제인이 무슨 이야기를 썼는지 대강 귀띔해 드렸으니, 그 애 편지로 돌아가 볼까요, 분명 그 애가 제가 대신 들려 드리는 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 제 이야기를 풀어 놨을 거예요.\"\n\n\"아무래도 저희는 그만 가 봐야겠어요.\" 엠마가 해리엇을 흘긋 보고는 일어서려 하며 말했다. \"아버지가 저희를 기다리실 거예요. 처음 이 집에 들어설 때만 해도 오 분 넘게 머물 생각은, 아니 그럴 여유가 없는 줄 알았거든요. 그저 베이츠 부인 안부도 안 여쭙고 문 앞을 지날 수가 없어서 들른 것뿐인데, 이렇게 즐겁게 붙들리고 말았네요! 하지만 이제 정말로 댁과 베이츠 부인께 작별 인사를 드려야겠어요.\"\n\n그리고 그녀를 붙들려는 온갖 권유도 끝내 통하지 않았다. 엠마는 거리로 다시 나섰다 — 비록 많은 것을 본의 아니게 떠안았고, 실은 제인 페어팩스 편지의 내용을 통째로 다 들어 버린 셈이었지만, 그래도 편지 그 자체만은 면할 수 있었다는 것이 다행스러웠다.\n\n# 제2권 2장\n\n제인 페어팩스는 고아였으니, 베이츠 부인의 막내딸이 남긴 외동딸이었다.\n\n보병 ——연대 페어팩스 중위와 미스 제인 베이츠의 혼인은 한때 자랑스럽고 기쁘고 희망과 관심으로 가득 찬 시절을 누렸으나, 이제 그 가운데 남은 것이라곤 아무것도 없었다 — 그가 머나먼 곳 전장에서 목숨을 잃었다는 서글픈 기억과, 얼마 지나지 않아 그 미망인이 폐병과 비탄에 시달리다 스러졌다는 기억과, 그리고 이 아이뿐이었다.\n\n태생으로 따지면 그 아이는 하이베리 사람이었다. 세 살에 어머니를 여의고 외할머니와 이모의 소유물이자 짐이자 위안이자 거두어들인 고아가 되었을 때, 그 아이가 이곳에 영영 붙박여 자랄 공산이 매우 커 보였다. 지극히 빠듯한 형편이 허락하는 것만 겨우 배우고, 인맥이나 향상의 이점이라곤 하나 없이 자라나, 자연이 그 아이에게 베푼 것 — 보기 좋은 용모와 좋은 이해력, 그리고 따뜻한 마음씨에 선의를 지닌 친척들 — 위에 그저 접붙여지듯 자랄 듯싶었다.\n\n그러나 그 아이 아버지의 한 벗이 품은 측은한 마음이 그 운명에 변화를 가져왔다. 그가 바로 캠벨 대령이었다. 그는 페어팩스를 빼어난 장교이자 더없이 훌륭한 청년으로 매우 높이 샀으며, 더 나아가 심한 진중 열병을 앓을 때 그에게서 받은 보살핌으로 목숨을 건졌다고 믿고 있었다. 그것은 그가 모른 척 넘길 수 없는 은혜였으니, 가엾은 페어팩스가 세상을 떠난 뒤 그가 영국으로 돌아와 무언가를 할 수 있게 되기까지 몇 해가 흘렀어도 마찬가지였다. 마침내 돌아오자 그는 그 아이를 찾아내 보살피기 시작했다. 그는 기혼자였으되, 살아 있는 자식이라곤 제인과 비슷한 또래의 딸 하나뿐이었다. 그리하여 제인은 그 집안의 손님이 되어 오래도록 머물며 모두의 귀염둥이로 자라났다. 그 아이가 아홉 살이 되기도 전에, 그 딸이 제인을 끔찍이 따르는 마음과 그 자신이 참된 벗이 되어 주고자 하는 바람이 한데 모여, 캠벨 대령이 그 아이의 교육 일체를 맡겠노라는 제안을 내놓기에 이르렀다. 그 제안은 받아들여졌고, 그때부터 제인은 캠벨 대령 집안 사람이 되어 오롯이 그들과 함께 살았으며, 외할머니는 이따금 찾아뵙는 데 그쳤다.\n\n계획인즉 그 아이를 남을 가르치는 사람으로 키운다는 것이었다. 아버지에게서 물려받은 몇백 파운드라는 보잘것없는 재산으로는 독립적인 생계가 도무지 불가능했기 때문이다. 그 아이를 달리 거두어 줄 형편은 캠벨 대령에게도 없었다. 봉급과 직책에서 나오는 수입은 넉넉했으나, 그의 재산은 그저 그만한 정도여서 응당 모두 딸의 몫이 되어야 했던 것이다. 다만 교육을 베풂으로써, 그는 그 아이가 훗날 떳떳한 생계를 꾸릴 방편을 마련해 주는 셈이 되리라 바랐다.\n\n이것이 제인 페어팩스의 내력이었다. 그 아이는 좋은 손에 맡겨져 캠벨 집안에게서 친절밖에는 모른 채 자랐으며, 더없이 훌륭한 교육을 받았다. 늘 올바른 마음과 풍부한 식견을 갖춘 사람들과 함께 살았기에, 그 아이의 마음과 이해력은 도야와 교양의 온갖 이점을 받았다. 또 캠벨 대령의 거처가 런던에 있은 덕에, 일류 선생들의 가르침으로 가벼운 재주 하나하나까지도 마음껏 갈고닦을 수 있었다. 그 아이의 성품과 재능은 우정이 베풀 수 있는 모든 것을 받기에 부족함이 없었다. 그리하여 열여덟이나 열아홉 무렵에는, 그토록 이른 나이에 아이들을 맡을 자격을 갖출 수 있는 한도에서, 스스로 교육의 직무를 능히 감당할 만했다. 그러나 그 아이는 너무도 사랑받았기에 떠나보낼 수가 없었다. 아버지도 어머니도 그 일을 거들 수 없었고, 딸도 그것을 견디지 못했다. 흉한 날은 미뤄졌다. 아직 너무 어리다고 정하기란 쉬운 일이었다. 그리하여 제인은 그들과 함께 머물며, 또 하나의 딸로서 우아한 사교계의 온갖 합리적인 즐거움과 가정과 오락의 분별 있는 어우러짐을 나누었으니, 다만 미래라는 한 가지 걸림돌만이, 이 모든 것이 머잖아 끝날지 모른다는 사실을 일깨우는 그 자신의 좋은 이해력이 던지는 진중한 암시만이 그늘로 남아 있었다.\n\n집안 전체의 애정, 그중에서도 특히 미스 캠벨의 따뜻한 정은, 제인이 미모에서나 소양에서나 뚜렷이 더 뛰어났다는 사정으로 인해 양쪽 모두에게 한층 더 명예로운 것이 되었다. 자연이 용모에 베푼 그 차이를 그 젊은 아가씨가 보지 못할 리 없었고, 제인의 더 뛰어난 지력 또한 그 부모가 느끼지 못할 리 없었다. 그럼에도 그들은 한결같은 애정으로 함께 지냈으니, 미스 캠벨이 결혼하기까지 그러했다. 미스 캠벨은 — 혼사에서 흔히 예측을 비웃으며, 빼어난 쪽보다 그저 그만한 쪽에 매력을 부여하곤 하는 그런 우연, 그런 행운 덕분에 — 알게 된 지 거의 얼마 되지도 않아 미스터 딕슨이라는, 부유하고 호감 가는 청년의 마음을 사로잡았으며, 어울리고도 행복한 자리에 정착하게 되었다. 반면 제인 페어팩스는 아직 제 빵벌이를 해야 할 처지였다.\n\n이 일은 아주 최근에 일어났으니, 그 덜 운 좋은 벗이 제 의무의 길로 들어서기 위한 어떤 시도라도 하기에는 너무도 최근이었다. 비록 이제는 그 자신의 판단이 시작점으로 정해 둔 나이에 다다랐는데도 말이다. 그 아이는 오래전부터 스물한 살을 그 시점으로 삼겠다고 결심해 두었다. 헌신적인 수련 수녀의 굳센 마음으로, 스물한 살이 되면 그 희생을 완수하고, 삶의 온갖 즐거움 — 합리적인 교제, 대등한 사교, 평안과 희망 — 에서 물러나 영영 고행과 극기의 길로 들어서겠노라 마음먹었던 것이다.\n\n캠벨 대령 부부의 분별로는 그런 결심에 반대할 수가 없었으나, 그들의 정으로는 반대했다. 그들이 살아 있는 한 그 아이가 애쓸 필요는 없을 것이요, 그들의 집은 영영 그 아이의 집이 되어 줄 수 있었다. 또 그들 자신의 편안함만 따지자면 그 아이를 온전히 곁에 두고 싶었을 터이다. 그러나 그것은 이기심일 것이었다 — 결국 닥칠 일이라면 차라리 빨리 닥치는 편이 나았다. 어쩌면 그들은, 어떤 지체의 유혹에도 맞서, 이제는 단념해야 할 그 안락과 여유의 즐거움을 그 아이가 맛보지 않도록 막아 주는 편이 더 다정하고 현명했으리라 느끼기 시작했는지도 모른다. 그래도 애정은 그 비참한 순간을 서둘러 앞당기지 않을 만한 그럴듯한 구실이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 붙잡으려 했다. 그 아이는 그들 딸이 결혼한 이래로 도통 몸이 온전치 못했다. 그러니 평소의 기력을 완전히 되찾기 전까지는, 그 아이가 의무를 떠맡는 것을 금해야만 했다. 그런 의무란 약해진 몸과 들쭉날쭉한 기분에 도무지 어울리지 않을뿐더러, 아무리 좋은 여건에서라도, 그럭저럭 견딜 만하게 해내려면 몸과 마음의 인간을 넘어선 완벽함 이상의 무언가가 필요해 보였던 것이다.\n\n자신이 그들을 따라 아일랜드에 가지 않은 일에 대해 이모에게 들려준 설명에는 진실밖에 없었으되, 다만 말하지 않은 진실이 몇 가지 있었을 수는 있다. 그들이 떠나 있는 동안을 하이베리에서 보내겠다는 것은 그 아이 자신의 선택이었다. 어쩌면 그토록 자기를 아껴 주는 그 다정한 친척들과 함께, 완전한 자유의 마지막 몇 달을 보내겠다는 것이었으리라. 그리고 캠벨 부부는 — 그 동기가 무엇이었든, 하나였든 둘이었든 셋이었든 — 그 결정을 흔쾌히 승낙하며, 그 아이의 건강 회복을 위해서라면 다른 무엇보다도 고향 땅의 공기 속에서 보내는 몇 달에 더 기대를 건다고 말했다. 어쨌든 분명한 것은 그 아이가 온다는 사실이었으며, 또 하이베리가 그토록 오래 약속받아 온 그 완전한 새 인물 — 미스터 프랭크 처칠 — 을 맞이하는 대신, 당분간은 겨우 이태 동안 떠나 있었던 새로움밖에는 가져다줄 게 없는 제인 페어팩스로 만족해야 한다는 사실이었다.\n\n엠마는 서운했다 — 마음에 들지도 않는 사람에게 긴긴 석 달 내내 예의를 차려야 하다니! 늘 바라는 것보다는 많이, 마땅히 해야 할 것보다는 적게 하게 될 처지라니! 어째서 그녀가 제인 페어팩스를 좋아하지 않는가는 대답하기 어려운 물음일지도 몰랐다. 언젠가 미스터 나이틀리는, 그녀가 제인에게서 정작 자기가 그렇게 여겨지길 바라는 바로 그 진정으로 소양 갖춘 젊은 아가씨의 모습을 보기 때문이라고 말한 적이 있었다. 그 비난을 그때는 한사코 반박하긴 했어도, 스스로를 돌아볼 때면 양심이 그녀를 온전히 무죄로 놓아주지 못하는 순간들이 있었다. 그러나 \"그 사람과는 도무지 친해질 수가 없었어. 어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠지만, 어찌나 쌀쌀맞고 새침한지 — 제가 호감을 사든 말든 도통 관심 없다는 듯한 그 태도 — 게다가 그 이모는 끝도 없이 떠들어 대는 사람이고! — 또 다들 그 사람을 두고 어찌나 야단법석인지! — 게다가 두 사람이 으레 그렇게 친해지리라고들 처음부터 멋대로 짐작했단 말이야 — 나이가 같다는 이유로, 다들 둘이 서로 끔찍이 좋아하게 마련이라고 넘겨짚은 거지.\" 이것이 그녀의 이유였다 — 이보다 나은 것은 없었다.\n\n이는 너무도 부당한 반감이어서 — 떠넘긴 흠 하나하나가 공상으로 부풀려진 탓에, 어지간히 오래 못 만났다가 처음 제인 페어팩스를 볼 때면 그녀는 으레 자기가 그 사람에게 못할 짓을 했다는 느낌을 받지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 이제, 이태 만에 도착한 제인에게 응당 해야 할 방문을 마쳤을 때, 그녀는 지난 이태 내내 깎아내려 온 바로 그 외양과 태도에 유독 강하게 마음이 끌렸다. 제인 페어팩스는 무척 우아했다, 놀라우리만치 우아했다. 그리고 그녀 자신도 우아함을 더없이 높이 사는 사람이었다. 키는 보기 좋아서, 거의 누구나 훤칠하다 여기되 아무도 너무 크다고는 못할 만한 딱 그만한 키였다. 몸매는 유달리 우아했고, 풍채는 살찐 쪽과 마른 쪽 사이의 더없이 알맞은 중간이었으니, 다만 어딘가 살짝 병약해 보이는 기색이 그 둘 중에서 더 있을 법한 화근을 짚어 주는 듯했다. 엠마는 이 모든 것을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 얼굴 — 그 이목구비 — 거기엔 그녀가 기억하던 것보다 한결 더 많은 아름다움이 깃들어 있었다. 반듯한 미모는 아니었으되 무척 호감 가는 아름다움이었다. 짙은 회색 눈동자에 검은 속눈썹과 눈썹은 일찍이 한 번도 칭찬을 받지 못한 적이 없었다. 그리고 그녀가 혈색이 없다며 늘 트집 잡곤 했던 그 피부는, 더 짙은 화색이라곤 정말이지 필요 없을 만큼 맑고 고왔다. 그것은 우아함을 으뜸가는 특색으로 삼는 종류의 아름다움이었으니, 그런 이상 그녀는 명예를 걸고, 제 모든 신조에 비추어, 그것을 찬탄하지 않을 수 없었다 — 우아함이라니, 몸의 것이든 마음의 것이든, 하이베리에서는 좀처럼 보기 힘든 것이었다. 그곳에서는 속되지 않다는 것 자체가 곧 품격이요 미덕이었다.\n\n요컨대 그 첫 방문 내내 그녀는 두 겹의 흡족함으로 — 즐거움의 감정과 공정을 베푸는 감정으로 — 제인 페어팩스를 바라보며 앉아 있었고, 다시는 그 사람을 미워하지 않으리라 마음먹고 있었다. 실로 그녀의 내력을, 그 처지를, 그 미모와 더불어 헤아려 보았을 때 — 이 모든 우아함이 어떤 운명으로 정해져 있는지, 그녀가 어떤 자리에서 굴러떨어지려는 참인지, 앞으로 어떻게 살아가게 될 것인지를 생각하니, 측은함과 존경 말고는 어떤 감정도 품을 수 없을 것만 같았다. 더구나 그 사람의 관심을 살 만한 익히 알려진 사정 하나하나에다, 그녀가 그토록 자연스럽게 스스로 떠올린 바, 미스터 딕슨을 향한 연정이라는 매우 그럴듯한 정황까지 보태진다면 더욱 그러했다. 그렇다면야 그 아이가 결심한 희생만큼 가엾고도 명예로운 것은 다시없을 터였다. 이제 엠마는 그 아이가 미스터 딕슨의 마음을 그 아내에게서 빼앗았다든가, 처음에 자기 상상이 들이밀었던 어떤 못된 짓을 저질렀다든가 하는 혐의를 기꺼이 벗겨 줄 마음이 들었다. 만일 그것이 사랑이었다면, 한쪽만의, 단순하고 외곬이며 이루어지지 못한 사랑이었으리라. 어쩌면 그 아이는 제 벗과 그가 나누는 대화에 함께 끼어 있는 동안, 저도 모르게 그 슬픈 독을 빨아들이고 있었는지도 모른다. 그리고 가장 선하고 가장 순수한 동기에서, 이제 아일랜드행을 스스로 마다하고, 머잖아 고된 의무의 길에 나섬으로써 그와 그의 인연으로부터 자신을 효과적으로 떼어 놓기로 결심한 것인지도 모른다.\n\n대체로 엠마는 그토록 누그러지고 너그러워진 마음으로 그 자리를 떠났던 터라, 집으로 걸어오는 길에 둘레를 두루 돌아보며 한탄하기까지 했다 — 하이베리에는 그 아이에게 독립을 안겨 줄 만한 청년이 하나도 없다고, 그 아이를 위해 궁리를 짜내 보고 싶은 사람이 하나도 없다고.\n\n이것은 사랑스러운 감정이었다 — 그러나 오래가지는 못했다. 제인 페어팩스를 향한 영원한 우정을 공공연히 선언하여 스스로를 옭아매기도 전에, 또 지난 편견과 오류를 철회하는 일이라곤 미스터 나이틀리에게 \"그 사람은 정말 어여뻐요. 어여쁜 것 그 이상이고요!\"라고 말한 것 말고는 더 한 일도 없는 사이에, 제인은 외할머니와 이모와 함께 하트필드에서 하룻저녁을 보냈고, 모든 것이 도로 예전 상태로 되돌아가고 말았다. 지난날의 거슬리던 점들이 다시금 고개를 들었다. 이모는 여전히 진력나는 사람이었다 — 아니, 한층 더 진력났으니, 이제는 조카의 재능에 대한 찬탄에다 조카의 건강에 대한 걱정까지 보태졌기 때문이다. 그리하여 사람들은, 조카가 아침으로 버터 바른 빵을 얼마나 조금밖에 안 먹는지, 저녁으로 양고기를 얼마나 얇게 한 점만 드는지에 대한 설명을 들어 주어야 했고, 더불어 어머니와 자신을 위해 만든 새 모자며 새 반짇주머니를 구경해 주어야 했다. 그리고 제인의 죄목들이 다시 솟아났다. 음악을 했다. 엠마는 어쩔 수 없이 연주를 했고, 으레 뒤따른 감사와 칭찬은 그녀에게는 꾸며 낸 진솔함이요 거드름 피우는 태도로만 비쳤으니, 다만 자기 자신의 한결 빼어난 연주를 한층 고상한 투로 뽐내려는 속셈으로만 보였다. 게다가 무엇보다 고약했던 것은, 그 사람이 어찌나 쌀쌀맞고 어찌나 조심스러운지! 도무지 그 사람의 진짜 속내에 다가갈 길이 없었다. 예의라는 외투를 칭칭 두른 채, 무엇 하나 함부로 내비치지 않기로 작정한 듯했다. 진저리가 나도록, 의심이 들도록 새침했다.\n\n온통 더없이 그런 가운데서도 더 그럴 수 있는 무언가가 있었다면, 그 사람은 웨이머스와 딕슨 부부에 관한 화제에서 무엇보다도 더 새침했다. 미스터 딕슨의 성품에 대해서도, 또 그와 함께 지내는 것을 제가 얼마나 소중히 여기는지에 대해서도, 그 혼사가 얼마나 어울리는지에 대한 견해에 대해서도, 진짜 속을 단 한 줌도 내비치지 않으려 작정한 듯했다. 죄다 두루뭉술한 찬동이요 매끄러운 말뿐, 또렷이 그려 내거나 가려내는 것이라곤 없었다. 그러나 그래 봐야 그 사람에게 득이 될 것은 없었다. 그 조심스러움은 헛수고였다. 엠마는 그 술수를 알아챘고, 처음의 짐작으로 되돌아갔다. 거기엔 십중팔구 제 마음 말고도 더 감출 것이 있는 듯했다. 어쩌면 미스터 딕슨은 한 벗을 다른 벗과 맞바꾸기 직전까지 갔던 것이거나, 아니면 장차 들어올 일만 이천 파운드 때문에 미스 캠벨에게 매여 있었던 것인지도 몰랐다.\n\n다른 화제들에서도 마찬가지의 새침함이 판을 쳤다. 그 사람과 미스터 프랭크 처칠은 같은 시기에 웨이머스에 있었다. 둘이 조금 아는 사이라는 것은 알려져 있었다. 그러나 그가 진짜로 어떤 사람인지에 대해서 엠마는 알맹이 있는 정보라곤 한 음절도 캐낼 수가 없었다. \"그분이 미남이던가요?\" — \"꽤 빼어난 청년으로들 여기는 것 같았어요.\" \"호감 가는 분이던가요?\" — \"대체로 그렇게들 생각했죠.\" \"분별 있는 청년으로 보이던가요? 식견 있는 청년으로요?\" — \"온천지에서나 런던의 평범한 교제에서 그런 점을 판단하기란 어려운 일이에요. 지금껏 미스터 처칠을 알아 온 것보다 훨씬 오래 알고 지내야만 그나마 안심하고 가늠할 수 있는 것이라곤 태도뿐이죠. 다들 그분의 태도를 흐뭇하게 여기는 것 같았어요.\" 엠마는 그 사람을 도저히 용서할 수 없었다.\n\n# 제2권 3장\n\n엠마는 그녀를 용서할 수 없었다. 그러나 자극도 원망도 미스터 나이틀리의 눈에는 띄지 않았으니—그는 그 모임에 함께 있었으면서도 양쪽에서 오직 합당한 배려와 흐뭇한 거동만을 보았던 터라—이튿날 아침 미스터 우드하우스와의 볼일로 다시 하트필드에 들렀을 때, 그 모임 전체에 대한 만족을 토로하고 있었다. 엠마의 아버지가 자리를 비웠더라면 했을 만큼 거리낌 없이는 아니었으나, 엠마에게 충분히 알아들을 만큼은 분명하게 말했다. 그는 늘 엠마가 제인에게 부당하다고 여겨 왔던 터라, 이제 그 개선의 기미를 짚어 내는 일이 무척 기뻤다.\n\n\"참으로 즐거운 저녁이었지요.\" 미스터 우드하우스를 설득해 필요한 일을 마치고, 다 이해하셨다는 다짐을 받고, 서류를 치운 뒤에 그가 입을 열었다. \"각별히 즐거웠습니다. 당신과 미스 페어팩스가 우리에게 아주 훌륭한 음악을 들려주셨고요. 어르신, 저는 그런 두 젊은 여인에게 저녁 내내 대접받으며 편히 앉아 있는 것보다 더 호사스러운 처지는 알지 못합니다. 어떤 때는 음악으로, 어떤 때는 대화로요. 미스 페어팩스도 그 저녁을 즐거이 보냈을 게 분명합니다, 엠마. 당신은 무엇 하나 소홀히 하지 않았으니까요. 그녀에게 그렇게나 많이 연주를 시킨 게 저는 기뻤습니다. 할머니 댁에는 악기가 없으니, 그건 정말로 흔치 않은 호사였을 테지요.\"\n\n\"마음에 들어 하시니 기쁘네요.\" 엠마가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"하지만 저는 하트필드의 손님에게 마땅히 베풀 도리를 자주 빠뜨리는 사람은 아니라고 생각하는데요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 얘야.\" 아버지가 곧바로 말했다. \"그것만은 네가 절대 그렇지 않다는 걸 내가 잘 안다. 너만큼 살뜰하고 예의 바른 사람은 어디에도 없지. 굳이 따지자면 너는 지나치게 살뜰하단다. 어젯밤 그 머핀 말이다—한 번만 돌렸어도 충분했을 것 같구나.\"\n\n\"아니요.\" 거의 동시에 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"당신은 자주 빠뜨리지 않습니다. 거동에서도, 이해에서도 자주 빠뜨리지 않지요. 그러니 제 말뜻을 알아들으시리라 봅니다.\"\n\n짓궂은 눈빛이 \"당신 말뜻쯤은 잘 알아들어요\"라고 말하고 있었으나, 그녀는 그저 \"미스 페어팩스는 속을 잘 안 보이는 사람이에요\"라고만 했다.\n\n\"늘 그렇다고 말씀드렸지요—조금은요. 하지만 그 속내 감춤 가운데 마땅히 허물어야 할 부분은, 다시 말해 자신 없음에 뿌리를 둔 부분은 곧 다 허물게 될 겁니다. 신중함에서 비롯된 것이라면 응당 존중해야 하고요.\"\n\n\"자신 없는 사람이라고 보시는군요. 저한테는 그렇게 안 보여요.\"\n\n\"엠마.\" 그가 의자에서 일어나 그녀 가까이 놓인 의자로 옮겨 앉으며 말했다. \"설마 그 저녁이 즐겁지 않았다고 말하려는 건 아니겠지요.\"\n\n\"아유! 아니에요. 저는 끈질기게 질문을 던진 제 인내심이 마음에 들었고, 그래서 얻어 낸 정보가 얼마나 빈약한지를 생각하니 우습기까지 했는걸요.\"\n\n\"실망스럽군요.\" 그가 한 대답은 그것뿐이었다.\n\n\"다들 즐거운 저녁을 보냈으면 좋겠구나.\" 미스터 우드하우스가 그 특유의 조용한 투로 말했다. \"나는 즐거웠다. 한 번은 난롯불이 좀 과하게 느껴졌지만, 그때 의자를 조금, 아주 조금 뒤로 물렸더니 거슬리지 않더구나. 미스 베이츠는 늘 그렇듯 말도 많고 기분도 좋아 보였어. 말이 좀 빠르긴 하다만. 그래도 아주 상냥한 사람이지. 베이츠 부인도 다른 식으로 그렇고. 나는 오랜 벗들이 좋아. 그리고 미스 제인 페어팩스는 아주 어여쁜 아가씨야. 정말로 어여쁘고 아주 행실 바른 아가씨지. 그 애도 그 저녁이 흐뭇했을 게다, 미스터 나이틀리. 엠마가 곁에 있었으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 어르신. 그리고 엠마도, 미스 페어팩스가 곁에 있었으니까요.\"\n\n엠마는 그의 근심을 알아챘고, 적어도 지금만이라도 그것을 달래 주고 싶어, 누구도 의심할 수 없을 진심을 담아 말했다—\n\n\"그분은 도무지 눈을 뗄 수 없는, 그런 우아한 사람이에요. 저는 늘 그분을 감탄하며 바라보게 되거든요. 그리고 마음속 깊이 그분을 가엾게 여기고요.\"\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 입 밖에 내고 싶은 것 이상으로 흡족해하는 얼굴이었다. 그가 미처 대꾸하기도 전에, 생각이 온통 베이츠 모녀에게 가 있던 미스터 우드하우스가 말했다—\n\n\"그 댁 형편이 그렇게 옹색하다니 참으로 딱한 일이야! 정말 딱한 일이지! 나도 늘 바랐지만—사람이 감히 해 볼 수 있는 일이라곤 어찌 그리 적은지—뭔가 흔치 않은 것을 자그맣게, 소소하게 선물하는 정도지—이번에 돼지를 한 마리 잡았으니, 엠마가 등심이나 다릿살을 보내 줄까 생각하는데. 아주 자그맣고 연한 부위야—하트필드 돼지고기는 다른 돼지고기와는 다르지—그래도 돼지고기는 돼지고기니—얘야, 엠마야, 그 댁에서 그걸 우리처럼 기름기 한 점 없이 곱게 부친 스테이크로 만들어 먹는다는 보장이 없는 한, 그리고 구워 먹지 않는다는 보장이 없는 한—구운 돼지고기는 어떤 위장도 견디지 못하니까—내 생각엔 다릿살을 보내는 편이 낫겠다—그렇지 않니, 얘야?\"\n\n\"아버지, 저는 뒷다리 한 짝을 통째로 보냈어요. 아버지가 그러길 바라실 줄 알았거든요. 아시다시피 다릿살은 소금에 절이면 되는데, 그게 아주 맛이 좋잖아요. 그리고 등심은 그 댁에서 원하는 대로 곧장 요리해 먹으면 되고요.\"\n\n\"잘했다, 얘야, 아주 잘했어. 미처 거기까진 생각 못 했다만, 그게 제일 좋은 방법이지. 다릿살을 너무 짜게 절이면 안 될 텐데. 그러니까 너무 짜게 절이지 않고, 우리 집 설이 삶듯 아주 푹 삶아서, 삶은 순무하고 당근이나 파스닙을 곁들여 아주 적당히만 먹는다면, 나는 그게 몸에 나쁘지 않다고 본다.\"\n\n\"엠마.\" 잠시 뒤 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"당신에게 알려 줄 소식이 하나 있습니다. 소식 좋아하시잖아요—여기 오는 길에 들은 이야기인데, 당신이 흥미로워할 것 같군요.\"\n\n\"소식이요! 아유! 그럼요, 저는 늘 소식을 좋아해요. 뭔데요?—왜 그렇게 웃으세요?—어디서 들으셨어요?—랜덜스에서요?\"\n\n그는 겨우 이 말만 할 수 있었다.\n\n\"아니요, 랜덜스가 아닙니다. 랜덜스 근처엔 가지도 않았어요.\" 그 순간 문이 활짝 열리며 미스 베이츠와 미스 페어팩스가 방으로 들어섰다. 감사의 말로 가득 차고 소식으로도 가득 찬 미스 베이츠는 어느 쪽부터 쏟아 내야 할지 갈피를 못 잡았다. 미스터 나이틀리는 자신의 기회가 날아갔으며, 이제 한마디도 자기 차지로 남지 않으리라는 것을 곧 알아차렸다.\n\n\"아유! 어르신, 오늘 아침 안녕하셨어요? 미스 우드하우스—저는 그만 압도되어 왔답니다. 그렇게 훌륭한 돼지 뒷다리라니요! 너무 후하세요! 소식 들으셨어요? 미스터 엘턴이 결혼한대요.\"\n\n엠마는 미스터 엘턴을 떠올릴 겨를조차 없었던 터라, 그 말을 듣자 완전히 놀란 나머지 살짝 움찔하고 살짝 얼굴을 붉히는 것을 피할 수 없었다.\n\n\"그게 제 소식이었습니다. 당신이 흥미로워할 줄 알았지요.\" 미스터 나이틀리가, 두 사람 사이에 오갔던 일의 일부를 확신한다는 듯한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그쪽은 대체 어디서 들으셨어요?\" 미스 베이츠가 외쳤다. \"대체 어디서 그걸 들으셨대요, 미스터 나이틀리? 제가 콜 부인의 쪽지를 받은 지 오 분도 안 됐는걸요—아니, 오 분도 더는 안 됐을 거예요—적어도 십 분쯤—제가 보닛에 스펜서까지 다 차려입고 막 나오려던 참이었거든요—돼지고기 일로 패티한테 한마디 더 하려고 잠깐 아래층에 내려갔던 것뿐이에요—제인은 복도에 서 있었고요—그렇지, 제인?—어머니가 우리 집에 그만큼 큰 절임통이 없을까 봐 어찌나 걱정하시던지요. 그래서 제가 내려가서 보고 오겠다고 했더니, 제인이 '제가 대신 내려갈까요? 이모는 감기 기운이 좀 있는 것 같고, 패티는 부엌을 닦고 있었잖아요' 하지 뭐예요.—'아유! 얘는' 하고 제가 말하는데—바로 그때 그 쪽지가 온 거예요. 어느 미스 호킨스라네요—제가 아는 건 그게 다예요. 바스의 미스 호킨스라고요. 그런데 미스터 나이틀리, 그쪽은 대체 어떻게 그걸 들으셨대요? 미스터 콜이 콜 부인한테 그 이야기를 한 바로 그 순간에, 부인이 자리에 앉아 저한테 편지를 쓴 건데요. 어느 미스 호킨스라니—\"\n\n\"한 시간 반쯤 전에 볼일로 미스터 콜과 함께 있었습니다. 제가 안내받아 들어갈 때 그가 막 엘턴의 편지를 읽던 참이라, 곧장 제게 건네주더군요.\"\n\n\"세상에! 그거 참—세상 누구에게나 이만큼 두루 흥미로운 소식은 없었을 거예요. 어르신, 정말이지 너무 후하세요. 어머니가 더없이 정중한 인사와 안부를, 그리고 천 번의 감사를 전하시면서, 정말이지 송구해 어쩔 줄 모르겠다고 하셨답니다.\"\n\n\"우리는 우리 하트필드 돼지고기가—\" 미스터 우드하우스가 대꾸했다. \"정말이지 다른 어떤 돼지고기보다도 월등히 낫다고 여기는 터라, 엠마와 나로서는 이보다 더 큰 기쁨이 없는—\"\n\n\"아유! 어르신, 어머니 말씀처럼, 저희 벗들은 저희한테 너무도 잘해 주신답니다. 큰 재산도 없으면서 바랄 수 있는 모든 걸 다 누리는 사람들이 세상에 있다면, 그게 바로 저희일 거예요. 저희야말로 '우리 몫은 아름다운 기업에 떨어졌도다'* 하고 말할 만하지요. 자아, 미스터 나이틀리, 그러니까 정말로 그 편지를 직접 보셨다는 거네요. 그래서—\"\n* '우리 몫은 아름다운 기업에 떨어졌도다': 구약 시편 16편 6절의 한 구절.\n\n\"짧았습니다—그저 알리려는 것뿐이었지요—하지만 물론 들떠 있고, 의기양양했습니다.\"—여기서 엠마를 향한 짓궂은 눈길이 스쳤다. \"그는 다행히도—정확한 표현은 잊었군요—그런 걸 기억할 까닭이 없으니까요. 당신 말대로, 어느 미스 호킨스와 결혼하게 됐다는 내용이었습니다. 글투로 보아 막 정해진 일인 듯싶더군요.\"\n\n\"미스터 엘턴이 결혼한다니!\" 엠마가 입을 뗄 수 있게 되자마자 말했다. \"온 세상이 그분의 행복을 빌어 줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"자리 잡기엔 아주 젊은데.\" 미스터 우드하우스의 소감이었다. \"서두르지 않는 편이 좋을 텐데. 내가 보기엔 지금 그대로도 아주 잘 지내는 것 같던데. 우리는 그가 하트필드에 오면 늘 반가웠지.\"\n\n\"우리 모두에게 새 이웃이 생기는 거예요, 미스 우드하우스!\" 미스 베이츠가 기쁘게 말했다. \"어머니가 어찌나 좋아하시는지!—가엾은 그 낡은 목사관에 안주인이 없는 걸 참을 수가 없다고 하시거든요. 정말이지 대단한 소식이에요. 제인, 너는 미스터 엘턴을 한 번도 못 봤지!—그러니 그렇게나 보고 싶어 하는 것도 무리가 아니구나.\"\n\n제인의 호기심은 그녀를 온통 사로잡을 만큼 그렇게 골똘한 것으로는 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"네—저는 미스터 엘턴을 한 번도 못 봤어요.\" 그 부름에 화들짝 정신을 차리며 그녀가 답했다. \"그분은—그분은 키가 큰 분인가요?\"\n\n\"그 물음엔 누가 답해야 할까요?\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"제 아버지라면 '그렇다'고, 미스터 나이틀리라면 '아니다'라고 하실 테고, 미스 베이츠와 저는 딱 알맞은 중간이라고 할 거예요. 미스 페어팩스, 여기 좀 더 오래 머물다 보면 알게 될 거예요. 미스터 엘턴은 하이베리에서 외모로나 마음으로나 완벽함의 기준이거든요.\"\n\n\"정말 맞는 말씀이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 곧 알게 될 거예요. 그분은 더없이 훌륭한 청년이지요—그런데 제인, 기억나니, 어제 내가 그분이 딱 미스터 페리만 한 키라고 했잖니. 미스 호킨스라니—분명 빼어난 아가씨일 거예요. 우리 어머니한테 어찌나 극진히 마음을 쓰시던지—더 잘 들으시라고 어머니더러 목사관 신도석에 앉으시라고까지 하셨지요. 아시다시피 어머니가 귀가 좀 어두우시거든요—심한 건 아니고, 그저 얼른 알아듣지를 못하시는 정도예요. 제인 말로는 캠벨 대령도 귀가 좀 어두우시다더라고요. 목욕이 좋을 듯싶다고—따뜻한 물 목욕 말이에요—여기셨다는데, 제인 말로는 오래가는 효험은 없었다고 해요. 아시다시피 캠벨 대령은 정말이지 저희한테 천사 같은 분이에요. 그리고 미스터 딕슨도 아주 매력적인 청년인 것 같아요. 대령께 손색이 없는 분이지요. 좋은 사람들끼리 어울리는 건 참으로 큰 행복이에요—게다가 그런 사람들은 늘 그렇게 어울리고요. 자, 이제 미스터 엘턴과 미스 호킨스가 그렇게 될 테고요. 콜 부부도 계시고, 정말 좋은 분들이지요. 그리고 페리네도요—미스터 페리와 페리 부인보다 더 행복하고 더 훌륭한 부부는 세상에 없을 거예요. 어르신,\" 미스터 우드하우스에게로 돌아서며, \"하이베리만 한 사교를 누리는 곳은 흔치 않다고 생각해요. 저는 늘 말하지요, 우리는 이웃 복을 타고났다고요.—어르신, 우리 어머니가 무엇보다 좋아하시는 게 하나 있다면, 그건 돼지고기랍니다—구운 돼지 등심이요—\"\n\n\"미스 호킨스가 누구인지, 어떤 사람인지, 그분이 그녀를 안 지 얼마나 됐는지에 관해서는,\" 엠마가 말했다. \"아마 알려진 게 아무것도 없겠지요. 그리 오래된 사이일 리는 없다는 느낌이 들어요. 그분이 떠난 지 겨우 사 주밖에 안 됐으니까요.\"\n\n아무도 내놓을 정보가 없었다. 몇 마디 더 신기해하고 나서 엠마가 말했다.\n\n\"말씀이 없으시네요, 미스 페어팩스—하지만 이 소식에 관심을 가져 주셨으면 해요. 요즈음 이런 주제로 그렇게 많이 듣고 보아 오신 분이, 미스 캠벨 일로 그 일에 그토록 깊이 관여하셨을 분이—미스터 엘턴과 미스 호킨스 일에 무심하다면 봐드릴 수가 없는걸요.\"\n\n\"미스터 엘턴을 보고 나면,\" 제인이 답했다. \"분명 관심이 생기겠지요—하지만 저는 그걸 봐야만 그렇게 되나 봐요. 그리고 미스 캠벨이 결혼한 지 몇 달이 지났으니, 그 인상도 좀 무뎌졌을 거예요.\"\n\n\"네, 그분이 떠난 지 딱 사 주 됐어요, 미스 우드하우스 말씀대로요.\" 미스 베이츠가 말했다. \"어제로 사 주째였지요.—어느 미스 호킨스라니!—글쎄요, 저는 늘 이 근방의 어느 아가씨가 될 거라고 막연히 생각했답니다. 그렇다고 제가 한 번이라도—언젠가 콜 부인이 저한테 귀띔하긴 했지만—저는 곧바로 '아니에요, 미스터 엘턴은 더없이 훌륭한 청년이지만—' 하고 말했지요—요컨대, 저는 그런 걸 알아채는 데 특별히 빠른 사람은 못 되나 봐요. 그런 척도 안 하고요. 제 눈앞에 있는 건 보지요. 그러면서도, 미스터 엘턴이 더 높은 곳을 바랐다 한들 누가 이상하게 여길 수 있겠어요—미스 우드하우스는 이렇게 마음 좋게 제가 수다 떨도록 내버려 두시네요. 제가 무슨 일이 있어도 누구 마음 상하게 할 사람이 아니라는 걸 아시니까요. 미스 스미스는 어떻게 지내요? 이젠 아주 회복된 것 같던데요. 미시즈 존 나이틀리한테서 요즘 소식 들으셨어요? 아유! 그 사랑스러운 어린것들. 제인, 너 알다시피 나는 늘 미스터 딕슨이 미스터 존 나이틀리를 닮았다고 생각하잖니. 외모 말이야—키가 크고, 그런 인상에다가—말수가 그리 많지 않은 것까지.\"\n\n\"전혀 아니에요, 이모. 닮은 데가 하나도 없어요.\"\n\n\"참 이상하구나! 하지만 사람은 누구든 미리 제대로 그려 보는 법이 없지. 한번 생각을 잡으면 그걸로 멋대로 내달리니까. 그러니까 미스터 딕슨이 엄밀히 말해 잘생긴 건 아니라는 거지?\"\n\n\"잘생겼다니요! 아유! 아니에요—전혀요—분명 평범한 편이에요. 평범하다고 말씀드렸잖아요.\"\n\n\"얘야, 너는 미스 캠벨이 그분을 평범하다고 인정하지 않으려 한다고 했고, 너 자신도—\"\n\n\"아유! 저로 말하면, 제 판단은 아무 쓸모가 없는걸요. 제가 호감을 품으면 그 사람은 늘 잘생겨 보이거든요. 다만 그분을 평범하다고 했을 땐, 그게 일반적인 평이라고 믿어서 그렇게 말한 거예요.\"\n\n\"자, 얘 제인아, 우리 이제 그만 가 봐야겠다. 날이 좋아 보이지 않고, 할머니가 안절부절못하실 거야. 너무 친절하게 대해 주시네요, 미스 우드하우스. 하지만 정말 이만 가 봐야겠어요. 정말이지 더없이 흐뭇한 소식이었어요. 저는 콜 부인 댁에 잠깐 들렀다 갈게요. 그래도 삼 분도 안 머물 거예요. 그리고 제인, 너는 곧장 집으로 가는 게 좋겠다—소나기를 맞게 하고 싶진 않으니까!—이 아이가 하이베리 덕에 벌써 나아진 것 같아요. 고맙습니다, 정말 그래요. 고더드 부인 댁엔 들르지 않을래요. 그분은 정말이지 삶은 돼지고기 말고는 아무것에도 관심이 없으신 것 같거든요. 다릿살을 요리하면 또 모를까요. 안녕히 계세요, 어르신. 아유! 미스터 나이틀리도 가시는군요. 어머, 그거 정말이지!—제인이 지치면 친절하게 팔을 빌려주시겠지요.—미스터 엘턴이라니, 미스 호킨스라니!—안녕히들 계세요.\"\n\n아버지와 단둘이 남은 엠마는, 젊은 사람들이 그렇게 서둘러 결혼하려 든다며—게다가 낯선 사람과 결혼하려 든다며—탄식하는 아버지에게 주의의 절반을 빼앗겼고, 나머지 절반은 그 일에 대한 자기 자신의 견해에 쓸 수 있었다. 그것은 그녀에게 재미있고 무척 반가운 소식이었으니, 미스터 엘턴이 그리 오래 괴로워하지는 못했다는 증거였기 때문이다. 그러나 해리엇이 안쓰러웠다. 해리엇은 분명 충격을 받을 터였다—그리고 그녀가 바랄 수 있는 것이라곤, 자기 입으로 먼저 알려 줌으로써 남에게서 불쑥 듣게 되는 일만은 막아 주는 것뿐이었다. 지금쯤이면 해리엇이 들를 무렵이었다. 오는 길에 미스 베이츠와 마주치기라도 한다면!—게다가 비가 내리기 시작하자, 엠마는 이 날씨 때문에 해리엇이 고더드 부인 댁에 발이 묶일 테고, 그러면 그 소식이 틀림없이 아무 마음의 준비도 없는 그녀에게 들이닥치리라고 예상할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n소나기는 거셌으나 짧았다. 그것이 그친 지 오 분도 안 되어 해리엇이 들어왔는데, 가슴 벅찬 채 부랴부랴 달려온 사람에게 어울릴 법한 바로 그 달아오르고 들뜬 얼굴이었다. 곧장 터져 나온 \"아유! 미스 우드하우스, 무슨 일이 있었는지 아세요!\"라는 외침은 그에 걸맞은 동요를 그대로 드러내고 있었다. 일격이 이미 가해진 마당이라, 엠마는 지금 가장 큰 친절은 들어 주는 것이라고 느꼈다. 해리엇은 거침없이, 들려줄 이야기를 다급하게 쏟아 냈다. 그녀는 삼십 분 전에 고더드 부인 댁을 나섰다—비가 올까 봐 두려웠다—금방이라도 퍼부을까 봐 두려웠다—하지만 하트필드에 먼저 닿을 수 있을 것 같았다—그래서 있는 힘껏 서둘렀다. 그런데 자기 가운을 짓고 있는 어느 젊은 여자의 집 앞을 지나다가, 잠깐 들어가서 어떻게 돼 가나 보자는 생각이 들었다. 거기 잠시도 머물지 않은 것 같은데, 나오자마자 비가 내리기 시작했고, 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다. 그래서 곧장 있는 힘껏 달려 포드 가게로 비를 피해 들어갔다.—포드 가게란 모직물점, 아마포점, 잡화점을 겸한 곳으로, 그 마을에서 규모로나 유행으로나 으뜸가는 상점이었다.—그래서 그녀는 거기 앉아, 세상 그 무엇도 생각지 못한 채, 족히 십 분쯤 있었다.—여기서 해리엇은 숨도 안 쉬고 이어 갔다. \"그런데 갑자기, 누가 들어왔는지 아세요—정말이지 너무도 묘했어요!—하긴 그 사람들은 늘 포드에서 물건을 샀으니까요—누가 들어왔느냐 하면, 엘리자베스 마틴과 그 오빠였어요!—어머, 미스 우드하우스! 생각 좀 해 보세요. 저는 기절하는 줄 알았어요. 어찌할 바를 몰랐죠. 저는 문 가까이에 앉아 있었는데—엘리자베스는 곧바로 저를 봤지만, 그분은 못 봤어요. 우산을 챙기느라 바빴거든요. 그 여자가 저를 본 게 분명한데, 곧바로 눈길을 돌리고 모른 체했어요. 그러고는 둘 다 가게 저 안쪽 끝으로 가 버렸고요. 그런데 저는 계속 문 가까이에 앉아 있었지 뭐예요!—아유! 어쩜! 저는 너무도 비참했어요! 분명 제 가운만큼이나 새하얗게 질려 있었을 거예요. 비 때문에 그냥 나갈 수도 없었고요. 하지만 그 자리만 아니라면 세상 어디라도 가 있고 싶었답니다.—아유! 어쩜, 미스 우드하우스—그러다 마침내, 그분이 돌아보고 저를 본 모양이에요. 물건 사는 걸 계속하는 대신 둘이 소곤거리기 시작했거든요. 분명 제 이야기를 하고 있었어요. 그리고 그분이 누이더러 저한테 말을 걸라고 설득하는 거라는 생각이 자꾸 들었어요—(그랬을까요, 미스 우드하우스?)—이내 그 여자가 다가왔으니까요—제 앞까지 바짝 다가와서 안부를 묻고는, 제가 원하면 악수라도 할 듯한 기색이었어요. 예전에 하던 것과 똑같은 식은 하나도 아니었어요. 그 여자가 변한 게 보였거든요. 하지만 그래도 아주 다정하게 굴려고 애쓰는 것 같긴 했고, 우리는 악수를 하고 한동안 서서 이야기를 나눴어요. 하지만 제가 무슨 말을 했는지는 도무지 모르겠어요—어찌나 떨었던지요!—그 여자가 이제 우리가 통 못 만나서 섭섭하다고 한 건 기억나요. 너무 다정한 말이다 싶었죠! 어머, 미스 우드하우스, 저는 정말이지 비참했어요! 그 무렵엔 비가 그치기 시작했고, 저는 무슨 일이 있어도 거기서 빠져나가야겠다고 마음먹었어요—그런데—생각 좀 해 보세요!—그분도 저를 향해 다가오고 있는 거예요—아시잖아요, 천천히, 마치 어쩔 줄 모르는 사람처럼요. 그렇게 다가와서 말을 걸었고, 저는 대답했죠—그리고 잠깐 서 있었는데, 어찌나 끔찍하던지요, 아시잖아요, 뭐라 말로 못 할 그런 기분이었어요. 그러다 용기를 내서, 비가 그쳤으니 가 봐야겠다고 했죠. 그래서 나섰어요. 그런데 문에서 세 발짝도 못 갔는데, 그분이 따라와서는, 제가 하트필드로 갈 거면 미스터 콜의 마구간 쪽으로 돌아가는 편이 훨씬 나을 거라고, 가까운 길은 이번 비에 죄다 잠겼을 거라는 말만 하더라고요. 아유! 어쩜, 저는 그 자리에서 죽는 줄 알았어요! 그래서 정말 감사하다고 했죠. 아시잖아요, 그보다 덜할 수는 없었으니까요. 그러고 나서 그분은 엘리자베스한테 돌아갔고, 저는 마구간 쪽으로 돌아왔어요—그랬을 거예요—하지만 제가 어디 있는지, 무슨 일이 어찌 된 건지 거의 알지도 못했답니다. 아유! 미스 우드하우스, 그런 일을 겪느니 차라리 무슨 일이든 했을 거예요. 그러면서도, 아시잖아요, 그분이 그렇게 상냥하고 친절하게 구는 걸 보니 어쩐지 흐뭇한 구석도 있더라고요. 엘리자베스도 그렇고요. 아유! 미스 우드하우스, 저한테 말 좀 해 주세요, 마음이 좀 편해지게요.\"\n\n엠마는 진심으로 그러고 싶었으나, 당장은 그럴 형편이 못 되었다. 그녀는 멈추어 생각해야만 했다. 그녀 자신부터 마음이 온전히 편치 않았다. 그 청년의 처신과 그 누이의 처신은 진실한 감정에서 비롯된 것으로 보였고, 그들을 가엾이 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 해리엇의 묘사대로라면, 그들의 거동에는 상처받은 애정과 진정한 섬세함이 흥미롭게 뒤섞여 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 전부터도 그들이 선의를 지닌 훌륭한 사람들이라고 믿어 왔던 터—그렇다면 이번 일이 그 인연의 해악에 무슨 차이를 만든단 말인가? 그것에 흔들리는 것은 어리석은 짓이었다. 물론 그는 그녀를 잃어 서운할 터—다들 서운할 터였다. 사랑뿐 아니라 야심도 십중팔구 꺾였으리라. 그들 모두 해리엇과 사귐으로써 신분이 오르기를 바랐을지 모른다. 게다가 해리엇의 묘사가 무슨 값어치가 있단 말인가?—그토록 쉽게 흡족해하고—그토록 분별이 없는 사람의 칭찬이 다 무슨 의미가 있겠는가?\n\n그녀는 마음을 다잡고, 지나간 일을 죄다 사소한 것으로, 그리고 곱씹을 만한 가치라곤 도무지 없는 것으로 치부함으로써 해리엇을 위로하려 애썼다.\n\n\"잠깐은 괴로울 수도 있겠지요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 당신은 더없이 잘 처신한 것 같아요. 게다가 이미 다 끝난 일이고요—첫 만남으로서야 다시는 일어날 리—일어날 수도 없는 일이니까, 더는 마음에 둘 필요가 없어요.\"\n\n해리엇은 \"정말 맞아요\"라고 했고, \"더는 마음에 두지 않겠다\"고도 했다. 그러나 여전히 그 이야기를 했다—여전히 그 이야기 말고는 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 그리하여 엠마는 마침내 마틴 식구들을 그녀의 머릿속에서 몰아내기 위해, 그토록 다정하고 조심스럽게 전하려 했던 그 소식을 서둘러 꺼낼 수밖에 없었다. 가엾은 해리엇의 그런 심정을—미스터 엘턴이 그녀에게 지녔던 의미가 그렇게 결말지어진 것을—기뻐해야 할지 화를 내야 할지, 부끄러워해야 할지 그저 우스워해야 할지, 그녀 자신도 도무지 알 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 미스터 엘턴의 권세는 차츰 되살아났다. 처음 들은 소식이 어제나 한 시간 전이었다면 느꼈을 만큼은 와닿지 않았지만, 그 흥미는 곧 커졌다. 그리하여 첫 대화가 끝나기도 전에, 그녀는 이 운 좋은 미스 호킨스를 둘러싼 온갖 호기심과 놀라움과 아쉬움, 아픔과 기쁨의 감정에 스스로 빠져들었으니, 그것은 마틴 식구들을 그녀의 상상 속에서 마땅한 종속의 자리에 도로 밀어 넣기에 충분한 것이었다.\n\n엠마는 차츰 그런 만남이 있었던 것을 도리어 다행으로 여기게 되었다. 그것은 첫 충격을 무디게 하는 데 보탬이 되었으면서도, 불안을 자아낼 만한 여파는 조금도 남기지 않았다. 지금 해리엇이 사는 형편으로는, 마틴 식구들이 그녀를 일부러 찾아 나서지 않는 한 그녀에게 닿을 수 없었다. 그런데 그들은 지금껏 그녀를 찾아 나설 용기도, 그렇게 몸을 굽힐 마음도 없었다. 해리엇이 오빠의 청혼을 거절한 뒤로 그 누이들은 한 번도 고더드 부인 댁에 온 적이 없었고, 어떤 필요로든, 아니 말을 나눌 기회조차 없이, 다시 한자리에 마주치는 일 없이 열두 달이 지날 법도 했다.\n\n# 제2권 4장\n\n인간이란 흥미로운 처지에 놓인 사람을 향해서는 어찌나 너그러운지, 결혼을 하거나 세상을 떠나는 젊은이라면 으레 좋은 말로 입에 오르내리게 마련이다.\n\n미스 호킨스라는 이름이 하이베리에서 처음 거론된 지 일주일이 채 지나기도 전에, 그녀는 어떤 경로로인가 용모와 마음씨에서 더할 나위 없는 장점을 두루 갖춘 사람으로 — 아름답고 우아하며 재예가 뛰어나고 더없이 상냥한 사람으로 밝혀져 있었다. 그리하여 미스터 엘턴이 직접 돌아와 자기 앞에 펼쳐진 행복한 전망을 한껏 자랑하고 그녀의 미덕에 관한 소문을 퍼뜨릴 즈음에는, 그가 새로이 보탤 일이라곤 그녀의 세례명을 알려 주고 그녀가 주로 누구의 곡을 연주하는지 말해 주는 것 말고는 거의 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n미스터 엘턴은 더없이 행복한 남자가 되어 돌아왔다. 떠날 때는 거절당하고 굴욕을 안고 — 강한 격려로만 보였던 일들이 줄줄이 이어진 끝에 한껏 부풀었던 희망이 꺾여 — 게다가 마땅한 여인을 잃었을 뿐 아니라 도무지 어울리지 않는 여인과 동급으로 격하되는 처지에 놓였었다. 그렇게 깊이 모욕당한 채 떠났던 그가 — 다른 여인과 약혼하여 돌아온 것이다 — 이런 경우에 으레 얻은 것이 잃은 것보다 나아 보이듯, 첫 번째 여인보다 당연히 한층 빼어난 여인과. 그는 명랑하고 흡족한 얼굴로, 의욕에 차 분주하게, 미스 우드하우스 따위는 안중에도 없고 미스 스미스 같은 것은 우습다는 듯 돌아왔다.\n\n매혹적인 오거스타 호킨스는 완벽한 미모와 미덕이라는 으레 따라붙는 온갖 장점에 더하여, 독립적인 재산을 — 언제나 만 파운드라 불릴 만한 수천 파운드를 — 소유하고 있었다. 이는 얼마간의 위신이자 또한 얼마간의 실속이 되는 대목이었다. 이야기는 그럴듯하게 들렸다. 그는 제 처지를 내던진 것이 아니라 — 만 파운드 남짓을 지닌 여인을 얻은 것이었다. 게다가 그 여인을 어찌나 신나게 빨리 얻었던지 — 소개받은 첫 시간이 지나기 무섭게 각별한 주목이 뒤따랐다. 그가 콜 부인에게 들려줄 이 연애의 발단과 진전에 관한 이야기는 어찌나 영광스러운지 — 그 단계들이 어찌나 빠르게 흘렀던지, 우연한 마주침에서 미스터 그린네 만찬으로, 다시 브라운 부인네 모임으로 — 미소와 홍조가 점점 의미심장해지고 — 의식과 동요가 풍성하게 흩뿌려지고 — 그 숙녀는 어찌나 쉽게 마음이 움직였던지 — 어찌나 다정하게 호의를 보였던지 — 한마디로, 더없이 알기 쉬운 표현을 빌리자면, 그를 받아들일 채비가 어찌나 충분했던지, 허영심과 분별심이 똑같이 흡족했다.\n\n그는 실체와 그림자를 — 재산과 연정을 함께 거머쥐었고, 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 행복한 남자였다. 오로지 자기 이야기와 제 일만 떠벌리며 — 축하받기를 기대하고 — 놀림감이 되어도 좋다는 듯이 — 거리낌 없는 다정한 미소를 지으며, 몇 주 전이라면 한층 조심스레 점잔을 빼며 대했을 이 고장의 모든 젊은 숙녀에게 이제 거침없이 말을 건넸다.\n\n혼례는 머지않은 일이었으니, 당사자들로서는 자기들 마음만 흡족하면 그만이라 그저 필요한 준비만 마치기를 기다리면 되었다. 그가 다시 바스로 떠날 무렵, 다음번에 하이베리에 들어설 때는 신부를 데려오리라는 것이 모두의 일반적인 예상이었고, 콜 부인의 어떤 눈빛도 그 예상을 부정하는 것 같지는 않았다.\n\n이번 짧은 체류 동안 엠마는 그를 거의 보지 못했다. 그러나 첫 대면이 끝났다는 것을 느끼기에는 충분할 만큼 보았고, 이제 그의 태도 전반에 퍼진 분함과 우쭐함이 뒤섞이는 바람에 그가 조금도 나아지지 않았다는 인상을 받았다. 사실 엠마는 자기가 도대체 어떻게 그를 호감 가는 사람이라 여긴 적이 있었던가 몹시 의아해지기 시작하던 참이었다. 그의 모습은 어떤 무척 불쾌한 감정과 떼려야 뗄 수 없이 얽혀 있었으므로, 자기 마음에 참회요 교훈이요 유익한 굴욕의 원천이 된다는 도덕적 관점에서가 아니라면, 다시는 그를 보지 않으리라는 확언을 얻을 수만 있다면 고마울 지경이었다. 그녀는 그가 잘되기를 진심으로 바랐다. 그러나 그는 그녀에게 고통을 주었으니, 그의 안녕은 이십 마일 밖에서나 가장 흡족하게 누려질 일이었다.\n\n그렇기는 해도 그가 하이베리에 계속 머무는 데서 오는 고통은 그의 결혼으로 분명 덜어질 터였다. 그 덕에 부질없는 근심도 숱하게 막아질 것이요 — 어색한 일도 숱하게 매끄러워질 것이었다. 엘턴 부인이라는 존재는 교제 방식을 바꾸는 데 좋은 핑계가 되어 줄 터였다. 예전의 친밀함도 말썽 없이 가라앉을 수 있었다. 그것은 거의 새로 예의의 삶을 시작하는 셈이 될 것이었다.\n\n그 숙녀 개인에 관해서라면 엠마는 그다지 깊이 생각하지 않았다. 미스터 엘턴에게야 충분히 어울리는 여자였다, 의심할 여지 없이. 하이베리에 어울릴 만큼은 재예를 갖추었고 — 충분히 어여뻤고 — 십중팔구 해리엇 곁에 서면 수수해 보일 정도였다. 가문에 관해서라면, 엠마는 마음이 더없이 편했다. 그가 그토록 자기 가문을 떠벌리고 해리엇을 업신여겼어도 결국 변변한 것은 아무것도 이루지 못했으리라 확신했기 때문이다. 그 점에서만큼은 진실에 다다를 수 있을 듯했다. 그녀가 어떤 사람인지는 불확실할 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 그녀가 누구인지는 알아낼 수 있었으니 — 만 파운드를 제쳐 두고 보면, 그녀가 해리엇보다 조금이라도 나은 점이 있어 보이지는 않았다. 그녀가 가져오는 것은 이름도, 혈통도, 인척도 아니었다. 미스 호킨스는 어느 브리스틀 — 물론 상인이라 불러야 마땅하겠지만 — 그 상인의 두 딸 가운데 막내였다. 다만 그가 평생 장사로 거둔 이익이 죄다 그토록 변변찮아 보였으므로, 그가 손댄 장사의 격 또한 무척 변변찮았으리라 짐작해도 부당하지는 않았다. 그녀는 해마다 겨울이면 얼마간 바스에서 지내곤 했으나, 그녀의 집은 브리스틀, 그것도 브리스틀의 한복판이었다. 아버지와 어머니는 몇 해 전 세상을 떴어도 외삼촌 하나가 남아 있었으니 — 법률 쪽 일을 하는 사람이었다 — 그에 관해 무릅쓰고 밝힌 것이라곤 법률 쪽 일을 한다는 것보다 더 또렷하게 떳떳한 내용은 없었고, 그 딸은 그와 함께 살았다. 엠마는 그가 어느 변호사 밑에서 부려지는 잡역꾼일 테고, 출세하기에는 너무 둔한 위인이리라 짐작했다. 그리고 그 인척의 온갖 영화는 죄다, 마차를 두 대나 부리는, 브리스틀 근처에서 사업을 크게 하는 신사와 아주 잘 결혼한 언니에게 달려 있는 듯했다! 그것이 이야기의 마무리요, 그것이 미스 호킨스의 영광이었다.\n\n이 모든 것에 대한 자기 심정을 해리엇에게 그대로 전해 줄 수만 있다면! 엠마는 말로 그녀를 사랑에 빠뜨렸다. 그러나 아아! 말로 그 사랑에서 빠져나오게 하기란 그처럼 쉬운 일이 아니었다. 해리엇의 마음속 그 숱한 빈자리를 채워 줄 대상이 지닌 매력은 말로 떨쳐 버릴 수 있는 것이 아니었다. 그가 다른 사람으로 대체될 수는 있을 터였다. 분명 정말로 그렇게 될 것이었다. 그보다 더 분명한 일도 없었다. 로버트 마틴 같은 사람으로도 충분할 정도였다. 그러나 그 밖에 다른 무엇으로도 그녀를 낫게 할 수는 없으리라고 엠마는 우려했다. 해리엇은 한번 시작하면 늘 사랑에 빠져 있는 부류의 사람이었다. 그런데 이제, 가엾기도 하지! 그녀는 미스터 엘턴이 다시 나타난 탓에 한결 더 나빠졌다. 어딘가에서 언제나 그의 모습을 흘끗흘끗 보게 되었다. 엠마가 그를 본 것은 단 한 번이었으나, 해리엇은 하루에도 두세 번씩, 영락없이 그와 막 마주치거나, 막 엇갈리거나, 막 그의 목소리를 듣거나, 막 그의 어깨를 보거나, 막 무슨 일이 일어나 그를 자기 환상 속에 — 놀라움과 추측이 부추기는 그 온기 속에 — 고스란히 간직하게 되곤 했다. 게다가 그녀는 끊임없이 그에 관한 이야기를 들었다. 하트필드에 있을 때를 빼면, 그녀는 늘 미스터 엘턴에게서 흠 하나 보지 못하고 그의 일을 논하는 것보다 더 흥미로운 일은 없다고 여기는 사람들 사이에 끼어 있었기 때문이다. 그러니 온갖 소문, 온갖 추측 — 이미 일어난 모든 일, 그의 살림을 꾸리는 데서 앞으로 일어날 법한 모든 일이, 수입이며 하인이며 가구까지 죄다 그녀 주위에서 끊임없이 들끓었다. 그를 향한 그녀의 호감은 변함없는 칭찬에 힘을 얻었고, 그녀의 회한은 미스 호킨스의 행복을 끝없이 되뇌는 통에 생생히 살아 있었으며, 그가 얼마나 푹 빠진 듯 보이는지를 줄곧 들먹이는 통에 그녀의 감정은 자극받았다! 집 앞을 지날 때의 그 태도며 — 모자를 눌러쓴 그 모양새까지, 죄다 그가 얼마나 사랑에 빠졌는지를 입증하는 증거라는 것이었다!\n\n만일 그것이 허락된 즐길거리였다면, 해리엇의 흔들리는 마음에 벗을 향한 아픔도 자신을 향한 자책도 없었다면, 엠마는 그 변덕을 보며 재미있어했을지도 모른다. 어떤 때는 미스터 엘턴이 우세하고 어떤 때는 마틴네가 우세했으며, 저마다 이따금 상대를 견제하는 데 쓸모가 있었다. 미스터 엘턴의 약혼은 미스터 마틴과 마주쳤던 동요를 가라앉히는 약이 되었다. 그 약혼을 알게 된 데서 비롯된 불행은, 며칠 뒤 엘리자베스 마틴이 고더드 부인 댁을 찾아온 덕에 얼마간 옆으로 밀려나 있었다. 해리엇은 집에 없었으나, 그녀 앞으로 쪽지 한 장이 마련되어 남겨져 있었으니, 꼭 마음을 울릴 만한 문체로 — 약간의 책망에 다정함을 듬뿍 섞어 — 쓴 것이었다. 미스터 엘턴이 직접 나타나기 전까지 그녀는 그 쪽지에 마음을 온통 빼앗겨, 답례로 무엇을 할 수 있을지 끊임없이 곰곰이 따지며, 차마 털어놓을 엄두는 못 내면서도 그 이상을 하고 싶어 했다. 그러나 미스터 엘턴이 직접 나타나자 그런 근심은 죄다 쫓겨났다. 그가 머무는 동안 마틴네는 잊혔다. 그리고 그가 다시 바스로 떠나는 바로 그날 아침, 엠마는 그로 인해 빚어진 시름을 얼마간 흩어 버리려, 해리엇이 엘리자베스 마틴의 방문에 답례하는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 판단했다.\n\n그 방문을 어떻게 갚을 것인가 — 무엇이 필요하고 — 무엇이 가장 무난할 것인가 하는 문제는 적이 망설여지는 고민거리였다. 와 달라는 초대를 받고도 어머니와 자매들을 아예 모른 척하는 것은 배은망덕한 일일 터였다. 그래서는 안 되었다. 그러나 그 교제를 다시 트는 데서 오는 위험이라니 — !\n\n한참을 궁리한 끝에도 엠마가 정할 수 있었던 최선이라곤, 해리엇이 답방을 가되 — 상대에게 분별이 있다면 이것이 그저 형식적인 교제일 뿐임을 납득시킬 만한 방식으로 가는 것이었다. 엠마는 그녀를 마차에 태우고 가 애비 밀에 내려 준 다음, 자기는 조금 더 멀리 마차를 몰았다가, 음흉한 청을 늘어놓거나 위태롭게 옛일로 되돌아갈 틈을 조금도 주지 않을 만큼 이내 그녀를 다시 데리러 가, 앞으로 어느 정도의 친밀함을 택할 것인지 더없이 단호하게 증명해 보일 작정이었다.\n\n그보다 나은 방도는 떠오르지 않았다. 그리고 거기에는 제 마음으로도 도무지 인정할 수 없는 무언가가 — 그럴싸하게 얼버무린 얼마간의 배은망덕이 깃들어 있었으나 — 그래도 그렇게 해야만 했으니, 안 그러면 해리엇은 대체 어찌 된단 말인가?\n\n# 제2권 5장\n\n해리엇은 방문이 도무지 내키지 않았다. 친구가 고더드 부인 댁으로 그녀를 데리러 오기 겨우 반 시간 전에, 액운이 그녀를 바로 그 자리로 이끌었던 것이다 — 그 순간 정육점 수레에 들어 올려지고 있던 트렁크 하나가 보였으니, 거기엔 \"바스, 화이트 하트, 필립 엘턴 목사 귀하\"라고 적혀 있었고, 그 수레는 트렁크를 역마차가 지나는 곳까지 실어다 줄 참이었다. 그리하여 이 세상의 모든 것이, 저 트렁크와 그 위에 적힌 글귀만 빼고는, 죄다 텅 빈 백지가 되어 버렸다.\n\n그래도 그녀는 갔다. 농장에 이르러, 격자 울타리에 받친 사과나무들 사이로 현관까지 뻗은 널찍하고 정갈한 자갈길 끝에서 마차를 내려야 했을 때, 지난가을 그토록 큰 기쁨을 안겨 주었던 온갖 광경이 눈에 들어오자, 마음속에 잔잔한 동요가 다시 일기 시작했다. 두 사람이 헤어질 때, 엠마는 해리엇이 무언가 두려움 섞인 호기심으로 주위를 둘러보는 것을 보았고, 그래서 방문을 정해 둔 십오 분을 넘기지 않게 하리라 마음먹었다. 엠마 자신은 그 시간 동안, 돈웰에 정착해 사는, 결혼한 옛 하녀를 만나러 갔다.\n\n십오 분이 지나자 엠마는 어김없이 흰 대문 앞으로 돌아왔고, 미스 스미스는 부름을 받자 지체 없이 나왔는데, 곁에는 마음을 졸이게 할 어떤 젊은 남자도 없었다. 해리엇은 자갈길을 홀로 내려왔다 — 어느 미스 마틴이 문가에 막 나타나 짐짓 의례적인 정중함으로 그녀를 배웅하는 듯했다.\n\n해리엇은 좀처럼 알아들을 만한 이야기를 들려주지 못했다. 너무 마음이 북받쳤던 것이다. 그러나 마침내 엠마는 그 말에서, 어떤 만남이었는지, 그것이 어떤 종류의 아픔을 자아내고 있는지를 헤아릴 만큼은 추려 들었다. 해리엇은 마틴 부인과 두 딸만 보았다고 했다. 그들은 그녀를 차갑게는 아니더라도 미심쩍은 태도로 맞았고, 거의 내내 그저 따분한 입에 발린 말밖에 오가지 않았다 — 마지막 순간에 이르러서야, 마틴 부인이 불쑥 미스 스미스가 부쩍 큰 것 같다고 말하면서, 한결 흥미로운 화제와 따뜻한 태도가 살아났다. 바로 그 방에서, 그녀는 지난 구월에 두 친구와 함께 키를 재었던 것이다. 창가의 벽판에는 연필로 그어 둔 표시와 적바림이 그대로 남아 있었다. 그가 해 준 것이었다. 그들 모두 그날을, 그 시각을, 그 모임을, 그 사정을 기억하는 듯했다 — 똑같은 자각, 똑같은 아쉬움을 느끼고, 예전의 그 다정한 사이로 기꺼이 되돌아갈 채비가 되어 보였다. 그리하여 다시 예전의 자기들 모습으로 돌아가던 참에 (해리엇은, 엠마도 짐작하지 않을 수 없었듯, 누구 못지않게 살갑고 즐거워질 준비가 되어 있었다), 마차가 다시 모습을 드러냈고, 모든 것이 끝나 버렸다. 그러자 방문의 격식과 그 짧음이 결정타로 사무쳤다. 여섯 달도 아닌 여섯 주를 고맙게 함께 보낸 이들에게 겨우 십사 분이라니! — 엠마는 그 광경을 머릿속에 그려 보지 않을 수 없었고, 그들이 얼마나 당연히 서운해할 만한지, 해리엇이 얼마나 자연스레 괴로워할 수밖에 없는지를 절감했다. 딱한 노릇이었다. 마틴네가 더 높은 신분이었더라면, 엠마는 무엇이든 기꺼이 내놓거나 견뎠으리라. 그들은 그만한 자격이 충분해서, 아주 조금만 더 높았더라도 족했으련만. 그러나 지금 형편으로서야, 달리 어찌할 수 있었겠는가? — 불가능한 일이었다! — 후회할 수는 없었다. 그들은 갈라서야만 했다. 그러나 그 과정에는 적잖은 아픔이 따랐고 — 이 순간 엠마 자신에게도 그러해서, 그녀는 곧 얼마간의 위안이 필요함을 느꼈고, 그것을 얻고자 랜덜스를 거쳐 집으로 돌아가기로 마음먹었다. 그녀의 마음은 미스터 엘턴과 마틴네 일에 진저리가 나 있었다. 랜덜스의 청량한 기운이 무엇보다 절실했다.\n\n좋은 계획이었다. 그러나 마차를 대문 앞에 댔을 때, \"주인 내외분 모두 안 계시다\"는 말이 들려왔다. 두 분 다 나가신 지 한참 되었고, 하인은 하트필드로 가신 듯하다고 했다.\n\n\"이런 야속한 일이 어디 있담.\" 마차를 돌리며 엠마가 외쳤다. \"이제 우린 두 분을 영락없이 못 만나게 됐어요. 어찌나 약이 오르는지! 이렇게 실망스러웠던 적이 또 있나 싶네요.\" 그러고는 마차 구석에 기대어, 투덜거림에 잠기거나 그것을 따져서 달래거나 — 아마 그 둘을 조금씩 — 했으니, 심성이 못되지 않은 사람의 마음이란 으레 이런 식으로 굴러가는 법이었다. 이윽고 마차가 멈췄다. 그녀가 고개를 들어 보니, 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인이 그녀에게 말을 걸려고 서 있던 탓에 멈춘 것이었다. 두 사람을 본 것만으로도 단박에 기쁨이 일었고, 그 목소리가 들려오자 기쁨은 한층 더 커졌다 — 미스터 웨스턴이 곧장 그녀에게 말을 건넸기 때문이다.\n\n\"안녕하시오? 안녕하시오? 우리는 방금 댁의 아버님과 앉아 있다 오는 길인데, 그토록 정정하신 걸 뵈니 반갑더이다. 프랭크가 내일 옵니다. 오늘 아침에 편지를 받았어요. 내일 저녁 시간이면 틀림없이 만나게 됩니다. 오늘은 옥스퍼드에 있고, 꼬박 두 주일 동안 머물 거예요. 내 이리될 줄 알았지요. 성탄절에 왔더라면 사흘도 못 묵었을 텐데, 나는 그때 안 온 게 늘 다행이라 여겼소. 이제 그 애한테 딱 맞는 날씨가 찾아올 참이니 — 화창하고, 보송보송하고, 안정된 날씨 말이오. 우리는 그 애와 함께 더없이 즐겁게 지낼 거요. 무엇 하나 바라던 대로 들어맞지 않은 게 없으니.\"\n\n이런 소식 앞에서는 버틸 재간이 없었고, 미스터 웨스턴처럼 행복에 겨운 얼굴이 미치는 영향을 피할 도리도 없었다 — 더구나 그 모든 것이, 비록 더 적고 더 잠잠하나 못지않게 핵심을 찌르는 그 아내의 말과 표정으로 뒷받침되고 있었으니. 웨스턴 부인이 그의 도착을 확실하다고 여긴다는 사실을 안 것만으로도, 엠마는 그것을 확실한 일로 여기기에 충분했고, 두 사람의 기쁨을 진심으로 함께 기뻐했다. 지쳐 가던 기운이 더없이 흐뭇하게 되살아났다. 닳고 닳은 과거는 다가오는 일의 신선함 속에 가라앉아 버렸다. 그리고 순간의 절반에 지나지 않는 생각의 빠른 흐름 속에서, 그녀는 이제 미스터 엘턴 이야기는 더 이상 입에 오르지 않기를 바랐다.\n\n미스터 웨스턴은 그녀에게 엔스컴에서의 약속들이 어떻게 되어 그의 아들이 꼬박 두 주일을 제 뜻대로 쓸 수 있게 되었는지, 그리고 여정의 길과 방법까지 소상히 들려주었다. 그녀는 귀 기울여 듣고, 미소 짓고, 축하의 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"머잖아 그 애를 하트필드로 데려오리다.\" 그가 끝맺으며 말했다.\n\n엠마는 이 말이 나오자 그의 아내가 그의 팔을 슬쩍 건드리는 것을 본 듯한 기분이 들었다.\n\n\"이만 가는 게 좋겠어요, 미스터 웨스턴.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아가씨들을 붙잡고 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 갈 채비는 됐소.\" 그러고는 다시 엠마를 돌아보며, \"하지만 그리 대단히 훌륭한 청년이리라 기대해선 안 됩니다. 아시다시피 내 입에서 나온 이야기만 들으셨으니까. 실은 별로 대수롭지 않은 녀석일 게요.\" — 그러나 그 순간 반짝이는 그의 두 눈은 사뭇 다른 확신을 말하고 있었다.\n\n엠마는 더없이 시치미 떼고 천연덕스러운 얼굴로, 아무것도 제 것으로 받아들이지 않는 투로 대꾸할 수 있었다.\n\n\"내일 나를 생각해 줘요, 엠마. 네 시쯤에요.\" 웨스턴 부인의 작별 당부였다. 얼마간 근심이 섞인 말이었고, 오로지 엠마만을 향한 것이었다.\n\n\"네 시라니! 장담컨대 그 애는 세 시면 여기 와 있을 거요.\" 미스터 웨스턴이 잽싸게 고쳐 말했다. 그렇게 더없이 흡족한 만남이 끝났다. 엠마의 기분은 행복이라 할 만큼 한껏 솟구쳤고, 모든 것이 사뭇 달라 보였다. 제임스와 그의 말들조차 아까만큼 굼떠 보이지 않았다. 산울타리를 바라보자니 적어도 딱총나무는 곧 싹이 트리라는 생각이 들었고, 해리엇을 돌아보았을 때는 그녀에게서도 봄기운 같은 것, 심지어 은은한 미소까지 엿보였다.\n\n그러나 \"미스터 프랭크 처칠은 옥스퍼드 말고 바스도 거쳐서 오나요?\" 하는 물음만큼은 그리 좋은 조짐이 못 되었다.\n\n그래도 지리에 밝아지는 일이든 마음의 평정이든 한꺼번에 올 수는 없는 법이었고, 엠마는 이제 그 둘 다 머잖아 오리라 마음 편히 믿을 만한 기분이었다.\n\n그 흥미로운 날의 아침이 밝았고, 웨스턴 부인의 충실한 제자는 열 시에도, 열한 시에도, 열두 시에도 네 시에 그녀를 생각하기로 한 약속을 잊지 않았다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는, 사랑하는, 근심 많은 친구.\" 자기 방에서 아래층으로 내려오며 그녀는 마음속으로 혼잣말을 했다. \"언제나 당신 자신만 빼고 누구의 편안함이든 지나치게 챙기는 분. 지금 그 자잘한 안절부절을 다 떨며, 다 잘되어 있는지 확인하려고 그 애 방을 들락날락하는 모습이 눈에 선해요.\" 그녀가 현관을 지날 때 괘종이 열두 시를 쳤다. \"열두 시구나. 네 시간 뒤에 당신을 생각하는 걸 잊지 않을게요. 그리고 내일 이맘때쯤이면, 아니 어쩌면 조금 더 늦게, 그분들이 다 함께 여기 들를 수도 있겠다는 생각을 하고 있을지도 몰라요. 분명 머잖아 그 애를 데려올 테니.\"\n\n그녀가 응접실 문을 열자, 두 신사가 아버지와 함께 앉아 있는 것이 보였다 — 미스터 웨스턴과 그의 아들이었다. 두 사람은 도착한 지 겨우 몇 분밖에 안 되었고, 미스터 웨스턴이 프랭크가 예정보다 하루 일찍 왔다는 설명을 채 마치지도, 아버지가 더없이 정중한 환영과 축하의 말 한복판에 있을 때, 엠마가 나타나 놀라움과 인사와 기쁨을 함께 나누게 되었다.\n\n그토록 오래 이야깃거리가 되고, 그토록 높은 관심을 받아 온 프랭크 처칠이 실제로 그녀 앞에 있었다 — 그가 그녀에게 소개되었고, 그녀는 그를 칭찬한 말들이 결코 지나치지 않았다고 여겼다. 그는 정말로 잘생긴 청년이었으니, 키며 풍채며 몸가짐이 모두 나무랄 데 없었고, 그 얼굴에는 아버지의 활기와 생기가 많이 어려 있었다. 그는 기민하고 영민해 보였다. 그녀는 곧장 그가 마음에 들리라는 것을 느꼈다. 게다가 그에게는 교양 있는 사람다운 편안한 태도와 선뜻 말을 건네는 붙임성이 있어서, 그가 그녀와 친해질 작정으로 왔으며 두 사람이 머잖아 친해질 수밖에 없으리라는 확신을 안겨 주었다.\n\n그는 전날 저녁에 랜덜스에 도착해 있었다. 그녀는 그가 반나절을 벌어 보려고 계획을 바꿔 더 일찍 떠나고 더 늦게까지 가고 더 빨리 달려온, 그 도착하고 싶어 한 열의가 흐뭇했다.\n\n\"내 어제 말하지 않았소.\" 미스터 웨스턴이 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"그 애가 정한 시간보다 먼저 올 거라고 다들한테 그랬잖소. 내가 옛날에 어떻게 했는지 떠올렸지요. 여행길에선 굼뜨게 갈 수가 없는 법이오. 계획한 것보다 자꾸 빨라지는 걸 어쩌지 못하지요. 그리고 기다림이 시작되기도 전에 친구들에게 불쑥 들이닥치는 그 기쁨은, 거기에 드는 얼마간의 수고보다 훨씬 값진 거랍니다.\"\n\n\"누릴 수만 있다면야 큰 기쁨이지요.\" 청년이 말했다. \"물론 제가 그렇게까지 멋대로 굴어도 될 집은 그리 많지 않습니다만. 그래도 우리 집으로 오는 길이니, 무엇이든 해도 되겠다 싶더군요.\"\n\n우리 집이라는 그 말에 아버지는 새삼 흐뭇한 눈길로 아들을 바라보았다. 엠마는 그가 사람의 마음을 사로잡을 줄 안다는 것을 곧장 확신했고, 뒤이어 나온 말들로 그 확신은 한층 굳어졌다. 그는 랜덜스가 무척 마음에 든다고, 더없이 훌륭하게 꾸며진 집이라고 했으며, 그곳이 자그마하다는 것조차 좀처럼 인정하려 들지 않았다. 그는 그 자리며, 하이베리로 가는 길이며, 하이베리 자체며, 더더욱 하트필드를 칭찬했고, 오직 제 고장만이 주는 그런 종류의 관심을 자기는 이 고장에 늘 품어 왔으며, 한번 와 보고 싶은 마음이 누구보다 컸노라고 공언했다. 그토록 다정한 정을 여태 한 번도 누려 보지 못했다는 것은 어딘가 미심쩍게 엠마의 머리를 스쳐 지나갔다. 그래도, 설령 그것이 거짓말이라 한들, 듣기 좋은 거짓말이었고, 듣기 좋게 다루어진 거짓말이었다. 그의 태도에는 꾸민 티나 과장한 티가 없었다. 그는 정말로 여느 때 없는 즐거움에 잠긴 듯이 보이고 또 말했다.\n\n두 사람의 화제는 대체로 이제 막 트인 사이에 으레 오갈 법한 것들이었다. 그의 쪽에서 묻는 말들이었다 — \"승마를 하시나요? 말 타고 다니기에 좋은 길이 있습니까? 산책하기 좋은 길은요? 이웃이 넓은 편인가요? 하이베리에는 어울릴 만한 사람들이 제법 있겠지요? 동네 안팎에 꽤 어여쁜 집들이 더러 보이던데요. 무도회는요 — 무도회가 열립니까? 음악을 즐기는 사람들이 모여 있나요?\"\n\n그러나 이 모든 것에 대한 답을 듣고 두 사람의 사이도 그만큼 진전되자, 그는 두 아버지가 서로 이야기에 빠져 있는 틈을 타 기회를 만들어, 자기 계모를 화제에 올려 그토록 멋들어진 칭찬과 그토록 따뜻한 흠모로, 또 아버지에게 안겨 준 행복과 자신을 더없이 다정하게 맞아 준 일에 대한 그토록 깊은 고마움으로 그녀를 말했으니, 이는 그가 사람을 기쁘게 할 줄 안다는, 그리고 분명 그녀의 환심을 사려 애쓸 만하다고 여긴다는 또 하나의 증거였다. 그는 웨스턴 부인이 더할 나위 없이 받아 마땅하다고 엠마가 익히 아는 것 이상으로는 한마디의 칭찬도 보태지 않았다. 그러나 그가 이 일을 거의 알 리 없는 것도 분명했다. 그는 무엇이 환영받을지를 알았다. 그 밖의 것은 거의 확신할 수 없었으리라. \"아버지의 결혼은,\" 그가 말하기를, \"더없이 현명한 처사였으며, 벗이라면 누구나 기뻐할 일이고, 그토록 큰 축복을 안겨 준 그 집안은 자기에게 더없이 큰 은혜를 베푼 것으로 길이 여겨져야 마땅하다\"는 것이었다.\n\n그는 미스 테일러의 미덕에 대해 엠마에게 고맙다고 할 수 있는 데까지 가까이 다가갔으되, 일이 으레 굴러가는 이치로 보자면 미스 우드하우스가 미스 테일러의 성품을 빚었다기보다는 미스 테일러가 미스 우드하우스의 성품을 빚었다고 보는 편이 옳으리라는 것을 아주 잊지는 않은 듯했다. 그리고 마침내, 마치 제 견해를 빙 돌아 본래의 표적에 가닿게 하려고 작정한 듯, 그는 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움에 대한 놀라움으로 이 모든 말을 마무리했다.\n\n\"우아하고 상냥한 태도야 진작 짐작하고 있었습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 솔직히, 이것저것 다 따져 보니, 그저 그런대로 봐줄 만한, 적잖이 나이 든 여인을 만나리라고 생각했을 뿐, 그 이상은 기대하지 않았답니다. 웨스턴 부인에게서 어여쁜 젊은 여인을 만나게 될 줄은 몰랐어요.\"\n\n\"제 마음으로야 웨스턴 부인이 아무리 완벽해 보여도 지나칠 게 없답니다.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"그분을 열여덟 살이라 짐작하셨다 해도 저는 흐뭇하게 들었을 거예요. 하지만 그분 본인은 그런 말을 쓰셨다고 당신과 한바탕 다투려 들걸요. 그분을 어여쁜 젊은 여인이라 말했다는 걸 눈치채게 하진 마세요.\"\n\n\"저도 그쯤은 분별할 줄 안답니다.\" 그가 답했다. \"아니요, 장담컨대 (정중하게 허리를 굽히며), 웨스턴 부인께 말씀드릴 때라면, 제가 표현이 지나치다는 소리를 들을 염려 없이 누구를 칭찬해도 좋을지쯤은 알아챌 겁니다.\"\n\n엠마는, 두 사람이 알고 지내면 무슨 일이 벌어질지에 대한 — 자기 마음을 단단히 사로잡은 그 똑같은 짐작이 — 그의 머릿속에도 한 번이라도 스쳐 지나갔을까 궁금했다. 그리고 그의 그 입에 발린 칭찬들을, 순순히 받아들인다는 표시로 봐야 할지, 아니면 거역한다는 증거로 봐야 할지도. 그의 됨됨이를 헤아리려면 그를 더 겪어 봐야 했다. 지금으로서는 그저 그 칭찬들이 듣기 좋다는 것만 느껴질 뿐이었다.\n\n미스터 웨스턴이 무슨 생각에 잠겨 있는지는 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그의 잰 눈길이 행복한 표정으로 두 사람 쪽을 흘끔거리는 것을 그녀는 거듭 잡아챘다. 그리고 그가 보지 않기로 작정했을 법한 순간조차, 그녀는 그가 종종 귀를 기울이고 있으리라 확신했다.\n\n그런 종류의 생각이라곤 아예 미치지 않는 제 아버지, 그런 통찰이나 의심이라곤 도무지 없는 그 점은 더없이 마음 놓이는 일이었다. 다행히도 그는 혼인을 미리 내다보지 못하는 만큼이나 그것을 찬성하지도 못하는 사람이었다 — 짜여 가는 혼사라면 무엇이든 늘 못마땅해하면서도, 그런 일이 있을까 미리 마음 졸인 적은 한 번도 없었다. 마치 그는, 어떤 두 사람이 혼인할 작정이라는 것이 증거로 들이닥치기 전까지는, 그들이 그토록 분별없을 리 없다고 여기는 듯했다. 엠마는 이 다행스러운 눈멂에 감사했다. 이제 아버지는, 단 하나의 언짢은 짐작이라는 걸림돌도 없이, 손님이 품었을 법한 어떤 배신도 미리 내다봄 없이, 타고난 마음씨 고운 정중함을 마음껏 베풀어, 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 여정에서 묵을 잠자리를 두고 — 길에서 이틀 밤이나 자야 했던 그 딱한 곤욕을 두고 — 살뜰히 안부를 물을 수 있었고, 그가 감기에 걸리지 않은 게 정녕 확실한지 알고 싶다는, 더없이 진솔하고 순수한 근심을 드러낼 수 있었다 — 다만 그것도, 하룻밤을 더 자고 나기 전까지는 청년 자신더러 마음 푹 놓아도 좋다고는 허락하지 못했지만.\n\n적당한 만큼 방문을 하고 나자, 미스터 웨스턴이 일어설 채비를 했다. \"이만 가 봐야겠다. 크라운 여관에 건초 일로 볼일이 있고, 포드 상점에서 웨스턴 부인 심부름도 여러 가지 있다. 그렇다고 누구든 덩달아 서두를 건 없다\"는 것이었다. 그 아들은 이 넌지시 던지는 말귀를 못 알아들을 만큼 교양 없지 않아, 곧바로 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"아버님은 더 멀리 볼일이 있어 가시니, 저는 이참에 한 번은 해야 할, 그러니 지금 해 두는 게 나을 방문을 다녀오겠습니다. 제게는 댁의 어느 이웃과 아는 사이라는 영광이 있는데요 (엠마를 돌아보며), 하이베리 안에, 혹은 그 근처에 사시는 어느 숙녀분입니다. 페어팩스라는 성을 쓰는 집안이지요. 집을 찾는 데야 어렵지 않으리라 봅니다만 — 정작 페어팩스는 본댁 성이 아닌 듯합니다. 차라리 반스나 베이츠라 해야겠지요. 그런 성을 쓰는 집안을 아시는지요?\"\n\n\"알다마다.\" 그의 아버지가 외쳤다. \"베이츠 부인 말이지. 우리가 그 댁을 지나쳐 왔잖니. 미스 베이츠가 창가에 있는 걸 봤단다. 옳지, 옳아, 너는 미스 페어팩스와 아는 사이지. 웨이머스에서 알고 지냈던 게 기억나는구나. 참한 아가씨야. 꼭 들러 보거라.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침에 굳이 들러야 할 까닭은 없습니다.\" 청년이 말했다. \"다른 날에 가도 마찬가지일 테니까요. 다만 웨이머스에서 그 정도로 알고 지낸 사이이긴 했는데—\"\n\n\"오! 오늘 가거라, 오늘 가. 미루지 마. 마땅히 해야 할 일이라면 아무리 빨리 해도 지나치지 않은 법이야. 게다가 한마디 일러두마, 프랭크. 여기서 그 아가씨에게 소홀한 기색을 보이는 일만큼은 부디 삼가야 한다. 너는 캠벨네 사람들과 함께 있던 그 아가씨를, 어울리는 누구와도 대등하던 그 아가씨를 보았지만, 여기서는 겨우 입에 풀칠할 형편의 가엾고 늙은 할머니와 함께 지낸단다. 일찍 들르지 않으면 무시하는 셈이 될 게야.\"\n\n아들은 수긍하는 낯빛이었다.\n\n\"그 아가씨가 그 인연을 이야기하는 걸 들은 적이 있어요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"아주 우아한 아가씨더군요.\"\n\n그는 그 말에 동의하긴 했으나, 워낙 잠잠한 \"네\" 한마디였던지라, 그녀는 그가 정말로 동의하는지 거의 의심이 들 지경이었다. 그러나 제인 페어팩스가 그것을 그저 평범하게 타고났다고 여겨질 정도라면, 사교계가 말하는 우아함이란 분명 사뭇 별스러운 종류일 터였다.\n\n\"전에 그 아가씨의 태도에 별달리 마음이 끌린 적이 없으셨다면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"오늘은 그러시리라 봐요. 그 아가씨를 제대로 보게 되실 거예요. 보고 또 듣게 되시겠지요 — 아니, 듣기는 아마 도무지 못 하실 거예요. 한시도 입을 다물지 않는 이모님이 계시거든요.\"\n\n\"미스 제인 페어팩스와 아는 사이시군요?\" 언제나 대화에 가장 늦게 끼어드는 미스터 우드하우스가 말했다. \"그럼 한마디 장담해 드리리다. 그 아가씨가 더없이 상냥한 처녀라는 걸 알게 되실 거요. 지금 할머니와 이모를 뵈러 와 묵고 있는데, 다들 무척 훌륭한 분들이오. 나는 그분들을 평생 알고 지냈소. 분명 당신을 보면 몹시 반가워하실 게요. 그리고 우리 집 하인 하나를 딸려 보내 길을 안내해 드리리다.\"\n\n\"어르신, 천만의 말씀입니다. 아버지가 일러 주시면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 자네 아버님은 그리 멀리 가시지 않잖소. 그분은 길 건너 저편 크라운 여관까지만 가시는데, 집이 워낙 많아 자네가 길을 못 찾고 헤맬 수도 있다오. 게다가 큰길로 다니지 않으면 길이 몹시 질척하지. 어쨌든 어디서 길을 건너는 게 제일 좋을지는 우리 집 마부가 일러 줄 수 있소.\"\n\n미스터 프랭크 처칠은 그래도 한사코 사양하며 짐짓 더없이 진지한 낯빛을 지었고, 그의 아버지는 \"여보게, 그건 전혀 그럴 필요가 없소. 프랭크는 물웅덩이쯤은 보면 알아채는 녀석이고, 베이츠 부인 댁이라면 크라운에서 깡총 한 번 뛰어 닿을 거리요\" 하고 큰 소리로 거들며 든든히 편들어 주었다.\n\n두 사람은 단둘이 가도 좋다는 허락을 받았다. 그리하여 한쪽은 다정하게 고개를 끄덕이고, 다른 한쪽은 우아하게 허리를 굽혀, 두 신사가 작별을 고했다. 엠마는 이렇게 시작된 사귐에 무척 흡족한 채로 남았고, 이제 하루 중 어느 때든 랜덜스에 있는 그들 모두를, 그들이 편안히 잘 지내리라는 온전한 믿음을 품고 떠올릴 수 있게 되었다.\n\n# 제2권 6장\n\n이튿날 아침 프랭크 처칠이 다시 찾아왔다. 그는 웨스턴 부인과 함께 왔는데, 부인에게도 하이베리에도 무척 다정하게 마음을 붙인 듯했다. 보아하니 그는 부인이 늘 산책을 나서는 시각이 될 때까지 집에서 더없이 정답게 함께 앉아 있었던 모양이었다. 그리고 어디로 걸을지 골라 보라는 청을 받자 곧장 하이베리로 정했다. \"어느 방향이든 아주 즐거운 산책길이 있으리라는 건 의심하지 않습니다. 하지만 제게 맡겨 주신다면 저는 늘 같은 곳을 고를 겁니다. 하이베리, 그 탁 트이고 명랑하고 행복해 보이는 하이베리야말로 변함없이 저를 끌어당기는 곳이지요.\" 웨스턴 부인에게 하이베리란 곧 하트필드를 뜻했고, 그에게도 같은 뜻으로 해석되기를 부인은 믿었다. 두 사람은 곧장 그리로 걸어갔다.\n\n엠마는 그들이 오리라고는 거의 예상하지 못했다. 아들이 아주 잘생겼다는 말을 듣겠다고 반 분쯤 들렀다 간 미스터 웨스턴은 그들의 계획을 전혀 몰랐기 때문이었다. 그래서 두 사람이 팔짱을 끼고 함께 집으로 걸어 올라오는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀에게는 반가운 놀라움이었다. 그녀는 그를 다시 보고 싶었고, 특히 웨스턴 부인과 함께 있는 모습을 보고 싶었다. 부인을 대하는 그의 태도에 따라 그에 대한 그녀의 평가가 정해질 참이었기 때문이다. 그 점에서 모자람이 있다면, 어떤 것으로도 그것을 메울 수 없을 터였다. 그러나 두 사람이 함께 있는 모습을 보고 그녀는 완전히 만족했다. 그가 도리를 다하는 것은 그저 고운 말이나 과장된 칭찬에 그치지 않았다. 부인을 대하는 그의 태도 전체보다 더 알맞고 흐뭇한 것은 없었으며, 부인을 벗으로 여기고 그 애정을 얻고자 하는 마음을 그보다 더 기껍게 드러내는 것은 없었다. 게다가 그들의 방문이 그날 오전 나머지 시간을 모두 채웠으므로, 엠마가 합당한 판단을 내릴 시간은 넉넉했다. 셋이서 한두 시간을 함께 거닐었다 — 처음에는 하트필드의 관목 숲을 한 바퀴 돌고, 그다음에는 하이베리에서. 그는 무엇에든 기뻐했고, 미스터 우드하우스의 귀에 들릴 만큼 하트필드를 흡족하게 칭송했다. 그리고 더 멀리 가 보기로 정해지자, 마을 전체를 알고 싶다는 바람을 털어놓으며, 엠마가 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 자주 칭찬하고 흥미를 보일 거리를 찾아냈다.\n\n그의 호기심을 끄는 것들 가운데 더러는 아주 갸륵한 마음씨를 드러냈다. 그는 아버지가 그토록 오래 살았고 또 할아버지의 집이기도 했던 그 집을 보여 달라고 청했다. 그리고 자기를 길러 준 늙은 유모가 아직 살아 있다는 것을 떠올리고는, 그 오두막을 찾아 거리 이 끝에서 저 끝까지 걸었다. 비록 어떤 추구나 관찰의 대목에서는 딱히 칭찬할 만한 점이 없기도 했지만, 그것들은 통틀어 하이베리 전반에 대한 호의를 보여 주었으니, 함께 있는 사람들에게는 그것이 분명 칭찬할 만한 점과 매우 비슷하게 느껴질 수밖에 없었다.\n\n엠마는 지켜보며 이렇게 단정했다. 지금 드러난 것과 같은 마음씨를 지닌 사람이라면, 그가 일부러 발길을 끊고 멀리 떨어져 있었다고는 도저히 볼 수 없으며, 어떤 역할을 꾸미거나 진심 없는 다짐을 늘어놓으며 자랑한 것도 아니었고, 미스터 나이틀리가 그를 두고 한 평가는 분명 공정하지 못했다고.\n\n그들이 처음 발을 멈춘 곳은 크라운 여관이었다. 그런 종류로는 가장 으뜸가는 곳이긴 했으나 보잘것없는 집이었고, 역마 두어 쌍을 매어 두었는데 이는 길에 손님이 몰려서라기보다 이웃의 편의를 위해서였다. 그의 일행은 그곳에서 무슨 흥미가 일어 발이 묶이리라고는 예상하지 못했다. 그러나 지나치다가 그들은 보기에도 나중에 덧붙인 그 큰 방의 내력을 들려주었다. 여러 해 전 무도회장으로 지어진 그 방은, 이 일대가 유난히 사람도 많고 춤판이 무르익던 시절에는 이따금 그렇게 쓰이기도 했었다. 그러나 그토록 빛나던 날들은 오래전에 지나가 버렸고, 이제 그 방이 쓰일 일이라곤 고작 이곳의 신사들과 어중간한 신사들 사이에 만들어진 휘스트 모임을 들여놓는 것이 가장 높은 쓰임새였다. 그는 곧바로 흥미를 보였다. 무도회장이라는 그 내력이 그의 마음을 사로잡았던 것이다. 그리고 그냥 지나치는 대신, 그는 활짝 열려 있는 위쪽의 훌륭한 미닫이창 두 짝 앞에 몇 분이나 멈춰 서서, 안을 들여다보며 그곳의 가능성을 가늠하고, 본래의 쓰임새가 끊겨 버린 것을 안타까워했다. 그는 그 방에서 아무런 흠도 보지 못했고, 그들이 일러 주는 흠도 하나 인정하려 들지 않았다. 아니, 길이도 넉넉하고 폭도 넉넉하고 보기에도 넉넉히 훌륭하다고. 안락하게 즐기기에 딱 알맞은 인원을 들일 수 있을 거라고. 적어도 겨울 내내 이 주에 한 번씩은 여기서 무도회를 열어야 마땅하다고. 어째서 미스 우드하우스가 이 방의 옛 좋은 시절을 되살리지 않았느냐고 — 하이베리에서라면 무엇이든 할 수 있는 그녀가! 이곳에 마땅한 가문이 부족하다는 것, 그리고 이곳과 그 바로 인근을 넘어서는 누구도 참석하도록 꾈 수 없으리라는 확신을 그들은 일러 주었다. 그러나 그는 수긍하지 않았다. 주위에 보이는 이렇게 번듯한 집들이 그만한 모임에 채울 인원도 못 댄다는 것을 그는 도무지 믿을 수 없었다. 그리고 구체적인 내막이 제시되고 가문들이 설명된 뒤에도, 그는 그렇게 뒤섞이는 불편이 무슨 대수일까 보냐고, 이튿날 아침이면 누구나 제자리로 돌아가는 데 조금의 어려움도 없으리라고, 여전히 인정하려 들지 않았다. 그는 춤을 추고 싶어 마음이 잔뜩 기운 젊은이답게 우겨 댔다. 그리고 엠마는 처칠 집안의 습성을 누르고 웨스턴 집안의 기질이 그토록 또렷이 이기는 것을 보고 적잖이 놀랐다. 그는 아버지의 온갖 생기와 활기, 명랑한 기분과 사교적인 성향을 다 지닌 듯했고, 엔스컴의 거만함이나 새침함은 조금도 없었다. 거만함으로 말하자면, 실은 어쩌면 모자랄 지경이었다. 신분의 뒤섞임에 무심한 그 태도는 마음의 품위 없음에 너무 가까웠다. 그러나 그는 자기가 가볍게 여기는 그 폐단을 헤아릴 처지가 못 되었다. 그것은 그저 발랄한 기운의 분출일 뿐이었다.\n* 휘스트(whist): 네 사람이 두 편으로 나뉘어 하는 카드놀이로, 19세기 영국 사교계에서 크게 유행했다.\n\n마침내 그는 설득에 못 이겨 크라운 여관 앞을 떠났다. 그리고 이제 베이츠 모녀가 세 들어 사는 집과 거의 마주 보게 되자, 엠마는 그가 어제 방문하기로 했던 것을 떠올리고는, 다녀왔는지 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아! 네.\" 그가 대답했다. \"마침 그 이야기를 하려던 참이었습니다. 아주 성공적인 방문이었어요. 세 분 다 뵈었지요. 그리고 미리 귀띔해 주신 덕에 무척 신세를 졌습니다. 그 말 많은 이모님이 저를 전혀 예상치 못한 틈에 덮쳤더라면, 저는 영락없이 죽었을 겁니다. 그래도 사정이 그러했으니, 저는 그저 더없이 당치 않게 긴 방문을 하고 만 것뿐이지요. 십 분이면 다 됐을 텐데요, 어쩌면 그게 적당하기도 했을 거고요. 아버지께는 분명 아버지보다 먼저 집에 가 있겠다고 말씀드렸는데 — 도무지 빠져나올 수가 없었어요, 틈이라곤 없었지요. 그러다 (다른 데서 저를 못 찾으신) 아버지가 마침내 그리로 합류하셨을 때, 저는 제가 거의 사십오 분이나 그분들과 앉아 있었다는 걸 알고 그야말로 깜짝 놀랐답니다. 그 인자하신 부인께서 그 전에는 빠져나갈 틈을 도무지 안 주셨거든요.\"\n\n\"그래서 미스 페어팩스는 어때 보이던가요?\"\n\n\"안색이 나쁘던데요, 아주 나빴어요 — 그러니까, 젊은 아가씨가 안색이 나쁘다고 해도 된다면 말입니다. 하지만 그런 표현은 좀처럼 쓸 수 없는 거지요, 웨스턴 부인, 그렇잖아요? 숙녀는 결코 안색이 나쁠 수 없으니까요. 그리고 진지하게 말하자면, 미스 페어팩스는 천성이 워낙 창백해서 거의 늘 건강이 좋지 않은 듯한 인상을 줍니다. 도무지 한심할 만큼 혈색이 없다고나 할까요.\"\n\n엠마는 이에 동의하지 않고, 미스 페어팩스의 혈색을 두둔하는 열변을 늘어놓기 시작했다. \"물론 결코 화사한 적은 없었지만, 평소에 병색이 도는 빛깔이라고는 인정할 수 없어요. 그리고 그 피부에는 부드럽고 섬세한 데가 있어서, 그녀 얼굴의 분위기에 남다른 우아함을 더해 주죠.\" 그는 마땅한 경의를 갖추어 귀를 기울였고, 똑같은 말을 하는 사람을 여럿 들어 보았다고 인정했다. 그러나 그래도 솔직히 말하자면, 자기로서는 건강한 혈색의 그 고운 광채가 없는 것을 무엇으로도 메울 수 없다고 했다. 이목구비가 평범한 경우에는 고운 혈색이 그 전부에 아름다움을 더해 주고, 이목구비가 훌륭한 경우에는 그 효과가 — 다행히 그는 그 효과가 무엇인지 굳이 그려 보일 필요가 없었다.\n\n\"뭐,\" 엠마가 말했다. \"취향은 따질 게 못 되죠. 적어도 혈색만 빼면 그녀를 흠모하시는 거네요.\"\n\n그는 고개를 가로저으며 웃었다. \"저는 미스 페어팩스와 그 혈색을 따로 떼어 놓을 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"웨이머스에서 자주 보셨어요? 같은 모임에 자주 어울리셨나요?\"\n\n이때 그들은 마침 포드 상점에 다가가고 있었는데, 그가 황급히 외쳤다. \"하! 이게 바로 아버지 말씀에 따르면 누구나 날마다 평생토록 드나든다는 그 가게로군요. 아버지 본인도 이레 가운데 엿새는 하이베리에 오시는데, 늘 포드에 볼일이 있다고 하시더군요. 폐가 안 되신다면, 부디 들어가 보지요. 제가 이곳 사람이라는 것을, 진짜 하이베리 시민이라는 것을 증명할 수 있게요. 포드에서 뭔가 사야겠어요. 그게 곧 제 시민권을 얻는 일이 될 테니까요. 장갑쯤은 팔겠지요.\"\n\n\"아! 그럼요, 장갑이며 뭐며 다 있어요. 그 애향심이 정말 감탄스럽네요. 하이베리에서 사랑받으실 거예요. 오시기 전부터도 인기가 대단하셨죠, 미스터 웨스턴의 아드님이니까요. 하지만 포드에서 반 기니만 쓰시면, 그 인기가 당신 자신의 미덕 위에 서게 될 거랍니다.\"\n\n그들이 들어섰다. 그리고 \"남성용 비버 모자\"며 \"요크 무두가죽\" 따위의 매끈하게 잘 묶인 꾸러미들이 내려져 진열대 위에 펼쳐지는 동안, 그가 말했다. \"그런데 죄송합니다, 미스 우드하우스, 제게 무슨 말씀을 하고 계셨지요. 제 애국심의 발작이 터지던 바로 그 순간에 뭔가 말씀하시던 참이었는데. 그 말씀을 놓치게 하지 말아 주십시오. 장담컨대 세상의 명성을 아무리 크게 떨친다 한들, 사사로운 삶의 행복을 하나라도 잃는 것을 메워 주지는 못할 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그저 여쭤본 거예요. 웨이머스에서 미스 페어팩스와 그 일행을 많이 알고 지내셨는지요.\"\n\n\"이제야 그 물음의 뜻을 알겠는데, 아주 불공정한 물음이라고 말씀드려야겠군요. 알고 지낸 정도를 결정하는 건 언제나 숙녀 쪽의 권리지요. 미스 페어팩스가 이미 자기 나름의 이야기를 했을 게 분명한데요. 그녀가 인정하기로 한 것보다 더 많이 주장해서 제 입장을 곤란하게 만들지는 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"세상에! 그 사람 못지않게 입이 무거우시네요. 하지만 그녀의 이야기는 무엇에 대해서든 짐작에 맡길 여지가 너무 많아요. 워낙 입이 무겁고, 누구에 대해서든 조금의 정보도 주기를 한사코 꺼리니, 정말이지 그녀와 알고 지낸 일이라면 무어든 마음대로 말씀하셔도 되겠다 싶을 정도예요.\"\n\n\"정말 그래도 되겠습니까? 그렇다면 진실을 말하지요, 그만큼 제게 잘 맞는 것도 없으니까요. 저는 웨이머스에서 그녀를 자주 만났습니다. 캠벨 가족과는 런던에서 조금 알고 지냈고, 웨이머스에서는 같은 무리에 아주 자주 어울렸지요. 캠벨 대령은 무척 유쾌한 분이고, 캠벨 부인은 다정하고 마음이 따뜻한 분입니다. 저는 그분들이 다 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"미스 페어팩스가 살면서 어떤 처지인지, 그러니까 무엇이 될 운명인지는 아시겠지요?\"\n\n\"네 — (다소 머뭇거리며) — 아는 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"민감한 화제로 들어서는구나, 엠마.\" 웨스턴 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내가 여기 있다는 걸 잊지 말아라. 미스터 프랭크 처칠은 네가 미스 페어팩스의 처지를 말하면 뭐라 답해야 할지 난감해하는걸. 내가 좀 더 멀리 떨어져 있어 주마.\"\n\n\"저는 정말이지 그 사람을,\" 엠마가 말했다. \"제 벗이자 더없이 소중한 벗이 아닌 다른 무엇으로 생각해 본 적이 없어요.\"\n\n그는 그런 마음을 온전히 이해하고 또 높이 산다는 표정이었다.\n\n장갑을 사고 다시 가게를 나선 뒤, 프랭크 처칠이 말했다. \"우리가 이야기하던 그 아가씨가 연주하는 걸 들어 보신 적 있습니까?\"\n\n\"들어 봤느냐고요!\" 엠마가 되받았다. \"그녀가 하이베리에 얼마나 깊이 속한 사람인지 잊으셨나 봐요. 우리 둘이 배우기 시작한 이래로 평생 해마다 그녀의 연주를 들었는걸요. 정말 매혹적으로 친답니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하세요? 정말로 판단할 줄 아는 누군가의 의견을 듣고 싶었어요. 제게는 그녀가 잘 치는 것처럼, 그러니까 상당한 취향을 갖추어 치는 것처럼 보였지만, 저 자신은 이 방면을 전혀 모르거든요. 음악을 무척 좋아하긴 해도, 누구의 연주를 판단할 솜씨나 자격은 조금도 없습니다. 저는 그녀의 연주가 칭송받는 것을 익히 들어 왔지요. 그리고 그녀가 잘 친다고 여겨진 증거 하나가 기억납니다. 어떤 남자가 — 음악에 무척 조예가 깊은 남자가, 게다가 다른 여자와 사랑에 빠져 — 약혼까지 하고 — 결혼을 코앞에 둔 사람이었는데 — 그러면서도 만일 문제의 그 아가씨가 대신 앉을 수만 있다면, 결코 그 다른 여자에게는 악기 앞에 앉으라고 청하지 않더라는 거예요. 한쪽을 들을 수 있는 한, 결코 다른 한쪽을 듣고 싶어 하는 것 같지 않았지요. 음악적 재능이 알려진 남자가 그러는 걸 보고, 저는 그게 어느 정도 증거가 된다고 생각했습니다.\"\n\n\"증거가 되고말고요!\" 엠마가 자못 즐거워하며 말했다. \"미스터 딕슨이 음악에 무척 조예가 깊은 분이라고요? 우리가 반 시간 안에 당신한테서 그분들 모두에 대해 알아내는 게, 미스 페어팩스가 반년 동안 베풀어 줄 것보다 더 많겠어요.\"\n\n\"네, 미스터 딕슨과 미스 캠벨이 바로 그 두 사람이었지요. 그리고 저는 그게 아주 강력한 증거라고 생각했습니다.\"\n\n\"분명히요 — 아주 강력했네요. 사실대로 말하자면, 제가 미스 캠벨이었다면 도무지 달갑지 않았을 만큼 훨씬 더 강력했어요. 사랑보다 음악이 더 많은 남자, 눈보다 귀가 더 밝은 남자, 제 감정보다 고운 소리에 더 예민한 남자라니 — 저는 그런 걸 봐줄 수가 없거든요. 미스 캠벨은 그걸 어떻게 받아들이는 것 같던가요?\"\n\n\"그게, 그녀의 아주 각별한 벗이었으니까요.\"\n\n\"위로랍시고 그게!\" 엠마가 웃으며 말했다. \"차라리 낯선 사람이 더 사랑받는 편이 낫지, 자기의 아주 각별한 벗이 사랑받는 건 — 낯선 사람이라면 다시 마주칠 일이 없을 수도 있지만 — 무엇이든 자기보다 더 잘하는 아주 각별한 벗이 늘 곁에 붙어 있는 그 비참함이라니! 가엾은 딕슨 부인! 뭐, 그녀가 아일랜드에 가서 자리 잡은 게 다행이네요.\"\n\n\"맞는 말씀입니다. 미스 캠벨로서는 그리 기분 좋은 일은 아니었지요. 하지만 그녀는 정말이지 그걸 마음에 두는 것 같지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 더 잘된 일이거나 — 더 못된 일이거나죠. 어느 쪽인지는 모르겠어요. 하지만 그게 그녀의 너그러움이든 아둔함이든, 우정의 빠름이든 감정의 무딤이든, 분명 마음에 두었을 사람이 한 명 있을 거예요. 미스 페어팩스 본인이요. 그 부당하고 위험한 편애를 분명 느꼈을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 점에 관해서는 — 저는 잘 —\"\n\n\"아! 제가 당신한테서, 아니 그 누구한테서든 미스 페어팩스의 속마음에 대한 이야기를 듣고 싶어 한다고 생각하진 마세요. 제 짐작에 그 마음은 본인 말고 어느 누구도 모를 테니까요. 하지만 미스터 딕슨이 청할 때마다 그녀가 계속 연주를 했다면, 무엇이든 마음대로 짐작해 볼 만하지요.\"\n\n\"그분들 사이에는 아주 더없이 좋은 사이가 있는 것처럼 보였는데요 —\" 그가 다소 빠르게 말을 꺼냈다가, 스스로를 멈추며 덧붙였다. \"하지만 그분들이 정말 어떤 사이였는지, 그 모든 게 무대 뒤에서는 어떠했을지 저로서는 말할 도리가 없습니다. 겉으로는 매끄러웠다고만 말씀드릴 수 있지요. 하지만 미스 페어팩스를 어릴 적부터 아셨던 당신이라면, 그녀의 성품에 대해서, 또 위기의 상황에서 그녀가 어떻게 처신할 법한지에 대해서, 저보다 더 잘 판단하실 수 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"분명 어릴 적부터 알았지요. 우리는 함께 아이였고 함께 여자가 되었어요. 그러니 우리가 친밀하리라고, 그녀가 친지들을 보러 올 때마다 서로 마음이 맞으리라고 짐작하는 게 자연스럽겠죠. 하지만 우리는 한 번도 그런 적이 없어요. 어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지 저도 잘 모르겠어요. 어쩌면 조금은 제 쪽의 못된 마음 탓일 수도 있죠. 늘 이모와 할머니, 그리고 그분들 무리 전체에게 그토록 떠받들리고 그토록 추어올려지는 아가씨를 보면, 저는 도무지 정이 안 가고 싫증부터 났거든요. 게다가 그 입 무거움 — 저는 그렇게 철두철미하게 입 무거운 사람에게는 도무지 마음을 붙일 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"정말이지 더없이 사람을 밀어내는 성질이지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"물론 더러는 아주 편리하기도 하겠지만, 결코 호감을 주진 않습니다. 입이 무거우면 안전하긴 해도, 끌리는 데가 없어요. 입 무거운 사람을 사랑할 수는 없는 노릇이지요.\"\n\n\"제 쪽으로 그 입 무거움이 풀리기 전까지는요. 그러고 나면 끌림은 오히려 더 클 수도 있겠죠. 하지만 누군가의 입 무거움을 깨뜨리는 수고를 무릅쓰고 벗을 하나 얻으려면, 저는 지금까지보다 훨씬 더 벗이, 아니 마음 맞는 말동무가 절실해져야 할 거예요. 미스 페어팩스와 저 사이의 친밀함이란 도무지 가당치 않은 일이에요. 그녀를 나쁘게 볼 이유는 없어요 — 조금도요 — 다만 말이며 태도가 그토록 극단적이고 한결같이 조심스럽다는 것, 누구에 대해서든 또렷한 생각을 내비치기를 그토록 두려워한다는 것이, 자칫 무언가 감출 게 있는 것 아닌가 하는 의심을 불러일으키기 쉽다는 점만 빼면요.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 말에 완전히 동의했다. 그렇게 오래 함께 거닐며 그렇게 똑같이 생각하고 나니, 엠마는 그와 어찌나 잘 아는 사이가 된 듯한지, 이것이 겨우 두 번째 만남이라는 게 도무지 믿기지 않을 지경이었다. 그는 그녀가 예상했던 것과 꼭 같지는 않았다. 어떤 생각들에 있어서는 세상물정에 닳은 구석이 덜했고, 응석받이로 자란 행운아 같은 면이 덜했으니, 그래서 그녀가 예상했던 것보다 나았다. 그의 생각들은 더 온건해 보였고, 그의 감정은 더 따뜻해 보였다. 그녀는 그가 미스터 엘턴의 집을 두고 보인 태도에 특히 깊은 인상을 받았다. 교회와 마찬가지로 그 집도 그는 일부러 가서 보려 했고, 그 집을 두고 흠을 잔뜩 잡는 일에는 그들과 한편이 되려 하지 않았다. 아니, 그는 그 집이 나쁜 집이라고는 믿을 수 없다고, 그런 집을 가졌다고 동정받을 만한 그런 집은 아니라고 했다. 사랑하는 여인과 함께 나눌 집이라면, 그 집을 가졌다고 동정받을 남자는 아무도 없으리라고. 그 안에는 참된 안락을 누리기에 넉넉한 공간이 있을 게 틀림없다고. 그 이상을 바라는 남자라면 분명 멍청이일 거라고.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 웃으며, 그가 뭘 알고 하는 소리가 아니라고 말했다. 늘 큰 집에서만 살아 와서, 그 크기에 얼마나 많은 이점과 편의가 따라붙는지 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없으니, 작은 집에 어쩔 수 없이 따르는 궁색함을 그가 헤아릴 리 없다는 것이었다. 그러나 엠마는 속으로, 그가 정말로 알고 하는 소리라고, 그리고 그가 일찍 자리 잡고 떳떳한 동기로 결혼하려는 아주 갸륵한 마음을 보이고 있다고 단정했다. 가정부의 방이 없다거나 집사의 식기실이 시원찮다거나 하는 것이 가정의 평화에 끼칠 폐단을 그가 미처 몰랐을지는 모른다. 그러나 엔스컴이 자기를 행복하게 해 줄 수 없다는 것을, 그리고 일단 마음을 붙인 사람이 생기면 일찍 살림을 차리기 위해 기꺼이 재산의 많은 부분을 내놓으리라는 것을, 그가 더없이 절실히 느끼고 있다는 것만은 분명했다.\n\n# 제2권 7장\n\n프랭크 처칠에 대한 엠마의 더없이 좋은 평가는, 그가 그저 머리를 자르려고 런던으로 떠났다는 말을 듣고 이튿날 조금 흔들렸다. 아침을 들다 별안간 변덕이 발동한 모양으로, 그는 역마차를 부르더니 길을 떠났는데, 저녁때까지 돌아올 작정이었으나 머리를 자른다는 것 말고는 이렇다 할 용건이 보이지 않았다. 그런 심부름 길을 위해 십육 마일을 두 번씩이나 오가는 데 해로울 것이야 분명 없었지만, 거기에는 그녀가 도무지 인정할 수 없는 멋부림과 부질없음의 기색이 감돌았다. 그것은 어제 그에게서 알아본 듯싶었던 계획의 합리성, 씀씀이의 절제, 나아가 사심 없는 따뜻한 마음씨와도 어울리지 않았다. 허영, 사치, 변화를 좋아하는 성정, 좋든 나쁘든 무언가를 하지 않고는 못 배기는 들뜬 기질, 아버지와 웨스턴 부인의 기쁨에 무심하고 제 행실이 남들 눈에 어떻게 비칠지 개의치 않는 무신경 — 그는 이 온갖 혐의를 받을 만했다. 아버지는 그저 그를 멋쟁이 허풍선이라 부르며 아주 재미난 이야깃거리로 여겼다. 그러나 웨스턴 부인이 그 일을 마뜩잖아한다는 것은, 그 일을 되도록 빨리 넘겨 버리며 \"젊은 사람이라면 누구나 자잘한 변덕쯤은 있게 마련이지요\" 하는 말 외에 다른 한마디도 보태지 않은 데서 충분히 드러났다.\n\n이 자그마한 흠을 빼고는, 그가 머무는 동안 지금까지 벗에게 좋은 인상만을 심어 주었다는 것을 엠마는 알 수 있었다. 웨스턴 부인은 그가 얼마나 살뜰하고 유쾌한 말동무가 되어 주는지 — 그 성품에서 얼마나 마음에 드는 점을 두루 보았는지를 거리낌 없이 들려주었다. 그는 무척 트인 성정인 듯했고, 분명 무척 쾌활하고 활기찬 사람이었다. 그의 생각에서 그릇된 데라곤 찾아볼 수 없었고, 옳은 데는 단연 많았다. 외삼촌 이야기를 따뜻한 존경을 담아 했고, 그 이야기를 즐겨 했으며, 외삼촌은 제멋대로 두기만 하면 세상에 둘도 없이 좋은 분일 거라고 했다. 외숙모에게는 정을 붙일 수 없었지만, 그래도 그녀의 친절만큼은 고마워하며 인정했고, 늘 존중하는 투로 말하려는 듯했다. 이 모든 것이 매우 미더웠다. 그리고 머리를 자르겠다는 그 불운한 변덕만 아니었다면, 그녀의 상상이 그에게 부여한 그 대단한 영예에 그가 걸맞지 않다고 할 만한 구석은 하나도 없었다 — 정말로 그녀를 사랑한다는 영예까지는 아니더라도, 적어도 그 문턱에 바짝 다가서 있고, 다만 그녀 자신의 무심함 덕에 가까스로 모면한 처지라는 영예 — (그녀는 여전히 결코 결혼하지 않겠다는 결심을 굳히고 있었으니) — 요컨대 두 집안의 모든 지인이 그녀의 짝으로 점찍어 둔 사람이라는 영예 말이다.\n\n미스터 웨스턴은 그 나름대로, 얼마간 무게가 실릴 만한 미덕 하나를 그 명세에 보탰다. 그는 프랭크가 그녀를 몹시 흠모하고 있다는 것 — 그녀를 무척 아름답고 무척 매력적이라 여긴다는 것을 그녀가 알게 해 주었다. 그를 두고 그토록 좋게 말할 거리가 많고 보니, 그녀는 그를 모질게 판단해서는 안 되겠다고 느꼈다. 웨스턴 부인이 말한 대로, \"젊은 사람이라면 누구나 자잘한 변덕쯤은 있게 마련\"이었다.\n\n서리에서 새로 사귄 이들 가운데, 그렇게 너그럽지만은 않은 사람이 하나 있었다. 대체로 그는 돈웰과 하이베리 두 교구를 통틀어 무척 호의적인 평가를 받았다. 그토록 잘생긴 젊은이 — 그토록 자주 미소 짓고 그토록 곱게 절하는 젊은이의 자잘한 지나침에는 너그러운 용서가 베풀어졌다. 그러나 그들 가운데, 절이며 미소 따위로는 그 준엄한 비판의 힘이 결코 누그러지지 않는 사람이 하나 있었으니, 바로 미스터 나이틀리였다. 그 일은 하트필드에서 그에게 전해졌다. 한순간 그는 잠자코 있었다. 그러나 엠마는 그가 거의 곧바로, 손에 든 신문을 들여다보며 혼잣말하는 것을 들었다. \"흠! 내가 짐작한 그대로, 시시하고 어리석은 녀석이로군.\" 그녀는 발끈하고 싶기도 했다. 그러나 한순간 살펴보고는, 그것이 정말로 제 속을 풀려는 말일 뿐 그녀를 자극하려던 것이 아님을 알아채고, 그냥 넘겨 버렸다.\n\n비록 한 가지 점에서는 달갑잖은 소식을 들고 온 것이었지만, 미스터 웨스턴 부부의 그날 아침 방문은 또 다른 점에서 유난히 때맞은 것이었다. 그들이 하트필드에 머무는 동안 어떤 일이 생겨 엠마가 그들의 조언을 구하고 싶게 되었고, 게다가 더욱 다행스럽게도, 그녀가 바란 것은 바로 그들이 건넨 그 조언이었다.\n\n일의 자초지종은 이러했다. 콜 부부는 하이베리에 자리 잡은 지 몇 해 된 사람들로, 더없이 좋은 부류였다 — 다정하고, 후하고, 잘난 체하지 않았다. 그러나 한편으로 그들은 출신이 변변치 못했고, 장사를 하는 데다, 점잖음으로 치자면 그저 그만한 정도였다. 처음 이 고장에 들어왔을 때만 해도 그들은 수입에 맞추어 조용히 살았고, 교제도 적었으며, 그 적은 교제마저 돈을 들이지 않았다. 그러나 근래 한두 해 사이에 형편이 상당히 펴졌으니 — 도시의 사업이 더 큰 이문을 내고, 운수가 대체로 그들에게 미소 지은 것이었다. 재산이 불어나니 안목도 높아졌다. 더 큰 집을 바라고, 더 많은 교제를 원하게 되었다. 그들은 집을 늘리고, 하인 수를 늘리고, 온갖 씀씀이를 늘렸다. 그리하여 어느덧 재산과 살림의 격식에서 하트필드 집안 다음가는 둘째가 되어 있었다. 그들의 사교 취향과 새 식당을 두고, 사람들은 으레 그들이 만찬 모임을 열리라 짐작했다. 이미 몇 차례 모임이 — 주로 독신 남자들끼리 — 열린 터였다. 격식 있는 일류 가문들 — 돈웰이며 하트필드며 랜덜스를 그들이 감히 청하리라고는 엠마로서는 도무지 상상하기 어려웠다. 설령 청한다 한들 무엇으로도 그녀를 그곳에 가게 할 수는 없으리라. 그러면서도 그녀는, 아버지의 알려진 습관 탓에 자신의 거절이 바라는 만큼의 무게를 갖지 못하리라는 것이 못내 아쉬웠다. 콜 부부는 그 나름대로 무척 존경할 만한 사람들이었으나, 윗자리 가문이 어떤 조건으로 그들을 찾아갈지 정하는 것이 그들이 할 일은 아니라는 점만은 깨우쳐 주어야 마땅했다. 이 교훈을 일러 줄 사람은 결국 자기뿐이리라는 것을 그녀는 몹시 두려워했다. 미스터 나이틀리에게는 거의 기대를 걸지 않았고, 미스터 웨스턴에게는 아예 걸지 않았다.\n\n그러나 이 주제넘은 짓에 어떻게 맞설지를 그녀는 그 일이 일어나기 여러 주 전부터 마음에 정해 둔 터라, 막상 그 모욕이 닥쳐왔을 때는 그녀의 심경이 사뭇 다른 데 가 있었다. 돈웰과 랜덜스에는 초대장이 왔는데, 아버지와 그녀에게는 아무것도 오지 않았다. 그것을 두고 웨스턴 부인이 \"댁에는 차마 그런 무례를 못 하는 거겠지요. 댁이 외식하지 않는다는 걸 다들 아니까요\" 하고 풀이해 준 것만으로는 영 흡족하지 않았다. 거절할 권리만이라도 가지고 싶었다고 그녀는 느꼈다. 그러고는, 그곳에 모일 사람들 — 바로 그녀가 가장 아끼는 이들로 꼭 채워진 모임을 머릿속에 자꾸자꾸 그려 보다가, 어쩌면 자신이 응하고 싶은 마음에 솔깃했을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 해리엇이 저녁에 그 자리에 온다 했고, 베이츠네도 온다 했다. 어제 하이베리를 거닐며 그들이 그 이야기를 나누었는데, 프랭크 처칠은 그녀가 빠진다는 것을 무척이나 안타까워했다. 그날 저녁이 무도회로 끝날 수도 있지 않겠느냐고, 그가 물어 왔던 것이다. 그 가능성 하나만으로도 그녀의 마음은 한층 더 약이 올랐다. 그리고 그 누락이 설령 예우의 뜻이었다 친들, 외로운 위엄 속에 홀로 남겨진다는 것은 변변찮은 위안일 뿐이었다.\n\n웨스턴 부부가 하트필드에 와 있는 동안 바로 그 초대장이 당도한 덕에, 그들의 자리가 그토록 반가웠던 것이다. 초대장을 읽고 처음 한 말은 \"물론 거절해야지요\"였으나, 곧바로 어떻게 하는 게 좋겠느냐고 두 사람에게 물었고, 가는 게 좋겠다는 그들의 조언은 더없이 신속하고 성공적으로 먹혀들었다.\n\n이것저것 다 따져 보니 그 모임에 마음이 아주 없지는 않았다고 그녀는 인정했다. 콜 부부는 더없이 격식 있게 자기들 뜻을 밝혔다 — 그 태도에 진심 어린 정성이 그토록 가득했고, 아버지에 대한 배려가 그토록 깊었다. \"더 일찍 함께해 주십사 청하고 싶었으나, 런던에서 접이식 병풍이 도착하기를 기다리던 참이었다. 그 병풍으로 미스터 우드하우스가 외풍을 쐬지 않도록 막아 드릴 수 있기를 바라며, 그리하면 그분께서 한결 흔쾌히 자리를 빛내 주시리라 여겼다\"는 것이었다. 결국 그녀는 무척 솔깃해졌다. 그리고 아버지의 안락을 소홀히 하지 않으면서 어떻게 해낼 수 있을지 — 베이츠 부인은 몰라도 고더드 부인만큼은 분명 아버지의 말벗이 되어 주리라는 것을 자기들끼리 간단히 정해 둔 다음, 이제 곧 다가올 날 딸이 만찬에 나가 저녁 내내 곁을 비우는 일을 미스터 우드하우스가 받아들이도록 구슬리기로 했다. 아버지가 가는 일이라면, 엠마는 아버지가 그게 될 법한 일이라고 여기는 것조차 바라지 않았다. 시각이 너무 늦을 테고, 모인 사람도 너무 많을 터였다. 그는 이내 꽤나 순순히 단념했다.\n\n\"나는 만찬 나들이를 좋아하지 않는단다.\" 그가 말했다. \"한 번도 좋아한 적이 없어. 엠마도 마찬가지지. 늦은 시각은 우리한테 안 맞아. 콜 부부가 그런 일을 벌인 게 안타깝구나. 차라리 내년 여름 어느 오후에 우리 집에 들러 함께 차나 들면 훨씬 좋으련만 — 오후 산책길에 들르면 될 게야. 우리 집 시각이 그리 이르니 그럴 수 있을 테고, 저녁 습기에 나서기 전에 집에 닿을 수도 있겠지. 여름 저녁의 이슬이란 누구에게도 쐬게 하고 싶지 않은 것이거든. 하지만 그쪽에서 우리 엠마와 함께 만찬을 들기를 그토록 간절히 바라고, 또 두 분이 다 그 자리에 계신 데다 미스터 나이틀리까지 곁에서 이 애를 보살펴 주실 테니, 굳이 막을 마음은 없다 — 날씨만 마땅하다면 말이지. 습하지도, 춥지도, 바람 불지도 않는 날이라면.\" 그러고는 웨스턴 부인을 돌아보며 가벼운 책망의 눈빛으로 말했다. \"아! 미스 테일러, 그대가 결혼만 안 했어도, 나와 함께 집에 있어 주었을 텐데.\"\n\n\"저런, 어르신,\" 미스터 웨스턴이 소리쳤다. \"제가 미스 테일러를 데려갔으니, 할 수만 있다면 그 빈자리를 채워 드리는 게 제 도리지요. 원하신다면 지금 당장 고더드 부인께 다녀오겠습니다.\"\n\n그러나 무엇이든 당장 해치워야 한다는 생각은 미스터 우드하우스의 불안을 가라앉히기는커녕 더욱 키웠다. 그것을 어떻게 다독여야 할지는 부인들이 더 잘 알았다. 미스터 웨스턴은 잠자코 있어야 했고, 모든 일은 차근차근 마련되어야 했다.\n\n이렇게 다루어 주니, 미스터 우드하우스는 이내 여느 때처럼 이야기를 나눌 만큼 마음이 가라앉았다. \"고더드 부인을 뵙게 된다면 기쁘겠구나. 나는 고더드 부인을 무척 마음에 들어 한단다. 엠마가 몇 자 적어 그분을 청하면 되겠지. 제임스가 그 쪽지를 전해 주면 될 게야. 하지만 무엇보다도 먼저, 콜 부인께 답장을 써야 한다.\"\n\n\"내 대신 양해를 구해 주려무나, 얘야 — 되도록 정중하게 말이다. 내가 영락없는 병약한 몸이라 아무 데도 못 가니, 그분들의 고마운 초대를 사양할 수밖에 없다고 전해 주려무나. 물론 안부 인사부터 시작해서 말이지. 너야 모든 걸 빈틈없이 잘할 게다. 어찌해야 하는지 내가 일러 줄 것도 없지. 화요일에 마차가 필요하다고 제임스에게 미리 일러 두는 걸 잊지 말아야 한다. 그 사람이 모는 거라면 너를 두고 아무 걱정 없다. 새 길이 난 뒤로는 그 집에 딱 한 번 가 봤을 뿐이지만, 그래도 제임스가 너를 아주 안전하게 데려다주리라는 건 의심치 않는단다. 그리고 도착하거든, 몇 시에 다시 데리러 오면 좋을지 그 사람한테 일러 둬야 한다. 이른 시각으로 정하는 게 좋을 게야. 늦게까지 있는 건 너도 싫을 테니. 차를 다 들고 나면 무척 피곤해질 거란다.\"\n\n\"하지만 아버지, 제가 피곤해지기도 전에 자리를 뜨기를 바라시는 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"아, 아니다, 얘야. 하지만 너는 곧 피곤해질 게야. 한꺼번에 떠들어 대는 사람이 많을 테니까. 너도 그 소란이 싫을 게다.\"\n\n\"하지만, 어르신,\" 미스터 웨스턴이 소리쳤다. \"엠마가 일찍 자리를 뜨면, 모임이 깨지고 말 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그래서 깨진다 한들 별 탈이야 있겠소.\" 미스터 우드하우스가 말했다. \"어느 모임이든 빨리 파할수록 좋은 법이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 콜 부부 눈에 어떻게 비칠지는 생각지 않으시는군요. 엠마가 차를 들자마자 곧장 일어선다면 언짢게 여길 수도 있습니다. 그들은 마음씨 좋은 사람들이고 제 권리 따위는 별로 따지지 않습니다만, 그래도 누가 서둘러 자리를 뜨는 것이 그리 큰 예우는 아니라고는 느낄 겁니다. 더구나 미스 우드하우스가 그런다면 그 자리의 누구보다도 더 입길에 오르겠지요. 콜 부부를 실망시키고 무안하게 하고 싶진 않으실 겁니다, 어르신, 분명히요. 세상에 둘도 없이 다정하고 좋은 사람들이고, 십 년째 어르신의 이웃이었으니까요.\"\n\n\"아니지요, 그건 결단코 안 될 일이오, 미스터 웨스턴. 일깨워 주어서 정말 고맙소이다. 그들에게 조금이라도 마음을 상하게 한다면 더없이 미안할 게요. 그들이 얼마나 점잖은 사람들인지 나도 안다오. 미스터 페리 말로는 미스터 콜이 맥주는 입에도 안 댄다더군. 겉보기엔 그렇게 안 보여도, 그 사람은 담즙 체질이라 — 미스터 콜은 담즙이 아주 많은 사람이거든. 아니지, 내가 그들에게 마음 아플 일을 끼치는 사람이 되어서야 쓰겠소. 얘야, 이 일은 다시 생각해 봐야겠다. 분명히, 콜 부부를 섭섭하게 할 위험을 무릅쓰느니, 너도 네 바라는 것보다 조금 더 머물러 주겠지. 피곤한 것쯤은 마음에 두지 말려무나. 너도 알다시피, 벗들 틈에 있으니 더없이 안전할 게야.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 아버지. 제 걱정은 조금도 없어요. 그리고 웨스턴 부인만큼 늦게까지 있는 것도 저로서는 조금도 거리낄 게 없어요 — 아버지 일만 아니라면요. 다만 아버지가 저를 기다리며 안 주무실까 봐 그게 걱정이에요. 고더드 부인과 함께 계시면 더없이 편안하게 지내시리라는 건 의심치 않아요. 그분이 피케*를 좋아하시잖아요. 하지만 그분이 댁으로 돌아가신 뒤에는, 평소 주무실 시각에 잠자리에 들지 않고 홀로 앉아 저를 기다리실까 봐 — 그 생각만 하면 제 마음이 도무지 편치 않아요. 기다리며 안 주무시겠다고 꼭 약속해 주셔야 해요.\"\n* 피케(piquet): 두 사람이 하는 프랑스식 카드놀이.\n\n그는 그렇게 하겠다고 했다 — 다만 그녀 쪽에서도 몇 가지 약속을 한다는 조건으로. 이를테면, 집에 돌아왔을 때 몸이 차거든 반드시 충분히 몸을 녹일 것, 시장하거든 무엇이든 먹을 것, 그녀의 몸종이 기다렸다 시중들 것, 그리고 설과 집사가 여느 때처럼 집안에 빈틈없는지 살필 것 — 그런 약속들이었다.\n\n# 제2권 8장\n\n프랭크 처칠이 다시 돌아왔다. 그가 아버지의 만찬을 기다리게 했다 하더라도, 하트필드에서는 그 일이 알려지지 않았다. 웨스턴 부인이 그가 미스터 우드하우스의 마음에 들기를 어찌나 바랐던지, 감출 수 있는 흠이라면 무엇 하나 드러내려 하지 않았던 까닭이다.\n\n그는 머리를 자르고 돌아와, 자기가 한 짓을 두고 더없이 점잖은 태도로 스스로를 비웃었으나, 정작 그 일을 부끄러워하는 기색은 조금도 없었다. 무안한 얼굴빛을 감추려 머리가 더 길었으면 하고 바랄 까닭도 없었고, 풀 죽은 기운을 돋우려 그 돈을 쓰지 않았더라면 하고 아쉬워할 까닭도 없었다. 그는 여전히 거침없고 활기찼다. 그를 보고 나서 엠마는 혼자 이렇게 도덕론을 폈다.\n\n\"꼭 그래야 하는 건지는 모르겠지만, 어리석은 일도 분별 있는 사람이 뻔뻔스럽게 해 버리면 더 이상 어리석지 않게 되는 건 분명해. 사악함은 언제까지나 사악함이지만, 어리석음은 언제나 어리석음은 아니야. 그건 그 일을 다루는 사람의 됨됨이에 달렸으니까. 미스터 나이틀리, 그분은 시시하고 어리석은 젊은이가 결코 아니야. 만약 그런 사람이었다면 이 일을 이렇게는 안 했겠지. 그는 그 일을 자랑스레 떠벌리거나, 아니면 부끄러워했을 거야. 멋쟁이의 허세든가, 제 허영조차 변호하지 못하는 나약한 마음의 발뺌이든가, 둘 중 하나였겠지. 아니야, 나는 그가 시시하지도 어리석지도 않다고 완전히 확신해.\"\n\n화요일이 되면 그를 다시 만날 즐거운 전망이 펼쳐질 터였다 — 그것도 지금까지보다 더 긴 시간 동안. 그의 전반적인 태도를 가늠해 보고, 그로 미루어 자기를 대하는 그의 태도가 무엇을 뜻하는지 헤아려 보며, 자기가 얼마나 빨리 냉랭한 기색을 띠어야 할지 짐작해 보고, 이제 처음으로 두 사람이 함께 있는 모습을 보게 될 사람들이 무어라 평할지 그려 볼 수도 있을 것이었다.\n\n자리가 미스터 콜의 집에 마련되어 있다는 것에도 불구하고 그녀는 무척 즐겁게 보낼 작정이었다. 미스터 엘턴이 한창 그녀의 호감을 사던 시절에도, 그의 결점들 가운데 미스터 콜과 어울려 만찬을 드는 버릇만큼 그녀의 심기를 거스른 것이 없었다는 사실을 잊을 수가 없으면서도 말이다.\n\n아버지의 안락은 넉넉히 보장되어 있었다. 베이츠 부인도 고더드 부인도 와 줄 수 있었던 것이다. 집을 나서기 전 그녀의 마지막 흐뭇한 임무는, 만찬 뒤 함께 앉아 있는 두 부인에게 인사를 드리는 일이었다. 아버지가 딸의 차림새가 곱다며 흐뭇하게 바라보는 동안, 그녀는 식사 중 아버지가 두 분의 건강을 염려한 나머지 마지못해 자제하도록 만들었을 그 일에 대한 보상으로, 두 부인에게 케이크를 큼직하게 잘라 드리고 포도주 잔을 가득 채워 드리며 할 수 있는 한 정성을 다했다. 그녀는 두 분을 위해 푸짐한 만찬을 차려 두었으니, 부디 그분들이 그것을 마음껏 드시도록 허락받았기를 바랄 따름이었다.\n\n그녀는 다른 마차 한 대를 뒤따라 미스터 콜의 집 문 앞에 이르렀고, 그것이 미스터 나이틀리의 마차임을 보고 기뻤다. 미스터 나이틀리는 말을 따로 두지 않았고, 여윳돈은 적은 데 비해 건강과 활력과 자립심은 넘쳐 났던 까닭에, 엠마가 보기에는 너무 자주 닥치는 대로 돌아다니며, 돈웰 애비의 주인다운 만큼 마차를 쓰지 않는 사람이었다. 이제 그녀에게는 마음에서 우러난 칭찬을 따끈따끈할 때 전할 기회가 생겼다. 그가 그녀를 부축해 내리려고 멈춰 섰던 것이다.\n\n\"이렇게 오셔야죠.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"신사답게요. 뵙게 되어 정말 기뻐요.\"\n\n그는 고맙다고 하면서 한마디 보탰다. \"우리가 같은 순간에 도착하다니 이런 행운이! 만약 응접실에서 처음 만났더라면, 자네가 나를 여느 때보다 더 신사답다고 알아봤을지 의심스러우니 말이야. 내가 어떤 모양으로 왔는지를 내 차림이나 태도로 분간하지는 못했을 테지.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 알아봤을 거예요. 분명히 알아봤을걸요. 사람들은 자기 격에 못 미치는 방식으로 들어올 때면 늘 무언가 의식하는 기색이나 부산한 데가 있거든요. 본인은 아주 태연하게 넘긴다고 생각하시겠지만, 그게 당신한테는 일종의 허세, 일부러 꾸민 무심한 태도예요. 그런 처지에 계신 당신을 만날 때마다 저는 늘 그걸 알아챈답니다. 지금은 애써 꾸밀 게 아무것도 없으시죠. 부끄러워하는 줄로 여겨질까 봐 겁낼 일도 없고요. 남보다 더 커 보이려고 안간힘을 쓰지도 않으시고요. 지금이야말로 당신과 한방에 들어가는 게 정말 기쁘겠어요.\"\n\n\"엉뚱한 아가씨로군!\" 그의 대꾸였으나, 결코 노여움이 담긴 말은 아니었다.\n\n엠마는 미스터 나이틀리에게 만족했던 만큼이나 나머지 손님들에게도 만족할 이유가 충분했다. 그녀는 기쁘지 않을 수 없는 따뜻한 존경 속에 맞아들여졌고, 바랄 수 있는 만큼의 대접을 한껏 받았다. 웨스턴 부부가 도착하자, 더없이 다정한 애정의 눈길과 더없이 강한 흠모의 시선이 남편과 아내 양쪽에서 그녀에게 쏟아졌다. 아들은 그녀를 제 각별한 관심사로 점찍은 양 쾌활하게 열을 올리며 다가왔고, 만찬 자리에서는 그가 자기 곁에 앉아 있는 것을 발견했는데 — 그녀가 굳게 믿기로는, 그의 약간의 솜씨가 발휘되지 않은 것도 아니었다.\n\n손님은 꽤 많은 편이었다. 다른 한 집안이 더 끼어 있었던 까닭이다 — 콜 부부가 자기네 지인 가운데 이름을 댈 수 있어 자랑스러워하는, 흠잡을 데 없는 점잖은 시골 집안이었다. 거기에 하이베리의 변호사 미스터 콕스 집안의 남자들도 함께였다. 그만 못한 여자들은 저녁에야 와서 합류할 것이었으니, 미스 베이츠와 미스 페어팩스, 미스 스미스가 그들이었다. 그러나 만찬 자리만으로도 이미 인원이 너무 많아 어떤 화제 하나가 두루 통하기는 어려웠다. 정치 이야기와 미스터 엘턴 이야기가 오가는 동안, 엠마는 옆 사람의 유쾌함에 온전히 마음을 내맡길 수 있었다. 그녀가 귀를 기울이지 않을 수 없게 만든 첫 번째 아득한 소리는 제인 페어팩스라는 이름이었다. 콜 부인이 그녀에 관해 무언가 무척 흥미로울 법한 이야기를 들려주는 모양이었다. 엠마는 귀를 기울였고, 과연 귀 기울일 만한 가치가 있음을 알았다. 엠마의 그 더없이 소중한 부분, 곧 그녀의 공상이 즐거운 양식을 공급받았다. 콜 부인이 들려준 이야기인즉, 그녀가 미스 베이츠를 찾아갔다가 방에 들어서자마자 피아노포르테 한 대를 보고 깜짝 놀랐다는 것이었다 — 무척 우아해 보이는 악기로, 그랜드피아노는 아니지만 큼직한 스퀘어 피아노포르테였다. 그리하여 그 뒤로 이어진 — 콜 부인 쪽의 놀라움과 캐물음과 축하, 그리고 미스 베이츠 쪽의 설명으로 이루어진 — 모든 대화의 알맹이이자 결말은, 이 피아노포르테가 바로 전날 브로드우드 상점에서 도착해 이모와 조카 둘 다 크게 놀랐다는 것 — 전혀 뜻밖이었다는 것이었다. 미스 베이츠의 말로는, 처음에 제인 자신도 어찌할 바를 몰라 대체 누가 이걸 주문했을지 도무지 짐작이 안 가 어리둥절해했으나 — 이제는 둘 다 그것이 단 한 곳에서 온 것일 수밖에 없다고 완전히 마음을 굳혔다는 것이었다. 물론 캠벨 대령에게서 온 것일 수밖에 없다고.\n\n\"달리는 짐작할 수가 없지요.\" 콜 부인이 덧붙였다. \"오히려 그게 한순간이라도 의심거리가 될 수 있었다는 게 놀라웠다니까요. 그런데 제인 말로는, 바로 얼마 전에 그분들한테서 편지를 받았는데 그에 관해선 한마디도 없었다는 거예요. 그분들 방식이야 제인이 제일 잘 알겠지만, 저라면 그 침묵을 선물할 뜻이 없다는 근거로는 안 보겠어요. 짐짓 깜짝 선물을 하려는 거겠죠.\"\n\n콜 부인의 말에 맞장구치는 이가 많았다. 그 화제로 입을 연 사람마다 한결같이 그것이 캠벨 대령에게서 온 것일 수밖에 없다고 확신했고, 그런 선물이 보내졌다는 사실에 한결같이 기뻐했다. 말할 거리를 가진 사람이 넉넉했던 덕에, 엠마는 콜 부인의 말에 계속 귀 기울이면서도 자기 나름대로 생각에 잠길 수 있었다.\n\n\"정말이지, 이보다 더 흐뭇한 이야기를 언제 들어 봤나 싶어요! 제인 페어팩스처럼 그렇게 즐겁게 연주하는 사람한테 악기가 없다는 건 늘 제 마음을 아프게 했거든요. 정말 부끄러운 일이다 싶었죠. 더구나 훌륭한 악기를 그냥 썩히는 집이 얼마나 많은지를 생각하면요. 이건 우리 자신 뺨을 때리는 격이긴 하지만요! 바로 어제 미스터 콜한테 그랬다니까요, 응접실에 놓인 우리 새 그랜드피아노를 보기가 정말 면목 없다고요. 저는 음표 하나 분간 못 하고, 이제 막 배우기 시작한 우리 어린 딸들도 어쩌면 끝내 아무것도 못 칠지 모르는데 말이에요. 그런데 가엾은 제인 페어팩스는 음악의 대가인데도 악기 비슷한 것 하나 없으니, 심심풀이로 칠 세상에서 가장 초라한 낡은 스피넷 한 대조차 없으니 말이에요. 바로 어제 미스터 콜한테 이렇게 말했더니 그이도 제 말에 전적으로 동의하더군요. 다만 그이는 워낙 음악을 끔찍이 좋아하는지라 사고 싶은 마음을 못 참고 사 버린 거예요. 우리 좋은 이웃 가운데 누군가가 이따금 우리보다 더 잘 써 주는 친절을 베풀어 주리라는 바람으로요. 정말이지 그게 이 악기를 산 이유라니까요 — 안 그러면 분명 부끄러워해야 마땅한 일이죠. 우리는 미스 우드하우스가 오늘 저녁 이걸 한번 쳐 주십사 청해 들어주시기를 큰 기대를 품고 있답니다.\"\n\n미스 우드하우스는 알맞게 응낙했고, 콜 부인의 어떤 말에서도 더 이상 끌어낼 것이 없음을 알자 프랭크 처칠 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"왜 웃으세요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그쪽은 왜 웃습니까?\"\n\n\"저요? 캠벨 대령이 그렇게 부유하고 인심이 후한 게 기뻐서 웃나 봐요. 후한 선물이잖아요.\"\n\n\"무척이나요.\"\n\n\"그런데 진작 보내지 않았다는 게 좀 이상하긴 해요.\"\n\n\"미스 페어팩스가 이곳에 이렇게 오래 머문 적이 전엔 없었으니 그런 게 아닐까요.\"\n\n\"아니면 그분들 자기네 악기를 쓰게 해 주지 그러셨을까요. 지금쯤 런던에 처박혀 아무도 손대지 않은 채 있을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그건 그랜드피아노라, 베이츠 부인 댁에는 너무 크다고 여기셨을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"하고 싶으신 말씀이야 뭐든 하셔도 좋아요. 하지만 당신 표정을 보니 이 일에 대한 당신 속생각이 제 생각과 아주 비슷한 게 분명한걸요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요. 제 생각엔 그쪽이 저한테 분에 넘치는 통찰력을 덧씌우시는 것 같은데요. 그쪽이 웃으니 저도 웃는 거고, 그쪽이 의심하는 건 아마 저도 따라 의심하게 되겠죠. 하지만 지금으로선 의심할 게 뭐가 있는지 모르겠어요. 캠벨 대령이 그 사람이 아니라면, 대체 누가 될 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"딕슨 부인은 어때요?\"\n\n\"딕슨 부인! 정말 그렇군요. 딕슨 부인은 생각도 못 했네요. 그분도 자기 아버지 못지않게 악기가 얼마나 반가운 선물일지 잘 알 테고요. 게다가 그 방식이라든가, 그 비밀스러움, 그 깜짝스러움은 나이 지긋한 남자보다는 젊은 여인의 꾀에 더 가깝지요. 딕슨 부인이 분명해요. 그쪽 의심이 제 의심을 이끌 거라고 말씀드렸잖습니까.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 의심을 더 넓혀서 미스터 딕슨까지 거기 넣으셔야죠.\"\n\n\"미스터 딕슨. 아주 좋습니다. 그래요, 이건 미스터 딕슨과 딕슨 부인의 공동 선물일 수밖에 없다는 게 단번에 와닿는군요. 며칠 전에 우리가 이야기했잖습니까, 그가 그녀의 연주를 그토록 뜨겁게 흠모한다고요.\"\n\n\"네, 그리고 그 대목에서 당신이 들려주신 이야기가, 제가 전부터 품어 온 생각 하나를 굳혀 주었어요. 미스터 딕슨이나 미스 페어팩스, 그 누구의 선의를 의심하려는 건 아니지만요, 저는 이렇게 의심하지 않을 수가 없어요 — 그가 그녀의 친구에게 청혼한 뒤 불행히도 그녀 쪽에게 사랑에 빠지고 말았거나, 아니면 그녀 쪽에서 그에게 품은 약간의 연정을 그가 눈치챘거나 둘 중 하나라고요. 정확히 맞히지 못한 채 스무 가지를 짐작할 수야 있겠지만요, 그녀가 캠벨 가족과 아일랜드로 가지 않고 하이베리로 오기로 한 데에는 분명 특별한 까닭이 있을 거예요. 여기서는 결핍과 고행의 삶을 살아야 하는데, 거기서는 온통 즐거움뿐이었을 텐데 말이에요. 고향 공기를 쐬어 보려 한다는 핑계로 말하자면, 저는 그걸 그저 둘러대는 말로밖에 안 봐요. 여름이었다면 통했을지도 모르죠. 하지만 일이월, 삼월에 고향 공기가 누구한테 무슨 도움이 된다는 거예요? 몸이 허약한 경우라면 대개 따뜻한 난롯불과 마차가 훨씬 더 요긴할 텐데, 분명 그녀한테도 그럴 거고요. 그쪽이 제 의심을 다 받아들이시라고는 안 할게요. 그토록 거룩하게 그러겠다고 공언하시긴 했지만요. 그래도 제 의심이 어떤 건지는 솔직히 말씀드린 거예요.\"\n\n\"그리고 정말이지, 그 의심엔 상당히 그럴듯한 데가 있군요. 미스터 딕슨이 친구의 음악보다 그녀의 음악을 더 좋아한다는 것만은 제가 장담할 수 있습니다. 아주 확실해요.\"\n\n\"게다가 그가 그녀의 목숨을 구했잖아요. 그 이야긴 들으셨어요? 뱃놀이였는데, 어쩌다 그녀가 배 밖으로 떨어지려던 걸 그가 붙잡았다죠.\"\n\n\"그랬지요. 저도 그 자리에 있었습니다 — 일행 중 하나였거든요.\"\n\n\"정말로요? 어머! 그런데 물론 아무것도 못 알아채셨겠죠, 당신한테는 이게 새로운 생각인 것 같으니까요. 제가 그 자리에 있었더라면, 무언가 알아냈을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그쪽이라면 그랬겠지요. 하지만 저는, 단순한 저는, 그저 사실밖에 못 봤어요. 미스 페어팩스가 배에서 거의 떨어질 뻔했고 미스터 딕슨이 그녀를 붙잡았다는 사실 말입니다. 순식간의 일이었어요. 그리고 그 뒤에 따른 충격과 놀람이 무척 컸고 훨씬 더 오래갔지만 — 정말이지 우리 가운데 누구라도 다시 마음을 가라앉히기까지 반 시간은 걸린 것 같은데 — 그건 너무 보편적인 동요라서 특별한 마음의 동요 같은 건 알아챌 수가 없었지요. 하지만 그쪽이라면 무언가 알아냈을지도 모른다는 걸 부정하려는 건 아닙니다.\"\n\n여기서 대화가 끊겼다. 두 사람은 다음 요리가 나오기까지의 꽤 긴 어색한 틈에 함께 끼어 다른 이들처럼 격식 차리고 점잖게 굴어야 했다. 그러나 식탁이 다시 무사히 채워지고, 구석마다 접시가 제자리에 정확히 놓이고, 분주함과 편안함이 두루 되살아나자 엠마가 말했다.\n\n\"이 피아노포르테가 도착했다는 게 제겐 결정적이에요. 조금 더 알고 싶었는데, 이걸로 충분히 알겠어요. 두고 보세요, 머잖아 이게 미스터 딕슨과 딕슨 부인이 보낸 선물이라는 말을 듣게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"그리고 딕슨 부부가 그 일을 전혀 모른다고 딱 잡아뗀다면, 우리는 그게 캠벨 가족에게서 온 거라고 결론지을 수밖에 없겠지요.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 그건 캠벨 가족에게서 온 게 아니라고 저는 확신해요. 미스 페어팩스도 그게 캠벨 가족에게서 온 게 아닌 줄 알고 있어요. 안 그랬다면 처음부터 그분들을 떠올렸을 테니까요. 감히 그분들로 단정할 수 있었다면 어리둥절해하지도 않았을 거예요. 제가 당신을 설득하진 못했을지 몰라도, 저 자신은 미스터 딕슨이 이 일의 장본인이라고 완전히 확신하고 있어요.\"\n\n\"제가 설득되지 않았다고 여기신다면, 정말이지 저를 섭섭하게 하시는 겁니다. 그쪽의 추론이 제 판단을 통째로 끌고 가는걸요. 처음에 그쪽이 캠벨 대령이 준 사람이라고 마음을 굳힌 줄로만 알았을 때는, 저도 그저 아버지의 자상함으로만 보았고, 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 일이라 여겼지요. 하지만 그쪽이 딕슨 부인을 들먹이자, 그게 따뜻한 여자들 사이 우정의 선물일 가능성이 훨씬 더 크겠구나 싶었습니다. 이제 제 눈에는 그게 사랑의 헌물 말고는 달리 보이질 않는군요.\"\n\n이 문제를 더 밀어붙일 까닭은 없었다. 그 확신은 진심인 듯했고, 그도 정말 그렇게 느끼는 얼굴이었다. 그녀는 더 말하지 않았고, 다른 화제들이 차례로 등장했다. 만찬의 나머지도 그렇게 흘러갔다. 후식이 뒤따랐고, 아이들이 들어와 여느 때 같은 대화의 흐름 속에서 이야깃거리가 되고 칭찬을 받았다. 영리한 말 몇 마디, 어처구니없이 어리석은 말 몇 마디가 오갔으나, 훨씬 더 많은 부분은 이쪽도 저쪽도 아니었으니 — 일상적인 이야기, 따분한 되풀이, 케케묵은 소식, 시시한 농담을 넘어서는 것은 하나도 없었다.\n\n부인들이 응접실에 들어선 지 얼마 안 되어, 다른 부인들이 저마다 떨어진 무리를 이루어 도착했다. 엠마는 자기의 그 각별한 어린 벗이 들어서는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그 위엄과 우아함을 두고 우쭐댈 수는 없을지언정, 그 꽃처럼 환한 사랑스러움과 꾸밈없는 태도를 사랑할 수 있었을 뿐 아니라, 어그러진 연정의 아픔 한가운데서도 그 많은 즐거움으로 마음을 달랠 수 있게 해 주는 그 가볍고 쾌활하고 감상에 젖지 않는 천성을 진심으로 기뻐할 수 있었다. 저기 그녀가 앉아 있었다 — 요 근래 얼마나 많은 눈물을 흘렸는지를 누가 짐작이나 했으랴? 모임에 끼어, 자기도 곱게 차려입고 남들이 곱게 차려입은 것을 바라보며, 앉아서 미소 짓고 예쁘게 보이며, 아무 말도 안 하는 것 — 그것이면 지금 이 시간의 행복으로 충분했다. 제인 페어팩스가 보기에도 몸가짐에서도 더 빼어나 보이는 것은 사실이었다. 그러나 엠마는 짐작했다, 제인이라면 차라리 해리엇과 마음을 맞바꾸고 싶었을지 모른다고. 사랑했다는, 그래, 미스터 엘턴조차 헛되이 사랑했다는 그 굴욕을 사들이는 대가로, 친구의 남편에게 사랑받고 있음을 아는 그 위험한 즐거움을 죄다 내어놓는 편을 기꺼이 택했을지 모른다고.\n\n이렇게 큰 모임에서는 엠마가 굳이 그녀에게 다가갈 필요가 없었다. 그녀는 피아노포르테 이야기를 꺼내고 싶지 않았다. 자기야말로 너무 깊이 그 비밀을 알고 있는 터라, 호기심이나 관심을 내비치는 것이 떳떳하지 못하다고 여겨, 일부러 거리를 두었다. 그러나 다른 이들이 거의 곧바로 그 화제를 꺼냈고, 그녀는 축하 인사를 받으며 떠오르는 의식의 홍조를, \"저의 더없이 훌륭하신 벗 캠벨 대령\"이라는 이름과 함께 묻어나는 죄책의 홍조를 보았다.\n\n마음씨 곱고 음악을 좋아하는 웨스턴 부인은 이 일에 특히 흥미를 느꼈다. 엠마는 그녀가 그 화제에 끈질기게 매달리는 모습에 웃음을 참을 수가 없었다. 음색이며 터치며 페달에 관해 그토록 묻고 할 말이 많으면서도, 그 일에 관해서는 되도록 말을 아끼고 싶다는 바람 — 엠마가 그 아리따운 여주인공의 얼굴에서 또렷이 읽어 낸 바람 — 을 전혀 눈치채지 못했던 것이다.\n\n이윽고 신사들 가운데 몇이 합류했고, 그 일찍 온 무리의 맨 앞은 다름 아닌 프랭크 처칠이었다. 그는 맨 먼저, 가장 잘생긴 모습으로 걸어 들어왔다. 지나가는 길에 미스 베이츠와 그 조카에게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 곧장 둥근 자리 맞은편으로 길을 내어 미스 우드하우스가 앉은 곳으로 향했고, 그녀 곁에 자리를 찾을 때까지 도무지 앉으려 들지 않았다. 엠마는 그 자리에 있는 사람마다 무슨 생각을 하고 있을지 알아챘다. 자기가 그의 목표였고, 누구나 그것을 알아볼 게 분명했다. 그녀는 그를 자기 벗 미스 스미스에게 소개했고, 그 뒤 적당한 틈틈이 두 사람이 서로를 어떻게 생각하는지 들었다. \"그는 그토록 사랑스러운 얼굴은 처음 보았고, 그녀의 천진함에 매료되었다.\" 그리고 그녀는, \"물론 그분께 너무 큰 칭찬이긴 하지만, 어딘가 미스터 엘턴을 좀 닮은 데가 있는 것 같았어요.\" 엠마는 분을 억누르고, 말없이 그녀에게서 고개를 돌리는 것으로 그쳤다.\n\n미스 페어팩스 쪽을 처음 흘끗 보았을 때 그녀와 그 신사 사이에 알아챔의 미소가 오갔다. 그러나 말을 삼가는 것이 가장 신중한 처사였다. 그는 그녀에게 말했다, 식당을 떠나고 싶어 안달이 났었노라고 — 오래 앉아 있는 걸 질색한다고 — 일어설 수 있으면 늘 제일 먼저 일어선다고 — 자기 아버지와 미스터 나이틀리, 미스터 콕스, 미스터 콜은 교구 일로 한창 바쁘게 남아 있다고. 그래도 자기가 남아 있는 동안은 꽤 즐거웠노라고, 대체로 신사답고 분별 있는 사람들이더라고. 그러고는 하이베리 전체를 어찌나 곱게 말하던지 — 유쾌한 집안이 그토록 넘친다고 여기던지 — 엠마는 자기가 이 고장을 좀 지나치게 얕보아 온 게 아닌가 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 그녀는 요크셔의 사교계에 관해 — 엔스컴 근방 이웃의 범위며 그 성격에 관해 그에게 물었다. 그의 대답에서 헤아릴 수 있기로는, 엔스컴에 관한 한 이렇다 할 일은 거의 없으며, 그 집안의 왕래는 대단한 집안들 사이에서만 이루어지되 아주 가까운 데는 한 집도 없고, 심지어 날을 잡고 초대를 수락한 뒤에도 처칠 부인이 외출할 만큼 몸과 기운이 받쳐 주지 않는 경우가 절반은 된다는 것이었다. 그 집안은 새로운 사람은 일절 방문하지 않는 것을 원칙으로 삼는다는 것, 그리고 그에게는 따로 약속이 있긴 해도 빠져나오거나 지인 하나를 하룻밤 데려오는 일조차 더러는 상당한 수완을 부리지 않고는 어렵다는 것이었다.\n\n엠마는 엔스컴이 만족스러울 리 없다는 것, 그리고 하이베리가 좋게 보아 준다면, 집에서 분에 넘치는 칩거를 견디는 젊은이의 마음에 마땅히 들 만하다는 것을 알았다. 엔스컴에서의 그의 비중은 아주 또렷했다. 그는 자랑하지 않았으나, 외삼촌이 어쩌지 못하는 일에서도 자기는 외숙모를 설득해 냈노라는 사실이 저절로 드러났다. 그녀가 웃으며 그 점을 짚자, 그는 (한두 가지를 빼면) 시간만 들이면 무슨 일이든 외숙모를 설득할 수 있으리라 믿는다고 털어놓았다. 자기 영향력이 통하지 않는 그 한두 가지 중 하나를 그가 곧 입에 올렸다. 그는 무척이나 외국에 나가고 싶어 했고 — 정말이지 여행을 허락받기를 간절히 바랐으나 — 외숙모가 들으려 하지 않더라는 것이었다. 그건 지난해의 일이었다. 그런데 이제는 그 소망이 예전 같지 않기 시작했노라고 그가 말했다.\n\n그가 입에 올리지 않은 그 설득되지 않는 점이 무엇인지, 엠마는 아버지에 대한 도리를 지키는 일이리라 짐작했다.\n\n\"제가 아주 비참한 사실을 하나 깨달았어요.\" 그가 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 말했다. \"내일이면 여기 온 지 일주일이에요 — 제 시간의 절반이죠. 날이 이렇게 빨리 가는 줄은 몰랐어요. 내일이면 일주일이라니! 그런데 이제 겨우 즐기기 시작했을 뿐이에요. 이제 막 웨스턴 부인이며 여러분과 사귀게 됐는데요! 떠올리기도 싫네요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 며칠 안 되는 시간 가운데 꼬박 하루를 머리 자르는 데 써 버린 걸 이제 후회하기 시작하실지도 모르겠네요.\"\n\n\"아뇨.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그건 전혀 후회할 일이 아니에요. 남에게 보일 만하다는 자신이 없으면 벗을 만나도 즐겁지가 않거든요.\"\n\n나머지 신사들이 이제 방에 들어선 터라, 엠마는 잠시 그에게서 몸을 돌려 미스터 콜의 이야기를 들어야 했다. 미스터 콜이 자리를 옮기고 다시 아까처럼 마음을 돌릴 수 있게 되었을 때, 그녀는 프랭크 처칠이 방 건너편의 미스 페어팩스를 — 마침 그녀가 바로 맞은편에 앉아 있었다 — 뚫어지게 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"무슨 일이세요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그가 흠칫했다. \"정신 차리게 해 줘서 고맙습니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"제가 무척 무례하게 굴었나 봐요. 그런데 정말 미스 페어팩스가 머리를 어찌나 이상하게 했는지 — 어찌나 별나게 했는지 — 도무지 눈을 뗄 수가 없네요. 저렇게 유별난 건 처음 봐요! 저 곱슬머리라니! 저건 분명 그녀만의 취향일 거예요. 저렇게 한 사람은 아무도 없거든요! 가서 저게 아일랜드 유행인지 물어봐야겠어요. 그럴까요? 그래요, 그래야겠어요 — 정말이지 그러겠어요 — 그녀가 어떻게 받아들이는지 두고 보세요. 얼굴을 붉히는지요.\"\n\n그는 곧장 가 버렸다. 이내 엠마는 그가 미스 페어팩스 앞에 서서 그녀에게 말을 거는 모습을 보았다. 그러나 그 말이 그 아가씨에게 어떤 영향을 미치는지로 말하자면, 그가 경솔하게도 두 사람 사이 정확히 한가운데, 미스 페어팩스 바로 정면에 자리를 잡은 탓에, 그녀는 아무것도 분간할 수가 없었다.\n\n그가 제 자리로 돌아오기도 전에, 그 자리는 웨스턴 부인이 차지했다.\n\n\"이게 큰 모임의 호사로움이죠.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"누구한테든 다가갈 수 있고, 무슨 말이든 할 수 있으니까요. 엠마, 너랑 이야기가 너무 하고 싶었단다. 나도 너처럼 무언가를 알아내고 계획을 세웠거든. 생각이 따끈따끈할 때 말해 줘야겠어. 미스 베이츠랑 그 조카가 어떻게 여기 왔는지 아니?\"\n\n\"어떻게요? 초대받은 거 아니에요?\"\n\n\"아, 그래 — 그런데 어떻게 여기까지 실려 왔느냐 말이야. 그 오는 방식 말이다.\"\n\n\"걸어왔겠죠. 달리 어떻게 오겠어요?\"\n\n\"맞아. 그런데 좀 전에 문득 이런 생각이 들더구나. 제인 페어팩스가 이렇게 밤늦게, 요즘처럼 추운 밤에 또 걸어서 집에 돌아간다면 얼마나 딱한 일일까 하고. 그 애를 바라보자니, 그렇게 돋보이는 모습은 처음 봤지만, 몸에 열이 올라 있어서 특히 감기 들기 쉽겠다는 생각이 퍼뜩 들었어. 가엾은 것! 그 생각을 도저히 견딜 수가 없더구나. 그래서 미스터 웨스턴이 방에 들어오자마자 붙들고 마차 이야기를 했지. 그이가 내 뜻을 얼마나 선뜻 받아들였는지는 짐작이 가겠지. 그이의 허락을 받고 나서, 곧장 미스 베이츠한테 가서 마차가 우리를 집에 데려다주기 전에 먼저 두 분이 쓰실 수 있게 대기할 거라고 일러 줬단다. 그러면 단번에 마음이 놓이실 것 같아서 말이야. 마음씨 고운 분! 더할 수 없이 고마워하셨지, 그야 물론이고. '세상에 자기만큼 운 좋은 사람이 또 없다'면서 — 그런데 거듭거듭 고맙다고 하시면서도 — '굳이 우리를 번거롭게 할 것 없다고, 미스터 나이틀리의 마차가 모셔 왔고 또 모셔다드리기로 했다'는 거야. 나는 정말 깜짝 놀랐어. 그야 무척 기뻤지. 하지만 정말 놀랐단다. 그렇게 더없이 친절한 배려라니 — 그토록 사려 깊은 배려라니! 남자 중에 그런 걸 생각해 낼 사람이 그리 많지 않거든. 한마디로, 그분의 평소 방식을 아는지라, 나는 애초에 마차를 쓴 게 두 분을 위해서가 아니었나 하는 생각이 자꾸 드는구나. 아무래도 자기 혼자라면 말 두 필을 매지 않았을 텐데, 그저 두 분을 도울 핑계로 그런 게 아닐까 싶어.\"\n\n\"충분히 그럴 만해요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"그보다 더 그럴듯한 일도 없죠. 미스터 나이틀리만큼 그런 일을 — 정말로 인정 많고 쓸모 있고 사려 깊고 너그러운 일이라면 무엇이든 할 만한 사람을 저는 알지 못해요. 그분은 멋부리는 분은 아니지만, 인정이 무척 많은 분이거든요. 그러니 제인 페어팩스의 병약함을 생각하면, 그분께는 이게 인정을 베풀 일로 보였을 거예요. 티 안 내는 친절을 행할 사람으로 미스터 나이틀리만 한 분을 저는 또 꼽을 수가 없네요. 오늘 그분께 말이 있었다는 건 제가 알아요 — 우리가 같이 도착했으니까요. 제가 그걸 두고 놀렸는데, 속내를 드러낼 말은 한마디도 안 하시더라고요.\"\n\n\"글쎄,\" 웨스턴 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"넌 이번 일에서 그분께 나보다 더 순박하고 사심 없는 자비심을 인정해 주는구나. 미스 베이츠가 말하는 동안, 내 머릿속에 의심 하나가 번뜩 떠올라 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었거든. 생각하면 할수록 더 그럴듯해 보여. 한마디로, 나는 미스터 나이틀리와 제인 페어팩스를 짝지었단다. 너랑 어울린 결과가 이거란다! 어떻게 생각하니?\"\n\n\"미스터 나이틀리랑 제인 페어팩스라고요!\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"어머나, 웨스턴 부인, 어떻게 그런 생각을 다 하셨어요? 미스터 나이틀리가! 미스터 나이틀리는 결혼하면 안 돼요! 어린 헨리를 돈웰에서 밀려나게 하실 생각은 아니죠? 아, 안 돼요, 안 돼. 돈웰은 헨리 거예요. 미스터 나이틀리가 결혼한다는 건 저는 도무지 찬성할 수가 없어요. 그리고 그건 전혀 있을 법한 일도 아니고요. 그런 생각을 하시다니 놀라워요.\"\n\n\"엠마, 무엇 때문에 그런 생각을 하게 됐는지 다 말했잖니. 나도 그 혼사를 바라는 건 아니야 — 가엾은 어린 헨리를 다치게 하고 싶지도 않고 — 하지만 정황이 그 생각을 떠올리게 해 준걸. 그리고 미스터 나이틀리가 정말로 결혼하고 싶어 한다면야, 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는 여섯 살배기 헨리를 위해서 그분더러 단념하라고 할 수야 없지 않겠니?\"\n\n\"네, 저는 그러겠어요. 헨리가 밀려나는 건 도저히 못 견뎌요. 미스터 나이틀리가 결혼이라니! 아뇨, 그런 생각은 한 번도 해 본 적이 없고, 이제 와서 받아들일 수도 없어요. 게다가 하필 제인 페어팩스라니, 그 많은 여자 중에서!\"\n\n\"아니, 그 애는 늘 그분이 가장 아끼는 사람이었잖니, 너도 잘 알면서.\"\n\n\"하지만 그런 혼사의 경솔함이라니요!\"\n\n\"나는 그 분별을 말하는 게 아니야. 그저 그 개연성을 말하는 거지.\"\n\n\"저는 거기서 아무 개연성도 못 보겠어요. 부인이 말씀하신 것보다 더 나은 근거가 없다면요. 그분의 인정 많음, 그 자비심으로 말하자면, 말씀드렸듯이 마차 일을 설명하기엔 그것만으로 충분하고도 남아요. 그분은 제인 페어팩스와는 별개로 베이츠 댁을 무척 아끼시잖아요. 늘 그 댁에 관심을 보여 드리는 걸 기뻐하시고요. 웨스턴 부인, 중매에는 손대지 마세요. 너무 서투르시거든요. 제인 페어팩스가 애비의 안주인이라뇨! 아, 안 돼요, 안 돼. 온몸의 감각이 다 거부하는걸요. 그분을 위해서라도, 그런 미친 짓은 하지 않으시면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"경솔하다고는 해도 좋아 — 하지만 미친 짓은 아니지. 재산의 불균형, 그리고 어쩌면 약간의 나이 차이를 빼면, 나는 어울리지 않는 데를 하나도 못 찾겠는걸.\"\n\n\"하지만 미스터 나이틀리는 결혼하고 싶어 하지 않아요. 그분은 그런 생각이 손톱만큼도 없는 게 분명해요. 그분 머릿속에 그런 걸 집어넣지 마세요. 그분이 왜 결혼을 하겠어요? 혼자서도 더없이 행복하신데요. 농장이며 양 떼며 서재며, 게다가 교구 전체를 건사하는 일까지 있고요. 그리고 동생 아이들을 끔찍이 아끼시잖아요. 시간을 채우려고든 마음을 채우려고든, 그분은 결혼할 필요가 없어요.\"\n\n\"엠마, 그분이 그렇게 여기는 한이야 그런 거지. 하지만 그분이 정말로 제인 페어팩스를 사랑한다면—\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼요! 그분은 제인 페어팩스한테 마음이 없어요. 사랑이라는 뜻으로는, 정말 없는 게 분명해요. 그녀나 그녀 가족한테 좋은 일이라면 무엇이든 해 주시겠지만, 그렇지만—\"\n\n\"글쎄,\" 웨스턴 부인이 웃으며 말했다. \"어쩌면 그분이 그들에게 해 줄 수 있는 가장 큰 선행이, 제인한테 그렇게 떳떳한 가정을 마련해 주는 일일지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"그게 그녀에게 좋은 일이라면, 그분 자신에게는 분명 나쁜 일일 거예요. 더없이 수치스럽고 격을 떨어뜨리는 인연이죠. 미스 베이츠가 자기 식구가 되는 걸 그분이 어떻게 견디겠어요? 그분이 제인과 결혼해 준 그 크나큰 친절을 두고 하루 종일 고맙다고 하며 애비를 들락거리는 걸요? '어쩌면 그렇게 친절하고 자상하신지! 하기야 늘 그렇게 친절한 이웃이셨지!' 그러다가 문장 절반쯤에서 휙 자기 어머니의 낡은 속치마 이야기로 새어 나가겠죠. '그게 그렇게 아주 낡은 속치마는 또 아니라고 — 아직도 한참 더 입을 수 있다고 — 정말이지, 우리 집 속치마들은 죄다 어찌나 튼튼한지 고마운 마음뿐이라고요.'\"\n\n\"창피한 줄 아세요, 엠마! 그분 흉내는 그만둬요. 양심에 거슬리는데도 자꾸 웃기잖니. 그리고 정말이지, 나는 미스터 나이틀리가 미스 베이츠 때문에 그리 시달릴 것 같지는 않은걸. 사소한 일에 그분은 짜증 내지 않거든. 그분이 떠들든 말든 내버려 두다가, 자기가 무슨 말이 하고 싶으면 그저 더 큰 소리로 말해서 그분 목소리를 덮어 버리면 그만이지. 하지만 문제는 그게 그분께 나쁜 인연이냐 아니냐가 아니라, 그분이 그걸 바라느냐 아니냐란다. 나는 그분이 바란다고 생각해. 그분이 제인 페어팩스를 두고 그렇게 높이 말하는 걸 들은 적이 있거든, 너도 분명 들었을 테고! 그분이 그 애한테 두는 관심이며 — 그 애 건강을 염려하는 마음이며 — 그 애한테 더 행복한 앞날이 없는 걸 안타까워하는 마음이며! 그 점들에 관해 그분이 그토록 뜨겁게 말하는 걸 내가 들었다니까! 그 애의 피아노포르테 연주며 목소리를 어찌나 흠모하는지! 그 애 노래라면 영영 들을 수 있겠다고 하는 것도 들은 적 있어. 아! 그리고 떠올랐던 생각 하나를 깜빡 잊을 뻔했네 — 누군가가 여기로 보낸 이 피아노포르테 말이다. 우리 모두 캠벨 가족의 선물이라고 여기며 그렇게 흡족해했지만, 혹시 미스터 나이틀리한테서 온 건 아닐까? 자꾸 그분이 의심스럽단다. 사랑에 빠지지 않았더라도, 그분이라면 딱 그런 일을 할 사람이거든.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그게 그분이 사랑에 빠졌다는 증거는 못 되겠네요. 하지만 저는 그게 그분이 할 법한 일이라고는 전혀 생각하지 않아요. 미스터 나이틀리는 무슨 일이든 은밀하게 하는 분이 아니거든요.\"\n\n\"그 애한테 악기가 없다며 그분이 거듭 한탄하는 걸 내가 들었어. 보통 같으면 그런 일이 그분 머리에 떠오를 법한 것보다 더 자주 말이야.\"\n\n\"좋아요. 그리고 그분이 정말 그녀에게 악기를 줄 작정이었다면, 그렇게 말씀하셨을 거예요.\"\n\n\"섬세한 마음에서 우러난 망설임이 있었을지도 모르지, 엠마. 나는 이게 그분한테서 온 거라는 느낌이 아주 강한걸. 만찬 때 콜 부인이 그 이야기를 했을 때 그분이 유난히 말이 없었던 게 분명하거든.\"\n\n\"부인은 어떤 생각을 붙잡으면 그걸 들고 냅다 달려가시는군요. 저더러 그런다고 여러 번 나무라셨으면서요. 저는 연정의 기미라곤 못 보겠어요. 피아노포르테에 관해서도 아무것도 안 믿고요. 미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스와 결혼할 생각을 조금이라도 품고 있다는 건, 증거가 나와야만 저를 설득할 수 있을 거예요.\"\n\n두 사람은 한동안 더 같은 식으로 이 문제를 두고 다투었고, 엠마가 벗의 마음을 차츰 누르며 우세를 점해 갔다. 둘 중에서는 웨스턴 부인이 양보하는 데 더 익숙했던 것이다. 그러다 방 안의 작은 부산함이 차가 끝나고 악기가 채비를 갖추는 중임을 알렸다. 그와 동시에 미스터 콜이 다가와 미스 우드하우스가 한번 쳐 주시는 영광을 베풀어 달라고 간청했다. 웨스턴 부인과의 대화에 열중하느라 미스 페어팩스 곁에 자리를 잡았다는 것 말고는 거의 보지 못했던 프랭크 처칠이 미스터 콜을 뒤따라와 더없이 간곡한 청을 보탰다. 모든 면에서 엠마가 먼저 나서는 편이 가장 알맞았던 터라, 그녀는 아주 적절하게 응했다.\n\n그녀는 자기 솜씨의 한계를 너무도 잘 알기에, 떳떳하게 해낼 수 있는 것 이상은 시도하지 않았다. 대체로 사람들이 좋아하는 자잘한 곡들에서는 취향도 활기도 모자라지 않았고, 자기 목소리에 반주를 잘 맞출 줄도 알았다. 노래에 곁들여진 한 반주가 그녀를 기분 좋게 놀라게 했으니 — 프랭크 처칠이 살짝, 그러나 정확하게 받아 넣은 두 번째 성부였다. 노래가 끝나자 그는 마땅히 그녀에게 용서를 구했고, 여느 때와 같은 일들이 뒤따랐다. 그는 듣기 좋은 목소리에 음악을 완벽히 안다는 핀잔을 들었고, 그것을 마땅히 부인했으며, 자기는 그 방면에 아무것도 모르고 목소리라곤 전혀 없다고 딱 잘라 우겼다. 두 사람은 다시 한 번 함께 노래했다. 그러고 나서 엠마는 자리를 미스 페어팩스에게 넘겼는데, 그녀의 연주는 노래든 악기든 자기 것보다 한없이 빼어나다는 것을 엠마는 스스로에게도 도무지 감출 수가 없었다.\n\n복잡한 심정으로 그녀는 악기를 둘러싼 무리에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 앉아 귀를 기울였다. 프랭크 처칠이 다시 노래했다. 두 사람은 웨이머스에서도 한두 번 함께 노래한 적이 있는 모양이었다. 그러나 미스터 나이틀리가 가장 귀 기울이는 이들 가운데 끼어 있는 광경이 곧 엠마의 마음 절반을 끌어가 버렸다. 그녀는 웨스턴 부인의 의심을 두고 생각의 갈래에 빠져들었고, 어우러진 목소리의 감미로운 가락은 그저 잠깐잠깐 그 흐름을 끊을 뿐이었다. 미스터 나이틀리가 결혼한다는 데 대한 그녀의 반감은 조금도 가라앉지 않았다. 거기서는 해악밖에 보이지 않았다. 그것은 미스터 존 나이틀리에게 큰 실망일 터였고, 따라서 이저벨라에게도 그러할 터였다. 아이들에게는 실질적인 손해 — 더없이 굴욕적인 변화이자 모두에게 막대한 손실 — 아버지의 일상의 안락에서도 큰 부분이 깎여 나갈 일이었다. 그리고 그녀 자신으로 말하자면, 제인 페어팩스가 돈웰 애비에 있다는 생각을 도무지 견딜 수가 없었다. 자기들 모두가 자리를 비켜 줘야 할 나이틀리 부인이라니! 아니야 — 미스터 나이틀리는 결코 결혼해서는 안 된다. 어린 헨리가 돈웰의 상속자로 남아 있어야 한다.\n\n이윽고 미스터 나이틀리가 뒤를 돌아보더니, 다가와 그녀 곁에 앉았다. 두 사람은 처음에는 그저 연주 이야기만 했다. 그의 감탄은 분명 무척 따뜻했다. 그러나 웨스턴 부인이 아니었다면 그게 마음에 걸리지도 않았으리라고 그녀는 생각했다. 그래도 일종의 시금석 삼아, 그녀는 이모와 조카를 데려다준 그의 친절을 입에 올리기 시작했다. 그의 대답이 그 일을 짤막하게 끊으려는 투이긴 했으나, 그녀는 그것이 단지 자기 자신의 어떤 친절에 대해 길게 늘어놓기 싫어하는 마음을 나타낼 뿐이라고 믿었다.\n\n\"저는 가끔 마음이 쓰여요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이런 자리에 우리 마차를 더 요긴하게 쓰지 못하는 게요. 그러고 싶은 마음이 없어서가 아니에요. 하지만 그런 일로 제임스더러 말을 매라고 하는 걸 아버지가 얼마나 말도 안 되는 일로 여기시는지 아시잖아요.\"\n\n\"그야 어림없는 일이지, 어림없고말고.\" 그가 대답했다. \"하지만 자네는 분명 그러고 싶을 때가 많을 거야.\" 그러고는 그 확신이 어찌나 기쁜 듯 미소 짓던지, 그녀는 한 걸음 더 나아가야겠다 싶었다.\n\n\"캠벨 가족이 보낸 이 선물 말인데요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 피아노포르테는 참 친절한 선물이에요.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 대답했는데, 눈곱만큼의 당황한 기색도 없었다. \"하지만 미리 언질을 주었더라면 더 좋았을 거야. 깜짝 선물이란 어리석은 짓이지. 기쁨이 더 커지는 것도 아니고, 불편은 외려 만만찮을 때가 많거든. 캠벨 대령쯤 되면 더 나은 판단을 했을 줄 알았는데.\"\n\n그 순간부터 엠마는 미스터 나이틀리가 그 악기를 주는 데 아무 관여도 하지 않았노라고 맹세라도 할 수 있었다. 그러나 그가 어떤 각별한 연정에서 온전히 자유로운지 — 실제로 마음에 두는 데가 없는지 — 는 조금 더 의심스러운 채로 남았다. 제인의 두 번째 노래가 끝나 갈 무렵, 그녀의 목소리가 탁해졌다.\n\n\"이만하면 됐어.\" 노래가 끝나자 그가 혼잣말처럼 말했다. \"하룻저녁에 노래는 충분히 했으니 — 이제 좀 쉬게.\"\n\n그러나 곧 또 한 곡이 청해졌다. \"한 곡만 더요. 결코 미스 페어팩스를 지치게 할 생각은 없지만, 딱 한 곡만 더 청하겠다는 거였다.\" 그리고 프랭크 처칠이 이렇게 말하는 게 들렸다. \"이건 힘들이지 않고 하실 수 있을 거예요. 앞부분은 아주 별것 아니거든요. 노래의 힘은 뒷부분에 실려 있고요.\"\n\n미스터 나이틀리가 노여워졌다.\n\n\"저 친구는,\" 그가 분개하며 말했다. \"제 목소리 자랑할 생각밖에 없군. 이래선 안 되지.\" 그러고는 마침 그 순간 가까이 지나가던 미스 베이츠를 건드리며 — \"미스 베이츠, 정신이 있으십니까, 조카가 저렇게 목이 쉬도록 노래하게 두시다니요? 가서 좀 말리세요. 다들 저 애한테 인정사정이 없군요.\"\n\n미스 베이츠는 제인을 향한 진정한 염려에 사로잡혀, 고맙다는 인사조차 미처 다 차리지 못한 채 앞으로 나서서 더 이상의 노래에 종지부를 찍었다. 여기서 그날 저녁의 음악회 순서는 끝이 났으니, 미스 우드하우스와 미스 페어팩스가 유일한 젊은 여성 연주자였던 까닭이다. 그러나 곧 (오 분도 안 되어) 정확히 어디서 비롯됐는지 아무도 모를 춤 제안이 콜 부부의 손에 어찌나 효과적으로 추진되었던지, 마땅한 자리를 내려고 모든 것이 빠르게 치워졌다. 컨트리 댄스에 일가견이 있는 웨스턴 부인이 피아노 앞에 자리를 잡고 거역할 수 없는 왈츠를 연주하기 시작했다. 더없이 어울리는 정중함으로 엠마에게 다가온 프랭크 처칠이 그녀의 손을 잡아 맨 앞으로 이끌었다.\n\n다른 젊은이들이 서로 짝을 짓기를 기다리는 동안, 엠마는 자기 목소리와 취향을 두고 쏟아지는 칭찬에도 불구하고 잠깐 둘러보아 미스터 나이틀리가 어찌하는지 살필 짬을 냈다. 이것이 시험이 될 터였다. 그는 본디 춤을 추는 사람이 아니었다. 만약 지금 그가 부리나케 제인 페어팩스에게 춤을 청한다면, 무언가를 점칠 수 있을 것이었다. 당장은 그런 기미가 없었다. 아니, 그는 콜 부인과 이야기를 나누고 있었다 — 무심하게 구경하고 있었다. 제인은 다른 누군가의 청을 받았고, 그는 여전히 콜 부인과 이야기 중이었다.\n\n엠마는 더 이상 헨리를 걱정하지 않았다. 헨리의 몫은 아직 안전했다. 그녀는 진정한 활기와 즐거움 속에 춤의 첫머리를 이끌었다. 다섯 쌍을 넘기지 못할 만큼이었으나, 그 드묾과 갑작스러움이 춤을 무척 즐겁게 만들었고, 자기에게 더없이 잘 맞는 짝이 있음을 그녀는 알았다. 두 사람은 눈여겨볼 만한 한 쌍이었다.\n\n안타깝게도 허락된 것은 두 곡뿐이었다. 밤이 깊어 가고 있었고, 미스 베이츠가 어머니 때문에 집에 돌아가기를 안달했다. 그리하여 한 번 더 시작하게 해 달라고 몇 번 청해 보다가, 그들은 웨스턴 부인에게 고마움을 표하고 아쉬운 얼굴을 지으며 그만둘 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 이게 잘된 일인지도 몰라요.\" 엠마를 마차까지 바래다주며 프랭크 처칠이 말했다. \"안 그랬으면 미스 페어팩스한테 청해야 했을 텐데, 당신 다음에 그녀의 맥없는 춤은 제 취향에 안 맞았을 테니까요.\"\n\n# 제2권 9장\n\n엠마는 콜 부부의 집에 가느라 위신을 굽힌 일을 후회하지 않았다. 그 방문은 이튿날 그녀에게 즐거운 기억거리를 많이 안겨 주었다. 품위 있는 은둔의 측면에서 잃었다고 여겨질 만한 것이 무엇이든, 인기의 광채로 넉넉히 보상받았을 터였다. 그녀는 콜 부부를 — 행복해질 자격이 있는 훌륭한 사람들을 — 분명 기쁘게 해 주었으리라! 그리고 쉽사리 사라지지 않을 이름을 그곳에 남겼으리라.\n\n완벽한 행복이란, 기억 속에서조차 흔치 않은 법이다. 그리고 그녀에게는 영 마음 편치 않은 두 가지가 있었다. 하나는, 제인 페어팩스의 마음에 대한 의심을 프랭크 처칠에게 흘려 버림으로써, 여자가 여자에게 지켜야 할 도리를 어긴 것은 아닌가 하는 점이었다. 옳은 일은 도무지 아니었다. 그러나 워낙 강하게 떠오른 생각이라 절로 새어 나가고 말았고, 자기가 들려준 말마다 그가 고분고분 수긍한 것은 그녀의 통찰력에 대한 찬사였으니, 입을 다물고 있었어야 마땅했다고 단정하기란 쉽지 않았다.\n\n후회되는 또 한 가지 일도 제인 페어팩스와 관련된 것이었는데, 이 점에 대해서는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 자신의 연주와 노래가 한참 못 미친다는 사실을 그녀는 가식 없이, 또렷이 후회했다. 어린 시절을 게으르게 보낸 일을 진심으로 가슴 아파했다 — 그러고는 자리에 앉아 한 시간 반을 맹렬히 연습했다.\n\n그러다 해리엇이 들어오는 바람에 멈추게 되었다. 해리엇의 칭찬으로 만족할 수 있는 사람이었다면, 그녀는 금세 위로받았을지도 몰랐다.\n\n\"아! 저도 엠마 양이나 미스 페어팩스만큼만 칠 수 있다면!\"\n\n\"우리 둘을 한데 묶지 말아요, 해리엇. 내 연주는 그분 연주와는 영 달라요. 등불이 햇빛과 다른 만큼이나요.\"\n\n\"어머나, 세상에 — 제 생각엔 엠마 양이 둘 중에 더 잘 치세요. 그분만큼은 충분히 잘 치시는걸요. 저는 정말이지 엠마 양 연주를 듣는 편이 훨씬 좋아요. 어젯밤에도 다들 엠마 양이 얼마나 잘 친다고들 했잖아요.\"\n\n\"조금이라도 아는 사람이라면 그 차이를 느꼈을 거예요. 사실은요, 해리엇, 내 연주는 딱 칭찬받을 만큼은 되지만, 제인 페어팩스는 그보다 훨씬 위예요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 저는 언제까지나 엠마 양이 그분만큼 잘 치신다고 생각할 거예요. 설령 차이가 있다 해도 아무도 알아채지 못할 거고요. 미스터 콜도 엠마 양이 얼마나 취향이 좋은지 말했고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠도 엠마 양의 취향을 두고 한참 이야기하면서, 자기는 기교보다 취향을 훨씬 높이 산다고 했어요.\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 제인 페어팩스는 둘 다 갖추었는걸요, 해리엇.\"\n\n\"정말요? 그분이 기교가 있는 건 봤지만, 취향까지 있는 줄은 몰랐어요. 그런 말은 아무도 안 했거든요. 게다가 저는 이탈리아 노래가 싫어요 — 한 마디도 알아들을 수가 없잖아요. 그리고 그분이 정말 그렇게 잘 친다 해도, 알다시피 그건 마땅히 해야 할 일에 지나지 않아요. 어차피 가르치는 일을 해야 할 테니까요. 어젯밤에 콕스네 자매가 그분이 어디 좋은 집안에 들어가게 될지 궁금해하더라고요. 엠마 양 보시기에 콕스네 자매는 어땠어요?\"\n\n\"늘 그렇듯이지요 — 아주 천박하고요.\"\n\n\"그 사람들이 저한테 뭘 얘기해 줬어요.\" 해리엇이 다소 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"별로 대단한 건 아니지만요.\"\n\n엠마는 미스터 엘턴 이야기가 나올까 두려우면서도, 그들이 무슨 이야기를 했는지 물을 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"그 사람들이 그러는데 — 지난 토요일에 미스터 마틴이 자기네 집에서 저녁을 먹었대요.\"\n\n\"어머!\"\n\n\"무슨 일로 그 집 아버지를 찾아왔는데, 저녁을 들고 가라고 붙잡으셨대요.\"\n\n\"어머!\"\n\n\"그 사람들이 그 사람 얘기를 한참 했어요. 특히 앤 콕스가요. 무슨 뜻이었는지는 모르겠지만, 내년 여름에 또 그 집에 가서 지낼 생각이 있냐고 묻더라고요.\"\n\n\"앤 콕스답게 무례하게 캐물으려던 거예요. 딱 그 앤 콕스가 할 법한 짓이죠.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 저녁 먹으러 온 날 아주 싹싹했다고 그러더라고요. 식사 때 자기 옆에 앉았다고요. 미스 내시는 콕스네 자매 둘 중 누구라도 그 사람과 기꺼이 결혼하고 싶어 할 거래요.\"\n\n\"그럴 만하죠 — 내 생각에 그 자매는 하이베리에서 둘째가라면 서러울 만큼 천박한 아가씨들이거든요.\"\n\n해리엇은 포드네 가게에 볼일이 있었다. 엠마는 함께 가는 편이 가장 신중하리라고 생각했다. 마틴 식구들과 또 우연히 마주칠 수도 있었고, 지금 같은 상태에서는 그것이 위험할 터였다.\n\n해리엇은 무엇에든 마음이 끌리고 한마디 말에도 이리저리 흔들리는 터라, 무엇 하나 사는 데 늘 한참이 걸렸다. 그녀가 여전히 모슬린 천을 들여다보며 마음을 바꾸고 있는 동안, 엠마는 심심풀이 삼아 문가로 갔다. 하이베리에서 가장 번화한 곳이라 한들 그 거리의 통행에서 큰 기대를 걸 수는 없었다. 미스터 페리가 바삐 지나가거나, 미스터 윌리엄 콕스가 사무실 문으로 들어가거나, 미스터 콜네 마차 말들이 운동을 마치고 돌아오거나, 고집 센 노새를 탄 떠돌이 우편 배달 소년이 지나가는 것이 그녀가 기대할 수 있는 가장 활기찬 광경이었다. 그러니 눈에 들어오는 것이라곤 쟁반을 든 푸주한, 가게에서 가득 찬 바구니를 들고 집으로 돌아가는 단정한 노파, 더러운 뼈다귀를 놓고 다투는 잡종개 두 마리, 빵집의 자그마한 활 모양 창 앞에 둘러서서 생강 빵을 곁눈질하며 어슬렁대는 아이들 한 줄뿐이었지만, 그녀는 불평할 까닭이 없음을 알았고, 그것만으로도 충분히 즐거웠다 — 여전히 문가에 서 있기에 충분할 만큼은. 생기 있고 편안한 마음이란 아무것도 보지 않고도 견딜 수 있으며, 또 무엇이든 마음에 차지 않는 것은 눈에 들어오지 않는 법이다.\n\n그녀는 랜덜스로 이어지는 길을 내려다보았다. 풍경이 넓어지더니, 두 사람이 나타났다. 웨스턴 부인과 그녀의 의붓아들이었다. 두 사람은 하이베리로 걸어 들어오는 중이었다 — 물론 하트필드로 가는 길이었으리라. 그러나 그들은 우선 베이츠 부인 댁에 들르려고 멈춰 섰다. 그 집은 포드네 가게보다 랜덜스에 조금 더 가까웠다. 두 사람이 막 문을 두드리려던 참에, 엠마와 눈이 마주쳤다. 곧장 그들은 길을 건너 그녀에게 다가왔다. 어제 모임의 유쾌함이 지금의 만남에 새삼 기쁨을 더해 주는 듯했다. 웨스턴 부인은 새 악기를 들으러 베이츠 댁에 들를 참이라고 알려 주었다.\n\n\"제 길동무가 그러는데요,\" 부인이 말했다. \"어젯밤 제가 미스 베이츠한테 오늘 아침에 꼭 오겠다고 분명히 약속했다는 거예요. 저는 도무지 기억이 안 나는데 말이죠. 날을 잡았던 줄은 몰랐어요. 하지만 이 사람이 그렇다고 하니, 지금 가는 길이랍니다.\"\n\n\"웨스턴 부인이 방문하시는 동안에는,\" 프랭크 처칠이 말했다. \"저는 미스 우드하우스 일행에 끼어, 하트필드에서 부인을 기다려도 되지 않을까요 — 집으로 가시는 길이라면요.\"\n\n웨스턴 부인은 실망했다.\n\n\"나는 자네도 함께 가는 줄 알았는데. 그 댁에서 무척 반가워할 텐데요.\"\n\n\"제가요! 저야 거치적거리기만 할 텐데요. 하지만, 어쩌면 — 여기서도 마찬가지로 거치적거릴지 모르겠군요. 미스 우드하우스는 저를 원치 않는 눈치인데요. 제 외숙모님도 물건을 고르실 때면 늘 저를 내쫓으세요. 제가 옆에서 안달하게 만들어 죽을 지경이라고요. 그런데 미스 우드하우스도 똑같은 말을 하고 싶은 얼굴인걸요. 제가 어찌해야 할까요?\"\n\n\"저는 제 볼일로 여기 있는 게 아니에요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"그저 친구를 기다리고 있을 뿐이죠. 곧 끝날 테고, 그러면 우리도 집으로 갈 거예요. 하지만 웨스턴 부인과 함께 가서 악기를 들어 보시는 편이 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 — 그렇게 권하신다면요. 하지만 (미소를 지으며) 만약 캠벨 대령이 부주의한 친구한테 일을 맡긴 거라면, 그래서 그 악기 소리가 영 시원치 않은 것으로 드러나면 — 제가 뭐라고 해야 할까요? 저는 웨스턴 부인께 아무 도움이 못 될 텐데요. 부인이야 혼자서도 거뜬하실 거예요. 거북한 진실도 부인의 입을 거치면 듣기 좋게 들리겠지만, 저는 예의 바른 거짓말에 관한 한 세상에서 가장 한심한 인간이거든요.\"\n\n\"저는 그런 말은 도통 안 믿어요.\" 엠마가 받았다. \"당신도 필요할 때면 이웃들만큼은 충분히 진실하지 못할 수 있다고 확신해요. 하지만 그 악기가 시원치 않다고 볼 까닭은 없어요. 오히려 정반대죠 — 어젯밤 미스 페어팩스의 의견을 제대로 알아들었다면 말이에요.\"\n\n\"같이 가요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"정 싫지만 않다면요. 오래 잡아 두지는 않을 거예요. 그러고 나서 하트필드로 가면 되죠. 그분들을 따라 하트필드로 가요. 정말이지 자네가 나와 함께 가 주면 좋겠어요. 큰 정성으로 여겨질 거예요! 게다가 나는 줄곧 자네가 그럴 작정인 줄로만 알았어요.\"\n\n그는 더는 할 말이 없었다. 하트필드라는 보상의 희망을 안고, 웨스턴 부인과 함께 베이츠 부인 댁 문으로 되돌아갔다. 엠마는 그들이 들어가는 것을 지켜본 뒤, 흥미진진한 계산대 앞에서 해리엇과 합류했다 — 무지 모슬린이 필요하다면 무늬 있는 것을 들여다봐야 소용없다는 것을, 또 파란 리본이 제아무리 곱다 한들 노란 무늬에는 도무지 어울리지 않으리라는 것을, 온 정신을 다해 그녀에게 납득시키려 애쓰면서. 마침내 모든 것이, 심지어 꾸러미를 어디로 보낼지까지 정해졌다.\n\n\"고더드 부인 댁으로 보내 드릴까요, 아가씨?\" 미시즈 포드가 물었다. \"네 — 아니요 — 네, 고더드 부인 댁으로요. 다만 제 견본 드레스가 하트필드에 있어서요. 아니, 하트필드로 보내 주세요, 괜찮으시다면요. 그런데 그러면 고더드 부인이 보고 싶어 하실 텐데요. 견본 드레스야 아무 날에나 집으로 가져갈 수 있고요. 하지만 리본은 당장 필요하니까 — 그러니 하트필드로 보내는 게 낫겠어요 — 적어도 리본은요. 미시즈 포드, 두 꾸러미로 나눠 주실 수 있죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"미시즈 포드한테 두 꾸러미로 만드는 수고를 끼칠 것까지야 없잖아요, 해리엇.\"\n\n\"그러게요, 그럴 것까지야 없죠.\"\n\n\"수고랄 것도 없답니다, 아가씨.\" 친절한 미시즈 포드가 말했다.\n\n\"아유, 하지만 정말이지 한 꾸러미로만 받는 편이 훨씬 좋겠어요. 그럼, 괜찮으시다면, 전부 고더드 부인 댁으로 보내 주세요 — 잘 모르겠네 — 아니, 제 생각엔, 미스 우드하우스, 그냥 하트필드로 보내게 하고 밤에 제가 집으로 가져가는 게 나을 것 같기도 하고요. 어떻게 하는 게 좋을까요?\"\n\n\"이 일에 단 반 초도 더 쓰지 않는 게 좋겠어요. 하트필드로 보내 주세요, 미시즈 포드.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 그게 훨씬 낫겠어요.\" 해리엇이 아주 만족스러운 듯 말했다. \"고더드 부인 댁으로 보내는 건 저도 영 마음에 안 들었거든요.\"\n\n목소리들이 가게로 다가왔다 — 아니, 목소리는 하나에 부인은 둘이라 해야 옳았다. 웨스턴 부인과 미스 베이츠가 문간에서 그들과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스, 이게 웬일이에요.\" 미스 베이츠가 말했다. \"저는 그저 부탁을 드리려고 부랴부랴 건너온 거예요. 잠깐만이라도 저희와 함께 앉아서, 새 악기에 대한 의견을 좀 들려주십사 하고요. 미스 우드하우스도, 미스 스미스도요. 안녕하세요, 미스 스미스? 덕분에 아주 잘 지냅니다. 그리고 제가 꼭 모셔 가고 싶어서, 웨스턴 부인께도 같이 가 달라고 부탁드렸지요.\"\n\n\"베이츠 부인과 미스 페어팩스도 모두 안녕하시기를 —\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지내요, 정말 고마워요. 저희 어머니는 더없이 정정하시고요. 제인도 어젯밤에 감기 한 번 안 들었답니다. 미스터 우드하우스는 어떠세요? 그렇게 좋으시다니 정말 기뻐요. 웨스턴 부인이 미스 우드하우스가 여기 계시다고 하시기에 — 아! 그래서 제가 그랬죠, 그럼 얼른 건너가야겠다고요. 미스 우드하우스라면 분명 제가 잠깐 건너가서 들어와 주십사 부탁드리는 걸 허락해 주실 거라고요. 저희 어머니가 보시면 얼마나 기뻐하실까 — 게다가 지금 이렇게 좋은 분들이 다 모이셨으니 거절하실 리가 없다고요. '아, 부디 그러시지요.' 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 그러더군요. '미스 우드하우스가 악기를 들고 내려 주실 의견이라면 들을 만한 가치가 있을 겁니다.' 그래서 제가 그랬죠, 그래도 두 분 중 한 분이 같이 가 주시면 더 확실할 것 같다고요. '아,' 그러고는 그러더라고요. '잠깐만 기다려 주세요, 이 일만 마치고요.' 글쎄, 믿으시겠어요, 미스 우드하우스, 그 사람이 세상에 둘도 없이 싹싹하게, 저희 어머니 안경의 대갈못을 끼워 넣고 있지 뭐예요. 오늘 아침에 그 대갈못이 빠졌거든요. 어찌나 친절한지! 어머니가 안경을 못 쓰셔서 — 쓸 수가 없으셨거든요. 그러고 보니, 사람이라면 누구나 안경을 두 개씩은 가지고 있어야 해요. 정말 그래야 한다니까요. 제인도 그렇게 말하더라고요. 저는 무엇보다 먼저 그걸 존 손더스한테 가져갈 생각이었는데, 아침내 이런저런 일이 자꾸 가로막더라고요. 처음엔 이 일, 다음엔 저 일, 뭐라 말할 수도 없을 만큼요. 한번은 패티가 와서 부엌 굴뚝을 청소해야 할 것 같다고 하더라고요. 아유, 그래서 제가 그랬죠, 패티, 그런 안 좋은 소식은 나한테 가져오지 말라고요. 지금 마님 안경 대갈못이 빠졌다고요. 그러고 나니 구운 사과가 왔어요. 미시즈 월리스가 자기 집 아이 편에 보냈더라고요. 그 월리스네 식구들은 우리한테 늘 더없이 정중하고 친절하답니다. 더러 미시즈 월리스가 무례할 때도 있고 아주 퉁명스럽게 대꾸한다는 말을 하는 사람들도 있던데, 우리는 그 댁한테서 더없는 정성 말고는 받아 본 적이 없어요. 그게 우리 단골 덕을 보려는 것일 리도 없는 게, 우리가 빵을 먹어 봐야 얼마나 먹겠어요? 셋뿐인데요. 게다가 지금은 사랑하는 제인까지 와 있지만 — 그 애는 정말 아무것도 안 먹어요. 아침을 얼마나 형편없이 드는지, 보시면 깜짝 놀라실 거예요. 어머니한테는 그 애가 얼마나 적게 먹는지 차마 알릴 수가 없어서 — 이 말 했다 저 말 했다 하면서 슬쩍 넘겨요. 그래도 한낮쯤 되면 배가 고파지는데, 그 애가 이 구운 사과만큼 좋아하는 게 없거든요. 게다가 더없이 몸에 좋고요. 며칠 전에 마침 길에서 미스터 페리를 만나 물어볼 기회가 있었답니다. 전에도 의심한 적은 없었지만요 — 미스터 우드하우스가 구운 사과를 권하시는 걸 하도 자주 들었으니까요. 미스터 우드하우스는 사과를 정말 몸에 좋게 만드는 방법은 그것뿐이라고 여기시는 것 같아요. 그래도 우리는 사과 경단도 아주 자주 먹어요. 패티가 사과 경단을 기가 막히게 만들거든요. 자, 웨스턴 부인, 부인이 잘 설득해 주셨으니, 이분들도 와 주시겠지요.\"\n\n엠마는 \"기꺼이 베이츠 부인을 뵈러 가겠다는 둥\" 응했고, 마침내 일행은 가게에서 나섰다. 미스 베이츠가 다음과 같은 인사로 잠시 더 머문 것 말고는 더 지체할 일도 없었다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 미시즈 포드? 어머, 미안해요. 아까는 못 봤네요. 읍내에서 매력적인 새 리본들을 잔뜩 들여놓으셨다면서요. 제인이 어제 아주 기뻐하며 돌아왔답니다. 고마워요, 장갑은 아주 잘 맞아요 — 손목께가 살짝 큰 것뿐인데, 제인이 줄이고 있어요.\"\n\n\"내가 무슨 얘기를 하고 있었더라?\" 모두 거리로 나서자, 미스 베이츠가 다시 입을 열며 말했다.\n\n엠마는 그 온갖 잡다한 이야기 가운데 그녀가 무엇을 붙들지 궁금했다.\n\n\"아무리 해도 무슨 얘기를 하던 중이었는지 기억이 안 나네요. 아! 어머니 안경 말이지. 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 어찌나 싹싹하던지! '아,' 그러더라고요. '제가 대갈못을 끼울 수 있을 것 같습니다. 저는 이런 일을 무척 좋아하거든요.' 그게 말이죠, 그 사람이 얼마나 그렇게나... 정말이지 이 말은 해야겠어요, 전부터 그 사람에 대해 들은 것도 많고 기대한 것도 컸지만, 그 사람은 그 모든 걸 한참 뛰어넘는다니까요... 웨스턴 부인, 진심으로 축하드려요, 더없이 따뜻한 마음으로요. 그 사람은 가장 자상한 부모라면 무엇이든... '아,' 그러더라고요. '제가 대갈못을 끼울 수 있겠는데요. 저는 이런 일을 무척 좋아하거든요.' 그 태도를 저는 절대로 잊지 못할 거예요. 그리고 제가 벽장에서 구운 사과를 꺼내 와서, 우리 친구분들이 좀 들어 주시면 정말 고맙겠다고 했더니, '아,' 그가 곧바로 그러더라고요. '과일 중에 이만큼 맛있는 건 없지요. 게다가 이건 제가 평생 본 집에서 구운 사과 가운데 가장 보기 좋은 것들인데요.' 그게 말이죠, 정말이지 그렇게나... 그리고 그 태도로 보아 빈말이 아니었던 게 분명해요. 사실 정말 더없이 맛있는 사과랍니다. 미시즈 월리스가 솜씨를 제대로 발휘해 주거든요 — 다만 우리는 두 번 넘게는 굽지 않는데, 미스터 우드하우스가 세 번은 구우라고 우리한테 약속을 시키셨지요 — 하지만 미스 우드하우스가 그 얘기는 입 밖에 안 내 주실 만큼 인자하시겠지요. 사과 자체가 굽기에는 더없이 좋은 품종인 게 틀림없어요. 전부 돈웰에서 온 거예요 — 미스터 나이틀리가 후하게 보내 주신 것 가운데 일부랍니다. 해마다 한 자루씩 보내 주시거든요. 그리고 정말이지 그 댁 나무에서 나는 사과처럼 오래가는 사과는 어디에도 없을 거예요 — 그런 나무가 두 그루 있나 봐요. 어머니 말씀이, 그 과수원은 당신 젊으셨을 적부터 늘 유명했다더군요. 그런데 며칠 전에는 정말 깜짝 놀랐지 뭐예요 — 미스터 나이틀리가 어느 날 아침에 들르셨는데, 마침 제인이 그 사과를 먹고 있었거든요. 그래서 사과 얘기가 나와서 그 애가 얼마나 잘 먹는지 말했더니, 우리 사과가 이제 다 떨어지지 않았느냐고 물으시더라고요. '분명 그러실 텐데요.' 그러시면서, '제가 좀 더 보내 드리지요. 저는 도저히 다 못 쓸 만큼 많이 가지고 있으니까요. 윌리엄 라킨스가 올해는 평소보다 더 많이 남겨 두게 해 주었거든요. 못 쓰게 되기 전에 좀 더 보내 드리겠습니다.' 그래서 제가 제발 그러지 마시라고 했죠 — 사실 우리 사과가 다 떨어졌다고 단정해서 말할 수는 없었거든요 — 정말 여섯 개밖에 안 남았지만요. 그래도 그건 다 제인 몫으로 둘 거고요. 게다가 그분이 이미 그렇게 후하게 주셨는데 더 보내 주시게 하는 건 도무지 견딜 수가 없었어요. 제인도 똑같이 말하더라고요. 그러고 그분이 가신 뒤에 그 애가 저랑 거의 다툴 뻔했지 뭐예요 — 아니, 다퉜다고 하면 안 되겠네요, 우리는 평생 다툰 적이 없으니까요. 하지만 제가 사과가 거의 다 떨어졌다고 털어놓은 걸 그 애가 무척 속상해했어요. 우리한테 아직 많이 남은 줄로 그분이 믿게 했어야 했다는 거예요. 아유, 그래서 제가 그랬죠, 얘야, 나도 할 수 있는 만큼은 말했단다, 하고요. 그런데 바로 그날 저녁에 윌리엄 라킨스가 사과를 큰 바구니로 한가득 들고 건너왔지 뭐예요. 똑같은 품종으로, 적어도 한 부셸은 됐어요. 그래서 제가 정말 고마워서 내려가 윌리엄 라킨스와 이야기를 나누며 별별 인사를 다 했지요, 짐작이 가실 거예요. 윌리엄 라킨스는 정말 오랜 지인이거든요! 저는 늘 그 사람 보는 게 반가워요. 그런데 나중에 패티한테 들으니, 윌리엄이 그러는데 그게 자기 주인 댁에 있던 그 품종 사과 전부였대요. 죄다 가져온 거라고요 — 이제 주인 댁에는 굽거나 삶을 사과가 한 개도 안 남았다는 거예요. 윌리엄 본인은 별로 개의치 않는 눈치였대요. 주인이 그렇게 많이 팔았다는 게 좋았던 거죠. 윌리엄은 알다시피 무엇보다 주인 이익을 먼저 생각하는 사람이거든요. 그래도 미시즈 호지스는 사과를 죄다 보내 버린 걸 무척 못마땅해하더래요. 주인이 올봄에 사과 타르트를 한 번도 더 못 드시게 된 걸 견딜 수 없어 했다는 거예요. 윌리엄이 패티한테 그 얘기를 했지만, 신경 쓰지 말라고, 우리한테는 절대 한마디도 하지 말라고 당부했대요. 미시즈 호지스가 더러 심통을 부리긴 해도, 그렇게 많은 자루를 팔았으니 남은 걸 누가 먹든 무슨 상관이냐고요. 그래서 패티가 저한테 그 얘기를 했는데, 저는 정말이지 너무 놀랐지 뭐예요! 미스터 나이틀리가 이 일을 아시게 되는 건 무슨 일이 있어도 싫어요! 그분이 얼마나 그렇게... 저는 제인한테도 알리지 않으려 했는데, 공교롭게도 미처 깨닫기 전에 그만 말해 버렸지 뭐예요.\"\n\n미스 베이츠는 패티가 문을 여는 바로 그 순간에 마침 이야기를 끝낸 참이었다. 그래서 손님들은 더 따라가 들을 만한 변변한 이야기 없이 계단을 올라갔다. 다만 그녀의 두서없는 호의의 소리만이 뒤를 쫓아왔다.\n\n\"부디 조심하세요, 웨스턴 부인, 모퉁이에 계단이 하나 있어요. 부디 조심하세요, 미스 우드하우스, 저희 집 계단이 좀 어두워서요 — 바랄 수 있는 것보다 좀 더 어둡고 좁답니다. 미스 스미스, 부디 조심하세요. 미스 우드하우스, 정말 걱정되네요, 발을 부딪치신 게 분명해요. 미스 스미스, 모퉁이에 계단 있어요.\"\n\n# 제2권 10장\n\n그들이 들어섰을 때 작은 거실의 풍경은 평온함 그 자체였다. 베이츠 부인은 여느 때의 소일거리를 빼앗긴 채 난롯불 한쪽에서 졸고 있었고, 프랭크 처칠은 그 곁의 탁자에서 부인의 안경을 손보느라 더없이 골몰해 있었으며, 제인 페어팩스는 그들에게 등을 돌린 채 서서 자기 피아노에 마음을 쏟고 있었다.\n\n그러나 그 청년은 그렇게 분주한 와중에도 엠마를 다시 본 순간 더없이 흐뭇한 낯빛을 지어 보일 줄 알았다.\n\n\"이거 반가운데요.\" 그가 다소 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"제가 어림잡은 것보다 적어도 십 분은 일찍 오셨군요. 보시다시피 저는 쓸모 있는 사람이 되어 보려 애쓰는 중입니다. 성공할 것 같은지 한번 말씀해 주시죠.\"\n\n\"아니,\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"아직도 못 끝냈어요? 이런 속도라면 은세공 직공 노릇으로는 입에 풀칠도 못 하겠는걸요.\"\n\n\"쉬지 않고 작업한 건 아닙니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"미스 페어팩스가 악기를 안정되게 세우려는 걸 거들고 있었거든요. 영 단단히 서질 않더군요. 바닥이 고르지 못한 탓인 것 같습니다. 보시다시피 다리 하나를 종이로 괴어 보고 있었어요. 이렇게 청에 응해 와 주시다니 참 친절하시군요. 그냥 서둘러 댁으로 가 버리시지나 않을까 은근히 걱정했거든요.\"\n\n그는 엠마를 자기 곁에 앉게끔 해 놓고, 그녀에게 가장 잘 구워진 사과를 골라 주랴, 작업을 거들거나 조언해 달라고 청하랴, 제인 페어팩스가 다시 피아노 앞에 앉을 채비가 완전히 될 때까지 줄곧 그 일에 매달려 있었다. 제인이 곧바로 채비가 되지 못하는 것은 그녀의 신경 상태 탓이라고 엠마는 짐작했다. 그녀는 아직 그 악기를 손에 넣은 지 얼마 되지 않아 감정 없이는 건반에 손을 댈 수 없었던 것이다. 마음을 다잡아야만 연주할 힘이 생기는 것이었다. 그 까닭이 무엇이든, 엠마는 그런 감정을 가엾이 여기지 않을 수 없었고, 다시는 그것을 이웃 앞에 드러나게 하지 않으리라 다짐하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n마침내 제인이 시작했고, 처음 몇 마디는 힘없이 짚였지만, 차츰 그 악기의 능력이 온전히 발휘되었다. 웨스턴 부인은 아까도 흡족해했고, 다시금 흡족해했다. 엠마도 그 찬사에 모두 동참했다. 그리하여 그 피아노는 안목 있는 평가를 두루 거쳐, 더할 나위 없이 촉망되는 물건이라는 판정을 받았다.\n\n\"캠벨 대령께서 누구에게 일을 맡기셨든,\" 프랭크 처칠이 엠마를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그 사람의 선택은 잘못되지 않았군요. 저는 웨이머스에서 캠벨 대령의 안목에 관해 적잖이 들었습니다. 그리고 이 높은 음들의 부드러움은 분명 대령과 그 일행 모두가 특히 귀하게 여길 만한 바로 그것이지요. 미스 페어팩스, 짐작건대 대령께서는 자기 친구에게 아주 세세한 지시를 내리셨거나, 아니면 직접 브로드우드사에 편지를 쓰셨겠지요. 그렇게 생각하지 않으십니까?\"\n\n제인은 돌아보지 않았다. 들어야 할 의무는 없었다. 마침 같은 순간에 웨스턴 부인이 그녀에게 말을 걸고 있었으니까.\n\n\"그건 공정하지 않아요.\" 엠마가 속삭였다. \"제 건 그저 되는대로 해 본 짐작이었어요. 저 사람을 괴롭히지 마세요.\"\n\n그는 미소를 머금고 고개를 가로저었는데, 의심도 거의 없고 자비도 거의 없는 얼굴이었다. 곧이어 그가 다시 시작했다.\n\n\"미스 페어팩스, 아일랜드에 있는 당신 벗들이 이번 일에 당신이 느낄 기쁨을 얼마나 함께 즐기고 있을까요. 분명 그분들은 당신 생각을 자주 하면서, 악기가 도착하는 날이 언제일까, 바로 그날이 언제일까 궁금해할 겁니다. 캠벨 대령께서 마침 이 무렵에 일이 진행되고 있다는 걸 알고 계실 거라 생각하십니까? 그분의 즉각적인 주문에 따른 결과라고 보십니까, 아니면 그저 두루뭉술한 지시만, 즉 시기는 정해 두지 않고 형편과 편의에 맡기는 식의 주문만 보내셨다고 보십니까?\"\n\n그가 말을 멈췄다. 그녀는 들을 수밖에 없었고, 대답을 피할 수도 없었다.\n\n\"캠벨 대령께서 편지를 보내 주시기 전까지는,\" 그녀가 애써 차분히 꾸민 목소리로 말했다. \"저로서는 무엇 하나 확신을 갖고 짐작할 수가 없어요. 그저 다 추측일 뿐이죠.\"\n\n\"추측이라—그렇지요, 추측이란 때로는 맞고 때로는 틀리는 법입니다. 저도 이 나사못을 단단히 박는 일을 얼마나 빨리 해낼 수 있을지 추측해 보고 싶군요. 미스 우드하우스, 사람이 한창 일에 몰두하면, 말을 하기는 한다 쳐도 어쩌면 그리 허튼소리만 나오는지요. 진짜 직공들이라면 입을 다물고 있겠지만, 우리 같은 신사 일꾼들은 일단 말 한마디라도 붙들면—미스 페어팩스가 추측에 관해 무슨 말씀을 하셨던가요. 자, 다 됐습니다. 부인,\" (베이츠 부인을 향해) \"안경을 돌려드리는 기쁨을 누리게 되었군요. 당장은 멀쩡하게 고쳐졌습니다.\"\n\n그는 모녀 양쪽에서 따뜻한 감사를 받았다. 그 가운데 딸 쪽의 인사를 조금 피해 보려고, 그는 피아노 쪽으로 가서, 아직 그 앞에 앉아 있던 미스 페어팩스에게 무엇이든 좀 더 연주해 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"부디 인심을 써 주신다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"어젯밤 우리가 췄던 그 왈츠 가운데 하나를 들려주시지요. 그 순간들을 다시 한번 살아 보게 해 주세요. 당신은 저만큼 그 시간을 즐기지 못하셨지요. 내내 지쳐 보이셨으니까요. 더 이상 추지 않게 되어 다행이라 여기셨을 겁니다. 하지만 저라면 반 시간만 더 출 수 있다면 온 세상을—사람이 내줄 수 있는 온 세상을 다 내주었을 거예요.\"\n\n그녀가 연주했다.\n\n\"사람을 행복하게 했던 곡을 다시 듣는다는 건 얼마나 큰 기쁨인지요! 제 기억이 틀리지 않다면, 저건 웨이머스에서 췄던 곡이군요.\"\n\n그녀는 잠시 그를 올려다보더니, 얼굴을 짙게 붉히고는, 다른 곡을 연주했다. 그는 피아노 가까이 놓인 의자에서 악보 몇 장을 집어 들고, 엠마를 향해 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"여기 제게는 아주 생소한 곡이 있군요. 이거 아십니까? 크레이머예요. 그리고 여기 새로 나온 아일랜드 가락집도 있고요. 그런 출처라면 능히 짐작할 만한 것이지요. 이것들은 다 악기와 함께 보내온 겁니다. 캠벨 대령께서 참으로 사려 깊으셨지요? 미스 페어팩스가 여기서는 악보를 구할 수 없으리란 걸 아셨던 거예요. 저는 그 배려의 그 대목이 특히 갸륵합니다. 진정 마음에서 우러나온 일임을 보여 주니까요. 무엇 하나 서둘러 한 것이 없고, 무엇 하나 어설픈 데가 없어요. 참된 애정만이 그런 일을 시킬 수 있는 법이지요.\"\n\n엠마는 그가 좀 덜 노골적이었으면 했지만, 재미있어하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러다 제인 페어팩스 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌을 때 미소의 잔영을 포착하고, 자의식에서 오는 그 짙은 홍조 가운데에도 은밀한 기쁨의 미소가 있었음을 알아차리고 나니, 재미있어함에 거리낌이 덜해졌고, 그녀에 대한 가책도 한결 가벼워졌다. 이 사랑스럽고 올곧고 흠 하나 없는 제인 페어팩스도, 보아하니 자못 나무람직한 감정을 품고 있는 것이었다.\n\n그는 악보를 모두 그녀에게 가져다주었고, 둘은 함께 그것을 들여다보았다. 엠마는 그 틈을 타 속삭였다.\n\n\"말이 너무 노골적이에요. 저 사람이 알아챘을 거예요.\"\n\n\"알아챘기를 바랍니다. 알아채 줬으면 하고요. 저는 제 속뜻이 조금도 부끄럽지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 솔직히 저는 절반쯤 부끄러워졌어요. 애초에 그런 생각을 떠올리지나 말 걸 그랬어요.\"\n\n\"그러셔서 저는 무척 기쁩니다. 게다가 그걸 제게 알려 주셔서요. 이제 저는 저 사람의 그 묘한 표정과 행동을 다 풀어낼 열쇠를 쥔 셈이니까요. 부끄러움은 저 사람한테 맡겨 두시죠. 잘못을 저질렀다면, 그걸 느껴야 마땅하지 않겠습니까.\"\n\n\"제 보기엔 저 사람에게 그 마음이 아주 없지는 않은 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"저한테는 그런 기색이 별로 안 보이는데요. 지금 이 순간 〈로빈 어데어〉를 연주하고 있잖습니까. 그 사람이 좋아하던 곡 말이죠.\"\n\n곧이어 미스 베이츠가 창가를 지나다가, 멀지 않은 곳에 말을 탄 미스터 나이틀리를 발견했다.\n\n\"어머나, 미스터 나이틀리네! 어떻게든 말 좀 붙여 봐야겠어요, 그저 고맙다는 인사라도 하게요. 여기 이 창은 안 열게요, 다들 감기 드실 테니까. 하지만 어머니 방으로 가면 되잖아요. 안에 누가 와 있는지 알면 분명 들어오실 거예요. 이렇게 다들 한자리에 모이시다니 정말 즐거운 일이에요! 우리 작은 방이 이런 영광을 다 누리네요!\"\n\n그녀는 말을 이어 가면서 이미 옆방으로 들어가 있었고, 그곳 여닫이창을 열어 곧장 미스터 나이틀리의 주의를 끌었으며, 두 사람의 대화는 한 마디 한 마디가, 마치 같은 방 안에서 오가기라도 한 듯 다른 사람들에게 또렷이 들렸다.\n\n\"안녕하세요? 안녕하세요? 네, 덕분에 아주 잘 지내요. 어젯밤 마차는 정말 고마웠어요. 우리 딱 맞춰 갔답니다. 어머니가 막 채비를 끝내셨거든요. 어서 들어오세요, 어서요. 여기 벗들이 와 계신 걸 보시게 될 거예요.\"\n\n미스 베이츠는 이렇게 운을 뗐고, 미스터 나이틀리도 제 차례에는 기어이 자기 말이 들리게 하겠다는 듯, 더없이 단호하고 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n\"조카분은 어떠십니까, 미스 베이츠? 댁의 모든 분 안부를 여쭙고 싶습니다만, 특히 조카분이요. 미스 페어팩스는 어떠신가요? 어젯밤에 감기나 들지 않으셨기를 바랍니다. 오늘은 좀 어떠세요? 미스 페어팩스가 어떤지 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n그래서 미스 베이츠는 그가 다른 어떤 말도 들으려 하기 전에 먼저 그 물음에 곧장 대답해야 했다. 듣고 있던 사람들은 재미있어했고, 웨스턴 부인은 엠마에게 의미심장한 눈길을 보냈다. 그러나 엠마는 여전히 한결같은 회의로 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"정말 고마워요! 마차 일로 정말이지 너무도 고마워요.\" 미스 베이츠가 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n그가 그녀의 말을 잘랐다.\n\n\"저는 킹스턴에 가는 길입니다. 뭐 해 드릴 일이라도 있을까요?\"\n\n\"아유, 세상에, 킹스턴이라니, 그러세요? 콜 부인이 일전에 킹스턴에서 뭘 좀 사 와야 한다고 하시던데요.\"\n\n\"콜 부인은 심부름 보낼 하인들이 있지 않습니까. 댁을 위해 뭐 해 드릴 일은 없나요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 고마워요. 그래도 어서 들어오세요. 안에 누가 와 계실 것 같으세요? 미스 우드하우스와 미스 스미스예요. 친절하게도 새 피아노를 들으러 와 주셨답니다. 말은 크라운 여관에 매어 두시고, 들어오세요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 그가 망설이는 투로 말했다. \"한 오 분쯤이라면 모를까요.\"\n\n\"그리고 여기 웨스턴 부인하고 미스터 프랭크 처칠도 와 계세요! 정말 즐거운 일이에요, 이렇게 벗들이 많이!\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 지금은 안 되겠어요, 고맙습니다. 이 분도 못 머뭅니다. 한시바삐 킹스턴으로 가야 해서요.\"\n\n\"아유, 들어오시라니까요. 다들 그렇게 반가워하실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 아니요. 방이 이미 충분히 찼겠는데요. 다음에 다른 날 들러서 피아노를 듣겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어머, 정말 아쉬워라! 아, 미스터 나이틀리, 어젯밤 모임은 얼마나 즐거웠는지요. 정말이지 더없이 유쾌했답니다. 그런 춤을 보신 적 있으세요? 정말 멋지지 않던가요? 미스 우드하우스와 미스터 프랭크 처칠 말이에요. 그에 견줄 만한 건 평생 본 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"오, 정말 무척 즐거웠지요. 그보다 못하게 말할 수가 없군요. 미스 우드하우스와 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 오가는 말을 죄다 듣고 계실 테니까요. 그리고,\" (목소리를 한층 더 높이며) \"미스 페어팩스도 언급하지 못할 까닭이 없지 싶군요. 제 보기에 미스 페어팩스는 춤을 아주 잘 추시고요. 그리고 웨스턴 부인으로 말하자면, 예외 없이 잉글랜드를 통틀어 제일가는 컨트리댄스 반주자이십니다. 자, 댁의 벗들에게 고마워하는 마음이 조금이라도 있다면, 답례로 당신과 저에 관해 무언가 꽤 큰 소리로 말해 줄 테지요. 하지만 저는 그걸 들으려고 머무를 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 미스터 나이틀리, 잠깐만요. 긴한 일이 있어요. 어찌나 놀랐는지요! 제인하고 저하고 둘 다 그 사과 일로 어찌나 놀랐는지 몰라요!\"\n\n\"이번엔 또 무슨 일입니까?\"\n\n\"저장해 두신 사과를 죄다 저희한테 보내 주시다니요. 잔뜩 있다고 하시더니, 이젠 하나도 안 남으셨다잖아요. 정말이지 어찌나 놀랐는지요! 호지스 부인이 화를 내실 만도 하죠. 윌리엄 라킨스가 여기서 그 얘기를 하더라고요. 그러시면 안 되는 거였어요, 정말이지 안 되는 거였다고요. 어머, 가 버리셨네. 저분은 도무지 고맙다는 인사를 못 견디시는 양반이에요. 그래도 이번엔 좀 머무르실 줄 알았는데. 그 얘길 안 하고 넘어갔으면 못내 아쉬웠을 거예요…… 자,\" (방으로 돌아오며) \"도무지 성공을 못 했네요. 미스터 나이틀리는 들르실 수가 없으시대요. 킹스턴에 가시는 길이라고요. 저더러 뭐 해 드릴 일이 없느냐고 물으시던데…….\"\n\n\"네,\" 제인이 말했다. \"그분의 친절한 제안, 다 들었어요. 죄다 들었답니다.\"\n\n\"아유, 그래, 얘야, 너도 들었을 만하지. 알다시피 문도 열려 있었고, 창문도 열려 있었고, 게다가 미스터 나이틀리가 큰 소리로 말씀하셨으니까. 죄다 들었을 게 분명하고말고. '킹스턴에서 뭐 해 드릴 일 없습니까?' 하시기에, 내가 마침 그 얘길 꺼냈더니…… 어머, 미스 우드하우스, 벌써 가셔야 하나요? 막 오신 것 같은데. 이렇게 와 주셔서 정말 고마워요.\"\n\n엠마는 정말 집에 돌아갈 때가 되었다고 느꼈다. 방문이 이미 오래 이어진 터였다. 그리고 시계를 살펴보니 오전 시간이 어찌나 많이 가 버렸는지, 웨스턴 부인과 그 일행도 함께 자리를 뜨면서, 랜덜스로 떠나기에 앞서 두 젊은 아가씨를 하트필드 대문까지 바래다주는 정도밖에는 여유를 낼 수 없었다.\n\n# 제2권 11장\n\n춤이라는 것 없이도 살아갈 수는 있을 것이다. 어떤 종류의 무도회에도 가지 못한 채 여러 달, 아주 여러 달을 잇따라 보내고도 몸에도 마음에도 이렇다 할 해를 입지 않은 젊은이들의 사례가 알려진 바 있으니까. 그러나 일단 시작이 되고 나면 — 빠른 움직임의 황홀함을 한번 맛보고 나면, 그것이 아무리 미미했다 한들 — 더 달라고 조르지 않을 만큼 둔한 사람이란 여간해서는 없는 법이다.\n\n프랭크 처칠은 하이베리에서 한 번 춤을 추어 본 터라 다시 한번 추기를 갈망했다. 그래서 미스터 우드하우스가 설득에 못 이겨 딸과 함께 랜덜스에서 저녁을 보내게 된 그 마지막 반 시간을, 두 젊은이는 이 일을 궁리하는 데 다 썼다. 처음 생각을 꺼낸 것도 프랭크였고, 그것을 밀고 나가는 데 가장 열을 올린 것도 프랭크였다. 어려운 점들을 가장 잘 헤아리고, 편의와 모양새를 가장 신경 쓴 쪽은 숙녀였으니까. 그래도 그녀에게는 적잖은 마음이 있었다 — 미스터 프랭크 처칠과 미스 우드하우스가 얼마나 멋지게 춤추는지를 사람들에게 다시 한번 보여 주고 싶은 마음, 제인 페어팩스와 견주어도 부끄럽지 않은 그 일을 해 보고 싶은 마음, 나아가 허영이라는 못된 부추김 따위 없이 그저 춤 그 자체를 즐기고 싶은 마음이. 그리하여 그녀는 먼저 두 사람이 있는 방을 발걸음으로 재어 얼마나 들어갈 수 있을지 가늠하는 일을 도왔고, 다음으로는 옆 응접실의 치수를 재었다 — 두 방의 크기가 정확히 같다는 미스터 웨스턴의 말이 어떻든, 이쪽이 조금이라도 더 넓기를 바라면서.\n\n미스터 콜의 집에서 시작했던 그 춤을 여기서 마저 끝내자는 것 — 같은 사람들을 다시 불러 모으고 같은 악사를 청하자는 것이 그의 첫 제안이자 청이었고, 이는 더없이 흔쾌한 동의를 얻었다. 미스터 웨스턴은 진심으로 즐거워하며 이 생각에 동참했고, 웨스턴 부인은 다들 추고 싶은 만큼 얼마든지 연주를 맡겠노라 기꺼이 자청했다. 그러고는 흥미진진한 일이 뒤따랐으니, 과연 누구누구가 올지 정확히 헤아리고, 짝마다 빠져서는 안 될 자리를 갈라 맡기는 일이었다.\n\n\"당신과 미스 스미스, 미스 페어팩스가 셋이고, 거기에 콕스 자매를 더하면 다섯이지요.\" 이 말이 몇 번이나 되풀이되었다. \"그리고 길버트 형제와 젊은 콕스, 우리 아버지에 저까지 있고, 미스터 나이틀리도 계시고요. 그래요, 즐기기에는 그만하면 충분해요. 당신과 미스 스미스, 미스 페어팩스가 셋이고, 콕스 자매를 더하면 다섯이고요. 다섯 쌍이면 자리는 넉넉하고말고요.\"\n\n그러나 곧 한편에서는 이런 말이 나왔다.\n\n\"그런데 다섯 쌍이 설 만큼 자리가 정말 넉넉할까요? 아무래도 그렇지 않을 것 같은데요.\"\n\n또 다른 편에서는 이런 말이 나왔다.\n\n\"그리고 따지고 보면 다섯 쌍으로는 일어서서 출 만한 값어치가 못 돼요. 진지하게 생각해 보면 다섯 쌍은 아무것도 아니거든요. 다섯 쌍을 초대한다는 건 말이 안 되죠. 그저 그 순간의 떠오른 생각이라면 모를까요.\"\n\n누군가는 미스 길버트가 오라비 집에 와 있을 거라며 다른 이들과 함께 초대해야 한다고 했다. 또 누군가는 미시즈 길버트도 청을 받았더라면 지난번 저녁에 춤을 추었으리라고 믿었다. 둘째 콕스를 위한 말도 한마디 끼어들었다. 그리고 마침내 미스터 웨스턴이 빠뜨려서는 안 될 사촌 한 집안과 빠뜨릴 수 없는 아주 오랜 지인 한 집안을 들먹이자, 다섯 쌍이 적어도 열 쌍은 되리라는 것이 확실해졌고, 도대체 어떤 식으로 이들을 다 들여놓을 수 있을지가 자못 흥미로운 궁리거리가 되었다.\n\n두 방의 문은 마침 서로 정면으로 마주 보고 있었다. \"두 방을 다 쓰면서 복도를 가로질러 춤을 추면 안 될까?\" 그것이 가장 좋은 안인 듯싶었으나, 그래도 여럿이 더 나은 방안을 바랄 만큼 흡족한 것은 아니었다. 엠마는 그러면 거북할 거라고 했고, 웨스턴 부인은 저녁 식사가 걱정이었으며, 미스터 우드하우스는 건강을 이유로 한사코 반대했다. 실로 그가 어찌나 언짢아하던지, 그 안은 더 밀고 나갈 수가 없었다.\n\n\"오! 안 됩니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"그건 더없이 경솔한 짓이에요. 엠마를 생각하면 도저히 못 견딜 일이오! 엠마는 몸이 튼튼하지 못해요. 지독한 감기에 걸릴 거요. 가엾은 어린 해리엇도 그럴 테고. 다들 그럴 거요. 웨스턴 부인, 부인도 꼼짝없이 앓아눕고 말 거요. 그런 무모한 이야기는 꺼내지도 못하게 하시오. 제발 그런 말은 못 하게 해 주시오. 저 젊은이는 (목소리를 낮추며) 아주 생각이 짧아요. 그 아버지께는 말하지 마시오만, 저 젊은이는 어딘가 미덥지가 못해요. 오늘 저녁 내내 문을 어찌나 자주 열어 대던지, 게다가 어찌나 무심하게 열어 둔 채로 두던지. 외풍은 도통 생각을 안 하는구려. 그를 미워하게 하려는 건 아니오만, 정말이지 저 젊은이는 어딘가 미덥지가 못해요!\"\n\n웨스턴 부인은 그런 비난이 안타까웠다. 그 말의 무게를 알기에, 그것을 누그러뜨리려고 할 수 있는 말은 다 했다. 이제 문이란 문은 모두 닫혔고, 복도를 쓰자는 안은 접어 두었으며, 두 사람이 있는 방에서만 춤을 추자는 처음의 안으로 다시 돌아갔다 — 그것도 프랭크 처칠이 어찌나 흔쾌히 앞장섰던지, 십오 분 전만 해도 다섯 쌍에 겨우 충분할 듯하던 그 자리를 이제는 열 쌍에게도 넉넉하다고 우겨 보려 들었다.\n\n\"우리가 너무 거창하게 생각했어요.\" 그가 말했다. \"쓸데없이 자리를 많이 잡았던 거죠. 열 쌍도 여기 거뜬히 설 수 있어요.\"\n\n엠마는 미심쩍어했다. \"그러면 북적댈 거예요 — 끔찍하게 북적댈 거라고요. 돌아설 자리도 없이 춤을 추는 것보다 더 고약한 일이 어디 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"지당한 말씀입니다.\" 그가 정색하며 답했다. \"그건 정말 고약하지요.\" 그러면서도 그는 여전히 자리를 재었고, 여전히 같은 말로 끝을 맺었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 열 쌍쯤은 그럭저럭 무리 없이 설 자리가 될 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 정말 막무가내네요. 그렇게 다닥다닥 붙어 서면 끔찍할 거예요! 북적대는 틈에서 춤추는 것만큼 즐거움과 동떨어진 일이 또 있을까요 — 그것도 좁은 방에서 북적대는 거라니!\"\n\n\"부정할 수 없는 말이지요.\" 그가 답했다. \"전적으로 동감입니다. 좁은 방에서 북적대다니 — 미스 우드하우스, 당신은 몇 마디로 그림을 그려 내는 재주가 있으시군요. 절묘해요, 정말 절묘합니다! 그렇긴 해도, 여기까지 진행해 놓고 보니 이 일을 포기하기가 영 내키질 않네요. 아버지가 실망하실 거예요 — 그리고 이래저래 — 글쎄요, 잘 모르겠습니다만 — 아무래도 저는 열 쌍쯤은 여기 거뜬히 설 수 있다는 쪽으로 마음이 기우는걸요.\"\n\n엠마는 그의 그 깍듯한 친절에 다소 고집스러운 면이 있어, 그녀와 춤추는 즐거움을 잃느니 차라리 맞서겠다는 심사임을 알아챘다. 그러나 그녀는 칭찬은 받아들이고 나머지는 눈감아 주었다. 만일 그녀가 언젠가 그와 결혼할 작정이었다면, 잠시 멈추어 곰곰이 따져 보며 그가 그녀를 좋아하는 마음의 값어치며 그 기질의 됨됨이를 헤아려 볼 만했을 것이다. 그러나 그저 알고 지내는 사이라는 목적에 비추어 보면, 그는 충분히 호감 가는 사람이었다.\n\n이튿날 한낮이 채 되기 전에 그는 하트필드에 와 있었다. 그가 어찌나 반가운 미소를 띠고 방에 들어서던지, 그 계획이 계속되고 있음이 분명했다. 곧 그가 한 가지 개선안을 알리러 왔음이 드러났다.\n\n\"자, 미스 우드하우스.\" 그가 거의 들어서자마자 입을 열었다. \"우리 아버지의 그 좁은 방들이 안긴 두려움 때문에 당신의 춤추고 싶은 마음이 아주 달아나 버리진 않았기를 바랍니다. 이 일에 관해 새 제안을 가지고 왔어요. 우리 아버지의 생각인데, 당신의 승낙만 떨어지면 곧장 실행에 옮길 참입니다. 이번에 계획 중인 그 작은 무도회에서 첫 두 곡을 당신과 함께 출 영광을 누려도 될까요 — 랜덜스가 아니라 크라운 여관에서 여는 무도회를요?\"\n\n\"크라운에서요!\"\n\n\"그래요. 당신과 미스터 우드하우스께서 반대만 안 하신다면 — 설마 반대하실 리 없겠지만 — 우리 아버지는 벗들이 그곳으로 자신을 찾아와 주는 호의를 베풀어 주기를 바라십니다. 거기라면 더 나은 자리를 마련해 드릴 수 있고, 랜덜스 못지않게 정성껏 맞이하겠다고 약속하실 수 있다는군요. 아버지가 직접 떠올린 생각이에요. 당신만 흡족하시다면 웨스턴 부인도 반대할 게 없다고 하셨고요. 우리 모두 같은 마음입니다. 오! 당신 말씀이 더없이 옳았어요! 랜덜스의 두 방 중 어느 쪽이든 열 쌍은 도저히 못 견딜 노릇이었을 테니까요! 끔찍했을 거예요! 내내 당신이 옳다는 걸 느끼고 있었으면서도, 어떻게든 자리를 마련하고 싶은 마음이 너무 앞선 나머지 물러서기가 싫었던 거죠. 멋진 맞바꿈 아닌가요? 승낙해 주시는 거죠 — 부디 승낙해 주시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인만 반대하지 않으신다면, 누구도 반대할 수 없는 안인 것 같네요. 제 생각엔 더없이 훌륭해요. 그리고 제 몫에 관한 한, 더없이 기쁠 거라고 답할 수 있어요. 이보다 더 나은 개선은 없을 것 같네요. 아버지, 정말 훌륭한 개선이라고 생각하지 않으세요?\"\n\n그녀는 아버지가 완전히 알아들을 때까지 같은 말을 되풀이하며 설명해야 했다. 그러고 나서도, 워낙 새로운 일인지라 받아들이게 하려면 한층 더 거듭 설득이 필요했다.\n\n\"아니다. 그건 도무지 개선이라고 할 수 없는 일이야 — 아주 나쁜 안이지 — 다른 것보다 훨씬 더 못해. 여관 방이라는 건 늘 눅눅하고 위험한 법이거든. 제대로 바람을 들인 적도 없고, 사람이 머물 만하지가 않아. 정 춤을 추겠다면 랜덜스에서 추는 편이 나아. 나는 평생 크라운의 그 방에 들어가 본 적이 없어 — 거길 지키는 사람들 얼굴조차 모른단다. 오! 안 돼 — 아주 나쁜 안이야. 크라운에서는 어디서보다도 더 고약한 감기에 걸리고 말 거다.\"\n\n\"제가 막 말씀드리려던 참이었는데요, 어르신.\" 프랭크 처칠이 말했다. \"이번 변경의 가장 큰 장점 가운데 하나가 바로 누구든 감기에 걸릴 위험이 거의 없다는 점이거든요 — 랜덜스보다 크라운이 그 위험이 훨씬 적어요! 미스터 페리야 이 변경을 아쉬워할 까닭이 있을지 모르겠습니다만, 그분 말고는 아무도 아쉬워할 사람이 없을걸요.\"\n\n\"이보게.\" 미스터 우드하우스가 자못 발끈하여 말했다. \"미스터 페리를 그런 부류의 사람으로 여긴다면 그건 자네가 크게 잘못 안 걸세. 미스터 페리는 우리 가운데 누구라도 아프면 더없이 마음을 쓰는 사람이야. 한데 나는 크라운의 그 방이 어째서 자네 아버지 댁보다 자네한테 더 안전하다는 건지 도통 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"바로 그 방이 더 넓다는 점 때문이지요, 어르신. 창문을 열 일이 아예 없을 테니까요 — 저녁 내내 단 한 번도요. 그리고 (어르신께서도 잘 아시다시피) 화근이 되는 게 바로 그 창문을 열어 더운 몸에 찬 공기를 들이는 못된 버릇이잖습니까.\"\n\n\"창문을 연다고! 한데 미스터 처칠, 랜덜스에서 누가 창문을 열 생각이나 하겠는가. 그렇게 경솔한 사람이 어디 있단 말인가! 나는 그런 일은 들어 본 적도 없어. 창문을 열어 놓고 춤을 추다니! 분명 자네 아버지도, 웨스턴 부인도 (전에 가엾은 미스 테일러였던 그분 말일세) 그런 일은 그냥 두고 보지 않을 걸세.\"\n\n\"아! 어르신 — 하지만 생각 없는 젊은 사람이 더러는 창문 휘장 뒤로 슬그머니 들어가, 아무도 눈치채지 못하게 창틀을 밀어 올리기도 하거든요. 저도 그런 경우를 여러 번 겪어 봤습니다.\"\n\n\"정말 그런가, 이 사람아? 맙소사! 나는 도무지 생각조차 못 했네. 하지만 나야 세상과 동떨어져 사는 사람이라, 듣는 이야기마다 놀랄 때가 많지. 어쨌거나 그렇다면 이야기가 달라지는군. 그리고 어쩌면, 우리가 차차 의논을 해 보면 — 하지만 이런 일은 어지간히 신중을 기해야 하는 법이야. 서둘러 결정할 수는 없어. 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인이 호의를 베풀어 언제 한번 아침나절에 들러 준다면, 함께 의논해서 어찌하면 좋을지 가늠해 볼 수 있겠지.\"\n\n\"하지만 어르신, 공교롭게도 제게 주어진 시간이 너무 빠듯해서요.\"\n\n\"오!\" 엠마가 끼어들었다. \"모든 걸 의논할 시간은 얼마든지 있어요. 조금도 서두를 게 없죠. 크라운에서 여는 걸로 꾸릴 수 있다면, 아버지, 말들한테도 아주 편할 거예요. 자기네 마구간이 코앞이니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇겠구나, 얘야. 그거야 큰 일이지. 제임스가 뭐라 불평을 하는 건 아니다만, 그래도 가능하다면 말들을 아껴 주는 게 옳지. 그 방들에 바람이 잘 들어 있다는 걸 내가 확신할 수만 있다면 좋으련만 — 한데 미시즈 스토크스를 믿어도 되겠느냐? 그게 미심쩍어. 나는 그 사람을 얼굴조차 모르거든.\"\n\n\"그런 종류의 일이라면 제가 다 책임지고 답할 수 있습니다, 어르신. 죄다 웨스턴 부인의 손에 맡겨질 테니까요. 웨스턴 부인이 모든 걸 도맡아 지휘하기로 했거든요.\"\n\n\"보세요, 아버지! 이제는 흡족하시겠죠 — 다름 아닌 우리의 그 다정한 웨스턴 부인이시잖아요. 세심함 그 자체이신 분요. 미스터 페리가 그 오래전에, 제가 홍역을 앓았을 때 뭐라고 하셨는지 기억나지 않으세요? '미스 테일러가 미스 엠마를 따뜻하게 싸매 주기로 했다면, 어르신께서는 아무 걱정 안 하셔도 됩니다.' 아버지가 그 말씀을 그분에 대한 더없는 칭찬이라며 얼마나 자주 되뇌셨는데요!\"\n\n\"그래, 정말 그랬지. 미스터 페리가 그리 말했고말고. 나는 그 말을 결코 잊지 못해. 가엾은 어린 엠마! 너는 홍역을 아주 심하게 앓았단다 — 그러니까, 페리가 그렇게 극진히 보살펴 주지 않았더라면 아주 심하게 앓았을 거란 말이지. 그는 일주일 동안 하루에 네 번씩 왔어. 처음부터 아주 순한 종류라고 했지 — 그게 우리한테 큰 위안이었단다. 하지만 홍역은 무서운 병이야. 가엾은 이저벨라의 어린것들이 언제고 홍역에 걸리거든, 부디 그 애가 페리를 부르면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n\"우리 아버지와 웨스턴 부인은 바로 지금 크라운에 가 계세요.\" 프랭크 처칠이 말했다. \"그 집이 쓸 만한지 살펴보고 계시지요. 두 분을 거기 남겨 두고, 저는 당신 의견이 듣고 싶어 견딜 수가 없어서 하트필드로 곧장 달려온 겁니다. 어쩌면 당신이 두 분과 합류해서 그 자리에서 조언을 해 주도록 설득할 수 있을까 싶어서요. 두 분 다 그렇게 전해 달라고 하셨습니다. 제가 당신을 거기 모셔 가도 좋다면, 두 분께 더없는 기쁨이 될 거예요. 당신 없이는 무엇 하나 흡족하게 결정할 수가 없으시거든요.\"\n\n엠마는 그런 회의에 불려 나가게 된 것이 더없이 기뻤다. 그녀가 가 있는 동안 모든 걸 곰곰이 생각해 보겠노라 아버지가 약속하자, 두 젊은이는 지체 없이 함께 크라운으로 떠났다. 그곳에 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인이 있었다. 그녀를 보고 그녀의 찬성을 얻게 되어 기뻐하며, 저마다 다른 식으로 무척 바쁘고 무척 행복했다. 부인은 다소 시름에 잠겨 있었고, 미스터 웨스턴은 무엇이든 더할 나위 없다고 여겼다.\n\n\"엠마.\" 부인이 말했다. \"이 벽지는 내가 생각했던 것보다 더 형편없네요. 봐요! 곳곳이 끔찍하게 더러운 게 보이죠. 그리고 징두리 판벽은 내가 상상했던 어떤 것보다도 더 누렇고 초라하고요.\"\n\n\"여보, 당신은 너무 따지는구려.\" 그녀의 남편이 말했다. \"그게 다 무슨 대수란 말이오? 촛불 아래선 그런 게 하나도 안 보일 거요. 촛불 아래선 랜덜스 못지않게 깨끗해 보일 테고. 우리 모임이 있는 밤에도 그런 건 전혀 눈에 안 띄잖소.\"\n\n여기서 숙녀들은 아마 이런 뜻이 담긴 눈길을 주고받았으리라. \"남자들은 무엇이 더럽고 안 더러운지 도통 모른다니까.\" 그리고 신사들은 어쩌면 저마다 속으로 이렇게 생각했으리라. \"여자들이란 그 사소한 호들갑이며 쓸데없는 걱정을 늘 달고 사는 법이지.\"\n\n그러나 신사들도 못 본 척하지 못할 난제가 하나 떠올랐다. 식사할 방에 관한 것이었다. 이 무도장이 지어질 무렵에는 만찬 따위는 고려되지 않았으니, 옆에 딸린 작은 카드놀이 방 하나가 보태진 것이 전부였다. 어찌해야 한단 말인가? 이 카드놀이 방은 지금도 카드놀이 방으로 쓰여야 할 터였다. 설령 자기네 넷이서 카드놀이는 필요 없다고 적당히 의결한다 한들, 그래도 편안하게 만찬을 들기에는 너무 작지 않은가? 그 용도에 한결 알맞은 크기의 다른 방을 마련할 수도 있었으나, 그 방은 집 반대편 끝에 있어서, 거기까지 가려면 길고 거추장스러운 복도를 지나야 했다. 이것이 난제였다. 웨스턴 부인은 그 복도에서 젊은 사람들이 외풍을 맞을까 봐 걱정이었고, 엠마도 신사들도 만찬 때 비참하게 부대껴야 할 처지는 도무지 견딜 수 없었다.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 정식 만찬은 차리지 말고, 그저 샌드위치 따위만 그 작은 방에 내놓자고 제안했다. 그러나 그것은 한심한 제안이라며 일축되었다. 만찬에 둘러앉지 않는 사사로운 무도회란 남녀의 권리를 짓밟는 파렴치한 사기라고 단죄되었고, 웨스턴 부인은 다시는 그런 말을 입에 올려서는 안 되었다. 그러자 부인은 다른 묘책의 길로 들어, 미심쩍은 그 방을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"이 방이 그렇게까지 작지는 않은 것 같아요. 아시다시피 우리가 많은 인원도 아니고요.\"\n\n그러는 동안 미스터 웨스턴은 긴 보폭으로 복도를 성큼성큼 걸어 다니며 큰 소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"여보, 당신은 이 복도가 길다고 말이 참 많구려. 따지고 보면 아무것도 아니야. 게다가 계단에서 들이치는 외풍이라곤 조금도 없고.\"\n\n\"우리 손님들이 대체로 어떤 배치를 가장 좋아할지 알 수만 있다면 좋겠어요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"대다수가 가장 흡족해할 일을 하는 게 우리 목표여야 할 텐데 — 그게 무엇일지 알 수만 있다면 말이죠.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 정말 그렇죠.\" 프랭크가 외쳤다. \"정말 옳은 말씀이에요. 이웃들의 의견이 필요하신 거죠. 그러실 만도 하고요. 그분들 가운데 좌장 격인 분들의 생각을 알 수만 있다면 — 가령 콜 댁 같은 분들요. 멀지 않은 데 사시잖아요. 제가 한번 찾아가 볼까요? 아니면 미스 베이츠는요? 그분은 더 가까이 사시니까요. 게다가 그 누구 못지않게 미스 베이츠야말로 나머지 사람들의 취향을 잘 헤아릴 분이 아닐까 싶기도 하고요. 아무래도 더 큰 회의가 필요한 것 같네요. 제가 가서 미스 베이츠더러 함께하자고 청해 올까요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요 — 정 그러시다면요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 다소 망설이며 말했다. \"그분이 무슨 도움이 될 거라고 생각하신다면요.\"\n\n\"미스 베이츠한테서는 쓸 만한 이야기를 하나도 못 들으실걸요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"그분은 그저 온통 기뻐하고 고마워하기만 할 뿐, 정작 아무 말도 안 해 주실 거예요. 당신 질문에 귀 기울이지조차 않으실 테고요. 미스 베이츠한테 의논해 봐야 무슨 득이 있을지 모르겠네요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그분은 어찌나 재미있는지, 정말이지 더없이 재미있는 분이잖아요! 저는 미스 베이츠가 떠드는 걸 듣는 게 아주 좋거든요. 그리고 알다시피 온 가족을 다 데려올 필요는 없으니까요.\"\n\n여기서 미스터 웨스턴이 끼어들었고, 어떤 제안이 나왔는지 듣고는 단호히 찬성했다.\n\n\"그래, 그렇게 하게, 프랭크. 가서 미스 베이츠를 모셔 와서 이 문제를 단번에 매듭짓세. 그분이 이 계획을 즐거워할 게 분명해. 그리고 어려운 일을 풀어 나가는 법을 보여 주기에 그분만큼 알맞은 사람도 없을 걸세. 미스 베이츠를 모셔 와. 우리가 지금 좀 까다롭게 굴고 있구먼. 그분이야말로 어떻게 하면 행복할 수 있는지를 보여 주는 살아 있는 본보기지. 아니, 두 분을 다 모셔 오게. 두 분 다 청해 와.\"\n\n\"두 분 다요, 어르신! 그 노부인이 오실 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"노부인이라니! 아니지, 당연히 그 젊은 아가씨를 말하는 거지. 프랭크, 자네가 조카는 빼고 이모만 모셔 온다면, 나는 자네를 아주 둔한 멍청이로 여길 걸세.\"\n\n\"오! 죄송합니다, 어르신. 제가 미처 떠올리지 못했네요. 물론 어르신께서 바라신다면, 두 분 다 모셔 오도록 애써 보겠습니다.\" 그러고는 그는 달려 나갔다.\n\n키 작고 단정하며 동작이 잰 이모와 그 우아한 조카를 모시고 그가 다시 나타나기 한참 전에 — 웨스턴 부인은 마음씨 곱고 어진 아내답게 그 복도를 다시 한번 살펴보고는, 그 폐단이 앞서 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 덜하다는 것을, 실로 아주 사소하다는 것을 깨달았다. 그리하여 결정의 어려움은 여기서 끝이 났다. 적어도 머릿속 궁리로는, 나머지가 죄다 더없이 매끄러웠다. 탁자며 의자, 조명과 음악, 차와 만찬에 관한 자잘한 채비는 죄다 저절로 정해지거나, 웨스턴 부인과 미시즈 스토크스가 아무 때나 의논해 처리하면 될 그저 사소한 일로 미뤄 두었다. 초대받은 사람은 누구나 분명 올 터였다. 프랭크는 이미 엔스컴에 편지를 띄워 보름 기한을 며칠 더 넘겨 머물게 해 달라고 청해 둔 참이었고, 그것이 거절될 리는 도무지 없었다. 그리하여 그것은 즐거운 무도회가 될 참이었다.\n\n미스 베이츠가 도착했을 때, 그녀는 더없이 진심으로 이것이 즐거운 무도회가 될 거라는 데 동의했다. 조언자로서는 쓸모가 없었으나, 찬동자로서는 (그쪽이 훨씬 더 안전한 자리였다) 진정 환영할 만했다. 두루 미치면서도 시시콜콜하고, 따뜻하면서도 그칠 줄 모르는 그녀의 찬사는 흐뭇하지 않을 수 없었다. 그리하여 다시 반 시간 동안, 모두가 이 방 저 방 사이를 오가며 누구는 의견을 내고 누구는 귀를 기울이며, 다 같이 다가올 일을 행복하게 즐겼다. 그 모임은, 엠마가 그날 저녁의 주인공에게 첫 두 곡을 확실히 약속받기 전에는, 또한 미스터 웨스턴이 아내에게 \"그 애가 엠마에게 청했다오, 여보. 잘됐어. 그럴 줄 알았다니까!\" 하고 속삭이는 것을 그녀가 엿듣기 전에는, 결코 끝나지 않았다.\n\n# 제2권 12장\n\n무도회의 전망이 엠마에게 완전히 흡족한 것이 되기에 모자란 것은 단 하나뿐이었다 — 그것이 프랭크 처칠이 서리에 머물도록 허락받은 기한 안의 날로 정해지는 일이었다. 미스터 웨스턴은 자신했지만, 엠마는 처칠 부부가 조카에게 보름을 단 하루라도 넘겨 머물도록 허락하지 않으리라는 것이 그리 불가능한 일이라고는 생각할 수 없었던 것이다. 그러나 그것은 가능한 일로 여겨지지 않았다. 준비에는 시간이 들어야 했고, 셋째 주에 접어들기 전에는 무엇 하나 제대로 갖추어질 수 없었으므로, 며칠 동안은 불확실한 가운데 계획하고 진행하고 바라야 했다 — 그 모든 것이 헛수고가 될 위험을 — 엠마가 보기에는 큰 위험을 무릅쓰고서.\n\n그러나 엔스컴은 너그러웠다. 말로는 아니더라도 실제로는 너그러웠다. 조카가 더 머물고 싶어 하는 것이 분명 그들의 마음에 들지는 않았으나, 반대하지는 않았다. 만사가 안전하고 순조로웠다. 그리고 한 가지 근심이 사라지면 대개 다른 근심이 그 자리를 차지하는 법이라, 이제 무도회를 확신하게 된 엠마는 다음 골칫거리로 미스터 나이틀리의 약 올리는 무관심을 떠안기 시작했다. 자신이 춤을 추지 않아서인지, 아니면 그 계획이 자기와 의논 없이 세워져서인지, 그는 그 일에 관심을 두지 않기로 작정한 듯했고, 그 일이 지금 어떤 호기심도 일으키지 못하게, 앞으로 어떤 즐거움도 주지 못하게 단단히 마음먹은 것 같았다. 엠마가 자진해서 이런저런 소식을 전해도, 그에게서 돌아오는 가장 호의적인 대답이라야 이런 정도였다.\n\n\"좋네. 웨스턴 내외가 몇 시간의 시끌벅적한 여흥을 위해 그 수고를 다 들일 만하다고 여긴다면, 나로서야 반대할 말은 없네. 다만 그들이 내 즐거움까지 골라 주지는 말았으면 하네. — 아, 그럼, 가긴 가야지. 거절할 수는 없으니까. 그리고 되도록 졸지 않으려고 애써 볼 작정이네. 하지만 솔직히 말하면, 집에 앉아 윌리엄 라킨스의 한 주 치 장부나 들여다보는 편이 훨씬 좋겠어. 훨씬 좋겠네. — 춤 구경이 즐겁다니! — 나는 천만에. 나는 그런 건 쳐다보지도 않네. 도대체 누가 그걸 본다는 건지 모르겠군. — 멋진 춤이란, 내가 보기엔, 미덕과 마찬가지로 그 자체가 보상일 수밖에 없어. 곁에 서 있는 사람들은 대개 전혀 딴생각을 하고 있게 마련이지.\"\n\n엠마는 이 말이 자신을 겨냥한 것임을 느꼈고, 그래서 적잖이 화가 났다. 그러나 그가 그토록 무관심하거나 그토록 못마땅해하는 것이 제인 페어팩스를 위해서는 아니었다. 그가 무도회를 깎아내리는 것은 제인의 감정에 따른 것이 아니었으니, 제인은 무도회 생각만으로도 유난히 큰 기쁨을 누리고 있었기 때문이다. 무도회는 제인을 활기차게 — 거리낌 없게 만들었고, 그녀는 제 입으로 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"아! 미스 우드하우스, 무도회를 망칠 일이 아무것도 일어나지 않기를 바라요. 그렇게 되면 얼마나 실망스러울까요! 저는 정말이지, 솔직히 말해서 무척이나 큰 기쁨으로 그날을 기다리고 있답니다.\"\n\n그러니 그가 윌리엄 라킨스와 함께 있는 편을 더 좋아한 것은 제인 페어팩스의 비위를 맞추기 위해서가 아니었다. 천만에! — 엠마는 그 추측에서 웨스턴 부인이 완전히 틀렸다는 것을 점점 더 확신하게 되었다. 그쪽에는 다정하고 측은히 여기는 마음이 듬뿍 있었으나 — 사랑은 없었다.\n\n아아! 그러나 미스터 나이틀리와 입씨름할 여유는 곧 없어졌다. 즐겁고 안심하던 이틀이 지나자마자 모든 것이 뒤엎어졌다. 미스터 처칠에게서 조카에게 당장 돌아오라고 재촉하는 편지가 왔다. 처칠 부인이 편치 않다는 것이었다 — 조카 없이는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 너무나 편치 않다고. 이틀 전 조카에게 편지를 쓸 때 이미 (남편의 말로는) 몹시 고통스러운 상태였으나, 남에게 고통을 주기 싫어하는 평소의 성정과 결코 제 몸은 생각하지 않는 한결같은 버릇 탓에 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않았던 것인데, 이제는 너무 아파서 미적거릴 수 없으니, 부디 지체 말고 엔스컴으로 떠나 달라고 간청해야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n이 편지의 골자는 웨스턴 부인의 쪽지를 통해 곧바로 엠마에게 전해졌다. 그가 떠나는 일은 피할 수 없었다. 그는 몇 시간 안에 가야 했다 — 다만 숙모를 진심으로 걱정하지는 않았기에, 떠나기 싫은 마음을 덜어 줄 것이 없었다. 그는 숙모의 병이 어떤 것인지 알고 있었다. 그 병은 오로지 숙모 자신에게 편할 때에만 도졌던 것이다.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그는 아침 식사 후에 서둘러 하이베리로 달려가, 그에게 조금이라도 관심을 가질 만한 그곳의 몇몇 벗에게 작별을 고할 시간밖에 낼 수 없으며, 머지않아 하트필드에 들를 것으로 보면 된다\"고.\n\n이 비참한 쪽지가 엠마의 아침 식사에 종지부를 찍었다. 그것을 한번 읽고 나니, 탄식하고 한탄하는 것 말고는 아무 일도 할 수 없었다. 무도회를 잃는 일 — 그 청년을 잃는 일 — 그리고 그 청년이 느끼고 있을 그 모든 감정이라니! — 너무나 비참했다! — 얼마나 즐거운 저녁이 되었을 것인가! — 모두가 그토록 행복하고! 그녀와 그녀의 파트너가 가장 행복하고! — \"내가 이렇게 될 거라고 말했지\"가 유일한 위안이었다.\n\n아버지의 심정은 사뭇 달랐다. 그는 주로 처칠 부인의 병을 생각하며 그녀가 어떤 치료를 받고 있는지 알고 싶어 했다. 그리고 무도회로 말하자면, 사랑하는 엠마가 실망한 것은 안타까운 일이지만, 모두 집에 있는 편이 더 안전하리라는 것이었다.\n\n엠마는 손님이 나타나기 한참 전부터 그를 맞을 채비를 끝내고 있었다. 그러나 이것이 그의 조급함을 어떻게든 깎아내리는 것이었다 해도, 막상 그가 왔을 때 그 슬픈 표정과 완전히 가라앉은 기색은 그를 만회시켜 줄 만했다. 그는 떠나는 일을 입에 올리기조차 너무 괴로워하는 듯했다. 그의 침울함은 더없이 또렷했다. 그는 처음 얼마 동안 정말로 생각에 잠겨 멍하니 앉아 있었고, 정신을 차렸다 싶으면 겨우 이런 말을 할 뿐이었다.\n\n\"온갖 끔찍한 일 중에서도 작별이 제일 끔찍합니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 또 오실 거잖아요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"이번이 랜덜스에 오는 마지막 방문은 아닐 테니까요.\"\n\n\"아! — (고개를 저으며) — 언제 다시 올 수 있을지 그것이 막막합니다! — 열성을 다해 애써 보겠습니다! — 그것이 제 모든 생각과 마음 쓰임의 목표가 될 거예요! — 그리고 외삼촌 내외분이 올봄에 런던으로 올라가시면 — 하지만 두렵게도 — 지난봄에는 꼼짝도 안 하셨지요 — 두렵게도 그건 영영 사라진 관례가 아닌가 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"우리의 가엾은 무도회는 아주 단념해야겠네요.\"\n\n\"아! 그 무도회 말입니다! — 우리는 왜 무얼 기다린 걸까요? — 왜 그 즐거움을 당장 붙잡지 못했을까요? — 행복이 준비 때문에, 어리석은 준비 때문에 망가지는 일이 얼마나 잦은지요! — 이렇게 될 거라고 당신이 말씀하셨지요. — 아! 미스 우드하우스, 당신은 어째서 늘 그렇게 옳으신지요?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 이번 경우만큼은 옳았던 것이 무척 유감스럽네요. 영리하기보다는 즐거웠던 편이 훨씬 좋았을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"제가 다시 올 수 있다면, 무도회는 그래도 열어야지요. 아버지께서 그것만 믿고 계세요. 약속을 잊지 마세요.\"\n\n엠마는 너그러운 표정을 지어 보였다.\n\n\"이번 보름이라니!\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"하루하루가 그 전날보다 더 소중하고 더 즐거웠어요! — 하루하루가 다른 어느 곳도 견딜 수 없게 만들었답니다. 하이베리에 남아 있을 수 있는 사람들은 행복하겠지요!\"\n\n\"이제 와서 우리를 그토록 후하게 쳐 주시니,\" 엠마가 웃으며 말했다. \"한 가지 여쭤 봐도 될까요. 처음에는 적잖이 미심쩍어하며 오신 것 아니었나요? 우리가 당신 기대를 좀 넘어선 것 아닐까요? 분명 그랬을 거예요. 우리를 좋아하게 되리라고는 그다지 기대하지 않으셨을 거라고 확신해요. 하이베리에 대해 즐거운 생각을 품으셨다면, 그렇게 오래 미루며 오시지 않았을 테니까요.\"\n\n그는 다소 켕기는 듯 웃었다. 그리고 비록 그 마음을 부인하기는 했으나, 엠마는 그것이 사실이었음을 확신했다.\n\n\"그래서 바로 오늘 아침에 떠나셔야 한다고요?\"\n\n\"네. 아버지가 여기서 저와 합류하실 거예요. 함께 걸어 돌아가야 하니, 저는 곧장 떠나야 합니다. 매 순간 아버지가 오시지 않을까 거의 조마조마할 지경이에요.\"\n\n\"벗인 미스 페어팩스와 미스 베이츠에게도 오 분조차 낼 수 없다고요? 운도 없으셔라! 미스 베이츠의 그 막강하고 논리정연한 정신력이 당신 마음을 다잡아 줄 수도 있었을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"네 — 거기엔 들렀습니다. 문 앞을 지나면서, 그러는 편이 낫겠다 싶었지요. 마땅히 할 일이었으니까요. 삼 분만 있으려고 들어갔는데, 미스 베이츠가 안 계셔서 발이 묶였어요. 외출 중이셨거든요. 들어오실 때까지 기다리지 않을 수 없을 것 같더군요. 그분은 누구나 웃을 수 있는, 아니 웃지 않을 수 없는 분이지만, 그렇다고 소홀히 대하고 싶지는 않은 분이지요. 그러니 그때 방문해 두는 편이 나았어요 —\"\n\n그는 머뭇거리더니, 일어나서 창가로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"요컨대,\" 그가 말했다. \"어쩌면, 미스 우드하우스 — 당신도 짐작이 전혀 없지는 않으실 듯한데 —\"\n\n그는 그녀의 생각을 읽으려는 듯 그녀를 바라보았다. 엠마는 무어라 말해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그것은 그녀가 바라지 않는, 무언가 더없이 진지한 일의 전조처럼 느껴졌다. 그래서 그것을 비켜 가게 하려는 마음에서, 억지로 입을 열어 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"옳은 말씀이세요. 그때 방문하시는 것이 가장 자연스러운 일이었지요 —\"\n\n그는 잠자코 있었다. 그가 자신을 바라보고 있다고, 아마 자신이 한 말을 곱씹으며 그 말투를 헤아려 보려 하고 있다고 그녀는 믿었다. 그가 한숨짓는 소리가 들렸다. 그에게는 한숨지을 까닭이 있다고 느끼는 것이 자연스러운 일이었다. 그는 그녀가 자신을 부추기고 있다고는 믿을 수 없었던 것이다. 어색한 몇 순간이 흐른 뒤, 그는 다시 자리에 앉았고, 한결 단호한 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"제게 남은 시간을 모두 하트필드에 바칠 수 있다고 느끼는 것만으로도 무언가 의미가 있었어요. 하트필드를 향한 제 마음은 더없이 따뜻합니다 —\"\n\n그는 또 말을 멈추었고, 또 일어섰으며, 몹시 당혹스러워하는 듯했다. — 그는 엠마가 짐작했던 것보다 그녀를 더 사랑하고 있었다. 그리고 그의 아버지가 나타나지 않았더라면 그 일이 어떻게 끝났을지 누가 알겠는가? 곧이어 미스터 우드하우스도 따라 들어왔고, 마음을 다잡아야 할 필요가 그를 진정시켰다.\n\n그러나 그 후 몇 분이 채 지나지 않아 지금의 시련은 끝이 났다. 미스터 웨스턴은 할 일이 있을 때면 언제나 민첩한 사람이었고, 피할 수 없는 궂은일을 미루지 못하는 만큼이나 미심쩍은 궂은일을 미리 내다보지도 못하는 사람인지라, \"갈 시간이오\"라고 말했다. 그러자 청년은 한숨지을 수도 있었고 또 실제로 한숨지었으나, 작별을 고하는 데 동의하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"여러분 소식은 다 듣게 될 거예요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그게 제 가장 큰 위안이지요. 여러분 사이에 일어나는 모든 일을 듣게 될 겁니다. 제가 웨스턴 부인께 편지 왕래를 부탁드려 두었어요. 친절하게도 약속해 주셨고요. 아! 정말로 멀리 있는 사람에게 관심이 있을 때, 편지를 주고받을 여성이 있다는 건 얼마나 큰 축복인지요! — 그분은 제게 모든 걸 알려 주실 거예요. 그분의 편지 속에서 저는 다시 사랑하는 하이베리에 와 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n더없이 다정한 악수와 더없이 진심 어린 \"안녕히 계세요\"로 그의 말은 끝났고, 문은 이내 프랭크 처칠을 밖에 남겨 둔 채 닫혔다. 통보도 짧았고 — 만남도 짧았다. 그는 가 버렸다. 그리고 엠마는 헤어지는 것이 너무나 서운했고, 그가 없으면 자기들의 그 작은 모임에 얼마나 큰 손실이 될지 너무나 절실히 내다본 나머지, 자기가 지나치게 서운해하고 지나치게 가슴 아파하는 것은 아닌지 두려워지기 시작했다.\n\n슬픈 변화였다. 그가 온 뒤로 두 사람은 거의 날마다 만나 왔던 것이다. 그가 랜덜스에 있었던 것이 지난 두 주에 엄청난 생기를 — 말로 다 할 수 없는 생기를 불어넣어 준 것은 분명했다. 아침마다 떠오르던 그를 만나리라는 생각, 그 기대, 그의 관심에 대한 확신, 그의 활기, 그의 거동! 더없이 행복한 보름이었으니, 거기서 내려와 하트필드의 평범한 일상으로 가라앉는 것은 적막한 일일 수밖에 없었다. 게다가 다른 모든 매력에 더하여, 그는 자신을 사랑한다고 그녀에게 거의 말하다시피 했던 것이다. 그가 어느 정도의 애정의 힘을, 혹은 어느 정도의 한결같음을 지녔는지는 또 다른 문제였다. 그러나 지금으로서는, 그가 자신에게 분명히 따뜻한 흠모를, 스스로 의식한 편애를 품고 있다는 것을 의심할 수 없었다. 그리고 이 확신이 그 밖의 모든 것과 어우러져, 그녀로 하여금 — 이에 반대했던 그간의 온갖 결심에도 불구하고 — 자신이 그를 조금쯤은 사랑하고 있는 게 틀림없다고 여기게 만들었다.\n\n\"분명 그래.\" 그녀는 말했다. \"이 무기력하고, 노곤하고, 멍한 기분, 앉아서 무얼 하기도 싫은 이 마음, 집 안의 모든 것이 따분하고 시시하게만 느껴지는 이 기분! — 나는 분명 사랑에 빠진 거야. 안 그렇다면 나는 세상에서 가장 별난 사람일 테지 — 적어도 몇 주 동안은. 뭐! 누군가에게 나쁜 일은 늘 다른 누군가에게는 좋은 일이지. 프랭크 처칠 때문은 아니더라도, 무도회 때문이라면 함께 슬퍼해 줄 사람이 많을 거야. 하지만 미스터 나이틀리는 좋아하겠지. 이제 그분은 마음만 내키면 그 사랑하는 윌리엄 라킨스와 함께 저녁을 보낼 수 있을 테니까.\"\n\n그러나 미스터 나이틀리는 의기양양한 기쁨 따위는 조금도 내비치지 않았다. 그는 자기로서는 유감스럽다고 말할 수 없었다 — 그랬다면 그 더없이 명랑한 표정이 그 말을 반박했을 것이다. 그러나 그는 다른 사람들이 실망한 것은 안타깝다고, 그것도 아주 침착하게 말했고, 적잖이 다정한 투로 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"엠마, 춤출 기회가 그토록 드문 자네야말로 정말 운이 없네그려. 자네는 운이 아주 없어!\"\n\n이 비통한 변화에 대한 제인 페어팩스의 솔직한 유감을 헤아려 볼 수 있게 그녀를 만나기까지는 며칠이 걸렸다. 그러나 막상 두 사람이 만났을 때, 제인의 그 태연함은 얄미웠다. 다만 그녀는 유난히 몸이 좋지 않았는데, 두통이 어찌나 심했던지, 무도회가 열렸더라도 제인은 참석할 수 없었을 것 같다고 그 이모가 단언할 정도였다. 그러니 그녀의 어울리지 않는 무관심의 얼마간을 병약함에서 오는 나른함 탓으로 돌려주는 것이 너그러운 일이었다.\n\n# 제2권 13장\n\n엠마는 자기가 사랑에 빠졌다는 것을 여전히 추호도 의심하지 않았다. 다만 그 정도가 얼마나 되는지에 대해서만 생각이 오락가락했다. 처음에는 꽤 깊다고 여겼다가, 나중에는 그저 약간일 뿐이라고 여겼다. 프랭크 처칠의 이야기를 듣는 것은 큰 즐거움이었다. 그리고 그를 위해서라도 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인을 보는 일이 그 어느 때보다 더 즐거웠다. 그녀는 그를 자주 생각했고, 그가 어떻게 지내는지, 기분은 어떤지, 숙모는 어떤지, 올봄에 다시 랜덜스로 올 가망이 있는지 알 수 있도록 편지가 오기를 못 견디게 기다렸다. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 자기가 불행하다고는 도무지 인정할 수 없었고, 첫날 아침이 지난 뒤로는 여느 때보다 일할 마음이 줄었다고도 인정할 수 없었다. 그녀는 여전히 분주하고 명랑했다. 그가 아무리 마음에 든다 해도, 그에게 결점이 있으리라고 상상할 수는 있었다. 게다가 그를 그토록 자주 생각하면서, 그림을 그리거나 바느질을 하며 앉아 두 사람의 연정이 무르익고 매듭지어지는 갖가지 흥미로운 구상을 천 가지나 짜내고, 흥미진진한 대화를 그려 보고, 우아한 편지를 지어내곤 했지만 — 그가 고백하는 상상 속 모든 장면의 결말은 언제나 그녀가 그를 거절하는 것이었다. 두 사람의 애정은 늘 우정으로 가라앉기 마련이었다. 무엇 하나 다정하고 매혹적이지 않은 것이 없는 이별이었으되, 어쨌든 두 사람은 헤어지기로 되어 있었다. 이 사실을 깨닫고 나니, 자기가 그리 깊이 사랑에 빠진 것은 아닐 수도 있다는 생각이 들었다. 아버지를 결코 떠나지 않겠다, 결코 결혼하지 않겠다는 이전부터 굳힌 결심이 있다 한들, 정말로 강렬한 연정이라면 그녀가 제 마음속에서 예상할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 갈등을 불러일으켰을 것이 분명했기 때문이다.\n\n\"나는 희생이라는 말을 도통 쓰지 않고 있어.\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. \"내가 짜낸 그 영리한 답변들, 그 우아한 거절들 가운데 어느 하나에도 희생을 한다는 암시가 들어 있질 않잖아. 아무래도 그 사람이 내 행복에 정말로 꼭 필요한 사람은 아닌 것 같아. 그렇다면 차라리 잘된 일이지. 나는 결단코 지금보다 더 많이 느끼도록 나 자신을 설득하지 않을 거야. 나는 이만하면 충분히 사랑에 빠진 거야. 더 빠진다면 오히려 곤란하지.\"\n\n대체로 그녀는 그의 감정에 대한 자기 판단에도 꼭 그만큼 흡족해했다.\n\n\"그 사람은 의심할 여지 없이 깊이 사랑에 빠졌어 — 모든 것이 그것을 말해 주는걸 — 정말이지 푹 빠진 거야! 그리고 그가 다시 오면, 그 연정이 그대로라면, 나는 그것을 부추기지 않도록 경계해야 해. 내 마음은 이미 확고하게 정해졌으니, 달리 행동한다면 그건 가장 변명의 여지 없는 짓이 될 거야. 그렇다고 그 사람이 지금껏 내가 자기를 부추겼다고 여길 수 있으리라는 건 아니야. 아니지, 내가 조금이라도 자기 감정을 함께 나눈다고 믿었더라면, 그토록 비참해하지는 않았을 테니까. 만약 부추김을 받았다고 생각했더라면, 헤어질 때 그의 표정과 말씨가 그렇지 않았을 거야. 그래도 어쨌든 나는 경계해야 해. 이건 그의 연정이 지금 그대로 이어진다는 가정 아래서 하는 말이지. 하지만 정말로 그러리라고 기대하는지는 모르겠어. 그 사람은 영 그런 부류의 남자가 아닌 것 같단 말이야. 나는 그의 한결같음이나 변함없음을 그리 믿지 않아. 그의 감정은 뜨겁지만, 나는 그것이 오히려 변덕스러우리라고 상상할 수 있어. 요컨대, 이 문제를 이리저리 따져 볼수록 내 행복이 더 깊이 얽히지 않은 것이 고마워질 따름이야. 나는 얼마 지나지 않아 다시 멀쩡해질 거야. 그러면 좋은 일 한 가지가 끝난 셈이지. 사람은 누구나 평생에 한 번은 사랑에 빠진다고들 하니, 나는 가볍게 넘어간 셈이 되겠지.\"\n\n그가 웨스턴 부인에게 보낸 편지가 도착하자, 엠마도 그것을 읽어 볼 수 있었다. 그녀는 그 편지를 어찌나 즐겁고 감탄스럽게 읽었던지, 처음에는 자기 감정이 이래도 되나 싶어 고개를 절레절레 젓고, 자기가 그 감정의 강도를 너무 낮잡아 본 게 아닌가 생각할 정도였다. 길고 잘 쓴 편지였다. 여정과 자기 심정을 소상히 적고, 자연스럽고도 떳떳한 온갖 애정과 감사와 존경을 담았으며, 매력적이라 할 만한 바깥 풍경과 고장의 모습을 하나하나 생기 있고 정확하게 그려 냈다. 이제는 사과나 염려를 늘어놓는 미심쩍은 미사여구가 없었다. 그것은 웨스턴 부인을 향한 진실한 감정의 언어였다. 하이베리에서 엔스컴으로 옮겨 가는 변화, 사교 생활의 으뜸가는 축복 몇 가지에서 두 곳이 이루는 대조도, 그것을 얼마나 절절히 느끼는지, 또 예의의 제약만 없었다면 얼마나 더 많은 말을 할 수 있었을지를 보여 줄 만큼만 딱 알맞게 건드려져 있었다. 그녀 자신의 이름이 주는 매력도 빠지지 않았다. '미스 우드하우스'라는 이름이 한 번 이상 등장했고, 언제나 무언가 기분 좋은 연관을 거느리고 있었으니 — 그녀의 안목을 칭찬하거나, 그녀가 했던 말을 떠올리거나 하는 식이었다. 그리고 그 이름이 마지막으로 그녀의 눈에 들어왔을 때, 비록 그런 거창한 찬사의 화환으로 꾸며져 있지는 않았으나, 그녀는 거기서 자기 영향력의 효과를 읽어 낼 수 있었고, 어쩌면 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 큰 찬사가 전해졌음을 인정할 수 있었다. 맨 아래 빈 모서리에 잔뜩 눌러 적은 이런 말이 있었던 것이다. \"아시다시피 화요일에는 미스 우드하우스의 어여쁜 친구분께 짬을 낼 틈이 한 순간도 없었습니다. 부디 그분께 제 변명과 작별 인사를 대신 전해 주십시오.\" 이것이 온전히 자기를 향한 것임을 엠마는 의심할 수 없었다. 해리엇이 기억된 것은 오직 그녀의 친구였기 때문이었다. 엔스컴에 관한 그의 소식과 전망은 예상했던 것보다 나쁘지도 좋지도 않았다. 처칠 부인은 회복 중이었고, 그는 아직 제 머릿속에서조차 랜덜스로 다시 올 날을 감히 못 박지 못하고 있었다.\n\n그러나 그 편지가 알맹이며 정서에서 아무리 흐뭇하고 마음을 들뜨게 했다 해도, 그것을 다시 접어 웨스턴 부인에게 돌려주고 나니, 엠마는 그것이 어떤 오래 가는 온기도 더해 주지 않았음을, 자기는 여전히 그 글쓴이 없이도 잘 지낼 수 있고, 그 또한 자기 없이 지내는 법을 배워야 함을 깨달았다. 그녀의 결심은 변함이 없었다. 거절하겠다는 결의는, 그 뒤에 이어질 그의 위안과 행복을 위한 계획이 보태지면서 한층 흥미로워졌을 뿐이다. 그가 해리엇을 떠올린 것, 그리고 그것을 감싼 \"어여쁜 친구\"라는 말이, 해리엇이 그의 애정에서 자기 뒤를 잇게 한다는 발상을 그녀에게 일깨워 주었다. 그게 불가능한 일일까? 아니지. 해리엇이 이해력에서 그보다 한참 못한 것은 분명했다. 그러나 그는 그녀의 사랑스러운 얼굴과 따뜻하고 꾸밈없는 태도에 무척 마음이 끌렸었고, 처지와 인연을 둘러싼 온갖 정황이 그녀에게 유리했다. 해리엇으로서는 정말이지 이롭고도 더없이 기쁜 일이 될 터였다.\n\n\"여기에 너무 골몰해선 안 돼.\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. \"이런 생각을 해선 안 돼. 이런 억측에 빠지는 것이 얼마나 위험한지 나도 알아. 하지만 이보다 더 이상한 일도 일어나곤 했는걸. 그리고 우리가 지금처럼 서로를 마음에 두지 않게 될 때, 이 일이야말로 내가 벌써부터 즐겁게 내다보고 있는 그런 종류의, 사심 없는 진정한 우정 속에 우리를 굳건히 자리 잡게 해 줄 수단이 될 거야.\"\n\n해리엇을 위해 위안거리 하나를 마련해 두는 것은 좋은 일이었으되, 그 공상은 어쩌다 한 번만 건드리는 것이 현명할지도 몰랐다. 바로 그쪽 방면에서 화근이 코앞에 닥쳐 있었기 때문이다. 하이베리의 화젯거리에서 프랭크 처칠의 도착이 미스터 엘턴의 약혼을 밀어내고 그 자리를 차지했듯이, 나중에 생긴 관심사가 처음 것을 완전히 짓눌렀듯이, 이제 프랭크 처칠이 사라지자 미스터 엘턴에 관한 일이 도무지 거부할 수 없는 형태로 부풀어 오르고 있었다. 그의 혼례 날짜가 정해졌다. 그는 곧 다시 그들 가운데로 돌아올 터였다. 미스터 엘턴과 그의 신부가. 엔스컴에서 온 첫 편지를 두고 이야기를 채 나누기도 전에 \"미스터 엘턴과 그의 신부\"가 누구의 입에나 오르내렸고, 프랭크 처칠은 잊혔다. 엠마는 그 소리에 진저리가 났다. 미스터 엘턴에게서 벗어나 행복하게 보낸 삼 주였다. 그리고 해리엇의 마음도 요즈음 차차 기운을 차렸으리라고 그녀는 기꺼이 바라던 참이었다. 적어도 미스터 웨스턴의 무도회를 눈앞에 두고 있던 동안에는 다른 일들에 무던히 무심해져 있었다. 그러나 이제는 너무도 분명해졌으니, 새 마차며 종소리며 그 모든 것을 거느린 실제의 도래 앞에서 버틸 만큼의 평정에 그녀가 이르지는 못했다는 사실이었다.\n\n가엾은 해리엇은 마음이 어찌나 들떠 안절부절못하던지, 엠마가 베풀 수 있는 온갖 종류의 설득과 위로와 보살핌이 죄다 필요할 지경이었다. 엠마는 그녀를 위해서라면 무엇을 해 줘도 지나치지 않다고, 해리엇에게는 자기의 모든 재간과 모든 인내를 받을 자격이 있다고 느꼈다. 그러나 아무 효과도 거두지 못하면서 끝없이 타이르는 일, 늘 동의를 얻으면서도 두 사람의 생각을 같게 만들지는 못하는 일은 고된 노릇이었다. 해리엇은 고분고분 귀를 기울이며 \"정말 그래요 — 미스 우드하우스가 말씀하신 그대로예요 — 그분들 생각은 할 가치도 없어요 — 이제 다시는 그분들 생각을 하지 않겠어요\" 하고 말했다. 그러나 화제를 돌려 봐야 아무 소용이 없었고, 그다음 반 시간이 지나도록 그녀는 여전히 전과 다름없이 엘턴 부부를 두고 안달하고 안절부절못했다. 마침내 엠마는 다른 쪽에서 그녀를 공략했다.\n\n\"해리엇, 당신이 미스터 엘턴의 결혼 문제로 그토록 마음을 빼앗기고 그토록 불행해하는 것이야말로, 당신이 나한테 할 수 있는 가장 따끔한 책망이에요. 내가 빠졌던 그 잘못에 대해 그보다 더 큰 질책은 없을 거예요. 그게 다 내가 한 일이라는 걸 나도 알아요. 그 일을 잊지 않았어요, 정말이에요. 나 자신이 속고서, 당신을 참으로 비참하게 속였으니 — 그건 내게 두고두고 괴로운 회상이 될 거예요. 내가 그 일을 잊어버릴 우려가 있다고는 생각하지 말아요.\"\n\n해리엇은 이 말이 너무도 사무쳐서, 다급한 탄성 몇 마디밖에 입에 올리지 못했다. 엠마는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"나는 나를 위해 마음을 다잡으라고, 나를 위해 미스터 엘턴 생각을 덜 하고 그 이야기를 덜 하라고 말한 게 아니에요. 차라리 당신 자신을 위해서 그러기를 바라는 거예요. 내 마음 편한 것보다 더 중요한 것을 위해서요 — 당신 안에 자제하는 습관이 들기를, 당신의 도리가 무엇인지 헤아리기를, 예의에 마음을 쓰기를, 남들의 의심을 사지 않으려 애쓰기를, 당신의 건강과 평판을 지키고 마음의 평온을 되찾기를 바라는 거예요. 이런 것들이야말로 내가 당신에게 거듭 일러 온 동기들이에요. 그것들은 무척 중요한 거예요 — 그런데 당신이 그것들을 충분히 절감하고 행동으로 옮기지 못하는 것이 나는 안타까워요. 내가 고통에서 벗어나는 일 따위는 한참 부차적인 문제예요. 나는 당신이 더 큰 고통에서 스스로를 구해 내기를 바라요. 어쩌면 나는 가끔, 해리엇이라면 마땅한 도리를 — 아니, 차라리 내게 친절한 일이 무엇인지를 — 잊지 않으리라고 느꼈는지도 모르겠어요.\"\n\n그녀의 애정에 호소한 이 말이 다른 그 무엇보다도 큰 효력을 발휘했다. 정말로 더없이 사랑하는 미스 우드하우스에게 고마움과 배려가 모자랐다는 생각에 해리엇은 한동안 비참해졌고, 격렬한 슬픔이 다독여져 가라앉은 뒤에도, 그 생각은 옳은 일을 하도록 그녀를 채근하고 그 일을 제법 견딜 만하게 떠받쳐 줄 만큼의 힘을 여전히 지니고 있었다.\n\n\"내 평생 가장 좋은 친구였던 미스 우드하우스한테 고마움이 모자라다니요! 미스 우드하우스만 한 분은 아무도 없는걸요! 저는 미스 우드하우스만큼 아끼는 사람이 세상에 없고요! 아, 미스 우드하우스, 내가 얼마나 배은망덕했던가요!\"\n\n이런 말들이, 표정과 태도가 보탤 수 있는 온갖 것의 도움까지 받으니, 엠마는 자기가 일찍이 해리엇을 이토록 사랑한 적도, 그녀의 애정을 이토록 높이 친 적도 없었다는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n\"마음의 다정함만 한 매력은 없어.\" 그녀는 나중에 혼잣말을 했다. \"거기에 견줄 만한 것은 아무것도 없어. 따뜻하고 다정한 마음에 정겹고 솔직한 태도까지 갖추면, 사람을 끄는 힘에서는 세상 어떤 명석한 머리도 다 이겨 낼 거야. 분명히 그럴 거야. 우리 다정한 아버지가 누구에게나 그토록 사랑받는 것도, 이저벨라가 그 모든 인기를 누리는 것도 다 마음의 다정함 덕분이지. 나한테는 그게 없어 — 하지만 나는 그것을 귀히 여기고 존중할 줄은 알아. 해리엇은 그것이 베푸는 온갖 매력과 온갖 복에서 나보다 한 수 위야. 사랑스러운 해리엇! 나는 살아 숨 쉬는 가장 명석한 머리, 가장 멀리 내다보는 눈, 가장 빈틈없는 판단력을 지닌 여자와도 당신을 바꾸지 않겠어. 아! 제인 페어팩스의 그 차가움이라니! 해리엇은 그런 여자 백 명에 맞먹는 가치가 있어. 그리고 아내로는 — 분별 있는 남자의 아내로는 — 그것이야말로 값을 매길 수 없는 보배지. 이름은 말하지 않겠어. 하지만 엠마를 마다하고 해리엇을 택하는 남자야말로 행복한 남자일 거야!\"\n\n# 제2권 14장\n\n엘턴 부인을 처음 본 것은 교회에서였다. 그러나 신앙은 방해받을지언정, 신도석에 앉은 신부 하나로 호기심이 채워질 리는 없었다. 그녀가 정말로 무척 예쁜지, 그저 그런대로 예쁜지, 아니면 전혀 예쁘지 않은지를 가리는 일은, 이제 곧 격식을 갖추어 치러야 할 방문에 맡겨 둘 수밖에 없었다.\n\n엠마는 호기심이라기보다는 자존심이나 도리에서 우러난 감정이 있어, 인사를 가장 나중에 드리는 일만은 없도록 하겠다고 마음먹었다. 그래서 이 골치 아픈 일을 되도록 빨리 끝내 버리려고, 해리엇을 꼭 데려가기로 작정했다.\n\n그녀로서는 석 달 전 그토록 헛된 꾀를 부려 부츠 끈을 고쳐 매겠다고 물러나 있던 바로 그 방에, 같은 방에 다시 들어서면서 그날 일을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 천 가지 거북한 생각이 되살아났다. 그 찬사들, 셔레이드, 그리고 끔찍한 실수들. 가엾은 해리엇도 그것을 떠올리지 않을 리 없으리라는 것은 짐작이 가고도 남았다. 그러나 해리엇은 아주 의젓하게 굴었고, 다만 좀 창백하고 말이 없을 뿐이었다. 방문은 으레 그렇듯 짧았다. 그것을 짧게 만들 만한 어색함과 심란함이 워낙 컸던 터라, 엠마는 그 부인에 대해 온전한 견해를 세우는 일도 스스로에게 허락하지 않았고, \"우아하게 차려입었고 무척 호감이 간다\"라는 무의미한 말 이상으로는 결코 견해를 내놓지 않기로 했다.\n* 셔레이드(charade): 한 단어를 음절로 나누어 각 음절과 전체를 수수께끼 시로 묘사하던 응접실 놀이.\n\n그녀는 사실 엘턴 부인이 마음에 들지 않았다. 서둘러 흠을 잡고 싶지는 않았으나, 거기엔 우아함이 없다고 의심했다. 편안함은 있되 우아함은 없었다. 젊은 여자, 낯선 사람, 신부 된 처지로는 그 편안함이 지나치게 많다고 거의 확신했다. 몸매는 그런대로 괜찮았고, 얼굴도 못나지는 않았다. 그러나 이목구비도, 분위기도, 목소리도, 몸가짐도 우아하지 않았다. 적어도 결국 그렇게 드러나리라고 엠마는 생각했다.\n\n미스터 엘턴으로 말하자면, 그 몸가짐이 도무지— 아니, 그녀는 그의 몸가짐을 두고 성급하거나 재치 부린 한마디를 스스로에게 허락하지 않으려 했다. 신혼 인사를 받는 일이란 어느 때든 어색한 의례여서, 그것을 훌륭히 치르려면 남자는 우아함으로만 똘똘 뭉쳐야 할 판이었다. 여자는 형편이 나았다. 고운 옷의 도움도, 수줍어할 특권도 누릴 수 있었으니까. 그러나 남자에게는 제 분별 하나뿐이었다. 그리고 가엾은 미스터 엘턴이, 방금 결혼한 여자와, 결혼하고 싶어 했던 여자와, 결혼하리라고 사람들이 점쳤던 여자와 한 방에 동시에 있게 되었으니 얼마나 유난히 운이 사나운 처지인가를 헤아리자, 그녀는 그가 그렇게 영리해 보이지 못하고, 그렇게 어색하게 꾸미고, 그렇게 진정한 편안함이라곤 없어 보이는 것도 마땅한 권리라고 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"저기요, 미스 우드하우스,\" 두 사람이 그 집을 나선 뒤, 벗이 먼저 말을 꺼내기를 헛되이 기다리다가 해리엇이 말했다. \"저기요, 미스 우드하우스,\" (부드러운 한숨과 함께) \"그분을 어떻게 보세요? 정말 매력적이지 않나요?\"\n\n엠마의 대답에는 작은 망설임이 있었다.\n\n\"아아! 그래요— 무척— 무척 호감이 가는 젊은 여인이에요.\"\n\n\"제 눈엔 아름다워 보여요, 정말이지 아름다워요.\"\n\n\"옷차림도 정말 곱더군요. 유난히 우아한 드레스였어요.\"\n\n\"그분이 사랑에 빠지신 게 조금도 놀랍지 않아요.\"\n\n\"아! 그럼요— 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니죠. 어여쁜 재산에다, 그분 앞에 나타났으니까요.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 해리엇이 다시 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"제 생각엔 그분도 그분께 무척 연정을 품으셨던 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르죠. 하지만 자기를 가장 사랑하는 여자와 결혼하는 게 모든 남자의 운명은 아니랍니다. 미스 호킨스는 아마 가정을 갖고 싶었고, 이게 자기가 받을 수 있는 가장 나은 청혼이라고 여겼겠죠.\"\n\n\"맞아요,\" 해리엇이 진지하게 말했다. \"그럴 만도 하죠, 이보다 더 나은 청혼은 누구도 받을 수 없었을 테니까요. 그럼요, 저는 두 분이 행복하기를 진심으로 바라요. 그리고 이제, 미스 우드하우스, 저는 두 분을 다시 봐도 별로 신경 쓰이지 않을 것 같아요. 그분은 여전히 그렇게 훌륭하시지만— 결혼하셨으니, 아시잖아요, 그건 완전히 다른 문제죠. 정말이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 걱정하지 않으셔도 돼요. 이제는 앉아서 그분을 흠모해도 큰 괴로움은 없거든요. 그분이 자기를 함부로 내던지지 않으셨다는 걸 아는 것만으로도 얼마나 위안이 되는지요! 그분은 정말 매력적인 젊은 여인 같고, 딱 그분께 어울리는 분이에요. 행복한 사람! 그분이 그 여자를 '오거스타'라고 부르시던걸요. 어찌나 다정한지요!\"\n\n답방을 받았을 때, 엠마는 마음을 정했다. 그때는 더 많이 보고 더 잘 판단할 수 있었다. 마침 해리엇이 하트필드에 없었고, 아버지가 자리를 지키며 미스터 엘턴을 상대해 준 덕에, 엠마는 그 부인과 단둘이 십오 분쯤 이야기를 나누며 차분히 그녀를 살필 수 있었다. 그 십오 분으로 엠마는 완전히 확신하게 되었다. 엘턴 부인은 허영심 많은 여자로, 자기 자신에 더없이 만족하며 제 무게를 대단히 여기는 사람이라는 것을. 빛나 보이고 무척 우월해 보이고 싶어 하지만, 그 몸가짐은 변변찮은 학교에서 익힌 것이라 건방지고 허물없다는 것을. 그녀의 생각이란 죄다 한 무리의 사람들과 한 가지 생활 양식에서 나온 것이라는 것을. 어리석지는 않다 해도 무지하며, 그녀와의 교제가 미스터 엘턴에게 결코 득이 되지 않으리라는 것을.\n\n해리엇이 더 나은 혼처였을 것이다. 본인이 현명하거나 세련되지는 못하다 해도, 그런 사람들과 미스터 엘턴을 이어 주었으리라. 그러나 미스 호킨스로 말하자면, 그 태평한 자만으로 미루어 보건대, 제 무리 가운데서는 가장 나은 축이었으리라 짐작해도 좋았다. 브리스틀 근처에 사는 부유한 형부가 이 혼맥의 자랑거리였고, 그 형부의 영지와 마차가 그 사람의 자랑거리였다.\n\n자리에 앉자마자 첫 화제는 메이플 그로브, \"우리 형부 미스터 서클링의 영지\"였다. 하트필드를 메이플 그로브에 견주는 비교였다. 하트필드의 정원은 작아도 단정하고 어여뻤으며, 집은 현대식으로 잘 지어진 것이었다. 엘턴 부인은 방의 크기와 현관과, 보거나 상상할 수 있는 온갖 것에 더없이 호의적인 인상을 받은 듯했다. \"정말이지 메이플 그로브와 꼭 닮았네요! 그 닮은 모습에 정말 놀랐어요! 이 방은 메이플 그로브의 아침 거실과 모양도 크기도 똑같네요. 우리 언니가 가장 좋아하는 방이죠.\" 미스터 엘턴에게 동의를 구하는 말이 이어졌다. \"놀랄 만큼 닮지 않았어요? 정말 메이플 그로브에 와 있는 것 같은 착각이 들 정도예요.\"\n\n\"그리고 저 계단도— 아시겠지만, 들어오면서 보니 계단이 어찌나 똑 닮았던지요. 집의 정확히 같은 자리에 놓여 있어요. 정말 탄성을 지르지 않을 수 없었답니다! 정말이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 제가 그토록 각별히 아끼는 곳, 메이플 그로브를 떠올리게 해 주니 저로서는 무척 기쁜 일이에요. 그곳에서 그토록 행복한 여러 달을 보냈으니까요!\" (가벼운 감상의 한숨과 함께) \"두말할 것 없이 매력적인 곳이죠. 그곳을 본 사람은 누구나 그 아름다움에 감탄해요. 하지만 제게는 그곳이 그야말로 한 채의 집이었어요. 미스 우드하우스, 당신도 저처럼 옮겨 심긴 처지가 되면, 떠나온 곳과 조금이라도 닮은 무언가를 만나는 일이 얼마나 기쁜지 아시게 될 거예요. 저는 늘 이게 결혼의 폐단 가운데 하나라고 말하곤 해요.\"\n\n엠마는 되도록 짤막하게 대꾸했다. 그러나 그저 제 입으로 떠들기만을 바라던 엘턴 부인에게는 그것으로 충분하고도 남았다.\n\n\"메이플 그로브와 어찌나 닮았는지요! 그것도 집만이 아니에요. 정원도, 정말이지, 제가 본 한에서는 놀랄 만큼 닮았어요. 메이플 그로브의 월계수도 여기처럼 우거져 있고, 놓인 모양새도 아주 비슷하답니다— 잔디밭 바로 건너편에요. 그리고 둘레에 벤치를 두른 멋지고 커다란 나무가 언뜻 보였는데, 어찌나 똑같이 떠오르게 하던지요! 우리 형부 부부도 이곳에 홀딱 반할 거예요. 자기 정원이 너른 사람들은 같은 양식이라면 무엇에든 기뻐하는 법이니까요.\"\n\n엠마는 그 말이 진실인지 의심스러웠다. 자기 정원이 너른 사람들은 남의 너른 정원에는 거의 관심이 없다는 것이 그녀의 확고한 생각이었다. 그러나 그토록 단단히 굳은 오류를 굳이 들이받을 가치는 없었던지라, 그저 이렇게만 대답했다.\n\n\"이 고장을 좀 더 보시고 나면, 하트필드를 너무 높이 평가하셨다고 여기실까 봐 걱정이에요. 서리 주에는 아름다운 곳이 가득하거든요.\"\n\n\"아! 그럼요, 저도 잘 알고 있어요. 거긴 영국의 정원이잖아요, 아시다시피. 서리는 영국의 정원이죠.\"\n\n\"그렇긴 해요. 하지만 우리가 그 명성에만 기대선 안 되겠죠. 제가 알기로는 서리 말고도 많은 주가 영국의 정원이라고 불리거든요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그럴 리가요.\" 엘턴 부인이 더없이 흡족한 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다. \"저는 서리 말고 다른 주를 그렇게 부르는 건 한 번도 못 들어 봤어요.\"\n\n엠마는 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"우리 형부 부부가 봄에, 늦어도 여름에는 와 주겠다고 약속했어요.\" 엘턴 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그때가 우리 둘러볼 시기가 되겠죠. 그분들이 우리와 함께 있는 동안 정말 여기저기 많이 둘러보게 될 거예요. 물론 그분들은 바루시-랜도를 가져올 테고, 그건 넷이 거뜬히 탈 수 있으니까요. 그러니 우리 마차는 말할 것도 없이, 이 고장의 여러 아름다운 곳을 아주 멋지게 둘러볼 수 있을 거예요. 그 계절에 설마 그분들이 이륜마차로 오시진 않겠죠. 정말이지 그때가 다가오면, 저는 단연코 바루시-랜도를 가져오시라고 권할 거예요. 그게 훨씬 나으니까요. 이렇게 아름다운 고장에 온 사람들에게는, 아시다시피 미스 우드하우스, 되도록 많은 곳을 보여 주고 싶은 게 인지상정이잖아요. 그리고 미스터 서클링은 둘러보는 걸 무척 좋아하세요. 지난여름에는 그 마차로 킹스웨스턴까지 두 번이나 더없이 즐겁게 둘러보았답니다, 바루시-랜도를 처음 들이고 막 얼마 안 됐을 때요. 미스 우드하우스, 이곳에서도 여름마다 그런 나들이가 많으시겠죠?\"\n* 바루시-랜도(barouche-landau): 접이식 덮개를 갖춘 사륜 호화 마차. 부의 과시 수단이었다.\n\n\"아니에요. 적어도 이 근방에서는요. 우리는 당신이 말씀하시는 그런 나들이를 부르는, 아주 눈에 띄는 명소들과는 거리가 좀 먼 편이에요. 그리고 우리는 아주 조용한 사람들이라서요, 아마도요. 놀이 계획을 꾸미기보다는 집에 머무는 쪽에 더 기우는 편이죠.\"\n\n\"아! 진짜 안락함으로 치면 집에 머무는 것만 한 게 없죠. 저만큼 집에 헌신하는 사람도 없을 거예요. 메이플 그로브에서는 그걸로 거의 소문난 사람이었어요. 셀리나 언니가 브리스틀로 나가려 할 때면 몇 번이나 이렇게 말했는지 몰라요. '이 애를 집에서 끌어낼 수가 도무지 없네. 동행 없이 바루시-랜도에 꼼짝없이 갇혀 가는 건 질색이지만, 어쩔 수 없이 나 혼자라도 가야겠어. 오거스타는 제 마음대로 두면 정원 울타리 밖으로는 한 발짝도 안 나설걸.' 언니가 몇 번이나 그렇게 말했답니다. 그래도 저는 완전한 칩거를 옹호하는 사람은 아니에요. 오히려 그 반대로, 사람이 사교에서 완전히 자기를 가둬 버리는 건 아주 나쁜 일이라고 생각해요. 너무 많지도 너무 적지도 않게, 적당한 정도로 세상과 어울리는 편이 훨씬 바람직하죠. 하지만 미스 우드하우스, 당신 처지는 제가 잘 이해해요.\" (미스터 우드하우스 쪽을 바라보며) \"아버님의 건강 상태가 큰 걸림돌이겠죠. 어째서 바스로 가 보지 않으세요? 정말 그러셔야 해요. 바스를 권해 드릴게요. 정말이지 그게 미스터 우드하우스께 좋으리라는 건 의심할 여지가 없답니다.\"\n\n\"아버지는 예전에 한두 번 가 보셨어요. 하지만 아무 효험도 못 보셨죠. 그리고 미스터 페리도, 그 이름을 아마 모르진 않으시겠지만, 지금이라고 더 도움이 되리라고는 전혀 생각하지 않아요.\"\n\n\"아! 그건 참 안타까운 일이네요. 정말이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 그 물이 몸에 맞는 사람한테는 어찌나 놀라운 효험을 주는지 몰라요. 제가 바스에서 지내는 동안 그런 사례를 얼마나 많이 봤다고요! 게다가 그렇게 활기찬 곳이라, 미스터 우드하우스의 기분에도 분명 도움이 될 거예요. 가끔 무척 가라앉으신다고 들었거든요. 그리고 당신께 좋은 점으로 말하자면, 굳이 애써 늘어놓을 필요도 없을 것 같아요. 젊은 사람에게 바스가 얼마나 이로운지는 대체로 다 아는 일이니까요. 그렇게 외진 생활을 해 온 당신에게는 더없이 매력적인 사교계 입문이 될 거예요. 제가 그곳의 가장 좋은 모임 몇 군데에 당장 자리를 마련해 드릴 수 있고요. 제가 편지 한 줄만 보내면 아는 사람들이 떼로 몰려올 거예요. 그리고 제 각별한 벗인 파트리지 부인은, 제가 바스에 머물 때면 늘 함께 지내던 분인데, 당신께 온갖 친절을 베풀어 주기를 무척 기뻐할 테고, 당신이 사교계에 나설 때 함께 다니기에 그야말로 딱 맞는 분이랍니다.\"\n\n엠마가 무례해지지 않고 견딜 수 있는 한계가 거기까지였다. 자기가 이른바 사교계 입문이라는 것을 엘턴 부인에게 신세 진다는 생각— 엘턴 부인의 벗이라는 사람의 비호 아래 사교계에 나선다는 생각— 그 벗이라는 게 아마도 하숙인을 받아 겨우 입에 풀칠이나 하는, 어떤 천박하고 요란한 과부일 텐데! 하트필드의 미스 우드하우스의 위엄이 그야말로 땅에 떨어진 격이었다!\n\n그러나 엠마는 던질 수 있었던 온갖 꾸지람을 꾹 참고, 그저 엘턴 부인에게 쌀쌀맞게 고맙다고만 했다. \"하지만 우리가 바스에 가는 일은 도무지 있을 수 없고요. 저는 그곳이 아버지보다 제게 더 맞으리라고도 그다지 확신하지 못하겠네요.\" 그러고는 더 큰 모욕과 분노를 막으려고 곧장 화제를 돌렸다.\n\n\"엘턴 부인, 음악을 하시는지는 여쭙지 않을게요. 이런 경우에는 대개 그분의 평판이 본인보다 먼저 닿게 마련이니까요. 하이베리에서는 당신이 뛰어난 연주자라는 걸 진작부터 알고 있었답니다.\"\n\n\"아! 아니에요, 정말로요. 그런 생각엔 한사코 반대해야겠어요. 뛰어난 연주자라니요! 정말이지 어림도 없는 말씀이에요. 그 정보가 얼마나 편파적인 데서 나온 것인지 생각해 보세요. 저는 음악을 끔찍이 좋아해요— 열렬히 좋아하죠. 제 벗들도 제게 안목이 아예 없지는 않다고 말하긴 해요. 하지만 그 밖의 것으로 말하면, 정말이지 제 연주는 더할 나위 없이 그저 그런 수준이랍니다. 미스 우드하우스, 당신이야말로 멋지게 연주하신다는 걸 저는 잘 알아요. 정말이지 제가 이렇게 음악을 즐기는 모임에 들어오게 됐다는 걸 알게 된 것이 더없는 만족이요, 위안이요, 기쁨이었어요. 저는 음악 없이는 도무지 못 살거든요. 제겐 음악이 삶의 필수품이에요. 메이플 그로브에서나 바스에서나 늘 무척 음악을 즐기는 모임에 익숙해져 있던 터라, 음악이 없었다면 그야말로 심각한 희생이 됐을 거예요. 미스터 엘턴이 제 앞날의 집 이야기를 하면서, 그 외진 데가 마음에 안 들까 봐 염려하실 때, 저는 그분께 솔직히 그렇게 말했답니다. 게다가 집도 변변찮으니— 제가 어떤 데 익숙해져 있었는지 아시니까— 당연히 그분도 영 걱정이 없진 않으셨죠. 그분이 그런 식으로 말씀하실 때, 저는 솔직히 말씀드렸어요. 세상이야 저는 단념할 수 있다고요— 파티든 무도회든 연극이든— 외진 생활은 조금도 두렵지 않으니까요. 제 안에 워낙 많은 자산을 지니고 있어서, 저한테는 세상이 꼭 필요한 게 아니거든요. 그런 거 없이도 아주 잘 지낼 수 있어요. 자기 안에 아무 자산이 없는 사람에게야 다른 문제겠지만, 제 자산은 저를 아주 독립적으로 만들어 주니까요. 그리고 익숙했던 것보다 작은 방으로 말하자면, 정말이지 한 번도 마음에 둔 적 없어요. 그런 종류의 희생이라면 무엇이든 너끈히 감당할 수 있다고 믿었거든요. 물론 메이플 그로브에서는 온갖 호사에 익숙해져 있었죠. 하지만 저는 그분께 분명히 말씀드렸어요. 제 행복에 마차 두 대가 꼭 필요한 것도, 널찍한 방이 꼭 필요한 것도 아니라고요. '하지만,' 제가 말했죠. '아주 솔직히 말하면, 음악을 즐기는 모임 비슷한 거라도 없으면 저는 못 살 것 같아요. 다른 건 아무것도 조건으로 걸지 않겠어요. 하지만 음악이 없으면 제 삶은 텅 빈 백지가 될 거예요.'\"\n\n\"미스터 엘턴이라면 하이베리에 무척 음악을 즐기는 모임이 있노라고 망설임 없이 장담하셨으리라고 봐야겠죠.\" 엠마가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그리고 그 동기를 헤아려 너그러이 봐줄 수 있는 정도 이상으로는 사실을 부풀리지 않으셨기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 정말로 그 점은 조금도 의심하지 않아요. 이런 모임에 들게 되어 저는 무척 기쁘답니다. 우리가 함께 자그마한 음악회를 자주 열게 되기를 바라요. 미스 우드하우스, 제 생각엔 당신과 제가 음악회 모임을 하나 만들어서, 당신 댁이나 우리 집에서 매주 정기적으로 모이면 좋겠어요. 괜찮은 계획 아닌가요? 우리가 힘을 좀 내면, 동지를 구하는 데 오래 걸리지 않을 거예요. 그런 종류의 일이라면 저한테는 특히 바람직하겠어요. 제가 연습을 게을리하지 않도록 부추기는 자극이 될 테니까요. 결혼한 여자들로 말하면, 아시다시피— 대체로 그들을 두고 도는 서글픈 이야기가 있잖아요. 음악을 너무도 쉽게 놓아 버리거든요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 음악을 그토록 끔찍이 좋아하시는데— 설마 그럴 위험이야 없겠죠?\"\n\n\"그러지 않기를 바라요. 하지만 정말이지 제 아는 사람들을 둘러보면 몸서리가 쳐진답니다. 셀리나 언니는 음악을 완전히 놓아 버렸어요— 악기에 손도 안 대요— 그렇게 곱게 연주했는데도요. 제프리스 부인도— 결혼 전엔 클라라 파트리지였죠— 마찬가지고요. 두 밀먼 자매, 지금의 버드 부인과 제임스 쿠퍼 부인도 그렇고요. 일일이 다 헤아릴 수도 없을 만큼 많아요. 정말이지 사람을 겁먹게 하기에 충분하죠. 저는 셀리나 언니한테 단단히 화가 나곤 했어요. 하지만 이제는 정말로 알 것 같아요, 결혼한 여자한테는 신경 써야 할 일이 한두 가지가 아니라는 걸요. 오늘 아침에도 저는 가정부와 반 시간이나 틀어박혀 있었던 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그런 종류의 일은 다,\" 엠마가 말했다. \"곧 아주 규칙적인 궤도에 오를 테니—\"\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 엘턴 부인이 웃으며 말했다. \"두고 봐야죠.\"\n\n엠마는 그녀가 그렇게나 단단히 음악을 등한히 하기로 작정한 것을 알고 더는 할 말이 없었다. 잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 엘턴 부인이 또 다른 화제를 골랐다.\n\n\"우리가 랜덜스에 다녀왔어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"두 분 다 댁에 계셨는데, 무척 유쾌한 분들 같더군요. 저는 두 분이 아주 마음에 들어요. 미스터 웨스턴은 훌륭한 분 같고요— 정말이지 벌써 제일 마음에 드는 분이 되셨답니다. 그리고 부인 쪽은 어찌나 진실로 선해 보이는지요— 어딘가 모성적이고 마음씨 따뜻한 데가 있어서, 단번에 사람의 마음을 사로잡거든요. 그분이 당신의 가정교사셨다죠?\"\n\n엠마는 거의 너무 놀라 대답이 안 나올 지경이었다. 그러나 엘턴 부인은 그렇다는 대답을 기다릴 새도 없이 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그렇게 들었던 터라, 그분이 그렇게나 귀부인다운 걸 보고 저는 좀 놀랐답니다! 하지만 정말이지 완전한 숙녀시더군요.\"\n\n\"웨스턴 부인의 몸가짐은,\" 엠마가 말했다. \"언제나 유난히 훌륭했어요. 그 단정함과 꾸밈없음과 우아함은 어떤 젊은 여자에게든 더없이 안전한 본보기가 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런데 우리가 거기 있는 동안 누가 들어왔는지 아세요?\"\n\n엠마는 도무지 종잡을 수가 없었다. 그 말투는 어떤 오랜 지인을 암시하는 듯했다— 그런데 그녀가 어떻게 짐작할 수 있겠는가?\n\n\"나이틀리요!\" 엘턴 부인이 말을 이었다. \"나이틀리 본인이요! 운이 좋지 않았어요? 요전번에 그분이 찾아오셨을 때 제가 집에 없었던 터라, 그전엔 한 번도 못 뵈었거든요. 게다가 미스터 엘턴의 그렇게 각별한 벗이시니, 당연히 무척 궁금했죠. '내 친구 나이틀리' 이야기가 어찌나 자주 나왔던지, 정말 보고 싶어서 안달이 날 지경이었답니다. 그리고 제 카로 스포소 명예를 위해 한마디 해야겠는데, 그분은 친구를 두고 부끄러워할 필요가 전혀 없으시더군요. 나이틀리는 완전한 신사세요. 저는 그분이 무척 마음에 들어요. 단연코, 제 생각엔, 아주 신사다운 분이에요.\"\n* 카로 스포소(caro sposo): '사랑하는 남편'이라는 뜻의 이탈리아어. 엘턴 부인이 멋을 부리려 자주 쓰는 말이다.\n\n다행히도 이제는 일어설 시간이었다. 그들은 떠났고, 엠마는 한숨 돌릴 수 있었다.\n\n\"못 견딜 여자야!\" 그녀의 첫 탄성이 그것이었다. \"짐작했던 것보다 더해. 도무지 못 견디겠어! 나이틀리라니! 도저히 믿을 수가 없네. 나이틀리라니! 평생 한 번도 본 적 없으면서, 그분을 나이틀리라고 부르다니! 게다가 그분이 신사라는 걸 발견하다니! 자기 미스터 엘턴이니, 자기 카로 스포소니, 자기 자산이니, 그 온갖 건방진 허세와 천한 출신의 겉치레를 부려 대는 한낱 벼락출세한 천박한 인간이! 글쎄, 미스터 나이틀리가 신사라는 걸 발견했다는 거야! 그분이 그 답례로 그 여자가 숙녀라는 걸 발견해 주실지는 의심스럽군. 도저히 믿을 수가 없어! 게다가 자기랑 내가 손잡고 음악회 모임을 만들자고 제안하다니! 누가 보면 우리가 절친한 벗인 줄 알겠어! 그리고 웨스턴 부인 일은! 나를 길러 준 사람이 숙녀라는 데 놀랐다니! 점입가경이군. 그런 사람은 평생 처음 봐. 내 예상을 훨씬 넘어서네. 해리엇은 견주는 것만으로도 욕보는 거야. 아아! 프랭크 처칠이 여기 있다면 그 여자를 두고 뭐라고 할까? 얼마나 화를 내면서도 또 얼마나 재미있어할까! 아아! 또 이러네— 곧장 그 사람 생각을 하고 있잖아. 늘 제일 먼저 떠오르는 사람! 내가 또 나를 들켜 버렸어! 프랭크 처칠은 어쩌면 이렇게 어김없이 떠오르는지!\"\n\n이 모든 생각이 어찌나 술술 흘러갔던지, 엘턴 부부가 떠나며 일으킨 부산함 끝에 아버지가 자세를 추스르고 막 입을 떼려 할 즈음에는, 엠마는 그런대로 귀 기울일 만한 상태가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"음, 얘야,\" 아버지가 천천히 말을 꺼냈다. \"전에 한 번도 본 적이 없던 사람치고는, 무척 어여쁜 젊은 부인 같더구나. 그리고 분명 너를 무척 마음에 들어 했을 게다. 말이 조금 너무 빠르긴 해. 귀에 좀 거슬리는, 약간 빠른 목소리가 있더구나. 하지만 내가 까다로운 거겠지. 나는 낯선 목소리는 영 좋아하지 않거든. 게다가 너나 가엾은 미스 테일러처럼 말하는 사람은 없으니까. 그래도 무척 친절하고 점잖게 처신하는 젊은 부인 같으니, 분명 그 사람한테 아주 좋은 아내가 될 게다. 그래도 내 생각엔 그 사람이 결혼하지 않는 편이 나았을 텐데. 이 기쁜 날에 그 사람과 엘턴 부인을 찾아뵙지 못한 것에 대해 최선을 다해 양해를 구했단다. 여름이 가기 전에는 꼭 찾아뵙고 싶다고 말했지. 하지만 진작 갔어야 했어. 신부를 찾아뵙지 않는 건 아주 무례한 일이거든. 아아! 이게 다 내가 한심한 병자라는 걸 보여 주는 게지! 하지만 나는 그 비카리지 골목으로 꺾어 드는 모퉁이가 영 싫구나.\"\n\n\"분명 아버지의 양해는 받아들여졌을 거예요. 미스터 엘턴은 아버지를 잘 아니까요.\"\n\n\"그래. 하지만 젊은 부인— 신부인데— 되도록 인사를 드렸어야 했어. 그러지 못한 건 큰 결례였지.\"\n\n\"하지만 아버지, 아버지는 결혼을 달가워하지 않으시잖아요. 그런데 어째서 신부에게 인사 드리는 일을 그렇게 안달하세요? 아버지한테는 그게 추천할 만한 일이 못 되어야죠. 신부를 그렇게 떠받들면 사람들한테 결혼을 부추기는 셈이잖아요.\"\n\n\"아니다, 얘야, 나는 누구한테도 결혼을 부추긴 적이 없어. 다만 부인에게는 늘 마땅한 예를 다 갖추고 싶을 뿐이지— 그중에서도 신부는 결코 소홀히 해서는 안 되는 거란다. 신부에게는 더 많은 예가 마땅히 돌아가야 하는 법이거든. 신부란, 얘야, 알다시피 다른 사람들이 누구든 간에 늘 자리에서 으뜸인 법이란다.\"\n\n\"그것 보세요, 아버지, 그게 결혼을 부추기는 게 아니면 뭐가 부추기는 건지 저는 모르겠네요. 그리고 아버지가 가엾은 젊은 아가씨들한테 던지는 그런 허영의 미끼에 손을 들어 주실 줄은 정말 몰랐어요.\"\n\n\"얘야, 너는 내 말을 못 알아듣는구나. 이건 그저 평범한 예의와 좋은 가풍의 문제일 뿐, 사람들에게 결혼을 부추기는 일과는 아무 상관도 없는 거란다.\"\n\n엠마는 그만두었다. 아버지가 점점 불안해하며 자기 말을 알아듣지 못했다. 그녀의 마음은 다시 엘턴 부인의 무례함으로 돌아갔고, 그 생각이 오래, 아주 오래도록 그녀를 사로잡았다.\n\n# 제2권 15장\n\n엠마는 그 뒤 어떤 새로운 사실을 알게 되었다 한들, 엘턴 부인에 대한 자신의 박한 평가를 거두어들일 필요는 없었다. 그녀의 관찰은 꽤 정확했던 것이다. 이 두 번째 만남에서 엠마에게 비친 엘턴 부인의 모습은, 그 후 다시 만날 때마다 한결같았다 — 자만에 차 있고, 주제넘으며, 무람없고, 무지하며, 천박했다. 약간의 미모와 약간의 교양은 있었으나 분별이 너무도 없어, 자신이 세상에 대한 우월한 식견을 지니고 와서 어느 시골 동네에 생기를 불어넣고 그곳을 개량해 주는 것이라 여겼고, 미스 호킨스가 차지했던 사교계의 자리란 오직 엘턴 부인이라는 신분으로만 능가할 수 있는 것이라 자부했다.\n\n미스터 엘턴이 그 아내와 조금이라도 다르게 생각한다고 볼 근거는 전혀 없었다. 그는 그저 아내에게 만족하는 정도가 아니라 자랑스러워하는 듯했다. 미스 우드하우스조차 견줄 수 없을 만한 여인을 하이베리에 데려왔노라 자축하는 기색이 역력했다. 그리고 그녀의 새 지인 대부분은 칭찬하는 쪽으로 기울었거나 평가하는 습관이 없는 사람들이라, 미스 베이츠의 호의를 따라가거나 신부가 스스로 내세우는 만큼 영리하고 상냥한 사람일 게 분명하다고 미루어 짐작하며 무척 흡족해했다. 그리하여 엘턴 부인에 대한 칭찬은 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로 입에서 입으로 옮아갔고, 미스 우드하우스는 그 흐름을 막지 않았다. 오히려 처음에 보탰던 평을 선뜻 이어 가며 \"참 상냥하고 차림새가 무척 우아한 분\"이라고 점잖게 말해 주었다.\n\n한 가지 점에서 엘턴 부인은 처음 보였던 것보다도 한층 더 나빠졌다. 엠마를 향한 감정이 달라진 것이다. 친밀해지려는 자신의 제안이 별 호응을 얻지 못한 데 기분이 상했던지, 이번에는 엘턴 부인 쪽에서 물러서더니 차츰 훨씬 차갑고 데면데면해졌다. 그 결과는 엠마에게 반가운 것이었으나, 그것을 빚어낸 적의는 엠마의 반감을 키울 수밖에 없었다. 게다가 엘턴 부인의 — 그리고 미스터 엘턴의 — 해리엇을 대하는 태도는 불쾌했다. 비웃는 듯하고 함부로 대하는 투였다. 엠마는 이것이 해리엇을 어서 낫게 하는 데 도움이 되기를 바랐으나, 그런 행동을 부추길 만한 심보를 떠올리면 두 사람이 한없이 천하게만 보였다. 가엾은 해리엇의 연정이 부부간의 거리낌 없는 속내 털어놓기의 제물이 되었으리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었고, 그 이야기 속에서 엠마 자신이 맡은 역할도 — 그녀에게는 가장 불리하게, 그에게는 가장 위안이 되게 색칠된 채로 — 십중팔구 함께 털어놓아졌을 터였다. 엠마는 당연히 두 사람이 함께 미워하는 대상이었다. 달리 할 말이 없을 때면 미스 우드하우스를 헐뜯기 시작하는 것은 언제나 손쉬운 일이었을 테고, 그녀에게 드러내 놓고 무례하게 보일 엄두는 못 내는 적의는 해리엇을 멸시하는 처사로 더 넓은 분풀이의 출구를 찾았다.\n\n엘턴 부인은 제인 페어팩스를 무척 마음에 들어 했다. 그것도 처음부터였다. 한 아가씨와 전쟁 상태에 들어서면 다른 아가씨를 추어올리게 마련이라고 짐작할 만한 시점부터가 아니라, 그야말로 맨 처음부터였다. 게다가 자연스럽고 합당한 찬탄을 표하는 데 그치지 않고 — 누가 청하지도, 사정하지도, 그럴 자격을 주지도 않았는데 — 그녀를 돕고 그녀의 벗이 되어 주려 안달이었다. 엠마가 그 신임을 잃기 전, 세 번째쯤 만났을 무렵에 엠마는 이 문제에 관한 엘턴 부인의 의협심 넘치는 포부를 죄다 듣게 되었다.\n\n\"제인 페어팩스는 정말이지 매력적이에요, 미스 우드하우스. 저는 제인 페어팩스 이야기라면 아주 열을 올린답니다. 사랑스럽고 흥미로운 사람이에요. 어찌나 온화하고 숙녀다운지 — 게다가 그런 재능까지요! 정말이지 저는 그분이 비범한 재능을 지녔다고 봐요. 연주가 더없이 훌륭하다는 말도 서슴없이 하겠어요. 음악이라면 저도 그 점을 단정 지어 말할 만큼은 아니까요. 아유! 정말 매력적인 사람이에요! 제 열성을 보고 웃으시겠지만 — 정말이지, 제 입에서는 제인 페어팩스 이야기밖에 안 나온다니까요. 게다가 그분 처지가 어찌나 사람 마음을 움직이는지! 미스 우드하우스, 우리가 발 벗고 나서서 그분을 위해 무언가 해 줘야 해요. 그분을 끌어내 줘야지요. 그만한 재능이 세상에 알려지지 않은 채 묻혀선 안 되는 거예요. 시인의 그 아름다운 시구를 들어 보셨을 거예요.\n\n'숱한 꽃이 아무도 보지 않는 데서 피어나\n    그 향기를 황량한 허공에 헛되이 흩뿌리도다.'\n\n사랑스러운 제인 페어팩스에게 이 시구가 들어맞게 두어선 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그럴 위험이 있다고는 도무지 생각되지 않는데요.\" 엠마는 차분하게 대답했다. \"그리고 미스 페어팩스의 처지를 좀 더 잘 아시고 그분이 캠벨 대령 부부와 어떤 가정을 이루며 지냈는지 이해하시게 되면, 그분의 재능이 알려지지 않은 채로 있으리라고는 생각지 않으시게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"아유! 하지만 친애하는 미스 우드하우스, 그분은 지금 그렇게 외진 곳에, 그렇게 묻혀서, 그렇게 내버려진 처지잖아요. 캠벨 댁에서 누렸던 이점이 무엇이었든 이제는 너무나 뻔하게 끝나 버렸어요! 그리고 제 생각엔 그분도 그걸 느끼고 있어요. 분명 그래요. 무척 수줍어하고 말이 없거든요. 격려가 아쉽다는 걸 느끼는 게 보여요. 저는 그 점 때문에 그분이 더 좋아요. 솔직히 말하면, 그게 제겐 호감이 가는 점이에요. 저는 수줍음을 아주 높이 산답니다 — 게다가 그런 사람은 정말 흔히 만나기 어렵잖아요. 그래도 처지가 좀 낮은 사람에게서 보이는 수줍음은 더없이 마음을 끄는 법이지요. 아유! 정말이지, 제인 페어팩스는 무척 사랑스러운 사람이고, 말로 다 못 할 만큼 제 관심을 끈답니다.\"\n\n\"부인께서 마음을 많이 쓰시는 것 같네요. 그렇지만 부인이든, 여기 있는 미스 페어팩스의 다른 지인이든, 그러니까 부인보다 그분을 더 오래 알아 온 사람들이든, 그분에게 어떤 다른 배려를 베풀 수 있을지는 잘 모르겠어요 —\"\n\n\"친애하는 미스 우드하우스, 과감히 나설 용기가 있는 사람이라면 굉장히 많은 일을 할 수 있어요. 미스 우드하우스와 저는 두려워할 게 없잖아요. 우리가 본보기를 보이면, 비록 모두가 우리 같은 처지는 아니라도 많은 이가 제 형편 닿는 데까지 따라올 거예요. 우리에게는 그분을 데려오고 집까지 모셔다 드릴 마차가 있고, 우리 살림은 언제든 제인 페어팩스 한 사람쯤 더해진다 해서 조금도 거추장스러울 게 없을 만한 규모니까요. 라이트가 저녁상을 차려 올렸는데 제인 페어팩스 말고 사람을 더 청한 걸 후회하게 만들 정도라면, 저는 무척 언짢을 거예요. 저는 그런 건 상상도 못 해요. 제가 익숙하게 살아온 걸 생각하면 그럴 리도 없고요. 살림에서 제가 빠지기 쉬운 가장 큰 위험은 어쩌면 정반대 쪽일지도 몰라요. 너무 많이 차리고 비용에 너무 무심한 쪽 말이에요. 어쩌면 마땅한 정도 이상으로 메이플 그로브가 제 본보기가 될지도 모르겠어요 — 그렇다고 우리가 수입 면에서 우리 형부 미스터 서클링과 어깨를 나란히 하는 척하는 건 결코 아니지만요. 어쨌든 제인 페어팩스에게 마음을 써 주기로 제 결심은 섰어요. 분명 자주 우리 집에 그분을 모실 거고, 어디든 기회만 닿으면 소개하고, 음악 모임을 열어 그분의 재능을 끌어내고, 알맞은 자리가 없나 늘 살필 거예요. 제 교제 범위가 워낙 넓어서, 머잖아 그분에게 맞는 무언가 소식을 듣게 될 거라고 거의 의심치 않아요. 그리고 물론 형부 부부가 우리 집에 오면 각별히 그분을 소개할 거예요. 분명 그분을 무척 좋아하실 거고요. 그분도 조금만 친해지면 두려움이 말끔히 가실 거예요. 두 분 다 더없이 사람을 편안하게 해 주는 분들이라, 그 태도에는 정말이지 그런 것밖에 없으니까요. 두 분이 와 계시는 동안엔 정말 자주 그분을 모실 거고, 단언컨대 우리가 이런저런 유람을 나갈 때면 가끔은 바루시-랜도에 그분 자리도 마련하게 될 거예요.\"\n* 바루시-랜도(barouche-landau): 두 가지 형태로 접을 수 있는 호화로운 사두마차.\n\n\"가엾은 제인 페어팩스!\" 엠마는 속으로 생각했다. \"당신은 이런 대접을 받을 까닭이 없어. 미스터 딕슨 일로 잘못을 저질렀을지는 몰라도, 이건 당신이 받아 마땅한 벌을 넘어서는 거야! 엘턴 부인의 친절과 비호라니! '제인 페어팩스, 또 제인 페어팩스.' 세상에! 저 여자가 설마 여기저기 돌아다니며 나를 '엠마 우드하우스, 엠마 우드하우스' 하고 불러 댄다고는 생각하지 말자! 하지만 정말이지, 저 여자의 방종한 혀에는 한계라는 게 없는 것 같구나!\"\n\n엠마는 다시는 그런 장광설을 들을 일이 없었다 — 오롯이 자기만을 향한, \"친애하는 미스 우드하우스\"로 역겹게 치장된 그런 말을 말이다. 그 직후 엘턴 부인 쪽에서 변화가 나타났고, 엠마는 평온하게 내버려졌다 — 엘턴 부인의 각별한 벗이 되도록 강요당하지도 않았고, 엘턴 부인의 지휘 아래 제인 페어팩스의 더없이 적극적인 후원자가 되어야 할 일도 없었다. 그저 다른 사람들과 더불어 두루뭉술하게, 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알게 되는 정도만 함께할 뿐이었다.\n\n엠마는 다소 흥미롭게 그 모든 것을 지켜보았다. 제인에 대한 엘턴 부인의 배려에 미스 베이츠가 보이는 고마움은, 더없이 순진무구하고 진심 어린 첫째가는 본보기였다. 엘턴 부인은 미스 베이츠가 떠받드는 인물 가운데 하나였으니 — 더없이 상냥하고, 더없이 붙임성 있고, 더없이 유쾌한 여인이었고 — 엘턴 부인이 그렇게 여겨지기를 바라던 바로 그만큼 교양 있고 너그러운 사람이었다. 엠마가 유일하게 놀란 점은, 제인 페어팩스가 그런 배려를 받아들이고 엘턴 부인을 그처럼 견뎌 내는 듯하다는 사실이었다. 제인이 엘턴 부부와 산책을 하고, 엘턴 부부와 함께 앉아 있고, 엘턴 부부와 하루를 보냈다는 소식이 들려왔다! 놀라운 일이었다! 미스 페어팩스의 취향이나 자존심이 목사관이 베풀 수 있는 그런 부류의 교제와 우정을 견딜 수 있으리라고는 도무지 믿기지 않았다.\n\n\"그분은 수수께끼야, 정말 수수께끼라니까!\" 엠마는 말했다. \"온갖 궁핍을 견뎌 가며 달이 가고 또 가도록 여기 머물기를 택하다니! 게다가 이제는, 늘 그토록 진실하고 너그러운 애정으로 자기를 아껴 온 더 나은 벗들에게로 돌아가는 대신, 엘턴 부인의 관심이라는 굴욕과 그 대화의 빈곤을 택하다니.\"\n\n제인은 표면상 석 달 예정으로 하이베리에 왔다. 캠벨 부부도 석 달 일정으로 아일랜드에 가 있었다. 그런데 이제 캠벨 부부는 적어도 한여름까지 머물기로 딸에게 약속했고, 제인에게도 그곳에 합류하라는 새 초대장이 도착해 있었다. 미스 베이츠의 말로는 — 모든 소식은 그녀에게서 나왔다 — 딕슨 부인이 더없이 간곡하게 편지를 써 보냈다고 했다. 제인이 가기만 하면 방도는 마련될 것이고, 하인도 보내고, 동행할 벗도 주선해서, 여행에 따르는 어려움이라곤 있을 수 없게 해 주겠다는 것이었다. 그런데도 제인은 거절했다는 것이다!\n\n\"이 초대를 거절하는 데는 겉으로 드러나는 것보다 더 강력한 어떤 동기가 있는 게 분명해.\" 이것이 엠마의 결론이었다. \"캠벨 부부가, 아니면 자기 스스로 부과한 일종의 속죄를 치르고 있는 게 틀림없어. 어딘가에 큰 두려움이, 큰 조심성이, 큰 결심이 있는 거야. 그분은 딕슨 부부와 함께 있어선 안 되는 거지. 누군가가 그런 명을 내린 거야. 그런데 어째서 엘턴 부부와는 함께 있겠다고 하는 걸까? 이건 완전히 별개의 수수께끼야.\"\n\n엘턴 부인에 대한 자신의 견해를 아는 몇 안 되는 사람들 앞에서 엠마가 그 부분에 대한 의문을 소리 내어 말하자, 웨스턴 부인이 제인을 두둔하며 이렇게 변명해 보았다.\n\n\"그분이 목사관에서 무슨 큰 즐거움을 누린다고야 생각할 수 없지요, 엠마. 하지만 늘 집에만 있는 것보다는 나으니까요. 이모님이야 착한 분이지만, 늘 곁에 있는 말동무로는 무척 따분할 게 분명해요. 미스 페어팩스가 무엇을 향해 가는지를 두고 그 취향을 나무라기 전에, 그분이 무엇을 떠나 있는지를 헤아려 봐야 해요.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀이에요, 웨스턴 부인.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 힘주어 말했다. \"미스 페어팩스도 우리 누구 못지않게 엘턴 부인에 대해 올바른 판단을 내릴 줄 압니다. 누구와 어울릴지 고를 수 있었다면 그녀를 고르진 않았겠지요. 하지만 (엠마를 향해 나무라는 듯한 미소를 지으며) 미스 페어팩스는 엘턴 부인에게서, 아무도 그녀에게 베풀지 않는 배려를 받고 있는 겁니다.\"\n\n엠마는 웨스턴 부인이 자기에게 슬쩍 눈길을 던지는 것을 느꼈고, 그 자신도 그의 열의에 놀랐다. 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 엠마는 이내 대답했다.\n\n\"엘턴 부인이 베푸는 그런 배려라면, 제 생각엔 미스 페어팩스를 흡족하게 하기보다 오히려 진저리 나게 할 것 같은데요. 엘턴 부인의 초대라면 무엇이든 반갑기는커녕 그 반대일 거라고 생각했거든요.\"\n\n\"미스 페어팩스가 제 마음과는 달리 끌려간 거라면 저는 별로 놀랍지 않을 것 같네요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"이모님이 그분 대신 엘턴 부인의 호의를 덥석 받아들이려고 열을 올렸을 테니까요. 가엾은 미스 베이츠가, 약간의 변화를 바라는 지극히 당연한 마음에서였다 해도, 자기 판단력이 일렀을 정도보다 더 친밀해 보이도록 조카를 떠밀어 그 일에 발을 들이게 했을 가능성이 아주 높지요.\"\n\n두 사람은 그가 다시 말해 주기를 사뭇 듣고 싶어 했다. 몇 분간 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 그가 말했다.\n\n\"또 한 가지 고려해야 할 점이 있습니다 — 엘턴 부인이 미스 페어팩스를 두고 말하는 것과, 그분에게 직접 말을 건넬 때는 다르다는 거지요. 우리 가운데 가장 솔직한 사람도, '그' 혹은 '그녀'라는 대명사와 면전의 '당신' 사이에 차이가 있다는 걸 다 압니다. 우리는 서로 직접 사귀고 부딪칠 때, 으레 갖추는 예의를 넘어서는 어떤 것의 영향을 다들 느끼지요 — 더 일찍부터 마음에 심어진 어떤 것 말입니다. 한 시간 전만 해도 잔뜩 품고 있었을지 모를 불쾌한 속내를, 막상 누구에게 대놓고 내비치지는 못하는 법입니다. 우리는 면전에서는 다르게 느끼는 거예요. 그리고 이게 일반 원리로서 작용하는 데다, 분명히 말하건대, 미스 페어팩스는 정신과 태도 양쪽의 우월함으로 엘턴 부인을 압도합니다. 그래서 얼굴을 마주하면 엘턴 부인은 그분에게 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼의 존중을 다 바칠 겁니다. 제인 페어팩스 같은 여인은 아마 엘턴 부인이 여태 한 번도 마주친 적 없는 부류일 테고요 — 아무리 허영이 심한들, 의식으로는 아닐지언정 행동에서만큼은 자신이 상대적으로 보잘것없다는 걸 인정하지 않을 수 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스를 얼마나 높이 평가하시는지 잘 알아요.\" 엠마가 말했다. 어린 헨리가 머릿속에 떠올라 있던 터라, 경계심과 조심스러움이 뒤섞여 달리 무슨 말을 해야 할지 망설여졌다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 대답했다. \"내가 그녀를 얼마나 높이 평가하는지는 누구든 알 수 있겠지.\"\n\n\"하지만 그래도요.\" 엠마는 짓궂은 표정으로 성급하게 말을 꺼냈으나 곧 멈췄다 — 그래도 최악의 사실을 단번에 알아 두는 편이 낫겠다 싶어 — 서둘러 말을 이었다. \"하지만 그래도, 그게 얼마나 높은 평가인지는 미스터 나이틀리 자신도 잘 모르고 계실지 몰라요. 언젠가 그 찬탄의 깊이에 스스로 깜짝 놀라시게 될지도 모르죠.\"\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 두꺼운 가죽 각반의 아래쪽 단추를 끼우느라 여념이 없었는데, 단추를 맞춰 끼우려는 그 안간힘 탓인지 아니면 다른 무슨 까닭에선지, 대답하는 동안 얼굴에 핏기가 올랐다.\n\n\"오! 그쪽 이야기였나? 하지만 자네는 한참 뒤처졌네. 미스터 콜이 그 얘기로 운을 뗀 게 벌써 여섯 주 전이야.\"\n\n그가 말을 멈췄다. 엠마는 웨스턴 부인이 발을 꾹 누르는 것을 느꼈으나, 자기 스스로도 어떻게 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 잠시 후 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"하지만 그런 일은 결코 없을 거라고 장담하네. 단언컨대 미스 페어팩스는, 내가 청한다 한들 나를 받아들이지 않을 거야 — 그리고 나는 절대 그녀에게 청하지 않을 게 분명하고.\"\n\n엠마는 웨스턴 부인이 발을 누른 것에 곱절로 답하며, 흐뭇한 나머지 이렇게 외쳤다.\n\n\"미스터 나이틀리는 허영심이 없으시군요. 그것만은 인정해 드릴게요.\"\n\n그는 그 말을 거의 듣지 못한 듯했다. 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었고 — 흡족하지 않은 기색을 드러내는 투로 — 이내 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까 자네가 나를 제인 페어팩스와 맺어 놓기로 작정했던 거로군?\"\n\n\"아니, 정말 그런 적 없어요. 중매를 선다고 저를 어찌나 야단치셨는데, 감히 미스터 나이틀리한테 그런 짓을 할 엄두를 내겠어요. 방금 한 말은 아무 뜻도 없었어요. 그런 말이야 으레 진지한 의미 따위 없이 하는 거잖아요. 아유! 아니에요, 맹세컨대 미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스든 어느 제인이든 결혼하기를 바라는 마음은 눈곱만큼도 없어요. 결혼하시면 이렇게 들러서 우리랑 편안하게 앉아 계시지도 못하실 텐데요.\"\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 다시 생각에 잠겼다. 그 골똘함의 결론은 이러했다. \"아니야, 엠마, 나는 그녀에 대한 내 찬탄의 깊이에 내가 깜짝 놀랄 일은 결코 없으리라고 생각하네. 단언컨대 나는 한 번도 그런 식으로 그녀를 생각해 본 적이 없어.\" 그러고는 이내 덧붙였다. \"제인 페어팩스는 무척 매력적인 아가씨지 — 하지만 그 제인 페어팩스조차 완벽하지는 않아. 흠이 하나 있지. 남자가 아내에게서 바랄 법한 솔직하고 트인 성품이 그녀에겐 없어.\"\n\n엠마는 그녀에게 흠이 있다는 말을 듣고 기뻐하지 않을 수 없었다. \"그렇군요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"그래서 미스터 콜은 곧 입을 다물게 하셨겠죠?\"\n\n\"그래, 아주 금방. 그가 슬쩍 운을 떼기에, 잘못 짚었다고 일러 줬지. 그랬더니 미안하다며 더는 말이 없더군. 콜은 이웃들보다 더 똑똑하거나 더 재치 있어 보이려는 사람이 아니니까.\"\n\n\"그 점에선 친애하는 엘턴 부인과 어쩜 그리 다른지요. 그분은 온 세상 누구보다 더 똑똑하고 더 재치 있어 보이고 싶어 하잖아요! 그분은 콜네 식구들을 두고 어떻게 말할지 궁금하네요 — 뭐라고 부를까요! 무람없는 천박함에 흠뻑 절은 호칭을 어디서 찾아낼 수 있을까요? 미스터 나이틀리는 그냥 '나이틀리'라고 부르던데 — 미스터 콜한테는 또 어떻게 할까요? 그러니 제인 페어팩스가 그분의 호의를 받아들이고 함께 어울리기로 한다 해서 제가 놀랄 일은 아니겠네요. 웨스턴 부인, 부인의 논리가 제겐 가장 설득력 있어요. 미스 페어팩스의 정신이 엘턴 부인을 누른다는 말보다는, 미스 베이츠한테서 벗어나고 싶은 유혹에 빠진다는 쪽이 훨씬 더 쉽게 납득돼요. 저는 엘턴 부인이 생각에서든 말에서든 행동에서든 자신이 못하다는 걸 인정하리라고는, 또 그 옹색한 교양의 규칙 말고 다른 어떤 절제에 매여 있으리라고는 도무지 믿지 못하겠어요. 그분이 손님에게 끊임없이 칭찬이며 격려며 도와주겠다는 제안을 퍼부어 모욕을 안기지 않으리라고는, 영구적인 일자리를 구해 주는 것부터 바루시-랜도에 태워 떠날 그 유쾌한 유람에 끼워 주는 것까지 자기의 거창한 포부를 끊임없이 늘어놓지 않으리라고는 상상이 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"제인 페어팩스에게도 감정이 있습니다.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"나는 그녀가 무정하다고 탓하는 게 아니에요. 내 짐작엔 그녀의 감수성은 강합니다 — 그리고 그 성품은 참고 견디는 힘에서, 인내에서, 자제력에서 더없이 훌륭하고요. 다만 트인 데가 모자라요. 그녀는 속을 잘 드러내지 않아요. 예전보다 더 그런 것 같습니다 — 그런데 나는 트인 성품을 사랑하거든요. 아니야 — 콜이 내가 그녀에게 마음을 두었다는 소문을 들먹이기 전에는, 그런 생각이 한 번도 머리에 떠오른 적이 없어. 나는 늘 찬탄과 즐거움으로 제인 페어팩스를 바라보고 그녀와 대화를 나눴지만 — 그 이상의 생각은 없었어.\"\n\n\"자, 웨스턴 부인.\" 그가 자리를 뜨자 엠마가 의기양양하게 말했다. \"이제 미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스와 결혼하는 일을 두고 뭐라 하시겠어요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 정말이지, 엠마. 내가 보기엔 그분이 그녀를 사랑하지 않는다는 생각에 너무도 골몰해 계셔서, 결국엔 그게 사랑하는 걸로 끝난다 해도 난 놀랄 것 같지 않구나. 날 때리진 말렴.\"\n\n# 제2권 16장\n\n하이베리 안팎에서 미스터 엘턴과 한 번이라도 왕래가 있던 사람이라면 누구나 그의 혼인에 마음을 써 주려 했다. 그와 그 부인을 위한 만찬 모임과 저녁 모임이 마련되었고, 초대가 어찌나 밀려드는지 부인은 곧 자기들에게는 한가한 날이라곤 없게 되리라는 즐거운 짐작을 하게 되었다.\n\n\"어떻게 돌아가는지 알겠어요.\" 부인이 말했다. \"제가 여러분 사이에서 어떤 생활을 하게 될지 훤히 보인다고요. 정말이지 우리는 흥청망청 놀 수밖에 없겠어요. 우리가 영락없이 유행의 중심이로군요. 시골 생활이라는 게 이런 거라면, 그다지 무서울 것도 없네요. 다음 주 월요일부터 토요일까지, 장담하건대 한가한 날이 하루도 없어요! 저보다 마음 둘 데가 적은 여자라도 심심할 틈은 없었을 거예요.\"\n\n어떤 초대든 그녀에게는 마다할 것이 없었다. 바스에서 익힌 습관 덕에 저녁 모임은 더없이 자연스러운 것이었고, 메이플 그로브에서는 만찬을 즐기는 취향을 키웠다. 응접실이 둘 있지 않은 것, 차 모임 과자가 변변치 못한 것, 하이베리의 카드 모임에 얼음이 없는 것에는 적이 충격을 받았다. 베이츠 부인, 페리 부인, 고더드 부인을 비롯한 이들은 세상물정을 아는 데서는 한참 뒤떨어져 있었으나, 모든 것을 어떻게 차려야 마땅한지 곧 자기가 본을 보여 줄 참이었다. 봄이 가기 전에 한 차례 아주 격조 높은 모임으로 그들의 호의에 답례해야 했다 — 그 모임에서는 카드 탁자마다 따로 촛불을 놓고 뜯지 않은 새 카드 묶음을 제대로 갖춰 차릴 것이며 — 정확히 알맞은 시각에 알맞은 순서로 다과를 돌리도록, 자기 집 하인만으로는 모자랄 만큼 많은 시중꾼을 그날 저녁을 위해 더 부를 작정이었다.\n\n한편 엠마는 하트필드에서 엘턴 부부를 위한 만찬을 베풀지 않고는 마음이 놓이지 않았다. 남들만큼 하지 않으면 안 될 일이었으니, 그러지 않으면 고약한 의심을 사고 옹졸한 앙심을 품을 사람으로 여겨질 판이었다. 만찬은 꼭 있어야 했다. 엠마가 십 분쯤 이야기를 늘어놓자 미스터 우드하우스는 별로 마다하지 않았고, 다만 자신은 식탁 끝자리에 앉지 않겠다는 늘 하던 조건을 달았는데, 그러자 그를 대신해 누가 그 자리에 앉을지 정하는 늘 있던 어려움이 어김없이 뒤따랐다.\n\n초대할 사람을 정하는 데는 별 고민이 필요 없었다. 엘턴 부부 외에 웨스턴 부부와 미스터 나이틀리는 당연했고, 거기까지는 두말할 것이 없었다. 그리고 여덟 번째 자리를 채우려면 가엾은 어린 해리엇을 청해야 한다는 것도 거의 그만큼 피할 수 없는 일이었다. 다만 이 초대만큼은 흔쾌한 마음으로 한 것이 아니어서, 해리엇이 사양하게 해 달라고 청해 왔을 때 엠마는 여러모로 각별히 기뻤다. \"되도록이면 그분과 한자리에 있고 싶지 않다고 했다. 아직은 그분과 그 매력적이고 행복한 부인이 함께 있는 모습을 보면서 마음이 편하기가 어렵다고 했다. 미스 우드하우스만 언짢지 않으시다면 차라리 집에 있겠다고 했다.\" 그것은 엠마가 바랄 만하다고 여겼더라면 바로 그렇게 바랐을 일이었다. 어린 벗의 의연함이 엠마는 흐뭇했다 — 사람들과 어울리기를 포기하고 집에 있겠다는 것은 그 아이로서는 분명 의연함이었으니까. 그리하여 이제 여덟 번째 자리에 정말로 앉히고 싶던 바로 그 사람, 제인 페어팩스를 청할 수 있게 되었다. 웨스턴 부인과 미스터 나이틀리와 지난번 이야기를 나눈 뒤로, 엠마는 제인 페어팩스를 두고 여느 때보다 더 양심의 가책을 느끼고 있었다. 미스터 나이틀리의 말이 마음에 걸려 떠나지 않았다. 그는 제인 페어팩스가 다른 누구도 베풀지 않는 관심을 엘턴 부인에게서만 받고 있다고 했다.\n\n\"정말 그래.\" 엠마는 혼잣말을 했다. \"적어도 나로 말하자면 그렇지. 그게 바로 그 말의 뜻이었으니까 — 정말 부끄러운 일이야. 동갑인 데다 — 줄곧 알고 지낸 사이인데 — 내가 더 좋은 벗이 되어 주었어야 했어. 이제는 절대 나를 좋아해 주지 않겠지. 너무 오래 그 애를 소홀히 했어. 그래도 이제부터는 지금까지보다 더 마음을 써 줘야지.\"\n\n초대는 모두 성사되었다. 다들 한가했고 다들 기뻐했다. 그러나 이 만찬을 앞둔 일들이 아직 끝난 것은 아니었다. 다소 운 나쁜 일이 하나 생겼다. 나이틀리네 큰 아이 둘이 봄에 외할아버지와 이모를 몇 주간 찾아오기로 되어 있었는데, 이제 그 아이들 아버지가 자기가 직접 데려와 하트필드에서 꼬박 하루를 묵겠다고 제안한 것이다 — 그런데 하필 그 하루가 바로 이 모임이 있는 날이었다. 직업상의 일정이 있어 미룰 수가 없었으니, 부녀는 둘 다 일이 이렇게 겹친 데 마음이 편치 않았다. 미스터 우드하우스는 한 식탁에 여덟 사람이 둘러앉는 것을 자기 신경이 견딜 수 있는 최대치로 여겼는데, 여기에 아홉 번째 사람이 생기게 된 것이었다. 엠마는 또 엠마대로, 마흔여덟 시간조차 만찬 모임과 마주치지 않고서는 하트필드에 다녀갈 수 없게 된 데 잔뜩 심기가 틀어진 아홉 번째 손님이 되리라는 것을 걱정했다.\n\n엠마는 자기 마음을 달래는 것보다는 아버지의 마음을 더 잘 달랬으니, 형부가 분명 아홉 번째 사람이 되기는 하지만 워낙 말수가 적은 사람이라 시끄러움이 늘어날 일은 거의 없을 거라고 일러 드렸다. 사실 엠마는 자기로서는 형부의 형님 대신 그 시무룩한 얼굴과 마지못한 대화를 맞은편에 두게 된 것이 못내 아쉬운 맞바꿈이라고 생각했다.\n\n일은 엠마보다 미스터 우드하우스에게 더 유리하게 풀렸다. 존 나이틀리는 왔지만, 미스터 웨스턴이 뜻밖에 시내로 불려 가게 되어 바로 그날 자리를 비울 수밖에 없게 된 것이다. 저녁에는 합류할 수 있을지 몰라도 만찬에는 결코 올 수 없게 되었다. 미스터 우드하우스는 마음이 아주 놓였다. 그렇게 편안해진 아버지의 모습에다 어린 사내아이들이 도착한 것, 그리고 자기 운명을 듣고도 의연하고 담담한 형부의 태도가 더해지자, 엠마의 언짢음마저 거의 가셨다.\n\n그날이 왔고, 일행은 시각에 맞춰 모였으며, 미스터 존 나이틀리는 일찌감치 상냥하게 굴기로 작정한 듯 보였다. 만찬을 기다리는 동안 형을 창가로 끌고 가는 대신, 그는 미스 페어팩스와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 레이스와 진주로 차릴 수 있는 한 우아하게 차린 엘턴 부인을, 그는 말없이 바라보기만 했다 — 이저벨라에게 일러 줄 만큼만 살펴 두려는 것이었다. 그러나 미스 페어팩스는 오랜 지인이자 조용한 아가씨여서, 그녀와는 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다. 그는 어린 아들들과 산책을 마치고 돌아오던 아침 식사 전에 그녀와 마주쳤는데, 그때 막 비가 듣기 시작하던 참이었다. 그 일에 관해 으레 하는 정중한 인사말이 자연스럽게 나왔다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 멀리까지는 안 나가셨기를 바랍니다, 미스 페어팩스. 안 그러면 분명 흠뻑 젖으셨을 텐데요. 저희도 간신히 제때 들어왔거든요. 곧장 돌아오셨기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"우체국까지만 갔어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"비가 많이 오기 전에 집에 닿았고요. 날마다 하는 심부름이에요. 여기 있을 때는 늘 제가 편지를 받아 오거든요. 수고도 덜고, 밖에 나갈 구실도 되니까요. 아침 식사 전 산책은 제게 좋답니다.\"\n\n\"빗속 산책이 좋을 리야 없겠지요.\"\n\n\"그야 그렇죠. 하지만 나설 때는 정말로 비가 오지는 않았어요.\"\n\n미스터 존 나이틀리는 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"그러니까, 산책을 꼭 하고 싶으셨던 거군요. 제가 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸을 때 댁 문에서 여섯 야드도 채 안 떨어진 곳이셨는데, 헨리와 존은 이미 한참 전부터 셀 수 없을 만큼 많은 빗방울을 보고 있었으니까요. 우체국이라는 게 우리 인생의 어느 한때는 그렇게 매력적인 법이지요. 제 나이까지 살아 보시면, 비를 무릅쓰고 받으러 갈 만한 편지란 없다고 여기기 시작하실 겁니다.\"\n\n살짝 얼굴을 붉히더니, 이런 대답이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"저는 결코 선생님 같은 처지에 놓일 수 있으리라고 기대해서는 안 되겠지요. 더없이 소중한 인연들에 둘러싸여 계시잖아요. 그러니 그저 나이가 든다고 해서 제가 편지에 무심해지리라고는 바랄 수가 없네요.\"\n\n\"무심이라니요! 오! 아닙니다 — 미스 페어팩스가 무심해지실 수 있으리라고는 생각해 본 적이 없어요. 편지란 무심할 수 있는 것이 아니지요. 대개는 아주 확실한 골칫거리니까요.\"\n\n\"업무 편지를 말씀하시는 거군요. 제 것은 우정의 편지예요.\"\n\n\"저는 그 둘 중 우정의 편지가 더 고약한 것이라고 종종 생각해 왔습니다.\" 그가 차분하게 대꾸했다. \"업무란 아시다시피 돈을 가져다주기도 하지만, 우정이 돈을 가져다주는 일은 좀처럼 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"아! 지금 진심으로 하시는 말씀은 아니죠. 저는 미스터 존 나이틀리를 너무 잘 알아요 — 그분이 누구 못지않게 우정의 가치를 잘 아신다는 걸 확신한답니다. 편지가 선생님께는 별것 아니라는 것, 저보다 훨씬 덜하시다는 것은 쉽게 믿을 수 있어요. 하지만 그 차이를 만드는 건 선생님이 저보다 열 살 위라는 사실이 아니에요. 나이가 아니라 처지죠. 선생님은 더없이 소중한 사람들을 늘 곁에 두고 계시지만, 저는 아마 다시는 그렇지 못할 거예요. 그러니 제 모든 애정보다 더 오래 살아남기 전까지는, 우체국이란 오늘보다 더 궂은 날씨에도 늘 저를 밖으로 끌어낼 힘을 지니고 있을 거라고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"제가 시간이 흐르면, 세월이 가면 미스 페어팩스도 달라지리라고 말한 것은,\" 존 나이틀리가 말했다. \"시간이 으레 가져오는 처지의 변화를 두고 한 말이었습니다. 저는 그 하나가 다른 하나를 품고 있다고 보거든요. 시간은 대개 날마다 마주하는 둘레 안에 들지 않는 모든 연정의 무게를 덜어 주는 법이지요 — 하지만 그것이 제가 미스 페어팩스를 두고 떠올린 변화는 아니었습니다. 오랜 친구로서, 미스 페어팩스, 십 년 뒤에는 미스 페어팩스도 저처럼 마음을 쏟을 사람들을 곁에 잔뜩 두시기를 바라게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n다정하게 한 말이었고, 거슬릴 데라곤 전혀 없었다. 흔쾌한 \"고맙습니다\"가 그 말을 웃어넘기려는 듯했으나, 발그레해진 얼굴과 떨리는 입술과 눈에 맺힌 눈물이, 웃음 이상으로 그 말이 와닿았음을 드러냈다. 이제 미스터 우드하우스가 그녀의 관심을 청했으니, 그는 이런 자리에서 늘 하던 버릇대로 손님들을 한 바퀴 돌며 부인들에게 각별한 인사말을 건네던 차에, 그녀에게서 그 순서를 마무리하던 참이었다. 그는 더없이 부드럽고 정중하게 말했다.\n\n\"미스 페어팩스, 오늘 아침 빗속에 나가셨다니 참으로 안되었군요. 젊은 아가씨들은 몸을 잘 돌봐야 해요. 젊은 아가씨들은 여린 화초나 다름없지요. 건강과 얼굴빛을 잘 보살펴야 합니다. 얘야, 양말은 갈아 신었느냐?\"\n\n\"네, 어르신, 정말 갈아 신었어요. 이렇게 마음 써 주셔서 정말 감사드립니다.\"\n\n\"미스 페어팩스, 젊은 아가씨들이야 응당 보살핌을 받게 마련이지요. 자네의 훌륭하신 할머님과 이모님도 평안하시기를 바라오. 그분들은 내 아주 오랜 벗들이라오. 건강이 받쳐 주어 내가 좀 더 나은 이웃이 될 수 있다면 좋으련만. 오늘 자네가 우리를 크게 빛내 주었소, 정말이오. 내 딸과 나는 자네의 고마운 마음을 깊이 느끼고 있고, 하트필드에서 자네를 보게 되어 더할 나위 없이 기쁘다오.\"\n\n마음씨 곱고 정중한 노인은 그제야 자리에 앉아, 제 도리를 다했으며 모든 아리따운 부인들을 반갑고 편안하게 맞이했다는 흐뭇함을 느낄 수 있었다.\n\n이때쯤 빗속 산책 이야기가 엘턴 부인에게까지 닿아, 이제 그녀의 잔소리가 제인을 향해 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"제인, 이게 무슨 소리예요? 빗속에 우체국엘 가다니! 이건 안 될 말이에요, 정말이지. 못된 아가씨 같으니, 어떻게 그런 짓을 할 수가 있어요? 내가 옆에서 챙겨 주지 못했다는 증거로군요.\"\n\n제인은 아주 참을성 있게, 감기 같은 건 들지 않았다고 잘라 말했다.\n\n\"오! 내겐 그런 말 마요. 정말이지 못 말릴 아가씨예요. 제 몸 하나 돌볼 줄을 몰라요. 우체국이라니, 세상에! 웨스턴 부인, 이런 얘기 들어 보신 적 있어요? 부인과 내가 단단히 우리 권위를 세워야겠어요.\"\n\n\"제 조언을 말씀드리자면,\" 웨스턴 부인이 다정하고 설득력 있게 말했다. \"정말이지 한마디 드리고 싶은 마음이 드네요. 미스 페어팩스, 그렇게 위험을 무릅쓰면 안 돼요. 그동안 심한 감기에 잘 걸리셨으니, 정말이지 각별히 조심하셔야 해요. 특히 이맘때는요. 봄철에는 늘 여느 때보다 더한 주의가 필요하다고 저는 생각한답니다. 다시 기침이 도질 위험을 무릅쓰느니, 편지는 한두 시간, 아니 반나절을 기다리는 편이 나아요. 미스 페어팩스도 그랬다고 느끼시지 않나요? 그럼요, 분명 미스 페어팩스는 너무나 분별 있는 분인걸요. 다시는 그런 일을 하지 않으실 얼굴이세요.\"\n\n\"오! 다시는 그런 일을 못 하게 해야죠,\" 엘턴 부인이 신이 나서 받았다. \"다시는 그런 짓을 하도록 두지 않을 거예요.\" 그러고는 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕이며 — \"무슨 조처를 마련해야 해요, 정말이지요. 제가 미스터 E.에게 말해 두겠어요. 우리 편지를 아침마다 받아 오는 사람이 (우리 집 하인 하나인데, 이름이 기억이 안 나네요) 미스 페어팩스 편지도 함께 물어다가 가져다 드리도록 할게요. 그러면 모든 곤란이 사라지잖아요. 그리고 우리한테서라면, 정말이지, 제인, 이런 편의쯤 받는 데 거리낄 게 하나도 없으리라고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"정말 친절하시네요.\" 제인이 말했다. \"하지만 저는 이른 아침 산책을 포기할 수가 없어요. 되도록 바깥에 많이 나가라는 권고를 받았거든요. 어디든 걸어야 하는데, 우체국이 그 목적지가 되는 거예요. 그리고 정말이지, 지금까지 궂은 아침을 겪은 적이 거의 없답니다.\"\n\n\"제인, 그 얘긴 더 마요. 이미 정해진 일이에요. 그러니까 (어색하게 웃으며) 제 주인이신 분의 동의 없이 제가 감히 정할 수 있는 한도 안에서는 말이죠. 웨스턴 부인도 아시잖아요, 부인과 나는 말을 조심해서 해야 한다는 걸요. 그래도 제인, 제 영향력이 아주 바닥난 건 아니라고 자부한답니다. 그러니 넘기 어려운 난관만 없다면, 그 일은 정해진 걸로 여겨 줘요.\"\n\n\"죄송하지만,\" 제인이 진지하게 말했다. \"저는 그런 조처에 도무지 동의할 수가 없어요. 댁의 하인을 공연히 그렇게 번거롭게 하다니요. 그 심부름이 제게 즐거운 일이 아니라면, 제가 여기 없을 때 늘 그러듯 할머님 댁에서 시키면 될 일이에요.\"\n\n\"오! 제인, 하지만 패티가 할 일이 얼마나 많은데요! 게다가 우리 집 하인을 부리는 게 그 사람한테는 호의를 베푸는 셈이고요.\"\n\n제인은 좀처럼 굽힐 뜻이 없어 보였다. 그러나 대답하는 대신 다시 미스터 존 나이틀리에게 말을 걸기 시작했다.\n\n\"우체국이란 참 놀라운 기관이에요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 규칙성과 신속함이라니! 그곳이 해야 하는 그 모든 일을, 그것도 그토록 잘 해내는 걸 생각하면, 정말이지 놀랍지 않나요!\"\n\n\"확실히 아주 잘 굴러가지요.\"\n\n\"실수나 차질이 나타나는 일이 어쩌면 그렇게 드문지요! 온 나라를 끊임없이 오가는 수천 통의 편지 가운데 잘못 배달되는 일조차 그토록 드물고 — 백만 통에 한 통도 실제로 분실되는 일이 없을 거예요! 더구나 알아봐야 할 그 갖가지 필체, 그것도 형편없는 필체들까지 생각하면, 놀라움이 더해지죠.\"\n\n\"직원들이 손에 익어 능숙해지는 거지요. 처음부터 눈과 손이 어느 정도 빨라야 하고, 그게 훈련으로 나아지는 거고요. 더 자세한 설명을 원하신다면,\" 그가 미소 지으며 말을 이었다. \"그들은 그 일에 대한 보수를 받습니다. 그게 그 많은 재간의 비결이지요. 사람들이 값을 치르니, 마땅히 잘 시중받아야 하는 거고요.\"\n\n필체의 갖가지에 관한 이야기가 좀 더 이어졌고, 으레 하는 소견들이 오갔다.\n\n\"이런 주장을 들은 적이 있습니다.\" 존 나이틀리가 말했다. \"한 집안에서는 비슷한 필체가 흔히 두드러진다고요. 같은 선생이 가르치는 데서야 충분히 자연스러운 일이지요. 하지만 바로 그 이유로, 그 닮음은 주로 여자들에게 국한되리라고 봅니다. 사내아이들은 어린 나이가 지나면 가르침을 거의 받지 못하고, 손에 잡히는 대로 아무 필체나 익히게 되니까요. 이저벨라와 엠마는 글씨를 아주 비슷하게 쓰는 것 같습니다. 저도 늘 둘의 글씨를 구별하지는 못했거든요.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 그의 형이 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"닮은 데가 있지. 무슨 뜻인지는 알겠네 — 하지만 엠마 글씨가 더 힘이 있네.\"\n\n\"이저벨라와 엠마는 둘 다 글씨가 아주 곱지요.\" 미스터 우드하우스가 말했다. \"예전부터 늘 그랬소. 그리고 가엾은 웨스턴 부인도 그렇고.\" 그러면서 그녀를 향해 절반은 한숨, 절반은 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"저는 어떤 신사분의 글씨도 본 적이 없는데 —\" 엠마가 운을 떼며 웨스턴 부인 쪽을 보았으나, 웨스턴 부인이 다른 누군가에게 귀를 기울이고 있는 것을 알아채고는 말을 멈췄다. 그 멈춤이 그녀에게 생각할 틈을 주었다. \"자, 그분을 어떻게 끌어들이지? 이 사람들 다 앞에서 곧장 그분 이름을 입에 올리는 게 그렇게 어려운 일일까? 굳이 빙 둘러 말해야 하나? 당신의 요크셔 친구분 — 요크셔에 있는 당신의 서신 상대 — 내가 아주 형편없다면 그렇게 둘러대겠지. 아니야, 나는 그분 이름을 조금도 거북하지 않게 부를 수 있어. 분명 점점 나아지고 있어. 자, 해 보자.\"\n\n웨스턴 부인의 귀가 비자, 엠마는 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"미스터 프랭크 처칠은 제가 본 신사분 글씨 가운데 가장 훌륭한 글씨를 쓰는 분이에요.\"\n\n\"나는 그 글씨가 감탄스럽지 않던걸.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"너무 작아 — 힘이 없어. 여자 글씨 같단 말이야.\"\n\n이 말은 두 부인 어느 쪽도 받아들이지 않았다. 두 사람은 그 부당한 험담에 맞서 그를 변호했다. \"아니에요, 결코 힘이 없지 않았어요 — 큰 글씨는 아니었지만 아주 또렷하고 분명 힘이 있었는걸요. 웨스턴 부인이 마침 그분 편지를 한 통이라도 가지고 계시지 않나요?\" 아니, 최근에 그에게서 편지를 받기는 했으나, 답장을 쓰고 나서 치워 두었다고 했다.\n\n\"우리가 저쪽 방에 있다면,\" 엠마가 말했다. \"제 글쓰기 책상이 있다면, 분명 견본 하나를 보여 드릴 수 있을 텐데요. 그분 쪽지를 한 장 가지고 있거든요. 웨스턴 부인, 기억 안 나세요? 언젠가 그분한테 부인 대신 글을 써 달라고 부탁하셨던 일요.\"\n\n\"그이가 자기더러 부탁받았다고 우긴 거였죠 —\"\n\n\"아무튼, 아무튼, 제게 그 쪽지가 있어요. 만찬 후에 보여 드려서 미스터 나이틀리를 납득시킬 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"오! 미스터 프랭크 처칠 같은 멋진 젊은이가,\" 미스터 나이틀리가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"미스 우드하우스 같은 아리따운 아가씨에게 편지를 쓸 때야, 당연히 제 솜씨를 한껏 발휘하겠지.\"\n\n만찬상이 차려졌다. 엘턴 부인은 누가 말을 건네기도 전에 채비를 마쳤고, 미스터 우드하우스가 그녀를 식당으로 모시게 해 달라는 청을 가지고 다가가기도 전에 이렇게 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"제가 먼저 가야 하나요? 늘 앞장서야 하다니 정말 민망하네요.\"\n\n자기 편지를 직접 받아 오는 일에 제인이 그토록 애를 쓰던 것을 엠마는 놓치지 않았다. 그 모든 것을 듣고 보았으니, 오늘 아침의 그 비 맞은 산책이 무언가 결실을 보았을지 다소 궁금했다. 그녀는 결실이 있었으리라 짐작했다. 누군가 아주 소중한 사람에게서 소식을 들으리라는 기대가 가득하지 않고서야 그렇게 단단히 마음먹고 나설 리 없었을 테고, 그것이 헛수고는 아니었으리라고 보았다. 평소보다 더 행복해 보이는 기색이 있다고, 얼굴빛에도 기분에도 윤기가 돈다고 여겼다.\n\n엠마는 한두 마디쯤 물어볼 수도 있었다 — 아일랜드 우편의 발송과 비용에 관해서 — 그 말이 혀끝까지 나왔다 — 그러나 참았다. 제인 페어팩스의 마음을 다치게 할 말은 한마디도 입에 올리지 않겠다고 단단히 작정한 것이다. 그리하여 두 사람은 다른 부인들을 따라 팔짱을 끼고 방을 나섰으니, 각자의 미모와 우아함에 더없이 어울리는 다정한 모습이었다.\n\n# 제2권 17장\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나고 부인들이 응접실로 돌아왔을 때, 엠마는 그들이 두 무리로 또렷이 갈라지는 것을 막기란 거의 불가능하다는 것을 알았다. 엘턴 부인은 그토록 고집스럽게 분별없이 판단하고 처신하며, 제인 페어팩스를 독차지하고 엠마 자신은 푸대접했던 것이다. 엠마와 웨스턴 부인은 거의 언제나 둘이서만 이야기를 나누거나 둘이서만 잠자코 있을 수밖에 없었다. 엘턴 부인이 다른 선택의 여지를 주지 않았다. 제인이 잠깐 그녀를 막아 보아도, 그녀는 곧 다시 시작했다. 두 사람 사이에 오가는 말은 많은 부분이, 특히 엘턴 부인 쪽이, 반쯤 속삭임이었으나, 그 주된 화제를 모를 수가 없었다. 우체국 — 감기 드는 일 — 편지 찾아오기 — 그리고 우정이 한참 동안 거론되었다. 그러고 나서 뒤를 이은 것은, 제인에게 적어도 그만큼은 불쾌할 것이 분명한 화제였으니 — 그녀에게 맞을 만한 일자리를 혹 들어 본 적이 있느냐는 물음과, 엘턴 부인이 골똘히 나서 보겠다는 다짐이었다.\n\n\"벌써 사월이 됐네요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신 일로 제가 무척 조바심이 난다니까요. 유월도 금세 닥칠 텐데요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 유월이라거나 다른 어느 달이라고 정해 둔 적이 없어요 — 그저 여름쯤이라고 어림했을 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 정말로 아무 소식도 못 들으셨어요?\"\n\n\"저는 알아보지도 않았는걸요. 아직은 알아보고 싶지 않아요.\"\n\n\"어머! 이봐요, 아무리 일찍 시작해도 이르지 않다니까요. 꼭 들어맞는 자리를 구하기가 얼마나 어려운지 당신은 모르시는 거예요.\"\n\n\"제가 모른다고요!\" 제인이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"엘턴 부인, 저만큼 그 일을 곱씹어 본 사람이 또 어디 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 저만큼 세상을 두루 보지 못했잖아요. 제일 좋은 자리마다 늘 지원자가 얼마나 몰리는지 모르실 거예요. 메이플 그로브 인근에서 저는 그런 일을 숱하게 봤거든요. 미스터 서클링의 사촌인 미시즈 브래그한테는 그야말로 끝도 없이 지원이 들어왔어요. 다들 그 댁에 들어가고 싶어 안달이었죠. 일류 사교계에서 노는 분이니까요. 교실에 밀랍 양초를 켜 둘 정도라니까요! 얼마나 탐나는 자리인지 짐작이 가시죠! 이 나라의 어느 집보다도, 미시즈 브래그 댁에 당신을 들여보내고 싶은 게 제 마음이에요.\"\n\n\"캠벨 대령 내외분이 한여름까지는 다시 런던으로 오실 거예요.\" 제인이 말했다. \"저는 그분들과 한동안 지내야 해요. 그분들도 분명 그러길 바라실 거고요. 그다음에야 아마 기꺼이 제 앞가림을 하게 되겠지요. 하지만 지금은 부인께서 알아보시는 수고를 하지 않으셨으면 해요.\"\n\n\"수고라니요! 아아, 당신의 그 조심성을 제가 알지요. 제게 폐가 될까 봐 걱정하시는 거잖아요. 하지만 이봐요, 제인, 캠벨 댁이라 해도 저만큼 당신 일에 마음을 쓰지는 못할 거예요. 하루 이틀 안에 미시즈 파트리지한테 편지를 써서, 합당한 자리가 있으면 빠짐없이 알아봐 달라고 단단히 일러둘 작정이에요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다만, 그분께는 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않으셨으면 해요. 때가 좀 더 다가오기 전까지는, 누구에게도 폐를 끼치고 싶지 않거든요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이봐요, 때가 다가오고 있다니까요. 벌써 사월이고, 유월이, 아니 칠월이라 쳐도, 코앞이에요. 그전에 해치워야 할 일이 이렇게나 많은데요. 당신의 그 미숙함이 정말 우습기까지 하네요! 당신에게 걸맞고, 당신 친구들이 당신을 위해 요구할 만한 그런 자리는 흔하디흔한 게 아니에요. 당장 부탁한다고 얻어지는 게 아니라고요. 정말이지, 정말이지, 우리는 곧장 알아보기 시작해야 해요.\"\n\n\"미안하지만요, 부인, 그건 결코 제 뜻이 아니에요. 저는 스스로도 알아보지 않을 거고, 제 친구들이 알아봐 주는 것도 싫어요. 때를 확실히 정하고 나면, 오래도록 일자리가 없을까 봐 걱정하지는 않아요. 런던에는 알아보기만 하면 곧 무언가를 내놓을 곳들이, 사무소들이 있으니까요 — 거래소들 말이에요. 사람의 살을 — 그렇게까지는 아니지만 — 사람의 지력을 사고파는 곳 말이지요.\"\n\n\"어머! 이봐요, 사람의 살이라뇨! 정말 깜짝 놀랐잖아요. 혹 노예무역을 꼬집으시는 거라면, 정말이지 미스터 서클링은 늘 폐지 쪽 편이셨답니다.\"\n\n\"그런 뜻이 아니었어요. 노예무역을 생각한 게 아니었어요.\" 제인이 대답했다. \"가정교사 거래를 두고 한 말이었어요, 정말로. 그 일을 영위하는 자들의 죄로 따지자면 분명 크게 다르겠지요. 하지만 그 희생자들의 더 큰 비참함으로 말하자면, 어느 쪽이 더하다고 저는 잘 모르겠어요. 다만 제가 하려던 말은, 그런 알선 사무소들이 있고, 거기에 부탁하면 머지않아 쓸 만한 자리를 만나게 되리란 건 의심하지 않는다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"쓸 만한 자리라니!\" 엘턴 부인이 되뇌었다. \"그래요, 자기 자신을 그렇게 낮춰 보는 당신 생각에야 그쯤도 맞을지 모르죠. 당신이 얼마나 겸손한 사람인지 제가 알아요. 하지만 당신 친구들은, 당신이 나서는 대로 아무거나 떠맡는 것을, 어떤 사교계에도 끼지 못하고 삶의 우아함이라곤 누릴 줄 모르는 집안의 하찮고 흔해 빠진 자리를 떠맡는 것을 결코 두고 보지 못할 거예요.\"\n\n\"무척 고마운 말씀이세요. 하지만 그 모든 것에 저는 정말 아무런 미련이 없어요. 부유한 집에 들어가는 게 제겐 별 의미가 없거든요. 오히려 굴욕만 더 커질 것 같아요. 견주는 데서 더 괴로울 테니까요. 신사 계급의 집안이면 그게 제가 내거는 조건의 전부예요.\"\n\n\"제가 당신을 알아요, 알지요. 당신이야 아무거나 떠맡으려 들 사람이죠. 하지만 저는 좀 더 까다로울 작정이고, 마음씨 고운 캠벨 댁도 분명 제 편을 들어 주실 거예요. 당신의 그 뛰어난 재능이라면, 일류 사교계에서 노닐 자격이 충분해요. 음악에 대한 식견 하나만으로도 당신은 조건을 직접 내걸 수 있고, 방을 원하는 만큼 쓸 수 있고, 그 집안과 마음껏 어울릴 수 있어요. 그러니까 — 잘은 모르지만 — 하프까지 다룰 줄 알았다면 그 모든 걸 할 수 있었을 거예요, 정말 그래요. 하지만 당신은 연주만큼이나 노래도 잘하니까요. 그래요, 정말이지 하프 없이도 원하는 조건을 내걸 수 있을 거라고 저는 믿어요. 그러니 캠벨 댁이나 제가 마음 편히 쉬기 전에, 당신은 더없이 즐겁고 명예롭고 편안한 자리에 꼭 자리 잡아야 하고, 또 그렇게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런 자리의 즐거움과 명예와 편안함을 한데 묶어 말씀하시는 건 옳은 말씀이네요.\" 제인이 말했다. \"그 셋은 어김없이 똑같은 크기일 게 분명하니까요. 어쨌든 저는 지금 저를 위해 무언가가 시도되기를 바라지 않는다는 점에서 아주 진지해요. 엘턴 부인, 정말 고맙습니다. 저를 생각해 주시는 분이라면 누구에게든 고마워요. 하지만 여름이 되기 전까지는 아무 일도 하지 않으셨으면 한다는 점에서 저는 정말 진지해요. 앞으로 두세 달은 지금 있는 곳에서, 지금 이대로 머물 거예요.\"\n\n\"저도 정말 진지하다니까요.\" 엘턴 부인이 명랑하게 대답했다. \"조금도 나무랄 데 없는 자리가 우리를 지나쳐 가지 않도록, 늘 눈을 부릅뜨고, 또 제 친구들도 부려 눈을 부릅뜨게 할 작정이에요.\"\n\n이런 투로 그녀는 끝없이 떠들어 댔다. 미스터 우드하우스가 방으로 들어오기 전까지는 어떤 것도 그녀를 온전히 멈추게 하지 못했다. 그가 들어서자 그녀의 허영심은 대상을 바꾸었고, 엠마는 그녀가 여전히 반쯤 속삭이는 소리로 제인에게 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다.\n\n\"보세요, 저의 사랑스러운 노신사께서 오시네요, 정말로요! 다른 남자분들보다 먼저 자리를 뜨고 오시다니, 저 정중한 기품 좀 보세요! 얼마나 사랑스러운 분인지! 정말이지 저는 저분이 더없이 좋아요. 저 예스럽고 고풍스러운 예의범절이 다 좋아요. 요즘의 그 격식 없음보다 제 취향에 훨씬 맞거든요. 요즘의 격식 없음은 종종 정나미가 떨어지더라고요. 하지만 이 마음씨 고운 노신사 미스터 우드하우스가 식사 자리에서 제게 건넨 정중한 인사들을 당신도 들었더라면! 어머! 정말이지 저는 우리 그이가 영락없이 질투하겠구나 싶을 정도였다니까요. 제가 꽤 마음에 드신 모양이에요. 제 드레스를 눈여겨보시더라고요. 어때요, 마음에 드세요? 셀리나가 골라 준 거예요. 곱긴 한데, 장식이 좀 과하지 않나 싶기도 해요. 저는 장식이 과한 건 정말 질색이거든요. 화려한 차림이라면 아주 끔찍해요. 지금은 장신구를 몇 개 달긴 해야 해요. 다들 그러길 기대하니까요. 신부라면, 아시다시피, 신부답게 보여야 하잖아요. 하지만 제 타고난 취향은 온통 소박함을 향해 있어요. 소박한 차림이 화려함보다 한없이 더 좋거든요. 하지만 제가 영 소수파인가 봐요. 옷차림의 소박함을 귀히 여기는 사람은 드문 것 같으니까요. 과시와 화려함이 전부인 세상이죠. 이 백은빛 포플린 드레스에도 이런 장식을 좀 달아 볼까 생각 중이에요. 잘 어울릴 것 같으세요?\"\n\n일행이 응접실에 다시 막 모여들었을 때, 미스터 웨스턴이 그들 가운데로 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 늦은 저녁 식사 시간에 맞춰 집으로 돌아왔다가, 식사가 끝나기 무섭게 하트필드까지 걸어온 것이었다. 눈썰미 좋은 이들은 이미 그를 너무도 기대하던 터라 놀라움까지는 없었지만, 큰 기쁨이 일었다. 미스터 우드하우스는 전에 그를 보았더라면 서운해했을 그만큼이나, 지금 그를 보고는 기뻐했다. 오직 존 나이틀리만이 말없는 경악에 빠졌다. 런던에서 하루 종일 일을 보고 나면 집에서 조용히 저녁을 보낼 수도 있었을 사람이, 다시 길을 나서 잠자리에 들 때까지 잡다한 무리 속에 끼어 있겠다고, 하루의 끝을 예의의 노고와 사람들의 소란 속에서 마무리하겠다고 남의 집까지 반 마일을 걸어왔다는 사실은, 그에게 깊은 충격이었다. 아침 여덟 시부터 줄곧 움직였으니 이제는 가만히 있을 수도 있었고, 오래 떠들었으니 입을 다물 수도 있었고, 한 번 아닌 여러 무리 속에 있었으니 혼자일 수도 있었을 그런 사람이! 그런 사람이 제 난롯가의 평온과 자유를 버리고, 차가운 진눈깨비 내리는 사월 저녁에 다시금 세상 속으로 뛰쳐나오다니! 손가락 하나만 까딱하면 당장 아내를 도로 데려갈 수 있었다면야 동기라도 있었을 테지만, 그가 온다고 해서 모임이 끝나기는커녕 오히려 길어질 판이었다. 존 나이틀리는 놀란 눈으로 그를 보다가, 어깨를 으쓱하고는 말했다. \"이건 저 사람한테도 좀처럼 못 믿을 일인데.\"\n\n한편 미스터 웨스턴은, 자신이 불러일으키고 있는 분개를 전혀 눈치채지 못한 채, 여느 때처럼 행복하고 쾌활했으며, 집을 떠나 어디서든 하루를 보내고 온 사람에게 주어지는 주된 화자 노릇을 온전히 누리며 좌중에서 사람들의 호감을 사고 있었다. 저녁 식사에 관한 아내의 물음에 답해 주어, 하인들에게 꼼꼼히 일러둔 분부가 하나도 빠짐없이 지켜졌음을 그녀에게 납득시키고, 들은 바깥소식을 두루 풀어놓은 다음, 그는 한 집안의 소식을 전하는 데로 나아갔는데, 그것은 주로 웨스턴 부인을 향한 것이었으나, 방 안의 모두에게도 대단히 흥미로우리라는 것을 그는 추호도 의심하지 않았다. 그는 그녀에게 편지 한 통을 건넸다. 프랭크가 보낸 것으로, 그녀 앞으로 온 편지였다. 그는 오는 길에 그것을 받아 들고는, 멋대로 뜯어본 것이었다.\n\n\"읽어 봐요, 읽어 봐.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신을 기쁘게 해 줄 테니. 몇 줄 안 돼요. 오래 안 걸려. 엠마한테도 읽어 줘요.\"\n\n두 부인이 함께 편지를 들여다보았고, 그동안 그는 내내 미소 지으며 그들에게 말을 건넸는데, 목소리는 조금 낮추었으나 누구에게나 또렷이 들렸다.\n\n\"자아, 보다시피 그 녀석이 온다는군. 좋은 소식이지, 내 생각엔. 자, 어떻게 생각해요? 내가 늘 그러지 않았소, 그 녀석이 곧 다시 여기 올 거라고? 안 그랬소? 앤, 여보, 내가 늘 그렇게 말했는데, 당신은 안 믿으려 했잖소? 다음 주에 런던에 온다잖아요. 길어야 다음 주쯤이라는 거지. 그 부인으로 말하면, 무슨 일이든 해치워야 할 때면 마귀처럼 조바심을 내는 사람이니까. 십중팔구 내일이나 토요일엔 거기 와 있을 거요. 부인의 병환이야, 뭐 당연히 아무것도 아니지. 어쨌든 프랭크가 다시 우리 곁에, 런던처럼 가까운 데 와 있게 된다니 더없이 좋은 일이야. 일단 오면 한참 머물 테고, 그러면 그 녀석이 시간의 절반은 우리하고 보낼 테지. 이거야말로 내가 바라던 바로 그거요. 자, 꽤 좋은 소식이지, 안 그래요? 다 읽었소? 엠마도 끝까지 읽었나? 넣어 둬요, 넣어 둬. 이 일은 언제 다시 한번 차근차근 이야기합시다. 지금은 안 되겠어. 다른 사람들한테는 그저 지나가는 말로 슬쩍 언급만 하리다.\"\n\n웨스턴 부인은 이 일로 더없이 흐뭇하고 기뻤다. 그녀의 표정도 말도 거리낄 것이 없었다. 그녀는 행복했고, 자신이 행복하다는 것을 알았으며, 자신이 마땅히 행복해야 한다는 것도 알았다. 그녀의 축하는 따뜻하고 거침이 없었다. 그러나 엠마는 그처럼 술술 말할 수가 없었다. 그녀는 제 감정을 가늠하고, 제 동요의 정도를 헤아려 보느라 조금 정신이 팔려 있었는데, 그 동요가 적지 않다고 그녀는 생각하는 편이었다.\n\n그러나 미스터 웨스턴은, 무언가를 찬찬히 살피기에는 너무 들떠 있었고, 남이 말하기를 바라기에는 너무 수다스러웠던지라, 그녀가 한 그만한 말에도 흡족해하며, 곧 자리를 옮겨 나머지 벗들도 기쁘게 해 주려 했다. 방 안 모두가 이미 엿들었을 것이 분명한 그 소식을, 일부나마 들려주는 것으로 말이다.\n\n그가 모두의 기쁨을 당연한 것으로 여긴 것은 다행이었다. 그렇지 않았다면 그는 미스터 우드하우스도 미스터 나이틀리도 딱히 기뻐하지 않는다고 여겼을 테니까. 웨스턴 부인과 엠마 다음으로, 기쁜 소식을 들을 자격이 첫째가는 이들이 바로 그 두 사람이었다. 그들에게서 미스 페어팩스에게로 옮겨 가려 했으나, 그녀가 존 나이틀리와 너무도 깊은 대화에 빠져 있어 끼어들기에는 지나치게 노골적인 방해가 될 듯했다. 그러다 자신이 엘턴 부인 가까이 있고 그녀의 관심도 마침 비어 있음을 알고는, 그는 자연히 그 화제를 그녀와 더불어 꺼내기 시작했다.\n\n# 제2권 18장\n\n\"곧 제 아들을 소개해 드리는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있기를 바랍니다.\" 미스터 웨스턴이 말했다.\n\n엘턴 부인은 그러한 바람에 자기를 향한 각별한 찬사가 담겨 있다고 기꺼이 여기며, 더없이 우아하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"프랭크 처칠이라는 사람 이야기는 들으셨겠지요.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"제 성을 쓰지는 않지만 제 아들이라는 것도 아실 테고요.\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요. 그분과 알고 지내게 되면 정말 기쁘겠어요. 미스터 엘턴도 분명 지체 없이 찾아가 인사할 거예요. 그리고 우리 둘 다 그분을 목사관에서 뵙게 되면 무척 기쁘겠지요.\"\n\n\"참으로 친절하시군요. 프랭크도 분명 더없이 기뻐할 겁니다. 그 애가 다음 주, 어쩌면 그보다 더 일찍 런던에 옵니다. 오늘 온 편지에서 그 소식을 들었어요. 오늘 아침에 오는 길에 편지를 받았는데, 아들 글씨가 보이기에 외람되이 뜯어 보았지요. 제게 온 게 아니라 웨스턴 부인에게 온 것이었는데도요. 그 애의 주된 편지 상대는 아내랍니다, 정말이지. 저는 편지를 받는 일이 거의 없어요.\"\n\n\"그래서 부인께 온 편지를 정말로 뜯어 보셨다는 거예요! 어머나, 미스터 웨스턴!(꾸민 듯이 웃으며) 그건 두고 볼 수 없는 일인데요. 정말이지 무척이나 위험한 선례라고요! 부디 이웃들이 그 본을 따르게 두지는 마세요. 정말이지, 이게 제가 각오해야 할 일이라면, 우리 결혼한 여자들도 분발하기 시작해야겠어요! 어머나, 미스터 웨스턴, 그런 분이신 줄은 정말 몰랐어요!\"\n\n\"아, 우리 남자들이란 한심한 작자들이지요. 부인께서도 조심하셔야 합니다, 엘턴 부인. 이 편지에 적혀 있기로는—짧은 편지인데, 급히 쓴 거라 그저 소식만 알리는 거지요—처칠 부인 때문에 다들 곧장 런던으로 올라온다는군요. 부인이 겨우내 몸이 좋지 않았는데, 엔스컴이 너무 춥다고 여기는 모양입니다. 그래서 다들 지체 없이 남쪽으로 옮긴다고요.\"\n\n\"어머나! 요크셔에서 말이지요. 엔스컴이 요크셔에 있던가요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 런던에서 백구십 마일쯤 떨어진 곳이니, 상당한 여정이지요.\"\n\n\"네, 정말이지 상당하네요. 메이플 그로브에서 런던까지보다 육십오 마일이나 더 머니까요. 하지만 미스터 웨스턴, 큰 재산을 가진 사람들에게 거리가 무슨 대수겠어요? 우리 형부 미스터 서클링이 가끔 얼마나 날아다니듯 다니는지 들으시면 깜짝 놀라실걸요. 믿기 어려우시겠지만, 한 주에 두 번이나 형부와 미스터 브래그가 네 마리 말을 매고 런던을 다녀왔답니다.\"\n\n\"엔스컴이 머니까 안 좋은 점은,\" 미스터 웨스턴이 말했다. \"우리가 듣기로 처칠 부인이 일주일 내내 소파에서 일어나지도 못한다는 겁니다. 프랭크가 보낸 지난번 편지에 따르면, 부인이 너무 기운이 없어 조카와 외삼촌 양쪽 팔을 다 붙들지 않고서는 온실에도 못 들어가겠다고 푸념했다지 뭡니까! 이 정도면, 아시겠지만, 어지간히 쇠약하다는 얘기지요. 그런데 이제는 런던에 오고 싶어 어찌나 안달인지, 길에서는 딱 두 밤만 자고 오겠답니다. 프랭크가 그렇게 써 보냈어요. 정말이지 연약한 부인들의 체질이란 참 별나기도 하지요, 엘턴 부인. 그건 인정해 주셔야 합니다.\"\n\n\"아니요, 정말이지 저는 아무것도 인정 못 해요. 저는 늘 우리 여자 편을 든답니다. 정말 그래요. 미리 말씀드리는데, 그 점에서는 제가 만만찮은 적수라는 걸 아시게 될 거예요. 저는 언제나 여자들 편을 들거든요. 그리고 정말이지, 셀리나가 여관에서 자는 일을 두고 어떻게 여기는지 아신다면, 처칠 부인이 그걸 피하려고 믿기 힘든 노력을 한다 해도 놀라지 않으실 거예요. 셀리나는 그게 정말 끔찍하다고 한답니다. 그리고 저도 그 까다로움을 조금 물려받은 것 같아요. 언니는 늘 자기 침대보를 챙겨 다니거든요. 아주 훌륭한 대비책이지요. 처칠 부인도 그렇게 하시나요?\"\n\n\"틀림없이 처칠 부인은 다른 어떤 귀부인이 한 일이든 빠짐없이 다 합니다. 처칠 부인은 이 나라 어떤 부인에게도 뒤지지 않을 분이지요—\"\n\n엘턴 부인이 다급하게 말을 가로챘다.\n\n\"어머, 미스터 웨스턴, 오해는 마세요. 셀리나는 정말이지 귀부인 행세를 하는 사람이 아니랍니다. 그런 식으로 넘겨짚지 마세요.\"\n\n\"아니라고요? 그렇다면 처칠 부인의 기준은 못 되겠군요. 그 사람은 누가 봐도 더없이 철저한 귀부인 행세를 하는 분이니 말입니다.\"\n\n엘턴 부인은 자기가 그렇게 열을 올려 부인한 것이 잘못이었다는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 언니가 귀부인답지 않은 사람이라고 믿게 만드는 것은 결코 그녀의 본뜻이 아니었다. 어쩌면 그렇게 둘러대는 데에는 패기가 모자랐는지도 몰랐다. 그래서 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 말을 거둬들일까 궁리하고 있는데, 미스터 웨스턴이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"짐작하셨겠지만, 처칠 부인은 제 마음에 그리 드는 사람이 아닙니다—하지만 이건 우리끼리만 하는 얘기예요. 부인은 프랭크를 무척 아끼니, 저로서는 그 사람을 험담하고 싶지 않습니다. 게다가 지금은 몸도 성치 않고요. 하기야 본인 말로는 늘 그래 왔다지만요. 아무에게나 할 말은 아닙니다만, 엘턴 부인, 저는 처칠 부인의 병이라는 걸 그다지 믿지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"정말 아프시다면, 어째서 바스에 가지 않으시는 거지요, 미스터 웨스턴? 바스나 클리프턴 같은 데로요.\" \"엔스컴이 너무 춥다고 머릿속에 박혀 버린 거지요. 실은, 제 생각엔 엔스컴이 지겨워진 겁니다. 이번에는 전에 없이 그곳에 오래 붙박여 있었으니, 이제 변화가 그리워지기 시작한 거지요. 외진 곳이거든요. 좋은 곳이긴 하지만 무척 외집니다.\"\n\n\"네—메이플 그로브처럼요, 분명히요. 메이플 그로브만큼 큰길에서 외떨어진 데도 없을 거예요. 사방을 둘러싼 그 어마어마한 숲이라니! 모든 것에서 차단된 듯한 느낌이에요—더없이 완벽한 은거지요. 그리고 처칠 부인은 아마 셀리나 같은 건강이나 활기가 없어서 그런 식의 칩거를 즐기지 못하는 거겠지요. 아니면, 시골살이에 어울릴 만큼 자기 안에 즐길 거리가 충분치 않은지도 모르고요. 저는 늘 말하지만, 여자는 즐길 거리가 아무리 많아도 지나치지 않거든요—그리고 저는 사교계에 전혀 기대지 않아도 될 만큼 제 안에 즐길 거리가 그렇게 많다는 게 정말 고맙답니다.\"\n\n\"프랭크가 이월에 두 주 동안 다녀갔습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 그 얘기는 들은 기억이 있어요. 그분이 다시 오시면 하이베리 사교계에 한 사람이 더 늘어 있는 걸 보게 되겠지요. 제가 감히 저 자신을 한 사람 더 늘었다고 칭할 수 있다면 말이에요. 하지만 어쩌면 그분은 세상에 저 같은 사람이 있다는 것조차 들어 본 적 없을지도 모르겠네요.\"\n\n이것은 찬사를 청하는 부름치고는 너무 노골적이어서 그냥 넘길 수가 없었으니, 미스터 웨스턴은 아주 점잖은 태도로 곧장 큰 소리로 답했다.\n\n\"부인! 부인 말고는 누구도 그런 일이 가능하리라고 상상하지 못할 겁니다. 부인을 못 들어 봤다니요! 요즘 웨스턴 부인의 편지는 엘턴 부인 이야기 말고는 거의 다른 얘기가 없는 것 같던데요.\"\n\n그는 할 도리를 다했으니 이제 다시 아들 이야기로 돌아갈 수 있었다.\n\n\"프랭크가 우리를 떠날 때만 해도,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그 애를 언제 다시 볼 수 있을지 도무지 알 수 없었어요. 그래서 오늘 소식이 갑절로 반가운 거지요. 정말이지 전혀 뜻밖이었습니다. 하기야, 저는 늘 그 애가 곧 다시 오리라고 강하게 믿었어요. 분명 무언가 좋은 일이 생기리라 확신했지요—그런데 아무도 제 말을 믿지 않았답니다. 그 애도 웨스턴 부인도 둘 다 끔찍하게 풀이 죽어 있었어요. '그 애가 어떻게 올 방도를 만들겠어요? 게다가 외삼촌 내외가 또 그 애를 보내 줄 리가 있겠어요?' 어쩌고 하면서요. 저는 늘 우리에게 유리한 무언가가 일어나리라 느꼈는데, 보세요, 정말 그렇게 됐지요. 엘턴 부인, 저는 살아오면서 이걸 깨달았답니다. 어느 달에 일이 어긋나게 돌아가면, 다음 달에는 반드시 나아진다는 것을요.\"\n\n\"맞는 말씀이에요, 미스터 웨스턴, 정말 맞는 말씀이에요. 그건 바로 제가 구애받던 시절에 어떤 신사분께 늘 하던 말이거든요. 그분은 일이 영 뜻대로 풀리지 않으면, 자기 마음에 맞을 만큼 빠르게 진행되지 않으면 곧잘 절망에 빠져서는, 이런 식이라면 우리한테 결혼의 신이 샛노란 예복을 입혀 줄 때쯤이면 분명 오월은 되어 있을 거라고 부르짖곤 했답니다. 아유, 그 우울한 생각을 떨쳐 내고 더 밝은 쪽을 보게 해 드리느라 제가 얼마나 애를 썼는지! 마차—마차 일로도 실망스러운 일이 있었어요. 어느 날 아침, 그분이 아주 절망에 빠진 얼굴로 제게 찾아왔던 게 기억나네요.\"\n* 결혼의 신(Hymen): 그리스 신화의 혼인의 신. 샛노란 예복은 혼례를 상징한다.\n\n그녀가 살짝 기침을 하느라 말을 멈추자, 미스터 웨스턴이 그 틈을 놓치지 않고 즉시 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"오월 말씀을 하셨지요. 처칠 부인이 엔스컴보다 따뜻한 곳에서—요컨대 런던에서 지내라는 지시를 받았거나, 아니면 스스로 그러기로 한 달이 바로 오월입니다. 그러니 우리는 봄 내내 프랭크가 자주 찾아오리라는 흐뭇한 전망을 갖게 된 거지요—바로 그러기에 골라잡았음 직한 계절이에요. 해가 거의 가장 길고, 날씨도 온화하고 쾌적해서 늘 밖으로 나가고 싶어지고, 그렇다고 거닐기에 너무 덥지도 않으니까요. 그 애가 지난번 왔을 때는 그런대로 잘 보냈습니다만, 비가 오고 축축하고 음울한 날씨가 많았지요. 아시다시피 이월에는 늘 그렇잖아요. 그래서 마음먹은 것의 절반도 못 했어요. 이제야말로 제때인 거지요. 이번에는 온전히 즐길 수 있을 겁니다. 게다가 엘턴 부인, 저는 잘 모르겠어요. 그 애가 정말로 집에 와 있는 것보다, 만남이 불확실한 채로 오늘이나 내일, 그것도 아무 때고 그 애가 들어설지 모른다고 늘 기대하는 그런 마음이 행복에 더 좋은 것은 아닐까 하고요. 제 생각엔 그런 것 같아요. 바로 그런 마음 상태가 사람에게 가장 큰 활기와 기쁨을 주는 것 같거든요. 부디 제 아들을 마음에 들어 하셨으면 합니다. 하지만 무슨 신동을 기대하시면 안 됩니다. 흔히들 그 애를 훌륭한 청년이라고 하지만, 신동을 기대하시진 마세요. 웨스턴 부인이 그 애를 무척이나 편애하는데, 짐작하시겠지만 그게 제겐 더없이 흐뭇한 일이지요. 아내는 그 애만 한 사람이 없다고 여긴답니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 분명히 말씀드리는데, 미스터 웨스턴, 제 평가도 분명 그분에게 호의적일 거라는 데 거의 의심이 없어요. 미스터 프랭크 처칠을 칭찬하는 말을 어찌나 많이 들었는지요. 그렇지만 한 가지 짚어 두는 게 공정하겠는데, 저는 늘 스스로 판단하는 사람이지, 결코 남의 말에 맹목적으로 끌려가는 사람이 아니거든요. 미리 말씀드리지만, 저는 그분의 됨됨이를 직접 본 그대로 판단할 거예요. 저는 아첨하는 사람이 아니니까요.\"\n\n미스터 웨스턴은 생각에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n\"제가,\" 이윽고 그가 말했다. \"가엾은 처칠 부인을 너무 모질게 말한 건 아닌지 모르겠군요. 정말로 아픈 거라면 부당한 소리를 한 게 미안할 노릇이지요. 하지만 그 사람 성격에는 제가 바라는 만큼 너그럽게 말하기 어렵게 만드는 구석들이 있어요. 엘턴 부인, 제가 그 집안과 어떤 인연이 있는지, 또 제가 어떤 대접을 받았는지 모르시진 않을 겁니다. 그런데 우리끼리 얘기지만, 그 모든 허물은 부인 탓이에요. 그녀가 부추긴 거지요. 그녀만 아니었으면 프랭크의 어머니가 그렇게 푸대접받는 일은 결코 없었을 겁니다. 미스터 처칠도 자존심이 있지요. 하지만 그 사람 자존심은 아내 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니에요. 그 사람 것은 누구도 해치지 않는, 그저 자기를 조금 무력하고 성가시게 만들 뿐인 조용하고 굼뜬 신사다운 자존심이지요. 하지만 그녀의 자존심은 오만이요 무례랍니다! 게다가 더더욱 참아 주기 어려운 건, 그녀에게는 내세울 만한 가문이나 혈통이 없다는 거예요. 미스터 처칠이 결혼할 때만 해도 그녀는 변변찮은 사람, 그저 한 신사의 딸일 뿐이었지요. 그런데 처칠 집안 사람이 된 뒤로는 거드름과 콧대를 내세우는 데 그 집안 누구보다 더 처칠다워졌답니다. 하지만 본디는, 정말이지, 벼락출세한 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"세상에! 어머나, 그건 한없이 화나는 일이겠어요! 저는 벼락출세한 사람이라면 정말 질색이거든요. 메이플 그로브 덕에 그런 부류라면 진절머리가 났답니다. 우리 동네에도 한 집안이 있는데, 어찌나 거드름을 피우는지 형부와 언니가 골치를 앓는다니까요! 처칠 부인 얘기를 들으니 당장 그 사람들이 떠오르네요. 텁먼이라는 사람들인데, 아주 최근에 그곳에 정착했고, 변변찮은 연줄을 잔뜩 짊어진 주제에 엄청나게 거드름을 피우면서, 오래전부터 자리 잡은 명문가들과 어깨를 나란히 하겠다고 기대한답니다. 웨스트 홀에 산 게 길어 봐야 일 년 반인데, 어떻게 재산을 모았는지는 아무도 몰라요. 버밍엄에서 왔다는데, 아시잖아요, 미스터 웨스턴, 버밍엄이란 데가 뭐 그리 기대할 만한 곳은 못 되지요. 버밍엄이라고 하면 큰 기대를 안 하게 되니까요. 저는 늘 그 이름엔 무언가 불길한 데가 있다고 말한답니다. 그런데도 텁먼 집안에 대해 확실히 알려진 건 그 이상 없어요. 정말이지 미심쩍은 게 한둘이 아니지만요. 그러면서도 그 행동거지를 보면 자기들이 형부 미스터 서클링과도 대등하다고 여기는 게 분명하다니까요. 하필이면 형부가 그 사람들과 가장 가까운 이웃 가운데 하나거든요. 정말이지 너무 못 봐줄 일이에요. 형부는 메이플 그로브에 십일 년이나 산 사람이고, 그 전에는 형부 아버님이 그곳을 갖고 계셨는데—적어도 제가 알기로는—거의 확실한데, 돌아가신 미스터 서클링 어른이 임종 전에 매입을 다 끝내셨거든요.\"\n\n이때 두 사람의 대화가 끊겼다. 차가 돌고 있었고, 미스터 웨스턴은 하고 싶은 말을 다 했던 터라 곧 자리를 떠날 기회를 잡았다.\n\n차를 마신 뒤, 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인, 그리고 미스터 엘턴이 미스터 우드하우스와 함께 카드놀이 자리에 앉았다. 남은 다섯은 저희끼리 알아서 시간을 보내게 되었는데, 엠마는 그들이 그리 잘 어울릴 것 같지 않다고 미심쩍어했다. 미스터 나이틀리는 대화에 별 흥미가 없어 보였고, 엘턴 부인은 관심을 받고 싶어 했으나 누구도 기울일 마음이 없었으며, 엠마 자신은 차라리 입을 다물고 싶을 만큼 심란한 상태였던 것이다.\n\n미스터 존 나이틀리가 형보다 말이 많은 사람임이 드러났다. 그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 떠날 참이었기에, 곧 이렇게 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"자, 엠마, 아이들에 관해서는 더 할 말이 없는 것 같네. 하지만 처제한테는 언니 편지가 있으니, 거기에 빠짐없이 다 적혀 있을 게 틀림없어. 내가 당부할 거라면 언니 것보다 훨씬 간단할 테고, 아마 결도 그리 같지 않을 거야. 내가 권하고 싶은 건 다 합쳐서 이거뿐이거든. 버릇 망쳐 놓지 말고, 약 함부로 먹이지 말 것.\"\n\n\"두 분 다 만족시켜 드릴 수 있으면 좋겠네요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"저는 힘닿는 데까지 아이들을 즐겁게 해 줄 작정이니, 그거면 언니한테는 충분할 거예요. 그리고 즐거우면 어설픈 응석받이질이나 약 먹이는 일은 끼어들 틈이 없을 테고요.\"\n\n\"그리고 아이들이 성가시다 싶으면, 다시 집으로 돌려보내야 하네.\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있겠네요. 그러실 거라고 생각하시는군요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"아이들이 처제 아버님께 너무 시끄러울 수도 있고—아니면 처제한테도 짐이 될 수 있다는 걸 내가 모르진 않거든. 요즘처럼 처제 사교 약속이 계속 늘어난다면 말이야.\"\n\n\"늘어난다고요!\"\n\n\"그럼. 지난 반년 동안 처제 생활 방식에 큰 변화가 생겼다는 건 처제도 느낄 텐데.\"\n\n\"변화라뇨! 아니요, 정말이지 그렇지 않은데요.\"\n\n\"전보다 사람들과 훨씬 자주 어울리게 된 건 의심할 여지가 없지. 바로 지금만 봐도 그래. 나는 겨우 하루 묵으러 내려온 건데, 처제는 만찬 약속에 매여 있잖아! 전에 언제 이런 일이, 아니 이 비슷한 일이라도 있었나? 동네에 사람이 늘어나니 처제도 더 어울리는 거지. 얼마 전까지만 해도 언니한테 가는 편지마다 새로운 놀이 이야기가 실려 왔어. 미스터 콜네 만찬이라든가, 크라운 여관의 무도회라든가 말이야. 랜덜스가, 오직 랜덜스 하나가 처제 생활에 만들어 낸 차이가 얼마나 큰데.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 그의 형이 얼른 말했다. \"다 랜덜스가 한 일이지.\"\n\n\"좋아—그런데 짐작건대 랜덜스가 앞으로도 지금보다 영향력이 줄어들 것 같지는 않으니, 엠마, 헨리와 존이 가끔 거치적거릴 수도 있겠다는 생각이 들어. 그렇거든, 그저 아이들을 집으로 돌려보내 주기만 하면 돼.\"\n\n\"아니,\" 미스터 나이틀리가 외쳤다. \"그럴 필요까진 없네. 그 애들은 돈웰로 보내면 돼. 나는 분명 한가할 테니까.\"\n\n\"어머, 정말,\" 엠마가 큰 소리로 말했다. \"재미있으시네요! 제 그 숱한 약속들 가운데 형부가 함께하지 않은 자리가 도대체 몇이나 되는지 알고 싶어지네요. 그리고 어째서 제가 아이들 돌볼 짬을 못 낼 위험에 빠졌다고들 여기시는지도요. 그 어마어마하다는 제 약속이란 게 다 뭐였죠? 콜네에서 한 번 만찬을 들었고—무도회 얘기가 나왔다가 끝내 열리지도 않았고요. 형부 마음은 알겠어요.(미스터 존 나이틀리에게 고개를 끄덕이며) 한꺼번에 그 많은 친구분들을 여기서 만난 그 행운이 형부에겐 너무 기뻐서 그냥 넘기실 수가 없는 거죠. 하지만 미스터 나이틀리는,(그를 돌아보며) 제가 두 시간 넘게 하트필드를 비우는 일이 얼마나, 얼마나 드문지 잘 아시면서, 어째서 제게 그런 줄줄이 이어지는 흥청거림을 점치시는지 도무지 모르겠네요. 그리고 우리 귀여운 조카들로 말하자면, 이건 꼭 말해야겠어요. 엠마 이모가 그 애들 돌볼 시간이 없다면, 나이틀리 큰아버지한테 맡긴들 그리 나을 것 같지는 않은데요. 큰아버지는 이모가 한 시간 집을 비울 때 다섯 시간쯤 집을 비우는 분이고—집에 있을 때조차 혼자 책을 읽거나 장부 정리나 하는 분이니까요.\"\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 웃지 않으려 애쓰는 듯했다. 그리고 엘턴 부인이 그에게 말을 걸기 시작하자, 어렵잖게 그러는 데 성공했다.\n\n# 제3권 1장\n\n아주 잠깐의 차분한 성찰만으로도 엠마는, 프랭크 처칠에 관한 이 소식을 듣고 자신이 동요한 까닭이 무엇인지 충분히 가늠할 수 있었다. 그녀는 곧, 자기 자신을 위해 불안하거나 거북함을 느끼는 것이 결코 아니라는 데 생각이 미쳤다 — 그것은 그를 위한 것이었다. 그녀 자신의 연정은 실로 아무것도 아닌 정도로 가라앉아 있었으니, 생각할 가치조차 없었다. 그러나 둘 중에서 의심할 바 없이 늘 훨씬 더 깊이 빠져 있던 쪽이 그였는데, 그가 떠날 때 지녔던 그 뜨거운 마음 그대로 돌아온다면, 그것은 무척 곤란한 일이 될 터였다. 두 달의 이별이 그를 식히지 못했다면, 그녀 앞에는 위험과 화근이 놓여 있는 셈이었다. 그를 위해서도 자신을 위해서도 신중함이 필요했다. 그녀는 자기 마음이 다시 얽히게 둘 생각이 없었고, 그러니 그의 마음을 부추기는 어떤 일도 피해야 할 의무가 있었다.\n\n그녀는 그가 끝내 분명한 고백을 하는 데까지 이르지 않게 막을 수 있기를 바랐다. 그렇게 된다면 지금 두 사람 사이의 교제에 더없이 괴로운 결말이 될 테니까! 그러면서도 그녀는 무언가 결정적인 일이 닥치리라는 예감을 떨칠 수 없었다. 마치 이 봄이 어떤 고비를, 어떤 사건을, 지금의 차분하고 평온한 상태를 뒤흔들 무언가를 가져오지 않고는 지나가지 않을 것만 같았다.\n\n프랭크 처칠의 심정에 대해 어떤 판단이라도 내릴 수 있게 되기까지는, 미스터 웨스턴이 내다본 것보다는 좀 더 길었으나 그리 오래 걸리지는 않았다. 엔스컴 일가는 짐작했던 것만큼 빨리 런던에 올라오지 못했으나, 그는 그 직후 곧 하이베리에 와 있었다. 그는 두어 시간을 머무르려고 말을 타고 내려왔다. 아직은 그 이상을 할 수 없었던 것이다. 그러나 랜덜스에서 곧장 하트필드로 왔기에, 그녀는 특유의 날렵한 관찰력을 한껏 발휘하여 그가 어떤 마음에 놓여 있는지, 자신은 어떻게 처신해야 하는지를 재빨리 가늠할 수 있었다. 두 사람은 더없이 다정하게 만났다. 그가 그녀를 보고 크게 기뻐하는 것은 의심할 바 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 거의 그 즉시, 그가 예전만큼 자신을 마음에 두고 있는지, 같은 다정함을 같은 정도로 느끼고 있는지 의심이 들었다. 그녀는 그를 찬찬히 살폈다. 그가 예전만큼 사랑에 빠져 있지는 않다는 것이 분명했다. 떨어져 지낸 데다, 아마도 그녀가 무심하리라는 확신까지 더해져, 이 지극히 자연스럽고 지극히 바람직한 결과를 빚어낸 것이었다.\n\n그는 한껏 들떠 있었다. 여느 때처럼 말하고 웃을 채비가 되어 있었고, 지난번 방문 이야기를 하고 옛이야기를 되짚는 것을 즐거워하는 듯했다. 그렇다고 동요가 없는 것도 아니었다. 그녀가 그의 상대적인 무심함을 읽어 낸 것은 그의 침착함에서가 아니었다. 그는 침착하지 않았다. 마음이 흔들리고 있는 것이 역력했고, 어딘지 안절부절못하는 데가 있었다. 활기차기는 했으나, 그것은 스스로도 만족하지 못하는 활기 같았다. 그러나 이 문제에 대한 그녀의 믿음을 굳힌 것은, 그가 겨우 십오 분만 머물다가 하이베리의 다른 집들을 마저 방문하겠다며 서둘러 떠난 일이었다. \"지나오는 길에 거리에서 옛 지인 한 무리를 보았다고 — 멈추지는 않았고, 한마디 이상 멈출 생각도 없었지만 — 자기가 들르지 않으면 그들이 서운해하리라 생각하는 허영심이 있어서, 하트필드에 더 오래 머물고 싶은 마음이야 굴뚝같지만 서둘러 가 봐야겠다고.\" 그가 예전만큼 사랑에 빠져 있지 않다는 데는 의심이 없었다. 그러나 흔들리는 마음도, 서둘러 떠난 것도, 완전히 나았다는 표시처럼 보이지는 않았다. 차라리 그녀는, 그것이 그녀의 위력이 되살아날까 두려워하는 마음과, 곁에 오래 머물며 제 마음을 시험하지 않으려는 조심스러운 결심을 내비친 것이라고 여기는 쪽으로 기울었다.\n\n이것이 열흘 동안 프랭크 처칠이 한 유일한 방문이었다. 그는 자주 오고 싶다고, 올 작정이라고 했으나 — 늘 무언가에 가로막혔다. 그의 외숙모가 그를 곁에서 떠나보내려 하지 않았던 것이다. 랜덜스에서 그가 한 설명이 그러했다. 그가 정녕 진심이었다면, 정말로 오려고 애썼다면, 처칠 부인이 런던으로 거처를 옮긴 것이 그녀의 고집스럽거나 신경질적인 병증에는 아무 도움이 되지 못했다고 보아야 했다. 그녀가 정말로 앓고 있다는 것만은 아주 분명했다. 랜덜스에서 그는 그 점만은 확신한다고 단언했다. 많은 것이 기분 탓일 수는 있어도, 돌이켜 보면 그녀가 반년 전보다 더 쇠약한 상태에 있다는 것만은 의심할 수 없었다. 그는 그녀의 병이, 보살핌과 약으로 고칠 수 없는 무언가에서 비롯되었다고는, 혹은 적어도 그녀에게 앞으로 살아갈 세월이 여러 해 남아 있지 않으리라고는 믿지 않았다. 그러나 아버지가 아무리 의심하며 다그쳐도, 그녀의 호소가 그저 상상의 산물일 뿐이라거나 그녀가 예전만큼 정정하다고는 끝내 말하려 들지 않았다.\n\n머잖아 런던이 그녀에게 맞는 곳이 아니라는 것이 드러났다. 그녀는 그 소음을 견디지 못했다. 신경이 끊임없이 자극받고 시달렸다. 그리하여 열흘이 다 갈 무렵, 그 조카가 랜덜스로 보낸 편지에 계획이 바뀌었다는 소식이 담겼다. 그들은 곧장 리치먼드로 거처를 옮길 작정이었다. 처칠 부인은 그곳에 있는 어느 명망 높은 인물의 의술을 권유받았고, 게다가 그 고장을 마음에 들어 했다. 사람들이 선호하는 자리에 가구까지 갖춰진 집 한 채를 빌렸고, 이 변화로 큰 차도가 있으리라 기대했다.\n\n엠마는, 프랭크가 이 결정을 두고 더없이 들뜬 마음으로 편지를 썼으며, 앞으로 두 달을 그토록 많은 정든 벗들과 이웃처럼 가까이 지낼 수 있다는 축복을 마음껏 만끽하는 듯 보인다는 이야기를 들었다 — 그 집은 오월과 유월 두 달을 빌린 것이었다. 그녀는, 그가 이제 자기들과 자주, 거의 자신이 바랄 수 있는 만큼이나 자주 함께할 수 있으리라는 확신에 차서 편지를 썼다는 말을 들었다.\n\n엠마는 미스터 웨스턴이 이 즐거운 전망을 어떻게 받아들이는지 알아챘다. 그는 그 전망이 약속하는 온갖 행복의 원천이 그녀라고 여기고 있었다. 그녀는 그렇지 않기를 바랐다. 두 달이면 그 진위가 가려질 터였다.\n\n미스터 웨스턴 자신의 행복만은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그는 더할 나위 없이 기뻐했다. 바로 그가 바라마지않던 상황이었다. 이제 정말로 프랭크가 이웃에 있는 셈이 되는 것이다. 젊은이에게 구 마일이 무어란 말인가? 한 시간 말달리면 될 거리였다. 그는 늘 건너오게 될 터였다. 그 점에서 리치먼드와 런던의 차이는, 그를 늘 보느냐 도무지 못 보느냐 하는 차이를 통째로 가를 만큼 컸다. 십육 마일 — 아니, 십팔 마일 — 맨체스터 거리까지는 족히 십팔 마일은 되었으니 — 그것은 만만찮은 장애물이었다. 행여 그가 빠져나올 수 있다 한들, 오가는 데 하루가 다 갈 터였다. 그를 런던에 두는 것은 아무 위안이 못 되었다. 그럴 바엔 엔스컴에 있는 것과 다를 바 없었다. 그러나 리치먼드는 허물없이 오가기에 꼭 알맞은 거리였다. 더 가까운 것보다 차라리 나았다!\n\n이 이사 덕에 즉시 확실해진 좋은 일이 하나 있었으니, 크라운 여관에서 열기로 한 무도회였다. 전에도 잊고 있던 것은 아니었으나, 날을 잡으려 해 봐야 부질없다는 것이 곧 받아들여졌었다. 그러나 이제는 무슨 일이 있어도 열기로 했다. 모든 준비가 다시 시작되었고, 처칠 일가가 리치먼드로 옮긴 직후, 외숙모가 거처를 옮긴 덕에 벌써 한결 나아진 듯하며 언제든 정해 주는 날에 스물네 시간쯤은 틀림없이 자기들과 함께할 수 있으리라는 프랭크의 짧은 편지 몇 줄이 도착하자, 그들은 되도록 이른 날을 잡기로 했다.\n\n미스터 웨스턴의 무도회는 정말로 열리는 일이 되었다. 하이베리의 젊은이들과 행복 사이를 가로막은 것은 이제 며칠 안 되는 내일들뿐이었다.\n\n미스터 우드하우스는 단념하고 받아들였다. 그래도 계절 덕에 그 화근이 한결 덜어졌다. 오월은 무슨 일에든 이월보다 나았으니까. 베이츠 부인이 그 저녁을 하트필드에서 보내기로 약조되었고, 제임스에게도 미리 일러두었으며, 그는 사랑하는 엠마가 나가 있는 동안 사랑하는 어린 헨리에게도 사랑하는 어린 존에게도 아무 탈이 없기를 낙관하며 바랐다.\n\n# 제3권 2장\n\n다시는 무도회를 가로막을 불운이 일어나지 않았다. 날이 다가왔고, 마침내 그날이 되었다. 노심초사하며 지켜본 오전이 지나고, 프랭크 처칠은 평소처럼 제 자신에 대한 확신에 가득 차서 저녁 식사 전에 랜덜스에 닿았으며, 모든 것이 무사했다.\n\n그와 엠마 사이에는 아직 두 번째 만남이 없었다. 크라운 여관의 그 방이 그 만남을 지켜볼 참이었다 — 하지만 인파 속에서의 흔한 만남보다는 나을 터였다. 미스터 웨스턴은 다른 사람들이 오기 전에 방의 적절함과 안락함에 대한 그녀의 의견을 듣고자, 자기들 뒤를 따라 되도록 일찍 그곳에 도착해 달라고 어찌나 간곡히 청했는지, 그녀로서는 거절할 수 없었고, 따라서 그 젊은이와 함께 한동안 한가로운 시간을 보내야 했다. 그녀는 해리엇을 데려가기로 했고, 두 사람은 알맞은 시각에 크라운으로 마차를 몰고 갔는데, 랜덜스 일행은 딱 적당할 만큼 앞서 도착해 있었다.\n\n프랭크 처칠은 망을 보고 있었던 듯했다. 말은 많이 하지 않았으나, 그의 눈은 오늘 저녁을 즐겁게 보내려는 마음을 분명히 드러냈다. 그들은 다 함께 거닐며 모든 것이 제대로 되어 있는지 살폈고, 몇 분도 채 지나지 않아 다른 마차에 실려 온 사람들이 합류했다. 엠마는 처음 그 마차 소리를 듣고 크게 놀라지 않을 수 없었다. \"이렇게 터무니없이 일찍!\" 하고 그녀는 외칠 뻔했다. 그러나 곧 그들이 자신과 마찬가지로 미스터 웨스턴의 판단을 도우려고 특별한 청을 받아 온 오랜 벗들의 일가임을 알게 되었다. 그리고 그 뒤를 바짝 따라, 같은 용건으로 똑같이 각별한 간청을 받고 일찍 와 달라는 부탁을 들은 사촌들의 마차가 또 한 대 들어오니, 마치 예비 점검을 위해 손님의 절반이 머잖아 한자리에 모일 듯한 형국이었다.\n\n엠마는 미스터 웨스턴이 의지하는 취향이 자기 취향만이 아님을 알아차렸고, 이토록 많은 친지와 속내를 나누는 벗을 둔 사람의 총애와 친밀함을 받는다는 것이 허영의 척도에서 결코 으뜸가는 영예는 아님을 깨달았다. 그녀는 그의 시원시원한 태도를 좋아했지만, 흉금을 트는 마음이 조금 덜했더라면 그가 더 높은 인품의 인물이 되었으리라 — 두루 미치는 자애로움, 그러나 두루 미치는 우정은 아닌 것이 한 사람을 마땅한 사람으로 만든다 — 그녀는 그런 사람을 머릿속에 그려 볼 수 있었다. 일행은 모두 거닐며 둘러보고 또 칭찬했다. 그러고는 달리 할 일이 없자, 난롯가에 반원처럼 둘러서서, 다른 화제가 나올 때까지 저마다 제 방식으로 오월인데도 저녁의 난롯불이 여전히 무척 기분 좋다는 사실을 새겼다.\n\n엠마는 비밀 자문관의 수가 아직 더 많지 않은 것이 미스터 웨스턴의 탓이 아님을 알게 되었다. 그들은 베이츠 부인의 집 앞에 마차를 세우고 동승을 권했으나, 그 이모와 조카딸은 엘턴 부부가 데려오기로 되어 있었던 것이다.\n\n프랭크는 그녀 곁에 서 있었으나, 가만히 있지는 못했다. 마음이 편치 않음을 드러내는 안절부절못함이 있었다. 그는 두리번거리다가, 문가로 갔다가, 다른 마차 소리에 귀를 기울였다 — 어서 시작하고 싶어 안달이거나, 아니면 줄곧 그녀 곁에 있는 것이 두렵거나.\n\n엘턴 부인 이야기가 나왔다. \"곧 오시겠지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"엘턴 부인을 무척 보고 싶습니다. 워낙 많은 말을 들어서요. 머잖아 도착하실 것 같은데요.\"\n\n마차 소리가 들렸다. 그는 즉시 움직였으나, 도로 돌아오며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 그분과 안면이 없다는 걸 깜빡했군요. 미스터 엘턴도 엘턴 부인도 본 적이 없어요. 제가 나설 일이 아니지요.\"\n\n미스터 엘턴과 엘턴 부인이 나타났고, 온갖 미소와 격식 차린 인사가 오갔다.\n\n\"한데 미스 베이츠와 미스 페어팩스는요?\" 미스터 웨스턴이 둘러보며 말했다. \"두 분을 모셔 오시는 줄 알았는데요.\"\n\n착오는 사소한 것이었다. 마차가 지금 그들을 데리러 보내졌다. 엠마는 엘턴 부인에 대한 프랭크의 첫인상이 어떨지 못내 궁금했다. 그가 그녀의 공들인 옷차림과 우아한 미소에 어떤 영향을 받을지. 그는 소개가 끝나자마자 그녀에게 아주 적절한 관심을 기울이며, 곧바로 의견을 세울 채비에 들어갔다.\n\n몇 분 만에 마차가 돌아왔다. 누군가 비 이야기를 했다. \"우산이 있는지 제가 살피겠습니다, 아버지.\" 프랭크가 아버지에게 말했다. \"미스 베이츠를 잊으면 안 되지요.\" 그러고는 가 버렸다. 미스터 웨스턴이 따라가려 했으나, 엘턴 부인이 그를 붙들어 그의 아들에 대한 자기 의견으로 그를 흡족하게 해 주었다. 어찌나 빠르게 말을 시작했는지, 결코 느리게 걷지 않던 그 젊은이조차 그 말이 안 들리는 데까지는 거의 가지 못했을 정도였다.\n\n\"정말이지 아주 훌륭한 청년이에요, 미스터 웨스턴. 아시다시피 제가 솔직히 말씀드렸지요, 제 나름의 의견을 세우겠다고요. 그런데 그분이 정말 흡족하다고 말씀드리게 되어 기뻐요 — 제 말을 믿으셔도 돼요. 저는 결코 빈말로 추어주는 법이 없거든요. 아주 잘생긴 청년이라고 보고, 태도는 딱 제가 좋아하고 인정하는 그대로예요 — 거드름이나 건방진 데라곤 손톱만큼도 없이, 참으로 신사다워요. 아셔야 할 것이, 저는 건방진 풋내기들을 끔찍이 싫어해요 — 아주 질색이지요. 메이플 그로브에서는 그런 자들이 결코 용납되지 않았어요. 미스터 서클링도 저도 도무지 그런 자들을 못 참았고, 가끔 아주 신랄한 말을 하곤 했죠! 거의 흠이 될 만큼 온순한 셀리나는 그들을 훨씬 잘 견뎌 냈지만요.\"\n\n그녀가 그의 아들 이야기를 하는 동안에는 미스터 웨스턴의 주의가 단단히 묶여 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 메이플 그로브에 이르자, 그는 막 도착해 맞이해야 할 숙녀들이 있음을 떠올리고는 흐뭇한 미소를 지으며 서둘러 자리를 떴다.\n\n엘턴 부인은 웨스턴 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰다. \"미스 베이츠와 제인을 태운 게 우리 마차인 게 틀림없어요. 우리 마부와 말들이 어찌나 날쌘지요! — 우리가 누구보다 빨리 달리는 것 같아요 — 벗을 위해 제 마차를 보내는 게 얼마나 기쁜 일인지요! — 친절하게도 부인이 권하셨다는 건 알지만, 다음번엔 그러실 필요가 전혀 없을 거예요. 제가 늘 그 두 사람을 챙기리란 걸 믿어 의심치 마세요.\"\n\n미스 베이츠와 미스 페어팩스가 두 신사의 호위를 받으며 방으로 들어왔고, 엘턴 부인은 그들을 맞이하는 것이 웨스턴 부인 못지않게 제 본분이라 여기는 듯했다. 그녀의 몸짓과 거동은 엠마처럼 지켜보는 이라면 누구나 알아챌 만했다. 그러나 그녀의 말은, 아니 모두의 말은, 들어오면서부터 떠들기 시작해 난롯가 무리에 끼어든 뒤로도 몇 분이 지나도록 끝나지 않는 미스 베이츠의 끝없는 말줄기 아래 곧 묻혀 버렸다. 문이 열리면서 그녀의 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"어쩜 이렇게 친절하실까요! — 비는 전혀 안 와요. 별것 아니에요. 저야 아무래도 좋고요. 신발도 아주 두툼하고요. 게다가 제인이 그러는데 — 어머나! (문 안에 들어서자마자) 어머나! 이거 정말 눈부셔요! — 정말 훌륭해요! — 솜씨가 기막히네요, 정말이지. 부족한 게 하나도 없어요. 상상도 못 했어요 — 불도 이렇게 환하게! — 제인, 제인, 봐! 이런 거 본 적 있니? 어머! 미스터 웨스턴, 정말 알라딘의 램프라도 가지셨나 봐요. 착한 미시즈 스토크스도 자기 방을 못 알아보겠어요. 들어오면서 봤는데, 입구에 서 계시더군요. '어머! 미시즈 스토크스,' 했는데 — 더 말할 짬이 없었네요.\" 이제 웨스턴 부인이 그녀를 맞았다. \"아주 잘 지내요, 고마워요, 부인. 평안하시길 바라요. 그렇다니 정말 기뻐요. 두통이라도 나실까 봐 어찌나 걱정했는데요! — 그렇게 자주 지나다니시는 걸 보고, 또 얼마나 수고가 많으셨을지 아니까요. 정말로 그렇다니 기뻐요. 아! 엘턴 부인, 마차 정말 고마워요! — 시간도 딱 좋았고요. 제인이랑 저랑 다 채비가 돼 있었어요. 말들을 한순간도 세워 두지 않았답니다. 더없이 편안한 마차예요. — 어머! 그리고 그 일로 말하자면 우리가 웨스턴 부인께도 감사를 드려야 마땅하지요. 엘턴 부인이 친절하게도 제인에게 쪽지를 보내 주셔서 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 우리가 — 한데 하루에 그렇게 두 가지 호의를! — 이런 이웃들이 또 어디 있겠어요. 제가 어머니께 그랬어요, '정말이지, 어머니 —.' 고마워요, 어머니는 놀랄 만큼 잘 지내세요. 미스터 우드하우스 댁에 가셨어요. 숄을 꼭 두르시게 했죠 — 저녁이 따뜻하지가 않아서요 — 크고 새로 산 숄을요 — 딕슨 부인의 결혼 선물이에요. 우리 어머니를 그렇게 생각해 주다니 어쩜 그리 다정한지! 웨이머스에서 샀대요, 아시잖아요 — 미스터 딕슨이 고른 거고요. 제인이 그러는데, 그들이 한동안 망설인 다른 게 세 개 더 있었대요. 캠벨 대령은 올리브색을 더 좋아하셨고요. 얘 제인아, 정말 발은 안 젖었니? — 겨우 한두 방울이었지만 어찌나 걱정인지 — 한데 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 어찌나 극진하게 — 그리고 디딜 매트까지 깔려 있어서 — 그 극진한 친절을 저는 결코 잊지 못할 거예요. — 아! 미스터 프랭크 처칠, 말씀드려야겠는데, 그 뒤로 우리 어머니 안경이 한 번도 탈이 없어요. 대갈못이 다시는 빠지지 않았답니다. 어머니가 당신의 그 착한 마음씨를 자주 입에 올리세요. 그렇지, 제인? — 우리가 미스터 프랭크 처칠 이야기를 자주 하지 않니? — 아! 여기 미스 우드하우스가 계시네 — 미스 우드하우스, 안녕하세요? — 저야 아주 잘 지내요, 고마워요, 정말 잘요. 이건 그야말로 요정 나라에서의 만남이로군요! — 이런 변신이라니! — 칭찬은 하면 안 되는 거 알아요 (엠마를 더없이 흐뭇하게 바라보며) — 그건 무례한 일이니까요 — 하지만 정말이지, 미스 우드하우스, 정말 — 제인 머리는 어때 보여요? — 보는 눈이 있으시잖아요 — 자기가 다 했어요. 머리를 어쩜 이렇게 하는지 정말 놀라워요! — 런던 미용사도 이렇겐 못 할 거예요. — 아! 휴스 박사님이시네 — 그리고 휴스 부인. 잠깐 가서 휴스 박사 내외께 인사를 드려야겠어요. — 안녕하세요? 안녕하세요? — 아주 잘 지내요, 고마워요. 이거 정말 즐겁지 않아요? — 그런데 그 다정한 미스터 리처드는 어디 계시죠? — 아! 저기 계시네. 방해하지 말아요. 아가씨들과 이야기하는 게 훨씬 낫지요. 안녕하세요, 미스터 리처드? — 요전 날 마을을 말 타고 지나가시는 거 봤어요 — 어머, 미시즈 오트웨이 아니세요! — 그리고 착한 미스터 오트웨이, 미스 오트웨이, 미스 캐럴라인. — 친구분이 이렇게나 많이! — 그리고 미스터 조지에 미스터 아서까지! — 안녕하세요? 다들 안녕하세요? — 아주 잘 지내요, 정말 고마워요. 이보다 좋을 수 없어요. — 또 마차 소리 안 들려요? — 누구실까? — 아마 그 착한 콜 댁이겠죠. — 정말이지, 이런 친구분들 틈에 서 있다니 황홀해요! 게다가 이렇게 멋진 불까지! — 아주 노릇노릇 익겠어요. 커피는 됐어요, 고마워요 — 저는 커피를 절대 안 마셔서요. — 차나 조금, 이따가요, 부탁드려요 — 급할 거 없어요 — 어머! 여기 오네요. 뭐든 다 이렇게 훌륭해서야!\"\n\n프랭크 처칠은 엠마 곁의 제자리로 돌아왔다. 미스 베이츠가 잠잠해지자마자, 그녀는 조금 뒤쪽에 서 있던 엘턴 부인과 미스 페어팩스의 대화를 본의 아니게 엿듣게 되었다. 그는 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그도 함께 엿듣고 있는지 어떤지는 가늠할 수 없었다. 엘턴 부인은 제인의 옷차림과 자태에 대해 한참 칭찬을 늘어놓았고 — 그 칭찬은 아주 조용하고 점잖게 받아들여졌다 — 그러고 나서는 자기도 칭찬을 받고 싶어 하는 기색이 역력했다. \"제 드레스는 어때요? — 가두리 장식은 어때요? — 라이트가 제 머리를 어떻게 매만졌어요?\" 같은 여러 질문이 이어졌고, 모두 인내심 어린 정중함으로 답해졌다. 그러더니 엘턴 부인이 말했다. \"저만큼 옷차림에 마음을 안 쓰는 사람도 없을 거예요 — 하지만 이런 자리에서는, 모두의 눈이 이렇게 제게 쏠리고, 또 웨스턴 댁에 대한 예의로 보더라도 — 이 무도회를 주로 저를 기리려고 여는 게 틀림없으니까요 — 남들보다 못해 보이고 싶진 않아요. 그런데 보니 이 방에 진주를 한 사람은 저밖에 없네요. — 그래서, 프랭크 처칠이 춤 솜씨가 일품이라죠. — 우리 둘 춤이 잘 맞을지 두고 봐야겠어요. — 프랭크 처칠은 정말이지 훌륭한 청년이에요. 아주 마음에 들어요.\"\n\n이 순간 프랭크가 어찌나 활기차게 말을 시작했는지, 엠마는 그가 자기에 대한 칭찬을 엿듣고는 더 듣고 싶지 않은 것이라 짐작하지 않을 수 없었다. 두 부인의 목소리는 한동안 묻혀 버렸다가, 또 한 번 말이 멎으면서 엘턴 부인의 목소리가 다시 또렷이 앞으로 나섰다. 미스터 엘턴이 막 그들에게 합류한 참이었고, 그의 아내가 외치고 있었다.\n\n\"어머! 드디어 우리 숨어 있는 데를 찾아내셨군요? — 방금 제인에게 말하던 참이었어요, 당신이 우리 소식이 궁금해 안달하기 시작할 거라고요.\"\n\n\"제인이라!\" 프랭크 처칠이 놀라움과 불쾌함이 어린 얼굴로 되뇌었다. \"허물없기도 하지 — 하지만 미스 페어팩스가 싫어하지는 않나 보군요.\"\n\n\"엘턴 부인 어때요?\" 엠마가 속삭이며 물었다.\n\n\"전혀요.\"\n\n\"배은망덕하시네요.\"\n\n\"배은망덕이라니! — 무슨 뜻입니까?\" 그러더니 찌푸린 얼굴을 미소로 바꾸며 — \"아니, 말하지 마세요 — 무슨 뜻인지 알고 싶지 않습니다. — 우리 아버지는 어디 계시죠? — 우리는 언제 춤을 추기로 했나요?\"\n\n엠마는 그를 도무지 이해할 수 없었다. 그는 이상한 기분에 빠진 듯했다. 그는 아버지를 찾으러 가 버렸으나, 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인을 모두 데리고 금세 돌아왔다. 그가 그들과 마주친 곳에 작은 난처함이 있었고, 그것은 엠마 앞에 내놓아야 할 문제였다. 엘턴 부인에게 무도회의 첫 춤을 청해야 한다는 생각, 그녀가 그것을 기대하리라는 생각이 방금 웨스턴 부인에게 떠올랐던 것이다. 이는 엠마에게 그 영예를 안기려던 그들 모두의 바람과 어긋나는 것이었다. 엠마는 이 서글픈 진실을 의연하게 들었다.\n\n\"그럼 그분의 짝으로 누구를 마땅한 상대로 세워야 할까요?\" 미스터 웨스턴이 말했다. \"프랭크가 자기에게 청해야 한다고 여길 텐데요.\"\n\n프랭크는 곧바로 엠마를 돌아보며 그녀가 전에 한 약속을 들이댔고, 이미 짝이 정해진 몸이라고 큰소리쳤는데, 그의 아버지는 그에 더없이 흡족한 얼굴이었다. 그러고 보니 웨스턴 부인이 바라는 것은 미스터 웨스턴 본인이 엘턴 부인과 춤추는 것이었고, 그들의 용건은 그를 그렇게 하도록 설득하는 일을 돕는 것이었으니, 이는 꽤 금세 이루어졌다. 미스터 웨스턴과 엘턴 부인이 앞장서고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠과 미스 우드하우스가 뒤를 따랐다. 엠마는 늘 이 무도회가 유독 자기를 위한 것이라 여겨 왔건만, 엘턴 부인 다음 둘째 자리에 서는 것을 감수해야 했다. 결혼을 생각하게 만들기에 거의 충분한 일이었다. 이때만큼은 허영이 완전히 충족된 엘턴 부인이 단연 우위에 있었다. 프랭크 처칠과 시작할 작정이었던 그녀였지만, 그 바꿈으로 손해 볼 것은 없었던 것이다. 미스터 웨스턴이라면 아들보다 윗길일 수도 있었으니까. 그러나 이 작은 흠집에도 불구하고 엠마는 즐거움에 미소 짓고 있었다. 짜이고 있는 줄의 어엿한 길이를 보는 것이 기뻤고, 흔치 않은 흥겨움의 시간이 여러 시간이나 앞에 놓여 있음을 느끼는 것이 기뻤다. 다른 그 무엇보다 그녀를 어지럽힌 것은 미스터 나이틀리가 춤을 추지 않는다는 사실이었다. 저기 그가, 서 있어선 안 될 구경꾼들 틈에 끼어 있었다. 그는 춤을 추어야 마땅했다 — 한 판이 끝날 때까지 춤에 관심 있는 척하는 남편들이며 아버지들이며 휘스트 노름꾼들 틈에 제 몸을 두어선 안 될 일이었다 — 그렇게나 젊어 보이는데! 어쩌면 그가 선 그 자리만큼 그를 돋보이게 한 곳은 어디에도 없었으리라. 나이 든 남자들의 우람한 몸집과 굽은 어깨들 사이에서, 그의 훤칠하고 단단하고 꼿꼿한 풍채는 모두의 눈길을 끌 수밖에 없겠다고 엠마는 느꼈다. 그리고 자기 짝을 빼고는, 그 줄에 늘어선 젊은이들 가운데 그와 견줄 만한 이가 하나도 없었다. 그가 몇 걸음 다가왔는데, 그 몇 걸음만으로도, 그가 마음만 먹는다면 얼마나 신사다운 몸가짐으로, 얼마나 자연스러운 우아함으로 춤을 추었을지가 충분히 드러났다. 그의 눈길과 마주칠 때마다 그녀는 그가 미소 짓게 만들었다. 그러나 대개 그는 진지한 얼굴이었다. 그가 무도회장을 좀 더 좋아할 수 있다면, 또 프랭크 처칠을 좀 더 좋아할 수 있다면 좋으련만. 그는 자주 그녀를 살피는 듯했다. 그가 그녀의 춤을 생각한다고 우쭐대선 안 될 일이었지만, 설령 그가 그녀의 처신을 흠잡고 있다 해도 그녀는 두렵지 않았다. 그녀와 짝 사이에는 시시덕거림 비슷한 것이 전혀 없었다. 둘은 연인이라기보다는 쾌활하고 허물없는 친구에 가까워 보였다. 프랭크 처칠이 예전만큼 그녀를 마음에 두지 않는다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다.\n\n무도회는 즐겁게 진행되었다. 웨스턴 부인의 노심초사한 보살핌과 쉼 없는 마음 씀이 헛되지 않았다. 모두가 행복해 보였다. 무도회가 다 끝나고서야 좀처럼 주어지는 법인 \"즐거운 무도회\"라는 칭찬이, 이 무도회가 막 시작된 시점부터 거듭 쏟아졌다. 아주 중요하고 기록해 둘 만한 사건으로 말하자면, 이런 모임에서 으레 그렇듯 이 무도회도 그리 풍성하지는 않았다. 그러나 엠마가 무언가 의미 있다고 여긴 일이 하나 있었다. 만찬 전 마지막 두 춤이 시작되었는데, 해리엇에게 짝이 없었던 것이다 — 앉아 있는 유일한 아가씨였다. 게다가 지금껏 춤추는 이들의 수가 어찌나 딱 맞아떨어졌던지, 어떻게 짝 없는 사람이 있을 수 있는지 의아할 지경이었다! 그러나 엠마의 의아함은 곧, 어슬렁거리는 미스터 엘턴을 보면서 잦아들었다. 피할 수만 있다면 그는 해리엇에게 춤을 청하지 않을 터였다. 그녀는 분명히 그러리라 확신했고 — 그가 금방이라도 카드 방으로 달아나 버리기를 시시각각 기다리고 있었다.\n\n그러나 달아나는 것은 그의 계획이 아니었다. 그는 앉아 있는 이들이 모여 있는 쪽으로 와서 몇 사람과 말을 나누고, 그들 앞을 거닐었다 — 마치 제 자유로움을, 그리고 그것을 지키겠다는 결의를 과시하려는 듯이. 그는 더러 미스 스미스의 바로 앞에 서기도 하고, 그녀 가까이에 있는 이들과 말을 섞기도 하는 것을 빠뜨리지 않았다. 엠마는 그것을 보았다. 그녀는 아직 춤을 추지 않고, 줄의 맨 아래에서 위로 올라오는 차례를 밟던 중이라 둘러볼 여유가 있었고, 고개를 조금만 돌려도 그 모든 것이 보였다. 줄의 절반쯤 올라왔을 때 그 무리 전체가 정확히 그녀 등 뒤에 있게 되어 더는 눈으로 지켜보지 않기로 했다. 그러나 미스터 엘턴이 어찌나 가까이 있었던지, 바로 그때 그와 웨스턴 부인 사이에 오간 대화의 한 마디 한 마디가 다 들렸다. 그리고 바로 위쪽에 서 있던 그의 아내가 함께 듣기만 하는 것이 아니라 의미심장한 눈짓으로 그를 부추기기까지 한다는 것도 알아차렸다. 마음씨 곱고 다정한 웨스턴 부인이 자리에서 일어나 그에게로 가서 말했다. \"춤 안 추세요, 미스터 엘턴?\" 그러자 그가 선뜻 대답했다. \"더없이 기꺼이요, 웨스턴 부인, 부인이 저와 추어 주신다면요.\"\n\n\"저요! — 어머! 아니에요 — 저보다 나은 짝을 구해 드릴게요. 저는 춤을 못 춰서요.\"\n\n\"미시즈 길버트가 추고 싶어 하신다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"정말 큰 기쁨이겠지요 — 제가 이제 슬슬 늙은 유부남이 다 되어 가는 데다 춤출 날들도 다 지난 듯하긴 합니다만, 미시즈 길버트 같은 오랜 벗과 추는 일이라면 언제든 무척 기쁠 테니까요.\"\n\n\"미시즈 길버트는 춤출 생각이 없어요. 하지만 짝 없는 아가씨가 한 분 있는데, 그분이 춤추는 걸 보면 참 좋겠어요 — 미스 스미스요.\" \"미스 스미스라고요! — 아! — 못 봤네요. — 정말 친절하시군요 — 제가 늙은 유부남만 아니라면야. — 하지만 제 춤출 날들은 다 지났답니다, 웨스턴 부인. 양해해 주세요. 그 밖에 무엇이든 분부만 하시면 더없이 기꺼이 하겠지만 — 제 춤출 날들은 다 지났어요.\"\n\n웨스턴 부인은 더 말하지 않았다. 그녀가 얼마나 놀라고 무안한 심정으로 제자리로 돌아갔을지 엠마는 짐작이 갔다. 이것이 바로 미스터 엘턴이었다! 그 상냥하고 친절하고 점잖던 미스터 엘턴이! 그녀는 잠시 둘러보았다. 그는 조금 떨어진 곳에서 미스터 나이틀리와 합류해 본격적인 대화를 나눌 채비를 하고 있었고, 그러는 사이 그와 그의 아내 사이에는 한껏 신난 미소가 오갔다.\n\n그녀는 다시 보지 않으려 했다. 가슴이 화끈 달아올랐고, 얼굴도 그만큼 화끈거릴까 봐 두려웠다.\n\n다음 순간, 더 행복한 광경이 그녀의 눈을 사로잡았다 — 미스터 나이틀리가 해리엇을 줄로 이끌고 있었던 것이다! 그 순간만큼 그녀가 더 놀란 적도, 더 기뻤던 적도 좀처럼 없었다. 그녀는 해리엇을 위해서나 자신을 위해서나 온통 기쁨과 고마움뿐이었고, 어서 그에게 고마움을 전하고 싶었다. 말을 건네기에는 너무 멀리 있었지만, 다시 그의 눈길을 붙잡을 수 있게 되자마자 그녀의 표정이 많은 것을 말해 주었다.\n\n그의 춤 솜씨는 그녀가 믿어 온 그대로, 더없이 훌륭했다. 그리고 앞서의 그 모진 상황만 아니었다면, 또 그 행복한 표정으로 드러나는 더할 나위 없는 즐거움과 영광스러운 기분만 아니었다면, 해리엇은 거의 지나치게 운 좋은 사람처럼 보였을 것이다. 그것은 결코 그녀에게서 헛되지 않았다. 해리엇은 그 어느 때보다 높이 뛰어올랐고, 가운데로 그 어느 때보다 멀리 날아갔으며, 줄곧 미소를 흘렸다.\n\n미스터 엘턴은 카드 방으로 물러나, (엠마가 믿기로는) 아주 멍청한 꼴이 되어 있었다. 그가 그 아내만큼 철면피는 아니라고 그녀는 생각했지만, 점점 그 아내를 닮아 가고 있었다. 엘턴 부인은 자기 짝에게 들으라는 듯 이렇게 말하며 제 속내의 한 자락을 드러냈다.\n\n\"나이틀리가 가엾은 꼬마 미스 스미스를 딱하게 여겼나 봐요! — 정말이지 마음씨가 곱기도 하지.\"\n\n만찬이 알려졌다. 자리 이동이 시작되었다. 그리고 그 순간부터 미스 베이츠는, 식탁에 앉아 숟가락을 집어 들 때까지 끊김 없이 그 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"제인, 제인, 얘 제인아, 어디 있니? — 여기 네 어깨 망토 있다. 웨스턴 부인이 어깨 망토를 꼭 두르라고 신신당부하시는구나. 복도에 외풍이 들까 걱정된다고, 손은 다 써 놨어도 말이다 — 문 하나는 못질을 해 막고 — 매트도 잔뜩 깔고 — 얘 제인아, 그러니 정말 꼭 둘러야 한다. 미스터 처칠, 어머! 너무 친절하셔라! 어쩜 이렇게 잘 둘러 주시는지! — 정말 고마워요! 춤도 정말 훌륭하셨고요! — 그래, 얘야, 내가 말한 대로 집에 달려가서 할머니를 자리에 모셔다 드리고 다시 왔는데, 아무도 내가 없었던 걸 몰랐단다 — 말한 대로, 한마디 없이 슬쩍 다녀온 거지. 할머니는 아주 평안하셨어, 미스터 우드하우스와 멋진 저녁을 보내셨다지 뭐냐, 두런두런 이야기도 잔뜩 하고, 주사위 놀이도 하고 — 떠나오기 전에 아래층에서 차도 끓이고, 비스킷에 구운 사과에 포도주까지 드셨대. 주사위를 던지면 어찌나 잘 나오던지 운이 기막혔다는구나. 그리고 너에 대해 잔뜩 물으시더라, 어떻게 즐겁게 지냈는지, 짝은 누구였는지. '어머!' 했지, '제가 제인 자리를 가로채진 않을래요. 미스터 조지 오트웨이와 춤추는 거 보고 왔는데, 내일 자기 입으로 다 말씀드리고 싶을 거예요. 첫 짝은 미스터 엘턴이었고, 다음은 누가 청할지 모르겠지만, 어쩌면 미스터 윌리엄 콕스일지도요.' 미스터 처칠, 너무 친절하셔라. — 차라리 다른 분이 더 좋지 않으세요? — 저는 부축이 필요한 사람이 아닌데요. 정말 친절도 하셔라. 어머나, 한쪽 팔엔 제인을, 다른 쪽 팔엔 저를! — 잠깐, 잠깐, 조금 물러서요, 엘턴 부인이 가시잖아요. 다정한 엘턴 부인, 어쩜 저리 우아하실까! — 아름다운 레이스 좀 봐! — 이제 우리 모두 그 뒤를 따라가는 거예요. 정말 오늘 밤의 여왕이셔! — 자, 여기 복도네. 계단 두 칸이다, 제인, 두 칸 조심하렴. 어머! 아니네, 한 칸뿐이야. 이런, 분명 두 칸인 줄 알았는데. 참 이상하기도 하지! 두 칸이라고 굳게 믿었는데 한 칸뿐이라니. 이런 안락함과 멋은 정말 처음 봐요 — 사방에 촛불이고요. — 제인, 네 할머니 이야기를 하던 중이었지 — 그런데 좀 서운한 일이 있었단다. — 구운 사과랑 비스킷이 그것대로야 훌륭하지, 알잖니. 그런데 처음에 흉선 요리를 곱게 만든 프리카세하고 아스파라거스가 나왔거든. 한데 착한 미스터 우드하우스가 아스파라거스가 덜 삶긴 것 같다고 죄다 도로 물리신 거야. 그런데 할머니가 흉선이랑 아스파라거스보다 좋아하시는 게 없잖니 — 그래서 좀 서운해하셨지. 하지만 우리는 아무한테도 이 얘긴 않기로 했어, 행여 다정한 미스 우드하우스 귀에 들어가면 무척 마음 쓰실 테니까! — 어머, 이거 정말 눈부셔! 너무 놀라워요! 이런 건 상상도 못 했어요! — 이런 우아함에 이런 풍성함이라니! — 이런 건 정말 본 적이 없는데 — 자, 우린 어디 앉을까? 어디 앉을까? 아무 데나요, 제인만 외풍을 안 맞으면요. 제가 어디 앉느냐는 아무 상관없어요. 어머! 이쪽을 권하시는 거예요? — 글쎄요, 정말이지, 미스터 처칠 — 너무 좋은 자리 같긴 한데 — 하지만 좋으실 대로요. 이 댁에서 당신이 정해 주시는 거라면 틀릴 리가 없죠. 얘 제인아, 우리가 할머니께 이 요리들의 절반인들 어떻게 다 기억해 전해 드릴 수 있을까? 수프까지 있네! 어머나! 이렇게 빨리 받으면 안 되는데, 냄새가 너무 좋아서, 도저히 안 먹을 수가 없네요.\"\n\n엠마는 만찬이 끝날 때까지 미스터 나이틀리와 이야기할 기회가 없었다. 그러나 모두 다시 무도회장에 모이자, 그녀의 눈은 그가 자기에게 와서 고맙다는 인사를 받도록 거부할 수 없이 그를 청했다. 그는 미스터 엘턴의 처사를 격하게 나무랐다. 그것은 용서할 수 없는 무례였다. 그리고 엘턴 부인의 그 표정 또한 마땅한 몫의 비난을 받았다.\n\n\"그들이 노린 건 해리엇만 상처 입히려던 게 아니었네.\" 그가 말했다. \"엠마, 어째서 그들이 자네의 적이 된 거지?\"\n\n그는 꿰뚫어 보는 듯한 미소를 지으며 바라보았고, 아무 대답이 없자 덧붙였다. \"그자야 어떻든, 그 부인이 자네에게 화낼 까닭은 없을 것 같은데. 그 짐작에 자네야 물론 아무 말 않겠지. 하지만 엠마, 솔직히 인정하게나, 자네가 그를 해리엇과 맺어 주고 싶어 했다는 걸.\"\n\n\"그랬어요.\" 엠마가 대답했다. \"그래서 그들이 저를 용서 못 하는 거예요.\"\n\n그는 고개를 가로저었으나, 거기엔 너그러운 미소가 어려 있었고, 그저 이렇게만 말했다.\n\n\"꾸짖진 않겠네. 자네 자신의 반성에 맡겨 두지.\"\n\n\"이렇게 비위만 맞춰 주는 것들에게 저를 맡겨도 되겠어요? — 제 허영심이 제게 틀렸다고 일러 줄 일이 한 번이라도 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"자네 허영심이야 아니지. 하지만 자네의 진지한 마음이라면. — 한쪽이 자네를 그릇 이끌면, 다른 한쪽이 분명 그걸 일러 줄 걸세.\"\n\n\"미스터 엘턴에 대해서는 제가 완전히 잘못 봤다는 걸 인정해요. 당신이 알아채고 저는 못 알아챈 그 옹졸함이 그에게 있더군요. 그리고 저는 그가 해리엇을 사랑한다고 철석같이 믿었고요. 그게 다 일련의 묘한 착오들 탓이었어요!\"\n\n\"그렇게나 많이 인정해 준 데 대한 답례로, 나도 자네에게 공평하게 한마디 하지. 자네라면 그가 제 손으로 고른 것보다 더 나은 짝을 골라 주었으리란 걸 말일세. — 해리엇 스미스에게는 엘턴 부인에게 전혀 없는 일류의 자질이 몇 가지 있어. 꾸밈없고, 한결같고, 천진한 아가씨지 — 분별과 안목이 있는 남자라면 누구든 엘턴 부인 같은 여자보다 한없이 더 좋아할 만한 사람이야. 해리엇은 내가 생각했던 것보다 말이 잘 통하더군.\"\n\n엠마는 더없이 흐뭇했다. 두 사람의 대화는 미스터 웨스턴이 모두에게 다시 춤을 추자고 외쳐 대는 소란에 끊겼다.\n\n\"자, 미스 우드하우스, 미스 오트웨이, 미스 페어팩스, 다들 뭣들 하고 계세요? — 자, 엠마, 동무들에게 본을 보여 줘요. 다들 게을러 빠졌군요! 다들 잠들었어요!\"\n\n\"전 준비됐어요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"부르시기만 하면요.\"\n\n\"누구랑 춤출 건가?\" 미스터 나이틀리가 물었다.\n\n그녀는 잠시 머뭇거리다가 대답했다. \"당신과요, 청해 주신다면요.\"\n\n\"추겠나?\" 그가 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요. 당신이 춤출 줄 안다는 걸 보여 주셨잖아요. 게다가 우리가 사실 그렇게까지 오누이 사이는 아니니, 조금도 흠 될 게 없다는 거 아시잖아요.\"\n\n\"오누이라! 아무렴, 천만에.\"\n\n# 제3권 3장\n\n미스터 나이틀리와 나눈 이 작은 해명은 엠마에게 적잖은 즐거움을 안겨 주었다. 그것은 무도회의 기분 좋은 기억 가운데 하나여서, 이튿날 아침 그녀는 그 기억을 음미하려고 잔디밭을 거닐었다. 엘턴 부부에 관해 두 사람이 그토록 잘 통하는 결론에 이르렀다는 것이, 남편과 아내 양쪽에 대한 견해가 그토록 일치한다는 것이 그녀는 더없이 기뻤다. 그리고 미스터 나이틀리가 해리엇을 칭찬해 준 것, 해리엇 편을 들어 양보해 준 것은 유독 흐뭇한 일이었다. 잠깐 동안 저녁의 나머지를 망쳐 놓을 듯 위협했던 엘턴 부부의 무례함이, 오히려 그날 저녁의 가장 큰 만족 가운데 몇 가지를 안겨 준 계기가 된 것이다. 게다가 그녀는 또 하나의 행복한 결과를 — 해리엇의 헛된 연정이 치유되리라는 것을 — 내다보고 있었다. 무도회장을 나서기 전 해리엇이 그 일을 이야기하던 태도로 보아, 그녀에게는 강한 희망이 있었다. 마치 해리엇의 눈이 갑자기 뜨여, 미스터 엘턴이 자기가 믿어 온 만큼 빼어난 사람은 아니라는 것을 알아볼 수 있게 된 듯했다. 열병은 지나갔으니, 해로운 친절로 그 맥박이 다시 빨라질까 봐 엠마가 두려워할 일은 거의 없었다. 그 이상으로 필요할지 모를, 따끔한 냉대라는 가르침은 엘턴 부부의 고약한 심사가 알아서 마련해 주리라고 그녀는 믿었다. 해리엇은 제정신을 차렸고, 프랭크 처칠은 사랑에 지나치게 빠져 있지 않으며, 미스터 나이틀리는 그녀와 다툴 마음이 없으니, 앞으로의 여름이 얼마나 행복할 것인가!\n\n그날 아침에는 프랭크 처칠을 보지 못하게 되어 있었다. 그는 한낮까지는 집에 들어가야 하기에 하트필드에 들르는 즐거움을 누릴 수 없겠다고 그녀에게 말해 두었다. 그녀는 그것을 아쉬워하지 않았다.\n\n이 모든 일을 정리하고, 두루 살펴보고, 모두 제자리에 놓고 나서, 두 어린 사내아이의 성화와 그 외할아버지의 요구를 감당할 만큼 기운을 새로이 추스른 채 막 집 쪽으로 돌아서려던 참에, 커다란 철 대문이 열리며 두 사람이 들어섰다 — 함께 있으리라고는 도무지 예상치 못했던 두 사람이었다 — 프랭크 처칠과, 그의 팔에 기댄 해리엇 — 정말로 해리엇이었다! 무언가 심상치 않은 일이 일어났다는 것을 깨닫는 데는 한순간으로 충분했다. 해리엇은 얼굴이 하얗게 질려 겁에 질려 있었고, 그는 그녀를 달래려 애쓰고 있었다. 철 대문과 현관문 사이는 스무 야드도 채 안 되었으므로, 세 사람은 곧 현관 안으로 들어섰고, 해리엇은 그 즉시 의자에 풀썩 주저앉으며 기절하고 말았다.\n\n기절한 젊은 아가씨는 깨워야 하고, 물음에는 답해야 하며, 놀라운 일은 설명되어야 한다. 그런 사건은 무척 흥미롭지만, 그 조마조마함이 오래갈 수는 없다. 몇 분 만에 엠마는 사건의 전말을 알게 되었다.\n\n미스 스미스와, 무도회에도 와 있었던 고더드 부인 댁의 또 다른 기숙생 미스 비커튼은 함께 산책을 나가, 한 길로 — 리치먼드로 가는 길로 — 접어들었는데, 그 길은 겉보기에는 안전할 만큼 사람이 다니는 듯했으나 두 사람을 놀라게 하는 일로 이끌었다. 하이베리에서 반 마일쯤 벗어난 곳에서 길은 갑작스레 굽이졌고, 양쪽으로 느릅나무가 짙게 그늘을 드리워 꽤 긴 구간이 무척 외졌다. 두 아가씨가 그 길로 어느 정도 들어섰을 때, 바로 앞 조금 떨어진 곳, 길가의 더 너른 잔디밭에서 한 무리의 집시*를 별안간 발견했다. 망을 보던 한 아이가 구걸하러 두 사람에게 다가왔다. 잔뜩 겁먹은 미스 비커튼은 크게 비명을 지르고는 해리엇에게 따라오라 외치며 가파른 둑 위로 뛰어올라, 꼭대기의 나지막한 산울타리를 넘어, 지름길로 하이베리로 돌아가려고 죽을힘을 다해 달아났다. 그러나 가엾은 해리엇은 따라갈 수가 없었다. 춤을 춘 뒤로 쥐가 심하게 났던 터라, 둑을 오르려 처음 시도하자마자 다시 쥐가 도져 도무지 꼼짝할 수 없는 지경이 된 것이다. 이런 상태로, 몹시 겁에 질린 채, 그녀는 그 자리에 남아 있을 수밖에 없었다.\n* 집시: 19세기 영국에서 떠돌이 생활을 하던 사람들로, 당시엔 흔히 경계와 멸시의 대상이 되었다.\n\n두 아가씨가 좀 더 용감했더라면 그 떠돌이들이 어떻게 굴었을지는 알 수 없는 노릇이다. 그러나 그렇게 공격을 부추기는 빌미를 그들이 그냥 넘길 리 없었으니, 해리엇은 이내 대여섯 명의 아이들에게 둘러싸여 시달리게 되었다. 우두머리는 건장한 여인과 큰 사내아이였고, 모두가 떠들썩하니 눈빛이 무례했다 — 입으로 대놓고 욕한 것은 아니었지만. 점점 더 겁에 질린 해리엇은 곧장 돈을 주겠다고 약속하고는 지갑을 꺼내 1실링을 쥐여 주며, 더는 바라지 말고 자기를 해치지 말아 달라고 빌었다. 그제야 그녀는, 비록 더디게나마 걸을 수 있어 그 자리를 벗어나려 했다 — 그러나 그녀의 공포와 그녀의 지갑은 너무도 유혹적이어서, 더 달라고 요구하는 무리 전체가 그녀를 뒤따랐다, 아니 오히려 에워쌌다.\n\n프랭크 처칠이 그녀를 발견한 것은 바로 이런 상황에서였으니, 그녀는 벌벌 떨며 흥정하고 있었고, 그들은 큰소리로 무례하게 굴고 있었다. 더없이 다행스러운 우연으로, 하이베리를 떠나는 일이 미뤄진 덕에 그가 이 절체절명의 순간에 그녀를 도우러 온 것이었다. 아침이 하도 화창하여 그는 걸어서 먼저 가고, 말은 다른 길로 하여 하이베리에서 일이 마일 벗어난 곳에서 자기를 맞도록 해 둔 참이었다 — 게다가 전날 밤 미스 베이츠에게 가위 한 자루를 빌렸다가 돌려주는 것을 깜빡한 탓에, 그 집 문 앞에 멈춰 잠깐 들어갔다 와야 했다. 그래서 그는 작정했던 것보다 늦어졌고, 걸어서 온 까닭에 거의 코앞에 이르도록 무리 전체에게 들키지 않았다. 그 여인과 사내아이가 해리엇에게 안겨 주던 공포가 이번에는 고스란히 그들 차지가 되었다. 그가 떠나올 때 그들은 완전히 겁에 질려 있었다. 해리엇은 그에게 간절히 매달려 말조차 거의 못 하면서도, 기운이 아주 다하기 전에 하트필드까지 다다를 만큼의 힘은 겨우 남아 있었다. 그녀를 하트필드로 데려온 것은 그의 생각이었으니, 그는 다른 곳은 떠올리지 못했던 것이다.\n\n이것이 이야기 전체의 — 그가 전해 준 것과, 해리엇이 정신과 말을 되찾자마자 들려준 것의 — 골자였다. 그는 그녀가 무사한 것을 확인하는 데 필요한 시간 이상은 머물 엄두를 내지 못했다. 이런저런 지체들로 그에게는 더 허비할 일 분의 여유도 없었다. 엠마가 고더드 부인에게는 해리엇이 안전하다고 알리고, 미스터 나이틀리에게는 그런 무리가 인근에 있다는 것을 알려 주겠다고 약속하자, 그는 해리엇과 자신을 위해 그녀가 입에 담을 수 있는 온갖 고마운 축복의 말을 들으며 길을 나섰다.\n\n이와 같은 모험은 — 훌륭한 젊은 남자와 사랑스러운 젊은 여자가 그런 식으로 한데 던져진 일은 — 아무리 차가운 가슴과 흔들림 없는 머리라도 어떤 생각을 떠올리지 않을 수 없는 법이다. 적어도 엠마는 그렇게 생각했다. 그녀가 본 것을 보았다면, 두 사람이 함께 나타난 모습을 목격하고 그 사연을 들었다면, 어학자인들, 문법학자인들, 아니 수학자인들, 두 사람이 서로에게 유독 흥미로운 사이가 되도록 정황이 작용해 왔다고 느끼지 않을 수 있었겠는가? 하물며 그녀처럼 공상가인 사람이라면, 추측과 예견으로 얼마나 더 활활 타오르겠는가! 더구나 그녀의 머릿속에는 이미 그토록 든든한 기대의 밑바탕이 마련되어 있었으니 말이다.\n\n참으로 별난 일이었다! 그녀의 기억으로는, 이 고장의 어떤 젊은 아가씨에게도 이런 종류의 일이 일어난 적이 없었다. 이런 종류의 마주침도, 놀람도 없었다. 그런데 이제 그 일이, 바로 그 사람에게, 바로 그 시각에 일어난 것이다 — 마침 그 다른 한 사람이 그녀를 구하려고 그곳을 지나가던 바로 그때에! 정말이지 너무도 별난 일이었다! 게다가 그녀가 알고 있는 대로, 이 무렵 두 사람의 마음 상태가 다 같이 호의적이었던 터라 그 일은 그녀에게 더욱 강렬하게 다가왔다. 그는 그녀 자신에게 품은 연정을 극복하려 애쓰는 중이었고, 그녀는 미스터 엘턴을 향한 열병에서 막 회복하는 중이었다. 마치 모든 것이 하나로 어우러져 가장 흥미로운 결말을 약속하는 듯했다. 이 사건이 두 사람을 서로에게 강하게 권하지 않는다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이었다.\n\n해리엇이 잠시 의식이 흐릿했던 동안 그와 나눈 몇 분간의 대화에서, 그는 그녀의 공포를, 그녀의 순진함을, 자기 팔을 붙들고 매달리던 그 절절함을 이야기했는데, 즐거워하고 흐뭇해하는 감수성이 어려 있었다. 그리고 마지막에, 해리엇 본인의 이야기가 끝난 뒤에는, 미스 비커튼의 그 가증스러운 어리석음에 대해 더없이 격한 말로 분개를 드러냈다. 그러나 모든 것은 떠밀지도 거들지도 말고 자연스러운 물길을 따라가게 두어야 했다. 그녀는 한 발짝도 움직이지 않을 것이며, 한마디 귀띔도 흘리지 않을 것이었다. 안 돼, 끼어드는 일이라면 이제 진절머리가 났다. 계획을 품는 것쯤이야 — 그저 수동적인 계획이라면 — 해로울 게 없었다. 그것은 한낱 바람에 지나지 않았다. 그 이상으로는 결코 나아가지 않을 작정이었다.\n\n엠마의 첫 번째 결심은 아버지가 이 일을 알지 못하게 하는 것이었다 — 그것이 얼마나 큰 근심과 놀람을 불러일으킬지 알았기에. 그러나 감추는 일이 불가능하리라는 것을 그녀는 곧 깨달았다. 반 시간도 못 되어 하이베리 전체에 소문이 퍼졌다. 그것은 말 많은 사람들, 곧 젊은 축과 신분 낮은 축의 마음을 사로잡기에 꼭 알맞은 사건이었으니, 이 고장의 젊은이며 하인이며 할 것 없이 모두가 곧 그 무시무시한 소식의 행복에 젖었다. 지난밤의 무도회는 집시들 속에 묻혀 버린 듯했다. 가엾은 미스터 우드하우스는 앉은 채로 부들부들 떨었고, 엠마가 예상했던 대로, 다시는 관목 숲 밖으로 나가지 않겠다는 약속을 받기 전에는 좀처럼 진정하지 못했다. 그날 남은 시간 내내 자신과 미스 우드하우스의 안부를 (이웃들은 그가 안부를 받는 것을 좋아한다는 것을 알았다), 그리고 미스 스미스의 안부를 묻는 문의가 줄을 이은 것은 그에게 얼마간 위안이 되었다. 게다가 그는 다들 아주 시원찮다고 답하는 즐거움까지 누렸다 — 사실과 딱 맞는 말은 아니었지만, 엠마는 아주 멀쩡했고 해리엇도 별반 다르지 않았으니까 — 그래도 엠마는 거기에 끼어들지 않았다. 그런 분의 자식으로서는 딱하게도 그녀는 대체로 너무나 건강한 처지였으니, 몸이 편찮다는 게 무엇인지 도통 모르고 살았기 때문이다. 그러니 아버지가 그녀를 위해 병을 지어내 주지 않으면, 그녀는 안부 전언 속에서 변변한 구실도 못 할 판이었다.\n\n집시들은 사법의 손길이 미치기를 기다리지 않았다. 그들은 부랴부랴 떠나 버렸다. 하이베리의 젊은 아가씨들은 그 공포가 시작되기도 전에 다시 안전하게 산책할 수 있었을 것이고, 그 모든 이야기는 이내 엠마와 그녀의 조카들에게만 의미 있는, 대단찮은 일로 쪼그라들었다. 그러나 그녀의 상상 속에서는 그것이 여전히 자리를 지켰고, 헨리와 존은 날마다 해리엇과 집시들 이야기를 해 달라고 졸랐으며, 그녀가 처음 들려준 이야기에서 아주 사소한 대목이라도 어긋나면 끈질기게 바로잡아 주었다.\n\n# 제3권 4장\n\n이 작은 사건이 있고 며칠 지나지 않은 어느 날 아침, 해리엇이 손에 작은 꾸러미를 들고 엠마를 찾아왔다. 자리에 앉아 한참 머뭇거리던 그녀가 이렇게 입을 열었다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스—혹시 시간 괜찮으시면—드리고 싶은 말씀이 있어요—일종의 고백 같은 거요—그러고 나면, 아시잖아요, 다 끝나는 거예요.\"\n\n엠마는 적잖이 놀랐지만, 어서 말해 보라고 청했다. 해리엇의 태도에는 어떤 진지함이 서려 있어서, 말 못지않게 그 태도가 무언가 예사롭지 않은 일을 예감하게 했다.\n\n\"이 일에 대해 당신께 아무것도 숨기지 않는 게 제 도리이고, 정말이지 제 바람이기도 해요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"다행히 어떤 점에서 저는 완전히 달라진 사람이 됐으니, 당신도 그걸 아시고 흐뭇해하셔야 마땅하잖아요. 필요 이상으로 말하고 싶진 않아요—그동안 그렇게 휘둘렸던 게 너무 부끄러워서요. 그래도 제 말뜻은 아실 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래요,\" 엠마가 말했다. \"이해했으면 좋겠네요.\"\n\n\"제가 어떻게 그렇게 오랫동안 헛된 마음을 품을 수 있었을까요!……\" 해리엇이 격하게 외쳤다. \"미친 짓이었던 것 같아요! 이제는 그분에게서 특별한 구석이라곤 하나도 안 보여요.—그분을 마주치든 말든 상관없어요—다만 둘 중 굳이 고르라면 안 마주치는 쪽이 낫겠지만요—사실 그분을 피하려고 아무리 먼 길이라도 돌아갈 수 있어요—하지만 그분 부인은 조금도 부럽지 않아요. 예전처럼 그 사람을 우러러보지도, 부러워하지도 않고요. 매력적인 분인 건 맞겠죠, 다 좋아요, 그래도 성미가 아주 고약하고 무례한 사람 같아요—그날 밤 그 표정을 저는 결코 못 잊을 거예요!—하지만, 정말이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 저는 그분이 잘못되기를 바라지 않아요.—아니에요, 두 분이 아무리 행복하게 산다 해도 제 마음에 다시 아픔이 일진 않을 거예요. 그리고 제가 진실을 말하고 있다는 걸 보여 드리려고, 이제 이걸 없애 버리려고요—진작 없앴어야 할 것을—애초에 간직하지 말았어야 할 것을요—저도 그건 잘 알아요(이렇게 말하며 얼굴을 붉혔다).—하지만 이제 전부 없애 버릴 거예요—그리고 꼭 당신이 보는 앞에서 하고 싶어요. 제가 얼마나 분별 있게 변했는지 봐 주시도록요. 이 꾸러미 안에 뭐가 들었는지 짐작 못 하시겠죠?\" 그녀가 사뭇 의식하는 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"전혀 모르겠는걸요.—그분이 무슨 물건이라도 준 적이 있어요?\"\n\n\"아니요—선물이라고는 못 해요. 그래도 제가 무척 소중히 여기던 것들이에요.\"\n\n그녀가 꾸러미를 엠마 쪽으로 내밀자, 엠마는 맨 위에 적힌 \"가장 소중한 보물\"이라는 글씨를 읽었다. 호기심이 잔뜩 일었다. 해리엇이 꾸러미를 펼치는 동안 엠마는 안달하며 지켜보았다. 은박지를 잔뜩 두른 안에 자그마하고 예쁜 턴브리지 세공 상자가 들어 있었고, 해리엇이 그것을 열었다. 더없이 부드러운 솜이 곱게 깔려 있었지만, 그 솜 말고 엠마의 눈에 들어온 것이라곤 자그마한 반창고 한 조각뿐이었다.\n\n\"자,\" 해리엇이 말했다. \"이제 분명 기억나실 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 정말 모르겠는데요.\"\n\n\"세상에! 바로 이 방에서, 우리가 여기서 만난 거의 마지막 날 중 하나였는데, 반창고를 두고 있었던 일을 잊으실 줄은 정말 몰랐어요!—제가 목이 아프기 바로 며칠 전이었어요—미스터 존 나이틀리 내외가 오시기 직전이요—아마 바로 그날 저녁이었던 것 같아요.—기억 안 나세요? 그분이 당신의 새 주머니칼로 손가락을 베고, 당신이 반창고를 권하셨던 거요?—그런데 당신한테는 마침 반창고가 없고 저한테는 있는 걸 아시니까, 당신이 저더러 그분께 드리라고 하셨잖아요. 그래서 제가 제 걸 꺼내 한 조각 잘라 드렸는데, 너무 컸던지 그분이 더 작게 잘라서, 한참을 그 남은 조각을 만지작거리시다가 저한테 돌려주셨어요. 그래서 그때, 제 어리석은 마음에, 저도 모르게 그걸 보물로 삼지 않을 수 없었어요—그래서 절대 쓰지 않으려고 따로 치워 두고, 가끔 큰 호사처럼 들여다보곤 했죠.\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 해리엇!\" 엠마가 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"당신 때문에 견딜 수 없을 만큼 내 자신이 부끄러워지네요. 기억나느냐고요? 그럼요, 이제 다 기억나요. 당신이 이 유물을 간직한 일만 빼고 전부요—그건 이 순간까지 까맣게 몰랐어요—하지만 손가락을 벤 일이며, 내가 반창고를 권하고 마침 가진 게 없다고 했던 일은요!—아! 내 죄, 내 죄!—그러면서 줄곧 주머니에 잔뜩 갖고 있었으면서!—내 그 못된 장난질 가운데 하나죠!—남은 평생을 두고두고 얼굴 붉히며 살아도 싸요.—자—(다시 자리에 앉으며)—계속해 봐요—그 다음엔요?\"\n\n\"그럼 정말로 직접 가지고 계셨던 거예요? 저는 한 번도 그런 줄 몰랐어요. 너무나 자연스럽게 하셔서요.\"\n\n\"그래서 정말로 그분을 위해서 이 반창고 조각을 따로 치워 뒀단 말이죠!\" 엠마가 부끄러움에서 벗어나 놀라움과 우스움 사이를 오가는 심정으로 말했다. 그러고는 속으로 가만히 덧붙였다. \"이런, 세상에! 프랭크 처칠이 만지작거리던 반창고 한 조각을 솜에 싸서 간직할 생각을 내가 도대체 언제 했겠어! 나는 이런 경지엔 도저히 못 미친다니까.\"\n\n\"여기,\" 해리엇이 다시 상자로 손을 돌리며 말을 이었다. \"여기 한층 더 값진 게 있어요. 더 값진 거였다는 뜻이에요. 이건 한때 정말로 그분 것이었거든요. 반창고는 한 번도 그분 것이었던 적이 없지만요.\"\n\n엠마는 그 한층 귀하다는 보물을 보고 싶어 몹시 달떴다. 그것은 낡은 연필의 끄트머리—심이 다 없어진 부분이었다.\n\n\"이건 정말 그분 거였어요.\" 해리엇이 말했다.—\"어느 날 아침 기억나세요?—아니, 모르실 거예요. 하지만 어느 날 아침에—정확한 날짜는 잊었지만요—아마 그날 저녁 바로 전 화요일이나 수요일이었을 거예요. 그분이 수첩에 뭔가를 적으려고 하셨어요. 가문비나무 맥주에 대한 거였죠. 미스터 나이틀리가 가문비나무 맥주 빚는 법을 들려주시던 참이라, 그분이 그걸 적어 두고 싶어 하셨거든요. 그런데 연필을 꺼내 보니 심이 너무 짧아서 금방 다 깎여 버려 쓸 수가 없었어요. 그래서 당신이 다른 걸 빌려주셨고, 이건 쓸모없다고 탁자에 그냥 남겨졌죠. 하지만 제가 그걸 눈여겨봤다가, 용기를 낼 수 있게 되자마자 얼른 집어 들었고, 그때부터 한 번도 손에서 놓지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"기억나요,\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"아주 또렷이 기억나요.—가문비나무 맥주 이야기를 했죠.—아! 그래요—미스터 나이틀리와 내가 둘 다 좋아한다고 했고, 미스터 엘턴은 자기도 기어이 좋아해 보겠다는 눈치였죠. 또렷이 기억나요.—가만있자, 미스터 나이틀리가 바로 여기 서 있지 않았나요? 바로 여기 서 있었던 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"아! 모르겠어요. 기억이 안 나요.—참 이상하죠, 그게 기억이 안 나요.—미스터 엘턴은 여기 앉아 계셨던 건 기억나요. 지금 제가 앉은 데쯤이요.\"—\n\n\"자, 계속해요.\"\n\n\"아! 그게 다예요. 더 보여 드릴 것도, 드릴 말씀도 없어요—이제 이 둘을 불 속에 던져 넣으려는 것만 빼면요. 제가 그러는 걸 당신이 봐 주셨으면 해요.\"\n\n\"내 가엾은 해리엇! 정말로 이런 것들을 보물처럼 간직하면서 행복을 느꼈던 거예요?\"\n\n\"네, 어리석게도요!—하지만 이제는 너무 부끄러워요. 이것들을 태워 없애듯 그렇게 쉽게 잊을 수 있으면 좋겠어요. 그분이 결혼한 뒤에도 무슨 기념물을 간직하는 건 정말 잘못된 일이었어요, 아시잖아요. 잘못인 줄 알면서도—그것들과 헤어질 만한 결심이 안 섰던 거죠.\"\n\n\"하지만 해리엇, 그 반창고까지 꼭 태워야 할까요?—낡은 연필 조각이야 두둔할 말이 한마디도 없지만, 반창고는 쓸모가 있을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"태워 버리는 게 마음이 더 편해요.\" 해리엇이 대답했다. \"저한텐 보기 싫은 물건이에요. 전부 없애 버려야 해요.—자, 들어간다, 이렇게—이제 끝, 다행이지 뭐예요! 미스터 엘턴은 이걸로 끝이에요.\"\n\n\"그러면,\" 엠마는 속으로 생각했다. \"미스터 처칠은 언제 시작되려나?\"\n\n얼마 지나지 않아 엠마는 그 시작이 이미 이루어졌다고 믿을 만한 근거를 얻었고, 비록 그 집시는 점을 쳐 주지 않았어도 해리엇의 운명만큼은 점지해 준 셈으로 판명될지 모른다는 기대를 품지 않을 수 없었다.—그 소동이 있고 보름쯤 지나, 두 사람은 충분한 해명에 이르렀는데, 그것도 전혀 의도치 않게 이루어진 일이었다. 마침 엠마가 그 생각을 하고 있지 않던 참이라, 듣게 된 소식이 한층 값지게 다가왔다. 그녀는 그저 시시한 잡담 끝에 이렇게 말했을 뿐이었다. \"그래요, 해리엇, 당신도 결혼하게 되면 이러저러하게 하라고 권하고 싶네요\"—그러고는 더 생각지 않았는데, 잠깐의 침묵 뒤에 해리엇이 자못 진지한 어조로 \"저는 결혼 안 할 거예요\"라고 하는 말이 들려왔다.\n\n그제야 엠마가 고개를 들었고, 곧장 어찌 된 영문인지 알아차렸다. 그러고는 잠시 모른 척 넘길지 말지 망설인 끝에 이렇게 답했다.\n\n\"결혼을 안 하겠다고요!—이건 또 새로운 결심이네요.\"\n\n\"하지만 결코 바꾸지 않을 결심이에요.\"\n\n또 한 번 짧게 머뭇거린 뒤. \"그게 설마—설마 미스터 엘턴 때문은 아니길 바라요?\"\n\n\"미스터 엘턴이라니요!\" 해리엇이 분개하여 외쳤다.—\"아! 아니에요\"—그리고 엠마는 겨우 이런 말을 알아들을 수 있었다. \"미스터 엘턴보다 훨씬 훌륭하신 분인걸요!\"\n\n그러자 엠마는 한참 더 생각에 잠겼다. 여기서 더 나아가지 말까?—그냥 넘기며 아무것도 눈치채지 못한 척할까?—그러면 해리엇은 자기를 냉담하다거나 화났다고 여길지도 몰랐다. 또 완전히 입을 다물고 있으면, 오히려 해리엇이 너무 많은 이야기를 들어 달라고 졸라 대는 쪽으로 몰릴 수도 있었다. 그런데 예전 같은 그런 격의 없음에는—희망이며 가능성을 그렇게 터놓고 빈번하게 늘어놓는 일에는—단호히 맞서기로 마음먹은 터였다.—말하고 알아 둘 작정인 것을 한꺼번에 말하고 알아 두는 편이 현명하리라 여겼다. 솔직하게 처리하는 것이 언제나 최선이었다. 이런 종류의 호소에 대해 어디까지 나아갈지는 미리 정해 두었으니, 자기 머릿속의 그 분별 있는 규칙을 서둘러 못 박아 두는 편이 양쪽 모두에게 안전할 터였다.—마음을 정한 그녀가 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"해리엇, 당신 말뜻을 모르는 척하진 않을게요. 결혼을 안 하겠다는 그 결심, 아니 차라리 그 예감은, 당신이 마음에 두는 사람이 처지로 보아 당신을 거들떠보기에는 너무나 까마득히 위에 있다는 생각에서 비롯된 거죠. 그렇지 않아요?\"\n\n\"아! 미스 우드하우스, 믿어 주세요, 저는 감히 그런 생각은—정말이지 저는 그렇게 정신 나가지 않았어요.—하지만 멀찍이서 그분을 우러러보는 것만으로도, 그분이 세상 누구보다도 한없이 훌륭하시다고 생각하는 것만으로도 제겐 기쁨이에요. 마땅히 가져야 할 그 감사와 경탄과 경의를 품고서요. 특히 저 같은 사람한테는 더욱 마땅한 일이죠.\"\n\n\"당신이 그러는 게 조금도 놀랍지 않아요, 해리엇. 그분이 당신에게 베푼 친절이라면 당신 마음을 데우고도 남았을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"친절이라니요! 아! 말로 다 못할 은혜였는걸요!—그날을 떠올리기만 해도, 그때 제가 느낀 그 모든 것이—그분이 다가오시는 걸 봤을 때—그 고귀한 모습이—그리고 그 직전의 비참함이라니. 그런 변화라니! 한순간에 그런 변화가! 더없는 비참함에서 더없는 행복으로요!\"\n\n\"아주 자연스러운 일이에요. 자연스럽고, 또 떳떳한 일이죠.—그래요, 떳떳한 일이라고 생각해요. 그토록 잘, 그토록 고마운 마음으로 사람을 고른 거니까요.—하지만 그게 행운으로 이어질 마음인지는 제가 약속해 드릴 수 없네요. 거기에 휩쓸리라고 권하진 않겠어요, 해리엇. 그 마음이 보답받으리라고는 결코 장담 못 해요. 자신이 무얼 하고 있는지 잘 생각해 봐요. 할 수 있을 때 마음을 다잡는 게 가장 현명할 거예요. 적어도 그분이 당신을 좋아한다는 확신이 서기 전에는 그 마음이 당신을 멀리 끌고 가게 두지 마요. 그분을 잘 살펴봐요. 그분의 처신을 당신 마음의 길잡이로 삼아요. 지금 이렇게 경고하는 건, 이 일에 대해 다시는 당신과 말하지 않을 작정이기 때문이에요. 어떤 간섭도 하지 않기로 마음먹었어요. 이제부터 저는 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는 거예요. 우리 입에서 그 누구의 이름도 다시는 나오지 않게 해요. 우리는 전에 아주 잘못했어요. 이제부터는 조심하기로 해요.—그분이 당신보다 윗자리에 있는 건 의심할 바 없고, 아주 심각한 반대와 장애가 있어 보이는 것도 사실이에요. 하지만 그래도, 해리엇, 이보다 더 놀라운 일들도 일어나곤 했고, 이보다 더 격차가 큰 혼사도 있었답니다. 그래도 몸조심해요. 너무 들뜨지 않았으면 좋겠어요. 다만, 일이 어떻게 끝나든, 당신이 그분께 마음을 두었다는 건 좋은 안목의 표시이고, 저는 언제까지나 그걸 높이 살 줄 안다는 것만은 믿어 줘요.\"\n\n해리엇은 말없이 순순한 감사의 뜻으로 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었다. 엠마는 이런 연정이 벗에게 결코 나쁘지 않으리라고 단단히 믿었다. 그 마음은 해리엇의 정신을 끌어올리고 세련되게 다듬어 줄 것이며—그녀를 타락의 위험에서 구해 주는 일이 분명할 터였다.\n\n# 제3권 5장\n\n이런 갖가지 계획과 희망과 묵계 속에서, 유월이 하트필드를 찾아왔다. 하이베리 전체로 보자면 이렇다 할 변화는 없었다. 엘턴 부부는 여전히 서클링 일가의 방문과, 그들의 바루시-랜도를 어떻게 써먹을지를 입에 올리고 있었다. 제인 페어팩스는 여전히 할머니 댁에 머물렀고, 캠벨 부부의 아일랜드 귀환이 또 한 번 미뤄져 한여름이 아니라 팔월로 잡힌 까닭에, 그녀가 그곳에 꼬박 두 달은 더 머물 모양이었다 — 적어도 자신을 위한답시고 분주히 움직이는 엘턴 부인의 성화를 물리치고, 제 뜻과는 무관하게 어느 흐뭇한 자리로 떠밀려 가는 일을 면할 수만 있다면 말이다.\n* 바루시-랜도: 접이식 덮개를 갖춘 사두마차로, 당시 부유층의 과시용 마차였다.\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 자기만 아는 어떤 까닭으로 진작부터 프랭크 처칠을 탐탁잖아했는데, 그 마뜩잖음이 점점 더해 갈 뿐이었다. 그는 처칠이 엠마에게 다가가는 데 무언가 이중적인 구석이 있지 않나 의심하기 시작했다. 엠마가 처칠의 표적이라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없어 보였다. 모든 것이 그렇게 말하고 있었다 — 처칠 자신의 살가운 태도, 그 아버지의 넌지시 던지는 말, 계모의 조심스러운 침묵, 이 모두가 한목소리였다. 말도, 행동도, 신중함도, 경솔함도 한결같이 같은 이야기를 들려주었다. 그러나 그토록 많은 사람이 그를 엠마에게 점지하고, 엠마 자신은 그를 해리엇에게 넘겨주려는 동안, 미스터 나이틀리는 처칠이 제인 페어팩스와 어떤 식으로든 장난을 치고 싶어 하는 게 아닌가 의심하기 시작했다. 그로서는 알 수 없는 일이었다. 그러나 두 사람 사이에 어떤 통하는 기미가 — 적어도 그에게는 그렇게 보였다 — 처칠 쪽의 흠모의 기색이 있었으니, 한번 눈에 들어온 이상, 엠마의 상상이 저지른 잘못 따위는 무엇 하나 답습하지 않겠다고 아무리 다짐해 본들, 그것이 아무 뜻 없는 것이라고는 도무지 자신을 설득할 수 없었다. 그 의심이 처음 일었을 때, 그녀는 자리에 없었다. 그는 엘턴 부부 댁에서 랜덜스 가족, 그리고 제인과 함께 식사를 하고 있었다. 그는 미스 페어팩스를 향한 한 차례의, 아니 한 차례 이상의 눈길을 보았으니, 미스 우드하우스를 흠모하는 사람의 눈길이라기엔 어딘가 어울리지 않아 보였다. 다시 그들과 자리를 함께했을 때, 그는 자신이 본 것을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었고, 카우퍼가 황혼녘 난롯불을 보며 읊었듯 —\n\n\"내가 본 것을 스스로 지어내는 것\"\n\n이 아닌 한, 프랭크 처칠과 제인 사이에 남모르는 호감이, 아니 남모르는 묵계까지 있지 않나 하는 한층 짙은 의심을 안기는 관찰을 피할 수도 없었다.\n\n어느 날 그는 식후에, 늘 그러듯 하트필드까지 걸어 올라와 저녁을 보내기로 했다. 엠마와 해리엇이 산책을 나가려던 참이라 그도 끼었고, 돌아오는 길에 더 큰 무리와 마주쳤다. 그들도 이쪽과 마찬가지로, 비가 올 듯한 날씨라 일찌감치 거니는 편이 현명하다고 여긴 사람들이었으니 — 미스터 웨스턴 부부와 그 아들, 그리고 미스 베이츠와 조카딸이 우연히 만났던 것이다. 모두가 한 무리를 이루었고, 하트필드 정문에 이르자 엠마는 이것이야말로 아버지가 반길 만한 종류의 방문임을 알기에, 다들 들어와 함께 차를 들자고 권했다. 랜덜스 일행은 곧장 응했다. 미스 베이츠가 꽤 긴 연설을 늘어놓았으나 귀 기울이는 사람은 거의 없었고, 그녀 역시 사랑스러운 미스 우드하우스의 더없이 친절한 초대를 받아들일 수 있겠다고 결론지었다.\n\n일행이 정원 안으로 접어들 때, 미스터 페리가 말을 타고 지나갔다. 신사들은 그의 말을 화제로 삼았다.\n\n\"그러고 보니,\" 곧이어 프랭크 처칠이 웨스턴 부인에게 말했다. \"미스터 페리가 마차를 장만하겠다던 계획은 어떻게 됐습니까?\"\n\n웨스턴 부인은 놀란 얼굴로 말했다. \"그분에게 그런 계획이 있었다는 건 처음 듣는데요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 부인께 들은 이야기입니다. 석 달 전에 편지로 적어 보내셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"제가요! 그럴 리가요!\"\n\n\"분명히 그러셨습니다. 똑똑히 기억해요. 곧 틀림없이 그렇게 될 일이라고 적으셨죠. 페리 부인이 누군가에게 말했고, 그 일로 무척 기뻐했다고요. 그게 다 그 부인이 설득한 덕분인데, 미스터 페리가 궂은 날씨에 나다니는 게 몸에 큰 해가 된다고 여겼기 때문이라고요. 이제는 기억나시죠?\"\n\n\"맹세코, 이 순간까지 그런 얘기는 들어 본 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"한 번도요! 정말, 한 번도라니!—어이쿠! 어찌 이럴 수가 있죠?—그럼 제가 꿈을 꾼 게 틀림없군요—하지만 저는 완전히 그렇게 믿고 있었는데—미스 스미스, 지친 사람처럼 걷고 계시네요. 집에 다다른 게 반가우시겠어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 얘긴가?—무슨 얘기야?\" 미스터 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"페리하고 마차라니? 페리가 마차를 장만한다는 거냐, 프랭크? 그럴 형편이 된다니 다행이로구나. 그 사람한테 직접 들은 거냐?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 아버지.\" 아들이 웃으며 대답했다. \"아무한테도 듣지 않은 모양이에요.—참 이상한 일이죠!—저는 정말이지 웨스턴 부인께서 몇 주 전 엔스컴으로 보내신 편지에 그 일을 이런저런 세세한 사정까지 적으셨다고 믿고 있었는데—본인이 그런 얘기는 한마디도 들은 적이 없다고 단언하시니, 결국 꿈이었던 게 분명하군요. 저는 꿈을 잘 꿉니다. 떠나 있을 때면 하이베리 사람들을 모조리 꿈에 봐요—그러다 각별한 친구들을 한 차례 다 거치고 나면, 그제야 미스터 페리 부부를 꿈꾸기 시작하죠.\"\n\n\"하지만 이상한 일이로구나,\" 그 아버지가 한마디 보탰다. \"엔스컴에서야 좀처럼 떠올릴 일도 없는 사람들을 두고 그렇게 조리 있게 이어진 꿈을 꾸었다니 말이다. 페리가 마차를 장만한다! 게다가 건강을 염려한 그 아내가 권해서라니—틀림없이 언젠가는 그렇게 될 일이지, 다만 좀 이른 셈일 뿐이고. 꿈이란 게 어떨 때는 어찌 그리 그럴듯한 분위기가 흐르는지! 또 어떨 때는 어찌 그리 터무니없는 것들로만 들끓는지! 그래, 프랭크, 네 꿈은 적어도 네가 떠나 있을 때도 하이베리를 마음에 두고 있다는 증거로구나. 엠마, 자네도 꿈을 잘 꾸는 편 아닌가?\"\n\n엠마는 들리지 않는 곳에 있었다. 손님들보다 앞서 서둘러 가, 그들이 들이닥치는 데 대비해 아버지를 채비시키느라, 미스터 웨스턴이 넌지시 건넨 말이 닿지 않는 곳에 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"저기, 사실대로 말하자면,\" 지난 이 분 동안 끼어들려고 헛되이 애쓰던 미스 베이츠가 외쳤다. \"이 얘기를 굳이 해야 한다면, 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 그럴 만도 했다는 건 부인할 수가 없는데—그분이 꿈을 꾸지 않았다는 말은 아니에요—저도 더러는 세상에 둘도 없이 희한한 꿈을 꾸곤 하니까요—하지만 이 일을 두고 물으신다면, 지난봄에 그런 얘기가 있었다는 걸 인정할 수밖에 없네요. 페리 부인이 몸소 저희 어머니께 그 얘기를 하셨고, 콜네 사람들도 저희만큼이나 알고 있었으니까요—하지만 아주 비밀스러운 일이라 다른 사람은 아무도 몰랐고, 사흘쯤만 입에 올랐을 뿐이에요. 페리 부인은 그분이 마차를 갖기를 무척 바라셨고, 어느 날 아침 어머니를 찾아오셔서 이제 설득에 성공한 것 같다며 신이 나서 말씀하셨죠. 제인, 우리가 집에 왔을 때 할머니가 그 얘기를 들려주신 거 기억 안 나니? 그날 어디로 산책을 다녀왔는지는 잊어버렸네—아마 랜덜스였을 거예요. 그래요, 랜덜스였던 것 같아요. 페리 부인은 늘 저희 어머니를 각별히 좋아하셨어요—하기야 어머니를 안 좋아하는 사람이 어디 있겠어요—그래서 어머니께 은밀히 그 얘길 하신 거고요. 물론 저희한테 전하시는 거야 마다하지 않으셨지만, 더는 새 나가면 안 되는 일이었죠. 그리고 그날부터 지금까지, 제가 아는 한 누구에게도 그 얘기를 입에 올린 적이 없어요. 그렇긴 해도, 한 번도 귀띔 같은 걸 흘린 적이 없다고 딱 잘라 장담하지는 못하겠어요. 저도 모르게 불쑥 무슨 말을 내뱉을 때가 있다는 걸 아니까요. 제가 워낙 말이 많잖아요. 좀 수다스러운 편이죠. 그래서 더러는 입에 담아선 안 될 걸 흘리기도 하고요. 저는 제인 같지가 못해요. 제인 같았으면 좋으련만. 제인은 세상의 무엇 하나 흘린 적이 없다고 제가 장담해요. 그 애가 어디 있더라?—아! 바로 뒤에 있네. 페리 부인이 오셨던 게 똑똑히 기억나요.—참말이지 별난 꿈이네요!\"\n\n일행이 현관으로 들어서던 참이었다. 미스터 나이틀리의 눈은 미스 베이츠보다 한발 앞서 제인을 흘긋 보았다. 프랭크 처칠의 얼굴에서 — 억눌렸거나 웃어넘긴 당혹의 빛을 본 듯한 그 얼굴에서 — 그는 저도 모르게 제인의 얼굴로 눈길을 돌렸다. 그러나 그녀는 정말로 뒤에 처져, 숄을 여미느라 분주했다. 미스터 웨스턴은 이미 안으로 들어갔다. 나머지 두 신사는 문가에 서서 그녀가 지나가도록 기다렸다. 미스터 나이틀리는 프랭크 처칠이 그녀의 눈을 마주치려고 작정한 게 아닌가 의심했다 — 그는 그녀를 뚫어지게 살피는 듯했다 — 그러나 정말 그랬다 한들 헛수고였으니, 제인은 두 사람 사이를 지나 현관으로 들어가면서 어느 쪽도 쳐다보지 않았다.\n\n더는 따져 물을 겨를도, 해명할 겨를도 없었다. 그 꿈 이야기는 묻어 두는 수밖에 없었고, 미스터 나이틀리도 나머지 사람들과 함께 그 크고 현대적인 둥근 식탁에 둘러앉아야 했다. 엠마가 하트필드에 들여놓은 그 식탁은, 사십 년 동안 하루 두 끼니가 비좁게 차려지던 자그마한 펨브룩 식탁 대신 그것을 그 자리에 놓고 아버지를 설득해 쓰게 만들 수 있는 사람은 엠마 말고는 없었다. 차 시간은 즐겁게 흘러갔고, 누구도 자리를 뜨려고 서두르는 기색이 없었다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스,\" 프랭크 처칠이 앉은 채로 손이 닿는 등 뒤의 탁자를 살피더니 말했다. \"조카분들이 알파벳 놀이 도구를—그 글자 상자를 가져가 버렸나요? 여기 놓여 있곤 했는데. 어디 있죠? 오늘 저녁은 어쩐지 따분한 기운이 도는 게, 여름이라기보다 겨울처럼 보내야 할 분위기네요. 언젠가 아침에 그 글자들로 재미나게 놀았잖아요. 다시 한번 골탕 먹여 드리고 싶은데요.\"\n\n엠마는 그 발상이 마음에 들었다. 상자를 꺼내 오니, 식탁은 금세 알파벳들로 어수선해졌는데, 그것을 쓰는 데 가장 열심인 사람은 단연 그 둘이었다. 두 사람은 서로에게, 또는 골탕 먹어 줄 만한 다른 아무에게나 빠르게 단어를 만들어 보였다. 이 놀이의 조용함은 미스터 우드하우스에게 특히 알맞았으니, 그는 미스터 웨스턴이 이따금 들여온 더 활기찬 놀이에 곧잘 시달리곤 했던 터였다. 그는 이제 흐뭇이 자리 잡고 앉아, \"가엾은 어린 손주들\"이 떠나간 것을 애틋한 우수에 잠겨 탄식하거나, 곁에 흩어진 글자를 집어 들 때마다 엠마가 이걸 얼마나 곱게 썼는지 다정히 가리켜 보이는 데 여념이 없었다.\n\n프랭크 처칠이 미스 페어팩스 앞에 한 단어를 놓았다. 그녀는 식탁을 슬쩍 둘러보더니 그것에 매달렸다. 프랭크는 엠마 옆에, 제인은 두 사람 맞은편에 있었고—미스터 나이틀리는 그들 모두를 볼 수 있는 자리에 있었다. 되도록 티 나지 않게 살피면서도 되도록 많이 보는 것이 그의 목적이었다. 단어가 풀렸고, 그녀는 희미한 미소와 함께 그것을 밀어냈다. 곧장 다른 글자들과 섞어 보이지 않게 묻어 둘 생각이었다면, 바로 맞은편을 보는 대신 식탁을 내려다보았어야 했다. 글자들이 섞이지 않았기 때문이다. 새 단어마다 들떠 달려들고도 하나도 풀어내지 못하던 해리엇이 곧장 그것을 집어 들고 풀이에 매달렸다. 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리 곁에 앉아 있던 터라 그에게 도움을 청했다. 그 단어는 '실수'였다. 해리엇이 의기양양하게 그것을 외치자, 제인의 뺨에 홍조가 어렸으니, 그렇지 않았다면 드러나지 않았을 어떤 의미를 그 단어에 입혔다. 미스터 나이틀리는 그것을 아까의 꿈 이야기와 연결지었다. 그러나 도대체 어찌 된 영문인지는 그의 짐작을 넘어서는 일이었다. 그가 그토록 아끼는 그 처녀의 섬세함과 분별이 어쩌면 이렇게 잠들어 버릴 수 있단 말인가! 그는 무언가 확실한 얽힘이 있는 게 분명하다고 우려했다. 가는 곳마다 음험함과 이중성이 그를 맞이하는 듯했다. 이 글자들은 한낱 수작과 잔꾀의 매개일 뿐이었다. 그것은 프랭크 처칠 쪽의 더 깊은 노름을 감추려고 고른 아이들 놀이였다.\n\n그는 큰 분노 속에서 그를 계속 지켜보았고, 그 못지않은 경악과 불신 속에서 눈먼 두 동행 또한 지켜보았다. 그는 짧은 단어 하나가 엠마를 위해 마련되어, 능청스럽고도 새치름한 눈길과 함께 그녀에게 건네지는 것을 보았다. 엠마가 그것을 금세 알아채고 무척 재미있어한다는 것을, 그러면서도 짐짓 나무라는 척하는 게 옳다고 여긴다는 것을 보았다. 그녀가 \"말도 안 돼! 부끄러운 줄 아세요!\" 하고 말했기 때문이다. 이어 프랭크 처칠이 제인 쪽을 흘긋 보며 \"이걸 그 사람한테 줄게요—그래도 될까요?\" 하고 말하는 것을, 그리고 엠마가 들뜬 웃음과 열의로 \"안 돼요, 안 돼, 그러면 안 돼요. 정말로 그러시면 안 돼요\" 하고 막아서는 것을 또렷이 들었다.\n\n그러나 일은 벌어지고 말았다. 마음 없이 사랑하는 듯, 비위 맞추는 일 없이도 호감을 사는 듯한 이 늠름한 청년은, 곧장 그 단어를 미스 페어팩스에게 건네며 유난히 차분하고 정중한 태도로 풀어 보기를 청했다. 그 단어가 무엇인지 알고 싶은 미스터 나이틀리의 지나친 호기심은 틈만 나면 그쪽으로 눈길을 던지게 했고, 오래지 않아 그것이 '딕슨'임을 알아보았다. 제인 페어팩스의 알아챔도 그와 발맞춘 듯했다. 그렇게 배열된 다섯 글자에 깃든 은밀한 뜻과 한 수 위의 속내를 헤아리는 데는 분명 그녀의 분별이 더 합당했다. 그녀는 역력히 언짢아하며 고개를 들었고, 자신이 살펴지고 있음을 알아채자 그가 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 깊이 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그러고는 \"고유 명사도 되는 줄은 몰랐네요\"라고만 말하며, 성난 기운까지 내비치며 글자들을 밀쳐 버렸고, 더는 어떤 단어가 주어져도 거기 매이지 않겠다고 작정한 얼굴이었다. 그녀는 공세를 펼친 이들에게서 얼굴을 돌려 이모 쪽을 향했다.\n\n\"그래, 정말 그렇구나, 얘야,\" 제인이 한마디도 하지 않았는데도 이모가 외쳤다. \"나도 막 같은 말을 하려던 참이었어. 정말 우리 일어설 때가 됐지. 저녁이 깊어 가고, 할머니가 우리를 기다리실 거야. 선생님, 너무도 친절하셔라. 정말로 이만 작별 인사를 드려야겠어요.\"\n\n제인이 자리에서 잽싸게 움직이는 것은, 이모가 미리 짐작한 그대로 채비가 되어 있었음을 증명했다. 그녀는 곧장 일어나 식탁을 떠나고 싶어 했으나, 너무 여럿이 함께 움직이는 통에 빠져나갈 수가 없었다. 미스터 나이틀리는 또 한 무더기의 글자가 그녀 쪽으로 안달하듯 밀려갔다가, 그녀가 들여다보지도 않고 단호하게 쓸어내 버리는 것을 본 듯했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 숄을 찾고 있었고—프랭크 처칠도 함께 찾고 있었다—날이 어둑해지고 방 안은 어수선했으니, 두 사람이 어떻게 헤어졌는지는 미스터 나이틀리도 알 수 없었다.\n\n그는 나머지 사람들이 모두 떠난 뒤에도 하트필드에 남았다. 자신이 본 것으로 머릿속이 가득 차 있었다. 어찌나 가득했는지, 관찰을 거들 양초가 들어왔을 때, 그는 반드시—그래, 분명히, 벗으로서—마음 졸이는 벗으로서—엠마에게 무언가 귀띔을 하고, 무언가 물어보아야만 했다. 그토록 위태로운 처지에 놓인 그녀를 보면서, 지켜 주려 애쓰지 않을 수는 없었다. 그것이 그의 도리였다.\n\n\"엠마,\" 그가 말했다. \"마지막에 자네와 미스 페어팩스에게 건네진 그 단어가 무엇이 그리 큰 재미를, 또 무엇이 그리 따가운 가시를 품고 있었는지 물어봐도 되겠나? 그 단어를 보긴 했는데, 그게 어떻게 한쪽에는 그토록 재미있고 다른 쪽에는 그토록 괴로운 것이 될 수 있는지 궁금하군.\"\n\n엠마는 몹시 당황했다. 그에게 사실대로 설명하는 것은 견딜 수 없는 일이었다. 그녀의 의심이 결코 가신 것은 아니었으나, 그것을 한때 입에 올린 적이 있다는 사실 자체가 정말이지 부끄러웠던 것이다.\n\n\"아유!\" 그녀가 역력한 곤혹 속에 외쳤다. \"다 별 뜻 없는 거예요. 우리끼리 그저 농담이었죠.\"\n\n\"그 농담이라는 게,\" 그가 진지하게 받았다. \"자네와 미스터 처칠 둘 사이에만 통하는 것이던데.\"\n\n그는 그녀가 다시 입을 열기를 바랐으나, 그녀는 그러지 않았다. 무슨 일이든 분주히 하는 편이 말하는 것보다 나았던 것이다. 그는 한동안 미심쩍은 채 앉아 있었다. 갖가지 불길한 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다. 참견—부질없는 참견. 엠마의 당황과 두 사람이 인정한 친밀함은, 그녀의 마음이 이미 매여 있음을 말해 주는 듯했다. 그래도 그는 말하기로 했다. 그녀의 안녕을 저버리기보다는, 달갑잖은 참견에 따를 그 무엇이든 무릅쓰는 편이 그녀에게 마땅한 도리였다. 그런 일을 두고 외면했다는 기억을 떠안느니, 무엇이든 마주하는 편이 나았다.\n\n\"엠마,\" 그가 마침내 진심 어린 다정함으로 말했다. \"우리가 얘기하던 그 신사와 숙녀 사이가 어느 정도의 친분인지, 자네가 완전히 안다고 생각하나?\"\n\n\"미스터 프랭크 처칠과 미스 페어팩스 사이요? 아유! 그럼요, 완전히 알죠.—왜 그걸 의심하시는데요?\"\n\n\"언젠가 한 번이라도, 그가 그녀를 흠모한다거나 그녀가 그를 흠모한다고 여길 만한 일이 없었나?\"\n\n\"없었어요, 한 번도요!\" 그녀가 더없이 거리낌 없는 열의로 외쳤다.—\"한순간의 이십분의 일만큼도 그런 생각이 떠오른 적이 없어요. 대체 어떻게 그런 생각이 머릿속에 드신 거예요?\"\n\n\"근래에 그 두 사람 사이에서 연정의 기미를 본 것 같았거든—어딘가 뜻을 담은 듯한 눈길들, 남들 보라고 한 건 아니었을 그런 눈길 말일세.\"\n\n\"아유! 정말 너무 우습네요. 당신도 상상의 나래를 펼치는 호사를 누릴 줄 아신다니 반갑기 그지없는데—하지만 그건 아니에요—첫 시도부터 찬물을 끼얹어 정말 미안하지만—정말이지 그건 아니에요. 두 사람 사이엔 흠모 따위 없어요, 장담해요. 당신 눈에 띈 그 정황들은 어떤 특이한 사정에서 비롯된 거예요—오히려 전혀 다른 성질의 감정이랄까—정확히 설명하기란 불가능하고요.—거기엔 어처구니없는 구석이 많아요—하지만 전할 수 있는 부분, 즉 이치에 닿는 부분만 말하자면, 두 사람은 세상 어느 두 존재만큼이나 서로에 대한 연정이나 흠모와는 거리가 멀다는 거예요. 그러니까, 그녀 쪽에 대해서는 그렇다고 짐작하는 거고, 그 신사 쪽에 대해서는 제가 장담할 수 있어요. 그 신사가 무심하다는 건 제가 보증하지요.\"\n\n그녀는 미스터 나이틀리를 휘청이게 할 만큼 확신에 차서, 그를 입 다물게 할 만큼 흡족하게 말했다. 그녀는 들떠 있었고, 대화를 더 이어 가고 싶어 했다. 그의 의심이 어떤 것인지 자세히 듣고, 눈길 하나하나가 어땠는지 묘사해 달라고 하며, 자신을 무척 즐겁게 하는 그 정황의 온갖 어디서며 어떻게를 캐묻고 싶었던 것이다. 그러나 그의 기분은 그녀의 들뜸에 발맞추지 못했다. 그는 자신이 쓸모가 없다는 것을 깨달았고, 그의 감정은 이야기를 잇기엔 너무 들끓어 있었다. 미스터 우드하우스의 다정한 습성이 한 해 내내 거의 매일 저녁 요구하는 그 난롯불에 부추겨져 영영 열병에 빠지는 일이 없도록, 그는 곧 황급히 작별을 고하고, 돈웰 애비의 서늘함과 고요 속으로 걸어 돌아갔다.\n\n# 제3권 6장\n\n서클링 부부가 곧 찾아오리라는 기대로 오랫동안 마음을 부풀려 온 끝에, 하이베리 사람들은 가을이 되기 전에는 도무지 올 수 없다는 소식을 듣고 분한 마음을 삭여야 했다. 그처럼 신선한 새 인물의 유입으로 그들의 지적 곳간을 채울 일은 당분간 없게 된 셈이었다. 날마다 오가는 소식의 교환에서 그들은 또다시 다른 화제들로 만족해야 했는데, 그것들은 한동안 서클링 부부의 방문과 한데 묶여 있던 것들이었다. 이를테면 날마다 다른 보고가 들려오는 듯한 처칠 부인의 건강 소식, 그리고 웨스턴 부인의 형편 같은 것이었다. 웨스턴 부인의 경우, 이웃들 모두가 아이의 출산이 다가오는 것으로 행복이 더해지듯, 그녀 자신의 행복도 마침내 아이가 태어남으로써 그만큼 커지기를 바랄 만했다.\n\n엘턴 부인은 무척 실망했다. 그것은 숱한 즐거움과 과시의 기회가 미뤄진다는 뜻이었다. 그녀의 소개와 추천도 죄다 기다려야 했고, 계획해 둔 모임마다 여전히 말로만 그쳐야 했다. 처음에는 그렇게 생각했지만, 조금 궁리해 보니 무엇이든 다 미룰 필요는 없다는 확신이 들었다. 서클링 부부가 안 온다고 해서 박스 힐로 나들이를 못 갈 까닭이 어디 있단 말인가? 가을에 그 부부와 다시 한번 가면 그만이었다. 그리하여 박스 힐로 가기로 정해졌다. 그런 모임이 있으리라는 것은 진작부터 두루 알려진 일이었고, 그것이 또 하나의 나들이를 떠올리게 했을 정도였다. 엠마는 박스 힐에 한 번도 가 본 적이 없었다. 누구나 그토록 가 볼 만하다고 입을 모으는 그곳을 보고 싶었기에, 그녀와 미스터 웨스턴은 어느 화창한 아침을 골라 그리로 마차를 몰기로 합의해 둔 터였다. 거기에 끼워 줄 사람은 엄선한 두세 명뿐이었고, 그 일은 조용하고 점잖고 우아하게, 엘턴 부부와 서클링 부부 식의 부산함과 준비, 으레 차려 먹고 마시는 절차며 피크닉의 과시와는 비할 데 없이 격이 높게 치러질 예정이었다.\n\n이것은 두 사람 사이에 워낙 잘 합의가 되어 있던 터라, 엠마는 미스터 웨스턴에게서 다음과 같은 말을 듣고는 적잖이 놀라고 약간 언짢지 않을 수 없었다. 엘턴 부인의 언니 내외가 그녀에게 바람을 맞힌 셈이 되었으니, 두 모임을 합쳐 함께 가자고 엘턴 부인에게 제안했다는 것이었다. 그리고 엘턴 부인이 아주 선뜻 응했으니, 엠마가 반대하지 않는 한 그렇게 하기로 했다는 것이었다. 그런데 엠마의 반대라는 것은 엘턴 부인을 몹시 싫어한다는 것 말고는 아무것도 아니었고, 그 점은 미스터 웨스턴도 이미 훤히 알고 있을 터라 새삼 끄집어낼 가치가 없었다. 그것을 입에 올리자면 미스터 웨스턴을 나무라는 일이 될 테고, 그것은 곧 그의 아내를 아프게 하는 일이 될 터였다. 그리하여 엠마는 어떻게든 피하고 싶었던 결정에 동의할 수밖에 없는 처지가 되었으니 — 어쩌면 엘턴 부인 일행에 끼었다는 말까지 듣게 될 굴욕에 자신을 내맡기는 결정이었다! 온갖 감정이 상했다. 겉으로 순순히 따르며 참아 넘긴 대가로, 미스터 웨스턴의 그 종잡을 수 없는 선의(善意)를 두고 마음속에서 호되게 곱씹는 빚이 무겁게 남았다.\n\n\"내가 한 일을 당신이 좋게 봐 주니 기쁘오.\" 그가 아주 편안하게 말했다. \"하지만 그러리라고 생각했소. 이런 계획은 사람 수가 적으면 아무 재미가 없거든. 일행은 많을수록 좋은 법이오. 일행이 많으면 그 자체로 흥이 보장되지요. 그리고 어쨌든 그 부인은 마음씨가 너그러운 사람이오. 빼놓을 수야 없지요.\"\n\n엠마는 그 어느 것도 소리 내어 부정하지 않았고, 마음속으로는 그 어느 것에도 동의하지 않았다.\n\n어느덧 유월 중순이라 날씨가 화창했다. 엘턴 부인은 날을 잡아 미스터 웨스턴과 비둘기 파이며 차가운 양고기 따위를 정하고 싶어 안달이 났는데, 그때 마차 끄는 말 한 마리가 다리를 절면서 모든 일이 답답한 불확실 속으로 빠져들었다. 그 말을 다시 쓸 수 있게 되기까지 몇 주가 걸릴지, 며칠이면 될지 알 수 없었다. 하지만 어떤 준비도 함부로 할 수가 없어, 모든 것이 침울한 정체에 빠졌다. 엘턴 부인의 임기응변으로는 이런 타격을 감당하기에 역부족이었다.\n\n\"이거야말로 정말 약 오르는 일 아니에요, 나이틀리?\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 나들이하기 좋은 날씨에! 이런 지연과 차질이라니, 정말이지 진절머리가 나요. 어쩌면 좋죠? 이러다간 한 해가 그냥 흘러가 버리고 아무것도 못 하겠어요. 작년 이맘때만 해도 우린 메이플 그로브에서 킹스 웨스턴까지 정말 즐거운 나들이를 했었다고요.\"\n\n\"돈웰로 나들이를 오시는 게 낫겠습니다.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 답했다. \"거긴 말 없이도 갈 수 있으니까요. 오셔서 제 딸기나 드시지요. 한창 익어 가고 있습니다.\"\n\n미스터 나이틀리가 처음에는 진심으로 한 말이 아니었다 해도, 이제는 그렇게 밀고 나갈 수밖에 없게 되었다. 그의 제안이 환호 속에 덥석 받아들여졌기 때문이다. \"어머! 그보다 좋은 일이 어디 있겠어요\"라는 말이 어조에서도 말 못지않게 또렷이 드러났다. 돈웰은 딸기밭으로 유명했고, 그것이 초대의 구실이 될 만했다. 하지만 구실 따위는 필요 없었다. 그저 어디론가 가고 싶을 뿐인 이 부인에게는 양배추밭이라도 충분히 유혹이 되었을 것이다. 그녀는 가겠노라고 거듭거듭 약속했으니 — 그가 의심한 것보다 훨씬 여러 번이었다 — 그러한 친밀의 증표, 그녀 스스로 각별한 예우라 여기기로 한 그 일에 무척이나 흡족해했다.\n\n\"저만 믿으세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"꼭 가고말고요. 날만 잡으시면 제가 가겠어요. 제인 페어팩스를 데려가도 괜찮겠죠?\"\n\n\"날을 잡을 수가 없습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"부인과 만나게 하고 싶은 다른 분들 몇과 이야기를 해 보기 전에는요.\"\n\n\"어머! 그런 건 다 제게 맡기세요. 제게 전권만 주시면 돼요. 제가 후원자 마님이잖아요, 아시다시피. 이건 제 모임이에요. 제가 친구들을 데려올게요.\"\n\n\"엘턴은 데려와 주시면 좋겠군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 다른 초대는 부인을 수고롭게 해 드리지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어머! 이제 보니 아주 능청을 떠시네요. 하지만 생각해 보세요. 제게 권한을 맡기는 걸 두려워하실 필요는 없어요. 저는 시집갈 자리나 노리는 풋내기 아가씨가 아니거든요. 아시다시피 결혼한 여자한테는 안심하고 권한을 줄 수 있는 거예요. 이건 제 모임이라니까요. 다 제게 맡기세요. 손님은 제가 초대하겠어요.\"\n\n\"안 됩니다.\" 그가 차분히 답했다. \"이 세상에 돈웰로 제 마음대로 손님을 초대해도 좋다고 제가 허락할 수 있는 결혼한 여자는 단 한 사람뿐인데, 그 한 사람은—\"\n\n\"—웨스턴 부인이겠죠.\" 엘턴 부인이 다소 분한 기색으로 말을 가로챘다.\n\n\"아니요. 나이틀리 부인입니다. 그리고 그런 사람이 생기기 전까지는 그런 일은 제가 알아서 처리하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어머! 정말 별난 양반이시네요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. 자기보다 더 우대받는 사람이 없다는 데 만족한 것이었다. \"당신은 익살꾼이라 하고 싶은 말은 무엇이든 하는군요. 영락없는 익살꾼이에요. 좋아요, 그럼 제인을 데려가겠어요 — 제인하고 그 이모분을요. 나머지는 당신께 맡길게요. 하트필드 식구들과 만나는 거야 저는 전혀 반대하지 않아요. 꺼리지 마세요. 당신이 그 식구들을 아끼는 줄 다 아니까요.\"\n\n\"제 청이 통한다면야 그분들도 분명 만나시게 될 겁니다. 돌아가는 길에 제가 미스 베이츠한테 들르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 전혀 그러실 필요 없어요. 제가 제인을 날마다 보는걸요. 하지만 좋으실 대로 하세요. 아시다시피 이건 오전 모임이에요, 나이틀리. 아주 소박한 거죠. 저는 챙 넓은 보닛을 쓰고, 작은 바구니 하나를 팔에 걸고 가겠어요. 자, 아마 이 분홍 리본 달린 바구니일 거예요. 보시다시피 이보다 더 소박할 수가 없죠. 제인도 꼭 이런 걸 들 거예요. 격식이나 과시 같은 건 없는 거예요. 일종의 집시* 모임 같은 거죠. 우리는 당신 정원을 거닐다가 딸기를 직접 따고, 나무 아래 앉을 거예요. 그 밖에 무엇을 준비하시든 다 바깥에서 하는 거예요. 아시다시피 그늘에 상을 차리는 거죠. 모든 걸 되도록 자연스럽고 소박하게요. 그게 당신 생각 아니세요?\"\n* 집시: 떠돌이 생활을 하는 사람들. 격식 없이 야외에서 즐기는 모임을 빗댄 표현.\n\n\"꼭 그렇지는 않습니다. 제가 생각하는 소박하고 자연스러운 것이란 식당에 상을 차리는 겁니다. 신사 숙녀들이, 거기에 하인들과 가구까지 딸린 사람들이 지닌 자연스러움과 소박함이란, 실내에서 식사를 할 때 가장 잘 지켜진다고 봅니다. 정원에서 딸기 따 먹는 데 싫증이 나시면, 집 안에 차가운 고기 요리가 있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 마음대로 하세요. 다만 너무 거창하게 차리지는 마시고요. 그런데 말이 나왔으니 말인데, 저나 저희 집 가정부가 의견으로라도 도움이 될 일이 있을까요? 부디 솔직히 말씀해 주세요, 나이틀리. 제가 호지스 부인하고 이야기를 한다든가, 뭘 좀 살펴봐 드리길 바라신다면—\"\n\n\"그러실 마음은 조금도 없으니, 마음만 감사히 받겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 하지만 무슨 어려움이라도 생기면, 저희 집 가정부가 아주 영리하답니다.\"\n\n\"단언컨대, 우리 집 가정부도 자기가 그 못지않게 영리하다고 자부하고 있어서, 누구의 도움이든 콧방귀를 뀔 겁니다.\"\n\n\"우리한테 당나귀가 있으면 좋을 텐데요. 우리 모두 당나귀를 타고 가는 거예요, 제인하고 미스 베이츠하고 저하고요 — 그리고 제 사랑하는 남편(caro sposo)은 옆에서 걷고요. 정말이지 그이한테 당나귀를 한 마리 사자고 말해 봐야겠어요. 시골 생활에서는 그게 일종의 필수품이라고 생각하거든요. 여자한테 아무리 소일거리가 많아도, 늘 집 안에만 틀어박혀 있을 수는 없는 노릇이잖아요. 그리고 아주 긴 산책도요, 아시다시피 — 여름엔 먼지가, 겨울엔 진창이 문제죠.\"\n\n\"돈웰과 하이베리 사이에서는 둘 다 만나실 일이 없을 겁니다. 돈웰 가는 길은 먼지가 인 적이 없고, 지금은 더없이 보송보송하니까요. 그래도 당나귀가 좋으시면 타고 오십시오. 콜 부인 것을 빌리시면 됩니다. 저는 무엇이든 되도록 부인 취향에 맞춰 드리고 싶거든요.\"\n\n\"그러시고말고요. 정말이지 저는 당신을 공정하게 봐요, 나의 좋은 친구여. 그 특유의 무뚝뚝하고 퉁명스러운 태도 아래, 당신이 더없이 따뜻한 마음을 지녔다는 걸 저는 알아요. 제가 우리 엘턴 씨한테도 늘 말하지만, 당신은 영락없는 익살꾼이에요. 그럼요, 정말이에요, 나이틀리, 이번 계획 전체에서 당신이 저한테 베푼 배려를 저는 충분히 느끼고 있어요. 저를 기쁘게 할 바로 그 일을 짚어 내셨다니까요.\"\n\n미스터 나이틀리에게는 그늘에 상을 차리기를 피하려는 또 다른 이유가 있었다. 그는 엠마뿐 아니라 미스터 우드하우스도 이 모임에 끌어들이고 싶었는데, 그들 중 누구라도 야외에 앉아 식사를 하면 미스터 우드하우스가 영락없이 탈이 나리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 미스터 우드하우스가 오전 마차 나들이며 돈웰에서 한두 시간 보내는 일이라는 그럴듯한 핑계에 넘어가, 제 발로 고생길에 들어서게 해서는 안 될 일이었다.\n\n그는 진심 어린 초대를 받았다. 어떤 숨은 두려움도 그의 순순한 믿음을 두고 그를 나무라지 못할 것이었다. 그는 응했다. 그는 이태 동안 돈웰에 가 본 적이 없었다. \"어느 아주 화창한 아침에 자기와 엠마와 해리엇이 가면 좋겠다. 사랑스러운 아가씨들이 정원을 거니는 동안 자기는 웨스턴 부인과 가만히 앉아 있으면 된다. 한낮이라면 지금쯤 그곳도 축축하지는 않을 게다. 그 옛집을 다시 보면 더없이 좋겠고, 엘턴 부부며 다른 이웃들을 만나는 것도 무척 기쁜 일이겠다. 어느 아주 화창한 아침에 자기와 엠마와 해리엇이 그리로 가는 데 아무런 거리낄 것이 없다. 미스터 나이틀리가 그들을 초대한 것은 참 잘한 일이다 — 무척 친절하고 분별 있는 처사다 — 밖에서 만찬을 드는 것보다 훨씬 사려 깊은 일이고말고. 자기는 밖에서 만찬 드는 걸 좋아하지 않으니까.\"\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 모두가 더없이 선뜻 동의해 주는 행운을 누렸다. 초대는 어디서나 워낙 잘 받아들여져서, 엘턴 부인처럼 다들 이 계획을 자기에게 베푼 각별한 예우로 받아들이는 것만 같았다. 엠마와 해리엇은 거기서 큰 즐거움을 얻으리라는 높은 기대를 내비쳤고, 미스터 웨스턴은 청하지도 않았는데 가능하면 프랭크를 데려와 합류시키겠노라고 약속했다 — 굳이 받지 않아도 좋았을 찬동과 감사의 표시였다. 그리하여 미스터 나이틀리는 그를 만나면 반갑겠다고 말할 수밖에 없었고, 미스터 웨스턴은 지체 없이 편지를 쓰고, 그가 오도록 온갖 논리를 다 동원하겠노라고 다짐했다.\n\n그러는 사이 다리를 절던 말이 어찌나 빨리 회복되었던지, 박스 힐 모임이 다시 즐거운 검토 대상으로 떠올랐다. 마침내 하루는 돈웰, 그 이튿날은 박스 힐로 정해졌는데 — 날씨도 더없이 알맞아 보였다.\n\n거의 한여름에 이른 무렵, 환한 한낮의 햇살 아래, 미스터 우드하우스는 창문 하나를 내린 마차로 무사히 모셔져 이 야외 모임에 함께하게 되었다. 그리고 애비에서 가장 안락한 방 하나, 오전 내내 불을 지펴 그를 위해 특별히 마련해 둔 방에 흐뭇하게 자리를 잡고, 더없이 편안하게, 무엇이 잘 갖춰졌는지 즐겁게 이야기할 채비를 하고서, 모두에게 와서 앉으라고, 몸을 더위에 달구지 말라고 권할 태세였다. 웨스턴 부인은 마치 일부러 지치려고, 또 내내 그의 곁에 앉아 있으려고 걸어온 사람처럼, 다른 이들이 모두 초대받거나 떠밀려 밖으로 나간 뒤에도 남아, 그의 참을성 있는 청자(聽者)이자 동조자가 되어 주었다.\n\n엠마가 애비에 와 본 지 워낙 오래되었던지라, 아버지가 편안한 것을 확인하자마자 그녀는 기꺼이 자리를 떠나 주위를 둘러보았다. 그녀 자신과 온 가족에게 언제나 그토록 흥미로울 수밖에 없는 이 집과 정원을, 더 세세히 관찰하고 더 정확히 이해하여 기억을 새로이 하고 바로잡고 싶은 열망에서였다.\n\n현재와 미래의 소유주와 맺은 인연이 떳떳이 보증해 줄 만한 온갖 정직한 자부심과 흡족함을 그녀는 느꼈다. 건물의 그 점잖은 규모와 양식을 바라보면서, 낮고 아늑하게 자리 잡은 그 적절하고 어울리며 개성 있는 위치를 바라보면서 — 시냇물이 적셔 주는 초원까지 뻗어 내려간 그 너른 정원을 바라보면서 — 애비는 옛날식의 무심한 조망 탓에 그 시내를 거의 내다보지도 못했지만 — 그리고 유행이나 사치로도 뽑아내지 못한, 줄지어 늘어서고 가로수처럼 이어진 그 무성한 수목을 바라보면서. 집은 하트필드보다 컸고, 그것과는 전혀 딴판이었다. 꽤 넓은 땅을 차지하고서, 두서없이 불규칙하게 뻗어 있었으며, 안락한 방이 많았고 멋진 방도 한둘 있었다. 그것은 마땅히 그래야 할 그대로였고, 보이는 그대로의 모습이었다 — 그리고 엠마는 혈통에서도 식견에서도 한 점 흠 없는, 그토록 진정한 양반 가문의 거처로서 그 집에 대한 존경심이 점점 더 커지는 것을 느꼈다. 존 나이틀리에게는 다소 까다로운 성미가 있었지만, 이저벨라는 나무랄 데 없이 결혼한 셈이었다. 언니는 그들에게 얼굴을 붉힐 만한 사람도, 가문도, 가산도 보태지 않았던 것이다. 이것들은 흐뭇한 감정이었고, 그녀는 다른 이들이 하는 대로 딸기밭 둘레로 모여야 할 때가 될 때까지 거닐며 그 감정에 마음껏 젖었다. 일행은 프랭크 처칠만 빼고 모두 모여 있었는데, 그는 리치먼드에서 곧 도착하리라 시시각각 기다려지는 참이었다. 엘턴 부인은 행복의 온갖 장비를 — 챙 넓은 보닛과 바구니를 — 갖추고서, 딸기를 따든 받든 이야기하든 앞장설 채비를 한껏 차린 채였다 — 이제는 딸기, 오직 딸기만을 생각하고 입에 올릴 수 있었다. \"잉글랜드 최고의 과일 — 누구나 좋아하는 것 — 늘 몸에 좋은 것. 여기가 가장 좋은 밭에 가장 좋은 품종. 직접 따 먹는 즐거움 — 정말로 즐기는 유일한 길. 아침이 단연 제일 좋은 때 — 도무지 물리지 않아 — 어느 품종이나 다 좋지만 — 하우보이가 단연 으뜸 — 비할 데가 없어 — 다른 건 거의 먹을 게 못 돼 — 하우보이는 아주 귀하지 — 칠리종이 더 낫고 — 흰 우드종이 풍미가 제일 — 런던의 딸기 값 — 브리스틀 근방엔 지천 — 메이플 그로브 — 재배법 — 밭을 언제 갈아엎을지 — 정원사들 생각은 영 딴판 — 정해진 법칙이 없어 — 정원사들은 도무지 제 방식을 못 버려 — 맛있는 과일 — 다만 많이 먹기엔 너무 진해 — 버찌만 못해 — 까치밥나무 열매가 더 개운하고 — 딸기 따기의 유일한 흠이라면 허리를 굽혀야 한다는 것 — 쨍쨍한 햇볕 — 죽도록 지쳤어 — 더는 못 견디겠어 — 가서 그늘에 좀 앉아야겠어.\"\n\n반 시간 동안 대화란 이런 식이었다 — 단 한 번 웨스턴 부인의 말로 끊겼을 뿐인데, 그녀가 의붓아들 걱정에 밖으로 나와 그가 왔는지 물었던 것이다 — 그녀는 조금 불안했다. 그가 탄 말에 무슨 일이 생겼을까 다소 걱정스러웠던 것이다.\n\n그럭저럭 그늘진 자리를 찾았다. 그러자 엠마는 엘턴 부인과 제인 페어팩스가 무슨 이야기를 하는지 본의 아니게 엿들을 수밖에 없게 되었다. 어떤 자리, 더없이 탐나는 자리가 화제였다. 엘턴 부인은 그날 아침에 그 소식을 받고는 황홀경에 빠져 있었다. 서클링 부인 댁은 아니고, 브래그 부인 댁도 아니었지만, 그 화려함과 영예에서 그 두 집에 조금 못 미칠 뿐이었다. 브래그 부인의 사촌 댁이자, 서클링 부인과 아는 사이이며, 메이플 그로브에 이름이 알려진 마님이었다. 더없이 좋고, 매력적이고, 격이 높고, 최상류 사교계, 그 권역, 그 계열, 그 지위 — 무엇 하나 빠질 게 없다는 것이었다. 그래서 엘턴 부인은 당장 그 제안을 받아들이지 못해 안달이었다. 그녀 쪽은 온통 열의와 활기와 의기양양함뿐이었다 — 그리고 미스 페어팩스가 지금으로서는 아무것에도 매이지 않겠노라며, 전에도 들은 바 있는 그 똑같은 이유들을 거듭 들어 자기 친구를 한사코 안심시키려 했지만, 엘턴 부인은 한사코 그 거절을 받아들이지 않았다. 그러면서도 엘턴 부인은 이튿날 우편으로 승낙 답장을 보낼 권한을 자기에게 달라고 우겨 댔다. 제인이 대체 그것을 어떻게 견디는지, 엠마로서는 놀라울 따름이었다. 제인은 정말로 괴로워 보였고, 정말로 날 선 말투로 말했다 — 그러다 마침내, 그녀답지 않게 단호한 행동으로 자리를 옮기자고 제안했다. \"산책이나 하면 어떨까요? 미스터 나이틀리가 정원을 — 정원 전부를 — 보여 주시지 않을까요? 전부 다 둘러보고 싶어요.\" 친구의 그 끈질김을 더는 견딜 수 없는 모양이었다.\n\n날이 더웠다. 한동안 흩어져 제각각, 셋이 함께 다니는 일도 거의 없이 정원을 거닌 끝에, 그들은 어느새 차례차례 서로를 따라 폭 넓고 길이가 짧은 보리수 가로수 길의 시원한 그늘로 들어섰다. 강에서 같은 거리를 두고 정원 너머로 뻗은 그 길은 정원 부지의 마무리인 듯했다. 그 길은 어디로도 이어지지 않았다. 다만 끝에 가서 높은 기둥이 선 낮은 돌담 너머의 전망이 펼쳐질 뿐이었는데, 그 기둥들은 세울 당시 한 번도 그곳에 있어 본 적 없는 진입로의 모양새를 내려고 의도한 듯했다. 그러나 그런 식의 마무리가 취향에 맞는지는 따져 볼 여지가 있다 해도, 그 길 자체는 더없이 호젓한 산책로였고, 그 끝을 닫는 전망은 무척이나 어여뻤다. 애비는 거의 그 기슭에 서 있던 그 제법 가파른 비탈은, 정원 부지를 벗어나면서 점점 더 가팔라졌다. 반 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에는 제법 깎아지른 듯 웅장한, 숲으로 잘 뒤덮인 둔덕이 있었다. 그리고 그 둔덕 기슭에는, 알맞게 자리 잡고 아늑하게 들어앉은 애비 밀 농장이 솟아 있었으니, 앞쪽으로는 초원이 펼쳐지고 강물은 그 둘레로 가깝고도 아름다운 곡선을 그리며 흘렀다.\n\n흐뭇한 전망이었다 — 눈에도 마음에도 흐뭇한. 잉글랜드의 푸르름, 잉글랜드의 경작지, 잉글랜드의 안락함이, 환하되 짓누르지는 않는 햇살 아래 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n이 산책로에서 엠마와 미스터 웨스턴은 다른 이들이 모두 모여 있는 것을 보았다. 그리고 이 전망 쪽으로, 엠마는 미스터 나이틀리와 해리엇이 나머지 사람들과 떨어져 조용히 앞장서 가는 것을 곧바로 알아보았다. 미스터 나이틀리와 해리엇이! 묘한 단둘이었지만, 그녀는 그 광경이 반가웠다. 한때 그가 해리엇을 동행으로 여기기조차 거북해하며 별 인사치레도 없이 등을 돌렸을 시절이 있었다. 그런데 지금 두 사람은 즐거운 대화를 나누는 듯했다. 또한 한때는 엠마가 애비 밀 농장이 그토록 잘 보이는 자리에 해리엇이 있는 것을 보면 안타까워했을 시절도 있었다. 그러나 지금은 그것이 두렵지 않았다. 그 농장은 번영과 아름다움의 온갖 부속물을 — 기름진 목초지며, 흩어진 양 떼며, 꽃핀 과수원이며, 가늘게 피어오르는 연기 기둥까지 — 죄다 거느린 채 안심하고 바라봐도 좋았다. 그녀는 담 곁에서 두 사람과 합류했는데, 둘은 주위를 둘러보기보다 이야기에 더 빠져 있었다. 그는 해리엇에게 농사법 따위에 관해 알려 주는 중이었고, 엠마는 이렇게 말하는 듯한 미소를 받았다. \"이건 내 일이니, 나는 이런 주제로 이야기할 권리가 있소. 로버트 마틴을 끌어들인다는 의심 따위 받지 않고서 말이오.\" 그녀는 그를 의심하지 않았다. 너무 오래된 이야기였다. 로버트 마틴은 아마 해리엇 생각을 그만두었을 것이다. 세 사람은 함께 산책로를 몇 차례 거닐었다. 그늘은 더없이 시원했고, 엠마는 그 시간이 그날 가장 즐거운 대목이라고 느꼈다.\n\n다음으로 자리를 옮긴 곳은 집이었다. 모두 안으로 들어가 식사를 해야 했던 것이다. 그리하여 다들 자리에 앉아 분주한데도, 프랭크 처칠은 여전히 오지 않았다. 웨스턴 부인은 보고 또 보았으나 헛일이었다. 그의 아버지는 불안하다는 내색을 하려 들지 않으며 그녀의 걱정을 웃어넘겼다. 하지만 그녀는 아들이 그 검은 암말을 좀 처분했으면 하는 바람을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그는 온다는 말을 여느 때보다 확실하게 해 둔 터였다. \"이모가 워낙 좋아지셔서 그쪽으로 건너가는 데 의심의 여지가 없다\"고. 그러나 처칠 부인의 상태란, 많은 이들이 기꺼이 일러 주었듯이, 어떤 합당한 기대를 품은 조카라도 실망시킬 만큼 그렇게 갑작스레 변하기 쉬운 것이었다. 그리하여 웨스턴 부인도 마침내, 처칠 부인의 무슨 발작 탓에 그가 오지 못하는 게 틀림없다고 믿거나, 적어도 그렇게 말하게끔 설득되었다. 이 문제가 거론되는 동안 엠마는 해리엇을 바라보았다. 해리엇은 아주 의젓하게 굴며 아무런 동요도 내비치지 않았다.\n\n차가운 식사가 끝나자, 일행은 아직 못 본 것을 보러 다시 한번 밖으로 나가기로 했다. 그 옛 애비의 양어지(養魚池)며, 어쩌면 이튿날 베기 시작할 클로버밭까지 가 보거나, 아니면 적어도 더워졌다가 다시 식어 가는 즐거움이라도 누려 보자는 것이었다. 미스터 우드하우스는 이미 정원에서 가장 높은 곳을 잠깐 한 바퀴 돌고 온 터였는데 — 그곳은 그조차도 강에서 올라오는 습기 따위는 없으리라 여기는 데였다 — 더는 움직이지 않았다. 그리고 그의 딸은, 웨스턴 부인이 남편의 권유로 기분 전환에 필요해 보이는 운동과 변화를 누리도록, 자기는 아버지 곁에 남기로 마음먹었다.\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 미스터 우드하우스를 즐겁게 해 드리려 할 수 있는 일을 다 해 두었다. 판화 책이며, 메달과 카메오와 산호와 조개껍데기를 담은 서랍이며, 그 진열장 안에 든 온갖 다른 가문의 수집품을, 그 오랜 벗이 오전 한나절을 보내는 데 쓰도록 미리 준비해 둔 것이었다. 그리고 그 친절은 더없이 잘 들어맞았다. 미스터 우드하우스는 무척이나 즐거웠다. 웨스턴 부인이 그 모든 것을 그에게 보여 주었고, 이제 그는 그 모든 것을 엠마에게 보여 주려던 참이었다. 그가 본 것에 도무지 안목이 없다는 점 말고는 아이를 닮은 구석이 없다는 점이 다행이었으니, 그는 느릿느릿하고, 한결같으며, 꼼꼼했다. 그러나 이 두 번째 구경이 시작되기 전에, 엠마는 잠깐 동안 마음껏 현관과 집 앞뜰을 살펴볼 요량으로 홀로 걸어 나갔다 — 그런데 거기 들어선 지 얼마 되지도 않아 제인 페어팩스가 정원에서 황급히, 무언가에서 달아나는 듯한 표정으로 들어왔다. 미스 우드하우스를 이렇게 빨리 만나리라곤 거의 예상치 못했던지라 처음에는 흠칫 놀랐다. 그러나 그녀가 찾던 사람이 바로 미스 우드하우스였다.\n\n\"부탁 하나 들어주시겠어요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 안 보인다고 누가 찾거든, 집에 갔다고 말씀해 주시겠어요? 저는 지금 바로 가려고요. 이모는 시간이 이렇게 늦은 줄도, 우리가 이렇게 오래 자리를 비운 줄도 모르세요. 하지만 분명 우릴 찾을 테니, 저는 지금 곧장 가기로 마음먹었어요. 아무한테도 이 얘긴 안 했어요. 괜히 폐만 끼치고 걱정만 안길 테니까요. 몇 분은 양어지로, 몇 분은 보리수 길로 갔어요. 다들 돌아오기 전까지는 제가 안 보여도 모를 거예요. 그러다 알아채거든, 제가 갔다고 좀 말씀해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"물론이죠, 원하신다면요. 하지만 혼자서 하이베리까지 걸어가시려는 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"네 — 무슨 일이야 있겠어요? 제가 걸음이 빨라요. 이십 분이면 집에 가요.\"\n\n\"하지만 너무 멀어요, 정말이지, 혼자 걷기엔요. 우리 아버지네 하인을 딸려 보낼게요. 마차를 내오라고 할게요. 오 분이면 돌아 나올 거예요.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 고마워요 — 하지만 절대 안 돼요. 차라리 걷는 게 좋아요. 그리고 제가 혼자 걷기를 무서워하다니요! 머지않아 남을 지켜 줘야 할지도 모르는 제가요!\"\n\n그녀는 몹시 격앙되어 말했다. 그러자 엠마가 진심으로 마음을 담아 답했다. \"그게 지금 당신을 위험에 내맡길 까닭은 될 수 없어요. 제가 마차를 내오라고 해야겠어요. 더위만 해도 위험할 거예요. 당신은 이미 지쳤잖아요.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"지쳤어요. 하지만 이건 그런 종류의 피로가 아니에요 — 빨리 걸으면 기운이 날 거예요. 미스 우드하우스, 우리 모두 마음이 지치는 게 어떤 건지 가끔은 알잖아요. 솔직히 제 마음은 다 소진됐어요. 저한테 베풀어 주실 수 있는 가장 큰 친절은, 제 뜻대로 하게 두시고, 꼭 필요할 때 제가 갔다고만 말씀해 주시는 거예요.\"\n\n엠마는 더 막을 말이 없었다. 그녀는 모든 것을 알아차렸다. 그래서 그 심정에 공감하며, 그녀가 당장 집을 떠나도록 도왔고, 벗다운 정성으로 그녀가 무사히 가는 것을 지켜보았다. 헤어지며 보낸 그녀의 눈길은 고마움에 차 있었다 — 그리고 헤어지며 한 그녀의 말, \"아! 미스 우드하우스, 가끔 혼자 있을 수 있다는 게 얼마나 위안이 되는지요!\"는 가득 찬 가슴에서 터져 나오는 듯했고, 그녀를 가장 아끼는 이들에게조차 그녀가 늘 견뎌 내야 하는 무언가를 어렴풋이 일러 주는 듯했다.\n\n\"하긴, 그런 집에! 그런 이모라니!\" 엠마는 다시 홀 안으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"정말 가엾어요. 그 정당한 끔찍함을 당신이 더 예민하게 내비칠수록, 나는 당신을 더 좋아하게 될 거예요.\"\n\n제인이 떠난 지 십오 분도 채 되지 않아, 그들이 겨우 베네치아의 산마르코 광장 풍경 몇 점을 들여다본 참에, 프랭크 처칠이 방으로 들어섰다. 엠마는 그를 생각하던 중도 아니었고, 그를 생각하는 것조차 잊고 있던 터였다 — 그러나 그를 보니 무척 반가웠다. 웨스턴 부인이 한시름 놓을 것이었다. 검은 암말에는 죄가 없었다. 처칠 부인을 그 까닭으로 짚었던 이들이 옳았던 것이다. 그는 이모의 병이 일시적으로 도지는 바람에 발이 묶여 있었다 — 몇 시간이나 이어진 신경 발작이었다 — 그래서 그는 아주 늦게까지 올 생각을 깡그리 접고 있었다. 그리고 길이 이렇게 더울 줄, 아무리 서둘러도 이렇게 늦을 줄 알았더라면 아예 오지 않았으리라는 것이었다. 더위가 극심했다. 이런 더위는 겪어 본 적이 없었다 — 차라리 집에 있을 걸 그랬다 싶기까지 했다 — 더위만큼 그를 못살게 구는 게 없었다 — 추위라면 아무리 심해도 견디겠지만, 더위는 도무지 못 참겠다는 것이었다 — 그러더니 그는 미스터 우드하우스의 가까스로 남은 불기운에서 되도록 멀찍이 떨어져, 무척이나 가련한 몰골로 주저앉았다.\n\n\"가만히 앉아 계시면 곧 시원해질 거예요.\" 엠마가 말했다.\n\n\"시원해지는 대로 곧장 돌아가야 합니다. 제가 빠지면 영 곤란한데도 — 제가 와야 한다고 어찌나들 못 박아 두셨던지요! 다들 곧 돌아가시겠죠. 일행이 다 흩어지는 거겠고요. 오는 길에 한 사람을 만났는데 — 이런 날씨에 미친 짓이지요! 그야말로 미친 짓이에요!\"\n\n엠마는 듣고 또 살피다가, 곧 프랭크 처칠의 상태는 심사가 뒤틀렸다는 그 적절한 표현으로 가장 잘 설명되겠다는 것을 알아챘다. 어떤 사람들은 더우면 으레 짜증을 냈다. 그것이 그의 체질일지도 몰랐다. 그리고 그녀는 이런 일시적인 푸념에는 먹고 마시는 것이 약일 때가 많다는 것을 알았기에, 그에게 뭔가 요기를 하라고 권했다. 식당에 가면 온갖 것이 넉넉히 있을 거라고 — 그러고는 인정스럽게 문을 가리켜 보였다.\n\n\"아니요 — 먹지 않겠습니다. 배도 안 고프고요. 더 더워지기만 할 겁니다.\" 그러나 이 분도 채 못 되어 그는 스스로에게 너그러워져서는, 가문비나무 맥주가 어쩌고 중얼거리며 걸어 나갔다. 엠마는 다시 온 신경을 아버지에게 돌리고는, 속으로 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"저 사람한테 더는 빠져 있지 않게 되어 다행이야. 더운 아침나절 하나에 저렇게 금세 심사가 뒤틀리는 남자는 사양이지. 해리엇의 달콤하고 느긋한 성미라면 저런 걸 개의치 않을 테고.\"\n\n그가 자리를 비운 동안은 아주 흡족하게 식사를 하고도 남을 만큼이었고, 돌아왔을 때는 한결 나아져 있었다 — 아주 시원해진 데다 — 평소의 그답게 예의 바른 모습으로 — 그들 곁에 의자를 바짝 끌어다 놓고 그들이 보고 있는 것에 관심을 가지며, 자기가 이렇게 늦은 것을 합당한 정도로 아쉬워할 줄도 알았다. 그는 기분이 최상은 아니었지만, 그것을 끌어올리려 애쓰는 듯했다. 그리고 마침내, 아주 기분 좋게 실없는 소리를 늘어놓기에 이르렀다. 그들은 스위스 풍경을 들여다보던 참이었다.\n\n\"이모가 회복되시는 대로, 저는 외국에 갈 겁니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"이런 곳들을 좀 보기 전엔 도무지 마음이 편치 않을 거예요. 언젠가 제 스케치를 보시게 되거나 — 제 여행기를 읽으시게 되거나 — 제 시를 읽으시게 될 겁니다. 무언가 해서 제 망신을 톡톡히 살 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르죠 — 하지만 스위스 스케치로는 아니에요. 당신은 스위스엔 절대 못 가요. 당신 외삼촌과 외숙모는 절대 당신을 잉글랜드 밖으로 내보내지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그분들도 함께 가시도록 끌어낼 수 있을지 모르죠. 외숙모한테 따뜻한 기후가 처방될 수도 있고요. 저는 우리 모두 외국에 가게 되리라는 기대를 절반 넘게 품고 있어요. 정말이에요. 오늘 아침, 머지않아 제가 외국에 가 있으리라는 강한 확신이 들거든요. 저는 여행을 해야 해요. 아무것도 안 하는 데 이젠 진력이 났어요. 변화가 필요해요. 진심이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 당신의 그 꿰뚫어 보는 눈이 어떻게 짐작하시든 간에 — 저는 잉글랜드가 지긋지긋해요 — 그리고 할 수만 있다면 내일이라도 떠나고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 번영과 호사에 진력이 난 거예요. 자기한테 고생거리를 몇 가지 지어내서, 머무는 데 만족할 수는 없나요?\"\n\n\"제가 번영과 호사에 진력이 났다고요! 단단히 잘못 보셨어요. 저는 제 자신을 번영했다고도, 호강한다고도 여기지 않아요. 저는 무엇 하나 실질적인 것에선 죄다 가로막혀 있어요. 제 자신을 도통 운 좋은 사람이라 여기지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그래도 처음 오셨을 때만큼 비참하지는 않잖아요. 가서 좀 더 먹고 마시고 오세요. 그럼 아주 괜찮아질 거예요. 차가운 고기 한 점 더, 마데이라 포도주에 물 탄 거 한 잔 더면, 우리 나머지 사람들하고 거의 비슷해질걸요.\"\n\n\"아니요 — 꼼짝 안 하겠습니다. 당신 곁에 앉아 있겠어요. 당신이 제 최고의 약이니까요.\"\n\n\"우리는 내일 박스 힐에 가요. 당신도 함께해요. 스위스는 아니지만, 변화가 그토록 절실한 젊은이한테는 그래도 뭔가는 되겠죠. 머물렀다가 우리랑 같이 가요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 절대 안 됩니다. 저는 저녁 선선할 때 집에 가야 해요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내일 아침 선선할 때 다시 오면 되잖아요.\"\n\n\"아니요 — 그럴 만한 가치가 없어요. 오면 저는 심사가 뒤틀릴 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 부디 리치먼드에 계세요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그러면 더 심사가 뒤틀릴걸요. 당신들이 다 거기 있는데 저만 빠진다고 생각하면 도저히 못 견디겠어요.\"\n\n\"그건 당신 스스로 풀어야 할 난제로군요. 뒤틀릴 정도는 당신이 알아서 고르세요. 저는 더는 강권하지 않겠어요.\"\n\n이제 나머지 일행이 돌아오는 중이었고, 곧 모두 한자리에 모였다. 더러는 프랭크 처칠을 보고 크게 기뻐했고, 더러는 아주 담담하게 받아들였다. 그러나 미스 페어팩스가 사라진 사연이 밝혀지자 사뭇 두루 안타까워하고 술렁였다. 이제 모두 돌아갈 때가 되었다는 말로 그 화제는 마무리되었고, 이튿날 일정을 짤막하게 마지막으로 매듭짓고서 그들은 헤어졌다. 프랭크 처칠은 빠지기 싫은 마음이 어찌나 커졌던지, 엠마에게 건넨 마지막 말은 이러했다.\n\n\"좋아요. 당신이 제가 남아서 일행에 끼기를 바라신다면, 그렇게 하겠습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 미소로 그 청을 받아들였다. 그리고 리치먼드에서 부르는 일이 아니고서는, 그 무엇도 이튿날 저녁 전에 그를 데려가지 못할 것이었다.\n\n# 제3권 7장\n\n박스 힐로 나서기에 더없이 좋은 날이었다. 일정과 채비, 시간 맞추기 등 다른 모든 외적 조건도 즐거운 모임이 되도록 두루 도왔다. 미스터 웨스턴이 전체를 도맡아 하트필드와 사제관 사이를 무난히 오가며 일을 보았고, 모두가 제때 모였다. 엠마와 해리엇이 함께 갔고, 미스 베이츠와 그 조카는 엘턴 부부와, 신사들은 말을 타고 갔다. 웨스턴 부인은 미스터 우드하우스 곁에 남았다. 그곳에 닿기만 하면 즐거워하는 일밖에 남지 않을 듯했다. 칠 마일을 즐거움에 부풀어 달려갔고, 처음 도착했을 때는 다들 한바탕 감탄을 쏟아 냈다. 그러나 하루를 통틀어 보면 어딘가 미진한 데가 있었다. 어찌해도 떨쳐지지 않는 나른함, 흥의 부족, 어울림의 부족이 있었다. 그들은 패가 너무 갈렸다. 엘턴 부부는 둘이서 걸었고, 미스터 나이틀리는 미스 베이츠와 제인을 맡았으며, 엠마와 해리엇은 프랭크 처칠 차지였다. 그리고 미스터 웨스턴은 그들을 좀 더 잘 어우러지게 하려고 애썼으나 헛수고였다. 처음엔 우연한 갈림처럼 보였으나, 끝내 그 짜임은 별로 달라지지 않았다. 사실 엘턴 부부는 어울려 가능한 한 상냥하게 굴기를 마다하지 않았다. 그러나 언덕에서 보낸 꼬박 두 시간 내내, 다른 패들 사이에는 어떤 멋진 경치로도, 어떤 차가운 간식으로도, 어떤 쾌활한 미스터 웨스턴으로도 풀어 낼 수 없을 만큼 강한 분리의 원리가 작동하는 듯했다.\n\n처음에 엠마에게 그것은 그야말로 따분함이었다. 프랭크 처칠이 그토록 말 없고 멍청해 보이는 것은 처음이었다. 들을 만한 말은 한마디도 하지 않았고 — 보면서도 보지 못했으며 — 분별 없이 감탄했고 — 그녀가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 듣고 있었다. 그가 그토록 따분하니, 해리엇 역시 따분해지는 것도 당연했다. 두 사람 다 못 견딜 지경이었다.\n\n다들 자리에 앉으니 한결 나았다. 엠마의 취향에는 훨씬 더 나았으니, 프랭크 처칠이 수다스럽고 명랑해지면서 그녀를 으뜸가는 상대로 삼은 것이다. 베풀 수 있는 온갖 각별한 배려가 그녀에게 쏟아졌다. 그녀를 즐겁게 하고 그녀의 눈에 들기만 하면 다른 데는 관심도 없는 듯했다 — 그리고 엠마는 분위기가 살아나는 것이 반갑고 추어주는 것도 싫지 않아, 그녀 역시 명랑하고 편안하게, 두 사람의 교제에서 가장 처음이자 가장 들떴던 시절에 보내곤 했던 그 다정한 격려를, 그 정중한 수작을 허락하는 빛을 모두 그에게 내주었다. 다만 이제 그녀 자신의 헤아림으로는 아무 의미 없는 일이었지만, 곁에서 지켜보는 대부분의 사람의 판단으로는 영어로 '시시덕거림'이라는 말 말고는 달리 표현하기 어려운 모양새였음이 분명했다. \"미스터 프랭크 처칠과 미스 우드하우스가 지나치게 시시덕거렸다.\" 두 사람은 바로 그런 말을 들을 빌미를 스스로 내주고 있었던 것이다 — 한 부인이 메이플 그로브로, 또 한 부인이 아일랜드로 그 소문을 편지에 실어 보낼 빌미를. 그렇다고 엠마가 무슨 진짜 행복에서 명랑하고 들떠 있었던 것은 아니었다. 오히려 기대했던 것보다 덜 행복하다고 느꼈기 때문이었다. 그녀는 실망했기에 웃었다. 그의 배려가 마음에 들었고, 그것이 우정에서든 흠모에서든 장난기에서든 더없이 분별 있다고 여겼으나, 그 배려가 그녀의 마음을 되돌려 놓지는 못했다. 그녀는 여전히 그를 벗으로만 여길 작정이었다.\n\n\"오늘 오라고 말해 주셔서 얼마나 고마운지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신이 아니었으면 저는 분명 이 모임의 즐거움을 송두리째 놓쳤을 겁니다. 다시 떠나 버리기로 아주 마음을 굳혔었거든요.\"\n\n\"네, 무척 토라져 계셨죠. 가장 좋은 딸기가 다 떨어진 다음에야 오신 것 말고는 뭣 때문이었는지 모르겠지만요. 저는 당신이 받을 자격보다 더 친절한 벗이었어요. 하지만 당신은 겸손하셨죠. 부디 오라고 명해 달라고 그렇게 사정하셨으니까요.\"\n\n\"토라졌다고는 하지 마십시오. 피곤했던 겁니다. 더위에 지쳤던 거죠.\"\n\n\"오늘이 더 더운데요.\"\n\n\"제 느낌으로는 아닙니다. 오늘은 더할 나위 없이 편안한걸요.\"\n\n\"당신이 명을 받들고 있으니까 편안한 거죠.\"\n\n\"당신의 명을요? — 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"아마 제가 그렇게 말하도록 유도하긴 했지만, 제 뜻은 스스로를 다스리는 것이었어요. 당신은 어제 어쩐 일인지 도를 넘어, 제 스스로 다잡던 데서 벗어나 달아나 버렸죠. 그러나 오늘은 다시 제자리로 돌아오셨고요 — 그리고 제가 늘 곁에 있어 드릴 수는 없으니, 당신의 성미가 제 다스림이 아니라 당신 스스로의 다스림 아래 있다고 믿는 편이 가장 좋겠지요.\"\n\n\"그게 그거지요. 저는 동기가 없으면 스스로를 다스릴 수가 없습니다. 당신이 말을 하든 않든 제게 명을 내리는 거니까요. 그리고 당신은 늘 제 곁에 있을 수 있습니다. 늘 제 곁에 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"어제 세 시부터 따져서 말이죠. 저의 영원한 영향력이 그보다 더 일찍 시작됐을 리는 없잖아요. 그랬다면 그 전에 그토록 심사가 뒤틀려 계시진 않았을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"어제 세 시라니요! 그게 당신이 잡은 날짜군요. 저는 처음 당신을 뵌 게 이월이라고 생각했는데요.\"\n\n\"당신의 정중한 수작은 정말이지 당해 낼 도리가 없네요. 하지만 (목소리를 낮추며) — 우리 둘 말고는 아무도 입을 열지 않는데, 입 다문 일곱 사람을 즐겁게 하자고 이런 실없는 소리를 늘어놓는 건 좀 지나친 일이에요.\"\n\n\"저는 부끄러울 말은 하나도 하지 않습니다.\" 그가 짓궂은 뻔뻔함으로 응수했다. \"저는 이월에 당신을 처음 뵈었습니다. 언덕 위 사람들이 다 들으라죠, 들을 수만 있다면. 제 목청을 한쪽으로는 미클햄까지, 다른 쪽으로는 도킹까지 퍼뜨리게 두십시오. 저는 이월에 당신을 처음 뵈었습니다.\" 그러고는 속삭이며 — \"우리 일행은 지독하게 멍청하군요. 저들을 깨우려면 뭘 해야 할까요? 무슨 실없는 소리든 통할 겁니다. 저들로 하여금 입을 열게 하지요. 신사 숙녀 여러분, 미스 우드하우스의 (어디에 계시든 그분이 좌장이십니다) 명을 받아 전합니다. 그분이 여러분 모두 무슨 생각을 하고 계신지 알고 싶어 하십니다.\"\n\n몇몇은 웃으며 너그러이 대답했다. 미스 베이츠는 한참을 떠들어 댔고, 엘턴 부인은 미스 우드하우스가 좌장이라는 생각에 잔뜩 부풀어 올랐다. 미스터 나이틀리의 대답이 가장 또렷했다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스는 우리가 다들 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지 정말로 듣고 싶은 것이 확실한가?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 엠마가 애써 태연하게 웃으며 외쳤다. \"절대로 그럴 리가요. 지금 그 화살을 맞는 일이야말로 제가 가장 사양하고 싶은 거예요. 여러분이 다들 무슨 생각을 하시는지, 그것만 빼고는 무엇이든 듣겠어요. 아주 다는 아니라고 해 두죠. 한두 분쯤은 (미스터 웨스턴과 해리엇을 흘끗 보며) 그 생각을 알아도 겁날 게 없을 분이 계실지도 모르니까요.\"\n\n\"그런 건,\" 엘턴 부인이 힘주어 외쳤다. \"제 생각엔 캐물어도 좋을 만큼 제게 허락된 일이 아니에요. 비록, 어쩌면, 이 모임의 보호자로서는 — 저는 한 번도 어떤 모임에서도 — 답사 모임이라느니 — 아가씨들이라느니 — 유부녀들이라느니 —\"\n\n그 중얼거림은 대개 남편을 향한 것이었고, 남편은 이렇게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"지당하오, 여보, 지당해. 정말 그렇소 — 참으로 듣도 보도 못한 일이지 — 하지만 더러 무슨 말이든 함부로 하는 부인네들이 있으니. 농담으로 넘기는 게 낫겠소. 당신께 합당한 대우가 무엇인지는 다들 아니까.\"\n\n\"안 되겠습니다.\" 프랭크가 엠마에게 속삭였다. \"대부분 비위가 상했어요. 좀 더 솜씨 있게 공략해 보지요. 신사 숙녀 여러분 — 미스 우드하우스의 명을 받아 전합니다. 그분께서는 여러분이 정확히 무슨 생각을 하시는지 알 권리는 포기하시고, 다만 두루 보아 아주 재미있는 것 한 가지씩만을 각자에게 청하십니다. 여기 저 말고도 일곱 분이 계신데 (그분께서 친절히도 저는 이미 무척 재미있는 사람이라 해 주셨고요), 각자에게 청하시는 건 이것뿐입니다 — 아주 재치 있는 것 한 가지, 산문이든 운문이든, 직접 지은 것이든 빌려 온 것이든 — 또는 그럭저럭 재치 있는 것 두 가지 — 또는 정말이지 따분하기 짝이 없는 것 세 가지. 그러면 그분께서 그 모두에 진심으로 웃어 주시겠다고 약속하십니다.\"\n\n\"오! 잘됐네요.\" 미스 베이츠가 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 마음 졸일 게 없겠어요. '정말이지 따분하기 짝이 없는 것 세 가지'라니. 그건 제게 딱 맞는 거잖아요, 안 그래요. 저야 입만 열면 따분한 소리 세 가지쯤은 틀림없이 할 테니까요, 그렇죠? (둘러보며 더없이 선량하게 모두의 동의를 구하면서) — 여러분도 다들 제가 그러리라 생각하시죠?\"\n\n엠마는 도저히 참을 수 없었다.\n\n\"아! 부인, 하지만 좀 곤란한 점이 있을지도 몰라요. 용서하세요 — 하지만 개수에 제한이 있을 텐데요 — 한 번에 세 가지까지만요.\"\n\n미스 베이츠는 엠마의 짐짓 점잔 빼는 태도에 속아 그 뜻을 곧바로 알아채지 못했다. 그러나 그 뜻이 불쑥 와닿자, 화를 내지는 못했지만, 살짝 붉어진 얼굴은 그것이 그녀를 아프게 할 수 있음을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"아! — 그렇군요 — 그럼요. 네, 무슨 뜻인지 알겠어요. (미스터 나이틀리 쪽으로 돌아서며) 입을 다물도록 애써 보지요. 제가 어지간히 거슬리는 사람인가 봐요. 안 그러면 오랜 벗한테 저런 말을 하셨을 리가 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"당신 계획이 마음에 드오.\" 미스터 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"찬성이오, 찬성. 나도 최선을 다해 보지. 수수께끼를 하나 만들고 있는데. 수수께끼는 몇 점쯤 쳐주려나?\"\n\n\"낮을 겁니다, 아버지, 아주 낮을 거예요.\" 아들이 대답했다. \"하지만 너그러이 봐 드리지요 — 특히 길을 트는 분께는요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"낮게 치지 않을 거예요. 미스터 웨스턴의 수수께끼라면 당신과 옆자리 분까지 면제해 드리죠. 자, 어서 들려주세요.\"\n\n\"나도 그게 그리 재치 있을지는 미덥지 않소.\" 미스터 웨스턴이 말했다. \"너무 뻔한 사실이라서 말이야. 하지만 여기 있소 — 알파벳 두 글자로 완벽을 나타내는 게 뭘까?\"\n\n\"두 글자라니요! — 완벽을 나타낸다고요! 정말 모르겠는데요.\"\n\n\"아! 절대 못 맞힐 거요. 당신은 (엠마에게) 분명 절대 못 맞힐 거요. — 내가 알려 주지. — M과 A. — 엠-마(Em-ma). — 알겠소?\"\n\n깨달음과 흐뭇함이 함께 찾아왔다. 그것은 지극히 시시한 재담이었을지 모르나, 엠마는 거기서 웃고 즐길 거리를 잔뜩 찾아냈다 — 프랭크와 해리엇도 마찬가지였다. — 나머지 일행에게는 그만큼 가닿지 않는 듯했다. 몇몇은 그것을 두고 영 멍한 얼굴이었고, 미스터 나이틀리는 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"이걸로 어떤 종류의 재치 있는 것이 필요한지가 분명해졌군. 미스터 웨스턴은 자기 몫을 아주 잘 해내셨소. 하지만 다른 사람들 기를 죄다 꺾어 놓으셨겠는걸. '완벽'이 이렇게 일찍 나와 버리면 곤란하지요.\"\n\n\"오! 저로 말하자면, 단연코 면제받아야겠어요.\" 엘턴 부인이 말했다. \"저는 정말이지 엄두도 못 내겠어요 — 저는 그런 걸 도통 좋아하지 않거든요. 한번은 제 이름을 가지고 만든 이합체시*를 받은 적이 있는데, 조금도 마음에 들지 않았답니다. 누가 보냈는지도 알았고요. 가증스러운 애송이였죠! — 누군지 아시잖아요 (남편에게 고갯짓하며). 이런 건 성탄절에 난롯가에 둘러앉았을 때나 어울리지, 제 생각엔 여름에 시골을 답사하러 다닐 때는 영 어울리지 않아요. 미스 우드하우스가 저를 너그러이 봐주셔야겠어요. 저는 아무에게나 재치 있는 것을 내드릴 그런 사람이 아니에요. 재담가인 척하지도 않고요. 제 나름의 생기야 넘치도록 있지만, 언제 말하고 언제 입을 다물지는 제가 가려서 판단하게 해 주셔야지요. 저희는 건너뛰어 주세요, 미스터 처칠. 미스터 E.와 나이틀리, 제인, 그리고 저까지 건너뛰어 주세요. 저희는 재치 있게 할 말이 하나도 없으니까요 — 한 사람도요.\"\n* 이합체시(acrostic): 각 행의 첫 글자를 위에서 아래로 모으면 어떤 단어나 이름이 되도록 지은 시.\n\n\"그래요, 그래, 제발 나를 건너뛰어 주시오.\" 남편이 비웃는 듯 의식하며 덧붙였다. \"나는 미스 우드하우스나 다른 어떤 아가씨를 즐겁게 할 말이라곤 하나도 없으니까. 늙은 유부남이란 영 쓸모가 없는 법이지. 우리는 좀 걸을까, 오거스타?\"\n\n\"그러죠, 기꺼이요. 한자리에서 이렇게 오래 답사하고 있자니 정말 지겨워요. 자, 제인, 내 다른 팔을 잡아요.\"\n\n그러나 제인은 사양했고, 부부는 걸어가 버렸다. \"행복한 한 쌍이군요!\" 두 사람이 들리지 않을 만큼 멀어지자마자 프랭크 처칠이 말했다. \"얼마나 잘 어울립니까! — 참 운도 좋지요 — 고작 공공장소에서 사귄 인연으로 저렇게 결혼했는데도 말이에요! — 제 생각엔 바스에서 겨우 몇 주 알고 지낸 사이였을걸요! 유난히 운이 좋았던 거죠! — 바스든 어떤 공공장소든, 한 사람의 성품을 진짜로 알게 해 줄 수 있느냐 하면 — 그건 다 아무것도 아니거든요. 알 길이 없으니까요. 여자를 자기 집에서, 늘 어울리는 사람들 틈에서, 평소 그대로의 모습으로 보아야만 비로소 제대로 된 판단을 내릴 수 있는 법이지요. 그게 못 미치면 죄다 짐작이고 운인데 — 대개는 불운으로 끝나고요. 짧은 교제만 믿고 제 몸을 묶었다가 평생을 두고 후회한 사내가 얼마나 많습니까!\"\n\n여태 제 편들끼리가 아니고는 좀처럼 입을 열지 않던 미스 페어팩스가 이번에는 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"그런 일이야 분명히 일어나지요.\" — 그녀는 기침 때문에 말을 멈췄다. 프랭크 처칠이 귀 기울이려 그녀 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀을 하시려던 거죠.\" 그가 진지하게 말했다. 그녀는 목소리를 가다듬었다.\n\n\"저는 다만 이런 말을 하려던 거예요. 그런 불행한 처지가 남자에게든 여자에게든 더러 닥치기는 하지만, 그리 흔한 일이라고는 생각되지 않는다고요. 성급하고 경솔한 연정이 생겨날 수는 있겠지요 — 하지만 대개는 나중에 거기서 회복할 시간이 있는 법이에요. 제 말씀의 뜻은, 불행한 인연을 두고두고 불편으로, 억압으로 떠안고 괴로워하는 사람은 (그 행복이 늘 우연의 손에 좌우될 수밖에 없는) 오직 마음이 약하고 줏대 없는 사람들뿐이라는 거예요.\"\n\n그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그저 바라보며 순순히 고개를 숙였을 뿐이다. 그러고는 이내 명랑한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"음, 저는 제 판단을 너무 못 미더워해서, 제가 결혼할 때는 누가 제 아내를 골라 주면 좋겠습니다. 그래 주시겠어요? (엠마 쪽으로 돌아서며.) 제 아내를 골라 주시겠습니까? — 당신이 점찍은 사람이라면 누구든 마음에 들 게 분명하거든요. 당신은 이 집안 일을 도맡으시잖아요. (아버지를 향해 미소 지으며.) 누구든 찾아 주세요. 서두를 건 없습니다. 양녀로 거두어, 가르쳐 주세요.\"\n\n\"그래서 저를 닮게 만들고요.\"\n\n\"꼭 그렇게요, 그럴 수만 있다면.\"\n\n\"좋아요. 그 일을 맡지요. 매력적인 아내를 갖게 해 드릴게요.\"\n\n\"무척 활달해야 하고, 개암빛 눈을 가져야 합니다. 다른 건 아무래도 좋아요. 저는 한 이 년 외국에 나가 있다가 — 돌아오면 당신께 제 아내를 찾으러 오겠습니다. 기억해 두세요.\"\n\n엠마가 그것을 잊을 염려는 없었다. 그것은 그녀가 가장 아끼는 마음을 모두 건드리는 부탁이었다. 해리엇이야말로 그가 말한 바로 그 사람 아니겠는가? 개암빛 눈만 빼면, 이 년이 더 지나는 사이에 그녀는 그가 바라는 모든 것을 갖추게 될지도 몰랐다. 어쩌면 그가 지금 이 순간에도 해리엇을 마음에 두고 있는지도 모를 일이었다. 누가 알겠는가? 교육을 그녀에게 맡긴다는 것이 그것을 넌지시 비추는 듯했다.\n\n\"자, 이모.\" 제인이 이모에게 말했다. \"이제 엘턴 부인한테 합류할까요?\"\n\n\"그러자꾸나, 얘야. 기꺼이. 나도 다 됐단다. 진작 그분과 함께 갈 채비가 돼 있었는데, 이래도 마찬가지지. 곧 따라잡을 게다. 저기 계시네 — 아니, 저건 다른 사람이구나. 아일랜드 마차 일행의 부인들 중 한 분이지, 그분하고는 전혀 다르잖니. — 어머, 세상에 —\"\n\n그들은 걸어가 버렸고, 반 분쯤 뒤에 미스터 나이틀리가 그 뒤를 따랐다. 미스터 웨스턴과 그 아들, 엠마와 해리엇만 남았다. 그러자 젊은이의 들뜸은 거의 거북할 지경으로 치솟았다. 엠마조차 마침내 추어주기와 흥겨움에 싫증이 나, 차라리 다른 누군가와 조용히 거닐거나, 거의 혼자 떨어져 아무도 신경 쓰지 않는 가운데 발아래 펼쳐진 아름다운 경치를 잠잠히 바라보고 있었으면 싶었다. 마차가 왔음을 알리려고 하인들이 그들을 찾아 두리번거리는 모습이 보이자 반가웠다. 그리고 짐을 챙겨 떠날 채비를 하는 부산함도, 제 마차를 먼저 대게 하려는 엘턴 부인의 안달도, 이 즐거움의 날의 미심쩍기 짝이 없는 흥취를 닫아 줄 조용한 귀갓길을 떠올리니 기꺼이 견딜 만했다. 이토록 안 맞는 사람들로 짜인 이런 모임에 또다시 끌려드는 일은 두 번 다시 없기를 그녀는 바랐다.\n\n마차를 기다리는 동안, 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리가 제 곁에 와 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 가까이에 아무도 없는지 살피듯 주위를 둘러보더니 말했다.\n\n\"엠마, 늘 해 오던 대로 한 번 더 말해야겠소. 어쩌면 허락받았다기보다 그저 참아 주어 온 권리인지도 모르지만, 그래도 나는 그 권리를 써야겠소. 나는 자네가 그르게 행동하는 걸 보고도 한마디 안 할 수가 없으니. 어떻게 미스 베이츠에게 그토록 매정하게 굴 수 있었나? 그런 성품에, 그런 나이에, 그런 처지의 여인에게 어떻게 그토록 무례한 재담을 던질 수가 있었나? — 엠마, 나는 자네가 그러리라고는 생각도 못 했네.\"\n\n엠마는 그 일을 떠올리고는 얼굴을 붉히며 미안해했지만, 웃어넘기려 했다.\n\n\"아니, 제가 그 말을 어떻게 안 할 수가 있었겠어요? — 누구라도 참기 어려웠을걸요. 그렇게 심한 것도 아니었어요. 그분은 분명 제 말뜻을 못 알아들으셨을 거예요.\"\n\n\"단언컨대 알아들었네. 자네 말뜻을 죄다 알아들었어. 그 뒤로도 그 이야기를 하더군. 그분이 그 이야기를 어떻게 하던지 자네가 들었더라면 좋았을 텐데 — 얼마나 솔직하고 너그럽게 하던지. 자네와 자네 아버지한테서 늘 받아 온 그 갖은 친절을, 그토록 따분할 수밖에 없는 자기와의 교제를 그렇게 참아 가며 베풀어 주었다고, 자네의 그 인내를 얼마나 우러러 말하던지 자네가 들었더라면.\"\n\n\"오!\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"그분만큼 마음씨 좋은 사람이 세상에 없다는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 인정하셔야죠, 그분에게는 안된 일이지만 좋은 점과 우스꽝스러운 점이 어쩜 그렇게 뒤섞여 있는지를요.\"\n\n\"뒤섞여 있지.\" 그가 말했다. \"인정하네. 그리고 그분이 넉넉한 처지라면, 좋은 점보다 우스꽝스러운 점이 이따금 더 두드러진들 나도 많이 봐줄 수 있을 거야. 그분이 재산 있는 여인이라면, 해롭지 않은 우스운 짓이야 운에 맡겨 두지, 말투 좀 함부로 한다고 자네와 다투지 않겠네. 그분이 처지가 자네와 대등하다면 — 하지만 엠마, 사정이 거기서 얼마나 동떨어져 있는지 생각해 보게. 그분은 가난해. 타고난 안락에서 굴러떨어졌고, 만일 늙도록 산다면 십중팔구 더 떨어질 거야. 그분의 처지야말로 자네의 연민을 받아 마땅하지. 정말이지 못된 짓이었네! 자네가 — 그분이 갓난아이 적부터 알아 온 자네가, 그분의 눈길이 영광이던 시절부터 자라나는 걸 지켜봐 온 자네가, 이제 와서 분별없는 들뜸과 그 순간의 우쭐함에 빠져 그분을 비웃고, 그분을 욕보이다니 — 그것도 조카 앞에서 — 게다가 다른 사람들 앞에서, 그중 많은 이가 (적어도 몇몇은 분명히) 자네가 그분을 어떻게 대하느냐에 따라 그대로 휘둘릴 사람들 앞에서 말이야. — 이런 말이 자네에게 달갑지 않다는 거 아네, 엠마 — 나에게도 결코 달가운 일이 아니야. 하지만 나는, 할 수 있는 동안에는, 자네에게 진실을 말해 두어야겠고, 말하고야 말겠네. 충직한 조언으로 내가 자네의 벗임을 보여 주는 것으로 만족하면서, 언젠가는 자네가 지금은 못 하더라도 나를 좀 더 옳게 평가해 주리라 믿으면서 말이야.\"\n\n이야기를 나누는 사이 그들은 마차 쪽으로 다가가고 있었다. 마차가 채비돼 있었고, 그녀가 다시 입을 열기도 전에 그는 그녀를 부축해 태웠다. 그는 그녀가 얼굴을 돌리고 입을 굳게 다물고 있던 그 심정을 잘못 읽었다. 그것은 오로지 자기 자신을 향한 분노와 수치와 깊은 자책이 뒤엉킨 것이었다. 그녀는 말을 할 수가 없었다. 그리고 마차에 오르며 잠시 무너지듯 뒤로 기댔다 — 그러다 작별 인사도 못 하고, 아무 감사의 표시도 못 하고, 보기에 뾰로통한 채로 헤어진 자신을 자책하며, 그게 아님을 보이려 목소리와 손짓을 다급히 내밀며 밖을 내다보았다. 그러나 너무 늦은 뒤였다. 그는 이미 돌아서 있었고, 말들은 움직이고 있었다. 그녀는 계속 뒤돌아보았으나 헛일이었다. 이윽고, 유난히 빠른 듯한 속도로, 마차는 어느새 언덕을 반쯤 내려와 모든 것을 저 멀리 뒤로 남겨 두고 있었다. 그녀는 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 — 거의 숨기지 못할 만큼 — 속이 상했다. 평생 어떤 일에서도 이토록 마음이 흔들리고, 수치스럽고, 비통했던 적은 없었다. 그녀는 너무도 세차게 정곡을 찔린 것이다. 그가 그려 보인 것이 진실임은 부인할 수 없었다. 그녀는 그것을 가슴으로 느꼈다. 어떻게 미스 베이츠에게 그토록 모질고, 그토록 잔인할 수 있었단 말인가! 어떻게 그녀가 귀히 여기는 사람의 눈에 그런 나쁜 평판을 자초할 수 있었단 말인가! 그리고 어떻게 그를, 감사도, 수긍도, 그저 흔한 다정함의 한마디조차 건네지 못한 채 떠나보낼 수 있었단 말인가!\n\n시간이 흘러도 그녀는 가라앉지 않았다. 곱씹을수록 그 마음은 더 깊어지기만 하는 듯했다. 이토록 가라앉아 본 적이 없었다. 다행히 말을 해야 할 일은 없었다. 곁에는 해리엇뿐이었는데, 그녀 역시 기운이 없는지 지쳐서 잠자코 있고 싶어 했다. 엠마는 집으로 오는 거의 내내 두 뺨을 타고 흐르는 눈물을, 그토록 유난스러운데도, 굳이 멈추려 들지 않은 채 그대로 두었다.\n\n# 제3권 8장\n\n박스 힐 소풍이 얼마나 비참한 계획이었던가 하는 생각이 그날 저녁 내내 엠마의 머릿속을 떠나지 않았다. 일행의 나머지 사람들이 그것을 어찌 여길지는 알 수 없었다. 저마다의 집에서, 저마다의 방식으로, 그들은 그날을 즐겁게 돌아보고 있을지도 몰랐다. 그러나 엠마가 보기에 그날 오전은 그녀가 지금껏 보낸 어떤 오전보다도 더 철저히 허비된 오전이었고, 그 당시로서는 어떤 이성적인 만족도 깡그리 결여된 시간이었으며, 떠올리면 떠올릴수록 더욱 진저리쳐지는 오전이었다. 아버지와 주사위 놀이를 하며 보내는 저녁 한나절이 차라리 그보다 행복이었으리라. 거기에는 참된 즐거움이 있었으니, 그곳에서 그녀는 하루 스물네 시간 중 가장 달콤한 시간을 아버지의 안락을 위해 내어 주고 있었고, 아버지의 그 애틋한 사랑과 신뢰 어린 존경이 분에 넘치는 것일지언정, 적어도 평소의 처신에서는 어떤 가혹한 책망에도 떳떳할 수 있다고 느꼈기 때문이었다. 딸로서, 그녀는 자신에게 마음이 없지는 않기를 바랐다. 그 누구도 그녀에게 \"어떻게 아버지께 그토록 매정할 수 있어?—할 수 있을 때, 꼭, 기어이 진실을 말해 주어야겠다\"라고 말할 수는 없기를 바랐다. 미스 베이츠에게는 다시는 그러지 않으리라—그래, 결코 다시는! 앞으로의 배려가 지난날을 지워 줄 수 있다면, 그녀는 용서받기를 바랄 수 있으리라. 자신이 자주 소홀했음을 양심이 일러 주었다. 어쩌면 행동보다는 생각에서 더 소홀했을 것이고, 멸시하고 무례했을 것이다. 그러나 더는 그래서는 안 되었다. 진정한 뉘우침의 뜨거움 속에서, 바로 다음 날 아침 그녀를 찾아가리라, 그리고 그것이 자기 쪽에서 시작하는 규칙적이고 대등하며 다정한 교제의 출발점이 되리라.\n\n이튿날이 오자 그녀는 여전히 굳은 결심으로, 아무것도 자신을 막지 못하도록 일찍 집을 나섰다. 가는 길에 미스터 나이틀리를 보게 될 법도 했고, 어쩌면 그가 방문하는 동안 들이닥칠지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 그렇다 해도 거리낄 것이 없었다. 그토록 정당하고 진실로 자신의 것인 뉘우침의 모습을 드러내는 것이 부끄럽지 않았다. 걸어가는 내내 그녀의 눈길은 돈웰 쪽을 향했으나, 그의 모습은 보이지 않았다.\n\n\"숙녀분들 모두 댁에 계십니다.\" 그녀는 이전까지 그 말이 반가웠던 적이 없었고, 그 복도에 들어서거나 계단을 오르며 기쁨을 주고자 하는 마음을 품어 본 적도 없었으니, 오직 은혜를 베풀거나 거두려는 마음, 아니면 나중에 비웃을 거리를 얻으려는 마음뿐이었다.\n\n그녀가 다가가자 한바탕 부산함이 일었다. 오가는 발걸음과 말소리가 분주했다. 미스 베이츠의 목소리가 들렸고, 무언가를 서둘러 해치워야 하는 모양이었다. 하녀는 겁먹고 어색한 얼굴로, 잠시만 기다려 주십사 청하더니, 너무 일찍 그녀를 들여보냈다. 이모와 조카는 둘 다 옆방으로 달아나는 듯이 보였다. 제인은 무척 아파 보이는 모습으로 그녀의 눈에 또렷이 스쳐 갔고, 문이 두 사람을 가려 닫히기 전에, 미스 베이츠가 이렇게 말하는 소리가 들렸다. \"그래, 얘야, 침대에 누워 있다고 내가 말해 주마, 정말이지 너는 그럴 만큼 충분히 아프니까.\"\n\n가엾은 늙은 베이츠 부인은 여느 때처럼 정중하고 겸손한 모습으로, 지금 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 잘 알아듣지 못하는 듯한 얼굴이었다.\n\n\"제인이 좀 안 좋은 것 같아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 잘 모르겠네요. 다들 괜찮다고 일러는 주는데. 딸아이가 곧 올 거예요, 미스 우드하우스. 의자가 보이시는지요. 헤티가 나가지 말았어야 했는데. 나는 워낙 거동이 변변치 못해서요—의자가 있으신가요, 아가씨? 편한 데 앉으시지요? 그 애가 분명 곧 올 겁니다.\"\n\n엠마는 진심으로 그러기를 바랐다. 미스 베이츠가 자신을 피해 멀리 떨어져 있을까 봐 잠시 두려운 마음이 들었다. 그러나 미스 베이츠는 곧 나타났다—\"참 기쁘고 황송하다\"며—그러나 엠마의 양심은, 이전과 같은 명랑한 수다스러움이 없다고, 표정과 거동에 예전만큼의 편안함이 없다고 일러 주었다. 미스 페어팩스의 안부를 더없이 다정하게 물으면, 어쩌면 옛정으로 돌아가는 길이 트이리라 그녀는 바랐다. 그 시도는 곧바로 효과를 본 듯했다.\n\n\"아! 미스 우드하우스, 어쩜 이리 친절하실까!—이미 들으셨겠지요—그래서 우리에게 축하해 주려고 오신 거겠지요. 정말이지 제게는 그다지 기쁜 일 같지가 않지만요—(눈물 한두 방울을 깜빡여 떨치며)—그렇게 오래 데리고 있다가 그 애와 헤어지자니, 우리에게는 무척 힘든 일이 되겠어요. 게다가 그 애는 지금 끔찍한 두통을 앓고 있답니다, 아침 내내 편지를 쓰느라고요—그 긴 편지들 있잖아요, 캠벨 대령과 딕슨 부인에게 써야 하는. '얘야,' 하고 제가 말했지요, '눈이 멀겠구나'—눈물이 그칠 새 없이 글썽거리고 있었거든요. 무리도 아니지요, 무리도 아니에요. 엄청난 변화니까요. 그리고 그 애가 놀랄 만큼 운이 좋긴 하지만—젊은 여자가 처음 일을 나가면서 이런 자리를 만난 일은 일찍이 없었을 거예요—그렇다고 우리를 은혜도 모르는 사람들로 여기지 말아 주세요, 미스 우드하우스, 이런 놀라운 행운을 두고 말이지요—(또 한 번 눈물을 흩뜨리며)—하지만, 가엾은 것! 그 애가 얼마나 두통에 시달리는지 보셨다면. 몹시 아플 때는, 아시겠지만, 어떤 축복도 그 진가만큼 온전히 느껴지지가 않는 법이니까요. 그 애는 더없이 풀이 죽어 있어요. 그 애를 보면, 그런 자리를 얻어 얼마나 기뻐하고 행복해하는지 아무도 짐작 못 할 거예요. 그 애가 나오지 못하는 것을 너그러이 봐주세요—거동이 안 되거든요—제 방으로 들어가 버렸어요—저는 침대에 좀 누워 있으라고 하고 싶은데요. '얘야,' 하고 제가 말했지요, '침대에 누워 있다고 내가 말해 두마.' 그래도 누워 있지를 않고, 방 안을 서성이고 있답니다. 하지만 이제 편지를 다 썼으니, 곧 괜찮아질 거라고 하더군요. 미스 우드하우스, 당신을 못 뵙게 되어 그 애가 무척 서운해할 텐데, 당신의 너그러움으로 봐주실 거예요. 문 앞에서 기다리게 해 드려서—저는 어찌나 부끄럽던지—그래도 어쩐지 한바탕 부산을 떨었지 뭐예요—마침 노크 소리를 못 들어서요, 당신이 계단에 오를 때까지 누가 오는지 몰랐거든요. '콜 부인일 거예요,' 하고 제가 말했지요, '틀림없어요. 이렇게 일찍 올 사람이 또 있겠어요.' '뭐,' 하고 그 애가 말했지요, '언젠가는 견뎌야 할 일이니, 지금이라고 안 될 것도 없죠.' 그런데 그때 패티가 들어와서 당신이라고 하지 뭐예요. '어머!' 하고 제가 말했지요, '미스 우드하우스로구나. 분명 그 애가 보고 싶어 할 거다.'—'아무도 못 만나겠어요,' 하고 그 애가 말했어요. 그러고는 벌떡 일어나 나가 버리려고 했지요. 그래서 당신을 기다리게 해 드린 거예요—얼마나 미안하고 부끄러웠는지요. '정 가야겠거든, 얘야,' 하고 제가 말했지요, '가야지, 그럼 침대에 누워 있다고 말해 두마.'\"\n\n엠마는 더없이 진심으로 마음이 움직였다. 그녀의 마음은 오래전부터 제인을 향해 차츰 너그러워지고 있었다. 그리고 지금 제인이 겪는 고통의 이 광경은 지난날의 너그럽지 못했던 온갖 의심을 치유해 주었고, 남은 것이라곤 오직 연민뿐이었다. 또한 지난날의 덜 공정하고 덜 다정했던 감정을 떠올리니, 제인이 정작 엠마 자신은 차마 만나 주지 못할 때라도 콜 부인이나 그 밖의 한결같은 벗을 만나기로 마음먹는 것은 지극히 당연한 일이었음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀는 느낀 대로, 간절한 안타까움과 염려를 담아 말했다—미스 베이츠에게서 들은 바, 이제 정말로 결정된 듯한 그 사정이 미스 페어팩스에게 가능한 한 이롭고 위안이 되기를 진심으로 바라면서. \"모두에게 혹독한 시련이겠어요. 저는 캠벨 대령이 돌아오실 때까지 미뤄지는 줄로만 알았는데요.\"\n\n\"어쩜 이리 친절하실까!\" 미스 베이츠가 대답했다. \"하지만 당신은 늘 친절하시지요.\"\n\n그런 \"늘\"이라는 말은 도저히 견딜 수가 없었다. 그래서 그녀의 끔찍한 감사 인사를 뚫고 나가려고, 엠마는 단도직입으로 물었다—\n\n\"미스 페어팩스가—여쭤봐도 될까요?—어디로 가게 되었나요?\"\n\n\"스몰리지 부인이라는 분께요—매력적인 분이지요—더없이 훌륭하시고요—세 어린 따님을 맡으러 가는 거예요—참 사랑스러운 아이들이지요. 그보다 더 안락함이 가득한 자리는 있을 수 없을 거예요. 아마 서클링 부인 댁이나 브래그 부인 댁이라면 모를까요. 그런데 스몰리지 부인은 두 댁 모두와 가깝게 지내시고, 게다가 바로 같은 동네에 사세요—메이플 그로브에서 겨우 사 마일 떨어진 곳이지요. 제인은 메이플 그로브에서 겨우 사 마일 거리에 있게 되는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그러니까 미스 페어팩스가 신세를 진 분은 엘턴 부인이시겠군요—\"\n\n\"네, 우리의 고마운 엘턴 부인이지요. 더없이 지칠 줄 모르는, 참된 벗이에요. 거절을 받아들이려 하지 않았답니다. 제인이 '싫다'고 하게 두질 않았어요. 제인이 처음 이 이야기를 들었을 때 (그저께였어요, 바로 우리가 돈웰에 갔던 그날 아침이지요), 제인이 처음 그 이야기를 들었을 때는, 당신이 말씀하신 바로 그 이유로, 그 제안을 거절하기로 단단히 마음먹고 있었거든요. 당신 말씀 그대로, 캠벨 대령이 돌아오실 때까지는 아무것도 결정짓지 않기로 작정해서, 지금으로서는 어떤 약속에도 응하지 않겠다고요—그래서 엘턴 부인에게 몇 번이고 그렇게 말했답니다—저는 정말이지 그 애가 마음을 바꾸리라고는 꿈에도 생각 못 했어요!—하지만 판단력이 결코 그르치는 법이 없는 그 고마운 엘턴 부인은, 저보다 멀리 내다보셨던 거지요. 그분처럼 그렇게 친절한 방식으로 버티며 제인의 대답을 받아들이지 않을 사람은 아무나 있는 게 아니에요. 그분은 제인이 바라는 대로 어제 그런 거절의 편지를 쓰는 일은 결단코 하지 않겠다고 딱 잘라 말씀하셨거든요. 기다리겠다고요—그러더니, 아니나 다를까, 어제저녁에 제인이 가기로 모든 게 정해진 거예요. 저로서는 그야말로 깜짝 놀랄 일이었죠! 전혀 짐작도 못 했어요!—제인이 엘턴 부인을 따로 불러서는, 스몰리지 부인 자리의 이점을 곰곰이 생각해 보니 받아들이기로 결심했다고 단번에 말씀드렸다지 뭐예요.—저는 모든 게 다 정해질 때까지 그 일을 한마디도 몰랐답니다.\"\n\n\"엘턴 부인과 저녁을 함께 보내셨군요?\"\n\n\"네, 우리 모두요. 엘턴 부인이 꼭 오라고 하셨거든요. 그렇게 정해진 거예요, 그 언덕에서요, 우리가 미스터 나이틀리와 함께 거닐고 있을 때 말이지요. '꼭 모두 우리 집에서 저녁을 보내셔야 해요,' 하고 그분이 말씀하셨어요—'단연코 모두 오셔야만 해요.'\"\n\n\"미스터 나이틀리도 거기 계셨던가요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 미스터 나이틀리는요. 그분은 처음부터 사양하셨어요. 엘턴 부인이 절대 빠지게 두지 않겠다고 하셔서 저는 그분이 오시리라 생각했는데, 안 오셨답니다—하지만 어머니와 제인과 저는 모두 거기 있었고, 정말이지 아주 유쾌한 저녁을 보냈어요. 그렇게 친절한 벗들과 함께라면, 아시잖아요, 미스 우드하우스, 언제나 즐거운 법이지요. 비록 다들 그날 오전의 모임 뒤라 좀 지쳐 보이긴 했지만요. 즐거움이라는 것도, 아시다시피, 사람을 지치게 하는 법이라서요—그리고 그중 누구도 그날을 그리 많이 즐긴 것 같지는 않았다고 해야겠네요. 그렇지만 저는 언제까지나 그날을 아주 유쾌한 모임으로 여길 거예요. 그리고 저를 끼워 주신 친절한 벗들께 더없이 고마운 마음이고요.\"\n\n\"미스 페어팩스는, 당신은 모르셨겠지만, 아마 그날 하루 종일 마음을 정하고 있었던 모양이지요?\"\n\n\"분명 그랬을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그때가 언제 오든, 그 애나 그 애의 모든 벗에게 달갑지 않은 일이겠지요—하지만 그 애의 일자리에 가능한 모든 위안이 따르기를 바라요—제 말은, 그 가족의 인품과 됨됨이 말이에요.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 사랑스러운 미스 우드하우스. 네, 정말이지, 그 자리에는 그 애를 행복하게 해 줄 만한 것이 세상에 있는 대로 다 갖춰져 있답니다. 서클링 댁과 브래그 댁을 빼고는, 엘턴 부인이 아는 모든 사람 중에 그토록 후하고 우아한 양육 살림은 또 없어요. 스몰리지 부인은, 더없이 사랑스러운 분이지요!—거의 메이플 그로브에 맞먹는 생활 양식이고요—아이들로 말하자면, 어린 서클링네 아이들과 어린 브래그네 아이들을 빼면, 어디에도 그렇게 우아하고 사랑스러운 아이들은 없답니다. 제인은 그토록 정중하고 친절한 대접을 받게 될 거예요!—오직 즐거움뿐, 즐거움으로 가득한 삶일 거예요.—게다가 급료라니요!—정말이지 그 급료를 당신께 입에 올릴 엄두가 안 나는군요, 미스 우드하우스. 큰 액수에 익숙하신 당신조차도, 제인 같은 젊은 사람에게 그만한 돈이 주어질 수 있다는 걸 좀처럼 믿지 못하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"아! 부인,\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"다른 아이들이 제가 기억하는 어릴 적 저와 조금이라도 닮았다면, 그런 자리에서 급료라고 일컬어지는 액수가 제가 지금껏 들어 본 것의 다섯 배라 해도, 그건 톡톡히 값을 치르고 버는 돈이라 여기겠어요.\"\n\n\"생각하시는 게 어쩜 그리 고결하실까요!\"\n\n\"그래서 미스 페어팩스는 언제 당신 곁을 떠나게 되나요?\"\n\n\"아주 곧, 정말이지 아주 곧이에요. 그게 제일 안타까운 점이죠. 보름 안이에요. 스몰리지 부인이 무척 서두르시거든요. 가엾은 어머니는 그 일을 어떻게 견뎌야 할지 모르세요. 그래서 저는 어머니가 그 생각을 떨치도록 애쓰면서, 자, 어머니, 우리 더는 그 생각 말기로 해요, 하고 말한답니다.\"\n\n\"그 애의 벗들은 모두 그 애를 잃게 되어 서운하겠지요. 그리고 캠벨 대령 부부는, 돌아오시기도 전에 그 애가 약속을 맺어 버린 것을 알면 서운해하시지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"네, 제인도 분명 그러실 거라고 해요. 하지만 그래도 이건 거절하는 게 도리에 맞다고 스스로 여길 수 없는 그런 자리거든요. 그 애가 엘턴 부인에게 한 말을 처음 제게 들려주었을 때, 그리고 바로 그 순간 엘턴 부인이 와서 그 일을 두고 제게 축하를 건네셨을 때, 저는 어찌나 놀랐던지요! 그게 차 마시기 전이었는데—가만있자—아니, 차 마시기 전일 리가 없어요, 우리가 막 카드놀이를 하려던 참이었으니까요—그래도 차 마시기 전이긴 했어요, 제가 이렇게 생각했던 게 기억나거든요—아! 아니지, 이제 생각났어요, 됐어요. 차 마시기 전에 무슨 일이 있긴 했는데, 그건 아니었어요. 미스터 엘턴이 차 마시기 전에 방에서 불려 나갔거든요, 늙은 존 애브디의 아들이 그분과 이야기를 하고 싶어 했어요. 가엾은 늙은 존, 저는 그분을 무척 존경한답니다. 그분은 제 가엾은 아버지의 서기로 스물일곱 해를 일하셨거든요. 그런데 이제, 가엾은 노인이, 자리보전을 하고 누워서, 관절에 류머티즘 통풍이 와 아주 편찮으세요—저는 오늘 그분을 뵈러 가야겠어요. 제인도, 외출이라도 할 수 있게 되면 분명 그럴 거예요. 그래서 가엾은 존의 아들이 미스터 엘턴에게 교구의 구제에 관해 의논하러 온 거예요. 그 아들은 제 형편이야 아주 넉넉하답니다, 아시다시피 크라운 여관의 우두머리 일꾼이고, 마부에다, 그런 온갖 일을 다 보거든요. 그래도 도움 없이는 아버지를 모실 수가 없는 거지요. 그래서 미스터 엘턴이 돌아오셔서, 그 존 마부가 들려준 이야기를 우리에게 전해 주셨는데, 그때 미스터 프랭크 처칠을 리치먼드로 데려가려고 랜덜스로 마차를 보낸 일이 드러났답니다. 그게 차 마시기 전에 있었던 일이에요. 제인이 엘턴 부인에게 말한 건 차 마신 다음이었고요.\"\n\n미스 베이츠는 엠마에게 이 사정이 자신에게 얼마나 전혀 새로운 것인지 말할 틈조차 좀처럼 주지 않았다. 그러나 그녀가, 엠마가 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 떠난 일의 세세한 사정을 모를 리가 있겠느냐고는 추호도 의심하지 않은 채 그 모든 것을 늘어놓기 시작했으니, 그것은 아무래도 상관없는 일이었다.\n\n이 일에 관해 미스터 엘턴이 그 마부에게서 들은 바는, 마부 자신이 아는 것과 랜덜스 하인들이 아는 것을 그러모은 것이었으니, 박스 힐에서 일행이 돌아온 직후 리치먼드에서 한 전령이 건너왔다는 것이었다—다만 그 전령은 예상된 것 이상은 아니었다. 그리고 미스터 처칠이 조카에게 몇 줄을 보냈는데, 대체로 처칠 부인에 관해 그런대로 괜찮은 소식을 담은 것으로, 다만 이튿날 아침 일찍을 넘기지 말고 돌아오기를 바란다는 내용이었다. 그러나 미스터 프랭크 처칠은 조금도 기다리지 않고 곧장 집으로 가기로 작정했고, 그의 말이 감기에 걸린 듯하자, 톰이 즉시 크라운 여관의 마차를 부르러 보내졌으며, 마부는 밖에 나가 서서 그것이 지나가는 것을 보았는데, 마부 소년이 꽤 빠른 속도로, 아주 안정되게 몰더라는 것이었다.\n\n이 모든 이야기에는 놀랍거나 흥미로울 만한 것이 하나도 없었고, 다만 이미 그녀의 마음을 사로잡고 있던 주제와 맞물리는 한에서만 엠마의 주의를 끌었다. 세상에서 처칠 부인이 지니는 비중과 제인 페어팩스가 지니는 비중 사이의 대조가 그녀를 쳤다. 한 사람은 전부였고, 다른 한 사람은 아무것도 아니었다—그녀는 여자의 운명이라는 것이 이토록 다르다는 데 잠겨 앉아, 자기 눈길이 어디에 머물러 있는지조차 까맣게 모르고 있다가, 미스 베이츠의 이런 말에 정신이 들었다.\n\n\"아, 무슨 생각을 하시는지 알겠어요, 그 피아노포르테 말이지요. 그건 어떻게 되는 거냐고요?—그래요, 옳은 말씀이에요. 가엾은 제인이 방금도 그 이야기를 하더라고요.—'너는 가야 해,' 하고 그 애가 말했지요. '너와 나는 헤어져야 해. 너는 여기 있을 까닭이 없으니까.—그래도 그냥 두자,' 하고 그 애가 말했어요. '캠벨 대령이 돌아오실 때까지 둘 자리를 마련해 두자. 그분께 이 일을 말씀드릴 거야. 그분이 나 대신 정해 주실 테니까. 그분이 내 모든 어려움을 풀어 주실 거야.'—그리고 오늘날까지도, 정말이지 저는, 그 애가 그게 그분의 선물이었는지 그분 따님의 선물이었는지조차 모르고 있다고 믿어요.\"\n\n이제 엠마는 그 피아노포르테를 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 지난날 자신이 멋대로 그려 댄 온갖 공상적이고 부당한 억측을 떠올리니 그것이 너무도 마뜩잖아, 곧 자신의 방문이 충분히 길었다고 여기기로 했다. 그리하여, 진심으로 느끼는 좋은 바람을 두고 차마 입에 올릴 수 있는 말은 모조리 되풀이한 뒤, 작별을 고했다.\n\n# 제3권 9장\n\n엠마는 집으로 걸어 돌아오는 동안, 생각에 잠긴 명상을 방해받지 않았다. 그러나 거실에 들어서자, 자기를 일깨워 줄 사람들이 와 있었다. 미스터 나이틀리와 해리엇이 그녀가 없는 사이에 와서 아버지와 함께 앉아 있었던 것이다. 미스터 나이틀리는 곧장 일어서더니, 평소보다 확연히 무거운 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"자네를 보지 않고는 떠날 수 없어서 말이지. 하지만 시간이 없으니 지금 곧장 가 봐야겠네. 런던으로 가서 존과 이저벨라하고 며칠 지내고 올 참이야. 부칠 물건이나 전할 말이 있나 — 아무도 챙겨 가지 않는 '사랑한다'는 인사 말고 말일세.\"\n\n\"전혀요. 그런데 이거 너무 갑작스러운 계획 아니에요?\"\n\n\"그렇지 — 좀 그런 편이지 — 얼마 전부터 생각은 하고 있었네.\"\n\n엠마는 그가 자기를 용서하지 않았다고 확신했다. 그는 평소의 그답지 않아 보였다. 그러나 시간이 흐르면 둘이 다시 벗이 되어야 한다는 것을 그에게 일러 주리라고 그녀는 생각했다. 그가 떠나려는 듯하면서도 떠나지 않고 서 있는 동안, 아버지가 묻기 시작했다.\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 무사히 다녀왔느냐? 그 갸륵한 옛 벗과 그 따님은 어떻더냐? 분명 네가 찾아간 걸 무척 고마워했겠지. 미스터 나이틀리, 우리 엠마가 베이츠 부인와 미스 베이츠를 찾아뵙고 온 길이라오. 아까도 말씀드렸지만. 그 애는 늘 그분들한테 그렇게 살뜰하다오!\"\n\n이 부당한 칭찬에 엠마의 얼굴이 붉어졌다. 그리고 많은 것을 담은 미소와 고갯짓으로, 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리를 바라보았다. 순간 그녀에게 유리한 인상이 곧바로 전해진 듯했다. 그의 눈이 그녀의 눈에서 진실을 받아들이고, 그녀의 마음속에 오간 모든 선한 것이 단번에 포착되어 인정받은 듯했다. 그는 따뜻한 존경의 빛을 띠고 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀는 마음 깊이 흐뭇했고 — 다음 순간, 그가 보인 예사롭지 않은 다정함의 작은 몸짓에 한층 더 흐뭇해졌다. 그가 그녀의 손을 잡았다. 자기가 먼저 손을 내민 것은 아닌지 그녀로서는 알 수 없었다 — 어쩌면 자기가 먼저 손을 내밀었는지도 몰랐다 — 그러나 그는 그녀의 손을 잡고, 꼭 쥐었으며, 분명 그 손을 입술로 가져가려는 찰나였다 — 그런데 무슨 변덕이 일었는지, 그는 돌연 손을 놓아 버렸다. 어째서 그런 거리낌을 느꼈는지, 거의 다 한 일을 어째서 마음을 바꿨는지, 그녀는 헤아릴 수 없었다. 멈추지 않았더라면 더 나은 판단이었으리라고 그녀는 생각했다. 그러나 그 의도만은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그리고 그의 태도가 워낙 그런 정중한 호의와는 거리가 멀어서였는지, 아니면 다른 무슨 까닭에서였는지, 그녀는 그 모습이 그에게 더없이 잘 어울린다고 생각했다. 그에게는 그토록 꾸밈없으면서도 그토록 기품 있는 무엇이 있었다. 그녀는 그 시도를 큰 만족과 함께 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 그것은 더없이 온전한 우정을 말해 주었다. 그는 그 직후 그들을 떠났다 — 순식간에 가 버렸다. 그는 늘 우유부단하지도 굼뜨지도 않은 정신의 민첩함으로 움직였지만, 이번에는 평소보다 더 돌연히 사라지는 듯했다.\n\n엠마는 미스 베이츠를 찾아간 것을 후회할 수는 없었으나, 십 분만 더 일찍 그 집을 나섰더라면 싶었다. 제인 페어팩스의 처지에 관해 미스터 나이틀리와 이야기 나눌 수 있었다면 큰 즐거움이었을 텐데. 그가 브런즈윅 스퀘어로 간다는 것도 후회할 일은 아니었으니, 그의 방문이 얼마나 큰 기쁨이 될지 알았기 때문이다 — 그래도 더 좋은 때에 가게 되었더라면, 그리고 좀 더 일찍 알려 주었더라면 더 반가웠을 텐데. 그러나 두 사람은 더없이 온전한 벗으로서 헤어졌다. 그의 표정이 담은 뜻과 끝맺지 못한 그 정중한 호의를 두고서는 잘못 알 리가 없었다 — 그 모두가 그녀가 그의 호의를 온전히 되찾았음을 일러 주려는 것이었다. 알고 보니 그는 반 시간이나 그들과 앉아 있었던 것이다. 좀 더 일찍 돌아오지 못한 것이 아쉬웠다!\n\n미스터 나이틀리가 런던으로 간다는 것, 그것도 그렇게 갑작스레, 게다가 말을 타고 간다는 것 — 이 모두가 무척 못마땅하리라는 것을 알았기에, 아버지의 생각을 그 불쾌함에서 돌리려는 마음에, 엠마는 제인 페어팩스 소식을 전했다. 그리고 그 효과에 건 기대는 어긋나지 않았다. 그것은 매우 쓸모 있는 제동이 되었다 — 아버지의 관심을 끌면서도 마음을 흔들지는 않았으니. 그는 제인 페어팩스가 가정교사로 나간다는 데에는 오래전부터 마음을 정해 둔 터라 명랑하게 이야기할 수 있었으나, 미스터 나이틀리가 런던으로 간다는 것은 뜻밖의 타격이었다.\n\n\"그 사람이 그렇게 편안하게 자리를 잡게 된다니 참 다행이구나, 얘야. 정말 다행이야. 엘턴 부인은 마음씨 좋고 상냥한 사람이고, 분명 그 댁 지인들도 마땅히 그래야 할 만한 사람들일 게다. 부디 습하지 않은 곳이고, 그 애의 건강을 잘 보살펴 주면 좋으련만. 그게 무엇보다 먼저여야지. 가엾은 미스 테일러가 나한테 늘 그래 주었던 것처럼 말이다. 알다시피 얘야, 그 애는 그 새 안주인한테 미스 테일러가 우리한테 그래 주었던 것 같은 사람이 되는 거란다. 그리고 한 가지 점에서는 더 나았으면 좋겠구나 — 그렇게 오래 제집처럼 지내다가 결국 떠나는 일은 없도록 말이다.\"\n\n이튿날에는 리치먼드에서 다른 모든 일을 뒷전으로 밀어내는 소식이 왔다. 처칠 부인의 부음을 알리는 급보가 랜덜스에 도착한 것이다! 조카가 그녀 때문에 서둘러 돌아가야 할 특별한 이유는 없었건만, 그가 돌아간 뒤 그녀는 서른여섯 시간을 채 넘기지 못하고 세상을 떠났다. 평소의 상태로는 짐작조차 못 했던 종류의 갑작스러운 발작이, 짧은 사투 끝에 그녀의 목숨을 앗아 간 것이다. 그 위세 등등하던 처칠 부인이 이제 이 세상 사람이 아니었다.\n\n그런 일이 으레 그러하듯 그렇게 받아들여졌다. 누구나 얼마간 엄숙하고 슬픈 표정을 지었다. 고인을 향한 애틋함, 남은 벗들에 대한 염려가 있었고 — 그리고 적당한 시간이 지나자, 그녀가 어디에 묻힐지에 대한 호기심도 일었다. 골드스미스는 우리에게 일러 준다. 사랑스러운 여인이 어리석음으로 빠져들 때, 그녀가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 죽는 것뿐이라고. 그리고 못마땅한 사람이 되는 쪽으로 빠져들 때에도, 죽음은 똑같이 권할 만한 일이니, 나쁜 평판을 씻어 내는 묘책이기 때문이다. 적어도 스물다섯 해 동안 미움받아 온 처칠 부인은, 이제 측은해하는 너그러움 속에서 입에 오르내렸다. 한 가지 점에서는 그녀가 온전히 옳았음이 밝혀졌다. 그녀가 정말로 중병을 앓고 있다고는 여태 아무도 인정해 준 적이 없었던 것이다. 이 사건이, 꾸며 낸 병이라는 온갖 변덕과 이기심의 혐의에서 그녀를 벗겨 주었다.\n* 골드스미스: 올리버 골드스미스의 소설 「웨이크필드의 목사」 속 노래 구절을 비튼 것.\n\n\"가엾은 처칠 부인! 분명 무척 고생했을 거야. 누가 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 더 — 끊임없는 고통이 사람의 성미를 시험했겠지. 슬픈 일이야 — 크나큰 충격이고 — 온갖 결점에도 불구하고, 미스터 처칠가 그 사람 없이 어찌 지낸단 말인가? 미스터 처칠의 상실은 정말이지 끔찍할 거야. 미스터 처칠는 결코 이겨 내지 못할 거야.\" 미스터 웨스턴조차 고개를 가로젓고 엄숙한 얼굴로 \"아! 가엾은 여인, 누가 이럴 줄 알았겠나!\" 하고는, 상복만은 되도록 훌륭하게 갖추기로 마음먹었다. 그의 아내는 자기가 넓게 감치고 있던 옷단을 앞에 두고 앉아, 참되고 한결같은 연민과 분별로 한숨지으며 사람의 도리를 곱씹었다. 이 일이 프랭크에게 어떤 영향을 줄까 하는 것이 두 사람의 가장 이른 생각 중 하나였다. 엠마에게도 그것은 매우 이른 추측이었다. 처칠 부인의 성격, 남편의 슬픔 — 그녀의 마음은 외경과 연민으로 그 둘을 훑고 지나가더니 — 이내 한결 가벼워진 마음으로, 이 사건이 프랭크에게 어떤 영향을 줄지, 얼마나 득이 될지, 얼마나 자유롭게 해 줄지에 머물렀다. 그녀는 일어날 법한 온갖 좋은 일을 단번에 떠올렸다. 이제 해리엇 스미스에게 품는 연정은 거칠 것이 하나도 없게 되었다. 아내가 없는 미스터 처칠을 두려워하는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 느긋하고 이끌기 쉬운 사람이라, 조카가 무슨 일이든 설득해 낼 수 있었다. 이제 바랄 것이라곤 그 조카가 정말로 그 연정을 품는 일뿐이었으니, 그 일에 온갖 호의를 품은 엠마로서도 그것이 이미 싹텄다고는 도무지 확신할 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n해리엇은 이 일에 매우 훌륭하게, 대단한 자제력을 보이며 처신했다. 한층 밝아진 희망을 속으로 어떻게 느꼈든, 그녀는 아무것도 드러내지 않았다. 엠마는 그녀에게서 한층 단단해진 성품의 증거를 보고 흐뭇해, 그 단단함을 위태롭게 할 만한 어떤 암시도 삼갔다. 그리하여 두 사람은 처칠 부인의 죽음을 서로 자제하며 이야기했다.\n\n랜덜스에는 프랭크에게서 짧은 편지들이 도착해, 그들의 형편과 계획 가운데 당장 중요한 것들을 모두 전했다. 미스터 처칠는 예상보다 잘 견디고 있었다. 장례 행렬이 요크셔로 떠난 뒤 그들이 처음 옮겨 갈 곳은 윈저에 사는 아주 오랜 벗의 집으로, 미스터 처칠가 지난 십 년 동안 찾아가겠노라 약속해 온 사람이었다. 지금으로서는 해리엇을 위해 할 수 있는 일이 아무것도 없었다. 엠마 쪽에서 할 수 있는 것이라곤 앞날을 위한 좋은 기원뿐이었다.\n\n더 다급한 일은 제인 페어팩스에게 마음을 써 주는 것이었다. 해리엇의 앞길이 열리는 동안 그녀의 앞길은 닫히고 있었고, 그녀와 맺어진 약속이 코앞에 닥쳐, 친절을 베풀고 싶어 하는 하이베리의 누구든 이제 더는 미룰 수 없게 되었던 것이다 — 그리고 엠마에게 그것은 가장 우선되는 바람으로까지 자라 있었다. 그녀에게는 지난날 자신의 냉담함만큼 큰 후회가 없을 정도였다. 그토록 여러 달 동안 소홀히 해 온 바로 그 사람이, 이제는 온갖 각별한 존중과 동정을 아낌없이 쏟아붓고 싶은 바로 그 사람이 된 것이다. 그녀는 그에게 도움이 되고 싶었다. 그녀와의 교제를 소중히 여긴다는 것을 보여 주고, 존경과 배려를 증명하고 싶었다. 그녀는 제인을 하트필드에서 하루 지내도록 설득하기로 마음먹었다. 그것을 권하는 쪽지가 쓰였다. 그러나 초대는 거절당했고, 그것도 말로 전해 온 전갈로였다. \"미스 페어팩스는 글을 쓸 만큼 몸이 좋지 못하다는 것\"이었다. 그리고 같은 날 아침 미스터 페리가 하트필드에 들렀을 때 드러난 바로는, 그녀는 본인의 뜻과는 달리 그가 왕진을 갈 정도로 몹시 편찮았으며, 심한 두통과 신경성 열에 시달리고 있어, 미스터 페리는 그녀가 예정된 시기에 스몰리지 부인 댁으로 갈 수 있을지조차 의심스러워했다. 그녀의 건강은 지금으로서는 완전히 무너진 듯했다 — 입맛도 아주 가셨다 — 그리고 절대적으로 위험한 증상은 없었고, 그 집안의 한결같은 걱정거리인 폐 질환을 건드릴 만한 것도 없었지만, 미스터 페리는 그녀가 마음에 걸렸다. 그는 그녀가 제 감당을 넘는 일을 떠맡았으며, 본인도 그것을 그렇게 느끼면서도 인정하려 들지 않는다고 생각했다. 그녀의 기운은 짓눌린 듯했다. 그가 보지 않을 수 없었던 것은, 지금 그녀가 사는 집이 신경성 질환에는 좋지 않다는 점이었다 — 늘 방 하나에 갇혀 지내야 했으니 — 그는 그렇지 않기를 바랐다 — 그리고 그녀의 착한 이모로 말하자면, 자신의 아주 오랜 벗이긴 하나, 그런 부류의 환자에게 가장 좋은 말동무는 못 된다고 인정할 수밖에 없었다. 그녀의 보살핌과 정성이야 의심할 바 없었다 — 사실 너무 지나칠 정도였다. 그는 미스 페어팩스가 그 보살핌에서 득보다 해를 더 입지 않을까 몹시 염려했다. 엠마는 더없이 따뜻한 관심으로 귀를 기울였다. 그녀가 점점 더 가엾어, 무언가 도움이 될 길을 찾으려 사방을 둘러보았다. 단 한두 시간만이라도 그녀를 이모에게서 떼어 내어, 바람과 풍경을 바꿔 주고, 조용하고 이치에 닿는 대화를 나누어 준다면 — 한두 시간만이라도 — 그녀에게 도움이 될지 몰랐다. 그래서 이튿날 아침, 엠마는 다시 편지를 써서, 그녀가 부릴 수 있는 가장 정성 어린 말로, 제인이 시간만 정해 준다면 어느 때든 마차로 데리러 가겠노라고 전했다 — 그런 운동이 환자에게 좋다는 미스터 페리의 단호한 소견이 있다는 말도 곁들여서. 답장은 이 짧은 쪽지뿐이었다.\n\n\"미스 페어팩스가 인사와 감사를 전합니다. 그러나 어떤 운동도 도무지 감당할 수 없는 형편입니다.\"\n\n엠마는 자기 편지가 그보다 나은 답을 받을 만했다고 느꼈다. 그러나 떨리며 들쭉날쭉한 글씨가 몸이 좋지 않음을 그토록 또렷이 드러내는 그 글을 두고 다툴 수는 없었다. 그저 어떻게 하면 남에게 보이거나 도움받기를 꺼리는 이 마음을 가장 잘 누그러뜨릴 수 있을까만 생각했다. 그리하여 그 답장에도 불구하고, 엠마는 마차를 대령시켜 베이츠 부인 댁으로 가서, 제인이 함께 나서도록 설득되기를 바랐다 — 그러나 소용없었다. 미스 베이츠가 마차 문간으로 나와, 온통 감사를 늘어놓으며, 바람을 쐬는 것이 더없이 큰 도움이 되리라는 생각에 더없이 간곡히 맞장구를 쳤다 — 그리고 전갈로 할 수 있는 일은 다 해 보았다 — 그러나 모두 헛일이었다. 미스 베이츠는 성과 없이 돌아올 수밖에 없었다. 제인은 도무지 설득되지 않았다. 외출하자는 말만 꺼내도 도리어 더 안 좋아지는 듯했다. 엠마는 자기가 직접 만나 제 솜씨를 시험해 볼 수 있었으면 했다. 그러나 미처 그 바람을 내비치기도 전에, 미스 베이츠는 무슨 일이 있어도 미스 우드하우스를 들이지 않겠다고 조카에게 약속한 처지임을 드러냈다. \"실은, 사정인즉슨, 가엾은 제인이 누구든 만나는 것을 견디지 못한다는 거예요 — 그 누구든 말이죠 — 엘턴 부인이야 차마 거절할 수 없었고 — 콜 부인이 어찌나 고집을 부리셨는지 — 페리 부인도 어찌나 말씀이 많으셨는지 — 그래도 그분들만 빼면, 제인은 정말이지 아무도 만나려 하지 않아요.\"\n\n엠마는 어디든 막무가내로 밀고 들어가는 엘턴 부인이나 페리 부인이나 콜 부인 같은 부류에 끼고 싶지 않았다. 그렇다고 자신에게 우선권 같은 것이 있다고 느낄 수도 없었다 — 그래서 그녀는 물러섰고, 다만 그토록 거들고 싶어 하던 조카의 입맛과 식사에 관해서만 미스 베이츠에게 더 물어보았다. 그 화제에 대해서는 가엾은 미스 베이츠가 무척 속상해하며, 또 무척 말이 많았다. 제인은 거의 아무것도 먹으려 들지 않는다는 것이었다. 미스터 페리는 자양분 있는 음식을 권했지만, 그들이 마련할 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든(또 누구도 이렇게 좋은 이웃을 둔 적이 없었건만) 그녀는 입에 대기 싫어한다는 것이었다.\n\n엠마는 집에 닿자 곧장 가정부를 불러 곳간을 살펴보게 했다. 그리고 아주 질 좋은 칡녹말을 더없이 다정한 쪽지와 함께 서둘러 미스 베이츠에게 보냈다. 반 시간 만에 그 칡녹말은 미스 베이츠의 천 마디 감사와 함께 되돌아왔다. \"그러나 사랑스러운 제인이 한사코 돌려보내야겠다고 하니 어쩔 수가 없었어요. 그 애가 도무지 먹을 수 없는 것이라서요 — 게다가, 자기는 무엇이 됐든 조금도 부족한 게 없다고 꼭 전해 달라며 우기는 거예요.\"\n\n훗날 엠마는, 어떤 운동도 감당할 수 없다는 핑계로 그렇게 단호히 마차 동행을 거절한 바로 그날 오후, 제인 페어팩스가 하이베리에서 좀 떨어진 풀밭을 거니는 모습이 목격되었다는 말을 들었다. 그러자 그녀는 — 모든 일을 한데 맞추어 보고는 — 제인이 자기에게서는 어떤 친절도 받지 않기로 작정했다는 것을 의심할 수 없었다. 그녀는 안타까웠다, 무척 안타까웠다. 이런 신경의 짜증과, 행동의 모순과, 들쭉날쭉한 기력 때문에 도리어 더 가엾어 보이는 그 처지에, 그녀의 마음은 아렸다. 그리고 자기가 마땅한 마음씨를 그토록 인정받지 못하고, 벗으로서 그토록 하찮게 여겨진다는 것이 분했다. 그러나 그녀에게는 자기 의도가 선했음을 안다는 위안과, 미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스를 도우려 한 자신의 온갖 시도를 다 알았더라면, 아니 그녀의 마음속까지 들여다볼 수 있었더라면, 이번만큼은 나무랄 것을 하나도 찾지 못했으리라고 스스로에게 말할 수 있는 위안이 있었다.\n\n# 제3권 10장\n\n처칠 부인이 세상을 떠난 지 열흘쯤 지난 어느 날 아침, 엠마는 미스터 웨스턴이 찾아왔다는 전갈을 받고 아래층으로 불려 내려갔다. 그는 \"오 분도 머물 수 없으나 긴히 그녀와 할 말이 있다\"고 했다. 그는 거실 문간에서 그녀를 맞았고, 평소의 음성으로 안부조차 변변히 묻는 둥 마는 둥 하더니 이내 목소리를 낮추어, 그녀의 아버지에게 들리지 않게 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 아무 때라도 랜덜스에 와 줄 수 있겠소? 부디 그래 주오, 될 수 있다면. 웨스턴 부인이 당신을 보고 싶어 하오. 꼭 만나야 한다는구려.\"\n\n\"편찮으신가요?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 전혀. 그저 조금 마음이 어지러울 뿐이오. 마차를 불러 당신에게 가려 했지만, 당신을 단둘이 만나야 해서 말이오. 그러니 알다시피—(아버지 쪽을 향해 고갯짓하며)—흠! 와 줄 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"물론이죠. 원하신다면 지금 당장이라도요. 그렇게 부탁하시는데 어떻게 거절하겠어요. 그런데 대체 무슨 일이죠? 정말 편찮으신 게 아니에요?\"\n\n\"날 믿으시오. 다만 더는 묻지 마오. 때가 되면 다 알게 될 거요. 도무지 알 수 없는 일이지! 하지만 쉿, 쉿!\"\n\n이 모든 게 무슨 뜻인지 짐작하는 일은 엠마로서도 불가능했다. 그의 표정으로 보아 정말로 중대한 무언가가 닥친 듯했지만, 벗이 무탈하다 하니 그녀는 애써 불안해하지 않기로 하고, 아버지에게 지금 산책을 나가겠노라 일러둔 뒤, 곧 미스터 웨스턴과 함께 집을 나서 빠른 걸음으로 랜덜스를 향해 길을 잡았다.\n\n\"자,\" 진입로 대문을 완전히 벗어나자 엠마가 말했다. \"이제, 미스터 웨스턴, 무슨 일이 있었는지 제게 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니.\" 그가 무겁게 답했다. \"내게 묻지 마오. 모든 걸 아내에게 맡기겠다고 약속했소. 나보다 그 사람이 당신에게 더 잘 전할 거요. 조급해하지 마오, 엠마. 어차피 너무 빨리 다 드러날 테니.\"\n\n\"제게 전한다고요?\" 엠마는 두려움에 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 외쳤다. \"맙소사! 미스터 웨스턴, 지금 당장 말씀해 주세요. 브런즈윅 스퀘어에 무슨 일이 생긴 거죠. 분명히 그래요. 말씀해 주세요. 당장 그게 뭔지 말씀해 주시기를 간청해요.\"\n\n\"아니, 정말이지 당신이 잘못 짚었소.\"\n\n\"미스터 웨스턴, 저를 가지고 장난하지 마세요. 제 가장 소중한 벗들이 지금 얼마나 많이 브런즈윅 스퀘어에 있는지 생각해 보세요. 그중 누구예요? 신성한 모든 것을 걸고 간청하니, 숨기려 들지 마세요.\"\n\n\"맹세컨대, 엠마.\"\n\n\"맹세라고요! 어째서 명예를 걸지 않으세요! 어째서 명예를 걸고, 그 일이 그들 중 누구와도 상관없다고 말씀하지 않으세요? 맙소사! 그 집안 사람과 관련되지 않았다면, 제게 전해야 할 일이 대체 뭐가 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고,\" 그가 아주 진지하게 말했다. \"그 일은 그렇지 않소. 나이틀리라는 이름을 가진 그 누구와도 손톱만큼도 관련이 없소.\"\n\n엠마는 용기를 되찾아 다시 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n\"내가 잘못 말했소.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그 일을 당신에게 '전한다'고 한 게 잘못이었소. 그런 표현을 쓰지 말았어야 했는데. 사실 그건 당신과는 상관없는 일이오. 오로지 나 자신에게 관계된 일이지. 그러니까, 우리는 그렇게 바라고 있소. 흠! 한마디로, 엠마, 그렇게 마음 졸일 일은 아니오. 유쾌한 일이 아니라는 말은 아니지만, 사정이 훨씬 더 나쁠 수도 있었으니까. 빨리 걸으면 곧 랜덜스에 닿을 거요.\"\n\n엠마는 기다리는 수밖에 없음을 깨달았고, 이제는 그러는 데 별 노력이 들지도 않았다. 그래서 더는 묻지 않고 그저 제 상상에 몸을 맡겼는데, 그 상상은 곧 그녀에게, 어쩌면 그게 무슨 금전 문제—이 집안 사정에서 불쾌한 성질의 무언가가 막 드러난 것이고, 리치먼드에서 일어난 근래의 일이 그것을 들춰낸 것이리라는 짐작을 안겨 주었다. 그녀의 상상은 무척이나 활발했다. 어쩌면 사생아가 대여섯이나 있어, 가엾은 프랭크가 상속에서 밀려난 것일지도! 이는 무척 달갑잖은 일일망정 그녀에게 고통이 되지는 않을 일이었다. 그것은 활기를 띤 호기심 그 이상은 거의 불러일으키지 못했다.\n\n\"말 탄 저 신사는 누구죠?\" 길을 가다가 그녀가 말했는데, 다른 뜻이 있어서라기보다는 미스터 웨스턴이 비밀을 지키는 일을 거들어 주려는 마음에서였다.\n\n\"모르겠소. 오트웨이네 사람 중 하나겠지. 프랭크는 아니오. 프랭크가 아니라는 건 장담하오. 당신은 그 애를 보지 못할 거요. 지금쯤이면 윈저로 가는 길 절반은 갔을 테니.\"\n\n\"그럼 아드님이 다녀갔나요?\"\n\n\"오! 그렇소. 몰랐소? 뭐, 됐소, 신경 쓰지 마오.\"\n\n그는 잠시 입을 다물었다가, 한결 조심스럽고 점잖은 어조로 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그렇소, 프랭크가 오늘 아침 우리가 어찌 지내는지 안부나 물으려고 잠깐 다녀갔소.\"\n\n두 사람은 서둘러 걸음을 재촉해 이내 랜덜스에 닿았다. \"자, 여보,\" 방에 들어서며 그가 말했다. \"이 사람을 데려왔으니, 이제 당신도 곧 나아지면 좋겠구려. 두 사람만 있게 해 주리다. 늦출 까닭이 없지. 필요하면 부르오. 멀리 가 있지 않을 테니.\" 그리고 그가 방을 나서기 전, 한층 낮은 목소리로 덧붙이는 말을 엠마는 또렷이 들었다. \"내 약속을 어김없이 지켰소. 이 사람은 눈곱만큼도 눈치채지 못했다오.\"\n\n웨스턴 부인의 얼굴이 어찌나 좋지 않고 그 기색에 동요가 가득하던지, 엠마의 불안은 더욱 커졌다. 두 사람만 남게 된 순간 그녀가 다급하게 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요, 부인? 무척 불쾌한 성질의 일이 일어났다는 건 알겠어요. 그게 뭔지 어서 말씀해 주세요. 오는 내내 영문도 모른 채 마음 졸이며 걸어왔어요. 우리 둘 다 이런 마음 졸임을 질색하잖아요. 제 마음 졸임을 더는 끌지 말아 주세요. 무슨 고민이든 털어놓으시면 마음이 한결 가벼워질 거예요.\"\n\n\"정말로 짐작 가는 게 없니?\" 웨스턴 부인이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"엠마, 네가, 네가 무슨 이야기를 듣게 될지 도무지 짐작 못 하겠니?\"\n\n\"미스터 프랭크 처칠과 관련된 일이라는 것까지는 짐작하겠어요.\"\n\n\"맞아. 그 사람과 관련된 일이야. 곧바로 말해 주마.\" (다시 바느질감을 들고는, 결코 고개를 들지 않으리라 작정한 듯한 모습이었다.) \"그 사람이 바로 오늘 아침 여기 왔었어. 더없이 뜻밖의 용건으로 말이지. 우리가 얼마나 놀랐는지 말로 다 할 수가 없구나. 어떤 일을 두고 제 아버지께 말씀드리러 온 거였어. 연정을 알리려고—\"\n\n그녀는 숨을 고르려 말을 멈췄다. 엠마는 먼저 자신을 떠올렸고, 그다음 해리엇을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"연정 그 이상이지, 사실은.\" 웨스턴 부인이 다시 입을 열었다. \"약혼이란다. 엄연한 약혼. 엠마, 뭐라고 할 테냐? 누군들 뭐라고 하겠니? 프랭크 처칠과 미스 페어팩스가 약혼했다는 게, 아니, 진작부터 오래도록 약혼한 사이였다는 게 알려지면 말이야!\"\n\n엠마는 놀라 펄쩍 뛰기까지 했고, 경악한 채 외쳤다.\n\n\"제인 페어팩스라고요! 맙소사! 진심이세요? 설마 그럴 리가요?\"\n\n\"놀라는 것도 무리가 아니야.\" 여전히 눈길을 돌린 채, 엠마가 추스를 틈을 주려는 듯 다급하게 말을 이으며 웨스턴 부인이 답했다. \"정말 놀랄 만하지. 하지만 사실이 그렇단다. 두 사람은 시월부터 줄곧 엄숙한 약혼 사이였어. 웨이머스에서 맺어져, 모두에게 비밀로 부쳐졌지. 그들 자신 말고는 그 누구도 몰랐어. 캠벨 댁도, 그 애 집안도, 그 사람 집안도. 너무도 놀라운 일이라, 사실이라고 완전히 납득하고서도 나조차 도무지 믿기지가 않는구나. 도저히 믿을 수가 없어. 나는 그 애를 잘 안다고 여겼는데.\"\n\n엠마는 무슨 말이 오가는지 거의 듣지 못했다. 그녀의 마음은 두 생각 사이에서 갈라져 있었다. 미스 페어팩스를 두고 그와 나눴던 지난날의 대화들, 그리고 가엾은 해리엇. 한동안 그녀는 그저 탄식만 하며 확인을, 거듭된 확인을 요구할 따름이었다.\n\n\"그러니까,\" 마침내 그녀가 정신을 추스르려 애쓰며 말했다. \"이건 적어도 반나절은 곱씹어 봐야 도무지 이해라도 할 수 있을 일이네요. 뭐라고요? 겨우내 그 사람과 약혼한 사이였다고요? 둘 중 누구도 하이베리에 오기 전부터요?\"\n\n\"시월부터 약혼이었어. 비밀 약혼. 이 일이 나를 무척이나 아프게 했단다, 엠마. 그 애 아버지도 똑같이 아프게 했고. 그 애 처신의 어느 대목은 우리가 도저히 용서할 수가 없어.\"\n\n엠마는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 답했다. \"부인 말씀을 못 알아듣는 척하지는 않을게요. 그리고 제가 드릴 수 있는 모든 위안을 드리자면, 부인께서 염려하시는 그런 결과가 그 사람의 제게 보인 관심에서 비롯되지 않았다는 걸 믿으셔도 좋아요.\"\n\n웨스턴 부인은 차마 믿기 두려운 듯 고개를 들었다. 그러나 엠마의 표정은 그 말처럼 흔들림이 없었다.\n\n\"지금 제가 완전히 무관심하다는 이 호언장담을 한결 수월하게 믿으시도록,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"한 가지 더 말씀드릴게요. 우리가 알고 지낸 초기에, 제가 정말로 그 사람을 좋아했던 시기가 있었어요. 그에게 마음을 둘 마음이 꽤나 있었던 때가요. 아니, 마음을 두었었죠. 그게 어떻게 사그라들었는지가 오히려 놀라운 일일지도 몰라요. 하지만 다행스럽게도, 그것은 사그라들었어요. 정말이지 얼마 전부터, 적어도 지난 석 달간은 그 사람에 대해 아무런 마음도 없었어요. 믿으셔도 돼요, 부인. 이건 있는 그대로의 진실이에요.\"\n\n웨스턴 부인은 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리며 그녀에게 입을 맞추었고, 말문이 트이자, 이 단언이 세상 그 무엇보다도 자신에게 큰 위안이 되었다고 일러 주었다.\n\n\"미스터 웨스턴도 나만큼이나 마음이 놓일 거야.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 일로 우리는 얼마나 마음을 졸였는지 몰라. 너희 둘이 서로 마음을 두기를 우리는 더없이 간절히 바랐단다. 그리고 정말 그런 줄로만 알고 있었지. 우리가 너를 두고 얼마나 마음을 졸였는지 짐작이 가니.\"\n\n\"저는 화를 면했어요. 그리고 제가 화를 면했다는 게 부인과 제게 고마운 놀라움일지도 모르죠. 하지만 그렇다고 그 사람의 죄가 면해지는 건 아니에요, 부인. 그리고 그 사람이 크게 잘못했다는 게 제 생각이라고 말해야겠어요. 마음과 신의를 이미 약속해 둔 처지에, 그렇게나 매인 데 없는 듯한 태도로 우리 가운데 끼어들 권리가 그에게 어디 있죠? 환심을 사려 애쓸 권리가, 분명히 그랬으니까요. 한 젊은 여자를 골라 끈질긴 관심으로 떠받들 권리가, 분명히 그랬으니까요. 정작 그가 다른 사람에게 매인 몸이었으면서 말이에요. 자기가 어떤 해악을 끼칠지 어떻게 알았겠어요? 저를 그에게 반하게 만들지 않으리라고 어떻게 장담했겠어요? 아주 잘못했어요, 정말이지 아주 잘못했어요.\"\n\n\"그 애가 한 말로 미루어, 엠마, 나는 오히려 이렇게 짐작이—\"\n\n\"그리고 그 여자는 어떻게 그런 행동을 견뎠을까요! 그것도 보통 침착함이 아니라 지독한 침착함이라니! 제 면전에서 다른 여자에게 거듭 관심이 베풀어지는 걸 지켜보면서도 노여워하지 않다니요. 그건 제가 도무지 이해할 수도 존중할 수도 없는 수준의 평온함이에요.\"\n\n\"두 사람 사이에 오해가 있었단다, 엠마. 그 애가 분명히 그렇게 말했어. 자세히 설명할 시간은 없었지. 여기 머문 게 겨우 십오 분뿐이었고, 그나마 머물 수 있는 시간조차 온전히 쓰지 못할 만큼 마음이 어지러운 상태였으니까. 그래도 오해가 있었다는 건 단호히 말했어. 실은 이번 사달이 그 오해 때문에 빚어진 듯하더구나. 그리고 그 오해라는 것도 어쩌면 그 애 처신의 부적절함에서 비롯되었을 가능성이 매우 크지.\"\n\n\"부적절함이라고요! 오! 부인, 그건 너무 점잖은 질책이에요. 부적절함을 한참, 한참 넘어선 일이라고요! 그 일은 그를 떨어뜨렸어요. 제 눈에 얼마나 떨어졌는지 말로 다 못 할 정도예요. 사내라면 마땅히 그래야 할 모습과 어쩌면 그리도 다른지요! 사내라면 인생의 매사에서 보여야 할, 그 곧은 정직함도, 진실과 원칙에 대한 그 엄정한 고수도, 술수와 옹졸함에 대한 그 경멸도 도무지 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"아니야, 엠마, 이제는 내가 그 애 편을 들어야겠구나. 이번 일에서는 그 애가 잘못했지만, 나는 그 애를 충분히 오래 알아 온 터라 그에게 좋은 자질이 많이, 아주 많이 있다고 장담할 수 있거든. 게다가—\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 엠마는 그녀의 말은 귀에 담지도 않고 외쳤다. \"스몰리지 부인 일도 있잖아요! 제인이 정말로 가정교사로 들어가기 일보 직전이었다고요! 그렇게 끔찍한 무신경함을 그가 대체 무슨 작정으로요? 그 여자가 그런 자리에 매이게 내버려 두다니. 아니, 그런 방도를 생각하게 내버려 두다니요!\"\n\n\"그 애는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 몰랐어, 엠마. 이 점에서는 내가 그 애의 누명을 온전히 벗겨 줄 수 있단다. 그건 그 애에게 알리지 않은, 그 여자 혼자만의 결심이었어. 적어도 그 애가 확신할 만한 방식으로는 알려지지 않았지. 어제까지도 그 여자의 계획에 대해서는 깜깜했다고 그 애가 말하더구나. 그런데 어떻게 된 건지 모르지만, 무슨 편지나 전갈로 갑자기 그 일을 알게 된 거야. 그리고 그 여자가 무얼 하려는지, 바로 그 계획을 알아챈 것이 그 애로 하여금 곧장 나서서, 외삼촌께 모든 걸 털어놓고, 그 너그러움에 자신을 내맡기고, 한마디로 그토록 오래 끌어온 그 비참한 은닉의 상태에 종지부를 찍게 만든 거란다.\"\n\n엠마는 한결 귀를 기울이기 시작했다.\n\n\"곧 그 애한테서 소식이 올 거야.\" 웨스턴 부인이 말을 이었다. \"헤어질 때 곧 편지하겠다고 했고, 지금은 들려줄 수 없는 자세한 사정을 많이 알려 주겠다고 약속하는 듯한 투로 말했거든. 그러니 우리 그 편지를 기다려 보자. 어쩌면 정상을 참작할 사정이 많이 담겨 있을지도 몰라. 지금은 도무지 이해할 수 없는 많은 일들이, 그 편지 덕에 납득되고 용서될지도 모르지. 우리 너무 모질게 굴지 말자. 그 애를 정죄하는 데 서두르지 말자. 인내심을 갖자꾸나. 나는 그 애를 사랑할 수밖에 없어. 그리고 이제 한 가지 점에서, 그 한 가지 핵심적인 점에서 마음이 놓이고 보니, 모든 일이 잘 풀리기를 진심으로 바라게 되고, 그러리라고 기대할 마음도 생기는구나. 그렇게 비밀과 은닉으로 점철된 처지에서 두 사람 다 무척이나 괴로웠을 게 분명해.\"\n\n\"그 사람의 괴로움은,\" 엠마가 무덤덤하게 답했다. \"별로 그를 해친 것 같지 않은데요. 그래, 미스터 처칠은 그 일을 어떻게 받아들이셨대요?\"\n\n\"조카에게 더없이 호의적으로 받아들였어. 거의 아무 어려움 없이 승낙해 주었지. 일주일 사이에 그 집안에서 벌어진 일을 생각해 보렴! 가엾은 처칠 부인이 살아 있는 동안에는 희망도, 기회도, 가능성도 없었을 텐데. 그런데 그 유해가 가문 납골당에서 채 안식에 들기도 전에, 그 남편은 부인이 요구했을 법한 것과 정반대로 행동하도록 설득당한 거야. 부당한 영향력이 무덤 너머까지 살아남지 않는다는 게 얼마나 다행인지! 그 사람은 거의 설득할 필요도 없이 승낙해 주었단다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 엠마는 속으로 생각했다. \"그 사람이라면 해리엇한테도 그만큼은 해 줬을 텐데.\"\n\n\"이게 어젯밤에 결정됐고, 프랭크는 오늘 아침 동이 트자마자 떠났어. 하이베리에 들러, 아마 베이츠 댁에서, 얼마간 머문 모양이더라. 그러고는 이리로 건너왔지. 하지만 외삼촌께 돌아가려고 어찌나 서두르던지—지금 그 어느 때보다 외삼촌께 자기가 더 필요하니까—내가 말했듯이 우리와는 겨우 십오 분밖에 머물지 못했어. 무척이나 동요한 모습이었어. 정말이지 무척이나. 내가 전에 본 그 어떤 모습과도 영판 딴사람 같아 보일 정도로 말이야. 게다가 다른 모든 일에 더해, 그 여자가 그토록 몹시 편찮은 걸—전혀 짐작도 못 했던 일을—알게 된 충격까지 겹쳤으니. 보아하니 마음고생을 단단히 한 기색이 역력했어.\"\n\n\"그런데 그 일이 정말로 그렇게 완벽한 비밀 속에 진행됐다고 믿으세요? 캠벨 댁도, 딕슨 부부도, 그 누구도 약혼을 몰랐다고요?\"\n\n엠마는 딕슨이라는 이름을 입에 올리며 살짝 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"아무도. 단 한 사람도. 자기들 두 사람 말고는 세상 그 누구도 몰랐다고 그 애가 딱 잘라 말했어.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"차차 그 생각에 익숙해지겠죠. 두 사람이 무척 행복하기를 바라요. 하지만 저는 이게 언제까지나 아주 가증스러운 처사라고 여길 거예요. 결국 위선과 기만의 체계, 첩보와 배신이 아니고 뭐였겠어요? 솔직함이니 꾸밈없음이니 떠벌리며 우리 가운데 끼어들고는, 우리 모두를 속으로 품평하려고 그런 밀약을 맺어 두다니! 우리는 겨우내 봄내내 여기서 완전히 농락당한 채, 다들 진실과 명예의 대등한 토대 위에 서 있다고 믿었어요. 그런데 우리 한복판에, 둘이 함께 들으라고 한 적 없는 감정과 말들을 여기저기 옮기고, 견주고, 두고 판단했을지 모를 두 사람이 있었던 거잖아요. 서로에 대해 영 달갑잖게 이야기되는 걸 들었다면, 그 대가는 그들이 감수해야죠!\"\n\n\"그 점에 대해서라면 나는 아주 마음이 편하단다.\" 웨스턴 부인이 답했다. \"나는 둘 중 어느 한쪽에 대해서도, 둘 다 들어서는 안 될 말은 다른 쪽에게 한 적이 없다고 확신하거든.\"\n\n\"운이 좋으셨네요. 부인의 유일한 실수는 제 귀에만 들어간 거였으니까요. 우리의 어떤 벗이 그 아가씨를 사랑한다고 상상하셨을 때 말이에요.\"\n\n\"그래. 하지만 나는 늘 미스 페어팩스를 진심으로 좋게 보아 온 터라, 어떤 실수를 하더라도 그녀를 나쁘게 말할 수는 없었을 거야. 그리고 그 애를 나쁘게 말하는 일이라면, 거기서는 내가 안전했을 테고.\"\n\n이때 미스터 웨스턴이 창문에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 나타났는데, 분명히 살피고 있던 모양이었다. 그의 아내는 들어오라고 청하는 눈짓을 보냈고, 그가 빙 돌아오는 동안 덧붙였다. \"자, 엠마, 부탁이니, 저이 마음을 놓이게 하고 이 혼사에 흡족해하게 할 만한 말과 표정을 모두 보여 주렴. 우리 이 일을 좋게 받아들이자. 사실 그 애에 대해서는 거의 모든 게 정당하게 좋은 쪽으로 말해질 수 있어. 우쭐할 만한 연줄이야 아니지만, 미스터 처칠이 그걸 개의치 않는데 우리가 왜 그러겠니? 게다가 그 애한테는, 그러니까 프랭크한테는, 내가 늘 인정해 온—그리고 이 한 번의 큰 일탈에도 불구하고 여전히 인정하고 싶은—그렇게 한결같은 성품과 좋은 판단력을 지닌 아가씨에게 마음을 둔 게 무척 다행스러운 일일 수도 있지. 그 애 처지를 생각하면, 그 잘못조차 얼마나 많이 변호될 수 있겠니!\"\n\n\"정말 그래요!\" 엠마가 마음을 담아 외쳤다. \"여자가 오직 제 생각만 한 걸 용서받을 수 있는 처지가 있다면, 바로 제인 페어팩스 같은 처지일 거예요. 그런 사람을 두고는 거의 이렇게 말할 수도 있겠죠. '세상은 그들의 것이 아니요, 세상의 법도 그들의 것이 아니다'라고요.\"\n\n그녀는 들어서는 미스터 웨스턴을 미소 띤 얼굴로 맞으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"정말이지 저를 곱게도 골려 주셨네요! 제 호기심을 가지고 장난치고 제 짐작하는 재주를 시험해 보려는 수작이었나 봐요. 하지만 정말 저를 겁먹게 하셨어요. 적어도 재산의 절반은 잃으신 줄 알았다니까요. 그런데 위로할 일은커녕, 알고 보니 축하할 일이잖아요. 미스터 웨스턴, 영국에서 가장 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 아가씨 가운데 한 사람을 따님으로 두게 되신 걸 진심으로 축하드려요.\"\n\n그와 아내 사이에 오간 한두 번의 눈짓이, 모든 게 이 인사말이 선언한 그대로임을 그에게 납득시켜 주었고, 그것이 그의 기분에 미친 행복한 효과는 즉각적이었다. 그의 태도와 음성은 여느 때의 활기를 되찾았다. 그는 진심으로 고마워하며 그녀의 손을 힘껏 맞잡고는, 이제 시간과 설득만 있으면 이 약혼을 그리 나쁘지 않은 일로 여기게 되리라는 것을 증명하는 투로 그 화제에 들어섰다. 그의 동행들은 경솔함을 누그러뜨리고 반대의 모서리를 매끈하게 다듬을 만한 이야기만 들려주었다. 그리하여 그들이 함께 그 일을 죄다 이야기해 보고, 하트필드로 돌아오는 산책길에 그가 엠마와 다시 한번 그 일을 죄다 이야기해 볼 무렵에는, 그는 완전히 마음을 누그러뜨려, 그것을 프랭크가 할 수 있었던 가장 잘한 일이라 여기는 데까지 그리 멀지 않은 상태가 되어 있었다.\n\n# 제3권 11장\n\n“해리엇, 가엾은 해리엇!” — 바로 그 말이었다. 그 말 속에 엠마가 떨쳐 낼 수 없는, 이 일의 진짜 비참함을 이루는 고통스러운 생각이 담겨 있었다. 프랭크 처칠은 그녀 자신에게 아주 못되게 굴었다 — 여러모로 아주 못되게. 그러나 그녀를 그토록 화나게 한 것은 그의 처신이라기보다 그녀 자신의 처신이었다. 해리엇을 두고 그가 그녀를 끌어들인 그 곤경이야말로 그의 잘못에 가장 짙은 색을 입힌 것이었다. 가엾은 해리엇! 두 번째로 그녀의 오해와 부추김에 놀아난 신세가 되다니. 미스터 나이틀리가 언젠가 \"엠마, 자네는 해리엇 스미스에게 좋은 벗이 못 되었어\" 하고 말했을 때, 그는 예언을 한 셈이었다. 그녀는 자기가 해리엇에게 해만 끼친 게 아닌가 두려웠다. 이번 경우에는 지난번처럼, 화근을 만든 유일하고 본래의 장본인으로 스스로를 책할 수는 없었던 것이 사실이다 — 그렇지 않았더라면 해리엇의 상상 속에 결코 떠오르지 않았을 감정을 심어 주었다고 자책할 수는 없었다. 해리엇은 엠마가 이 일에 관해 한마디 귀띔하기도 전에 이미 프랭크 처칠을 흠모하고 그를 마음에 둔다고 털어놓았으니까. 그러나 막을 수도 있었던 것을 부추겼다는 점에서는 엠마도 완전히 죄가 있다고 느꼈다. 그런 감정에 빠져들고 그것이 자라나는 것을 막을 수도 있었다. 그녀의 영향력이면 충분했을 것이다. 그리고 이제 그녀는 자기가 그것을 막았어야 했음을 절절히 깨달았다. 그녀는 너무도 빈약한 근거에 벗의 행복을 걸어 왔던 것이다. 상식이 있었다면 해리엇에게, 그를 마음에 두어서는 안 되며, 그가 그녀를 마음에 둘 가능성은 오백 분의 일에 불과하다고 일러 주었을 터였다. \"하지만 상식으로 말하자면,\" 그녀는 덧붙였다. \"나는 그것과 별 인연이 없었나 봐.\"\n\n그녀는 자기 자신에게 몹시 화가 났다. 프랭크 처칠에게도 함께 화를 낼 수 없었더라면, 그것은 견딜 수 없는 일이었으리라. 제인 페어팩스로 말하자면, 적어도 지금 그녀에 대한 근심에서는 마음을 놓아도 좋았다. 해리엇만으로도 걱정거리는 충분했다. 제인 때문에 더는 마음 아파할 필요가 없었으니, 그녀의 고통과 병약함이 같은 데서 나온 것인 이상 함께 치유될 것이 분명했다. 그녀가 보잘것없고 불행하던 나날은 끝났다. 머지않아 건강하고 행복하고 형편이 펴질 것이었다. 이제 엠마는 자신의 호의가 어째서 그토록 박대당했는지 짐작할 수 있었다. 이 깨달음이 자잘한 일들의 까닭마저 줄줄이 풀어 주었다. 틀림없이 질투 때문이었다. 제인의 눈에 자신은 연적이었으니, 자신이 내미는 도움이나 호의가 무엇이든 퇴짜를 맞는 것도 무리가 아니었다. 하트필드 마차로 바람을 쐬자는 권유는 고문 틀이었을 것이고, 하트필드 곳간에서 내온 칡즙은 독약이나 다름없었으리라. 그녀는 이제 모든 것을 이해했다. 그리고 분노에 깃든 불공정과 이기심에서 마음을 떼어 낼 수 있는 한, 제인 페어팩스가 분에 넘치는 영달이나 행복을 누리지는 않으리라는 것을 인정했다. 그러나 가엾은 해리엇이야말로 마음을 온통 사로잡는 짐이었다! 다른 누구에게 나누어 줄 동정심은 거의 남아 있지 않았다. 엠마는 이 두 번째 실연이 첫 번째보다 더 혹독하지 않을까 못내 두려웠다. 그 대상의 비할 데 없이 우월한 자격을 생각하면 마땅히 그래야 했고, 해리엇의 마음에 끼친 영향이 한층 강해 보여 그녀를 말수 적고 자제하게 만든 것으로 미루어 보아도 그러할 것이었다. 그러나 어쨌든 이 괴로운 진실을 알려야 했고, 그것도 되도록 빨리 알려야 했다. 미스터 웨스턴이 헤어질 무렵에 한 당부 가운데 비밀 엄수가 들어 있었다. \"당분간 이 일은 철저히 비밀에 부쳐야 한다. 미스터 처칠이 이를 거듭 강조했는데, 얼마 전에 여읜 아내에 대한 예의의 표시였고, 그것이 마땅한 도리라는 데에는 누구나 동의했다.\" 엠마는 그러마고 약속했다. 그러나 그래도 해리엇만은 예외여야 했다. 그것이 그녀의 더 큰 의무였다.\n\n분한 마음에도 불구하고, 그녀는 웨스턴 부인이 방금 자신에게 치른 것과 똑같은 괴롭고 까다로운 임무를 이제 자신이 해리엇에게 치러야 한다는 것이 거의 우습게 느껴지는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다. 그토록 조심스럽게 자신에게 전해진 소식을, 이제는 자신이 다른 사람에게 조심스럽게 전해야 하는 것이었다. 해리엇의 발소리와 목소리가 들리자 그녀의 가슴이 빠르게 뛰었다. 가엾은 웨스턴 부인도 랜덜스로 다가갈 때 이런 심정이었으리라. 이 고백의 결말도 그만큼 닮을 수 있다면! 그러나 불행히도, 그럴 가망은 없었다.\n\n\"어머, 미스 우드하우스!\" 해리엇이 방으로 다급히 들어서며 외쳤다. \"이런 희한한 소식이 또 어디 있어요?\"\n\n\"무슨 소식 말이에요?\" 엠마는 해리엇이 정말로 무슨 귀띔이라도 받았는지 표정으로도 목소리로도 짐작할 수 없어 이렇게 되물었다.\n\n\"제인 페어팩스 일이요. 이렇게 이상한 얘기 들어 보셨어요? 어머! 저한테는 솔직히 말씀하셔도 돼요. 미스터 웨스턴이 직접 저한테 얘기해 주셨거든요. 방금 그분을 만났어요. 절대 비밀이라고 하시기에, 미스 우드하우스 말고는 누구한테도 말할 생각을 안 하지만, 미스 우드하우스는 이미 알고 계신다고 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"미스터 웨스턴이 뭐라고 하셨는데요?\" 엠마는 여전히 어리둥절해하며 물었다.\n\n\"아! 다 말씀해 주셨어요. 제인 페어팩스와 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 결혼할 거고, 두 사람이 오래전부터 남몰래 약혼한 사이였다고요. 정말 희한하죠!\"\n\n과연 그것은 너무도 희한한 일이었다. 그러나 해리엇의 태도가 어찌나 별난지, 엠마는 그것을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알 수 없었다. 해리엇의 성격이 완전히 달라진 것만 같았다. 그녀는 이 소식에 동요도, 실망도, 별다른 근심도 내비치지 않으려는 듯했다. 엠마는 그녀를 바라보았으나, 도무지 입을 뗄 수가 없었다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스는 짐작하셨어요,\" 해리엇이 외쳤다. \"그분이 그 여자를 사랑한다는 걸요? 미스 우드하우스라면 아셨을지도 몰라요. (말하면서 얼굴을 붉혔다) 누구 마음이든 꿰뚫어 보시는 분이니까요. 하지만 다른 사람은 아무도—\"\n\n\"맹세코,\" 엠마가 말했다. \"내게 그런 재주가 있는지 의심스러워지기 시작하는걸요. 해리엇, 정말로 내게 물을 수 있어요? 내가 — 드러내 놓고는 아니더라도 은근히 — 당신더러 그 마음에 따르라고 부추기던 바로 그때, 그가 다른 여자에게 마음을 두고 있다고 내가 상상이나 했겠는지를요? 한 시간 전까지만 해도 나는 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 제인 페어팩스에게 손톱만큼이라도 마음이 있으리라고는 전혀 의심하지 않았어요. 만약 그랬다면 당연히 그에 맞게 당신에게 주의를 주었으리란 건 믿어 주세요.\"\n\n\"저요!\" 해리엇이 얼굴을 붉히고 깜짝 놀라며 외쳤다. \"왜 저한테 주의를 주신다는 거예요? 제가 미스터 프랭크 처칠을 마음에 둔다고 생각하시는 건 아니죠.\"\n\n\"이 일을 그렇게 단호하게 말하니 정말 기뻐요.\" 엠마가 미소 지으며 대꾸했다. \"하지만 한때 — 그리 멀지도 않은 때에 — 당신이 그분을 마음에 두고 있다고 내가 알아듣게끔 한 적이 있다는 건 부정하지 않겠죠?\"\n\n\"그분을요! 절대로, 절대로요. 미스 우드하우스, 어떻게 저를 그렇게 오해하실 수 있어요?\" 해리엇은 괴로워하며 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"해리엇!\" 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 엠마가 외쳤다. \"그게 무슨 말이에요? 세상에! 무슨 말이에요? 당신을 오해했다니! 그럼 내가 짐작해도 되나요—?\"\n\n그녀는 한마디도 더 잇지 못했다. 목소리가 나오지 않았다. 그녀는 자리에 앉아, 해리엇이 대답할 때까지 큰 두려움 속에 기다렸다.\n\n조금 떨어진 곳에서 얼굴을 돌린 채 서 있던 해리엇은 곧바로 입을 열지 않았다. 그리고 마침내 말을 꺼냈을 때, 그 목소리는 엠마의 것 못지않게 떨렸다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스가 저를 오해하실 수 있으리라고는,\" 그녀가 입을 떼었다. \"생각도 못 했어요! 우리가 그분 이름을 절대 입에 올리지 않기로 한 건 알지만 — 그분이 다른 누구보다 한없이 뛰어난 분이라는 걸 생각하면, 제가 다른 사람을 두고 한 말이라고 여겨지리라고는 도저히 상상도 못 했어요. 미스터 프랭크 처칠이라니요! 그분 곁에서 누가 미스터 프랭크 처칠한테 눈길이나 주겠어요. 제게도 그분 곁에선 아무것도 아닌 미스터 프랭크 처칠 따위를 마음에 둘 만큼 천한 안목은 없다고요. 그런데 미스 우드하우스가 그렇게 오해하셨다니, 놀라울 따름이에요! 정말이지, 미스 우드하우스가 제 연정을 온전히 인정하고 부추겨 주실 거라고 믿지 않았다면, 처음엔 감히 그분을 마음에 둔다는 게 너무 큰 주제넘은 짓이라고 여겼을 거예요. 처음에, 미스 우드하우스가 이보다 더 놀라운 일도 있었다고, 더 큰 신분 차이를 넘은 혼사도 있었다고 (그게 바로 미스 우드하우스가 하신 말씀이에요) 말씀해 주시지 않았다면 — 저는 감히 마음을 내맡기지도, 가능하리라 생각하지도 못했을 거예요 — 하지만 늘 그분을 알고 지내신 미스 우드하우스가—\"\n\n\"해리엇!\" 엠마가 결연히 정신을 다잡으며 외쳤다. \"이제 서로 분명히 해 둡시다. 더 오해할 여지가 없도록요. 당신이 말하는 게 — 미스터 나이틀리인가요?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 당연하죠. 다른 사람을 두고 한 말일 리가 없잖아요 — 그래서 저는 미스 우드하우스도 아시는 줄 알았어요. 우리가 그분 얘기를 할 때, 더없이 또렷했는걸요.\"\n\n\"꼭 그렇진 않아요.\" 엠마가 애써 침착하게 대꾸했다. \"그때 당신이 한 말은 죄다 내겐 다른 사람을 두고 한 얘기로 들렸으니까요. 당신이 미스터 프랭크 처칠의 이름을 댔다고 거의 단언할 수도 있어요. 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 집시들에게서 당신을 지켜 준 그 일을 분명 입에 올렸잖아요.\"\n\n\"어머! 미스 우드하우스, 어쩜 그렇게 깜빡하세요!\"\n\n\"해리엇, 그때 내가 한 말의 요지는 똑똑히 기억해요. 나는 당신의 연정이 놀랍지 않다고, 그분이 당신에게 베푼 그 도움을 생각하면 지극히 자연스러운 일이라고 말했어요. 그러자 당신은 그 말에 동의하면서, 그 도움에 대한 고마움을 아주 뜨겁게 토로했고, 그분이 당신을 구하러 나서는 것을 봤을 때 어떤 심정이었는지까지 이야기했어요. 그 인상이 내 기억에 또렷이 남아 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"어머, 세상에,\" 해리엇이 외쳤다. \"이제 무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요. 하지만 그때 저는 전혀 다른 일을 떠올리고 있었어요. 집시도 아니고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠도 아니었어요. 아니에요! (다소 들뜬 목소리로) 저는 훨씬 더 소중한 일을 생각하고 있었어요 — 미스터 엘턴이 저와 춤추기를 마다하고, 방 안에 다른 짝도 없었을 때, 미스터 나이틀리가 다가와 제게 춤을 청해 주신 일 말이에요. 그게 바로 그 다정한 행동이었고, 그게 바로 그 고결한 자애와 너그러움이었고, 그게 바로 제가 그분이 세상 다른 누구보다 얼마나 뛰어난 분인지 느끼기 시작하게 한 그 은혜였어요.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"이건 정말 불운하기 짝이 없는 — 가장 통탄할 오해였군요! 이를 어쩐담?\"\n\n\"제 말을 알아들으셨다면 부추기지 않으셨겠죠? 그래도 적어도, 그 다른 분이 상대였을 때보다 제 처지가 더 나빠질 건 없어요. 그리고 이제는 — 가능한 일이에요—\"\n\n그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추었다. 엠마는 입을 뗄 수 없었다.\n\n\"놀랍지 않아요, 미스 우드하우스,\" 그녀가 다시 입을 열었다. \"저에 관해서든 누구에 관해서든, 미스 우드하우스가 그 두 분 사이에 큰 차이를 느끼시는 게요. 한 분이 다른 분보다 저보다 오억 배는 더 높은 분이라고 여기시겠죠. 하지만 바라건대, 미스 우드하우스, 만에 하나 — 만약에 — 이상하게 들릴지 몰라도 — 하지만 아시잖아요, 이보다 더 놀라운 일도 있었다고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠과 저 사이보다 더 큰 신분 차이를 넘은 혼사도 있었다고, 그건 미스 우드하우스가 직접 하신 말씀이에요. 그러니 이 같은 일도 전에 일어난 적이 있는 것 같고 — 그러니 제가 이루 말할 수 없이 운이 좋아서 — 만약 미스터 나이틀리가 정말로 — 만약 그분이 신분 차이를 개의치 않으신다면, 바라건대, 미스 우드하우스, 이 일에 반대하시거나 길을 막으려 하지는 않으시겠죠. 하지만 미스 우드하우스는 그러기엔 너무 좋은 분이세요, 분명히요.\"\n\n해리엇은 창가 한쪽에 서 있었다. 엠마는 경악하여 그녀를 돌아보며 다급히 물었다.\n\n\"미스터 나이틀리가 당신의 마음에 화답하리라는 무슨 낌새라도 있나요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 해리엇이 겸손하게, 그러나 겁먹지 않고 대답했다. \"있다고 말씀드려야겠어요.\"\n\n엠마는 곧바로 눈길을 거두었다. 그리고 몇 분 동안 굳은 자세로 말없이 골똘히 생각에 잠겼다. 자기 마음을 깨닫는 데는 몇 분이면 충분했다. 그녀 같은 머리는 일단 의심의 문이 열리면 빠르게 나아갔다. 그녀는 진실의 전모에 가닿았고, 그것을 받아들였고, 인정했다. 해리엇이 프랭크 처칠보다 미스터 나이틀리를 사랑한다는 것이 어째서 이토록 더 나쁘단 말인가? 해리엇이 화답을 바랄 만한 무슨 희망을 품고 있다는 것이 어째서 그 화근을 이토록 끔찍하게 키운단 말인가? 화살 같은 빠르기로 그녀를 꿰뚫고 지나간 생각은, 미스터 나이틀리는 자기 자신 말고는 그 누구와도 결혼해서는 안 된다는 것이었다!\n\n바로 그 몇 분 사이에, 자기 마음과 더불어 자기 처신도 눈앞에 펼쳐졌다. 그녀는 일찍이 누려 본 적 없는 또렷함으로 그 모든 것을 보았다. 해리엇에게 자기가 얼마나 그릇되게 처신해 왔던가! 얼마나 무심하고, 얼마나 무례하고, 얼마나 분별없고, 얼마나 매정한 처신이었던가! 어떤 맹목이, 어떤 광기가 자기를 여기까지 이끌었던가! 그 깨달음은 무서운 힘으로 그녀를 강타했고, 그녀는 세상의 온갖 나쁜 이름을 다 갖다 붙일 채비가 되어 있었다. 그러나 이 모든 허물에도 불구하고 자기 자신에 대한 얼마간의 존중심 — 자기 체면에 대한 약간의 염려와, 해리엇에 대한 강한 정의감이 (미스터 나이틀리에게 사랑받고 있다고 믿는 처녀에게 동정 따위는 필요 없었지만, 정의는 지금 어떤 냉대로도 그녀를 불행하게 만들어선 안 된다고 요구했다) 엠마에게 그대로 앉아, 침착하게, 심지어 겉으로는 다정하게 더 견뎌 낼 결심을 안겨 주었다. 사실 자기 자신을 위해서라도, 해리엇의 희망이 어디까지 뻗쳐 있는지를 캐물어 두는 것이 마땅했다. 게다가 해리엇은 그토록 자발적으로 베풀고 지켜 온 호의와 관심을 잃을 만한 짓을 한 것도 아니었고, 한 번도 그녀를 옳게 이끈 적 없는 사람에게 박대당할 만한 짓을 한 것도 아니었다. 그리하여 그녀는 상념에서 정신을 차리고 격한 감정을 누른 뒤, 다시 해리엇에게로 몸을 돌려, 한결 다정한 어조로 대화를 이었다. 애초에 이 대화를 끌어낸 화제, 곧 제인 페어팩스의 그 놀라운 사연으로 말하자면, 그것은 이미 까맣게 가라앉아 잊혀 버린 뒤였다. 두 사람 다 미스터 나이틀리와 자기 자신 말고는 아무것도 머릿속에 없었다.\n\n그다지 불행하지 않은 몽상에 잠겨 서 있던 해리엇은, 미스 우드하우스 같은 심판자이자 벗이 이제 격려하는 태도로 자신을 그 몽상에서 불러내 주자 못내 기뻤고, 그저 권유만 바라던 차였다. 그리하여 떨리면서도 한껏 들뜬 기쁨으로 자기 희망의 내력을 풀어놓았다. 묻고 듣는 동안 엠마의 떨림은 해리엇의 것보다 잘 감추어졌으나, 결코 덜하지 않았다. 목소리는 흔들리지 않았다. 그러나 그녀의 마음은, 이런 식의 자기 발견과 이렇게 들이닥치는 위협, 갑작스럽고 당혹스러운 감정들의 혼란이 빚어낼 수밖에 없는 온갖 동요에 휩싸여 있었다. 그녀는 속으로 무던히 괴로워하면서도 겉으로는 큰 인내심을 발휘하며 해리엇의 자초지종을 들었다. 조리 있거나 잘 정돈되었거나 능숙하게 전달되리라고는 기대할 수 없는 이야기였다. 그러나 그 두서없고 군더더기 많은 서술에서 알맹이만 추려 내면, 그것은 그녀의 기운을 가라앉히기에 충분한 내용을 담고 있었다 — 특히 미스터 나이틀리가 해리엇을 한껏 좋게 보게 되었음을 뒷받침하는, 엠마 자신의 기억이 더해 주는 정황들과 더불어.\n\n해리엇은 그 결정적인 두 차례의 춤 이후로 줄곧 그의 태도에 달라진 점이 있음을 의식해 왔다고 했다. 엠마는 그가 그때 해리엇을 기대 이상으로 훌륭하다고 여겼다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그날 저녁부터, 아니면 적어도 미스 우드하우스가 그분을 마음에 두라고 부추긴 무렵부터, 해리엇은 그가 전보다 훨씬 자주 자기에게 말을 걸고, 자기를 대하는 태도가 정말이지 사뭇 달라졌음을 — 다정하고 상냥한 태도였다! — 느끼기 시작했다고 했다. 근래 들어 그녀는 그것을 점점 더 또렷이 알아차렸다. 다 함께 산책할 때면 그는 그토록 자주 다가와 그녀 곁에서 걸으며 그토록 즐겁게 이야기를 나누었다! 그는 그녀와 가까워지고 싶어 하는 듯했다. 엠마도 그것이 정말로 그러했음을 알고 있었다. 그녀 역시 그 변화를 거의 같은 정도로 자주 보아 왔던 것이다. 해리엇은 그가 인정하고 칭찬한 말들을 되풀이했고 — 엠마는 그것이 자신이 알고 있던 그의 해리엇에 대한 견해와 더없이 일치한다고 느꼈다. 그는 그녀가 꾸밈도 거짓도 없다는 점을, 소박하고 정직하고 너그러운 감성을 지녔다는 점을 칭찬했다. 그가 해리엇에게서 그런 미덕을 본다는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그는 한 번 이상 그녀 앞에서 그 점을 길게 말한 적이 있었다. 해리엇의 기억에 살아 있는 많은 것들, 그에게서 받은 관심의 자잘한 정황들 — 눈길 한 번, 말 한마디, 의자를 옮겨 앉은 일, 넌지시 비친 찬사, 미루어 짐작되는 편애 — 은 엠마가 의심하지 않은 탓에 알아차리지 못한 것들이었다. 그것을 본 사람에게는 숱한 증거를 품고 반 시간은 늘어놓을 수 있는 정황들이, 이제 그것을 듣는 사람에게는 미처 분간되지 못한 채 지나갔던 것이다. 그러나 언급된 가장 최근의 두 사건, 해리엇에게 가장 강한 약속처럼 보인 그 둘은, 엠마 자신도 얼마간 증언할 수 있는 일이었다. 첫째는, 돈웰의 라임나무 길에서 그가 그녀를 다른 사람들과 떨어뜨려 함께 거닐었던 일이었다. 엠마가 가기 전부터 두 사람은 한동안 거닐고 있었고, 그는 (그녀가 확신하기로) 일부러 그녀를 무리에서 떼어 자기 쪽으로 끌어내려 애썼던 것이다 — 그리고 처음에 그는 전에 없이, 정말이지 아주 각별한 투로 그녀에게 말을 걸었다! (해리엇은 얼굴을 붉히지 않고는 그 일을 떠올릴 수 없었다.) 그는 거의 그녀에게, 그녀의 마음이 누구에게 매여 있는지를 묻는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀가 (미스 우드하우스가) 합류할 낌새가 보이자마자, 그는 화제를 바꾸어 농사 이야기를 꺼냈다. 둘째는, 그가 하트필드에 머문 바로 그 마지막 날 아침, 엠마가 방문에서 돌아오기 전까지 거의 반 시간을 그녀와 마주 앉아 이야기를 나눈 일이었다 — 들어올 때만 해도 오 분도 못 머문다고 했으면서. 게다가 그는 대화 중에, 런던에 가야 하긴 하지만 집을 떠나는 것이 정말로 영 마음에 내키지 않는다고 그녀에게 말했다는 것이었다. 이는 (엠마가 느끼기에) 그가 자기에게 인정한 것보다 훨씬 더 깊은 속내였다. 이 한 가지 일에 드러난, 해리엇을 향한 더 큰 신뢰의 정도가 그녀에게 모진 아픔을 안겼다.\n\n그 두 정황 가운데 첫째에 관하여, 그녀는 잠시 생각한 끝에 다음과 같은 물음을 감히 던져 보았다. \"혹시 그게 아닐까요? 당신 생각엔 그분이 당신의 마음 상태를 캐물은 것 같지만, 사실은 미스터 마틴을 넌지시 가리킨 게 아닐까요 — 미스터 마틴의 이해를 염두에 두신 게 아닐까요?\" 그러나 해리엇은 그 의심을 단호히 물리쳤다.\n\n\"미스터 마틴이요! 천만에요! 미스터 마틴 얘기는 한마디도 없었어요. 이제는 미스터 마틴을 마음에 두거나, 그런 의심을 살 만큼 어리석진 않다고요.\"\n\n해리엇은 증언을 마치자, 자기에게 희망을 품을 만한 근거가 충분하지 않으냐고 사랑하는 미스 우드하우스에게 판단을 구했다.\n\n\"처음엔 감히 그런 생각은 엄두도 못 냈을 거예요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"미스 우드하우스가 아니었다면요. 그분을 잘 살피고, 그분의 태도를 제 태도의 기준으로 삼으라고 하셨잖아요 — 그래서 그렇게 했어요. 그런데 이제는 제가 그분을 가질 자격이 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어요. 그리고 만약 그분이 정말 저를 택하신다면, 그게 그렇게까지 놀라운 일은 아닐 거라고요.\"\n\n이 말이 불러일으킨 쓰디쓴 감정들, 그 숱한 쓰디쓴 감정들 탓에, 엠마는 안간힘을 다 짜내고서야 겨우 이렇게 대답할 수 있었다.\n\n\"해리엇, 이것만은 감히 단언할게요. 미스터 나이틀리는 세상에서 어떤 여자에게든 자기 마음이 실제보다 더한 양 일부러 믿게 할 사람이 결코 못 되는 분이에요.\"\n\n해리엇은 이토록 흡족한 한마디에 벗을 떠받들기라도 할 기세였다. 그리고 그 순간 끔찍한 고행이 되었을 황홀과 애틋함의 폭발에서 엠마를 구해 준 것은 아버지의 발소리였다. 그는 현관을 지나오고 있었다. 해리엇은 너무 동요한 나머지 그와 마주칠 수가 없었다. \"도무지 마음을 가라앉힐 수 없으니 — 미스터 우드하우스가 놀라실 테니 — 가는 편이 낫겠어요.\" 그리하여 벗의 선선한 권유에 따라 그녀는 다른 문으로 빠져나갔다. 그리고 그녀가 떠난 순간, 엠마의 감정이 절로 터져 나왔다. \"오, 하느님! 차라리 저 애를 만나지나 말 것을!\"\n\n그날의 남은 시간과 그날 밤은 그녀의 생각을 다 담기에도 모자랐다. 그녀는 지난 몇 시간 사이에 들이닥친 모든 일의 혼란 속에서 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 매 순간이 새로운 놀라움을 안겼고, 그 모든 놀라움은 그녀에게 굴욕거리가 될 수밖에 없었다. 이 모든 것을 어찌 이해한단 말인가! 자기가 이토록 자신에게 부려 온, 그 안에서 살아온 기만들을 어찌 이해한단 말인가! 제 머리와 마음의 그 실수들, 그 맹목이라니! 그녀는 가만히 앉아도 보고, 이리저리 거닐어도 보고, 제 방으로도 가 보고, 관목 숲으로도 나가 보았다 — 어느 자리에서든, 어떤 자세로든, 그녀는 자기가 더없이 나약하게 처신했음을, 더없이 굴욕적인 정도로 남에게 속아 왔음을, 그보다 더 굴욕적인 정도로 자기 자신을 속여 왔음을, 자기가 비참하며 아마도 오늘이 비참함의 시작에 지나지 않으리라는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n자기 마음을 이해하는 것, 철저히 이해하는 것이 첫 번째 노력이었다. 아버지의 부름이 허락하는 모든 한가한 순간이, 그리고 저도 모르게 정신이 딴 데로 흐르는 모든 순간이 그 한 점으로 향했다.\n\n미스터 나이틀리가 지금 모든 감정이 한목소리로 일러 주듯 그토록 소중한 사람이 된 지는 언제부터였던가? 그의 영향력이, 그토록 큰 영향력이 시작된 것은 언제부터였던가? 한때 잠시 프랭크 처칠이 차지했던 그 자리를, 그가 그녀의 애정 속에서 물려받은 것은 언제부터였던가? 그녀는 지난날을 돌아보았다. 두 사람을 견주어 보았다 — 후자를 알게 된 이후로 그녀의 평가 속에 늘 서 있던 그대로 견주어 보았다 — 그리고 만약 그녀가, 아아! 어떤 복된 행운으로든 그 비교를 해 볼 생각이라도 들었더라면 언제든 그렇게 견주었을 그대로. 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리를 한없이 우월한 사람으로 여기지 않은 때가, 그의 호의를 한없이 가장 소중한 것으로 여기지 않은 때가 단 한 번도 없었음을 깨달았다. 그녀는 자기가 스스로를 설득하고, 공상하고, 그 반대로 행동하면서 줄곧 망상에 빠져 자기 마음을 까맣게 모르고 있었음을 — 한마디로, 프랭크 처칠 따위는 애초에 정말로 마음에 둔 적이 한 번도 없었음을 깨달았다.\n\n이것이 첫 번째 상념의 결론이었다. 이것이, 첫 번째 물음에 관하여 그녀가 도달한 자기 자신에 대한 앎이었다 — 그것도 오래 걸리지 않고 도달한. 그녀는 더없이 비통하게 분개했다. 자기에게 드러난 단 하나의 감정 — 미스터 나이틀리에 대한 애정 — 을 빼면 모든 감정이 부끄러웠다. 그녀 마음의 다른 모든 부분이 역겨웠다.\n\n견딜 수 없는 허영심으로 그녀는 누구의 마음이든 다 꿰뚫어 안다고 믿었다. 용서받을 수 없는 오만으로 누구의 운명이든 다 짜 맞추려 들었다. 그녀가 두루 틀렸음이 드러났고, 게다가 아무 일도 안 한 것조차 아니었다 — 화근을 만들었으니까. 그녀는 해리엇에게, 자기 자신에게, 그리고 못내 두렵게도 미스터 나이틀리에게까지 화를 끼친 것이다. 이 더없이 어울리지 않는 결합이 정말로 이루어진다면, 그 시작을 만들었다는 모든 비난은 그녀에게 돌아갈 것이었다. 그의 연정으로 말하자면, 오직 해리엇의 마음을 의식한 데서 생겨난 것이라고 믿을 수밖에 없었으니까. 그리고 설령 그렇지 않다 해도, 그녀의 어리석음이 아니었다면 그는 애초에 해리엇을 알지도 못했을 것이었다.\n\n미스터 나이틀리와 해리엇 스미스라니! 그것은 이런 종류의 온갖 놀라움을 멀찌감치 따돌리는 결합이었다. 프랭크 처칠과 제인 페어팩스의 연정 따위는 이에 견주면 진부하고, 낡고, 김빠진 것이 되어 — 놀라움을 자아내지도, 신분 차이를 드러내지도, 입에 올리거나 생각할 거리를 내놓지도 못했다. 미스터 나이틀리와 해리엇 스미스라니! 그쪽엔 이 무슨 높이뛰기며! 그분에겐 이 무슨 추락이란 말인가! 이 일이 세상의 평판 속에서 그를 얼마나 끌어내릴지, 그를 두고 일어날 비웃음과 조롱과 웃음거리를, 그의 동생의 굴욕과 멸시를, 그 자신에게 닥칠 천 가지 곤란을 미리 그려 보는 것이 엠마에게는 끔찍했다. 그럴 수 있는 일일까? 아니, 불가능한 일이었다. 그러나 그것은 불가능과는 멀고, 아주 멀었다. 일류의 능력을 지닌 남자가 한참 못한 재간에 사로잡히는 것이 새삼스러운 일이던가? 어쩌면 너무 바빠 짝을 찾을 겨를도 없던 남자가, 제 발로 찾아 나선 처녀의 전리품이 되는 것이 새삼스러운 일이던가? 이 세상에서 어울리지 않고, 모순되고, 부조화한 무언가가 있다는 것이, 또는 우연과 정황이 (제이의 원인으로서) 인간의 운명을 좌우한다는 것이 새삼스러운 일이던가?\n\n오! 애초에 해리엇을 끌어들이지나 말았더라면! 그녀가 마땅히 있어야 할 자리, 그분이 그녀에게 있어야 한다고 일러 준 그 자리에 그녀를 그냥 두었더라면! 그 어떤 말로도 표현할 수 없는 어리석음으로, 그녀가 응당 속해야 할 처지에서 그녀를 행복하고 떳떳하게 해 주었을 나무랄 데 없는 젊은이와의 혼사를 가로막지나 않았더라면 — 모든 것이 무사했을 것이고, 이 끔찍한 후일담 따위는 하나도 없었으리라.\n\n해리엇이 어떻게 감히 미스터 나이틀리에게까지 마음을 끌어올릴 엄두를 냈단 말인가! 어떻게 그런 분의 간택을 받았다고, 확실히 확인되기도 전에 감히 공상할 수 있단 말인가! 그러나 해리엇은 예전보다 덜 겸손하고, 거리낌도 덜했다. 정신으로든 처지로든 자신이 못하다는 것을 거의 느끼지 못하는 듯했다. 미스터 엘턴이 자기와 결혼하는 것이 격을 낮추는 일임은 제법 의식하던 그녀가, 이제 미스터 나이틀리에 관해서는 그조차 의식하지 못하는 듯했다. 아아! 그것도 그녀 자신이 한 짓이 아니던가? 해리엇에게 제 잘난 줄 아는 생각을 애써 심어 준 것이 그녀 말고 누구였던가? 가능하다면 더 높이 올라가야 한다고, 세상에서 높은 자리에 오를 자격이 충분하다고 그녀에게 가르친 것이 그녀 자신 말고 누구였던가? 해리엇이 겸손하던 데서 허영에 차게 되었다면, 그것 또한 그녀가 한 짓이었다.\n\n# 제3권 12장\n\n이제껏, 그것을 잃을지도 모른다는 위협에 처하기 전까지, 엠마는 자신의 행복이 미스터 나이틀리에게 가장 첫째가는 사람이 되는 일 — 그의 관심과 애정에서 첫째가는 일 — 에 얼마나 많이 기대고 있었는지 미처 알지 못했다. 그것이 그러하다는 데 만족하고, 그것을 제 몫으로 여기며, 그녀는 별다른 생각 없이 그것을 누려 왔다. 오직 누군가에게 그 자리를 빼앗길지도 모른다는 두려움 속에서야, 그것이 얼마나 형언할 수 없이 소중했는지를 깨달았던 것이다. 오래도록, 아주 오래도록, 자신이 첫째였다고 그녀는 느꼈다. 그에게는 여자 친척이 따로 없었기에, 그녀의 자리에 견줄 권리를 가진 이라곤 이저벨라뿐이었고, 그가 이저벨라를 어느 만큼 사랑하고 존중하는지는 그녀가 늘 정확히 알고 있었다. 지난 여러 해 동안 그녀 자신이 그에게 첫째가는 사람이었다. 그럴 자격이 있었던 것은 아니었다. 그녀는 종종 그를 소홀히 했고 어깃장을 놓았으며, 그의 조언을 무시하고 심지어 일부러 그를 거스르기까지 했다. 그의 미덕을 절반은 알아보지 못했고, 제 잘난 착각과 오만한 자평을 그가 인정해 주지 않는다고 다투기도 했다. 그래도 가족 같은 정과 오랜 습관, 그리고 더없이 훌륭한 마음씨로, 그는 그녀를 사랑해 왔으며, 어린 소녀였을 적부터 그녀를 지켜보아 왔다 — 그녀를 더 나은 사람으로 만들려는 노력과, 그녀가 옳게 행하기를 바라는 염려로써. 그 누구도 그런 마음을 조금이라도 함께 나눈 적이 없었다. 온갖 결점에도 불구하고, 자신이 그에게 소중한 사람임을 그녀는 알았다. 어쩌면 아주 소중하다고 말해도 되지 않을까? — 그러나 여기서 뒤따라 떠오를 수밖에 없는 희망의 속삭임이 고개를 들 때면, 그녀는 감히 그것에 빠져들 엄두를 내지 못했다. 해리엇 스미스라면 미스터 나이틀리에게서 각별히, 오직 자기만을, 열렬히 사랑받기에 모자라지 않다고 스스로 여길 수 있을지 모른다. 하지만 그녀는 그럴 수 없었다. 그가 자신에게 품은 애정에 어떤 눈먼 구석이 있으리라는 생각으로 자위할 수는 없었다. 그것이 얼마나 공평무사한지를 보여 주는 증거를 그녀는 바로 얼마 전에 받지 않았던가. 미스 베이츠를 대한 자기 행동에 그가 얼마나 충격을 받았던가! 그 일을 두고 그가 그녀에게 얼마나 거리낌 없이, 얼마나 단호하게 제 뜻을 밝혔던가! 그 무례에 비추어 지나친 말은 아니었으나 — 곧고 올바른 정의감과 맑은 눈을 가진 선의보다 부드러운 어떤 감정에서 나온 것이라고 보기에는, 훨씬, 훨씬 지나치게 단호했다. 지금 문제가 되고 있는 그런 종류의 애정을 그가 자신에게 품을 수 있으리라는 희망은 그녀에게 없었다. 희망이라는 이름에 값할 만한 것은 아무것도 없었다. 다만 한 가지 희망이 있었으니 (어떤 때는 희미하고, 어떤 때는 훨씬 강한), 해리엇이 스스로를 속이고 있는 것일지도 모른다는, 그가 자기에게 보이는 관심을 과대평가하고 있는 것일지도 모른다는 희망이었다. 그렇게 되기를 그녀는 바라야만 했다 — 그를 위해서 — 설령 그 결과로 그녀 자신에게 남는 것이 그가 평생 독신으로 지내는 일뿐이라 해도. 정말이지 그것만 확실하다면, 그가 결코 누구와도 결혼하지 않으리라는 것만 확실하다면, 그녀는 더없이 만족하리라 믿었다. 그가 자신과 아버지에게 변함없는 미스터 나이틀리로, 온 세상에 변함없는 미스터 나이틀리로 남아 주기만 한다면. 돈웰과 하트필드가 그 귀한 우정과 신뢰의 왕래를 조금도 잃지 않기만 한다면, 그녀의 평온은 온전히 지켜질 터였다. 사실 결혼이란 그녀에게 어울리지 않는 일이었다. 그것은 아버지에게 진 의무와도, 아버지를 향한 마음과도 양립할 수 없었다. 그 무엇도 그녀를 아버지에게서 떼어 놓아서는 안 되었다. 설령 미스터 나이틀리가 청혼한다 해도, 그녀는 결혼하지 않으리라.\n\n해리엇이 실망하게 되기를 그녀는 간절히 바랄 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 두 사람을 다시 함께 볼 수 있게 되면, 적어도 그럴 가망이 얼마나 되는지는 가늠해 볼 수 있으리라 기대했다. 앞으로는 더없이 면밀하게 두 사람을 지켜보리라. 지금껏 지켜보던 이들조차 비참하리만치 잘못 읽어 왔으면서도, 이번 일에서는 자신이 눈멀 수 있다는 것을 그녀는 도무지 인정할 수 없었다. 그가 날마다 돌아오기를 기다리고 있었다. 머지않아 관찰할 기회가 주어질 터였다 — 그녀의 생각이 어느 한 갈래로 흐를 때면, 그 머지않음이 끔찍하게 느껴졌다. 그동안에는 해리엇을 만나지 않기로 마음먹었다. 이 일을 더 이야기해 봐야 두 사람 누구에게도 득이 되지 않을 것이요, 이 문제에도 득이 될 게 없었다. 의심할 여지가 남아 있는 한 그녀는 납득당하지 않기로 작정했으나, 그렇다고 해리엇의 확신에 맞설 근거도 없었다. 말을 나눠 봐야 부아만 돋울 뿐이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 해리엇에게 다정하되 단호한 편지를 써서, 당분간은 하트필드에 오지 말아 달라고 청했다. 어떤 한 가지 주제를 두고 더 이상 은밀한 의논을 나누는 일은 피하는 편이 낫다는 것이 자신의 확신임을 인정하면서, 며칠이 지난 뒤에 — 다른 사람들이 함께 있는 자리가 아니라면 — 다시 만난다면 (그녀가 꺼리는 것은 오직 단둘이 마주하는 일뿐이었다), 어제 나눈 대화 따위는 잊은 듯이 행동할 수 있으리라 기대한다고 적었다. 해리엇은 순순히 따랐고, 옳다고 여겼으며, 고마워했다.\n\n이 문제가 막 정리되었을 때, 손님 하나가 찾아와, 자나 깨나 지난 스물네 시간 동안 그녀의 마음을 사로잡고 있던 단 하나의 주제에서 엠마의 생각을 조금이나마 떼어 놓았다. 다름 아닌 웨스턴 부인이었으니, 장차 며느리가 될 사람을 보고 오는 길에 집으로 돌아가는 도중 하트필드에 들른 것이었다. 그토록 흥미로운 만남의 자초지종을 죄다 들려주려는 마음에서였는데, 그것은 제 즐거움을 위해서이기도 했지만 엠마에 대한 의무에서이기도 했다.\n\n미스터 웨스턴이 베이츠 부인 댁까지 부인과 동행하여, 이 긴요한 예방에서 자기 몫을 더없이 훌륭하게 해냈다. 그런 뒤 부인은 미스 페어팩스를 꾀어 함께 바람을 쐬러 나갔다가 돌아온 참이라, 어색한 감정의 거추장스러움을 죄다 안은 채 베이츠 부인의 응접실에서 보낸 십오 분이 줄 수 있었던 것보다 훨씬 많은 이야깃거리를, 그것도 훨씬 흡족하게 전할 이야깃거리를 가지고 온 것이었다.\n\n엠마에게도 얼마간의 호기심은 있었다. 그래서 벗이 이야기를 들려주는 동안 그 호기심을 한껏 살렸다. 웨스턴 부인 자신도 적잖이 마음을 졸이며 그 방문에 나섰다. 우선은 지금 당장 가지 않았으면 했고, 대신 미스 페어팩스에게 편지만 쓰도록 허락받기를, 그리고 이 격식 차린 방문은 시간이 조금 흘러 미스터 처칠이 약혼이 알려지는 일에 마음을 누그러뜨릴 수 있을 때까지 미루기를 바랐던 것이다. 여러모로 헤아려 볼 때, 그런 방문은 소문을 낳지 않고서는 이뤄질 수 없다고 여겼기 때문이다. 그러나 미스터 웨스턴의 생각은 달랐다. 그는 미스 페어팩스와 그 식구들에게 자신의 흡족함을 보이고 싶은 마음이 간절했고, 그 일로 어떤 의심이 일 수 있으리라고는 생각하지 않았다. 설령 의심이 인다 한들 대수로울 게 없다고 여겼으니, \"그런 일이야,\" 그가 한 말로는, \"어차피 다 퍼지게 마련이라\"는 것이었다. 엠마는 빙긋 웃었고, 미스터 웨스턴이 그렇게 말할 만한 근거가 충분하다고 느꼈다. 어쨌든 그들은 갔던 것이다 — 그리고 그 아가씨가 내비친 곤혹과 당혹은 누가 보아도 대단했다. 그녀는 거의 한마디도 입에 올리지 못했고, 눈길 하나 몸짓 하나에 자의식 때문에 얼마나 깊이 괴로워하는지가 죄다 드러났다. 그 노부인의 잔잔하고 진심 어린 만족과, 평소처럼 말을 잇지 못할 만큼 들떠 버린 그 딸의 황홀한 기쁨은, 흐뭇하면서도 거의 가슴 뭉클한 광경이었다. 두 사람은 그 행복 속에서 어찌나 진실로 존경스러웠고, 그 모든 감정에 어찌나 사심이 없었던지, 제 생각은 그토록 적게 하면서 제인을 그토록 많이, 또 누구든 그토록 많이 생각하니, 온갖 다정한 마음이 그들을 향해 절로 움직였다. 미스 페어팩스가 최근에 앓았던 일은 웨스턴 부인이 함께 바람을 쐬러 가자고 청할 그럴듯한 구실이 되어 주었다. 처음에는 뒷걸음치며 사양했으나, 거듭 권하자 응했다. 그리고 마차로 도는 동안 웨스턴 부인은 부드럽게 격려하여 그녀의 어색함을 어지간히 풀어 주었고, 마침내 그 중대한 주제를 두고 이야기를 나누게끔 이끌었다. 처음 맞이할 적에 보인 무례해 보이는 침묵에 대한 사과며, 자신과 미스터 웨스턴을 향해 늘 품고 있던 고마움의 더없이 따뜻한 표현이, 자연히 이야기의 물꼬를 텄을 것이다. 그러나 그런 토로가 일단락되자, 두 사람은 약혼의 현재와 앞날에 관해 많은 이야기를 나누었다. 모든 것이 그토록 오래 마음속에 갇혀 있던 동행에게는 그런 대화가 더없는 위안이 되리라고 웨스턴 부인은 확신했으며, 그 주제에 관해 그녀가 한 말이 모두 무척 마음에 들었다.\n\n\"그토록 여러 달 동안 숨기고 지내는 사이에 자기가 겪은 그 고통에 대해 말이지,\" 웨스턴 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그 애는 격하게 토로하더구나. 이건 그 애가 한 말 그대로야. '약혼한 뒤로 행복한 순간이 더러는 없었다고는 못 하겠어요. 하지만 단 한 시간의 평온한 축복도 누려 본 적이 없다는 건 말할 수 있어요.' — 엠마, 그 말을 내뱉던 떨리는 입술이, 내 가슴으로 느낀 하나의 증거였단다.\"\n\n\"가엾어라!\" 엠마가 말했다. \"그럼 그분은 은밀한 약혼에 응했던 걸 잘못이라고 여기는 거군요?\"\n\n\"잘못이라니! 그 애가 스스로를 나무라는 만큼 그 애를 나무랄 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없을 거다. '그 결과로,' 그 애가 그러더구나, '저는 끝없는 고통의 상태에 놓였어요. 그리고 마땅히 그래야 했고요. 하지만 잘못된 행실이 가져올 수 있는 온갖 벌을 다 받는다 해도, 잘못된 행실인 건 매한가지예요. 고통이 속죄가 되지는 않아요. 저는 결코 죄 없는 사람이 될 수 없어요. 저는 옳고 그름에 대한 제 모든 감각을 거슬러 행동해 왔어요. 모든 일이 다행스럽게 풀려 가는 것도, 제가 지금 받고 있는 친절도, 제 양심이 말하기로는 받아서는 안 될 것들이에요.' '부인,' 그 애가 이어서 말하길, '제가 잘못 배웠다고는 생각지 마세요. 저를 길러 주신 분들의 가르침이나 보살핌에 어떤 허물도 돌리지 말아 주세요. 잘못은 온전히 제 것이에요. 그리고 정말이지, 지금의 형편이 베풀어 주는 듯한 온갖 변명거리에도 불구하고, 저는 이 일을 캠벨 대령께 알리는 것이 여전히 두려워요.'\"\n\n\"가엾어라!\" 엠마가 다시 말했다. \"그럼 그분이 그를 지나치게 사랑하는 거겠죠, 아마. 오직 연정 때문에만 그 약혼을 맺기에 이르렀을 거예요. 애정이 판단을 압도해 버린 게 분명해요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그 애가 그를 더없이 사랑하는 건 의심할 여지가 없지.\"\n\n\"아무래도,\" 엠마가 한숨을 쉬며 답했다. \"제가 그분을 불행하게 만드는 데 자주 한몫했던 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"네 쪽에서야, 얘야, 아주 악의 없이 한 일이지. 하지만 그 애가 전에 그가 우리한테 슬쩍 흘렸던 그 오해들을 들먹였을 때, 아마 그 마음속에 그런 게 얼마쯤 있었던 게다. 자기가 빠져든 그 잘못이 낳은 한 가지 당연한 결과는, 그 애가 한 말로는, 자기를 사리에 어긋나는 사람으로 만들었다는 거야. 잘못을 저질렀다는 자의식이 그 애를 숱한 불안에 내몰았고, 그래서 그 애를 그가 견디기 힘들 만큼 — 정말로 견디기 힘들 만큼 — 트집 잡고 짜증 내는 사람으로 만들어 버렸다는 거지. '저는,' 그 애가 그러더구나, '그분의 기질과 기분을 마땅히 헤아렸어야 했는데 그러질 못했어요 — 그분의 그 유쾌한 기분과, 그 명랑함, 그 장난기 어린 성품을요. 다른 상황이었다면 분명, 처음에 그랬던 것처럼 늘 저를 사로잡았을 텐데요.' 그러더니 그 애는 너 이야기를 꺼냈어. 그리고 네가 자기가 앓는 동안 베풀어 준 그 큰 친절 이야기를 하더구나. 그 모든 게 어떻게 얽혀 있는지를 내게 알려 주는 듯한 홍조를 띤 채, 기회가 닿거든 너에게 고맙다고 전해 달라고 하더라 — 아무리 고마워해도 모자라다면서 — 자기를 위해 베풀어 준 온갖 바람과 온갖 노력에 대해서 말이야. 자기가 너한테 제대로 된 인사 한 번 못 했다는 걸 그 애도 알고 있더구나.\"\n\n\"그분이 지금 행복하다는 걸 제가 모른다면,\" 엠마가 진지하게 말했다. \"물론 그 까다로운 양심에서 비롯되는 자잘한 흠이야 있겠지만, 그래도 행복할 게 분명한데 — 모른다면 이런 고맙다는 말은 견딜 수 없을 거예요. 아아! 웨스턴 부인, 제가 미스 페어팩스에게 끼친 해와 베푼 득을 따져 적은 장부가 있다면요! 자아 (스스로를 다잡으며, 좀 더 밝아지려 애쓰면서), 이건 다 잊어버려야겠죠. 이렇게 흥미로운 이야기를 전해 주시다니 참 다정도 하셔라. 그 이야기들이 그분을 더없이 돋보이게 해 주네요. 그분은 분명 무척 선한 사람이에요 — 부디 아주 행복하기를 바라요. 행운이 그 사람 편에 있는 게 마땅하죠. 미덕은 죄다 그분 편에 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n이런 결론을 웨스턴 부인이 대꾸 없이 넘길 수는 없었다. 그녀는 거의 모든 면에서 프랭크를 좋게 여겼고, 무엇보다 그를 무척 사랑했기에, 그 변호는 간절했다. 그녀는 더없이 조리 있게, 그리고 적어도 그에 못지않은 애정을 담아 이야기했다 — 그러나 엠마의 주의를 붙들기에는 늘어놓을 말이 너무 많았다. 엠마의 마음은 어느새 브런즈윅 스퀘어로, 아니면 돈웰로 가 있었고, 귀 기울이려는 노력조차 잊고 말았다. 그래서 웨스턴 부인이 \"있잖니, 우리가 그토록 애타게 기다리는 그 편지가 아직 안 왔단다. 하지만 곧 오겠지\" 하고 말을 맺었을 때, 엠마는 대답하기 전에 잠시 멈칫할 수밖에 없었고, 마침내 아무렇게나 대답할 수밖에 없었다. 그들이 그토록 애타게 기다린다는 그 편지가 도대체 무슨 편지였는지 도무지 떠올릴 수 없었으니 말이다.\n\n\"몸은 괜찮니, 엠마?\" 헤어지면서 웨스턴 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"아! 멀쩡해요. 아시잖아요, 저는 늘 멀쩡한걸요. 편지 오면 꼭 되도록 빨리 소식 전해 주세요.\"\n\n웨스턴 부인의 이야기는 엠마에게 더 많은, 달갑잖은 생각거리를 안겨 주었다. 미스 페어팩스를 향한 존경과 연민을, 또 그동안 그녀에게 저지른 부당함에 대한 자각을 키워 놓았던 것이다. 그녀와 더 가까이 지내려 애쓰지 않았던 것을 엠마는 쓰라리게 뉘우쳤고, 어느 만큼은 분명 그 원인이 되었던 시샘하는 마음을 떠올리며 얼굴을 붉혔다. 미스 페어팩스에게 어느 모로 보나 마땅했던 그 관심을 기울이라는 미스터 나이틀리의 익히 알려진 바람을 따랐더라면, 그녀를 더 알려고 애썼더라면, 친밀해지려 제 몫을 다했더라면, 해리엇 스미스가 아니라 그곳에서 벗을 찾으려 했더라면 — 지금 그녀를 짓누르는 모든 아픔에서 십중팔구 벗어날 수 있었으리라. 태생도, 재능도, 교양도, 한쪽이 고맙게 받아들일 동무로 삼기에 똑같이 어울리는 사람임을 가리키고 있었건만 — 다른 한쪽은, 도대체 무엇이었던가? 설령 두 사람이 끝내 절친한 벗이 되지 못했다 하더라도, 이 중대한 일에서 미스 페어팩스의 속내를 결코 털어놓는 상대가 되지 못했다 하더라도 — 그쪽이 가장 그럴 법한 일이었지만 — 그래도 마땅한 만큼, 또 그럴 수 있었던 만큼 그녀를 알았더라면, 미스터 딕슨에 대한 부적절한 연정이라는 그 끔찍한 의심에서만은 벗어날 수 있었으리라. 그 의심을 엠마는 어리석게도 제 손으로 지어내 마음에 품었을 뿐 아니라, 용서받지 못할 만큼 함부로 입 밖에 냈던 것이다. 그리고 프랭크 처칠의 경박함이나 부주의함을 통해, 그 생각이 제인의 섬세한 감정에 적잖은 괴로움의 빌미가 되었을까 봐 그녀는 몹시 두려웠다. 미스 페어팩스가 하이베리에 온 뒤로 그녀를 에워쌌던 온갖 화근 가운데, 정작 제일가는 화근이 자기 자신이었으리라고 엠마는 확신했다. 자신은 줄곧 적이었음이 분명했다. 세 사람이 함께 있을 때면 언제나, 자기가 제인 페어팩스의 평온을 수천 번씩 찔러 대지 않은 적이 없었다. 그리고 박스 힐에서의 그 일은, 아마도 더는 견딜 수 없게 된 한 마음의 고뇌였으리라.\n\n이날 저녁은 하트필드에서 무척 길고도 우울하게 흘러갔다. 날씨까지 음울함에 한몫을 보탰다. 차갑고 사나운 비가 들이쳤고, 칠월다운 것이라곤 바람이 잎을 앗아 가는 나무와 관목, 그리고 그토록 모진 광경을 그만큼 더 오래 보이게 할 뿐인 길고 긴 낮의 길이밖에 없었다.\n\n날씨는 미스터 우드하우스에게 영향을 미쳤고, 그는 딸이 거의 쉴 새 없이 마음을 써 주고, 일찍이 그 절반도 들인 적 없는 애를 쏟아야만 그럭저럭 견딜 만하게 지냈다. 그것은 웨스턴 부인이 혼례를 올린 날 저녁, 둘이서 처음으로 쓸쓸히 마주 앉았던 그날을 떠올리게 했다. 그러나 그때는 차를 마시고 얼마 안 되어 미스터 나이틀리가 걸어 들어와 온갖 우울한 상념을 흩어 주지 않았던가. 아아! 그런 방문들이 전해 주던, 하트필드의 매력에 대한 그 즐거운 증거들도, 머지않아 끝나 버릴지 모를 일이었다. 그때 다가오는 겨울의 결핍을 두고 그녀가 그려 보았던 그림은 빗나간 것으로 드러났다. 어떤 벗도 그들을 저버리지 않았고, 어떤 즐거움도 잃지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 지금의 이 불길한 예감만은 그와 비슷한 어긋남을 겪지 않으리라고 그녀는 두려워했다. 지금 그녀 앞에 펼쳐진 전망은, 온전히 떨쳐 낼 수 없을 만큼 — 아니, 일부나마 환해질 수조차 없을 만큼 — 위협적이었다. 그녀의 벗들이 이루는 무리 안에서 일어날 수 있는 일이 죄다 일어난다면, 하트필드는 견주어 보면 버려진 곳이나 다름없으리라. 그러면 그녀는 무너진 행복의 기운만 가지고 아버지를 위로하는 처지에 남겨질 터였다.\n\n랜덜스에서 태어날 아이는 그곳에서 그녀 자신보다도 더 소중한 끈이 될 것이요, 웨스턴 부인의 마음과 시간은 그 아이에게 다 쏠릴 터였다. 그들은 그녀를 잃을 것이요, 아마 어지간히는 그 남편마저 잃을 것이었다. 프랭크 처칠은 더 이상 그들 사이로 돌아오지 않을 것이요, 미스 페어팩스도 머지않아 하이베리 사람이기를 그칠 것이라고 보는 게 이치에 맞았다. 두 사람은 결혼하여 엔스컴이나 그 근처에 자리 잡을 것이었다. 좋은 것은 죄다 거두어져 버릴 터였다. 그리고 이 상실들에 돈웰의 상실까지 더해진다면, 그들이 손닿을 만한 곳에 명랑하거나 사리 밝은 교제라곤 무엇이 남겠는가? 미스터 나이틀리가 더 이상 저녁의 안락을 찾아 이곳에 오지 않게 된다니! 마치 제 집을 그들의 집과 언제든 바꿀 마음이라도 있는 양, 시도 때도 없이 걸어 들어오는 일이 더 이상 없게 된다니! 그것을 어찌 견딘단 말인가? 그리고 만약 그를 해리엇 때문에 잃게 된다면, 앞으로 그가 해리엇과 함께하는 데서 바라던 모든 것을 찾았다고 여겨야 한다면, 해리엇이 바로 그가 택한 사람, 첫째가는 사람, 가장 소중한 사람, 그 벗, 존재의 온갖 최상의 축복을 기대하며 바라보는 그 아내가 된다면 — 그것이 다 제 손으로 빚어낸 일이었다는, 그 마음에서 좀처럼 멀어지지 않는 생각 말고, 무엇이 엠마의 비참함을 더 키울 수 있겠는가?\n\n이런 지경에 이를 때면, 그녀는 흠칫 놀라거나, 무거운 한숨을 내쉬거나, 심지어는 몇 초쯤 방 안을 서성이는 것을 막을 도리가 없었다. 그리고 위안이나 평정 비슷한 무엇이라도 길어 올릴 수 있는 단 하나의 원천은, 앞으로 더 나은 처신을 하겠다는 제 결심과, 비록 다가올 겨울이며 앞날의 모든 겨울이 지난날에 견주어 기운과 명랑함에서는 못할지언정, 그래도 그것이 자신을 더 사리 밝고, 자기 자신을 더 잘 아는 사람으로 만들어 주고, 지나간 뒤에 뉘우칠 것을 더 적게 남겨 주리라는 희망이었다.\n\n# 제3권 13장\n\n이튿날 아침에도 날씨는 좀처럼 변함이 없었다. 그리고 같은 적막과 같은 우울이 하트필드를 지배하는 듯했다. 그러나 오후가 되자 하늘이 개었다. 바람은 한결 부드러운 방향으로 바뀌었고, 구름은 걷혔으며, 해가 모습을 드러냈다. 다시 여름이었다. 그런 전환이 안겨 주는 온갖 들뜸에 휩싸여, 엠마는 되도록 빨리 바깥으로 나가기로 마음먹었다. 폭풍이 지난 뒤의 잔잔하고 따뜻하며 눈부신 자연, 그 절묘한 풍경과 향기와 감촉이 이토록 그녀를 끌어당긴 적은 없었다. 그것이 차츰 가져다줄 평온함을 그녀는 갈망했다. 그리하여 미스터 페리가 식사 직후 찾아와 아버지에게 한 시간쯤 한가로이 곁을 내어 주자, 그녀는 지체 없이 관목 숲으로 서둘러 나갔다. 거기서 기운을 새로이 하고 생각을 조금 덜어 낸 채 몇 차례 거닐던 그때, 미스터 나이틀리가 정원 문을 지나 그녀 쪽으로 다가오는 것이 보였다. 그가 런던에서 돌아왔다는 것을 처음 알린 순간이었다. 바로 직전까지도 그녀는 그가 틀림없이 십육 마일 밖에 있으리라 여기며 그를 생각하던 참이었다. 마음을 추스를 시간은 그저 한순간뿐이었다. 침착하고 차분해야 했다. 반 분도 지나지 않아 두 사람은 마주 섰다. \"안녕하세요\" 하는 인사는 양쪽 모두 조용하고 어색했다. 그녀가 공통의 벗들 안부를 물었고, 모두 잘 지낸다고 했다. 언제 그들과 헤어졌느냐고? 바로 그날 아침이었다. 오는 길이 비에 젖었겠다고. 그렇다고 했다. 그가 그녀와 함께 걸을 작정임을 그녀는 알아차렸다. \"식당을 잠깐 들여다보았는데, 거기서는 자기가 필요치 않은 듯하여 바깥에 있는 편이 좋았다\"는 것이었다. 그가 표정도 말투도 즐거워 보이지 않는다고 그녀는 느꼈다. 그리고 그 까닭으로 그녀의 불안이 가장 먼저 떠올린 것은, 어쩌면 그가 동생에게 자기 계획을 털어놓았다가 그것이 받아들여진 모양에 마음이 상한 것이 아닐까 하는 생각이었다.\n\n두 사람은 함께 걸었다. 그는 말이 없었다. 그가 자꾸 자기를 바라보며, 그녀가 내보이고 싶은 것 이상으로 얼굴을 더 자세히 살피려 한다고 그녀는 느꼈다. 그리고 그 믿음은 또 다른 두려움을 낳았다. 어쩌면 그는 해리엇을 향한 자신의 연정을 그녀에게 이야기하고 싶은 것인지도 몰랐다. 입을 떼도 좋을 만한 격려를 기다리며 살피고 있는 것인지도 몰랐다. 그녀는 그런 화제로 길을 터 줄 만한 기력이 도무지 나지 않았고, 날 수도 없었다. 모든 것을 그가 직접 해내야 했다. 그러면서도 이 침묵을 견딜 수가 없었다. 그에게는 더없이 어울리지 않는 침묵이었다. 그녀는 곰곰이 생각하고 마음을 정한 뒤, 애써 미소를 지으며 입을 열었다.\n\n\"이제 돌아오셨으니 들으실 소식이 하나 있어요. 좀 놀라실 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 그가 조용히, 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. \"어떤 종류의 소식인데?\"\n\n\"아! 세상에서 가장 좋은 종류죠. 혼사예요.\"\n\n그녀가 더 말할 뜻이 없다는 것을 확인하려는 듯 잠시 기다린 뒤, 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"미스 페어팩스와 프랭크 처칠을 말하는 거라면, 이미 들었어.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그럴 수가 있어요?\" 엠마가 발그레 달아오른 뺨을 그에게로 돌리며 외쳤다. 말하는 동안, 그가 오는 길에 고더드 부인 댁에 들렀을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었던 것이다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 미스터 웨스턴한테서 교구 일로 몇 줄 받았는데, 그 끝에 무슨 일이 있었는지를 간략히 적어 두었더군.\"\n\n엠마는 적이 안심이 되어, 곧 한결 차분해진 목소리로 말할 수 있었다.\n\n\"당신은 아마 우리 가운데 누구보다도 덜 놀라셨을 거예요. 진작에 의심하고 계셨으니까요. 언젠가 제게 주의를 주려 하셨던 걸 잊지 않았어요. 그때 새겨들었어야 했는데, 하지만—\" (목소리가 가라앉고 무거운 한숨이 따랐다) \"저는 아무래도 눈먼 채로 살게 마련인가 봐요.\"\n\n한두 순간 아무 말도 오가지 않았고, 그녀는 자기가 무슨 특별한 관심을 불러일으켰으리라고는 미처 짐작하지 못하고 있었다. 그러다 자기 팔이 그의 팔에 끼워져 그의 가슴께로 끌어당겨지는 것을 느꼈고, 그가 깊은 감정이 어린 어조로 나직이 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다.\n\n\"세월이, 가장 사랑하는 엠마, 세월이 그 상처를 아물게 해 줄 거야. 자네의 그 뛰어난 분별력이, 아버지를 위한 자네의 노력이—나는 자네가 결코 스스로를 그냥 내버려 두지 않으리라는 걸 알아—.\" 그가 그녀의 팔을 다시 한번 힘주어 끼며, 한층 끊어지고 가라앉은 어조로 덧붙였다. \"더없이 따뜻한 우정의 마음으로—분노가—고약한 악당 같으니!\" 그러고는 더 크고 한결 다잡은 어조로 끝맺었다. \"그자는 곧 떠날 거야. 곧 요크셔로 가겠지. 나는 그녀가 안됐어. 그녀는 더 나은 운명을 누릴 자격이 있는데.\"\n\n엠마는 그의 말뜻을 알아들었다. 그리고 그토록 다정한 배려가 불러일으킨 기쁨의 설렘에서 벗어나자마자 대답했다.\n\n\"정말 친절하시네요. 하지만 오해하고 계세요. 바로잡아 드려야겠어요. 저는 그런 종류의 동정이 필요하지 않아요. 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알아보지 못한 탓에, 저는 그 두 사람을 두고 늘 부끄러워해야 할 방식으로 행동했고, 어리석게도 갖은 말과 행동을 하고픈 유혹에 넘어가, 그것이 언짢은 추측을 살 빌미가 될 수도 있게 되었어요. 하지만 그 비밀을 더 일찍 알지 못한 것을 후회할 다른 이유는 없어요.\"\n\n\"엠마!\" 그가 그녀를 간절히 바라보며 외쳤다. \"정말, 정말 그런가?\" 그러나 곧 스스로를 다잡으며—\"아니, 아니, 자네 말뜻을 알겠어—용서하게—자네가 그만큼이라도 말해 주니 기쁘네. 그자는 정말 아쉬워할 만한 사람이 아니야! 그리고 머지않아, 그것이 자네의 이성뿐 아니라 그 이상의 무언가가 인정하는 바가 되리라고 믿네. 자네의 연정이 더 깊이 얽히지 않은 게 다행이야! 솔직히 말해, 나는 자네의 태도만 보고는 자네가 느끼는 감정이 어느 정도인지 도무지 확신할 수가 없었어. 다만 호감이 있다는 것만은 분명했지. 그자가 받을 자격이 있다고는 한 번도 믿지 않았던 그런 호감이 말이야. 그자는 사내라는 이름에 먹칠을 하는 자야. 그런데 그 사랑스러운 아가씨가 그자에게 상으로 주어진단 말인가? 제인, 제인, 자네는 불행해질 거야.\"\n\n\"미스터 나이틀리.\" 엠마가 명랑하게 굴려 애썼으나 실은 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 말했다. \"저는 아주 별난 처지에 놓였어요. 당신이 계속 오해하시도록 둘 수는 없어요. 그렇지만 어쩌면, 제 태도가 그런 인상을 주었으니, 우리가 이야기하는 그 사람에게 제가 한 번도 마음을 둔 적이 없었다고 고백하는 것이, 여자가 그 반대를 고백할 때 느낌 직한 만큼이나 부끄러운 일이 될지도 모르겠어요. 하지만 저는 정말 한 번도 그런 적이 없어요.\"\n\n그는 완전한 침묵 속에서 들었다. 그녀는 그가 말해 주기를 바랐으나, 그는 입을 열지 않았다. 그의 너그러움을 받을 자격을 얻으려면 더 말해야겠다고 그녀는 짐작했다. 그러나 그의 눈에 비친 자기 모습을 또다시 낮춰야 하는 것은 괴로운 일이었다. 그래도 그녀는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"제 행동을 변명할 거리는 거의 없어요. 그분의 관심에 마음이 흔들려, 짐짓 기뻐하는 척하도록 저 자신을 내버려 두었어요. 아마 흔해 빠진 이야기겠죠—흔한 경우고요—저 같은 여자가 그 전에도 수백 명은 겪었을 일에 지나지 않을 거예요. 그렇다 해도 저처럼 분별력을 자처하는 사람에게는 더 변명의 여지가 없는 일이겠지요. 여러 사정이 그 유혹을 거들었어요. 그분은 미스터 웨스턴의 아들이고, 늘 이곳에 드나들었으며, 저는 늘 그분이 무척 유쾌하다고 여겼어요. 그러니, 요컨대\" (한숨을 쉬며) \"그 까닭을 아무리 그럴듯하게 부풀려 본들, 결국 모두 한 가지로 모여요. 제 허영심이 추어올려진 것이고, 그래서 저는 그분의 관심을 허용한 거예요. 하지만 근래에는—사실 한동안은—그것이 무슨 의미를 지닌다고는 전혀 생각지 않았어요. 그저 하나의 버릇, 하나의 장난일 뿐, 제 쪽에서 진지하게 받아들일 것은 아무것도 아니라고 여겼어요. 그분은 저를 속였지만, 저를 해치지는 않았어요. 저는 한 번도 그분에게 마음을 둔 적이 없으니까요. 그리고 이제는 그분의 행동을 어지간히 이해할 수 있어요. 그분은 저를 끌어들이려 한 적이 없었어요. 그건 그저 다른 사람과의 진짜 관계를 감추려는 눈가림일 뿐이었어요. 그분의 목적은 주위 모든 사람의 눈을 가리는 것이었고, 저보다 더 감쪽같이 속아 넘어간 사람은 아무도 없었으리라 확신해요—다만 제가 속아 넘어가지는 않았다는 점, 그게 제 행운이었다는 점, 요컨대 어찌어찌하여 그분에게서 무사할 수 있었다는 점만은 빼고요.\"\n\n여기서 그녀는 대답을 기대했다. 적어도 자기 행동이 이해할 만하다는 한두 마디 말을. 그러나 그는 잠자코 있었고, 그녀가 판단하기로는 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯했다. 마침내, 어지간히 평소의 어조로 그가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 프랭크 처칠을 한 번도 높이 평가한 적이 없어. 하지만 어쩌면 내가 그를 과소평가했는지도 모르겠어. 그와의 사귐이 워낙 보잘것없었으니까. 그리고 설령 지금껏 내가 그를 과소평가한 게 아니라 해도, 그가 앞으로 잘되어 갈 수는 있겠지. 그런 여자를 곁에 두었으니 가망이 있는 거야. 나는 그가 잘못되기를 바랄 까닭이 없어. 그리고 그의 됨됨이와 행실에 자신의 행복이 달려 있을 그녀를 위해서라도, 나는 분명 그가 잘되기를 바랄 거야.\"\n\n\"두 사람이 함께 행복하리라는 건 의심치 않아요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"서로에게 아주 깊고 진실하게 마음을 두고 있다고 믿어요.\"\n\n\"그자는 더없이 운 좋은 사내야!\" 미스터 나이틀리가 힘주어 대꾸했다. \"그렇게 인생 이른 시기에—스물세 살에—사내가 아내를 고르면 대개 잘못 고르고 마는 그 나이에 말이야. 스물세 살에 그런 행운을 거머쥐다니! 그 사내 앞에는, 인간으로서 따져 볼 수 있는 한, 얼마나 많은 행복의 세월이 펼쳐져 있는가! 그런 여자의 사랑을, 사심 없는 사랑을 보장받은 셈이지—제인 페어팩스의 인품이 그 사심 없음을 보증해 주니까. 모든 것이 그에게 유리해. 처지의 대등함—내 말은, 사교계와 관련된 한, 그리고 중요한 온갖 습관과 몸가짐에 있어서 그렇다는 거야. 단 한 가지를 빼고는 모든 점에서 대등하지. 그리고 그 한 가지마저도, 그녀의 마음이 순결함은 의심할 여지가 없으니, 도리어 그의 행복을 더해 줄 것이 분명해. 그녀에게 모자란 단 하나의 이점을, 그가 베풀어 줄 몫이 될 테니 말이야. 사내라면 누구나 자기가 데려오는 곳보다 더 나은 보금자리를 여자에게 주고 싶어 하는 법이고, 그녀의 마음에 의심할 바가 없는 처지에서 그렇게 해 줄 수 있는 사내라면, 내 생각에 세상에서 가장 행복한 인간일 거야. 프랭크 처칠은 정말이지 운명의 총아야. 모든 일이 그에게 좋은 쪽으로 풀려. 어느 온천 휴양지에서 한 젊은 여자를 만나, 그 마음을 얻고, 소홀히 대해도 그녀를 지치게 만들 수조차 없었어. 그와 그의 온 집안이 그를 위한 완벽한 아내를 찾아 온 세상을 뒤졌다 한들, 그녀보다 나은 여자는 찾지 못했을 거야. 외숙모가 걸림돌이었지. 그런데 외숙모가 죽었어. 그는 그저 입만 떼면 그만이고. 그의 식구들은 그의 행복을 거들어 주려고 안달이지. 그는 모두를 함부로 대했건만, 다들 기꺼이 그를 용서해 주고 있어. 정말이지 운 좋은 사내야!\"\n\n\"꼭 그를 부러워하시는 것처럼 말씀하시네요.\"\n\n\"그래, 나는 그를 부러워해, 엠마. 한 가지 점에서 그는 내 부러움의 대상이야.\"\n\n엠마는 더 말할 수 없었다. 두 사람은 해리엇에게서 반 문장도 채 떨어지지 않은 듯했고, 그녀의 즉각적인 심정은 되도록이면 그 화제를 피하자는 것이었다. 그녀는 계획을 세웠다—전혀 다른 이야기를 꺼내리라고. 브런즈윅 스퀘어의 아이들 이야기를. 그저 입을 떼려고 숨을 고르고 있던 차에, 미스터 나이틀리가 이렇게 말해 그녀를 흠칫 놀라게 했다.\n\n\"그 부러움의 대목이 무엇인지 묻지 않으려는군. 보아하니 호기심 따위는 갖지 않기로 작정했어. 현명한 일이지. 하지만 나는 현명할 수가 없어. 엠마, 자네가 묻지 않으려는 것을 내가 말해야겠어. 다음 순간 입 밖에 내지 말걸 하고 후회할지언정.\"\n\n\"아! 그러시면, 말씀하지 마세요, 말씀하지 마세요.\" 그녀가 다급히 외쳤다. \"조금 시간을 두고, 곰곰이 생각하시고, 섣불리 다짐하지 마세요.\"\n\n\"고맙네.\" 그가 깊은 굴욕이 어린 어조로 말했고, 그 뒤로는 한 음절도 따라 나오지 않았다.\n\n엠마는 그에게 고통을 안기는 것을 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 속내를 털어놓고 싶어 했고—어쩌면 의논하고 싶어 했다. 무슨 대가를 치르더라도, 그녀는 들어 주리라. 그의 결심을 거들어 주거나, 그 결심과 그를 화해시켜 줄 수도 있으리라. 해리엇을 마땅히 칭찬해 주거나, 그가 자유로운 몸임을 일깨워 줌으로써, 그처럼 단호한 사람에게는 어떤 결정보다도 더 견디기 힘들 그 우유부단함에서 그를 풀어 줄 수도 있으리라. 두 사람은 집에 다다랐다.\n\n\"들어가려는 거지?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 엠마가 대답했다. 그가 여전히 가라앉은 태도로 말하는 데 마음을 굳힌 터였다. \"한 바퀴 더 돌고 싶어요. 미스터 페리가 아직 안 가셨거든요.\" 그러고는 몇 걸음 더 나아간 뒤 덧붙였다. \"조금 전에 제가 무례하게 당신을 가로막아, 마음을 아프게 해 드린 것 같아요. 하지만 벗으로서 제게 터놓고 말씀하고 싶으시거나, 마음에 품고 계신 무언가에 대해 제 의견을 묻고 싶으시다면—벗으로서, 정말이지 저를 마음껏 부리셔도 좋아요. 무엇이든 들어 드릴게요. 제 생각을 있는 그대로 말씀드릴게요.\"\n\n\"벗으로서라!\" 미스터 나이틀리가 되뇌었다. \"엠마, 그건 아무래도 내가 두려워하는 말이야—아니, 나는 바라지 않아—아니, 잠깐, 그래, 내가 왜 망설여야 하지?—이미 감추기에는 너무 멀리 와 버렸는걸. 엠마, 자네의 제안을 받아들이겠어—이상하게 보일지 몰라도, 받아들이고, 벗으로서 자네에게 나를 맡기겠어. 그러니 말해 주게, 내게는 끝내 가망이라곤 없는 건가?\"\n\n그가 그 물음을 눈빛으로 던지려 간절함에 사로잡혀 멈춰 섰고, 그 눈빛이 그녀를 압도했다.\n\n\"내가 가장 사랑하는 엠마.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 한 시간의 대화가 어떻게 끝나든, 자네는 언제까지나 내게 가장 사랑하는 사람일 테니까—내가 가장 사랑하고 가장 아끼는 엠마—지금 당장 말해 주게. 거절할 거라면 '아니요'라고 말해.\"—그녀는 정말이지 아무 말도 할 수 없었다.—\"말이 없군.\" 그가 무척 생기에 차 외쳤다. \"한마디도 없어! 지금으로서는 더 바라지 않겠네.\"\n\n엠마는 이 순간의 격동에 그만 주저앉을 지경이었다. 더없이 행복한 꿈에서 깨어날지 모른다는 두려움이, 어쩌면 가장 두드러진 감정이었다.\n\n\"나는 그럴듯한 말을 늘어놓을 줄 모르네, 엠마.\" 그가 곧 다시 입을 열었다. 그리고 그것은 어지간히 설득력 있을 만큼 진실하고 단호하며 또렷한 다정함이 어린 어조였다. \"내가 자네를 덜 사랑한다면, 그 일을 두고 더 그럴싸하게 떠들 수 있을지도 모르지. 하지만 자네는 내가 어떤 사람인지 알잖아. 내게서는 진실밖에 듣지 못한다는 걸. 나는 자네를 나무라고 훈계했고, 자네는 영국의 어떤 여자도 견뎌 내지 못했을 만큼 그것을 견뎌 주었어. 지금 내가 자네에게 들려주려는 진실도, 그때처럼 잘 참아 주게, 가장 사랑하는 엠마. 그 진실을 내미는 방식도 어쩌면 추천할 구석이라곤 거의 없을 테니까. 하느님은 아시겠지만, 나는 참으로 무미건조한 구애자였어. 하지만 자네는 내 말뜻을 알아. 그래, 보다시피, 자네는 내 마음을 이해해—그리고 할 수 있다면 그 마음에 응해 주겠지. 지금으로서는 그저 한 번, 자네 목소리를 한 번 듣기만을 청하네.\"\n\n그가 말하는 동안, 엠마의 머릿속은 더없이 분주했고, 생각의 그 놀라운 속도로—그러면서도 한 마디도 놓치지 않은 채—전체의 정확한 진실을 단번에 붙들어 깨달을 수 있었다. 해리엇의 희망이 송두리째 근거 없는 것이었음을, 하나의 착각이요 망상이었음을, 그것도 자기 자신의 어떤 망상 못지않게 완전한 망상이었음을. 해리엇은 아무것도 아니었고, 모든 것은 자기 자신이었음을. 해리엇에 관해 그녀가 했던 말이 죄다 그녀 자신의 감정을 토로하는 말로 받아들여졌고, 그녀의 동요와 의혹과 망설임과 낙심이 모두 그녀 자신에게서 비롯된 낙심으로 여겨졌음을. 그리고 이 모든 확신을, 그에 따르는 온갖 행복의 광채와 더불어 깨달을 시간이 있었을 뿐 아니라, 해리엇의 비밀이 자기 입에서 새어 나가지 않은 것을 기뻐하고, 또 결코 새어 나가게 해서는 안 되며 그러지도 않으리라 다짐할 시간까지 있었다. 그것이 이제 그녀가 가엾은 벗에게 베풀 수 있는 유일한 도움이었다. 둘 중에서 해리엇이야말로 비할 수 없이 더 자격 있는 사람이니 그에게 애정을 자기에게서 해리엇에게로 옮겨 달라고 간청하게끔 그녀를 부추겼을 법한 그런 감정의 영웅적 행위라든가—혹은 그가 둘 다와 결혼할 수는 없으니, 아무 이유도 밝히지 않은 채 그 자리에서 영영 그를 거절하기로 결심하는 한결 단순한 숭고함조차도, 엠마에게는 없었다. 그녀는 해리엇을 아프게, 또 뉘우치는 마음으로 가엾이 여겼다. 그러나 있음 직하거나 사리에 맞는 모든 것에 거스르는, 미친 듯이 날뛰는 관대함의 비약 따위는 그녀의 머릿속에 들어오지 않았다. 그녀는 벗을 그릇된 길로 이끌었고, 그것은 영영 그녀의 마음을 책망하는 일이 되리라. 그러나 그녀의 판단력은 그 감정만큼이나 굳건했으며, 그에게 그런 결합—더없이 어울리지 않고 격을 떨어뜨리는 결합—을 단호히 배격하는 점에서는 여느 때처럼 흔들림이 없었다. 그녀가 갈 길은 분명했다, 비록 아주 순탄하지는 않았어도. 그리하여 그토록 간곡한 청을 받은 그녀는 입을 열었다. 무어라 말했던가? 물론, 마땅히 해야 할 말을 했다. 숙녀란 늘 그러는 법이다. 절망할 필요는 없다는 것을 내비치고, 그가 직접 더 말하도록 청할 만큼의 말을 했다. 그는 한때 절망했었다. 한동안 모든 희망을 짓뭉개 버린, 조심하고 입을 다물라는 그런 단속의 말을 들었던 것이다. 그녀가 먼저 그의 말을 듣기를 거절했으니까. 그 변화는 어쩌면 다소 갑작스러웠는지도 몰랐다. 한 바퀴 더 돌자던 그녀의 제안이며, 방금 제 손으로 끝맺은 대화를 다시 잇자던 것이, 조금 별나게 보였을지도 몰랐다! 그녀 자신도 그 앞뒤가 맞지 않음을 느꼈다. 그러나 미스터 나이틀리는 더없이 너그러이 그것을 받아들였고, 더 이상의 해명을 구하지 않았다.\n\n좀처럼, 참으로 좀처럼, 완전한 진실이 인간의 어떤 고백에 깃드는 일은 없다. 무언가가 조금 꾸며지거나 조금 잘못 전해지지 않는 경우는 드문 법이다. 그러나 이 경우처럼, 비록 행동은 잘못 전해졌을지언정 감정만은 그렇지 않다면, 그것은 그리 중요하지 않을지도 모른다. 미스터 나이틀리로서는, 엠마가 실제로 지닌 것보다 더 누그러진 마음이나, 그의 마음을 받아들일 채비가 더 갖춰진 마음을 그녀에게 잘못 헤아릴 까닭이 없었던 것이다.\n\n사실 그는 자기 자신의 영향력에 대해 전혀 짐작하지 못하고 있었다. 그것을 시험해 볼 생각 따위는 조금도 없이 그녀를 따라 관목 숲으로 들어선 것이었다. 그는 엠마가 프랭크 처칠의 약혼을 어떻게 견디고 있는지 보고 싶은 걱정에 찾아온 것이었지, 무슨 사심에서가 아니라, 아니 아무 속셈도 없이, 다만 그녀가 틈을 내어 준다면 그녀를 달래거나 조언해 주려는 마음에서였다. 그 나머지는 순간의 작용이었고, 그가 들은 말이 그의 감정에 곧장 미친 결과였다. 그녀가 프랭크 처칠에게 완전히 무심하다는 것, 그를 향해 조금도 얽매이지 않은 마음을 지녔다는 것—그 기쁜 확신이, 머지않아 자기가 직접 그녀의 사랑을 얻을 수도 있으리라는 희망을 낳았다. 그러나 그것은 당장의 희망은 아니었다. 그는 다만, 한순간 간절함이 판단을 이긴 끝에, 그녀를 끌어들이려는 자기 시도를 그녀가 막지는 않는다는 말만이라도 듣기를 바랐을 뿐이었다. 차츰 열리기 시작한 더 큰 희망은 그만큼 더 황홀했다. 할 수만 있다면 빚어 내도록 허락해 달라고 청하던 그 애정이, 이미 그의 것이었다니! 반 시간도 채 못 되어, 그는 더없이 괴로운 심정에서, 달리 부를 이름이 없을 만큼 완전한 행복과 다름없는 무언가로 옮아가 있었다.\n\n그녀의 변화도 그에 못지않았다. 이 한 번의 반 시간이 두 사람 각자에게 사랑받고 있다는 똑같이 소중한 확신을 안겨 주었고, 각자에게서 똑같은 정도의 무지와 질투와 불신을 걷어 내 주었다. 그의 쪽에는 오래 묵은 질투가 있었으니, 프랭크 처칠이 도착한 때만큼, 아니 그가 오리라 기대되던 때만큼이나 해묵은 질투였다. 그는 거의 같은 무렵부터 엠마를 사랑하고 또 프랭크 처칠을 질투해 왔으니, 아마 한쪽 감정이 다른 한쪽을 그에게 일깨워 준 셈이었다. 그를 시골에서 떠나게 한 것도 바로 프랭크 처칠을 향한 그 질투였다. 박스 힐 모임이 그가 떠나기로 마음을 정하게 했다. 그처럼 허락되고 부추겨진 관심을 또다시 지켜보는 일에서 자신을 구해 내려는 것이었다. 그는 무심해지는 법을 배우러 떠났던 것이다. 그러나 그가 간 곳이 잘못되었다. 동생의 집에는 가정의 행복이 너무 많았고, 그곳에서 여자는 너무도 사랑스러운 모습을 하고 있었다. 이저벨라는 너무도 엠마를 닮아 있었다—다만 그 두드러진 모자란 점들에서만 달랐을 뿐인데, 그 점들이 도리어 엠마를 늘 더 눈부시게 그의 앞에 떠올려 주었으니, 설령 머무는 기간이 더 길었던들 무슨 소용이 있었으랴. 그래도 그는 날마다 꿋꿋이 버티며 머물렀다—바로 그날 아침 우편이 제인 페어팩스의 사연을 전해 올 때까지. 그러자, 응당 느낄 수밖에 없는, 아니, 프랭크 처칠이 엠마에게 도무지 어울리는 사람이라고는 한 번도 믿지 않았던 터라 거리낌 없이 느낀 그 기쁨과 더불어, 그녀를 향한 애틋한 염려가, 그녀를 향한 사무치는 근심이 어찌나 큰지, 그는 더 이상 머물 수가 없었다. 그는 빗속을 뚫고 말을 달려 집으로 돌아왔고, 식사를 마치자마자 곧장 걸어 올라온 것이었다—이 더없이 사랑스럽고 더없이 훌륭한 사람이, 그 온갖 흠에도 불구하고 흠 하나 없는 그 사람이, 이 발각을 어떻게 견디고 있는지 보려고.\n\n그는 그녀가 동요하고 풀이 죽어 있는 것을 보았다. 프랭크 처칠은 악당이었다. 그는 그녀가 그를 한 번도 사랑한 적이 없다고 단언하는 것을 들었다. 프랭크 처칠의 됨됨이도 그리 절망적이지는 않았다. 두 사람이 집으로 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 손길로도 말로도 그의 엠마였다. 그리고 그 순간 그가 프랭크 처칠을 떠올릴 수 있었다면, 그를 아주 괜찮은 사내쯤으로 여겼을지도 몰랐다.\n\n# 제3권 14장\n\n엠마가 집 안으로 지니고 들어온 마음은, 조금 전 들고 나갔던 마음과 어쩌면 그토록 다른 것이었을까!—그때만 해도 그녀는 고작 괴로움이 잠시나마 누그러지기를 감히 바랐을 뿐이었다.—그런데 지금 그녀는 더없이 황홀한 행복의 설렘에 휩싸여 있었고, 더구나 그 행복이란, 설렘이 가라앉고 나면 한층 더 커지리라고 믿어 의심치 않을 만한 것이었다.\n\n그들은 차를 들려고 자리에 앉았다—똑같은 사람들이 똑같은 식탁에 둘러앉았다—이 자리가 얼마나 여러 번 모였던가!—그리고 그녀의 눈이 잔디밭의 똑같은 관목 숲에 얼마나 여러 번 머물렀으며, 서녘 해의 똑같은 아름다운 빛을 얼마나 여러 번 바라보았던가!—그러나 이런 마음 상태로는 한 번도 없었고, 이와 비슷한 어떤 것으로도 한 번도 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 평소의 자기 모습을 그러모아 세심한 안주인 노릇은커녕 세심한 딸 노릇이라도 해내기가 여간 힘들지 않았다.\n\n가엾은 미스터 우드하우스는, 자신이 그토록 진심으로 맞아들이고, 마차로 오는 길에 행여 감기라도 걸리지 않았을까 그토록 노심초사 걱정하는 그 남자의 가슴속에서, 자기를 향해 어떤 음모가 꾸며지고 있는지 까맣게 몰랐다.—만약 그 속마음을 들여다볼 수 있었더라면, 폐 따위는 거의 안중에도 없었으리라. 그러나 다가오는 화근을 손톱만큼도 상상하지 못하고, 두 사람의 표정이나 거동에서 그 어떤 심상찮은 기미도 전혀 알아채지 못한 채, 그는 더없이 편안하게 미스터 페리에게서 들은 소식들을 죄다 두 사람에게 옮기며, 정작 그들이 자기에게 들려줄 수 있었을 이야기는 까맣게 모른 채 무척 흐뭇한 얼굴로 떠들어 댔다.\n\n미스터 나이틀리가 그들과 함께 있는 동안은 엠마의 열기가 계속되었다. 그러나 그가 떠나고 나자, 그녀는 조금씩 가라앉고 누그러지기 시작했다.—그리고 그런 저녁의 대가로 치러야 했던 잠 못 드는 밤을 지새우는 동안, 그녀는 곰곰이 따져 보아야 할 매우 심각한 문제 한두 가지를 발견하고는, 자신의 행복마저 얼마간의 불순물을 섞을 수밖에 없음을 느꼈다. 아버지—그리고 해리엇. 혼자 있노라면 그 둘이 제각기 거는 권리의 무게가 고스란히 느껴지지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 어떻게 하면 두 사람의 평안을 모두 최대한 지켜 줄 수 있을까가 문제였다. 아버지에 관해서는 곧 답이 나오는 문제였다. 미스터 나이틀리가 무엇을 청할지는 아직 잘 몰랐지만, 자기 마음과 아주 짧게 의논한 끝에, 아버지 곁을 결코 떠나지 않으리라는 가장 엄숙한 결심이 섰다.—그녀는 그런 생각을 떠올린 것조차 생각의 죄인 양 눈물을 흘릴 지경이었다. 아버지가 살아 계신 동안은 그저 약혼한 사이로만 남아 있어야 한다. 그러나 그녀는, 자기를 떼어 갈 위험만 사라진다면, 도리어 아버지에게 더 큰 위안이 될 수 있으리라고 스스로를 다독였다.—해리엇에게 어떻게 하는 것이 최선일지는 더 어려운 결정이었다.—어떻게 하면 쓸데없는 아픔을 면하게 해 줄 수 있을까. 어떻게 하면 조금이라도 보상을 해 줄 수 있을까. 어떻게 하면 그녀에게 가장 덜 적으로 보일 수 있을까?—이 문제들에 관해서는 그녀의 당혹과 괴로움이 무척 컸다.—그래서 그녀의 마음은, 일찍이 그것을 에워쌌던 온갖 쓰라린 자책과 서글픈 후회를 몇 번이고 되짚어 나가야 했다.—그녀가 끝내 결심할 수 있었던 것은 다만, 여전히 해리엇과 마주치는 일은 피하고, 꼭 전해야 할 모든 일은 편지로 알리리라는 것뿐이었다. 또 바로 지금 그녀를 한동안 하이베리에서 떼어 놓을 수 있다면 이루 말할 수 없이 바람직하리라는 것—그리고 한 가지 계획을 더 마음껏 그려 보며—브런즈윅 스퀘어로 그녀를 초대받게 하는 일이 어쩌면 가능할지도 모른다고 거의 결심하기에 이르렀다.—이저벨라는 해리엇을 마음에 들어 했었다. 그리고 런던에서 몇 주를 보내고 나면 그녀도 얼마간 기분 전환이 될 것이다.—거리와 상점들과 아이들, 그 새로움과 다채로움에서 해리엇이 득을 보지 못할 리는 없으리라는 것이 그녀의 생각이었다.—어쨌거나 그것은, 모든 것을 빚진 처지인 자기로서 마음을 쓰고 친절을 베푼다는 증거가 될 터였다. 당분간의 이별이요, 모두가 다시 한자리에 모여야 할 그 괴로운 날을 미루어 두는 일이기도 했다.\n\n그녀는 일찍 일어나, 해리엇에게 보낼 편지를 썼다. 그 일을 하고 나니 어찌나 마음이 무겁고 거의 슬퍼졌던지, 아침을 들러 하트필드로 걸어 올라온 미스터 나이틀리는 결코 너무 일찍 온 셈이 아니었다. 그러고 나서 그와 함께, 글자 그대로 또 비유적으로, 똑같은 자리를 다시 더듬어 보며 슬쩍 빼낸 반 시간은, 어제저녁의 행복을 제 몫만큼 되찾는 데 꼭 필요한 것이었다.\n\n그가 그녀 곁을 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않았을 때—다른 누군가를 생각할 마음이 손톱만큼이라도 일 만큼의 시간은 결코 아니었을 때—랜덜스에서 편지 한 통이 왔다.—아주 두툼한 편지였다.—그 안에 무엇이 들었을지 짐작이 갔기에, 그녀는 그것을 읽어야 한다는 사실이 마뜩잖았다.—그녀는 이제 프랭크 처칠을 향해 더없이 너그러운 마음이었다. 아무런 해명도 필요 없었고, 그저 자기 생각에만 잠겨 있고 싶을 뿐이었다.—그리고 그가 쓴 글을 무엇 하나 이해하기로 말하자면, 자기로서는 도무지 그럴 재간이 없으리라고 확신했다.—그러나 어쨌든 헤쳐 나가야 했다. 그녀는 봉투를 열었다. 과연 그러했다.—웨스턴 부인이 그녀에게 보내는 짧은 편지가, 프랭크가 웨스턴 부인에게 보낸 편지를 안내하고 있었다.\n\n\"사랑하는 엠마, 동봉한 것을 너에게 전하게 되어 더없이 기쁘구나. 네가 이것에 얼마나 온당한 평가를 내려 줄지 잘 알고, 또 그것이 좋은 결과를 가져오리라는 것도 거의 의심치 않는단다.—이제 우리가 이 글쓴이를 두고 크게 의견이 갈리는 일은 다시 없으리라 생각해. 하지만 긴 서두로 너를 붙잡아 두지는 않으마.—우리는 아주 잘 지낸단다.—이 편지가, 요즘 내가 느끼던 자잘한 초조함을 죄다 낫게 해 주었어.—화요일에 보니 네 안색이 영 마음에 들지 않더구나. 하지만 날씨가 궂은 아침이었으니까. 너는 날씨에 영향받는다는 걸 결코 인정하지 않겠지만, 북동풍이 불면 누구든 다 느끼는 법이란다.—화요일 오후와 어제 아침의 그 폭풍우 속에서 네 가엾은 아버님이 무척 걱정되었지만, 간밤에 미스터 페리를 통해 그분이 병이 나지 않으셨다는 소식을 듣고 마음을 놓았단다.\n\n이만 줄이마,\nA. W.\"\n\n[웨스턴 부인에게.]\n\n윈저—7월.\n\n친애하는 부인께,\n\n\"어제 제가 제 뜻을 알아들으실 만하게 말씀드렸다면, 이 편지를 기다리고 계시겠지요. 하지만 기다리셨든 아니든, 너그럽고 관대한 마음으로 읽어 주시리라는 것을 압니다.—부인은 더할 나위 없이 선량한 분이시고, 제 지난 행실의 어떤 부분들을 헤아려 주시자면 그 선량함을 다 동원하셔도 모자라리라 믿습니다.—그러나 저는, 저를 원망할 까닭이 그보다 더 많았던 한 사람에게서 이미 용서를 받았습니다. 이 글을 쓰는 동안 용기가 솟습니다. 형편이 좋은 자가 겸손하기란 무척 어려운 법이지요. 저는 이미 두 번의 용서를 청하는 일에서 그만한 성공을 거두었기에, 어쩌면 부인의 용서와, 또 제게 노여워할 만한 까닭이 있었던 부인의 벗들의 용서마저 너무 쉽게 확신해 버릴 위험에 빠질지도 모르겠습니다.—제가 처음 랜덜스에 도착했을 때 제 처지가 어떠했는지, 그 정확한 본질을 모두 헤아려 주셔야 합니다. 저를, 무슨 일이 있어도 지켜야 할 비밀을 지닌 사람으로 여겨 주셔야 합니다. 사실이 그러했습니다. 그런 은폐를 요구하는 처지에 저를 둘 권리가 제게 있었느냐는 것은 별개의 문제이지요. 그것을 여기서 따지지는 않겠습니다. 그것을 권리라고 여기게 만든 제 유혹에 관해서라면, 트집 잡으려는 분이 계시면 누구든 하이베리에 있는, 아래는 미닫이창이고 위는 여닫이창인 벽돌집 한 채를 보시라고 권하겠습니다. 저는 감히 그녀에게 드러내 놓고 마음을 고백할 수 없었습니다. 당시 엔스컴의 형편에서 제가 겪던 어려움은 굳이 설명할 필요도 없을 만큼 너무도 잘 알려져 있겠지요. 그래도 저는 다행히, 웨이머스에서 헤어지기 전에 뜻을 이루어, 이 세상에서 가장 올곧은 여인의 마음을 자비로이 굽혀 비밀 약혼에 응하게 할 수 있었습니다.—만약 그녀가 거절했더라면 저는 미쳐 버렸을 것입니다.—그러나 부인은 이렇게 물으시겠지요, 그래서 이런 짓을 하면서 무엇을 바랐느냐고?—무엇을 내다보았느냐고?—무엇이든, 그 모든 것을—시간을, 우연을, 형편을, 더딘 효험을, 갑작스러운 폭발을, 끈기와 권태를, 건강과 병환을 바랐습니다. 온갖 좋은 가능성이 제 앞에 있었고, 그녀에게서 변치 않는 마음과 서신 왕래의 약속을 얻어 냄으로써 가장 큰 축복부터 확보해 둔 셈이었습니다. 더 설명이 필요하시다면, 친애하는 부인, 저는 부인 남편의 아들이라는 영예를 지녔고, 또 좋은 일을 바라는 기질을 물려받는 이점을 누렸으니, 그 어떤 집이나 땅의 유산도 그 가치에 견줄 수 없는 것이지요.—그러니 이런 형편에 놓인 채, 랜덜스로 첫 방문을 오는 저를 그려 보십시오.—그리고 여기서 저는 잘못을 자각합니다. 그 방문은 더 일찍 했어야 마땅했으니까요. 돌이켜 보시면, 제가 미스 페어팩스가 하이베리에 와 있을 때까지 오지 않았음을 아시게 될 것입니다. 그리고 푸대접받은 분이 바로 부인이시니, 부인은 곧바로 저를 용서해 주시겠지요. 다만 저는 아버지의 동정에 호소해야 하니, 제가 그분 댁을 멀리하는 동안은 그만큼 부인을 알게 되는 축복을 잃고 있었음을 일깨워 드려야겠습니다. 부인 댁에서 보낸 그 더없이 행복했던 두 주 동안의 제 처신은, 부디 한 가지 점만 빼고는 비난받을 빌미를 주지 않았기를 바랍니다. 이제 저는 부인 댁에 속해 있던 동안의 제 행동 가운데, 정작 가장 중요한, 유일하게 중요한 부분에 이르렀습니다. 제 자신을 불안하게 하고 매우 정성스러운 해명을 요구하는 부분이지요. 더없는 존경과 더없이 따뜻한 우정을 담아 미스 우드하우스를 언급합니다. 어쩌면 아버지께서는, 거기에 더없는 부끄러움을 담아야 한다고 덧붙이고 싶어 하실지도 모르겠군요.—어제 아버지께서 흘리신 몇 마디 말씀이 그분의 생각을 드러냈고, 제가 얼마간의 책망을 받아 마땅하다는 것도 인정합니다.—미스 우드하우스를 향한 제 처신은, 아마도 마땅한 정도를 넘어선 무언가를 내비쳤을 것입니다.—제게 그토록 긴요했던 은폐를 돕기 위해, 저는 우리가 곧장 빠져든 그런 친밀함을 허용된 정도 이상으로 이용하는 데까지 이끌려 갔습니다.—미스 우드하우스가 제 표면상의 목표였음은 부인할 수 없습니다—그러나 부인은 이 고백을 믿어 주시리라 확신합니다, 만약 제가 그녀의 무관심을 확신하지 못했더라면, 그 어떤 이기적인 속셈에도 이끌려 그 일을 계속하지는 않았으리라는 것을요.—미스 우드하우스가 아무리 상냥하고 유쾌한 분이라 해도, 그녀는 제게 연정을 품을 법한 젊은 여성이라는 인상을 한 번도 준 적이 없습니다. 그리고 그녀가 저에게 연정을 품을 기색이 조금도 없다는 것은, 제 바람인 동시에 제 확신이기도 했습니다.—그녀는 제 친절을 편안하고 다정하며 너그러운 장난기로 받아 주었는데, 그것이 제게는 꼭 알맞았습니다. 우리는 서로를 이해하고 있는 듯했습니다. 우리 사이의 처지로 보면, 그런 친절은 그녀가 받아 마땅한 것이었고, 또 그렇게 받아들여졌습니다.—미스 우드하우스가 그 두 주가 끝나기 전에 정말로 저를 알아채기 시작했는지는, 저로서는 말할 수 없습니다.—제가 작별 인사를 하러 갔을 때, 저는 진실을 고백하기 직전까지 갔던 것이 기억나고, 그때 저는 그녀가 의심을 품지 않은 것은 아니라고 생각했습니다. 그러나 그 뒤로 그녀가 적어도 어느 정도는 저를 간파했음을 저는 의심치 않습니다.—전부를 짐작하지는 못했을지 몰라도, 그녀의 영민함이 일부는 꿰뚫어 보았을 것입니다. 그것을 저는 의심할 수 없습니다. 이 일이 지금의 제약에서 풀려나는 날이 오면, 이것이 그녀에게 전혀 뜻밖의 일은 아니었음을 부인도 아시게 될 것입니다. 그녀는 자주 그런 기미를 제게 내비쳤습니다. 무도회에서 그녀가 제게, 제가 미스 페어팩스에게 친절을 베풀어 준 엘턴 부인에게 고마워해야 마땅하다고 말하던 것이 기억납니다.—그녀를 향한 제 처신의 이 내력을, 부디 부인과 아버지께서 잘못 보셨던 부분에 대한 큰 참작 사유로 받아들여 주시기를 바랍니다. 두 분이 저를 엠마 우드하우스에게 죄를 지은 자로 여기시는 동안은, 저는 두 분 어느 쪽에게서도 아무것도 받을 자격이 없었으니까요. 이 점에서 저를 무죄로 놓아주시고, 또 허락되는 때가 오면, 제가 누이를 향한 듯한 애정으로 깊이 아끼는, 그래서 그녀도 저만큼이나 깊고 행복한 사랑에 빠지기를 간절히 바라는, 바로 그 엠마 우드하우스의 사면과 축복을 제게 얻어다 주십시오.—제가 그 두 주 동안 했던 그 어떤 이상한 말이나 행동에 대해서도, 이제 부인은 그 열쇠를 쥐고 계십니다. 제 마음은 하이베리에 있었고, 제 일이라곤 제 몸뚱이를 되도록 자주, 되도록 의심을 사지 않으면서 그곳으로 옮겨 가는 것이었습니다. 혹시 무슨 기이한 점들이 기억나시거든, 모두 제대로 된 사정에 비추어 헤아려 주십시오.—그토록 말이 많았던 그 피아노에 관해서는, 그것을 주문한 일을 미스 F—는 전혀 몰랐다는 것만 말씀드리면 충분하겠습니다. 만약 그녀에게 선택권이 주어졌더라면, 그녀는 결코 제가 그것을 보내도록 두지 않았을 것입니다.—이 약혼 기간 내내 그녀가 보여 준 마음의 섬세함은, 친애하는 부인, 제가 온당히 그려 내기에는 너무도 벅찬 것입니다. 머지않아, 간절히 바라건대, 부인께서도 그녀를 속속들이 아시게 될 것입니다.—어떤 묘사로도 그녀를 다 그려 낼 수 없습니다. 그녀가 어떤 사람인지는 그녀 스스로 부인께 말해 드려야겠지요—그렇다고 말로는 아닙니다. 자기 장점을 그토록 일부러 감추려 드는 사람은 일찍이 없었으니까요.—생각보다 길어지고 있는 이 편지를 쓰기 시작한 뒤로, 저는 그녀에게서 소식을 받았습니다.—그녀는 자기 건강이 좋다고 전하지만, 그녀는 결코 불평하는 법이 없으니, 저는 감히 그 말을 믿지 못하겠습니다. 그녀의 안색에 대한 부인의 의견을 듣고 싶습니다. 부인이 머지않아 그녀를 찾아가시리라는 것을 압니다. 그녀는 그 방문을 두려워하며 지내고 있습니다. 어쩌면 이미 다녀오셨는지도 모르겠군요. 지체 없이 소식 전해 주십시오. 저는 수천 가지 자세한 사정이 알고 싶어 안달이 나 있습니다. 제가 랜덜스에 머문 시간이 얼마나 짧았는지, 그리고 그때 제가 얼마나 얼떨떨하고 미친 상태였는지 떠올려 주십시오. 그리고 저는 아직도 별반 나아지지 않았으니, 여전히 행복 때문이든 비참함 때문이든 제정신이 아닙니다. 제가 받은 친절과 호의를, 그녀의 훌륭함과 인내를, 외삼촌의 너그러움을 생각하면 저는 기뻐서 미칠 지경입니다. 그러나 제가 그녀에게 안긴 온갖 불안을, 그리고 제가 용서받을 자격이 얼마나 없는지를 떠올리면, 저는 분해서 미칠 지경입니다. 그녀를 다시 한 번 볼 수만 있다면!—그러나 아직은 그것을 청해서는 안 됩니다. 외삼촌이 제게 너무 잘해 주셨으니, 더 졸라 댈 수는 없지요.—이 긴 편지에 그래도 더 보태야겠습니다. 부인이 들으셔야 할 이야기를 아직 다 듣지 못하셨으니까요. 어제는 어떤 조리 있는 자초지종도 들려드릴 수 없었습니다. 그러나 이 일이 터져 나온 그 갑작스러움, 그리고 어떤 면에서는 그 때아닌 갑작스러움은 설명이 필요합니다. 부인도 짐작하시겠지만, 지난달 26일의 일이 곧바로 제게 더없이 행복한 앞날을 열어 주기는 했어도, 저는 그토록 이른 조치를 감히 취하지는 않았을 것입니다. 다만 한시도 지체할 수 없게 만든 매우 특별한 사정이 아니었더라면 말이지요. 저 자신부터도 그토록 성급한 일이라면 움츠러들었을 것이고, 그녀로 말하자면 제 망설임 하나하나를 몇 곱절이나 더 강하고 더 섬세하게 느꼈을 것입니다.—그러나 제게는 달리 길이 없었습니다. 그녀가 그 여자와 서둘러 맺은 그 약속이—여기서, 친애하는 부인, 저는 마음을 가다듬고 추스르기 위해 갑작스레 펜을 놓아야 했습니다.—들판을 거닐다 왔고, 이제는, 바라건대, 이 편지의 나머지를 마땅한 모습으로 써 내려갈 만큼 제정신이 들었습니다.—사실 이것은 제게 더없이 부끄러운 회고입니다. 저는 수치스럽게 행동했습니다. 그리고 여기서 저는, 미스 W에게 보인 제 처신이 미스 F의 마음을 상하게 한 점에서 크게 책망받아 마땅했음을 인정합니다. 그녀는 그것을 못마땅해했고, 그것만으로도 충분했어야 마땅합니다.—진실을 감추기 위해서라는 제 변명을, 그녀는 충분하다고 여기지 않았습니다.—그녀는 언짢아했고, 저는 그것이 부당하다고 여겼습니다. 저는 수천 번이나 그녀가 쓸데없이 까다롭고 조심스럽다고 여겼고, 심지어 냉정하다고까지 여겼습니다. 그러나 그녀는 언제나 옳았습니다. 만약 제가 그녀의 판단을 따라, 그녀가 온당하다고 여긴 수준으로 제 기분을 가라앉혔더라면, 저는 제 평생 겪어 본 가장 큰 불행을 면했을 것입니다.—우리는 다투었습니다.—돈웰에서 보낸 그 아침을 기억하십니까?—바로 그곳에서, 그 전에 일어났던 자잘한 불만들이 죄다 한꺼번에 곪아 터졌습니다. 저는 늦었고, 혼자 집으로 걸어가는 그녀를 만나 함께 걷고 싶었지만, 그녀는 그것을 허락하지 않았습니다. 그녀는 단호히 거절했고, 그때 저는 그것을 더없이 부당하다고 여겼습니다. 그러나 지금은, 거기서 매우 자연스럽고 한결같은 분별의 태도밖에 보이지 않습니다. 저는 세상의 눈을 우리 약혼에서 돌려놓겠다고, 한 시간 동안 다른 여인에게 나무랄 만큼 유난스럽게 굴어 놓고서, 바로 다음 순간 그녀더러, 앞서 기울인 온갖 조심을 죄다 헛되게 만들지도 모를 제안에 응하라는 것이었으니 말입니다.—만약 우리가 돈웰과 하이베리 사이를 함께 걷다가 누군가에게 들켰더라면, 진실이 의심받을 수밖에 없었겠지요.—그래도 저는 노여워할 만큼 어리석었습니다.—저는 그녀의 애정을 의심했습니다. 이튿날 박스 힐에서는 더욱 의심했습니다. 그날 제 쪽에서 보인 그런 처신—그토록 수치스럽고 무례하게 그녀를 외면하고, 그토록 노골적으로 미스 W에게 정성을 쏟는, 분별 있는 여인이라면 도저히 견딜 수 없었을 그런 처신에 화가 난 그녀가, 제게 더없이 알아들을 만한 말의 형태로 자기 노여움을 드러냈으니까요.—요컨대, 친애하는 부인, 그것은 그녀 쪽에서는 나무랄 데 없고 제 쪽에서는 가증스러운 다툼이었습니다. 그리고 저는 그날 저녁 곧바로 리치먼드로 돌아갔습니다. 이튿날 아침까지 부인 댁에 머물 수도 있었는데, 오로지 그녀에게 되도록 많이 화를 내고 싶다는 그 한 가지 이유 때문에 말이지요. 그때조차도 저는, 머지않아 화해할 작정이 없을 만큼 바보는 아니었습니다. 다만 저는 상처 입은 사람이었고, 그녀의 냉정함에 상처 입었으니, 먼저 화해를 청해 와야 할 사람은 그녀라고 작정한 채 떠났던 것입니다.—부인이 박스 힐 일행에 끼지 않으셨던 것을 저는 늘 다행으로 여길 것입니다. 만약 부인이 그곳에서 제 처신을 목격하셨더라면, 다시는 저를 좋게 보지 않으셨으리라고 저는 거의 확신합니다. 그것이 그녀에게 미친 영향은, 그것이 곧바로 불러온 결심에서 드러납니다. 제가 정말로 랜덜스를 떠난 것을 알자마자, 그녀는 그 주제넘은 엘턴 부인의 제안을 받아들였습니다. 그건 그렇고, 그 여자가 그녀를 대해 온 그 모든 방식은 늘 제 마음을 분노와 증오로 가득 채웠습니다. 제게 그토록 넉넉히 베풀어진 그 너그러운 인내의 마음을 두고 제가 시비를 걸어서는 안 되겠지요. 그러나 그렇지만 않았더라면, 저는 그 여자가 그녀를 향해 보인 그 인내의 몫에 대해 목청껏 항의했을 것입니다.—'제인'이라니, 참!—보시다시피 저는 아직, 부인께조차도 그녀를 그 이름으로 부르는 일을 제 자신에게 허락하지 않았습니다. 그러니 생각해 보십시오, 그 이름이 엘턴 부부 사이에서, 쓸데없는 되풀이의 온갖 천박함과 제멋대로 우월하다 여기는 온갖 무례함을 띠고 함부로 오가는 것을 들어야 했을 때, 제가 무엇을 견뎌야 했을지를. 조금만 더 참아 주십시오, 곧 끝납니다.—그녀는 그 제안을 받아들이며, 저와 완전히 결별하기로 결심하고는, 이튿날 제게 편지를 써서 우리가 다시는 만나지 않을 것이라고 통고했습니다.—그녀는 이 약혼이 서로에게 후회와 비참함의 근원임을 느꼈으니, 그것을 깨뜨린다는 것이었습니다.—이 편지는 가엾은 제 외숙모가 돌아가시던 바로 그 아침에 제게 닿았습니다. 저는 한 시간 안에 답장을 썼습니다. 그러나 제 마음의 혼란과, 한꺼번에 제게 쏟아진 숱한 일들 탓에, 제 답장은 그날 부친 다른 숱한 편지들과 함께 보내지는 대신, 제 책상 서랍에 잠겨 버리고 말았습니다. 그리고 저는, 몇 줄에 지나지 않았어도 그녀를 만족시키기에 충분히 썼다고 믿고는, 아무런 불안 없이 지냈습니다.—그녀에게서 다시 곧바로 소식이 오지 않는 것이 다소 실망스럽기는 했지만, 저는 그녀를 위해 핑계를 만들어 주었고, 또 너무 바빴으며—덧붙여도 될까요?—제 앞날에 대한 전망이 너무 밝아서 트집을 잡을 기분이 아니었습니다.—우리는 윈저로 옮겨 갔습니다. 그리고 이틀 뒤 저는 그녀에게서 소포 하나를 받았는데, 제가 보낸 편지들이 죄다 되돌아온 것이었습니다!—그와 함께 우편으로 몇 줄이 왔는데, 자기가 마지막으로 보낸 편지에 손톱만큼의 답장도 받지 못해 몹시 놀랐다는 것이었습니다. 그리고 이런 문제에서의 침묵은 달리 해석될 여지가 없으니, 또 부차적인 모든 정리를 되도록 빨리 매듭짓는 것이 서로에게 똑같이 바람직할 터이니, 자기는 이제 안전한 인편으로 제 편지를 모두 돌려보내며, 만약 제가 자기 편지를 바로 손에 넣어 일주일 안에 하이베리로 보낼 수 없다면, 그 기간이 지난 뒤에는 다음 주소로 부쳐 달라고 했습니다. 요컨대, 브리스틀 근처 미스터 스몰리지 댁이라는 그 온전한 주소가 제 눈을 빤히 노려보고 있었습니다. 저는 그 이름을, 그 장소를 알고 있었고, 그 일의 자초지종을 죄다 알고 있었기에, 그녀가 무슨 일을 벌여 왔는지 단박에 알아차렸습니다. 그것은 제가 익히 알던 그녀의 단호한 성품과 완벽하게 들어맞는 일이었습니다. 그리고 앞선 편지에서 그런 계획에 대해 일절 비치지 않고 비밀을 지킨 것 또한, 그 노심초사하는 섬세함을 똑같이 잘 보여 주는 일이었습니다. 그녀로서는 저를 위협하는 것처럼 보이는 일을 죽어도 하기 싫었을 테니까요.—그 충격을 상상해 보십시오. 제 자신의 실수를 실제로 알아차리기 전까지, 제가 우편의 실수를 두고 얼마나 길길이 날뛰었을지 상상해 보십시오.—어찌해야 했을까요?—길은 단 하나뿐이었습니다.—외삼촌께 말씀드려야 했습니다. 그분의 승낙 없이는 다시는 제 말이 받아들여지기를 바랄 수 없었으니까요.—저는 말씀드렸습니다. 형편이 제게 유리했습니다. 얼마 전의 그 일이 그분의 자존심을 누그러뜨려 놓았고, 그래서 그분은 제가 예상했던 것보다 일찍, 완전히 화해하시고 응낙해 주셨습니다. 그리고 마침내, 가엾은 분이, 깊은 한숨과 함께, 제가 결혼 생활에서 당신이 누리셨던 만큼의 행복을 찾기를 바란다고 말씀하실 수 있었습니다.—저는 그것이 다른 종류의 행복이 되리라고 느꼈습니다.—제가 그분께 사정을 털어놓느라 겪어야 했던 고통을, 모든 것이 걸려 있던 동안의 그 마음 졸임을 두고 저를 가엾이 여기시겠습니까?—아니요, 제가 하이베리에 닿아, 제가 그녀를 얼마나 병들게 만들었는지를 볼 때까지는 저를 가엾이 여기지 마십시오. 제가 그녀의 핼쑥하고 병든 얼굴을 볼 때까지는 저를 가엾이 여기지 마십시오.—저는 그분들의 늦은 아침 식사 시간을 알고 있었기에, 그녀를 혼자 만날 좋은 기회가 분명히 있으리라 확신하는 그 시각에 하이베리에 닿았습니다.—저는 실망하지 않았습니다. 그리고 마침내 제 여정의 목적에서도 실망하지 않았습니다. 저는 매우 온당하고 매우 정당한 노여움을 한참이나 달래어 가라앉혀야 했습니다. 그러나 그것도 다 끝났습니다. 우리는 화해했고, 그 어느 때보다도 더, 훨씬 더 서로를 아끼게 되었으며, 이제 우리 사이에 한순간의 불안도 다시는 일어날 수 없을 것입니다. 이제, 친애하는 부인, 부인을 놓아드리겠습니다. 그러나 이 말씀을 드리기 전에는 끝맺을 수 없었습니다. 그동안 제게 베풀어 주신 그 모든 친절에 천 번 만 번 감사드리며, 부인의 마음이 그녀를 향해 베풀어 줄 그 보살핌에는 만 번 더 감사드립니다.—제가 받아 마땅한 것보다 더 행복해질 길에 들어섰다고 부인이 여기신다면, 저도 부인의 생각에 전적으로 동감입니다.—미스 W는 저를 행운의 자식이라 부릅니다. 그녀의 말이 옳기를 바랍니다.—한 가지 점에서만큼은 제 행운이 의심할 여지가 없으니, 그것은 곧 제 자신을 이렇게 적을 수 있다는 것입니다,\n\n부인의 은혜를 입은 다정한 아들,\n\nF. C. 웨스턴 처칠 올림.\n\n# 제3권 15장\n\n이 편지는 엠마의 마음을 파고들 수밖에 없었다. 그러지 않으리라 미리 다짐했건만, 그녀는 웨스턴 부인이 예언한 대로 그 편지를 온전히 정당하게 평가하지 않을 수 없었다. 자기 이름이 나오는 대목에 이르자 더는 견딜 수가 없었다. 자신에 관한 줄줄이가 흥미로웠고, 거의 모든 줄이 흐뭇했다. 그리고 그 매혹이 사그라든 뒤에도, 편지를 쓴 이를 향한 옛 호의가 절로 되살아나면서, 또 이 순간 사랑을 그린 어떤 그림이든 그녀에게 풍기지 않을 수 없는 강렬한 끌림으로, 화제는 여전히 흥을 잃지 않았다. 그녀는 끝까지 다 읽기 전에는 멈추지 못했다. 그가 잘못했다는 것을 느끼지 않을 수 없었으나, 그래도 그는 그녀가 짐작했던 것보다 덜 잘못했고 — 괴로워했으며, 몹시 후회하고 있었고 — 웨스턴 부인에게 그토록 고마워하고, 미스 페어팩스를 그토록 사랑했으며, 또 그녀 자신이 그토록 행복했으니, 매정하게 굴 도리가 없었다. 만약 그가 방으로 들어왔다면, 그녀는 여느 때처럼 진심으로 그와 악수를 나누었으리라.\n\n그녀는 편지를 어찌나 좋게 여겼던지, 미스터 나이틀리가 다시 찾아오자 그것을 읽어 보라고 권했다. 웨스턴 부인이 이 편지가 전해지기를, 특히 미스터 나이틀리처럼 그의 처신에서 그토록 흠잡을 데를 많이 본 사람에게 전해지기를 바라리라고 확신했다.\n\n\"기꺼이 훑어보지.\" 그가 말했다. \"그런데 길어 보이는군. 밤에 집으로 가져가겠네.\"\n\n그러나 그래서는 안 되었다. 저녁에 미스터 웨스턴이 들를 예정이었으므로, 그 편으로 돌려보내야 했다.\n\n\"자네와 이야기를 나누는 편이 더 좋네만,\" 그가 대답했다. \"정당하게 평가할 일이라니, 그렇게 하지.\"\n\n그가 읽기 시작했으나, 거의 곧바로 멈추고 말했다. \"몇 달 전에 이 신사가 새어머니에게 보낸 편지 가운데 하나를 보여 주겠다고 했더라면, 엠마, 이렇게 무덤덤하게 받아 들지는 않았을 텐데.\"\n\n그는 조금 더 나아가 혼자 읽다가, 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"흠! 제법 그럴듯한 인사로 시작하는군. 하지만 저게 저 사람 방식이지. 한 사람의 문체를 다른 사람의 잣대로 삼아서는 안 되겠지. 매정하게 굴지 않겠네.\"\n\n\"내가 읽으면서 의견을 소리 내어 말하는 게 자연스러울 것 같군.\" 그가 곧이어 덧붙였다. \"그렇게 하면 자네 곁에 있는 기분이 들 테니. 시간도 그리 많이 까먹지 않을 거고. 하지만 자네가 싫다면—\"\n\n\"천만에요. 그렇게 해 주셨으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n미스터 나이틀리는 한결 신이 나서 다시 읽기로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"유혹 운운하는 대목에서 시치미를 떼는군.\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 잘못한 걸 알면서도, 이치에 닿는 변명은 하나도 못 내놓고 있어. 못쓰겠어. 애초에 약혼을 하지 말았어야지. '아버지의 기질' 운운하지만, 제 아버지에게 부당한 소리야. 미스터 웨스턴의 낙천적인 기질은 그의 올곧고 떳떳한 노력 위에 내린 축복이었어. 미스터 웨스턴은 지금의 온갖 안락을 손에 넣기 전에 먼저 그것을 벌어들였단 말이지. 정말 옳은 말이야. 미스 페어팩스가 이곳에 오기 전에는 그도 오지 않았으니.\"\n\n\"잊지 않고 있어요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"당신이 그 사람더러 마음만 먹었으면 더 일찍 올 수 있었으리라고 얼마나 단언하셨는지를요. 그 대목을 참 너그럽게 넘기시네요. 하지만 당신 말이 완전히 옳았어요.\"\n\n\"내 판단이 아주 공정했던 건 아니야, 엠마. 그래도 말이지 — 자네가 그 일에 얽혀 있지 않았더라도 — 나는 여전히 그를 미덥잖게 여겼을 것 같네.\"\n\n미스 우드하우스에 관한 대목에 이르자, 그는 그것 전부를 소리 내어 읽지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀에 관한 모든 구절을, 미소를 머금고, 눈짓을 보내고, 고개를 가로젓고, 동의나 못마땅함의, 혹은 단지 애정의 말 한두 마디를 내용에 맞춰 곁들이며 읽었다. 그러나 끝에 가서는 진지하게, 차분히 생각을 가다듬은 끝에 이렇게 맺었다.\n\n\"아주 못됐어. 그래도 더 나쁠 수도 있었지만. 더없이 위험한 노름을 한 셈이야. 무사히 빠져나온 걸 두고 너무 많이 결과 덕만 보고 있어. 자네를 기준 삼아 제 처신을 가늠할 줄도 모르고. 사실 늘 제 욕심에 속아, 제 편의 말고는 자잘한 것엔 도통 마음을 쓰지 않았던 거야. 자네가 제 비밀을 꿰뚫어 봤다고 멋대로 짐작하다니. 그럴 만도 하지! 제 마음이 음모로 가득 차 있으니, 남도 그러려니 의심하는 거야. 비밀이니, 술수니 — 그런 것들이 사람의 분별을 얼마나 비뚤어지게 하는지! 나의 엠마, 무엇이든 다, 우리가 서로를 대할 때 진실과 진심이 얼마나 아름다운지를 점점 더 또렷이 증명해 주는 게 아닐까?\"\n\n엠마는 그 말에 동의했다. 다만 해리엇을 떠올리며 마음이 찔려 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 그 까닭을 솔직하게 설명할 수는 없었다.\n\n\"계속 읽으시는 게 좋겠어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그가 그렇게 했으나, 이내 다시 멈추고 말했다. \"피아노포르테라! 아! 이건 아주, 아주 젊은 남자나 할 짓이야. 그 불편함이 기쁨을 한참 넘어서지는 않을지 헤아리기엔 너무 어린 거지. 정말이지 애송이 같은 꿍꿍이야! 여자가 차라리 사양하고 싶어 한다는 걸 뻔히 알면서도, 굳이 애정의 증표를 건네려 드는 남자의 심사를 나는 이해할 수가 없어. 게다가 그녀가 막을 수만 있었다면 그 악기가 오지 못하게 했으리라는 걸 그도 알고 있었으니.\"\n\n그 뒤로는 한동안 멈추지 않고 죽 나아갔다. 프랭크 처칠이 부끄럽게 처신했노라 고백하는 대목에 이르러서야, 처음으로 지나가는 한마디 이상이 필요해졌다.\n\n\"전적으로 동감일세, 선생.\" 그 대목에서 그가 한 말이었다. \"자네는 정말 부끄럽게 처신했어. 자네가 쓴 줄 가운데 이보다 더 참된 줄은 없네.\" 그리고 곧이어 나오는, 두 사람의 의견이 갈린 근원과, 그가 제인 페어팩스의 옳고 그름의 감각에 정면으로 어긋나게 행동하기를 고집한 대목을 다 읽고 나서, 그는 더 길게 멈추고 말했다. \"이건 아주 못됐어. 그는 그녀를 부추겨 자기 때문에 극도로 곤란하고 거북한 처지에 빠지게 해 놓고, 그녀가 쓸데없이 괴로워하지 않도록 막는 일을 무엇보다 먼저 챙겼어야 했어. 그 편지 왕래를 이어 가는 일에서, 그녀는 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 어려움과 싸워야 했을 거야. 설령 이치에 닿지 않는 꺼림칙함이라 해도 그는 마땅히 존중했어야 하건만 — 그녀의 꺼림칙함은 모조리 이치에 닿는 것이었어. 우리는 그녀의 단 하나의 잘못을 헤아려, 그녀가 그 약혼에 동의한 것이 그릇된 일이었음을 떠올리면서, 그녀가 이런 벌받는 처지에 놓였던 것을 견뎌 내야 하네.\"\n\n엠마는 그가 이제 박스 힐 모임 대목에 다다르고 있음을 알고 마음이 편치 않아졌다. 자기 처신이 어찌나 온당치 못했던가! 그녀는 깊이 부끄러웠고, 다음에 그가 지을 표정이 조금 두려웠다. 그러나 그는 모두 차분히, 주의 깊게, 사소한 말 한마디 없이 읽어 내려갔다. 그리고 행여 괴로움을 줄까 봐 곧바로 거두어 버린, 그녀를 향한 한순간의 눈길 말고는 — 박스 힐에 관한 기억은 아예 존재하지 않는 듯했다.\n\n\"우리의 어진 벗 엘턴 부부의 섬세함에 대해서는 별로 좋게 말할 게 없군.\" 그가 다음으로 한 말이었다. \"그의 심정은 자연스러워. 뭐라고! 정말로 그와 완전히 갈라서기로 결심했다니! 그녀는 그 약혼이 둘 모두에게 후회와 불행의 근원임을 느끼고 — 그것을 깨뜨렸어. 이게 그의 처신에 대한 그녀의 판단을 얼마나 잘 보여 주는지! 음, 이 남자는 참으로 유별난—\"\n\n\"아니, 아니에요, 계속 읽으세요. 그가 얼마나 괴로워하는지 보시게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"그러기를 바라네.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 냉랭하게 대답하고는 편지로 돌아갔다. \"'스몰리지!' 이게 무슨 뜻이지? 이게 다 무슨 소리야?\"\n\n\"그 사람은 스몰리지 부인네 아이들의 가정교사로 가기로 약속했었거든요. 엘턴 부인의 절친한 벗이자, 메이플 그로브의 이웃이래요. 그런데 말이 나온 김에, 엘턴 부인은 이 낭패를 어떻게 견디고 있을지 궁금하네요.\"\n\n\"아무 말도 말게, 나의 엠마. 자네가 나더러 읽으라고 시켜 놓고는. 엘턴 부인 이야기조차도. 이제 딱 한 장 남았어. 곧 끝나네. 이 사람, 편지 한번 별나게 쓰는군!\"\n\n\"그 사람을 좀 더 너그러운 마음으로 읽어 주셨으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"음, 여기엔 진심이 담겨 있긴 하군. 그녀가 아픈 걸 보고 정말로 괴로워한 모양이야. 분명 그가 그녀를 아낀다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없어. '그 어느 때보다, 훨씬 더 소중한.' 이런 화해의 가치를 그가 오래도록 모두 느끼며 살기를 바라네. 수천, 수만이라는 말까지 써 가며 어지간히 후하게 감사를 늘어놓는군. '내가 받을 자격 이상으로 행복하다.' 자, 그 점에서는 제 분수를 아는 셈이야. '미스 우드하우스는 나를 행운의 자식이라 부른다.' 미스 우드하우스가 한 말이라고, 그래? 그리고 멋들어진 마무리고 — 자, 이게 편지일세. 행운의 자식이라! 자네가 그에게 붙여 준 별명이었나, 그래?\"\n\n\"제가 그러는 만큼 당신은 이 편지가 그리 마음에 들지 않으신 모양이지만, 그래도 적어도, 적어도 이 편지를 보고 그를 좀 더 좋게 여기시게 됐기를 바라요. 이 편지가 당신에게 그를 위해 조금이라도 도움이 됐기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"그래, 분명 도움이 됐어. 그는 큰 잘못을 저질렀어. 무분별하고 경솔한 잘못 말이야. 그리고 그가 받을 자격 이상으로 행복하게 살 것 같다는 데는 나도 그의 의견에 크게 동감하네. 하지만 그래도, 그가 의심할 바 없이 미스 페어팩스에게 진정으로 마음을 두고 있고, 머지않아 — 그러기를 바라네만 — 늘 그녀와 함께 지내는 이점을 누리게 될 테니, 그의 성품이 나아져, 자기 성품에 모자란 한결같음과 섬세한 원칙을 그녀의 성품에서 얻게 되리라고 나는 기꺼이 믿겠네. 자, 이제 다른 이야기를 좀 하세. 지금 내 마음엔 다른 사람의 일이 너무도 깊이 걸려 있어서, 더는 프랭크 처칠을 생각할 수가 없네. 오늘 아침 자네 곁을 떠난 뒤로, 엠마, 내 마음은 한 가지 일을 두고 고되게 매달려 왔다네.\"\n\n이어 그 이야기가 나왔다. 그것은 미스터 나이틀리가 사랑하는 여인에게조차 쓰던, 꾸밈없고 담박하며 신사다운 말투로 전해졌다. 어떻게 하면 그녀의 아버지의 행복을 해치지 않고 청혼할 수 있을까 하는 것이었다. 엠마의 대답은 첫마디부터 준비되어 있었다. \"사랑하는 아버지가 살아 계시는 동안에는, 그녀에게 어떤 처지의 변화도 있을 수 없다. 그녀는 결코 아버지 곁을 떠날 수 없다.\" 그러나 이 대답 가운데 일부만이 받아들여졌다. 그녀가 아버지 곁을 떠날 수 없다는 점은, 미스터 나이틀리도 그녀만큼이나 절감했다. 그러나 그 밖의 어떤 변화도 있을 수 없다는 점에는 동의할 수 없었다. 그는 그 일을 더없이 깊이, 더없이 골똘히 곱씹어 온 터였다. 처음에는 미스터 우드하우스를 설득해 그녀와 함께 돈웰로 옮겨 가게 하기를 바랐다. 그것이 될 법한 일이라 믿고 싶었으나, 미스터 우드하우스를 아는 그로서는 오래 자신을 속일 수가 없었다. 그래서 이제 그는 솔직히 털어놓았다. 그런 이주는 그녀 아버지의 안락을, 어쩌면 목숨까지도 위태롭게 할 노릇이니, 결코 무릅써서는 안 된다는 것이 자신의 생각이라고. 미스터 우드하우스를 하트필드에서 떼어 내다니! 아니, 그것은 시도해서도 안 될 일이라고 그는 느꼈다. 그러나 이것을 단념하고 떠오른 계획이라면, 그의 가장 사랑하는 엠마도 어느 모로 보나 마다하지 않으리라 그는 믿었다. 그것은 곧, 자신이 하트필드로 들어와 사는 것이었다. 그녀 아버지의 행복이 — 바꿔 말하면 그의 목숨이 — 하트필드를 그녀의 집으로 계속 유지하기를 요구하는 한, 그곳을 자신의 집으로도 삼겠다는 것이었다.\n\n그들 모두가 돈웰로 옮겨 가는 일이라면, 엠마도 이미 잠깐 생각해 본 적이 있었다. 그처럼 그녀도 그 계획을 따져 보고는 물려 두었던 터였다. 그러나 이런 대안만은 떠올려 본 적이 없었다. 그녀는 거기 담긴 온갖 애정을 잘 알았다. 돈웰을 떠난다면 그가 시간과 습관의 독립을 크게 희생해야 하리라는 것을, 제 집이 없이 그녀의 아버지와 늘 함께 산다면 견뎌야 할 일이 많고도 많으리라는 것을 그녀는 느꼈다. 그녀는 생각해 보겠노라 약속했고, 그에게도 더 생각해 보라고 권했다. 그러나 그는 어떤 숙고로도 이 일에 대한 자신의 바람이나 의견을 바꿀 수 없으리라 굳게 확신하고 있었다. 그는 이미 아주 오래, 아주 차분히 헤아려 본 일이라고 그녀에게 장담했다. 혼자 생각에 잠기려고 아침 내내 윌리엄 라킨스에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 걸었노라고.\n\n\"아! 미처 손쓰지 못한 난관이 하나 있어요.\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"윌리엄 라킨스가 분명 그걸 못마땅해할 거예요. 제 승낙을 청하기 전에 먼저 그의 동의부터 얻으셔야겠는데요.\"\n\n그래도 그녀는 생각해 보겠노라 약속했다. 게다가 그것을 아주 좋은 계획이라 여길 작정으로 생각해 보겠노라고, 거의 약속하다시피 했다.\n\n주목할 만한 점은, 엠마가 이제 돈웰 애비를 여러, 아주 여러 관점에서 두루 헤아리기 시작하면서도, 한때 그토록 끈질기게 챙겼던 상속 예정자로서의 권리, 그러니까 조카 헨리에게 손해가 미친다는 생각만은 단 한 번도 떠오르지 않았다는 것이다. 그 가엾은 어린것에게 닥칠지 모를 차이를 그녀는 마땅히 떠올려야 했건만, 그저 그 일을 두고 짐짓 시침 떼는 듯 의미심장한 미소를 지었을 뿐이다. 그러고는 미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스든 그 누구든 결혼하는 것을 자신이 그토록 격렬하게 싫어했던, 그러나 그 무렵에는 언니로서, 이모로서의 애틋한 염려 탓으로만 돌렸던, 그 진짜 원인을 알아채고는 재미있어했다.\n\n그의 이 청혼, 결혼해서 하트필드에 눌러 살자는 이 계획은 — 곱씹으면 곱씹을수록 더욱 흐뭇해졌다. 그의 불리한 점은 줄어드는 듯했고, 그녀 자신의 이점은 늘어나는 듯했으며, 둘이 함께 누릴 좋음이 온갖 흠을 압도하는 듯했다. 앞으로 닥쳐올 근심스럽고 적막한 나날에 곁에 둘 그런 동무라니! 세월이 흐를수록 우울함만 더해 갈 그 모든 의무와 근심을 함께 나눌 그런 반려라니!\n\n가엾은 해리엇만 아니었다면 그녀는 더없이 행복했으리라. 그러나 그녀 자신의 온갖 축복은 하나같이 벗의 괴로움을 얽어매고 키우는 듯했으니, 이제 그 벗은 하트필드에서마저 멀어져야 할 판이었다. 엠마가 제 몫으로 마련해 두는 그 즐거운 가족 모임에서, 가엾은 해리엇은 그저 자비로운 조심성에서라도 멀찍이 떼어 놓아야 했다. 그녀는 어느 모로 보나 잃기만 하는 쪽이었다. 엠마는 앞으로 해리엇이 빠지는 것을 제 즐거움이 줄어드는 일로 한탄할 수는 없었다. 그런 모임에서 해리엇은 도움이 되기는커녕 차라리 짐스러운 존재일 터였다. 그러나 그 가엾은 처녀 본인에게는, 받아 마땅치 않은 벌받는 처지로 그녀를 몰아넣는, 유달리 가혹한 운명인 듯했다.\n\n물론 시간이 흐르면 미스터 나이틀리도 잊힐 것이다. 다시 말해 다른 누군가로 자리바꿈할 것이다. 그러나 그 일이 그리 빨리 일어나리라고는 기대할 수 없었다. 미스터 나이틀리 본인은 그 치유를 돕는 일이라고는 아무것도 하지 않을 테니 — 미스터 엘턴과는 다른 셈이었다. 미스터 나이틀리는 늘 그토록 다정하고, 그토록 정이 깊으며, 모두에게 그토록 진심으로 사려 깊은 사람이라, 지금보다 덜 흠모받아 마땅할 까닭이 결코 없을 터였다. 게다가 해리엇이 한 해에 세 남자 넘게 사랑에 빠질 수 있으리라 기대하는 것은, 아무리 해리엇이라 해도 정말이지 지나친 바람이었다.\n\n# 제3권 16장\n\n해리엇이 자신만큼이나 마주치는 일을 피하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알게 된 것은 엠마에게 더없는 위안이었다. 편지를 주고받는 것만도 충분히 고통스러웠다. 하물며 얼굴을 맞대야 했다면 얼마나 더 고약했겠는가!\n\n해리엇은 충분히 짐작이 가는 방식으로 자기 마음을 드러냈으니, 책망도 없었고 푸대접받았다는 기색도 겉으로 드러내지 않았다. 그러나 엠마는 그 글투에 어딘가 원망 비슷한 것, 원망에 가까운 무언가가 서려 있다고 느꼈고, 그것은 두 사람이 떨어져 지내야 할 까닭을 더욱 키워 주었다. 어쩌면 그저 그녀 자신의 양심 탓이었는지도 모른다. 그러나 이런 타격을 받고서도 조금도 원망하지 않을 수 있는 것은 천사뿐이리라 싶었다.\n\n이저벨라의 초대를 받아 내는 데에는 아무런 어려움이 없었고, 게다가 꾸며 낼 필요도 없이 충분한 명분이 있다는 점이 다행이었다. 이가 하나 좋지 않았던 것이다. 해리엇은 진작부터 치과 의사에게 보이고 싶어 했고 그 마음은 한동안 이어진 것이었다. 미시즈 존 나이틀리는 도움이 될 수 있다는 데 기뻐했다. 그녀에게는 누군가의 건강이 좋지 않다는 것이야말로 더없이 반가운 명분이었으니 — 치과 의사를 미스터 윙필드만큼 좋아하는 것은 아니었으나, 해리엇을 자기 보살핌 아래 두고 싶어 안달이었다. 언니 쪽에서 그렇게 정해지자, 엠마는 벗에게 이 일을 제안했고 해리엇이 아주 쉽게 마음을 돌린다는 것을 알았다. 해리엇은 가기로 했다. 적어도 보름 동안 초대받았고, 미스터 우드하우스의 마차로 데려다주기로 했다. 모든 것이 정해지고 모든 것이 마무리되어, 해리엇은 브런즈윅 스퀘어에 무사히 자리를 잡았다.\n\n이제 엠마는 정말로 미스터 나이틀리의 방문을 즐길 수 있었다. 이제 진정한 행복 속에서 이야기하고 귀 기울일 수 있었으니, 가까이에 실망한 마음 하나가 있다는 것을, 자기 자신이 길을 잘못 들게 한 그 감정 때문에 바로 그 순간, 멀지 않은 곳에서 누군가가 얼마나 많은 것을 견디고 있을지를 떠올릴 때마다 그녀를 따라다니던 그 부당함과 죄책감, 그 무엇보다 고통스러운 무언가에 더는 가로막히지 않았다.\n\n해리엇이 고더드 부인 댁에 있는 것과 런던에 있는 것이, 어쩌면 엠마의 감정에 분에 넘치는 차이를 만들어 냈는지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀는 런던에 있는 해리엇을 떠올릴 때면, 지난 일을 잊게 해 주고 자기 자신에게서 빠져나오게 해 줄 만한 호기심거리며 소일거리에 둘러싸인 모습을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n해리엇이 차지하고 있던 마음속 그 자리를 다른 어떤 근심이 곧바로 메우도록 내버려 두지 않을 작정이었다. 그녀 앞에는 알려야 할 일이 하나 있었으니, 오직 그녀만이 해낼 수 있는 일 — 아버지께 자신의 약혼을 고백하는 일이었다. 그러나 지금으로서는 그 일에 손대고 싶지 않았다. 그녀는 웨스턴 부인이 무사하고 평안해질 때까지 그 고백을 미루기로 마음먹었다. 이 시기에 자기가 사랑하는 사람들 가운데 또 하나의 동요를 던져 넣어서는 안 되었다. 그리고 정해진 때가 오기도 전에, 미리 앞당겨 자기 자신을 그 괴로움이 좀먹게 두어서도 안 되었다. 더 뜨겁지만 더 마음을 흔드는 온갖 기쁨에 화관을 씌워 줄, 적어도 보름간의 여유와 마음의 평화는 마땅히 그녀의 몫이어야 했다.\n\n그녀는 곧, 의무이자 즐거움으로서 똑같이, 이 마음의 휴일 가운데 반 시간을 미스 페어팩스를 찾아가는 데 쓰기로 마음먹었다. 가야 마땅했고 — 또 그녀가 몹시 보고 싶었다. 두 사람의 지금 처지가 닮았다는 것이 다른 온갖 선의의 동기를 더욱 부추겼다. 그것은 남몰래 누리는 만족이리라. 그러나 앞날이 비슷하다는 의식은, 제인이 무슨 이야기를 들려주든 거기에 기울일 관심을 한층 더 깊게 해 줄 것이 분명했다.\n\n그녀는 갔다 — 한 번은 마차로 그 집 문 앞까지 갔으나 헛걸음을 했고, 박스 힐 다녀온 다음 날 아침 이후로는 그 집에 들어가 본 적이 없었다. 그날 가엾은 제인은 엠마의 마음을 연민으로 가득 채울 만큼 큰 고통에 빠져 있었으니, 정작 그 고통의 가장 깊은 속내는 짐작조차 못 했지만. 여전히 환영받지 못할까 봐 두려운 마음에, 두 사람이 집에 있다는 것을 확인하고도 통로에서 기다리며 이름을 올려 보내기로 했다. 패티가 그녀의 이름을 알리는 소리가 들렸다. 그러나 전에 가엾은 미스 베이츠가 그토록 흐뭇하게 알아들을 만하게 일으켰던 그런 부산함은 뒤따르지 않았다. 그렇다. 들려온 것이라곤 \"이리 올라오시라고 해라\" 하는 즉각적인 대답뿐이었다 — 그리고 잠시 뒤, 계단에서 제인이 직접 그녀를 맞았으니, 다른 어떤 방식으로 맞이해도 충분치 않다는 듯 다급히 앞으로 나오는 것이었다. 엠마는 그녀가 그토록 좋아 보이고, 그토록 사랑스럽고, 그토록 매력적인 모습을 본 적이 없었다. 거기에는 의식하는 빛과 생기와 따스함이 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴이나 태도에 일찍이 모자랐을 법한 것이 이제는 무엇 하나 모자라지 않았다. 그녀는 손을 내밀며 다가와, 낮지만 더없이 진심 어린 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"정말 너무나 친절하세요! 미스 우드하우스, 저로서는 도저히 표현할 수가 없어요 — 부디 믿어 주시기를 — 이렇게 아무 말도 못 하는 저를 용서해 주세요.\"\n\n엠마는 흐뭇했고, 곧 말이 모자라지 않다는 것을 보여 주었을 터였다. 거실에서 흘러나오는 엘턴 부인의 목소리가 그녀를 멈춰 세우지만 않았던들. 그 바람에 다정함과 축하의 온갖 감정을, 그저 더없이 진심을 담아 손을 꼭 쥐는 것 하나로 압축하는 편이 상책이 되었다.\n\n베이츠 부인과 엘턴 부인이 함께 있었다. 미스 베이츠는 외출 중이었으니, 그것이 앞서의 고요함을 설명해 주었다. 엠마는 엘턴 부인이 다른 데 있었으면 싶었지만, 누구에게든 너그러울 만한 기분이었다. 그리고 엘턴 부인이 평소답지 않게 상냥하게 맞아 주었으므로, 이 마주침이 두 사람에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않기를 바랐다.\n\n엠마는 곧 자신이 엘턴 부인의 속내를 꿰뚫어 보았다고, 그녀가 자기와 마찬가지로 들떠 있는 까닭을 알아챘다고 믿었다. 그것은 미스 페어팩스의 비밀을 함께 나누고 있다는 것, 그리고 다른 사람들에게는 아직 비밀인 그 일을 자기는 알고 있다고 여기는 것이었다. 엠마는 그녀의 표정에서 그 징후를 곧바로 읽어 냈다. 그리고 베이츠 부인에게 인사치레를 건네며 그 마음씨 고운 노부인의 대답에 귀 기울이는 척하는 동안, 그녀가 안절부절못하며 비밀을 과시하듯, 미스 페어팩스에게 소리 내어 읽어 주던 듯한 편지 한 통을 접어 옆에 놓인 자줏빛에 금장식이 된 손가방에 도로 넣는 것을, 그러면서 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕이며 이렇게 말하는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"나머지는 다음에 마저 보면 되죠. 알잖아요. 당신하고 나하고는 기회가 없을 리 없으니까요. 게다가 사실 요긴한 건 벌써 다 들으셨고요. 나는 그저 미시즈 에스가 우리 사과를 받아들였고 화나지 않았다는 걸 보여 드리고 싶었을 뿐이에요. 글솜씨가 어찌나 사랑스러운지 보세요. 아! 정말 다정한 분이에요! 당신이 갔더라면 푹 빠지셨을 텐데요. — 그래도 더는 한마디도 마요. 우리 신중하게 굴어요 — 아주 얌전하게 처신하자고요. — 쉿! — 그 시구 기억하시죠 — 무슨 시였는지 지금은 잊었네요.\n\n이는 한 숙녀가 얽힌 일이니,\n다른 모든 것이 그 앞에 물러섬을 그대도 아시리라.\n\n그러니 내가 말하건대, 자기, 우리 경우엔 '숙녀'라는 자리에 — 쉿! 현명한 분께는 한마디면 족하죠. — 제 기분이 아주 좋아 보이죠, 안 그래요? 하지만 미시즈 에스 일로는 당신 마음을 놓이게 해 드리고 싶어요. 보다시피 내가 잘 둘러댄 덕에 그분 마음이 아주 누그러졌으니까요.\"\n\n그리고 엠마가 그저 고개를 돌려 베이츠 부인의 뜨개질을 바라보았을 뿐인데도, 그녀는 반쯤 속삭이듯 덧붙였다.\n\n\"보세요, 나는 이름은 하나도 입에 올리지 않았어요. — 오! 그럼요. 국무대신만큼이나 신중하게요. 내가 정말 솜씨 좋게 처리했죠.\"\n\n엠마는 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그것은 빤히 드러나는 과시였고, 기회만 닿으면 거듭되었다. 다 함께 날씨며 웨스턴 부인 이야기로 한동안 화기애애하게 떠든 끝에, 엠마는 느닷없이 이런 말을 듣게 되었다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스, 우리 여기 이 깜찍하고 당돌한 친구가 어쩜 이렇게 곱게 회복했는지 그렇게 생각지 않으세요? — 이 사람을 낫게 한 것이 미스터 페리의 더없는 공이라고 생각지 않으세요? — (여기서 제인을 향해 의미심장한 곁눈질을 보냈다.) 정말이지, 페리가 놀랄 만큼 짧은 시일에 이 사람을 회복시켰지 뭐예요! — 아! 가장 안 좋았을 때 내가 본 것처럼 당신도 보셨더라면!\" — 그리고 베이츠 부인이 엠마에게 뭔가 말하고 있을 때, 그녀는 더 낮게 속삭였다. \"페리가 받았을지 모를 어떤 도움에 대해선 우리 한마디도 안 해요. 윈저에서 온 어떤 젊은 의사 양반에 대해선 한마디도요. — 오! 그럼요. 공은 죄다 페리 몫이어야죠.\"\n\n\"미스 우드하우스, 박스 힐 모임 이후로 통 뵐 기회가 없었네요.\" 그녀가 잠시 뒤 입을 열었다. \"아주 즐거운 모임이었죠. 그래도 뭔가 모자랐던 것 같아요. 어쩐지 — 그러니까, 몇몇 분들 흥에 자그마한 먹구름이 드리워진 것 같았다고나 할까요. — 적어도 내 눈엔 그래 보였어요. 내가 잘못 봤을 수도 있지만요. 어쨌든 그래도 한 번 더 가 보고 싶어질 만큼은 됐던 것 같아요. 두 분 다 어때요, 그때 그 일행을 다시 모아서, 날씨 좋을 때 박스 힐을 또 한 번 탐방해 보는 거요? — 똑같은 일행이어야 해요, 알잖아요, 똑같은 일행으로, 한 사람도 빠짐없이요.\"\n\n그 직후 미스 베이츠가 들어왔고, 엠마는 그녀가 자기에게 건넨 첫마디의 어수선함에 절로 웃음이 나는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다. 무슨 말을 해도 될지 몰라 망설이면서도, 모든 것을 다 말하고 싶어 안달이 난 데서 비롯된 것이리라 짐작했다.\n\n\"고마워요, 미스 우드하우스, 정말 친절도 하시지. — 뭐라 말할 수가 없네요 — 네, 정말, 잘 알지요 — 사랑하는 제인의 앞날이 — 그러니까, 그런 뜻이 아니라. — 하지만 얘는 곱게 회복했답니다. — 미스터 우드하우스는 안녕하시고요? — 정말 기뻐요. — 도무지 제 힘으로는. — 보시다시피 여기 이렇게 다복한 작은 모임이라니까요. — 네, 정말요. — 매력적인 청년 — 그러니까 — 아주아주 다정한 분이지요. 미스터 페리, 그 착한 분 말이에요! — 제인을 어찌나 살뜰히 보살피는지!\" — 그리고 엘턴 부인이 와 있다는 것에 대한 그 대단한, 여느 때보다도 더한 감사의 기쁨으로 미루어, 엠마는 목사관 쪽에서 제인을 향한 원망이 자그맣게 비쳤던 적이 있었고 이제 그것이 너그러이 풀린 것이로구나 짐작했다. 사실 두어 마디 속삭임이 오가면서 그것은 짐작을 넘어선 사실이 되었으니, 엘턴 부인이 목소리를 높여 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 나 여기 있어요, 다정한 벗이여. 그리고 여기 어찌나 오래 있었는지, 다른 데였다면 사과를 해야 마땅하다 싶을 정도예요. 하지만 실은 내 군주님이자 주인님을 기다리는 중이랍니다. 여기서 나와 만나서 두 분께 인사를 드리겠다고 약속했거든요.\"\n\n\"어머! 우리가 미스터 엘턴의 방문을 받는 기쁨을 누리게 된다고요? — 그거야말로 더없는 영광이죠! 신사분들은 오전 방문을 좋아하지 않는 데다, 미스터 엘턴은 그렇게나 바쁘신 분이니까요.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇답니다, 미스 베이츠. — 그이는 정말이지 아침부터 밤까지 바빠요. — 무슨 핑계든 대고 사람들이 끝없이 찾아오거든요. — 치안판사며 구빈 감독관이며 교구 위원들이 늘 그이 의견을 듣고 싶어 해요. 그이 없이는 아무 일도 못 하는 모양이에요. — '정말이지, 미스터 이,' 하고 내가 곧잘 그러죠, '나라면 못 해요, 차라리 당신이 하세요. — 나한테 그 절반만큼이라도 청하는 사람들이 몰려들면 내 크레용이며 악기는 어찌 될지 모르겠어요.' — 지금도 형편없는 게, 둘 다 용서 못 할 만큼 내팽개치고 있다니까요. — 이 보름 동안 한 마디도 연주를 못 한 것 같아요. — 하지만 그이가 오긴 와요, 정말이에요. 그럼요, 여러분 모두에게 인사하려고 일부러요.\" 그러고는 엠마에게 들리지 않게 손을 들어 입을 가리며 — \"축하 방문이죠, 알다시피. — 오! 그럼요, 절대 빠질 수 없는 일이고말고요.\"\n\n미스 베이츠는 어찌나 흐뭇한 얼굴로 둘러보던지 — !\n\n\"나이틀리한테서 풀려나는 대로 곧장 나한테 오겠다고 약속했어요. 그런데 그이하고 나이틀리가 둘이 틀어박혀 깊이 의논을 하고 있다지 뭐예요. — 미스터 이가 나이틀리의 오른팔이거든요.\"\n\n엠마는 죽어도 웃지 않으려 애쓰며 그저 이렇게만 말했다. \"미스터 엘턴이 돈웰까지 걸어가셨나요? — 더운 길을 걸으셨겠네요.\"\n\n\"오! 아니에요, 크라운에서 하는 모임, 정기 모임이에요. 웨스턴하고 콜도 거기 있을 거예요. 하지만 사람들이란 으레 앞장서는 이들 얘기만 하게 마련이잖아요. — 내 짐작엔 미스터 이하고 나이틀리가 모든 걸 제 뜻대로 주무를걸요.\"\n\n\"날을 잘못 아신 게 아닐까요?\" 엠마가 말했다. \"크라운에서 하는 모임은 내일이라고 거의 확신하는데요. — 미스터 나이틀리가 어제 하트필드에 오셔서 토요일이라고 말씀하셨거든요.\"\n\n\"오! 아니에요, 모임은 분명 오늘이에요.\" 엘턴 부인 쪽에서는 어떤 착오도 있을 수 없다는 듯한 무뚝뚝한 대답이 돌아왔다. — \"내가 보기엔,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"이 교구야말로 여태껏 본 중에 제일 성가신 교구 같아요. 메이플 그로브에선 이런 일은 들어 본 적도 없는데 말이죠.\"\n\n\"거기 교구는 작았으니까요.\" 제인이 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지, 자기, 그건 나도 모르겠어요. 그 얘긴 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없거든요.\"\n\n\"하지만 학교가 작았다는 걸 보면 알 수 있죠. 당신이 직접 말씀하셨잖아요, 당신 언니하고 미시즈 브래그가 후원하는 학교라고요. 학교는 그거 하나뿐이고 아이가 스물다섯도 안 됐다고요.\"\n\n\"아! 영리한 사람 같으니, 그거 정말 맞는 말이에요. 어쩜 그렇게 생각이 깊은 머리를 가졌담! 제인, 내가 늘 하는 말이지만, 당신하고 나를 한데 섞어서 흔들어 놓을 수만 있다면 우리가 얼마나 완벽한 성품을 이룰까요. 내 발랄함하고 당신 진중함이면 완벽이 나올 텐데. — 그렇다고 어떤 사람들이 이미 당신을 완벽하다고 여기지 않을 거라고 넌지시 비추려는 건 아니지만요. — 하지만 쉿! — 한마디도 마요, 제발.\"\n\n쓸데없는 당부 같았다. 제인이 말을 건네고 싶어 한 상대는 엘턴 부인이 아니라 미스 우드하우스였으니, 엠마는 그것을 또렷이 알아보았다. 예의가 허락하는 한 그녀를 각별히 대하고 싶어 하는 마음이 역력했으나, 그것은 좀처럼 눈길 한 번을 넘어서지 못했다.\n\n미스터 엘턴이 모습을 드러냈다. 그의 부인은 반짝이는 발랄함을 한껏 발휘하여 그를 맞았다.\n\n\"정말 갸륵하시네요, 여보. 정말이지. 나를 여기 먼저 보내 놓고 이렇게 한참이나 황송하게 행차하지 않으시다니, 내 친구들에게 짐이나 되라고요! — 하지만 당신이 얼마나 순종적인 사람을 상대하는지는 잘 아셨겠죠. 내 군주님이자 주인님이 나타나기 전엔 내가 꼼짝도 안 하리라는 걸요. — 한 시간을 이렇게 여기 앉아서 이 아가씨들에게 진정한 부부간 순종의 본보기를 보여 주고 있었답니다 — 그게 얼마나 빨리 필요해질지 누가 알겠어요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n미스터 엘턴은 어찌나 덥고 지쳤던지, 이 모든 재담이 헛되이 흩어지는 듯했다. 다른 숙녀들에게 인사치레는 차려야 했지만, 그다음으로 그가 매달린 일이라곤 자기가 겪은 더위와 헛걸음한 길을 두고 신세를 한탄하는 것이었다.\n\n\"돈웰에 도착했더니,\" 그가 말했다. \"나이틀리를 도무지 찾을 수가 없지 뭐요. 참 이상한 일이지! 도저히 영문을 모르겠소! 오늘 아침에 내가 쪽지를 보냈고, 한 시까지는 분명 집에 있겠다고 답을 보내왔는데도 말이오.\"\n\n\"돈웰이라고요!\" 그의 아내가 외쳤다. — \"여보, 당신 돈웰에 갔던 게 아니잖아요! — 크라운을 말하는 거겠죠. 크라운에서 하는 모임에서 오는 길이면서.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니오, 그건 내일이오. 그리고 바로 그 일 때문에 오늘 꼭 나이틀리를 봐야 했단 말이오. — 어쩜 그렇게 끔찍하게 푹푹 찌는 아침이람! — 게다가 들판을 가로질러 갔으니 — (몹시 푸대접받았다는 어조로) — 그래서 더 고약했지. 그러고도 집에 없다니! 정말이지 나는 영 마음에 안 드오. 사과 한마디 남기질 않았고, 내게 전언도 없었소. 가정부는 내가 올 줄 몰랐다고 잡아떼더군. — 참 별난 일이지! — 그리고 그가 어느 쪽으로 갔는지 아는 사람도 통 없었소. 어쩌면 하트필드, 어쩌면 애비 밀, 어쩌면 제 숲속으로 갔는지. — 미스 우드하우스, 이건 우리 벗 나이틀리답지 않은 일이오! — 이 일을 설명해 주실 수 있겠소?\"\n\n엠마는 정말이지 참으로 별난 일이며 자기로서는 그를 두둔할 말이 한마디도 없다고 우기며 재미있어했다.\n\n\"도무지 상상이 안 가요,\" 엘턴 부인이 (아내로서 마땅히 느낄 모욕감을 느끼며) 말했다. \"하필이면 세상에 다른 누구도 아닌 당신한테 그이가 어떻게 그런 짓을 할 수 있는지 도무지 상상이 안 간다니까요! 잊힐 사람이라곤 도무지 생각할 수 없는 바로 그 사람한테요! — 여보, 그이가 분명 당신께 전언을 남겼을 거예요, 틀림없어요. — 아무리 나이틀리라도 그렇게까지 별나진 않을 테고요. 그이 하인들이 깜빡한 거죠. 장담컨대 바로 그랬을 거예요. 돈웰 하인들이라면 충분히 그럴 만하죠. 내가 늘 보지만 하나같이 굼뜨고 칠칠치 못하거든요. — 정말이지 그 집 해리 같은 작자는 어떤 일이 있어도 우리 집 사이드보드 앞에 세워 두지 않을 거예요. 그리고 미시즈 호지스로 말하자면, 라이트가 영 시원찮게 여긴답니다. — 라이트한테 조리법을 주겠다고 약속해 놓고 끝내 안 보냈거든요.\"\n\n\"집 가까이 갔을 때 윌리엄 라킨스를 만났는데,\" 미스터 엘턴이 말을 이었다. \"주인이 집에 안 계실 거라고 하더군. 하지만 나는 그 말을 안 믿었지. — 윌리엄도 좀 심사가 뒤틀린 것 같았소. 요즘 주인한테 무슨 일이 생겼는지 도무지 모르겠다면서, 좀처럼 말 한마디 붙여 볼 수가 없다지 뭐요. 윌리엄이 무엇을 바라든 그건 나하고 상관없는 일이지만, 내가 오늘 나이틀리를 봐야 한다는 건 정말이지 대단히 중요한 일이오. 그러니 이렇게 더운 길을 공연히 걸어 헛수고만 했다는 게 여간 심각한 곤란이 아니란 말이오.\"\n\n엠마는 곧장 집으로 돌아가는 것이 상책이라고 느꼈다. 십중팔구 바로 지금 그곳에서 누군가가 그녀를 기다리고 있을 터였다. 그리고 윌리엄 라킨스에게야 그렇다 치더라도, 미스터 나이틀리가 미스터 엘턴에게 결례를 더 거듭하는 일만은 막아 줄 수 있을지 몰랐다.\n\n작별 인사를 하며, 그녀는 미스 페어팩스가 한사코 자기를 방 밖까지, 심지어 아래층까지 배웅해 주려는 것을 알고 기뻤다. 그것은 곧바로 활용한 기회를 그녀에게 주었으니, 그녀는 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 기회가 없었던 게 차라리 잘된 일인지도 몰라요. 당신이 다른 벗들에 둘러싸여 있지 않았더라면, 나는 어떤 화제를 꺼내고, 이것저것 묻고, 그리 온당치 못한 데까지 터놓고 말하고 싶은 유혹에 빠졌을지도 모르거든요. — 분명히 무례하게 굴었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"오!\" 제인이 외쳤다. 얼굴을 붉히며 머뭇거리는 그 모습이, 엠마가 보기엔 평소의 그 우아한 침착함 따위보다 한없이 더 잘 어울렸다. \"아무 위험도 없었을 거예요. 위험이 있었다면 제가 당신을 지루하게 해 드릴 위험이었겠죠. 당신이 관심을 보여 주시는 것보다 더 저를 기쁘게 해 줄 수는 없었을 거예요 — . 정말이에요, 미스 우드하우스,\" (한결 차분하게 말하며) \"제가 잘못했다는, 아주 크게 잘못했다는 의식을 안고 있는 처지라, 그 호평을 가장 잃고 싶지 않은 벗들이 그래도 저를 그렇게까지 역겨워하지는 않는다는 걸 아는 것이 저로서는 유달리 위안이 돼요 — 하고 싶은 말의 절반을 할 시간조차 없네요. 사과하고, 변명하고, 저 자신을 위해 뭔가 호소하고 싶어요. 그게 너무나 마땅하다고 느껴서요. 하지만 안타깝게도 — 한마디로, 당신의 연민이 제 편이 되어 주지 않는다면 —\"\n\n\"오! 당신은 너무 조심스러워요, 정말로요.\" 엠마가 따뜻하게 외치며 그녀의 손을 잡았다. \"당신은 나한테 사과할 게 하나도 없어요. 그리고 당신이 사과해야 할 법한 사람이라면 누구나 더없이 만족하고, 심지어 기뻐하고 있는걸요 —\"\n\n\"정말 친절하시네요. 하지만 제가 당신께 어떻게 굴었는지는 제가 알아요. — 그렇게 냉랭하고 꾸며 댄 태도라니! — 저는 늘 어떤 역할을 연기하고 있었거든요. — 그건 속임수의 나날이었어요! — 제가 당신을 역겹게 했을 게 분명해요.\"\n\n\"제발 더는 말하지 마요. 사과는 죄다 내 쪽에서 해야 마땅하다는 느낌이 드는걸요. 우리 당장 서로 용서하기로 해요. 해야 할 일이라면 무엇이든 되도록 빨리 해 버려야죠. 그리고 그 점에선 우리 마음이 조금도 시간을 끌지 않으리라 생각해요. 윈저에서 반가운 소식이 오기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"아주 반가운 소식이요.\"\n\n\"그리고 다음 소식이라면, 짐작건대, 우리가 당신을 떠나보내게 되리라는 거겠죠 — 이제 막 당신을 알게 됐는데 말이에요.\"\n\n\"오! 그 모든 일로 말하자면야, 물론 아직은 아무것도 생각할 수 없죠. 캠벨 대령 내외분이 부르실 때까지는 여기 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 아직 확정된 건 아무것도 없을지 모르죠,\" 엠마가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"하지만, 실례지만, 생각은 해 봐야 할 일이잖아요.\"\n\n제인이 그 미소에 미소로 답하며 말했다.\n\n\"맞는 말씀이에요. 생각은 해 봤어요. 그리고 당신께는 털어놓을게요 (분명 안전하리라 믿으니까요), 엔스컴에서 미스터 처칠과 함께 사는 데까지는 정해졌어요. 적어도 석 달은 깊은 상복을 입어야 하지만, 그게 지나고 나면 더 기다릴 일은 없을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 고마워요. — 이게 바로 내가 확인하고 싶던 거예요. — 오! 정해지고 터놓는 것을 내가 얼마나 좋아하는지 당신이 안다면! — 잘 가요, 잘 가요.\"\n\n# 제3권 17장\n\n웨스턴 부인의 벗들은 모두 그녀가 무사한 데 안도했다. 그녀가 잘해 냈다는 흡족함에 무언가가 더해질 수 있었다면, 그것은 그녀가 딸아이의 어머니가 되었다는 사실을 안 일이었다. 엠마는 처음부터 미스 웨스턴이기를 바라는 마음이 확고했다. 훗날 그 아이를 이저벨라의 두 아들 가운데 누군가와 맺어 줄 속셈이 있어서라고는 결코 인정하려 들지 않았으나, 딸아이가 아버지에게도 어머니에게도 가장 잘 어울리리라고 확신하고 있었다. 미스터 웨스턴에게는 큰 위안이 되리라 — 그도 나이가 들수록, 십 년 뒤면 미스터 웨스턴인들 늙어 가지 않으랴 — 집에서 결코 쫓겨나는 법 없는 아이의 장난과 허튼소리, 변덕과 공상으로 난롯가가 흥겨워진다면 말이다. 그리고 웨스턴 부인으로 말하자면 — 딸아이가 그녀에게 더없이 잘 맞으리라는 것은 누구도 의심하지 못할 일이었고, 가르치는 데 그토록 능한 사람이 그 능력을 다시 펼치지 못한다면 참으로 아까운 노릇일 터였다.\n\n\"그분께는, 아시다시피, 저를 데리고 연습해 본 이점이 있잖아요.\" 엠마가 말을 이었다. \"마담 드 장리스의 『아델라이드와 테오도르』에서 달만 남작 부인이 오스탈리 백작 부인에게 했던 것처럼요. 그러니 이제 우리는 그분이 자기 자신의 어린 아델라이드를 한층 완벽한 방식으로 교육하는 걸 보게 되겠죠.\"\n* 마담 드 장리스(Madame de Genlis): 18세기 프랑스 작가. 『아델라이드와 테오도르』(1782)는 자녀 교육을 다룬 서간체 교육소설.\n\n\"다시 말해,\" 미스터 나이틀리가 대꾸했다. \"그분은 자네를 어를 때보다 그 아이를 한층 더 어르면서도, 자기는 아이를 조금도 어르지 않는다고 믿게 되겠지. 차이라면 그것뿐일 거야.\"\n\n\"가엾어라!\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"그런 식이면 그 아이는 어찌 되겠어요?\"\n\n\"아주 나쁠 거야 없지. 수천 명이 다 그렇게 자란다네. 어릴 적엔 못 봐줄 아이였다가, 자라면서 스스로를 바로잡는 거야. 나는 응석받이들에 대한 미움이 이제 죄다 사라지고 있다네, 사랑하는 엠마. 내 모든 행복을 그대에게 빚진 내가, 그 아이들을 모질게 대한다면 그야말로 끔찍한 배은망덕이 아니겠나?\"\n\n엠마는 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"하지만 저는 남들의 응석을 막아 주려던 당신의 온갖 노력의 도움을 받았는걸요. 그게 없었다면 제 분별만으로 저를 바로잡을 수 있었을지 의심스러워요.\"\n\n\"그런가? 나는 의심하지 않아. 자네에게 이해력을 준 건 천성이고, 원칙을 준 건 미스 테일러였지. 자네는 잘됐을 거야. 내 참견은 득이 될 만큼이나 해가 되기도 십상이었어. '저 사람이 무슨 권리로 나를 훈계해?'라고 자네가 말하는 건 지극히 자연스러운 일이었고 — 그게 못마땅한 방식으로 행해졌다고 느끼는 것도, 안타깝지만 지극히 자연스러운 일이었지. 나는 내가 자네에게 무슨 득이 되었다고 믿지 않아. 득은 죄다 내 차지였지. 자네를 더없이 애틋한 애정의 대상으로 삼았으니까. 자네 생각을 그토록 자주 하면서 흠까지 다 사랑하지 않을 도리가 없었고, 그 숱한 잘못을 떠올리고 또 떠올린 끝에, 적어도 자네가 열세 살 적부터 줄곧 자네를 사랑해 온 거라네.\"\n\n\"당신이 제게 도움이 되었던 건 분명해요.\" 엠마가 외쳤다. \"저는 아주 자주 당신에게서 옳은 영향을 받았어요 — 그때 인정하려 한 것보다 훨씬 자주요. 당신이 제게 득이 되었다는 건 정말 확실해요. 그리고 가엾은 어린 애나 웨스턴이 응석받이로 자랄 운명이라면, 당신이 제게 해 주신 만큼 그 아이에게도 해 주시는 게 가장 큰 자비일 거예요. 그 아이가 열세 살 됐을 때 사랑에 빠지시는 것만 빼고요.\"\n\n\"자네가 어린 계집애였을 적에, 그 건방진 표정을 지으며 나한테 얼마나 자주 이렇게 말했는지 — '미스터 나이틀리, 저는 이러이러한 걸 할 거예요. 아빠가 해도 된댔어요. 아니면 미스 테일러가 허락했어요' — 내가 못마땅해할 줄 뻔히 알면서 말이지. 그런 경우에 내 참견은 자네에게 안 좋은 기분을 하나에서 둘로 늘려 줄 뿐이었어.\"\n\n\"제가 참 사랑스러운 아이였군요! 당신이 제 말들을 그토록 애틋하게 기억하시는 것도 무리가 아니네요.\"\n\n\"'미스터 나이틀리.' 자네는 늘 나를 '미스터 나이틀리'라고 불렀지. 습관이 되어 그리 딱딱하게 들리지는 않지만 — 그래도 딱딱한 건 딱딱해. 나는 자네가 나를 다른 무언가로 불러 주면 좋겠는데, 그게 뭔지는 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"한번은 제가 그 사랑스러운 발작 가운데 한 번을 일으켜 당신을 '조지'라고 부른 적이 있어요. 한 십 년 전에요. 당신이 기분 상할 줄 알고 그렇게 불렀던 건데, 아무 반대도 안 하시니까 다시는 안 불렀죠.\"\n\n\"그럼 지금은 나를 '조지'라고 못 부르겠나?\"\n\n\"불가능해요! 저는 당신을 절대로 '미스터 나이틀리' 말고는 못 불러요. 엘턴 부인의 그 우아한 간결함을 흉내 내어 당신을 '미스터 케이'라고 부르겠다는 약속조차 못 하겠어요. 하지만 약속할게요.\" 그녀가 곧 웃으며 얼굴을 붉히고 덧붙였다. \"딱 한 번은 당신을 세례명으로 부르겠다고 약속할게요. 언제라고는 말 안 할래요. 하지만 어디서일지는 아마 짐작하시겠죠 — 아무개가 아무개를 좋을 때나 궂을 때나 함께하겠노라 맞아들이는 그 건물 안에서요.\"\n\n엠마는 그의 더 나은 분별이 자기에게 베풀어 주었을 한 가지 중요한 도움에 대해 더 솔직하게 정당한 인정을 해 줄 수 없는 것이 못내 안타까웠다 — 여자로서 저지른 온갖 어리석음 가운데 가장 못난 짓, 곧 해리엇 스미스와의 고집스러운 친밀함으로부터 자기를 구해 주었을 그 충고에 대해. 그러나 그것은 너무도 민감한 주제였다. 그녀는 그 이야기를 꺼낼 수 없었다. 두 사람 사이에 해리엇이 입에 오르는 일은 아주 드물었다. 그쪽으로서는, 단지 그녀를 떠올리지 않아서일 수도 있었다. 그러나 엠마는 차라리 그것을 그의 섬세함 탓으로, 그리고 몇몇 정황으로 미루어 두 사람의 우정이 시들어 가고 있다는 의심 탓으로 돌리고 싶었다. 그녀 자신도 알고 있었다 — 다른 어떤 상황에서 헤어졌더라면 두 사람은 분명 더 자주 편지를 주고받았으리라는 것을, 그리고 지금처럼 거의 전적으로 이저벨라의 편지에만 소식을 의지하지는 않았으리라는 것을. 그도 그 점을 눈치챘을지 몰랐다. 그를 향해 숨김을 해야만 하는 고통은, 해리엇을 불행하게 만들었다는 고통에 조금도 못지않았다.\n\n이저벨라는 그 손님에 대해 기대할 수 있는 만큼 좋은 소식을 전해 왔다. 처음 도착했을 때는 기운이 없어 보인다고 여겼는데, 치과 의사에게 진찰을 받아야 했으니 지극히 당연한 일로 보였고, 그 일이 끝난 뒤로는 해리엇이 예전에 알던 모습과 다르다고는 보지 않는 것 같았다. 물론 이저벨라가 그리 예리한 관찰자는 아니었으나, 해리엇이 아이들과 함께 놀 수 없을 만큼 기운이 없었다면 그것마저 놓치지는 않았으리라. 엠마의 위안과 희망은 해리엇이 더 오래 머물게 되면서 더없이 흐뭇하게 이어졌다. 그녀의 보름은 적어도 한 달은 될 듯했다. 미스터 존 나이틀리 부부가 팔월에 내려올 예정이었고, 해리엇은 그들이 데리고 돌아올 수 있을 때까지 머물러 달라는 청을 받았다.\n\n\"존은 자네 친구 얘기는 꺼내지도 않더군.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"여기 그의 답장이 있으니, 보고 싶으면 보게.\"\n\n그것은 그가 결혼할 작정이라고 알린 데 대한 답장이었다. 엠마는 그가 그 일에 대해 무어라 했을지 알고 싶은 조바심으로 가득 차, 자기 친구가 언급되지 않았다는 말에도 조금도 움츠러들지 않고, 몹시도 다급한 손길로 그것을 받아 들었다.\n\n\"존은 형제답게 내 행복을 함께 기뻐해 주고 있어.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 그는 빈말을 하는 사람이 아니야. 그가 자네에게도 더없이 형제 같은 애정을 품고 있다는 걸 내가 잘 알지만, 그는 미사여구를 늘어놓는 일과는 워낙 거리가 멀어서, 다른 젊은 여자라면 자기 칭찬에 그가 꽤 냉랭하다고 여길지도 모르지. 그래도 나는 자네가 그가 쓴 걸 보는 게 두렵지 않아.\"\n\n\"분별 있는 사람답게 썼네요.\" 편지를 다 읽고 엠마가 대꾸했다. \"그분의 진실함이 존경스러워요. 이 약혼의 행운이 죄다 제 쪽에 있다고 여기시는 게 아주 분명하지만, 그래도 제가 머지않아 당신이 이미 그렇다고 여기시는 만큼 당신의 애정에 걸맞은 사람으로 자라 가리라는 희망을 버리지는 않으시는군요. 다르게 해석될 만한 말을 하셨다면, 저는 믿지 않았을 거예요.\"\n\n\"엠마, 그는 그런 뜻이 아니야. 그저 이런 뜻이지 —\"\n\n\"저와 그분이 우리 둘을 견주어 매기는 평가에는 별 차이가 없을 거예요.\" 그녀가 자못 진지한 미소를 띠고 말을 가로챘다. \"어쩌면 그분이 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 차이가 적을걸요. 우리가 격식이나 거리낌 없이 그 이야기를 나눌 수만 있다면요.\"\n\n\"엠마, 사랑하는 엠마 —\"\n\n\"아!\" 그녀가 한결 더 환한 명랑함으로 외쳤다. \"당신 형님이 저를 공정하게 봐 주지 않는다 싶거든, 우리 아버지가 이 비밀을 아실 때까지 기다렸다가 아버지 의견을 들어 보세요. 분명히 말하는데, 아버지는 당신을 공정하게 봐 주는 것과는 훨씬 더 거리가 멀 거예요. 모든 행복, 모든 이득이 당신 쪽에 있고, 모든 미덕은 제 쪽에 있다고 여기실걸요. 아버지 입에서 곧장 '가엾은 엠마' 신세로 떨어지지나 않으면 다행이고요. 짓눌린 가치를 향한 아버지의 애틋한 측은지심은 그보다 더 나아갈 데가 없거든요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"자네 아버지께서도, 우리가 대등한 가치가 줄 수 있는 온갖 권리를 다 갖추어 함께 행복할 자격이 있다는 걸, 존만큼이나 쉽게 납득해 주시면 좋으련만. 존 편지의 한 대목이 재미있던데 — 눈치챘나? — 내 소식이 자기를 아주 놀라게 하지는 않았다고, 오히려 그런 종류의 무언가를 듣게 되리라 짐작하고 있었다고 한 대목 말이야.\"\n\n\"제가 형님을 제대로 이해했다면, 그분 말씀은 그저 당신이 결혼할 생각을 어느 정도 품고 있었다는 데까지일 거예요. 제 생각은 전혀 못 하셨을걸요. 그 점에 대해선 전혀 마음의 준비가 안 되신 것 같던데요.\"\n\n\"맞아, 맞아. 하지만 그가 내 마음을 그토록 깊이 들여다봤다는 게 재미있어. 대체 뭘 보고 판단한 걸까? 이번이라고 다른 때보다 내가 결혼하리라 짐작할 만한 기운이나 말투의 차이가 있었다고는 의식하지 못하겠는데. 하지만 그랬던 모양이지. 요전에 그들 집에 머무는 동안 무언가 다른 점이 있었을 거야. 아무래도 평소만큼 아이들하고 놀아 주지 못한 것 같아. 어느 날 저녁엔 가엾은 사내아이들이 이러더군. '삼촌은 요새 늘 피곤한가 봐요.'\"\n\n이 소식이 더 멀리 퍼지고, 다른 사람들이 그것을 어떻게 받아들이는지 시험해 볼 때가 다가오고 있었다. 웨스턴 부인이 미스터 우드하우스의 방문을 받아들일 만큼 충분히 회복되자, 엠마는 그녀의 온화한 설득이 이 일에 한몫해 주기를 바라는 마음에서, 먼저 집에서 알린 다음 랜덜스에서 알리기로 마음먹었다. 하지만 끝내 아버지에게 이 일을 어떻게 털어놓는단 말인가! 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리가 자리를 비운 어느 시각에 반드시 해내기로 자신을 묶어 두었다. 그러지 않으면 막상 닥쳐서는 가슴이 무너져 미루고 말 터였으니까. 미스터 나이틀리는 정해진 시각에 와서 그녀가 떼어 놓은 첫머리를 이어 가기로 했다. 그녀는 말을 꺼내야만 했고, 그것도 명랑하게 꺼내야만 했다. 자기가 침울한 어조를 보임으로써 이 일을 아버지에게 한층 더 확정된 비탄거리로 만들어서는 안 되었다. 그것을 불행으로 여기는 기색을 보여서는 안 되었다. 그녀는 끌어모을 수 있는 온 기운을 다해, 먼저 아버지가 무언가 이상한 일에 대비하도록 한 다음, 몇 마디로 말했다 — 만약 아버지의 동의와 승낙을 얻을 수 있다면, 그것은 모두의 행복을 도모하려는 계획이니 별 어려움 없이 따라오리라 믿는다고, 그리하여 자기와 미스터 나이틀리가 결혼할 작정이며, 그렇게 되면 하트필드는 아버지가 두 딸과 웨스턴 부인 다음으로 세상에서 가장 사랑하는 줄 자기가 아는 바로 그 사람과 늘 함께하게 되리라고.\n\n가엾은 양반! 처음에 그것은 그에게 적잖은 충격이었고, 그는 진지하게 딸을 만류하려 애썼다. 그녀는 한 번 아니라 여러 번, 자기는 결코 결혼하지 않겠노라 늘 말해 왔다는 사실을 일깨움 받았고, 독신으로 지내는 편이 훨씬 나으리라는 다짐을 들었으며, 가엾은 이저벨라와 가엾은 미스 테일러 이야기까지 들었다. 그러나 소용없었다. 엠마는 아버지에게 다정하게 매달려 웃으며 말했다 — 이렇게 될 수밖에 없다고, 그리고 자기를 이저벨라나 웨스턴 부인과 한데 묶지 말아 달라고. 그들의 결혼은 하트필드에서 그들을 데려갔으니 정말이지 침울한 변화였지만, 자기는 하트필드를 떠나지 않을 것이며, 늘 여기 있을 것이며, 식구 수에도 안락함에도 더 좋아지는 쪽으로 말고는 아무 변화도 들이지 않을 것이라고. 그리고 일단 이 생각에 익숙해지시기만 하면, 미스터 나이틀리가 늘 곁에 있어서 아버지가 훨씬 더 행복해지시리라 확신한다고. 아버지는 미스터 나이틀리를 무척 사랑하지 않으시던가? 그러신다는 걸 부인하지 못하시리라 그녀는 확신했다. 일이 생기면 미스터 나이틀리 말고 누구와 의논하고 싶어 하셨던가? 누가 그토록 도움이 되고, 누가 그토록 기꺼이 편지를 대신 써 주고, 누가 그토록 흔쾌히 거들어 주었던가? 누가 그토록 쾌활하고, 그토록 자상하고, 그토록 아버지를 따랐던가? 그를 늘 곁에 두고 싶지 않으시던가? 그래, 그건 다 옳은 말이었다. 미스터 나이틀리는 아무리 자주 와도 좋았다. 날마다 보아도 좋았다. 하지만 지금도 날마다 보지 않느냐. 그러니 지금까지 해 온 대로 그냥 지내면 안 되겠느냐.\n\n미스터 우드하우스는 쉬이 마음을 풀지 못했다. 그러나 최악의 고비는 넘긴 셈이었고, 생각의 씨는 뿌려졌으니, 나머지는 시간과 끊임없는 되풀이가 해 줄 일이었다. 엠마의 간청과 다짐에 이어 미스터 나이틀리의 그것이 뒤따랐고, 엠마를 향한 그의 애정 어린 칭찬은 이 주제에 일종의 반가움마저 입혀 주었다. 그리하여 아버지는 적당한 기회가 있을 때마다 두 사람에게서 번갈아 이 이야기를 듣는 일에 곧 익숙해졌다. 두 사람은 이저벨라가 더없이 강력한 찬성의 편지로 보태 줄 수 있는 온갖 지원을 받았고, 웨스턴 부인은 첫 만남에서부터 이 일을 가장 쓸모 있는 빛 아래서 바라볼 채비가 되어 있었다 — 첫째로 이미 정해진 일로서, 둘째로 좋은 일로서. 미스터 우드하우스의 마음에는 그 두 가지 권유가 거의 같은 무게라는 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다. 그렇게 될 일로 합의가 모아졌고, 늘 그가 따르던 사람들이 하나같이 그것이 그의 행복을 위한 일이라 장담해 주었으며, 그 자신도 거의 그것을 받아들이는 듯한 마음이 얼마간 든 끝에, 그는 차차 — 어쩌면 한두 해 뒤쯤이라면 — 그 결혼이 정말로 이루어진다 해도 그리 나쁘지만은 않을지 모른다고 생각하기 시작했다.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 이 일을 두고 그에게 한 모든 말에서 어떤 역을 꾸민 것도, 감정을 가장한 것도 아니었다. 엠마가 처음 이 일을 그녀에게 털어놓았을 때 그녀는 더없이 놀랐다 — 그토록 놀란 적이 다시없을 만큼. 그러나 그녀는 그 안에서 오직 모두의 행복이 더해지는 것만을 보았고, 그를 한껏 다그치는 데 아무 거리낌도 없었다. 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리를 어찌나 높이 사는지 그가 자기의 더없이 소중한 엠마조차 받을 자격이 있다고 여길 정도였다. 게다가 그것은 모든 면에서 그토록 온당하고, 어울리고, 흠잡을 데 없는 인연이었으며, 한 가지 면에서, 더없이 중요한 한 가지 점에서 유독 적합하고 유달리 다행스러운 일이라, 이제는 엠마가 다른 어떤 사람에게 마음을 주었더라면 도저히 안전하지 못했으리라는 듯, 그리고 진작 그것을 생각하고 바라지 못한 자기야말로 둘도 없이 어리석은 사람이었던 듯 여겨질 지경이었다. 엠마에게 청혼할 만한 계급의 남자들 가운데, 하트필드를 위해 제 집을 버릴 사람이 과연 몇이나 되겠는가! 게다가 미스터 나이틀리 말고 누가 미스터 우드하우스를 이해하고 견뎌 내어 그런 살림을 바람직하게 만들 수 있겠는가! 가엾은 미스터 우드하우스를 어떻게 처리하느냐 하는 어려움은, 프랭크와 엠마를 맺어 주려던 남편의 계획에서도 그녀 자신의 계획에서도 늘 느껴지던 바였다. 엔스컴과 하트필드의 권리를 어떻게 조정하느냐가 한결같은 걸림돌이었다 — 그녀보다는 미스터 웨스턴이 덜 인정한 바였지만. 그러나 그조차도 이 문제를 매번 이렇게 마무리하는 것 이상은 해내지 못했다 — \"그런 일이야 절로 해결되겠지. 젊은이들이 길을 찾아낼 거야.\" 그러나 지금 이 일에는 미래에 대한 막연한 억측으로 떠넘길 거리가 하나도 없었다. 모든 것이 옳고, 모든 것이 환히 트였으며, 모든 것이 대등했다. 어느 쪽에도 희생이라 할 만한 희생이 없었다. 그것은 그 자체로 더없이 행복이 약속된 결합이었고, 그것을 가로막거나 미룰 실질적이고 합당한 어려움이 단 하나도 없었다.\n\n웨스턴 부인은 무릎에 갓난아기를 앉히고서 이런 생각에 잠겨, 세상에서 가장 행복한 여자들 가운데 하나였다. 그 기쁨에 무언가 더 보탤 것이 있었다면, 그것은 아기가 첫 모자 한 벌을 머지않아 머리가 자라 못 쓰게 되리라는 것을 알아챈 일이었다.\n\n이 소식은 퍼지는 곳마다 한결같이 놀라움이었다. 미스터 웨스턴도 오 분 동안은 제 몫의 놀라움을 누렸다. 그러나 오 분이면 그 빠른 머리에 이 생각을 익히기에 충분했다. 그는 이 혼사의 이점들을 알아보았고, 아내 못지않은 한결같음으로 그것을 기뻐했다. 그러나 그 경이로움은 이내 아무것도 아닌 것이 되었으며, 한 시간이 채 못 되어 그는 자기가 늘 이 일을 예견해 왔노라 믿기에 그리 멀지 않았다.\n\n\"이건 비밀이렷다.\" 그가 말했다. \"이런 일은 으레 비밀인 법이지, 모두가 다 알고 있다는 게 드러나기 전까지는 말이야. 언제부터 말해도 되는지나 일러 주구려. 제인이 짐작이라도 하고 있을지 궁금하군.\"\n\n그는 이튿날 아침 하이베리로 가서 그 점을 확인했다. 그는 그녀에게 그 소식을 알렸다. 그녀가 딸이나 다름없지 않은가, 자기의 맏딸이 아닌가? 그녀에게 말해야만 했다. 그리고 미스 베이츠가 자리에 있었으니, 그것은 당연히 곧이어 콜 부인과 페리 부인과 엘턴 부인에게로 흘러갔다. 그것은 당사자들이 각오한 그대로였다. 그들은 그 소식이 랜덜스에 알려진 시각으로부터, 얼마나 빨리 하이베리 전역으로 번질지 헤아려 두었고, 자신들이 여러 집안의 저녁 자리에서 화젯거리가 되리라는 것을 자못 영리하게 떠올리고 있었다.\n\n대체로 이 혼사는 무척 좋은 평을 받았다. 어떤 이들은 그가, 또 어떤 이들은 그녀가 가장 운이 좋다고 여겼다. 한 무리는 그들이 다 함께 돈웰로 옮겨 가고 하트필드는 존 나이틀리 부부에게 넘기는 게 좋겠다고 권할 만했고, 또 한 무리는 양쪽 하인들 사이에 불화가 생기리라 점칠 만했다. 그래도 전체적으로 보면 단 한 군데, 곧 목사관을 빼고는 이렇다 할 반대가 일지 않았다. 거기서는 그 놀라움이 어떤 흡족함으로도 누그러지지 않았다. 미스터 엘턴은 아내에 비하면 이 일에 별 관심이 없었다. 그는 그저 \"이제 그 아가씨의 자존심이 흡족해졌으면 좋겠다\"고 바랐고, \"그녀가 잡을 수만 있다면 나이틀리를 잡으려고 늘 마음먹고 있었던 모양\"이라고 짐작했으며, 하트필드에 산다는 대목에 이르러서는 대담하게도 이렇게 내뱉을 수 있었다 — \"나라면 질색인데, 차라리 그 사람이 낫지!\" 그러나 엘턴 부인은 정말이지 몹시도 심란해했다. \"가엾은 나이틀리! 딱한 양반!— 그에게 참 안된 일이지.\" 그녀는 더없이 마음을 졸였다. 그는 비록 아주 별난 사람이긴 해도 천 가지 좋은 자질을 갖춘 사람이었으니까. 어쩌면 그렇게 넘어갔단 말인가? 그가 사랑에 빠졌다고는 도무지 여겨지지 않았다 — 눈곱만큼도. 가엾은 나이틀리! 이제 그와 나누던 그 즐거운 교제도 다 끝장이로구나. 그가 우리 집에 청할 때마다 와서 식사하기를 얼마나 좋아했던가! 하지만 그것도 이제 다 끝이지. 딱한 양반! 그녀를 위해 마련될 돈웰 탐방도 이제 없으리라. 오! 안 되지. 매사에 찬물을 끼얹을 나이틀리 부인이 떡하니 버티고 있을 테니. 더없이 못마땅한 일! 그래도 그녀는 요전에 가정부를 험담해 둔 것이 조금도 후회되지 않았다. 함께 산다니 끔찍한 작정이로다. 그게 잘될 리가 없지. 그녀는 메이플 그로브 근처에서 그것을 시도했다가 첫 분기가 끝나기도 전에 갈라설 수밖에 없었던 한 집안을 알고 있었다.\n\n# 제3권 18장\n\n세월이 흘렀다. 며칠만 더 지나면 런던의 일행이 도착할 참이었다. 마음을 졸이게 하는 변화였다. 그것이 자신을 한껏 동요시키고 슬프게 하리라 여기며 어느 날 아침 엠마가 그 생각에 잠겨 있을 때, 미스터 나이틀리가 들어왔고, 괴로운 생각은 한쪽으로 밀려났다. 처음의 즐거운 잡담이 끝나자 그는 말이 없었다. 그러더니 한결 진지한 어조로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"할 말이 있어, 엠마. 소식이 하나 있네.\"\n\n\"좋은 거예요, 나쁜 거예요?\" 그녀가 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 얼른 물었다.\n\n\"어느 쪽이라 해야 할지 나도 모르겠군.\"\n\n\"아! 좋은 거예요, 분명히. 얼굴에 다 나타나는걸요. 웃음을 참고 계시잖아요.\"\n\n\"걱정이군.\" 그가 얼굴을 가다듬으며 말했다. \"정말이지 걱정이야, 엠마, 이 이야기를 들으면 자네가 웃지 못할 것 같아서.\"\n\n\"그럴 리가요! 그런데 왜요? 당신을 기쁘게 하거나 즐겁게 하는 일이 저까지 기쁘게 하고 즐겁게 하지 않을 거라고는 도무지 상상이 안 되는걸요.\"\n\n\"한 가지 주제가 있지.\" 그가 대답했다. \"한 가지뿐이길 바라네만, 우리 둘이 생각이 같지 않은 주제 말이야.\" 그는 다시 미소를 띤 채 그녀의 얼굴에 시선을 고정하고 잠시 말을 멈추었다. \"짐작 가는 거 없나? 기억 안 나? 해리엇 스미스 말이야.\"\n\n그 이름에 그녀의 두 뺨이 붉어졌고, 무엇인지는 몰라도 무언가가 두려워졌다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 그녀에게서 직접 무슨 소식을 들은 건가?\" 그가 외쳤다. \"들은 것 같은데. 다 알고 있는 것 같군.\"\n\n\"아니요, 못 들었어요. 아무것도 몰라요. 어서 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"최악의 경우를 각오하고 있군, 보아하니. 그래, 아주 안 좋은 일이야. 해리엇 스미스가 로버트 마틴과 결혼한다네.\"\n\n엠마는 흠칫 놀랐는데, 도무지 각오하고 있던 사람 같지는 않았다. 그리고 그녀의 두 눈은 간절히 응시하며 \"안 돼요, 그럴 리 없어요!\"라고 말하고 있었지만, 입술은 다물려 있었다.\n\n\"정말이라네.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 말을 이었다. \"로버트 마틴 본인에게서 들었어. 채 반 시간도 안 됐네, 나와 헤어진 게.\"\n\n그녀는 여전히 더없이 말 많은 놀라움을 담고 그를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"내가 걱정한 그대로, 자네는 이 일이 영 마뜩잖은 게로군, 엠마. 우리 생각이 같다면 좋으련만. 하지만 머잖아 같아질 거야. 시간이 흐르면, 분명 우리 둘 중 누구 하나는 생각이 달라질 테니. 그동안은 이 이야기를 길게 할 필요 없겠지.\"\n\n\"오해하신 거예요, 완전히 오해하신 거예요.\" 그녀가 기운을 내며 대답했다. \"그런 일이 지금 저를 불행하게 만들어서가 아니라, 도무지 믿기지가 않아서 그래요. 있을 수 없는 일 같은걸요! 해리엇 스미스가 로버트 마틴을 받아들였다는 말씀은 아니실 거예요. 설마 그가 그녀에게 다시 청혼했다는 말씀도 — 아직은 아니죠. 그가 그럴 작정이라는 말씀이시겠죠.\"\n\n\"그가 청혼을 했다는 말이야.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 미소를 띠었으나 단호한 결단으로 대답했다. \"그리고 승낙을 받았네.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"이런!\" 그러고는 얼굴을 숙이는 핑계로 반짇고리에 손을 뻗어, 분명 자신이 드러내고 있을 그 더없는 기쁨과 즐거움의 감정을 죄다 감추며 덧붙였다. \"이런, 자, 이제 다 말씀해 주세요. 알아듣게 설명해 주세요. 어떻게, 어디서, 언제요? 죄다 알려 주세요. 이렇게 놀란 적이 없어요 — 그래도 이게 저를 불행하게 만들진 않아요, 정말이에요. 어떻게, 어떻게 이런 일이 가능했죠?\"\n\n\"아주 간단한 이야기야. 그가 사흘 전에 볼일이 있어 런던에 갔는데, 내가 존에게 보내려던 서류 몇 장을 그 편에 맡겼지. 그가 존의 사무실에 그 서류를 전했더니, 존이 그날 저녁 애슬리 극장에 가는 자기들 일행에 끼라고 청한 거야. 위로 두 사내아이를 애슬리에 데려갈 참이었거든. 일행은 우리 아우 부부와 헨리, 존 — 그리고 미스 스미스였네. 내 친구 로버트가 거절할 수 있었겠나. 그들이 가는 길에 그를 데리러 들렀고, 다들 더없이 즐거워했지. 그리고 아우가 다음 날 함께 저녁을 들자고 청했고 — 그가 그렇게 했지 — 그 자리에서 (내가 듣기로는) 해리엇에게 말할 기회를 얻었고, 헛되이 말하지 않은 게 분명해. 그녀가 승낙함으로써, 그가 받을 자격이 있는 만큼이나 그를 행복하게 만들어 주었거든. 그는 어제 마차로 내려와서, 오늘 아침 식사 직후에 곧장 나를 찾아와 그간의 일을 보고했네. 먼저 내 용건을, 그다음에 제 일을. 어떻게, 어디서, 언제에 관해 내가 들려줄 수 있는 건 이게 다일세. 자네 친구 해리엇은 만나면 훨씬 긴 이야기를 들려줄 거야. 오직 여자의 말솜씨라야 흥미롭게 만들 수 있는 자잘한 세부까지 죄다 말해 주겠지. 우리끼리의 대화에서야 큰 줄거리만 다루니까. 그래도 한마디 하자면, 로버트 마틴의 마음은 그로서도, 또 내가 보기에도 한껏 넘쳐 보이더군. 그리고 별로 요점은 아니었지만, 애슬리에서 그 박스석을 나설 때 아우가 미시즈 존 나이틀리와 어린 존을 맡고, 자기는 미스 스미스와 헨리를 데리고 뒤따랐다는 것, 그리고 한때 어찌나 사람이 붐볐던지 미스 스미스가 좀 불안해했다는 것까지 말하더군.\"\n\n그는 말을 멈췄다. 엠마는 당장은 무슨 대답도 시도할 엄두가 나지 않았다. 입을 열면 분명 도무지 사리에 닿지 않을 만큼 큰 행복을 들켜 버릴 터였다. 잠시 기다려야 했다. 그러지 않으면 그가 자기를 미쳤다고 여길 테니. 그녀의 침묵이 그를 불안하게 했고, 그는 잠시 그녀를 살피더니 덧붙였다.\n\n\"엠마, 내 사랑, 이 일이 지금 자네를 불행하게 만들지는 않을 거라 했지. 하지만 자네가 예상한 것보다 더 큰 아픔을 주는 것 같아 걱정이야. 그의 처지가 좋지 않은 건 사실이지. 하지만 자네 친구를 만족시키는 일이라 여겨 주어야 해. 그리고 자네가 그를 알아 갈수록 점점 더 좋게 여기게 되리라는 건 내가 장담하지. 그의 분별과 올바른 신념은 자네를 흐뭇하게 할 거야. 사람 됨됨이로 말하자면, 자네 친구가 그보다 더 든든한 손에 맡겨지기를 바랄 순 없을 걸세. 그의 사회적 신분이야 내가 바꿀 수 있다면 바꾸고 싶지만 — 이건 정말 대단한 말이야, 엠마. 자네는 윌리엄 라킨스를 두고 나를 놀리지만, 로버트 마틴도 그만큼이나 아쉬운 사람이야.\"\n\n그는 그녀가 고개를 들고 미소 짓기를 바랐다. 이제 너무 활짝 웃지는 않을 만큼 마음을 추스른 그녀는 — 미소를 지으며 — 명랑하게 대답했다.\n\n\"이 혼사를 두고 저를 달래려고 애쓰실 필요 없어요. 해리엇이 더없이 잘하고 있다고 생각하는걸요. 그녀의 집안 쪽이 그의 집안보다 못할 수도 있죠. 인품의 떳떳함으로 따지면, 그게 못하다는 건 의심할 여지가 없고요. 제가 잠자코 있었던 건 그저 놀라서였어요, 너무 놀라서. 이게 얼마나 갑작스럽게 닥쳤는지 당신은 상상도 못 하실 거예요! 제가 얼마나 유달리 마음의 준비가 안 돼 있었는지를요! 바로 얼마 전까지만 해도 그녀가 그에게 전보다 훨씬 더, 아주아주 단단히 마음을 닫았다고 믿을 만한 이유가 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"자네 친구는 자네가 제일 잘 알겠지.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 대답했다. \"하지만 내가 보기에 그녀는 성품이 곱고 마음이 여린 아가씨라, 자기를 사랑한다고 말해 주는 젊은이에게 아주아주 단단히 마음을 닫고 있을 사람 같지는 않던데.\"\n\n엠마는 웃음을 참지 못하고 대답했다. \"정말이지, 당신도 그녀를 저만큼이나 잘 아시는 것 같네요. 하지만 미스터 나이틀리, 그녀가 정말로 명백하고 분명하게 그를 받아들였다고 완전히 확신하세요? 머잖아 그럴 수도 있다고는 짐작했지만 — 벌써요? 그를 오해하신 건 아니고요? 두 분이 다른 이야기들도 하고 계셨잖아요. 용건이며 가축 품평회며 새 파종기 같은 거요 — 그렇게 많은 주제가 뒤섞인 통에 혹시 그를 잘못 알아들으신 건 아닐까요? 그가 확신한 게 해리엇의 손이 아니라 — 어떤 이름난 황소의 치수였을지도 모르잖아요.\"\n\n이 순간 엠마의 마음속에서 미스터 나이틀리와 로버트 마틴의 얼굴과 분위기의 대조가 어찌나 강렬했던지, 또 바로 얼마 전 해리엇 쪽에서 일어났던 모든 일의 기억이 어찌나 강렬했던지, 그토록 힘주어 말했던 그 말 — \"아니요, 저는 로버트 마틴 따위는 생각하지 않을 만큼은 분별이 있어요\" — 의 울림이 어찌나 생생했던지, 그녀는 정말로 이 소식이 어느 정도 성급한 것으로 판명되기를 기대하고 있었다. 달리 될 리가 없었다.\n\n\"감히 그런 말을 하는 건가?\" 미스터 나이틀리가 외쳤다. \"감히 나를, 남이 무슨 이야기를 하는지도 못 알아들을 만큼 멍청이라고 생각하는 거야? 무슨 벌을 받아야 마땅하지?\"\n\n\"아! 저야 늘 최상의 대접을 받아 마땅하죠. 다른 건 결코 참지 않으니까요. 그러니 분명하고 직접적인 대답을 해 주셔야 해요. 미스터 마틴과 해리엇이 지금 어떤 사이가 되었는지, 그 조건을 제대로 알고 계신다고 정말로 확신하세요?\"\n\n\"확실히 알고 있지.\" 그가 또렷하게 말했다. \"그녀가 자기를 받아들였다고 그가 내게 말했고, 그가 쓴 말에는 모호한 구석이라곤, 의심스러운 데라곤 하나도 없었어. 그리고 분명히 그럴 수밖에 없다는 증거를 하나 댈 수 있을 것 같군. 그가 이제 어떻게 해야 할지 내 의견을 물었거든. 그녀의 친척이나 친구들에 관해 알아볼 사람으로 고더드 부인 말고는 떠오르는 이가 없다더군. 고더드 부인을 찾아가는 것 말고 더 마땅한 방도를 내가 댈 수 있었겠나? 나는 그럴 수 없다고 단언했지. 그러자 그는 오늘 안으로 그녀를 만나 보겠다고 하더군.\"\n\n\"완전히 만족했어요.\" 엠마가 더없이 환한 미소로 대답했다. \"그리고 진심으로 두 사람이 행복하기를 빌어요.\"\n\n\"이 문제로 전에 이야기 나눴을 때와는 자네가 사뭇 달라졌군.\"\n\n\"그러길 바라요 — 그때 저는 바보였으니까요.\"\n\n\"나도 달라졌네. 이제는 해리엇의 온갖 좋은 점들을 자네에게 기꺼이 인정해 줄 수 있게 됐으니. 자네를 위해서, 또 로버트 마틴을 위해서 (나는 늘 그가 변함없이 그녀를 사랑한다고 믿을 만한 이유가 있었거든) 그녀와 친해지려고 얼마간 애를 썼어. 그녀와 이야기를 꽤 많이 나누었지. 자네도 내가 그러는 걸 봤을 거야. 실은 가끔, 자네가 내가 가엾은 마틴의 편을 들고 있다고 절반쯤 의심하는 것 같다는 생각이 들기도 했어. 결코 그런 건 아니었지만. 어쨌든 내가 살펴본 모든 것으로 보아, 그녀는 꾸밈없고 사랑스러운 아가씨로, 아주 올바른 생각과 아주 진지하고 올바른 신념을 지녔으며, 가정생활의 애정과 보람에 제 행복을 두는 사람이라고 확신하게 됐어. 이 가운데 많은 부분은 분명 그녀가 자네 덕분이라 할 수 있을 거야.\"\n\n\"제 덕분이라뇨!\" 엠마가 고개를 저으며 외쳤다. \"아! 가엾은 해리엇!\"\n\n그래도 그녀는 자제하며, 받을 자격보다 조금 더한 칭찬을 잠자코 받아들였다.\n\n그들의 대화는 곧 아버지가 들어오면서 끝났다. 그녀는 아쉽지 않았다. 혼자 있고 싶었다. 마음이 들뜨고 어리둥절한 상태라 도무지 차분할 수가 없었다. 춤추고 노래하고 탄성을 지르고 싶은 기분이었다. 이리저리 거닐며, 혼잣말을 하고, 웃고, 곰곰이 되짚어 보기 전에는 무엇 하나 이성적인 일을 할 수가 없었다.\n\n아버지의 용건은, 이제 날마다 다니는 랜덜스 나들이 채비로 제임스가 말을 매러 나갔다는 것을 알리는 일이었다. 그래서 그녀는 곧바로 빠져나갈 핑계가 생겼다.\n\n그녀가 느낀 그 기쁨, 그 고마움, 그 더없는 환희는 가히 짐작할 만하다. 해리엇의 앞날에 대한 전망에서 유일한 근심거리이자 불순물이 이렇게 사라지고 나니, 그녀는 정말로 안심하기에는 지나치게 행복해질 위험에 빠질 지경이었다. 더 바랄 게 무엇이 있겠는가? 아무것도 없었다 — 그의 뜻과 판단이 늘 자신의 것보다 그토록 빼어났던 그 사람에게 더 어울리는 사람이 되는 것 말고는. 아무것도 없었다 — 지난날 어리석음의 교훈이 앞으로 그녀에게 겸손과 신중함을 가르쳐 주는 것 말고는.\n\n그녀는 진지했다, 그 감사와 그 결심에 있어 무척 진지했다. 그러면서도 더러는 그 한복판에서조차 웃음이 터지는 것을 막을 길이 없었다. 이런 결말이라니 웃지 않을 수가 있나! 다섯 주 전 그 처량한 실연의 끝이 이렇게 나다니! 이런 마음이라니 — 이런 해리엇이라니!\n\n이제 그녀가 돌아오는 일이 즐거움이 되리라. 모든 것이 즐거움이 되리라. 로버트 마틴을 알아 가는 것도 큰 즐거움이 되리라.\n\n그녀의 가장 진지하고 진심 어린 행복들 가운데서도 높은 자리를 차지한 것은, 미스터 나이틀리에게 무언가를 숨겨야 할 필요가 머잖아 모두 끝나리라는 생각이었다. 그토록 행하기 싫었던 그 위장, 얼버무림, 비밀이 머잖아 끝날 수 있으리라. 이제 그녀는, 자신의 천성이 의무로서 기꺼이 받아들이려는 그 온전하고 완전한 신뢰를 그에게 바칠 일을 고대할 수 있었다.\n\n더없이 들뜨고 행복한 기분으로 그녀는 아버지와 함께 길을 나섰다. 그의 말을 늘 귀담아듣지는 않았으나 늘 맞장구는 쳐 주었다. 그리고 말로든 침묵으로든, 날마다 랜덜스에 가지 않으면 가엾은 웨스턴 부인이 실망하리라는 그의 편안한 확신을 모르는 척 거들어 주었다.\n\n그들이 도착했다. 웨스턴 부인은 응접실에 혼자 있었다. 그런데 아기 이야기를 막 전해 듣고, 미스터 우드하우스가 청한 대로 와 주어 고맙다는 인사를 받기가 무섭게, 블라인드 너머로 창가를 지나가는 두 사람의 모습이 언뜻 눈에 들어왔다.\n\n\"프랭크와 미스 페어팩스예요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"마침 그 얘기를 해 드리려던 참이었는데, 오늘 아침 그 애가 도착하는 걸 보고 우리가 얼마나 기쁘게 놀랐는지요. 내일까지 머물고요, 미스 페어팩스도 오늘 하루를 우리와 함께 보내자고 설득해 두었답니다. 들어오고 있군요, 그러길 바라요.\"\n\n삼십 초쯤 지나 두 사람이 방으로 들어왔다. 엠마는 그를 보고 무척 반가웠다. 하지만 어딘가 어색함이 있었다 — 양쪽 모두 거북한 기억이 한둘이 아니었다. 두 사람은 선뜻 미소 지으며 만났으나, 처음에는 무슨 말을 하기 어려울 만큼 서로 의식하고 있었다. 다들 다시 자리에 앉고 나자 한동안 좌중에 어찌나 침묵이 흐르던지, 엠마는 프랭크 처칠을 다시 한번, 그것도 제인과 함께 보고 싶다던 오래 품어 온 바람, 지금에야 채워진 그 바람이 과연 그만큼의 기쁨을 안겨 줄지 의심이 들기 시작했다. 그러나 미스터 웨스턴이 일행에 합류하고 아기를 데려오자, 더는 화젯거리도 활기도 모자라지 않게 되었다. 프랭크 처칠이 그녀에게 다가와 말할 용기와 기회도 모자라지 않았다.\n\n\"미스 우드하우스, 감사 인사를 드려야겠습니다. 웨스턴 부인의 편지 가운데 하나에서 보내 주신, 그토록 너그럽게 용서하시는 전언에 대해서요. 시간이 흘렀다고 용서하실 마음이 줄어들지는 않으셨기를 바랍니다. 그때 하신 말씀을 거두지 않으시기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 정말이에요.\" 엠마가 말문을 열게 된 것이 더없이 기뻐 외쳤다. \"조금도 그렇지 않아요. 당신을 만나 악수하게 되어 — 그리고 직접 축하를 전하게 되어 특별히 기뻐요.\"\n\n그는 진심을 다해 그녀에게 고마워했고, 한동안 진지한 감정으로 자신의 감사와 행복을 이야기했다.\n\n\"그녀가 좋아 보이지 않습니까?\" 그가 제인 쪽으로 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"전보다 한결 좋아 보이죠? 아버지와 웨스턴 부인이 그녀를 얼마나 끔찍이 아끼시는지 보세요.\"\n\n그러나 그의 기분은 곧 다시 살아났고, 캠벨 부부가 돌아올 예정이라는 이야기를 꺼낸 뒤, 웃음 띤 눈으로 딕슨이라는 이름을 입에 올렸다. 엠마는 얼굴을 붉히며 자기 앞에서 그 이름을 입에 담지 못하게 했다.\n\n\"그 일은 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없어요.\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"이루 말할 수 없는 부끄러움 없이는요.\"\n\n\"부끄러움이라면 죄다 제 몫이죠, 아니 그래야 마땅하고요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"한데 정말로 조금도 의심하지 못하셨던 겁니까? 후반에 말입니다. 처음엔 전혀 못 하셨던 거 압니다.\"\n\n\"눈곱만큼도 의심하지 못했어요, 정말이에요.\"\n\n\"그것참 신기하군요. 한번은 거의 말할 뻔했는데 — 그때 말해 버렸더라면 — 그편이 나았을 거예요. 하지만 저는 늘 그릇된 짓만 했어도, 그게 죄다 아주 못된 그릇된 짓이라, 저한테 아무 득도 안 됐죠. 차라리 비밀의 약속을 깨고 당신께 모든 걸 털어놓았더라면 훨씬 나은 잘못이 됐을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"이제 와 후회할 가치도 없어요.\" 엠마가 말했다.\n\n\"바라건대.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"외삼촌이 랜덜스에 한번 와 주시도록 설득되면 좋겠어요. 그분이 그녀를 소개받고 싶어 하시거든요. 캠벨 부부가 돌아오면 런던에서 그분들을 만날 거고, 그녀를 북쪽으로 데려갈 수 있을 때까지 거기 머무를 작정입니다. 한데 지금은 그녀와 이렇게 멀리 떨어져 있으니 — 괴롭지 않겠습니까, 미스 우드하우스? 오늘 아침까지, 화해한 날 이후로 우리는 한 번도 못 만났답니다. 제가 가엾지 않으세요?\"\n\n엠마가 어찌나 다정하게 가엾다는 말을 건넸던지, 그는 갑자기 명랑한 생각이 솟구쳐 외쳤다.\n\n\"아! 그러고 보니.\" 그러더니 목소리를 낮추고 잠시 짐짓 시치미를 떼며 — \"미스터 나이틀리는 잘 지내시죠?\" 그가 말을 멈췄다. 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 웃었다. \"당신이 제 편지를 보신 거 압니다. 거기서 제가 당신을 위해 빌었던 바람도 기억하실 테고요. 이번엔 제가 축하를 돌려드릴게요. 정말이지 그 소식을 더없이 따뜻한 관심과 만족으로 들었답니다. 그분은 제가 감히 칭찬할 수도 없는 분이죠.\"\n\n엠마는 기뻤고, 그가 그 어조로 계속 말해 주기만을 바랐다. 그러나 다음 순간 그의 마음은 제 일과 제 제인에게로 옮아갔고, 그가 이어 한 말은 이러했다.\n\n\"이런 살결을 본 적 있으세요? 이런 매끄러움이라니! 이런 고움이라니! 그러면서도 마냥 희기만 한 건 아니죠. 그녀를 흰 살결이라고는 할 수 없어요. 짙은 속눈썹과 머리칼과 어우러진 더없이 보기 드문 살빛이라 — 더없이 돋보이는 살빛이라니까요! 그 안에 그토록 기품이 어려 있고요. 아름다움에 꼭 알맞을 만큼의 혈색이죠.\"\n\n\"저야 늘 그녀의 살빛을 칭찬했어요.\" 엠마가 짓궂게 대답했다. \"그런데 당신이 그녀가 너무 창백하다고 흠을 잡던 때가 있지 않았던가요? 우리가 처음 그녀 이야기를 꺼냈을 적에요. 까맣게 잊으셨어요?\"\n\n\"아! 아니, 제가 얼마나 뻔뻔한 작자였던지! 어찌 감히 그런 —\"\n\n그러나 그는 그 기억에 어찌나 유쾌하게 웃었던지, 엠마는 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"제 짐작엔 말이죠, 당신은 그때 그 곤혹스러움의 한복판에서도 우리 모두를 감쪽같이 속이는 데 큰 재미를 보셨을 것 같아요. 분명 그러셨을 거예요. 분명 그게 당신께 위안이 됐을 거고요.\"\n\n\"아! 아니, 아니, 아니에요 — 어떻게 저를 그런 사람으로 의심하실 수 있어요? 저는 세상에서 가장 가련한 비참한 놈이었는데요!\"\n\n\"즐거움을 못 느낄 만큼 비참하지는 않으셨겠죠. 우리 모두를 깜빡 속이고 있다는 걸 느끼는 게 분명 당신껜 큰 재미의 원천이었을 거예요. 어쩌면 제가 더 쉽게 의심하는지도 몰라요. 솔직히 말하면, 같은 처지였다면 저 자신한테도 얼마간 재미있는 일이었을 것 같거든요. 우리 둘 사이엔 좀 닮은 데가 있는 것 같아요.\"\n\n그가 고개를 숙여 보였다.\n\n\"성정으론 아닐지라도,\" 그녀가 이내 참된 감회가 어린 표정으로 덧붙였다. \"운명으로는 닮은 데가 있죠. 우리 둘 다 우리보다 훨씬 뛰어난 사람과 맺어질 듯한 그 운명 말이에요.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 맞아요.\" 그가 열렬히 대답했다. \"아니, 당신 쪽으로는 맞지 않아요. 당신보다 뛰어난 사람이 있을 수 없으니까요. 하지만 제 쪽으로는 더없이 맞는 말이에요. 그녀는 완벽한 천사거든요. 보세요. 몸짓 하나하나가 천사 같지 않습니까? 저 목선이 돌아가는 모양을 보세요. 아버지를 올려다볼 때 저 눈을 보세요. 들으면 기뻐하실 텐데\" (고개를 기울이고 진지하게 속삭이며) \"외삼촌이 그녀에게 외숙모의 보석을 죄다 물려주실 작정이랍니다. 새로 세공을 할 거예요. 저는 그중 몇 개로 머리 장식을 만들기로 마음먹었어요. 그녀의 짙은 머리칼에 얹으면 아름답지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"정말 아름다울 거예요.\" 엠마가 대답했다. 어찌나 다정하게 말했던지 그가 고마운 마음에 불쑥 외쳤다.\n\n\"당신을 다시 뵙게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지요! 게다가 이렇게 좋은 모습으로요! 이 만남을 놓쳤다면 세상을 다 준대도 아까웠을 거예요. 당신이 안 오셨더라면 분명 제가 하트필드로 찾아갔을 겁니다.\"\n\n다른 이들은 아기 이야기를 하고 있었는데, 웨스턴 부인이 어젯밤 잠깐 겪은 작은 놀람을 들려주는 중이었다. 아기가 영 좋지 않아 보였던 것이다. 자기가 어리석었던 것 같다고는 했지만, 그래도 가슴이 철렁했고, 하마터면 삼십 초만 더 있었으면 미스터 페리를 부르러 사람을 보낼 뻔했다고 했다. 어쩌면 부끄러워해야 할 일이지만, 미스터 웨스턴도 자기만큼이나 안절부절못했다고 했다. 그래도 십 분쯤 지나자 아기는 다시 멀쩡해졌다고 했다. 이것이 그녀의 이야기였다. 그리고 그것은 미스터 우드하우스에게 유독 흥미로운 이야기였으니, 그는 페리를 부르러 보낼 생각을 한 그녀를 무척 칭찬하면서, 다만 정말로 그러지 않은 것만은 아쉬워했다. \"아기가 조금이라도, 단 한순간이라도 이상해 보이면 언제든 페리를 부르러 보내야 해요. 아무리 빨리 놀라도 지나치지 않고, 아무리 자주 페리를 불러도 지나치지 않으니까요. 어쩌면 어젯밤에 그가 안 온 게 안된 일이에요. 아기가 지금은 멀쩡해 보이긴 해도, 그만하면 아주 멀쩡하긴 해도, 페리가 한번 봤더라면 십중팔구 더 나았을 테니 말이오.\"\n\n프랭크 처칠이 그 이름을 알아챘다.\n\n\"페리라고요!\" 그가 엠마에게 말하면서, 말하는 동안 미스 페어팩스의 눈길을 붙잡으려 했다. \"제 친구 미스터 페리 말이군요! 미스터 페리에 관해 다들 무슨 이야기를 하시는 거죠? 그가 오늘 아침 여기 왔던가요? 그리고 그는 요즘 어떻게 다니죠? 마차라도 한 대 장만했나요?\"\n\n엠마는 곧 기억해 내고 그의 뜻을 알아챘다. 그리고 그녀가 함께 웃는 동안, 제인이 안 들리는 척 애쓰면서도 실은 정말로 듣고 있다는 것이 그 표정에서 역력히 드러났다.\n\n\"그 참 별난 제 꿈이었지요!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"그 생각만 하면 웃음이 안 나올 수가 없어요. 그녀가 듣고 있어요, 듣고 있다니까요, 미스 우드하우스. 저 뺨에서, 저 미소에서, 찌푸려 보려는 저 헛된 안간힘에서 다 보입니다. 그녀를 보세요. 바로 지금 이 순간, 제게 그 소식을 전해 준 그녀 자신의 편지 구절이 그녀 눈앞을 스쳐 지나가고 있는 게 — 그 온갖 실수가 그녀 앞에 죄다 펼쳐져 있는 게 — 다른 이들 말을 듣는 척하면서도 그 외엔 아무것에도 마음을 못 쓰고 있는 게 안 보이세요?\"\n\n제인은 한순간 완연한 미소를 짓지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그를 향해 돌아서며 무언가를 의식하는 듯한, 낮지만 흔들림 없는 목소리로 말할 때까지 그 미소가 얼마간 남아 있었다.\n\n\"그런 기억들을 어떻게 견디시는지, 저로선 놀라울 따름이에요! 그것들이 가끔 비집고 들어오긴 하죠 — 하지만 어떻게 스스로 그것들을 불러들이실 수가 있죠!\"\n\n그는 대꾸로 할 말이 많았고, 무척 재미나게 받아넘겼다. 그러나 그 입씨름에서 엠마의 마음은 주로 제인 쪽에 가 있었다. 그리고 랜덜스를 나서며 자연스레 두 남자를 견주어 보게 되자, 그녀는 프랭크 처칠을 만나 그토록 반가웠고 또 실제로 우정 어린 마음으로 그를 여기고 있으면서도, 미스터 나이틀리의 인품이 얼마나 높이 빼어난지를 그 어느 때보다 절실히 느꼈다. 이 더없이 행복한 날의 행복은, 이 견줌이 자아낸 그의 가치에 대한 생기 넘치는 음미 속에서 비로소 완성되었다.\n\n# 제3권 19장\n\n엠마가 여전히 이따금 해리엇 때문에 마음을 졸이고, 그녀가 정말로 미스터 나이틀리에 대한 연정에서 벗어날 수 있는지, 그리고 정말로 한 점 치우침 없는 마음으로 다른 남자를 받아들일 수 있는지 잠시 의심하기도 했지만, 그런 막연한 불안이 되살아나 그녀를 괴롭히는 것도 오래가지 않았다. 며칠 지나지 않아 런던의 일행이 도착했고, 해리엇과 단둘이 한 시간을 보낼 기회를 얻기가 무섭게 그녀는 완전히 마음을 놓았다 — 영문은 알 수 없었으나! — 로버트 마틴이 미스터 나이틀리를 깨끗이 밀어내고, 이제 해리엇의 행복에 대한 모든 그림을 채우고 있다는 것을.\n\n해리엇은 조금 거북해했고 — 처음에는 어쩐지 멋쩍은 기색이었다. 그러나 한번 지난날 자기가 주제넘고 어리석었으며 스스로를 속여 왔노라고 인정하고 나자, 그 고통과 혼란은 말과 함께 스러져 버린 듯, 과거에는 아무런 미련도 남지 않고 현재와 미래에 대한 더없이 벅찬 기쁨만이 가득했다. 친구의 인정으로 말하자면, 엠마가 더없이 무조건적인 축하로 그녀를 맞아 그런 종류의 두려움은 단번에 말끔히 걷어 주었으니까. — 해리엇은 애슬리 극장에서 보낸 그 저녁이며 이튿날의 만찬을 시시콜콜 들려주는 것이 더없이 행복했고, 그 모든 일을 한없는 기쁨에 잠겨 곱씹을 수 있었다. 그러나 그런 시시콜콜한 이야기들이 무엇을 설명해 주었던가? — 사실인즉, 엠마도 이제는 인정할 수밖에 없었으니, 해리엇은 언제나 로버트 마틴을 좋아해 왔던 것이며, 그가 변함없이 그녀를 사랑한 것은 거역할 수 없는 힘이었던 것이다. — 그 너머의 일은 엠마에게 영영 알 수 없는 것으로 남을 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그러나 이 일은 더없이 기쁜 것이었고, 날마다 그렇게 여길 새로운 이유가 생겨났다. — 해리엇의 혈통이 밝혀졌다. 그녀는 어느 상인의 딸로 드러났는데, 그동안 그녀가 누려 온 안락한 부양을 감당할 만큼 넉넉하고, 또 그 일을 늘 숨기고 싶어 할 만큼은 점잖은 사람이었다. — 엠마가 한때 그토록 선뜻 보증하려 들었던 양반의 핏줄이란 게 고작 이러했던 것이다! — 어쩌면 여느 신사의 핏줄 못지않게 깨끗한 피였을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀가 미스터 나이틀리에게 — 또는 처칠 집안에 — 아니 심지어 미스터 엘턴에게까지 짝지어 주려 했던 것이 대체 어떤 인연이었단 말인가! — 고귀한 가문으로도 재산으로도 표백되지 않은 사생아의 오점은, 그야말로 오점이었을 터였다.\n\n아버지 쪽에서는 아무런 반대도 없었다. 그 청년은 후하게 대접받았다. 모든 것이 마땅히 그래야 할 대로였다. 이제 하트필드에 소개된 로버트 마틴과 알고 지내게 되면서, 엠마는 그에게서 작은 친구에게 더없이 좋은 짝이 되어 줄 만한 분별과 인품의 면모를 남김없이 인정했다. 마음씨 고운 남자라면 누구와 살더라도 해리엇이 행복하리라는 것을 의심하지 않았다. 그러나 그와 더불어, 그가 마련해 준 가정에서라면 그 이상을 — 안정과 든든함과 향상을 바랄 수 있을 것이었다. 해리엇은 자기를 사랑하고 자기보다 분별 있는 이들 한가운데 자리 잡게 될 것이며, 안전할 만큼 한갓진 곳에서 명랑할 만큼 분주히 지내게 될 것이었다. 결코 유혹에 이끌릴 일도, 유혹이 그녀를 찾아오도록 방치될 일도 없을 것이었다. 그녀는 떳떳하고 행복하게 살아갈 것이었다. 그리하여 엠마는 그런 남자에게서 이토록 한결같고 끈질긴 애정을 불러일으켰으니 해리엇이야말로 세상에서 가장 운 좋은 사람이라고 인정했다. — 아니, 꼭 가장 운 좋은 사람은 아니라 해도, 자기 자신에게만은 양보해 둘 정도라고.\n\n해리엇은 마틴 집안과 얽힌 약속들에 자연히 끌려, 하트필드에 점점 뜸하게 드나들게 되었으니, 그것은 아쉬워할 일이 아니었다. — 그녀와 엠마 사이의 친밀함은 가라앉아야 마땅했고, 그 우정은 한결 잔잔한 종류의 호의로 바뀌어야 했다. 그리고 다행히도, 마땅히 그러해야 하고 또 그럴 수밖에 없는 일이 이미 시작되고 있는 듯했으며, 그것도 더없이 점진적이고 자연스러운 모양새로였다.\n\n구월이 끝나기 전에 엠마는 해리엇을 데리고 교회에 가, 그녀의 손이 로버트 마틴에게 건네지는 것을 더없는 만족 속에 지켜보았으니, 그 앞에 미스터 엘턴이 서 있어 떠오르는 어떤 기억으로도 그 만족은 흠집 하나 나지 않았다. — 실로 그 순간 엠마의 눈에는 미스터 엘턴이, 어쩌면 다음번엔 제단 앞에서 자기에게 축복을 내려 줄 성직자로밖에는 거의 보이지 않았을 것이다. — 셋 가운데 가장 늦게 약혼한 로버트 마틴과 해리엇 스미스가 가장 먼저 결혼한 한 쌍이 되었다.\n\n제인 페어팩스는 이미 하이베리를 떠나, 사랑하는 보금자리에서 캠벨 일가와 함께 누리던 안락함으로 돌아가 있었다. — 두 미스터 처칠 또한 런던에 머물고 있었고, 그들은 그저 십일월을 기다리고 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n엠마와 미스터 나이틀리는, 감히 정할 수 있는 한 가장 이른 때로 그 사이의 한 달을 택해 두었다. — 두 사람은 존과 이저벨라가 아직 하트필드에 머무는 동안 혼례를 치르기로 마음먹었는데, 그래야 바닷가로 보름간 여행을 다녀올 수 있을 터였고, 그것이 계획이었다. — 존과 이저벨라를 비롯해 다른 모든 벗들도 그 계획을 한마음으로 찬성했다. 그러나 미스터 우드하우스는 — 미스터 우드하우스를 어떻게 승낙하게 한단 말인가? — 두 사람의 혼인을 여태껏 그저 먼 훗날의 일로만 입에 올리던 그를.\n\n처음 이 일을 넌지시 꺼냈을 때 그는 어찌나 비참해하던지, 두 사람은 거의 단념할 지경이었다. — 두 번째로 운을 떼었을 때는 정말이지 덜 괴로워했다. — 그는 이 일이 일어나고야 말 것이며, 자기로서는 막을 수 없으리라고 생각하기 시작했으니 — 체념으로 가는 마음에 더없이 가망 있는 한 걸음이었다. 그러나 여전히 그는 마음이 편치 않았다. 아니, 어찌나 편치 않은 기색이던지 딸의 용기가 꺾일 지경이었다. 아버지가 괴로워하는 것을, 자기가 버림받았다고 여기는 것을 차마 볼 수가 없었다. 그리고 일단 일이 끝나고 나면 그 괴로움도 곧 끝나리라는 두 미스터 나이틀리의 장담에 머리로는 거의 수긍하면서도, 엠마는 망설였고 — 더는 밀고 나가지 못했다.\n\n이렇게 마음을 졸이던 와중에 두 사람을 도운 것은, 미스터 우드하우스의 마음에 갑작스러운 깨우침이 비친 것도, 그의 신경 체계에 무슨 놀라운 변화가 생긴 것도 아니요, 바로 그 같은 신경 체계가 다른 방식으로 작동한 덕분이었다. — 어느 날 밤 웨스턴 부인의 가금사가 털려 칠면조가 몽땅 없어졌으니 — 영락없이 사람의 솜씨였다. 이웃의 다른 가금사들도 피해를 입었다. — 미스터 우드하우스의 겁먹은 머릿속에서 좀도둑질은 곧 가택 침입이었다. — 그는 몹시 불안해했고, 사위가 지켜 주고 있다는 든든함이 없었더라면 평생 밤마다 처참한 공포에 시달렸을 것이다. 두 미스터 나이틀리의 기운과 결단, 그리고 침착함은 그에게 더없는 의지가 되었다. 그들 중 누구라도 자기와 식구들을 지켜 주는 한 하트필드는 안전했다. — 그러나 미스터 존 나이틀리는 십일월 첫째 주가 끝날 무렵엔 다시 런던으로 가야 했다.\n\n이 근심이 가져온 결과는, 딸이 그 순간 감히 바랐던 것보다 훨씬 더 자발적이고 흔쾌한 승낙이 떨어져, 그녀가 혼례 날짜를 정할 수 있게 되었다는 것이었다 — 그리하여 미스터 엘턴은 로버트 마틴 부부의 결혼으로부터 한 달이 채 못 되어, 미스터 나이틀리와 미스 우드하우스의 손을 맞잡게 해 달라는 부름을 받았다.\n\n그 혼례는 당사자들이 화려함이나 떠들썩함에 도통 취미가 없는 여느 혼례와 매한가지였다. 그래서 엘턴 부인은 남편이 시시콜콜 늘어놓은 이야기를 듣고는, 그 모든 것이 지독히 초라하며 자기 혼례보다 한참 떨어진다고 여겼다. — \"흰 공단도 쥐꼬리만큼, 레이스 면사포도 몇 개 없고, 그야말로 가엾기 짝이 없는 일이지! — 셀리나가 들으면 눈이 휘둥그레질 거야.\" 그러나 이런 부족함에도 불구하고, 그 예식을 지켜본 한 줌의 참된 벗들이 품은 소망과 희망과 믿음과 예언은, 그 결합의 더없는 행복으로 남김없이 응답을 받았다.\n\n끝";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-158"]=[[["# Volume I — Chapter I", "# 제1권 1장"]], [["Emma Woodhouse, handsome, clever, and rich, with a comfortable home and happy disposition, seemed to unite some of the best blessings of existence; and had lived nearly twenty-one years in the world with very little to distress or vex her.", "엠마 우드하우스는 아름답고 영리하며 부유한 데다, 안락한 집과 쾌활한 천성까지 갖추어, 삶이 줄 수 있는 최상의 축복 몇 가지를 한 몸에 모은 듯한 사람이었다. 그리하여 이 세상에 태어나 스물한 해가 다 되도록, 그녀를 괴롭히거나 성가시게 한 일은 거의 없었다."]], [["She was the youngest of the two daughters of a most affectionate, indulgent father; and had, in consequence of her sister’s marriage, been mistress of his house from a very early period.", "그녀는 더없이 자애롭고 너그러운 아버지의 두 딸 가운데 막내였고, 언니가 결혼한 덕에 아주 일찍부터 집안의 안주인 노릇을 해 왔다."], ["Her mother had died too long ago for her to have more than an indistinct remembrance of her caresses; and her place had been supplied by an excellent woman as governess, who had fallen little short of a mother in affection.", "어머니는 너무 오래전에 돌아가셔서, 그 손길에 대한 어렴풋한 기억밖에 남아 있지 않았다. 그리고 그 빈자리는 가정교사로 들어온 한 훌륭한 여인이 채워 주었으니, 애정에 있어서는 어머니에 거의 못지않은 사람이었다."]], [["Sixteen years had Miss Taylor been in Mr. Woodhouse’s family, less as a governess than a friend, very fond of both daughters, but particularly of Emma.", "미스 테일러가 우드하우스 집안에 머문 지 열여섯 해 — 가정교사라기보다는 벗에 가까웠고, 두 딸을 모두 아꼈으나 그중에서도 엠마를 각별히 사랑했다."], ["Between them it was more the intimacy of sisters.", "두 사람 사이는 차라리 자매의 친밀함이었다."], ["Even before Miss Taylor had ceased to hold the nominal office of governess, the mildness of her temper had hardly allowed her to impose any restraint; and the shadow of authority being now long passed away, they had been living together as friend and friend very mutually attached, and Emma doing just what she liked; highly esteeming Miss Taylor’s judgment, but directed chiefly by her own.", "미스 테일러가 가정교사라는 이름뿐인 직책을 내려놓기 전부터도, 그 온화한 성품은 무엇 하나 억누르는 법이 없었다. 그리고 권위의 그림자마저 걷힌 지 오래인 지금, 두 사람은 서로 깊이 아끼는 벗과 벗으로 함께 살아왔고, 엠마는 그저 제 하고 싶은 대로 했다 — 미스 테일러의 판단을 높이 사면서도, 따르는 것은 주로 자기 자신의 판단이었지만."]], [["The real evils, indeed, of Emma’s situation were the power of having rather too much her own way, and a disposition to think a little too well of herself; these were the disadvantages which threatened alloy to her many enjoyments.", "실로 엠마의 처지에서 진짜 화근이라 할 것은, 매사를 제 뜻대로 너무 많이 할 수 있는 권한과, 자기 자신을 조금 지나치게 좋게 여기는 기질이었다. 이 둘이야말로 그녀의 숱한 즐거움에 흠집을 낼 우려가 있는 불리한 조건이었다."], ["The danger, however, was at present so unperceived, that they did not by any means rank as misfortunes with her.", "그러나 그 위험은 지금으로서는 워낙 감지되지 않아서, 그녀에게는 도무지 불행 축에 끼지 못했다."]], [["Sorrow came—a gentle sorrow—but not at all in the shape of any disagreeable consciousness.—Miss Taylor married.", "슬픔이 찾아왔다 — 잔잔한 슬픔이 — 그러나 결코 불쾌한 자각의 모습은 아니었다. 미스 테일러가 결혼한 것이다."], ["It was Miss Taylor’s loss which first brought grief.", "처음으로 비탄을 안겨 준 것은 미스 테일러를 잃는 일이었다."], ["It was on the wedding-day of this beloved friend that Emma first sat in mournful thought of any continuance.", "이 사랑하는 벗의 혼인날에야 엠마는 처음으로, 이 상실이 이어지리라는 침울한 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있었다."], ["The wedding over, and the bride-people gone, her father and herself were left to dine together, with no prospect of a third to cheer a long evening.", "식이 끝나고 신부 일행이 떠나자, 긴 저녁을 밝혀 줄 세 번째 사람도 기약 없이, 아버지와 단둘이 저녁을 들게 되었다."], ["Her father composed himself to sleep after dinner, as usual, and she had then only to sit and think of what she had lost.", "아버지는 여느 때처럼 식사 뒤 잠자리에 들 채비를 했고, 그러고 나니 그녀에게는 잃어버린 것을 곱씹으며 앉아 있는 일밖에 남지 않았다."]], [["The event had every promise of happiness for her friend. Mr.", "그 혼사는 벗에게 온갖 행복을 약속하는 일이었다."], ["Weston was a man of unexceptionable character, easy fortune, suitable age, and pleasant manners; and there was some satisfaction in considering with what self-denying, generous friendship she had always wished and promoted the match; but it was a black morning’s work for her.", "미스터 웨스턴은 나무랄 데 없는 인품에 넉넉한 재산, 어울리는 나이와 유쾌한 몸가짐을 갖춘 사람이었다. 자신이 늘 얼마나 헌신적이고 너그러운 우정으로 이 혼사를 바라고 밀어 왔는가를 떠올리면 흐뭇한 구석도 없지 않았다."], ["The want of Miss Taylor would be felt every hour of every day.", "그러나 그녀에게는 암담한 아침나절이었다."], ["She recalled her past kindness—the kindness, the affection of sixteen years—how she had taught and how she had played with her from five years old—how she had devoted all her powers to attach and amuse her in health—and how nursed her through the various illnesses of childhood.", "미스 테일러의 빈자리는 날마다, 시간마다 사무칠 터였다. 그녀는 지난날의 다정함을 떠올렸다 — 열여섯 해의 다정함과 애정을 — 다섯 살 적부터 어떻게 가르쳐 주고 어떻게 함께 놀아 주었는지를 — 건강할 때는 곁에 붙어 즐겁게 해 주려고 온 힘을 쏟고, 어린 시절의 갖은 병치레 때는 어떻게 간호해 주었는지를."], ["A large debt of gratitude was owing here; but the intercourse of the last seven years, the equal footing and perfect unreserve which had soon followed Isabella’s marriage, on their being left to each other, was yet a dearer, tenderer recollection.", "여기에 진 고마움의 빚이 컸다. 그러나 이저벨라가 결혼하고 둘만 남게 되면서 곧 자리 잡은 지난 칠 년의 교제, 그 대등한 사이와 흉허물 없는 격의 없음은 그보다 더 귀하고 애틋한 기억이었다."], ["She had been a friend and companion such as few possessed: intelligent, well-informed, useful, gentle, knowing all the ways of the family, interested in all its concerns, and peculiarly interested in herself, in every pleasure, every scheme of hers—one to whom she could speak every thought as it arose, and who had such an affection for her as could never find fault.", "그녀는 흔치 않은 벗이자 말동무였다. 총명하고 박식하고 쓸모 있고 온화하며, 집안의 모든 사정에 밝고 집안의 모든 관심사에 마음을 쏟았으며, 그중에서도 유독 그녀 자신에게, 그녀의 모든 기쁨과 모든 계획에 마음을 쏟았다 — 떠오르는 생각은 무엇이든 그대로 말할 수 있고, 결코 흠을 잡는 법이 없을 만큼 그녀를 사랑해 주는 사람이었다."]], [["How was she to bear the change?—It was true that her friend was going only half a mile from them; but Emma was aware that great must be the difference between a Mrs.", "이 변화를 어찌 견딘단 말인가? 벗이 가는 곳이 겨우 반 마일 밖인 것은 사실이었다."], ["Weston, only half a mile from them, and a Miss Taylor in the house; and with all her advantages, natural and domestic, she was now in great danger of suffering from intellectual solitude.", "그러나 반 마일 밖의 웨스턴 부인과 한집안의 미스 테일러 사이에는 크나큰 차이가 있으리라는 것을 엠마는 알고 있었다. 타고난 조건과 집안 형편이 그토록 유리한데도, 그녀는 이제 정신의 고독에 시달릴 큰 위험에 놓여 있었다."], ["She dearly loved her father, but he was no companion for her.", "아버지를 끔찍이 사랑했지만, 아버지는 그녀의 말동무가 되지 못했다."], ["He could not meet her in conversation, rational or playful.", "진지한 대화든 장난스러운 대화든, 아버지는 그녀를 받아 주지 못했다."]], [["The evil of the actual disparity in their ages (and Mr.", "부녀간의 실제 나이 차이에서 오는 곤란은 (미스터 우드하우스는 일찍 결혼한 편이 아니었다) 그의 체질과 습성 탓에 한층 더했다. 평생을 병약가*로 살아 몸도 마음도 움직이는 일이 없던 그는 나이보다 훨씬 늙은이처럼 굴었다."], ["Woodhouse had not married early) was much increased by his constitution and habits; for having been a valetudinarian all his life, without activity of mind or body, he was a much older man in ways than in years; and though everywhere beloved for the friendliness of his heart and his amiable temper, his talents could not have recommended him at any time.", "따뜻한 마음씨와 상냥한 성품으로 어딜 가나 사랑받았으나, 그 재간이 그를 돋보이게 한 적은 한 번도 없었다. * 병약가(valetudinarian): 실제 병보다 건강 걱정에 사로잡혀 사는 사람."]], [["Her sister, though comparatively but little removed by matrimony, being settled in London, only sixteen miles off, was much beyond her daily reach; and many a long October and November evening must be struggled through at Hartfield, before Christmas brought the next visit from Isabella and her husband, and their little children, to fill the house, and give her pleasant society again.", "언니는 결혼으로 그리 멀리 떠난 것은 아니어서 겨우 십육 마일 밖 런던에 자리 잡았지만, 날마다 오갈 수 있는 거리는 결코 아니었다. 성탄절이 되어 이저벨라 부부와 어린아이들이 다시 찾아와 집을 채우고 즐거운 어울림을 돌려주기까지, 시월과 십일월의 기나긴 저녁들을 하트필드에서 견뎌 내야만 했다."]], [["Highbury, the large and populous village, almost amounting to a town, to which Hartfield, in spite of its separate lawn, and shrubberies, and name, did really belong, afforded her no equals.", "하이베리는 읍내라 해도 좋을 만큼 크고 사람 많은 마을이었고, 하트필드는 잔디밭과 관목 숲과 이름이 따로 있다 한들 실은 그 마을에 속해 있었지만, 그곳에 그녀와 어깨를 나란히 할 사람은 없었다."], ["The Woodhouses were first in consequence there.", "우드하우스 집안은 그곳에서 첫째가는 가문이었다."], ["All looked up to them.", "모두가 그들을 우러러보았다."], ["She had many acquaintance in the place, for her father was universally civil, but not one among them who could be accepted in lieu of Miss Taylor for even half a day.", "아버지가 누구에게나 깍듯한 덕에 그녀도 아는 사람이야 많았으나, 그 가운데 미스 테일러를 한나절만이라도 대신할 만한 이는 하나도 없었다. 쓸쓸한 변화였다."], ["It was a melancholy change; and Emma could not but sigh over it, and wish for impossible things, till her father awoke, and made it necessary to be cheerful.", "엠마는 한숨을 쉬며 이룰 수 없는 일들을 바라는 수밖에 없었다. 이윽고 아버지가 잠에서 깨어, 명랑한 얼굴을 지어야 할 때까지."], ["His spirits required support.", "아버지의 기운은 떠받쳐 드려야 했다. 그는 신경이 예민하고 쉬이 의기소침해지는 사람이었다."], ["He was a nervous man, easily depressed; fond of every body that he was used to, and hating to part with them; hating change of every kind.", "정든 사람이라면 누구든 좋아했고, 헤어지기를 싫어했으며, 어떤 종류의 변화든 질색이었다. 변화의 근원인 혼인은 언제나 마뜩잖은 것이었다."], ["Matrimony, as the origin of change, was always disagreeable; and he was by no means yet reconciled to his own daughter’s marrying, nor could ever speak of her but with compassion, though it had been entirely a match of affection, when he was now obliged to part with Miss Taylor too; and from his habits of gentle selfishness, and of being never able to suppose that other people could feel differently from himself, he was very much disposed to think Miss Taylor had done as sad a thing for herself as for them, and would have been a great deal happier if she had spent all the rest of her life at Hartfield.", "제 딸의 결혼조차 — 그것이 온전히 애정으로 맺어진 혼사였는데도 — 아직 도무지 받아들이지 못해 딸 이야기가 나오면 으레 측은해하는 투로만 말하던 차에, 이제 미스 테일러와도 헤어져야 했던 것이다. 게다가 악의 없는 이기심이 몸에 배어, 남들이 자기와 다르게 느낄 수 있다는 것을 도무지 상상하지 못하는 사람인지라, 미스 테일러가 자기들에게만이 아니라 그녀 자신에게도 못할 짓을 했으며, 남은 평생을 하트필드에서 보냈더라면 훨씬 행복했으리라고 굳게 여기는 쪽이었다."], ["Emma smiled and chatted as cheerfully as she could, to keep him from such thoughts; but when tea came, it was impossible for him not to say exactly as he had said at dinner,", "엠마는 아버지가 그런 생각에 빠지지 않도록 힘닿는 데까지 명랑하게 웃고 떠들었다. 그러나 차가 나오자, 아버지는 저녁상에서 했던 말을 토씨 하나 다르지 않게 또 하지 않고는 배기지 못했다."]], [["“Poor Miss Taylor!—I wish she were here again.", "\"가엾은 미스 테일러! 다시 여기 있어 주면 좋으련만."], ["What a pity it is that Mr. Weston ever thought of her!”", "미스터 웨스턴이 그 사람한테 마음을 두다니, 이렇게 애석한 일이 있나!\""]], [["“I cannot agree with you, papa; you know I cannot.", "\"아버지, 저는 그 말씀엔 동의할 수 없어요."], ["Mr.", "아시잖아요."], ["Weston is such a good-humoured, pleasant, excellent man, that he thoroughly deserves a good wife;—and you would not have had Miss Taylor live with us for ever, and bear all my odd humours, when she might have a house of her own?”", "미스터 웨스턴은 그렇게 쾌활하고 유쾌하고 훌륭한 분이니, 좋은 아내를 맞을 자격이 충분하고말고요. 그리고 미스 테일러가 자기 집을 가질 수 있는데, 평생 우리 집에 살면서 저의 별난 변덕이나 다 받아 주기를 바라실 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“A house of her own!—But where is the advantage of a house of her own?", "\"자기 집이라니! 자기 집이 뭐가 좋단 말이냐?"], ["This is three times as large.—And you have never any odd humours, my dear.”", "여기가 세 배는 넓은데. 그리고 얘야, 너는 별난 변덕 같은 건 부린 적이 없단다.\""]], [["“How often we shall be going to see them, and they coming to see us!—We shall be always meeting!", "\"우리가 얼마나 자주 보러 가고, 그분들이 얼마나 자주 보러 오겠어요! 늘 만나게 될 거예요!"], ["We must begin; we must go and pay wedding visit very soon.”", "시작은 우리가 해야죠. 조만간 신혼 인사를 하러 가야 해요.\""]], [["“My dear, how am I to get so far?", "\"얘야, 내가 그 먼 데를 어떻게 간단 말이냐?"], ["Randalls is such a distance.", "랜덜스가 어디 가까운 거리냐."], ["I could not walk half so far.”", "나는 그 절반도 못 걷는다.\""]], [["“No, papa, nobody thought of your walking.", "\"아유, 아버지, 걸어가시라는 사람이 어디 있어요."], ["We must go in the carriage, to be sure.”", "당연히 마차로 가야죠.\""]], [["“The carriage!", "\"마차라니!"], ["But James will not like to put the horses to for such a little way;—and where are the poor horses to be while we are paying our visit?”", "그 정도 거리에 말을 매자면 제임스가 싫어할 텐데. 그리고 우리가 인사를 드리는 동안 그 가엾은 말들은 어디에 둔단 말이냐?\""]], [["“They are to be put into Mr. Weston’s stable, papa.", "\"미스터 웨스턴네 마구간에 두면 되죠, 아버지."], ["You know we have settled all that already.", "그건 벌써 다 정해 둔 일이잖아요."], ["We talked it all over with Mr. Weston last night.", "어젯밤에 미스터 웨스턴하고 다 이야기해 뒀어요."], ["And as for James, you may be very sure he will always like going to Randalls, because of his daughter’s being housemaid there.", "그리고 제임스라면 염려 마세요. 딸이 랜덜스에서 하녀로 일하고 있으니, 랜덜스 가는 길이라면 언제든 좋아할 게 분명해요."], ["I only doubt whether he will ever take us anywhere else.", "오히려 다른 데는 데려다주려 할지 그게 의심스러울 지경이에요."], ["That was your doing, papa.", "그게 다 아버지가 하신 일이에요."], ["You got Hannah that good place.", "해나한테 그 좋은 자리를 구해 주셨잖아요."], ["Nobody thought of Hannah till you mentioned her—James is so obliged to you!”", "아버지가 말을 꺼내기 전엔 아무도 해나를 생각하지 못했는걸요 — 제임스가 아버지께 얼마나 고마워하는데요!\""]], [["“I am very glad I did think of her.", "\"그 아이를 생각해 내길 참 잘했지. 운이 참 좋았어."], ["It was very lucky, for I would not have had poor James think himself slighted upon any account; and I am sure she will make a very good servant: she is a civil, pretty-spoken girl; I have a great opinion of her.", "가엾은 제임스가 어떤 일로든 푸대접받았다고 여기게 하고 싶진 않았으니까. 그리고 그 애는 분명 아주 좋은 하녀가 될 게다."], ["Whenever I see her, she always curtseys and asks me how I do, in a very pretty manner; and when you have had her here to do needlework, I observe she always turns the lock of the door the right way and never bangs it.", "예의 바르고 말씨가 고운 아이야. 나는 그 애를 아주 높이 산단다."], ["I am sure she will be an excellent servant; and it will be a great comfort to poor Miss Taylor to have somebody about her that she is used to see.", "그 애는 나를 볼 때마다 어찌나 곱게 무릎을 굽혀 인사하며 안부를 묻는지. 또 네가 바느질을 시키려고 이리 불렀을 때 보니, 문고리를 꼭 바르게 돌려 닫지 쾅 닫는 법이 없더구나."], ["Whenever James goes over to see his daughter, you know, she will be hearing of us.", "분명 훌륭한 하녀가 될 게야. 그리고 가엾은 미스 테일러한테도, 낯익은 사람이 곁에 있다는 게 큰 위안이 되겠지."], ["He will be able to tell her how we all are.”", "제임스가 딸을 보러 건너갈 때마다 우리 소식을 듣게 될 테니 말이다. 우리가 다들 어찌 지내는지 전해 줄 수 있을 게야.\""]], [["Emma spared no exertions to maintain this happier flow of ideas, and hoped, by the help of backgammon, to get her father tolerably through the evening, and be attacked by no regrets but her own.", "엠마는 이 한결 밝아진 생각의 물줄기가 끊기지 않도록 온갖 애를 다 썼고, 주사위 놀이의 힘을 빌려 아버지가 저녁나절을 그럭저럭 넘기고, 아쉬움이라면 오직 저 혼자만의 것이 되기를 바랐다."], ["The backgammon-table was placed; but a visitor immediately afterwards walked in and made it unnecessary.", "주사위 놀이판이 차려졌다. 그러나 곧바로 손님이 들어서면서 그것은 필요 없게 되었다."]], [["Mr. Knightley, a sensible man about seven or eight-and-thirty, was not only a very old and intimate friend of the family, but particularly connected with it, as the elder brother of Isabella’s husband.", "미스터 나이틀리는 서른일고여덟쯤 된 분별 있는 남자로, 이 집안의 아주 오랜 절친한 벗일 뿐 아니라 이저벨라 남편의 형으로서 각별한 인연까지 있는 사람이었다."], ["He lived about a mile from Highbury, was a frequent visitor, and always welcome, and at this time more welcome than usual, as coming directly from their mutual connexions in London.", "하이베리에서 일 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 살며 자주 드나드는, 언제나 반가운 손님이었는데, 이번에는 런던의 그 사돈집에서 곧장 오는 길이라 여느 때보다 더욱 반가웠다."], ["He had returned to a late dinner, after some days’ absence, and now walked up to Hartfield to say that all were well in Brunswick Square.", "며칠 집을 비웠다가 돌아와 늦은 저녁을 들고는, 브런즈윅 스퀘어의 모두가 잘 지낸다는 소식을 전하러 하트필드까지 걸어 올라온 것이었다."], ["It was a happy circumstance, and animated Mr. Woodhouse for some time.", "반가운 소식이라 미스터 우드하우스도 한동안 생기가 돌았다."], ["Mr. Knightley had a cheerful manner, which always did him good; and his many inquiries after “poor Isabella”", "미스터 나이틀리의 쾌활한 태도는 언제나 그에게 약이 되었다."], ["and her children were answered most satisfactorily. When this was over, Mr.", "\"가엾은 이저벨라\"와 아이들의 안부를 묻는 갖은 물음에 더없이 흡족한 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["Woodhouse gratefully observed, “It is very kind of you, Mr.", "그것이 끝나자 미스터 우드하우스가 고마워하며 말했다."], ["Knightley, to come out at this late hour to call upon us.", "\"이 늦은 시간에 우리를 찾아 주시다니 참으로 고맙소, 미스터 나이틀리."], ["I am afraid you must have had a shocking walk.”", "오시는 길이 끔찍하지 않았을까 걱정이오.\""]], [["“Not at all, sir.", "\"천만에요."], ["It is a beautiful moonlight night; and so mild that I must draw back from your great fire.”", "달빛이 아름다운 밤입니다. 어찌나 포근한지, 이 훌륭한 난롯불에서 오히려 물러앉아야겠는걸요.\""]], [["“But you must have found it very damp and dirty.", "\"하지만 길이 몹시 질척하고 더러웠을 텐데."], ["I wish you may not catch cold.”", "감기나 들지 않으시면 좋으련만.\""]], [["“Dirty, sir!", "\"더럽다니요!"], ["Look at my shoes.", "제 신을 보십시오."], ["Not a speck on them.”", "흙 한 점 안 묻었습니다.\""]], [["“Well!", "\"거참!"], ["that is quite surprising, for we have had a vast deal of rain here.", "그것 놀랍군. 여긴 비가 어지간히 왔는데."], ["It rained dreadfully hard for half an hour while we were at breakfast.", "아침을 드는 동안 반 시간을 어찌나 무섭게 퍼붓던지."], ["I wanted them to put off the wedding.”", "나는 혼례를 미루자고까지 했다오.\""]], [["“By the bye—I have not wished you joy.", "\"그러고 보니 — 아직 축하 인사를 못 드렸군요."], ["Being pretty well aware of what sort of joy you must both be feeling, I have been in no hurry with my congratulations; but I hope it all went off tolerably well.", "두 분이 어떤 기쁨을 느끼고 계실지 잘 알기에, 서둘러 축하드리지 않았습니다. 그래도 일이 그럭저럭 잘 치러졌기를 바랍니다."], ["How did you all behave?", "다들 어떠셨습니까?"], ["Who cried most?”", "누가 제일 많이 울던가요?\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["poor Miss Taylor!", "가엾은 미스 테일러!"], ["’Tis a sad business.”", "참 딱한 일이지.\""]], [["“Poor Mr.", "\"가엾은 건 우드하우스 부녀라고 해 주시지요. '가엾은 미스 테일러'라고는 도저히 못 하겠습니다."], ["and Miss Woodhouse, if you please; but I cannot possibly say ‘poor Miss Taylor.’ I have a great regard for you and Emma; but when it comes to the question of dependence or independence!—At any rate, it must be better to have only one to please than two.”", "어르신과 엠마를 높이 사기는 합니다만, 매인 몸이냐 제 몸의 주인이냐 하는 문제가 되면야! 어쨌든 비위를 맞출 사람이 둘에서 하나로 주는 편이 나은 게 분명하지요.\""]], [["“Especially when one of those two is such a fanciful, troublesome creature!”", "\"그 둘 중 하나가 이렇게 변덕스럽고 성가신 사람이라면 더더욱요!\""], ["said Emma playfully.", "엠마가 장난스럽게 말했다."], ["“That is what you have in your head, I know—and what you would certainly say if my father were not by.”", "\"그게 지금 머릿속에 든 생각이죠, 다 알아요 — 아버지만 안 계셨으면 분명 그렇게 말했을 거예요.\""]], [["“I believe it is very true, my dear, indeed,”", "\"그 말이 정말 맞는 것 같구나, 얘야.\""], ["said Mr. Woodhouse, with a sigh.", "미스터 우드하우스가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“I am afraid I am sometimes very fanciful and troublesome.”", "\"나는 아무래도 가끔 무척 변덕스럽고 성가신 사람인가 보다.\""]], [["“My dearest papa!", "\"아버지도 참!"], ["You do not think I could mean you, or suppose Mr.", "제가 아버지를 두고 한 말이라고 생각하시는 건 아니죠?"], ["Knightley to mean you.", "미스터 나이틀리가 아버지를 두고 한 말이라고요?"], ["What a horrible idea! Oh no!", "세상에 그런 끔찍한 생각이!"], ["I meant only myself. Mr.", "아니에요, 제 얘기였어요."], ["Knightley loves to find fault with me, you know—in a joke—it is all a joke.", "미스터 나이틀리는 저한테 흠잡기를 좋아하잖아요 — 농담으로요 — 다 농담이에요."], ["We always say what we like to one another.”", "우리는 늘 서로 하고 싶은 말을 다 하고 사는걸요.\""]], [["Mr.", "사실 미스터 나이틀리는 엠마 우드하우스에게서 결점을 볼 줄 아는 몇 안 되는 사람 가운데 하나였고, 그것을 그녀에게 말해 주는 유일한 사람이었다."], ["Knightley, in fact, was one of the few people who could see faults in Emma Woodhouse, and the only one who ever told her of them: and though this was not particularly agreeable to Emma herself, she knew it would be so much less so to her father, that she would not have him really suspect such a circumstance as her not being thought perfect by every body.", "엠마 자신에게도 그리 달가운 일은 아니었지만, 아버지에게는 훨씬 더 달갑지 않으리라는 것을 알기에, 세상 모두가 딸을 완벽하다고 여기지는 않는다는 사실을 아버지가 정말로 눈치채게 하고 싶지는 않았다."]], [["“Emma knows I never flatter her,” said Mr.", "\"엠마는 내가 빈말로 추어주는 법이 없다는 걸 압니다.\""], ["Knightley, “but I meant no reflection on any body.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"하지만 누구를 두고 한 말은 아니었습니다."], ["Miss Taylor has been used to have two persons to please; she will now have but one.", "미스 테일러는 비위 맞출 사람 둘에 익숙했는데, 이제는 하나만 맞추면 되니까요."], ["The chances are that she must be a gainer.”", "십중팔구 그녀가 득을 보는 셈이지요.\""]], [["“Well,” said Emma, willing to let it pass—“you want to hear about the wedding; and I shall be happy to tell you, for we all behaved charmingly. Every body was punctual, every body in their best looks: not a tear, and hardly a long face to be seen. Oh no; we all felt that we were going to be only half a mile apart, and were sure of meeting every day.”", "\"자아,\" 엠마가 그 말은 그냥 넘기기로 하고 말했다. \"혼례 이야기가 듣고 싶으신 거죠. 기꺼이 들려 드릴게요. 우리 모두 더없이 의젓하게 굴었으니까요. 한 사람도 늦지 않았고, 다들 더없이 고운 차림이었어요. 눈물 한 방울 없었고, 시무룩한 얼굴도 거의 안 보였죠. 그럼요. 겨우 반 마일 떨어져 사는 것뿐이니 날마다 만나게 되리라는 걸 모두 알고 있었는걸요.\""]], [["“Dear Emma bears every thing so well,”", "\"우리 엠마는 무엇이든 참 잘 견딘다오.\""], ["said her father. “But, Mr.", "아버지가 말했다."], ["Knightley, she is really very sorry to lose poor Miss Taylor, and I am sure she will miss her more than she thinks for.”", "\"하지만 미스터 나이틀리, 이 애는 가엾은 미스 테일러를 잃어서 정말로 몹시 서운해하고 있소. 분명 저 생각보다 훨씬 더 그리워하게 될 게요.\""]], [["Emma turned away her head, divided between tears and smiles.", "엠마는 눈물과 미소 사이에서 고개를 돌렸다."], ["“It is impossible that Emma should not miss such a companion,”", "\"엠마가 그런 벗을 그리워하지 않을 리야 없지요.\""], ["said Mr. Knightley.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말했다."], ["“We should not like her so well as we do, sir, if we could suppose it; but she knows how much the marriage is to Miss Taylor’s advantage; she knows how very acceptable it must be, at Miss Taylor’s time of life, to be settled in a home of her own, and how important to her to be secure of a comfortable provision, and therefore cannot allow herself to feel so much pain as pleasure.", "\"그럴 수 있는 사람이라면 우리가 이렇게까지 좋아하지 않을 겁니다. 하지만 엠마는 이 결혼이 미스 테일러에게 얼마나 득이 되는지 알고, 미스 테일러 나이에 제 집을 갖고 자리 잡는 일이 얼마나 반가운 일인지, 안락한 살림이 보장된다는 게 얼마나 중요한지도 압니다."], ["Every friend of Miss Taylor must be glad to have her so happily married.”", "그러니 기쁨보다 아픔이 크도록 제 마음을 내버려 둘 수 없는 거지요. 미스 테일러의 벗이라면 누구나 그녀가 이토록 행복하게 결혼한 것을 기뻐해야 마땅합니다.\""]], [["“And you have forgotten one matter of joy to me,”", "\"그리고 제게 또 하나의 기쁨이 있다는 걸 잊으셨네요.\" 엠마가 말했다."], ["said Emma, “and a very considerable one—that I made the match myself.", "\"그것도 아주 큰 기쁨을요 — 이 혼사를 제가 맺어 줬다는 것 말이에요. 사 년 전에 제가 맺어 준 거예요."], ["I made the match, you know, four years ago; and to have it take place, and be proved in the right, when so many people said Mr. Weston would never marry again, may comfort me for any thing.”", "그 많은 사람이 미스터 웨스턴은 절대 재혼하지 않을 거라고들 했는데, 이 혼사가 이루어져서 제가 옳았다는 게 증명됐으니, 그거면 어떤 일이든 위로가 되고도 남죠.\""]], [["Mr. Knightley shook his head at her.", "미스터 나이틀리는 그녀를 향해 고개를 가로저었다."], ["Her father fondly replied, “Ah!", "아버지는 애정 어린 투로 답했다."], ["my dear, I wish you would not make matches and foretell things, for whatever you say always comes to pass.", "\"아이고, 얘야, 제발 혼사를 맺어 주고 앞일을 점치는 일은 그만두려무나. 네가 말하는 건 죄다 들어맞으니 말이다."], ["Pray do not make any more matches.”", "부디 더는 중매를 서지 말아 다오.\""]], [["“I promise you to make none for myself, papa; but I must, indeed, for other people.", "\"제 혼사는 안 맺겠다고 약속드릴게요, 아버지. 하지만 남들 혼사는 정말이지 그만둘 수 없어요."], ["It is the greatest amusement in the world!", "세상에 이만한 재미가 또 없는걸요!"], ["And after such success, you know!—Every body said that Mr.", "게다가 이런 성공을 거둔 다음인데요!"], ["Weston would never marry again.", "미스터 웨스턴은 절대 재혼하지 않을 거라고 다들 말했어요."], ["Oh dear, no! Mr.", "어림도 없죠!"], ["Weston, who had been a widower so long, and who seemed so perfectly comfortable without a wife, so constantly occupied either in his business in town or among his friends here, always acceptable wherever he went, always cheerful—Mr.", "그렇게 오래 홀아비로 지내면서 아내 없이도 더없이 편안해 보이고, 읍내 일이다 이곳 친구들이다 늘 바쁘게 지내고, 어딜 가나 환영받고 늘 쾌활한 미스터 웨스턴 — 마음만 먹으면 일 년에 단 하루 저녁도 혼자 보낼 일이 없는 분인데."], ["Weston need not spend a single evening in the year alone if he did not like it. Oh no!", "아유, 그럼요!"], ["Mr. Weston certainly would never marry again.", "미스터 웨스턴은 절대로 재혼하지 않을 거라고요."], ["Some people even talked of a promise to his wife on her deathbed, and others of the son and the uncle not letting him.", "죽은 부인에게 임종 자리에서 약속했다느니, 아들과 외삼촌이 허락하지 않는다느니 하는 말까지 있었죠."], ["All manner of solemn nonsense was talked on the subject, but I believed none of it.", "이 일을 두고 별별 진지한 헛소리가 다 돌았지만, 저는 하나도 믿지 않았어요."]], [["“Ever since the day—about four years ago—that Miss Taylor and I met with him in Broadway Lane, when, because it began to drizzle, he darted away with so much gallantry, and borrowed two umbrellas for us from Farmer Mitchell’s, I made up my mind on the subject.", "\"사 년쯤 전 그날 — 미스 테일러와 제가 브로드웨이 골목에서 그분과 마주친 날, 이슬비가 듣기 시작하니까 그분이 어찌나 늠름하게 내달려 미첼 농장에서 우산을 두 개나 빌려다 주시던지 — 그날부터 저는 마음을 정했어요."], ["I planned the match from that hour; and when such success has blessed me in this instance, dear papa, you cannot think that I shall leave off match-making.”", "그 시간부터 이 혼사를 계획한 거예요. 이번 일에 이런 성공이 따라 줬는데, 아버지, 설마 제가 중매를 그만두리라고 생각하시는 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“I do not understand what you mean by ‘success,’” said Mr.", "\"그 '성공'이라는 말을 무슨 뜻으로 쓰는지 모르겠군.\""], ["Knightley. “Success supposes endeavour.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말했다."], ["Your time has been properly and delicately spent, if you have been endeavouring for the last four years to bring about this marriage.", "\"성공이란 노력을 전제로 하는 말이야. 지난 사 년을 이 결혼을 성사시키려 애쓰며 보냈다면야 시간을 알맞고 곱게 쓴 셈이지."], ["A worthy employment for a young lady’s mind!", "젊은 아가씨의 머리에 썩 어울리는 소일거리니까!"], ["But if, which I rather imagine, your making the match, as you call it, means only your planning it, your saying to yourself one idle day, ‘I think it would be a very good thing for Miss Taylor if Mr.", "하지만 내 짐작대로, 자네가 말하는 중매라는 게 그저 계획해 본 것뿐이라면 — 한가한 어느 날 '미스터 웨스턴이 미스 테일러와 결혼하면 그녀한테 참 좋을 텐데'"], ["Weston were to marry her,’ and saying it again to yourself every now and then afterwards, why do you talk of success?", "하고 혼잣말을 하고, 그 뒤로 이따금 같은 말을 되뇌었을 뿐이라면 — 무슨 성공을 말하는 건가?"], ["Where is your merit?", "자네 공이 어디 있지?"], ["What are you proud of?", "뭐가 자랑스러운 건가?"], ["You made a lucky guess; and that is all that can be said.”", "운 좋게 알아맞힌 것뿐이야. 그 이상은 말할 게 없네.\""]], [["“And have you never known the pleasure and triumph of a lucky guess?—I pity you.—I thought you cleverer—for, depend upon it a lucky guess is never merely luck.", "\"운 좋게 알아맞히는 기쁨과 통쾌함을 한 번도 못 느껴 보셨어요?"], ["There is always some talent in it.", "가엾기도 하셔라."], ["And as to my poor word ‘success,’ which you quarrel with, I do not know that I am so entirely without any claim to it.", "더 영리하신 줄 알았는데요 — 정말이지, 운 좋은 짐작은 결코 그저 운만은 아니거든요. 거기엔 언제나 얼마간의 재능이 들어 있어요."], ["You have drawn two pretty pictures; but I think there may be a third—a something between the do-nothing and the do-all.", "그리고 트집을 잡으시는 제 가련한 '성공'이라는 말로 말하자면, 제가 그 말을 쓸 자격이 아주 없지는 않은 것 같은데요. 두 폭의 그럴듯한 그림을 그려 주셨지만, 세 번째 그림도 있을 수 있다고 생각해요 — 아무것도 안 하는 쪽과 다 하는 쪽, 그 중간 어디쯤요."], ["If I had not promoted Mr. Weston’s visits here, and given many little encouragements, and smoothed many little matters, it might not have come to any thing after all.", "제가 미스터 웨스턴이 우리 집에 드나들도록 부추기지 않았다면, 자잘한 격려를 숱하게 보태고 자잘한 일들을 숱하게 매만지지 않았다면, 결국 아무 일도 안 됐을지 몰라요."], ["I think you must know Hartfield enough to comprehend that.”", "하트필드를 아실 만큼 아시는 분이면 그쯤은 아실 텐데요.\""]], [["“A straightforward, open-hearted man like Weston, and a rational, unaffected woman like Miss Taylor, may be safely left to manage their own concerns.", "\"웨스턴처럼 시원시원하고 흉금 없는 남자와 미스 테일러처럼 분별 있고 꾸밈없는 여자라면, 제 일은 제게 맡겨 두어도 안심이야."], ["You are more likely to have done harm to yourself, than good to them, by interference.”", "끼어들어 봐야 그들에게 득이 되기보다 자네 자신에게 해가 되기 십상이지.\""]], [["“Emma never thinks of herself, if she can do good to others,”", "\"엠마는 남에게 좋은 일을 할 수 있다면 제 생각은 도통 안 하는 아이라오.\" 미스터 우드하우스가 절반쯤만 알아듣고 거들었다."], ["rejoined Mr. Woodhouse, understanding but in part.", "\"하지만 얘야, 부디 더는 중매를 서지 말아 다오."], ["“But, my dear, pray do not make any more matches; they are silly things, and break up one’s family circle grievously.”", "그건 어리석은 짓이야. 한 집안의 단란함을 무참히 깨뜨리는 일이란다.\""]], [["“Only one more, papa; only for Mr.", "\"한 번만 더요, 아버지."], ["Elton. Poor Mr.", "미스터 엘턴 것만요."], ["Elton! You like Mr.", "가엾은 미스터 엘턴!"], ["Elton, papa,—I must look about for a wife for him.", "아버지도 미스터 엘턴을 좋아하시잖아요 — 그분 아내가 될 사람을 제가 찾아봐야 해요. 하이베리에는 그분에게 어울리는 사람이 없어요."], ["There is nobody in Highbury who deserves him—and he has been here a whole year, and has fitted up his house so comfortably, that it would be a shame to have him single any longer—and I thought when he was joining their hands to-day, he looked so very much as if he would like to have the same kind office done for him!", "여기 온 지 벌써 만 일 년에, 집도 그렇게 아늑하게 꾸며 놓으셨는데, 더 오래 혼자 두는 건 면목 없는 일이죠. 오늘 두 사람의 손을 맞잡게 해 주실 때 보니, 당신도 누가 똑같이 해 드리면 좋겠다는 얼굴이 역력하시던걸요!"], ["I think very well of Mr.", "저는 미스터 엘턴을 아주 좋게 생각해요."], ["Elton, and this is the only way I have of doing him a service.”", "그리고 이게 제가 그분께 보답할 수 있는 유일한 길이고요.\""]], [["“Mr. Elton is a very pretty young man, to be sure, and a very good young man, and I have a great regard for him.", "\"미스터 엘턴이야 아주 훤한 젊은이고말고."], ["But if you want to shew him any attention, my dear, ask him to come and dine with us some day.", "아주 착실한 젊은이지. 나도 그 사람을 무척 아낀단다."], ["That will be a much better thing. I dare say Mr.", "하지만 얘야, 그 사람한테 마음을 써 주고 싶거든 언제 한번 저녁이나 들러 오라고 하려무나."], ["Knightley will be so kind as to meet him.”", "그게 훨씬 나은 일이야. 미스터 나이틀리도 고맙게 자리를 함께해 주시겠지.\""]], [["“With a great deal of pleasure, sir, at any time,” said Mr.", "\"언제든 기꺼이 그러지요.\""], ["Knightley, laughing, “and I agree with you entirely, that it will be a much better thing.", "미스터 나이틀리가 웃으며 말했다. \"그리고 그게 훨씬 나은 일이라는 데 전적으로 동감입니다."], ["Invite him to dinner, Emma, and help him to the best of the fish and the chicken, but leave him to chuse his own wife.", "엠마, 저녁에 초대해서 생선과 닭고기 가장 좋은 부위를 덜어 주게. 다만 아내는 본인이 고르게 두고."], ["Depend upon it, a man of six or seven-and-twenty can take care of himself.”", "알아 두게, 스물예닐곱 먹은 남자는 제 앞가림은 할 줄 안다네.\""]], [["# Volume I — Chapter II", "# 제1권 2장"]], [["Mr. Weston was a native of Highbury, and born of a respectable family, which for the last two or three generations had been rising into gentility and property.", "미스터 웨스턴은 하이베리 토박이로, 지난 두세 대에 걸쳐 신사 계급의 지위와 재산을 점차 쌓아 온 점잖은 집안에서 태어났다."], ["He had received a good education, but, on succeeding early in life to a small independence, had become indisposed for any of the more homely pursuits in which his brothers were engaged, and had satisfied an active, cheerful mind and social temper by entering into the militia of his county, then embodied.", "그는 좋은 교육을 받았으나, 젊어서 일찌감치 얼마 안 되는 독립적 재산을 물려받게 되자, 형제들이 종사하던 보다 소박한 생업 따위에는 흥미를 잃고 말았다. 그리하여 활달하고 명랑한 마음과 사교적인 기질을 만족시키려고, 당시 소집되어 있던 고장의 민병대에 들어갔다."]], [["Captain Weston was a general favourite; and when the chances of his military life had introduced him to Miss Churchill, of a great Yorkshire family, and Miss Churchill fell in love with him, nobody was surprized, except her brother and his wife, who had never seen him, and who were full of pride and importance, which the connexion would offend.", "웨스턴 대위는 누구에게나 사랑받는 사람이었다. 군 생활의 우연이 그를 요크셔의 명문가 출신인 미스 처칠과 만나게 했고, 미스 처칠이 그에게 반했을 때, 그를 한 번도 본 적 없으면서 자긍심과 위세가 대단하여 이런 인연이 그 자존심을 건드릴 그녀의 오라버니와 올케를 제외하고는 아무도 놀라지 않았다."]], [["Miss Churchill, however, being of age, and with the full command of her fortune—though her fortune bore no proportion to the family-estate—was not to be dissuaded from the marriage, and it took place, to the infinite mortification of Mr. and Mrs.", "그러나 미스 처칠은 성년인 데다 제 재산을 마음대로 다룰 수 있었던지라 — 그 재산이 집안의 영지에 견줄 만한 것은 결코 아니었지만 — 이 혼인을 단념시킬 수 없었고, 마침내 식이 치러졌으니, 미스터 처칠과 처칠 부인으로서는 이만저만 분한 일이 아니어서 격식에 맞추어 그녀와 의절했다."], ["Churchill, who threw her off with due decorum. It was an unsuitable connexion, and did not produce much happiness.", "어울리지 않는 인연이었고, 그리 많은 행복을 가져다주지도 못했다."], ["Mrs. Weston ought to have found more in it, for she had a husband whose warm heart and sweet temper made him think every thing due to her in return for the great goodness of being in love with him; but though she had one sort of spirit, she had not the best.", "웨스턴 부인은 그 안에서 더 많은 행복을 찾았어야 마땅했으니, 따뜻한 마음과 다정한 성품으로 그녀가 자기를 사랑해 주는 그 크나큰 은혜에 보답하느라 무엇이든 다 해 주어야 한다고 여기는 남편을 두었기 때문이다."], ["She had resolution enough to pursue her own will in spite of her brother, but not enough to refrain from unreasonable regrets at that brother’s unreasonable anger, nor from missing the luxuries of her former home.", "그러나 그녀에게는 어떤 종류의 기개는 있었으되, 가장 훌륭한 기개는 없었다. 오라버니의 반대를 무릅쓰고 제 뜻을 밀고 나갈 결단력은 있었으나, 그 오라버니의 부당한 노여움에 부당한 회한을 품지 않을 만큼은 못 되었고, 옛집의 호사를 그리워하지 않을 만큼도 못 되었다."], ["They lived beyond their income, but still it was nothing in comparison of Enscombe: she did not cease to love her husband, but she wanted at once to be the wife of Captain Weston, and Miss Churchill of Enscombe.", "두 사람은 수입 이상으로 살았으나, 그래도 엔스컴에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀는 남편을 사랑하기를 그치지 않았으나, 웨스턴 대위의 아내이면서 동시에 엔스컴의 미스 처칠이고도 싶어 했다."]], [["Captain Weston, who had been considered, especially by the Churchills, as making such an amazing match, was proved to have much the worst of the bargain; for when his wife died, after a three years’ marriage, he was rather a poorer man than at first, and with a child to maintain.", "웨스턴 대위는, 특히 처칠 집안 사람들에게는 놀라운 혼처를 잡은 사람으로 여겨졌으나, 정작 거래에서 훨씬 손해를 본 쪽으로 드러났다. 결혼한 지 삼 년 만에 아내가 죽었을 때, 그는 처음보다 오히려 가난해진 데다 부양해야 할 아이까지 딸린 처지였기 때문이다."], ["From the expense of the child, however, he was soon relieved.", "그렇지만 아이를 키우는 비용에서는 곧 벗어났다."], ["The boy had, with the additional softening claim of a lingering illness of his mother’s, been the means of a sort of reconciliation; and Mr.", "사내아이는, 어머니의 길고 더딘 병환이라는 마음 누그러뜨리는 명분까지 더해져, 일종의 화해의 매개가 되었다."], ["and Mrs. Churchill, having no children of their own, nor any other young creature of equal kindred to care for, offered to take the whole charge of the little Frank soon after her decease.", "자기 자식이 없고, 같은 핏줄로 보살필 만한 다른 어린것도 없던 미스터 처칠과 처칠 부인은, 그녀가 세상을 뜬 지 얼마 되지 않아 어린 프랭크를 통째로 떠맡겠다고 나섰다."], ["Some scruples and some reluctance the widower-father may be supposed to have felt; but as they were overcome by other considerations, the child was given up to the care and the wealth of the Churchills, and he had only his own comfort to seek, and his own situation to improve as he could.", "홀아비가 된 아버지로서는 얼마간의 거리낌과 망설임을 느꼈으리라 짐작할 만하다. 그러나 다른 사정들에 밀려 그 마음이 누그러지자, 아이는 처칠 집안의 보살핌과 재산에 맡겨졌고, 그에게는 오직 제 한 몸의 안락을 도모하고 제 처지를 형편 닿는 대로 나아지게 하는 일만 남았다."]], [["A complete change of life became desirable.", "삶을 송두리째 바꾸는 것이 바람직해졌다."], ["He quitted the militia and engaged in trade, having brothers already established in a good way in London, which afforded him a favourable opening.", "그는 민병대를 떠나 장사에 뛰어들었는데, 형제들이 이미 런던에서 자리를 잘 잡아 두어 그에게 유리한 길을 열어 주었다."], ["It was a concern which brought just employment enough.", "적당한 일거리를 가져다주는 정도의 사업이었다."], ["He had still a small house in Highbury, where most of his leisure days were spent; and between useful occupation and the pleasures of society, the next eighteen or twenty years of his life passed cheerfully away.", "그는 여전히 하이베리에 작은 집 한 채를 두고, 한가한 날들을 대개 그곳에서 보냈다. 그렇게 쓸모 있는 일과 사교의 즐거움 사이에서, 그 뒤 십팔 년인가 이십 년의 세월이 명랑하게 흘러갔다."], ["He had, by that time, realised an easy competence—enough to secure the purchase of a little estate adjoining Highbury, which he had always longed for—enough to marry a woman as portionless even as Miss Taylor, and to live according to the wishes of his own friendly and social disposition.", "그 무렵이 되자 그는 어지간한 재산을 일구어 두었으니 — 늘 마음에 두고 있던 하이베리 인근의 작은 영지를 사들이기에 넉넉하고 — 미스 테일러만큼이나 지참금 없는 여인을 아내로 맞고도, 다정하고 사교적인 제 성향에 맞추어 살아가기에 넉넉했다."]], [["It was now some time since Miss Taylor had begun to influence his schemes; but as it was not the tyrannic influence of youth on youth, it had not shaken his determination of never settling till he could purchase Randalls, and the sale of Randalls was long looked forward to; but he had gone steadily on, with these objects in view, till they were accomplished. He had made his fortune, bought his house, and obtained his wife; and was beginning a new period of existence, with every probability of greater happiness than in any yet passed through. He had never been an unhappy man; his own temper had secured him from that, even in his first marriage; but his second must shew him how delightful a well-judging and truly amiable woman could be, and must give him the pleasantest proof of its being a great deal better to choose than to be chosen, to excite gratitude than to feel it.", "미스 테일러가 그의 계획에 영향을 미치기 시작한 지도 어느덧 얼마간 되었다. 그러나 그것이 젊은이가 젊은이에게 휘두르는 횡포 같은 영향은 아니었으므로, 랜덜스를 사들이기 전에는 절대로 살림을 차리지 않겠다는 그의 결심을 흔들어 놓지는 못했고, 랜덜스가 매물로 나오기를 오래도록 기다려야 했다. 그러나 그는 이 목표들을 눈앞에 두고 꾸준히 나아가 마침내 그것들을 이루었다. 재산을 일구었고, 집을 사들였으며, 아내를 얻었다. 그리하여 그는 인생의 새로운 시기를 시작하려는 참이었으니, 지금껏 지나온 어느 때보다도 더 큰 행복을 누릴 공산이 다분했다. 그는 한 번도 불행한 사람이었던 적이 없었다. 첫 결혼 때조차 그의 천성이 그를 그런 데서 지켜 주었다. 그러나 두 번째 결혼은 분별 있고 진실로 사랑스러운 여인이 얼마나 즐거운 존재인지 그에게 보여 줄 터였고, 선택받기보다 선택하는 편이, 고마움을 느끼기보다 고마움을 불러일으키는 편이 훨씬 더 낫다는 가장 흐뭇한 증거를 안겨 줄 터였다."]], [["He had only himself to please in his choice: his fortune was his own; for as to Frank, it was more than being tacitly brought up as his uncle’s heir, it had become so avowed an adoption as to have him assume the name of Churchill on coming of age.", "그가 자기 선택에서 만족시켜야 할 사람은 오직 자기 자신뿐이었다. 재산도 온전히 그의 것이었다."], ["It was most unlikely, therefore, that he should ever want his father’s assistance. His father had no apprehension of it.", "프랭크로 말하자면, 그저 외삼촌의 상속자로 암묵리에 길러지는 정도를 넘어, 성년이 되면 처칠이라는 성을 따르게 할 만큼 공공연한 양자로 인정된 처지였기 때문이다."], ["The aunt was a capricious woman, and governed her husband entirely; but it was not in Mr.", "그러니 그가 아버지의 도움을 필요로 할 일은 거의 없을 듯했다. 아버지도 그런 걱정은 없었다."], ["Weston’s nature to imagine that any caprice could be strong enough to affect one so dear, and, as he believed, so deservedly dear.", "외숙모는 변덕스러운 여자로 남편을 완전히 휘어잡고 있었으나, 그토록 사랑스럽고, 또 그가 믿기로 마땅히 사랑받을 만한 아이에게 그 어떤 변덕인들 영향을 미칠 만큼 강할 수 있다고는, 미스터 웨스턴의 천성으로는 도무지 상상이 되지 않았다."], ["He saw his son every year in London, and was proud of him; and his fond report of him as a very fine young man had made Highbury feel a sort of pride in him too.", "그는 해마다 런던에서 아들을 만났고 그를 자랑스러워했다. 그가 아주 훌륭한 청년이라고 흐뭇하게 전하는 통에, 하이베리 사람들도 그를 두고 일종의 자랑스러움을 느끼게 되었다."], ["He was looked on as sufficiently belonging to the place to make his merits and prospects a kind of common concern.", "그는 이 고장에 속한 사람으로 충분히 여겨졌으므로, 그의 장점과 앞날은 일종의 공동의 관심사가 되었다."]], [["Mr. Frank Churchill was one of the boasts of Highbury, and a lively curiosity to see him prevailed, though the compliment was so little returned that he had never been there in his life.", "미스터 프랭크 처칠은 하이베리의 자랑거리 가운데 하나였고, 그를 한번 보고 싶다는 활기찬 호기심이 널리 퍼져 있었다."], ["His coming to visit his father had been often talked of but never achieved.", "정작 그 호의에 대한 보답은 거의 없어서, 그는 평생 단 한 번도 이곳에 온 적이 없었지만 말이다. 아버지를 보러 온다는 이야기는 자주 오갔으나, 한 번도 실현되지 않았다."]], [["Now, upon his father’s marriage, it was very generally proposed, as a most proper attention, that the visit should take place.", "이제 아버지가 결혼한 마당이니, 그 방문이 마땅히 행해져야 할 더없이 적절한 도리라는 의견이 두루 나왔다."], ["There was not a dissentient voice on the subject, either when Mrs. Perry drank tea with Mrs.", "페리 부인이 베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠와 차를 마실 때든, 베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠가 그 답방을 갔을 때든, 이 문제에 대해 이견을 내는 목소리는 하나도 없었다."], ["and Miss Bates, or when Mrs. and Miss Bates returned the visit.", "이제야말로 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 그들 사이에 와야 할 때였다."], ["Now was the time for Mr. Frank Churchill to come among them; and the hope strengthened when it was understood that he had written to his new mother on the occasion.", "그리고 그가 이 일을 맞아 새어머니에게 편지를 썼다는 사실이 알려지자 그 기대는 한층 굳어졌다."], ["For a few days, every morning visit in Highbury included some mention of the handsome letter Mrs. Weston had received.", "며칠 동안 하이베리에서 오가는 아침 방문마다, 웨스턴 부인이 받은 그 훌륭한 편지 이야기가 빠지지 않았다."], ["“I suppose you have heard of the handsome letter Mr. Frank Churchill has written to Mrs.", "\"미스터 프랭크 처칠이 웨스턴 부인에게 보낸 그 훌륭한 편지 이야기는 들으셨겠지요?"], ["Weston? I understand it was a very handsome letter, indeed.", "정말이지 아주 훌륭한 편지였다더군요."], ["Mr. Woodhouse told me of it.", "미스터 우드하우스가 제게 말씀해 주셨어요."], ["Mr. Woodhouse saw the letter, and he says he never saw such a handsome letter in his life.”", "미스터 우드하우스가 그 편지를 직접 보셨는데, 평생 그렇게 훌륭한 편지는 본 적이 없다고 하시던걸요.\""]], [["It was, indeed, a highly prized letter. Mrs.", "그것은 과연 대단히 귀하게 여겨진 편지였다."], ["Weston had, of course, formed a very favourable idea of the young man; and such a pleasing attention was an irresistible proof of his great good sense, and a most welcome addition to every source and every expression of congratulation which her marriage had already secured.", "웨스턴 부인은 물론 그 청년에 대해 매우 호의적인 인상을 품게 되었다. 그토록 흐뭇한 배려야말로 그의 뛰어난 분별을 보여 주는 거역할 수 없는 증거였고, 그녀의 결혼이 이미 확보해 둔 온갖 축하의 원천과 표현에 더없이 반가운 보탬이 되었다."], ["She felt herself a most fortunate woman; and she had lived long enough to know how fortunate she might well be thought, where the only regret was for a partial separation from friends whose friendship for her had never cooled, and who could ill bear to part with her.", "그녀는 스스로를 더없이 운 좋은 여인이라 느꼈다. 그리고 자기가 어찌 그토록 운 좋게 여겨질 만한지를 알 만큼 살아온 터였으니, 유일한 아쉬움이라면 자신에 대한 우정이 결코 식은 적 없고 자신과 헤어지기를 못내 아쉬워하는 벗들과 얼마간 떨어지게 된 것뿐이었기 때문이다."]], [["She knew that at times she must be missed; and could not think, without pain, of Emma’s losing a single pleasure, or suffering an hour’s ennui, from the want of her companionableness: but dear Emma was of no feeble character; she was more equal to her situation than most girls would have been, and had sense, and energy, and spirits that might be hoped would bear her well and happily through its little difficulties and privations.", "그녀는 때때로 사람들이 자기를 그리워하리라는 것을 알았다. 그리고 자기가 곁에 있어 주지 못해 엠마가 단 하나의 즐거움이라도 잃거나 한 시간이라도 따분함에 시달릴 일을 생각하면 마음이 아팠다."], ["And then there was such comfort in the very easy distance of Randalls from Hartfield, so convenient for even solitary female walking, and in Mr.", "그러나 사랑스러운 엠마는 결코 나약한 성품이 아니었다. 그녀는 여느 아가씨들보다 제 처지를 잘 감당할 만한 아이였고, 분별과 활력과 기운을 갖추어, 그 자잘한 어려움과 결핍을 무던하고 행복하게 헤쳐 나가리라 기대할 만했다."], ["Weston’s disposition and circumstances, which would make the approaching season no hindrance to their spending half the evenings in the week together.", "게다가 랜덜스가 하트필드에서 아주 만만한 거리에 있어, 여자 혼자 걷기에도 무리가 없다는 점에는 그렇게 위안이 되었고, 미스터 웨스턴의 성품과 형편 또한 그러하여, 다가오는 계절에도 일주일에 절반의 저녁은 함께 보내는 데 아무런 지장이 없을 터였다."]], [["Her situation was altogether the subject of hours of gratitude to Mrs.", "그녀의 처지는 통틀어 웨스턴 부인에게 몇 시간씩 이어지는 감사의 까닭이 되었고, 아쉬움이라야 잠깐잠깐의 순간뿐이었다."], ["Weston, and of moments only of regret; and her satisfaction—her more than satisfaction—her cheerful enjoyment, was so just and so apparent, that Emma, well as she knew her father, was sometimes taken by surprize at his being still able to pity ‘poor Miss Taylor,’ when they left her at Randalls in the centre of every domestic comfort, or saw her go away in the evening attended by her pleasant husband to a carriage of her own.", "그녀의 만족 — 만족 이상의 것 — 그 명랑한 즐거움이 어찌나 온당하고 또렷한지, 엠마는 아버지를 잘 알면서도, 온갖 안락의 한복판인 랜덜스에 그녀를 두고 올 때나, 저녁이면 유쾌한 남편의 시중을 받으며 제 마차에 오르는 그녀를 볼 때, 아버지가 여전히 \"가엾은 미스 테일러\"라며 가여워할 수 있다는 데에 이따금 놀라곤 했다. 그러나 그녀가 떠날 때면 미스터 우드하우스는 으레 나직이 한숨을 쉬며 이렇게 말하지 않는 법이 없었다."], ["But never did she go without Mr.", "\"아아, 가엾은 미스 테일러!"], ["Woodhouse’s giving a gentle sigh, and saying, “Ah, poor Miss Taylor! She would be very glad to stay.”", "저 사람은 여기 머물고 싶어 할 텐데.\""]], [["There was no recovering Miss Taylor—nor much likelihood of ceasing to pity her; but a few weeks brought some alleviation to Mr.", "미스 테일러를 되찾을 길은 없었고, 그녀를 가여워하기를 그칠 가망도 별로 없었다. 그러나 몇 주가 지나자 미스터 우드하우스에게도 얼마간 위안이 찾아왔다."], ["Woodhouse. The compliments of his neighbours were over; he was no longer teased by being wished joy of so sorrowful an event; and the wedding-cake, which had been a great distress to him, was all eat up.", "이웃들의 인사치레도 끝났고, 그토록 슬픈 일을 두고 기뻐하라는 덕담에 시달릴 일도 더는 없었으며, 그를 큰 고통에 빠뜨렸던 그 혼례 케이크도 죄다 먹어 치워졌다."], ["His own stomach could bear nothing rich, and he could never believe other people to be different from himself.", "그 자신의 위장은 기름진 것을 하나도 받아들이지 못했고, 그는 남들이 자기와 다를 수 있다고는 도무지 믿지 못했다."], ["What was unwholesome to him he regarded as unfit for any body; and he had, therefore, earnestly tried to dissuade them from having any wedding-cake at all, and when that proved vain, as earnestly tried to prevent any body’s eating it. He had been at the pains of consulting Mr.", "자기에게 해로운 것이면 그 누구에게도 마땅찮은 것이라 여겼으므로, 그는 혼례 케이크 따위는 아예 만들지 말라고 간곡히 만류하려 애썼고, 그것이 헛수고로 돌아가자, 이번에는 아무도 그것을 먹지 못하게 하려고 한사코 애를 썼다."], ["Perry, the apothecary, on the subject. Mr.", "그는 이 문제를 두고 약제사 미스터 페리에게 의견을 구하는 수고까지 마다하지 않았다."], ["Perry was an intelligent, gentlemanlike man, whose frequent visits were one of the comforts of Mr. Woodhouse’s life; and upon being applied to, he could not but acknowledge (though it seemed rather against the bias of inclination) that wedding-cake might certainly disagree with many—perhaps with most people, unless taken moderately.", "미스터 페리는 총명하고 신사다운 사람으로, 그가 자주 드나드는 것이 미스터 우드하우스의 삶의 위안 가운데 하나였는데, 막상 물음을 받자 (자기 뜻과는 다소 어긋나는 듯 보였음에도) 인정하지 않을 수 없었으니, 혼례 케이크란 적당히 먹지 않는 한 많은 사람에게 — 어쩌면 대다수에게 — 분명 탈을 낼 수 있다는 것이었다."], ["With such an opinion, in confirmation of his own, Mr.", "자기 견해를 뒷받침하는 이런 소견을 손에 넣은 미스터 우드하우스는, 그 신혼부부를 찾는 손님마다 이를 일러 영향을 미치고 싶어 했다."], ["Woodhouse hoped to influence every visitor of the newly married pair; but still the cake was eaten; and there was no rest for his benevolent nerves till it was all gone.", "그러나 케이크는 그래도 다 먹혔고, 그것이 죄다 없어지기까지 그의 어진 신경에는 도무지 안식이 없었다."]], [["There was a strange rumour in Highbury of all the little Perrys being seen with a slice of Mrs.", "하이베리에는 페리네 어린것들이 저마다 웨스턴 부인의 혼례 케이크를 한 조각씩 손에 들고 있는 것을 보았다는 이상한 소문이 돌았다."], ["Weston’s wedding-cake in their hands: but Mr. Woodhouse would never believe it.", "그러나 미스터 우드하우스는 결코 그 말을 믿으려 들지 않았다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter III", "# 제1권 3장"]], [["Mr. Woodhouse was fond of society in his own way.", "미스터 우드하우스는 그 나름의 방식으로 사람들과 어울리기를 좋아했다."], ["He liked very much to have his friends come and see him; and from various united causes, from his long residence at Hartfield, and his good nature, from his fortune, his house, and his daughter, he could command the visits of his own little circle, in a great measure, as he liked.", "벗들이 찾아와 만나는 것을 무척 즐겼는데, 여러 가지가 한데 어우러진 까닭에 — 하트필드에서 오래 살아온 데다 천성이 너그러웠고, 거기에 재산과 저택과 딸까지 더해진 덕에 — 자신의 작은 교우 모임의 방문만큼은 대개 제 뜻대로 청할 수 있었다."], ["He had not much intercourse with any families beyond that circle; his horror of late hours, and large dinner-parties, made him unfit for any acquaintance but such as would visit him on his own terms.", "그 모임 너머의 어느 집안과도 그는 그리 왕래가 없었다. 늦은 시간과 큰 만찬을 질색하는 탓에, 그가 정한 조건대로 찾아와 줄 사람이 아니고서는 누구와도 사귈 수 없었던 것이다."], ["Fortunately for him, Highbury, including Randalls in the same parish, and Donwell Abbey in the parish adjoining, the seat of Mr. Knightley, comprehended many such.", "다행히도 하이베리는 — 같은 교구에 속한 랜덜스와, 미스터 나이틀리의 영지인 이웃 교구 돈웰 애비까지 아울러 — 그런 사람을 여럿 품고 있었다."], ["Not unfrequently, through Emma’s persuasion, he had some of the chosen and the best to dine with him: but evening parties were what he preferred; and, unless he fancied himself at any time unequal to company, there was scarcely an evening in the week in which Emma could not make up a card-table for him.", "엠마가 권하는 덕에 그는 골라 뽑은 가장 좋은 손님 몇을 만찬에 부르는 일도 드물지 않았으나, 그가 더 좋아한 것은 저녁 모임이었다. 그리고 그가 어쩌다 사람 만날 기운이 없다 싶지 않는 한, 일주일 가운데 엠마가 그를 위해 카드판 하나 짜 주지 못할 저녁은 거의 없었다."]], [["Real, long-standing regard brought the Westons and Mr. Knightley; and by Mr.", "웨스턴 부부와 미스터 나이틀리는 진실하고 오래된 정으로 찾아왔다."], ["Elton, a young man living alone without liking it, the privilege of exchanging any vacant evening of his own blank solitude for the elegancies and society of Mr. Woodhouse’s drawing-room, and the smiles of his lovely daughter, was in no danger of being thrown away.", "그리고 혼자 사는 것을 달가워하지 않는 청년 미스터 엘턴으로 말하자면, 텅 빈 고독으로 비어 있는 제 저녁 한때를 미스터 우드하우스의 응접실의 우아함과 사교, 그리고 그 사랑스러운 딸의 미소와 맞바꾸는 특권을 허투루 흘려보낼 까닭이 없었다."]], [["After these came a second set; among the most come-at-able of whom were Mrs. and Miss Bates, and Mrs.", "이들 다음으로는 두 번째 부류가 있었으니, 그중에서도 가장 부르기 좋은 이들이 베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠, 그리고 고더드 부인 — 하트필드의 초대라면 거의 언제든 응할 채비가 되어 있는 세 여인이었다."], ["Goddard, three ladies almost always at the service of an invitation from Hartfield, and who were fetched and carried home so often, that Mr. Woodhouse thought it no hardship for either James or the horses.", "어찌나 자주 마차로 모셔 오고 모셔 가는지, 미스터 우드하우스는 그것이 제임스에게도 말들에게도 고된 일이 아니라고 여겼다."], ["Had it taken place only once a year, it would have been a grievance.", "그것이 일 년에 단 한 번뿐인 일이었다면 짐스럽게 느꼈을 테지만."]], [["Mrs. Bates, the widow of a former vicar of Highbury, was a very old lady, almost past every thing but tea and quadrille.", "베이츠 부인은 하이베리의 전임 교구 목사를 여읜 미망인으로, 차와 카드리유*를 빼면 거의 무엇도 할 수 없는 매우 늙은 부인이었다."], ["She lived with her single daughter in a very small way, and was considered with all the regard and respect which a harmless old lady, under such untoward circumstances, can excite.", "그녀는 시집 못 간 딸과 함께 아주 검소하게 살았고, 그처럼 딱한 처지에 놓인 무해한 노부인이 불러일으킬 만한 온갖 호의와 존경을 한 몸에 받았다."], ["Her daughter enjoyed a most uncommon degree of popularity for a woman neither young, handsome, rich, nor married.", "그 딸은 젊지도, 곱지도, 부유하지도, 결혼도 하지 않은 여자치고는 더없이 드물 만큼 인기를 누렸다."], ["Miss Bates stood in the very worst predicament in the world for having much of the public favour; and she had no intellectual superiority to make atonement to herself, or frighten those who might hate her into outward respect.", "미스 베이츠는 세상의 호의를 듬뿍 받기에는 이보다 더 나쁠 수 없는 처지에 놓여 있었다. 게다가 그 부족함을 스스로 메워 주거나, 그녀를 미워할지 모를 이들을 겉으로나마 굽히게 할 만한 지적 우월함도 없었다."], ["She had never boasted either beauty or cleverness.", "그녀는 미모도 영민함도 자랑해 본 적이 없었다."], ["Her youth had passed without distinction, and her middle of life was devoted to the care of a failing mother, and the endeavour to make a small income go as far as possible.", "젊은 날은 이렇다 할 두각 없이 흘러갔고, 중년은 쇠약해 가는 어머니를 돌보는 일과, 변변찮은 수입을 한 푼이라도 더 늘려 쓰려는 안간힘에 바쳐졌다. 그런데도 그녀는 행복한 여자였고, 누구도 호의 없이는 그 이름을 입에 올리지 않는 여자였다."], ["And yet she was a happy woman, and a woman whom no one named without good-will.", "그처럼 놀라운 일을 빚어낸 것은 다름 아닌 그녀 자신의 두루 미치는 선의와 만족할 줄 아는 마음이었다."], ["It was her own universal good-will and contented temper which worked such wonders.", "그녀는 모든 사람을 사랑했고, 모든 사람의 행복에 마음을 썼으며, 모든 사람의 장점을 재빨리 알아보았다."], ["She loved every body, was interested in every body’s happiness, quicksighted to every body’s merits; thought herself a most fortunate creature, and surrounded with blessings in such an excellent mother, and so many good neighbours and friends, and a home that wanted for nothing.", "자신을 더없이 운 좋은 사람으로 여겼고, 그토록 훌륭한 어머니와 그 많은 좋은 이웃과 벗, 그리고 무엇 하나 부족함 없는 보금자리로 둘러싸인 복받은 사람이라 생각했다. 그 천성의 꾸밈없음과 쾌활함, 만족할 줄 알고 감사할 줄 아는 마음은 누구에게나 그녀를 추천할 만한 것이었으며, 그녀 자신에게는 행복의 금광이었다."], ["The simplicity and cheerfulness of her nature, her contented and grateful spirit, were a recommendation to every body, and a mine of felicity to herself. She was a great talker upon little matters, which exactly suited Mr.", "그녀는 사소한 일을 두고 끝없이 떠드는 수다쟁이였는데, 별것 아닌 전언과 무해한 잡담으로 가득 찬 그 말솜씨가 미스터 우드하우스에게는 더할 나위 없이 잘 맞았다."], ["Woodhouse, full of trivial communications and harmless gossip.", "* 카드리유: 네 사람이 하는 옛 카드놀이."]], [["Mrs. Goddard was the mistress of a School—not of a seminary, or an establishment, or any thing which professed, in long sentences of refined nonsense, to combine liberal acquirements with elegant morality, upon new principles and new systems—and where young ladies for enormous pay might be screwed out of health and into vanity—but a real, honest, old-fashioned Boarding-school, where a reasonable quantity of accomplishments were sold at a reasonable price, and where girls might be sent to be out of the way, and scramble themselves into a little education, without any danger of coming back prodigies.", "고더드 부인은 어느 학교의 교장이었다 — 그러나 그것은 신식 원리니 신식 제도니 하며, 세련된 헛소리를 늘어놓는 긴 문장으로 교양과 우아한 도덕을 결합한다고 떠벌리는, 그리고 어린 아가씨들이 엄청난 수업료를 내고 건강을 쥐어짜내 허영심만 부풀려 가는 그런 학원이나 양성소 따위가 아니었다 — 그저 진짜배기의 정직하고 구식인 기숙학교로, 거기서는 적당한 양의 교양을 적당한 값에 팔았고, 거기로 보내진 여자아이들은 거추장스럽지 않게 떨어져 있으면서 약간의 교육을 그럭저럭 주워 담을 뿐, 무슨 신동이 되어 돌아올 위험 따위는 결코 없었다."], ["Mrs. Goddard’s school was in high repute—and very deservedly; for Highbury was reckoned a particularly healthy spot: she had an ample house and garden, gave the children plenty of wholesome food, let them run about a great deal in the summer, and in winter dressed their chilblains with her own hands.", "고더드 부인의 학교는 평판이 아주 좋았는데 — 마땅히 그럴 만했다."], ["It was no wonder that a train of twenty young couple now walked after her to church.", "하이베리는 유난히 건강에 좋은 곳으로 꼽혔으니 말이다."], ["She was a plain, motherly kind of woman, who had worked hard in her youth, and now thought herself entitled to the occasional holiday of a tea-visit; and having formerly owed much to Mr.", "그녀에게는 널찍한 집과 정원이 있었고, 아이들에게 건강에 좋은 음식을 넉넉히 먹였으며, 여름에는 마음껏 뛰놀게 했고, 겨울에는 손수 아이들의 동상을 싸매 주었다. 지금 스무 쌍의 처녀들이 줄지어 그녀를 뒤따라 교회에 가는 것도 놀랄 일이 아니었다."], ["Woodhouse’s kindness, felt his particular claim on her to leave her neat parlour, hung round with fancy-work, whenever she could, and win or lose a few sixpences by his fireside.", "그녀는 수수하고 어머니 같은 여인으로, 젊어서 모질게 일했고 이제는 가끔 차 모임에 나가 쉬어도 좋을 자격이 있다고 여겼다. 그리고 지난날 미스터 우드하우스의 친절에 많은 신세를 진 터라, 자수품으로 빙 둘러 꾸민 그 정갈한 거실을 형편 닿는 대로 마다하고 나와, 그의 난롯가에서 몇 닢의 동전을 따거나 잃어 주는 것을 그에 대한 각별한 도리로 여겼다."]], [["These were the ladies whom Emma found herself very frequently able to collect; and happy was she, for her father’s sake, in the power; though, as far as she was herself concerned, it was no remedy for the absence of Mrs.", "이들이 바로 엠마가 매우 자주 불러 모을 수 있었던 부인들이었다. 그녀는 아버지를 위해 그럴 힘이 있다는 것이 기뻤다."], ["Weston.", "다만 그녀 자신으로 말하자면, 그것이 웨스턴 부인의 부재를 달래 주는 약은 못 되었다."], ["She was delighted to see her father look comfortable, and very much pleased with herself for contriving things so well; but the quiet prosings of three such women made her feel that every evening so spent was indeed one of the long evenings she had fearfully anticipated.", "아버지가 편안해 보이는 것이 반가웠고, 일을 그토록 잘 꾸려 낸 자신이 무척 대견하기도 했다. 그러나 그런 세 여인의 잔잔한 푸념과 늘어놓는 이야기를 듣고 있노라면, 그렇게 보낸 저녁 하나하나가 그녀가 두려워하며 내다보았던 바로 그 기나긴 저녁임을 새삼 절감하게 되었다."]], [["As she sat one morning, looking forward to exactly such a close of the present day, a note was brought from Mrs.", "어느 날 아침 그녀가 앉아서 오늘 하루도 꼭 그렇게 저물겠거니 내다보고 있을 때, 고더드 부인에게서 쪽지 한 장이 왔다."], ["Goddard, requesting, in most respectful terms, to be allowed to bring Miss Smith with her; a most welcome request: for Miss Smith was a girl of seventeen, whom Emma knew very well by sight, and had long felt an interest in, on account of her beauty.", "더없이 정중한 말로 미스 스미스를 데려와도 좋겠느냐는 청이었다 — 더없이 반가운 청이었다. 미스 스미스는 열일곱 살 난 아가씨로, 엠마는 그 얼굴은 익히 알고 있었고, 그 미모 덕에 오래전부터 관심을 두어 온 터였다."], ["A very gracious invitation was returned, and the evening no longer dreaded by the fair mistress of the mansion.", "아주 흔쾌한 초대의 답이 갔고, 이 저택의 아름다운 안주인은 더 이상 그 저녁을 두려워하지 않게 되었다."]], [["Harriet Smith was the natural daughter of somebody.", "해리엇 스미스는 누군가의 사생아였다."], ["Somebody had placed her, several years back, at Mrs. Goddard’s school, and somebody had lately raised her from the condition of scholar to that of parlour-boarder.", "몇 해 전 누군가가 그녀를 고더드 부인의 학교에 들여보냈고, 근래에 누군가가 그녀를 일반 학생의 신분에서 거실 기숙생의 자리로 올려 주었다."], ["This was all that was generally known of her history.", "그녀의 내력에 관해 세상에 알려진 것은 그게 전부였다."], ["She had no visible friends but what had been acquired at Highbury, and was now just returned from a long visit in the country to some young ladies who had been at school there with her.", "하이베리에서 사귄 사람들 말고는 이렇다 할 친구도 없었으며, 지금은 그 학교에서 함께 지냈던 몇몇 아가씨를 만나러 시골에 오래 다녀온 길이었다."]], [["She was a very pretty girl, and her beauty happened to be of a sort which Emma particularly admired.", "그녀는 무척 예쁜 아가씨였고, 그 아름다움이 마침 엠마가 특히 좋아하는 부류의 것이었다."], ["She was short, plump, and fair, with a fine bloom, blue eyes, light hair, regular features, and a look of great sweetness, and, before the end of the evening, Emma was as much pleased with her manners as her person, and quite determined to continue the acquaintance.", "키가 자그마하고 통통하며 살결이 희고 발그레한 혈색에, 푸른 눈과 옅은 빛깔의 머리칼, 단정한 이목구비, 그리고 더없이 사랑스러운 표정을 지니고 있었다. 저녁이 다 가기도 전에 엠마는 그 용모만큼이나 몸가짐에도 흡족해져, 이 교제를 이어 가기로 단단히 마음먹었다."]], [["She was not struck by any thing remarkably clever in Miss Smith’s conversation, but she found her altogether very engaging—not inconveniently shy, not unwilling to talk—and yet so far from pushing, shewing so proper and becoming a deference, seeming so pleasantly grateful for being admitted to Hartfield, and so artlessly impressed by the appearance of every thing in so superior a style to what she had been used to, that she must have good sense, and deserve encouragement.", "미스 스미스의 대화에서 무슨 눈에 띄게 영리한 구석에 감탄한 것은 아니었으나, 엠마는 그녀가 통틀어 무척 마음을 끄는 사람임을 알았다 — 거북할 만큼 수줍어하지도 않고, 말하기를 꺼리지도 않으면서, 그러면서도 결코 나서는 법이 없고, 더없이 적절하고 어울리는 공손함을 보이며, 하트필드에 받아들여진 것을 그토록 사랑스럽게 고마워하고, 평소에 익숙했던 것보다 한층 격조 높은 만사의 풍경에 그토록 꾸밈없이 감탄하는 모습이었다. 그러니 분별이 있는 게 분명하고, 북돋아 줄 만한 사람이었다."], ["Encouragement should be given.", "마땅히 북돋아 주어야 했다."], ["Those soft blue eyes, and all those natural graces, should not be wasted on the inferior society of Highbury and its connexions.", "저 부드러운 푸른 눈과 저 모든 타고난 우아함을, 하이베리와 그 언저리의 변변찮은 사교에 헛되이 흘려보내서는 안 될 일이었다."], ["The acquaintance she had already formed were unworthy of her.", "그녀가 이미 맺은 교제는 그녀에게 어울리지 않았다."], ["The friends from whom she had just parted, though very good sort of people, must be doing her harm.", "그녀가 방금 헤어지고 온 그 친구들은, 비록 퍽 괜찮은 사람들이라 해도, 그녀에게 해가 되고 있는 게 분명했다."], ["They were a family of the name of Martin, whom Emma well knew by character, as renting a large farm of Mr. Knightley, and residing in the parish of Donwell—very creditably, she believed—she knew Mr.", "그들은 마틴이라는 집안 사람들로, 엠마는 그 평판을 익히 알고 있었으니 — 미스터 나이틀리의 큰 농장을 빌려 돈웰 교구에서 사는 이들이었고, 그녀가 보기에 꽤 떳떳한 살림이었으며, 미스터 나이틀리가 그들을 높이 친다는 것도 알고 있었다 — 그래도 그들은 거칠고 다듬어지지 않은 사람들임이 분명했고, 조금만 더 지식과 우아함을 갖추면 흠잡을 데 없이 완벽해질 아가씨와 가깝게 지내기에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 이들이었다."], ["Knightley thought highly of them—but they must be coarse and unpolished, and very unfit to be the intimates of a girl who wanted only a little more knowledge and elegance to be quite perfect.", "자기가 그녀를 보살펴 주리라. 자기가 그녀를 다듬어 주리라."], ["She would notice her; she would improve her; she would detach her from her bad acquaintance, and introduce her into good society; she would form her opinions and her manners.", "그 못된 교제에서 떼어 내어 좋은 사교계로 이끌어 들이리라. 그녀의 견해와 몸가짐을 자기가 빚어 주리라."], ["It would be an interesting, and certainly a very kind undertaking; highly becoming her own situation in life, her leisure, and powers.", "흥미로운 일이요, 분명히 더없이 친절한 사업이 될 터였다. 그녀 자신의 생활 형편과 여유와 능력에 썩 잘 어울리는 일이었다."]], [["She was so busy in admiring those soft blue eyes, in talking and listening, and forming all these schemes in the in-betweens, that the evening flew away at a very unusual rate; and the supper-table, which always closed such parties, and for which she had been used to sit and watch the due time, was all set out and ready, and moved forwards to the fire, before she was aware.", "그녀는 그 부드러운 푸른 눈에 감탄하랴, 이야기하고 또 들으랴, 그 사이사이로 이 모든 계획을 짜내랴 어찌나 분주했던지, 저녁나절이 여느 때와 사뭇 다른 속도로 휙 지나가 버렸다. 늘 그런 모임의 끝을 알리며 차려지던 야식상은 — 평소 같으면 적당한 때를 가늠하며 지켜보고 앉아 있던 그 상은 — 어느새 다 차려져 난로 쪽으로 옮겨질 때까지도 그녀는 미처 알아채지 못했다."], ["With an alacrity beyond the common impulse of a spirit which yet was never indifferent to the credit of doing every thing well and attentively, with the real good-will of a mind delighted with its own ideas, did she then do all the honours of the meal, and help and recommend the minced chicken and scalloped oysters, with an urgency which she knew would be acceptable to the early hours and civil scruples of their guests.", "매사를 잘, 빈틈없이 해내는 평판에는 결코 무심하지 않으면서도, 평소의 충동을 넘어서는 민첩함으로, 제 생각에 스스로 흐뭇해하는 마음에서 우러난 진정한 선의로, 그녀는 그제야 식사 자리의 온갖 시중을 도맡았다. 다진 닭고기와 굴 그라탕을 손님들에게 권하며 덜어 주었는데, 그 권하는 정성이 손님들의 이른 잠자리 습관과 예의 바른 사양심에도 기꺼이 받아들여지리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다."]], [["Upon such occasions poor Mr.", "이런 자리에서 가엾은 미스터 우드하우스의 심사는 안타까운 싸움을 벌였다."], ["Woodhouse’s feelings were in sad warfare.", "그는 식탁보를 까는 것을 좋아했으니, 그것이 그의 젊은 시절 풍습이었기 때문이다."], ["He loved to have the cloth laid, because it had been the fashion of his youth, but his conviction of suppers being very unwholesome made him rather sorry to see any thing put on it; and while his hospitality would have welcomed his visitors to every thing, his care for their health made him grieve that they would eat.", "그러나 야식이 건강에 매우 해롭다는 그의 신념 탓에, 그 위에 무엇이 차려지는 것을 보면 도리어 마음이 언짢았다. 손님 대접하는 마음으로야 무엇이든 다 내놓고 싶었지만, 그들의 건강을 염려하는 마음에 그들이 먹는다는 것 자체가 안타까웠던 것이다."]], [["Such another small basin of thin gruel as his own was all that he could, with thorough self-approbation, recommend; though he might constrain himself, while the ladies were comfortably clearing the nicer things, to say:", "자기가 드는 것과 똑같은, 그런 묽은 죽 한 그릇 정도라야 그가 떳떳한 마음으로 권할 수 있는 전부였다. 그래도 부인들이 더 좋은 음식들을 흐뭇하게 비워 내는 동안, 그는 애써 자신을 다잡으며 이렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["“Mrs. Bates, let me propose your venturing on one of these eggs.", "\"베이츠 부인, 이 달걀을 하나 드셔 보시지요."], ["An egg boiled very soft is not unwholesome.", "아주 살짝 삶은 달걀은 해롭지 않답니다."], ["Serle understands boiling an egg better than any body.", "설이 달걀 삶는 솜씨는 누구보다 낫지요."], ["I would not recommend an egg boiled by any body else; but you need not be afraid, they are very small, you see—one of our small eggs will not hurt you.", "다른 사람이 삶은 달걀이라면 권하지 않겠습니다만, 염려 마세요, 보시다시피 아주 작은 것들이라 — 우리 집 작은 달걀 하나쯤이야 해롭지 않을 겁니다."], ["Miss Bates, let Emma help you to a little bit of tart—a very little bit.", "미스 베이츠, 엠마가 타르트를 조금만 덜어 드리게 할게요 — 아주 조금만요."], ["Ours are all apple-tarts.", "우리 건 다 사과 타르트랍니다."], ["You need not be afraid of unwholesome preserves here.", "여기서는 몸에 해로운 절임 따위는 걱정 안 하셔도 돼요."], ["I do not advise the custard.", "커스터드는 권하지 않겠어요."], ["Mrs. Goddard, what say you to half a glass of wine?", "고더드 부인, 포도주 반 잔쯤은 어떠십니까?"], ["A small half-glass, put into a tumbler of water?", "작은 잔으로 반 잔을, 물 채운 컵에 타서요?"], ["I do not think it could disagree with you.”", "그 정도면 부인께 거슬리지 않을 것 같은데요.\""]], [["Emma allowed her father to talk—but supplied her visitors in a much more satisfactory style, and on the present evening had particular pleasure in sending them away happy.", "엠마는 아버지가 떠들게 내버려 두었다 — 그러면서도 손님들에게는 훨씬 만족스러운 방식으로 음식을 차려 냈고, 오늘 저녁에는 그들을 행복하게 돌려보내는 데서 각별한 기쁨을 느꼈다."], ["The happiness of Miss Smith was quite equal to her intentions.", "미스 스미스의 행복은 엠마가 의도한 그대로였다."], ["Miss Woodhouse was so great a personage in Highbury, that the prospect of the introduction had given as much panic as pleasure; but the humble, grateful little girl went off with highly gratified feelings, delighted with the affability with which Miss Woodhouse had treated her all the evening, and actually shaken hands with her at last!", "미스 우드하우스는 하이베리에서 워낙 대단한 인물인지라, 그녀에게 소개된다는 전망은 기쁨만큼이나 큰 공포를 안겨 주었던 것이다. 그러나 겸손하고 감사할 줄 아는 이 어린 아가씨는 더없이 흐뭇한 마음을 안고 떠나갔으니, 미스 우드하우스가 저녁 내내 보여 준 다정함에 감격했고, 마지막에는 정말로 그녀와 손까지 맞잡았던 것이다!"]], [["# Volume I — Chapter IV", "# 제1권 4장"]], [["Harriet Smith’s intimacy at Hartfield was soon a settled thing.", "해리엇 스미스가 하트필드에 드나드는 일은 곧 기정사실이 되었다."], ["Quick and decided in her ways, Emma lost no time in inviting, encouraging, and telling her to come very often; and as their acquaintance increased, so did their satisfaction in each other.", "매사에 빠르고 결단력 있는 엠마는 지체 없이 그녀를 초대하고 부추기고 자주 오라고 일렀으며, 친분이 깊어질수록 서로에 대한 흡족함도 깊어 갔다."], ["As a walking companion, Emma had very early foreseen how useful she might find her.", "산책 동무로서 엠마는 일찍부터 그녀가 얼마나 쓸모 있을지를 내다보고 있었다."], ["In that respect Mrs. Weston’s loss had been important.", "그 점에서 웨스턴 부인의 빈자리는 컸다."], ["Her father never went beyond the shrubbery, where two divisions of the ground sufficed him for his long walk, or his short, as the year varied; and since Mrs.", "아버지는 관목 숲 밖으로는 결코 나가지 않았고, 철에 따라 긴 산책이든 짧은 산책이든 그곳을 두 갈래로 나눈 길로 족했다."], ["Weston’s marriage her exercise had been too much confined.", "그리고 웨스턴 부인이 결혼한 뒤로 그녀의 운동은 너무 좁은 데 갇혀 있었다."], ["She had ventured once alone to Randalls, but it was not pleasant; and a Harriet Smith, therefore, one whom she could summon at any time to a walk, would be a valuable addition to her privileges.", "한번은 혼자 랜덜스까지 가 보았으나 즐겁지 않았다. 그러니 언제든 불러내어 산책을 청할 수 있는 해리엇 스미스 같은 사람은 그녀의 특권에 더해질 값진 보탬이었다."], ["But in every respect, as she saw more of her, she approved her, and was confirmed in all her kind designs.", "그러나 어느 모로 보나, 그녀를 알아 갈수록 엠마는 그녀를 마음에 들어 했고, 자신의 온갖 자애로운 계획에 더욱 확신을 굳혔다."]], [["Harriet certainly was not clever, but she had a sweet, docile, grateful disposition, was totally free from conceit, and only desiring to be guided by any one she looked up to.", "해리엇은 분명 영리하지는 않았으나, 상냥하고 고분고분하며 고마움을 아는 천성에, 우쭐대는 구석이라곤 전혀 없었고, 그저 자신이 우러러보는 누군가에게 이끌리기만을 바랐다."], ["Her early attachment to herself was very amiable; and her inclination for good company, and power of appreciating what was elegant and clever, shewed that there was no want of taste, though strength of understanding must not be expected.", "일찌감치 엠마에게 보인 애착은 더없이 사랑스러웠다. 그리고 좋은 사람들과 어울리려는 성향과, 우아하고 영리한 것을 알아보는 능력은, 이해력의 깊이까지는 바랄 수 없을지언정 취향이 모자라지는 않음을 보여 주었다."], ["Altogether she was quite convinced of Harriet Smith’s being exactly the young friend she wanted—exactly the something which her home required.", "무엇보다도 엠마는, 해리엇 스미스야말로 자신이 바라던 바로 그 어린 벗이라고 — 자기 집이 필요로 하던 바로 그 무엇이라고 확신했다."], ["Such a friend as Mrs. Weston was out of the question.", "웨스턴 부인 같은 벗은 애초에 말이 안 되었다."], ["Two such could never be granted.", "그런 사람이 둘이나 주어질 리 없었다."], ["Two such she did not want.", "그런 사람이 둘이나 필요하지도 않았다."], ["It was quite a different sort of thing, a sentiment distinct and independent. Mrs.", "이것은 전혀 다른 종류의 것, 별개의 독립된 감정이었다."], ["Weston was the object of a regard which had its basis in gratitude and esteem.", "웨스턴 부인은 고마움과 존경에 뿌리를 둔 애정의 대상이었다."], ["Harriet would be loved as one to whom she could be useful.", "해리엇은 자신이 도움을 줄 수 있는 사람으로서 사랑받을 터였다."], ["For Mrs. Weston there was nothing to be done; for Harriet every thing.", "웨스턴 부인에게는 해 줄 일이 없었으나, 해리엇에게는 무엇이든 해 줄 일이 있었다."]], [["Her first attempts at usefulness were in an endeavour to find out who were the parents, but Harriet could not tell.", "쓸모 있는 사람이 되어 보려는 그녀의 첫 시도는 부모가 누구인지 알아내려는 노력이었으나, 해리엇은 말해 줄 수가 없었다."], ["She was ready to tell every thing in her power, but on this subject questions were vain.", "자기가 할 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 다 말할 채비가 되어 있었지만, 이 문제에서는 물어봐야 헛일이었다."], ["Emma was obliged to fancy what she liked—but she could never believe that in the same situation she should not have discovered the truth.", "엠마는 좋을 대로 상상하는 수밖에 없었다 — 그러나 같은 처지였다면 자기라면 진실을 캐내지 못했을 리 없다는 생각만은 결코 버릴 수 없었다."], ["Harriet had no penetration.", "해리엇에게는 통찰이 없었다."], ["She had been satisfied to hear and believe just what Mrs. Goddard chose to tell her; and looked no farther.", "그녀는 고더드 부인이 골라 들려주는 말만 듣고 믿는 데 만족했고, 그 너머는 들여다보지 않았다."]], [["Mrs. Goddard, and the teachers, and the girls and the affairs of the school in general, formed naturally a great part of the conversation—and but for her acquaintance with the Martins of Abbey-Mill Farm, it must have been the whole.", "고더드 부인, 그리고 선생들과 여학생들과 학교의 갖가지 일들이 자연히 대화의 큰 부분을 차지했다 — 그리고 애비 밀 농장의 마틴 댁과 친분이 없었다면, 그것이 대화의 전부였을 것이다."], ["But the Martins occupied her thoughts a good deal; she had spent two very happy months with them, and now loved to talk of the pleasures of her visit, and describe the many comforts and wonders of the place.", "그러나 마틴 댁은 그녀의 마음을 적잖이 차지하고 있었다."], ["Emma encouraged her talkativeness—amused by such a picture of another set of beings, and enjoying the youthful simplicity which could speak with so much exultation of Mrs. Martin’s having “two parlours, two very good parlours, indeed; one of them quite as large as Mrs.", "그녀는 그 집에서 무척 행복한 두 달을 보냈고, 이제는 그 방문의 즐거움을 이야기하고 그곳의 갖은 안락함과 신기함을 늘어놓기를 좋아했다."], ["Goddard’s drawing-room; and of her having an upper maid who had lived five-and-twenty years with her; and of their having eight cows, two of them Alderneys, and one a little Welch cow, a very pretty little Welch cow indeed; and of Mrs. Martin’s saying as she was so fond of it, it should be called her cow; and of their having a very handsome summer-house in their garden, where some day next year they were all to drink tea:—a very handsome summer-house, large enough to hold a dozen people.”", "엠마는 그녀의 수다를 부추겼다 — 또 다른 부류의 인간 군상을 그린 그런 그림이 재미있었고, 마틴 부인에게 \"거실이 둘이나, 그것도 아주 훌륭한 거실이 둘이나 있는데, 그중 하나는 고더드 부인의 응접실만큼이나 넓다는 것, 마틴 부인 곁에서 스물다섯 해를 산 상급 하녀가 있다는 것, 소가 여덟 마리인데 그중 둘은 올더니종이고 한 마리는 작은 웨일스 소로 정말이지 아주 예쁘장한 작은 웨일스 소라는 것, 해리엇이 그 소를 어찌나 아끼는지 마틴 부인이 그건 해리엇의 소라고 불러야겠다고 말했다는 것, 정원에 아주 근사한 정자가 있어서 내년 어느 날 다들 그곳에서 차를 마실 거라는 것 — 열두 사람은 너끈히 들어갈 만큼 큰, 아주 근사한 정자라는 것\"을 이토록 의기양양하게 떠벌릴 수 있는 그 앳된 천진함이 즐거웠던 것이다."]], [["For some time she was amused, without thinking beyond the immediate cause; but as she came to understand the family better, other feelings arose.", "한동안 엠마는 그 직접적인 원인 너머는 생각하지 않은 채 재미있어했다. 그러나 그 집안을 더 잘 알게 되면서 다른 감정이 일었다."], ["She had taken up a wrong idea, fancying it was a mother and daughter, a son and son’s wife, who all lived together; but when it appeared that the Mr.", "그녀는 잘못된 생각을 품고 있었으니, 어머니와 딸, 아들과 며느리가 모두 함께 사는 줄로 짐작했던 것이다."], ["Martin, who bore a part in the narrative, and was always mentioned with approbation for his great good-nature in doing something or other, was a single man; that there was no young Mrs. Martin, no wife in the case; she did suspect danger to her poor little friend from all this hospitality and kindness, and that, if she were not taken care of, she might be required to sink herself forever.", "그러나 그 이야기에서 한몫을 차지하며 늘 무언가에 베푼 그 큰 호의를 칭찬받곤 하던 그 미스터 마틴이 독신 남자라는 것 — 젊은 마틴 부인이라곤 없고, 이 경우에 아내라곤 없다는 것이 드러나자, 그녀는 이 온갖 환대와 친절이 가엾은 어린 벗에게 위험이 될까 봐 의심했고, 잘 지켜 주지 않으면 해리엇이 영영 제 신분을 떨어뜨리게 될지도 모른다고 생각했다."]], [["With this inspiriting notion, her questions increased in number and meaning; and she particularly led Harriet to talk more of Mr.", "이 가슴 부푸는 생각에 힘입어, 그녀의 질문은 수와 의도가 부쩍 늘었다. 그녀는 특히 해리엇이 미스터 마틴 이야기를 더 하도록 이끌었고, 해리엇은 그것을 싫어하는 기색이 전혀 없었다."], ["Martin, and there was evidently no dislike to it. Harriet was very ready to speak of the share he had had in their moonlight walks and merry evening games; and dwelt a good deal upon his being so very good-humoured and obliging.", "해리엇은 달빛 산책과 흥겨운 저녁놀이에서 그가 한몫했던 일을 기꺼이 이야기했고, 그가 어찌나 쾌활하고 친절한지를 한참이나 늘어놓았다."], ["He had gone three miles round one day in order to bring her some walnuts, because she had said how fond she was of them, and in every thing else he was so very obliging.", "한번은 그녀가 호두를 좋아한다고 말했다는 이유로, 그가 호두를 가져다주려고 삼 마일이나 빙 돌아 다녀왔으며, 다른 모든 일에서도 그토록 친절했다는 것이다."], ["He had his shepherd’s son into the parlour one night on purpose to sing to her.", "어느 날 밤에는 그녀에게 노래를 들려주려고 일부러 목동의 아들을 거실로 불러들였다고 했다."], ["She was very fond of singing.", "그녀는 노래를 무척 좋아했다."], ["He could sing a little himself.", "그도 노래를 조금 부를 줄 알았다."], ["She believed he was very clever, and understood every thing.", "그녀가 보기에 그는 아주 영리하고 무엇이든 다 아는 사람이었다."], ["He had a very fine flock, and, while she was with them, he had been bid more for his wool than any body in the country.", "그는 아주 훌륭한 양 떼를 거느렸고, 그녀가 그 집에 머무는 동안 그의 양털값으로 이 고장 누구보다도 높은 값이 매겨졌다."], ["She believed every body spoke well of him.", "그녀가 보기에 모두가 그를 좋게 말했다."], ["His mother and sisters were very fond of him. Mrs.", "어머니와 누이들도 그를 무척 아꼈다."], ["Martin had told her one day (and there was a blush as she said it,) that it was impossible for any body to be a better son, and therefore she was sure, whenever he married, he would make a good husband.", "어느 날 마틴 부인은 그녀에게 (이 말을 할 때 해리엇은 얼굴을 붉혔다) 누구도 그보다 더 좋은 아들일 수는 없으니, 그가 언제 결혼하든 분명 좋은 남편이 될 거라고 말했다고 했다. 그렇다고 마틴 부인이 그를 결혼시키고 싶어 한 건 아니라고 했다."], ["Not that she wanted him to marry. She was in no hurry at all.", "전혀 서두를 게 없다고 했다는 것이다."]], [["“Well done, Mrs.", "\"잘하셨어요, 마틴 부인!\""], ["Martin!” thought Emma.", "엠마는 속으로 생각했다."], ["“You know what you are about.”", "\"댁이 무슨 속셈인지 다 알겠네요.\""]], [["“And when she had come away, Mrs. Martin was so very kind as to send Mrs. Goddard a beautiful goose—the finest goose Mrs. Goddard had ever seen. Mrs. Goddard had dressed it on a Sunday, and asked all the three teachers, Miss Nash, and Miss Prince, and Miss Richardson, to sup with her.”", "\"그리고 제가 떠나올 때, 마틴 부인은 어찌나 친절하신지 고더드 부인께 멋진 거위를 한 마리 보내 주셨어요 — 고더드 부인이 여태 본 중에 가장 좋은 거위였어요. 고더드 부인은 그걸 일요일에 요리해서 선생님 세 분을, 미스 내시랑 미스 프린스랑 미스 리처드슨을 모두 저녁 식사에 부르셨어요.\""]], [["“Mr. Martin, I suppose, is not a man of information beyond the line of his own business?", "\"미스터 마틴은 아마 제 일 분야 말고는 별로 아는 게 없는 사람이겠지?"], ["He does not read?”", "책은 안 읽고?\""]], [["“Oh yes!—that is, no—I do not know—but I believe he has read a good deal—but not what you would think any thing of.", "\"아, 읽어요! — 그러니까, 아니 — 모르겠어요 — 하지만 제 생각엔 꽤 많이 읽은 것 같아요 — 미스 우드하우스가 대단하게 여길 만한 건 아니지만요."], ["He reads the Agricultural Reports, and some other books that lay in one of the window seats—but he reads all them to himself.", "농업 보고서를 읽고, 창가 자리에 놓여 있던 다른 책들도 몇 권 읽어요 — 그래도 그건 다 혼자 읽는 거예요."], ["But sometimes of an evening, before we went to cards, he would read something aloud out of the Elegant Extracts, very entertaining.", "하지만 가끔 저녁에, 우리가 카드놀이를 하기 전에 그분이 『우아한 발췌집』에서 무언가를 소리 내어 읽어 주곤 했는데, 아주 재미있었어요."], ["And I know he has read the Vicar of Wakefield.", "그리고 그분이 『웨이크필드의 목사』를 읽은 것도 알아요."], ["He never read the Romance of the Forest, nor The Children of the Abbey.", "『숲속의 로맨스』나 『수도원의 아이들』은 한 번도 안 읽었고요."], ["He had never heard of such books before I mentioned them, but he is determined to get them now as soon as ever he can.”", "제가 말하기 전엔 그런 책이 있는 줄도 몰랐지만, 이제는 할 수 있는 대로 빨리 그 책들을 구해 보겠다고 단단히 마음먹었어요.\""]], [["The next question was—", "다음 질문은 이러했다 —"]], [["“What sort of looking man is Mr. Martin?”", "\"미스터 마틴은 어떻게 생긴 사람이야?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["not handsome—not at all handsome.", "잘생기진 않았어요 — 전혀 잘생기진 않았죠."], ["I thought him very plain at first, but I do not think him so plain now.", "처음엔 아주 못생겼다고 생각했는데, 지금은 그렇게 못생겼다고 생각하지 않아요."], ["One does not, you know, after a time.", "아시다시피 시간이 좀 지나면 그렇게 안 되잖아요."], ["But did you never see him?", "그런데 한 번도 그분을 못 보셨어요?"], ["He is in Highbury every now and then, and he is sure to ride through every week in his way to Kingston.", "그분은 이따금 하이베리에 오고, 매주 킹스턴에 가는 길에 꼭 말을 타고 지나가요."], ["He has passed you very often.”", "미스 우드하우스 곁을 아주 여러 번 지나갔는걸요.\""]], [["“That may be, and I may have seen him fifty times, but without having any idea of his name.", "\"그럴 수도 있겠지. 쉰 번쯤 봤을지도 모르고."], ["A young farmer, whether on horseback or on foot, is the very last sort of person to raise my curiosity.", "하지만 이름은 도통 알 길이 없었어. 젊은 농부란, 말을 탔든 걸어가든, 내 호기심을 끌 만한 사람으론 가장 거리가 먼 부류거든."], ["The yeomanry are precisely the order of people with whom I feel I can have nothing to do.", "자영농이야말로 내가 도무지 상관할 일이 없다고 느끼는 바로 그 계층이고."], ["A degree or two lower, and a creditable appearance might interest me; I might hope to be useful to their families in some way or other.", "한두 단계만 더 낮으면, 또 행색이 그럴듯하면 내 관심을 끌지도 모르지. 그런 사람들 집안에는 어떤 식으로든 내가 도움이 되어 줄 수 있기를 바라기도 할 테고."], ["But a farmer can need none of my help, and is, therefore, in one sense, as much above my notice as in every other he is below it.”", "하지만 농부는 내 도움이 필요할 게 없으니, 어떤 의미에서는 내 눈길이 미칠 데 너머에 있는 셈이야 — 다른 모든 면에서는 내 눈길 아래에 있는 만큼이나 말이지.\""]], [["“To be sure.", "\"그럼요."], ["Oh yes!", "아, 네!"], ["It is not likely you should ever have observed him; but he knows you very well indeed—I mean by sight.”", "미스 우드하우스가 그분을 눈여겨봤을 리 없겠지요. 하지만 그분은 미스 우드하우스를 정말 잘 알아요 — 그러니까 얼굴은요.\""]], [["“I have no doubt of his being a very respectable young man.", "\"그가 아주 점잖은 청년이라는 건 의심하지 않아."], ["I know, indeed, that he is so, and, as such, wish him well.", "정말이지 그렇다는 걸 알고, 그러니 그가 잘되기를 바라지."], ["What do you imagine his age to be?”", "그 사람 나이는 몇이나 됐을 것 같아?\""]], [["“He was four-and-twenty the 8th of last June, and my birthday is the 23rd just a fortnight and a day’s difference—which is very odd.”", "\"지난 유월 팔 일에 스물네 살이 됐고, 제 생일은 그달 이십삼 일이니 딱 보름하고 하루 차이예요 — 참 신기하죠.\""]], [["“Only four-and-twenty.", "\"겨우 스물넷."], ["That is too young to settle.", "자리 잡기엔 너무 어리네."], ["His mother is perfectly right not to be in a hurry.", "그분 어머니가 서두르지 않는 건 더없이 옳은 일이야."], ["They seem very comfortable as they are, and if she were to take any pains to marry him, she would probably repent it.", "지금 그대로도 아주 편안해 보이는데, 어머니가 굳이 그를 결혼시키려고 애쓰면 아마 후회하게 될걸."], ["Six years hence, if he could meet with a good sort of young woman in the same rank as his own, with a little money, it might be very desirable.”", "한 육 년쯤 뒤에, 자기와 같은 신분에 돈도 조금 있는 괜찮은 색싯감을 만난다면 모를까, 그땐 아주 바람직하겠지.\""]], [["“Six years hence!", "\"육 년 뒤요!"], ["Dear Miss Woodhouse, he would be thirty years old!”", "어머나, 미스 우드하우스, 그럼 그분은 서른 살이 되잖아요!\""]], [["“Well, and that is as early as most men can afford to marry, who are not born to an independence.", "\"그래, 그게 독립적인 재산을 타고나지 못한 남자 대다수가 결혼할 형편이 되는 가장 이른 나이야."], ["Mr. Martin, I imagine, has his fortune entirely to make—cannot be at all beforehand with the world.", "내 짐작에 미스터 마틴은 제 재산을 온전히 처음부터 일궈야 하는 처지일 테고 — 세상에서 한 발도 앞서 있지 못할 테지."], ["Whatever money he might come into when his father died, whatever his share of the family property, it is, I dare say, all afloat, all employed in his stock, and so forth; and though, with diligence and good luck, he may be rich in time, it is next to impossible that he should have realised any thing yet.”", "아버지가 돌아가실 때 얼마간 물려받든, 집안 재산에서 제 몫이 얼마든, 그건 분명 죄다 묶여 있고, 죄다 가축이며 그런 데 들어가 있을 거야. 그러니 부지런함과 운이 따른다면 언젠가는 부자가 될지 몰라도, 아직 무언가를 실하게 모아 두었을 리는 거의 없지.\""]], [["“To be sure, so it is.", "\"그럼요, 정말 그래요."], ["But they live very comfortably.", "하지만 그분들은 아주 편안하게 살아요."], ["They have no indoors man, else they do not want for any thing; and Mrs.", "집안 남자 일꾼은 없지만, 그것 말고는 부족한 게 없거든요."], ["Martin talks of taking a boy another year.”", "그리고 마틴 부인은 내년에 사내아이를 하나 들일까 한다고 하셨어요.\""]], [["“I wish you may not get into a scrape, Harriet, whenever he does marry;—I mean, as to being acquainted with his wife—for though his sisters, from a superior education, are not to be altogether objected to, it does not follow that he might marry any body at all fit for you to notice.", "\"해리엇, 그가 언제 결혼하든 네가 곤란한 처지에 빠지지 않기를 바라 — 그러니까 그의 아내와 사귀게 되는 일에서 말이야. 그의 누이들이야 교육을 더 받았으니 아주 못 사귈 사람은 아니라 해도, 그렇다고 그가 네가 알 만한 사람을 아내로 맞으리란 보장은 없으니까."], ["The misfortune of your birth ought to make you particularly careful as to your associates.", "네 출생의 불운을 생각하면 너는 사귀는 사람을 각별히 가려야 마땅해."], ["There can be no doubt of your being a gentleman’s daughter, and you must support your claim to that station by every thing within your own power, or there will be plenty of people who would take pleasure in degrading you.”", "네가 신사의 딸이라는 데는 의심의 여지가 없으니, 너는 네 힘이 닿는 온갖 방법으로 그 신분에 대한 권리를 지켜야 해. 안 그러면 너를 깎아내리며 고소해할 사람이 수두룩할 테니까.\""]], [["“Yes, to be sure, I suppose there are.", "\"네, 그럼요, 그런 사람들이 있겠지요."], ["But while I visit at Hartfield, and you are so kind to me, Miss Woodhouse, I am not afraid of what any body can do.”", "하지만 제가 하트필드에 드나들고, 미스 우드하우스가 이렇게 잘해 주시는 한, 누가 무슨 짓을 한들 저는 두렵지 않아요.\""]], [["“You understand the force of influence pretty well, Harriet; but I would have you so firmly established in good society, as to be independent even of Hartfield and Miss Woodhouse.", "\"영향력의 힘을 제법 잘 아는구나, 해리엇. 하지만 나는 네가 좋은 사람들 틈에 단단히 자리를 잡아서, 하트필드와 미스 우드하우스에게마저 기대지 않아도 될 만큼 되었으면 좋겠어."], ["I want to see you permanently well connected, and to that end it will be advisable to have as few odd acquaintance as may be; and, therefore, I say that if you should still be in this country when Mr.", "나는 네가 줄곧 좋은 인연을 맺고 사는 걸 보고 싶고, 그러자면 별난 친지는 되도록 적게 두는 게 좋아."], ["Martin marries, I wish you may not be drawn in by your intimacy with the sisters, to be acquainted with the wife, who will probably be some mere farmer’s daughter, without education.”", "그래서 하는 말인데, 미스터 마틴이 결혼할 때 네가 여전히 이 고장에 있다면, 그의 누이들과 친하다는 이유로 그 아내와 사귀게 되는 일에 끌려들지 않았으면 해. 그 아내라는 사람은 십중팔구 교육이라곤 못 받은 그저 그런 농부의 딸일 테니까.\""]], [["“To be sure.", "\"그럼요."], ["Yes. Not that I think Mr.", "네."], ["Martin would ever marry any body but what had had some education—and been very well brought up. However, I do not mean to set up my opinion against yours—and I am sure I shall not wish for the acquaintance of his wife.", "그렇다고 제가 미스터 마틴이 교육도 좀 받고 아주 잘 자란 사람 말고 다른 누구와 결혼하리라 생각하는 건 아니에요 — 하지만 제 의견을 미스 우드하우스 의견에 맞세울 생각은 없어요 — 그리고 그분 아내와 사귀고 싶어 하지 않을 게 분명하고요."], ["I shall always have a great regard for the Miss Martins, especially Elizabeth, and should be very sorry to give them up, for they are quite as well educated as me.", "저는 늘 마틴 자매를, 특히 엘리자베스를 무척 좋아할 거고, 그분들과 멀어진다면 정말 슬플 거예요. 그분들은 저만큼이나 교육을 잘 받았으니까요."], ["But if he marries a very ignorant, vulgar woman, certainly I had better not visit her, if I can help it.”", "하지만 그분이 아주 무지하고 천박한 여자와 결혼한다면, 가능한 한 그 여자네는 안 찾아가는 게 분명 나을 거예요.\""]], [["Emma watched her through the fluctuations of this speech, and saw no alarming symptoms of love.", "엠마는 이 말의 갈피갈피를 헤아리며 해리엇을 살폈으나, 사랑에 빠진 위태로운 징후는 보이지 않았다."], ["The young man had been the first admirer, but she trusted there was no other hold, and that there would be no serious difficulty, on Harriet’s side, to oppose any friendly arrangement of her own.", "그 청년이 첫 흠모자이긴 했어도, 그녀가 보기에 달리 마음을 붙들린 데는 없었고, 자신이 마련해 줄 어떤 다정한 혼처에 맞설 만한 심각한 걸림돌이 해리엇 쪽에 있지도 않을 듯했다."]], [["They met Mr. Martin the very next day, as they were walking on the Donwell road.", "바로 다음 날, 돈웰 길을 걷다가 두 사람은 미스터 마틴과 마주쳤다."], ["He was on foot, and after looking very respectfully at her, looked with most unfeigned satisfaction at her companion.", "그는 걸어오고 있었는데, 엠마를 아주 공손하게 바라본 뒤, 그 동행을 더없이 꾸밈없는 흡족함으로 바라보았다. 엠마는 이렇게 살펴볼 기회가 생긴 것이 싫지 않았다."], ["Emma was not sorry to have such an opportunity of survey; and walking a few yards forward, while they talked together, soon made her quick eye sufficiently acquainted with Mr. Robert Martin.", "두 사람이 이야기를 나누는 동안 그녀는 몇 야드쯤 앞서 걸으면서, 곧 그 빠른 눈으로 로버트 마틴이라는 인물을 충분히 파악했다."], ["His appearance was very neat, and he looked like a sensible young man, but his person had no other advantage; and when he came to be contrasted with gentlemen, she thought he must lose all the ground he had gained in Harriet’s inclination.", "그의 행색은 아주 깔끔했고, 분별 있는 청년처럼 보였으나, 그 사람됨에는 그 밖의 장점이라곤 없었다. 그리고 신사들과 견주게 되면, 그가 해리엇의 호감 속에서 얻어 둔 입지를 죄다 잃고 말리라고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["Harriet was not insensible of manner; she had voluntarily noticed her father’s gentleness with admiration as well as wonder.", "해리엇은 태도에 둔감하지 않았다. 그녀는 엠마 아버지의 부드러움을 누가 시키지도 않았는데 감탄과 함께 신기해하며 알아본 적이 있었다."], ["Mr. Martin looked as if he did not know what manner was.", "그런데 미스터 마틴은 태도라는 게 무엇인지 도무지 모르는 사람처럼 보였다."]], [["They remained but a few minutes together, as Miss Woodhouse must not be kept waiting; and Harriet then came running to her with a smiling face, and in a flutter of spirits, which Miss Woodhouse hoped very soon to compose.", "미스 우드하우스를 기다리게 해서는 안 되었기에 두 사람은 잠깐만 함께 있었다. 그러고 나서 해리엇은 미소 띤 얼굴로, 들뜬 마음에 설레며 엠마에게 달려왔다 — 미스 우드하우스가 머잖아 가라앉혀 주기를 바라는 그 설렘으로."]], [["“Only think of our happening to meet him!—How very odd!", "\"우리가 그분과 마주치다니, 생각 좀 해 보세요!"], ["It was quite a chance, he said, that he had not gone round by Randalls.", "— 정말 신기하죠! 그분 말로는 랜덜스 쪽으로 돌아가지 않은 게 순전히 우연이었대요."], ["He did not think we ever walked this road.", "우리가 이 길로 걸으리라곤 생각도 못 했대요."], ["He thought we walked towards Randalls most days.", "우리가 거의 매일 랜덜스 쪽으로 걷는 줄 알았다는 거예요."], ["He has not been able to get the Romance of the Forest yet.", "그분은 아직 『숲속의 로맨스』를 못 구하셨대요."], ["He was so busy the last time he was at Kingston that he quite forgot it, but he goes again to-morrow.", "지난번 킹스턴에 갔을 때 너무 바빠서 깜빡 잊으셨다는데, 내일 또 가신대요."], ["So very odd we should happen to meet!", "우리가 마주치다니 정말 신기하죠!"], ["Well, Miss Woodhouse, is he like what you expected?", "그런데 미스 우드하우스, 그분이 생각하셨던 그대로인가요?"], ["What do you think of him?", "어떻게 보세요?"], ["Do you think him so very plain?”", "정말 그렇게 못생겼다고 보세요?\""]], [["“He is very plain, undoubtedly—remarkably plain:—but that is nothing compared with his entire want of gentility.", "\"못생긴 건 분명해 — 두드러지게 못생겼지. 하지만 그건 그 사람한테 품위라곤 전혀 없다는 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니야."], ["I had no right to expect much, and I did not expect much; but I had no idea that he could be so very clownish, so totally without air.", "내가 많은 걸 기대할 권리야 없었고, 많이 기대하지도 않았어. 하지만 그가 그렇게까지 촌스러울 줄은, 그렇게까지 풍채라곤 없을 줄은 미처 몰랐어."], ["I had imagined him, I confess, a degree or two nearer gentility.”", "솔직히 나는 그가 한두 단계는 품위에 더 가까운 사람일 줄 알았거든.\""]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"그렇긴 해요.\""], ["said Harriet, in a mortified voice, “he is not so genteel as real gentlemen.”", "해리엇이 풀 죽은 목소리로 말했다. \"진짜 신사들만큼 품위 있진 않죠.\""]], [["“I think, Harriet, since your acquaintance with us, you have been repeatedly in the company of some such very real gentlemen, that you must yourself be struck with the difference in Mr.", "\"해리엇, 너는 우리와 알고 지내면서 그런 진짜 신사들과 거듭 자리를 함께해 왔으니, 미스터 마틴의 차이가 너 스스로도 눈에 띌 거야."], ["Martin. At Hartfield, you have had very good specimens of well educated, well bred men.", "하트필드에서 너는 잘 배우고 잘 자란 남자들의 아주 훌륭한 본보기를 봐 왔잖아."], ["I should be surprized if, after seeing them, you could be in company with Mr. Martin again without perceiving him to be a very inferior creature—and rather wondering at yourself for having ever thought him at all agreeable before.", "그런 사람들을 보고 난 다음에 다시 미스터 마틴과 자리를 함께하면서도 그가 한참 못한 사람이라는 걸 못 알아본다면, 또 전에는 그를 어떻게 그래도 괜찮은 사람이라 여겼었나 스스로 의아해하지 않는다면, 나는 깜짝 놀랄 거야."], ["Do not you begin to feel that now?", "이제 그런 게 느껴지기 시작하지 않니?"], ["Were not you struck?", "눈에 확 들어오지 않았어?"], ["I am sure you must have been struck by his awkward look and abrupt manner, and the uncouthness of a voice which I heard to be wholly unmodulated as I stood here.”", "그 어색한 표정과 퉁명스러운 태도, 그리고 내가 여기 서서 들으니 가락이라곤 도무지 없던 그 거친 목소리에 분명 너도 깜짝 놀랐을 텐데.\""]], [["“Certainly, he is not like Mr. Knightley.", "\"확실히 그분은 미스터 나이틀리 같진 않아요."], ["He has not such a fine air and way of walking as Mr. Knightley.", "미스터 나이틀리처럼 그렇게 멋진 풍채와 걸음걸이는 못 가졌죠."], ["I see the difference plain enough.", "그 차이는 저도 똑똑히 알겠어요."], ["But Mr. Knightley is so very fine a man!”", "하지만 미스터 나이틀리는 정말이지 아주 훌륭한 분이잖아요!\""]], [["“Mr. Knightley’s air is so remarkably good that it is not fair to compare Mr.", "\"미스터 나이틀리의 풍채야 워낙 두드러지게 좋으니, 미스터 마틴을 그분과 견주는 건 공평하지 않아."], ["Martin with him. You might not see one in a hundred with gentleman so plainly written as in Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리만큼 '신사'라고 또렷이 적혀 있는 사람은 백에 하나 볼까 말까일걸."], ["Knightley. But he is not the only gentleman you have been lately used to.", "하지만 네가 요즘 만난 신사가 그분만 있는 건 아니잖아."], ["What say you to Mr. Weston and Mr.", "미스터 웨스턴이랑 미스터 엘턴은 어떻게 생각해?"], ["Elton? Compare Mr.", "미스터 마틴을 그 두 분 가운데 어느 쪽과든 견줘 봐."], ["Martin with either of them. Compare their manner of carrying themselves; of walking; of speaking; of being silent.", "몸가짐을, 걸음걸이를, 말투를, 또 입을 다물고 있는 모습을 견줘 보라고."], ["You must see the difference.”", "그 차이가 분명 보일 거야.\""]], [["“Oh yes!—there is a great difference.", "\"아, 네! — 큰 차이가 있죠."], ["But Mr. Weston is almost an old man.", "하지만 미스터 웨스턴은 거의 노인이잖아요."], ["Mr. Weston must be between forty and fifty.”", "미스터 웨스턴은 마흔에서 쉰 사이쯤 됐을 거예요.\""]], [["“Which makes his good manners the more valuable.", "\"바로 그래서 그분의 훌륭한 태도가 더 값진 거야."], ["The older a person grows, Harriet, the more important it is that their manners should not be bad; the more glaring and disgusting any loudness, or coarseness, or awkwardness becomes.", "사람은 나이가 들수록, 해리엇, 그 태도가 나쁘지 않은 게 더 중요해지거든. 그럴수록 어떤 시끄러움이나 거칠음이나 어색함이든 더 도드라지고 역겨워지는 법이야."], ["What is passable in youth is detestable in later age. Mr.", "젊을 때 봐 넘길 만한 게 늙어서는 진저리 나는 게 되지."], ["Martin is now awkward and abrupt; what will he be at Mr. Weston’s time of life?”", "미스터 마틴은 지금도 어색하고 퉁명스러운데, 미스터 웨스턴 나이가 되면 어떻게 될까?\""]], [["“There is no saying, indeed,”", "\"정말 뭐라 말할 수가 없네요.\""], ["replied Harriet rather solemnly.", "해리엇이 자못 진지하게 대답했다."]], [["“But there may be pretty good guessing.", "\"하지만 꽤 그럴듯하게 짐작은 할 수 있지."], ["He will be a completely gross, vulgar farmer, totally inattentive to appearances, and thinking of nothing but profit and loss.”", "그는 행색 따위는 도무지 신경도 안 쓰고, 이익이니 손해니 하는 것밖에 모르는, 영락없이 천하고 상스러운 농부가 되어 있을 거야.\""]], [["“Will he, indeed?", "\"정말 그럴까요?"], ["That will be very bad.”", "그럼 아주 안 좋겠네요.\""]], [["“How much his business engrosses him already is very plain from the circumstance of his forgetting to inquire for the book you recommended.", "\"제 일에 벌써 얼마나 골몰해 있는지는, 네가 권한 그 책을 물어보기를 잊어버린 것만 봐도 빤하지."], ["He was a great deal too full of the market to think of any thing else—which is just as it should be, for a thriving man.", "그는 장터 생각에 너무 푹 빠져서 다른 건 생각할 겨를이 없었던 거야 — 하긴 잘나가는 사람한테는 마땅히 그래야 할 일이지."], ["What has he to do with books?", "그가 책하고 무슨 상관이 있겠어?"], ["And I have no doubt that he will thrive, and be a very rich man in time—and his being illiterate and coarse need not disturb us.”", "그리고 그가 분명 잘되어서 언젠가는 아주 부자가 될 거라는 것도 의심하지 않아 — 그러니 그가 무식하고 거칠다는 건 우리가 신경 쓸 일이 아니야.\""]], [["“I wonder he did not remember the book”—was all Harriet’s answer, and spoken with a degree of grave displeasure which Emma thought might be safely left to itself.", "\"그분이 그 책을 기억 못 한 게 이상하네요\" — 이것이 해리엇의 대답 전부였고, 자못 진지하게 못마땅해하는 투로 한 말이라, 엠마는 그쯤은 그냥 내버려 두어도 무방하리라 여겼다."], ["She, therefore, said no more for some time.", "그래서 한동안 더 말하지 않았다."], ["Her next beginning was,", "그녀가 다음으로 꺼낸 말은 이러했다."]], [["“In one respect, perhaps, Mr. Elton’s manners are superior to Mr.", "\"한 가지 점에서는, 아마, 미스터 엘턴의 태도가 미스터 나이틀리나 미스터 웨스턴보다 나을 거야."], ["Knightley’s or Mr.", "더 부드럽거든."], ["Weston’s. They have more gentleness.", "본보기로 삼기에 더 안전하지."], ["They might be more safely held up as a pattern. There is an openness, a quickness, almost a bluntness in Mr.", "미스터 웨스턴에게는 솔직함과 시원시원함, 거의 무뚝뚝하달 만한 데가 있는데, 사람들은 다들 그런 점을 그분에게서는 좋아해."], ["Weston, which every body likes in him, because there is so much good-humour with it—but that would not do to be copied.", "거기에 쾌활함이 워낙 많이 배어 있으니까 — 하지만 그건 따라 해서는 안 될 거야. 미스터 나이틀리의 그 거침없고 단호하고 위엄 있는 태도도 마찬가지지."], ["Neither would Mr.", "그분에게는 아주 잘 어울리지만 말이야."], ["Knightley’s downright, decided, commanding sort of manner, though it suits him very well; his figure, and look, and situation in life seem to allow it; but if any young man were to set about copying him, he would not be sufferable.", "그분의 풍채며 인상이며 사회적 처지가 그걸 허락하는 것 같거든. 하지만 어떤 젊은 남자가 그분을 따라 하려 든다면, 봐줄 수가 없을 거야."], ["On the contrary, I think a young man might be very safely recommended to take Mr. Elton as a model.", "반대로, 젊은 남자한테는 미스터 엘턴을 본보기로 삼으라고 아주 안심하고 권할 만하다고 생각해."], ["Mr. Elton is good-humoured, cheerful, obliging, and gentle.", "미스터 엘턴은 쾌활하고 밝고 친절하고 부드럽거든."], ["He seems to me to be grown particularly gentle of late.", "내가 보기에 요즘 들어 특히 더 부드러워진 것 같아."], ["I do not know whether he has any design of ingratiating himself with either of us, Harriet, by additional softness, but it strikes me that his manners are softer than they used to be.", "미스터 엘턴이 한층 더 다정하게 굴어서 우리 둘 중 누구한테든 환심을 사려는 속셈이 있는 건지는 모르겠지만, 해리엇, 그분 태도가 전보다 부드러워졌다는 건 분명 눈에 띄어."], ["If he means any thing, it must be to please you.", "무슨 뜻이 있다면, 그건 너를 기쁘게 하려는 걸 거야."], ["Did not I tell you what he said of you the other day?”", "요전 날 그분이 너를 두고 한 말, 내가 안 들려줬던가?\""]], [["She then repeated some warm personal praise which she had drawn from Mr.", "그러고서 그녀는 미스터 엘턴에게서 끌어낸 따뜻한 인물 칭찬을 몇 마디 옮겼는데, 이제는 그 말에 온전히 무게를 실어 주었다."], ["Elton, and now did full justice to; and Harriet blushed and smiled, and said she had always thought Mr. Elton very agreeable.", "해리엇은 얼굴을 붉히며 미소 짓고는, 자기도 늘 미스터 엘턴을 아주 괜찮은 분이라 생각했다고 말했다."]], [["Mr. Elton was the very person fixed on by Emma for driving the young farmer out of Harriet’s head.", "미스터 엘턴이야말로 해리엇의 머릿속에서 그 젊은 농부를 몰아내려고 엠마가 점찍어 둔 바로 그 사람이었다."], ["She thought it would be an excellent match; and only too palpably desirable, natural, and probable, for her to have much merit in planning it.", "그녀는 그것이 더없이 훌륭한 혼처가 되리라 생각했다. 다만 너무도 빤히 바람직하고 자연스럽고 그럼 직한 일이라, 그것을 계획한 자기 공을 크게 내세울 수가 없을 정도였다."], ["She feared it was what every body else must think of and predict.", "다른 사람들도 누구나 떠올리고 점쳐 볼 게 분명한 일이라 못내 마음에 걸렸다."], ["It was not likely, however, that any body should have equalled her in the date of the plan, as it had entered her brain during the very first evening of Harriet’s coming to Hartfield.", "그러나 계획을 떠올린 시점에서는 누구도 자기를 따라올 수 없으리라 — 그 생각은 해리엇이 하트필드에 온 바로 그 첫날 저녁에 이미 그녀의 머릿속에 들어왔으니 말이다. 곱씹어 볼수록 그 합당함에 대한 확신은 더욱 커졌다."], ["The longer she considered it, the greater was her sense of its expediency. Mr.", "미스터 엘턴의 처지는 더없이 알맞았다."], ["Elton’s situation was most suitable, quite the gentleman himself, and without low connexions; at the same time, not of any family that could fairly object to the doubtful birth of Harriet.", "그 자신 어엿한 신사인 데다 천한 연줄이 없었고, 동시에 해리엇의 미심쩍은 출생을 정당하게 트집 잡을 만한 집안 출신도 아니었다. 그는 해리엇에게 안락한 보금자리를 줄 수 있었고, 엠마는 수입도 아주 넉넉하리라 짐작했다."], ["He had a comfortable home for her, and Emma imagined a very sufficient income; for though the vicarage of Highbury was not large, he was known to have some independent property; and she thought very highly of him as a good-humoured, well-meaning, respectable young man, without any deficiency of useful understanding or knowledge of the world.", "하이베리의 목사관이 큰 편은 아니어도, 그가 얼마간 독립적인 재산을 가졌다고 알려져 있었기 때문이다. 게다가 그녀는 그를, 쾌활하고 선의가 넘치는 점잖은 청년으로, 쓸모 있는 분별이나 세상 물정에 대한 식견에 모자람이 없는 사람으로 아주 높이 보고 있었다."]], [["She had already satisfied herself that he thought Harriet a beautiful girl, which she trusted, with such frequent meetings at Hartfield, was foundation enough on his side; and on Harriet’s there could be little doubt that the idea of being preferred by him would have all the usual weight and efficacy.", "그녀는 그가 해리엇을 아름다운 아가씨로 여긴다는 것을 이미 확인해 둔 터였고, 하트필드에서 그토록 자주 만나다 보면 그 점이 그의 편에서는 충분한 토대가 되리라 믿었다. 그리고 해리엇 편에서는, 그에게서 마음을 받고 있다는 생각이 으레 그렇듯 충분한 무게와 효력을 발휘하리라는 데 거의 의심이 없었다."], ["And he was really a very pleasing young man, a young man whom any woman not fastidious might like.", "게다가 그는 정말이지 아주 호감 가는 청년이었고, 까다롭지 않은 여자라면 누구든 좋아할 만한 청년이었다."], ["He was reckoned very handsome; his person much admired in general, though not by her, there being a want of elegance of feature which she could not dispense with:—but the girl who could be gratified by a Robert Martin’s riding about the country to get walnuts for her might very well be conquered by Mr.", "그는 아주 잘생겼다고들 했다. 그 사람됨은 대체로 많은 이의 찬탄을 받았다 — 그녀 자신은 아니었지만, 그녀로서는 도무지 양보할 수 없는 이목구비의 우아함이 그에게는 모자랐던 것이다."], ["Elton’s admiration.", "그러나 로버트 마틴이 호두를 구해다 주려고 들판을 누비고 다닌 것에 흐뭇해할 수 있는 아가씨라면, 미스터 엘턴의 흠모쯤에는 너끈히 마음을 빼앗길 만했다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter V", "# 제1권 5장"]], [["“I do not know what your opinion may be, Mrs.", "\"엠마와 해리엇 스미스 사이의 그 대단한 친밀함을 두고 부인께서 어떻게 보시는지는 모르겠습니다만,\""], ["Weston,” said Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말했다."], ["Knightley, “of this great intimacy between Emma and Harriet Smith, but I think it a bad thing.”", "\"저는 그것을 나쁜 일이라고 봅니다.\""]], [["“A bad thing!", "\"나쁜 일이라뇨!"], ["Do you really think it a bad thing?—why so?”", "정말로 나쁜 일이라고 보세요? 어째서요?\""]], [["“I think they will neither of them do the other any good.”", "\"두 사람 다 서로에게 아무런 득이 되지 못할 거라고 생각합니다.\""]], [["“You surprize me!", "\"놀랍군요!"], ["Emma must do Harriet good: and by supplying her with a new object of interest, Harriet may be said to do Emma good.", "엠마는 분명 해리엇에게 득이 될 거예요. 그리고 새로운 관심거리를 줌으로써, 해리엇도 엠마에게 득이 된다고 할 수 있고요."], ["I have been seeing their intimacy with the greatest pleasure.", "저는 두 사람의 친밀함을 더없이 흐뭇하게 지켜봐 왔는걸요."], ["How very differently we feel!—Not think they will do each other any good!", "우리가 어쩜 이리 다르게 느낄까요! 서로에게 아무 득이 안 되리라니요!"], ["This will certainly be the beginning of one of our quarrels about Emma, Mr. Knightley.”", "미스터 나이틀리, 이건 분명 엠마를 두고 벌이는 우리 말다툼의 시작이 되겠네요.\""]], [["“Perhaps you think I am come on purpose to quarrel with you, knowing Weston to be out, and that you must still fight your own battle.”", "\"제가 일부러 부인과 다투려고 찾아온 거라 여기실지도 모르겠군요. 웨스턴이 출타 중이니 부인 혼자서 싸움을 감당해야 한다는 걸 알고 말입니다.\""]], [["“Mr. Weston would undoubtedly support me, if he were here, for he thinks exactly as I do on the subject.", "\"미스터 웨스턴이 여기 계셨다면 분명 제 편을 들었을 거예요."], ["We were speaking of it only yesterday, and agreeing how fortunate it was for Emma, that there should be such a girl in Highbury for her to associate with.", "이 문제에 관해서라면 그분도 저와 똑같이 생각하시니까요. 바로 어제도 우리가 그 이야기를 나누며, 엠마가 어울릴 만한 그런 아가씨가 하이베리에 있다는 게 얼마나 다행한 일인지 의견을 모았는걸요."], ["Mr. Knightley, I shall not allow you to be a fair judge in this case.", "미스터 나이틀리, 저는 이 일에서 당신을 공정한 심판으로 인정해 드릴 수 없어요."], ["You are so much used to live alone, that you do not know the value of a companion; and, perhaps no man can be a good judge of the comfort a woman feels in the society of one of her own sex, after being used to it all her life.", "당신은 워낙 혼자 사는 데 익숙해서 말동무의 가치를 모르세요. 그리고 평생을 그렇게 지내 온 여자가 같은 여자의 벗에게서 느끼는 위안이 어떤 것인지, 그건 아마 어떤 남자도 제대로 가늠하지 못할 거예요."], ["I can imagine your objection to Harriet Smith.", "해리엇 스미스를 두고 당신이 어떤 반론을 펴실지는 짐작이 가요."], ["She is not the superior young woman which Emma’s friend ought to be.", "엠마의 벗이라면 마땅히 갖추어야 할, 그런 빼어난 아가씨는 못 되지요."], ["But on the other hand, as Emma wants to see her better informed, it will be an inducement to her to read more herself.", "하지만 다른 한편으로, 엠마가 해리엇을 더 식견 있는 사람으로 만들고 싶어 하니, 그게 엠마 자신도 더 많이 읽도록 이끄는 자극이 될 거예요."], ["They will read together.", "두 사람이 함께 책을 읽겠지요."], ["She means it, I know.”", "엠마가 그럴 작정인 걸 저는 알아요.\""]], [["“Emma has been meaning to read more ever since she was twelve years old.", "\"엠마는 열두 살 적부터 책을 더 많이 읽을 작정이었답니다. 여러 시기에 걸쳐 엠마가 작성해 둔, 차근차근 끝까지 읽으려던 책 목록을 저는 한두 번 본 게 아니에요."], ["I have seen a great many lists of her drawing-up at various times of books that she meant to read regularly through—and very good lists they were—very well chosen, and very neatly arranged—sometimes alphabetically, and sometimes by some other rule.", "아주 훌륭한 목록이었지요. 더없이 잘 골랐고, 더없이 정갈하게 정리돼 있었어요."], ["The list she drew up when only fourteen—I remember thinking it did her judgment so much credit, that I preserved it some time; and I dare say she may have made out a very good list now.", "어떤 때는 알파벳순으로, 또 어떤 때는 다른 규칙대로요. 겨우 열네 살에 작성한 그 목록은, 제가 보기에 엠마의 판단력을 어찌나 잘 보여 주던지, 한동안 간직해 두었을 정도였습니다."], ["But I have done with expecting any course of steady reading from Emma.", "지금쯤은 아마 또 아주 훌륭한 목록을 하나 만들어 두었겠지요. 하지만 엠마에게서 꾸준한 독서가 이어지기를 기대하는 일은 저는 진작에 접었습니다."], ["She will never submit to any thing requiring industry and patience, and a subjection of the fancy to the understanding.", "그 아이는 근면과 인내를, 그리고 공상을 이해력에 복종시키는 일을 결코 받아들이지 못해요. 미스 테일러도 자극하지 못한 일을, 해리엇 스미스가 해낼 리 없다고 저는 자신 있게 단언합니다."], ["Where Miss Taylor failed to stimulate, I may safely affirm that Harriet Smith will do nothing.—You never could persuade her to read half so much as you wished.—You know you could not.”", "부인께서도 바라시는 만큼 엠마를 책 읽게 만들지 못하셨잖아요. 못 하셨다는 걸 잘 아시면서.\""]], [["“I dare say,”", "\"제 생각엔,\""], ["replied Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인이 미소 지으며 답했다."], ["Weston, smiling, “that I thought so then;—but since we have parted, I can never remember Emma’s omitting to do any thing I wished.”", "\"그때는 저도 그렇게 여겼던 것 같아요. 하지만 우리가 헤어진 뒤로는, 제가 바라는 일을 엠마가 하지 않은 적이 한 번이라도 있었는지 도무지 기억나지 않는걸요.\""]], [["“There is hardly any desiring to refresh such a memory as that,”—said Mr.", "\"그런 종류의 기억이라면 굳이 되살릴 마음이 좀처럼 들지 않겠지요.\""], ["Knightley, feelingly; and for a moment or two he had done.", "미스터 나이틀리가 감회 어린 투로 말했다. 그러고는 한두 순간 입을 다물었다."], ["“But I,”", "\"하지만 저는,\""], ["he soon added, “who have had no such charm thrown over my senses, must still see, hear, and remember.", "그가 곧 덧붙였다. \"그런 마법에 감각을 사로잡힌 적이 없으니, 여전히 보고 듣고 기억할 수밖에 없습니다."], ["Emma is spoiled by being the cleverest of her family.", "엠마는 제 식구 중에서 가장 영리하다는 탓에 버릇이 나빠졌어요."], ["At ten years old, she had the misfortune of being able to answer questions which puzzled her sister at seventeen.", "열 살 적에 이미, 열일곱 살 언니를 쩔쩔매게 한 물음에 척척 답할 줄 아는 불운을 타고났지요."], ["She was always quick and assured: Isabella slow and diffident.", "늘 영리하고 자신만만했고, 이저벨라는 굼뜨고 수줍었습니다."], ["And ever since she was twelve, Emma has been mistress of the house and of you all.", "그리고 열두 살 이래로, 엠마는 그 집과 부인을 포함한 모두의 안주인 노릇을 해 왔어요."], ["In her mother she lost the only person able to cope with her.", "어머니를 잃으면서, 엠마는 자기를 감당할 수 있는 유일한 사람을 잃은 셈입니다."], ["She inherits her mother’s talents, and must have been under subjection to her.”", "엠마는 어머니의 재능을 물려받았으니, 어머니 밑에서라면 분명 복종하며 자랐을 테지요.\""]], [["“I should have been sorry, Mr. Knightley, to be dependent on your recommendation, had I quitted Mr.", "\"미스터 나이틀리, 제가 만일 우드하우스 댁을 떠나 다른 자리를 구해야 했다면, 당신의 추천에 기대야 하는 처지가 안 됐기를 바랄 따름이에요."], ["Woodhouse’s family and wanted another situation; I do not think you would have spoken a good word for me to any body.", "당신이라면 누구에게도 저를 좋게 말해 주지 않았을 것 같으니까요."], ["I am sure you always thought me unfit for the office I held.”", "당신은 분명 제가 맡은 그 직책에 어울리지 않는 사람이라고 늘 여기셨을 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said he, smiling.", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“You are better placed here; very fit for a wife, but not at all for a governess.", "\"부인은 여기에 계신 편이 더 좋아요. 아내로는 더없이 적격이지만, 가정교사로는 도무지 아니었습니다."], ["But you were preparing yourself to be an excellent wife all the time you were at Hartfield.", "그래도 부인은 하트필드에 계신 내내 훌륭한 아내가 될 준비를 하고 계셨던 거지요. 부인의 역량이 약속하는 만큼 엠마에게 온전한 교육을 베풀지는 못했을지 몰라도, 정작 부인은 엠마에게서 아주 훌륭한 교육을 받고 계셨던 겁니다."], ["You might not give Emma such a complete education as your powers would seem to promise; but you were receiving a very good education from her, on the very material matrimonial point of submitting your own will, and doing as you were bid; and if Weston had asked me to recommend him a wife, I should certainly have named Miss Taylor.”", "다름 아닌 혼인에서 가장 긴요한 그 대목 — 자기 뜻을 굽히고 시키는 대로 하는 법 말입니다. 그러니 웨스턴이 제게 아내를 추천해 달라고 했다면, 저는 분명 미스 테일러를 꼽았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고마워요."], ["There will be very little merit in making a good wife to such a man as Mr. Weston.”", "미스터 웨스턴 같은 분에게 좋은 아내가 되는 데야 무슨 큰 공이 있겠어요.\""]], [["“Why, to own the truth, I am afraid you are rather thrown away, and that with every disposition to bear, there will be nothing to be borne.", "\"실은 말입니다, 저는 부인이 차라리 그 재능이 아깝게 쓰이는 게 아닐까, 견디려는 온갖 마음가짐을 갖춰 두었건만 정작 견딜 거리가 하나도 없는 게 아닐까 걱정입니다."], ["We will not despair, however.", "그렇다고 절망할 일은 아니지요."], ["Weston may grow cross from the wantonness of comfort, or his son may plague him.”", "웨스턴이 안락함에 겨워 까다로워질 수도 있고, 아들이 그를 속 썩일 수도 있으니까요.\""]], [["“I hope not that.—It is not likely.", "\"그것만은 아니길 바라요. 그럴 것 같지도 않고요."], ["No, Mr. Knightley, do not foretell vexation from that quarter.”", "안 돼요, 미스터 나이틀리, 그쪽에서 골칫거리가 생기리라고 점치지 마세요.\""]], [["“Not I, indeed. I only name possibilities.", "\"점치는 거야 제가 아니지요."], ["I do not pretend to Emma’s genius for foretelling and guessing.", "저는 그저 있을 법한 일들을 들어 본 것뿐입니다. 점치고 알아맞히는 엠마의 천재성을 제가 흉내 낼 리야 있겠습니까."], ["I hope, with all my heart, the young man may be a Weston in merit, and a Churchill in fortune.—But Harriet Smith—I have not half done about Harriet Smith.", "진심으로 바라건대, 그 젊은이가 인품으로는 웨스턴 같고 재산으로는 처칠 같기를요. 하지만 해리엇 스미스 — 해리엇 스미스에 관해서는 아직 절반도 못 했습니다."], ["I think her the very worst sort of companion that Emma could possibly have.", "저는 그 아이가 엠마에게 있을 수 있는 가장 나쁜 부류의 말동무라고 봅니다."], ["She knows nothing herself, and looks upon Emma as knowing every thing.", "자기는 아무것도 모르면서, 엠마는 모든 걸 다 안다고 우러러보지요."], ["She is a flatterer in all her ways; and so much the worse, because undesigned.", "하는 짓마다 아첨이에요. 게다가 그게 의도된 게 아니라서 더 나쁩니다."], ["Her ignorance is hourly flattery.", "그 무지가 매시간 아첨인 셈이지요."], ["How can Emma imagine she has any thing to learn herself, while Harriet is presenting such a delightful inferiority?", "해리엇이 그토록 흐뭇한 열등함을 떡하니 내보이는 마당에, 엠마가 자기도 배울 게 있다고 어찌 생각이나 하겠습니까?"], ["And as for Harriet, I will venture to say that she cannot gain by the acquaintance.", "또 해리엇으로 말하자면, 그 아이가 이 사귐으로 얻을 게 없다고 저는 감히 말하겠습니다."], ["Hartfield will only put her out of conceit with all the other places she belongs to.", "하트필드는 그 아이가 본디 속한 다른 모든 곳을 못마땅하게 여기도록 만들 뿐이에요."], ["She will grow just refined enough to be uncomfortable with those among whom birth and circumstances have placed her home.", "그 아이는 딱 불편할 만큼만 세련되어져서, 출생과 형편이 정해 준 제 보금자리의 사람들과 어울리기 거북해질 겁니다."], ["I am much mistaken if Emma’s doctrines give any strength of mind, or tend at all to make a girl adapt herself rationally to the varieties of her situation in life.—They only give a little polish.”", "엠마의 가르침이 정신에 어떤 힘을 길러 준다거나, 아가씨가 제 삶의 갖가지 처지에 분별 있게 적응하도록 이끈다고 한다면 — 제가 크게 틀린 거겠지요. 그건 그저 약간의 광택만 입혀 줄 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“I either depend more upon Emma’s good sense than you do, or am more anxious for her present comfort; for I cannot lament the acquaintance.", "\"제가 당신보다 엠마의 양식을 더 믿거나, 아니면 엠마의 지금 행복을 더 염려하는 거겠지요. 저로서는 그 사귐을 한탄할 수가 없으니까요."], ["How well she looked last night!”", "엠마가 어젯밤에 얼마나 고와 보였는지 몰라요!\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["you would rather talk of her person than her mind, would you?", "부인은 엠마의 마음보다 외모를 이야기하고 싶으신 게로군요?"], ["Very well; I shall not attempt to deny Emma’s being pretty.”", "좋습니다. 엠마가 예쁘다는 것까지 부정할 생각은 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Pretty!", "\"예쁘다니요!"], ["say beautiful rather.", "차라리 아름답다고 하셔야죠."], ["Can you imagine any thing nearer perfect beauty than Emma altogether—face and figure?”", "얼굴이며 자태며, 엠마만큼 완벽한 아름다움에 가까운 걸 상상이나 하실 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["“I do not know what I could imagine, but I confess that I have seldom seen a face or figure more pleasing to me than hers.", "\"무얼 상상할 수 있을지는 모르겠지만, 솔직히 말해 엠마만큼 제 눈에 흡족한 얼굴이나 자태는 좀처럼 본 적이 없다는 건 인정하겠습니다."], ["But I am a partial old friend.”", "하지만 저야 한쪽으로 기운 오랜 벗이니까요.\""]], [["“Such an eye!—the true hazle eye—and so brilliant!", "\"그 눈매! 진짜 개암빛 눈동자가 어찌나 빛나는지!"], ["regular features, open countenance, with a complexion!", "반듯한 이목구비에, 환히 트인 표정에, 그 혈색이라니!"], ["oh!", "오!"], ["what a bloom of full health, and such a pretty height and size; such a firm and upright figure!", "무르익은 건강이 피워 낸 그 화사함, 그리고 그 어여쁜 키와 몸매, 그 단단하고 꼿꼿한 자태!"], ["There is health, not merely in her bloom, but in her air, her head, her glance.", "화사함만 건강한 게 아니라, 분위기며 머리를 두는 모양새며 눈길까지 건강이 배어 있어요."], ["One hears sometimes of a child being ‘the picture of health;’ now, Emma always gives me the idea of being the complete picture of grown-up health.", "어떤 아이를 두고 '건강의 화신'이라고들 하잖아요. 그런데 엠마는 늘 제게 다 자란 건강의 완벽한 화신 같다는 느낌을 줘요."], ["She is loveliness itself.", "사랑스러움 그 자체예요."], ["Mr. Knightley, is not she?”", "미스터 나이틀리, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“I have not a fault to find with her person,”", "\"엠마의 외모에는 트집 잡을 데가 없습니다.\""], ["he replied.", "그가 답했다."], ["“I think her all you describe.", "\"부인이 묘사하신 그대로라고 생각해요. 저도 엠마를 바라보는 게 좋습니다."], ["I love to look at her; and I will add this praise, that I do not think her personally vain.", "그리고 이 칭찬도 보태지요. 엠마가 제 외모를 두고 허영을 부리지는 않는다고 봅니다."], ["Considering how very handsome she is, she appears to be little occupied with it; her vanity lies another way.", "그토록 빼어나게 아름다운데도, 정작 그쪽에는 별로 마음을 쏟지 않는 것 같아요. 엠마의 허영은 다른 데 있습니다."], ["Mrs. Weston, I am not to be talked out of my dislike of Harriet Smith, or my dread of its doing them both harm.”", "웨스턴 부인, 해리엇 스미스에 대한 제 못마땅함이나, 그 사귐이 두 사람 모두에게 해가 되리라는 제 우려는, 말로 거두어지지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“And I, Mr. Knightley, am equally stout in my confidence of its not doing them any harm.", "\"그리고 저는, 미스터 나이틀리, 그 사귐이 두 사람에게 아무런 해도 끼치지 않으리라는 확신에서 똑같이 굳건해요."], ["With all dear Emma’s little faults, she is an excellent creature.", "우리 사랑스러운 엠마에게 자잘한 결점이 다 있다 해도, 그 아이는 더없이 훌륭한 사람이에요. 더 나은 딸을, 더 다정한 동생을, 더 진실한 벗을 어디서 보겠어요?"], ["Where shall we see a better daughter, or a kinder sister, or a truer friend?", "아니에요, 아니에요. 그 아이에게는 믿어도 좋을 자질이 있어요."], ["No, no; she has qualities which may be trusted; she will never lead any one really wrong; she will make no lasting blunder; where Emma errs once, she is in the right a hundred times.”", "누구를 정말로 그릇된 길로 이끄는 일은 결코 없을 거고, 두고두고 후회할 실수도 저지르지 않을 거예요. 엠마가 한 번 그르치는 동안, 백 번은 옳을 사람이니까요.\""]], [["“Very well; I will not plague you any more.", "\"좋습니다. 더는 부인을 성가시게 하지 않겠습니다."], ["Emma shall be an angel, and I will keep my spleen to myself till Christmas brings John and Isabella.", "엠마는 천사라 칩시다. 저는 성탄절에 존과 이저벨라가 올 때까지 제 울화는 혼자 삭이지요."], ["John loves Emma with a reasonable and therefore not a blind affection, and Isabella always thinks as he does; except when he is not quite frightened enough about the children.", "존은 엠마를 합당한, 그래서 맹목적이지 않은 애정으로 사랑하고, 이저벨라는 늘 남편과 똑같이 생각하니까요. 다만 남편이 아이들 일로 충분히 겁먹지 않을 때만 빼고요."], ["I am sure of having their opinions with me.”", "두 사람의 의견은 제 편이리라 확신합니다.\""]], [["“I know that you all love her really too well to be unjust or unkind; but excuse me, Mr.", "\"당신들 모두 그 아이를 정말이지 너무 깊이 사랑하니, 불공정하거나 매정하게 굴 리야 없겠지요."], ["Knightley, if I take the liberty (I consider myself, you know, as having somewhat of the privilege of speech that Emma’s mother might have had) the liberty of hinting that I do not think any possible good can arise from Harriet Smith’s intimacy being made a matter of much discussion among you.", "하지만 미스터 나이틀리, 제가 한마디 무람없이 거들어도 양해해 주세요. (아시다시피 저는 엠마의 어머니가 가졌을 법한 발언권을 얼마쯤은 가졌다고 자처하니까요.) 무람없이 한 가지 일러두자면, 해리엇 스미스와의 친밀함을 당신들 사이에서 이러쿵저러쿵 많이 따지는 일에서 무슨 좋은 결과가 나올 수 있을 것 같지가 않아요."], ["Pray excuse me; but supposing any little inconvenience may be apprehended from the intimacy, it cannot be expected that Emma, accountable to nobody but her father, who perfectly approves the acquaintance, should put an end to it, so long as it is a source of pleasure to herself.", "부디 양해해 주세요. 그러나 설령 그 친밀함에서 자잘한 불편이 우려된다 한들, 그 사귐을 흔쾌히 인정하는 아버지 말고는 누구에게도 책임질 일이 없는 엠마가, 그게 그 아이 자신에게 기쁨의 원천인 한, 그 사귐을 끝내리라고는 기대할 수 없는 노릇이지요."], ["It has been so many years my province to give advice, that you cannot be surprized, Mr.", "조언하는 일이 제 소관이었던 게 워낙 여러 해라서요."], ["Knightley, at this little remains of office.”", "미스터 나이틀리, 직분의 이런 자투리가 남아 있다 한들 놀라지는 마세요.\""]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"천만에요.\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["cried he; “I am much obliged to you for it.", "\"그 점, 부인께 무척 감사드립니다. 아주 좋은 조언이에요."], ["It is very good advice, and it shall have a better fate than your advice has often found; for it shall be attended to.”", "그리고 이 조언은 부인의 조언들이 흔히 맞닥뜨린 운명보다 더 나은 운명을 누리게 될 겁니다. 받들어질 테니까요.\""]], [["“Mrs. John Knightley is easily alarmed, and might be made unhappy about her sister.”", "\"미시즈 존 나이틀리는 쉬이 놀라는 사람이라, 동생 일로 마음이 상할 수도 있어요.\""]], [["“Be satisfied,”", "\"안심하십시오.\""], ["said he, “I will not raise any outcry.", "그가 말했다. \"저는 어떤 소동도 일으키지 않을 겁니다."], ["I will keep my ill-humour to myself.", "제 언짢음은 혼자 간직하지요."], ["I have a very sincere interest in Emma.", "저는 엠마에게 아주 진실한 관심을 두고 있습니다."], ["Isabella does not seem more my sister; has never excited a greater interest; perhaps hardly so great.", "제게 이저벨라가 엠마보다 더 누이 같지도 않고, 더 큰 관심을 불러일으킨 적도 없어요. 어쩌면 그만큼도 못 되지요."], ["There is an anxiety, a curiosity in what one feels for Emma.", "엠마를 향해 느끼는 마음에는 어떤 염려, 어떤 궁금함이 있습니다."], ["I wonder what will become of her!”", "저 아이가 앞으로 어찌 될지 궁금하단 말입니다!\""]], [["“So do I,” said Mrs.", "\"저도 그래요.\""], ["Weston gently, “very much.”", "웨스턴 부인이 부드럽게 말했다. \"아주 많이요.\""]], [["“She always declares she will never marry, which, of course, means just nothing at all.", "\"엠마는 늘 자기는 결코 결혼하지 않을 거라고 공언하지요. 물론 그건 아무 뜻도 없는 소리지만요."], ["But I have no idea that she has yet ever seen a man she cared for.", "하지만 그 아이가 지금껏 마음에 둘 만한 남자를 본 적이 있을 것 같지는 않습니다."], ["It would not be a bad thing for her to be very much in love with a proper object.", "합당한 상대에게 흠뻑 빠져 보는 것도 엠마에게 나쁘지 않을 텐데요."], ["I should like to see Emma in love, and in some doubt of a return; it would do her good.", "저는 엠마가 사랑에 빠지되, 그 사랑이 되돌아올지 어떨지 얼마쯤 마음 졸이는 모습을 보고 싶습니다. 그게 그 아이에게 약이 될 테니까요."], ["But there is nobody hereabouts to attach her; and she goes so seldom from home.”", "하지만 이 근방에는 엠마의 마음을 붙들 사람이 없고, 그 아이는 좀처럼 집을 떠나는 일이 없으니 말입니다.\""]], [["“There does, indeed, seem as little to tempt her to break her resolution at present,”", "\"정말이지 지금으로서는 그 아이가 제 결심을 깰 만한 유혹거리가 거의 없는 것 같아요.\""], ["said Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인이 말했다."], ["Weston, “as can well be; and while she is so happy at Hartfield, I cannot wish her to be forming any attachment which would be creating such difficulties on poor Mr.", "\"그럴 만한 게 도무지 없다시피 하지요. 그리고 그 아이가 하트필드에서 그토록 행복한 마당에, 가엾은 미스터 우드하우스 때문에 그런 곤란을 빚을 연정을 품게 되기를 저로서는 바랄 수가 없어요."], ["Woodhouse’s account.", "지금 엠마에게 혼인을 권하지는 않아요."], ["I do not recommend matrimony at present to Emma, though I mean no slight to the state, I assure you.”", "그렇다고 혼인이라는 것을 깔보는 건 결코 아니에요, 정말이에요.\""]], [["Part of her meaning was to conceal some favourite thoughts of her own and Mr. Weston’s on the subject, as much as possible.", "그녀가 그렇게 말한 데에는, 이 문제를 두고 자신과 미스터 웨스턴이 은근히 품어 온 어떤 속내를 되도록 감추려는 뜻도 얼마쯤 있었다."], ["There were wishes at Randalls respecting Emma’s destiny, but it was not desirable to have them suspected; and the quiet transition which Mr.", "랜덜스에는 엠마의 앞날에 관한 어떤 바람이 있었으나, 그것이 의심받는 일은 바람직하지 않았다. 그리고 곧이어 미스터 나이틀리가 \"웨스턴은 날씨를 어찌 보던가요?"], ["Knightley soon afterwards made to “What does Weston think of the weather; shall we have rain?” convinced her that he had nothing more to say or surmise about Hartfield.", "비가 오려나요?\"라고 조용히 화제를 돌리는 것을 보고, 그녀는 그가 하트필드에 관해 더는 할 말도, 짐작할 일도 없다는 것을 확신했다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter VI", "# 제1권 6장"]], [["Emma could not feel a doubt of having given Harriet’s fancy a proper direction and raised the gratitude of her young vanity to a very good purpose, for she found her decidedly more sensible than before of Mr.", "엠마는 해리엇의 공상을 올바른 방향으로 이끌었으며, 어린 허영심에서 우러난 고마움을 아주 좋은 목적에 쏟게 했다는 데 의심을 품을 수 없었다."], ["Elton’s being a remarkably handsome man, with most agreeable manners; and as she had no hesitation in following up the assurance of his admiration by agreeable hints, she was soon pretty confident of creating as much liking on Harriet’s side, as there could be any occasion for.", "해리엇이 미스터 엘턴을 놀랄 만큼 잘생긴 남자에 더없이 호감 가는 태도를 지닌 사람으로 여기는 정도가, 전보다 한결 또렷해진 것을 알아차렸기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 흠모하고 있다는 확신에 호감 가는 암시를 슬쩍슬쩍 덧붙이는 데 조금도 망설임이 없었으므로, 엠마는 해리엇 쪽에서도 필요한 만큼의 호감이 자라나리라고 곧 꽤 자신하게 되었다."], ["She was quite convinced of Mr. Elton’s being in the fairest way of falling in love, if not in love already.", "미스터 엘턴이 아직 사랑에 빠진 것은 아닐지언정, 사랑에 빠지기에 더없이 좋은 길에 들어섰다는 것을 그녀는 확신했다."], ["She had no scruple with regard to him.", "그에 대해서는 거리낄 것이 없었다."], ["He talked of Harriet, and praised her so warmly, that she could not suppose any thing wanting which a little time would not add.", "그는 해리엇 이야기를 했고, 어찌나 따뜻하게 칭찬을 하는지, 시간이 조금만 더해 주면 모자랄 것이 하나도 없으리라 여겨졌다."], ["His perception of the striking improvement of Harriet’s manner, since her introduction at Hartfield, was not one of the least agreeable proofs of his growing attachment.", "해리엇의 태도가 하트필드에 드나든 뒤로 눈에 띄게 나아졌음을 알아본 것도, 그의 연정이 자라나고 있다는 적잖이 흐뭇한 증거 가운데 하나였다."]], [["“You have given Miss Smith all that she required,”", "\"미스 스미스에게 필요한 것을 모두 갖추어 주셨군요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “you have made her graceful and easy.", "\"기품 있고 편안한 사람으로 만들어 주셨어요."], ["She was a beautiful creature when she came to you, but, in my opinion, the attractions you have added are infinitely superior to what she received from nature.”", "미스 우드하우스에게 처음 왔을 때도 아름다운 사람이었지만, 제 생각으로는 보태 주신 매력이 타고난 것보다 한없이 윗길입니다.\""]], [["“I am glad you think I have been useful to her; but Harriet only wanted drawing out, and receiving a few, very few hints.", "\"제가 그 애에게 도움이 되었다고 생각해 주시니 기쁘네요. 하지만 해리엇은 그저 끌어내 주고, 아주 약간의 암시만 받으면 될 아이였어요."], ["She had all the natural grace of sweetness of temper and artlessness in herself.", "다정한 성품과 꾸밈없음에서 우러나는 타고난 기품을 본래 다 지니고 있었거든요."], ["I have done very little.”", "제가 한 일은 별것 없어요.\""]], [["“If it were admissible to contradict a lady,”", "\"숙녀의 말씀에 반박하는 것이 허락된다면야.\""], ["said the gallant Mr. Elton—", "점잖은 미스터 엘턴이 말했다."]], [["“I have perhaps given her a little more decision of character, have taught her to think on points which had not fallen in her way before.”", "\"제가 어쩌면 그 애에게 성격에 결단력을 조금 더해 주었는지는 모르겠네요. 전에는 미처 생각해 보지 못했던 문제들을 생각하도록 가르쳐 주었으니까요.\""]], [["“Exactly so; that is what principally strikes me.", "\"바로 그겁니다. 제 눈에 가장 두드러진 것이 바로 그 점이에요."], ["So much superadded decision of character!", "그토록 더해진 성격의 결단력이라니!"], ["Skilful has been the hand!”", "솜씨 좋은 손길이었군요!\""]], [["“Great has been the pleasure, I am sure.", "\"정말 더없는 기쁨이었답니다."], ["I never met with a disposition more truly amiable.”", "그토록 진실로 사랑스러운 천성을 가진 사람은 처음 만나 봤어요.\""]], [["“I have no doubt of it.”", "\"의심할 여지가 없지요.\""], ["And it was spoken with a sort of sighing animation, which had a vast deal of the lover.", "그렇게 말하는 그의 어조에는 한숨 섞인 활기가 깃들어 있어, 연인다운 구석이 다분했다."], ["She was not less pleased another day with the manner in which he seconded a sudden wish of hers, to have Harriet’s picture.", "다른 날에는 해리엇의 초상화를 갖고 싶다는 그녀의 갑작스러운 바람을 그가 거드는 태도에도 그녀는 못지않게 흐뭇했다."]], [["“Did you ever have your likeness taken, Harriet?”", "\"해리엇, 초상화를 그려 본 적이 있나요?\""], ["said she: “did you ever sit for your picture?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"초상화 모델로 앉아 본 적은요?\""]], [["Harriet was on the point of leaving the room, and only stopt to say, with a very interesting naïveté,", "해리엇은 막 방을 나서려던 참이었는데, 멈춰 서서 무척 흥미로운 천진함으로 이렇게만 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["dear, no, never.”", "아니에요, 한 번도요.\""]], [["No sooner was she out of sight, than Emma exclaimed,", "그녀가 시야에서 사라지자마자 엠마가 외쳤다."]], [["“What an exquisite possession a good picture of her would be!", "\"저 애를 잘 그린 초상화가 있다면 얼마나 더없이 귀한 보물이겠어요!"], ["I would give any money for it.", "얼마를 주고서라도 갖고 싶을 정도예요."], ["I almost long to attempt her likeness myself.", "제가 직접 저 애를 그려 보고 싶을 지경이라니까요."], ["You do not know it I dare say, but two or three years ago I had a great passion for taking likenesses, and attempted several of my friends, and was thought to have a tolerable eye in general.", "모르고 계시겠지만, 두어 해 전에 저는 초상화에 무척 빠져서 친구들 몇몇을 그려 보았는데, 대체로 눈썰미가 그럭저럭 괜찮다는 평을 들었답니다."], ["But from one cause or another, I gave it up in disgust.", "그런데 이런저런 까닭으로 진력이 나서 손을 놔 버렸지요."], ["But really, I could almost venture, if Harriet would sit to me.", "하지만 정말이지, 해리엇이 모델로 앉아만 준다면 한번 해 볼 만도 해요."], ["It would be such a delight to have her picture!”", "저 애의 초상화를 갖는다면 얼마나 기쁠까요!\""]], [["“Let me entreat you,”", "\"제발 부탁드립니다.\""], ["cried Mr. Elton; “it would indeed be a delight!", "미스터 엘턴이 외쳤다."], ["Let me entreat you, Miss Woodhouse, to exercise so charming a talent in favour of your friend.", "\"정말이지 더없는 기쁨일 거예요! 미스 우드하우스, 부디 그 매력 넘치는 재능을 친구분을 위해 발휘해 주십시오."], ["I know what your drawings are.", "저는 미스 우드하우스의 그림이 어떤지 압니다."], ["How could you suppose me ignorant?", "제가 모를 거라고 생각하셨나요?"], ["Is not this room rich in specimens of your landscapes and flowers; and has not Mrs.", "이 방만 해도 미스 우드하우스의 풍경화와 꽃 그림이 그득하지 않습니까."], ["Weston some inimitable figure-pieces in her drawing-room, at Randalls?”", "랜덜스의 웨스턴 부인 응접실에도 누구도 흉내 못 낼 인물화가 몇 점 걸려 있지 않습니까?\""]], [["Yes, good man!—thought Emma—but what has all that to do with taking likenesses?", "그래요, 착한 양반!—엠마는 생각했다—하지만 그게 다 초상화 그리는 것과 무슨 상관이람?"], ["You know nothing of drawing.", "당신은 그림에 대해 아무것도 모르잖아요."], ["Don’t pretend to be in raptures about mine.", "내 그림에 황홀해하는 척하지 말아요."], ["Keep your raptures for Harriet’s face.", "그 황홀함은 해리엇의 얼굴을 위해 아껴 두세요."], ["“Well, if you give me such kind encouragement, Mr. Elton, I believe I shall try what I can do.", "\"그래요, 그렇게 친절히 격려해 주신다면, 미스터 엘턴, 제가 할 수 있는 데까지 한번 해 봐야겠네요."], ["Harriet’s features are very delicate, which makes a likeness difficult; and yet there is a peculiarity in the shape of the eye and the lines about the mouth which one ought to catch.”", "해리엇의 이목구비는 무척 섬세해서 닮게 그리기가 어렵답니다. 그래도 눈매와 입가의 선에 독특한 데가 있으니, 그걸 꼭 잡아내야겠지요.\""]], [["“Exactly so—The shape of the eye and the lines about the mouth—I have not a doubt of your success.", "\"바로 그렇습니다. 눈매와 입가의 선이라—성공하시리라는 걸 추호도 의심치 않습니다."], ["Pray, pray attempt it.", "부디, 부디 한번 해 보십시오."], ["As you will do it, it will indeed, to use your own words, be an exquisite possession.”", "미스 우드하우스가 그리신다면, 정말이지 당신 말씀 그대로, 더없이 귀한 보물이 될 거예요.\""]], [["“But I am afraid, Mr. Elton, Harriet will not like to sit.", "\"하지만 미스터 엘턴, 해리엇이 모델로 앉기를 싫어할까 봐 걱정이에요."], ["She thinks so little of her own beauty.", "자기 미모를 워낙 대수롭잖게 여기거든요."], ["Did not you observe her manner of answering me?", "제 물음에 답하던 태도를 못 보셨어요?"], ["How completely it meant, ‘why should my picture be drawn?’”", "'내 초상화를 왜 그려요?' 하는 뜻이 어찌나 역력하던지요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["yes, I observed it, I assure you.", "그럼요, 저도 봤습니다, 정말이에요."], ["It was not lost on me.", "놓치지 않았어요."], ["But still I cannot imagine she would not be persuaded.”", "하지만 그래도 설득이 안 될 거라고는 도무지 생각되지 않는걸요.\""]], [["Harriet was soon back again, and the proposal almost immediately made; and she had no scruples which could stand many minutes against the earnest pressing of both the others.", "해리엇은 곧 돌아왔고, 제안은 거의 곧바로 꺼내졌다. 그녀의 거리낌은 두 사람의 진지한 채근 앞에서 몇 분도 버티지 못했다."], ["Emma wished to go to work directly, and therefore produced the portfolio containing her various attempts at portraits, for not one of them had ever been finished, that they might decide together on the best size for Harriet.", "엠마는 곧장 작업에 들어가고 싶었으므로, 해리엇에게 맞는 가장 좋은 크기를 함께 정해 보려고, 자신의 갖가지 초상화 시도를 담은 화첩을 꺼내 왔다—그 가운데 완성된 것은 단 한 점도 없었다."], ["Her many beginnings were displayed.", "그녀가 시작만 해 둔 많은 그림이 펼쳐졌다."], ["Miniatures, half-lengths, whole-lengths, pencil, crayon, and water-colours had been all tried in turn.", "세밀화, 반신상, 전신상, 연필화, 크레용화, 수채화—차례로 다 시도해 본 터였다."], ["She had always wanted to do every thing, and had made more progress both in drawing and music than many might have done with so little labour as she would ever submit to.", "그녀는 늘 무엇이든 다 해 보고 싶어 했고, 그림에서나 음악에서나, 어지간히 손에 익혔을 만한 노력보다 훨씬 적게 들이고도 어지간한 사람들보다 많이 나아간 편이었다."], ["She played and sang;—and drew in almost every style; but steadiness had always been wanting; and in nothing had she approached the degree of excellence which she would have been glad to command, and ought not to have failed of.", "그녀는 연주도 하고 노래도 불렀으며, 거의 모든 화풍으로 그림도 그렸다. 그러나 늘 끈기가 모자랐고, 어느 것 하나도, 마음만 먹었다면 능히 이르렀을 법한—그리고 마땅히 실패하지 말았어야 할—그 탁월함의 경지에는 다가가지 못했다."], ["She was not much deceived as to her own skill either as an artist or a musician, but she was not unwilling to have others deceived, or sorry to know her reputation for accomplishment often higher than it deserved.", "화가로서든 음악가로서든 제 솜씨에 대해 크게 속고 있는 것은 아니었으나, 남들이 속는 것을 마다하지도 않았고, 제 재주에 대한 평판이 받을 만한 것보다 흔히 높다는 것을 알고도 서운해하지 않았다."]], [["There was merit in every drawing—in the least finished, perhaps the most; her style was spirited; but had there been much less, or had there been ten times more, the delight and admiration of her two companions would have been the same.", "어느 그림에나 나름의 좋은 점이 있었다—가장 덜 완성된 것에 어쩌면 가장 많이 있었는지도 모른다. 그녀의 화풍은 생기가 넘쳤다."], ["They were both in ecstasies.", "그러나 그것이 훨씬 못했다 한들, 혹은 열 곱절 더 뛰어났다 한들, 두 동행의 기쁨과 감탄은 마찬가지였으리라."], ["A likeness pleases every body; and Miss Woodhouse’s performances must be capital.", "둘 다 황홀경에 빠져 있었다. 닮게 그린 초상화란 누구에게나 기쁨을 주는 법이고, 미스 우드하우스의 솜씨라면 당연히 일품일 터였다."]], [["“No great variety of faces for you,”", "\"두 분께 보여 드릴 얼굴이 그리 다양하지는 못해요.\""], ["said Emma.", "엠마가 말했다."], ["“I had only my own family to study from.", "\"본보기로 삼을 게 제 가족뿐이었거든요."], ["There is my father—another of my father—but the idea of sitting for his picture made him so nervous, that I could only take him by stealth; neither of them very like therefore.", "이건 우리 아버지—이건 또 우리 아버지인데—초상화 모델로 앉으신다는 생각만 해도 어찌나 안절부절못하시던지, 몰래 그리는 수밖에 없었지 뭐예요. 그래서 둘 다 그리 닮지는 않았어요."], ["Mrs. Weston again, and again, and again, you see.", "이건 또 웨스턴 부인이에요, 보세요, 또 웨스턴 부인이고, 또 웨스턴 부인이죠."], ["Dear Mrs. Weston!", "사랑스러운 웨스턴 부인!"], ["always my kindest friend on every occasion.", "무슨 일에든 언제나 제게 가장 다정한 벗이에요."], ["She would sit whenever I asked her.", "부탁만 하면 언제든 앉아 주셨거든요. 이건 우리 언니예요."], ["There is my sister; and really quite her own little elegant figure!—and the face not unlike.", "정말이지 언니 특유의 그 자그맣고 우아한 자태 그대로죠!—얼굴도 그리 안 닮은 건 아니고요. 좀 더 오래 앉아 줬더라면 아주 닮게 그렸을 텐데, 어찌나 제게 네 아이를 그리라고 다그치던지 가만히 있지를 않았어요."], ["I should have made a good likeness of her, if she would have sat longer, but she was in such a hurry to have me draw her four children that she would not be quiet.", "그래서 여기, 그 네 아이 가운데 셋을 그려 본 것들이 죄다 나오네요. 자, 보세요, 헨리와 존과 벨라가 종이 이쪽 끝에서 저쪽 끝까지 있죠."], ["Then, here come all my attempts at three of those four children;—there they are, Henry and John and Bella, from one end of the sheet to the other, and any one of them might do for any one of the rest.", "누구를 골라 봐도 그 누구라 해도 통할 만하답니다. 언니가 어찌나 애들을 그려 달라고 안달이던지 거절할 수가 없었어요."], ["She was so eager to have them drawn that I could not refuse; but there is no making children of three or four years old stand still you know; nor can it be very easy to take any likeness of them, beyond the air and complexion, unless they are coarser featured than any of mama’s children ever were.", "하지만 세 살 네 살 먹은 아이들을 가만히 세워 둘 수는 없는 노릇이잖아요. 게다가 분위기와 혈색을 넘어 닮게 그리기란—우리 언니 자식들이 어느 누구보다 이목구비가 거칠기라도 했다면 또 모를까—여간 어려운 일이 아니에요."], ["Here is my sketch of the fourth, who was a baby.", "이건 넷째를 그린 거예요, 그땐 갓난아기였죠."], ["I took him as he was sleeping on the sofa, and it is as strong a likeness of his cockade as you would wish to see.", "소파에서 잠든 모습 그대로 그렸는데, 머리쓰개만큼은 더 바랄 게 없을 만큼 닮게 그렸답니다."], ["He had nestled down his head most conveniently.", "머리를 어찌나 알맞게 파묻고 있던지요."], ["That’s very like.", "그건 정말 닮았어요."], ["I am rather proud of little George.", "어린 조지는 좀 자랑스럽다니까요."], ["The corner of the sofa is very good.", "소파 모서리도 아주 잘 됐고요."], ["Then here is my last,”—unclosing a pretty sketch of a gentleman in small size, whole-length—“my last and my best—my brother, Mr. John Knightley.—This did not want much of being finished, when I put it away in a pet, and vowed I would never take another likeness.", "그리고 이게 마지막 거예요.\"—신사를 작은 크기로 그린 어여쁜 전신상 스케치를 펼치며—\"마지막이자 가장 잘된 거죠—우리 형부, 미스터 존 나이틀리예요.—이건 거의 완성될 뻔하다가 제가 토라져서 치워 버리고는, 다시는 누구의 초상화도 그리지 않겠다고 맹세해 버렸지 뭐예요."], ["I could not help being provoked; for after all my pains, and when I had really made a very good likeness of it—(Mrs.", "약이 오를 수밖에 없었어요."], ["Weston and I were quite agreed in thinking it very like)—only too handsome—too flattering—but that was a fault on the right side”—after all this, came poor dear Isabella’s cold approbation of—“Yes, it was a little like—but to be sure it did not do him justice.", "그 온갖 공을 들여서 정말 아주 닮게 그려 놓았는데—(웨스턴 부인도 저도 정말 빼다 박았다는 데 의견이 딱 맞았어요)—그저 너무 잘생기게, 너무 후하게 그렸을 뿐이었지만, 그래 봐야 그건 좋은 쪽으로 난 흠이잖아요—이 모든 게 다 끝난 다음에, 가엾고 사랑스러운 이저벨라가 무덤덤하게 하는 칭찬이라는 게 고작—'그래, 좀 닮긴 했네—그래도 정말이지 그이를 제대로 살리진 못했어'였어요. 우리는 형부가 모델로 앉도록 설득하느라 어지간히 애를 먹었거든요."], ["We had had a great deal of trouble in persuading him to sit at all.", "큰 선심이라도 쓰는 양 굴어서, 도무지 더는 못 견디겠더라고요."], ["It was made a great favour of; and altogether it was more than I could bear; and so I never would finish it, to have it apologised over as an unfavourable likeness, to every morning visitor in Brunswick Square;—and, as I said, I did then forswear ever drawing any body again.", "그래서 끝내 완성하지 않기로 했어요. 안 그러면 브런즈윅 스퀘어로 아침마다 찾아오는 손님들한테 일일이, 잘 안 닮은 초상화라고 변명을 늘어놓아야 할 테니까요.—그래서, 말씀드렸듯이, 그때 다시는 누구도 그리지 않겠다고 작정했던 거예요."], ["But for Harriet’s sake, or rather for my own, and as there are no husbands and wives in the case at present, I will break my resolution now.”", "하지만 해리엇을 위해서, 아니 차라리 저 자신을 위해서, 게다가 지금으로서는 부부가 얽힌 경우도 아니니, 이제 그 결심을 깨려고요.\""]], [["Mr. Elton seemed very properly struck and delighted by the idea, and was repeating, “No husbands and wives in the case at present indeed, as you observe.", "미스터 엘턴은 그 말에 적잖이 마음이 동하고 흐뭇해진 듯이, \"지금으로서는 부부가 얽힌 경우도 아니지요, 정말이지 말씀하신 대로요."], ["Exactly so.", "바로 그렇습니다."], ["No husbands and wives,”", "부부가 얽힌 경우가 아니지요.\""], ["with so interesting a consciousness, that Emma began to consider whether she had not better leave them together at once.", "하고 어찌나 의미심장하게 곱씹던지, 엠마는 차라리 지금 당장 둘만 남겨 두는 편이 낫지 않을까 생각하기 시작했다."], ["But as she wanted to be drawing, the declaration must wait a little longer.", "그러나 그림을 그리고 싶었으므로, 그 선언은 좀 더 미루어 두는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["She had soon fixed on the size and sort of portrait.", "그녀는 곧 초상화의 크기와 종류를 정했다."], ["It was to be a whole-length in water-colours, like Mr. John Knightley’s, and was destined, if she could please herself, to hold a very honourable station over the mantelpiece.", "미스터 존 나이틀리의 것처럼 수채 전신상으로 하기로 했고, 마음에 흡족하게만 그려진다면 벽난로 선반 위 아주 영예로운 자리를 차지할 운명이었다."]], [["The sitting began; and Harriet, smiling and blushing, and afraid of not keeping her attitude and countenance, presented a very sweet mixture of youthful expression to the steady eyes of the artist.", "모델로 앉히는 일이 시작되었다. 해리엇은 미소 짓고 얼굴을 붉히며, 자세와 표정을 제대로 지키지 못할까 봐 안절부절못했으니, 화가의 차분한 눈에는 더없이 사랑스러운 앳된 표정의 어우러짐이 비쳤다."], ["But there was no doing any thing, with Mr. Elton fidgeting behind her and watching every touch.", "그러나 미스터 엘턴이 뒤에서 안절부절 서성이며 붓질 하나하나를 지켜보는 통에 도무지 아무것도 할 수가 없었다."], ["She gave him credit for stationing himself where he might gaze and gaze again without offence; but was really obliged to put an end to it, and request him to place himself elsewhere.", "그가 흠 잡힐 일 없이 마음껏 보고 또 볼 수 있는 자리를 잡은 것은 가상한 일이었으나, 결국 그녀는 그것을 그만두게 하고 다른 데로 가 달라고 청할 수밖에 없었다."], ["It then occurred to her to employ him in reading.", "그러다 문득 그에게 책을 읽혀야겠다는 생각이 떠올랐다."]], [["“If he would be so good as to read to them, it would be a kindness indeed!", "\"그가 친절을 베풀어 책을 읽어 준다면 그야말로 고마운 일일 텐데!"], ["It would amuse away the difficulties of her part, and lessen the irksomeness of Miss Smith’s.”", "그러면 그녀 쪽의 어려움을 잊게 해 주고, 미스 스미스 쪽의 따분함도 덜어 줄 테니까.\""]], [["Mr. Elton was only too happy.", "미스터 엘턴은 더없이 기뻐했다."], ["Harriet listened, and Emma drew in peace.", "해리엇은 귀를 기울였고, 엠마는 평온히 그림을 그렸다. 그가 여전히 자주 와서 들여다보는 것은 너그러이 봐줄 수밖에 없었다."], ["She must allow him to be still frequently coming to look; any thing less would certainly have been too little in a lover; and he was ready at the smallest intermission of the pencil, to jump up and see the progress, and be charmed.—There was no being displeased with such an encourager, for his admiration made him discern a likeness almost before it was possible.", "그보다 덜했다면 연인으로서 너무 미적지근했을 테니까. 그는 붓이 잠깐만 멈춰도 벌떡 일어나 진척을 보고 황홀해할 채비가 되어 있었다.—이런 격려자에게 언짢아할 수는 없는 노릇이었으니, 그의 감탄은 닮은 점이 채 드러나기도 전에 그것을 알아보게 만들 정도였다."], ["She could not respect his eye, but his love and his complaisance were unexceptionable.", "그의 눈썰미는 존경할 수 없었으나, 그의 사랑과 살가움만큼은 흠잡을 데가 없었다."]], [["The sitting was altogether very satisfactory; she was quite enough pleased with the first day’s sketch to wish to go on.", "모델로 앉히는 일은 전체적으로 무척 만족스러웠다. 첫날의 스케치에 그녀는 계속 이어 갈 마음이 들 만큼 흡족했다."], ["There was no want of likeness, she had been fortunate in the attitude, and as she meant to throw in a little improvement to the figure, to give a little more height, and considerably more elegance, she had great confidence of its being in every way a pretty drawing at last, and of its filling its destined place with credit to them both—a standing memorial of the beauty of one, the skill of the other, and the friendship of both; with as many other agreeable associations as Mr.", "닮은 점이 모자라지 않았고, 자세도 운 좋게 잘 잡혔다."], ["Elton’s very promising attachment was likely to add.", "게다가 자태에 약간 손을 보태 키를 조금 더 키우고 우아함을 한결 더 보탤 작정이었으므로, 끝내는 모든 면에서 어여쁜 그림이 되어, 정해진 자리를 두 사람 모두에게 영예로이 채워 주리라 크게 자신했다—한쪽의 미모와 다른 한쪽의 솜씨, 그리고 둘의 우정을 길이 기리는 기념물이 되리라고, 거기에 미스터 엘턴의 더없이 유망한 연정이 보태 줄 법한 온갖 흐뭇한 연상까지 곁들여서."]], [["Harriet was to sit again the next day; and Mr. Elton, just as he ought, entreated for the permission of attending and reading to them again.", "해리엇은 이튿날 다시 앉기로 했고, 미스터 엘턴은 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로, 다시 와서 책을 읽어 주게 해 달라고 청했다."]], [["“By all means.", "\"그럼요."], ["We shall be most happy to consider you as one of the party.”", "함께해 주시면 저희야 더없이 기쁘죠.\""]], [["The same civilities and courtesies, the same success and satisfaction, took place on the morrow, and accompanied the whole progress of the picture, which was rapid and happy.", "이튿날에도 똑같은 정중함과 예의, 똑같은 성공과 만족이 펼쳐졌고, 그것은 빠르고 흐뭇하게 진척되는 그림의 전 과정에 함께했다."], ["Every body who saw it was pleased, but Mr. Elton was in continual raptures, and defended it through every criticism.", "그 그림을 본 사람은 누구나 흡족해했으나, 미스터 엘턴은 끊임없이 황홀경에 빠져, 어떤 비평에 맞서서도 그것을 옹호했다."]], [["“Miss Woodhouse has given her friend the only beauty she wanted,”—observed Mrs.", "\"미스 우드하우스가 친구분에게 부족했던 단 하나의 아름다움을 더해 주셨네요.\""], ["Weston to him—not in the least suspecting that she was addressing a lover.—“The expression of the eye is most correct, but Miss Smith has not those eyebrows and eyelashes.", "웨스턴 부인이 그에게 말했다—자신이 연인에게 건네는 말이라고는 추호도 의심치 못한 채.—\"눈매의 표현이 더없이 정확해요. 하지만 미스 스미스는 저런 눈썹과 속눈썹을 갖고 있지 않잖아요."], ["It is the fault of her face that she has them not.”", "그게 없는 건 그 애 얼굴의 흠이지만요.\""]], [["“Do you think so?”", "\"그렇게 보십니까?\""], ["replied he.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“I cannot agree with you.", "\"저는 동의할 수 없는걸요."], ["It appears to me a most perfect resemblance in every feature.", "제게는 어느 이목구비 하나 더없이 완벽하게 닮아 보입니다."], ["I never saw such a likeness in my life.", "평생 이렇게 닮은 그림은 본 적이 없어요."], ["We must allow for the effect of shade, you know.”", "음영의 효과도 감안해야지요, 아시잖습니까.\""]], [["“You have made her too tall, Emma,”", "\"엠마, 키를 너무 크게 그렸군.\""], ["said Mr. Knightley.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말했다."]], [["Emma knew that she had, but would not own it; and Mr. Elton warmly added,", "엠마도 그런 줄 알았으나 인정하려 들지 않았고, 미스터 엘턴이 열을 올리며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Oh no!", "\"아, 아니에요!"], ["certainly not too tall; not in the least too tall.", "결코 너무 크지 않습니다. 조금도 너무 크지 않아요."], ["Consider, she is sitting down—which naturally presents a different—which in short gives exactly the idea—and the proportions must be preserved, you know.", "생각해 보십시오, 미스 스미스는 앉아 있잖습니까—그러면 자연히 다른—요컨대 딱 그런 느낌을 주는 거지요—그리고 비례는 지켜져야 하는 법이잖습니까, 아시다시피."], ["Proportions, fore-shortening.—Oh no!", "비례 말입니다, 단축법요.—아, 아니에요!"], ["it gives one exactly the idea of such a height as Miss Smith’s.", "이건 딱 미스 스미스만 한 키의 느낌을 줍니다."], ["Exactly so indeed!”", "정말이지 바로 그래요!\""]], [["“It is very pretty,”", "\"아주 예쁘구나.\""], ["said Mr. Woodhouse.", "미스터 우드하우스가 말했다."], ["“So prettily done!", "\"참 예쁘게도 그렸어!"], ["Just as your drawings always are, my dear.", "네 그림은 늘 그렇듯이 말이다, 얘야."], ["I do not know any body who draws so well as you do.", "너만큼 그림을 잘 그리는 사람은 본 적이 없단다."], ["The only thing I do not thoroughly like is, that she seems to be sitting out of doors, with only a little shawl over her shoulders—and it makes one think she must catch cold.”", "다만 한 가지 영 마음에 안 드는 건, 어깨에 숄만 살짝 걸친 채 바깥에 앉아 있는 것처럼 보인다는 거야—그러니 자꾸 감기에 걸릴까 봐 걱정이 되는구나.\""]], [["“But, my dear papa, it is supposed to be summer; a warm day in summer.", "\"하지만 아버지, 이건 여름이라는 설정이에요. 여름의 따뜻한 날이요."], ["Look at the tree.”", "저 나무를 보세요.\""]], [["“But it is never safe to sit out of doors, my dear.”", "\"그래도 바깥에 앉아 있는 건 도통 안전하지가 않단다, 얘야.\""]], [["“You, sir, may say any thing,” cried Mr.", "\"어르신께서야 무슨 말씀이든 하셔도 좋습니다.\""], ["Elton, “but I must confess that I regard it as a most happy thought, the placing of Miss Smith out of doors; and the tree is touched with such inimitable spirit!", "미스터 엘턴이 외쳤다. \"하지만 저는 고백건대, 미스 스미스를 바깥에 둔 것이야말로 더없이 멋진 착상이라고 봅니다."], ["Any other situation would have been much less in character.", "그리고 저 나무는 누구도 흉내 못 낼 생기로 그려졌고요!"], ["The naïveté of Miss Smith’s manners—and altogether—Oh, it is most admirable!", "다른 어떤 배경이었다면 훨씬 덜 어울렸을 거예요. 미스 스미스의 천진한 태도와—그리고 전체적으로—아, 정말 감탄스럽습니다!"], ["I cannot keep my eyes from it.", "도무지 눈을 뗄 수가 없어요."], ["I never saw such a likeness.”", "평생 이렇게 닮은 그림은 본 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["The next thing wanted was to get the picture framed; and here were a few difficulties.", "다음으로 필요한 것은 그림을 액자에 넣는 일이었는데, 여기에 자잘한 어려움이 좀 있었다."], ["It must be done directly; it must be done in London; the order must go through the hands of some intelligent person whose taste could be depended on; and Isabella, the usual doer of all commissions, must not be applied to, because it was December, and Mr.", "당장 해야 하고, 런던에서 해야 하며, 그 주문은 안목을 믿을 만한 분별 있는 누군가의 손을 거쳐야 했다. 그리고 늘 온갖 심부름을 도맡던 이저벨라에게는 부탁할 수 없었으니, 때가 십이월이라 미스터 우드하우스가 딸이 십이월의 안개 속에 집 밖으로 나선다는 생각을 견디지 못했기 때문이다."], ["Woodhouse could not bear the idea of her stirring out of her house in the fogs of December. But no sooner was the distress known to Mr.", "그러나 이 곤란이 미스터 엘턴의 귀에 들어가자마자 곧 해소되었다."], ["Elton, than it was removed. His gallantry was always on the alert.", "그의 친절은 언제나 빈틈없이 대기하고 있었다."], ["“Might he be trusted with the commission, what infinite pleasure should he have in executing it!", "\"그 심부름을 제게 맡겨 주신다면, 그걸 해내는 데 얼마나 더없는 기쁨을 느끼겠습니까!"], ["he could ride to London at any time.", "저야 언제든 런던에 다녀올 수 있고요."], ["It was impossible to say how much he should be gratified by being employed on such an errand.”", "그런 심부름에 쓰임받는다면 얼마나 기쁠지 이루 말할 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“He was too good!—she could not endure the thought!—she would not give him such a troublesome office for the world,”—brought on the desired repetition of entreaties and assurances,—and a very few minutes settled the business.", "\"너무 친절하셔라!—차마 그런 생각은 할 수 없어요!—세상없어도 그런 번거로운 일을 맡길 수는 없고말고요.\"—이런 말이 바라던 대로 거듭되는 간청과 다짐을 불러왔고, 불과 몇 분 만에 일이 매듭지어졌다."]], [["Mr.", "미스터 엘턴이 그림을 런던으로 가져가 액자를 고르고 지시를 내리기로 했다."], ["Elton was to take the drawing to London, chuse the frame, and give the directions; and Emma thought she could so pack it as to ensure its safety without much incommoding him, while he seemed mostly fearful of not being incommoded enough.", "그리고 엠마는 그가 그리 불편하지 않게 안전을 보장할 수 있도록 잘 포장할 수 있겠다고 생각했으나, 정작 그는 충분히 불편을 떠맡지 못할까 봐 도리어 걱정하는 듯했다."]], [["“What a precious deposit!”", "\"이 얼마나 소중한 맡김인지요!\""], ["said he with a tender sigh, as he received it.", "그는 그것을 받아 들며 애틋한 한숨과 함께 말했다."]], [["“This man is almost too gallant to be in love,” thought Emma.", "이 사람은 사랑에 빠지기에는 너무 점잖은 게 아닐까—엠마는 생각했다—그렇게 말하고 싶지만, 사랑에 빠지는 데에도 백 가지 다른 방식이 있을 테지."], ["“I should say so, but that I suppose there may be a hundred different ways of being in love.", "그는 훌륭한 청년이고, 해리엇에게 딱 맞을 거야. 본인 말마따나 '바로 그렇습니다'가 될 테고."], ["He is an excellent young man, and will suit Harriet exactly; it will be an ‘Exactly so,’ as he says himself; but he does sigh and languish, and study for compliments rather more than I could endure as a principal.", "하지만 한숨을 쉬고 시름에 잠기고 또 그렇게 듣기 좋은 말을 짜내는 모양새는, 그게 상대가 나라면 도저히 못 견딜 정도지."], ["I come in for a pretty good share as a second.", "둘째 가는 상대로서야 어지간히 한몫 받는 셈이지만."], ["But it is his gratitude on Harriet’s account.”", "하지만 이건 다 해리엇을 위한 그의 고마움 때문이야."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter VII", "# 제1권 7장"]], [["The very day of Mr. Elton’s going to London produced a fresh occasion for Emma’s services towards her friend.", "미스터 엘턴이 런던으로 떠난 바로 그날, 엠마가 벗을 위해 솜씨를 발휘할 새로운 기회가 찾아왔다."], ["Harriet had been at Hartfield, as usual, soon after breakfast; and, after a time, had gone home to return again to dinner: she returned, and sooner than had been talked of, and with an agitated, hurried look, announcing something extraordinary to have happened which she was longing to tell.", "해리엇은 여느 때처럼 아침을 먹은 지 얼마 되지 않아 하트필드에 와 있었고, 한동안 머물다가 저녁상에 맞추어 다시 오기로 하고 집으로 돌아갔다. 그런데 이야기했던 것보다 일찍, 들떠서 황급한 얼굴로 돌아와, 무언가 예사롭지 않은 일이 일어났다며 어서 털어놓고 싶어 안달이었다."], ["Half a minute brought it all out.", "반 분이 채 못 되어 모든 사연이 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["She had heard, as soon as she got back to Mrs. Goddard’s, that Mr.", "고더드 부인 댁으로 돌아가자마자 들으니, 미스터 마틴이 한 시간 전에 다녀갔는데, 해리엇이 집에 없고 곧 돌아올 기미도 없자 누이 가운데 하나가 보낸 작은 꾸러미를 두고 갔다는 것이었다."], ["Martin had been there an hour before, and finding she was not at home, nor particularly expected, had left a little parcel for her from one of his sisters, and gone away; and on opening this parcel, she had actually found, besides the two songs which she had lent Elizabeth to copy, a letter to herself; and this letter was from him, from Mr. Martin, and contained a direct proposal of marriage.", "그런데 그 꾸러미를 풀어 보니, 엘리자베스에게 베껴 쓰라고 빌려준 노래 두 곡 말고도 자기 앞으로 온 편지가 한 통 들어 있었고, 그 편지가 바로 그에게서, 미스터 마틴에게서 온 것으로, 정식 청혼이 담겨 있었다는 것이다."], ["“Who could have thought it?", "\"누가 그런 걸 생각이나 했겠어요?"], ["She was so surprized she did not know what to do.", "어찌나 놀랐는지 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠더라고요."], ["Yes, quite a proposal of marriage; and a very good letter, at least she thought so.", "네, 정말 청혼이에요. 그리고 아주 좋은 편지였어요, 적어도 저는 그렇게 생각했어요."], ["And he wrote as if he really loved her very much—but she did not know—and so, she was come as fast as she could to ask Miss Woodhouse what she should do.—”", "정말로 저를 무척 사랑하는 것처럼 썼더라고요 — 그런데 저는 잘 모르겠어서 — 그래서 미스 우드하우스께 어떻게 해야 좋을지 여쭤보려고 한달음에 달려온 거예요.\""], ["Emma was half-ashamed of her friend for seeming so pleased and so doubtful.", "엠마는 그토록 기뻐하면서도 그토록 망설이는 벗의 모습이 절반쯤은 부끄럽게 여겨졌다."]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"어머나,\""], ["she cried, “the young man is determined not to lose any thing for want of asking.", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"이 청년, 물어보지 않아서 놓치는 일은 없게 하겠다고 단단히 작정했나 봐요."], ["He will connect himself well if he can.”", "할 수만 있다면 좋은 데로 연을 맺어 두자는 거죠.\""]], [["“Will you read the letter?”", "\"읽어 보시겠어요?\""], ["cried Harriet.", "해리엇이 외쳤다."], ["“Pray do.", "\"제발요."], ["I’d rather you would.”", "미스 우드하우스께서 읽어 주시면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["Emma was not sorry to be pressed.", "엠마는 권하는 것이 싫지 않았다."], ["She read, and was surprized.", "편지를 읽고는 놀랐다."], ["The style of the letter was much above her expectation.", "편지의 문체가 기대보다 훨씬 윗길이었다."], ["There were not merely no grammatical errors, but as a composition it would not have disgraced a gentleman; the language, though plain, was strong and unaffected, and the sentiments it conveyed very much to the credit of the writer.", "문법에 틀린 데가 없는 것은 물론이요, 글로서도 신사의 체면을 깎을 만한 데가 없었다. 표현은 꾸밈없이 평이했으나 힘 있고 가식이 없었으며, 거기 담긴 마음은 쓴 사람을 크게 돋보이게 했다."], ["It was short, but expressed good sense, warm attachment, liberality, propriety, even delicacy of feeling.", "짧았지만, 분별과 따뜻한 연정과 너그러움과 예의, 심지어 섬세한 감정까지 잘 드러나 있었다."], ["She paused over it, while Harriet stood anxiously watching for her opinion, with a “Well, well,”", "엠마가 편지를 두고 머뭇거리는 동안, 해리엇은 그 의견을 애타게 기다리며 곁에 서서 \"그래서, 그래서요\""], ["and was at last forced to add, “Is it a good letter?", "하고 보채다가, 마침내 한마디 보태지 않을 수 없었다. \"좋은 편지인가요?"], ["or is it too short?”", "아니면 너무 짧은가요?\""]], [["“Yes, indeed, a very good letter,”", "\"네, 정말이지 아주 좋은 편지예요.\""], ["replied Emma rather slowly—“so good a letter, Harriet, that every thing considered, I think one of his sisters must have helped him.", "엠마가 다소 천천히 대답했다. \"해리엇, 이렇게 좋은 편지라면, 모든 걸 미루어 볼 때 누이 가운데 하나가 거들어 준 게 분명해요."], ["I can hardly imagine the young man whom I saw talking with you the other day could express himself so well, if left quite to his own powers, and yet it is not the style of a woman; no, certainly, it is too strong and concise; not diffuse enough for a woman.", "요전 날 당신과 이야기 나누던 그 청년이, 온전히 제 힘으로 이만큼 표현할 수 있으리라고는 도무지 상상하기 어려운걸요. 그렇다고 여자의 문체도 아니에요."], ["No doubt he is a sensible man, and I suppose may have a natural talent for—thinks strongly and clearly—and when he takes a pen in hand, his thoughts naturally find proper words.", "아니, 분명 그래요. 너무 힘 있고 간결하거든요."], ["It is so with some men.", "여자가 쓰기엔 충분히 늘어지지 않았어요."], ["Yes, I understand the sort of mind.", "분명 분별 있는 사람이고, 타고난 재능이 있는 모양이에요 — 생각이 힘 있고 명료한 거죠 — 그런 사람은 펜을 들면 생각이 절로 알맞은 말을 찾아내거든요. 그런 남자들이 더러 있어요."], ["Vigorous, decided, with sentiments to a certain point, not coarse.", "네, 어떤 부류의 머리인지 알겠어요. 활기차고, 단호하고, 어느 선까지는 감정도 있되, 거칠지는 않은 사람."], ["A better written letter, Harriet (returning it,) than I had expected.”", "해리엇, 내가 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 잘 쓴 편지예요.\" 그러면서 편지를 돌려주었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래서요.\""], ["said the still waiting Harriet;—“well—and—and what shall I do?”", "여전히 기다리던 해리엇이 말했다. \"그래서 — 그럼 — 그럼 저는 어떻게 해야 하죠?\""]], [["“What shall you do!", "\"어떻게 해야 하냐니요!"], ["In what respect?", "무엇에 대해서요?"], ["Do you mean with regard to this letter?”", "이 편지에 대해서 말하는 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“But what are you in doubt of?", "\"그런데 뭘 망설이는 거예요?"], ["You must answer it of course—and speedily.”", "당연히 답장을 해야죠 — 그것도 빨리요.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["But what shall I say?", "그런데 뭐라고 써야 하죠?"], ["Dear Miss Woodhouse, do advise me.”", "미스 우드하우스, 제발 좀 일러 주세요.\""]], [["“Oh no, no!", "\"아니, 안 돼요!"], ["the letter had much better be all your own.", "편지는 온전히 당신 손으로 쓰는 게 훨씬 나아요."], ["You will express yourself very properly, I am sure.", "분명 아주 적절하게 표현할 수 있을 거예요. 당신 뜻이 안 통할 염려는 없어요."], ["There is no danger of your not being intelligible, which is the first thing.", "그게 무엇보다 중요한 거고요. 뜻이 분명해야 해요."], ["Your meaning must be unequivocal; no doubts or demurs: and such expressions of gratitude and concern for the pain you are inflicting as propriety requires, will present themselves unbidden to your mind, I am persuaded.", "흐리멍덩하거나 머뭇거리는 데가 없이요. 그리고 당신이 상대에게 안기는 아픔에 대한 감사와 염려의 표현은, 예의가 요구하는 대로, 청하지 않아도 당신 마음속에 절로 떠오를 거예요."], ["You need not be prompted to write with the appearance of sorrow for his disappointment.”", "틀림없어요. 그 사람의 실망을 안타까워하는 기색으로 쓰라고 일러 줄 필요도 없죠.\""]], [["“You think I ought to refuse him then,”", "\"그럼 제가 그분을 거절해야 한다고 보시는 거네요.\""], ["said Harriet, looking down.", "해리엇이 고개를 떨구며 말했다."]], [["“Ought to refuse him!", "\"거절해야 하느냐니요!"], ["My dear Harriet, what do you mean?", "해리엇, 그게 무슨 말이에요?"], ["Are you in any doubt as to that?", "그 점을 두고 망설이는 거예요?"], ["I thought—but I beg your pardon, perhaps I have been under a mistake.", "나는 — 아니, 미안해요. 어쩌면 내가 잘못 생각하고 있었나 봐요."], ["I certainly have been misunderstanding you, if you feel in doubt as to the purport of your answer.", "당신이 답장의 취지를 두고 망설이는 거라면, 내가 분명 당신을 오해하고 있었어요."], ["I had imagined you were consulting me only as to the wording of it.”", "나는 그저 답장을 어떤 말로 쓸지만 의논하는 줄 알았거든요.\""]], [["Harriet was silent.", "해리엇은 잠자코 있었다."], ["With a little reserve of manner, Emma continued:", "엠마는 다소 거리를 둔 태도로 말을 이었다."]], [["“You mean to return a favourable answer, I collect.”", "\"받아들이는 쪽으로 답장할 생각이군요. 내가 보기엔요.\""]], [["“No, I do not; that is, I do not mean—What shall I do?", "\"아니에요, 그렇지 않아요. 그러니까, 그럴 생각이 — 저는 어떻게 해야 하죠?"], ["What would you advise me to do?", "어떻게 하라고 일러 주시겠어요?"], ["Pray, dear Miss Woodhouse, tell me what I ought to do.”", "제발, 미스 우드하우스, 제가 어떻게 해야 하는지 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“I shall not give you any advice, Harriet.", "\"나는 아무 조언도 하지 않겠어요, 해리엇."], ["I will have nothing to do with it.", "이 일엔 관여하지 않을래요."], ["This is a point which you must settle with your feelings.”", "이건 당신이 당신 마음과 의논해서 정해야 할 문제예요.\""]], [["“I had no notion that he liked me so very much,”", "\"그분이 저를 그렇게까지 좋아하는 줄은 미처 몰랐어요.\""], ["said Harriet, contemplating the letter.", "해리엇이 편지를 들여다보며 말했다."], ["For a little while Emma persevered in her silence; but beginning to apprehend the bewitching flattery of that letter might be too powerful, she thought it best to say,", "엠마는 한동안 입을 다물고 버텼다. 그러나 그 편지의 홀리는 듯한 달콤한 말이 너무 큰 힘을 발휘할까 염려되기 시작하자, 이렇게 말하는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했다."]], [["“I lay it down as a general rule, Harriet, that if a woman doubts as to whether she should accept a man or not, she certainly ought to refuse him.", "\"해리엇, 나는 이걸 하나의 일반 원칙으로 삼아요. 여자가 어떤 남자를 받아들여야 할지 말지 망설인다면, 그건 분명 거절해야 한다는 뜻이라고요."], ["If she can hesitate as to ‘Yes,’ she ought to say ‘No’ directly.", "'네' 하기를 머뭇거릴 정도라면 곧장 '아니요'라고 해야 해요."], ["It is not a state to be safely entered into with doubtful feelings, with half a heart.", "미심쩍은 마음, 마음의 절반만 가지고 무사히 들어설 수 있는 처지가 아니거든요."], ["I thought it my duty as a friend, and older than yourself, to say thus much to you.", "당신보다 나이도 위인 벗으로서, 이만큼은 말해 주는 게 도리라고 생각했어요."], ["But do not imagine that I want to influence you.”", "하지만 내가 당신을 좌지우지하려 한다고는 생각하지 말아요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머! 아니에요, 미스 우드하우스는 너무 다정한 분이라 그러실 리가 — 하지만 제가 어떻게 하는 게 제일 좋을지 그것만 일러 주시면 — 아니, 아니에요, 그런 뜻이 아니라 — 말씀하신 대로, 마음이란 단단히 정해야 하는 거죠 — 머뭇거려선 안 되고요 — 아주 중대한 일이니까요."], ["no, I am sure you are a great deal too kind to—but if you would just advise me what I had best do—No, no, I do not mean that—As you say, one’s mind ought to be quite made up—One should not be hesitating—It is a very serious thing.—It will be safer to say ‘No,’ perhaps.—Do you think I had better say ‘No?’”", "어쩌면 '아니요'라고 하는 편이 더 안전할지도 몰라요. '아니요'라고 하는 게 낫다고 보세요?\""]], [["“Not for the world,”", "\"무슨 일이 있어도,\""], ["said Emma, smiling graciously, “would I advise you either way.", "엠마가 너그러이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"나는 이렇게도 저렇게도 권하지 않을 거예요."], ["You must be the best judge of your own happiness.", "당신 자신의 행복은 당신이 가장 잘 판단할 수 있어요."], ["If you prefer Mr. Martin to every other person; if you think him the most agreeable man you have ever been in company with, why should you hesitate?", "만약 다른 누구보다 미스터 마틴이 좋다면, 지금껏 함께해 본 사람 중 그분이 가장 마음에 드는 사람이라고 생각한다면, 망설일 까닭이 어디 있겠어요?"], ["You blush, Harriet.—Does any body else occur to you at this moment under such a definition?", "얼굴이 붉어지는군요, 해리엇. 지금 이 순간, 그런 조건에 들어맞는 다른 누군가가 떠오르는 건 아니에요?"], ["Harriet, Harriet, do not deceive yourself; do not be run away with by gratitude and compassion.", "해리엇, 해리엇, 자신을 속이지 말아요. 고마움과 동정에 휩쓸리지 말고요."], ["At this moment whom are you thinking of?”", "지금 이 순간, 누구를 생각하고 있죠?\""]], [["The symptoms were favourable.—Instead of answering, Harriet turned away confused, and stood thoughtfully by the fire; and though the letter was still in her hand, it was now mechanically twisted about without regard.", "징조가 좋았다. 해리엇은 대답 대신 당황하여 고개를 돌리고, 생각에 잠긴 채 난롯가에 서 있었다."], ["Emma waited the result with impatience, but not without strong hopes.", "편지는 아직 손에 쥐고 있었지만, 이제는 무심결에 만지작거리며 비틀고 있을 뿐이었다. 엠마는 그 결과를 안달하며 기다렸으나, 강한 희망이 없지 않았다."], ["At last, with some hesitation, Harriet said—", "마침내 해리엇이 다소 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“Miss Woodhouse, as you will not give me your opinion, I must do as well as I can by myself; and I have now quite determined, and really almost made up my mind—to refuse Mr.", "\"미스 우드하우스, 의견을 안 주시겠다니, 제 힘으로 할 수 있는 데까지 해 봐야겠지요."], ["Martin.", "그래서 이제 완전히 결심했어요."], ["Do you think I am right?”", "정말이지 거의 마음을 굳혔어요 — 미스터 마틴을 거절하기로요. 제가 옳다고 보세요?\""]], [["“Perfectly, perfectly right, my dearest Harriet; you are doing just what you ought.", "\"더없이 옳아요, 더없이 옳고말고요, 사랑하는 해리엇. 당신은 마땅히 해야 할 일을 하고 있는 거예요."], ["While you were at all in suspense I kept my feelings to myself, but now that you are so completely decided I have no hesitation in approving.", "당신이 조금이라도 갈피를 못 잡는 동안엔 내 마음을 드러내지 않았지만, 이제 이렇게 완전히 결심했으니 나도 망설임 없이 찬성해요."], ["Dear Harriet, I give myself joy of this.", "사랑하는 해리엇, 이 일이 나는 기뻐요."], ["It would have grieved me to lose your acquaintance, which must have been the consequence of your marrying Mr.", "당신과의 교제를 잃었다면 나는 슬펐을 거예요. 미스터 마틴과 결혼했다면 분명 그렇게 됐을 테니까요."], ["Martin. While you were in the smallest degree wavering, I said nothing about it, because I would not influence; but it would have been the loss of a friend to me.", "당신이 조금이라도 흔들리는 동안엔 좌지우지하고 싶지 않아서 아무 말도 안 했지만, 그건 내게 벗 하나를 잃는 일이었을 거예요."], ["I could not have visited Mrs. Robert Martin, of Abbey-Mill Farm.", "나는 애비 밀 농장의 미시즈 로버트 마틴을 찾아갈 수는 없었을 테니까요."], ["Now I am secure of you for ever.”", "이제 당신을 영원히 내 곁에 둘 수 있게 됐어요.\""]], [["Harriet had not surmised her own danger, but the idea of it struck her forcibly.", "해리엇은 자기가 처했던 위험을 짐작도 못 하고 있었으나, 그 생각이 떠오르자 큰 충격으로 다가왔다."]], [["“You could not have visited me!”", "\"저를 찾아오실 수 없었을 거라고요!\" 그녀가 질겁한 얼굴로 외쳤다."], ["she cried, looking aghast.", "\"네, 정말 그러셨겠죠."], ["“No, to be sure you could not; but I never thought of that before.", "그런데 저는 그런 건 미처 생각도 못 했어요. 그건 정말 너무 끔찍했을 거예요!"], ["That would have been too dreadful!—What an escape!—Dear Miss Woodhouse, I would not give up the pleasure and honour of being intimate with you for any thing in the world.”", "큰일 날 뻔했네요! 미스 우드하우스, 저는 세상 그 무엇과도 당신과 가깝게 지내는 기쁨과 영광을 바꾸지 않겠어요.\""]], [["“Indeed, Harriet, it would have been a severe pang to lose you; but it must have been.", "\"정말이지, 해리엇, 당신을 잃는 건 내게 모진 아픔이었을 거예요. 하지만 어쩔 수 없는 일이었겠죠."], ["You would have thrown yourself out of all good society.", "당신은 스스로를 모든 좋은 사교계 바깥으로 내던지는 셈이 됐을 테고요."], ["I must have given you up.”", "나는 당신을 포기할 수밖에 없었을 거예요.\""]], [["“Dear me!—How should I ever have borne it!", "\"어머나! 그걸 제가 어떻게 견뎠겠어요!"], ["It would have killed me never to come to Hartfield any more!”", "다시는 하트필드에 못 온다면 저는 죽고 말았을 거예요!\""]], [["“Dear affectionate creature!—You banished to Abbey-Mill Farm!—You confined to the society of the illiterate and vulgar all your life!", "\"이 다정하고 사랑스러운 사람! 당신이 애비 밀 농장으로 쫓겨 가다니!"], ["I wonder how the young man could have the assurance to ask it.", "당신이 평생토록 무식하고 천한 사람들 틈에 갇혀 지내다니! 그 청년이 어떻게 그런 걸 청할 배짱이 났는지 모르겠어요."], ["He must have a pretty good opinion of himself.”", "자기를 어지간히 대단하게 여기는 모양이에요.\""]], [["“I do not think he is conceited either, in general,”", "\"그분이 대체로 우쭐대는 사람이라곤 생각하지 않아요.\" 해리엇이 그런 비난에 양심이 걸려 말했다."], ["said Harriet, her conscience opposing such censure; “at least, he is very good natured, and I shall always feel much obliged to him, and have a great regard for—but that is quite a different thing from—and you know, though he may like me, it does not follow that I should—and certainly I must confess that since my visiting here I have seen people—and if one comes to compare them, person and manners, there is no comparison at all, one is so very handsome and agreeable.", "\"적어도 마음씨가 아주 착한 분이고, 저는 늘 그분께 무척 고마운 마음일 거예요. 또 아주 좋게 생각하고 — 하지만 그건 완전히 다른 얘기죠 — 게다가 아시다시피, 그분이 저를 좋아한다고 해서 제가 꼭 — 그리고 솔직히 고백하자면, 여기 드나들면서부터 저도 여러 사람을 보았는걸요 — 그래서 그분들을 견주어 본다면, 인물이며 몸가짐이며 도무지 비교가 안 돼요."], ["However, I do really think Mr.", "어떤 분은 정말이지 그렇게 잘생기고 유쾌한걸요."], ["Martin a very amiable young man, and have a great opinion of him; and his being so much attached to me—and his writing such a letter—but as to leaving you, it is what I would not do upon any consideration.”", "그래도 저는 정말로 미스터 마틴을 아주 호감 가는 청년이라 생각하고, 아주 좋게 보고 있어요. 그리고 저를 그토록 마음에 두고 — 그런 편지까지 써 주시고 — 하지만 당신을 떠나는 일이라면, 그건 어떤 경우에도 하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Thank you, thank you, my own sweet little friend.", "\"고마워요, 고마워요, 내 사랑스러운 작은 벗."], ["We will not be parted.", "우리는 헤어지지 않을 거예요."], ["A woman is not to marry a man merely because she is asked, or because he is attached to her, and can write a tolerable letter.”", "여자가 그저 청혼을 받았다고 해서, 또는 상대가 자기를 마음에 두고 그럭저럭 봐줄 만한 편지를 쓸 줄 안다고 해서 결혼해야 하는 건 아니에요.\""]], [["“Oh no;—and it is but a short letter too.”", "\"어머, 그럼요. 게다가 편지도 짧기만 한걸요.\""]], [["Emma felt the bad taste of her friend, but let it pass with a “very true; and it would be a small consolation to her, for the clownish manner which might be offending her every hour of the day, to know that her husband could write a good letter.”", "엠마는 벗의 형편없는 취향이 느껴졌으나, 그냥 넘기며 말했다. \"정말 그래요. 그리고 하루 종일 시도 때도 없이 거슬릴 상스러운 태도를 두고, 남편이 편지 하나는 잘 쓴다는 게 위안이 된다 한들 그게 얼마나 되겠어요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["yes, very.", "네, 정말요."], ["Nobody cares for a letter; the thing is, to be always happy with pleasant companions.", "편지 같은 건 아무도 신경 안 쓰죠. 중요한 건 마음에 드는 사람들과 늘 행복하게 지내는 거고요."], ["I am quite determined to refuse him.", "저는 그분을 거절하기로 완전히 마음먹었어요."], ["But how shall I do?", "그런데 어떻게 하죠?"], ["What shall I say?”", "뭐라고 써야 하죠?\""]], [["Emma assured her there would be no difficulty in the answer, and advised its being written directly, which was agreed to, in the hope of her assistance; and though Emma continued to protest against any assistance being wanted, it was in fact given in the formation of every sentence.", "엠마는 답장에 어려울 게 없으리라고 안심시키며 곧장 쓰라고 권했고, 그러면 도와주리라는 기대에 해리엇은 이에 따랐다. 엠마는 계속 도움 같은 건 필요 없다고 우겼지만, 실은 문장 하나하나를 짓는 데 손을 보탰다."], ["The looking over his letter again, in replying to it, had such a softening tendency, that it was particularly necessary to brace her up with a few decisive expressions; and she was so very much concerned at the idea of making him unhappy, and thought so much of what his mother and sisters would think and say, and was so anxious that they should not fancy her ungrateful, that Emma believed if the young man had come in her way at that moment, he would have been accepted after all.", "답장을 쓰느라 그의 편지를 다시 들여다보는 일이 마음을 자꾸 누그러뜨리는 통에, 단호한 표현 몇 마디로 그녀를 다잡아 주는 것이 특히 필요했다. 게다가 해리엇은 그를 불행하게 만든다는 생각에 어찌나 마음을 졸이고, 그의 어머니와 누이들이 어떻게 생각하고 무어라 말할지에 어찌나 마음을 쓰며, 그들이 자기를 배은망덕한 사람으로 여기지 않을까 어찌나 노심초사하던지, 엠마는 만약 그 청년이 그 순간 눈앞에 나타나기라도 했다면 결국 받아들여지고 말았으리라고 생각했다."]], [["This letter, however, was written, and sealed, and sent.", "그래도 이 편지는 다 쓰여 봉인되어 부쳐졌다."], ["The business was finished, and Harriet safe.", "일은 마무리되었고, 해리엇은 무사했다."], ["She was rather low all the evening, but Emma could allow for her amiable regrets, and sometimes relieved them by speaking of her own affection, sometimes by bringing forward the idea of Mr. Elton.", "그녀는 저녁 내내 다소 기운이 없었으나, 엠마는 그 갸륵한 아쉬움을 너그러이 헤아려 주었고, 때로는 자기 애정을 말하며, 때로는 미스터 엘턴의 생각을 끄집어내며 그 마음을 달래 주었다."]], [["“I shall never be invited to Abbey-Mill again,”", "\"다시는 애비 밀에 초대받지 못하겠죠.\""], ["was said in rather a sorrowful tone.", "다소 서글픈 어조로 한 말이었다."]], [["“Nor, if you were, could I ever bear to part with you, my Harriet.", "\"설사 초대받는다 해도, 나는 당신과 헤어지는 걸 도무지 견딜 수 없을 거예요, 내 해리엇."], ["You are a great deal too necessary at Hartfield to be spared to Abbey-Mill.”", "당신은 하트필드에 너무나 꼭 필요한 사람이라, 애비 밀에 내줄 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“And I am sure I should never want to go there; for I am never happy but at Hartfield.”", "\"그리고 저도 거기 가고 싶을 일은 결코 없을 거예요. 저는 하트필드에서만 행복하니까요.\""]], [["Some time afterwards it was, “I think Mrs.", "얼마쯤 지나서는 이런 말이었다."], ["Goddard would be very much surprized if she knew what had happened.", "\"고더드 부인이 이번 일을 아시면 무척 놀라실 것 같아요."], ["I am sure Miss Nash would—for Miss Nash thinks her own sister very well married, and it is only a linen-draper.”", "미스 내시도 분명 그럴 거고요 — 미스 내시는 자기 언니가 아주 잘 결혼했다고 여기는데, 그래 봐야 포목상인걸요.\""]], [["“One should be sorry to see greater pride or refinement in the teacher of a school, Harriet.", "\"학교 선생이 그보다 더한 자만이나 까다로움을 보인다면 안타까운 일이겠죠, 해리엇."], ["I dare say Miss Nash would envy you such an opportunity as this of being married.", "장담컨대 미스 내시는 당신이 이렇게 결혼할 기회를 얻은 걸 부러워할 거예요."], ["Even this conquest would appear valuable in her eyes.", "이런 정도의 정복마저 그분 눈에는 값져 보일 테니까요."], ["As to any thing superior for you, I suppose she is quite in the dark.", "당신에게 더 나은 무언가가 있다는 건, 짐작건대 그분은 전혀 모르고 있을 거고요."], ["The attentions of a certain person can hardly be among the tittle-tattle of Highbury yet.", "어떤 분의 관심이 아직 하이베리의 잡담거리에 올랐을 리는 없으니까요."], ["Hitherto I fancy you and I are the only people to whom his looks and manners have explained themselves.”", "지금까지 그분의 눈길과 몸가짐이 무얼 말하는지 알아챈 건, 당신과 나뿐일 거예요.\""]], [["Harriet blushed and smiled, and said something about wondering that people should like her so much.", "해리엇은 얼굴을 붉히며 미소 지었고, 사람들이 자기를 그토록 좋아하는 게 이상하다는 식의 말을 했다."], ["The idea of Mr.", "미스터 엘턴 생각은 분명 마음을 들뜨게 했다."], ["Elton was certainly cheering; but still, after a time, she was tender-hearted again towards the rejected Mr. Martin.", "그러나 여전히, 얼마 지나자 그녀는 거절당한 미스터 마틴 쪽으로 다시 마음이 여려졌다."]], [["“Now he has got my letter,”", "\"이제 그분이 제 편지를 받았겠죠.\""], ["said she softly.", "그녀가 나직이 말했다."], ["“I wonder what they are all doing—whether his sisters know—if he is unhappy, they will be unhappy too.", "\"다들 어떻게 하고 있을까요 — 누이들도 알게 됐을까요 — 그분이 괴로워하면 누이들도 같이 괴로워할 텐데."], ["I hope he will not mind it so very much.”", "그분이 너무 마음 아파하지 않으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Let us think of those among our absent friends who are more cheerfully employed,”", "\"멀리 있는 우리 벗들 중에 좀 더 즐겁게 지내는 사람을 생각하자고요.\""], ["cried Emma.", "엠마가 외쳤다."], ["“At this moment, perhaps, Mr. Elton is shewing your picture to his mother and sisters, telling how much more beautiful is the original, and after being asked for it five or six times, allowing them to hear your name, your own dear name.”", "\"어쩌면 지금 이 순간, 미스터 엘턴은 자기 어머니와 누이들에게 당신 그림을 보여 주면서, 실물은 이보다 얼마나 더 아름다운지 늘어놓고, 대여섯 번이나 졸린 끝에야 마침내 당신 이름을, 당신의 그 사랑스러운 이름을 들려주고 있을지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“My picture!—But he has left my picture in Bond-street.”", "\"제 그림이요! 하지만 그분은 그림을 본드 가에 두고 가셨는걸요.\""]], [["“Has he so!—Then I know nothing of Mr. Elton.", "\"그랬어요?"], ["No, my dear little modest Harriet, depend upon it the picture will not be in Bond-street till just before he mounts his horse to-morrow.", "그렇다면 내가 미스터 엘턴을 도통 잘못 본 거겠죠. 아니에요, 내 다정하고 겸손한 해리엇, 믿어도 좋아요."], ["It is his companion all this evening, his solace, his delight.", "그 그림은 내일 그분이 말에 오르기 직전까지는 본드 가에 들어가지 않을 거예요."], ["It opens his designs to his family, it introduces you among them, it diffuses through the party those pleasantest feelings of our nature, eager curiosity and warm prepossession.", "오늘 저녁 내내 그건 그분의 동무요, 위안이요, 기쁨이에요. 그게 그분의 속내를 가족에게 열어 보이고, 당신을 그들 사이에 소개하며, 그 자리에 우리 본성에서 가장 즐거운 감정들 — 간절한 호기심과 따뜻한 호의 — 을 퍼뜨리고 있는 거죠."], ["How cheerful, how animated, how suspicious, how busy their imaginations all are!”", "그들의 상상이 지금 얼마나 들뜨고, 얼마나 활기차고, 얼마나 미심쩍어하고, 얼마나 분주할까요!\""]], [["Harriet smiled again, and her smiles grew stronger.", "해리엇은 다시 미소 지었고, 그 미소는 점점 짙어졌다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter VIII", "# 제1권 8장"]], [["Harriet slept at Hartfield that night.", "해리엇은 그날 밤 하트필드에서 묵었다."], ["For some weeks past she had been spending more than half her time there, and gradually getting to have a bed-room appropriated to herself; and Emma judged it best in every respect, safest and kindest, to keep her with them as much as possible just at present.", "지난 몇 주 동안 그녀는 그곳에서 보내는 시간이 절반을 넘었고, 차츰 제 방 하나를 따로 갖다시피 하게 되었다. 엠마는 모든 면에서, 가장 안전하고 가장 다정한 길은 지금 같은 때 되도록 그녀를 곁에 두는 것이라 판단했다."], ["She was obliged to go the next morning for an hour or two to Mrs. Goddard’s, but it was then to be settled that she should return to Hartfield, to make a regular visit of some days.", "해리엇은 이튿날 아침 한두 시간 고더드 부인 댁에 다녀와야 했으나, 그러고 나서 하트필드로 돌아와 며칠간 정식으로 머물기로 정해 두었다."]], [["While she was gone, Mr. Knightley called, and sat some time with Mr. Woodhouse and Emma, till Mr. Woodhouse, who had previously made up his mind to walk out, was persuaded by his daughter not to defer it, and was induced by the entreaties of both, though against the scruples of his own civility, to leave Mr. Knightley for that purpose. Mr. Knightley, who had nothing of ceremony about him, was offering by his short, decided answers, an amusing contrast to the protracted apologies and civil hesitations of the other.", "해리엇이 자리를 비운 사이 미스터 나이틀리가 찾아와 미스터 우드하우스와 엠마와 함께 한동안 앉아 있었다. 미스터 우드하우스는 본디 산책을 나갈 작정이었던지라, 딸의 권유에 미루지 않기로 했고, 두 사람의 청에 못 이겨 — 본인의 예의범절로는 마음에 걸려 하면서도 — 그 일을 위해 미스터 나이틀리를 두고 자리를 떴다. 격식 따위는 도무지 모르는 미스터 나이틀리는 짧고 단호한 대답으로, 상대편의 장황한 사과와 정중한 망설임과 우스운 대조를 이루었다."]], [["“Well, I believe, if you will excuse me, Mr. Knightley, if you will not consider me as doing a very rude thing, I shall take Emma’s advice and go out for a quarter of an hour.", "\"그럼, 미스터 나이틀리, 양해해 주신다면, 제가 무척 무례한 짓을 한다고 여기지 않으신다면, 엠마의 권유대로 십오 분쯤 나갔다 오겠소."], ["As the sun is out, I believe I had better take my three turns while I can.", "해가 났으니 가능할 때 세 바퀴를 돌아 두는 편이 좋겠지."], ["I treat you without ceremony, Mr. Knightley.", "격식 없이 대하는 것이오, 미스터 나이틀리."], ["We invalids think we are privileged people.”", "우리 같은 병약한 사람은 특권이 있다고 여긴다오.\""]], [["“My dear sir, do not make a stranger of me.”", "\"어르신, 저를 손님 취급하지 마십시오.\""]], [["“I leave an excellent substitute in my daughter.", "\"내 딸이라는 훌륭한 대리인을 남겨 두고 가는 것이니."], ["Emma will be happy to entertain you.", "엠마가 기꺼이 상대해 드릴 게요."], ["And therefore I think I will beg your excuse and take my three turns—my winter walk.”", "그러니 부디 양해해 주시면 세 바퀴를 — 겨울 산책을 다녀오리다.\""]], [["“You cannot do better, sir.”", "\"더없이 좋은 일이지요, 어르신.\""]], [["“I would ask for the pleasure of your company, Mr.", "\"동행을 청하고 싶소만, 미스터 나이틀리, 나는 걸음이 몹시 느려서 내 보폭이 지루하실 게요."], ["Knightley, but I am a very slow walker, and my pace would be tedious to you; and, besides, you have another long walk before you, to Donwell Abbey.”", "게다가 당신은 돈웰 애비까지 또 먼 길을 걸어가야 하지 않소.\""]], [["“Thank you, sir, thank you; I am going this moment myself; and I think the sooner you go the better.", "\"고맙습니다, 어르신, 고맙습니다. 저도 지금 막 일어서려던 참입니다."], ["I will fetch your greatcoat and open the garden door for you.”", "그리고 어르신께서 어서 다녀오실수록 좋겠습니다. 외투를 가져다 드리고 정원 문을 열어 드리지요.\""]], [["Mr. Woodhouse at last was off; but Mr.", "마침내 미스터 우드하우스가 나갔다."], ["Knightley, instead of being immediately off likewise, sat down again, seemingly inclined for more chat.", "그러나 미스터 나이틀리는 곧바로 따라 일어서는 대신 다시 앉았으니, 좀 더 이야기를 나누고 싶은 눈치였다."], ["He began speaking of Harriet, and speaking of her with more voluntary praise than Emma had ever heard before.", "그는 해리엇 이야기를 꺼냈는데, 엠마가 일찍이 들어 본 적 없을 만큼 자청해서 칭찬하는 투였다."]], [["“I cannot rate her beauty as you do,”", "\"나는 자네만큼 그 아이의 미모를 높이 치지는 않네.\""], ["said he; “but she is a pretty little creature, and I am inclined to think very well of her disposition.", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 예쁘장한 아이인 건 분명하고, 성품도 아주 좋게 보아도 되겠다 싶어."], ["Her character depends upon those she is with; but in good hands she will turn out a valuable woman.”", "그 아이의 성격은 어울리는 상대에 따라 달라지겠지만, 좋은 손에 맡겨지면 쓸 만한 여인으로 자라날 거야.\""]], [["“I am glad you think so; and the good hands, I hope, may not be wanting.”", "\"그렇게 봐 주시니 기뻐요. 그리고 그 좋은 손이라는 것도, 부족하지 않기를 바라고요.\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"이런,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “you are anxious for a compliment, so I will tell you that you have improved her.", "\"칭찬을 듣고 싶어 안달이군. 그래, 말해 주지."], ["You have cured her of her school-girl’s giggle; she really does you credit.”", "자네가 그 아이를 나아지게 한 건 맞아. 여학생티 나던 키득거림을 고쳐 놓았으니, 정말 자네 덕을 톡톡히 본 셈이야.\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고마워요."], ["I should be mortified indeed if I did not believe I had been of some use; but it is not every body who will bestow praise where they may.", "제가 조금이나마 도움이 됐다고 믿지 못한다면 정말 면목이 없겠죠. 하지만 칭찬할 만한 데서 칭찬해 주는 사람이 다들 그런 건 아니에요."], ["You do not often overpower me with it.”", "미스터 나이틀리는 저한테 칭찬을 퍼부어 기를 죽이는 일이 흔치 않잖아요.\""]], [["“You are expecting her again, you say, this morning?”", "\"오늘 아침에 그 아이가 다시 온다고 했지?\""]], [["“Almost every moment.", "\"이제 곧 올 거예요."], ["She has been gone longer already than she intended.”", "본인이 작정한 시간보다 벌써 늦었는걸요.\""]], [["“Something has happened to delay her; some visitors perhaps.”", "\"무슨 일로 지체되는 모양이군. 손님이 왔는지도 모르지.\""]], [["“Highbury gossips!—Tiresome wretches!”", "\"하이베리의 수다쟁이들이겠죠! 진절머리 나는 사람들 같으니!\""]], [["“Harriet may not consider every body tiresome that you would.”", "\"해리엇은 자네라면 진절머리 친다고 할 사람들을 다 그렇게 여기지는 않을지도 몰라.\""]], [["Emma knew this was too true for contradiction, and therefore said nothing.", "엠마는 이것이 반박할 수 없을 만큼 옳은 말임을 알았기에 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["He presently added, with a smile,", "잠시 후 그가 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."]], [["“I do not pretend to fix on times or places, but I must tell you that I have good reason to believe your little friend will soon hear of something to her advantage.”", "\"시간이나 장소까지 콕 짚을 생각은 없네만, 자네의 그 어린 친구가 머지않아 자기에게 이로운 소식을 듣게 되리라고 믿을 만한 충분한 근거가 있다는 건 말해 둬야겠어.\""]], [["“Indeed!", "\"어머나!"], ["how so?", "어째서요?"], ["of what sort?”", "어떤 종류의 소식인데요?\""]], [["“A very serious sort, I assure you;”", "\"아주 진지한 종류의 일이야, 분명히.\""], ["still smiling.", "그는 여전히 미소를 띤 채 말했다."]], [["“Very serious!", "\"아주 진지한 일이라니!"], ["I can think of but one thing—Who is in love with her?", "짚이는 건 한 가지뿐인데요 — 누가 그 애를 사랑하고 있죠?"], ["Who makes you their confidant?”", "누가 미스터 나이틀리한테 속내를 털어놓던가요?\""]], [["Emma was more than half in hopes of Mr. Elton’s having dropt a hint. Mr. Knightley was a sort of general friend and adviser, and she knew Mr. Elton looked up to him.", "엠마는 미스터 엘턴이 무슨 언질이라도 흘렸기를 절반 넘게 기대했다. 미스터 나이틀리는 누구에게나 벗이자 조언자 같은 사람이었고, 미스터 엘턴이 그를 우러러본다는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다."]], [["“I have reason to think,”", "\"내가 보기엔,\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he replied, “that Harriet Smith will soon have an offer of marriage, and from a most unexceptionable quarter:—Robert Martin is the man.", "\"해리엇 스미스가 머지않아 청혼을 받게 될 거야. 그것도 나무랄 데 하나 없는 쪽에서 말이지."], ["Her visit to Abbey-Mill, this summer, seems to have done his business.", "로버트 마틴이 바로 그 사람이네. 올여름 애비 밀에 다녀온 게 일을 단단히 매듭지은 모양이야."], ["He is desperately in love and means to marry her.”", "그 친구는 사랑에 흠뻑 빠져 그 아이와 결혼하려고 마음먹었어.\""]], [["“He is very obliging,”", "\"참 고마운 분이네요.\""], ["said Emma; “but is he sure that Harriet means to marry him?”", "엠마가 말했다. \"하지만 해리엇이 그분과 결혼할 마음이 있다고 확신하시는 거예요?\""]], [["“Well, well, means to make her an offer then.", "\"아니, 아니, 그럼 청혼을 하려 든다고 해 두지."], ["Will that do?", "그러면 됐나?"], ["He came to the Abbey two evenings ago, on purpose to consult me about it.", "그 친구가 이틀 전 저녁에 일부러 그 일을 의논하려고 애비로 찾아왔어."], ["He knows I have a thorough regard for him and all his family, and, I believe, considers me as one of his best friends.", "내가 자기와 자기 식구 모두를 깊이 아낀다는 걸 알고, 나를 자기의 가장 좋은 벗 가운데 하나로 여기는 모양이야."], ["He came to ask me whether I thought it would be imprudent in him to settle so early; whether I thought her too young: in short, whether I approved his choice altogether; having some apprehension perhaps of her being considered (especially since your making so much of her) as in a line of society above him.", "이렇게 일찍 살림을 차리는 게 경솔한 일이 아닐지, 그 아이가 너무 어리다고 봐야 할지 — 한마디로 자기 선택을 내가 전적으로 찬성하는지 물으러 온 거지. 어쩌면 (특히 자네가 그 아이를 그토록 떠받든 뒤로) 그 아이가 자기보다 윗줄 사람으로 여겨지지 않을까 하는 걱정도 좀 있었던 게야."], ["I was very much pleased with all that he said.", "나는 그가 한 말이 죄다 무척 마음에 들었어."], ["I never hear better sense from any one than Robert Martin.", "로버트 마틴만큼 분별 있는 소리를 하는 사람을 나는 본 적이 없네."], ["He always speaks to the purpose; open, straightforward, and very well judging.", "그는 늘 요점을 짚어 말하지. 솔직하고, 거침없고, 판단력이 아주 뛰어나."], ["He told me every thing; his circumstances and plans, and what they all proposed doing in the event of his marriage.", "자기 사정과 계획, 결혼하게 되면 식구들이 어떻게 할 작정인지까지 전부 다 이야기해 주더군."], ["He is an excellent young man, both as son and brother.", "그는 아들로 보나 형제로 보나 훌륭한 청년이야."], ["I had no hesitation in advising him to marry.", "나는 망설임 없이 결혼하라고 일러 줬어."], ["He proved to me that he could afford it; and that being the case, I was convinced he could not do better.", "자기가 결혼을 감당할 형편이 된다는 걸 내게 입증해 보였고, 그렇다면 이보다 더 나은 선택은 없으리라고 나도 확신했으니까."], ["I praised the fair lady too, and altogether sent him away very happy.", "그 처자도 함께 칭찬해 주었고, 결국 그를 아주 흐뭇하게 돌려보냈지."], ["If he had never esteemed my opinion before, he would have thought highly of me then; and, I dare say, left the house thinking me the best friend and counsellor man ever had.", "그가 전에는 내 의견을 대수롭잖게 여겼더라도, 그때만큼은 나를 높이 보았을 거야. 그리고 분명 세상에 둘도 없는 좋은 벗이자 조언자라고 여기며 우리 집을 나섰을 게야."], ["This happened the night before last.", "이게 그저께 밤의 일이지."], ["Now, as we may fairly suppose, he would not allow much time to pass before he spoke to the lady, and as he does not appear to have spoken yesterday, it is not unlikely that he should be at Mrs.", "자, 우리가 무리 없이 짐작하건대, 그가 그 아이에게 말을 꺼내기까지 오래 시간을 끌었을 리는 없어. 그런데 어제는 말을 꺼낸 것 같지 않으니, 오늘쯤 고더드 부인 댁에 가 있을 법도 하지."], ["Goddard’s to-day; and she may be detained by a visitor, without thinking him at all a tiresome wretch.”", "그러면 그 아이는 손님 때문에 발이 묶인 거고, 그 손님을 진절머리 나는 사람이라고는 전혀 여기지 않을 거야.\""]], [["“Pray, Mr. Knightley,”", "\"그런데 미스터 나이틀리,\""], ["said Emma, who had been smiling to herself through a great part of this speech, “how do you know that Mr.", "이 이야기의 상당 부분을 혼자 미소 지으며 듣고 있던 엠마가 말했다."], ["Martin did not speak yesterday?”", "\"미스터 마틴이 어제 말을 꺼내지 않았다는 걸 어떻게 아세요?\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론,\""], ["replied he, surprized, “I do not absolutely know it; but it may be inferred.", "그가 놀라며 대답했다. \"딱 잘라 안다는 건 아니지."], ["Was not she the whole day with you?”", "하지만 미루어 짐작할 수 있잖나. 그 아이가 온종일 자네와 함께 있지 않았나?\""]], [["“Come,”", "\"이런,\""], ["said she, “I will tell you something, in return for what you have told me.", "그녀가 말했다. \"들려주신 이야기에 대한 답례로 한 가지 알려 드릴게요."], ["He did speak yesterday—that is, he wrote, and was refused.”", "그분은 어제 말을 꺼냈어요 — 그러니까, 편지를 썼고, 거절당했죠.\""]], [["This was obliged to be repeated before it could be believed; and Mr.", "이 말은 곧이들리기까지 몇 번을 되풀이해야 했다."], ["Knightley actually looked red with surprize and displeasure, as he stood up, in tall indignation, and said,", "미스터 나이틀리는 실제로 놀라움과 언짢음으로 얼굴이 붉어진 채 분연히 자리에서 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“Then she is a greater simpleton than I ever believed her.", "\"그렇다면 그 아이는 내가 여태 여긴 것보다 더한 멍청이로군."], ["What is the foolish girl about?”", "그 어리석은 처자가 대체 무슨 짓인가?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["to be sure,”", "그럼요.\""], ["cried Emma, “it is always incomprehensible to a man that a woman should ever refuse an offer of marriage.", "엠마가 외쳤다. \"여자가 청혼을 거절할 수 있다는 건 남자한테 늘 도무지 이해 못 할 일이죠."], ["A man always imagines a woman to be ready for any body who asks her.”", "남자는 여자란 자기에게 청하는 사람이라면 누구든 받아들일 준비가 돼 있다고 늘 상상하니까요.\""]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"말도 안 돼!"], ["a man does not imagine any such thing.", "남자는 그런 상상 따위 안 해."], ["But what is the meaning of this?", "한데 이게 대체 어찌 된 영문인가?"], ["Harriet Smith refuse Robert Martin?", "해리엇 스미스가 로버트 마틴을 거절했다고?"], ["madness, if it is so; but I hope you are mistaken.”", "그게 사실이라면 미친 짓이야. 하지만 자네가 잘못 안 거였으면 좋겠군.\""]], [["“I saw her answer!—nothing could be clearer.”", "\"제가 그 애의 답장을 봤어요! 그보다 더 분명할 순 없죠.\""]], [["“You saw her answer!—you wrote her answer too.", "\"자네가 그 답장을 봤다 — 자네가 그 답장을 쓰기도 했겠지."], ["Emma, this is your doing.", "엠마, 이건 자네 짓이야."], ["You persuaded her to refuse him.”", "자네가 그 아이를 부추겨 거절하게 한 거야.\""]], [["“And if I did, (which, however, I am far from allowing) I should not feel that I had done wrong. Mr.", "\"설령 그랬다 해도 (그렇다고 인정하는 건 결코 아니지만요) 잘못했다는 느낌은 안 들 거예요."], ["Martin is a very respectable young man, but I cannot admit him to be Harriet’s equal; and am rather surprized indeed that he should have ventured to address her.", "미스터 마틴은 아주 점잖은 청년이지만, 그분을 해리엇과 동등하다고는 인정할 수 없거든요. 오히려 그분이 감히 그 애한테 말을 건넨 게 좀 놀라울 정도예요."], ["By your account, he does seem to have had some scruples.", "미스터 나이틀리 말씀으로는 그분도 얼마간 망설임이 있었던 모양인데."], ["It is a pity that they were ever got over.”", "그 망설임을 끝내 떨쳐 버린 게 안타까운 일이죠.\""]], [["“Not Harriet’s equal!” exclaimed Mr.", "\"해리엇과 동등하지 않다니!\""], ["Knightley loudly and warmly; and with calmer asperity, added, a few moments afterwards, “No, he is not her equal indeed, for he is as much her superior in sense as in situation.", "미스터 나이틀리가 큰 소리로 격하게 외쳤다. 그러고는 잠시 후 한결 가라앉은, 그러나 날 선 어조로 덧붙였다."], ["Emma, your infatuation about that girl blinds you.", "\"그래, 그는 정말 그 아이와 동등하지 않아."], ["What are Harriet Smith’s claims, either of birth, nature or education, to any connexion higher than Robert Martin?", "처지로 보나 분별로 보나 그가 그 아이보다 훨씬 윗길이니까. 엠마, 그 처자에게 홀딱 빠져서 자네 눈이 멀었어."], ["She is the natural daughter of nobody knows whom, with probably no settled provision at all, and certainly no respectable relations.", "해리엇 스미스가 출생으로 보나, 천성으로 보나, 교육으로 보나, 로버트 마틴보다 윗줄과 인연을 맺을 무슨 자격이 있나? 누구의 자식인지도 모르는 사생아에, 정해진 재산이라곤 십중팔구 한 푼도 없고, 변변한 친척이라곤 분명히 하나도 없어."], ["She is known only as parlour-boarder at a common school.", "그저 평범한 학교의 기숙 하숙생으로나 알려졌을 뿐이지."], ["She is not a sensible girl, nor a girl of any information.", "분별 있는 처자도 아니고, 무얼 아는 처자도 아니야."], ["She has been taught nothing useful, and is too young and too simple to have acquired any thing herself.", "쓸모 있는 건 하나도 배운 게 없고, 스스로 익히기엔 너무 어리고 너무 단순하지."], ["At her age she can have no experience, and with her little wit, is not very likely ever to have any that can avail her.", "그 나이에 무슨 경험이 있을 리 없고, 그 변변찮은 머리로는 앞으로도 쓸 만한 경험을 얻을 가망이 별로 없어."], ["She is pretty, and she is good tempered, and that is all.", "예쁘고 성격이 좋다 — 그게 다야."], ["My only scruple in advising the match was on his account, as being beneath his deserts, and a bad connexion for him.", "내가 그 혼사를 일러 주면서 유일하게 마음에 걸렸던 건 그 친구 쪽이었어. 그 아이가 그의 자격에는 못 미치고, 그에게는 안 좋은 인연이라는 점에서 말이지."], ["I felt that, as to fortune, in all probability he might do much better; and that as to a rational companion or useful helpmate, he could not do worse.", "재산으로 따지면 그가 십중팔구 훨씬 나은 짝을 만날 수 있을 거고, 이치를 아는 반려나 쓸모 있는 조력자로 따지면 이보다 못한 선택은 없으리라 여겼으니까."], ["But I could not reason so to a man in love, and was willing to trust to there being no harm in her, to her having that sort of disposition, which, in good hands, like his, might be easily led aright and turn out very well.", "하지만 사랑에 빠진 남자에게 그렇게 따져 말할 수는 없었고, 그 아이가 나쁜 데 없는 아이라는 것, 그의 손 같은 좋은 손에 맡겨지면 쉽사리 바로 이끌려 아주 잘될 그런 성품을 지녔다는 것을 믿어 보기로 한 거야."], ["The advantage of the match I felt to be all on her side; and had not the smallest doubt (nor have I now) that there would be a general cry-out upon her extreme good luck.", "이 혼사의 이득은 죄다 그 아이 쪽에 있다고 느꼈고, 그 아이의 더없는 행운을 두고 다들 한목소리로 떠들어 댈 거라는 데 추호의 의심도 없었어 (지금도 없고)."], ["Even your satisfaction I made sure of.", "자네의 만족까지도 나는 확신했지."], ["It crossed my mind immediately that you would not regret your friend’s leaving Highbury, for the sake of her being settled so well.", "자네 친구가 그토록 잘 자리 잡는 것이니, 그 아이가 하이베리를 떠나는 걸 자네가 아쉬워하지 않으리라는 생각이 곧장 들었거든."], ["I remember saying to myself, ‘Even Emma, with all her partiality for Harriet, will think this a good match.’”", "'아무리 해리엇을 편애하는 엠마라도 이건 좋은 혼사라고 여길 거야.' 이렇게 혼잣말한 게 기억나는군.\""]], [["“I cannot help wondering at your knowing so little of Emma as to say any such thing.", "\"그런 말씀을 하실 만큼 엠마를 그토록 모르신다는 데 놀라지 않을 수가 없네요."], ["What!", "뭐라고요!"], ["think a farmer, (and with all his sense and all his merit Mr. Martin is nothing more,) a good match for my intimate friend!", "농장주를 (그리고 미스터 마틴은 아무리 분별 있고 미덕이 넘쳐도 그 이상은 아니죠) 제 친한 친구의 좋은 혼처라 여긴다고요!"], ["Not regret her leaving Highbury for the sake of marrying a man whom I could never admit as an acquaintance of my own!", "제가 결코 제 지인으로 인정할 수 없는 남자와 결혼하려고 하이베리를 떠나는데 아쉬워하지 않는다고요! 제게 그런 마음이 있을 수 있다고 여기시다니 의아하네요."], ["I wonder you should think it possible for me to have such feelings.", "분명히 말씀드리지만, 제 마음은 전혀 달라요. 미스터 나이틀리의 말씀은 결코 공정하지 못하다고 봐요."], ["I assure you mine are very different.", "해리엇의 자격에 대해 정당하지 않으세요."], ["I must think your statement by no means fair.", "그건 저뿐 아니라 다른 사람들도 아주 다르게 헤아릴 거예요."], ["You are not just to Harriet’s claims. They would be estimated very differently by others as well as myself; Mr.", "미스터 마틴이 둘 중 더 부유할지는 몰라도, 사교계의 지위로는 의심할 바 없이 그 애보다 아래예요."], ["Martin may be the richest of the two, but he is undoubtedly her inferior as to rank in society.—The sphere in which she moves is much above his.—It would be a degradation.”", "그 애가 움직이는 영역은 그분보다 훨씬 위고요. 그건 격을 떨어뜨리는 일이에요.\""]], [["“A degradation to illegitimacy and ignorance, to be married to a respectable, intelligent gentleman-farmer!”", "\"점잖고 지각 있는 신사 농장주와 결혼하는 게, 사생아 신분에 무지한 처자에게 격을 떨어뜨리는 일이라니!\""]], [["“As to the circumstances of her birth, though in a legal sense she may be called Nobody, it will not hold in common sense. She is not to pay for the offence of others, by being held below the level of those with whom she is brought up.—There can scarcely be a doubt that her father is a gentleman—and a gentleman of fortune.—Her allowance is very liberal; nothing has ever been grudged for her improvement or comfort.—That she is a gentleman’s daughter, is indubitable to me; that she associates with gentlemen’s daughters, no one, I apprehend, will deny.—She is superior to Mr. Robert Martin.”", "\"출생의 사정으로 말하자면, 법적인 뜻으로는 그 애를 '아무것도 아닌 사람'이라 부를 수 있을지 몰라도, 상식으로는 통하지 않아요. 함께 자란 사람들보다 아래로 내몰려 남의 잘못 값을 그 애가 치를 까닭은 없죠. 그 애 아버지가 신사라는 건 — 그것도 재산 있는 신사라는 건 거의 의심할 여지가 없어요. 그 애한테 주는 돈도 아주 넉넉하고요. 그 애의 교양이나 안락을 위해 인색하게 군 적이 한 번도 없어요. 그 애가 신사의 딸이라는 건 제겐 명백한 사실이에요. 그 애가 신사의 딸들과 어울린다는 것도, 제 생각엔 아무도 부정 못 할 거고요. 그 애는 미스터 로버트 마틴보다 윗길이에요.\""]], [["“Whoever might be her parents,” said Mr.", "\"그 아이의 부모가 누구든,\""], ["Knightley, “whoever may have had the charge of her, it does not appear to have been any part of their plan to introduce her into what you would call good society.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"그 아이를 맡아 키운 이가 누구든, 자네가 좋은 사교계라 부를 곳에 그 아이를 들여보내는 건 애당초 그들 계획에 들어 있지 않았던 모양이야."], ["After receiving a very indifferent education she is left in Mrs.", "변변찮은 교육을 받은 뒤 그 아이는 고더드 부인 손에 맡겨져 제 알아서 살아가게 되었지."], ["Goddard’s hands to shift as she can;—to move, in short, in Mrs. Goddard’s line, to have Mrs.", "한마디로 고더드 부인의 부류 안에서 움직이고, 고더드 부인의 지인들과 어울리도록 말이야."], ["Goddard’s acquaintance. Her friends evidently thought this good enough for her; and it was good enough.", "그 아이의 후견인들은 분명 그 정도면 그 아이에게 충분하다고 여겼고, 실제로도 충분했어."], ["She desired nothing better herself.", "그 아이 자신도 그 이상을 바라지 않았고."], ["Till you chose to turn her into a friend, her mind had no distaste for her own set, nor any ambition beyond it.", "자네가 그 아이를 친구로 삼기로 마음먹기 전까지, 그 아이는 제 부류를 싫어하는 마음도 없었고 그 너머를 넘보는 야심도 없었어."], ["She was as happy as possible with the Martins in the summer.", "여름 내내 마틴네와 더없이 행복하게 지냈잖나."], ["She had no sense of superiority then.", "그때는 우월감 따위 없었어."], ["If she has it now, you have given it.", "지금 그게 있다면, 자네가 심어 준 거지."], ["You have been no friend to Harriet Smith, Emma.", "엠마, 자네는 해리엇 스미스에게 좋은 친구가 못 됐어."], ["Robert Martin would never have proceeded so far, if he had not felt persuaded of her not being disinclined to him.", "로버트 마틴은 그 아이가 자기를 싫어하지 않는다는 확신이 없었다면 이렇게까지 나아가지 않았을 거야."], ["I know him well.", "나는 그를 잘 알아."], ["He has too much real feeling to address any woman on the haphazard of selfish passion.", "그는 진정한 감정이 너무 깊은 사람이라, 제멋대로의 욕심에 기대 되는대로 여자에게 말을 건넬 사람이 아니야."], ["And as to conceit, he is the farthest from it of any man I know.", "그리고 자만으로 말하자면, 내가 아는 누구보다도 그것과 거리가 먼 사람이지."], ["Depend upon it he had encouragement.”", "분명히 말해 두는데, 그는 격려를 받았던 거야.\""]], [["It was most convenient to Emma not to make a direct reply to this assertion; she chose rather to take up her own line of the subject again.", "이 단정에 곧장 대꾸하지 않는 편이 엠마에게는 가장 편했다. 그녀는 차라리 제가 펴던 논점으로 다시 돌아가기를 택했다."]], [["“You are a very warm friend to Mr.", "\"미스터 마틴한테는 아주 열렬한 벗이시군요."], ["Martin; but, as I said before, are unjust to Harriet.", "하지만 앞서 말씀드렸듯, 해리엇한테는 부당하세요."], ["Harriet’s claims to marry well are not so contemptible as you represent them.", "해리엇이 좋은 혼처를 만날 자격은 말씀하시는 것만큼 하찮지 않아요. 영리한 처자는 아니지만, 미스터 나이틀리가 아시는 것보다 분별이 있고, 그 지각을 그렇게 깔보듯 말씀하실 만큼은 아니에요."], ["She is not a clever girl, but she has better sense than you are aware of, and does not deserve to have her understanding spoken of so slightingly.", "하지만 그 점은 접어 두고, 미스터 나이틀리 말씀대로 그저 예쁘고 성격 좋은 게 다라고 쳐도, 한 가지만 말씀드릴게요. 그 애가 그것들을 지닌 정도로 보면, 세상 사람들 일반에게는 결코 사소한 장점이 아니에요."], ["Waiving that point, however, and supposing her to be, as you describe her, only pretty and good-natured, let me tell you, that in the degree she possesses them, they are not trivial recommendations to the world in general, for she is, in fact, a beautiful girl, and must be thought so by ninety-nine people out of an hundred; and till it appears that men are much more philosophic on the subject of beauty than they are generally supposed; till they do fall in love with well-informed minds instead of handsome faces, a girl, with such loveliness as Harriet, has a certainty of being admired and sought after, of having the power of chusing from among many, consequently a claim to be nice.", "사실 그 애는 아름다운 처자고, 백 사람 중 아흔아홉은 그렇게 여길 게 틀림없으니까요. 그리고 남자들이 미모라는 문제에서 흔히 여겨지는 것보다 훨씬 철학적인 존재임이 드러나기 전까지는 — 그러니까 잘생긴 얼굴 대신 박식한 정신과 사랑에 빠지기 전까지는 — 해리엇만 한 사랑스러움을 지닌 처자는 흠모받고 구애받을 게 확실하고, 여럿 가운데서 고를 힘이 있으며, 따라서 까다롭게 굴 자격이 있어요."], ["Her good-nature, too, is not so very slight a claim, comprehending, as it does, real, thorough sweetness of temper and manner, a very humble opinion of herself, and a great readiness to be pleased with other people.", "그 애의 좋은 성품 또한 그리 가벼운 자격이 아니에요. 거기엔 더없이 진실하고 온전한 성격과 태도의 다정함, 자기 자신에 대한 아주 겸손한 평가, 남을 기꺼이 좋아하는 한결같은 마음이 다 들어 있으니까요."], ["I am very much mistaken if your sex in general would not think such beauty, and such temper, the highest claims a woman could possess.”", "그런 미모와 그런 성품이야말로 여자가 지닐 수 있는 최고의 자격이라고 남자들이 대체로 여기지 않는다면, 제가 크게 잘못 안 거겠죠.\""]], [["“Upon my word, Emma, to hear you abusing the reason you have, is almost enough to make me think so too.", "\"맹세코, 엠마, 자네가 그 멀쩡한 분별을 그렇게 함부로 써 대는 걸 듣고 있자니, 나마저 거의 그 말이 맞다고 여길 지경이군."], ["Better be without sense, than misapply it as you do.”", "그렇게 잘못 쓸 바엔 차라리 분별이 없는 편이 낫겠어.\""]], [["“To be sure!”", "\"그럼요!\""], ["cried she playfully.", "그녀가 장난스럽게 외쳤다."], ["“I know that is the feeling of you all.", "\"그게 남자분들 모두의 속마음인 줄 다 알아요."], ["I know that such a girl as Harriet is exactly what every man delights in—what at once bewitches his senses and satisfies his judgment. Oh!", "해리엇 같은 처자야말로 모든 남자가 좋아하는 바로 그런 여자라는 걸요 — 단번에 감각을 홀리는 동시에 분별까지 만족시키는 여자죠."], ["Harriet may pick and chuse.", "어머! 해리엇은 골라잡으면 돼요."], ["Were you, yourself, ever to marry, she is the very woman for you.", "미스터 나이틀리, 당신이 만약 결혼이라도 하신다면, 그 애야말로 당신께 꼭 맞는 여자예요."], ["And is she, at seventeen, just entering into life, just beginning to be known, to be wondered at because she does not accept the first offer she receives?", "그런데 그 애가 이제 열일곱에, 막 세상에 발을 들이고, 이제 막 사람들 입에 오르내리기 시작한 마당에, 첫 청혼을 받아들이지 않았다고 의아해할 일인가요?"], ["No—pray let her have time to look about her.”", "아니죠 — 부디 그 애한테 둘러볼 시간을 좀 주세요.\""]], [["“I have always thought it a very foolish intimacy,” said Mr.", "\"나는 늘 그게 아주 어리석은 사귐이라 여겨 왔어.\""], ["Knightley presently, “though I have kept my thoughts to myself; but I now perceive that it will be a very unfortunate one for Harriet.", "미스터 나이틀리가 잠시 후 말했다. \"생각을 내색하지 않았을 뿐이지."], ["You will puff her up with such ideas of her own beauty, and of what she has a claim to, that, in a little while, nobody within her reach will be good enough for her.", "하지만 이제 보니 해리엇한테는 아주 불행한 사귐이 되겠군. 자네는 그 아이한테 제 미모며 자기가 누릴 자격이 있다는 것에 관해 그런 생각을 잔뜩 불어넣어, 머지않아 그 아이 닿는 범위 안엔 그 아이에게 어울릴 만한 사람이 하나도 없게 될 거야."], ["Vanity working on a weak head, produces every sort of mischief.", "약한 머리에 작용하는 허영은 온갖 화근을 빚어내지."], ["Nothing so easy as for a young lady to raise her expectations too high.", "젊은 아가씨가 제 기대를 지나치게 높이 끌어올리기만큼 쉬운 일도 없어."], ["Miss Harriet Smith may not find offers of marriage flow in so fast, though she is a very pretty girl.", "미스 해리엇 스미스가 아무리 예쁜 처자라 해도, 청혼이 그렇게 줄줄이 밀려들지는 않을걸."], ["Men of sense, whatever you may chuse to say, do not want silly wives.", "자네가 무어라 말하든, 분별 있는 남자들은 어리석은 아내를 원하지 않아."], ["Men of family would not be very fond of connecting themselves with a girl of such obscurity—and most prudent men would be afraid of the inconvenience and disgrace they might be involved in, when the mystery of her parentage came to be revealed.", "좋은 집안 남자들은 그런 출신 불분명한 처자와 인연 맺기를 그리 좋아하지 않을 거고, 가장 신중한 남자들은 그 아이 부모의 비밀이 드러날 때 휘말릴 불편과 망신을 두려워할 거야. 그 아이를 로버트 마틴과 결혼시켜."], ["Let her marry Robert Martin, and she is safe, respectable, and happy for ever; but if you encourage her to expect to marry greatly, and teach her to be satisfied with nothing less than a man of consequence and large fortune, she may be a parlour-boarder at Mrs.", "그러면 그 아이는 평생 안전하고 점잖고 행복할 거야."], ["Goddard’s all the rest of her life—or, at least, (for Harriet Smith is a girl who will marry somebody or other,) till she grow desperate, and is glad to catch at the old writing-master’s son.”", "하지만 자네가 그 아이더러 대단한 혼처를 바라도록 부추기고, 지체 높고 재산 많은 남자가 아니면 안 된다고 여기도록 가르친다면, 그 아이는 평생 고더드 부인 댁 기숙 하숙생으로 남거나 — 아니면 적어도 (해리엇 스미스는 어떻게든 누군가와 결혼할 처자니까) 자포자기할 만큼 절박해져서야 그 늙은 글쓰기 선생 아들이라도 덥석 붙잡고 기뻐하게 될 거야.\""]], [["“We think so very differently on this point, Mr. Knightley, that there can be no use in canvassing it.", "\"이 점에 관해선 우리 생각이 너무 달라서, 미스터 나이틀리, 따져 봐야 아무 소용 없겠어요."], ["We shall only be making each other more angry.", "서로 더 화만 돋울 뿐이죠."], ["But as to my letting her marry Robert Martin, it is impossible; she has refused him, and so decidedly, I think, as must prevent any second application.", "하지만 제가 그 애를 로버트 마틴과 결혼하게 내버려 둔다는 건 불가능해요. 그 애는 그분을 거절했고, 제가 보기엔 다시 청할 엄두를 내지 못할 만큼 단호하게 거절했어요."], ["She must abide by the evil of having refused him, whatever it may be; and as to the refusal itself, I will not pretend to say that I might not influence her a little; but I assure you there was very little for me or for any body to do.", "그분을 거절한 데서 비롯될 손해가 무엇이든 그 애는 감수해야겠죠. 거절 자체로 말하자면, 제가 그 애에게 조금 영향을 미쳤을 수도 있다는 건 부정하지 않겠어요."], ["His appearance is so much against him, and his manner so bad, that if she ever were disposed to favour him, she is not now.", "하지만 분명히 말씀드리는데, 저나 누구나 할 일이 별로 없었어요. 그분 외모는 그 애 마음에 영 거슬리고 태도도 너무 나빠서, 설령 한때 그 애가 그분에게 호의를 품을 마음이 있었다 해도, 지금은 아니에요."], ["I can imagine, that before she had seen any body superior, she might tolerate him.", "더 나은 사람을 본 적 없을 때라면 그 애가 그분을 견뎌 냈을 수도 있겠다 싶긴 해요."], ["He was the brother of her friends, and he took pains to please her; and altogether, having seen nobody better (that must have been his great assistant) she might not, while she was at Abbey-Mill, find him disagreeable.", "그분은 그 애 친구들의 오라비였고, 그 애 마음에 들려고 애를 썼으니까요. 그리고 무엇보다 더 나은 사람을 본 적이 없었으니 (그게 그분한테 큰 도움이 됐을 거예요) 애비 밀에 있는 동안엔 그 애도 그분을 그리 싫어하지 않았을지 몰라요."], ["But the case is altered now.", "하지만 이제는 사정이 달라졌어요."], ["She knows now what gentlemen are; and nothing but a gentleman in education and manner has any chance with Harriet.”", "그 애는 이제 신사가 어떤 사람인지 알거든요. 교육과 태도가 신사다운 사람이 아니고서는 해리엇한테 가망이 없어요.\""]], [["“Nonsense, errant nonsense, as ever was talked!” cried Mr.", "\"허튼소리, 들어 본 중에 가장 새빨간 허튼소리야!\""], ["Knightley.—“Robert Martin’s manners have sense, sincerity, and good-humour to recommend them; and his mind has more true gentility than Harriet Smith could understand.”", "미스터 나이틀리가 외쳤다. \"로버트 마틴의 태도엔 분별과 진실함과 쾌활함이 받쳐 주고 있고, 그의 마음엔 해리엇 스미스가 이해할 수 있는 것보다 더 참된 기품이 깃들어 있어.\""]], [["Emma made no answer, and tried to look cheerfully unconcerned, but was really feeling uncomfortable and wanting him very much to be gone.", "엠마는 아무 대꾸도 않고 짐짓 명랑하게 무심한 척하려 했으나, 실은 마음이 불편했고 그가 어서 가 주기를 몹시 바랐다. 그녀는 제가 한 일을 뉘우치지는 않았다."], ["She did not repent what she had done; she still thought herself a better judge of such a point of female right and refinement than he could be; but yet she had a sort of habitual respect for his judgment in general, which made her dislike having it so loudly against her; and to have him sitting just opposite to her in angry state, was very disagreeable.", "여자의 권리와 품위에 관한 그런 문제라면 그보다 자기가 더 나은 판단자라고 여전히 여겼다. 그러면서도 그의 판단 일반에 대해서는 몸에 밴 일종의 존중이 있었던지라, 그것이 그토록 큰 소리로 자기를 거스르는 것이 싫었다."], ["Some minutes passed in this unpleasant silence, with only one attempt on Emma’s side to talk of the weather, but he made no answer.", "게다가 그가 화난 채로 바로 맞은편에 앉아 있는 것이 무척 거북했다. 이 불편한 침묵 속에 몇 분이 흘렀고, 엠마 쪽에서 날씨 이야기로 한 번 운을 떼 보았으나 그는 대꾸하지 않았다."], ["He was thinking.", "그는 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["The result of his thoughts appeared at last in these words.", "그 생각의 결과가 마침내 이런 말로 나타났다."]], [["“Robert Martin has no great loss—if he can but think so; and I hope it will not be long before he does. Your views for Harriet are best known to yourself; but as you make no secret of your love of match-making, it is fair to suppose that views, and plans, and projects you have;—and as a friend I shall just hint to you that if Elton is the man, I think it will be all labour in vain.”", "\"로버트 마틴이 잃은 건 별로 크지 않아 — 그렇게 여길 수만 있다면 말이지. 그가 오래지 않아 그렇게 여기게 되기를 바라네. 자네가 해리엇을 두고 무슨 속셈인지는 자네만 알겠지. 하지만 중매하기를 좋아하는 걸 자네가 숨기지 않으니, 무슨 속셈이며 계획이며 꿍꿍이가 있으리라 보는 게 마땅하겠지. 벗으로서 한마디만 귀띔하겠는데, 만약 엘턴이 그 상대라면, 그건 죄다 헛수고가 될 거라고 보네.\""]], [["Emma laughed and disclaimed.", "엠마는 웃으며 아니라고 발뺌했다."], ["He continued,", "그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Depend upon it, Elton will not do.", "\"분명히 말하지만, 엘턴은 안 돼."], ["Elton is a very good sort of man, and a very respectable vicar of Highbury, but not at all likely to make an imprudent match.", "엘턴은 아주 괜찮은 사람이고, 하이베리의 아주 점잖은 부목사야. 하지만 경솔한 혼사를 맺을 사람은 결코 아니지."], ["He knows the value of a good income as well as any body.", "그는 누구 못지않게 좋은 수입의 가치를 잘 알아."], ["Elton may talk sentimentally, but he will act rationally.", "엘턴이 감상적으로 말할지는 몰라도, 행동은 분별 있게 할 거야."], ["He is as well acquainted with his own claims, as you can be with Harriet’s.", "자네가 해리엇의 자격을 잘 아는 만큼이나 그는 제 자격을 잘 알고 있어."], ["He knows that he is a very handsome young man, and a great favourite wherever he goes; and from his general way of talking in unreserved moments, when there are only men present, I am convinced that he does not mean to throw himself away.", "자기가 아주 잘생긴 청년이고 어딜 가나 큰 인기를 누린다는 걸 알지. 그리고 남자들만 있는 자리에서 거리낌 없이 말할 때 그가 늘 하는 이야기로 미루어 보면, 나는 그가 자기 자신을 함부로 내던질 생각이 없다고 확신하네."], ["I have heard him speak with great animation of a large family of young ladies that his sisters are intimate with, who have all twenty thousand pounds apiece.”", "자기 누이들과 친한 어느 처녀들 일가 이야기를 그가 아주 신나게 하는 걸 들은 적이 있는데, 그 처녀들은 하나같이 이만 파운드씩 가졌다더군.\""]], [["“I am very much obliged to you,” said Emma, laughing again.", "\"정말 고마운 말씀이네요.\""], ["“If I had set my heart on Mr.", "엠마가 다시 웃으며 말했다."], ["Elton’s marrying Harriet, it would have been very kind to open my eyes; but at present I only want to keep Harriet to myself.", "\"제가 만약 미스터 엘턴이 해리엇과 결혼하도록 마음먹고 있었다면, 제 눈을 뜨게 해 주신 게 참 친절한 일이었겠죠. 하지만 지금 저는 그저 해리엇을 제 곁에 두고 싶을 뿐이에요."], ["I have done with match-making indeed.", "중매는 정말이지 그만뒀어요."], ["I could never hope to equal my own doings at Randalls.", "랜덜스에서 제가 해낸 일에 견줄 만한 건 다시는 바랄 수 없을 테니까요."], ["I shall leave off while I am well.”", "한창 잘나갈 때 손을 떼려고요.\""]], [["“Good morning to you,”—said he, rising and walking off abruptly.", "\"그럼 안녕히.\" 그가 일어서며 불쑥 걸어 나가며 말했다."], ["He was very much vexed.", "그는 몹시 언짢았다."], ["He felt the disappointment of the young man, and was mortified to have been the means of promoting it, by the sanction he had given; and the part which he was persuaded Emma had taken in the affair, was provoking him exceedingly.", "그 청년의 실망이 느껴졌고, 자기가 찬성해 준 탓에 그 일을 부추긴 빌미가 되었다는 게 분했다. 게다가 이 일에 엠마가 손을 댔다고 확신하는 바, 그것이 그의 부아를 한껏 돋우고 있었다."]], [["Emma remained in a state of vexation too; but there was more indistinctness in the causes of her’s, than in his.", "엠마 역시 언짢은 상태로 남았다. 그러나 그녀가 언짢은 까닭은 그의 경우보다 한층 흐릿했다."], ["She did not always feel so absolutely satisfied with herself, so entirely convinced that her opinions were right and her adversary’s wrong, as Mr. Knightley.", "그녀는 미스터 나이틀리만큼 늘 자기 자신에 그토록 완전히 만족하거나, 제 의견은 옳고 상대의 의견은 그르다고 그토록 온전히 확신하지는 못했다."], ["He walked off in more complete self-approbation than he left for her.", "그는 그녀가 남긴 것보다 훨씬 더 흡족한 자기만족 속에 걸어 나갔다."], ["She was not so materially cast down, however, but that a little time and the return of Harriet were very adequate restoratives.", "그렇다고 그녀가 그리 심하게 풀이 죽은 것은 아니었으니, 약간의 시간과 해리엇의 귀가만으로도 충분히 회복될 만했다."], ["Harriet’s staying away so long was beginning to make her uneasy.", "해리엇이 그토록 오래 안 돌아오는 것이 슬슬 불안해지던 참이었다."], ["The possibility of the young man’s coming to Mrs. Goddard’s that morning, and meeting with Harriet and pleading his own cause, gave alarming ideas.", "그 청년이 그날 아침 고더드 부인 댁에 찾아와 해리엇을 만나 제 사정을 호소할지도 모른다는 가능성이 불안한 상상을 불러일으켰다."], ["The dread of such a failure after all became the prominent uneasiness; and when Harriet appeared, and in very good spirits, and without having any such reason to give for her long absence, she felt a satisfaction which settled her with her own mind, and convinced her, that let Mr.", "결국 그런 낭패에 대한 두려움이 가장 큰 불안으로 떠올랐다. 그런데 해리엇이 나타났고, 그것도 아주 기분이 좋은 채로, 오래 자리를 비운 데 그런 까닭이라곤 댈 것 하나 없이 돌아오자, 엠마는 마음이 가라앉는 만족을 느꼈다."], ["Knightley think or say what he would, she had done nothing which woman’s friendship and woman’s feelings would not justify.", "그리고 미스터 나이틀리가 무어라 생각하든 말하든, 자신은 여자의 우정과 여자의 마음이 정당하다 인정할 만한 일밖에 하지 않았다고 확신하게 되었다."]], [["He had frightened her a little about Mr.", "그는 미스터 엘턴에 관해 그녀를 조금 겁먹게 했었다."], ["Elton; but when she considered that Mr. Knightley could not have observed him as she had done, neither with the interest, nor (she must be allowed to tell herself, in spite of Mr.", "그러나 미스터 나이틀리가 자기만큼 그를 눈여겨봤을 리 없다는 것 — 그런 관심을 가지고 본 것도 아니거니와, (미스터 나이틀리가 어떻게 자처하든, 이 정도는 스스로 말해도 되겠지만) 이런 문제에서 자기 같은 관찰자만 한 안목으로 본 것도 아니라는 것, 게다가 그가 그 말을 성급하게, 화난 김에 내뱉었다는 것을 헤아리고 나니, 그녀는 그가 무얼 알고 한 말이라기보다 분에 못 이겨 그렇게 되기를 바라며 한 말이라고 믿을 수 있었다."], ["Knightley’s pretensions) with the skill of such an observer on such a question as herself, that he had spoken it hastily and in anger, she was able to believe, that he had rather said what he wished resentfully to be true, than what he knew any thing about. He certainly might have heard Mr.", "그가 자기보다 미스터 엘턴이 거리낌 없이 말하는 것을 더 많이 들었을 수는 있고, 미스터 엘턴이 돈 문제에 관해 경솔하거나 무분별한 성품이 아닐 수도 있다."], ["Elton speak with more unreserve than she had ever done, and Mr.", "오히려 그런 일에 자연스레 신경을 좀 쓰는 편일 수도 있겠지."], ["Elton might not be of an imprudent, inconsiderate disposition as to money matters; he might naturally be rather attentive than otherwise to them; but then, Mr.", "하지만 미스터 나이틀리는 온갖 타산적 동기와 맞서 싸우는 강렬한 연정의 힘을 충분히 헤아리지 못한 것이다. 미스터 나이틀리는 그런 연정을 보지 못했으니, 당연히 그 위력 따위 안중에도 없었다."], ["Knightley did not make due allowance for the influence of a strong passion at war with all interested motives. Mr.", "그러나 그녀는 그것을 너무 많이 보아 온 터라, 그 연정이 본디 합당한 신중함이 일으킬 법한 어떤 망설임도 다 이겨 내리라는 데 한 점 의심이 없었다."], ["Knightley saw no such passion, and of course thought nothing of its effects; but she saw too much of it to feel a doubt of its overcoming any hesitations that a reasonable prudence might originally suggest; and more than a reasonable, becoming degree of prudence, she was very sure did not belong to Mr. Elton.", "그리고 미스터 엘턴에게는 합당하고 적절한 정도를 넘는 신중함 따위는 없다고 그녀는 아주 확신했다."]], [["Harriet’s cheerful look and manner established hers: she came back, not to think of Mr.", "해리엇의 명랑한 낯빛과 태도가 엠마의 마음을 안정시켰다."], ["Martin, but to talk of Mr. Elton.", "그녀는 미스터 마틴을 떠올리려고가 아니라 미스터 엘턴 이야기를 하려고 돌아온 것이었다."], ["Miss Nash had been telling her something, which she repeated immediately with great delight. Mr.", "미스 내시가 그녀에게 무슨 이야기를 해 줬는데, 그것을 그녀는 곧장 아주 신이 나서 되풀이했다."], ["Perry had been to Mrs. Goddard’s to attend a sick child, and Miss Nash had seen him, and he had told Miss Nash, that as he was coming back yesterday from Clayton Park, he had met Mr.", "미스터 페리가 아픈 아이를 보러 고더드 부인 댁에 왔었고, 미스 내시가 그를 보았는데, 그가 미스 내시에게 이렇게 말했다는 것이다."], ["Elton, and found to his great surprize, that Mr. Elton was actually on his road to London, and not meaning to return till the morrow, though it was the whist-club night, which he had been never known to miss before; and Mr.", "어제 클레이턴 파크에서 돌아오는 길에 미스터 엘턴과 마주쳤는데, 놀랍게도 미스터 엘턴이 실제로 런던으로 가는 길이며, 이튿날까지는 돌아오지 않을 작정이더라는 것이었다."], ["Perry had remonstrated with him about it, and told him how shabby it was in him, their best player, to absent himself, and tried very much to persuade him to put off his journey only one day; but it would not do; Mr.", "그날이 한 번도 빠진 적 없다고 알려진 휘스트 클럽이 있는 밤인데도 말이다. 미스터 페리가 그 점을 두고 그에게 핀잔을 주며, 가장 잘 치는 사람이 자리를 비우다니 너무하지 않느냐 하고는, 여행을 하루만 미루라고 한참을 설득했지만 소용없었다."], ["Elton had been determined to go on, and had said in a very particular way indeed, that he was going on business which he would not put off for any inducement in the world; and something about a very enviable commission, and being the bearer of something exceedingly precious.", "미스터 엘턴은 그대로 가기로 단단히 마음먹은 채, 세상 어떤 유혹에도 미룰 수 없는 볼일이 있어 간다고 아주 의미심장한 투로 말했다는 것이다. 그러면서 무척 부러움 살 만한 임무니, 더없이 소중한 무언가를 전하러 가는 길이니 하는 이야기를 했다고 한다."], ["Mr. Perry could not quite understand him, but he was very sure there must be a lady in the case, and he told him so; and Mr.", "미스터 페리는 그 말을 잘 이해할 수 없었으나, 분명 거기엔 어떤 숙녀가 얽혀 있는 게 틀림없다고 여겨 그렇게 말했더니, 미스터 엘턴은 그저 무척 의미심장하게 미소 지을 뿐, 잔뜩 들뜬 채로 말을 달려 가 버렸다는 것이다."], ["Elton only looked very conscious and smiling, and rode off in great spirits. Miss Nash had told her all this, and had talked a great deal more about Mr.", "미스 내시가 그녀에게 이 이야기를 죄다 들려주고는 미스터 엘턴에 관해 한참 더 떠들었으며, 그녀를 아주 의미심장하게 바라보면서 이렇게 말했다고 한다."], ["Elton; and said, looking so very significantly at her, “that she did not pretend to understand what his business might be, but she only knew that any woman whom Mr.", "그의 볼일이 무엇인지 안다고 하는 건 아니지만, 미스터 엘턴이 마음에 둘 만한 여자라면 누구든 세상에서 가장 운 좋은 여자라고 생각한다고."], ["Elton could prefer, she should think the luckiest woman in the world; for, beyond a doubt, Mr.", "미모로 보나 상냥함으로 보나 미스터 엘턴에게 견줄 사람은 분명 없으니까."], ["Elton had not his equal for beauty or agreeableness.”", "* 휘스트: 네 사람이 둘씩 짝을 지어 겨루는 18~19세기 영국 카드놀이로, 브리지의 전신."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter IX", "# 제1권 9장"]], [["Mr. Knightley might quarrel with her, but Emma could not quarrel with herself.", "미스터 나이틀리는 그녀와 다툴 수 있었을지 몰라도, 엠마는 자기 자신과 다툴 수는 없었다."], ["He was so much displeased, that it was longer than usual before he came to Hartfield again; and when they did meet, his grave looks shewed that she was not forgiven.", "그가 어찌나 못마땅해했던지, 다시 하트필드를 찾기까지는 여느 때보다 오래 걸렸다. 그리고 마침내 두 사람이 마주쳤을 때, 그의 굳은 표정은 그녀가 아직 용서받지 못했음을 말해 주었다."], ["She was sorry, but could not repent.", "그녀는 미안했지만 뉘우칠 수는 없었다."], ["On the contrary, her plans and proceedings were more and more justified and endeared to her by the general appearances of the next few days.", "오히려 그 뒤 며칠간 두루 펼쳐지는 정황은, 자신의 계획과 처사를 점점 더 정당해 보이게 하고 점점 더 흐뭇하게 만들어 주었다."]], [["The Picture, elegantly framed, came safely to hand soon after Mr.", "미스터 엘턴이 돌아오고 얼마 지나지 않아, 우아하게 액자에 끼운 그 그림이 무사히 당도했다."], ["Elton’s return, and being hung over the mantelpiece of the common sitting-room, he got up to look at it, and sighed out his half sentences of admiration just as he ought; and as for Harriet’s feelings, they were visibly forming themselves into as strong and steady an attachment as her youth and sort of mind admitted.", "그것을 평소 쓰는 거실 벽난로 위에 걸어 두자, 그는 일어서서 그것을 바라보며 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 감탄의 토막말을 한숨처럼 내뱉었다."], ["Emma was soon perfectly satisfied of Mr. Martin’s being no otherwise remembered, than as he furnished a contrast with Mr.", "해리엇의 마음으로 말하자면, 그녀의 나이와 천성이 허락하는 한도에서 가장 강하고 한결같은 연정으로 또렷이 영글어 가는 것이 눈에 보였다."], ["Elton, of the utmost advantage to the latter.", "엠마는 곧, 미스터 마틴이 미스터 엘턴을 더없이 돋보이게 해 주는 대조 인물로서가 아니라면 달리 기억되는 일은 없으리라는 데에 완전히 만족했다."]], [["Her views of improving her little friend’s mind, by a great deal of useful reading and conversation, had never yet led to more than a few first chapters, and the intention of going on to-morrow.", "유익한 독서와 대화를 잔뜩 시켜 어린 벗의 지성을 길러 주려던 그녀의 포부는, 여태껏 첫 몇 장(章)과 내일은 더 나아가자는 다짐 이상으로 진척된 적이 없었다."], ["It was much easier to chat than to study; much pleasanter to let her imagination range and work at Harriet’s fortune, than to be labouring to enlarge her comprehension or exercise it on sober facts; and the only literary pursuit which engaged Harriet at present, the only mental provision she was making for the evening of life, was the collecting and transcribing all the riddles of every sort that she could meet with, into a thin quarto of hot-pressed paper, made up by her friend, and ornamented with ciphers and trophies.", "공부하기보다 수다 떠는 편이 훨씬 쉬웠고, 차분한 사실들로 해리엇의 이해력을 넓히거나 단련시키려 애쓰는 것보다, 상상의 나래를 펴 해리엇의 앞날을 그려 보는 편이 훨씬 즐거웠다. 그리고 지금 해리엇을 사로잡은 유일한 문학적 소일거리, 인생의 황혼을 위해 그녀가 마련해 두는 유일한 정신의 양식이라고는, 마주치는 온갖 종류의 수수께끼를 죄다 모아, 벗이 만들어 주고 암호와 트로피 무늬로 꾸며 준 얄팍한 4절판 광택지 공책에 베껴 옮기는 일이었다."]], [["In this age of literature, such collections on a very grand scale are not uncommon.", "이 문학의 시대에, 그런 수집은 아주 대규모라 해도 드물지 않았다."], ["Miss Nash, head-teacher at Mrs. Goddard’s, had written out at least three hundred; and Harriet, who had taken the first hint of it from her, hoped, with Miss Woodhouse’s help, to get a great many more.", "고더드 부인 학교의 수석 교사인 미스 내시는 적어도 삼백 편은 적어 두었고, 그녀에게서 처음 그 착상을 얻은 해리엇은 미스 우드하우스의 도움으로 그보다 훨씬 많이 모으기를 바랐다."], ["Emma assisted with her invention, memory and taste; and as Harriet wrote a very pretty hand, it was likely to be an arrangement of the first order, in form as well as quantity.", "엠마는 자신의 창의력과 기억력과 안목으로 거들었다. 게다가 해리엇이 글씨를 아주 예쁘게 쓰는 터라, 그 공책은 양으로 보나 모양으로 보나 일류의 작품이 될 듯했다."]], [["Mr. Woodhouse was almost as much interested in the business as the girls, and tried very often to recollect something worth their putting in.", "미스터 우드하우스도 두 아가씨 못지않게 이 일에 관심을 두어, 공책에 넣을 만한 것을 떠올려 보려고 자주 애를 썼다."], ["“So many clever riddles as there used to be when he was young—he wondered he could not remember them!", "\"내가 젊었을 적엔 영리한 수수께끼가 그렇게나 많았는데 — 어째서 그게 기억이 안 나는지 모르겠구나!"], ["but he hoped he should in time.”", "하지만 차차 떠오르겠지.\""], ["And it always ended in “Kitty, a fair but frozen maid.”", "그리고 그것은 언제나 \"키티, 곱지만 얼어붙은 처녀\"로 끝나곤 했다."]], [["His good friend Perry, too, whom he had spoken to on the subject, did not at present recollect any thing of the riddle kind; but he had desired Perry to be upon the watch, and as he went about so much, something, he thought, might come from that quarter.", "이 일에 관해 의논한 그의 좋은 벗 미스터 페리 역시 지금으로서는 수수께끼류라곤 아무것도 떠올리지 못했다. 그러나 그는 페리에게 늘 눈여겨봐 달라고 부탁해 두었고, 페리는 워낙 여기저기 많이 돌아다니는 사람이니 그쪽에서 뭔가 나오지 않을까 생각했다."]], [["It was by no means his daughter’s wish that the intellects of Highbury in general should be put under requisition.", "하이베리 사람들의 지력을 두루 차출하는 것은 결코 그의 딸이 바라는 바가 아니었다. 엠마가 도움을 청한 사람은 미스터 엘턴 하나뿐이었다."], ["Mr. Elton was the only one whose assistance she asked.", "정말 좋은 수수께끼나 셔레이드, 또는 재담이 생각나거든 좀 보태 달라고 그를 청했다."], ["He was invited to contribute any really good enigmas, charades, or conundrums that he might recollect; and she had the pleasure of seeing him most intently at work with his recollections; and at the same time, as she could perceive, most earnestly careful that nothing ungallant, nothing that did not breathe a compliment to the sex should pass his lips.", "그리고 그녀는, 그가 기억을 더듬으며 더없이 골몰하는 모습을 흐뭇하게 지켜보았다. 동시에, 그녀가 알아챈 바로는, 신사답지 못한 것이나 여성에 대한 찬사가 깃들지 않은 것은 한마디도 입에 올리지 않으려고 그가 무척이나 정성스레 조심하는 것도 보였다."], ["They owed to him their two or three politest puzzles; and the joy and exultation with which at last he recalled, and rather sentimentally recited, that well-known charade,", "두세 편의 더없이 점잖은 수수께끼는 그의 덕이었다. 그리고 그가 마침내 그 유명한 셔레이드를 떠올려, 자못 감상적으로 읊었을 때의 그 기쁨과 의기양양함이란—"]], [["My first doth affliction denote, Which my second is destin’d to feel And my whole is the best antidote That affliction to soften and heal.—", "내 첫째는 괴로움을 가리키니,\n    둘째가 그것을 느끼게 마련이라. 허나 내 전체는 더없이 좋은 약,\n    그 괴로움을 누그러뜨려 낫게 하리라.—"]], [["made her quite sorry to acknowledge that they had transcribed it some pages ago already.", "— 그 시를 두고, 우리가 이미 몇 장 앞에 베껴 두었노라고 인정하기가 그녀로서는 자못 미안할 지경이었다. * 셔레이드(수수께끼 시): 한 단어를 음절별로 나누어 각 음절과 전체를 시구로 묘사해 알아맞히게 하는 말놀이."]], [["“Why will not you write one yourself for us, Mr.", "\"미스터 엘턴, 직접 한 편 지어 주시지 그러세요?\""], ["Elton?”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “that is the only security for its freshness; and nothing could be easier to you.”", "\"그래야 새것임이 보장되는 유일한 길이잖아요. 그리고 당신께야 그보다 쉬운 일도 없을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Oh no!", "\"오, 아닙니다!"], ["he had never written, hardly ever, any thing of the kind in his life.", "저는 평생 그런 걸 지어 본 적이 없습니다, 거의 한 번도요. 이렇게 둔한 사람이라니!"], ["The stupidest fellow!", "미스 우드하우스조차도—\""], ["He was afraid not even Miss Woodhouse”—he stopt a moment—“or Miss Smith could inspire him.”", "그가 잠시 말을 멈추었다. \"—아니, 미스 스미스조차도 제게 영감을 불어넣지 못할까 두렵습니다.\""]], [["The very next day however produced some proof of inspiration.", "그러나 바로 이튿날, 영감의 증거 비슷한 것이 나타났다."], ["He called for a few moments, just to leave a piece of paper on the table containing, as he said, a charade, which a friend of his had addressed to a young lady, the object of his admiration, but which, from his manner, Emma was immediately convinced must be his own.", "그가 잠깐 들러, 탁자 위에 종이 한 장을 놓고 갔던 것이다. 그의 말로는 어떤 벗이 자신이 흠모하는 한 젊은 아가씨에게 보낸 셔레이드라고 했지만, 그 태도로 보아 엠마는 그것이 분명 그 자신의 작품이리라고 곧장 확신했다."]], [["“I do not offer it for Miss Smith’s collection,”", "\"미스 스미스의 수집첩에 넣으시라고 드리는 건 아닙니다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Being my friend’s, I have no right to expose it in any degree to the public eye, but perhaps you may not dislike looking at it.”", "\"제 벗의 것이니, 제가 이를 조금이라도 세상에 내보일 권리는 없지요. 하지만 한번 들여다보시는 거야 싫지 않으실 듯해서요.\""]], [["The speech was more to Emma than to Harriet, which Emma could understand.", "이 말은 해리엇보다 엠마를 향한 것이었고, 엠마는 그 까닭을 알 만했다."], ["There was deep consciousness about him, and he found it easier to meet her eye than her friend’s.", "그에게는 깊은 자의식이 서려 있었고, 그는 벗의 눈보다 그녀의 눈을 마주하는 편이 더 수월하다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["He was gone the next moment:—after another moment’s pause,", "그는 곧 떠나갔다. 다시 잠깐의 침묵이 흐른 뒤,"]], [["“Take it,”", "\"받아요.\""], ["said Emma, smiling, and pushing the paper towards Harriet—“it is for you.", "엠마가 미소 지으며, 종이를 해리엇 쪽으로 밀어 주며 말했다. \"당신 거예요."], ["Take your own.”", "당신 것이니 가지세요.\""]], [["But Harriet was in a tremor, and could not touch it; and Emma, never loth to be first, was obliged to examine it herself.", "그러나 해리엇은 떨고 있어 그것에 손도 대지 못했다. 그리고 무슨 일이든 앞장서기를 마다하지 않는 엠마는, 별수 없이 자기가 직접 들여다보아야 했다."]], [["To Miss——", "미스 ㅇㅇㅇ에게"]], [["CHARADE.", "셔레이드"]], [["My first displays the wealth and pomp of kings, Lords of the earth!", "내 첫째는 임금들의 부귀와 영화를 펼쳐 보이니,\n    땅의 주인들이로다!"], ["their luxury and ease.", "그들의 사치와 안일이여."], ["Another view of man, my second brings, Behold him there, the monarch of the seas!", "인간의 또 다른 면모를 내 둘째가 가져오니,\n    저기 보라, 바다의 군주를!"]], [["But ah!", "허나 아아!"], ["united, what reverse we have!", "둘이 하나로 합치면 어떤 역전이 펼쳐지는가!"], ["Man’s boasted power and freedom, all are flown; Lord of the earth and sea, he bends a slave, And woman, lovely woman, reigns alone.", "인간이 뽐내던 권세도 자유도 죄다 사라지고,\n땅과 바다의 주인은 노예로 굽실거리니,\n    여인이, 사랑스러운 여인이 홀로 군림하도다."]], [["Thy ready wit the word will soon supply, May its approval beam in that soft eye!", "그대의 재치가 그 낱말을 곧 일러 주리니,\n    그 부드러운 눈에 허락의 빛이 비치기를!"]], [["She cast her eye over it, pondered, caught the meaning, read it through again to be quite certain, and quite mistress of the lines, and then passing it to Harriet, sat happily smiling, and saying to herself, while Harriet was puzzling over the paper in all the confusion of hope and dulness, “Very well, Mr.", "그녀는 그 위로 눈길을 죽 훑고, 곰곰 생각하다 뜻을 알아채고는, 확실히 해 두고 시구를 완전히 제 것으로 삼기 위해 한 번 더 통독했다. 그러고는 그것을 해리엇에게 건네주고, 흐뭇하게 미소 지으며 앉아 있었다."], ["Elton, very well indeed. I have read worse charades.", "해리엇이 희망과 둔함이 뒤섞인 혼란 속에 그 종이를 들여다보며 쩔쩔매는 동안, 엠마는 속으로 이렇게 되뇌었다."], ["Courtship—a very good hint.", "\"아주 좋아요, 미스터 엘턴, 정말 훌륭해요."], ["I give you credit for it.", "이보다 못한 셔레이드도 읽어 봤는걸요."], ["This is feeling your way.", "구애(courtship) — 아주 좋은 암시예요. 그 점은 인정해 드리죠."], ["This is saying very plainly—‘Pray, Miss Smith, give me leave to pay my addresses to you.", "이건 길을 더듬어 보는 거예요. 이건 아주 분명하게 이렇게 말하는 거죠 — '미스 스미스, 부디 제가 당신께 구애할 수 있도록 허락해 주십시오."], ["Approve my charade and my intentions in the same glance.’", "제 셔레이드와 제 마음을 같은 눈길로 받아 주십시오.'"]], [["May its approval beam in that soft eye!", "그 부드러운 눈에 허락의 빛이 비치기를!"]], [["Harriet exactly.", "해리엇 그대로구나."], ["Soft is the very word for her eye—of all epithets, the justest that could be given.", "부드럽다는 건 그 애 눈에 꼭 맞는 말이야 — 온갖 수식어 가운데 더없이 알맞은 표현이지."]], [["Thy ready wit the word will soon supply.", "그대의 재치가 그 낱말을 곧 일러 주리니."]], [["Humph—Harriet’s ready wit!", "흠 — 해리엇의 재치라!"], ["All the better.", "차라리 잘됐어."], ["A man must be very much in love, indeed, to describe her so.", "남자가 그 애를 그렇게 묘사할 정도라면, 정말이지 단단히 사랑에 빠진 게 분명하지."], ["Ah! Mr.", "아아!"], ["Knightley, I wish you had the benefit of this; I think this would convince you.", "미스터 나이틀리, 당신께서 이걸 보셨으면 좋으련만. 이거라면 당신도 수긍하실걸요."], ["For once in your life you would be obliged to own yourself mistaken.", "평생 한 번쯤은 당신이 틀렸다고 인정하지 않을 수 없으실 거예요."], ["An excellent charade indeed!", "정말이지 훌륭한 셔레이드예요!"], ["and very much to the purpose.", "그리고 더없이 딱 들어맞고요."], ["Things must come to a crisis soon now.”", "이제 일이 곧 고비를 맞겠어.\""]], [["She was obliged to break off from these very pleasant observations, which were otherwise of a sort to run into great length, by the eagerness of Harriet’s wondering questions.", "이 더없이 흐뭇한 사색은 그러지 않았다면 한참을 이어졌을 법한 것이었으나, 영문을 모르겠다는 해리엇의 다급한 물음에 끊겼다."]], [["“What can it be, Miss Woodhouse?—what can it be?", "\"이게 뭘까요, 미스 우드하우스? 뭘까요?"], ["I have not an idea—I cannot guess it in the least.", "저는 도통 모르겠어요 — 조금도 짐작이 안 가요."], ["What can it possibly be?", "대체 뭘 수 있을까요?"], ["Do try to find it out, Miss Woodhouse.", "좀 알아내 주세요, 미스 우드하우스."], ["Do help me.", "좀 도와주세요."], ["I never saw any thing so hard.", "이렇게 어려운 건 처음 봐요."], ["Is it kingdom?", "왕국(kingdom)일까요?"], ["I wonder who the friend was—and who could be the young lady.", "그 벗이 누군지, 그 젊은 아가씨가 누군지 궁금해요."], ["Do you think it is a good one?", "잘 지은 것 같아요?"], ["Can it be woman?", "여인(woman)일까요?"]], [["And woman, lovely woman, reigns alone.", "여인이, 사랑스러운 여인이 홀로 군림하도다."]], [["Can it be Neptune?", "넵튠(Neptune)일까요?"]], [["Behold him there, the monarch of the seas!", "저기 보라, 바다의 군주를!"]], [["Or a trident? or a mermaid?", "아니면 삼지창(trident)?"], ["or a shark? Oh, no!", "인어(mermaid)?"], ["shark is only one syllable.", "상어(shark)? 아유, 아니에요!"], ["It must be very clever, or he would not have brought it.", "상어는 한 음절뿐인걸요. 분명 아주 영리한 거겠죠, 안 그러면 가져오지도 않으셨을 테니까요."], ["Oh!", "아아!"], ["Miss Woodhouse, do you think we shall ever find it out?”", "미스 우드하우스, 우리가 정말 알아낼 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“Mermaids and sharks!", "\"인어니 상어니!"], ["Nonsense!", "말도 안 돼요!"], ["My dear Harriet, what are you thinking of?", "해리엇, 무슨 생각을 하는 거예요?"], ["Where would be the use of his bringing us a charade made by a friend upon a mermaid or a shark?", "그분이 인어나 상어를 두고 벗이 지은 셔레이드를 가져다줄 까닭이 어디 있겠어요?"], ["Give me the paper and listen.", "그 종이를 이리 줘 보고, 들어 봐요."]], [["For Miss ———, read Miss Smith.", "미스 ㅇㅇㅇ에게, 라는 건 미스 스미스에게, 라고 읽으세요."]], [["My first displays the wealth and pomp of kings, Lords of the earth!", "내 첫째는 임금들의 부귀와 영화를 펼쳐 보이니,\n    땅의 주인들이로다!"], ["their luxury and ease.", "그들의 사치와 안일이여."]], [["That is court.", "이건 궁정(court)이에요."]], [["Another view of man, my second brings; Behold him there, the monarch of the seas!", "인간의 또 다른 면모를 내 둘째가 가져오니,\n    저기 보라, 바다의 군주를!"]], [["That is ship;—plain as it can be.—Now for the cream.", "이건 배(ship) — 이보다 더 명백할 수가 없죠. 자, 이제 알맹이예요."]], [["But ah!", "허나 아아!"], ["united, (courtship, you know,) what reverse we have!", "둘이 하나로 합치면, (구애courtship 말이에요, 알겠죠,) 어떤 역전이 펼쳐지는가!"], ["Man’s boasted power and freedom, all are flown. Lord of the earth and sea, he bends a slave, And woman, lovely woman, reigns alone.", "인간이 뽐내던 권세도 자유도 죄다 사라지고,\n땅과 바다의 주인은 노예로 굽실거리니,\n    여인이, 사랑스러운 여인이 홀로 군림하도다."]], [["A very proper compliment!—and then follows the application, which I think, my dear Harriet, you cannot find much difficulty in comprehending.", "아주 적절한 찬사로군요! 그리고 그 뒤에 적용 구절이 따르는데, 해리엇, 이건 알아듣기에 그리 어렵지 않을 거예요."], ["Read it in comfort to yourself.", "마음 편히 혼자 읽어 봐요."], ["There can be no doubt of its being written for you and to you.”", "이게 당신을 위해, 당신에게 쓰였다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없어요.\""]], [["Harriet could not long resist so delightful a persuasion.", "해리엇은 그토록 기쁜 설득을 오래 거스를 수가 없었다."], ["She read the concluding lines, and was all flutter and happiness.", "마무리 시구를 읽고는, 온통 설렘과 행복에 휩싸였다."], ["She could not speak.", "그녀는 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["But she was not wanted to speak.", "그러나 말할 필요는 없었다."], ["It was enough for her to feel.", "그녀로서는 그저 느끼는 것만으로 충분했다."], ["Emma spoke for her.", "엠마가 대신 말해 주었다."]], [["“There is so pointed, and so particular a meaning in this compliment,”", "\"이 찬사에는 너무도 콕 집어 가리키는, 너무도 각별한 뜻이 담겨 있어요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “that I cannot have a doubt as to Mr. Elton’s intentions.", "\"그래서 저는 미스터 엘턴의 마음에 대해 추호의 의심도 품을 수가 없어요."], ["You are his object—and you will soon receive the completest proof of it.", "당신이 그분의 마음이 향한 사람이에요 — 머잖아 그 가장 완벽한 증거를 받게 될 거예요."], ["I thought it must be so.", "저는 분명 그러리라 생각했어요."], ["I thought I could not be so deceived; but now, it is clear; the state of his mind is as clear and decided, as my wishes on the subject have been ever since I knew you.", "제가 그렇게까지 잘못 볼 리 없다고 생각했고요. 그런데 이제 분명해졌어요."], ["Yes, Harriet, just so long have I been wanting the very circumstance to happen that has happened.", "그분의 마음 상태는, 당신을 알게 된 뒤로 줄곧 이 일에 대해 제가 품어 온 바람만큼이나 분명하고 확고해요."], ["I could never tell whether an attachment between you and Mr. Elton were most desirable or most natural.", "그래요, 해리엇, 바로 그만큼 오래 저는 지금 일어난 바로 이 일이 일어나기를 바라 왔어요."], ["Its probability and its eligibility have really so equalled each other! I am very happy.", "당신과 미스터 엘턴 사이의 연정이 가장 바람직한 것인지 가장 자연스러운 것인지, 저는 도무지 가릴 수가 없었어요."], ["I congratulate you, my dear Harriet, with all my heart.", "그 개연성과 그 합당함이 정말이지 그토록 엇비슷했거든요!"], ["This is an attachment which a woman may well feel pride in creating.", "저는 정말 기뻐요. 진심으로 축하해요, 해리엇."], ["This is a connexion which offers nothing but good.", "이건 여자가 맺어 주었다는 데에 능히 자부심을 느낄 만한 연정이에요."], ["It will give you every thing that you want—consideration, independence, a proper home—it will fix you in the centre of all your real friends, close to Hartfield and to me, and confirm our intimacy for ever.", "이건 좋은 것밖에 가져다주지 않는 인연이고요. 당신이 바라는 모든 것을 줄 거예요 — 존중, 자립, 번듯한 가정을요 — 당신을 진정한 벗들 한가운데, 하트필드와 제 곁에 붙박아 두고, 우리 사이의 친밀함을 영원히 굳혀 줄 거예요."], ["This, Harriet, is an alliance which can never raise a blush in either of us.”", "이건, 해리엇, 우리 둘 중 누구의 얼굴도 결코 붉히게 하지 않을 결합이에요.\""]], [["“Dear Miss Woodhouse!”—and “Dear Miss Woodhouse,”", "\"미스 우드하우스, 정말 고마워요!\""], ["was all that Harriet, with many tender embraces could articulate at first; but when they did arrive at something more like conversation, it was sufficiently clear to her friend that she saw, felt, anticipated, and remembered just as she ought.", "— 그리고 \"미스 우드하우스, 고마워요\"라는 말만이, 다정하게 몇 번이고 끌어안으며 해리엇이 처음에 겨우 입에 올릴 수 있는 전부였다. 그러나 두 사람이 대화다운 대화에 이르렀을 때, 해리엇이 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 보고, 느끼고, 기대하고, 기억하고 있음이 벗에게 충분히 분명해졌다."], ["Mr. Elton’s superiority had very ample acknowledgment.", "미스터 엘턴의 출중함은 더없이 충분한 인정을 받았다."]], [["“Whatever you say is always right,”", "\"미스 우드하우스가 하시는 말씀은 언제나 옳아요.\""], ["cried Harriet, “and therefore I suppose, and believe, and hope it must be so; but otherwise I could not have imagined it.", "해리엇이 외쳤다. \"그러니 저도 그러려니 짐작하고, 믿고, 바라요 — 그렇지 않고서야 저로서는 도무지 상상도 못 했을 거예요."], ["It is so much beyond any thing I deserve.", "이건 제가 누릴 자격을 한참 넘어서는 일이거든요."], ["Mr. Elton, who might marry any body!", "누구하고든 결혼할 수 있는 미스터 엘턴인데!"], ["There cannot be two opinions about him.", "그분에 대해선 두말할 게 없죠. 그렇게 출중한 분이니까요."], ["He is so very superior.", "그 어여쁜 시구를 좀 보세요 — '미스 ㅇㅇㅇ에게.'"], ["Only think of those sweet verses—‘To Miss ———.’ Dear me, how clever!—Could it really be meant for me?”", "어머나, 어쩜 이리 영리하실까! 정말로 저를 두고 쓰신 걸까요?\""]], [["“I cannot make a question, or listen to a question about that.", "\"그 점에 대해선 저는 의문을 제기할 수도, 의문을 들을 수도 없어요."], ["It is a certainty.", "그건 확실한 일이에요."], ["Receive it on my judgment.", "제 판단을 믿고 받아들여요."], ["It is a sort of prologue to the play, a motto to the chapter; and will be soon followed by matter-of-fact prose.”", "이건 연극의 서막 같은 것, 한 장(章)의 머리에 붙는 제사(題詞) 같은 거예요. 머잖아 사실 그대로의 산문이 뒤따를 거고요.\""]], [["“It is a sort of thing which nobody could have expected.", "\"이건 아무도 예상 못 했을 일이에요."], ["I am sure, a month ago, I had no more idea myself!—The strangest things do take place!”", "정말이지, 한 달 전만 해도 저 자신도 생각조차 못 했는걸요! — 세상에 별일이 다 일어나네요!\""]], [["“When Miss Smiths and Mr. Eltons get acquainted—they do indeed—and really it is strange; it is out of the common course that what is so evidently, so palpably desirable—what courts the pre-arrangement of other people, should so immediately shape itself into the proper form.", "\"미스 스미스 같은 사람들과 미스터 엘턴 같은 사람들이 서로 알게 되면 — 정말 그렇게 되는 법이고요 — 또 정말이지 신기한 일이에요."], ["You and Mr. Elton are by situation called together; you belong to one another by every circumstance of your respective homes.", "이렇게 너무도 뚜렷이, 너무도 분명히 바람직한 것 — 남들이 미리 주선하고 싶어 할 만한 것이, 이토록 당장에 제대로 된 모양새로 빚어진다는 건 흔한 일이 아니거든요."], ["Your marrying will be equal to the match at Randalls.", "당신과 미스터 엘턴은 처지로 보아 서로 부름받은 사이예요. 두 분은 각자의 가정 형편 모든 면에서 서로에게 속해 있고요."], ["There does seem to be a something in the air of Hartfield which gives love exactly the right direction, and sends it into the very channel where it ought to flow.", "두 분의 결혼은 랜덜스의 혼사에 맞먹는 일이 될 거예요. 하트필드의 공기에는 정말이지 무언가가 있는 것 같아요 — 사랑에 꼭 알맞은 방향을 잡아 주고, 마땅히 흘러야 할 바로 그 물길로 흘려보내 주는 무언가가요."]], [["The course of true love never did run smooth—", "진정한 사랑의 길은 결코 순탄히 흐른 적이 없으니 —"]], [["A Hartfield edition of Shakespeare would have a long note on that passage.”", "하트필드판 셰익스피어라면 그 구절에 긴 주석을 달아야 할 거예요.\" * 인용은 셰익스피어 「한여름 밤의 꿈」의 한 구절."]], [["“That Mr. Elton should really be in love with me,—me, of all people, who did not know him, to speak to him, at Michaelmas!", "\"미스터 엘턴이 정말로 저를 사랑하다니 — 하고많은 사람 중에 저를, 미카엘 축일* 무렵만 해도 말을 나누기는커녕 알지도 못했던 저를!"], ["And he, the very handsomest man that ever was, and a man that every body looks up to, quite like Mr. Knightley!", "게다가 그분은 여태껏 본 중에 가장 잘생긴 남자고, 모두가 우러러보는 분, 꼭 미스터 나이틀리 같은 분인데!"], ["His company so sought after, that every body says he need not eat a single meal by himself if he does not chuse it; that he has more invitations than there are days in the week.", "그분을 찾는 데가 어찌나 많은지, 본인만 마음먹지 않으면 끼니 한 번 혼자 들 일이 없다고들, 일주일 날수보다 초대받는 자리가 더 많다고들 다들 말하잖아요. 그리고 교회에서 또 얼마나 훌륭하신데요!"], ["And so excellent in the Church!", "미스 내시는 그분이 하이베리에 오신 뒤로 설교하신 성경 구절을 죄다 적어 두었대요. 어머나!"], ["Miss Nash has put down all the texts he has ever preached from since he came to Highbury.", "그분을 처음 뵈었던 때를 돌이켜 보면! 제가 어찌 짐작이나 했겠어요!"], ["Dear me! When I look back to the first time I saw him!", "— 그분이 지나가신다는 말을 듣고 애벗 자매하고 제가 앞방으로 달려가 블라인드 틈으로 엿보았는데, 미스 내시가 와서 우리를 야단쳐 쫓아 놓고는 본인이 남아서 들여다보았어요."], ["How little did I think!—The two Abbots and I ran into the front room and peeped through the blind when we heard he was going by, and Miss Nash came and scolded us away, and staid to look through herself; however, she called me back presently, and let me look too, which was very good-natured.", "그래도 곧 저를 다시 불러서 저도 보게 해 주었으니, 참 마음씨가 고우셨죠. 그리고 그분이 어찌나 멋져 보이던지!"], ["And how beautiful we thought he looked!", "미스터 콜과 팔짱을 끼고 계셨어요.\" * 미카엘 축일(Michaelmas): 9월 29일."], ["He was arm-in-arm with Mr. Cole.”", "영국에서 분기·임차 기한의 기준이 되던 날."]], [["“This is an alliance which, whoever—whatever your friends may be, must be agreeable to them, provided at least they have common sense; and we are not to be addressing our conduct to fools. If they are anxious to see you happily married, here is a man whose amiable character gives every assurance of it;—if they wish to have you settled in the same country and circle which they have chosen to place you in, here it will be accomplished; and if their only object is that you should, in the common phrase, be well married, here is the comfortable fortune, the respectable establishment, the rise in the world which must satisfy them.”", "\"이건, 당신의 벗들이 누구든 — 무엇이든 간에, 적어도 그들에게 분별이 있다면야 흡족해할 결합이에요. 우리야 어리석은 자들에게 처신을 맞출 까닭이 없으니까요. 그들이 당신을 행복하게 결혼시키고 싶어 한다면, 여기 그 어진 성품으로 그것을 온전히 보장해 줄 남자가 있어요. 그들이 당신을, 자기들이 골라 자리 잡게 해 준 바로 그 고장과 그 사교 범위 안에 정착시키고 싶어 한다면, 여기서 그게 이루어질 거고요. 그리고 그들의 유일한 바람이 흔히 말하듯 당신이 좋은 데 시집가는 것뿐이라면, 여기 그들을 만족시킬 만한 넉넉한 재산, 번듯한 가정, 출세가 있어요.\""]], [["“Yes, very true.", "\"네, 정말 그래요."], ["How nicely you talk; I love to hear you.", "어쩜 그리 멋지게 말씀하세요. 듣고 있으면 참 좋아요."], ["You understand every thing.", "미스 우드하우스는 뭐든 다 아시네요."], ["You and Mr. Elton are one as clever as the other.", "미스 우드하우스와 미스터 엘턴은 두 분 다 똑같이 영리하시고요."], ["This charade!—If I had studied a twelvemonth, I could never have made any thing like it.”", "이 셔레이드 좀 봐요! — 제가 일 년을 공부했어도 이런 건 결코 못 지었을 거예요.\""]], [["“I thought he meant to try his skill, by his manner of declining it yesterday.”", "\"어제 사양하시던 그 태도로 보아, 솜씨를 한번 시험해 보실 작정이구나 싶었어요.\""]], [["“I do think it is, without exception, the best charade I ever read.”", "\"이건 정말이지, 두말할 것 없이 제가 읽어 본 중에 가장 훌륭한 셔레이드예요.\""]], [["“I never read one more to the purpose, certainly.”", "\"이보다 더 딱 들어맞는 건 분명 한 번도 못 봤어요.\""]], [["“It is as long again as almost all we have had before.”", "\"이건 전에 우리가 봤던 거의 모든 것보다 두 배는 길어요.\""]], [["“I do not consider its length as particularly in its favour.", "\"길이가 특별히 그것의 장점이라고는 생각지 않아요."], ["Such things in general cannot be too short.”", "이런 건 대체로 짧으면 짧을수록 좋거든요.\""]], [["Harriet was too intent on the lines to hear.", "해리엇은 그 시구에 너무 골몰해 있어 듣지 못했다."], ["The most satisfactory comparisons were rising in her mind.", "더없이 흡족한 비교들이 그녀의 머릿속에 떠오르고 있었다."]], [["“It is one thing,”", "\"이건 별개의 일이에요.\""], ["said she, presently—her cheeks in a glow—“to have very good sense in a common way, like every body else, and if there is any thing to say, to sit down and write a letter, and say just what you must, in a short way; and another, to write verses and charades like this.”", "이윽고 그녀가, 두 뺨을 발갛게 물들이며 말했다. \"남들처럼 평범하게 분별이 좀 있어서, 할 말이 있으면 앉아서 편지를 써, 꼭 해야 할 말을 짤막하게 적는 거하고요 — 이렇게 시구나 셔레이드를 짓는 거하고는요.\""]], [["Emma could not have desired a more spirited rejection of Mr. Martin’s prose.", "미스터 마틴의 산문에 대한 이보다 더 기운찬 거부를 엠마는 바랄 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["“Such sweet lines!”", "\"이렇게 어여쁜 시구라니!\" 해리엇이 말을 이었다."], ["continued Harriet—“these two last!—But how shall I ever be able to return the paper, or say I have found it out?—Oh!", "\"이 마지막 두 줄 좀 봐요! — 그런데 이 종이를 대체 어떻게 돌려드리죠, 아니면 제가 답을 알아냈다고 어떻게 말하고요?"], ["Miss Woodhouse, what can we do about that?”", "— 아아! 미스 우드하우스, 그 일을 어떻게 하면 좋을까요?\""]], [["“Leave it to me.", "\"제게 맡겨요. 당신은 아무것도 하지 말고요."], ["You do nothing.", "오늘 저녁이면 그분이 분명 여기 오실 거예요."], ["He will be here this evening, I dare say, and then I will give it him back, and some nonsense or other will pass between us, and you shall not be committed.—Your soft eyes shall chuse their own time for beaming.", "그때 제가 돌려드리고, 우리끼리 이런저런 실없는 말이 오갈 테니, 당신이 나설 일은 없을 거예요. — 당신의 부드러운 눈은 빛을 발할 때를 스스로 고르면 돼요."], ["Trust to me.”", "저만 믿어요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아아!"], ["Miss Woodhouse, what a pity that I must not write this beautiful charade into my book!", "미스 우드하우스, 이 아름다운 셔레이드를 제 공책에 적어 넣을 수 없다니 정말 아쉬워요!"], ["I am sure I have not got one half so good.”", "분명 이 절반만큼 좋은 것도 저는 한 편도 못 가졌는걸요.\""]], [["“Leave out the two last lines, and there is no reason why you should not write it into your book.”", "\"마지막 두 줄만 빼면, 공책에 적어 넣지 못할 까닭이 없어요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아아!"], ["but those two lines are”—", "하지만 그 두 줄은 —\""]], [["—“The best of all.", "\"가장 좋은 부분이죠. 인정해요."], ["Granted;—for private enjoyment; and for private enjoyment keep them.", "— 혼자만의 즐거움을 위해서요. 그러니 혼자 간직할 몫으로 두세요."], ["They are not at all the less written you know, because you divide them.", "알다시피, 둘로 나눈다고 해서 그 줄이 쓰이지 않은 게 되는 건 아니에요."], ["The couplet does not cease to be, nor does its meaning change.", "그 대구가 사라지는 것도, 그 뜻이 달라지는 것도 아니고요."], ["But take it away, and all appropriation ceases, and a very pretty gallant charade remains, fit for any collection.", "하지만 그 부분을 떼어 내면, 모든 임자 표시가 사라지고, 어느 수집첩에든 어울릴 만한 아주 어여쁜 점잖은 셔레이드만 남게 되죠."], ["Depend upon it, he would not like to have his charade slighted, much better than his passion.", "정말이에요, 그분은 자기 셔레이드가 푸대접받는 걸, 자기 연정이 푸대접받는 것보다 더 싫어하실 거예요."], ["A poet in love must be encouraged in both capacities, or neither.", "사랑에 빠진 시인이라면 양쪽 자격 모두에서 북돋아 주든가, 아니면 둘 다 그만두든가 해야 하는 법이거든요."], ["Give me the book, I will write it down, and then there can be no possible reflection on you.”", "공책을 이리 줘 봐요, 제가 적어 넣을게요. 그러면 당신한테 돌아올 책망 같은 건 전혀 없을 거예요.\""]], [["Harriet submitted, though her mind could hardly separate the parts, so as to feel quite sure that her friend were not writing down a declaration of love.", "해리엇은 따랐다. 다만 그녀의 머릿속에서는 그 부분들을 잘라 내기가 좀처럼 어려워, 벗이 적어 넣는 것이 사랑의 고백이 아니라고는 영 확신할 수가 없었다."], ["It seemed too precious an offering for any degree of publicity.", "그것은 조금이라도 세상에 내놓기엔 너무도 소중한 선물처럼 여겨졌다."]], [["“I shall never let that book go out of my own hands,”", "\"이 공책은 결코 제 손에서 내놓지 않을 거예요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Very well,”", "\"아주 좋아요.\" 엠마가 대답했다."], ["replied Emma; “a most natural feeling; and the longer it lasts, the better I shall be pleased.", "\"더없이 자연스러운 마음이죠. 그리고 그 마음이 오래갈수록 저도 더 기쁠 거예요."], ["But here is my father coming: you will not object to my reading the charade to him.", "그런데 마침 아버지가 오시네요. 이 셔레이드를 아버지께 읽어 드려도 싫지 않겠죠?"], ["It will be giving him so much pleasure!", "그러면 아버지가 무척 기뻐하실 거예요!"], ["He loves any thing of the sort, and especially any thing that pays woman a compliment.", "아버지는 이런 종류를 다 좋아하시거든요. 특히 여성에게 찬사를 바치는 거라면 더더욱요."], ["He has the tenderest spirit of gallantry towards us all!—You must let me read it to him.”", "우리 모두를 향한 다정한 기사도 정신이 그득한 분이세요! — 제가 아버지께 읽어 드리게 해 줘요.\""]], [["Harriet looked grave.", "해리엇의 표정이 굳어졌다."]], [["“My dear Harriet, you must not refine too much upon this charade.—You will betray your feelings improperly, if you are too conscious and too quick, and appear to affix more meaning, or even quite all the meaning which may be affixed to it.", "\"해리엇, 이 셔레이드를 두고 너무 깊이 파고들면 안 돼요. — 너무 의식하고 너무 성급하게 굴어서, 거기 담겼을 법한 의미보다 더 많은 의미를, 아니 거기 담길 수 있는 의미 전부를 갖다 붙이는 듯이 보이면, 당신 마음을 엉뚱하게 들켜 버리고 말 거예요."], ["Do not be overpowered by such a little tribute of admiration.", "이런 자그마한 찬사 하나에 짓눌리지 말아요."], ["If he had been anxious for secrecy, he would not have left the paper while I was by; but he rather pushed it towards me than towards you.", "그분이 정말 비밀로 하고 싶었다면, 제가 곁에 있는데 그 종이를 두고 가지 않았을 거예요. 게다가 그분은 그걸 당신보다는 오히려 제 쪽으로 밀어 놓으셨고요."], ["Do not let us be too solemn on the business.", "이 일을 두고 우리가 너무 엄숙해지지는 말기로 해요."], ["He has encouragement enough to proceed, without our sighing out our souls over this charade.”", "우리가 이 셔레이드를 두고 영혼까지 한숨으로 쏟아 내지 않더라도, 그분이 다음 단계로 나아갈 격려는 충분하니까요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아아!"], ["no—I hope I shall not be ridiculous about it.", "그래요 — 이 일로 제가 우스워지는 일은 없었으면 좋겠어요."], ["Do as you please.”", "좋으실 대로 하세요.\""]], [["Mr.", "미스터 우드하우스가 들어와, 그가 아주 자주 던지는 물음을 또 꺼내며 곧장 그 화제로 이끌었다."], ["Woodhouse came in, and very soon led to the subject again, by the recurrence of his very frequent inquiry of “Well, my dears, how does your book go on?—Have you got any thing fresh?”", "\"그래, 얘들아, 너희 공책은 잘되어 가니? — 뭐 새것이라도 좀 생겼고?\""]], [["“Yes, papa; we have something to read you, something quite fresh.", "\"네, 아버지. 읽어 드릴 게 있어요, 아주 새것이에요."], ["A piece of paper was found on the table this morning—(dropt, we suppose, by a fairy)—containing a very pretty charade, and we have just copied it in.”", "오늘 아침에 탁자 위에서 종이 한 장이 발견됐는데 — (요정이 떨어뜨리고 간 거겠죠) — 아주 어여쁜 셔레이드가 적혀 있어서, 방금 베껴 넣었어요.\""]], [["She read it to him, just as he liked to have any thing read, slowly and distinctly, and two or three times over, with explanations of every part as she proceeded—and he was very much pleased, and, as she had foreseen, especially struck with the complimentary conclusion.", "그녀는 아버지가 무엇이든 읽어 주는 걸 좋아하는 그대로, 천천히 또박또박, 두세 번씩 거듭 읽어 드렸고, 읽어 나가면서 구절마다 풀이도 곁들였다. 그러자 아버지는 무척 기뻐했고, 그녀가 미리 내다본 대로, 특히 그 찬사로 맺는 마무리에 깊이 감동했다."]], [["“Aye, that’s very just, indeed, that’s very properly said.", "\"옳거니, 정말 지당하구나, 정말 제대로 한 말이야. 참으로 옳은 말이지."], ["Very true.", "'여인이, 사랑스러운 여인이'라."], ["‘Woman, lovely woman.’ It is such a pretty charade, my dear, that I can easily guess what fairy brought it.—Nobody could have written so prettily, but you, Emma.”", "얘야, 어찌나 어여쁜 셔레이드인지, 어떤 요정이 가져왔는지 쉬이 짐작이 가는구나. — 너 말고는 누구도 이렇게 어여쁘게 쓸 수 없을 게다, 엠마.\""]], [["Emma only nodded, and smiled.—After a little thinking, and a very tender sigh, he added,", "엠마는 그저 고개를 끄덕이며 미소 지었다. 잠깐 생각에 잠겼다가, 자못 다정한 한숨을 내쉬고는 아버지가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아아!"], ["it is no difficulty to see who you take after!", "네가 누구를 닮았는지는 보면 알지!"], ["Your dear mother was so clever at all those things!", "네 어머니는 그런 일에 어찌나 영리했는지!"], ["If I had but her memory!", "나한테도 그이만 한 기억력이 있다면 좋으련만!"], ["But I can remember nothing;—not even that particular riddle which you have heard me mention; I can only recollect the first stanza; and there are several.", "하지만 나는 도통 아무것도 기억나질 않아 — 너한테 말한 적 있는 그 수수께끼조차도 말이다. 첫 연밖에 기억나지 않는구나, 여러 연이나 되는데도."]], [["Kitty, a fair but frozen maid, Kindled a flame I yet deplore, The hood-wink’d boy I called to aid, Though of his near approach afraid, So fatal to my suit before.", "키티, 곱지만 얼어붙은 처녀,\n    아직도 한스러운 불길을 지폈네. 눈 가린 그 소년을 나는 도움 청해 불렀으나,\n가까이 다가오는 그가 두려웠으니,\n    전엔 내 구애에 그토록 치명적이었던 까닭에."]], [["And that is all that I can recollect of it—but it is very clever all the way through.", "내가 기억하는 건 그게 다란다 — 하지만 처음부터 끝까지 아주 영리한 거야."], ["But I think, my dear, you said you had got it.”", "그런데 얘야, 네가 그걸 가졌다고 했던 것 같구나.\""]], [["“Yes, papa, it is written out in our second page.", "\"네, 아버지. 우리 공책 두 번째 쪽에 적혀 있어요."], ["We copied it from the Elegant Extracts.", "『우아한 발췌집』에서 베껴 왔거든요."], ["It was Garrick’s, you know.”", "개릭의 작품이잖아요.\""]], [["“Aye, very true.—I wish I could recollect more of it.", "\"옳지, 정말 그렇지. — 더 기억해 낼 수 있으면 좋으련만."]], [["Kitty, a fair but frozen maid.", "키티, 곱지만 얼어붙은 처녀."]], [["The name makes me think of poor Isabella; for she was very near being christened Catherine after her grandmama.", "그 이름을 들으니 가엾은 이저벨라가 생각나는구나. 그 애가 하마터면 외할머니를 따라 캐서린이라고 세례명을 받을 뻔했으니 말이다."], ["I hope we shall have her here next week.", "다음 주에는 그 애가 여기 와 주면 좋겠는데."], ["Have you thought, my dear, where you shall put her—and what room there will be for the children?”", "얘야, 그 애를 어디에 들일지 생각해 두었니 — 아이들이 쓸 방은 어떻게 되고?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아유!"], ["yes—she will have her own room, of course; the room she always has;—and there is the nursery for the children,—just as usual, you know.", "그럼요 — 언니야 물론 자기 방을 쓰겠죠. 늘 쓰던 그 방요."], ["Why should there be any change?”", "그리고 아이들을 위해선 육아실이 있고요 — 늘 그래 왔듯이요, 아시잖아요. 뭘 바꿀 까닭이 있겠어요?\""]], [["“I do not know, my dear—but it is so long since she was here!—not since last Easter, and then only for a few days.—Mr. John Knightley’s being a lawyer is very inconvenient.—Poor Isabella!—she is sadly taken away from us all!—and how sorry she will be when she comes, not to see Miss Taylor here!”", "\"글쎄다, 얘야 — 하지만 그 애가 여기 안 온 지 하도 오래라서! — 지난 부활절 이후로 한 번도, 그것도 겨우 며칠뿐이었잖니. — 미스터 존 나이틀리가 변호사라는 게 참 불편한 노릇이야. — 가엾은 이저벨라! — 우리 모두에게서 그렇게 떨어져 살다니! — 그리고 와서 미스 테일러가 여기 없는 걸 보면 얼마나 서운해할꼬!\""]], [["“She will not be surprized, papa, at least.”", "\"적어도 놀라지는 않을 거예요, 아버지.\""]], [["“I do not know, my dear.", "\"글쎄다, 얘야."], ["I am sure I was very much surprized when I first heard she was going to be married.”", "나야 그 애가 결혼한다는 말을 처음 들었을 때 정말이지 무척 놀랐단다.\""]], [["“We must ask Mr. and Mrs. Weston to dine with us, while Isabella is here.”", "\"언니가 여기 있는 동안, 미스터 웨스턴 부부를 초대해서 함께 식사하면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Yes, my dear, if there is time.—But—(in a very depressed tone)—she is coming for only one week.", "\"그래, 얘야, 시간이 되거든 그러려무나. — 하지만 — (자못 풀 죽은 목소리로) — 그 애는 겨우 한 주만 오는 거잖니."], ["There will not be time for any thing.”", "뭘 할 시간이 없겠구나.\""]], [["“It is unfortunate that they cannot stay longer—but it seems a case of necessity.", "\"더 오래 머물 수 없다니 안타까운 일이에요 — 하지만 어쩔 수 없는 사정인가 봐요. 미스터 존 나이틀리가 28일에는 다시 시내에 가 있어야 하니까요."], ["Mr.", "그래도 아버지, 우리는 고맙게 여겨야 해요."], ["John Knightley must be in town again on the 28th, and we ought to be thankful, papa, that we are to have the whole of the time they can give to the country, that two or three days are not to be taken out for the Abbey.", "그 부부가 시골에 내어 줄 수 있는 시간 전부를 우리가 차지하게 됐고, 그중 이삼 일을 애비에 떼어 주지 않아도 되니까요."], ["Mr. Knightley promises to give up his claim this Christmas—though you know it is longer since they were with him, than with us.”", "미스터 나이틀리가 이번 성탄절엔 자기 몫을 양보하겠다고 약속했거든요 — 따지고 보면 그 부부가 우리 집보다 그분 집에 안 간 지가 더 오래됐는데도 말이에요.\""]], [["“It would be very hard, indeed, my dear, if poor Isabella were to be anywhere but at Hartfield.”", "\"가엾은 이저벨라가 하트필드 아닌 다른 데 있어야 한다면, 그건 정말이지 너무 가혹한 일이지, 얘야.\""]], [["Mr. Woodhouse could never allow for Mr.", "미스터 우드하우스는 형으로서 동생에게 가지는 미스터 나이틀리의 권리도, 자기 말고 다른 누가 이저벨라에게 가지는 권리도, 도무지 인정하지 못했다."], ["Knightley’s claims on his brother, or any body’s claims on Isabella, except his own. He sat musing a little while, and then said,", "그는 잠시 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있다가 말했다."]], [["“But I do not see why poor Isabella should be obliged to go back so soon, though he does.", "\"하지만 그 사람이 그래야 한다고 해서, 가엾은 이저벨라까지 그렇게 빨리 돌아가야 할 까닭은 모르겠구나."], ["I think, Emma, I shall try and persuade her to stay longer with us.", "엠마야, 내가 한번 그 애더러 우리하고 좀 더 머물자고 설득해 봐야겠다."], ["She and the children might stay very well.”", "그 애하고 아이들이야 얼마든지 더 있어도 될 텐데.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아아!"], ["papa—that is what you never have been able to accomplish, and I do not think you ever will.", "아버지 — 그건 한 번도 해내신 적 없는 일이고, 앞으로도 못 하시리라고 봐요."], ["Isabella cannot bear to stay behind her husband.”", "언니는 남편을 두고 뒤처져 머무르는 걸 견디지 못하거든요.\""]], [["This was too true for contradiction. Unwelcome as it was, Mr.", "이는 반박하기엔 너무도 맞는 말이었다."], ["Woodhouse could only give a submissive sigh; and as Emma saw his spirits affected by the idea of his daughter’s attachment to her husband, she immediately led to such a branch of the subject as must raise them.", "달갑지 않은 말이긴 했어도, 미스터 우드하우스는 그저 체념의 한숨을 내쉴 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 딸이 남편에게 그토록 매여 있다는 생각에 아버지의 기운이 꺾이는 것을 본 엠마는, 곧장 그 기운을 북돋울 만한 화제의 갈래로 이끌었다."]], [["“Harriet must give us as much of her company as she can while my brother and sister are here.", "\"오라버니 부부가 여기 와 있는 동안, 해리엇이 되도록 자주 함께해 주어야 해요."], ["I am sure she will be pleased with the children.", "분명 아이들을 좋아하게 될 거예요."], ["We are very proud of the children, are not we, papa?", "우리는 그 아이들이 무척 자랑스럽잖아요, 그렇죠, 아버지?"], ["I wonder which she will think the handsomest, Henry or John?”", "헨리하고 존 중에 누가 더 잘생겼다고 할지 궁금해요.\""]], [["“Aye, I wonder which she will.", "\"옳지, 누구라고 할지 나도 궁금하구나."], ["Poor little dears, how glad they will be to come.", "가여운 것들, 오게 되면 얼마나 좋아할꼬."], ["They are very fond of being at Hartfield, Harriet.”", "그 애들은 하트필드에 오는 걸 무척 좋아한단다, 해리엇.\""]], [["“I dare say they are, sir.", "\"분명 그러실 거예요, 선생님."], ["I am sure I do not know who is not.”", "하트필드를 안 좋아하는 사람이 누가 있겠어요.\""]], [["“Henry is a fine boy, but John is very like his mama.", "\"헨리는 잘생긴 사내아이지만, 존은 제 엄마를 꼭 닮았지."], ["Henry is the eldest, he was named after me, not after his father.", "헨리가 맏이인데, 제 아버지가 아니라 나를 따라 이름을 지었단다."], ["John, the second, is named after his father.", "둘째인 존이 제 아버지 이름을 땄고."], ["Some people are surprized, I believe, that the eldest was not, but Isabella would have him called Henry, which I thought very pretty of her.", "맏이가 아버지 이름을 안 따른 걸 의아해하는 사람도 있는가 본데, 이저벨라가 굳이 헨리라고 부르고 싶어 했으니, 나는 그게 참 어여쁜 마음이라고 생각했지. 게다가 정말이지 아주 영리한 아이야."], ["And he is a very clever boy, indeed.", "다들 놀랍도록 영리하단다. 그리고 어찌나 어여쁜 짓을 많이 하는지."], ["They are all remarkably clever; and they have so many pretty ways.", "내 의자 옆에 와 서서는 '할아버지, 끈 한 가닥만 주실래요?'"], ["They will come and stand by my chair, and say, ‘Grandpapa, can you give me a bit of string?’ and once Henry asked me for a knife, but I told him knives were only made for grandpapas.", "하고 묻는단다. 한번은 헨리가 칼을 달라고 해서, 칼은 할아버지들이나 쓰라고 만든 거라고 일러 주었지."], ["I think their father is too rough with them very often.”", "그 애들 아비는 너무 거칠게 대할 때가 많은 것 같아.\""]], [["“He appears rough to you,”", "\"아버지께 거칠어 보이는 거예요.\" 엠마가 말했다."], ["said Emma, “because you are so very gentle yourself; but if you could compare him with other papas, you would not think him rough.", "\"아버지가 워낙 더없이 다정하신 분이니까요. 하지만 다른 아버지들하고 견주어 보시면, 거칠다고는 생각지 않으실 거예요."], ["He wishes his boys to be active and hardy; and if they misbehave, can give them a sharp word now and then; but he is an affectionate father—certainly Mr.", "그분은 아들들이 활달하고 다부지게 자라기를 바라시는 거예요. 그래서 애들이 잘못하면 이따금 따끔하게 한마디 하시는 거고요."], ["John Knightley is an affectionate father.", "하지만 다정한 아버지세요 — 미스터 존 나이틀리는 분명 다정한 아버지예요."], ["The children are all fond of him.”", "아이들도 다 그분을 따르고요.\""]], [["“And then their uncle comes in, and tosses them up to the ceiling in a very frightful way!”", "\"그러고 나면 또 외삼촌이 들어와서, 그 애들을 천장까지 막 던져 올린다니까, 어찌나 무시무시한지!\""]], [["“But they like it, papa; there is nothing they like so much.", "\"하지만 애들은 그걸 좋아해요, 아버지. 그보다 좋아하는 게 없는걸요."], ["It is such enjoyment to them, that if their uncle did not lay down the rule of their taking turns, whichever began would never give way to the other.”", "그게 어찌나 신나는지, 외삼촌이 번갈아 하라는 규칙을 정해 두지 않으면, 먼저 시작한 애가 다른 애한테 절대 양보하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Well, I cannot understand it.”", "\"글쎄, 나는 도무지 모르겠구나.\""]], [["“That is the case with us all, papa.", "\"우리 모두가 다 그런 거예요, 아버지."], ["One half of the world cannot understand the pleasures of the other.”", "세상의 절반은 나머지 절반의 즐거움을 이해하지 못하는 법이니까요.\""]], [["Later in the morning, and just as the girls were going to separate in preparation for the regular four o’clock dinner, the hero of this inimitable charade walked in again.", "아침나절도 한참 지나, 두 아가씨가 으레 네 시에 드는 식사를 채비하러 막 헤어지려던 참에, 이 견줄 데 없는 셔레이드의 주인공이 다시 들어섰다. 해리엇은 고개를 돌렸다."], ["Harriet turned away; but Emma could receive him with the usual smile, and her quick eye soon discerned in his the consciousness of having made a push—of having thrown a die; and she imagined he was come to see how it might turn up.", "그러나 엠마는 여느 때의 미소로 그를 맞을 수 있었고, 그녀의 날카로운 눈은 곧 그의 눈에서 한 수를 던졌다는 — 주사위를 굴렸다는 자의식을 알아챘다. 그리고 그가 그것이 어떻게 나오는지 보러 왔으리라고 짐작했다."], ["His ostensible reason, however, was to ask whether Mr. Woodhouse’s party could be made up in the evening without him, or whether he should be in the smallest degree necessary at Hartfield.", "그러나 그의 표면적인 명분은, 오늘 저녁 미스터 우드하우스의 카드놀이 모임이 자기 없이도 꾸려질 수 있을지, 아니면 자기가 하트필드에 조금이라도 꼭 있어야 할지를 묻는 것이었다."], ["If he were, every thing else must give way; but otherwise his friend Cole had been saying so much about his dining with him—had made such a point of it, that he had promised him conditionally to come.", "만일 그래야 한다면 다른 일은 다 제쳐 두겠지만, 그렇지 않다면 그의 벗 미스터 콜이 함께 식사하자고 어찌나 거듭 청하던지 — 어찌나 그것에 못을 박던지, 사정이 되면 가겠노라고 그가 조건부로 약속해 둔 터였다."]], [["Emma thanked him, but could not allow of his disappointing his friend on their account; her father was sure of his rubber. He re-urged—she re-declined; and he seemed then about to make his bow, when taking the paper from the table, she returned it—", "엠마는 고마워하면서도, 자기들 때문에 그가 벗을 실망시키는 일은 허락할 수 없다고 했다. 아버지의 휘스트 한판은 틀림없이 꾸려질 거라고 했다. 그가 다시 청했고 — 그녀가 다시 사양했다. 그러고 나서 그가 막 인사하고 물러가려는 듯하자, 그녀는 탁자에서 그 종이를 집어 그에게 돌려주었다. * 휘스트(whist): 네 사람이 둘씩 짝을 지어 하는 카드놀이. 한 판을 러버(rubber)라 한다."]], [["“Oh! here is the charade you were so obliging as to leave with us; thank you for the sight of it.", "\"아, 여기 친절하게도 우리에게 남겨 주신 그 셔레이드요."], ["We admired it so much, that I have ventured to write it into Miss Smith’s collection.", "보여 주셔서 고마워요. 우리가 어찌나 감탄했던지, 제가 외람되게도 미스 스미스의 수집첩에 적어 넣었어요."], ["Your friend will not take it amiss I hope.", "당신 벗께서 언짢게 여기지 않으셨으면 좋겠네요."], ["Of course I have not transcribed beyond the first eight lines.”", "물론 앞의 여덟 줄까지밖에 베끼지 않았고요.\""]], [["Mr. Elton certainly did not very well know what to say.", "미스터 엘턴은 정녕 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐다."], ["He looked rather doubtingly—rather confused; said something about “honour,”—glanced at Emma and at Harriet, and then seeing the book open on the table, took it up, and examined it very attentively.", "그는 자못 미심쩍은 듯, 자못 당황한 얼굴로 \"영광\"이라는 둥 무어라 중얼거리더니, 엠마와 해리엇을 번갈아 흘긋 보았다. 그러다 탁자 위에 공책이 펼쳐져 있는 것을 보고는, 그것을 집어 들어 아주 유심히 살펴보았다."], ["With the view of passing off an awkward moment, Emma smilingly said,", "어색한 순간을 넘기려는 뜻으로, 엠마가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“You must make my apologies to your friend; but so good a charade must not be confined to one or two.", "\"당신 벗께 제 대신 사과를 전해 주세요. 하지만 이렇게 훌륭한 셔레이드를 한두 사람한테만 가두어 둘 수야 없잖아요."], ["He may be sure of every woman’s approbation while he writes with such gallantry.”", "그분이 이토록 정중하게 쓰시는 한, 어느 여성에게서든 칭찬받으리라는 건 분명한 일이에요.\""]], [["“I have no hesitation in saying,” replied Mr.", "\"저는 서슴없이 말씀드릴 수 있습니다.\""], ["Elton, though hesitating a good deal while he spoke; “I have no hesitation in saying—at least if my friend feels at all as I do—I have not the smallest doubt that, could he see his little effusion honoured as I see it, (looking at the book again, and replacing it on the table), he would consider it as the proudest moment of his life.”", "미스터 엘턴이, 말하는 내내 꽤나 머뭇거리면서도 대답했다. \"서슴없이 말씀드리건대 — 적어도 제 벗이 저와 조금이라도 같은 심정이라면 말이지요 — 그가 만일 자신의 그 자그마한 시구가 제가 보는 것처럼 이렇게 영예롭게 대접받는 걸 본다면 (다시 공책을 보고는, 그것을 탁자에 도로 내려놓으며), 분명 자기 생애 가장 자랑스러운 순간이라 여길 거라는 데에 추호의 의심도 없습니다.\""]], [["After this speech he was gone as soon as possible.", "이 말을 마치고 그는 되도록 서둘러 떠났다."], ["Emma could not think it too soon; for with all his good and agreeable qualities, there was a sort of parade in his speeches which was very apt to incline her to laugh.", "엠마로서는 너무 이르다고 여길 수가 없었다. 그의 온갖 어질고 호감 가는 자질에도 불구하고, 그의 말투에는 일종의 과장된 허세가 있어 그녀를 자꾸만 웃음 짓게 만들기 십상이었던 것이다."], ["She ran away to indulge the inclination, leaving the tender and the sublime of pleasure to Harriet’s share.", "그녀는 그 충동을 마음껏 풀어내려고 달아났고, 다정하고 숭고한 기쁨의 몫일랑 해리엇에게 남겨 두었다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter X", "# 제1권 10장"]], [["Though now the middle of December, there had yet been no weather to prevent the young ladies from tolerably regular exercise; and on the morrow, Emma had a charitable visit to pay to a poor sick family, who lived a little way out of Highbury.", "이제 십이월 중순이었지만, 아직 두 아가씨가 제법 규칙적인 산책을 못 할 만한 날씨는 없었다. 다음 날, 엠마는 하이베리에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 사는 가난한 병자 가족에게 자선의 방문을 가야 할 일이 있었다."]], [["Their road to this detached cottage was down Vicarage Lane, a lane leading at right angles from the broad, though irregular, main street of the place; and, as may be inferred, containing the blessed abode of Mr.", "이 외딴 오두막으로 가는 길은 비커리지 골목을 따라 나 있었으니, 이 골목은 들쭉날쭉하긴 해도 넓은 마을 큰길에서 직각으로 갈라져 뻗은 길이었고, 짐작할 수 있듯 미스터 엘턴의 복된 거처가 자리한 곳이었다. 먼저 변변찮은 집 몇 채를 지나고 나면, 골목을 사분의 일 마일쯤 내려간 곳에 목사관이 솟아 있었다."], ["Elton.", "낡고 그리 좋다 할 수 없는 집으로, 더는 가까울 수 없을 만큼 길에 바싹 붙어 있었다."], ["A few inferior dwellings were first to be passed, and then, about a quarter of a mile down the lane rose the Vicarage, an old and not very good house, almost as close to the road as it could be.", "위치의 이점이라곤 없었지만, 지금 주인이 무척 말끔하게 단장해 놓은 집이었다. 그런 집인지라, 두 벗이 걸음을 늦추고 눈여겨보지 않은 채 그 앞을 지나칠 도리는 없었다."], ["It had no advantage of situation; but had been very much smartened up by the present proprietor; and, such as it was, there could be no possibility of the two friends passing it without a slackened pace and observing eyes.—Emma’s remark was—", "엠마의 말은 이러했다."]], [["“There it is.", "\"저기예요."], ["There go you and your riddle-book one of these days.”—Harriet’s was—", "언젠가는 당신도 그 수수께끼 시집을 들고 저리로 들어가게 될 거예요.\" 해리엇의 말은 이러했다."]], [["“Oh, what a sweet house!—How very beautiful!—There are the yellow curtains that Miss Nash admires so much.”", "\"어머, 정말 아름다운 집이에요! 어쩜 저렇게 예쁠까요! 미스 내시가 그렇게 칭찬하던 노란 커튼이 저기 있네요.\""]], [["“I do not often walk this way now,”", "\"요즘은 이 길로 잘 안 다녀요.\" 두 사람이 걸음을 옮기며 엠마가 말했다."], ["said Emma, as they proceeded, “but then there will be an inducement, and I shall gradually get intimately acquainted with all the hedges, gates, pools and pollards of this part of Highbury.”", "\"하지만 머잖아 끌릴 만한 핑계가 생기겠죠. 그러면 하이베리 이 일대의 울타리며 대문이며 못이며 가지치기한 나무까지 죄다 차차 속속들이 알게 될 거예요.\""]], [["Harriet, she found, had never in her life been inside the Vicarage, and her curiosity to see it was so extreme, that, considering exteriors and probabilities, Emma could only class it, as a proof of love, with Mr. Elton’s seeing ready wit in her.", "알고 보니 해리엇은 평생 한 번도 목사관 안에 들어가 본 적이 없었고, 그 안을 보고 싶어 하는 호기심이 어찌나 대단한지, 외관과 여러 가능성을 따져 보건대 엠마로서는 그것을 사랑의 증거로밖에 분류할 수 없었으니, 미스터 엘턴이 자기에게서 빈틈없는 기지를 보아낸 것과 같은 부류였다."]], [["“I wish we could contrive it,”", "\"어떻게든 들어갈 수 있으면 좋으련만.\" 엠마가 말했다."], ["said she; “but I cannot think of any tolerable pretence for going in;—no servant that I want to inquire about of his housekeeper—no message from my father.”", "\"그런데 들어갈 만한 그럴듯한 핑계가 도무지 떠오르질 않네요. 그 집 가정부에게 물어볼 하인 자리도 없고, 아버지가 전하라는 말씀도 없으니.\""]], [["She pondered, but could think of nothing.", "엠마는 곰곰이 궁리했지만 아무것도 떠오르지 않았다."], ["After a mutual silence of some minutes, Harriet thus began again—", "둘 다 몇 분쯤 말이 없다가, 해리엇이 다시 이렇게 입을 열었다."]], [["“I do so wonder, Miss Woodhouse, that you should not be married, or going to be married!", "\"미스 우드하우스, 저는 정말 이상해요. 어떻게 미스 우드하우스가 아직 결혼도 안 하고, 결혼할 생각도 없으신지!"], ["so charming as you are!”—", "그렇게 사랑스러우신데!\""]], [["Emma laughed, and replied,", "엠마는 웃으며 대답했다."]], [["“My being charming, Harriet, is not quite enough to induce me to marry; I must find other people charming—one other person at least.", "\"내가 사랑스럽다는 것만으로는, 해리엇, 결혼할 마음이 들기에 영 부족해요. 나도 다른 사람을 사랑스럽게 여겨야죠 — 적어도 단 한 사람은요."], ["And I am not only, not going to be married, at present, but have very little intention of ever marrying at all.”", "그런데 나는 지금 결혼할 생각이 없을 뿐 아니라, 평생 결혼할 마음이 거의 없답니다.\""]], [["“Ah!—so you say; but I cannot believe it.”", "\"어머! 그렇게 말씀하셔도, 저는 믿을 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“I must see somebody very superior to any one I have seen yet, to be tempted; Mr.", "\"마음이 동하려면, 지금껏 본 누구보다 훨씬 뛰어난 사람을 만나야 할 거예요. 미스터 엘턴은, 알다시피,\""], ["Elton, you know, (recollecting herself,) is out of the question: and I do not wish to see any such person.", "(제정신을 차리며) \"논외고요. 그리고 나는 그런 사람을 만나고 싶지도 않아요."], ["I would rather not be tempted.", "차라리 마음이 동하지 않는 편이 나아요."], ["I cannot really change for the better.", "나는 정말이지 지금보다 나아질 수가 없거든요."], ["If I were to marry, I must expect to repent it.”", "결혼이라도 했다간 후회할 게 뻔하죠.\""]], [["“Dear me!—it is so odd to hear a woman talk so!”—", "\"세상에! 여자가 그런 말을 하는 걸 들으니 어찌나 별난지!\""]], [["“I have none of the usual inducements of women to marry.", "\"나에겐 여자들이 흔히 결혼하는 까닭이 하나도 없어요. 사랑에 빠진다면야, 정말이지 이야기가 다르겠죠!"], ["Were I to fall in love, indeed, it would be a different thing!", "하지만 나는 한 번도 사랑에 빠져 본 적이 없어요. 그건 내 방식도, 내 천성도 아니에요."], ["but I never have been in love; it is not my way, or my nature; and I do not think I ever shall.", "앞으로도 그럴 일은 없을 것 같고요. 그리고 사랑도 없이 지금 같은 처지를 바꾼다면, 분명 나는 바보일 거예요."], ["And, without love, I am sure I should be a fool to change such a situation as mine.", "재산도 아쉽지 않고, 할 일도 아쉽지 않고, 지위도 아쉽지 않아요."], ["Fortune I do not want; employment I do not want; consequence I do not want: I believe few married women are half as much mistress of their husband’s house as I am of Hartfield; and never, never could I expect to be so truly beloved and important; so always first and always right in any man’s eyes as I am in my father’s.”", "내가 하트필드를 다스리는 것의 절반만큼이라도 제 남편 집의 안주인 노릇을 하는 결혼한 여자는 드물 거예요. 그리고 우리 아버지 눈에 비치는 만큼 이토록 진심으로 사랑받고 소중히 여겨지는 일, 어떤 남자 눈에든 늘 첫째이고 늘 옳은 사람으로 비치는 일은, 결코, 결코 다시 바랄 수 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“But then, to be an old maid at last, like Miss Bates!”", "\"하지만 그러다 결국 미스 베이츠처럼 노처녀로 늙으시면요!\""]], [["“That is as formidable an image as you could present, Harriet; and if I thought I should ever be like Miss Bates!", "\"그건 당신이 들이밀 수 있는 가장 무시무시한 그림이네요, 해리엇. 내가 미스 베이츠처럼 될 것 같다는 생각이 든다면!"], ["so silly—so satisfied—so smiling—so prosing—so undistinguishing and unfastidious—and so apt to tell every thing relative to every body about me, I would marry to-morrow.", "그렇게 어리석고 — 그렇게 만족하고 — 그렇게 헤프게 웃고 — 그렇게 지루하게 늘어놓고 — 그렇게 가릴 줄도 까다로울 줄도 모르고 — 주변 사람 누구에 관한 일이든 죄다 떠벌리는 사람이 될 것 같다면, 나는 당장 내일이라도 결혼할 거예요."], ["But between us, I am convinced there never can be any likeness, except in being unmarried.”", "하지만 우리끼리 말인데, 결혼 안 한 것 말고는 닮을 구석이라곤 절대 있을 수 없다고 확신해요.\""]], [["“But still, you will be an old maid!", "\"그래도 어쨌든 노처녀가 되시는 거잖아요!"], ["and that’s so dreadful!”", "그건 너무 끔찍해요!\""]], [["“Never mind, Harriet, I shall not be a poor old maid; and it is poverty only which makes celibacy contemptible to a generous public! A single woman, with a very narrow income, must be a ridiculous, disagreeable old maid! the proper sport of boys and girls, but a single woman, of good fortune, is always respectable, and may be as sensible and pleasant as any body else. And the distinction is not quite so much against the candour and common sense of the world as appears at first; for a very narrow income has a tendency to contract the mind, and sour the temper. Those who can barely live, and who live perforce in a very small, and generally very inferior, society, may well be illiberal and cross. This does not apply, however, to Miss Bates; she is only too good natured and too silly to suit me; but, in general, she is very much to the taste of every body, though single and though poor. Poverty certainly has not contracted her mind: I really believe, if she had only a shilling in the world, she would be very likely to give away sixpence of it; and nobody is afraid of her: that is a great charm.”", "\"걱정 말아요, 해리엇, 나는 가난한 노처녀는 되지 않을 테니까요. 너그러운 세상 사람들 눈에 독신을 경멸스럽게 만드는 건 오직 가난뿐이거든요! 수입이 아주 빠듯한 독신 여자는 우스꽝스럽고 못마땅한 노처녀가 되기 마련이죠! 사내아이 계집아이 들이 놀려 먹기 딱 좋은 감이고요. 하지만 재산이 넉넉한 독신 여자라면 언제나 떳떳하고, 다른 누구 못지않게 분별 있고 유쾌할 수 있어요. 그리고 이 차이가 세상의 공정함과 양식을 거스르는 것이 처음 보이는 것만큼 심하지는 않아요. 수입이 아주 빠듯하면 마음이 옹졸해지고 성미가 비뚤어지기 십상이거든요. 겨우 입에 풀칠하는 사람들, 그것도 어쩔 수 없이 아주 좁고 대개 아주 못한 사람들 틈에서 사는 이들이라면, 인색하고 까다로워지는 게 당연하죠. 하지만 이건 미스 베이츠에겐 해당이 안 돼요. 그분은 그저 너무 사람이 좋고 너무 어수룩해서 내 취향에 안 맞을 뿐이에요. 그래도 결혼도 안 하고 가난한데도, 대체로 누구에게나 무척 사랑받는 분이죠. 가난이 그분 마음을 옹졸하게 만들지는 분명 못했어요. 정말이지, 그분은 세상에 가진 게 일 실링뿐이라도, 그중 육 펜스는 선뜻 남에게 내줄 분이거든요. 그리고 아무도 그분을 무서워하지 않죠. 그게 큰 매력이에요.\""]], [["“Dear me!", "\"세상에!"], ["but what shall you do?", "그렇지만 그럼 어떻게 하시려고요?"], ["how shall you employ yourself when you grow old?”", "나이 들면 무얼 하며 지내시려고요?\""]], [["“If I know myself, Harriet, mine is an active, busy mind, with a great many independent resources; and I do not perceive why I should be more in want of employment at forty or fifty than one-and-twenty.", "\"내가 나를 안다면, 해리엇, 나는 활동적이고 분주한 사람이라 혼자서도 즐길 거리가 무척 많아요. 그러니 마흔이나 쉰에, 스물한 살 때보다 더 할 일이 아쉬울 까닭이 없을 것 같아요."], ["Woman’s usual occupations of hand and mind will be as open to me then as they are now; or with no important variation.", "여자가 손과 머리로 흔히 하는 일들은 그때도 지금과 다름없이 내게 열려 있을 테니까요. 큰 변화 없이요."], ["If I draw less, I shall read more; if I give up music, I shall take to carpet-work.", "그림을 덜 그리면 책을 더 읽고, 음악을 그만두면 양탄자 짜기를 시작하겠죠."], ["And as for objects of interest, objects for the affections, which is in truth the great point of inferiority, the want of which is really the great evil to be avoided in not marrying, I shall be very well off, with all the children of a sister I love so much, to care about.", "그리고 관심을 쏟을 대상, 애정을 쏟을 대상으로 말하자면 — 사실 그게 가장 부족한 점이고, 결혼하지 않을 때 정말로 피해야 할 큰 화근이 바로 그것의 결핍이지만 — 그 점에서도 나는 아주 넉넉할 거예요. 내가 그토록 사랑하는 언니의 아이들이 다 있고, 그 아이들을 보살필 수 있으니까요."], ["There will be enough of them, in all probability, to supply every sort of sensation that declining life can need.", "십중팔구 그 아이들만으로도 기우는 인생이 필요로 하는 온갖 감정을 채우고 남을 거예요."], ["There will be enough for every hope and every fear; and though my attachment to none can equal that of a parent, it suits my ideas of comfort better than what is warmer and blinder.", "온갖 희망과 온갖 두려움을 채우기에 충분할 테고요. 그 아이들 누구에게 쏟는 애정도 부모의 그것에는 못 미치겠지만, 그래도 더 뜨겁고 더 맹목적인 것보다는 내 안락에 대한 생각에 한결 잘 맞아요."], ["My nephews and nieces!—I shall often have a niece with me.”", "내 조카들이요! 나는 곧잘 조카딸을 곁에 두게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“Do you know Miss Bates’s niece?", "\"미스 베이츠의 조카딸을 아세요?"], ["That is, I know you must have seen her a hundred times—but are you acquainted?”", "그러니까, 분명 백 번은 보셨겠지만 — 아는 사이세요?\""]], [["“Oh! yes; we are always forced to be acquainted whenever she comes to Highbury.", "\"아, 그럼요."], ["By the bye, that is almost enough to put one out of conceit with a niece.", "그 애가 하이베리에 올 때마다 우리는 늘 어쩔 수 없이 아는 사이가 되죠. 그러고 보니, 조카딸이라면 정나미가 떨어질 만도 한 게 바로 그 일이에요."], ["Heaven forbid!", "천만에!"], ["at least, that I should ever bore people half so much about all the Knightleys together, as she does about Jane Fairfax.", "적어도 나는, 그 사람이 제인 페어팩스 이야기를 늘어놓는 만큼이라도, 나이틀리네 사람들 모두를 두고 사람들을 지겹게 만드는 일은 절대 없을 거예요."], ["One is sick of the very name of Jane Fairfax.", "제인 페어팩스라는 이름만 들어도 진절머리가 나요."], ["Every letter from her is read forty times over; her compliments to all friends go round and round again; and if she does but send her aunt the pattern of a stomacher, or knit a pair of garters for her grandmother, one hears of nothing else for a month.", "그 애한테서 온 편지는 한 통 한 통 마흔 번씩 읽어 대고, 친구들 모두에게 안부 전한 것은 돌고 돌아 되풀이되죠. 그 애가 고모한테 가슴받이 본 하나를 보내거나 할머니한테 양말대님 한 켤레를 떠 보내기라도 하면, 한 달 내내 그 이야기밖에 안 들려요."], ["I wish Jane Fairfax very well; but she tires me to death.”", "나는 제인 페어팩스가 잘되길 진심으로 바라지만, 그 애 이야기는 정말이지 사람을 진저리 나게 해요.\""]], [["They were now approaching the cottage, and all idle topics were superseded.", "이제 둘은 그 오두막에 다가서고 있었고, 한가한 화제는 죄다 밀려났다."], ["Emma was very compassionate; and the distresses of the poor were as sure of relief from her personal attention and kindness, her counsel and her patience, as from her purse.", "엠마는 인정이 무척 많았다. 가난한 이들의 곤경은 그녀의 지갑에서뿐 아니라, 그녀가 손수 기울이는 관심과 친절, 조언과 인내에서 어김없이 구제를 받았다."], ["She understood their ways, could allow for their ignorance and their temptations, had no romantic expectations of extraordinary virtue from those for whom education had done so little; entered into their troubles with ready sympathy, and always gave her assistance with as much intelligence as good-will.", "그녀는 그들의 생활 방식을 이해했고, 그들의 무지와 유혹을 너그러이 헤아렸으며, 교육이 거의 베풀어지지 못한 이들에게서 비범한 미덕을 바라는 낭만적 기대 따위는 없었다. 그녀는 선뜻 동정하며 그들의 고난 속으로 들어갔고, 늘 선의 못지않은 지혜로써 도움을 주었다."], ["In the present instance, it was sickness and poverty together which she came to visit; and after remaining there as long as she could give comfort or advice, she quitted the cottage with such an impression of the scene as made her say to Harriet, as they walked away,", "이번 경우는 병과 가난이 겹친 집을 찾은 것이었다. 위안이나 조언을 줄 수 있는 한 그곳에 머문 뒤, 엠마는 그 정경에서 받은 인상을 가슴에 품고 오두막을 나섰으니, 멀어져 가며 해리엇에게 이렇게 말할 정도였다."]], [["“These are the sights, Harriet, to do one good.", "\"이런 광경이야말로, 해리엇, 사람에게 약이 되는 거예요. 이런 걸 보고 나면 다른 모든 게 얼마나 하찮아 보이는지!"], ["How trifling they make every thing else appear!—I feel now as if I could think of nothing but these poor creatures all the rest of the day; and yet, who can say how soon it may all vanish from my mind?”", "지금은 하루 종일 이 가엾은 사람들 생각밖에 못 할 것 같아요. 그런데도, 이 모든 게 머릿속에서 얼마나 빨리 사라져 버릴지 누가 알겠어요?\""]], [["“Very true,”", "\"정말 맞아요.\""], ["said Harriet.", "해리엇이 말했다."], ["“Poor creatures!", "\"가엾은 사람들!"], ["one can think of nothing else.”", "다른 건 아무것도 생각할 수가 없네요.\""]], [["“And really, I do not think the impression will soon be over,”", "\"그리고 정말이지, 이 인상이 그리 쉬이 가시지는 않을 것 같아요.\""], ["said Emma, as she crossed the low hedge, and tottering footstep which ended the narrow, slippery path through the cottage garden, and brought them into the lane again.", "엠마가 오두막 정원을 가로지르는 좁고 미끄러운 길의 끝에 놓인 낮은 울타리와 비틀거릴 만한 디딤돌을 넘어, 다시 골목으로 들어서며 말했다."], ["“I do not think it will,”", "\"가시지 않을 거예요.\""], ["stopping to look once more at all the outward wretchedness of the place, and recall the still greater within.", "그러고는 걸음을 멈추고, 그곳의 온갖 겉으로 드러난 비참함을 한 번 더 바라보며, 안쪽의 한층 더한 비참함을 되새겼다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["dear, no,”", "그럼요, 절대로요.\""], ["said her companion.", "길동무가 말했다."]], [["They walked on.", "둘은 계속 걸었다."], ["The lane made a slight bend; and when that bend was passed, Mr. Elton was immediately in sight; and so near as to give Emma time only to say farther,", "골목은 살짝 굽이졌고, 그 굽이를 지나자마자 미스터 엘턴이 곧장 눈에 들어왔으니, 어찌나 가까운지 엠마는 겨우 이 말을 덧붙일 틈밖에 없었다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Harriet, here comes a very sudden trial of our stability in good thoughts.", "해리엇, 우리의 갸륵한 마음이 얼마나 단단한지 시험할 일이 아주 갑작스레 닥쳐오네요. 자,\""], ["Well, (smiling,) I hope it may be allowed that if compassion has produced exertion and relief to the sufferers, it has done all that is truly important.", "(미소 지으며) \"이렇게 보면 되지 않을까요. 동정심이 고통받는 이들에게 수고와 구제를 베풀게 했다면, 그것은 정말로 중요한 일을 다 한 거라고요."], ["If we feel for the wretched, enough to do all we can for them, the rest is empty sympathy, only distressing to ourselves.”", "우리가 비참한 이들을 가엾이 여기고, 그들을 위해 할 수 있는 일을 다할 만큼 마음을 쓴다면, 그 나머지는 공허한 동정일 뿐, 우리 자신만 괴롭힐 따름이죠.\""]], [["Harriet could just answer, “Oh!", "해리엇이 겨우 \"어머! 그럼요, 맞아요.\""], ["dear, yes,” before the gentleman joined them.", "하고 대답하자마자 그 신사가 두 사람과 합류했다."], ["The wants and sufferings of the poor family, however, were the first subject on meeting.", "그렇긴 해도, 만나서 처음 꺼낸 화제는 가난한 가족의 궁핍과 고통이었다."], ["He had been going to call on them.", "그도 마침 그 집에 들르려던 참이었다."], ["His visit he would now defer; but they had a very interesting parley about what could be done and should be done.", "이제 그 방문은 미루기로 했다. 그러나 무엇을 할 수 있고 무엇을 해야 할지를 두고 자못 흥미로운 의논이 오갔다."], ["Mr. Elton then turned back to accompany them.", "그러고서 미스터 엘턴은 발길을 돌려 두 사람을 따라나섰다."]], [["“To fall in with each other on such an errand as this,”", "\"이런 일을 하다 마주치다니.\" 엠마는 생각했다."], ["thought Emma; “to meet in a charitable scheme; this will bring a great increase of love on each side.", "\"자선의 계획에서 만나다니. 이건 양쪽 모두의 연정을 크게 더해 줄 거야."], ["I should not wonder if it were to bring on the declaration.", "이러다 고백이 터져 나온다 해도 놀랍지 않겠어."], ["It must, if I were not here.", "내가 여기 없다면 분명 그리될 텐데."], ["I wish I were anywhere else.”", "차라리 내가 딴 데 있었으면.\""]], [["Anxious to separate herself from them as far as she could, she soon afterwards took possession of a narrow footpath, a little raised on one side of the lane, leaving them together in the main road.", "두 사람에게서 되도록 멀찍이 떨어지고 싶은 마음에, 엠마는 곧 골목 한쪽으로 조금 돋워진 좁은 오솔길로 올라서고, 둘은 큰길에 함께 남겨 두었다. 그러나 그곳에 오른 지 채 이 분도 안 되어, 의지하고 흉내 내는 해리엇의 습성 탓에 그녀까지 따라 올라오고 있음을, 요컨대 곧 둘 다 자기 뒤를 따르게 되리라는 것을 깨달았다."], ["But she had not been there two minutes when she found that Harriet’s habits of dependence and imitation were bringing her up too, and that, in short, they would both be soon after her.", "이래선 안 될 일이었다. 엠마는 즉시 발을 멈추고, 반장화의 끈을 좀 고쳐 매야겠다는 핑계로 오솔길을 가득 차지하고 몸을 굽힌 채, 두 사람더러 부디 먼저 가 주면 자기는 곧 반 분 안에 따라가겠다고 청했다."], ["This would not do; she immediately stopped, under pretence of having some alteration to make in the lacing of her half-boot, and stooping down in complete occupation of the footpath, begged them to have the goodness to walk on, and she would follow in half a minute.", "둘은 시키는 대로 했다. 그리고 장화 손질을 마칠 만하다고 여겨질 무렵, 엠마는 더 지체할 수 있게 된 위안을 손에 넣었으니, 오두막에서 나온 한 아이가 분부받은 대로 단지를 들고 하트필드에서 묽은 죽을 받아 가려고 길을 나서다가 엠마를 따라잡은 것이었다."], ["They did as they were desired; and by the time she judged it reasonable to have done with her boot, she had the comfort of farther delay in her power, being overtaken by a child from the cottage, setting out, according to orders, with her pitcher, to fetch broth from Hartfield.", "이 아이 곁에서 함께 걸으며 말을 걸고 이것저것 묻는 일은 세상 더없이 자연스러운 일이었고, 만약 그때 무슨 꿍꿍이 없이 행동하고 있었다면 더없이 자연스러운 일이 되었을 터였다. 이 덕분에 다른 두 사람은 여전히 앞서갈 수 있었고, 그녀를 기다려야 할 의무도 지지 않았다."], ["To walk by the side of this child, and talk to and question her, was the most natural thing in the world, or would have been the most natural, had she been acting just then without design; and by this means the others were still able to keep ahead, without any obligation of waiting for her.", "그렇지만 엠마는 본의 아니게 그들에게 다가가고 말았다. 아이의 걸음은 빨랐고 그들의 걸음은 다소 느렸던 것이다."], ["She gained on them, however, involuntarily: the child’s pace was quick, and theirs rather slow; and she was the more concerned at it, from their being evidently in a conversation which interested them.", "게다가 두 사람이 보아하니 자기들끼리 흥미로운 대화에 빠져 있던 터라, 그녀로서는 더더욱 마음이 쓰였다. 미스터 엘턴은 활기차게 말하고 있었고, 해리엇은 무척 흐뭇한 표정으로 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["Mr. Elton was speaking with animation, Harriet listening with a very pleased attention; and Emma, having sent the child on, was beginning to think how she might draw back a little more, when they both looked around, and she was obliged to join them.", "엠마는 아이를 먼저 보내 놓고, 어떻게 하면 좀 더 뒤로 물러설 수 있을까 궁리하기 시작하던 참이었는데, 그때 두 사람이 동시에 돌아보는 바람에 어쩔 수 없이 그들과 합류해야 했다."]], [["Mr.", "미스터 엘턴은 여전히 이야기를 이어 가며, 무언가 흥미로운 시시콜콜한 대목에 빠져 있었다."], ["Elton was still talking, still engaged in some interesting detail; and Emma experienced some disappointment when she found that he was only giving his fair companion an account of the yesterday’s party at his friend Cole’s, and that she was come in herself for the Stilton cheese, the north Wiltshire, the butter, the celery, the beet-root, and all the dessert.", "그런데 알고 보니 그는 그저 자신의 어여쁜 길동무에게 어제 친구 콜의 집에서 있었던 모임 이야기를 들려주고 있을 뿐이었고, 화제에 오른 것이라곤 스틸턴 치즈, 노스 윌트셔 치즈, 버터, 셀러리, 비트, 그리고 온갖 후식이었으니, 엠마는 적잖이 실망했다."]], [["“This would soon have led to something better, of course,”", "\"이게 곧 더 나은 이야기로 이어졌을 게 분명해.\""], ["was her consoling reflection; “any thing interests between those who love; and any thing will serve as introduction to what is near the heart.", "그녀는 이렇게 자신을 달랬다. \"서로 사랑하는 사이라면 무엇이든 흥미로운 법이고, 무엇이든 마음 깊은 곳의 화제로 들어가는 실마리가 되는 법이니까."], ["If I could but have kept longer away!”", "내가 좀 더 오래 떨어져 있을 수만 있었더라면!\""]], [["They now walked on together quietly, till within view of the vicarage pales, when a sudden resolution, of at least getting Harriet into the house, made her again find something very much amiss about her boot, and fall behind to arrange it once more.", "이제 셋은 조용히 함께 걸어, 목사관 울타리가 보이는 데까지 이르렀다. 그때 적어도 해리엇만이라도 그 집 안으로 들여보내겠다는 결심이 불현듯 떠오른 엠마는, 또다시 장화에 무언가 몹시 잘못된 데가 있는 척하며 뒤로 처져 다시 한번 그것을 손보았다."], ["She then broke the lace off short, and dexterously throwing it into a ditch, was presently obliged to entreat them to stop, and acknowledged her inability to put herself to rights so as to be able to walk home in tolerable comfort.", "이번에는 끈을 짧게 뚝 끊어, 솜씨 좋게 도랑에 던져 버리고는, 곧 두 사람에게 멈춰 달라고 청하지 않을 수 없게 되어, 도저히 매무새를 바로잡아 집까지 그럭저럭 편히 걸어갈 수가 없겠다고 실토했다."]], [["“Part of my lace is gone,”", "\"끈이 한쪽 떨어져 나가 버렸어요.\""], ["said she, “and I do not know how I am to contrive.", "엠마가 말했다. \"어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠네요."], ["I really am a most troublesome companion to you both, but I hope I am not often so ill-equipped. Mr.", "정말 두 분께 더없이 성가신 길동무지만, 제가 이렇게 차림새가 엉망인 일이 그리 자주는 없으면 좋겠어요."], ["Elton, I must beg leave to stop at your house, and ask your housekeeper for a bit of ribband or string, or any thing just to keep my boot on.”", "미스터 엘턴, 댁에 좀 들러서, 가정부에게 리본이나 끈, 아니 장화를 붙들어 매 둘 만한 것이면 무엇이든 한 조각 청해도 될까요?\""]], [["Mr. Elton looked all happiness at this proposition; and nothing could exceed his alertness and attention in conducting them into his house and endeavouring to make every thing appear to advantage.", "미스터 엘턴은 이 제안에 더없이 흡족한 기색이었고, 두 사람을 자기 집으로 안내하며 무엇 하나라도 돋보이게 하려고 보이는 그 민첩함과 정성은 따를 자가 없었다."], ["The room they were taken into was the one he chiefly occupied, and looking forwards; behind it was another with which it immediately communicated; the door between them was open, and Emma passed into it with the housekeeper to receive her assistance in the most comfortable manner.", "두 사람이 안내받아 든 방은 그가 주로 쓰는 앞쪽 방이었고, 그 뒤로 곧장 통하는 또 다른 방이 있었다. 두 방 사이의 문은 열려 있었고, 엠마는 가정부와 함께 그 방으로 들어가 더없이 편안하게 도움을 받았다."], ["She was obliged to leave the door ajar as she found it; but she fully intended that Mr.", "엠마는 문을 발견했을 때 그대로 빠끔히 열어 둘 수밖에 없었다."], ["Elton should close it.", "그러나 미스터 엘턴이 그 문을 닫아 주기를 단단히 기대하고 있었다."], ["It was not closed, however, it still remained ajar; but by engaging the housekeeper in incessant conversation, she hoped to make it practicable for him to chuse his own subject in the adjoining room.", "그렇지만 문은 닫히지 않았고, 여전히 빠끔히 열린 채였다. 다만 엠마는 가정부를 쉴 새 없는 대화에 붙들어 둠으로써, 그가 옆방에서 마음껏 제 화제를 고를 수 있게 해 주기를 바랐다."], ["For ten minutes she could hear nothing but herself.", "십 분 동안 엠마의 귀에는 제 목소리밖에 들리지 않았다."], ["It could be protracted no longer.", "더는 시간을 끌 수 없었다."], ["She was then obliged to be finished, and make her appearance.", "그제야 엠마는 손질을 마치고 모습을 드러내야만 했다."]], [["The lovers were standing together at one of the windows.", "두 연인은 창가에 함께 서 있었다. 더없이 좋은 광경이었다."], ["It had a most favourable aspect; and, for half a minute, Emma felt the glory of having schemed successfully.", "그리고 반 분 동안 엠마는 계략을 멋지게 성공시킨 영광을 만끽했다. 그러나 안 될 일이었다."], ["But it would not do; he had not come to the point.", "그는 아직 본론에 이르지 못했던 것이다. 그는 더없이 상냥하고 더없이 유쾌했다."], ["He had been most agreeable, most delightful; he had told Harriet that he had seen them go by, and had purposely followed them; other little gallantries and allusions had been dropt, but nothing serious.", "그는 해리엇에게, 두 사람이 지나가는 것을 보고 일부러 뒤따라왔노라 말했다. 그 밖에도 자잘한 정중함과 넌지시 던지는 말들이 오갔으나, 진지한 것은 하나도 없었다."]], [["“Cautious, very cautious,”", "\"조심스럽기도 해라, 정말 조심스러워.\""], ["thought Emma; “he advances inch by inch, and will hazard nothing till he believes himself secure.”", "엠마는 생각했다. \"한 치 한 치 다가가면서, 확실하다 싶기 전엔 아무것도 걸지 않으려는 거야.\""]], [["Still, however, though every thing had not been accomplished by her ingenious device, she could not but flatter herself that it had been the occasion of much present enjoyment to both, and must be leading them forward to the great event.", "그렇지만 자신의 기발한 꾀로 모든 것이 이루어진 것은 아니었어도, 엠마는 그것이 적어도 지금 두 사람에게 큰 즐거움을 안겨 준 계기였으며, 분명 두 사람을 그 위대한 사건으로 이끌어 가고 있으리라고 스스로를 추어올리지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter XI", "# 제1권 11장"]], [["Mr. Elton must now be left to himself.", "이제 미스터 엘턴은 그 자신에게 내맡겨 둘 수밖에 없었다."], ["It was no longer in Emma’s power to superintend his happiness or quicken his measures.", "그의 행복을 살피거나 그의 발걸음을 재촉하는 일은 더 이상 엠마의 능력 밖이었다."], ["The coming of her sister’s family was so very near at hand, that first in anticipation, and then in reality, it became henceforth her prime object of interest; and during the ten days of their stay at Hartfield it was not to be expected—she did not herself expect—that any thing beyond occasional, fortuitous assistance could be afforded by her to the lovers.", "언니네 식구가 오는 날이 워낙 코앞으로 다가와, 처음에는 기대 속에서, 이윽고 실제로, 그것이 그녀의 가장 큰 관심사가 되었다. 그리고 그들이 하트필드에 머무는 열흘 동안, 그 두 연인에게 엠마가 이따금 우연히 거드는 것 이상의 도움을 줄 수 있으리라고는 기대할 수 없었다 — 그녀 자신도 그것을 기대하지 않았다."], ["They might advance rapidly if they would, however; they must advance somehow or other whether they would or no.", "그러나 그들도 마음만 먹으면 빠르게 나아갈 수 있으리라. 좋든 싫든 어떻게든 나아가야만 하는 일이었다."], ["She hardly wished to have more leisure for them.", "엠마는 그들을 위해 더 많은 여유를 갖고 싶은 마음도 별로 없었다."], ["There are people, who the more you do for them, the less they will do for themselves.", "세상에는 해 주면 해 줄수록 스스로는 점점 덜 하려 드는 사람들이 있는 법이니까."]], [["Mr. and Mrs.", "미스터 존 나이틀리 부부는 여느 때보다 오래 서리를 떠나 있었던 터라, 당연히 평소보다 한층 큰 관심을 불러일으켰다."], ["John Knightley, from having been longer than usual absent from Surry, were exciting of course rather more than the usual interest.", "올해까지, 결혼 이후 긴 휴가는 매번 하트필드와 돈웰 애비로 나누어 보내 왔다."], ["Till this year, every long vacation since their marriage had been divided between Hartfield and Donwell Abbey; but all the holidays of this autumn had been given to sea-bathing for the children, and it was therefore many months since they had been seen in a regular way by their Surry connexions, or seen at all by Mr.", "그러나 올가을 휴가는 모두 아이들의 해수욕에 바쳐졌으므로, 서리의 친척들이 그들을 제대로 본 지도 여러 달이 지났고, 미스터 우드하우스는 아예 한 번도 보지 못한 터였다 — 가엾은 이저벨라를 위해서라도 런던까지 그 먼 길을 가도록 도무지 그를 설득할 수 없었으니 말이다."], ["Woodhouse, who could not be induced to get so far as London, even for poor Isabella’s sake; and who consequently was now most nervously and apprehensively happy in forestalling this too short visit.", "그리하여 그는 이 너무 짧은 방문을 앞두고 더없이 초조하고 조마조마한 행복에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["He thought much of the evils of the journey for her, and not a little of the fatigues of his own horses and coachman who were to bring some of the party the last half of the way; but his alarms were needless; the sixteen miles being happily accomplished, and Mr.", "그는 딸에게 여정이 안겨 줄 갖은 고생을 골똘히 걱정했고, 일행의 마지막 절반을 태워 와야 할 자기 집 말들과 마부의 고단함도 적잖이 염려했다. 그러나 그 근심은 부질없었다."], ["and Mrs. John Knightley, their five children, and a competent number of nursery-maids, all reaching Hartfield in safety.", "십육 마일은 다행히 무사히 지나갔고, 미스터 존 나이틀리 부부와 다섯 아이, 그리고 충분한 수의 유모들이 모두 무탈히 하트필드에 닿았다."], ["The bustle and joy of such an arrival, the many to be talked to, welcomed, encouraged, and variously dispersed and disposed of, produced a noise and confusion which his nerves could not have borne under any other cause, nor have endured much longer even for this; but the ways of Hartfield and the feelings of her father were so respected by Mrs.", "그런 도착에 따르는 부산함과 기쁨, 말을 건네고 맞이하고 다독이고 이리저리 흩어 놓고 자리를 잡아 주어야 할 그 많은 사람들이 빚어내는 소란과 혼잡은, 다른 어떤 이유로 일어났다면 그의 신경이 도무지 견디지 못했을 것이고, 이 일을 위해서라도 그리 오래 버티지는 못했을 것이다."], ["John Knightley, that in spite of maternal solicitude for the immediate enjoyment of her little ones, and for their having instantly all the liberty and attendance, all the eating and drinking, and sleeping and playing, which they could possibly wish for, without the smallest delay, the children were never allowed to be long a disturbance to him, either in themselves or in any restless attendance on them.", "그러나 미시즈 존 나이틀리는 하트필드의 법도와 아버지의 마음을 워낙 깍듯이 헤아렸기에, 제 어린것들이 한시바삐 즐거움을 누리고, 그 아이들이 바랄 법한 온갖 자유와 시중, 먹고 마시고 자고 노는 모든 것을 조금의 지체도 없이 곧장 누리기를 바라는 어미의 애틋한 마음에도 불구하고, 아이들이 그 자체로든 아니면 그 아이들을 부산스레 돌보는 일로든 오래도록 그를 성가시게 하는 일은 결코 없도록 했다."]], [["Mrs. John Knightley was a pretty, elegant little woman, of gentle, quiet manners, and a disposition remarkably amiable and affectionate; wrapt up in her family; a devoted wife, a doating mother, and so tenderly attached to her father and sister that, but for these higher ties, a warmer love might have seemed impossible.", "미시즈 존 나이틀리는 예쁘장하고 우아한 자그마한 여인으로, 부드럽고 조용한 몸가짐에 천성이 유난히 상냥하고 다정했다."], ["She could never see a fault in any of them.", "가족에게 푹 빠져 헌신적인 아내요, 자식에게 애지중지하는 어미였으며, 아버지와 동생에게도 어찌나 살뜰히 매여 있던지, 이 더 높은 인연들이 아니었더라면 그보다 더 따뜻한 사랑이란 있을 수 없을 듯싶을 정도였다."], ["She was not a woman of strong understanding or any quickness; and with this resemblance of her father, she inherited also much of his constitution; was delicate in her own health, over-careful of that of her children, had many fears and many nerves, and was as fond of her own Mr.", "그녀는 그들 가운데 누구에게서도 결코 흠을 보지 못했다."], ["Wingfield in town as her father could be of Mr. Perry.", "그녀는 명민한 분별력이나 어떤 재빠름을 갖춘 여인은 아니었다."], ["They were alike too, in a general benevolence of temper, and a strong habit of regard for every old acquaintance.", "그리고 아버지를 그렇게 닮은 까닭에 그의 체질도 많이 물려받아, 제 건강은 허약하면서 아이들 건강에는 지나치게 노심초사했으며, 두려움도 많고 신경도 많아, 아버지가 미스터 페리를 아끼는 만큼이나 런던의 미스터 윙필드를 따랐다. 두 사람은 또한 너그러운 천성과, 옛 지인이라면 누구든 한결같이 살뜰히 여기는 강한 습성에서도 닮은꼴이었다."]], [["Mr. John Knightley was a tall, gentleman-like, and very clever man; rising in his profession, domestic, and respectable in his private character; but with reserved manners which prevented his being generally pleasing; and capable of being sometimes out of humour.", "미스터 존 나이틀리는 키가 크고 신사다우며 아주 영리한 사람으로, 자기 직업에서 두각을 나타내고 있었고, 가정적이며 사사로운 됨됨이도 존경받을 만했다."], ["He was not an ill-tempered man, not so often unreasonably cross as to deserve such a reproach; but his temper was not his great perfection; and, indeed, with such a worshipping wife, it was hardly possible that any natural defects in it should not be increased.", "그러나 무뚝뚝한 몸가짐 탓에 두루 호감을 사지는 못했고, 때로 언짢은 기색을 드러낼 줄도 아는 사람이었다. 성미가 고약한 사람은 아니었고, 그런 비난을 들을 만큼 까닭 없이 자주 신경질을 부리지도 않았다."], ["The extreme sweetness of her temper must hurt his.", "그러나 그 성미가 그의 가장 빼어난 덕은 아니었다. 더구나 그토록 숭배하는 아내를 두고서, 그 천성의 어떤 결점이 더 자라나지 않기란 거의 불가능한 일이었다."], ["He had all the clearness and quickness of mind which she wanted, and he could sometimes act an ungracious, or say a severe thing.", "그녀의 더없이 다정한 성품이 도리어 그의 성미를 거스르는 셈이었다. 그에게는 그녀에게 없는 명석함과 재빠름이 두루 있었고, 그래서 가끔은 무례하게 굴거나 매정한 말을 내뱉을 줄도 알았다."]], [["He was not a great favourite with his fair sister-in-law.", "그는 그 어여쁜 처제에게 그리 사랑받는 사람은 아니었다."], ["Nothing wrong in him escaped her.", "그의 어떤 잘못도 그녀의 눈을 비껴가지 못했다."], ["She was quick in feeling the little injuries to Isabella, which Isabella never felt herself.", "그녀는 이저벨라가 정작 자신은 한 번도 느끼지 못하는 자잘한 상처들을 재빨리 알아챘다. 어쩌면 그가 이저벨라의 동생에게 듣기 좋은 말이라도 곧잘 해 주는 사람이었다면 더 많은 것을 눈감아 주었을지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps she might have passed over more had his manners been flattering to Isabella’s sister, but they were only those of a calmly kind brother and friend, without praise and without blindness; but hardly any degree of personal compliment could have made her regardless of that greatest fault of all in her eyes which he sometimes fell into, the want of respectful forbearance towards her father.", "그러나 그의 태도란 그저 차분하고 다정한 오빠이자 벗의 것일 뿐, 칭찬도 없고 그렇다고 눈멀어 감싸 주는 법도 없었다. 그렇지만 어떤 개인적인 찬사로도, 그녀의 눈에 가장 큰 잘못으로 비치는 것 — 그가 이따금 저지르고 마는, 아버지에 대한 공손한 인내의 결여 — 만큼은 그녀가 못 본 척할 수 없었다."], ["There he had not always the patience that could have been wished. Mr.", "그 대목에서 그는 바랄 만한 만큼의 참을성을 늘 갖추고 있지는 못했다."], ["Woodhouse’s peculiarities and fidgetiness were sometimes provoking him to a rational remonstrance or sharp retort equally ill-bestowed.", "미스터 우드하우스의 별난 버릇과 안달이 때로 그를 자극해, 분별 있는 항변이든 날카로운 응수든 똑같이 어울리지 않는 말을 내뱉게 했던 것이다."], ["It did not often happen; for Mr.", "자주 있는 일은 아니었다."], ["John Knightley had really a great regard for his father-in-law, and generally a strong sense of what was due to him; but it was too often for Emma’s charity, especially as there was all the pain of apprehension frequently to be endured, though the offence came not.", "미스터 존 나이틀리는 사실 장인을 무척 존경했고, 대체로 장인에 대한 도리를 깊이 헤아렸으니까. 그러나 엠마의 너그러움이 감당하기에는 그 횟수가 너무 잦았다."], ["The beginning, however, of every visit displayed none but the properest feelings, and this being of necessity so short might be hoped to pass away in unsullied cordiality.", "더구나 실제로 거슬리는 일이 일어나지 않을 때조차, 그런 일이 벌어질까 봐 마음 졸여야 하는 고통을 줄곧 견뎌야 했으니 더욱 그러했다. 그래도 방문이 시작될 무렵에는 언제나 더없이 마땅한 마음만이 드러나는 법이었고, 이번 방문은 으레 그렇듯 워낙 짧을 수밖에 없으니 흠 없는 화기애애함 속에 지나가기를 바랄 만했다."], ["They had not been long seated and composed when Mr. Woodhouse, with a melancholy shake of the head and a sigh, called his daughter’s attention to the sad change at Hartfield since she had been there last.", "자리에 앉아 미처 마음을 가라앉히기도 전에, 미스터 우드하우스가 침울하게 고개를 가로젓고 한숨을 쉬며, 지난번 딸이 다녀간 이후 하트필드에 닥친 슬픈 변화에 대해 딸의 주의를 끌었다."]], [["“Ah, my dear,”", "\"아이고, 얘야,\""], ["said he, “poor Miss Taylor—It is a grievous business.”", "그가 말했다. \"가엾은 미스 테일러 말이다 — 참으로 딱한 일이지.\""]], [["“Oh yes, sir,” cried she with ready sympathy, “how you must miss her! And dear Emma, too!—What a dreadful loss to you both!—I have been so grieved for you.—I could not imagine how you could possibly do without her.—It is a sad change indeed.—But I hope she is pretty well, sir.”", "\"아유, 그러게요, 아버지.\" 그녀가 곧장 동정 어린 목소리로 외쳤다. \"얼마나 그분이 그리우시겠어요! 사랑하는 엠마도요! 두 분께 이 얼마나 끔찍한 상실인지요! 저는 두 분 생각에 어찌나 마음이 아팠는지 몰라요. 그분 없이 어떻게 지내실까 도무지 상상이 안 갔어요. 정말이지 슬픈 변화예요. 그래도 그분은 꽤 잘 지내시는 거죠, 아버지?\""]], [["“Pretty well, my dear—I hope—pretty well.—I do not know but that the place agrees with her tolerably.”", "\"꽤 잘 지내는 듯하구나, 얘야 — 그러기를 바란다 — 꽤 잘 지내는 게지. 그래도 그곳 물이 그 사람한테 그럭저럭 맞는 모양이긴 하다만.\""]], [["Mr. John Knightley here asked Emma quietly whether there were any doubts of the air of Randalls.", "여기서 미스터 존 나이틀리가 엠마에게 조용히, 랜덜스의 공기에 무슨 미심쩍은 데라도 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아유!"], ["no—none in the least.", "아니에요 — 조금도요."], ["I never saw Mrs. Weston better in my life—never looking so well.", "저는 평생 웨스턴 부인이 그렇게 좋아 보이는 걸 본 적이 없어요 — 그렇게 안색이 좋은 적이 없었죠."], ["Papa is only speaking his own regret.”", "아버지는 그저 당신 자신의 서운함을 말씀하시는 것뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Very much to the honour of both,”", "\"두 분 모두에게 참으로 명예로운 일이군.\""], ["was the handsome reply.", "점잖은 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“And do you see her, sir, tolerably often?”", "\"그래서 아버지, 그분을 그럭저럭 자주 만나시나요?\""], ["asked Isabella in the plaintive tone which just suited her father.", "이저벨라가 제 아버지에게 꼭 어울리는 가련한 목소리로 물었다."]], [["Mr.", "미스터 우드하우스가 머뭇거렸다."], ["Woodhouse hesitated.—“Not near so often, my dear, as I could wish.”", "\"내가 바라는 만큼 그리 자주는 못 본다, 얘야.\""]], [["“Oh! papa, we have missed seeing them but one entire day since they married.", "\"아유!"], ["Either in the morning or evening of every day, excepting one, have we seen either Mr.", "아버지, 우리가 그분들을 못 본 날이라곤 결혼한 뒤로 통틀어 하루밖에 없는걸요."], ["Weston or Mrs. Weston, and generally both, either at Randalls or here—and as you may suppose, Isabella, most frequently here.", "그 하루를 빼고는 날마다 아침이든 저녁이든 미스터 웨스턴이나 웨스턴 부인을, 대개는 두 분 다 뵈었어요 — 랜덜스에서든 여기서든요."], ["They are very, very kind in their visits.", "그리고 언니도 짐작하겠지만, 거의 다 여기서였죠."], ["Mr. Weston is really as kind as herself.", "그분들은 정말이지 더없이 친절하게 와 주세요."], ["Papa, if you speak in that melancholy way, you will be giving Isabella a false idea of us all.", "미스터 웨스턴은 정말 부인 못지않게 다정한 분이고요."], ["Every body must be aware that Miss Taylor must be missed, but every body ought also to be assured that Mr.", "아버지, 그렇게 침울하게 말씀하시면 언니가 우리 모두에 대해 잘못된 생각을 갖게 될 거예요."], ["and Mrs. Weston do really prevent our missing her by any means to the extent we ourselves anticipated—which is the exact truth.”", "미스 테일러가 그립다는 건 누구나 알 만한 일이지만, 미스터 웨스턴 부부가 우리가 미리 짐작했던 만큼은 결코 그분을 그리워하지 않도록 정말로 막아 주고 있다는 것도 모두가 똑똑히 알아 둬야 해요 — 이게 한 치도 틀림없는 사실이거든요.\""]], [["“Just as it should be,” said Mr.", "\"바로 그래야 마땅하지요.\""], ["John Knightley, “and just as I hoped it was from your letters.", "미스터 존 나이틀리가 말했다. \"그리고 처제 편지를 보고 내가 짐작했던 그대로네요."], ["Her wish of shewing you attention could not be doubted, and his being a disengaged and social man makes it all easy.", "그분이 처제에게 마음을 써 주고 싶어 하리라는 건 의심할 여지가 없고, 그분 남편이 매인 데 없이 사교적인 사람이니 만사가 수월한 거지요. 여보, 내가 늘 말하지 않았소."], ["I have been always telling you, my love, that I had no idea of the change being so very material to Hartfield as you apprehended; and now you have Emma’s account, I hope you will be satisfied.”", "그 변화가 당신이 걱정하던 것만큼 하트필드에 대단한 일은 아닐 거라고 말이오. 이제 엠마 얘기까지 들었으니, 부디 마음이 놓이기를 바라오.\""]], [["“Why, to be sure,”", "\"글쎄다, 그야 물론,\""], ["said Mr. Woodhouse—“yes, certainly—I cannot deny that Mrs.", "미스터 우드하우스가 말했다."], ["Weston, poor Mrs. Weston, does come and see us pretty often—but then—she is always obliged to go away again.”", "\"그래, 분명히 — 웨스턴 부인이, 그 가엾은 웨스턴 부인이 우리를 꽤 자주 보러 오는 거야 나도 부인할 수 없지 — 하지만 그 사람은 — 늘 다시 돌아가야만 하잖니.\""]], [["“It would be very hard upon Mr.", "\"만약 그분이 안 돌아가면 미스터 웨스턴이 무척 곤란하실 거예요, 아버지."], ["Weston if she did not, papa.—You quite forget poor Mr. Weston.”", "가엾은 미스터 웨스턴은 아주 잊으셨군요.\""]], [["“I think, indeed,”", "\"제 생각에도 정말,\""], ["said John Knightley pleasantly, “that Mr.", "존 나이틀리가 유쾌하게 말했다."], ["Weston has some little claim.", "\"미스터 웨스턴에게도 얼마간 권리가 있지요."], ["You and I, Emma, will venture to take the part of the poor husband.", "엠마, 자네와 나는 그 가엾은 남편 편을 한번 들어 봅시다."], ["I, being a husband, and you not being a wife, the claims of the man may very likely strike us with equal force.", "나는 남편이고 자네는 아내가 아니니, 남자의 권리라는 게 우리 둘에게는 똑같은 무게로 와닿을 법하지요."], ["As for Isabella, she has been married long enough to see the convenience of putting all the Mr. Westons aside as much as she can.”", "이저벨라로 말하자면, 결혼한 지 오래라 미스터 웨스턴이고 뭐고 죄다 한껏 제쳐 두는 편이 편하다는 걸 알 만큼은 됐고요.\""]], [["“Me, my love,”", "\"저요, 여보?\""], ["cried his wife, hearing and understanding only in part.— “Are you talking about me?—I am sure nobody ought to be, or can be, a greater advocate for matrimony than I am; and if it had not been for the misery of her leaving Hartfield, I should never have thought of Miss Taylor but as the most fortunate woman in the world; and as to slighting Mr.", "그 아내가 일부만 듣고 일부만 알아듣고는 외쳤다."], ["Weston, that excellent Mr.", "\"제 얘기를 하시는 거예요?"], ["Weston, I think there is nothing he does not deserve.", "결혼을 두고 저보다 더 든든한 옹호자가 있을 리도, 있을 수도 없다고요."], ["I believe he is one of the very best-tempered men that ever existed.", "미스 테일러가 하트필드를 떠나는 그 비참함만 아니었다면, 저는 그분을 세상에서 가장 운 좋은 여인으로밖에 생각하지 않았을 거예요. 그리고 미스터 웨스턴을 가볍게 여기다니요, 그 훌륭한 미스터 웨스턴을 — 그분이 받을 자격이 없는 게 하나도 없다고 생각하는걸요."], ["Excepting yourself and your brother, I do not know his equal for temper.", "그분은 세상에 둘도 없이 성품이 좋은 분 가운데 하나일 거예요. 당신과 시아주버님만 빼면, 성미로 따져 그분에게 견줄 사람을 저는 모르겠어요."], ["I shall never forget his flying Henry’s kite for him that very windy day last Easter—and ever since his particular kindness last September twelvemonth in writing that note, at twelve o’clock at night, on purpose to assure me that there was no scarlet fever at Cobham, I have been convinced there could not be a more feeling heart nor a better man in existence.—If any body can deserve him, it must be Miss Taylor.”", "지난 부활절 그 바람 몹시 불던 날, 헨리의 연을 대신 날려 주시던 그 모습을 저는 영영 못 잊을 거예요 — 그리고 재작년 구월에, 콥햄에 성홍열이 돌지 않는다고 일부러 안심시켜 주려고 밤 열두 시에 그 쪽지를 써 보내 주신 그 각별한 친절 이후로, 그분보다 더 마음 따뜻하고 더 훌륭한 사람은 세상에 없으리라고 저는 확신하게 됐어요. 누구든 그분에게 어울릴 자격이 있는 사람이라면, 그건 분명 미스 테일러일 거예요.\""]], [["“Where is the young man?”", "\"그 젊은이는 어디 있소?\""], ["said John Knightley.", "존 나이틀리가 말했다."], ["“Has he been here on this occasion—or has he not?”", "\"이번에 여기 왔었소 — 안 왔었소?\""]], [["“He has not been here yet,”", "\"아직 안 왔어요.\""], ["replied Emma.", "엠마가 대답했다."], ["“There was a strong expectation of his coming soon after the marriage, but it ended in nothing; and I have not heard him mentioned lately.”", "\"결혼하고 곧 오리라는 기대가 자못 컸지만, 결국 아무 일도 없이 끝났어요. 그리고 요즘은 그분 얘기가 나오는 걸 통 듣지 못했고요.\""]], [["“But you should tell them of the letter, my dear,”", "\"하지만 얘야, 너 그 편지 얘기를 해 드려야지.\""], ["said her father.", "그녀의 아버지가 말했다."], ["“He wrote a letter to poor Mrs. Weston, to congratulate her, and a very proper, handsome letter it was.", "\"그 사람이 가엾은 웨스턴 부인에게 축하 편지를 써 보냈는데, 아주 격식 있고 훌륭한 편지였단다."], ["She shewed it to me.", "부인이 그걸 내게 보여 줬지."], ["I thought it very well done of him indeed.", "나는 그 사람이 참 잘했다고 여겼다."], ["Whether it was his own idea you know, one cannot tell.", "그게 자기 생각에서 나온 건지는 모를 일이지만 말이다."], ["He is but young, and his uncle, perhaps—”", "그 사람은 아직 젊고, 외삼촌이 어쩌면 —\""]], [["“My dear papa, he is three-and-twenty.", "\"아버지도 참, 그분은 스물셋이에요."], ["You forget how time passes.”", "세월 가는 걸 잊으셨군요.\""]], [["“Three-and-twenty!—is he indeed?—Well, I could not have thought it—and he was but two years old when he lost his poor mother!", "\"스물셋이라! 정말 그러냐?"], ["Well, time does fly indeed!—and my memory is very bad.", "거참, 도무지 그럴 줄은 몰랐구나 — 가엾은 어미를 여의었을 때 그 애가 겨우 두 살이었는데!"], ["However, it was an exceeding good, pretty letter, and gave Mr. and Mrs.", "거참, 세월이 정말 쏜살같구나!"], ["Weston a great deal of pleasure.", "게다가 내 기억력은 영 형편없고."], ["I remember it was written from Weymouth, and dated Sept.", "어쨌든 그건 더없이 훌륭하고 멋진 편지였고, 미스터 웨스턴 부부에게 큰 기쁨을 안겨 줬단다."], ["28th—and began, ‘My dear Madam,’ but I forget how it went on; and it was signed ‘F.", "내 기억에 그건 웨이머스에서 쓴 거였고, 날짜가 구월 이십팔일이었지 — 그리고 '친애하는 부인께'로 시작했는데, 그다음은 어떻게 이어졌는지 잊었구나. 그리고 'F."], ["C.", "C."], ["Weston Churchill.’—I remember that perfectly.”", "웨스턴 처칠'이라고 서명했지 — 그건 똑똑히 기억한단다.\""]], [["“How very pleasing and proper of him!”", "\"그 사람 참 마음씨 곱고 반듯하기도 하지!\""], ["cried the good-hearted Mrs. John Knightley.", "마음씨 착한 미시즈 존 나이틀리가 외쳤다."], ["“I have no doubt of his being a most amiable young man.", "\"더없이 사랑스러운 젊은이임에 틀림없어요."], ["But how sad it is that he should not live at home with his father!", "그렇지만 아버지와 함께 집에서 살지 못한다니 얼마나 슬픈 일인지요!"], ["There is something so shocking in a child’s being taken away from his parents and natural home!", "자식이 제 부모와 타고난 보금자리에서 떼어내진다니, 거기엔 어쩐지 너무도 충격적인 데가 있어요!"], ["I never could comprehend how Mr. Weston could part with him.", "미스터 웨스턴이 어떻게 그 애를 보낼 수 있었는지 저는 도무지 이해할 수가 없어요."], ["To give up one’s child!", "제 자식을 내준다니요!"], ["I really never could think well of any body who proposed such a thing to any body else.”", "정말이지 남에게 그런 일을 권하는 사람이라면 누구든 저는 좋게 볼 수가 없을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Nobody ever did think well of the Churchills, I fancy,”", "\"처칠 집안을 좋게 본 사람은 애당초 아무도 없을걸.\" 미스터 존 나이틀리가 냉랭하게 말했다."], ["observed Mr. John Knightley coolly.", "\"하지만 당신이 헨리나 존을 내줄 때 느낄 법한 그 심정을 미스터 웨스턴도 느꼈으리라고는 생각지 마시오."], ["“But you need not imagine Mr.", "미스터 웨스턴은 감정이 깊은 사람이라기보다는 오히려 느긋하고 쾌활한 사람이오. 매사를 있는 그대로 받아들이고 어떻게든 거기서 즐거움을 만들어 내는 사람이지."], ["Weston to have felt what you would feel in giving up Henry or John. Mr.", "짐작건대 그의 위안이란 가정의 정 같은 것보다는, 이른바 사교라는 데에, 그러니까 이웃들과 일주일에 다섯 번씩 먹고 마시고 휘스트를 칠 수 있는 그 능력에 훨씬 더 기대고 있을 거요."], ["Weston is rather an easy, cheerful-tempered man, than a man of strong feelings; he takes things as he finds them, and makes enjoyment of them somehow or other, depending, I suspect, much more upon what is called society for his comforts, that is, upon the power of eating and drinking, and playing whist with his neighbours five times a week, than upon family affection, or any thing that home affords.”", "집안이 주는 것 따위보다 말이오.\" * 휘스트: 네 사람이 둘씩 짝을 지어 하는 카드놀이로, 19세기 영국 상류층의 대표적인 사교 오락."]], [["Emma could not like what bordered on a reflection on Mr.", "엠마는 미스터 웨스턴을 흠잡는 듯한 말이 영 마음에 들지 않아 한마디 받아칠까 하는 마음이 반쯤 일었으나, 꾹 참고 그냥 흘려보냈다."], ["Weston, and had half a mind to take it up; but she struggled, and let it pass.", "될 수 있으면 평화를 지키고 싶었다."], ["She would keep the peace if possible; and there was something honourable and valuable in the strong domestic habits, the all-sufficiency of home to himself, whence resulted her brother’s disposition to look down on the common rate of social intercourse, and those to whom it was important.—It had a high claim to forbearance.", "게다가 그 강한 가정적 습성, 제 집이 제게는 부족함 없이 족하다는 그 마음에는 어딘가 떳떳하고 값진 데가 있었으니 — 형부가 흔한 수준의 사교를, 또 그런 사교를 소중히 여기는 사람들을 내려다보는 기질도 거기서 비롯된 것이었다. 그것은 너그러이 봐줄 만한 충분한 자격이 있었다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter XII", "# 제1권 12장"]], [["Mr. Knightley was to dine with them—rather against the inclination of Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리가 그들과 함께 저녁을 들기로 되어 있었다."], ["Woodhouse, who did not like that any one should share with him in Isabella’s first day.", "미스터 우드하우스로서는 썩 내키지 않는 일이었으니, 이저벨라가 돌아와 함께 보내는 첫날을 누구와도 나누고 싶지 않았던 까닭이다. 그러나 엠마의 옳고 그름에 대한 분별이 이를 정해 두었다."], ["Emma’s sense of right however had decided it; and besides the consideration of what was due to each brother, she had particular pleasure, from the circumstance of the late disagreement between Mr. Knightley and herself, in procuring him the proper invitation.", "두 형제에게 마땅히 갖추어야 할 도리를 헤아린 것은 물론이거니와, 얼마 전 미스터 나이틀리와 자기 사이에 있었던 불화 탓에, 그에게 합당한 초대를 보내는 일이 그녀에게는 각별한 즐거움이기도 했다."]], [["She hoped they might now become friends again.", "이제 두 사람이 다시 벗이 될 수 있기를 그녀는 바랐다."], ["She thought it was time to make up.", "화해할 때가 되었다고 여긴 것이다."], ["Making-up indeed would not do.", "사실 화해라 할 것까지는 없었다."], ["She certainly had not been in the wrong, and he would never own that he had.", "자기가 그릇된 것은 결코 아니었고, 그 또한 자기가 잘못했다고 인정할 리 없었다. 어느 한쪽의 양보란 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Concession must be out of the question; but it was time to appear to forget that they had ever quarrelled; and she hoped it might rather assist the restoration of friendship, that when he came into the room she had one of the children with her—the youngest, a nice little girl about eight months old, who was now making her first visit to Hartfield, and very happy to be danced about in her aunt’s arms.", "그러나 그들이 다툰 적이 있었다는 사실 자체를 잊은 척할 때는 되었다. 그가 방으로 들어섰을 때 그녀가 아이 하나를 안고 있었다는 점이 우정의 회복에 도움이 되기를 그녀는 바랐다 — 가장 어린아이, 생후 여덟 달쯤 된 예쁘장한 여자아이로, 이제 처음으로 하트필드를 찾아온 참이었고, 이모 품에 안겨 둥기둥기 어르는 손길이 무척이나 즐거운 듯했다."], ["It did assist; for though he began with grave looks and short questions, he was soon led on to talk of them all in the usual way, and to take the child out of her arms with all the unceremoniousness of perfect amity.", "과연 도움이 되었다. 그가 처음에는 굳은 표정과 짤막한 물음으로 시작했으나, 이내 여느 때처럼 그 아이들 모두에 관한 이야기로 이끌려 들어갔고, 더없이 다정한 사이의 격의 없는 태도로 아이를 그녀의 품에서 받아 안았던 것이다."], ["Emma felt they were friends again; and the conviction giving her at first great satisfaction, and then a little sauciness, she could not help saying, as he was admiring the baby,", "엠마는 두 사람이 다시 벗이 되었음을 느꼈다. 그 확신이 처음에는 큰 만족을 주더니, 이윽고 약간의 장난기를 부추겨, 그가 아기를 어르며 들여다보고 있을 때 그녀는 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“What a comfort it is, that we think alike about our nephews and nieces.", "\"우리가 조카들에 대해서는 생각이 똑같다는 게 얼마나 마음 든든한지 몰라요."], ["As to men and women, our opinions are sometimes very different; but with regard to these children, I observe we never disagree.”", "남자와 여자 이야기로 가면 우리 의견이 영 갈릴 때가 더러 있지만, 이 아이들에 관해서라면 한 번도 어긋난 적이 없잖아요.\""]], [["“If you were as much guided by nature in your estimate of men and women, and as little under the power of fancy and whim in your dealings with them, as you are where these children are concerned, we might always think alike.”", "\"자네가 남자와 여자를 가늠할 때에도 이 아이들을 대할 때만큼이나 천성을 길잡이로 삼고, 공상이나 변덕에 휘둘리지 않는다면, 우리 생각은 늘 같을 수 있을 텐데.\""]], [["“To be sure—our discordancies must always arise from my being in the wrong.”", "\"그럼요 — 우리 사이의 불화는 언제나 제가 그릇된 데서 비롯되는 거겠죠.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said he, smiling—“and reason good.", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그럴 만한 이유가 있지."], ["I was sixteen years old when you were born.”", "자네가 태어났을 때 나는 열여섯 살이었으니까.\""]], [["“A material difference then,”", "\"그땐 대단한 차이였죠.\" 그녀가 받았다."], ["she replied—“and no doubt you were much my superior in judgment at that period of our lives; but does not the lapse of one-and-twenty years bring our understandings a good deal nearer?”", "\"그 시절 우리 둘의 처지로 보면, 분별에서 당신이 저보다 훨씬 위였던 게 틀림없고요. 하지만 스물한 해가 흘렀으니, 우리 견식도 어지간히 가까워지지 않았을까요?\""]], [["“Yes—a good deal nearer.”", "\"그래 — 어지간히 가까워졌지.\""]], [["“But still, not near enough to give me a chance of being right, if we think differently.”", "\"하지만 그래도, 우리 생각이 갈릴 때 제가 옳을 가능성을 줄 만큼은 아니라는 거죠.\""]], [["“I have still the advantage of you by sixteen years’ experience, and by not being a pretty young woman and a spoiled child.", "\"나는 여전히 십육 년의 경험만큼 자네보다 앞서 있고, 게다가 예쁘장한 젊은 아가씨도 아니고 응석받이 어린애도 아니라는 이점까지 있지."], ["Come, my dear Emma, let us be friends, and say no more about it.", "자, 엠마, 우리 그만 화해하고 이 일은 더 들추지 마세."], ["Tell your aunt, little Emma, that she ought to set you a better example than to be renewing old grievances, and that if she were not wrong before, she is now.”", "꼬마 엠마야, 네 이모더러 일러 주려무나 — 묵은 불평을 되살리는 것보다 너한테 더 나은 본을 보여야 하는 거라고. 그리고 전에는 틀리지 않았다 쳐도, 지금은 틀린 거라고 말이다.\""]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"맞아요.\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["she cried—“very true.", "\"정말 맞는 말이에요."], ["Little Emma, grow up a better woman than your aunt.", "꼬마 엠마야, 너는 자라서 네 이모보다 나은 여자가 되렴."], ["Be infinitely cleverer and not half so conceited. Now, Mr.", "한없이 더 영리하고, 그 절반만큼도 우쭐대지 말고."], ["Knightley, a word or two more, and I have done.", "자, 미스터 나이틀리, 한두 마디만 더 하고 끝낼게요."], ["As far as good intentions went, we were both right, and I must say that no effects on my side of the argument have yet proved wrong.", "좋은 뜻으로 한 일이라는 점에서는 우리 둘 다 옳았고, 제 쪽 주장으로 인한 결과 가운데 잘못된 것으로 드러난 건 아직 하나도 없다고 말씀드려야겠어요."], ["I only want to know that Mr. Martin is not very, very bitterly disappointed.”", "저는 그저 미스터 마틴이 정말이지 몹시, 쓰라리게 낙심한 건 아니라는 걸 알고 싶을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“A man cannot be more so,”", "\"남자가 그보다 더 낙심할 수는 없을 정도일세.\""], ["was his short, full answer.", "짤막하나 빈틈없는 대답이었다."]], [["“Ah!—Indeed I am very sorry.—Come, shake hands with me.”", "\"아! 정말이지 마음이 아프네요. 자, 저와 손을 맞잡아요.\""]], [["This had just taken place and with great cordiality, when John Knightley made his appearance, and “How d’ye do, George?”", "그렇게 더없이 따뜻하게 두 사람이 막 손을 맞잡았을 때, 존 나이틀리가 모습을 드러냈다. \"잘 지냈나, 조지?\""], ["and “John, how are you?”", "\"존, 자네는 어떤가?\""], ["succeeded in the true English style, burying under a calmness that seemed all but indifference, the real attachment which would have led either of them, if requisite, to do every thing for the good of the other.", "하는 인사가 진짜 영국식으로 오갔으니, 거의 무심해 보일 만큼 잔잔한 겉모습 아래에, 필요하다면 어느 쪽이든 상대를 위해 무엇이든 해 줄 진정한 정을 묻어 두는 식이었다."]], [["The evening was quiet and conversable, as Mr.", "저녁나절은 조용하고 도란도란 이야기 나누기 좋은 시간이었으니, 미스터 우드하우스가 사랑하는 이저벨라와 정겹게 이야기를 나누고자 카드놀이를 아예 마다한 까닭이었다."], ["Woodhouse declined cards entirely for the sake of comfortable talk with his dear Isabella, and the little party made two natural divisions; on one side he and his daughter; on the other the two Mr.", "자그마한 일행은 자연스레 두 패로 나뉘었다. 한쪽에는 그와 딸이, 다른 쪽에는 두 미스터 나이틀리가 있었다."], ["Knightleys; their subjects totally distinct, or very rarely mixing—and Emma only occasionally joining in one or the other.", "화제는 완전히 갈려 거의 섞이는 법이 없었고 — 엠마만이 이따금 이쪽저쪽에 끼어들 따름이었다."]], [["The brothers talked of their own concerns and pursuits, but principally of those of the elder, whose temper was by much the most communicative, and who was always the greater talker.", "형제는 저마다의 일과 관심사를 이야기했으나, 주로는 형 쪽의 것이었으니, 기질이 훨씬 더 말이 많은 편이었고 늘 더 많이 떠드는 쪽이었다."], ["As a magistrate, he had generally some point of law to consult John about, or, at least, some curious anecdote to give; and as a farmer, as keeping in hand the home-farm at Donwell, he had to tell what every field was to bear next year, and to give all such local information as could not fail of being interesting to a brother whose home it had equally been the longest part of his life, and whose attachments were strong.", "치안 판사로서 그는 대개 법률상의 어떤 점을 존에게 물을 거리가 있거나, 적어도 들려줄 만한 흥미로운 일화 하나쯤은 있었다. 또 농부로서, 돈웰의 직영 농장을 손수 건사하는 처지였으니, 어느 밭에 내년 무엇을 심을지를 일러 주고, 동생에게 흥미롭지 않을 수 없는 갖가지 고장 소식을 전해야 했다 — 그곳은 동생에게도 인생의 가장 긴 세월 동안 똑같이 고향이었고, 그 애착이 깊은 까닭이었다."], ["The plan of a drain, the change of a fence, the felling of a tree, and the destination of every acre for wheat, turnips, or spring corn, was entered into with as much equality of interest by John, as his cooler manners rendered possible; and if his willing brother ever left him any thing to inquire about, his inquiries even approached a tone of eagerness.", "배수로를 놓을 계획이며, 울타리를 바꾸는 일이며, 나무 한 그루를 베는 일이며, 한 에이커 한 에이커가 밀밭이 될지 순무밭이 될지 봄보리밭이 될지 하는 이야기까지, 존은 그의 차분한 성미가 허락하는 한도껏 똑같은 관심으로 함께 파고들었다. 그리고 기꺼이 떠드는 형이 물어볼 거리를 조금이라도 남겨 두는 날이면, 그 물음에는 열의에 가까운 어조까지 묻어났다."]], [["While they were thus comfortably occupied, Mr. Woodhouse was enjoying a full flow of happy regrets and fearful affection with his daughter.", "이렇게 그들이 흐뭇하게 이야기에 빠져 있는 동안, 미스터 우드하우스는 딸을 붙들고 행복한 아쉬움과 근심 어린 애정을 한껏 쏟아 내며 즐기고 있었다."]], [["“My poor dear Isabella,”", "\"가엾은 우리 이저벨라.\""], ["said he, fondly taking her hand, and interrupting, for a few moments, her busy labours for some one of her five children—“How long it is, how terribly long since you were here!", "그가 다정하게 딸의 손을 잡으며, 다섯 아이 가운데 누군가를 위해 분주히 손을 놀리던 그녀를 잠시 멈춰 세우고 말했다. \"어쩌면 이렇게 오랜만이냐, 끔찍이도 오랜만이로구나, 네가 여기 온 게!"], ["And how tired you must be after your journey!", "그 먼 길을 오느라 얼마나 고단하겠니!"], ["You must go to bed early, my dear—and I recommend a little gruel to you before you go.—You and I will have a nice basin of gruel together.", "오늘은 일찍 자거라, 얘야 — 그리고 자기 전에 묽은 죽을 좀 들기를 권하마. 너랑 나랑 같이 죽 한 사발씩 들자꾸나."], ["My dear Emma, suppose we all have a little gruel.”", "엠마야, 우리 다 같이 죽을 좀 드는 게 어떻겠니.\""]], [["Emma could not suppose any such thing, knowing as she did, that both the Mr. Knightleys were as unpersuadable on that article as herself;—and two basins only were ordered.", "엠마는 도무지 그럴 마음이 들지 않았으니, 두 미스터 나이틀리가 그 죽이라는 물건에 대해서는 자기만큼이나 끄떡없다는 걸 잘 아는 까닭이었다."], ["After a little more discourse in praise of gruel, with some wondering at its not being taken every evening by every body, he proceeded to say, with an air of grave reflection,", "그리하여 죽은 딱 두 사발만 시켰다. 죽을 기리는 이야기가 조금 더 오가고, 어찌하여 사람마다 저녁마다 그것을 들지 않는지 의아해하는 말까지 곁들인 끝에, 그는 자못 진지하게 생각에 잠긴 표정으로 말을 이었다."]], [["“It was an awkward business, my dear, your spending the autumn at South End instead of coming here.", "\"얘야, 그건 좀 잘못된 일이었어. 가을을 여기 오지 않고 사우스엔드에서 보내다니 말이다."], ["I never had much opinion of the sea air.”", "나는 바닷바람을 그리 대단하게 여긴 적이 없단다.\""]], [["“Mr. Wingfield most strenuously recommended it, sir—or we should not have gone.", "\"미스터 윙필드가 한사코 권한 거예요, 아버지 — 안 그랬으면 가지도 않았어요."], ["He recommended it for all the children, but particularly for the weakness in little Bella’s throat,—both sea air and bathing.”", "아이들 모두한테 좋다고 했지만, 특히 우리 벨라의 목이 약한 데 좋다고요 — 바닷바람도 해수욕도요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["my dear, but Perry had many doubts about the sea doing her any good; and as to myself, I have been long perfectly convinced, though perhaps I never told you so before, that the sea is very rarely of use to any body.", "얘야, 하지만 페리는 바다가 그 애한테 좋을지 못내 미심쩍어했단다. 그리고 나로 말하자면, 전에 너한테 말한 적은 없는 것 같다만, 바다가 누구한테든 이로운 경우란 아주 드물다고 오래전부터 확실히 믿어 왔어."], ["I am sure it almost killed me once.”", "한번은 바다가 나를 죽일 뻔했다니까.\""]], [["“Come, come,”", "\"자, 자.\""], ["cried Emma, feeling this to be an unsafe subject, “I must beg you not to talk of the sea.", "엠마가 위험한 화제임을 느끼고 끼어들었다. \"제발 바다 이야기는 그만하셨으면 해요."], ["It makes me envious and miserable;—I who have never seen it!", "저는 부러워서 속상하단 말이에요 — 한 번도 본 적이 없는 제가요!"], ["South End is prohibited, if you please.", "사우스엔드는 금지예요, 부탁이에요."], ["My dear Isabella, I have not heard you make one inquiry about Mr.", "그런데 이저벨라 언니, 언니가 미스터 페리 안부 묻는 걸 아직 한 번도 못 들었네."], ["Perry yet; and he never forgets you.”", "그분은 늘 언니를 잊지 않는데.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["good Mr. Perry—how is he, sir?”", "그 좋으신 미스터 페리 — 그분은 어떠세요, 아버지?\""]], [["“Why, pretty well; but not quite well.", "\"글쎄, 그럭저럭 괜찮긴 한데, 아주 좋지는 않단다."], ["Poor Perry is bilious, and he has not time to take care of himself—he tells me he has not time to take care of himself—which is very sad—but he is always wanted all round the country.", "가엾은 페리는 담즙이 안 좋은데, 제 몸 돌볼 틈이 없어 — 제 몸 돌볼 틈이 없다고 나한테 그러더구나 — 참 딱한 노릇이지 — 하지만 온 고장에서 늘 그 사람을 찾으니 말이다."], ["I suppose there is not a man in such practice anywhere.", "그만큼 환자가 몰리는 의원은 어디에도 없을 게다."], ["But then there is not so clever a man any where.”", "하기야 그만큼 솜씨 좋은 사람도 어디에도 없으니.\""]], [["“And Mrs. Perry and the children, how are they?", "\"그럼 페리 부인하고 아이들은요?"], ["do the children grow?", "다들 잘 있어요? 아이들은 많이 컸고요?"], ["I have a great regard for Mr. Perry.", "저는 미스터 페리를 아주 좋게 여겨요."], ["I hope he will be calling soon.", "곧 한번 들러 주시면 좋겠어요."], ["He will be so pleased to see my little ones.”", "우리 어린것들을 보면 무척 반가워하실 텐데.\""]], [["“I hope he will be here to-morrow, for I have a question or two to ask him about myself of some consequence.", "\"내일 와 줬으면 한단다. 나도 내 몸에 관해 좀 중요한 걸 한두 가지 물어볼 게 있거든."], ["And, my dear, whenever he comes, you had better let him look at little Bella’s throat.”", "그리고 얘야, 그 사람이 오거든 우리 벨라의 목을 한번 봐 달라고 하는 게 좋겠다.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["my dear sir, her throat is so much better that I have hardly any uneasiness about it.", "아버지, 그 애 목은 한결 좋아져서 이젠 거의 걱정이 없을 정도예요."], ["Either bathing has been of the greatest service to her, or else it is to be attributed to an excellent embrocation of Mr. Wingfield’s, which we have been applying at times ever since August.”", "해수욕이 그 애한테 더없이 도움이 됐거나, 아니면 팔월부터 줄곧 이따금 발라 온 미스터 윙필드의 그 훌륭한 도포약 덕분이거나일 거예요.\""]], [["“It is not very likely, my dear, that bathing should have been of use to her—and if I had known you were wanting an embrocation, I would have spoken to—", "\"얘야, 해수욕이 그 애한테 도움이 됐을 성싶지는 않구나 — 그리고 네가 도포약이 필요했던 줄 알았더라면, 내가 말을 넣었을 텐데 —\""]], [["“You seem to me to have forgotten Mrs. and Miss Bates,”", "\"아버지, 아무래도 베이츠 부인하고 미스 베이츠는 까맣게 잊으신 모양이에요.\""], ["said Emma, “I have not heard one inquiry after them.”", "엠마가 말했다. \"그분들 안부를 한마디도 안 물으시잖아요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["the good Bateses—I am quite ashamed of myself—but you mention them in most of your letters.", "그 착한 베이츠네 — 내가 다 부끄럽구나 — 하지만 너는 편지마다 거의 그분들 이야기를 하더구나. 다들 잘 계시면 좋겠다."], ["I hope they are quite well.", "그 마음씨 좋으신 베이츠 노부인 — 내일 찾아뵙고 아이들도 데려가야겠어. 그분들은 늘 우리 아이들을 보면 그렇게 반가워하시니까."], ["Good old Mrs.", "그리고 그 더없이 훌륭하신 미스 베이츠!"], ["Bates—I will call upon her to-morrow, and take my children.—They are always so pleased to see my children.—And that excellent Miss Bates!—such thorough worthy people!—How are they, sir?”", "어쩌면 그렇게들 한결같이 갸륵한 분들인지! 그분들은 어떠세요, 아버지?\""]], [["“Why, pretty well, my dear, upon the whole.", "\"글쎄, 대체로는 그럭저럭 잘 지내신단다, 얘야."], ["But poor Mrs. Bates had a bad cold about a month ago.”", "하지만 가엾은 베이츠 부인이 한 달쯤 전에 고약한 감기에 걸리셨지.\""]], [["“How sorry I am!", "\"저런, 어쩌나!"], ["But colds were never so prevalent as they have been this autumn.", "하지만 감기가 올가을처럼 흔했던 적이 없었어요."], ["Mr. Wingfield told me that he has never known them more general or heavy—except when it has been quite an influenza.”", "미스터 윙필드 말로는, 거의 독감이 돌 때 말고는 이렇게 두루두루, 이렇게 심하게 걸리는 건 본 적이 없다고 하던걸요.\""]], [["“That has been a good deal the case, my dear; but not to the degree you mention.", "\"그런 면이 적잖아 있긴 했지, 얘야. 하지만 네가 말하는 정도까지는 아니란다."], ["Perry says that colds have been very general, but not so heavy as he has very often known them in November.", "페리 말로는 감기가 두루 돌긴 했어도, 십일월에 흔히 보던 만큼 심하지는 않다더구나."], ["Perry does not call it altogether a sickly season.”", "페리는 통틀어 병치레가 많은 철이라고는 하지 않더라.\""]], [["“No, I do not know that Mr. Wingfield considers it very sickly except—", "\"네, 미스터 윙필드도 딱히 그렇게까지 병치레가 많다고 여기는 것 같지는 않아요, 다만 —\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["my poor dear child, the truth is, that in London it is always a sickly season.", "가엾은 내 새끼, 사실은 런던이란 데가 늘 병치레가 많은 철이란 말이다."], ["Nobody is healthy in London, nobody can be.", "런던에서는 누구도 건강하지 못하고, 건강할 수가 없어."], ["It is a dreadful thing to have you forced to live there!", "너를 거기서 살게 하다니, 끔찍한 일이지! 그렇게 멀리서!"], ["so far off!—and the air so bad!”", "게다가 공기는 또 그렇게 나쁘고!\""]], [["“No, indeed—we are not at all in a bad air.", "\"아니에요, 정말이지 우리는 결코 나쁜 공기 속에 사는 게 아니에요."], ["Our part of London is very superior to most others!—You must not confound us with London in general, my dear sir.", "우리 동네는 런던에서도 다른 데보다 훨씬 좋은 곳인걸요! 아버지, 우리를 런던 전체랑 한데 묶어 생각하시면 안 돼요."], ["The neighbourhood of Brunswick Square is very different from almost all the rest.", "브런즈윅 스퀘어 일대는 다른 데 거의 전부와는 아주 다르거든요."], ["We are so very airy!", "우리 동네는 정말이지 바람이 잘 통해요!"], ["I should be unwilling, I own, to live in any other part of the town;—there is hardly any other that I could be satisfied to have my children in: but we are so remarkably airy!—Mr.", "솔직히 저는 런던의 다른 어디에서도 살고 싶지 않아요 — 우리 아이들을 두고 만족할 만한 곳이 그 밖엔 거의 없거든요. 하지만 우리 동네는 유난히 바람이 잘 통한답니다!"], ["Wingfield thinks the vicinity of Brunswick Square decidedly the most favourable as to air.”", "미스터 윙필드도 공기로 치면 브런즈윅 스퀘어 부근이 단연 가장 좋다고 여기는걸요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["my dear, it is not like Hartfield.", "얘야, 그래도 하트필드만은 못하지."], ["You make the best of it—but after you have been a week at Hartfield, you are all of you different creatures; you do not look like the same.", "너는 좋게만 말하지만 — 너희가 하트필드에서 한 주만 지내고 나면 다들 딴사람이 되거든. 도무지 같은 사람 같지가 않아."], ["Now I cannot say, that I think you are any of you looking well at present.”", "그런데 지금은, 너희 중 누구도 안색이 좋아 보인다고는 못 하겠구나.\""]], [["“I am sorry to hear you say so, sir; but I assure you, excepting those little nervous head-aches and palpitations which I am never entirely free from anywhere, I am quite well myself; and if the children were rather pale before they went to bed, it was only because they were a little more tired than usual, from their journey and the happiness of coming.", "\"그렇게 말씀하시니 마음이 안 좋네요, 아버지. 하지만 정말이지, 어딜 가든 영 떨쳐 내지 못하는 그 자잘한 신경성 두통과 가슴 두근거림만 빼면, 저는 아주 건강해요."], ["I hope you will think better of their looks to-morrow; for I assure you Mr.", "그리고 아이들이 잠들기 전에 좀 창백해 보였다면, 그건 그저 여행에다 여기 온 기쁨까지 더해 평소보다 좀 더 고단했던 탓일 뿐이에요."], ["Wingfield told me, that he did not believe he had ever sent us off altogether, in such good case.", "내일은 그 애들 안색을 더 좋게 봐 주시면 좋겠어요. 정말이지 미스터 윙필드가 그러는데, 우리를 이렇게 다들 좋은 상태로 떠나보낸 적은 없는 것 같다고 했거든요."], ["I trust, at least, that you do not think Mr. Knightley looking ill,”", "적어도 미스터 나이틀리만큼은 안색이 나쁘다고 여기지 않으시겠죠.\""], ["turning her eyes with affectionate anxiety towards her husband.", "그러면서 그녀는 애정 어린 근심을 담아 남편 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸다."]], [["“Middling, my dear; I cannot compliment you.", "\"그저 그렇구나, 얘야. 빈말로 추어줄 수가 없는걸."], ["I think Mr. John Knightley very far from looking well.”", "미스터 존 나이틀리는 안색이 좋은 것과는 한참 멀어 보인다.\""]], [["“What is the matter, sir?—Did you speak to me?”", "\"무슨 일입니까, 어르신? 저더러 하신 말씀인가요?\""], ["cried Mr. John Knightley, hearing his own name.", "자기 이름이 들리자 미스터 존 나이틀리가 외쳤다."]], [["“I am sorry to find, my love, that my father does not think you looking well—but I hope it is only from being a little fatigued.", "\"여보, 아버지께서 당신 안색이 좋아 보이지 않는다고 하셔서 마음이 안 좋네요 — 하지만 그저 좀 피곤한 탓이길 바라요."], ["I could have wished, however, as you know, that you had seen Mr. Wingfield before you left home.”", "그래도 알다시피, 집을 떠나기 전에 미스터 윙필드를 한번 봤더라면 싶었어요.\""]], [["“My dear Isabella,”—exclaimed he hastily—“pray do not concern yourself about my looks.", "\"이저벨라.\" 그가 황급히 외쳤다."], ["Be satisfied with doctoring and coddling yourself and the children, and let me look as I chuse.”", "\"제발 내 안색 따위는 걱정하지 마시오. 당신은 당신하고 아이들이나 약 먹이고 싸고도느라 만족하시고, 나는 내키는 대로 보이게 내버려 두구려.\""]], [["“I did not thoroughly understand what you were telling your brother,”", "\"형부가 미스터 나이틀리께 하시던 얘기를 제가 제대로 못 알아들었어요.\" 엠마가 외쳤다."], ["cried Emma, “about your friend Mr. Graham’s intending to have a bailiff from Scotland, to look after his new estate.", "\"형부 친구분 미스터 그레이엄이 새로 산 영지를 관리하려고 스코틀랜드에서 집사를 데려올 작정이라는 거 말이에요."], ["What will it answer?", "그게 잘될까요?"], ["Will not the old prejudice be too strong?”", "그 해묵은 편견이 너무 세지 않을까요?\""]], [["And she talked in this way so long and successfully that, when forced to give her attention again to her father and sister, she had nothing worse to hear than Isabella’s kind inquiry after Jane Fairfax; and Jane Fairfax, though no great favourite with her in general, she was at that moment very happy to assist in praising.", "그러고는 이런 식으로 어찌나 길게, 그리고 솜씨 좋게 이야기를 끌어갔던지, 다시 아버지와 언니 쪽으로 주의를 돌려야 했을 무렵에는, 이저벨라가 제인 페어팩스의 안부를 다정하게 묻는 것 말고는 더 고약한 이야기를 들을 일이 없었다. 그리고 제인 페어팩스로 말하자면, 평소에는 엠마가 그리 마음에 들어 하는 상대는 아니었으나, 그 순간만큼은 그녀를 함께 칭찬해 주는 일이 무척이나 반가웠다."]], [["“That sweet, amiable Jane Fairfax!” said Mrs.", "\"그 사랑스럽고 다정한 제인 페어팩스!\""], ["John Knightley.—“It is so long since I have seen her, except now and then for a moment accidentally in town!", "미시즈 존 나이틀리가 말했다. \"어쩌다 시내에서 잠깐 우연히 마주친 것 말고는, 그 애를 본 지 정말 오래됐어!"], ["What happiness it must be to her good old grandmother and excellent aunt, when she comes to visit them!", "그 애가 찾아가면 그 착하신 노할머니랑 훌륭하신 이모님은 얼마나 좋으실까!"], ["I always regret excessively on dear Emma’s account that she cannot be more at Highbury; but now their daughter is married, I suppose Colonel and Mrs.", "나는 우리 엠마를 봐서라도 그 애가 하이베리에 더 자주 못 있는 게 늘 너무너무 아쉬워. 하지만 이제 딸이 결혼했으니, 캠벨 대령 부부도 그 애를 도무지 떼어 놓지 못하실 거야."], ["Campbell will not be able to part with her at all. She would be such a delightful companion for Emma.”", "엠마한테 정말 더없이 즐거운 말동무가 될 텐데.\""]], [["Mr. Woodhouse agreed to it all, but added,", "미스터 우드하우스는 그 모든 말에 동의하면서도 한마디 덧붙였다."]], [["“Our little friend Harriet Smith, however, is just such another pretty kind of young person.", "\"하지만 우리 어린 벗 해리엇 스미스도 꼭 그만큼이나 예쁘장하고 참한 아가씨란다."], ["You will like Harriet.", "너도 해리엇을 좋아하게 될 게다."], ["Emma could not have a better companion than Harriet.”", "엠마한테 해리엇보다 더 나은 말동무는 없을 거야.\""]], [["“I am most happy to hear it—but only Jane Fairfax one knows to be so very accomplished and superior!—and exactly Emma’s age.”", "\"그 말을 들으니 정말 기뻐요 — 하지만 그렇게 더없이 교양 있고 빼어난 사람으로 알려진 건 제인 페어팩스뿐이잖아요! 게다가 나이도 엠마하고 꼭 같고요.\""]], [["This topic was discussed very happily, and others succeeded of similar moment, and passed away with similar harmony; but the evening did not close without a little return of agitation.", "이 화제는 무척 화기애애하게 논의되었고, 비슷한 정도의 다른 화제들이 뒤를 이어 비슷한 화목함 속에 흘러갔다. 그러나 그날 저녁은 작은 소동이 한 차례 되살아나는 일 없이는 끝나지 않았다."], ["The gruel came and supplied a great deal to be said—much praise and many comments—undoubting decision of its wholesomeness for every constitution, and pretty severe Philippics upon the many houses where it was never met with tolerably;—but, unfortunately, among the failures which the daughter had to instance, the most recent, and therefore most prominent, was in her own cook at South End, a young woman hired for the time, who never had been able to understand what she meant by a basin of nice smooth gruel, thin, but not too thin.", "죽이 나오자 할 말거리가 잔뜩 생겼던 것이다 — 갖은 찬사와 숱한 품평, 어떤 체질에도 이롭다는 일말의 의심 없는 단언, 그리고 그것을 변변하게 내놓는 법이 없는 여러 집에 대한 자못 매서운 성토 — 그러나 공교롭게도, 딸이 들 수 있는 실패 사례들 가운데 가장 최근의 것이라 그만큼 두드러진 것이, 다름 아닌 사우스엔드에서 둔 자기 집 요리사 이야기였다. 그 철에 잠깐 고용한 젊은 여자였는데, 묽되 너무 묽지는 않은, 곱고 매끈한 죽 한 사발이 무슨 말인지 도무지 알아듣질 못했다는 것이다."], ["Often as she had wished for and ordered it, she had never been able to get any thing tolerable.", "아무리 들고 싶어 청해도, 변변한 것을 한 번도 얻어 본 적이 없다고 했다."], ["Here was a dangerous opening.", "여기서 위험한 틈이 열렸다."]], [["“Ah!” said Mr.", "\"아!\""], ["Woodhouse, shaking his head and fixing his eyes on her with tender concern.—The ejaculation in Emma’s ear expressed, “Ah!", "미스터 우드하우스가 고개를 가로저으며 딱한 근심을 담아 딸에게 눈길을 고정한 채 내뱉었다. 엠마의 귀에는 그 탄식이 이렇게 들렸다."], ["there is no end of the sad consequences of your going to South End.", "\"아! 사우스엔드에 간 일로 인한 딱한 결과는 끝이 없구나."], ["It does not bear talking of.”", "입에 올리기조차 못할 일이야.\""], ["And for a little while she hoped he would not talk of it, and that a silent rumination might suffice to restore him to the relish of his own smooth gruel.", "그래서 그녀는 잠시나마 아버지가 그 일을 입에 올리지 않기를, 잠자코 곱씹는 것만으로 그가 제 매끈한 죽 맛을 다시 즐길 기분이 돌아오기를 바랐다."], ["After an interval of some minutes, however, he began with,", "그러나 몇 분쯤 사이를 두고 나서, 그는 이렇게 운을 뗐다."]], [["“I shall always be very sorry that you went to the sea this autumn, instead of coming here.”", "\"올가을에 여기 오지 않고 바다로 간 일은, 두고두고 못내 안타까울 게다.\""]], [["“But why should you be sorry, sir?—I assure you, it did the children a great deal of good.”", "\"하지만 왜 안타까워하세요, 아버지? 정말이지 그게 아이들한테 큰 도움이 됐는걸요.\""]], [["“And, moreover, if you must go to the sea, it had better not have been to South End.", "\"게다가 말이다, 꼭 바다로 가야 했다면, 사우스엔드만은 아니었어야 했어."], ["South End is an unhealthy place.", "사우스엔드는 건강에 안 좋은 곳이란다."], ["Perry was surprized to hear you had fixed upon South End.”", "페리도 네가 사우스엔드로 정했다는 말에 놀라더구나.\""]], [["“I know there is such an idea with many people, but indeed it is quite a mistake, sir.—We all had our health perfectly well there, never found the least inconvenience from the mud; and Mr.", "\"많은 사람이 그런 생각을 갖고 있다는 건 저도 알아요, 하지만 정말이지 그건 잘못된 생각이에요, 아버지. 우리는 거기서 다들 더없이 건강하게 지냈고, 진흙 때문에 조금도 불편한 적이 없었어요."], ["Wingfield says it is entirely a mistake to suppose the place unhealthy; and I am sure he may be depended on, for he thoroughly understands the nature of the air, and his own brother and family have been there repeatedly.”", "그리고 미스터 윙필드 말로는 그곳을 건강에 안 좋다고 여기는 건 완전히 잘못이라고 했어요. 그 사람 말은 믿을 만하다고 확신해요, 공기의 성질을 속속들이 아는 사람인 데다, 자기 친형 식구도 거기를 여러 번 다녀왔으니까요.\""]], [["“You should have gone to Cromer, my dear, if you went anywhere.—Perry was a week at Cromer once, and he holds it to be the best of all the sea-bathing places.", "\"어딜 가려거든, 크로머로 갔어야지, 얘야. 페리도 한번 크로머에서 한 주를 보냈는데, 해수욕장 중에 거기가 단연 최고라고 한단다."], ["A fine open sea, he says, and very pure air.", "탁 트인 멋진 바다에, 공기도 아주 맑다더구나."], ["And, by what I understand, you might have had lodgings there quite away from the sea—a quarter of a mile off—very comfortable.", "그리고 내가 듣기로는, 거기서는 바다랑 아주 떨어진 데에 — 사분의 일 마일쯤 떨어진 데에 — 아주 편안한 숙소를 잡을 수 있었다더라."], ["You should have consulted Perry.”", "페리한테 의논했어야 했어.\""]], [["“But, my dear sir, the difference of the journey;—only consider how great it would have been.—An hundred miles, perhaps, instead of forty.”", "\"하지만 아버지, 길의 차이가 있잖아요. 그게 얼마나 컸을지 한번 생각해 보세요. 사십 마일이 아니라, 어쩌면 백 마일이었을 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["my dear, as Perry says, where health is at stake, nothing else should be considered; and if one is to travel, there is not much to chuse between forty miles and an hundred.—Better not move at all, better stay in London altogether than travel forty miles to get into a worse air.", "얘야, 페리 말마따나, 건강이 걸린 일이라면 다른 건 따질 게 못 된단다. 그리고 기왕 길을 나서야 한다면, 사십 마일이나 백 마일이나 별 차이가 없는 거야."], ["This is just what Perry said.", "차라리 아예 움직이지 않는 게 낫지, 사십 마일을 가서 더 나쁜 공기 속에 들어가느니 그냥 런던에 죽 머무는 게 낫고말고."], ["It seemed to him a very ill-judged measure.”", "이게 바로 페리가 한 말이란다. 그 사람 보기엔 영 잘못 판단한 처사였던 게지.\""]], [["Emma’s attempts to stop her father had been vain; and when he had reached such a point as this, she could not wonder at her brother-in-law’s breaking out.", "아버지를 멈추려는 엠마의 시도는 헛수고였다. 그리고 그가 이런 지경에까지 이르고 보니, 형부가 발끈하고 나선 것도 그녀로서는 이상할 게 없었다."]], [["“Mr. Perry,”", "\"미스터 페리는 말이지요.\""], ["said he, in a voice of very strong displeasure, “would do as well to keep his opinion till it is asked for.", "그가 자못 못마땅한 목소리로 말했다. \"물어볼 때까지 제 의견을 잠자코 간직하는 편이 나을 겁니다."], ["Why does he make it any business of his, to wonder at what I do?—at my taking my family to one part of the coast or another?—I may be allowed, I hope, the use of my judgment as well as Mr.", "어째서 그 사람이 내가 하는 일을 두고 의아해하는 걸 제 일거리로 삼는 겁니까? 내가 식구를 해안의 이쪽으로 데려가든 저쪽으로 데려가든 말이지요."], ["Perry.—I want his directions no more than his drugs.”", "미스터 페리한테 그럴 자격이 있다면, 나한테도 내 판단을 쓸 자격쯤은 있을 줄 압니다."], ["He paused—and growing cooler in a moment, added, with only sarcastic dryness, “If Mr.", "나는 그 사람 약이 필요 없는 만큼이나 그 사람 지시도 필요 없어요.\""], ["Perry can tell me how to convey a wife and five children a distance of an hundred and thirty miles with no greater expense or inconvenience than a distance of forty, I should be as willing to prefer Cromer to South End as he could himself.”", "그는 말을 멈추었고 — 이내 좀 누그러져, 다만 비꼬는 듯한 무미건조함만을 담아 덧붙였다. \"미스터 페리가, 아내와 다섯 아이를 백삼십 마일이나 되는 거리로 사십 마일짜리 거리만큼의 비용과 수고만으로 옮기는 방도를 알려 줄 수 있다면야, 나도 그 사람 못지않게 기꺼이 사우스엔드보다 크로머를 택하겠습니다.\""]], [["“True, true,” cried Mr.", "\"맞아, 맞아.\""], ["Knightley, with most ready interposition—“very true.", "미스터 나이틀리가 더없이 잽싸게 끼어들며 외쳤다. \"정말 맞는 말일세."], ["That’s a consideration indeed.—But John, as to what I was telling you of my idea of moving the path to Langham, of turning it more to the right that it may not cut through the home meadows, I cannot conceive any difficulty.", "그건 정말 따져 볼 만한 점이지. 그런데 존, 내가 자네한테 들려주던 그 생각 말일세, 랭엄으로 가는 길을 옮기는 것 말이야 — 직영 목초지를 가로지르지 않도록 좀 더 오른쪽으로 트는 것 말일세, 나는 무슨 어려움이 있을지 도무지 모르겠어."], ["I should not attempt it, if it were to be the means of inconvenience to the Highbury people, but if you call to mind exactly the present line of the path....", "하이베리 사람들한테 불편이 되는 일이라면 시도조차 안 하겠지만, 자네가 지금 그 길이 난 자리를 정확히 떠올려 보면……"], ["The only way of proving it, however, will be to turn to our maps.", "하지만 그걸 따져 볼 유일한 길은 지도를 들여다보는 거겠지."], ["I shall see you at the Abbey to-morrow morning I hope, and then we will look them over, and you shall give me your opinion.”", "내일 아침 애비에서 자네를 볼 수 있으면 좋겠네. 그때 함께 지도를 살펴보고, 자네 의견을 들려주게나.\""]], [["Mr.", "미스터 우드하우스는 벗 페리를 향한 그토록 모진 비난에 적잖이 동요했으니, 사실 그는 자기도 모르는 사이에 자신의 숱한 감정과 표현을 그 페리의 입에 갖다 붙여 온 터였다."], ["Woodhouse was rather agitated by such harsh reflections on his friend Perry, to whom he had, in fact, though unconsciously, been attributing many of his own feelings and expressions;—but the soothing attentions of his daughters gradually removed the present evil, and the immediate alertness of one brother, and better recollections of the other, prevented any renewal of it.", "그러나 두 딸의 어르고 다독이는 보살핌이 차츰 눈앞의 화근을 걷어 냈고, 한 형제의 그 즉각적인 기민함과 다른 형제의 누그러진 마음이 그 일이 되살아나는 것을 막아 주었다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter XIII", "# 제1권 13장"]], [["There could hardly be a happier creature in the world than Mrs.", "미시즈 존 나이틀리는 이번 하트필드 방문 동안만큼은 세상에 둘도 없이 행복한 사람이었을 것이다."], ["John Knightley, in this short visit to Hartfield, going about every morning among her old acquaintance with her five children, and talking over what she had done every evening with her father and sister.", "아침마다 다섯 아이를 데리고 옛 친지들을 두루 찾아다니고, 저녁마다 그날 한 일을 아버지며 동생과 두루 이야기하며 지냈으니 말이다."], ["She had nothing to wish otherwise, but that the days did not pass so swiftly.", "그녀에게는 달리 바랄 것이 하나도 없었다 — 다만 날들이 이토록 빨리 흘러가지만 않았으면 했을 뿐."], ["It was a delightful visit;—perfect, in being much too short.", "더없이 즐거운 방문이었다. 너무 짧다는 점에서 완벽한 방문이었다."]], [["In general their evenings were less engaged with friends than their mornings; but one complete dinner engagement, and out of the house too, there was no avoiding, though at Christmas.", "대체로 그들의 저녁나절은 아침보다는 벗들과 어울리는 일이 적었다. 그러나 단 한 차례의 정식 만찬 약속만은 — 그것도 집을 떠나 가야 하는 약속이었는데 — 비록 성탄절이라 해도 피할 도리가 없었다."], ["Mr. Weston would take no denial; they must all dine at Randalls one day;—even Mr.", "미스터 웨스턴은 거절을 용납하지 않았다."], ["Woodhouse was persuaded to think it a possible thing in preference to a division of the party.", "다들 하루 날을 잡아 랜덜스에서 만찬을 들어야 했다. 미스터 우드하우스조차도, 일행이 둘로 갈라지는 것보다는 그쪽이 차라리 가능한 일이라고 여기도록 설득당하고 말았다."]], [["How they were all to be conveyed, he would have made a difficulty if he could, but as his son and daughter’s carriage and horses were actually at Hartfield, he was not able to make more than a simple question on that head; it hardly amounted to a doubt; nor did it occupy Emma long to convince him that they might in one of the carriages find room for Harriet also.", "다들 어떻게 가야 하느냐 하는 문제에서, 그는 트집을 잡을 수 있었다면 잡으려 들었을 것이다. 그러나 아들 내외의 마차와 말이 실제로 하트필드에 와 있었으니, 그 점에 관해서는 그저 간단한 물음 하나밖에 던지지 못했다. 그것은 의심이라고 할 것도 못 되었다. 게다가 마차 한 대에 해리엇이 탈 자리도 충분히 나리라는 것을 아버지에게 납득시키는 데에도 엠마는 오래 걸리지 않았다."]], [["Harriet, Mr. Elton, and Mr.", "해리엇과 미스터 엘턴, 그리고 미스터 나이틀리 — 그들만의 각별한 동아리 — 가 그들과 자리를 함께하도록 초대받은 사람의 전부였다."], ["Knightley, their own especial set, were the only persons invited to meet them;—the hours were to be early, as well as the numbers few; Mr.", "인원이 적은 만큼 시간도 일렀다."], ["Woodhouse’s habits and inclination being consulted in every thing.", "매사에 미스터 우드하우스의 습관과 취향이 고려되었던 것이다."]], [["The evening before this great event (for it was a very great event that Mr. Woodhouse should dine out, on the 24th of December) had been spent by Harriet at Hartfield, and she had gone home so much indisposed with a cold, that, but for her own earnest wish of being nursed by Mrs.", "이 큰 행사 전날 저녁(미스터 우드하우스가 십이월 이십사일에 밖에서 만찬을 든다는 것은 실로 대단한 행사였으니까)을 해리엇은 하트필드에서 보냈고, 감기 기운이 어찌나 심해진 채 집으로 돌아갔던지, 고더드 부인의 간호를 받고 싶다는 본인의 간곡한 소망만 아니었던들 엠마는 그녀를 집으로 보내지 못했을 것이다."], ["Goddard, Emma could not have allowed her to leave the house. Emma called on her the next day, and found her doom already signed with regard to Randalls.", "이튿날 엠마가 그녀를 찾아갔을 때, 랜덜스 일에 관한 한 그녀의 운명은 이미 결정 나 있었다."], ["She was very feverish and had a bad sore throat: Mrs.", "그녀는 열이 몹시 높았고 목이 심하게 부어 있었다."], ["Goddard was full of care and affection, Mr. Perry was talked of, and Harriet herself was too ill and low to resist the authority which excluded her from this delightful engagement, though she could not speak of her loss without many tears.", "고더드 부인은 근심과 애정으로 가득했고, 미스터 페리 이야기가 오갔으며, 해리엇 자신도 그 즐거운 약속에서 자기를 빼놓는 권위에 맞서기에는 너무 아프고 기운이 없었다 — 비록 그 상실을 이야기하면서 눈물을 한참 쏟지 않을 수 없었지만."]], [["Emma sat with her as long as she could, to attend her in Mrs. Goddard’s unavoidable absences, and raise her spirits by representing how much Mr.", "엠마는 고더드 부인이 자리를 비울 수밖에 없는 동안 해리엇을 돌봐 주려고 되도록 오래 곁에 앉아 있었고, 그녀의 상태를 알게 되면 미스터 엘턴이 얼마나 풀이 죽을지를 그려 보여 주며 기운을 북돋아 주었다."], ["Elton’s would be depressed when he knew her state; and left her at last tolerably comfortable, in the sweet dependence of his having a most comfortless visit, and of their all missing her very much.", "그러고는 마침내, 미스터 엘턴이 더없이 위안 없는 방문을 하게 되리라는, 그리고 모두가 그녀를 몹시 그리워하리라는 달콤한 기대에 기대게 하여, 그녀를 그럭저럭 편안한 상태로 두고 떠났다."], ["She had not advanced many yards from Mrs. Goddard’s door, when she was met by Mr.", "고더드 부인의 집 문에서 몇 걸음 채 나아가기도 전에, 그녀는 다름 아닌 미스터 엘턴과 마주쳤다."], ["Elton himself, evidently coming towards it, and as they walked on slowly together in conversation about the invalid—of whom he, on the rumour of considerable illness, had been going to inquire, that he might carry some report of her to Hartfield—they were overtaken by Mr.", "그는 분명 그 문을 향해 오고 있던 차였다. 두 사람이 환자에 대한 이야기를 나누며 천천히 함께 걷는 동안 — 그는 그녀가 꽤 심하게 앓는다는 소문을 듣고, 하트필드에 그 소식을 전할 수 있도록 안부를 물으러 가던 길이었다 — 미스터 존 나이틀리가 두 큰아들을 데리고 돈웰에서의 일과 같은 방문을 마치고 돌아오다 그들을 따라잡았다."], ["John Knightley returning from the daily visit to Donwell, with his two eldest boys, whose healthy, glowing faces shewed all the benefit of a country run, and seemed to ensure a quick despatch of the roast mutton and rice pudding they were hastening home for.", "시골을 한바탕 뛰어다닌 덕에 두 아이의 건강하고 발그레한 얼굴은 그 효험을 고스란히 드러내고 있었고, 부랴부랴 집으로 향하는 양고기 구이며 쌀 푸딩을 단숨에 해치우리라는 것을 예고하는 듯했다."], ["They joined company and proceeded together.", "그들은 한 무리가 되어 함께 나아갔다."], ["Emma was just describing the nature of her friend’s complaint;—“a throat very much inflamed, with a great deal of heat about her, a quick, low pulse, &c.", "엠마는 마침 자기 벗이 앓는 병의 성질을 설명하고 있었다 — \"목이 몹시 부었고, 온몸에 열이 대단하며, 맥은 빠르고 약하고,\" 등등."], ["and she was sorry to find from Mrs. Goddard that Harriet was liable to very bad sore-throats, and had often alarmed her with them.”", "\"그리고 해리엇이 목이 심하게 붓는 병에 잘 걸리는 체질이라 여러 번 자기를 놀라게 했다는 것을 고더드 부인에게서 듣고 마음이 안 좋았다\"는 것이었다."], ["Mr. Elton looked all alarm on the occasion, as he exclaimed,", "이 대목에서 미스터 엘턴은 더없이 놀란 얼굴로 외쳤다."]], [["“A sore-throat!—I hope not infectious.", "\"목이 부었다고요! 옮는 병이 아니어야 할 텐데요."], ["I hope not of a putrid infectious sort.", "부패성 전염병 같은 게 아니어야 할 텐데요."], ["Has Perry seen her?", "페리가 진찰했습니까?"], ["Indeed you should take care of yourself as well as of your friend.", "정말이지 벗뿐 아니라 본인 몸도 챙기셔야 합니다."], ["Let me entreat you to run no risks.", "부디 조금이라도 위험을 무릅쓰지 마시기를 간청드립니다."], ["Why does not Perry see her?”", "왜 페리가 진찰하지 않습니까?\""]], [["Emma, who was not really at all frightened herself, tranquillised this excess of apprehension by assurances of Mrs.", "사실 조금도 겁이 나지 않았던 엠마는, 고더드 부인의 경험과 보살핌을 들어 안심시킴으로써 이 지나친 염려를 가라앉혔다."], ["Goddard’s experience and care; but as there must still remain a degree of uneasiness which she could not wish to reason away, which she would rather feed and assist than not, she added soon afterwards—as if quite another subject,", "그러나 여전히 어느 정도의 불안은 남아 있을 수밖에 없었고, 그것을 그녀는 굳이 따져서 없애 주고 싶지 않았으며 — 차라리 부추기고 거들어 주고 싶었으므로 — 조금 뒤에 마치 전혀 다른 화제인 양 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“It is so cold, so very cold—and looks and feels so very much like snow, that if it were to any other place or with any other party, I should really try not to go out to-day—and dissuade my father from venturing; but as he has made up his mind, and does not seem to feel the cold himself, I do not like to interfere, as I know it would be so great a disappointment to Mr. and Mrs.", "\"날이 어찌나 추운지, 정말이지 추워요 — 보기에도 느낌에도 꼭 눈이 올 것만 같으니, 다른 곳에 가는 거라거나 다른 일행과 가는 거라면 저는 정말 오늘 외출하지 않으려고 애쓸 거예요 — 아버지께도 무리하지 마시라고 말리고요."], ["Weston. But, upon my word, Mr.", "하지만 아버지께서 이미 마음을 정하셨고, 정작 본인은 추위를 느끼지도 않으시는 것 같으니, 끼어들고 싶지가 않네요."], ["Elton, in your case, I should certainly excuse myself.", "그랬다간 미스터 웨스턴 내외분께 얼마나 큰 실망을 안겨 드릴지 잘 아니까요."], ["You appear to me a little hoarse already, and when you consider what demand of voice and what fatigues to-morrow will bring, I think it would be no more than common prudence to stay at home and take care of yourself to-night.”", "하지만 정말이지, 미스터 엘턴, 당신 같은 경우라면 저는 분명히 사양하겠어요. 제가 보기에 벌써 목이 조금 잠기신 것 같은데, 내일 어떤 목소리가 필요하고 또 얼마나 고단할지를 생각하면, 오늘 밤은 집에 머물며 몸을 돌보시는 게 그저 평범한 분별이 아닐까 싶어요.\""]], [["Mr.", "미스터 엘턴은 무어라 대답해야 좋을지 잘 모르겠다는 얼굴이었다."], ["Elton looked as if he did not very well know what answer to make; which was exactly the case; for though very much gratified by the kind care of such a fair lady, and not liking to resist any advice of her’s, he had not really the least inclination to give up the visit;—but Emma, too eager and busy in her own previous conceptions and views to hear him impartially, or see him with clear vision, was very well satisfied with his muttering acknowledgment of its being “very cold, certainly very cold,”", "실제로도 꼭 그러했다. 이토록 어여쁜 숙녀의 친절한 보살핌에 무척 흐뭇하고, 그녀의 어떤 조언도 거스르고 싶지 않았지만, 그렇다고 그 방문을 포기할 마음은 정말이지 조금도 없었던 것이다."], ["and walked on, rejoicing in having extricated him from Randalls, and secured him the power of sending to inquire after Harriet every hour of the evening.", "그러나 엠마는 자기가 미리 품어 둔 생각과 견해에 너무 골똘하고 분주한 나머지, 그를 공정하게 듣거나 또렷한 눈으로 보지 못했으므로, \"춥군요, 분명히 추워요\" 하고 우물거리는 그의 대답에 아주 흡족해하며, 그를 랜덜스에서 빼냈고 그가 저녁 내내 시간마다 해리엇의 안부를 물으러 사람을 보낼 여유를 확보해 주었다는 데 기뻐하며 걸음을 계속했다."]], [["“You do quite right,”", "\"아주 잘 생각하셨어요.\""], ["said she;—“we will make your apologies to Mr.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["and Mrs. Weston.”", "\"미스터 웨스턴 내외분께는 우리가 대신 사과를 전해 드릴게요.\""]], [["But hardly had she so spoken, when she found her brother was civilly offering a seat in his carriage, if the weather were Mr. Elton’s only objection, and Mr.", "그러나 그렇게 말하기가 무섭게, 그녀는 자기 형부가 정중하게도, 날씨만이 미스터 엘턴의 유일한 거리낌이라면 자기 마차에 한 자리를 내주겠다고 제안하는 것을, 그리고 미스터 엘턴이 실제로 흔쾌한 만족을 드러내며 그 제안을 덥석 받아들이는 것을 보게 되었다."], ["Elton actually accepting the offer with much prompt satisfaction.", "일은 그렇게 결정 나 버렸다."], ["It was a done thing; Mr. Elton was to go, and never had his broad handsome face expressed more pleasure than at this moment; never had his smile been stronger, nor his eyes more exulting than when he next looked at her.", "미스터 엘턴은 가게 되었고, 그 넓적하고 잘생긴 얼굴이 이 순간만큼 기쁨을 드러낸 적은 일찍이 없었으며, 다음 순간 그녀를 바라볼 때만큼 그 미소가 짙고 그 눈이 의기양양했던 적도 없었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"이런,\" 그녀는 속으로 생각했다."], ["said she to herself, “this is most strange!—After I had got him off so well, to chuse to go into company, and leave Harriet ill behind!—Most strange indeed!—But there is, I believe, in many men, especially single men, such an inclination—such a passion for dining out—a dinner engagement is so high in the class of their pleasures, their employments, their dignities, almost their duties, that any thing gives way to it—and this must be the case with Mr.", "\"참 별일도 다 있네! 애써 그렇게 잘 빼내 줬더니, 도리어 사람들 틈에 끼겠다고 골라잡고는 아픈 해리엇을 두고 가다니!"], ["Elton; a most valuable, amiable, pleasing young man undoubtedly, and very much in love with Harriet; but still, he cannot refuse an invitation, he must dine out wherever he is asked.", "정말이지 별일도 다 있어! 하긴 많은 남자들에게는, 특히 독신 남자들에게는 그런 성향이 — 밖에서 만찬을 드는 일에 대한 그런 열정이 있는가 봐."], ["What a strange thing love is!", "만찬 약속이라는 게 그들의 즐거움, 그들의 일과, 그들의 체면, 거의 그들의 의무 가운데서도 워낙 높은 자리를 차지하는지라, 그 앞에서는 무엇이든 양보하게 되는 거지 — 미스터 엘턴도 분명 그런 경우일 거야. 두말할 것 없이 더없이 귀하고 상냥하고 호감 가는 청년이고, 해리엇을 무척 사랑하지만 — 그래도 초대를 거절하지는 못하는 거고, 청을 받으면 어디든 가서 만찬을 들어야 하는 거지."], ["he can see ready wit in Harriet, but will not dine alone for her.”", "사랑이란 참 이상한 것이로구나! 해리엇의 영민한 재치는 알아보면서도, 정작 그녀를 위해서는 혼자 식사하는 일조차 못 하니 말이야.\""]], [["Soon afterwards Mr. Elton quitted them, and she could not but do him the justice of feeling that there was a great deal of sentiment in his manner of naming Harriet at parting; in the tone of his voice while assuring her that he should call at Mrs.", "곧 미스터 엘턴이 그들 곁을 떠났는데, 헤어질 무렵 해리엇을 입에 올리던 그의 태도에 적지 않은 진심이 담겨 있었음을 그녀는 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Goddard’s for news of her fair friend, the last thing before he prepared for the happiness of meeting her again, when he hoped to be able to give a better report; and he sighed and smiled himself off in a way that left the balance of approbation much in his favour.", "그가 어여쁜 벗의 소식을 알아보러 고더드 부인의 집에 들르겠노라고 — 그녀를 다시 만나는 행복을 준비하기 직전에 가장 마지막으로 할 일로 그러겠노라고, 그때는 더 나은 소식을 전할 수 있기를 바란다고 — 그녀에게 다짐할 때의 그 목소리의 어조에 말이다. 그러고서 그는 한숨을 쉬고 미소를 지으며 물러갔는데, 그 모양새가 호감의 저울추를 그에게 한껏 기울게 했다."]], [["After a few minutes of entire silence between them, John Knightley began with—", "두 사람 사이에 몇 분간 완전한 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 존 나이틀리가 입을 열었다."]], [["“I never in my life saw a man more intent on being agreeable than Mr. Elton.", "\"내 평생 미스터 엘턴만큼 호감을 사려고 애쓰는 사람은 본 적이 없습니다."], ["It is downright labour to him where ladies are concerned.", "숙녀들이 얽힌 자리에서는 그게 아예 노역 수준이지요."], ["With men he can be rational and unaffected, but when he has ladies to please, every feature works.”", "남자들끼리 있을 때는 사리에 밝고 꾸밈도 없는데, 비위를 맞춰야 할 숙녀가 있으면 얼굴의 근육 하나하나가 다 움직인다니까요.\""]], [["“Mr. Elton’s manners are not perfect,”", "\"미스터 엘턴의 태도가 완벽하지는 않죠.\""], ["replied Emma; “but where there is a wish to please, one ought to overlook, and one does overlook a great deal.", "엠마가 대답했다. \"하지만 호감을 사려는 마음이 있는 데서는, 누구든 많은 것을 너그러이 보아 넘겨야 마땅하고, 또 실제로 보아 넘기게 되는 법이에요."], ["Where a man does his best with only moderate powers, he will have the advantage over negligent superiority.", "어떤 사람이 그저 그만한 재간으로 최선을 다한다면, 무심한 우월함보다는 나은 평가를 받게 마련이죠."], ["There is such perfect good-temper and good-will in Mr. Elton as one cannot but value.”", "미스터 엘턴에게는 누구든 높이 사지 않을 수 없는 더없이 온화한 성품과 선의가 있어요.\""]], [["“Yes,” said Mr.", "\"그렇지요.\""], ["John Knightley presently, with some slyness, “he seems to have a great deal of good-will towards you.”", "미스터 존 나이틀리가 이윽고 짐짓 능청스럽게 말했다. \"처제한테 선의가 무척 넘쳐 보이긴 합디다.\""]], [["“Me!”", "\"저요!\""], ["she replied with a smile of astonishment, “are you imagining me to be Mr.", "그녀가 놀란 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다."], ["Elton’s object?”", "\"지금 저를 미스터 엘턴의 목표로 상상하고 계신 거예요?\""]], [["“Such an imagination has crossed me, I own, Emma; and if it never occurred to you before, you may as well take it into consideration now.”", "\"그런 상상이 머리를 스친 건 사실입니다, 엠마. 그리고 지금껏 처제 머릿속엔 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없다면, 이참에 한번 고려해 보는 것도 좋을 거예요.\""]], [["“Mr.", "\"미스터 엘턴이 저를 사랑한다니!"], ["Elton in love with me!—What an idea!”", "별 생각을 다 하시네요!\""]], [["“I do not say it is so; but you will do well to consider whether it is so or not, and to regulate your behaviour accordingly.", "\"그렇다고 단정하는 건 아닙니다. 다만 그런지 아닌지를 잘 헤아려 보고, 거기에 맞게 처신하는 게 좋을 거란 말이지요."], ["I think your manners to him encouraging.", "내가 보기엔 처제가 그 사람한테 하는 태도가 그를 부추기는 데가 있어요."], ["I speak as a friend, Emma.", "벗으로서 하는 말입니다, 엠마."], ["You had better look about you, and ascertain what you do, and what you mean to do.”", "주위를 잘 살펴서, 처제가 무얼 하고 있고 무얼 하려는지 똑똑히 알아 두는 게 좋을 거예요.\""]], [["“I thank you; but I assure you you are quite mistaken.", "\"고맙긴 한데요, 정말이지 형부가 완전히 잘못 짚으셨어요."], ["Mr. Elton and I are very good friends, and nothing more;”", "미스터 엘턴과 저는 아주 좋은 친구일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니에요.\""], ["and she walked on, amusing herself in the consideration of the blunders which often arise from a partial knowledge of circumstances, of the mistakes which people of high pretensions to judgment are for ever falling into; and not very well pleased with her brother for imagining her blind and ignorant, and in want of counsel.", "그러고서 그녀는, 사정을 어설피 아는 데서 흔히 빚어지는 헛다리짚음을, 판단력에 자부심이 높은 사람들이 언제나 빠지고 마는 오류를 곰곰 따져 보며 혼자 즐거워하면서 걸음을 계속했다. 그리고 자기를 눈멀고 무지하여 충고가 필요한 사람으로 여기는 형부에게 그리 흡족하지 않았다."], ["He said no more.", "그는 더는 말하지 않았다."]], [["Mr.", "미스터 우드하우스는 이 방문에 어찌나 단단히 마음을 굳혀 두었던지, 점점 심해지는 추위에도 거기서 물러설 생각이 도무지 없는 듯했고, 마침내 더없이 정확한 시각에 큰딸과 함께 자기 마차로 길을 나섰다."], ["Woodhouse had so completely made up his mind to the visit, that in spite of the increasing coldness, he seemed to have no idea of shrinking from it, and set forward at last most punctually with his eldest daughter in his own carriage, with less apparent consciousness of the weather than either of the others; too full of the wonder of his own going, and the pleasure it was to afford at Randalls to see that it was cold, and too well wrapt up to feel it.", "날씨에 대한 의식도 다른 두 사람보다 겉으로는 덜해 보였다. 자기가 외출한다는 사실의 경이로움과, 랜덜스에 가져다줄 기쁨에 너무 들떠 추운 줄도 몰랐고, 또 워낙 단단히 싸맨 터라 추위를 느끼지도 못했던 것이다."], ["The cold, however, was severe; and by the time the second carriage was in motion, a few flakes of snow were finding their way down, and the sky had the appearance of being so overcharged as to want only a milder air to produce a very white world in a very short time.", "그러나 추위는 매서웠다. 두 번째 마차가 움직일 무렵에는 눈송이 몇 점이 길을 찾아 내려오고 있었고, 하늘은 잔뜩 무겁게 내려앉아, 공기가 조금만 누그러지면 순식간에 온 세상을 새하얗게 만들 태세였다."]], [["Emma soon saw that her companion was not in the happiest humour.", "엠마는 곧 자기 동행이 그리 흐뭇한 기분이 아님을 알아챘다."], ["The preparing and the going abroad in such weather, with the sacrifice of his children after dinner, were evils, were disagreeables at least, which Mr.", "이런 날씨에 채비를 하고 밖으로 나서야 한다는 것은, 게다가 만찬 뒤에는 아이들을 두고 와야 한다는 희생까지 더해져, 미스터 존 나이틀리가 결코 좋아할 수 없는 고역이요, 적어도 못마땅한 일이었다."], ["John Knightley did not by any means like; he anticipated nothing in the visit that could be at all worth the purchase; and the whole of their drive to the vicarage was spent by him in expressing his discontent.", "그는 이 방문에서 그 대가를 치를 만한 가치가 있을 일이라곤 조금도 기대하지 않았다. 그리하여 목사관까지 가는 마차 길 내내 그는 자기 불만을 늘어놓는 데 시간을 다 썼다."]], [["“A man,” said he, “must have a very good opinion of himself when he asks people to leave their own fireside, and encounter such a day as this, for the sake of coming to see him. He must think himself a most agreeable fellow; I could not do such a thing. It is the greatest absurdity—Actually snowing at this moment!—The folly of not allowing people to be comfortable at home—and the folly of people’s not staying comfortably at home when they can! If we were obliged to go out such an evening as this, by any call of duty or business, what a hardship we should deem it;—and here are we, probably with rather thinner clothing than usual, setting forward voluntarily, without excuse, in defiance of the voice of nature, which tells man, in every thing given to his view or his feelings, to stay at home himself, and keep all under shelter that he can;—here are we setting forward to spend five dull hours in another man’s house, with nothing to say or to hear that was not said and heard yesterday, and may not be said and heard again to-morrow. Going in dismal weather, to return probably in worse;—four horses and four servants taken out for nothing but to convey five idle, shivering creatures into colder rooms and worse company than they might have had at home.”", "\"사람이,\" 그가 말했다. \"남더러 제 집 난롯가를 떠나서 이런 날을 무릅쓰고 자기를 보러 오라고 청할 때는, 자기 자신을 어지간히도 대단하게 여기는 게 분명합니다. 자기가 더없이 호감 가는 사람이라고 생각하는 거지요. 나라면 그런 짓은 못 합니다. 이런 어처구니없는 일이 또 어디 있어요 — 바로 지금 이 순간에도 눈이 오고 있잖습니까! 사람들이 집에서 편히 지내도록 두지 않는 어리석음 — 그리고 편히 집에 있을 수 있는데도 그러지 못하는 사람들의 어리석음이라니! 만약 우리가 어떤 의무나 볼일 때문에 이런 저녁에 나가야만 한다면, 우리는 그걸 얼마나 큰 고역으로 여기겠습니까. 그런데 보세요, 평소보다 도리어 얇은 옷을 걸친 채로, 자청해서, 변명거리도 없이, 자연의 목소리를 거스르며 길을 나서고 있지 않습니까. 자연은 사람의 눈과 마음에 비치는 온갖 것을 통해, 제 집에 머물고 거둘 수 있는 모든 것을 지붕 아래 거두라고 일러 주는데 말입니다. 그런데 우리는 이렇게 길을 나서서, 남의 집에서 따분한 다섯 시간을 보내려 하고 있어요. 어제 주고받은 말, 또 내일도 주고받을 말 말고는 할 말도 들을 말도 없는 그런 자리를요. 음울한 날씨에 갔다가, 아마도 더 고약한 날씨에 돌아오겠지요 — 말 네 마리와 하인 넷을 끌어내는 게, 한가하고 추위에 떠는 다섯 사람을, 집에 있었으면 누렸을 것보다 더 추운 방과 더 못한 자리로 실어 나르는 일밖에 더 됩니까.\""]], [["Emma did not find herself equal to give the pleased assent, which no doubt he was in the habit of receiving, to emulate the “Very true, my love,”", "엠마는, 그가 으레 받아 왔을 흐뭇한 맞장구를 — 늘 그의 길동무가 베풀어 주었을 \"정말 맞아요, 여보\""], ["which must have been usually administered by his travelling companion; but she had resolution enough to refrain from making any answer at all.", "같은 말을 — 흉내 낼 마음이 도무지 들지 않았다. 그러나 아예 아무 대답도 하지 않을 만큼의 결단력은 있었다."], ["She could not be complying, she dreaded being quarrelsome; her heroism reached only to silence.", "맞장구를 칠 수도 없었고, 그렇다고 다투는 것은 무서웠으니, 그녀의 영웅적 기개는 침묵에까지밖에 미치지 못했다."], ["She allowed him to talk, and arranged the glasses, and wrapped herself up, without opening her lips.", "그녀는 그가 떠들도록 내버려 두고, 유리창을 매만지고 몸을 단단히 싸맸을 뿐, 입술 한 번 떼지 않았다."]], [["They arrived, the carriage turned, the step was let down, and Mr. Elton, spruce, black, and smiling, was with them instantly.", "마차가 닿고, 방향을 틀고, 발판이 내려지자, 말쑥하고 검은 차림에 미소 띤 미스터 엘턴이 곧장 그들 앞에 와 있었다."], ["Emma thought with pleasure of some change of subject. Mr.", "엠마는 화제가 좀 바뀌겠구나 싶어 반가웠다."], ["Elton was all obligation and cheerfulness; he was so very cheerful in his civilities indeed, that she began to think he must have received a different account of Harriet from what had reached her.", "미스터 엘턴은 온통 정중함과 쾌활함 그 자체였다. 어찌나 쾌활하게 예를 차리던지, 그녀는 그가 자기에게 전해진 것과는 다른 해리엇의 소식을 들은 게 아닐까 생각하기 시작했다."], ["She had sent while dressing, and the answer had been, “Much the same—not better.”", "그녀는 옷을 갈아입는 동안 사람을 보내 물었는데, 돌아온 대답은 \"별 차도 없음 — 나아지지 않음\"이었던 것이다."]], [["“My report from Mrs. Goddard’s,”", "\"제가 고더드 부인 댁에서 받은 소식은,\""], ["said she presently, “was not so pleasant as I had hoped—‘Not better’ was my answer.”", "그녀가 이윽고 말했다. \"바라던 것만큼 좋진 않았어요 — '나아지지 않았다'는 게 제가 받은 대답이었죠.\""]], [["His face lengthened immediately; and his voice was the voice of sentiment as he answered.", "그의 얼굴은 즉시 길쭉해졌고, 대답하는 그의 목소리는 진정 어린 목소리였다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["no—I am grieved to find—I was on the point of telling you that when I called at Mrs.", "아니 — 그 소식을 들으니 마음이 아픕니다 — 마침 말씀드리려던 참이었어요."], ["Goddard’s door, which I did the very last thing before I returned to dress, I was told that Miss Smith was not better, by no means better, rather worse.", "제가 옷을 갈아입으러 돌아가기 직전 가장 마지막 일로 고더드 부인 댁 문에 들렀더니, 미스 스미스가 나아지지 않았다고, 도무지 차도가 없을뿐더러 오히려 더 나빠졌다고 하더군요."], ["Very much grieved and concerned—I had flattered myself that she must be better after such a cordial as I knew had been given her in the morning.”", "정말이지 슬프고 걱정스러워요 — 아침에 들었다시피 그렇게 좋은 강심제를 들었으니 분명 좀 나아졌으리라고 혼자 기대하고 있었거든요.\""]], [["Emma smiled and answered—“My visit was of use to the nervous part of her complaint, I hope; but not even I can charm away a sore throat; it is a most severe cold indeed.", "엠마는 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"제 방문이 그 병의 신경 쪽에는 도움이 됐기를 바라요."], ["Mr.", "하지만 저라 한들 부은 목을 마법으로 낫게 해 줄 수는 없죠."], ["Perry has been with her, as you probably heard.”", "정말 지독한 감기예요. 미스터 페리가 다녀갔어요, 들으셨겠지만.\""]], [["“Yes—I imagined—that is—I did not—”", "\"네 — 그런 줄 알았습니다 — 그러니까 — 사실은 못 들었지만 —\""]], [["“He has been used to her in these complaints, and I hope to-morrow morning will bring us both a more comfortable report.", "\"미스터 페리는 해리엇의 그런 증상에 익숙하니까, 내일 아침이면 우리 둘 다 더 마음 놓을 소식을 받게 되길 바라요."], ["But it is impossible not to feel uneasiness.", "하지만 불안하지 않을 수가 없네요."], ["Such a sad loss to our party to-day!”", "오늘 우리 모임에 이렇게 안타까운 빈자리라니!\""]], [["“Dreadful!—Exactly so, indeed.—She will be missed every moment.”", "\"끔찍하지요! 정말이지 꼭 그렇습니다. 매 순간 그녀가 아쉬울 거예요.\""]], [["This was very proper; the sigh which accompanied it was really estimable; but it should have lasted longer.", "여기까지는 아주 온당했다. 거기 곁들인 한숨도 진정 가상한 것이었다."], ["Emma was rather in dismay when only half a minute afterwards he began to speak of other things, and in a voice of the greatest alacrity and enjoyment.", "그러나 그것은 좀 더 오래 갔어야 했다. 겨우 삼십 초 뒤에 그가 다른 이야기를, 그것도 더없이 활기차고 흥겨운 목소리로 꺼내기 시작했을 때, 엠마는 어지간히 당황하고 말았다."]], [["“What an excellent device,”", "\"마차에 양가죽을 쓰다니,\""], ["said he, “the use of a sheepskin for carriages.", "그가 말했다. \"이 얼마나 기막힌 고안입니까."], ["How very comfortable they make it;—impossible to feel cold with such precautions.", "얼마나 편안하게 해 주는지요. 이렇게 대비를 해 두면 추위라곤 느낄 수가 없어요."], ["The contrivances of modern days indeed have rendered a gentleman’s carriage perfectly complete.", "정말이지 요즘의 고안들은 신사의 마차를 더없이 완벽하게 만들어 놓았어요."], ["One is so fenced and guarded from the weather, that not a breath of air can find its way unpermitted.", "날씨로부터 어찌나 단단히 막아 주고 지켜 주는지, 허락 없이는 바람 한 줄기도 비집고 들어올 수가 없습니다."], ["Weather becomes absolutely of no consequence.", "날씨가 아예 아무 상관도 없어지는 거지요."], ["It is a very cold afternoon—but in this carriage we know nothing of the matter.—Ha!", "무척 추운 오후입니다만 — 이 마차 안에서는 그런 걸 도통 모르고 있잖아요. 허!"], ["snows a little I see.”", "눈이 좀 오는군요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["said John Knightley, “and I think we shall have a good deal of it.”", "존 나이틀리가 말했다. \"꽤 많이 올 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Christmas weather,”", "\"성탄절 날씨답군요.\""], ["observed Mr. Elton.", "미스터 엘턴이 말했다."], ["“Quite seasonable; and extremely fortunate we may think ourselves that it did not begin yesterday, and prevent this day’s party, which it might very possibly have done, for Mr.", "\"딱 제철에 어울리지요. 그리고 어제 시작되어서 오늘 모임을 막아 버리지 않은 게 우리로서는 더없는 행운이라고 여길 만합니다."], ["Woodhouse would hardly have ventured had there been much snow on the ground; but now it is of no consequence.", "어제 시작됐더라면 충분히 그럴 수도 있었지요. 땅에 눈이 많이 쌓였더라면 미스터 우드하우스가 좀처럼 길을 나서려 하지 않으셨을 테니까요."], ["This is quite the season indeed for friendly meetings.", "하지만 이제는 아무 상관 없습니다. 지금이야말로 정녕 정다운 모임의 계절이지요."], ["At Christmas every body invites their friends about them, and people think little of even the worst weather.", "성탄절이면 누구나 벗들을 곁으로 불러들이고, 아무리 고약한 날씨라도 대수롭잖게 여기는 법입니다."], ["I was snowed up at a friend’s house once for a week.", "저는 언젠가 한 친구 집에서 일주일이나 눈에 갇힌 적이 있었지요."], ["Nothing could be pleasanter.", "그보다 더 즐거운 일이 없었습니다."], ["I went for only one night, and could not get away till that very day se’nnight.”", "하룻밤만 묵을 작정으로 갔는데, 꼭 그날로부터 이레째가 되어서야 빠져나올 수 있었어요.\""]], [["Mr. John Knightley looked as if he did not comprehend the pleasure, but said only, coolly,", "미스터 존 나이틀리는 그 즐거움을 도무지 이해하지 못하겠다는 얼굴이었지만, 그저 쌀쌀맞게 한마디만 했다."]], [["“I cannot wish to be snowed up a week at Randalls.”", "\"나는 랜덜스에서 일주일씩 눈에 갇히고 싶은 마음은 없군요.\""]], [["At another time Emma might have been amused, but she was too much astonished now at Mr. Elton’s spirits for other feelings.", "다른 때 같았으면 엠마도 재미있어했겠지만, 지금은 미스터 엘턴의 들뜬 기분에 너무 놀란 나머지 다른 감정을 느낄 겨를이 없었다."], ["Harriet seemed quite forgotten in the expectation of a pleasant party.", "즐거운 모임에 대한 기대 속에서 해리엇은 까맣게 잊힌 듯했다."]], [["“We are sure of excellent fires,”", "\"틀림없이 훌륭한 난롯불이 있을 거고,\""], ["continued he, “and every thing in the greatest comfort.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["Charming people, Mr.", "\"무엇 하나 더없이 안락할 겁니다."], ["and Mrs. Weston;—Mrs.", "매력적인 분들이지요, 미스터 웨스턴 내외분은."], ["Weston indeed is much beyond praise, and he is exactly what one values, so hospitable, and so fond of society;—it will be a small party, but where small parties are select, they are perhaps the most agreeable of any.", "웨스턴 부인은 정말이지 칭찬으로는 모자란 분이고, 그분은 또 누구나 귀히 여길 만한 그런 분이에요. 그렇게 손님 대접에 후하고, 또 그렇게 사람들과 어울리기를 좋아하시니까요."], ["Mr. Weston’s dining-room does not accommodate more than ten comfortably; and for my part, I would rather, under such circumstances, fall short by two than exceed by two.", "작은 모임이 되겠지만, 작은 모임이 엄선된 사람들로 이루어졌을 때는 아마 어떤 모임보다도 더 즐거운 법이지요."], ["I think you will agree with me, (turning with a soft air to Emma,) I think I shall certainly have your approbation, though Mr.", "미스터 웨스턴의 식당은 열 명을 넘기면 편안하게 들이지 못하는데, 저로서는 이런 경우에 둘이 넘치느니 차라리 둘이 모자라는 편이 낫습니다. 제 생각에 동의하시겠지요.\""], ["Knightley perhaps, from being used to the large parties of London, may not quite enter into our feelings.”", "(엠마를 향해 부드러운 태도로 돌아서며) \"분명 제 편을 들어 주시리라 믿어요. 미스터 나이틀리는 런던의 큰 모임들에 익숙하신지라 우리 마음을 썩 잘 이해하지 못하실지도 모르지만요.\""]], [["“I know nothing of the large parties of London, sir—I never dine with any body.”", "\"저는 런던의 큰 모임 같은 건 전혀 모릅니다, 미스터 엘턴 — 누구와도 만찬을 들지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“Indeed!", "\"정말입니까!\""], ["(in a tone of wonder and pity,) I had no idea that the law had been so great a slavery.", "(놀라움과 동정이 섞인 어조로) \"법조계 일이 그렇게나 큰 굴레인 줄은 몰랐습니다."], ["Well, sir, the time must come when you will be paid for all this, when you will have little labour and great enjoyment.”", "그래도 미스터 나이틀리, 이 모든 수고에 보답을 받으실 때가 분명 올 겁니다. 일은 적고 즐거움은 많아질 날이 말이지요.\""]], [["“My first enjoyment,”", "\"제 첫 번째 즐거움은,\""], ["replied John Knightley, as they passed through the sweep-gate, “will be to find myself safe at Hartfield again.”", "마차가 진입로 문을 지날 때 존 나이틀리가 대답했다. \"하트필드에 무사히 다시 돌아와 있는 걸 보는 일이 될 겁니다.\""]], [["# Volume I — Chapter XIV", "# 제1권 14장"]], [["Some change of countenance was necessary for each gentleman as they walked into Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인의 응접실로 들어서면서 두 신사는 각기 얼굴빛을 조금씩 고쳐야 했다 — 미스터 엘턴은 들뜬 표정을 가라앉혀야 했고, 미스터 존 나이틀리는 언짢은 기색을 흩어 버려야 했다."], ["Weston’s drawing-room;—Mr. Elton must compose his joyous looks, and Mr.", "미스터 엘턴은 웃음을 줄여야 했고, 미스터 존 나이틀리는 웃음을 늘려야 그 자리에 어울릴 수 있었다."], ["John Knightley disperse his ill-humour.", "엠마만이 천성이 이끄는 대로 굴 수 있었고, 자신이 행복한 그대로 행복해 보일 수 있었다."], ["Mr. Elton must smile less, and Mr.", "그녀에게 웨스턴 부부와 함께 있는 것은 진정한 즐거움이었다."], ["John Knightley more, to fit them for the place.—Emma only might be as nature prompted, and shew herself just as happy as she was. To her it was real enjoyment to be with the Westons.", "미스터 웨스턴은 더없이 좋아하는 사람이었고, 그의 아내만큼 거리낌 없이 이야기를 나눌 수 있는 상대는 이 세상 어디에도 없었다."], ["Mr. Weston was a great favourite, and there was not a creature in the world to whom she spoke with such unreserve, as to his wife; not any one, to whom she related with such conviction of being listened to and understood, of being always interesting and always intelligible, the little affairs, arrangements, perplexities, and pleasures of her father and herself.", "자신과 아버지의 자잘한 일들, 살림의 짜임새, 고민거리며 즐거움을 — 늘 귀 기울여 들어 주고 이해해 주며, 언제나 흥미로워하고 언제나 알아들어 주리라는 확신을 가지고 털어놓을 수 있는 사람은 그녀 말고는 없었다."], ["She could tell nothing of Hartfield, in which Mrs. Weston had not a lively concern; and half an hour’s uninterrupted communication of all those little matters on which the daily happiness of private life depends, was one of the first gratifications of each.", "하트필드의 어떤 이야기를 꺼내든 웨스턴 부인이 진심으로 마음 써 주지 않는 것이 없었으니, 사사로운 삶의 나날의 행복이 달려 있는 그 온갖 자잘한 일들을 반 시간 동안 끊김 없이 주고받는 것이야말로 두 사람 모두에게 으뜸가는 기쁨 가운데 하나였다."]], [["This was a pleasure which perhaps the whole day’s visit might not afford, which certainly did not belong to the present half-hour; but the very sight of Mrs.", "이는 어쩌면 하루 종일의 방문으로도 누리지 못할 즐거움이었고, 지금 이 반 시간에는 분명 허락되지 않을 즐거움이었다."], ["Weston, her smile, her touch, her voice was grateful to Emma, and she determined to think as little as possible of Mr. Elton’s oddities, or of any thing else unpleasant, and enjoy all that was enjoyable to the utmost.", "그러나 웨스턴 부인을 보는 것만으로도, 그 미소와 손길과 목소리만으로도 엠마는 흐뭇했고, 미스터 엘턴의 별난 행동이나 그 밖의 거슬리는 일은 되도록 적게 생각하며 즐길 수 있는 것을 한껏 즐기리라 마음먹었다."]], [["The misfortune of Harriet’s cold had been pretty well gone through before her arrival. Mr.", "해리엇이 감기에 걸렸다는 불운에 대해서는 그녀가 도착하기 전에 이미 꽤 충분히 다루어진 뒤였다."], ["Woodhouse had been safely seated long enough to give the history of it, besides all the history of his own and Isabella’s coming, and of Emma’s being to follow, and had indeed just got to the end of his satisfaction that James should come and see his daughter, when the others appeared, and Mrs.", "미스터 우드하우스는 진작 자리를 잡고 앉아 그 내력을 다 풀어 놓았고, 그뿐 아니라 자신과 이저벨라가 오게 된 사연, 엠마가 뒤따라오기로 한 일까지 죄다 늘어놓았으며, 제임스가 건너와 제 딸을 보게 된다는 사실에 흐뭇해하던 이야기를 막 끝맺던 참에 다른 사람들이 들어섰다."], ["Weston, who had been almost wholly engrossed by her attentions to him, was able to turn away and welcome her dear Emma.", "그동안 거의 온통 그를 살피는 일에만 매달려 있던 웨스턴 부인은 그제야 몸을 돌려 사랑하는 엠마를 맞이할 수 있었다."]], [["Emma’s project of forgetting Mr. Elton for a while made her rather sorry to find, when they had all taken their places, that he was close to her.", "한동안 미스터 엘턴을 잊어 보자던 엠마의 계획은, 모두가 자리를 잡고 나서 보니 그가 바로 옆에 앉아 있다는 것을 알게 되어 적잖이 언짢았다."], ["The difficulty was great of driving his strange insensibility towards Harriet, from her mind, while he not only sat at her elbow, but was continually obtruding his happy countenance on her notice, and solicitously addressing her upon every occasion.", "그가 옆자리에 앉아 있을 뿐 아니라, 행복한 얼굴을 끊임없이 그녀의 눈앞에 들이밀고, 무슨 일이 있을 때마다 살뜰하게 말을 걸어 오니, 해리엇을 향한 그 기이한 무심함을 머릿속에서 몰아내기란 여간 어려운 일이 아니었다."], ["Instead of forgetting him, his behaviour was such that she could not avoid the internal suggestion of “Can it really be as my brother imagined?", "그를 잊기는커녕, 그의 행동거지가 어찌나 그러한지 그녀는 속으로 이런 생각이 드는 것을 어쩌지 못했다. \"정말로 형부가 짐작한 그대로일까?"], ["can it be possible for this man to be beginning to transfer his affections from Harriet to me?—Absurd and insufferable!”—Yet he would be so anxious for her being perfectly warm, would be so interested about her father, and so delighted with Mrs.", "이 사람이 해리엇에게 두었던 마음을 나에게로 옮기기 시작했단 말인가? — 터무니없고 견딜 수 없는 일이야!\""], ["Weston; and at last would begin admiring her drawings with so much zeal and so little knowledge as seemed terribly like a would-be lover, and made it some effort with her to preserve her good manners.", "그런데도 그는 그녀가 조금이라도 추울세라 안달하고, 그녀의 아버지에 대해 그토록 관심을 보이며, 웨스턴 부인에게 그토록 기뻐하더니, 급기야는 그녀의 그림을 어찌나 열을 올려 — 그러면서도 어찌나 아는 것 없이 — 칭찬하기 시작하던지, 영락없이 구애하려 드는 사람 같아서, 그녀로서는 예의를 지키느라 적잖이 애를 써야 했다."], ["For her own sake she could not be rude; and for Harriet’s, in the hope that all would yet turn out right, she was even positively civil; but it was an effort; especially as something was going on amongst the others, in the most overpowering period of Mr.", "자기 체면을 위해서라도 무례하게 굴 수는 없었고, 해리엇을 위해서는 그래도 모든 일이 잘 풀리리라는 바람에서 도리어 분명하게 정중히 굴기까지 했다. 그러나 그것은 안간힘이었다."], ["Elton’s nonsense, which she particularly wished to listen to. She heard enough to know that Mr.", "더구나 미스터 엘턴의 부질없는 수작이 가장 견디기 힘들게 이어지던 바로 그 순간에, 다른 사람들 사이에서 그녀가 유독 귀 기울이고 싶은 무언가가 오가고 있었으니 말이다."], ["Weston was giving some information about his son; she heard the words “my son,” and “Frank,”", "미스터 웨스턴이 자기 아들에 대한 무슨 소식을 전하고 있다는 것은 알아들을 만큼은 들었다."], ["and “my son,” repeated several times over; and, from a few other half-syllables very much suspected that he was announcing an early visit from his son; but before she could quiet Mr.", "\"우리 아들\"이며 \"프랭크\"며 \"우리 아들\"이라는 말이 몇 번이고 되풀이되는 것을 들었고, 띄엄띄엄 들린 다른 몇 마디 토막으로 미루어, 아들이 머지않아 찾아오리라는 것을 알리는 중이라고 강하게 짐작했다."], ["Elton, the subject was so completely past that any reviving question from her would have been awkward.", "그러나 미스터 엘턴을 잠재우기도 전에 그 화제는 어찌나 말끔히 지나가 버렸는지, 그녀가 다시 끄집어내려고 무슨 질문을 던진다면 어색하기 짝이 없을 노릇이었다."]], [["Now, it so happened that in spite of Emma’s resolution of never marrying, there was something in the name, in the idea of Mr.", "그런데 마침 이런 일이 있었다. 결코 결혼하지 않으리라는 엠마의 결심에도 불구하고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠이라는 이름에는, 그를 떠올린다는 것 자체에는 늘 그녀의 마음을 끄는 무언가가 있었던 것이다."], ["Frank Churchill, which always interested her.", "그녀는 — 특히 그의 아버지가 미스 테일러와 결혼한 뒤로 — 자주 이런 생각을 하곤 했다."], ["She had frequently thought—especially since his father’s marriage with Miss Taylor—that if she were to marry, he was the very person to suit her in age, character and condition.", "만일 자신이 결혼이란 걸 하게 된다면, 나이며 성품이며 처지로 보아 그야말로 자기에게 딱 맞는 사람이 바로 그 사람이라고. 두 집안 사이의 이 인연으로 보아, 그는 어쩐지 온전히 그녀에게 속한 사람 같았다."], ["He seemed by this connexion between the families, quite to belong to her.", "두 사람을 아는 이라면 누구나 이 혼사를 떠올릴 수밖에 없으리라고 그녀는 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She could not but suppose it to be a match that every body who knew them must think of. That Mr.", "웨스턴 부부가 그 생각을 하고 있다는 것은 아주 강하게 확신하던 바였다."], ["and Mrs. Weston did think of it, she was very strongly persuaded; and though not meaning to be induced by him, or by any body else, to give up a situation which she believed more replete with good than any she could change it for, she had a great curiosity to see him, a decided intention of finding him pleasant, of being liked by him to a certain degree, and a sort of pleasure in the idea of their being coupled in their friends’ imaginations.", "그러면서도 그에게든 다른 누구에게든 이끌려, 지금의 처지 — 바꿀 수 있는 그 어떤 처지보다 좋은 것이 그득하다고 믿는 이 처지 — 를 내놓을 마음은 없으면서도, 그녀는 그를 만나 보고 싶은 큰 호기심을 품었고, 그를 유쾌한 사람이라고 여기기로, 또 그에게 어느 정도는 사랑받기로 단단히 작정해 두었으며, 두 사람이 벗들의 상상 속에서 짝지어진다는 생각에 일종의 즐거움마저 느끼고 있었다."]], [["With such sensations, Mr.", "이런 심사인지라 미스터 엘턴의 친절은 끔찍하게 때를 잘못 만난 셈이었다."], ["Elton’s civilities were dreadfully ill-timed; but she had the comfort of appearing very polite, while feeling very cross—and of thinking that the rest of the visit could not possibly pass without bringing forward the same information again, or the substance of it, from the open-hearted Mr. Weston.—So it proved;—for when happily released from Mr.", "그래도 속으로는 몹시 짜증을 내면서 겉으로는 더없이 정중해 보일 수 있다는 위안이 있었고, 또 이 방문이 끝나기 전에는 흉금을 터놓는 미스터 웨스턴에게서 같은 소식을, 적어도 그 알맹이를 반드시 다시 듣게 되리라는 생각도 위안이 되었다."], ["Elton, and seated by Mr.", "과연 그렇게 되었다."], ["Weston, at dinner, he made use of the very first interval in the cares of hospitality, the very first leisure from the saddle of mutton, to say to her,", "미스터 엘턴에게서 다행히 풀려나 식탁에서 미스터 웨스턴 곁에 앉게 되자, 그는 손님 접대의 분주함에서 처음으로 한숨 돌릴 틈이 나자마자, 양 다리 고기에서 처음으로 손을 놓을 여유가 생기자마자 그녀에게 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“We want only two more to be just the right number.", "\"꼭 알맞은 수가 되려면 딱 두 사람만 더 있으면 됩니다."], ["I should like to see two more here,—your pretty little friend, Miss Smith, and my son—and then I should say we were quite complete.", "이 자리에 두 사람을 더 보았으면 싶군요 — 아가씨의 어여쁜 친구 미스 스미스와 우리 아들 말입니다 — 그러면 더할 나위 없이 완벽하다고 하겠지요."], ["I believe you did not hear me telling the others in the drawing-room that we are expecting Frank.", "우리가 프랭크를 기다리고 있다는 걸 응접실에서 다른 분들께 이야기할 때 아가씨는 못 들었을 겁니다."], ["I had a letter from him this morning, and he will be with us within a fortnight.”", "오늘 아침에 그 애한테서 편지가 왔는데, 보름 안으로 우리한테 오겠다는군요.\""]], [["Emma spoke with a very proper degree of pleasure; and fully assented to his proposition of Mr. Frank Churchill and Miss Smith making their party quite complete.", "엠마는 더없이 마땅한 정도의 기쁨을 담아 말했고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠과 미스 스미스가 있어야 이 모임이 완벽해진다는 그의 말에 흔쾌히 동의했다."]], [["“He has been wanting to come to us,” continued Mr.", "\"그 애는 9월부터 줄곧 우리한테 오고 싶어 했지요.\""], ["Weston, “ever since September: every letter has been full of it; but he cannot command his own time.", "미스터 웨스턴이 말을 이었다. \"편지마다 그 이야기로 가득했어요."], ["He has those to please who must be pleased, and who (between ourselves) are sometimes to be pleased only by a good many sacrifices.", "하지만 제 시간을 제 마음대로 쓰지 못하는 처지라서요. 비위를 맞춰야만 하는 사람들이 있는데, 그 사람들이라는 게 (우리끼리 이야기지만) 더러는 적잖은 희생을 치러야만 비위가 맞춰지거든요."], ["But now I have no doubt of seeing him here about the second week in January.”", "하지만 이번에는 1월 둘째 주쯤이면 틀림없이 여기서 그 애를 보게 되리라 봅니다.\""]], [["“What a very great pleasure it will be to you!", "\"얼마나 큰 기쁨이시겠어요!"], ["and Mrs. Weston is so anxious to be acquainted with him, that she must be almost as happy as yourself.”", "게다가 웨스턴 부인도 그분과 알고 지내기를 그토록 고대하고 계시니, 거의 미스터 웨스턴만큼이나 행복하시겠네요.\""]], [["“Yes, she would be, but that she thinks there will be another put-off.", "\"그럼요, 그럴 테지요. 다만 또 한 번 미뤄지지 않을까 걱정하고 있어서 말입니다."], ["She does not depend upon his coming so much as I do: but she does not know the parties so well as I do.", "그 사람은 나만큼 그 애가 올 거라고 믿질 못해요. 하지만 그 사람은 나만큼 그쪽 사람들을 잘 알지 못하니까요."], ["The case, you see, is—(but this is quite between ourselves: I did not mention a syllable of it in the other room.", "사정인즉슨 이렇습니다 — (그렇지만 이건 어디까지나 우리끼리 이야기입니다. 저 방에서는 한마디도 입에 올리지 않았어요."], ["There are secrets in all families, you know)—The case is, that a party of friends are invited to pay a visit at Enscombe in January; and that Frank’s coming depends upon their being put off.", "어느 집안에나 비밀은 있는 법이니까요) — 사정인즉, 1월에 친구 한 무리가 엔스컴으로 방문 초대를 받았는데, 프랭크가 오느냐 마느냐가 그 사람들의 방문이 미뤄지느냐에 달려 있다는 겁니다. 그쪽이 미뤄지지 않으면 그 애는 꼼짝도 못 해요."], ["If they are not put off, he cannot stir.", "하지만 나는 미뤄질 줄로 압니다. 엔스컴의 어떤 지체 높으신 부인이 유독 질색하는 집안이거든요."], ["But I know they will, because it is a family that a certain lady, of some consequence, at Enscombe, has a particular dislike to: and though it is thought necessary to invite them once in two or three years, they always are put off when it comes to the point.", "그래서 두세 해에 한 번쯤은 초대해야 한다고들 여기면서도, 막상 때가 되면 늘 미뤄지고 말지요. 그 결말에 대해서는 나는 손톱만큼도 의심하지 않아요."], ["I have not the smallest doubt of the issue.", "1월 중순 전에 여기서 프랭크를 보리라는 것은, 내가 지금 여기 있다는 것만큼이나 확실하다고 봅니다."], ["I am as confident of seeing Frank here before the middle of January, as I am of being here myself: but your good friend there (nodding towards the upper end of the table) has so few vagaries herself, and has been so little used to them at Hartfield, that she cannot calculate on their effects, as I have been long in the practice of doing.”", "하지만 저기 저 댁의 좋은 친구분은\" — 그는 식탁 윗자리 쪽으로 고개를 끄덕여 보였다 — \"본인이 워낙 변덕이 없는 사람인 데다 하트필드에서도 그런 일을 거의 겪어 본 적이 없으니, 변덕이라는 게 어떤 결과를 빚는지를 나처럼 오래 겪어 온 사람만큼 가늠하지 못하는 거지요.\""]], [["“I am sorry there should be any thing like doubt in the case,”", "\"이 일에 의심할 구석이 있다니 안타깝네요.\" 엠마가 대답했다."], ["replied Emma; “but am disposed to side with you, Mr. Weston.", "\"하지만 저는 미스터 웨스턴 편을 들고 싶어요."], ["If you think he will come, I shall think so too; for you know Enscombe.”", "그분이 올 거라고 생각하신다면, 저도 그렇게 생각하겠어요. 엔스컴을 잘 아시는 분이니까요.\""]], [["“Yes—I have some right to that knowledge; though I have never been at the place in my life.—She is an odd woman!—But I never allow myself to speak ill of her, on Frank’s account; for I do believe her to be very fond of him. I used to think she was not capable of being fond of any body, except herself: but she has always been kind to him (in her way—allowing for little whims and caprices, and expecting every thing to be as she likes). And it is no small credit, in my opinion, to him, that he should excite such an affection; for, though I would not say it to any body else, she has no more heart than a stone to people in general; and the devil of a temper.”", "\"그렇지요 — 그걸 안다고 할 만한 자격은 좀 있는 셈입니다. 정작 그곳에는 평생 한 번도 가 본 적이 없지만요. 참 별난 여자예요! 그렇지만 나는 프랭크를 봐서라도 그 사람을 함부로 헐뜯지 않으려 합니다. 그 사람이 그 애를 무척 아끼는 것만은 정말이라고 믿거든요. 예전엔 그 사람이 자기 자신 말고는 누구도 아낄 줄 모르는 사람이라고 여겼는데, 그 애한테만은 늘 다정했어요 (그 사람 나름의 방식으로 말이지요 — 자잘한 변덕과 까탈을 눈감아 주고, 무엇이든 제 뜻대로 되기를 바라는 걸 참아 준다면요). 그리고 내 생각에, 그런 애정을 불러일으킨다는 건 그 애한테 적잖이 자랑스러운 일입니다. 다른 사람한테는 못할 말이지만, 그 사람은 사람들 전반에게는 돌멩이만큼도 정이 없고, 성미는 악마 같으니 말입니다.\""]], [["Emma liked the subject so well, that she began upon it, to Mrs. Weston, very soon after their moving into the drawing-room: wishing her joy—yet observing, that she knew the first meeting must be rather alarming.— Mrs.", "엠마는 이 화제가 어찌나 마음에 들었던지, 응접실로 자리를 옮긴 지 얼마 되지 않아 웨스턴 부인에게 그 이야기를 꺼냈다 — 기쁘시겠다고 하면서도, 첫 만남이라는 게 꽤나 마음 졸이는 일임은 안다고 덧붙였다."], ["Weston agreed to it; but added, that she should be very glad to be secure of undergoing the anxiety of a first meeting at the time talked of: “for I cannot depend upon his coming.", "웨스턴 부인은 거기에 동의했지만, 이렇게 보탰다. 말이 나온 그 시기에 첫 만남의 조마조마함을 겪게 되는 것이 차라리 확실하다면 더없이 기쁘겠다고."], ["I cannot be so sanguine as Mr. Weston.", "\"그 애가 올 거라고 확신할 수가 없어서요."], ["I am very much afraid that it will all end in nothing.", "나는 미스터 웨스턴만큼 낙관할 수가 없네요. 결국 모두 흐지부지되고 말까 봐 정말 걱정이에요."], ["Mr. Weston, I dare say, has been telling you exactly how the matter stands?”", "미스터 웨스턴이 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 그쪽 사정을 그대로 말씀드렸겠지요?\""]], [["“Yes—it seems to depend upon nothing but the ill-humour of Mrs. Churchill, which I imagine to be the most certain thing in the world.”", "\"네 — 모든 게 그저 처칠 부인의 언짢은 기분에 달려 있는 듯하던데, 제 생각엔 그게 세상에서 가장 확실한 것 같던걸요.\""]], [["“My Emma!” replied Mrs.", "\"엠마!\""], ["Weston, smiling, “what is the certainty of caprice?”", "웨스턴 부인이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"변덕에 무슨 확실함이 있겠어요?\""], ["Then turning to Isabella, who had not been attending before—“You must know, my dear Mrs.", "그러고는 그때까지 듣고 있지 않던 이저벨라에게로 몸을 돌렸다 — \"이걸 아셔야 해요, 미시즈 나이틀리."], ["Knightley, that we are by no means so sure of seeing Mr. Frank Churchill, in my opinion, as his father thinks.", "내 생각에 우리는 그 애 아버지가 여기는 것처럼 미스터 프랭크 처칠을 보게 되리라고 결코 그렇게 확신할 수가 없답니다."], ["It depends entirely upon his aunt’s spirits and pleasure; in short, upon her temper.", "모든 게 오로지 그 애 외숙모의 기분과 흥에 달려 있어요. 한마디로 그분 성미에 달린 거지요."], ["To you—to my two daughters—I may venture on the truth. Mrs.", "부인께는 — 내 두 딸에게는 — 사실대로 털어놓아도 되겠지요."], ["Churchill rules at Enscombe, and is a very odd-tempered woman; and his coming now, depends upon her being willing to spare him.”", "처칠 부인이 엔스컴을 다스리는데, 성미가 아주 별난 여자예요. 그러니 그 애가 지금 오느냐 마느냐는 그분이 그 애를 기꺼이 내어 주려는지에 달려 있답니다.\""]], [["“Oh, Mrs.", "\"아, 처칠 부인이요."], ["Churchill; every body knows Mrs. Churchill,”", "처칠 부인이야 누구나 알지요.\""], ["replied Isabella: “and I am sure I never think of that poor young man without the greatest compassion.", "이저벨라가 대답했다. \"그리고 저는 정말이지 그 가엾은 청년을 생각할 때마다 한없는 측은함이 들어요."], ["To be constantly living with an ill-tempered person, must be dreadful.", "성미 고약한 사람과 끊임없이 함께 산다는 건 끔찍한 일임에 틀림없어요."], ["It is what we happily have never known any thing of; but it must be a life of misery.", "우리야 다행히 그런 걸 한 번도 겪어 보지 못했지만, 그건 분명 비참한 삶일 거예요."], ["What a blessing, that she never had any children!", "그래도 그분에게 아이가 없었다는 게 얼마나 다행인지요!"], ["Poor little creatures, how unhappy she would have made them!”", "가엾은 어린것들, 그분이 얼마나 불행하게 만들었겠어요!\""]], [["Emma wished she had been alone with Mrs. Weston.", "엠마는 웨스턴 부인과 단둘이 있었더라면 좋았을 것을, 하고 생각했다."], ["She should then have heard more: Mrs.", "그랬더라면 더 많은 이야기를 들었을 텐데."], ["Weston would speak to her, with a degree of unreserve which she would not hazard with Isabella; and, she really believed, would scarcely try to conceal any thing relative to the Churchills from her, excepting those views on the young man, of which her own imagination had already given her such instinctive knowledge.", "웨스턴 부인은 이저벨라에게는 차마 못 할 만큼의 거리낌 없는 정도로 그녀에게 이야기해 주었을 테고, 처칠 집안에 관한 일이라면 무엇 하나 그녀에게 감추려 들지 않으리라고 그녀는 정말로 믿었다 — 그 청년에 관한 속내, 그러니까 그녀 자신의 상상이 이미 본능처럼 일러 준 그 생각만은 빼고 말이다."], ["But at present there was nothing more to be said.", "그러나 지금으로서는 더 할 이야기가 없었다."], ["Mr. Woodhouse very soon followed them into the drawing-room.", "미스터 우드하우스가 이내 그들을 따라 응접실로 들어왔던 것이다."], ["To be sitting long after dinner, was a confinement that he could not endure.", "식사 뒤에 오래 앉아 있는 것은 그가 견딜 수 없는 속박이었다."], ["Neither wine nor conversation was any thing to him; and gladly did he move to those with whom he was always comfortable.", "그에게는 포도주도 대화도 아무 의미가 없었으니, 늘 마음 편한 사람들 곁으로 기꺼이 옮겨 온 것이다."]], [["While he talked to Isabella, however, Emma found an opportunity of saying,", "그가 이저벨라와 이야기를 나누는 동안, 그래도 엠마는 이렇게 말할 기회를 잡았다."]], [["“And so you do not consider this visit from your son as by any means certain.", "\"그러니까 아드님의 이번 방문을 결코 확실하다고는 여기지 않으시는군요."], ["I am sorry for it.", "안타까워요."], ["The introduction must be unpleasant, whenever it takes place; and the sooner it could be over, the better.”", "그 인사라는 게 언제 이루어지든 마음 편치 않은 일일 테니, 빨리 끝날수록 좋을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Yes; and every delay makes one more apprehensive of other delays.", "\"그래요. 게다가 미뤄질 때마다 다른 일도 또 미뤄지지 않을까 더 조마조마해지지요."], ["Even if this family, the Braithwaites, are put off, I am still afraid that some excuse may be found for disappointing us.", "설령 그 집안, 그러니까 브레이스웨이트 집안의 방문이 미뤄진다 해도, 우리를 실망시킬 또 다른 핑계가 어디선가 나올까 봐 여전히 걱정이에요."], ["I cannot bear to imagine any reluctance on his side; but I am sure there is a great wish on the Churchills’ to keep him to themselves.", "그 애 쪽에 무슨 내키지 않는 마음이 있다고는 차마 상상하기도 싫지만, 처칠 집안 쪽에는 그 애를 제 사람으로만 붙들어 두려는 큰 바람이 있는 게 분명해요."], ["There is jealousy.", "시샘하는 거지요."], ["They are jealous even of his regard for his father.", "그 애가 제 아버지를 위하는 마음까지도 시샘한다니까요."], ["In short, I can feel no dependence on his coming, and I wish Mr. Weston were less sanguine.”", "한마디로 나는 그 애가 오리라고 도무지 믿을 수가 없어서, 미스터 웨스턴이 좀 덜 낙관하셨으면 싶어요.\""]], [["“He ought to come,”", "\"와야지요.\""], ["said Emma.", "엠마가 말했다."], ["“If he could stay only a couple of days, he ought to come; and one can hardly conceive a young man’s not having it in his power to do as much as that.", "\"단 이틀만 머물 수 있다 해도 와야 마땅해요. 청년이 그 정도 일조차 제 뜻대로 못 한다니, 도무지 상상하기 어려운 일인걸요."], ["A young woman, if she fall into bad hands, may be teased, and kept at a distance from those she wants to be with; but one cannot comprehend a young man’s being under such restraint, as not to be able to spend a week with his father, if he likes it.”", "젊은 여자라면 나쁜 사람들 손에 떨어질 경우 시달림을 당하고, 함께 있고 싶은 사람들과 멀찍이 떨어져 지내야 할 수도 있지요. 하지만 젊은 남자가, 제가 원한다면 아버지와 일주일도 함께 보내지 못할 만큼 그런 속박 아래 놓인다는 건 도무지 이해할 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“One ought to be at Enscombe, and know the ways of the family, before one decides upon what he can do,”", "\"그 애가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 단정하려면, 직접 엔스컴에 가 보고 그 집안의 방식을 알아야 해요.\" 웨스턴 부인이 대답했다."], ["replied Mrs. Weston.", "\"어쩌면 어느 집안이든 그 한 사람의 행동을 판단할 때는 똑같이 조심해야 할 테지요."], ["“One ought to use the same caution, perhaps, in judging of the conduct of any one individual of any one family; but Enscombe, I believe, certainly must not be judged by general rules: she is so very unreasonable; and every thing gives way to her.”", "하지만 엔스컴만은, 정말이지 일반적인 잣대로 판단해서는 안 될 거예요. 그 사람이 어찌나 막무가내인지, 모든 게 그 사람 앞에 무릎을 꿇으니까요.\""]], [["“But she is so fond of the nephew: he is so very great a favourite.", "\"하지만 그분이 그 조카를 그토록 아끼시잖아요."], ["Now, according to my idea of Mrs.", "그 조카가 그렇게나 끔찍이 사랑받는 사람이고요."], ["Churchill, it would be most natural, that while she makes no sacrifice for the comfort of the husband, to whom she owes every thing, while she exercises incessant caprice towards him, she should frequently be governed by the nephew, to whom she owes nothing at all.”", "자, 처칠 부인에 대한 제 생각으로는 이러는 게 가장 자연스러울 텐데요. 그분이 모든 것을 빚진 남편의 편안함을 위해서는 아무런 희생도 치르지 않고, 그 남편에게는 끊임없이 변덕을 부리면서, 정작 아무것도 빚진 게 없는 조카에게는 자주 휘둘린다는 거 말이에요.\""]], [["“My dearest Emma, do not pretend, with your sweet temper, to understand a bad one, or to lay down rules for it: you must let it go its own way.", "\"사랑하는 엠마, 그 고운 성미를 가지고 못된 성미를 이해한다고, 거기에 무슨 법칙을 세운다고 우기지 말아요. 그런 건 제멋대로 가게 내버려 둬야 한답니다."], ["I have no doubt of his having, at times, considerable influence; but it may be perfectly impossible for him to know beforehand when it will be.”", "그 애한테 때로 상당한 영향력이 있다는 건 의심하지 않아요. 하지만 그 영향력이 언제 통할지를 그 애가 미리 안다는 건 도무지 불가능한 일일지도 모르지요.\""]], [["Emma listened, and then coolly said, “I shall not be satisfied, unless he comes.”", "엠마는 듣고 있다가, 이윽고 차분하게 말했다. \"그분이 오지 않으면 저는 만족하지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["“He may have a great deal of influence on some points,”", "\"그 애가 어떤 일에는 큰 영향을 미칠 수도 있겠지요.\""], ["continued Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["Weston, “and on others, very little: and among those, on which she is beyond his reach, it is but too likely, may be this very circumstance of his coming away from them to visit us.”", "\"그러나 또 어떤 일에는 거의 미치지 못할 거예요. 그리고 그 애의 힘이 미치지 못하는 일들 가운데에는, 다름 아닌 바로 이번 일 — 그 사람들을 떠나 우리를 보러 온다는 이 일이 들어 있을 가능성이 너무도 크답니다.\""]], [["# Volume I — Chapter XV", "# 제1권 15장"]], [["Mr.", "미스터 우드하우스는 곧 차를 들 채비를 마쳤다."], ["Woodhouse was soon ready for his tea; and when he had drank his tea he was quite ready to go home; and it was as much as his three companions could do, to entertain away his notice of the lateness of the hour, before the other gentlemen appeared.", "그리고 차를 마시고 나니 이내 집으로 돌아갈 채비도 다 된 셈이었다. 다른 신사들이 나타나기 전까지 시간이 늦었다는 사실에 그가 주의를 돌리지 않도록 막는 것은 세 동무가 할 수 있는 일의 전부였다."], ["Mr. Weston was chatty and convivial, and no friend to early separations of any sort; but at last the drawing-room party did receive an augmentation.", "미스터 웨스턴은 수다스럽고 흥겨운 사람이라 어떤 종류의 이른 작별도 좋아하지 않았다."], ["Mr. Elton, in very good spirits, was one of the first to walk in.", "그러나 마침내 응접실 무리에 인원이 보태졌다."], ["Mrs. Weston and Emma were sitting together on a sofa.", "미스터 엘턴이 무척 기분이 좋은 얼굴로 가장 먼저 걸어 들어온 사람 가운데 하나였다."], ["He joined them immediately, and, with scarcely an invitation, seated himself between them.", "웨스턴 부인과 엠마는 소파에 나란히 앉아 있었다. 그는 곧장 두 사람에게 다가오더니, 청하지도 않았는데 둘 사이에 자리를 잡고 앉았다."]], [["Emma, in good spirits too, from the amusement afforded her mind by the expectation of Mr.", "엠마 또한 프랭크 처칠을 기대하는 데서 오는 즐거움으로 기분이 좋았던 터라, 그의 최근의 무례함을 기꺼이 잊고 예전처럼 그를 흡족하게 대할 마음이 들었다."], ["Frank Churchill, was willing to forget his late improprieties, and be as well satisfied with him as before, and on his making Harriet his very first subject, was ready to listen with most friendly smiles.", "그리고 그가 해리엇을 맨 첫머리 화제로 꺼내자, 더없이 다정한 미소로 귀를 기울일 채비가 되어 있었다."]], [["He professed himself extremely anxious about her fair friend—her fair, lovely, amiable friend. “Did she know?—had she heard any thing about her, since their being at Randalls?—he felt much anxiety—he must confess that the nature of her complaint alarmed him considerably.” And in this style he talked on for some time very properly, not much attending to any answer, but altogether sufficiently awake to the terror of a bad sore throat; and Emma was quite in charity with him.", "그는 그녀의 어여쁜 벗을 — 그 어여쁘고, 사랑스럽고, 상냥한 벗을 — 몹시 염려하는 기색을 내비쳤다. \"혹 소식을 들으셨는지요? 랜덜스에 오신 뒤로 그녀에 관해 무어라도 들으신 게 있는지요? 저는 무척 마음이 쓰입니다. 솔직히 말씀드려, 그 병세의 성질이 저를 적잖이 놀라게 했다는 것을 고백하지 않을 수 없군요.\" 이런 투로 그는 한동안 아주 적절하게 말을 이어 갔다. 무슨 대답이 돌아오든 그리 귀담아듣지는 않았으나, 고약한 인후염의 무서움에는 충분히 깨어 있는 사람처럼 굴었다. 그리하여 엠마는 그에게 꽤 너그러운 마음이 되었다."]], [["But at last there seemed a perverse turn; it seemed all at once as if he were more afraid of its being a bad sore throat on her account, than on Harriet’s—more anxious that she should escape the infection, than that there should be no infection in the complaint.", "그러나 마침내 어딘가 비뚤어진 전환이 일어나는 듯했다. 별안간, 그가 해리엇을 두고 고약한 인후염을 걱정하기보다 그녀를 두고 더 두려워하는 것처럼 보이기 시작한 것이다."], ["He began with great earnestness to entreat her to refrain from visiting the sick-chamber again, for the present—to entreat her to promise him not to venture into such hazard till he had seen Mr.", "그 병에 전염성이 없기를 바라기보다, 그녀가 전염을 피하기를 더 안달하는 듯했다. 그는 더없이 진지하게, 당분간은 다시 그 병실을 찾지 말아 달라고 간청하기 시작했다."], ["Perry and learnt his opinion; and though she tried to laugh it off and bring the subject back into its proper course, there was no putting an end to his extreme solicitude about her.", "자기가 미스터 페리를 만나 그 의견을 듣기 전까지는 그런 위험을 무릅쓰지 않겠다고 약속해 달라고 그녀에게 청했다. 그녀가 웃어넘기며 화제를 본래의 줄기로 되돌리려 애썼지만, 그녀를 향한 그의 지나친 노심초사를 그칠 길이 없었다."], ["She was vexed.", "그녀는 짜증이 났다."], ["It did appear—there was no concealing it—exactly like the pretence of being in love with her, instead of Harriet; an inconstancy, if real, the most contemptible and abominable!", "그것은 — 감출 길도 없이 — 영락없이 해리엇이 아니라 그녀 자신에게 연정을 품은 척하는 꼴이었다. 변심이라니, 그것이 진심이라면 더없이 경멸스럽고 가증스러운 일이었다!"], ["and she had difficulty in behaving with temper. He turned to Mrs.", "그녀는 평정을 지키며 처신하기가 힘들었다."], ["Weston to implore her assistance, “Would not she give him her support?—would not she add her persuasions to his, to induce Miss Woodhouse not to go to Mrs.", "그는 웨스턴 부인 쪽으로 돌아서서 그 도움을 호소했다. \"부인께서 제 편을 들어 주시지 않겠습니까?"], ["Goddard’s till it were certain that Miss Smith’s disorder had no infection?", "미스 스미스의 병에 전염성이 없다는 것이 확실해지기 전까지는 미스 우드하우스가 고더드 부인 댁에 가지 못하도록, 제 설득에 부인의 설득을 보태 주시지 않겠습니까?"], ["He could not be satisfied without a promise—would not she give him her influence in procuring it?”", "저는 약속을 받지 않고는 마음이 놓이질 않습니다. 부인께서 그 약속을 받아 내는 데 영향력을 좀 보태 주시지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“So scrupulous for others,”", "\"남들 일에는 이토록 세심하면서,\""], ["he continued, “and yet so careless for herself!", "그가 말을 이었다. \"정작 자신에게는 이토록 무심하시다니요!"], ["She wanted me to nurse my cold by staying at home to-day, and yet will not promise to avoid the danger of catching an ulcerated sore throat herself.", "미스 우드하우스는 오늘 제가 집에 머물며 감기를 다스리기를 바라셨으면서, 정작 본인은 곪은 인후염에 걸릴 위험을 피하겠다는 약속은 하려 들지 않으십니다."], ["Is this fair, Mrs.", "이게 공평합니까, 웨스턴 부인?"], ["Weston?—Judge between us.", "우리 둘 사이를 판가름해 주십시오."], ["Have not I some right to complain?", "제가 불평할 권리가 좀 있지 않습니까?"], ["I am sure of your kind support and aid.”", "부인의 자상한 지지와 도움을 믿어 의심치 않습니다.\""]], [["Emma saw Mrs.", "엠마는 웨스턴 부인의 놀라는 기색을 보았고, 그 놀라움이 클 수밖에 없으리라고 느꼈다."], ["Weston’s surprize, and felt that it must be great, at an address which, in words and manner, was assuming to himself the right of first interest in her; and as for herself, she was too much provoked and offended to have the power of directly saying any thing to the purpose.", "말로 보나 태도로 보나, 그녀에 대해 가장 먼저 관심을 기울일 권리를 제 것으로 떠맡는 듯한 수작이었으니 말이다. 정작 그녀 자신은 너무도 화가 나고 모욕을 느낀 나머지 곧장 무어라 똑 부러지게 말할 힘조차 없었다."], ["She could only give him a look; but it was such a look as she thought must restore him to his senses, and then left the sofa, removing to a seat by her sister, and giving her all her attention.", "그저 눈길 하나를 던지는 것이 고작이었지만, 그것은 그를 제정신으로 돌려놓고도 남으리라 여겨질 만한 눈길이었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 소파를 떠나 언니 곁의 자리로 옮겨 가, 온통 그쪽에만 마음을 쏟았다."]], [["She had not time to know how Mr. Elton took the reproof, so rapidly did another subject succeed; for Mr.", "미스터 엘턴이 그 질책을 어떻게 받아들였는지 알아볼 겨를도 없이, 어찌나 빠르게 또 다른 화제가 뒤를 이었는지 모른다."], ["John Knightley now came into the room from examining the weather, and opened on them all with the information of the ground being covered with snow, and of its still snowing fast, with a strong drifting wind; concluding with these words to Mr.", "날씨를 살피고 온 미스터 존 나이틀리가 마침 방으로 들어서더니, 땅이 온통 눈에 덮였고 지금도 거센 바람에 눈발이 휘몰아치며 여전히 펑펑 쏟아지고 있다는 소식으로 좌중을 향해 입을 열었던 것이다."], ["Woodhouse:", "그러고는 미스터 우드하우스를 향해 이런 말로 끝을 맺었다."]], [["“This will prove a spirited beginning of your winter engagements, sir.", "\"이번 겨울 나들이를 아주 패기 넘치게 여시게 됐군요, 어르신."], ["Something new for your coachman and horses to be making their way through a storm of snow.”", "마부와 말들로서는 눈보라를 헤치고 길을 내야 하니, 자못 새로운 일이 되겠습니다.\""]], [["Poor Mr.", "가엾은 미스터 우드하우스는 너무 놀라 입을 다물고 말았다."], ["Woodhouse was silent from consternation; but every body else had something to say; every body was either surprized or not surprized, and had some question to ask, or some comfort to offer.", "그러나 다른 사람들은 저마다 할 말이 있었다. 모두가 놀라거나 놀라지 않거나 했고, 던질 물음이나 건넬 위로를 한마디씩 가지고 있었다."], ["Mrs. Weston and Emma tried earnestly to cheer him and turn his attention from his son-in-law, who was pursuing his triumph rather unfeelingly.", "웨스턴 부인과 엠마는 그의 기운을 북돋우고, 사뭇 매정하게 제 승리를 몰아붙이는 사위에게서 그의 주의를 돌리려 진지하게 애썼다."]], [["“I admired your resolution very much, sir,”", "\"어르신의 결단이 참으로 감탄스러웠습니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “in venturing out in such weather, for of course you saw there would be snow very soon.", "\"이런 날씨에 길을 나서시다니요. 물론 어르신께서도 곧 눈이 오리라는 걸 보셨겠지요."], ["Every body must have seen the snow coming on.", "누구라도 눈이 오는 기미를 알아챘을 테니까요."], ["I admired your spirit; and I dare say we shall get home very well.", "어르신의 그 패기에 감탄했습니다. 무사히 잘 돌아가게 되리라고 믿습니다."], ["Another hour or two’s snow can hardly make the road impassable; and we are two carriages; if one is blown over in the bleak part of the common field there will be the other at hand.", "한두 시간 더 눈이 온다 한들 길이 통 막혀 버리지는 않을 테고, 게다가 우리는 마차가 둘이지 않습니까. 하나가 저 휑한 공유지 벌판에서 뒤집힌다 해도, 다른 하나가 곁에 있을 테니까요."], ["I dare say we shall be all safe at Hartfield before midnight.”", "자정 전에는 모두 무사히 하트필드에 닿으리라 봅니다.\""]], [["Mr. Weston, with triumph of a different sort, was confessing that he had known it to be snowing some time, but had not said a word, lest it should make Mr.", "미스터 웨스턴은 또 다른 종류의 의기양양함으로, 자기는 얼마 전부터 눈이 오는 줄 알고 있었으나 미스터 우드하우스를 불편하게 하여 서둘러 떠날 핑계가 될까 봐 한마디도 하지 않았노라 털어놓았다."], ["Woodhouse uncomfortable, and be an excuse for his hurrying away.", "귀로를 막을 만큼 눈이 쌓였다거나 쌓일 성싶다는 이야기는 그저 우스개일 뿐, 다들 아무 어려움 없이 가게 되리라고 그는 보았다."], ["As to there being any quantity of snow fallen or likely to fall to impede their return, that was a mere joke; he was afraid they would find no difficulty.", "그는 오히려 길이 막혀 모두를 랜덜스에 붙들어 둘 수 있으면 좋겠다고 했다."], ["He wished the road might be impassable, that he might be able to keep them all at Randalls; and with the utmost good-will was sure that accommodation might be found for every body, calling on his wife to agree with him, that with a little contrivance, every body might be lodged, which she hardly knew how to do, from the consciousness of there being but two spare rooms in the house.", "그러고는 더없는 호의로, 조금만 머리를 쓰면 누구든 다 묵을 자리를 마련할 수 있다고 장담하며, 아내에게 동의를 구했다. 그러나 집에 손님방이 둘밖에 없음을 알고 있던 아내로서는 어떻게 그리할 수 있을지 막막하기만 했다."]], [["“What is to be done, my dear Emma?—what is to be done?”", "\"어쩌면 좋으냐, 우리 엠마야? 어쩌면 좋으냐?\""], ["was Mr. Woodhouse’s first exclamation, and all that he could say for some time.", "이것이 미스터 우드하우스의 첫 탄식이었고, 한동안은 그가 할 수 있는 말의 전부였다."], ["To her he looked for comfort; and her assurances of safety, her representation of the excellence of the horses, and of James, and of their having so many friends about them, revived him a little.", "그는 위로를 구하며 그녀를 바라보았다. 무사하리라는 그녀의 다짐, 말들과 제임스가 얼마나 훌륭한지, 또 곁에 든든한 벗들이 얼마나 많은지에 대한 그녀의 설명이 그를 조금이나마 되살려 놓았다."]], [["His eldest daughter’s alarm was equal to his own.", "큰딸의 불안은 아버지에 못지않았다."], ["The horror of being blocked up at Randalls, while her children were at Hartfield, was full in her imagination; and fancying the road to be now just passable for adventurous people, but in a state that admitted no delay, she was eager to have it settled, that her father and Emma should remain at Randalls, while she and her husband set forward instantly through all the possible accumulations of drifted snow that might impede them.", "제 아이들이 하트필드에 있는데 자기가 랜덜스에 발이 묶이는 끔찍한 상상이 머릿속에 가득했던 것이다. 그녀는 길이 지금이라면 모험심 있는 사람에게는 그럭저럭 다닐 만하되 한시도 지체해서는 안 될 형편이라 여기고, 아버지와 엠마는 랜덜스에 남고 자기 부부는 길을 막을지도 모를 온갖 눈더미를 무릅쓰고 당장 떠나는 것으로 일을 매듭짓고 싶어 안달했다."]], [["“You had better order the carriage directly, my love,”", "\"여보, 어서 마차를 부르라 이르세요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “I dare say we shall be able to get along, if we set off directly; and if we do come to any thing very bad, I can get out and walk.", "\"당장 출발하면 그럭저럭 갈 수 있을 거예요. 정 못 갈 만큼 사나운 데를 만나면 내려서 걸으면 되고요."], ["I am not at all afraid.", "저는 조금도 무섭지 않아요."], ["I should not mind walking half the way.", "절반쯤은 걷는 것도 괜찮아요."], ["I could change my shoes, you know, the moment I got home; and it is not the sort of thing that gives me cold.”", "집에 닿는 즉시 신을 갈아 신으면 되잖아요. 그 정도로 감기 드는 사람도 아니고요.\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"저런!\""], ["replied he.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“Then, my dear Isabella, it is the most extraordinary sort of thing in the world, for in general every thing does give you cold.", "\"그렇다면, 우리 이저벨라야, 그건 세상에서 둘도 없이 별난 일이로구나. 너는 대개 무슨 일에나 감기가 드는 아이니 말이다."], ["Walk home!—you are prettily shod for walking home, I dare say.", "걸어서 집에 간다니! 걸어가기에 참 어여쁘게도 신을 차려 신었겠구나."], ["It will be bad enough for the horses.”", "말들에게도 충분히 험한 길이 될 텐데.\""]], [["Isabella turned to Mrs. Weston for her approbation of the plan.", "이저벨라는 그 계획에 동의를 구하고자 웨스턴 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["Mrs. Weston could only approve.", "웨스턴 부인으로서는 찬성하는 수밖에 없었다."], ["Isabella then went to Emma; but Emma could not so entirely give up the hope of their being all able to get away; and they were still discussing the point, when Mr.", "이저벨라는 다시 엠마에게로 갔다. 그러나 엠마는 모두가 무사히 떠날 수 있으리라는 희망을 그렇게까지 완전히 접을 수가 없었다."], ["Knightley, who had left the room immediately after his brother’s first report of the snow, came back again, and told them that he had been out of doors to examine, and could answer for there not being the smallest difficulty in their getting home, whenever they liked it, either now or an hour hence.", "두 사람이 여전히 그 문제를 놓고 의논하고 있는데, 동생이 처음 눈 소식을 전하자마자 곧장 방을 나섰던 미스터 나이틀리가 돌아와, 자기가 밖에 나가 살펴보고 왔노라고, 지금이든 한 시간 뒤든 원하는 때 언제고 모두 집에 돌아가는 데 조금의 어려움도 없으리라 장담한다고 말했다."], ["He had gone beyond the sweep—some way along the Highbury road—the snow was nowhere above half an inch deep—in many places hardly enough to whiten the ground; a very few flakes were falling at present, but the clouds were parting, and there was every appearance of its being soon over.", "그는 진입로를 지나 하이베리 길을 얼마쯤 걸어 보았는데, 눈은 어디고 반 인치를 넘는 데가 없고 여러 곳은 땅을 희끗하게 덮을 만큼도 못 되더라고 했다. 지금은 눈송이 몇 점이 떨어질 뿐이며, 구름이 갈라지고 있어 곧 그칠 기색이 완연하더라는 것이었다."], ["He had seen the coachmen, and they both agreed with him in there being nothing to apprehend.", "마부들도 만나 보았는데, 둘 다 염려할 것이 없다는 그의 말에 동의하더라고 했다."]], [["To Isabella, the relief of such tidings was very great, and they were scarcely less acceptable to Emma on her father’s account, who was immediately set as much at ease on the subject as his nervous constitution allowed; but the alarm that had been raised could not be appeased so as to admit of any comfort for him while he continued at Randalls. He was satisfied of there being no present danger in returning home, but no assurances could convince him that it was safe to stay; and while the others were variously urging and recommending, Mr. Knightley and Emma settled it in a few brief sentences: thus—", "이저벨라에게는 그런 소식이 주는 안도가 무척 컸다. 엠마에게도 아버지를 생각하면 그에 못지않게 반가운 소식이었다. 아버지는 이 일에 관해서만큼은 그 예민한 체질이 허락하는 한도 내에서 당장 마음을 놓았다. 그러나 일단 일어난 불안은 그가 랜덜스에 머무는 동안에는 어떤 위로도 받아들일 수 없을 만큼 좀처럼 가라앉지 않았다. 그는 지금 집으로 돌아가는 데 위험이 없다는 점에는 만족했으나, 머물러 있는 편이 안전하다는 것은 어떤 다짐으로도 납득시킬 수 없었다. 다른 사람들이 저마다 이러쿵저러쿵 권하고 채근하는 동안, 미스터 나이틀리와 엠마는 짤막한 몇 마디로 일을 매듭지었다. 이런 식으로."]], [["“Your father will not be easy; why do not you go?”", "\"아버님이 마음을 놓지 못하실 거야. 어서 가지 그래?\""]], [["“I am ready, if the others are.”", "\"다른 사람들만 준비되면 저는 됐어요.\""]], [["“Shall I ring the bell?”", "\"종을 울릴까?\""]], [["“Yes, do.”", "\"네, 그래 주세요.\""]], [["And the bell was rung, and the carriages spoken for.", "그리하여 종이 울리고, 마차를 대기시키라 일렀다."], ["A few minutes more, and Emma hoped to see one troublesome companion deposited in his own house, to get sober and cool, and the other recover his temper and happiness when this visit of hardship were over.", "몇 분만 더 지나면, 골치 아픈 동무 하나는 제 집에 내려놓아 술도 깨고 머리도 식히게 되고, 다른 하나는 이 고역스러운 방문이 끝나면 본래의 기분과 행복을 되찾으리라고 엠마는 바랐다."]], [["The carriage came: and Mr.", "마차가 왔다."], ["Woodhouse, always the first object on such occasions, was carefully attended to his own by Mr. Knightley and Mr.", "그리고 이런 자리에서는 늘 첫째로 챙겨야 할 미스터 우드하우스가 미스터 나이틀리와 미스터 웨스턴의 정성스러운 부축을 받으며 제 마차로 안내되었다."], ["Weston; but not all that either could say could prevent some renewal of alarm at the sight of the snow which had actually fallen, and the discovery of a much darker night than he had been prepared for.", "그러나 둘이 무어라 말한들, 실제로 내린 눈을 보고 또 예상보다 훨씬 어두운 밤이라는 것을 알게 되자 다시금 일어나는 불안을 막을 길은 없었다."], ["“He was afraid they should have a very bad drive.", "\"길이 몹시 험할까 봐 걱정이라고."], ["He was afraid poor Isabella would not like it.", "가엾은 이저벨라가 싫어할까 봐 걱정이라고."], ["And there would be poor Emma in the carriage behind.", "게다가 가엾은 엠마는 뒤따르는 마차에 타게 되니."], ["He did not know what they had best do.", "어찌해야 가장 좋을지 모르겠다고."], ["They must keep as much together as they could;”", "되도록 바짝 붙어서 가야 한다고.\""], ["and James was talked to, and given a charge to go very slow and wait for the other carriage.", "그리하여 제임스에게 일러, 아주 천천히 가며 다른 마차를 기다리라는 분부가 내려졌다."]], [["Isabella stept in after her father; John Knightley, forgetting that he did not belong to their party, stept in after his wife very naturally; so that Emma found, on being escorted and followed into the second carriage by Mr.", "이저벨라는 아버지를 따라 마차에 올랐다. 존 나이틀리는 자기가 그쪽 일행이 아니라는 것을 깜빡한 채 아주 자연스럽게 아내를 따라 올라탔다."], ["Elton, that the door was to be lawfully shut on them, and that they were to have a tête-à-tête drive.", "그리하여 엠마는 미스터 엘턴의 호위와 뒤따름 속에 두 번째 마차에 오르고 보니, 문이 둘만 남긴 채 떳떳이 닫히게 되었고, 단둘이 마주 앉아 가게 되었음을 알게 되었다."], ["It would not have been the awkwardness of a moment, it would have been rather a pleasure, previous to the suspicions of this very day; she could have talked to him of Harriet, and the three-quarters of a mile would have seemed but one.", "바로 오늘 그런 의심이 일기 전이었다면 한순간의 어색함은커녕 오히려 즐거운 일이었을 것이다. 해리엇 이야기를 나눌 수 있었을 테고, 사분의 삼 마일 길도 그저 한순간처럼 여겨졌을 것이다."], ["But now, she would rather it had not happened.", "그러나 지금은, 차라리 이런 일이 없었더라면 싶었다."], ["She believed he had been drinking too much of Mr. Weston’s good wine, and felt sure that he would want to be talking nonsense.", "그가 미스터 웨스턴의 좋은 포도주를 너무 많이 마신 것 같았고, 분명 또 허튼소리를 늘어놓고 싶어 하리라는 확신이 들었다."]], [["To restrain him as much as might be, by her own manners, she was immediately preparing to speak with exquisite calmness and gravity of the weather and the night; but scarcely had she begun, scarcely had they passed the sweep-gate and joined the other carriage, than she found her subject cut up—her hand seized—her attention demanded, and Mr.", "되도록 그를 자제시키고자 제 몸가짐으로라도 그리하려고, 그녀는 날씨와 밤에 대해 더없이 차분하고 진지한 어조로 말을 꺼낼 채비를 곧장 했다. 그러나 채 말을 시작하기도 전에, 진입로 문을 지나 다른 마차와 합류하기 무섭게, 그녀는 제 화제가 잘려 나가고 — 손이 붙잡히고 — 주의를 요구당하고, 미스터 엘턴이 실제로 격렬하게 사랑을 고백하고 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["Elton actually making violent love to her: availing himself of the precious opportunity, declaring sentiments which must be already well known, hoping—fearing—adoring—ready to die if she refused him; but flattering himself that his ardent attachment and unequalled love and unexampled passion could not fail of having some effect, and in short, very much resolved on being seriously accepted as soon as possible.", "그는 이 귀한 기회를 틈타, 이미 빤히 알고 계실 것이 분명한 마음을 토로하고 — 바라고 — 두려워하고 — 흠모하며 — 거절하시면 죽겠노라 했다. 그러면서도 자신의 뜨거운 연정과 견줄 데 없는 사랑과 전례 없는 열정이 효험을 보지 못할 리 없으리라 스스로 흐뭇해했고, 한마디로 되도록 빨리 진지하게 받아들여지기로 단단히 작정한 모양이었다."], ["It really was so.", "정말로 그러했다."], ["Without scruple—without apology—without much apparent diffidence, Mr. Elton, the lover of Harriet, was professing himself her lover.", "거리낌도 없이 — 사과도 없이 — 별로 머뭇거리는 기색도 없이, 해리엇을 사모하던 미스터 엘턴이 엠마 자신을 사모한다고 고백하고 있었다."], ["She tried to stop him; but vainly; he would go on, and say it all.", "그녀는 그를 막아 보려 했으나 헛일이었다. 그는 기어이 말을 이어, 할 말을 다 쏟아 내려 했다."], ["Angry as she was, the thought of the moment made her resolve to restrain herself when she did speak.", "화가 치밀었지만, 그 순간의 생각으로 그녀는 정작 입을 열 때는 자제하기로 마음먹었다."], ["She felt that half this folly must be drunkenness, and therefore could hope that it might belong only to the passing hour.", "이 어리석음의 절반은 취기 탓이리라 여겨졌고, 그러니 그것이 한때의 일로 그치기를 바랄 수 있었다."], ["Accordingly, with a mixture of the serious and the playful, which she hoped would best suit his half and half state, she replied,", "그리하여, 반쯤 취하고 반쯤 깬 그의 상태에 가장 잘 맞으리라 싶은, 진지함과 장난기가 뒤섞인 어조로 그녀는 대답했다."]], [["“I am very much astonished, Mr.", "\"무척 놀랍군요, 미스터 엘턴. 저한테 이런 말씀을요!"], ["Elton. This to me!", "정신을 어디 두셨어요."], ["you forget yourself—you take me for my friend—any message to Miss Smith I shall be happy to deliver; but no more of this to me, if you please.”", "제 벗과 저를 혼동하고 계시잖아요. 미스 스미스에게 전할 말씀이 있으시면 기꺼이 전해 드리겠지만, 저한테는 부디 더 이상 이러지 마세요.\""]], [["“Miss Smith!—message to Miss Smith!—What could she possibly mean!”—And he repeated her words with such assurance of accent, such boastful pretence of amazement, that she could not help replying with quickness,", "\"미스 스미스라니요! 미스 스미스에게 전할 말씀이라니요! 도대체 무슨 뜻으로 하시는 말씀입니까!\" 그러고는 어찌나 자신만만한 어조로, 어찌나 과장된 놀라움을 가장하며 그녀의 말을 되받던지, 그녀는 얼른 이렇게 응수하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Mr.", "\"미스터 엘턴, 이거야말로 둘도 없이 해괴한 처사예요!"], ["Elton, this is the most extraordinary conduct!", "제가 이걸 이해할 길은 한 가지뿐이군요."], ["and I can account for it only in one way; you are not yourself, or you could not speak either to me, or of Harriet, in such a manner.", "당신은 지금 제정신이 아니에요. 그렇지 않고서야 저한테든 해리엇에 대해서든 이런 식으로 말씀하실 리 없죠."], ["Command yourself enough to say no more, and I will endeavour to forget it.”", "더는 아무 말씀 마실 만큼만 자제하세요. 그러면 저도 잊으려 애써 보겠어요.\""]], [["But Mr. Elton had only drunk wine enough to elevate his spirits, not at all to confuse his intellects.", "그러나 미스터 엘턴은 기분이 들뜰 만큼만 포도주를 마셨을 뿐, 정신이 흐려질 정도는 결코 아니었다."], ["He perfectly knew his own meaning; and having warmly protested against her suspicion as most injurious, and slightly touched upon his respect for Miss Smith as her friend,—but acknowledging his wonder that Miss Smith should be mentioned at all,—he resumed the subject of his own passion, and was very urgent for a favourable answer.", "그는 제 뜻을 또렷이 알고 있었다. 그는 그녀의 그런 의심이야말로 더없이 모욕적이라며 열을 올려 항변하고는, 미스 스미스의 벗으로서 그녀를 존중한다는 뜻을 슬쩍 비치면서도 — 미스 스미스가 거론되었다는 것 자체가 의아하다고 시인하면서 — 다시 제 열정의 화제로 돌아가, 호의적인 대답을 들려 달라고 몹시 다그쳤다."]], [["As she thought less of his inebriety, she thought more of his inconstancy and presumption; and with fewer struggles for politeness, replied,", "그의 취기를 덜 생각하게 되니, 그의 변심과 주제넘음이 더 크게 다가왔다. 그리하여 예의를 차리려는 안간힘을 한결 덜고 그녀는 대답했다."]], [["“It is impossible for me to doubt any longer.", "\"이제 더는 의심할 여지가 없군요."], ["You have made yourself too clear.", "너무도 분명하게 드러내셨으니까요."], ["Mr. Elton, my astonishment is much beyond any thing I can express.", "미스터 엘턴, 제 놀라움은 무어라 표현할 수 있는 정도를 한참 넘어섰어요."], ["After such behaviour, as I have witnessed during the last month, to Miss Smith—such attentions as I have been in the daily habit of observing—to be addressing me in this manner—this is an unsteadiness of character, indeed, which I had not supposed possible!", "지난 한 달 동안 미스 스미스에게 보여 온 그런 행동을 제 눈으로 똑똑히 보았고 — 날마다 지켜보는 게 일과였던 그런 관심을 보여 놓고 — 저한테 이런 식으로 말씀하시다니. 이거야말로 제가 도무지 있을 법하다고 여기지 못했던 성품의 변덕이군요!"], ["Believe me, sir, I am far, very far, from gratified in being the object of such professions.”", "믿어 주세요, 선생님. 저는 그런 고백의 대상이 된 것이 조금도, 정말이지 조금도 기쁘지 않습니다.\""]], [["“Good Heaven!”", "\"맙소사!\" 미스터 엘턴이 외쳤다."], ["cried Mr.", "\"도대체 이게 무슨 말씀입니까?"], ["Elton, “what can be the meaning of this?—Miss Smith!—I never thought of Miss Smith in the whole course of my existence—never paid her any attentions, but as your friend: never cared whether she were dead or alive, but as your friend.", "미스 스미스라니요! 저는 살아오는 내내 미스 스미스를 단 한 번도 마음에 둔 적이 없습니다."], ["If she has fancied otherwise, her own wishes have misled her, and I am very sorry—extremely sorry—But, Miss Smith, indeed!—Oh!", "그녀에게 관심을 기울인 것이 있다면 오로지 당신의 벗이기 때문이었지요. 그녀가 죽었는지 살았는지조차 당신의 벗으로서가 아니면 마음 쓴 적이 없습니다."], ["Miss Woodhouse! who can think of Miss Smith, when Miss Woodhouse is near!", "그녀가 달리 짐작했다면, 그건 제 바람이 아니라 그녀 자신의 바람이 그녀를 그르친 것이니, 참으로 안됐습니다 — 더없이 안된 일이지요."], ["No, upon my honour, there is no unsteadiness of character.", "하지만, 미스 스미스라니요, 정말이지! 아!"], ["I have thought only of you.", "미스 우드하우스! 미스 우드하우스가 곁에 계신데 누가 미스 스미스 따위를 생각하겠습니까!"], ["I protest against having paid the smallest attention to any one else.", "아니요, 제 명예를 걸고, 성품의 변덕 같은 건 없습니다. 저는 오직 당신만을 생각해 왔어요."], ["Every thing that I have said or done, for many weeks past, has been with the sole view of marking my adoration of yourself.", "다른 누구에게도 손톱만큼의 관심을 기울인 적이 없다고 단언합니다. 지난 여러 주 동안 제가 한 말이나 행동은 죄다 당신을 향한 흠모를 드러내려는 단 하나의 뜻에서 나온 것입니다."], ["You cannot really, seriously, doubt it.", "설마 정말로, 진지하게, 그걸 의심하시는 건 아니겠지요."], ["No!—(in an accent meant to be insinuating)—I am sure you have seen and understood me.”", "아니에요! (애써 은근한 척하는 어조로) 당신은 분명 저를 알아보고 헤아리셨을 겁니다.\""]], [["It would be impossible to say what Emma felt, on hearing this—which of all her unpleasant sensations was uppermost.", "이 말을 들은 엠마의 심정이 어떠했는지, 그 온갖 언짢은 감정 가운데 무엇이 가장 위에 있었는지는 도무지 말로 다 할 수 없었다."], ["She was too completely overpowered to be immediately able to reply: and two moments of silence being ample encouragement for Mr.", "그녀는 너무도 완전히 압도된 나머지 곧장 대답할 수가 없었다."], ["Elton’s sanguine state of mind, he tried to take her hand again, as he joyously exclaimed—", "그리고 두 순간의 침묵이 미스터 엘턴의 들뜬 심사에는 더없이 넉넉한 격려가 되었으니, 그는 다시 그녀의 손을 잡으려 하며 기쁨에 겨워 외쳤다."]], [["“Charming Miss Woodhouse!", "\"매혹적인 미스 우드하우스!"], ["allow me to interpret this interesting silence.", "이 의미심장한 침묵을 제가 풀이하도록 허락해 주십시오."], ["It confesses that you have long understood me.”", "이건 당신이 진작부터 저를 헤아리고 계셨음을 고백하는 거지요.\""]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"아니에요, 선생님.\""], ["cried Emma, “it confesses no such thing.", "엠마가 소리쳤다. \"그건 그런 걸 고백하는 게 결코 아니에요."], ["So far from having long understood you, I have been in a most complete error with respect to your views, till this moment.", "진작부터 헤아리기는커녕, 저는 이 순간까지 당신의 속내에 관해 더없이 완벽한 착각에 빠져 있었어요. 저로 말하자면, 당신이 무슨 감정에든 빠져들고 계셨다면 참으로 유감입니다 — 그건 제 바람과 더없이 거리가 먼 일이니까요."], ["As to myself, I am very sorry that you should have been giving way to any feelings—Nothing could be farther from my wishes—your attachment to my friend Harriet—your pursuit of her, (pursuit, it appeared,) gave me great pleasure, and I have been very earnestly wishing you success: but had I supposed that she were not your attraction to Hartfield, I should certainly have thought you judged ill in making your visits so frequent.", "당신이 제 벗 해리엇에게 품은 연정이 — 그녀를 향한 당신의 구애가, (구애로 보였으니까요) 저를 무척 기쁘게 했고, 저는 당신의 성공을 진심으로 간절히 바라 왔어요. 하지만 그녀가 당신이 하트필드에 오시는 이유가 아니었다는 걸 알았더라면, 그렇게 잦은 방문이야말로 당신의 판단이 잘못되었다고 분명 여겼을 거예요."], ["Am I to believe that you have never sought to recommend yourself particularly to Miss Smith?—that you have never thought seriously of her?”", "그래, 당신이 미스 스미스에게 각별히 잘 보이려 한 적이 한 번도 없었다는 말씀이세요? 그녀를 진지하게 마음에 둔 적이 한 번도 없었다고요?\""]], [["“Never, madam,” cried he, affronted in his turn: “never, I assure you. I think seriously of Miss Smith!—Miss Smith is a very good sort of girl; and I should be happy to see her respectably settled. I wish her extremely well: and, no doubt, there are men who might not object to—Every body has their level: but as for myself, I am not, I think, quite so much at a loss. I need not so totally despair of an equal alliance, as to be addressing myself to Miss Smith!—No, madam, my visits to Hartfield have been for yourself only; and the encouragement I received—”", "\"한 번도 없습니다, 부인.\" 이번에는 그가 발끈해서 외쳤다. \"단 한 번도, 분명히 말씀드립니다. 제가 미스 스미스를 진지하게 마음에 둔다니요! 미스 스미스는 꽤 괜찮은 처녀이긴 하지요. 그녀가 번듯하게 자리 잡는 것을 본다면 저도 기쁠 겁니다. 그녀가 더없이 잘되기를 바라고요. 그리고 물론, 그녀에게라면 마다하지 않을 남자들도 있겠지요 — 사람마다 제 분수가 있는 법이니까요. 하지만 저로 말할 것 같으면, 제가 그렇게까지 막막한 처지는 아니라고 봅니다. 대등한 혼처를 그토록 단념하여 미스 스미스 따위에게 말을 건넬 만큼은 아니란 말입니다! 아니요, 부인, 제가 하트필드를 찾은 것은 오로지 당신 때문이었습니다. 그리고 제가 받은 격려로 말하자면 —\""]], [["“Encouragement!—I give you encouragement!—Sir, you have been entirely mistaken in supposing it.", "\"격려라니요! 제가 당신에게 격려를 했다고요!"], ["I have seen you only as the admirer of my friend.", "선생님, 그렇게 짐작하셨다면 완전히 잘못 아신 거예요."], ["In no other light could you have been more to me than a common acquaintance.", "저는 당신을 그저 제 벗을 흠모하는 분으로만 보았어요. 그 외에 다른 의미로는, 당신은 저한테 그저 흔한 지인 이상은 결코 아니었습니다."], ["I am exceedingly sorry: but it is well that the mistake ends where it does.", "참으로 유감이에요. 하지만 그 오해가 여기서 끝나는 게 다행이고요."], ["Had the same behaviour continued, Miss Smith might have been led into a misconception of your views; not being aware, probably, any more than myself, of the very great inequality which you are so sensible of.", "같은 행동이 계속되었더라면, 미스 스미스가 당신의 속내를 오해하게 되었을지도 모르죠. 당신이 그토록 절감하시는 그 엄청난 신분의 격차를, 그녀도 저처럼 미처 몰랐을 테니까요."], ["But, as it is, the disappointment is single, and, I trust, will not be lasting.", "하지만 이렇게 된 이상, 실망하는 사람은 하나뿐이고, 또 오래가지도 않으리라 믿어요."], ["I have no thoughts of matrimony at present.”", "저는 지금으로선 결혼할 생각이 전혀 없습니다.\""]], [["He was too angry to say another word; her manner too decided to invite supplication; and in this state of swelling resentment, and mutually deep mortification, they had to continue together a few minutes longer, for the fears of Mr.", "그는 너무 화가 나 더는 한마디도 못 했다. 그녀의 태도 또한 너무 단호하여 애원을 청할 여지가 없었다."], ["Woodhouse had confined them to a foot-pace.", "이렇게 부풀어 오르는 원망과 서로의 깊은 굴욕 속에서, 두 사람은 몇 분을 더 함께 있어야 했다. 미스터 우드하우스의 염려 탓에 두 마차가 걸음걸이만 한 속도로 묶여 있었던 것이다."], ["If there had not been so much anger, there would have been desperate awkwardness; but their straightforward emotions left no room for the little zigzags of embarrassment.", "그토록 분노가 크지 않았더라면 절망스러운 어색함이 감돌았겠지만, 두 사람의 거침없는 감정은 자잘하게 우물쭈물할 여지를 남기지 않았다. 마차가 어느 결에 비커리지 골목으로 접어들었는지, 어디서 멈춰 섰는지도 모른 채, 두 사람은 별안간 그의 집 문 앞에 닿아 있었다."], ["Without knowing when the carriage turned into Vicarage Lane, or when it stopped, they found themselves, all at once, at the door of his house; and he was out before another syllable passed.—Emma then felt it indispensable to wish him a good night.", "그는 또 한마디 말이 오가기도 전에 마차에서 내려 버렸다. 그제야 엠마는 잘 자라는 인사를 건네지 않을 수 없다고 느꼈다."], ["The compliment was just returned, coldly and proudly; and, under indescribable irritation of spirits, she was then conveyed to Hartfield.", "그 인사는 차갑고 거만하게, 꼭 그만큼만 돌아왔다. 그리고 형언할 수 없는 심사의 동요 속에서, 그녀는 하트필드로 실려 갔다."]], [["There she was welcomed, with the utmost delight, by her father, who had been trembling for the dangers of a solitary drive from Vicarage Lane—turning a corner which he could never bear to think of—and in strange hands—a mere common coachman—no James; and there it seemed as if her return only were wanted to make every thing go well: for Mr. John Knightley, ashamed of his ill-humour, was now all kindness and attention; and so particularly solicitous for the comfort of her father, as to seem—if not quite ready to join him in a basin of gruel—perfectly sensible of its being exceedingly wholesome; and the day was concluding in peace and comfort to all their little party, except herself.—But her mind had never been in such perturbation; and it needed a very strong effort to appear attentive and cheerful till the usual hour of separating allowed her the relief of quiet reflection.", "그곳에서 그녀는 아버지의 더없이 기쁜 환대를 받았다. 아버지는 비커리지 골목에서 혼자 마차를 타고 오는 위험에 — 그가 도무지 떠올리기조차 견딜 수 없는 모퉁이를 돌아오는 길에 — 그것도 낯선 손에, 그저 흔해 빠진 마부에게, 제임스도 없이 맡겨진다는 생각에 떨고 있었던 것이다. 그곳에서는 마치 그녀가 돌아오기만 하면 만사가 잘 풀릴 듯싶었다. 미스터 존 나이틀리도 제 심술을 부끄러워하며 이제는 온통 다정함과 정성으로 굴었고, 장인의 안락을 어찌나 각별히 챙기던지, 함께 묽은 죽 한 그릇을 들 채비까지는 아니어도 그것이 더없이 몸에 좋다는 것만큼은 충분히 헤아리는 듯 보였다. 그리하여 그날 하루는 그 작은 일행 모두에게 — 그녀 한 사람만 빼고 — 평온하고 안락하게 저물어 갔다. 그러나 그녀의 마음은 일찍이 그토록 어지러웠던 적이 없었다. 그래서 헤어질 때가 되어 비로소 그녀에게 조용한 사색의 안식을 허락해 줄 때까지, 줄곧 주의 깊고 명랑한 척하려면 무던히도 힘써야 했다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter XVI", "# 제1권 16장"]], [["The hair was curled, and the maid sent away, and Emma sat down to think and be miserable.—It was a wretched business indeed!—Such an overthrow of every thing she had been wishing for!—Such a development of every thing most unwelcome!—Such a blow for Harriet!—that was the worst of all. Every part of it brought pain and humiliation, of some sort or other; but, compared with the evil to Harriet, all was light; and she would gladly have submitted to feel yet more mistaken—more in error—more disgraced by mis-judgment, than she actually was, could the effects of her blunders have been confined to herself.", "머리 손질이 끝나고 하녀를 물리자, 엠마는 앉아서 생각에 잠겼고 또 비참해졌다. 참으로 끔찍한 일이었다! 그녀가 바라 온 모든 것이 이토록 뒤집히다니! 더없이 달갑잖은 일들이 이토록 한꺼번에 드러나다니! 해리엇에게 이런 타격이라니! 그것이 무엇보다도 최악이었다. 이 일의 어느 구석을 보아도 이런저런 종류의 아픔과 굴욕이 따라왔다. 그러나 해리엇이 겪을 화에 견주면 다른 것은 죄다 가벼웠다. 자신의 실수가 빚은 결과가 오직 제게만 미칠 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 실제보다 더 많이 틀리고, 더 그르치고, 그릇된 판단으로 더 망신당하는 일조차 기꺼이 감수했으리라."]], [["“If I had not persuaded Harriet into liking the man, I could have borne any thing.", "\"내가 해리엇을 부추겨 그 사람을 좋아하게 만들지만 않았더라면, 무엇이든 견딜 수 있었을 텐데."], ["He might have doubled his presumption to me—but poor Harriet!”", "그 사람이 나한테 그런 주제넘은 짓을 두 배로 했어도 좋았어. 하지만 가엾은 해리엇은!\""]], [["How she could have been so deceived!—He protested that he had never thought seriously of Harriet—never!", "어쩌면 자신이 그렇게까지 속을 수 있었을까! 그는 해리엇을 진지하게 생각한 적이 한 번도 없다고, 단 한 번도 없다고 단언했다."], ["She looked back as well as she could; but it was all confusion.", "그녀는 할 수 있는 한 지난 일을 되짚어 보았으나, 모든 것이 뒤죽박죽이었다."], ["She had taken up the idea, she supposed, and made every thing bend to it.", "짐작건대 자신이 어떤 생각을 한번 품고는 모든 것을 거기에 끼워 맞춘 모양이었다."], ["His manners, however, must have been unmarked, wavering, dubious, or she could not have been so misled.", "그러나 그의 태도도 분명 어딘가 모호하고, 흔들리고, 알쏭달쏭한 데가 있었을 것이다. 그렇지 않고서야 그녀가 그토록 잘못 헤아릴 리 없었다."]], [["The picture!—How eager he had been about the picture!—and the charade!—and an hundred other circumstances;—how clearly they had seemed to point at Harriet. To be sure, the charade, with its “ready wit”—but then the “soft eyes”—in fact it suited neither; it was a jumble without taste or truth. Who could have seen through such thick-headed nonsense?", "그 초상화! 그가 그 초상화에 얼마나 열을 올렸던가! 그리고 그 수수께끼 시! 게다가 그 밖에도 수백 가지 정황이 있었다. 그 모든 것이 해리엇을 가리키는 것이 어쩌면 그렇게도 또렷해 보였던지. 하기야 그 수수께끼 시의 \"민첩한 재치\"는 — 그렇지만 또 \"부드러운 눈동자\"는 — 사실 어느 쪽에도 맞지 않았다. 그것은 운치도 진실도 없는 뒤죽박죽이었다. 그런 멍청한 헛소리를 누가 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["Certainly she had often, especially of late, thought his manners to herself unnecessarily gallant; but it had passed as his way, as a mere error of judgment, of knowledge, of taste, as one proof among others that he had not always lived in the best society, that with all the gentleness of his address, true elegance was sometimes wanting; but, till this very day, she had never, for an instant, suspected it to mean any thing but grateful respect to her as Harriet’s friend.", "물론 그녀도 자주, 특히 요즈음 들어서는, 그가 자신을 대하는 태도가 쓸데없이 정중하다고 여기긴 했다. 그러나 그것을 그저 그의 버릇으로, 판단력이나 식견이나 안목의 단순한 흠으로 넘겼던 것이다 — 그가 늘 최상류 사회에서만 살아온 것은 아니어서, 그토록 부드러운 몸가짐에도 불구하고 더러 참된 우아함이 결여되곤 한다는 여러 증거 가운데 하나로. 그러나 바로 오늘에 이르기까지, 그녀는 그것이 해리엇의 벗인 자신에게 보내는 고마운 경의 외에 다른 무엇을 뜻한다고는 한순간도 의심해 본 적이 없었다."]], [["To Mr. John Knightley was she indebted for her first idea on the subject, for the first start of its possibility.", "이 문제에 관한 첫 생각, 그 가능성을 처음 떠올리게 해 준 사람은 미스터 존 나이틀리였다."], ["There was no denying that those brothers had penetration. She remembered what Mr.", "그 형제에게 통찰력이 있다는 것은 부정할 수 없었다."], ["Knightley had once said to her about Mr. Elton, the caution he had given, the conviction he had professed that Mr.", "그녀는 언젠가 미스터 나이틀리가 미스터 엘턴을 두고 했던 말, 그가 일러 준 경계의 말, 미스터 엘턴이 결코 분별없이 결혼하지는 않으리라던 그 확신을 떠올렸다."], ["Elton would never marry indiscreetly; and blushed to think how much truer a knowledge of his character had been there shewn than any she had reached herself.", "그리고 거기 드러난 그의 인품에 대한 식견이 자신이 다다른 그 어떤 것보다 얼마나 더 진실에 가까웠는지를 생각하니 얼굴이 화끈거렸다."], ["It was dreadfully mortifying; but Mr.", "끔찍하게 면목 없는 일이었다."], ["Elton was proving himself, in many respects, the very reverse of what she had meant and believed him; proud, assuming, conceited; very full of his own claims, and little concerned about the feelings of others.", "그러나 미스터 엘턴은 여러모로 그녀가 의도하고 믿어 온 것과는 정반대의 사람임을 스스로 입증하고 있었다 — 거만하고, 주제넘고, 우쭐대고, 제 권리 주장으로 가득 차서 남의 감정에는 거의 무심한 사람이었다."]], [["Contrary to the usual course of things, Mr. Elton’s wanting to pay his addresses to her had sunk him in her opinion.", "여느 경우의 통례와는 반대로, 미스터 엘턴이 자신에게 구애하려 했다는 사실은 도리어 그녀의 평가에서 그를 끌어내렸다."], ["His professions and his proposals did him no service.", "그의 고백도 청혼도 그에게 아무런 득이 되지 못했다."], ["She thought nothing of his attachment, and was insulted by his hopes.", "그녀는 그의 연정을 대수롭지 않게 여겼고, 그의 기대에 모욕감을 느꼈다."], ["He wanted to marry well, and having the arrogance to raise his eyes to her, pretended to be in love; but she was perfectly easy as to his not suffering any disappointment that need be cared for.", "그는 잘 결혼하고 싶었던 것이고, 자신에게까지 눈을 들이대는 오만을 부려 사랑에 빠진 척한 것이었다. 그러나 그녀로서는 그가 마음 써 줄 만한 실망 따위는 조금도 겪지 않으리라는 점에 대해 완전히 마음이 놓였다."], ["There had been no real affection either in his language or manners.", "그의 말에도 태도에도 참된 애정이라곤 없었다."], ["Sighs and fine words had been given in abundance; but she could hardly devise any set of expressions, or fancy any tone of voice, less allied with real love.", "한숨과 고운 말은 차고 넘치게 쏟아 냈으나, 참된 사랑과 이만큼 동떨어진 표현의 묶음이나 목소리의 가락은 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없을 정도였다."], ["She need not trouble herself to pity him.", "그를 가엾이 여기느라 마음 쓸 필요도 없었다."], ["He only wanted to aggrandise and enrich himself; and if Miss Woodhouse of Hartfield, the heiress of thirty thousand pounds, were not quite so easily obtained as he had fancied, he would soon try for Miss Somebody else with twenty, or with ten.", "그는 그저 제 몸을 키우고 살림을 불리고 싶었을 뿐이다. 그러니 하트필드의 미스 우드하우스, 삼만 파운드의 상속녀가 제 짐작만큼 그리 호락호락 손에 들어오지 않는다면, 그는 곧 이만 파운드짜리, 아니 만 파운드짜리 미스 아무개를 노릴 사람이었다."]], [["But—that he should talk of encouragement, should consider her as aware of his views, accepting his attentions, meaning (in short), to marry him!—should suppose himself her equal in connexion or mind!—look down upon her friend, so well understanding the gradations of rank below him, and be so blind to what rose above, as to fancy himself shewing no presumption in addressing her!—It was most provoking.", "그러나 — 그가 자신이 부추김을 받았다는 듯이 말하다니, 자신을 그의 속셈을 알고 그의 관심을 받아들이며 (한마디로) 그와 결혼할 작정인 여자로 여기다니! 자신을 가문에서나 식견에서나 그와 대등한 사람이라 생각하다니! 자기보다 아래인 신분의 등급은 그토록 잘 가려내면서도, 위로 솟은 것에 대해서는 그토록 눈이 어두워, 자신에게 구애하는 것이 조금도 주제넘은 짓이 아니라고 여기다니! 그것은 더없이 분통 터지는 일이었다."]], [["Perhaps it was not fair to expect him to feel how very much he was her inferior in talent, and all the elegancies of mind.", "어쩌면 그가 재능에서나, 정신의 온갖 우아함에서나 자신보다 한참 못하다는 것을 느끼기를 바라는 것은 공정치 않은 일인지도 몰랐다. 그런 대등함이 없다는 바로 그 사실이 그가 그것을 알아채지 못하게 막았을 수도 있으니까."], ["The very want of such equality might prevent his perception of it; but he must know that in fortune and consequence she was greatly his superior.", "그러나 재산과 지체에서는 그녀가 그보다 훨씬 위라는 것쯤은 그도 분명 알았을 것이다. 우드하우스 집안이 여러 대에 걸쳐 하트필드에 자리 잡아 온, 아주 유서 깊은 가문의 방계라는 것 — 그리고 엘턴 집안은 이름 없는 가문이라는 것을 그도 분명 알았을 것이다."], ["He must know that the Woodhouses had been settled for several generations at Hartfield, the younger branch of a very ancient family—and that the Eltons were nobody.", "하트필드의 토지 재산이야 보잘것없어서, 하이베리의 나머지 땅이 죄다 속해 있는 돈웰 애비 영지에 박힌 일종의 작은 틈에 지나지 않았다. 그러나 다른 출처에서 나오는 그들의 재산은, 그 밖의 모든 지체에 있어서 그들을 돈웰 애비 그 자체에도 거의 뒤지지 않게 해 줄 만한 것이었다."], ["The landed property of Hartfield certainly was inconsiderable, being but a sort of notch in the Donwell Abbey estate, to which all the rest of Highbury belonged; but their fortune, from other sources, was such as to make them scarcely secondary to Donwell Abbey itself, in every other kind of consequence; and the Woodhouses had long held a high place in the consideration of the neighbourhood which Mr.", "게다가 우드하우스 집안은 오래도록 이웃의 높은 존경을 받아 왔으니, 미스터 엘턴이 이 고장에 처음 발을 들인 것은 채 이태도 안 되는 일로, 장사로 맺은 인연 말고는 아무 연줄도 없고, 제 처지와 깍듯함 외에는 눈에 띌 만한 아무것도 없이 그저 스스로 길을 헤쳐 나가려던 처지였다. 그런데도 그는 그녀가 자신에게 반했다고 믿었던 것이다."], ["Elton had first entered not two years ago, to make his way as he could, without any alliances but in trade, or any thing to recommend him to notice but his situation and his civility.—But he had fancied her in love with him; that evidently must have been his dependence; and after raving a little about the seeming incongruity of gentle manners and a conceited head, Emma was obliged in common honesty to stop and admit that her own behaviour to him had been so complaisant and obliging, so full of courtesy and attention, as (supposing her real motive unperceived) might warrant a man of ordinary observation and delicacy, like Mr.", "그것이 분명 그의 믿는 구석이었으리라. 그리고 부드러운 몸가짐과 우쭐한 머릿속이 어울리지 않는 듯하다며 한동안 분개해 보던 끝에, 엠마는 양심상 멈추고 인정하지 않을 수 없었다 — 그를 대한 자신의 행동이 어찌나 살갑고 고분고분하며, 어찌나 예의와 관심으로 가득했던지 (자신의 진짜 속내를 그가 알아채지 못했다고 친다면), 미스터 엘턴 정도의 평범한 관찰력과 섬세함을 지닌 사내라면 자신이 단연 사랑받고 있다고 믿을 만도 했다는 것을."], ["Elton, in fancying himself a very decided favourite. If she had so misinterpreted his feelings, she had little right to wonder that he, with self-interest to blind him, should have mistaken hers.", "만약 자신이 그토록 그의 감정을 잘못 읽었다면, 사욕에 눈이 먼 그가 자신의 감정을 오해했다 한들 놀랄 자격이 거의 없는 셈이었다."]], [["The first error and the worst lay at her door.", "첫 번째 잘못, 그것도 가장 큰 잘못은 그녀 자신에게 있었다."], ["It was foolish, it was wrong, to take so active a part in bringing any two people together.", "어떤 두 사람을 맺어 주려고 그렇게 적극적으로 끼어든 것은 어리석고도 그릇된 일이었다."], ["It was adventuring too far, assuming too much, making light of what ought to be serious, a trick of what ought to be simple.", "그것은 너무 멀리 나아간 모험이요, 너무 많은 것을 함부로 떠맡은 일이며, 진지하게 다루어야 할 일을 가벼이 여기고, 단순해야 할 일을 술수로 만든 짓이었다."], ["She was quite concerned and ashamed, and resolved to do such things no more.", "그녀는 못내 걱정스럽고 부끄러웠으며, 다시는 그런 일을 하지 않으리라 결심했다."]], [["“Here have I,”", "\"이렇게 됐어.\""], ["said she, “actually talked poor Harriet into being very much attached to this man.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. \"내가 기어이 가엾은 해리엇을 구슬려 이 남자한테 깊이 빠지게 만들어 놓았어."], ["She might never have thought of him but for me; and certainly never would have thought of him with hope, if I had not assured her of his attachment, for she is as modest and humble as I used to think him.", "나만 없었으면 그 애는 그 사람을 떠올리지도 않았을 거야. 그리고 내가 그 사람의 연정을 장담하지만 않았어도, 결코 희망을 품고 그 사람을 생각하진 않았을 거야 — 그 애는 내가 전에 그 사람을 그리 여겼듯이, 그만큼 겸손하고 자신을 낮추는 아이니까."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["that I had been satisfied with persuading her not to accept young Martin.", "그 젊은 마틴을 받아들이지 말라고 설득한 데서 만족했어야 했는데."], ["There I was quite right.", "거기까진 내가 아주 옳았어."], ["That was well done of me; but there I should have stopped, and left the rest to time and chance.", "그건 잘한 일이었어. 하지만 거기서 멈추고, 나머지는 세월과 운에 맡겼어야 했어."], ["I was introducing her into good company, and giving her the opportunity of pleasing some one worth having; I ought not to have attempted more.", "나는 그 애를 좋은 사람들 사이로 끌어 주고, 가질 만한 누군가의 마음에 들 기회를 주고 있었던 거야. 그 이상을 시도해선 안 되는 거였어."], ["But now, poor girl, her peace is cut up for some time.", "그런데 이제 가엾은 그 애는 한동안 마음의 평화를 잃게 됐어."], ["I have been but half a friend to her; and if she were not to feel this disappointment so very much, I am sure I have not an idea of any body else who would be at all desirable for her;—William Coxe—Oh!", "나는 그 애한테 반쪽짜리 벗밖에 못 됐어. 그리고 그 애가 이번 실망을 그렇게 심하게 느끼지 않는다 쳐도, 그 애한테 조금이라도 어울릴 만한 다른 사람은 정말이지 도무지 떠오르질 않아."], ["no, I could not endure William Coxe—a pert young lawyer.”", "윌리엄 콕스 — 아! 안 돼, 윌리엄 콕스는 못 참아 — 건방진 젊은 변호사라니.\""]], [["She stopt to blush and laugh at her own relapse, and then resumed a more serious, more dispiriting cogitation upon what had been, and might be, and must be.", "그녀는 자신이 또 옛 버릇으로 되돌아간 것에 얼굴을 붉히며 웃다가 멈추었고, 이내 지나간 일, 있을 법한 일, 있을 수밖에 없는 일에 대해 한층 진지하고 한층 풀 죽은 사색으로 돌아갔다."], ["The distressing explanation she had to make to Harriet, and all that poor Harriet would be suffering, with the awkwardness of future meetings, the difficulties of continuing or discontinuing the acquaintance, of subduing feelings, concealing resentment, and avoiding eclat, were enough to occupy her in most unmirthful reflections some time longer, and she went to bed at last with nothing settled but the conviction of her having blundered most dreadfully.", "해리엇에게 해야 할 그 괴로운 해명, 그리고 가엾은 해리엇이 겪을 온갖 일들 — 앞으로의 만남이 어색해지리라는 것, 교제를 이어 갈지 끊을지 정하기가 곤란하리라는 것, 감정을 억누르고 분한 마음을 감추며 소동이 나지 않게 피해야 하는 일들 — 이런 것만으로도 한동안 더 그녀를 더없이 우울한 상념에 잠기게 하기 충분했다. 그리하여 마침내 잠자리에 들었으나, 정해진 것이라곤 자신이 더없이 끔찍하게 일을 그르쳤다는 확신뿐이었다."]], [["To youth and natural cheerfulness like Emma’s, though under temporary gloom at night, the return of day will hardly fail to bring return of spirits.", "엠마처럼 젊고 천성이 쾌활한 사람에게는, 비록 밤에는 잠시 우울에 잠긴다 해도, 날이 다시 밝으면 좀처럼 기운이 돌아오지 않는 법이 없다."], ["The youth and cheerfulness of morning are in happy analogy, and of powerful operation; and if the distress be not poignant enough to keep the eyes unclosed, they will be sure to open to sensations of softened pain and brighter hope.", "아침의 젊음과 쾌활함은 행복한 조응을 이루며 강한 힘으로 작용한다. 그리고 그 괴로움이 두 눈을 감기지 못할 만큼 사무치는 것이 아니라면, 그 눈은 틀림없이 한결 누그러진 아픔과 한층 밝아진 희망의 감각으로 떠지게 마련이다."]], [["Emma got up on the morrow more disposed for comfort than she had gone to bed, more ready to see alleviations of the evil before her, and to depend on getting tolerably out of it.", "이튿날 엠마는 잠자리에 들 때보다 한결 마음 편한 기분으로 일어났다. 눈앞에 놓인 화를 누그러뜨릴 방도가 보이고, 그럭저럭 거기서 벗어나리라는 기대를 걸 수 있었다."]], [["It was a great consolation that Mr. Elton should not be really in love with her, or so particularly amiable as to make it shocking to disappoint him—that Harriet’s nature should not be of that superior sort in which the feelings are most acute and retentive—and that there could be no necessity for any body’s knowing what had passed except the three principals, and especially for her father’s being given a moment’s uneasiness about it.", "미스터 엘턴이 자신을 정말로 사랑하는 것이 아니라는 것, 그를 실망시키는 것이 끔찍하게 여겨질 만큼 그가 그리 각별히 사랑스러운 사람도 아니라는 것 — 해리엇의 천성이 감정이 가장 예민하고 오래 가는 그런 우월한 부류는 아니라는 것 — 그리고 일어난 일을 굳이 세 당사자 말고는 아무도 알 필요가 없으며, 특히 아버지에게는 단 한순간의 불안도 안겨 드릴 까닭이 없다는 것, 이 모두가 큰 위안이었다."]], [["These were very cheering thoughts; and the sight of a great deal of snow on the ground did her further service, for any thing was welcome that might justify their all three being quite asunder at present.", "이런 생각들은 무척 마음을 북돋아 주었다. 게다가 땅에 두껍게 쌓인 눈이 보이는 것도 한층 도움이 되었으니, 지금으로서는 셋이 서로 완전히 떨어져 지내는 것을 정당화해 줄 만한 일이라면 무엇이든 반가웠던 것이다."]], [["The weather was most favourable for her; though Christmas Day, she could not go to church.", "날씨는 그녀에게 더없이 유리했다. 성탄절이었으나 교회에 갈 수 없었다."], ["Mr. Woodhouse would have been miserable had his daughter attempted it, and she was therefore safe from either exciting or receiving unpleasant and most unsuitable ideas.", "딸이 그러려 들었다면 미스터 우드하우스는 비탄에 빠졌을 터, 그래서 그녀는 달갑잖고 도무지 어울리지도 않는 생각을 불러일으키거나 받게 될 염려에서 안전했다."], ["The ground covered with snow, and the atmosphere in that unsettled state between frost and thaw, which is of all others the most unfriendly for exercise, every morning beginning in rain or snow, and every evening setting in to freeze, she was for many days a most honourable prisoner.", "땅은 눈으로 덮였고, 대기는 얼음과 해빙 사이의 그 어수선한 상태에 있었으니 — 운동에는 무엇보다도 고약한 날씨로, 아침마다 비나 눈으로 시작하고 저녁마다 얼어붙으니 — 그녀는 여러 날 동안 더없이 떳떳한 갇힌 몸이 되었다."], ["No intercourse with Harriet possible but by note; no church for her on Sunday any more than on Christmas Day; and no need to find excuses for Mr. Elton’s absenting himself.", "해리엇과는 쪽지 말고는 어떤 왕래도 불가능했고, 일요일에도 성탄절과 마찬가지로 교회에 갈 수 없었으며, 미스터 엘턴이 자리를 비우는 데 대한 구실을 찾을 필요도 없었다."]], [["It was weather which might fairly confine every body at home; and though she hoped and believed him to be really taking comfort in some society or other, it was very pleasant to have her father so well satisfied with his being all alone in his own house, too wise to stir out; and to hear him say to Mr.", "누구든 집 안에 묶어 둘 만한 날씨였다. 그리고 그가 어딘가 누군가의 모임에서 정말로 위안을 얻고 있으리라 바라고 또 믿기는 했지만, 그가 사려 깊게도 바깥출입을 삼간 채 제 집에 홀로 있다는 데 아버지가 그토록 흡족해하시는 모습을 보는 것은 무척 흐뭇한 일이었다."], ["Knightley, whom no weather could keep entirely from them,—", "게다가 어떤 날씨로도 그들에게서 완전히 떼어 놓을 수 없는 미스터 나이틀리에게 아버지가 이렇게 말하는 것을 듣는 것도 그러했다."]], [["“Ah! Mr. Knightley, why do not you stay at home like poor Mr. Elton?”", "\"아, 미스터 나이틀리, 어째서 가엾은 미스터 엘턴처럼 집에 가만히 계시질 않소?\""]], [["These days of confinement would have been, but for her private perplexities, remarkably comfortable, as such seclusion exactly suited her brother, whose feelings must always be of great importance to his companions; and he had, besides, so thoroughly cleared off his ill-humour at Randalls, that his amiableness never failed him during the rest of his stay at Hartfield.", "이 갇혀 지낸 날들은, 그녀 혼자만의 골칫거리만 아니었다면 유난히 편안했을 것이다. 이런 칩거가 형부의 취향에 딱 맞았기 때문이다 — 형부의 기분이란 늘 함께 지내는 이들에게 큰 영향을 미치기 마련이었는데, 게다가 그는 랜덜스에서 묻혀 온 언짢음을 어찌나 말끔히 털어 버렸던지, 하트필드에 머무는 내내 그 상냥함이 한 번도 흐트러지지 않았다."], ["He was always agreeable and obliging, and speaking pleasantly of every body.", "그는 늘 유쾌하고 다정했으며, 누구에 대해서든 좋게 말했다."], ["But with all the hopes of cheerfulness, and all the present comfort of delay, there was still such an evil hanging over her in the hour of explanation with Harriet, as made it impossible for Emma to be ever perfectly at ease.", "그러나 쾌활함을 향한 온갖 기대에도, 또 미루어 둔 데서 오는 지금의 온갖 편안함에도, 해리엇과 해명을 나눌 그 시간에는 여전히 그토록 큰 화가 그녀의 머리 위에 드리워 있어, 엠마로서는 도무지 마음을 온전히 놓을 수가 없었다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter XVII", "# 제1권 17장"]], [["Mr. and Mrs.", "미스터 존 나이틀리 부부가 하트필드에 오래 머물지는 않았다."], ["John Knightley were not detained long at Hartfield. The weather soon improved enough for those to move who must move; and Mr.", "떠나야 할 사람들이 떠날 수 있을 만큼 날씨가 곧 풀렸고, 미스터 우드하우스는 여느 때처럼 딸에게 아이들을 모두 데리고 남으라고 설득해 보았으나, 결국 일행이 모두 길을 나서는 것을 지켜볼 수밖에 없었으며, 가엾은 이저벨라의 운명을 두고 탄식하는 자리로 되돌아오고 말았다."], ["Woodhouse having, as usual, tried to persuade his daughter to stay behind with all her children, was obliged to see the whole party set off, and return to his lamentations over the destiny of poor Isabella;—which poor Isabella, passing her life with those she doated on, full of their merits, blind to their faults, and always innocently busy, might have been a model of right feminine happiness.", "그런데 그 가엾은 이저벨라로 말하자면, 끔찍이 사랑하는 사람들과 더불어 살며, 그들의 장점으로 가득 차고 그들의 결점에는 눈이 멀어, 늘 천진하게 바쁘게 지내니, 올바른 여성적 행복의 본보기라 할 만한 사람이었다."]], [["The evening of the very day on which they went brought a note from Mr. Elton to Mr.", "그들이 떠난 바로 그날 저녁, 미스터 엘턴이 미스터 우드하우스에게 보낸 쪽지가 왔으니, 길고도 정중하며 격식을 갖춘 쪽지였다."], ["Woodhouse, a long, civil, ceremonious note, to say, with Mr.", "미스터 엘턴의 깍듯한 인사와 더불어, 다음과 같은 내용이었다."], ["Elton’s best compliments, “that he was proposing to leave Highbury the following morning in his way to Bath; where, in compliance with the pressing entreaties of some friends, he had engaged to spend a few weeks, and very much regretted the impossibility he was under, from various circumstances of weather and business, of taking a personal leave of Mr.", "\"이튿날 아침 하이베리를 떠나 바스로 향할 작정이며, 그곳에서 몇몇 벗들의 간곡한 청에 따라 몇 주를 머물기로 약속하였으나, 날씨며 용무며 여러 사정으로 미스터 우드하우스를 직접 찾아뵙고 작별 인사를 드릴 수 없게 된 것을 몹시 애석히 여긴다."], ["Woodhouse, of whose friendly civilities he should ever retain a grateful sense—and had Mr. Woodhouse any commands, should be happy to attend to them.”", "그가 베풀어 주신 다정한 호의에 대해서는 언제까지나 고마운 마음을 간직할 것이며, 미스터 우드하우스께서 분부하실 일이 있으시면 기꺼이 받들겠노라\"는 것이었다."]], [["Emma was most agreeably surprized.—Mr.", "엠마는 더없이 흐뭇하게 놀랐다."], ["Elton’s absence just at this time was the very thing to be desired.", "하필 이 시점에 미스터 엘턴이 자리를 비운다는 것은 바라 마지않던 바로 그 일이었다."], ["She admired him for contriving it, though not able to give him much credit for the manner in which it was announced.", "그가 이런 일을 꾸며 낸 것이 기특했으나, 그것을 알려 온 방식만큼은 그리 높이 사 줄 수 없었다."], ["Resentment could not have been more plainly spoken than in a civility to her father, from which she was so pointedly excluded.", "그토록 노골적으로 자신을 빼놓고 아버지에게만 보낸 정중함 속에, 원망이 이보다 더 분명하게 드러날 수는 없었으리라. 그녀는 그 첫머리의 인사말에서조차 한자리를 얻지 못했다."], ["She had not even a share in his opening compliments.—Her name was not mentioned;—and there was so striking a change in all this, and such an ill-judged solemnity of leave-taking in his graceful acknowledgments, as she thought, at first, could not escape her father’s suspicion.", "그녀의 이름은 거론되지 않았다. 이 모든 데에서 어찌나 두드러진 변화가 있고, 그 우아한 사례의 말 속에 작별 인사의 분별없는 엄숙함이 어찌나 짙던지, 처음에 그녀는 이것이 아버지의 의심을 피하지 못하리라 여겼다."]], [["It did, however.—Her father was quite taken up with the surprize of so sudden a journey, and his fears that Mr.", "그러나 의심을 피했다."], ["Elton might never get safely to the end of it, and saw nothing extraordinary in his language.", "아버지는 그토록 갑작스러운 여행에 놀라고, 미스터 엘턴이 무사히 그 끝에 가닿지 못할까 걱정하는 데 온통 마음을 빼앗긴 터라, 그 글에서 이상한 점이라곤 아무것도 보지 못했다."], ["It was a very useful note, for it supplied them with fresh matter for thought and conversation during the rest of their lonely evening.", "그 쪽지는 무척 쓸모가 있었으니, 두 사람만의 적적한 저녁 나머지를 채울 새로운 생각거리와 이야깃거리를 제공해 주었기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Woodhouse talked over his alarms, and Emma was in spirits to persuade them away with all her usual promptitude.", "미스터 우드하우스는 자기 걱정들을 늘어놓았고, 엠마는 여느 때처럼 재빠른 솜씨로 그 걱정들을 달래어 물리칠 만큼 기운이 좋았다."]], [["She now resolved to keep Harriet no longer in the dark.", "이제 그녀는 해리엇을 더는 모르는 채로 두지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["She had reason to believe her nearly recovered from her cold, and it was desirable that she should have as much time as possible for getting the better of her other complaint before the gentleman’s return. She went to Mrs.", "해리엇이 감기에서 거의 나았다고 믿을 만한 근거가 있었고, 그 신사가 돌아오기 전에 또 다른 병을 떨쳐 낼 시간을 되도록 많이 갖는 편이 바람직했다."], ["Goddard’s accordingly the very next day, to undergo the necessary penance of communication; and a severe one it was.—She had to destroy all the hopes which she had been so industriously feeding—to appear in the ungracious character of the one preferred—and acknowledge herself grossly mistaken and mis-judging in all her ideas on one subject, all her observations, all her convictions, all her prophecies for the last six weeks.", "그리하여 바로 이튿날 그녀는 고더드 부인의 집을 찾아가, 사실을 털어놓는 필요한 속죄의 절차를 치렀으니, 그것은 혹독한 속죄였다. 그토록 부지런히 키워 온 모든 희망을 부숴 버려야 했고, 자기가 그 선택받은 사람이라는 야박한 처지로 나서야 했으며, 지난 여섯 주 동안 한 가지 문제에 관한 자신의 모든 생각, 모든 관찰, 모든 확신, 모든 예언이 죄다 어처구니없이 틀렸고 잘못된 판단이었음을 인정해야 했던 것이다."]], [["The confession completely renewed her first shame—and the sight of Harriet’s tears made her think that she should never be in charity with herself again.", "이 고백은 처음의 부끄러움을 송두리째 되살려 놓았다. 그리고 해리엇의 눈물을 보고 있자니, 다시는 스스로에게 떳떳할 수 없으리라는 생각마저 들었다."]], [["Harriet bore the intelligence very well—blaming nobody—and in every thing testifying such an ingenuousness of disposition and lowly opinion of herself, as must appear with particular advantage at that moment to her friend.", "해리엇은 그 소식을 아주 의젓하게 받아들였다. 누구도 탓하지 않았고, 매사에 어찌나 천진한 성품과 자신을 낮추는 겸손함을 드러내던지, 바로 그 순간 그 벗의 눈에는 그것이 유난히 도드라져 보일 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Emma was in the humour to value simplicity and modesty to the utmost; and all that was amiable, all that ought to be attaching, seemed on Harriet’s side, not her own.", "엠마는 그 순간 소박함과 겸손함을 더없이 높이 사고 싶은 심정이었다. 사랑스러운 모든 것, 마음을 끌 만한 모든 것이 자기 쪽이 아니라 해리엇 쪽에 있는 듯했다."], ["Harriet did not consider herself as having any thing to complain of.", "해리엇은 자기에게 불평할 거리가 조금이라도 있다고 여기지 않았다."], ["The affection of such a man as Mr. Elton would have been too great a distinction.—She never could have deserved him—and nobody but so partial and kind a friend as Miss Woodhouse would have thought it possible.", "미스터 엘턴 같은 남자의 애정이라면 자기에게는 과분한 영광이었을 것이고, 결코 그를 누릴 자격이 없었으며, 미스 우드하우스처럼 편애 어린 다정한 벗이 아니고서야 누구도 그것이 가능하리라 생각하지 못했으리라는 것이었다."]], [["Her tears fell abundantly—but her grief was so truly artless, that no dignity could have made it more respectable in Emma’s eyes—and she listened to her and tried to console her with all her heart and understanding—really for the time convinced that Harriet was the superior creature of the two—and that to resemble her would be more for her own welfare and happiness than all that genius or intelligence could do.", "해리엇의 눈물은 그칠 줄 모르고 흘렀다. 그러나 그 슬픔이 어찌나 진실로 꾸밈없던지, 어떤 위엄을 갖추었다 한들 엠마의 눈에 그보다 더 존중스러워 보일 수는 없었으리라. 엠마는 온 마음과 온 지혜를 다해 해리엇의 말에 귀 기울이고 위로하려 애썼다. 그러는 동안만큼은, 둘 중에 더 나은 사람은 해리엇이며, 그녀를 닮는 것이야말로 천재성이나 영민함이 할 수 있는 그 무엇보다 자신의 안녕과 행복에 보탬이 되리라고 정말로 믿었던 것이다."]], [["It was rather too late in the day to set about being simple-minded and ignorant; but she left her with every previous resolution confirmed of being humble and discreet, and repressing imagination all the rest of her life.", "이제 와서 소박하고 무지한 사람이 되어 보겠다고 마음먹기에는 너무 늦은 때였다. 그러나 그녀는 겸손하고 신중해지자고, 그리고 남은 평생 상상의 날개를 억누르자고 미리 다진 결심을 모조리 굳힌 채 해리엇을 떠났다."], ["Her second duty now, inferior only to her father’s claims, was to promote Harriet’s comfort, and endeavour to prove her own affection in some better method than by match-making.", "이제 그녀의 두 번째 의무는, 오직 아버지에 대한 의무에만 자리를 양보하는 그 의무는, 해리엇을 편안하게 해 주고, 중매보다 더 나은 방법으로 자신의 애정을 증명해 보이려 애쓰는 것이었다."], ["She got her to Hartfield, and shewed her the most unvarying kindness, striving to occupy and amuse her, and by books and conversation, to drive Mr. Elton from her thoughts.", "그녀는 해리엇을 하트필드로 데려와 한결같은 친절을 베풀었고, 책과 대화로 그녀의 마음을 붙들고 즐겁게 해 주며 미스터 엘턴을 그 생각에서 몰아내려 힘썼다."]], [["Time, she knew, must be allowed for this being thoroughly done; and she could suppose herself but an indifferent judge of such matters in general, and very inadequate to sympathise in an attachment to Mr.", "이 일이 온전히 이루어지려면 시간이 필요하다는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그리고 그녀로서는 이런 일에 대해 대체로 변변찮은 판단자에 지나지 않으며, 더구나 미스터 엘턴을 향한 연정에 공감하기에는 턱없이 부족하다고 스스로 인정할 수 있었다."], ["Elton in particular; but it seemed to her reasonable that at Harriet’s age, and with the entire extinction of all hope, such a progress might be made towards a state of composure by the time of Mr. Elton’s return, as to allow them all to meet again in the common routine of acquaintance, without any danger of betraying sentiments or increasing them.", "그러나 해리엇의 나이로 보아, 그리고 모든 희망이 완전히 사그라든 마당이니, 미스터 엘턴이 돌아올 무렵에는 마음의 평정을 향해 어지간히 나아가 있어서, 감정을 들키거나 더 키울 위험 없이 다 함께 예사로운 교제의 일상 속에서 다시 만날 수 있으리라고 보는 것이 사리에 맞을 듯했다."]], [["Harriet did think him all perfection, and maintained the non-existence of any body equal to him in person or goodness—and did, in truth, prove herself more resolutely in love than Emma had foreseen; but yet it appeared to her so natural, so inevitable to strive against an inclination of that sort unrequited, that she could not comprehend its continuing very long in equal force.", "해리엇은 정말로 그를 완벽 그 자체로 여겼고, 인물에서나 인품에서나 그에 견줄 사람은 세상에 없노라 우겼다. 그리고 실제로 엠마가 내다본 것보다 훨씬 더 단단히 사랑에 빠진 것으로 드러났다. 그러나 보답 없는 그런 마음에 맞서 싸우는 것은 너무도 당연하고 불가피한 일로 여겨졌으므로, 그 마음이 그토록 오래 같은 힘으로 이어지리라고는 도무지 짐작할 수 없었다."]], [["If Mr. Elton, on his return, made his own indifference as evident and indubitable as she could not doubt he would anxiously do, she could not imagine Harriet’s persisting to place her happiness in the sight or the recollection of him.", "미스터 엘턴이 돌아와서, 의심할 바 없이 애써 그러하리라 그녀가 믿어 마지않듯이, 자신의 무관심을 분명하고 의심할 여지없이 드러내 보인다면, 해리엇이 그를 보거나 떠올리는 데에 끝끝내 자기 행복을 걸어 두리라고는 상상할 수 없었다."]], [["Their being fixed, so absolutely fixed, in the same place, was bad for each, for all three.", "세 사람이 같은 곳에 붙박여, 그토록 꼼짝없이 붙박여 있다는 것은 저마다에게, 셋 모두에게 나쁜 일이었다."], ["Not one of them had the power of removal, or of effecting any material change of society.", "누구 하나 그곳을 떠날 수도, 교제의 형편을 이렇다 할 만큼 바꿔 놓을 수도 없었다."], ["They must encounter each other, and make the best of it.", "그들은 서로 마주칠 수밖에 없었고, 그 안에서 최선을 다하는 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Harriet was farther unfortunate in the tone of her companions at Mrs. Goddard’s; Mr.", "해리엇은 고더드 부인 집에 있는 동무들의 말투 탓에 한층 더 불운했다."], ["Elton being the adoration of all the teachers and great girls in the school; and it must be at Hartfield only that she could have any chance of hearing him spoken of with cooling moderation or repellent truth.", "미스터 엘턴은 그 학교의 모든 선생과 큰 여학생들이 떠받드는 우상이었기 때문이다. 그에 관한 이야기를 차분히 가라앉히는 절도나 마음을 식히는 진실과 더불어 들을 가망이 있는 곳은 오직 하트필드뿐이었다."], ["Where the wound had been given, there must the cure be found if anywhere; and Emma felt that, till she saw her in the way of cure, there could be no true peace for herself.", "상처가 난 곳, 거기에서 치유도 찾아야 하는 법이니, 어디에서든 찾을 수 있다면 그러했다. 그리고 엠마는, 해리엇이 치유의 길로 들어선 것을 보기 전에는 자기에게도 참된 평안은 있을 수 없으리라고 느꼈다."]], [["# Volume I — Chapter XVIII", "# 제1권 18장"]], [["Mr. Frank Churchill did not come.", "미스터 프랭크 처칠은 오지 않았다."], ["When the time proposed drew near, Mrs.", "약속한 날이 가까워지자, 핑계를 적은 편지가 도착함으로써 웨스턴 부인의 염려가 들어맞았다."], ["Weston’s fears were justified in the arrival of a letter of excuse.", "당분간은 도저히 시간을 낼 수 없다는 것이었다. \"더할 나위 없는 낙심과 유감\"을 안고서."], ["For the present, he could not be spared, to his “very great mortification and regret; but still he looked forward with the hope of coming to Randalls at no distant period.”", "\"그래도 머지않은 때에 랜덜스로 갈 수 있으리라는 희망만은 잃지 않고 있습니다.\" 하고 그는 적었다."]], [["Mrs. Weston was exceedingly disappointed—much more disappointed, in fact, than her husband, though her dependence on seeing the young man had been so much more sober: but a sanguine temper, though for ever expecting more good than occurs, does not always pay for its hopes by any proportionate depression.", "웨스턴 부인은 몹시 실망했다 — 실은 남편보다 훨씬 더 실망했는데, 그 젊은이를 만나리라는 기대는 그녀 쪽이 훨씬 차분했는데도 그러했다."], ["It soon flies over the present failure, and begins to hope again.", "그러나 낙천적인 기질이란, 일어나는 것보다 늘 더 많은 좋은 일을 기대하면서도, 그 기대에 걸맞은 침울함으로 늘 그 값을 치르는 것은 아니다. 그것은 이내 눈앞의 차질을 훌쩍 뛰어넘어, 다시 희망을 품기 시작한다."], ["For half an hour Mr.", "미스터 웨스턴은 반 시간쯤 놀라고 서운해했다."], ["Weston was surprized and sorry; but then he began to perceive that Frank’s coming two or three months later would be a much better plan; better time of year; better weather; and that he would be able, without any doubt, to stay considerably longer with them than if he had come sooner.", "그러나 곧 프랭크가 두세 달 뒤에 오는 편이 훨씬 나은 계획이라는 것을, 더 좋은 철에, 더 좋은 날씨에 오는 셈이고, 게다가 일찍 왔을 때보다 의심할 바 없이 훨씬 더 오래 함께 머물 수 있으리라는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다."]], [["These feelings rapidly restored his comfort, while Mrs.", "이런 느낌들이 그의 마음을 금세 도로 편안하게 해 준 반면, 좀 더 걱정 많은 성미인 웨스턴 부인은 핑계와 미룸이 되풀이되는 일밖에 내다보지 못했다."], ["Weston, of a more apprehensive disposition, foresaw nothing but a repetition of excuses and delays; and after all her concern for what her husband was to suffer, suffered a great deal more herself.", "그리하여 남편이 겪을 마음고생을 그토록 염려하고도, 정작 더 큰 고생을 한 것은 그녀 자신이었다."]], [["Emma was not at this time in a state of spirits to care really about Mr. Frank Churchill’s not coming, except as a disappointment at Randalls.", "이 무렵 엠마는, 랜덜스 쪽의 실망이라는 점을 빼고는, 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 오지 않는 일을 진심으로 마음에 둘 만한 심경이 아니었다."], ["The acquaintance at present had no charm for her.", "그와 알고 지내는 일이 지금으로서는 아무런 매력도 없었다."], ["She wanted, rather, to be quiet, and out of temptation; but still, as it was desirable that she should appear, in general, like her usual self, she took care to express as much interest in the circumstance, and enter as warmly into Mr.", "그녀는 차라리 조용히, 유혹에서 벗어나 있고 싶었다."], ["and Mrs. Weston’s disappointment, as might naturally belong to their friendship.", "그래도 겉으로는 대체로 평소의 자기다워 보이는 것이 바람직했으므로, 그들의 우정으로 보아 마땅할 만큼의 관심을 그 일에 드러내고, 미스터·미시즈 웨스턴의 실망에 그만큼 따뜻하게 동조하려고 신경을 썼다."]], [["She was the first to announce it to Mr.", "그녀는 그 소식을 미스터 나이틀리에게 가장 먼저 알린 사람이었다."], ["Knightley; and exclaimed quite as much as was necessary, (or, being acting a part, perhaps rather more,) at the conduct of the Churchills, in keeping him away.", "그리고 프랭크를 못 오게 붙들어 둔 처칠 집안의 처사를 두고, 꼭 필요한 만큼은 (어쩌면 연기를 하느라 다소 지나치게) 탄식해 보였다."], ["She then proceeded to say a good deal more than she felt, of the advantage of such an addition to their confined society in Surry; the pleasure of looking at somebody new; the gala-day to Highbury entire, which the sight of him would have made; and ending with reflections on the Churchills again, found herself directly involved in a disagreement with Mr.", "이어서 그녀는, 답답한 서리 지방의 한정된 사교계에 그런 새 인물이 보태지는 이점을, 새 얼굴을 바라보는 즐거움을, 그를 보았더라면 하이베리 전체에 베풀어졌을 축제 같은 하루를, 실제로 느끼는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이 늘어놓았다. 그러다 다시 처칠 집안에 대한 비난으로 말을 맺으려는 참에, 자신이 미스터 나이틀리와 곧장 의견 다툼에 빠져 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["Knightley; and, to her great amusement, perceived that she was taking the other side of the question from her real opinion, and making use of Mrs. Weston’s arguments against herself.", "그리고 무척 우습게도, 자신이 본심과는 정반대 편을 들면서, 웨스턴 부인의 논리를 끌어다 도리어 제 주장에 맞세우고 있음을 깨달았다."]], [["“The Churchills are very likely in fault,”", "\"처칠 집안 잘못일 공산이 크지.\" 미스터 나이틀리가 냉정하게 말했다."], ["said Mr. Knightley, coolly; “but I dare say he might come if he would.”", "\"하지만 마음만 먹으면 그 사람도 올 수 있었을 게 분명해.\""]], [["“I do not know why you should say so.", "\"왜 그렇게 말씀하시는지 모르겠네요."], ["He wishes exceedingly to come; but his uncle and aunt will not spare him.”", "그분은 오고 싶어 못 견뎌 하는데, 외삼촌과 외숙모가 놓아주질 않는다잖아요.\""]], [["“I cannot believe that he has not the power of coming, if he made a point of it.", "\"마음만 단단히 먹었다면 올 힘이 없었으리라곤 믿기지 않아."], ["It is too unlikely, for me to believe it without proof.”", "증거 없이 그대로 믿기엔 너무 그럴듯하지 않은 얘기야.\""]], [["“How odd you are!", "\"별스러우셔라!"], ["What has Mr. Frank Churchill done, to make you suppose him such an unnatural creature?”", "미스터 프랭크 처칠이 대체 무슨 짓을 했기에, 그렇게 인정머리 없는 사람으로 단정하시는 거예요?\""]], [["“I am not supposing him at all an unnatural creature, in suspecting that he may have learnt to be above his connexions, and to care very little for any thing but his own pleasure, from living with those who have always set him the example of it.", "\"인정머리 없는 사람이라고 단정하는 게 결코 아니야. 다만, 늘 그런 본을 보여 온 사람들과 함께 살다 보니, 제 인척들을 우습게 여기고 제 즐거움 말고는 무엇에도 마음 쓰지 않는 버릇이 들었으리라고 의심하는 것뿐이지."], ["It is a great deal more natural than one could wish, that a young man, brought up by those who are proud, luxurious, and selfish, should be proud, luxurious, and selfish too.", "거만하고 사치스럽고 이기적인 자들 손에 자란 젊은이가 저 또한 거만하고 사치스럽고 이기적인 사람이 되는 것은, 차라리 바라는 것보다 훨씬 자연스러운 일이거든."], ["If Frank Churchill had wanted to see his father, he would have contrived it between September and January.", "프랭크 처칠이 정말로 제 아버지를 보고 싶었다면, 구월에서 일월 사이에 어떻게든 짬을 내었을 거야. 그 나이의 남자가 — 대체 몇이지?"], ["A man at his age—what is he?—three or four-and-twenty—cannot be without the means of doing as much as that.", "스물서넛? — 그만한 일을 해낼 방도가 없을 리 없어."], ["It is impossible.”", "그건 불가능한 얘기야.\""]], [["“That’s easily said, and easily felt by you, who have always been your own master.", "\"그건 늘 자기 몸의 주인으로 살아오신 분이니 쉽게 하시는 말씀이고, 쉽게 드는 생각이죠."], ["You are the worst judge in the world, Mr. Knightley, of the difficulties of dependence.", "미스터 나이틀리, 매인 몸의 고충에 대해서라면 당신은 세상에서 가장 형편없는 판관이세요."], ["You do not know what it is to have tempers to manage.”", "남의 비위를 맞춰 가며 사는 게 어떤 건지 모르시잖아요.\""]], [["“It is not to be conceived that a man of three or four-and-twenty should not have liberty of mind or limb to that amount.", "\"스물서넛 먹은 남자가 그만한 정도의 정신적·신체적 자유조차 없다는 건 도무지 생각할 수 없는 일이야."], ["He cannot want money—he cannot want leisure.", "돈이 부족할 리 없고 — 한가한 시간이 부족할 리도 없지."], ["We know, on the contrary, that he has so much of both, that he is glad to get rid of them at the idlest haunts in the kingdom.", "오히려 우리가 아는 바로는, 그가 그 둘을 어찌나 넉넉히 가졌는지, 이 나라에서 제일 한량 같은 곳들에서 그것들을 기꺼이 탕진하고 다닐 정도잖아."], ["We hear of him for ever at some watering-place or other.", "어느 휴양지에서든 늘 그의 소식이 들려오니까."], ["A little while ago, he was at Weymouth.", "얼마 전에도 웨이머스에 있었지."], ["This proves that he can leave the Churchills.”", "이건 그가 처칠 집안을 떠날 수 있다는 증거야.\""]], [["“Yes, sometimes he can.”", "\"네, 가끔은요.\""]], [["“And those times are whenever he thinks it worth his while; whenever there is any temptation of pleasure.”", "\"그리고 그 '가끔'이란, 제가 그럴 만한 가치가 있다고 여길 때마다, 즐거움의 유혹이 있을 때마다겠지.\""]], [["“It is very unfair to judge of any body’s conduct, without an intimate knowledge of their situation.", "\"누군가의 처지를 속속들이 알지도 못하면서 그 행실을 판단하는 건 몹시 부당한 일이에요."], ["Nobody, who has not been in the interior of a family, can say what the difficulties of any individual of that family may be.", "한 집안의 속사정에 들어가 보지 않은 사람은, 그 집안 어느 한 사람의 고충이 어떠한지 함부로 말할 수 없어요."], ["We ought to be acquainted with Enscombe, and with Mrs. Churchill’s temper, before we pretend to decide upon what her nephew can do.", "그 조카가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 단정하기 전에, 우리는 먼저 엔스컴이 어떤 곳인지, 처칠 부인의 성미가 어떤지부터 알아야 마땅하죠."], ["He may, at times, be able to do a great deal more than he can at others.”", "그 사람은 어떤 때는 다른 때보다 훨씬 많은 일을 할 수 있을지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“There is one thing, Emma, which a man can always do, if he chuses, and that is, his duty; not by manoeuvring and finessing, but by vigour and resolution.", "\"엠마, 한 가지만은 사람이 마음만 먹으면 언제든 할 수 있어. 바로 제 도리야 — 술수를 부리거나 잔재주를 피워서가 아니라, 기개와 결단으로 말이지."], ["It is Frank Churchill’s duty to pay this attention to his father.", "아버지에게 이만한 관심을 표하는 것은 프랭크 처칠의 도리야."], ["He knows it to be so, by his promises and messages; but if he wished to do it, it might be done.", "본인도 그렇다는 걸 알고 있어, 제가 한 약속과 전갈로 보아 말이지. 그러니 정말로 하고 싶었다면 해낼 수 있었을 거야."], ["A man who felt rightly would say at once, simply and resolutely, to Mrs.", "도리를 옳게 느끼는 남자라면, 처칠 부인에게 즉시 이렇게 말했겠지 — 간단하고 단호하게."], ["Churchill—‘Every sacrifice of mere pleasure you will always find me ready to make to your convenience; but I must go and see my father immediately.", "'한낱 즐거움을 희생하는 일이라면 외숙모님 편하시도록 언제든 기꺼이 응하겠습니다. 하지만 지금 당장 가서 아버지를 뵈어야겠습니다."], ["I know he would be hurt by my failing in such a mark of respect to him on the present occasion.", "이런 때에 그분께 마땅히 표해야 할 예의를 저버린다면 아버지께서 상심하시리라는 걸 압니다."], ["I shall, therefore, set off to-morrow.’—If he would say so to her at once, in the tone of decision becoming a man, there would be no opposition made to his going.”", "그러니 내일 떠나겠습니다.' — 이렇게 남자다운 결단의 어조로 단번에 말했더라면, 그가 가는 일에 아무 반대도 없었을 거야.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"없겠죠.\""], ["said Emma, laughing; “but perhaps there might be some made to his coming back again.", "엠마가 웃으며 말했다. \"하지만 다시 돌아오는 일에는 어쩌면 반대가 좀 있었을지도 몰라요."], ["Such language for a young man entirely dependent, to use!—Nobody but you, Mr.", "전적으로 남에게 매여 사는 젊은이가 그런 말을 입에 올리다니요! 미스터 나이틀리, 당신 말고는 아무도 그게 가능하다고 상상하지 못할 거예요."], ["Knightley, would imagine it possible.", "하지만 당신은, 자기 처지와 정반대인 처지에서는 무엇이 필요한지 도무지 짐작도 못 하시는걸요."], ["But you have not an idea of what is requisite in situations directly opposite to your own. Mr.", "미스터 프랭크 처칠이 자기를 길러 주고 또 앞날까지 책임져 줄 외삼촌·외숙모에게 그런 연설을 하다니요!"], ["Frank Churchill to be making such a speech as that to the uncle and aunt, who have brought him up, and are to provide for him!—Standing up in the middle of the room, I suppose, and speaking as loud as he could!—How can you imagine such conduct practicable?”", "방 한가운데 떡 버티고 서서, 목청껏 소리 높여 말하는 모습이라도 그리시는 거예요? 그런 행동을 어떻게 실행할 수 있다고 상상하시죠?\""]], [["“Depend upon it, Emma, a sensible man would find no difficulty in it.", "\"엠마, 분별 있는 남자라면 그쯤은 조금도 어렵지 않게 해낸다고 믿어도 좋아."], ["He would feel himself in the right; and the declaration—made, of course, as a man of sense would make it, in a proper manner—would do him more good, raise him higher, fix his interest stronger with the people he depended on, than all that a line of shifts and expedients can ever do.", "제가 옳다는 걸 느낄 테고, 그 선언은 — 물론 분별 있는 사람이 으레 하듯 온당한 태도로 한 것이겠지 — 온갖 둘러대기와 임시방편을 다 동원하는 것보다 그에게 훨씬 이로울 거야. 그의 입지를 높여 주고, 그가 의지하는 사람들 사이에서 그의 신망을 더 단단히 굳혀 줄 테니까."], ["Respect would be added to affection.", "애정에 존경까지 더해질 거란 말이지. 그들은 그를 믿어도 되겠다고 느끼게 될 거야."], ["They would feel that they could trust him; that the nephew who had done rightly by his father, would do rightly by them; for they know, as well as he does, as well as all the world must know, that he ought to pay this visit to his father; and while meanly exerting their power to delay it, are in their hearts not thinking the better of him for submitting to their whims.", "아버지에게 도리를 다한 조카라면 자기들에게도 도리를 다하리라고 말이지. 그도 알고, 그들도 알고, 온 세상이 알 수밖에 없는 사실이거든 — 그가 마땅히 아버지를 찾아뵈어야 한다는 것을."], ["Respect for right conduct is felt by every body.", "한편으로는 옹졸하게 권력을 휘둘러 그 걸음을 미루게 하면서도, 그가 제 변덕에 순순히 따르는 것을 두고 마음속으로는 결코 그를 더 높이 보지 않는단 말이지."], ["If he would act in this sort of manner, on principle, consistently, regularly, their little minds would bend to his.”", "옳은 행실에 대한 존경은 누구나 느끼는 거야. 그가 이런 식으로, 원칙에 따라 한결같이, 흐트러짐 없이 처신한다면, 그들의 옹졸한 마음이 도리어 그에게 굽혀 들 거야.\""]], [["“I rather doubt that.", "\"글쎄요, 그건 좀 의심스러운데요."], ["You are very fond of bending little minds; but where little minds belong to rich people in authority, I think they have a knack of swelling out, till they are quite as unmanageable as great ones.", "당신은 옹졸한 마음을 굽히게 하는 일을 무척 좋아하시지만, 옹졸한 마음이 권세 쥔 부자에게 붙어 있으면, 그건 한껏 부풀어 올라서 결국 큰 마음 못지않게 다루기 힘들어지는 재주가 있거든요."], ["I can imagine, that if you, as you are, Mr. Knightley, were to be transported and placed all at once in Mr.", "상상은 할 수 있어요 — 지금 그대로의 당신, 미스터 나이틀리가 어느 날 갑자기 옮겨져 미스터 프랭크 처칠의 처지에 놓인다면, 당신은 방금 그분에게 권하신 말과 행동을 그대로 해낼 수 있겠죠."], ["Frank Churchill’s situation, you would be able to say and do just what you have been recommending for him; and it might have a very good effect.", "그리고 효과도 아주 좋을 거예요. 처칠 집안은 한마디 대꾸도 못 할지 몰라요."], ["The Churchills might not have a word to say in return; but then, you would have no habits of early obedience and long observance to break through.", "하지만 그건 당신에게는 떨쳐 내야 할, 어릴 적부터 몸에 밴 복종의 습관도, 오랜 순종의 관습도 없기 때문이죠."], ["To him who has, it might not be so easy to burst forth at once into perfect independence, and set all their claims on his gratitude and regard at nought.", "그런 게 몸에 밴 사람으로서는, 단번에 완전한 독립으로 박차고 일어나 그들이 제 고마움과 존경에 거는 온갖 권리를 깡그리 무시한다는 게 그리 쉽지 않을지도 몰라요."], ["He may have as strong a sense of what would be right, as you can have, without being so equal, under particular circumstances, to act up to it.”", "무엇이 옳은지에 대한 분별이야 그분도 당신 못지않게 강할지 모르지만, 특정한 상황에서 그것을 실행에 옮기는 일에서는 그만큼 대등하지 못할 수도 있는 거예요.\""]], [["“Then it would not be so strong a sense.", "\"그렇다면 그건 그만큼 강한 분별이 아니야."], ["If it failed to produce equal exertion, it could not be an equal conviction.”", "똑같은 노력을 끌어내지 못한다면, 그건 똑같은 신념일 수 없으니까.\""]], [["“Oh, the difference of situation and habit!", "\"아, 처지와 습관의 차이라니까요!"], ["I wish you would try to understand what an amiable young man may be likely to feel in directly opposing those, whom as child and boy he has been looking up to all his life.”", "어릴 적부터 줄곧 우러러보며 자라 온 사람들을 정면으로 거스를 때, 마음씨 고운 젊은이가 어떤 심정일지를 좀 헤아려 보려고 애써 주셨으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Our amiable young man is a very weak young man, if this be the first occasion of his carrying through a resolution to do right against the will of others.", "\"그 '마음씨 고운 젊은이'가 옳은 일을 하려는 결심을 남들의 뜻을 거스르며 끝까지 밀어붙이는 게 이번이 처음이라면, 그건 아주 나약한 젊은이야."], ["It ought to have been a habit with him by this time, of following his duty, instead of consulting expediency.", "이쯤 됐으면 편의를 따지는 대신 제 도리를 좇는 일이 벌써 그에게 습관이 되었어야 마땅하지."], ["I can allow for the fears of the child, but not of the man.", "아이의 두려움이야 봐줄 수 있어도, 어른의 두려움은 봐줄 수 없어."], ["As he became rational, he ought to have roused himself and shaken off all that was unworthy in their authority.", "철이 들면서 그는 마땅히 분발하여, 그들 권위 속의 떳떳지 못한 것을 다 떨쳐 냈어야 해."], ["He ought to have opposed the first attempt on their side to make him slight his father.", "제 아버지를 가벼이 여기게 하려는 그들의 첫 시도에 맞섰어야 한다고."], ["Had he begun as he ought, there would have been no difficulty now.”", "마땅히 해야 할 대로 시작했더라면, 지금 와서 무슨 어려움이 있을 리 없지.\""]], [["“We shall never agree about him,”", "\"그분에 대해서라면 우리는 영영 의견이 안 맞겠네요.\" 엠마가 소리쳤다."], ["cried Emma; “but that is nothing extraordinary.", "\"하지만 그건 별로 이상한 일도 아니죠. 저는 그분이 나약한 젊은이라곤 조금도 생각하지 않아요."], ["I have not the least idea of his being a weak young man: I feel sure that he is not.", "절대 아니라고 확신해요. 미스터 웨스턴이라면 제 아들이라 해도 어리석음에 눈멀지 않으실 거예요."], ["Mr. Weston would not be blind to folly, though in his own son; but he is very likely to have a more yielding, complying, mild disposition than would suit your notions of man’s perfection.", "다만 그분은, 당신이 생각하시는 완벽한 남자상에 들어맞기보다는, 좀 더 무르고 순응적이고 온순한 성품일 공산이 크죠."], ["I dare say he has; and though it may cut him off from some advantages, it will secure him many others.”", "분명 그러리라 싶어요. 그리고 그런 성품 탓에 어떤 이점은 잃을지 몰라도, 그 대신 다른 많은 이점을 확실히 얻게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes; all the advantages of sitting still when he ought to move, and of leading a life of mere idle pleasure, and fancying himself extremely expert in finding excuses for it.", "\"그렇겠지. 움직여야 할 때 가만 앉아 있는 온갖 이점이며, 한낱 게으른 즐거움의 삶을 누리면서, 거기에 변명거리를 찾아내는 데 제가 더없이 능하다고 우쭐대는 이점까지 말이지."], ["He can sit down and write a fine flourishing letter, full of professions and falsehoods, and persuade himself that he has hit upon the very best method in the world of preserving peace at home and preventing his father’s having any right to complain.", "그는 앉아서, 다짐과 거짓말로 가득한 번지르르한 편지나 한 통 써 내려가고는, 집안의 평화를 지키면서 아버지에게 불평할 빌미조차 주지 않을 세상에서 가장 좋은 방법을 자기가 찾아냈다고 스스로를 설득하겠지."], ["His letters disgust me.”", "그자의 편지들은 정말 역겨워.\""]], [["“Your feelings are singular.", "\"느끼시는 바가 참 별나시네요."], ["They seem to satisfy every body else.”", "다른 사람들은 다들 그 편지에 흡족해하는 것 같던데요.\""]], [["“I suspect they do not satisfy Mrs. Weston.", "\"웨스턴 부인은 흡족하지 않으리라 짐작해."], ["They hardly can satisfy a woman of her good sense and quick feelings: standing in a mother’s place, but without a mother’s affection to blind her.", "그만한 양식과 예민한 감성을 지닌 여인이 흡족할 리 없지. 어머니 자리에 서 있으되, 눈을 멀게 할 어머니의 애정은 없는 사람이니까."], ["It is on her account that attention to Randalls is doubly due, and she must doubly feel the omission.", "랜덜스에 대한 관심이 갑절로 마땅한 것은 바로 그녀 때문이고, 그녀로서는 그 소홀함을 갑절로 느낄 수밖에 없어."], ["Had she been a person of consequence herself, he would have come I dare say; and it would not have signified whether he did or no.", "그녀가 만약 대단한 신분의 사람이었다면, 그는 분명 왔을 거야. 그리고 그랬다면 그가 오든 말든 별 상관도 없었겠지."], ["Can you think your friend behindhand in these sort of considerations?", "자네 친구가 이런 종류의 생각에 뒤처져 있을 것 같나? 그녀가 이런 말을 마음속으로 수도 없이 되뇌지 않으리라 보나?"], ["Do you suppose she does not often say all this to herself?", "아니야, 엠마, 자네의 그 마음씨 고운 젊은이는 프랑스어로나 고울 뿐, 영어로는 곱지가 못해."], ["No, Emma, your amiable young man can be amiable only in French, not in English.", "그는 무척 '상냥'하고, 예의범절도 아주 훌륭하고, 더없이 붙임성 있는 사람일지 몰라."], ["He may be very ‘amiable,’ have very good manners, and be very agreeable; but he can have no English delicacy towards the feelings of other people: nothing really amiable about him.”", "하지만 남의 감정을 헤아리는 영국식 섬세함이라곤 없어. 진정으로 마음씨 고운 구석이라곤 하나도 없는 사람이지.\""]], [["“You seem determined to think ill of him.”", "\"기어이 그분을 나쁘게 보기로 작정하셨군요.\""]], [["“Me!—not at all,”", "\"내가?"], ["replied Mr.", "천만에.\""], ["Knightley, rather displeased; “I do not want to think ill of him.", "미스터 나이틀리가 다소 언짢은 듯 대꾸했다. \"그를 나쁘게 보고 싶은 마음은 없어."], ["I should be as ready to acknowledge his merits as any other man; but I hear of none, except what are merely personal; that he is well-grown and good-looking, with smooth, plausible manners.”", "나도 누구 못지않게 기꺼이 그의 장점을 인정할 거야. 하지만 들리는 거라곤 한낱 외모에 관한 것뿐이지 — 체격 좋고 인물 훤하며, 매끄럽고 그럴듯한 몸가짐을 지녔다는 것 말고는.\""]], [["“Well, if he have nothing else to recommend him, he will be a treasure at Highbury.", "\"뭐, 달리 내세울 게 없다 해도, 그분은 하이베리에서 보배가 될 거예요."], ["We do not often look upon fine young men, well-bred and agreeable.", "우리가 잘 자라고 붙임성 있는 훤칠한 젊은이를 자주 보는 것도 아니잖아요."], ["We must not be nice and ask for all the virtues into the bargain.", "그러니 까다롭게 굴면서 거기에 온갖 미덕까지 덤으로 갖추라고 요구해서는 안 되죠."], ["Cannot you imagine, Mr. Knightley, what a sensation his coming will produce?", "미스터 나이틀리, 그분이 오면 어떤 소동이 벌어질지 상상도 안 되세요?"], ["There will be but one subject throughout the parishes of Donwell and Highbury; but one interest—one object of curiosity; it will be all Mr.", "돈웰과 하이베리 두 교구를 통틀어 화제는 오직 하나뿐일 거예요. 오직 하나의 관심사, 하나의 호기심의 대상 — 온통 미스터 프랭크 처칠 얘기뿐이겠죠."], ["Frank Churchill; we shall think and speak of nobody else.”", "다들 다른 사람은 생각도 입에 올리지도 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“You will excuse my being so much over-powered.", "\"내가 그토록 압도당하지 못하는 건 양해해 주게."], ["If I find him conversable, I shall be glad of his acquaintance; but if he is only a chattering coxcomb, he will not occupy much of my time or thoughts.”", "말이 통하는 사람이라면 알고 지내게 되어 기쁘겠지만, 그저 재잘대는 멋쟁이 허풍선이에 불과하다면, 내 시간이나 생각을 그리 많이 차지하진 못할 거야.\""]], [["“My idea of him is, that he can adapt his conversation to the taste of every body, and has the power as well as the wish of being universally agreeable.", "\"제 생각엔, 그분은 누구의 취향에든 맞춰 대화할 줄 알고, 또 모든 사람에게 두루 호감을 살 만한 능력과 더불어 그러고자 하는 마음까지 지닌 분이에요. 당신에게는 농사 얘기를, 저에게는 그림이나 음악 얘기를, 그런 식으로 누구에게든 맞춰 가면서요."], ["To you, he will talk of farming; to me, of drawing or music; and so on to every body, having that general information on all subjects which will enable him to follow the lead, or take the lead, just as propriety may require, and to speak extremely well on each; that is my idea of him.”", "온갖 주제에 대한 두루뭉술한 지식을 갖춘 덕에, 자리에 어울리는 대로 남의 말을 따라가기도 하고 직접 이끌기도 하면서, 어느 화제든 아주 능란하게 풀어낼 수 있을 거예요. 그게 제가 그분을 그리는 모습이에요.\""]], [["“And mine,” said Mr.", "\"내가 그리는 모습은 말이지,\""], ["Knightley warmly, “is, that if he turn out any thing like it, he will be the most insufferable fellow breathing!", "미스터 나이틀리가 발끈하여 말했다. \"만약 그가 그 비슷한 사람으로 드러난다면, 살아 숨 쉬는 인간 가운데 가장 못 견딜 작자가 되리라는 거야!"], ["What! at three-and-twenty to be the king of his company—the great man—the practised politician, who is to read every body’s character, and make every body’s talents conduce to the display of his own superiority; to be dispensing his flatteries around, that he may make all appear like fools compared with himself!", "아니, 스물셋에 좌중의 왕 노릇이라니 — 거물 행세에, 모두의 성품을 읽어 내고 모두의 재주를 끌어다 제 잘난 점을 돋보이게 하는 데 써먹는, 닳고 닳은 정객이라니 — 사방에 아첨을 뿌려 대며, 저 자신에 비하면 다들 바보로 보이게 만들다니!"], ["My dear Emma, your own good sense could not endure such a puppy when it came to the point.”", "엠마, 정작 그 자리에 닥치면 자네의 그 분별력으로도 그런 멋쟁이 풋내기를 결코 견디지 못할걸.\""]], [["“I will say no more about him,”", "\"그분 얘긴 더는 안 할래요.\""], ["cried Emma, “you turn every thing to evil.", "엠마가 소리쳤다. \"당신은 뭐든 다 나쁘게만 돌리시니까요."], ["We are both prejudiced; you against, I for him; and we have no chance of agreeing till he is really here.”", "우리 둘 다 편견에 사로잡혀 있어요 — 당신은 그분을 반대하는 쪽으로, 저는 두둔하는 쪽으로요. 그러니 그분이 정말로 여기 오기 전까지는 의견이 맞을 가망이 없네요.\""]], [["“Prejudiced!", "\"편견이라니!"], ["I am not prejudiced.”", "나는 편견 같은 거 없어.\""]], [["“But I am very much, and without being at all ashamed of it.", "\"하지만 저는 잔뜩 있어요."], ["My love for Mr. and Mrs.", "그리고 조금도 부끄럽지 않고요."], ["Weston gives me a decided prejudice in his favour.”", "미스터·미시즈 웨스턴을 사랑하는 마음 때문에, 저는 그분을 두둔하는 쪽으로 확실히 편견을 갖고 있는걸요.\""]], [["“He is a person I never think of from one month’s end to another,” said Mr.", "\"그 사람은 내가 한 달이 가고 또 한 달이 가도 도통 생각조차 안 하는 사람이야.\""], ["Knightley, with a degree of vexation, which made Emma immediately talk of something else, though she could not comprehend why he should be angry.", "미스터 나이틀리가 어느 정도 짜증 섞인 투로 말했다. 그 바람에 엠마는 곧장 다른 화제로 말을 돌렸으나, 그가 왜 화를 내는지는 도무지 알 수 없었다."]], [["To take a dislike to a young man, only because he appeared to be of a different disposition from himself, was unworthy the real liberality of mind which she was always used to acknowledge in him; for with all the high opinion of himself, which she had often laid to his charge, she had never before for a moment supposed it could make him unjust to the merit of another.", "한 젊은이가 그저 자기와 기질이 다른 듯 보인다는 이유만으로 그를 못마땅해하는 것은, 그녀가 늘 그에게서 인정해 온 진정 너그러운 마음에 어울리지 않는 일이었다. 그녀가 종종 그를 두고 자부심이 지나치다고 탓해 오기는 했어도, 그 자부심이 그로 하여금 남의 장점에 부당해지게 할 수 있으리라고는 이제껏 단 한 순간도 생각해 본 적이 없었기 때문이다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter I", "# 제2권 1장"]], [["Emma and Harriet had been walking together one morning, and, in Emma’s opinion, had been talking enough of Mr. Elton for that day.", "엠마와 해리엇은 어느 날 아침 함께 산책을 했는데, 엠마가 보기에 그날 하루치로는 미스터 엘턴 이야기를 충분히 한 셈이었다."], ["She could not think that Harriet’s solace or her own sins required more; and she was therefore industriously getting rid of the subject as they returned;—but it burst out again when she thought she had succeeded, and after speaking some time of what the poor must suffer in winter, and receiving no other answer than a very plaintive—“Mr.", "해리엇의 위안에도 자신의 죄에도 그 이상은 필요치 않으리라 여긴 엠마는, 돌아오는 길 내내 부지런히 그 화제를 떨쳐 내려 애썼다. 그러나 성공했다 싶을 무렵 그것은 다시 불쑥 솟아올랐고, 겨울에 가난한 사람들이 얼마나 고생할지를 한참 이야기한 끝에 돌아온 대답이라고는 더없이 구슬픈 — \"미스터 엘턴은 가난한 사람들한테 어찌나 잘해 주는지!\""], ["Elton is so good to the poor!” she found something else must be done.", "한마디뿐이라, 엠마는 무언가 다른 수를 써야겠다고 깨달았다."]], [["They were just approaching the house where lived Mrs. and Miss Bates.", "마침 두 사람은 베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠가 사는 집 가까이에 이르러 있었다."], ["She determined to call upon them and seek safety in numbers.", "엠마는 그 집에 들러 머릿수로 안전을 도모하기로 마음먹었다."], ["There was always sufficient reason for such an attention; Mrs.", "그런 친절을 베풀 만한 명분이야 언제나 충분했다."], ["and Miss Bates loved to be called on, and she knew she was considered by the very few who presumed ever to see imperfection in her, as rather negligent in that respect, and as not contributing what she ought to the stock of their scanty comforts.", "베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠는 방문받기를 좋아했고, 또 엠마는 자기에게서 결점을 볼 줄 아는 극소수의 사람들 사이에서 자기가 이 점에 다소 소홀하다는 평을 듣는다는 것을, 그들의 빈약한 위안거리에 마땅히 보태야 할 몫을 보태지 않는다는 평을 듣는다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["She had had many a hint from Mr. Knightley and some from her own heart, as to her deficiency—but none were equal to counteract the persuasion of its being very disagreeable,—a waste of time—tiresome women—and all the horror of being in danger of falling in with the second-rate and third-rate of Highbury, who were calling on them for ever, and therefore she seldom went near them.", "이 부족함에 대해서는 미스터 나이틀리에게서 여러 번 귀띔을 들었고 제 마음으로부터도 더러 들었지만 — 그 어느 것도, 그것이 몹시 거북한 일이라는 생각을, 시간 낭비요, 따분한 여자들이요, 그들을 끊임없이 찾아드는 하이베리의 이류 삼류 인사들과 어울릴 위험이라는 온갖 끔찍함을 이겨 낼 만큼은 못 되었다."], ["But now she made the sudden resolution of not passing their door without going in—observing, as she proposed it to Harriet, that, as well as she could calculate, they were just now quite safe from any letter from Jane Fairfax.", "그래서 엠마는 좀처럼 그들 근처에 가지 않았다. 그러나 이번에는 문 앞을 그냥 지나치지 않고 들어가 보리라 불쑥 결심했고 — 해리엇에게 그 뜻을 밝히면서, 자기가 셈해 본 바로는 지금쯤이면 제인 페어팩스의 편지 따위는 안전하게 피할 수 있는 때라고 일렀다."]], [["The house belonged to people in business. Mrs.", "그 집은 장사하는 사람들의 소유였다."], ["and Miss Bates occupied the drawing-room floor; and there, in the very moderate-sized apartment, which was every thing to them, the visitors were most cordially and even gratefully welcomed; the quiet neat old lady, who with her knitting was seated in the warmest corner, wanting even to give up her place to Miss Woodhouse, and her more active, talking daughter, almost ready to overpower them with care and kindness, thanks for their visit, solicitude for their shoes, anxious inquiries after Mr.", "베이츠 부인과 미스 베이츠는 응접실 층을 쓰고 있었다. 그곳, 그들에게는 전부나 다름없는 그 아주 아담한 방에서 두 손님은 더없이 다정하게, 심지어 고마워하는 마음으로까지 환대받았다."], ["Woodhouse’s health, cheerful communications about her mother’s, and sweet-cake from the beaufet—“Mrs. Cole had just been there, just called in for ten minutes, and had been so good as to sit an hour with them, and she had taken a piece of cake and been so kind as to say she liked it very much; and, therefore, she hoped Miss Woodhouse and Miss Smith would do them the favour to eat a piece too.”", "가장 따뜻한 구석에 뜨개질감을 들고 앉아 있던 조용하고 단정한 노부인은 미스 우드하우스에게 자리까지 내주려 했고, 더 활동적이고 말 많은 딸은 미스 우드하우스 일행을 보살핌과 친절로 거의 압도할 지경이었다 — 방문해 주어 고맙다는 인사, 신발 걱정, 미스터 우드하우스의 건강을 묻는 근심 어린 물음, 어머니의 건강에 대한 명랑한 소식, 그리고 찬장에서 꺼낸 달콤한 케이크까지 — \"콜 부인이 방금 다녀가셨어요, 잠깐 십 분만 들르신다더니 어찌나 친절하신지 한 시간이나 함께 앉아 계셨지 뭐예요, 게다가 케이크도 한 조각 드시고는 어찌나 고맙게도 아주 맛있다고 말씀해 주시던지요, 그래서 미스 우드하우스랑 미스 스미스도 한 조각씩 들어 주시면 정말 고맙겠어요.\""]], [["The mention of the Coles was sure to be followed by that of Mr. Elton.", "콜 부부 이야기가 나오면 으레 미스터 엘턴 이야기가 뒤따르기 마련이었다."], ["There was intimacy between them, and Mr. Cole had heard from Mr.", "두 집은 가깝게 지내는 사이였고, 미스터 콜은 미스터 엘턴이 떠난 뒤로 그에게서 소식을 들은 터였다."], ["Elton since his going away.", "엠마는 무슨 이야기가 나올지 알았다."], ["Emma knew what was coming; they must have the letter over again, and settle how long he had been gone, and how much he was engaged in company, and what a favourite he was wherever he went, and how full the Master of the Ceremonies’ ball had been; and she went through it very well, with all the interest and all the commendation that could be requisite, and always putting forward to prevent Harriet’s being obliged to say a word.", "그 편지를 다시 한번 들어야 할 테고, 그가 떠난 지 얼마나 됐는지, 사교 모임에 얼마나 자주 어울리는지, 가는 곳마다 얼마나 인기인지, 무도회 주관자가 연 무도회가 얼마나 성황이었는지를 두루 짚어야 할 터였다. 엠마는 필요한 만큼의 관심과 칭찬을 다 갖추어 그 일을 아주 무난히 치러 냈고, 해리엇이 한마디라도 해야 하는 처지에 놓이지 않도록 늘 앞장서서 나섰다."]], [["This she had been prepared for when she entered the house; but meant, having once talked him handsomely over, to be no farther incommoded by any troublesome topic, and to wander at large amongst all the Mistresses and Misses of Highbury, and their card-parties.", "이 정도는 집에 들어설 때부터 각오한 바였다. 그러나 일단 그를 한바탕 그럴듯하게 이야기하고 나면 그다음부터는 어떤 성가신 화제에도 더 시달리지 않고, 하이베리의 온갖 부인네들과 아가씨들과 그들의 카드 모임 사이를 마음껏 누비고 다닐 작정이었다."], ["She had not been prepared to have Jane Fairfax succeed Mr.", "미스터 엘턴의 뒤를 제인 페어팩스가 이어받으리라고는 미처 대비하지 못했다."], ["Elton; but he was actually hurried off by Miss Bates, she jumped away from him at last abruptly to the Coles, to usher in a letter from her niece.", "그런데 미스 베이츠가 정말로 그를 서둘러 밀어내고는, 마침내 그에게서 콜 부부 쪽으로 불쑥 건너뛰더니, 조카에게서 온 편지 이야기로 끌고 가는 것이었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["yes—Mr. Elton, I understand—certainly as to dancing—Mrs.", "그럼요 — 미스터 엘턴, 알겠어요 — 춤 이야기야 물론이고요 — 콜 부인이 그러시는데 바스의 무도회장에서 춤이 — 콜 부인이 어찌나 친절하신지 한참을 함께 앉아 제인 이야기를 나눠 주셨어요."], ["Cole was telling me that dancing at the rooms at Bath was—Mrs.", "들어오시자마자 제인 안부부터 물으시던걸요, 거기서 제인이 어찌나 사랑받는지요."], ["Cole was so kind as to sit some time with us, talking of Jane; for as soon as she came in, she began inquiring after her, Jane is so very great a favourite there.", "제인이 우리랑 함께 있을 때면 콜 부인은 친절을 어떻게 다 베풀어야 할지 모르실 정도예요. 정말이지 제인은 누구 못지않게 그런 대접을 받을 자격이 있고말고요."], ["Whenever she is with us, Mrs.", "그래서 곧장 안부를 물으시면서 이러시지 뭐예요."], ["Cole does not know how to shew her kindness enough; and I must say that Jane deserves it as much as any body can.", "'요즘 제인한테서 소식 못 들으셨겠죠, 편지 쓸 때가 아직 안 됐으니까요.' 그래서 제가 얼른 '아니에요, 정말 들었어요, 바로 오늘 아침에 편지가 왔답니다.'"], ["And so she began inquiring after her directly, saying, ‘I know you cannot have heard from Jane lately, because it is not her time for writing;’ and when I immediately said, ‘But indeed we have, we had a letter this very morning,’ I do not know that I ever saw any body more surprized.", "했더니, 그렇게 놀라는 사람을 제가 본 적이 있나 싶어요. '정말로요?"], ["‘Have you, upon your honour?’ said she; ‘well, that is quite unexpected.", "맹세코요?' 하시면서 '어머, 그거 정말 뜻밖이네요."], ["Do let me hear what she says.’”", "어디 뭐라고 썼는지 좀 들려주세요.' 하시는 거예요.\""]], [["Emma’s politeness was at hand directly, to say, with smiling interest—", "엠마는 곧바로 예의를 차려, 미소 띤 관심으로 말했다 —"]], [["“Have you heard from Miss Fairfax so lately?", "\"미스 페어팩스한테서 그렇게 최근에 소식을 들으셨어요?"], ["I am extremely happy.", "정말 기쁘네요."], ["I hope she is well?”", "그분이 건강하시면 좋겠어요?\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고마워요."], ["You are so kind!”", "어쩜 그렇게 친절하신지!\""], ["replied the happily deceived aunt, while eagerly hunting for the letter.—“Oh!", "행복하게 속아 넘어간 이모는 부지런히 편지를 찾으면서 대답했다. \"아!"], ["here it is.", "여기 있네요."], ["I was sure it could not be far off; but I had put my huswife upon it, you see, without being aware, and so it was quite hid, but I had it in my hand so very lately that I was almost sure it must be on the table. I was reading it to Mrs.", "멀리 갈 리가 없다 했지요, 그런데 글쎄 나도 모르게 반짇고리를 그 위에 올려놓는 바람에 그만 가려져 버렸지 뭐예요, 하지만 조금 전까지 손에 들고 있었으니 분명 탁자 위 어디 있겠거니 했지요."], ["Cole, and since she went away, I was reading it again to my mother, for it is such a pleasure to her—a letter from Jane—that she can never hear it often enough; so I knew it could not be far off, and here it is, only just under my huswife—and since you are so kind as to wish to hear what she says;—but, first of all, I really must, in justice to Jane, apologise for her writing so short a letter—only two pages you see—hardly two—and in general she fills the whole paper and crosses half.", "콜 부인한테 읽어 드리고 있었고, 그분이 가신 뒤로는 어머니께 다시 읽어 드리던 참이었어요, 어머니는 제인 편지를 어찌나 좋아하시는지 — 아무리 들어도 또 듣고 싶어 하시거든요. 그러니 멀리 갈 리 없다 했죠, 자 여기, 바로 반짇고리 밑에 있었네요 — 그리고 이렇게 친절하게도 뭐라고 썼는지 듣고 싶어 하시니까요 — 하지만 무엇보다 먼저, 제인을 위해 공정을 기하자면, 이렇게 짧은 편지를 쓴 것을 정말 사과드려야겠어요 — 보세요, 겨우 두 장이에요 — 두 장도 안 되네요 — 평소엔 종이를 가득 채우고 가로질러 또 절반을 채우는 아이거든요."], ["My mother often wonders that I can make it out so well.", "어머니는 내가 그걸 어쩜 그렇게 잘 읽어 내는지 자주 신기해하세요. 편지를 막 펼치면 으레 이러시지요."], ["She often says, when the letter is first opened, ‘Well, Hetty, now I think you will be put to it to make out all that checker-work’—don’t you, ma’am?—And then I tell her, I am sure she would contrive to make it out herself, if she had nobody to do it for her—every word of it—I am sure she would pore over it till she had made out every word.", "'얘, 헤티야, 이 격자무늬 글씨를 다 읽어 내려면 이번엔 좀 애먹겠구나.' — 그렇죠, 어머니?"], ["And, indeed, though my mother’s eyes are not so good as they were, she can see amazingly well still, thank God!", "— 그러면 제가 그러죠, 대신 읽어 줄 사람이 아무도 없으면 어머니 혼자서도 분명 어떻게든 읽어 내실 거라고요 — 한 자도 빠짐없이요 — 한 자 한 자 다 읽어 낼 때까지 들여다보고 또 들여다보실 게 분명해요. 그리고 정말이지, 어머니 눈이 예전만은 못해도 아직 신기할 만큼 잘 보세요, 다행스럽게도요!"], ["with the help of spectacles.", "안경의 도움을 받으면요."], ["It is such a blessing!", "이런 복이 어디 있어요!"], ["My mother’s are really very good indeed.", "우리 어머니 눈은 정말 아주 좋으세요. 제인이 여기 있을 때면 자주 이래요."], ["Jane often says, when she is here, ‘I am sure, grandmama, you must have had very strong eyes to see as you do—and so much fine work as you have done too!—I only wish my eyes may last me as well.’”", "'할머니, 그렇게 잘 보시는 걸 보면 분명 아주 좋은 눈을 타고나셨나 봐요 — 게다가 그 고운 바느질은 또 얼마나 많이 하셨고요! — 제 눈도 그렇게 오래 버텨 주기만 바랄 뿐이에요.'\""]], [["All this spoken extremely fast obliged Miss Bates to stop for breath; and Emma said something very civil about the excellence of Miss Fairfax’s handwriting.", "이 모든 말을 어찌나 빠르게 쏟아냈는지 미스 베이츠는 숨을 돌리려고 멈춰야 했다. 엠마는 미스 페어팩스의 빼어난 필체에 대해 무척 정중한 말을 한마디 건넸다."]], [["“You are extremely kind,”", "\"정말 친절하시네요.\""], ["replied Miss Bates, highly gratified; “you who are such a judge, and write so beautifully yourself.", "미스 베이츠가 몹시 흐뭇해하며 대답했다. \"그런 걸 보실 줄 아는 분이, 본인도 그렇게 아름답게 쓰시는 분이 말이에요."], ["I am sure there is nobody’s praise that could give us so much pleasure as Miss Woodhouse’s.", "정말이지 미스 우드하우스의 칭찬만큼 우리를 기쁘게 해 줄 칭찬은 어디에도 없을 거예요."], ["My mother does not hear; she is a little deaf you know.", "어머니는 못 들으세요, 아시다시피 귀가 좀 어두우셔서요."], ["Ma’am,”", "어머니,\""], ["addressing her, “do you hear what Miss Woodhouse is so obliging to say about Jane’s handwriting?”", "노부인을 향해 돌아서며, \"미스 우드하우스가 제인 글씨에 대해서 이렇게 고맙게 해 주시는 말씀, 들리세요?\""]], [["And Emma had the advantage of hearing her own silly compliment repeated twice over before the good old lady could comprehend it.", "그리하여 엠마는 그 착한 노부인이 알아듣기까지 자신의 어리석은 칭찬이 두 번이나 거듭되는 것을 들어야 하는 처지에 놓였다."], ["She was pondering, in the meanwhile, upon the possibility, without seeming very rude, of making her escape from Jane Fairfax’s letter, and had almost resolved on hurrying away directly under some slight excuse, when Miss Bates turned to her again and seized her attention.", "그러는 동안 엠마는, 너무 무례해 보이지 않으면서 제인 페어팩스의 편지에서 빠져나갈 방법이 없을까 궁리하다가, 적당한 핑계를 대고 당장 서둘러 자리를 뜨기로 거의 마음을 굳히던 참이었는데, 미스 베이츠가 다시 그녀를 향해 돌아서며 주의를 사로잡았다."]], [["“My mother’s deafness is very trifling you see—just nothing at all.", "\"우리 어머니 귀 어두운 건 정말 별것 아니에요, 보시다시피요 — 거의 아무것도 아닌걸요."], ["By only raising my voice, and saying any thing two or three times over, she is sure to hear; but then she is used to my voice.", "목소리만 좀 높이고 무슨 말이든 두세 번만 되풀이하면 분명 들으세요. 하긴 제 목소리에 익으셨으니까요."], ["But it is very remarkable that she should always hear Jane better than she does me.", "그런데 참 신기한 게, 늘 저보다 제인 말을 더 잘 들으신다는 거예요."], ["Jane speaks so distinct!", "제인은 발음이 어찌나 또렷한지요!"], ["However, she will not find her grandmama at all deafer than she was two years ago; which is saying a great deal at my mother’s time of life—and it really is full two years, you know, since she was here.", "어쨌든 제인은 할머니가 이 년 전보다 조금도 더 귀가 어두워지지 않았다는 걸 알게 될 거예요. 어머니 연세에 그건 정말 대단한 거지요 — 그러고 보니 제인이 여기 다녀간 지가 정말 꼬박 이 년이 됐네요."], ["We never were so long without seeing her before, and as I was telling Mrs. Cole, we shall hardly know how to make enough of her now.”", "전에는 이렇게 오래 못 본 적이 없었는데, 콜 부인한테도 말했지만, 이제 그 애를 어떻게 대접해야 충분할지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“Are you expecting Miss Fairfax here soon?”", "\"미스 페어팩스가 곧 여기로 오나요?\""]], [["“Oh yes; next week.”", "\"아 그럼요, 다음 주에요.\""]], [["“Indeed!—that must be a very great pleasure.”", "\"정말요! 그거 참 큰 기쁨이시겠어요.\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고마워요."], ["You are very kind.", "정말 친절하시네요."], ["Yes, next week.", "그래요, 다음 주예요."], ["Every body is so surprized; and every body says the same obliging things.", "다들 어찌나 놀라는지요, 그리고 다들 똑같이 고마운 말씀들을 해 주세요."], ["I am sure she will be as happy to see her friends at Highbury, as they can be to see her.", "분명 그 애도 하이베리의 친구들을 보고 그들이 그 애를 보는 만큼이나 기뻐할 거예요."], ["Yes, Friday or Saturday; she cannot say which, because Colonel Campbell will be wanting the carriage himself one of those days.", "그래요, 금요일이나 토요일이에요. 그 애도 어느 쪽인지는 못 정한대요, 그 며칠 중 하루는 캠벨 대령이 마차를 직접 쓰셔야 하거든요."], ["So very good of them to send her the whole way!", "그 먼 길을 줄곧 마차로 보내 주시다니 어찌나 고마운지요!"], ["But they always do, you know.", "하지만 늘 그렇게 해 주신답니다, 아시잖아요."], ["Oh yes, Friday or Saturday next.", "아 그럼요, 다음 주 금요일이나 토요일이에요."], ["That is what she writes about.", "그 애가 그 이야기를 쓴 거예요."], ["That is the reason of her writing out of rule, as we call it; for, in the common course, we should not have heard from her before next Tuesday or Wednesday.”", "그게 바로, 우리가 흔히 말하듯, 그 애가 정해진 때를 벗어나서 편지를 쓴 까닭이지요. 평소대로라면 다음 주 화요일이나 수요일이나 돼야 소식을 들었을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Yes, so I imagined.", "\"그래요, 저도 그렇게 짐작했어요."], ["I was afraid there could be little chance of my hearing any thing of Miss Fairfax to-day.”", "오늘 미스 페어팩스 소식을 들을 가망은 거의 없겠거니 했거든요.\""]], [["“So obliging of you!", "\"어쩜 그렇게 다정하신지!"], ["No, we should not have heard, if it had not been for this particular circumstance, of her being to come here so soon.", "맞아요, 이렇게 곧 온다는 특별한 사정만 아니었으면 우리도 못 들었을 거예요. 어머니가 어찌나 좋아하시는지요!"], ["My mother is so delighted!—for she is to be three months with us at least.", "— 적어도 석 달은 우리랑 함께 지낼 거니까요."], ["Three months, she says so, positively, as I am going to have the pleasure of reading to you.", "석 달이요, 그 애가 분명히 그렇게 썼어요, 제가 이제 그 기쁜 편지를 읽어 드릴 참인걸요."], ["The case is, you see, that the Campbells are going to Ireland. Mrs.", "사정인즉슨 이래요, 캠벨 대령 식구가 아일랜드로 가게 됐거든요."], ["Dixon has persuaded her father and mother to come over and see her directly.", "딕슨 부인이 부모님더러 당장 건너와서 보고 가시라고 졸랐대요."], ["They had not intended to go over till the summer, but she is so impatient to see them again—for till she married, last October, she was never away from them so much as a week, which must make it very strange to be in different kingdoms, I was going to say, but however different countries, and so she wrote a very urgent letter to her mother—or her father, I declare I do not know which it was, but we shall see presently in Jane’s letter—wrote in Mr.", "본래는 여름이나 돼야 건너갈 작정이셨는데, 그 애가 부모님을 다시 보고 싶어서 어찌나 안달인지요 — 작년 시월에 결혼하기 전까지는 단 일주일도 부모님과 떨어져 본 적이 없는 애라, 서로 다른 왕국에, 라고 할 뻔했네요, 하여튼 서로 다른 나라에 있다는 게 무척 낯설 거예요. 그래서 어머니께 — 아니 아버지께였나, 정말이지 어느 쪽이었는지 모르겠지만 곧 제인 편지에서 알게 되겠지요 — 아주 다급한 편지를 써 보냈대요, 미스터 딕슨 이름까지 함께 넣어서, 당장 건너오시라고요, 그러면 더블린에서 마중을 나가 자기들 시골 저택인 발리크레이그로 모셔 가겠다고요, 아주 아름다운 곳인가 봐요."], ["Dixon’s name as well as her own, to press their coming over directly, and they would give them the meeting in Dublin, and take them back to their country seat, Baly-craig, a beautiful place, I fancy.", "제인이 그 아름다움에 대해 많이 들었거든요. 미스터 딕슨한테서 말이에요 — 다른 누구한테서 들었다는 얘기는 못 들었어요."], ["Jane has heard a great deal of its beauty; from Mr. Dixon, I mean—I do not know that she ever heard about it from any body else; but it was very natural, you know, that he should like to speak of his own place while he was paying his addresses—and as Jane used to be very often walking out with them—for Colonel and Mrs.", "하지만 당연한 일이죠, 구애하는 동안 자기 고향 이야기를 하고 싶었을 테니까요 — 그리고 제인이 그들과 자주 함께 산책을 다니곤 했거든요 — 캠벨 대령 내외분이 따님이 미스터 딕슨하고 단둘이서만 자주 산책 나가는 걸 무척 조심하셨으니까요, 그건 제가 조금도 탓할 일이 아니지요."], ["Campbell were very particular about their daughter’s not walking out often with only Mr.", "그러니 미스터 딕슨이 미스 캠벨한테 아일랜드의 제 고향 이야기를 들려줄 때면 제인도 죄다 듣게 마련이었던 거예요."], ["Dixon, for which I do not at all blame them; of course she heard every thing he might be telling Miss Campbell about his own home in Ireland; and I think she wrote us word that he had shewn them some drawings of the place, views that he had taken himself.", "그러고 보니 제인이 우리한테 써 보냈던 것 같아요, 그분이 그곳 그림을 몇 점 보여 줬다고요, 손수 그린 경치들을요."], ["He is a most amiable, charming young man, I believe.", "정말 더없이 상냥하고 매력적인 청년인가 봐요."], ["Jane was quite longing to go to Ireland, from his account of things.”", "제인은 그분 이야기를 듣고 아일랜드에 어찌나 가고 싶어 했는지 몰라요.\""]], [["At this moment, an ingenious and animating suspicion entering Emma’s brain with regard to Jane Fairfax, this charming Mr.", "이 순간, 제인 페어팩스와 이 매력적인 미스터 딕슨과 아일랜드에 가지 않는 일을 두고 묘하게 사람을 들뜨게 하는 의심이 엠마의 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["Dixon, and the not going to Ireland, she said, with the insidious design of farther discovery,", "그래서 그녀는 좀 더 캐내려는 음흉한 속셈으로 말했다."]], [["“You must feel it very fortunate that Miss Fairfax should be allowed to come to you at such a time. Considering the very particular friendship between her and Mrs.", "\"미스 페어팩스가 하필 이런 때에 여기 오게 됐으니 정말 다행이라고 여기시겠어요."], ["Dixon, you could hardly have expected her to be excused from accompanying Colonel and Mrs. Campbell.”", "그분과 딕슨 부인 사이가 그토록 각별한 우정인데, 캠벨 대령 내외분을 따라가지 않아도 된다는 건 좀처럼 기대하기 어려웠을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Very true, very true, indeed.", "\"정말 맞아요, 정말 맞는 말씀이에요."], ["The very thing that we have always been rather afraid of; for we should not have liked to have her at such a distance from us, for months together—not able to come if any thing was to happen.", "바로 그게 우리가 늘 좀 겁내던 일이거든요. 몇 달씩이나 그렇게 먼 데 그 애를 두고 싶지 않았으니까요 — 무슨 일이 생겨도 올 수 없을 테니 말이에요."], ["But you see, every thing turns out for the best. They want her (Mr.", "그런데 보세요, 모든 게 다 잘 풀리는걸요."], ["and Mrs. Dixon) excessively to come over with Colonel and Mrs.", "그쪽에서도 (미스터 딕슨 내외분 말이에요) 캠벨 대령 내외분과 함께 건너오라고 그 애한테 어찌나 간곡히 청했는지요, 그건 정말 틀림없어요."], ["Campbell; quite depend upon it; nothing can be more kind or pressing than their joint invitation, Jane says, as you will hear presently; Mr.", "그 양쪽이 함께 보낸 초대만큼 친절하고 간곡한 건 없을 거라고 제인이 그러는데, 이제 곧 들으시게 될 거예요."], ["Dixon does not seem in the least backward in any attention.", "미스터 딕슨은 어떤 마음 씀씀이에도 조금도 인색한 분이 아닌가 봐요."], ["He is a most charming young man.", "정말 더없이 매력적인 청년이에요."], ["Ever since the service he rendered Jane at Weymouth, when they were out in that party on the water, and she, by the sudden whirling round of something or other among the sails, would have been dashed into the sea at once, and actually was all but gone, if he had not, with the greatest presence of mind, caught hold of her habit— (I can never think of it without trembling!)—But ever since we had the history of that day, I have been so fond of Mr. Dixon!”", "웨이머스에서 제인한테 베푼 그 일 이후로 줄곧요, 그 뱃놀이 모임에 나갔을 때 말이에요, 돛대 사이에서 뭔가가 갑자기 휙 돌아오는 바람에 제인이 하마터면 그대로 바다에 처박힐 뻔했거든요, 정말 거의 빠질 뻔했는데, 그분이 더없이 침착하게 그 애 옷자락을 붙잡지 않았더라면요 — (생각만 해도 늘 몸서리가 쳐져요!) — 그날 이야기를 들은 뒤로 저는 미스터 딕슨이 어찌나 좋은지 몰라요!\""]], [["“But, in spite of all her friends’ urgency, and her own wish of seeing Ireland, Miss Fairfax prefers devoting the time to you and Mrs. Bates?”", "\"하지만 친구들이 그토록 권하고 본인도 아일랜드를 보고 싶어 했는데도, 미스 페어팩스는 그 시간을 댁과 베이츠 부인께 바치는 쪽을 택한 거군요?\""]], [["“Yes—entirely her own doing, entirely her own choice; and Colonel and Mrs.", "\"네 — 온전히 그 애 뜻이고, 온전히 그 애 선택이에요."], ["Campbell think she does quite right, just what they should recommend; and indeed they particularly wish her to try her native air, as she has not been quite so well as usual lately.”", "캠벨 대령 내외분도 그 애가 아주 잘하는 거라고, 바로 당신들이 권하고 싶었던 거라고 여기세요. 게다가 두 분이 그 애더러 고향 공기를 꼭 좀 쐬어 보라고 특별히 바라셨거든요, 요즘 그 애가 평소만큼 건강하지가 못해서요.\""]], [["“I am concerned to hear of it.", "\"그 말씀을 들으니 걱정이 되네요."], ["I think they judge wisely. But Mrs.", "두 분이 현명하게 판단하신 것 같아요."], ["Dixon must be very much disappointed. Mrs.", "하지만 딕슨 부인은 무척 서운하시겠어요."], ["Dixon, I understand, has no remarkable degree of personal beauty; is not, by any means, to be compared with Miss Fairfax.”", "제가 알기로 딕슨 부인은 특별히 빼어난 미모를 가진 분은 아니지요. 도저히 미스 페어팩스에 견줄 바가 못 되고요.\""]], [["“Oh! no.", "\"아!"], ["You are very obliging to say such things—but certainly not.", "그럼요. 그런 말씀까지 해 주시다니 정말 다정도 하셔라 — 하지만 분명 아니지요."], ["There is no comparison between them.", "두 사람을 견준다는 건 말이 안 돼요."], ["Miss Campbell always was absolutely plain—but extremely elegant and amiable.”", "미스 캠벨은 늘 정말이지 평범했지요 — 그래도 더없이 우아하고 상냥했고요.\""]], [["“Yes, that of course.”", "\"네, 그야 물론이죠.\""]], [["“Jane caught a bad cold, poor thing!", "\"제인이 그만 지독한 감기에 걸렸지 뭐예요, 가엾어라!"], ["so long ago as the 7th of November, (as I am going to read to you,) and has never been well since.", "벌써 십일월 칠일에요 (이제 읽어 드릴게요), 그 뒤로 영 낫질 않았대요."], ["A long time, is not it, for a cold to hang upon her?", "감기가 그렇게 오래 붙어 있다니 너무하지 않아요?"], ["She never mentioned it before, because she would not alarm us.", "우리를 걱정시키지 않으려고 그동안 한 번도 말을 안 했던 거예요. 꼭 그 애답죠!"], ["Just like her!", "어쩜 그렇게 사려 깊은지요!"], ["so considerate!—But however, she is so far from well, that her kind friends the Campbells think she had better come home, and try an air that always agrees with her; and they have no doubt that three or four months at Highbury will entirely cure her—and it is certainly a great deal better that she should come here, than go to Ireland, if she is unwell.", "— 하지만 어쨌든 영 낫질 않으니까, 그 친절한 친구분들 캠벨 대령 내외분이 그 애더러 집에 돌아와 늘 잘 맞던 공기를 쐬는 게 좋겠다고 여기신 거예요. 하이베리에서 서너 달 지내면 말끔히 나으리라고 두 분도 믿어 의심치 않으세요 — 몸이 안 좋다면야 아일랜드로 가는 것보다 이리로 오는 편이 확실히 훨씬 나은 거고요."], ["Nobody could nurse her, as we should do.”", "우리만큼 그 애를 간호해 줄 사람은 아무 데도 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["“It appears to me the most desirable arrangement in the world.”", "\"제가 보기에도 이만큼 더 바랄 나위 없는 일이 없네요.\""]], [["“And so she is to come to us next Friday or Saturday, and the Campbells leave town in their way to Holyhead the Monday following—as you will find from Jane’s letter.", "\"그래서 그 애가 다음 주 금요일이나 토요일에 우리한테 오고, 캠벨 대령 내외분은 그다음 월요일에 홀리헤드로 가시는 길에 런던을 떠나신대요 — 제인 편지를 보시면 다 나와 있어요."], ["So sudden!—You may guess, dear Miss Woodhouse, what a flurry it has thrown me in!", "어찌나 갑작스러운지요! 미스 우드하우스, 제가 얼마나 부산스러워졌는지 짐작이 가시지요!"], ["If it was not for the drawback of her illness—but I am afraid we must expect to see her grown thin, and looking very poorly.", "그 애 병만 아니라면야 — 하지만 아무래도 그 애가 야위고 안색이 무척 안 좋아진 걸 보게 될 각오를 해야 할 것 같아요."], ["I must tell you what an unlucky thing happened to me, as to that.", "그것 때문에 제가 얼마나 운 나쁜 일을 겪었는지 말씀드려야겠어요."], ["I always make a point of reading Jane’s letters through to myself first, before I read them aloud to my mother, you know, for fear of there being any thing in them to distress her.", "제가 늘 어머니께 큰 소리로 읽어 드리기 전에 먼저 혼자서 제인 편지를 끝까지 읽어 보거든요, 아시다시피요, 혹시 어머니를 속상하게 할 만한 게 있을까 봐서요. 제인이 그렇게 해 달라고 부탁해서 늘 그렇게 한답니다."], ["Jane desired me to do it, so I always do: and so I began to-day with my usual caution; but no sooner did I come to the mention of her being unwell, than I burst out, quite frightened, with ‘Bless me!", "그래서 오늘도 평소대로 조심해서 읽기 시작했는데, 그 애가 몸이 안 좋다는 대목에 이르자마자 그만 깜짝 놀라서 '어머나! 가엾은 제인이 아프구나!'"], ["poor Jane is ill!’—which my mother, being on the watch, heard distinctly, and was sadly alarmed at.", "하고 불쑥 소리치고 만 거예요 — 그걸 마침 귀를 기울이고 계시던 어머니가 또렷이 들으시고는 그만 몹시 놀라셨지 뭐예요."], ["However, when I read on, I found it was not near so bad as I had fancied at first; and I make so light of it now to her, that she does not think much about it.", "그래도 계속 읽어 보니 처음 생각만큼 그렇게 나쁘지가 않더라고요. 그래서 지금은 어머니께 아주 가볍게 말씀드려서 별로 마음에 두지 않으세요."], ["But I cannot imagine how I could be so off my guard.", "그런데 제가 어쩜 그렇게 방심할 수 있었는지 모르겠어요."], ["If Jane does not get well soon, we will call in Mr. Perry.", "제인이 곧 낫지 않으면 미스터 페리를 부를 거예요."], ["The expense shall not be thought of; and though he is so liberal, and so fond of Jane that I dare say he would not mean to charge any thing for attendance, we could not suffer it to be so, you know.", "비용은 따질 게 아니지요. 그분이 워낙 후하시고 제인을 어찌나 아끼시는지 진찰비 같은 건 한 푼도 안 받으려 하시겠지만, 그래도 우리가 그렇게 둘 수야 없지요, 아시잖아요."], ["He has a wife and family to maintain, and is not to be giving away his time.", "그분도 부양할 처자식이 있으니, 시간을 그저 거저 내주실 수는 없는 노릇이에요."], ["Well, now I have just given you a hint of what Jane writes about, we will turn to her letter, and I am sure she tells her own story a great deal better than I can tell it for her.”", "자, 이제 제인이 무슨 이야기를 썼는지 대강 귀띔해 드렸으니, 그 애 편지로 돌아가 볼까요, 분명 그 애가 제가 대신 들려 드리는 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 제 이야기를 풀어 놨을 거예요.\""]], [["“I am afraid we must be running away,”", "\"아무래도 저희는 그만 가 봐야겠어요.\""], ["said Emma, glancing at Harriet, and beginning to rise—“My father will be expecting us.", "엠마가 해리엇을 흘긋 보고는 일어서려 하며 말했다. \"아버지가 저희를 기다리실 거예요."], ["I had no intention, I thought I had no power of staying more than five minutes, when I first entered the house.", "처음 이 집에 들어설 때만 해도 오 분 넘게 머물 생각은, 아니 그럴 여유가 없는 줄 알았거든요."], ["I merely called, because I would not pass the door without inquiring after Mrs. Bates; but I have been so pleasantly detained!", "그저 베이츠 부인 안부도 안 여쭙고 문 앞을 지날 수가 없어서 들른 것뿐인데, 이렇게 즐겁게 붙들리고 말았네요!"], ["Now, however, we must wish you and Mrs. Bates good morning.”", "하지만 이제 정말로 댁과 베이츠 부인께 작별 인사를 드려야겠어요.\""]], [["And not all that could be urged to detain her succeeded.", "그리고 그녀를 붙들려는 온갖 권유도 끝내 통하지 않았다."], ["She regained the street—happy in this, that though much had been forced on her against her will, though she had in fact heard the whole substance of Jane Fairfax’s letter, she had been able to escape the letter itself.", "엠마는 거리로 다시 나섰다 — 비록 많은 것을 본의 아니게 떠안았고, 실은 제인 페어팩스 편지의 내용을 통째로 다 들어 버린 셈이었지만, 그래도 편지 그 자체만은 면할 수 있었다는 것이 다행스러웠다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter II", "# 제2권 2장"]], [["Jane Fairfax was an orphan, the only child of Mrs. Bates’s youngest daughter.", "제인 페어팩스는 고아였으니, 베이츠 부인의 막내딸이 남긴 외동딸이었다."]], [["The marriage of Lieut. Fairfax of the ——regiment of infantry, and Miss Jane Bates, had had its day of fame and pleasure, hope and interest; but nothing now remained of it, save the melancholy remembrance of him dying in action abroad—of his widow sinking under consumption and grief soon afterwards—and this girl.", "보병 ——연대 페어팩스 중위와 미스 제인 베이츠의 혼인은 한때 자랑스럽고 기쁘고 희망과 관심으로 가득 찬 시절을 누렸으나, 이제 그 가운데 남은 것이라곤 아무것도 없었다 — 그가 머나먼 곳 전장에서 목숨을 잃었다는 서글픈 기억과, 얼마 지나지 않아 그 미망인이 폐병과 비탄에 시달리다 스러졌다는 기억과, 그리고 이 아이뿐이었다."]], [["By birth she belonged to Highbury: and when at three years old, on losing her mother, she became the property, the charge, the consolation, the foundling of her grandmother and aunt, there had seemed every probability of her being permanently fixed there; of her being taught only what very limited means could command, and growing up with no advantages of connexion or improvement, to be engrafted on what nature had given her in a pleasing person, good understanding, and warm-hearted, well-meaning relations.", "태생으로 따지면 그 아이는 하이베리 사람이었다. 세 살에 어머니를 여의고 외할머니와 이모의 소유물이자 짐이자 위안이자 거두어들인 고아가 되었을 때, 그 아이가 이곳에 영영 붙박여 자랄 공산이 매우 커 보였다. 지극히 빠듯한 형편이 허락하는 것만 겨우 배우고, 인맥이나 향상의 이점이라곤 하나 없이 자라나, 자연이 그 아이에게 베푼 것 — 보기 좋은 용모와 좋은 이해력, 그리고 따뜻한 마음씨에 선의를 지닌 친척들 — 위에 그저 접붙여지듯 자랄 듯싶었다."]], [["But the compassionate feelings of a friend of her father gave a change to her destiny.", "그러나 그 아이 아버지의 한 벗이 품은 측은한 마음이 그 운명에 변화를 가져왔다."], ["This was Colonel Campbell, who had very highly regarded Fairfax, as an excellent officer and most deserving young man; and farther, had been indebted to him for such attentions, during a severe camp-fever, as he believed had saved his life.", "그가 바로 캠벨 대령이었다. 그는 페어팩스를 빼어난 장교이자 더없이 훌륭한 청년으로 매우 높이 샀으며, 더 나아가 심한 진중 열병을 앓을 때 그에게서 받은 보살핌으로 목숨을 건졌다고 믿고 있었다."], ["These were claims which he did not learn to overlook, though some years passed away from the death of poor Fairfax, before his own return to England put any thing in his power.", "그것은 그가 모른 척 넘길 수 없는 은혜였으니, 가엾은 페어팩스가 세상을 떠난 뒤 그가 영국으로 돌아와 무언가를 할 수 있게 되기까지 몇 해가 흘렀어도 마찬가지였다. 마침내 돌아오자 그는 그 아이를 찾아내 보살피기 시작했다."], ["When he did return, he sought out the child and took notice of her.", "그는 기혼자였으되, 살아 있는 자식이라곤 제인과 비슷한 또래의 딸 하나뿐이었다."], ["He was a married man, with only one living child, a girl, about Jane’s age: and Jane became their guest, paying them long visits and growing a favourite with all; and before she was nine years old, his daughter’s great fondness for her, and his own wish of being a real friend, united to produce an offer from Colonel Campbell of undertaking the whole charge of her education.", "그리하여 제인은 그 집안의 손님이 되어 오래도록 머물며 모두의 귀염둥이로 자라났다. 그 아이가 아홉 살이 되기도 전에, 그 딸이 제인을 끔찍이 따르는 마음과 그 자신이 참된 벗이 되어 주고자 하는 바람이 한데 모여, 캠벨 대령이 그 아이의 교육 일체를 맡겠노라는 제안을 내놓기에 이르렀다."], ["It was accepted; and from that period Jane had belonged to Colonel Campbell’s family, and had lived with them entirely, only visiting her grandmother from time to time.", "그 제안은 받아들여졌고, 그때부터 제인은 캠벨 대령 집안 사람이 되어 오롯이 그들과 함께 살았으며, 외할머니는 이따금 찾아뵙는 데 그쳤다."]], [["The plan was that she should be brought up for educating others; the very few hundred pounds which she inherited from her father making independence impossible. To provide for her otherwise was out of Colonel Campbell’s power; for though his income, by pay and appointments, was handsome, his fortune was moderate and must be all his daughter’s; but, by giving her an education, he hoped to be supplying the means of respectable subsistence hereafter.", "계획인즉 그 아이를 남을 가르치는 사람으로 키운다는 것이었다. 아버지에게서 물려받은 몇백 파운드라는 보잘것없는 재산으로는 독립적인 생계가 도무지 불가능했기 때문이다. 그 아이를 달리 거두어 줄 형편은 캠벨 대령에게도 없었다. 봉급과 직책에서 나오는 수입은 넉넉했으나, 그의 재산은 그저 그만한 정도여서 응당 모두 딸의 몫이 되어야 했던 것이다. 다만 교육을 베풂으로써, 그는 그 아이가 훗날 떳떳한 생계를 꾸릴 방편을 마련해 주는 셈이 되리라 바랐다."]], [["Such was Jane Fairfax’s history.", "이것이 제인 페어팩스의 내력이었다."], ["She had fallen into good hands, known nothing but kindness from the Campbells, and been given an excellent education.", "그 아이는 좋은 손에 맡겨져 캠벨 집안에게서 친절밖에는 모른 채 자랐으며, 더없이 훌륭한 교육을 받았다."], ["Living constantly with right-minded and well-informed people, her heart and understanding had received every advantage of discipline and culture; and Colonel Campbell’s residence being in London, every lighter talent had been done full justice to, by the attendance of first-rate masters.", "늘 올바른 마음과 풍부한 식견을 갖춘 사람들과 함께 살았기에, 그 아이의 마음과 이해력은 도야와 교양의 온갖 이점을 받았다. 또 캠벨 대령의 거처가 런던에 있은 덕에, 일류 선생들의 가르침으로 가벼운 재주 하나하나까지도 마음껏 갈고닦을 수 있었다."], ["Her disposition and abilities were equally worthy of all that friendship could do; and at eighteen or nineteen she was, as far as such an early age can be qualified for the care of children, fully competent to the office of instruction herself; but she was too much beloved to be parted with.", "그 아이의 성품과 재능은 우정이 베풀 수 있는 모든 것을 받기에 부족함이 없었다. 그리하여 열여덟이나 열아홉 무렵에는, 그토록 이른 나이에 아이들을 맡을 자격을 갖출 수 있는 한도에서, 스스로 교육의 직무를 능히 감당할 만했다."], ["Neither father nor mother could promote, and the daughter could not endure it.", "그러나 그 아이는 너무도 사랑받았기에 떠나보낼 수가 없었다. 아버지도 어머니도 그 일을 거들 수 없었고, 딸도 그것을 견디지 못했다."], ["The evil day was put off.", "흉한 날은 미뤄졌다."], ["It was easy to decide that she was still too young; and Jane remained with them, sharing, as another daughter, in all the rational pleasures of an elegant society, and a judicious mixture of home and amusement, with only the drawback of the future, the sobering suggestions of her own good understanding to remind her that all this might soon be over.", "아직 너무 어리다고 정하기란 쉬운 일이었다. 그리하여 제인은 그들과 함께 머물며, 또 하나의 딸로서 우아한 사교계의 온갖 합리적인 즐거움과 가정과 오락의 분별 있는 어우러짐을 나누었으니, 다만 미래라는 한 가지 걸림돌만이, 이 모든 것이 머잖아 끝날지 모른다는 사실을 일깨우는 그 자신의 좋은 이해력이 던지는 진중한 암시만이 그늘로 남아 있었다."]], [["The affection of the whole family, the warm attachment of Miss Campbell in particular, was the more honourable to each party from the circumstance of Jane’s decided superiority both in beauty and acquirements.", "집안 전체의 애정, 그중에서도 특히 미스 캠벨의 따뜻한 정은, 제인이 미모에서나 소양에서나 뚜렷이 더 뛰어났다는 사정으로 인해 양쪽 모두에게 한층 더 명예로운 것이 되었다."], ["That nature had given it in feature could not be unseen by the young woman, nor could her higher powers of mind be unfelt by the parents.", "자연이 용모에 베푼 그 차이를 그 젊은 아가씨가 보지 못할 리 없었고, 제인의 더 뛰어난 지력 또한 그 부모가 느끼지 못할 리 없었다."], ["They continued together with unabated regard however, till the marriage of Miss Campbell, who by that chance, that luck which so often defies anticipation in matrimonial affairs, giving attraction to what is moderate rather than to what is superior, engaged the affections of Mr.", "그럼에도 그들은 한결같은 애정으로 함께 지냈으니, 미스 캠벨이 결혼하기까지 그러했다. 미스 캠벨은 — 혼사에서 흔히 예측을 비웃으며, 빼어난 쪽보다 그저 그만한 쪽에 매력을 부여하곤 하는 그런 우연, 그런 행운 덕분에 — 알게 된 지 거의 얼마 되지도 않아 미스터 딕슨이라는, 부유하고 호감 가는 청년의 마음을 사로잡았으며, 어울리고도 행복한 자리에 정착하게 되었다."], ["Dixon, a young man, rich and agreeable, almost as soon as they were acquainted; and was eligibly and happily settled, while Jane Fairfax had yet her bread to earn.", "반면 제인 페어팩스는 아직 제 빵벌이를 해야 할 처지였다."]], [["This event had very lately taken place; too lately for any thing to be yet attempted by her less fortunate friend towards entering on her path of duty; though she had now reached the age which her own judgment had fixed on for beginning.", "이 일은 아주 최근에 일어났으니, 그 덜 운 좋은 벗이 제 의무의 길로 들어서기 위한 어떤 시도라도 하기에는 너무도 최근이었다. 비록 이제는 그 자신의 판단이 시작점으로 정해 둔 나이에 다다랐는데도 말이다."], ["She had long resolved that one-and-twenty should be the period.", "그 아이는 오래전부터 스물한 살을 그 시점으로 삼겠다고 결심해 두었다."], ["With the fortitude of a devoted novitiate, she had resolved at one-and-twenty to complete the sacrifice, and retire from all the pleasures of life, of rational intercourse, equal society, peace and hope, to penance and mortification for ever.", "헌신적인 수련 수녀의 굳센 마음으로, 스물한 살이 되면 그 희생을 완수하고, 삶의 온갖 즐거움 — 합리적인 교제, 대등한 사교, 평안과 희망 — 에서 물러나 영영 고행과 극기의 길로 들어서겠노라 마음먹었던 것이다."]], [["The good sense of Colonel and Mrs. Campbell could not oppose such a resolution, though their feelings did.", "캠벨 대령 부부의 분별로는 그런 결심에 반대할 수가 없었으나, 그들의 정으로는 반대했다."], ["As long as they lived, no exertions would be necessary, their home might be hers for ever; and for their own comfort they would have retained her wholly; but this would be selfishness:—what must be at last, had better be soon.", "그들이 살아 있는 한 그 아이가 애쓸 필요는 없을 것이요, 그들의 집은 영영 그 아이의 집이 되어 줄 수 있었다. 또 그들 자신의 편안함만 따지자면 그 아이를 온전히 곁에 두고 싶었을 터이다."], ["Perhaps they began to feel it might have been kinder and wiser to have resisted the temptation of any delay, and spared her from a taste of such enjoyments of ease and leisure as must now be relinquished.", "그러나 그것은 이기심일 것이었다 — 결국 닥칠 일이라면 차라리 빨리 닥치는 편이 나았다. 어쩌면 그들은, 어떤 지체의 유혹에도 맞서, 이제는 단념해야 할 그 안락과 여유의 즐거움을 그 아이가 맛보지 않도록 막아 주는 편이 더 다정하고 현명했으리라 느끼기 시작했는지도 모른다."], ["Still, however, affection was glad to catch at any reasonable excuse for not hurrying on the wretched moment.", "그래도 애정은 그 비참한 순간을 서둘러 앞당기지 않을 만한 그럴듯한 구실이라면 무엇이든 기꺼이 붙잡으려 했다. 그 아이는 그들 딸이 결혼한 이래로 도통 몸이 온전치 못했다."], ["She had never been quite well since the time of their daughter’s marriage; and till she should have completely recovered her usual strength, they must forbid her engaging in duties, which, so far from being compatible with a weakened frame and varying spirits, seemed, under the most favourable circumstances, to require something more than human perfection of body and mind to be discharged with tolerable comfort.", "그러니 평소의 기력을 완전히 되찾기 전까지는, 그 아이가 의무를 떠맡는 것을 금해야만 했다. 그런 의무란 약해진 몸과 들쭉날쭉한 기분에 도무지 어울리지 않을뿐더러, 아무리 좋은 여건에서라도, 그럭저럭 견딜 만하게 해내려면 몸과 마음의 인간을 넘어선 완벽함 이상의 무언가가 필요해 보였던 것이다."]], [["With regard to her not accompanying them to Ireland, her account to her aunt contained nothing but truth, though there might be some truths not told.", "자신이 그들을 따라 아일랜드에 가지 않은 일에 대해 이모에게 들려준 설명에는 진실밖에 없었으되, 다만 말하지 않은 진실이 몇 가지 있었을 수는 있다. 그들이 떠나 있는 동안을 하이베리에서 보내겠다는 것은 그 아이 자신의 선택이었다."], ["It was her own choice to give the time of their absence to Highbury; to spend, perhaps, her last months of perfect liberty with those kind relations to whom she was so very dear: and the Campbells, whatever might be their motive or motives, whether single, or double, or treble, gave the arrangement their ready sanction, and said, that they depended more on a few months spent in her native air, for the recovery of her health, than on any thing else.", "어쩌면 그토록 자기를 아껴 주는 그 다정한 친척들과 함께, 완전한 자유의 마지막 몇 달을 보내겠다는 것이었으리라. 그리고 캠벨 부부는 — 그 동기가 무엇이었든, 하나였든 둘이었든 셋이었든 — 그 결정을 흔쾌히 승낙하며, 그 아이의 건강 회복을 위해서라면 다른 무엇보다도 고향 땅의 공기 속에서 보내는 몇 달에 더 기대를 건다고 말했다."], ["Certain it was that she was to come; and that Highbury, instead of welcoming that perfect novelty which had been so long promised it—Mr. Frank Churchill—must put up for the present with Jane Fairfax, who could bring only the freshness of a two years’ absence.", "어쨌든 분명한 것은 그 아이가 온다는 사실이었으며, 또 하이베리가 그토록 오래 약속받아 온 그 완전한 새 인물 — 미스터 프랭크 처칠 — 을 맞이하는 대신, 당분간은 겨우 이태 동안 떠나 있었던 새로움밖에는 가져다줄 게 없는 제인 페어팩스로 만족해야 한다는 사실이었다."]], [["Emma was sorry;—to have to pay civilities to a person she did not like through three long months!—to be always doing more than she wished, and less than she ought!", "엠마는 서운했다 — 마음에 들지도 않는 사람에게 긴긴 석 달 내내 예의를 차려야 하다니! 늘 바라는 것보다는 많이, 마땅히 해야 할 것보다는 적게 하게 될 처지라니!"], ["Why she did not like Jane Fairfax might be a difficult question to answer; Mr.", "어째서 그녀가 제인 페어팩스를 좋아하지 않는가는 대답하기 어려운 물음일지도 몰랐다. 언젠가 미스터 나이틀리는, 그녀가 제인에게서 정작 자기가 그렇게 여겨지길 바라는 바로 그 진정으로 소양 갖춘 젊은 아가씨의 모습을 보기 때문이라고 말한 적이 있었다."], ["Knightley had once told her it was because she saw in her the really accomplished young woman, which she wanted to be thought herself; and though the accusation had been eagerly refuted at the time, there were moments of self-examination in which her conscience could not quite acquit her.", "그 비난을 그때는 한사코 반박하긴 했어도, 스스로를 돌아볼 때면 양심이 그녀를 온전히 무죄로 놓아주지 못하는 순간들이 있었다. 그러나 \"그 사람과는 도무지 친해질 수가 없었어."], ["But “she could never get acquainted with her: she did not know how it was, but there was such coldness and reserve—such apparent indifference whether she pleased or not—and then, her aunt was such an eternal talker!—and she was made such a fuss with by every body!—and it had been always imagined that they were to be so intimate—because their ages were the same, every body had supposed they must be so fond of each other.”", "어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠지만, 어찌나 쌀쌀맞고 새침한지 — 제가 호감을 사든 말든 도통 관심 없다는 듯한 그 태도 — 게다가 그 이모는 끝도 없이 떠들어 대는 사람이고! — 또 다들 그 사람을 두고 어찌나 야단법석인지!"], ["These were her reasons—she had no better.", "— 게다가 두 사람이 으레 그렇게 친해지리라고들 처음부터 멋대로 짐작했단 말이야 — 나이가 같다는 이유로, 다들 둘이 서로 끔찍이 좋아하게 마련이라고 넘겨짚은 거지.\" 이것이 그녀의 이유였다 — 이보다 나은 것은 없었다."]], [["It was a dislike so little just—every imputed fault was so magnified by fancy, that she never saw Jane Fairfax the first time after any considerable absence, without feeling that she had injured her; and now, when the due visit was paid, on her arrival, after a two years’ interval, she was particularly struck with the very appearance and manners, which for those two whole years she had been depreciating.", "이는 너무도 부당한 반감이어서 — 떠넘긴 흠 하나하나가 공상으로 부풀려진 탓에, 어지간히 오래 못 만났다가 처음 제인 페어팩스를 볼 때면 그녀는 으레 자기가 그 사람에게 못할 짓을 했다는 느낌을 받지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 이제, 이태 만에 도착한 제인에게 응당 해야 할 방문을 마쳤을 때, 그녀는 지난 이태 내내 깎아내려 온 바로 그 외양과 태도에 유독 강하게 마음이 끌렸다."], ["Jane Fairfax was very elegant, remarkably elegant; and she had herself the highest value for elegance.", "제인 페어팩스는 무척 우아했다, 놀라우리만치 우아했다. 그리고 그녀 자신도 우아함을 더없이 높이 사는 사람이었다."], ["Her height was pretty, just such as almost every body would think tall, and nobody could think very tall; her figure particularly graceful; her size a most becoming medium, between fat and thin, though a slight appearance of ill-health seemed to point out the likeliest evil of the two.", "키는 보기 좋아서, 거의 누구나 훤칠하다 여기되 아무도 너무 크다고는 못할 만한 딱 그만한 키였다. 몸매는 유달리 우아했고, 풍채는 살찐 쪽과 마른 쪽 사이의 더없이 알맞은 중간이었으니, 다만 어딘가 살짝 병약해 보이는 기색이 그 둘 중에서 더 있을 법한 화근을 짚어 주는 듯했다."], ["Emma could not but feel all this; and then, her face—her features—there was more beauty in them altogether than she had remembered; it was not regular, but it was very pleasing beauty.", "엠마는 이 모든 것을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그 얼굴 — 그 이목구비 — 거기엔 그녀가 기억하던 것보다 한결 더 많은 아름다움이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Her eyes, a deep grey, with dark eye-lashes and eyebrows, had never been denied their praise; but the skin, which she had been used to cavil at, as wanting colour, had a clearness and delicacy which really needed no fuller bloom.", "반듯한 미모는 아니었으되 무척 호감 가는 아름다움이었다. 짙은 회색 눈동자에 검은 속눈썹과 눈썹은 일찍이 한 번도 칭찬을 받지 못한 적이 없었다."], ["It was a style of beauty, of which elegance was the reigning character, and as such, she must, in honour, by all her principles, admire it:—elegance, which, whether of person or of mind, she saw so little in Highbury.", "그리고 그녀가 혈색이 없다며 늘 트집 잡곤 했던 그 피부는, 더 짙은 화색이라곤 정말이지 필요 없을 만큼 맑고 고왔다. 그것은 우아함을 으뜸가는 특색으로 삼는 종류의 아름다움이었으니, 그런 이상 그녀는 명예를 걸고, 제 모든 신조에 비추어, 그것을 찬탄하지 않을 수 없었다 — 우아함이라니, 몸의 것이든 마음의 것이든, 하이베리에서는 좀처럼 보기 힘든 것이었다."], ["There, not to be vulgar, was distinction, and merit.", "그곳에서는 속되지 않다는 것 자체가 곧 품격이요 미덕이었다."]], [["In short, she sat, during the first visit, looking at Jane Fairfax with twofold complacency; the sense of pleasure and the sense of rendering justice, and was determining that she would dislike her no longer.", "요컨대 그 첫 방문 내내 그녀는 두 겹의 흡족함으로 — 즐거움의 감정과 공정을 베푸는 감정으로 — 제인 페어팩스를 바라보며 앉아 있었고, 다시는 그 사람을 미워하지 않으리라 마음먹고 있었다."], ["When she took in her history, indeed, her situation, as well as her beauty; when she considered what all this elegance was destined to, what she was going to sink from, how she was going to live, it seemed impossible to feel any thing but compassion and respect; especially, if to every well-known particular entitling her to interest, were added the highly probable circumstance of an attachment to Mr.", "실로 그녀의 내력을, 그 처지를, 그 미모와 더불어 헤아려 보았을 때 — 이 모든 우아함이 어떤 운명으로 정해져 있는지, 그녀가 어떤 자리에서 굴러떨어지려는 참인지, 앞으로 어떻게 살아가게 될 것인지를 생각하니, 측은함과 존경 말고는 어떤 감정도 품을 수 없을 것만 같았다. 더구나 그 사람의 관심을 살 만한 익히 알려진 사정 하나하나에다, 그녀가 그토록 자연스럽게 스스로 떠올린 바, 미스터 딕슨을 향한 연정이라는 매우 그럴듯한 정황까지 보태진다면 더욱 그러했다."], ["Dixon, which she had so naturally started to herself. In that case, nothing could be more pitiable or more honourable than the sacrifices she had resolved on.", "그렇다면야 그 아이가 결심한 희생만큼 가엾고도 명예로운 것은 다시없을 터였다."], ["Emma was very willing now to acquit her of having seduced Mr. Dixon’s affections from his wife, or of any thing mischievous which her imagination had suggested at first.", "이제 엠마는 그 아이가 미스터 딕슨의 마음을 그 아내에게서 빼앗았다든가, 처음에 자기 상상이 들이밀었던 어떤 못된 짓을 저질렀다든가 하는 혐의를 기꺼이 벗겨 줄 마음이 들었다."], ["If it were love, it might be simple, single, successless love on her side alone.", "만일 그것이 사랑이었다면, 한쪽만의, 단순하고 외곬이며 이루어지지 못한 사랑이었으리라."], ["She might have been unconsciously sucking in the sad poison, while a sharer of his conversation with her friend; and from the best, the purest of motives, might now be denying herself this visit to Ireland, and resolving to divide herself effectually from him and his connexions by soon beginning her career of laborious duty.", "어쩌면 그 아이는 제 벗과 그가 나누는 대화에 함께 끼어 있는 동안, 저도 모르게 그 슬픈 독을 빨아들이고 있었는지도 모른다. 그리고 가장 선하고 가장 순수한 동기에서, 이제 아일랜드행을 스스로 마다하고, 머잖아 고된 의무의 길에 나섬으로써 그와 그의 인연으로부터 자신을 효과적으로 떼어 놓기로 결심한 것인지도 모른다."]], [["Upon the whole, Emma left her with such softened, charitable feelings, as made her look around in walking home, and lament that Highbury afforded no young man worthy of giving her independence; nobody that she could wish to scheme about for her.", "대체로 엠마는 그토록 누그러지고 너그러워진 마음으로 그 자리를 떠났던 터라, 집으로 걸어오는 길에 둘레를 두루 돌아보며 한탄하기까지 했다 — 하이베리에는 그 아이에게 독립을 안겨 줄 만한 청년이 하나도 없다고, 그 아이를 위해 궁리를 짜내 보고 싶은 사람이 하나도 없다고."]], [["These were charming feelings—but not lasting.", "이것은 사랑스러운 감정이었다 — 그러나 오래가지는 못했다."], ["Before she had committed herself by any public profession of eternal friendship for Jane Fairfax, or done more towards a recantation of past prejudices and errors, than saying to Mr.", "제인 페어팩스를 향한 영원한 우정을 공공연히 선언하여 스스로를 옭아매기도 전에, 또 지난 편견과 오류를 철회하는 일이라곤 미스터 나이틀리에게 \"그 사람은 정말 어여뻐요. 어여쁜 것 그 이상이고요!\"라고 말한 것 말고는 더 한 일도 없는 사이에, 제인은 외할머니와 이모와 함께 하트필드에서 하룻저녁을 보냈고, 모든 것이 도로 예전 상태로 되돌아가고 말았다."], ["Knightley, “She certainly is handsome; she is better than handsome!”", "지난날의 거슬리던 점들이 다시금 고개를 들었다."], ["Jane had spent an evening at Hartfield with her grandmother and aunt, and every thing was relapsing much into its usual state. Former provocations reappeared.", "이모는 여전히 진력나는 사람이었다 — 아니, 한층 더 진력났으니, 이제는 조카의 재능에 대한 찬탄에다 조카의 건강에 대한 걱정까지 보태졌기 때문이다."], ["The aunt was as tiresome as ever; more tiresome, because anxiety for her health was now added to admiration of her powers; and they had to listen to the description of exactly how little bread and butter she ate for breakfast, and how small a slice of mutton for dinner, as well as to see exhibitions of new caps and new workbags for her mother and herself; and Jane’s offences rose again.", "그리하여 사람들은, 조카가 아침으로 버터 바른 빵을 얼마나 조금밖에 안 먹는지, 저녁으로 양고기를 얼마나 얇게 한 점만 드는지에 대한 설명을 들어 주어야 했고, 더불어 어머니와 자신을 위해 만든 새 모자며 새 반짇주머니를 구경해 주어야 했다. 그리고 제인의 죄목들이 다시 솟아났다."], ["They had music; Emma was obliged to play; and the thanks and praise which necessarily followed appeared to her an affectation of candour, an air of greatness, meaning only to shew off in higher style her own very superior performance.", "음악을 했다. 엠마는 어쩔 수 없이 연주를 했고, 으레 뒤따른 감사와 칭찬은 그녀에게는 꾸며 낸 진솔함이요 거드름 피우는 태도로만 비쳤으니, 다만 자기 자신의 한결 빼어난 연주를 한층 고상한 투로 뽐내려는 속셈으로만 보였다."], ["She was, besides, which was the worst of all, so cold, so cautious!", "게다가 무엇보다 고약했던 것은, 그 사람이 어찌나 쌀쌀맞고 어찌나 조심스러운지!"], ["There was no getting at her real opinion.", "도무지 그 사람의 진짜 속내에 다가갈 길이 없었다."], ["Wrapt up in a cloak of politeness, she seemed determined to hazard nothing.", "예의라는 외투를 칭칭 두른 채, 무엇 하나 함부로 내비치지 않기로 작정한 듯했다."], ["She was disgustingly, was suspiciously reserved.", "진저리가 나도록, 의심이 들도록 새침했다."]], [["If any thing could be more, where all was most, she was more reserved on the subject of Weymouth and the Dixons than any thing.", "온통 더없이 그런 가운데서도 더 그럴 수 있는 무언가가 있었다면, 그 사람은 웨이머스와 딕슨 부부에 관한 화제에서 무엇보다도 더 새침했다."], ["She seemed bent on giving no real insight into Mr. Dixon’s character, or her own value for his company, or opinion of the suitableness of the match.", "미스터 딕슨의 성품에 대해서도, 또 그와 함께 지내는 것을 제가 얼마나 소중히 여기는지에 대해서도, 그 혼사가 얼마나 어울리는지에 대한 견해에 대해서도, 진짜 속을 단 한 줌도 내비치지 않으려 작정한 듯했다."], ["It was all general approbation and smoothness; nothing delineated or distinguished.", "죄다 두루뭉술한 찬동이요 매끄러운 말뿐, 또렷이 그려 내거나 가려내는 것이라곤 없었다."], ["It did her no service however.", "그러나 그래 봐야 그 사람에게 득이 될 것은 없었다."], ["Her caution was thrown away.", "그 조심스러움은 헛수고였다."], ["Emma saw its artifice, and returned to her first surmises.", "엠마는 그 술수를 알아챘고, 처음의 짐작으로 되돌아갔다."], ["There probably was something more to conceal than her own preference; Mr.", "거기엔 십중팔구 제 마음 말고도 더 감출 것이 있는 듯했다."], ["Dixon, perhaps, had been very near changing one friend for the other, or been fixed only to Miss Campbell, for the sake of the future twelve thousand pounds.", "어쩌면 미스터 딕슨은 한 벗을 다른 벗과 맞바꾸기 직전까지 갔던 것이거나, 아니면 장차 들어올 일만 이천 파운드 때문에 미스 캠벨에게 매여 있었던 것인지도 몰랐다."]], [["The like reserve prevailed on other topics. She and Mr.", "다른 화제들에서도 마찬가지의 새침함이 판을 쳤다."], ["Frank Churchill had been at Weymouth at the same time.", "그 사람과 미스터 프랭크 처칠은 같은 시기에 웨이머스에 있었다. 둘이 조금 아는 사이라는 것은 알려져 있었다."], ["It was known that they were a little acquainted; but not a syllable of real information could Emma procure as to what he truly was.", "그러나 그가 진짜로 어떤 사람인지에 대해서 엠마는 알맹이 있는 정보라곤 한 음절도 캐낼 수가 없었다. \"그분이 미남이던가요?\""], ["“Was he handsome?”—“She believed he was reckoned a very fine young man.”", "— \"꽤 빼어난 청년으로들 여기는 것 같았어요.\" \"호감 가는 분이던가요?\""], ["“Was he agreeable?”—“He was generally thought so.”", "— \"대체로 그렇게들 생각했죠.\" \"분별 있는 청년으로 보이던가요?"], ["“Did he appear a sensible young man; a young man of information?”—“At a watering-place, or in a common London acquaintance, it was difficult to decide on such points.", "식견 있는 청년으로요?\" — \"온천지에서나 런던의 평범한 교제에서 그런 점을 판단하기란 어려운 일이에요."], ["Manners were all that could be safely judged of, under a much longer knowledge than they had yet had of Mr. Churchill.", "지금껏 미스터 처칠을 알아 온 것보다 훨씬 오래 알고 지내야만 그나마 안심하고 가늠할 수 있는 것이라곤 태도뿐이죠."], ["She believed every body found his manners pleasing.”", "다들 그분의 태도를 흐뭇하게 여기는 것 같았어요.\""], ["Emma could not forgive her.", "엠마는 그 사람을 도저히 용서할 수 없었다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter III", "# 제2권 3장"]], [["Emma could not forgive her;—but as neither provocation nor resentment were discerned by Mr.", "엠마는 그녀를 용서할 수 없었다."], ["Knightley, who had been of the party, and had seen only proper attention and pleasing behaviour on each side, he was expressing the next morning, being at Hartfield again on business with Mr.", "그러나 자극도 원망도 미스터 나이틀리의 눈에는 띄지 않았으니—그는 그 모임에 함께 있었으면서도 양쪽에서 오직 합당한 배려와 흐뭇한 거동만을 보았던 터라—이튿날 아침 미스터 우드하우스와의 볼일로 다시 하트필드에 들렀을 때, 그 모임 전체에 대한 만족을 토로하고 있었다."], ["Woodhouse, his approbation of the whole; not so openly as he might have done had her father been out of the room, but speaking plain enough to be very intelligible to Emma.", "엠마의 아버지가 자리를 비웠더라면 했을 만큼 거리낌 없이는 아니었으나, 엠마에게 충분히 알아들을 만큼은 분명하게 말했다."], ["He had been used to think her unjust to Jane, and had now great pleasure in marking an improvement.", "그는 늘 엠마가 제인에게 부당하다고 여겨 왔던 터라, 이제 그 개선의 기미를 짚어 내는 일이 무척 기뻤다."]], [["“A very pleasant evening,” he began, as soon as Mr.", "\"참으로 즐거운 저녁이었지요.\""], ["Woodhouse had been talked into what was necessary, told that he understood, and the papers swept away;—“particularly pleasant.", "미스터 우드하우스를 설득해 필요한 일을 마치고, 다 이해하셨다는 다짐을 받고, 서류를 치운 뒤에 그가 입을 열었다. \"각별히 즐거웠습니다."], ["You and Miss Fairfax gave us some very good music.", "당신과 미스 페어팩스가 우리에게 아주 훌륭한 음악을 들려주셨고요."], ["I do not know a more luxurious state, sir, than sitting at one’s ease to be entertained a whole evening by two such young women; sometimes with music and sometimes with conversation.", "어르신, 저는 그런 두 젊은 여인에게 저녁 내내 대접받으며 편히 앉아 있는 것보다 더 호사스러운 처지는 알지 못합니다. 어떤 때는 음악으로, 어떤 때는 대화로요."], ["I am sure Miss Fairfax must have found the evening pleasant, Emma.", "미스 페어팩스도 그 저녁을 즐거이 보냈을 게 분명합니다, 엠마."], ["You left nothing undone.", "당신은 무엇 하나 소홀히 하지 않았으니까요."], ["I was glad you made her play so much, for having no instrument at her grandmother’s, it must have been a real indulgence.”", "그녀에게 그렇게나 많이 연주를 시킨 게 저는 기뻤습니다. 할머니 댁에는 악기가 없으니, 그건 정말로 흔치 않은 호사였을 테지요.\""]], [["“I am happy you approved,”", "\"마음에 들어 하시니 기쁘네요.\""], ["said Emma, smiling; “but I hope I am not often deficient in what is due to guests at Hartfield.”", "엠마가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"하지만 저는 하트필드의 손님에게 마땅히 베풀 도리를 자주 빠뜨리는 사람은 아니라고 생각하는데요.\""]], [["“No, my dear,”", "\"그렇고말고, 얘야.\""], ["said her father instantly; “that I am sure you are not.", "아버지가 곧바로 말했다. \"그것만은 네가 절대 그렇지 않다는 걸 내가 잘 안다."], ["There is nobody half so attentive and civil as you are.", "너만큼 살뜰하고 예의 바른 사람은 어디에도 없지."], ["If any thing, you are too attentive.", "굳이 따지자면 너는 지나치게 살뜰하단다."], ["The muffin last night—if it had been handed round once, I think it would have been enough.”", "어젯밤 그 머핀 말이다—한 번만 돌렸어도 충분했을 것 같구나.\""]], [["“No,” said Mr.", "\"아니요.\""], ["Knightley, nearly at the same time; “you are not often deficient; not often deficient either in manner or comprehension.", "거의 동시에 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"당신은 자주 빠뜨리지 않습니다."], ["I think you understand me, therefore.”", "거동에서도, 이해에서도 자주 빠뜨리지 않지요. 그러니 제 말뜻을 알아들으시리라 봅니다.\""]], [["An arch look expressed—“I understand you well enough;” but she said only, “Miss Fairfax is reserved.”", "짓궂은 눈빛이 \"당신 말뜻쯤은 잘 알아들어요\"라고 말하고 있었으나, 그녀는 그저 \"미스 페어팩스는 속을 잘 안 보이는 사람이에요\"라고만 했다."]], [["“I always told you she was—a little; but you will soon overcome all that part of her reserve which ought to be overcome, all that has its foundation in diffidence.", "\"늘 그렇다고 말씀드렸지요—조금은요. 하지만 그 속내 감춤 가운데 마땅히 허물어야 할 부분은, 다시 말해 자신 없음에 뿌리를 둔 부분은 곧 다 허물게 될 겁니다."], ["What arises from discretion must be honoured.”", "신중함에서 비롯된 것이라면 응당 존중해야 하고요.\""]], [["“You think her diffident.", "\"자신 없는 사람이라고 보시는군요."], ["I do not see it.”", "저한테는 그렇게 안 보여요.\""]], [["“My dear Emma,”", "\"엠마.\""], ["said he, moving from his chair into one close by her, “you are not going to tell me, I hope, that you had not a pleasant evening.”", "그가 의자에서 일어나 그녀 가까이 놓인 의자로 옮겨 앉으며 말했다. \"설마 그 저녁이 즐겁지 않았다고 말하려는 건 아니겠지요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아유!"], ["no; I was pleased with my own perseverance in asking questions; and amused to think how little information I obtained.”", "아니에요. 저는 끈질기게 질문을 던진 제 인내심이 마음에 들었고, 그래서 얻어 낸 정보가 얼마나 빈약한지를 생각하니 우습기까지 했는걸요.\""]], [["“I am disappointed,”", "\"실망스럽군요.\""], ["was his only answer.", "그가 한 대답은 그것뿐이었다."]], [["“I hope every body had a pleasant evening,” said Mr.", "\"다들 즐거운 저녁을 보냈으면 좋겠구나.\""], ["Woodhouse, in his quiet way. “I had.", "미스터 우드하우스가 그 특유의 조용한 투로 말했다."], ["Once, I felt the fire rather too much; but then I moved back my chair a little, a very little, and it did not disturb me.", "\"나는 즐거웠다. 한 번은 난롯불이 좀 과하게 느껴졌지만, 그때 의자를 조금, 아주 조금 뒤로 물렸더니 거슬리지 않더구나."], ["Miss Bates was very chatty and good-humoured, as she always is, though she speaks rather too quick.", "미스 베이츠는 늘 그렇듯 말도 많고 기분도 좋아 보였어. 말이 좀 빠르긴 하다만."], ["However, she is very agreeable, and Mrs.", "그래도 아주 상냥한 사람이지. 베이츠 부인도 다른 식으로 그렇고."], ["Bates too, in a different way.", "나는 오랜 벗들이 좋아."], ["I like old friends; and Miss Jane Fairfax is a very pretty sort of young lady, a very pretty and a very well-behaved young lady indeed.", "그리고 미스 제인 페어팩스는 아주 어여쁜 아가씨야. 정말로 어여쁘고 아주 행실 바른 아가씨지."], ["She must have found the evening agreeable, Mr.", "그 애도 그 저녁이 흐뭇했을 게다, 미스터 나이틀리."], ["Knightley, because she had Emma.”", "엠마가 곁에 있었으니 말이오.\""]], [["“True, sir; and Emma, because she had Miss Fairfax.”", "\"그렇지요, 어르신. 그리고 엠마도, 미스 페어팩스가 곁에 있었으니까요.\""]], [["Emma saw his anxiety, and wishing to appease it, at least for the present, said, and with a sincerity which no one could question—", "엠마는 그의 근심을 알아챘고, 적어도 지금만이라도 그것을 달래 주고 싶어, 누구도 의심할 수 없을 진심을 담아 말했다—"]], [["“She is a sort of elegant creature that one cannot keep one’s eyes from.", "\"그분은 도무지 눈을 뗄 수 없는, 그런 우아한 사람이에요."], ["I am always watching her to admire; and I do pity her from my heart.”", "저는 늘 그분을 감탄하며 바라보게 되거든요. 그리고 마음속 깊이 그분을 가엾게 여기고요.\""]], [["Mr. Knightley looked as if he were more gratified than he cared to express; and before he could make any reply, Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리는 입 밖에 내고 싶은 것 이상으로 흡족해하는 얼굴이었다."], ["Woodhouse, whose thoughts were on the Bates’s, said—", "그가 미처 대꾸하기도 전에, 생각이 온통 베이츠 모녀에게 가 있던 미스터 우드하우스가 말했다—"]], [["“It is a great pity that their circumstances should be so confined!", "\"그 댁 형편이 그렇게 옹색하다니 참으로 딱한 일이야!"], ["a great pity indeed!", "정말 딱한 일이지!"], ["and I have often wished—but it is so little one can venture to do—small, trifling presents, of any thing uncommon—Now we have killed a porker, and Emma thinks of sending them a loin or a leg; it is very small and delicate—Hartfield pork is not like any other pork—but still it is pork—and, my dear Emma, unless one could be sure of their making it into steaks, nicely fried, as ours are fried, without the smallest grease, and not roast it, for no stomach can bear roast pork—I think we had better send the leg—do not you think so, my dear?”", "나도 늘 바랐지만—사람이 감히 해 볼 수 있는 일이라곤 어찌 그리 적은지—뭔가 흔치 않은 것을 자그맣게, 소소하게 선물하는 정도지—이번에 돼지를 한 마리 잡았으니, 엠마가 등심이나 다릿살을 보내 줄까 생각하는데. 아주 자그맣고 연한 부위야—하트필드 돼지고기는 다른 돼지고기와는 다르지—그래도 돼지고기는 돼지고기니—얘야, 엠마야, 그 댁에서 그걸 우리처럼 기름기 한 점 없이 곱게 부친 스테이크로 만들어 먹는다는 보장이 없는 한, 그리고 구워 먹지 않는다는 보장이 없는 한—구운 돼지고기는 어떤 위장도 견디지 못하니까—내 생각엔 다릿살을 보내는 편이 낫겠다—그렇지 않니, 얘야?\""]], [["“My dear papa, I sent the whole hind-quarter.", "\"아버지, 저는 뒷다리 한 짝을 통째로 보냈어요."], ["I knew you would wish it.", "아버지가 그러길 바라실 줄 알았거든요."], ["There will be the leg to be salted, you know, which is so very nice, and the loin to be dressed directly in any manner they like.”", "아시다시피 다릿살은 소금에 절이면 되는데, 그게 아주 맛이 좋잖아요. 그리고 등심은 그 댁에서 원하는 대로 곧장 요리해 먹으면 되고요.\""]], [["“That’s right, my dear, very right.", "\"잘했다, 얘야, 아주 잘했어."], ["I had not thought of it before, but that is the best way.", "미처 거기까진 생각 못 했다만, 그게 제일 좋은 방법이지."], ["They must not over-salt the leg; and then, if it is not over-salted, and if it is very thoroughly boiled, just as Serle boils ours, and eaten very moderately of, with a boiled turnip, and a little carrot or parsnip, I do not consider it unwholesome.”", "다릿살을 너무 짜게 절이면 안 될 텐데. 그러니까 너무 짜게 절이지 않고, 우리 집 설이 삶듯 아주 푹 삶아서, 삶은 순무하고 당근이나 파스닙을 곁들여 아주 적당히만 먹는다면, 나는 그게 몸에 나쁘지 않다고 본다.\""]], [["“Emma,” said Mr.", "\"엠마.\""], ["Knightley presently, “I have a piece of news for you.", "잠시 뒤 미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"당신에게 알려 줄 소식이 하나 있습니다."], ["You like news—and I heard an article in my way hither that I think will interest you.”", "소식 좋아하시잖아요—여기 오는 길에 들은 이야기인데, 당신이 흥미로워할 것 같군요.\""]], [["“News! Oh!", "\"소식이요!"], ["yes, I always like news.", "아유! 그럼요, 저는 늘 소식을 좋아해요."], ["What is it?—why do you smile so?—where did you hear it?—at Randalls?”", "뭔데요?—왜 그렇게 웃으세요?—어디서 들으셨어요?—랜덜스에서요?\""]], [["He had time only to say,", "그는 겨우 이 말만 할 수 있었다."]], [["“No, not at Randalls; I have not been near Randalls,”", "\"아니요, 랜덜스가 아닙니다. 랜덜스 근처엔 가지도 않았어요.\""], ["when the door was thrown open, and Miss Bates and Miss Fairfax walked into the room.", "그 순간 문이 활짝 열리며 미스 베이츠와 미스 페어팩스가 방으로 들어섰다."], ["Full of thanks, and full of news, Miss Bates knew not which to give quickest. Mr.", "감사의 말로 가득 차고 소식으로도 가득 찬 미스 베이츠는 어느 쪽부터 쏟아 내야 할지 갈피를 못 잡았다."], ["Knightley soon saw that he had lost his moment, and that not another syllable of communication could rest with him.", "미스터 나이틀리는 자신의 기회가 날아갔으며, 이제 한마디도 자기 차지로 남지 않으리라는 것을 곧 알아차렸다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아유!"], ["my dear sir, how are you this morning?", "어르신, 오늘 아침 안녕하셨어요?"], ["My dear Miss Woodhouse—I come quite over-powered.", "미스 우드하우스—저는 그만 압도되어 왔답니다."], ["Such a beautiful hind-quarter of pork!", "그렇게 훌륭한 돼지 뒷다리라니요!"], ["You are too bountiful!", "너무 후하세요!"], ["Have you heard the news?", "소식 들으셨어요?"], ["Mr. Elton is going to be married.”", "미스터 엘턴이 결혼한대요.\""]], [["Emma had not had time even to think of Mr. Elton, and she was so completely surprized that she could not avoid a little start, and a little blush, at the sound.", "엠마는 미스터 엘턴을 떠올릴 겨를조차 없었던 터라, 그 말을 듣자 완전히 놀란 나머지 살짝 움찔하고 살짝 얼굴을 붉히는 것을 피할 수 없었다."]], [["“There is my news:—I thought it would interest you,”", "\"그게 제 소식이었습니다. 당신이 흥미로워할 줄 알았지요.\""], ["said Mr. Knightley, with a smile which implied a conviction of some part of what had passed between them.", "미스터 나이틀리가, 두 사람 사이에 오갔던 일의 일부를 확신한다는 듯한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“But where could you hear it?”", "\"하지만 그쪽은 대체 어디서 들으셨어요?\""], ["cried Miss Bates.", "미스 베이츠가 외쳤다."], ["“Where could you possibly hear it, Mr. Knightley?", "\"대체 어디서 그걸 들으셨대요, 미스터 나이틀리?"], ["For it is not five minutes since I received Mrs. Cole’s note—no, it cannot be more than five—or at least ten—for I had got my bonnet and spencer on, just ready to come out—I was only gone down to speak to Patty again about the pork—Jane was standing in the passage—were not you, Jane?—for my mother was so afraid that we had not any salting-pan large enough.", "제가 콜 부인의 쪽지를 받은 지 오 분도 안 됐는걸요—아니, 오 분도 더는 안 됐을 거예요—적어도 십 분쯤—제가 보닛에 스펜서까지 다 차려입고 막 나오려던 참이었거든요—돼지고기 일로 패티한테 한마디 더 하려고 잠깐 아래층에 내려갔던 것뿐이에요—제인은 복도에 서 있었고요—그렇지, 제인?—어머니가 우리 집에 그만큼 큰 절임통이 없을까 봐 어찌나 걱정하시던지요."], ["So I said I would go down and see, and Jane said, ‘Shall I go down instead?", "그래서 제가 내려가서 보고 오겠다고 했더니, 제인이 '제가 대신 내려갈까요?"], ["for I think you have a little cold, and Patty has been washing the kitchen.’—‘Oh!", "이모는 감기 기운이 좀 있는 것 같고, 패티는 부엌을 닦고 있었잖아요' 하지 뭐예요.—'아유!"], ["my dear,’ said I—well, and just then came the note.", "얘는' 하고 제가 말하는데—바로 그때 그 쪽지가 온 거예요."], ["A Miss Hawkins—that’s all I know.", "어느 미스 호킨스라네요—제가 아는 건 그게 다예요."], ["A Miss Hawkins of Bath. But, Mr.", "바스의 미스 호킨스라고요."], ["Knightley, how could you possibly have heard it? for the very moment Mr.", "그런데 미스터 나이틀리, 그쪽은 대체 어떻게 그걸 들으셨대요?"], ["Cole told Mrs. Cole of it, she sat down and wrote to me.", "미스터 콜이 콜 부인한테 그 이야기를 한 바로 그 순간에, 부인이 자리에 앉아 저한테 편지를 쓴 건데요."], ["A Miss Hawkins—”", "어느 미스 호킨스라니—\""]], [["“I was with Mr. Cole on business an hour and a half ago.", "\"한 시간 반쯤 전에 볼일로 미스터 콜과 함께 있었습니다."], ["He had just read Elton’s letter as I was shewn in, and handed it to me directly.”", "제가 안내받아 들어갈 때 그가 막 엘턴의 편지를 읽던 참이라, 곧장 제게 건네주더군요.\""]], [["“Well!", "\"세상에!"], ["that is quite—I suppose there never was a piece of news more generally interesting.", "그거 참—세상 누구에게나 이만큼 두루 흥미로운 소식은 없었을 거예요."], ["My dear sir, you really are too bountiful.", "어르신, 정말이지 너무 후하세요."], ["My mother desires her very best compliments and regards, and a thousand thanks, and says you really quite oppress her.”", "어머니가 더없이 정중한 인사와 안부를, 그리고 천 번의 감사를 전하시면서, 정말이지 송구해 어쩔 줄 모르겠다고 하셨답니다.\""]], [["“We consider our Hartfield pork,” replied Mr.", "\"우리는 우리 하트필드 돼지고기가—\""], ["Woodhouse—“indeed it certainly is, so very superior to all other pork, that Emma and I cannot have a greater pleasure than—”", "미스터 우드하우스가 대꾸했다. \"정말이지 다른 어떤 돼지고기보다도 월등히 낫다고 여기는 터라, 엠마와 나로서는 이보다 더 큰 기쁨이 없는—\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아유!"], ["my dear sir, as my mother says, our friends are only too good to us.", "어르신, 어머니 말씀처럼, 저희 벗들은 저희한테 너무도 잘해 주신답니다."], ["If ever there were people who, without having great wealth themselves, had every thing they could wish for, I am sure it is us.", "큰 재산도 없으면서 바랄 수 있는 모든 걸 다 누리는 사람들이 세상에 있다면, 그게 바로 저희일 거예요. 저희야말로 '우리 몫은 아름다운 기업에 떨어졌도다'* 하고 말할 만하지요."], ["We may well say that ‘our lot is cast in a goodly heritage.’ Well, Mr.", "자아, 미스터 나이틀리, 그러니까 정말로 그 편지를 직접 보셨다는 거네요. 그래서—\""], ["Knightley, and so you actually saw the letter; well—”", "* '우리 몫은 아름다운 기업에 떨어졌도다': 구약 시편 16편 6절의 한 구절."]], [["“It was short—merely to announce—but cheerful, exulting, of course.”— Here was a sly glance at Emma.", "\"짧았습니다—그저 알리려는 것뿐이었지요—하지만 물론 들떠 있고, 의기양양했습니다.\"—여기서 엠마를 향한 짓궂은 눈길이 스쳤다."], ["“He had been so fortunate as to—I forget the precise words—one has no business to remember them.", "\"그는 다행히도—정확한 표현은 잊었군요—그런 걸 기억할 까닭이 없으니까요."], ["The information was, as you state, that he was going to be married to a Miss Hawkins.", "당신 말대로, 어느 미스 호킨스와 결혼하게 됐다는 내용이었습니다."], ["By his style, I should imagine it just settled.”", "글투로 보아 막 정해진 일인 듯싶더군요.\""]], [["“Mr. Elton going to be married!”", "\"미스터 엘턴이 결혼한다니!\""], ["said Emma, as soon as she could speak.", "엠마가 입을 뗄 수 있게 되자마자 말했다."], ["“He will have every body’s wishes for his happiness.”", "\"온 세상이 그분의 행복을 빌어 줄 거예요.\""]], [["“He is very young to settle,”", "\"자리 잡기엔 아주 젊은데.\""], ["was Mr. Woodhouse’s observation.", "미스터 우드하우스의 소감이었다."], ["“He had better not be in a hurry.", "\"서두르지 않는 편이 좋을 텐데."], ["He seemed to me very well off as he was.", "내가 보기엔 지금 그대로도 아주 잘 지내는 것 같던데."], ["We were always glad to see him at Hartfield.”", "우리는 그가 하트필드에 오면 늘 반가웠지.\""]], [["“A new neighbour for us all, Miss Woodhouse!”", "\"우리 모두에게 새 이웃이 생기는 거예요, 미스 우드하우스!\""], ["said Miss Bates, joyfully; “my mother is so pleased!—she says she cannot bear to have the poor old Vicarage without a mistress.", "미스 베이츠가 기쁘게 말했다. \"어머니가 어찌나 좋아하시는지!—가엾은 그 낡은 목사관에 안주인이 없는 걸 참을 수가 없다고 하시거든요."], ["This is great news, indeed.", "정말이지 대단한 소식이에요."], ["Jane, you have never seen Mr. Elton!—no wonder that you have such a curiosity to see him.”", "제인, 너는 미스터 엘턴을 한 번도 못 봤지!—그러니 그렇게나 보고 싶어 하는 것도 무리가 아니구나.\""]], [["Jane’s curiosity did not appear of that absorbing nature as wholly to occupy her.", "제인의 호기심은 그녀를 온통 사로잡을 만큼 그렇게 골똘한 것으로는 보이지 않았다."]], [["“No—I have never seen Mr. Elton,”", "\"네—저는 미스터 엘턴을 한 번도 못 봤어요.\""], ["she replied, starting on this appeal; “is he—is he a tall man?”", "그 부름에 화들짝 정신을 차리며 그녀가 답했다. \"그분은—그분은 키가 큰 분인가요?\""]], [["“Who shall answer that question?”", "\"그 물음엔 누가 답해야 할까요?\""], ["cried Emma.", "엠마가 외쳤다."], ["“My father would say ‘yes,’ Mr. Knightley ‘no;’ and Miss Bates and I that he is just the happy medium.", "\"제 아버지라면 '그렇다'고, 미스터 나이틀리라면 '아니다'라고 하실 테고, 미스 베이츠와 저는 딱 알맞은 중간이라고 할 거예요."], ["When you have been here a little longer, Miss Fairfax, you will understand that Mr.", "미스 페어팩스, 여기 좀 더 오래 머물다 보면 알게 될 거예요."], ["Elton is the standard of perfection in Highbury, both in person and mind.”", "미스터 엘턴은 하이베리에서 외모로나 마음으로나 완벽함의 기준이거든요.\""]], [["“Very true, Miss Woodhouse, so she will.", "\"정말 맞는 말씀이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 곧 알게 될 거예요."], ["He is the very best young man—But, my dear Jane, if you remember, I told you yesterday he was precisely the height of Mr. Perry.", "그분은 더없이 훌륭한 청년이지요—그런데 제인, 기억나니, 어제 내가 그분이 딱 미스터 페리만 한 키라고 했잖니."], ["Miss Hawkins,—I dare say, an excellent young woman.", "미스 호킨스라니—분명 빼어난 아가씨일 거예요."], ["His extreme attention to my mother—wanting her to sit in the vicarage pew, that she might hear the better, for my mother is a little deaf, you know—it is not much, but she does not hear quite quick.", "우리 어머니한테 어찌나 극진히 마음을 쓰시던지—더 잘 들으시라고 어머니더러 목사관 신도석에 앉으시라고까지 하셨지요. 아시다시피 어머니가 귀가 좀 어두우시거든요—심한 건 아니고, 그저 얼른 알아듣지를 못하시는 정도예요."], ["Jane says that Colonel Campbell is a little deaf.", "제인 말로는 캠벨 대령도 귀가 좀 어두우시다더라고요."], ["He fancied bathing might be good for it—the warm bath—but she says it did him no lasting benefit.", "목욕이 좋을 듯싶다고—따뜻한 물 목욕 말이에요—여기셨다는데, 제인 말로는 오래가는 효험은 없었다고 해요."], ["Colonel Campbell, you know, is quite our angel. And Mr.", "아시다시피 캠벨 대령은 정말이지 저희한테 천사 같은 분이에요."], ["Dixon seems a very charming young man, quite worthy of him.", "그리고 미스터 딕슨도 아주 매력적인 청년인 것 같아요. 대령께 손색이 없는 분이지요."], ["It is such a happiness when good people get together—and they always do.", "좋은 사람들끼리 어울리는 건 참으로 큰 행복이에요—게다가 그런 사람들은 늘 그렇게 어울리고요. 자, 이제 미스터 엘턴과 미스 호킨스가 그렇게 될 테고요."], ["Now, here will be Mr.", "콜 부부도 계시고, 정말 좋은 분들이지요."], ["Elton and Miss Hawkins; and there are the Coles, such very good people; and the Perrys—I suppose there never was a happier or a better couple than Mr. and Mrs.", "그리고 페리네도요—미스터 페리와 페리 부인보다 더 행복하고 더 훌륭한 부부는 세상에 없을 거예요."], ["Perry. I say, sir,”", "어르신,\""], ["turning to Mr. Woodhouse, “I think there are few places with such society as Highbury.", "미스터 우드하우스에게로 돌아서며, \"하이베리만 한 사교를 누리는 곳은 흔치 않다고 생각해요."], ["I always say, we are quite blessed in our neighbours.—My dear sir, if there is one thing my mother loves better than another, it is pork—a roast loin of pork—”", "저는 늘 말하지요, 우리는 이웃 복을 타고났다고요.—어르신, 우리 어머니가 무엇보다 좋아하시는 게 하나 있다면, 그건 돼지고기랍니다—구운 돼지 등심이요—\""]], [["“As to who, or what Miss Hawkins is, or how long he has been acquainted with her,”", "\"미스 호킨스가 누구인지, 어떤 사람인지, 그분이 그녀를 안 지 얼마나 됐는지에 관해서는,\""], ["said Emma, “nothing I suppose can be known.", "엠마가 말했다. \"아마 알려진 게 아무것도 없겠지요."], ["One feels that it cannot be a very long acquaintance.", "그리 오래된 사이일 리는 없다는 느낌이 들어요."], ["He has been gone only four weeks.”", "그분이 떠난 지 겨우 사 주밖에 안 됐으니까요.\""]], [["Nobody had any information to give; and, after a few more wonderings, Emma said,", "아무도 내놓을 정보가 없었다. 몇 마디 더 신기해하고 나서 엠마가 말했다."]], [["“You are silent, Miss Fairfax—but I hope you mean to take an interest in this news.", "\"말씀이 없으시네요, 미스 페어팩스—하지만 이 소식에 관심을 가져 주셨으면 해요."], ["You, who have been hearing and seeing so much of late on these subjects, who must have been so deep in the business on Miss Campbell’s account—we shall not excuse your being indifferent about Mr. Elton and Miss Hawkins.”", "요즈음 이런 주제로 그렇게 많이 듣고 보아 오신 분이, 미스 캠벨 일로 그 일에 그토록 깊이 관여하셨을 분이—미스터 엘턴과 미스 호킨스 일에 무심하다면 봐드릴 수가 없는걸요.\""]], [["“When I have seen Mr. Elton,”", "\"미스터 엘턴을 보고 나면,\""], ["replied Jane, “I dare say I shall be interested—but I believe it requires that with me.", "제인이 답했다. \"분명 관심이 생기겠지요—하지만 저는 그걸 봐야만 그렇게 되나 봐요."], ["And as it is some months since Miss Campbell married, the impression may be a little worn off.”", "그리고 미스 캠벨이 결혼한 지 몇 달이 지났으니, 그 인상도 좀 무뎌졌을 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes, he has been gone just four weeks, as you observe, Miss Woodhouse,”", "\"네, 그분이 떠난 지 딱 사 주 됐어요, 미스 우드하우스 말씀대로요.\""], ["said Miss Bates, “four weeks yesterday.—A Miss Hawkins!—Well, I had always rather fancied it would be some young lady hereabouts; not that I ever—Mrs.", "미스 베이츠가 말했다. \"어제로 사 주째였지요.—어느 미스 호킨스라니!—글쎄요, 저는 늘 이 근방의 어느 아가씨가 될 거라고 막연히 생각했답니다."], ["Cole once whispered to me—but I immediately said, ‘No, Mr.", "그렇다고 제가 한 번이라도—언젠가 콜 부인이 저한테 귀띔하긴 했지만—저는 곧바로 '아니에요, 미스터 엘턴은 더없이 훌륭한 청년이지만—'"], ["Elton is a most worthy young man—but’—In short, I do not think I am particularly quick at those sort of discoveries.", "하고 말했지요—요컨대, 저는 그런 걸 알아채는 데 특별히 빠른 사람은 못 되나 봐요."], ["I do not pretend to it.", "그런 척도 안 하고요."], ["What is before me, I see.", "제 눈앞에 있는 건 보지요."], ["At the same time, nobody could wonder if Mr. Elton should have aspired—Miss Woodhouse lets me chatter on, so good-humouredly.", "그러면서도, 미스터 엘턴이 더 높은 곳을 바랐다 한들 누가 이상하게 여길 수 있겠어요—미스 우드하우스는 이렇게 마음 좋게 제가 수다 떨도록 내버려 두시네요."], ["She knows I would not offend for the world. How does Miss Smith do?", "제가 무슨 일이 있어도 누구 마음 상하게 할 사람이 아니라는 걸 아시니까요."], ["She seems quite recovered now.", "미스 스미스는 어떻게 지내요?"], ["Have you heard from Mrs.", "이젠 아주 회복된 것 같던데요."], ["John Knightley lately? Oh!", "미시즈 존 나이틀리한테서 요즘 소식 들으셨어요?"], ["those dear little children.", "아유! 그 사랑스러운 어린것들."], ["Jane, do you know I always fancy Mr. Dixon like Mr.", "제인, 너 알다시피 나는 늘 미스터 딕슨이 미스터 존 나이틀리를 닮았다고 생각하잖니."], ["John Knightley. I mean in person—tall, and with that sort of look—and not very talkative.”", "외모 말이야—키가 크고, 그런 인상에다가—말수가 그리 많지 않은 것까지.\""]], [["“Quite wrong, my dear aunt; there is no likeness at all.”", "\"전혀 아니에요, 이모. 닮은 데가 하나도 없어요.\""]], [["“Very odd!", "\"참 이상하구나!"], ["but one never does form a just idea of any body beforehand.", "하지만 사람은 누구든 미리 제대로 그려 보는 법이 없지."], ["One takes up a notion, and runs away with it.", "한번 생각을 잡으면 그걸로 멋대로 내달리니까."], ["Mr. Dixon, you say, is not, strictly speaking, handsome?”", "그러니까 미스터 딕슨이 엄밀히 말해 잘생긴 건 아니라는 거지?\""]], [["“Handsome!", "\"잘생겼다니요!"], ["Oh!", "아유!"], ["no—far from it—certainly plain.", "아니에요—전혀요—분명 평범한 편이에요."], ["I told you he was plain.”", "평범하다고 말씀드렸잖아요.\""]], [["“My dear, you said that Miss Campbell would not allow him to be plain, and that you yourself—”", "\"얘야, 너는 미스 캠벨이 그분을 평범하다고 인정하지 않으려 한다고 했고, 너 자신도—\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아유!"], ["as for me, my judgment is worth nothing.", "저로 말하면, 제 판단은 아무 쓸모가 없는걸요."], ["Where I have a regard, I always think a person well-looking.", "제가 호감을 품으면 그 사람은 늘 잘생겨 보이거든요."], ["But I gave what I believed the general opinion, when I called him plain.”", "다만 그분을 평범하다고 했을 땐, 그게 일반적인 평이라고 믿어서 그렇게 말한 거예요.\""]], [["“Well, my dear Jane, I believe we must be running away.", "\"자, 얘 제인아, 우리 이제 그만 가 봐야겠다."], ["The weather does not look well, and grandmama will be uneasy.", "날이 좋아 보이지 않고, 할머니가 안절부절못하실 거야."], ["You are too obliging, my dear Miss Woodhouse; but we really must take leave.", "너무 친절하게 대해 주시네요, 미스 우드하우스. 하지만 정말 이만 가 봐야겠어요."], ["This has been a most agreeable piece of news indeed.", "정말이지 더없이 흐뭇한 소식이었어요."], ["I shall just go round by Mrs.", "저는 콜 부인 댁에 잠깐 들렀다 갈게요."], ["Cole’s; but I shall not stop three minutes: and, Jane, you had better go home directly—I would not have you out in a shower!—We think she is the better for Highbury already.", "그래도 삼 분도 안 머물 거예요. 그리고 제인, 너는 곧장 집으로 가는 게 좋겠다—소나기를 맞게 하고 싶진 않으니까!—이 아이가 하이베리 덕에 벌써 나아진 것 같아요."], ["Thank you, we do indeed.", "고맙습니다, 정말 그래요."], ["I shall not attempt calling on Mrs.", "고더드 부인 댁엔 들르지 않을래요."], ["Goddard, for I really do not think she cares for any thing but boiled pork: when we dress the leg it will be another thing.", "그분은 정말이지 삶은 돼지고기 말고는 아무것에도 관심이 없으신 것 같거든요. 다릿살을 요리하면 또 모를까요."], ["Good morning to you, my dear sir.", "안녕히 계세요, 어르신."], ["Oh! Mr.", "아유!"], ["Knightley is coming too.", "미스터 나이틀리도 가시는군요."], ["Well, that is so very!—I am sure if Jane is tired, you will be so kind as to give her your arm.—Mr. Elton, and Miss Hawkins!—Good morning to you.”", "어머, 그거 정말이지!—제인이 지치면 친절하게 팔을 빌려주시겠지요.—미스터 엘턴이라니, 미스 호킨스라니!—안녕히들 계세요.\""]], [["Emma, alone with her father, had half her attention wanted by him while he lamented that young people would be in such a hurry to marry—and to marry strangers too—and the other half she could give to her own view of the subject.", "아버지와 단둘이 남은 엠마는, 젊은 사람들이 그렇게 서둘러 결혼하려 든다며—게다가 낯선 사람과 결혼하려 든다며—탄식하는 아버지에게 주의의 절반을 빼앗겼고, 나머지 절반은 그 일에 대한 자기 자신의 견해에 쓸 수 있었다."], ["It was to herself an amusing and a very welcome piece of news, as proving that Mr.", "그것은 그녀에게 재미있고 무척 반가운 소식이었으니, 미스터 엘턴이 그리 오래 괴로워하지는 못했다는 증거였기 때문이다."], ["Elton could not have suffered long; but she was sorry for Harriet: Harriet must feel it—and all that she could hope was, by giving the first information herself, to save her from hearing it abruptly from others.", "그러나 해리엇이 안쓰러웠다. 해리엇은 분명 충격을 받을 터였다—그리고 그녀가 바랄 수 있는 것이라곤, 자기 입으로 먼저 알려 줌으로써 남에게서 불쑥 듣게 되는 일만은 막아 주는 것뿐이었다."], ["It was now about the time that she was likely to call.", "지금쯤이면 해리엇이 들를 무렵이었다."], ["If she were to meet Miss Bates in her way!—and upon its beginning to rain, Emma was obliged to expect that the weather would be detaining her at Mrs. Goddard’s, and that the intelligence would undoubtedly rush upon her without preparation.", "오는 길에 미스 베이츠와 마주치기라도 한다면!—게다가 비가 내리기 시작하자, 엠마는 이 날씨 때문에 해리엇이 고더드 부인 댁에 발이 묶일 테고, 그러면 그 소식이 틀림없이 아무 마음의 준비도 없는 그녀에게 들이닥치리라고 예상할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["The shower was heavy, but short; and it had not been over five minutes, when in came Harriet, with just the heated, agitated look which hurrying thither with a full heart was likely to give; and the “Oh!", "소나기는 거셌으나 짧았다. 그것이 그친 지 오 분도 안 되어 해리엇이 들어왔는데, 가슴 벅찬 채 부랴부랴 달려온 사람에게 어울릴 법한 바로 그 달아오르고 들뜬 얼굴이었다."], ["Miss Woodhouse, what do you think has happened!”", "곧장 터져 나온 \"아유! 미스 우드하우스, 무슨 일이 있었는지 아세요!\"라는 외침은 그에 걸맞은 동요를 그대로 드러내고 있었다."], ["which instantly burst forth, had all the evidence of corresponding perturbation.", "일격이 이미 가해진 마당이라, 엠마는 지금 가장 큰 친절은 들어 주는 것이라고 느꼈다. 해리엇은 거침없이, 들려줄 이야기를 다급하게 쏟아 냈다."], ["As the blow was given, Emma felt that she could not now shew greater kindness than in listening; and Harriet, unchecked, ran eagerly through what she had to tell.", "그녀는 삼십 분 전에 고더드 부인 댁을 나섰다—비가 올까 봐 두려웠다—금방이라도 퍼부을까 봐 두려웠다—하지만 하트필드에 먼저 닿을 수 있을 것 같았다—그래서 있는 힘껏 서둘렀다. 그런데 자기 가운을 짓고 있는 어느 젊은 여자의 집 앞을 지나다가, 잠깐 들어가서 어떻게 돼 가나 보자는 생각이 들었다."], ["“She had set out from Mrs.", "거기 잠시도 머물지 않은 것 같은데, 나오자마자 비가 내리기 시작했고, 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."], ["Goddard’s half an hour ago—she had been afraid it would rain—she had been afraid it would pour down every moment—but she thought she might get to Hartfield first—she had hurried on as fast as possible; but then, as she was passing by the house where a young woman was making up a gown for her, she thought she would just step in and see how it went on; and though she did not seem to stay half a moment there, soon after she came out it began to rain, and she did not know what to do; so she ran on directly, as fast as she could, and took shelter at Ford’s.”—Ford’s was the principal woollen-draper, linen-draper, and haberdasher’s shop united; the shop first in size and fashion in the place.—“And so, there she had set, without an idea of any thing in the world, full ten minutes, perhaps—when, all of a sudden, who should come in—to be sure it was so very odd!—but they always dealt at Ford’s—who should come in, but Elizabeth Martin and her brother!—Dear Miss Woodhouse!", "그래서 곧장 있는 힘껏 달려 포드 가게로 비를 피해 들어갔다.—포드 가게란 모직물점, 아마포점, 잡화점을 겸한 곳으로, 그 마을에서 규모로나 유행으로나 으뜸가는 상점이었다.—그래서 그녀는 거기 앉아, 세상 그 무엇도 생각지 못한 채, 족히 십 분쯤 있었다.—여기서 해리엇은 숨도 안 쉬고 이어 갔다. \"그런데 갑자기, 누가 들어왔는지 아세요—정말이지 너무도 묘했어요!—하긴 그 사람들은 늘 포드에서 물건을 샀으니까요—누가 들어왔느냐 하면, 엘리자베스 마틴과 그 오빠였어요!—어머, 미스 우드하우스!"], ["only think.", "생각 좀 해 보세요. 저는 기절하는 줄 알았어요."], ["I thought I should have fainted.", "어찌할 바를 몰랐죠. 저는 문 가까이에 앉아 있었는데—엘리자베스는 곧바로 저를 봤지만, 그분은 못 봤어요."], ["I did not know what to do.", "우산을 챙기느라 바빴거든요. 그 여자가 저를 본 게 분명한데, 곧바로 눈길을 돌리고 모른 체했어요."], ["I was sitting near the door—Elizabeth saw me directly; but he did not; he was busy with the umbrella.", "그러고는 둘 다 가게 저 안쪽 끝으로 가 버렸고요. 그런데 저는 계속 문 가까이에 앉아 있었지 뭐예요!—아유!"], ["I am sure she saw me, but she looked away directly, and took no notice; and they both went to quite the farther end of the shop; and I kept sitting near the door!—Oh!", "어쩜! 저는 너무도 비참했어요!"], ["dear; I was so miserable!", "분명 제 가운만큼이나 새하얗게 질려 있었을 거예요. 비 때문에 그냥 나갈 수도 없었고요."], ["I am sure I must have been as white as my gown.", "하지만 그 자리만 아니라면 세상 어디라도 가 있고 싶었답니다.—아유! 어쩜, 미스 우드하우스—그러다 마침내, 그분이 돌아보고 저를 본 모양이에요."], ["I could not go away you know, because of the rain; but I did so wish myself anywhere in the world but there.—Oh!", "물건 사는 걸 계속하는 대신 둘이 소곤거리기 시작했거든요. 분명 제 이야기를 하고 있었어요."], ["dear, Miss Woodhouse—well, at last, I fancy, he looked round and saw me; for instead of going on with her buyings, they began whispering to one another.", "그리고 그분이 누이더러 저한테 말을 걸라고 설득하는 거라는 생각이 자꾸 들었어요—(그랬을까요, 미스 우드하우스?)—이내 그 여자가 다가왔으니까요—제 앞까지 바짝 다가와서 안부를 묻고는, 제가 원하면 악수라도 할 듯한 기색이었어요. 예전에 하던 것과 똑같은 식은 하나도 아니었어요."], ["I am sure they were talking of me; and I could not help thinking that he was persuading her to speak to me—(do you think he was, Miss Woodhouse?)—for presently she came forward—came quite up to me, and asked me how I did, and seemed ready to shake hands, if I would.", "그 여자가 변한 게 보였거든요. 하지만 그래도 아주 다정하게 굴려고 애쓰는 것 같긴 했고, 우리는 악수를 하고 한동안 서서 이야기를 나눴어요."], ["She did not do any of it in the same way that she used; I could see she was altered; but, however, she seemed to try to be very friendly, and we shook hands, and stood talking some time; but I know no more what I said—I was in such a tremble!—I remember she said she was sorry we never met now; which I thought almost too kind!", "하지만 제가 무슨 말을 했는지는 도무지 모르겠어요—어찌나 떨었던지요!—그 여자가 이제 우리가 통 못 만나서 섭섭하다고 한 건 기억나요. 너무 다정한 말이다 싶었죠!"], ["Dear, Miss Woodhouse, I was absolutely miserable!", "어머, 미스 우드하우스, 저는 정말이지 비참했어요! 그 무렵엔 비가 그치기 시작했고, 저는 무슨 일이 있어도 거기서 빠져나가야겠다고 마음먹었어요—그런데—생각 좀 해 보세요!—그분도 저를 향해 다가오고 있는 거예요—아시잖아요, 천천히, 마치 어쩔 줄 모르는 사람처럼요."], ["By that time, it was beginning to hold up, and I was determined that nothing should stop me from getting away—and then—only think!—I found he was coming up towards me too—slowly you know, and as if he did not quite know what to do; and so he came and spoke, and I answered—and I stood for a minute, feeling dreadfully, you know, one can’t tell how; and then I took courage, and said it did not rain, and I must go; and so off I set; and I had not got three yards from the door, when he came after me, only to say, if I was going to Hartfield, he thought I had much better go round by Mr.", "그렇게 다가와서 말을 걸었고, 저는 대답했죠—그리고 잠깐 서 있었는데, 어찌나 끔찍하던지요, 아시잖아요, 뭐라 말로 못 할 그런 기분이었어요. 그러다 용기를 내서, 비가 그쳤으니 가 봐야겠다고 했죠."], ["Cole’s stables, for I should find the near way quite floated by this rain.", "그래서 나섰어요. 그런데 문에서 세 발짝도 못 갔는데, 그분이 따라와서는, 제가 하트필드로 갈 거면 미스터 콜의 마구간 쪽으로 돌아가는 편이 훨씬 나을 거라고, 가까운 길은 이번 비에 죄다 잠겼을 거라는 말만 하더라고요."], ["Oh!", "아유!"], ["dear, I thought it would have been the death of me!", "어쩜, 저는 그 자리에서 죽는 줄 알았어요! 그래서 정말 감사하다고 했죠."], ["So I said, I was very much obliged to him: you know I could not do less; and then he went back to Elizabeth, and I came round by the stables—I believe I did—but I hardly knew where I was, or any thing about it.", "아시잖아요, 그보다 덜할 수는 없었으니까요. 그러고 나서 그분은 엘리자베스한테 돌아갔고, 저는 마구간 쪽으로 돌아왔어요—그랬을 거예요—하지만 제가 어디 있는지, 무슨 일이 어찌 된 건지 거의 알지도 못했답니다."], ["Oh!", "아유!"], ["Miss Woodhouse, I would rather done any thing than have it happen: and yet, you know, there was a sort of satisfaction in seeing him behave so pleasantly and so kindly.", "미스 우드하우스, 그런 일을 겪느니 차라리 무슨 일이든 했을 거예요. 그러면서도, 아시잖아요, 그분이 그렇게 상냥하고 친절하게 구는 걸 보니 어쩐지 흐뭇한 구석도 있더라고요."], ["And Elizabeth, too. Oh!", "엘리자베스도 그렇고요."], ["Miss Woodhouse, do talk to me and make me comfortable again.”", "아유! 미스 우드하우스, 저한테 말 좀 해 주세요, 마음이 좀 편해지게요.\""]], [["Very sincerely did Emma wish to do so; but it was not immediately in her power.", "엠마는 진심으로 그러고 싶었으나, 당장은 그럴 형편이 못 되었다."], ["She was obliged to stop and think.", "그녀는 멈추어 생각해야만 했다."], ["She was not thoroughly comfortable herself.", "그녀 자신부터 마음이 온전히 편치 않았다."], ["The young man’s conduct, and his sister’s, seemed the result of real feeling, and she could not but pity them.", "그 청년의 처신과 그 누이의 처신은 진실한 감정에서 비롯된 것으로 보였고, 그들을 가엾이 여기지 않을 수 없었다."], ["As Harriet described it, there had been an interesting mixture of wounded affection and genuine delicacy in their behaviour.", "해리엇의 묘사대로라면, 그들의 거동에는 상처받은 애정과 진정한 섬세함이 흥미롭게 뒤섞여 있었다."], ["But she had believed them to be well-meaning, worthy people before; and what difference did this make in the evils of the connexion?", "그러나 그녀는 전부터도 그들이 선의를 지닌 훌륭한 사람들이라고 믿어 왔던 터—그렇다면 이번 일이 그 인연의 해악에 무슨 차이를 만든단 말인가?"], ["It was folly to be disturbed by it.", "그것에 흔들리는 것은 어리석은 짓이었다."], ["Of course, he must be sorry to lose her—they must be all sorry.", "물론 그는 그녀를 잃어 서운할 터—다들 서운할 터였다."], ["Ambition, as well as love, had probably been mortified.", "사랑뿐 아니라 야심도 십중팔구 꺾였으리라."], ["They might all have hoped to rise by Harriet’s acquaintance: and besides, what was the value of Harriet’s description?—So easily pleased—so little discerning;—what signified her praise?", "그들 모두 해리엇과 사귐으로써 신분이 오르기를 바랐을지 모른다. 게다가 해리엇의 묘사가 무슨 값어치가 있단 말인가?—그토록 쉽게 흡족해하고—그토록 분별이 없는 사람의 칭찬이 다 무슨 의미가 있겠는가?"]], [["She exerted herself, and did try to make her comfortable, by considering all that had passed as a mere trifle, and quite unworthy of being dwelt on,", "그녀는 마음을 다잡고, 지나간 일을 죄다 사소한 것으로, 그리고 곱씹을 만한 가치라곤 도무지 없는 것으로 치부함으로써 해리엇을 위로하려 애썼다."]], [["“It might be distressing, for the moment,”", "\"잠깐은 괴로울 수도 있겠지요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “but you seem to have behaved extremely well; and it is over—and may never—can never, as a first meeting, occur again, and therefore you need not think about it.”", "\"하지만 당신은 더없이 잘 처신한 것 같아요. 게다가 이미 다 끝난 일이고요—첫 만남으로서야 다시는 일어날 리—일어날 수도 없는 일이니까, 더는 마음에 둘 필요가 없어요.\""]], [["Harriet said, “very true,” and she “would not think about it;”", "해리엇은 \"정말 맞아요\"라고 했고, \"더는 마음에 두지 않겠다\"고도 했다."], ["but still she talked of it—still she could talk of nothing else; and Emma, at last, in order to put the Martins out of her head, was obliged to hurry on the news, which she had meant to give with so much tender caution; hardly knowing herself whether to rejoice or be angry, ashamed or only amused, at such a state of mind in poor Harriet—such a conclusion of Mr.", "그러나 여전히 그 이야기를 했다—여전히 그 이야기 말고는 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 그리하여 엠마는 마침내 마틴 식구들을 그녀의 머릿속에서 몰아내기 위해, 그토록 다정하고 조심스럽게 전하려 했던 그 소식을 서둘러 꺼낼 수밖에 없었다."], ["Elton’s importance with her!", "가엾은 해리엇의 그런 심정을—미스터 엘턴이 그녀에게 지녔던 의미가 그렇게 결말지어진 것을—기뻐해야 할지 화를 내야 할지, 부끄러워해야 할지 그저 우스워해야 할지, 그녀 자신도 도무지 알 수 없었다."]], [["Mr. Elton’s rights, however, gradually revived.", "그러나 미스터 엘턴의 권세는 차츰 되살아났다."], ["Though she did not feel the first intelligence as she might have done the day before, or an hour before, its interest soon increased; and before their first conversation was over, she had talked herself into all the sensations of curiosity, wonder and regret, pain and pleasure, as to this fortunate Miss Hawkins, which could conduce to place the Martins under proper subordination in her fancy.", "처음 들은 소식이 어제나 한 시간 전이었다면 느꼈을 만큼은 와닿지 않았지만, 그 흥미는 곧 커졌다. 그리하여 첫 대화가 끝나기도 전에, 그녀는 이 운 좋은 미스 호킨스를 둘러싼 온갖 호기심과 놀라움과 아쉬움, 아픔과 기쁨의 감정에 스스로 빠져들었으니, 그것은 마틴 식구들을 그녀의 상상 속에서 마땅한 종속의 자리에 도로 밀어 넣기에 충분한 것이었다."]], [["Emma learned to be rather glad that there had been such a meeting.", "엠마는 차츰 그런 만남이 있었던 것을 도리어 다행으로 여기게 되었다."], ["It had been serviceable in deadening the first shock, without retaining any influence to alarm.", "그것은 첫 충격을 무디게 하는 데 보탬이 되었으면서도, 불안을 자아낼 만한 여파는 조금도 남기지 않았다."], ["As Harriet now lived, the Martins could not get at her, without seeking her, where hitherto they had wanted either the courage or the condescension to seek her; for since her refusal of the brother, the sisters never had been at Mrs.", "지금 해리엇이 사는 형편으로는, 마틴 식구들이 그녀를 일부러 찾아 나서지 않는 한 그녀에게 닿을 수 없었다. 그런데 그들은 지금껏 그녀를 찾아 나설 용기도, 그렇게 몸을 굽힐 마음도 없었다."], ["Goddard’s; and a twelvemonth might pass without their being thrown together again, with any necessity, or even any power of speech.", "해리엇이 오빠의 청혼을 거절한 뒤로 그 누이들은 한 번도 고더드 부인 댁에 온 적이 없었고, 어떤 필요로든, 아니 말을 나눌 기회조차 없이, 다시 한자리에 마주치는 일 없이 열두 달이 지날 법도 했다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter IV", "# 제2권 4장"]], [["Human nature is so well disposed towards those who are in interesting situations, that a young person, who either marries or dies, is sure of being kindly spoken of.", "인간이란 흥미로운 처지에 놓인 사람을 향해서는 어찌나 너그러운지, 결혼을 하거나 세상을 떠나는 젊은이라면 으레 좋은 말로 입에 오르내리게 마련이다."]], [["A week had not passed since Miss Hawkins’s name was first mentioned in Highbury, before she was, by some means or other, discovered to have every recommendation of person and mind; to be handsome, elegant, highly accomplished, and perfectly amiable: and when Mr.", "미스 호킨스라는 이름이 하이베리에서 처음 거론된 지 일주일이 채 지나기도 전에, 그녀는 어떤 경로로인가 용모와 마음씨에서 더할 나위 없는 장점을 두루 갖춘 사람으로 — 아름답고 우아하며 재예가 뛰어나고 더없이 상냥한 사람으로 밝혀져 있었다."], ["Elton himself arrived to triumph in his happy prospects, and circulate the fame of her merits, there was very little more for him to do, than to tell her Christian name, and say whose music she principally played.", "그리하여 미스터 엘턴이 직접 돌아와 자기 앞에 펼쳐진 행복한 전망을 한껏 자랑하고 그녀의 미덕에 관한 소문을 퍼뜨릴 즈음에는, 그가 새로이 보탤 일이라곤 그녀의 세례명을 알려 주고 그녀가 주로 누구의 곡을 연주하는지 말해 주는 것 말고는 거의 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["Mr. Elton returned, a very happy man.", "미스터 엘턴은 더없이 행복한 남자가 되어 돌아왔다."], ["He had gone away rejected and mortified—disappointed in a very sanguine hope, after a series of what appeared to him strong encouragement; and not only losing the right lady, but finding himself debased to the level of a very wrong one.", "떠날 때는 거절당하고 굴욕을 안고 — 강한 격려로만 보였던 일들이 줄줄이 이어진 끝에 한껏 부풀었던 희망이 꺾여 — 게다가 마땅한 여인을 잃었을 뿐 아니라 도무지 어울리지 않는 여인과 동급으로 격하되는 처지에 놓였었다."], ["He had gone away deeply offended—he came back engaged to another—and to another as superior, of course, to the first, as under such circumstances what is gained always is to what is lost.", "그렇게 깊이 모욕당한 채 떠났던 그가 — 다른 여인과 약혼하여 돌아온 것이다 — 이런 경우에 으레 얻은 것이 잃은 것보다 나아 보이듯, 첫 번째 여인보다 당연히 한층 빼어난 여인과."], ["He came back gay and self-satisfied, eager and busy, caring nothing for Miss Woodhouse, and defying Miss Smith.", "그는 명랑하고 흡족한 얼굴로, 의욕에 차 분주하게, 미스 우드하우스 따위는 안중에도 없고 미스 스미스 같은 것은 우습다는 듯 돌아왔다."]], [["The charming Augusta Hawkins, in addition to all the usual advantages of perfect beauty and merit, was in possession of an independent fortune, of so many thousands as would always be called ten; a point of some dignity, as well as some convenience: the story told well; he had not thrown himself away—he had gained a woman of 10,000 l.", "매혹적인 오거스타 호킨스는 완벽한 미모와 미덕이라는 으레 따라붙는 온갖 장점에 더하여, 독립적인 재산을 — 언제나 만 파운드라 불릴 만한 수천 파운드를 — 소유하고 있었다. 이는 얼마간의 위신이자 또한 얼마간의 실속이 되는 대목이었다."], ["or thereabouts; and he had gained her with such delightful rapidity—the first hour of introduction had been so very soon followed by distinguishing notice; the history which he had to give Mrs.", "이야기는 그럴듯하게 들렸다. 그는 제 처지를 내던진 것이 아니라 — 만 파운드 남짓을 지닌 여인을 얻은 것이었다."], ["Cole of the rise and progress of the affair was so glorious—the steps so quick, from the accidental rencontre, to the dinner at Mr.", "게다가 그 여인을 어찌나 신나게 빨리 얻었던지 — 소개받은 첫 시간이 지나기 무섭게 각별한 주목이 뒤따랐다."], ["Green’s, and the party at Mrs. Brown’s—smiles and blushes rising in importance—with consciousness and agitation richly scattered—the lady had been so easily impressed—so sweetly disposed—had in short, to use a most intelligible phrase, been so very ready to have him, that vanity and prudence were equally contented.", "그가 콜 부인에게 들려줄 이 연애의 발단과 진전에 관한 이야기는 어찌나 영광스러운지 — 그 단계들이 어찌나 빠르게 흘렀던지, 우연한 마주침에서 미스터 그린네 만찬으로, 다시 브라운 부인네 모임으로 — 미소와 홍조가 점점 의미심장해지고 — 의식과 동요가 풍성하게 흩뿌려지고 — 그 숙녀는 어찌나 쉽게 마음이 움직였던지 — 어찌나 다정하게 호의를 보였던지 — 한마디로, 더없이 알기 쉬운 표현을 빌리자면, 그를 받아들일 채비가 어찌나 충분했던지, 허영심과 분별심이 똑같이 흡족했다."]], [["He had caught both substance and shadow—both fortune and affection, and was just the happy man he ought to be; talking only of himself and his own concerns—expecting to be congratulated—ready to be laughed at—and, with cordial, fearless smiles, now addressing all the young ladies of the place, to whom, a few weeks ago, he would have been more cautiously gallant.", "그는 실체와 그림자를 — 재산과 연정을 함께 거머쥐었고, 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 행복한 남자였다. 오로지 자기 이야기와 제 일만 떠벌리며 — 축하받기를 기대하고 — 놀림감이 되어도 좋다는 듯이 — 거리낌 없는 다정한 미소를 지으며, 몇 주 전이라면 한층 조심스레 점잔을 빼며 대했을 이 고장의 모든 젊은 숙녀에게 이제 거침없이 말을 건넸다."]], [["The wedding was no distant event, as the parties had only themselves to please, and nothing but the necessary preparations to wait for; and when he set out for Bath again, there was a general expectation, which a certain glance of Mrs.", "혼례는 머지않은 일이었으니, 당사자들로서는 자기들 마음만 흡족하면 그만이라 그저 필요한 준비만 마치기를 기다리면 되었다."], ["Cole’s did not seem to contradict, that when he next entered Highbury he would bring his bride.", "그가 다시 바스로 떠날 무렵, 다음번에 하이베리에 들어설 때는 신부를 데려오리라는 것이 모두의 일반적인 예상이었고, 콜 부인의 어떤 눈빛도 그 예상을 부정하는 것 같지는 않았다."]], [["During his present short stay, Emma had barely seen him; but just enough to feel that the first meeting was over, and to give her the impression of his not being improved by the mixture of pique and pretension, now spread over his air.", "이번 짧은 체류 동안 엠마는 그를 거의 보지 못했다. 그러나 첫 대면이 끝났다는 것을 느끼기에는 충분할 만큼 보았고, 이제 그의 태도 전반에 퍼진 분함과 우쭐함이 뒤섞이는 바람에 그가 조금도 나아지지 않았다는 인상을 받았다."], ["She was, in fact, beginning very much to wonder that she had ever thought him pleasing at all; and his sight was so inseparably connected with some very disagreeable feelings, that, except in a moral light, as a penance, a lesson, a source of profitable humiliation to her own mind, she would have been thankful to be assured of never seeing him again.", "사실 엠마는 자기가 도대체 어떻게 그를 호감 가는 사람이라 여긴 적이 있었던가 몹시 의아해지기 시작하던 참이었다. 그의 모습은 어떤 무척 불쾌한 감정과 떼려야 뗄 수 없이 얽혀 있었으므로, 자기 마음에 참회요 교훈이요 유익한 굴욕의 원천이 된다는 도덕적 관점에서가 아니라면, 다시는 그를 보지 않으리라는 확언을 얻을 수만 있다면 고마울 지경이었다."], ["She wished him very well; but he gave her pain, and his welfare twenty miles off would administer most satisfaction.", "그녀는 그가 잘되기를 진심으로 바랐다. 그러나 그는 그녀에게 고통을 주었으니, 그의 안녕은 이십 마일 밖에서나 가장 흡족하게 누려질 일이었다."]], [["The pain of his continued residence in Highbury, however, must certainly be lessened by his marriage.", "그렇기는 해도 그가 하이베리에 계속 머무는 데서 오는 고통은 그의 결혼으로 분명 덜어질 터였다."], ["Many vain solicitudes would be prevented—many awkwardnesses smoothed by it. A Mrs.", "그 덕에 부질없는 근심도 숱하게 막아질 것이요 — 어색한 일도 숱하게 매끄러워질 것이었다."], ["Elton would be an excuse for any change of intercourse; former intimacy might sink without remark.", "엘턴 부인이라는 존재는 교제 방식을 바꾸는 데 좋은 핑계가 되어 줄 터였다. 예전의 친밀함도 말썽 없이 가라앉을 수 있었다."], ["It would be almost beginning their life of civility again.", "그것은 거의 새로 예의의 삶을 시작하는 셈이 될 것이었다."]], [["Of the lady, individually, Emma thought very little.", "그 숙녀 개인에 관해서라면 엠마는 그다지 깊이 생각하지 않았다."], ["She was good enough for Mr.", "미스터 엘턴에게야 충분히 어울리는 여자였다, 의심할 여지 없이."], ["Elton, no doubt; accomplished enough for Highbury—handsome enough—to look plain, probably, by Harriet’s side.", "하이베리에 어울릴 만큼은 재예를 갖추었고 — 충분히 어여뻤고 — 십중팔구 해리엇 곁에 서면 수수해 보일 정도였다."], ["As to connexion, there Emma was perfectly easy; persuaded, that after all his own vaunted claims and disdain of Harriet, he had done nothing.", "가문에 관해서라면, 엠마는 마음이 더없이 편했다. 그가 그토록 자기 가문을 떠벌리고 해리엇을 업신여겼어도 결국 변변한 것은 아무것도 이루지 못했으리라 확신했기 때문이다."], ["On that article, truth seemed attainable.", "그 점에서만큼은 진실에 다다를 수 있을 듯했다."], ["What she was, must be uncertain; but who she was, might be found out; and setting aside the 10,000 l., it did not appear that she was at all Harriet’s superior.", "그녀가 어떤 사람인지는 불확실할 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 그녀가 누구인지는 알아낼 수 있었으니 — 만 파운드를 제쳐 두고 보면, 그녀가 해리엇보다 조금이라도 나은 점이 있어 보이지는 않았다."], ["She brought no name, no blood, no alliance.", "그녀가 가져오는 것은 이름도, 혈통도, 인척도 아니었다."], ["Miss Hawkins was the youngest of the two daughters of a Bristol—merchant, of course, he must be called; but, as the whole of the profits of his mercantile life appeared so very moderate, it was not unfair to guess the dignity of his line of trade had been very moderate also.", "미스 호킨스는 어느 브리스틀 — 물론 상인이라 불러야 마땅하겠지만 — 그 상인의 두 딸 가운데 막내였다. 다만 그가 평생 장사로 거둔 이익이 죄다 그토록 변변찮아 보였으므로, 그가 손댄 장사의 격 또한 무척 변변찮았으리라 짐작해도 부당하지는 않았다."], ["Part of every winter she had been used to spend in Bath; but Bristol was her home, the very heart of Bristol; for though the father and mother had died some years ago, an uncle remained—in the law line—nothing more distinctly honourable was hazarded of him, than that he was in the law line; and with him the daughter had lived.", "그녀는 해마다 겨울이면 얼마간 바스에서 지내곤 했으나, 그녀의 집은 브리스틀, 그것도 브리스틀의 한복판이었다. 아버지와 어머니는 몇 해 전 세상을 떴어도 외삼촌 하나가 남아 있었으니 — 법률 쪽 일을 하는 사람이었다 — 그에 관해 무릅쓰고 밝힌 것이라곤 법률 쪽 일을 한다는 것보다 더 또렷하게 떳떳한 내용은 없었고, 그 딸은 그와 함께 살았다."], ["Emma guessed him to be the drudge of some attorney, and too stupid to rise.", "엠마는 그가 어느 변호사 밑에서 부려지는 잡역꾼일 테고, 출세하기에는 너무 둔한 위인이리라 짐작했다."], ["And all the grandeur of the connexion seemed dependent on the elder sister, who was very well married, to a gentleman in a great way, near Bristol, who kept two carriages!", "그리고 그 인척의 온갖 영화는 죄다, 마차를 두 대나 부리는, 브리스틀 근처에서 사업을 크게 하는 신사와 아주 잘 결혼한 언니에게 달려 있는 듯했다!"], ["That was the wind-up of the history; that was the glory of Miss Hawkins.", "그것이 이야기의 마무리요, 그것이 미스 호킨스의 영광이었다."]], [["Could she but have given Harriet her feelings about it all!", "이 모든 것에 대한 자기 심정을 해리엇에게 그대로 전해 줄 수만 있다면! 엠마는 말로 그녀를 사랑에 빠뜨렸다."], ["She had talked her into love; but, alas!", "그러나 아아! 말로 그 사랑에서 빠져나오게 하기란 그처럼 쉬운 일이 아니었다."], ["she was not so easily to be talked out of it.", "해리엇의 마음속 그 숱한 빈자리를 채워 줄 대상이 지닌 매력은 말로 떨쳐 버릴 수 있는 것이 아니었다."], ["The charm of an object to occupy the many vacancies of Harriet’s mind was not to be talked away.", "그가 다른 사람으로 대체될 수는 있을 터였다. 분명 정말로 그렇게 될 것이었다."], ["He might be superseded by another; he certainly would indeed; nothing could be clearer; even a Robert Martin would have been sufficient; but nothing else, she feared, would cure her.", "그보다 더 분명한 일도 없었다. 로버트 마틴 같은 사람으로도 충분할 정도였다."], ["Harriet was one of those, who, having once begun, would be always in love. And now, poor girl!", "그러나 그 밖에 다른 무엇으로도 그녀를 낫게 할 수는 없으리라고 엠마는 우려했다."], ["she was considerably worse from this reappearance of Mr. Elton.", "해리엇은 한번 시작하면 늘 사랑에 빠져 있는 부류의 사람이었다."], ["She was always having a glimpse of him somewhere or other.", "그런데 이제, 가엾기도 하지! 그녀는 미스터 엘턴이 다시 나타난 탓에 한결 더 나빠졌다."], ["Emma saw him only once; but two or three times every day Harriet was sure just to meet with him, or just to miss him, just to hear his voice, or see his shoulder, just to have something occur to preserve him in her fancy, in all the favouring warmth of surprize and conjecture.", "어딘가에서 언제나 그의 모습을 흘끗흘끗 보게 되었다. 엠마가 그를 본 것은 단 한 번이었으나, 해리엇은 하루에도 두세 번씩, 영락없이 그와 막 마주치거나, 막 엇갈리거나, 막 그의 목소리를 듣거나, 막 그의 어깨를 보거나, 막 무슨 일이 일어나 그를 자기 환상 속에 — 놀라움과 추측이 부추기는 그 온기 속에 — 고스란히 간직하게 되곤 했다."], ["She was, moreover, perpetually hearing about him; for, excepting when at Hartfield, she was always among those who saw no fault in Mr.", "게다가 그녀는 끊임없이 그에 관한 이야기를 들었다."], ["Elton, and found nothing so interesting as the discussion of his concerns; and every report, therefore, every guess—all that had already occurred, all that might occur in the arrangement of his affairs, comprehending income, servants, and furniture, was continually in agitation around her.", "하트필드에 있을 때를 빼면, 그녀는 늘 미스터 엘턴에게서 흠 하나 보지 못하고 그의 일을 논하는 것보다 더 흥미로운 일은 없다고 여기는 사람들 사이에 끼어 있었기 때문이다. 그러니 온갖 소문, 온갖 추측 — 이미 일어난 모든 일, 그의 살림을 꾸리는 데서 앞으로 일어날 법한 모든 일이, 수입이며 하인이며 가구까지 죄다 그녀 주위에서 끊임없이 들끓었다."], ["Her regard was receiving strength by invariable praise of him, and her regrets kept alive, and feelings irritated by ceaseless repetitions of Miss Hawkins’s happiness, and continual observation of, how much he seemed attached!—his air as he walked by the house—the very sitting of his hat, being all in proof of how much he was in love!", "그를 향한 그녀의 호감은 변함없는 칭찬에 힘을 얻었고, 그녀의 회한은 미스 호킨스의 행복을 끝없이 되뇌는 통에 생생히 살아 있었으며, 그가 얼마나 푹 빠진 듯 보이는지를 줄곧 들먹이는 통에 그녀의 감정은 자극받았다! 집 앞을 지날 때의 그 태도며 — 모자를 눌러쓴 그 모양새까지, 죄다 그가 얼마나 사랑에 빠졌는지를 입증하는 증거라는 것이었다!"]], [["Had it been allowable entertainment, had there been no pain to her friend, or reproach to herself, in the waverings of Harriet’s mind, Emma would have been amused by its variations.", "만일 그것이 허락된 즐길거리였다면, 해리엇의 흔들리는 마음에 벗을 향한 아픔도 자신을 향한 자책도 없었다면, 엠마는 그 변덕을 보며 재미있어했을지도 모른다."], ["Sometimes Mr. Elton predominated, sometimes the Martins; and each was occasionally useful as a check to the other.", "어떤 때는 미스터 엘턴이 우세하고 어떤 때는 마틴네가 우세했으며, 저마다 이따금 상대를 견제하는 데 쓸모가 있었다."], ["Mr. Elton’s engagement had been the cure of the agitation of meeting Mr.", "미스터 엘턴의 약혼은 미스터 마틴과 마주쳤던 동요를 가라앉히는 약이 되었다."], ["Martin. The unhappiness produced by the knowledge of that engagement had been a little put aside by Elizabeth Martin’s calling at Mrs.", "그 약혼을 알게 된 데서 비롯된 불행은, 며칠 뒤 엘리자베스 마틴이 고더드 부인 댁을 찾아온 덕에 얼마간 옆으로 밀려나 있었다."], ["Goddard’s a few days afterwards. Harriet had not been at home; but a note had been prepared and left for her, written in the very style to touch; a small mixture of reproach, with a great deal of kindness; and till Mr.", "해리엇은 집에 없었으나, 그녀 앞으로 쪽지 한 장이 마련되어 남겨져 있었으니, 꼭 마음을 울릴 만한 문체로 — 약간의 책망에 다정함을 듬뿍 섞어 — 쓴 것이었다."], ["Elton himself appeared, she had been much occupied by it, continually pondering over what could be done in return, and wishing to do more than she dared to confess.", "미스터 엘턴이 직접 나타나기 전까지 그녀는 그 쪽지에 마음을 온통 빼앗겨, 답례로 무엇을 할 수 있을지 끊임없이 곰곰이 따지며, 차마 털어놓을 엄두는 못 내면서도 그 이상을 하고 싶어 했다."], ["But Mr. Elton, in person, had driven away all such cares.", "그러나 미스터 엘턴이 직접 나타나자 그런 근심은 죄다 쫓겨났다."], ["While he staid, the Martins were forgotten; and on the very morning of his setting off for Bath again, Emma, to dissipate some of the distress it occasioned, judged it best for her to return Elizabeth Martin’s visit.", "그가 머무는 동안 마틴네는 잊혔다. 그리고 그가 다시 바스로 떠나는 바로 그날 아침, 엠마는 그로 인해 빚어진 시름을 얼마간 흩어 버리려, 해리엇이 엘리자베스 마틴의 방문에 답례하는 것이 가장 좋겠다고 판단했다."]], [["How that visit was to be acknowledged—what would be necessary—and what might be safest, had been a point of some doubtful consideration.", "그 방문을 어떻게 갚을 것인가 — 무엇이 필요하고 — 무엇이 가장 무난할 것인가 하는 문제는 적이 망설여지는 고민거리였다."], ["Absolute neglect of the mother and sisters, when invited to come, would be ingratitude.", "와 달라는 초대를 받고도 어머니와 자매들을 아예 모른 척하는 것은 배은망덕한 일일 터였다."], ["It must not be: and yet the danger of a renewal of the acquaintance—!", "그래서는 안 되었다. 그러나 그 교제를 다시 트는 데서 오는 위험이라니 — !"]], [["After much thinking, she could determine on nothing better, than Harriet’s returning the visit; but in a way that, if they had understanding, should convince them that it was to be only a formal acquaintance.", "한참을 궁리한 끝에도 엠마가 정할 수 있었던 최선이라곤, 해리엇이 답방을 가되 — 상대에게 분별이 있다면 이것이 그저 형식적인 교제일 뿐임을 납득시킬 만한 방식으로 가는 것이었다."], ["She meant to take her in the carriage, leave her at the Abbey Mill, while she drove a little farther, and call for her again so soon, as to allow no time for insidious applications or dangerous recurrences to the past, and give the most decided proof of what degree of intimacy was chosen for the future.", "엠마는 그녀를 마차에 태우고 가 애비 밀에 내려 준 다음, 자기는 조금 더 멀리 마차를 몰았다가, 음흉한 청을 늘어놓거나 위태롭게 옛일로 되돌아갈 틈을 조금도 주지 않을 만큼 이내 그녀를 다시 데리러 가, 앞으로 어느 정도의 친밀함을 택할 것인지 더없이 단호하게 증명해 보일 작정이었다."]], [["She could think of nothing better: and though there was something in it which her own heart could not approve—something of ingratitude, merely glossed over—it must be done, or what would become of Harriet?", "그보다 나은 방도는 떠오르지 않았다. 그리고 거기에는 제 마음으로도 도무지 인정할 수 없는 무언가가 — 그럴싸하게 얼버무린 얼마간의 배은망덕이 깃들어 있었으나 — 그래도 그렇게 해야만 했으니, 안 그러면 해리엇은 대체 어찌 된단 말인가?"]], [["# Volume II — Chapter V", "# 제2권 5장"]], [["Small heart had Harriet for visiting.", "해리엇은 방문이 도무지 내키지 않았다."], ["Only half an hour before her friend called for her at Mrs. Goddard’s, her evil stars had led her to the very spot where, at that moment, a trunk, directed to The Rev.", "친구가 고더드 부인 댁으로 그녀를 데리러 오기 겨우 반 시간 전에, 액운이 그녀를 바로 그 자리로 이끌었던 것이다 — 그 순간 정육점 수레에 들어 올려지고 있던 트렁크 하나가 보였으니, 거기엔 \"바스, 화이트 하트, 필립 엘턴 목사 귀하\"라고 적혀 있었고, 그 수레는 트렁크를 역마차가 지나는 곳까지 실어다 줄 참이었다."], ["Philip Elton, White-Hart, Bath, was to be seen under the operation of being lifted into the butcher’s cart, which was to convey it to where the coaches past; and every thing in this world, excepting that trunk and the direction, was consequently a blank.", "그리하여 이 세상의 모든 것이, 저 트렁크와 그 위에 적힌 글귀만 빼고는, 죄다 텅 빈 백지가 되어 버렸다."]], [["She went, however; and when they reached the farm, and she was to be put down, at the end of the broad, neat gravel walk, which led between espalier apple-trees to the front door, the sight of every thing which had given her so much pleasure the autumn before, was beginning to revive a little local agitation; and when they parted, Emma observed her to be looking around with a sort of fearful curiosity, which determined her not to allow the visit to exceed the proposed quarter of an hour.", "그래도 그녀는 갔다. 농장에 이르러, 격자 울타리에 받친 사과나무들 사이로 현관까지 뻗은 널찍하고 정갈한 자갈길 끝에서 마차를 내려야 했을 때, 지난가을 그토록 큰 기쁨을 안겨 주었던 온갖 광경이 눈에 들어오자, 마음속에 잔잔한 동요가 다시 일기 시작했다."], ["She went on herself, to give that portion of time to an old servant who was married, and settled in Donwell.", "두 사람이 헤어질 때, 엠마는 해리엇이 무언가 두려움 섞인 호기심으로 주위를 둘러보는 것을 보았고, 그래서 방문을 정해 둔 십오 분을 넘기지 않게 하리라 마음먹었다. 엠마 자신은 그 시간 동안, 돈웰에 정착해 사는, 결혼한 옛 하녀를 만나러 갔다."]], [["The quarter of an hour brought her punctually to the white gate again; and Miss Smith receiving her summons, was with her without delay, and unattended by any alarming young man.", "십오 분이 지나자 엠마는 어김없이 흰 대문 앞으로 돌아왔고, 미스 스미스는 부름을 받자 지체 없이 나왔는데, 곁에는 마음을 졸이게 할 어떤 젊은 남자도 없었다."], ["She came solitarily down the gravel walk—a Miss Martin just appearing at the door, and parting with her seemingly with ceremonious civility.", "해리엇은 자갈길을 홀로 내려왔다 — 어느 미스 마틴이 문가에 막 나타나 짐짓 의례적인 정중함으로 그녀를 배웅하는 듯했다."]], [["Harriet could not very soon give an intelligible account.", "해리엇은 좀처럼 알아들을 만한 이야기를 들려주지 못했다."], ["She was feeling too much; but at last Emma collected from her enough to understand the sort of meeting, and the sort of pain it was creating.", "너무 마음이 북받쳤던 것이다. 그러나 마침내 엠마는 그 말에서, 어떤 만남이었는지, 그것이 어떤 종류의 아픔을 자아내고 있는지를 헤아릴 만큼은 추려 들었다."], ["She had seen only Mrs. Martin and the two girls.", "해리엇은 마틴 부인과 두 딸만 보았다고 했다."], ["They had received her doubtingly, if not coolly; and nothing beyond the merest commonplace had been talked almost all the time—till just at last, when Mrs. Martin’s saying, all of a sudden, that she thought Miss Smith was grown, had brought on a more interesting subject, and a warmer manner.", "그들은 그녀를 차갑게는 아니더라도 미심쩍은 태도로 맞았고, 거의 내내 그저 따분한 입에 발린 말밖에 오가지 않았다 — 마지막 순간에 이르러서야, 마틴 부인이 불쑥 미스 스미스가 부쩍 큰 것 같다고 말하면서, 한결 흥미로운 화제와 따뜻한 태도가 살아났다."], ["In that very room she had been measured last September, with her two friends.", "바로 그 방에서, 그녀는 지난 구월에 두 친구와 함께 키를 재었던 것이다."], ["There were the pencilled marks and memorandums on the wainscot by the window.", "창가의 벽판에는 연필로 그어 둔 표시와 적바림이 그대로 남아 있었다."], ["He had done it.", "그가 해 준 것이었다."], ["They all seemed to remember the day, the hour, the party, the occasion—to feel the same consciousness, the same regrets—to be ready to return to the same good understanding; and they were just growing again like themselves, (Harriet, as Emma must suspect, as ready as the best of them to be cordial and happy,) when the carriage reappeared, and all was over.", "그들 모두 그날을, 그 시각을, 그 모임을, 그 사정을 기억하는 듯했다 — 똑같은 자각, 똑같은 아쉬움을 느끼고, 예전의 그 다정한 사이로 기꺼이 되돌아갈 채비가 되어 보였다. 그리하여 다시 예전의 자기들 모습으로 돌아가던 참에 (해리엇은, 엠마도 짐작하지 않을 수 없었듯, 누구 못지않게 살갑고 즐거워질 준비가 되어 있었다), 마차가 다시 모습을 드러냈고, 모든 것이 끝나 버렸다."], ["The style of the visit, and the shortness of it, were then felt to be decisive.", "그러자 방문의 격식과 그 짧음이 결정타로 사무쳤다. 여섯 달도 아닌 여섯 주를 고맙게 함께 보낸 이들에게 겨우 십사 분이라니!"], ["Fourteen minutes to be given to those with whom she had thankfully passed six weeks not six months ago!—Emma could not but picture it all, and feel how justly they might resent, how naturally Harriet must suffer.", "— 엠마는 그 광경을 머릿속에 그려 보지 않을 수 없었고, 그들이 얼마나 당연히 서운해할 만한지, 해리엇이 얼마나 자연스레 괴로워할 수밖에 없는지를 절감했다. 딱한 노릇이었다."], ["It was a bad business.", "마틴네가 더 높은 신분이었더라면, 엠마는 무엇이든 기꺼이 내놓거나 견뎠으리라."], ["She would have given a great deal, or endured a great deal, to have had the Martins in a higher rank of life.", "그들은 그만한 자격이 충분해서, 아주 조금만 더 높았더라도 족했으련만. 그러나 지금 형편으로서야, 달리 어찌할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["They were so deserving, that a little higher should have been enough: but as it was, how could she have done otherwise?—Impossible!—She could not repent.", "— 불가능한 일이었다! — 후회할 수는 없었다."], ["They must be separated; but there was a great deal of pain in the process—so much to herself at this time, that she soon felt the necessity of a little consolation, and resolved on going home by way of Randalls to procure it.", "그들은 갈라서야만 했다. 그러나 그 과정에는 적잖은 아픔이 따랐고 — 이 순간 엠마 자신에게도 그러해서, 그녀는 곧 얼마간의 위안이 필요함을 느꼈고, 그것을 얻고자 랜덜스를 거쳐 집으로 돌아가기로 마음먹었다."], ["Her mind was quite sick of Mr. Elton and the Martins.", "그녀의 마음은 미스터 엘턴과 마틴네 일에 진저리가 나 있었다."], ["The refreshment of Randalls was absolutely necessary.", "랜덜스의 청량한 기운이 무엇보다 절실했다."]], [["It was a good scheme; but on driving to the door they heard that neither “master nor mistress was at home;”", "좋은 계획이었다. 그러나 마차를 대문 앞에 댔을 때, \"주인 내외분 모두 안 계시다\"는 말이 들려왔다."], ["they had both been out some time; the man believed they were gone to Hartfield.", "두 분 다 나가신 지 한참 되었고, 하인은 하트필드로 가신 듯하다고 했다."]], [["“This is too bad,”", "\"이런 야속한 일이 어디 있담.\""], ["cried Emma, as they turned away.", "마차를 돌리며 엠마가 외쳤다."], ["“And now we shall just miss them; too provoking!—I do not know when I have been so disappointed.”", "\"이제 우린 두 분을 영락없이 못 만나게 됐어요. 어찌나 약이 오르는지!"], ["And she leaned back in the corner, to indulge her murmurs, or to reason them away; probably a little of both—such being the commonest process of a not ill-disposed mind.", "이렇게 실망스러웠던 적이 또 있나 싶네요.\" 그러고는 마차 구석에 기대어, 투덜거림에 잠기거나 그것을 따져서 달래거나 — 아마 그 둘을 조금씩 — 했으니, 심성이 못되지 않은 사람의 마음이란 으레 이런 식으로 굴러가는 법이었다."], ["Presently the carriage stopt; she looked up; it was stopt by Mr.", "이윽고 마차가 멈췄다."], ["and Mrs. Weston, who were standing to speak to her.", "그녀가 고개를 들어 보니, 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인이 그녀에게 말을 걸려고 서 있던 탓에 멈춘 것이었다."], ["There was instant pleasure in the sight of them, and still greater pleasure was conveyed in sound—for Mr. Weston immediately accosted her with,", "두 사람을 본 것만으로도 단박에 기쁨이 일었고, 그 목소리가 들려오자 기쁨은 한층 더 커졌다 — 미스터 웨스턴이 곧장 그녀에게 말을 건넸기 때문이다."]], [["“How d’ye do?—how d’ye do?—We have been sitting with your father—glad to see him so well. Frank comes to-morrow—I had a letter this morning—we see him to-morrow by dinner-time to a certainty—he is at Oxford to-day, and he comes for a whole fortnight; I knew it would be so. If he had come at Christmas he could not have staid three days; I was always glad he did not come at Christmas; now we are going to have just the right weather for him, fine, dry, settled weather. We shall enjoy him completely; every thing has turned out exactly as we could wish.”", "\"안녕하시오? 안녕하시오? 우리는 방금 댁의 아버님과 앉아 있다 오는 길인데, 그토록 정정하신 걸 뵈니 반갑더이다. 프랭크가 내일 옵니다. 오늘 아침에 편지를 받았어요. 내일 저녁 시간이면 틀림없이 만나게 됩니다. 오늘은 옥스퍼드에 있고, 꼬박 두 주일 동안 머물 거예요. 내 이리될 줄 알았지요. 성탄절에 왔더라면 사흘도 못 묵었을 텐데, 나는 그때 안 온 게 늘 다행이라 여겼소. 이제 그 애한테 딱 맞는 날씨가 찾아올 참이니 — 화창하고, 보송보송하고, 안정된 날씨 말이오. 우리는 그 애와 함께 더없이 즐겁게 지낼 거요. 무엇 하나 바라던 대로 들어맞지 않은 게 없으니.\""]], [["There was no resisting such news, no possibility of avoiding the influence of such a happy face as Mr. Weston’s, confirmed as it all was by the words and the countenance of his wife, fewer and quieter, but not less to the purpose.", "이런 소식 앞에서는 버틸 재간이 없었고, 미스터 웨스턴처럼 행복에 겨운 얼굴이 미치는 영향을 피할 도리도 없었다 — 더구나 그 모든 것이, 비록 더 적고 더 잠잠하나 못지않게 핵심을 찌르는 그 아내의 말과 표정으로 뒷받침되고 있었으니."], ["To know that she thought his coming certain was enough to make Emma consider it so, and sincerely did she rejoice in their joy.", "웨스턴 부인이 그의 도착을 확실하다고 여긴다는 사실을 안 것만으로도, 엠마는 그것을 확실한 일로 여기기에 충분했고, 두 사람의 기쁨을 진심으로 함께 기뻐했다. 지쳐 가던 기운이 더없이 흐뭇하게 되살아났다."], ["It was a most delightful reanimation of exhausted spirits.", "닳고 닳은 과거는 다가오는 일의 신선함 속에 가라앉아 버렸다."], ["The worn-out past was sunk in the freshness of what was coming; and in the rapidity of half a moment’s thought, she hoped Mr. Elton would now be talked of no more.", "그리고 순간의 절반에 지나지 않는 생각의 빠른 흐름 속에서, 그녀는 이제 미스터 엘턴 이야기는 더 이상 입에 오르지 않기를 바랐다."]], [["Mr.", "미스터 웨스턴은 그녀에게 엔스컴에서의 약속들이 어떻게 되어 그의 아들이 꼬박 두 주일을 제 뜻대로 쓸 수 있게 되었는지, 그리고 여정의 길과 방법까지 소상히 들려주었다."], ["Weston gave her the history of the engagements at Enscombe, which allowed his son to answer for having an entire fortnight at his command, as well as the route and the method of his journey; and she listened, and smiled, and congratulated.", "그녀는 귀 기울여 듣고, 미소 짓고, 축하의 말을 건넸다."]], [["“I shall soon bring him over to Hartfield,”", "\"머잖아 그 애를 하트필드로 데려오리다.\""], ["said he, at the conclusion.", "그가 끝맺으며 말했다."]], [["Emma could imagine she saw a touch of the arm at this speech, from his wife.", "엠마는 이 말이 나오자 그의 아내가 그의 팔을 슬쩍 건드리는 것을 본 듯한 기분이 들었다."]], [["“We had better move on, Mr. Weston,”", "\"이만 가는 게 좋겠어요, 미스터 웨스턴.\""], ["said she, “we are detaining the girls.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"아가씨들을 붙잡고 있잖아요.\""]], [["“Well, well, I am ready;”—and turning again to Emma, “but you must not be expecting such a very fine young man; you have only had my account you know; I dare say he is really nothing extraordinary:”—though his own sparkling eyes at the moment were speaking a very different conviction.", "\"그래, 그래, 갈 채비는 됐소.\" 그러고는 다시 엠마를 돌아보며, \"하지만 그리 대단히 훌륭한 청년이리라 기대해선 안 됩니다. 아시다시피 내 입에서 나온 이야기만 들으셨으니까. 실은 별로 대수롭지 않은 녀석일 게요.\" — 그러나 그 순간 반짝이는 그의 두 눈은 사뭇 다른 확신을 말하고 있었다."]], [["Emma could look perfectly unconscious and innocent, and answer in a manner that appropriated nothing.", "엠마는 더없이 시치미 떼고 천연덕스러운 얼굴로, 아무것도 제 것으로 받아들이지 않는 투로 대꾸할 수 있었다."]], [["“Think of me to-morrow, my dear Emma, about four o’clock,”", "\"내일 나를 생각해 줘요, 엠마."], ["was Mrs.", "네 시쯤에요.\""], ["Weston’s parting injunction; spoken with some anxiety, and meant only for her.", "웨스턴 부인의 작별 당부였다. 얼마간 근심이 섞인 말이었고, 오로지 엠마만을 향한 것이었다."]], [["“Four o’clock!—depend upon it he will be here by three,”", "\"네 시라니! 장담컨대 그 애는 세 시면 여기 와 있을 거요.\""], ["was Mr. Weston’s quick amendment; and so ended a most satisfactory meeting.", "미스터 웨스턴이 잽싸게 고쳐 말했다."], ["Emma’s spirits were mounted quite up to happiness; every thing wore a different air; James and his horses seemed not half so sluggish as before.", "그렇게 더없이 흡족한 만남이 끝났다. 엠마의 기분은 행복이라 할 만큼 한껏 솟구쳤고, 모든 것이 사뭇 달라 보였다."], ["When she looked at the hedges, she thought the elder at least must soon be coming out; and when she turned round to Harriet, she saw something like a look of spring, a tender smile even there.", "제임스와 그의 말들조차 아까만큼 굼떠 보이지 않았다. 산울타리를 바라보자니 적어도 딱총나무는 곧 싹이 트리라는 생각이 들었고, 해리엇을 돌아보았을 때는 그녀에게서도 봄기운 같은 것, 심지어 은은한 미소까지 엿보였다."]], [["“Will Mr.", "그러나 \"미스터 프랭크 처칠은 옥스퍼드 말고 바스도 거쳐서 오나요?\""], ["Frank Churchill pass through Bath as well as Oxford?”—was a question, however, which did not augur much.", "하는 물음만큼은 그리 좋은 조짐이 못 되었다."]], [["But neither geography nor tranquillity could come all at once, and Emma was now in a humour to resolve that they should both come in time.", "그래도 지리에 밝아지는 일이든 마음의 평정이든 한꺼번에 올 수는 없는 법이었고, 엠마는 이제 그 둘 다 머잖아 오리라 마음 편히 믿을 만한 기분이었다."]], [["The morning of the interesting day arrived, and Mrs. Weston’s faithful pupil did not forget either at ten, or eleven, or twelve o’clock, that she was to think of her at four.", "그 흥미로운 날의 아침이 밝았고, 웨스턴 부인의 충실한 제자는 열 시에도, 열한 시에도, 열두 시에도 네 시에 그녀를 생각하기로 한 약속을 잊지 않았다."]], [["“My dear, dear anxious friend,”—said she, in mental soliloquy, while walking downstairs from her own room, “always overcareful for every body’s comfort but your own; I see you now in all your little fidgets, going again and again into his room, to be sure that all is right.” The clock struck twelve as she passed through the hall. “’Tis twelve; I shall not forget to think of you four hours hence; and by this time to-morrow, perhaps, or a little later, I may be thinking of the possibility of their all calling here. I am sure they will bring him soon.”", "\"내 사랑하는, 사랑하는, 근심 많은 친구.\" 자기 방에서 아래층으로 내려오며 그녀는 마음속으로 혼잣말을 했다. \"언제나 당신 자신만 빼고 누구의 편안함이든 지나치게 챙기는 분. 지금 그 자잘한 안절부절을 다 떨며, 다 잘되어 있는지 확인하려고 그 애 방을 들락날락하는 모습이 눈에 선해요.\" 그녀가 현관을 지날 때 괘종이 열두 시를 쳤다. \"열두 시구나. 네 시간 뒤에 당신을 생각하는 걸 잊지 않을게요. 그리고 내일 이맘때쯤이면, 아니 어쩌면 조금 더 늦게, 그분들이 다 함께 여기 들를 수도 있겠다는 생각을 하고 있을지도 몰라요. 분명 머잖아 그 애를 데려올 테니.\""]], [["She opened the parlour door, and saw two gentlemen sitting with her father—Mr. Weston and his son.", "그녀가 응접실 문을 열자, 두 신사가 아버지와 함께 앉아 있는 것이 보였다 — 미스터 웨스턴과 그의 아들이었다."], ["They had been arrived only a few minutes, and Mr. Weston had scarcely finished his explanation of Frank’s being a day before his time, and her father was yet in the midst of his very civil welcome and congratulations, when she appeared, to have her share of surprize, introduction, and pleasure.", "두 사람은 도착한 지 겨우 몇 분밖에 안 되었고, 미스터 웨스턴이 프랭크가 예정보다 하루 일찍 왔다는 설명을 채 마치지도, 아버지가 더없이 정중한 환영과 축하의 말 한복판에 있을 때, 엠마가 나타나 놀라움과 인사와 기쁨을 함께 나누게 되었다."]], [["The Frank Churchill so long talked of, so high in interest, was actually before her—he was presented to her, and she did not think too much had been said in his praise; he was a very good looking young man; height, air, address, all were unexceptionable, and his countenance had a great deal of the spirit and liveliness of his father’s; he looked quick and sensible. She felt immediately that she should like him; and there was a well-bred ease of manner, and a readiness to talk, which convinced her that he came intending to be acquainted with her, and that acquainted they soon must be.", "그토록 오래 이야깃거리가 되고, 그토록 높은 관심을 받아 온 프랭크 처칠이 실제로 그녀 앞에 있었다 — 그가 그녀에게 소개되었고, 그녀는 그를 칭찬한 말들이 결코 지나치지 않았다고 여겼다. 그는 정말로 잘생긴 청년이었으니, 키며 풍채며 몸가짐이 모두 나무랄 데 없었고, 그 얼굴에는 아버지의 활기와 생기가 많이 어려 있었다. 그는 기민하고 영민해 보였다. 그녀는 곧장 그가 마음에 들리라는 것을 느꼈다. 게다가 그에게는 교양 있는 사람다운 편안한 태도와 선뜻 말을 건네는 붙임성이 있어서, 그가 그녀와 친해질 작정으로 왔으며 두 사람이 머잖아 친해질 수밖에 없으리라는 확신을 안겨 주었다."]], [["He had reached Randalls the evening before.", "그는 전날 저녁에 랜덜스에 도착해 있었다."], ["She was pleased with the eagerness to arrive which had made him alter his plan, and travel earlier, later, and quicker, that he might gain half a day.", "그녀는 그가 반나절을 벌어 보려고 계획을 바꿔 더 일찍 떠나고 더 늦게까지 가고 더 빨리 달려온, 그 도착하고 싶어 한 열의가 흐뭇했다."]], [["“I told you yesterday,” cried Mr.", "\"내 어제 말하지 않았소.\""], ["Weston with exultation, “I told you all that he would be here before the time named.", "미스터 웨스턴이 의기양양하게 외쳤다. \"그 애가 정한 시간보다 먼저 올 거라고 다들한테 그랬잖소."], ["I remembered what I used to do myself.", "내가 옛날에 어떻게 했는지 떠올렸지요. 여행길에선 굼뜨게 갈 수가 없는 법이오."], ["One cannot creep upon a journey; one cannot help getting on faster than one has planned; and the pleasure of coming in upon one’s friends before the look-out begins, is worth a great deal more than any little exertion it needs.”", "계획한 것보다 자꾸 빨라지는 걸 어쩌지 못하지요. 그리고 기다림이 시작되기도 전에 친구들에게 불쑥 들이닥치는 그 기쁨은, 거기에 드는 얼마간의 수고보다 훨씬 값진 거랍니다.\""]], [["“It is a great pleasure where one can indulge in it,”", "\"누릴 수만 있다면야 큰 기쁨이지요.\" 청년이 말했다."], ["said the young man, “though there are not many houses that I should presume on so far; but in coming home I felt I might do any thing.”", "\"물론 제가 그렇게까지 멋대로 굴어도 될 집은 그리 많지 않습니다만. 그래도 우리 집으로 오는 길이니, 무엇이든 해도 되겠다 싶더군요.\""]], [["The word home made his father look on him with fresh complacency.", "우리 집이라는 그 말에 아버지는 새삼 흐뭇한 눈길로 아들을 바라보았다."], ["Emma was directly sure that he knew how to make himself agreeable; the conviction was strengthened by what followed.", "엠마는 그가 사람의 마음을 사로잡을 줄 안다는 것을 곧장 확신했고, 뒤이어 나온 말들로 그 확신은 한층 굳어졌다."], ["He was very much pleased with Randalls, thought it a most admirably arranged house, would hardly allow it even to be very small, admired the situation, the walk to Highbury, Highbury itself, Hartfield still more, and professed himself to have always felt the sort of interest in the country which none but one’s own country gives, and the greatest curiosity to visit it.", "그는 랜덜스가 무척 마음에 든다고, 더없이 훌륭하게 꾸며진 집이라고 했으며, 그곳이 자그마하다는 것조차 좀처럼 인정하려 들지 않았다. 그는 그 자리며, 하이베리로 가는 길이며, 하이베리 자체며, 더더욱 하트필드를 칭찬했고, 오직 제 고장만이 주는 그런 종류의 관심을 자기는 이 고장에 늘 품어 왔으며, 한번 와 보고 싶은 마음이 누구보다 컸노라고 공언했다."], ["That he should never have been able to indulge so amiable a feeling before, passed suspiciously through Emma’s brain; but still, if it were a falsehood, it was a pleasant one, and pleasantly handled.", "그토록 다정한 정을 여태 한 번도 누려 보지 못했다는 것은 어딘가 미심쩍게 엠마의 머리를 스쳐 지나갔다. 그래도, 설령 그것이 거짓말이라 한들, 듣기 좋은 거짓말이었고, 듣기 좋게 다루어진 거짓말이었다."], ["His manner had no air of study or exaggeration.", "그의 태도에는 꾸민 티나 과장한 티가 없었다."], ["He did really look and speak as if in a state of no common enjoyment.", "그는 정말로 여느 때 없는 즐거움에 잠긴 듯이 보이고 또 말했다."]], [["Their subjects in general were such as belong to an opening acquaintance. On his side were the inquiries,—“Was she a horsewoman?—Pleasant rides?—Pleasant walks?—Had they a large neighbourhood?—Highbury, perhaps, afforded society enough?—There were several very pretty houses in and about it.—Balls—had they balls?—Was it a musical society?”", "두 사람의 화제는 대체로 이제 막 트인 사이에 으레 오갈 법한 것들이었다. 그의 쪽에서 묻는 말들이었다 — \"승마를 하시나요? 말 타고 다니기에 좋은 길이 있습니까? 산책하기 좋은 길은요? 이웃이 넓은 편인가요? 하이베리에는 어울릴 만한 사람들이 제법 있겠지요? 동네 안팎에 꽤 어여쁜 집들이 더러 보이던데요. 무도회는요 — 무도회가 열립니까? 음악을 즐기는 사람들이 모여 있나요?\""]], [["But when satisfied on all these points, and their acquaintance proportionably advanced, he contrived to find an opportunity, while their two fathers were engaged with each other, of introducing his mother-in-law, and speaking of her with so much handsome praise, so much warm admiration, so much gratitude for the happiness she secured to his father, and her very kind reception of himself, as was an additional proof of his knowing how to please—and of his certainly thinking it worth while to try to please her.", "그러나 이 모든 것에 대한 답을 듣고 두 사람의 사이도 그만큼 진전되자, 그는 두 아버지가 서로 이야기에 빠져 있는 틈을 타 기회를 만들어, 자기 계모를 화제에 올려 그토록 멋들어진 칭찬과 그토록 따뜻한 흠모로, 또 아버지에게 안겨 준 행복과 자신을 더없이 다정하게 맞아 준 일에 대한 그토록 깊은 고마움으로 그녀를 말했으니, 이는 그가 사람을 기쁘게 할 줄 안다는, 그리고 분명 그녀의 환심을 사려 애쓸 만하다고 여긴다는 또 하나의 증거였다."], ["He did not advance a word of praise beyond what she knew to be thoroughly deserved by Mrs.", "그는 웨스턴 부인이 더할 나위 없이 받아 마땅하다고 엠마가 익히 아는 것 이상으로는 한마디의 칭찬도 보태지 않았다."], ["Weston; but, undoubtedly he could know very little of the matter.", "그러나 그가 이 일을 거의 알 리 없는 것도 분명했다."], ["He understood what would be welcome; he could be sure of little else.", "그는 무엇이 환영받을지를 알았다. 그 밖의 것은 거의 확신할 수 없었으리라."], ["“His father’s marriage,”", "\"아버지의 결혼은,\""], ["he said, “had been the wisest measure, every friend must rejoice in it; and the family from whom he had received such a blessing must be ever considered as having conferred the highest obligation on him.”", "그가 말하기를, \"더없이 현명한 처사였으며, 벗이라면 누구나 기뻐할 일이고, 그토록 큰 축복을 안겨 준 그 집안은 자기에게 더없이 큰 은혜를 베푼 것으로 길이 여겨져야 마땅하다\"는 것이었다."]], [["He got as near as he could to thanking her for Miss Taylor’s merits, without seeming quite to forget that in the common course of things it was to be rather supposed that Miss Taylor had formed Miss Woodhouse’s character, than Miss Woodhouse Miss Taylor’s.", "그는 미스 테일러의 미덕에 대해 엠마에게 고맙다고 할 수 있는 데까지 가까이 다가갔으되, 일이 으레 굴러가는 이치로 보자면 미스 우드하우스가 미스 테일러의 성품을 빚었다기보다는 미스 테일러가 미스 우드하우스의 성품을 빚었다고 보는 편이 옳으리라는 것을 아주 잊지는 않은 듯했다."], ["And at last, as if resolved to qualify his opinion completely for travelling round to its object, he wound it all up with astonishment at the youth and beauty of her person.", "그리고 마침내, 마치 제 견해를 빙 돌아 본래의 표적에 가닿게 하려고 작정한 듯, 그는 그녀의 젊음과 아름다움에 대한 놀라움으로 이 모든 말을 마무리했다."]], [["“Elegant, agreeable manners, I was prepared for,”", "\"우아하고 상냥한 태도야 진작 짐작하고 있었습니다.\""], ["said he; “but I confess that, considering every thing, I had not expected more than a very tolerably well-looking woman of a certain age; I did not know that I was to find a pretty young woman in Mrs.", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 솔직히, 이것저것 다 따져 보니, 그저 그런대로 봐줄 만한, 적잖이 나이 든 여인을 만나리라고 생각했을 뿐, 그 이상은 기대하지 않았답니다."], ["Weston.”", "웨스턴 부인에게서 어여쁜 젊은 여인을 만나게 될 줄은 몰랐어요.\""]], [["“You cannot see too much perfection in Mrs.", "\"제 마음으로야 웨스턴 부인이 아무리 완벽해 보여도 지나칠 게 없답니다.\""], ["Weston for my feelings,”", "엠마가 말했다."], ["said Emma; “were you to guess her to be eighteen, I should listen with pleasure; but she would be ready to quarrel with you for using such words.", "\"그분을 열여덟 살이라 짐작하셨다 해도 저는 흐뭇하게 들었을 거예요. 하지만 그분 본인은 그런 말을 쓰셨다고 당신과 한바탕 다투려 들걸요."], ["Don’t let her imagine that you have spoken of her as a pretty young woman.”", "그분을 어여쁜 젊은 여인이라 말했다는 걸 눈치채게 하진 마세요.\""]], [["“I hope I should know better,”", "\"저도 그쯤은 분별할 줄 안답니다.\" 그가 답했다."], ["he replied; “no, depend upon it, (with a gallant bow,) that in addressing Mrs. Weston I should understand whom I might praise without any danger of being thought extravagant in my terms.”", "\"아니요, 장담컨대 (정중하게 허리를 굽히며), 웨스턴 부인께 말씀드릴 때라면, 제가 표현이 지나치다는 소리를 들을 염려 없이 누구를 칭찬해도 좋을지쯤은 알아챌 겁니다.\""]], [["Emma wondered whether the same suspicion of what might be expected from their knowing each other, which had taken strong possession of her mind, had ever crossed his; and whether his compliments were to be considered as marks of acquiescence, or proofs of defiance.", "엠마는, 두 사람이 알고 지내면 무슨 일이 벌어질지에 대한 — 자기 마음을 단단히 사로잡은 그 똑같은 짐작이 — 그의 머릿속에도 한 번이라도 스쳐 지나갔을까 궁금했다. 그리고 그의 그 입에 발린 칭찬들을, 순순히 받아들인다는 표시로 봐야 할지, 아니면 거역한다는 증거로 봐야 할지도."], ["She must see more of him to understand his ways; at present she only felt they were agreeable.", "그의 됨됨이를 헤아리려면 그를 더 겪어 봐야 했다. 지금으로서는 그저 그 칭찬들이 듣기 좋다는 것만 느껴질 뿐이었다."]], [["She had no doubt of what Mr. Weston was often thinking about.", "미스터 웨스턴이 무슨 생각에 잠겨 있는지는 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["His quick eye she detected again and again glancing towards them with a happy expression; and even, when he might have determined not to look, she was confident that he was often listening.", "그의 잰 눈길이 행복한 표정으로 두 사람 쪽을 흘끔거리는 것을 그녀는 거듭 잡아챘다. 그리고 그가 보지 않기로 작정했을 법한 순간조차, 그녀는 그가 종종 귀를 기울이고 있으리라 확신했다."]], [["Her own father’s perfect exemption from any thought of the kind, the entire deficiency in him of all such sort of penetration or suspicion, was a most comfortable circumstance.", "그런 종류의 생각이라곤 아예 미치지 않는 제 아버지, 그런 통찰이나 의심이라곤 도무지 없는 그 점은 더없이 마음 놓이는 일이었다."], ["Happily he was not farther from approving matrimony than from foreseeing it.—Though always objecting to every marriage that was arranged, he never suffered beforehand from the apprehension of any; it seemed as if he could not think so ill of any two persons’ understanding as to suppose they meant to marry till it were proved against them.", "다행히도 그는 혼인을 미리 내다보지 못하는 만큼이나 그것을 찬성하지도 못하는 사람이었다 — 짜여 가는 혼사라면 무엇이든 늘 못마땅해하면서도, 그런 일이 있을까 미리 마음 졸인 적은 한 번도 없었다. 마치 그는, 어떤 두 사람이 혼인할 작정이라는 것이 증거로 들이닥치기 전까지는, 그들이 그토록 분별없을 리 없다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["She blessed the favouring blindness.", "엠마는 이 다행스러운 눈멂에 감사했다."], ["He could now, without the drawback of a single unpleasant surmise, without a glance forward at any possible treachery in his guest, give way to all his natural kind-hearted civility in solicitous inquiries after Mr. Frank Churchill’s accommodation on his journey, through the sad evils of sleeping two nights on the road, and express very genuine unmixed anxiety to know that he had certainly escaped catching cold—which, however, he could not allow him to feel quite assured of himself till after another night.", "이제 아버지는, 단 하나의 언짢은 짐작이라는 걸림돌도 없이, 손님이 품었을 법한 어떤 배신도 미리 내다봄 없이, 타고난 마음씨 고운 정중함을 마음껏 베풀어, 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 여정에서 묵을 잠자리를 두고 — 길에서 이틀 밤이나 자야 했던 그 딱한 곤욕을 두고 — 살뜰히 안부를 물을 수 있었고, 그가 감기에 걸리지 않은 게 정녕 확실한지 알고 싶다는, 더없이 진솔하고 순수한 근심을 드러낼 수 있었다 — 다만 그것도, 하룻밤을 더 자고 나기 전까지는 청년 자신더러 마음 푹 놓아도 좋다고는 허락하지 못했지만."]], [["A reasonable visit paid, Mr. Weston began to move.—“He must be going.", "적당한 만큼 방문을 하고 나자, 미스터 웨스턴이 일어설 채비를 했다."], ["He had business at the Crown about his hay, and a great many errands for Mrs.", "\"이만 가 봐야겠다. 크라운 여관에 건초 일로 볼일이 있고, 포드 상점에서 웨스턴 부인 심부름도 여러 가지 있다."], ["Weston at Ford’s, but he need not hurry any body else.”", "그렇다고 누구든 덩달아 서두를 건 없다\"는 것이었다."], ["His son, too well bred to hear the hint, rose immediately also, saying,", "그 아들은 이 넌지시 던지는 말귀를 못 알아들을 만큼 교양 없지 않아, 곧바로 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“As you are going farther on business, sir, I will take the opportunity of paying a visit, which must be paid some day or other, and therefore may as well be paid now.", "\"아버님은 더 멀리 볼일이 있어 가시니, 저는 이참에 한 번은 해야 할, 그러니 지금 해 두는 게 나을 방문을 다녀오겠습니다."], ["I have the honour of being acquainted with a neighbour of yours, (turning to Emma,) a lady residing in or near Highbury; a family of the name of Fairfax.", "제게는 댁의 어느 이웃과 아는 사이라는 영광이 있는데요 (엠마를 돌아보며), 하이베리 안에, 혹은 그 근처에 사시는 어느 숙녀분입니다. 페어팩스라는 성을 쓰는 집안이지요."], ["I shall have no difficulty, I suppose, in finding the house; though Fairfax, I believe, is not the proper name—I should rather say Barnes, or Bates.", "집을 찾는 데야 어렵지 않으리라 봅니다만 — 정작 페어팩스는 본댁 성이 아닌 듯합니다. 차라리 반스나 베이츠라 해야겠지요."], ["Do you know any family of that name?”", "그런 성을 쓰는 집안을 아시는지요?\""]], [["“To be sure we do,”", "\"알다마다.\" 그의 아버지가 외쳤다."], ["cried his father; “Mrs.", "\"베이츠 부인 말이지."], ["Bates—we passed her house—I saw Miss Bates at the window.", "우리가 그 댁을 지나쳐 왔잖니. 미스 베이츠가 창가에 있는 걸 봤단다."], ["True, true, you are acquainted with Miss Fairfax; I remember you knew her at Weymouth, and a fine girl she is.", "옳지, 옳아, 너는 미스 페어팩스와 아는 사이지. 웨이머스에서 알고 지냈던 게 기억나는구나."], ["Call upon her, by all means.”", "참한 아가씨야. 꼭 들러 보거라.\""]], [["“There is no necessity for my calling this morning,”", "\"오늘 아침에 굳이 들러야 할 까닭은 없습니다.\" 청년이 말했다."], ["said the young man; “another day would do as well; but there was that degree of acquaintance at Weymouth which—”", "\"다른 날에 가도 마찬가지일 테니까요. 다만 웨이머스에서 그 정도로 알고 지낸 사이이긴 했는데—\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["go to-day, go to-day.", "오늘 가거라, 오늘 가."], ["Do not defer it.", "미루지 마."], ["What is right to be done cannot be done too soon.", "마땅히 해야 할 일이라면 아무리 빨리 해도 지나치지 않은 법이야."], ["And, besides, I must give you a hint, Frank; any want of attention to her here should be carefully avoided.", "게다가 한마디 일러두마, 프랭크. 여기서 그 아가씨에게 소홀한 기색을 보이는 일만큼은 부디 삼가야 한다."], ["You saw her with the Campbells, when she was the equal of every body she mixed with, but here she is with a poor old grandmother, who has barely enough to live on.", "너는 캠벨네 사람들과 함께 있던 그 아가씨를, 어울리는 누구와도 대등하던 그 아가씨를 보았지만, 여기서는 겨우 입에 풀칠할 형편의 가엾고 늙은 할머니와 함께 지낸단다."], ["If you do not call early it will be a slight.”", "일찍 들르지 않으면 무시하는 셈이 될 게야.\""]], [["The son looked convinced.", "아들은 수긍하는 낯빛이었다."]], [["“I have heard her speak of the acquaintance,”", "\"그 아가씨가 그 인연을 이야기하는 걸 들은 적이 있어요.\""], ["said Emma; “she is a very elegant young woman.”", "엠마가 말했다. \"아주 우아한 아가씨더군요.\""]], [["He agreed to it, but with so quiet a “Yes,”", "그는 그 말에 동의하긴 했으나, 워낙 잠잠한 \"네\""], ["as inclined her almost to doubt his real concurrence; and yet there must be a very distinct sort of elegance for the fashionable world, if Jane Fairfax could be thought only ordinarily gifted with it.", "한마디였던지라, 그녀는 그가 정말로 동의하는지 거의 의심이 들 지경이었다. 그러나 제인 페어팩스가 그것을 그저 평범하게 타고났다고 여겨질 정도라면, 사교계가 말하는 우아함이란 분명 사뭇 별스러운 종류일 터였다."]], [["“If you were never particularly struck by her manners before,”", "\"전에 그 아가씨의 태도에 별달리 마음이 끌린 적이 없으셨다면,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “I think you will to-day.", "\"오늘은 그러시리라 봐요. 그 아가씨를 제대로 보게 되실 거예요."], ["You will see her to advantage; see her and hear her—no, I am afraid you will not hear her at all, for she has an aunt who never holds her tongue.”", "보고 또 듣게 되시겠지요 — 아니, 듣기는 아마 도무지 못 하실 거예요. 한시도 입을 다물지 않는 이모님이 계시거든요.\""]], [["“You are acquainted with Miss Jane Fairfax, sir, are you?”", "\"미스 제인 페어팩스와 아는 사이시군요?\" 언제나 대화에 가장 늦게 끼어드는 미스터 우드하우스가 말했다."], ["said Mr.", "\"그럼 한마디 장담해 드리리다."], ["Woodhouse, always the last to make his way in conversation; “then give me leave to assure you that you will find her a very agreeable young lady.", "그 아가씨가 더없이 상냥한 처녀라는 걸 알게 되실 거요."], ["She is staying here on a visit to her grandmama and aunt, very worthy people; I have known them all my life.", "지금 할머니와 이모를 뵈러 와 묵고 있는데, 다들 무척 훌륭한 분들이오. 나는 그분들을 평생 알고 지냈소."], ["They will be extremely glad to see you, I am sure; and one of my servants shall go with you to shew you the way.”", "분명 당신을 보면 몹시 반가워하실 게요. 그리고 우리 집 하인 하나를 딸려 보내 길을 안내해 드리리다.\""]], [["“My dear sir, upon no account in the world; my father can direct me.”", "\"어르신, 천만의 말씀입니다. 아버지가 일러 주시면 됩니다.\""]], [["“But your father is not going so far; he is only going to the Crown, quite on the other side of the street, and there are a great many houses; you might be very much at a loss, and it is a very dirty walk, unless you keep on the footpath; but my coachman can tell you where you had best cross the street.”", "\"하지만 자네 아버님은 그리 멀리 가시지 않잖소. 그분은 길 건너 저편 크라운 여관까지만 가시는데, 집이 워낙 많아 자네가 길을 못 찾고 헤맬 수도 있다오. 게다가 큰길로 다니지 않으면 길이 몹시 질척하지. 어쨌든 어디서 길을 건너는 게 제일 좋을지는 우리 집 마부가 일러 줄 수 있소.\""]], [["Mr. Frank Churchill still declined it, looking as serious as he could, and his father gave his hearty support by calling out, “My good friend, this is quite unnecessary; Frank knows a puddle of water when he sees it, and as to Mrs.", "미스터 프랭크 처칠은 그래도 한사코 사양하며 짐짓 더없이 진지한 낯빛을 지었고, 그의 아버지는 \"여보게, 그건 전혀 그럴 필요가 없소."], ["Bates’s, he may get there from the Crown in a hop, step, and jump.”", "프랭크는 물웅덩이쯤은 보면 알아채는 녀석이고, 베이츠 부인 댁이라면 크라운에서 깡총 한 번 뛰어 닿을 거리요\" 하고 큰 소리로 거들며 든든히 편들어 주었다."]], [["They were permitted to go alone; and with a cordial nod from one, and a graceful bow from the other, the two gentlemen took leave.", "두 사람은 단둘이 가도 좋다는 허락을 받았다. 그리하여 한쪽은 다정하게 고개를 끄덕이고, 다른 한쪽은 우아하게 허리를 굽혀, 두 신사가 작별을 고했다."], ["Emma remained very well pleased with this beginning of the acquaintance, and could now engage to think of them all at Randalls any hour of the day, with full confidence in their comfort.", "엠마는 이렇게 시작된 사귐에 무척 흡족한 채로 남았고, 이제 하루 중 어느 때든 랜덜스에 있는 그들 모두를, 그들이 편안히 잘 지내리라는 온전한 믿음을 품고 떠올릴 수 있게 되었다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter VI", "# 제2권 6장"]], [["The next morning brought Mr. Frank Churchill again.", "이튿날 아침 프랭크 처칠이 다시 찾아왔다."], ["He came with Mrs. Weston, to whom and to Highbury he seemed to take very cordially.", "그는 웨스턴 부인과 함께 왔는데, 부인에게도 하이베리에도 무척 다정하게 마음을 붙인 듯했다."], ["He had been sitting with her, it appeared, most companionably at home, till her usual hour of exercise; and on being desired to chuse their walk, immediately fixed on Highbury.—“He did not doubt there being very pleasant walks in every direction, but if left to him, he should always chuse the same.", "보아하니 그는 부인이 늘 산책을 나서는 시각이 될 때까지 집에서 더없이 정답게 함께 앉아 있었던 모양이었다. 그리고 어디로 걸을지 골라 보라는 청을 받자 곧장 하이베리로 정했다."], ["Highbury, that airy, cheerful, happy-looking Highbury, would be his constant attraction.”—Highbury, with Mrs.", "\"어느 방향이든 아주 즐거운 산책길이 있으리라는 건 의심하지 않습니다. 하지만 제게 맡겨 주신다면 저는 늘 같은 곳을 고를 겁니다."], ["Weston, stood for Hartfield; and she trusted to its bearing the same construction with him.", "하이베리, 그 탁 트이고 명랑하고 행복해 보이는 하이베리야말로 변함없이 저를 끌어당기는 곳이지요.\" 웨스턴 부인에게 하이베리란 곧 하트필드를 뜻했고, 그에게도 같은 뜻으로 해석되기를 부인은 믿었다."], ["They walked thither directly.", "두 사람은 곧장 그리로 걸어갔다."]], [["Emma had hardly expected them: for Mr.", "엠마는 그들이 오리라고는 거의 예상하지 못했다."], ["Weston, who had called in for half a minute, in order to hear that his son was very handsome, knew nothing of their plans; and it was an agreeable surprize to her, therefore, to perceive them walking up to the house together, arm in arm.", "아들이 아주 잘생겼다는 말을 듣겠다고 반 분쯤 들렀다 간 미스터 웨스턴은 그들의 계획을 전혀 몰랐기 때문이었다. 그래서 두 사람이 팔짱을 끼고 함께 집으로 걸어 올라오는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀에게는 반가운 놀라움이었다."], ["She was wanting to see him again, and especially to see him in company with Mrs.", "그녀는 그를 다시 보고 싶었고, 특히 웨스턴 부인과 함께 있는 모습을 보고 싶었다."], ["Weston, upon his behaviour to whom her opinion of him was to depend.", "부인을 대하는 그의 태도에 따라 그에 대한 그녀의 평가가 정해질 참이었기 때문이다."], ["If he were deficient there, nothing should make amends for it.", "그 점에서 모자람이 있다면, 어떤 것으로도 그것을 메울 수 없을 터였다."], ["But on seeing them together, she became perfectly satisfied.", "그러나 두 사람이 함께 있는 모습을 보고 그녀는 완전히 만족했다."], ["It was not merely in fine words or hyperbolical compliment that he paid his duty; nothing could be more proper or pleasing than his whole manner to her—nothing could more agreeably denote his wish of considering her as a friend and securing her affection.", "그가 도리를 다하는 것은 그저 고운 말이나 과장된 칭찬에 그치지 않았다. 부인을 대하는 그의 태도 전체보다 더 알맞고 흐뭇한 것은 없었으며, 부인을 벗으로 여기고 그 애정을 얻고자 하는 마음을 그보다 더 기껍게 드러내는 것은 없었다."], ["And there was time enough for Emma to form a reasonable judgment, as their visit included all the rest of the morning.", "게다가 그들의 방문이 그날 오전 나머지 시간을 모두 채웠으므로, 엠마가 합당한 판단을 내릴 시간은 넉넉했다."], ["They were all three walking about together for an hour or two—first round the shrubberies of Hartfield, and afterwards in Highbury.", "셋이서 한두 시간을 함께 거닐었다 — 처음에는 하트필드의 관목 숲을 한 바퀴 돌고, 그다음에는 하이베리에서."], ["He was delighted with every thing; admired Hartfield sufficiently for Mr.", "그는 무엇에든 기뻐했고, 미스터 우드하우스의 귀에 들릴 만큼 하트필드를 흡족하게 칭송했다."], ["Woodhouse’s ear; and when their going farther was resolved on, confessed his wish to be made acquainted with the whole village, and found matter of commendation and interest much oftener than Emma could have supposed.", "그리고 더 멀리 가 보기로 정해지자, 마을 전체를 알고 싶다는 바람을 털어놓으며, 엠마가 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 자주 칭찬하고 흥미를 보일 거리를 찾아냈다."]], [["Some of the objects of his curiosity spoke very amiable feelings.", "그의 호기심을 끄는 것들 가운데 더러는 아주 갸륵한 마음씨를 드러냈다. 그는 아버지가 그토록 오래 살았고 또 할아버지의 집이기도 했던 그 집을 보여 달라고 청했다."], ["He begged to be shewn the house which his father had lived in so long, and which had been the home of his father’s father; and on recollecting that an old woman who had nursed him was still living, walked in quest of her cottage from one end of the street to the other; and though in some points of pursuit or observation there was no positive merit, they shewed, altogether, a good-will towards Highbury in general, which must be very like a merit to those he was with.", "그리고 자기를 길러 준 늙은 유모가 아직 살아 있다는 것을 떠올리고는, 그 오두막을 찾아 거리 이 끝에서 저 끝까지 걸었다. 비록 어떤 추구나 관찰의 대목에서는 딱히 칭찬할 만한 점이 없기도 했지만, 그것들은 통틀어 하이베리 전반에 대한 호의를 보여 주었으니, 함께 있는 사람들에게는 그것이 분명 칭찬할 만한 점과 매우 비슷하게 느껴질 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Emma watched and decided, that with such feelings as were now shewn, it could not be fairly supposed that he had been ever voluntarily absenting himself; that he had not been acting a part, or making a parade of insincere professions; and that Mr.", "엠마는 지켜보며 이렇게 단정했다."], ["Knightley certainly had not done him justice.", "지금 드러난 것과 같은 마음씨를 지닌 사람이라면, 그가 일부러 발길을 끊고 멀리 떨어져 있었다고는 도저히 볼 수 없으며, 어떤 역할을 꾸미거나 진심 없는 다짐을 늘어놓으며 자랑한 것도 아니었고, 미스터 나이틀리가 그를 두고 한 평가는 분명 공정하지 못했다고."]], [["Their first pause was at the Crown Inn, an inconsiderable house, though the principal one of the sort, where a couple of pair of post-horses were kept, more for the convenience of the neighbourhood than from any run on the road; and his companions had not expected to be detained by any interest excited there; but in passing it they gave the history of the large room visibly added; it had been built many years ago for a ball-room, and while the neighbourhood had been in a particularly populous, dancing state, had been occasionally used as such;—but such brilliant days had long passed away, and now the highest purpose for which it was ever wanted was to accommodate a whist club established among the gentlemen and half-gentlemen of the place.", "그들이 처음 발을 멈춘 곳은 크라운 여관이었다. 그런 종류로는 가장 으뜸가는 곳이긴 했으나 보잘것없는 집이었고, 역마 두어 쌍을 매어 두었는데 이는 길에 손님이 몰려서라기보다 이웃의 편의를 위해서였다."], ["He was immediately interested.", "그의 일행은 그곳에서 무슨 흥미가 일어 발이 묶이리라고는 예상하지 못했다. 그러나 지나치다가 그들은 보기에도 나중에 덧붙인 그 큰 방의 내력을 들려주었다."], ["Its character as a ball-room caught him; and instead of passing on, he stopt for several minutes at the two superior sashed windows which were open, to look in and contemplate its capabilities, and lament that its original purpose should have ceased.", "여러 해 전 무도회장으로 지어진 그 방은, 이 일대가 유난히 사람도 많고 춤판이 무르익던 시절에는 이따금 그렇게 쓰이기도 했었다. 그러나 그토록 빛나던 날들은 오래전에 지나가 버렸고, 이제 그 방이 쓰일 일이라곤 고작 이곳의 신사들과 어중간한 신사들 사이에 만들어진 휘스트 모임을 들여놓는 것이 가장 높은 쓰임새였다."], ["He saw no fault in the room, he would acknowledge none which they suggested.", "그는 곧바로 흥미를 보였다. 무도회장이라는 그 내력이 그의 마음을 사로잡았던 것이다."], ["No, it was long enough, broad enough, handsome enough. It would hold the very number for comfort.", "그리고 그냥 지나치는 대신, 그는 활짝 열려 있는 위쪽의 훌륭한 미닫이창 두 짝 앞에 몇 분이나 멈춰 서서, 안을 들여다보며 그곳의 가능성을 가늠하고, 본래의 쓰임새가 끊겨 버린 것을 안타까워했다."], ["They ought to have balls there at least every fortnight through the winter.", "그는 그 방에서 아무런 흠도 보지 못했고, 그들이 일러 주는 흠도 하나 인정하려 들지 않았다. 아니, 길이도 넉넉하고 폭도 넉넉하고 보기에도 넉넉히 훌륭하다고."], ["Why had not Miss Woodhouse revived the former good old days of the room?—She who could do any thing in Highbury!", "안락하게 즐기기에 딱 알맞은 인원을 들일 수 있을 거라고. 적어도 겨울 내내 이 주에 한 번씩은 여기서 무도회를 열어야 마땅하다고."], ["The want of proper families in the place, and the conviction that none beyond the place and its immediate environs could be tempted to attend, were mentioned; but he was not satisfied.", "어째서 미스 우드하우스가 이 방의 옛 좋은 시절을 되살리지 않았느냐고 — 하이베리에서라면 무엇이든 할 수 있는 그녀가! 이곳에 마땅한 가문이 부족하다는 것, 그리고 이곳과 그 바로 인근을 넘어서는 누구도 참석하도록 꾈 수 없으리라는 확신을 그들은 일러 주었다."], ["He could not be persuaded that so many good-looking houses as he saw around him, could not furnish numbers enough for such a meeting; and even when particulars were given and families described, he was still unwilling to admit that the inconvenience of such a mixture would be any thing, or that there would be the smallest difficulty in every body’s returning into their proper place the next morning.", "그러나 그는 수긍하지 않았다. 주위에 보이는 이렇게 번듯한 집들이 그만한 모임에 채울 인원도 못 댄다는 것을 그는 도무지 믿을 수 없었다."], ["He argued like a young man very much bent on dancing; and Emma was rather surprized to see the constitution of the Weston prevail so decidedly against the habits of the Churchills.", "그리고 구체적인 내막이 제시되고 가문들이 설명된 뒤에도, 그는 그렇게 뒤섞이는 불편이 무슨 대수일까 보냐고, 이튿날 아침이면 누구나 제자리로 돌아가는 데 조금의 어려움도 없으리라고, 여전히 인정하려 들지 않았다. 그는 춤을 추고 싶어 마음이 잔뜩 기운 젊은이답게 우겨 댔다."], ["He seemed to have all the life and spirit, cheerful feelings, and social inclinations of his father, and nothing of the pride or reserve of Enscombe.", "그리고 엠마는 처칠 집안의 습성을 누르고 웨스턴 집안의 기질이 그토록 또렷이 이기는 것을 보고 적잖이 놀랐다. 그는 아버지의 온갖 생기와 활기, 명랑한 기분과 사교적인 성향을 다 지닌 듯했고, 엔스컴의 거만함이나 새침함은 조금도 없었다."], ["Of pride, indeed, there was, perhaps, scarcely enough; his indifference to a confusion of rank, bordered too much on inelegance of mind.", "거만함으로 말하자면, 실은 어쩌면 모자랄 지경이었다. 신분의 뒤섞임에 무심한 그 태도는 마음의 품위 없음에 너무 가까웠다."], ["He could be no judge, however, of the evil he was holding cheap.", "그러나 그는 자기가 가볍게 여기는 그 폐단을 헤아릴 처지가 못 되었다. 그것은 그저 발랄한 기운의 분출일 뿐이었다."], ["It was but an effusion of lively spirits.", "* 휘스트(whist): 네 사람이 두 편으로 나뉘어 하는 카드놀이로, 19세기 영국 사교계에서 크게 유행했다."]], [["At last he was persuaded to move on from the front of the Crown; and being now almost facing the house where the Bateses lodged, Emma recollected his intended visit the day before, and asked him if he had paid it.", "마침내 그는 설득에 못 이겨 크라운 여관 앞을 떠났다. 그리고 이제 베이츠 모녀가 세 들어 사는 집과 거의 마주 보게 되자, 엠마는 그가 어제 방문하기로 했던 것을 떠올리고는, 다녀왔는지 물었다."]], [["“Yes, oh!", "\"네, 아! 네.\""], ["yes”—he replied; “I was just going to mention it.", "그가 대답했다. \"마침 그 이야기를 하려던 참이었습니다."], ["A very successful visit:—I saw all the three ladies; and felt very much obliged to you for your preparatory hint.", "아주 성공적인 방문이었어요. 세 분 다 뵈었지요."], ["If the talking aunt had taken me quite by surprize, it must have been the death of me.", "그리고 미리 귀띔해 주신 덕에 무척 신세를 졌습니다. 그 말 많은 이모님이 저를 전혀 예상치 못한 틈에 덮쳤더라면, 저는 영락없이 죽었을 겁니다."], ["As it was, I was only betrayed into paying a most unreasonable visit.", "그래도 사정이 그러했으니, 저는 그저 더없이 당치 않게 긴 방문을 하고 만 것뿐이지요. 십 분이면 다 됐을 텐데요, 어쩌면 그게 적당하기도 했을 거고요."], ["Ten minutes would have been all that was necessary, perhaps all that was proper; and I had told my father I should certainly be at home before him—but there was no getting away, no pause; and, to my utter astonishment, I found, when he (finding me nowhere else) joined me there at last, that I had been actually sitting with them very nearly three-quarters of an hour.", "아버지께는 분명 아버지보다 먼저 집에 가 있겠다고 말씀드렸는데 — 도무지 빠져나올 수가 없었어요, 틈이라곤 없었지요. 그러다 (다른 데서 저를 못 찾으신) 아버지가 마침내 그리로 합류하셨을 때, 저는 제가 거의 사십오 분이나 그분들과 앉아 있었다는 걸 알고 그야말로 깜짝 놀랐답니다."], ["The good lady had not given me the possibility of escape before.”", "그 인자하신 부인께서 그 전에는 빠져나갈 틈을 도무지 안 주셨거든요.\""]], [["“And how did you think Miss Fairfax looking?”", "\"그래서 미스 페어팩스는 어때 보이던가요?\""]], [["“Ill, very ill—that is, if a young lady can ever be allowed to look ill.", "\"안색이 나쁘던데요, 아주 나빴어요 — 그러니까, 젊은 아가씨가 안색이 나쁘다고 해도 된다면 말입니다."], ["But the expression is hardly admissible, Mrs. Weston, is it?", "하지만 그런 표현은 좀처럼 쓸 수 없는 거지요, 웨스턴 부인, 그렇잖아요?"], ["Ladies can never look ill.", "숙녀는 결코 안색이 나쁠 수 없으니까요."], ["And, seriously, Miss Fairfax is naturally so pale, as almost always to give the appearance of ill health.—A most deplorable want of complexion.”", "그리고 진지하게 말하자면, 미스 페어팩스는 천성이 워낙 창백해서 거의 늘 건강이 좋지 않은 듯한 인상을 줍니다. 도무지 한심할 만큼 혈색이 없다고나 할까요.\""]], [["Emma would not agree to this, and began a warm defence of Miss Fairfax’s complexion.", "엠마는 이에 동의하지 않고, 미스 페어팩스의 혈색을 두둔하는 열변을 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["“It was certainly never brilliant, but she would not allow it to have a sickly hue in general; and there was a softness and delicacy in her skin which gave peculiar elegance to the character of her face.”", "\"물론 결코 화사한 적은 없었지만, 평소에 병색이 도는 빛깔이라고는 인정할 수 없어요. 그리고 그 피부에는 부드럽고 섬세한 데가 있어서, 그녀 얼굴의 분위기에 남다른 우아함을 더해 주죠.\""], ["He listened with all due deference; acknowledged that he had heard many people say the same—but yet he must confess, that to him nothing could make amends for the want of the fine glow of health.", "그는 마땅한 경의를 갖추어 귀를 기울였고, 똑같은 말을 하는 사람을 여럿 들어 보았다고 인정했다. 그러나 그래도 솔직히 말하자면, 자기로서는 건강한 혈색의 그 고운 광채가 없는 것을 무엇으로도 메울 수 없다고 했다."], ["Where features were indifferent, a fine complexion gave beauty to them all; and where they were good, the effect was—fortunately he need not attempt to describe what the effect was.", "이목구비가 평범한 경우에는 고운 혈색이 그 전부에 아름다움을 더해 주고, 이목구비가 훌륭한 경우에는 그 효과가 — 다행히 그는 그 효과가 무엇인지 굳이 그려 보일 필요가 없었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"뭐,\" 엠마가 말했다."], ["said Emma, “there is no disputing about taste.—At least you admire her except her complexion.”", "\"취향은 따질 게 못 되죠. 적어도 혈색만 빼면 그녀를 흠모하시는 거네요.\""]], [["He shook his head and laughed.—“I cannot separate Miss Fairfax and her complexion.”", "그는 고개를 가로저으며 웃었다. \"저는 미스 페어팩스와 그 혈색을 따로 떼어 놓을 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“Did you see her often at Weymouth?", "\"웨이머스에서 자주 보셨어요?"], ["Were you often in the same society?”", "같은 모임에 자주 어울리셨나요?\""]], [["At this moment they were approaching Ford’s, and he hastily exclaimed, “Ha!", "이때 그들은 마침 포드 상점에 다가가고 있었는데, 그가 황급히 외쳤다."], ["this must be the very shop that every body attends every day of their lives, as my father informs me.", "\"하! 이게 바로 아버지 말씀에 따르면 누구나 날마다 평생토록 드나든다는 그 가게로군요."], ["He comes to Highbury himself, he says, six days out of the seven, and has always business at Ford’s.", "아버지 본인도 이레 가운데 엿새는 하이베리에 오시는데, 늘 포드에 볼일이 있다고 하시더군요."], ["If it be not inconvenient to you, pray let us go in, that I may prove myself to belong to the place, to be a true citizen of Highbury.", "폐가 안 되신다면, 부디 들어가 보지요. 제가 이곳 사람이라는 것을, 진짜 하이베리 시민이라는 것을 증명할 수 있게요."], ["I must buy something at Ford’s.", "포드에서 뭔가 사야겠어요."], ["It will be taking out my freedom.—I dare say they sell gloves.”", "그게 곧 제 시민권을 얻는 일이 될 테니까요. 장갑쯤은 팔겠지요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["yes, gloves and every thing.", "그럼요, 장갑이며 뭐며 다 있어요."], ["I do admire your patriotism.", "그 애향심이 정말 감탄스럽네요."], ["You will be adored in Highbury.", "하이베리에서 사랑받으실 거예요."], ["You were very popular before you came, because you were Mr.", "오시기 전부터도 인기가 대단하셨죠, 미스터 웨스턴의 아드님이니까요."], ["Weston’s son—but lay out half a guinea at Ford’s, and your popularity will stand upon your own virtues.”", "하지만 포드에서 반 기니만 쓰시면, 그 인기가 당신 자신의 미덕 위에 서게 될 거랍니다.\""]], [["They went in; and while the sleek, well-tied parcels of “Men’s Beavers”", "그들이 들어섰다."], ["and “York Tan”", "그리고 \"남성용 비버 모자\"며 \"요크 무두가죽\""], ["were bringing down and displaying on the counter, he said—“But I beg your pardon, Miss Woodhouse, you were speaking to me, you were saying something at the very moment of this burst of my amor patriae.", "따위의 매끈하게 잘 묶인 꾸러미들이 내려져 진열대 위에 펼쳐지는 동안, 그가 말했다. \"그런데 죄송합니다, 미스 우드하우스, 제게 무슨 말씀을 하고 계셨지요."], ["Do not let me lose it.", "제 애국심의 발작이 터지던 바로 그 순간에 뭔가 말씀하시던 참이었는데."], ["I assure you the utmost stretch of public fame would not make me amends for the loss of any happiness in private life.”", "그 말씀을 놓치게 하지 말아 주십시오. 장담컨대 세상의 명성을 아무리 크게 떨친다 한들, 사사로운 삶의 행복을 하나라도 잃는 것을 메워 주지는 못할 테니까요.\""]], [["“I merely asked, whether you had known much of Miss Fairfax and her party at Weymouth.”", "\"그저 여쭤본 거예요. 웨이머스에서 미스 페어팩스와 그 일행을 많이 알고 지내셨는지요.\""]], [["“And now that I understand your question, I must pronounce it to be a very unfair one.", "\"이제야 그 물음의 뜻을 알겠는데, 아주 불공정한 물음이라고 말씀드려야겠군요."], ["It is always the lady’s right to decide on the degree of acquaintance.", "알고 지낸 정도를 결정하는 건 언제나 숙녀 쪽의 권리지요."], ["Miss Fairfax must already have given her account.—I shall not commit myself by claiming more than she may chuse to allow.”", "미스 페어팩스가 이미 자기 나름의 이야기를 했을 게 분명한데요. 그녀가 인정하기로 한 것보다 더 많이 주장해서 제 입장을 곤란하게 만들지는 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“Upon my word!", "\"세상에!"], ["you answer as discreetly as she could do herself.", "그 사람 못지않게 입이 무거우시네요."], ["But her account of every thing leaves so much to be guessed, she is so very reserved, so very unwilling to give the least information about any body, that I really think you may say what you like of your acquaintance with her.”", "하지만 그녀의 이야기는 무엇에 대해서든 짐작에 맡길 여지가 너무 많아요. 워낙 입이 무겁고, 누구에 대해서든 조금의 정보도 주기를 한사코 꺼리니, 정말이지 그녀와 알고 지낸 일이라면 무어든 마음대로 말씀하셔도 되겠다 싶을 정도예요.\""]], [["“May I, indeed?—Then I will speak the truth, and nothing suits me so well.", "\"정말 그래도 되겠습니까? 그렇다면 진실을 말하지요, 그만큼 제게 잘 맞는 것도 없으니까요."], ["I met her frequently at Weymouth.", "저는 웨이머스에서 그녀를 자주 만났습니다."], ["I had known the Campbells a little in town; and at Weymouth we were very much in the same set.", "캠벨 가족과는 런던에서 조금 알고 지냈고, 웨이머스에서는 같은 무리에 아주 자주 어울렸지요."], ["Colonel Campbell is a very agreeable man, and Mrs. Campbell a friendly, warm-hearted woman.", "캠벨 대령은 무척 유쾌한 분이고, 캠벨 부인은 다정하고 마음이 따뜻한 분입니다."], ["I like them all.”", "저는 그분들이 다 좋습니다.\""]], [["“You know Miss Fairfax’s situation in life, I conclude; what she is destined to be?”", "\"미스 페어팩스가 살면서 어떤 처지인지, 그러니까 무엇이 될 운명인지는 아시겠지요?\""]], [["“Yes—(rather hesitatingly)—I believe I do.”", "\"네 — (다소 머뭇거리며) — 아는 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“You get upon delicate subjects, Emma,”", "\"민감한 화제로 들어서는구나, 엠마.\""], ["said Mrs. Weston smiling; “remember that I am here.—Mr.", "웨스턴 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["Frank Churchill hardly knows what to say when you speak of Miss Fairfax’s situation in life.", "\"내가 여기 있다는 걸 잊지 말아라. 미스터 프랭크 처칠은 네가 미스 페어팩스의 처지를 말하면 뭐라 답해야 할지 난감해하는걸."], ["I will move a little farther off.”", "내가 좀 더 멀리 떨어져 있어 주마.\""]], [["“I certainly do forget to think of her,”", "\"저는 정말이지 그 사람을,\" 엠마가 말했다."], ["said Emma, “as having ever been any thing but my friend and my dearest friend.”", "\"제 벗이자 더없이 소중한 벗이 아닌 다른 무엇으로 생각해 본 적이 없어요.\""]], [["He looked as if he fully understood and honoured such a sentiment.", "그는 그런 마음을 온전히 이해하고 또 높이 산다는 표정이었다."]], [["When the gloves were bought, and they had quitted the shop again, “Did you ever hear the young lady we were speaking of, play?”", "장갑을 사고 다시 가게를 나선 뒤, 프랭크 처칠이 말했다."], ["said Frank Churchill.", "\"우리가 이야기하던 그 아가씨가 연주하는 걸 들어 보신 적 있습니까?\""]], [["“Ever hear her!”", "\"들어 봤느냐고요!\""], ["repeated Emma.", "엠마가 되받았다."], ["“You forget how much she belongs to Highbury.", "\"그녀가 하이베리에 얼마나 깊이 속한 사람인지 잊으셨나 봐요."], ["I have heard her every year of our lives since we both began.", "우리 둘이 배우기 시작한 이래로 평생 해마다 그녀의 연주를 들었는걸요."], ["She plays charmingly.”", "정말 매혹적으로 친답니다.\""]], [["“You think so, do you?—I wanted the opinion of some one who could really judge. She appeared to me to play well, that is, with considerable taste, but I know nothing of the matter myself.—I am excessively fond of music, but without the smallest skill or right of judging of any body’s performance.—I have been used to hear her’s admired; and I remember one proof of her being thought to play well:—a man, a very musical man, and in love with another woman—engaged to her—on the point of marriage—would yet never ask that other woman to sit down to the instrument, if the lady in question could sit down instead—never seemed to like to hear one if he could hear the other. That, I thought, in a man of known musical talent, was some proof.”", "\"그렇게 생각하세요? 정말로 판단할 줄 아는 누군가의 의견을 듣고 싶었어요. 제게는 그녀가 잘 치는 것처럼, 그러니까 상당한 취향을 갖추어 치는 것처럼 보였지만, 저 자신은 이 방면을 전혀 모르거든요. 음악을 무척 좋아하긴 해도, 누구의 연주를 판단할 솜씨나 자격은 조금도 없습니다. 저는 그녀의 연주가 칭송받는 것을 익히 들어 왔지요. 그리고 그녀가 잘 친다고 여겨진 증거 하나가 기억납니다. 어떤 남자가 — 음악에 무척 조예가 깊은 남자가, 게다가 다른 여자와 사랑에 빠져 — 약혼까지 하고 — 결혼을 코앞에 둔 사람이었는데 — 그러면서도 만일 문제의 그 아가씨가 대신 앉을 수만 있다면, 결코 그 다른 여자에게는 악기 앞에 앉으라고 청하지 않더라는 거예요. 한쪽을 들을 수 있는 한, 결코 다른 한쪽을 듣고 싶어 하는 것 같지 않았지요. 음악적 재능이 알려진 남자가 그러는 걸 보고, 저는 그게 어느 정도 증거가 된다고 생각했습니다.\""]], [["“Proof indeed!”", "\"증거가 되고말고요!\""], ["said Emma, highly amused.—“Mr.", "엠마가 자못 즐거워하며 말했다."], ["Dixon is very musical, is he?", "\"미스터 딕슨이 음악에 무척 조예가 깊은 분이라고요?"], ["We shall know more about them all, in half an hour, from you, than Miss Fairfax would have vouchsafed in half a year.”", "우리가 반 시간 안에 당신한테서 그분들 모두에 대해 알아내는 게, 미스 페어팩스가 반년 동안 베풀어 줄 것보다 더 많겠어요.\""]], [["“Yes, Mr.", "\"네, 미스터 딕슨과 미스 캠벨이 바로 그 두 사람이었지요."], ["Dixon and Miss Campbell were the persons; and I thought it a very strong proof.”", "그리고 저는 그게 아주 강력한 증거라고 생각했습니다.\""]], [["“Certainly—very strong it was; to own the truth, a great deal stronger than, if I had been Miss Campbell, would have been at all agreeable to me.", "\"분명히요 — 아주 강력했네요. 사실대로 말하자면, 제가 미스 캠벨이었다면 도무지 달갑지 않았을 만큼 훨씬 더 강력했어요."], ["I could not excuse a man’s having more music than love—more ear than eye—a more acute sensibility to fine sounds than to my feelings.", "사랑보다 음악이 더 많은 남자, 눈보다 귀가 더 밝은 남자, 제 감정보다 고운 소리에 더 예민한 남자라니 — 저는 그런 걸 봐줄 수가 없거든요."], ["How did Miss Campbell appear to like it?”", "미스 캠벨은 그걸 어떻게 받아들이는 것 같던가요?\""]], [["“It was her very particular friend, you know.”", "\"그게, 그녀의 아주 각별한 벗이었으니까요.\""]], [["“Poor comfort!”", "\"위로랍시고 그게!\""], ["said Emma, laughing.", "엠마가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“One would rather have a stranger preferred than one’s very particular friend—with a stranger it might not recur again—but the misery of having a very particular friend always at hand, to do every thing better than one does oneself!—Poor Mrs.", "\"차라리 낯선 사람이 더 사랑받는 편이 낫지, 자기의 아주 각별한 벗이 사랑받는 건 — 낯선 사람이라면 다시 마주칠 일이 없을 수도 있지만 — 무엇이든 자기보다 더 잘하는 아주 각별한 벗이 늘 곁에 붙어 있는 그 비참함이라니! 가엾은 딕슨 부인!"], ["Dixon! Well, I am glad she is gone to settle in Ireland.”", "뭐, 그녀가 아일랜드에 가서 자리 잡은 게 다행이네요.\""]], [["“You are right.", "\"맞는 말씀입니다."], ["It was not very flattering to Miss Campbell; but she really did not seem to feel it.”", "미스 캠벨로서는 그리 기분 좋은 일은 아니었지요. 하지만 그녀는 정말이지 그걸 마음에 두는 것 같지 않았어요.\""]], [["“So much the better—or so much the worse:—I do not know which.", "\"그렇다면 더 잘된 일이거나 — 더 못된 일이거나죠."], ["But be it sweetness or be it stupidity in her—quickness of friendship, or dulness of feeling—there was one person, I think, who must have felt it: Miss Fairfax herself.", "어느 쪽인지는 모르겠어요. 하지만 그게 그녀의 너그러움이든 아둔함이든, 우정의 빠름이든 감정의 무딤이든, 분명 마음에 두었을 사람이 한 명 있을 거예요."], ["She must have felt the improper and dangerous distinction.”", "미스 페어팩스 본인이요. 그 부당하고 위험한 편애를 분명 느꼈을 거예요.\""]], [["“As to that—I do not—”", "\"그 점에 관해서는 — 저는 잘 —\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["do not imagine that I expect an account of Miss Fairfax’s sensations from you, or from any body else.", "제가 당신한테서, 아니 그 누구한테서든 미스 페어팩스의 속마음에 대한 이야기를 듣고 싶어 한다고 생각하진 마세요."], ["They are known to no human being, I guess, but herself.", "제 짐작에 그 마음은 본인 말고 어느 누구도 모를 테니까요."], ["But if she continued to play whenever she was asked by Mr. Dixon, one may guess what one chuses.”", "하지만 미스터 딕슨이 청할 때마다 그녀가 계속 연주를 했다면, 무엇이든 마음대로 짐작해 볼 만하지요.\""]], [["“There appeared such a perfectly good understanding among them all—”", "\"그분들 사이에는 아주 더없이 좋은 사이가 있는 것처럼 보였는데요 —\""], ["he began rather quickly, but checking himself, added, “however, it is impossible for me to say on what terms they really were—how it might all be behind the scenes.", "그가 다소 빠르게 말을 꺼냈다가, 스스로를 멈추며 덧붙였다. \"하지만 그분들이 정말 어떤 사이였는지, 그 모든 게 무대 뒤에서는 어떠했을지 저로서는 말할 도리가 없습니다."], ["I can only say that there was smoothness outwardly.", "겉으로는 매끄러웠다고만 말씀드릴 수 있지요."], ["But you, who have known Miss Fairfax from a child, must be a better judge of her character, and of how she is likely to conduct herself in critical situations, than I can be.”", "하지만 미스 페어팩스를 어릴 적부터 아셨던 당신이라면, 그녀의 성품에 대해서, 또 위기의 상황에서 그녀가 어떻게 처신할 법한지에 대해서, 저보다 더 잘 판단하실 수 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“I have known her from a child, undoubtedly; we have been children and women together; and it is natural to suppose that we should be intimate,—that we should have taken to each other whenever she visited her friends.", "\"분명 어릴 적부터 알았지요. 우리는 함께 아이였고 함께 여자가 되었어요."], ["But we never did.", "그러니 우리가 친밀하리라고, 그녀가 친지들을 보러 올 때마다 서로 마음이 맞으리라고 짐작하는 게 자연스럽겠죠. 하지만 우리는 한 번도 그런 적이 없어요."], ["I hardly know how it has happened; a little, perhaps, from that wickedness on my side which was prone to take disgust towards a girl so idolized and so cried up as she always was, by her aunt and grandmother, and all their set.", "어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지 저도 잘 모르겠어요. 어쩌면 조금은 제 쪽의 못된 마음 탓일 수도 있죠."], ["And then, her reserve—I never could attach myself to any one so completely reserved.”", "늘 이모와 할머니, 그리고 그분들 무리 전체에게 그토록 떠받들리고 그토록 추어올려지는 아가씨를 보면, 저는 도무지 정이 안 가고 싫증부터 났거든요. 게다가 그 입 무거움 — 저는 그렇게 철두철미하게 입 무거운 사람에게는 도무지 마음을 붙일 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“It is a most repulsive quality, indeed,”", "\"정말이지 더없이 사람을 밀어내는 성질이지요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Oftentimes very convenient, no doubt, but never pleasing.", "\"물론 더러는 아주 편리하기도 하겠지만, 결코 호감을 주진 않습니다."], ["There is safety in reserve, but no attraction.", "입이 무거우면 안전하긴 해도, 끌리는 데가 없어요."], ["One cannot love a reserved person.”", "입 무거운 사람을 사랑할 수는 없는 노릇이지요.\""]], [["“Not till the reserve ceases towards oneself; and then the attraction may be the greater.", "\"제 쪽으로 그 입 무거움이 풀리기 전까지는요. 그러고 나면 끌림은 오히려 더 클 수도 있겠죠."], ["But I must be more in want of a friend, or an agreeable companion, than I have yet been, to take the trouble of conquering any body’s reserve to procure one.", "하지만 누군가의 입 무거움을 깨뜨리는 수고를 무릅쓰고 벗을 하나 얻으려면, 저는 지금까지보다 훨씬 더 벗이, 아니 마음 맞는 말동무가 절실해져야 할 거예요."], ["Intimacy between Miss Fairfax and me is quite out of the question.", "미스 페어팩스와 저 사이의 친밀함이란 도무지 가당치 않은 일이에요."], ["I have no reason to think ill of her—not the least—except that such extreme and perpetual cautiousness of word and manner, such a dread of giving a distinct idea about any body, is apt to suggest suspicions of there being something to conceal.”", "그녀를 나쁘게 볼 이유는 없어요 — 조금도요 — 다만 말이며 태도가 그토록 극단적이고 한결같이 조심스럽다는 것, 누구에 대해서든 또렷한 생각을 내비치기를 그토록 두려워한다는 것이, 자칫 무언가 감출 게 있는 것 아닌가 하는 의심을 불러일으키기 쉽다는 점만 빼면요.\""]], [["He perfectly agreed with her: and after walking together so long, and thinking so much alike, Emma felt herself so well acquainted with him, that she could hardly believe it to be only their second meeting.", "그는 그녀의 말에 완전히 동의했다. 그렇게 오래 함께 거닐며 그렇게 똑같이 생각하고 나니, 엠마는 그와 어찌나 잘 아는 사이가 된 듯한지, 이것이 겨우 두 번째 만남이라는 게 도무지 믿기지 않을 지경이었다."], ["He was not exactly what she had expected; less of the man of the world in some of his notions, less of the spoiled child of fortune, therefore better than she had expected.", "그는 그녀가 예상했던 것과 꼭 같지는 않았다. 어떤 생각들에 있어서는 세상물정에 닳은 구석이 덜했고, 응석받이로 자란 행운아 같은 면이 덜했으니, 그래서 그녀가 예상했던 것보다 나았다."], ["His ideas seemed more moderate—his feelings warmer.", "그의 생각들은 더 온건해 보였고, 그의 감정은 더 따뜻해 보였다."], ["She was particularly struck by his manner of considering Mr.", "그녀는 그가 미스터 엘턴의 집을 두고 보인 태도에 특히 깊은 인상을 받았다."], ["Elton’s house, which, as well as the church, he would go and look at, and would not join them in finding much fault with.", "교회와 마찬가지로 그 집도 그는 일부러 가서 보려 했고, 그 집을 두고 흠을 잔뜩 잡는 일에는 그들과 한편이 되려 하지 않았다."], ["No, he could not believe it a bad house; not such a house as a man was to be pitied for having.", "아니, 그는 그 집이 나쁜 집이라고는 믿을 수 없다고, 그런 집을 가졌다고 동정받을 만한 그런 집은 아니라고 했다."], ["If it were to be shared with the woman he loved, he could not think any man to be pitied for having that house.", "사랑하는 여인과 함께 나눌 집이라면, 그 집을 가졌다고 동정받을 남자는 아무도 없으리라고."], ["There must be ample room in it for every real comfort.", "그 안에는 참된 안락을 누리기에 넉넉한 공간이 있을 게 틀림없다고."], ["The man must be a blockhead who wanted more.", "그 이상을 바라는 남자라면 분명 멍청이일 거라고."]], [["Mrs. Weston laughed, and said he did not know what he was talking about.", "웨스턴 부인은 웃으며, 그가 뭘 알고 하는 소리가 아니라고 말했다."], ["Used only to a large house himself, and without ever thinking how many advantages and accommodations were attached to its size, he could be no judge of the privations inevitably belonging to a small one.", "늘 큰 집에서만 살아 와서, 그 크기에 얼마나 많은 이점과 편의가 따라붙는지 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없으니, 작은 집에 어쩔 수 없이 따르는 궁색함을 그가 헤아릴 리 없다는 것이었다."], ["But Emma, in her own mind, determined that he did know what he was talking about, and that he shewed a very amiable inclination to settle early in life, and to marry, from worthy motives.", "그러나 엠마는 속으로, 그가 정말로 알고 하는 소리라고, 그리고 그가 일찍 자리 잡고 떳떳한 동기로 결혼하려는 아주 갸륵한 마음을 보이고 있다고 단정했다."], ["He might not be aware of the inroads on domestic peace to be occasioned by no housekeeper’s room, or a bad butler’s pantry, but no doubt he did perfectly feel that Enscombe could not make him happy, and that whenever he were attached, he would willingly give up much of wealth to be allowed an early establishment.", "가정부의 방이 없다거나 집사의 식기실이 시원찮다거나 하는 것이 가정의 평화에 끼칠 폐단을 그가 미처 몰랐을지는 모른다. 그러나 엔스컴이 자기를 행복하게 해 줄 수 없다는 것을, 그리고 일단 마음을 붙인 사람이 생기면 일찍 살림을 차리기 위해 기꺼이 재산의 많은 부분을 내놓으리라는 것을, 그가 더없이 절실히 느끼고 있다는 것만은 분명했다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter VII", "# 제2권 7장"]], [["Emma’s very good opinion of Frank Churchill was a little shaken the following day, by hearing that he was gone off to London, merely to have his hair cut.", "프랭크 처칠에 대한 엠마의 더없이 좋은 평가는, 그가 그저 머리를 자르려고 런던으로 떠났다는 말을 듣고 이튿날 조금 흔들렸다."], ["A sudden freak seemed to have seized him at breakfast, and he had sent for a chaise and set off, intending to return to dinner, but with no more important view that appeared than having his hair cut.", "아침을 들다 별안간 변덕이 발동한 모양으로, 그는 역마차를 부르더니 길을 떠났는데, 저녁때까지 돌아올 작정이었으나 머리를 자른다는 것 말고는 이렇다 할 용건이 보이지 않았다."], ["There was certainly no harm in his travelling sixteen miles twice over on such an errand; but there was an air of foppery and nonsense in it which she could not approve.", "그런 심부름 길을 위해 십육 마일을 두 번씩이나 오가는 데 해로울 것이야 분명 없었지만, 거기에는 그녀가 도무지 인정할 수 없는 멋부림과 부질없음의 기색이 감돌았다."], ["It did not accord with the rationality of plan, the moderation in expense, or even the unselfish warmth of heart, which she had believed herself to discern in him yesterday.", "그것은 어제 그에게서 알아본 듯싶었던 계획의 합리성, 씀씀이의 절제, 나아가 사심 없는 따뜻한 마음씨와도 어울리지 않았다."], ["Vanity, extravagance, love of change, restlessness of temper, which must be doing something, good or bad; heedlessness as to the pleasure of his father and Mrs. Weston, indifferent as to how his conduct might appear in general; he became liable to all these charges.", "허영, 사치, 변화를 좋아하는 성정, 좋든 나쁘든 무언가를 하지 않고는 못 배기는 들뜬 기질, 아버지와 웨스턴 부인의 기쁨에 무심하고 제 행실이 남들 눈에 어떻게 비칠지 개의치 않는 무신경 — 그는 이 온갖 혐의를 받을 만했다."], ["His father only called him a coxcomb, and thought it a very good story; but that Mrs.", "아버지는 그저 그를 멋쟁이 허풍선이라 부르며 아주 재미난 이야깃거리로 여겼다."], ["Weston did not like it, was clear enough, by her passing it over as quickly as possible, and making no other comment than that “all young people would have their little whims.”", "그러나 웨스턴 부인이 그 일을 마뜩잖아한다는 것은, 그 일을 되도록 빨리 넘겨 버리며 \"젊은 사람이라면 누구나 자잘한 변덕쯤은 있게 마련이지요\" 하는 말 외에 다른 한마디도 보태지 않은 데서 충분히 드러났다."]], [["With the exception of this little blot, Emma found that his visit hitherto had given her friend only good ideas of him.", "이 자그마한 흠을 빼고는, 그가 머무는 동안 지금까지 벗에게 좋은 인상만을 심어 주었다는 것을 엠마는 알 수 있었다. 웨스턴 부인은 그가 얼마나 살뜰하고 유쾌한 말동무가 되어 주는지 — 그 성품에서 얼마나 마음에 드는 점을 두루 보았는지를 거리낌 없이 들려주었다."], ["Mrs.", "그는 무척 트인 성정인 듯했고, 분명 무척 쾌활하고 활기찬 사람이었다."], ["Weston was very ready to say how attentive and pleasant a companion he made himself—how much she saw to like in his disposition altogether.", "그의 생각에서 그릇된 데라곤 찾아볼 수 없었고, 옳은 데는 단연 많았다."], ["He appeared to have a very open temper—certainly a very cheerful and lively one; she could observe nothing wrong in his notions, a great deal decidedly right; he spoke of his uncle with warm regard, was fond of talking of him—said he would be the best man in the world if he were left to himself; and though there was no being attached to the aunt, he acknowledged her kindness with gratitude, and seemed to mean always to speak of her with respect.", "외삼촌 이야기를 따뜻한 존경을 담아 했고, 그 이야기를 즐겨 했으며, 외삼촌은 제멋대로 두기만 하면 세상에 둘도 없이 좋은 분일 거라고 했다. 외숙모에게는 정을 붙일 수 없었지만, 그래도 그녀의 친절만큼은 고마워하며 인정했고, 늘 존중하는 투로 말하려는 듯했다."], ["This was all very promising; and, but for such an unfortunate fancy for having his hair cut, there was nothing to denote him unworthy of the distinguished honour which her imagination had given him; the honour, if not of being really in love with her, of being at least very near it, and saved only by her own indifference—(for still her resolution held of never marrying)—the honour, in short, of being marked out for her by all their joint acquaintance.", "이 모든 것이 매우 미더웠다. 그리고 머리를 자르겠다는 그 불운한 변덕만 아니었다면, 그녀의 상상이 그에게 부여한 그 대단한 영예에 그가 걸맞지 않다고 할 만한 구석은 하나도 없었다 — 정말로 그녀를 사랑한다는 영예까지는 아니더라도, 적어도 그 문턱에 바짝 다가서 있고, 다만 그녀 자신의 무심함 덕에 가까스로 모면한 처지라는 영예 — (그녀는 여전히 결코 결혼하지 않겠다는 결심을 굳히고 있었으니) — 요컨대 두 집안의 모든 지인이 그녀의 짝으로 점찍어 둔 사람이라는 영예 말이다."]], [["Mr. Weston, on his side, added a virtue to the account which must have some weight.", "미스터 웨스턴은 그 나름대로, 얼마간 무게가 실릴 만한 미덕 하나를 그 명세에 보탰다."], ["He gave her to understand that Frank admired her extremely—thought her very beautiful and very charming; and with so much to be said for him altogether, she found she must not judge him harshly.", "그는 프랭크가 그녀를 몹시 흠모하고 있다는 것 — 그녀를 무척 아름답고 무척 매력적이라 여긴다는 것을 그녀가 알게 해 주었다. 그를 두고 그토록 좋게 말할 거리가 많고 보니, 그녀는 그를 모질게 판단해서는 안 되겠다고 느꼈다."], ["As Mrs. Weston observed, “all young people would have their little whims.”", "웨스턴 부인이 말한 대로, \"젊은 사람이라면 누구나 자잘한 변덕쯤은 있게 마련\"이었다."]], [["There was one person among his new acquaintance in Surry, not so leniently disposed.", "서리에서 새로 사귄 이들 가운데, 그렇게 너그럽지만은 않은 사람이 하나 있었다. 대체로 그는 돈웰과 하이베리 두 교구를 통틀어 무척 호의적인 평가를 받았다."], ["In general he was judged, throughout the parishes of Donwell and Highbury, with great candour; liberal allowances were made for the little excesses of such a handsome young man—one who smiled so often and bowed so well; but there was one spirit among them not to be softened, from its power of censure, by bows or smiles—Mr.", "그토록 잘생긴 젊은이 — 그토록 자주 미소 짓고 그토록 곱게 절하는 젊은이의 자잘한 지나침에는 너그러운 용서가 베풀어졌다. 그러나 그들 가운데, 절이며 미소 따위로는 그 준엄한 비판의 힘이 결코 누그러지지 않는 사람이 하나 있었으니, 바로 미스터 나이틀리였다."], ["Knightley.", "그 일은 하트필드에서 그에게 전해졌다."], ["The circumstance was told him at Hartfield; for the moment, he was silent; but Emma heard him almost immediately afterwards say to himself, over a newspaper he held in his hand, “Hum!", "한순간 그는 잠자코 있었다. 그러나 엠마는 그가 거의 곧바로, 손에 든 신문을 들여다보며 혼잣말하는 것을 들었다."], ["just the trifling, silly fellow I took him for.”", "\"흠! 내가 짐작한 그대로, 시시하고 어리석은 녀석이로군.\""], ["She had half a mind to resent; but an instant’s observation convinced her that it was really said only to relieve his own feelings, and not meant to provoke; and therefore she let it pass.", "그녀는 발끈하고 싶기도 했다. 그러나 한순간 살펴보고는, 그것이 정말로 제 속을 풀려는 말일 뿐 그녀를 자극하려던 것이 아님을 알아채고, 그냥 넘겨 버렸다."]], [["Although in one instance the bearers of not good tidings, Mr. and Mrs.", "비록 한 가지 점에서는 달갑잖은 소식을 들고 온 것이었지만, 미스터 웨스턴 부부의 그날 아침 방문은 또 다른 점에서 유난히 때맞은 것이었다."], ["Weston’s visit this morning was in another respect particularly opportune. Something occurred while they were at Hartfield, to make Emma want their advice; and, which was still more lucky, she wanted exactly the advice they gave.", "그들이 하트필드에 머무는 동안 어떤 일이 생겨 엠마가 그들의 조언을 구하고 싶게 되었고, 게다가 더욱 다행스럽게도, 그녀가 바란 것은 바로 그들이 건넨 그 조언이었다."]], [["This was the occurrence:—The Coles had been settled some years in Highbury, and were very good sort of people—friendly, liberal, and unpretending; but, on the other hand, they were of low origin, in trade, and only moderately genteel.", "일의 자초지종은 이러했다. 콜 부부는 하이베리에 자리 잡은 지 몇 해 된 사람들로, 더없이 좋은 부류였다 — 다정하고, 후하고, 잘난 체하지 않았다."], ["On their first coming into the country, they had lived in proportion to their income, quietly, keeping little company, and that little unexpensively; but the last year or two had brought them a considerable increase of means—the house in town had yielded greater profits, and fortune in general had smiled on them.", "그러나 한편으로 그들은 출신이 변변치 못했고, 장사를 하는 데다, 점잖음으로 치자면 그저 그만한 정도였다. 처음 이 고장에 들어왔을 때만 해도 그들은 수입에 맞추어 조용히 살았고, 교제도 적었으며, 그 적은 교제마저 돈을 들이지 않았다."], ["With their wealth, their views increased; their want of a larger house, their inclination for more company.", "그러나 근래 한두 해 사이에 형편이 상당히 펴졌으니 — 도시의 사업이 더 큰 이문을 내고, 운수가 대체로 그들에게 미소 지은 것이었다. 재산이 불어나니 안목도 높아졌다."], ["They added to their house, to their number of servants, to their expenses of every sort; and by this time were, in fortune and style of living, second only to the family at Hartfield.", "더 큰 집을 바라고, 더 많은 교제를 원하게 되었다. 그들은 집을 늘리고, 하인 수를 늘리고, 온갖 씀씀이를 늘렸다."], ["Their love of society, and their new dining-room, prepared every body for their keeping dinner-company; and a few parties, chiefly among the single men, had already taken place.", "그리하여 어느덧 재산과 살림의 격식에서 하트필드 집안 다음가는 둘째가 되어 있었다. 그들의 사교 취향과 새 식당을 두고, 사람들은 으레 그들이 만찬 모임을 열리라 짐작했다."], ["The regular and best families Emma could hardly suppose they would presume to invite—neither Donwell, nor Hartfield, nor Randalls.", "이미 몇 차례 모임이 — 주로 독신 남자들끼리 — 열린 터였다. 격식 있는 일류 가문들 — 돈웰이며 하트필드며 랜덜스를 그들이 감히 청하리라고는 엠마로서는 도무지 상상하기 어려웠다."], ["Nothing should tempt her to go, if they did; and she regretted that her father’s known habits would be giving her refusal less meaning than she could wish.", "설령 청한다 한들 무엇으로도 그녀를 그곳에 가게 할 수는 없으리라. 그러면서도 그녀는, 아버지의 알려진 습관 탓에 자신의 거절이 바라는 만큼의 무게를 갖지 못하리라는 것이 못내 아쉬웠다."], ["The Coles were very respectable in their way, but they ought to be taught that it was not for them to arrange the terms on which the superior families would visit them.", "콜 부부는 그 나름대로 무척 존경할 만한 사람들이었으나, 윗자리 가문이 어떤 조건으로 그들을 찾아갈지 정하는 것이 그들이 할 일은 아니라는 점만은 깨우쳐 주어야 마땅했다."], ["This lesson, she very much feared, they would receive only from herself; she had little hope of Mr.", "이 교훈을 일러 줄 사람은 결국 자기뿐이리라는 것을 그녀는 몹시 두려워했다."], ["Knightley, none of Mr. Weston.", "미스터 나이틀리에게는 거의 기대를 걸지 않았고, 미스터 웨스턴에게는 아예 걸지 않았다."]], [["But she had made up her mind how to meet this presumption so many weeks before it appeared, that when the insult came at last, it found her very differently affected.", "그러나 이 주제넘은 짓에 어떻게 맞설지를 그녀는 그 일이 일어나기 여러 주 전부터 마음에 정해 둔 터라, 막상 그 모욕이 닥쳐왔을 때는 그녀의 심경이 사뭇 다른 데 가 있었다."], ["Donwell and Randalls had received their invitation, and none had come for her father and herself; and Mrs.", "돈웰과 랜덜스에는 초대장이 왔는데, 아버지와 그녀에게는 아무것도 오지 않았다."], ["Weston’s accounting for it with “I suppose they will not take the liberty with you; they know you do not dine out,”", "그것을 두고 웨스턴 부인이 \"댁에는 차마 그런 무례를 못 하는 거겠지요. 댁이 외식하지 않는다는 걸 다들 아니까요\""], ["was not quite sufficient.", "하고 풀이해 준 것만으로는 영 흡족하지 않았다."], ["She felt that she should like to have had the power of refusal; and afterwards, as the idea of the party to be assembled there, consisting precisely of those whose society was dearest to her, occurred again and again, she did not know that she might not have been tempted to accept.", "거절할 권리만이라도 가지고 싶었다고 그녀는 느꼈다. 그러고는, 그곳에 모일 사람들 — 바로 그녀가 가장 아끼는 이들로 꼭 채워진 모임을 머릿속에 자꾸자꾸 그려 보다가, 어쩌면 자신이 응하고 싶은 마음에 솔깃했을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Harriet was to be there in the evening, and the Bateses.", "해리엇이 저녁에 그 자리에 온다 했고, 베이츠네도 온다 했다."], ["They had been speaking of it as they walked about Highbury the day before, and Frank Churchill had most earnestly lamented her absence.", "어제 하이베리를 거닐며 그들이 그 이야기를 나누었는데, 프랭크 처칠은 그녀가 빠진다는 것을 무척이나 안타까워했다."], ["Might not the evening end in a dance? had been a question of his.", "그날 저녁이 무도회로 끝날 수도 있지 않겠느냐고, 그가 물어 왔던 것이다."], ["The bare possibility of it acted as a farther irritation on her spirits; and her being left in solitary grandeur, even supposing the omission to be intended as a compliment, was but poor comfort.", "그 가능성 하나만으로도 그녀의 마음은 한층 더 약이 올랐다. 그리고 그 누락이 설령 예우의 뜻이었다 친들, 외로운 위엄 속에 홀로 남겨진다는 것은 변변찮은 위안일 뿐이었다."]], [["It was the arrival of this very invitation while the Westons were at Hartfield, which made their presence so acceptable; for though her first remark, on reading it, was that “of course it must be declined,”", "웨스턴 부부가 하트필드에 와 있는 동안 바로 그 초대장이 당도한 덕에, 그들의 자리가 그토록 반가웠던 것이다."], ["she so very soon proceeded to ask them what they advised her to do, that their advice for her going was most prompt and successful.", "초대장을 읽고 처음 한 말은 \"물론 거절해야지요\"였으나, 곧바로 어떻게 하는 게 좋겠느냐고 두 사람에게 물었고, 가는 게 좋겠다는 그들의 조언은 더없이 신속하고 성공적으로 먹혀들었다."]], [["She owned that, considering every thing, she was not absolutely without inclination for the party.", "이것저것 다 따져 보니 그 모임에 마음이 아주 없지는 않았다고 그녀는 인정했다."], ["The Coles expressed themselves so properly—there was so much real attention in the manner of it—so much consideration for her father. “They would have solicited the honour earlier, but had been waiting the arrival of a folding-screen from London, which they hoped might keep Mr.", "콜 부부는 더없이 격식 있게 자기들 뜻을 밝혔다 — 그 태도에 진심 어린 정성이 그토록 가득했고, 아버지에 대한 배려가 그토록 깊었다."], ["Woodhouse from any draught of air, and therefore induce him the more readily to give them the honour of his company.”", "\"더 일찍 함께해 주십사 청하고 싶었으나, 런던에서 접이식 병풍이 도착하기를 기다리던 참이었다."], ["Upon the whole, she was very persuadable; and it being briefly settled among themselves how it might be done without neglecting his comfort—how certainly Mrs.", "그 병풍으로 미스터 우드하우스가 외풍을 쐬지 않도록 막아 드릴 수 있기를 바라며, 그리하면 그분께서 한결 흔쾌히 자리를 빛내 주시리라 여겼다\"는 것이었다."], ["Goddard, if not Mrs.", "결국 그녀는 무척 솔깃해졌다."], ["Bates, might be depended on for bearing him company—Mr. Woodhouse was to be talked into an acquiescence of his daughter’s going out to dinner on a day now near at hand, and spending the whole evening away from him.", "그리고 아버지의 안락을 소홀히 하지 않으면서 어떻게 해낼 수 있을지 — 베이츠 부인은 몰라도 고더드 부인만큼은 분명 아버지의 말벗이 되어 주리라는 것을 자기들끼리 간단히 정해 둔 다음, 이제 곧 다가올 날 딸이 만찬에 나가 저녁 내내 곁을 비우는 일을 미스터 우드하우스가 받아들이도록 구슬리기로 했다."], ["As for his going, Emma did not wish him to think it possible, the hours would be too late, and the party too numerous.", "아버지가 가는 일이라면, 엠마는 아버지가 그게 될 법한 일이라고 여기는 것조차 바라지 않았다. 시각이 너무 늦을 테고, 모인 사람도 너무 많을 터였다."], ["He was soon pretty well resigned.", "그는 이내 꽤나 순순히 단념했다."]], [["“I am not fond of dinner-visiting,” said he—“I never was.", "\"나는 만찬 나들이를 좋아하지 않는단다.\""], ["No more is Emma.", "그가 말했다."], ["Late hours do not agree with us.", "\"한 번도 좋아한 적이 없어."], ["I am sorry Mr.", "엠마도 마찬가지지."], ["and Mrs. Cole should have done it.", "늦은 시각은 우리한테 안 맞아."], ["I think it would be much better if they would come in one afternoon next summer, and take their tea with us—take us in their afternoon walk; which they might do, as our hours are so reasonable, and yet get home without being out in the damp of the evening.", "콜 부부가 그런 일을 벌인 게 안타깝구나. 차라리 내년 여름 어느 오후에 우리 집에 들러 함께 차나 들면 훨씬 좋으련만 — 오후 산책길에 들르면 될 게야."], ["The dews of a summer evening are what I would not expose any body to.", "우리 집 시각이 그리 이르니 그럴 수 있을 테고, 저녁 습기에 나서기 전에 집에 닿을 수도 있겠지."], ["However, as they are so very desirous to have dear Emma dine with them, and as you will both be there, and Mr.", "여름 저녁의 이슬이란 누구에게도 쐬게 하고 싶지 않은 것이거든."], ["Knightley too, to take care of her, I cannot wish to prevent it, provided the weather be what it ought, neither damp, nor cold, nor windy.”", "하지만 그쪽에서 우리 엠마와 함께 만찬을 들기를 그토록 간절히 바라고, 또 두 분이 다 그 자리에 계신 데다 미스터 나이틀리까지 곁에서 이 애를 보살펴 주실 테니, 굳이 막을 마음은 없다 — 날씨만 마땅하다면 말이지. 습하지도, 춥지도, 바람 불지도 않는 날이라면.\""], ["Then turning to Mrs. Weston, with a look of gentle reproach—“Ah!", "그러고는 웨스턴 부인을 돌아보며 가벼운 책망의 눈빛으로 말했다."], ["Miss Taylor, if you had not married, you would have staid at home with me.”", "\"아! 미스 테일러, 그대가 결혼만 안 했어도, 나와 함께 집에 있어 주었을 텐데.\""]], [["“Well, sir,” cried Mr.", "\"저런, 어르신,\""], ["Weston, “as I took Miss Taylor away, it is incumbent on me to supply her place, if I can; and I will step to Mrs.", "미스터 웨스턴이 소리쳤다. \"제가 미스 테일러를 데려갔으니, 할 수만 있다면 그 빈자리를 채워 드리는 게 제 도리지요."], ["Goddard in a moment, if you wish it.”", "원하신다면 지금 당장 고더드 부인께 다녀오겠습니다.\""]], [["But the idea of any thing to be done in a moment, was increasing, not lessening, Mr.", "그러나 무엇이든 당장 해치워야 한다는 생각은 미스터 우드하우스의 불안을 가라앉히기는커녕 더욱 키웠다."], ["Woodhouse’s agitation. The ladies knew better how to allay it.", "그것을 어떻게 다독여야 할지는 부인들이 더 잘 알았다."], ["Mr. Weston must be quiet, and every thing deliberately arranged.", "미스터 웨스턴은 잠자코 있어야 했고, 모든 일은 차근차근 마련되어야 했다."]], [["With this treatment, Mr. Woodhouse was soon composed enough for talking as usual.", "이렇게 다루어 주니, 미스터 우드하우스는 이내 여느 때처럼 이야기를 나눌 만큼 마음이 가라앉았다."], ["“He should be happy to see Mrs.", "\"고더드 부인을 뵙게 된다면 기쁘겠구나."], ["Goddard. He had a great regard for Mrs.", "나는 고더드 부인을 무척 마음에 들어 한단다."], ["Goddard; and Emma should write a line, and invite her.", "엠마가 몇 자 적어 그분을 청하면 되겠지."], ["James could take the note.", "제임스가 그 쪽지를 전해 주면 될 게야."], ["But first of all, there must be an answer written to Mrs. Cole.”", "하지만 무엇보다도 먼저, 콜 부인께 답장을 써야 한다.\""]], [["“You will make my excuses, my dear, as civilly as possible.", "\"내 대신 양해를 구해 주려무나, 얘야 — 되도록 정중하게 말이다."], ["You will say that I am quite an invalid, and go no where, and therefore must decline their obliging invitation; beginning with my compliments, of course.", "내가 영락없는 병약한 몸이라 아무 데도 못 가니, 그분들의 고마운 초대를 사양할 수밖에 없다고 전해 주려무나. 물론 안부 인사부터 시작해서 말이지."], ["But you will do every thing right.", "너야 모든 걸 빈틈없이 잘할 게다."], ["I need not tell you what is to be done.", "어찌해야 하는지 내가 일러 줄 것도 없지."], ["We must remember to let James know that the carriage will be wanted on Tuesday.", "화요일에 마차가 필요하다고 제임스에게 미리 일러 두는 걸 잊지 말아야 한다."], ["I shall have no fears for you with him.", "그 사람이 모는 거라면 너를 두고 아무 걱정 없다."], ["We have never been there above once since the new approach was made; but still I have no doubt that James will take you very safely.", "새 길이 난 뒤로는 그 집에 딱 한 번 가 봤을 뿐이지만, 그래도 제임스가 너를 아주 안전하게 데려다주리라는 건 의심치 않는단다."], ["And when you get there, you must tell him at what time you would have him come for you again; and you had better name an early hour.", "그리고 도착하거든, 몇 시에 다시 데리러 오면 좋을지 그 사람한테 일러 둬야 한다. 이른 시각으로 정하는 게 좋을 게야."], ["You will not like staying late.", "늦게까지 있는 건 너도 싫을 테니."], ["You will get very tired when tea is over.”", "차를 다 들고 나면 무척 피곤해질 거란다.\""]], [["“But you would not wish me to come away before I am tired, papa?”", "\"하지만 아버지, 제가 피곤해지기도 전에 자리를 뜨기를 바라시는 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“Oh! no, my love; but you will soon be tired.", "\"아, 아니다, 얘야."], ["There will be a great many people talking at once.", "하지만 너는 곧 피곤해질 게야. 한꺼번에 떠들어 대는 사람이 많을 테니까."], ["You will not like the noise.”", "너도 그 소란이 싫을 게다.\""]], [["“But, my dear sir,” cried Mr.", "\"하지만, 어르신,\""], ["Weston, “if Emma comes away early, it will be breaking up the party.”", "미스터 웨스턴이 소리쳤다. \"엠마가 일찍 자리를 뜨면, 모임이 깨지고 말 텐데요.\""]], [["“And no great harm if it does,”", "\"그래서 깨진다 한들 별 탈이야 있겠소.\""], ["said Mr. Woodhouse.", "미스터 우드하우스가 말했다."], ["“The sooner every party breaks up, the better.”", "\"어느 모임이든 빨리 파할수록 좋은 법이지요.\""]], [["“But you do not consider how it may appear to the Coles.", "\"하지만 그게 콜 부부 눈에 어떻게 비칠지는 생각지 않으시는군요."], ["Emma’s going away directly after tea might be giving offence.", "엠마가 차를 들자마자 곧장 일어선다면 언짢게 여길 수도 있습니다."], ["They are good-natured people, and think little of their own claims; but still they must feel that any body’s hurrying away is no great compliment; and Miss Woodhouse’s doing it would be more thought of than any other person’s in the room.", "그들은 마음씨 좋은 사람들이고 제 권리 따위는 별로 따지지 않습니다만, 그래도 누가 서둘러 자리를 뜨는 것이 그리 큰 예우는 아니라고는 느낄 겁니다. 더구나 미스 우드하우스가 그런다면 그 자리의 누구보다도 더 입길에 오르겠지요."], ["You would not wish to disappoint and mortify the Coles, I am sure, sir; friendly, good sort of people as ever lived, and who have been your neighbours these ten years.”", "콜 부부를 실망시키고 무안하게 하고 싶진 않으실 겁니다, 어르신, 분명히요. 세상에 둘도 없이 다정하고 좋은 사람들이고, 십 년째 어르신의 이웃이었으니까요.\""]], [["“No, upon no account in the world, Mr.", "\"아니지요, 그건 결단코 안 될 일이오, 미스터 웨스턴."], ["Weston; I am much obliged to you for reminding me.", "일깨워 주어서 정말 고맙소이다."], ["I should be extremely sorry to be giving them any pain.", "그들에게 조금이라도 마음을 상하게 한다면 더없이 미안할 게요."], ["I know what worthy people they are.", "그들이 얼마나 점잖은 사람들인지 나도 안다오."], ["Perry tells me that Mr. Cole never touches malt liquor.", "미스터 페리 말로는 미스터 콜이 맥주는 입에도 안 댄다더군."], ["You would not think it to look at him, but he is bilious—Mr. Cole is very bilious.", "겉보기엔 그렇게 안 보여도, 그 사람은 담즙 체질이라 — 미스터 콜은 담즙이 아주 많은 사람이거든."], ["No, I would not be the means of giving them any pain. My dear Emma, we must consider this.", "아니지, 내가 그들에게 마음 아플 일을 끼치는 사람이 되어서야 쓰겠소."], ["I am sure, rather than run the risk of hurting Mr.", "얘야, 이 일은 다시 생각해 봐야겠다."], ["and Mrs. Cole, you would stay a little longer than you might wish.", "분명히, 콜 부부를 섭섭하게 할 위험을 무릅쓰느니, 너도 네 바라는 것보다 조금 더 머물러 주겠지."], ["You will not regard being tired.", "피곤한 것쯤은 마음에 두지 말려무나."], ["You will be perfectly safe, you know, among your friends.”", "너도 알다시피, 벗들 틈에 있으니 더없이 안전할 게야.\""]], [["“Oh yes, papa.", "\"그럼요, 아버지."], ["I have no fears at all for myself; and I should have no scruples of staying as late as Mrs.", "제 걱정은 조금도 없어요. 그리고 웨스턴 부인만큼 늦게까지 있는 것도 저로서는 조금도 거리낄 게 없어요 — 아버지 일만 아니라면요."], ["Weston, but on your account.", "다만 아버지가 저를 기다리며 안 주무실까 봐 그게 걱정이에요."], ["I am only afraid of your sitting up for me.", "고더드 부인과 함께 계시면 더없이 편안하게 지내시리라는 건 의심치 않아요."], ["I am not afraid of your not being exceedingly comfortable with Mrs. Goddard.", "그분이 피케*를 좋아하시잖아요."], ["She loves piquet, you know; but when she is gone home, I am afraid you will be sitting up by yourself, instead of going to bed at your usual time—and the idea of that would entirely destroy my comfort.", "하지만 그분이 댁으로 돌아가신 뒤에는, 평소 주무실 시각에 잠자리에 들지 않고 홀로 앉아 저를 기다리실까 봐 — 그 생각만 하면 제 마음이 도무지 편치 않아요. 기다리며 안 주무시겠다고 꼭 약속해 주셔야 해요.\""], ["You must promise me not to sit up.”", "* 피케(piquet): 두 사람이 하는 프랑스식 카드놀이."]], [["He did, on the condition of some promises on her side: such as that, if she came home cold, she would be sure to warm herself thoroughly; if hungry, that she would take something to eat; that her own maid should sit up for her; and that Serle and the butler should see that every thing were safe in the house, as usual.", "그는 그렇게 하겠다고 했다 — 다만 그녀 쪽에서도 몇 가지 약속을 한다는 조건으로. 이를테면, 집에 돌아왔을 때 몸이 차거든 반드시 충분히 몸을 녹일 것, 시장하거든 무엇이든 먹을 것, 그녀의 몸종이 기다렸다 시중들 것, 그리고 설과 집사가 여느 때처럼 집안에 빈틈없는지 살필 것 — 그런 약속들이었다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter VIII", "# 제2권 8장"]], [["Frank Churchill came back again; and if he kept his father’s dinner waiting, it was not known at Hartfield; for Mrs.", "프랭크 처칠이 다시 돌아왔다. 그가 아버지의 만찬을 기다리게 했다 하더라도, 하트필드에서는 그 일이 알려지지 않았다."], ["Weston was too anxious for his being a favourite with Mr. Woodhouse, to betray any imperfection which could be concealed.", "웨스턴 부인이 그가 미스터 우드하우스의 마음에 들기를 어찌나 바랐던지, 감출 수 있는 흠이라면 무엇 하나 드러내려 하지 않았던 까닭이다."]], [["He came back, had had his hair cut, and laughed at himself with a very good grace, but without seeming really at all ashamed of what he had done.", "그는 머리를 자르고 돌아와, 자기가 한 짓을 두고 더없이 점잖은 태도로 스스로를 비웃었으나, 정작 그 일을 부끄러워하는 기색은 조금도 없었다."], ["He had no reason to wish his hair longer, to conceal any confusion of face; no reason to wish the money unspent, to improve his spirits.", "무안한 얼굴빛을 감추려 머리가 더 길었으면 하고 바랄 까닭도 없었고, 풀 죽은 기운을 돋우려 그 돈을 쓰지 않았더라면 하고 아쉬워할 까닭도 없었다."], ["He was quite as undaunted and as lively as ever; and, after seeing him, Emma thus moralised to herself:—", "그는 여전히 거침없고 활기찼다. 그를 보고 나서 엠마는 혼자 이렇게 도덕론을 폈다."]], [["“I do not know whether it ought to be so, but certainly silly things do cease to be silly if they are done by sensible people in an impudent way.", "\"꼭 그래야 하는 건지는 모르겠지만, 어리석은 일도 분별 있는 사람이 뻔뻔스럽게 해 버리면 더 이상 어리석지 않게 되는 건 분명해."], ["Wickedness is always wickedness, but folly is not always folly.—It depends upon the character of those who handle it.", "사악함은 언제까지나 사악함이지만, 어리석음은 언제나 어리석음은 아니야. 그건 그 일을 다루는 사람의 됨됨이에 달렸으니까."], ["Mr. Knightley, he is not a trifling, silly young man.", "미스터 나이틀리, 그분은 시시하고 어리석은 젊은이가 결코 아니야."], ["If he were, he would have done this differently.", "만약 그런 사람이었다면 이 일을 이렇게는 안 했겠지."], ["He would either have gloried in the achievement, or been ashamed of it.", "그는 그 일을 자랑스레 떠벌리거나, 아니면 부끄러워했을 거야."], ["There would have been either the ostentation of a coxcomb, or the evasions of a mind too weak to defend its own vanities.—No, I am perfectly sure that he is not trifling or silly.”", "멋쟁이의 허세든가, 제 허영조차 변호하지 못하는 나약한 마음의 발뺌이든가, 둘 중 하나였겠지. 아니야, 나는 그가 시시하지도 어리석지도 않다고 완전히 확신해.\""]], [["With Tuesday came the agreeable prospect of seeing him again, and for a longer time than hitherto; of judging of his general manners, and by inference, of the meaning of his manners towards herself; of guessing how soon it might be necessary for her to throw coldness into her air; and of fancying what the observations of all those might be, who were now seeing them together for the first time.", "화요일이 되면 그를 다시 만날 즐거운 전망이 펼쳐질 터였다 — 그것도 지금까지보다 더 긴 시간 동안. 그의 전반적인 태도를 가늠해 보고, 그로 미루어 자기를 대하는 그의 태도가 무엇을 뜻하는지 헤아려 보며, 자기가 얼마나 빨리 냉랭한 기색을 띠어야 할지 짐작해 보고, 이제 처음으로 두 사람이 함께 있는 모습을 보게 될 사람들이 무어라 평할지 그려 볼 수도 있을 것이었다."]], [["She meant to be very happy, in spite of the scene being laid at Mr. Cole’s; and without being able to forget that among the failings of Mr.", "자리가 미스터 콜의 집에 마련되어 있다는 것에도 불구하고 그녀는 무척 즐겁게 보낼 작정이었다."], ["Elton, even in the days of his favour, none had disturbed her more than his propensity to dine with Mr. Cole.", "미스터 엘턴이 한창 그녀의 호감을 사던 시절에도, 그의 결점들 가운데 미스터 콜과 어울려 만찬을 드는 버릇만큼 그녀의 심기를 거스른 것이 없었다는 사실을 잊을 수가 없으면서도 말이다."]], [["Her father’s comfort was amply secured, Mrs.", "아버지의 안락은 넉넉히 보장되어 있었다. 베이츠 부인도 고더드 부인도 와 줄 수 있었던 것이다."], ["Bates as well as Mrs.", "집을 나서기 전 그녀의 마지막 흐뭇한 임무는, 만찬 뒤 함께 앉아 있는 두 부인에게 인사를 드리는 일이었다."], ["Goddard being able to come; and her last pleasing duty, before she left the house, was to pay her respects to them as they sat together after dinner; and while her father was fondly noticing the beauty of her dress, to make the two ladies all the amends in her power, by helping them to large slices of cake and full glasses of wine, for whatever unwilling self-denial his care of their constitution might have obliged them to practise during the meal.—She had provided a plentiful dinner for them; she wished she could know that they had been allowed to eat it.", "아버지가 딸의 차림새가 곱다며 흐뭇하게 바라보는 동안, 그녀는 식사 중 아버지가 두 분의 건강을 염려한 나머지 마지못해 자제하도록 만들었을 그 일에 대한 보상으로, 두 부인에게 케이크를 큼직하게 잘라 드리고 포도주 잔을 가득 채워 드리며 할 수 있는 한 정성을 다했다. 그녀는 두 분을 위해 푸짐한 만찬을 차려 두었으니, 부디 그분들이 그것을 마음껏 드시도록 허락받았기를 바랄 따름이었다."]], [["She followed another carriage to Mr. Cole’s door; and was pleased to see that it was Mr.", "그녀는 다른 마차 한 대를 뒤따라 미스터 콜의 집 문 앞에 이르렀고, 그것이 미스터 나이틀리의 마차임을 보고 기뻤다."], ["Knightley’s; for Mr. Knightley keeping no horses, having little spare money and a great deal of health, activity, and independence, was too apt, in Emma’s opinion, to get about as he could, and not use his carriage so often as became the owner of Donwell Abbey.", "미스터 나이틀리는 말을 따로 두지 않았고, 여윳돈은 적은 데 비해 건강과 활력과 자립심은 넘쳐 났던 까닭에, 엠마가 보기에는 너무 자주 닥치는 대로 돌아다니며, 돈웰 애비의 주인다운 만큼 마차를 쓰지 않는 사람이었다."], ["She had an opportunity now of speaking her approbation while warm from her heart, for he stopped to hand her out.", "이제 그녀에게는 마음에서 우러난 칭찬을 따끈따끈할 때 전할 기회가 생겼다. 그가 그녀를 부축해 내리려고 멈춰 섰던 것이다."]], [["“This is coming as you should do,”", "\"이렇게 오셔야죠.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she; “like a gentleman.—I am quite glad to see you.”", "\"신사답게요. 뵙게 되어 정말 기뻐요.\""]], [["He thanked her, observing, “How lucky that we should arrive at the same moment!", "그는 고맙다고 하면서 한마디 보탰다. \"우리가 같은 순간에 도착하다니 이런 행운이!"], ["for, if we had met first in the drawing-room, I doubt whether you would have discerned me to be more of a gentleman than usual.—You might not have distinguished how I came, by my look or manner.”", "만약 응접실에서 처음 만났더라면, 자네가 나를 여느 때보다 더 신사답다고 알아봤을지 의심스러우니 말이야. 내가 어떤 모양으로 왔는지를 내 차림이나 태도로 분간하지는 못했을 테지.\""]], [["“Yes I should, I am sure I should.", "\"아뇨, 알아봤을 거예요."], ["There is always a look of consciousness or bustle when people come in a way which they know to be beneath them.", "분명히 알아봤을걸요. 사람들은 자기 격에 못 미치는 방식으로 들어올 때면 늘 무언가 의식하는 기색이나 부산한 데가 있거든요."], ["You think you carry it off very well, I dare say, but with you it is a sort of bravado, an air of affected unconcern; I always observe it whenever I meet you under those circumstances.", "본인은 아주 태연하게 넘긴다고 생각하시겠지만, 그게 당신한테는 일종의 허세, 일부러 꾸민 무심한 태도예요. 그런 처지에 계신 당신을 만날 때마다 저는 늘 그걸 알아챈답니다."], ["Now you have nothing to try for.", "지금은 애써 꾸밀 게 아무것도 없으시죠."], ["You are not afraid of being supposed ashamed.", "부끄러워하는 줄로 여겨질까 봐 겁낼 일도 없고요."], ["You are not striving to look taller than any body else.", "남보다 더 커 보이려고 안간힘을 쓰지도 않으시고요."], ["Now I shall really be very happy to walk into the same room with you.”", "지금이야말로 당신과 한방에 들어가는 게 정말 기쁘겠어요.\""]], [["“Nonsensical girl!”", "\"엉뚱한 아가씨로군!\""], ["was his reply, but not at all in anger.", "그의 대꾸였으나, 결코 노여움이 담긴 말은 아니었다."]], [["Emma had as much reason to be satisfied with the rest of the party as with Mr. Knightley.", "엠마는 미스터 나이틀리에게 만족했던 만큼이나 나머지 손님들에게도 만족할 이유가 충분했다."], ["She was received with a cordial respect which could not but please, and given all the consequence she could wish for.", "그녀는 기쁘지 않을 수 없는 따뜻한 존경 속에 맞아들여졌고, 바랄 수 있는 만큼의 대접을 한껏 받았다."], ["When the Westons arrived, the kindest looks of love, the strongest of admiration were for her, from both husband and wife; the son approached her with a cheerful eagerness which marked her as his peculiar object, and at dinner she found him seated by her—and, as she firmly believed, not without some dexterity on his side.", "웨스턴 부부가 도착하자, 더없이 다정한 애정의 눈길과 더없이 강한 흠모의 시선이 남편과 아내 양쪽에서 그녀에게 쏟아졌다. 아들은 그녀를 제 각별한 관심사로 점찍은 양 쾌활하게 열을 올리며 다가왔고, 만찬 자리에서는 그가 자기 곁에 앉아 있는 것을 발견했는데 — 그녀가 굳게 믿기로는, 그의 약간의 솜씨가 발휘되지 않은 것도 아니었다."]], [["The party was rather large, as it included one other family, a proper unobjectionable country family, whom the Coles had the advantage of naming among their acquaintance, and the male part of Mr.", "손님은 꽤 많은 편이었다. 다른 한 집안이 더 끼어 있었던 까닭이다 — 콜 부부가 자기네 지인 가운데 이름을 댈 수 있어 자랑스러워하는, 흠잡을 데 없는 점잖은 시골 집안이었다."], ["Cox’s family, the lawyer of Highbury.", "거기에 하이베리의 변호사 미스터 콕스 집안의 남자들도 함께였다."], ["The less worthy females were to come in the evening, with Miss Bates, Miss Fairfax, and Miss Smith; but already, at dinner, they were too numerous for any subject of conversation to be general; and, while politics and Mr.", "그만 못한 여자들은 저녁에야 와서 합류할 것이었으니, 미스 베이츠와 미스 페어팩스, 미스 스미스가 그들이었다. 그러나 만찬 자리만으로도 이미 인원이 너무 많아 어떤 화제 하나가 두루 통하기는 어려웠다."], ["Elton were talked over, Emma could fairly surrender all her attention to the pleasantness of her neighbour.", "정치 이야기와 미스터 엘턴 이야기가 오가는 동안, 엠마는 옆 사람의 유쾌함에 온전히 마음을 내맡길 수 있었다. 그녀가 귀를 기울이지 않을 수 없게 만든 첫 번째 아득한 소리는 제인 페어팩스라는 이름이었다."], ["The first remote sound to which she felt herself obliged to attend, was the name of Jane Fairfax.", "콜 부인이 그녀에 관해 무언가 무척 흥미로울 법한 이야기를 들려주는 모양이었다. 엠마는 귀를 기울였고, 과연 귀 기울일 만한 가치가 있음을 알았다."], ["Mrs. Cole seemed to be relating something of her that was expected to be very interesting.", "엠마의 그 더없이 소중한 부분, 곧 그녀의 공상이 즐거운 양식을 공급받았다."], ["She listened, and found it well worth listening to.", "콜 부인이 들려준 이야기인즉, 그녀가 미스 베이츠를 찾아갔다가 방에 들어서자마자 피아노포르테 한 대를 보고 깜짝 놀랐다는 것이었다 — 무척 우아해 보이는 악기로, 그랜드피아노는 아니지만 큼직한 스퀘어 피아노포르테였다."], ["That very dear part of Emma, her fancy, received an amusing supply. Mrs.", "그리하여 그 뒤로 이어진 — 콜 부인 쪽의 놀라움과 캐물음과 축하, 그리고 미스 베이츠 쪽의 설명으로 이루어진 — 모든 대화의 알맹이이자 결말은, 이 피아노포르테가 바로 전날 브로드우드 상점에서 도착해 이모와 조카 둘 다 크게 놀랐다는 것 — 전혀 뜻밖이었다는 것이었다."], ["Cole was telling that she had been calling on Miss Bates, and as soon as she entered the room had been struck by the sight of a pianoforte—a very elegant looking instrument—not a grand, but a large-sized square pianoforte; and the substance of the story, the end of all the dialogue which ensued of surprize, and inquiry, and congratulations on her side, and explanations on Miss Bates’s, was, that this pianoforte had arrived from Broadwood’s the day before, to the great astonishment of both aunt and niece—entirely unexpected; that at first, by Miss Bates’s account, Jane herself was quite at a loss, quite bewildered to think who could possibly have ordered it—but now, they were both perfectly satisfied that it could be from only one quarter;—of course it must be from Colonel Campbell.", "미스 베이츠의 말로는, 처음에 제인 자신도 어찌할 바를 몰라 대체 누가 이걸 주문했을지 도무지 짐작이 안 가 어리둥절해했으나 — 이제는 둘 다 그것이 단 한 곳에서 온 것일 수밖에 없다고 완전히 마음을 굳혔다는 것이었다. 물론 캠벨 대령에게서 온 것일 수밖에 없다고."]], [["“One can suppose nothing else,”", "\"달리는 짐작할 수가 없지요.\""], ["added Mrs.", "콜 부인이 덧붙였다."], ["Cole, “and I was only surprized that there could ever have been a doubt.", "\"오히려 그게 한순간이라도 의심거리가 될 수 있었다는 게 놀라웠다니까요."], ["But Jane, it seems, had a letter from them very lately, and not a word was said about it.", "그런데 제인 말로는, 바로 얼마 전에 그분들한테서 편지를 받았는데 그에 관해선 한마디도 없었다는 거예요."], ["She knows their ways best; but I should not consider their silence as any reason for their not meaning to make the present.", "그분들 방식이야 제인이 제일 잘 알겠지만, 저라면 그 침묵을 선물할 뜻이 없다는 근거로는 안 보겠어요."], ["They might chuse to surprize her.”", "짐짓 깜짝 선물을 하려는 거겠죠.\""]], [["Mrs.", "콜 부인의 말에 맞장구치는 이가 많았다."], ["Cole had many to agree with her; every body who spoke on the subject was equally convinced that it must come from Colonel Campbell, and equally rejoiced that such a present had been made; and there were enough ready to speak to allow Emma to think her own way, and still listen to Mrs.", "그 화제로 입을 연 사람마다 한결같이 그것이 캠벨 대령에게서 온 것일 수밖에 없다고 확신했고, 그런 선물이 보내졌다는 사실에 한결같이 기뻐했다."], ["Cole.", "말할 거리를 가진 사람이 넉넉했던 덕에, 엠마는 콜 부인의 말에 계속 귀 기울이면서도 자기 나름대로 생각에 잠길 수 있었다."]], [["“I declare, I do not know when I have heard any thing that has given me more satisfaction!—It always has quite hurt me that Jane Fairfax, who plays so delightfully, should not have an instrument. It seemed quite a shame, especially considering how many houses there are where fine instruments are absolutely thrown away. This is like giving ourselves a slap, to be sure! and it was but yesterday I was telling Mr. Cole, I really was ashamed to look at our new grand pianoforte in the drawing-room, while I do not know one note from another, and our little girls, who are but just beginning, perhaps may never make any thing of it; and there is poor Jane Fairfax, who is mistress of music, has not any thing of the nature of an instrument, not even the pitifullest old spinet in the world, to amuse herself with.—I was saying this to Mr. Cole but yesterday, and he quite agreed with me; only he is so particularly fond of music that he could not help indulging himself in the purchase, hoping that some of our good neighbours might be so obliging occasionally to put it to a better use than we can; and that really is the reason why the instrument was bought—or else I am sure we ought to be ashamed of it.—We are in great hopes that Miss Woodhouse may be prevailed with to try it this evening.”", "\"정말이지, 이보다 더 흐뭇한 이야기를 언제 들어 봤나 싶어요! 제인 페어팩스처럼 그렇게 즐겁게 연주하는 사람한테 악기가 없다는 건 늘 제 마음을 아프게 했거든요. 정말 부끄러운 일이다 싶었죠. 더구나 훌륭한 악기를 그냥 썩히는 집이 얼마나 많은지를 생각하면요. 이건 우리 자신 뺨을 때리는 격이긴 하지만요! 바로 어제 미스터 콜한테 그랬다니까요, 응접실에 놓인 우리 새 그랜드피아노를 보기가 정말 면목 없다고요. 저는 음표 하나 분간 못 하고, 이제 막 배우기 시작한 우리 어린 딸들도 어쩌면 끝내 아무것도 못 칠지 모르는데 말이에요. 그런데 가엾은 제인 페어팩스는 음악의 대가인데도 악기 비슷한 것 하나 없으니, 심심풀이로 칠 세상에서 가장 초라한 낡은 스피넷 한 대조차 없으니 말이에요. 바로 어제 미스터 콜한테 이렇게 말했더니 그이도 제 말에 전적으로 동의하더군요. 다만 그이는 워낙 음악을 끔찍이 좋아하는지라 사고 싶은 마음을 못 참고 사 버린 거예요. 우리 좋은 이웃 가운데 누군가가 이따금 우리보다 더 잘 써 주는 친절을 베풀어 주리라는 바람으로요. 정말이지 그게 이 악기를 산 이유라니까요 — 안 그러면 분명 부끄러워해야 마땅한 일이죠. 우리는 미스 우드하우스가 오늘 저녁 이걸 한번 쳐 주십사 청해 들어주시기를 큰 기대를 품고 있답니다.\""]], [["Miss Woodhouse made the proper acquiescence; and finding that nothing more was to be entrapped from any communication of Mrs. Cole’s, turned to Frank Churchill.", "미스 우드하우스는 알맞게 응낙했고, 콜 부인의 어떤 말에서도 더 이상 끌어낼 것이 없음을 알자 프랭크 처칠 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Why do you smile?”", "\"왜 웃으세요?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Nay, why do you?”", "\"아니, 그쪽은 왜 웃습니까?\""]], [["“Me!—I suppose I smile for pleasure at Colonel Campbell’s being so rich and so liberal.—It is a handsome present.”", "\"저요? 캠벨 대령이 그렇게 부유하고 인심이 후한 게 기뻐서 웃나 봐요. 후한 선물이잖아요.\""]], [["“Very.”", "\"무척이나요.\""]], [["“I rather wonder that it was never made before.”", "\"그런데 진작 보내지 않았다는 게 좀 이상하긴 해요.\""]], [["“Perhaps Miss Fairfax has never been staying here so long before.”", "\"미스 페어팩스가 이곳에 이렇게 오래 머문 적이 전엔 없었으니 그런 게 아닐까요.\""]], [["“Or that he did not give her the use of their own instrument—which must now be shut up in London, untouched by any body.”", "\"아니면 그분들 자기네 악기를 쓰게 해 주지 그러셨을까요. 지금쯤 런던에 처박혀 아무도 손대지 않은 채 있을 텐데요.\""]], [["“That is a grand pianoforte, and he might think it too large for Mrs. Bates’s house.”", "\"그건 그랜드피아노라, 베이츠 부인 댁에는 너무 크다고 여기셨을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“You may say what you chuse—but your countenance testifies that your thoughts on this subject are very much like mine.”", "\"하고 싶으신 말씀이야 뭐든 하셔도 좋아요. 하지만 당신 표정을 보니 이 일에 대한 당신 속생각이 제 생각과 아주 비슷한 게 분명한걸요.\""]], [["“I do not know.", "\"글쎄요."], ["I rather believe you are giving me more credit for acuteness than I deserve.", "제 생각엔 그쪽이 저한테 분에 넘치는 통찰력을 덧씌우시는 것 같은데요."], ["I smile because you smile, and shall probably suspect whatever I find you suspect; but at present I do not see what there is to question.", "그쪽이 웃으니 저도 웃는 거고, 그쪽이 의심하는 건 아마 저도 따라 의심하게 되겠죠. 하지만 지금으로선 의심할 게 뭐가 있는지 모르겠어요."], ["If Colonel Campbell is not the person, who can be?”", "캠벨 대령이 그 사람이 아니라면, 대체 누가 될 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“What do you say to Mrs. Dixon?”", "\"딕슨 부인은 어때요?\""]], [["“Mrs. Dixon!", "\"딕슨 부인!"], ["very true indeed.", "정말 그렇군요."], ["I had not thought of Mrs. Dixon.", "딕슨 부인은 생각도 못 했네요."], ["She must know as well as her father, how acceptable an instrument would be; and perhaps the mode of it, the mystery, the surprize, is more like a young woman’s scheme than an elderly man’s.", "그분도 자기 아버지 못지않게 악기가 얼마나 반가운 선물일지 잘 알 테고요. 게다가 그 방식이라든가, 그 비밀스러움, 그 깜짝스러움은 나이 지긋한 남자보다는 젊은 여인의 꾀에 더 가깝지요."], ["It is Mrs. Dixon, I dare say.", "딕슨 부인이 분명해요."], ["I told you that your suspicions would guide mine.”", "그쪽 의심이 제 의심을 이끌 거라고 말씀드렸잖습니까.\""]], [["“If so, you must extend your suspicions and comprehend Mr. Dixon in them.”", "\"그렇다면 의심을 더 넓혀서 미스터 딕슨까지 거기 넣으셔야죠.\""]], [["“Mr.", "\"미스터 딕슨."], ["Dixon.—Very well.", "아주 좋습니다."], ["Yes, I immediately perceive that it must be the joint present of Mr. and Mrs.", "그래요, 이건 미스터 딕슨과 딕슨 부인의 공동 선물일 수밖에 없다는 게 단번에 와닿는군요."], ["Dixon. We were speaking the other day, you know, of his being so warm an admirer of her performance.”", "며칠 전에 우리가 이야기했잖습니까, 그가 그녀의 연주를 그토록 뜨겁게 흠모한다고요.\""]], [["“Yes, and what you told me on that head, confirmed an idea which I had entertained before.—I do not mean to reflect upon the good intentions of either Mr.", "\"네, 그리고 그 대목에서 당신이 들려주신 이야기가, 제가 전부터 품어 온 생각 하나를 굳혀 주었어요."], ["Dixon or Miss Fairfax, but I cannot help suspecting either that, after making his proposals to her friend, he had the misfortune to fall in love with her, or that he became conscious of a little attachment on her side.", "미스터 딕슨이나 미스 페어팩스, 그 누구의 선의를 의심하려는 건 아니지만요, 저는 이렇게 의심하지 않을 수가 없어요 — 그가 그녀의 친구에게 청혼한 뒤 불행히도 그녀 쪽에게 사랑에 빠지고 말았거나, 아니면 그녀 쪽에서 그에게 품은 약간의 연정을 그가 눈치챘거나 둘 중 하나라고요."], ["One might guess twenty things without guessing exactly the right; but I am sure there must be a particular cause for her chusing to come to Highbury instead of going with the Campbells to Ireland.", "정확히 맞히지 못한 채 스무 가지를 짐작할 수야 있겠지만요, 그녀가 캠벨 가족과 아일랜드로 가지 않고 하이베리로 오기로 한 데에는 분명 특별한 까닭이 있을 거예요. 여기서는 결핍과 고행의 삶을 살아야 하는데, 거기서는 온통 즐거움뿐이었을 텐데 말이에요."], ["Here, she must be leading a life of privation and penance; there it would have been all enjoyment.", "고향 공기를 쐬어 보려 한다는 핑계로 말하자면, 저는 그걸 그저 둘러대는 말로밖에 안 봐요."], ["As to the pretence of trying her native air, I look upon that as a mere excuse.—In the summer it might have passed; but what can any body’s native air do for them in the months of January, February, and March?", "여름이었다면 통했을지도 모르죠. 하지만 일이월, 삼월에 고향 공기가 누구한테 무슨 도움이 된다는 거예요?"], ["Good fires and carriages would be much more to the purpose in most cases of delicate health, and I dare say in her’s.", "몸이 허약한 경우라면 대개 따뜻한 난롯불과 마차가 훨씬 더 요긴할 텐데, 분명 그녀한테도 그럴 거고요. 그쪽이 제 의심을 다 받아들이시라고는 안 할게요."], ["I do not require you to adopt all my suspicions, though you make so noble a profession of doing it, but I honestly tell you what they are.”", "그토록 거룩하게 그러겠다고 공언하시긴 했지만요. 그래도 제 의심이 어떤 건지는 솔직히 말씀드린 거예요.\""]], [["“And, upon my word, they have an air of great probability. Mr.", "\"그리고 정말이지, 그 의심엔 상당히 그럴듯한 데가 있군요."], ["Dixon’s preference of her music to her friend’s, I can answer for being very decided.”", "미스터 딕슨이 친구의 음악보다 그녀의 음악을 더 좋아한다는 것만은 제가 장담할 수 있습니다. 아주 확실해요.\""]], [["“And then, he saved her life.", "\"게다가 그가 그녀의 목숨을 구했잖아요. 그 이야긴 들으셨어요?"], ["Did you ever hear of that?—A water party; and by some accident she was falling overboard. He caught her.”", "뱃놀이였는데, 어쩌다 그녀가 배 밖으로 떨어지려던 걸 그가 붙잡았다죠.\""]], [["“He did.", "\"그랬지요."], ["I was there—one of the party.”", "저도 그 자리에 있었습니다 — 일행 중 하나였거든요.\""]], [["“Were you really?—Well!—But you observed nothing of course, for it seems to be a new idea to you.—If I had been there, I think I should have made some discoveries.”", "\"정말로요? 어머! 그런데 물론 아무것도 못 알아채셨겠죠, 당신한테는 이게 새로운 생각인 것 같으니까요. 제가 그 자리에 있었더라면, 무언가 알아냈을 텐데요.\""]], [["“I dare say you would; but I, simple I, saw nothing but the fact, that Miss Fairfax was nearly dashed from the vessel and that Mr.", "\"그쪽이라면 그랬겠지요. 하지만 저는, 단순한 저는, 그저 사실밖에 못 봤어요."], ["Dixon caught her.—It was the work of a moment.", "미스 페어팩스가 배에서 거의 떨어질 뻔했고 미스터 딕슨이 그녀를 붙잡았다는 사실 말입니다."], ["And though the consequent shock and alarm was very great and much more durable—indeed I believe it was half an hour before any of us were comfortable again—yet that was too general a sensation for any thing of peculiar anxiety to be observable.", "순식간의 일이었어요. 그리고 그 뒤에 따른 충격과 놀람이 무척 컸고 훨씬 더 오래갔지만 — 정말이지 우리 가운데 누구라도 다시 마음을 가라앉히기까지 반 시간은 걸린 것 같은데 — 그건 너무 보편적인 동요라서 특별한 마음의 동요 같은 건 알아챌 수가 없었지요."], ["I do not mean to say, however, that you might not have made discoveries.”", "하지만 그쪽이라면 무언가 알아냈을지도 모른다는 걸 부정하려는 건 아닙니다.\""]], [["The conversation was here interrupted.", "여기서 대화가 끊겼다."], ["They were called on to share in the awkwardness of a rather long interval between the courses, and obliged to be as formal and as orderly as the others; but when the table was again safely covered, when every corner dish was placed exactly right, and occupation and ease were generally restored, Emma said,", "두 사람은 다음 요리가 나오기까지의 꽤 긴 어색한 틈에 함께 끼어 다른 이들처럼 격식 차리고 점잖게 굴어야 했다. 그러나 식탁이 다시 무사히 채워지고, 구석마다 접시가 제자리에 정확히 놓이고, 분주함과 편안함이 두루 되살아나자 엠마가 말했다."]], [["“The arrival of this pianoforte is decisive with me.", "\"이 피아노포르테가 도착했다는 게 제겐 결정적이에요."], ["I wanted to know a little more, and this tells me quite enough. Depend upon it, we shall soon hear that it is a present from Mr.", "조금 더 알고 싶었는데, 이걸로 충분히 알겠어요."], ["and Mrs. Dixon.”", "두고 보세요, 머잖아 이게 미스터 딕슨과 딕슨 부인이 보낸 선물이라는 말을 듣게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“And if the Dixons should absolutely deny all knowledge of it we must conclude it to come from the Campbells.”", "\"그리고 딕슨 부부가 그 일을 전혀 모른다고 딱 잡아뗀다면, 우리는 그게 캠벨 가족에게서 온 거라고 결론지을 수밖에 없겠지요.\""]], [["“No, I am sure it is not from the Campbells.", "\"아뇨, 그건 캠벨 가족에게서 온 게 아니라고 저는 확신해요."], ["Miss Fairfax knows it is not from the Campbells, or they would have been guessed at first.", "미스 페어팩스도 그게 캠벨 가족에게서 온 게 아닌 줄 알고 있어요. 안 그랬다면 처음부터 그분들을 떠올렸을 테니까요."], ["She would not have been puzzled, had she dared fix on them.", "감히 그분들로 단정할 수 있었다면 어리둥절해하지도 않았을 거예요."], ["I may not have convinced you perhaps, but I am perfectly convinced myself that Mr. Dixon is a principal in the business.”", "제가 당신을 설득하진 못했을지 몰라도, 저 자신은 미스터 딕슨이 이 일의 장본인이라고 완전히 확신하고 있어요.\""]], [["“Indeed you injure me if you suppose me unconvinced.", "\"제가 설득되지 않았다고 여기신다면, 정말이지 저를 섭섭하게 하시는 겁니다."], ["Your reasonings carry my judgment along with them entirely.", "그쪽의 추론이 제 판단을 통째로 끌고 가는걸요."], ["At first, while I supposed you satisfied that Colonel Campbell was the giver, I saw it only as paternal kindness, and thought it the most natural thing in the world.", "처음에 그쪽이 캠벨 대령이 준 사람이라고 마음을 굳힌 줄로만 알았을 때는, 저도 그저 아버지의 자상함으로만 보았고, 세상에서 가장 자연스러운 일이라 여겼지요."], ["But when you mentioned Mrs. Dixon, I felt how much more probable that it should be the tribute of warm female friendship.", "하지만 그쪽이 딕슨 부인을 들먹이자, 그게 따뜻한 여자들 사이 우정의 선물일 가능성이 훨씬 더 크겠구나 싶었습니다."], ["And now I can see it in no other light than as an offering of love.”", "이제 제 눈에는 그게 사랑의 헌물 말고는 달리 보이질 않는군요.\""]], [["There was no occasion to press the matter farther.", "이 문제를 더 밀어붙일 까닭은 없었다. 그 확신은 진심인 듯했고, 그도 정말 그렇게 느끼는 얼굴이었다."], ["The conviction seemed real; he looked as if he felt it.", "그녀는 더 말하지 않았고, 다른 화제들이 차례로 등장했다. 만찬의 나머지도 그렇게 흘러갔다."], ["She said no more, other subjects took their turn; and the rest of the dinner passed away; the dessert succeeded, the children came in, and were talked to and admired amid the usual rate of conversation; a few clever things said, a few downright silly, but by much the larger proportion neither the one nor the other—nothing worse than everyday remarks, dull repetitions, old news, and heavy jokes.", "후식이 뒤따랐고, 아이들이 들어와 여느 때 같은 대화의 흐름 속에서 이야깃거리가 되고 칭찬을 받았다. 영리한 말 몇 마디, 어처구니없이 어리석은 말 몇 마디가 오갔으나, 훨씬 더 많은 부분은 이쪽도 저쪽도 아니었으니 — 일상적인 이야기, 따분한 되풀이, 케케묵은 소식, 시시한 농담을 넘어서는 것은 하나도 없었다."]], [["The ladies had not been long in the drawing-room, before the other ladies, in their different divisions, arrived.", "부인들이 응접실에 들어선 지 얼마 안 되어, 다른 부인들이 저마다 떨어진 무리를 이루어 도착했다."], ["Emma watched the entree of her own particular little friend; and if she could not exult in her dignity and grace, she could not only love the blooming sweetness and the artless manner, but could most heartily rejoice in that light, cheerful, unsentimental disposition which allowed her so many alleviations of pleasure, in the midst of the pangs of disappointed affection.", "엠마는 자기의 그 각별한 어린 벗이 들어서는 모습을 지켜보았다. 그 위엄과 우아함을 두고 우쭐댈 수는 없을지언정, 그 꽃처럼 환한 사랑스러움과 꾸밈없는 태도를 사랑할 수 있었을 뿐 아니라, 어그러진 연정의 아픔 한가운데서도 그 많은 즐거움으로 마음을 달랠 수 있게 해 주는 그 가볍고 쾌활하고 감상에 젖지 않는 천성을 진심으로 기뻐할 수 있었다."], ["There she sat—and who would have guessed how many tears she had been lately shedding?", "저기 그녀가 앉아 있었다 — 요 근래 얼마나 많은 눈물을 흘렸는지를 누가 짐작이나 했으랴?"], ["To be in company, nicely dressed herself and seeing others nicely dressed, to sit and smile and look pretty, and say nothing, was enough for the happiness of the present hour.", "모임에 끼어, 자기도 곱게 차려입고 남들이 곱게 차려입은 것을 바라보며, 앉아서 미소 짓고 예쁘게 보이며, 아무 말도 안 하는 것 — 그것이면 지금 이 시간의 행복으로 충분했다."], ["Jane Fairfax did look and move superior; but Emma suspected she might have been glad to change feelings with Harriet, very glad to have purchased the mortification of having loved—yes, of having loved even Mr.", "제인 페어팩스가 보기에도 몸가짐에서도 더 빼어나 보이는 것은 사실이었다. 그러나 엠마는 짐작했다, 제인이라면 차라리 해리엇과 마음을 맞바꾸고 싶었을지 모른다고."], ["Elton in vain—by the surrender of all the dangerous pleasure of knowing herself beloved by the husband of her friend.", "사랑했다는, 그래, 미스터 엘턴조차 헛되이 사랑했다는 그 굴욕을 사들이는 대가로, 친구의 남편에게 사랑받고 있음을 아는 그 위험한 즐거움을 죄다 내어놓는 편을 기꺼이 택했을지 모른다고."]], [["In so large a party it was not necessary that Emma should approach her.", "이렇게 큰 모임에서는 엠마가 굳이 그녀에게 다가갈 필요가 없었다. 그녀는 피아노포르테 이야기를 꺼내고 싶지 않았다."], ["She did not wish to speak of the pianoforte, she felt too much in the secret herself, to think the appearance of curiosity or interest fair, and therefore purposely kept at a distance; but by the others, the subject was almost immediately introduced, and she saw the blush of consciousness with which congratulations were received, the blush of guilt which accompanied the name of “my excellent friend Colonel Campbell.”", "자기야말로 너무 깊이 그 비밀을 알고 있는 터라, 호기심이나 관심을 내비치는 것이 떳떳하지 못하다고 여겨, 일부러 거리를 두었다. 그러나 다른 이들이 거의 곧바로 그 화제를 꺼냈고, 그녀는 축하 인사를 받으며 떠오르는 의식의 홍조를, \"저의 더없이 훌륭하신 벗 캠벨 대령\"이라는 이름과 함께 묻어나는 죄책의 홍조를 보았다."]], [["Mrs.", "마음씨 곱고 음악을 좋아하는 웨스턴 부인은 이 일에 특히 흥미를 느꼈다."], ["Weston, kind-hearted and musical, was particularly interested by the circumstance, and Emma could not help being amused at her perseverance in dwelling on the subject; and having so much to ask and to say as to tone, touch, and pedal, totally unsuspicious of that wish of saying as little about it as possible, which she plainly read in the fair heroine’s countenance.", "엠마는 그녀가 그 화제에 끈질기게 매달리는 모습에 웃음을 참을 수가 없었다. 음색이며 터치며 페달에 관해 그토록 묻고 할 말이 많으면서도, 그 일에 관해서는 되도록 말을 아끼고 싶다는 바람 — 엠마가 그 아리따운 여주인공의 얼굴에서 또렷이 읽어 낸 바람 — 을 전혀 눈치채지 못했던 것이다."]], [["They were soon joined by some of the gentlemen; and the very first of the early was Frank Churchill.", "이윽고 신사들 가운데 몇이 합류했고, 그 일찍 온 무리의 맨 앞은 다름 아닌 프랭크 처칠이었다."], ["In he walked, the first and the handsomest; and after paying his compliments en passant to Miss Bates and her niece, made his way directly to the opposite side of the circle, where sat Miss Woodhouse; and till he could find a seat by her, would not sit at all.", "그는 맨 먼저, 가장 잘생긴 모습으로 걸어 들어왔다. 지나가는 길에 미스 베이츠와 그 조카에게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 곧장 둥근 자리 맞은편으로 길을 내어 미스 우드하우스가 앉은 곳으로 향했고, 그녀 곁에 자리를 찾을 때까지 도무지 앉으려 들지 않았다."], ["Emma divined what every body present must be thinking.", "엠마는 그 자리에 있는 사람마다 무슨 생각을 하고 있을지 알아챘다."], ["She was his object, and every body must perceive it.", "자기가 그의 목표였고, 누구나 그것을 알아볼 게 분명했다."], ["She introduced him to her friend, Miss Smith, and, at convenient moments afterwards, heard what each thought of the other.", "그녀는 그를 자기 벗 미스 스미스에게 소개했고, 그 뒤 적당한 틈틈이 두 사람이 서로를 어떻게 생각하는지 들었다."], ["“He had never seen so lovely a face, and was delighted with her naïveté.”", "\"그는 그토록 사랑스러운 얼굴은 처음 보았고, 그녀의 천진함에 매료되었다.\""], ["And she, “Only to be sure it was paying him too great a compliment, but she did think there were some looks a little like Mr.", "그리고 그녀는, \"물론 그분께 너무 큰 칭찬이긴 하지만, 어딘가 미스터 엘턴을 좀 닮은 데가 있는 것 같았어요.\""], ["Elton.” Emma restrained her indignation, and only turned from her in silence.", "엠마는 분을 억누르고, 말없이 그녀에게서 고개를 돌리는 것으로 그쳤다."]], [["Smiles of intelligence passed between her and the gentleman on first glancing towards Miss Fairfax; but it was most prudent to avoid speech.", "미스 페어팩스 쪽을 처음 흘끗 보았을 때 그녀와 그 신사 사이에 알아챔의 미소가 오갔다. 그러나 말을 삼가는 것이 가장 신중한 처사였다."], ["He told her that he had been impatient to leave the dining-room—hated sitting long—was always the first to move when he could—that his father, Mr. Knightley, Mr.", "그는 그녀에게 말했다, 식당을 떠나고 싶어 안달이 났었노라고 — 오래 앉아 있는 걸 질색한다고 — 일어설 수 있으면 늘 제일 먼저 일어선다고 — 자기 아버지와 미스터 나이틀리, 미스터 콕스, 미스터 콜은 교구 일로 한창 바쁘게 남아 있다고."], ["Cox, and Mr. Cole, were left very busy over parish business—that as long as he had staid, however, it had been pleasant enough, as he had found them in general a set of gentlemanlike, sensible men; and spoke so handsomely of Highbury altogether—thought it so abundant in agreeable families—that Emma began to feel she had been used to despise the place rather too much.", "그래도 자기가 남아 있는 동안은 꽤 즐거웠노라고, 대체로 신사답고 분별 있는 사람들이더라고."], ["She questioned him as to the society in Yorkshire—the extent of the neighbourhood about Enscombe, and the sort; and could make out from his answers that, as far as Enscombe was concerned, there was very little going on, that their visitings were among a range of great families, none very near; and that even when days were fixed, and invitations accepted, it was an even chance that Mrs.", "그러고는 하이베리 전체를 어찌나 곱게 말하던지 — 유쾌한 집안이 그토록 넘친다고 여기던지 — 엠마는 자기가 이 고장을 좀 지나치게 얕보아 온 게 아닌가 하는 생각이 들기 시작했다. 그녀는 요크셔의 사교계에 관해 — 엔스컴 근방 이웃의 범위며 그 성격에 관해 그에게 물었다."], ["Churchill were not in health and spirits for going; that they made a point of visiting no fresh person; and that, though he had his separate engagements, it was not without difficulty, without considerable address at times, that he could get away, or introduce an acquaintance for a night.", "그의 대답에서 헤아릴 수 있기로는, 엔스컴에 관한 한 이렇다 할 일은 거의 없으며, 그 집안의 왕래는 대단한 집안들 사이에서만 이루어지되 아주 가까운 데는 한 집도 없고, 심지어 날을 잡고 초대를 수락한 뒤에도 처칠 부인이 외출할 만큼 몸과 기운이 받쳐 주지 않는 경우가 절반은 된다는 것이었다. 그 집안은 새로운 사람은 일절 방문하지 않는 것을 원칙으로 삼는다는 것, 그리고 그에게는 따로 약속이 있긴 해도 빠져나오거나 지인 하나를 하룻밤 데려오는 일조차 더러는 상당한 수완을 부리지 않고는 어렵다는 것이었다."]], [["She saw that Enscombe could not satisfy, and that Highbury, taken at its best, might reasonably please a young man who had more retirement at home than he liked.", "엠마는 엔스컴이 만족스러울 리 없다는 것, 그리고 하이베리가 좋게 보아 준다면, 집에서 분에 넘치는 칩거를 견디는 젊은이의 마음에 마땅히 들 만하다는 것을 알았다."], ["His importance at Enscombe was very evident.", "엔스컴에서의 그의 비중은 아주 또렷했다."], ["He did not boast, but it naturally betrayed itself, that he had persuaded his aunt where his uncle could do nothing, and on her laughing and noticing it, he owned that he believed (excepting one or two points) he could with time persuade her to any thing.", "그는 자랑하지 않았으나, 외삼촌이 어쩌지 못하는 일에서도 자기는 외숙모를 설득해 냈노라는 사실이 저절로 드러났다. 그녀가 웃으며 그 점을 짚자, 그는 (한두 가지를 빼면) 시간만 들이면 무슨 일이든 외숙모를 설득할 수 있으리라 믿는다고 털어놓았다."], ["One of those points on which his influence failed, he then mentioned.", "자기 영향력이 통하지 않는 그 한두 가지 중 하나를 그가 곧 입에 올렸다."], ["He had wanted very much to go abroad—had been very eager indeed to be allowed to travel—but she would not hear of it.", "그는 무척이나 외국에 나가고 싶어 했고 — 정말이지 여행을 허락받기를 간절히 바랐으나 — 외숙모가 들으려 하지 않더라는 것이었다."], ["This had happened the year before.", "그건 지난해의 일이었다."], ["Now, he said, he was beginning to have no longer the same wish.", "그런데 이제는 그 소망이 예전 같지 않기 시작했노라고 그가 말했다."]], [["The unpersuadable point, which he did not mention, Emma guessed to be good behaviour to his father.", "그가 입에 올리지 않은 그 설득되지 않는 점이 무엇인지, 엠마는 아버지에 대한 도리를 지키는 일이리라 짐작했다."]], [["“I have made a most wretched discovery,”", "\"제가 아주 비참한 사실을 하나 깨달았어요.\""], ["said he, after a short pause.— “I have been here a week to-morrow—half my time.", "그가 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 말했다. \"내일이면 여기 온 지 일주일이에요 — 제 시간의 절반이죠."], ["I never knew days fly so fast.", "날이 이렇게 빨리 가는 줄은 몰랐어요."], ["A week to-morrow!—And I have hardly begun to enjoy myself.", "내일이면 일주일이라니! 그런데 이제 겨우 즐기기 시작했을 뿐이에요."], ["But just got acquainted with Mrs.", "이제 막 웨스턴 부인이며 여러분과 사귀게 됐는데요!"], ["Weston, and others!—I hate the recollection.”", "떠올리기도 싫네요.\""]], [["“Perhaps you may now begin to regret that you spent one whole day, out of so few, in having your hair cut.”", "\"그렇게 며칠 안 되는 시간 가운데 꼬박 하루를 머리 자르는 데 써 버린 걸 이제 후회하기 시작하실지도 모르겠네요.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아뇨.\""], ["said he, smiling, “that is no subject of regret at all.", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그건 전혀 후회할 일이 아니에요."], ["I have no pleasure in seeing my friends, unless I can believe myself fit to be seen.”", "남에게 보일 만하다는 자신이 없으면 벗을 만나도 즐겁지가 않거든요.\""]], [["The rest of the gentlemen being now in the room, Emma found herself obliged to turn from him for a few minutes, and listen to Mr. Cole.", "나머지 신사들이 이제 방에 들어선 터라, 엠마는 잠시 그에게서 몸을 돌려 미스터 콜의 이야기를 들어야 했다."], ["When Mr. Cole had moved away, and her attention could be restored as before, she saw Frank Churchill looking intently across the room at Miss Fairfax, who was sitting exactly opposite.", "미스터 콜이 자리를 옮기고 다시 아까처럼 마음을 돌릴 수 있게 되었을 때, 그녀는 프랭크 처칠이 방 건너편의 미스 페어팩스를 — 마침 그녀가 바로 맞은편에 앉아 있었다 — 뚫어지게 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이세요?\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["He started.", "그가 흠칫했다."], ["“Thank you for rousing me,”", "\"정신 차리게 해 줘서 고맙습니다.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he replied.", "\"제가 무척 무례하게 굴었나 봐요. 그런데 정말 미스 페어팩스가 머리를 어찌나 이상하게 했는지 — 어찌나 별나게 했는지 — 도무지 눈을 뗄 수가 없네요."], ["“I believe I have been very rude; but really Miss Fairfax has done her hair in so odd a way—so very odd a way—that I cannot keep my eyes from her.", "저렇게 유별난 건 처음 봐요! 저 곱슬머리라니!"], ["I never saw any thing so outrée!—Those curls!—This must be a fancy of her own.", "저건 분명 그녀만의 취향일 거예요. 저렇게 한 사람은 아무도 없거든요!"], ["I see nobody else looking like her!—I must go and ask her whether it is an Irish fashion.", "가서 저게 아일랜드 유행인지 물어봐야겠어요. 그럴까요?"], ["Shall I?—Yes, I will—I declare I will—and you shall see how she takes it;—whether she colours.”", "그래요, 그래야겠어요 — 정말이지 그러겠어요 — 그녀가 어떻게 받아들이는지 두고 보세요. 얼굴을 붉히는지요.\""]], [["He was gone immediately; and Emma soon saw him standing before Miss Fairfax, and talking to her; but as to its effect on the young lady, as he had improvidently placed himself exactly between them, exactly in front of Miss Fairfax, she could absolutely distinguish nothing.", "그는 곧장 가 버렸다. 이내 엠마는 그가 미스 페어팩스 앞에 서서 그녀에게 말을 거는 모습을 보았다. 그러나 그 말이 그 아가씨에게 어떤 영향을 미치는지로 말하자면, 그가 경솔하게도 두 사람 사이 정확히 한가운데, 미스 페어팩스 바로 정면에 자리를 잡은 탓에, 그녀는 아무것도 분간할 수가 없었다."]], [["Before he could return to his chair, it was taken by Mrs. Weston.", "그가 제 자리로 돌아오기도 전에, 그 자리는 웨스턴 부인이 차지했다."]], [["“This is the luxury of a large party,”", "\"이게 큰 모임의 호사로움이죠.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she:—“one can get near every body, and say every thing.", "\"누구한테든 다가갈 수 있고, 무슨 말이든 할 수 있으니까요."], ["My dear Emma, I am longing to talk to you.", "엠마, 너랑 이야기가 너무 하고 싶었단다."], ["I have been making discoveries and forming plans, just like yourself, and I must tell them while the idea is fresh.", "나도 너처럼 무언가를 알아내고 계획을 세웠거든. 생각이 따끈따끈할 때 말해 줘야겠어."], ["Do you know how Miss Bates and her niece came here?”", "미스 베이츠랑 그 조카가 어떻게 여기 왔는지 아니?\""]], [["“How?—They were invited, were not they?”", "\"어떻게요? 초대받은 거 아니에요?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아, 그래 — 그런데 어떻게 여기까지 실려 왔느냐 말이야."], ["yes—but how they were conveyed hither?—the manner of their coming?”", "그 오는 방식 말이다.\""]], [["“They walked, I conclude.", "\"걸어왔겠죠."], ["How else could they come?”", "달리 어떻게 오겠어요?\""]], [["“Very true.—Well, a little while ago it occurred to me how very sad it would be to have Jane Fairfax walking home again, late at night, and cold as the nights are now.", "\"맞아. 그런데 좀 전에 문득 이런 생각이 들더구나."], ["And as I looked at her, though I never saw her appear to more advantage, it struck me that she was heated, and would therefore be particularly liable to take cold.", "제인 페어팩스가 이렇게 밤늦게, 요즘처럼 추운 밤에 또 걸어서 집에 돌아간다면 얼마나 딱한 일일까 하고. 그 애를 바라보자니, 그렇게 돋보이는 모습은 처음 봤지만, 몸에 열이 올라 있어서 특히 감기 들기 쉽겠다는 생각이 퍼뜩 들었어."], ["Poor girl!", "가엾은 것!"], ["I could not bear the idea of it; so, as soon as Mr.", "그 생각을 도저히 견딜 수가 없더구나."], ["Weston came into the room, and I could get at him, I spoke to him about the carriage.", "그래서 미스터 웨스턴이 방에 들어오자마자 붙들고 마차 이야기를 했지. 그이가 내 뜻을 얼마나 선뜻 받아들였는지는 짐작이 가겠지."], ["You may guess how readily he came into my wishes; and having his approbation, I made my way directly to Miss Bates, to assure her that the carriage would be at her service before it took us home; for I thought it would be making her comfortable at once.", "그이의 허락을 받고 나서, 곧장 미스 베이츠한테 가서 마차가 우리를 집에 데려다주기 전에 먼저 두 분이 쓰실 수 있게 대기할 거라고 일러 줬단다. 그러면 단번에 마음이 놓이실 것 같아서 말이야."], ["Good soul!", "마음씨 고운 분!"], ["she was as grateful as possible, you may be sure.", "더할 수 없이 고마워하셨지, 그야 물론이고."], ["‘Nobody was ever so fortunate as herself!’—but with many, many thanks—‘there was no occasion to trouble us, for Mr.", "'세상에 자기만큼 운 좋은 사람이 또 없다'면서 — 그런데 거듭거듭 고맙다고 하시면서도 — '굳이 우리를 번거롭게 할 것 없다고, 미스터 나이틀리의 마차가 모셔 왔고 또 모셔다드리기로 했다'는 거야. 나는 정말 깜짝 놀랐어."], ["Knightley’s carriage had brought, and was to take them home again.’ I was quite surprized;—very glad, I am sure; but really quite surprized.", "그야 무척 기뻤지. 하지만 정말 놀랐단다."], ["Such a very kind attention—and so thoughtful an attention!—the sort of thing that so few men would think of.", "그렇게 더없이 친절한 배려라니 — 그토록 사려 깊은 배려라니! 남자 중에 그런 걸 생각해 낼 사람이 그리 많지 않거든."], ["And, in short, from knowing his usual ways, I am very much inclined to think that it was for their accommodation the carriage was used at all.", "한마디로, 그분의 평소 방식을 아는지라, 나는 애초에 마차를 쓴 게 두 분을 위해서가 아니었나 하는 생각이 자꾸 드는구나."], ["I do suspect he would not have had a pair of horses for himself, and that it was only as an excuse for assisting them.”", "아무래도 자기 혼자라면 말 두 필을 매지 않았을 텐데, 그저 두 분을 도울 핑계로 그런 게 아닐까 싶어.\""]], [["“Very likely,”", "\"충분히 그럴 만해요.\""], ["said Emma—“nothing more likely.", "엠마가 말했다. \"그보다 더 그럴듯한 일도 없죠."], ["I know no man more likely than Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리만큼 그런 일을 — 정말로 인정 많고 쓸모 있고 사려 깊고 너그러운 일이라면 무엇이든 할 만한 사람을 저는 알지 못해요."], ["Knightley to do the sort of thing—to do any thing really good-natured, useful, considerate, or benevolent.", "그분은 멋부리는 분은 아니지만, 인정이 무척 많은 분이거든요. 그러니 제인 페어팩스의 병약함을 생각하면, 그분께는 이게 인정을 베풀 일로 보였을 거예요."], ["He is not a gallant man, but he is a very humane one; and this, considering Jane Fairfax’s ill-health, would appear a case of humanity to him;—and for an act of unostentatious kindness, there is nobody whom I would fix on more than on Mr. Knightley.", "티 안 내는 친절을 행할 사람으로 미스터 나이틀리만 한 분을 저는 또 꼽을 수가 없네요."], ["I know he had horses to-day—for we arrived together; and I laughed at him about it, but he said not a word that could betray.”", "오늘 그분께 말이 있었다는 건 제가 알아요 — 우리가 같이 도착했으니까요. 제가 그걸 두고 놀렸는데, 속내를 드러낼 말은 한마디도 안 하시더라고요.\""]], [["“Well,” said Mrs.", "\"글쎄,\""], ["Weston, smiling, “you give him credit for more simple, disinterested benevolence in this instance than I do; for while Miss Bates was speaking, a suspicion darted into my head, and I have never been able to get it out again.", "웨스턴 부인이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"넌 이번 일에서 그분께 나보다 더 순박하고 사심 없는 자비심을 인정해 주는구나."], ["The more I think of it, the more probable it appears.", "미스 베이츠가 말하는 동안, 내 머릿속에 의심 하나가 번뜩 떠올라 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었거든. 생각하면 할수록 더 그럴듯해 보여."], ["In short, I have made a match between Mr. Knightley and Jane Fairfax.", "한마디로, 나는 미스터 나이틀리와 제인 페어팩스를 짝지었단다."], ["See the consequence of keeping you company!—What do you say to it?”", "너랑 어울린 결과가 이거란다! 어떻게 생각하니?\""]], [["“Mr. Knightley and Jane Fairfax!”", "\"미스터 나이틀리랑 제인 페어팩스라고요!\""], ["exclaimed Emma.", "엠마가 외쳤다."], ["“Dear Mrs. Weston, how could you think of such a thing?—Mr.", "\"어머나, 웨스턴 부인, 어떻게 그런 생각을 다 하셨어요?"], ["Knightley!—Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리가!"], ["Knightley must not marry!—You would not have little Henry cut out from Donwell?—Oh!", "미스터 나이틀리는 결혼하면 안 돼요! 어린 헨리를 돈웰에서 밀려나게 하실 생각은 아니죠?"], ["no, no, Henry must have Donwell.", "아, 안 돼요, 안 돼."], ["I cannot at all consent to Mr.", "돈웰은 헨리 거예요."], ["Knightley’s marrying; and I am sure it is not at all likely.", "미스터 나이틀리가 결혼한다는 건 저는 도무지 찬성할 수가 없어요."], ["I am amazed that you should think of such a thing.”", "그리고 그건 전혀 있을 법한 일도 아니고요. 그런 생각을 하시다니 놀라워요.\""]], [["“My dear Emma, I have told you what led me to think of it.", "\"엠마, 무엇 때문에 그런 생각을 하게 됐는지 다 말했잖니."], ["I do not want the match—I do not want to injure dear little Henry—but the idea has been given me by circumstances; and if Mr.", "나도 그 혼사를 바라는 건 아니야 — 가엾은 어린 헨리를 다치게 하고 싶지도 않고 — 하지만 정황이 그 생각을 떠올리게 해 준걸."], ["Knightley really wished to marry, you would not have him refrain on Henry’s account, a boy of six years old, who knows nothing of the matter?”", "그리고 미스터 나이틀리가 정말로 결혼하고 싶어 한다면야, 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는 여섯 살배기 헨리를 위해서 그분더러 단념하라고 할 수야 없지 않겠니?\""]], [["“Yes, I would.", "\"네, 저는 그러겠어요."], ["I could not bear to have Henry supplanted.—Mr.", "헨리가 밀려나는 건 도저히 못 견뎌요. 미스터 나이틀리가 결혼이라니!"], ["Knightley marry!—No, I have never had such an idea, and I cannot adopt it now.", "아뇨, 그런 생각은 한 번도 해 본 적이 없고, 이제 와서 받아들일 수도 없어요."], ["And Jane Fairfax, too, of all women!”", "게다가 하필 제인 페어팩스라니, 그 많은 여자 중에서!\""]], [["“Nay, she has always been a first favourite with him, as you very well know.”", "\"아니, 그 애는 늘 그분이 가장 아끼는 사람이었잖니, 너도 잘 알면서.\""]], [["“But the imprudence of such a match!”", "\"하지만 그런 혼사의 경솔함이라니요!\""]], [["“I am not speaking of its prudence; merely its probability.”", "\"나는 그 분별을 말하는 게 아니야. 그저 그 개연성을 말하는 거지.\""]], [["“I see no probability in it, unless you have any better foundation than what you mention.", "\"저는 거기서 아무 개연성도 못 보겠어요. 부인이 말씀하신 것보다 더 나은 근거가 없다면요."], ["His good-nature, his humanity, as I tell you, would be quite enough to account for the horses.", "그분의 인정 많음, 그 자비심으로 말하자면, 말씀드렸듯이 마차 일을 설명하기엔 그것만으로 충분하고도 남아요."], ["He has a great regard for the Bateses, you know, independent of Jane Fairfax—and is always glad to shew them attention.", "그분은 제인 페어팩스와는 별개로 베이츠 댁을 무척 아끼시잖아요. 늘 그 댁에 관심을 보여 드리는 걸 기뻐하시고요."], ["My dear Mrs. Weston, do not take to match-making.", "웨스턴 부인, 중매에는 손대지 마세요."], ["You do it very ill.", "너무 서투르시거든요."], ["Jane Fairfax mistress of the Abbey!—Oh!", "제인 페어팩스가 애비의 안주인이라뇨! 아, 안 돼요, 안 돼."], ["no, no;—every feeling revolts.", "온몸의 감각이 다 거부하는걸요."], ["For his own sake, I would not have him do so mad a thing.”", "그분을 위해서라도, 그런 미친 짓은 하지 않으시면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Imprudent, if you please—but not mad.", "\"경솔하다고는 해도 좋아 — 하지만 미친 짓은 아니지."], ["Excepting inequality of fortune, and perhaps a little disparity of age, I can see nothing unsuitable.”", "재산의 불균형, 그리고 어쩌면 약간의 나이 차이를 빼면, 나는 어울리지 않는 데를 하나도 못 찾겠는걸.\""]], [["“But Mr. Knightley does not want to marry.", "\"하지만 미스터 나이틀리는 결혼하고 싶어 하지 않아요."], ["I am sure he has not the least idea of it.", "그분은 그런 생각이 손톱만큼도 없는 게 분명해요. 그분 머릿속에 그런 걸 집어넣지 마세요."], ["Do not put it into his head.", "그분이 왜 결혼을 하겠어요?"], ["Why should he marry?—He is as happy as possible by himself; with his farm, and his sheep, and his library, and all the parish to manage; and he is extremely fond of his brother’s children.", "혼자서도 더없이 행복하신데요. 농장이며 양 떼며 서재며, 게다가 교구 전체를 건사하는 일까지 있고요."], ["He has no occasion to marry, either to fill up his time or his heart.”", "그리고 동생 아이들을 끔찍이 아끼시잖아요. 시간을 채우려고든 마음을 채우려고든, 그분은 결혼할 필요가 없어요.\""]], [["“My dear Emma, as long as he thinks so, it is so; but if he really loves Jane Fairfax—”", "\"엠마, 그분이 그렇게 여기는 한이야 그런 거지. 하지만 그분이 정말로 제인 페어팩스를 사랑한다면—\""]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"말도 안 돼요!"], ["He does not care about Jane Fairfax.", "그분은 제인 페어팩스한테 마음이 없어요."], ["In the way of love, I am sure he does not.", "사랑이라는 뜻으로는, 정말 없는 게 분명해요."], ["He would do any good to her, or her family; but—”", "그녀나 그녀 가족한테 좋은 일이라면 무엇이든 해 주시겠지만, 그렇지만—\""]], [["“Well,” said Mrs.", "\"글쎄,\""], ["Weston, laughing, “perhaps the greatest good he could do them, would be to give Jane such a respectable home.”", "웨스턴 부인이 웃으며 말했다. \"어쩌면 그분이 그들에게 해 줄 수 있는 가장 큰 선행이, 제인한테 그렇게 떳떳한 가정을 마련해 주는 일일지도 모르지.\""]], [["“If it would be good to her, I am sure it would be evil to himself; a very shameful and degrading connexion. How would he bear to have Miss Bates belonging to him?—To have her haunting the Abbey, and thanking him all day long for his great kindness in marrying Jane?—‘So very kind and obliging!—But he always had been such a very kind neighbour!’ And then fly off, through half a sentence, to her mother’s old petticoat. ‘Not that it was such a very old petticoat either—for still it would last a great while—and, indeed, she must thankfully say that their petticoats were all very strong.’”", "\"그게 그녀에게 좋은 일이라면, 그분 자신에게는 분명 나쁜 일일 거예요. 더없이 수치스럽고 격을 떨어뜨리는 인연이죠. 미스 베이츠가 자기 식구가 되는 걸 그분이 어떻게 견디겠어요? 그분이 제인과 결혼해 준 그 크나큰 친절을 두고 하루 종일 고맙다고 하며 애비를 들락거리는 걸요? '어쩌면 그렇게 친절하고 자상하신지! 하기야 늘 그렇게 친절한 이웃이셨지!' 그러다가 문장 절반쯤에서 휙 자기 어머니의 낡은 속치마 이야기로 새어 나가겠죠. '그게 그렇게 아주 낡은 속치마는 또 아니라고 — 아직도 한참 더 입을 수 있다고 — 정말이지, 우리 집 속치마들은 죄다 어찌나 튼튼한지 고마운 마음뿐이라고요.'\""]], [["“For shame, Emma!", "\"창피한 줄 아세요, 엠마!"], ["Do not mimic her.", "그분 흉내는 그만둬요."], ["You divert me against my conscience.", "양심에 거슬리는데도 자꾸 웃기잖니."], ["And, upon my word, I do not think Mr. Knightley would be much disturbed by Miss Bates.", "그리고 정말이지, 나는 미스터 나이틀리가 미스 베이츠 때문에 그리 시달릴 것 같지는 않은걸."], ["Little things do not irritate him.", "사소한 일에 그분은 짜증 내지 않거든."], ["She might talk on; and if he wanted to say any thing himself, he would only talk louder, and drown her voice.", "그분이 떠들든 말든 내버려 두다가, 자기가 무슨 말이 하고 싶으면 그저 더 큰 소리로 말해서 그분 목소리를 덮어 버리면 그만이지."], ["But the question is not, whether it would be a bad connexion for him, but whether he wishes it; and I think he does.", "하지만 문제는 그게 그분께 나쁜 인연이냐 아니냐가 아니라, 그분이 그걸 바라느냐 아니냐란다. 나는 그분이 바란다고 생각해."], ["I have heard him speak, and so must you, so very highly of Jane Fairfax!", "그분이 제인 페어팩스를 두고 그렇게 높이 말하는 걸 들은 적이 있거든, 너도 분명 들었을 테고!"], ["The interest he takes in her—his anxiety about her health—his concern that she should have no happier prospect!", "그분이 그 애한테 두는 관심이며 — 그 애 건강을 염려하는 마음이며 — 그 애한테 더 행복한 앞날이 없는 걸 안타까워하는 마음이며!"], ["I have heard him express himself so warmly on those points!—Such an admirer of her performance on the pianoforte, and of her voice!", "그 점들에 관해 그분이 그토록 뜨겁게 말하는 걸 내가 들었다니까! 그 애의 피아노포르테 연주며 목소리를 어찌나 흠모하는지!"], ["I have heard him say that he could listen to her for ever.", "그 애 노래라면 영영 들을 수 있겠다고 하는 것도 들은 적 있어."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["and I had almost forgotten one idea that occurred to me—this pianoforte that has been sent here by somebody—though we have all been so well satisfied to consider it a present from the Campbells, may it not be from Mr.", "그리고 떠올랐던 생각 하나를 깜빡 잊을 뻔했네 — 누군가가 여기로 보낸 이 피아노포르테 말이다. 우리 모두 캠벨 가족의 선물이라고 여기며 그렇게 흡족해했지만, 혹시 미스터 나이틀리한테서 온 건 아닐까?"], ["Knightley? I cannot help suspecting him.", "자꾸 그분이 의심스럽단다."], ["I think he is just the person to do it, even without being in love.”", "사랑에 빠지지 않았더라도, 그분이라면 딱 그런 일을 할 사람이거든.\""]], [["“Then it can be no argument to prove that he is in love.", "\"그렇다면 그게 그분이 사랑에 빠졌다는 증거는 못 되겠네요."], ["But I do not think it is at all a likely thing for him to do.", "하지만 저는 그게 그분이 할 법한 일이라고는 전혀 생각하지 않아요."], ["Mr. Knightley does nothing mysteriously.”", "미스터 나이틀리는 무슨 일이든 은밀하게 하는 분이 아니거든요.\""]], [["“I have heard him lamenting her having no instrument repeatedly; oftener than I should suppose such a circumstance would, in the common course of things, occur to him.”", "\"그 애한테 악기가 없다며 그분이 거듭 한탄하는 걸 내가 들었어. 보통 같으면 그런 일이 그분 머리에 떠오를 법한 것보다 더 자주 말이야.\""]], [["“Very well; and if he had intended to give her one, he would have told her so.”", "\"좋아요. 그리고 그분이 정말 그녀에게 악기를 줄 작정이었다면, 그렇게 말씀하셨을 거예요.\""]], [["“There might be scruples of delicacy, my dear Emma.", "\"섬세한 마음에서 우러난 망설임이 있었을지도 모르지, 엠마."], ["I have a very strong notion that it comes from him.", "나는 이게 그분한테서 온 거라는 느낌이 아주 강한걸."], ["I am sure he was particularly silent when Mrs. Cole told us of it at dinner.”", "만찬 때 콜 부인이 그 이야기를 했을 때 그분이 유난히 말이 없었던 게 분명하거든.\""]], [["“You take up an idea, Mrs.", "\"부인은 어떤 생각을 붙잡으면 그걸 들고 냅다 달려가시는군요."], ["Weston, and run away with it; as you have many a time reproached me with doing.", "저더러 그런다고 여러 번 나무라셨으면서요. 저는 연정의 기미라곤 못 보겠어요."], ["I see no sign of attachment—I believe nothing of the pianoforte—and proof only shall convince me that Mr.", "피아노포르테에 관해서도 아무것도 안 믿고요."], ["Knightley has any thought of marrying Jane Fairfax.”", "미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스와 결혼할 생각을 조금이라도 품고 있다는 건, 증거가 나와야만 저를 설득할 수 있을 거예요.\""]], [["They combated the point some time longer in the same way; Emma rather gaining ground over the mind of her friend; for Mrs.", "두 사람은 한동안 더 같은 식으로 이 문제를 두고 다투었고, 엠마가 벗의 마음을 차츰 누르며 우세를 점해 갔다."], ["Weston was the most used of the two to yield; till a little bustle in the room shewed them that tea was over, and the instrument in preparation;—and at the same moment Mr.", "둘 중에서는 웨스턴 부인이 양보하는 데 더 익숙했던 것이다. 그러다 방 안의 작은 부산함이 차가 끝나고 악기가 채비를 갖추는 중임을 알렸다."], ["Cole approaching to entreat Miss Woodhouse would do them the honour of trying it.", "그와 동시에 미스터 콜이 다가와 미스 우드하우스가 한번 쳐 주시는 영광을 베풀어 달라고 간청했다."], ["Frank Churchill, of whom, in the eagerness of her conversation with Mrs. Weston, she had been seeing nothing, except that he had found a seat by Miss Fairfax, followed Mr.", "웨스턴 부인과의 대화에 열중하느라 미스 페어팩스 곁에 자리를 잡았다는 것 말고는 거의 보지 못했던 프랭크 처칠이 미스터 콜을 뒤따라와 더없이 간곡한 청을 보탰다."], ["Cole, to add his very pressing entreaties; and as, in every respect, it suited Emma best to lead, she gave a very proper compliance.", "모든 면에서 엠마가 먼저 나서는 편이 가장 알맞았던 터라, 그녀는 아주 적절하게 응했다."]], [["She knew the limitations of her own powers too well to attempt more than she could perform with credit; she wanted neither taste nor spirit in the little things which are generally acceptable, and could accompany her own voice well.", "그녀는 자기 솜씨의 한계를 너무도 잘 알기에, 떳떳하게 해낼 수 있는 것 이상은 시도하지 않았다. 대체로 사람들이 좋아하는 자잘한 곡들에서는 취향도 활기도 모자라지 않았고, 자기 목소리에 반주를 잘 맞출 줄도 알았다."], ["One accompaniment to her song took her agreeably by surprize—a second, slightly but correctly taken by Frank Churchill.", "노래에 곁들여진 한 반주가 그녀를 기분 좋게 놀라게 했으니 — 프랭크 처칠이 살짝, 그러나 정확하게 받아 넣은 두 번째 성부였다."], ["Her pardon was duly begged at the close of the song, and every thing usual followed.", "노래가 끝나자 그는 마땅히 그녀에게 용서를 구했고, 여느 때와 같은 일들이 뒤따랐다."], ["He was accused of having a delightful voice, and a perfect knowledge of music; which was properly denied; and that he knew nothing of the matter, and had no voice at all, roundly asserted.", "그는 듣기 좋은 목소리에 음악을 완벽히 안다는 핀잔을 들었고, 그것을 마땅히 부인했으며, 자기는 그 방면에 아무것도 모르고 목소리라곤 전혀 없다고 딱 잘라 우겼다."], ["They sang together once more; and Emma would then resign her place to Miss Fairfax, whose performance, both vocal and instrumental, she never could attempt to conceal from herself, was infinitely superior to her own.", "두 사람은 다시 한 번 함께 노래했다. 그러고 나서 엠마는 자리를 미스 페어팩스에게 넘겼는데, 그녀의 연주는 노래든 악기든 자기 것보다 한없이 빼어나다는 것을 엠마는 스스로에게도 도무지 감출 수가 없었다."]], [["With mixed feelings, she seated herself at a little distance from the numbers round the instrument, to listen.", "복잡한 심정으로 그녀는 악기를 둘러싼 무리에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 앉아 귀를 기울였다."], ["Frank Churchill sang again.", "프랭크 처칠이 다시 노래했다."], ["They had sung together once or twice, it appeared, at Weymouth.", "두 사람은 웨이머스에서도 한두 번 함께 노래한 적이 있는 모양이었다."], ["But the sight of Mr. Knightley among the most attentive, soon drew away half Emma’s mind; and she fell into a train of thinking on the subject of Mrs.", "그러나 미스터 나이틀리가 가장 귀 기울이는 이들 가운데 끼어 있는 광경이 곧 엠마의 마음 절반을 끌어가 버렸다."], ["Weston’s suspicions, to which the sweet sounds of the united voices gave only momentary interruptions.", "그녀는 웨스턴 부인의 의심을 두고 생각의 갈래에 빠져들었고, 어우러진 목소리의 감미로운 가락은 그저 잠깐잠깐 그 흐름을 끊을 뿐이었다."], ["Her objections to Mr. Knightley’s marrying did not in the least subside.", "미스터 나이틀리가 결혼한다는 데 대한 그녀의 반감은 조금도 가라앉지 않았다."], ["She could see nothing but evil in it.", "거기서는 해악밖에 보이지 않았다."], ["It would be a great disappointment to Mr. John Knightley; consequently to Isabella.", "그것은 미스터 존 나이틀리에게 큰 실망일 터였고, 따라서 이저벨라에게도 그러할 터였다."], ["A real injury to the children—a most mortifying change, and material loss to them all;—a very great deduction from her father’s daily comfort—and, as to herself, she could not at all endure the idea of Jane Fairfax at Donwell Abbey.", "아이들에게는 실질적인 손해 — 더없이 굴욕적인 변화이자 모두에게 막대한 손실 — 아버지의 일상의 안락에서도 큰 부분이 깎여 나갈 일이었다. 그리고 그녀 자신으로 말하자면, 제인 페어팩스가 돈웰 애비에 있다는 생각을 도무지 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["A Mrs. Knightley for them all to give way to!—No—Mr.", "자기들 모두가 자리를 비켜 줘야 할 나이틀리 부인이라니!"], ["Knightley must never marry.", "아니야 — 미스터 나이틀리는 결코 결혼해서는 안 된다."], ["Little Henry must remain the heir of Donwell.", "어린 헨리가 돈웰의 상속자로 남아 있어야 한다."]], [["Presently Mr. Knightley looked back, and came and sat down by her.", "이윽고 미스터 나이틀리가 뒤를 돌아보더니, 다가와 그녀 곁에 앉았다."], ["They talked at first only of the performance.", "두 사람은 처음에는 그저 연주 이야기만 했다. 그의 감탄은 분명 무척 따뜻했다."], ["His admiration was certainly very warm; yet she thought, but for Mrs. Weston, it would not have struck her.", "그러나 웨스턴 부인이 아니었다면 그게 마음에 걸리지도 않았으리라고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["As a sort of touchstone, however, she began to speak of his kindness in conveying the aunt and niece; and though his answer was in the spirit of cutting the matter short, she believed it to indicate only his disinclination to dwell on any kindness of his own.", "그래도 일종의 시금석 삼아, 그녀는 이모와 조카를 데려다준 그의 친절을 입에 올리기 시작했다. 그의 대답이 그 일을 짤막하게 끊으려는 투이긴 했으나, 그녀는 그것이 단지 자기 자신의 어떤 친절에 대해 길게 늘어놓기 싫어하는 마음을 나타낼 뿐이라고 믿었다."]], [["“I often feel concern,”", "\"저는 가끔 마음이 쓰여요.\""], ["said she, “that I dare not make our carriage more useful on such occasions.", "그녀가 말했다. \"이런 자리에 우리 마차를 더 요긴하게 쓰지 못하는 게요."], ["It is not that I am without the wish; but you know how impossible my father would deem it that James should put-to for such a purpose.”", "그러고 싶은 마음이 없어서가 아니에요. 하지만 그런 일로 제임스더러 말을 매라고 하는 걸 아버지가 얼마나 말도 안 되는 일로 여기시는지 아시잖아요.\""]], [["“Quite out of the question, quite out of the question,”", "\"그야 어림없는 일이지, 어림없고말고.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he replied;—“but you must often wish it, I am sure.”", "\"하지만 자네는 분명 그러고 싶을 때가 많을 거야.\""], ["And he smiled with such seeming pleasure at the conviction, that she must proceed another step.", "그러고는 그 확신이 어찌나 기쁜 듯 미소 짓던지, 그녀는 한 걸음 더 나아가야겠다 싶었다."]], [["“This present from the Campbells,”", "\"캠벨 가족이 보낸 이 선물 말인데요.\""], ["said she—“this pianoforte is very kindly given.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"이 피아노포르테는 참 친절한 선물이에요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["he replied, and without the smallest apparent embarrassment.—“But they would have done better had they given her notice of it.", "그가 대답했는데, 눈곱만큼의 당황한 기색도 없었다. \"하지만 미리 언질을 주었더라면 더 좋았을 거야."], ["Surprizes are foolish things.", "깜짝 선물이란 어리석은 짓이지."], ["The pleasure is not enhanced, and the inconvenience is often considerable.", "기쁨이 더 커지는 것도 아니고, 불편은 외려 만만찮을 때가 많거든."], ["I should have expected better judgment in Colonel Campbell.”", "캠벨 대령쯤 되면 더 나은 판단을 했을 줄 알았는데.\""]], [["From that moment, Emma could have taken her oath that Mr. Knightley had had no concern in giving the instrument.", "그 순간부터 엠마는 미스터 나이틀리가 그 악기를 주는 데 아무 관여도 하지 않았노라고 맹세라도 할 수 있었다."], ["But whether he were entirely free from peculiar attachment—whether there were no actual preference—remained a little longer doubtful.", "그러나 그가 어떤 각별한 연정에서 온전히 자유로운지 — 실제로 마음에 두는 데가 없는지 — 는 조금 더 의심스러운 채로 남았다."], ["Towards the end of Jane’s second song, her voice grew thick.", "제인의 두 번째 노래가 끝나 갈 무렵, 그녀의 목소리가 탁해졌다."]], [["“That will do,”", "\"이만하면 됐어.\""], ["said he, when it was finished, thinking aloud—“you have sung quite enough for one evening—now be quiet.”", "노래가 끝나자 그가 혼잣말처럼 말했다. \"하룻저녁에 노래는 충분히 했으니 — 이제 좀 쉬게.\""]], [["Another song, however, was soon begged for.", "그러나 곧 또 한 곡이 청해졌다. \"한 곡만 더요."], ["“One more;—they would not fatigue Miss Fairfax on any account, and would only ask for one more.”", "결코 미스 페어팩스를 지치게 할 생각은 없지만, 딱 한 곡만 더 청하겠다는 거였다.\""], ["And Frank Churchill was heard to say, “I think you could manage this without effort; the first part is so very trifling.", "그리고 프랭크 처칠이 이렇게 말하는 게 들렸다. \"이건 힘들이지 않고 하실 수 있을 거예요."], ["The strength of the song falls on the second.”", "앞부분은 아주 별것 아니거든요. 노래의 힘은 뒷부분에 실려 있고요.\""]], [["Mr. Knightley grew angry.", "미스터 나이틀리가 노여워졌다."]], [["“That fellow,”", "\"저 친구는,\""], ["said he, indignantly, “thinks of nothing but shewing off his own voice.", "그가 분개하며 말했다. \"제 목소리 자랑할 생각밖에 없군."], ["This must not be.”", "이래선 안 되지.\""], ["And touching Miss Bates, who at that moment passed near—“Miss Bates, are you mad, to let your niece sing herself hoarse in this manner?", "그러고는 마침 그 순간 가까이 지나가던 미스 베이츠를 건드리며 — \"미스 베이츠, 정신이 있으십니까, 조카가 저렇게 목이 쉬도록 노래하게 두시다니요?"], ["Go, and interfere.", "가서 좀 말리세요."], ["They have no mercy on her.”", "다들 저 애한테 인정사정이 없군요.\""]], [["Miss Bates, in her real anxiety for Jane, could hardly stay even to be grateful, before she stept forward and put an end to all farther singing.", "미스 베이츠는 제인을 향한 진정한 염려에 사로잡혀, 고맙다는 인사조차 미처 다 차리지 못한 채 앞으로 나서서 더 이상의 노래에 종지부를 찍었다."], ["Here ceased the concert part of the evening, for Miss Woodhouse and Miss Fairfax were the only young lady performers; but soon (within five minutes) the proposal of dancing—originating nobody exactly knew where—was so effectually promoted by Mr.", "여기서 그날 저녁의 음악회 순서는 끝이 났으니, 미스 우드하우스와 미스 페어팩스가 유일한 젊은 여성 연주자였던 까닭이다. 그러나 곧 (오 분도 안 되어) 정확히 어디서 비롯됐는지 아무도 모를 춤 제안이 콜 부부의 손에 어찌나 효과적으로 추진되었던지, 마땅한 자리를 내려고 모든 것이 빠르게 치워졌다."], ["and Mrs. Cole, that every thing was rapidly clearing away, to give proper space.", "컨트리 댄스에 일가견이 있는 웨스턴 부인이 피아노 앞에 자리를 잡고 거역할 수 없는 왈츠를 연주하기 시작했다."], ["Mrs. Weston, capital in her country-dances, was seated, and beginning an irresistible waltz; and Frank Churchill, coming up with most becoming gallantry to Emma, had secured her hand, and led her up to the top.", "더없이 어울리는 정중함으로 엠마에게 다가온 프랭크 처칠이 그녀의 손을 잡아 맨 앞으로 이끌었다."]], [["While waiting till the other young people could pair themselves off, Emma found time, in spite of the compliments she was receiving on her voice and her taste, to look about, and see what became of Mr. Knightley.", "다른 젊은이들이 서로 짝을 짓기를 기다리는 동안, 엠마는 자기 목소리와 취향을 두고 쏟아지는 칭찬에도 불구하고 잠깐 둘러보아 미스터 나이틀리가 어찌하는지 살필 짬을 냈다."], ["This would be a trial.", "이것이 시험이 될 터였다."], ["He was no dancer in general.", "그는 본디 춤을 추는 사람이 아니었다."], ["If he were to be very alert in engaging Jane Fairfax now, it might augur something.", "만약 지금 그가 부리나케 제인 페어팩스에게 춤을 청한다면, 무언가를 점칠 수 있을 것이었다. 당장은 그런 기미가 없었다."], ["There was no immediate appearance. No; he was talking to Mrs.", "아니, 그는 콜 부인과 이야기를 나누고 있었다 — 무심하게 구경하고 있었다."], ["Cole—he was looking on unconcerned; Jane was asked by somebody else, and he was still talking to Mrs. Cole.", "제인은 다른 누군가의 청을 받았고, 그는 여전히 콜 부인과 이야기 중이었다."]], [["Emma had no longer an alarm for Henry; his interest was yet safe; and she led off the dance with genuine spirit and enjoyment.", "엠마는 더 이상 헨리를 걱정하지 않았다. 헨리의 몫은 아직 안전했다."], ["Not more than five couple could be mustered; but the rarity and the suddenness of it made it very delightful, and she found herself well matched in a partner.", "그녀는 진정한 활기와 즐거움 속에 춤의 첫머리를 이끌었다. 다섯 쌍을 넘기지 못할 만큼이었으나, 그 드묾과 갑작스러움이 춤을 무척 즐겁게 만들었고, 자기에게 더없이 잘 맞는 짝이 있음을 그녀는 알았다."], ["They were a couple worth looking at.", "두 사람은 눈여겨볼 만한 한 쌍이었다."]], [["Two dances, unfortunately, were all that could be allowed.", "안타깝게도 허락된 것은 두 곡뿐이었다."], ["It was growing late, and Miss Bates became anxious to get home, on her mother’s account.", "밤이 깊어 가고 있었고, 미스 베이츠가 어머니 때문에 집에 돌아가기를 안달했다."], ["After some attempts, therefore, to be permitted to begin again, they were obliged to thank Mrs. Weston, look sorrowful, and have done.", "그리하여 한 번 더 시작하게 해 달라고 몇 번 청해 보다가, 그들은 웨스턴 부인에게 고마움을 표하고 아쉬운 얼굴을 지으며 그만둘 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“Perhaps it is as well,”", "\"어쩌면 이게 잘된 일인지도 몰라요.\""], ["said Frank Churchill, as he attended Emma to her carriage.", "엠마를 마차까지 바래다주며 프랭크 처칠이 말했다."], ["“I must have asked Miss Fairfax, and her languid dancing would not have agreed with me, after yours.”", "\"안 그랬으면 미스 페어팩스한테 청해야 했을 텐데, 당신 다음에 그녀의 맥없는 춤은 제 취향에 안 맞았을 테니까요.\""]], [["# Volume II — Chapter IX", "# 제2권 9장"]], [["Emma did not repent her condescension in going to the Coles.", "엠마는 콜 부부의 집에 가느라 위신을 굽힌 일을 후회하지 않았다."], ["The visit afforded her many pleasant recollections the next day; and all that she might be supposed to have lost on the side of dignified seclusion, must be amply repaid in the splendour of popularity.", "그 방문은 이튿날 그녀에게 즐거운 기억거리를 많이 안겨 주었다. 품위 있는 은둔의 측면에서 잃었다고 여겨질 만한 것이 무엇이든, 인기의 광채로 넉넉히 보상받았을 터였다."], ["She must have delighted the Coles—worthy people, who deserved to be made happy!—And left a name behind her that would not soon die away.", "그녀는 콜 부부를 — 행복해질 자격이 있는 훌륭한 사람들을 — 분명 기쁘게 해 주었으리라! 그리고 쉽사리 사라지지 않을 이름을 그곳에 남겼으리라."]], [["Perfect happiness, even in memory, is not common; and there were two points on which she was not quite easy.", "완벽한 행복이란, 기억 속에서조차 흔치 않은 법이다. 그리고 그녀에게는 영 마음 편치 않은 두 가지가 있었다."], ["She doubted whether she had not transgressed the duty of woman by woman, in betraying her suspicions of Jane Fairfax’s feelings to Frank Churchill.", "하나는, 제인 페어팩스의 마음에 대한 의심을 프랭크 처칠에게 흘려 버림으로써, 여자가 여자에게 지켜야 할 도리를 어긴 것은 아닌가 하는 점이었다."], ["It was hardly right; but it had been so strong an idea, that it would escape her, and his submission to all that she told, was a compliment to her penetration, which made it difficult for her to be quite certain that she ought to have held her tongue.", "옳은 일은 도무지 아니었다. 그러나 워낙 강하게 떠오른 생각이라 절로 새어 나가고 말았고, 자기가 들려준 말마다 그가 고분고분 수긍한 것은 그녀의 통찰력에 대한 찬사였으니, 입을 다물고 있었어야 마땅했다고 단정하기란 쉽지 않았다."]], [["The other circumstance of regret related also to Jane Fairfax; and there she had no doubt.", "후회되는 또 한 가지 일도 제인 페어팩스와 관련된 것이었는데, 이 점에 대해서는 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["She did unfeignedly and unequivocally regret the inferiority of her own playing and singing.", "자신의 연주와 노래가 한참 못 미친다는 사실을 그녀는 가식 없이, 또렷이 후회했다."], ["She did most heartily grieve over the idleness of her childhood—and sat down and practised vigorously an hour and a half.", "어린 시절을 게으르게 보낸 일을 진심으로 가슴 아파했다 — 그러고는 자리에 앉아 한 시간 반을 맹렬히 연습했다."]], [["She was then interrupted by Harriet’s coming in; and if Harriet’s praise could have satisfied her, she might soon have been comforted.", "그러다 해리엇이 들어오는 바람에 멈추게 되었다. 해리엇의 칭찬으로 만족할 수 있는 사람이었다면, 그녀는 금세 위로받았을지도 몰랐다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["if I could but play as well as you and Miss Fairfax!”", "저도 엠마 양이나 미스 페어팩스만큼만 칠 수 있다면!\""]], [["“Don’t class us together, Harriet.", "\"우리 둘을 한데 묶지 말아요, 해리엇."], ["My playing is no more like her’s, than a lamp is like sunshine.”", "내 연주는 그분 연주와는 영 달라요. 등불이 햇빛과 다른 만큼이나요.\""]], [["“Oh! dear—I think you play the best of the two.", "\"어머나, 세상에 — 제 생각엔 엠마 양이 둘 중에 더 잘 치세요."], ["I think you play quite as well as she does.", "그분만큼은 충분히 잘 치시는걸요."], ["I am sure I had much rather hear you.", "저는 정말이지 엠마 양 연주를 듣는 편이 훨씬 좋아요."], ["Every body last night said how well you played.”", "어젯밤에도 다들 엠마 양이 얼마나 잘 친다고들 했잖아요.\""]], [["“Those who knew any thing about it, must have felt the difference.", "\"조금이라도 아는 사람이라면 그 차이를 느꼈을 거예요."], ["The truth is, Harriet, that my playing is just good enough to be praised, but Jane Fairfax’s is much beyond it.”", "사실은요, 해리엇, 내 연주는 딱 칭찬받을 만큼은 되지만, 제인 페어팩스는 그보다 훨씬 위예요.\""]], [["“Well, I always shall think that you play quite as well as she does, or that if there is any difference nobody would ever find it out.", "\"글쎄요, 저는 언제까지나 엠마 양이 그분만큼 잘 치신다고 생각할 거예요."], ["Mr. Cole said how much taste you had; and Mr.", "설령 차이가 있다 해도 아무도 알아채지 못할 거고요."], ["Frank Churchill talked a great deal about your taste, and that he valued taste much more than execution.”", "미스터 콜도 엠마 양이 얼마나 취향이 좋은지 말했고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠도 엠마 양의 취향을 두고 한참 이야기하면서, 자기는 기교보다 취향을 훨씬 높이 산다고 했어요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["but Jane Fairfax has them both, Harriet.”", "하지만 제인 페어팩스는 둘 다 갖추었는걸요, 해리엇.\""]], [["“Are you sure?", "\"정말요?"], ["I saw she had execution, but I did not know she had any taste.", "그분이 기교가 있는 건 봤지만, 취향까지 있는 줄은 몰랐어요."], ["Nobody talked about it.", "그런 말은 아무도 안 했거든요."], ["And I hate Italian singing.—There is no understanding a word of it.", "게다가 저는 이탈리아 노래가 싫어요 — 한 마디도 알아들을 수가 없잖아요."], ["Besides, if she does play so very well, you know, it is no more than she is obliged to do, because she will have to teach.", "그리고 그분이 정말 그렇게 잘 친다 해도, 알다시피 그건 마땅히 해야 할 일에 지나지 않아요. 어차피 가르치는 일을 해야 할 테니까요."], ["The Coxes were wondering last night whether she would get into any great family.", "어젯밤에 콕스네 자매가 그분이 어디 좋은 집안에 들어가게 될지 궁금해하더라고요."], ["How did you think the Coxes looked?”", "엠마 양 보시기에 콕스네 자매는 어땠어요?\""]], [["“Just as they always do—very vulgar.”", "\"늘 그렇듯이지요 — 아주 천박하고요.\""]], [["“They told me something,”", "\"그 사람들이 저한테 뭘 얘기해 줬어요.\""], ["said Harriet rather hesitatingly; “but it is nothing of any consequence.”", "해리엇이 다소 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"별로 대단한 건 아니지만요.\""]], [["Emma was obliged to ask what they had told her, though fearful of its producing Mr. Elton.", "엠마는 미스터 엘턴 이야기가 나올까 두려우면서도, 그들이 무슨 이야기를 했는지 물을 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“They told me—that Mr. Martin dined with them last Saturday.”", "\"그 사람들이 그러는데 — 지난 토요일에 미스터 마틴이 자기네 집에서 저녁을 먹었대요.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"어머!\""]], [["“He came to their father upon some business, and he asked him to stay to dinner.”", "\"무슨 일로 그 집 아버지를 찾아왔는데, 저녁을 들고 가라고 붙잡으셨대요.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"어머!\""]], [["“They talked a great deal about him, especially Anne Cox.", "\"그 사람들이 그 사람 얘기를 한참 했어요. 특히 앤 콕스가요."], ["I do not know what she meant, but she asked me if I thought I should go and stay there again next summer.”", "무슨 뜻이었는지는 모르겠지만, 내년 여름에 또 그 집에 가서 지낼 생각이 있냐고 묻더라고요.\""]], [["“She meant to be impertinently curious, just as such an Anne Cox should be.”", "\"앤 콕스답게 무례하게 캐물으려던 거예요. 딱 그 앤 콕스가 할 법한 짓이죠.\""]], [["“She said he was very agreeable the day he dined there.", "\"그 사람이 저녁 먹으러 온 날 아주 싹싹했다고 그러더라고요."], ["He sat by her at dinner.", "식사 때 자기 옆에 앉았다고요."], ["Miss Nash thinks either of the Coxes would be very glad to marry him.”", "미스 내시는 콕스네 자매 둘 중 누구라도 그 사람과 기꺼이 결혼하고 싶어 할 거래요.\""]], [["“Very likely.—I think they are, without exception, the most vulgar girls in Highbury.”", "\"그럴 만하죠 — 내 생각에 그 자매는 하이베리에서 둘째가라면 서러울 만큼 천박한 아가씨들이거든요.\""]], [["Harriet had business at Ford’s.—Emma thought it most prudent to go with her.", "해리엇은 포드네 가게에 볼일이 있었다. 엠마는 함께 가는 편이 가장 신중하리라고 생각했다."], ["Another accidental meeting with the Martins was possible, and in her present state, would be dangerous.", "마틴 식구들과 또 우연히 마주칠 수도 있었고, 지금 같은 상태에서는 그것이 위험할 터였다."]], [["Harriet, tempted by every thing and swayed by half a word, was always very long at a purchase; and while she was still hanging over muslins and changing her mind, Emma went to the door for amusement.—Much could not be hoped from the traffic of even the busiest part of Highbury;—Mr.", "해리엇은 무엇에든 마음이 끌리고 한마디 말에도 이리저리 흔들리는 터라, 무엇 하나 사는 데 늘 한참이 걸렸다. 그녀가 여전히 모슬린 천을 들여다보며 마음을 바꾸고 있는 동안, 엠마는 심심풀이 삼아 문가로 갔다."], ["Perry walking hastily by, Mr. William Cox letting himself in at the office-door, Mr.", "하이베리에서 가장 번화한 곳이라 한들 그 거리의 통행에서 큰 기대를 걸 수는 없었다."], ["Cole’s carriage-horses returning from exercise, or a stray letter-boy on an obstinate mule, were the liveliest objects she could presume to expect; and when her eyes fell only on the butcher with his tray, a tidy old woman travelling homewards from shop with her full basket, two curs quarrelling over a dirty bone, and a string of dawdling children round the baker’s little bow-window eyeing the gingerbread, she knew she had no reason to complain, and was amused enough; quite enough still to stand at the door.", "미스터 페리가 바삐 지나가거나, 미스터 윌리엄 콕스가 사무실 문으로 들어가거나, 미스터 콜네 마차 말들이 운동을 마치고 돌아오거나, 고집 센 노새를 탄 떠돌이 우편 배달 소년이 지나가는 것이 그녀가 기대할 수 있는 가장 활기찬 광경이었다. 그러니 눈에 들어오는 것이라곤 쟁반을 든 푸주한, 가게에서 가득 찬 바구니를 들고 집으로 돌아가는 단정한 노파, 더러운 뼈다귀를 놓고 다투는 잡종개 두 마리, 빵집의 자그마한 활 모양 창 앞에 둘러서서 생강 빵을 곁눈질하며 어슬렁대는 아이들 한 줄뿐이었지만, 그녀는 불평할 까닭이 없음을 알았고, 그것만으로도 충분히 즐거웠다 — 여전히 문가에 서 있기에 충분할 만큼은."], ["A mind lively and at ease, can do with seeing nothing, and can see nothing that does not answer.", "생기 있고 편안한 마음이란 아무것도 보지 않고도 견딜 수 있으며, 또 무엇이든 마음에 차지 않는 것은 눈에 들어오지 않는 법이다."]], [["She looked down the Randalls road.", "그녀는 랜덜스로 이어지는 길을 내려다보았다."], ["The scene enlarged; two persons appeared; Mrs.", "풍경이 넓어지더니, 두 사람이 나타났다. 웨스턴 부인과 그녀의 의붓아들이었다."], ["Weston and her son-in-law; they were walking into Highbury;—to Hartfield of course.", "두 사람은 하이베리로 걸어 들어오는 중이었다 — 물론 하트필드로 가는 길이었으리라. 그러나 그들은 우선 베이츠 부인 댁에 들르려고 멈춰 섰다."], ["They were stopping, however, in the first place at Mrs.", "그 집은 포드네 가게보다 랜덜스에 조금 더 가까웠다. 두 사람이 막 문을 두드리려던 참에, 엠마와 눈이 마주쳤다."], ["Bates’s; whose house was a little nearer Randalls than Ford’s; and had all but knocked, when Emma caught their eye.—Immediately they crossed the road and came forward to her; and the agreeableness of yesterday’s engagement seemed to give fresh pleasure to the present meeting.", "곧장 그들은 길을 건너 그녀에게 다가왔다. 어제 모임의 유쾌함이 지금의 만남에 새삼 기쁨을 더해 주는 듯했다."], ["Mrs. Weston informed her that she was going to call on the Bateses, in order to hear the new instrument.", "웨스턴 부인은 새 악기를 들으러 베이츠 댁에 들를 참이라고 알려 주었다."]], [["“For my companion tells me,”", "\"제 길동무가 그러는데요,\""], ["said she, “that I absolutely promised Miss Bates last night, that I would come this morning.", "부인이 말했다. \"어젯밤 제가 미스 베이츠한테 오늘 아침에 꼭 오겠다고 분명히 약속했다는 거예요."], ["I was not aware of it myself.", "저는 도무지 기억이 안 나는데 말이죠."], ["I did not know that I had fixed a day, but as he says I did, I am going now.”", "날을 잡았던 줄은 몰랐어요. 하지만 이 사람이 그렇다고 하니, 지금 가는 길이랍니다.\""]], [["“And while Mrs. Weston pays her visit, I may be allowed, I hope,”", "\"웨스턴 부인이 방문하시는 동안에는,\""], ["said Frank Churchill, “to join your party and wait for her at Hartfield—if you are going home.”", "프랭크 처칠이 말했다. \"저는 미스 우드하우스 일행에 끼어, 하트필드에서 부인을 기다려도 되지 않을까요 — 집으로 가시는 길이라면요.\""]], [["Mrs. Weston was disappointed.", "웨스턴 부인은 실망했다."]], [["“I thought you meant to go with me.", "\"나는 자네도 함께 가는 줄 알았는데."], ["They would be very much pleased.”", "그 댁에서 무척 반가워할 텐데요.\""]], [["“Me!", "\"제가요!"], ["I should be quite in the way.", "저야 거치적거리기만 할 텐데요."], ["But, perhaps—I may be equally in the way here.", "하지만, 어쩌면 — 여기서도 마찬가지로 거치적거릴지 모르겠군요."], ["Miss Woodhouse looks as if she did not want me.", "미스 우드하우스는 저를 원치 않는 눈치인데요."], ["My aunt always sends me off when she is shopping.", "제 외숙모님도 물건을 고르실 때면 늘 저를 내쫓으세요."], ["She says I fidget her to death; and Miss Woodhouse looks as if she could almost say the same.", "제가 옆에서 안달하게 만들어 죽을 지경이라고요. 그런데 미스 우드하우스도 똑같은 말을 하고 싶은 얼굴인걸요."], ["What am I to do?”", "제가 어찌해야 할까요?\""]], [["“I am here on no business of my own,”", "\"저는 제 볼일로 여기 있는 게 아니에요.\""], ["said Emma; “I am only waiting for my friend.", "엠마가 말했다. \"그저 친구를 기다리고 있을 뿐이죠."], ["She will probably have soon done, and then we shall go home.", "곧 끝날 테고, 그러면 우리도 집으로 갈 거예요."], ["But you had better go with Mrs. Weston and hear the instrument.”", "하지만 웨스턴 부인과 함께 가서 악기를 들어 보시는 편이 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Well—if you advise it.—But (with a smile) if Colonel Campbell should have employed a careless friend, and if it should prove to have an indifferent tone—what shall I say?", "\"그럼 — 그렇게 권하신다면요. 하지만 (미소를 지으며) 만약 캠벨 대령이 부주의한 친구한테 일을 맡긴 거라면, 그래서 그 악기 소리가 영 시원치 않은 것으로 드러나면 — 제가 뭐라고 해야 할까요?"], ["I shall be no support to Mrs. Weston.", "저는 웨스턴 부인께 아무 도움이 못 될 텐데요."], ["She might do very well by herself.", "부인이야 혼자서도 거뜬하실 거예요."], ["A disagreeable truth would be palatable through her lips, but I am the wretchedest being in the world at a civil falsehood.”", "거북한 진실도 부인의 입을 거치면 듣기 좋게 들리겠지만, 저는 예의 바른 거짓말에 관한 한 세상에서 가장 한심한 인간이거든요.\""]], [["“I do not believe any such thing,”", "\"저는 그런 말은 도통 안 믿어요.\" 엠마가 받았다."], ["replied Emma.—“I am persuaded that you can be as insincere as your neighbours, when it is necessary; but there is no reason to suppose the instrument is indifferent.", "\"당신도 필요할 때면 이웃들만큼은 충분히 진실하지 못할 수 있다고 확신해요. 하지만 그 악기가 시원치 않다고 볼 까닭은 없어요."], ["Quite otherwise indeed, if I understood Miss Fairfax’s opinion last night.”", "오히려 정반대죠 — 어젯밤 미스 페어팩스의 의견을 제대로 알아들었다면 말이에요.\""]], [["“Do come with me,” said Mrs.", "\"같이 가요.\""], ["Weston, “if it be not very disagreeable to you.", "웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"정 싫지만 않다면요."], ["It need not detain us long.", "오래 잡아 두지는 않을 거예요."], ["We will go to Hartfield afterwards.", "그러고 나서 하트필드로 가면 되죠."], ["We will follow them to Hartfield.", "그분들을 따라 하트필드로 가요."], ["I really wish you to call with me.", "정말이지 자네가 나와 함께 가 주면 좋겠어요."], ["It will be felt so great an attention!", "큰 정성으로 여겨질 거예요!"], ["and I always thought you meant it.”", "게다가 나는 줄곧 자네가 그럴 작정인 줄로만 알았어요.\""]], [["He could say no more; and with the hope of Hartfield to reward him, returned with Mrs.", "그는 더는 할 말이 없었다."], ["Weston to Mrs. Bates’s door.", "하트필드라는 보상의 희망을 안고, 웨스턴 부인과 함께 베이츠 부인 댁 문으로 되돌아갔다."], ["Emma watched them in, and then joined Harriet at the interesting counter,—trying, with all the force of her own mind, to convince her that if she wanted plain muslin it was of no use to look at figured; and that a blue ribbon, be it ever so beautiful, would still never match her yellow pattern.", "엠마는 그들이 들어가는 것을 지켜본 뒤, 흥미진진한 계산대 앞에서 해리엇과 합류했다 — 무지 모슬린이 필요하다면 무늬 있는 것을 들여다봐야 소용없다는 것을, 또 파란 리본이 제아무리 곱다 한들 노란 무늬에는 도무지 어울리지 않으리라는 것을, 온 정신을 다해 그녀에게 납득시키려 애쓰면서."], ["At last it was all settled, even to the destination of the parcel.", "마침내 모든 것이, 심지어 꾸러미를 어디로 보낼지까지 정해졌다."]], [["“Should I send it to Mrs.", "\"고더드 부인 댁으로 보내 드릴까요, 아가씨?\""], ["Goddard’s, ma’am?”", "미시즈 포드가 물었다."], ["asked Mrs. Ford.—“Yes—no—yes, to Mrs.", "\"네 — 아니요 — 네, 고더드 부인 댁으로요."], ["Goddard’s. Only my pattern gown is at Hartfield.", "다만 제 견본 드레스가 하트필드에 있어서요."], ["No, you shall send it to Hartfield, if you please. But then, Mrs.", "아니, 하트필드로 보내 주세요, 괜찮으시다면요."], ["Goddard will want to see it.—And I could take the pattern gown home any day.", "그런데 그러면 고더드 부인이 보고 싶어 하실 텐데요. 견본 드레스야 아무 날에나 집으로 가져갈 수 있고요."], ["But I shall want the ribbon directly—so it had better go to Hartfield—at least the ribbon.", "하지만 리본은 당장 필요하니까 — 그러니 하트필드로 보내는 게 낫겠어요 — 적어도 리본은요."], ["You could make it into two parcels, Mrs. Ford, could not you?”", "미시즈 포드, 두 꾸러미로 나눠 주실 수 있죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“It is not worth while, Harriet, to give Mrs. Ford the trouble of two parcels.”", "\"미시즈 포드한테 두 꾸러미로 만드는 수고를 끼칠 것까지야 없잖아요, 해리엇.\""]], [["“No more it is.”", "\"그러게요, 그럴 것까지야 없죠.\""]], [["“No trouble in the world, ma’am,”", "\"수고랄 것도 없답니다, 아가씨.\""], ["said the obliging Mrs. Ford.", "친절한 미시즈 포드가 말했다."]], [["“Oh! but indeed I would much rather have it only in one.", "\"아유, 하지만 정말이지 한 꾸러미로만 받는 편이 훨씬 좋겠어요."], ["Then, if you please, you shall send it all to Mrs. Goddard’s—I do not know—No, I think, Miss Woodhouse, I may just as well have it sent to Hartfield, and take it home with me at night.", "그럼, 괜찮으시다면, 전부 고더드 부인 댁으로 보내 주세요 — 잘 모르겠네 — 아니, 제 생각엔, 미스 우드하우스, 그냥 하트필드로 보내게 하고 밤에 제가 집으로 가져가는 게 나을 것 같기도 하고요."], ["What do you advise?”", "어떻게 하는 게 좋을까요?\""]], [["“That you do not give another half-second to the subject.", "\"이 일에 단 반 초도 더 쓰지 않는 게 좋겠어요."], ["To Hartfield, if you please, Mrs. Ford.”", "하트필드로 보내 주세요, 미시즈 포드.\""]], [["“Aye, that will be much best,”", "\"맞아요, 그게 훨씬 낫겠어요.\" 해리엇이 아주 만족스러운 듯 말했다."], ["said Harriet, quite satisfied, “I should not at all like to have it sent to Mrs. Goddard’s.”", "\"고더드 부인 댁으로 보내는 건 저도 영 마음에 안 들었거든요.\""]], [["Voices approached the shop—or rather one voice and two ladies: Mrs.", "목소리들이 가게로 다가왔다 — 아니, 목소리는 하나에 부인은 둘이라 해야 옳았다."], ["Weston and Miss Bates met them at the door.", "웨스턴 부인과 미스 베이츠가 문간에서 그들과 마주쳤다."]], [["“My dear Miss Woodhouse,”", "\"미스 우드하우스, 이게 웬일이에요.\" 미스 베이츠가 말했다."], ["said the latter, “I am just run across to entreat the favour of you to come and sit down with us a little while, and give us your opinion of our new instrument; you and Miss Smith.", "\"저는 그저 부탁을 드리려고 부랴부랴 건너온 거예요. 잠깐만이라도 저희와 함께 앉아서, 새 악기에 대한 의견을 좀 들려주십사 하고요."], ["How do you do, Miss Smith?—Very well I thank you.—And I begged Mrs.", "미스 우드하우스도, 미스 스미스도요. 안녕하세요, 미스 스미스?"], ["Weston to come with me, that I might be sure of succeeding.”", "덕분에 아주 잘 지냅니다. 그리고 제가 꼭 모셔 가고 싶어서, 웨스턴 부인께도 같이 가 달라고 부탁드렸지요.\""]], [["“I hope Mrs. Bates and Miss Fairfax are—”", "\"베이츠 부인과 미스 페어팩스도 모두 안녕하시기를 —\""]], [["“Very well, I am much obliged to you.", "\"아주 잘 지내요, 정말 고마워요."], ["My mother is delightfully well; and Jane caught no cold last night.", "저희 어머니는 더없이 정정하시고요. 제인도 어젯밤에 감기 한 번 안 들었답니다."], ["How is Mr.", "미스터 우드하우스는 어떠세요?"], ["Woodhouse?—I am so glad to hear such a good account.", "그렇게 좋으시다니 정말 기뻐요. 웨스턴 부인이 미스 우드하우스가 여기 계시다고 하시기에 — 아!"], ["Mrs. Weston told me you were here.—Oh!", "그래서 제가 그랬죠, 그럼 얼른 건너가야겠다고요."], ["then, said I, I must run across, I am sure Miss Woodhouse will allow me just to run across and entreat her to come in; my mother will be so very happy to see her—and now we are such a nice party, she cannot refuse.—‘Aye, pray do,’ said Mr.", "미스 우드하우스라면 분명 제가 잠깐 건너가서 들어와 주십사 부탁드리는 걸 허락해 주실 거라고요. 저희 어머니가 보시면 얼마나 기뻐하실까 — 게다가 지금 이렇게 좋은 분들이 다 모이셨으니 거절하실 리가 없다고요."], ["Frank Churchill, ‘Miss Woodhouse’s opinion of the instrument will be worth having.’—But, said I, I shall be more sure of succeeding if one of you will go with me.—‘Oh,’ said he, ‘wait half a minute, till I have finished my job;’—For, would you believe it, Miss Woodhouse, there he is, in the most obliging manner in the world, fastening in the rivet of my mother’s spectacles.—The rivet came out, you know, this morning.—So very obliging!—For my mother had no use of her spectacles—could not put them on.", "'아, 부디 그러시지요.' 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 그러더군요."], ["And, by the bye, every body ought to have two pair of spectacles; they should indeed.", "'미스 우드하우스가 악기를 들고 내려 주실 의견이라면 들을 만한 가치가 있을 겁니다.' 그래서 제가 그랬죠, 그래도 두 분 중 한 분이 같이 가 주시면 더 확실할 것 같다고요."], ["Jane said so.", "'아,' 그러고는 그러더라고요."], ["I meant to take them over to John Saunders the first thing I did, but something or other hindered me all the morning; first one thing, then another, there is no saying what, you know.", "'잠깐만 기다려 주세요, 이 일만 마치고요.' 글쎄, 믿으시겠어요, 미스 우드하우스, 그 사람이 세상에 둘도 없이 싹싹하게, 저희 어머니 안경의 대갈못을 끼워 넣고 있지 뭐예요."], ["At one time Patty came to say she thought the kitchen chimney wanted sweeping.", "오늘 아침에 그 대갈못이 빠졌거든요. 어찌나 친절한지!"], ["Oh, said I, Patty do not come with your bad news to me.", "어머니가 안경을 못 쓰셔서 — 쓸 수가 없으셨거든요. 그러고 보니, 사람이라면 누구나 안경을 두 개씩은 가지고 있어야 해요."], ["Here is the rivet of your mistress’s spectacles out.", "정말 그래야 한다니까요. 제인도 그렇게 말하더라고요."], ["Then the baked apples came home, Mrs.", "저는 무엇보다 먼저 그걸 존 손더스한테 가져갈 생각이었는데, 아침내 이런저런 일이 자꾸 가로막더라고요. 처음엔 이 일, 다음엔 저 일, 뭐라 말할 수도 없을 만큼요."], ["Wallis sent them by her boy; they are extremely civil and obliging to us, the Wallises, always—I have heard some people say that Mrs.", "한번은 패티가 와서 부엌 굴뚝을 청소해야 할 것 같다고 하더라고요. 아유, 그래서 제가 그랬죠, 패티, 그런 안 좋은 소식은 나한테 가져오지 말라고요."], ["Wallis can be uncivil and give a very rude answer, but we have never known any thing but the greatest attention from them.", "지금 마님 안경 대갈못이 빠졌다고요. 그러고 나니 구운 사과가 왔어요."], ["And it cannot be for the value of our custom now, for what is our consumption of bread, you know?", "미시즈 월리스가 자기 집 아이 편에 보냈더라고요. 그 월리스네 식구들은 우리한테 늘 더없이 정중하고 친절하답니다."], ["Only three of us.—besides dear Jane at present—and she really eats nothing—makes such a shocking breakfast, you would be quite frightened if you saw it.", "더러 미시즈 월리스가 무례할 때도 있고 아주 퉁명스럽게 대꾸한다는 말을 하는 사람들도 있던데, 우리는 그 댁한테서 더없는 정성 말고는 받아 본 적이 없어요. 그게 우리 단골 덕을 보려는 것일 리도 없는 게, 우리가 빵을 먹어 봐야 얼마나 먹겠어요?"], ["I dare not let my mother know how little she eats—so I say one thing and then I say another, and it passes off.", "셋뿐인데요. 게다가 지금은 사랑하는 제인까지 와 있지만 — 그 애는 정말 아무것도 안 먹어요."], ["But about the middle of the day she gets hungry, and there is nothing she likes so well as these baked apples, and they are extremely wholesome, for I took the opportunity the other day of asking Mr.", "아침을 얼마나 형편없이 드는지, 보시면 깜짝 놀라실 거예요. 어머니한테는 그 애가 얼마나 적게 먹는지 차마 알릴 수가 없어서 — 이 말 했다 저 말 했다 하면서 슬쩍 넘겨요."], ["Perry; I happened to meet him in the street.", "그래도 한낮쯤 되면 배가 고파지는데, 그 애가 이 구운 사과만큼 좋아하는 게 없거든요."], ["Not that I had any doubt before—I have so often heard Mr.", "게다가 더없이 몸에 좋고요. 며칠 전에 마침 길에서 미스터 페리를 만나 물어볼 기회가 있었답니다."], ["Woodhouse recommend a baked apple. I believe it is the only way that Mr.", "전에도 의심한 적은 없었지만요 — 미스터 우드하우스가 구운 사과를 권하시는 걸 하도 자주 들었으니까요."], ["Woodhouse thinks the fruit thoroughly wholesome.", "미스터 우드하우스는 사과를 정말 몸에 좋게 만드는 방법은 그것뿐이라고 여기시는 것 같아요."], ["We have apple-dumplings, however, very often.", "그래도 우리는 사과 경단도 아주 자주 먹어요."], ["Patty makes an excellent apple-dumpling.", "패티가 사과 경단을 기가 막히게 만들거든요."], ["Well, Mrs. Weston, you have prevailed, I hope, and these ladies will oblige us.”", "자, 웨스턴 부인, 부인이 잘 설득해 주셨으니, 이분들도 와 주시겠지요.\""]], [["Emma would be “very happy to wait on Mrs. Bates, &c.,”", "엠마는 \"기꺼이 베이츠 부인을 뵈러 가겠다는 둥\""], ["and they did at last move out of the shop, with no farther delay from Miss Bates than,", "응했고, 마침내 일행은 가게에서 나섰다. 미스 베이츠가 다음과 같은 인사로 잠시 더 머문 것 말고는 더 지체할 일도 없었다."]], [["“How do you do, Mrs. Ford?", "\"안녕하세요, 미시즈 포드?"], ["I beg your pardon.", "어머, 미안해요."], ["I did not see you before.", "아까는 못 봤네요."], ["I hear you have a charming collection of new ribbons from town.", "읍내에서 매력적인 새 리본들을 잔뜩 들여놓으셨다면서요."], ["Jane came back delighted yesterday.", "제인이 어제 아주 기뻐하며 돌아왔답니다."], ["Thank ye, the gloves do very well—only a little too large about the wrist; but Jane is taking them in.”", "고마워요, 장갑은 아주 잘 맞아요 — 손목께가 살짝 큰 것뿐인데, 제인이 줄이고 있어요.\""]], [["“What was I talking of?”", "\"내가 무슨 얘기를 하고 있었더라?\""], ["said she, beginning again when they were all in the street.", "모두 거리로 나서자, 미스 베이츠가 다시 입을 열며 말했다."]], [["Emma wondered on what, of all the medley, she would fix.", "엠마는 그 온갖 잡다한 이야기 가운데 그녀가 무엇을 붙들지 궁금했다."]], [["“I declare I cannot recollect what I was talking of.—Oh!", "\"아무리 해도 무슨 얘기를 하던 중이었는지 기억이 안 나네요."], ["my mother’s spectacles.", "아! 어머니 안경 말이지."], ["So very obliging of Mr.", "미스터 프랭크 처칠이 어찌나 싹싹하던지!"], ["Frank Churchill!", "'아,' 그러더라고요."], ["‘Oh!’ said he, ‘I do think I can fasten the rivet; I like a job of this kind excessively.’—Which you know shewed him to be so very....", "'제가 대갈못을 끼울 수 있을 것 같습니다. 저는 이런 일을 무척 좋아하거든요.'"], ["Indeed I must say that, much as I had heard of him before and much as I had expected, he very far exceeds any thing....", "그게 말이죠, 그 사람이 얼마나 그렇게나... 정말이지 이 말은 해야겠어요, 전부터 그 사람에 대해 들은 것도 많고 기대한 것도 컸지만, 그 사람은 그 모든 걸 한참 뛰어넘는다니까요..."], ["I do congratulate you, Mrs. Weston, most warmly.", "웨스턴 부인, 진심으로 축하드려요, 더없이 따뜻한 마음으로요."], ["He seems every thing the fondest parent could....", "그 사람은 가장 자상한 부모라면 무엇이든... '아,'"], ["‘Oh!’ said he, ‘I can fasten the rivet.", "그러더라고요. '제가 대갈못을 끼울 수 있겠는데요."], ["I like a job of that sort excessively.’ I never shall forget his manner.", "저는 이런 일을 무척 좋아하거든요.' 그 태도를 저는 절대로 잊지 못할 거예요."], ["And when I brought out the baked apples from the closet, and hoped our friends would be so very obliging as to take some, ‘Oh!’ said he directly, ‘there is nothing in the way of fruit half so good, and these are the finest-looking home-baked apples I ever saw in my life.’ That, you know, was so very....", "그리고 제가 벽장에서 구운 사과를 꺼내 와서, 우리 친구분들이 좀 들어 주시면 정말 고맙겠다고 했더니, '아,' 그가 곧바로 그러더라고요."], ["And I am sure, by his manner, it was no compliment.", "'과일 중에 이만큼 맛있는 건 없지요. 게다가 이건 제가 평생 본 집에서 구운 사과 가운데 가장 보기 좋은 것들인데요.'"], ["Indeed they are very delightful apples, and Mrs.", "그게 말이죠, 정말이지 그렇게나... 그리고 그 태도로 보아 빈말이 아니었던 게 분명해요."], ["Wallis does them full justice—only we do not have them baked more than twice, and Mr.", "사실 정말 더없이 맛있는 사과랍니다. 미시즈 월리스가 솜씨를 제대로 발휘해 주거든요 — 다만 우리는 두 번 넘게는 굽지 않는데, 미스터 우드하우스가 세 번은 구우라고 우리한테 약속을 시키셨지요 — 하지만 미스 우드하우스가 그 얘기는 입 밖에 안 내 주실 만큼 인자하시겠지요."], ["Woodhouse made us promise to have them done three times—but Miss Woodhouse will be so good as not to mention it.", "사과 자체가 굽기에는 더없이 좋은 품종인 게 틀림없어요. 전부 돈웰에서 온 거예요 — 미스터 나이틀리가 후하게 보내 주신 것 가운데 일부랍니다."], ["The apples themselves are the very finest sort for baking, beyond a doubt; all from Donwell—some of Mr.", "해마다 한 자루씩 보내 주시거든요. 그리고 정말이지 그 댁 나무에서 나는 사과처럼 오래가는 사과는 어디에도 없을 거예요 — 그런 나무가 두 그루 있나 봐요."], ["Knightley’s most liberal supply.", "어머니 말씀이, 그 과수원은 당신 젊으셨을 적부터 늘 유명했다더군요."], ["He sends us a sack every year; and certainly there never was such a keeping apple anywhere as one of his trees—I believe there is two of them.", "그런데 며칠 전에는 정말 깜짝 놀랐지 뭐예요 — 미스터 나이틀리가 어느 날 아침에 들르셨는데, 마침 제인이 그 사과를 먹고 있었거든요. 그래서 사과 얘기가 나와서 그 애가 얼마나 잘 먹는지 말했더니, 우리 사과가 이제 다 떨어지지 않았느냐고 물으시더라고요."], ["My mother says the orchard was always famous in her younger days.", "'분명 그러실 텐데요.' 그러시면서, '제가 좀 더 보내 드리지요."], ["But I was really quite shocked the other day—for Mr.", "저는 도저히 다 못 쓸 만큼 많이 가지고 있으니까요."], ["Knightley called one morning, and Jane was eating these apples, and we talked about them and said how much she enjoyed them, and he asked whether we were not got to the end of our stock.", "윌리엄 라킨스가 올해는 평소보다 더 많이 남겨 두게 해 주었거든요. 못 쓰게 되기 전에 좀 더 보내 드리겠습니다.'"], ["‘I am sure you must be,’ said he, ‘and I will send you another supply; for I have a great many more than I can ever use.", "그래서 제가 제발 그러지 마시라고 했죠 — 사실 우리 사과가 다 떨어졌다고 단정해서 말할 수는 없었거든요 — 정말 여섯 개밖에 안 남았지만요. 그래도 그건 다 제인 몫으로 둘 거고요."], ["William Larkins let me keep a larger quantity than usual this year.", "게다가 그분이 이미 그렇게 후하게 주셨는데 더 보내 주시게 하는 건 도무지 견딜 수가 없었어요. 제인도 똑같이 말하더라고요."], ["I will send you some more, before they get good for nothing.’ So I begged he would not—for really as to ours being gone, I could not absolutely say that we had a great many left—it was but half a dozen indeed; but they should be all kept for Jane; and I could not at all bear that he should be sending us more, so liberal as he had been already; and Jane said the same.", "그러고 그분이 가신 뒤에 그 애가 저랑 거의 다툴 뻔했지 뭐예요 — 아니, 다퉜다고 하면 안 되겠네요, 우리는 평생 다툰 적이 없으니까요. 하지만 제가 사과가 거의 다 떨어졌다고 털어놓은 걸 그 애가 무척 속상해했어요."], ["And when he was gone, she almost quarrelled with me—No, I should not say quarrelled, for we never had a quarrel in our lives; but she was quite distressed that I had owned the apples were so nearly gone; she wished I had made him believe we had a great many left.", "우리한테 아직 많이 남은 줄로 그분이 믿게 했어야 했다는 거예요. 아유, 그래서 제가 그랬죠, 얘야, 나도 할 수 있는 만큼은 말했단다, 하고요."], ["Oh, said I, my dear, I did say as much as I could.", "그런데 바로 그날 저녁에 윌리엄 라킨스가 사과를 큰 바구니로 한가득 들고 건너왔지 뭐예요."], ["However, the very same evening William Larkins came over with a large basket of apples, the same sort of apples, a bushel at least, and I was very much obliged, and went down and spoke to William Larkins and said every thing, as you may suppose.", "똑같은 품종으로, 적어도 한 부셸은 됐어요. 그래서 제가 정말 고마워서 내려가 윌리엄 라킨스와 이야기를 나누며 별별 인사를 다 했지요, 짐작이 가실 거예요."], ["William Larkins is such an old acquaintance!", "윌리엄 라킨스는 정말 오랜 지인이거든요!"], ["I am always glad to see him.", "저는 늘 그 사람 보는 게 반가워요."], ["But, however, I found afterwards from Patty, that William said it was all the apples of that sort his master had; he had brought them all—and now his master had not one left to bake or boil.", "그런데 나중에 패티한테 들으니, 윌리엄이 그러는데 그게 자기 주인 댁에 있던 그 품종 사과 전부였대요. 죄다 가져온 거라고요 — 이제 주인 댁에는 굽거나 삶을 사과가 한 개도 안 남았다는 거예요."], ["William did not seem to mind it himself, he was so pleased to think his master had sold so many; for William, you know, thinks more of his master’s profit than any thing; but Mrs.", "윌리엄 본인은 별로 개의치 않는 눈치였대요. 주인이 그렇게 많이 팔았다는 게 좋았던 거죠."], ["Hodges, he said, was quite displeased at their being all sent away.", "윌리엄은 알다시피 무엇보다 주인 이익을 먼저 생각하는 사람이거든요."], ["She could not bear that her master should not be able to have another apple-tart this spring.", "그래도 미시즈 호지스는 사과를 죄다 보내 버린 걸 무척 못마땅해하더래요. 주인이 올봄에 사과 타르트를 한 번도 더 못 드시게 된 걸 견딜 수 없어 했다는 거예요."], ["He told Patty this, but bid her not mind it, and be sure not to say any thing to us about it, for Mrs.", "윌리엄이 패티한테 그 얘기를 했지만, 신경 쓰지 말라고, 우리한테는 절대 한마디도 하지 말라고 당부했대요."], ["Hodges would be cross sometimes, and as long as so many sacks were sold, it did not signify who ate the remainder.", "미시즈 호지스가 더러 심통을 부리긴 해도, 그렇게 많은 자루를 팔았으니 남은 걸 누가 먹든 무슨 상관이냐고요."], ["And so Patty told me, and I was excessively shocked indeed!", "그래서 패티가 저한테 그 얘기를 했는데, 저는 정말이지 너무 놀랐지 뭐예요!"], ["I would not have Mr. Knightley know any thing about it for the world!", "미스터 나이틀리가 이 일을 아시게 되는 건 무슨 일이 있어도 싫어요!"], ["He would be so very....", "그분이 얼마나 그렇게..."], ["I wanted to keep it from Jane’s knowledge; but, unluckily, I had mentioned it before I was aware.”", "저는 제인한테도 알리지 않으려 했는데, 공교롭게도 미처 깨닫기 전에 그만 말해 버렸지 뭐예요.\""]], [["Miss Bates had just done as Patty opened the door; and her visitors walked upstairs without having any regular narration to attend to, pursued only by the sounds of her desultory good-will.", "미스 베이츠는 패티가 문을 여는 바로 그 순간에 마침 이야기를 끝낸 참이었다. 그래서 손님들은 더 따라가 들을 만한 변변한 이야기 없이 계단을 올라갔다. 다만 그녀의 두서없는 호의의 소리만이 뒤를 쫓아왔다."]], [["“Pray take care, Mrs. Weston, there is a step at the turning.", "\"부디 조심하세요, 웨스턴 부인, 모퉁이에 계단이 하나 있어요."], ["Pray take care, Miss Woodhouse, ours is rather a dark staircase—rather darker and narrower than one could wish.", "부디 조심하세요, 미스 우드하우스, 저희 집 계단이 좀 어두워서요 — 바랄 수 있는 것보다 좀 더 어둡고 좁답니다."], ["Miss Smith, pray take care.", "미스 스미스, 부디 조심하세요."], ["Miss Woodhouse, I am quite concerned, I am sure you hit your foot.", "미스 우드하우스, 정말 걱정되네요, 발을 부딪치신 게 분명해요."], ["Miss Smith, the step at the turning.”", "미스 스미스, 모퉁이에 계단 있어요.\""]], [["# Volume II — Chapter X", "# 제2권 10장"]], [["The appearance of the little sitting-room as they entered, was tranquillity itself; Mrs.", "그들이 들어섰을 때 작은 거실의 풍경은 평온함 그 자체였다."], ["Bates, deprived of her usual employment, slumbering on one side of the fire, Frank Churchill, at a table near her, most deedily occupied about her spectacles, and Jane Fairfax, standing with her back to them, intent on her pianoforte.", "베이츠 부인은 여느 때의 소일거리를 빼앗긴 채 난롯불 한쪽에서 졸고 있었고, 프랭크 처칠은 그 곁의 탁자에서 부인의 안경을 손보느라 더없이 골몰해 있었으며, 제인 페어팩스는 그들에게 등을 돌린 채 서서 자기 피아노에 마음을 쏟고 있었다."]], [["Busy as he was, however, the young man was yet able to shew a most happy countenance on seeing Emma again.", "그러나 그 청년은 그렇게 분주한 와중에도 엠마를 다시 본 순간 더없이 흐뭇한 낯빛을 지어 보일 줄 알았다."]], [["“This is a pleasure,”", "\"이거 반가운데요.\""], ["said he, in rather a low voice, “coming at least ten minutes earlier than I had calculated.", "그가 다소 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"제가 어림잡은 것보다 적어도 십 분은 일찍 오셨군요."], ["You find me trying to be useful; tell me if you think I shall succeed.”", "보시다시피 저는 쓸모 있는 사람이 되어 보려 애쓰는 중입니다. 성공할 것 같은지 한번 말씀해 주시죠.\""]], [["“What!” said Mrs.", "\"아니,\""], ["Weston, “have not you finished it yet?", "웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"아직도 못 끝냈어요?"], ["you would not earn a very good livelihood as a working silversmith at this rate.”", "이런 속도라면 은세공 직공 노릇으로는 입에 풀칠도 못 하겠는걸요.\""]], [["“I have not been working uninterruptedly,”", "\"쉬지 않고 작업한 건 아닙니다.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["he replied, “I have been assisting Miss Fairfax in trying to make her instrument stand steadily, it was not quite firm; an unevenness in the floor, I believe.", "\"미스 페어팩스가 악기를 안정되게 세우려는 걸 거들고 있었거든요. 영 단단히 서질 않더군요."], ["You see we have been wedging one leg with paper.", "바닥이 고르지 못한 탓인 것 같습니다. 보시다시피 다리 하나를 종이로 괴어 보고 있었어요."], ["This was very kind of you to be persuaded to come.", "이렇게 청에 응해 와 주시다니 참 친절하시군요."], ["I was almost afraid you would be hurrying home.”", "그냥 서둘러 댁으로 가 버리시지나 않을까 은근히 걱정했거든요.\""]], [["He contrived that she should be seated by him; and was sufficiently employed in looking out the best baked apple for her, and trying to make her help or advise him in his work, till Jane Fairfax was quite ready to sit down to the pianoforte again. That she was not immediately ready, Emma did suspect to arise from the state of her nerves; she had not yet possessed the instrument long enough to touch it without emotion; she must reason herself into the power of performance; and Emma could not but pity such feelings, whatever their origin, and could not but resolve never to expose them to her neighbour again.", "그는 엠마를 자기 곁에 앉게끔 해 놓고, 그녀에게 가장 잘 구워진 사과를 골라 주랴, 작업을 거들거나 조언해 달라고 청하랴, 제인 페어팩스가 다시 피아노 앞에 앉을 채비가 완전히 될 때까지 줄곧 그 일에 매달려 있었다. 제인이 곧바로 채비가 되지 못하는 것은 그녀의 신경 상태 탓이라고 엠마는 짐작했다. 그녀는 아직 그 악기를 손에 넣은 지 얼마 되지 않아 감정 없이는 건반에 손을 댈 수 없었던 것이다. 마음을 다잡아야만 연주할 힘이 생기는 것이었다. 그 까닭이 무엇이든, 엠마는 그런 감정을 가엾이 여기지 않을 수 없었고, 다시는 그것을 이웃 앞에 드러나게 하지 않으리라 다짐하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["At last Jane began, and though the first bars were feebly given, the powers of the instrument were gradually done full justice to.", "마침내 제인이 시작했고, 처음 몇 마디는 힘없이 짚였지만, 차츰 그 악기의 능력이 온전히 발휘되었다. 웨스턴 부인은 아까도 흡족해했고, 다시금 흡족해했다."], ["Mrs.", "엠마도 그 찬사에 모두 동참했다."], ["Weston had been delighted before, and was delighted again; Emma joined her in all her praise; and the pianoforte, with every proper discrimination, was pronounced to be altogether of the highest promise.", "그리하여 그 피아노는 안목 있는 평가를 두루 거쳐, 더할 나위 없이 촉망되는 물건이라는 판정을 받았다."]], [["“Whoever Colonel Campbell might employ,”", "\"캠벨 대령께서 누구에게 일을 맡기셨든,\""], ["said Frank Churchill, with a smile at Emma, “the person has not chosen ill.", "프랭크 처칠이 엠마를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그 사람의 선택은 잘못되지 않았군요."], ["I heard a good deal of Colonel Campbell’s taste at Weymouth; and the softness of the upper notes I am sure is exactly what he and all that party would particularly prize.", "저는 웨이머스에서 캠벨 대령의 안목에 관해 적잖이 들었습니다. 그리고 이 높은 음들의 부드러움은 분명 대령과 그 일행 모두가 특히 귀하게 여길 만한 바로 그것이지요."], ["I dare say, Miss Fairfax, that he either gave his friend very minute directions, or wrote to Broadwood himself.", "미스 페어팩스, 짐작건대 대령께서는 자기 친구에게 아주 세세한 지시를 내리셨거나, 아니면 직접 브로드우드사에 편지를 쓰셨겠지요."], ["Do not you think so?”", "그렇게 생각하지 않으십니까?\""]], [["Jane did not look round.", "제인은 돌아보지 않았다."], ["She was not obliged to hear.", "들어야 할 의무는 없었다."], ["Mrs. Weston had been speaking to her at the same moment.", "마침 같은 순간에 웨스턴 부인이 그녀에게 말을 걸고 있었으니까."]], [["“It is not fair,”", "\"그건 공정하지 않아요.\""], ["said Emma, in a whisper; “mine was a random guess.", "엠마가 속삭였다. \"제 건 그저 되는대로 해 본 짐작이었어요."], ["Do not distress her.”", "저 사람을 괴롭히지 마세요.\""]], [["He shook his head with a smile, and looked as if he had very little doubt and very little mercy.", "그는 미소를 머금고 고개를 가로저었는데, 의심도 거의 없고 자비도 거의 없는 얼굴이었다."], ["Soon afterwards he began again,", "곧이어 그가 다시 시작했다."]], [["“How much your friends in Ireland must be enjoying your pleasure on this occasion, Miss Fairfax.", "\"미스 페어팩스, 아일랜드에 있는 당신 벗들이 이번 일에 당신이 느낄 기쁨을 얼마나 함께 즐기고 있을까요."], ["I dare say they often think of you, and wonder which will be the day, the precise day of the instrument’s coming to hand.", "분명 그분들은 당신 생각을 자주 하면서, 악기가 도착하는 날이 언제일까, 바로 그날이 언제일까 궁금해할 겁니다."], ["Do you imagine Colonel Campbell knows the business to be going forward just at this time?—Do you imagine it to be the consequence of an immediate commission from him, or that he may have sent only a general direction, an order indefinite as to time, to depend upon contingencies and conveniences?”", "캠벨 대령께서 마침 이 무렵에 일이 진행되고 있다는 걸 알고 계실 거라 생각하십니까? 그분의 즉각적인 주문에 따른 결과라고 보십니까, 아니면 그저 두루뭉술한 지시만, 즉 시기는 정해 두지 않고 형편과 편의에 맡기는 식의 주문만 보내셨다고 보십니까?\""]], [["He paused.", "그가 말을 멈췄다."], ["She could not but hear; she could not avoid answering,", "그녀는 들을 수밖에 없었고, 대답을 피할 수도 없었다."]], [["“Till I have a letter from Colonel Campbell,”", "\"캠벨 대령께서 편지를 보내 주시기 전까지는,\""], ["said she, in a voice of forced calmness, “I can imagine nothing with any confidence.", "그녀가 애써 차분히 꾸민 목소리로 말했다. \"저로서는 무엇 하나 확신을 갖고 짐작할 수가 없어요."], ["It must be all conjecture.”", "그저 다 추측일 뿐이죠.\""]], [["“Conjecture—aye, sometimes one conjectures right, and sometimes one conjectures wrong.", "\"추측이라—그렇지요, 추측이란 때로는 맞고 때로는 틀리는 법입니다."], ["I wish I could conjecture how soon I shall make this rivet quite firm.", "저도 이 나사못을 단단히 박는 일을 얼마나 빨리 해낼 수 있을지 추측해 보고 싶군요."], ["What nonsense one talks, Miss Woodhouse, when hard at work, if one talks at all;—your real workmen, I suppose, hold their tongues; but we gentlemen labourers if we get hold of a word—Miss Fairfax said something about conjecturing.", "미스 우드하우스, 사람이 한창 일에 몰두하면, 말을 하기는 한다 쳐도 어쩌면 그리 허튼소리만 나오는지요. 진짜 직공들이라면 입을 다물고 있겠지만, 우리 같은 신사 일꾼들은 일단 말 한마디라도 붙들면—미스 페어팩스가 추측에 관해 무슨 말씀을 하셨던가요."], ["There, it is done.", "자, 다 됐습니다."], ["I have the pleasure, madam, (to Mrs.", "부인,\""], ["Bates,) of restoring your spectacles, healed for the present.”", "(베이츠 부인을 향해) \"안경을 돌려드리는 기쁨을 누리게 되었군요. 당장은 멀쩡하게 고쳐졌습니다.\""]], [["He was very warmly thanked both by mother and daughter; to escape a little from the latter, he went to the pianoforte, and begged Miss Fairfax, who was still sitting at it, to play something more.", "그는 모녀 양쪽에서 따뜻한 감사를 받았다. 그 가운데 딸 쪽의 인사를 조금 피해 보려고, 그는 피아노 쪽으로 가서, 아직 그 앞에 앉아 있던 미스 페어팩스에게 무엇이든 좀 더 연주해 달라고 청했다."]], [["“If you are very kind,”", "\"부디 인심을 써 주신다면,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “it will be one of the waltzes we danced last night;—let me live them over again.", "\"어젯밤 우리가 췄던 그 왈츠 가운데 하나를 들려주시지요. 그 순간들을 다시 한번 살아 보게 해 주세요."], ["You did not enjoy them as I did; you appeared tired the whole time.", "당신은 저만큼 그 시간을 즐기지 못하셨지요. 내내 지쳐 보이셨으니까요."], ["I believe you were glad we danced no longer; but I would have given worlds—all the worlds one ever has to give—for another half-hour.”", "더 이상 추지 않게 되어 다행이라 여기셨을 겁니다. 하지만 저라면 반 시간만 더 출 수 있다면 온 세상을—사람이 내줄 수 있는 온 세상을 다 내주었을 거예요.\""]], [["She played.", "그녀가 연주했다."]], [["“What felicity it is to hear a tune again which has made one happy!—If I mistake not that was danced at Weymouth.”", "\"사람을 행복하게 했던 곡을 다시 듣는다는 건 얼마나 큰 기쁨인지요! 제 기억이 틀리지 않다면, 저건 웨이머스에서 췄던 곡이군요.\""]], [["She looked up at him for a moment, coloured deeply, and played something else.", "그녀는 잠시 그를 올려다보더니, 얼굴을 짙게 붉히고는, 다른 곡을 연주했다."], ["He took some music from a chair near the pianoforte, and turning to Emma, said,", "그는 피아노 가까이 놓인 의자에서 악보 몇 장을 집어 들고, 엠마를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“Here is something quite new to me.", "\"여기 제게는 아주 생소한 곡이 있군요. 이거 아십니까?"], ["Do you know it?—Cramer.—And here are a new set of Irish melodies.", "크레이머예요. 그리고 여기 새로 나온 아일랜드 가락집도 있고요."], ["That, from such a quarter, one might expect.", "그런 출처라면 능히 짐작할 만한 것이지요."], ["This was all sent with the instrument.", "이것들은 다 악기와 함께 보내온 겁니다."], ["Very thoughtful of Colonel Campbell, was not it?—He knew Miss Fairfax could have no music here.", "캠벨 대령께서 참으로 사려 깊으셨지요? 미스 페어팩스가 여기서는 악보를 구할 수 없으리란 걸 아셨던 거예요."], ["I honour that part of the attention particularly; it shews it to have been so thoroughly from the heart.", "저는 그 배려의 그 대목이 특히 갸륵합니다. 진정 마음에서 우러나온 일임을 보여 주니까요."], ["Nothing hastily done; nothing incomplete.", "무엇 하나 서둘러 한 것이 없고, 무엇 하나 어설픈 데가 없어요."], ["True affection only could have prompted it.”", "참된 애정만이 그런 일을 시킬 수 있는 법이지요.\""]], [["Emma wished he would be less pointed, yet could not help being amused; and when on glancing her eye towards Jane Fairfax she caught the remains of a smile, when she saw that with all the deep blush of consciousness, there had been a smile of secret delight, she had less scruple in the amusement, and much less compunction with respect to her.—This amiable, upright, perfect Jane Fairfax was apparently cherishing very reprehensible feelings.", "엠마는 그가 좀 덜 노골적이었으면 했지만, 재미있어하지 않을 수 없었다. 그러다 제인 페어팩스 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌을 때 미소의 잔영을 포착하고, 자의식에서 오는 그 짙은 홍조 가운데에도 은밀한 기쁨의 미소가 있었음을 알아차리고 나니, 재미있어함에 거리낌이 덜해졌고, 그녀에 대한 가책도 한결 가벼워졌다. 이 사랑스럽고 올곧고 흠 하나 없는 제인 페어팩스도, 보아하니 자못 나무람직한 감정을 품고 있는 것이었다."]], [["He brought all the music to her, and they looked it over together.—Emma took the opportunity of whispering,", "그는 악보를 모두 그녀에게 가져다주었고, 둘은 함께 그것을 들여다보았다. 엠마는 그 틈을 타 속삭였다."]], [["“You speak too plain.", "\"말이 너무 노골적이에요."], ["She must understand you.”", "저 사람이 알아챘을 거예요.\""]], [["“I hope she does.", "\"알아챘기를 바랍니다."], ["I would have her understand me.", "알아채 줬으면 하고요."], ["I am not in the least ashamed of my meaning.”", "저는 제 속뜻이 조금도 부끄럽지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“But really, I am half ashamed, and wish I had never taken up the idea.”", "\"하지만 솔직히 저는 절반쯤 부끄러워졌어요. 애초에 그런 생각을 떠올리지나 말 걸 그랬어요.\""]], [["“I am very glad you did, and that you communicated it to me.", "\"그러셔서 저는 무척 기쁩니다. 게다가 그걸 제게 알려 주셔서요."], ["I have now a key to all her odd looks and ways.", "이제 저는 저 사람의 그 묘한 표정과 행동을 다 풀어낼 열쇠를 쥔 셈이니까요."], ["Leave shame to her.", "부끄러움은 저 사람한테 맡겨 두시죠."], ["If she does wrong, she ought to feel it.”", "잘못을 저질렀다면, 그걸 느껴야 마땅하지 않겠습니까.\""]], [["“She is not entirely without it, I think.”", "\"제 보기엔 저 사람에게 그 마음이 아주 없지는 않은 것 같아요.\""]], [["“I do not see much sign of it.", "\"저한테는 그런 기색이 별로 안 보이는데요."], ["She is playing Robin Adair at this moment—his favourite.”", "지금 이 순간 〈로빈 어데어〉를 연주하고 있잖습니까. 그 사람이 좋아하던 곡 말이죠.\""]], [["Shortly afterwards Miss Bates, passing near the window, descried Mr. Knightley on horse-back not far off.", "곧이어 미스 베이츠가 창가를 지나다가, 멀지 않은 곳에 말을 탄 미스터 나이틀리를 발견했다."]], [["“Mr. Knightley I declare!—I must speak to him if possible, just to thank him.", "\"어머나, 미스터 나이틀리네!"], ["I will not open the window here; it would give you all cold; but I can go into my mother’s room you know.", "어떻게든 말 좀 붙여 봐야겠어요, 그저 고맙다는 인사라도 하게요. 여기 이 창은 안 열게요, 다들 감기 드실 테니까."], ["I dare say he will come in when he knows who is here.", "하지만 어머니 방으로 가면 되잖아요. 안에 누가 와 있는지 알면 분명 들어오실 거예요."], ["Quite delightful to have you all meet so!—Our little room so honoured!”", "이렇게 다들 한자리에 모이시다니 정말 즐거운 일이에요! 우리 작은 방이 이런 영광을 다 누리네요!\""]], [["She was in the adjoining chamber while she still spoke, and opening the casement there, immediately called Mr. Knightley’s attention, and every syllable of their conversation was as distinctly heard by the others, as if it had passed within the same apartment.", "그녀는 말을 이어 가면서 이미 옆방으로 들어가 있었고, 그곳 여닫이창을 열어 곧장 미스터 나이틀리의 주의를 끌었으며, 두 사람의 대화는 한 마디 한 마디가, 마치 같은 방 안에서 오가기라도 한 듯 다른 사람들에게 또렷이 들렸다."]], [["“How d’ ye do?—how d’ye do?—Very well, I thank you.", "\"안녕하세요? 안녕하세요?"], ["So obliged to you for the carriage last night.", "네, 덕분에 아주 잘 지내요. 어젯밤 마차는 정말 고마웠어요."], ["We were just in time; my mother just ready for us.", "우리 딱 맞춰 갔답니다. 어머니가 막 채비를 끝내셨거든요."], ["Pray come in; do come in.", "어서 들어오세요, 어서요."], ["You will find some friends here.”", "여기 벗들이 와 계신 걸 보시게 될 거예요.\""]], [["So began Miss Bates; and Mr. Knightley seemed determined to be heard in his turn, for most resolutely and commandingly did he say,", "미스 베이츠는 이렇게 운을 뗐고, 미스터 나이틀리도 제 차례에는 기어이 자기 말이 들리게 하겠다는 듯, 더없이 단호하고 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["“How is your niece, Miss Bates?—I want to inquire after you all, but particularly your niece.", "\"조카분은 어떠십니까, 미스 베이츠? 댁의 모든 분 안부를 여쭙고 싶습니다만, 특히 조카분이요."], ["How is Miss Fairfax?—I hope she caught no cold last night.", "미스 페어팩스는 어떠신가요? 어젯밤에 감기나 들지 않으셨기를 바랍니다."], ["How is she to-day?", "오늘은 좀 어떠세요?"], ["Tell me how Miss Fairfax is.”", "미스 페어팩스가 어떤지 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["And Miss Bates was obliged to give a direct answer before he would hear her in any thing else.", "그래서 미스 베이츠는 그가 다른 어떤 말도 들으려 하기 전에 먼저 그 물음에 곧장 대답해야 했다."], ["The listeners were amused; and Mrs. Weston gave Emma a look of particular meaning.", "듣고 있던 사람들은 재미있어했고, 웨스턴 부인은 엠마에게 의미심장한 눈길을 보냈다."], ["But Emma still shook her head in steady scepticism.", "그러나 엠마는 여전히 한결같은 회의로 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“So obliged to you!—so very much obliged to you for the carriage,”", "\"정말 고마워요! 마차 일로 정말이지 너무도 고마워요.\""], ["resumed Miss Bates.", "미스 베이츠가 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["He cut her short with,", "그가 그녀의 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“I am going to Kingston.", "\"저는 킹스턴에 가는 길입니다."], ["Can I do any thing for you?”", "뭐 해 드릴 일이라도 있을까요?\""]], [["“Oh! dear, Kingston—are you?—Mrs.", "\"아유, 세상에, 킹스턴이라니, 그러세요?"], ["Cole was saying the other day she wanted something from Kingston.”", "콜 부인이 일전에 킹스턴에서 뭘 좀 사 와야 한다고 하시던데요.\""]], [["“Mrs. Cole has servants to send.", "\"콜 부인은 심부름 보낼 하인들이 있지 않습니까."], ["Can I do any thing for you?”", "댁을 위해 뭐 해 드릴 일은 없나요?\""]], [["“No, I thank you.", "\"아니요, 고마워요."], ["But do come in.", "그래도 어서 들어오세요."], ["Who do you think is here?—Miss Woodhouse and Miss Smith; so kind as to call to hear the new pianoforte.", "안에 누가 와 계실 것 같으세요? 미스 우드하우스와 미스 스미스예요."], ["Do put up your horse at the Crown, and come in.”", "친절하게도 새 피아노를 들으러 와 주셨답니다. 말은 크라운 여관에 매어 두시고, 들어오세요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"글쎄요,\""], ["said he, in a deliberating manner, “for five minutes, perhaps.”", "그가 망설이는 투로 말했다. \"한 오 분쯤이라면 모를까요.\""]], [["“And here is Mrs. Weston and Mr.", "\"그리고 여기 웨스턴 부인하고 미스터 프랭크 처칠도 와 계세요!"], ["Frank Churchill too!—Quite delightful; so many friends!”", "정말 즐거운 일이에요, 이렇게 벗들이 많이!\""]], [["“No, not now, I thank you. I could not stay two minutes.", "\"아닙니다, 지금은 안 되겠어요, 고맙습니다."], ["I must get on to Kingston as fast as I can.”", "이 분도 못 머뭅니다. 한시바삐 킹스턴으로 가야 해서요.\""]], [["“Oh! do come in.", "\"아유, 들어오시라니까요."], ["They will be so very happy to see you.”", "다들 그렇게 반가워하실 텐데요.\""]], [["“No, no; your room is full enough.", "\"아니요, 아니요. 방이 이미 충분히 찼겠는데요."], ["I will call another day, and hear the pianoforte.”", "다음에 다른 날 들러서 피아노를 듣겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well, I am so sorry!—Oh!", "\"어머, 정말 아쉬워라! 아, 미스터 나이틀리, 어젯밤 모임은 얼마나 즐거웠는지요."], ["Mr.", "정말이지 더없이 유쾌했답니다."], ["Knightley, what a delightful party last night; how extremely pleasant.—Did you ever see such dancing?—Was not it delightful?—Miss Woodhouse and Mr.", "그런 춤을 보신 적 있으세요? 정말 멋지지 않던가요?"], ["Frank Churchill; I never saw any thing equal to it.”", "미스 우드하우스와 미스터 프랭크 처칠 말이에요. 그에 견줄 만한 건 평생 본 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“Oh! very delightful indeed; I can say nothing less, for I suppose Miss Woodhouse and Mr.", "\"오, 정말 무척 즐거웠지요."], ["Frank Churchill are hearing every thing that passes.", "그보다 못하게 말할 수가 없군요. 미스 우드하우스와 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 오가는 말을 죄다 듣고 계실 테니까요."], ["And (raising his voice still more) I do not see why Miss Fairfax should not be mentioned too.", "그리고,\" (목소리를 한층 더 높이며) \"미스 페어팩스도 언급하지 못할 까닭이 없지 싶군요."], ["I think Miss Fairfax dances very well; and Mrs.", "제 보기에 미스 페어팩스는 춤을 아주 잘 추시고요."], ["Weston is the very best country-dance player, without exception, in England.", "그리고 웨스턴 부인으로 말하자면, 예외 없이 잉글랜드를 통틀어 제일가는 컨트리댄스 반주자이십니다."], ["Now, if your friends have any gratitude, they will say something pretty loud about you and me in return; but I cannot stay to hear it.”", "자, 댁의 벗들에게 고마워하는 마음이 조금이라도 있다면, 답례로 당신과 저에 관해 무언가 꽤 큰 소리로 말해 줄 테지요. 하지만 저는 그걸 들으려고 머무를 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아, 미스터 나이틀리, 잠깐만요."], ["Mr.", "긴한 일이 있어요."], ["Knightley, one moment more; something of consequence—so shocked!—Jane and I are both so shocked about the apples!”", "어찌나 놀랐는지요! 제인하고 저하고 둘 다 그 사과 일로 어찌나 놀랐는지 몰라요!\""]], [["“What is the matter now?”", "\"이번엔 또 무슨 일입니까?\""]], [["“To think of your sending us all your store apples.", "\"저장해 두신 사과를 죄다 저희한테 보내 주시다니요."], ["You said you had a great many, and now you have not one left.", "잔뜩 있다고 하시더니, 이젠 하나도 안 남으셨다잖아요."], ["We really are so shocked!", "정말이지 어찌나 놀랐는지요!"], ["Mrs. Hodges may well be angry.", "호지스 부인이 화를 내실 만도 하죠."], ["William Larkins mentioned it here.", "윌리엄 라킨스가 여기서 그 얘기를 하더라고요."], ["You should not have done it, indeed you should not.", "그러시면 안 되는 거였어요, 정말이지 안 되는 거였다고요."], ["Ah! he is off.", "어머, 가 버리셨네."], ["He never can bear to be thanked.", "저분은 도무지 고맙다는 인사를 못 견디시는 양반이에요."], ["But I thought he would have staid now, and it would have been a pity not to have mentioned....", "그래도 이번엔 좀 머무르실 줄 알았는데. 그 얘길 안 하고 넘어갔으면 못내 아쉬웠을 거예요……"], ["Well, (returning to the room,) I have not been able to succeed.", "자,\" (방으로 돌아오며) \"도무지 성공을 못 했네요."], ["Mr. Knightley cannot stop.", "미스터 나이틀리는 들르실 수가 없으시대요."], ["He is going to Kingston.", "킹스턴에 가시는 길이라고요."], ["He asked me if he could do any thing....”", "저더러 뭐 해 드릴 일이 없느냐고 물으시던데…….\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\" 제인이 말했다."], ["said Jane, “we heard his kind offers, we heard every thing.”", "\"그분의 친절한 제안, 다 들었어요. 죄다 들었답니다.\""]], [["“Oh! yes, my dear, I dare say you might, because you know, the door was open, and the window was open, and Mr.", "\"아유, 그래, 얘야, 너도 들었을 만하지."], ["Knightley spoke loud.", "알다시피 문도 열려 있었고, 창문도 열려 있었고, 게다가 미스터 나이틀리가 큰 소리로 말씀하셨으니까. 죄다 들었을 게 분명하고말고."], ["You must have heard every thing to be sure.", "'킹스턴에서 뭐 해 드릴 일 없습니까?' 하시기에, 내가 마침 그 얘길 꺼냈더니……"], ["‘Can I do any thing for you at Kingston?’ said he; so I just mentioned....", "어머, 미스 우드하우스, 벌써 가셔야 하나요? 막 오신 것 같은데."], ["Oh! Miss Woodhouse, must you be going?—You seem but just come—so very obliging of you.”", "이렇게 와 주셔서 정말 고마워요.\""]], [["Emma found it really time to be at home; the visit had already lasted long; and on examining watches, so much of the morning was perceived to be gone, that Mrs.", "엠마는 정말 집에 돌아갈 때가 되었다고 느꼈다. 방문이 이미 오래 이어진 터였다."], ["Weston and her companion taking leave also, could allow themselves only to walk with the two young ladies to Hartfield gates, before they set off for Randalls.", "그리고 시계를 살펴보니 오전 시간이 어찌나 많이 가 버렸는지, 웨스턴 부인과 그 일행도 함께 자리를 뜨면서, 랜덜스로 떠나기에 앞서 두 젊은 아가씨를 하트필드 대문까지 바래다주는 정도밖에는 여유를 낼 수 없었다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter XI", "# 제2권 11장"]], [["It may be possible to do without dancing entirely.", "춤이라는 것 없이도 살아갈 수는 있을 것이다."], ["Instances have been known of young people passing many, many months successively, without being at any ball of any description, and no material injury accrue either to body or mind;—but when a beginning is made—when the felicities of rapid motion have once been, though slightly, felt—it must be a very heavy set that does not ask for more.", "어떤 종류의 무도회에도 가지 못한 채 여러 달, 아주 여러 달을 잇따라 보내고도 몸에도 마음에도 이렇다 할 해를 입지 않은 젊은이들의 사례가 알려진 바 있으니까. 그러나 일단 시작이 되고 나면 — 빠른 움직임의 황홀함을 한번 맛보고 나면, 그것이 아무리 미미했다 한들 — 더 달라고 조르지 않을 만큼 둔한 사람이란 여간해서는 없는 법이다."]], [["Frank Churchill had danced once at Highbury, and longed to dance again; and the last half-hour of an evening which Mr.", "프랭크 처칠은 하이베리에서 한 번 춤을 추어 본 터라 다시 한번 추기를 갈망했다. 그래서 미스터 우드하우스가 설득에 못 이겨 딸과 함께 랜덜스에서 저녁을 보내게 된 그 마지막 반 시간을, 두 젊은이는 이 일을 궁리하는 데 다 썼다."], ["Woodhouse was persuaded to spend with his daughter at Randalls, was passed by the two young people in schemes on the subject.", "처음 생각을 꺼낸 것도 프랭크였고, 그것을 밀고 나가는 데 가장 열을 올린 것도 프랭크였다. 어려운 점들을 가장 잘 헤아리고, 편의와 모양새를 가장 신경 쓴 쪽은 숙녀였으니까."], ["Frank’s was the first idea; and his the greatest zeal in pursuing it; for the lady was the best judge of the difficulties, and the most solicitous for accommodation and appearance. But still she had inclination enough for shewing people again how delightfully Mr.", "그래도 그녀에게는 적잖은 마음이 있었다 — 미스터 프랭크 처칠과 미스 우드하우스가 얼마나 멋지게 춤추는지를 사람들에게 다시 한번 보여 주고 싶은 마음, 제인 페어팩스와 견주어도 부끄럽지 않은 그 일을 해 보고 싶은 마음, 나아가 허영이라는 못된 부추김 따위 없이 그저 춤 그 자체를 즐기고 싶은 마음이."], ["Frank Churchill and Miss Woodhouse danced—for doing that in which she need not blush to compare herself with Jane Fairfax—and even for simple dancing itself, without any of the wicked aids of vanity—to assist him first in pacing out the room they were in to see what it could be made to hold—and then in taking the dimensions of the other parlour, in the hope of discovering, in spite of all that Mr. Weston could say of their exactly equal size, that it was a little the largest.", "그리하여 그녀는 먼저 두 사람이 있는 방을 발걸음으로 재어 얼마나 들어갈 수 있을지 가늠하는 일을 도왔고, 다음으로는 옆 응접실의 치수를 재었다 — 두 방의 크기가 정확히 같다는 미스터 웨스턴의 말이 어떻든, 이쪽이 조금이라도 더 넓기를 바라면서."]], [["His first proposition and request, that the dance begun at Mr. Cole’s should be finished there—that the same party should be collected, and the same musician engaged, met with the readiest acquiescence.", "미스터 콜의 집에서 시작했던 그 춤을 여기서 마저 끝내자는 것 — 같은 사람들을 다시 불러 모으고 같은 악사를 청하자는 것이 그의 첫 제안이자 청이었고, 이는 더없이 흔쾌한 동의를 얻었다."], ["Mr. Weston entered into the idea with thorough enjoyment, and Mrs.", "미스터 웨스턴은 진심으로 즐거워하며 이 생각에 동참했고, 웨스턴 부인은 다들 추고 싶은 만큼 얼마든지 연주를 맡겠노라 기꺼이 자청했다."], ["Weston most willingly undertook to play as long as they could wish to dance; and the interesting employment had followed, of reckoning up exactly who there would be, and portioning out the indispensable division of space to every couple.", "그러고는 흥미진진한 일이 뒤따랐으니, 과연 누구누구가 올지 정확히 헤아리고, 짝마다 빠져서는 안 될 자리를 갈라 맡기는 일이었다."]], [["“You and Miss Smith, and Miss Fairfax, will be three, and the two Miss Coxes five,”", "\"당신과 미스 스미스, 미스 페어팩스가 셋이고, 거기에 콕스 자매를 더하면 다섯이지요.\""], ["had been repeated many times over.", "이 말이 몇 번이나 되풀이되었다."], ["“And there will be the two Gilberts, young Cox, my father, and myself, besides Mr. Knightley.", "\"그리고 길버트 형제와 젊은 콕스, 우리 아버지에 저까지 있고, 미스터 나이틀리도 계시고요."], ["Yes, that will be quite enough for pleasure.", "그래요, 즐기기에는 그만하면 충분해요."], ["You and Miss Smith, and Miss Fairfax, will be three, and the two Miss Coxes five; and for five couple there will be plenty of room.”", "당신과 미스 스미스, 미스 페어팩스가 셋이고, 콕스 자매를 더하면 다섯이고요. 다섯 쌍이면 자리는 넉넉하고말고요.\""]], [["But soon it came to be on one side,", "그러나 곧 한편에서는 이런 말이 나왔다."]], [["“But will there be good room for five couple?—I really do not think there will.”", "\"그런데 다섯 쌍이 설 만큼 자리가 정말 넉넉할까요? 아무래도 그렇지 않을 것 같은데요.\""]], [["On another,", "또 다른 편에서는 이런 말이 나왔다."]], [["“And after all, five couple are not enough to make it worth while to stand up.", "\"그리고 따지고 보면 다섯 쌍으로는 일어서서 출 만한 값어치가 못 돼요."], ["Five couple are nothing, when one thinks seriously about it.", "진지하게 생각해 보면 다섯 쌍은 아무것도 아니거든요."], ["It will not do to invite five couple.", "다섯 쌍을 초대한다는 건 말이 안 되죠."], ["It can be allowable only as the thought of the moment.”", "그저 그 순간의 떠오른 생각이라면 모를까요.\""]], [["Somebody said that Miss Gilbert was expected at her brother’s, and must be invited with the rest.", "누군가는 미스 길버트가 오라비 집에 와 있을 거라며 다른 이들과 함께 초대해야 한다고 했다."], ["Somebody else believed Mrs. Gilbert would have danced the other evening, if she had been asked.", "또 누군가는 미시즈 길버트도 청을 받았더라면 지난번 저녁에 춤을 추었으리라고 믿었다."], ["A word was put in for a second young Cox; and at last, Mr.", "둘째 콕스를 위한 말도 한마디 끼어들었다."], ["Weston naming one family of cousins who must be included, and another of very old acquaintance who could not be left out, it became a certainty that the five couple would be at least ten, and a very interesting speculation in what possible manner they could be disposed of.", "그리고 마침내 미스터 웨스턴이 빠뜨려서는 안 될 사촌 한 집안과 빠뜨릴 수 없는 아주 오랜 지인 한 집안을 들먹이자, 다섯 쌍이 적어도 열 쌍은 되리라는 것이 확실해졌고, 도대체 어떤 식으로 이들을 다 들여놓을 수 있을지가 자못 흥미로운 궁리거리가 되었다."]], [["The doors of the two rooms were just opposite each other.", "두 방의 문은 마침 서로 정면으로 마주 보고 있었다."], ["“Might not they use both rooms, and dance across the passage?”", "\"두 방을 다 쓰면서 복도를 가로질러 춤을 추면 안 될까?\""], ["It seemed the best scheme; and yet it was not so good but that many of them wanted a better. Emma said it would be awkward; Mrs.", "그것이 가장 좋은 안인 듯싶었으나, 그래도 여럿이 더 나은 방안을 바랄 만큼 흡족한 것은 아니었다."], ["Weston was in distress about the supper; and Mr. Woodhouse opposed it earnestly, on the score of health.", "엠마는 그러면 거북할 거라고 했고, 웨스턴 부인은 저녁 식사가 걱정이었으며, 미스터 우드하우스는 건강을 이유로 한사코 반대했다."], ["It made him so very unhappy, indeed, that it could not be persevered in.", "실로 그가 어찌나 언짢아하던지, 그 안은 더 밀고 나갈 수가 없었다."]], [["“Oh! no,”", "\"오!"], ["said he; “it would be the extreme of imprudence.", "안 됩니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["I could not bear it for Emma!—Emma is not strong.", "\"그건 더없이 경솔한 짓이에요. 엠마를 생각하면 도저히 못 견딜 일이오!"], ["She would catch a dreadful cold.", "엠마는 몸이 튼튼하지 못해요. 지독한 감기에 걸릴 거요."], ["So would poor little Harriet.", "가엾은 어린 해리엇도 그럴 테고."], ["So you would all. Mrs.", "다들 그럴 거요."], ["Weston, you would be quite laid up; do not let them talk of such a wild thing.", "웨스턴 부인, 부인도 꼼짝없이 앓아눕고 말 거요. 그런 무모한 이야기는 꺼내지도 못하게 하시오."], ["Pray do not let them talk of it.", "제발 그런 말은 못 하게 해 주시오."], ["That young man (speaking lower) is very thoughtless.", "저 젊은이는 (목소리를 낮추며) 아주 생각이 짧아요."], ["Do not tell his father, but that young man is not quite the thing.", "그 아버지께는 말하지 마시오만, 저 젊은이는 어딘가 미덥지가 못해요."], ["He has been opening the doors very often this evening, and keeping them open very inconsiderately.", "오늘 저녁 내내 문을 어찌나 자주 열어 대던지, 게다가 어찌나 무심하게 열어 둔 채로 두던지."], ["He does not think of the draught.", "외풍은 도통 생각을 안 하는구려."], ["I do not mean to set you against him, but indeed he is not quite the thing!”", "그를 미워하게 하려는 건 아니오만, 정말이지 저 젊은이는 어딘가 미덥지가 못해요!\""]], [["Mrs. Weston was sorry for such a charge.", "웨스턴 부인은 그런 비난이 안타까웠다."], ["She knew the importance of it, and said every thing in her power to do it away.", "그 말의 무게를 알기에, 그것을 누그러뜨리려고 할 수 있는 말은 다 했다."], ["Every door was now closed, the passage plan given up, and the first scheme of dancing only in the room they were in resorted to again; and with such good-will on Frank Churchill’s part, that the space which a quarter of an hour before had been deemed barely sufficient for five couple, was now endeavoured to be made out quite enough for ten.", "이제 문이란 문은 모두 닫혔고, 복도를 쓰자는 안은 접어 두었으며, 두 사람이 있는 방에서만 춤을 추자는 처음의 안으로 다시 돌아갔다 — 그것도 프랭크 처칠이 어찌나 흔쾌히 앞장섰던지, 십오 분 전만 해도 다섯 쌍에 겨우 충분할 듯하던 그 자리를 이제는 열 쌍에게도 넉넉하다고 우겨 보려 들었다."]], [["“We were too magnificent,”", "\"우리가 너무 거창하게 생각했어요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“We allowed unnecessary room.", "\"쓸데없이 자리를 많이 잡았던 거죠."], ["Ten couple may stand here very well.”", "열 쌍도 여기 거뜬히 설 수 있어요.\""]], [["Emma demurred.", "엠마는 미심쩍어했다."], ["“It would be a crowd—a sad crowd; and what could be worse than dancing without space to turn in?”", "\"그러면 북적댈 거예요 — 끔찍하게 북적댈 거라고요. 돌아설 자리도 없이 춤을 추는 것보다 더 고약한 일이 어디 있겠어요?\""]], [["“Very true,”", "\"지당한 말씀입니다.\""], ["he gravely replied; “it was very bad.”", "그가 정색하며 답했다. \"그건 정말 고약하지요.\""], ["But still he went on measuring, and still he ended with,", "그러면서도 그는 여전히 자리를 재었고, 여전히 같은 말로 끝을 맺었다."]], [["“I think there will be very tolerable room for ten couple.”", "\"제 생각엔 열 쌍쯤은 그럭저럭 무리 없이 설 자리가 될 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니에요, 아니에요.\""], ["said she, “you are quite unreasonable.", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 정말 막무가내네요."], ["It would be dreadful to be standing so close!", "그렇게 다닥다닥 붙어 서면 끔찍할 거예요!"], ["Nothing can be farther from pleasure than to be dancing in a crowd—and a crowd in a little room!”", "북적대는 틈에서 춤추는 것만큼 즐거움과 동떨어진 일이 또 있을까요 — 그것도 좁은 방에서 북적대는 거라니!\""]], [["“There is no denying it,”", "\"부정할 수 없는 말이지요.\""], ["he replied.", "그가 답했다."], ["“I agree with you exactly.", "\"전적으로 동감입니다."], ["A crowd in a little room—Miss Woodhouse, you have the art of giving pictures in a few words.", "좁은 방에서 북적대다니 — 미스 우드하우스, 당신은 몇 마디로 그림을 그려 내는 재주가 있으시군요."], ["Exquisite, quite exquisite!—Still, however, having proceeded so far, one is unwilling to give the matter up.", "절묘해요, 정말 절묘합니다! 그렇긴 해도, 여기까지 진행해 놓고 보니 이 일을 포기하기가 영 내키질 않네요."], ["It would be a disappointment to my father—and altogether—I do not know that—I am rather of opinion that ten couple might stand here very well.”", "아버지가 실망하실 거예요 — 그리고 이래저래 — 글쎄요, 잘 모르겠습니다만 — 아무래도 저는 열 쌍쯤은 여기 거뜬히 설 수 있다는 쪽으로 마음이 기우는걸요.\""]], [["Emma perceived that the nature of his gallantry was a little self-willed, and that he would rather oppose than lose the pleasure of dancing with her; but she took the compliment, and forgave the rest.", "엠마는 그의 그 깍듯한 친절에 다소 고집스러운 면이 있어, 그녀와 춤추는 즐거움을 잃느니 차라리 맞서겠다는 심사임을 알아챘다. 그러나 그녀는 칭찬은 받아들이고 나머지는 눈감아 주었다."], ["Had she intended ever to marry him, it might have been worth while to pause and consider, and try to understand the value of his preference, and the character of his temper; but for all the purposes of their acquaintance, he was quite amiable enough.", "만일 그녀가 언젠가 그와 결혼할 작정이었다면, 잠시 멈추어 곰곰이 따져 보며 그가 그녀를 좋아하는 마음의 값어치며 그 기질의 됨됨이를 헤아려 볼 만했을 것이다. 그러나 그저 알고 지내는 사이라는 목적에 비추어 보면, 그는 충분히 호감 가는 사람이었다."]], [["Before the middle of the next day, he was at Hartfield; and he entered the room with such an agreeable smile as certified the continuance of the scheme.", "이튿날 한낮이 채 되기 전에 그는 하트필드에 와 있었다. 그가 어찌나 반가운 미소를 띠고 방에 들어서던지, 그 계획이 계속되고 있음이 분명했다."], ["It soon appeared that he came to announce an improvement.", "곧 그가 한 가지 개선안을 알리러 왔음이 드러났다."]], [["“Well, Miss Woodhouse,”", "\"자, 미스 우드하우스.\""], ["he almost immediately began, “your inclination for dancing has not been quite frightened away, I hope, by the terrors of my father’s little rooms.", "그가 거의 들어서자마자 입을 열었다. \"우리 아버지의 그 좁은 방들이 안긴 두려움 때문에 당신의 춤추고 싶은 마음이 아주 달아나 버리진 않았기를 바랍니다."], ["I bring a new proposal on the subject:—a thought of my father’s, which waits only your approbation to be acted upon.", "이 일에 관해 새 제안을 가지고 왔어요. 우리 아버지의 생각인데, 당신의 승낙만 떨어지면 곧장 실행에 옮길 참입니다."], ["May I hope for the honour of your hand for the two first dances of this little projected ball, to be given, not at Randalls, but at the Crown Inn?”", "이번에 계획 중인 그 작은 무도회에서 첫 두 곡을 당신과 함께 출 영광을 누려도 될까요 — 랜덜스가 아니라 크라운 여관에서 여는 무도회를요?\""]], [["“The Crown!”", "\"크라운에서요!\""]], [["“Yes; if you and Mr.", "\"그래요."], ["Woodhouse see no objection, and I trust you cannot, my father hopes his friends will be so kind as to visit him there.", "당신과 미스터 우드하우스께서 반대만 안 하신다면 — 설마 반대하실 리 없겠지만 — 우리 아버지는 벗들이 그곳으로 자신을 찾아와 주는 호의를 베풀어 주기를 바라십니다."], ["Better accommodations, he can promise them, and not a less grateful welcome than at Randalls.", "거기라면 더 나은 자리를 마련해 드릴 수 있고, 랜덜스 못지않게 정성껏 맞이하겠다고 약속하실 수 있다는군요."], ["It is his own idea. Mrs.", "아버지가 직접 떠올린 생각이에요."], ["Weston sees no objection to it, provided you are satisfied.", "당신만 흡족하시다면 웨스턴 부인도 반대할 게 없다고 하셨고요. 우리 모두 같은 마음입니다."], ["This is what we all feel.", "오! 당신 말씀이 더없이 옳았어요!"], ["Oh! you were perfectly right!", "랜덜스의 두 방 중 어느 쪽이든 열 쌍은 도저히 못 견딜 노릇이었을 테니까요!"], ["Ten couple, in either of the Randalls rooms, would have been insufferable!—Dreadful!—I felt how right you were the whole time, but was too anxious for securing any thing to like to yield.", "끔찍했을 거예요! 내내 당신이 옳다는 걸 느끼고 있었으면서도, 어떻게든 자리를 마련하고 싶은 마음이 너무 앞선 나머지 물러서기가 싫었던 거죠."], ["Is not it a good exchange?—You consent—I hope you consent?”", "멋진 맞바꿈 아닌가요? 승낙해 주시는 거죠 — 부디 승낙해 주시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“It appears to me a plan that nobody can object to, if Mr.", "\"미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인만 반대하지 않으신다면, 누구도 반대할 수 없는 안인 것 같네요."], ["and Mrs. Weston do not.", "제 생각엔 더없이 훌륭해요."], ["I think it admirable; and, as far as I can answer for myself, shall be most happy—It seems the only improvement that could be.", "그리고 제 몫에 관한 한, 더없이 기쁠 거라고 답할 수 있어요. 이보다 더 나은 개선은 없을 것 같네요."], ["Papa, do you not think it an excellent improvement?”", "아버지, 정말 훌륭한 개선이라고 생각하지 않으세요?\""]], [["She was obliged to repeat and explain it, before it was fully comprehended; and then, being quite new, farther representations were necessary to make it acceptable.", "그녀는 아버지가 완전히 알아들을 때까지 같은 말을 되풀이하며 설명해야 했다. 그러고 나서도, 워낙 새로운 일인지라 받아들이게 하려면 한층 더 거듭 설득이 필요했다."]], [["“No; he thought it very far from an improvement—a very bad plan—much worse than the other.", "\"아니다. 그건 도무지 개선이라고 할 수 없는 일이야 — 아주 나쁜 안이지 — 다른 것보다 훨씬 더 못해."], ["A room at an inn was always damp and dangerous; never properly aired, or fit to be inhabited.", "여관 방이라는 건 늘 눅눅하고 위험한 법이거든. 제대로 바람을 들인 적도 없고, 사람이 머물 만하지가 않아."], ["If they must dance, they had better dance at Randalls.", "정 춤을 추겠다면 랜덜스에서 추는 편이 나아."], ["He had never been in the room at the Crown in his life—did not know the people who kept it by sight.—Oh!", "나는 평생 크라운의 그 방에 들어가 본 적이 없어 — 거길 지키는 사람들 얼굴조차 모른단다. 오!"], ["no—a very bad plan.", "안 돼 — 아주 나쁜 안이야."], ["They would catch worse colds at the Crown than anywhere.”", "크라운에서는 어디서보다도 더 고약한 감기에 걸리고 말 거다.\""]], [["“I was going to observe, sir,”", "\"제가 막 말씀드리려던 참이었는데요, 어르신.\""], ["said Frank Churchill, “that one of the great recommendations of this change would be the very little danger of any body’s catching cold—so much less danger at the Crown than at Randalls!", "프랭크 처칠이 말했다. \"이번 변경의 가장 큰 장점 가운데 하나가 바로 누구든 감기에 걸릴 위험이 거의 없다는 점이거든요 — 랜덜스보다 크라운이 그 위험이 훨씬 적어요!"], ["Mr. Perry might have reason to regret the alteration, but nobody else could.”", "미스터 페리야 이 변경을 아쉬워할 까닭이 있을지 모르겠습니다만, 그분 말고는 아무도 아쉬워할 사람이 없을걸요.\""]], [["“Sir,” said Mr.", "\"이보게.\""], ["Woodhouse, rather warmly, “you are very much mistaken if you suppose Mr.", "미스터 우드하우스가 자못 발끈하여 말했다."], ["Perry to be that sort of character. Mr.", "\"미스터 페리를 그런 부류의 사람으로 여긴다면 그건 자네가 크게 잘못 안 걸세."], ["Perry is extremely concerned when any of us are ill.", "미스터 페리는 우리 가운데 누구라도 아프면 더없이 마음을 쓰는 사람이야."], ["But I do not understand how the room at the Crown can be safer for you than your father’s house.”", "한데 나는 크라운의 그 방이 어째서 자네 아버지 댁보다 자네한테 더 안전하다는 건지 도통 모르겠군.\""]], [["“From the very circumstance of its being larger, sir.", "\"바로 그 방이 더 넓다는 점 때문이지요, 어르신."], ["We shall have no occasion to open the windows at all—not once the whole evening; and it is that dreadful habit of opening the windows, letting in cold air upon heated bodies, which (as you well know, sir) does the mischief.”", "창문을 열 일이 아예 없을 테니까요 — 저녁 내내 단 한 번도요. 그리고 (어르신께서도 잘 아시다시피) 화근이 되는 게 바로 그 창문을 열어 더운 몸에 찬 공기를 들이는 못된 버릇이잖습니까.\""]], [["“Open the windows!—but surely, Mr.", "\"창문을 연다고!"], ["Churchill, nobody would think of opening the windows at Randalls.", "한데 미스터 처칠, 랜덜스에서 누가 창문을 열 생각이나 하겠는가. 그렇게 경솔한 사람이 어디 있단 말인가!"], ["Nobody could be so imprudent!", "나는 그런 일은 들어 본 적도 없어."], ["I never heard of such a thing.", "창문을 열어 놓고 춤을 추다니!"], ["Dancing with open windows!—I am sure, neither your father nor Mrs. Weston (poor Miss Taylor that was) would suffer it.”", "분명 자네 아버지도, 웨스턴 부인도 (전에 가엾은 미스 테일러였던 그분 말일세) 그런 일은 그냥 두고 보지 않을 걸세.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["sir—but a thoughtless young person will sometimes step behind a window-curtain, and throw up a sash, without its being suspected.", "어르신 — 하지만 생각 없는 젊은 사람이 더러는 창문 휘장 뒤로 슬그머니 들어가, 아무도 눈치채지 못하게 창틀을 밀어 올리기도 하거든요."], ["I have often known it done myself.”", "저도 그런 경우를 여러 번 겪어 봤습니다.\""]], [["“Have you indeed, sir?—Bless me!", "\"정말 그런가, 이 사람아? 맙소사!"], ["I never could have supposed it.", "나는 도무지 생각조차 못 했네."], ["But I live out of the world, and am often astonished at what I hear.", "하지만 나야 세상과 동떨어져 사는 사람이라, 듣는 이야기마다 놀랄 때가 많지."], ["However, this does make a difference; and, perhaps, when we come to talk it over—but these sort of things require a good deal of consideration.", "어쨌거나 그렇다면 이야기가 달라지는군. 그리고 어쩌면, 우리가 차차 의논을 해 보면 — 하지만 이런 일은 어지간히 신중을 기해야 하는 법이야."], ["One cannot resolve upon them in a hurry. If Mr.", "서둘러 결정할 수는 없어."], ["and Mrs. Weston will be so obliging as to call here one morning, we may talk it over, and see what can be done.”", "미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인이 호의를 베풀어 언제 한번 아침나절에 들러 준다면, 함께 의논해서 어찌하면 좋을지 가늠해 볼 수 있겠지.\""]], [["“But, unfortunately, sir, my time is so limited—”", "\"하지만 어르신, 공교롭게도 제게 주어진 시간이 너무 빠듯해서요.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["interrupted Emma, “there will be plenty of time for talking every thing over.", "엠마가 끼어들었다. \"모든 걸 의논할 시간은 얼마든지 있어요."], ["There is no hurry at all.", "조금도 서두를 게 없죠."], ["If it can be contrived to be at the Crown, papa, it will be very convenient for the horses.", "크라운에서 여는 걸로 꾸릴 수 있다면, 아버지, 말들한테도 아주 편할 거예요."], ["They will be so near their own stable.”", "자기네 마구간이 코앞이니까요.\""]], [["“So they will, my dear.", "\"그렇겠구나, 얘야."], ["That is a great thing.", "그거야 큰 일이지."], ["Not that James ever complains; but it is right to spare our horses when we can.", "제임스가 뭐라 불평을 하는 건 아니다만, 그래도 가능하다면 말들을 아껴 주는 게 옳지."], ["If I could be sure of the rooms being thoroughly aired—but is Mrs. Stokes to be trusted?", "그 방들에 바람이 잘 들어 있다는 걸 내가 확신할 수만 있다면 좋으련만 — 한데 미시즈 스토크스를 믿어도 되겠느냐?"], ["I doubt it.", "그게 미심쩍어."], ["I do not know her, even by sight.”", "나는 그 사람을 얼굴조차 모르거든.\""]], [["“I can answer for every thing of that nature, sir, because it will be under Mrs.", "\"그런 종류의 일이라면 제가 다 책임지고 답할 수 있습니다, 어르신."], ["Weston’s care. Mrs.", "죄다 웨스턴 부인의 손에 맡겨질 테니까요."], ["Weston undertakes to direct the whole.”", "웨스턴 부인이 모든 걸 도맡아 지휘하기로 했거든요.\""]], [["“There, papa!—Now you must be satisfied—Our own dear Mrs.", "\"보세요, 아버지! 이제는 흡족하시겠죠 — 다름 아닌 우리의 그 다정한 웨스턴 부인이시잖아요."], ["Weston, who is carefulness itself.", "세심함 그 자체이신 분요."], ["Do not you remember what Mr. Perry said, so many years ago, when I had the measles?", "미스터 페리가 그 오래전에, 제가 홍역을 앓았을 때 뭐라고 하셨는지 기억나지 않으세요?"], ["‘If Miss Taylor undertakes to wrap Miss Emma up, you need not have any fears, sir.’ How often have I heard you speak of it as such a compliment to her!”", "'미스 테일러가 미스 엠마를 따뜻하게 싸매 주기로 했다면, 어르신께서는 아무 걱정 안 하셔도 됩니다.' 아버지가 그 말씀을 그분에 대한 더없는 칭찬이라며 얼마나 자주 되뇌셨는데요!\""]], [["“Aye, very true.", "\"그래, 정말 그랬지."], ["Mr. Perry did say so.", "미스터 페리가 그리 말했고말고."], ["I shall never forget it.", "나는 그 말을 결코 잊지 못해."], ["Poor little Emma!", "가엾은 어린 엠마!"], ["You were very bad with the measles; that is, you would have been very bad, but for Perry’s great attention.", "너는 홍역을 아주 심하게 앓았단다 — 그러니까, 페리가 그렇게 극진히 보살펴 주지 않았더라면 아주 심하게 앓았을 거란 말이지."], ["He came four times a day for a week.", "그는 일주일 동안 하루에 네 번씩 왔어."], ["He said, from the first, it was a very good sort—which was our great comfort; but the measles are a dreadful complaint.", "처음부터 아주 순한 종류라고 했지 — 그게 우리한테 큰 위안이었단다. 하지만 홍역은 무서운 병이야."], ["I hope whenever poor Isabella’s little ones have the measles, she will send for Perry.”", "가엾은 이저벨라의 어린것들이 언제고 홍역에 걸리거든, 부디 그 애가 페리를 부르면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["“My father and Mrs. Weston are at the Crown at this moment,”", "\"우리 아버지와 웨스턴 부인은 바로 지금 크라운에 가 계세요.\""], ["said Frank Churchill, “examining the capabilities of the house.", "프랭크 처칠이 말했다. \"그 집이 쓸 만한지 살펴보고 계시지요."], ["I left them there and came on to Hartfield, impatient for your opinion, and hoping you might be persuaded to join them and give your advice on the spot.", "두 분을 거기 남겨 두고, 저는 당신 의견이 듣고 싶어 견딜 수가 없어서 하트필드로 곧장 달려온 겁니다. 어쩌면 당신이 두 분과 합류해서 그 자리에서 조언을 해 주도록 설득할 수 있을까 싶어서요."], ["I was desired to say so from both.", "두 분 다 그렇게 전해 달라고 하셨습니다."], ["It would be the greatest pleasure to them, if you could allow me to attend you there.", "제가 당신을 거기 모셔 가도 좋다면, 두 분께 더없는 기쁨이 될 거예요."], ["They can do nothing satisfactorily without you.”", "당신 없이는 무엇 하나 흡족하게 결정할 수가 없으시거든요.\""]], [["Emma was most happy to be called to such a council; and her father, engaging to think it all over while she was gone, the two young people set off together without delay for the Crown.", "엠마는 그런 회의에 불려 나가게 된 것이 더없이 기뻤다. 그녀가 가 있는 동안 모든 걸 곰곰이 생각해 보겠노라 아버지가 약속하자, 두 젊은이는 지체 없이 함께 크라운으로 떠났다."], ["There were Mr. and Mrs.", "그곳에 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인이 있었다."], ["Weston; delighted to see her and receive her approbation, very busy and very happy in their different way; she, in some little distress; and he, finding every thing perfect.", "그녀를 보고 그녀의 찬성을 얻게 되어 기뻐하며, 저마다 다른 식으로 무척 바쁘고 무척 행복했다. 부인은 다소 시름에 잠겨 있었고, 미스터 웨스턴은 무엇이든 더할 나위 없다고 여겼다."]], [["“Emma,”", "\"엠마.\""], ["said she, “this paper is worse than I expected.", "부인이 말했다. \"이 벽지는 내가 생각했던 것보다 더 형편없네요."], ["Look!", "봐요!"], ["in places you see it is dreadfully dirty; and the wainscot is more yellow and forlorn than any thing I could have imagined.”", "곳곳이 끔찍하게 더러운 게 보이죠. 그리고 징두리 판벽은 내가 상상했던 어떤 것보다도 더 누렇고 초라하고요.\""]], [["“My dear, you are too particular,”", "\"여보, 당신은 너무 따지는구려.\""], ["said her husband.", "그녀의 남편이 말했다."], ["“What does all that signify?", "\"그게 다 무슨 대수란 말이오?"], ["You will see nothing of it by candlelight.", "촛불 아래선 그런 게 하나도 안 보일 거요."], ["It will be as clean as Randalls by candlelight.", "촛불 아래선 랜덜스 못지않게 깨끗해 보일 테고."], ["We never see any thing of it on our club-nights.”", "우리 모임이 있는 밤에도 그런 건 전혀 눈에 안 띄잖소.\""]], [["The ladies here probably exchanged looks which meant, “Men never know when things are dirty or not;”", "여기서 숙녀들은 아마 이런 뜻이 담긴 눈길을 주고받았으리라. \"남자들은 무엇이 더럽고 안 더러운지 도통 모른다니까.\""], ["and the gentlemen perhaps thought each to himself, “Women will have their little nonsenses and needless cares.”", "그리고 신사들은 어쩌면 저마다 속으로 이렇게 생각했으리라. \"여자들이란 그 사소한 호들갑이며 쓸데없는 걱정을 늘 달고 사는 법이지.\""]], [["One perplexity, however, arose, which the gentlemen did not disdain.", "그러나 신사들도 못 본 척하지 못할 난제가 하나 떠올랐다."], ["It regarded a supper-room.", "식사할 방에 관한 것이었다."], ["At the time of the ballroom’s being built, suppers had not been in question; and a small card-room adjoining, was the only addition.", "이 무도장이 지어질 무렵에는 만찬 따위는 고려되지 않았으니, 옆에 딸린 작은 카드놀이 방 하나가 보태진 것이 전부였다."], ["What was to be done?", "어찌해야 한단 말인가?"], ["This card-room would be wanted as a card-room now; or, if cards were conveniently voted unnecessary by their four selves, still was it not too small for any comfortable supper?", "이 카드놀이 방은 지금도 카드놀이 방으로 쓰여야 할 터였다. 설령 자기네 넷이서 카드놀이는 필요 없다고 적당히 의결한다 한들, 그래도 편안하게 만찬을 들기에는 너무 작지 않은가?"], ["Another room of much better size might be secured for the purpose; but it was at the other end of the house, and a long awkward passage must be gone through to get at it.", "그 용도에 한결 알맞은 크기의 다른 방을 마련할 수도 있었으나, 그 방은 집 반대편 끝에 있어서, 거기까지 가려면 길고 거추장스러운 복도를 지나야 했다."], ["This made a difficulty.", "이것이 난제였다."], ["Mrs. Weston was afraid of draughts for the young people in that passage; and neither Emma nor the gentlemen could tolerate the prospect of being miserably crowded at supper.", "웨스턴 부인은 그 복도에서 젊은 사람들이 외풍을 맞을까 봐 걱정이었고, 엠마도 신사들도 만찬 때 비참하게 부대껴야 할 처지는 도무지 견딜 수 없었다."]], [["Mrs. Weston proposed having no regular supper; merely sandwiches, &c., set out in the little room; but that was scouted as a wretched suggestion.", "웨스턴 부인은 정식 만찬은 차리지 말고, 그저 샌드위치 따위만 그 작은 방에 내놓자고 제안했다."], ["A private dance, without sitting down to supper, was pronounced an infamous fraud upon the rights of men and women; and Mrs.", "그러나 그것은 한심한 제안이라며 일축되었다. 만찬에 둘러앉지 않는 사사로운 무도회란 남녀의 권리를 짓밟는 파렴치한 사기라고 단죄되었고, 웨스턴 부인은 다시는 그런 말을 입에 올려서는 안 되었다."], ["Weston must not speak of it again. She then took another line of expediency, and looking into the doubtful room, observed,", "그러자 부인은 다른 묘책의 길로 들어, 미심쩍은 그 방을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“I do not think it is so very small.", "\"이 방이 그렇게까지 작지는 않은 것 같아요."], ["We shall not be many, you know.”", "아시다시피 우리가 많은 인원도 아니고요.\""]], [["And Mr. Weston at the same time, walking briskly with long steps through the passage, was calling out,", "그러는 동안 미스터 웨스턴은 긴 보폭으로 복도를 성큼성큼 걸어 다니며 큰 소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“You talk a great deal of the length of this passage, my dear.", "\"여보, 당신은 이 복도가 길다고 말이 참 많구려."], ["It is a mere nothing after all; and not the least draught from the stairs.”", "따지고 보면 아무것도 아니야. 게다가 계단에서 들이치는 외풍이라곤 조금도 없고.\""]], [["“I wish,” said Mrs.", "\"우리 손님들이 대체로 어떤 배치를 가장 좋아할지 알 수만 있다면 좋겠어요.\""], ["Weston, “one could know which arrangement our guests in general would like best.", "웨스턴 부인이 말했다."], ["To do what would be most generally pleasing must be our object—if one could but tell what that would be.”", "\"대다수가 가장 흡족해할 일을 하는 게 우리 목표여야 할 텐데 — 그게 무엇일지 알 수만 있다면 말이죠.\""]], [["“Yes, very true,”", "\"맞아요, 정말 그렇죠.\""], ["cried Frank, “very true.", "프랭크가 외쳤다. \"정말 옳은 말씀이에요."], ["You want your neighbours’ opinions.", "이웃들의 의견이 필요하신 거죠."], ["I do not wonder at you.", "그러실 만도 하고요."], ["If one could ascertain what the chief of them—the Coles, for instance.", "그분들 가운데 좌장 격인 분들의 생각을 알 수만 있다면 — 가령 콜 댁 같은 분들요."], ["They are not far off.", "멀지 않은 데 사시잖아요."], ["Shall I call upon them?", "제가 한번 찾아가 볼까요?"], ["Or Miss Bates?", "아니면 미스 베이츠는요?"], ["She is still nearer.—And I do not know whether Miss Bates is not as likely to understand the inclinations of the rest of the people as any body.", "그분은 더 가까이 사시니까요. 게다가 그 누구 못지않게 미스 베이츠야말로 나머지 사람들의 취향을 잘 헤아릴 분이 아닐까 싶기도 하고요."], ["I think we do want a larger council.", "아무래도 더 큰 회의가 필요한 것 같네요."], ["Suppose I go and invite Miss Bates to join us?”", "제가 가서 미스 베이츠더러 함께하자고 청해 올까요?\""]], [["“Well—if you please,” said Mrs.", "\"글쎄요 — 정 그러시다면요.\""], ["Weston rather hesitating, “if you think she will be of any use.”", "웨스턴 부인이 다소 망설이며 말했다. \"그분이 무슨 도움이 될 거라고 생각하신다면요.\""]], [["“You will get nothing to the purpose from Miss Bates,”", "\"미스 베이츠한테서는 쓸 만한 이야기를 하나도 못 들으실걸요.\""], ["said Emma.", "엠마가 말했다."], ["“She will be all delight and gratitude, but she will tell you nothing.", "\"그분은 그저 온통 기뻐하고 고마워하기만 할 뿐, 정작 아무 말도 안 해 주실 거예요."], ["She will not even listen to your questions.", "당신 질문에 귀 기울이지조차 않으실 테고요."], ["I see no advantage in consulting Miss Bates.”", "미스 베이츠한테 의논해 봐야 무슨 득이 있을지 모르겠네요.\""]], [["“But she is so amusing, so extremely amusing!", "\"하지만 그분은 어찌나 재미있는지, 정말이지 더없이 재미있는 분이잖아요!"], ["I am very fond of hearing Miss Bates talk.", "저는 미스 베이츠가 떠드는 걸 듣는 게 아주 좋거든요."], ["And I need not bring the whole family, you know.”", "그리고 알다시피 온 가족을 다 데려올 필요는 없으니까요.\""]], [["Here Mr. Weston joined them, and on hearing what was proposed, gave it his decided approbation.", "여기서 미스터 웨스턴이 끼어들었고, 어떤 제안이 나왔는지 듣고는 단호히 찬성했다."]], [["“Aye, do, Frank.—Go and fetch Miss Bates, and let us end the matter at once.", "\"그래, 그렇게 하게, 프랭크. 가서 미스 베이츠를 모셔 와서 이 문제를 단번에 매듭짓세."], ["She will enjoy the scheme, I am sure; and I do not know a properer person for shewing us how to do away difficulties.", "그분이 이 계획을 즐거워할 게 분명해. 그리고 어려운 일을 풀어 나가는 법을 보여 주기에 그분만큼 알맞은 사람도 없을 걸세."], ["Fetch Miss Bates.", "미스 베이츠를 모셔 와."], ["We are growing a little too nice.", "우리가 지금 좀 까다롭게 굴고 있구먼."], ["She is a standing lesson of how to be happy.", "그분이야말로 어떻게 하면 행복할 수 있는지를 보여 주는 살아 있는 본보기지."], ["But fetch them both.", "아니, 두 분을 다 모셔 오게."], ["Invite them both.”", "두 분 다 청해 와.\""]], [["“Both sir!", "\"두 분 다요, 어르신!"], ["Can the old lady?”...", "그 노부인이 오실 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“The old lady!", "\"노부인이라니!"], ["No, the young lady, to be sure.", "아니지, 당연히 그 젊은 아가씨를 말하는 거지."], ["I shall think you a great blockhead, Frank, if you bring the aunt without the niece.”", "프랭크, 자네가 조카는 빼고 이모만 모셔 온다면, 나는 자네를 아주 둔한 멍청이로 여길 걸세.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I beg your pardon, sir.", "죄송합니다, 어르신."], ["I did not immediately recollect.", "제가 미처 떠올리지 못했네요."], ["Undoubtedly if you wish it, I will endeavour to persuade them both.”", "물론 어르신께서 바라신다면, 두 분 다 모셔 오도록 애써 보겠습니다.\""], ["And away he ran.", "그러고는 그는 달려 나갔다."]], [["Long before he reappeared, attending the short, neat, brisk-moving aunt, and her elegant niece,—Mrs. Weston, like a sweet-tempered woman and a good wife, had examined the passage again, and found the evils of it much less than she had supposed before—indeed very trifling; and here ended the difficulties of decision.", "키 작고 단정하며 동작이 잰 이모와 그 우아한 조카를 모시고 그가 다시 나타나기 한참 전에 — 웨스턴 부인은 마음씨 곱고 어진 아내답게 그 복도를 다시 한번 살펴보고는, 그 폐단이 앞서 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 덜하다는 것을, 실로 아주 사소하다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["All the rest, in speculation at least, was perfectly smooth.", "그리하여 결정의 어려움은 여기서 끝이 났다. 적어도 머릿속 궁리로는, 나머지가 죄다 더없이 매끄러웠다."], ["All the minor arrangements of table and chair, lights and music, tea and supper, made themselves; or were left as mere trifles to be settled at any time between Mrs. Weston and Mrs.", "탁자며 의자, 조명과 음악, 차와 만찬에 관한 자잘한 채비는 죄다 저절로 정해지거나, 웨스턴 부인과 미시즈 스토크스가 아무 때나 의논해 처리하면 될 그저 사소한 일로 미뤄 두었다."], ["Stokes.—Every body invited, was certainly to come; Frank had already written to Enscombe to propose staying a few days beyond his fortnight, which could not possibly be refused.", "초대받은 사람은 누구나 분명 올 터였다. 프랭크는 이미 엔스컴에 편지를 띄워 보름 기한을 며칠 더 넘겨 머물게 해 달라고 청해 둔 참이었고, 그것이 거절될 리는 도무지 없었다."], ["And a delightful dance it was to be.", "그리하여 그것은 즐거운 무도회가 될 참이었다."]], [["Most cordially, when Miss Bates arrived, did she agree that it must.", "미스 베이츠가 도착했을 때, 그녀는 더없이 진심으로 이것이 즐거운 무도회가 될 거라는 데 동의했다."], ["As a counsellor she was not wanted; but as an approver, (a much safer character,) she was truly welcome.", "조언자로서는 쓸모가 없었으나, 찬동자로서는 (그쪽이 훨씬 더 안전한 자리였다) 진정 환영할 만했다."], ["Her approbation, at once general and minute, warm and incessant, could not but please; and for another half-hour they were all walking to and fro, between the different rooms, some suggesting, some attending, and all in happy enjoyment of the future.", "두루 미치면서도 시시콜콜하고, 따뜻하면서도 그칠 줄 모르는 그녀의 찬사는 흐뭇하지 않을 수 없었다. 그리하여 다시 반 시간 동안, 모두가 이 방 저 방 사이를 오가며 누구는 의견을 내고 누구는 귀를 기울이며, 다 같이 다가올 일을 행복하게 즐겼다."], ["The party did not break up without Emma’s being positively secured for the two first dances by the hero of the evening, nor without her overhearing Mr.", "그 모임은, 엠마가 그날 저녁의 주인공에게 첫 두 곡을 확실히 약속받기 전에는, 또한 미스터 웨스턴이 아내에게 \"그 애가 엠마에게 청했다오, 여보."], ["Weston whisper to his wife, “He has asked her, my dear.", "잘됐어. 그럴 줄 알았다니까!\""], ["That’s right. I knew he would!”", "하고 속삭이는 것을 그녀가 엿듣기 전에는, 결코 끝나지 않았다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter XII", "# 제2권 12장"]], [["One thing only was wanting to make the prospect of the ball completely satisfactory to Emma—its being fixed for a day within the granted term of Frank Churchill’s stay in Surry; for, in spite of Mr.", "무도회의 전망이 엠마에게 완전히 흡족한 것이 되기에 모자란 것은 단 하나뿐이었다 — 그것이 프랭크 처칠이 서리에 머물도록 허락받은 기한 안의 날로 정해지는 일이었다."], ["Weston’s confidence, she could not think it so very impossible that the Churchills might not allow their nephew to remain a day beyond his fortnight.", "미스터 웨스턴은 자신했지만, 엠마는 처칠 부부가 조카에게 보름을 단 하루라도 넘겨 머물도록 허락하지 않으리라는 것이 그리 불가능한 일이라고는 생각할 수 없었던 것이다."], ["But this was not judged feasible.", "그러나 그것은 가능한 일로 여겨지지 않았다."], ["The preparations must take their time, nothing could be properly ready till the third week were entered on, and for a few days they must be planning, proceeding and hoping in uncertainty—at the risk—in her opinion, the great risk, of its being all in vain.", "준비에는 시간이 들어야 했고, 셋째 주에 접어들기 전에는 무엇 하나 제대로 갖추어질 수 없었으므로, 며칠 동안은 불확실한 가운데 계획하고 진행하고 바라야 했다 — 그 모든 것이 헛수고가 될 위험을 — 엠마가 보기에는 큰 위험을 무릅쓰고서."]], [["Enscombe however was gracious, gracious in fact, if not in word.", "그러나 엔스컴은 너그러웠다. 말로는 아니더라도 실제로는 너그러웠다."], ["His wish of staying longer evidently did not please; but it was not opposed.", "조카가 더 머물고 싶어 하는 것이 분명 그들의 마음에 들지는 않았으나, 반대하지는 않았다."], ["All was safe and prosperous; and as the removal of one solicitude generally makes way for another, Emma, being now certain of her ball, began to adopt as the next vexation Mr.", "만사가 안전하고 순조로웠다. 그리고 한 가지 근심이 사라지면 대개 다른 근심이 그 자리를 차지하는 법이라, 이제 무도회를 확신하게 된 엠마는 다음 골칫거리로 미스터 나이틀리의 약 올리는 무관심을 떠안기 시작했다."], ["Knightley’s provoking indifference about it. Either because he did not dance himself, or because the plan had been formed without his being consulted, he seemed resolved that it should not interest him, determined against its exciting any present curiosity, or affording him any future amusement.", "자신이 춤을 추지 않아서인지, 아니면 그 계획이 자기와 의논 없이 세워져서인지, 그는 그 일에 관심을 두지 않기로 작정한 듯했고, 그 일이 지금 어떤 호기심도 일으키지 못하게, 앞으로 어떤 즐거움도 주지 못하게 단단히 마음먹은 것 같았다."], ["To her voluntary communications Emma could get no more approving reply, than,", "엠마가 자진해서 이런저런 소식을 전해도, 그에게서 돌아오는 가장 호의적인 대답이라야 이런 정도였다."]], [["“Very well. If the Westons think it worth while to be at all this trouble for a few hours of noisy entertainment, I have nothing to say against it, but that they shall not chuse pleasures for me.—Oh! yes, I must be there; I could not refuse; and I will keep as much awake as I can; but I would rather be at home, looking over William Larkins’s week’s account; much rather, I confess.—Pleasure in seeing dancing!—not I, indeed—I never look at it—I do not know who does.—Fine dancing, I believe, like virtue, must be its own reward. Those who are standing by are usually thinking of something very different.”", "\"좋네. 웨스턴 내외가 몇 시간의 시끌벅적한 여흥을 위해 그 수고를 다 들일 만하다고 여긴다면, 나로서야 반대할 말은 없네. 다만 그들이 내 즐거움까지 골라 주지는 말았으면 하네. — 아, 그럼, 가긴 가야지. 거절할 수는 없으니까. 그리고 되도록 졸지 않으려고 애써 볼 작정이네. 하지만 솔직히 말하면, 집에 앉아 윌리엄 라킨스의 한 주 치 장부나 들여다보는 편이 훨씬 좋겠어. 훨씬 좋겠네. — 춤 구경이 즐겁다니! — 나는 천만에. 나는 그런 건 쳐다보지도 않네. 도대체 누가 그걸 본다는 건지 모르겠군. — 멋진 춤이란, 내가 보기엔, 미덕과 마찬가지로 그 자체가 보상일 수밖에 없어. 곁에 서 있는 사람들은 대개 전혀 딴생각을 하고 있게 마련이지.\""]], [["This Emma felt was aimed at her; and it made her quite angry.", "엠마는 이 말이 자신을 겨냥한 것임을 느꼈고, 그래서 적잖이 화가 났다."], ["It was not in compliment to Jane Fairfax however that he was so indifferent, or so indignant; he was not guided by her feelings in reprobating the ball, for she enjoyed the thought of it to an extraordinary degree.", "그러나 그가 그토록 무관심하거나 그토록 못마땅해하는 것이 제인 페어팩스를 위해서는 아니었다. 그가 무도회를 깎아내리는 것은 제인의 감정에 따른 것이 아니었으니, 제인은 무도회 생각만으로도 유난히 큰 기쁨을 누리고 있었기 때문이다."], ["It made her animated—open hearted—she voluntarily said;—", "무도회는 제인을 활기차게 — 거리낌 없게 만들었고, 그녀는 제 입으로 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["Miss Woodhouse, I hope nothing may happen to prevent the ball.", "미스 우드하우스, 무도회를 망칠 일이 아무것도 일어나지 않기를 바라요."], ["What a disappointment it would be!", "그렇게 되면 얼마나 실망스러울까요!"], ["I do look forward to it, I own, with very great pleasure.”", "저는 정말이지, 솔직히 말해서 무척이나 큰 기쁨으로 그날을 기다리고 있답니다.\""]], [["It was not to oblige Jane Fairfax therefore that he would have preferred the society of William Larkins.", "그러니 그가 윌리엄 라킨스와 함께 있는 편을 더 좋아한 것은 제인 페어팩스의 비위를 맞추기 위해서가 아니었다. 천만에!"], ["No!—she was more and more convinced that Mrs. Weston was quite mistaken in that surmise.", "— 엠마는 그 추측에서 웨스턴 부인이 완전히 틀렸다는 것을 점점 더 확신하게 되었다."], ["There was a great deal of friendly and of compassionate attachment on his side—but no love.", "그쪽에는 다정하고 측은히 여기는 마음이 듬뿍 있었으나 — 사랑은 없었다."]], [["Alas!", "아아!"], ["there was soon no leisure for quarrelling with Mr. Knightley.", "그러나 미스터 나이틀리와 입씨름할 여유는 곧 없어졌다."], ["Two days of joyful security were immediately followed by the over-throw of every thing.", "즐겁고 안심하던 이틀이 지나자마자 모든 것이 뒤엎어졌다. 미스터 처칠에게서 조카에게 당장 돌아오라고 재촉하는 편지가 왔다."], ["A letter arrived from Mr. Churchill to urge his nephew’s instant return.", "처칠 부인이 편치 않다는 것이었다 — 조카 없이는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 너무나 편치 않다고."], ["Mrs. Churchill was unwell—far too unwell to do without him; she had been in a very suffering state (so said her husband) when writing to her nephew two days before, though from her usual unwillingness to give pain, and constant habit of never thinking of herself, she had not mentioned it; but now she was too ill to trifle, and must entreat him to set off for Enscombe without delay.", "이틀 전 조카에게 편지를 쓸 때 이미 (남편의 말로는) 몹시 고통스러운 상태였으나, 남에게 고통을 주기 싫어하는 평소의 성정과 결코 제 몸은 생각하지 않는 한결같은 버릇 탓에 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않았던 것인데, 이제는 너무 아파서 미적거릴 수 없으니, 부디 지체 말고 엔스컴으로 떠나 달라고 간청해야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["The substance of this letter was forwarded to Emma, in a note from Mrs. Weston, instantly.", "이 편지의 골자는 웨스턴 부인의 쪽지를 통해 곧바로 엠마에게 전해졌다."], ["As to his going, it was inevitable.", "그가 떠나는 일은 피할 수 없었다."], ["He must be gone within a few hours, though without feeling any real alarm for his aunt, to lessen his repugnance.", "그는 몇 시간 안에 가야 했다 — 다만 숙모를 진심으로 걱정하지는 않았기에, 떠나기 싫은 마음을 덜어 줄 것이 없었다."], ["He knew her illnesses; they never occurred but for her own convenience.", "그는 숙모의 병이 어떤 것인지 알고 있었다. 그 병은 오로지 숙모 자신에게 편할 때에만 도졌던 것이다."]], [["Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인은 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["Weston added, “that he could only allow himself time to hurry to Highbury, after breakfast, and take leave of the few friends there whom he could suppose to feel any interest in him; and that he might be expected at Hartfield very soon.”", "\"그는 아침 식사 후에 서둘러 하이베리로 달려가, 그에게 조금이라도 관심을 가질 만한 그곳의 몇몇 벗에게 작별을 고할 시간밖에 낼 수 없으며, 머지않아 하트필드에 들를 것으로 보면 된다\"고."]], [["This wretched note was the finale of Emma’s breakfast.", "이 비참한 쪽지가 엠마의 아침 식사에 종지부를 찍었다."], ["When once it had been read, there was no doing any thing, but lament and exclaim.", "그것을 한번 읽고 나니, 탄식하고 한탄하는 것 말고는 아무 일도 할 수 없었다. 무도회를 잃는 일 — 그 청년을 잃는 일 — 그리고 그 청년이 느끼고 있을 그 모든 감정이라니!"], ["The loss of the ball—the loss of the young man—and all that the young man might be feeling!—It was too wretched!—Such a delightful evening as it would have been!—Every body so happy!", "— 너무나 비참했다! — 얼마나 즐거운 저녁이 되었을 것인가!"], ["and she and her partner the happiest!—“I said it would be so,”", "— 모두가 그토록 행복하고! 그녀와 그녀의 파트너가 가장 행복하고!"], ["was the only consolation.", "— \"내가 이렇게 될 거라고 말했지\"가 유일한 위안이었다."]], [["Her father’s feelings were quite distinct. He thought principally of Mrs.", "아버지의 심정은 사뭇 달랐다."], ["Churchill’s illness, and wanted to know how she was treated; and as for the ball, it was shocking to have dear Emma disappointed; but they would all be safer at home.", "그는 주로 처칠 부인의 병을 생각하며 그녀가 어떤 치료를 받고 있는지 알고 싶어 했다. 그리고 무도회로 말하자면, 사랑하는 엠마가 실망한 것은 안타까운 일이지만, 모두 집에 있는 편이 더 안전하리라는 것이었다."]], [["Emma was ready for her visitor some time before he appeared; but if this reflected at all upon his impatience, his sorrowful look and total want of spirits when he did come might redeem him.", "엠마는 손님이 나타나기 한참 전부터 그를 맞을 채비를 끝내고 있었다. 그러나 이것이 그의 조급함을 어떻게든 깎아내리는 것이었다 해도, 막상 그가 왔을 때 그 슬픈 표정과 완전히 가라앉은 기색은 그를 만회시켜 줄 만했다."], ["He felt the going away almost too much to speak of it.", "그는 떠나는 일을 입에 올리기조차 너무 괴로워하는 듯했다."], ["His dejection was most evident.", "그의 침울함은 더없이 또렷했다."], ["He sat really lost in thought for the first few minutes; and when rousing himself, it was only to say,", "그는 처음 얼마 동안 정말로 생각에 잠겨 멍하니 앉아 있었고, 정신을 차렸다 싶으면 겨우 이런 말을 할 뿐이었다."]], [["“Of all horrid things, leave-taking is the worst.”", "\"온갖 끔찍한 일 중에서도 작별이 제일 끔찍합니다.\""]], [["“But you will come again,”", "\"하지만 또 오실 거잖아요.\""], ["said Emma.", "엠마가 말했다."], ["“This will not be your only visit to Randalls.”", "\"이번이 랜덜스에 오는 마지막 방문은 아닐 테니까요.\""]], [["“Ah!—(shaking his head)—the uncertainty of when I may be able to return!—I shall try for it with a zeal!—It will be the object of all my thoughts and cares!—and if my uncle and aunt go to town this spring—but I am afraid—they did not stir last spring—I am afraid it is a custom gone for ever.”", "\"아! — (고개를 저으며) — 언제 다시 올 수 있을지 그것이 막막합니다! — 열성을 다해 애써 보겠습니다! — 그것이 제 모든 생각과 마음 쓰임의 목표가 될 거예요! — 그리고 외삼촌 내외분이 올봄에 런던으로 올라가시면 — 하지만 두렵게도 — 지난봄에는 꼼짝도 안 하셨지요 — 두렵게도 그건 영영 사라진 관례가 아닌가 싶습니다.\""]], [["“Our poor ball must be quite given up.”", "\"우리의 가엾은 무도회는 아주 단념해야겠네요.\""]], [["“Ah! that ball!—why did we wait for any thing?—why not seize the pleasure at once?—How often is happiness destroyed by preparation, foolish preparation!—You told us it would be so.—Oh! Miss Woodhouse, why are you always so right?”", "\"아! 그 무도회 말입니다! — 우리는 왜 무얼 기다린 걸까요? — 왜 그 즐거움을 당장 붙잡지 못했을까요? — 행복이 준비 때문에, 어리석은 준비 때문에 망가지는 일이 얼마나 잦은지요! — 이렇게 될 거라고 당신이 말씀하셨지요. — 아! 미스 우드하우스, 당신은 어째서 늘 그렇게 옳으신지요?\""]], [["“Indeed, I am very sorry to be right in this instance.", "\"정말이지, 이번 경우만큼은 옳았던 것이 무척 유감스럽네요."], ["I would much rather have been merry than wise.”", "영리하기보다는 즐거웠던 편이 훨씬 좋았을 텐데요.\""]], [["“If I can come again, we are still to have our ball.", "\"제가 다시 올 수 있다면, 무도회는 그래도 열어야지요."], ["My father depends on it.", "아버지께서 그것만 믿고 계세요."], ["Do not forget your engagement.”", "약속을 잊지 마세요.\""]], [["Emma looked graciously.", "엠마는 너그러운 표정을 지어 보였다."]], [["“Such a fortnight as it has been!”", "\"이번 보름이라니!\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he continued; “every day more precious and more delightful than the day before!—every day making me less fit to bear any other place.", "\"하루하루가 그 전날보다 더 소중하고 더 즐거웠어요! — 하루하루가 다른 어느 곳도 견딜 수 없게 만들었답니다."], ["Happy those, who can remain at Highbury!”", "하이베리에 남아 있을 수 있는 사람들은 행복하겠지요!\""]], [["“As you do us such ample justice now,”", "\"이제 와서 우리를 그토록 후하게 쳐 주시니,\" 엠마가 웃으며 말했다."], ["said Emma, laughing, “I will venture to ask, whether you did not come a little doubtfully at first?", "\"한 가지 여쭤 봐도 될까요. 처음에는 적잖이 미심쩍어하며 오신 것 아니었나요?"], ["Do not we rather surpass your expectations?", "우리가 당신 기대를 좀 넘어선 것 아닐까요?"], ["I am sure we do.", "분명 그랬을 거예요."], ["I am sure you did not much expect to like us.", "우리를 좋아하게 되리라고는 그다지 기대하지 않으셨을 거라고 확신해요."], ["You would not have been so long in coming, if you had had a pleasant idea of Highbury.”", "하이베리에 대해 즐거운 생각을 품으셨다면, 그렇게 오래 미루며 오시지 않았을 테니까요.\""]], [["He laughed rather consciously; and though denying the sentiment, Emma was convinced that it had been so.", "그는 다소 켕기는 듯 웃었다. 그리고 비록 그 마음을 부인하기는 했으나, 엠마는 그것이 사실이었음을 확신했다."]], [["“And you must be off this very morning?”", "\"그래서 바로 오늘 아침에 떠나셔야 한다고요?\""]], [["“Yes; my father is to join me here: we shall walk back together, and I must be off immediately.", "\"네. 아버지가 여기서 저와 합류하실 거예요."], ["I am almost afraid that every moment will bring him.”", "함께 걸어 돌아가야 하니, 저는 곧장 떠나야 합니다. 매 순간 아버지가 오시지 않을까 거의 조마조마할 지경이에요.\""]], [["“Not five minutes to spare even for your friends Miss Fairfax and Miss Bates?", "\"벗인 미스 페어팩스와 미스 베이츠에게도 오 분조차 낼 수 없다고요?"], ["How unlucky!", "운도 없으셔라!"], ["Miss Bates’s powerful, argumentative mind might have strengthened yours.”", "미스 베이츠의 그 막강하고 논리정연한 정신력이 당신 마음을 다잡아 줄 수도 있었을 텐데요.\""]], [["“Yes—I have called there; passing the door, I thought it better.", "\"네 — 거기엔 들렀습니다. 문 앞을 지나면서, 그러는 편이 낫겠다 싶었지요."], ["It was a right thing to do.", "마땅히 할 일이었으니까요."], ["I went in for three minutes, and was detained by Miss Bates’s being absent.", "삼 분만 있으려고 들어갔는데, 미스 베이츠가 안 계셔서 발이 묶였어요."], ["She was out; and I felt it impossible not to wait till she came in.", "외출 중이셨거든요. 들어오실 때까지 기다리지 않을 수 없을 것 같더군요."], ["She is a woman that one may, that one must laugh at; but that one would not wish to slight.", "그분은 누구나 웃을 수 있는, 아니 웃지 않을 수 없는 분이지만, 그렇다고 소홀히 대하고 싶지는 않은 분이지요."], ["It was better to pay my visit, then”—", "그러니 그때 방문해 두는 편이 나았어요 —\""]], [["He hesitated, got up, walked to a window.", "그는 머뭇거리더니, 일어나서 창가로 걸어갔다."]], [["“In short,”", "\"요컨대,\""], ["said he, “perhaps, Miss Woodhouse—I think you can hardly be quite without suspicion”—", "그가 말했다. \"어쩌면, 미스 우드하우스 — 당신도 짐작이 전혀 없지는 않으실 듯한데 —\""]], [["He looked at her, as if wanting to read her thoughts.", "그는 그녀의 생각을 읽으려는 듯 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["She hardly knew what to say.", "엠마는 무어라 말해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["It seemed like the forerunner of something absolutely serious, which she did not wish.", "그것은 그녀가 바라지 않는, 무언가 더없이 진지한 일의 전조처럼 느껴졌다."], ["Forcing herself to speak, therefore, in the hope of putting it by, she calmly said,", "그래서 그것을 비켜 가게 하려는 마음에서, 억지로 입을 열어 차분하게 말했다."]], [["“You are quite in the right; it was most natural to pay your visit, then”—", "\"옳은 말씀이세요. 그때 방문하시는 것이 가장 자연스러운 일이었지요 —\""]], [["He was silent.", "그는 잠자코 있었다."], ["She believed he was looking at her; probably reflecting on what she had said, and trying to understand the manner.", "그가 자신을 바라보고 있다고, 아마 자신이 한 말을 곱씹으며 그 말투를 헤아려 보려 하고 있다고 그녀는 믿었다."], ["She heard him sigh.", "그가 한숨짓는 소리가 들렸다."], ["It was natural for him to feel that he had cause to sigh.", "그에게는 한숨지을 까닭이 있다고 느끼는 것이 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["He could not believe her to be encouraging him.", "그는 그녀가 자신을 부추기고 있다고는 믿을 수 없었던 것이다."], ["A few awkward moments passed, and he sat down again; and in a more determined manner said,", "어색한 몇 순간이 흐른 뒤, 그는 다시 자리에 앉았고, 한결 단호한 태도로 말했다."]], [["“It was something to feel that all the rest of my time might be given to Hartfield.", "\"제게 남은 시간을 모두 하트필드에 바칠 수 있다고 느끼는 것만으로도 무언가 의미가 있었어요."], ["My regard for Hartfield is most warm”—", "하트필드를 향한 제 마음은 더없이 따뜻합니다 —\""]], [["He stopt again, rose again, and seemed quite embarrassed.—He was more in love with her than Emma had supposed; and who can say how it might have ended, if his father had not made his appearance?", "그는 또 말을 멈추었고, 또 일어섰으며, 몹시 당혹스러워하는 듯했다."], ["Mr.", "— 그는 엠마가 짐작했던 것보다 그녀를 더 사랑하고 있었다."], ["Woodhouse soon followed; and the necessity of exertion made him composed.", "그리고 그의 아버지가 나타나지 않았더라면 그 일이 어떻게 끝났을지 누가 알겠는가? 곧이어 미스터 우드하우스도 따라 들어왔고, 마음을 다잡아야 할 필요가 그를 진정시켰다."]], [["A very few minutes more, however, completed the present trial.", "그러나 그 후 몇 분이 채 지나지 않아 지금의 시련은 끝이 났다."], ["Mr. Weston, always alert when business was to be done, and as incapable of procrastinating any evil that was inevitable, as of foreseeing any that was doubtful, said, “It was time to go;”", "미스터 웨스턴은 할 일이 있을 때면 언제나 민첩한 사람이었고, 피할 수 없는 궂은일을 미루지 못하는 만큼이나 미심쩍은 궂은일을 미리 내다보지도 못하는 사람인지라, \"갈 시간이오\"라고 말했다."], ["and the young man, though he might and did sigh, could not but agree, to take leave.", "그러자 청년은 한숨지을 수도 있었고 또 실제로 한숨지었으나, 작별을 고하는 데 동의하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“I shall hear about you all,”", "\"여러분 소식은 다 듣게 될 거예요.\""], ["said he; “that is my chief consolation.", "그가 말했다. \"그게 제 가장 큰 위안이지요."], ["I shall hear of every thing that is going on among you.", "여러분 사이에 일어나는 모든 일을 듣게 될 겁니다."], ["I have engaged Mrs. Weston to correspond with me.", "제가 웨스턴 부인께 편지 왕래를 부탁드려 두었어요."], ["She has been so kind as to promise it.", "친절하게도 약속해 주셨고요."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["the blessing of a female correspondent, when one is really interested in the absent!—she will tell me every thing.", "정말로 멀리 있는 사람에게 관심이 있을 때, 편지를 주고받을 여성이 있다는 건 얼마나 큰 축복인지요! — 그분은 제게 모든 걸 알려 주실 거예요."], ["In her letters I shall be at dear Highbury again.”", "그분의 편지 속에서 저는 다시 사랑하는 하이베리에 와 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["A very friendly shake of the hand, a very earnest “Good-bye,”", "더없이 다정한 악수와 더없이 진심 어린 \"안녕히 계세요\"로 그의 말은 끝났고, 문은 이내 프랭크 처칠을 밖에 남겨 둔 채 닫혔다."], ["closed the speech, and the door had soon shut out Frank Churchill.", "통보도 짧았고 — 만남도 짧았다. 그는 가 버렸다."], ["Short had been the notice—short their meeting; he was gone; and Emma felt so sorry to part, and foresaw so great a loss to their little society from his absence as to begin to be afraid of being too sorry, and feeling it too much.", "그리고 엠마는 헤어지는 것이 너무나 서운했고, 그가 없으면 자기들의 그 작은 모임에 얼마나 큰 손실이 될지 너무나 절실히 내다본 나머지, 자기가 지나치게 서운해하고 지나치게 가슴 아파하는 것은 아닌지 두려워지기 시작했다."]], [["It was a sad change.", "슬픈 변화였다."], ["They had been meeting almost every day since his arrival.", "그가 온 뒤로 두 사람은 거의 날마다 만나 왔던 것이다."], ["Certainly his being at Randalls had given great spirit to the last two weeks—indescribable spirit; the idea, the expectation of seeing him which every morning had brought, the assurance of his attentions, his liveliness, his manners!", "그가 랜덜스에 있었던 것이 지난 두 주에 엄청난 생기를 — 말로 다 할 수 없는 생기를 불어넣어 준 것은 분명했다. 아침마다 떠오르던 그를 만나리라는 생각, 그 기대, 그의 관심에 대한 확신, 그의 활기, 그의 거동!"], ["It had been a very happy fortnight, and forlorn must be the sinking from it into the common course of Hartfield days.", "더없이 행복한 보름이었으니, 거기서 내려와 하트필드의 평범한 일상으로 가라앉는 것은 적막한 일일 수밖에 없었다. 게다가 다른 모든 매력에 더하여, 그는 자신을 사랑한다고 그녀에게 거의 말하다시피 했던 것이다."], ["To complete every other recommendation, he had almost told her that he loved her.", "그가 어느 정도의 애정의 힘을, 혹은 어느 정도의 한결같음을 지녔는지는 또 다른 문제였다."], ["What strength, or what constancy of affection he might be subject to, was another point; but at present she could not doubt his having a decidedly warm admiration, a conscious preference of herself; and this persuasion, joined to all the rest, made her think that she must be a little in love with him, in spite of every previous determination against it.", "그러나 지금으로서는, 그가 자신에게 분명히 따뜻한 흠모를, 스스로 의식한 편애를 품고 있다는 것을 의심할 수 없었다. 그리고 이 확신이 그 밖의 모든 것과 어우러져, 그녀로 하여금 — 이에 반대했던 그간의 온갖 결심에도 불구하고 — 자신이 그를 조금쯤은 사랑하고 있는 게 틀림없다고 여기게 만들었다."]], [["“I certainly must,”", "\"분명 그래.\""], ["said she.", "그녀는 말했다."], ["“This sensation of listlessness, weariness, stupidity, this disinclination to sit down and employ myself, this feeling of every thing’s being dull and insipid about the house!— I must be in love; I should be the oddest creature in the world if I were not—for a few weeks at least.", "\"이 무기력하고, 노곤하고, 멍한 기분, 앉아서 무얼 하기도 싫은 이 마음, 집 안의 모든 것이 따분하고 시시하게만 느껴지는 이 기분! — 나는 분명 사랑에 빠진 거야."], ["Well! evil to some is always good to others.", "안 그렇다면 나는 세상에서 가장 별난 사람일 테지 — 적어도 몇 주 동안은."], ["I shall have many fellow-mourners for the ball, if not for Frank Churchill; but Mr.", "뭐! 누군가에게 나쁜 일은 늘 다른 누군가에게는 좋은 일이지."], ["Knightley will be happy.", "프랭크 처칠 때문은 아니더라도, 무도회 때문이라면 함께 슬퍼해 줄 사람이 많을 거야."], ["He may spend the evening with his dear William Larkins now if he likes.”", "하지만 미스터 나이틀리는 좋아하겠지. 이제 그분은 마음만 내키면 그 사랑하는 윌리엄 라킨스와 함께 저녁을 보낼 수 있을 테니까.\""]], [["Mr. Knightley, however, shewed no triumphant happiness.", "그러나 미스터 나이틀리는 의기양양한 기쁨 따위는 조금도 내비치지 않았다."], ["He could not say that he was sorry on his own account; his very cheerful look would have contradicted him if he had; but he said, and very steadily, that he was sorry for the disappointment of the others, and with considerable kindness added,", "그는 자기로서는 유감스럽다고 말할 수 없었다 — 그랬다면 그 더없이 명랑한 표정이 그 말을 반박했을 것이다. 그러나 그는 다른 사람들이 실망한 것은 안타깝다고, 그것도 아주 침착하게 말했고, 적잖이 다정한 투로 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“You, Emma, who have so few opportunities of dancing, you are really out of luck; you are very much out of luck!”", "\"엠마, 춤출 기회가 그토록 드문 자네야말로 정말 운이 없네그려. 자네는 운이 아주 없어!\""]], [["It was some days before she saw Jane Fairfax, to judge of her honest regret in this woeful change; but when they did meet, her composure was odious.", "이 비통한 변화에 대한 제인 페어팩스의 솔직한 유감을 헤아려 볼 수 있게 그녀를 만나기까지는 며칠이 걸렸다. 그러나 막상 두 사람이 만났을 때, 제인의 그 태연함은 얄미웠다."], ["She had been particularly unwell, however, suffering from headache to a degree, which made her aunt declare, that had the ball taken place, she did not think Jane could have attended it; and it was charity to impute some of her unbecoming indifference to the languor of ill-health.", "다만 그녀는 유난히 몸이 좋지 않았는데, 두통이 어찌나 심했던지, 무도회가 열렸더라도 제인은 참석할 수 없었을 것 같다고 그 이모가 단언할 정도였다. 그러니 그녀의 어울리지 않는 무관심의 얼마간을 병약함에서 오는 나른함 탓으로 돌려주는 것이 너그러운 일이었다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter XIII", "# 제2권 13장"]], [["Emma continued to entertain no doubt of her being in love.", "엠마는 자기가 사랑에 빠졌다는 것을 여전히 추호도 의심하지 않았다. 다만 그 정도가 얼마나 되는지에 대해서만 생각이 오락가락했다."], ["Her ideas only varied as to the how much.", "처음에는 꽤 깊다고 여겼다가, 나중에는 그저 약간일 뿐이라고 여겼다."], ["At first, she thought it was a good deal; and afterwards, but little.", "프랭크 처칠의 이야기를 듣는 것은 큰 즐거움이었다. 그리고 그를 위해서라도 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인을 보는 일이 그 어느 때보다 더 즐거웠다."], ["She had great pleasure in hearing Frank Churchill talked of; and, for his sake, greater pleasure than ever in seeing Mr. and Mrs.", "그녀는 그를 자주 생각했고, 그가 어떻게 지내는지, 기분은 어떤지, 숙모는 어떤지, 올봄에 다시 랜덜스로 올 가망이 있는지 알 수 있도록 편지가 오기를 못 견디게 기다렸다."], ["Weston; she was very often thinking of him, and quite impatient for a letter, that she might know how he was, how were his spirits, how was his aunt, and what was the chance of his coming to Randalls again this spring.", "그러나 다른 한편으로, 자기가 불행하다고는 도무지 인정할 수 없었고, 첫날 아침이 지난 뒤로는 여느 때보다 일할 마음이 줄었다고도 인정할 수 없었다. 그녀는 여전히 분주하고 명랑했다."], ["But, on the other hand, she could not admit herself to be unhappy, nor, after the first morning, to be less disposed for employment than usual; she was still busy and cheerful; and, pleasing as he was, she could yet imagine him to have faults; and farther, though thinking of him so much, and, as she sat drawing or working, forming a thousand amusing schemes for the progress and close of their attachment, fancying interesting dialogues, and inventing elegant letters; the conclusion of every imaginary declaration on his side was that she refused him.", "그가 아무리 마음에 든다 해도, 그에게 결점이 있으리라고 상상할 수는 있었다. 게다가 그를 그토록 자주 생각하면서, 그림을 그리거나 바느질을 하며 앉아 두 사람의 연정이 무르익고 매듭지어지는 갖가지 흥미로운 구상을 천 가지나 짜내고, 흥미진진한 대화를 그려 보고, 우아한 편지를 지어내곤 했지만 — 그가 고백하는 상상 속 모든 장면의 결말은 언제나 그녀가 그를 거절하는 것이었다."], ["Their affection was always to subside into friendship.", "두 사람의 애정은 늘 우정으로 가라앉기 마련이었다."], ["Every thing tender and charming was to mark their parting; but still they were to part.", "무엇 하나 다정하고 매혹적이지 않은 것이 없는 이별이었으되, 어쨌든 두 사람은 헤어지기로 되어 있었다."], ["When she became sensible of this, it struck her that she could not be very much in love; for in spite of her previous and fixed determination never to quit her father, never to marry, a strong attachment certainly must produce more of a struggle than she could foresee in her own feelings.", "이 사실을 깨닫고 나니, 자기가 그리 깊이 사랑에 빠진 것은 아닐 수도 있다는 생각이 들었다. 아버지를 결코 떠나지 않겠다, 결코 결혼하지 않겠다는 이전부터 굳힌 결심이 있다 한들, 정말로 강렬한 연정이라면 그녀가 제 마음속에서 예상할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 큰 갈등을 불러일으켰을 것이 분명했기 때문이다."]], [["“I do not find myself making any use of the word sacrifice,”", "\"나는 희생이라는 말을 도통 쓰지 않고 있어.\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["said she.—“In not one of all my clever replies, my delicate negatives, is there any allusion to making a sacrifice.", "\"내가 짜낸 그 영리한 답변들, 그 우아한 거절들 가운데 어느 하나에도 희생을 한다는 암시가 들어 있질 않잖아."], ["I do suspect that he is not really necessary to my happiness.", "아무래도 그 사람이 내 행복에 정말로 꼭 필요한 사람은 아닌 것 같아."], ["So much the better.", "그렇다면 차라리 잘된 일이지."], ["I certainly will not persuade myself to feel more than I do.", "나는 결단코 지금보다 더 많이 느끼도록 나 자신을 설득하지 않을 거야."], ["I am quite enough in love.", "나는 이만하면 충분히 사랑에 빠진 거야."], ["I should be sorry to be more.”", "더 빠진다면 오히려 곤란하지.\""]], [["Upon the whole, she was equally contented with her view of his feelings.", "대체로 그녀는 그의 감정에 대한 자기 판단에도 꼭 그만큼 흡족해했다."]], [["“He is undoubtedly very much in love—every thing denotes it—very much in love indeed!—and when he comes again, if his affection continue, I must be on my guard not to encourage it.—It would be most inexcusable to do otherwise, as my own mind is quite made up. Not that I imagine he can think I have been encouraging him hitherto. No, if he had believed me at all to share his feelings, he would not have been so wretched. Could he have thought himself encouraged, his looks and language at parting would have been different.—Still, however, I must be on my guard. This is in the supposition of his attachment continuing what it now is; but I do not know that I expect it will; I do not look upon him to be quite the sort of man—I do not altogether build upon his steadiness or constancy.—His feelings are warm, but I can imagine them rather changeable.—Every consideration of the subject, in short, makes me thankful that my happiness is not more deeply involved.—I shall do very well again after a little while—and then, it will be a good thing over; for they say every body is in love once in their lives, and I shall have been let off easily.”", "\"그 사람은 의심할 여지 없이 깊이 사랑에 빠졌어 — 모든 것이 그것을 말해 주는걸 — 정말이지 푹 빠진 거야! 그리고 그가 다시 오면, 그 연정이 그대로라면, 나는 그것을 부추기지 않도록 경계해야 해. 내 마음은 이미 확고하게 정해졌으니, 달리 행동한다면 그건 가장 변명의 여지 없는 짓이 될 거야. 그렇다고 그 사람이 지금껏 내가 자기를 부추겼다고 여길 수 있으리라는 건 아니야. 아니지, 내가 조금이라도 자기 감정을 함께 나눈다고 믿었더라면, 그토록 비참해하지는 않았을 테니까. 만약 부추김을 받았다고 생각했더라면, 헤어질 때 그의 표정과 말씨가 그렇지 않았을 거야. 그래도 어쨌든 나는 경계해야 해. 이건 그의 연정이 지금 그대로 이어진다는 가정 아래서 하는 말이지. 하지만 정말로 그러리라고 기대하는지는 모르겠어. 그 사람은 영 그런 부류의 남자가 아닌 것 같단 말이야. 나는 그의 한결같음이나 변함없음을 그리 믿지 않아. 그의 감정은 뜨겁지만, 나는 그것이 오히려 변덕스러우리라고 상상할 수 있어. 요컨대, 이 문제를 이리저리 따져 볼수록 내 행복이 더 깊이 얽히지 않은 것이 고마워질 따름이야. 나는 얼마 지나지 않아 다시 멀쩡해질 거야. 그러면 좋은 일 한 가지가 끝난 셈이지. 사람은 누구나 평생에 한 번은 사랑에 빠진다고들 하니, 나는 가볍게 넘어간 셈이 되겠지.\""]], [["When his letter to Mrs.", "그가 웨스턴 부인에게 보낸 편지가 도착하자, 엠마도 그것을 읽어 볼 수 있었다."], ["Weston arrived, Emma had the perusal of it; and she read it with a degree of pleasure and admiration which made her at first shake her head over her own sensations, and think she had undervalued their strength.", "그녀는 그 편지를 어찌나 즐겁고 감탄스럽게 읽었던지, 처음에는 자기 감정이 이래도 되나 싶어 고개를 절레절레 젓고, 자기가 그 감정의 강도를 너무 낮잡아 본 게 아닌가 생각할 정도였다. 길고 잘 쓴 편지였다."], ["It was a long, well-written letter, giving the particulars of his journey and of his feelings, expressing all the affection, gratitude, and respect which was natural and honourable, and describing every thing exterior and local that could be supposed attractive, with spirit and precision.", "여정과 자기 심정을 소상히 적고, 자연스럽고도 떳떳한 온갖 애정과 감사와 존경을 담았으며, 매력적이라 할 만한 바깥 풍경과 고장의 모습을 하나하나 생기 있고 정확하게 그려 냈다."], ["No suspicious flourishes now of apology or concern; it was the language of real feeling towards Mrs.", "이제는 사과나 염려를 늘어놓는 미심쩍은 미사여구가 없었다. 그것은 웨스턴 부인을 향한 진실한 감정의 언어였다."], ["Weston; and the transition from Highbury to Enscombe, the contrast between the places in some of the first blessings of social life was just enough touched on to shew how keenly it was felt, and how much more might have been said but for the restraints of propriety.—The charm of her own name was not wanting.", "하이베리에서 엔스컴으로 옮겨 가는 변화, 사교 생활의 으뜸가는 축복 몇 가지에서 두 곳이 이루는 대조도, 그것을 얼마나 절절히 느끼는지, 또 예의의 제약만 없었다면 얼마나 더 많은 말을 할 수 있었을지를 보여 줄 만큼만 딱 알맞게 건드려져 있었다. 그녀 자신의 이름이 주는 매력도 빠지지 않았다."], ["Miss Woodhouse appeared more than once, and never without a something of pleasing connexion, either a compliment to her taste, or a remembrance of what she had said; and in the very last time of its meeting her eye, unadorned as it was by any such broad wreath of gallantry, she yet could discern the effect of her influence and acknowledge the greatest compliment perhaps of all conveyed.", "'미스 우드하우스'라는 이름이 한 번 이상 등장했고, 언제나 무언가 기분 좋은 연관을 거느리고 있었으니 — 그녀의 안목을 칭찬하거나, 그녀가 했던 말을 떠올리거나 하는 식이었다. 그리고 그 이름이 마지막으로 그녀의 눈에 들어왔을 때, 비록 그런 거창한 찬사의 화환으로 꾸며져 있지는 않았으나, 그녀는 거기서 자기 영향력의 효과를 읽어 낼 수 있었고, 어쩌면 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 큰 찬사가 전해졌음을 인정할 수 있었다."], ["Compressed into the very lowest vacant corner were these words—“I had not a spare moment on Tuesday, as you know, for Miss Woodhouse’s beautiful little friend.", "맨 아래 빈 모서리에 잔뜩 눌러 적은 이런 말이 있었던 것이다. \"아시다시피 화요일에는 미스 우드하우스의 어여쁜 친구분께 짬을 낼 틈이 한 순간도 없었습니다."], ["Pray make my excuses and adieus to her.”", "부디 그분께 제 변명과 작별 인사를 대신 전해 주십시오.\""], ["This, Emma could not doubt, was all for herself.", "이것이 온전히 자기를 향한 것임을 엠마는 의심할 수 없었다."], ["Harriet was remembered only from being her friend.", "해리엇이 기억된 것은 오직 그녀의 친구였기 때문이었다."], ["His information and prospects as to Enscombe were neither worse nor better than had been anticipated; Mrs.", "엔스컴에 관한 그의 소식과 전망은 예상했던 것보다 나쁘지도 좋지도 않았다."], ["Churchill was recovering, and he dared not yet, even in his own imagination, fix a time for coming to Randalls again.", "처칠 부인은 회복 중이었고, 그는 아직 제 머릿속에서조차 랜덜스로 다시 올 날을 감히 못 박지 못하고 있었다."]], [["Gratifying, however, and stimulative as was the letter in the material part, its sentiments, she yet found, when it was folded up and returned to Mrs. Weston, that it had not added any lasting warmth, that she could still do without the writer, and that he must learn to do without her.", "그러나 그 편지가 알맹이며 정서에서 아무리 흐뭇하고 마음을 들뜨게 했다 해도, 그것을 다시 접어 웨스턴 부인에게 돌려주고 나니, 엠마는 그것이 어떤 오래 가는 온기도 더해 주지 않았음을, 자기는 여전히 그 글쓴이 없이도 잘 지낼 수 있고, 그 또한 자기 없이 지내는 법을 배워야 함을 깨달았다."], ["Her intentions were unchanged.", "그녀의 결심은 변함이 없었다."], ["Her resolution of refusal only grew more interesting by the addition of a scheme for his subsequent consolation and happiness.", "거절하겠다는 결의는, 그 뒤에 이어질 그의 위안과 행복을 위한 계획이 보태지면서 한층 흥미로워졌을 뿐이다."], ["His recollection of Harriet, and the words which clothed it, the “beautiful little friend,”", "그가 해리엇을 떠올린 것, 그리고 그것을 감싼 \"어여쁜 친구\"라는 말이, 해리엇이 그의 애정에서 자기 뒤를 잇게 한다는 발상을 그녀에게 일깨워 주었다. 그게 불가능한 일일까?"], ["suggested to her the idea of Harriet’s succeeding her in his affections.", "아니지. 해리엇이 이해력에서 그보다 한참 못한 것은 분명했다."], ["Was it impossible?—No.—Harriet undoubtedly was greatly his inferior in understanding; but he had been very much struck with the loveliness of her face and the warm simplicity of her manner; and all the probabilities of circumstance and connexion were in her favour.—For Harriet, it would be advantageous and delightful indeed.", "그러나 그는 그녀의 사랑스러운 얼굴과 따뜻하고 꾸밈없는 태도에 무척 마음이 끌렸었고, 처지와 인연을 둘러싼 온갖 정황이 그녀에게 유리했다. 해리엇으로서는 정말이지 이롭고도 더없이 기쁜 일이 될 터였다."]], [["“I must not dwell upon it,”", "\"여기에 너무 골몰해선 안 돼.\""], ["said she.—“I must not think of it.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. \"이런 생각을 해선 안 돼."], ["I know the danger of indulging such speculations.", "이런 억측에 빠지는 것이 얼마나 위험한지 나도 알아."], ["But stranger things have happened; and when we cease to care for each other as we do now, it will be the means of confirming us in that sort of true disinterested friendship which I can already look forward to with pleasure.”", "하지만 이보다 더 이상한 일도 일어나곤 했는걸. 그리고 우리가 지금처럼 서로를 마음에 두지 않게 될 때, 이 일이야말로 내가 벌써부터 즐겁게 내다보고 있는 그런 종류의, 사심 없는 진정한 우정 속에 우리를 굳건히 자리 잡게 해 줄 수단이 될 거야.\""]], [["It was well to have a comfort in store on Harriet’s behalf, though it might be wise to let the fancy touch it seldom; for evil in that quarter was at hand.", "해리엇을 위해 위안거리 하나를 마련해 두는 것은 좋은 일이었으되, 그 공상은 어쩌다 한 번만 건드리는 것이 현명할지도 몰랐다."], ["As Frank Churchill’s arrival had succeeded Mr.", "바로 그쪽 방면에서 화근이 코앞에 닥쳐 있었기 때문이다."], ["Elton’s engagement in the conversation of Highbury, as the latest interest had entirely borne down the first, so now upon Frank Churchill’s disappearance, Mr. Elton’s concerns were assuming the most irresistible form.—His wedding-day was named.", "하이베리의 화젯거리에서 프랭크 처칠의 도착이 미스터 엘턴의 약혼을 밀어내고 그 자리를 차지했듯이, 나중에 생긴 관심사가 처음 것을 완전히 짓눌렀듯이, 이제 프랭크 처칠이 사라지자 미스터 엘턴에 관한 일이 도무지 거부할 수 없는 형태로 부풀어 오르고 있었다."], ["He would soon be among them again; Mr. Elton and his bride.", "그의 혼례 날짜가 정해졌다."], ["There was hardly time to talk over the first letter from Enscombe before “Mr.", "그는 곧 다시 그들 가운데로 돌아올 터였다. 미스터 엘턴과 그의 신부가."], ["Elton and his bride” was in every body’s mouth, and Frank Churchill was forgotten.", "엔스컴에서 온 첫 편지를 두고 이야기를 채 나누기도 전에 \"미스터 엘턴과 그의 신부\"가 누구의 입에나 오르내렸고, 프랭크 처칠은 잊혔다."], ["Emma grew sick at the sound.", "엠마는 그 소리에 진저리가 났다."], ["She had had three weeks of happy exemption from Mr.", "미스터 엘턴에게서 벗어나 행복하게 보낸 삼 주였다."], ["Elton; and Harriet’s mind, she had been willing to hope, had been lately gaining strength. With Mr.", "그리고 해리엇의 마음도 요즈음 차차 기운을 차렸으리라고 그녀는 기꺼이 바라던 참이었다."], ["Weston’s ball in view at least, there had been a great deal of insensibility to other things; but it was now too evident that she had not attained such a state of composure as could stand against the actual approach—new carriage, bell-ringing, and all.", "적어도 미스터 웨스턴의 무도회를 눈앞에 두고 있던 동안에는 다른 일들에 무던히 무심해져 있었다. 그러나 이제는 너무도 분명해졌으니, 새 마차며 종소리며 그 모든 것을 거느린 실제의 도래 앞에서 버틸 만큼의 평정에 그녀가 이르지는 못했다는 사실이었다."]], [["Poor Harriet was in a flutter of spirits which required all the reasonings and soothings and attentions of every kind that Emma could give.", "가엾은 해리엇은 마음이 어찌나 들떠 안절부절못하던지, 엠마가 베풀 수 있는 온갖 종류의 설득과 위로와 보살핌이 죄다 필요할 지경이었다."], ["Emma felt that she could not do too much for her, that Harriet had a right to all her ingenuity and all her patience; but it was heavy work to be for ever convincing without producing any effect, for ever agreed to, without being able to make their opinions the same.", "엠마는 그녀를 위해서라면 무엇을 해 줘도 지나치지 않다고, 해리엇에게는 자기의 모든 재간과 모든 인내를 받을 자격이 있다고 느꼈다. 그러나 아무 효과도 거두지 못하면서 끝없이 타이르는 일, 늘 동의를 얻으면서도 두 사람의 생각을 같게 만들지는 못하는 일은 고된 노릇이었다."], ["Harriet listened submissively, and said “it was very true—it was just as Miss Woodhouse described—it was not worth while to think about them—and she would not think about them any longer”", "해리엇은 고분고분 귀를 기울이며 \"정말 그래요 — 미스 우드하우스가 말씀하신 그대로예요 — 그분들 생각은 할 가치도 없어요 — 이제 다시는 그분들 생각을 하지 않겠어요\" 하고 말했다."], ["but no change of subject could avail, and the next half-hour saw her as anxious and restless about the Eltons as before.", "그러나 화제를 돌려 봐야 아무 소용이 없었고, 그다음 반 시간이 지나도록 그녀는 여전히 전과 다름없이 엘턴 부부를 두고 안달하고 안절부절못했다."], ["At last Emma attacked her on another ground.", "마침내 엠마는 다른 쪽에서 그녀를 공략했다."]], [["“Your allowing yourself to be so occupied and so unhappy about Mr. Elton’s marrying, Harriet, is the strongest reproach you can make me.", "\"해리엇, 당신이 미스터 엘턴의 결혼 문제로 그토록 마음을 빼앗기고 그토록 불행해하는 것이야말로, 당신이 나한테 할 수 있는 가장 따끔한 책망이에요."], ["You could not give me a greater reproof for the mistake I fell into.", "내가 빠졌던 그 잘못에 대해 그보다 더 큰 질책은 없을 거예요."], ["It was all my doing, I know.", "그게 다 내가 한 일이라는 걸 나도 알아요."], ["I have not forgotten it, I assure you.—Deceived myself, I did very miserably deceive you—and it will be a painful reflection to me for ever.", "그 일을 잊지 않았어요, 정말이에요. 나 자신이 속고서, 당신을 참으로 비참하게 속였으니 — 그건 내게 두고두고 괴로운 회상이 될 거예요."], ["Do not imagine me in danger of forgetting it.”", "내가 그 일을 잊어버릴 우려가 있다고는 생각하지 말아요.\""]], [["Harriet felt this too much to utter more than a few words of eager exclamation.", "해리엇은 이 말이 너무도 사무쳐서, 다급한 탄성 몇 마디밖에 입에 올리지 못했다."], ["Emma continued,", "엠마는 말을 이었다."]], [["“I have not said, exert yourself Harriet for my sake; think less, talk less of Mr.", "\"나는 나를 위해 마음을 다잡으라고, 나를 위해 미스터 엘턴 생각을 덜 하고 그 이야기를 덜 하라고 말한 게 아니에요."], ["Elton for my sake; because for your own sake rather, I would wish it to be done, for the sake of what is more important than my comfort, a habit of self-command in you, a consideration of what is your duty, an attention to propriety, an endeavour to avoid the suspicions of others, to save your health and credit, and restore your tranquillity.", "차라리 당신 자신을 위해서 그러기를 바라는 거예요. 내 마음 편한 것보다 더 중요한 것을 위해서요 — 당신 안에 자제하는 습관이 들기를, 당신의 도리가 무엇인지 헤아리기를, 예의에 마음을 쓰기를, 남들의 의심을 사지 않으려 애쓰기를, 당신의 건강과 평판을 지키고 마음의 평온을 되찾기를 바라는 거예요."], ["These are the motives which I have been pressing on you.", "이런 것들이야말로 내가 당신에게 거듭 일러 온 동기들이에요."], ["They are very important—and sorry I am that you cannot feel them sufficiently to act upon them.", "그것들은 무척 중요한 거예요 — 그런데 당신이 그것들을 충분히 절감하고 행동으로 옮기지 못하는 것이 나는 안타까워요."], ["My being saved from pain is a very secondary consideration.", "내가 고통에서 벗어나는 일 따위는 한참 부차적인 문제예요."], ["I want you to save yourself from greater pain.", "나는 당신이 더 큰 고통에서 스스로를 구해 내기를 바라요."], ["Perhaps I may sometimes have felt that Harriet would not forget what was due—or rather what would be kind by me.”", "어쩌면 나는 가끔, 해리엇이라면 마땅한 도리를 — 아니, 차라리 내게 친절한 일이 무엇인지를 — 잊지 않으리라고 느꼈는지도 모르겠어요.\""]], [["This appeal to her affections did more than all the rest.", "그녀의 애정에 호소한 이 말이 다른 그 무엇보다도 큰 효력을 발휘했다."], ["The idea of wanting gratitude and consideration for Miss Woodhouse, whom she really loved extremely, made her wretched for a while, and when the violence of grief was comforted away, still remained powerful enough to prompt to what was right and support her in it very tolerably.", "정말로 더없이 사랑하는 미스 우드하우스에게 고마움과 배려가 모자랐다는 생각에 해리엇은 한동안 비참해졌고, 격렬한 슬픔이 다독여져 가라앉은 뒤에도, 그 생각은 옳은 일을 하도록 그녀를 채근하고 그 일을 제법 견딜 만하게 떠받쳐 줄 만큼의 힘을 여전히 지니고 있었다."]], [["“You, who have been the best friend I ever had in my life—Want gratitude to you!—Nobody is equal to you!—I care for nobody as I do for you!—Oh!", "\"내 평생 가장 좋은 친구였던 미스 우드하우스한테 고마움이 모자라다니요! 미스 우드하우스만 한 분은 아무도 없는걸요!"], ["Miss Woodhouse, how ungrateful I have been!”", "저는 미스 우드하우스만큼 아끼는 사람이 세상에 없고요! 아, 미스 우드하우스, 내가 얼마나 배은망덕했던가요!\""]], [["Such expressions, assisted as they were by every thing that look and manner could do, made Emma feel that she had never loved Harriet so well, nor valued her affection so highly before.", "이런 말들이, 표정과 태도가 보탤 수 있는 온갖 것의 도움까지 받으니, 엠마는 자기가 일찍이 해리엇을 이토록 사랑한 적도, 그녀의 애정을 이토록 높이 친 적도 없었다는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["“There is no charm equal to tenderness of heart,”", "\"마음의 다정함만 한 매력은 없어.\" 그녀는 나중에 혼잣말을 했다."], ["said she afterwards to herself.", "\"거기에 견줄 만한 것은 아무것도 없어."], ["“There is nothing to be compared to it.", "따뜻하고 다정한 마음에 정겹고 솔직한 태도까지 갖추면, 사람을 끄는 힘에서는 세상 어떤 명석한 머리도 다 이겨 낼 거야. 분명히 그럴 거야."], ["Warmth and tenderness of heart, with an affectionate, open manner, will beat all the clearness of head in the world, for attraction, I am sure it will.", "우리 다정한 아버지가 누구에게나 그토록 사랑받는 것도, 이저벨라가 그 모든 인기를 누리는 것도 다 마음의 다정함 덕분이지. 나한테는 그게 없어 — 하지만 나는 그것을 귀히 여기고 존중할 줄은 알아."], ["It is tenderness of heart which makes my dear father so generally beloved—which gives Isabella all her popularity.—I have it not—but I know how to prize and respect it.—Harriet is my superior in all the charm and all the felicity it gives.", "해리엇은 그것이 베푸는 온갖 매력과 온갖 복에서 나보다 한 수 위야. 사랑스러운 해리엇!"], ["Dear Harriet!—I would not change you for the clearest-headed, longest-sighted, best-judging female breathing.", "나는 살아 숨 쉬는 가장 명석한 머리, 가장 멀리 내다보는 눈, 가장 빈틈없는 판단력을 지닌 여자와도 당신을 바꾸지 않겠어. 아!"], ["Oh!", "제인 페어팩스의 그 차가움이라니!"], ["the coldness of a Jane Fairfax!—Harriet is worth a hundred such—And for a wife—a sensible man’s wife—it is invaluable.", "해리엇은 그런 여자 백 명에 맞먹는 가치가 있어. 그리고 아내로는 — 분별 있는 남자의 아내로는 — 그것이야말로 값을 매길 수 없는 보배지."], ["I mention no names; but happy the man who changes Emma for Harriet!”", "이름은 말하지 않겠어. 하지만 엠마를 마다하고 해리엇을 택하는 남자야말로 행복한 남자일 거야!\""]], [["# Volume II — Chapter XIV", "# 제2권 14장"]], [["Mrs.", "엘턴 부인을 처음 본 것은 교회에서였다."], ["Elton was first seen at church: but though devotion might be interrupted, curiosity could not be satisfied by a bride in a pew, and it must be left for the visits in form which were then to be paid, to settle whether she were very pretty indeed, or only rather pretty, or not pretty at all.", "그러나 신앙은 방해받을지언정, 신도석에 앉은 신부 하나로 호기심이 채워질 리는 없었다. 그녀가 정말로 무척 예쁜지, 그저 그런대로 예쁜지, 아니면 전혀 예쁘지 않은지를 가리는 일은, 이제 곧 격식을 갖추어 치러야 할 방문에 맡겨 둘 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Emma had feelings, less of curiosity than of pride or propriety, to make her resolve on not being the last to pay her respects; and she made a point of Harriet’s going with her, that the worst of the business might be gone through as soon as possible.", "엠마는 호기심이라기보다는 자존심이나 도리에서 우러난 감정이 있어, 인사를 가장 나중에 드리는 일만은 없도록 하겠다고 마음먹었다. 그래서 이 골치 아픈 일을 되도록 빨리 끝내 버리려고, 해리엇을 꼭 데려가기로 작정했다."]], [["She could not enter the house again, could not be in the same room to which she had with such vain artifice retreated three months ago, to lace up her boot, without recollecting.", "그녀로서는 석 달 전 그토록 헛된 꾀를 부려 부츠 끈을 고쳐 매겠다고 물러나 있던 바로 그 방에, 같은 방에 다시 들어서면서 그날 일을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 천 가지 거북한 생각이 되살아났다."], ["A thousand vexatious thoughts would recur.", "그 찬사들, 셔레이드, 그리고 끔찍한 실수들. 가엾은 해리엇도 그것을 떠올리지 않을 리 없으리라는 것은 짐작이 가고도 남았다."], ["Compliments, charades, and horrible blunders; and it was not to be supposed that poor Harriet should not be recollecting too; but she behaved very well, and was only rather pale and silent.", "그러나 해리엇은 아주 의젓하게 굴었고, 다만 좀 창백하고 말이 없을 뿐이었다. 방문은 으레 그렇듯 짧았다."], ["The visit was of course short; and there was so much embarrassment and occupation of mind to shorten it, that Emma would not allow herself entirely to form an opinion of the lady, and on no account to give one, beyond the nothing-meaning terms of being “elegantly dressed, and very pleasing.”", "그것을 짧게 만들 만한 어색함과 심란함이 워낙 컸던 터라, 엠마는 그 부인에 대해 온전한 견해를 세우는 일도 스스로에게 허락하지 않았고, \"우아하게 차려입었고 무척 호감이 간다\"라는 무의미한 말 이상으로는 결코 견해를 내놓지 않기로 했다. * 셔레이드(charade): 한 단어를 음절로 나누어 각 음절과 전체를 수수께끼 시로 묘사하던 응접실 놀이."]], [["She did not really like her.", "그녀는 사실 엘턴 부인이 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["She would not be in a hurry to find fault, but she suspected that there was no elegance;—ease, but not elegance.— She was almost sure that for a young woman, a stranger, a bride, there was too much ease.", "서둘러 흠을 잡고 싶지는 않았으나, 거기엔 우아함이 없다고 의심했다. 편안함은 있되 우아함은 없었다."], ["Her person was rather good; her face not unpretty; but neither feature, nor air, nor voice, nor manner, were elegant.", "젊은 여자, 낯선 사람, 신부 된 처지로는 그 편안함이 지나치게 많다고 거의 확신했다. 몸매는 그런대로 괜찮았고, 얼굴도 못나지는 않았다."], ["Emma thought at least it would turn out so.", "그러나 이목구비도, 분위기도, 목소리도, 몸가짐도 우아하지 않았다. 적어도 결국 그렇게 드러나리라고 엠마는 생각했다."]], [["As for Mr. Elton, his manners did not appear—but no, she would not permit a hasty or a witty word from herself about his manners.", "미스터 엘턴으로 말하자면, 그 몸가짐이 도무지— 아니, 그녀는 그의 몸가짐을 두고 성급하거나 재치 부린 한마디를 스스로에게 허락하지 않으려 했다."], ["It was an awkward ceremony at any time to be receiving wedding visits, and a man had need be all grace to acquit himself well through it.", "신혼 인사를 받는 일이란 어느 때든 어색한 의례여서, 그것을 훌륭히 치르려면 남자는 우아함으로만 똘똘 뭉쳐야 할 판이었다. 여자는 형편이 나았다."], ["The woman was better off; she might have the assistance of fine clothes, and the privilege of bashfulness, but the man had only his own good sense to depend on; and when she considered how peculiarly unlucky poor Mr.", "고운 옷의 도움도, 수줍어할 특권도 누릴 수 있었으니까. 그러나 남자에게는 제 분별 하나뿐이었다."], ["Elton was in being in the same room at once with the woman he had just married, the woman he had wanted to marry, and the woman whom he had been expected to marry, she must allow him to have the right to look as little wise, and to be as much affectedly, and as little really easy as could be.", "그리고 가엾은 미스터 엘턴이, 방금 결혼한 여자와, 결혼하고 싶어 했던 여자와, 결혼하리라고 사람들이 점쳤던 여자와 한 방에 동시에 있게 되었으니 얼마나 유난히 운이 사나운 처지인가를 헤아리자, 그녀는 그가 그렇게 영리해 보이지 못하고, 그렇게 어색하게 꾸미고, 그렇게 진정한 편안함이라곤 없어 보이는 것도 마땅한 권리라고 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Well, Miss Woodhouse,” said Harriet, when they had quitted the house, and after waiting in vain for her friend to begin; “Well, Miss Woodhouse, (with a gentle sigh,) what do you think of her?—Is not she very charming?”", "\"저기요, 미스 우드하우스,\" 두 사람이 그 집을 나선 뒤, 벗이 먼저 말을 꺼내기를 헛되이 기다리다가 해리엇이 말했다. \"저기요, 미스 우드하우스,\" (부드러운 한숨과 함께) \"그분을 어떻게 보세요? 정말 매력적이지 않나요?\""]], [["There was a little hesitation in Emma’s answer.", "엠마의 대답에는 작은 망설임이 있었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아아!"], ["yes—very—a very pleasing young woman.”", "그래요— 무척— 무척 호감이 가는 젊은 여인이에요.\""]], [["“I think her beautiful, quite beautiful.”", "\"제 눈엔 아름다워 보여요, 정말이지 아름다워요.\""]], [["“Very nicely dressed, indeed; a remarkably elegant gown.”", "\"옷차림도 정말 곱더군요. 유난히 우아한 드레스였어요.\""]], [["“I am not at all surprized that he should have fallen in love.”", "\"그분이 사랑에 빠지신 게 조금도 놀랍지 않아요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["no—there is nothing to surprize one at all.—A pretty fortune; and she came in his way.”", "그럼요— 조금도 놀랄 일이 아니죠. 어여쁜 재산에다, 그분 앞에 나타났으니까요.\""]], [["“I dare say,”", "\"제 생각엔,\""], ["returned Harriet, sighing again, “I dare say she was very much attached to him.”", "해리엇이 다시 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"제 생각엔 그분도 그분께 무척 연정을 품으셨던 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Perhaps she might; but it is not every man’s fate to marry the woman who loves him best.", "\"그럴지도 모르죠. 하지만 자기를 가장 사랑하는 여자와 결혼하는 게 모든 남자의 운명은 아니랍니다."], ["Miss Hawkins perhaps wanted a home, and thought this the best offer she was likely to have.”", "미스 호킨스는 아마 가정을 갖고 싶었고, 이게 자기가 받을 수 있는 가장 나은 청혼이라고 여겼겠죠.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"맞아요,\""], ["said Harriet earnestly, “and well she might, nobody could ever have a better.", "해리엇이 진지하게 말했다. \"그럴 만도 하죠, 이보다 더 나은 청혼은 누구도 받을 수 없었을 테니까요."], ["Well, I wish them happy with all my heart.", "그럼요, 저는 두 분이 행복하기를 진심으로 바라요."], ["And now, Miss Woodhouse, I do not think I shall mind seeing them again.", "그리고 이제, 미스 우드하우스, 저는 두 분을 다시 봐도 별로 신경 쓰이지 않을 것 같아요."], ["He is just as superior as ever;—but being married, you know, it is quite a different thing.", "그분은 여전히 그렇게 훌륭하시지만— 결혼하셨으니, 아시잖아요, 그건 완전히 다른 문제죠."], ["No, indeed, Miss Woodhouse, you need not be afraid; I can sit and admire him now without any great misery.", "정말이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 걱정하지 않으셔도 돼요. 이제는 앉아서 그분을 흠모해도 큰 괴로움은 없거든요."], ["To know that he has not thrown himself away, is such a comfort!—She does seem a charming young woman, just what he deserves.", "그분이 자기를 함부로 내던지지 않으셨다는 걸 아는 것만으로도 얼마나 위안이 되는지요! 그분은 정말 매력적인 젊은 여인 같고, 딱 그분께 어울리는 분이에요."], ["Happy creature!", "행복한 사람!"], ["He called her ‘Augusta.’ How delightful!”", "그분이 그 여자를 '오거스타'라고 부르시던걸요. 어찌나 다정한지요!\""]], [["When the visit was returned, Emma made up her mind.", "답방을 받았을 때, 엠마는 마음을 정했다. 그때는 더 많이 보고 더 잘 판단할 수 있었다."], ["She could then see more and judge better.", "마침 해리엇이 하트필드에 없었고, 아버지가 자리를 지키며 미스터 엘턴을 상대해 준 덕에, 엠마는 그 부인과 단둘이 십오 분쯤 이야기를 나누며 차분히 그녀를 살필 수 있었다."], ["From Harriet’s happening not to be at Hartfield, and her father’s being present to engage Mr.", "그 십오 분으로 엠마는 완전히 확신하게 되었다. 엘턴 부인은 허영심 많은 여자로, 자기 자신에 더없이 만족하며 제 무게를 대단히 여기는 사람이라는 것을."], ["Elton, she had a quarter of an hour of the lady’s conversation to herself, and could composedly attend to her; and the quarter of an hour quite convinced her that Mrs.", "빛나 보이고 무척 우월해 보이고 싶어 하지만, 그 몸가짐은 변변찮은 학교에서 익힌 것이라 건방지고 허물없다는 것을. 그녀의 생각이란 죄다 한 무리의 사람들과 한 가지 생활 양식에서 나온 것이라는 것을."], ["Elton was a vain woman, extremely well satisfied with herself, and thinking much of her own importance; that she meant to shine and be very superior, but with manners which had been formed in a bad school, pert and familiar; that all her notions were drawn from one set of people, and one style of living; that if not foolish she was ignorant, and that her society would certainly do Mr. Elton no good.", "어리석지는 않다 해도 무지하며, 그녀와의 교제가 미스터 엘턴에게 결코 득이 되지 않으리라는 것을."]], [["Harriet would have been a better match.", "해리엇이 더 나은 혼처였을 것이다."], ["If not wise or refined herself, she would have connected him with those who were; but Miss Hawkins, it might be fairly supposed from her easy conceit, had been the best of her own set.", "본인이 현명하거나 세련되지는 못하다 해도, 그런 사람들과 미스터 엘턴을 이어 주었으리라. 그러나 미스 호킨스로 말하자면, 그 태평한 자만으로 미루어 보건대, 제 무리 가운데서는 가장 나은 축이었으리라 짐작해도 좋았다."], ["The rich brother-in-law near Bristol was the pride of the alliance, and his place and his carriages were the pride of him.", "브리스틀 근처에 사는 부유한 형부가 이 혼맥의 자랑거리였고, 그 형부의 영지와 마차가 그 사람의 자랑거리였다."]], [["The very first subject after being seated was Maple Grove, “My brother Mr.", "자리에 앉자마자 첫 화제는 메이플 그로브, \"우리 형부 미스터 서클링의 영지\"였다. 하트필드를 메이플 그로브에 견주는 비교였다."], ["Suckling’s seat;”—a comparison of Hartfield to Maple Grove.", "하트필드의 정원은 작아도 단정하고 어여뻤으며, 집은 현대식으로 잘 지어진 것이었다. 엘턴 부인은 방의 크기와 현관과, 보거나 상상할 수 있는 온갖 것에 더없이 호의적인 인상을 받은 듯했다."], ["The grounds of Hartfield were small, but neat and pretty; and the house was modern and well-built.", "\"정말이지 메이플 그로브와 꼭 닮았네요! 그 닮은 모습에 정말 놀랐어요!"], ["Mrs. Elton seemed most favourably impressed by the size of the room, the entrance, and all that she could see or imagine.", "이 방은 메이플 그로브의 아침 거실과 모양도 크기도 똑같네요."], ["“Very like Maple Grove indeed!—She was quite struck by the likeness!—That room was the very shape and size of the morning-room at Maple Grove; her sister’s favourite room.”—Mr.", "우리 언니가 가장 좋아하는 방이죠.\" 미스터 엘턴에게 동의를 구하는 말이 이어졌다."], ["Elton was appealed to.—“Was not it astonishingly like?—She could really almost fancy herself at Maple Grove.”", "\"놀랄 만큼 닮지 않았어요? 정말 메이플 그로브에 와 있는 것 같은 착각이 들 정도예요.\""]], [["“And the staircase—You know, as I came in, I observed how very like the staircase was; placed exactly in the same part of the house.", "\"그리고 저 계단도— 아시겠지만, 들어오면서 보니 계단이 어찌나 똑 닮았던지요. 집의 정확히 같은 자리에 놓여 있어요."], ["I really could not help exclaiming!", "정말 탄성을 지르지 않을 수 없었답니다!"], ["I assure you, Miss Woodhouse, it is very delightful to me, to be reminded of a place I am so extremely partial to as Maple Grove.", "정말이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 제가 그토록 각별히 아끼는 곳, 메이플 그로브를 떠올리게 해 주니 저로서는 무척 기쁜 일이에요."], ["I have spent so many happy months there!", "그곳에서 그토록 행복한 여러 달을 보냈으니까요!\""], ["(with a little sigh of sentiment). A charming place, undoubtedly.", "(가벼운 감상의 한숨과 함께) \"두말할 것 없이 매력적인 곳이죠."], ["Every body who sees it is struck by its beauty; but to me, it has been quite a home.", "그곳을 본 사람은 누구나 그 아름다움에 감탄해요. 하지만 제게는 그곳이 그야말로 한 채의 집이었어요."], ["Whenever you are transplanted, like me, Miss Woodhouse, you will understand how very delightful it is to meet with any thing at all like what one has left behind.", "미스 우드하우스, 당신도 저처럼 옮겨 심긴 처지가 되면, 떠나온 곳과 조금이라도 닮은 무언가를 만나는 일이 얼마나 기쁜지 아시게 될 거예요."], ["I always say this is quite one of the evils of matrimony.”", "저는 늘 이게 결혼의 폐단 가운데 하나라고 말하곤 해요.\""]], [["Emma made as slight a reply as she could; but it was fully sufficient for Mrs.", "엠마는 되도록 짤막하게 대꾸했다."], ["Elton, who only wanted to be talking herself.", "그러나 그저 제 입으로 떠들기만을 바라던 엘턴 부인에게는 그것으로 충분하고도 남았다."]], [["“So extremely like Maple Grove!", "\"메이플 그로브와 어찌나 닮았는지요!"], ["And it is not merely the house—the grounds, I assure you, as far as I could observe, are strikingly like.", "그것도 집만이 아니에요. 정원도, 정말이지, 제가 본 한에서는 놀랄 만큼 닮았어요."], ["The laurels at Maple Grove are in the same profusion as here, and stand very much in the same way—just across the lawn; and I had a glimpse of a fine large tree, with a bench round it, which put me so exactly in mind!", "메이플 그로브의 월계수도 여기처럼 우거져 있고, 놓인 모양새도 아주 비슷하답니다— 잔디밭 바로 건너편에요. 그리고 둘레에 벤치를 두른 멋지고 커다란 나무가 언뜻 보였는데, 어찌나 똑같이 떠오르게 하던지요!"], ["My brother and sister will be enchanted with this place.", "우리 형부 부부도 이곳에 홀딱 반할 거예요."], ["People who have extensive grounds themselves are always pleased with any thing in the same style.”", "자기 정원이 너른 사람들은 같은 양식이라면 무엇에든 기뻐하는 법이니까요.\""]], [["Emma doubted the truth of this sentiment.", "엠마는 그 말이 진실인지 의심스러웠다."], ["She had a great idea that people who had extensive grounds themselves cared very little for the extensive grounds of any body else; but it was not worth while to attack an error so double-dyed, and therefore only said in reply,", "자기 정원이 너른 사람들은 남의 너른 정원에는 거의 관심이 없다는 것이 그녀의 확고한 생각이었다. 그러나 그토록 단단히 굳은 오류를 굳이 들이받을 가치는 없었던지라, 그저 이렇게만 대답했다."]], [["“When you have seen more of this country, I am afraid you will think you have overrated Hartfield.", "\"이 고장을 좀 더 보시고 나면, 하트필드를 너무 높이 평가하셨다고 여기실까 봐 걱정이에요."], ["Surry is full of beauties.”", "서리 주에는 아름다운 곳이 가득하거든요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["yes, I am quite aware of that.", "그럼요, 저도 잘 알고 있어요."], ["It is the garden of England, you know.", "거긴 영국의 정원이잖아요, 아시다시피."], ["Surry is the garden of England.”", "서리는 영국의 정원이죠.\""]], [["“Yes; but we must not rest our claims on that distinction.", "\"그렇긴 해요. 하지만 우리가 그 명성에만 기대선 안 되겠죠."], ["Many counties, I believe, are called the garden of England, as well as Surry.”", "제가 알기로는 서리 말고도 많은 주가 영국의 정원이라고 불리거든요.\""]], [["“No, I fancy not,”", "\"아니에요, 그럴 리가요.\""], ["replied Mrs. Elton, with a most satisfied smile.", "엘턴 부인이 더없이 흡족한 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다."], ["“I never heard any county but Surry called so.”", "\"저는 서리 말고 다른 주를 그렇게 부르는 건 한 번도 못 들어 봤어요.\""]], [["Emma was silenced.", "엠마는 입을 다물었다."]], [["“My brother and sister have promised us a visit in the spring, or summer at farthest,”", "\"우리 형부 부부가 봄에, 늦어도 여름에는 와 주겠다고 약속했어요.\" 엘턴 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["continued Mrs. Elton; “and that will be our time for exploring.", "\"그때가 우리 둘러볼 시기가 되겠죠."], ["While they are with us, we shall explore a great deal, I dare say.", "그분들이 우리와 함께 있는 동안 정말 여기저기 많이 둘러보게 될 거예요."], ["They will have their barouche-landau, of course, which holds four perfectly; and therefore, without saying any thing of our carriage, we should be able to explore the different beauties extremely well.", "물론 그분들은 바루시-랜도를 가져올 테고, 그건 넷이 거뜬히 탈 수 있으니까요. 그러니 우리 마차는 말할 것도 없이, 이 고장의 여러 아름다운 곳을 아주 멋지게 둘러볼 수 있을 거예요."], ["They would hardly come in their chaise, I think, at that season of the year.", "그 계절에 설마 그분들이 이륜마차로 오시진 않겠죠."], ["Indeed, when the time draws on, I shall decidedly recommend their bringing the barouche-landau; it will be so very much preferable.", "정말이지 그때가 다가오면, 저는 단연코 바루시-랜도를 가져오시라고 권할 거예요. 그게 훨씬 나으니까요."], ["When people come into a beautiful country of this sort, you know, Miss Woodhouse, one naturally wishes them to see as much as possible; and Mr.", "이렇게 아름다운 고장에 온 사람들에게는, 아시다시피 미스 우드하우스, 되도록 많은 곳을 보여 주고 싶은 게 인지상정이잖아요."], ["Suckling is extremely fond of exploring.", "그리고 미스터 서클링은 둘러보는 걸 무척 좋아하세요."], ["We explored to King’s-Weston twice last summer, in that way, most delightfully, just after their first having the barouche-landau.", "지난여름에는 그 마차로 킹스웨스턴까지 두 번이나 더없이 즐겁게 둘러보았답니다, 바루시-랜도를 처음 들이고 막 얼마 안 됐을 때요. 미스 우드하우스, 이곳에서도 여름마다 그런 나들이가 많으시겠죠?\""], ["You have many parties of that kind here, I suppose, Miss Woodhouse, every summer?”", "* 바루시-랜도(barouche-landau): 접이식 덮개를 갖춘 사륜 호화 마차. 부의 과시 수단이었다."]], [["“No; not immediately here. We are rather out of distance of the very striking beauties which attract the sort of parties you speak of; and we are a very quiet set of people, I believe; more disposed to stay at home than engage in schemes of pleasure.”", "\"아니에요. 적어도 이 근방에서는요. 우리는 당신이 말씀하시는 그런 나들이를 부르는, 아주 눈에 띄는 명소들과는 거리가 좀 먼 편이에요. 그리고 우리는 아주 조용한 사람들이라서요, 아마도요. 놀이 계획을 꾸미기보다는 집에 머무는 쪽에 더 기우는 편이죠.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["there is nothing like staying at home for real comfort.", "진짜 안락함으로 치면 집에 머무는 것만 한 게 없죠."], ["Nobody can be more devoted to home than I am.", "저만큼 집에 헌신하는 사람도 없을 거예요."], ["I was quite a proverb for it at Maple Grove.", "메이플 그로브에서는 그걸로 거의 소문난 사람이었어요."], ["Many a time has Selina said, when she has been going to Bristol, ‘I really cannot get this girl to move from the house.", "셀리나 언니가 브리스틀로 나가려 할 때면 몇 번이나 이렇게 말했는지 몰라요. '이 애를 집에서 끌어낼 수가 도무지 없네."], ["I absolutely must go in by myself, though I hate being stuck up in the barouche-landau without a companion; but Augusta, I believe, with her own good-will, would never stir beyond the park paling.’ Many a time has she said so; and yet I am no advocate for entire seclusion.", "동행 없이 바루시-랜도에 꼼짝없이 갇혀 가는 건 질색이지만, 어쩔 수 없이 나 혼자라도 가야겠어. 오거스타는 제 마음대로 두면 정원 울타리 밖으로는 한 발짝도 안 나설걸.'"], ["I think, on the contrary, when people shut themselves up entirely from society, it is a very bad thing; and that it is much more advisable to mix in the world in a proper degree, without living in it either too much or too little.", "언니가 몇 번이나 그렇게 말했답니다. 그래도 저는 완전한 칩거를 옹호하는 사람은 아니에요."], ["I perfectly understand your situation, however, Miss Woodhouse—(looking towards Mr.", "오히려 그 반대로, 사람이 사교에서 완전히 자기를 가둬 버리는 건 아주 나쁜 일이라고 생각해요. 너무 많지도 너무 적지도 않게, 적당한 정도로 세상과 어울리는 편이 훨씬 바람직하죠."], ["Woodhouse), Your father’s state of health must be a great drawback.", "하지만 미스 우드하우스, 당신 처지는 제가 잘 이해해요.\" (미스터 우드하우스 쪽을 바라보며) \"아버님의 건강 상태가 큰 걸림돌이겠죠."], ["Why does not he try Bath?—Indeed he should.", "어째서 바스로 가 보지 않으세요? 정말 그러셔야 해요."], ["Let me recommend Bath to you.", "바스를 권해 드릴게요."], ["I assure you I have no doubt of its doing Mr. Woodhouse good.”", "정말이지 그게 미스터 우드하우스께 좋으리라는 건 의심할 여지가 없답니다.\""]], [["“My father tried it more than once, formerly; but without receiving any benefit; and Mr.", "\"아버지는 예전에 한두 번 가 보셨어요. 하지만 아무 효험도 못 보셨죠."], ["Perry, whose name, I dare say, is not unknown to you, does not conceive it would be at all more likely to be useful now.”", "그리고 미스터 페리도, 그 이름을 아마 모르진 않으시겠지만, 지금이라고 더 도움이 되리라고는 전혀 생각하지 않아요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["that’s a great pity; for I assure you, Miss Woodhouse, where the waters do agree, it is quite wonderful the relief they give.", "그건 참 안타까운 일이네요. 정말이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 그 물이 몸에 맞는 사람한테는 어찌나 놀라운 효험을 주는지 몰라요."], ["In my Bath life, I have seen such instances of it!", "제가 바스에서 지내는 동안 그런 사례를 얼마나 많이 봤다고요!"], ["And it is so cheerful a place, that it could not fail of being of use to Mr.", "게다가 그렇게 활기찬 곳이라, 미스터 우드하우스의 기분에도 분명 도움이 될 거예요."], ["Woodhouse’s spirits, which, I understand, are sometimes much depressed.", "가끔 무척 가라앉으신다고 들었거든요."], ["And as to its recommendations to you, I fancy I need not take much pains to dwell on them.", "그리고 당신께 좋은 점으로 말하자면, 굳이 애써 늘어놓을 필요도 없을 것 같아요."], ["The advantages of Bath to the young are pretty generally understood.", "젊은 사람에게 바스가 얼마나 이로운지는 대체로 다 아는 일이니까요."], ["It would be a charming introduction for you, who have lived so secluded a life; and I could immediately secure you some of the best society in the place.", "그렇게 외진 생활을 해 온 당신에게는 더없이 매력적인 사교계 입문이 될 거예요. 제가 그곳의 가장 좋은 모임 몇 군데에 당장 자리를 마련해 드릴 수 있고요."], ["A line from me would bring you a little host of acquaintance; and my particular friend, Mrs.", "제가 편지 한 줄만 보내면 아는 사람들이 떼로 몰려올 거예요."], ["Partridge, the lady I have always resided with when in Bath, would be most happy to shew you any attentions, and would be the very person for you to go into public with.”", "그리고 제 각별한 벗인 파트리지 부인은, 제가 바스에 머물 때면 늘 함께 지내던 분인데, 당신께 온갖 친절을 베풀어 주기를 무척 기뻐할 테고, 당신이 사교계에 나설 때 함께 다니기에 그야말로 딱 맞는 분이랍니다.\""]], [["It was as much as Emma could bear, without being impolite.", "엠마가 무례해지지 않고 견딜 수 있는 한계가 거기까지였다."], ["The idea of her being indebted to Mrs. Elton for what was called an introduction—of her going into public under the auspices of a friend of Mrs.", "자기가 이른바 사교계 입문이라는 것을 엘턴 부인에게 신세 진다는 생각— 엘턴 부인의 벗이라는 사람의 비호 아래 사교계에 나선다는 생각— 그 벗이라는 게 아마도 하숙인을 받아 겨우 입에 풀칠이나 하는, 어떤 천박하고 요란한 과부일 텐데!"], ["Elton’s—probably some vulgar, dashing widow, who, with the help of a boarder, just made a shift to live!—The dignity of Miss Woodhouse, of Hartfield, was sunk indeed!", "하트필드의 미스 우드하우스의 위엄이 그야말로 땅에 떨어진 격이었다!"]], [["She restrained herself, however, from any of the reproofs she could have given, and only thanked Mrs.", "그러나 엠마는 던질 수 있었던 온갖 꾸지람을 꾹 참고, 그저 엘턴 부인에게 쌀쌀맞게 고맙다고만 했다."], ["Elton coolly; “but their going to Bath was quite out of the question; and she was not perfectly convinced that the place might suit her better than her father.”", "\"하지만 우리가 바스에 가는 일은 도무지 있을 수 없고요. 저는 그곳이 아버지보다 제게 더 맞으리라고도 그다지 확신하지 못하겠네요.\""], ["And then, to prevent farther outrage and indignation, changed the subject directly.", "그러고는 더 큰 모욕과 분노를 막으려고 곧장 화제를 돌렸다."]], [["“I do not ask whether you are musical, Mrs. Elton.", "\"엘턴 부인, 음악을 하시는지는 여쭙지 않을게요."], ["Upon these occasions, a lady’s character generally precedes her; and Highbury has long known that you are a superior performer.”", "이런 경우에는 대개 그분의 평판이 본인보다 먼저 닿게 마련이니까요. 하이베리에서는 당신이 뛰어난 연주자라는 걸 진작부터 알고 있었답니다.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["no, indeed; I must protest against any such idea.", "아니에요, 정말로요. 그런 생각엔 한사코 반대해야겠어요."], ["A superior performer!—very far from it, I assure you.", "뛰어난 연주자라니요! 정말이지 어림도 없는 말씀이에요."], ["Consider from how partial a quarter your information came.", "그 정보가 얼마나 편파적인 데서 나온 것인지 생각해 보세요."], ["I am doatingly fond of music—passionately fond;—and my friends say I am not entirely devoid of taste; but as to any thing else, upon my honour my performance is mediocre to the last degree.", "저는 음악을 끔찍이 좋아해요— 열렬히 좋아하죠."], ["You, Miss Woodhouse, I well know, play delightfully.", "제 벗들도 제게 안목이 아예 없지는 않다고 말하긴 해요."], ["I assure you it has been the greatest satisfaction, comfort, and delight to me, to hear what a musical society I am got into.", "하지만 그 밖의 것으로 말하면, 정말이지 제 연주는 더할 나위 없이 그저 그런 수준이랍니다."], ["I absolutely cannot do without music.", "미스 우드하우스, 당신이야말로 멋지게 연주하신다는 걸 저는 잘 알아요."], ["It is a necessary of life to me; and having always been used to a very musical society, both at Maple Grove and in Bath, it would have been a most serious sacrifice.", "정말이지 제가 이렇게 음악을 즐기는 모임에 들어오게 됐다는 걸 알게 된 것이 더없는 만족이요, 위안이요, 기쁨이었어요. 저는 음악 없이는 도무지 못 살거든요."], ["I honestly said as much to Mr. E.", "제겐 음악이 삶의 필수품이에요."], ["when he was speaking of my future home, and expressing his fears lest the retirement of it should be disagreeable; and the inferiority of the house too—knowing what I had been accustomed to—of course he was not wholly without apprehension.", "메이플 그로브에서나 바스에서나 늘 무척 음악을 즐기는 모임에 익숙해져 있던 터라, 음악이 없었다면 그야말로 심각한 희생이 됐을 거예요. 미스터 엘턴이 제 앞날의 집 이야기를 하면서, 그 외진 데가 마음에 안 들까 봐 염려하실 때, 저는 그분께 솔직히 그렇게 말했답니다."], ["When he was speaking of it in that way, I honestly said that the world I could give up—parties, balls, plays—for I had no fear of retirement.", "게다가 집도 변변찮으니— 제가 어떤 데 익숙해져 있었는지 아시니까— 당연히 그분도 영 걱정이 없진 않으셨죠. 그분이 그런 식으로 말씀하실 때, 저는 솔직히 말씀드렸어요."], ["Blessed with so many resources within myself, the world was not necessary to me.", "세상이야 저는 단념할 수 있다고요— 파티든 무도회든 연극이든— 외진 생활은 조금도 두렵지 않으니까요. 제 안에 워낙 많은 자산을 지니고 있어서, 저한테는 세상이 꼭 필요한 게 아니거든요."], ["I could do very well without it.", "그런 거 없이도 아주 잘 지낼 수 있어요."], ["To those who had no resources it was a different thing; but my resources made me quite independent.", "자기 안에 아무 자산이 없는 사람에게야 다른 문제겠지만, 제 자산은 저를 아주 독립적으로 만들어 주니까요."], ["And as to smaller-sized rooms than I had been used to, I really could not give it a thought.", "그리고 익숙했던 것보다 작은 방으로 말하자면, 정말이지 한 번도 마음에 둔 적 없어요. 그런 종류의 희생이라면 무엇이든 너끈히 감당할 수 있다고 믿었거든요."], ["I hoped I was perfectly equal to any sacrifice of that description.", "물론 메이플 그로브에서는 온갖 호사에 익숙해져 있었죠. 하지만 저는 그분께 분명히 말씀드렸어요."], ["Certainly I had been accustomed to every luxury at Maple Grove; but I did assure him that two carriages were not necessary to my happiness, nor were spacious apartments.", "제 행복에 마차 두 대가 꼭 필요한 것도, 널찍한 방이 꼭 필요한 것도 아니라고요. '하지만,'"], ["‘But,’ said I, ‘to be quite honest, I do not think I can live without something of a musical society.", "제가 말했죠. '아주 솔직히 말하면, 음악을 즐기는 모임 비슷한 거라도 없으면 저는 못 살 것 같아요."], ["I condition for nothing else; but without music, life would be a blank to me.’”", "다른 건 아무것도 조건으로 걸지 않겠어요. 하지만 음악이 없으면 제 삶은 텅 빈 백지가 될 거예요.'\""]], [["“We cannot suppose,” said Emma, smiling, “that Mr.", "\"미스터 엘턴이라면 하이베리에 무척 음악을 즐기는 모임이 있노라고 망설임 없이 장담하셨으리라고 봐야겠죠.\""], ["Elton would hesitate to assure you of there being a very musical society in Highbury; and I hope you will not find he has outstepped the truth more than may be pardoned, in consideration of the motive.”", "엠마가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그리고 그 동기를 헤아려 너그러이 봐줄 수 있는 정도 이상으로는 사실을 부풀리지 않으셨기를 바라요.\""]], [["“No, indeed, I have no doubts at all on that head.", "\"아니에요, 정말로 그 점은 조금도 의심하지 않아요."], ["I am delighted to find myself in such a circle.", "이런 모임에 들게 되어 저는 무척 기쁘답니다."], ["I hope we shall have many sweet little concerts together.", "우리가 함께 자그마한 음악회를 자주 열게 되기를 바라요."], ["I think, Miss Woodhouse, you and I must establish a musical club, and have regular weekly meetings at your house, or ours.", "미스 우드하우스, 제 생각엔 당신과 제가 음악회 모임을 하나 만들어서, 당신 댁이나 우리 집에서 매주 정기적으로 모이면 좋겠어요."], ["Will not it be a good plan?", "괜찮은 계획 아닌가요?"], ["If we exert ourselves, I think we shall not be long in want of allies.", "우리가 힘을 좀 내면, 동지를 구하는 데 오래 걸리지 않을 거예요. 그런 종류의 일이라면 저한테는 특히 바람직하겠어요."], ["Something of that nature would be particularly desirable for me, as an inducement to keep me in practice; for married women, you know—there is a sad story against them, in general.", "제가 연습을 게을리하지 않도록 부추기는 자극이 될 테니까요. 결혼한 여자들로 말하면, 아시다시피— 대체로 그들을 두고 도는 서글픈 이야기가 있잖아요."], ["They are but too apt to give up music.”", "음악을 너무도 쉽게 놓아 버리거든요.\""]], [["“But you, who are so extremely fond of it—there can be no danger, surely?”", "\"하지만 당신은 음악을 그토록 끔찍이 좋아하시는데— 설마 그럴 위험이야 없겠죠?\""]], [["“I should hope not; but really when I look around among my acquaintance, I tremble.", "\"그러지 않기를 바라요. 하지만 정말이지 제 아는 사람들을 둘러보면 몸서리가 쳐진답니다."], ["Selina has entirely given up music—never touches the instrument—though she played sweetly.", "셀리나 언니는 음악을 완전히 놓아 버렸어요— 악기에 손도 안 대요— 그렇게 곱게 연주했는데도요."], ["And the same may be said of Mrs.", "제프리스 부인도— 결혼 전엔 클라라 파트리지였죠— 마찬가지고요."], ["Jeffereys—Clara Partridge, that was—and of the two Milmans, now Mrs. Bird and Mrs.", "두 밀먼 자매, 지금의 버드 부인과 제임스 쿠퍼 부인도 그렇고요."], ["James Cooper; and of more than I can enumerate.", "일일이 다 헤아릴 수도 없을 만큼 많아요."], ["Upon my word it is enough to put one in a fright.", "정말이지 사람을 겁먹게 하기에 충분하죠."], ["I used to be quite angry with Selina; but really I begin now to comprehend that a married woman has many things to call her attention.", "저는 셀리나 언니한테 단단히 화가 나곤 했어요. 하지만 이제는 정말로 알 것 같아요, 결혼한 여자한테는 신경 써야 할 일이 한두 가지가 아니라는 걸요."], ["I believe I was half an hour this morning shut up with my housekeeper.”", "오늘 아침에도 저는 가정부와 반 시간이나 틀어박혀 있었던 것 같아요.\""]], [["“But every thing of that kind,”", "\"하지만 그런 종류의 일은 다,\""], ["said Emma, “will soon be in so regular a train—”", "엠마가 말했다. \"곧 아주 규칙적인 궤도에 오를 테니—\""]], [["“Well,” said Mrs.", "\"글쎄요,\""], ["Elton, laughing, “we shall see.”", "엘턴 부인이 웃으며 말했다. \"두고 봐야죠.\""]], [["Emma, finding her so determined upon neglecting her music, had nothing more to say; and, after a moment’s pause, Mrs.", "엠마는 그녀가 그렇게나 단단히 음악을 등한히 하기로 작정한 것을 알고 더는 할 말이 없었다."], ["Elton chose another subject.", "잠시 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 엘턴 부인이 또 다른 화제를 골랐다."]], [["“We have been calling at Randalls,”", "\"우리가 랜덜스에 다녀왔어요.\""], ["said she, “and found them both at home; and very pleasant people they seem to be.", "그녀가 말했다. \"두 분 다 댁에 계셨는데, 무척 유쾌한 분들 같더군요."], ["I like them extremely. Mr.", "저는 두 분이 아주 마음에 들어요."], ["Weston seems an excellent creature—quite a first-rate favourite with me already, I assure you.", "미스터 웨스턴은 훌륭한 분 같고요— 정말이지 벌써 제일 마음에 드는 분이 되셨답니다."], ["And she appears so truly good—there is something so motherly and kind-hearted about her, that it wins upon one directly.", "그리고 부인 쪽은 어찌나 진실로 선해 보이는지요— 어딘가 모성적이고 마음씨 따뜻한 데가 있어서, 단번에 사람의 마음을 사로잡거든요."], ["She was your governess, I think?”", "그분이 당신의 가정교사셨다죠?\""]], [["Emma was almost too much astonished to answer; but Mrs.", "엠마는 거의 너무 놀라 대답이 안 나올 지경이었다."], ["Elton hardly waited for the affirmative before she went on.", "그러나 엘턴 부인은 그렇다는 대답을 기다릴 새도 없이 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“Having understood as much, I was rather astonished to find her so very lady-like!", "\"그렇게 들었던 터라, 그분이 그렇게나 귀부인다운 걸 보고 저는 좀 놀랐답니다!"], ["But she is really quite the gentlewoman.”", "하지만 정말이지 완전한 숙녀시더군요.\""]], [["“Mrs. Weston’s manners,”", "\"웨스턴 부인의 몸가짐은,\""], ["said Emma, “were always particularly good.", "엠마가 말했다. \"언제나 유난히 훌륭했어요."], ["Their propriety, simplicity, and elegance, would make them the safest model for any young woman.”", "그 단정함과 꾸밈없음과 우아함은 어떤 젊은 여자에게든 더없이 안전한 본보기가 될 거예요.\""]], [["“And who do you think came in while we were there?”", "\"그런데 우리가 거기 있는 동안 누가 들어왔는지 아세요?\""]], [["Emma was quite at a loss.", "엠마는 도무지 종잡을 수가 없었다."], ["The tone implied some old acquaintance—and how could she possibly guess?", "그 말투는 어떤 오랜 지인을 암시하는 듯했다— 그런데 그녀가 어떻게 짐작할 수 있겠는가?"]], [["“Knightley!”", "\"나이틀리요!\""], ["continued Mrs.", "엘턴 부인이 말을 이었다. \"나이틀리 본인이요!"], ["Elton; “Knightley himself!—Was not it lucky?—for, not being within when he called the other day, I had never seen him before; and of course, as so particular a friend of Mr.", "운이 좋지 않았어요? 요전번에 그분이 찾아오셨을 때 제가 집에 없었던 터라, 그전엔 한 번도 못 뵈었거든요."], ["E.’s, I had a great curiosity.", "게다가 미스터 엘턴의 그렇게 각별한 벗이시니, 당연히 무척 궁금했죠. '내 친구 나이틀리'"], ["‘My friend Knightley’ had been so often mentioned, that I was really impatient to see him; and I must do my cara sposo the justice to say that he need not be ashamed of his friend.", "이야기가 어찌나 자주 나왔던지, 정말 보고 싶어서 안달이 날 지경이었답니다. 그리고 제 카로 스포소 명예를 위해 한마디 해야겠는데, 그분은 친구를 두고 부끄러워할 필요가 전혀 없으시더군요."], ["Knightley is quite the gentleman.", "나이틀리는 완전한 신사세요. 저는 그분이 무척 마음에 들어요."], ["I like him very much.", "단연코, 제 생각엔, 아주 신사다운 분이에요.\""], ["Decidedly, I think, a very gentleman-like man.”", "* 카로 스포소(caro sposo): '사랑하는 남편'이라는 뜻의 이탈리아어. 엘턴 부인이 멋을 부리려 자주 쓰는 말이다."]], [["Happily, it was now time to be gone.", "다행히도 이제는 일어설 시간이었다."], ["They were off; and Emma could breathe.", "그들은 떠났고, 엠마는 한숨 돌릴 수 있었다."]], [["“Insufferable woman!”", "\"못 견딜 여자야!\""], ["was her immediate exclamation.", "그녀의 첫 탄성이 그것이었다."], ["“Worse than I had supposed.", "\"짐작했던 것보다 더해."], ["Absolutely insufferable!", "도무지 못 견디겠어!"], ["Knightley!—I could not have believed it.", "나이틀리라니! 도저히 믿을 수가 없네."], ["Knightley!—never seen him in her life before, and call him Knightley!—and discover that he is a gentleman!", "나이틀리라니! 평생 한 번도 본 적 없으면서, 그분을 나이틀리라고 부르다니!"], ["A little upstart, vulgar being, with her Mr.", "게다가 그분이 신사라는 걸 발견하다니!"], ["E., and her caro sposo, and her resources, and all her airs of pert pretension and underbred finery.", "자기 미스터 엘턴이니, 자기 카로 스포소니, 자기 자산이니, 그 온갖 건방진 허세와 천한 출신의 겉치레를 부려 대는 한낱 벼락출세한 천박한 인간이!"], ["Actually to discover that Mr. Knightley is a gentleman!", "글쎄, 미스터 나이틀리가 신사라는 걸 발견했다는 거야!"], ["I doubt whether he will return the compliment, and discover her to be a lady.", "그분이 그 답례로 그 여자가 숙녀라는 걸 발견해 주실지는 의심스럽군."], ["I could not have believed it!", "도저히 믿을 수가 없어!"], ["And to propose that she and I should unite to form a musical club!", "게다가 자기랑 내가 손잡고 음악회 모임을 만들자고 제안하다니!"], ["One would fancy we were bosom friends! And Mrs.", "누가 보면 우리가 절친한 벗인 줄 알겠어!"], ["Weston!—Astonished that the person who had brought me up should be a gentlewoman!", "그리고 웨스턴 부인 일은! 나를 길러 준 사람이 숙녀라는 데 놀랐다니!"], ["Worse and worse.", "점입가경이군."], ["I never met with her equal.", "그런 사람은 평생 처음 봐."], ["Much beyond my hopes.", "내 예상을 훨씬 넘어서네."], ["Harriet is disgraced by any comparison. Oh!", "해리엇은 견주는 것만으로도 욕보는 거야."], ["what would Frank Churchill say to her, if he were here?", "아아! 프랭크 처칠이 여기 있다면 그 여자를 두고 뭐라고 할까?"], ["How angry and how diverted he would be!", "얼마나 화를 내면서도 또 얼마나 재미있어할까! 아아!"], ["Ah! there I am—thinking of him directly.", "또 이러네— 곧장 그 사람 생각을 하고 있잖아."], ["Always the first person to be thought of!", "늘 제일 먼저 떠오르는 사람!"], ["How I catch myself out!", "내가 또 나를 들켜 버렸어!"], ["Frank Churchill comes as regularly into my mind!”—", "프랭크 처칠은 어쩌면 이렇게 어김없이 떠오르는지!\""]], [["All this ran so glibly through her thoughts, that by the time her father had arranged himself, after the bustle of the Eltons’ departure, and was ready to speak, she was very tolerably capable of attending.", "이 모든 생각이 어찌나 술술 흘러갔던지, 엘턴 부부가 떠나며 일으킨 부산함 끝에 아버지가 자세를 추스르고 막 입을 떼려 할 즈음에는, 엠마는 그런대로 귀 기울일 만한 상태가 되어 있었다."]], [["“Well, my dear,”", "\"음, 얘야,\""], ["he deliberately began, “considering we never saw her before, she seems a very pretty sort of young lady; and I dare say she was very much pleased with you.", "아버지가 천천히 말을 꺼냈다. \"전에 한 번도 본 적이 없던 사람치고는, 무척 어여쁜 젊은 부인 같더구나."], ["She speaks a little too quick.", "그리고 분명 너를 무척 마음에 들어 했을 게다."], ["A little quickness of voice there is which rather hurts the ear.", "말이 조금 너무 빠르긴 해. 귀에 좀 거슬리는, 약간 빠른 목소리가 있더구나."], ["But I believe I am nice; I do not like strange voices; and nobody speaks like you and poor Miss Taylor.", "하지만 내가 까다로운 거겠지. 나는 낯선 목소리는 영 좋아하지 않거든."], ["However, she seems a very obliging, pretty-behaved young lady, and no doubt will make him a very good wife.", "게다가 너나 가엾은 미스 테일러처럼 말하는 사람은 없으니까. 그래도 무척 친절하고 점잖게 처신하는 젊은 부인 같으니, 분명 그 사람한테 아주 좋은 아내가 될 게다."], ["Though I think he had better not have married.", "그래도 내 생각엔 그 사람이 결혼하지 않는 편이 나았을 텐데."], ["I made the best excuses I could for not having been able to wait on him and Mrs.", "이 기쁜 날에 그 사람과 엘턴 부인을 찾아뵙지 못한 것에 대해 최선을 다해 양해를 구했단다."], ["Elton on this happy occasion; I said that I hoped I should in the course of the summer.", "여름이 가기 전에는 꼭 찾아뵙고 싶다고 말했지."], ["But I ought to have gone before.", "하지만 진작 갔어야 했어."], ["Not to wait upon a bride is very remiss.", "신부를 찾아뵙지 않는 건 아주 무례한 일이거든. 아아!"], ["Ah! it shews what a sad invalid I am!", "이게 다 내가 한심한 병자라는 걸 보여 주는 게지!"], ["But I do not like the corner into Vicarage Lane.”", "하지만 나는 그 비카리지 골목으로 꺾어 드는 모퉁이가 영 싫구나.\""]], [["“I dare say your apologies were accepted, sir.", "\"분명 아버지의 양해는 받아들여졌을 거예요."], ["Mr. Elton knows you.”", "미스터 엘턴은 아버지를 잘 아니까요.\""]], [["“Yes: but a young lady—a bride—I ought to have paid my respects to her if possible.", "\"그래. 하지만 젊은 부인— 신부인데— 되도록 인사를 드렸어야 했어."], ["It was being very deficient.”", "그러지 못한 건 큰 결례였지.\""]], [["“But, my dear papa, you are no friend to matrimony; and therefore why should you be so anxious to pay your respects to a bride?", "\"하지만 아버지, 아버지는 결혼을 달가워하지 않으시잖아요. 그런데 어째서 신부에게 인사 드리는 일을 그렇게 안달하세요?"], ["It ought to be no recommendation to you.", "아버지한테는 그게 추천할 만한 일이 못 되어야죠."], ["It is encouraging people to marry if you make so much of them.”", "신부를 그렇게 떠받들면 사람들한테 결혼을 부추기는 셈이잖아요.\""]], [["“No, my dear, I never encouraged any body to marry, but I would always wish to pay every proper attention to a lady—and a bride, especially, is never to be neglected.", "\"아니다, 얘야, 나는 누구한테도 결혼을 부추긴 적이 없어. 다만 부인에게는 늘 마땅한 예를 다 갖추고 싶을 뿐이지— 그중에서도 신부는 결코 소홀히 해서는 안 되는 거란다."], ["More is avowedly due to her.", "신부에게는 더 많은 예가 마땅히 돌아가야 하는 법이거든."], ["A bride, you know, my dear, is always the first in company, let the others be who they may.”", "신부란, 얘야, 알다시피 다른 사람들이 누구든 간에 늘 자리에서 으뜸인 법이란다.\""]], [["“Well, papa, if this is not encouragement to marry, I do not know what is.", "\"그것 보세요, 아버지, 그게 결혼을 부추기는 게 아니면 뭐가 부추기는 건지 저는 모르겠네요."], ["And I should never have expected you to be lending your sanction to such vanity-baits for poor young ladies.”", "그리고 아버지가 가엾은 젊은 아가씨들한테 던지는 그런 허영의 미끼에 손을 들어 주실 줄은 정말 몰랐어요.\""]], [["“My dear, you do not understand me.", "\"얘야, 너는 내 말을 못 알아듣는구나."], ["This is a matter of mere common politeness and good-breeding, and has nothing to do with any encouragement to people to marry.”", "이건 그저 평범한 예의와 좋은 가풍의 문제일 뿐, 사람들에게 결혼을 부추기는 일과는 아무 상관도 없는 거란다.\""]], [["Emma had done.", "엠마는 그만두었다."], ["Her father was growing nervous, and could not understand her.", "아버지가 점점 불안해하며 자기 말을 알아듣지 못했다."], ["Her mind returned to Mrs. Elton’s offences, and long, very long, did they occupy her.", "그녀의 마음은 다시 엘턴 부인의 무례함으로 돌아갔고, 그 생각이 오래, 아주 오래도록 그녀를 사로잡았다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter XV", "# 제2권 15장"]], [["Emma was not required, by any subsequent discovery, to retract her ill opinion of Mrs. Elton.", "엠마는 그 뒤 어떤 새로운 사실을 알게 되었다 한들, 엘턴 부인에 대한 자신의 박한 평가를 거두어들일 필요는 없었다."], ["Her observation had been pretty correct.", "그녀의 관찰은 꽤 정확했던 것이다."], ["Such as Mrs. Elton appeared to her on this second interview, such she appeared whenever they met again,—self-important, presuming, familiar, ignorant, and ill-bred.", "이 두 번째 만남에서 엠마에게 비친 엘턴 부인의 모습은, 그 후 다시 만날 때마다 한결같았다 — 자만에 차 있고, 주제넘으며, 무람없고, 무지하며, 천박했다."], ["She had a little beauty and a little accomplishment, but so little judgment that she thought herself coming with superior knowledge of the world, to enliven and improve a country neighbourhood; and conceived Miss Hawkins to have held such a place in society as Mrs. Elton’s consequence only could surpass.", "약간의 미모와 약간의 교양은 있었으나 분별이 너무도 없어, 자신이 세상에 대한 우월한 식견을 지니고 와서 어느 시골 동네에 생기를 불어넣고 그곳을 개량해 주는 것이라 여겼고, 미스 호킨스가 차지했던 사교계의 자리란 오직 엘턴 부인이라는 신분으로만 능가할 수 있는 것이라 자부했다."]], [["There was no reason to suppose Mr. Elton thought at all differently from his wife.", "미스터 엘턴이 그 아내와 조금이라도 다르게 생각한다고 볼 근거는 전혀 없었다."], ["He seemed not merely happy with her, but proud.", "그는 그저 아내에게 만족하는 정도가 아니라 자랑스러워하는 듯했다."], ["He had the air of congratulating himself on having brought such a woman to Highbury, as not even Miss Woodhouse could equal; and the greater part of her new acquaintance, disposed to commend, or not in the habit of judging, following the lead of Miss Bates’s good-will, or taking it for granted that the bride must be as clever and as agreeable as she professed herself, were very well satisfied; so that Mrs.", "미스 우드하우스조차 견줄 수 없을 만한 여인을 하이베리에 데려왔노라 자축하는 기색이 역력했다. 그리고 그녀의 새 지인 대부분은 칭찬하는 쪽으로 기울었거나 평가하는 습관이 없는 사람들이라, 미스 베이츠의 호의를 따라가거나 신부가 스스로 내세우는 만큼 영리하고 상냥한 사람일 게 분명하다고 미루어 짐작하며 무척 흡족해했다."], ["Elton’s praise passed from one mouth to another as it ought to do, unimpeded by Miss Woodhouse, who readily continued her first contribution and talked with a good grace of her being “very pleasant and very elegantly dressed.”", "그리하여 엘턴 부인에 대한 칭찬은 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로 입에서 입으로 옮아갔고, 미스 우드하우스는 그 흐름을 막지 않았다. 오히려 처음에 보탰던 평을 선뜻 이어 가며 \"참 상냥하고 차림새가 무척 우아한 분\"이라고 점잖게 말해 주었다."]], [["In one respect Mrs.", "한 가지 점에서 엘턴 부인은 처음 보였던 것보다도 한층 더 나빠졌다."], ["Elton grew even worse than she had appeared at first.", "엠마를 향한 감정이 달라진 것이다."], ["Her feelings altered towards Emma.—Offended, probably, by the little encouragement which her proposals of intimacy met with, she drew back in her turn and gradually became much more cold and distant; and though the effect was agreeable, the ill-will which produced it was necessarily increasing Emma’s dislike.", "친밀해지려는 자신의 제안이 별 호응을 얻지 못한 데 기분이 상했던지, 이번에는 엘턴 부인 쪽에서 물러서더니 차츰 훨씬 차갑고 데면데면해졌다. 그 결과는 엠마에게 반가운 것이었으나, 그것을 빚어낸 적의는 엠마의 반감을 키울 수밖에 없었다."], ["Her manners, too—and Mr. Elton’s, were unpleasant towards Harriet.", "게다가 엘턴 부인의 — 그리고 미스터 엘턴의 — 해리엇을 대하는 태도는 불쾌했다."], ["They were sneering and negligent.", "비웃는 듯하고 함부로 대하는 투였다."], ["Emma hoped it must rapidly work Harriet’s cure; but the sensations which could prompt such behaviour sunk them both very much.—It was not to be doubted that poor Harriet’s attachment had been an offering to conjugal unreserve, and her own share in the story, under a colouring the least favourable to her and the most soothing to him, had in all likelihood been given also.", "엠마는 이것이 해리엇을 어서 낫게 하는 데 도움이 되기를 바랐으나, 그런 행동을 부추길 만한 심보를 떠올리면 두 사람이 한없이 천하게만 보였다. 가엾은 해리엇의 연정이 부부간의 거리낌 없는 속내 털어놓기의 제물이 되었으리라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었고, 그 이야기 속에서 엠마 자신이 맡은 역할도 — 그녀에게는 가장 불리하게, 그에게는 가장 위안이 되게 색칠된 채로 — 십중팔구 함께 털어놓아졌을 터였다."], ["She was, of course, the object of their joint dislike.—When they had nothing else to say, it must be always easy to begin abusing Miss Woodhouse; and the enmity which they dared not shew in open disrespect to her, found a broader vent in contemptuous treatment of Harriet.", "엠마는 당연히 두 사람이 함께 미워하는 대상이었다. 달리 할 말이 없을 때면 미스 우드하우스를 헐뜯기 시작하는 것은 언제나 손쉬운 일이었을 테고, 그녀에게 드러내 놓고 무례하게 보일 엄두는 못 내는 적의는 해리엇을 멸시하는 처사로 더 넓은 분풀이의 출구를 찾았다."]], [["Mrs. Elton took a great fancy to Jane Fairfax; and from the first.", "엘턴 부인은 제인 페어팩스를 무척 마음에 들어 했다."], ["Not merely when a state of warfare with one young lady might be supposed to recommend the other, but from the very first; and she was not satisfied with expressing a natural and reasonable admiration—but without solicitation, or plea, or privilege, she must be wanting to assist and befriend her.—Before Emma had forfeited her confidence, and about the third time of their meeting, she heard all Mrs.", "그것도 처음부터였다. 한 아가씨와 전쟁 상태에 들어서면 다른 아가씨를 추어올리게 마련이라고 짐작할 만한 시점부터가 아니라, 그야말로 맨 처음부터였다."], ["Elton’s knight-errantry on the subject.—", "게다가 자연스럽고 합당한 찬탄을 표하는 데 그치지 않고 — 누가 청하지도, 사정하지도, 그럴 자격을 주지도 않았는데 — 그녀를 돕고 그녀의 벗이 되어 주려 안달이었다. 엠마가 그 신임을 잃기 전, 세 번째쯤 만났을 무렵에 엠마는 이 문제에 관한 엘턴 부인의 의협심 넘치는 포부를 죄다 듣게 되었다."]], [["“Jane Fairfax is absolutely charming, Miss Woodhouse.—I quite rave about Jane Fairfax.—A sweet, interesting creature.", "\"제인 페어팩스는 정말이지 매력적이에요, 미스 우드하우스. 저는 제인 페어팩스 이야기라면 아주 열을 올린답니다."], ["So mild and ladylike—and with such talents!—I assure you I think she has very extraordinary talents.", "사랑스럽고 흥미로운 사람이에요. 어찌나 온화하고 숙녀다운지 — 게다가 그런 재능까지요!"], ["I do not scruple to say that she plays extremely well.", "정말이지 저는 그분이 비범한 재능을 지녔다고 봐요. 연주가 더없이 훌륭하다는 말도 서슴없이 하겠어요."], ["I know enough of music to speak decidedly on that point. Oh!", "음악이라면 저도 그 점을 단정 지어 말할 만큼은 아니까요."], ["she is absolutely charming!", "아유! 정말 매력적인 사람이에요!"], ["You will laugh at my warmth—but, upon my word, I talk of nothing but Jane Fairfax.—And her situation is so calculated to affect one!—Miss Woodhouse, we must exert ourselves and endeavour to do something for her.", "제 열성을 보고 웃으시겠지만 — 정말이지, 제 입에서는 제인 페어팩스 이야기밖에 안 나온다니까요. 게다가 그분 처지가 어찌나 사람 마음을 움직이는지!"], ["We must bring her forward.", "미스 우드하우스, 우리가 발 벗고 나서서 그분을 위해 무언가 해 줘야 해요. 그분을 끌어내 줘야지요."], ["Such talent as hers must not be suffered to remain unknown.—I dare say you have heard those charming lines of the poet,", "그만한 재능이 세상에 알려지지 않은 채 묻혀선 안 되는 거예요. 시인의 그 아름다운 시구를 들어 보셨을 거예요."]], [["‘Full many a flower is born to blush unseen, ‘And waste its fragrance on the desert air.’", "'숱한 꽃이 아무도 보지 않는 데서 피어나\n    그 향기를 황량한 허공에 헛되이 흩뿌리도다.'"]], [["We must not allow them to be verified in sweet Jane Fairfax.”", "사랑스러운 제인 페어팩스에게 이 시구가 들어맞게 두어선 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“I cannot think there is any danger of it,”", "\"그럴 위험이 있다고는 도무지 생각되지 않는데요.\" 엠마는 차분하게 대답했다."], ["was Emma’s calm answer—“and when you are better acquainted with Miss Fairfax’s situation and understand what her home has been, with Colonel and Mrs. Campbell, I have no idea that you will suppose her talents can be unknown.”", "\"그리고 미스 페어팩스의 처지를 좀 더 잘 아시고 그분이 캠벨 대령 부부와 어떤 가정을 이루며 지냈는지 이해하시게 되면, 그분의 재능이 알려지지 않은 채로 있으리라고는 생각지 않으시게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아유!"], ["but dear Miss Woodhouse, she is now in such retirement, such obscurity, so thrown away.—Whatever advantages she may have enjoyed with the Campbells are so palpably at an end!", "하지만 친애하는 미스 우드하우스, 그분은 지금 그렇게 외진 곳에, 그렇게 묻혀서, 그렇게 내버려진 처지잖아요. 캠벨 댁에서 누렸던 이점이 무엇이었든 이제는 너무나 뻔하게 끝나 버렸어요!"], ["And I think she feels it. I am sure she does.", "그리고 제 생각엔 그분도 그걸 느끼고 있어요."], ["She is very timid and silent.", "분명 그래요. 무척 수줍어하고 말이 없거든요."], ["One can see that she feels the want of encouragement.", "격려가 아쉽다는 걸 느끼는 게 보여요."], ["I like her the better for it.", "저는 그 점 때문에 그분이 더 좋아요."], ["I must confess it is a recommendation to me.", "솔직히 말하면, 그게 제겐 호감이 가는 점이에요."], ["I am a great advocate for timidity—and I am sure one does not often meet with it.—But in those who are at all inferior, it is extremely prepossessing.", "저는 수줍음을 아주 높이 산답니다 — 게다가 그런 사람은 정말 흔히 만나기 어렵잖아요. 그래도 처지가 좀 낮은 사람에게서 보이는 수줍음은 더없이 마음을 끄는 법이지요."], ["Oh!", "아유!"], ["I assure you, Jane Fairfax is a very delightful character, and interests me more than I can express.”", "정말이지, 제인 페어팩스는 무척 사랑스러운 사람이고, 말로 다 못 할 만큼 제 관심을 끈답니다.\""]], [["“You appear to feel a great deal—but I am not aware how you or any of Miss Fairfax’s acquaintance here, any of those who have known her longer than yourself, can shew her any other attention than”—", "\"부인께서 마음을 많이 쓰시는 것 같네요. 그렇지만 부인이든, 여기 있는 미스 페어팩스의 다른 지인이든, 그러니까 부인보다 그분을 더 오래 알아 온 사람들이든, 그분에게 어떤 다른 배려를 베풀 수 있을지는 잘 모르겠어요 —\""]], [["“My dear Miss Woodhouse, a vast deal may be done by those who dare to act.", "\"친애하는 미스 우드하우스, 과감히 나설 용기가 있는 사람이라면 굉장히 많은 일을 할 수 있어요."], ["You and I need not be afraid.", "미스 우드하우스와 저는 두려워할 게 없잖아요."], ["If we set the example, many will follow it as far as they can; though all have not our situations.", "우리가 본보기를 보이면, 비록 모두가 우리 같은 처지는 아니라도 많은 이가 제 형편 닿는 데까지 따라올 거예요."], ["We have carriages to fetch and convey her home, and we live in a style which could not make the addition of Jane Fairfax, at any time, the least inconvenient.—I should be extremely displeased if Wright were to send us up such a dinner, as could make me regret having asked more than Jane Fairfax to partake of it.", "우리에게는 그분을 데려오고 집까지 모셔다 드릴 마차가 있고, 우리 살림은 언제든 제인 페어팩스 한 사람쯤 더해진다 해서 조금도 거추장스러울 게 없을 만한 규모니까요. 라이트가 저녁상을 차려 올렸는데 제인 페어팩스 말고 사람을 더 청한 걸 후회하게 만들 정도라면, 저는 무척 언짢을 거예요."], ["I have no idea of that sort of thing.", "저는 그런 건 상상도 못 해요. 제가 익숙하게 살아온 걸 생각하면 그럴 리도 없고요."], ["It is not likely that I should, considering what I have been used to.", "살림에서 제가 빠지기 쉬운 가장 큰 위험은 어쩌면 정반대 쪽일지도 몰라요. 너무 많이 차리고 비용에 너무 무심한 쪽 말이에요."], ["My greatest danger, perhaps, in housekeeping, may be quite the other way, in doing too much, and being too careless of expense.", "어쩌면 마땅한 정도 이상으로 메이플 그로브가 제 본보기가 될지도 모르겠어요 — 그렇다고 우리가 수입 면에서 우리 형부 미스터 서클링과 어깨를 나란히 하는 척하는 건 결코 아니지만요. 어쨌든 제인 페어팩스에게 마음을 써 주기로 제 결심은 섰어요."], ["Maple Grove will probably be my model more than it ought to be—for we do not at all affect to equal my brother, Mr.", "분명 자주 우리 집에 그분을 모실 거고, 어디든 기회만 닿으면 소개하고, 음악 모임을 열어 그분의 재능을 끌어내고, 알맞은 자리가 없나 늘 살필 거예요. 제 교제 범위가 워낙 넓어서, 머잖아 그분에게 맞는 무언가 소식을 듣게 될 거라고 거의 의심치 않아요."], ["Suckling, in income.—However, my resolution is taken as to noticing Jane Fairfax.—I shall certainly have her very often at my house, shall introduce her wherever I can, shall have musical parties to draw out her talents, and shall be constantly on the watch for an eligible situation.", "그리고 물론 형부 부부가 우리 집에 오면 각별히 그분을 소개할 거예요. 분명 그분을 무척 좋아하실 거고요."], ["My acquaintance is so very extensive, that I have little doubt of hearing of something to suit her shortly.—I shall introduce her, of course, very particularly to my brother and sister when they come to us.", "그분도 조금만 친해지면 두려움이 말끔히 가실 거예요. 두 분 다 더없이 사람을 편안하게 해 주는 분들이라, 그 태도에는 정말이지 그런 것밖에 없으니까요."], ["I am sure they will like her extremely; and when she gets a little acquainted with them, her fears will completely wear off, for there really is nothing in the manners of either but what is highly conciliating.—I shall have her very often indeed while they are with me, and I dare say we shall sometimes find a seat for her in the barouche-landau in some of our exploring parties.”", "두 분이 와 계시는 동안엔 정말 자주 그분을 모실 거고, 단언컨대 우리가 이런저런 유람을 나갈 때면 가끔은 바루시-랜도에 그분 자리도 마련하게 될 거예요.\" * 바루시-랜도(barouche-landau): 두 가지 형태로 접을 수 있는 호화로운 사두마차."]], [["“Poor Jane Fairfax!”—thought Emma.—“You have not deserved this.", "\"가엾은 제인 페어팩스!\" 엠마는 속으로 생각했다."], ["You may have done wrong with regard to Mr.", "\"당신은 이런 대접을 받을 까닭이 없어. 미스터 딕슨 일로 잘못을 저질렀을지는 몰라도, 이건 당신이 받아 마땅한 벌을 넘어서는 거야!"], ["Dixon, but this is a punishment beyond what you can have merited!—The kindness and protection of Mrs.", "엘턴 부인의 친절과 비호라니! '제인 페어팩스, 또 제인 페어팩스.'"], ["Elton!—‘Jane Fairfax and Jane Fairfax.’ Heavens!", "세상에! 저 여자가 설마 여기저기 돌아다니며 나를 '엠마 우드하우스, 엠마 우드하우스'"], ["Let me not suppose that she dares go about, Emma Woodhouse-ing me!—But upon my honour, there seems no limits to the licentiousness of that woman’s tongue!”", "하고 불러 댄다고는 생각하지 말자! 하지만 정말이지, 저 여자의 방종한 혀에는 한계라는 게 없는 것 같구나!\""]], [["Emma had not to listen to such paradings again—to any so exclusively addressed to herself—so disgustingly decorated with a “dear Miss Woodhouse.” The change on Mrs.", "엠마는 다시는 그런 장광설을 들을 일이 없었다 — 오롯이 자기만을 향한, \"친애하는 미스 우드하우스\"로 역겹게 치장된 그런 말을 말이다."], ["Elton’s side soon afterwards appeared, and she was left in peace—neither forced to be the very particular friend of Mrs. Elton, nor, under Mrs.", "그 직후 엘턴 부인 쪽에서 변화가 나타났고, 엠마는 평온하게 내버려졌다 — 엘턴 부인의 각별한 벗이 되도록 강요당하지도 않았고, 엘턴 부인의 지휘 아래 제인 페어팩스의 더없이 적극적인 후원자가 되어야 할 일도 없었다."], ["Elton’s guidance, the very active patroness of Jane Fairfax, and only sharing with others in a general way, in knowing what was felt, what was meditated, what was done.", "그저 다른 사람들과 더불어 두루뭉술하게, 무슨 일이 일어났는지 알게 되는 정도만 함께할 뿐이었다."]], [["She looked on with some amusement.—Miss Bates’s gratitude for Mrs.", "엠마는 다소 흥미롭게 그 모든 것을 지켜보았다."], ["Elton’s attentions to Jane was in the first style of guileless simplicity and warmth.", "제인에 대한 엘턴 부인의 배려에 미스 베이츠가 보이는 고마움은, 더없이 순진무구하고 진심 어린 첫째가는 본보기였다."], ["She was quite one of her worthies—the most amiable, affable, delightful woman—just as accomplished and condescending as Mrs. Elton meant to be considered.", "엘턴 부인은 미스 베이츠가 떠받드는 인물 가운데 하나였으니 — 더없이 상냥하고, 더없이 붙임성 있고, 더없이 유쾌한 여인이었고 — 엘턴 부인이 그렇게 여겨지기를 바라던 바로 그만큼 교양 있고 너그러운 사람이었다."], ["Emma’s only surprize was that Jane Fairfax should accept those attentions and tolerate Mrs. Elton as she seemed to do.", "엠마가 유일하게 놀란 점은, 제인 페어팩스가 그런 배려를 받아들이고 엘턴 부인을 그처럼 견뎌 내는 듯하다는 사실이었다."], ["She heard of her walking with the Eltons, sitting with the Eltons, spending a day with the Eltons!", "제인이 엘턴 부부와 산책을 하고, 엘턴 부부와 함께 앉아 있고, 엘턴 부부와 하루를 보냈다는 소식이 들려왔다!"], ["This was astonishing!—She could not have believed it possible that the taste or the pride of Miss Fairfax could endure such society and friendship as the Vicarage had to offer.", "놀라운 일이었다! 미스 페어팩스의 취향이나 자존심이 목사관이 베풀 수 있는 그런 부류의 교제와 우정을 견딜 수 있으리라고는 도무지 믿기지 않았다."]], [["“She is a riddle, quite a riddle!”", "\"그분은 수수께끼야, 정말 수수께끼라니까!\""], ["said she.—“To chuse to remain here month after month, under privations of every sort!", "엠마는 말했다. \"온갖 궁핍을 견뎌 가며 달이 가고 또 가도록 여기 머물기를 택하다니!"], ["And now to chuse the mortification of Mrs. Elton’s notice and the penury of her conversation, rather than return to the superior companions who have always loved her with such real, generous affection.”", "게다가 이제는, 늘 그토록 진실하고 너그러운 애정으로 자기를 아껴 온 더 나은 벗들에게로 돌아가는 대신, 엘턴 부인의 관심이라는 굴욕과 그 대화의 빈곤을 택하다니.\""]], [["Jane had come to Highbury professedly for three months; the Campbells were gone to Ireland for three months; but now the Campbells had promised their daughter to stay at least till Midsummer, and fresh invitations had arrived for her to join them there.", "제인은 표면상 석 달 예정으로 하이베리에 왔다. 캠벨 부부도 석 달 일정으로 아일랜드에 가 있었다."], ["According to Miss Bates—it all came from her—Mrs.", "그런데 이제 캠벨 부부는 적어도 한여름까지 머물기로 딸에게 약속했고, 제인에게도 그곳에 합류하라는 새 초대장이 도착해 있었다."], ["Dixon had written most pressingly.", "미스 베이츠의 말로는 — 모든 소식은 그녀에게서 나왔다 — 딕슨 부인이 더없이 간곡하게 편지를 써 보냈다고 했다."], ["Would Jane but go, means were to be found, servants sent, friends contrived—no travelling difficulty allowed to exist; but still she had declined it!", "제인이 가기만 하면 방도는 마련될 것이고, 하인도 보내고, 동행할 벗도 주선해서, 여행에 따르는 어려움이라곤 있을 수 없게 해 주겠다는 것이었다. 그런데도 제인은 거절했다는 것이다!"]], [["“She must have some motive, more powerful than appears, for refusing this invitation,”", "\"이 초대를 거절하는 데는 겉으로 드러나는 것보다 더 강력한 어떤 동기가 있는 게 분명해.\""], ["was Emma’s conclusion.", "이것이 엠마의 결론이었다."], ["“She must be under some sort of penance, inflicted either by the Campbells or herself.", "\"캠벨 부부가, 아니면 자기 스스로 부과한 일종의 속죄를 치르고 있는 게 틀림없어."], ["There is great fear, great caution, great resolution somewhere.—She is not to be with the Dixons.", "어딘가에 큰 두려움이, 큰 조심성이, 큰 결심이 있는 거야. 그분은 딕슨 부부와 함께 있어선 안 되는 거지."], ["The decree is issued by somebody.", "누군가가 그런 명을 내린 거야."], ["But why must she consent to be with the Eltons?—Here is quite a separate puzzle.”", "그런데 어째서 엘턴 부부와는 함께 있겠다고 하는 걸까? 이건 완전히 별개의 수수께끼야.\""]], [["Upon her speaking her wonder aloud on that part of the subject, before the few who knew her opinion of Mrs. Elton, Mrs. Weston ventured this apology for Jane.", "엘턴 부인에 대한 자신의 견해를 아는 몇 안 되는 사람들 앞에서 엠마가 그 부분에 대한 의문을 소리 내어 말하자, 웨스턴 부인이 제인을 두둔하며 이렇게 변명해 보았다."]], [["“We cannot suppose that she has any great enjoyment at the Vicarage, my dear Emma—but it is better than being always at home.", "\"그분이 목사관에서 무슨 큰 즐거움을 누린다고야 생각할 수 없지요, 엠마. 하지만 늘 집에만 있는 것보다는 나으니까요."], ["Her aunt is a good creature, but, as a constant companion, must be very tiresome.", "이모님이야 착한 분이지만, 늘 곁에 있는 말동무로는 무척 따분할 게 분명해요."], ["We must consider what Miss Fairfax quits, before we condemn her taste for what she goes to.”", "미스 페어팩스가 무엇을 향해 가는지를 두고 그 취향을 나무라기 전에, 그분이 무엇을 떠나 있는지를 헤아려 봐야 해요.\""]], [["“You are right, Mrs.", "\"옳은 말씀이에요, 웨스턴 부인.\""], ["Weston,” said Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리가 힘주어 말했다."], ["Knightley warmly, “Miss Fairfax is as capable as any of us of forming a just opinion of Mrs.", "\"미스 페어팩스도 우리 누구 못지않게 엘턴 부인에 대해 올바른 판단을 내릴 줄 압니다."], ["Elton. Could she have chosen with whom to associate, she would not have chosen her.", "누구와 어울릴지 고를 수 있었다면 그녀를 고르진 않았겠지요."], ["But (with a reproachful smile at Emma) she receives attentions from Mrs. Elton, which nobody else pays her.”", "하지만 (엠마를 향해 나무라는 듯한 미소를 지으며) 미스 페어팩스는 엘턴 부인에게서, 아무도 그녀에게 베풀지 않는 배려를 받고 있는 겁니다.\""]], [["Emma felt that Mrs. Weston was giving her a momentary glance; and she was herself struck by his warmth.", "엠마는 웨스턴 부인이 자기에게 슬쩍 눈길을 던지는 것을 느꼈고, 그 자신도 그의 열의에 놀랐다."], ["With a faint blush, she presently replied,", "살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 엠마는 이내 대답했다."]], [["“Such attentions as Mrs. Elton’s, I should have imagined, would rather disgust than gratify Miss Fairfax.", "\"엘턴 부인이 베푸는 그런 배려라면, 제 생각엔 미스 페어팩스를 흡족하게 하기보다 오히려 진저리 나게 할 것 같은데요."], ["Mrs. Elton’s invitations I should have imagined any thing but inviting.”", "엘턴 부인의 초대라면 무엇이든 반갑기는커녕 그 반대일 거라고 생각했거든요.\""]], [["“I should not wonder,” said Mrs.", "\"미스 페어팩스가 제 마음과는 달리 끌려간 거라면 저는 별로 놀랍지 않을 것 같네요.\""], ["Weston, “if Miss Fairfax were to have been drawn on beyond her own inclination, by her aunt’s eagerness in accepting Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인이 말했다. \"이모님이 그분 대신 엘턴 부인의 호의를 덥석 받아들이려고 열을 올렸을 테니까요."], ["Elton’s civilities for her. Poor Miss Bates may very likely have committed her niece and hurried her into a greater appearance of intimacy than her own good sense would have dictated, in spite of the very natural wish of a little change.”", "가엾은 미스 베이츠가, 약간의 변화를 바라는 지극히 당연한 마음에서였다 해도, 자기 판단력이 일렀을 정도보다 더 친밀해 보이도록 조카를 떠밀어 그 일에 발을 들이게 했을 가능성이 아주 높지요.\""]], [["Both felt rather anxious to hear him speak again; and after a few minutes silence, he said,", "두 사람은 그가 다시 말해 주기를 사뭇 듣고 싶어 했다. 몇 분간 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 그가 말했다."]], [["“Another thing must be taken into consideration too—Mrs.", "\"또 한 가지 고려해야 할 점이 있습니다 — 엘턴 부인이 미스 페어팩스를 두고 말하는 것과, 그분에게 직접 말을 건넬 때는 다르다는 거지요."], ["Elton does not talk to Miss Fairfax as she speaks of her.", "우리 가운데 가장 솔직한 사람도, '그' 혹은 '그녀'라는 대명사와 면전의 '당신'"], ["We all know the difference between the pronouns he or she and thou, the plainest spoken amongst us; we all feel the influence of a something beyond common civility in our personal intercourse with each other—a something more early implanted.", "사이에 차이가 있다는 걸 다 압니다. 우리는 서로 직접 사귀고 부딪칠 때, 으레 갖추는 예의를 넘어서는 어떤 것의 영향을 다들 느끼지요 — 더 일찍부터 마음에 심어진 어떤 것 말입니다."], ["We cannot give any body the disagreeable hints that we may have been very full of the hour before.", "한 시간 전만 해도 잔뜩 품고 있었을지 모를 불쾌한 속내를, 막상 누구에게 대놓고 내비치지는 못하는 법입니다."], ["We feel things differently.", "우리는 면전에서는 다르게 느끼는 거예요."], ["And besides the operation of this, as a general principle, you may be sure that Miss Fairfax awes Mrs.", "그리고 이게 일반 원리로서 작용하는 데다, 분명히 말하건대, 미스 페어팩스는 정신과 태도 양쪽의 우월함으로 엘턴 부인을 압도합니다."], ["Elton by her superiority both of mind and manner; and that, face to face, Mrs. Elton treats her with all the respect which she has a claim to.", "그래서 얼굴을 마주하면 엘턴 부인은 그분에게 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼의 존중을 다 바칠 겁니다."], ["Such a woman as Jane Fairfax probably never fell in Mrs. Elton’s way before—and no degree of vanity can prevent her acknowledging her own comparative littleness in action, if not in consciousness.”", "제인 페어팩스 같은 여인은 아마 엘턴 부인이 여태 한 번도 마주친 적 없는 부류일 테고요 — 아무리 허영이 심한들, 의식으로는 아닐지언정 행동에서만큼은 자신이 상대적으로 보잘것없다는 걸 인정하지 않을 수 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["“I know how highly you think of Jane Fairfax,”", "\"미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스를 얼마나 높이 평가하시는지 잘 알아요.\""], ["said Emma.", "엠마가 말했다."], ["Little Henry was in her thoughts, and a mixture of alarm and delicacy made her irresolute what else to say.", "어린 헨리가 머릿속에 떠올라 있던 터라, 경계심과 조심스러움이 뒤섞여 달리 무슨 말을 해야 할지 망설여졌다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["he replied, “any body may know how highly I think of her.”", "그가 대답했다. \"내가 그녀를 얼마나 높이 평가하는지는 누구든 알 수 있겠지.\""]], [["“And yet,”", "\"하지만 그래도요.\""], ["said Emma, beginning hastily and with an arch look, but soon stopping—it was better, however, to know the worst at once—she hurried on—“And yet, perhaps, you may hardly be aware yourself how highly it is.", "엠마는 짓궂은 표정으로 성급하게 말을 꺼냈으나 곧 멈췄다 — 그래도 최악의 사실을 단번에 알아 두는 편이 낫겠다 싶어 — 서둘러 말을 이었다. \"하지만 그래도, 그게 얼마나 높은 평가인지는 미스터 나이틀리 자신도 잘 모르고 계실지 몰라요."], ["The extent of your admiration may take you by surprize some day or other.”", "언젠가 그 찬탄의 깊이에 스스로 깜짝 놀라시게 될지도 모르죠.\""]], [["Mr. Knightley was hard at work upon the lower buttons of his thick leather gaiters, and either the exertion of getting them together, or some other cause, brought the colour into his face, as he answered,", "미스터 나이틀리는 두꺼운 가죽 각반의 아래쪽 단추를 끼우느라 여념이 없었는데, 단추를 맞춰 끼우려는 그 안간힘 탓인지 아니면 다른 무슨 까닭에선지, 대답하는 동안 얼굴에 핏기가 올랐다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["are you there?—But you are miserably behindhand.", "그쪽 이야기였나? 하지만 자네는 한참 뒤처졌네."], ["Mr. Cole gave me a hint of it six weeks ago.”", "미스터 콜이 그 얘기로 운을 뗀 게 벌써 여섯 주 전이야.\""]], [["He stopped.—Emma felt her foot pressed by Mrs.", "그가 말을 멈췄다."], ["Weston, and did not herself know what to think.", "엠마는 웨스턴 부인이 발을 꾹 누르는 것을 느꼈으나, 자기 스스로도 어떻게 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["In a moment he went on—", "잠시 후 그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“That will never be, however, I can assure you.", "\"하지만 그런 일은 결코 없을 거라고 장담하네."], ["Miss Fairfax, I dare say, would not have me if I were to ask her—and I am very sure I shall never ask her.”", "단언컨대 미스 페어팩스는, 내가 청한다 한들 나를 받아들이지 않을 거야 — 그리고 나는 절대 그녀에게 청하지 않을 게 분명하고.\""]], [["Emma returned her friend’s pressure with interest; and was pleased enough to exclaim,", "엠마는 웨스턴 부인이 발을 누른 것에 곱절로 답하며, 흐뭇한 나머지 이렇게 외쳤다."]], [["“You are not vain, Mr. Knightley.", "\"미스터 나이틀리는 허영심이 없으시군요."], ["I will say that for you.”", "그것만은 인정해 드릴게요.\""]], [["He seemed hardly to hear her; he was thoughtful—and in a manner which shewed him not pleased, soon afterwards said,", "그는 그 말을 거의 듣지 못한 듯했다. 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있었고 — 흡족하지 않은 기색을 드러내는 투로 — 이내 말했다."]], [["“So you have been settling that I should marry Jane Fairfax?”", "\"그러니까 자네가 나를 제인 페어팩스와 맺어 놓기로 작정했던 거로군?\""]], [["“No indeed I have not.", "\"아니, 정말 그런 적 없어요."], ["You have scolded me too much for match-making, for me to presume to take such a liberty with you.", "중매를 선다고 저를 어찌나 야단치셨는데, 감히 미스터 나이틀리한테 그런 짓을 할 엄두를 내겠어요."], ["What I said just now, meant nothing.", "방금 한 말은 아무 뜻도 없었어요."], ["One says those sort of things, of course, without any idea of a serious meaning.", "그런 말이야 으레 진지한 의미 따위 없이 하는 거잖아요. 아유!"], ["Oh! no, upon my word I have not the smallest wish for your marrying Jane Fairfax or Jane any body.", "아니에요, 맹세컨대 미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스든 어느 제인이든 결혼하기를 바라는 마음은 눈곱만큼도 없어요."], ["You would not come in and sit with us in this comfortable way, if you were married.”", "결혼하시면 이렇게 들러서 우리랑 편안하게 앉아 계시지도 못하실 텐데요.\""]], [["Mr. Knightley was thoughtful again.", "미스터 나이틀리는 다시 생각에 잠겼다."], ["The result of his reverie was, “No, Emma, I do not think the extent of my admiration for her will ever take me by surprize.—I never had a thought of her in that way, I assure you.”", "그 골똘함의 결론은 이러했다. \"아니야, 엠마, 나는 그녀에 대한 내 찬탄의 깊이에 내가 깜짝 놀랄 일은 결코 없으리라고 생각하네."], ["And soon afterwards, “Jane Fairfax is a very charming young woman—but not even Jane Fairfax is perfect.", "단언컨대 나는 한 번도 그런 식으로 그녀를 생각해 본 적이 없어.\" 그러고는 이내 덧붙였다."], ["She has a fault.", "\"제인 페어팩스는 무척 매력적인 아가씨지 — 하지만 그 제인 페어팩스조차 완벽하지는 않아."], ["She has not the open temper which a man would wish for in a wife.”", "흠이 하나 있지. 남자가 아내에게서 바랄 법한 솔직하고 트인 성품이 그녀에겐 없어.\""]], [["Emma could not but rejoice to hear that she had a fault.", "엠마는 그녀에게 흠이 있다는 말을 듣고 기뻐하지 않을 수 없었다. \"그렇군요.\""], ["“Well,”", "엠마가 말했다."], ["said she, “and you soon silenced Mr. Cole, I suppose?”", "\"그래서 미스터 콜은 곧 입을 다물게 하셨겠죠?\""]], [["“Yes, very soon.", "\"그래, 아주 금방."], ["He gave me a quiet hint; I told him he was mistaken; he asked my pardon and said no more.", "그가 슬쩍 운을 떼기에, 잘못 짚었다고 일러 줬지. 그랬더니 미안하다며 더는 말이 없더군."], ["Cole does not want to be wiser or wittier than his neighbours.”", "콜은 이웃들보다 더 똑똑하거나 더 재치 있어 보이려는 사람이 아니니까.\""]], [["“In that respect how unlike dear Mrs.", "\"그 점에선 친애하는 엘턴 부인과 어쩜 그리 다른지요."], ["Elton, who wants to be wiser and wittier than all the world!", "그분은 온 세상 누구보다 더 똑똑하고 더 재치 있어 보이고 싶어 하잖아요!"], ["I wonder how she speaks of the Coles—what she calls them!", "그분은 콜네 식구들을 두고 어떻게 말할지 궁금하네요 — 뭐라고 부를까요!"], ["How can she find any appellation for them, deep enough in familiar vulgarity?", "무람없는 천박함에 흠뻑 절은 호칭을 어디서 찾아낼 수 있을까요?"], ["She calls you, Knightley—what can she do for Mr. Cole?", "미스터 나이틀리는 그냥 '나이틀리'라고 부르던데 — 미스터 콜한테는 또 어떻게 할까요?"], ["And so I am not to be surprized that Jane Fairfax accepts her civilities and consents to be with her.", "그러니 제인 페어팩스가 그분의 호의를 받아들이고 함께 어울리기로 한다 해서 제가 놀랄 일은 아니겠네요."], ["Mrs. Weston, your argument weighs most with me.", "웨스턴 부인, 부인의 논리가 제겐 가장 설득력 있어요."], ["I can much more readily enter into the temptation of getting away from Miss Bates, than I can believe in the triumph of Miss Fairfax’s mind over Mrs. Elton.", "미스 페어팩스의 정신이 엘턴 부인을 누른다는 말보다는, 미스 베이츠한테서 벗어나고 싶은 유혹에 빠진다는 쪽이 훨씬 더 쉽게 납득돼요."], ["I have no faith in Mrs. Elton’s acknowledging herself the inferior in thought, word, or deed; or in her being under any restraint beyond her own scanty rule of good-breeding.", "저는 엘턴 부인이 생각에서든 말에서든 행동에서든 자신이 못하다는 걸 인정하리라고는, 또 그 옹색한 교양의 규칙 말고 다른 어떤 절제에 매여 있으리라고는 도무지 믿지 못하겠어요."], ["I cannot imagine that she will not be continually insulting her visitor with praise, encouragement, and offers of service; that she will not be continually detailing her magnificent intentions, from the procuring her a permanent situation to the including her in those delightful exploring parties which are to take place in the barouche-landau.”", "그분이 손님에게 끊임없이 칭찬이며 격려며 도와주겠다는 제안을 퍼부어 모욕을 안기지 않으리라고는, 영구적인 일자리를 구해 주는 것부터 바루시-랜도에 태워 떠날 그 유쾌한 유람에 끼워 주는 것까지 자기의 거창한 포부를 끊임없이 늘어놓지 않으리라고는 상상이 안 돼요.\""]], [["“Jane Fairfax has feeling,” said Mr.", "\"제인 페어팩스에게도 감정이 있습니다.\""], ["Knightley—“I do not accuse her of want of feeling.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말했다. \"나는 그녀가 무정하다고 탓하는 게 아니에요."], ["Her sensibilities, I suspect, are strong—and her temper excellent in its power of forbearance, patience, self-control; but it wants openness.", "내 짐작엔 그녀의 감수성은 강합니다 — 그리고 그 성품은 참고 견디는 힘에서, 인내에서, 자제력에서 더없이 훌륭하고요. 다만 트인 데가 모자라요."], ["She is reserved, more reserved, I think, than she used to be—And I love an open temper.", "그녀는 속을 잘 드러내지 않아요. 예전보다 더 그런 것 같습니다 — 그런데 나는 트인 성품을 사랑하거든요."], ["No—till Cole alluded to my supposed attachment, it had never entered my head.", "아니야 — 콜이 내가 그녀에게 마음을 두었다는 소문을 들먹이기 전에는, 그런 생각이 한 번도 머리에 떠오른 적이 없어."], ["I saw Jane Fairfax and conversed with her, with admiration and pleasure always—but with no thought beyond.”", "나는 늘 찬탄과 즐거움으로 제인 페어팩스를 바라보고 그녀와 대화를 나눴지만 — 그 이상의 생각은 없었어.\""]], [["“Well, Mrs. Weston,”", "\"자, 웨스턴 부인.\""], ["said Emma triumphantly when he left them, “what do you say now to Mr.", "그가 자리를 뜨자 엠마가 의기양양하게 말했다."], ["Knightley’s marrying Jane Fairfax?”", "\"이제 미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스와 결혼하는 일을 두고 뭐라 하시겠어요?\""]], [["“Why, really, dear Emma, I say that he is so very much occupied by the idea of not being in love with her, that I should not wonder if it were to end in his being so at last.", "\"글쎄, 정말이지, 엠마. 내가 보기엔 그분이 그녀를 사랑하지 않는다는 생각에 너무도 골몰해 계셔서, 결국엔 그게 사랑하는 걸로 끝난다 해도 난 놀랄 것 같지 않구나."], ["Do not beat me.”", "날 때리진 말렴.\""]], [["# Volume II — Chapter XVI", "# 제2권 16장"]], [["Every body in and about Highbury who had ever visited Mr. Elton, was disposed to pay him attention on his marriage.", "하이베리 안팎에서 미스터 엘턴과 한 번이라도 왕래가 있던 사람이라면 누구나 그의 혼인에 마음을 써 주려 했다."], ["Dinner-parties and evening-parties were made for him and his lady; and invitations flowed in so fast that she had soon the pleasure of apprehending they were never to have a disengaged day.", "그와 그 부인을 위한 만찬 모임과 저녁 모임이 마련되었고, 초대가 어찌나 밀려드는지 부인은 곧 자기들에게는 한가한 날이라곤 없게 되리라는 즐거운 짐작을 하게 되었다."]], [["“I see how it is,” said she.", "\"어떻게 돌아가는지 알겠어요.\""], ["“I see what a life I am to lead among you.", "부인이 말했다. \"제가 여러분 사이에서 어떤 생활을 하게 될지 훤히 보인다고요."], ["Upon my word we shall be absolutely dissipated.", "정말이지 우리는 흥청망청 놀 수밖에 없겠어요."], ["We really seem quite the fashion.", "우리가 영락없이 유행의 중심이로군요."], ["If this is living in the country, it is nothing very formidable.", "시골 생활이라는 게 이런 거라면, 그다지 무서울 것도 없네요."], ["From Monday next to Saturday, I assure you we have not a disengaged day!—A woman with fewer resources than I have, need not have been at a loss.”", "다음 주 월요일부터 토요일까지, 장담하건대 한가한 날이 하루도 없어요! 저보다 마음 둘 데가 적은 여자라도 심심할 틈은 없었을 거예요.\""]], [["No invitation came amiss to her. Her Bath habits made evening-parties perfectly natural to her, and Maple Grove had given her a taste for dinners.", "어떤 초대든 그녀에게는 마다할 것이 없었다."], ["She was a little shocked at the want of two drawing rooms, at the poor attempt at rout-cakes, and there being no ice in the Highbury card-parties. Mrs.", "바스에서 익힌 습관 덕에 저녁 모임은 더없이 자연스러운 것이었고, 메이플 그로브에서는 만찬을 즐기는 취향을 키웠다."], ["Bates, Mrs. Perry, Mrs.", "응접실이 둘 있지 않은 것, 차 모임 과자가 변변치 못한 것, 하이베리의 카드 모임에 얼음이 없는 것에는 적이 충격을 받았다."], ["Goddard and others, were a good deal behind-hand in knowledge of the world, but she would soon shew them how every thing ought to be arranged.", "베이츠 부인, 페리 부인, 고더드 부인을 비롯한 이들은 세상물정을 아는 데서는 한참 뒤떨어져 있었으나, 모든 것을 어떻게 차려야 마땅한지 곧 자기가 본을 보여 줄 참이었다."], ["In the course of the spring she must return their civilities by one very superior party—in which her card-tables should be set out with their separate candles and unbroken packs in the true style—and more waiters engaged for the evening than their own establishment could furnish, to carry round the refreshments at exactly the proper hour, and in the proper order.", "봄이 가기 전에 한 차례 아주 격조 높은 모임으로 그들의 호의에 답례해야 했다 — 그 모임에서는 카드 탁자마다 따로 촛불을 놓고 뜯지 않은 새 카드 묶음을 제대로 갖춰 차릴 것이며 — 정확히 알맞은 시각에 알맞은 순서로 다과를 돌리도록, 자기 집 하인만으로는 모자랄 만큼 많은 시중꾼을 그날 저녁을 위해 더 부를 작정이었다."]], [["Emma, in the meanwhile, could not be satisfied without a dinner at Hartfield for the Eltons.", "한편 엠마는 하트필드에서 엘턴 부부를 위한 만찬을 베풀지 않고는 마음이 놓이지 않았다."], ["They must not do less than others, or she should be exposed to odious suspicions, and imagined capable of pitiful resentment.", "남들만큼 하지 않으면 안 될 일이었으니, 그러지 않으면 고약한 의심을 사고 옹졸한 앙심을 품을 사람으로 여겨질 판이었다."], ["A dinner there must be.", "만찬은 꼭 있어야 했다."], ["After Emma had talked about it for ten minutes, Mr. Woodhouse felt no unwillingness, and only made the usual stipulation of not sitting at the bottom of the table himself, with the usual regular difficulty of deciding who should do it for him.", "엠마가 십 분쯤 이야기를 늘어놓자 미스터 우드하우스는 별로 마다하지 않았고, 다만 자신은 식탁 끝자리에 앉지 않겠다는 늘 하던 조건을 달았는데, 그러자 그를 대신해 누가 그 자리에 앉을지 정하는 늘 있던 어려움이 어김없이 뒤따랐다."]], [["The persons to be invited, required little thought.", "초대할 사람을 정하는 데는 별 고민이 필요 없었다."], ["Besides the Eltons, it must be the Westons and Mr.", "엘턴 부부 외에 웨스턴 부부와 미스터 나이틀리는 당연했고, 거기까지는 두말할 것이 없었다."], ["Knightley; so far it was all of course—and it was hardly less inevitable that poor little Harriet must be asked to make the eighth:—but this invitation was not given with equal satisfaction, and on many accounts Emma was particularly pleased by Harriet’s begging to be allowed to decline it.", "그리고 여덟 번째 자리를 채우려면 가엾은 어린 해리엇을 청해야 한다는 것도 거의 그만큼 피할 수 없는 일이었다. 다만 이 초대만큼은 흔쾌한 마음으로 한 것이 아니어서, 해리엇이 사양하게 해 달라고 청해 왔을 때 엠마는 여러모로 각별히 기뻤다."], ["“She would rather not be in his company more than she could help.", "\"되도록이면 그분과 한자리에 있고 싶지 않다고 했다."], ["She was not yet quite able to see him and his charming happy wife together, without feeling uncomfortable.", "아직은 그분과 그 매력적이고 행복한 부인이 함께 있는 모습을 보면서 마음이 편하기가 어렵다고 했다."], ["If Miss Woodhouse would not be displeased, she would rather stay at home.”", "미스 우드하우스만 언짢지 않으시다면 차라리 집에 있겠다고 했다.\""], ["It was precisely what Emma would have wished, had she deemed it possible enough for wishing.", "그것은 엠마가 바랄 만하다고 여겼더라면 바로 그렇게 바랐을 일이었다."], ["She was delighted with the fortitude of her little friend—for fortitude she knew it was in her to give up being in company and stay at home; and she could now invite the very person whom she really wanted to make the eighth, Jane Fairfax.— Since her last conversation with Mrs.", "어린 벗의 의연함이 엠마는 흐뭇했다 — 사람들과 어울리기를 포기하고 집에 있겠다는 것은 그 아이로서는 분명 의연함이었으니까. 그리하여 이제 여덟 번째 자리에 정말로 앉히고 싶던 바로 그 사람, 제인 페어팩스를 청할 수 있게 되었다."], ["Weston and Mr. Knightley, she was more conscience-stricken about Jane Fairfax than she had often been.—Mr.", "웨스턴 부인과 미스터 나이틀리와 지난번 이야기를 나눈 뒤로, 엠마는 제인 페어팩스를 두고 여느 때보다 더 양심의 가책을 느끼고 있었다."], ["Knightley’s words dwelt with her.", "미스터 나이틀리의 말이 마음에 걸려 떠나지 않았다."], ["He had said that Jane Fairfax received attentions from Mrs. Elton which nobody else paid her.", "그는 제인 페어팩스가 다른 누구도 베풀지 않는 관심을 엘턴 부인에게서만 받고 있다고 했다."]], [["“This is very true,”", "\"정말 그래.\" 엠마는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["said she, “at least as far as relates to me, which was all that was meant—and it is very shameful.—Of the same age—and always knowing her—I ought to have been more her friend.—She will never like me now.", "\"적어도 나로 말하자면 그렇지. 그게 바로 그 말의 뜻이었으니까 — 정말 부끄러운 일이야."], ["I have neglected her too long.", "동갑인 데다 — 줄곧 알고 지낸 사이인데 — 내가 더 좋은 벗이 되어 주었어야 했어. 이제는 절대 나를 좋아해 주지 않겠지."], ["But I will shew her greater attention than I have done.”", "너무 오래 그 애를 소홀히 했어. 그래도 이제부터는 지금까지보다 더 마음을 써 줘야지.\""]], [["Every invitation was successful.", "초대는 모두 성사되었다. 다들 한가했고 다들 기뻐했다."], ["They were all disengaged and all happy.—The preparatory interest of this dinner, however, was not yet over.", "그러나 이 만찬을 앞둔 일들이 아직 끝난 것은 아니었다. 다소 운 나쁜 일이 하나 생겼다."], ["A circumstance rather unlucky occurred.", "나이틀리네 큰 아이 둘이 봄에 외할아버지와 이모를 몇 주간 찾아오기로 되어 있었는데, 이제 그 아이들 아버지가 자기가 직접 데려와 하트필드에서 꼬박 하루를 묵겠다고 제안한 것이다 — 그런데 하필 그 하루가 바로 이 모임이 있는 날이었다."], ["The two eldest little Knightleys were engaged to pay their grandpapa and aunt a visit of some weeks in the spring, and their papa now proposed bringing them, and staying one whole day at Hartfield—which one day would be the very day of this party.—His professional engagements did not allow of his being put off, but both father and daughter were disturbed by its happening so.", "직업상의 일정이 있어 미룰 수가 없었으니, 부녀는 둘 다 일이 이렇게 겹친 데 마음이 편치 않았다. 미스터 우드하우스는 한 식탁에 여덟 사람이 둘러앉는 것을 자기 신경이 견딜 수 있는 최대치로 여겼는데, 여기에 아홉 번째 사람이 생기게 된 것이었다."], ["Mr. Woodhouse considered eight persons at dinner together as the utmost that his nerves could bear—and here would be a ninth—and Emma apprehended that it would be a ninth very much out of humour at not being able to come even to Hartfield for forty-eight hours without falling in with a dinner-party.", "엠마는 또 엠마대로, 마흔여덟 시간조차 만찬 모임과 마주치지 않고서는 하트필드에 다녀갈 수 없게 된 데 잔뜩 심기가 틀어진 아홉 번째 손님이 되리라는 것을 걱정했다."]], [["She comforted her father better than she could comfort herself, by representing that though he certainly would make them nine, yet he always said so little, that the increase of noise would be very immaterial.", "엠마는 자기 마음을 달래는 것보다는 아버지의 마음을 더 잘 달랬으니, 형부가 분명 아홉 번째 사람이 되기는 하지만 워낙 말수가 적은 사람이라 시끄러움이 늘어날 일은 거의 없을 거라고 일러 드렸다."], ["She thought it in reality a sad exchange for herself, to have him with his grave looks and reluctant conversation opposed to her instead of his brother.", "사실 엠마는 자기로서는 형부의 형님 대신 그 시무룩한 얼굴과 마지못한 대화를 맞은편에 두게 된 것이 못내 아쉬운 맞바꿈이라고 생각했다."]], [["The event was more favourable to Mr. Woodhouse than to Emma.", "일은 엠마보다 미스터 우드하우스에게 더 유리하게 풀렸다."], ["John Knightley came; but Mr. Weston was unexpectedly summoned to town and must be absent on the very day.", "존 나이틀리는 왔지만, 미스터 웨스턴이 뜻밖에 시내로 불려 가게 되어 바로 그날 자리를 비울 수밖에 없게 된 것이다."], ["He might be able to join them in the evening, but certainly not to dinner. Mr.", "저녁에는 합류할 수 있을지 몰라도 만찬에는 결코 올 수 없게 되었다."], ["Woodhouse was quite at ease; and the seeing him so, with the arrival of the little boys and the philosophic composure of her brother on hearing his fate, removed the chief of even Emma’s vexation.", "미스터 우드하우스는 마음이 아주 놓였다. 그렇게 편안해진 아버지의 모습에다 어린 사내아이들이 도착한 것, 그리고 자기 운명을 듣고도 의연하고 담담한 형부의 태도가 더해지자, 엠마의 언짢음마저 거의 가셨다."]], [["The day came, the party were punctually assembled, and Mr. John Knightley seemed early to devote himself to the business of being agreeable.", "그날이 왔고, 일행은 시각에 맞춰 모였으며, 미스터 존 나이틀리는 일찌감치 상냥하게 굴기로 작정한 듯 보였다."], ["Instead of drawing his brother off to a window while they waited for dinner, he was talking to Miss Fairfax. Mrs.", "만찬을 기다리는 동안 형을 창가로 끌고 가는 대신, 그는 미스 페어팩스와 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Elton, as elegant as lace and pearls could make her, he looked at in silence—wanting only to observe enough for Isabella’s information—but Miss Fairfax was an old acquaintance and a quiet girl, and he could talk to her.", "레이스와 진주로 차릴 수 있는 한 우아하게 차린 엘턴 부인을, 그는 말없이 바라보기만 했다 — 이저벨라에게 일러 줄 만큼만 살펴 두려는 것이었다. 그러나 미스 페어팩스는 오랜 지인이자 조용한 아가씨여서, 그녀와는 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다."], ["He had met her before breakfast as he was returning from a walk with his little boys, when it had been just beginning to rain.", "그는 어린 아들들과 산책을 마치고 돌아오던 아침 식사 전에 그녀와 마주쳤는데, 그때 막 비가 듣기 시작하던 참이었다."], ["It was natural to have some civil hopes on the subject, and he said,", "그 일에 관해 으레 하는 정중한 인사말이 자연스럽게 나왔다."]], [["“I hope you did not venture far, Miss Fairfax, this morning, or I am sure you must have been wet.—We scarcely got home in time.", "\"오늘 아침 멀리까지는 안 나가셨기를 바랍니다, 미스 페어팩스. 안 그러면 분명 흠뻑 젖으셨을 텐데요."], ["I hope you turned directly.”", "저희도 간신히 제때 들어왔거든요. 곧장 돌아오셨기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“I went only to the post-office,”", "\"우체국까지만 갔어요.\""], ["said she, “and reached home before the rain was much.", "그녀가 말했다. \"비가 많이 오기 전에 집에 닿았고요."], ["It is my daily errand.", "날마다 하는 심부름이에요."], ["I always fetch the letters when I am here.", "여기 있을 때는 늘 제가 편지를 받아 오거든요."], ["It saves trouble, and is a something to get me out.", "수고도 덜고, 밖에 나갈 구실도 되니까요."], ["A walk before breakfast does me good.”", "아침 식사 전 산책은 제게 좋답니다.\""]], [["“Not a walk in the rain, I should imagine.”", "\"빗속 산책이 좋을 리야 없겠지요.\""]], [["“No, but it did not absolutely rain when I set out.”", "\"그야 그렇죠. 하지만 나설 때는 정말로 비가 오지는 않았어요.\""]], [["Mr. John Knightley smiled, and replied,", "미스터 존 나이틀리는 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“That is to say, you chose to have your walk, for you were not six yards from your own door when I had the pleasure of meeting you; and Henry and John had seen more drops than they could count long before.", "\"그러니까, 산책을 꼭 하고 싶으셨던 거군요. 제가 뵙는 기쁨을 누렸을 때 댁 문에서 여섯 야드도 채 안 떨어진 곳이셨는데, 헨리와 존은 이미 한참 전부터 셀 수 없을 만큼 많은 빗방울을 보고 있었으니까요."], ["The post-office has a great charm at one period of our lives.", "우체국이라는 게 우리 인생의 어느 한때는 그렇게 매력적인 법이지요."], ["When you have lived to my age, you will begin to think letters are never worth going through the rain for.”", "제 나이까지 살아 보시면, 비를 무릅쓰고 받으러 갈 만한 편지란 없다고 여기기 시작하실 겁니다.\""]], [["There was a little blush, and then this answer,", "살짝 얼굴을 붉히더니, 이런 대답이 돌아왔다."]], [["“I must not hope to be ever situated as you are, in the midst of every dearest connexion, and therefore I cannot expect that simply growing older should make me indifferent about letters.”", "\"저는 결코 선생님 같은 처지에 놓일 수 있으리라고 기대해서는 안 되겠지요. 더없이 소중한 인연들에 둘러싸여 계시잖아요. 그러니 그저 나이가 든다고 해서 제가 편지에 무심해지리라고는 바랄 수가 없네요.\""]], [["“Indifferent!", "\"무심이라니요!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["no—I never conceived you could become indifferent.", "아닙니다 — 미스 페어팩스가 무심해지실 수 있으리라고는 생각해 본 적이 없어요."], ["Letters are no matter of indifference; they are generally a very positive curse.”", "편지란 무심할 수 있는 것이 아니지요. 대개는 아주 확실한 골칫거리니까요.\""]], [["“You are speaking of letters of business; mine are letters of friendship.”", "\"업무 편지를 말씀하시는 거군요. 제 것은 우정의 편지예요.\""]], [["“I have often thought them the worst of the two,”", "\"저는 그 둘 중 우정의 편지가 더 고약한 것이라고 종종 생각해 왔습니다.\""], ["replied he coolly.", "그가 차분하게 대꾸했다."], ["“Business, you know, may bring money, but friendship hardly ever does.”", "\"업무란 아시다시피 돈을 가져다주기도 하지만, 우정이 돈을 가져다주는 일은 좀처럼 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you are not serious now. I know Mr.", "지금 진심으로 하시는 말씀은 아니죠."], ["John Knightley too well—I am very sure he understands the value of friendship as well as any body.", "저는 미스터 존 나이틀리를 너무 잘 알아요 — 그분이 누구 못지않게 우정의 가치를 잘 아신다는 걸 확신한답니다. 편지가 선생님께는 별것 아니라는 것, 저보다 훨씬 덜하시다는 것은 쉽게 믿을 수 있어요."], ["I can easily believe that letters are very little to you, much less than to me, but it is not your being ten years older than myself which makes the difference, it is not age, but situation.", "하지만 그 차이를 만드는 건 선생님이 저보다 열 살 위라는 사실이 아니에요. 나이가 아니라 처지죠."], ["You have every body dearest to you always at hand, I, probably, never shall again; and therefore till I have outlived all my affections, a post-office, I think, must always have power to draw me out, in worse weather than to-day.”", "선생님은 더없이 소중한 사람들을 늘 곁에 두고 계시지만, 저는 아마 다시는 그렇지 못할 거예요. 그러니 제 모든 애정보다 더 오래 살아남기 전까지는, 우체국이란 오늘보다 더 궂은 날씨에도 늘 저를 밖으로 끌어낼 힘을 지니고 있을 거라고 생각해요.\""]], [["“When I talked of your being altered by time, by the progress of years,”", "\"제가 시간이 흐르면, 세월이 가면 미스 페어팩스도 달라지리라고 말한 것은,\""], ["said John Knightley, “I meant to imply the change of situation which time usually brings.", "존 나이틀리가 말했다. \"시간이 으레 가져오는 처지의 변화를 두고 한 말이었습니다."], ["I consider one as including the other.", "저는 그 하나가 다른 하나를 품고 있다고 보거든요."], ["Time will generally lessen the interest of every attachment not within the daily circle—but that is not the change I had in view for you.", "시간은 대개 날마다 마주하는 둘레 안에 들지 않는 모든 연정의 무게를 덜어 주는 법이지요 — 하지만 그것이 제가 미스 페어팩스를 두고 떠올린 변화는 아니었습니다."], ["As an old friend, you will allow me to hope, Miss Fairfax, that ten years hence you may have as many concentrated objects as I have.”", "오랜 친구로서, 미스 페어팩스, 십 년 뒤에는 미스 페어팩스도 저처럼 마음을 쏟을 사람들을 곁에 잔뜩 두시기를 바라게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["It was kindly said, and very far from giving offence. A pleasant “thank you”", "다정하게 한 말이었고, 거슬릴 데라곤 전혀 없었다."], ["seemed meant to laugh it off, but a blush, a quivering lip, a tear in the eye, shewed that it was felt beyond a laugh. Her attention was now claimed by Mr.", "흔쾌한 \"고맙습니다\"가 그 말을 웃어넘기려는 듯했으나, 발그레해진 얼굴과 떨리는 입술과 눈에 맺힌 눈물이, 웃음 이상으로 그 말이 와닿았음을 드러냈다."], ["Woodhouse, who being, according to his custom on such occasions, making the circle of his guests, and paying his particular compliments to the ladies, was ending with her—and with all his mildest urbanity, said,", "이제 미스터 우드하우스가 그녀의 관심을 청했으니, 그는 이런 자리에서 늘 하던 버릇대로 손님들을 한 바퀴 돌며 부인들에게 각별한 인사말을 건네던 차에, 그녀에게서 그 순서를 마무리하던 참이었다. 그는 더없이 부드럽고 정중하게 말했다."]], [["“I am very sorry to hear, Miss Fairfax, of your being out this morning in the rain.", "\"미스 페어팩스, 오늘 아침 빗속에 나가셨다니 참으로 안되었군요."], ["Young ladies should take care of themselves.—Young ladies are delicate plants.", "젊은 아가씨들은 몸을 잘 돌봐야 해요. 젊은 아가씨들은 여린 화초나 다름없지요."], ["They should take care of their health and their complexion.", "건강과 얼굴빛을 잘 보살펴야 합니다."], ["My dear, did you change your stockings?”", "얘야, 양말은 갈아 신었느냐?\""]], [["“Yes, sir, I did indeed; and I am very much obliged by your kind solicitude about me.”", "\"네, 어르신, 정말 갈아 신었어요. 이렇게 마음 써 주셔서 정말 감사드립니다.\""]], [["“My dear Miss Fairfax, young ladies are very sure to be cared for.—I hope your good grand-mama and aunt are well.", "\"미스 페어팩스, 젊은 아가씨들이야 응당 보살핌을 받게 마련이지요. 자네의 훌륭하신 할머님과 이모님도 평안하시기를 바라오."], ["They are some of my very old friends.", "그분들은 내 아주 오랜 벗들이라오."], ["I wish my health allowed me to be a better neighbour.", "건강이 받쳐 주어 내가 좀 더 나은 이웃이 될 수 있다면 좋으련만."], ["You do us a great deal of honour to-day, I am sure.", "오늘 자네가 우리를 크게 빛내 주었소, 정말이오."], ["My daughter and I are both highly sensible of your goodness, and have the greatest satisfaction in seeing you at Hartfield.”", "내 딸과 나는 자네의 고마운 마음을 깊이 느끼고 있고, 하트필드에서 자네를 보게 되어 더할 나위 없이 기쁘다오.\""]], [["The kind-hearted, polite old man might then sit down and feel that he had done his duty, and made every fair lady welcome and easy.", "마음씨 곱고 정중한 노인은 그제야 자리에 앉아, 제 도리를 다했으며 모든 아리따운 부인들을 반갑고 편안하게 맞이했다는 흐뭇함을 느낄 수 있었다."]], [["By this time, the walk in the rain had reached Mrs. Elton, and her remonstrances now opened upon Jane.", "이때쯤 빗속 산책 이야기가 엘턴 부인에게까지 닿아, 이제 그녀의 잔소리가 제인을 향해 터져 나왔다."]], [["“My dear Jane, what is this I hear?—Going to the post-office in the rain!—This must not be, I assure you.—You sad girl, how could you do such a thing?—It is a sign I was not there to take care of you.”", "\"제인, 이게 무슨 소리예요? 빗속에 우체국엘 가다니! 이건 안 될 말이에요, 정말이지. 못된 아가씨 같으니, 어떻게 그런 짓을 할 수가 있어요? 내가 옆에서 챙겨 주지 못했다는 증거로군요.\""]], [["Jane very patiently assured her that she had not caught any cold.", "제인은 아주 참을성 있게, 감기 같은 건 들지 않았다고 잘라 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["do not tell me.", "내겐 그런 말 마요."], ["You really are a very sad girl, and do not know how to take care of yourself.—To the post-office indeed!", "정말이지 못 말릴 아가씨예요. 제 몸 하나 돌볼 줄을 몰라요."], ["Mrs. Weston, did you ever hear the like?", "우체국이라니, 세상에!"], ["You and I must positively exert our authority.”", "웨스턴 부인, 이런 얘기 들어 보신 적 있어요? 부인과 내가 단단히 우리 권위를 세워야겠어요.\""]], [["“My advice,” said Mrs.", "\"제 조언을 말씀드리자면,\""], ["Weston kindly and persuasively, “I certainly do feel tempted to give.", "웨스턴 부인이 다정하고 설득력 있게 말했다. \"정말이지 한마디 드리고 싶은 마음이 드네요."], ["Miss Fairfax, you must not run such risks.—Liable as you have been to severe colds, indeed you ought to be particularly careful, especially at this time of year.", "미스 페어팩스, 그렇게 위험을 무릅쓰면 안 돼요. 그동안 심한 감기에 잘 걸리셨으니, 정말이지 각별히 조심하셔야 해요."], ["The spring I always think requires more than common care.", "특히 이맘때는요. 봄철에는 늘 여느 때보다 더한 주의가 필요하다고 저는 생각한답니다."], ["Better wait an hour or two, or even half a day for your letters, than run the risk of bringing on your cough again.", "다시 기침이 도질 위험을 무릅쓰느니, 편지는 한두 시간, 아니 반나절을 기다리는 편이 나아요."], ["Now do not you feel that you had?", "미스 페어팩스도 그랬다고 느끼시지 않나요?"], ["Yes, I am sure you are much too reasonable.", "그럼요, 분명 미스 페어팩스는 너무나 분별 있는 분인걸요."], ["You look as if you would not do such a thing again.”", "다시는 그런 일을 하지 않으실 얼굴이세요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["she shall not do such a thing again,”", "다시는 그런 일을 못 하게 해야죠,\""], ["eagerly rejoined Mrs. Elton.", "엘턴 부인이 신이 나서 받았다."], ["“We will not allow her to do such a thing again:”—and nodding significantly—“there must be some arrangement made, there must indeed.", "\"다시는 그런 짓을 하도록 두지 않을 거예요.\" 그러고는 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕이며 — \"무슨 조처를 마련해야 해요, 정말이지요."], ["I shall speak to Mr. E.", "제가 미스터 E.에게 말해 두겠어요."], ["The man who fetches our letters every morning (one of our men, I forget his name) shall inquire for yours too and bring them to you.", "우리 편지를 아침마다 받아 오는 사람이 (우리 집 하인 하나인데, 이름이 기억이 안 나네요) 미스 페어팩스 편지도 함께 물어다가 가져다 드리도록 할게요."], ["That will obviate all difficulties you know; and from us I really think, my dear Jane, you can have no scruple to accept such an accommodation.”", "그러면 모든 곤란이 사라지잖아요. 그리고 우리한테서라면, 정말이지, 제인, 이런 편의쯤 받는 데 거리낄 게 하나도 없으리라고 생각해요.\""]], [["“You are extremely kind,”", "\"정말 친절하시네요.\" 제인이 말했다."], ["said Jane; “but I cannot give up my early walk.", "\"하지만 저는 이른 아침 산책을 포기할 수가 없어요. 되도록 바깥에 많이 나가라는 권고를 받았거든요."], ["I am advised to be out of doors as much as I can, I must walk somewhere, and the post-office is an object; and upon my word, I have scarcely ever had a bad morning before.”", "어디든 걸어야 하는데, 우체국이 그 목적지가 되는 거예요. 그리고 정말이지, 지금까지 궂은 아침을 겪은 적이 거의 없답니다.\""]], [["“My dear Jane, say no more about it.", "\"제인, 그 얘긴 더 마요."], ["The thing is determined, that is (laughing affectedly) as far as I can presume to determine any thing without the concurrence of my lord and master.", "이미 정해진 일이에요. 그러니까 (어색하게 웃으며) 제 주인이신 분의 동의 없이 제가 감히 정할 수 있는 한도 안에서는 말이죠."], ["You know, Mrs. Weston, you and I must be cautious how we express ourselves.", "웨스턴 부인도 아시잖아요, 부인과 나는 말을 조심해서 해야 한다는 걸요."], ["But I do flatter myself, my dear Jane, that my influence is not entirely worn out.", "그래도 제인, 제 영향력이 아주 바닥난 건 아니라고 자부한답니다."], ["If I meet with no insuperable difficulties therefore, consider that point as settled.”", "그러니 넘기 어려운 난관만 없다면, 그 일은 정해진 걸로 여겨 줘요.\""]], [["“Excuse me,”", "\"죄송하지만,\""], ["said Jane earnestly, “I cannot by any means consent to such an arrangement, so needlessly troublesome to your servant.", "제인이 진지하게 말했다. \"저는 그런 조처에 도무지 동의할 수가 없어요."], ["If the errand were not a pleasure to me, it could be done, as it always is when I am not here, by my grandmama’s.”", "댁의 하인을 공연히 그렇게 번거롭게 하다니요. 그 심부름이 제게 즐거운 일이 아니라면, 제가 여기 없을 때 늘 그러듯 할머님 댁에서 시키면 될 일이에요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["my dear; but so much as Patty has to do!—And it is a kindness to employ our men.”", "제인, 하지만 패티가 할 일이 얼마나 많은데요! 게다가 우리 집 하인을 부리는 게 그 사람한테는 호의를 베푸는 셈이고요.\""]], [["Jane looked as if she did not mean to be conquered; but instead of answering, she began speaking again to Mr.", "제인은 좀처럼 굽힐 뜻이 없어 보였다."], ["John Knightley.", "그러나 대답하는 대신 다시 미스터 존 나이틀리에게 말을 걸기 시작했다."]], [["“The post-office is a wonderful establishment!”", "\"우체국이란 참 놀라운 기관이에요!\""], ["said she.—“The regularity and despatch of it!", "그녀가 말했다. \"그 규칙성과 신속함이라니!"], ["If one thinks of all that it has to do, and all that it does so well, it is really astonishing!”", "그곳이 해야 하는 그 모든 일을, 그것도 그토록 잘 해내는 걸 생각하면, 정말이지 놀랍지 않나요!\""]], [["“It is certainly very well regulated.”", "\"확실히 아주 잘 굴러가지요.\""]], [["“So seldom that any negligence or blunder appears!", "\"실수나 차질이 나타나는 일이 어쩌면 그렇게 드문지요!"], ["So seldom that a letter, among the thousands that are constantly passing about the kingdom, is even carried wrong—and not one in a million, I suppose, actually lost!", "온 나라를 끊임없이 오가는 수천 통의 편지 가운데 잘못 배달되는 일조차 그토록 드물고 — 백만 통에 한 통도 실제로 분실되는 일이 없을 거예요!"], ["And when one considers the variety of hands, and of bad hands too, that are to be deciphered, it increases the wonder.”", "더구나 알아봐야 할 그 갖가지 필체, 그것도 형편없는 필체들까지 생각하면, 놀라움이 더해지죠.\""]], [["“The clerks grow expert from habit.—They must begin with some quickness of sight and hand, and exercise improves them.", "\"직원들이 손에 익어 능숙해지는 거지요. 처음부터 눈과 손이 어느 정도 빨라야 하고, 그게 훈련으로 나아지는 거고요."], ["If you want any farther explanation,”", "더 자세한 설명을 원하신다면,\""], ["continued he, smiling, “they are paid for it.", "그가 미소 지으며 말을 이었다. \"그들은 그 일에 대한 보수를 받습니다."], ["That is the key to a great deal of capacity.", "그게 그 많은 재간의 비결이지요."], ["The public pays and must be served well.”", "사람들이 값을 치르니, 마땅히 잘 시중받아야 하는 거고요.\""]], [["The varieties of handwriting were farther talked of, and the usual observations made.", "필체의 갖가지에 관한 이야기가 좀 더 이어졌고, 으레 하는 소견들이 오갔다."]], [["“I have heard it asserted,”", "\"이런 주장을 들은 적이 있습니다.\" 존 나이틀리가 말했다."], ["said John Knightley, “that the same sort of handwriting often prevails in a family; and where the same master teaches, it is natural enough.", "\"한 집안에서는 비슷한 필체가 흔히 두드러진다고요. 같은 선생이 가르치는 데서야 충분히 자연스러운 일이지요."], ["But for that reason, I should imagine the likeness must be chiefly confined to the females, for boys have very little teaching after an early age, and scramble into any hand they can get.", "하지만 바로 그 이유로, 그 닮음은 주로 여자들에게 국한되리라고 봅니다. 사내아이들은 어린 나이가 지나면 가르침을 거의 받지 못하고, 손에 잡히는 대로 아무 필체나 익히게 되니까요."], ["Isabella and Emma, I think, do write very much alike.", "이저벨라와 엠마는 글씨를 아주 비슷하게 쓰는 것 같습니다."], ["I have not always known their writing apart.”", "저도 늘 둘의 글씨를 구별하지는 못했거든요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said his brother hesitatingly, “there is a likeness.", "그의 형이 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"닮은 데가 있지."], ["I know what you mean—but Emma’s hand is the strongest.”", "무슨 뜻인지는 알겠네 — 하지만 엠마 글씨가 더 힘이 있네.\""]], [["“Isabella and Emma both write beautifully,” said Mr.", "\"이저벨라와 엠마는 둘 다 글씨가 아주 곱지요.\""], ["Woodhouse; “and always did.", "미스터 우드하우스가 말했다. \"예전부터 늘 그랬소."], ["And so does poor Mrs.", "그리고 가엾은 웨스턴 부인도 그렇고.\""], ["Weston”—with half a sigh and half a smile at her.", "그러면서 그녀를 향해 절반은 한숨, 절반은 미소를 지었다."]], [["“I never saw any gentleman’s handwriting”—Emma began, looking also at Mrs.", "\"저는 어떤 신사분의 글씨도 본 적이 없는데 —\" 엠마가 운을 떼며 웨스턴 부인 쪽을 보았으나, 웨스턴 부인이 다른 누군가에게 귀를 기울이고 있는 것을 알아채고는 말을 멈췄다."], ["Weston; but stopped, on perceiving that Mrs.", "그 멈춤이 그녀에게 생각할 틈을 주었다. \"자, 그분을 어떻게 끌어들이지?"], ["Weston was attending to some one else—and the pause gave her time to reflect, “Now, how am I going to introduce him?—Am I unequal to speaking his name at once before all these people?", "이 사람들 다 앞에서 곧장 그분 이름을 입에 올리는 게 그렇게 어려운 일일까? 굳이 빙 둘러 말해야 하나?"], ["Is it necessary for me to use any roundabout phrase?—Your Yorkshire friend—your correspondent in Yorkshire;—that would be the way, I suppose, if I were very bad.—No, I can pronounce his name without the smallest distress.", "당신의 요크셔 친구분 — 요크셔에 있는 당신의 서신 상대 — 내가 아주 형편없다면 그렇게 둘러대겠지. 아니야, 나는 그분 이름을 조금도 거북하지 않게 부를 수 있어."], ["I certainly get better and better.—Now for it.”", "분명 점점 나아지고 있어. 자, 해 보자.\""]], [["Mrs. Weston was disengaged and Emma began again—“Mr.", "웨스턴 부인의 귀가 비자, 엠마는 다시 말을 꺼냈다."], ["Frank Churchill writes one of the best gentleman’s hands I ever saw.”", "\"미스터 프랭크 처칠은 제가 본 신사분 글씨 가운데 가장 훌륭한 글씨를 쓰는 분이에요.\""]], [["“I do not admire it,”", "\"나는 그 글씨가 감탄스럽지 않던걸.\""], ["said Mr. Knightley.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말했다."], ["“It is too small—wants strength.", "\"너무 작아 — 힘이 없어."], ["It is like a woman’s writing.”", "여자 글씨 같단 말이야.\""]], [["This was not submitted to by either lady.", "이 말은 두 부인 어느 쪽도 받아들이지 않았다."], ["They vindicated him against the base aspersion.", "두 사람은 그 부당한 험담에 맞서 그를 변호했다."], ["“No, it by no means wanted strength—it was not a large hand, but very clear and certainly strong.", "\"아니에요, 결코 힘이 없지 않았어요 — 큰 글씨는 아니었지만 아주 또렷하고 분명 힘이 있었는걸요."], ["Had not Mrs. Weston any letter about her to produce?”", "웨스턴 부인이 마침 그분 편지를 한 통이라도 가지고 계시지 않나요?\""], ["No, she had heard from him very lately, but having answered the letter, had put it away.", "아니, 최근에 그에게서 편지를 받기는 했으나, 답장을 쓰고 나서 치워 두었다고 했다."]], [["“If we were in the other room,”", "\"우리가 저쪽 방에 있다면,\""], ["said Emma, “if I had my writing-desk, I am sure I could produce a specimen.", "엠마가 말했다. \"제 글쓰기 책상이 있다면, 분명 견본 하나를 보여 드릴 수 있을 텐데요."], ["I have a note of his.—Do not you remember, Mrs.", "그분 쪽지를 한 장 가지고 있거든요. 웨스턴 부인, 기억 안 나세요?"], ["Weston, employing him to write for you one day?”", "언젠가 그분한테 부인 대신 글을 써 달라고 부탁하셨던 일요.\""]], [["“He chose to say he was employed”—", "\"그이가 자기더러 부탁받았다고 우긴 거였죠 —\""]], [["“Well, well, I have that note; and can shew it after dinner to convince Mr.", "\"아무튼, 아무튼, 제게 그 쪽지가 있어요."], ["Knightley.”", "만찬 후에 보여 드려서 미스터 나이틀리를 납득시킬 수 있어요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["when a gallant young man, like Mr.", "미스터 프랭크 처칠 같은 멋진 젊은이가,\""], ["Frank Churchill,” said Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."], ["Knightley dryly, “writes to a fair lady like Miss Woodhouse, he will, of course, put forth his best.”", "\"미스 우드하우스 같은 아리따운 아가씨에게 편지를 쓸 때야, 당연히 제 솜씨를 한껏 발휘하겠지.\""]], [["Dinner was on table.—Mrs.", "만찬상이 차려졌다."], ["Elton, before she could be spoken to, was ready; and before Mr. Woodhouse had reached her with his request to be allowed to hand her into the dining-parlour, was saying—", "엘턴 부인은 누가 말을 건네기도 전에 채비를 마쳤고, 미스터 우드하우스가 그녀를 식당으로 모시게 해 달라는 청을 가지고 다가가기도 전에 이렇게 말하고 있었다."]], [["“Must I go first?", "\"제가 먼저 가야 하나요?"], ["I really am ashamed of always leading the way.”", "늘 앞장서야 하다니 정말 민망하네요.\""]], [["Jane’s solicitude about fetching her own letters had not escaped Emma.", "자기 편지를 직접 받아 오는 일에 제인이 그토록 애를 쓰던 것을 엠마는 놓치지 않았다."], ["She had heard and seen it all; and felt some curiosity to know whether the wet walk of this morning had produced any.", "그 모든 것을 듣고 보았으니, 오늘 아침의 그 비 맞은 산책이 무언가 결실을 보았을지 다소 궁금했다. 그녀는 결실이 있었으리라 짐작했다."], ["She suspected that it had; that it would not have been so resolutely encountered but in full expectation of hearing from some one very dear, and that it had not been in vain.", "누군가 아주 소중한 사람에게서 소식을 들으리라는 기대가 가득하지 않고서야 그렇게 단단히 마음먹고 나설 리 없었을 테고, 그것이 헛수고는 아니었으리라고 보았다."], ["She thought there was an air of greater happiness than usual—a glow both of complexion and spirits.", "평소보다 더 행복해 보이는 기색이 있다고, 얼굴빛에도 기분에도 윤기가 돈다고 여겼다."]], [["She could have made an inquiry or two, as to the expedition and the expense of the Irish mails;—it was at her tongue’s end—but she abstained.", "엠마는 한두 마디쯤 물어볼 수도 있었다 — 아일랜드 우편의 발송과 비용에 관해서 — 그 말이 혀끝까지 나왔다 — 그러나 참았다."], ["She was quite determined not to utter a word that should hurt Jane Fairfax’s feelings; and they followed the other ladies out of the room, arm in arm, with an appearance of good-will highly becoming to the beauty and grace of each.", "제인 페어팩스의 마음을 다치게 할 말은 한마디도 입에 올리지 않겠다고 단단히 작정한 것이다. 그리하여 두 사람은 다른 부인들을 따라 팔짱을 끼고 방을 나섰으니, 각자의 미모와 우아함에 더없이 어울리는 다정한 모습이었다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter XVII", "# 제2권 17장"]], [["When the ladies returned to the drawing-room after dinner, Emma found it hardly possible to prevent their making two distinct parties;—with so much perseverance in judging and behaving ill did Mrs.", "저녁 식사가 끝나고 부인들이 응접실로 돌아왔을 때, 엠마는 그들이 두 무리로 또렷이 갈라지는 것을 막기란 거의 불가능하다는 것을 알았다. 엘턴 부인은 그토록 고집스럽게 분별없이 판단하고 처신하며, 제인 페어팩스를 독차지하고 엠마 자신은 푸대접했던 것이다."], ["Elton engross Jane Fairfax and slight herself. She and Mrs.", "엠마와 웨스턴 부인은 거의 언제나 둘이서만 이야기를 나누거나 둘이서만 잠자코 있을 수밖에 없었다."], ["Weston were obliged to be almost always either talking together or silent together.", "엘턴 부인이 다른 선택의 여지를 주지 않았다."], ["Mrs. Elton left them no choice.", "제인이 잠깐 그녀를 막아 보아도, 그녀는 곧 다시 시작했다."], ["If Jane repressed her for a little time, she soon began again; and though much that passed between them was in a half-whisper, especially on Mrs.", "두 사람 사이에 오가는 말은 많은 부분이, 특히 엘턴 부인 쪽이, 반쯤 속삭임이었으나, 그 주된 화제를 모를 수가 없었다. 우체국 — 감기 드는 일 — 편지 찾아오기 — 그리고 우정이 한참 동안 거론되었다."], ["Elton’s side, there was no avoiding a knowledge of their principal subjects: The post-office—catching cold—fetching letters—and friendship, were long under discussion; and to them succeeded one, which must be at least equally unpleasant to Jane—inquiries whether she had yet heard of any situation likely to suit her, and professions of Mrs. Elton’s meditated activity.", "그러고 나서 뒤를 이은 것은, 제인에게 적어도 그만큼은 불쾌할 것이 분명한 화제였으니 — 그녀에게 맞을 만한 일자리를 혹 들어 본 적이 있느냐는 물음과, 엘턴 부인이 골똘히 나서 보겠다는 다짐이었다."]], [["“Here is April come!”", "\"벌써 사월이 됐네요!\""], ["said she, “I get quite anxious about you.", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신 일로 제가 무척 조바심이 난다니까요."], ["June will soon be here.”", "유월도 금세 닥칠 텐데요.\""]], [["“But I have never fixed on June or any other month—merely looked forward to the summer in general.”", "\"하지만 저는 유월이라거나 다른 어느 달이라고 정해 둔 적이 없어요 — 그저 여름쯤이라고 어림했을 뿐이지요.\""]], [["“But have you really heard of nothing?”", "\"하지만 정말로 아무 소식도 못 들으셨어요?\""]], [["“I have not even made any inquiry; I do not wish to make any yet.”", "\"저는 알아보지도 않았는걸요. 아직은 알아보고 싶지 않아요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["my dear, we cannot begin too early; you are not aware of the difficulty of procuring exactly the desirable thing.”", "이봐요, 아무리 일찍 시작해도 이르지 않다니까요. 꼭 들어맞는 자리를 구하기가 얼마나 어려운지 당신은 모르시는 거예요.\""]], [["“I not aware!”", "\"제가 모른다고요!\""], ["said Jane, shaking her head; “dear Mrs.", "제인이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다."], ["Elton, who can have thought of it as I have done?”", "\"엘턴 부인, 저만큼 그 일을 곱씹어 본 사람이 또 어디 있겠어요?\""]], [["“But you have not seen so much of the world as I have.", "\"하지만 당신은 저만큼 세상을 두루 보지 못했잖아요."], ["You do not know how many candidates there always are for the first situations.", "제일 좋은 자리마다 늘 지원자가 얼마나 몰리는지 모르실 거예요. 메이플 그로브 인근에서 저는 그런 일을 숱하게 봤거든요."], ["I saw a vast deal of that in the neighbourhood round Maple Grove. A cousin of Mr.", "미스터 서클링의 사촌인 미시즈 브래그한테는 그야말로 끝도 없이 지원이 들어왔어요."], ["Suckling, Mrs. Bragge, had such an infinity of applications; every body was anxious to be in her family, for she moves in the first circle.", "다들 그 댁에 들어가고 싶어 안달이었죠."], ["Wax-candles in the schoolroom!", "일류 사교계에서 노는 분이니까요."], ["You may imagine how desirable!", "교실에 밀랍 양초를 켜 둘 정도라니까요!"], ["Of all houses in the kingdom Mrs.", "얼마나 탐나는 자리인지 짐작이 가시죠!"], ["Bragge’s is the one I would most wish to see you in.”", "이 나라의 어느 집보다도, 미시즈 브래그 댁에 당신을 들여보내고 싶은 게 제 마음이에요.\""]], [["“Colonel and Mrs. Campbell are to be in town again by midsummer,”", "\"캠벨 대령 내외분이 한여름까지는 다시 런던으로 오실 거예요.\""], ["said Jane.", "제인이 말했다."], ["“I must spend some time with them; I am sure they will want it;—afterwards I may probably be glad to dispose of myself.", "\"저는 그분들과 한동안 지내야 해요. 그분들도 분명 그러길 바라실 거고요."], ["But I would not wish you to take the trouble of making any inquiries at present.”", "그다음에야 아마 기꺼이 제 앞가림을 하게 되겠지요. 하지만 지금은 부인께서 알아보시는 수고를 하지 않으셨으면 해요.\""]], [["“Trouble!", "\"수고라니요!"], ["aye, I know your scruples.", "아아, 당신의 그 조심성을 제가 알지요."], ["You are afraid of giving me trouble; but I assure you, my dear Jane, the Campbells can hardly be more interested about you than I am.", "제게 폐가 될까 봐 걱정하시는 거잖아요. 하지만 이봐요, 제인, 캠벨 댁이라 해도 저만큼 당신 일에 마음을 쓰지는 못할 거예요."], ["I shall write to Mrs. Partridge in a day or two, and shall give her a strict charge to be on the look-out for any thing eligible.”", "하루 이틀 안에 미시즈 파트리지한테 편지를 써서, 합당한 자리가 있으면 빠짐없이 알아봐 달라고 단단히 일러둘 작정이에요.\""]], [["“Thank you, but I would rather you did not mention the subject to her; till the time draws nearer, I do not wish to be giving any body trouble.”", "\"고맙습니다만, 그분께는 그 이야기를 꺼내지 않으셨으면 해요. 때가 좀 더 다가오기 전까지는, 누구에게도 폐를 끼치고 싶지 않거든요.\""]], [["“But, my dear child, the time is drawing near; here is April, and June, or say even July, is very near, with such business to accomplish before us. Your inexperience really amuses me! A situation such as you deserve, and your friends would require for you, is no everyday occurrence, is not obtained at a moment’s notice; indeed, indeed, we must begin inquiring directly.”", "\"하지만 이봐요, 때가 다가오고 있다니까요. 벌써 사월이고, 유월이, 아니 칠월이라 쳐도, 코앞이에요. 그전에 해치워야 할 일이 이렇게나 많은데요. 당신의 그 미숙함이 정말 우습기까지 하네요! 당신에게 걸맞고, 당신 친구들이 당신을 위해 요구할 만한 그런 자리는 흔하디흔한 게 아니에요. 당장 부탁한다고 얻어지는 게 아니라고요. 정말이지, 정말이지, 우리는 곧장 알아보기 시작해야 해요.\""]], [["“Excuse me, ma’am, but this is by no means my intention; I make no inquiry myself, and should be sorry to have any made by my friends.", "\"미안하지만요, 부인, 그건 결코 제 뜻이 아니에요. 저는 스스로도 알아보지 않을 거고, 제 친구들이 알아봐 주는 것도 싫어요."], ["When I am quite determined as to the time, I am not at all afraid of being long unemployed.", "때를 확실히 정하고 나면, 오래도록 일자리가 없을까 봐 걱정하지는 않아요."], ["There are places in town, offices, where inquiry would soon produce something—Offices for the sale—not quite of human flesh—but of human intellect.”", "런던에는 알아보기만 하면 곧 무언가를 내놓을 곳들이, 사무소들이 있으니까요 — 거래소들 말이에요. 사람의 살을 — 그렇게까지는 아니지만 — 사람의 지력을 사고파는 곳 말이지요.\""]], [["“Oh! my dear, human flesh!", "\"어머!"], ["You quite shock me; if you mean a fling at the slave-trade, I assure you Mr.", "이봐요, 사람의 살이라뇨! 정말 깜짝 놀랐잖아요."], ["Suckling was always rather a friend to the abolition.”", "혹 노예무역을 꼬집으시는 거라면, 정말이지 미스터 서클링은 늘 폐지 쪽 편이셨답니다.\""]], [["“I did not mean, I was not thinking of the slave-trade,” replied Jane; “governess-trade, I assure you, was all that I had in view; widely different certainly as to the guilt of those who carry it on; but as to the greater misery of the victims, I do not know where it lies. But I only mean to say that there are advertising offices, and that by applying to them I should have no doubt of very soon meeting with something that would do.”", "\"그런 뜻이 아니었어요. 노예무역을 생각한 게 아니었어요.\" 제인이 대답했다. \"가정교사 거래를 두고 한 말이었어요, 정말로. 그 일을 영위하는 자들의 죄로 따지자면 분명 크게 다르겠지요. 하지만 그 희생자들의 더 큰 비참함으로 말하자면, 어느 쪽이 더하다고 저는 잘 모르겠어요. 다만 제가 하려던 말은, 그런 알선 사무소들이 있고, 거기에 부탁하면 머지않아 쓸 만한 자리를 만나게 되리란 건 의심하지 않는다는 거예요.\""]], [["“Something that would do!”", "\"쓸 만한 자리라니!\" 엘턴 부인이 되뇌었다."], ["repeated Mrs. Elton.", "\"그래요, 자기 자신을 그렇게 낮춰 보는 당신 생각에야 그쯤도 맞을지 모르죠."], ["“Aye, that may suit your humble ideas of yourself;—I know what a modest creature you are; but it will not satisfy your friends to have you taking up with any thing that may offer, any inferior, commonplace situation, in a family not moving in a certain circle, or able to command the elegancies of life.”", "당신이 얼마나 겸손한 사람인지 제가 알아요. 하지만 당신 친구들은, 당신이 나서는 대로 아무거나 떠맡는 것을, 어떤 사교계에도 끼지 못하고 삶의 우아함이라곤 누릴 줄 모르는 집안의 하찮고 흔해 빠진 자리를 떠맡는 것을 결코 두고 보지 못할 거예요.\""]], [["“You are very obliging; but as to all that, I am very indifferent; it would be no object to me to be with the rich; my mortifications, I think, would only be the greater; I should suffer more from comparison. A gentleman’s family is all that I should condition for.”", "\"무척 고마운 말씀이세요. 하지만 그 모든 것에 저는 정말 아무런 미련이 없어요. 부유한 집에 들어가는 게 제겐 별 의미가 없거든요. 오히려 굴욕만 더 커질 것 같아요. 견주는 데서 더 괴로울 테니까요. 신사 계급의 집안이면 그게 제가 내거는 조건의 전부예요.\""]], [["“I know you, I know you; you would take up with any thing; but I shall be a little more nice, and I am sure the good Campbells will be quite on my side; with your superior talents, you have a right to move in the first circle. Your musical knowledge alone would entitle you to name your own terms, have as many rooms as you like, and mix in the family as much as you chose;—that is—I do not know—if you knew the harp, you might do all that, I am very sure; but you sing as well as play;—yes, I really believe you might, even without the harp, stipulate for what you chose;—and you must and shall be delightfully, honourably and comfortably settled before the Campbells or I have any rest.”", "\"제가 당신을 알아요, 알지요. 당신이야 아무거나 떠맡으려 들 사람이죠. 하지만 저는 좀 더 까다로울 작정이고, 마음씨 고운 캠벨 댁도 분명 제 편을 들어 주실 거예요. 당신의 그 뛰어난 재능이라면, 일류 사교계에서 노닐 자격이 충분해요. 음악에 대한 식견 하나만으로도 당신은 조건을 직접 내걸 수 있고, 방을 원하는 만큼 쓸 수 있고, 그 집안과 마음껏 어울릴 수 있어요. 그러니까 — 잘은 모르지만 — 하프까지 다룰 줄 알았다면 그 모든 걸 할 수 있었을 거예요, 정말 그래요. 하지만 당신은 연주만큼이나 노래도 잘하니까요. 그래요, 정말이지 하프 없이도 원하는 조건을 내걸 수 있을 거라고 저는 믿어요. 그러니 캠벨 댁이나 제가 마음 편히 쉬기 전에, 당신은 더없이 즐겁고 명예롭고 편안한 자리에 꼭 자리 잡아야 하고, 또 그렇게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“You may well class the delight, the honour, and the comfort of such a situation together,”", "\"그런 자리의 즐거움과 명예와 편안함을 한데 묶어 말씀하시는 건 옳은 말씀이네요.\" 제인이 말했다."], ["said Jane, “they are pretty sure to be equal; however, I am very serious in not wishing any thing to be attempted at present for me.", "\"그 셋은 어김없이 똑같은 크기일 게 분명하니까요. 어쨌든 저는 지금 저를 위해 무언가가 시도되기를 바라지 않는다는 점에서 아주 진지해요."], ["I am exceedingly obliged to you, Mrs.", "엘턴 부인, 정말 고맙습니다."], ["Elton, I am obliged to any body who feels for me, but I am quite serious in wishing nothing to be done till the summer.", "저를 생각해 주시는 분이라면 누구에게든 고마워요. 하지만 여름이 되기 전까지는 아무 일도 하지 않으셨으면 한다는 점에서 저는 정말 진지해요."], ["For two or three months longer I shall remain where I am, and as I am.”", "앞으로 두세 달은 지금 있는 곳에서, 지금 이대로 머물 거예요.\""]], [["“And I am quite serious too, I assure you,”", "\"저도 정말 진지하다니까요.\" 엘턴 부인이 명랑하게 대답했다."], ["replied Mrs. Elton gaily, “in resolving to be always on the watch, and employing my friends to watch also, that nothing really unexceptionable may pass us.”", "\"조금도 나무랄 데 없는 자리가 우리를 지나쳐 가지 않도록, 늘 눈을 부릅뜨고, 또 제 친구들도 부려 눈을 부릅뜨게 할 작정이에요.\""]], [["In this style she ran on; never thoroughly stopped by any thing till Mr.", "이런 투로 그녀는 끝없이 떠들어 댔다. 미스터 우드하우스가 방으로 들어오기 전까지는 어떤 것도 그녀를 온전히 멈추게 하지 못했다."], ["Woodhouse came into the room; her vanity had then a change of object, and Emma heard her saying in the same half-whisper to Jane,", "그가 들어서자 그녀의 허영심은 대상을 바꾸었고, 엠마는 그녀가 여전히 반쯤 속삭이는 소리로 제인에게 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."]], [["“Here comes this dear old beau of mine, I protest!—Only think of his gallantry in coming away before the other men!—what a dear creature he is;—I assure you I like him excessively. I admire all that quaint, old-fashioned politeness; it is much more to my taste than modern ease; modern ease often disgusts me. But this good old Mr. Woodhouse, I wish you had heard his gallant speeches to me at dinner. Oh! I assure you I began to think my cara sposo would be absolutely jealous. I fancy I am rather a favourite; he took notice of my gown. How do you like it?—Selina’s choice—handsome, I think, but I do not know whether it is not over-trimmed; I have the greatest dislike to the idea of being over-trimmed—quite a horror of finery. I must put on a few ornaments now, because it is expected of me. A bride, you know, must appear like a bride, but my natural taste is all for simplicity; a simple style of dress is so infinitely preferable to finery. But I am quite in the minority, I believe; few people seem to value simplicity of dress,—show and finery are every thing. I have some notion of putting such a trimming as this to my white and silver poplin. Do you think it will look well?”", "\"보세요, 저의 사랑스러운 노신사께서 오시네요, 정말로요! 다른 남자분들보다 먼저 자리를 뜨고 오시다니, 저 정중한 기품 좀 보세요! 얼마나 사랑스러운 분인지! 정말이지 저는 저분이 더없이 좋아요. 저 예스럽고 고풍스러운 예의범절이 다 좋아요. 요즘의 그 격식 없음보다 제 취향에 훨씬 맞거든요. 요즘의 격식 없음은 종종 정나미가 떨어지더라고요. 하지만 이 마음씨 고운 노신사 미스터 우드하우스가 식사 자리에서 제게 건넨 정중한 인사들을 당신도 들었더라면! 어머! 정말이지 저는 우리 그이가 영락없이 질투하겠구나 싶을 정도였다니까요. 제가 꽤 마음에 드신 모양이에요. 제 드레스를 눈여겨보시더라고요. 어때요, 마음에 드세요? 셀리나가 골라 준 거예요. 곱긴 한데, 장식이 좀 과하지 않나 싶기도 해요. 저는 장식이 과한 건 정말 질색이거든요. 화려한 차림이라면 아주 끔찍해요. 지금은 장신구를 몇 개 달긴 해야 해요. 다들 그러길 기대하니까요. 신부라면, 아시다시피, 신부답게 보여야 하잖아요. 하지만 제 타고난 취향은 온통 소박함을 향해 있어요. 소박한 차림이 화려함보다 한없이 더 좋거든요. 하지만 제가 영 소수파인가 봐요. 옷차림의 소박함을 귀히 여기는 사람은 드문 것 같으니까요. 과시와 화려함이 전부인 세상이죠. 이 백은빛 포플린 드레스에도 이런 장식을 좀 달아 볼까 생각 중이에요. 잘 어울릴 것 같으세요?\""]], [["The whole party were but just reassembled in the drawing-room when Mr.", "일행이 응접실에 다시 막 모여들었을 때, 미스터 웨스턴이 그들 가운데로 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Weston made his appearance among them. He had returned to a late dinner, and walked to Hartfield as soon as it was over.", "그는 늦은 저녁 식사 시간에 맞춰 집으로 돌아왔다가, 식사가 끝나기 무섭게 하트필드까지 걸어온 것이었다."], ["He had been too much expected by the best judges, for surprize—but there was great joy. Mr.", "눈썰미 좋은 이들은 이미 그를 너무도 기대하던 터라 놀라움까지는 없었지만, 큰 기쁨이 일었다."], ["Woodhouse was almost as glad to see him now, as he would have been sorry to see him before.", "미스터 우드하우스는 전에 그를 보았더라면 서운해했을 그만큼이나, 지금 그를 보고는 기뻐했다. 오직 존 나이틀리만이 말없는 경악에 빠졌다."], ["John Knightley only was in mute astonishment.—That a man who might have spent his evening quietly at home after a day of business in London, should set off again, and walk half a mile to another man’s house, for the sake of being in mixed company till bed-time, of finishing his day in the efforts of civility and the noise of numbers, was a circumstance to strike him deeply.", "런던에서 하루 종일 일을 보고 나면 집에서 조용히 저녁을 보낼 수도 있었을 사람이, 다시 길을 나서 잠자리에 들 때까지 잡다한 무리 속에 끼어 있겠다고, 하루의 끝을 예의의 노고와 사람들의 소란 속에서 마무리하겠다고 남의 집까지 반 마일을 걸어왔다는 사실은, 그에게 깊은 충격이었다. 아침 여덟 시부터 줄곧 움직였으니 이제는 가만히 있을 수도 있었고, 오래 떠들었으니 입을 다물 수도 있었고, 한 번 아닌 여러 무리 속에 있었으니 혼자일 수도 있었을 그런 사람이!"], ["A man who had been in motion since eight o’clock in the morning, and might now have been still, who had been long talking, and might have been silent, who had been in more than one crowd, and might have been alone!—Such a man, to quit the tranquillity and independence of his own fireside, and on the evening of a cold sleety April day rush out again into the world!—Could he by a touch of his finger have instantly taken back his wife, there would have been a motive; but his coming would probably prolong rather than break up the party.", "그런 사람이 제 난롯가의 평온과 자유를 버리고, 차가운 진눈깨비 내리는 사월 저녁에 다시금 세상 속으로 뛰쳐나오다니! 손가락 하나만 까딱하면 당장 아내를 도로 데려갈 수 있었다면야 동기라도 있었을 테지만, 그가 온다고 해서 모임이 끝나기는커녕 오히려 길어질 판이었다."], ["John Knightley looked at him with amazement, then shrugged his shoulders, and said, “I could not have believed it even of him.”", "존 나이틀리는 놀란 눈으로 그를 보다가, 어깨를 으쓱하고는 말했다. \"이건 저 사람한테도 좀처럼 못 믿을 일인데.\""]], [["Mr. Weston meanwhile, perfectly unsuspicious of the indignation he was exciting, happy and cheerful as usual, and with all the right of being principal talker, which a day spent anywhere from home confers, was making himself agreeable among the rest; and having satisfied the inquiries of his wife as to his dinner, convincing her that none of all her careful directions to the servants had been forgotten, and spread abroad what public news he had heard, was proceeding to a family communication, which, though principally addressed to Mrs.", "한편 미스터 웨스턴은, 자신이 불러일으키고 있는 분개를 전혀 눈치채지 못한 채, 여느 때처럼 행복하고 쾌활했으며, 집을 떠나 어디서든 하루를 보내고 온 사람에게 주어지는 주된 화자 노릇을 온전히 누리며 좌중에서 사람들의 호감을 사고 있었다."], ["Weston, he had not the smallest doubt of being highly interesting to every body in the room.", "저녁 식사에 관한 아내의 물음에 답해 주어, 하인들에게 꼼꼼히 일러둔 분부가 하나도 빠짐없이 지켜졌음을 그녀에게 납득시키고, 들은 바깥소식을 두루 풀어놓은 다음, 그는 한 집안의 소식을 전하는 데로 나아갔는데, 그것은 주로 웨스턴 부인을 향한 것이었으나, 방 안의 모두에게도 대단히 흥미로우리라는 것을 그는 추호도 의심하지 않았다. 그는 그녀에게 편지 한 통을 건넸다."], ["He gave her a letter, it was from Frank, and to herself; he had met with it in his way, and had taken the liberty of opening it.", "프랭크가 보낸 것으로, 그녀 앞으로 온 편지였다. 그는 오는 길에 그것을 받아 들고는, 멋대로 뜯어본 것이었다."]], [["“Read it, read it,” said he, “it will give you pleasure; only a few lines—will not take you long; read it to Emma.”", "\"읽어 봐요, 읽어 봐.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신을 기쁘게 해 줄 테니. 몇 줄 안 돼요. 오래 안 걸려. 엠마한테도 읽어 줘요.\""]], [["The two ladies looked over it together; and he sat smiling and talking to them the whole time, in a voice a little subdued, but very audible to every body.", "두 부인이 함께 편지를 들여다보았고, 그동안 그는 내내 미소 지으며 그들에게 말을 건넸는데, 목소리는 조금 낮추었으나 누구에게나 또렷이 들렸다."]], [["“Well, he is coming, you see; good news, I think.", "\"자아, 보다시피 그 녀석이 온다는군. 좋은 소식이지, 내 생각엔."], ["Well, what do you say to it?—I always told you he would be here again soon, did not I?—Anne, my dear, did not I always tell you so, and you would not believe me?—In town next week, you see—at the latest, I dare say; for she is as impatient as the black gentleman when any thing is to be done; most likely they will be there to-morrow or Saturday.", "자, 어떻게 생각해요? 내가 늘 그러지 않았소, 그 녀석이 곧 다시 여기 올 거라고?"], ["As to her illness, all nothing of course.", "안 그랬소? 앤, 여보, 내가 늘 그렇게 말했는데, 당신은 안 믿으려 했잖소?"], ["But it is an excellent thing to have Frank among us again, so near as town.", "다음 주에 런던에 온다잖아요. 길어야 다음 주쯤이라는 거지."], ["They will stay a good while when they do come, and he will be half his time with us.", "그 부인으로 말하면, 무슨 일이든 해치워야 할 때면 마귀처럼 조바심을 내는 사람이니까. 십중팔구 내일이나 토요일엔 거기 와 있을 거요."], ["This is precisely what I wanted.", "부인의 병환이야, 뭐 당연히 아무것도 아니지. 어쨌든 프랭크가 다시 우리 곁에, 런던처럼 가까운 데 와 있게 된다니 더없이 좋은 일이야."], ["Well, pretty good news, is not it?", "일단 오면 한참 머물 테고, 그러면 그 녀석이 시간의 절반은 우리하고 보낼 테지. 이거야말로 내가 바라던 바로 그거요."], ["Have you finished it?", "자, 꽤 좋은 소식이지, 안 그래요?"], ["Has Emma read it all?", "다 읽었소? 엠마도 끝까지 읽었나?"], ["Put it up, put it up; we will have a good talk about it some other time, but it will not do now.", "넣어 둬요, 넣어 둬. 이 일은 언제 다시 한번 차근차근 이야기합시다."], ["I shall only just mention the circumstance to the others in a common way.”", "지금은 안 되겠어. 다른 사람들한테는 그저 지나가는 말로 슬쩍 언급만 하리다.\""]], [["Mrs. Weston was most comfortably pleased on the occasion.", "웨스턴 부인은 이 일로 더없이 흐뭇하고 기뻤다."], ["Her looks and words had nothing to restrain them.", "그녀의 표정도 말도 거리낄 것이 없었다."], ["She was happy, she knew she was happy, and knew she ought to be happy.", "그녀는 행복했고, 자신이 행복하다는 것을 알았으며, 자신이 마땅히 행복해야 한다는 것도 알았다."], ["Her congratulations were warm and open; but Emma could not speak so fluently.", "그녀의 축하는 따뜻하고 거침이 없었다. 그러나 엠마는 그처럼 술술 말할 수가 없었다."], ["She was a little occupied in weighing her own feelings, and trying to understand the degree of her agitation, which she rather thought was considerable.", "그녀는 제 감정을 가늠하고, 제 동요의 정도를 헤아려 보느라 조금 정신이 팔려 있었는데, 그 동요가 적지 않다고 그녀는 생각하는 편이었다."]], [["Mr.", "그러나 미스터 웨스턴은, 무언가를 찬찬히 살피기에는 너무 들떠 있었고, 남이 말하기를 바라기에는 너무 수다스러웠던지라, 그녀가 한 그만한 말에도 흡족해하며, 곧 자리를 옮겨 나머지 벗들도 기쁘게 해 주려 했다."], ["Weston, however, too eager to be very observant, too communicative to want others to talk, was very well satisfied with what she did say, and soon moved away to make the rest of his friends happy by a partial communication of what the whole room must have overheard already.", "방 안 모두가 이미 엿들었을 것이 분명한 그 소식을, 일부나마 들려주는 것으로 말이다."]], [["It was well that he took every body’s joy for granted, or he might not have thought either Mr. Woodhouse or Mr.", "그가 모두의 기쁨을 당연한 것으로 여긴 것은 다행이었다."], ["Knightley particularly delighted. They were the first entitled, after Mrs.", "그렇지 않았다면 그는 미스터 우드하우스도 미스터 나이틀리도 딱히 기뻐하지 않는다고 여겼을 테니까."], ["Weston and Emma, to be made happy;—from them he would have proceeded to Miss Fairfax, but she was so deep in conversation with John Knightley, that it would have been too positive an interruption; and finding himself close to Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인과 엠마 다음으로, 기쁜 소식을 들을 자격이 첫째가는 이들이 바로 그 두 사람이었다. 그들에게서 미스 페어팩스에게로 옮겨 가려 했으나, 그녀가 존 나이틀리와 너무도 깊은 대화에 빠져 있어 끼어들기에는 지나치게 노골적인 방해가 될 듯했다."], ["Elton, and her attention disengaged, he necessarily began on the subject with her.", "그러다 자신이 엘턴 부인 가까이 있고 그녀의 관심도 마침 비어 있음을 알고는, 그는 자연히 그 화제를 그녀와 더불어 꺼내기 시작했다."]], [["# Volume II — Chapter XVIII", "# 제2권 18장"]], [["“I hope I shall soon have the pleasure of introducing my son to you,”", "\"곧 제 아들을 소개해 드리는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있기를 바랍니다.\""], ["said Mr. Weston.", "미스터 웨스턴이 말했다."]], [["Mrs. Elton, very willing to suppose a particular compliment intended her by such a hope, smiled most graciously.", "엘턴 부인은 그러한 바람에 자기를 향한 각별한 찬사가 담겨 있다고 기꺼이 여기며, 더없이 우아하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“You have heard of a certain Frank Churchill, I presume,”", "\"프랭크 처칠이라는 사람 이야기는 들으셨겠지요.\""], ["he continued—“and know him to be my son, though he does not bear my name.”", "그가 말을 이었다. \"제 성을 쓰지는 않지만 제 아들이라는 것도 아실 테고요.\""]], [["“Oh! yes, and I shall be very happy in his acquaintance.", "\"아, 그럼요."], ["I am sure Mr.", "그분과 알고 지내게 되면 정말 기쁘겠어요."], ["Elton will lose no time in calling on him; and we shall both have great pleasure in seeing him at the Vicarage.”", "미스터 엘턴도 분명 지체 없이 찾아가 인사할 거예요. 그리고 우리 둘 다 그분을 목사관에서 뵙게 되면 무척 기쁘겠지요.\""]], [["“You are very obliging.—Frank will be extremely happy, I am sure.— He is to be in town next week, if not sooner.", "\"참으로 친절하시군요. 프랭크도 분명 더없이 기뻐할 겁니다."], ["We have notice of it in a letter to-day.", "그 애가 다음 주, 어쩌면 그보다 더 일찍 런던에 옵니다. 오늘 온 편지에서 그 소식을 들었어요."], ["I met the letters in my way this morning, and seeing my son’s hand, presumed to open it—though it was not directed to me—it was to Mrs.", "오늘 아침에 오는 길에 편지를 받았는데, 아들 글씨가 보이기에 외람되이 뜯어 보았지요. 제게 온 게 아니라 웨스턴 부인에게 온 것이었는데도요."], ["Weston. She is his principal correspondent, I assure you.", "그 애의 주된 편지 상대는 아내랍니다, 정말이지."], ["I hardly ever get a letter.”", "저는 편지를 받는 일이 거의 없어요.\""]], [["“And so you absolutely opened what was directed to her!", "\"그래서 부인께 온 편지를 정말로 뜯어 보셨다는 거예요! 어머나, 미스터 웨스턴!(꾸민 듯이 웃으며) 그건 두고 볼 수 없는 일인데요."], ["Oh! Mr.", "정말이지 무척이나 위험한 선례라고요!"], ["Weston—(laughing affectedly) I must protest against that.—A most dangerous precedent indeed!—I beg you will not let your neighbours follow your example.—Upon my word, if this is what I am to expect, we married women must begin to exert ourselves!—Oh!", "부디 이웃들이 그 본을 따르게 두지는 마세요. 정말이지, 이게 제가 각오해야 할 일이라면, 우리 결혼한 여자들도 분발하기 시작해야겠어요!"], ["Mr. Weston, I could not have believed it of you!”", "어머나, 미스터 웨스턴, 그런 분이신 줄은 정말 몰랐어요!\""]], [["“Aye, we men are sad fellows.", "\"아, 우리 남자들이란 한심한 작자들이지요."], ["You must take care of yourself, Mrs.", "부인께서도 조심하셔야 합니다, 엘턴 부인."], ["Elton.—This letter tells us—it is a short letter—written in a hurry, merely to give us notice—it tells us that they are all coming up to town directly, on Mrs.", "이 편지에 적혀 있기로는—짧은 편지인데, 급히 쓴 거라 그저 소식만 알리는 거지요—처칠 부인 때문에 다들 곧장 런던으로 올라온다는군요."], ["Churchill’s account—she has not been well the whole winter, and thinks Enscombe too cold for her—so they are all to move southward without loss of time.”", "부인이 겨우내 몸이 좋지 않았는데, 엔스컴이 너무 춥다고 여기는 모양입니다. 그래서 다들 지체 없이 남쪽으로 옮긴다고요.\""]], [["“Indeed!—from Yorkshire, I think.", "\"어머나! 요크셔에서 말이지요."], ["Enscombe is in Yorkshire?”", "엔스컴이 요크셔에 있던가요?\""]], [["“Yes, they are about one hundred and ninety miles from London, a considerable journey.”", "\"그렇습니다. 런던에서 백구십 마일쯤 떨어진 곳이니, 상당한 여정이지요.\""]], [["“Yes, upon my word, very considerable.", "\"네, 정말이지 상당하네요."], ["Sixty-five miles farther than from Maple Grove to London.", "메이플 그로브에서 런던까지보다 육십오 마일이나 더 머니까요."], ["But what is distance, Mr. Weston, to people of large fortune?—You would be amazed to hear how my brother, Mr.", "하지만 미스터 웨스턴, 큰 재산을 가진 사람들에게 거리가 무슨 대수겠어요?"], ["Suckling, sometimes flies about. You will hardly believe me—but twice in one week he and Mr.", "우리 형부 미스터 서클링이 가끔 얼마나 날아다니듯 다니는지 들으시면 깜짝 놀라실걸요."], ["Bragge went to London and back again with four horses.”", "믿기 어려우시겠지만, 한 주에 두 번이나 형부와 미스터 브래그가 네 마리 말을 매고 런던을 다녀왔답니다.\""]], [["“The evil of the distance from Enscombe,”", "\"엔스컴이 머니까 안 좋은 점은,\""], ["said Mr. Weston, “is, that Mrs.", "미스터 웨스턴이 말했다."], ["Churchill, as we understand, has not been able to leave the sofa for a week together.", "\"우리가 듣기로 처칠 부인이 일주일 내내 소파에서 일어나지도 못한다는 겁니다."], ["In Frank’s last letter she complained, he said, of being too weak to get into her conservatory without having both his arm and his uncle’s!", "프랭크가 보낸 지난번 편지에 따르면, 부인이 너무 기운이 없어 조카와 외삼촌 양쪽 팔을 다 붙들지 않고서는 온실에도 못 들어가겠다고 푸념했다지 뭡니까!"], ["This, you know, speaks a great degree of weakness—but now she is so impatient to be in town, that she means to sleep only two nights on the road.—So Frank writes word.", "이 정도면, 아시겠지만, 어지간히 쇠약하다는 얘기지요. 그런데 이제는 런던에 오고 싶어 어찌나 안달인지, 길에서는 딱 두 밤만 자고 오겠답니다."], ["Certainly, delicate ladies have very extraordinary constitutions, Mrs.", "프랭크가 그렇게 써 보냈어요. 정말이지 연약한 부인들의 체질이란 참 별나기도 하지요, 엘턴 부인."], ["Elton. You must grant me that.”", "그건 인정해 주셔야 합니다.\""]], [["“No, indeed, I shall grant you nothing.", "\"아니요, 정말이지 저는 아무것도 인정 못 해요."], ["I always take the part of my own sex.", "저는 늘 우리 여자 편을 든답니다."], ["I do indeed.", "정말 그래요."], ["I give you notice—You will find me a formidable antagonist on that point.", "미리 말씀드리는데, 그 점에서는 제가 만만찮은 적수라는 걸 아시게 될 거예요."], ["I always stand up for women—and I assure you, if you knew how Selina feels with respect to sleeping at an inn, you would not wonder at Mrs.", "저는 언제나 여자들 편을 들거든요. 그리고 정말이지, 셀리나가 여관에서 자는 일을 두고 어떻게 여기는지 아신다면, 처칠 부인이 그걸 피하려고 믿기 힘든 노력을 한다 해도 놀라지 않으실 거예요."], ["Churchill’s making incredible exertions to avoid it.", "셀리나는 그게 정말 끔찍하다고 한답니다."], ["Selina says it is quite horror to her—and I believe I have caught a little of her nicety.", "그리고 저도 그 까다로움을 조금 물려받은 것 같아요."], ["She always travels with her own sheets; an excellent precaution.", "언니는 늘 자기 침대보를 챙겨 다니거든요. 아주 훌륭한 대비책이지요."], ["Does Mrs. Churchill do the same?”", "처칠 부인도 그렇게 하시나요?\""]], [["“Depend upon it, Mrs. Churchill does every thing that any other fine lady ever did.", "\"틀림없이 처칠 부인은 다른 어떤 귀부인이 한 일이든 빠짐없이 다 합니다."], ["Mrs. Churchill will not be second to any lady in the land for”—", "처칠 부인은 이 나라 어떤 부인에게도 뒤지지 않을 분이지요—\""]], [["Mrs. Elton eagerly interposed with,", "엘턴 부인이 다급하게 말을 가로챘다."]], [["“Oh! Mr.", "\"어머, 미스터 웨스턴, 오해는 마세요."], ["Weston, do not mistake me. Selina is no fine lady, I assure you.", "셀리나는 정말이지 귀부인 행세를 하는 사람이 아니랍니다."], ["Do not run away with such an idea.”", "그런 식으로 넘겨짚지 마세요.\""]], [["“Is not she?", "\"아니라고요?"], ["Then she is no rule for Mrs.", "그렇다면 처칠 부인의 기준은 못 되겠군요."], ["Churchill, who is as thorough a fine lady as any body ever beheld.”", "그 사람은 누가 봐도 더없이 철저한 귀부인 행세를 하는 분이니 말입니다.\""]], [["Mrs. Elton began to think she had been wrong in disclaiming so warmly.", "엘턴 부인은 자기가 그렇게 열을 올려 부인한 것이 잘못이었다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."], ["It was by no means her object to have it believed that her sister was not a fine lady; perhaps there was want of spirit in the pretence of it;—and she was considering in what way she had best retract, when Mr.", "언니가 귀부인답지 않은 사람이라고 믿게 만드는 것은 결코 그녀의 본뜻이 아니었다. 어쩌면 그렇게 둘러대는 데에는 패기가 모자랐는지도 몰랐다."], ["Weston went on.", "그래서 어떻게 하면 가장 잘 말을 거둬들일까 궁리하고 있는데, 미스터 웨스턴이 말을 이었다."]], [["“Mrs. Churchill is not much in my good graces, as you may suspect—but this is quite between ourselves.", "\"짐작하셨겠지만, 처칠 부인은 제 마음에 그리 드는 사람이 아닙니다—하지만 이건 우리끼리만 하는 얘기예요."], ["She is very fond of Frank, and therefore I would not speak ill of her.", "부인은 프랭크를 무척 아끼니, 저로서는 그 사람을 험담하고 싶지 않습니다."], ["Besides, she is out of health now; but that indeed, by her own account, she has always been.", "게다가 지금은 몸도 성치 않고요."], ["I would not say so to every body, Mrs.", "하기야 본인 말로는 늘 그래 왔다지만요."], ["Elton, but I have not much faith in Mrs. Churchill’s illness.”", "아무에게나 할 말은 아닙니다만, 엘턴 부인, 저는 처칠 부인의 병이라는 걸 그다지 믿지 않습니다.\""]], [["“If she is really ill, why not go to Bath, Mr.", "\"정말 아프시다면, 어째서 바스에 가지 않으시는 거지요, 미스터 웨스턴?"], ["Weston?—To Bath, or to Clifton?”", "바스나 클리프턴 같은 데로요.\""], ["“She has taken it into her head that Enscombe is too cold for her.", "\"엔스컴이 너무 춥다고 머릿속에 박혀 버린 거지요."], ["The fact is, I suppose, that she is tired of Enscombe.", "실은, 제 생각엔 엔스컴이 지겨워진 겁니다."], ["She has now been a longer time stationary there, than she ever was before, and she begins to want change.", "이번에는 전에 없이 그곳에 오래 붙박여 있었으니, 이제 변화가 그리워지기 시작한 거지요."], ["It is a retired place.", "외진 곳이거든요."], ["A fine place, but very retired.”", "좋은 곳이긴 하지만 무척 외집니다.\""]], [["“Aye—like Maple Grove, I dare say.", "\"네—메이플 그로브처럼요, 분명히요."], ["Nothing can stand more retired from the road than Maple Grove.", "메이플 그로브만큼 큰길에서 외떨어진 데도 없을 거예요."], ["Such an immense plantation all round it!", "사방을 둘러싼 그 어마어마한 숲이라니!"], ["You seem shut out from every thing—in the most complete retirement.—And Mrs.", "모든 것에서 차단된 듯한 느낌이에요—더없이 완벽한 은거지요."], ["Churchill probably has not health or spirits like Selina to enjoy that sort of seclusion.", "그리고 처칠 부인은 아마 셀리나 같은 건강이나 활기가 없어서 그런 식의 칩거를 즐기지 못하는 거겠지요."], ["Or, perhaps she may not have resources enough in herself to be qualified for a country life.", "아니면, 시골살이에 어울릴 만큼 자기 안에 즐길 거리가 충분치 않은지도 모르고요."], ["I always say a woman cannot have too many resources—and I feel very thankful that I have so many myself as to be quite independent of society.”", "저는 늘 말하지만, 여자는 즐길 거리가 아무리 많아도 지나치지 않거든요—그리고 저는 사교계에 전혀 기대지 않아도 될 만큼 제 안에 즐길 거리가 그렇게 많다는 게 정말 고맙답니다.\""]], [["“Frank was here in February for a fortnight.”", "\"프랭크가 이월에 두 주 동안 다녀갔습니다.\""]], [["“So I remember to have heard.", "\"네, 그 얘기는 들은 기억이 있어요."], ["He will find an addition to the society of Highbury when he comes again; that is, if I may presume to call myself an addition.", "그분이 다시 오시면 하이베리 사교계에 한 사람이 더 늘어 있는 걸 보게 되겠지요. 제가 감히 저 자신을 한 사람 더 늘었다고 칭할 수 있다면 말이에요."], ["But perhaps he may never have heard of there being such a creature in the world.”", "하지만 어쩌면 그분은 세상에 저 같은 사람이 있다는 것조차 들어 본 적 없을지도 모르겠네요.\""]], [["This was too loud a call for a compliment to be passed by, and Mr. Weston, with a very good grace, immediately exclaimed,", "이것은 찬사를 청하는 부름치고는 너무 노골적이어서 그냥 넘길 수가 없었으니, 미스터 웨스턴은 아주 점잖은 태도로 곧장 큰 소리로 답했다."]], [["“My dear madam!", "\"부인!"], ["Nobody but yourself could imagine such a thing possible.", "부인 말고는 누구도 그런 일이 가능하리라고 상상하지 못할 겁니다."], ["Not heard of you!—I believe Mrs.", "부인을 못 들어 봤다니요!"], ["Weston’s letters lately have been full of very little else than Mrs. Elton.”", "요즘 웨스턴 부인의 편지는 엘턴 부인 이야기 말고는 거의 다른 얘기가 없는 것 같던데요.\""]], [["He had done his duty and could return to his son.", "그는 할 도리를 다했으니 이제 다시 아들 이야기로 돌아갈 수 있었다."]], [["“When Frank left us,”", "\"프랭크가 우리를 떠날 때만 해도,\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["continued he, “it was quite uncertain when we might see him again, which makes this day’s news doubly welcome.", "\"그 애를 언제 다시 볼 수 있을지 도무지 알 수 없었어요. 그래서 오늘 소식이 갑절로 반가운 거지요."], ["It has been completely unexpected.", "정말이지 전혀 뜻밖이었습니다."], ["That is, I always had a strong persuasion he would be here again soon, I was sure something favourable would turn up—but nobody believed me.", "하기야, 저는 늘 그 애가 곧 다시 오리라고 강하게 믿었어요. 분명 무언가 좋은 일이 생기리라 확신했지요—그런데 아무도 제 말을 믿지 않았답니다."], ["He and Mrs. Weston were both dreadfully desponding.", "그 애도 웨스턴 부인도 둘 다 끔찍하게 풀이 죽어 있었어요."], ["‘How could he contrive to come?", "'그 애가 어떻게 올 방도를 만들겠어요?"], ["And how could it be supposed that his uncle and aunt would spare him again?’ and so forth—I always felt that something would happen in our favour; and so it has, you see.", "게다가 외삼촌 내외가 또 그 애를 보내 줄 리가 있겠어요?' 어쩌고 하면서요."], ["I have observed, Mrs.", "저는 늘 우리에게 유리한 무언가가 일어나리라 느꼈는데, 보세요, 정말 그렇게 됐지요."], ["Elton, in the course of my life, that if things are going untowardly one month, they are sure to mend the next.”", "엘턴 부인, 저는 살아오면서 이걸 깨달았답니다. 어느 달에 일이 어긋나게 돌아가면, 다음 달에는 반드시 나아진다는 것을요.\""]], [["“Very true, Mr.", "\"맞는 말씀이에요, 미스터 웨스턴, 정말 맞는 말씀이에요."], ["Weston, perfectly true.", "그건 바로 제가 구애받던 시절에 어떤 신사분께 늘 하던 말이거든요. 그분은 일이 영 뜻대로 풀리지 않으면, 자기 마음에 맞을 만큼 빠르게 진행되지 않으면 곧잘 절망에 빠져서는, 이런 식이라면 우리한테 결혼의 신이 샛노란 예복을 입혀 줄 때쯤이면 분명 오월은 되어 있을 거라고 부르짖곤 했답니다."], ["It is just what I used to say to a certain gentleman in company in the days of courtship, when, because things did not go quite right, did not proceed with all the rapidity which suited his feelings, he was apt to be in despair, and exclaim that he was sure at this rate it would be May before Hymen’s saffron robe would be put on for us.", "아유, 그 우울한 생각을 떨쳐 내고 더 밝은 쪽을 보게 해 드리느라 제가 얼마나 애를 썼는지! 마차—마차 일로도 실망스러운 일이 있었어요."], ["Oh! the pains I have been at to dispel those gloomy ideas and give him cheerfuller views!", "어느 날 아침, 그분이 아주 절망에 빠진 얼굴로 제게 찾아왔던 게 기억나네요.\""], ["The carriage—we had disappointments about the carriage;—one morning, I remember, he came to me quite in despair.”", "* 결혼의 신(Hymen): 그리스 신화의 혼인의 신. 샛노란 예복은 혼례를 상징한다."]], [["She was stopped by a slight fit of coughing, and Mr. Weston instantly seized the opportunity of going on.", "그녀가 살짝 기침을 하느라 말을 멈추자, 미스터 웨스턴이 그 틈을 놓치지 않고 즉시 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“You were mentioning May. May is the very month which Mrs.", "\"오월 말씀을 하셨지요."], ["Churchill is ordered, or has ordered herself, to spend in some warmer place than Enscombe—in short, to spend in London; so that we have the agreeable prospect of frequent visits from Frank the whole spring—precisely the season of the year which one should have chosen for it: days almost at the longest; weather genial and pleasant, always inviting one out, and never too hot for exercise.", "처칠 부인이 엔스컴보다 따뜻한 곳에서—요컨대 런던에서 지내라는 지시를 받았거나, 아니면 스스로 그러기로 한 달이 바로 오월입니다. 그러니 우리는 봄 내내 프랭크가 자주 찾아오리라는 흐뭇한 전망을 갖게 된 거지요—바로 그러기에 골라잡았음 직한 계절이에요."], ["When he was here before, we made the best of it; but there was a good deal of wet, damp, cheerless weather; there always is in February, you know, and we could not do half that we intended.", "해가 거의 가장 길고, 날씨도 온화하고 쾌적해서 늘 밖으로 나가고 싶어지고, 그렇다고 거닐기에 너무 덥지도 않으니까요. 그 애가 지난번 왔을 때는 그런대로 잘 보냈습니다만, 비가 오고 축축하고 음울한 날씨가 많았지요."], ["Now will be the time.", "아시다시피 이월에는 늘 그렇잖아요. 그래서 마음먹은 것의 절반도 못 했어요."], ["This will be complete enjoyment; and I do not know, Mrs.", "이제야말로 제때인 거지요. 이번에는 온전히 즐길 수 있을 겁니다."], ["Elton, whether the uncertainty of our meetings, the sort of constant expectation there will be of his coming in to-day or to-morrow, and at any hour, may not be more friendly to happiness than having him actually in the house.", "게다가 엘턴 부인, 저는 잘 모르겠어요. 그 애가 정말로 집에 와 있는 것보다, 만남이 불확실한 채로 오늘이나 내일, 그것도 아무 때고 그 애가 들어설지 모른다고 늘 기대하는 그런 마음이 행복에 더 좋은 것은 아닐까 하고요."], ["I think it is so.", "제 생각엔 그런 것 같아요."], ["I think it is the state of mind which gives most spirit and delight.", "바로 그런 마음 상태가 사람에게 가장 큰 활기와 기쁨을 주는 것 같거든요."], ["I hope you will be pleased with my son; but you must not expect a prodigy.", "부디 제 아들을 마음에 들어 하셨으면 합니다. 하지만 무슨 신동을 기대하시면 안 됩니다."], ["He is generally thought a fine young man, but do not expect a prodigy.", "흔히들 그 애를 훌륭한 청년이라고 하지만, 신동을 기대하시진 마세요."], ["Mrs. Weston’s partiality for him is very great, and, as you may suppose, most gratifying to me.", "웨스턴 부인이 그 애를 무척이나 편애하는데, 짐작하시겠지만 그게 제겐 더없이 흐뭇한 일이지요."], ["She thinks nobody equal to him.”", "아내는 그 애만 한 사람이 없다고 여긴답니다.\""]], [["“And I assure you, Mr. Weston, I have very little doubt that my opinion will be decidedly in his favour.", "\"그리고 분명히 말씀드리는데, 미스터 웨스턴, 제 평가도 분명 그분에게 호의적일 거라는 데 거의 의심이 없어요."], ["I have heard so much in praise of Mr.", "미스터 프랭크 처칠을 칭찬하는 말을 어찌나 많이 들었는지요."], ["Frank Churchill.—At the same time it is fair to observe, that I am one of those who always judge for themselves, and are by no means implicitly guided by others.", "그렇지만 한 가지 짚어 두는 게 공정하겠는데, 저는 늘 스스로 판단하는 사람이지, 결코 남의 말에 맹목적으로 끌려가는 사람이 아니거든요."], ["I give you notice that as I find your son, so I shall judge of him.—I am no flatterer.”", "미리 말씀드리지만, 저는 그분의 됨됨이를 직접 본 그대로 판단할 거예요. 저는 아첨하는 사람이 아니니까요.\""]], [["Mr. Weston was musing.", "미스터 웨스턴은 생각에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["“I hope,”", "\"제가,\""], ["said he presently, “I have not been severe upon poor Mrs.", "이윽고 그가 말했다. \"가엾은 처칠 부인을 너무 모질게 말한 건 아닌지 모르겠군요."], ["Churchill.", "정말로 아픈 거라면 부당한 소리를 한 게 미안할 노릇이지요."], ["If she is ill I should be sorry to do her injustice; but there are some traits in her character which make it difficult for me to speak of her with the forbearance I could wish.", "하지만 그 사람 성격에는 제가 바라는 만큼 너그럽게 말하기 어렵게 만드는 구석들이 있어요. 엘턴 부인, 제가 그 집안과 어떤 인연이 있는지, 또 제가 어떤 대접을 받았는지 모르시진 않을 겁니다."], ["You cannot be ignorant, Mrs.", "그런데 우리끼리 얘기지만, 그 모든 허물은 부인 탓이에요."], ["Elton, of my connexion with the family, nor of the treatment I have met with; and, between ourselves, the whole blame of it is to be laid to her.", "그녀가 부추긴 거지요. 그녀만 아니었으면 프랭크의 어머니가 그렇게 푸대접받는 일은 결코 없었을 겁니다."], ["She was the instigator.", "미스터 처칠도 자존심이 있지요."], ["Frank’s mother would never have been slighted as she was but for her. Mr.", "하지만 그 사람 자존심은 아내 것에 비하면 아무것도 아니에요."], ["Churchill has pride; but his pride is nothing to his wife’s: his is a quiet, indolent, gentlemanlike sort of pride that would harm nobody, and only make himself a little helpless and tiresome; but her pride is arrogance and insolence!", "그 사람 것은 누구도 해치지 않는, 그저 자기를 조금 무력하고 성가시게 만들 뿐인 조용하고 굼뜬 신사다운 자존심이지요. 하지만 그녀의 자존심은 오만이요 무례랍니다!"], ["And what inclines one less to bear, she has no fair pretence of family or blood.", "게다가 더더욱 참아 주기 어려운 건, 그녀에게는 내세울 만한 가문이나 혈통이 없다는 거예요. 미스터 처칠이 결혼할 때만 해도 그녀는 변변찮은 사람, 그저 한 신사의 딸일 뿐이었지요."], ["She was nobody when he married her, barely the daughter of a gentleman; but ever since her being turned into a Churchill she has out-Churchill’d them all in high and mighty claims: but in herself, I assure you, she is an upstart.”", "그런데 처칠 집안 사람이 된 뒤로는 거드름과 콧대를 내세우는 데 그 집안 누구보다 더 처칠다워졌답니다. 하지만 본디는, 정말이지, 벼락출세한 사람이에요.\""]], [["“Only think!", "\"세상에!"], ["well, that must be infinitely provoking!", "어머나, 그건 한없이 화나는 일이겠어요!"], ["I have quite a horror of upstarts.", "저는 벼락출세한 사람이라면 정말 질색이거든요."], ["Maple Grove has given me a thorough disgust to people of that sort; for there is a family in that neighbourhood who are such an annoyance to my brother and sister from the airs they give themselves!", "메이플 그로브 덕에 그런 부류라면 진절머리가 났답니다. 우리 동네에도 한 집안이 있는데, 어찌나 거드름을 피우는지 형부와 언니가 골치를 앓는다니까요!"], ["Your description of Mrs. Churchill made me think of them directly.", "처칠 부인 얘기를 들으니 당장 그 사람들이 떠오르네요."], ["People of the name of Tupman, very lately settled there, and encumbered with many low connexions, but giving themselves immense airs, and expecting to be on a footing with the old established families.", "텁먼이라는 사람들인데, 아주 최근에 그곳에 정착했고, 변변찮은 연줄을 잔뜩 짊어진 주제에 엄청나게 거드름을 피우면서, 오래전부터 자리 잡은 명문가들과 어깨를 나란히 하겠다고 기대한답니다. 웨스트 홀에 산 게 길어 봐야 일 년 반인데, 어떻게 재산을 모았는지는 아무도 몰라요."], ["A year and a half is the very utmost that they can have lived at West Hall; and how they got their fortune nobody knows.", "버밍엄에서 왔다는데, 아시잖아요, 미스터 웨스턴, 버밍엄이란 데가 뭐 그리 기대할 만한 곳은 못 되지요."], ["They came from Birmingham, which is not a place to promise much, you know, Mr.", "버밍엄이라고 하면 큰 기대를 안 하게 되니까요. 저는 늘 그 이름엔 무언가 불길한 데가 있다고 말한답니다."], ["Weston. One has not great hopes from Birmingham.", "그런데도 텁먼 집안에 대해 확실히 알려진 건 그 이상 없어요."], ["I always say there is something direful in the sound: but nothing more is positively known of the Tupmans, though a good many things I assure you are suspected; and yet by their manners they evidently think themselves equal even to my brother, Mr.", "정말이지 미심쩍은 게 한둘이 아니지만요. 그러면서도 그 행동거지를 보면 자기들이 형부 미스터 서클링과도 대등하다고 여기는 게 분명하다니까요."], ["Suckling, who happens to be one of their nearest neighbours.", "하필이면 형부가 그 사람들과 가장 가까운 이웃 가운데 하나거든요."], ["It is infinitely too bad. Mr.", "정말이지 너무 못 봐줄 일이에요."], ["Suckling, who has been eleven years a resident at Maple Grove, and whose father had it before him—I believe, at least—I am almost sure that old Mr. Suckling had completed the purchase before his death.”", "형부는 메이플 그로브에 십일 년이나 산 사람이고, 그 전에는 형부 아버님이 그곳을 갖고 계셨는데—적어도 제가 알기로는—거의 확실한데, 돌아가신 미스터 서클링 어른이 임종 전에 매입을 다 끝내셨거든요.\""]], [["They were interrupted.", "이때 두 사람의 대화가 끊겼다."], ["Tea was carrying round, and Mr. Weston, having said all that he wanted, soon took the opportunity of walking away.", "차가 돌고 있었고, 미스터 웨스턴은 하고 싶은 말을 다 했던 터라 곧 자리를 떠날 기회를 잡았다."]], [["After tea, Mr. and Mrs. Weston, and Mr. Elton sat down with Mr. Woodhouse to cards. The remaining five were left to their own powers, and Emma doubted their getting on very well; for Mr. Knightley seemed little disposed for conversation; Mrs. Elton was wanting notice, which nobody had inclination to pay, and she was herself in a worry of spirits which would have made her prefer being silent.", "차를 마신 뒤, 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인, 그리고 미스터 엘턴이 미스터 우드하우스와 함께 카드놀이 자리에 앉았다. 남은 다섯은 저희끼리 알아서 시간을 보내게 되었는데, 엠마는 그들이 그리 잘 어울릴 것 같지 않다고 미심쩍어했다. 미스터 나이틀리는 대화에 별 흥미가 없어 보였고, 엘턴 부인은 관심을 받고 싶어 했으나 누구도 기울일 마음이 없었으며, 엠마 자신은 차라리 입을 다물고 싶을 만큼 심란한 상태였던 것이다."]], [["Mr. John Knightley proved more talkative than his brother.", "미스터 존 나이틀리가 형보다 말이 많은 사람임이 드러났다."], ["He was to leave them early the next day; and he soon began with—", "그는 이튿날 아침 일찍 떠날 참이었기에, 곧 이렇게 말문을 열었다."]], [["“Well, Emma, I do not believe I have any thing more to say about the boys; but you have your sister’s letter, and every thing is down at full length there we may be sure. My charge would be much more concise than her’s, and probably not much in the same spirit; all that I have to recommend being comprised in, do not spoil them, and do not physic them.”", "\"자, 엠마, 아이들에 관해서는 더 할 말이 없는 것 같네. 하지만 처제한테는 언니 편지가 있으니, 거기에 빠짐없이 다 적혀 있을 게 틀림없어. 내가 당부할 거라면 언니 것보다 훨씬 간단할 테고, 아마 결도 그리 같지 않을 거야. 내가 권하고 싶은 건 다 합쳐서 이거뿐이거든. 버릇 망쳐 놓지 말고, 약 함부로 먹이지 말 것.\""]], [["“I rather hope to satisfy you both,”", "\"두 분 다 만족시켜 드릴 수 있으면 좋겠네요.\" 엠마가 말했다."], ["said Emma, “for I shall do all in my power to make them happy, which will be enough for Isabella; and happiness must preclude false indulgence and physic.”", "\"저는 힘닿는 데까지 아이들을 즐겁게 해 줄 작정이니, 그거면 언니한테는 충분할 거예요. 그리고 즐거우면 어설픈 응석받이질이나 약 먹이는 일은 끼어들 틈이 없을 테고요.\""]], [["“And if you find them troublesome, you must send them home again.”", "\"그리고 아이들이 성가시다 싶으면, 다시 집으로 돌려보내야 하네.\""]], [["“That is very likely.", "\"그럴 수도 있겠네요."], ["You think so, do not you?”", "그러실 거라고 생각하시는군요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“I hope I am aware that they may be too noisy for your father—or even may be some encumbrance to you, if your visiting engagements continue to increase as much as they have done lately.”", "\"아이들이 처제 아버님께 너무 시끄러울 수도 있고—아니면 처제한테도 짐이 될 수 있다는 걸 내가 모르진 않거든. 요즘처럼 처제 사교 약속이 계속 늘어난다면 말이야.\""]], [["“Increase!”", "\"늘어난다고요!\""]], [["“Certainly; you must be sensible that the last half-year has made a great difference in your way of life.”", "\"그럼. 지난 반년 동안 처제 생활 방식에 큰 변화가 생겼다는 건 처제도 느낄 텐데.\""]], [["“Difference!", "\"변화라뇨!"], ["No indeed I am not.”", "아니요, 정말이지 그렇지 않은데요.\""]], [["“There can be no doubt of your being much more engaged with company than you used to be.", "\"전보다 사람들과 훨씬 자주 어울리게 된 건 의심할 여지가 없지."], ["Witness this very time.", "바로 지금만 봐도 그래."], ["Here am I come down for only one day, and you are engaged with a dinner-party!—When did it happen before, or any thing like it?", "나는 겨우 하루 묵으러 내려온 건데, 처제는 만찬 약속에 매여 있잖아! 전에 언제 이런 일이, 아니 이 비슷한 일이라도 있었나?"], ["Your neighbourhood is increasing, and you mix more with it.", "동네에 사람이 늘어나니 처제도 더 어울리는 거지."], ["A little while ago, every letter to Isabella brought an account of fresh gaieties; dinners at Mr.", "얼마 전까지만 해도 언니한테 가는 편지마다 새로운 놀이 이야기가 실려 왔어."], ["Cole’s, or balls at the Crown.", "미스터 콜네 만찬이라든가, 크라운 여관의 무도회라든가 말이야."], ["The difference which Randalls, Randalls alone makes in your goings-on, is very great.”", "랜덜스가, 오직 랜덜스 하나가 처제 생활에 만들어 낸 차이가 얼마나 큰데.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래,\""], ["said his brother quickly, “it is Randalls that does it all.”", "그의 형이 얼른 말했다. \"다 랜덜스가 한 일이지.\""]], [["“Very well—and as Randalls, I suppose, is not likely to have less influence than heretofore, it strikes me as a possible thing, Emma, that Henry and John may be sometimes in the way.", "\"좋아—그런데 짐작건대 랜덜스가 앞으로도 지금보다 영향력이 줄어들 것 같지는 않으니, 엠마, 헨리와 존이 가끔 거치적거릴 수도 있겠다는 생각이 들어."], ["And if they are, I only beg you to send them home.”", "그렇거든, 그저 아이들을 집으로 돌려보내 주기만 하면 돼.\""]], [["“No,” cried Mr.", "\"아니,\""], ["Knightley, “that need not be the consequence.", "미스터 나이틀리가 외쳤다. \"그럴 필요까진 없네."], ["Let them be sent to Donwell.", "그 애들은 돈웰로 보내면 돼."], ["I shall certainly be at leisure.”", "나는 분명 한가할 테니까.\""]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"어머, 정말,\""], ["exclaimed Emma, “you amuse me!", "엠마가 큰 소리로 말했다. \"재미있으시네요!"], ["I should like to know how many of all my numerous engagements take place without your being of the party; and why I am to be supposed in danger of wanting leisure to attend to the little boys.", "제 그 숱한 약속들 가운데 형부가 함께하지 않은 자리가 도대체 몇이나 되는지 알고 싶어지네요. 그리고 어째서 제가 아이들 돌볼 짬을 못 낼 위험에 빠졌다고들 여기시는지도요."], ["These amazing engagements of mine—what have they been?", "그 어마어마하다는 제 약속이란 게 다 뭐였죠?"], ["Dining once with the Coles—and having a ball talked of, which never took place. I can understand you—(nodding at Mr.", "콜네에서 한 번 만찬을 들었고—무도회 얘기가 나왔다가 끝내 열리지도 않았고요."], ["John Knightley)—your good fortune in meeting with so many of your friends at once here, delights you too much to pass unnoticed. But you, (turning to Mr.", "형부 마음은 알겠어요.(미스터 존 나이틀리에게 고개를 끄덕이며) 한꺼번에 그 많은 친구분들을 여기서 만난 그 행운이 형부에겐 너무 기뻐서 그냥 넘기실 수가 없는 거죠."], ["Knightley,) who know how very, very seldom I am ever two hours from Hartfield, why you should foresee such a series of dissipation for me, I cannot imagine.", "하지만 미스터 나이틀리는,(그를 돌아보며) 제가 두 시간 넘게 하트필드를 비우는 일이 얼마나, 얼마나 드문지 잘 아시면서, 어째서 제게 그런 줄줄이 이어지는 흥청거림을 점치시는지 도무지 모르겠네요. 그리고 우리 귀여운 조카들로 말하자면, 이건 꼭 말해야겠어요."], ["And as to my dear little boys, I must say, that if Aunt Emma has not time for them, I do not think they would fare much better with Uncle Knightley, who is absent from home about five hours where she is absent one—and who, when he is at home, is either reading to himself or settling his accounts.”", "엠마 이모가 그 애들 돌볼 시간이 없다면, 나이틀리 큰아버지한테 맡긴들 그리 나을 것 같지는 않은데요. 큰아버지는 이모가 한 시간 집을 비울 때 다섯 시간쯤 집을 비우는 분이고—집에 있을 때조차 혼자 책을 읽거나 장부 정리나 하는 분이니까요.\""]], [["Mr. Knightley seemed to be trying not to smile; and succeeded without difficulty, upon Mrs.", "미스터 나이틀리는 웃지 않으려 애쓰는 듯했다."], ["Elton’s beginning to talk to him.", "그리고 엘턴 부인이 그에게 말을 걸기 시작하자, 어렵잖게 그러는 데 성공했다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter I", "# 제3권 1장"]], [["A very little quiet reflection was enough to satisfy Emma as to the nature of her agitation on hearing this news of Frank Churchill.", "아주 잠깐의 차분한 성찰만으로도 엠마는, 프랭크 처칠에 관한 이 소식을 듣고 자신이 동요한 까닭이 무엇인지 충분히 가늠할 수 있었다."], ["She was soon convinced that it was not for herself she was feeling at all apprehensive or embarrassed; it was for him.", "그녀는 곧, 자기 자신을 위해 불안하거나 거북함을 느끼는 것이 결코 아니라는 데 생각이 미쳤다 — 그것은 그를 위한 것이었다."], ["Her own attachment had really subsided into a mere nothing; it was not worth thinking of;—but if he, who had undoubtedly been always so much the most in love of the two, were to be returning with the same warmth of sentiment which he had taken away, it would be very distressing.", "그녀 자신의 연정은 실로 아무것도 아닌 정도로 가라앉아 있었으니, 생각할 가치조차 없었다. 그러나 둘 중에서 의심할 바 없이 늘 훨씬 더 깊이 빠져 있던 쪽이 그였는데, 그가 떠날 때 지녔던 그 뜨거운 마음 그대로 돌아온다면, 그것은 무척 곤란한 일이 될 터였다."], ["If a separation of two months should not have cooled him, there were dangers and evils before her:—caution for him and for herself would be necessary.", "두 달의 이별이 그를 식히지 못했다면, 그녀 앞에는 위험과 화근이 놓여 있는 셈이었다. 그를 위해서도 자신을 위해서도 신중함이 필요했다."], ["She did not mean to have her own affections entangled again, and it would be incumbent on her to avoid any encouragement of his.", "그녀는 자기 마음이 다시 얽히게 둘 생각이 없었고, 그러니 그의 마음을 부추기는 어떤 일도 피해야 할 의무가 있었다."]], [["She wished she might be able to keep him from an absolute declaration.", "그녀는 그가 끝내 분명한 고백을 하는 데까지 이르지 않게 막을 수 있기를 바랐다."], ["That would be so very painful a conclusion of their present acquaintance!", "그렇게 된다면 지금 두 사람 사이의 교제에 더없이 괴로운 결말이 될 테니까!"], ["and yet, she could not help rather anticipating something decisive.", "그러면서도 그녀는 무언가 결정적인 일이 닥치리라는 예감을 떨칠 수 없었다."], ["She felt as if the spring would not pass without bringing a crisis, an event, a something to alter her present composed and tranquil state.", "마치 이 봄이 어떤 고비를, 어떤 사건을, 지금의 차분하고 평온한 상태를 뒤흔들 무언가를 가져오지 않고는 지나가지 않을 것만 같았다."]], [["It was not very long, though rather longer than Mr. Weston had foreseen, before she had the power of forming some opinion of Frank Churchill’s feelings.", "프랭크 처칠의 심정에 대해 어떤 판단이라도 내릴 수 있게 되기까지는, 미스터 웨스턴이 내다본 것보다는 좀 더 길었으나 그리 오래 걸리지는 않았다."], ["The Enscombe family were not in town quite so soon as had been imagined, but he was at Highbury very soon afterwards.", "엔스컴 일가는 짐작했던 것만큼 빨리 런던에 올라오지 못했으나, 그는 그 직후 곧 하이베리에 와 있었다. 그는 두어 시간을 머무르려고 말을 타고 내려왔다."], ["He rode down for a couple of hours; he could not yet do more; but as he came from Randalls immediately to Hartfield, she could then exercise all her quick observation, and speedily determine how he was influenced, and how she must act.", "아직은 그 이상을 할 수 없었던 것이다. 그러나 랜덜스에서 곧장 하트필드로 왔기에, 그녀는 특유의 날렵한 관찰력을 한껏 발휘하여 그가 어떤 마음에 놓여 있는지, 자신은 어떻게 처신해야 하는지를 재빨리 가늠할 수 있었다."], ["They met with the utmost friendliness.", "두 사람은 더없이 다정하게 만났다."], ["There could be no doubt of his great pleasure in seeing her.", "그가 그녀를 보고 크게 기뻐하는 것은 의심할 바 없었다."], ["But she had an almost instant doubt of his caring for her as he had done, of his feeling the same tenderness in the same degree.", "그러나 그녀는 거의 그 즉시, 그가 예전만큼 자신을 마음에 두고 있는지, 같은 다정함을 같은 정도로 느끼고 있는지 의심이 들었다."], ["She watched him well.", "그녀는 그를 찬찬히 살폈다."], ["It was a clear thing he was less in love than he had been.", "그가 예전만큼 사랑에 빠져 있지는 않다는 것이 분명했다."], ["Absence, with the conviction probably of her indifference, had produced this very natural and very desirable effect.", "떨어져 지낸 데다, 아마도 그녀가 무심하리라는 확신까지 더해져, 이 지극히 자연스럽고 지극히 바람직한 결과를 빚어낸 것이었다."]], [["He was in high spirits; as ready to talk and laugh as ever, and seemed delighted to speak of his former visit, and recur to old stories: and he was not without agitation.", "그는 한껏 들떠 있었다. 여느 때처럼 말하고 웃을 채비가 되어 있었고, 지난번 방문 이야기를 하고 옛이야기를 되짚는 것을 즐거워하는 듯했다."], ["It was not in his calmness that she read his comparative indifference.", "그렇다고 동요가 없는 것도 아니었다. 그녀가 그의 상대적인 무심함을 읽어 낸 것은 그의 침착함에서가 아니었다."], ["He was not calm; his spirits were evidently fluttered; there was restlessness about him.", "그는 침착하지 않았다. 마음이 흔들리고 있는 것이 역력했고, 어딘지 안절부절못하는 데가 있었다."], ["Lively as he was, it seemed a liveliness that did not satisfy himself; but what decided her belief on the subject, was his staying only a quarter of an hour, and hurrying away to make other calls in Highbury.", "활기차기는 했으나, 그것은 스스로도 만족하지 못하는 활기 같았다. 그러나 이 문제에 대한 그녀의 믿음을 굳힌 것은, 그가 겨우 십오 분만 머물다가 하이베리의 다른 집들을 마저 방문하겠다며 서둘러 떠난 일이었다."], ["“He had seen a group of old acquaintance in the street as he passed—he had not stopped, he would not stop for more than a word—but he had the vanity to think they would be disappointed if he did not call, and much as he wished to stay longer at Hartfield, he must hurry off.”", "\"지나오는 길에 거리에서 옛 지인 한 무리를 보았다고 — 멈추지는 않았고, 한마디 이상 멈출 생각도 없었지만 — 자기가 들르지 않으면 그들이 서운해하리라 생각하는 허영심이 있어서, 하트필드에 더 오래 머물고 싶은 마음이야 굴뚝같지만 서둘러 가 봐야겠다고.\" 그가 예전만큼 사랑에 빠져 있지 않다는 데는 의심이 없었다."], ["She had no doubt as to his being less in love—but neither his agitated spirits, nor his hurrying away, seemed like a perfect cure; and she was rather inclined to think it implied a dread of her returning power, and a discreet resolution of not trusting himself with her long.", "그러나 흔들리는 마음도, 서둘러 떠난 것도, 완전히 나았다는 표시처럼 보이지는 않았다. 차라리 그녀는, 그것이 그녀의 위력이 되살아날까 두려워하는 마음과, 곁에 오래 머물며 제 마음을 시험하지 않으려는 조심스러운 결심을 내비친 것이라고 여기는 쪽으로 기울었다."]], [["This was the only visit from Frank Churchill in the course of ten days.", "이것이 열흘 동안 프랭크 처칠이 한 유일한 방문이었다."], ["He was often hoping, intending to come—but was always prevented.", "그는 자주 오고 싶다고, 올 작정이라고 했으나 — 늘 무언가에 가로막혔다."], ["His aunt could not bear to have him leave her.", "그의 외숙모가 그를 곁에서 떠나보내려 하지 않았던 것이다."], ["Such was his own account at Randall’s.", "랜덜스에서 그가 한 설명이 그러했다."], ["If he were quite sincere, if he really tried to come, it was to be inferred that Mrs. Churchill’s removal to London had been of no service to the wilful or nervous part of her disorder.", "그가 정녕 진심이었다면, 정말로 오려고 애썼다면, 처칠 부인이 런던으로 거처를 옮긴 것이 그녀의 고집스럽거나 신경질적인 병증에는 아무 도움이 되지 못했다고 보아야 했다."], ["That she was really ill was very certain; he had declared himself convinced of it, at Randalls.", "그녀가 정말로 앓고 있다는 것만은 아주 분명했다. 랜덜스에서 그는 그 점만은 확신한다고 단언했다."], ["Though much might be fancy, he could not doubt, when he looked back, that she was in a weaker state of health than she had been half a year ago.", "많은 것이 기분 탓일 수는 있어도, 돌이켜 보면 그녀가 반년 전보다 더 쇠약한 상태에 있다는 것만은 의심할 수 없었다."], ["He did not believe it to proceed from any thing that care and medicine might not remove, or at least that she might not have many years of existence before her; but he could not be prevailed on, by all his father’s doubts, to say that her complaints were merely imaginary, or that she was as strong as ever.", "그는 그녀의 병이, 보살핌과 약으로 고칠 수 없는 무언가에서 비롯되었다고는, 혹은 적어도 그녀에게 앞으로 살아갈 세월이 여러 해 남아 있지 않으리라고는 믿지 않았다. 그러나 아버지가 아무리 의심하며 다그쳐도, 그녀의 호소가 그저 상상의 산물일 뿐이라거나 그녀가 예전만큼 정정하다고는 끝내 말하려 들지 않았다."]], [["It soon appeared that London was not the place for her.", "머잖아 런던이 그녀에게 맞는 곳이 아니라는 것이 드러났다."], ["She could not endure its noise.", "그녀는 그 소음을 견디지 못했다."], ["Her nerves were under continual irritation and suffering; and by the ten days’ end, her nephew’s letter to Randalls communicated a change of plan.", "신경이 끊임없이 자극받고 시달렸다. 그리하여 열흘이 다 갈 무렵, 그 조카가 랜덜스로 보낸 편지에 계획이 바뀌었다는 소식이 담겼다."], ["They were going to remove immediately to Richmond.", "그들은 곧장 리치먼드로 거처를 옮길 작정이었다."], ["Mrs. Churchill had been recommended to the medical skill of an eminent person there, and had otherwise a fancy for the place.", "처칠 부인은 그곳에 있는 어느 명망 높은 인물의 의술을 권유받았고, 게다가 그 고장을 마음에 들어 했다."], ["A ready-furnished house in a favourite spot was engaged, and much benefit expected from the change.", "사람들이 선호하는 자리에 가구까지 갖춰진 집 한 채를 빌렸고, 이 변화로 큰 차도가 있으리라 기대했다."]], [["Emma heard that Frank wrote in the highest spirits of this arrangement, and seemed most fully to appreciate the blessing of having two months before him of such near neighbourhood to many dear friends—for the house was taken for May and June.", "엠마는, 프랭크가 이 결정을 두고 더없이 들뜬 마음으로 편지를 썼으며, 앞으로 두 달을 그토록 많은 정든 벗들과 이웃처럼 가까이 지낼 수 있다는 축복을 마음껏 만끽하는 듯 보인다는 이야기를 들었다 — 그 집은 오월과 유월 두 달을 빌린 것이었다."], ["She was told that now he wrote with the greatest confidence of being often with them, almost as often as he could even wish.", "그녀는, 그가 이제 자기들과 자주, 거의 자신이 바랄 수 있는 만큼이나 자주 함께할 수 있으리라는 확신에 차서 편지를 썼다는 말을 들었다."]], [["Emma saw how Mr. Weston understood these joyous prospects.", "엠마는 미스터 웨스턴이 이 즐거운 전망을 어떻게 받아들이는지 알아챘다."], ["He was considering her as the source of all the happiness they offered.", "그는 그 전망이 약속하는 온갖 행복의 원천이 그녀라고 여기고 있었다."], ["She hoped it was not so.", "그녀는 그렇지 않기를 바랐다."], ["Two months must bring it to the proof.", "두 달이면 그 진위가 가려질 터였다."]], [["Mr. Weston’s own happiness was indisputable.", "미스터 웨스턴 자신의 행복만은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["He was quite delighted.", "그는 더할 나위 없이 기뻐했다."], ["It was the very circumstance he could have wished for.", "바로 그가 바라마지않던 상황이었다."], ["Now, it would be really having Frank in their neighbourhood.", "이제 정말로 프랭크가 이웃에 있는 셈이 되는 것이다."], ["What were nine miles to a young man?—An hour’s ride.", "젊은이에게 구 마일이 무어란 말인가?"], ["He would be always coming over.", "한 시간 말달리면 될 거리였다."], ["The difference in that respect of Richmond and London was enough to make the whole difference of seeing him always and seeing him never.", "그는 늘 건너오게 될 터였다. 그 점에서 리치먼드와 런던의 차이는, 그를 늘 보느냐 도무지 못 보느냐 하는 차이를 통째로 가를 만큼 컸다."], ["Sixteen miles—nay, eighteen—it must be full eighteen to Manchester-street—was a serious obstacle.", "십육 마일 — 아니, 십팔 마일 — 맨체스터 거리까지는 족히 십팔 마일은 되었으니 — 그것은 만만찮은 장애물이었다."], ["Were he ever able to get away, the day would be spent in coming and returning.", "행여 그가 빠져나올 수 있다 한들, 오가는 데 하루가 다 갈 터였다. 그를 런던에 두는 것은 아무 위안이 못 되었다."], ["There was no comfort in having him in London; he might as well be at Enscombe; but Richmond was the very distance for easy intercourse.", "그럴 바엔 엔스컴에 있는 것과 다를 바 없었다. 그러나 리치먼드는 허물없이 오가기에 꼭 알맞은 거리였다."], ["Better than nearer!", "더 가까운 것보다 차라리 나았다!"]], [["One good thing was immediately brought to a certainty by this removal,—the ball at the Crown.", "이 이사 덕에 즉시 확실해진 좋은 일이 하나 있었으니, 크라운 여관에서 열기로 한 무도회였다."], ["It had not been forgotten before, but it had been soon acknowledged vain to attempt to fix a day.", "전에도 잊고 있던 것은 아니었으나, 날을 잡으려 해 봐야 부질없다는 것이 곧 받아들여졌었다."], ["Now, however, it was absolutely to be; every preparation was resumed, and very soon after the Churchills had removed to Richmond, a few lines from Frank, to say that his aunt felt already much better for the change, and that he had no doubt of being able to join them for twenty-four hours at any given time, induced them to name as early a day as possible.", "그러나 이제는 무슨 일이 있어도 열기로 했다. 모든 준비가 다시 시작되었고, 처칠 일가가 리치먼드로 옮긴 직후, 외숙모가 거처를 옮긴 덕에 벌써 한결 나아진 듯하며 언제든 정해 주는 날에 스물네 시간쯤은 틀림없이 자기들과 함께할 수 있으리라는 프랭크의 짧은 편지 몇 줄이 도착하자, 그들은 되도록 이른 날을 잡기로 했다."]], [["Mr. Weston’s ball was to be a real thing.", "미스터 웨스턴의 무도회는 정말로 열리는 일이 되었다."], ["A very few to-morrows stood between the young people of Highbury and happiness.", "하이베리의 젊은이들과 행복 사이를 가로막은 것은 이제 며칠 안 되는 내일들뿐이었다."]], [["Mr. Woodhouse was resigned.", "미스터 우드하우스는 단념하고 받아들였다."], ["The time of year lightened the evil to him.", "그래도 계절 덕에 그 화근이 한결 덜어졌다."], ["May was better for every thing than February.", "오월은 무슨 일에든 이월보다 나았으니까."], ["Mrs. Bates was engaged to spend the evening at Hartfield, James had due notice, and he sanguinely hoped that neither dear little Henry nor dear little John would have any thing the matter with them, while dear Emma were gone.", "베이츠 부인이 그 저녁을 하트필드에서 보내기로 약조되었고, 제임스에게도 미리 일러두었으며, 그는 사랑하는 엠마가 나가 있는 동안 사랑하는 어린 헨리에게도 사랑하는 어린 존에게도 아무 탈이 없기를 낙관하며 바랐다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter II", "# 제3권 2장"]], [["No misfortune occurred, again to prevent the ball.", "다시는 무도회를 가로막을 불운이 일어나지 않았다."], ["The day approached, the day arrived; and after a morning of some anxious watching, Frank Churchill, in all the certainty of his own self, reached Randalls before dinner, and every thing was safe.", "날이 다가왔고, 마침내 그날이 되었다. 노심초사하며 지켜본 오전이 지나고, 프랭크 처칠은 평소처럼 제 자신에 대한 확신에 가득 차서 저녁 식사 전에 랜덜스에 닿았으며, 모든 것이 무사했다."]], [["No second meeting had there yet been between him and Emma.", "그와 엠마 사이에는 아직 두 번째 만남이 없었다."], ["The room at the Crown was to witness it;—but it would be better than a common meeting in a crowd. Mr.", "크라운 여관의 그 방이 그 만남을 지켜볼 참이었다 — 하지만 인파 속에서의 흔한 만남보다는 나을 터였다."], ["Weston had been so very earnest in his entreaties for her arriving there as soon as possible after themselves, for the purpose of taking her opinion as to the propriety and comfort of the rooms before any other persons came, that she could not refuse him, and must therefore spend some quiet interval in the young man’s company.", "미스터 웨스턴은 다른 사람들이 오기 전에 방의 적절함과 안락함에 대한 그녀의 의견을 듣고자, 자기들 뒤를 따라 되도록 일찍 그곳에 도착해 달라고 어찌나 간곡히 청했는지, 그녀로서는 거절할 수 없었고, 따라서 그 젊은이와 함께 한동안 한가로운 시간을 보내야 했다."], ["She was to convey Harriet, and they drove to the Crown in good time, the Randalls party just sufficiently before them.", "그녀는 해리엇을 데려가기로 했고, 두 사람은 알맞은 시각에 크라운으로 마차를 몰고 갔는데, 랜덜스 일행은 딱 적당할 만큼 앞서 도착해 있었다."]], [["Frank Churchill seemed to have been on the watch; and though he did not say much, his eyes declared that he meant to have a delightful evening.", "프랭크 처칠은 망을 보고 있었던 듯했다. 말은 많이 하지 않았으나, 그의 눈은 오늘 저녁을 즐겁게 보내려는 마음을 분명히 드러냈다."], ["They all walked about together, to see that every thing was as it should be; and within a few minutes were joined by the contents of another carriage, which Emma could not hear the sound of at first, without great surprize.", "그들은 다 함께 거닐며 모든 것이 제대로 되어 있는지 살폈고, 몇 분도 채 지나지 않아 다른 마차에 실려 온 사람들이 합류했다. 엠마는 처음 그 마차 소리를 듣고 크게 놀라지 않을 수 없었다."], ["“So unreasonably early!”", "\"이렇게 터무니없이 일찍!\""], ["she was going to exclaim; but she presently found that it was a family of old friends, who were coming, like herself, by particular desire, to help Mr.", "하고 그녀는 외칠 뻔했다. 그러나 곧 그들이 자신과 마찬가지로 미스터 웨스턴의 판단을 도우려고 특별한 청을 받아 온 오랜 벗들의 일가임을 알게 되었다."], ["Weston’s judgment; and they were so very closely followed by another carriage of cousins, who had been entreated to come early with the same distinguishing earnestness, on the same errand, that it seemed as if half the company might soon be collected together for the purpose of preparatory inspection.", "그리고 그 뒤를 바짝 따라, 같은 용건으로 똑같이 각별한 간청을 받고 일찍 와 달라는 부탁을 들은 사촌들의 마차가 또 한 대 들어오니, 마치 예비 점검을 위해 손님의 절반이 머잖아 한자리에 모일 듯한 형국이었다."]], [["Emma perceived that her taste was not the only taste on which Mr. Weston depended, and felt, that to be the favourite and intimate of a man who had so many intimates and confidantes, was not the very first distinction in the scale of vanity.", "엠마는 미스터 웨스턴이 의지하는 취향이 자기 취향만이 아님을 알아차렸고, 이토록 많은 친지와 속내를 나누는 벗을 둔 사람의 총애와 친밀함을 받는다는 것이 허영의 척도에서 결코 으뜸가는 영예는 아님을 깨달았다."], ["She liked his open manners, but a little less of open-heartedness would have made him a higher character.—General benevolence, but not general friendship, made a man what he ought to be.—She could fancy such a man.", "그녀는 그의 시원시원한 태도를 좋아했지만, 흉금을 트는 마음이 조금 덜했더라면 그가 더 높은 인품의 인물이 되었으리라 — 두루 미치는 자애로움, 그러나 두루 미치는 우정은 아닌 것이 한 사람을 마땅한 사람으로 만든다 — 그녀는 그런 사람을 머릿속에 그려 볼 수 있었다."], ["The whole party walked about, and looked, and praised again; and then, having nothing else to do, formed a sort of half-circle round the fire, to observe in their various modes, till other subjects were started, that, though May, a fire in the evening was still very pleasant.", "일행은 모두 거닐며 둘러보고 또 칭찬했다. 그러고는 달리 할 일이 없자, 난롯가에 반원처럼 둘러서서, 다른 화제가 나올 때까지 저마다 제 방식으로 오월인데도 저녁의 난롯불이 여전히 무척 기분 좋다는 사실을 새겼다."]], [["Emma found that it was not Mr. Weston’s fault that the number of privy councillors was not yet larger.", "엠마는 비밀 자문관의 수가 아직 더 많지 않은 것이 미스터 웨스턴의 탓이 아님을 알게 되었다."], ["They had stopped at Mrs. Bates’s door to offer the use of their carriage, but the aunt and niece were to be brought by the Eltons.", "그들은 베이츠 부인의 집 앞에 마차를 세우고 동승을 권했으나, 그 이모와 조카딸은 엘턴 부부가 데려오기로 되어 있었던 것이다."]], [["Frank was standing by her, but not steadily; there was a restlessness, which shewed a mind not at ease.", "프랭크는 그녀 곁에 서 있었으나, 가만히 있지는 못했다. 마음이 편치 않음을 드러내는 안절부절못함이 있었다."], ["He was looking about, he was going to the door, he was watching for the sound of other carriages,—impatient to begin, or afraid of being always near her.", "그는 두리번거리다가, 문가로 갔다가, 다른 마차 소리에 귀를 기울였다 — 어서 시작하고 싶어 안달이거나, 아니면 줄곧 그녀 곁에 있는 것이 두렵거나."]], [["Mrs. Elton was spoken of.", "엘턴 부인 이야기가 나왔다."], ["“I think she must be here soon,”", "\"곧 오시겠지요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he. “I have a great curiosity to see Mrs.", "\"엘턴 부인을 무척 보고 싶습니다."], ["Elton, I have heard so much of her.", "워낙 많은 말을 들어서요."], ["It cannot be long, I think, before she comes.”", "머잖아 도착하실 것 같은데요.\""]], [["A carriage was heard.", "마차 소리가 들렸다."], ["He was on the move immediately; but coming back, said,", "그는 즉시 움직였으나, 도로 돌아오며 말했다."]], [["“I am forgetting that I am not acquainted with her.", "\"제가 그분과 안면이 없다는 걸 깜빡했군요."], ["I have never seen either Mr. or Mrs.", "미스터 엘턴도 엘턴 부인도 본 적이 없어요."], ["Elton. I have no business to put myself forward.”", "제가 나설 일이 아니지요.\""]], [["Mr. and Mrs. Elton appeared; and all the smiles and the proprieties passed.", "미스터 엘턴과 엘턴 부인이 나타났고, 온갖 미소와 격식 차린 인사가 오갔다."]], [["“But Miss Bates and Miss Fairfax!”", "\"한데 미스 베이츠와 미스 페어팩스는요?\""], ["said Mr. Weston, looking about.", "미스터 웨스턴이 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“We thought you were to bring them.”", "\"두 분을 모셔 오시는 줄 알았는데요.\""]], [["The mistake had been slight.", "착오는 사소한 것이었다."], ["The carriage was sent for them now.", "마차가 지금 그들을 데리러 보내졌다."], ["Emma longed to know what Frank’s first opinion of Mrs.", "엠마는 엘턴 부인에 대한 프랭크의 첫인상이 어떨지 못내 궁금했다."], ["Elton might be; how he was affected by the studied elegance of her dress, and her smiles of graciousness.", "그가 그녀의 공들인 옷차림과 우아한 미소에 어떤 영향을 받을지."], ["He was immediately qualifying himself to form an opinion, by giving her very proper attention, after the introduction had passed.", "그는 소개가 끝나자마자 그녀에게 아주 적절한 관심을 기울이며, 곧바로 의견을 세울 채비에 들어갔다."]], [["In a few minutes the carriage returned.—Somebody talked of rain.—“I will see that there are umbrellas, sir,”", "몇 분 만에 마차가 돌아왔다. 누군가 비 이야기를 했다."], ["said Frank to his father: “Miss Bates must not be forgotten:”", "\"우산이 있는지 제가 살피겠습니다, 아버지.\" 프랭크가 아버지에게 말했다."], ["and away he went. Mr.", "\"미스 베이츠를 잊으면 안 되지요.\""], ["Weston was following; but Mrs.", "그러고는 가 버렸다."], ["Elton detained him, to gratify him by her opinion of his son; and so briskly did she begin, that the young man himself, though by no means moving slowly, could hardly be out of hearing.", "미스터 웨스턴이 따라가려 했으나, 엘턴 부인이 그를 붙들어 그의 아들에 대한 자기 의견으로 그를 흡족하게 해 주었다. 어찌나 빠르게 말을 시작했는지, 결코 느리게 걷지 않던 그 젊은이조차 그 말이 안 들리는 데까지는 거의 가지 못했을 정도였다."]], [["“A very fine young man indeed, Mr. Weston.", "\"정말이지 아주 훌륭한 청년이에요, 미스터 웨스턴."], ["You know I candidly told you I should form my own opinion; and I am happy to say that I am extremely pleased with him.—You may believe me.", "아시다시피 제가 솔직히 말씀드렸지요, 제 나름의 의견을 세우겠다고요. 그런데 그분이 정말 흡족하다고 말씀드리게 되어 기뻐요 — 제 말을 믿으셔도 돼요."], ["I never compliment.", "저는 결코 빈말로 추어주는 법이 없거든요."], ["I think him a very handsome young man, and his manners are precisely what I like and approve—so truly the gentleman, without the least conceit or puppyism.", "아주 잘생긴 청년이라고 보고, 태도는 딱 제가 좋아하고 인정하는 그대로예요 — 거드름이나 건방진 데라곤 손톱만큼도 없이, 참으로 신사다워요."], ["You must know I have a vast dislike to puppies—quite a horror of them.", "아셔야 할 것이, 저는 건방진 풋내기들을 끔찍이 싫어해요 — 아주 질색이지요."], ["They were never tolerated at Maple Grove. Neither Mr.", "메이플 그로브에서는 그런 자들이 결코 용납되지 않았어요."], ["Suckling nor me had ever any patience with them; and we used sometimes to say very cutting things!", "미스터 서클링도 저도 도무지 그런 자들을 못 참았고, 가끔 아주 신랄한 말을 하곤 했죠!"], ["Selina, who is mild almost to a fault, bore with them much better.”", "거의 흠이 될 만큼 온순한 셀리나는 그들을 훨씬 잘 견뎌 냈지만요.\""]], [["While she talked of his son, Mr.", "그녀가 그의 아들 이야기를 하는 동안에는 미스터 웨스턴의 주의가 단단히 묶여 있었다."], ["Weston’s attention was chained; but when she got to Maple Grove, he could recollect that there were ladies just arriving to be attended to, and with happy smiles must hurry away.", "그러나 그녀가 메이플 그로브에 이르자, 그는 막 도착해 맞이해야 할 숙녀들이 있음을 떠올리고는 흐뭇한 미소를 지으며 서둘러 자리를 떴다."]], [["Mrs. Elton turned to Mrs.", "엘턴 부인은 웨스턴 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["Weston. “I have no doubt of its being our carriage with Miss Bates and Jane.", "\"미스 베이츠와 제인을 태운 게 우리 마차인 게 틀림없어요."], ["Our coachman and horses are so extremely expeditious!—I believe we drive faster than any body.—What a pleasure it is to send one’s carriage for a friend!—I understand you were so kind as to offer, but another time it will be quite unnecessary.", "우리 마부와 말들이 어찌나 날쌘지요! — 우리가 누구보다 빨리 달리는 것 같아요 — 벗을 위해 제 마차를 보내는 게 얼마나 기쁜 일인지요!"], ["You may be very sure I shall always take care of them.”", "— 친절하게도 부인이 권하셨다는 건 알지만, 다음번엔 그러실 필요가 전혀 없을 거예요. 제가 늘 그 두 사람을 챙기리란 걸 믿어 의심치 마세요.\""]], [["Miss Bates and Miss Fairfax, escorted by the two gentlemen, walked into the room; and Mrs.", "미스 베이츠와 미스 페어팩스가 두 신사의 호위를 받으며 방으로 들어왔고, 엘턴 부인은 그들을 맞이하는 것이 웨스턴 부인 못지않게 제 본분이라 여기는 듯했다."], ["Elton seemed to think it as much her duty as Mrs. Weston’s to receive them.", "그녀의 몸짓과 거동은 엠마처럼 지켜보는 이라면 누구나 알아챌 만했다."], ["Her gestures and movements might be understood by any one who looked on like Emma; but her words, every body’s words, were soon lost under the incessant flow of Miss Bates, who came in talking, and had not finished her speech under many minutes after her being admitted into the circle at the fire.", "그러나 그녀의 말은, 아니 모두의 말은, 들어오면서부터 떠들기 시작해 난롯가 무리에 끼어든 뒤로도 몇 분이 지나도록 끝나지 않는 미스 베이츠의 끝없는 말줄기 아래 곧 묻혀 버렸다."], ["As the door opened she was heard,", "문이 열리면서 그녀의 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“So very obliging of you!—No rain at all.", "\"어쩜 이렇게 친절하실까요! — 비는 전혀 안 와요."], ["Nothing to signify.", "별것 아니에요. 저야 아무래도 좋고요."], ["I do not care for myself.", "신발도 아주 두툼하고요. 게다가 제인이 그러는데 — 어머나!"], ["Quite thick shoes.", "(문 안에 들어서자마자) 어머나! 이거 정말 눈부셔요!"], ["And Jane declares—Well!—(as soon as she was within the door) Well!", "— 정말 훌륭해요! — 솜씨가 기막히네요, 정말이지."], ["This is brilliant indeed!—This is admirable!—Excellently contrived, upon my word.", "부족한 게 하나도 없어요. 상상도 못 했어요 — 불도 이렇게 환하게!"], ["Nothing wanting.", "— 제인, 제인, 봐!"], ["Could not have imagined it.—So well lighted up!—Jane, Jane, look!—did you ever see any thing?", "이런 거 본 적 있니?"], ["Oh! Mr.", "어머!"], ["Weston, you must really have had Aladdin’s lamp.", "미스터 웨스턴, 정말 알라딘의 램프라도 가지셨나 봐요."], ["Good Mrs. Stokes would not know her own room again.", "착한 미시즈 스토크스도 자기 방을 못 알아보겠어요."], ["I saw her as I came in; she was standing in the entrance.", "들어오면서 봤는데, 입구에 서 계시더군요."], ["‘Oh! Mrs.", "'어머!"], ["Stokes,’ said I—but I had not time for more.”", "미시즈 스토크스,' 했는데 — 더 말할 짬이 없었네요.\""], ["She was now met by Mrs.", "이제 웨스턴 부인이 그녀를 맞았다."], ["Weston.—“Very well, I thank you, ma’am.", "\"아주 잘 지내요, 고마워요, 부인."], ["I hope you are quite well.", "평안하시길 바라요."], ["Very happy to hear it.", "그렇다니 정말 기뻐요."], ["So afraid you might have a headache!—seeing you pass by so often, and knowing how much trouble you must have.", "두통이라도 나실까 봐 어찌나 걱정했는데요! — 그렇게 자주 지나다니시는 걸 보고, 또 얼마나 수고가 많으셨을지 아니까요."], ["Delighted to hear it indeed. Ah!", "정말로 그렇다니 기뻐요."], ["dear Mrs.", "아!"], ["Elton, so obliged to you for the carriage!—excellent time.", "엘턴 부인, 마차 정말 고마워요! — 시간도 딱 좋았고요."], ["Jane and I quite ready.", "제인이랑 저랑 다 채비가 돼 있었어요."], ["Did not keep the horses a moment.", "말들을 한순간도 세워 두지 않았답니다."], ["Most comfortable carriage.—Oh!", "더없이 편안한 마차예요. — 어머!"], ["and I am sure our thanks are due to you, Mrs. Weston, on that score.", "그리고 그 일로 말하자면 우리가 웨스턴 부인께도 감사를 드려야 마땅하지요."], ["Mrs. Elton had most kindly sent Jane a note, or we should have been.—But two such offers in one day!—Never were such neighbours.", "엘턴 부인이 친절하게도 제인에게 쪽지를 보내 주셔서 망정이지, 안 그랬으면 우리가 — 한데 하루에 그렇게 두 가지 호의를!"], ["I said to my mother, ‘Upon my word, ma’am—.’ Thank you, my mother is remarkably well.", "— 이런 이웃들이 또 어디 있겠어요. 제가 어머니께 그랬어요, '정말이지, 어머니 —.'"], ["Gone to Mr. Woodhouse’s.", "고마워요, 어머니는 놀랄 만큼 잘 지내세요."], ["I made her take her shawl—for the evenings are not warm—her large new shawl— Mrs.", "미스터 우드하우스 댁에 가셨어요. 숄을 꼭 두르시게 했죠 — 저녁이 따뜻하지가 않아서요 — 크고 새로 산 숄을요 — 딕슨 부인의 결혼 선물이에요."], ["Dixon’s wedding-present.—So kind of her to think of my mother!", "우리 어머니를 그렇게 생각해 주다니 어쩜 그리 다정한지!"], ["Bought at Weymouth, you know—Mr. Dixon’s choice.", "웨이머스에서 샀대요, 아시잖아요 — 미스터 딕슨이 고른 거고요."], ["There were three others, Jane says, which they hesitated about some time.", "제인이 그러는데, 그들이 한동안 망설인 다른 게 세 개 더 있었대요. 캠벨 대령은 올리브색을 더 좋아하셨고요."], ["Colonel Campbell rather preferred an olive.", "얘 제인아, 정말 발은 안 젖었니?"], ["My dear Jane, are you sure you did not wet your feet?—It was but a drop or two, but I am so afraid:—but Mr.", "— 겨우 한두 방울이었지만 어찌나 걱정인지 — 한데 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 어찌나 극진하게 — 그리고 디딜 매트까지 깔려 있어서 — 그 극진한 친절을 저는 결코 잊지 못할 거예요. — 아!"], ["Frank Churchill was so extremely—and there was a mat to step upon—I shall never forget his extreme politeness.—Oh! Mr.", "미스터 프랭크 처칠, 말씀드려야겠는데, 그 뒤로 우리 어머니 안경이 한 번도 탈이 없어요."], ["Frank Churchill, I must tell you my mother’s spectacles have never been in fault since; the rivet never came out again.", "대갈못이 다시는 빠지지 않았답니다. 어머니가 당신의 그 착한 마음씨를 자주 입에 올리세요."], ["My mother often talks of your good-nature.", "그렇지, 제인? — 우리가 미스터 프랭크 처칠 이야기를 자주 하지 않니?"], ["Does not she, Jane?—Do not we often talk of Mr.", "— 아! 여기 미스 우드하우스가 계시네 — 미스 우드하우스, 안녕하세요?"], ["Frank Churchill?—Ah!", "— 저야 아주 잘 지내요, 고마워요, 정말 잘요."], ["here’s Miss Woodhouse.—Dear Miss Woodhouse, how do you do?—Very well I thank you, quite well.", "이건 그야말로 요정 나라에서의 만남이로군요! — 이런 변신이라니!"], ["This is meeting quite in fairy-land!—Such a transformation!—Must not compliment, I know (eyeing Emma most complacently)—that would be rude—but upon my word, Miss Woodhouse, you do look—how do you like Jane’s hair?—You are a judge.—She did it all herself.", "— 칭찬은 하면 안 되는 거 알아요 (엠마를 더없이 흐뭇하게 바라보며) — 그건 무례한 일이니까요 — 하지만 정말이지, 미스 우드하우스, 정말 — 제인 머리는 어때 보여요?"], ["Quite wonderful how she does her hair!—No hairdresser from London I think could.—Ah!", "— 보는 눈이 있으시잖아요 — 자기가 다 했어요. 머리를 어쩜 이렇게 하는지 정말 놀라워요!"], ["Dr. Hughes I declare—and Mrs.", "— 런던 미용사도 이렇겐 못 할 거예요."], ["Hughes.", "— 아!"], ["Must go and speak to Dr. and Mrs.", "휴스 박사님이시네 — 그리고 휴스 부인."], ["Hughes for a moment.—How do you do?", "잠깐 가서 휴스 박사 내외께 인사를 드려야겠어요. — 안녕하세요?"], ["How do you do?—Very well, I thank you.", "안녕하세요? — 아주 잘 지내요, 고마워요."], ["This is delightful, is not it?—Where’s dear Mr.", "이거 정말 즐겁지 않아요? — 그런데 그 다정한 미스터 리처드는 어디 계시죠?"], ["Richard?—Oh!", "— 아!"], ["there he is.", "저기 계시네."], ["Don’t disturb him.", "방해하지 말아요."], ["Much better employed talking to the young ladies.", "아가씨들과 이야기하는 게 훨씬 낫지요."], ["How do you do, Mr.", "안녕하세요, 미스터 리처드?"], ["Richard?—I saw you the other day as you rode through the town—Mrs.", "— 요전 날 마을을 말 타고 지나가시는 거 봤어요 — 어머, 미시즈 오트웨이 아니세요! — 그리고 착한 미스터 오트웨이, 미스 오트웨이, 미스 캐럴라인."], ["Otway, I protest!—and good Mr.", "— 친구분이 이렇게나 많이!"], ["Otway, and Miss Otway and Miss Caroline.—Such a host of friends!—and Mr.", "— 그리고 미스터 조지에 미스터 아서까지! — 안녕하세요?"], ["George and Mr.", "다들 안녕하세요?"], ["Arthur!—How do you do?", "— 아주 잘 지내요, 정말 고마워요. 이보다 좋을 수 없어요."], ["How do you all do?—Quite well, I am much obliged to you.", "— 또 마차 소리 안 들려요? — 누구실까?"], ["Never better.—Don’t I hear another carriage?—Who can this be?—very likely the worthy Coles.—Upon my word, this is charming to be standing about among such friends!", "— 아마 그 착한 콜 댁이겠죠. — 정말이지, 이런 친구분들 틈에 서 있다니 황홀해요!"], ["And such a noble fire!—I am quite roasted.", "게다가 이렇게 멋진 불까지! — 아주 노릇노릇 익겠어요."], ["No coffee, I thank you, for me—never take coffee.—A little tea if you please, sir, by and bye,—no hurry—Oh!", "커피는 됐어요, 고마워요 — 저는 커피를 절대 안 마셔서요. — 차나 조금, 이따가요, 부탁드려요 — 급할 거 없어요 — 어머!"], ["here it comes.", "여기 오네요."], ["Every thing so good!”", "뭐든 다 이렇게 훌륭해서야!\""]], [["Frank Churchill returned to his station by Emma; and as soon as Miss Bates was quiet, she found herself necessarily overhearing the discourse of Mrs.", "프랭크 처칠은 엠마 곁의 제자리로 돌아왔다. 미스 베이츠가 잠잠해지자마자, 그녀는 조금 뒤쪽에 서 있던 엘턴 부인과 미스 페어팩스의 대화를 본의 아니게 엿듣게 되었다."], ["Elton and Miss Fairfax, who were standing a little way behind her.—He was thoughtful.", "그는 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그도 함께 엿듣고 있는지 어떤지는 가늠할 수 없었다."], ["Whether he were overhearing too, she could not determine.", "엘턴 부인은 제인의 옷차림과 자태에 대해 한참 칭찬을 늘어놓았고 — 그 칭찬은 아주 조용하고 점잖게 받아들여졌다 — 그러고 나서는 자기도 칭찬을 받고 싶어 하는 기색이 역력했다."], ["After a good many compliments to Jane on her dress and look, compliments very quietly and properly taken, Mrs.", "\"제 드레스는 어때요? — 가두리 장식은 어때요?"], ["Elton was evidently wanting to be complimented herself—and it was, “How do you like my gown?—How do you like my trimming?—How has Wright done my hair?”—with many other relative questions, all answered with patient politeness.", "— 라이트가 제 머리를 어떻게 매만졌어요?\" 같은 여러 질문이 이어졌고, 모두 인내심 어린 정중함으로 답해졌다."], ["Mrs.", "그러더니 엘턴 부인이 말했다."], ["Elton then said, “Nobody can think less of dress in general than I do—but upon such an occasion as this, when every body’s eyes are so much upon me, and in compliment to the Westons—who I have no doubt are giving this ball chiefly to do me honour—I would not wish to be inferior to others.", "\"저만큼 옷차림에 마음을 안 쓰는 사람도 없을 거예요 — 하지만 이런 자리에서는, 모두의 눈이 이렇게 제게 쏠리고, 또 웨스턴 댁에 대한 예의로 보더라도 — 이 무도회를 주로 저를 기리려고 여는 게 틀림없으니까요 — 남들보다 못해 보이고 싶진 않아요. 그런데 보니 이 방에 진주를 한 사람은 저밖에 없네요."], ["And I see very few pearls in the room except mine.—So Frank Churchill is a capital dancer, I understand.—We shall see if our styles suit.—A fine young man certainly is Frank Churchill.", "— 그래서, 프랭크 처칠이 춤 솜씨가 일품이라죠. — 우리 둘 춤이 잘 맞을지 두고 봐야겠어요."], ["I like him very well.”", "— 프랭크 처칠은 정말이지 훌륭한 청년이에요. 아주 마음에 들어요.\""]], [["At this moment Frank began talking so vigorously, that Emma could not but imagine he had overheard his own praises, and did not want to hear more;—and the voices of the ladies were drowned for a while, till another suspension brought Mrs.", "이 순간 프랭크가 어찌나 활기차게 말을 시작했는지, 엠마는 그가 자기에 대한 칭찬을 엿듣고는 더 듣고 싶지 않은 것이라 짐작하지 않을 수 없었다. 두 부인의 목소리는 한동안 묻혀 버렸다가, 또 한 번 말이 멎으면서 엘턴 부인의 목소리가 다시 또렷이 앞으로 나섰다."], ["Elton’s tones again distinctly forward.—Mr. Elton had just joined them, and his wife was exclaiming,", "미스터 엘턴이 막 그들에게 합류한 참이었고, 그의 아내가 외치고 있었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["you have found us out at last, have you, in our seclusion?—I was this moment telling Jane, I thought you would begin to be impatient for tidings of us.”", "드디어 우리 숨어 있는 데를 찾아내셨군요? — 방금 제인에게 말하던 참이었어요, 당신이 우리 소식이 궁금해 안달하기 시작할 거라고요.\""]], [["“Jane!”—repeated Frank Churchill, with a look of surprize and displeasure.—“That is easy—but Miss Fairfax does not disapprove it, I suppose.”", "\"제인이라!\" 프랭크 처칠이 놀라움과 불쾌함이 어린 얼굴로 되뇌었다. \"허물없기도 하지 — 하지만 미스 페어팩스가 싫어하지는 않나 보군요.\""]], [["“How do you like Mrs. Elton?”", "\"엘턴 부인 어때요?\""], ["said Emma in a whisper.", "엠마가 속삭이며 물었다."]], [["“Not at all.”", "\"전혀요.\""]], [["“You are ungrateful.”", "\"배은망덕하시네요.\""]], [["“Ungrateful!—What do you mean?” Then changing from a frown to a smile—“No, do not tell me—I do not want to know what you mean.—Where is my father?—When are we to begin dancing?”", "\"배은망덕이라니! — 무슨 뜻입니까?\" 그러더니 찌푸린 얼굴을 미소로 바꾸며 — \"아니, 말하지 마세요 — 무슨 뜻인지 알고 싶지 않습니다. — 우리 아버지는 어디 계시죠? — 우리는 언제 춤을 추기로 했나요?\""]], [["Emma could hardly understand him; he seemed in an odd humour.", "엠마는 그를 도무지 이해할 수 없었다. 그는 이상한 기분에 빠진 듯했다."], ["He walked off to find his father, but was quickly back again with both Mr. and Mrs.", "그는 아버지를 찾으러 가 버렸으나, 미스터 웨스턴과 웨스턴 부인을 모두 데리고 금세 돌아왔다."], ["Weston. He had met with them in a little perplexity, which must be laid before Emma.", "그가 그들과 마주친 곳에 작은 난처함이 있었고, 그것은 엠마 앞에 내놓아야 할 문제였다."], ["It had just occurred to Mrs. Weston that Mrs.", "엘턴 부인에게 무도회의 첫 춤을 청해야 한다는 생각, 그녀가 그것을 기대하리라는 생각이 방금 웨스턴 부인에게 떠올랐던 것이다."], ["Elton must be asked to begin the ball; that she would expect it; which interfered with all their wishes of giving Emma that distinction.—Emma heard the sad truth with fortitude.", "이는 엠마에게 그 영예를 안기려던 그들 모두의 바람과 어긋나는 것이었다. 엠마는 이 서글픈 진실을 의연하게 들었다."]], [["“And what are we to do for a proper partner for her?”", "\"그럼 그분의 짝으로 누구를 마땅한 상대로 세워야 할까요?\""], ["said Mr. Weston.", "미스터 웨스턴이 말했다."], ["“She will think Frank ought to ask her.”", "\"프랭크가 자기에게 청해야 한다고 여길 텐데요.\""]], [["Frank turned instantly to Emma, to claim her former promise; and boasted himself an engaged man, which his father looked his most perfect approbation of—and it then appeared that Mrs.", "프랭크는 곧바로 엠마를 돌아보며 그녀가 전에 한 약속을 들이댔고, 이미 짝이 정해진 몸이라고 큰소리쳤는데, 그의 아버지는 그에 더없이 흡족한 얼굴이었다. 그러고 보니 웨스턴 부인이 바라는 것은 미스터 웨스턴 본인이 엘턴 부인과 춤추는 것이었고, 그들의 용건은 그를 그렇게 하도록 설득하는 일을 돕는 것이었으니, 이는 꽤 금세 이루어졌다."], ["Weston was wanting him to dance with Mrs.", "미스터 웨스턴과 엘턴 부인이 앞장서고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠과 미스 우드하우스가 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Elton himself, and that their business was to help to persuade him into it, which was done pretty soon.—Mr.", "엠마는 늘 이 무도회가 유독 자기를 위한 것이라 여겨 왔건만, 엘턴 부인 다음 둘째 자리에 서는 것을 감수해야 했다."], ["Weston and Mrs. Elton led the way, Mr.", "결혼을 생각하게 만들기에 거의 충분한 일이었다."], ["Frank Churchill and Miss Woodhouse followed. Emma must submit to stand second to Mrs.", "이때만큼은 허영이 완전히 충족된 엘턴 부인이 단연 우위에 있었다."], ["Elton, though she had always considered the ball as peculiarly for her.", "프랭크 처칠과 시작할 작정이었던 그녀였지만, 그 바꿈으로 손해 볼 것은 없었던 것이다. 미스터 웨스턴이라면 아들보다 윗길일 수도 있었으니까."], ["It was almost enough to make her think of marrying. Mrs.", "그러나 이 작은 흠집에도 불구하고 엠마는 즐거움에 미소 짓고 있었다."], ["Elton had undoubtedly the advantage, at this time, in vanity completely gratified; for though she had intended to begin with Frank Churchill, she could not lose by the change. Mr.", "짜이고 있는 줄의 어엿한 길이를 보는 것이 기뻤고, 흔치 않은 흥겨움의 시간이 여러 시간이나 앞에 놓여 있음을 느끼는 것이 기뻤다."], ["Weston might be his son’s superior.—In spite of this little rub, however, Emma was smiling with enjoyment, delighted to see the respectable length of the set as it was forming, and to feel that she had so many hours of unusual festivity before her.—She was more disturbed by Mr.", "다른 그 무엇보다 그녀를 어지럽힌 것은 미스터 나이틀리가 춤을 추지 않는다는 사실이었다. 저기 그가, 서 있어선 안 될 구경꾼들 틈에 끼어 있었다."], ["Knightley’s not dancing than by any thing else.—There he was, among the standers-by, where he ought not to be; he ought to be dancing,—not classing himself with the husbands, and fathers, and whist-players, who were pretending to feel an interest in the dance till their rubbers were made up,—so young as he looked!—He could not have appeared to greater advantage perhaps anywhere, than where he had placed himself.", "그는 춤을 추어야 마땅했다 — 한 판이 끝날 때까지 춤에 관심 있는 척하는 남편들이며 아버지들이며 휘스트 노름꾼들 틈에 제 몸을 두어선 안 될 일이었다 — 그렇게나 젊어 보이는데! 어쩌면 그가 선 그 자리만큼 그를 돋보이게 한 곳은 어디에도 없었으리라."], ["His tall, firm, upright figure, among the bulky forms and stooping shoulders of the elderly men, was such as Emma felt must draw every body’s eyes; and, excepting her own partner, there was not one among the whole row of young men who could be compared with him.—He moved a few steps nearer, and those few steps were enough to prove in how gentlemanlike a manner, with what natural grace, he must have danced, would he but take the trouble.—Whenever she caught his eye, she forced him to smile; but in general he was looking grave.", "나이 든 남자들의 우람한 몸집과 굽은 어깨들 사이에서, 그의 훤칠하고 단단하고 꼿꼿한 풍채는 모두의 눈길을 끌 수밖에 없겠다고 엠마는 느꼈다. 그리고 자기 짝을 빼고는, 그 줄에 늘어선 젊은이들 가운데 그와 견줄 만한 이가 하나도 없었다."], ["She wished he could love a ballroom better, and could like Frank Churchill better.—He seemed often observing her.", "그가 몇 걸음 다가왔는데, 그 몇 걸음만으로도, 그가 마음만 먹는다면 얼마나 신사다운 몸가짐으로, 얼마나 자연스러운 우아함으로 춤을 추었을지가 충분히 드러났다. 그의 눈길과 마주칠 때마다 그녀는 그가 미소 짓게 만들었다."], ["She must not flatter herself that he thought of her dancing, but if he were criticising her behaviour, she did not feel afraid.", "그러나 대개 그는 진지한 얼굴이었다. 그가 무도회장을 좀 더 좋아할 수 있다면, 또 프랭크 처칠을 좀 더 좋아할 수 있다면 좋으련만."], ["There was nothing like flirtation between her and her partner.", "그는 자주 그녀를 살피는 듯했다. 그가 그녀의 춤을 생각한다고 우쭐대선 안 될 일이었지만, 설령 그가 그녀의 처신을 흠잡고 있다 해도 그녀는 두렵지 않았다."], ["They seemed more like cheerful, easy friends, than lovers.", "그녀와 짝 사이에는 시시덕거림 비슷한 것이 전혀 없었다."], ["That Frank Churchill thought less of her than he had done, was indubitable.", "둘은 연인이라기보다는 쾌활하고 허물없는 친구에 가까워 보였다. 프랭크 처칠이 예전만큼 그녀를 마음에 두지 않는다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."]], [["The ball proceeded pleasantly.", "무도회는 즐겁게 진행되었다."], ["The anxious cares, the incessant attentions of Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인의 노심초사한 보살핌과 쉼 없는 마음 씀이 헛되지 않았다."], ["Weston, were not thrown away.", "모두가 행복해 보였다."], ["Every body seemed happy; and the praise of being a delightful ball, which is seldom bestowed till after a ball has ceased to be, was repeatedly given in the very beginning of the existence of this.", "무도회가 다 끝나고서야 좀처럼 주어지는 법인 \"즐거운 무도회\"라는 칭찬이, 이 무도회가 막 시작된 시점부터 거듭 쏟아졌다. 아주 중요하고 기록해 둘 만한 사건으로 말하자면, 이런 모임에서 으레 그렇듯 이 무도회도 그리 풍성하지는 않았다."], ["Of very important, very recordable events, it was not more productive than such meetings usually are.", "그러나 엠마가 무언가 의미 있다고 여긴 일이 하나 있었다. 만찬 전 마지막 두 춤이 시작되었는데, 해리엇에게 짝이 없었던 것이다 — 앉아 있는 유일한 아가씨였다."], ["There was one, however, which Emma thought something of.—The two last dances before supper were begun, and Harriet had no partner;—the only young lady sitting down;—and so equal had been hitherto the number of dancers, that how there could be any one disengaged was the wonder!—But Emma’s wonder lessened soon afterwards, on seeing Mr.", "게다가 지금껏 춤추는 이들의 수가 어찌나 딱 맞아떨어졌던지, 어떻게 짝 없는 사람이 있을 수 있는지 의아할 지경이었다! 그러나 엠마의 의아함은 곧, 어슬렁거리는 미스터 엘턴을 보면서 잦아들었다."], ["Elton sauntering about.", "피할 수만 있다면 그는 해리엇에게 춤을 청하지 않을 터였다."], ["He would not ask Harriet to dance if it were possible to be avoided: she was sure he would not—and she was expecting him every moment to escape into the card-room.", "그녀는 분명히 그러리라 확신했고 — 그가 금방이라도 카드 방으로 달아나 버리기를 시시각각 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Escape, however, was not his plan.", "그러나 달아나는 것은 그의 계획이 아니었다."], ["He came to the part of the room where the sitters-by were collected, spoke to some, and walked about in front of them, as if to shew his liberty, and his resolution of maintaining it.", "그는 앉아 있는 이들이 모여 있는 쪽으로 와서 몇 사람과 말을 나누고, 그들 앞을 거닐었다 — 마치 제 자유로움을, 그리고 그것을 지키겠다는 결의를 과시하려는 듯이."], ["He did not omit being sometimes directly before Miss Smith, or speaking to those who were close to her.—Emma saw it.", "그는 더러 미스 스미스의 바로 앞에 서기도 하고, 그녀 가까이에 있는 이들과 말을 섞기도 하는 것을 빠뜨리지 않았다."], ["She was not yet dancing; she was working her way up from the bottom, and had therefore leisure to look around, and by only turning her head a little she saw it all.", "엠마는 그것을 보았다. 그녀는 아직 춤을 추지 않고, 줄의 맨 아래에서 위로 올라오는 차례를 밟던 중이라 둘러볼 여유가 있었고, 고개를 조금만 돌려도 그 모든 것이 보였다."], ["When she was half-way up the set, the whole group were exactly behind her, and she would no longer allow her eyes to watch; but Mr.", "줄의 절반쯤 올라왔을 때 그 무리 전체가 정확히 그녀 등 뒤에 있게 되어 더는 눈으로 지켜보지 않기로 했다."], ["Elton was so near, that she heard every syllable of a dialogue which just then took place between him and Mrs.", "그러나 미스터 엘턴이 어찌나 가까이 있었던지, 바로 그때 그와 웨스턴 부인 사이에 오간 대화의 한 마디 한 마디가 다 들렸다."], ["Weston; and she perceived that his wife, who was standing immediately above her, was not only listening also, but even encouraging him by significant glances.—The kind-hearted, gentle Mrs.", "그리고 바로 위쪽에 서 있던 그의 아내가 함께 듣기만 하는 것이 아니라 의미심장한 눈짓으로 그를 부추기기까지 한다는 것도 알아차렸다."], ["Weston had left her seat to join him and say, “Do not you dance, Mr. Elton?”", "마음씨 곱고 다정한 웨스턴 부인이 자리에서 일어나 그에게로 가서 말했다."], ["to which his prompt reply was, “Most readily, Mrs.", "\"춤 안 추세요, 미스터 엘턴?\" 그러자 그가 선뜻 대답했다."], ["Weston, if you will dance with me.”", "\"더없이 기꺼이요, 웨스턴 부인, 부인이 저와 추어 주신다면요.\""]], [["“Me!—oh!", "\"저요!"], ["no—I would get you a better partner than myself.", "— 어머! 아니에요 — 저보다 나은 짝을 구해 드릴게요."], ["I am no dancer.”", "저는 춤을 못 춰서요.\""]], [["“If Mrs.", "\"미시즈 길버트가 추고 싶어 하신다면,\""], ["Gilbert wishes to dance,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I shall have great pleasure, I am sure—for, though beginning to feel myself rather an old married man, and that my dancing days are over, it would give me very great pleasure at any time to stand up with an old friend like Mrs. Gilbert.”", "\"정말 큰 기쁨이겠지요 — 제가 이제 슬슬 늙은 유부남이 다 되어 가는 데다 춤출 날들도 다 지난 듯하긴 합니다만, 미시즈 길버트 같은 오랜 벗과 추는 일이라면 언제든 무척 기쁠 테니까요.\""]], [["“Mrs. Gilbert does not mean to dance, but there is a young lady disengaged whom I should be very glad to see dancing—Miss Smith.”", "\"미시즈 길버트는 춤출 생각이 없어요."], ["“Miss Smith!—oh!—I had not observed.—You are extremely obliging—and if I were not an old married man.—But my dancing days are over, Mrs.", "하지만 짝 없는 아가씨가 한 분 있는데, 그분이 춤추는 걸 보면 참 좋겠어요 — 미스 스미스요.\" \"미스 스미스라고요!"], ["Weston.", "— 아! — 못 봤네요."], ["You will excuse me.", "— 정말 친절하시군요 — 제가 늙은 유부남만 아니라면야. — 하지만 제 춤출 날들은 다 지났답니다, 웨스턴 부인."], ["Any thing else I should be most happy to do, at your command—but my dancing days are over.”", "양해해 주세요. 그 밖에 무엇이든 분부만 하시면 더없이 기꺼이 하겠지만 — 제 춤출 날들은 다 지났어요.\""]], [["Mrs. Weston said no more; and Emma could imagine with what surprize and mortification she must be returning to her seat.", "웨스턴 부인은 더 말하지 않았다."], ["This was Mr. Elton!", "그녀가 얼마나 놀라고 무안한 심정으로 제자리로 돌아갔을지 엠마는 짐작이 갔다."], ["the amiable, obliging, gentle Mr.", "이것이 바로 미스터 엘턴이었다!"], ["Elton.—She looked round for a moment; he had joined Mr.", "그 상냥하고 친절하고 점잖던 미스터 엘턴이! 그녀는 잠시 둘러보았다."], ["Knightley at a little distance, and was arranging himself for settled conversation, while smiles of high glee passed between him and his wife.", "그는 조금 떨어진 곳에서 미스터 나이틀리와 합류해 본격적인 대화를 나눌 채비를 하고 있었고, 그러는 사이 그와 그의 아내 사이에는 한껏 신난 미소가 오갔다."]], [["She would not look again.", "그녀는 다시 보지 않으려 했다."], ["Her heart was in a glow, and she feared her face might be as hot.", "가슴이 화끈 달아올랐고, 얼굴도 그만큼 화끈거릴까 봐 두려웠다."]], [["In another moment a happier sight caught her;—Mr.", "다음 순간, 더 행복한 광경이 그녀의 눈을 사로잡았다 — 미스터 나이틀리가 해리엇을 줄로 이끌고 있었던 것이다!"], ["Knightley leading Harriet to the set!—Never had she been more surprized, seldom more delighted, than at that instant.", "그 순간만큼 그녀가 더 놀란 적도, 더 기뻤던 적도 좀처럼 없었다."], ["She was all pleasure and gratitude, both for Harriet and herself, and longed to be thanking him; and though too distant for speech, her countenance said much, as soon as she could catch his eye again.", "그녀는 해리엇을 위해서나 자신을 위해서나 온통 기쁨과 고마움뿐이었고, 어서 그에게 고마움을 전하고 싶었다. 말을 건네기에는 너무 멀리 있었지만, 다시 그의 눈길을 붙잡을 수 있게 되자마자 그녀의 표정이 많은 것을 말해 주었다."]], [["His dancing proved to be just what she had believed it, extremely good; and Harriet would have seemed almost too lucky, if it had not been for the cruel state of things before, and for the very complete enjoyment and very high sense of the distinction which her happy features announced.", "그의 춤 솜씨는 그녀가 믿어 온 그대로, 더없이 훌륭했다. 그리고 앞서의 그 모진 상황만 아니었다면, 또 그 행복한 표정으로 드러나는 더할 나위 없는 즐거움과 영광스러운 기분만 아니었다면, 해리엇은 거의 지나치게 운 좋은 사람처럼 보였을 것이다."], ["It was not thrown away on her, she bounded higher than ever, flew farther down the middle, and was in a continual course of smiles.", "그것은 결코 그녀에게서 헛되지 않았다. 해리엇은 그 어느 때보다 높이 뛰어올랐고, 가운데로 그 어느 때보다 멀리 날아갔으며, 줄곧 미소를 흘렸다."]], [["Mr. Elton had retreated into the card-room, looking (Emma trusted) very foolish.", "미스터 엘턴은 카드 방으로 물러나, (엠마가 믿기로는) 아주 멍청한 꼴이 되어 있었다."], ["She did not think he was quite so hardened as his wife, though growing very like her;—she spoke some of her feelings, by observing audibly to her partner,", "그가 그 아내만큼 철면피는 아니라고 그녀는 생각했지만, 점점 그 아내를 닮아 가고 있었다. 엘턴 부인은 자기 짝에게 들으라는 듯 이렇게 말하며 제 속내의 한 자락을 드러냈다."]], [["“Knightley has taken pity on poor little Miss Smith!—Very good-natured, I declare.”", "\"나이틀리가 가엾은 꼬마 미스 스미스를 딱하게 여겼나 봐요! — 정말이지 마음씨가 곱기도 하지.\""]], [["Supper was announced.", "만찬이 알려졌다."], ["The move began; and Miss Bates might be heard from that moment, without interruption, till her being seated at table and taking up her spoon.", "자리 이동이 시작되었다. 그리고 그 순간부터 미스 베이츠는, 식탁에 앉아 숟가락을 집어 들 때까지 끊김 없이 그 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“Jane, Jane, my dear Jane, where are you?—Here is your tippet.", "\"제인, 제인, 얘 제인아, 어디 있니? — 여기 네 어깨 망토 있다."], ["Mrs. Weston begs you to put on your tippet.", "웨스턴 부인이 어깨 망토를 꼭 두르라고 신신당부하시는구나."], ["She says she is afraid there will be draughts in the passage, though every thing has been done—One door nailed up—Quantities of matting—My dear Jane, indeed you must.", "복도에 외풍이 들까 걱정된다고, 손은 다 써 놨어도 말이다 — 문 하나는 못질을 해 막고 — 매트도 잔뜩 깔고 — 얘 제인아, 그러니 정말 꼭 둘러야 한다. 미스터 처칠, 어머!"], ["Mr. Churchill, oh!", "너무 친절하셔라!"], ["you are too obliging!", "어쩜 이렇게 잘 둘러 주시는지!"], ["How well you put it on!—so gratified!", "— 정말 고마워요! 춤도 정말 훌륭하셨고요!"], ["Excellent dancing indeed!—Yes, my dear, I ran home, as I said I should, to help grandmama to bed, and got back again, and nobody missed me.—I set off without saying a word, just as I told you. Grandmama was quite well, had a charming evening with Mr.", "— 그래, 얘야, 내가 말한 대로 집에 달려가서 할머니를 자리에 모셔다 드리고 다시 왔는데, 아무도 내가 없었던 걸 몰랐단다 — 말한 대로, 한마디 없이 슬쩍 다녀온 거지."], ["Woodhouse, a vast deal of chat, and backgammon.—Tea was made downstairs, biscuits and baked apples and wine before she came away: amazing luck in some of her throws: and she inquired a great deal about you, how you were amused, and who were your partners.", "할머니는 아주 평안하셨어, 미스터 우드하우스와 멋진 저녁을 보내셨다지 뭐냐, 두런두런 이야기도 잔뜩 하고, 주사위 놀이도 하고 — 떠나오기 전에 아래층에서 차도 끓이고, 비스킷에 구운 사과에 포도주까지 드셨대. 주사위를 던지면 어찌나 잘 나오던지 운이 기막혔다는구나."], ["‘Oh!’ said I, ‘I shall not forestall Jane; I left her dancing with Mr.", "그리고 너에 대해 잔뜩 물으시더라, 어떻게 즐겁게 지냈는지, 짝은 누구였는지. '어머!'"], ["George Otway; she will love to tell you all about it herself to-morrow: her first partner was Mr.", "했지, '제가 제인 자리를 가로채진 않을래요. 미스터 조지 오트웨이와 춤추는 거 보고 왔는데, 내일 자기 입으로 다 말씀드리고 싶을 거예요."], ["Elton, I do not know who will ask her next, perhaps Mr.", "첫 짝은 미스터 엘턴이었고, 다음은 누가 청할지 모르겠지만, 어쩌면 미스터 윌리엄 콕스일지도요.' 미스터 처칠, 너무 친절하셔라."], ["William Cox.’ My dear sir, you are too obliging.—Is there nobody you would not rather?—I am not helpless.", "— 차라리 다른 분이 더 좋지 않으세요? — 저는 부축이 필요한 사람이 아닌데요."], ["Sir, you are most kind.", "정말 친절도 하셔라."], ["Upon my word, Jane on one arm, and me on the other!—Stop, stop, let us stand a little back, Mrs.", "어머나, 한쪽 팔엔 제인을, 다른 쪽 팔엔 저를! — 잠깐, 잠깐, 조금 물러서요, 엘턴 부인이 가시잖아요."], ["Elton is going; dear Mrs.", "다정한 엘턴 부인, 어쩜 저리 우아하실까!"], ["Elton, how elegant she looks!—Beautiful lace!—Now we all follow in her train.", "— 아름다운 레이스 좀 봐! — 이제 우리 모두 그 뒤를 따라가는 거예요."], ["Quite the queen of the evening!—Well, here we are at the passage.", "정말 오늘 밤의 여왕이셔! — 자, 여기 복도네."], ["Two steps, Jane, take care of the two steps.", "계단 두 칸이다, 제인, 두 칸 조심하렴. 어머!"], ["Oh! no, there is but one.", "아니네, 한 칸뿐이야."], ["Well, I was persuaded there were two.", "이런, 분명 두 칸인 줄 알았는데."], ["How very odd!", "참 이상하기도 하지!"], ["I was convinced there were two, and there is but one.", "두 칸이라고 굳게 믿었는데 한 칸뿐이라니. 이런 안락함과 멋은 정말 처음 봐요 — 사방에 촛불이고요."], ["I never saw any thing equal to the comfort and style—Candles everywhere.—I was telling you of your grandmama, Jane,—There was a little disappointment.—The baked apples and biscuits, excellent in their way, you know; but there was a delicate fricassee of sweetbread and some asparagus brought in at first, and good Mr.", "— 제인, 네 할머니 이야기를 하던 중이었지 — 그런데 좀 서운한 일이 있었단다. — 구운 사과랑 비스킷이 그것대로야 훌륭하지, 알잖니."], ["Woodhouse, not thinking the asparagus quite boiled enough, sent it all out again.", "그런데 처음에 흉선 요리를 곱게 만든 프리카세하고 아스파라거스가 나왔거든. 한데 착한 미스터 우드하우스가 아스파라거스가 덜 삶긴 것 같다고 죄다 도로 물리신 거야."], ["Now there is nothing grandmama loves better than sweetbread and asparagus—so she was rather disappointed, but we agreed we would not speak of it to any body, for fear of its getting round to dear Miss Woodhouse, who would be so very much concerned!—Well, this is brilliant!", "그런데 할머니가 흉선이랑 아스파라거스보다 좋아하시는 게 없잖니 — 그래서 좀 서운해하셨지. 하지만 우리는 아무한테도 이 얘긴 않기로 했어, 행여 다정한 미스 우드하우스 귀에 들어가면 무척 마음 쓰실 테니까!"], ["I am all amazement!", "— 어머, 이거 정말 눈부셔!"], ["could not have supposed any thing!—Such elegance and profusion!—I have seen nothing like it since—Well, where shall we sit?", "너무 놀라워요! 이런 건 상상도 못 했어요!"], ["where shall we sit?", "— 이런 우아함에 이런 풍성함이라니!"], ["Anywhere, so that Jane is not in a draught.", "— 이런 건 정말 본 적이 없는데 — 자, 우린 어디 앉을까? 어디 앉을까?"], ["Where I sit is of no consequence. Oh!", "아무 데나요, 제인만 외풍을 안 맞으면요."], ["do you recommend this side?—Well, I am sure, Mr.", "제가 어디 앉느냐는 아무 상관없어요. 어머!"], ["Churchill—only it seems too good—but just as you please.", "이쪽을 권하시는 거예요? — 글쎄요, 정말이지, 미스터 처칠 — 너무 좋은 자리 같긴 한데 — 하지만 좋으실 대로요."], ["What you direct in this house cannot be wrong.", "이 댁에서 당신이 정해 주시는 거라면 틀릴 리가 없죠."], ["Dear Jane, how shall we ever recollect half the dishes for grandmama?", "얘 제인아, 우리가 할머니께 이 요리들의 절반인들 어떻게 다 기억해 전해 드릴 수 있을까?"], ["Soup too! Bless me!", "수프까지 있네!"], ["I should not be helped so soon, but it smells most excellent, and I cannot help beginning.”", "어머나! 이렇게 빨리 받으면 안 되는데, 냄새가 너무 좋아서, 도저히 안 먹을 수가 없네요.\""]], [["Emma had no opportunity of speaking to Mr.", "엠마는 만찬이 끝날 때까지 미스터 나이틀리와 이야기할 기회가 없었다."], ["Knightley till after supper; but, when they were all in the ballroom again, her eyes invited him irresistibly to come to her and be thanked.", "그러나 모두 다시 무도회장에 모이자, 그녀의 눈은 그가 자기에게 와서 고맙다는 인사를 받도록 거부할 수 없이 그를 청했다."], ["He was warm in his reprobation of Mr.", "그는 미스터 엘턴의 처사를 격하게 나무랐다."], ["Elton’s conduct; it had been unpardonable rudeness; and Mrs.", "그것은 용서할 수 없는 무례였다."], ["Elton’s looks also received the due share of censure.", "그리고 엘턴 부인의 그 표정 또한 마땅한 몫의 비난을 받았다."]], [["“They aimed at wounding more than Harriet,”", "\"그들이 노린 건 해리엇만 상처 입히려던 게 아니었네.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Emma, why is it that they are your enemies?”", "\"엠마, 어째서 그들이 자네의 적이 된 거지?\""]], [["He looked with smiling penetration; and, on receiving no answer, added, “She ought not to be angry with you, I suspect, whatever he may be.—To that surmise, you say nothing, of course; but confess, Emma, that you did want him to marry Harriet.”", "그는 꿰뚫어 보는 듯한 미소를 지으며 바라보았고, 아무 대답이 없자 덧붙였다. \"그자야 어떻든, 그 부인이 자네에게 화낼 까닭은 없을 것 같은데. 그 짐작에 자네야 물론 아무 말 않겠지. 하지만 엠마, 솔직히 인정하게나, 자네가 그를 해리엇과 맺어 주고 싶어 했다는 걸.\""]], [["“I did,”", "\"그랬어요.\""], ["replied Emma, “and they cannot forgive me.”", "엠마가 대답했다. \"그래서 그들이 저를 용서 못 하는 거예요.\""]], [["He shook his head; but there was a smile of indulgence with it, and he only said,", "그는 고개를 가로저었으나, 거기엔 너그러운 미소가 어려 있었고, 그저 이렇게만 말했다."]], [["“I shall not scold you.", "\"꾸짖진 않겠네."], ["I leave you to your own reflections.”", "자네 자신의 반성에 맡겨 두지.\""]], [["“Can you trust me with such flatterers?—Does my vain spirit ever tell me I am wrong?”", "\"이렇게 비위만 맞춰 주는 것들에게 저를 맡겨도 되겠어요? — 제 허영심이 제게 틀렸다고 일러 줄 일이 한 번이라도 있겠어요?\""]], [["“Not your vain spirit, but your serious spirit.—If one leads you wrong, I am sure the other tells you of it.”", "\"자네 허영심이야 아니지. 하지만 자네의 진지한 마음이라면. — 한쪽이 자네를 그릇 이끌면, 다른 한쪽이 분명 그걸 일러 줄 걸세.\""]], [["“I do own myself to have been completely mistaken in Mr. Elton.", "\"미스터 엘턴에 대해서는 제가 완전히 잘못 봤다는 걸 인정해요."], ["There is a littleness about him which you discovered, and which I did not: and I was fully convinced of his being in love with Harriet.", "당신이 알아채고 저는 못 알아챈 그 옹졸함이 그에게 있더군요. 그리고 저는 그가 해리엇을 사랑한다고 철석같이 믿었고요."], ["It was through a series of strange blunders!”", "그게 다 일련의 묘한 착오들 탓이었어요!\""]], [["“And, in return for your acknowledging so much, I will do you the justice to say, that you would have chosen for him better than he has chosen for himself.—Harriet Smith has some first-rate qualities, which Mrs.", "\"그렇게나 많이 인정해 준 데 대한 답례로, 나도 자네에게 공평하게 한마디 하지. 자네라면 그가 제 손으로 고른 것보다 더 나은 짝을 골라 주었으리란 걸 말일세."], ["Elton is totally without.", "— 해리엇 스미스에게는 엘턴 부인에게 전혀 없는 일류의 자질이 몇 가지 있어."], ["An unpretending, single-minded, artless girl—infinitely to be preferred by any man of sense and taste to such a woman as Mrs. Elton.", "꾸밈없고, 한결같고, 천진한 아가씨지 — 분별과 안목이 있는 남자라면 누구든 엘턴 부인 같은 여자보다 한없이 더 좋아할 만한 사람이야."], ["I found Harriet more conversable than I expected.”", "해리엇은 내가 생각했던 것보다 말이 잘 통하더군.\""]], [["Emma was extremely gratified.—They were interrupted by the bustle of Mr.", "엠마는 더없이 흐뭇했다."], ["Weston calling on every body to begin dancing again.", "두 사람의 대화는 미스터 웨스턴이 모두에게 다시 춤을 추자고 외쳐 대는 소란에 끊겼다."]], [["“Come Miss Woodhouse, Miss Otway, Miss Fairfax, what are you all doing?—Come Emma, set your companions the example.", "\"자, 미스 우드하우스, 미스 오트웨이, 미스 페어팩스, 다들 뭣들 하고 계세요? — 자, 엠마, 동무들에게 본을 보여 줘요."], ["Every body is lazy!", "다들 게을러 빠졌군요!"], ["Every body is asleep!”", "다들 잠들었어요!\""]], [["“I am ready,”", "\"전 준비됐어요.\""], ["said Emma, “whenever I am wanted.”", "엠마가 말했다. \"부르시기만 하면요.\""]], [["“Whom are you going to dance with?”", "\"누구랑 춤출 건가?\""], ["asked Mr. Knightley.", "미스터 나이틀리가 물었다."]], [["She hesitated a moment, and then replied, “With you, if you will ask me.”", "그녀는 잠시 머뭇거리다가 대답했다. \"당신과요, 청해 주신다면요.\""]], [["“Will you?”", "\"추겠나?\""], ["said he, offering his hand.", "그가 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Indeed I will.", "\"그럼요."], ["You have shewn that you can dance, and you know we are not really so much brother and sister as to make it at all improper.”", "당신이 춤출 줄 안다는 걸 보여 주셨잖아요. 게다가 우리가 사실 그렇게까지 오누이 사이는 아니니, 조금도 흠 될 게 없다는 거 아시잖아요.\""]], [["“Brother and sister!", "\"오누이라!"], ["no, indeed.”", "아무렴, 천만에.\""]], [["# Volume III — Chapter III", "# 제3권 3장"]], [["This little explanation with Mr. Knightley gave Emma considerable pleasure.", "미스터 나이틀리와 나눈 이 작은 해명은 엠마에게 적잖은 즐거움을 안겨 주었다."], ["It was one of the agreeable recollections of the ball, which she walked about the lawn the next morning to enjoy.—She was extremely glad that they had come to so good an understanding respecting the Eltons, and that their opinions of both husband and wife were so much alike; and his praise of Harriet, his concession in her favour, was peculiarly gratifying.", "그것은 무도회의 기분 좋은 기억 가운데 하나여서, 이튿날 아침 그녀는 그 기억을 음미하려고 잔디밭을 거닐었다. 엘턴 부부에 관해 두 사람이 그토록 잘 통하는 결론에 이르렀다는 것이, 남편과 아내 양쪽에 대한 견해가 그토록 일치한다는 것이 그녀는 더없이 기뻤다."], ["The impertinence of the Eltons, which for a few minutes had threatened to ruin the rest of her evening, had been the occasion of some of its highest satisfactions; and she looked forward to another happy result—the cure of Harriet’s infatuation.—From Harriet’s manner of speaking of the circumstance before they quitted the ballroom, she had strong hopes.", "그리고 미스터 나이틀리가 해리엇을 칭찬해 준 것, 해리엇 편을 들어 양보해 준 것은 유독 흐뭇한 일이었다. 잠깐 동안 저녁의 나머지를 망쳐 놓을 듯 위협했던 엘턴 부부의 무례함이, 오히려 그날 저녁의 가장 큰 만족 가운데 몇 가지를 안겨 준 계기가 된 것이다."], ["It seemed as if her eyes were suddenly opened, and she were enabled to see that Mr.", "게다가 그녀는 또 하나의 행복한 결과를 — 해리엇의 헛된 연정이 치유되리라는 것을 — 내다보고 있었다."], ["Elton was not the superior creature she had believed him.", "무도회장을 나서기 전 해리엇이 그 일을 이야기하던 태도로 보아, 그녀에게는 강한 희망이 있었다."], ["The fever was over, and Emma could harbour little fear of the pulse being quickened again by injurious courtesy.", "마치 해리엇의 눈이 갑자기 뜨여, 미스터 엘턴이 자기가 믿어 온 만큼 빼어난 사람은 아니라는 것을 알아볼 수 있게 된 듯했다."], ["She depended on the evil feelings of the Eltons for supplying all the discipline of pointed neglect that could be farther requisite.—Harriet rational, Frank Churchill not too much in love, and Mr.", "열병은 지나갔으니, 해로운 친절로 그 맥박이 다시 빨라질까 봐 엠마가 두려워할 일은 거의 없었다. 그 이상으로 필요할지 모를, 따끔한 냉대라는 가르침은 엘턴 부부의 고약한 심사가 알아서 마련해 주리라고 그녀는 믿었다."], ["Knightley not wanting to quarrel with her, how very happy a summer must be before her!", "해리엇은 제정신을 차렸고, 프랭크 처칠은 사랑에 지나치게 빠져 있지 않으며, 미스터 나이틀리는 그녀와 다툴 마음이 없으니, 앞으로의 여름이 얼마나 행복할 것인가!"]], [["She was not to see Frank Churchill this morning.", "그날 아침에는 프랭크 처칠을 보지 못하게 되어 있었다."], ["He had told her that he could not allow himself the pleasure of stopping at Hartfield, as he was to be at home by the middle of the day.", "그는 한낮까지는 집에 들어가야 하기에 하트필드에 들르는 즐거움을 누릴 수 없겠다고 그녀에게 말해 두었다."], ["She did not regret it.", "그녀는 그것을 아쉬워하지 않았다."]], [["Having arranged all these matters, looked them through, and put them all to rights, she was just turning to the house with spirits freshened up for the demands of the two little boys, as well as of their grandpapa, when the great iron sweep-gate opened, and two persons entered whom she had never less expected to see together—Frank Churchill, with Harriet leaning on his arm—actually Harriet!—A moment sufficed to convince her that something extraordinary had happened.", "이 모든 일을 정리하고, 두루 살펴보고, 모두 제자리에 놓고 나서, 두 어린 사내아이의 성화와 그 외할아버지의 요구를 감당할 만큼 기운을 새로이 추스른 채 막 집 쪽으로 돌아서려던 참에, 커다란 철 대문이 열리며 두 사람이 들어섰다 — 함께 있으리라고는 도무지 예상치 못했던 두 사람이었다 — 프랭크 처칠과, 그의 팔에 기댄 해리엇 — 정말로 해리엇이었다! 무언가 심상치 않은 일이 일어났다는 것을 깨닫는 데는 한순간으로 충분했다."], ["Harriet looked white and frightened, and he was trying to cheer her.—The iron gates and the front-door were not twenty yards asunder;—they were all three soon in the hall, and Harriet immediately sinking into a chair fainted away.", "해리엇은 얼굴이 하얗게 질려 겁에 질려 있었고, 그는 그녀를 달래려 애쓰고 있었다. 철 대문과 현관문 사이는 스무 야드도 채 안 되었으므로, 세 사람은 곧 현관 안으로 들어섰고, 해리엇은 그 즉시 의자에 풀썩 주저앉으며 기절하고 말았다."]], [["A young lady who faints, must be recovered; questions must be answered, and surprizes be explained.", "기절한 젊은 아가씨는 깨워야 하고, 물음에는 답해야 하며, 놀라운 일은 설명되어야 한다."], ["Such events are very interesting, but the suspense of them cannot last long.", "그런 사건은 무척 흥미롭지만, 그 조마조마함이 오래갈 수는 없다."], ["A few minutes made Emma acquainted with the whole.", "몇 분 만에 엠마는 사건의 전말을 알게 되었다."]], [["Miss Smith, and Miss Bickerton, another parlour boarder at Mrs.", "미스 스미스와, 무도회에도 와 있었던 고더드 부인 댁의 또 다른 기숙생 미스 비커튼은 함께 산책을 나가, 한 길로 — 리치먼드로 가는 길로 — 접어들었는데, 그 길은 겉보기에는 안전할 만큼 사람이 다니는 듯했으나 두 사람을 놀라게 하는 일로 이끌었다."], ["Goddard’s, who had been also at the ball, had walked out together, and taken a road, the Richmond road, which, though apparently public enough for safety, had led them into alarm.—About half a mile beyond Highbury, making a sudden turn, and deeply shaded by elms on each side, it became for a considerable stretch very retired; and when the young ladies had advanced some way into it, they had suddenly perceived at a small distance before them, on a broader patch of greensward by the side, a party of gipsies.", "하이베리에서 반 마일쯤 벗어난 곳에서 길은 갑작스레 굽이졌고, 양쪽으로 느릅나무가 짙게 그늘을 드리워 꽤 긴 구간이 무척 외졌다. 두 아가씨가 그 길로 어느 정도 들어섰을 때, 바로 앞 조금 떨어진 곳, 길가의 더 너른 잔디밭에서 한 무리의 집시*를 별안간 발견했다."], ["A child on the watch, came towards them to beg; and Miss Bickerton, excessively frightened, gave a great scream, and calling on Harriet to follow her, ran up a steep bank, cleared a slight hedge at the top, and made the best of her way by a short cut back to Highbury.", "망을 보던 한 아이가 구걸하러 두 사람에게 다가왔다. 잔뜩 겁먹은 미스 비커튼은 크게 비명을 지르고는 해리엇에게 따라오라 외치며 가파른 둑 위로 뛰어올라, 꼭대기의 나지막한 산울타리를 넘어, 지름길로 하이베리로 돌아가려고 죽을힘을 다해 달아났다."], ["But poor Harriet could not follow.", "그러나 가엾은 해리엇은 따라갈 수가 없었다. 춤을 춘 뒤로 쥐가 심하게 났던 터라, 둑을 오르려 처음 시도하자마자 다시 쥐가 도져 도무지 꼼짝할 수 없는 지경이 된 것이다."], ["She had suffered very much from cramp after dancing, and her first attempt to mount the bank brought on such a return of it as made her absolutely powerless—and in this state, and exceedingly terrified, she had been obliged to remain.", "이런 상태로, 몹시 겁에 질린 채, 그녀는 그 자리에 남아 있을 수밖에 없었다. * 집시: 19세기 영국에서 떠돌이 생활을 하던 사람들로, 당시엔 흔히 경계와 멸시의 대상이 되었다."]], [["How the trampers might have behaved, had the young ladies been more courageous, must be doubtful; but such an invitation for attack could not be resisted; and Harriet was soon assailed by half a dozen children, headed by a stout woman and a great boy, all clamorous, and impertinent in look, though not absolutely in word.—More and more frightened, she immediately promised them money, and taking out her purse, gave them a shilling, and begged them not to want more, or to use her ill.—She was then able to walk, though but slowly, and was moving away—but her terror and her purse were too tempting, and she was followed, or rather surrounded, by the whole gang, demanding more.", "두 아가씨가 좀 더 용감했더라면 그 떠돌이들이 어떻게 굴었을지는 알 수 없는 노릇이다. 그러나 그렇게 공격을 부추기는 빌미를 그들이 그냥 넘길 리 없었으니, 해리엇은 이내 대여섯 명의 아이들에게 둘러싸여 시달리게 되었다. 우두머리는 건장한 여인과 큰 사내아이였고, 모두가 떠들썩하니 눈빛이 무례했다 — 입으로 대놓고 욕한 것은 아니었지만. 점점 더 겁에 질린 해리엇은 곧장 돈을 주겠다고 약속하고는 지갑을 꺼내 1실링을 쥐여 주며, 더는 바라지 말고 자기를 해치지 말아 달라고 빌었다. 그제야 그녀는, 비록 더디게나마 걸을 수 있어 그 자리를 벗어나려 했다 — 그러나 그녀의 공포와 그녀의 지갑은 너무도 유혹적이어서, 더 달라고 요구하는 무리 전체가 그녀를 뒤따랐다, 아니 오히려 에워쌌다."]], [["In this state Frank Churchill had found her, she trembling and conditioning, they loud and insolent.", "프랭크 처칠이 그녀를 발견한 것은 바로 이런 상황에서였으니, 그녀는 벌벌 떨며 흥정하고 있었고, 그들은 큰소리로 무례하게 굴고 있었다."], ["By a most fortunate chance his leaving Highbury had been delayed so as to bring him to her assistance at this critical moment.", "더없이 다행스러운 우연으로, 하이베리를 떠나는 일이 미뤄진 덕에 그가 이 절체절명의 순간에 그녀를 도우러 온 것이었다."], ["The pleasantness of the morning had induced him to walk forward, and leave his horses to meet him by another road, a mile or two beyond Highbury—and happening to have borrowed a pair of scissors the night before of Miss Bates, and to have forgotten to restore them, he had been obliged to stop at her door, and go in for a few minutes: he was therefore later than he had intended; and being on foot, was unseen by the whole party till almost close to them.", "아침이 하도 화창하여 그는 걸어서 먼저 가고, 말은 다른 길로 하여 하이베리에서 일이 마일 벗어난 곳에서 자기를 맞도록 해 둔 참이었다 — 게다가 전날 밤 미스 베이츠에게 가위 한 자루를 빌렸다가 돌려주는 것을 깜빡한 탓에, 그 집 문 앞에 멈춰 잠깐 들어갔다 와야 했다. 그래서 그는 작정했던 것보다 늦어졌고, 걸어서 온 까닭에 거의 코앞에 이르도록 무리 전체에게 들키지 않았다."], ["The terror which the woman and boy had been creating in Harriet was then their own portion.", "그 여인과 사내아이가 해리엇에게 안겨 주던 공포가 이번에는 고스란히 그들 차지가 되었다."], ["He had left them completely frightened; and Harriet eagerly clinging to him, and hardly able to speak, had just strength enough to reach Hartfield, before her spirits were quite overcome.", "그가 떠나올 때 그들은 완전히 겁에 질려 있었다. 해리엇은 그에게 간절히 매달려 말조차 거의 못 하면서도, 기운이 아주 다하기 전에 하트필드까지 다다를 만큼의 힘은 겨우 남아 있었다."], ["It was his idea to bring her to Hartfield: he had thought of no other place.", "그녀를 하트필드로 데려온 것은 그의 생각이었으니, 그는 다른 곳은 떠올리지 못했던 것이다."]], [["This was the amount of the whole story,—of his communication and of Harriet’s as soon as she had recovered her senses and speech.—He dared not stay longer than to see her well; these several delays left him not another minute to lose; and Emma engaging to give assurance of her safety to Mrs.", "이것이 이야기 전체의 — 그가 전해 준 것과, 해리엇이 정신과 말을 되찾자마자 들려준 것의 — 골자였다. 그는 그녀가 무사한 것을 확인하는 데 필요한 시간 이상은 머물 엄두를 내지 못했다."], ["Goddard, and notice of there being such a set of people in the neighbourhood to Mr.", "이런저런 지체들로 그에게는 더 허비할 일 분의 여유도 없었다."], ["Knightley, he set off, with all the grateful blessings that she could utter for her friend and herself.", "엠마가 고더드 부인에게는 해리엇이 안전하다고 알리고, 미스터 나이틀리에게는 그런 무리가 인근에 있다는 것을 알려 주겠다고 약속하자, 그는 해리엇과 자신을 위해 그녀가 입에 담을 수 있는 온갖 고마운 축복의 말을 들으며 길을 나섰다."]], [["Such an adventure as this,—a fine young man and a lovely young woman thrown together in such a way, could hardly fail of suggesting certain ideas to the coldest heart and the steadiest brain.", "이와 같은 모험은 — 훌륭한 젊은 남자와 사랑스러운 젊은 여자가 그런 식으로 한데 던져진 일은 — 아무리 차가운 가슴과 흔들림 없는 머리라도 어떤 생각을 떠올리지 않을 수 없는 법이다. 적어도 엠마는 그렇게 생각했다."], ["So Emma thought, at least.", "그녀가 본 것을 보았다면, 두 사람이 함께 나타난 모습을 목격하고 그 사연을 들었다면, 어학자인들, 문법학자인들, 아니 수학자인들, 두 사람이 서로에게 유독 흥미로운 사이가 되도록 정황이 작용해 왔다고 느끼지 않을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Could a linguist, could a grammarian, could even a mathematician have seen what she did, have witnessed their appearance together, and heard their history of it, without feeling that circumstances had been at work to make them peculiarly interesting to each other?—How much more must an imaginist, like herself, be on fire with speculation and foresight!—especially with such a groundwork of anticipation as her mind had already made.", "하물며 그녀처럼 공상가인 사람이라면, 추측과 예견으로 얼마나 더 활활 타오르겠는가! 더구나 그녀의 머릿속에는 이미 그토록 든든한 기대의 밑바탕이 마련되어 있었으니 말이다."]], [["It was a very extraordinary thing!", "참으로 별난 일이었다! 그녀의 기억으로는, 이 고장의 어떤 젊은 아가씨에게도 이런 종류의 일이 일어난 적이 없었다."], ["Nothing of the sort had ever occurred before to any young ladies in the place, within her memory; no rencontre, no alarm of the kind;—and now it had happened to the very person, and at the very hour, when the other very person was chancing to pass by to rescue her!—It certainly was very extraordinary!—And knowing, as she did, the favourable state of mind of each at this period, it struck her the more.", "이런 종류의 마주침도, 놀람도 없었다. 그런데 이제 그 일이, 바로 그 사람에게, 바로 그 시각에 일어난 것이다 — 마침 그 다른 한 사람이 그녀를 구하려고 그곳을 지나가던 바로 그때에!"], ["He was wishing to get the better of his attachment to herself, she just recovering from her mania for Mr.", "정말이지 너무도 별난 일이었다! 게다가 그녀가 알고 있는 대로, 이 무렵 두 사람의 마음 상태가 다 같이 호의적이었던 터라 그 일은 그녀에게 더욱 강렬하게 다가왔다."], ["Elton. It seemed as if every thing united to promise the most interesting consequences.", "그는 그녀 자신에게 품은 연정을 극복하려 애쓰는 중이었고, 그녀는 미스터 엘턴을 향한 열병에서 막 회복하는 중이었다."], ["It was not possible that the occurrence should not be strongly recommending each to the other.", "마치 모든 것이 하나로 어우러져 가장 흥미로운 결말을 약속하는 듯했다. 이 사건이 두 사람을 서로에게 강하게 권하지 않는다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이었다."]], [["In the few minutes’ conversation which she had yet had with him, while Harriet had been partially insensible, he had spoken of her terror, her naïveté, her fervour as she seized and clung to his arm, with a sensibility amused and delighted; and just at last, after Harriet’s own account had been given, he had expressed his indignation at the abominable folly of Miss Bickerton in the warmest terms.", "해리엇이 잠시 의식이 흐릿했던 동안 그와 나눈 몇 분간의 대화에서, 그는 그녀의 공포를, 그녀의 순진함을, 자기 팔을 붙들고 매달리던 그 절절함을 이야기했는데, 즐거워하고 흐뭇해하는 감수성이 어려 있었다. 그리고 마지막에, 해리엇 본인의 이야기가 끝난 뒤에는, 미스 비커튼의 그 가증스러운 어리석음에 대해 더없이 격한 말로 분개를 드러냈다."], ["Every thing was to take its natural course, however, neither impelled nor assisted.", "그러나 모든 것은 떠밀지도 거들지도 말고 자연스러운 물길을 따라가게 두어야 했다."], ["She would not stir a step, nor drop a hint.", "그녀는 한 발짝도 움직이지 않을 것이며, 한마디 귀띔도 흘리지 않을 것이었다."], ["No, she had had enough of interference.", "안 돼, 끼어드는 일이라면 이제 진절머리가 났다."], ["There could be no harm in a scheme, a mere passive scheme.", "계획을 품는 것쯤이야 — 그저 수동적인 계획이라면 — 해로울 게 없었다."], ["It was no more than a wish.", "그것은 한낱 바람에 지나지 않았다."], ["Beyond it she would on no account proceed.", "그 이상으로는 결코 나아가지 않을 작정이었다."]], [["Emma’s first resolution was to keep her father from the knowledge of what had passed,—aware of the anxiety and alarm it would occasion: but she soon felt that concealment must be impossible.", "엠마의 첫 번째 결심은 아버지가 이 일을 알지 못하게 하는 것이었다 — 그것이 얼마나 큰 근심과 놀람을 불러일으킬지 알았기에. 그러나 감추는 일이 불가능하리라는 것을 그녀는 곧 깨달았다."], ["Within half an hour it was known all over Highbury.", "반 시간도 못 되어 하이베리 전체에 소문이 퍼졌다."], ["It was the very event to engage those who talk most, the young and the low; and all the youth and servants in the place were soon in the happiness of frightful news.", "그것은 말 많은 사람들, 곧 젊은 축과 신분 낮은 축의 마음을 사로잡기에 꼭 알맞은 사건이었으니, 이 고장의 젊은이며 하인이며 할 것 없이 모두가 곧 그 무시무시한 소식의 행복에 젖었다."], ["The last night’s ball seemed lost in the gipsies.", "지난밤의 무도회는 집시들 속에 묻혀 버린 듯했다."], ["Poor Mr. Woodhouse trembled as he sat, and, as Emma had foreseen, would scarcely be satisfied without their promising never to go beyond the shrubbery again.", "가엾은 미스터 우드하우스는 앉은 채로 부들부들 떨었고, 엠마가 예상했던 대로, 다시는 관목 숲 밖으로 나가지 않겠다는 약속을 받기 전에는 좀처럼 진정하지 못했다."], ["It was some comfort to him that many inquiries after himself and Miss Woodhouse (for his neighbours knew that he loved to be inquired after), as well as Miss Smith, were coming in during the rest of the day; and he had the pleasure of returning for answer, that they were all very indifferent—which, though not exactly true, for she was perfectly well, and Harriet not much otherwise, Emma would not interfere with.", "그날 남은 시간 내내 자신과 미스 우드하우스의 안부를 (이웃들은 그가 안부를 받는 것을 좋아한다는 것을 알았다), 그리고 미스 스미스의 안부를 묻는 문의가 줄을 이은 것은 그에게 얼마간 위안이 되었다. 게다가 그는 다들 아주 시원찮다고 답하는 즐거움까지 누렸다 — 사실과 딱 맞는 말은 아니었지만, 엠마는 아주 멀쩡했고 해리엇도 별반 다르지 않았으니까 — 그래도 엠마는 거기에 끼어들지 않았다."], ["She had an unhappy state of health in general for the child of such a man, for she hardly knew what indisposition was; and if he did not invent illnesses for her, she could make no figure in a message.", "그런 분의 자식으로서는 딱하게도 그녀는 대체로 너무나 건강한 처지였으니, 몸이 편찮다는 게 무엇인지 도통 모르고 살았기 때문이다. 그러니 아버지가 그녀를 위해 병을 지어내 주지 않으면, 그녀는 안부 전언 속에서 변변한 구실도 못 할 판이었다."]], [["The gipsies did not wait for the operations of justice; they took themselves off in a hurry.", "집시들은 사법의 손길이 미치기를 기다리지 않았다. 그들은 부랴부랴 떠나 버렸다."], ["The young ladies of Highbury might have walked again in safety before their panic began, and the whole history dwindled soon into a matter of little importance but to Emma and her nephews:—in her imagination it maintained its ground, and Henry and John were still asking every day for the story of Harriet and the gipsies, and still tenaciously setting her right if she varied in the slightest particular from the original recital.", "하이베리의 젊은 아가씨들은 그 공포가 시작되기도 전에 다시 안전하게 산책할 수 있었을 것이고, 그 모든 이야기는 이내 엠마와 그녀의 조카들에게만 의미 있는, 대단찮은 일로 쪼그라들었다. 그러나 그녀의 상상 속에서는 그것이 여전히 자리를 지켰고, 헨리와 존은 날마다 해리엇과 집시들 이야기를 해 달라고 졸랐으며, 그녀가 처음 들려준 이야기에서 아주 사소한 대목이라도 어긋나면 끈질기게 바로잡아 주었다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter IV", "# 제3권 4장"]], [["A very few days had passed after this adventure, when Harriet came one morning to Emma with a small parcel in her hand, and after sitting down and hesitating, thus began:", "이 작은 사건이 있고 며칠 지나지 않은 어느 날 아침, 해리엇이 손에 작은 꾸러미를 들고 엠마를 찾아왔다. 자리에 앉아 한참 머뭇거리던 그녀가 이렇게 입을 열었다."]], [["“Miss Woodhouse—if you are at leisure—I have something that I should like to tell you—a sort of confession to make—and then, you know, it will be over.”", "\"미스 우드하우스—혹시 시간 괜찮으시면—드리고 싶은 말씀이 있어요—일종의 고백 같은 거요—그러고 나면, 아시잖아요, 다 끝나는 거예요.\""]], [["Emma was a good deal surprized; but begged her to speak.", "엠마는 적잖이 놀랐지만, 어서 말해 보라고 청했다."], ["There was a seriousness in Harriet’s manner which prepared her, quite as much as her words, for something more than ordinary.", "해리엇의 태도에는 어떤 진지함이 서려 있어서, 말 못지않게 그 태도가 무언가 예사롭지 않은 일을 예감하게 했다."]], [["“It is my duty, and I am sure it is my wish,” she continued, “to have no reserves with you on this subject.", "\"이 일에 대해 당신께 아무것도 숨기지 않는 게 제 도리이고, 정말이지 제 바람이기도 해요.\""], ["As I am happily quite an altered creature in one respect, it is very fit that you should have the satisfaction of knowing it.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"다행히 어떤 점에서 저는 완전히 달라진 사람이 됐으니, 당신도 그걸 아시고 흐뭇해하셔야 마땅하잖아요."], ["I do not want to say more than is necessary—I am too much ashamed of having given way as I have done, and I dare say you understand me.”", "필요 이상으로 말하고 싶진 않아요—그동안 그렇게 휘둘렸던 게 너무 부끄러워서요. 그래도 제 말뜻은 아실 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["said Emma, “I hope I do.”", "엠마가 말했다. \"이해했으면 좋겠네요.\""]], [["“How I could so long a time be fancying myself!...”", "\"제가 어떻게 그렇게 오랫동안 헛된 마음을 품을 수 있었을까요!……\" 해리엇이 격하게 외쳤다."], ["cried Harriet, warmly.", "\"미친 짓이었던 것 같아요!"], ["“It seems like madness!", "이제는 그분에게서 특별한 구석이라곤 하나도 안 보여요.—그분을 마주치든 말든 상관없어요—다만 둘 중 굳이 고르라면 안 마주치는 쪽이 낫겠지만요—사실 그분을 피하려고 아무리 먼 길이라도 돌아갈 수 있어요—하지만 그분 부인은 조금도 부럽지 않아요. 예전처럼 그 사람을 우러러보지도, 부러워하지도 않고요."], ["I can see nothing at all extraordinary in him now.—I do not care whether I meet him or not—except that of the two I had rather not see him—and indeed I would go any distance round to avoid him—but I do not envy his wife in the least; I neither admire her nor envy her, as I have done: she is very charming, I dare say, and all that, but I think her very ill-tempered and disagreeable—I shall never forget her look the other night!—However, I assure you, Miss Woodhouse, I wish her no evil.—No, let them be ever so happy together, it will not give me another moment’s pang: and to convince you that I have been speaking truth, I am now going to destroy—what I ought to have destroyed long ago—what I ought never to have kept—I know that very well (blushing as she spoke).—However, now I will destroy it all—and it is my particular wish to do it in your presence, that you may see how rational I am grown.", "매력적인 분인 건 맞겠죠, 다 좋아요, 그래도 성미가 아주 고약하고 무례한 사람 같아요—그날 밤 그 표정을 저는 결코 못 잊을 거예요!—하지만, 정말이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 저는 그분이 잘못되기를 바라지 않아요.—아니에요, 두 분이 아무리 행복하게 산다 해도 제 마음에 다시 아픔이 일진 않을 거예요. 그리고 제가 진실을 말하고 있다는 걸 보여 드리려고, 이제 이걸 없애 버리려고요—진작 없앴어야 할 것을—애초에 간직하지 말았어야 할 것을요—저도 그건 잘 알아요(이렇게 말하며 얼굴을 붉혔다).—하지만 이제 전부 없애 버릴 거예요—그리고 꼭 당신이 보는 앞에서 하고 싶어요."], ["Cannot you guess what this parcel holds?”", "제가 얼마나 분별 있게 변했는지 봐 주시도록요. 이 꾸러미 안에 뭐가 들었는지 짐작 못 하시겠죠?\""], ["said she, with a conscious look.", "그녀가 사뭇 의식하는 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“Not the least in the world.—Did he ever give you any thing?”", "\"전혀 모르겠는걸요.—그분이 무슨 물건이라도 준 적이 있어요?\""]], [["“No—I cannot call them gifts; but they are things that I have valued very much.”", "\"아니요—선물이라고는 못 해요. 그래도 제가 무척 소중히 여기던 것들이에요.\""]], [["She held the parcel towards her, and Emma read the words Most precious treasures on the top.", "그녀가 꾸러미를 엠마 쪽으로 내밀자, 엠마는 맨 위에 적힌 \"가장 소중한 보물\"이라는 글씨를 읽었다."], ["Her curiosity was greatly excited.", "호기심이 잔뜩 일었다."], ["Harriet unfolded the parcel, and she looked on with impatience.", "해리엇이 꾸러미를 펼치는 동안 엠마는 안달하며 지켜보았다."], ["Within abundance of silver paper was a pretty little Tunbridge-ware box, which Harriet opened: it was well lined with the softest cotton; but, excepting the cotton, Emma saw only a small piece of court-plaister.", "은박지를 잔뜩 두른 안에 자그마하고 예쁜 턴브리지 세공 상자가 들어 있었고, 해리엇이 그것을 열었다. 더없이 부드러운 솜이 곱게 깔려 있었지만, 그 솜 말고 엠마의 눈에 들어온 것이라곤 자그마한 반창고 한 조각뿐이었다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Harriet, “you must recollect.”", "해리엇이 말했다. \"이제 분명 기억나실 거예요.\""]], [["“No, indeed I do not.”", "\"아니, 정말 모르겠는데요.\""]], [["“Dear me!", "\"세상에!"], ["I should not have thought it possible you could forget what passed in this very room about court-plaister, one of the very last times we ever met in it!—It was but a very few days before I had my sore throat—just before Mr. and Mrs.", "바로 이 방에서, 우리가 여기서 만난 거의 마지막 날 중 하나였는데, 반창고를 두고 있었던 일을 잊으실 줄은 정말 몰랐어요!—제가 목이 아프기 바로 며칠 전이었어요—미스터 존 나이틀리 내외가 오시기 직전이요—아마 바로 그날 저녁이었던 것 같아요.—기억 안 나세요?"], ["John Knightley came—I think the very evening.—Do not you remember his cutting his finger with your new penknife, and your recommending court-plaister?—But, as you had none about you, and knew I had, you desired me to supply him; and so I took mine out and cut him a piece; but it was a great deal too large, and he cut it smaller, and kept playing some time with what was left, before he gave it back to me.", "그분이 당신의 새 주머니칼로 손가락을 베고, 당신이 반창고를 권하셨던 거요?—그런데 당신한테는 마침 반창고가 없고 저한테는 있는 걸 아시니까, 당신이 저더러 그분께 드리라고 하셨잖아요. 그래서 제가 제 걸 꺼내 한 조각 잘라 드렸는데, 너무 컸던지 그분이 더 작게 잘라서, 한참을 그 남은 조각을 만지작거리시다가 저한테 돌려주셨어요."], ["And so then, in my nonsense, I could not help making a treasure of it—so I put it by never to be used, and looked at it now and then as a great treat.”", "그래서 그때, 제 어리석은 마음에, 저도 모르게 그걸 보물로 삼지 않을 수 없었어요—그래서 절대 쓰지 않으려고 따로 치워 두고, 가끔 큰 호사처럼 들여다보곤 했죠.\""]], [["“My dearest Harriet!”", "\"내 사랑하는 해리엇!\""], ["cried Emma, putting her hand before her face, and jumping up, “you make me more ashamed of myself than I can bear.", "엠마가 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 벌떡 일어서며 외쳤다. \"당신 때문에 견딜 수 없을 만큼 내 자신이 부끄러워지네요."], ["Remember it?", "기억나느냐고요?"], ["Aye, I remember it all now; all, except your saving this relic—I knew nothing of that till this moment—but the cutting the finger, and my recommending court-plaister, and saying I had none about me!—Oh!", "그럼요, 이제 다 기억나요. 당신이 이 유물을 간직한 일만 빼고 전부요—그건 이 순간까지 까맣게 몰랐어요—하지만 손가락을 벤 일이며, 내가 반창고를 권하고 마침 가진 게 없다고 했던 일은요!—아!"], ["my sins, my sins!—And I had plenty all the while in my pocket!—One of my senseless tricks!—I deserve to be under a continual blush all the rest of my life.—Well—(sitting down again)—go on—what else?”", "내 죄, 내 죄!—그러면서 줄곧 주머니에 잔뜩 갖고 있었으면서!—내 그 못된 장난질 가운데 하나죠!—남은 평생을 두고두고 얼굴 붉히며 살아도 싸요.—자—(다시 자리에 앉으며)—계속해 봐요—그 다음엔요?\""]], [["“And had you really some at hand yourself?", "\"그럼 정말로 직접 가지고 계셨던 거예요?"], ["I am sure I never suspected it, you did it so naturally.”", "저는 한 번도 그런 줄 몰랐어요. 너무나 자연스럽게 하셔서요.\""]], [["“And so you actually put this piece of court-plaister by for his sake!”", "\"그래서 정말로 그분을 위해서 이 반창고 조각을 따로 치워 뒀단 말이죠!\""], ["said Emma, recovering from her state of shame and feeling divided between wonder and amusement.", "엠마가 부끄러움에서 벗어나 놀라움과 우스움 사이를 오가는 심정으로 말했다."], ["And secretly she added to herself, “Lord bless me!", "그러고는 속으로 가만히 덧붙였다. \"이런, 세상에!"], ["when should I ever have thought of putting by in cotton a piece of court-plaister that Frank Churchill had been pulling about!", "프랭크 처칠이 만지작거리던 반창고 한 조각을 솜에 싸서 간직할 생각을 내가 도대체 언제 했겠어!"], ["I never was equal to this.”", "나는 이런 경지엔 도저히 못 미친다니까.\""]], [["“Here,” resumed Harriet, turning to her box again, “here is something still more valuable, I mean that has been more valuable, because this is what did really once belong to him, which the court-plaister never did.”", "\"여기,\" 해리엇이 다시 상자로 손을 돌리며 말을 이었다. \"여기 한층 더 값진 게 있어요. 더 값진 거였다는 뜻이에요. 이건 한때 정말로 그분 것이었거든요. 반창고는 한 번도 그분 것이었던 적이 없지만요.\""]], [["Emma was quite eager to see this superior treasure.", "엠마는 그 한층 귀하다는 보물을 보고 싶어 몹시 달떴다."], ["It was the end of an old pencil,—the part without any lead.", "그것은 낡은 연필의 끄트머리—심이 다 없어진 부분이었다."]], [["“This was really his,”", "\"이건 정말 그분 거였어요.\" 해리엇이 말했다.—\"어느 날 아침 기억나세요?—아니, 모르실 거예요."], ["said Harriet.—“Do not you remember one morning?—no, I dare say you do not.", "하지만 어느 날 아침에—정확한 날짜는 잊었지만요—아마 그날 저녁 바로 전 화요일이나 수요일이었을 거예요. 그분이 수첩에 뭔가를 적으려고 하셨어요."], ["But one morning—I forget exactly the day—but perhaps it was the Tuesday or Wednesday before that evening, he wanted to make a memorandum in his pocket-book; it was about spruce-beer.", "가문비나무 맥주에 대한 거였죠. 미스터 나이틀리가 가문비나무 맥주 빚는 법을 들려주시던 참이라, 그분이 그걸 적어 두고 싶어 하셨거든요."], ["Mr. Knightley had been telling him something about brewing spruce-beer, and he wanted to put it down; but when he took out his pencil, there was so little lead that he soon cut it all away, and it would not do, so you lent him another, and this was left upon the table as good for nothing.", "그런데 연필을 꺼내 보니 심이 너무 짧아서 금방 다 깎여 버려 쓸 수가 없었어요."], ["But I kept my eye on it; and, as soon as I dared, caught it up, and never parted with it again from that moment.”", "그래서 당신이 다른 걸 빌려주셨고, 이건 쓸모없다고 탁자에 그냥 남겨졌죠. 하지만 제가 그걸 눈여겨봤다가, 용기를 낼 수 있게 되자마자 얼른 집어 들었고, 그때부터 한 번도 손에서 놓지 않았어요.\""]], [["“I do remember it,”", "\"기억나요,\" 엠마가 외쳤다."], ["cried Emma; “I perfectly remember it.—Talking about spruce-beer.—Oh! yes—Mr.", "\"아주 또렷이 기억나요.—가문비나무 맥주 이야기를 했죠.—아!"], ["Knightley and I both saying we liked it, and Mr. Elton’s seeming resolved to learn to like it too.", "그래요—미스터 나이틀리와 내가 둘 다 좋아한다고 했고, 미스터 엘턴은 자기도 기어이 좋아해 보겠다는 눈치였죠."], ["I perfectly remember it.—Stop; Mr. Knightley was standing just here, was not he?", "또렷이 기억나요.—가만있자, 미스터 나이틀리가 바로 여기 서 있지 않았나요?"], ["I have an idea he was standing just here.”", "바로 여기 서 있었던 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I do not know.", "모르겠어요."], ["I cannot recollect.—It is very odd, but I cannot recollect.—Mr.", "기억이 안 나요.—참 이상하죠, 그게 기억이 안 나요.—미스터 엘턴은 여기 앉아 계셨던 건 기억나요."], ["Elton was sitting here, I remember, much about where I am now.”—", "지금 제가 앉은 데쯤이요.\"—"]], [["“Well, go on.”", "\"자, 계속해요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["that’s all.", "그게 다예요."], ["I have nothing more to shew you, or to say—except that I am now going to throw them both behind the fire, and I wish you to see me do it.”", "더 보여 드릴 것도, 드릴 말씀도 없어요—이제 이 둘을 불 속에 던져 넣으려는 것만 빼면요. 제가 그러는 걸 당신이 봐 주셨으면 해요.\""]], [["“My poor dear Harriet!", "\"내 가엾은 해리엇!"], ["and have you actually found happiness in treasuring up these things?”", "정말로 이런 것들을 보물처럼 간직하면서 행복을 느꼈던 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes, simpleton as I was!—but I am quite ashamed of it now, and wish I could forget as easily as I can burn them.", "\"네, 어리석게도요!—하지만 이제는 너무 부끄러워요. 이것들을 태워 없애듯 그렇게 쉽게 잊을 수 있으면 좋겠어요."], ["It was very wrong of me, you know, to keep any remembrances, after he was married.", "그분이 결혼한 뒤에도 무슨 기념물을 간직하는 건 정말 잘못된 일이었어요, 아시잖아요."], ["I knew it was—but had not resolution enough to part with them.”", "잘못인 줄 알면서도—그것들과 헤어질 만한 결심이 안 섰던 거죠.\""]], [["“But, Harriet, is it necessary to burn the court-plaister?—I have not a word to say for the bit of old pencil, but the court-plaister might be useful.”", "\"하지만 해리엇, 그 반창고까지 꼭 태워야 할까요?—낡은 연필 조각이야 두둔할 말이 한마디도 없지만, 반창고는 쓸모가 있을 텐데요.\""]], [["“I shall be happier to burn it,”", "\"태워 버리는 게 마음이 더 편해요.\""], ["replied Harriet.", "해리엇이 대답했다."], ["“It has a disagreeable look to me.", "\"저한텐 보기 싫은 물건이에요."], ["I must get rid of every thing.—There it goes, and there is an end, thank Heaven!", "전부 없애 버려야 해요.—자, 들어간다, 이렇게—이제 끝, 다행이지 뭐예요!"], ["of Mr. Elton.”", "미스터 엘턴은 이걸로 끝이에요.\""]], [["“And when,”", "\"그러면,\""], ["thought Emma, “will there be a beginning of Mr.", "엠마는 속으로 생각했다."], ["Churchill?”", "\"미스터 처칠은 언제 시작되려나?\""]], [["She had soon afterwards reason to believe that the beginning was already made, and could not but hope that the gipsy, though she had told no fortune, might be proved to have made Harriet’s.—About a fortnight after the alarm, they came to a sufficient explanation, and quite undesignedly.", "얼마 지나지 않아 엠마는 그 시작이 이미 이루어졌다고 믿을 만한 근거를 얻었고, 비록 그 집시는 점을 쳐 주지 않았어도 해리엇의 운명만큼은 점지해 준 셈으로 판명될지 모른다는 기대를 품지 않을 수 없었다.—그 소동이 있고 보름쯤 지나, 두 사람은 충분한 해명에 이르렀는데, 그것도 전혀 의도치 않게 이루어진 일이었다."], ["Emma was not thinking of it at the moment, which made the information she received more valuable.", "마침 엠마가 그 생각을 하고 있지 않던 참이라, 듣게 된 소식이 한층 값지게 다가왔다."], ["She merely said, in the course of some trivial chat, “Well, Harriet, whenever you marry I would advise you to do so and so”—and thought no more of it, till after a minute’s silence she heard Harriet say in a very serious tone, “I shall never marry.”", "그녀는 그저 시시한 잡담 끝에 이렇게 말했을 뿐이었다. \"그래요, 해리엇, 당신도 결혼하게 되면 이러저러하게 하라고 권하고 싶네요\"—그러고는 더 생각지 않았는데, 잠깐의 침묵 뒤에 해리엇이 자못 진지한 어조로 \"저는 결혼 안 할 거예요\"라고 하는 말이 들려왔다."]], [["Emma then looked up, and immediately saw how it was; and after a moment’s debate, as to whether it should pass unnoticed or not, replied,", "그제야 엠마가 고개를 들었고, 곧장 어찌 된 영문인지 알아차렸다. 그러고는 잠시 모른 척 넘길지 말지 망설인 끝에 이렇게 답했다."]], [["“Never marry!—This is a new resolution.”", "\"결혼을 안 하겠다고요!—이건 또 새로운 결심이네요.\""]], [["“It is one that I shall never change, however.”", "\"하지만 결코 바꾸지 않을 결심이에요.\""]], [["After another short hesitation, “I hope it does not proceed from—I hope it is not in compliment to Mr.", "또 한 번 짧게 머뭇거린 뒤."], ["Elton?”", "\"그게 설마—설마 미스터 엘턴 때문은 아니길 바라요?\""]], [["“Mr. Elton indeed!”", "\"미스터 엘턴이라니요!\""], ["cried Harriet indignantly.—“Oh!", "해리엇이 분개하여 외쳤다.—\"아! 아니에요\"—그리고 엠마는 겨우 이런 말을 알아들을 수 있었다."], ["no”—and Emma could just catch the words, “so superior to Mr. Elton!”", "\"미스터 엘턴보다 훨씬 훌륭하신 분인걸요!\""]], [["She then took a longer time for consideration.", "그러자 엠마는 한참 더 생각에 잠겼다. 여기서 더 나아가지 말까?—그냥 넘기며 아무것도 눈치채지 못한 척할까?—그러면 해리엇은 자기를 냉담하다거나 화났다고 여길지도 몰랐다."], ["Should she proceed no farther?—should she let it pass, and seem to suspect nothing?—Perhaps Harriet might think her cold or angry if she did; or perhaps if she were totally silent, it might only drive Harriet into asking her to hear too much; and against any thing like such an unreserve as had been, such an open and frequent discussion of hopes and chances, she was perfectly resolved.—She believed it would be wiser for her to say and know at once, all that she meant to say and know.", "또 완전히 입을 다물고 있으면, 오히려 해리엇이 너무 많은 이야기를 들어 달라고 졸라 대는 쪽으로 몰릴 수도 있었다. 그런데 예전 같은 그런 격의 없음에는—희망이며 가능성을 그렇게 터놓고 빈번하게 늘어놓는 일에는—단호히 맞서기로 마음먹은 터였다.—말하고 알아 둘 작정인 것을 한꺼번에 말하고 알아 두는 편이 현명하리라 여겼다."], ["Plain dealing was always best.", "솔직하게 처리하는 것이 언제나 최선이었다."], ["She had previously determined how far she would proceed, on any application of the sort; and it would be safer for both, to have the judicious law of her own brain laid down with speed.—She was decided, and thus spoke—", "이런 종류의 호소에 대해 어디까지 나아갈지는 미리 정해 두었으니, 자기 머릿속의 그 분별 있는 규칙을 서둘러 못 박아 두는 편이 양쪽 모두에게 안전할 터였다.—마음을 정한 그녀가 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Harriet, I will not affect to be in doubt of your meaning.", "\"해리엇, 당신 말뜻을 모르는 척하진 않을게요."], ["Your resolution, or rather your expectation of never marrying, results from an idea that the person whom you might prefer, would be too greatly your superior in situation to think of you.", "결혼을 안 하겠다는 그 결심, 아니 차라리 그 예감은, 당신이 마음에 두는 사람이 처지로 보아 당신을 거들떠보기에는 너무나 까마득히 위에 있다는 생각에서 비롯된 거죠."], ["Is not it so?”", "그렇지 않아요?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아! 미스 우드하우스, 믿어 주세요, 저는 감히 그런 생각은—정말이지 저는 그렇게 정신 나가지 않았어요.—하지만 멀찍이서 그분을 우러러보는 것만으로도, 그분이 세상 누구보다도 한없이 훌륭하시다고 생각하는 것만으로도 제겐 기쁨이에요."], ["Miss Woodhouse, believe me I have not the presumption to suppose— Indeed I am not so mad.—But it is a pleasure to me to admire him at a distance—and to think of his infinite superiority to all the rest of the world, with the gratitude, wonder, and veneration, which are so proper, in me especially.”", "마땅히 가져야 할 그 감사와 경탄과 경의를 품고서요. 특히 저 같은 사람한테는 더욱 마땅한 일이죠.\""]], [["“I am not at all surprized at you, Harriet.", "\"당신이 그러는 게 조금도 놀랍지 않아요, 해리엇."], ["The service he rendered you was enough to warm your heart.”", "그분이 당신에게 베푼 친절이라면 당신 마음을 데우고도 남았을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Service! oh!", "\"친절이라니요!"], ["it was such an inexpressible obligation!—The very recollection of it, and all that I felt at the time—when I saw him coming—his noble look—and my wretchedness before.", "아! 말로 다 못할 은혜였는걸요!—그날을 떠올리기만 해도, 그때 제가 느낀 그 모든 것이—그분이 다가오시는 걸 봤을 때—그 고귀한 모습이—그리고 그 직전의 비참함이라니."], ["Such a change!", "그런 변화라니!"], ["In one moment such a change!", "한순간에 그런 변화가!"], ["From perfect misery to perfect happiness!”", "더없는 비참함에서 더없는 행복으로요!\""]], [["“It is very natural.", "\"아주 자연스러운 일이에요."], ["It is natural, and it is honourable.—Yes, honourable, I think, to chuse so well and so gratefully.—But that it will be a fortunate preference is more than I can promise.", "자연스럽고, 또 떳떳한 일이죠.—그래요, 떳떳한 일이라고 생각해요. 그토록 잘, 그토록 고마운 마음으로 사람을 고른 거니까요.—하지만 그게 행운으로 이어질 마음인지는 제가 약속해 드릴 수 없네요."], ["I do not advise you to give way to it, Harriet.", "거기에 휩쓸리라고 권하진 않겠어요, 해리엇."], ["I do not by any means engage for its being returned.", "그 마음이 보답받으리라고는 결코 장담 못 해요."], ["Consider what you are about.", "자신이 무얼 하고 있는지 잘 생각해 봐요."], ["Perhaps it will be wisest in you to check your feelings while you can: at any rate do not let them carry you far, unless you are persuaded of his liking you.", "할 수 있을 때 마음을 다잡는 게 가장 현명할 거예요. 적어도 그분이 당신을 좋아한다는 확신이 서기 전에는 그 마음이 당신을 멀리 끌고 가게 두지 마요."], ["Be observant of him.", "그분을 잘 살펴봐요."], ["Let his behaviour be the guide of your sensations.", "그분의 처신을 당신 마음의 길잡이로 삼아요."], ["I give you this caution now, because I shall never speak to you again on the subject.", "지금 이렇게 경고하는 건, 이 일에 대해 다시는 당신과 말하지 않을 작정이기 때문이에요. 어떤 간섭도 하지 않기로 마음먹었어요."], ["I am determined against all interference.", "이제부터 저는 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는 거예요."], ["Henceforward I know nothing of the matter.", "우리 입에서 그 누구의 이름도 다시는 나오지 않게 해요."], ["Let no name ever pass our lips.", "우리는 전에 아주 잘못했어요."], ["We were very wrong before; we will be cautious now.—He is your superior, no doubt, and there do seem objections and obstacles of a very serious nature; but yet, Harriet, more wonderful things have taken place, there have been matches of greater disparity.", "이제부터는 조심하기로 해요.—그분이 당신보다 윗자리에 있는 건 의심할 바 없고, 아주 심각한 반대와 장애가 있어 보이는 것도 사실이에요. 하지만 그래도, 해리엇, 이보다 더 놀라운 일들도 일어나곤 했고, 이보다 더 격차가 큰 혼사도 있었답니다."], ["But take care of yourself.", "그래도 몸조심해요."], ["I would not have you too sanguine; though, however it may end, be assured your raising your thoughts to him, is a mark of good taste which I shall always know how to value.”", "너무 들뜨지 않았으면 좋겠어요. 다만, 일이 어떻게 끝나든, 당신이 그분께 마음을 두었다는 건 좋은 안목의 표시이고, 저는 언제까지나 그걸 높이 살 줄 안다는 것만은 믿어 줘요.\""]], [["Harriet kissed her hand in silent and submissive gratitude.", "해리엇은 말없이 순순한 감사의 뜻으로 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었다."], ["Emma was very decided in thinking such an attachment no bad thing for her friend.", "엠마는 이런 연정이 벗에게 결코 나쁘지 않으리라고 단단히 믿었다."], ["Its tendency would be to raise and refine her mind—and it must be saving her from the danger of degradation.", "그 마음은 해리엇의 정신을 끌어올리고 세련되게 다듬어 줄 것이며—그녀를 타락의 위험에서 구해 주는 일이 분명할 터였다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter V", "# 제3권 5장"]], [["In this state of schemes, and hopes, and connivance, June opened upon Hartfield.", "이런 갖가지 계획과 희망과 묵계 속에서, 유월이 하트필드를 찾아왔다."], ["To Highbury in general it brought no material change.", "하이베리 전체로 보자면 이렇다 할 변화는 없었다."], ["The Eltons were still talking of a visit from the Sucklings, and of the use to be made of their barouche-landau; and Jane Fairfax was still at her grandmother’s; and as the return of the Campbells from Ireland was again delayed, and August, instead of Midsummer, fixed for it, she was likely to remain there full two months longer, provided at least she were able to defeat Mrs.", "엘턴 부부는 여전히 서클링 일가의 방문과, 그들의 바루시-랜도를 어떻게 써먹을지를 입에 올리고 있었다. 제인 페어팩스는 여전히 할머니 댁에 머물렀고, 캠벨 부부의 아일랜드 귀환이 또 한 번 미뤄져 한여름이 아니라 팔월로 잡힌 까닭에, 그녀가 그곳에 꼬박 두 달은 더 머물 모양이었다 — 적어도 자신을 위한답시고 분주히 움직이는 엘턴 부인의 성화를 물리치고, 제 뜻과는 무관하게 어느 흐뭇한 자리로 떠밀려 가는 일을 면할 수만 있다면 말이다."], ["Elton’s activity in her service, and save herself from being hurried into a delightful situation against her will.", "* 바루시-랜도: 접이식 덮개를 갖춘 사두마차로, 당시 부유층의 과시용 마차였다."]], [["Mr. Knightley, who, for some reason best known to himself, had certainly taken an early dislike to Frank Churchill, was only growing to dislike him more.", "미스터 나이틀리는 자기만 아는 어떤 까닭으로 진작부터 프랭크 처칠을 탐탁잖아했는데, 그 마뜩잖음이 점점 더해 갈 뿐이었다."], ["He began to suspect him of some double dealing in his pursuit of Emma.", "그는 처칠이 엠마에게 다가가는 데 무언가 이중적인 구석이 있지 않나 의심하기 시작했다."], ["That Emma was his object appeared indisputable.", "엠마가 처칠의 표적이라는 것은 의심할 여지가 없어 보였다."], ["Every thing declared it; his own attentions, his father’s hints, his mother-in-law’s guarded silence; it was all in unison; words, conduct, discretion, and indiscretion, told the same story.", "모든 것이 그렇게 말하고 있었다 — 처칠 자신의 살가운 태도, 그 아버지의 넌지시 던지는 말, 계모의 조심스러운 침묵, 이 모두가 한목소리였다. 말도, 행동도, 신중함도, 경솔함도 한결같이 같은 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["But while so many were devoting him to Emma, and Emma herself making him over to Harriet, Mr. Knightley began to suspect him of some inclination to trifle with Jane Fairfax.", "그러나 그토록 많은 사람이 그를 엠마에게 점지하고, 엠마 자신은 그를 해리엇에게 넘겨주려는 동안, 미스터 나이틀리는 처칠이 제인 페어팩스와 어떤 식으로든 장난을 치고 싶어 하는 게 아닌가 의심하기 시작했다."], ["He could not understand it; but there were symptoms of intelligence between them—he thought so at least—symptoms of admiration on his side, which, having once observed, he could not persuade himself to think entirely void of meaning, however he might wish to escape any of Emma’s errors of imagination.", "그로서는 알 수 없는 일이었다. 그러나 두 사람 사이에 어떤 통하는 기미가 — 적어도 그에게는 그렇게 보였다 — 처칠 쪽의 흠모의 기색이 있었으니, 한번 눈에 들어온 이상, 엠마의 상상이 저지른 잘못 따위는 무엇 하나 답습하지 않겠다고 아무리 다짐해 본들, 그것이 아무 뜻 없는 것이라고는 도무지 자신을 설득할 수 없었다."], ["She was not present when the suspicion first arose.", "그 의심이 처음 일었을 때, 그녀는 자리에 없었다."], ["He was dining with the Randalls family, and Jane, at the Eltons’; and he had seen a look, more than a single look, at Miss Fairfax, which, from the admirer of Miss Woodhouse, seemed somewhat out of place.", "그는 엘턴 부부 댁에서 랜덜스 가족, 그리고 제인과 함께 식사를 하고 있었다. 그는 미스 페어팩스를 향한 한 차례의, 아니 한 차례 이상의 눈길을 보았으니, 미스 우드하우스를 흠모하는 사람의 눈길이라기엔 어딘가 어울리지 않아 보였다."], ["When he was again in their company, he could not help remembering what he had seen; nor could he avoid observations which, unless it were like Cowper and his fire at twilight,", "다시 그들과 자리를 함께했을 때, 그는 자신이 본 것을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었고, 카우퍼가 황혼녘 난롯불을 보며 읊었듯 —"]], [["“Myself creating what I saw,”", "\"내가 본 것을 스스로 지어내는 것\""]], [["brought him yet stronger suspicion of there being a something of private liking, of private understanding even, between Frank Churchill and Jane.", "이 아닌 한, 프랭크 처칠과 제인 사이에 남모르는 호감이, 아니 남모르는 묵계까지 있지 않나 하는 한층 짙은 의심을 안기는 관찰을 피할 수도 없었다."]], [["He had walked up one day after dinner, as he very often did, to spend his evening at Hartfield.", "어느 날 그는 식후에, 늘 그러듯 하트필드까지 걸어 올라와 저녁을 보내기로 했다. 엠마와 해리엇이 산책을 나가려던 참이라 그도 끼었고, 돌아오는 길에 더 큰 무리와 마주쳤다."], ["Emma and Harriet were going to walk; he joined them; and, on returning, they fell in with a larger party, who, like themselves, judged it wisest to take their exercise early, as the weather threatened rain; Mr. and Mrs.", "그들도 이쪽과 마찬가지로, 비가 올 듯한 날씨라 일찌감치 거니는 편이 현명하다고 여긴 사람들이었으니 — 미스터 웨스턴 부부와 그 아들, 그리고 미스 베이츠와 조카딸이 우연히 만났던 것이다."], ["Weston and their son, Miss Bates and her niece, who had accidentally met. They all united; and, on reaching Hartfield gates, Emma, who knew it was exactly the sort of visiting that would be welcome to her father, pressed them all to go in and drink tea with him.", "모두가 한 무리를 이루었고, 하트필드 정문에 이르자 엠마는 이것이야말로 아버지가 반길 만한 종류의 방문임을 알기에, 다들 들어와 함께 차를 들자고 권했다."], ["The Randalls party agreed to it immediately; and after a pretty long speech from Miss Bates, which few persons listened to, she also found it possible to accept dear Miss Woodhouse’s most obliging invitation.", "랜덜스 일행은 곧장 응했다. 미스 베이츠가 꽤 긴 연설을 늘어놓았으나 귀 기울이는 사람은 거의 없었고, 그녀 역시 사랑스러운 미스 우드하우스의 더없이 친절한 초대를 받아들일 수 있겠다고 결론지었다."]], [["As they were turning into the grounds, Mr. Perry passed by on horseback.", "일행이 정원 안으로 접어들 때, 미스터 페리가 말을 타고 지나갔다."], ["The gentlemen spoke of his horse.", "신사들은 그의 말을 화제로 삼았다."]], [["“By the bye,”", "\"그러고 보니,\""], ["said Frank Churchill to Mrs. Weston presently, “what became of Mr.", "곧이어 프랭크 처칠이 웨스턴 부인에게 말했다."], ["Perry’s plan of setting up his carriage?”", "\"미스터 페리가 마차를 장만하겠다던 계획은 어떻게 됐습니까?\""]], [["Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인은 놀란 얼굴로 말했다."], ["Weston looked surprized, and said, “I did not know that he ever had any such plan.”", "\"그분에게 그런 계획이 있었다는 건 처음 듣는데요.\""]], [["“Nay, I had it from you.", "\"아니요, 부인께 들은 이야기입니다."], ["You wrote me word of it three months ago.”", "석 달 전에 편지로 적어 보내셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Me!", "\"제가요!"], ["impossible!”", "그럴 리가요!\""]], [["“Indeed you did.", "\"분명히 그러셨습니다."], ["I remember it perfectly.", "똑똑히 기억해요."], ["You mentioned it as what was certainly to be very soon.", "곧 틀림없이 그렇게 될 일이라고 적으셨죠."], ["Mrs. Perry had told somebody, and was extremely happy about it.", "페리 부인이 누군가에게 말했고, 그 일로 무척 기뻐했다고요."], ["It was owing to her persuasion, as she thought his being out in bad weather did him a great deal of harm.", "그게 다 그 부인이 설득한 덕분인데, 미스터 페리가 궂은 날씨에 나다니는 게 몸에 큰 해가 된다고 여겼기 때문이라고요."], ["You must remember it now?”", "이제는 기억나시죠?\""]], [["“Upon my word I never heard of it till this moment.”", "\"맹세코, 이 순간까지 그런 얘기는 들어 본 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“Never!", "\"한 번도요!"], ["really, never!—Bless me!", "정말, 한 번도라니!—어이쿠!"], ["how could it be?—Then I must have dreamt it—but I was completely persuaded—Miss Smith, you walk as if you were tired.", "어찌 이럴 수가 있죠?—그럼 제가 꿈을 꾼 게 틀림없군요—하지만 저는 완전히 그렇게 믿고 있었는데—미스 스미스, 지친 사람처럼 걷고 계시네요."], ["You will not be sorry to find yourself at home.”", "집에 다다른 게 반가우시겠어요.\""]], [["“What is this?—What is this?” cried Mr.", "\"무슨 얘긴가?—무슨 얘기야?\""], ["Weston, “about Perry and a carriage?", "미스터 웨스턴이 외쳤다. \"페리하고 마차라니?"], ["Is Perry going to set up his carriage, Frank?", "페리가 마차를 장만한다는 거냐, 프랭크?"], ["I am glad he can afford it.", "그럴 형편이 된다니 다행이로구나."], ["You had it from himself, had you?”", "그 사람한테 직접 들은 거냐?\""]], [["“No, sir,” replied his son, laughing, “I seem to have had it from nobody.—Very odd!—I really was persuaded of Mrs.", "\"아닙니다, 아버지.\""], ["Weston’s having mentioned it in one of her letters to Enscombe, many weeks ago, with all these particulars—but as she declares she never heard a syllable of it before, of course it must have been a dream.", "아들이 웃으며 대답했다. \"아무한테도 듣지 않은 모양이에요.—참 이상한 일이죠!—저는 정말이지 웨스턴 부인께서 몇 주 전 엔스컴으로 보내신 편지에 그 일을 이런저런 세세한 사정까지 적으셨다고 믿고 있었는데—본인이 그런 얘기는 한마디도 들은 적이 없다고 단언하시니, 결국 꿈이었던 게 분명하군요."], ["I am a great dreamer. I dream of every body at Highbury when I am away—and when I have gone through my particular friends, then I begin dreaming of Mr.", "저는 꿈을 잘 꿉니다."], ["and Mrs. Perry.”", "떠나 있을 때면 하이베리 사람들을 모조리 꿈에 봐요—그러다 각별한 친구들을 한 차례 다 거치고 나면, 그제야 미스터 페리 부부를 꿈꾸기 시작하죠.\""]], [["“It is odd though,”", "\"하지만 이상한 일이로구나,\""], ["observed his father, “that you should have had such a regular connected dream about people whom it was not very likely you should be thinking of at Enscombe.", "그 아버지가 한마디 보탰다. \"엔스컴에서야 좀처럼 떠올릴 일도 없는 사람들을 두고 그렇게 조리 있게 이어진 꿈을 꾸었다니 말이다."], ["Perry’s setting up his carriage!", "페리가 마차를 장만한다!"], ["and his wife’s persuading him to it, out of care for his health—just what will happen, I have no doubt, some time or other; only a little premature.", "게다가 건강을 염려한 그 아내가 권해서라니—틀림없이 언젠가는 그렇게 될 일이지, 다만 좀 이른 셈일 뿐이고."], ["What an air of probability sometimes runs through a dream!", "꿈이란 게 어떨 때는 어찌 그리 그럴듯한 분위기가 흐르는지!"], ["And at others, what a heap of absurdities it is!", "또 어떨 때는 어찌 그리 터무니없는 것들로만 들끓는지!"], ["Well, Frank, your dream certainly shews that Highbury is in your thoughts when you are absent.", "그래, 프랭크, 네 꿈은 적어도 네가 떠나 있을 때도 하이베리를 마음에 두고 있다는 증거로구나."], ["Emma, you are a great dreamer, I think?”", "엠마, 자네도 꿈을 잘 꾸는 편 아닌가?\""]], [["Emma was out of hearing.", "엠마는 들리지 않는 곳에 있었다."], ["She had hurried on before her guests to prepare her father for their appearance, and was beyond the reach of Mr. Weston’s hint.", "손님들보다 앞서 서둘러 가, 그들이 들이닥치는 데 대비해 아버지를 채비시키느라, 미스터 웨스턴이 넌지시 건넨 말이 닿지 않는 곳에 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Why, to own the truth,”", "\"저기, 사실대로 말하자면,\""], ["cried Miss Bates, who had been trying in vain to be heard the last two minutes, “if I must speak on this subject, there is no denying that Mr.", "지난 이 분 동안 끼어들려고 헛되이 애쓰던 미스 베이츠가 외쳤다."], ["Frank Churchill might have—I do not mean to say that he did not dream it—I am sure I have sometimes the oddest dreams in the world—but if I am questioned about it, I must acknowledge that there was such an idea last spring; for Mrs.", "\"이 얘기를 굳이 해야 한다면, 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 그럴 만도 했다는 건 부인할 수가 없는데—그분이 꿈을 꾸지 않았다는 말은 아니에요—저도 더러는 세상에 둘도 없이 희한한 꿈을 꾸곤 하니까요—하지만 이 일을 두고 물으신다면, 지난봄에 그런 얘기가 있었다는 걸 인정할 수밖에 없네요."], ["Perry herself mentioned it to my mother, and the Coles knew of it as well as ourselves—but it was quite a secret, known to nobody else, and only thought of about three days. Mrs.", "페리 부인이 몸소 저희 어머니께 그 얘기를 하셨고, 콜네 사람들도 저희만큼이나 알고 있었으니까요—하지만 아주 비밀스러운 일이라 다른 사람은 아무도 몰랐고, 사흘쯤만 입에 올랐을 뿐이에요."], ["Perry was very anxious that he should have a carriage, and came to my mother in great spirits one morning because she thought she had prevailed.", "페리 부인은 그분이 마차를 갖기를 무척 바라셨고, 어느 날 아침 어머니를 찾아오셔서 이제 설득에 성공한 것 같다며 신이 나서 말씀하셨죠. 제인, 우리가 집에 왔을 때 할머니가 그 얘기를 들려주신 거 기억 안 나니?"], ["Jane, don’t you remember grandmama’s telling us of it when we got home?", "그날 어디로 산책을 다녀왔는지는 잊어버렸네—아마 랜덜스였을 거예요. 그래요, 랜덜스였던 것 같아요."], ["I forget where we had been walking to—very likely to Randalls; yes, I think it was to Randalls. Mrs.", "페리 부인은 늘 저희 어머니를 각별히 좋아하셨어요—하기야 어머니를 안 좋아하는 사람이 어디 있겠어요—그래서 어머니께 은밀히 그 얘길 하신 거고요."], ["Perry was always particularly fond of my mother—indeed I do not know who is not—and she had mentioned it to her in confidence; she had no objection to her telling us, of course, but it was not to go beyond: and, from that day to this, I never mentioned it to a soul that I know of.", "물론 저희한테 전하시는 거야 마다하지 않으셨지만, 더는 새 나가면 안 되는 일이었죠. 그리고 그날부터 지금까지, 제가 아는 한 누구에게도 그 얘기를 입에 올린 적이 없어요."], ["At the same time, I will not positively answer for my having never dropt a hint, because I know I do sometimes pop out a thing before I am aware.", "그렇긴 해도, 한 번도 귀띔 같은 걸 흘린 적이 없다고 딱 잘라 장담하지는 못하겠어요. 저도 모르게 불쑥 무슨 말을 내뱉을 때가 있다는 걸 아니까요."], ["I am a talker, you know; I am rather a talker; and now and then I have let a thing escape me which I should not.", "제가 워낙 말이 많잖아요. 좀 수다스러운 편이죠."], ["I am not like Jane; I wish I were.", "그래서 더러는 입에 담아선 안 될 걸 흘리기도 하고요. 저는 제인 같지가 못해요."], ["I will answer for it she never betrayed the least thing in the world.", "제인 같았으면 좋으련만. 제인은 세상의 무엇 하나 흘린 적이 없다고 제가 장담해요."], ["Where is she?—Oh! just behind.", "그 애가 어디 있더라?—아!"], ["Perfectly remember Mrs.", "바로 뒤에 있네."], ["Perry’s coming.—Extraordinary dream, indeed!”", "페리 부인이 오셨던 게 똑똑히 기억나요.—참말이지 별난 꿈이네요!\""]], [["They were entering the hall. Mr.", "일행이 현관으로 들어서던 참이었다."], ["Knightley’s eyes had preceded Miss Bates’s in a glance at Jane.", "미스터 나이틀리의 눈은 미스 베이츠보다 한발 앞서 제인을 흘긋 보았다."], ["From Frank Churchill’s face, where he thought he saw confusion suppressed or laughed away, he had involuntarily turned to hers; but she was indeed behind, and too busy with her shawl.", "프랭크 처칠의 얼굴에서 — 억눌렸거나 웃어넘긴 당혹의 빛을 본 듯한 그 얼굴에서 — 그는 저도 모르게 제인의 얼굴로 눈길을 돌렸다. 그러나 그녀는 정말로 뒤에 처져, 숄을 여미느라 분주했다."], ["Mr. Weston had walked in.", "미스터 웨스턴은 이미 안으로 들어갔다."], ["The two other gentlemen waited at the door to let her pass.", "나머지 두 신사는 문가에 서서 그녀가 지나가도록 기다렸다."], ["Mr. Knightley suspected in Frank Churchill the determination of catching her eye—he seemed watching her intently—in vain, however, if it were so—Jane passed between them into the hall, and looked at neither.", "미스터 나이틀리는 프랭크 처칠이 그녀의 눈을 마주치려고 작정한 게 아닌가 의심했다 — 그는 그녀를 뚫어지게 살피는 듯했다 — 그러나 정말 그랬다 한들 헛수고였으니, 제인은 두 사람 사이를 지나 현관으로 들어가면서 어느 쪽도 쳐다보지 않았다."]], [["There was no time for farther remark or explanation. The dream must be borne with, and Mr.", "더는 따져 물을 겨를도, 해명할 겨를도 없었다."], ["Knightley must take his seat with the rest round the large modern circular table which Emma had introduced at Hartfield, and which none but Emma could have had power to place there and persuade her father to use, instead of the small-sized Pembroke, on which two of his daily meals had, for forty years been crowded.", "그 꿈 이야기는 묻어 두는 수밖에 없었고, 미스터 나이틀리도 나머지 사람들과 함께 그 크고 현대적인 둥근 식탁에 둘러앉아야 했다. 엠마가 하트필드에 들여놓은 그 식탁은, 사십 년 동안 하루 두 끼니가 비좁게 차려지던 자그마한 펨브룩 식탁 대신 그것을 그 자리에 놓고 아버지를 설득해 쓰게 만들 수 있는 사람은 엠마 말고는 없었다."], ["Tea passed pleasantly, and nobody seemed in a hurry to move.", "차 시간은 즐겁게 흘러갔고, 누구도 자리를 뜨려고 서두르는 기색이 없었다."]], [["“Miss Woodhouse,”", "\"미스 우드하우스,\""], ["said Frank Churchill, after examining a table behind him, which he could reach as he sat, “have your nephews taken away their alphabets—their box of letters?", "프랭크 처칠이 앉은 채로 손이 닿는 등 뒤의 탁자를 살피더니 말했다. \"조카분들이 알파벳 놀이 도구를—그 글자 상자를 가져가 버렸나요?"], ["It used to stand here.", "여기 놓여 있곤 했는데."], ["Where is it?", "어디 있죠?"], ["This is a sort of dull-looking evening, that ought to be treated rather as winter than summer.", "오늘 저녁은 어쩐지 따분한 기운이 도는 게, 여름이라기보다 겨울처럼 보내야 할 분위기네요."], ["We had great amusement with those letters one morning.", "언젠가 아침에 그 글자들로 재미나게 놀았잖아요."], ["I want to puzzle you again.”", "다시 한번 골탕 먹여 드리고 싶은데요.\""]], [["Emma was pleased with the thought; and producing the box, the table was quickly scattered over with alphabets, which no one seemed so much disposed to employ as their two selves.", "엠마는 그 발상이 마음에 들었다. 상자를 꺼내 오니, 식탁은 금세 알파벳들로 어수선해졌는데, 그것을 쓰는 데 가장 열심인 사람은 단연 그 둘이었다."], ["They were rapidly forming words for each other, or for any body else who would be puzzled.", "두 사람은 서로에게, 또는 골탕 먹어 줄 만한 다른 아무에게나 빠르게 단어를 만들어 보였다."], ["The quietness of the game made it particularly eligible for Mr. Woodhouse, who had often been distressed by the more animated sort, which Mr.", "이 놀이의 조용함은 미스터 우드하우스에게 특히 알맞았으니, 그는 미스터 웨스턴이 이따금 들여온 더 활기찬 놀이에 곧잘 시달리곤 했던 터였다."], ["Weston had occasionally introduced, and who now sat happily occupied in lamenting, with tender melancholy, over the departure of the “poor little boys,” or in fondly pointing out, as he took up any stray letter near him, how beautifully Emma had written it.", "그는 이제 흐뭇이 자리 잡고 앉아, \"가엾은 어린 손주들\"이 떠나간 것을 애틋한 우수에 잠겨 탄식하거나, 곁에 흩어진 글자를 집어 들 때마다 엠마가 이걸 얼마나 곱게 썼는지 다정히 가리켜 보이는 데 여념이 없었다."]], [["Frank Churchill placed a word before Miss Fairfax.", "프랭크 처칠이 미스 페어팩스 앞에 한 단어를 놓았다."], ["She gave a slight glance round the table, and applied herself to it.", "그녀는 식탁을 슬쩍 둘러보더니 그것에 매달렸다. 프랭크는 엠마 옆에, 제인은 두 사람 맞은편에 있었고—미스터 나이틀리는 그들 모두를 볼 수 있는 자리에 있었다."], ["Frank was next to Emma, Jane opposite to them—and Mr.", "되도록 티 나지 않게 살피면서도 되도록 많이 보는 것이 그의 목적이었다."], ["Knightley so placed as to see them all; and it was his object to see as much as he could, with as little apparent observation.", "단어가 풀렸고, 그녀는 희미한 미소와 함께 그것을 밀어냈다. 곧장 다른 글자들과 섞어 보이지 않게 묻어 둘 생각이었다면, 바로 맞은편을 보는 대신 식탁을 내려다보았어야 했다."], ["The word was discovered, and with a faint smile pushed away.", "글자들이 섞이지 않았기 때문이다."], ["If meant to be immediately mixed with the others, and buried from sight, she should have looked on the table instead of looking just across, for it was not mixed; and Harriet, eager after every fresh word, and finding out none, directly took it up, and fell to work.", "새 단어마다 들떠 달려들고도 하나도 풀어내지 못하던 해리엇이 곧장 그것을 집어 들고 풀이에 매달렸다. 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리 곁에 앉아 있던 터라 그에게 도움을 청했다."], ["She was sitting by Mr. Knightley, and turned to him for help.", "그 단어는 '실수'였다."], ["The word was blunder; and as Harriet exultingly proclaimed it, there was a blush on Jane’s cheek which gave it a meaning not otherwise ostensible. Mr.", "해리엇이 의기양양하게 그것을 외치자, 제인의 뺨에 홍조가 어렸으니, 그렇지 않았다면 드러나지 않았을 어떤 의미를 그 단어에 입혔다."], ["Knightley connected it with the dream; but how it could all be, was beyond his comprehension.", "미스터 나이틀리는 그것을 아까의 꿈 이야기와 연결지었다. 그러나 도대체 어찌 된 영문인지는 그의 짐작을 넘어서는 일이었다."], ["How the delicacy, the discretion of his favourite could have been so lain asleep!", "그가 그토록 아끼는 그 처녀의 섬세함과 분별이 어쩌면 이렇게 잠들어 버릴 수 있단 말인가!"], ["He feared there must be some decided involvement.", "그는 무언가 확실한 얽힘이 있는 게 분명하다고 우려했다."], ["Disingenuousness and double dealing seemed to meet him at every turn.", "가는 곳마다 음험함과 이중성이 그를 맞이하는 듯했다."], ["These letters were but the vehicle for gallantry and trick.", "이 글자들은 한낱 수작과 잔꾀의 매개일 뿐이었다."], ["It was a child’s play, chosen to conceal a deeper game on Frank Churchill’s part.", "그것은 프랭크 처칠 쪽의 더 깊은 노름을 감추려고 고른 아이들 놀이였다."]], [["With great indignation did he continue to observe him; with great alarm and distrust, to observe also his two blinded companions.", "그는 큰 분노 속에서 그를 계속 지켜보았고, 그 못지않은 경악과 불신 속에서 눈먼 두 동행 또한 지켜보았다. 그는 짧은 단어 하나가 엠마를 위해 마련되어, 능청스럽고도 새치름한 눈길과 함께 그녀에게 건네지는 것을 보았다."], ["He saw a short word prepared for Emma, and given to her with a look sly and demure.", "엠마가 그것을 금세 알아채고 무척 재미있어한다는 것을, 그러면서도 짐짓 나무라는 척하는 게 옳다고 여긴다는 것을 보았다."], ["He saw that Emma had soon made it out, and found it highly entertaining, though it was something which she judged it proper to appear to censure; for she said, “Nonsense!", "그녀가 \"말도 안 돼! 부끄러운 줄 아세요!\""], ["for shame!”", "하고 말했기 때문이다."], ["He heard Frank Churchill next say, with a glance towards Jane, “I will give it to her—shall I?”—and as clearly heard Emma opposing it with eager laughing warmth.", "이어 프랭크 처칠이 제인 쪽을 흘긋 보며 \"이걸 그 사람한테 줄게요—그래도 될까요?\" 하고 말하는 것을, 그리고 엠마가 들뜬 웃음과 열의로 \"안 돼요, 안 돼, 그러면 안 돼요."], ["“No, no, you must not; you shall not, indeed.”", "정말로 그러시면 안 돼요\" 하고 막아서는 것을 또렷이 들었다."]], [["It was done however.", "그러나 일은 벌어지고 말았다."], ["This gallant young man, who seemed to love without feeling, and to recommend himself without complaisance, directly handed over the word to Miss Fairfax, and with a particular degree of sedate civility entreated her to study it. Mr.", "마음 없이 사랑하는 듯, 비위 맞추는 일 없이도 호감을 사는 듯한 이 늠름한 청년은, 곧장 그 단어를 미스 페어팩스에게 건네며 유난히 차분하고 정중한 태도로 풀어 보기를 청했다."], ["Knightley’s excessive curiosity to know what this word might be, made him seize every possible moment for darting his eye towards it, and it was not long before he saw it to be Dixon.", "그 단어가 무엇인지 알고 싶은 미스터 나이틀리의 지나친 호기심은 틈만 나면 그쪽으로 눈길을 던지게 했고, 오래지 않아 그것이 '딕슨'임을 알아보았다."], ["Jane Fairfax’s perception seemed to accompany his; her comprehension was certainly more equal to the covert meaning, the superior intelligence, of those five letters so arranged.", "제인 페어팩스의 알아챔도 그와 발맞춘 듯했다. 그렇게 배열된 다섯 글자에 깃든 은밀한 뜻과 한 수 위의 속내를 헤아리는 데는 분명 그녀의 분별이 더 합당했다."], ["She was evidently displeased; looked up, and seeing herself watched, blushed more deeply than he had ever perceived her, and saying only, “I did not know that proper names were allowed,”", "그녀는 역력히 언짢아하며 고개를 들었고, 자신이 살펴지고 있음을 알아채자 그가 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 깊이 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["pushed away the letters with even an angry spirit, and looked resolved to be engaged by no other word that could be offered.", "그러고는 \"고유 명사도 되는 줄은 몰랐네요\"라고만 말하며, 성난 기운까지 내비치며 글자들을 밀쳐 버렸고, 더는 어떤 단어가 주어져도 거기 매이지 않겠다고 작정한 얼굴이었다."], ["Her face was averted from those who had made the attack, and turned towards her aunt.", "그녀는 공세를 펼친 이들에게서 얼굴을 돌려 이모 쪽을 향했다."]], [["“Aye, very true, my dear,”", "\"그래, 정말 그렇구나, 얘야,\""], ["cried the latter, though Jane had not spoken a word—“I was just going to say the same thing.", "제인이 한마디도 하지 않았는데도 이모가 외쳤다. \"나도 막 같은 말을 하려던 참이었어."], ["It is time for us to be going indeed.", "정말 우리 일어설 때가 됐지."], ["The evening is closing in, and grandmama will be looking for us.", "저녁이 깊어 가고, 할머니가 우리를 기다리실 거야."], ["My dear sir, you are too obliging.", "선생님, 너무도 친절하셔라."], ["We really must wish you good night.”", "정말로 이만 작별 인사를 드려야겠어요.\""]], [["Jane’s alertness in moving, proved her as ready as her aunt had preconceived.", "제인이 자리에서 잽싸게 움직이는 것은, 이모가 미리 짐작한 그대로 채비가 되어 있었음을 증명했다."], ["She was immediately up, and wanting to quit the table; but so many were also moving, that she could not get away; and Mr.", "그녀는 곧장 일어나 식탁을 떠나고 싶어 했으나, 너무 여럿이 함께 움직이는 통에 빠져나갈 수가 없었다."], ["Knightley thought he saw another collection of letters anxiously pushed towards her, and resolutely swept away by her unexamined.", "미스터 나이틀리는 또 한 무더기의 글자가 그녀 쪽으로 안달하듯 밀려갔다가, 그녀가 들여다보지도 않고 단호하게 쓸어내 버리는 것을 본 듯했다."], ["She was afterwards looking for her shawl—Frank Churchill was looking also—it was growing dusk, and the room was in confusion; and how they parted, Mr. Knightley could not tell.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 숄을 찾고 있었고—프랭크 처칠도 함께 찾고 있었다—날이 어둑해지고 방 안은 어수선했으니, 두 사람이 어떻게 헤어졌는지는 미스터 나이틀리도 알 수 없었다."]], [["He remained at Hartfield after all the rest, his thoughts full of what he had seen; so full, that when the candles came to assist his observations, he must—yes, he certainly must, as a friend—an anxious friend—give Emma some hint, ask her some question.", "그는 나머지 사람들이 모두 떠난 뒤에도 하트필드에 남았다. 자신이 본 것으로 머릿속이 가득 차 있었다."], ["He could not see her in a situation of such danger, without trying to preserve her.", "어찌나 가득했는지, 관찰을 거들 양초가 들어왔을 때, 그는 반드시—그래, 분명히, 벗으로서—마음 졸이는 벗으로서—엠마에게 무언가 귀띔을 하고, 무언가 물어보아야만 했다. 그토록 위태로운 처지에 놓인 그녀를 보면서, 지켜 주려 애쓰지 않을 수는 없었다."], ["It was his duty.", "그것이 그의 도리였다."]], [["“Pray, Emma,”", "\"엠마,\""], ["said he, “may I ask in what lay the great amusement, the poignant sting of the last word given to you and Miss Fairfax?", "그가 말했다. \"마지막에 자네와 미스 페어팩스에게 건네진 그 단어가 무엇이 그리 큰 재미를, 또 무엇이 그리 따가운 가시를 품고 있었는지 물어봐도 되겠나?"], ["I saw the word, and am curious to know how it could be so very entertaining to the one, and so very distressing to the other.”", "그 단어를 보긴 했는데, 그게 어떻게 한쪽에는 그토록 재미있고 다른 쪽에는 그토록 괴로운 것이 될 수 있는지 궁금하군.\""]], [["Emma was extremely confused.", "엠마는 몹시 당황했다."], ["She could not endure to give him the true explanation; for though her suspicions were by no means removed, she was really ashamed of having ever imparted them.", "그에게 사실대로 설명하는 것은 견딜 수 없는 일이었다. 그녀의 의심이 결코 가신 것은 아니었으나, 그것을 한때 입에 올린 적이 있다는 사실 자체가 정말이지 부끄러웠던 것이다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아유!\" 그녀가 역력한 곤혹 속에 외쳤다."], ["she cried in evident embarrassment, “it all meant nothing; a mere joke among ourselves.”", "\"다 별 뜻 없는 거예요. 우리끼리 그저 농담이었죠.\""]], [["“The joke,”", "\"그 농담이라는 게,\" 그가 진지하게 받았다."], ["he replied gravely, “seemed confined to you and Mr. Churchill.”", "\"자네와 미스터 처칠 둘 사이에만 통하는 것이던데.\""]], [["He had hoped she would speak again, but she did not.", "그는 그녀가 다시 입을 열기를 바랐으나, 그녀는 그러지 않았다."], ["She would rather busy herself about any thing than speak.", "무슨 일이든 분주히 하는 편이 말하는 것보다 나았던 것이다."], ["He sat a little while in doubt.", "그는 한동안 미심쩍은 채 앉아 있었다."], ["A variety of evils crossed his mind.", "갖가지 불길한 생각이 머릿속을 스쳤다."], ["Interference—fruitless interference.", "참견—부질없는 참견."], ["Emma’s confusion, and the acknowledged intimacy, seemed to declare her affection engaged.", "엠마의 당황과 두 사람이 인정한 친밀함은, 그녀의 마음이 이미 매여 있음을 말해 주는 듯했다."], ["Yet he would speak.", "그래도 그는 말하기로 했다."], ["He owed it to her, to risk any thing that might be involved in an unwelcome interference, rather than her welfare; to encounter any thing, rather than the remembrance of neglect in such a cause.", "그녀의 안녕을 저버리기보다는, 달갑잖은 참견에 따를 그 무엇이든 무릅쓰는 편이 그녀에게 마땅한 도리였다. 그런 일을 두고 외면했다는 기억을 떠안느니, 무엇이든 마주하는 편이 나았다."]], [["“My dear Emma,”", "\"엠마,\""], ["said he at last, with earnest kindness, “do you think you perfectly understand the degree of acquaintance between the gentleman and lady we have been speaking of?”", "그가 마침내 진심 어린 다정함으로 말했다. \"우리가 얘기하던 그 신사와 숙녀 사이가 어느 정도의 친분인지, 자네가 완전히 안다고 생각하나?\""]], [["“Between Mr. Frank Churchill and Miss Fairfax?", "\"미스터 프랭크 처칠과 미스 페어팩스 사이요?"], ["Oh!", "아유!"], ["yes, perfectly.—Why do you make a doubt of it?”", "그럼요, 완전히 알죠.—왜 그걸 의심하시는데요?\""]], [["“Have you never at any time had reason to think that he admired her, or that she admired him?”", "\"언젠가 한 번이라도, 그가 그녀를 흠모한다거나 그녀가 그를 흠모한다고 여길 만한 일이 없었나?\""]], [["“Never, never!”", "\"없었어요, 한 번도요!\""], ["she cried with a most open eagerness—“Never, for the twentieth part of a moment, did such an idea occur to me.", "그녀가 더없이 거리낌 없는 열의로 외쳤다.—\"한순간의 이십분의 일만큼도 그런 생각이 떠오른 적이 없어요."], ["And how could it possibly come into your head?”", "대체 어떻게 그런 생각이 머릿속에 드신 거예요?\""]], [["“I have lately imagined that I saw symptoms of attachment between them—certain expressive looks, which I did not believe meant to be public.”", "\"근래에 그 두 사람 사이에서 연정의 기미를 본 것 같았거든—어딘가 뜻을 담은 듯한 눈길들, 남들 보라고 한 건 아니었을 그런 눈길 말일세.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아유!"], ["you amuse me excessively.", "정말 너무 우습네요."], ["I am delighted to find that you can vouchsafe to let your imagination wander—but it will not do—very sorry to check you in your first essay—but indeed it will not do.", "당신도 상상의 나래를 펼치는 호사를 누릴 줄 아신다니 반갑기 그지없는데—하지만 그건 아니에요—첫 시도부터 찬물을 끼얹어 정말 미안하지만—정말이지 그건 아니에요."], ["There is no admiration between them, I do assure you; and the appearances which have caught you, have arisen from some peculiar circumstances—feelings rather of a totally different nature—it is impossible exactly to explain:—there is a good deal of nonsense in it—but the part which is capable of being communicated, which is sense, is, that they are as far from any attachment or admiration for one another, as any two beings in the world can be.", "두 사람 사이엔 흠모 따위 없어요, 장담해요. 당신 눈에 띈 그 정황들은 어떤 특이한 사정에서 비롯된 거예요—오히려 전혀 다른 성질의 감정이랄까—정확히 설명하기란 불가능하고요.—거기엔 어처구니없는 구석이 많아요—하지만 전할 수 있는 부분, 즉 이치에 닿는 부분만 말하자면, 두 사람은 세상 어느 두 존재만큼이나 서로에 대한 연정이나 흠모와는 거리가 멀다는 거예요."], ["That is, I presume it to be so on her side, and I can answer for its being so on his.", "그러니까, 그녀 쪽에 대해서는 그렇다고 짐작하는 거고, 그 신사 쪽에 대해서는 제가 장담할 수 있어요."], ["I will answer for the gentleman’s indifference.”", "그 신사가 무심하다는 건 제가 보증하지요.\""]], [["She spoke with a confidence which staggered, with a satisfaction which silenced, Mr. Knightley.", "그녀는 미스터 나이틀리를 휘청이게 할 만큼 확신에 차서, 그를 입 다물게 할 만큼 흡족하게 말했다."], ["She was in gay spirits, and would have prolonged the conversation, wanting to hear the particulars of his suspicions, every look described, and all the wheres and hows of a circumstance which highly entertained her: but his gaiety did not meet hers.", "그녀는 들떠 있었고, 대화를 더 이어 가고 싶어 했다. 그의 의심이 어떤 것인지 자세히 듣고, 눈길 하나하나가 어땠는지 묘사해 달라고 하며, 자신을 무척 즐겁게 하는 그 정황의 온갖 어디서며 어떻게를 캐묻고 싶었던 것이다."], ["He found he could not be useful, and his feelings were too much irritated for talking.", "그러나 그의 기분은 그녀의 들뜸에 발맞추지 못했다."], ["That he might not be irritated into an absolute fever, by the fire which Mr.", "그는 자신이 쓸모가 없다는 것을 깨달았고, 그의 감정은 이야기를 잇기엔 너무 들끓어 있었다."], ["Woodhouse’s tender habits required almost every evening throughout the year, he soon afterwards took a hasty leave, and walked home to the coolness and solitude of Donwell Abbey.", "미스터 우드하우스의 다정한 습성이 한 해 내내 거의 매일 저녁 요구하는 그 난롯불에 부추겨져 영영 열병에 빠지는 일이 없도록, 그는 곧 황급히 작별을 고하고, 돈웰 애비의 서늘함과 고요 속으로 걸어 돌아갔다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter VI", "# 제3권 6장"]], [["After being long fed with hopes of a speedy visit from Mr. and Mrs.", "서클링 부부가 곧 찾아오리라는 기대로 오랫동안 마음을 부풀려 온 끝에, 하이베리 사람들은 가을이 되기 전에는 도무지 올 수 없다는 소식을 듣고 분한 마음을 삭여야 했다."], ["Suckling, the Highbury world were obliged to endure the mortification of hearing that they could not possibly come till the autumn.", "그처럼 신선한 새 인물의 유입으로 그들의 지적 곳간을 채울 일은 당분간 없게 된 셈이었다."], ["No such importation of novelties could enrich their intellectual stores at present. In the daily interchange of news, they must be again restricted to the other topics with which for a while the Sucklings’ coming had been united, such as the last accounts of Mrs.", "날마다 오가는 소식의 교환에서 그들은 또다시 다른 화제들로 만족해야 했는데, 그것들은 한동안 서클링 부부의 방문과 한데 묶여 있던 것들이었다."], ["Churchill, whose health seemed every day to supply a different report, and the situation of Mrs.", "이를테면 날마다 다른 보고가 들려오는 듯한 처칠 부인의 건강 소식, 그리고 웨스턴 부인의 형편 같은 것이었다."], ["Weston, whose happiness it was to be hoped might eventually be as much increased by the arrival of a child, as that of all her neighbours was by the approach of it.", "웨스턴 부인의 경우, 이웃들 모두가 아이의 출산이 다가오는 것으로 행복이 더해지듯, 그녀 자신의 행복도 마침내 아이가 태어남으로써 그만큼 커지기를 바랄 만했다."]], [["Mrs. Elton was very much disappointed.", "엘턴 부인은 무척 실망했다."], ["It was the delay of a great deal of pleasure and parade.", "그것은 숱한 즐거움과 과시의 기회가 미뤄진다는 뜻이었다."], ["Her introductions and recommendations must all wait, and every projected party be still only talked of.", "그녀의 소개와 추천도 죄다 기다려야 했고, 계획해 둔 모임마다 여전히 말로만 그쳐야 했다."], ["So she thought at first;—but a little consideration convinced her that every thing need not be put off.", "처음에는 그렇게 생각했지만, 조금 궁리해 보니 무엇이든 다 미룰 필요는 없다는 확신이 들었다."], ["Why should not they explore to Box Hill though the Sucklings did not come?", "서클링 부부가 안 온다고 해서 박스 힐로 나들이를 못 갈 까닭이 어디 있단 말인가?"], ["They could go there again with them in the autumn.", "가을에 그 부부와 다시 한번 가면 그만이었다."], ["It was settled that they should go to Box Hill.", "그리하여 박스 힐로 가기로 정해졌다."], ["That there was to be such a party had been long generally known: it had even given the idea of another.", "그런 모임이 있으리라는 것은 진작부터 두루 알려진 일이었고, 그것이 또 하나의 나들이를 떠올리게 했을 정도였다. 엠마는 박스 힐에 한 번도 가 본 적이 없었다."], ["Emma had never been to Box Hill; she wished to see what every body found so well worth seeing, and she and Mr. Weston had agreed to chuse some fine morning and drive thither.", "누구나 그토록 가 볼 만하다고 입을 모으는 그곳을 보고 싶었기에, 그녀와 미스터 웨스턴은 어느 화창한 아침을 골라 그리로 마차를 몰기로 합의해 둔 터였다."], ["Two or three more of the chosen only were to be admitted to join them, and it was to be done in a quiet, unpretending, elegant way, infinitely superior to the bustle and preparation, the regular eating and drinking, and picnic parade of the Eltons and the Sucklings.", "거기에 끼워 줄 사람은 엄선한 두세 명뿐이었고, 그 일은 조용하고 점잖고 우아하게, 엘턴 부부와 서클링 부부 식의 부산함과 준비, 으레 차려 먹고 마시는 절차며 피크닉의 과시와는 비할 데 없이 격이 높게 치러질 예정이었다."]], [["This was so very well understood between them, that Emma could not but feel some surprise, and a little displeasure, on hearing from Mr. Weston that he had been proposing to Mrs.", "이것은 두 사람 사이에 워낙 잘 합의가 되어 있던 터라, 엠마는 미스터 웨스턴에게서 다음과 같은 말을 듣고는 적잖이 놀라고 약간 언짢지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Elton, as her brother and sister had failed her, that the two parties should unite, and go together; and that as Mrs.", "엘턴 부인의 언니 내외가 그녀에게 바람을 맞힌 셈이 되었으니, 두 모임을 합쳐 함께 가자고 엘턴 부인에게 제안했다는 것이었다."], ["Elton had very readily acceded to it, so it was to be, if she had no objection.", "그리고 엘턴 부인이 아주 선뜻 응했으니, 엠마가 반대하지 않는 한 그렇게 하기로 했다는 것이었다."], ["Now, as her objection was nothing but her very great dislike of Mrs. Elton, of which Mr.", "그런데 엠마의 반대라는 것은 엘턴 부인을 몹시 싫어한다는 것 말고는 아무것도 아니었고, 그 점은 미스터 웨스턴도 이미 훤히 알고 있을 터라 새삼 끄집어낼 가치가 없었다."], ["Weston must already be perfectly aware, it was not worth bringing forward again:—it could not be done without a reproof to him, which would be giving pain to his wife; and she found herself therefore obliged to consent to an arrangement which she would have done a great deal to avoid; an arrangement which would probably expose her even to the degradation of being said to be of Mrs.", "그것을 입에 올리자면 미스터 웨스턴을 나무라는 일이 될 테고, 그것은 곧 그의 아내를 아프게 하는 일이 될 터였다. 그리하여 엠마는 어떻게든 피하고 싶었던 결정에 동의할 수밖에 없는 처지가 되었으니 — 어쩌면 엘턴 부인 일행에 끼었다는 말까지 듣게 될 굴욕에 자신을 내맡기는 결정이었다!"], ["Elton’s party!", "온갖 감정이 상했다."], ["Every feeling was offended; and the forbearance of her outward submission left a heavy arrear due of secret severity in her reflections on the unmanageable goodwill of Mr. Weston’s temper.", "겉으로 순순히 따르며 참아 넘긴 대가로, 미스터 웨스턴의 그 종잡을 수 없는 선의(善意)를 두고 마음속에서 호되게 곱씹는 빚이 무겁게 남았다."]], [["“I am glad you approve of what I have done,”", "\"내가 한 일을 당신이 좋게 봐 주니 기쁘오.\""], ["said he very comfortably.", "그가 아주 편안하게 말했다."], ["“But I thought you would.", "\"하지만 그러리라고 생각했소."], ["Such schemes as these are nothing without numbers.", "이런 계획은 사람 수가 적으면 아무 재미가 없거든."], ["One cannot have too large a party.", "일행은 많을수록 좋은 법이오."], ["A large party secures its own amusement.", "일행이 많으면 그 자체로 흥이 보장되지요."], ["And she is a good-natured woman after all.", "그리고 어쨌든 그 부인은 마음씨가 너그러운 사람이오."], ["One could not leave her out.”", "빼놓을 수야 없지요.\""]], [["Emma denied none of it aloud, and agreed to none of it in private.", "엠마는 그 어느 것도 소리 내어 부정하지 않았고, 마음속으로는 그 어느 것에도 동의하지 않았다."]], [["It was now the middle of June, and the weather fine; and Mrs.", "어느덧 유월 중순이라 날씨가 화창했다."], ["Elton was growing impatient to name the day, and settle with Mr. Weston as to pigeon-pies and cold lamb, when a lame carriage-horse threw every thing into sad uncertainty.", "엘턴 부인은 날을 잡아 미스터 웨스턴과 비둘기 파이며 차가운 양고기 따위를 정하고 싶어 안달이 났는데, 그때 마차 끄는 말 한 마리가 다리를 절면서 모든 일이 답답한 불확실 속으로 빠져들었다."], ["It might be weeks, it might be only a few days, before the horse were useable; but no preparations could be ventured on, and it was all melancholy stagnation.", "그 말을 다시 쓸 수 있게 되기까지 몇 주가 걸릴지, 며칠이면 될지 알 수 없었다. 하지만 어떤 준비도 함부로 할 수가 없어, 모든 것이 침울한 정체에 빠졌다."], ["Mrs. Elton’s resources were inadequate to such an attack.", "엘턴 부인의 임기응변으로는 이런 타격을 감당하기에 역부족이었다."]], [["“Is not this most vexatious, Knightley?”", "\"이거야말로 정말 약 오르는 일 아니에요, 나이틀리?\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["she cried.—“And such weather for exploring!—These delays and disappointments are quite odious.", "\"이렇게 나들이하기 좋은 날씨에! 이런 지연과 차질이라니, 정말이지 진절머리가 나요."], ["What are we to do?—The year will wear away at this rate, and nothing done.", "어쩌면 좋죠? 이러다간 한 해가 그냥 흘러가 버리고 아무것도 못 하겠어요."], ["Before this time last year I assure you we had had a delightful exploring party from Maple Grove to Kings Weston.”", "작년 이맘때만 해도 우린 메이플 그로브에서 킹스 웨스턴까지 정말 즐거운 나들이를 했었다고요.\""]], [["“You had better explore to Donwell,”", "\"돈웰로 나들이를 오시는 게 낫겠습니다.\""], ["replied Mr. Knightley.", "미스터 나이틀리가 답했다."], ["“That may be done without horses.", "\"거긴 말 없이도 갈 수 있으니까요."], ["Come, and eat my strawberries.", "오셔서 제 딸기나 드시지요."], ["They are ripening fast.”", "한창 익어 가고 있습니다.\""]], [["If Mr. Knightley did not begin seriously, he was obliged to proceed so, for his proposal was caught at with delight; and the “Oh!", "미스터 나이틀리가 처음에는 진심으로 한 말이 아니었다 해도, 이제는 그렇게 밀고 나갈 수밖에 없게 되었다."], ["I should like it of all things,”", "그의 제안이 환호 속에 덥석 받아들여졌기 때문이다."], ["was not plainer in words than manner.", "\"어머! 그보다 좋은 일이 어디 있겠어요\"라는 말이 어조에서도 말 못지않게 또렷이 드러났다."], ["Donwell was famous for its strawberry-beds, which seemed a plea for the invitation: but no plea was necessary; cabbage-beds would have been enough to tempt the lady, who only wanted to be going somewhere.", "돈웰은 딸기밭으로 유명했고, 그것이 초대의 구실이 될 만했다. 하지만 구실 따위는 필요 없었다."], ["She promised him again and again to come—much oftener than he doubted—and was extremely gratified by such a proof of intimacy, such a distinguishing compliment as she chose to consider it.", "그저 어디론가 가고 싶을 뿐인 이 부인에게는 양배추밭이라도 충분히 유혹이 되었을 것이다. 그녀는 가겠노라고 거듭거듭 약속했으니 — 그가 의심한 것보다 훨씬 여러 번이었다 — 그러한 친밀의 증표, 그녀 스스로 각별한 예우라 여기기로 한 그 일에 무척이나 흡족해했다."]], [["“You may depend upon me,”", "\"저만 믿으세요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I certainly will come.", "\"꼭 가고말고요."], ["Name your day, and I will come.", "날만 잡으시면 제가 가겠어요."], ["You will allow me to bring Jane Fairfax?”", "제인 페어팩스를 데려가도 괜찮겠죠?\""]], [["“I cannot name a day,”", "\"날을 잡을 수가 없습니다.\""], ["said he, “till I have spoken to some others whom I would wish to meet you.”", "그가 말했다. \"부인과 만나게 하고 싶은 다른 분들 몇과 이야기를 해 보기 전에는요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["leave all that to me.", "그런 건 다 제게 맡기세요."], ["Only give me a carte-blanche.—I am Lady Patroness, you know.", "제게 전권만 주시면 돼요. 제가 후원자 마님이잖아요, 아시다시피."], ["It is my party.", "이건 제 모임이에요."], ["I will bring friends with me.”", "제가 친구들을 데려올게요.\""]], [["“I hope you will bring Elton,”", "\"엘턴은 데려와 주시면 좋겠군요.\""], ["said he: “but I will not trouble you to give any other invitations.”", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 다른 초대는 부인을 수고롭게 해 드리지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["now you are looking very sly.", "이제 보니 아주 능청을 떠시네요."], ["But consider—you need not be afraid of delegating power to me.", "하지만 생각해 보세요. 제게 권한을 맡기는 걸 두려워하실 필요는 없어요."], ["I am no young lady on her preferment.", "저는 시집갈 자리나 노리는 풋내기 아가씨가 아니거든요."], ["Married women, you know, may be safely authorised.", "아시다시피 결혼한 여자한테는 안심하고 권한을 줄 수 있는 거예요."], ["It is my party.", "이건 제 모임이라니까요."], ["Leave it all to me.", "다 제게 맡기세요."], ["I will invite your guests.”", "손님은 제가 초대하겠어요.\""]], [["“No,”—he calmly replied,—“there is but one married woman in the world whom I can ever allow to invite what guests she pleases to Donwell, and that one is—”", "\"안 됩니다.\" 그가 차분히 답했다. \"이 세상에 돈웰로 제 마음대로 손님을 초대해도 좋다고 제가 허락할 수 있는 결혼한 여자는 단 한 사람뿐인데, 그 한 사람은—\""]], [["“—Mrs. Weston, I suppose,”", "\"—웨스턴 부인이겠죠.\""], ["interrupted Mrs. Elton, rather mortified.", "엘턴 부인이 다소 분한 기색으로 말을 가로챘다."]], [["“No—Mrs.", "\"아니요."], ["Knightley;—and till she is in being, I will manage such matters myself.”", "나이틀리 부인입니다. 그리고 그런 사람이 생기기 전까지는 그런 일은 제가 알아서 처리하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"어머!"], ["you are an odd creature!”", "정말 별난 양반이시네요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["she cried, satisfied to have no one preferred to herself.—“You are a humourist, and may say what you like.", "자기보다 더 우대받는 사람이 없다는 데 만족한 것이었다. \"당신은 익살꾼이라 하고 싶은 말은 무엇이든 하는군요."], ["Quite a humourist.", "영락없는 익살꾼이에요."], ["Well, I shall bring Jane with me—Jane and her aunt.—The rest I leave to you.", "좋아요, 그럼 제인을 데려가겠어요 — 제인하고 그 이모분을요. 나머지는 당신께 맡길게요."], ["I have no objections at all to meeting the Hartfield family.", "하트필드 식구들과 만나는 거야 저는 전혀 반대하지 않아요."], ["Don’t scruple.", "꺼리지 마세요."], ["I know you are attached to them.”", "당신이 그 식구들을 아끼는 줄 다 아니까요.\""]], [["“You certainly will meet them if I can prevail; and I shall call on Miss Bates in my way home.”", "\"제 청이 통한다면야 그분들도 분명 만나시게 될 겁니다. 돌아가는 길에 제가 미스 베이츠한테 들르겠습니다.\""]], [["“That’s quite unnecessary; I see Jane every day:—but as you like.", "\"그건 전혀 그러실 필요 없어요. 제가 제인을 날마다 보는걸요."], ["It is to be a morning scheme, you know, Knightley; quite a simple thing.", "하지만 좋으실 대로 하세요. 아시다시피 이건 오전 모임이에요, 나이틀리."], ["I shall wear a large bonnet, and bring one of my little baskets hanging on my arm.", "아주 소박한 거죠. 저는 챙 넓은 보닛을 쓰고, 작은 바구니 하나를 팔에 걸고 가겠어요."], ["Here,—probably this basket with pink ribbon.", "자, 아마 이 분홍 리본 달린 바구니일 거예요. 보시다시피 이보다 더 소박할 수가 없죠."], ["Nothing can be more simple, you see.", "제인도 꼭 이런 걸 들 거예요. 격식이나 과시 같은 건 없는 거예요."], ["And Jane will have such another.", "일종의 집시* 모임 같은 거죠."], ["There is to be no form or parade—a sort of gipsy party.", "우리는 당신 정원을 거닐다가 딸기를 직접 따고, 나무 아래 앉을 거예요. 그 밖에 무엇을 준비하시든 다 바깥에서 하는 거예요."], ["We are to walk about your gardens, and gather the strawberries ourselves, and sit under trees;—and whatever else you may like to provide, it is to be all out of doors—a table spread in the shade, you know.", "아시다시피 그늘에 상을 차리는 거죠. 모든 걸 되도록 자연스럽고 소박하게요."], ["Every thing as natural and simple as possible.", "그게 당신 생각 아니세요?\" * 집시: 떠돌이 생활을 하는 사람들."], ["Is not that your idea?”", "격식 없이 야외에서 즐기는 모임을 빗댄 표현."]], [["“Not quite.", "\"꼭 그렇지는 않습니다."], ["My idea of the simple and the natural will be to have the table spread in the dining-room.", "제가 생각하는 소박하고 자연스러운 것이란 식당에 상을 차리는 겁니다."], ["The nature and the simplicity of gentlemen and ladies, with their servants and furniture, I think is best observed by meals within doors.", "신사 숙녀들이, 거기에 하인들과 가구까지 딸린 사람들이 지닌 자연스러움과 소박함이란, 실내에서 식사를 할 때 가장 잘 지켜진다고 봅니다."], ["When you are tired of eating strawberries in the garden, there shall be cold meat in the house.”", "정원에서 딸기 따 먹는 데 싫증이 나시면, 집 안에 차가운 고기 요리가 있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well—as you please; only don’t have a great set out.", "\"좋아요, 마음대로 하세요. 다만 너무 거창하게 차리지는 마시고요."], ["And, by the bye, can I or my housekeeper be of any use to you with our opinion?—Pray be sincere, Knightley.", "그런데 말이 나왔으니 말인데, 저나 저희 집 가정부가 의견으로라도 도움이 될 일이 있을까요? 부디 솔직히 말씀해 주세요, 나이틀리."], ["If you wish me to talk to Mrs. Hodges, or to inspect anything—”", "제가 호지스 부인하고 이야기를 한다든가, 뭘 좀 살펴봐 드리길 바라신다면—\""]], [["“I have not the least wish for it, I thank you.”", "\"그러실 마음은 조금도 없으니, 마음만 감사히 받겠습니다.\""]], [["“Well—but if any difficulties should arise, my housekeeper is extremely clever.”", "\"좋아요, 하지만 무슨 어려움이라도 생기면, 저희 집 가정부가 아주 영리하답니다.\""]], [["“I will answer for it, that mine thinks herself full as clever, and would spurn any body’s assistance.”", "\"단언컨대, 우리 집 가정부도 자기가 그 못지않게 영리하다고 자부하고 있어서, 누구의 도움이든 콧방귀를 뀔 겁니다.\""]], [["“I wish we had a donkey.", "\"우리한테 당나귀가 있으면 좋을 텐데요."], ["The thing would be for us all to come on donkeys, Jane, Miss Bates, and me—and my caro sposo walking by.", "우리 모두 당나귀를 타고 가는 거예요, 제인하고 미스 베이츠하고 저하고요 — 그리고 제 사랑하는 남편(caro sposo)은 옆에서 걷고요. 정말이지 그이한테 당나귀를 한 마리 사자고 말해 봐야겠어요."], ["I really must talk to him about purchasing a donkey.", "시골 생활에서는 그게 일종의 필수품이라고 생각하거든요."], ["In a country life I conceive it to be a sort of necessary; for, let a woman have ever so many resources, it is not possible for her to be always shut up at home;—and very long walks, you know—in summer there is dust, and in winter there is dirt.”", "여자한테 아무리 소일거리가 많아도, 늘 집 안에만 틀어박혀 있을 수는 없는 노릇이잖아요. 그리고 아주 긴 산책도요, 아시다시피 — 여름엔 먼지가, 겨울엔 진창이 문제죠.\""]], [["“You will not find either, between Donwell and Highbury.", "\"돈웰과 하이베리 사이에서는 둘 다 만나실 일이 없을 겁니다."], ["Donwell Lane is never dusty, and now it is perfectly dry.", "돈웰 가는 길은 먼지가 인 적이 없고, 지금은 더없이 보송보송하니까요."], ["Come on a donkey, however, if you prefer it.", "그래도 당나귀가 좋으시면 타고 오십시오."], ["You can borrow Mrs. Cole’s.", "콜 부인 것을 빌리시면 됩니다."], ["I would wish every thing to be as much to your taste as possible.”", "저는 무엇이든 되도록 부인 취향에 맞춰 드리고 싶거든요.\""]], [["“That I am sure you would.", "\"그러시고말고요."], ["Indeed I do you justice, my good friend.", "정말이지 저는 당신을 공정하게 봐요, 나의 좋은 친구여."], ["Under that peculiar sort of dry, blunt manner, I know you have the warmest heart. As I tell Mr.", "그 특유의 무뚝뚝하고 퉁명스러운 태도 아래, 당신이 더없이 따뜻한 마음을 지녔다는 걸 저는 알아요."], ["E., you are a thorough humourist.—Yes, believe me, Knightley, I am fully sensible of your attention to me in the whole of this scheme.", "제가 우리 엘턴 씨한테도 늘 말하지만, 당신은 영락없는 익살꾼이에요. 그럼요, 정말이에요, 나이틀리, 이번 계획 전체에서 당신이 저한테 베푼 배려를 저는 충분히 느끼고 있어요."], ["You have hit upon the very thing to please me.”", "저를 기쁘게 할 바로 그 일을 짚어 내셨다니까요.\""]], [["Mr. Knightley had another reason for avoiding a table in the shade.", "미스터 나이틀리에게는 그늘에 상을 차리기를 피하려는 또 다른 이유가 있었다."], ["He wished to persuade Mr. Woodhouse, as well as Emma, to join the party; and he knew that to have any of them sitting down out of doors to eat would inevitably make him ill.", "그는 엠마뿐 아니라 미스터 우드하우스도 이 모임에 끌어들이고 싶었는데, 그들 중 누구라도 야외에 앉아 식사를 하면 미스터 우드하우스가 영락없이 탈이 나리라는 것을 알았기 때문이다."], ["Mr. Woodhouse must not, under the specious pretence of a morning drive, and an hour or two spent at Donwell, be tempted away to his misery.", "미스터 우드하우스가 오전 마차 나들이며 돈웰에서 한두 시간 보내는 일이라는 그럴듯한 핑계에 넘어가, 제 발로 고생길에 들어서게 해서는 안 될 일이었다."]], [["He was invited on good faith.", "그는 진심 어린 초대를 받았다."], ["No lurking horrors were to upbraid him for his easy credulity.", "어떤 숨은 두려움도 그의 순순한 믿음을 두고 그를 나무라지 못할 것이었다."], ["He did consent.", "그는 응했다."], ["He had not been at Donwell for two years.", "그는 이태 동안 돈웰에 가 본 적이 없었다."], ["“Some very fine morning, he, and Emma, and Harriet, could go very well; and he could sit still with Mrs.", "\"어느 아주 화창한 아침에 자기와 엠마와 해리엇이 가면 좋겠다. 사랑스러운 아가씨들이 정원을 거니는 동안 자기는 웨스턴 부인과 가만히 앉아 있으면 된다."], ["Weston, while the dear girls walked about the gardens.", "한낮이라면 지금쯤 그곳도 축축하지는 않을 게다."], ["He did not suppose they could be damp now, in the middle of the day.", "그 옛집을 다시 보면 더없이 좋겠고, 엘턴 부부며 다른 이웃들을 만나는 것도 무척 기쁜 일이겠다."], ["He should like to see the old house again exceedingly, and should be very happy to meet Mr.", "어느 아주 화창한 아침에 자기와 엠마와 해리엇이 그리로 가는 데 아무런 거리낄 것이 없다."], ["and Mrs. Elton, and any other of his neighbours.—He could not see any objection at all to his, and Emma’s, and Harriet’s going there some very fine morning.", "미스터 나이틀리가 그들을 초대한 것은 참 잘한 일이다 — 무척 친절하고 분별 있는 처사다 — 밖에서 만찬을 드는 것보다 훨씬 사려 깊은 일이고말고."], ["He thought it very well done of Mr. Knightley to invite them—very kind and sensible—much cleverer than dining out.—He was not fond of dining out.”", "자기는 밖에서 만찬 드는 걸 좋아하지 않으니까.\""]], [["Mr. Knightley was fortunate in every body’s most ready concurrence.", "미스터 나이틀리는 모두가 더없이 선뜻 동의해 주는 행운을 누렸다."], ["The invitation was everywhere so well received, that it seemed as if, like Mrs. Elton, they were all taking the scheme as a particular compliment to themselves.—Emma and Harriet professed very high expectations of pleasure from it; and Mr.", "초대는 어디서나 워낙 잘 받아들여져서, 엘턴 부인처럼 다들 이 계획을 자기에게 베푼 각별한 예우로 받아들이는 것만 같았다."], ["Weston, unasked, promised to get Frank over to join them, if possible; a proof of approbation and gratitude which could have been dispensed with.—Mr.", "엠마와 해리엇은 거기서 큰 즐거움을 얻으리라는 높은 기대를 내비쳤고, 미스터 웨스턴은 청하지도 않았는데 가능하면 프랭크를 데려와 합류시키겠노라고 약속했다 — 굳이 받지 않아도 좋았을 찬동과 감사의 표시였다."], ["Knightley was then obliged to say that he should be glad to see him; and Mr. Weston engaged to lose no time in writing, and spare no arguments to induce him to come.", "그리하여 미스터 나이틀리는 그를 만나면 반갑겠다고 말할 수밖에 없었고, 미스터 웨스턴은 지체 없이 편지를 쓰고, 그가 오도록 온갖 논리를 다 동원하겠노라고 다짐했다."]], [["In the meanwhile the lame horse recovered so fast, that the party to Box Hill was again under happy consideration; and at last Donwell was settled for one day, and Box Hill for the next,—the weather appearing exactly right.", "그러는 사이 다리를 절던 말이 어찌나 빨리 회복되었던지, 박스 힐 모임이 다시 즐거운 검토 대상으로 떠올랐다. 마침내 하루는 돈웰, 그 이튿날은 박스 힐로 정해졌는데 — 날씨도 더없이 알맞아 보였다."]], [["Under a bright mid-day sun, at almost Midsummer, Mr.", "거의 한여름에 이른 무렵, 환한 한낮의 햇살 아래, 미스터 우드하우스는 창문 하나를 내린 마차로 무사히 모셔져 이 야외 모임에 함께하게 되었다."], ["Woodhouse was safely conveyed in his carriage, with one window down, to partake of this al-fresco party; and in one of the most comfortable rooms in the Abbey, especially prepared for him by a fire all the morning, he was happily placed, quite at his ease, ready to talk with pleasure of what had been achieved, and advise every body to come and sit down, and not to heat themselves.—Mrs.", "그리고 애비에서 가장 안락한 방 하나, 오전 내내 불을 지펴 그를 위해 특별히 마련해 둔 방에 흐뭇하게 자리를 잡고, 더없이 편안하게, 무엇이 잘 갖춰졌는지 즐겁게 이야기할 채비를 하고서, 모두에게 와서 앉으라고, 몸을 더위에 달구지 말라고 권할 태세였다."], ["Weston, who seemed to have walked there on purpose to be tired, and sit all the time with him, remained, when all the others were invited or persuaded out, his patient listener and sympathiser.", "웨스턴 부인은 마치 일부러 지치려고, 또 내내 그의 곁에 앉아 있으려고 걸어온 사람처럼, 다른 이들이 모두 초대받거나 떠밀려 밖으로 나간 뒤에도 남아, 그의 참을성 있는 청자(聽者)이자 동조자가 되어 주었다."]], [["It was so long since Emma had been at the Abbey, that as soon as she was satisfied of her father’s comfort, she was glad to leave him, and look around her; eager to refresh and correct her memory with more particular observation, more exact understanding of a house and grounds which must ever be so interesting to her and all her family.", "엠마가 애비에 와 본 지 워낙 오래되었던지라, 아버지가 편안한 것을 확인하자마자 그녀는 기꺼이 자리를 떠나 주위를 둘러보았다. 그녀 자신과 온 가족에게 언제나 그토록 흥미로울 수밖에 없는 이 집과 정원을, 더 세세히 관찰하고 더 정확히 이해하여 기억을 새로이 하고 바로잡고 싶은 열망에서였다."]], [["She felt all the honest pride and complacency which her alliance with the present and future proprietor could fairly warrant, as she viewed the respectable size and style of the building, its suitable, becoming, characteristic situation, low and sheltered—its ample gardens stretching down to meadows washed by a stream, of which the Abbey, with all the old neglect of prospect, had scarcely a sight—and its abundance of timber in rows and avenues, which neither fashion nor extravagance had rooted up.—The house was larger than Hartfield, and totally unlike it, covering a good deal of ground, rambling and irregular, with many comfortable, and one or two handsome rooms.—It was just what it ought to be, and it looked what it was—and Emma felt an increasing respect for it, as the residence of a family of such true gentility, untainted in blood and understanding.—Some faults of temper John Knightley had; but Isabella had connected herself unexceptionably. She had given them neither men, nor names, nor places, that could raise a blush. These were pleasant feelings, and she walked about and indulged them till it was necessary to do as the others did, and collect round the strawberry-beds.—The whole party were assembled, excepting Frank Churchill, who was expected every moment from Richmond; and Mrs. Elton, in all her apparatus of happiness, her large bonnet and her basket, was very ready to lead the way in gathering, accepting, or talking—strawberries, and only strawberries, could now be thought or spoken of.—“The best fruit in England—every body’s favourite—always wholesome.—These the finest beds and finest sorts.—Delightful to gather for one’s self—the only way of really enjoying them.—Morning decidedly the best time—never tired—every sort good—hautboy infinitely superior—no comparison—the others hardly eatable—hautboys very scarce—Chili preferred—white wood finest flavour of all—price of strawberries in London—abundance about Bristol—Maple Grove—cultivation—beds when to be renewed—gardeners thinking exactly different—no general rule—gardeners never to be put out of their way—delicious fruit—only too rich to be eaten much of—inferior to cherries—currants more refreshing—only objection to gathering strawberries the stooping—glaring sun—tired to death—could bear it no longer—must go and sit in the shade.”", "현재와 미래의 소유주와 맺은 인연이 떳떳이 보증해 줄 만한 온갖 정직한 자부심과 흡족함을 그녀는 느꼈다. 건물의 그 점잖은 규모와 양식을 바라보면서, 낮고 아늑하게 자리 잡은 그 적절하고 어울리며 개성 있는 위치를 바라보면서 — 시냇물이 적셔 주는 초원까지 뻗어 내려간 그 너른 정원을 바라보면서 — 애비는 옛날식의 무심한 조망 탓에 그 시내를 거의 내다보지도 못했지만 — 그리고 유행이나 사치로도 뽑아내지 못한, 줄지어 늘어서고 가로수처럼 이어진 그 무성한 수목을 바라보면서. 집은 하트필드보다 컸고, 그것과는 전혀 딴판이었다. 꽤 넓은 땅을 차지하고서, 두서없이 불규칙하게 뻗어 있었으며, 안락한 방이 많았고 멋진 방도 한둘 있었다. 그것은 마땅히 그래야 할 그대로였고, 보이는 그대로의 모습이었다 — 그리고 엠마는 혈통에서도 식견에서도 한 점 흠 없는, 그토록 진정한 양반 가문의 거처로서 그 집에 대한 존경심이 점점 더 커지는 것을 느꼈다. 존 나이틀리에게는 다소 까다로운 성미가 있었지만, 이저벨라는 나무랄 데 없이 결혼한 셈이었다. 언니는 그들에게 얼굴을 붉힐 만한 사람도, 가문도, 가산도 보태지 않았던 것이다. 이것들은 흐뭇한 감정이었고, 그녀는 다른 이들이 하는 대로 딸기밭 둘레로 모여야 할 때가 될 때까지 거닐며 그 감정에 마음껏 젖었다. 일행은 프랭크 처칠만 빼고 모두 모여 있었는데, 그는 리치먼드에서 곧 도착하리라 시시각각 기다려지는 참이었다. 엘턴 부인은 행복의 온갖 장비를 — 챙 넓은 보닛과 바구니를 — 갖추고서, 딸기를 따든 받든 이야기하든 앞장설 채비를 한껏 차린 채였다 — 이제는 딸기, 오직 딸기만을 생각하고 입에 올릴 수 있었다. \"잉글랜드 최고의 과일 — 누구나 좋아하는 것 — 늘 몸에 좋은 것. 여기가 가장 좋은 밭에 가장 좋은 품종. 직접 따 먹는 즐거움 — 정말로 즐기는 유일한 길. 아침이 단연 제일 좋은 때 — 도무지 물리지 않아 — 어느 품종이나 다 좋지만 — 하우보이가 단연 으뜸 — 비할 데가 없어 — 다른 건 거의 먹을 게 못 돼 — 하우보이는 아주 귀하지 — 칠리종이 더 낫고 — 흰 우드종이 풍미가 제일 — 런던의 딸기 값 — 브리스틀 근방엔 지천 — 메이플 그로브 — 재배법 — 밭을 언제 갈아엎을지 — 정원사들 생각은 영 딴판 — 정해진 법칙이 없어 — 정원사들은 도무지 제 방식을 못 버려 — 맛있는 과일 — 다만 많이 먹기엔 너무 진해 — 버찌만 못해 — 까치밥나무 열매가 더 개운하고 — 딸기 따기의 유일한 흠이라면 허리를 굽혀야 한다는 것 — 쨍쨍한 햇볕 — 죽도록 지쳤어 — 더는 못 견디겠어 — 가서 그늘에 좀 앉아야겠어.\""]], [["Such, for half an hour, was the conversation—interrupted only once by Mrs.", "반 시간 동안 대화란 이런 식이었다 — 단 한 번 웨스턴 부인의 말로 끊겼을 뿐인데, 그녀가 의붓아들 걱정에 밖으로 나와 그가 왔는지 물었던 것이다 — 그녀는 조금 불안했다."], ["Weston, who came out, in her solicitude after her son-in-law, to inquire if he were come—and she was a little uneasy.—She had some fears of his horse.", "그가 탄 말에 무슨 일이 생겼을까 다소 걱정스러웠던 것이다."]], [["Seats tolerably in the shade were found; and now Emma was obliged to overhear what Mrs.", "그럭저럭 그늘진 자리를 찾았다. 그러자 엠마는 엘턴 부인과 제인 페어팩스가 무슨 이야기를 하는지 본의 아니게 엿들을 수밖에 없게 되었다."], ["Elton and Jane Fairfax were talking of.—A situation, a most desirable situation, was in question.", "어떤 자리, 더없이 탐나는 자리가 화제였다. 엘턴 부인은 그날 아침에 그 소식을 받고는 황홀경에 빠져 있었다."], ["Mrs. Elton had received notice of it that morning, and was in raptures.", "서클링 부인 댁은 아니고, 브래그 부인 댁도 아니었지만, 그 화려함과 영예에서 그 두 집에 조금 못 미칠 뿐이었다."], ["It was not with Mrs. Suckling, it was not with Mrs.", "브래그 부인의 사촌 댁이자, 서클링 부인과 아는 사이이며, 메이플 그로브에 이름이 알려진 마님이었다."], ["Bragge, but in felicity and splendour it fell short only of them: it was with a cousin of Mrs.", "더없이 좋고, 매력적이고, 격이 높고, 최상류 사교계, 그 권역, 그 계열, 그 지위 — 무엇 하나 빠질 게 없다는 것이었다."], ["Bragge, an acquaintance of Mrs. Suckling, a lady known at Maple Grove.", "그래서 엘턴 부인은 당장 그 제안을 받아들이지 못해 안달이었다."], ["Delightful, charming, superior, first circles, spheres, lines, ranks, every thing—and Mrs.", "그녀 쪽은 온통 열의와 활기와 의기양양함뿐이었다 — 그리고 미스 페어팩스가 지금으로서는 아무것에도 매이지 않겠노라며, 전에도 들은 바 있는 그 똑같은 이유들을 거듭 들어 자기 친구를 한사코 안심시키려 했지만, 엘턴 부인은 한사코 그 거절을 받아들이지 않았다. 그러면서도 엘턴 부인은 이튿날 우편으로 승낙 답장을 보낼 권한을 자기에게 달라고 우겨 댔다."], ["Elton was wild to have the offer closed with immediately.—On her side, all was warmth, energy, and triumph—and she positively refused to take her friend’s negative, though Miss Fairfax continued to assure her that she would not at present engage in any thing, repeating the same motives which she had been heard to urge before.—Still Mrs.", "제인이 대체 그것을 어떻게 견디는지, 엠마로서는 놀라울 따름이었다. 제인은 정말로 괴로워 보였고, 정말로 날 선 말투로 말했다 — 그러다 마침내, 그녀답지 않게 단호한 행동으로 자리를 옮기자고 제안했다."], ["Elton insisted on being authorised to write an acquiescence by the morrow’s post.—How Jane could bear it at all, was astonishing to Emma.—She did look vexed, she did speak pointedly—and at last, with a decision of action unusual to her, proposed a removal.—“Should not they walk?", "\"산책이나 하면 어떨까요? 미스터 나이틀리가 정원을 — 정원 전부를 — 보여 주시지 않을까요?"], ["Would not Mr.", "전부 다 둘러보고 싶어요.\""], ["Knightley shew them the gardens—all the gardens?—She wished to see the whole extent.”—The pertinacity of her friend seemed more than she could bear.", "친구의 그 끈질김을 더는 견딜 수 없는 모양이었다."]], [["It was hot; and after walking some time over the gardens in a scattered, dispersed way, scarcely any three together, they insensibly followed one another to the delicious shade of a broad short avenue of limes, which stretching beyond the garden at an equal distance from the river, seemed the finish of the pleasure grounds.—It led to nothing; nothing but a view at the end over a low stone wall with high pillars, which seemed intended, in their erection, to give the appearance of an approach to the house, which never had been there. Disputable, however, as might be the taste of such a termination, it was in itself a charming walk, and the view which closed it extremely pretty.—The considerable slope, at nearly the foot of which the Abbey stood, gradually acquired a steeper form beyond its grounds; and at half a mile distant was a bank of considerable abruptness and grandeur, well clothed with wood;—and at the bottom of this bank, favourably placed and sheltered, rose the Abbey Mill Farm, with meadows in front, and the river making a close and handsome curve around it.", "날이 더웠다. 한동안 흩어져 제각각, 셋이 함께 다니는 일도 거의 없이 정원을 거닌 끝에, 그들은 어느새 차례차례 서로를 따라 폭 넓고 길이가 짧은 보리수 가로수 길의 시원한 그늘로 들어섰다. 강에서 같은 거리를 두고 정원 너머로 뻗은 그 길은 정원 부지의 마무리인 듯했다. 그 길은 어디로도 이어지지 않았다. 다만 끝에 가서 높은 기둥이 선 낮은 돌담 너머의 전망이 펼쳐질 뿐이었는데, 그 기둥들은 세울 당시 한 번도 그곳에 있어 본 적 없는 진입로의 모양새를 내려고 의도한 듯했다. 그러나 그런 식의 마무리가 취향에 맞는지는 따져 볼 여지가 있다 해도, 그 길 자체는 더없이 호젓한 산책로였고, 그 끝을 닫는 전망은 무척이나 어여뻤다. 애비는 거의 그 기슭에 서 있던 그 제법 가파른 비탈은, 정원 부지를 벗어나면서 점점 더 가팔라졌다. 반 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에는 제법 깎아지른 듯 웅장한, 숲으로 잘 뒤덮인 둔덕이 있었다. 그리고 그 둔덕 기슭에는, 알맞게 자리 잡고 아늑하게 들어앉은 애비 밀 농장이 솟아 있었으니, 앞쪽으로는 초원이 펼쳐지고 강물은 그 둘레로 가깝고도 아름다운 곡선을 그리며 흘렀다."]], [["It was a sweet view—sweet to the eye and the mind.", "흐뭇한 전망이었다 — 눈에도 마음에도 흐뭇한."], ["English verdure, English culture, English comfort, seen under a sun bright, without being oppressive.", "잉글랜드의 푸르름, 잉글랜드의 경작지, 잉글랜드의 안락함이, 환하되 짓누르지는 않는 햇살 아래 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["In this walk Emma and Mr.", "이 산책로에서 엠마와 미스터 웨스턴은 다른 이들이 모두 모여 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Weston found all the others assembled; and towards this view she immediately perceived Mr.", "그리고 이 전망 쪽으로, 엠마는 미스터 나이틀리와 해리엇이 나머지 사람들과 떨어져 조용히 앞장서 가는 것을 곧바로 알아보았다."], ["Knightley and Harriet distinct from the rest, quietly leading the way.", "미스터 나이틀리와 해리엇이! 묘한 단둘이었지만, 그녀는 그 광경이 반가웠다."], ["Mr. Knightley and Harriet!—It was an odd tête-à-tête; but she was glad to see it.—There had been a time when he would have scorned her as a companion, and turned from her with little ceremony.", "한때 그가 해리엇을 동행으로 여기기조차 거북해하며 별 인사치레도 없이 등을 돌렸을 시절이 있었다."], ["Now they seemed in pleasant conversation.", "그런데 지금 두 사람은 즐거운 대화를 나누는 듯했다."], ["There had been a time also when Emma would have been sorry to see Harriet in a spot so favourable for the Abbey Mill Farm; but now she feared it not.", "또한 한때는 엠마가 애비 밀 농장이 그토록 잘 보이는 자리에 해리엇이 있는 것을 보면 안타까워했을 시절도 있었다. 그러나 지금은 그것이 두렵지 않았다."], ["It might be safely viewed with all its appendages of prosperity and beauty, its rich pastures, spreading flocks, orchard in blossom, and light column of smoke ascending.—She joined them at the wall, and found them more engaged in talking than in looking around.", "그 농장은 번영과 아름다움의 온갖 부속물을 — 기름진 목초지며, 흩어진 양 떼며, 꽃핀 과수원이며, 가늘게 피어오르는 연기 기둥까지 — 죄다 거느린 채 안심하고 바라봐도 좋았다. 그녀는 담 곁에서 두 사람과 합류했는데, 둘은 주위를 둘러보기보다 이야기에 더 빠져 있었다."], ["He was giving Harriet information as to modes of agriculture, etc.", "그는 해리엇에게 농사법 따위에 관해 알려 주는 중이었고, 엠마는 이렇게 말하는 듯한 미소를 받았다. \"이건 내 일이니, 나는 이런 주제로 이야기할 권리가 있소."], ["and Emma received a smile which seemed to say, “These are my own concerns.", "로버트 마틴을 끌어들인다는 의심 따위 받지 않고서 말이오.\" 그녀는 그를 의심하지 않았다."], ["I have a right to talk on such subjects, without being suspected of introducing Robert Martin.”—She did not suspect him.", "너무 오래된 이야기였다. 로버트 마틴은 아마 해리엇 생각을 그만두었을 것이다."], ["It was too old a story.—Robert Martin had probably ceased to think of Harriet.—They took a few turns together along the walk.—The shade was most refreshing, and Emma found it the pleasantest part of the day.", "세 사람은 함께 산책로를 몇 차례 거닐었다. 그늘은 더없이 시원했고, 엠마는 그 시간이 그날 가장 즐거운 대목이라고 느꼈다."]], [["The next remove was to the house; they must all go in and eat;—and they were all seated and busy, and still Frank Churchill did not come.", "다음으로 자리를 옮긴 곳은 집이었다."], ["Mrs. Weston looked, and looked in vain.", "모두 안으로 들어가 식사를 해야 했던 것이다."], ["His father would not own himself uneasy, and laughed at her fears; but she could not be cured of wishing that he would part with his black mare.", "그리하여 다들 자리에 앉아 분주한데도, 프랭크 처칠은 여전히 오지 않았다. 웨스턴 부인은 보고 또 보았으나 헛일이었다."], ["He had expressed himself as to coming, with more than common certainty.", "그의 아버지는 불안하다는 내색을 하려 들지 않으며 그녀의 걱정을 웃어넘겼다."], ["“His aunt was so much better, that he had not a doubt of getting over to them.”—Mrs.", "하지만 그녀는 아들이 그 검은 암말을 좀 처분했으면 하는 바람을 떨칠 수 없었다. 그는 온다는 말을 여느 때보다 확실하게 해 둔 터였다."], ["Churchill’s state, however, as many were ready to remind her, was liable to such sudden variation as might disappoint her nephew in the most reasonable dependence—and Mrs.", "\"이모가 워낙 좋아지셔서 그쪽으로 건너가는 데 의심의 여지가 없다\"고. 그러나 처칠 부인의 상태란, 많은 이들이 기꺼이 일러 주었듯이, 어떤 합당한 기대를 품은 조카라도 실망시킬 만큼 그렇게 갑작스레 변하기 쉬운 것이었다."], ["Weston was at last persuaded to believe, or to say, that it must be by some attack of Mrs.", "그리하여 웨스턴 부인도 마침내, 처칠 부인의 무슨 발작 탓에 그가 오지 못하는 게 틀림없다고 믿거나, 적어도 그렇게 말하게끔 설득되었다."], ["Churchill that he was prevented coming.—Emma looked at Harriet while the point was under consideration; she behaved very well, and betrayed no emotion.", "이 문제가 거론되는 동안 엠마는 해리엇을 바라보았다. 해리엇은 아주 의젓하게 굴며 아무런 동요도 내비치지 않았다."]], [["The cold repast was over, and the party were to go out once more to see what had not yet been seen, the old Abbey fish-ponds; perhaps get as far as the clover, which was to be begun cutting on the morrow, or, at any rate, have the pleasure of being hot, and growing cool again.—Mr.", "차가운 식사가 끝나자, 일행은 아직 못 본 것을 보러 다시 한번 밖으로 나가기로 했다. 그 옛 애비의 양어지(養魚池)며, 어쩌면 이튿날 베기 시작할 클로버밭까지 가 보거나, 아니면 적어도 더워졌다가 다시 식어 가는 즐거움이라도 누려 보자는 것이었다."], ["Woodhouse, who had already taken his little round in the highest part of the gardens, where no damps from the river were imagined even by him, stirred no more; and his daughter resolved to remain with him, that Mrs.", "미스터 우드하우스는 이미 정원에서 가장 높은 곳을 잠깐 한 바퀴 돌고 온 터였는데 — 그곳은 그조차도 강에서 올라오는 습기 따위는 없으리라 여기는 데였다 — 더는 움직이지 않았다."], ["Weston might be persuaded away by her husband to the exercise and variety which her spirits seemed to need.", "그리고 그의 딸은, 웨스턴 부인이 남편의 권유로 기분 전환에 필요해 보이는 운동과 변화를 누리도록, 자기는 아버지 곁에 남기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리는 미스터 우드하우스를 즐겁게 해 드리려 할 수 있는 일을 다 해 두었다."], ["Knightley had done all in his power for Mr.", "판화 책이며, 메달과 카메오와 산호와 조개껍데기를 담은 서랍이며, 그 진열장 안에 든 온갖 다른 가문의 수집품을, 그 오랜 벗이 오전 한나절을 보내는 데 쓰도록 미리 준비해 둔 것이었다. 그리고 그 친절은 더없이 잘 들어맞았다."], ["Woodhouse’s entertainment.", "미스터 우드하우스는 무척이나 즐거웠다. 웨스턴 부인이 그 모든 것을 그에게 보여 주었고, 이제 그는 그 모든 것을 엠마에게 보여 주려던 참이었다."], ["Books of engravings, drawers of medals, cameos, corals, shells, and every other family collection within his cabinets, had been prepared for his old friend, to while away the morning; and the kindness had perfectly answered.", "그가 본 것에 도무지 안목이 없다는 점 말고는 아이를 닮은 구석이 없다는 점이 다행이었으니, 그는 느릿느릿하고, 한결같으며, 꼼꼼했다. 그러나 이 두 번째 구경이 시작되기 전에, 엠마는 잠깐 동안 마음껏 현관과 집 앞뜰을 살펴볼 요량으로 홀로 걸어 나갔다 — 그런데 거기 들어선 지 얼마 되지도 않아 제인 페어팩스가 정원에서 황급히, 무언가에서 달아나는 듯한 표정으로 들어왔다."], ["Mr. Woodhouse had been exceedingly well amused.", "미스 우드하우스를 이렇게 빨리 만나리라곤 거의 예상치 못했던지라 처음에는 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["Mrs. Weston had been shewing them all to him, and now he would shew them all to Emma;—fortunate in having no other resemblance to a child, than in a total want of taste for what he saw, for he was slow, constant, and methodical.—Before this second looking over was begun, however, Emma walked into the hall for the sake of a few moments’ free observation of the entrance and ground-plot of the house—and was hardly there, when Jane Fairfax appeared, coming quickly in from the garden, and with a look of escape.—Little expecting to meet Miss Woodhouse so soon, there was a start at first; but Miss Woodhouse was the very person she was in quest of.", "그러나 그녀가 찾던 사람이 바로 미스 우드하우스였다."]], [["“Will you be so kind,” said she, “when I am missed, as to say that I am gone home?—I am going this moment.—My aunt is not aware how late it is, nor how long we have been absent—but I am sure we shall be wanted, and I am determined to go directly.—I have said nothing about it to any body. It would only be giving trouble and distress. Some are gone to the ponds, and some to the lime walk. Till they all come in I shall not be missed; and when they do, will you have the goodness to say that I am gone?”", "\"부탁 하나 들어주시겠어요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 안 보인다고 누가 찾거든, 집에 갔다고 말씀해 주시겠어요? 저는 지금 바로 가려고요. 이모는 시간이 이렇게 늦은 줄도, 우리가 이렇게 오래 자리를 비운 줄도 모르세요. 하지만 분명 우릴 찾을 테니, 저는 지금 곧장 가기로 마음먹었어요. 아무한테도 이 얘긴 안 했어요. 괜히 폐만 끼치고 걱정만 안길 테니까요. 몇 분은 양어지로, 몇 분은 보리수 길로 갔어요. 다들 돌아오기 전까지는 제가 안 보여도 모를 거예요. 그러다 알아채거든, 제가 갔다고 좀 말씀해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["“Certainly, if you wish it;—but you are not going to walk to Highbury alone?”", "\"물론이죠, 원하신다면요. 하지만 혼자서 하이베리까지 걸어가시려는 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“Yes—what should hurt me?—I walk fast.", "\"네 — 무슨 일이야 있겠어요?"], ["I shall be at home in twenty minutes.”", "제가 걸음이 빨라요. 이십 분이면 집에 가요.\""]], [["“But it is too far, indeed it is, to be walking quite alone.", "\"하지만 너무 멀어요, 정말이지, 혼자 걷기엔요."], ["Let my father’s servant go with you.—Let me order the carriage.", "우리 아버지네 하인을 딸려 보낼게요. 마차를 내오라고 할게요."], ["It can be round in five minutes.”", "오 분이면 돌아 나올 거예요.\""]], [["“Thank you, thank you—but on no account.—I would rather walk.—And for me to be afraid of walking alone!—I, who may so soon have to guard others!”", "\"고마워요, 고마워요 — 하지만 절대 안 돼요. 차라리 걷는 게 좋아요. 그리고 제가 혼자 걷기를 무서워하다니요! 머지않아 남을 지켜 줘야 할지도 모르는 제가요!\""]], [["She spoke with great agitation; and Emma very feelingly replied, “That can be no reason for your being exposed to danger now.", "그녀는 몹시 격앙되어 말했다. 그러자 엠마가 진심으로 마음을 담아 답했다."], ["I must order the carriage.", "\"그게 지금 당신을 위험에 내맡길 까닭은 될 수 없어요. 제가 마차를 내오라고 해야겠어요."], ["The heat even would be danger.—You are fatigued already.”", "더위만 해도 위험할 거예요. 당신은 이미 지쳤잖아요.\""]], [["“I am,”—she answered—“I am fatigued; but it is not the sort of fatigue—quick walking will refresh me.—Miss Woodhouse, we all know at times what it is to be wearied in spirits. Mine, I confess, are exhausted. The greatest kindness you can shew me, will be to let me have my own way, and only say that I am gone when it is necessary.”", "\"맞아요.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"지쳤어요. 하지만 이건 그런 종류의 피로가 아니에요 — 빨리 걸으면 기운이 날 거예요. 미스 우드하우스, 우리 모두 마음이 지치는 게 어떤 건지 가끔은 알잖아요. 솔직히 제 마음은 다 소진됐어요. 저한테 베풀어 주실 수 있는 가장 큰 친절은, 제 뜻대로 하게 두시고, 꼭 필요할 때 제가 갔다고만 말씀해 주시는 거예요.\""]], [["Emma had not another word to oppose.", "엠마는 더 막을 말이 없었다."], ["She saw it all; and entering into her feelings, promoted her quitting the house immediately, and watched her safely off with the zeal of a friend.", "그녀는 모든 것을 알아차렸다. 그래서 그 심정에 공감하며, 그녀가 당장 집을 떠나도록 도왔고, 벗다운 정성으로 그녀가 무사히 가는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["Her parting look was grateful—and her parting words, “Oh!", "헤어지며 보낸 그녀의 눈길은 고마움에 차 있었다 — 그리고 헤어지며 한 그녀의 말, \"아!"], ["Miss Woodhouse, the comfort of being sometimes alone!”—seemed to burst from an overcharged heart, and to describe somewhat of the continual endurance to be practised by her, even towards some of those who loved her best.", "미스 우드하우스, 가끔 혼자 있을 수 있다는 게 얼마나 위안이 되는지요!\"는 가득 찬 가슴에서 터져 나오는 듯했고, 그녀를 가장 아끼는 이들에게조차 그녀가 늘 견뎌 내야 하는 무언가를 어렴풋이 일러 주는 듯했다."]], [["“Such a home, indeed!", "\"하긴, 그런 집에!"], ["such an aunt!”", "그런 이모라니!\""], ["said Emma, as she turned back into the hall again.", "엠마는 다시 홀 안으로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“I do pity you.", "\"정말 가엾어요."], ["And the more sensibility you betray of their just horrors, the more I shall like you.”", "그 정당한 끔찍함을 당신이 더 예민하게 내비칠수록, 나는 당신을 더 좋아하게 될 거예요.\""]], [["Jane had not been gone a quarter of an hour, and they had only accomplished some views of St.", "제인이 떠난 지 십오 분도 채 되지 않아, 그들이 겨우 베네치아의 산마르코 광장 풍경 몇 점을 들여다본 참에, 프랭크 처칠이 방으로 들어섰다."], ["Mark’s Place, Venice, when Frank Churchill entered the room.", "엠마는 그를 생각하던 중도 아니었고, 그를 생각하는 것조차 잊고 있던 터였다 — 그러나 그를 보니 무척 반가웠다."], ["Emma had not been thinking of him, she had forgotten to think of him—but she was very glad to see him.", "웨스턴 부인이 한시름 놓을 것이었다. 검은 암말에는 죄가 없었다."], ["Mrs. Weston would be at ease.", "처칠 부인을 그 까닭으로 짚었던 이들이 옳았던 것이다."], ["The black mare was blameless; they were right who had named Mrs. Churchill as the cause.", "그는 이모의 병이 일시적으로 도지는 바람에 발이 묶여 있었다 — 몇 시간이나 이어진 신경 발작이었다 — 그래서 그는 아주 늦게까지 올 생각을 깡그리 접고 있었다."], ["He had been detained by a temporary increase of illness in her; a nervous seizure, which had lasted some hours—and he had quite given up every thought of coming, till very late;—and had he known how hot a ride he should have, and how late, with all his hurry, he must be, he believed he should not have come at all.", "그리고 길이 이렇게 더울 줄, 아무리 서둘러도 이렇게 늦을 줄 알았더라면 아예 오지 않았으리라는 것이었다. 더위가 극심했다."], ["The heat was excessive; he had never suffered any thing like it—almost wished he had staid at home—nothing killed him like heat—he could bear any degree of cold, etc., but heat was intolerable—and he sat down, at the greatest possible distance from the slight remains of Mr. Woodhouse’s fire, looking very deplorable.", "이런 더위는 겪어 본 적이 없었다 — 차라리 집에 있을 걸 그랬다 싶기까지 했다 — 더위만큼 그를 못살게 구는 게 없었다 — 추위라면 아무리 심해도 견디겠지만, 더위는 도무지 못 참겠다는 것이었다 — 그러더니 그는 미스터 우드하우스의 가까스로 남은 불기운에서 되도록 멀찍이 떨어져, 무척이나 가련한 몰골로 주저앉았다."]], [["“You will soon be cooler, if you sit still,”", "\"가만히 앉아 계시면 곧 시원해질 거예요.\""], ["said Emma.", "엠마가 말했다."]], [["“As soon as I am cooler I shall go back again.", "\"시원해지는 대로 곧장 돌아가야 합니다."], ["I could very ill be spared—but such a point had been made of my coming!", "제가 빠지면 영 곤란한데도 — 제가 와야 한다고 어찌나들 못 박아 두셨던지요!"], ["You will all be going soon I suppose; the whole party breaking up.", "다들 곧 돌아가시겠죠. 일행이 다 흩어지는 거겠고요."], ["I met one as I came—Madness in such weather!—absolute madness!”", "오는 길에 한 사람을 만났는데 — 이런 날씨에 미친 짓이지요! 그야말로 미친 짓이에요!\""]], [["Emma listened, and looked, and soon perceived that Frank Churchill’s state might be best defined by the expressive phrase of being out of humour.", "엠마는 듣고 또 살피다가, 곧 프랭크 처칠의 상태는 심사가 뒤틀렸다는 그 적절한 표현으로 가장 잘 설명되겠다는 것을 알아챘다. 어떤 사람들은 더우면 으레 짜증을 냈다."], ["Some people were always cross when they were hot.", "그것이 그의 체질일지도 몰랐다."], ["Such might be his constitution; and as she knew that eating and drinking were often the cure of such incidental complaints, she recommended his taking some refreshment; he would find abundance of every thing in the dining-room—and she humanely pointed out the door.", "그리고 그녀는 이런 일시적인 푸념에는 먹고 마시는 것이 약일 때가 많다는 것을 알았기에, 그에게 뭔가 요기를 하라고 권했다. 식당에 가면 온갖 것이 넉넉히 있을 거라고 — 그러고는 인정스럽게 문을 가리켜 보였다."]], [["“No—he should not eat.", "\"아니요 — 먹지 않겠습니다."], ["He was not hungry; it would only make him hotter.”", "배도 안 고프고요. 더 더워지기만 할 겁니다.\""], ["In two minutes, however, he relented in his own favour; and muttering something about spruce-beer, walked off.", "그러나 이 분도 채 못 되어 그는 스스로에게 너그러워져서는, 가문비나무 맥주가 어쩌고 중얼거리며 걸어 나갔다."], ["Emma returned all her attention to her father, saying in secret—", "엠마는 다시 온 신경을 아버지에게 돌리고는, 속으로 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“I am glad I have done being in love with him.", "\"저 사람한테 더는 빠져 있지 않게 되어 다행이야."], ["I should not like a man who is so soon discomposed by a hot morning.", "더운 아침나절 하나에 저렇게 금세 심사가 뒤틀리는 남자는 사양이지."], ["Harriet’s sweet easy temper will not mind it.”", "해리엇의 달콤하고 느긋한 성미라면 저런 걸 개의치 않을 테고.\""]], [["He was gone long enough to have had a very comfortable meal, and came back all the better—grown quite cool—and, with good manners, like himself—able to draw a chair close to them, take an interest in their employment; and regret, in a reasonable way, that he should be so late.", "그가 자리를 비운 동안은 아주 흡족하게 식사를 하고도 남을 만큼이었고, 돌아왔을 때는 한결 나아져 있었다 — 아주 시원해진 데다 — 평소의 그답게 예의 바른 모습으로 — 그들 곁에 의자를 바짝 끌어다 놓고 그들이 보고 있는 것에 관심을 가지며, 자기가 이렇게 늦은 것을 합당한 정도로 아쉬워할 줄도 알았다."], ["He was not in his best spirits, but seemed trying to improve them; and, at last, made himself talk nonsense very agreeably.", "그는 기분이 최상은 아니었지만, 그것을 끌어올리려 애쓰는 듯했다. 그리고 마침내, 아주 기분 좋게 실없는 소리를 늘어놓기에 이르렀다."], ["They were looking over views in Swisserland.", "그들은 스위스 풍경을 들여다보던 참이었다."]], [["“As soon as my aunt gets well, I shall go abroad,”", "\"이모가 회복되시는 대로, 저는 외국에 갈 겁니다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I shall never be easy till I have seen some of these places.", "\"이런 곳들을 좀 보기 전엔 도무지 마음이 편치 않을 거예요."], ["You will have my sketches, some time or other, to look at—or my tour to read—or my poem.", "언젠가 제 스케치를 보시게 되거나 — 제 여행기를 읽으시게 되거나 — 제 시를 읽으시게 될 겁니다."], ["I shall do something to expose myself.”", "무언가 해서 제 망신을 톡톡히 살 테니까요.\""]], [["“That may be—but not by sketches in Swisserland.", "\"그럴지도 모르죠 — 하지만 스위스 스케치로는 아니에요."], ["You will never go to Swisserland.", "당신은 스위스엔 절대 못 가요."], ["Your uncle and aunt will never allow you to leave England.”", "당신 외삼촌과 외숙모는 절대 당신을 잉글랜드 밖으로 내보내지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“They may be induced to go too.", "\"그분들도 함께 가시도록 끌어낼 수 있을지 모르죠."], ["A warm climate may be prescribed for her.", "외숙모한테 따뜻한 기후가 처방될 수도 있고요."], ["I have more than half an expectation of our all going abroad.", "저는 우리 모두 외국에 가게 되리라는 기대를 절반 넘게 품고 있어요."], ["I assure you I have.", "정말이에요."], ["I feel a strong persuasion, this morning, that I shall soon be abroad.", "오늘 아침, 머지않아 제가 외국에 가 있으리라는 강한 확신이 들거든요."], ["I ought to travel.", "저는 여행을 해야 해요."], ["I am tired of doing nothing.", "아무것도 안 하는 데 이젠 진력이 났어요."], ["I want a change.", "변화가 필요해요."], ["I am serious, Miss Woodhouse, whatever your penetrating eyes may fancy—I am sick of England—and would leave it to-morrow, if I could.”", "진심이에요, 미스 우드하우스, 당신의 그 꿰뚫어 보는 눈이 어떻게 짐작하시든 간에 — 저는 잉글랜드가 지긋지긋해요 — 그리고 할 수만 있다면 내일이라도 떠나고 싶어요.\""]], [["“You are sick of prosperity and indulgence.", "\"당신은 번영과 호사에 진력이 난 거예요."], ["Cannot you invent a few hardships for yourself, and be contented to stay?”", "자기한테 고생거리를 몇 가지 지어내서, 머무는 데 만족할 수는 없나요?\""]], [["“I sick of prosperity and indulgence!", "\"제가 번영과 호사에 진력이 났다고요!"], ["You are quite mistaken.", "단단히 잘못 보셨어요."], ["I do not look upon myself as either prosperous or indulged.", "저는 제 자신을 번영했다고도, 호강한다고도 여기지 않아요."], ["I am thwarted in every thing material.", "저는 무엇 하나 실질적인 것에선 죄다 가로막혀 있어요."], ["I do not consider myself at all a fortunate person.”", "제 자신을 도통 운 좋은 사람이라 여기지 않습니다.\""]], [["“You are not quite so miserable, though, as when you first came.", "\"그래도 처음 오셨을 때만큼 비참하지는 않잖아요."], ["Go and eat and drink a little more, and you will do very well.", "가서 좀 더 먹고 마시고 오세요. 그럼 아주 괜찮아질 거예요."], ["Another slice of cold meat, another draught of Madeira and water, will make you nearly on a par with the rest of us.”", "차가운 고기 한 점 더, 마데이라 포도주에 물 탄 거 한 잔 더면, 우리 나머지 사람들하고 거의 비슷해질걸요.\""]], [["“No—I shall not stir.", "\"아니요 — 꼼짝 안 하겠습니다."], ["I shall sit by you.", "당신 곁에 앉아 있겠어요."], ["You are my best cure.”", "당신이 제 최고의 약이니까요.\""]], [["“We are going to Box Hill to-morrow;—you will join us.", "\"우리는 내일 박스 힐에 가요. 당신도 함께해요."], ["It is not Swisserland, but it will be something for a young man so much in want of a change.", "스위스는 아니지만, 변화가 그토록 절실한 젊은이한테는 그래도 뭔가는 되겠죠."], ["You will stay, and go with us?”", "머물렀다가 우리랑 같이 가요.\""]], [["“No, certainly not; I shall go home in the cool of the evening.”", "\"아니요, 절대 안 됩니다. 저는 저녁 선선할 때 집에 가야 해요.\""]], [["“But you may come again in the cool of to-morrow morning.”", "\"하지만 내일 아침 선선할 때 다시 오면 되잖아요.\""]], [["“No—It will not be worth while.", "\"아니요 — 그럴 만한 가치가 없어요."], ["If I come, I shall be cross.”", "오면 저는 심사가 뒤틀릴 테니까요.\""]], [["“Then pray stay at Richmond.”", "\"그럼 부디 리치먼드에 계세요.\""]], [["“But if I do, I shall be crosser still.", "\"하지만 그러면 더 심사가 뒤틀릴걸요."], ["I can never bear to think of you all there without me.”", "당신들이 다 거기 있는데 저만 빠진다고 생각하면 도저히 못 견디겠어요.\""]], [["“These are difficulties which you must settle for yourself.", "\"그건 당신 스스로 풀어야 할 난제로군요."], ["Chuse your own degree of crossness.", "뒤틀릴 정도는 당신이 알아서 고르세요."], ["I shall press you no more.”", "저는 더는 강권하지 않겠어요.\""]], [["The rest of the party were now returning, and all were soon collected.", "이제 나머지 일행이 돌아오는 중이었고, 곧 모두 한자리에 모였다."], ["With some there was great joy at the sight of Frank Churchill; others took it very composedly; but there was a very general distress and disturbance on Miss Fairfax’s disappearance being explained.", "더러는 프랭크 처칠을 보고 크게 기뻐했고, 더러는 아주 담담하게 받아들였다. 그러나 미스 페어팩스가 사라진 사연이 밝혀지자 사뭇 두루 안타까워하고 술렁였다."], ["That it was time for every body to go, concluded the subject; and with a short final arrangement for the next day’s scheme, they parted.", "이제 모두 돌아갈 때가 되었다는 말로 그 화제는 마무리되었고, 이튿날 일정을 짤막하게 마지막으로 매듭짓고서 그들은 헤어졌다."], ["Frank Churchill’s little inclination to exclude himself increased so much, that his last words to Emma were,", "프랭크 처칠은 빠지기 싫은 마음이 어찌나 커졌던지, 엠마에게 건넨 마지막 말은 이러했다."]], [["“Well;—if you wish me to stay and join the party, I will.”", "\"좋아요. 당신이 제가 남아서 일행에 끼기를 바라신다면, 그렇게 하겠습니다.\""]], [["She smiled her acceptance; and nothing less than a summons from Richmond was to take him back before the following evening.", "그녀는 미소로 그 청을 받아들였다. 그리고 리치먼드에서 부르는 일이 아니고서는, 그 무엇도 이튿날 저녁 전에 그를 데려가지 못할 것이었다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter VII", "# 제3권 7장"]], [["They had a very fine day for Box Hill; and all the other outward circumstances of arrangement, accommodation, and punctuality, were in favour of a pleasant party.", "박스 힐로 나서기에 더없이 좋은 날이었다. 일정과 채비, 시간 맞추기 등 다른 모든 외적 조건도 즐거운 모임이 되도록 두루 도왔다."], ["Mr. Weston directed the whole, officiating safely between Hartfield and the Vicarage, and every body was in good time.", "미스터 웨스턴이 전체를 도맡아 하트필드와 사제관 사이를 무난히 오가며 일을 보았고, 모두가 제때 모였다."], ["Emma and Harriet went together; Miss Bates and her niece, with the Eltons; the gentlemen on horseback. Mrs.", "엠마와 해리엇이 함께 갔고, 미스 베이츠와 그 조카는 엘턴 부부와, 신사들은 말을 타고 갔다."], ["Weston remained with Mr. Woodhouse.", "웨스턴 부인은 미스터 우드하우스 곁에 남았다."], ["Nothing was wanting but to be happy when they got there.", "그곳에 닿기만 하면 즐거워하는 일밖에 남지 않을 듯했다."], ["Seven miles were travelled in expectation of enjoyment, and every body had a burst of admiration on first arriving; but in the general amount of the day there was deficiency.", "칠 마일을 즐거움에 부풀어 달려갔고, 처음 도착했을 때는 다들 한바탕 감탄을 쏟아 냈다. 그러나 하루를 통틀어 보면 어딘가 미진한 데가 있었다."], ["There was a languor, a want of spirits, a want of union, which could not be got over.", "어찌해도 떨쳐지지 않는 나른함, 흥의 부족, 어울림의 부족이 있었다."], ["They separated too much into parties.", "그들은 패가 너무 갈렸다."], ["The Eltons walked together; Mr. Knightley took charge of Miss Bates and Jane; and Emma and Harriet belonged to Frank Churchill.", "엘턴 부부는 둘이서 걸었고, 미스터 나이틀리는 미스 베이츠와 제인을 맡았으며, 엠마와 해리엇은 프랭크 처칠 차지였다."], ["And Mr. Weston tried, in vain, to make them harmonise better.", "그리고 미스터 웨스턴은 그들을 좀 더 잘 어우러지게 하려고 애썼으나 헛수고였다."], ["It seemed at first an accidental division, but it never materially varied.", "처음엔 우연한 갈림처럼 보였으나, 끝내 그 짜임은 별로 달라지지 않았다."], ["Mr. and Mrs.", "사실 엘턴 부부는 어울려 가능한 한 상냥하게 굴기를 마다하지 않았다."], ["Elton, indeed, shewed no unwillingness to mix, and be as agreeable as they could; but during the two whole hours that were spent on the hill, there seemed a principle of separation, between the other parties, too strong for any fine prospects, or any cold collation, or any cheerful Mr. Weston, to remove.", "그러나 언덕에서 보낸 꼬박 두 시간 내내, 다른 패들 사이에는 어떤 멋진 경치로도, 어떤 차가운 간식으로도, 어떤 쾌활한 미스터 웨스턴으로도 풀어 낼 수 없을 만큼 강한 분리의 원리가 작동하는 듯했다."]], [["At first it was downright dulness to Emma.", "처음에 엠마에게 그것은 그야말로 따분함이었다."], ["She had never seen Frank Churchill so silent and stupid.", "프랭크 처칠이 그토록 말 없고 멍청해 보이는 것은 처음이었다."], ["He said nothing worth hearing—looked without seeing—admired without intelligence—listened without knowing what she said.", "들을 만한 말은 한마디도 하지 않았고 — 보면서도 보지 못했으며 — 분별 없이 감탄했고 — 그녀가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 듣고 있었다."], ["While he was so dull, it was no wonder that Harriet should be dull likewise; and they were both insufferable.", "그가 그토록 따분하니, 해리엇 역시 따분해지는 것도 당연했다. 두 사람 다 못 견딜 지경이었다."]], [["When they all sat down it was better; to her taste a great deal better, for Frank Churchill grew talkative and gay, making her his first object.", "다들 자리에 앉으니 한결 나았다. 엠마의 취향에는 훨씬 더 나았으니, 프랭크 처칠이 수다스럽고 명랑해지면서 그녀를 으뜸가는 상대로 삼은 것이다."], ["Every distinguishing attention that could be paid, was paid to her.", "베풀 수 있는 온갖 각별한 배려가 그녀에게 쏟아졌다."], ["To amuse her, and be agreeable in her eyes, seemed all that he cared for—and Emma, glad to be enlivened, not sorry to be flattered, was gay and easy too, and gave him all the friendly encouragement, the admission to be gallant, which she had ever given in the first and most animating period of their acquaintance; but which now, in her own estimation, meant nothing, though in the judgment of most people looking on it must have had such an appearance as no English word but flirtation could very well describe.", "그녀를 즐겁게 하고 그녀의 눈에 들기만 하면 다른 데는 관심도 없는 듯했다 — 그리고 엠마는 분위기가 살아나는 것이 반갑고 추어주는 것도 싫지 않아, 그녀 역시 명랑하고 편안하게, 두 사람의 교제에서 가장 처음이자 가장 들떴던 시절에 보내곤 했던 그 다정한 격려를, 그 정중한 수작을 허락하는 빛을 모두 그에게 내주었다. 다만 이제 그녀 자신의 헤아림으로는 아무 의미 없는 일이었지만, 곁에서 지켜보는 대부분의 사람의 판단으로는 영어로 '시시덕거림'이라는 말 말고는 달리 표현하기 어려운 모양새였음이 분명했다."], ["“Mr. Frank Churchill and Miss Woodhouse flirted together excessively.”", "\"미스터 프랭크 처칠과 미스 우드하우스가 지나치게 시시덕거렸다.\""], ["They were laying themselves open to that very phrase—and to having it sent off in a letter to Maple Grove by one lady, to Ireland by another.", "두 사람은 바로 그런 말을 들을 빌미를 스스로 내주고 있었던 것이다 — 한 부인이 메이플 그로브로, 또 한 부인이 아일랜드로 그 소문을 편지에 실어 보낼 빌미를."], ["Not that Emma was gay and thoughtless from any real felicity; it was rather because she felt less happy than she had expected.", "그렇다고 엠마가 무슨 진짜 행복에서 명랑하고 들떠 있었던 것은 아니었다. 오히려 기대했던 것보다 덜 행복하다고 느꼈기 때문이었다."], ["She laughed because she was disappointed; and though she liked him for his attentions, and thought them all, whether in friendship, admiration, or playfulness, extremely judicious, they were not winning back her heart.", "그녀는 실망했기에 웃었다. 그의 배려가 마음에 들었고, 그것이 우정에서든 흠모에서든 장난기에서든 더없이 분별 있다고 여겼으나, 그 배려가 그녀의 마음을 되돌려 놓지는 못했다."], ["She still intended him for her friend.", "그녀는 여전히 그를 벗으로만 여길 작정이었다."]], [["“How much I am obliged to you,”", "\"오늘 오라고 말해 주셔서 얼마나 고마운지요.\""], ["said he, “for telling me to come to-day!—If it had not been for you, I should certainly have lost all the happiness of this party.", "그가 말했다. \"당신이 아니었으면 저는 분명 이 모임의 즐거움을 송두리째 놓쳤을 겁니다."], ["I had quite determined to go away again.”", "다시 떠나 버리기로 아주 마음을 굳혔었거든요.\""]], [["“Yes, you were very cross; and I do not know what about, except that you were too late for the best strawberries.", "\"네, 무척 토라져 계셨죠. 가장 좋은 딸기가 다 떨어진 다음에야 오신 것 말고는 뭣 때문이었는지 모르겠지만요."], ["I was a kinder friend than you deserved.", "저는 당신이 받을 자격보다 더 친절한 벗이었어요."], ["But you were humble.", "하지만 당신은 겸손하셨죠."], ["You begged hard to be commanded to come.”", "부디 오라고 명해 달라고 그렇게 사정하셨으니까요.\""]], [["“Don’t say I was cross.", "\"토라졌다고는 하지 마십시오."], ["I was fatigued.", "피곤했던 겁니다."], ["The heat overcame me.”", "더위에 지쳤던 거죠.\""]], [["“It is hotter to-day.”", "\"오늘이 더 더운데요.\""]], [["“Not to my feelings.", "\"제 느낌으로는 아닙니다."], ["I am perfectly comfortable to-day.”", "오늘은 더할 나위 없이 편안한걸요.\""]], [["“You are comfortable because you are under command.”", "\"당신이 명을 받들고 있으니까 편안한 거죠.\""]], [["“Your command?—Yes.”", "\"당신의 명을요? — 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Perhaps I intended you to say so, but I meant self-command.", "\"아마 제가 그렇게 말하도록 유도하긴 했지만, 제 뜻은 스스로를 다스리는 것이었어요."], ["You had, somehow or other, broken bounds yesterday, and run away from your own management; but to-day you are got back again—and as I cannot be always with you, it is best to believe your temper under your own command rather than mine.”", "당신은 어제 어쩐 일인지 도를 넘어, 제 스스로 다잡던 데서 벗어나 달아나 버렸죠. 그러나 오늘은 다시 제자리로 돌아오셨고요 — 그리고 제가 늘 곁에 있어 드릴 수는 없으니, 당신의 성미가 제 다스림이 아니라 당신 스스로의 다스림 아래 있다고 믿는 편이 가장 좋겠지요.\""]], [["“It comes to the same thing.", "\"그게 그거지요."], ["I can have no self-command without a motive.", "저는 동기가 없으면 스스로를 다스릴 수가 없습니다."], ["You order me, whether you speak or not.", "당신이 말을 하든 않든 제게 명을 내리는 거니까요."], ["And you can be always with me.", "그리고 당신은 늘 제 곁에 있을 수 있습니다."], ["You are always with me.”", "늘 제 곁에 있는걸요.\""]], [["“Dating from three o’clock yesterday.", "\"어제 세 시부터 따져서 말이죠."], ["My perpetual influence could not begin earlier, or you would not have been so much out of humour before.”", "저의 영원한 영향력이 그보다 더 일찍 시작됐을 리는 없잖아요. 그랬다면 그 전에 그토록 심사가 뒤틀려 계시진 않았을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Three o’clock yesterday!", "\"어제 세 시라니요!"], ["That is your date.", "그게 당신이 잡은 날짜군요."], ["I thought I had seen you first in February.”", "저는 처음 당신을 뵌 게 이월이라고 생각했는데요.\""]], [["“Your gallantry is really unanswerable.", "\"당신의 정중한 수작은 정말이지 당해 낼 도리가 없네요."], ["But (lowering her voice)—nobody speaks except ourselves, and it is rather too much to be talking nonsense for the entertainment of seven silent people.”", "하지만 (목소리를 낮추며) — 우리 둘 말고는 아무도 입을 열지 않는데, 입 다문 일곱 사람을 즐겁게 하자고 이런 실없는 소리를 늘어놓는 건 좀 지나친 일이에요.\""]], [["“I say nothing of which I am ashamed,”", "\"저는 부끄러울 말은 하나도 하지 않습니다.\""], ["replied he, with lively impudence.", "그가 짓궂은 뻔뻔함으로 응수했다."], ["“I saw you first in February.", "\"저는 이월에 당신을 처음 뵈었습니다."], ["Let every body on the Hill hear me if they can.", "언덕 위 사람들이 다 들으라죠, 들을 수만 있다면."], ["Let my accents swell to Mickleham on one side, and Dorking on the other.", "제 목청을 한쪽으로는 미클햄까지, 다른 쪽으로는 도킹까지 퍼뜨리게 두십시오."], ["I saw you first in February.”", "저는 이월에 당신을 처음 뵈었습니다.\""], ["And then whispering—“Our companions are excessively stupid.", "그러고는 속삭이며 — \"우리 일행은 지독하게 멍청하군요."], ["What shall we do to rouse them?", "저들을 깨우려면 뭘 해야 할까요?"], ["Any nonsense will serve.", "무슨 실없는 소리든 통할 겁니다."], ["They shall talk.", "저들로 하여금 입을 열게 하지요."], ["Ladies and gentlemen, I am ordered by Miss Woodhouse (who, wherever she is, presides) to say, that she desires to know what you are all thinking of?”", "신사 숙녀 여러분, 미스 우드하우스의 (어디에 계시든 그분이 좌장이십니다) 명을 받아 전합니다. 그분이 여러분 모두 무슨 생각을 하고 계신지 알고 싶어 하십니다.\""]], [["Some laughed, and answered good-humouredly.", "몇몇은 웃으며 너그러이 대답했다."], ["Miss Bates said a great deal; Mrs. Elton swelled at the idea of Miss Woodhouse’s presiding; Mr.", "미스 베이츠는 한참을 떠들어 댔고, 엘턴 부인은 미스 우드하우스가 좌장이라는 생각에 잔뜩 부풀어 올랐다."], ["Knightley’s answer was the most distinct.", "미스터 나이틀리의 대답이 가장 또렷했다."]], [["“Is Miss Woodhouse sure that she would like to hear what we are all thinking of?”", "\"미스 우드하우스는 우리가 다들 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지 정말로 듣고 싶은 것이 확실한가?\""]], [["“Oh! no, no”—cried Emma, laughing as carelessly as she could—“Upon no account in the world.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요.\""], ["It is the very last thing I would stand the brunt of just now.", "엠마가 애써 태연하게 웃으며 외쳤다. \"절대로 그럴 리가요."], ["Let me hear any thing rather than what you are all thinking of.", "지금 그 화살을 맞는 일이야말로 제가 가장 사양하고 싶은 거예요."], ["I will not say quite all. There are one or two, perhaps, (glancing at Mr.", "여러분이 다들 무슨 생각을 하시는지, 그것만 빼고는 무엇이든 듣겠어요."], ["Weston and Harriet,) whose thoughts I might not be afraid of knowing.”", "아주 다는 아니라고 해 두죠. 한두 분쯤은 (미스터 웨스턴과 해리엇을 흘끗 보며) 그 생각을 알아도 겁날 게 없을 분이 계실지도 모르니까요.\""]], [["“It is a sort of thing,” cried Mrs.", "\"그런 건,\""], ["Elton emphatically, “which I should not have thought myself privileged to inquire into.", "엘턴 부인이 힘주어 외쳤다. \"제 생각엔 캐물어도 좋을 만큼 제게 허락된 일이 아니에요."], ["Though, perhaps, as the Chaperon of the party—I never was in any circle—exploring parties—young ladies—married women—”", "비록, 어쩌면, 이 모임의 보호자로서는 — 저는 한 번도 어떤 모임에서도 — 답사 모임이라느니 — 아가씨들이라느니 — 유부녀들이라느니 —\""]], [["Her mutterings were chiefly to her husband; and he murmured, in reply,", "그 중얼거림은 대개 남편을 향한 것이었고, 남편은 이렇게 대꾸했다."]], [["“Very true, my love, very true.", "\"지당하오, 여보, 지당해."], ["Exactly so, indeed—quite unheard of—but some ladies say any thing.", "정말 그렇소 — 참으로 듣도 보도 못한 일이지 — 하지만 더러 무슨 말이든 함부로 하는 부인네들이 있으니."], ["Better pass it off as a joke.", "농담으로 넘기는 게 낫겠소."], ["Every body knows what is due to you.”", "당신께 합당한 대우가 무엇인지는 다들 아니까.\""]], [["“It will not do,”", "\"안 되겠습니다.\""], ["whispered Frank to Emma; “they are most of them affronted.", "프랭크가 엠마에게 속삭였다. \"대부분 비위가 상했어요."], ["I will attack them with more address.", "좀 더 솜씨 있게 공략해 보지요."], ["Ladies and gentlemen—I am ordered by Miss Woodhouse to say, that she waives her right of knowing exactly what you may all be thinking of, and only requires something very entertaining from each of you, in a general way.", "신사 숙녀 여러분 — 미스 우드하우스의 명을 받아 전합니다. 그분께서는 여러분이 정확히 무슨 생각을 하시는지 알 권리는 포기하시고, 다만 두루 보아 아주 재미있는 것 한 가지씩만을 각자에게 청하십니다."], ["Here are seven of you, besides myself, (who, she is pleased to say, am very entertaining already,) and she only demands from each of you either one thing very clever, be it prose or verse, original or repeated—or two things moderately clever—or three things very dull indeed, and she engages to laugh heartily at them all.”", "여기 저 말고도 일곱 분이 계신데 (그분께서 친절히도 저는 이미 무척 재미있는 사람이라 해 주셨고요), 각자에게 청하시는 건 이것뿐입니다 — 아주 재치 있는 것 한 가지, 산문이든 운문이든, 직접 지은 것이든 빌려 온 것이든 — 또는 그럭저럭 재치 있는 것 두 가지 — 또는 정말이지 따분하기 짝이 없는 것 세 가지. 그러면 그분께서 그 모두에 진심으로 웃어 주시겠다고 약속하십니다.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["very well,”", "잘됐네요.\""], ["exclaimed Miss Bates, “then I need not be uneasy.", "미스 베이츠가 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 마음 졸일 게 없겠어요."], ["‘Three things very dull indeed.’ That will just do for me, you know.", "'정말이지 따분하기 짝이 없는 것 세 가지'라니. 그건 제게 딱 맞는 거잖아요, 안 그래요."], ["I shall be sure to say three dull things as soon as ever I open my mouth, shan’t I?", "저야 입만 열면 따분한 소리 세 가지쯤은 틀림없이 할 테니까요, 그렇죠?"], ["(looking round with the most good-humoured dependence on every body’s assent)—Do not you all think I shall?”", "(둘러보며 더없이 선량하게 모두의 동의를 구하면서) — 여러분도 다들 제가 그러리라 생각하시죠?\""]], [["Emma could not resist.", "엠마는 도저히 참을 수 없었다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["ma’am, but there may be a difficulty.", "부인, 하지만 좀 곤란한 점이 있을지도 몰라요."], ["Pardon me—but you will be limited as to number—only three at once.”", "용서하세요 — 하지만 개수에 제한이 있을 텐데요 — 한 번에 세 가지까지만요.\""]], [["Miss Bates, deceived by the mock ceremony of her manner, did not immediately catch her meaning; but, when it burst on her, it could not anger, though a slight blush shewed that it could pain her.", "미스 베이츠는 엠마의 짐짓 점잔 빼는 태도에 속아 그 뜻을 곧바로 알아채지 못했다. 그러나 그 뜻이 불쑥 와닿자, 화를 내지는 못했지만, 살짝 붉어진 얼굴은 그것이 그녀를 아프게 할 수 있음을 보여 주었다."]], [["“Ah!—well—to be sure.", "\"아! — 그렇군요 — 그럼요."], ["Yes, I see what she means, (turning to Mr.", "네, 무슨 뜻인지 알겠어요."], ["Knightley,) and I will try to hold my tongue.", "(미스터 나이틀리 쪽으로 돌아서며) 입을 다물도록 애써 보지요."], ["I must make myself very disagreeable, or she would not have said such a thing to an old friend.”", "제가 어지간히 거슬리는 사람인가 봐요. 안 그러면 오랜 벗한테 저런 말을 하셨을 리가 없으니까요.\""]], [["“I like your plan,”", "\"당신 계획이 마음에 드오.\""], ["cried Mr. Weston.", "미스터 웨스턴이 외쳤다."], ["“Agreed, agreed.", "\"찬성이오, 찬성."], ["I will do my best.", "나도 최선을 다해 보지."], ["I am making a conundrum.", "수수께끼를 하나 만들고 있는데."], ["How will a conundrum reckon?”", "수수께끼는 몇 점쯤 쳐주려나?\""]], [["“Low, I am afraid, sir, very low,”", "\"낮을 겁니다, 아버지, 아주 낮을 거예요.\""], ["answered his son;—“but we shall be indulgent—especially to any one who leads the way.”", "아들이 대답했다. \"하지만 너그러이 봐 드리지요 — 특히 길을 트는 분께는요.\""]], [["“No, no,” said Emma, “it will not reckon low.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요.\""], ["A conundrum of Mr.", "엠마가 말했다."], ["Weston’s shall clear him and his next neighbour.", "\"낮게 치지 않을 거예요. 미스터 웨스턴의 수수께끼라면 당신과 옆자리 분까지 면제해 드리죠."], ["Come, sir, pray let me hear it.”", "자, 어서 들려주세요.\""]], [["“I doubt its being very clever myself,”", "\"나도 그게 그리 재치 있을지는 미덥지 않소.\""], ["said Mr. Weston.", "미스터 웨스턴이 말했다."], ["“It is too much a matter of fact, but here it is.—What two letters of the alphabet are there, that express perfection?”", "\"너무 뻔한 사실이라서 말이야. 하지만 여기 있소 — 알파벳 두 글자로 완벽을 나타내는 게 뭘까?\""]], [["“What two letters!—express perfection!", "\"두 글자라니요! — 완벽을 나타낸다고요!"], ["I am sure I do not know.”", "정말 모르겠는데요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you will never guess.", "절대 못 맞힐 거요. 당신은 (엠마에게) 분명 절대 못 맞힐 거요."], ["You, (to Emma), I am certain, will never guess.—I will tell you.—M.", "— 내가 알려 주지. — M과 A."], ["and A.—Em-ma.—Do you understand?”", "— 엠-마(Em-ma). — 알겠소?\""]], [["Understanding and gratification came together.", "깨달음과 흐뭇함이 함께 찾아왔다."], ["It might be a very indifferent piece of wit, but Emma found a great deal to laugh at and enjoy in it—and so did Frank and Harriet.—It did not seem to touch the rest of the party equally; some looked very stupid about it, and Mr.", "그것은 지극히 시시한 재담이었을지 모르나, 엠마는 거기서 웃고 즐길 거리를 잔뜩 찾아냈다 — 프랭크와 해리엇도 마찬가지였다. — 나머지 일행에게는 그만큼 가닿지 않는 듯했다."], ["Knightley gravely said,", "몇몇은 그것을 두고 영 멍한 얼굴이었고, 미스터 나이틀리는 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“This explains the sort of clever thing that is wanted, and Mr.", "\"이걸로 어떤 종류의 재치 있는 것이 필요한지가 분명해졌군."], ["Weston has done very well for himself; but he must have knocked up every body else.", "미스터 웨스턴은 자기 몫을 아주 잘 해내셨소. 하지만 다른 사람들 기를 죄다 꺾어 놓으셨겠는걸."], ["Perfection should not have come quite so soon.”", "'완벽'이 이렇게 일찍 나와 버리면 곤란하지요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["for myself, I protest I must be excused,” said Mrs.", "저로 말하자면, 단연코 면제받아야겠어요.\""], ["Elton; “I really cannot attempt—I am not at all fond of the sort of thing.", "엘턴 부인이 말했다. \"저는 정말이지 엄두도 못 내겠어요 — 저는 그런 걸 도통 좋아하지 않거든요."], ["I had an acrostic once sent to me upon my own name, which I was not at all pleased with.", "한번은 제 이름을 가지고 만든 이합체시*를 받은 적이 있는데, 조금도 마음에 들지 않았답니다."], ["I knew who it came from.", "누가 보냈는지도 알았고요."], ["An abominable puppy!—You know who I mean (nodding to her husband).", "가증스러운 애송이였죠! — 누군지 아시잖아요 (남편에게 고갯짓하며)."], ["These kind of things are very well at Christmas, when one is sitting round the fire; but quite out of place, in my opinion, when one is exploring about the country in summer.", "이런 건 성탄절에 난롯가에 둘러앉았을 때나 어울리지, 제 생각엔 여름에 시골을 답사하러 다닐 때는 영 어울리지 않아요."], ["Miss Woodhouse must excuse me.", "미스 우드하우스가 저를 너그러이 봐주셔야겠어요."], ["I am not one of those who have witty things at every body’s service.", "저는 아무에게나 재치 있는 것을 내드릴 그런 사람이 아니에요."], ["I do not pretend to be a wit.", "재담가인 척하지도 않고요."], ["I have a great deal of vivacity in my own way, but I really must be allowed to judge when to speak and when to hold my tongue.", "제 나름의 생기야 넘치도록 있지만, 언제 말하고 언제 입을 다물지는 제가 가려서 판단하게 해 주셔야지요. 저희는 건너뛰어 주세요, 미스터 처칠."], ["Pass us, if you please, Mr. Churchill.", "미스터 E.와 나이틀리, 제인, 그리고 저까지 건너뛰어 주세요."], ["Pass Mr. E., Knightley, Jane, and myself.", "저희는 재치 있게 할 말이 하나도 없으니까요 — 한 사람도요.\""], ["We have nothing clever to say—not one of us.", "* 이합체시(acrostic): 각 행의 첫 글자를 위에서 아래로 모으면 어떤 단어나 이름이 되도록 지은 시."]], [["“Yes, yes, pray pass me,”", "\"그래요, 그래, 제발 나를 건너뛰어 주시오.\""], ["added her husband, with a sort of sneering consciousness; “I have nothing to say that can entertain Miss Woodhouse, or any other young lady.", "남편이 비웃는 듯 의식하며 덧붙였다. \"나는 미스 우드하우스나 다른 어떤 아가씨를 즐겁게 할 말이라곤 하나도 없으니까."], ["An old married man—quite good for nothing.", "늙은 유부남이란 영 쓸모가 없는 법이지."], ["Shall we walk, Augusta?”", "우리는 좀 걸을까, 오거스타?\""]], [["“With all my heart.", "\"그러죠, 기꺼이요."], ["I am really tired of exploring so long on one spot.", "한자리에서 이렇게 오래 답사하고 있자니 정말 지겨워요."], ["Come, Jane, take my other arm.”", "자, 제인, 내 다른 팔을 잡아요.\""]], [["Jane declined it, however, and the husband and wife walked off.", "그러나 제인은 사양했고, 부부는 걸어가 버렸다."], ["“Happy couple!”", "\"행복한 한 쌍이군요!\""], ["said Frank Churchill, as soon as they were out of hearing:—“How well they suit one another!—Very lucky—marrying as they did, upon an acquaintance formed only in a public place!—They only knew each other, I think, a few weeks in Bath!", "두 사람이 들리지 않을 만큼 멀어지자마자 프랭크 처칠이 말했다. \"얼마나 잘 어울립니까!"], ["Peculiarly lucky!—for as to any real knowledge of a person’s disposition that Bath, or any public place, can give—it is all nothing; there can be no knowledge.", "— 참 운도 좋지요 — 고작 공공장소에서 사귄 인연으로 저렇게 결혼했는데도 말이에요! — 제 생각엔 바스에서 겨우 몇 주 알고 지낸 사이였을걸요!"], ["It is only by seeing women in their own homes, among their own set, just as they always are, that you can form any just judgment.", "유난히 운이 좋았던 거죠! — 바스든 어떤 공공장소든, 한 사람의 성품을 진짜로 알게 해 줄 수 있느냐 하면 — 그건 다 아무것도 아니거든요."], ["Short of that, it is all guess and luck—and will generally be ill-luck.", "알 길이 없으니까요. 여자를 자기 집에서, 늘 어울리는 사람들 틈에서, 평소 그대로의 모습으로 보아야만 비로소 제대로 된 판단을 내릴 수 있는 법이지요."], ["How many a man has committed himself on a short acquaintance, and rued it all the rest of his life!”", "그게 못 미치면 죄다 짐작이고 운인데 — 대개는 불운으로 끝나고요. 짧은 교제만 믿고 제 몸을 묶었다가 평생을 두고 후회한 사내가 얼마나 많습니까!\""]], [["Miss Fairfax, who had seldom spoken before, except among her own confederates, spoke now.", "여태 제 편들끼리가 아니고는 좀처럼 입을 열지 않던 미스 페어팩스가 이번에는 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Such things do occur, undoubtedly.”—She was stopped by a cough.", "\"그런 일이야 분명히 일어나지요.\" — 그녀는 기침 때문에 말을 멈췄다."], ["Frank Churchill turned towards her to listen.", "프랭크 처칠이 귀 기울이려 그녀 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“You were speaking,”", "\"무슨 말씀을 하시려던 거죠.\""], ["said he, gravely.", "그가 진지하게 말했다."], ["She recovered her voice.", "그녀는 목소리를 가다듬었다."]], [["“I was only going to observe, that though such unfortunate circumstances do sometimes occur both to men and women, I cannot imagine them to be very frequent.", "\"저는 다만 이런 말을 하려던 거예요. 그런 불행한 처지가 남자에게든 여자에게든 더러 닥치기는 하지만, 그리 흔한 일이라고는 생각되지 않는다고요."], ["A hasty and imprudent attachment may arise—but there is generally time to recover from it afterwards.", "성급하고 경솔한 연정이 생겨날 수는 있겠지요 — 하지만 대개는 나중에 거기서 회복할 시간이 있는 법이에요."], ["I would be understood to mean, that it can be only weak, irresolute characters, (whose happiness must be always at the mercy of chance,) who will suffer an unfortunate acquaintance to be an inconvenience, an oppression for ever.”", "제 말씀의 뜻은, 불행한 인연을 두고두고 불편으로, 억압으로 떠안고 괴로워하는 사람은 (그 행복이 늘 우연의 손에 좌우될 수밖에 없는) 오직 마음이 약하고 줏대 없는 사람들뿐이라는 거예요.\""]], [["He made no answer; merely looked, and bowed in submission; and soon afterwards said, in a lively tone,", "그는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그저 바라보며 순순히 고개를 숙였을 뿐이다. 그러고는 이내 명랑한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Well, I have so little confidence in my own judgment, that whenever I marry, I hope some body will chuse my wife for me.", "\"음, 저는 제 판단을 너무 못 미더워해서, 제가 결혼할 때는 누가 제 아내를 골라 주면 좋겠습니다."], ["Will you?", "그래 주시겠어요?"], ["(turning to Emma.) Will you chuse a wife for me?—I am sure I should like any body fixed on by you.", "(엠마 쪽으로 돌아서며.) 제 아내를 골라 주시겠습니까? — 당신이 점찍은 사람이라면 누구든 마음에 들 게 분명하거든요."], ["You provide for the family, you know, (with a smile at his father).", "당신은 이 집안 일을 도맡으시잖아요. (아버지를 향해 미소 지으며.) 누구든 찾아 주세요."], ["Find some body for me.", "서두를 건 없습니다."], ["I am in no hurry. Adopt her, educate her.”", "양녀로 거두어, 가르쳐 주세요.\""]], [["“And make her like myself.”", "\"그래서 저를 닮게 만들고요.\""]], [["“By all means, if you can.”", "\"꼭 그렇게요, 그럴 수만 있다면.\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋아요."], ["I undertake the commission.", "그 일을 맡지요."], ["You shall have a charming wife.”", "매력적인 아내를 갖게 해 드릴게요.\""]], [["“She must be very lively, and have hazle eyes.", "\"무척 활달해야 하고, 개암빛 눈을 가져야 합니다."], ["I care for nothing else.", "다른 건 아무래도 좋아요."], ["I shall go abroad for a couple of years—and when I return, I shall come to you for my wife.", "저는 한 이 년 외국에 나가 있다가 — 돌아오면 당신께 제 아내를 찾으러 오겠습니다."], ["Remember.”", "기억해 두세요.\""]], [["Emma was in no danger of forgetting.", "엠마가 그것을 잊을 염려는 없었다."], ["It was a commission to touch every favourite feeling.", "그것은 그녀가 가장 아끼는 마음을 모두 건드리는 부탁이었다."], ["Would not Harriet be the very creature described?", "해리엇이야말로 그가 말한 바로 그 사람 아니겠는가?"], ["Hazle eyes excepted, two years more might make her all that he wished.", "개암빛 눈만 빼면, 이 년이 더 지나는 사이에 그녀는 그가 바라는 모든 것을 갖추게 될지도 몰랐다."], ["He might even have Harriet in his thoughts at the moment; who could say?", "어쩌면 그가 지금 이 순간에도 해리엇을 마음에 두고 있는지도 모를 일이었다. 누가 알겠는가?"], ["Referring the education to her seemed to imply it.", "교육을 그녀에게 맡긴다는 것이 그것을 넌지시 비추는 듯했다."]], [["“Now, ma’am,”", "\"자, 이모.\" 제인이 이모에게 말했다."], ["said Jane to her aunt, “shall we join Mrs. Elton?”", "\"이제 엘턴 부인한테 합류할까요?\""]], [["“If you please, my dear.", "\"그러자꾸나, 얘야."], ["With all my heart.", "기꺼이."], ["I am quite ready.", "나도 다 됐단다."], ["I was ready to have gone with her, but this will do just as well.", "진작 그분과 함께 갈 채비가 돼 있었는데, 이래도 마찬가지지."], ["We shall soon overtake her.", "곧 따라잡을 게다."], ["There she is—no, that’s somebody else.", "저기 계시네 — 아니, 저건 다른 사람이구나."], ["That’s one of the ladies in the Irish car party, not at all like her.—Well, I declare—”", "아일랜드 마차 일행의 부인들 중 한 분이지, 그분하고는 전혀 다르잖니. — 어머, 세상에 —\""]], [["They walked off, followed in half a minute by Mr. Knightley.", "그들은 걸어가 버렸고, 반 분쯤 뒤에 미스터 나이틀리가 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Mr. Weston, his son, Emma, and Harriet, only remained; and the young man’s spirits now rose to a pitch almost unpleasant.", "미스터 웨스턴과 그 아들, 엠마와 해리엇만 남았다."], ["Even Emma grew tired at last of flattery and merriment, and wished herself rather walking quietly about with any of the others, or sitting almost alone, and quite unattended to, in tranquil observation of the beautiful views beneath her.", "그러자 젊은이의 들뜸은 거의 거북할 지경으로 치솟았다. 엠마조차 마침내 추어주기와 흥겨움에 싫증이 나, 차라리 다른 누군가와 조용히 거닐거나, 거의 혼자 떨어져 아무도 신경 쓰지 않는 가운데 발아래 펼쳐진 아름다운 경치를 잠잠히 바라보고 있었으면 싶었다."], ["The appearance of the servants looking out for them to give notice of the carriages was a joyful sight; and even the bustle of collecting and preparing to depart, and the solicitude of Mrs.", "마차가 왔음을 알리려고 하인들이 그들을 찾아 두리번거리는 모습이 보이자 반가웠다."], ["Elton to have her carriage first, were gladly endured, in the prospect of the quiet drive home which was to close the very questionable enjoyments of this day of pleasure.", "그리고 짐을 챙겨 떠날 채비를 하는 부산함도, 제 마차를 먼저 대게 하려는 엘턴 부인의 안달도, 이 즐거움의 날의 미심쩍기 짝이 없는 흥취를 닫아 줄 조용한 귀갓길을 떠올리니 기꺼이 견딜 만했다."], ["Such another scheme, composed of so many ill-assorted people, she hoped never to be betrayed into again.", "이토록 안 맞는 사람들로 짜인 이런 모임에 또다시 끌려드는 일은 두 번 다시 없기를 그녀는 바랐다."]], [["While waiting for the carriage, she found Mr. Knightley by her side.", "마차를 기다리는 동안, 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리가 제 곁에 와 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["He looked around, as if to see that no one were near, and then said,", "그는 가까이에 아무도 없는지 살피듯 주위를 둘러보더니 말했다."]], [["“Emma, I must once more speak to you as I have been used to do: a privilege rather endured than allowed, perhaps, but I must still use it.", "\"엠마, 늘 해 오던 대로 한 번 더 말해야겠소. 어쩌면 허락받았다기보다 그저 참아 주어 온 권리인지도 모르지만, 그래도 나는 그 권리를 써야겠소."], ["I cannot see you acting wrong, without a remonstrance.", "나는 자네가 그르게 행동하는 걸 보고도 한마디 안 할 수가 없으니."], ["How could you be so unfeeling to Miss Bates?", "어떻게 미스 베이츠에게 그토록 매정하게 굴 수 있었나?"], ["How could you be so insolent in your wit to a woman of her character, age, and situation?—Emma, I had not thought it possible.”", "그런 성품에, 그런 나이에, 그런 처지의 여인에게 어떻게 그토록 무례한 재담을 던질 수가 있었나? — 엠마, 나는 자네가 그러리라고는 생각도 못 했네.\""]], [["Emma recollected, blushed, was sorry, but tried to laugh it off.", "엠마는 그 일을 떠올리고는 얼굴을 붉히며 미안해했지만, 웃어넘기려 했다."]], [["“Nay, how could I help saying what I did?—Nobody could have helped it.", "\"아니, 제가 그 말을 어떻게 안 할 수가 있었겠어요? — 누구라도 참기 어려웠을걸요."], ["It was not so very bad.", "그렇게 심한 것도 아니었어요."], ["I dare say she did not understand me.”", "그분은 분명 제 말뜻을 못 알아들으셨을 거예요.\""]], [["“I assure you she did.", "\"단언컨대 알아들었네."], ["She felt your full meaning.", "자네 말뜻을 죄다 알아들었어."], ["She has talked of it since.", "그 뒤로도 그 이야기를 하더군."], ["I wish you could have heard how she talked of it—with what candour and generosity.", "그분이 그 이야기를 어떻게 하던지 자네가 들었더라면 좋았을 텐데 — 얼마나 솔직하고 너그럽게 하던지."], ["I wish you could have heard her honouring your forbearance, in being able to pay her such attentions, as she was for ever receiving from yourself and your father, when her society must be so irksome.”", "자네와 자네 아버지한테서 늘 받아 온 그 갖은 친절을, 그토록 따분할 수밖에 없는 자기와의 교제를 그렇게 참아 가며 베풀어 주었다고, 자네의 그 인내를 얼마나 우러러 말하던지 자네가 들었더라면.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\" 엠마가 외쳤다."], ["cried Emma, “I know there is not a better creature in the world: but you must allow, that what is good and what is ridiculous are most unfortunately blended in her.”", "\"그분만큼 마음씨 좋은 사람이 세상에 없다는 건 저도 알아요. 하지만 인정하셔야죠, 그분에게는 안된 일이지만 좋은 점과 우스꽝스러운 점이 어쩜 그렇게 뒤섞여 있는지를요.\""]], [["“They are blended,”", "\"뒤섞여 있지.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “I acknowledge; and, were she prosperous, I could allow much for the occasional prevalence of the ridiculous over the good.", "\"인정하네. 그리고 그분이 넉넉한 처지라면, 좋은 점보다 우스꽝스러운 점이 이따금 더 두드러진들 나도 많이 봐줄 수 있을 거야."], ["Were she a woman of fortune, I would leave every harmless absurdity to take its chance, I would not quarrel with you for any liberties of manner.", "그분이 재산 있는 여인이라면, 해롭지 않은 우스운 짓이야 운에 맡겨 두지, 말투 좀 함부로 한다고 자네와 다투지 않겠네. 그분이 처지가 자네와 대등하다면 — 하지만 엠마, 사정이 거기서 얼마나 동떨어져 있는지 생각해 보게."], ["Were she your equal in situation—but, Emma, consider how far this is from being the case.", "그분은 가난해. 타고난 안락에서 굴러떨어졌고, 만일 늙도록 산다면 십중팔구 더 떨어질 거야."], ["She is poor; she has sunk from the comforts she was born to; and, if she live to old age, must probably sink more.", "그분의 처지야말로 자네의 연민을 받아 마땅하지. 정말이지 못된 짓이었네!"], ["Her situation should secure your compassion.", "자네가 — 그분이 갓난아이 적부터 알아 온 자네가, 그분의 눈길이 영광이던 시절부터 자라나는 걸 지켜봐 온 자네가, 이제 와서 분별없는 들뜸과 그 순간의 우쭐함에 빠져 그분을 비웃고, 그분을 욕보이다니 — 그것도 조카 앞에서 — 게다가 다른 사람들 앞에서, 그중 많은 이가 (적어도 몇몇은 분명히) 자네가 그분을 어떻게 대하느냐에 따라 그대로 휘둘릴 사람들 앞에서 말이야."], ["It was badly done, indeed!", "— 이런 말이 자네에게 달갑지 않다는 거 아네, 엠마 — 나에게도 결코 달가운 일이 아니야."], ["You, whom she had known from an infant, whom she had seen grow up from a period when her notice was an honour, to have you now, in thoughtless spirits, and the pride of the moment, laugh at her, humble her—and before her niece, too—and before others, many of whom (certainly some,) would be entirely guided by your treatment of her.—This is not pleasant to you, Emma—and it is very far from pleasant to me; but I must, I will,—I will tell you truths while I can; satisfied with proving myself your friend by very faithful counsel, and trusting that you will some time or other do me greater justice than you can do now.”", "하지만 나는, 할 수 있는 동안에는, 자네에게 진실을 말해 두어야겠고, 말하고야 말겠네. 충직한 조언으로 내가 자네의 벗임을 보여 주는 것으로 만족하면서, 언젠가는 자네가 지금은 못 하더라도 나를 좀 더 옳게 평가해 주리라 믿으면서 말이야.\""]], [["While they talked, they were advancing towards the carriage; it was ready; and, before she could speak again, he had handed her in.", "이야기를 나누는 사이 그들은 마차 쪽으로 다가가고 있었다. 마차가 채비돼 있었고, 그녀가 다시 입을 열기도 전에 그는 그녀를 부축해 태웠다."], ["He had misinterpreted the feelings which had kept her face averted, and her tongue motionless.", "그는 그녀가 얼굴을 돌리고 입을 굳게 다물고 있던 그 심정을 잘못 읽었다."], ["They were combined only of anger against herself, mortification, and deep concern.", "그것은 오로지 자기 자신을 향한 분노와 수치와 깊은 자책이 뒤엉킨 것이었다. 그녀는 말을 할 수가 없었다."], ["She had not been able to speak; and, on entering the carriage, sunk back for a moment overcome—then reproaching herself for having taken no leave, making no acknowledgment, parting in apparent sullenness, she looked out with voice and hand eager to shew a difference; but it was just too late.", "그리고 마차에 오르며 잠시 무너지듯 뒤로 기댔다 — 그러다 작별 인사도 못 하고, 아무 감사의 표시도 못 하고, 보기에 뾰로통한 채로 헤어진 자신을 자책하며, 그게 아님을 보이려 목소리와 손짓을 다급히 내밀며 밖을 내다보았다. 그러나 너무 늦은 뒤였다."], ["He had turned away, and the horses were in motion.", "그는 이미 돌아서 있었고, 말들은 움직이고 있었다."], ["She continued to look back, but in vain; and soon, with what appeared unusual speed, they were half way down the hill, and every thing left far behind.", "그녀는 계속 뒤돌아보았으나 헛일이었다. 이윽고, 유난히 빠른 듯한 속도로, 마차는 어느새 언덕을 반쯤 내려와 모든 것을 저 멀리 뒤로 남겨 두고 있었다."], ["She was vexed beyond what could have been expressed—almost beyond what she could conceal.", "그녀는 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 — 거의 숨기지 못할 만큼 — 속이 상했다."], ["Never had she felt so agitated, mortified, grieved, at any circumstance in her life.", "평생 어떤 일에서도 이토록 마음이 흔들리고, 수치스럽고, 비통했던 적은 없었다."], ["She was most forcibly struck.", "그녀는 너무도 세차게 정곡을 찔린 것이다."], ["The truth of this representation there was no denying.", "그가 그려 보인 것이 진실임은 부인할 수 없었다."], ["She felt it at her heart.", "그녀는 그것을 가슴으로 느꼈다."], ["How could she have been so brutal, so cruel to Miss Bates!", "어떻게 미스 베이츠에게 그토록 모질고, 그토록 잔인할 수 있었단 말인가!"], ["How could she have exposed herself to such ill opinion in any one she valued!", "어떻게 그녀가 귀히 여기는 사람의 눈에 그런 나쁜 평판을 자초할 수 있었단 말인가!"], ["And how suffer him to leave her without saying one word of gratitude, of concurrence, of common kindness!", "그리고 어떻게 그를, 감사도, 수긍도, 그저 흔한 다정함의 한마디조차 건네지 못한 채 떠나보낼 수 있었단 말인가!"]], [["Time did not compose her.", "시간이 흘러도 그녀는 가라앉지 않았다."], ["As she reflected more, she seemed but to feel it more.", "곱씹을수록 그 마음은 더 깊어지기만 하는 듯했다."], ["She never had been so depressed.", "이토록 가라앉아 본 적이 없었다."], ["Happily it was not necessary to speak.", "다행히 말을 해야 할 일은 없었다."], ["There was only Harriet, who seemed not in spirits herself, fagged, and very willing to be silent; and Emma felt the tears running down her cheeks almost all the way home, without being at any trouble to check them, extraordinary as they were.", "곁에는 해리엇뿐이었는데, 그녀 역시 기운이 없는지 지쳐서 잠자코 있고 싶어 했다. 엠마는 집으로 오는 거의 내내 두 뺨을 타고 흐르는 눈물을, 그토록 유난스러운데도, 굳이 멈추려 들지 않은 채 그대로 두었다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter VIII", "# 제3권 8장"]], [["The wretchedness of a scheme to Box Hill was in Emma’s thoughts all the evening.", "박스 힐 소풍이 얼마나 비참한 계획이었던가 하는 생각이 그날 저녁 내내 엠마의 머릿속을 떠나지 않았다."], ["How it might be considered by the rest of the party, she could not tell.", "일행의 나머지 사람들이 그것을 어찌 여길지는 알 수 없었다."], ["They, in their different homes, and their different ways, might be looking back on it with pleasure; but in her view it was a morning more completely misspent, more totally bare of rational satisfaction at the time, and more to be abhorred in recollection, than any she had ever passed.", "저마다의 집에서, 저마다의 방식으로, 그들은 그날을 즐겁게 돌아보고 있을지도 몰랐다. 그러나 엠마가 보기에 그날 오전은 그녀가 지금껏 보낸 어떤 오전보다도 더 철저히 허비된 오전이었고, 그 당시로서는 어떤 이성적인 만족도 깡그리 결여된 시간이었으며, 떠올리면 떠올릴수록 더욱 진저리쳐지는 오전이었다."], ["A whole evening of back-gammon with her father, was felicity to it.", "아버지와 주사위 놀이를 하며 보내는 저녁 한나절이 차라리 그보다 행복이었으리라."], ["There, indeed, lay real pleasure, for there she was giving up the sweetest hours of the twenty-four to his comfort; and feeling that, unmerited as might be the degree of his fond affection and confiding esteem, she could not, in her general conduct, be open to any severe reproach.", "거기에는 참된 즐거움이 있었으니, 그곳에서 그녀는 하루 스물네 시간 중 가장 달콤한 시간을 아버지의 안락을 위해 내어 주고 있었고, 아버지의 그 애틋한 사랑과 신뢰 어린 존경이 분에 넘치는 것일지언정, 적어도 평소의 처신에서는 어떤 가혹한 책망에도 떳떳할 수 있다고 느꼈기 때문이었다."], ["As a daughter, she hoped she was not without a heart.", "딸로서, 그녀는 자신에게 마음이 없지는 않기를 바랐다."], ["She hoped no one could have said to her, “How could you be so unfeeling to your father?—I must, I will tell you truths while I can.”", "그 누구도 그녀에게 \"어떻게 아버지께 그토록 매정할 수 있어?—할 수 있을 때, 꼭, 기어이 진실을 말해 주어야겠다\"라고 말할 수는 없기를 바랐다."], ["Miss Bates should never again—no, never!", "미스 베이츠에게는 다시는 그러지 않으리라—그래, 결코 다시는!"], ["If attention, in future, could do away the past, she might hope to be forgiven.", "앞으로의 배려가 지난날을 지워 줄 수 있다면, 그녀는 용서받기를 바랄 수 있으리라."], ["She had been often remiss, her conscience told her so; remiss, perhaps, more in thought than fact; scornful, ungracious.", "자신이 자주 소홀했음을 양심이 일러 주었다. 어쩌면 행동보다는 생각에서 더 소홀했을 것이고, 멸시하고 무례했을 것이다."], ["But it should be so no more.", "그러나 더는 그래서는 안 되었다."], ["In the warmth of true contrition, she would call upon her the very next morning, and it should be the beginning, on her side, of a regular, equal, kindly intercourse.", "진정한 뉘우침의 뜨거움 속에서, 바로 다음 날 아침 그녀를 찾아가리라, 그리고 그것이 자기 쪽에서 시작하는 규칙적이고 대등하며 다정한 교제의 출발점이 되리라."]], [["She was just as determined when the morrow came, and went early, that nothing might prevent her.", "이튿날이 오자 그녀는 여전히 굳은 결심으로, 아무것도 자신을 막지 못하도록 일찍 집을 나섰다."], ["It was not unlikely, she thought, that she might see Mr. Knightley in her way; or, perhaps, he might come in while she were paying her visit.", "가는 길에 미스터 나이틀리를 보게 될 법도 했고, 어쩌면 그가 방문하는 동안 들이닥칠지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["She had no objection.", "그렇다 해도 거리낄 것이 없었다."], ["She would not be ashamed of the appearance of the penitence, so justly and truly hers.", "그토록 정당하고 진실로 자신의 것인 뉘우침의 모습을 드러내는 것이 부끄럽지 않았다."], ["Her eyes were towards Donwell as she walked, but she saw him not.", "걸어가는 내내 그녀의 눈길은 돈웰 쪽을 향했으나, 그의 모습은 보이지 않았다."]], [["“The ladies were all at home.”", "\"숙녀분들 모두 댁에 계십니다.\""], ["She had never rejoiced at the sound before, nor ever before entered the passage, nor walked up the stairs, with any wish of giving pleasure, but in conferring obligation, or of deriving it, except in subsequent ridicule.", "그녀는 이전까지 그 말이 반가웠던 적이 없었고, 그 복도에 들어서거나 계단을 오르며 기쁨을 주고자 하는 마음을 품어 본 적도 없었으니, 오직 은혜를 베풀거나 거두려는 마음, 아니면 나중에 비웃을 거리를 얻으려는 마음뿐이었다."]], [["There was a bustle on her approach; a good deal of moving and talking.", "그녀가 다가가자 한바탕 부산함이 일었다. 오가는 발걸음과 말소리가 분주했다."], ["She heard Miss Bates’s voice, something was to be done in a hurry; the maid looked frightened and awkward; hoped she would be pleased to wait a moment, and then ushered her in too soon.", "미스 베이츠의 목소리가 들렸고, 무언가를 서둘러 해치워야 하는 모양이었다. 하녀는 겁먹고 어색한 얼굴로, 잠시만 기다려 주십사 청하더니, 너무 일찍 그녀를 들여보냈다."], ["The aunt and niece seemed both escaping into the adjoining room.", "이모와 조카는 둘 다 옆방으로 달아나는 듯이 보였다."], ["Jane she had a distinct glimpse of, looking extremely ill; and, before the door had shut them out, she heard Miss Bates saying, “Well, my dear, I shall say you are laid down upon the bed, and I am sure you are ill enough.”", "제인은 무척 아파 보이는 모습으로 그녀의 눈에 또렷이 스쳐 갔고, 문이 두 사람을 가려 닫히기 전에, 미스 베이츠가 이렇게 말하는 소리가 들렸다. \"그래, 얘야, 침대에 누워 있다고 내가 말해 주마, 정말이지 너는 그럴 만큼 충분히 아프니까.\""]], [["Poor old Mrs. Bates, civil and humble as usual, looked as if she did not quite understand what was going on.", "가엾은 늙은 베이츠 부인은 여느 때처럼 정중하고 겸손한 모습으로, 지금 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 잘 알아듣지 못하는 듯한 얼굴이었다."]], [["“I am afraid Jane is not very well,”", "\"제인이 좀 안 좋은 것 같아요.\""], ["said she, “but I do not know; they tell me she is well.", "그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 잘 모르겠네요."], ["I dare say my daughter will be here presently, Miss Woodhouse.", "다들 괜찮다고 일러는 주는데. 딸아이가 곧 올 거예요, 미스 우드하우스."], ["I hope you find a chair.", "의자가 보이시는지요."], ["I wish Hetty had not gone.", "헤티가 나가지 말았어야 했는데."], ["I am very little able—Have you a chair, ma’am?", "나는 워낙 거동이 변변치 못해서요—의자가 있으신가요, 아가씨?"], ["Do you sit where you like?", "편한 데 앉으시지요?"], ["I am sure she will be here presently.”", "그 애가 분명 곧 올 겁니다.\""]], [["Emma seriously hoped she would.", "엠마는 진심으로 그러기를 바랐다."], ["She had a moment’s fear of Miss Bates keeping away from her.", "미스 베이츠가 자신을 피해 멀리 떨어져 있을까 봐 잠시 두려운 마음이 들었다."], ["But Miss Bates soon came—“Very happy and obliged”—but Emma’s conscience told her that there was not the same cheerful volubility as before—less ease of look and manner.", "그러나 미스 베이츠는 곧 나타났다—\"참 기쁘고 황송하다\"며—그러나 엠마의 양심은, 이전과 같은 명랑한 수다스러움이 없다고, 표정과 거동에 예전만큼의 편안함이 없다고 일러 주었다."], ["A very friendly inquiry after Miss Fairfax, she hoped, might lead the way to a return of old feelings.", "미스 페어팩스의 안부를 더없이 다정하게 물으면, 어쩌면 옛정으로 돌아가는 길이 트이리라 그녀는 바랐다."], ["The touch seemed immediate.", "그 시도는 곧바로 효과를 본 듯했다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Miss Woodhouse, how kind you are!—I suppose you have heard—and are come to give us joy.", "미스 우드하우스, 어쩜 이리 친절하실까!—이미 들으셨겠지요—그래서 우리에게 축하해 주려고 오신 거겠지요."], ["This does not seem much like joy, indeed, in me—(twinkling away a tear or two)—but it will be very trying for us to part with her, after having had her so long, and she has a dreadful headache just now, writing all the morning:—such long letters, you know, to be written to Colonel Campbell, and Mrs.", "정말이지 제게는 그다지 기쁜 일 같지가 않지만요—(눈물 한두 방울을 깜빡여 떨치며)—그렇게 오래 데리고 있다가 그 애와 헤어지자니, 우리에게는 무척 힘든 일이 되겠어요. 게다가 그 애는 지금 끔찍한 두통을 앓고 있답니다, 아침 내내 편지를 쓰느라고요—그 긴 편지들 있잖아요, 캠벨 대령과 딕슨 부인에게 써야 하는."], ["Dixon.", "'얘야,'"], ["‘My dear,’ said I, ‘you will blind yourself’—for tears were in her eyes perpetually.", "하고 제가 말했지요, '눈이 멀겠구나'—눈물이 그칠 새 없이 글썽거리고 있었거든요."], ["One cannot wonder, one cannot wonder.", "무리도 아니지요, 무리도 아니에요. 엄청난 변화니까요."], ["It is a great change; and though she is amazingly fortunate—such a situation, I suppose, as no young woman before ever met with on first going out—do not think us ungrateful, Miss Woodhouse, for such surprising good fortune—(again dispersing her tears)—but, poor dear soul!", "그리고 그 애가 놀랄 만큼 운이 좋긴 하지만—젊은 여자가 처음 일을 나가면서 이런 자리를 만난 일은 일찍이 없었을 거예요—그렇다고 우리를 은혜도 모르는 사람들로 여기지 말아 주세요, 미스 우드하우스, 이런 놀라운 행운을 두고 말이지요—(또 한 번 눈물을 흩뜨리며)—하지만, 가엾은 것! 그 애가 얼마나 두통에 시달리는지 보셨다면."], ["if you were to see what a headache she has.", "몹시 아플 때는, 아시겠지만, 어떤 축복도 그 진가만큼 온전히 느껴지지가 않는 법이니까요. 그 애는 더없이 풀이 죽어 있어요."], ["When one is in great pain, you know one cannot feel any blessing quite as it may deserve.", "그 애를 보면, 그런 자리를 얻어 얼마나 기뻐하고 행복해하는지 아무도 짐작 못 할 거예요. 그 애가 나오지 못하는 것을 너그러이 봐주세요—거동이 안 되거든요—제 방으로 들어가 버렸어요—저는 침대에 좀 누워 있으라고 하고 싶은데요."], ["She is as low as possible.", "'얘야,'"], ["To look at her, nobody would think how delighted and happy she is to have secured such a situation.", "하고 제가 말했지요, '침대에 누워 있다고 내가 말해 두마.' 그래도 누워 있지를 않고, 방 안을 서성이고 있답니다."], ["You will excuse her not coming to you—she is not able—she is gone into her own room—I want her to lie down upon the bed.", "하지만 이제 편지를 다 썼으니, 곧 괜찮아질 거라고 하더군요. 미스 우드하우스, 당신을 못 뵙게 되어 그 애가 무척 서운해할 텐데, 당신의 너그러움으로 봐주실 거예요."], ["‘My dear,’ said I, ‘I shall say you are laid down upon the bed:’ but, however, she is not; she is walking about the room.", "문 앞에서 기다리게 해 드려서—저는 어찌나 부끄럽던지—그래도 어쩐지 한바탕 부산을 떨었지 뭐예요—마침 노크 소리를 못 들어서요, 당신이 계단에 오를 때까지 누가 오는지 몰랐거든요. '콜 부인일 거예요,'"], ["But, now that she has written her letters, she says she shall soon be well.", "하고 제가 말했지요, '틀림없어요. 이렇게 일찍 올 사람이 또 있겠어요.'"], ["She will be extremely sorry to miss seeing you, Miss Woodhouse, but your kindness will excuse her.", "'뭐,' 하고 그 애가 말했지요, '언젠가는 견뎌야 할 일이니, 지금이라고 안 될 것도 없죠.'"], ["You were kept waiting at the door—I was quite ashamed—but somehow there was a little bustle—for it so happened that we had not heard the knock, and till you were on the stairs, we did not know any body was coming.", "그런데 그때 패티가 들어와서 당신이라고 하지 뭐예요. '어머!'"], ["‘It is only Mrs. Cole,’ said I, ‘depend upon it.", "하고 제가 말했지요, '미스 우드하우스로구나."], ["Nobody else would come so early.’ ‘Well,’ said she, ‘it must be borne some time or other, and it may as well be now.’ But then Patty came in, and said it was you.", "분명 그 애가 보고 싶어 할 거다.'—'아무도 못 만나겠어요,' 하고 그 애가 말했어요."], ["‘Oh!’ said I, ‘it is Miss Woodhouse: I am sure you will like to see her.’—‘I can see nobody,’ said she; and up she got, and would go away; and that was what made us keep you waiting—and extremely sorry and ashamed we were.", "그러고는 벌떡 일어나 나가 버리려고 했지요. 그래서 당신을 기다리게 해 드린 거예요—얼마나 미안하고 부끄러웠는지요."], ["‘If you must go, my dear,’ said I, ‘you must, and I will say you are laid down upon the bed.’”", "'정 가야겠거든, 얘야,' 하고 제가 말했지요, '가야지, 그럼 침대에 누워 있다고 말해 두마.'\""]], [["Emma was most sincerely interested.", "엠마는 더없이 진심으로 마음이 움직였다."], ["Her heart had been long growing kinder towards Jane; and this picture of her present sufferings acted as a cure of every former ungenerous suspicion, and left her nothing but pity; and the remembrance of the less just and less gentle sensations of the past, obliged her to admit that Jane might very naturally resolve on seeing Mrs.", "그녀의 마음은 오래전부터 제인을 향해 차츰 너그러워지고 있었다."], ["Cole or any other steady friend, when she might not bear to see herself.", "그리고 지금 제인이 겪는 고통의 이 광경은 지난날의 너그럽지 못했던 온갖 의심을 치유해 주었고, 남은 것이라곤 오직 연민뿐이었다."], ["She spoke as she felt, with earnest regret and solicitude—sincerely wishing that the circumstances which she collected from Miss Bates to be now actually determined on, might be as much for Miss Fairfax’s advantage and comfort as possible.", "또한 지난날의 덜 공정하고 덜 다정했던 감정을 떠올리니, 제인이 정작 엠마 자신은 차마 만나 주지 못할 때라도 콜 부인이나 그 밖의 한결같은 벗을 만나기로 마음먹는 것은 지극히 당연한 일이었음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀는 느낀 대로, 간절한 안타까움과 염려를 담아 말했다—미스 베이츠에게서 들은 바, 이제 정말로 결정된 듯한 그 사정이 미스 페어팩스에게 가능한 한 이롭고 위안이 되기를 진심으로 바라면서."], ["“It must be a severe trial to them all.", "\"모두에게 혹독한 시련이겠어요."], ["She had understood it was to be delayed till Colonel Campbell’s return.”", "저는 캠벨 대령이 돌아오실 때까지 미뤄지는 줄로만 알았는데요.\""]], [["“So very kind!”", "\"어쩜 이리 친절하실까!\""], ["replied Miss Bates.", "미스 베이츠가 대답했다."], ["“But you are always kind.”", "\"하지만 당신은 늘 친절하시지요.\""]], [["There was no bearing such an “always;”", "그런 \"늘\"이라는 말은 도저히 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["and to break through her dreadful gratitude, Emma made the direct inquiry of—", "그래서 그녀의 끔찍한 감사 인사를 뚫고 나가려고, 엠마는 단도직입으로 물었다—"]], [["“Where—may I ask?—is Miss Fairfax going?”", "\"미스 페어팩스가—여쭤봐도 될까요?—어디로 가게 되었나요?\""]], [["“To a Mrs. Smallridge—charming woman—most superior—to have the charge of her three little girls—delightful children.", "\"스몰리지 부인이라는 분께요—매력적인 분이지요—더없이 훌륭하시고요—세 어린 따님을 맡으러 가는 거예요—참 사랑스러운 아이들이지요."], ["Impossible that any situation could be more replete with comfort; if we except, perhaps, Mrs.", "그보다 더 안락함이 가득한 자리는 있을 수 없을 거예요."], ["Suckling’s own family, and Mrs. Bragge’s; but Mrs.", "아마 서클링 부인 댁이나 브래그 부인 댁이라면 모를까요."], ["Smallridge is intimate with both, and in the very same neighbourhood:—lives only four miles from Maple Grove.", "그런데 스몰리지 부인은 두 댁 모두와 가깝게 지내시고, 게다가 바로 같은 동네에 사세요—메이플 그로브에서 겨우 사 마일 떨어진 곳이지요."], ["Jane will be only four miles from Maple Grove.”", "제인은 메이플 그로브에서 겨우 사 마일 거리에 있게 되는 거예요.\""]], [["“Mrs. Elton, I suppose, has been the person to whom Miss Fairfax owes—”", "\"그러니까 미스 페어팩스가 신세를 진 분은 엘턴 부인이시겠군요—\""]], [["“Yes, our good Mrs. Elton.", "\"네, 우리의 고마운 엘턴 부인이지요."], ["The most indefatigable, true friend.", "더없이 지칠 줄 모르는, 참된 벗이에요. 거절을 받아들이려 하지 않았답니다."], ["She would not take a denial.", "제인이 '싫다'고 하게 두질 않았어요."], ["She would not let Jane say, ‘No;’ for when Jane first heard of it, (it was the day before yesterday, the very morning we were at Donwell,) when Jane first heard of it, she was quite decided against accepting the offer, and for the reasons you mention; exactly as you say, she had made up her mind to close with nothing till Colonel Campbell’s return, and nothing should induce her to enter into any engagement at present—and so she told Mrs.", "제인이 처음 이 이야기를 들었을 때 (그저께였어요, 바로 우리가 돈웰에 갔던 그날 아침이지요), 제인이 처음 그 이야기를 들었을 때는, 당신이 말씀하신 바로 그 이유로, 그 제안을 거절하기로 단단히 마음먹고 있었거든요. 당신 말씀 그대로, 캠벨 대령이 돌아오실 때까지는 아무것도 결정짓지 않기로 작정해서, 지금으로서는 어떤 약속에도 응하지 않겠다고요—그래서 엘턴 부인에게 몇 번이고 그렇게 말했답니다—저는 정말이지 그 애가 마음을 바꾸리라고는 꿈에도 생각 못 했어요!—하지만 판단력이 결코 그르치는 법이 없는 그 고마운 엘턴 부인은, 저보다 멀리 내다보셨던 거지요."], ["Elton over and over again—and I am sure I had no more idea that she would change her mind!—but that good Mrs.", "그분처럼 그렇게 친절한 방식으로 버티며 제인의 대답을 받아들이지 않을 사람은 아무나 있는 게 아니에요."], ["Elton, whose judgment never fails her, saw farther than I did.", "그분은 제인이 바라는 대로 어제 그런 거절의 편지를 쓰는 일은 결단코 하지 않겠다고 딱 잘라 말씀하셨거든요."], ["It is not every body that would have stood out in such a kind way as she did, and refuse to take Jane’s answer; but she positively declared she would not write any such denial yesterday, as Jane wished her; she would wait—and, sure enough, yesterday evening it was all settled that Jane should go. Quite a surprize to me!", "기다리겠다고요—그러더니, 아니나 다를까, 어제저녁에 제인이 가기로 모든 게 정해진 거예요."], ["I had not the least idea!—Jane took Mrs.", "저로서는 그야말로 깜짝 놀랄 일이었죠!"], ["Elton aside, and told her at once, that upon thinking over the advantages of Mrs. Smallridge’s situation, she had come to the resolution of accepting it.—I did not know a word of it till it was all settled.”", "전혀 짐작도 못 했어요!—제인이 엘턴 부인을 따로 불러서는, 스몰리지 부인 자리의 이점을 곰곰이 생각해 보니 받아들이기로 결심했다고 단번에 말씀드렸다지 뭐예요.—저는 모든 게 다 정해질 때까지 그 일을 한마디도 몰랐답니다.\""]], [["“You spent the evening with Mrs. Elton?”", "\"엘턴 부인과 저녁을 함께 보내셨군요?\""]], [["“Yes, all of us; Mrs.", "\"네, 우리 모두요."], ["Elton would have us come.", "엘턴 부인이 꼭 오라고 하셨거든요."], ["It was settled so, upon the hill, while we were walking about with Mr. Knightley.", "그렇게 정해진 거예요, 그 언덕에서요, 우리가 미스터 나이틀리와 함께 거닐고 있을 때 말이지요."], ["‘You must all spend your evening with us,’ said she—‘I positively must have you all come.’”", "'꼭 모두 우리 집에서 저녁을 보내셔야 해요,' 하고 그분이 말씀하셨어요—'단연코 모두 오셔야만 해요.'\""]], [["“Mr. Knightley was there too, was he?”", "\"미스터 나이틀리도 거기 계셨던가요?\""]], [["“No, not Mr. Knightley; he declined it from the first; and though I thought he would come, because Mrs.", "\"아니요, 미스터 나이틀리는요."], ["Elton declared she would not let him off, he did not;—but my mother, and Jane, and I, were all there, and a very agreeable evening we had.", "그분은 처음부터 사양하셨어요. 엘턴 부인이 절대 빠지게 두지 않겠다고 하셔서 저는 그분이 오시리라 생각했는데, 안 오셨답니다—하지만 어머니와 제인과 저는 모두 거기 있었고, 정말이지 아주 유쾌한 저녁을 보냈어요."], ["Such kind friends, you know, Miss Woodhouse, one must always find agreeable, though every body seemed rather fagged after the morning’s party.", "그렇게 친절한 벗들과 함께라면, 아시잖아요, 미스 우드하우스, 언제나 즐거운 법이지요. 비록 다들 그날 오전의 모임 뒤라 좀 지쳐 보이긴 했지만요."], ["Even pleasure, you know, is fatiguing—and I cannot say that any of them seemed very much to have enjoyed it.", "즐거움이라는 것도, 아시다시피, 사람을 지치게 하는 법이라서요—그리고 그중 누구도 그날을 그리 많이 즐긴 것 같지는 않았다고 해야겠네요."], ["However, I shall always think it a very pleasant party, and feel extremely obliged to the kind friends who included me in it.”", "그렇지만 저는 언제까지나 그날을 아주 유쾌한 모임으로 여길 거예요. 그리고 저를 끼워 주신 친절한 벗들께 더없이 고마운 마음이고요.\""]], [["“Miss Fairfax, I suppose, though you were not aware of it, had been making up her mind the whole day?”", "\"미스 페어팩스는, 당신은 모르셨겠지만, 아마 그날 하루 종일 마음을 정하고 있었던 모양이지요?\""]], [["“I dare say she had.”", "\"분명 그랬을 거예요.\""]], [["“Whenever the time may come, it must be unwelcome to her and all her friends—but I hope her engagement will have every alleviation that is possible—I mean, as to the character and manners of the family.”", "\"그때가 언제 오든, 그 애나 그 애의 모든 벗에게 달갑지 않은 일이겠지요—하지만 그 애의 일자리에 가능한 모든 위안이 따르기를 바라요—제 말은, 그 가족의 인품과 됨됨이 말이에요.\""]], [["“Thank you, dear Miss Woodhouse.", "\"고마워요, 사랑스러운 미스 우드하우스."], ["Yes, indeed, there is every thing in the world that can make her happy in it.", "네, 정말이지, 그 자리에는 그 애를 행복하게 해 줄 만한 것이 세상에 있는 대로 다 갖춰져 있답니다."], ["Except the Sucklings and Bragges, there is not such another nursery establishment, so liberal and elegant, in all Mrs. Elton’s acquaintance.", "서클링 댁과 브래그 댁을 빼고는, 엘턴 부인이 아는 모든 사람 중에 그토록 후하고 우아한 양육 살림은 또 없어요."], ["Mrs. Smallridge, a most delightful woman!—A style of living almost equal to Maple Grove—and as to the children, except the little Sucklings and little Bragges, there are not such elegant sweet children anywhere.", "스몰리지 부인은, 더없이 사랑스러운 분이지요!—거의 메이플 그로브에 맞먹는 생활 양식이고요—아이들로 말하자면, 어린 서클링네 아이들과 어린 브래그네 아이들을 빼면, 어디에도 그렇게 우아하고 사랑스러운 아이들은 없답니다."], ["Jane will be treated with such regard and kindness!—It will be nothing but pleasure, a life of pleasure.—And her salary!—I really cannot venture to name her salary to you, Miss Woodhouse.", "제인은 그토록 정중하고 친절한 대접을 받게 될 거예요!—오직 즐거움뿐, 즐거움으로 가득한 삶일 거예요.—게다가 급료라니요!—정말이지 그 급료를 당신께 입에 올릴 엄두가 안 나는군요, 미스 우드하우스."], ["Even you, used as you are to great sums, would hardly believe that so much could be given to a young person like Jane.”", "큰 액수에 익숙하신 당신조차도, 제인 같은 젊은 사람에게 그만한 돈이 주어질 수 있다는 걸 좀처럼 믿지 못하실 거예요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["madam,”", "부인,\""], ["cried Emma, “if other children are at all like what I remember to have been myself, I should think five times the amount of what I have ever yet heard named as a salary on such occasions, dearly earned.”", "엠마가 외쳤다. \"다른 아이들이 제가 기억하는 어릴 적 저와 조금이라도 닮았다면, 그런 자리에서 급료라고 일컬어지는 액수가 제가 지금껏 들어 본 것의 다섯 배라 해도, 그건 톡톡히 값을 치르고 버는 돈이라 여기겠어요.\""]], [["“You are so noble in your ideas!”", "\"생각하시는 게 어쩜 그리 고결하실까요!\""]], [["“And when is Miss Fairfax to leave you?”", "\"그래서 미스 페어팩스는 언제 당신 곁을 떠나게 되나요?\""]], [["“Very soon, very soon, indeed; that’s the worst of it.", "\"아주 곧, 정말이지 아주 곧이에요. 그게 제일 안타까운 점이죠."], ["Within a fortnight.", "보름 안이에요."], ["Mrs. Smallridge is in a great hurry.", "스몰리지 부인이 무척 서두르시거든요."], ["My poor mother does not know how to bear it.", "가엾은 어머니는 그 일을 어떻게 견뎌야 할지 모르세요."], ["So then, I try to put it out of her thoughts, and say, Come ma’am, do not let us think about it any more.”", "그래서 저는 어머니가 그 생각을 떨치도록 애쓰면서, 자, 어머니, 우리 더는 그 생각 말기로 해요, 하고 말한답니다.\""]], [["“Her friends must all be sorry to lose her; and will not Colonel and Mrs.", "\"그 애의 벗들은 모두 그 애를 잃게 되어 서운하겠지요."], ["Campbell be sorry to find that she has engaged herself before their return?”", "그리고 캠벨 대령 부부는, 돌아오시기도 전에 그 애가 약속을 맺어 버린 것을 알면 서운해하시지 않을까요?\""]], [["“Yes; Jane says she is sure they will; but yet, this is such a situation as she cannot feel herself justified in declining.", "\"네, 제인도 분명 그러실 거라고 해요."], ["I was so astonished when she first told me what she had been saying to Mrs.", "하지만 그래도 이건 거절하는 게 도리에 맞다고 스스로 여길 수 없는 그런 자리거든요."], ["Elton, and when Mrs. Elton at the same moment came congratulating me upon it!", "그 애가 엘턴 부인에게 한 말을 처음 제게 들려주었을 때, 그리고 바로 그 순간 엘턴 부인이 와서 그 일을 두고 제게 축하를 건네셨을 때, 저는 어찌나 놀랐던지요!"], ["It was before tea—stay—no, it could not be before tea, because we were just going to cards—and yet it was before tea, because I remember thinking—Oh!", "그게 차 마시기 전이었는데—가만있자—아니, 차 마시기 전일 리가 없어요, 우리가 막 카드놀이를 하려던 참이었으니까요—그래도 차 마시기 전이긴 했어요, 제가 이렇게 생각했던 게 기억나거든요—아! 아니지, 이제 생각났어요, 됐어요."], ["no, now I recollect, now I have it; something happened before tea, but not that. Mr.", "차 마시기 전에 무슨 일이 있긴 했는데, 그건 아니었어요."], ["Elton was called out of the room before tea, old John Abdy’s son wanted to speak with him.", "미스터 엘턴이 차 마시기 전에 방에서 불려 나갔거든요, 늙은 존 애브디의 아들이 그분과 이야기를 하고 싶어 했어요. 가엾은 늙은 존, 저는 그분을 무척 존경한답니다."], ["Poor old John, I have a great regard for him; he was clerk to my poor father twenty-seven years; and now, poor old man, he is bed-ridden, and very poorly with the rheumatic gout in his joints—I must go and see him to-day; and so will Jane, I am sure, if she gets out at all.", "그분은 제 가엾은 아버지의 서기로 스물일곱 해를 일하셨거든요. 그런데 이제, 가엾은 노인이, 자리보전을 하고 누워서, 관절에 류머티즘 통풍이 와 아주 편찮으세요—저는 오늘 그분을 뵈러 가야겠어요."], ["And poor John’s son came to talk to Mr.", "제인도, 외출이라도 할 수 있게 되면 분명 그럴 거예요."], ["Elton about relief from the parish; he is very well to do himself, you know, being head man at the Crown, ostler, and every thing of that sort, but still he cannot keep his father without some help; and so, when Mr.", "그래서 가엾은 존의 아들이 미스터 엘턴에게 교구의 구제에 관해 의논하러 온 거예요. 그 아들은 제 형편이야 아주 넉넉하답니다, 아시다시피 크라운 여관의 우두머리 일꾼이고, 마부에다, 그런 온갖 일을 다 보거든요."], ["Elton came back, he told us what John ostler had been telling him, and then it came out about the chaise having been sent to Randalls to take Mr.", "그래도 도움 없이는 아버지를 모실 수가 없는 거지요. 그래서 미스터 엘턴이 돌아오셔서, 그 존 마부가 들려준 이야기를 우리에게 전해 주셨는데, 그때 미스터 프랭크 처칠을 리치먼드로 데려가려고 랜덜스로 마차를 보낸 일이 드러났답니다."], ["Frank Churchill to Richmond. That was what happened before tea.", "그게 차 마시기 전에 있었던 일이에요."], ["It was after tea that Jane spoke to Mrs. Elton.”", "제인이 엘턴 부인에게 말한 건 차 마신 다음이었고요.\""]], [["Miss Bates would hardly give Emma time to say how perfectly new this circumstance was to her; but as without supposing it possible that she could be ignorant of any of the particulars of Mr.", "미스 베이츠는 엠마에게 이 사정이 자신에게 얼마나 전혀 새로운 것인지 말할 틈조차 좀처럼 주지 않았다."], ["Frank Churchill’s going, she proceeded to give them all, it was of no consequence.", "그러나 그녀가, 엠마가 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 떠난 일의 세세한 사정을 모를 리가 있겠느냐고는 추호도 의심하지 않은 채 그 모든 것을 늘어놓기 시작했으니, 그것은 아무래도 상관없는 일이었다."]], [["What Mr. Elton had learned from the ostler on the subject, being the accumulation of the ostler’s own knowledge, and the knowledge of the servants at Randalls, was, that a messenger had come over from Richmond soon after the return of the party from Box Hill—which messenger, however, had been no more than was expected; and that Mr.", "이 일에 관해 미스터 엘턴이 그 마부에게서 들은 바는, 마부 자신이 아는 것과 랜덜스 하인들이 아는 것을 그러모은 것이었으니, 박스 힐에서 일행이 돌아온 직후 리치먼드에서 한 전령이 건너왔다는 것이었다—다만 그 전령은 예상된 것 이상은 아니었다."], ["Churchill had sent his nephew a few lines, containing, upon the whole, a tolerable account of Mrs. Churchill, and only wishing him not to delay coming back beyond the next morning early; but that Mr.", "그리고 미스터 처칠이 조카에게 몇 줄을 보냈는데, 대체로 처칠 부인에 관해 그런대로 괜찮은 소식을 담은 것으로, 다만 이튿날 아침 일찍을 넘기지 말고 돌아오기를 바란다는 내용이었다."], ["Frank Churchill having resolved to go home directly, without waiting at all, and his horse seeming to have got a cold, Tom had been sent off immediately for the Crown chaise, and the ostler had stood out and seen it pass by, the boy going a good pace, and driving very steady.", "그러나 미스터 프랭크 처칠은 조금도 기다리지 않고 곧장 집으로 가기로 작정했고, 그의 말이 감기에 걸린 듯하자, 톰이 즉시 크라운 여관의 마차를 부르러 보내졌으며, 마부는 밖에 나가 서서 그것이 지나가는 것을 보았는데, 마부 소년이 꽤 빠른 속도로, 아주 안정되게 몰더라는 것이었다."]], [["There was nothing in all this either to astonish or interest, and it caught Emma’s attention only as it united with the subject which already engaged her mind. The contrast between Mrs.", "이 모든 이야기에는 놀랍거나 흥미로울 만한 것이 하나도 없었고, 다만 이미 그녀의 마음을 사로잡고 있던 주제와 맞물리는 한에서만 엠마의 주의를 끌었다."], ["Churchill’s importance in the world, and Jane Fairfax’s, struck her; one was every thing, the other nothing—and she sat musing on the difference of woman’s destiny, and quite unconscious on what her eyes were fixed, till roused by Miss Bates’s saying,", "세상에서 처칠 부인이 지니는 비중과 제인 페어팩스가 지니는 비중 사이의 대조가 그녀를 쳤다. 한 사람은 전부였고, 다른 한 사람은 아무것도 아니었다—그녀는 여자의 운명이라는 것이 이토록 다르다는 데 잠겨 앉아, 자기 눈길이 어디에 머물러 있는지조차 까맣게 모르고 있다가, 미스 베이츠의 이런 말에 정신이 들었다."]], [["“Aye, I see what you are thinking of, the pianoforte.", "\"아, 무슨 생각을 하시는지 알겠어요, 그 피아노포르테 말이지요. 그건 어떻게 되는 거냐고요?—그래요, 옳은 말씀이에요."], ["What is to become of that?—Very true.", "가엾은 제인이 방금도 그 이야기를 하더라고요.—'너는 가야 해,' 하고 그 애가 말했지요."], ["Poor dear Jane was talking of it just now.—‘You must go,’ said she.", "'너와 나는 헤어져야 해. 너는 여기 있을 까닭이 없으니까.—그래도 그냥 두자,'"], ["‘You and I must part.", "하고 그 애가 말했어요."], ["You will have no business here.—Let it stay, however,’ said she; ‘give it houseroom till Colonel Campbell comes back.", "'캠벨 대령이 돌아오실 때까지 둘 자리를 마련해 두자. 그분께 이 일을 말씀드릴 거야."], ["I shall talk about it to him; he will settle for me; he will help me out of all my difficulties.’—And to this day, I do believe, she knows not whether it was his present or his daughter’s.”", "그분이 나 대신 정해 주실 테니까. 그분이 내 모든 어려움을 풀어 주실 거야.'—그리고 오늘날까지도, 정말이지 저는, 그 애가 그게 그분의 선물이었는지 그분 따님의 선물이었는지조차 모르고 있다고 믿어요.\""]], [["Now Emma was obliged to think of the pianoforte; and the remembrance of all her former fanciful and unfair conjectures was so little pleasing, that she soon allowed herself to believe her visit had been long enough; and, with a repetition of every thing that she could venture to say of the good wishes which she really felt, took leave.", "이제 엠마는 그 피아노포르테를 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 지난날 자신이 멋대로 그려 댄 온갖 공상적이고 부당한 억측을 떠올리니 그것이 너무도 마뜩잖아, 곧 자신의 방문이 충분히 길었다고 여기기로 했다. 그리하여, 진심으로 느끼는 좋은 바람을 두고 차마 입에 올릴 수 있는 말은 모조리 되풀이한 뒤, 작별을 고했다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter IX", "# 제3권 9장"]], [["Emma’s pensive meditations, as she walked home, were not interrupted; but on entering the parlour, she found those who must rouse her.", "엠마는 집으로 걸어 돌아오는 동안, 생각에 잠긴 명상을 방해받지 않았다. 그러나 거실에 들어서자, 자기를 일깨워 줄 사람들이 와 있었다."], ["Mr. Knightley and Harriet had arrived during her absence, and were sitting with her father.—Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리와 해리엇이 그녀가 없는 사이에 와서 아버지와 함께 앉아 있었던 것이다."], ["Knightley immediately got up, and in a manner decidedly graver than usual, said,", "미스터 나이틀리는 곧장 일어서더니, 평소보다 확연히 무거운 태도로 말했다."]], [["“I would not go away without seeing you, but I have no time to spare, and therefore must now be gone directly.", "\"자네를 보지 않고는 떠날 수 없어서 말이지. 하지만 시간이 없으니 지금 곧장 가 봐야겠네."], ["I am going to London, to spend a few days with John and Isabella.", "런던으로 가서 존과 이저벨라하고 며칠 지내고 올 참이야."], ["Have you any thing to send or say, besides the ‘love,’ which nobody carries?”", "부칠 물건이나 전할 말이 있나 — 아무도 챙겨 가지 않는 '사랑한다'는 인사 말고 말일세.\""]], [["“Nothing at all.", "\"전혀요."], ["But is not this a sudden scheme?”", "그런데 이거 너무 갑작스러운 계획 아니에요?\""]], [["“Yes—rather—I have been thinking of it some little time.”", "\"그렇지 — 좀 그런 편이지 — 얼마 전부터 생각은 하고 있었네.\""]], [["Emma was sure he had not forgiven her; he looked unlike himself.", "엠마는 그가 자기를 용서하지 않았다고 확신했다. 그는 평소의 그답지 않아 보였다."], ["Time, however, she thought, would tell him that they ought to be friends again.", "그러나 시간이 흐르면 둘이 다시 벗이 되어야 한다는 것을 그에게 일러 주리라고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["While he stood, as if meaning to go, but not going—her father began his inquiries.", "그가 떠나려는 듯하면서도 떠나지 않고 서 있는 동안, 아버지가 묻기 시작했다."]], [["“Well, my dear, and did you get there safely?—And how did you find my worthy old friend and her daughter?—I dare say they must have been very much obliged to you for coming.", "\"그래, 얘야, 무사히 다녀왔느냐? 그 갸륵한 옛 벗과 그 따님은 어떻더냐?"], ["Dear Emma has been to call on Mrs.", "분명 네가 찾아간 걸 무척 고마워했겠지."], ["and Miss Bates, Mr. Knightley, as I told you before.", "미스터 나이틀리, 우리 엠마가 베이츠 부인와 미스 베이츠를 찾아뵙고 온 길이라오."], ["She is always so attentive to them!”", "아까도 말씀드렸지만. 그 애는 늘 그분들한테 그렇게 살뜰하다오!\""]], [["Emma’s colour was heightened by this unjust praise; and with a smile, and shake of the head, which spoke much, she looked at Mr. Knightley.—It seemed as if there were an instantaneous impression in her favour, as if his eyes received the truth from hers, and all that had passed of good in her feelings were at once caught and honoured.— He looked at her with a glow of regard. She was warmly gratified—and in another moment still more so, by a little movement of more than common friendliness on his part.—He took her hand;—whether she had not herself made the first motion, she could not say—she might, perhaps, have rather offered it—but he took her hand, pressed it, and certainly was on the point of carrying it to his lips—when, from some fancy or other, he suddenly let it go.—Why he should feel such a scruple, why he should change his mind when it was all but done, she could not perceive.—He would have judged better, she thought, if he had not stopped.—The intention, however, was indubitable; and whether it was that his manners had in general so little gallantry, or however else it happened, but she thought nothing became him more.—It was with him, of so simple, yet so dignified a nature.—She could not but recall the attempt with great satisfaction. It spoke such perfect amity.—He left them immediately afterwards—gone in a moment. He always moved with the alertness of a mind which could neither be undecided nor dilatory, but now he seemed more sudden than usual in his disappearance.", "이 부당한 칭찬에 엠마의 얼굴이 붉어졌다. 그리고 많은 것을 담은 미소와 고갯짓으로, 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리를 바라보았다. 순간 그녀에게 유리한 인상이 곧바로 전해진 듯했다. 그의 눈이 그녀의 눈에서 진실을 받아들이고, 그녀의 마음속에 오간 모든 선한 것이 단번에 포착되어 인정받은 듯했다. 그는 따뜻한 존경의 빛을 띠고 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀는 마음 깊이 흐뭇했고 — 다음 순간, 그가 보인 예사롭지 않은 다정함의 작은 몸짓에 한층 더 흐뭇해졌다. 그가 그녀의 손을 잡았다. 자기가 먼저 손을 내민 것은 아닌지 그녀로서는 알 수 없었다 — 어쩌면 자기가 먼저 손을 내밀었는지도 몰랐다 — 그러나 그는 그녀의 손을 잡고, 꼭 쥐었으며, 분명 그 손을 입술로 가져가려는 찰나였다 — 그런데 무슨 변덕이 일었는지, 그는 돌연 손을 놓아 버렸다. 어째서 그런 거리낌을 느꼈는지, 거의 다 한 일을 어째서 마음을 바꿨는지, 그녀는 헤아릴 수 없었다. 멈추지 않았더라면 더 나은 판단이었으리라고 그녀는 생각했다. 그러나 그 의도만은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그리고 그의 태도가 워낙 그런 정중한 호의와는 거리가 멀어서였는지, 아니면 다른 무슨 까닭에서였는지, 그녀는 그 모습이 그에게 더없이 잘 어울린다고 생각했다. 그에게는 그토록 꾸밈없으면서도 그토록 기품 있는 무엇이 있었다. 그녀는 그 시도를 큰 만족과 함께 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 그것은 더없이 온전한 우정을 말해 주었다. 그는 그 직후 그들을 떠났다 — 순식간에 가 버렸다. 그는 늘 우유부단하지도 굼뜨지도 않은 정신의 민첩함으로 움직였지만, 이번에는 평소보다 더 돌연히 사라지는 듯했다."]], [["Emma could not regret her having gone to Miss Bates, but she wished she had left her ten minutes earlier;—it would have been a great pleasure to talk over Jane Fairfax’s situation with Mr. Knightley.—Neither would she regret that he should be going to Brunswick Square, for she knew how much his visit would be enjoyed—but it might have happened at a better time—and to have had longer notice of it, would have been pleasanter.—They parted thorough friends, however; she could not be deceived as to the meaning of his countenance, and his unfinished gallantry;—it was all done to assure her that she had fully recovered his good opinion.—He had been sitting with them half an hour, she found. It was a pity that she had not come back earlier!", "엠마는 미스 베이츠를 찾아간 것을 후회할 수는 없었으나, 십 분만 더 일찍 그 집을 나섰더라면 싶었다. 제인 페어팩스의 처지에 관해 미스터 나이틀리와 이야기 나눌 수 있었다면 큰 즐거움이었을 텐데. 그가 브런즈윅 스퀘어로 간다는 것도 후회할 일은 아니었으니, 그의 방문이 얼마나 큰 기쁨이 될지 알았기 때문이다 — 그래도 더 좋은 때에 가게 되었더라면, 그리고 좀 더 일찍 알려 주었더라면 더 반가웠을 텐데. 그러나 두 사람은 더없이 온전한 벗으로서 헤어졌다. 그의 표정이 담은 뜻과 끝맺지 못한 그 정중한 호의를 두고서는 잘못 알 리가 없었다 — 그 모두가 그녀가 그의 호의를 온전히 되찾았음을 일러 주려는 것이었다. 알고 보니 그는 반 시간이나 그들과 앉아 있었던 것이다. 좀 더 일찍 돌아오지 못한 것이 아쉬웠다!"]], [["In the hope of diverting her father’s thoughts from the disagreeableness of Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리가 런던으로 간다는 것, 그것도 그렇게 갑작스레, 게다가 말을 타고 간다는 것 — 이 모두가 무척 못마땅하리라는 것을 알았기에, 아버지의 생각을 그 불쾌함에서 돌리려는 마음에, 엠마는 제인 페어팩스 소식을 전했다."], ["Knightley’s going to London; and going so suddenly; and going on horseback, which she knew would be all very bad; Emma communicated her news of Jane Fairfax, and her dependence on the effect was justified; it supplied a very useful check,—interested, without disturbing him.", "그리고 그 효과에 건 기대는 어긋나지 않았다. 그것은 매우 쓸모 있는 제동이 되었다 — 아버지의 관심을 끌면서도 마음을 흔들지는 않았으니."], ["He had long made up his mind to Jane Fairfax’s going out as governess, and could talk of it cheerfully, but Mr. Knightley’s going to London had been an unexpected blow.", "그는 제인 페어팩스가 가정교사로 나간다는 데에는 오래전부터 마음을 정해 둔 터라 명랑하게 이야기할 수 있었으나, 미스터 나이틀리가 런던으로 간다는 것은 뜻밖의 타격이었다."]], [["“I am very glad, indeed, my dear, to hear she is to be so comfortably settled.", "\"그 사람이 그렇게 편안하게 자리를 잡게 된다니 참 다행이구나, 얘야. 정말 다행이야."], ["Mrs. Elton is very good-natured and agreeable, and I dare say her acquaintance are just what they ought to be.", "엘턴 부인은 마음씨 좋고 상냥한 사람이고, 분명 그 댁 지인들도 마땅히 그래야 할 만한 사람들일 게다."], ["I hope it is a dry situation, and that her health will be taken good care of.", "부디 습하지 않은 곳이고, 그 애의 건강을 잘 보살펴 주면 좋으련만."], ["It ought to be a first object, as I am sure poor Miss Taylor’s always was with me.", "그게 무엇보다 먼저여야지. 가엾은 미스 테일러가 나한테 늘 그래 주었던 것처럼 말이다."], ["You know, my dear, she is going to be to this new lady what Miss Taylor was to us.", "알다시피 얘야, 그 애는 그 새 안주인한테 미스 테일러가 우리한테 그래 주었던 것 같은 사람이 되는 거란다."], ["And I hope she will be better off in one respect, and not be induced to go away after it has been her home so long.”", "그리고 한 가지 점에서는 더 나았으면 좋겠구나 — 그렇게 오래 제집처럼 지내다가 결국 떠나는 일은 없도록 말이다.\""]], [["The following day brought news from Richmond to throw every thing else into the background.", "이튿날에는 리치먼드에서 다른 모든 일을 뒷전으로 밀어내는 소식이 왔다."], ["An express arrived at Randalls to announce the death of Mrs.", "처칠 부인의 부음을 알리는 급보가 랜덜스에 도착한 것이다!"], ["Churchill! Though her nephew had had no particular reason to hasten back on her account, she had not lived above six-and-thirty hours after his return.", "조카가 그녀 때문에 서둘러 돌아가야 할 특별한 이유는 없었건만, 그가 돌아간 뒤 그녀는 서른여섯 시간을 채 넘기지 못하고 세상을 떠났다."], ["A sudden seizure of a different nature from any thing foreboded by her general state, had carried her off after a short struggle.", "평소의 상태로는 짐작조차 못 했던 종류의 갑작스러운 발작이, 짧은 사투 끝에 그녀의 목숨을 앗아 간 것이다."], ["The great Mrs. Churchill was no more.", "그 위세 등등하던 처칠 부인이 이제 이 세상 사람이 아니었다."]], [["It was felt as such things must be felt.", "그런 일이 으레 그러하듯 그렇게 받아들여졌다. 누구나 얼마간 엄숙하고 슬픈 표정을 지었다."], ["Every body had a degree of gravity and sorrow; tenderness towards the departed, solicitude for the surviving friends; and, in a reasonable time, curiosity to know where she would be buried.", "고인을 향한 애틋함, 남은 벗들에 대한 염려가 있었고 — 그리고 적당한 시간이 지나자, 그녀가 어디에 묻힐지에 대한 호기심도 일었다. 골드스미스는 우리에게 일러 준다."], ["Goldsmith tells us, that when lovely woman stoops to folly, she has nothing to do but to die; and when she stoops to be disagreeable, it is equally to be recommended as a clearer of ill-fame.", "사랑스러운 여인이 어리석음으로 빠져들 때, 그녀가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 죽는 것뿐이라고. 그리고 못마땅한 사람이 되는 쪽으로 빠져들 때에도, 죽음은 똑같이 권할 만한 일이니, 나쁜 평판을 씻어 내는 묘책이기 때문이다."], ["Mrs. Churchill, after being disliked at least twenty-five years, was now spoken of with compassionate allowances.", "적어도 스물다섯 해 동안 미움받아 온 처칠 부인은, 이제 측은해하는 너그러움 속에서 입에 오르내렸다."], ["In one point she was fully justified.", "한 가지 점에서는 그녀가 온전히 옳았음이 밝혀졌다."], ["She had never been admitted before to be seriously ill.", "그녀가 정말로 중병을 앓고 있다고는 여태 아무도 인정해 준 적이 없었던 것이다. 이 사건이, 꾸며 낸 병이라는 온갖 변덕과 이기심의 혐의에서 그녀를 벗겨 주었다."], ["The event acquitted her of all the fancifulness, and all the selfishness of imaginary complaints.", "* 골드스미스: 올리버 골드스미스의 소설 「웨이크필드의 목사」 속 노래 구절을 비튼 것."]], [["“Poor Mrs. Churchill!", "\"가엾은 처칠 부인!"], ["no doubt she had been suffering a great deal: more than any body had ever supposed—and continual pain would try the temper.", "분명 무척 고생했을 거야. 누가 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 더 — 끊임없는 고통이 사람의 성미를 시험했겠지."], ["It was a sad event—a great shock—with all her faults, what would Mr. Churchill do without her?", "슬픈 일이야 — 크나큰 충격이고 — 온갖 결점에도 불구하고, 미스터 처칠가 그 사람 없이 어찌 지낸단 말인가?"], ["Mr. Churchill’s loss would be dreadful indeed.", "미스터 처칠의 상실은 정말이지 끔찍할 거야."], ["Mr. Churchill would never get over it.”—Even Mr.", "미스터 처칠는 결코 이겨 내지 못할 거야.\""], ["Weston shook his head, and looked solemn, and said, “Ah!", "미스터 웨스턴조차 고개를 가로젓고 엄숙한 얼굴로 \"아!"], ["poor woman, who would have thought it!”", "가엾은 여인, 누가 이럴 줄 알았겠나!\""], ["and resolved, that his mourning should be as handsome as possible; and his wife sat sighing and moralising over her broad hems with a commiseration and good sense, true and steady.", "하고는, 상복만은 되도록 훌륭하게 갖추기로 마음먹었다. 그의 아내는 자기가 넓게 감치고 있던 옷단을 앞에 두고 앉아, 참되고 한결같은 연민과 분별로 한숨지으며 사람의 도리를 곱씹었다."], ["How it would affect Frank was among the earliest thoughts of both.", "이 일이 프랭크에게 어떤 영향을 줄까 하는 것이 두 사람의 가장 이른 생각 중 하나였다."], ["It was also a very early speculation with Emma.", "엠마에게도 그것은 매우 이른 추측이었다."], ["The character of Mrs. Churchill, the grief of her husband—her mind glanced over them both with awe and compassion—and then rested with lightened feelings on how Frank might be affected by the event, how benefited, how freed.", "처칠 부인의 성격, 남편의 슬픔 — 그녀의 마음은 외경과 연민으로 그 둘을 훑고 지나가더니 — 이내 한결 가벼워진 마음으로, 이 사건이 프랭크에게 어떤 영향을 줄지, 얼마나 득이 될지, 얼마나 자유롭게 해 줄지에 머물렀다."], ["She saw in a moment all the possible good.", "그녀는 일어날 법한 온갖 좋은 일을 단번에 떠올렸다."], ["Now, an attachment to Harriet Smith would have nothing to encounter. Mr.", "이제 해리엇 스미스에게 품는 연정은 거칠 것이 하나도 없게 되었다."], ["Churchill, independent of his wife, was feared by nobody; an easy, guidable man, to be persuaded into any thing by his nephew.", "아내가 없는 미스터 처칠을 두려워하는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 느긋하고 이끌기 쉬운 사람이라, 조카가 무슨 일이든 설득해 낼 수 있었다."], ["All that remained to be wished was, that the nephew should form the attachment, as, with all her goodwill in the cause, Emma could feel no certainty of its being already formed.", "이제 바랄 것이라곤 그 조카가 정말로 그 연정을 품는 일뿐이었으니, 그 일에 온갖 호의를 품은 엠마로서도 그것이 이미 싹텄다고는 도무지 확신할 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["Harriet behaved extremely well on the occasion, with great self-command.", "해리엇은 이 일에 매우 훌륭하게, 대단한 자제력을 보이며 처신했다."], ["What ever she might feel of brighter hope, she betrayed nothing.", "한층 밝아진 희망을 속으로 어떻게 느꼈든, 그녀는 아무것도 드러내지 않았다."], ["Emma was gratified, to observe such a proof in her of strengthened character, and refrained from any allusion that might endanger its maintenance.", "엠마는 그녀에게서 한층 단단해진 성품의 증거를 보고 흐뭇해, 그 단단함을 위태롭게 할 만한 어떤 암시도 삼갔다."], ["They spoke, therefore, of Mrs. Churchill’s death with mutual forbearance.", "그리하여 두 사람은 처칠 부인의 죽음을 서로 자제하며 이야기했다."]], [["Short letters from Frank were received at Randalls, communicating all that was immediately important of their state and plans. Mr.", "랜덜스에는 프랭크에게서 짧은 편지들이 도착해, 그들의 형편과 계획 가운데 당장 중요한 것들을 모두 전했다."], ["Churchill was better than could be expected; and their first removal, on the departure of the funeral for Yorkshire, was to be to the house of a very old friend in Windsor, to whom Mr.", "미스터 처칠는 예상보다 잘 견디고 있었다. 장례 행렬이 요크셔로 떠난 뒤 그들이 처음 옮겨 갈 곳은 윈저에 사는 아주 오랜 벗의 집으로, 미스터 처칠가 지난 십 년 동안 찾아가겠노라 약속해 온 사람이었다."], ["Churchill had been promising a visit the last ten years.", "지금으로서는 해리엇을 위해 할 수 있는 일이 아무것도 없었다."], ["At present, there was nothing to be done for Harriet; good wishes for the future were all that could yet be possible on Emma’s side.", "엠마 쪽에서 할 수 있는 것이라곤 앞날을 위한 좋은 기원뿐이었다."]], [["It was a more pressing concern to shew attention to Jane Fairfax, whose prospects were closing, while Harriet’s opened, and whose engagements now allowed of no delay in any one at Highbury, who wished to shew her kindness—and with Emma it was grown into a first wish.", "더 다급한 일은 제인 페어팩스에게 마음을 써 주는 것이었다. 해리엇의 앞길이 열리는 동안 그녀의 앞길은 닫히고 있었고, 그녀와 맺어진 약속이 코앞에 닥쳐, 친절을 베풀고 싶어 하는 하이베리의 누구든 이제 더는 미룰 수 없게 되었던 것이다 — 그리고 엠마에게 그것은 가장 우선되는 바람으로까지 자라 있었다."], ["She had scarcely a stronger regret than for her past coldness; and the person, whom she had been so many months neglecting, was now the very one on whom she would have lavished every distinction of regard or sympathy.", "그녀에게는 지난날 자신의 냉담함만큼 큰 후회가 없을 정도였다. 그토록 여러 달 동안 소홀히 해 온 바로 그 사람이, 이제는 온갖 각별한 존중과 동정을 아낌없이 쏟아붓고 싶은 바로 그 사람이 된 것이다."], ["She wanted to be of use to her; wanted to shew a value for her society, and testify respect and consideration.", "그녀는 그에게 도움이 되고 싶었다. 그녀와의 교제를 소중히 여긴다는 것을 보여 주고, 존경과 배려를 증명하고 싶었다."], ["She resolved to prevail on her to spend a day at Hartfield.", "그녀는 제인을 하트필드에서 하루 지내도록 설득하기로 마음먹었다."], ["A note was written to urge it.", "그것을 권하는 쪽지가 쓰였다."], ["The invitation was refused, and by a verbal message.", "그러나 초대는 거절당했고, 그것도 말로 전해 온 전갈로였다."], ["“Miss Fairfax was not well enough to write;” and when Mr.", "\"미스 페어팩스는 글을 쓸 만큼 몸이 좋지 못하다는 것\"이었다."], ["Perry called at Hartfield, the same morning, it appeared that she was so much indisposed as to have been visited, though against her own consent, by himself, and that she was suffering under severe headaches, and a nervous fever to a degree, which made him doubt the possibility of her going to Mrs. Smallridge’s at the time proposed.", "그리고 같은 날 아침 미스터 페리가 하트필드에 들렀을 때 드러난 바로는, 그녀는 본인의 뜻과는 달리 그가 왕진을 갈 정도로 몹시 편찮았으며, 심한 두통과 신경성 열에 시달리고 있어, 미스터 페리는 그녀가 예정된 시기에 스몰리지 부인 댁으로 갈 수 있을지조차 의심스러워했다."], ["Her health seemed for the moment completely deranged—appetite quite gone—and though there were no absolutely alarming symptoms, nothing touching the pulmonary complaint, which was the standing apprehension of the family, Mr. Perry was uneasy about her.", "그녀의 건강은 지금으로서는 완전히 무너진 듯했다 — 입맛도 아주 가셨다 — 그리고 절대적으로 위험한 증상은 없었고, 그 집안의 한결같은 걱정거리인 폐 질환을 건드릴 만한 것도 없었지만, 미스터 페리는 그녀가 마음에 걸렸다."], ["He thought she had undertaken more than she was equal to, and that she felt it so herself, though she would not own it.", "그는 그녀가 제 감당을 넘는 일을 떠맡았으며, 본인도 그것을 그렇게 느끼면서도 인정하려 들지 않는다고 생각했다."], ["Her spirits seemed overcome.", "그녀의 기운은 짓눌린 듯했다."], ["Her present home, he could not but observe, was unfavourable to a nervous disorder:—confined always to one room;—he could have wished it otherwise—and her good aunt, though his very old friend, he must acknowledge to be not the best companion for an invalid of that description.", "그가 보지 않을 수 없었던 것은, 지금 그녀가 사는 집이 신경성 질환에는 좋지 않다는 점이었다 — 늘 방 하나에 갇혀 지내야 했으니 — 그는 그렇지 않기를 바랐다 — 그리고 그녀의 착한 이모로 말하자면, 자신의 아주 오랜 벗이긴 하나, 그런 부류의 환자에게 가장 좋은 말동무는 못 된다고 인정할 수밖에 없었다."], ["Her care and attention could not be questioned; they were, in fact, only too great.", "그녀의 보살핌과 정성이야 의심할 바 없었다 — 사실 너무 지나칠 정도였다."], ["He very much feared that Miss Fairfax derived more evil than good from them.", "그는 미스 페어팩스가 그 보살핌에서 득보다 해를 더 입지 않을까 몹시 염려했다."], ["Emma listened with the warmest concern; grieved for her more and more, and looked around eager to discover some way of being useful.", "엠마는 더없이 따뜻한 관심으로 귀를 기울였다. 그녀가 점점 더 가엾어, 무언가 도움이 될 길을 찾으려 사방을 둘러보았다."], ["To take her—be it only an hour or two—from her aunt, to give her change of air and scene, and quiet rational conversation, even for an hour or two, might do her good; and the following morning she wrote again to say, in the most feeling language she could command, that she would call for her in the carriage at any hour that Jane would name—mentioning that she had Mr.", "단 한두 시간만이라도 그녀를 이모에게서 떼어 내어, 바람과 풍경을 바꿔 주고, 조용하고 이치에 닿는 대화를 나누어 준다면 — 한두 시간만이라도 — 그녀에게 도움이 될지 몰랐다. 그래서 이튿날 아침, 엠마는 다시 편지를 써서, 그녀가 부릴 수 있는 가장 정성 어린 말로, 제인이 시간만 정해 준다면 어느 때든 마차로 데리러 가겠노라고 전했다 — 그런 운동이 환자에게 좋다는 미스터 페리의 단호한 소견이 있다는 말도 곁들여서."], ["Perry’s decided opinion, in favour of such exercise for his patient. The answer was only in this short note:", "답장은 이 짧은 쪽지뿐이었다."]], [["“Miss Fairfax’s compliments and thanks, but is quite unequal to any exercise.”", "\"미스 페어팩스가 인사와 감사를 전합니다. 그러나 어떤 운동도 도무지 감당할 수 없는 형편입니다.\""]], [["Emma felt that her own note had deserved something better; but it was impossible to quarrel with words, whose tremulous inequality shewed indisposition so plainly, and she thought only of how she might best counteract this unwillingness to be seen or assisted.", "엠마는 자기 편지가 그보다 나은 답을 받을 만했다고 느꼈다. 그러나 떨리며 들쭉날쭉한 글씨가 몸이 좋지 않음을 그토록 또렷이 드러내는 그 글을 두고 다툴 수는 없었다."], ["In spite of the answer, therefore, she ordered the carriage, and drove to Mrs.", "그저 어떻게 하면 남에게 보이거나 도움받기를 꺼리는 이 마음을 가장 잘 누그러뜨릴 수 있을까만 생각했다."], ["Bates’s, in the hope that Jane would be induced to join her—but it would not do;—Miss Bates came to the carriage door, all gratitude, and agreeing with her most earnestly in thinking an airing might be of the greatest service—and every thing that message could do was tried—but all in vain.", "그리하여 그 답장에도 불구하고, 엠마는 마차를 대령시켜 베이츠 부인 댁으로 가서, 제인이 함께 나서도록 설득되기를 바랐다 — 그러나 소용없었다. 미스 베이츠가 마차 문간으로 나와, 온통 감사를 늘어놓으며, 바람을 쐬는 것이 더없이 큰 도움이 되리라는 생각에 더없이 간곡히 맞장구를 쳤다 — 그리고 전갈로 할 수 있는 일은 다 해 보았다 — 그러나 모두 헛일이었다."], ["Miss Bates was obliged to return without success; Jane was quite unpersuadable; the mere proposal of going out seemed to make her worse.—Emma wished she could have seen her, and tried her own powers; but, almost before she could hint the wish, Miss Bates made it appear that she had promised her niece on no account to let Miss Woodhouse in.", "미스 베이츠는 성과 없이 돌아올 수밖에 없었다. 제인은 도무지 설득되지 않았다."], ["“Indeed, the truth was, that poor dear Jane could not bear to see any body—any body at all—Mrs.", "외출하자는 말만 꺼내도 도리어 더 안 좋아지는 듯했다. 엠마는 자기가 직접 만나 제 솜씨를 시험해 볼 수 있었으면 했다."], ["Elton, indeed, could not be denied—and Mrs. Cole had made such a point—and Mrs.", "그러나 미처 그 바람을 내비치기도 전에, 미스 베이츠는 무슨 일이 있어도 미스 우드하우스를 들이지 않겠다고 조카에게 약속한 처지임을 드러냈다."], ["Perry had said so much—but, except them, Jane would really see nobody.”", "\"실은, 사정인즉슨, 가엾은 제인이 누구든 만나는 것을 견디지 못한다는 거예요 — 그 누구든 말이죠 — 엘턴 부인이야 차마 거절할 수 없었고 — 콜 부인이 어찌나 고집을 부리셨는지 — 페리 부인도 어찌나 말씀이 많으셨는지 — 그래도 그분들만 빼면, 제인은 정말이지 아무도 만나려 하지 않아요.\""]], [["Emma did not want to be classed with the Mrs. Eltons, the Mrs.", "엠마는 어디든 막무가내로 밀고 들어가는 엘턴 부인이나 페리 부인이나 콜 부인 같은 부류에 끼고 싶지 않았다."], ["Perrys, and the Mrs. Coles, who would force themselves anywhere; neither could she feel any right of preference herself—she submitted, therefore, and only questioned Miss Bates farther as to her niece’s appetite and diet, which she longed to be able to assist.", "그렇다고 자신에게 우선권 같은 것이 있다고 느낄 수도 없었다 — 그래서 그녀는 물러섰고, 다만 그토록 거들고 싶어 하던 조카의 입맛과 식사에 관해서만 미스 베이츠에게 더 물어보았다."], ["On that subject poor Miss Bates was very unhappy, and very communicative; Jane would hardly eat any thing:—Mr.", "그 화제에 대해서는 가엾은 미스 베이츠가 무척 속상해하며, 또 무척 말이 많았다. 제인은 거의 아무것도 먹으려 들지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["Perry recommended nourishing food; but every thing they could command (and never had any body such good neighbours) was distasteful.", "미스터 페리는 자양분 있는 음식을 권했지만, 그들이 마련할 수 있는 것이라면 무엇이든(또 누구도 이렇게 좋은 이웃을 둔 적이 없었건만) 그녀는 입에 대기 싫어한다는 것이었다."]], [["Emma, on reaching home, called the housekeeper directly, to an examination of her stores; and some arrowroot of very superior quality was speedily despatched to Miss Bates with a most friendly note. In half an hour the arrowroot was returned, with a thousand thanks from Miss Bates, but “dear Jane would not be satisfied without its being sent back; it was a thing she could not take—and, moreover, she insisted on her saying, that she was not at all in want of any thing.”", "엠마는 집에 닿자 곧장 가정부를 불러 곳간을 살펴보게 했다. 그리고 아주 질 좋은 칡녹말을 더없이 다정한 쪽지와 함께 서둘러 미스 베이츠에게 보냈다. 반 시간 만에 그 칡녹말은 미스 베이츠의 천 마디 감사와 함께 되돌아왔다. \"그러나 사랑스러운 제인이 한사코 돌려보내야겠다고 하니 어쩔 수가 없었어요. 그 애가 도무지 먹을 수 없는 것이라서요 — 게다가, 자기는 무엇이 됐든 조금도 부족한 게 없다고 꼭 전해 달라며 우기는 거예요.\""]], [["When Emma afterwards heard that Jane Fairfax had been seen wandering about the meadows, at some distance from Highbury, on the afternoon of the very day on which she had, under the plea of being unequal to any exercise, so peremptorily refused to go out with her in the carriage, she could have no doubt—putting every thing together—that Jane was resolved to receive no kindness from her.", "훗날 엠마는, 어떤 운동도 감당할 수 없다는 핑계로 그렇게 단호히 마차 동행을 거절한 바로 그날 오후, 제인 페어팩스가 하이베리에서 좀 떨어진 풀밭을 거니는 모습이 목격되었다는 말을 들었다. 그러자 그녀는 — 모든 일을 한데 맞추어 보고는 — 제인이 자기에게서는 어떤 친절도 받지 않기로 작정했다는 것을 의심할 수 없었다."], ["She was sorry, very sorry.", "그녀는 안타까웠다, 무척 안타까웠다."], ["Her heart was grieved for a state which seemed but the more pitiable from this sort of irritation of spirits, inconsistency of action, and inequality of powers; and it mortified her that she was given so little credit for proper feeling, or esteemed so little worthy as a friend: but she had the consolation of knowing that her intentions were good, and of being able to say to herself, that could Mr.", "이런 신경의 짜증과, 행동의 모순과, 들쭉날쭉한 기력 때문에 도리어 더 가엾어 보이는 그 처지에, 그녀의 마음은 아렸다. 그리고 자기가 마땅한 마음씨를 그토록 인정받지 못하고, 벗으로서 그토록 하찮게 여겨진다는 것이 분했다."], ["Knightley have been privy to all her attempts of assisting Jane Fairfax, could he even have seen into her heart, he would not, on this occasion, have found any thing to reprove.", "그러나 그녀에게는 자기 의도가 선했음을 안다는 위안과, 미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스를 도우려 한 자신의 온갖 시도를 다 알았더라면, 아니 그녀의 마음속까지 들여다볼 수 있었더라면, 이번만큼은 나무랄 것을 하나도 찾지 못했으리라고 스스로에게 말할 수 있는 위안이 있었다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter X", "# 제3권 10장"]], [["One morning, about ten days after Mrs. Churchill’s decease, Emma was called downstairs to Mr.", "처칠 부인이 세상을 떠난 지 열흘쯤 지난 어느 날 아침, 엠마는 미스터 웨스턴이 찾아왔다는 전갈을 받고 아래층으로 불려 내려갔다."], ["Weston, who “could not stay five minutes, and wanted particularly to speak with her.”—He met her at the parlour-door, and hardly asking her how she did, in the natural key of his voice, sunk it immediately, to say, unheard by her father,", "그는 \"오 분도 머물 수 없으나 긴히 그녀와 할 말이 있다\"고 했다. 그는 거실 문간에서 그녀를 맞았고, 평소의 음성으로 안부조차 변변히 묻는 둥 마는 둥 하더니 이내 목소리를 낮추어, 그녀의 아버지에게 들리지 않게 말했다."]], [["“Can you come to Randalls at any time this morning?—Do, if it be possible.", "\"오늘 아침 아무 때라도 랜덜스에 와 줄 수 있겠소? 부디 그래 주오, 될 수 있다면."], ["Mrs. Weston wants to see you.", "웨스턴 부인이 당신을 보고 싶어 하오."], ["She must see you.”", "꼭 만나야 한다는구려.\""]], [["“Is she unwell?”", "\"편찮으신가요?\""]], [["“No, no, not at all—only a little agitated. She would have ordered the carriage, and come to you, but she must see you alone, and that you know—(nodding towards her father)—Humph!—Can you come?”", "\"아니, 아니, 전혀. 그저 조금 마음이 어지러울 뿐이오. 마차를 불러 당신에게 가려 했지만, 당신을 단둘이 만나야 해서 말이오. 그러니 알다시피—(아버지 쪽을 향해 고갯짓하며)—흠! 와 줄 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Certainly.", "\"물론이죠."], ["This moment, if you please.", "원하신다면 지금 당장이라도요."], ["It is impossible to refuse what you ask in such a way.", "그렇게 부탁하시는데 어떻게 거절하겠어요."], ["But what can be the matter?—Is she really not ill?”", "그런데 대체 무슨 일이죠? 정말 편찮으신 게 아니에요?\""]], [["“Depend upon me—but ask no more questions.", "\"날 믿으시오. 다만 더는 묻지 마오."], ["You will know it all in time.", "때가 되면 다 알게 될 거요."], ["The most unaccountable business!", "도무지 알 수 없는 일이지!"], ["But hush, hush!”", "하지만 쉿, 쉿!\""]], [["To guess what all this meant, was impossible even for Emma.", "이 모든 게 무슨 뜻인지 짐작하는 일은 엠마로서도 불가능했다."], ["Something really important seemed announced by his looks; but, as her friend was well, she endeavoured not to be uneasy, and settling it with her father, that she would take her walk now, she and Mr. Weston were soon out of the house together and on their way at a quick pace for Randalls.", "그의 표정으로 보아 정말로 중대한 무언가가 닥친 듯했지만, 벗이 무탈하다 하니 그녀는 애써 불안해하지 않기로 하고, 아버지에게 지금 산책을 나가겠노라 일러둔 뒤, 곧 미스터 웨스턴과 함께 집을 나서 빠른 걸음으로 랜덜스를 향해 길을 잡았다."]], [["“Now,”—said Emma, when they were fairly beyond the sweep gates,—“now Mr.", "\"자,\" 진입로 대문을 완전히 벗어나자 엠마가 말했다."], ["Weston, do let me know what has happened.”", "\"이제, 미스터 웨스턴, 무슨 일이 있었는지 제게 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“No, no,”—he gravely replied.—“Don’t ask me.", "\"아니, 아니.\" 그가 무겁게 답했다."], ["I promised my wife to leave it all to her.", "\"내게 묻지 마오. 모든 걸 아내에게 맡기겠다고 약속했소."], ["She will break it to you better than I can.", "나보다 그 사람이 당신에게 더 잘 전할 거요."], ["Do not be impatient, Emma; it will all come out too soon.”", "조급해하지 마오, 엠마. 어차피 너무 빨리 다 드러날 테니.\""]], [["“Break it to me,”", "\"제게 전한다고요?\""], ["cried Emma, standing still with terror.—“Good God!—Mr.", "엠마는 두려움에 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 외쳤다. \"맙소사!"], ["Weston, tell me at once.—Something has happened in Brunswick Square.", "미스터 웨스턴, 지금 당장 말씀해 주세요. 브런즈윅 스퀘어에 무슨 일이 생긴 거죠."], ["I know it has.", "분명히 그래요."], ["Tell me, I charge you tell me this moment what it is.”", "말씀해 주세요. 당장 그게 뭔지 말씀해 주시기를 간청해요.\""]], [["“No, indeed you are mistaken.”—", "\"아니, 정말이지 당신이 잘못 짚었소.\""]], [["“Mr.", "\"미스터 웨스턴, 저를 가지고 장난하지 마세요."], ["Weston do not trifle with me.—Consider how many of my dearest friends are now in Brunswick Square.", "제 가장 소중한 벗들이 지금 얼마나 많이 브런즈윅 스퀘어에 있는지 생각해 보세요."], ["Which of them is it?—I charge you by all that is sacred, not to attempt concealment.”", "그중 누구예요? 신성한 모든 것을 걸고 간청하니, 숨기려 들지 마세요.\""]], [["“Upon my word, Emma.”—", "\"맹세컨대, 엠마.\""]], [["“Your word!—why not your honour!—why not say upon your honour, that it has nothing to do with any of them? Good Heavens!—What can be to be broke to me, that does not relate to one of that family?”", "\"맹세라고요! 어째서 명예를 걸지 않으세요! 어째서 명예를 걸고, 그 일이 그들 중 누구와도 상관없다고 말씀하지 않으세요? 맙소사! 그 집안 사람과 관련되지 않았다면, 제게 전해야 할 일이 대체 뭐가 있겠어요?\""]], [["“Upon my honour,”", "\"명예를 걸고,\""], ["said he very seriously, “it does not.", "그가 아주 진지하게 말했다."], ["It is not in the smallest degree connected with any human being of the name of Knightley.”", "\"그 일은 그렇지 않소. 나이틀리라는 이름을 가진 그 누구와도 손톱만큼도 관련이 없소.\""]], [["Emma’s courage returned, and she walked on.", "엠마는 용기를 되찾아 다시 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["“I was wrong,” he continued, “in talking of its being broke to you. I should not have used the expression. In fact, it does not concern you—it concerns only myself,—that is, we hope.—Humph!—In short, my dear Emma, there is no occasion to be so uneasy about it. I don’t say that it is not a disagreeable business—but things might be much worse.—If we walk fast, we shall soon be at Randalls.”", "\"내가 잘못 말했소.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그 일을 당신에게 '전한다'고 한 게 잘못이었소. 그런 표현을 쓰지 말았어야 했는데. 사실 그건 당신과는 상관없는 일이오. 오로지 나 자신에게 관계된 일이지. 그러니까, 우리는 그렇게 바라고 있소. 흠! 한마디로, 엠마, 그렇게 마음 졸일 일은 아니오. 유쾌한 일이 아니라는 말은 아니지만, 사정이 훨씬 더 나쁠 수도 있었으니까. 빨리 걸으면 곧 랜덜스에 닿을 거요.\""]], [["Emma found that she must wait; and now it required little effort.", "엠마는 기다리는 수밖에 없음을 깨달았고, 이제는 그러는 데 별 노력이 들지도 않았다."], ["She asked no more questions therefore, merely employed her own fancy, and that soon pointed out to her the probability of its being some money concern—something just come to light, of a disagreeable nature in the circumstances of the family,—something which the late event at Richmond had brought forward.", "그래서 더는 묻지 않고 그저 제 상상에 몸을 맡겼는데, 그 상상은 곧 그녀에게, 어쩌면 그게 무슨 금전 문제—이 집안 사정에서 불쾌한 성질의 무언가가 막 드러난 것이고, 리치먼드에서 일어난 근래의 일이 그것을 들춰낸 것이리라는 짐작을 안겨 주었다."], ["Her fancy was very active.", "그녀의 상상은 무척이나 활발했다."], ["Half a dozen natural children, perhaps—and poor Frank cut off!—This, though very undesirable, would be no matter of agony to her.", "어쩌면 사생아가 대여섯이나 있어, 가엾은 프랭크가 상속에서 밀려난 것일지도! 이는 무척 달갑잖은 일일망정 그녀에게 고통이 되지는 않을 일이었다."], ["It inspired little more than an animating curiosity.", "그것은 활기를 띤 호기심 그 이상은 거의 불러일으키지 못했다."]], [["“Who is that gentleman on horseback?”", "\"말 탄 저 신사는 누구죠?\""], ["said she, as they proceeded—speaking more to assist Mr. Weston in keeping his secret, than with any other view.", "길을 가다가 그녀가 말했는데, 다른 뜻이 있어서라기보다는 미스터 웨스턴이 비밀을 지키는 일을 거들어 주려는 마음에서였다."]], [["“I do not know.—One of the Otways.—Not Frank;—it is not Frank, I assure you.", "\"모르겠소. 오트웨이네 사람 중 하나겠지."], ["You will not see him.", "프랭크는 아니오. 프랭크가 아니라는 건 장담하오."], ["He is half way to Windsor by this time.”", "당신은 그 애를 보지 못할 거요. 지금쯤이면 윈저로 가는 길 절반은 갔을 테니.\""]], [["“Has your son been with you, then?”", "\"그럼 아드님이 다녀갔나요?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오! 그렇소."], ["yes—did not you know?—Well, well, never mind.”", "몰랐소? 뭐, 됐소, 신경 쓰지 마오.\""]], [["For a moment he was silent; and then added, in a tone much more guarded and demure,", "그는 잠시 입을 다물었다가, 한결 조심스럽고 점잖은 어조로 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes, Frank came over this morning, just to ask us how we did.”", "\"그렇소, 프랭크가 오늘 아침 우리가 어찌 지내는지 안부나 물으려고 잠깐 다녀갔소.\""]], [["They hurried on, and were speedily at Randalls.—“Well, my dear,”", "두 사람은 서둘러 걸음을 재촉해 이내 랜덜스에 닿았다. \"자, 여보,\""], ["said he, as they entered the room—“I have brought her, and now I hope you will soon be better.", "방에 들어서며 그가 말했다. \"이 사람을 데려왔으니, 이제 당신도 곧 나아지면 좋겠구려."], ["I shall leave you together.", "두 사람만 있게 해 주리다. 늦출 까닭이 없지."], ["There is no use in delay.", "필요하면 부르오. 멀리 가 있지 않을 테니.\""], ["I shall not be far off, if you want me.”—And Emma distinctly heard him add, in a lower tone, before he quitted the room,—“I have been as good as my word.", "그리고 그가 방을 나서기 전, 한층 낮은 목소리로 덧붙이는 말을 엠마는 또렷이 들었다. \"내 약속을 어김없이 지켰소."], ["She has not the least idea.”", "이 사람은 눈곱만큼도 눈치채지 못했다오.\""]], [["Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인의 얼굴이 어찌나 좋지 않고 그 기색에 동요가 가득하던지, 엠마의 불안은 더욱 커졌다."], ["Weston was looking so ill, and had an air of so much perturbation, that Emma’s uneasiness increased; and the moment they were alone, she eagerly said,", "두 사람만 남게 된 순간 그녀가 다급하게 말했다."]], [["“What is it my dear friend?", "\"무슨 일이에요, 부인?"], ["Something of a very unpleasant nature, I find, has occurred;—do let me know directly what it is.", "무척 불쾌한 성질의 일이 일어났다는 건 알겠어요. 그게 뭔지 어서 말씀해 주세요."], ["I have been walking all this way in complete suspense.", "오는 내내 영문도 모른 채 마음 졸이며 걸어왔어요."], ["We both abhor suspense. Do not let mine continue longer.", "우리 둘 다 이런 마음 졸임을 질색하잖아요."], ["It will do you good to speak of your distress, whatever it may be.”", "제 마음 졸임을 더는 끌지 말아 주세요. 무슨 고민이든 털어놓으시면 마음이 한결 가벼워질 거예요.\""]], [["“Have you indeed no idea?”", "\"정말로 짐작 가는 게 없니?\""], ["said Mrs. Weston in a trembling voice.", "웨스턴 부인이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Cannot you, my dear Emma—cannot you form a guess as to what you are to hear?”", "\"엠마, 네가, 네가 무슨 이야기를 듣게 될지 도무지 짐작 못 하겠니?\""]], [["“So far as that it relates to Mr. Frank Churchill, I do guess.”", "\"미스터 프랭크 처칠과 관련된 일이라는 것까지는 짐작하겠어요.\""]], [["“You are right.", "\"맞아."], ["It does relate to him, and I will tell you directly;”", "그 사람과 관련된 일이야. 곧바로 말해 주마.\""], ["(resuming her work, and seeming resolved against looking up.) “He has been here this very morning, on a most extraordinary errand.", "(다시 바느질감을 들고는, 결코 고개를 들지 않으리라 작정한 듯한 모습이었다.) \"그 사람이 바로 오늘 아침 여기 왔었어. 더없이 뜻밖의 용건으로 말이지."], ["It is impossible to express our surprize.", "우리가 얼마나 놀랐는지 말로 다 할 수가 없구나."], ["He came to speak to his father on a subject,—to announce an attachment—”", "어떤 일을 두고 제 아버지께 말씀드리러 온 거였어. 연정을 알리려고—\""]], [["She stopped to breathe.", "그녀는 숨을 고르려 말을 멈췄다."], ["Emma thought first of herself, and then of Harriet.", "엠마는 먼저 자신을 떠올렸고, 그다음 해리엇을 떠올렸다."]], [["“More than an attachment, indeed,” resumed Mrs. Weston; “an engagement—a positive engagement.—What will you say, Emma—what will any body say, when it is known that Frank Churchill and Miss Fairfax are engaged;—nay, that they have been long engaged!”", "\"연정 그 이상이지, 사실은.\" 웨스턴 부인이 다시 입을 열었다. \"약혼이란다. 엄연한 약혼. 엠마, 뭐라고 할 테냐? 누군들 뭐라고 하겠니? 프랭크 처칠과 미스 페어팩스가 약혼했다는 게, 아니, 진작부터 오래도록 약혼한 사이였다는 게 알려지면 말이야!\""]], [["Emma even jumped with surprize;—and, horror-struck, exclaimed,", "엠마는 놀라 펄쩍 뛰기까지 했고, 경악한 채 외쳤다."]], [["“Jane Fairfax!—Good God!", "\"제인 페어팩스라고요!"], ["You are not serious?", "맙소사! 진심이세요?"], ["You do not mean it?”", "설마 그럴 리가요?\""]], [["“You may well be amazed,” returned Mrs.", "\"놀라는 것도 무리가 아니야.\""], ["Weston, still averting her eyes, and talking on with eagerness, that Emma might have time to recover— “You may well be amazed.", "여전히 눈길을 돌린 채, 엠마가 추스를 틈을 주려는 듯 다급하게 말을 이으며 웨스턴 부인이 답했다. \"정말 놀랄 만하지."], ["But it is even so.", "하지만 사실이 그렇단다. 두 사람은 시월부터 줄곧 엄숙한 약혼 사이였어."], ["There has been a solemn engagement between them ever since October—formed at Weymouth, and kept a secret from every body.", "웨이머스에서 맺어져, 모두에게 비밀로 부쳐졌지. 그들 자신 말고는 그 누구도 몰랐어."], ["Not a creature knowing it but themselves—neither the Campbells, nor her family, nor his.—It is so wonderful, that though perfectly convinced of the fact, it is yet almost incredible to myself.", "캠벨 댁도, 그 애 집안도, 그 사람 집안도. 너무도 놀라운 일이라, 사실이라고 완전히 납득하고서도 나조차 도무지 믿기지가 않는구나."], ["I can hardly believe it.—I thought I knew him.”", "도저히 믿을 수가 없어. 나는 그 애를 잘 안다고 여겼는데.\""]], [["Emma scarcely heard what was said.—Her mind was divided between two ideas—her own former conversations with him about Miss Fairfax; and poor Harriet;—and for some time she could only exclaim, and require confirmation, repeated confirmation.", "엠마는 무슨 말이 오가는지 거의 듣지 못했다. 그녀의 마음은 두 생각 사이에서 갈라져 있었다. 미스 페어팩스를 두고 그와 나눴던 지난날의 대화들, 그리고 가엾은 해리엇. 한동안 그녀는 그저 탄식만 하며 확인을, 거듭된 확인을 요구할 따름이었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그러니까,\" 마침내 그녀가 정신을 추스르려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["said she at last, trying to recover herself; “this is a circumstance which I must think of at least half a day, before I can at all comprehend it.", "\"이건 적어도 반나절은 곱씹어 봐야 도무지 이해라도 할 수 있을 일이네요. 뭐라고요?"], ["What!—engaged to her all the winter—before either of them came to Highbury?”", "겨우내 그 사람과 약혼한 사이였다고요? 둘 중 누구도 하이베리에 오기 전부터요?\""]], [["“Engaged since October,—secretly engaged.—It has hurt me, Emma, very much.", "\"시월부터 약혼이었어. 비밀 약혼."], ["It has hurt his father equally.", "이 일이 나를 무척이나 아프게 했단다, 엠마."], ["Some part of his conduct we cannot excuse.”", "그 애 아버지도 똑같이 아프게 했고. 그 애 처신의 어느 대목은 우리가 도저히 용서할 수가 없어.\""]], [["Emma pondered a moment, and then replied, “I will not pretend not to understand you; and to give you all the relief in my power, be assured that no such effect has followed his attentions to me, as you are apprehensive of.”", "엠마는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 답했다. \"부인 말씀을 못 알아듣는 척하지는 않을게요. 그리고 제가 드릴 수 있는 모든 위안을 드리자면, 부인께서 염려하시는 그런 결과가 그 사람의 제게 보인 관심에서 비롯되지 않았다는 걸 믿으셔도 좋아요.\""]], [["Mrs.", "웨스턴 부인은 차마 믿기 두려운 듯 고개를 들었다."], ["Weston looked up, afraid to believe; but Emma’s countenance was as steady as her words.", "그러나 엠마의 표정은 그 말처럼 흔들림이 없었다."]], [["“That you may have less difficulty in believing this boast, of my present perfect indifference,”", "\"지금 제가 완전히 무관심하다는 이 호언장담을 한결 수월하게 믿으시도록,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["she continued, “I will farther tell you, that there was a period in the early part of our acquaintance, when I did like him, when I was very much disposed to be attached to him—nay, was attached—and how it came to cease, is perhaps the wonder.", "\"한 가지 더 말씀드릴게요. 우리가 알고 지낸 초기에, 제가 정말로 그 사람을 좋아했던 시기가 있었어요."], ["Fortunately, however, it did cease.", "그에게 마음을 둘 마음이 꽤나 있었던 때가요. 아니, 마음을 두었었죠."], ["I have really for some time past, for at least these three months, cared nothing about him.", "그게 어떻게 사그라들었는지가 오히려 놀라운 일일지도 몰라요. 하지만 다행스럽게도, 그것은 사그라들었어요."], ["You may believe me, Mrs. Weston.", "정말이지 얼마 전부터, 적어도 지난 석 달간은 그 사람에 대해 아무런 마음도 없었어요."], ["This is the simple truth.”", "믿으셔도 돼요, 부인. 이건 있는 그대로의 진실이에요.\""]], [["Mrs. Weston kissed her with tears of joy; and when she could find utterance, assured her, that this protestation had done her more good than any thing else in the world could do.", "웨스턴 부인은 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리며 그녀에게 입을 맞추었고, 말문이 트이자, 이 단언이 세상 그 무엇보다도 자신에게 큰 위안이 되었다고 일러 주었다."]], [["“Mr. Weston will be almost as much relieved as myself,”", "\"미스터 웨스턴도 나만큼이나 마음이 놓일 거야.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“On this point we have been wretched.", "\"이 일로 우리는 얼마나 마음을 졸였는지 몰라. 너희 둘이 서로 마음을 두기를 우리는 더없이 간절히 바랐단다."], ["It was our darling wish that you might be attached to each other—and we were persuaded that it was so.— Imagine what we have been feeling on your account.”", "그리고 정말 그런 줄로만 알고 있었지. 우리가 너를 두고 얼마나 마음을 졸였는지 짐작이 가니.\""]], [["“I have escaped; and that I should escape, may be a matter of grateful wonder to you and myself. But this does not acquit him, Mrs. Weston; and I must say, that I think him greatly to blame. What right had he to come among us with affection and faith engaged, and with manners so very disengaged? What right had he to endeavour to please, as he certainly did—to distinguish any one young woman with persevering attention, as he certainly did—while he really belonged to another?—How could he tell what mischief he might be doing?—How could he tell that he might not be making me in love with him?—very wrong, very wrong indeed.”", "\"저는 화를 면했어요. 그리고 제가 화를 면했다는 게 부인과 제게 고마운 놀라움일지도 모르죠. 하지만 그렇다고 그 사람의 죄가 면해지는 건 아니에요, 부인. 그리고 그 사람이 크게 잘못했다는 게 제 생각이라고 말해야겠어요. 마음과 신의를 이미 약속해 둔 처지에, 그렇게나 매인 데 없는 듯한 태도로 우리 가운데 끼어들 권리가 그에게 어디 있죠? 환심을 사려 애쓸 권리가, 분명히 그랬으니까요. 한 젊은 여자를 골라 끈질긴 관심으로 떠받들 권리가, 분명히 그랬으니까요. 정작 그가 다른 사람에게 매인 몸이었으면서 말이에요. 자기가 어떤 해악을 끼칠지 어떻게 알았겠어요? 저를 그에게 반하게 만들지 않으리라고 어떻게 장담했겠어요? 아주 잘못했어요, 정말이지 아주 잘못했어요.\""]], [["“From something that he said, my dear Emma, I rather imagine—”", "\"그 애가 한 말로 미루어, 엠마, 나는 오히려 이렇게 짐작이—\""]], [["“And how could she bear such behaviour!", "\"그리고 그 여자는 어떻게 그런 행동을 견뎠을까요!"], ["Composure with a witness!", "그것도 보통 침착함이 아니라 지독한 침착함이라니!"], ["to look on, while repeated attentions were offering to another woman, before her face, and not resent it.—That is a degree of placidity, which I can neither comprehend nor respect.”", "제 면전에서 다른 여자에게 거듭 관심이 베풀어지는 걸 지켜보면서도 노여워하지 않다니요. 그건 제가 도무지 이해할 수도 존중할 수도 없는 수준의 평온함이에요.\""]], [["“There were misunderstandings between them, Emma; he said so expressly.", "\"두 사람 사이에 오해가 있었단다, 엠마. 그 애가 분명히 그렇게 말했어."], ["He had not time to enter into much explanation.", "자세히 설명할 시간은 없었지."], ["He was here only a quarter of an hour, and in a state of agitation which did not allow the full use even of the time he could stay—but that there had been misunderstandings he decidedly said.", "여기 머문 게 겨우 십오 분뿐이었고, 그나마 머물 수 있는 시간조차 온전히 쓰지 못할 만큼 마음이 어지러운 상태였으니까. 그래도 오해가 있었다는 건 단호히 말했어."], ["The present crisis, indeed, seemed to be brought on by them; and those misunderstandings might very possibly arise from the impropriety of his conduct.”", "실은 이번 사달이 그 오해 때문에 빚어진 듯하더구나. 그리고 그 오해라는 것도 어쩌면 그 애 처신의 부적절함에서 비롯되었을 가능성이 매우 크지.\""]], [["“Impropriety! Oh!", "\"부적절함이라고요!"], ["Mrs.", "오!"], ["Weston—it is too calm a censure.", "부인, 그건 너무 점잖은 질책이에요. 부적절함을 한참, 한참 넘어선 일이라고요!"], ["Much, much beyond impropriety!—It has sunk him, I cannot say how it has sunk him in my opinion.", "그 일은 그를 떨어뜨렸어요. 제 눈에 얼마나 떨어졌는지 말로 다 못 할 정도예요."], ["So unlike what a man should be!—None of that upright integrity, that strict adherence to truth and principle, that disdain of trick and littleness, which a man should display in every transaction of his life.”", "사내라면 마땅히 그래야 할 모습과 어쩌면 그리도 다른지요! 사내라면 인생의 매사에서 보여야 할, 그 곧은 정직함도, 진실과 원칙에 대한 그 엄정한 고수도, 술수와 옹졸함에 대한 그 경멸도 도무지 없잖아요.\""]], [["“Nay, dear Emma, now I must take his part; for though he has been wrong in this instance, I have known him long enough to answer for his having many, very many, good qualities; and—”", "\"아니야, 엠마, 이제는 내가 그 애 편을 들어야겠구나. 이번 일에서는 그 애가 잘못했지만, 나는 그 애를 충분히 오래 알아 온 터라 그에게 좋은 자질이 많이, 아주 많이 있다고 장담할 수 있거든. 게다가—\""]], [["“Good God!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["cried Emma, not attending to her.—“Mrs.", "엠마는 그녀의 말은 귀에 담지도 않고 외쳤다."], ["Smallridge, too!", "\"스몰리지 부인 일도 있잖아요!"], ["Jane actually on the point of going as governess!", "제인이 정말로 가정교사로 들어가기 일보 직전이었다고요!"], ["What could he mean by such horrible indelicacy?", "그렇게 끔찍한 무신경함을 그가 대체 무슨 작정으로요?"], ["To suffer her to engage herself—to suffer her even to think of such a measure!”", "그 여자가 그런 자리에 매이게 내버려 두다니. 아니, 그런 방도를 생각하게 내버려 두다니요!\""]], [["“He knew nothing about it, Emma.", "\"그 애는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 몰랐어, 엠마."], ["On this article I can fully acquit him.", "이 점에서는 내가 그 애의 누명을 온전히 벗겨 줄 수 있단다. 그건 그 애에게 알리지 않은, 그 여자 혼자만의 결심이었어."], ["It was a private resolution of hers, not communicated to him—or at least not communicated in a way to carry conviction.—Till yesterday, I know he said he was in the dark as to her plans.", "적어도 그 애가 확신할 만한 방식으로는 알려지지 않았지. 어제까지도 그 여자의 계획에 대해서는 깜깜했다고 그 애가 말하더구나."], ["They burst on him, I do not know how, but by some letter or message—and it was the discovery of what she was doing, of this very project of hers, which determined him to come forward at once, own it all to his uncle, throw himself on his kindness, and, in short, put an end to the miserable state of concealment that had been carrying on so long.”", "그런데 어떻게 된 건지 모르지만, 무슨 편지나 전갈로 갑자기 그 일을 알게 된 거야. 그리고 그 여자가 무얼 하려는지, 바로 그 계획을 알아챈 것이 그 애로 하여금 곧장 나서서, 외삼촌께 모든 걸 털어놓고, 그 너그러움에 자신을 내맡기고, 한마디로 그토록 오래 끌어온 그 비참한 은닉의 상태에 종지부를 찍게 만든 거란다.\""]], [["Emma began to listen better.", "엠마는 한결 귀를 기울이기 시작했다."]], [["“I am to hear from him soon,”", "\"곧 그 애한테서 소식이 올 거야.\""], ["continued Mrs. Weston.", "웨스턴 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["“He told me at parting, that he should soon write; and he spoke in a manner which seemed to promise me many particulars that could not be given now.", "\"헤어질 때 곧 편지하겠다고 했고, 지금은 들려줄 수 없는 자세한 사정을 많이 알려 주겠다고 약속하는 듯한 투로 말했거든. 그러니 우리 그 편지를 기다려 보자."], ["Let us wait, therefore, for this letter. It may bring many extenuations.", "어쩌면 정상을 참작할 사정이 많이 담겨 있을지도 몰라."], ["It may make many things intelligible and excusable which now are not to be understood.", "지금은 도무지 이해할 수 없는 많은 일들이, 그 편지 덕에 납득되고 용서될지도 모르지."], ["Don’t let us be severe, don’t let us be in a hurry to condemn him.", "우리 너무 모질게 굴지 말자. 그 애를 정죄하는 데 서두르지 말자."], ["Let us have patience.", "인내심을 갖자꾸나."], ["I must love him; and now that I am satisfied on one point, the one material point, I am sincerely anxious for its all turning out well, and ready to hope that it may.", "나는 그 애를 사랑할 수밖에 없어. 그리고 이제 한 가지 점에서, 그 한 가지 핵심적인 점에서 마음이 놓이고 보니, 모든 일이 잘 풀리기를 진심으로 바라게 되고, 그러리라고 기대할 마음도 생기는구나."], ["They must both have suffered a great deal under such a system of secresy and concealment.”", "그렇게 비밀과 은닉으로 점철된 처지에서 두 사람 다 무척이나 괴로웠을 게 분명해.\""]], [["“His sufferings,”", "\"그 사람의 괴로움은,\""], ["replied Emma dryly, “do not appear to have done him much harm.", "엠마가 무덤덤하게 답했다. \"별로 그를 해친 것 같지 않은데요."], ["Well, and how did Mr. Churchill take it?”", "그래, 미스터 처칠은 그 일을 어떻게 받아들이셨대요?\""]], [["“Most favourably for his nephew—gave his consent with scarcely a difficulty.", "\"조카에게 더없이 호의적으로 받아들였어. 거의 아무 어려움 없이 승낙해 주었지."], ["Conceive what the events of a week have done in that family! While poor Mrs.", "일주일 사이에 그 집안에서 벌어진 일을 생각해 보렴!"], ["Churchill lived, I suppose there could not have been a hope, a chance, a possibility;—but scarcely are her remains at rest in the family vault, than her husband is persuaded to act exactly opposite to what she would have required.", "가엾은 처칠 부인이 살아 있는 동안에는 희망도, 기회도, 가능성도 없었을 텐데. 그런데 그 유해가 가문 납골당에서 채 안식에 들기도 전에, 그 남편은 부인이 요구했을 법한 것과 정반대로 행동하도록 설득당한 거야."], ["What a blessing it is, when undue influence does not survive the grave!—He gave his consent with very little persuasion.”", "부당한 영향력이 무덤 너머까지 살아남지 않는다는 게 얼마나 다행인지! 그 사람은 거의 설득할 필요도 없이 승낙해 주었단다.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["thought Emma, “he would have done as much for Harriet.”", "엠마는 속으로 생각했다. \"그 사람이라면 해리엇한테도 그만큼은 해 줬을 텐데.\""]], [["“This was settled last night, and Frank was off with the light this morning. He stopped at Highbury, at the Bates’s, I fancy, some time—and then came on hither; but was in such a hurry to get back to his uncle, to whom he is just now more necessary than ever, that, as I tell you, he could stay with us but a quarter of an hour.—He was very much agitated—very much, indeed—to a degree that made him appear quite a different creature from any thing I had ever seen him before.—In addition to all the rest, there had been the shock of finding her so very unwell, which he had had no previous suspicion of—and there was every appearance of his having been feeling a great deal.”", "\"이게 어젯밤에 결정됐고, 프랭크는 오늘 아침 동이 트자마자 떠났어. 하이베리에 들러, 아마 베이츠 댁에서, 얼마간 머문 모양이더라. 그러고는 이리로 건너왔지. 하지만 외삼촌께 돌아가려고 어찌나 서두르던지—지금 그 어느 때보다 외삼촌께 자기가 더 필요하니까—내가 말했듯이 우리와는 겨우 십오 분밖에 머물지 못했어. 무척이나 동요한 모습이었어. 정말이지 무척이나. 내가 전에 본 그 어떤 모습과도 영판 딴사람 같아 보일 정도로 말이야. 게다가 다른 모든 일에 더해, 그 여자가 그토록 몹시 편찮은 걸—전혀 짐작도 못 했던 일을—알게 된 충격까지 겹쳤으니. 보아하니 마음고생을 단단히 한 기색이 역력했어.\""]], [["“And do you really believe the affair to have been carrying on with such perfect secresy?—The Campbells, the Dixons, did none of them know of the engagement?”", "\"그런데 그 일이 정말로 그렇게 완벽한 비밀 속에 진행됐다고 믿으세요? 캠벨 댁도, 딕슨 부부도, 그 누구도 약혼을 몰랐다고요?\""]], [["Emma could not speak the name of Dixon without a little blush.", "엠마는 딕슨이라는 이름을 입에 올리며 살짝 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“None; not one.", "\"아무도."], ["He positively said that it had been known to no being in the world but their two selves.”", "단 한 사람도. 자기들 두 사람 말고는 세상 그 누구도 몰랐다고 그 애가 딱 잘라 말했어.\""]], [["“Well,” said Emma, “I suppose we shall gradually grow reconciled to the idea, and I wish them very happy. But I shall always think it a very abominable sort of proceeding. What has it been but a system of hypocrisy and deceit,—espionage, and treachery?—To come among us with professions of openness and simplicity; and such a league in secret to judge us all!—Here have we been, the whole winter and spring, completely duped, fancying ourselves all on an equal footing of truth and honour, with two people in the midst of us who may have been carrying round, comparing and sitting in judgment on sentiments and words that were never meant for both to hear.—They must take the consequence, if they have heard each other spoken of in a way not perfectly agreeable!”", "\"그렇군요.\" 엠마가 말했다. \"차차 그 생각에 익숙해지겠죠. 두 사람이 무척 행복하기를 바라요. 하지만 저는 이게 언제까지나 아주 가증스러운 처사라고 여길 거예요. 결국 위선과 기만의 체계, 첩보와 배신이 아니고 뭐였겠어요? 솔직함이니 꾸밈없음이니 떠벌리며 우리 가운데 끼어들고는, 우리 모두를 속으로 품평하려고 그런 밀약을 맺어 두다니! 우리는 겨우내 봄내내 여기서 완전히 농락당한 채, 다들 진실과 명예의 대등한 토대 위에 서 있다고 믿었어요. 그런데 우리 한복판에, 둘이 함께 들으라고 한 적 없는 감정과 말들을 여기저기 옮기고, 견주고, 두고 판단했을지 모를 두 사람이 있었던 거잖아요. 서로에 대해 영 달갑잖게 이야기되는 걸 들었다면, 그 대가는 그들이 감수해야죠!\""]], [["“I am quite easy on that head,”", "\"그 점에 대해서라면 나는 아주 마음이 편하단다.\""], ["replied Mrs. Weston.", "웨스턴 부인이 답했다."], ["“I am very sure that I never said any thing of either to the other, which both might not have heard.”", "\"나는 둘 중 어느 한쪽에 대해서도, 둘 다 들어서는 안 될 말은 다른 쪽에게 한 적이 없다고 확신하거든.\""]], [["“You are in luck.—Your only blunder was confined to my ear, when you imagined a certain friend of ours in love with the lady.”", "\"운이 좋으셨네요. 부인의 유일한 실수는 제 귀에만 들어간 거였으니까요. 우리의 어떤 벗이 그 아가씨를 사랑한다고 상상하셨을 때 말이에요.\""]], [["“True.", "\"그래."], ["But as I have always had a thoroughly good opinion of Miss Fairfax, I never could, under any blunder, have spoken ill of her; and as to speaking ill of him, there I must have been safe.”", "하지만 나는 늘 미스 페어팩스를 진심으로 좋게 보아 온 터라, 어떤 실수를 하더라도 그녀를 나쁘게 말할 수는 없었을 거야. 그리고 그 애를 나쁘게 말하는 일이라면, 거기서는 내가 안전했을 테고.\""]], [["At this moment Mr. Weston appeared at a little distance from the window, evidently on the watch.", "이때 미스터 웨스턴이 창문에서 조금 떨어진 곳에 나타났는데, 분명히 살피고 있던 모양이었다."], ["His wife gave him a look which invited him in; and, while he was coming round, added, “Now, dearest Emma, let me intreat you to say and look every thing that may set his heart at ease, and incline him to be satisfied with the match.", "그의 아내는 들어오라고 청하는 눈짓을 보냈고, 그가 빙 돌아오는 동안 덧붙였다. \"자, 엠마, 부탁이니, 저이 마음을 놓이게 하고 이 혼사에 흡족해하게 할 만한 말과 표정을 모두 보여 주렴."], ["Let us make the best of it—and, indeed, almost every thing may be fairly said in her favour.", "우리 이 일을 좋게 받아들이자. 사실 그 애에 대해서는 거의 모든 게 정당하게 좋은 쪽으로 말해질 수 있어."], ["It is not a connexion to gratify; but if Mr. Churchill does not feel that, why should we?", "우쭐할 만한 연줄이야 아니지만, 미스터 처칠이 그걸 개의치 않는데 우리가 왜 그러겠니?"], ["and it may be a very fortunate circumstance for him, for Frank, I mean, that he should have attached himself to a girl of such steadiness of character and good judgment as I have always given her credit for—and still am disposed to give her credit for, in spite of this one great deviation from the strict rule of right.", "게다가 그 애한테는, 그러니까 프랭크한테는, 내가 늘 인정해 온—그리고 이 한 번의 큰 일탈에도 불구하고 여전히 인정하고 싶은—그렇게 한결같은 성품과 좋은 판단력을 지닌 아가씨에게 마음을 둔 게 무척 다행스러운 일일 수도 있지."], ["And how much may be said in her situation for even that error!”", "그 애 처지를 생각하면, 그 잘못조차 얼마나 많이 변호될 수 있겠니!\""]], [["“Much, indeed!”", "\"정말 그래요!\""], ["cried Emma feelingly.", "엠마가 마음을 담아 외쳤다. \"여자가 오직 제 생각만 한 걸 용서받을 수 있는 처지가 있다면, 바로 제인 페어팩스 같은 처지일 거예요."], ["“If a woman can ever be excused for thinking only of herself, it is in a situation like Jane Fairfax’s.—Of such, one may almost say, that ‘the world is not their’s, nor the world’s law.’”", "그런 사람을 두고는 거의 이렇게 말할 수도 있겠죠. '세상은 그들의 것이 아니요, 세상의 법도 그들의 것이 아니다'라고요.\""]], [["She met Mr. Weston on his entrance, with a smiling countenance, exclaiming,", "그녀는 들어서는 미스터 웨스턴을 미소 띤 얼굴로 맞으며 외쳤다."]], [["“A very pretty trick you have been playing me, upon my word!", "\"정말이지 저를 곱게도 골려 주셨네요!"], ["This was a device, I suppose, to sport with my curiosity, and exercise my talent of guessing. But you really frightened me.", "제 호기심을 가지고 장난치고 제 짐작하는 재주를 시험해 보려는 수작이었나 봐요."], ["I thought you had lost half your property, at least.", "하지만 정말 저를 겁먹게 하셨어요."], ["And here, instead of its being a matter of condolence, it turns out to be one of congratulation.—I congratulate you, Mr.", "적어도 재산의 절반은 잃으신 줄 알았다니까요. 그런데 위로할 일은커녕, 알고 보니 축하할 일이잖아요."], ["Weston, with all my heart, on the prospect of having one of the most lovely and accomplished young women in England for your daughter.”", "미스터 웨스턴, 영국에서 가장 사랑스럽고 재주 많은 아가씨 가운데 한 사람을 따님으로 두게 되신 걸 진심으로 축하드려요.\""]], [["A glance or two between him and his wife, convinced him that all was as right as this speech proclaimed; and its happy effect on his spirits was immediate.", "그와 아내 사이에 오간 한두 번의 눈짓이, 모든 게 이 인사말이 선언한 그대로임을 그에게 납득시켜 주었고, 그것이 그의 기분에 미친 행복한 효과는 즉각적이었다."], ["His air and voice recovered their usual briskness: he shook her heartily and gratefully by the hand, and entered on the subject in a manner to prove, that he now only wanted time and persuasion to think the engagement no very bad thing.", "그의 태도와 음성은 여느 때의 활기를 되찾았다. 그는 진심으로 고마워하며 그녀의 손을 힘껏 맞잡고는, 이제 시간과 설득만 있으면 이 약혼을 그리 나쁘지 않은 일로 여기게 되리라는 것을 증명하는 투로 그 화제에 들어섰다."], ["His companions suggested only what could palliate imprudence, or smooth objections; and by the time they had talked it all over together, and he had talked it all over again with Emma, in their walk back to Hartfield, he was become perfectly reconciled, and not far from thinking it the very best thing that Frank could possibly have done.", "그의 동행들은 경솔함을 누그러뜨리고 반대의 모서리를 매끈하게 다듬을 만한 이야기만 들려주었다. 그리하여 그들이 함께 그 일을 죄다 이야기해 보고, 하트필드로 돌아오는 산책길에 그가 엠마와 다시 한번 그 일을 죄다 이야기해 볼 무렵에는, 그는 완전히 마음을 누그러뜨려, 그것을 프랭크가 할 수 있었던 가장 잘한 일이라 여기는 데까지 그리 멀지 않은 상태가 되어 있었다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter XI", "# 제3권 11장"]], [["“Harriet, poor Harriet!”—Those were the words; in them lay the tormenting ideas which Emma could not get rid of, and which constituted the real misery of the business to her.", "“해리엇, 가엾은 해리엇!” — 바로 그 말이었다."], ["Frank Churchill had behaved very ill by herself—very ill in many ways,—but it was not so much his behaviour as her own, which made her so angry with him.", "그 말 속에 엠마가 떨쳐 낼 수 없는, 이 일의 진짜 비참함을 이루는 고통스러운 생각이 담겨 있었다. 프랭크 처칠은 그녀 자신에게 아주 못되게 굴었다 — 여러모로 아주 못되게."], ["It was the scrape which he had drawn her into on Harriet’s account, that gave the deepest hue to his offence.—Poor Harriet!", "그러나 그녀를 그토록 화나게 한 것은 그의 처신이라기보다 그녀 자신의 처신이었다. 해리엇을 두고 그가 그녀를 끌어들인 그 곤경이야말로 그의 잘못에 가장 짙은 색을 입힌 것이었다."], ["to be a second time the dupe of her misconceptions and flattery.", "가엾은 해리엇! 두 번째로 그녀의 오해와 부추김에 놀아난 신세가 되다니."], ["Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리가 언젠가 \"엠마, 자네는 해리엇 스미스에게 좋은 벗이 못 되었어\" 하고 말했을 때, 그는 예언을 한 셈이었다."], ["Knightley had spoken prophetically, when he once said, “Emma, you have been no friend to Harriet Smith.”—She was afraid she had done her nothing but disservice.—It was true that she had not to charge herself, in this instance as in the former, with being the sole and original author of the mischief; with having suggested such feelings as might otherwise never have entered Harriet’s imagination; for Harriet had acknowledged her admiration and preference of Frank Churchill before she had ever given her a hint on the subject; but she felt completely guilty of having encouraged what she might have repressed.", "그녀는 자기가 해리엇에게 해만 끼친 게 아닌가 두려웠다. 이번 경우에는 지난번처럼, 화근을 만든 유일하고 본래의 장본인으로 스스로를 책할 수는 없었던 것이 사실이다 — 그렇지 않았더라면 해리엇의 상상 속에 결코 떠오르지 않았을 감정을 심어 주었다고 자책할 수는 없었다."], ["She might have prevented the indulgence and increase of such sentiments.", "해리엇은 엠마가 이 일에 관해 한마디 귀띔하기도 전에 이미 프랭크 처칠을 흠모하고 그를 마음에 둔다고 털어놓았으니까. 그러나 막을 수도 있었던 것을 부추겼다는 점에서는 엠마도 완전히 죄가 있다고 느꼈다."], ["Her influence would have been enough.", "그런 감정에 빠져들고 그것이 자라나는 것을 막을 수도 있었다. 그녀의 영향력이면 충분했을 것이다."], ["And now she was very conscious that she ought to have prevented them.—She felt that she had been risking her friend’s happiness on most insufficient grounds.", "그리고 이제 그녀는 자기가 그것을 막았어야 했음을 절절히 깨달았다. 그녀는 너무도 빈약한 근거에 벗의 행복을 걸어 왔던 것이다."], ["Common sense would have directed her to tell Harriet, that she must not allow herself to think of him, and that there were five hundred chances to one against his ever caring for her.—“But, with common sense,”", "상식이 있었다면 해리엇에게, 그를 마음에 두어서는 안 되며, 그가 그녀를 마음에 둘 가능성은 오백 분의 일에 불과하다고 일러 주었을 터였다. \"하지만 상식으로 말하자면,\""], ["she added, “I am afraid I have had little to do.”", "그녀는 덧붙였다. \"나는 그것과 별 인연이 없었나 봐.\""]], [["She was extremely angry with herself.", "그녀는 자기 자신에게 몹시 화가 났다."], ["If she could not have been angry with Frank Churchill too, it would have been dreadful.—As for Jane Fairfax, she might at least relieve her feelings from any present solicitude on her account.", "프랭크 처칠에게도 함께 화를 낼 수 없었더라면, 그것은 견딜 수 없는 일이었으리라. 제인 페어팩스로 말하자면, 적어도 지금 그녀에 대한 근심에서는 마음을 놓아도 좋았다."], ["Harriet would be anxiety enough; she need no longer be unhappy about Jane, whose troubles and whose ill-health having, of course, the same origin, must be equally under cure.—Her days of insignificance and evil were over.—She would soon be well, and happy, and prosperous.—Emma could now imagine why her own attentions had been slighted.", "해리엇만으로도 걱정거리는 충분했다. 제인 때문에 더는 마음 아파할 필요가 없었으니, 그녀의 고통과 병약함이 같은 데서 나온 것인 이상 함께 치유될 것이 분명했다."], ["This discovery laid many smaller matters open.", "그녀가 보잘것없고 불행하던 나날은 끝났다."], ["No doubt it had been from jealousy.—In Jane’s eyes she had been a rival; and well might any thing she could offer of assistance or regard be repulsed.", "머지않아 건강하고 행복하고 형편이 펴질 것이었다. 이제 엠마는 자신의 호의가 어째서 그토록 박대당했는지 짐작할 수 있었다."], ["An airing in the Hartfield carriage would have been the rack, and arrowroot from the Hartfield storeroom must have been poison.", "이 깨달음이 자잘한 일들의 까닭마저 줄줄이 풀어 주었다. 틀림없이 질투 때문이었다."], ["She understood it all; and as far as her mind could disengage itself from the injustice and selfishness of angry feelings, she acknowledged that Jane Fairfax would have neither elevation nor happiness beyond her desert.", "제인의 눈에 자신은 연적이었으니, 자신이 내미는 도움이나 호의가 무엇이든 퇴짜를 맞는 것도 무리가 아니었다. 하트필드 마차로 바람을 쐬자는 권유는 고문 틀이었을 것이고, 하트필드 곳간에서 내온 칡즙은 독약이나 다름없었으리라."], ["But poor Harriet was such an engrossing charge!", "그녀는 이제 모든 것을 이해했다."], ["There was little sympathy to be spared for any body else.", "그리고 분노에 깃든 불공정과 이기심에서 마음을 떼어 낼 수 있는 한, 제인 페어팩스가 분에 넘치는 영달이나 행복을 누리지는 않으리라는 것을 인정했다. 그러나 가엾은 해리엇이야말로 마음을 온통 사로잡는 짐이었다!"], ["Emma was sadly fearful that this second disappointment would be more severe than the first.", "다른 누구에게 나누어 줄 동정심은 거의 남아 있지 않았다. 엠마는 이 두 번째 실연이 첫 번째보다 더 혹독하지 않을까 못내 두려웠다."], ["Considering the very superior claims of the object, it ought; and judging by its apparently stronger effect on Harriet’s mind, producing reserve and self-command, it would.—She must communicate the painful truth, however, and as soon as possible.", "그 대상의 비할 데 없이 우월한 자격을 생각하면 마땅히 그래야 했고, 해리엇의 마음에 끼친 영향이 한층 강해 보여 그녀를 말수 적고 자제하게 만든 것으로 미루어 보아도 그러할 것이었다. 그러나 어쨌든 이 괴로운 진실을 알려야 했고, 그것도 되도록 빨리 알려야 했다."], ["An injunction of secresy had been among Mr. Weston’s parting words.", "미스터 웨스턴이 헤어질 무렵에 한 당부 가운데 비밀 엄수가 들어 있었다."], ["“For the present, the whole affair was to be completely a secret. Mr.", "\"당분간 이 일은 철저히 비밀에 부쳐야 한다."], ["Churchill had made a point of it, as a token of respect to the wife he had so very recently lost; and every body admitted it to be no more than due decorum.”—Emma had promised; but still Harriet must be excepted.", "미스터 처칠이 이를 거듭 강조했는데, 얼마 전에 여읜 아내에 대한 예의의 표시였고, 그것이 마땅한 도리라는 데에는 누구나 동의했다.\" 엠마는 그러마고 약속했다."], ["It was her superior duty.", "그러나 그래도 해리엇만은 예외여야 했다. 그것이 그녀의 더 큰 의무였다."]], [["In spite of her vexation, she could not help feeling it almost ridiculous, that she should have the very same distressing and delicate office to perform by Harriet, which Mrs. Weston had just gone through by herself.", "분한 마음에도 불구하고, 그녀는 웨스턴 부인이 방금 자신에게 치른 것과 똑같은 괴롭고 까다로운 임무를 이제 자신이 해리엇에게 치러야 한다는 것이 거의 우습게 느껴지는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다."], ["The intelligence, which had been so anxiously announced to her, she was now to be anxiously announcing to another.", "그토록 조심스럽게 자신에게 전해진 소식을, 이제는 자신이 다른 사람에게 조심스럽게 전해야 하는 것이었다."], ["Her heart beat quick on hearing Harriet’s footstep and voice; so, she supposed, had poor Mrs.", "해리엇의 발소리와 목소리가 들리자 그녀의 가슴이 빠르게 뛰었다."], ["Weston felt when she was approaching Randalls.", "가엾은 웨스턴 부인도 랜덜스로 다가갈 때 이런 심정이었으리라."], ["Could the event of the disclosure bear an equal resemblance!—But of that, unfortunately, there could be no chance.", "이 고백의 결말도 그만큼 닮을 수 있다면! 그러나 불행히도, 그럴 가망은 없었다."]], [["“Well, Miss Woodhouse!”", "\"어머, 미스 우드하우스!\""], ["cried Harriet, coming eagerly into the room—“is not this the oddest news that ever was?”", "해리엇이 방으로 다급히 들어서며 외쳤다. \"이런 희한한 소식이 또 어디 있어요?\""]], [["“What news do you mean?”", "\"무슨 소식 말이에요?\""], ["replied Emma, unable to guess, by look or voice, whether Harriet could indeed have received any hint.", "엠마는 해리엇이 정말로 무슨 귀띔이라도 받았는지 표정으로도 목소리로도 짐작할 수 없어 이렇게 되물었다."]], [["“About Jane Fairfax.", "\"제인 페어팩스 일이요."], ["Did you ever hear any thing so strange?", "이렇게 이상한 얘기 들어 보셨어요? 어머!"], ["Oh!—you need not be afraid of owning it to me, for Mr.", "저한테는 솔직히 말씀하셔도 돼요."], ["Weston has told me himself. I met him just now.", "미스터 웨스턴이 직접 저한테 얘기해 주셨거든요."], ["He told me it was to be a great secret; and, therefore, I should not think of mentioning it to any body but you, but he said you knew it.”", "방금 그분을 만났어요. 절대 비밀이라고 하시기에, 미스 우드하우스 말고는 누구한테도 말할 생각을 안 하지만, 미스 우드하우스는 이미 알고 계신다고 하셨어요.\""]], [["“What did Mr.", "\"미스터 웨스턴이 뭐라고 하셨는데요?\""], ["Weston tell you?”—said Emma, still perplexed.", "엠마는 여전히 어리둥절해하며 물었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["he told me all about it; that Jane Fairfax and Mr.", "다 말씀해 주셨어요."], ["Frank Churchill are to be married, and that they have been privately engaged to one another this long while.", "제인 페어팩스와 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 결혼할 거고, 두 사람이 오래전부터 남몰래 약혼한 사이였다고요."], ["How very odd!”", "정말 희한하죠!\""]], [["It was, indeed, so odd; Harriet’s behaviour was so extremely odd, that Emma did not know how to understand it.", "과연 그것은 너무도 희한한 일이었다. 그러나 해리엇의 태도가 어찌나 별난지, 엠마는 그것을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Her character appeared absolutely changed.", "해리엇의 성격이 완전히 달라진 것만 같았다."], ["She seemed to propose shewing no agitation, or disappointment, or peculiar concern in the discovery.", "그녀는 이 소식에 동요도, 실망도, 별다른 근심도 내비치지 않으려는 듯했다."], ["Emma looked at her, quite unable to speak.", "엠마는 그녀를 바라보았으나, 도무지 입을 뗄 수가 없었다."]], [["“Had you any idea,” cried Harriet, “of his being in love with her?—You, perhaps, might.—You (blushing as she spoke) who can see into every body’s heart; but nobody else—”", "\"미스 우드하우스는 짐작하셨어요,\" 해리엇이 외쳤다. \"그분이 그 여자를 사랑한다는 걸요? 미스 우드하우스라면 아셨을지도 몰라요. (말하면서 얼굴을 붉혔다) 누구 마음이든 꿰뚫어 보시는 분이니까요. 하지만 다른 사람은 아무도—\""]], [["“Upon my word,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["said Emma, “I begin to doubt my having any such talent.", "엠마가 말했다. \"내게 그런 재주가 있는지 의심스러워지기 시작하는걸요."], ["Can you seriously ask me, Harriet, whether I imagined him attached to another woman at the very time that I was—tacitly, if not openly—encouraging you to give way to your own feelings?—I never had the slightest suspicion, till within the last hour, of Mr.", "해리엇, 정말로 내게 물을 수 있어요? 내가 — 드러내 놓고는 아니더라도 은근히 — 당신더러 그 마음에 따르라고 부추기던 바로 그때, 그가 다른 여자에게 마음을 두고 있다고 내가 상상이나 했겠는지를요?"], ["Frank Churchill’s having the least regard for Jane Fairfax.", "한 시간 전까지만 해도 나는 미스터 프랭크 처칠이 제인 페어팩스에게 손톱만큼이라도 마음이 있으리라고는 전혀 의심하지 않았어요."], ["You may be very sure that if I had, I should have cautioned you accordingly.”", "만약 그랬다면 당연히 그에 맞게 당신에게 주의를 주었으리란 건 믿어 주세요.\""]], [["“Me!”", "\"저요!\""], ["cried Harriet, colouring, and astonished.", "해리엇이 얼굴을 붉히고 깜짝 놀라며 외쳤다. \"왜 저한테 주의를 주신다는 거예요?"], ["“Why should you caution me?—You do not think I care about Mr. Frank Churchill.”", "제가 미스터 프랭크 처칠을 마음에 둔다고 생각하시는 건 아니죠.\""]], [["“I am delighted to hear you speak so stoutly on the subject,”", "\"이 일을 그렇게 단호하게 말하니 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["replied Emma, smiling; “but you do not mean to deny that there was a time—and not very distant either—when you gave me reason to understand that you did care about him?”", "엠마가 미소 지으며 대꾸했다. \"하지만 한때 — 그리 멀지도 않은 때에 — 당신이 그분을 마음에 두고 있다고 내가 알아듣게끔 한 적이 있다는 건 부정하지 않겠죠?\""]], [["“Him!—never, never.", "\"그분을요! 절대로, 절대로요."], ["Dear Miss Woodhouse, how could you so mistake me?”", "미스 우드하우스, 어떻게 저를 그렇게 오해하실 수 있어요?\""], ["turning away distressed.", "해리엇은 괴로워하며 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Harriet!” cried Emma, after a moment’s pause—“What do you mean?—Good Heaven! what do you mean?—Mistake you!—Am I to suppose then?—”", "\"해리엇!\" 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 엠마가 외쳤다. \"그게 무슨 말이에요? 세상에! 무슨 말이에요? 당신을 오해했다니! 그럼 내가 짐작해도 되나요—?\""]], [["She could not speak another word.—Her voice was lost; and she sat down, waiting in great terror till Harriet should answer.", "그녀는 한마디도 더 잇지 못했다. 목소리가 나오지 않았다. 그녀는 자리에 앉아, 해리엇이 대답할 때까지 큰 두려움 속에 기다렸다."]], [["Harriet, who was standing at some distance, and with face turned from her, did not immediately say any thing; and when she did speak, it was in a voice nearly as agitated as Emma’s.", "조금 떨어진 곳에서 얼굴을 돌린 채 서 있던 해리엇은 곧바로 입을 열지 않았다. 그리고 마침내 말을 꺼냈을 때, 그 목소리는 엠마의 것 못지않게 떨렸다."]], [["“I should not have thought it possible,”", "\"미스 우드하우스가 저를 오해하실 수 있으리라고는,\""], ["she began, “that you could have misunderstood me!", "그녀가 입을 떼었다. \"생각도 못 했어요!"], ["I know we agreed never to name him—but considering how infinitely superior he is to every body else, I should not have thought it possible that I could be supposed to mean any other person.", "우리가 그분 이름을 절대 입에 올리지 않기로 한 건 알지만 — 그분이 다른 누구보다 한없이 뛰어난 분이라는 걸 생각하면, 제가 다른 사람을 두고 한 말이라고 여겨지리라고는 도저히 상상도 못 했어요."], ["Mr. Frank Churchill, indeed!", "미스터 프랭크 처칠이라니요!"], ["I do not know who would ever look at him in the company of the other.", "그분 곁에서 누가 미스터 프랭크 처칠한테 눈길이나 주겠어요."], ["I hope I have a better taste than to think of Mr. Frank Churchill, who is like nobody by his side.", "제게도 그분 곁에선 아무것도 아닌 미스터 프랭크 처칠 따위를 마음에 둘 만큼 천한 안목은 없다고요."], ["And that you should have been so mistaken, is amazing!—I am sure, but for believing that you entirely approved and meant to encourage me in my attachment, I should have considered it at first too great a presumption almost, to dare to think of him.", "그런데 미스 우드하우스가 그렇게 오해하셨다니, 놀라울 따름이에요! 정말이지, 미스 우드하우스가 제 연정을 온전히 인정하고 부추겨 주실 거라고 믿지 않았다면, 처음엔 감히 그분을 마음에 둔다는 게 너무 큰 주제넘은 짓이라고 여겼을 거예요."], ["At first, if you had not told me that more wonderful things had happened; that there had been matches of greater disparity (those were your very words);—I should not have dared to give way to—I should not have thought it possible—But if you, who had been always acquainted with him—”", "처음에, 미스 우드하우스가 이보다 더 놀라운 일도 있었다고, 더 큰 신분 차이를 넘은 혼사도 있었다고 (그게 바로 미스 우드하우스가 하신 말씀이에요) 말씀해 주시지 않았다면 — 저는 감히 마음을 내맡기지도, 가능하리라 생각하지도 못했을 거예요 — 하지만 늘 그분을 알고 지내신 미스 우드하우스가—\""]], [["“Harriet!”", "\"해리엇!\""], ["cried Emma, collecting herself resolutely—“Let us understand each other now, without the possibility of farther mistake.", "엠마가 결연히 정신을 다잡으며 외쳤다. \"이제 서로 분명히 해 둡시다."], ["Are you speaking of—Mr.", "더 오해할 여지가 없도록요."], ["Knightley?”", "당신이 말하는 게 — 미스터 나이틀리인가요?\""]], [["“To be sure I am.", "\"그럼요, 당연하죠."], ["I never could have an idea of any body else—and so I thought you knew.", "다른 사람을 두고 한 말일 리가 없잖아요 — 그래서 저는 미스 우드하우스도 아시는 줄 알았어요."], ["When we talked about him, it was as clear as possible.”", "우리가 그분 얘기를 할 때, 더없이 또렷했는걸요.\""]], [["“Not quite,”", "\"꼭 그렇진 않아요.\""], ["returned Emma, with forced calmness, “for all that you then said, appeared to me to relate to a different person.", "엠마가 애써 침착하게 대꾸했다. \"그때 당신이 한 말은 죄다 내겐 다른 사람을 두고 한 얘기로 들렸으니까요."], ["I could almost assert that you had named Mr. Frank Churchill.", "당신이 미스터 프랭크 처칠의 이름을 댔다고 거의 단언할 수도 있어요."], ["I am sure the service Mr. Frank Churchill had rendered you, in protecting you from the gipsies, was spoken of.”", "미스터 프랭크 처칠이 집시들에게서 당신을 지켜 준 그 일을 분명 입에 올렸잖아요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["Miss Woodhouse, how you do forget!”", "미스 우드하우스, 어쩜 그렇게 깜빡하세요!\""]], [["“My dear Harriet, I perfectly remember the substance of what I said on the occasion.", "\"해리엇, 그때 내가 한 말의 요지는 똑똑히 기억해요. 나는 당신의 연정이 놀랍지 않다고, 그분이 당신에게 베푼 그 도움을 생각하면 지극히 자연스러운 일이라고 말했어요."], ["I told you that I did not wonder at your attachment; that considering the service he had rendered you, it was extremely natural:—and you agreed to it, expressing yourself very warmly as to your sense of that service, and mentioning even what your sensations had been in seeing him come forward to your rescue.—The impression of it is strong on my memory.”", "그러자 당신은 그 말에 동의하면서, 그 도움에 대한 고마움을 아주 뜨겁게 토로했고, 그분이 당신을 구하러 나서는 것을 봤을 때 어떤 심정이었는지까지 이야기했어요. 그 인상이 내 기억에 또렷이 남아 있는걸요.\""]], [["“Oh, dear,”", "\"어머, 세상에,\""], ["cried Harriet, “now I recollect what you mean; but I was thinking of something very different at the time.", "해리엇이 외쳤다."], ["It was not the gipsies—it was not Mr.", "\"이제 무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요."], ["Frank Churchill that I meant. No!", "하지만 그때 저는 전혀 다른 일을 떠올리고 있었어요."], ["(with some elevation) I was thinking of a much more precious circumstance—of Mr.", "집시도 아니고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠도 아니었어요. 아니에요!"], ["Knightley’s coming and asking me to dance, when Mr. Elton would not stand up with me; and when there was no other partner in the room.", "(다소 들뜬 목소리로) 저는 훨씬 더 소중한 일을 생각하고 있었어요 — 미스터 엘턴이 저와 춤추기를 마다하고, 방 안에 다른 짝도 없었을 때, 미스터 나이틀리가 다가와 제게 춤을 청해 주신 일 말이에요."], ["That was the kind action; that was the noble benevolence and generosity; that was the service which made me begin to feel how superior he was to every other being upon earth.”", "그게 바로 그 다정한 행동이었고, 그게 바로 그 고결한 자애와 너그러움이었고, 그게 바로 제가 그분이 세상 다른 누구보다 얼마나 뛰어난 분인지 느끼기 시작하게 한 그 은혜였어요.\""]], [["“Good God!”", "\"맙소사!\" 엠마가 외쳤다."], ["cried Emma, “this has been a most unfortunate—most deplorable mistake!—What is to be done?”", "\"이건 정말 불운하기 짝이 없는 — 가장 통탄할 오해였군요! 이를 어쩐담?\""]], [["“You would not have encouraged me, then, if you had understood me?", "\"제 말을 알아들으셨다면 부추기지 않으셨겠죠?"], ["At least, however, I cannot be worse off than I should have been, if the other had been the person; and now—it is possible—”", "그래도 적어도, 그 다른 분이 상대였을 때보다 제 처지가 더 나빠질 건 없어요. 그리고 이제는 — 가능한 일이에요—\""]], [["She paused a few moments.", "그녀는 잠시 말을 멈추었다."], ["Emma could not speak.", "엠마는 입을 뗄 수 없었다."]], [["“I do not wonder, Miss Woodhouse,”", "\"놀랍지 않아요, 미스 우드하우스,\""], ["she resumed, “that you should feel a great difference between the two, as to me or as to any body.", "그녀가 다시 입을 열었다. \"저에 관해서든 누구에 관해서든, 미스 우드하우스가 그 두 분 사이에 큰 차이를 느끼시는 게요."], ["You must think one five hundred million times more above me than the other.", "한 분이 다른 분보다 저보다 오억 배는 더 높은 분이라고 여기시겠죠."], ["But I hope, Miss Woodhouse, that supposing—that if—strange as it may appear—. But you know they were your own words, that more wonderful things had happened, matches of greater disparity had taken place than between Mr.", "하지만 바라건대, 미스 우드하우스, 만에 하나 — 만약에 — 이상하게 들릴지 몰라도 — 하지만 아시잖아요, 이보다 더 놀라운 일도 있었다고, 미스터 프랭크 처칠과 저 사이보다 더 큰 신분 차이를 넘은 혼사도 있었다고, 그건 미스 우드하우스가 직접 하신 말씀이에요."], ["Frank Churchill and me; and, therefore, it seems as if such a thing even as this, may have occurred before—and if I should be so fortunate, beyond expression, as to—if Mr. Knightley should really—if he does not mind the disparity, I hope, dear Miss Woodhouse, you will not set yourself against it, and try to put difficulties in the way.", "그러니 이 같은 일도 전에 일어난 적이 있는 것 같고 — 그러니 제가 이루 말할 수 없이 운이 좋아서 — 만약 미스터 나이틀리가 정말로 — 만약 그분이 신분 차이를 개의치 않으신다면, 바라건대, 미스 우드하우스, 이 일에 반대하시거나 길을 막으려 하지는 않으시겠죠."], ["But you are too good for that, I am sure.”", "하지만 미스 우드하우스는 그러기엔 너무 좋은 분이세요, 분명히요.\""]], [["Harriet was standing at one of the windows.", "해리엇은 창가 한쪽에 서 있었다."], ["Emma turned round to look at her in consternation, and hastily said,", "엠마는 경악하여 그녀를 돌아보며 다급히 물었다."]], [["“Have you any idea of Mr. Knightley’s returning your affection?”", "\"미스터 나이틀리가 당신의 마음에 화답하리라는 무슨 낌새라도 있나요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["replied Harriet modestly, but not fearfully—“I must say that I have.”", "해리엇이 겸손하게, 그러나 겁먹지 않고 대답했다. \"있다고 말씀드려야겠어요.\""]], [["Emma’s eyes were instantly withdrawn; and she sat silently meditating, in a fixed attitude, for a few minutes.", "엠마는 곧바로 눈길을 거두었다. 그리고 몇 분 동안 굳은 자세로 말없이 골똘히 생각에 잠겼다."], ["A few minutes were sufficient for making her acquainted with her own heart.", "자기 마음을 깨닫는 데는 몇 분이면 충분했다."], ["A mind like hers, once opening to suspicion, made rapid progress.", "그녀 같은 머리는 일단 의심의 문이 열리면 빠르게 나아갔다."], ["She touched—she admitted—she acknowledged the whole truth.", "그녀는 진실의 전모에 가닿았고, 그것을 받아들였고, 인정했다."], ["Why was it so much worse that Harriet should be in love with Mr. Knightley, than with Frank Churchill?", "해리엇이 프랭크 처칠보다 미스터 나이틀리를 사랑한다는 것이 어째서 이토록 더 나쁘단 말인가?"], ["Why was the evil so dreadfully increased by Harriet’s having some hope of a return?", "해리엇이 화답을 바랄 만한 무슨 희망을 품고 있다는 것이 어째서 그 화근을 이토록 끔찍하게 키운단 말인가?"], ["It darted through her, with the speed of an arrow, that Mr. Knightley must marry no one but herself!", "화살 같은 빠르기로 그녀를 꿰뚫고 지나간 생각은, 미스터 나이틀리는 자기 자신 말고는 그 누구와도 결혼해서는 안 된다는 것이었다!"]], [["Her own conduct, as well as her own heart, was before her in the same few minutes.", "바로 그 몇 분 사이에, 자기 마음과 더불어 자기 처신도 눈앞에 펼쳐졌다. 그녀는 일찍이 누려 본 적 없는 또렷함으로 그 모든 것을 보았다."], ["She saw it all with a clearness which had never blessed her before.", "해리엇에게 자기가 얼마나 그릇되게 처신해 왔던가! 얼마나 무심하고, 얼마나 무례하고, 얼마나 분별없고, 얼마나 매정한 처신이었던가!"], ["How improperly had she been acting by Harriet!", "어떤 맹목이, 어떤 광기가 자기를 여기까지 이끌었던가!"], ["How inconsiderate, how indelicate, how irrational, how unfeeling had been her conduct!", "그 깨달음은 무서운 힘으로 그녀를 강타했고, 그녀는 세상의 온갖 나쁜 이름을 다 갖다 붙일 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["What blindness, what madness, had led her on! It struck her with dreadful force, and she was ready to give it every bad name in the world.", "그러나 이 모든 허물에도 불구하고 자기 자신에 대한 얼마간의 존중심 — 자기 체면에 대한 약간의 염려와, 해리엇에 대한 강한 정의감이 (미스터 나이틀리에게 사랑받고 있다고 믿는 처녀에게 동정 따위는 필요 없었지만, 정의는 지금 어떤 냉대로도 그녀를 불행하게 만들어선 안 된다고 요구했다) 엠마에게 그대로 앉아, 침착하게, 심지어 겉으로는 다정하게 더 견뎌 낼 결심을 안겨 주었다."], ["Some portion of respect for herself, however, in spite of all these demerits—some concern for her own appearance, and a strong sense of justice by Harriet—(there would be no need of compassion to the girl who believed herself loved by Mr.", "사실 자기 자신을 위해서라도, 해리엇의 희망이 어디까지 뻗쳐 있는지를 캐물어 두는 것이 마땅했다. 게다가 해리엇은 그토록 자발적으로 베풀고 지켜 온 호의와 관심을 잃을 만한 짓을 한 것도 아니었고, 한 번도 그녀를 옳게 이끈 적 없는 사람에게 박대당할 만한 짓을 한 것도 아니었다."], ["Knightley—but justice required that she should not be made unhappy by any coldness now,) gave Emma the resolution to sit and endure farther with calmness, with even apparent kindness.—For her own advantage indeed, it was fit that the utmost extent of Harriet’s hopes should be enquired into; and Harriet had done nothing to forfeit the regard and interest which had been so voluntarily formed and maintained—or to deserve to be slighted by the person, whose counsels had never led her right.—Rousing from reflection, therefore, and subduing her emotion, she turned to Harriet again, and, in a more inviting accent, renewed the conversation; for as to the subject which had first introduced it, the wonderful story of Jane Fairfax, that was quite sunk and lost.—Neither of them thought but of Mr.", "그리하여 그녀는 상념에서 정신을 차리고 격한 감정을 누른 뒤, 다시 해리엇에게로 몸을 돌려, 한결 다정한 어조로 대화를 이었다. 애초에 이 대화를 끌어낸 화제, 곧 제인 페어팩스의 그 놀라운 사연으로 말하자면, 그것은 이미 까맣게 가라앉아 잊혀 버린 뒤였다."], ["Knightley and themselves.", "두 사람 다 미스터 나이틀리와 자기 자신 말고는 아무것도 머릿속에 없었다."]], [["Harriet, who had been standing in no unhappy reverie, was yet very glad to be called from it, by the now encouraging manner of such a judge, and such a friend as Miss Woodhouse, and only wanted invitation, to give the history of her hopes with great, though trembling delight.—Emma’s tremblings as she asked, and as she listened, were better concealed than Harriet’s, but they were not less. Her voice was not unsteady; but her mind was in all the perturbation that such a development of self, such a burst of threatening evil, such a confusion of sudden and perplexing emotions, must create.—She listened with much inward suffering, but with great outward patience, to Harriet’s detail.—Methodical, or well arranged, or very well delivered, it could not be expected to be; but it contained, when separated from all the feebleness and tautology of the narration, a substance to sink her spirit—especially with the corroborating circumstances, which her own memory brought in favour of Mr. Knightley’s most improved opinion of Harriet.", "그다지 불행하지 않은 몽상에 잠겨 서 있던 해리엇은, 미스 우드하우스 같은 심판자이자 벗이 이제 격려하는 태도로 자신을 그 몽상에서 불러내 주자 못내 기뻤고, 그저 권유만 바라던 차였다. 그리하여 떨리면서도 한껏 들뜬 기쁨으로 자기 희망의 내력을 풀어놓았다. 묻고 듣는 동안 엠마의 떨림은 해리엇의 것보다 잘 감추어졌으나, 결코 덜하지 않았다. 목소리는 흔들리지 않았다. 그러나 그녀의 마음은, 이런 식의 자기 발견과 이렇게 들이닥치는 위협, 갑작스럽고 당혹스러운 감정들의 혼란이 빚어낼 수밖에 없는 온갖 동요에 휩싸여 있었다. 그녀는 속으로 무던히 괴로워하면서도 겉으로는 큰 인내심을 발휘하며 해리엇의 자초지종을 들었다. 조리 있거나 잘 정돈되었거나 능숙하게 전달되리라고는 기대할 수 없는 이야기였다. 그러나 그 두서없고 군더더기 많은 서술에서 알맹이만 추려 내면, 그것은 그녀의 기운을 가라앉히기에 충분한 내용을 담고 있었다 — 특히 미스터 나이틀리가 해리엇을 한껏 좋게 보게 되었음을 뒷받침하는, 엠마 자신의 기억이 더해 주는 정황들과 더불어."]], [["Harriet had been conscious of a difference in his behaviour ever since those two decisive dances.—Emma knew that he had, on that occasion, found her much superior to his expectation. From that evening, or at least from the time of Miss Woodhouse’s encouraging her to think of him, Harriet had begun to be sensible of his talking to her much more than he had been used to do, and of his having indeed quite a different manner towards her; a manner of kindness and sweetness!—Latterly she had been more and more aware of it. When they had been all walking together, he had so often come and walked by her, and talked so very delightfully!—He seemed to want to be acquainted with her. Emma knew it to have been very much the case. She had often observed the change, to almost the same extent.—Harriet repeated expressions of approbation and praise from him—and Emma felt them to be in the closest agreement with what she had known of his opinion of Harriet. He praised her for being without art or affectation, for having simple, honest, generous, feelings.—She knew that he saw such recommendations in Harriet; he had dwelt on them to her more than once.—Much that lived in Harriet’s memory, many little particulars of the notice she had received from him, a look, a speech, a removal from one chair to another, a compliment implied, a preference inferred, had been unnoticed, because unsuspected, by Emma. Circumstances that might swell to half an hour’s relation, and contained multiplied proofs to her who had seen them, had passed undiscerned by her who now heard them; but the two latest occurrences to be mentioned, the two of strongest promise to Harriet, were not without some degree of witness from Emma herself.—The first, was his walking with her apart from the others, in the lime-walk at Donwell, where they had been walking some time before Emma came, and he had taken pains (as she was convinced) to draw her from the rest to himself—and at first, he had talked to her in a more particular way than he had ever done before, in a very particular way indeed!—(Harriet could not recall it without a blush.) He seemed to be almost asking her, whether her affections were engaged.—But as soon as she (Miss Woodhouse) appeared likely to join them, he changed the subject, and began talking about farming:—The second, was his having sat talking with her nearly half an hour before Emma came back from her visit, the very last morning of his being at Hartfield—though, when he first came in, he had said that he could not stay five minutes—and his having told her, during their conversation, that though he must go to London, it was very much against his inclination that he left home at all, which was much more (as Emma felt) than he had acknowledged to her. The superior degree of confidence towards Harriet, which this one article marked, gave her severe pain.", "해리엇은 그 결정적인 두 차례의 춤 이후로 줄곧 그의 태도에 달라진 점이 있음을 의식해 왔다고 했다. 엠마는 그가 그때 해리엇을 기대 이상으로 훌륭하다고 여겼다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그날 저녁부터, 아니면 적어도 미스 우드하우스가 그분을 마음에 두라고 부추긴 무렵부터, 해리엇은 그가 전보다 훨씬 자주 자기에게 말을 걸고, 자기를 대하는 태도가 정말이지 사뭇 달라졌음을 — 다정하고 상냥한 태도였다! — 느끼기 시작했다고 했다. 근래 들어 그녀는 그것을 점점 더 또렷이 알아차렸다. 다 함께 산책할 때면 그는 그토록 자주 다가와 그녀 곁에서 걸으며 그토록 즐겁게 이야기를 나누었다! 그는 그녀와 가까워지고 싶어 하는 듯했다. 엠마도 그것이 정말로 그러했음을 알고 있었다. 그녀 역시 그 변화를 거의 같은 정도로 자주 보아 왔던 것이다. 해리엇은 그가 인정하고 칭찬한 말들을 되풀이했고 — 엠마는 그것이 자신이 알고 있던 그의 해리엇에 대한 견해와 더없이 일치한다고 느꼈다. 그는 그녀가 꾸밈도 거짓도 없다는 점을, 소박하고 정직하고 너그러운 감성을 지녔다는 점을 칭찬했다. 그가 해리엇에게서 그런 미덕을 본다는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그는 한 번 이상 그녀 앞에서 그 점을 길게 말한 적이 있었다. 해리엇의 기억에 살아 있는 많은 것들, 그에게서 받은 관심의 자잘한 정황들 — 눈길 한 번, 말 한마디, 의자를 옮겨 앉은 일, 넌지시 비친 찬사, 미루어 짐작되는 편애 — 은 엠마가 의심하지 않은 탓에 알아차리지 못한 것들이었다. 그것을 본 사람에게는 숱한 증거를 품고 반 시간은 늘어놓을 수 있는 정황들이, 이제 그것을 듣는 사람에게는 미처 분간되지 못한 채 지나갔던 것이다. 그러나 언급된 가장 최근의 두 사건, 해리엇에게 가장 강한 약속처럼 보인 그 둘은, 엠마 자신도 얼마간 증언할 수 있는 일이었다. 첫째는, 돈웰의 라임나무 길에서 그가 그녀를 다른 사람들과 떨어뜨려 함께 거닐었던 일이었다. 엠마가 가기 전부터 두 사람은 한동안 거닐고 있었고, 그는 (그녀가 확신하기로) 일부러 그녀를 무리에서 떼어 자기 쪽으로 끌어내려 애썼던 것이다 — 그리고 처음에 그는 전에 없이, 정말이지 아주 각별한 투로 그녀에게 말을 걸었다! (해리엇은 얼굴을 붉히지 않고는 그 일을 떠올릴 수 없었다.) 그는 거의 그녀에게, 그녀의 마음이 누구에게 매여 있는지를 묻는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀가 (미스 우드하우스가) 합류할 낌새가 보이자마자, 그는 화제를 바꾸어 농사 이야기를 꺼냈다. 둘째는, 그가 하트필드에 머문 바로 그 마지막 날 아침, 엠마가 방문에서 돌아오기 전까지 거의 반 시간을 그녀와 마주 앉아 이야기를 나눈 일이었다 — 들어올 때만 해도 오 분도 못 머문다고 했으면서. 게다가 그는 대화 중에, 런던에 가야 하긴 하지만 집을 떠나는 것이 정말로 영 마음에 내키지 않는다고 그녀에게 말했다는 것이었다. 이는 (엠마가 느끼기에) 그가 자기에게 인정한 것보다 훨씬 더 깊은 속내였다. 이 한 가지 일에 드러난, 해리엇을 향한 더 큰 신뢰의 정도가 그녀에게 모진 아픔을 안겼다."]], [["On the subject of the first of the two circumstances, she did, after a little reflection, venture the following question.", "그 두 정황 가운데 첫째에 관하여, 그녀는 잠시 생각한 끝에 다음과 같은 물음을 감히 던져 보았다. \"혹시 그게 아닐까요?"], ["“Might he not?—Is not it possible, that when enquiring, as you thought, into the state of your affections, he might be alluding to Mr. Martin—he might have Mr.", "당신 생각엔 그분이 당신의 마음 상태를 캐물은 것 같지만, 사실은 미스터 마틴을 넌지시 가리킨 게 아닐까요 — 미스터 마틴의 이해를 염두에 두신 게 아닐까요?\""], ["Martin’s interest in view? But Harriet rejected the suspicion with spirit.", "그러나 해리엇은 그 의심을 단호히 물리쳤다."]], [["“Mr. Martin!", "\"미스터 마틴이요!"], ["No indeed!—There was not a hint of Mr. Martin.", "천만에요!"], ["I hope I know better now, than to care for Mr.", "미스터 마틴 얘기는 한마디도 없었어요."], ["Martin, or to be suspected of it.”", "이제는 미스터 마틴을 마음에 두거나, 그런 의심을 살 만큼 어리석진 않다고요.\""]], [["When Harriet had closed her evidence, she appealed to her dear Miss Woodhouse, to say whether she had not good ground for hope.", "해리엇은 증언을 마치자, 자기에게 희망을 품을 만한 근거가 충분하지 않으냐고 사랑하는 미스 우드하우스에게 판단을 구했다."]], [["“I never should have presumed to think of it at first,”", "\"처음엔 감히 그런 생각은 엄두도 못 냈을 거예요,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “but for you.", "\"미스 우드하우스가 아니었다면요."], ["You told me to observe him carefully, and let his behaviour be the rule of mine—and so I have.", "그분을 잘 살피고, 그분의 태도를 제 태도의 기준으로 삼으라고 하셨잖아요 — 그래서 그렇게 했어요."], ["But now I seem to feel that I may deserve him; and that if he does chuse me, it will not be any thing so very wonderful.”", "그런데 이제는 제가 그분을 가질 자격이 있을지도 모른다는 생각이 들어요. 그리고 만약 그분이 정말 저를 택하신다면, 그게 그렇게까지 놀라운 일은 아닐 거라고요.\""]], [["The bitter feelings occasioned by this speech, the many bitter feelings, made the utmost exertion necessary on Emma’s side, to enable her to say on reply,", "이 말이 불러일으킨 쓰디쓴 감정들, 그 숱한 쓰디쓴 감정들 탓에, 엠마는 안간힘을 다 짜내고서야 겨우 이렇게 대답할 수 있었다."]], [["“Harriet, I will only venture to declare, that Mr.", "\"해리엇, 이것만은 감히 단언할게요."], ["Knightley is the last man in the world, who would intentionally give any woman the idea of his feeling for her more than he really does.”", "미스터 나이틀리는 세상에서 어떤 여자에게든 자기 마음이 실제보다 더한 양 일부러 믿게 할 사람이 결코 못 되는 분이에요.\""]], [["Harriet seemed ready to worship her friend for a sentence so satisfactory; and Emma was only saved from raptures and fondness, which at that moment would have been dreadful penance, by the sound of her father’s footsteps.", "해리엇은 이토록 흡족한 한마디에 벗을 떠받들기라도 할 기세였다. 그리고 그 순간 끔찍한 고행이 되었을 황홀과 애틋함의 폭발에서 엠마를 구해 준 것은 아버지의 발소리였다."], ["He was coming through the hall.", "그는 현관을 지나오고 있었다."], ["Harriet was too much agitated to encounter him.", "해리엇은 너무 동요한 나머지 그와 마주칠 수가 없었다."], ["“She could not compose herself— Mr.", "\"도무지 마음을 가라앉힐 수 없으니 — 미스터 우드하우스가 놀라실 테니 — 가는 편이 낫겠어요.\""], ["Woodhouse would be alarmed—she had better go;”—with most ready encouragement from her friend, therefore, she passed off through another door—and the moment she was gone, this was the spontaneous burst of Emma’s feelings: “Oh God!", "그리하여 벗의 선선한 권유에 따라 그녀는 다른 문으로 빠져나갔다. 그리고 그녀가 떠난 순간, 엠마의 감정이 절로 터져 나왔다."], ["that I had never seen her!”", "\"오, 하느님! 차라리 저 애를 만나지나 말 것을!\""]], [["The rest of the day, the following night, were hardly enough for her thoughts.—She was bewildered amidst the confusion of all that had rushed on her within the last few hours. Every moment had brought a fresh surprize; and every surprize must be matter of humiliation to her.—How to understand it all! How to understand the deceptions she had been thus practising on herself, and living under!—The blunders, the blindness of her own head and heart!—she sat still, she walked about, she tried her own room, she tried the shrubbery—in every place, every posture, she perceived that she had acted most weakly; that she had been imposed on by others in a most mortifying degree; that she had been imposing on herself in a degree yet more mortifying; that she was wretched, and should probably find this day but the beginning of wretchedness.", "그날의 남은 시간과 그날 밤은 그녀의 생각을 다 담기에도 모자랐다. 그녀는 지난 몇 시간 사이에 들이닥친 모든 일의 혼란 속에서 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 매 순간이 새로운 놀라움을 안겼고, 그 모든 놀라움은 그녀에게 굴욕거리가 될 수밖에 없었다. 이 모든 것을 어찌 이해한단 말인가! 자기가 이토록 자신에게 부려 온, 그 안에서 살아온 기만들을 어찌 이해한단 말인가! 제 머리와 마음의 그 실수들, 그 맹목이라니! 그녀는 가만히 앉아도 보고, 이리저리 거닐어도 보고, 제 방으로도 가 보고, 관목 숲으로도 나가 보았다 — 어느 자리에서든, 어떤 자세로든, 그녀는 자기가 더없이 나약하게 처신했음을, 더없이 굴욕적인 정도로 남에게 속아 왔음을, 그보다 더 굴욕적인 정도로 자기 자신을 속여 왔음을, 자기가 비참하며 아마도 오늘이 비참함의 시작에 지나지 않으리라는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["To understand, thoroughly understand her own heart, was the first endeavour.", "자기 마음을 이해하는 것, 철저히 이해하는 것이 첫 번째 노력이었다."], ["To that point went every leisure moment which her father’s claims on her allowed, and every moment of involuntary absence of mind.", "아버지의 부름이 허락하는 모든 한가한 순간이, 그리고 저도 모르게 정신이 딴 데로 흐르는 모든 순간이 그 한 점으로 향했다."]], [["How long had Mr. Knightley been so dear to her, as every feeling declared him now to be?", "미스터 나이틀리가 지금 모든 감정이 한목소리로 일러 주듯 그토록 소중한 사람이 된 지는 언제부터였던가?"], ["When had his influence, such influence begun?— When had he succeeded to that place in her affection, which Frank Churchill had once, for a short period, occupied?—She looked back; she compared the two—compared them, as they had always stood in her estimation, from the time of the latter’s becoming known to her—and as they must at any time have been compared by her, had it—oh!", "그의 영향력이, 그토록 큰 영향력이 시작된 것은 언제부터였던가? 한때 잠시 프랭크 처칠이 차지했던 그 자리를, 그가 그녀의 애정 속에서 물려받은 것은 언제부터였던가?"], ["had it, by any blessed felicity, occurred to her, to institute the comparison.—She saw that there never had been a time when she did not consider Mr.", "그녀는 지난날을 돌아보았다. 두 사람을 견주어 보았다 — 후자를 알게 된 이후로 그녀의 평가 속에 늘 서 있던 그대로 견주어 보았다 — 그리고 만약 그녀가, 아아!"], ["Knightley as infinitely the superior, or when his regard for her had not been infinitely the most dear.", "어떤 복된 행운으로든 그 비교를 해 볼 생각이라도 들었더라면 언제든 그렇게 견주었을 그대로."], ["She saw, that in persuading herself, in fancying, in acting to the contrary, she had been entirely under a delusion, totally ignorant of her own heart—and, in short, that she had never really cared for Frank Churchill at all!", "그녀는 미스터 나이틀리를 한없이 우월한 사람으로 여기지 않은 때가, 그의 호의를 한없이 가장 소중한 것으로 여기지 않은 때가 단 한 번도 없었음을 깨달았다. 그녀는 자기가 스스로를 설득하고, 공상하고, 그 반대로 행동하면서 줄곧 망상에 빠져 자기 마음을 까맣게 모르고 있었음을 — 한마디로, 프랭크 처칠 따위는 애초에 정말로 마음에 둔 적이 한 번도 없었음을 깨달았다."]], [["This was the conclusion of the first series of reflection.", "이것이 첫 번째 상념의 결론이었다. 이것이, 첫 번째 물음에 관하여 그녀가 도달한 자기 자신에 대한 앎이었다 — 그것도 오래 걸리지 않고 도달한."], ["This was the knowledge of herself, on the first question of inquiry, which she reached; and without being long in reaching it.—She was most sorrowfully indignant; ashamed of every sensation but the one revealed to her—her affection for Mr.", "그녀는 더없이 비통하게 분개했다. 자기에게 드러난 단 하나의 감정 — 미스터 나이틀리에 대한 애정 — 을 빼면 모든 감정이 부끄러웠다."], ["Knightley.—Every other part of her mind was disgusting.", "그녀 마음의 다른 모든 부분이 역겨웠다."]], [["With insufferable vanity had she believed herself in the secret of every body’s feelings; with unpardonable arrogance proposed to arrange every body’s destiny.", "견딜 수 없는 허영심으로 그녀는 누구의 마음이든 다 꿰뚫어 안다고 믿었다. 용서받을 수 없는 오만으로 누구의 운명이든 다 짜 맞추려 들었다."], ["She was proved to have been universally mistaken; and she had not quite done nothing—for she had done mischief.", "그녀가 두루 틀렸음이 드러났고, 게다가 아무 일도 안 한 것조차 아니었다 — 화근을 만들었으니까. 그녀는 해리엇에게, 자기 자신에게, 그리고 못내 두렵게도 미스터 나이틀리에게까지 화를 끼친 것이다."], ["She had brought evil on Harriet, on herself, and she too much feared, on Mr.", "이 더없이 어울리지 않는 결합이 정말로 이루어진다면, 그 시작을 만들었다는 모든 비난은 그녀에게 돌아갈 것이었다."], ["Knightley.—Were this most unequal of all connexions to take place, on her must rest all the reproach of having given it a beginning; for his attachment, she must believe to be produced only by a consciousness of Harriet’s;—and even were this not the case, he would never have known Harriet at all but for her folly.", "그의 연정으로 말하자면, 오직 해리엇의 마음을 의식한 데서 생겨난 것이라고 믿을 수밖에 없었으니까. 그리고 설령 그렇지 않다 해도, 그녀의 어리석음이 아니었다면 그는 애초에 해리엇을 알지도 못했을 것이었다."]], [["Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리와 해리엇 스미스라니!"], ["Knightley and Harriet Smith!—It was a union to distance every wonder of the kind.—The attachment of Frank Churchill and Jane Fairfax became commonplace, threadbare, stale in the comparison, exciting no surprize, presenting no disparity, affording nothing to be said or thought.—Mr.", "그것은 이런 종류의 온갖 놀라움을 멀찌감치 따돌리는 결합이었다. 프랭크 처칠과 제인 페어팩스의 연정 따위는 이에 견주면 진부하고, 낡고, 김빠진 것이 되어 — 놀라움을 자아내지도, 신분 차이를 드러내지도, 입에 올리거나 생각할 거리를 내놓지도 못했다."], ["Knightley and Harriet Smith!—Such an elevation on her side!", "미스터 나이틀리와 해리엇 스미스라니! 그쪽엔 이 무슨 높이뛰기며!"], ["Such a debasement on his!", "그분에겐 이 무슨 추락이란 말인가! 이 일이 세상의 평판 속에서 그를 얼마나 끌어내릴지, 그를 두고 일어날 비웃음과 조롱과 웃음거리를, 그의 동생의 굴욕과 멸시를, 그 자신에게 닥칠 천 가지 곤란을 미리 그려 보는 것이 엠마에게는 끔찍했다."], ["It was horrible to Emma to think how it must sink him in the general opinion, to foresee the smiles, the sneers, the merriment it would prompt at his expense; the mortification and disdain of his brother, the thousand inconveniences to himself.—Could it be?—No; it was impossible.", "그럴 수 있는 일일까? 아니, 불가능한 일이었다."], ["And yet it was far, very far, from impossible.—Was it a new circumstance for a man of first-rate abilities to be captivated by very inferior powers?", "그러나 그것은 불가능과는 멀고, 아주 멀었다. 일류의 능력을 지닌 남자가 한참 못한 재간에 사로잡히는 것이 새삼스러운 일이던가?"], ["Was it new for one, perhaps too busy to seek, to be the prize of a girl who would seek him?—Was it new for any thing in this world to be unequal, inconsistent, incongruous—or for chance and circumstance (as second causes) to direct the human fate?", "어쩌면 너무 바빠 짝을 찾을 겨를도 없던 남자가, 제 발로 찾아 나선 처녀의 전리품이 되는 것이 새삼스러운 일이던가? 이 세상에서 어울리지 않고, 모순되고, 부조화한 무언가가 있다는 것이, 또는 우연과 정황이 (제이의 원인으로서) 인간의 운명을 좌우한다는 것이 새삼스러운 일이던가?"]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["had she never brought Harriet forward!", "애초에 해리엇을 끌어들이지나 말았더라면!"], ["Had she left her where she ought, and where he had told her she ought!—Had she not, with a folly which no tongue could express, prevented her marrying the unexceptionable young man who would have made her happy and respectable in the line of life to which she ought to belong—all would have been safe; none of this dreadful sequel would have been.", "그녀가 마땅히 있어야 할 자리, 그분이 그녀에게 있어야 한다고 일러 준 그 자리에 그녀를 그냥 두었더라면! 그 어떤 말로도 표현할 수 없는 어리석음으로, 그녀가 응당 속해야 할 처지에서 그녀를 행복하고 떳떳하게 해 주었을 나무랄 데 없는 젊은이와의 혼사를 가로막지나 않았더라면 — 모든 것이 무사했을 것이고, 이 끔찍한 후일담 따위는 하나도 없었으리라."]], [["How Harriet could ever have had the presumption to raise her thoughts to Mr.", "해리엇이 어떻게 감히 미스터 나이틀리에게까지 마음을 끌어올릴 엄두를 냈단 말인가! 어떻게 그런 분의 간택을 받았다고, 확실히 확인되기도 전에 감히 공상할 수 있단 말인가!"], ["Knightley!—How she could dare to fancy herself the chosen of such a man till actually assured of it!—But Harriet was less humble, had fewer scruples than formerly.—Her inferiority, whether of mind or situation, seemed little felt.—She had seemed more sensible of Mr.", "그러나 해리엇은 예전보다 덜 겸손하고, 거리낌도 덜했다. 정신으로든 처지로든 자신이 못하다는 것을 거의 느끼지 못하는 듯했다."], ["Elton’s being to stoop in marrying her, than she now seemed of Mr.", "미스터 엘턴이 자기와 결혼하는 것이 격을 낮추는 일임은 제법 의식하던 그녀가, 이제 미스터 나이틀리에 관해서는 그조차 의식하지 못하는 듯했다. 아아!"], ["Knightley’s.—Alas!", "그것도 그녀 자신이 한 짓이 아니던가?"], ["was not that her own doing too?", "해리엇에게 제 잘난 줄 아는 생각을 애써 심어 준 것이 그녀 말고 누구였던가?"], ["Who had been at pains to give Harriet notions of self-consequence but herself?—Who but herself had taught her, that she was to elevate herself if possible, and that her claims were great to a high worldly establishment?—If Harriet, from being humble, were grown vain, it was her doing too.", "가능하다면 더 높이 올라가야 한다고, 세상에서 높은 자리에 오를 자격이 충분하다고 그녀에게 가르친 것이 그녀 자신 말고 누구였던가? 해리엇이 겸손하던 데서 허영에 차게 되었다면, 그것 또한 그녀가 한 짓이었다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter XII", "# 제3권 12장"]], [["Till now that she was threatened with its loss, Emma had never known how much of her happiness depended on being first with Mr.", "이제껏, 그것을 잃을지도 모른다는 위협에 처하기 전까지, 엠마는 자신의 행복이 미스터 나이틀리에게 가장 첫째가는 사람이 되는 일 — 그의 관심과 애정에서 첫째가는 일 — 에 얼마나 많이 기대고 있었는지 미처 알지 못했다. 그것이 그러하다는 데 만족하고, 그것을 제 몫으로 여기며, 그녀는 별다른 생각 없이 그것을 누려 왔다."], ["Knightley, first in interest and affection.—Satisfied that it was so, and feeling it her due, she had enjoyed it without reflection; and only in the dread of being supplanted, found how inexpressibly important it had been.—Long, very long, she felt she had been first; for, having no female connexions of his own, there had been only Isabella whose claims could be compared with hers, and she had always known exactly how far he loved and esteemed Isabella.", "오직 누군가에게 그 자리를 빼앗길지도 모른다는 두려움 속에서야, 그것이 얼마나 형언할 수 없이 소중했는지를 깨달았던 것이다. 오래도록, 아주 오래도록, 자신이 첫째였다고 그녀는 느꼈다."], ["She had herself been first with him for many years past.", "그에게는 여자 친척이 따로 없었기에, 그녀의 자리에 견줄 권리를 가진 이라곤 이저벨라뿐이었고, 그가 이저벨라를 어느 만큼 사랑하고 존중하는지는 그녀가 늘 정확히 알고 있었다. 지난 여러 해 동안 그녀 자신이 그에게 첫째가는 사람이었다."], ["She had not deserved it; she had often been negligent or perverse, slighting his advice, or even wilfully opposing him, insensible of half his merits, and quarrelling with him because he would not acknowledge her false and insolent estimate of her own—but still, from family attachment and habit, and thorough excellence of mind, he had loved her, and watched over her from a girl, with an endeavour to improve her, and an anxiety for her doing right, which no other creature had at all shared.", "그럴 자격이 있었던 것은 아니었다. 그녀는 종종 그를 소홀히 했고 어깃장을 놓았으며, 그의 조언을 무시하고 심지어 일부러 그를 거스르기까지 했다."], ["In spite of all her faults, she knew she was dear to him; might she not say, very dear?—When the suggestions of hope, however, which must follow here, presented themselves, she could not presume to indulge them.", "그의 미덕을 절반은 알아보지 못했고, 제 잘난 착각과 오만한 자평을 그가 인정해 주지 않는다고 다투기도 했다. 그래도 가족 같은 정과 오랜 습관, 그리고 더없이 훌륭한 마음씨로, 그는 그녀를 사랑해 왔으며, 어린 소녀였을 적부터 그녀를 지켜보아 왔다 — 그녀를 더 나은 사람으로 만들려는 노력과, 그녀가 옳게 행하기를 바라는 염려로써."], ["Harriet Smith might think herself not unworthy of being peculiarly, exclusively, passionately loved by Mr.", "그 누구도 그런 마음을 조금이라도 함께 나눈 적이 없었다. 온갖 결점에도 불구하고, 자신이 그에게 소중한 사람임을 그녀는 알았다."], ["Knightley. She could not.", "어쩌면 아주 소중하다고 말해도 되지 않을까?"], ["She could not flatter herself with any idea of blindness in his attachment to her.", "— 그러나 여기서 뒤따라 떠오를 수밖에 없는 희망의 속삭임이 고개를 들 때면, 그녀는 감히 그것에 빠져들 엄두를 내지 못했다. 해리엇 스미스라면 미스터 나이틀리에게서 각별히, 오직 자기만을, 열렬히 사랑받기에 모자라지 않다고 스스로 여길 수 있을지 모른다."], ["She had received a very recent proof of its impartiality.—How shocked had he been by her behaviour to Miss Bates!", "하지만 그녀는 그럴 수 없었다. 그가 자신에게 품은 애정에 어떤 눈먼 구석이 있으리라는 생각으로 자위할 수는 없었다."], ["How directly, how strongly had he expressed himself to her on the subject!—Not too strongly for the offence—but far, far too strongly to issue from any feeling softer than upright justice and clear-sighted goodwill.—She had no hope, nothing to deserve the name of hope, that he could have that sort of affection for herself which was now in question; but there was a hope (at times a slight one, at times much stronger,) that Harriet might have deceived herself, and be overrating his regard for her.—Wish it she must, for his sake—be the consequence nothing to herself, but his remaining single all his life.", "그것이 얼마나 공평무사한지를 보여 주는 증거를 그녀는 바로 얼마 전에 받지 않았던가. 미스 베이츠를 대한 자기 행동에 그가 얼마나 충격을 받았던가!"], ["Could she be secure of that, indeed, of his never marrying at all, she believed she should be perfectly satisfied.—Let him but continue the same Mr.", "그 일을 두고 그가 그녀에게 얼마나 거리낌 없이, 얼마나 단호하게 제 뜻을 밝혔던가! 그 무례에 비추어 지나친 말은 아니었으나 — 곧고 올바른 정의감과 맑은 눈을 가진 선의보다 부드러운 어떤 감정에서 나온 것이라고 보기에는, 훨씬, 훨씬 지나치게 단호했다."], ["Knightley to her and her father, the same Mr.", "지금 문제가 되고 있는 그런 종류의 애정을 그가 자신에게 품을 수 있으리라는 희망은 그녀에게 없었다. 희망이라는 이름에 값할 만한 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["Knightley to all the world; let Donwell and Hartfield lose none of their precious intercourse of friendship and confidence, and her peace would be fully secured.—Marriage, in fact, would not do for her.", "다만 한 가지 희망이 있었으니 (어떤 때는 희미하고, 어떤 때는 훨씬 강한), 해리엇이 스스로를 속이고 있는 것일지도 모른다는, 그가 자기에게 보이는 관심을 과대평가하고 있는 것일지도 모른다는 희망이었다. 그렇게 되기를 그녀는 바라야만 했다 — 그를 위해서 — 설령 그 결과로 그녀 자신에게 남는 것이 그가 평생 독신으로 지내는 일뿐이라 해도."], ["It would be incompatible with what she owed to her father, and with what she felt for him.", "정말이지 그것만 확실하다면, 그가 결코 누구와도 결혼하지 않으리라는 것만 확실하다면, 그녀는 더없이 만족하리라 믿었다. 그가 자신과 아버지에게 변함없는 미스터 나이틀리로, 온 세상에 변함없는 미스터 나이틀리로 남아 주기만 한다면."], ["Nothing should separate her from her father.", "돈웰과 하트필드가 그 귀한 우정과 신뢰의 왕래를 조금도 잃지 않기만 한다면, 그녀의 평온은 온전히 지켜질 터였다. 사실 결혼이란 그녀에게 어울리지 않는 일이었다."], ["She would not marry, even if she were asked by Mr.", "그것은 아버지에게 진 의무와도, 아버지를 향한 마음과도 양립할 수 없었다. 그 무엇도 그녀를 아버지에게서 떼어 놓아서는 안 되었다."], ["Knightley.", "설령 미스터 나이틀리가 청혼한다 해도, 그녀는 결혼하지 않으리라."]], [["It must be her ardent wish that Harriet might be disappointed; and she hoped, that when able to see them together again, she might at least be able to ascertain what the chances for it were.—She should see them henceforward with the closest observance; and wretchedly as she had hitherto misunderstood even those she was watching, she did not know how to admit that she could be blinded here.—He was expected back every day. The power of observation would be soon given—frightfully soon it appeared when her thoughts were in one course. In the meanwhile, she resolved against seeing Harriet.—It would do neither of them good, it would do the subject no good, to be talking of it farther.—She was resolved not to be convinced, as long as she could doubt, and yet had no authority for opposing Harriet’s confidence. To talk would be only to irritate.—She wrote to her, therefore, kindly, but decisively, to beg that she would not, at present, come to Hartfield; acknowledging it to be her conviction, that all farther confidential discussion of one topic had better be avoided; and hoping, that if a few days were allowed to pass before they met again, except in the company of others—she objected only to a tête-à-tête—they might be able to act as if they had forgotten the conversation of yesterday.—Harriet submitted, and approved, and was grateful.", "해리엇이 실망하게 되기를 그녀는 간절히 바랄 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 두 사람을 다시 함께 볼 수 있게 되면, 적어도 그럴 가망이 얼마나 되는지는 가늠해 볼 수 있으리라 기대했다. 앞으로는 더없이 면밀하게 두 사람을 지켜보리라. 지금껏 지켜보던 이들조차 비참하리만치 잘못 읽어 왔으면서도, 이번 일에서는 자신이 눈멀 수 있다는 것을 그녀는 도무지 인정할 수 없었다. 그가 날마다 돌아오기를 기다리고 있었다. 머지않아 관찰할 기회가 주어질 터였다 — 그녀의 생각이 어느 한 갈래로 흐를 때면, 그 머지않음이 끔찍하게 느껴졌다. 그동안에는 해리엇을 만나지 않기로 마음먹었다. 이 일을 더 이야기해 봐야 두 사람 누구에게도 득이 되지 않을 것이요, 이 문제에도 득이 될 게 없었다. 의심할 여지가 남아 있는 한 그녀는 납득당하지 않기로 작정했으나, 그렇다고 해리엇의 확신에 맞설 근거도 없었다. 말을 나눠 봐야 부아만 돋울 뿐이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 해리엇에게 다정하되 단호한 편지를 써서, 당분간은 하트필드에 오지 말아 달라고 청했다. 어떤 한 가지 주제를 두고 더 이상 은밀한 의논을 나누는 일은 피하는 편이 낫다는 것이 자신의 확신임을 인정하면서, 며칠이 지난 뒤에 — 다른 사람들이 함께 있는 자리가 아니라면 — 다시 만난다면 (그녀가 꺼리는 것은 오직 단둘이 마주하는 일뿐이었다), 어제 나눈 대화 따위는 잊은 듯이 행동할 수 있으리라 기대한다고 적었다. 해리엇은 순순히 따랐고, 옳다고 여겼으며, 고마워했다."]], [["This point was just arranged, when a visitor arrived to tear Emma’s thoughts a little from the one subject which had engrossed them, sleeping or waking, the last twenty-four hours—Mrs.", "이 문제가 막 정리되었을 때, 손님 하나가 찾아와, 자나 깨나 지난 스물네 시간 동안 그녀의 마음을 사로잡고 있던 단 하나의 주제에서 엠마의 생각을 조금이나마 떼어 놓았다."], ["Weston, who had been calling on her daughter-in-law elect, and took Hartfield in her way home, almost as much in duty to Emma as in pleasure to herself, to relate all the particulars of so interesting an interview.", "다름 아닌 웨스턴 부인이었으니, 장차 며느리가 될 사람을 보고 오는 길에 집으로 돌아가는 도중 하트필드에 들른 것이었다. 그토록 흥미로운 만남의 자초지종을 죄다 들려주려는 마음에서였는데, 그것은 제 즐거움을 위해서이기도 했지만 엠마에 대한 의무에서이기도 했다."]], [["Mr. Weston had accompanied her to Mrs.", "미스터 웨스턴이 베이츠 부인 댁까지 부인과 동행하여, 이 긴요한 예방에서 자기 몫을 더없이 훌륭하게 해냈다."], ["Bates’s, and gone through his share of this essential attention most handsomely; but she having then induced Miss Fairfax to join her in an airing, was now returned with much more to say, and much more to say with satisfaction, than a quarter of an hour spent in Mrs. Bates’s parlour, with all the encumbrance of awkward feelings, could have afforded.", "그런 뒤 부인은 미스 페어팩스를 꾀어 함께 바람을 쐬러 나갔다가 돌아온 참이라, 어색한 감정의 거추장스러움을 죄다 안은 채 베이츠 부인의 응접실에서 보낸 십오 분이 줄 수 있었던 것보다 훨씬 많은 이야깃거리를, 그것도 훨씬 흡족하게 전할 이야깃거리를 가지고 온 것이었다."]], [["A little curiosity Emma had; and she made the most of it while her friend related.", "엠마에게도 얼마간의 호기심은 있었다. 그래서 벗이 이야기를 들려주는 동안 그 호기심을 한껏 살렸다."], ["Mrs. Weston had set off to pay the visit in a good deal of agitation herself; and in the first place had wished not to go at all at present, to be allowed merely to write to Miss Fairfax instead, and to defer this ceremonious call till a little time had passed, and Mr.", "웨스턴 부인 자신도 적잖이 마음을 졸이며 그 방문에 나섰다."], ["Churchill could be reconciled to the engagement’s becoming known; as, considering every thing, she thought such a visit could not be paid without leading to reports:—but Mr.", "우선은 지금 당장 가지 않았으면 했고, 대신 미스 페어팩스에게 편지만 쓰도록 허락받기를, 그리고 이 격식 차린 방문은 시간이 조금 흘러 미스터 처칠이 약혼이 알려지는 일에 마음을 누그러뜨릴 수 있을 때까지 미루기를 바랐던 것이다."], ["Weston had thought differently; he was extremely anxious to shew his approbation to Miss Fairfax and her family, and did not conceive that any suspicion could be excited by it; or if it were, that it would be of any consequence; for “such things,”", "여러모로 헤아려 볼 때, 그런 방문은 소문을 낳지 않고서는 이뤄질 수 없다고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["he observed, “always got about.” Emma smiled, and felt that Mr.", "그러나 미스터 웨스턴의 생각은 달랐다."], ["Weston had very good reason for saying so. They had gone, in short—and very great had been the evident distress and confusion of the lady.", "그는 미스 페어팩스와 그 식구들에게 자신의 흡족함을 보이고 싶은 마음이 간절했고, 그 일로 어떤 의심이 일 수 있으리라고는 생각하지 않았다."], ["She had hardly been able to speak a word, and every look and action had shewn how deeply she was suffering from consciousness.", "설령 의심이 인다 한들 대수로울 게 없다고 여겼으니, \"그런 일이야,\" 그가 한 말로는, \"어차피 다 퍼지게 마련이라\"는 것이었다."], ["The quiet, heart-felt satisfaction of the old lady, and the rapturous delight of her daughter—who proved even too joyous to talk as usual, had been a gratifying, yet almost an affecting, scene.", "엠마는 빙긋 웃었고, 미스터 웨스턴이 그렇게 말할 만한 근거가 충분하다고 느꼈다. 어쨌든 그들은 갔던 것이다 — 그리고 그 아가씨가 내비친 곤혹과 당혹은 누가 보아도 대단했다."], ["They were both so truly respectable in their happiness, so disinterested in every sensation; thought so much of Jane; so much of every body, and so little of themselves, that every kindly feeling was at work for them.", "그녀는 거의 한마디도 입에 올리지 못했고, 눈길 하나 몸짓 하나에 자의식 때문에 얼마나 깊이 괴로워하는지가 죄다 드러났다. 그 노부인의 잔잔하고 진심 어린 만족과, 평소처럼 말을 잇지 못할 만큼 들떠 버린 그 딸의 황홀한 기쁨은, 흐뭇하면서도 거의 가슴 뭉클한 광경이었다."], ["Miss Fairfax’s recent illness had offered a fair plea for Mrs. Weston to invite her to an airing; she had drawn back and declined at first, but, on being pressed had yielded; and, in the course of their drive, Mrs.", "두 사람은 그 행복 속에서 어찌나 진실로 존경스러웠고, 그 모든 감정에 어찌나 사심이 없었던지, 제 생각은 그토록 적게 하면서 제인을 그토록 많이, 또 누구든 그토록 많이 생각하니, 온갖 다정한 마음이 그들을 향해 절로 움직였다."], ["Weston had, by gentle encouragement, overcome so much of her embarrassment, as to bring her to converse on the important subject.", "미스 페어팩스가 최근에 앓았던 일은 웨스턴 부인이 함께 바람을 쐬러 가자고 청할 그럴듯한 구실이 되어 주었다. 처음에는 뒷걸음치며 사양했으나, 거듭 권하자 응했다."], ["Apologies for her seemingly ungracious silence in their first reception, and the warmest expressions of the gratitude she was always feeling towards herself and Mr.", "그리고 마차로 도는 동안 웨스턴 부인은 부드럽게 격려하여 그녀의 어색함을 어지간히 풀어 주었고, 마침내 그 중대한 주제를 두고 이야기를 나누게끔 이끌었다."], ["Weston, must necessarily open the cause; but when these effusions were put by, they had talked a good deal of the present and of the future state of the engagement. Mrs.", "처음 맞이할 적에 보인 무례해 보이는 침묵에 대한 사과며, 자신과 미스터 웨스턴을 향해 늘 품고 있던 고마움의 더없이 따뜻한 표현이, 자연히 이야기의 물꼬를 텄을 것이다."], ["Weston was convinced that such conversation must be the greatest relief to her companion, pent up within her own mind as every thing had so long been, and was very much pleased with all that she had said on the subject.", "그러나 그런 토로가 일단락되자, 두 사람은 약혼의 현재와 앞날에 관해 많은 이야기를 나누었다. 모든 것이 그토록 오래 마음속에 갇혀 있던 동행에게는 그런 대화가 더없는 위안이 되리라고 웨스턴 부인은 확신했으며, 그 주제에 관해 그녀가 한 말이 모두 무척 마음에 들었다."]], [["“On the misery of what she had suffered, during the concealment of so many months,”", "\"그토록 여러 달 동안 숨기고 지내는 사이에 자기가 겪은 그 고통에 대해 말이지,\" 웨스턴 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["continued Mrs.", "\"그 애는 격하게 토로하더구나."], ["Weston, “she was energetic.", "이건 그 애가 한 말 그대로야."], ["This was one of her expressions.", "'약혼한 뒤로 행복한 순간이 더러는 없었다고는 못 하겠어요."], ["‘I will not say, that since I entered into the engagement I have not had some happy moments; but I can say, that I have never known the blessing of one tranquil hour:’—and the quivering lip, Emma, which uttered it, was an attestation that I felt at my heart.”", "하지만 단 한 시간의 평온한 축복도 누려 본 적이 없다는 건 말할 수 있어요.' — 엠마, 그 말을 내뱉던 떨리는 입술이, 내 가슴으로 느낀 하나의 증거였단다.\""]], [["“Poor girl!”", "\"가엾어라!\""], ["said Emma.", "엠마가 말했다."], ["“She thinks herself wrong, then, for having consented to a private engagement?”", "\"그럼 그분은 은밀한 약혼에 응했던 걸 잘못이라고 여기는 거군요?\""]], [["“Wrong!", "\"잘못이라니!"], ["No one, I believe, can blame her more than she is disposed to blame herself.", "그 애가 스스로를 나무라는 만큼 그 애를 나무랄 수 있는 사람은 아무도 없을 거다. '그 결과로,'"], ["‘The consequence,’ said she, ‘has been a state of perpetual suffering to me; and so it ought.", "그 애가 그러더구나, '저는 끝없는 고통의 상태에 놓였어요. 그리고 마땅히 그래야 했고요."], ["But after all the punishment that misconduct can bring, it is still not less misconduct.", "하지만 잘못된 행실이 가져올 수 있는 온갖 벌을 다 받는다 해도, 잘못된 행실인 건 매한가지예요. 고통이 속죄가 되지는 않아요."], ["Pain is no expiation.", "저는 결코 죄 없는 사람이 될 수 없어요."], ["I never can be blameless.", "저는 옳고 그름에 대한 제 모든 감각을 거슬러 행동해 왔어요."], ["I have been acting contrary to all my sense of right; and the fortunate turn that every thing has taken, and the kindness I am now receiving, is what my conscience tells me ought not to be.’ ‘Do not imagine, madam,’ she continued, ‘that I was taught wrong.", "모든 일이 다행스럽게 풀려 가는 것도, 제가 지금 받고 있는 친절도, 제 양심이 말하기로는 받아서는 안 될 것들이에요.' '부인,'"], ["Do not let any reflection fall on the principles or the care of the friends who brought me up.", "그 애가 이어서 말하길, '제가 잘못 배웠다고는 생각지 마세요. 저를 길러 주신 분들의 가르침이나 보살핌에 어떤 허물도 돌리지 말아 주세요."], ["The error has been all my own; and I do assure you that, with all the excuse that present circumstances may appear to give, I shall yet dread making the story known to Colonel Campbell.’”", "잘못은 온전히 제 것이에요. 그리고 정말이지, 지금의 형편이 베풀어 주는 듯한 온갖 변명거리에도 불구하고, 저는 이 일을 캠벨 대령께 알리는 것이 여전히 두려워요.'\""]], [["“Poor girl!”", "\"가엾어라!\""], ["said Emma again.", "엠마가 다시 말했다."], ["“She loves him then excessively, I suppose.", "\"그럼 그분이 그를 지나치게 사랑하는 거겠죠, 아마."], ["It must have been from attachment only, that she could be led to form the engagement.", "오직 연정 때문에만 그 약혼을 맺기에 이르렀을 거예요."], ["Her affection must have overpowered her judgment.”", "애정이 판단을 압도해 버린 게 분명해요.\""]], [["“Yes, I have no doubt of her being extremely attached to him.”", "\"그래, 그 애가 그를 더없이 사랑하는 건 의심할 여지가 없지.\""]], [["“I am afraid,”", "\"아무래도,\""], ["returned Emma, sighing, “that I must often have contributed to make her unhappy.”", "엠마가 한숨을 쉬며 답했다. \"제가 그분을 불행하게 만드는 데 자주 한몫했던 것 같아요.\""]], [["“On your side, my love, it was very innocently done.", "\"네 쪽에서야, 얘야, 아주 악의 없이 한 일이지."], ["But she probably had something of that in her thoughts, when alluding to the misunderstandings which he had given us hints of before.", "하지만 그 애가 전에 그가 우리한테 슬쩍 흘렸던 그 오해들을 들먹였을 때, 아마 그 마음속에 그런 게 얼마쯤 있었던 게다. 자기가 빠져든 그 잘못이 낳은 한 가지 당연한 결과는, 그 애가 한 말로는, 자기를 사리에 어긋나는 사람으로 만들었다는 거야."], ["One natural consequence of the evil she had involved herself in,”", "잘못을 저질렀다는 자의식이 그 애를 숱한 불안에 내몰았고, 그래서 그 애를 그가 견디기 힘들 만큼 — 정말로 견디기 힘들 만큼 — 트집 잡고 짜증 내는 사람으로 만들어 버렸다는 거지. '저는,'"], ["she said, “was that of making her unreasonable.", "그 애가 그러더구나, '그분의 기질과 기분을 마땅히 헤아렸어야 했는데 그러질 못했어요 — 그분의 그 유쾌한 기분과, 그 명랑함, 그 장난기 어린 성품을요."], ["The consciousness of having done amiss, had exposed her to a thousand inquietudes, and made her captious and irritable to a degree that must have been—that had been—hard for him to bear.", "다른 상황이었다면 분명, 처음에 그랬던 것처럼 늘 저를 사로잡았을 텐데요.' 그러더니 그 애는 너 이야기를 꺼냈어."], ["‘I did not make the allowances,’ said she, ‘which I ought to have done, for his temper and spirits—his delightful spirits, and that gaiety, that playfulness of disposition, which, under any other circumstances, would, I am sure, have been as constantly bewitching to me, as they were at first.’ She then began to speak of you, and of the great kindness you had shewn her during her illness; and with a blush which shewed me how it was all connected, desired me, whenever I had an opportunity, to thank you—I could not thank you too much—for every wish and every endeavour to do her good.", "그리고 네가 자기가 앓는 동안 베풀어 준 그 큰 친절 이야기를 하더구나. 그 모든 게 어떻게 얽혀 있는지를 내게 알려 주는 듯한 홍조를 띤 채, 기회가 닿거든 너에게 고맙다고 전해 달라고 하더라 — 아무리 고마워해도 모자라다면서 — 자기를 위해 베풀어 준 온갖 바람과 온갖 노력에 대해서 말이야."], ["She was sensible that you had never received any proper acknowledgment from herself.”", "자기가 너한테 제대로 된 인사 한 번 못 했다는 걸 그 애도 알고 있더구나.\""]], [["“If I did not know her to be happy now,”", "\"그분이 지금 행복하다는 걸 제가 모른다면,\""], ["said Emma, seriously, “which, in spite of every little drawback from her scrupulous conscience, she must be, I could not bear these thanks;—for, oh!", "엠마가 진지하게 말했다. \"물론 그 까다로운 양심에서 비롯되는 자잘한 흠이야 있겠지만, 그래도 행복할 게 분명한데 — 모른다면 이런 고맙다는 말은 견딜 수 없을 거예요."], ["Mrs.", "아아!"], ["Weston, if there were an account drawn up of the evil and the good I have done Miss Fairfax!—Well (checking herself, and trying to be more lively), this is all to be forgotten.", "웨스턴 부인, 제가 미스 페어팩스에게 끼친 해와 베푼 득을 따져 적은 장부가 있다면요! 자아 (스스로를 다잡으며, 좀 더 밝아지려 애쓰면서), 이건 다 잊어버려야겠죠."], ["You are very kind to bring me these interesting particulars.", "이렇게 흥미로운 이야기를 전해 주시다니 참 다정도 하셔라."], ["They shew her to the greatest advantage.", "그 이야기들이 그분을 더없이 돋보이게 해 주네요."], ["I am sure she is very good—I hope she will be very happy.", "그분은 분명 무척 선한 사람이에요 — 부디 아주 행복하기를 바라요."], ["It is fit that the fortune should be on his side, for I think the merit will be all on hers.”", "행운이 그 사람 편에 있는 게 마땅하죠. 미덕은 죄다 그분 편에 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["Such a conclusion could not pass unanswered by Mrs. Weston.", "이런 결론을 웨스턴 부인이 대꾸 없이 넘길 수는 없었다."], ["She thought well of Frank in almost every respect; and, what was more, she loved him very much, and her defence was, therefore, earnest.", "그녀는 거의 모든 면에서 프랭크를 좋게 여겼고, 무엇보다 그를 무척 사랑했기에, 그 변호는 간절했다."], ["She talked with a great deal of reason, and at least equal affection—but she had too much to urge for Emma’s attention; it was soon gone to Brunswick Square or to Donwell; she forgot to attempt to listen; and when Mrs.", "그녀는 더없이 조리 있게, 그리고 적어도 그에 못지않은 애정을 담아 이야기했다 — 그러나 엠마의 주의를 붙들기에는 늘어놓을 말이 너무 많았다. 엠마의 마음은 어느새 브런즈윅 스퀘어로, 아니면 돈웰로 가 있었고, 귀 기울이려는 노력조차 잊고 말았다."], ["Weston ended with, “We have not yet had the letter we are so anxious for, you know, but I hope it will soon come,”", "그래서 웨스턴 부인이 \"있잖니, 우리가 그토록 애타게 기다리는 그 편지가 아직 안 왔단다. 하지만 곧 오겠지\""], ["she was obliged to pause before she answered, and at last obliged to answer at random, before she could at all recollect what letter it was which they were so anxious for.", "하고 말을 맺었을 때, 엠마는 대답하기 전에 잠시 멈칫할 수밖에 없었고, 마침내 아무렇게나 대답할 수밖에 없었다. 그들이 그토록 애타게 기다린다는 그 편지가 도대체 무슨 편지였는지 도무지 떠올릴 수 없었으니 말이다."]], [["“Are you well, my Emma?”", "\"몸은 괜찮니, 엠마?\""], ["was Mrs. Weston’s parting question.", "헤어지면서 웨스턴 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["perfectly.", "멀쩡해요."], ["I am always well, you know.", "아시잖아요, 저는 늘 멀쩡한걸요."], ["Be sure to give me intelligence of the letter as soon as possible.”", "편지 오면 꼭 되도록 빨리 소식 전해 주세요.\""]], [["Mrs. Weston’s communications furnished Emma with more food for unpleasant reflection, by increasing her esteem and compassion, and her sense of past injustice towards Miss Fairfax.", "웨스턴 부인의 이야기는 엠마에게 더 많은, 달갑잖은 생각거리를 안겨 주었다."], ["She bitterly regretted not having sought a closer acquaintance with her, and blushed for the envious feelings which had certainly been, in some measure, the cause. Had she followed Mr.", "미스 페어팩스를 향한 존경과 연민을, 또 그동안 그녀에게 저지른 부당함에 대한 자각을 키워 놓았던 것이다."], ["Knightley’s known wishes, in paying that attention to Miss Fairfax, which was every way her due; had she tried to know her better; had she done her part towards intimacy; had she endeavoured to find a friend there instead of in Harriet Smith; she must, in all probability, have been spared from every pain which pressed on her now.—Birth, abilities, and education, had been equally marking one as an associate for her, to be received with gratitude; and the other—what was she?—Supposing even that they had never become intimate friends; that she had never been admitted into Miss Fairfax’s confidence on this important matter—which was most probable—still, in knowing her as she ought, and as she might, she must have been preserved from the abominable suspicions of an improper attachment to Mr.", "그녀와 더 가까이 지내려 애쓰지 않았던 것을 엠마는 쓰라리게 뉘우쳤고, 어느 만큼은 분명 그 원인이 되었던 시샘하는 마음을 떠올리며 얼굴을 붉혔다. 미스 페어팩스에게 어느 모로 보나 마땅했던 그 관심을 기울이라는 미스터 나이틀리의 익히 알려진 바람을 따랐더라면, 그녀를 더 알려고 애썼더라면, 친밀해지려 제 몫을 다했더라면, 해리엇 스미스가 아니라 그곳에서 벗을 찾으려 했더라면 — 지금 그녀를 짓누르는 모든 아픔에서 십중팔구 벗어날 수 있었으리라."], ["Dixon, which she had not only so foolishly fashioned and harboured herself, but had so unpardonably imparted; an idea which she greatly feared had been made a subject of material distress to the delicacy of Jane’s feelings, by the levity or carelessness of Frank Churchill’s.", "태생도, 재능도, 교양도, 한쪽이 고맙게 받아들일 동무로 삼기에 똑같이 어울리는 사람임을 가리키고 있었건만 — 다른 한쪽은, 도대체 무엇이었던가? 설령 두 사람이 끝내 절친한 벗이 되지 못했다 하더라도, 이 중대한 일에서 미스 페어팩스의 속내를 결코 털어놓는 상대가 되지 못했다 하더라도 — 그쪽이 가장 그럴 법한 일이었지만 — 그래도 마땅한 만큼, 또 그럴 수 있었던 만큼 그녀를 알았더라면, 미스터 딕슨에 대한 부적절한 연정이라는 그 끔찍한 의심에서만은 벗어날 수 있었으리라."], ["Of all the sources of evil surrounding the former, since her coming to Highbury, she was persuaded that she must herself have been the worst.", "그 의심을 엠마는 어리석게도 제 손으로 지어내 마음에 품었을 뿐 아니라, 용서받지 못할 만큼 함부로 입 밖에 냈던 것이다. 그리고 프랭크 처칠의 경박함이나 부주의함을 통해, 그 생각이 제인의 섬세한 감정에 적잖은 괴로움의 빌미가 되었을까 봐 그녀는 몹시 두려웠다."], ["She must have been a perpetual enemy.", "미스 페어팩스가 하이베리에 온 뒤로 그녀를 에워쌌던 온갖 화근 가운데, 정작 제일가는 화근이 자기 자신이었으리라고 엠마는 확신했다. 자신은 줄곧 적이었음이 분명했다."], ["They never could have been all three together, without her having stabbed Jane Fairfax’s peace in a thousand instances; and on Box Hill, perhaps, it had been the agony of a mind that would bear no more.", "세 사람이 함께 있을 때면 언제나, 자기가 제인 페어팩스의 평온을 수천 번씩 찔러 대지 않은 적이 없었다. 그리고 박스 힐에서의 그 일은, 아마도 더는 견딜 수 없게 된 한 마음의 고뇌였으리라."]], [["The evening of this day was very long, and melancholy, at Hartfield.", "이날 저녁은 하트필드에서 무척 길고도 우울하게 흘러갔다."], ["The weather added what it could of gloom.", "날씨까지 음울함에 한몫을 보탰다."], ["A cold stormy rain set in, and nothing of July appeared but in the trees and shrubs, which the wind was despoiling, and the length of the day, which only made such cruel sights the longer visible.", "차갑고 사나운 비가 들이쳤고, 칠월다운 것이라곤 바람이 잎을 앗아 가는 나무와 관목, 그리고 그토록 모진 광경을 그만큼 더 오래 보이게 할 뿐인 길고 긴 낮의 길이밖에 없었다."]], [["The weather affected Mr. Woodhouse, and he could only be kept tolerably comfortable by almost ceaseless attention on his daughter’s side, and by exertions which had never cost her half so much before.", "날씨는 미스터 우드하우스에게 영향을 미쳤고, 그는 딸이 거의 쉴 새 없이 마음을 써 주고, 일찍이 그 절반도 들인 적 없는 애를 쏟아야만 그럭저럭 견딜 만하게 지냈다."], ["It reminded her of their first forlorn tête-à-tête, on the evening of Mrs. Weston’s wedding-day; but Mr.", "그것은 웨스턴 부인이 혼례를 올린 날 저녁, 둘이서 처음으로 쓸쓸히 마주 앉았던 그날을 떠올리게 했다."], ["Knightley had walked in then, soon after tea, and dissipated every melancholy fancy.", "그러나 그때는 차를 마시고 얼마 안 되어 미스터 나이틀리가 걸어 들어와 온갖 우울한 상념을 흩어 주지 않았던가."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["such delightful proofs of Hartfield’s attraction, as those sort of visits conveyed, might shortly be over.", "그런 방문들이 전해 주던, 하트필드의 매력에 대한 그 즐거운 증거들도, 머지않아 끝나 버릴지 모를 일이었다."], ["The picture which she had then drawn of the privations of the approaching winter, had proved erroneous; no friends had deserted them, no pleasures had been lost.—But her present forebodings she feared would experience no similar contradiction.", "그때 다가오는 겨울의 결핍을 두고 그녀가 그려 보았던 그림은 빗나간 것으로 드러났다. 어떤 벗도 그들을 저버리지 않았고, 어떤 즐거움도 잃지 않았던 것이다."], ["The prospect before her now, was threatening to a degree that could not be entirely dispelled—that might not be even partially brightened.", "그러나 지금의 이 불길한 예감만은 그와 비슷한 어긋남을 겪지 않으리라고 그녀는 두려워했다. 지금 그녀 앞에 펼쳐진 전망은, 온전히 떨쳐 낼 수 없을 만큼 — 아니, 일부나마 환해질 수조차 없을 만큼 — 위협적이었다."], ["If all took place that might take place among the circle of her friends, Hartfield must be comparatively deserted; and she left to cheer her father with the spirits only of ruined happiness.", "그녀의 벗들이 이루는 무리 안에서 일어날 수 있는 일이 죄다 일어난다면, 하트필드는 견주어 보면 버려진 곳이나 다름없으리라. 그러면 그녀는 무너진 행복의 기운만 가지고 아버지를 위로하는 처지에 남겨질 터였다."]], [["The child to be born at Randalls must be a tie there even dearer than herself; and Mrs. Weston’s heart and time would be occupied by it.", "랜덜스에서 태어날 아이는 그곳에서 그녀 자신보다도 더 소중한 끈이 될 것이요, 웨스턴 부인의 마음과 시간은 그 아이에게 다 쏠릴 터였다."], ["They should lose her; and, probably, in great measure, her husband also.—Frank Churchill would return among them no more; and Miss Fairfax, it was reasonable to suppose, would soon cease to belong to Highbury.", "그들은 그녀를 잃을 것이요, 아마 어지간히는 그 남편마저 잃을 것이었다. 프랭크 처칠은 더 이상 그들 사이로 돌아오지 않을 것이요, 미스 페어팩스도 머지않아 하이베리 사람이기를 그칠 것이라고 보는 게 이치에 맞았다."], ["They would be married, and settled either at or near Enscombe.", "두 사람은 결혼하여 엔스컴이나 그 근처에 자리 잡을 것이었다. 좋은 것은 죄다 거두어져 버릴 터였다."], ["All that were good would be withdrawn; and if to these losses, the loss of Donwell were to be added, what would remain of cheerful or of rational society within their reach? Mr.", "그리고 이 상실들에 돈웰의 상실까지 더해진다면, 그들이 손닿을 만한 곳에 명랑하거나 사리 밝은 교제라곤 무엇이 남겠는가?"], ["Knightley to be no longer coming there for his evening comfort!—No longer walking in at all hours, as if ever willing to change his own home for their’s!—How was it to be endured?", "미스터 나이틀리가 더 이상 저녁의 안락을 찾아 이곳에 오지 않게 된다니! 마치 제 집을 그들의 집과 언제든 바꿀 마음이라도 있는 양, 시도 때도 없이 걸어 들어오는 일이 더 이상 없게 된다니!"], ["And if he were to be lost to them for Harriet’s sake; if he were to be thought of hereafter, as finding in Harriet’s society all that he wanted; if Harriet were to be the chosen, the first, the dearest, the friend, the wife to whom he looked for all the best blessings of existence; what could be increasing Emma’s wretchedness but the reflection never far distant from her mind, that it had been all her own work?", "그것을 어찌 견딘단 말인가? 그리고 만약 그를 해리엇 때문에 잃게 된다면, 앞으로 그가 해리엇과 함께하는 데서 바라던 모든 것을 찾았다고 여겨야 한다면, 해리엇이 바로 그가 택한 사람, 첫째가는 사람, 가장 소중한 사람, 그 벗, 존재의 온갖 최상의 축복을 기대하며 바라보는 그 아내가 된다면 — 그것이 다 제 손으로 빚어낸 일이었다는, 그 마음에서 좀처럼 멀어지지 않는 생각 말고, 무엇이 엠마의 비참함을 더 키울 수 있겠는가?"]], [["When it came to such a pitch as this, she was not able to refrain from a start, or a heavy sigh, or even from walking about the room for a few seconds—and the only source whence any thing like consolation or composure could be drawn, was in the resolution of her own better conduct, and the hope that, however inferior in spirit and gaiety might be the following and every future winter of her life to the past, it would yet find her more rational, more acquainted with herself, and leave her less to regret when it were gone.", "이런 지경에 이를 때면, 그녀는 흠칫 놀라거나, 무거운 한숨을 내쉬거나, 심지어는 몇 초쯤 방 안을 서성이는 것을 막을 도리가 없었다. 그리고 위안이나 평정 비슷한 무엇이라도 길어 올릴 수 있는 단 하나의 원천은, 앞으로 더 나은 처신을 하겠다는 제 결심과, 비록 다가올 겨울이며 앞날의 모든 겨울이 지난날에 견주어 기운과 명랑함에서는 못할지언정, 그래도 그것이 자신을 더 사리 밝고, 자기 자신을 더 잘 아는 사람으로 만들어 주고, 지나간 뒤에 뉘우칠 것을 더 적게 남겨 주리라는 희망이었다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter XIII", "# 제3권 13장"]], [["The weather continued much the same all the following morning; and the same loneliness, and the same melancholy, seemed to reign at Hartfield—but in the afternoon it cleared; the wind changed into a softer quarter; the clouds were carried off; the sun appeared; it was summer again.", "이튿날 아침에도 날씨는 좀처럼 변함이 없었다. 그리고 같은 적막과 같은 우울이 하트필드를 지배하는 듯했다."], ["With all the eagerness which such a transition gives, Emma resolved to be out of doors as soon as possible.", "그러나 오후가 되자 하늘이 개었다. 바람은 한결 부드러운 방향으로 바뀌었고, 구름은 걷혔으며, 해가 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Never had the exquisite sight, smell, sensation of nature, tranquil, warm, and brilliant after a storm, been more attractive to her.", "다시 여름이었다. 그런 전환이 안겨 주는 온갖 들뜸에 휩싸여, 엠마는 되도록 빨리 바깥으로 나가기로 마음먹었다."], ["She longed for the serenity they might gradually introduce; and on Mr.", "폭풍이 지난 뒤의 잔잔하고 따뜻하며 눈부신 자연, 그 절묘한 풍경과 향기와 감촉이 이토록 그녀를 끌어당긴 적은 없었다. 그것이 차츰 가져다줄 평온함을 그녀는 갈망했다."], ["Perry’s coming in soon after dinner, with a disengaged hour to give her father, she lost no time in hurrying into the shrubbery.—There, with spirits freshened, and thoughts a little relieved, she had taken a few turns, when she saw Mr.", "그리하여 미스터 페리가 식사 직후 찾아와 아버지에게 한 시간쯤 한가로이 곁을 내어 주자, 그녀는 지체 없이 관목 숲으로 서둘러 나갔다. 거기서 기운을 새로이 하고 생각을 조금 덜어 낸 채 몇 차례 거닐던 그때, 미스터 나이틀리가 정원 문을 지나 그녀 쪽으로 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["Knightley passing through the garden door, and coming towards her.—It was the first intimation of his being returned from London.", "그가 런던에서 돌아왔다는 것을 처음 알린 순간이었다. 바로 직전까지도 그녀는 그가 틀림없이 십육 마일 밖에 있으리라 여기며 그를 생각하던 참이었다."], ["She had been thinking of him the moment before, as unquestionably sixteen miles distant.—There was time only for the quickest arrangement of mind.", "마음을 추스를 시간은 그저 한순간뿐이었다. 침착하고 차분해야 했다."], ["She must be collected and calm.", "반 분도 지나지 않아 두 사람은 마주 섰다. \"안녕하세요\""], ["In half a minute they were together.", "하는 인사는 양쪽 모두 조용하고 어색했다. 그녀가 공통의 벗들 안부를 물었고, 모두 잘 지낸다고 했다."], ["The “How d’ye do’s”", "언제 그들과 헤어졌느냐고?"], ["were quiet and constrained on each side.", "바로 그날 아침이었다. 오는 길이 비에 젖었겠다고."], ["She asked after their mutual friends; they were all well.—When had he left them?—Only that morning.", "그렇다고 했다. 그가 그녀와 함께 걸을 작정임을 그녀는 알아차렸다."], ["He must have had a wet ride.—Yes.—He meant to walk with her, she found.", "\"식당을 잠깐 들여다보았는데, 거기서는 자기가 필요치 않은 듯하여 바깥에 있는 편이 좋았다\"는 것이었다."], ["“He had just looked into the dining-room, and as he was not wanted there, preferred being out of doors.”—She thought he neither looked nor spoke cheerfully; and the first possible cause for it, suggested by her fears, was, that he had perhaps been communicating his plans to his brother, and was pained by the manner in which they had been received.", "그가 표정도 말투도 즐거워 보이지 않는다고 그녀는 느꼈다. 그리고 그 까닭으로 그녀의 불안이 가장 먼저 떠올린 것은, 어쩌면 그가 동생에게 자기 계획을 털어놓았다가 그것이 받아들여진 모양에 마음이 상한 것이 아닐까 하는 생각이었다."]], [["They walked together.", "두 사람은 함께 걸었다."], ["He was silent.", "그는 말이 없었다."], ["She thought he was often looking at her, and trying for a fuller view of her face than it suited her to give.", "그가 자꾸 자기를 바라보며, 그녀가 내보이고 싶은 것 이상으로 얼굴을 더 자세히 살피려 한다고 그녀는 느꼈다. 그리고 그 믿음은 또 다른 두려움을 낳았다."], ["And this belief produced another dread.", "어쩌면 그는 해리엇을 향한 자신의 연정을 그녀에게 이야기하고 싶은 것인지도 몰랐다."], ["Perhaps he wanted to speak to her, of his attachment to Harriet; he might be watching for encouragement to begin.—She did not, could not, feel equal to lead the way to any such subject.", "입을 떼도 좋을 만한 격려를 기다리며 살피고 있는 것인지도 몰랐다. 그녀는 그런 화제로 길을 터 줄 만한 기력이 도무지 나지 않았고, 날 수도 없었다."], ["He must do it all himself.", "모든 것을 그가 직접 해내야 했다."], ["Yet she could not bear this silence.", "그러면서도 이 침묵을 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["With him it was most unnatural.", "그에게는 더없이 어울리지 않는 침묵이었다."], ["She considered—resolved—and, trying to smile, began—", "그녀는 곰곰이 생각하고 마음을 정한 뒤, 애써 미소를 지으며 입을 열었다."]], [["“You have some news to hear, now you are come back, that will rather surprize you.”", "\"이제 돌아오셨으니 들으실 소식이 하나 있어요. 좀 놀라실 거예요.\""]], [["“Have I?”", "\"그래?\""], ["said he quietly, and looking at her; “of what nature?”", "그가 조용히, 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. \"어떤 종류의 소식인데?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["the best nature in the world—a wedding.”", "세상에서 가장 좋은 종류죠. 혼사예요.\""]], [["After waiting a moment, as if to be sure she intended to say no more, he replied,", "그녀가 더 말할 뜻이 없다는 것을 확인하려는 듯 잠시 기다린 뒤, 그가 대답했다."]], [["“If you mean Miss Fairfax and Frank Churchill, I have heard that already.”", "\"미스 페어팩스와 프랭크 처칠을 말하는 거라면, 이미 들었어.\""]], [["“How is it possible?”", "\"어떻게 그럴 수가 있어요?\" 엠마가 발그레 달아오른 뺨을 그에게로 돌리며 외쳤다."], ["cried Emma, turning her glowing cheeks towards him; for, while she spoke, it occurred to her that he might have called at Mrs. Goddard’s in his way.", "말하는 동안, 그가 오는 길에 고더드 부인 댁에 들렀을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었던 것이다."]], [["“I had a few lines on parish business from Mr. Weston this morning, and at the end of them he gave me a brief account of what had happened.”", "\"오늘 아침 미스터 웨스턴한테서 교구 일로 몇 줄 받았는데, 그 끝에 무슨 일이 있었는지를 간략히 적어 두었더군.\""]], [["Emma was quite relieved, and could presently say, with a little more composure,", "엠마는 적이 안심이 되어, 곧 한결 차분해진 목소리로 말할 수 있었다."]], [["“You probably have been less surprized than any of us, for you have had your suspicions.—I have not forgotten that you once tried to give me a caution.—I wish I had attended to it—but—(with a sinking voice and a heavy sigh) I seem to have been doomed to blindness.”", "\"당신은 아마 우리 가운데 누구보다도 덜 놀라셨을 거예요. 진작에 의심하고 계셨으니까요. 언젠가 제게 주의를 주려 하셨던 걸 잊지 않았어요. 그때 새겨들었어야 했는데, 하지만—\" (목소리가 가라앉고 무거운 한숨이 따랐다) \"저는 아무래도 눈먼 채로 살게 마련인가 봐요.\""]], [["For a moment or two nothing was said, and she was unsuspicious of having excited any particular interest, till she found her arm drawn within his, and pressed against his heart, and heard him thus saying, in a tone of great sensibility, speaking low,", "한두 순간 아무 말도 오가지 않았고, 그녀는 자기가 무슨 특별한 관심을 불러일으켰으리라고는 미처 짐작하지 못하고 있었다. 그러다 자기 팔이 그의 팔에 끼워져 그의 가슴께로 끌어당겨지는 것을 느꼈고, 그가 깊은 감정이 어린 어조로 나직이 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."]], [["“Time, my dearest Emma, time will heal the wound.—Your own excellent sense—your exertions for your father’s sake—I know you will not allow yourself—.”", "\"세월이, 가장 사랑하는 엠마, 세월이 그 상처를 아물게 해 줄 거야. 자네의 그 뛰어난 분별력이, 아버지를 위한 자네의 노력이—나는 자네가 결코 스스로를 그냥 내버려 두지 않으리라는 걸 알아—.\""], ["Her arm was pressed again, as he added, in a more broken and subdued accent, “The feelings of the warmest friendship—Indignation—Abominable scoundrel!”—And in a louder, steadier tone, he concluded with, “He will soon be gone.", "그가 그녀의 팔을 다시 한번 힘주어 끼며, 한층 끊어지고 가라앉은 어조로 덧붙였다. \"더없이 따뜻한 우정의 마음으로—분노가—고약한 악당 같으니!\""], ["They will soon be in Yorkshire.", "그러고는 더 크고 한결 다잡은 어조로 끝맺었다. \"그자는 곧 떠날 거야."], ["I am sorry for her.", "곧 요크셔로 가겠지."], ["She deserves a better fate.”", "나는 그녀가 안됐어. 그녀는 더 나은 운명을 누릴 자격이 있는데.\""]], [["Emma understood him; and as soon as she could recover from the flutter of pleasure, excited by such tender consideration, replied,", "엠마는 그의 말뜻을 알아들었다. 그리고 그토록 다정한 배려가 불러일으킨 기쁨의 설렘에서 벗어나자마자 대답했다."]], [["“You are very kind—but you are mistaken—and I must set you right.— I am not in want of that sort of compassion. My blindness to what was going on, led me to act by them in a way that I must always be ashamed of, and I was very foolishly tempted to say and do many things which may well lay me open to unpleasant conjectures, but I have no other reason to regret that I was not in the secret earlier.”", "\"정말 친절하시네요. 하지만 오해하고 계세요. 바로잡아 드려야겠어요. 저는 그런 종류의 동정이 필요하지 않아요. 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알아보지 못한 탓에, 저는 그 두 사람을 두고 늘 부끄러워해야 할 방식으로 행동했고, 어리석게도 갖은 말과 행동을 하고픈 유혹에 넘어가, 그것이 언짢은 추측을 살 빌미가 될 수도 있게 되었어요. 하지만 그 비밀을 더 일찍 알지 못한 것을 후회할 다른 이유는 없어요.\""]], [["“Emma!” cried he, looking eagerly at her, “are you, indeed?”—but checking himself—“No, no, I understand you—forgive me—I am pleased that you can say even so much.—He is no object of regret, indeed! and it will not be very long, I hope, before that becomes the acknowledgment of more than your reason.—Fortunate that your affections were not farther entangled!—I could never, I confess, from your manners, assure myself as to the degree of what you felt—I could only be certain that there was a preference—and a preference which I never believed him to deserve.—He is a disgrace to the name of man.—And is he to be rewarded with that sweet young woman?—Jane, Jane, you will be a miserable creature.”", "\"엠마!\" 그가 그녀를 간절히 바라보며 외쳤다. \"정말, 정말 그런가?\" 그러나 곧 스스로를 다잡으며—\"아니, 아니, 자네 말뜻을 알겠어—용서하게—자네가 그만큼이라도 말해 주니 기쁘네. 그자는 정말 아쉬워할 만한 사람이 아니야! 그리고 머지않아, 그것이 자네의 이성뿐 아니라 그 이상의 무언가가 인정하는 바가 되리라고 믿네. 자네의 연정이 더 깊이 얽히지 않은 게 다행이야! 솔직히 말해, 나는 자네의 태도만 보고는 자네가 느끼는 감정이 어느 정도인지 도무지 확신할 수가 없었어. 다만 호감이 있다는 것만은 분명했지. 그자가 받을 자격이 있다고는 한 번도 믿지 않았던 그런 호감이 말이야. 그자는 사내라는 이름에 먹칠을 하는 자야. 그런데 그 사랑스러운 아가씨가 그자에게 상으로 주어진단 말인가? 제인, 제인, 자네는 불행해질 거야.\""]], [["“Mr.", "\"미스터 나이틀리.\""], ["Knightley,”", "엠마가 명랑하게 굴려 애썼으나 실은 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 말했다."], ["said Emma, trying to be lively, but really confused—“I am in a very extraordinary situation.", "\"저는 아주 별난 처지에 놓였어요. 당신이 계속 오해하시도록 둘 수는 없어요."], ["I cannot let you continue in your error; and yet, perhaps, since my manners gave such an impression, I have as much reason to be ashamed of confessing that I never have been at all attached to the person we are speaking of, as it might be natural for a woman to feel in confessing exactly the reverse.—But I never have.”", "그렇지만 어쩌면, 제 태도가 그런 인상을 주었으니, 우리가 이야기하는 그 사람에게 제가 한 번도 마음을 둔 적이 없었다고 고백하는 것이, 여자가 그 반대를 고백할 때 느낌 직한 만큼이나 부끄러운 일이 될지도 모르겠어요. 하지만 저는 정말 한 번도 그런 적이 없어요.\""]], [["He listened in perfect silence.", "그는 완전한 침묵 속에서 들었다."], ["She wished him to speak, but he would not.", "그녀는 그가 말해 주기를 바랐으나, 그는 입을 열지 않았다."], ["She supposed she must say more before she were entitled to his clemency; but it was a hard case to be obliged still to lower herself in his opinion.", "그의 너그러움을 받을 자격을 얻으려면 더 말해야겠다고 그녀는 짐작했다. 그러나 그의 눈에 비친 자기 모습을 또다시 낮춰야 하는 것은 괴로운 일이었다."], ["She went on, however.", "그래도 그녀는 말을 이었다."]], [["“I have very little to say for my own conduct.—I was tempted by his attentions, and allowed myself to appear pleased.—An old story, probably—a common case—and no more than has happened to hundreds of my sex before; and yet it may not be the more excusable in one who sets up as I do for Understanding.", "\"제 행동을 변명할 거리는 거의 없어요. 그분의 관심에 마음이 흔들려, 짐짓 기뻐하는 척하도록 저 자신을 내버려 두었어요."], ["Many circumstances assisted the temptation.", "아마 흔해 빠진 이야기겠죠—흔한 경우고요—저 같은 여자가 그 전에도 수백 명은 겪었을 일에 지나지 않을 거예요. 그렇다 해도 저처럼 분별력을 자처하는 사람에게는 더 변명의 여지가 없는 일이겠지요."], ["He was the son of Mr.", "여러 사정이 그 유혹을 거들었어요."], ["Weston—he was continually here—I always found him very pleasant—and, in short, for (with a sigh) let me swell out the causes ever so ingeniously, they all centre in this at last—my vanity was flattered, and I allowed his attentions.", "그분은 미스터 웨스턴의 아들이고, 늘 이곳에 드나들었으며, 저는 늘 그분이 무척 유쾌하다고 여겼어요. 그러니, 요컨대\""], ["Latterly, however—for some time, indeed—I have had no idea of their meaning any thing.—I thought them a habit, a trick, nothing that called for seriousness on my side.", "(한숨을 쉬며) \"그 까닭을 아무리 그럴듯하게 부풀려 본들, 결국 모두 한 가지로 모여요. 제 허영심이 추어올려진 것이고, 그래서 저는 그분의 관심을 허용한 거예요."], ["He has imposed on me, but he has not injured me.", "하지만 근래에는—사실 한동안은—그것이 무슨 의미를 지닌다고는 전혀 생각지 않았어요. 그저 하나의 버릇, 하나의 장난일 뿐, 제 쪽에서 진지하게 받아들일 것은 아무것도 아니라고 여겼어요."], ["I have never been attached to him.", "그분은 저를 속였지만, 저를 해치지는 않았어요."], ["And now I can tolerably comprehend his behaviour.", "저는 한 번도 그분에게 마음을 둔 적이 없으니까요. 그리고 이제는 그분의 행동을 어지간히 이해할 수 있어요."], ["He never wished to attach me.", "그분은 저를 끌어들이려 한 적이 없었어요."], ["It was merely a blind to conceal his real situation with another.—It was his object to blind all about him; and no one, I am sure, could be more effectually blinded than myself—except that I was not blinded—that it was my good fortune—that, in short, I was somehow or other safe from him.”", "그건 그저 다른 사람과의 진짜 관계를 감추려는 눈가림일 뿐이었어요. 그분의 목적은 주위 모든 사람의 눈을 가리는 것이었고, 저보다 더 감쪽같이 속아 넘어간 사람은 아무도 없었으리라 확신해요—다만 제가 속아 넘어가지는 않았다는 점, 그게 제 행운이었다는 점, 요컨대 어찌어찌하여 그분에게서 무사할 수 있었다는 점만은 빼고요.\""]], [["She had hoped for an answer here—for a few words to say that her conduct was at least intelligible; but he was silent; and, as far as she could judge, deep in thought.", "여기서 그녀는 대답을 기대했다. 적어도 자기 행동이 이해할 만하다는 한두 마디 말을."], ["At last, and tolerably in his usual tone, he said,", "그러나 그는 잠자코 있었고, 그녀가 판단하기로는 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯했다. 마침내, 어지간히 평소의 어조로 그가 말했다."]], [["“I have never had a high opinion of Frank Churchill.—I can suppose, however, that I may have underrated him. My acquaintance with him has been but trifling.—And even if I have not underrated him hitherto, he may yet turn out well.—With such a woman he has a chance.—I have no motive for wishing him ill—and for her sake, whose happiness will be involved in his good character and conduct, I shall certainly wish him well.”", "\"나는 프랭크 처칠을 한 번도 높이 평가한 적이 없어. 하지만 어쩌면 내가 그를 과소평가했는지도 모르겠어. 그와의 사귐이 워낙 보잘것없었으니까. 그리고 설령 지금껏 내가 그를 과소평가한 게 아니라 해도, 그가 앞으로 잘되어 갈 수는 있겠지. 그런 여자를 곁에 두었으니 가망이 있는 거야. 나는 그가 잘못되기를 바랄 까닭이 없어. 그리고 그의 됨됨이와 행실에 자신의 행복이 달려 있을 그녀를 위해서라도, 나는 분명 그가 잘되기를 바랄 거야.\""]], [["“I have no doubt of their being happy together,”", "\"두 사람이 함께 행복하리라는 건 의심치 않아요.\""], ["said Emma; “I believe them to be very mutually and very sincerely attached.”", "엠마가 말했다. \"서로에게 아주 깊고 진실하게 마음을 두고 있다고 믿어요.\""]], [["“He is a most fortunate man!” returned Mr. Knightley, with energy. “So early in life—at three-and-twenty—a period when, if a man chuses a wife, he generally chuses ill. At three-and-twenty to have drawn such a prize! What years of felicity that man, in all human calculation, has before him!—Assured of the love of such a woman—the disinterested love, for Jane Fairfax’s character vouches for her disinterestedness; every thing in his favour,—equality of situation—I mean, as far as regards society, and all the habits and manners that are important; equality in every point but one—and that one, since the purity of her heart is not to be doubted, such as must increase his felicity, for it will be his to bestow the only advantages she wants.—A man would always wish to give a woman a better home than the one he takes her from; and he who can do it, where there is no doubt of her regard, must, I think, be the happiest of mortals.—Frank Churchill is, indeed, the favourite of fortune. Every thing turns out for his good.—He meets with a young woman at a watering-place, gains her affection, cannot even weary her by negligent treatment—and had he and all his family sought round the world for a perfect wife for him, they could not have found her superior.—His aunt is in the way.—His aunt dies.—He has only to speak.—His friends are eager to promote his happiness.—He had used every body ill—and they are all delighted to forgive him.—He is a fortunate man indeed!”", "\"그자는 더없이 운 좋은 사내야!\" 미스터 나이틀리가 힘주어 대꾸했다. \"그렇게 인생 이른 시기에—스물세 살에—사내가 아내를 고르면 대개 잘못 고르고 마는 그 나이에 말이야. 스물세 살에 그런 행운을 거머쥐다니! 그 사내 앞에는, 인간으로서 따져 볼 수 있는 한, 얼마나 많은 행복의 세월이 펼쳐져 있는가! 그런 여자의 사랑을, 사심 없는 사랑을 보장받은 셈이지—제인 페어팩스의 인품이 그 사심 없음을 보증해 주니까. 모든 것이 그에게 유리해. 처지의 대등함—내 말은, 사교계와 관련된 한, 그리고 중요한 온갖 습관과 몸가짐에 있어서 그렇다는 거야. 단 한 가지를 빼고는 모든 점에서 대등하지. 그리고 그 한 가지마저도, 그녀의 마음이 순결함은 의심할 여지가 없으니, 도리어 그의 행복을 더해 줄 것이 분명해. 그녀에게 모자란 단 하나의 이점을, 그가 베풀어 줄 몫이 될 테니 말이야. 사내라면 누구나 자기가 데려오는 곳보다 더 나은 보금자리를 여자에게 주고 싶어 하는 법이고, 그녀의 마음에 의심할 바가 없는 처지에서 그렇게 해 줄 수 있는 사내라면, 내 생각에 세상에서 가장 행복한 인간일 거야. 프랭크 처칠은 정말이지 운명의 총아야. 모든 일이 그에게 좋은 쪽으로 풀려. 어느 온천 휴양지에서 한 젊은 여자를 만나, 그 마음을 얻고, 소홀히 대해도 그녀를 지치게 만들 수조차 없었어. 그와 그의 온 집안이 그를 위한 완벽한 아내를 찾아 온 세상을 뒤졌다 한들, 그녀보다 나은 여자는 찾지 못했을 거야. 외숙모가 걸림돌이었지. 그런데 외숙모가 죽었어. 그는 그저 입만 떼면 그만이고. 그의 식구들은 그의 행복을 거들어 주려고 안달이지. 그는 모두를 함부로 대했건만, 다들 기꺼이 그를 용서해 주고 있어. 정말이지 운 좋은 사내야!\""]], [["“You speak as if you envied him.”", "\"꼭 그를 부러워하시는 것처럼 말씀하시네요.\""]], [["“And I do envy him, Emma.", "\"그래, 나는 그를 부러워해, 엠마."], ["In one respect he is the object of my envy.”", "한 가지 점에서 그는 내 부러움의 대상이야.\""]], [["Emma could say no more.", "엠마는 더 말할 수 없었다."], ["They seemed to be within half a sentence of Harriet, and her immediate feeling was to avert the subject, if possible.", "두 사람은 해리엇에게서 반 문장도 채 떨어지지 않은 듯했고, 그녀의 즉각적인 심정은 되도록이면 그 화제를 피하자는 것이었다."], ["She made her plan; she would speak of something totally different—the children in Brunswick Square; and she only waited for breath to begin, when Mr.", "그녀는 계획을 세웠다—전혀 다른 이야기를 꺼내리라고. 브런즈윅 스퀘어의 아이들 이야기를."], ["Knightley startled her, by saying,", "그저 입을 떼려고 숨을 고르고 있던 차에, 미스터 나이틀리가 이렇게 말해 그녀를 흠칫 놀라게 했다."]], [["“You will not ask me what is the point of envy.—You are determined, I see, to have no curiosity.—You are wise—but I cannot be wise. Emma, I must tell you what you will not ask, though I may wish it unsaid the next moment.”", "\"그 부러움의 대목이 무엇인지 묻지 않으려는군. 보아하니 호기심 따위는 갖지 않기로 작정했어. 현명한 일이지. 하지만 나는 현명할 수가 없어. 엠마, 자네가 묻지 않으려는 것을 내가 말해야겠어. 다음 순간 입 밖에 내지 말걸 하고 후회할지언정.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["then, don’t speak it, don’t speak it,”", "그러시면, 말씀하지 마세요, 말씀하지 마세요.\""], ["she eagerly cried.", "그녀가 다급히 외쳤다."], ["“Take a little time, consider, do not commit yourself.”", "\"조금 시간을 두고, 곰곰이 생각하시고, 섣불리 다짐하지 마세요.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙네.\""], ["said he, in an accent of deep mortification, and not another syllable followed.", "그가 깊은 굴욕이 어린 어조로 말했고, 그 뒤로는 한 음절도 따라 나오지 않았다."]], [["Emma could not bear to give him pain.", "엠마는 그에게 고통을 안기는 것을 견딜 수 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 속내를 털어놓고 싶어 했고—어쩌면 의논하고 싶어 했다."], ["He was wishing to confide in her—perhaps to consult her;—cost her what it would, she would listen.", "무슨 대가를 치르더라도, 그녀는 들어 주리라. 그의 결심을 거들어 주거나, 그 결심과 그를 화해시켜 줄 수도 있으리라."], ["She might assist his resolution, or reconcile him to it; she might give just praise to Harriet, or, by representing to him his own independence, relieve him from that state of indecision, which must be more intolerable than any alternative to such a mind as his.—They had reached the house.", "해리엇을 마땅히 칭찬해 주거나, 그가 자유로운 몸임을 일깨워 줌으로써, 그처럼 단호한 사람에게는 어떤 결정보다도 더 견디기 힘들 그 우유부단함에서 그를 풀어 줄 수도 있으리라. 두 사람은 집에 다다랐다."]], [["“You are going in, I suppose?”", "\"들어가려는 거지?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“No,”—replied Emma—quite confirmed by the depressed manner in which he still spoke—“I should like to take another turn.", "\"아니요.\""], ["Mr.", "엠마가 대답했다."], ["Perry is not gone.”", "그가 여전히 가라앉은 태도로 말하는 데 마음을 굳힌 터였다. \"한 바퀴 더 돌고 싶어요."], ["And, after proceeding a few steps, she added—“I stopped you ungraciously, just now, Mr.", "미스터 페리가 아직 안 가셨거든요.\" 그러고는 몇 걸음 더 나아간 뒤 덧붙였다."], ["Knightley, and, I am afraid, gave you pain.—But if you have any wish to speak openly to me as a friend, or to ask my opinion of any thing that you may have in contemplation—as a friend, indeed, you may command me.—I will hear whatever you like.", "\"조금 전에 제가 무례하게 당신을 가로막아, 마음을 아프게 해 드린 것 같아요. 하지만 벗으로서 제게 터놓고 말씀하고 싶으시거나, 마음에 품고 계신 무언가에 대해 제 의견을 묻고 싶으시다면—벗으로서, 정말이지 저를 마음껏 부리셔도 좋아요."], ["I will tell you exactly what I think.”", "무엇이든 들어 드릴게요. 제 생각을 있는 그대로 말씀드릴게요.\""]], [["“As a friend!”—repeated Mr. Knightley.—“Emma, that I fear is a word—No, I have no wish—Stay, yes, why should I hesitate?—I have gone too far already for concealment.—Emma, I accept your offer—Extraordinary as it may seem, I accept it, and refer myself to you as a friend.—Tell me, then, have I no chance of ever succeeding?”", "\"벗으로서라!\" 미스터 나이틀리가 되뇌었다. \"엠마, 그건 아무래도 내가 두려워하는 말이야—아니, 나는 바라지 않아—아니, 잠깐, 그래, 내가 왜 망설여야 하지?—이미 감추기에는 너무 멀리 와 버렸는걸. 엠마, 자네의 제안을 받아들이겠어—이상하게 보일지 몰라도, 받아들이고, 벗으로서 자네에게 나를 맡기겠어. 그러니 말해 주게, 내게는 끝내 가망이라곤 없는 건가?\""]], [["He stopped in his earnestness to look the question, and the expression of his eyes overpowered her.", "그가 그 물음을 눈빛으로 던지려 간절함에 사로잡혀 멈춰 섰고, 그 눈빛이 그녀를 압도했다."]], [["“My dearest Emma,”", "\"내가 가장 사랑하는 엠마.\""], ["said he, “for dearest you will always be, whatever the event of this hour’s conversation, my dearest, most beloved Emma—tell me at once.", "그가 말했다. \"이 한 시간의 대화가 어떻게 끝나든, 자네는 언제까지나 내게 가장 사랑하는 사람일 테니까—내가 가장 사랑하고 가장 아끼는 엠마—지금 당장 말해 주게."], ["Say ‘No,’ if it is to be said.”—She could really say nothing.—“You are silent,”", "거절할 거라면 '아니요'라고 말해.\"—그녀는 정말이지 아무 말도 할 수 없었다.—\"말이 없군.\""], ["he cried, with great animation; “absolutely silent!", "그가 무척 생기에 차 외쳤다. \"한마디도 없어!"], ["at present I ask no more.”", "지금으로서는 더 바라지 않겠네.\""]], [["Emma was almost ready to sink under the agitation of this moment.", "엠마는 이 순간의 격동에 그만 주저앉을 지경이었다."], ["The dread of being awakened from the happiest dream, was perhaps the most prominent feeling.", "더없이 행복한 꿈에서 깨어날지 모른다는 두려움이, 어쩌면 가장 두드러진 감정이었다."]], [["“I cannot make speeches, Emma:” he soon resumed; and in a tone of such sincere, decided, intelligible tenderness as was tolerably convincing.—“If I loved you less, I might be able to talk about it more. But you know what I am.—You hear nothing but truth from me.—I have blamed you, and lectured you, and you have borne it as no other woman in England would have borne it.—Bear with the truths I would tell you now, dearest Emma, as well as you have borne with them. The manner, perhaps, may have as little to recommend them. God knows, I have been a very indifferent lover.—But you understand me.—Yes, you see, you understand my feelings—and will return them if you can. At present, I ask only to hear, once to hear your voice.”", "\"나는 그럴듯한 말을 늘어놓을 줄 모르네, 엠마.\" 그가 곧 다시 입을 열었다. 그리고 그것은 어지간히 설득력 있을 만큼 진실하고 단호하며 또렷한 다정함이 어린 어조였다. \"내가 자네를 덜 사랑한다면, 그 일을 두고 더 그럴싸하게 떠들 수 있을지도 모르지. 하지만 자네는 내가 어떤 사람인지 알잖아. 내게서는 진실밖에 듣지 못한다는 걸. 나는 자네를 나무라고 훈계했고, 자네는 영국의 어떤 여자도 견뎌 내지 못했을 만큼 그것을 견뎌 주었어. 지금 내가 자네에게 들려주려는 진실도, 그때처럼 잘 참아 주게, 가장 사랑하는 엠마. 그 진실을 내미는 방식도 어쩌면 추천할 구석이라곤 거의 없을 테니까. 하느님은 아시겠지만, 나는 참으로 무미건조한 구애자였어. 하지만 자네는 내 말뜻을 알아. 그래, 보다시피, 자네는 내 마음을 이해해—그리고 할 수 있다면 그 마음에 응해 주겠지. 지금으로서는 그저 한 번, 자네 목소리를 한 번 듣기만을 청하네.\""]], [["While he spoke, Emma’s mind was most busy, and, with all the wonderful velocity of thought, had been able—and yet without losing a word—to catch and comprehend the exact truth of the whole; to see that Harriet’s hopes had been entirely groundless, a mistake, a delusion, as complete a delusion as any of her own—that Harriet was nothing; that she was every thing herself; that what she had been saying relative to Harriet had been all taken as the language of her own feelings; and that her agitation, her doubts, her reluctance, her discouragement, had been all received as discouragement from herself.—And not only was there time for these convictions, with all their glow of attendant happiness; there was time also to rejoice that Harriet’s secret had not escaped her, and to resolve that it need not, and should not.—It was all the service she could now render her poor friend; for as to any of that heroism of sentiment which might have prompted her to entreat him to transfer his affection from herself to Harriet, as infinitely the most worthy of the two—or even the more simple sublimity of resolving to refuse him at once and for ever, without vouchsafing any motive, because he could not marry them both, Emma had it not. She felt for Harriet, with pain and with contrition; but no flight of generosity run mad, opposing all that could be probable or reasonable, entered her brain. She had led her friend astray, and it would be a reproach to her for ever; but her judgment was as strong as her feelings, and as strong as it had ever been before, in reprobating any such alliance for him, as most unequal and degrading. Her way was clear, though not quite smooth.—She spoke then, on being so entreated.—What did she say?—Just what she ought, of course. A lady always does.—She said enough to shew there need not be despair—and to invite him to say more himself. He had despaired at one period; he had received such an injunction to caution and silence, as for the time crushed every hope;—she had begun by refusing to hear him.—The change had perhaps been somewhat sudden;—her proposal of taking another turn, her renewing the conversation which she had just put an end to, might be a little extraordinary!—She felt its inconsistency; but Mr. Knightley was so obliging as to put up with it, and seek no farther explanation.", "그가 말하는 동안, 엠마의 머릿속은 더없이 분주했고, 생각의 그 놀라운 속도로—그러면서도 한 마디도 놓치지 않은 채—전체의 정확한 진실을 단번에 붙들어 깨달을 수 있었다. 해리엇의 희망이 송두리째 근거 없는 것이었음을, 하나의 착각이요 망상이었음을, 그것도 자기 자신의 어떤 망상 못지않게 완전한 망상이었음을. 해리엇은 아무것도 아니었고, 모든 것은 자기 자신이었음을. 해리엇에 관해 그녀가 했던 말이 죄다 그녀 자신의 감정을 토로하는 말로 받아들여졌고, 그녀의 동요와 의혹과 망설임과 낙심이 모두 그녀 자신에게서 비롯된 낙심으로 여겨졌음을. 그리고 이 모든 확신을, 그에 따르는 온갖 행복의 광채와 더불어 깨달을 시간이 있었을 뿐 아니라, 해리엇의 비밀이 자기 입에서 새어 나가지 않은 것을 기뻐하고, 또 결코 새어 나가게 해서는 안 되며 그러지도 않으리라 다짐할 시간까지 있었다. 그것이 이제 그녀가 가엾은 벗에게 베풀 수 있는 유일한 도움이었다. 둘 중에서 해리엇이야말로 비할 수 없이 더 자격 있는 사람이니 그에게 애정을 자기에게서 해리엇에게로 옮겨 달라고 간청하게끔 그녀를 부추겼을 법한 그런 감정의 영웅적 행위라든가—혹은 그가 둘 다와 결혼할 수는 없으니, 아무 이유도 밝히지 않은 채 그 자리에서 영영 그를 거절하기로 결심하는 한결 단순한 숭고함조차도, 엠마에게는 없었다. 그녀는 해리엇을 아프게, 또 뉘우치는 마음으로 가엾이 여겼다. 그러나 있음 직하거나 사리에 맞는 모든 것에 거스르는, 미친 듯이 날뛰는 관대함의 비약 따위는 그녀의 머릿속에 들어오지 않았다. 그녀는 벗을 그릇된 길로 이끌었고, 그것은 영영 그녀의 마음을 책망하는 일이 되리라. 그러나 그녀의 판단력은 그 감정만큼이나 굳건했으며, 그에게 그런 결합—더없이 어울리지 않고 격을 떨어뜨리는 결합—을 단호히 배격하는 점에서는 여느 때처럼 흔들림이 없었다. 그녀가 갈 길은 분명했다, 비록 아주 순탄하지는 않았어도. 그리하여 그토록 간곡한 청을 받은 그녀는 입을 열었다. 무어라 말했던가? 물론, 마땅히 해야 할 말을 했다. 숙녀란 늘 그러는 법이다. 절망할 필요는 없다는 것을 내비치고, 그가 직접 더 말하도록 청할 만큼의 말을 했다. 그는 한때 절망했었다. 한동안 모든 희망을 짓뭉개 버린, 조심하고 입을 다물라는 그런 단속의 말을 들었던 것이다. 그녀가 먼저 그의 말을 듣기를 거절했으니까. 그 변화는 어쩌면 다소 갑작스러웠는지도 몰랐다. 한 바퀴 더 돌자던 그녀의 제안이며, 방금 제 손으로 끝맺은 대화를 다시 잇자던 것이, 조금 별나게 보였을지도 몰랐다! 그녀 자신도 그 앞뒤가 맞지 않음을 느꼈다. 그러나 미스터 나이틀리는 더없이 너그러이 그것을 받아들였고, 더 이상의 해명을 구하지 않았다."]], [["Seldom, very seldom, does complete truth belong to any human disclosure; seldom can it happen that something is not a little disguised, or a little mistaken; but where, as in this case, though the conduct is mistaken, the feelings are not, it may not be very material.—Mr.", "좀처럼, 참으로 좀처럼, 완전한 진실이 인간의 어떤 고백에 깃드는 일은 없다. 무언가가 조금 꾸며지거나 조금 잘못 전해지지 않는 경우는 드문 법이다."], ["Knightley could not impute to Emma a more relenting heart than she possessed, or a heart more disposed to accept of his.", "그러나 이 경우처럼, 비록 행동은 잘못 전해졌을지언정 감정만은 그렇지 않다면, 그것은 그리 중요하지 않을지도 모른다. 미스터 나이틀리로서는, 엠마가 실제로 지닌 것보다 더 누그러진 마음이나, 그의 마음을 받아들일 채비가 더 갖춰진 마음을 그녀에게 잘못 헤아릴 까닭이 없었던 것이다."]], [["He had, in fact, been wholly unsuspicious of his own influence. He had followed her into the shrubbery with no idea of trying it. He had come, in his anxiety to see how she bore Frank Churchill’s engagement, with no selfish view, no view at all, but of endeavouring, if she allowed him an opening, to soothe or to counsel her.—The rest had been the work of the moment, the immediate effect of what he heard, on his feelings. The delightful assurance of her total indifference towards Frank Churchill, of her having a heart completely disengaged from him, had given birth to the hope, that, in time, he might gain her affection himself;—but it had been no present hope—he had only, in the momentary conquest of eagerness over judgment, aspired to be told that she did not forbid his attempt to attach her.—The superior hopes which gradually opened were so much the more enchanting.—The affection, which he had been asking to be allowed to create, if he could, was already his!—Within half an hour, he had passed from a thoroughly distressed state of mind, to something so like perfect happiness, that it could bear no other name.", "사실 그는 자기 자신의 영향력에 대해 전혀 짐작하지 못하고 있었다. 그것을 시험해 볼 생각 따위는 조금도 없이 그녀를 따라 관목 숲으로 들어선 것이었다. 그는 엠마가 프랭크 처칠의 약혼을 어떻게 견디고 있는지 보고 싶은 걱정에 찾아온 것이었지, 무슨 사심에서가 아니라, 아니 아무 속셈도 없이, 다만 그녀가 틈을 내어 준다면 그녀를 달래거나 조언해 주려는 마음에서였다. 그 나머지는 순간의 작용이었고, 그가 들은 말이 그의 감정에 곧장 미친 결과였다. 그녀가 프랭크 처칠에게 완전히 무심하다는 것, 그를 향해 조금도 얽매이지 않은 마음을 지녔다는 것—그 기쁜 확신이, 머지않아 자기가 직접 그녀의 사랑을 얻을 수도 있으리라는 희망을 낳았다. 그러나 그것은 당장의 희망은 아니었다. 그는 다만, 한순간 간절함이 판단을 이긴 끝에, 그녀를 끌어들이려는 자기 시도를 그녀가 막지는 않는다는 말만이라도 듣기를 바랐을 뿐이었다. 차츰 열리기 시작한 더 큰 희망은 그만큼 더 황홀했다. 할 수만 있다면 빚어 내도록 허락해 달라고 청하던 그 애정이, 이미 그의 것이었다니! 반 시간도 채 못 되어, 그는 더없이 괴로운 심정에서, 달리 부를 이름이 없을 만큼 완전한 행복과 다름없는 무언가로 옮아가 있었다."]], [["Her change was equal.—This one half-hour had given to each the same precious certainty of being beloved, had cleared from each the same degree of ignorance, jealousy, or distrust.—On his side, there had been a long-standing jealousy, old as the arrival, or even the expectation, of Frank Churchill.—He had been in love with Emma, and jealous of Frank Churchill, from about the same period, one sentiment having probably enlightened him as to the other. It was his jealousy of Frank Churchill that had taken him from the country.—The Box Hill party had decided him on going away. He would save himself from witnessing again such permitted, encouraged attentions.—He had gone to learn to be indifferent.—But he had gone to a wrong place. There was too much domestic happiness in his brother’s house; woman wore too amiable a form in it; Isabella was too much like Emma—differing only in those striking inferiorities, which always brought the other in brilliancy before him, for much to have been done, even had his time been longer.—He had stayed on, however, vigorously, day after day—till this very morning’s post had conveyed the history of Jane Fairfax.—Then, with the gladness which must be felt, nay, which he did not scruple to feel, having never believed Frank Churchill to be at all deserving Emma, was there so much fond solicitude, so much keen anxiety for her, that he could stay no longer. He had ridden home through the rain; and had walked up directly after dinner, to see how this sweetest and best of all creatures, faultless in spite of all her faults, bore the discovery.", "그녀의 변화도 그에 못지않았다. 이 한 번의 반 시간이 두 사람 각자에게 사랑받고 있다는 똑같이 소중한 확신을 안겨 주었고, 각자에게서 똑같은 정도의 무지와 질투와 불신을 걷어 내 주었다. 그의 쪽에는 오래 묵은 질투가 있었으니, 프랭크 처칠이 도착한 때만큼, 아니 그가 오리라 기대되던 때만큼이나 해묵은 질투였다. 그는 거의 같은 무렵부터 엠마를 사랑하고 또 프랭크 처칠을 질투해 왔으니, 아마 한쪽 감정이 다른 한쪽을 그에게 일깨워 준 셈이었다. 그를 시골에서 떠나게 한 것도 바로 프랭크 처칠을 향한 그 질투였다. 박스 힐 모임이 그가 떠나기로 마음을 정하게 했다. 그처럼 허락되고 부추겨진 관심을 또다시 지켜보는 일에서 자신을 구해 내려는 것이었다. 그는 무심해지는 법을 배우러 떠났던 것이다. 그러나 그가 간 곳이 잘못되었다. 동생의 집에는 가정의 행복이 너무 많았고, 그곳에서 여자는 너무도 사랑스러운 모습을 하고 있었다. 이저벨라는 너무도 엠마를 닮아 있었다—다만 그 두드러진 모자란 점들에서만 달랐을 뿐인데, 그 점들이 도리어 엠마를 늘 더 눈부시게 그의 앞에 떠올려 주었으니, 설령 머무는 기간이 더 길었던들 무슨 소용이 있었으랴. 그래도 그는 날마다 꿋꿋이 버티며 머물렀다—바로 그날 아침 우편이 제인 페어팩스의 사연을 전해 올 때까지. 그러자, 응당 느낄 수밖에 없는, 아니, 프랭크 처칠이 엠마에게 도무지 어울리는 사람이라고는 한 번도 믿지 않았던 터라 거리낌 없이 느낀 그 기쁨과 더불어, 그녀를 향한 애틋한 염려가, 그녀를 향한 사무치는 근심이 어찌나 큰지, 그는 더 이상 머물 수가 없었다. 그는 빗속을 뚫고 말을 달려 집으로 돌아왔고, 식사를 마치자마자 곧장 걸어 올라온 것이었다—이 더없이 사랑스럽고 더없이 훌륭한 사람이, 그 온갖 흠에도 불구하고 흠 하나 없는 그 사람이, 이 발각을 어떻게 견디고 있는지 보려고."]], [["He had found her agitated and low.—Frank Churchill was a villain.— He heard her declare that she had never loved him. Frank Churchill’s character was not desperate.—She was his own Emma, by hand and word, when they returned into the house; and if he could have thought of Frank Churchill then, he might have deemed him a very good sort of fellow.", "그는 그녀가 동요하고 풀이 죽어 있는 것을 보았다. 프랭크 처칠은 악당이었다. 그는 그녀가 그를 한 번도 사랑한 적이 없다고 단언하는 것을 들었다. 프랭크 처칠의 됨됨이도 그리 절망적이지는 않았다. 두 사람이 집으로 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 손길로도 말로도 그의 엠마였다. 그리고 그 순간 그가 프랭크 처칠을 떠올릴 수 있었다면, 그를 아주 괜찮은 사내쯤으로 여겼을지도 몰랐다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter XIV", "# 제3권 14장"]], [["What totally different feelings did Emma take back into the house from what she had brought out!—she had then been only daring to hope for a little respite of suffering;—she was now in an exquisite flutter of happiness, and such happiness moreover as she believed must still be greater when the flutter should have passed away.", "엠마가 집 안으로 지니고 들어온 마음은, 조금 전 들고 나갔던 마음과 어쩌면 그토록 다른 것이었을까!—그때만 해도 그녀는 고작 괴로움이 잠시나마 누그러지기를 감히 바랐을 뿐이었다.—그런데 지금 그녀는 더없이 황홀한 행복의 설렘에 휩싸여 있었고, 더구나 그 행복이란, 설렘이 가라앉고 나면 한층 더 커지리라고 믿어 의심치 않을 만한 것이었다."]], [["They sat down to tea—the same party round the same table—how often it had been collected!—and how often had her eyes fallen on the same shrubs in the lawn, and observed the same beautiful effect of the western sun!—But never in such a state of spirits, never in any thing like it; and it was with difficulty that she could summon enough of her usual self to be the attentive lady of the house, or even the attentive daughter.", "그들은 차를 들려고 자리에 앉았다—똑같은 사람들이 똑같은 식탁에 둘러앉았다—이 자리가 얼마나 여러 번 모였던가!—그리고 그녀의 눈이 잔디밭의 똑같은 관목 숲에 얼마나 여러 번 머물렀으며, 서녘 해의 똑같은 아름다운 빛을 얼마나 여러 번 바라보았던가!—그러나 이런 마음 상태로는 한 번도 없었고, 이와 비슷한 어떤 것으로도 한 번도 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 평소의 자기 모습을 그러모아 세심한 안주인 노릇은커녕 세심한 딸 노릇이라도 해내기가 여간 힘들지 않았다."]], [["Poor Mr. Woodhouse little suspected what was plotting against him in the breast of that man whom he was so cordially welcoming, and so anxiously hoping might not have taken cold from his ride.—Could he have seen the heart, he would have cared very little for the lungs; but without the most distant imagination of the impending evil, without the slightest perception of any thing extraordinary in the looks or ways of either, he repeated to them very comfortably all the articles of news he had received from Mr.", "가엾은 미스터 우드하우스는, 자신이 그토록 진심으로 맞아들이고, 마차로 오는 길에 행여 감기라도 걸리지 않았을까 그토록 노심초사 걱정하는 그 남자의 가슴속에서, 자기를 향해 어떤 음모가 꾸며지고 있는지 까맣게 몰랐다.—만약 그 속마음을 들여다볼 수 있었더라면, 폐 따위는 거의 안중에도 없었으리라."], ["Perry, and talked on with much self-contentment, totally unsuspicious of what they could have told him in return.", "그러나 다가오는 화근을 손톱만큼도 상상하지 못하고, 두 사람의 표정이나 거동에서 그 어떤 심상찮은 기미도 전혀 알아채지 못한 채, 그는 더없이 편안하게 미스터 페리에게서 들은 소식들을 죄다 두 사람에게 옮기며, 정작 그들이 자기에게 들려줄 수 있었을 이야기는 까맣게 모른 채 무척 흐뭇한 얼굴로 떠들어 댔다."]], [["As long as Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리가 그들과 함께 있는 동안은 엠마의 열기가 계속되었다."], ["Knightley remained with them, Emma’s fever continued; but when he was gone, she began to be a little tranquillised and subdued—and in the course of the sleepless night, which was the tax for such an evening, she found one or two such very serious points to consider, as made her feel, that even her happiness must have some alloy.", "그러나 그가 떠나고 나자, 그녀는 조금씩 가라앉고 누그러지기 시작했다.—그리고 그런 저녁의 대가로 치러야 했던 잠 못 드는 밤을 지새우는 동안, 그녀는 곰곰이 따져 보아야 할 매우 심각한 문제 한두 가지를 발견하고는, 자신의 행복마저 얼마간의 불순물을 섞을 수밖에 없음을 느꼈다. 아버지—그리고 해리엇."], ["Her father—and Harriet.", "혼자 있노라면 그 둘이 제각기 거는 권리의 무게가 고스란히 느껴지지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 어떻게 하면 두 사람의 평안을 모두 최대한 지켜 줄 수 있을까가 문제였다."], ["She could not be alone without feeling the full weight of their separate claims; and how to guard the comfort of both to the utmost, was the question.", "아버지에 관해서는 곧 답이 나오는 문제였다. 미스터 나이틀리가 무엇을 청할지는 아직 잘 몰랐지만, 자기 마음과 아주 짧게 의논한 끝에, 아버지 곁을 결코 떠나지 않으리라는 가장 엄숙한 결심이 섰다.—그녀는 그런 생각을 떠올린 것조차 생각의 죄인 양 눈물을 흘릴 지경이었다."], ["With respect to her father, it was a question soon answered.", "아버지가 살아 계신 동안은 그저 약혼한 사이로만 남아 있어야 한다. 그러나 그녀는, 자기를 떼어 갈 위험만 사라진다면, 도리어 아버지에게 더 큰 위안이 될 수 있으리라고 스스로를 다독였다.—해리엇에게 어떻게 하는 것이 최선일지는 더 어려운 결정이었다.—어떻게 하면 쓸데없는 아픔을 면하게 해 줄 수 있을까."], ["She hardly knew yet what Mr.", "어떻게 하면 조금이라도 보상을 해 줄 수 있을까."], ["Knightley would ask; but a very short parley with her own heart produced the most solemn resolution of never quitting her father.—She even wept over the idea of it, as a sin of thought.", "어떻게 하면 그녀에게 가장 덜 적으로 보일 수 있을까?—이 문제들에 관해서는 그녀의 당혹과 괴로움이 무척 컸다.—그래서 그녀의 마음은, 일찍이 그것을 에워쌌던 온갖 쓰라린 자책과 서글픈 후회를 몇 번이고 되짚어 나가야 했다.—그녀가 끝내 결심할 수 있었던 것은 다만, 여전히 해리엇과 마주치는 일은 피하고, 꼭 전해야 할 모든 일은 편지로 알리리라는 것뿐이었다. 또 바로 지금 그녀를 한동안 하이베리에서 떼어 놓을 수 있다면 이루 말할 수 없이 바람직하리라는 것—그리고 한 가지 계획을 더 마음껏 그려 보며—브런즈윅 스퀘어로 그녀를 초대받게 하는 일이 어쩌면 가능할지도 모른다고 거의 결심하기에 이르렀다.—이저벨라는 해리엇을 마음에 들어 했었다."], ["While he lived, it must be only an engagement; but she flattered herself, that if divested of the danger of drawing her away, it might become an increase of comfort to him.—How to do her best by Harriet, was of more difficult decision;—how to spare her from any unnecessary pain; how to make her any possible atonement; how to appear least her enemy?—On these subjects, her perplexity and distress were very great—and her mind had to pass again and again through every bitter reproach and sorrowful regret that had ever surrounded it.—She could only resolve at last, that she would still avoid a meeting with her, and communicate all that need be told by letter; that it would be inexpressibly desirable to have her removed just now for a time from Highbury, and—indulging in one scheme more—nearly resolve, that it might be practicable to get an invitation for her to Brunswick Square.—Isabella had been pleased with Harriet; and a few weeks spent in London must give her some amusement.—She did not think it in Harriet’s nature to escape being benefited by novelty and variety, by the streets, the shops, and the children.—At any rate, it would be a proof of attention and kindness in herself, from whom every thing was due; a separation for the present; an averting of the evil day, when they must all be together again.", "그리고 런던에서 몇 주를 보내고 나면 그녀도 얼마간 기분 전환이 될 것이다.—거리와 상점들과 아이들, 그 새로움과 다채로움에서 해리엇이 득을 보지 못할 리는 없으리라는 것이 그녀의 생각이었다.—어쨌거나 그것은, 모든 것을 빚진 처지인 자기로서 마음을 쓰고 친절을 베푼다는 증거가 될 터였다. 당분간의 이별이요, 모두가 다시 한자리에 모여야 할 그 괴로운 날을 미루어 두는 일이기도 했다."]], [["She rose early, and wrote her letter to Harriet; an employment which left her so very serious, so nearly sad, that Mr.", "그녀는 일찍 일어나, 해리엇에게 보낼 편지를 썼다."], ["Knightley, in walking up to Hartfield to breakfast, did not arrive at all too soon; and half an hour stolen afterwards to go over the same ground again with him, literally and figuratively, was quite necessary to reinstate her in a proper share of the happiness of the evening before.", "그 일을 하고 나니 어찌나 마음이 무겁고 거의 슬퍼졌던지, 아침을 들러 하트필드로 걸어 올라온 미스터 나이틀리는 결코 너무 일찍 온 셈이 아니었다. 그러고 나서 그와 함께, 글자 그대로 또 비유적으로, 똑같은 자리를 다시 더듬어 보며 슬쩍 빼낸 반 시간은, 어제저녁의 행복을 제 몫만큼 되찾는 데 꼭 필요한 것이었다."]], [["He had not left her long, by no means long enough for her to have the slightest inclination for thinking of any body else, when a letter was brought her from Randalls—a very thick letter;—she guessed what it must contain, and deprecated the necessity of reading it.—She was now in perfect charity with Frank Churchill; she wanted no explanations, she wanted only to have her thoughts to herself—and as for understanding any thing he wrote, she was sure she was incapable of it.—It must be waded through, however.", "그가 그녀 곁을 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않았을 때—다른 누군가를 생각할 마음이 손톱만큼이라도 일 만큼의 시간은 결코 아니었을 때—랜덜스에서 편지 한 통이 왔다.—아주 두툼한 편지였다.—그 안에 무엇이 들었을지 짐작이 갔기에, 그녀는 그것을 읽어야 한다는 사실이 마뜩잖았다.—그녀는 이제 프랭크 처칠을 향해 더없이 너그러운 마음이었다. 아무런 해명도 필요 없었고, 그저 자기 생각에만 잠겨 있고 싶을 뿐이었다.—그리고 그가 쓴 글을 무엇 하나 이해하기로 말하자면, 자기로서는 도무지 그럴 재간이 없으리라고 확신했다.—그러나 어쨌든 헤쳐 나가야 했다."], ["She opened the packet; it was too surely so;—a note from Mrs.", "그녀는 봉투를 열었다."], ["Weston to herself, ushered in the letter from Frank to Mrs. Weston.", "과연 그러했다.—웨스턴 부인이 그녀에게 보내는 짧은 편지가, 프랭크가 웨스턴 부인에게 보낸 편지를 안내하고 있었다."]], [["“I have the greatest pleasure, my dear Emma, in forwarding to you the enclosed.", "\"사랑하는 엠마, 동봉한 것을 너에게 전하게 되어 더없이 기쁘구나."], ["I know what thorough justice you will do it, and have scarcely a doubt of its happy effect.—I think we shall never materially disagree about the writer again; but I will not delay you by a long preface.—We are quite well.—This letter has been the cure of all the little nervousness I have been feeling lately.—I did not quite like your looks on Tuesday, but it was an ungenial morning; and though you will never own being affected by weather, I think every body feels a north-east wind.—I felt for your dear father very much in the storm of Tuesday afternoon and yesterday morning, but had the comfort of hearing last night, by Mr.", "네가 이것에 얼마나 온당한 평가를 내려 줄지 잘 알고, 또 그것이 좋은 결과를 가져오리라는 것도 거의 의심치 않는단다.—이제 우리가 이 글쓴이를 두고 크게 의견이 갈리는 일은 다시 없으리라 생각해. 하지만 긴 서두로 너를 붙잡아 두지는 않으마.—우리는 아주 잘 지낸단다.—이 편지가, 요즘 내가 느끼던 자잘한 초조함을 죄다 낫게 해 주었어.—화요일에 보니 네 안색이 영 마음에 들지 않더구나."], ["Perry, that it had not made him ill.", "하지만 날씨가 궂은 아침이었으니까. 너는 날씨에 영향받는다는 걸 결코 인정하지 않겠지만, 북동풍이 불면 누구든 다 느끼는 법이란다.—화요일 오후와 어제 아침의 그 폭풍우 속에서 네 가엾은 아버님이 무척 걱정되었지만, 간밤에 미스터 페리를 통해 그분이 병이 나지 않으셨다는 소식을 듣고 마음을 놓았단다."]], [["“Yours ever, “A.", "이만 줄이마,\nA."], ["W.”", "W.\""]], [["[To Mrs. Weston.]", "[웨스턴 부인에게.]"]], [["Windsor—July.", "윈저—7월."]], [["MY DEAR MADAM,", "친애하는 부인께,"]], [["“If I made myself intelligible yesterday, this letter will be expected; but expected or not, I know it will be read with candour and indulgence.—You are all goodness, and I believe there will be need of even all your goodness to allow for some parts of my past conduct.—But I have been forgiven by one who had still more to resent.", "\"어제 제가 제 뜻을 알아들으실 만하게 말씀드렸다면, 이 편지를 기다리고 계시겠지요. 하지만 기다리셨든 아니든, 너그럽고 관대한 마음으로 읽어 주시리라는 것을 압니다.—부인은 더할 나위 없이 선량한 분이시고, 제 지난 행실의 어떤 부분들을 헤아려 주시자면 그 선량함을 다 동원하셔도 모자라리라 믿습니다.—그러나 저는, 저를 원망할 까닭이 그보다 더 많았던 한 사람에게서 이미 용서를 받았습니다."], ["My courage rises while I write.", "이 글을 쓰는 동안 용기가 솟습니다."], ["It is very difficult for the prosperous to be humble.", "형편이 좋은 자가 겸손하기란 무척 어려운 법이지요."], ["I have already met with such success in two applications for pardon, that I may be in danger of thinking myself too sure of yours, and of those among your friends who have had any ground of offence.—You must all endeavour to comprehend the exact nature of my situation when I first arrived at Randalls; you must consider me as having a secret which was to be kept at all hazards.", "저는 이미 두 번의 용서를 청하는 일에서 그만한 성공을 거두었기에, 어쩌면 부인의 용서와, 또 제게 노여워할 만한 까닭이 있었던 부인의 벗들의 용서마저 너무 쉽게 확신해 버릴 위험에 빠질지도 모르겠습니다.—제가 처음 랜덜스에 도착했을 때 제 처지가 어떠했는지, 그 정확한 본질을 모두 헤아려 주셔야 합니다. 저를, 무슨 일이 있어도 지켜야 할 비밀을 지닌 사람으로 여겨 주셔야 합니다."], ["This was the fact.", "사실이 그러했습니다."], ["My right to place myself in a situation requiring such concealment, is another question.", "그런 은폐를 요구하는 처지에 저를 둘 권리가 제게 있었느냐는 것은 별개의 문제이지요."], ["I shall not discuss it here.", "그것을 여기서 따지지는 않겠습니다."], ["For my temptation to think it a right, I refer every caviller to a brick house, sashed windows below, and casements above, in Highbury.", "그것을 권리라고 여기게 만든 제 유혹에 관해서라면, 트집 잡으려는 분이 계시면 누구든 하이베리에 있는, 아래는 미닫이창이고 위는 여닫이창인 벽돌집 한 채를 보시라고 권하겠습니다. 저는 감히 그녀에게 드러내 놓고 마음을 고백할 수 없었습니다."], ["I dared not address her openly; my difficulties in the then state of Enscombe must be too well known to require definition; and I was fortunate enough to prevail, before we parted at Weymouth, and to induce the most upright female mind in the creation to stoop in charity to a secret engagement.—Had she refused, I should have gone mad.—But you will be ready to say, what was your hope in doing this?—What did you look forward to?—To any thing, every thing—to time, chance, circumstance, slow effects, sudden bursts, perseverance and weariness, health and sickness.", "당시 엔스컴의 형편에서 제가 겪던 어려움은 굳이 설명할 필요도 없을 만큼 너무도 잘 알려져 있겠지요. 그래도 저는 다행히, 웨이머스에서 헤어지기 전에 뜻을 이루어, 이 세상에서 가장 올곧은 여인의 마음을 자비로이 굽혀 비밀 약혼에 응하게 할 수 있었습니다.—만약 그녀가 거절했더라면 저는 미쳐 버렸을 것입니다.—그러나 부인은 이렇게 물으시겠지요, 그래서 이런 짓을 하면서 무엇을 바랐느냐고?—무엇을 내다보았느냐고?—무엇이든, 그 모든 것을—시간을, 우연을, 형편을, 더딘 효험을, 갑작스러운 폭발을, 끈기와 권태를, 건강과 병환을 바랐습니다."], ["Every possibility of good was before me, and the first of blessings secured, in obtaining her promises of faith and correspondence.", "온갖 좋은 가능성이 제 앞에 있었고, 그녀에게서 변치 않는 마음과 서신 왕래의 약속을 얻어 냄으로써 가장 큰 축복부터 확보해 둔 셈이었습니다. 더 설명이 필요하시다면, 친애하는 부인, 저는 부인 남편의 아들이라는 영예를 지녔고, 또 좋은 일을 바라는 기질을 물려받는 이점을 누렸으니, 그 어떤 집이나 땅의 유산도 그 가치에 견줄 수 없는 것이지요.—그러니 이런 형편에 놓인 채, 랜덜스로 첫 방문을 오는 저를 그려 보십시오.—그리고 여기서 저는 잘못을 자각합니다."], ["If you need farther explanation, I have the honour, my dear madam, of being your husband’s son, and the advantage of inheriting a disposition to hope for good, which no inheritance of houses or lands can ever equal the value of.—See me, then, under these circumstances, arriving on my first visit to Randalls;—and here I am conscious of wrong, for that visit might have been sooner paid.", "그 방문은 더 일찍 했어야 마땅했으니까요. 돌이켜 보시면, 제가 미스 페어팩스가 하이베리에 와 있을 때까지 오지 않았음을 아시게 될 것입니다."], ["You will look back and see that I did not come till Miss Fairfax was in Highbury; and as you were the person slighted, you will forgive me instantly; but I must work on my father’s compassion, by reminding him, that so long as I absented myself from his house, so long I lost the blessing of knowing you.", "그리고 푸대접받은 분이 바로 부인이시니, 부인은 곧바로 저를 용서해 주시겠지요. 다만 저는 아버지의 동정에 호소해야 하니, 제가 그분 댁을 멀리하는 동안은 그만큼 부인을 알게 되는 축복을 잃고 있었음을 일깨워 드려야겠습니다."], ["My behaviour, during the very happy fortnight which I spent with you, did not, I hope, lay me open to reprehension, excepting on one point.", "부인 댁에서 보낸 그 더없이 행복했던 두 주 동안의 제 처신은, 부디 한 가지 점만 빼고는 비난받을 빌미를 주지 않았기를 바랍니다. 이제 저는 부인 댁에 속해 있던 동안의 제 행동 가운데, 정작 가장 중요한, 유일하게 중요한 부분에 이르렀습니다."], ["And now I come to the principal, the only important part of my conduct while belonging to you, which excites my own anxiety, or requires very solicitous explanation.", "제 자신을 불안하게 하고 매우 정성스러운 해명을 요구하는 부분이지요. 더없는 존경과 더없이 따뜻한 우정을 담아 미스 우드하우스를 언급합니다."], ["With the greatest respect, and the warmest friendship, do I mention Miss Woodhouse; my father perhaps will think I ought to add, with the deepest humiliation.—A few words which dropped from him yesterday spoke his opinion, and some censure I acknowledge myself liable to.—My behaviour to Miss Woodhouse indicated, I believe, more than it ought.—In order to assist a concealment so essential to me, I was led on to make more than an allowable use of the sort of intimacy into which we were immediately thrown.—I cannot deny that Miss Woodhouse was my ostensible object—but I am sure you will believe the declaration, that had I not been convinced of her indifference, I would not have been induced by any selfish views to go on.—Amiable and delightful as Miss Woodhouse is, she never gave me the idea of a young woman likely to be attached; and that she was perfectly free from any tendency to being attached to me, was as much my conviction as my wish.—She received my attentions with an easy, friendly, goodhumoured playfulness, which exactly suited me.", "어쩌면 아버지께서는, 거기에 더없는 부끄러움을 담아야 한다고 덧붙이고 싶어 하실지도 모르겠군요.—어제 아버지께서 흘리신 몇 마디 말씀이 그분의 생각을 드러냈고, 제가 얼마간의 책망을 받아 마땅하다는 것도 인정합니다.—미스 우드하우스를 향한 제 처신은, 아마도 마땅한 정도를 넘어선 무언가를 내비쳤을 것입니다.—제게 그토록 긴요했던 은폐를 돕기 위해, 저는 우리가 곧장 빠져든 그런 친밀함을 허용된 정도 이상으로 이용하는 데까지 이끌려 갔습니다.—미스 우드하우스가 제 표면상의 목표였음은 부인할 수 없습니다—그러나 부인은 이 고백을 믿어 주시리라 확신합니다, 만약 제가 그녀의 무관심을 확신하지 못했더라면, 그 어떤 이기적인 속셈에도 이끌려 그 일을 계속하지는 않았으리라는 것을요.—미스 우드하우스가 아무리 상냥하고 유쾌한 분이라 해도, 그녀는 제게 연정을 품을 법한 젊은 여성이라는 인상을 한 번도 준 적이 없습니다. 그리고 그녀가 저에게 연정을 품을 기색이 조금도 없다는 것은, 제 바람인 동시에 제 확신이기도 했습니다.—그녀는 제 친절을 편안하고 다정하며 너그러운 장난기로 받아 주었는데, 그것이 제게는 꼭 알맞았습니다."], ["We seemed to understand each other.", "우리는 서로를 이해하고 있는 듯했습니다."], ["From our relative situation, those attentions were her due, and were felt to be so.—Whether Miss Woodhouse began really to understand me before the expiration of that fortnight, I cannot say;—when I called to take leave of her, I remember that I was within a moment of confessing the truth, and I then fancied she was not without suspicion; but I have no doubt of her having since detected me, at least in some degree.—She may not have surmised the whole, but her quickness must have penetrated a part.", "우리 사이의 처지로 보면, 그런 친절은 그녀가 받아 마땅한 것이었고, 또 그렇게 받아들여졌습니다.—미스 우드하우스가 그 두 주가 끝나기 전에 정말로 저를 알아채기 시작했는지는, 저로서는 말할 수 없습니다.—제가 작별 인사를 하러 갔을 때, 저는 진실을 고백하기 직전까지 갔던 것이 기억나고, 그때 저는 그녀가 의심을 품지 않은 것은 아니라고 생각했습니다. 그러나 그 뒤로 그녀가 적어도 어느 정도는 저를 간파했음을 저는 의심치 않습니다.—전부를 짐작하지는 못했을지 몰라도, 그녀의 영민함이 일부는 꿰뚫어 보았을 것입니다."], ["I cannot doubt it.", "그것을 저는 의심할 수 없습니다."], ["You will find, whenever the subject becomes freed from its present restraints, that it did not take her wholly by surprize.", "이 일이 지금의 제약에서 풀려나는 날이 오면, 이것이 그녀에게 전혀 뜻밖의 일은 아니었음을 부인도 아시게 될 것입니다."], ["She frequently gave me hints of it.", "그녀는 자주 그런 기미를 제게 내비쳤습니다."], ["I remember her telling me at the ball, that I owed Mrs. Elton gratitude for her attentions to Miss Fairfax.—I hope this history of my conduct towards her will be admitted by you and my father as great extenuation of what you saw amiss.", "무도회에서 그녀가 제게, 제가 미스 페어팩스에게 친절을 베풀어 준 엘턴 부인에게 고마워해야 마땅하다고 말하던 것이 기억납니다.—그녀를 향한 제 처신의 이 내력을, 부디 부인과 아버지께서 잘못 보셨던 부분에 대한 큰 참작 사유로 받아들여 주시기를 바랍니다."], ["While you considered me as having sinned against Emma Woodhouse, I could deserve nothing from either.", "두 분이 저를 엠마 우드하우스에게 죄를 지은 자로 여기시는 동안은, 저는 두 분 어느 쪽에게서도 아무것도 받을 자격이 없었으니까요."], ["Acquit me here, and procure for me, when it is allowable, the acquittal and good wishes of that said Emma Woodhouse, whom I regard with so much brotherly affection, as to long to have her as deeply and as happily in love as myself.—Whatever strange things I said or did during that fortnight, you have now a key to.", "이 점에서 저를 무죄로 놓아주시고, 또 허락되는 때가 오면, 제가 누이를 향한 듯한 애정으로 깊이 아끼는, 그래서 그녀도 저만큼이나 깊고 행복한 사랑에 빠지기를 간절히 바라는, 바로 그 엠마 우드하우스의 사면과 축복을 제게 얻어다 주십시오.—제가 그 두 주 동안 했던 그 어떤 이상한 말이나 행동에 대해서도, 이제 부인은 그 열쇠를 쥐고 계십니다."], ["My heart was in Highbury, and my business was to get my body thither as often as might be, and with the least suspicion.", "제 마음은 하이베리에 있었고, 제 일이라곤 제 몸뚱이를 되도록 자주, 되도록 의심을 사지 않으면서 그곳으로 옮겨 가는 것이었습니다."], ["If you remember any queernesses, set them all to the right account.—Of the pianoforte so much talked of, I feel it only necessary to say, that its being ordered was absolutely unknown to Miss F—, who would never have allowed me to send it, had any choice been given her.—The delicacy of her mind throughout the whole engagement, my dear madam, is much beyond my power of doing justice to.", "혹시 무슨 기이한 점들이 기억나시거든, 모두 제대로 된 사정에 비추어 헤아려 주십시오.—그토록 말이 많았던 그 피아노에 관해서는, 그것을 주문한 일을 미스 F—는 전혀 몰랐다는 것만 말씀드리면 충분하겠습니다. 만약 그녀에게 선택권이 주어졌더라면, 그녀는 결코 제가 그것을 보내도록 두지 않았을 것입니다.—이 약혼 기간 내내 그녀가 보여 준 마음의 섬세함은, 친애하는 부인, 제가 온당히 그려 내기에는 너무도 벅찬 것입니다."], ["You will soon, I earnestly hope, know her thoroughly yourself.—No description can describe her.", "머지않아, 간절히 바라건대, 부인께서도 그녀를 속속들이 아시게 될 것입니다.—어떤 묘사로도 그녀를 다 그려 낼 수 없습니다."], ["She must tell you herself what she is—yet not by word, for never was there a human creature who would so designedly suppress her own merit.—Since I began this letter, which will be longer than I foresaw, I have heard from her.—She gives a good account of her own health; but as she never complains, I dare not depend.", "그녀가 어떤 사람인지는 그녀 스스로 부인께 말해 드려야겠지요—그렇다고 말로는 아닙니다. 자기 장점을 그토록 일부러 감추려 드는 사람은 일찍이 없었으니까요.—생각보다 길어지고 있는 이 편지를 쓰기 시작한 뒤로, 저는 그녀에게서 소식을 받았습니다.—그녀는 자기 건강이 좋다고 전하지만, 그녀는 결코 불평하는 법이 없으니, 저는 감히 그 말을 믿지 못하겠습니다."], ["I want to have your opinion of her looks.", "그녀의 안색에 대한 부인의 의견을 듣고 싶습니다."], ["I know you will soon call on her; she is living in dread of the visit.", "부인이 머지않아 그녀를 찾아가시리라는 것을 압니다. 그녀는 그 방문을 두려워하며 지내고 있습니다."], ["Perhaps it is paid already.", "어쩌면 이미 다녀오셨는지도 모르겠군요."], ["Let me hear from you without delay; I am impatient for a thousand particulars.", "지체 없이 소식 전해 주십시오. 저는 수천 가지 자세한 사정이 알고 싶어 안달이 나 있습니다."], ["Remember how few minutes I was at Randalls, and in how bewildered, how mad a state: and I am not much better yet; still insane either from happiness or misery.", "제가 랜덜스에 머문 시간이 얼마나 짧았는지, 그리고 그때 제가 얼마나 얼떨떨하고 미친 상태였는지 떠올려 주십시오. 그리고 저는 아직도 별반 나아지지 않았으니, 여전히 행복 때문이든 비참함 때문이든 제정신이 아닙니다."], ["When I think of the kindness and favour I have met with, of her excellence and patience, and my uncle’s generosity, I am mad with joy: but when I recollect all the uneasiness I occasioned her, and how little I deserve to be forgiven, I am mad with anger.", "제가 받은 친절과 호의를, 그녀의 훌륭함과 인내를, 외삼촌의 너그러움을 생각하면 저는 기뻐서 미칠 지경입니다. 그러나 제가 그녀에게 안긴 온갖 불안을, 그리고 제가 용서받을 자격이 얼마나 없는지를 떠올리면, 저는 분해서 미칠 지경입니다."], ["If I could but see her again!—But I must not propose it yet.", "그녀를 다시 한 번 볼 수만 있다면!—그러나 아직은 그것을 청해서는 안 됩니다."], ["My uncle has been too good for me to encroach.—I must still add to this long letter.", "외삼촌이 제게 너무 잘해 주셨으니, 더 졸라 댈 수는 없지요.—이 긴 편지에 그래도 더 보태야겠습니다."], ["You have not heard all that you ought to hear.", "부인이 들으셔야 할 이야기를 아직 다 듣지 못하셨으니까요."], ["I could not give any connected detail yesterday; but the suddenness, and, in one light, the unseasonableness with which the affair burst out, needs explanation; for though the event of the 26th ult., as you will conclude, immediately opened to me the happiest prospects, I should not have presumed on such early measures, but from the very particular circumstances, which left me not an hour to lose.", "어제는 어떤 조리 있는 자초지종도 들려드릴 수 없었습니다. 그러나 이 일이 터져 나온 그 갑작스러움, 그리고 어떤 면에서는 그 때아닌 갑작스러움은 설명이 필요합니다."], ["I should myself have shrunk from any thing so hasty, and she would have felt every scruple of mine with multiplied strength and refinement.—But I had no choice.", "부인도 짐작하시겠지만, 지난달 26일의 일이 곧바로 제게 더없이 행복한 앞날을 열어 주기는 했어도, 저는 그토록 이른 조치를 감히 취하지는 않았을 것입니다. 다만 한시도 지체할 수 없게 만든 매우 특별한 사정이 아니었더라면 말이지요."], ["The hasty engagement she had entered into with that woman—Here, my dear madam, I was obliged to leave off abruptly, to recollect and compose myself.—I have been walking over the country, and am now, I hope, rational enough to make the rest of my letter what it ought to be.—It is, in fact, a most mortifying retrospect for me.", "저 자신부터도 그토록 성급한 일이라면 움츠러들었을 것이고, 그녀로 말하자면 제 망설임 하나하나를 몇 곱절이나 더 강하고 더 섬세하게 느꼈을 것입니다.—그러나 제게는 달리 길이 없었습니다. 그녀가 그 여자와 서둘러 맺은 그 약속이—여기서, 친애하는 부인, 저는 마음을 가다듬고 추스르기 위해 갑작스레 펜을 놓아야 했습니다.—들판을 거닐다 왔고, 이제는, 바라건대, 이 편지의 나머지를 마땅한 모습으로 써 내려갈 만큼 제정신이 들었습니다.—사실 이것은 제게 더없이 부끄러운 회고입니다."], ["I behaved shamefully.", "저는 수치스럽게 행동했습니다."], ["And here I can admit, that my manners to Miss W., in being unpleasant to Miss F., were highly blameable.", "그리고 여기서 저는, 미스 W에게 보인 제 처신이 미스 F의 마음을 상하게 한 점에서 크게 책망받아 마땅했음을 인정합니다."], ["She disapproved them, which ought to have been enough.—My plea of concealing the truth she did not think sufficient.—She was displeased; I thought unreasonably so: I thought her, on a thousand occasions, unnecessarily scrupulous and cautious: I thought her even cold.", "그녀는 그것을 못마땅해했고, 그것만으로도 충분했어야 마땅합니다.—진실을 감추기 위해서라는 제 변명을, 그녀는 충분하다고 여기지 않았습니다.—그녀는 언짢아했고, 저는 그것이 부당하다고 여겼습니다. 저는 수천 번이나 그녀가 쓸데없이 까다롭고 조심스럽다고 여겼고, 심지어 냉정하다고까지 여겼습니다."], ["But she was always right.", "그러나 그녀는 언제나 옳았습니다."], ["If I had followed her judgment, and subdued my spirits to the level of what she deemed proper, I should have escaped the greatest unhappiness I have ever known.—We quarrelled.— Do you remember the morning spent at Donwell?—There every little dissatisfaction that had occurred before came to a crisis.", "만약 제가 그녀의 판단을 따라, 그녀가 온당하다고 여긴 수준으로 제 기분을 가라앉혔더라면, 저는 제 평생 겪어 본 가장 큰 불행을 면했을 것입니다.—우리는 다투었습니다.—돈웰에서 보낸 그 아침을 기억하십니까?—바로 그곳에서, 그 전에 일어났던 자잘한 불만들이 죄다 한꺼번에 곪아 터졌습니다."], ["I was late; I met her walking home by herself, and wanted to walk with her, but she would not suffer it.", "저는 늦었고, 혼자 집으로 걸어가는 그녀를 만나 함께 걷고 싶었지만, 그녀는 그것을 허락하지 않았습니다."], ["She absolutely refused to allow me, which I then thought most unreasonable.", "그녀는 단호히 거절했고, 그때 저는 그것을 더없이 부당하다고 여겼습니다."], ["Now, however, I see nothing in it but a very natural and consistent degree of discretion.", "그러나 지금은, 거기서 매우 자연스럽고 한결같은 분별의 태도밖에 보이지 않습니다."], ["While I, to blind the world to our engagement, was behaving one hour with objectionable particularity to another woman, was she to be consenting the next to a proposal which might have made every previous caution useless?—Had we been met walking together between Donwell and Highbury, the truth must have been suspected.—I was mad enough, however, to resent.—I doubted her affection.", "저는 세상의 눈을 우리 약혼에서 돌려놓겠다고, 한 시간 동안 다른 여인에게 나무랄 만큼 유난스럽게 굴어 놓고서, 바로 다음 순간 그녀더러, 앞서 기울인 온갖 조심을 죄다 헛되게 만들지도 모를 제안에 응하라는 것이었으니 말입니다.—만약 우리가 돈웰과 하이베리 사이를 함께 걷다가 누군가에게 들켰더라면, 진실이 의심받을 수밖에 없었겠지요.—그래도 저는 노여워할 만큼 어리석었습니다.—저는 그녀의 애정을 의심했습니다. 이튿날 박스 힐에서는 더욱 의심했습니다."], ["I doubted it more the next day on Box Hill; when, provoked by such conduct on my side, such shameful, insolent neglect of her, and such apparent devotion to Miss W., as it would have been impossible for any woman of sense to endure, she spoke her resentment in a form of words perfectly intelligible to me.—In short, my dear madam, it was a quarrel blameless on her side, abominable on mine; and I returned the same evening to Richmond, though I might have staid with you till the next morning, merely because I would be as angry with her as possible.", "그날 제 쪽에서 보인 그런 처신—그토록 수치스럽고 무례하게 그녀를 외면하고, 그토록 노골적으로 미스 W에게 정성을 쏟는, 분별 있는 여인이라면 도저히 견딜 수 없었을 그런 처신에 화가 난 그녀가, 제게 더없이 알아들을 만한 말의 형태로 자기 노여움을 드러냈으니까요.—요컨대, 친애하는 부인, 그것은 그녀 쪽에서는 나무랄 데 없고 제 쪽에서는 가증스러운 다툼이었습니다. 그리고 저는 그날 저녁 곧바로 리치먼드로 돌아갔습니다."], ["Even then, I was not such a fool as not to mean to be reconciled in time; but I was the injured person, injured by her coldness, and I went away determined that she should make the first advances.—I shall always congratulate myself that you were not of the Box Hill party.", "이튿날 아침까지 부인 댁에 머물 수도 있었는데, 오로지 그녀에게 되도록 많이 화를 내고 싶다는 그 한 가지 이유 때문에 말이지요. 그때조차도 저는, 머지않아 화해할 작정이 없을 만큼 바보는 아니었습니다."], ["Had you witnessed my behaviour there, I can hardly suppose you would ever have thought well of me again.", "다만 저는 상처 입은 사람이었고, 그녀의 냉정함에 상처 입었으니, 먼저 화해를 청해 와야 할 사람은 그녀라고 작정한 채 떠났던 것입니다.—부인이 박스 힐 일행에 끼지 않으셨던 것을 저는 늘 다행으로 여길 것입니다. 만약 부인이 그곳에서 제 처신을 목격하셨더라면, 다시는 저를 좋게 보지 않으셨으리라고 저는 거의 확신합니다."], ["Its effect upon her appears in the immediate resolution it produced: as soon as she found I was really gone from Randalls, she closed with the offer of that officious Mrs.", "그것이 그녀에게 미친 영향은, 그것이 곧바로 불러온 결심에서 드러납니다. 제가 정말로 랜덜스를 떠난 것을 알자마자, 그녀는 그 주제넘은 엘턴 부인의 제안을 받아들였습니다."], ["Elton; the whole system of whose treatment of her, by the bye, has ever filled me with indignation and hatred.", "그건 그렇고, 그 여자가 그녀를 대해 온 그 모든 방식은 늘 제 마음을 분노와 증오로 가득 채웠습니다. 제게 그토록 넉넉히 베풀어진 그 너그러운 인내의 마음을 두고 제가 시비를 걸어서는 안 되겠지요."], ["I must not quarrel with a spirit of forbearance which has been so richly extended towards myself; but, otherwise, I should loudly protest against the share of it which that woman has known.—‘Jane,’ indeed!—You will observe that I have not yet indulged myself in calling her by that name, even to you.", "그러나 그렇지만 않았더라면, 저는 그 여자가 그녀를 향해 보인 그 인내의 몫에 대해 목청껏 항의했을 것입니다.—'제인'이라니, 참!—보시다시피 저는 아직, 부인께조차도 그녀를 그 이름으로 부르는 일을 제 자신에게 허락하지 않았습니다. 그러니 생각해 보십시오, 그 이름이 엘턴 부부 사이에서, 쓸데없는 되풀이의 온갖 천박함과 제멋대로 우월하다 여기는 온갖 무례함을 띠고 함부로 오가는 것을 들어야 했을 때, 제가 무엇을 견뎌야 했을지를."], ["Think, then, what I must have endured in hearing it bandied between the Eltons with all the vulgarity of needless repetition, and all the insolence of imaginary superiority.", "조금만 더 참아 주십시오, 곧 끝납니다.—그녀는 그 제안을 받아들이며, 저와 완전히 결별하기로 결심하고는, 이튿날 제게 편지를 써서 우리가 다시는 만나지 않을 것이라고 통고했습니다.—그녀는 이 약혼이 서로에게 후회와 비참함의 근원임을 느꼈으니, 그것을 깨뜨린다는 것이었습니다.—이 편지는 가엾은 제 외숙모가 돌아가시던 바로 그 아침에 제게 닿았습니다. 저는 한 시간 안에 답장을 썼습니다."], ["Have patience with me, I shall soon have done.—She closed with this offer, resolving to break with me entirely, and wrote the next day to tell me that we never were to meet again.—She felt the engagement to be a source of repentance and misery to each: she dissolved it.—This letter reached me on the very morning of my poor aunt’s death.", "그러나 제 마음의 혼란과, 한꺼번에 제게 쏟아진 숱한 일들 탓에, 제 답장은 그날 부친 다른 숱한 편지들과 함께 보내지는 대신, 제 책상 서랍에 잠겨 버리고 말았습니다. 그리고 저는, 몇 줄에 지나지 않았어도 그녀를 만족시키기에 충분히 썼다고 믿고는, 아무런 불안 없이 지냈습니다.—그녀에게서 다시 곧바로 소식이 오지 않는 것이 다소 실망스럽기는 했지만, 저는 그녀를 위해 핑계를 만들어 주었고, 또 너무 바빴으며—덧붙여도 될까요?—제 앞날에 대한 전망이 너무 밝아서 트집을 잡을 기분이 아니었습니다.—우리는 윈저로 옮겨 갔습니다."], ["I answered it within an hour; but from the confusion of my mind, and the multiplicity of business falling on me at once, my answer, instead of being sent with all the many other letters of that day, was locked up in my writing-desk; and I, trusting that I had written enough, though but a few lines, to satisfy her, remained without any uneasiness.—I was rather disappointed that I did not hear from her again speedily; but I made excuses for her, and was too busy, and—may I add?—too cheerful in my views to be captious.—We removed to Windsor; and two days afterwards I received a parcel from her, my own letters all returned!—and a few lines at the same time by the post, stating her extreme surprize at not having had the smallest reply to her last; and adding, that as silence on such a point could not be misconstrued, and as it must be equally desirable to both to have every subordinate arrangement concluded as soon as possible, she now sent me, by a safe conveyance, all my letters, and requested, that if I could not directly command hers, so as to send them to Highbury within a week, I would forward them after that period to her at—: in short, the full direction to Mr.", "그리고 이틀 뒤 저는 그녀에게서 소포 하나를 받았는데, 제가 보낸 편지들이 죄다 되돌아온 것이었습니다!—그와 함께 우편으로 몇 줄이 왔는데, 자기가 마지막으로 보낸 편지에 손톱만큼의 답장도 받지 못해 몹시 놀랐다는 것이었습니다. 그리고 이런 문제에서의 침묵은 달리 해석될 여지가 없으니, 또 부차적인 모든 정리를 되도록 빨리 매듭짓는 것이 서로에게 똑같이 바람직할 터이니, 자기는 이제 안전한 인편으로 제 편지를 모두 돌려보내며, 만약 제가 자기 편지를 바로 손에 넣어 일주일 안에 하이베리로 보낼 수 없다면, 그 기간이 지난 뒤에는 다음 주소로 부쳐 달라고 했습니다."], ["Smallridge’s, near Bristol, stared me in the face.", "요컨대, 브리스틀 근처 미스터 스몰리지 댁이라는 그 온전한 주소가 제 눈을 빤히 노려보고 있었습니다."], ["I knew the name, the place, I knew all about it, and instantly saw what she had been doing.", "저는 그 이름을, 그 장소를 알고 있었고, 그 일의 자초지종을 죄다 알고 있었기에, 그녀가 무슨 일을 벌여 왔는지 단박에 알아차렸습니다."], ["It was perfectly accordant with that resolution of character which I knew her to possess; and the secrecy she had maintained, as to any such design in her former letter, was equally descriptive of its anxious delicacy.", "그것은 제가 익히 알던 그녀의 단호한 성품과 완벽하게 들어맞는 일이었습니다. 그리고 앞선 편지에서 그런 계획에 대해 일절 비치지 않고 비밀을 지킨 것 또한, 그 노심초사하는 섬세함을 똑같이 잘 보여 주는 일이었습니다."], ["For the world would not she have seemed to threaten me.—Imagine the shock; imagine how, till I had actually detected my own blunder, I raved at the blunders of the post.—What was to be done?—One thing only.—I must speak to my uncle.", "그녀로서는 저를 위협하는 것처럼 보이는 일을 죽어도 하기 싫었을 테니까요.—그 충격을 상상해 보십시오. 제 자신의 실수를 실제로 알아차리기 전까지, 제가 우편의 실수를 두고 얼마나 길길이 날뛰었을지 상상해 보십시오.—어찌해야 했을까요?—길은 단 하나뿐이었습니다.—외삼촌께 말씀드려야 했습니다."], ["Without his sanction I could not hope to be listened to again.—I spoke; circumstances were in my favour; the late event had softened away his pride, and he was, earlier than I could have anticipated, wholly reconciled and complying; and could say at last, poor man!", "그분의 승낙 없이는 다시는 제 말이 받아들여지기를 바랄 수 없었으니까요.—저는 말씀드렸습니다. 형편이 제게 유리했습니다."], ["with a deep sigh, that he wished I might find as much happiness in the marriage state as he had done.—I felt that it would be of a different sort.—Are you disposed to pity me for what I must have suffered in opening the cause to him, for my suspense while all was at stake?—No; do not pity me till I reached Highbury, and saw how ill I had made her.", "얼마 전의 그 일이 그분의 자존심을 누그러뜨려 놓았고, 그래서 그분은 제가 예상했던 것보다 일찍, 완전히 화해하시고 응낙해 주셨습니다. 그리고 마침내, 가엾은 분이, 깊은 한숨과 함께, 제가 결혼 생활에서 당신이 누리셨던 만큼의 행복을 찾기를 바란다고 말씀하실 수 있었습니다.—저는 그것이 다른 종류의 행복이 되리라고 느꼈습니다.—제가 그분께 사정을 털어놓느라 겪어야 했던 고통을, 모든 것이 걸려 있던 동안의 그 마음 졸임을 두고 저를 가엾이 여기시겠습니까?—아니요, 제가 하이베리에 닿아, 제가 그녀를 얼마나 병들게 만들었는지를 볼 때까지는 저를 가엾이 여기지 마십시오."], ["Do not pity me till I saw her wan, sick looks.—I reached Highbury at the time of day when, from my knowledge of their late breakfast hour, I was certain of a good chance of finding her alone.—I was not disappointed; and at last I was not disappointed either in the object of my journey.", "제가 그녀의 핼쑥하고 병든 얼굴을 볼 때까지는 저를 가엾이 여기지 마십시오.—저는 그분들의 늦은 아침 식사 시간을 알고 있었기에, 그녀를 혼자 만날 좋은 기회가 분명히 있으리라 확신하는 그 시각에 하이베리에 닿았습니다.—저는 실망하지 않았습니다. 그리고 마침내 제 여정의 목적에서도 실망하지 않았습니다."], ["A great deal of very reasonable, very just displeasure I had to persuade away.", "저는 매우 온당하고 매우 정당한 노여움을 한참이나 달래어 가라앉혀야 했습니다. 그러나 그것도 다 끝났습니다."], ["But it is done; we are reconciled, dearer, much dearer, than ever, and no moment’s uneasiness can ever occur between us again.", "우리는 화해했고, 그 어느 때보다도 더, 훨씬 더 서로를 아끼게 되었으며, 이제 우리 사이에 한순간의 불안도 다시는 일어날 수 없을 것입니다."], ["Now, my dear madam, I will release you; but I could not conclude before.", "이제, 친애하는 부인, 부인을 놓아드리겠습니다. 그러나 이 말씀을 드리기 전에는 끝맺을 수 없었습니다."], ["A thousand and a thousand thanks for all the kindness you have ever shewn me, and ten thousand for the attentions your heart will dictate towards her.—If you think me in a way to be happier than I deserve, I am quite of your opinion.—Miss W.", "그동안 제게 베풀어 주신 그 모든 친절에 천 번 만 번 감사드리며, 부인의 마음이 그녀를 향해 베풀어 줄 그 보살핌에는 만 번 더 감사드립니다.—제가 받아 마땅한 것보다 더 행복해질 길에 들어섰다고 부인이 여기신다면, 저도 부인의 생각에 전적으로 동감입니다.—미스 W는 저를 행운의 자식이라 부릅니다."], ["calls me the child of good fortune. I hope she is right.—In one respect, my good fortune is undoubted, that of being able to subscribe myself,", "그녀의 말이 옳기를 바랍니다.—한 가지 점에서만큼은 제 행운이 의심할 여지가 없으니, 그것은 곧 제 자신을 이렇게 적을 수 있다는 것입니다,"]], [["Your obliged and affectionate Son,", "부인의 은혜를 입은 다정한 아들,"]], [["F. C.", "F."], ["WESTON CHURCHILL.", "C. 웨스턴 처칠 올림."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter XV", "# 제3권 15장"]], [["This letter must make its way to Emma’s feelings.", "이 편지는 엠마의 마음을 파고들 수밖에 없었다."], ["She was obliged, in spite of her previous determination to the contrary, to do it all the justice that Mrs. Weston foretold.", "그러지 않으리라 미리 다짐했건만, 그녀는 웨스턴 부인이 예언한 대로 그 편지를 온전히 정당하게 평가하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["As soon as she came to her own name, it was irresistible; every line relating to herself was interesting, and almost every line agreeable; and when this charm ceased, the subject could still maintain itself, by the natural return of her former regard for the writer, and the very strong attraction which any picture of love must have for her at that moment.", "자기 이름이 나오는 대목에 이르자 더는 견딜 수가 없었다. 자신에 관한 줄줄이가 흥미로웠고, 거의 모든 줄이 흐뭇했다."], ["She never stopt till she had gone through the whole; and though it was impossible not to feel that he had been wrong, yet he had been less wrong than she had supposed—and he had suffered, and was very sorry—and he was so grateful to Mrs.", "그리고 그 매혹이 사그라든 뒤에도, 편지를 쓴 이를 향한 옛 호의가 절로 되살아나면서, 또 이 순간 사랑을 그린 어떤 그림이든 그녀에게 풍기지 않을 수 없는 강렬한 끌림으로, 화제는 여전히 흥을 잃지 않았다. 그녀는 끝까지 다 읽기 전에는 멈추지 못했다."], ["Weston, and so much in love with Miss Fairfax, and she was so happy herself, that there was no being severe; and could he have entered the room, she must have shaken hands with him as heartily as ever.", "그가 잘못했다는 것을 느끼지 않을 수 없었으나, 그래도 그는 그녀가 짐작했던 것보다 덜 잘못했고 — 괴로워했으며, 몹시 후회하고 있었고 — 웨스턴 부인에게 그토록 고마워하고, 미스 페어팩스를 그토록 사랑했으며, 또 그녀 자신이 그토록 행복했으니, 매정하게 굴 도리가 없었다. 만약 그가 방으로 들어왔다면, 그녀는 여느 때처럼 진심으로 그와 악수를 나누었으리라."]], [["She thought so well of the letter, that when Mr. Knightley came again, she desired him to read it. She was sure of Mrs. Weston’s wishing it to be communicated; especially to one, who, like Mr. Knightley, had seen so much to blame in his conduct.", "그녀는 편지를 어찌나 좋게 여겼던지, 미스터 나이틀리가 다시 찾아오자 그것을 읽어 보라고 권했다. 웨스턴 부인이 이 편지가 전해지기를, 특히 미스터 나이틀리처럼 그의 처신에서 그토록 흠잡을 데를 많이 본 사람에게 전해지기를 바라리라고 확신했다."]], [["“I shall be very glad to look it over,”", "\"기꺼이 훑어보지.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he; “but it seems long.", "\"그런데 길어 보이는군."], ["I will take it home with me at night.”", "밤에 집으로 가져가겠네.\""]], [["But that would not do. Mr.", "그러나 그래서는 안 되었다."], ["Weston was to call in the evening, and she must return it by him.", "저녁에 미스터 웨스턴이 들를 예정이었으므로, 그 편으로 돌려보내야 했다."]], [["“I would rather be talking to you,”", "\"자네와 이야기를 나누는 편이 더 좋네만,\""], ["he replied; “but as it seems a matter of justice, it shall be done.”", "그가 대답했다. \"정당하게 평가할 일이라니, 그렇게 하지.\""]], [["He began—stopping, however, almost directly to say, “Had I been offered the sight of one of this gentleman’s letters to his mother-in-law a few months ago, Emma, it would not have been taken with such indifference.”", "그가 읽기 시작했으나, 거의 곧바로 멈추고 말했다. \"몇 달 전에 이 신사가 새어머니에게 보낸 편지 가운데 하나를 보여 주겠다고 했더라면, 엠마, 이렇게 무덤덤하게 받아 들지는 않았을 텐데.\""]], [["He proceeded a little farther, reading to himself; and then, with a smile, observed, “Humph!", "그는 조금 더 나아가 혼자 읽다가, 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"흠!"], ["a fine complimentary opening: But it is his way.", "제법 그럴듯한 인사로 시작하는군. 하지만 저게 저 사람 방식이지."], ["One man’s style must not be the rule of another’s.", "한 사람의 문체를 다른 사람의 잣대로 삼아서는 안 되겠지."], ["We will not be severe.”", "매정하게 굴지 않겠네.\""]], [["“It will be natural for me,” he added shortly afterwards, “to speak my opinion aloud as I read.", "\"내가 읽으면서 의견을 소리 내어 말하는 게 자연스러울 것 같군.\""], ["By doing it, I shall feel that I am near you.", "그가 곧이어 덧붙였다. \"그렇게 하면 자네 곁에 있는 기분이 들 테니."], ["It will not be so great a loss of time: but if you dislike it—”", "시간도 그리 많이 까먹지 않을 거고. 하지만 자네가 싫다면—\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"천만에요."], ["I should wish it.”", "그렇게 해 주셨으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["Mr. Knightley returned to his reading with greater alacrity.", "미스터 나이틀리는 한결 신이 나서 다시 읽기로 돌아갔다."]], [["“He trifles here,”", "\"유혹 운운하는 대목에서 시치미를 떼는군.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “as to the temptation.", "\"제가 잘못한 걸 알면서도, 이치에 닿는 변명은 하나도 못 내놓고 있어. 못쓰겠어."], ["He knows he is wrong, and has nothing rational to urge.—Bad.—He ought not to have formed the engagement.—‘His father’s disposition:’—he is unjust, however, to his father.", "애초에 약혼을 하지 말았어야지. '아버지의 기질'"], ["Mr.", "운운하지만, 제 아버지에게 부당한 소리야."], ["Weston’s sanguine temper was a blessing on all his upright and honourable exertions; but Mr.", "미스터 웨스턴의 낙천적인 기질은 그의 올곧고 떳떳한 노력 위에 내린 축복이었어. 미스터 웨스턴은 지금의 온갖 안락을 손에 넣기 전에 먼저 그것을 벌어들였단 말이지."], ["Weston earned every present comfort before he endeavoured to gain it.—Very true; he did not come till Miss Fairfax was here.”", "정말 옳은 말이야. 미스 페어팩스가 이곳에 오기 전에는 그도 오지 않았으니.\""]], [["“And I have not forgotten,”", "\"잊지 않고 있어요.\" 엠마가 말했다."], ["said Emma, “how sure you were that he might have come sooner if he would.", "\"당신이 그 사람더러 마음만 먹었으면 더 일찍 올 수 있었으리라고 얼마나 단언하셨는지를요."], ["You pass it over very handsomely—but you were perfectly right.”", "그 대목을 참 너그럽게 넘기시네요. 하지만 당신 말이 완전히 옳았어요.\""]], [["“I was not quite impartial in my judgment, Emma:—but yet, I think—had you not been in the case—I should still have distrusted him.”", "\"내 판단이 아주 공정했던 건 아니야, 엠마. 그래도 말이지 — 자네가 그 일에 얽혀 있지 않았더라도 — 나는 여전히 그를 미덥잖게 여겼을 것 같네.\""]], [["When he came to Miss Woodhouse, he was obliged to read the whole of it aloud—all that related to her, with a smile; a look; a shake of the head; a word or two of assent, or disapprobation; or merely of love, as the subject required; concluding, however, seriously, and, after steady reflection, thus—", "미스 우드하우스에 관한 대목에 이르자, 그는 그것 전부를 소리 내어 읽지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀에 관한 모든 구절을, 미소를 머금고, 눈짓을 보내고, 고개를 가로젓고, 동의나 못마땅함의, 혹은 단지 애정의 말 한두 마디를 내용에 맞춰 곁들이며 읽었다. 그러나 끝에 가서는 진지하게, 차분히 생각을 가다듬은 끝에 이렇게 맺었다."]], [["“Very bad—though it might have been worse.—Playing a most dangerous game. Too much indebted to the event for his acquittal.—No judge of his own manners by you.—Always deceived in fact by his own wishes, and regardless of little besides his own convenience.—Fancying you to have fathomed his secret. Natural enough!—his own mind full of intrigue, that he should suspect it in others.—Mystery; Finesse—how they pervert the understanding! My Emma, does not every thing serve to prove more and more the beauty of truth and sincerity in all our dealings with each other?”", "\"아주 못됐어. 그래도 더 나쁠 수도 있었지만. 더없이 위험한 노름을 한 셈이야. 무사히 빠져나온 걸 두고 너무 많이 결과 덕만 보고 있어. 자네를 기준 삼아 제 처신을 가늠할 줄도 모르고. 사실 늘 제 욕심에 속아, 제 편의 말고는 자잘한 것엔 도통 마음을 쓰지 않았던 거야. 자네가 제 비밀을 꿰뚫어 봤다고 멋대로 짐작하다니. 그럴 만도 하지! 제 마음이 음모로 가득 차 있으니, 남도 그러려니 의심하는 거야. 비밀이니, 술수니 — 그런 것들이 사람의 분별을 얼마나 비뚤어지게 하는지! 나의 엠마, 무엇이든 다, 우리가 서로를 대할 때 진실과 진심이 얼마나 아름다운지를 점점 더 또렷이 증명해 주는 게 아닐까?\""]], [["Emma agreed to it, and with a blush of sensibility on Harriet’s account, which she could not give any sincere explanation of.", "엠마는 그 말에 동의했다. 다만 해리엇을 떠올리며 마음이 찔려 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 그 까닭을 솔직하게 설명할 수는 없었다."]], [["“You had better go on,”", "\"계속 읽으시는 게 좋겠어요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["He did so, but very soon stopt again to say, “the pianoforte!", "그가 그렇게 했으나, 이내 다시 멈추고 말했다. \"피아노포르테라!"], ["Ah!", "아! 이건 아주, 아주 젊은 남자나 할 짓이야."], ["That was the act of a very, very young man, one too young to consider whether the inconvenience of it might not very much exceed the pleasure.", "그 불편함이 기쁨을 한참 넘어서지는 않을지 헤아리기엔 너무 어린 거지. 정말이지 애송이 같은 꿍꿍이야!"], ["A boyish scheme, indeed!—I cannot comprehend a man’s wishing to give a woman any proof of affection which he knows she would rather dispense with; and he did know that she would have prevented the instrument’s coming if she could.”", "여자가 차라리 사양하고 싶어 한다는 걸 뻔히 알면서도, 굳이 애정의 증표를 건네려 드는 남자의 심사를 나는 이해할 수가 없어. 게다가 그녀가 막을 수만 있었다면 그 악기가 오지 못하게 했으리라는 걸 그도 알고 있었으니.\""]], [["After this, he made some progress without any pause.", "그 뒤로는 한동안 멈추지 않고 죽 나아갔다."], ["Frank Churchill’s confession of having behaved shamefully was the first thing to call for more than a word in passing.", "프랭크 처칠이 부끄럽게 처신했노라 고백하는 대목에 이르러서야, 처음으로 지나가는 한마디 이상이 필요해졌다."]], [["“I perfectly agree with you, sir,”—was then his remark.", "\"전적으로 동감일세, 선생.\" 그 대목에서 그가 한 말이었다."], ["“You did behave very shamefully.", "\"자네는 정말 부끄럽게 처신했어."], ["You never wrote a truer line.”", "자네가 쓴 줄 가운데 이보다 더 참된 줄은 없네.\""], ["And having gone through what immediately followed of the basis of their disagreement, and his persisting to act in direct opposition to Jane Fairfax’s sense of right, he made a fuller pause to say, “This is very bad.—He had induced her to place herself, for his sake, in a situation of extreme difficulty and uneasiness, and it should have been his first object to prevent her from suffering unnecessarily.—She must have had much more to contend with, in carrying on the correspondence, than he could.", "그리고 곧이어 나오는, 두 사람의 의견이 갈린 근원과, 그가 제인 페어팩스의 옳고 그름의 감각에 정면으로 어긋나게 행동하기를 고집한 대목을 다 읽고 나서, 그는 더 길게 멈추고 말했다. \"이건 아주 못됐어."], ["He should have respected even unreasonable scruples, had there been such; but hers were all reasonable.", "그는 그녀를 부추겨 자기 때문에 극도로 곤란하고 거북한 처지에 빠지게 해 놓고, 그녀가 쓸데없이 괴로워하지 않도록 막는 일을 무엇보다 먼저 챙겼어야 했어. 그 편지 왕래를 이어 가는 일에서, 그녀는 그보다 훨씬 더 많은 어려움과 싸워야 했을 거야."], ["We must look to her one fault, and remember that she had done a wrong thing in consenting to the engagement, to bear that she should have been in such a state of punishment.”", "설령 이치에 닿지 않는 꺼림칙함이라 해도 그는 마땅히 존중했어야 하건만 — 그녀의 꺼림칙함은 모조리 이치에 닿는 것이었어. 우리는 그녀의 단 하나의 잘못을 헤아려, 그녀가 그 약혼에 동의한 것이 그릇된 일이었음을 떠올리면서, 그녀가 이런 벌받는 처지에 놓였던 것을 견뎌 내야 하네.\""]], [["Emma knew that he was now getting to the Box Hill party, and grew uncomfortable.", "엠마는 그가 이제 박스 힐 모임 대목에 다다르고 있음을 알고 마음이 편치 않아졌다."], ["Her own behaviour had been so very improper!", "자기 처신이 어찌나 온당치 못했던가!"], ["She was deeply ashamed, and a little afraid of his next look.", "그녀는 깊이 부끄러웠고, 다음에 그가 지을 표정이 조금 두려웠다."], ["It was all read, however, steadily, attentively, and without the smallest remark; and, excepting one momentary glance at her, instantly withdrawn, in the fear of giving pain—no remembrance of Box Hill seemed to exist.", "그러나 그는 모두 차분히, 주의 깊게, 사소한 말 한마디 없이 읽어 내려갔다. 그리고 행여 괴로움을 줄까 봐 곧바로 거두어 버린, 그녀를 향한 한순간의 눈길 말고는 — 박스 힐에 관한 기억은 아예 존재하지 않는 듯했다."]], [["“There is no saying much for the delicacy of our good friends, the Eltons,” was his next observation.—“His feelings are natural.—What! actually resolve to break with him entirely!—She felt the engagement to be a source of repentance and misery to each—she dissolved it.—What a view this gives of her sense of his behaviour!—Well, he must be a most extraordinary—”", "\"우리의 어진 벗 엘턴 부부의 섬세함에 대해서는 별로 좋게 말할 게 없군.\" 그가 다음으로 한 말이었다. \"그의 심정은 자연스러워. 뭐라고! 정말로 그와 완전히 갈라서기로 결심했다니! 그녀는 그 약혼이 둘 모두에게 후회와 불행의 근원임을 느끼고 — 그것을 깨뜨렸어. 이게 그의 처신에 대한 그녀의 판단을 얼마나 잘 보여 주는지! 음, 이 남자는 참으로 유별난—\""]], [["“Nay, nay, read on.—You will find how very much he suffers.”", "\"아니, 아니에요, 계속 읽으세요. 그가 얼마나 괴로워하는지 보시게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“I hope he does,”", "\"그러기를 바라네.\""], ["replied Mr. Knightley coolly, and resuming the letter.", "미스터 나이틀리가 냉랭하게 대답하고는 편지로 돌아갔다."], ["“‘Smallridge!’—What does this mean?", "\"'스몰리지!' 이게 무슨 뜻이지?"], ["What is all this?”", "이게 다 무슨 소리야?\""]], [["“She had engaged to go as governess to Mrs.", "\"그 사람은 스몰리지 부인네 아이들의 가정교사로 가기로 약속했었거든요."], ["Smallridge’s children—a dear friend of Mrs.", "엘턴 부인의 절친한 벗이자, 메이플 그로브의 이웃이래요."], ["Elton’s—a neighbour of Maple Grove; and, by the bye, I wonder how Mrs. Elton bears the disappointment?”", "그런데 말이 나온 김에, 엘턴 부인은 이 낭패를 어떻게 견디고 있을지 궁금하네요.\""]], [["“Say nothing, my dear Emma, while you oblige me to read—not even of Mrs.", "\"아무 말도 말게, 나의 엠마. 자네가 나더러 읽으라고 시켜 놓고는."], ["Elton. Only one page more.", "엘턴 부인 이야기조차도."], ["I shall soon have done.", "이제 딱 한 장 남았어. 곧 끝나네."], ["What a letter the man writes!”", "이 사람, 편지 한번 별나게 쓰는군!\""]], [["“I wish you would read it with a kinder spirit towards him.”", "\"그 사람을 좀 더 너그러운 마음으로 읽어 주셨으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["“Well, there is feeling here.—He does seem to have suffered in finding her ill.—Certainly, I can have no doubt of his being fond of her. ‘Dearer, much dearer than ever.’ I hope he may long continue to feel all the value of such a reconciliation.—He is a very liberal thanker, with his thousands and tens of thousands.—‘Happier than I deserve.’ Come, he knows himself there. ‘Miss Woodhouse calls me the child of good fortune.’—Those were Miss Woodhouse’s words, were they?— And a fine ending—and there is the letter. The child of good fortune! That was your name for him, was it?”", "\"음, 여기엔 진심이 담겨 있긴 하군. 그녀가 아픈 걸 보고 정말로 괴로워한 모양이야. 분명 그가 그녀를 아낀다는 데는 의심의 여지가 없어. '그 어느 때보다, 훨씬 더 소중한.' 이런 화해의 가치를 그가 오래도록 모두 느끼며 살기를 바라네. 수천, 수만이라는 말까지 써 가며 어지간히 후하게 감사를 늘어놓는군. '내가 받을 자격 이상으로 행복하다.' 자, 그 점에서는 제 분수를 아는 셈이야. '미스 우드하우스는 나를 행운의 자식이라 부른다.' 미스 우드하우스가 한 말이라고, 그래? 그리고 멋들어진 마무리고 — 자, 이게 편지일세. 행운의 자식이라! 자네가 그에게 붙여 준 별명이었나, 그래?\""]], [["“You do not appear so well satisfied with his letter as I am; but still you must, at least I hope you must, think the better of him for it.", "\"제가 그러는 만큼 당신은 이 편지가 그리 마음에 들지 않으신 모양이지만, 그래도 적어도, 적어도 이 편지를 보고 그를 좀 더 좋게 여기시게 됐기를 바라요."], ["I hope it does him some service with you.”", "이 편지가 당신에게 그를 위해 조금이라도 도움이 됐기를 바라요.\""]], [["“Yes, certainly it does.", "\"그래, 분명 도움이 됐어."], ["He has had great faults, faults of inconsideration and thoughtlessness; and I am very much of his opinion in thinking him likely to be happier than he deserves: but still as he is, beyond a doubt, really attached to Miss Fairfax, and will soon, it may be hoped, have the advantage of being constantly with her, I am very ready to believe his character will improve, and acquire from hers the steadiness and delicacy of principle that it wants.", "그는 큰 잘못을 저질렀어. 무분별하고 경솔한 잘못 말이야."], ["And now, let me talk to you of something else.", "그리고 그가 받을 자격 이상으로 행복하게 살 것 같다는 데는 나도 그의 의견에 크게 동감하네."], ["I have another person’s interest at present so much at heart, that I cannot think any longer about Frank Churchill.", "하지만 그래도, 그가 의심할 바 없이 미스 페어팩스에게 진정으로 마음을 두고 있고, 머지않아 — 그러기를 바라네만 — 늘 그녀와 함께 지내는 이점을 누리게 될 테니, 그의 성품이 나아져, 자기 성품에 모자란 한결같음과 섬세한 원칙을 그녀의 성품에서 얻게 되리라고 나는 기꺼이 믿겠네. 자, 이제 다른 이야기를 좀 하세."], ["Ever since I left you this morning, Emma, my mind has been hard at work on one subject.”", "지금 내 마음엔 다른 사람의 일이 너무도 깊이 걸려 있어서, 더는 프랭크 처칠을 생각할 수가 없네. 오늘 아침 자네 곁을 떠난 뒤로, 엠마, 내 마음은 한 가지 일을 두고 고되게 매달려 왔다네.\""]], [["The subject followed; it was in plain, unaffected, gentlemanlike English, such as Mr.", "이어 그 이야기가 나왔다. 그것은 미스터 나이틀리가 사랑하는 여인에게조차 쓰던, 꾸밈없고 담박하며 신사다운 말투로 전해졌다."], ["Knightley used even to the woman he was in love with, how to be able to ask her to marry him, without attacking the happiness of her father.", "어떻게 하면 그녀의 아버지의 행복을 해치지 않고 청혼할 수 있을까 하는 것이었다. 엠마의 대답은 첫마디부터 준비되어 있었다."], ["Emma’s answer was ready at the first word.", "\"사랑하는 아버지가 살아 계시는 동안에는, 그녀에게 어떤 처지의 변화도 있을 수 없다."], ["“While her dear father lived, any change of condition must be impossible for her.", "그녀는 결코 아버지 곁을 떠날 수 없다.\" 그러나 이 대답 가운데 일부만이 받아들여졌다."], ["She could never quit him.” Part only of this answer, however, was admitted.", "그녀가 아버지 곁을 떠날 수 없다는 점은, 미스터 나이틀리도 그녀만큼이나 절감했다."], ["The impossibility of her quitting her father, Mr.", "그러나 그 밖의 어떤 변화도 있을 수 없다는 점에는 동의할 수 없었다."], ["Knightley felt as strongly as herself; but the inadmissibility of any other change, he could not agree to.", "그는 그 일을 더없이 깊이, 더없이 골똘히 곱씹어 온 터였다. 처음에는 미스터 우드하우스를 설득해 그녀와 함께 돈웰로 옮겨 가게 하기를 바랐다."], ["He had been thinking it over most deeply, most intently; he had at first hoped to induce Mr.", "그것이 될 법한 일이라 믿고 싶었으나, 미스터 우드하우스를 아는 그로서는 오래 자신을 속일 수가 없었다."], ["Woodhouse to remove with her to Donwell; he had wanted to believe it feasible, but his knowledge of Mr.", "그래서 이제 그는 솔직히 털어놓았다. 그런 이주는 그녀 아버지의 안락을, 어쩌면 목숨까지도 위태롭게 할 노릇이니, 결코 무릅써서는 안 된다는 것이 자신의 생각이라고."], ["Woodhouse would not suffer him to deceive himself long; and now he confessed his persuasion, that such a transplantation would be a risk of her father’s comfort, perhaps even of his life, which must not be hazarded.", "미스터 우드하우스를 하트필드에서 떼어 내다니! 아니, 그것은 시도해서도 안 될 일이라고 그는 느꼈다."], ["Mr. Woodhouse taken from Hartfield!—No, he felt that it ought not to be attempted.", "그러나 이것을 단념하고 떠오른 계획이라면, 그의 가장 사랑하는 엠마도 어느 모로 보나 마다하지 않으리라 그는 믿었다."], ["But the plan which had arisen on the sacrifice of this, he trusted his dearest Emma would not find in any respect objectionable; it was, that he should be received at Hartfield; that so long as her father’s happiness—in other words, his life—required Hartfield to continue her home, it should be his likewise.", "그것은 곧, 자신이 하트필드로 들어와 사는 것이었다. 그녀 아버지의 행복이 — 바꿔 말하면 그의 목숨이 — 하트필드를 그녀의 집으로 계속 유지하기를 요구하는 한, 그곳을 자신의 집으로도 삼겠다는 것이었다."]], [["Of their all removing to Donwell, Emma had already had her own passing thoughts.", "그들 모두가 돈웰로 옮겨 가는 일이라면, 엠마도 이미 잠깐 생각해 본 적이 있었다."], ["Like him, she had tried the scheme and rejected it; but such an alternative as this had not occurred to her.", "그처럼 그녀도 그 계획을 따져 보고는 물려 두었던 터였다. 그러나 이런 대안만은 떠올려 본 적이 없었다."], ["She was sensible of all the affection it evinced.", "그녀는 거기 담긴 온갖 애정을 잘 알았다."], ["She felt that, in quitting Donwell, he must be sacrificing a great deal of independence of hours and habits; that in living constantly with her father, and in no house of his own, there would be much, very much, to be borne with.", "돈웰을 떠난다면 그가 시간과 습관의 독립을 크게 희생해야 하리라는 것을, 제 집이 없이 그녀의 아버지와 늘 함께 산다면 견뎌야 할 일이 많고도 많으리라는 것을 그녀는 느꼈다."], ["She promised to think of it, and advised him to think of it more; but he was fully convinced, that no reflection could alter his wishes or his opinion on the subject.", "그녀는 생각해 보겠노라 약속했고, 그에게도 더 생각해 보라고 권했다. 그러나 그는 어떤 숙고로도 이 일에 대한 자신의 바람이나 의견을 바꿀 수 없으리라 굳게 확신하고 있었다."], ["He had given it, he could assure her, very long and calm consideration; he had been walking away from William Larkins the whole morning, to have his thoughts to himself.", "그는 이미 아주 오래, 아주 차분히 헤아려 본 일이라고 그녀에게 장담했다. 혼자 생각에 잠기려고 아침 내내 윌리엄 라킨스에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 걸었노라고."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["there is one difficulty unprovided for,”", "미처 손쓰지 못한 난관이 하나 있어요.\""], ["cried Emma.", "엠마가 외쳤다."], ["“I am sure William Larkins will not like it.", "\"윌리엄 라킨스가 분명 그걸 못마땅해할 거예요."], ["You must get his consent before you ask mine.”", "제 승낙을 청하기 전에 먼저 그의 동의부터 얻으셔야겠는데요.\""]], [["She promised, however, to think of it; and pretty nearly promised, moreover, to think of it, with the intention of finding it a very good scheme.", "그래도 그녀는 생각해 보겠노라 약속했다. 게다가 그것을 아주 좋은 계획이라 여길 작정으로 생각해 보겠노라고, 거의 약속하다시피 했다."]], [["It is remarkable, that Emma, in the many, very many, points of view in which she was now beginning to consider Donwell Abbey, was never struck with any sense of injury to her nephew Henry, whose rights as heir-expectant had formerly been so tenaciously regarded.", "주목할 만한 점은, 엠마가 이제 돈웰 애비를 여러, 아주 여러 관점에서 두루 헤아리기 시작하면서도, 한때 그토록 끈질기게 챙겼던 상속 예정자로서의 권리, 그러니까 조카 헨리에게 손해가 미친다는 생각만은 단 한 번도 떠오르지 않았다는 것이다."], ["Think she must of the possible difference to the poor little boy; and yet she only gave herself a saucy conscious smile about it, and found amusement in detecting the real cause of that violent dislike of Mr.", "그 가엾은 어린것에게 닥칠지 모를 차이를 그녀는 마땅히 떠올려야 했건만, 그저 그 일을 두고 짐짓 시침 떼는 듯 의미심장한 미소를 지었을 뿐이다."], ["Knightley’s marrying Jane Fairfax, or any body else, which at the time she had wholly imputed to the amiable solicitude of the sister and the aunt.", "그러고는 미스터 나이틀리가 제인 페어팩스든 그 누구든 결혼하는 것을 자신이 그토록 격렬하게 싫어했던, 그러나 그 무렵에는 언니로서, 이모로서의 애틋한 염려 탓으로만 돌렸던, 그 진짜 원인을 알아채고는 재미있어했다."]], [["This proposal of his, this plan of marrying and continuing at Hartfield—the more she contemplated it, the more pleasing it became.", "그의 이 청혼, 결혼해서 하트필드에 눌러 살자는 이 계획은 — 곱씹으면 곱씹을수록 더욱 흐뭇해졌다."], ["His evils seemed to lessen, her own advantages to increase, their mutual good to outweigh every drawback.", "그의 불리한 점은 줄어드는 듯했고, 그녀 자신의 이점은 늘어나는 듯했으며, 둘이 함께 누릴 좋음이 온갖 흠을 압도하는 듯했다."], ["Such a companion for herself in the periods of anxiety and cheerlessness before her!—Such a partner in all those duties and cares to which time must be giving increase of melancholy!", "앞으로 닥쳐올 근심스럽고 적막한 나날에 곁에 둘 그런 동무라니! 세월이 흐를수록 우울함만 더해 갈 그 모든 의무와 근심을 함께 나눌 그런 반려라니!"]], [["She would have been too happy but for poor Harriet; but every blessing of her own seemed to involve and advance the sufferings of her friend, who must now be even excluded from Hartfield.", "가엾은 해리엇만 아니었다면 그녀는 더없이 행복했으리라. 그러나 그녀 자신의 온갖 축복은 하나같이 벗의 괴로움을 얽어매고 키우는 듯했으니, 이제 그 벗은 하트필드에서마저 멀어져야 할 판이었다."], ["The delightful family party which Emma was securing for herself, poor Harriet must, in mere charitable caution, be kept at a distance from.", "엠마가 제 몫으로 마련해 두는 그 즐거운 가족 모임에서, 가엾은 해리엇은 그저 자비로운 조심성에서라도 멀찍이 떼어 놓아야 했다."], ["She would be a loser in every way.", "그녀는 어느 모로 보나 잃기만 하는 쪽이었다."], ["Emma could not deplore her future absence as any deduction from her own enjoyment.", "엠마는 앞으로 해리엇이 빠지는 것을 제 즐거움이 줄어드는 일로 한탄할 수는 없었다."], ["In such a party, Harriet would be rather a dead weight than otherwise; but for the poor girl herself, it seemed a peculiarly cruel necessity that was to be placing her in such a state of unmerited punishment.", "그런 모임에서 해리엇은 도움이 되기는커녕 차라리 짐스러운 존재일 터였다. 그러나 그 가엾은 처녀 본인에게는, 받아 마땅치 않은 벌받는 처지로 그녀를 몰아넣는, 유달리 가혹한 운명인 듯했다."]], [["In time, of course, Mr.", "물론 시간이 흐르면 미스터 나이틀리도 잊힐 것이다."], ["Knightley would be forgotten, that is, supplanted; but this could not be expected to happen very early. Mr.", "다시 말해 다른 누군가로 자리바꿈할 것이다."], ["Knightley himself would be doing nothing to assist the cure;—not like Mr.", "그러나 그 일이 그리 빨리 일어나리라고는 기대할 수 없었다."], ["Elton. Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리 본인은 그 치유를 돕는 일이라고는 아무것도 하지 않을 테니 — 미스터 엘턴과는 다른 셈이었다."], ["Knightley, always so kind, so feeling, so truly considerate for every body, would never deserve to be less worshipped than now; and it really was too much to hope even of Harriet, that she could be in love with more than three men in one year.", "미스터 나이틀리는 늘 그토록 다정하고, 그토록 정이 깊으며, 모두에게 그토록 진심으로 사려 깊은 사람이라, 지금보다 덜 흠모받아 마땅할 까닭이 결코 없을 터였다. 게다가 해리엇이 한 해에 세 남자 넘게 사랑에 빠질 수 있으리라 기대하는 것은, 아무리 해리엇이라 해도 정말이지 지나친 바람이었다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter XVI", "# 제3권 16장"]], [["It was a very great relief to Emma to find Harriet as desirous as herself to avoid a meeting.", "해리엇이 자신만큼이나 마주치는 일을 피하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알게 된 것은 엠마에게 더없는 위안이었다."], ["Their intercourse was painful enough by letter.", "편지를 주고받는 것만도 충분히 고통스러웠다."], ["How much worse, had they been obliged to meet!", "하물며 얼굴을 맞대야 했다면 얼마나 더 고약했겠는가!"]], [["Harriet expressed herself very much as might be supposed, without reproaches, or apparent sense of ill-usage; and yet Emma fancied there was a something of resentment, a something bordering on it in her style, which increased the desirableness of their being separate.—It might be only her own consciousness; but it seemed as if an angel only could have been quite without resentment under such a stroke.", "해리엇은 충분히 짐작이 가는 방식으로 자기 마음을 드러냈으니, 책망도 없었고 푸대접받았다는 기색도 겉으로 드러내지 않았다. 그러나 엠마는 그 글투에 어딘가 원망 비슷한 것, 원망에 가까운 무언가가 서려 있다고 느꼈고, 그것은 두 사람이 떨어져 지내야 할 까닭을 더욱 키워 주었다. 어쩌면 그저 그녀 자신의 양심 탓이었는지도 모른다. 그러나 이런 타격을 받고서도 조금도 원망하지 않을 수 있는 것은 천사뿐이리라 싶었다."]], [["She had no difficulty in procuring Isabella’s invitation; and she was fortunate in having a sufficient reason for asking it, without resorting to invention.—There was a tooth amiss.", "이저벨라의 초대를 받아 내는 데에는 아무런 어려움이 없었고, 게다가 꾸며 낼 필요도 없이 충분한 명분이 있다는 점이 다행이었다. 이가 하나 좋지 않았던 것이다."], ["Harriet really wished, and had wished some time, to consult a dentist. Mrs.", "해리엇은 진작부터 치과 의사에게 보이고 싶어 했고 그 마음은 한동안 이어진 것이었다."], ["John Knightley was delighted to be of use; any thing of ill health was a recommendation to her—and though not so fond of a dentist as of a Mr.", "미시즈 존 나이틀리는 도움이 될 수 있다는 데 기뻐했다. 그녀에게는 누군가의 건강이 좋지 않다는 것이야말로 더없이 반가운 명분이었으니 — 치과 의사를 미스터 윙필드만큼 좋아하는 것은 아니었으나, 해리엇을 자기 보살핌 아래 두고 싶어 안달이었다."], ["Wingfield, she was quite eager to have Harriet under her care.—When it was thus settled on her sister’s side, Emma proposed it to her friend, and found her very persuadable.—Harriet was to go; she was invited for at least a fortnight; she was to be conveyed in Mr.", "언니 쪽에서 그렇게 정해지자, 엠마는 벗에게 이 일을 제안했고 해리엇이 아주 쉽게 마음을 돌린다는 것을 알았다. 해리엇은 가기로 했다."], ["Woodhouse’s carriage.—It was all arranged, it was all completed, and Harriet was safe in Brunswick Square.", "적어도 보름 동안 초대받았고, 미스터 우드하우스의 마차로 데려다주기로 했다. 모든 것이 정해지고 모든 것이 마무리되어, 해리엇은 브런즈윅 스퀘어에 무사히 자리를 잡았다."]], [["Now Emma could, indeed, enjoy Mr.", "이제 엠마는 정말로 미스터 나이틀리의 방문을 즐길 수 있었다."], ["Knightley’s visits; now she could talk, and she could listen with true happiness, unchecked by that sense of injustice, of guilt, of something most painful, which had haunted her when remembering how disappointed a heart was near her, how much might at that moment, and at a little distance, be enduring by the feelings which she had led astray herself.", "이제 진정한 행복 속에서 이야기하고 귀 기울일 수 있었으니, 가까이에 실망한 마음 하나가 있다는 것을, 자기 자신이 길을 잘못 들게 한 그 감정 때문에 바로 그 순간, 멀지 않은 곳에서 누군가가 얼마나 많은 것을 견디고 있을지를 떠올릴 때마다 그녀를 따라다니던 그 부당함과 죄책감, 그 무엇보다 고통스러운 무언가에 더는 가로막히지 않았다."]], [["The difference of Harriet at Mrs.", "해리엇이 고더드 부인 댁에 있는 것과 런던에 있는 것이, 어쩌면 엠마의 감정에 분에 넘치는 차이를 만들어 냈는지도 모른다."], ["Goddard’s, or in London, made perhaps an unreasonable difference in Emma’s sensations; but she could not think of her in London without objects of curiosity and employment, which must be averting the past, and carrying her out of herself.", "그러나 그녀는 런던에 있는 해리엇을 떠올릴 때면, 지난 일을 잊게 해 주고 자기 자신에게서 빠져나오게 해 줄 만한 호기심거리며 소일거리에 둘러싸인 모습을 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["She would not allow any other anxiety to succeed directly to the place in her mind which Harriet had occupied.", "해리엇이 차지하고 있던 마음속 그 자리를 다른 어떤 근심이 곧바로 메우도록 내버려 두지 않을 작정이었다. 그녀 앞에는 알려야 할 일이 하나 있었으니, 오직 그녀만이 해낼 수 있는 일 — 아버지께 자신의 약혼을 고백하는 일이었다."], ["There was a communication before her, one which she only could be competent to make—the confession of her engagement to her father; but she would have nothing to do with it at present.—She had resolved to defer the disclosure till Mrs.", "그러나 지금으로서는 그 일에 손대고 싶지 않았다. 그녀는 웨스턴 부인이 무사하고 평안해질 때까지 그 고백을 미루기로 마음먹었다."], ["Weston were safe and well.", "이 시기에 자기가 사랑하는 사람들 가운데 또 하나의 동요를 던져 넣어서는 안 되었다."], ["No additional agitation should be thrown at this period among those she loved—and the evil should not act on herself by anticipation before the appointed time.—A fortnight, at least, of leisure and peace of mind, to crown every warmer, but more agitating, delight, should be hers.", "그리고 정해진 때가 오기도 전에, 미리 앞당겨 자기 자신을 그 괴로움이 좀먹게 두어서도 안 되었다. 더 뜨겁지만 더 마음을 흔드는 온갖 기쁨에 화관을 씌워 줄, 적어도 보름간의 여유와 마음의 평화는 마땅히 그녀의 몫이어야 했다."]], [["She soon resolved, equally as a duty and a pleasure, to employ half an hour of this holiday of spirits in calling on Miss Fairfax.—She ought to go—and she was longing to see her; the resemblance of their present situations increasing every other motive of goodwill. It would be a secret satisfaction; but the consciousness of a similarity of prospect would certainly add to the interest with which she should attend to any thing Jane might communicate.", "그녀는 곧, 의무이자 즐거움으로서 똑같이, 이 마음의 휴일 가운데 반 시간을 미스 페어팩스를 찾아가는 데 쓰기로 마음먹었다. 가야 마땅했고 — 또 그녀가 몹시 보고 싶었다. 두 사람의 지금 처지가 닮았다는 것이 다른 온갖 선의의 동기를 더욱 부추겼다. 그것은 남몰래 누리는 만족이리라. 그러나 앞날이 비슷하다는 의식은, 제인이 무슨 이야기를 들려주든 거기에 기울일 관심을 한층 더 깊게 해 줄 것이 분명했다."]], [["She went—she had driven once unsuccessfully to the door, but had not been into the house since the morning after Box Hill, when poor Jane had been in such distress as had filled her with compassion, though all the worst of her sufferings had been unsuspected.—The fear of being still unwelcome, determined her, though assured of their being at home, to wait in the passage, and send up her name.—She heard Patty announcing it; but no such bustle succeeded as poor Miss Bates had before made so happily intelligible.—No; she heard nothing but the instant reply of, “Beg her to walk up;”—and a moment afterwards she was met on the stairs by Jane herself, coming eagerly forward, as if no other reception of her were felt sufficient.—Emma had never seen her look so well, so lovely, so engaging. There was consciousness, animation, and warmth; there was every thing which her countenance or manner could ever have wanted.— She came forward with an offered hand; and said, in a low, but very feeling tone,", "그녀는 갔다 — 한 번은 마차로 그 집 문 앞까지 갔으나 헛걸음을 했고, 박스 힐 다녀온 다음 날 아침 이후로는 그 집에 들어가 본 적이 없었다. 그날 가엾은 제인은 엠마의 마음을 연민으로 가득 채울 만큼 큰 고통에 빠져 있었으니, 정작 그 고통의 가장 깊은 속내는 짐작조차 못 했지만. 여전히 환영받지 못할까 봐 두려운 마음에, 두 사람이 집에 있다는 것을 확인하고도 통로에서 기다리며 이름을 올려 보내기로 했다. 패티가 그녀의 이름을 알리는 소리가 들렸다. 그러나 전에 가엾은 미스 베이츠가 그토록 흐뭇하게 알아들을 만하게 일으켰던 그런 부산함은 뒤따르지 않았다. 그렇다. 들려온 것이라곤 \"이리 올라오시라고 해라\" 하는 즉각적인 대답뿐이었다 — 그리고 잠시 뒤, 계단에서 제인이 직접 그녀를 맞았으니, 다른 어떤 방식으로 맞이해도 충분치 않다는 듯 다급히 앞으로 나오는 것이었다. 엠마는 그녀가 그토록 좋아 보이고, 그토록 사랑스럽고, 그토록 매력적인 모습을 본 적이 없었다. 거기에는 의식하는 빛과 생기와 따스함이 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴이나 태도에 일찍이 모자랐을 법한 것이 이제는 무엇 하나 모자라지 않았다. 그녀는 손을 내밀며 다가와, 낮지만 더없이 진심 어린 어조로 말했다."]], [["“This is most kind, indeed!—Miss Woodhouse, it is impossible for me to express—I hope you will believe—Excuse me for being so entirely without words.”", "\"정말 너무나 친절하세요! 미스 우드하우스, 저로서는 도저히 표현할 수가 없어요 — 부디 믿어 주시기를 — 이렇게 아무 말도 못 하는 저를 용서해 주세요.\""]], [["Emma was gratified, and would soon have shewn no want of words, if the sound of Mrs.", "엠마는 흐뭇했고, 곧 말이 모자라지 않다는 것을 보여 주었을 터였다."], ["Elton’s voice from the sitting-room had not checked her, and made it expedient to compress all her friendly and all her congratulatory sensations into a very, very earnest shake of the hand.", "거실에서 흘러나오는 엘턴 부인의 목소리가 그녀를 멈춰 세우지만 않았던들. 그 바람에 다정함과 축하의 온갖 감정을, 그저 더없이 진심을 담아 손을 꼭 쥐는 것 하나로 압축하는 편이 상책이 되었다."]], [["Mrs. Bates and Mrs.", "베이츠 부인과 엘턴 부인이 함께 있었다."], ["Elton were together. Miss Bates was out, which accounted for the previous tranquillity.", "미스 베이츠는 외출 중이었으니, 그것이 앞서의 고요함을 설명해 주었다."], ["Emma could have wished Mrs. Elton elsewhere; but she was in a humour to have patience with every body; and as Mrs.", "엠마는 엘턴 부인이 다른 데 있었으면 싶었지만, 누구에게든 너그러울 만한 기분이었다."], ["Elton met her with unusual graciousness, she hoped the rencontre would do them no harm.", "그리고 엘턴 부인이 평소답지 않게 상냥하게 맞아 주었으므로, 이 마주침이 두 사람에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않기를 바랐다."]], [["She soon believed herself to penetrate Mrs. Elton’s thoughts, and understand why she was, like herself, in happy spirits; it was being in Miss Fairfax’s confidence, and fancying herself acquainted with what was still a secret to other people.", "엠마는 곧 자신이 엘턴 부인의 속내를 꿰뚫어 보았다고, 그녀가 자기와 마찬가지로 들떠 있는 까닭을 알아챘다고 믿었다."], ["Emma saw symptoms of it immediately in the expression of her face; and while paying her own compliments to Mrs.", "그것은 미스 페어팩스의 비밀을 함께 나누고 있다는 것, 그리고 다른 사람들에게는 아직 비밀인 그 일을 자기는 알고 있다고 여기는 것이었다."], ["Bates, and appearing to attend to the good old lady’s replies, she saw her with a sort of anxious parade of mystery fold up a letter which she had apparently been reading aloud to Miss Fairfax, and return it into the purple and gold reticule by her side, saying, with significant nods,", "엠마는 그녀의 표정에서 그 징후를 곧바로 읽어 냈다. 그리고 베이츠 부인에게 인사치레를 건네며 그 마음씨 고운 노부인의 대답에 귀 기울이는 척하는 동안, 그녀가 안절부절못하며 비밀을 과시하듯, 미스 페어팩스에게 소리 내어 읽어 주던 듯한 편지 한 통을 접어 옆에 놓인 자줏빛에 금장식이 된 손가방에 도로 넣는 것을, 그러면서 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕이며 이렇게 말하는 것을 보았다."]], [["“We can finish this some other time, you know.", "\"나머지는 다음에 마저 보면 되죠. 알잖아요."], ["You and I shall not want opportunities.", "당신하고 나하고는 기회가 없을 리 없으니까요."], ["And, in fact, you have heard all the essential already.", "게다가 사실 요긴한 건 벌써 다 들으셨고요. 나는 그저 미시즈 에스가 우리 사과를 받아들였고 화나지 않았다는 걸 보여 드리고 싶었을 뿐이에요."], ["I only wanted to prove to you that Mrs.", "글솜씨가 어찌나 사랑스러운지 보세요. 아!"], ["S. admits our apology, and is not offended.", "정말 다정한 분이에요!"], ["You see how delightfully she writes.", "당신이 갔더라면 푹 빠지셨을 텐데요."], ["Oh! she is a sweet creature!", "— 그래도 더는 한마디도 마요."], ["You would have doated on her, had you gone.—But not a word more.", "우리 신중하게 굴어요 — 아주 얌전하게 처신하자고요. — 쉿!"], ["Let us be discreet—quite on our good behaviour.—Hush!—You remember those lines—I forget the poem at this moment:", "— 그 시구 기억하시죠 — 무슨 시였는지 지금은 잊었네요."]], [["“For when a lady’s in the case, “You know all other things give place.”", "이는 한 숙녀가 얽힌 일이니,\n다른 모든 것이 그 앞에 물러섬을 그대도 아시리라."]], [["Now I say, my dear, in our case, for lady, read——mum!", "그러니 내가 말하건대, 자기, 우리 경우엔 '숙녀'라는 자리에 — 쉿!"], ["a word to the wise.—I am in a fine flow of spirits, an’t I?", "현명한 분께는 한마디면 족하죠. — 제 기분이 아주 좋아 보이죠, 안 그래요?"], ["But I want to set your heart at ease as to Mrs.", "하지만 미시즈 에스 일로는 당신 마음을 놓이게 해 드리고 싶어요."], ["S.—My representation, you see, has quite appeased her.”", "보다시피 내가 잘 둘러댄 덕에 그분 마음이 아주 누그러졌으니까요.\""]], [["And again, on Emma’s merely turning her head to look at Mrs. Bates’s knitting, she added, in a half whisper,", "그리고 엠마가 그저 고개를 돌려 베이츠 부인의 뜨개질을 바라보았을 뿐인데도, 그녀는 반쯤 속삭이듯 덧붙였다."]], [["“I mentioned no names, you will observe.—Oh!", "\"보세요, 나는 이름은 하나도 입에 올리지 않았어요. — 오!"], ["no; cautious as a minister of state.", "그럼요. 국무대신만큼이나 신중하게요."], ["I managed it extremely well.”", "내가 정말 솜씨 좋게 처리했죠.\""]], [["Emma could not doubt.", "엠마는 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["It was a palpable display, repeated on every possible occasion.", "그것은 빤히 드러나는 과시였고, 기회만 닿으면 거듭되었다."], ["When they had all talked a little while in harmony of the weather and Mrs. Weston, she found herself abruptly addressed with,", "다 함께 날씨며 웨스턴 부인 이야기로 한동안 화기애애하게 떠든 끝에, 엠마는 느닷없이 이런 말을 듣게 되었다."]], [["“Do not you think, Miss Woodhouse, our saucy little friend here is charmingly recovered?—Do not you think her cure does Perry the highest credit?—(here was a side-glance of great meaning at Jane.) Upon my word, Perry has restored her in a wonderful short time!—Oh! if you had seen her, as I did, when she was at the worst!”—And when Mrs. Bates was saying something to Emma, whispered farther, “We do not say a word of any assistance that Perry might have; not a word of a certain young physician from Windsor.—Oh! no; Perry shall have all the credit.”", "\"미스 우드하우스, 우리 여기 이 깜찍하고 당돌한 친구가 어쩜 이렇게 곱게 회복했는지 그렇게 생각지 않으세요? — 이 사람을 낫게 한 것이 미스터 페리의 더없는 공이라고 생각지 않으세요? — (여기서 제인을 향해 의미심장한 곁눈질을 보냈다.) 정말이지, 페리가 놀랄 만큼 짧은 시일에 이 사람을 회복시켰지 뭐예요! — 아! 가장 안 좋았을 때 내가 본 것처럼 당신도 보셨더라면!\" — 그리고 베이츠 부인이 엠마에게 뭔가 말하고 있을 때, 그녀는 더 낮게 속삭였다. \"페리가 받았을지 모를 어떤 도움에 대해선 우리 한마디도 안 해요. 윈저에서 온 어떤 젊은 의사 양반에 대해선 한마디도요. — 오! 그럼요. 공은 죄다 페리 몫이어야죠.\""]], [["“I have scarce had the pleasure of seeing you, Miss Woodhouse,”", "\"미스 우드하우스, 박스 힐 모임 이후로 통 뵐 기회가 없었네요.\""], ["she shortly afterwards began, “since the party to Box Hill.", "그녀가 잠시 뒤 입을 열었다."], ["Very pleasant party.", "\"아주 즐거운 모임이었죠."], ["But yet I think there was something wanting.", "그래도 뭔가 모자랐던 것 같아요."], ["Things did not seem—that is, there seemed a little cloud upon the spirits of some.—So it appeared to me at least, but I might be mistaken.", "어쩐지 — 그러니까, 몇몇 분들 흥에 자그마한 먹구름이 드리워진 것 같았다고나 할까요. — 적어도 내 눈엔 그래 보였어요."], ["However, I think it answered so far as to tempt one to go again.", "내가 잘못 봤을 수도 있지만요. 어쨌든 그래도 한 번 더 가 보고 싶어질 만큼은 됐던 것 같아요."], ["What say you both to our collecting the same party, and exploring to Box Hill again, while the fine weather lasts?—It must be the same party, you know, quite the same party, not one exception.”", "두 분 다 어때요, 그때 그 일행을 다시 모아서, 날씨 좋을 때 박스 힐을 또 한 번 탐방해 보는 거요? — 똑같은 일행이어야 해요, 알잖아요, 똑같은 일행으로, 한 사람도 빠짐없이요.\""]], [["Soon after this Miss Bates came in, and Emma could not help being diverted by the perplexity of her first answer to herself, resulting, she supposed, from doubt of what might be said, and impatience to say every thing.", "그 직후 미스 베이츠가 들어왔고, 엠마는 그녀가 자기에게 건넨 첫마디의 어수선함에 절로 웃음이 나는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다. 무슨 말을 해도 될지 몰라 망설이면서도, 모든 것을 다 말하고 싶어 안달이 난 데서 비롯된 것이리라 짐작했다."]], [["“Thank you, dear Miss Woodhouse, you are all kindness.—It is impossible to say—Yes, indeed, I quite understand—dearest Jane’s prospects—that is, I do not mean.—But she is charmingly recovered.—How is Mr. Woodhouse?—I am so glad.—Quite out of my power.—Such a happy little circle as you find us here.—Yes, indeed.—Charming young man!—that is—so very friendly; I mean good Mr. Perry!—such attention to Jane!”—And from her great, her more than commonly thankful delight towards Mrs. Elton for being there, Emma guessed that there had been a little show of resentment towards Jane, from the vicarage quarter, which was now graciously overcome.—After a few whispers, indeed, which placed it beyond a guess, Mrs. Elton, speaking louder, said,", "\"고마워요, 미스 우드하우스, 정말 친절도 하시지. — 뭐라 말할 수가 없네요 — 네, 정말, 잘 알지요 — 사랑하는 제인의 앞날이 — 그러니까, 그런 뜻이 아니라. — 하지만 얘는 곱게 회복했답니다. — 미스터 우드하우스는 안녕하시고요? — 정말 기뻐요. — 도무지 제 힘으로는. — 보시다시피 여기 이렇게 다복한 작은 모임이라니까요. — 네, 정말요. — 매력적인 청년 — 그러니까 — 아주아주 다정한 분이지요. 미스터 페리, 그 착한 분 말이에요! — 제인을 어찌나 살뜰히 보살피는지!\" — 그리고 엘턴 부인이 와 있다는 것에 대한 그 대단한, 여느 때보다도 더한 감사의 기쁨으로 미루어, 엠마는 목사관 쪽에서 제인을 향한 원망이 자그맣게 비쳤던 적이 있었고 이제 그것이 너그러이 풀린 것이로구나 짐작했다. 사실 두어 마디 속삭임이 오가면서 그것은 짐작을 넘어선 사실이 되었으니, 엘턴 부인이 목소리를 높여 말했다."]], [["“Yes, here I am, my good friend; and here I have been so long, that anywhere else I should think it necessary to apologise; but, the truth is, that I am waiting for my lord and master.", "\"그래요, 나 여기 있어요, 다정한 벗이여. 그리고 여기 어찌나 오래 있었는지, 다른 데였다면 사과를 해야 마땅하다 싶을 정도예요."], ["He promised to join me here, and pay his respects to you.”", "하지만 실은 내 군주님이자 주인님을 기다리는 중이랍니다. 여기서 나와 만나서 두 분께 인사를 드리겠다고 약속했거든요.\""]], [["“What!", "\"어머!"], ["are we to have the pleasure of a call from Mr.", "우리가 미스터 엘턴의 방문을 받는 기쁨을 누리게 된다고요?"], ["Elton?—That will be a favour indeed!", "— 그거야말로 더없는 영광이죠!"], ["for I know gentlemen do not like morning visits, and Mr. Elton’s time is so engaged.”", "신사분들은 오전 방문을 좋아하지 않는 데다, 미스터 엘턴은 그렇게나 바쁘신 분이니까요.\""]], [["“Upon my word it is, Miss Bates.—He really is engaged from morning to night.—There is no end of people’s coming to him, on some pretence or other.—The magistrates, and overseers, and churchwardens, are always wanting his opinion. They seem not able to do any thing without him.—‘Upon my word, Mr. E.,’ I often say, ‘rather you than I.—I do not know what would become of my crayons and my instrument, if I had half so many applicants.’—Bad enough as it is, for I absolutely neglect them both to an unpardonable degree.—I believe I have not played a bar this fortnight.—However, he is coming, I assure you: yes, indeed, on purpose to wait on you all.” And putting up her hand to screen her words from Emma—“A congratulatory visit, you know.—Oh! yes, quite indispensable.”", "\"정말 그렇답니다, 미스 베이츠. — 그이는 정말이지 아침부터 밤까지 바빠요. — 무슨 핑계든 대고 사람들이 끝없이 찾아오거든요. — 치안판사며 구빈 감독관이며 교구 위원들이 늘 그이 의견을 듣고 싶어 해요. 그이 없이는 아무 일도 못 하는 모양이에요. — '정말이지, 미스터 이,' 하고 내가 곧잘 그러죠, '나라면 못 해요, 차라리 당신이 하세요. — 나한테 그 절반만큼이라도 청하는 사람들이 몰려들면 내 크레용이며 악기는 어찌 될지 모르겠어요.' — 지금도 형편없는 게, 둘 다 용서 못 할 만큼 내팽개치고 있다니까요. — 이 보름 동안 한 마디도 연주를 못 한 것 같아요. — 하지만 그이가 오긴 와요, 정말이에요. 그럼요, 여러분 모두에게 인사하려고 일부러요.\" 그러고는 엠마에게 들리지 않게 손을 들어 입을 가리며 — \"축하 방문이죠, 알다시피. — 오! 그럼요, 절대 빠질 수 없는 일이고말고요.\""]], [["Miss Bates looked about her, so happily—!", "미스 베이츠는 어찌나 흐뭇한 얼굴로 둘러보던지 — !"]], [["“He promised to come to me as soon as he could disengage himself from Knightley; but he and Knightley are shut up together in deep consultation.—Mr.", "\"나이틀리한테서 풀려나는 대로 곧장 나한테 오겠다고 약속했어요. 그런데 그이하고 나이틀리가 둘이 틀어박혀 깊이 의논을 하고 있다지 뭐예요."], ["E. is Knightley’s right hand.”", "— 미스터 이가 나이틀리의 오른팔이거든요.\""]], [["Emma would not have smiled for the world, and only said, “Is Mr.", "엠마는 죽어도 웃지 않으려 애쓰며 그저 이렇게만 말했다."], ["Elton gone on foot to Donwell?—He will have a hot walk.”", "\"미스터 엘턴이 돈웰까지 걸어가셨나요? — 더운 길을 걸으셨겠네요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["no, it is a meeting at the Crown, a regular meeting.", "아니에요, 크라운에서 하는 모임, 정기 모임이에요."], ["Weston and Cole will be there too; but one is apt to speak only of those who lead.—I fancy Mr.", "웨스턴하고 콜도 거기 있을 거예요. 하지만 사람들이란 으레 앞장서는 이들 얘기만 하게 마련이잖아요."], ["E. and Knightley have every thing their own way.”", "— 내 짐작엔 미스터 이하고 나이틀리가 모든 걸 제 뜻대로 주무를걸요.\""]], [["“Have not you mistaken the day?” said Emma.", "\"날을 잘못 아신 게 아닐까요?\""], ["“I am almost certain that the meeting at the Crown is not till to-morrow.—Mr.", "엠마가 말했다. \"크라운에서 하는 모임은 내일이라고 거의 확신하는데요."], ["Knightley was at Hartfield yesterday, and spoke of it as for Saturday.”", "— 미스터 나이틀리가 어제 하트필드에 오셔서 토요일이라고 말씀하셨거든요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["no, the meeting is certainly to-day,”", "아니에요, 모임은 분명 오늘이에요.\""], ["was the abrupt answer, which denoted the impossibility of any blunder on Mrs.", "엘턴 부인 쪽에서는 어떤 착오도 있을 수 없다는 듯한 무뚝뚝한 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["Elton’s side.—“I do believe,”", "— \"내가 보기엔,\""], ["she continued, “this is the most troublesome parish that ever was.", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"이 교구야말로 여태껏 본 중에 제일 성가신 교구 같아요."], ["We never heard of such things at Maple Grove.”", "메이플 그로브에선 이런 일은 들어 본 적도 없는데 말이죠.\""]], [["“Your parish there was small,”", "\"거기 교구는 작았으니까요.\""], ["said Jane.", "제인이 말했다."]], [["“Upon my word, my dear, I do not know, for I never heard the subject talked of.”", "\"정말이지, 자기, 그건 나도 모르겠어요. 그 얘긴 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없거든요.\""]], [["“But it is proved by the smallness of the school, which I have heard you speak of, as under the patronage of your sister and Mrs.", "\"하지만 학교가 작았다는 걸 보면 알 수 있죠. 당신이 직접 말씀하셨잖아요, 당신 언니하고 미시즈 브래그가 후원하는 학교라고요."], ["Bragge; the only school, and not more than five-and-twenty children.”", "학교는 그거 하나뿐이고 아이가 스물다섯도 안 됐다고요.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["you clever creature, that’s very true.", "영리한 사람 같으니, 그거 정말 맞는 말이에요. 어쩜 그렇게 생각이 깊은 머리를 가졌담!"], ["What a thinking brain you have!", "제인, 내가 늘 하는 말이지만, 당신하고 나를 한데 섞어서 흔들어 놓을 수만 있다면 우리가 얼마나 완벽한 성품을 이룰까요."], ["I say, Jane, what a perfect character you and I should make, if we could be shaken together.", "내 발랄함하고 당신 진중함이면 완벽이 나올 텐데. — 그렇다고 어떤 사람들이 이미 당신을 완벽하다고 여기지 않을 거라고 넌지시 비추려는 건 아니지만요."], ["My liveliness and your solidity would produce perfection.—Not that I presume to insinuate, however, that some people may not think you perfection already.—But hush!—not a word, if you please.”", "— 하지만 쉿! — 한마디도 마요, 제발.\""]], [["It seemed an unnecessary caution; Jane was wanting to give her words, not to Mrs.", "쓸데없는 당부 같았다. 제인이 말을 건네고 싶어 한 상대는 엘턴 부인이 아니라 미스 우드하우스였으니, 엠마는 그것을 또렷이 알아보았다."], ["Elton, but to Miss Woodhouse, as the latter plainly saw. The wish of distinguishing her, as far as civility permitted, was very evident, though it could not often proceed beyond a look.", "예의가 허락하는 한 그녀를 각별히 대하고 싶어 하는 마음이 역력했으나, 그것은 좀처럼 눈길 한 번을 넘어서지 못했다."]], [["Mr. Elton made his appearance.", "미스터 엘턴이 모습을 드러냈다."], ["His lady greeted him with some of her sparkling vivacity.", "그의 부인은 반짝이는 발랄함을 한껏 발휘하여 그를 맞았다."]], [["“Very pretty, sir, upon my word; to send me on here, to be an encumbrance to my friends, so long before you vouchsafe to come!—But you knew what a dutiful creature you had to deal with. You knew I should not stir till my lord and master appeared.—Here have I been sitting this hour, giving these young ladies a sample of true conjugal obedience—for who can say, you know, how soon it may be wanted?”", "\"정말 갸륵하시네요, 여보. 정말이지. 나를 여기 먼저 보내 놓고 이렇게 한참이나 황송하게 행차하지 않으시다니, 내 친구들에게 짐이나 되라고요! — 하지만 당신이 얼마나 순종적인 사람을 상대하는지는 잘 아셨겠죠. 내 군주님이자 주인님이 나타나기 전엔 내가 꼼짝도 안 하리라는 걸요. — 한 시간을 이렇게 여기 앉아서 이 아가씨들에게 진정한 부부간 순종의 본보기를 보여 주고 있었답니다 — 그게 얼마나 빨리 필요해질지 누가 알겠어요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["Mr. Elton was so hot and tired, that all this wit seemed thrown away.", "미스터 엘턴은 어찌나 덥고 지쳤던지, 이 모든 재담이 헛되이 흩어지는 듯했다."], ["His civilities to the other ladies must be paid; but his subsequent object was to lament over himself for the heat he was suffering, and the walk he had had for nothing.", "다른 숙녀들에게 인사치레는 차려야 했지만, 그다음으로 그가 매달린 일이라곤 자기가 겪은 더위와 헛걸음한 길을 두고 신세를 한탄하는 것이었다."]], [["“When I got to Donwell,”", "\"돈웰에 도착했더니,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “Knightley could not be found. Very odd!", "\"나이틀리를 도무지 찾을 수가 없지 뭐요."], ["very unaccountable!", "참 이상한 일이지!"], ["after the note I sent him this morning, and the message he returned, that he should certainly be at home till one.”", "도저히 영문을 모르겠소! 오늘 아침에 내가 쪽지를 보냈고, 한 시까지는 분명 집에 있겠다고 답을 보내왔는데도 말이오.\""]], [["“Donwell!”", "\"돈웰이라고요!\""], ["cried his wife.—“My dear Mr.", "그의 아내가 외쳤다. — \"여보, 당신 돈웰에 갔던 게 아니잖아요!"], ["E., you have not been to Donwell!—You mean the Crown; you come from the meeting at the Crown.”", "— 크라운을 말하는 거겠죠. 크라운에서 하는 모임에서 오는 길이면서.\""]], [["“No, no, that’s to-morrow; and I particularly wanted to see Knightley to-day on that very account.—Such a dreadful broiling morning!—I went over the fields too—(speaking in a tone of great ill-usage,) which made it so much the worse. And then not to find him at home! I assure you I am not at all pleased. And no apology left, no message for me. The housekeeper declared she knew nothing of my being expected.—Very extraordinary!—And nobody knew at all which way he was gone. Perhaps to Hartfield, perhaps to the Abbey Mill, perhaps into his woods.—Miss Woodhouse, this is not like our friend Knightley!—Can you explain it?”", "\"아니, 아니오, 그건 내일이오. 그리고 바로 그 일 때문에 오늘 꼭 나이틀리를 봐야 했단 말이오. — 어쩜 그렇게 끔찍하게 푹푹 찌는 아침이람! — 게다가 들판을 가로질러 갔으니 — (몹시 푸대접받았다는 어조로) — 그래서 더 고약했지. 그러고도 집에 없다니! 정말이지 나는 영 마음에 안 드오. 사과 한마디 남기질 않았고, 내게 전언도 없었소. 가정부는 내가 올 줄 몰랐다고 잡아떼더군. — 참 별난 일이지! — 그리고 그가 어느 쪽으로 갔는지 아는 사람도 통 없었소. 어쩌면 하트필드, 어쩌면 애비 밀, 어쩌면 제 숲속으로 갔는지. — 미스 우드하우스, 이건 우리 벗 나이틀리답지 않은 일이오! — 이 일을 설명해 주실 수 있겠소?\""]], [["Emma amused herself by protesting that it was very extraordinary, indeed, and that she had not a syllable to say for him.", "엠마는 정말이지 참으로 별난 일이며 자기로서는 그를 두둔할 말이 한마디도 없다고 우기며 재미있어했다."]], [["“I cannot imagine,” said Mrs.", "\"도무지 상상이 안 가요,\""], ["Elton, (feeling the indignity as a wife ought to do,) “I cannot imagine how he could do such a thing by you, of all people in the world!", "엘턴 부인이 (아내로서 마땅히 느낄 모욕감을 느끼며) 말했다. \"하필이면 세상에 다른 누구도 아닌 당신한테 그이가 어떻게 그런 짓을 할 수 있는지 도무지 상상이 안 간다니까요!"], ["The very last person whom one should expect to be forgotten!—My dear Mr.", "잊힐 사람이라곤 도무지 생각할 수 없는 바로 그 사람한테요! — 여보, 그이가 분명 당신께 전언을 남겼을 거예요, 틀림없어요."], ["E., he must have left a message for you, I am sure he must.—Not even Knightley could be so very eccentric;—and his servants forgot it.", "— 아무리 나이틀리라도 그렇게까지 별나진 않을 테고요. 그이 하인들이 깜빡한 거죠."], ["Depend upon it, that was the case: and very likely to happen with the Donwell servants, who are all, I have often observed, extremely awkward and remiss.—I am sure I would not have such a creature as his Harry stand at our sideboard for any consideration.", "장담컨대 바로 그랬을 거예요. 돈웰 하인들이라면 충분히 그럴 만하죠."], ["And as for Mrs.", "내가 늘 보지만 하나같이 굼뜨고 칠칠치 못하거든요. — 정말이지 그 집 해리 같은 작자는 어떤 일이 있어도 우리 집 사이드보드 앞에 세워 두지 않을 거예요."], ["Hodges, Wright holds her very cheap indeed.—She promised Wright a receipt, and never sent it.”", "그리고 미시즈 호지스로 말하자면, 라이트가 영 시원찮게 여긴답니다. — 라이트한테 조리법을 주겠다고 약속해 놓고 끝내 안 보냈거든요.\""]], [["“I met William Larkins,”", "\"집 가까이 갔을 때 윌리엄 라킨스를 만났는데,\""], ["continued Mr.", "미스터 엘턴이 말을 이었다."], ["Elton, “as I got near the house, and he told me I should not find his master at home, but I did not believe him.—William seemed rather out of humour.", "\"주인이 집에 안 계실 거라고 하더군. 하지만 나는 그 말을 안 믿었지."], ["He did not know what was come to his master lately, he said, but he could hardly ever get the speech of him.", "— 윌리엄도 좀 심사가 뒤틀린 것 같았소. 요즘 주인한테 무슨 일이 생겼는지 도무지 모르겠다면서, 좀처럼 말 한마디 붙여 볼 수가 없다지 뭐요."], ["I have nothing to do with William’s wants, but it really is of very great importance that I should see Knightley to-day; and it becomes a matter, therefore, of very serious inconvenience that I should have had this hot walk to no purpose.”", "윌리엄이 무엇을 바라든 그건 나하고 상관없는 일이지만, 내가 오늘 나이틀리를 봐야 한다는 건 정말이지 대단히 중요한 일이오. 그러니 이렇게 더운 길을 공연히 걸어 헛수고만 했다는 게 여간 심각한 곤란이 아니란 말이오.\""]], [["Emma felt that she could not do better than go home directly.", "엠마는 곧장 집으로 돌아가는 것이 상책이라고 느꼈다."], ["In all probability she was at this very time waited for there; and Mr.", "십중팔구 바로 지금 그곳에서 누군가가 그녀를 기다리고 있을 터였다."], ["Knightley might be preserved from sinking deeper in aggression towards Mr. Elton, if not towards William Larkins.", "그리고 윌리엄 라킨스에게야 그렇다 치더라도, 미스터 나이틀리가 미스터 엘턴에게 결례를 더 거듭하는 일만은 막아 줄 수 있을지 몰랐다."]], [["She was pleased, on taking leave, to find Miss Fairfax determined to attend her out of the room, to go with her even downstairs; it gave her an opportunity which she immediately made use of, to say,", "작별 인사를 하며, 그녀는 미스 페어팩스가 한사코 자기를 방 밖까지, 심지어 아래층까지 배웅해 주려는 것을 알고 기뻤다. 그것은 곧바로 활용한 기회를 그녀에게 주었으니, 그녀는 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“It is as well, perhaps, that I have not had the possibility.", "\"어쩌면 기회가 없었던 게 차라리 잘된 일인지도 몰라요."], ["Had you not been surrounded by other friends, I might have been tempted to introduce a subject, to ask questions, to speak more openly than might have been strictly correct.—I feel that I should certainly have been impertinent.”", "당신이 다른 벗들에 둘러싸여 있지 않았더라면, 나는 어떤 화제를 꺼내고, 이것저것 묻고, 그리 온당치 못한 데까지 터놓고 말하고 싶은 유혹에 빠졌을지도 모르거든요. — 분명히 무례하게 굴었을 거예요.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["cried Jane, with a blush and an hesitation which Emma thought infinitely more becoming to her than all the elegance of all her usual composure—“there would have been no danger.", "제인이 외쳤다. 얼굴을 붉히며 머뭇거리는 그 모습이, 엠마가 보기엔 평소의 그 우아한 침착함 따위보다 한없이 더 잘 어울렸다."], ["The danger would have been of my wearying you.", "\"아무 위험도 없었을 거예요. 위험이 있었다면 제가 당신을 지루하게 해 드릴 위험이었겠죠."], ["You could not have gratified me more than by expressing an interest—.", "당신이 관심을 보여 주시는 것보다 더 저를 기쁘게 해 줄 수는 없었을 거예요 — ."], ["Indeed, Miss Woodhouse, (speaking more collectedly,) with the consciousness which I have of misconduct, very great misconduct, it is particularly consoling to me to know that those of my friends, whose good opinion is most worth preserving, are not disgusted to such a degree as to—I have not time for half that I could wish to say.", "정말이에요, 미스 우드하우스,\" (한결 차분하게 말하며) \"제가 잘못했다는, 아주 크게 잘못했다는 의식을 안고 있는 처지라, 그 호평을 가장 잃고 싶지 않은 벗들이 그래도 저를 그렇게까지 역겨워하지는 않는다는 걸 아는 것이 저로서는 유달리 위안이 돼요 — 하고 싶은 말의 절반을 할 시간조차 없네요."], ["I long to make apologies, excuses, to urge something for myself.", "사과하고, 변명하고, 저 자신을 위해 뭔가 호소하고 싶어요."], ["I feel it so very due.", "그게 너무나 마땅하다고 느껴서요."], ["But, unfortunately—in short, if your compassion does not stand my friend—”", "하지만 안타깝게도 — 한마디로, 당신의 연민이 제 편이 되어 주지 않는다면 —\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["you are too scrupulous, indeed you are,”", "당신은 너무 조심스러워요, 정말로요.\""], ["cried Emma warmly, and taking her hand.", "엠마가 따뜻하게 외치며 그녀의 손을 잡았다."], ["“You owe me no apologies; and every body to whom you might be supposed to owe them, is so perfectly satisfied, so delighted even—”", "\"당신은 나한테 사과할 게 하나도 없어요. 그리고 당신이 사과해야 할 법한 사람이라면 누구나 더없이 만족하고, 심지어 기뻐하고 있는걸요 —\""]], [["“You are very kind, but I know what my manners were to you.—So cold and artificial!—I had always a part to act.—It was a life of deceit!—I know that I must have disgusted you.”", "\"정말 친절하시네요. 하지만 제가 당신께 어떻게 굴었는지는 제가 알아요. — 그렇게 냉랭하고 꾸며 댄 태도라니! — 저는 늘 어떤 역할을 연기하고 있었거든요. — 그건 속임수의 나날이었어요! — 제가 당신을 역겹게 했을 게 분명해요.\""]], [["“Pray say no more.", "\"제발 더는 말하지 마요."], ["I feel that all the apologies should be on my side.", "사과는 죄다 내 쪽에서 해야 마땅하다는 느낌이 드는걸요."], ["Let us forgive each other at once.", "우리 당장 서로 용서하기로 해요."], ["We must do whatever is to be done quickest, and I think our feelings will lose no time there.", "해야 할 일이라면 무엇이든 되도록 빨리 해 버려야죠. 그리고 그 점에선 우리 마음이 조금도 시간을 끌지 않으리라 생각해요."], ["I hope you have pleasant accounts from Windsor?”", "윈저에서 반가운 소식이 오기를 바라요.\""]], [["“Very.”", "\"아주 반가운 소식이요.\""]], [["“And the next news, I suppose, will be, that we are to lose you—just as I begin to know you.”", "\"그리고 다음 소식이라면, 짐작건대, 우리가 당신을 떠나보내게 되리라는 거겠죠 — 이제 막 당신을 알게 됐는데 말이에요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["as to all that, of course nothing can be thought of yet.", "그 모든 일로 말하자면야, 물론 아직은 아무것도 생각할 수 없죠."], ["I am here till claimed by Colonel and Mrs. Campbell.”", "캠벨 대령 내외분이 부르실 때까지는 여기 있을 거예요.\""]], [["“Nothing can be actually settled yet, perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면 아직 확정된 건 아무것도 없을지 모르죠,\""], ["replied Emma, smiling—“but, excuse me, it must be thought of.”", "엠마가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"하지만, 실례지만, 생각은 해 봐야 할 일이잖아요.\""]], [["The smile was returned as Jane answered,", "제인이 그 미소에 미소로 답하며 말했다."]], [["“You are very right; it has been thought of.", "\"맞는 말씀이에요. 생각은 해 봤어요."], ["And I will own to you, (I am sure it will be safe), that so far as our living with Mr. Churchill at Enscombe, it is settled.", "그리고 당신께는 털어놓을게요 (분명 안전하리라 믿으니까요), 엔스컴에서 미스터 처칠과 함께 사는 데까지는 정해졌어요."], ["There must be three months, at least, of deep mourning; but when they are over, I imagine there will be nothing more to wait for.”", "적어도 석 달은 깊은 상복을 입어야 하지만, 그게 지나고 나면 더 기다릴 일은 없을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Thank you, thank you.—This is just what I wanted to be assured of.—Oh! if you knew how much I love every thing that is decided and open!—Good-bye, good-bye.”", "\"고마워요, 고마워요. — 이게 바로 내가 확인하고 싶던 거예요. — 오! 정해지고 터놓는 것을 내가 얼마나 좋아하는지 당신이 안다면! — 잘 가요, 잘 가요.\""]], [["# Volume III — Chapter XVII", "# 제3권 17장"]], [["Mrs. Weston’s friends were all made happy by her safety; and if the satisfaction of her well-doing could be increased to Emma, it was by knowing her to be the mother of a little girl.", "웨스턴 부인의 벗들은 모두 그녀가 무사한 데 안도했다."], ["She had been decided in wishing for a Miss Weston. She would not acknowledge that it was with any view of making a match for her, hereafter, with either of Isabella’s sons; but she was convinced that a daughter would suit both father and mother best.", "그녀가 잘해 냈다는 흡족함에 무언가가 더해질 수 있었다면, 그것은 그녀가 딸아이의 어머니가 되었다는 사실을 안 일이었다."], ["It would be a great comfort to Mr. Weston, as he grew older—and even Mr.", "엠마는 처음부터 미스 웨스턴이기를 바라는 마음이 확고했다."], ["Weston might be growing older ten years hence—to have his fireside enlivened by the sports and the nonsense, the freaks and the fancies of a child never banished from home; and Mrs.", "훗날 그 아이를 이저벨라의 두 아들 가운데 누군가와 맺어 줄 속셈이 있어서라고는 결코 인정하려 들지 않았으나, 딸아이가 아버지에게도 어머니에게도 가장 잘 어울리리라고 확신하고 있었다."], ["Weston—no one could doubt that a daughter would be most to her; and it would be quite a pity that any one who so well knew how to teach, should not have their powers in exercise again.", "미스터 웨스턴에게는 큰 위안이 되리라 — 그도 나이가 들수록, 십 년 뒤면 미스터 웨스턴인들 늙어 가지 않으랴 — 집에서 결코 쫓겨나는 법 없는 아이의 장난과 허튼소리, 변덕과 공상으로 난롯가가 흥겨워진다면 말이다. 그리고 웨스턴 부인으로 말하자면 — 딸아이가 그녀에게 더없이 잘 맞으리라는 것은 누구도 의심하지 못할 일이었고, 가르치는 데 그토록 능한 사람이 그 능력을 다시 펼치지 못한다면 참으로 아까운 노릇일 터였다."]], [["“She has had the advantage, you know, of practising on me,” she continued—“like La Baronne d’Almane on La Comtesse d’Ostalis, in Madame de Genlis’ Adelaide and Theodore, and we shall now see her own little Adelaide educated on a more perfect plan.”", "\"그분께는, 아시다시피, 저를 데리고 연습해 본 이점이 있잖아요.\" 엠마가 말을 이었다. \"마담 드 장리스의 『아델라이드와 테오도르』에서 달만 남작 부인이 오스탈리 백작 부인에게 했던 것처럼요. 그러니 이제 우리는 그분이 자기 자신의 어린 아델라이드를 한층 완벽한 방식으로 교육하는 걸 보게 되겠죠.\" * 마담 드 장리스(Madame de Genlis): 18세기 프랑스 작가. 『아델라이드와 테오도르』(1782)는 자녀 교육을 다룬 서간체 교육소설."]], [["“That is,” replied Mr.", "\"다시 말해,\""], ["Knightley, “she will indulge her even more than she did you, and believe that she does not indulge her at all.", "미스터 나이틀리가 대꾸했다. \"그분은 자네를 어를 때보다 그 아이를 한층 더 어르면서도, 자기는 아이를 조금도 어르지 않는다고 믿게 되겠지."], ["It will be the only difference.”", "차이라면 그것뿐일 거야.\""]], [["“Poor child!”", "\"가엾어라!\""], ["cried Emma; “at that rate, what will become of her?”", "엠마가 외쳤다. \"그런 식이면 그 아이는 어찌 되겠어요?\""]], [["“Nothing very bad.—The fate of thousands.", "\"아주 나쁠 거야 없지. 수천 명이 다 그렇게 자란다네."], ["She will be disagreeable in infancy, and correct herself as she grows older.", "어릴 적엔 못 봐줄 아이였다가, 자라면서 스스로를 바로잡는 거야."], ["I am losing all my bitterness against spoilt children, my dearest Emma.", "나는 응석받이들에 대한 미움이 이제 죄다 사라지고 있다네, 사랑하는 엠마."], ["I, who am owing all my happiness to you, would not it be horrible ingratitude in me to be severe on them?”", "내 모든 행복을 그대에게 빚진 내가, 그 아이들을 모질게 대한다면 그야말로 끔찍한 배은망덕이 아니겠나?\""]], [["Emma laughed, and replied: “But I had the assistance of all your endeavours to counteract the indulgence of other people.", "엠마는 웃으며 대꾸했다. \"하지만 저는 남들의 응석을 막아 주려던 당신의 온갖 노력의 도움을 받았는걸요."], ["I doubt whether my own sense would have corrected me without it.”", "그게 없었다면 제 분별만으로 저를 바로잡을 수 있었을지 의심스러워요.\""]], [["“Do you?—I have no doubt.", "\"그런가? 나는 의심하지 않아."], ["Nature gave you understanding:—Miss Taylor gave you principles.", "자네에게 이해력을 준 건 천성이고, 원칙을 준 건 미스 테일러였지."], ["You must have done well.", "자네는 잘됐을 거야."], ["My interference was quite as likely to do harm as good.", "내 참견은 득이 될 만큼이나 해가 되기도 십상이었어."], ["It was very natural for you to say, what right has he to lecture me?—and I am afraid very natural for you to feel that it was done in a disagreeable manner.", "'저 사람이 무슨 권리로 나를 훈계해?'라고 자네가 말하는 건 지극히 자연스러운 일이었고 — 그게 못마땅한 방식으로 행해졌다고 느끼는 것도, 안타깝지만 지극히 자연스러운 일이었지."], ["I do not believe I did you any good.", "나는 내가 자네에게 무슨 득이 되었다고 믿지 않아."], ["The good was all to myself, by making you an object of the tenderest affection to me.", "득은 죄다 내 차지였지. 자네를 더없이 애틋한 애정의 대상으로 삼았으니까."], ["I could not think about you so much without doating on you, faults and all; and by dint of fancying so many errors, have been in love with you ever since you were thirteen at least.”", "자네 생각을 그토록 자주 하면서 흠까지 다 사랑하지 않을 도리가 없었고, 그 숱한 잘못을 떠올리고 또 떠올린 끝에, 적어도 자네가 열세 살 적부터 줄곧 자네를 사랑해 온 거라네.\""]], [["“I am sure you were of use to me,”", "\"당신이 제게 도움이 되었던 건 분명해요.\""], ["cried Emma.", "엠마가 외쳤다."], ["“I was very often influenced rightly by you—oftener than I would own at the time.", "\"저는 아주 자주 당신에게서 옳은 영향을 받았어요 — 그때 인정하려 한 것보다 훨씬 자주요."], ["I am very sure you did me good.", "당신이 제게 득이 되었다는 건 정말 확실해요."], ["And if poor little Anna Weston is to be spoiled, it will be the greatest humanity in you to do as much for her as you have done for me, except falling in love with her when she is thirteen.”", "그리고 가엾은 어린 애나 웨스턴이 응석받이로 자랄 운명이라면, 당신이 제게 해 주신 만큼 그 아이에게도 해 주시는 게 가장 큰 자비일 거예요. 그 아이가 열세 살 됐을 때 사랑에 빠지시는 것만 빼고요.\""]], [["“How often, when you were a girl, have you said to me, with one of your saucy looks—‘Mr.", "\"자네가 어린 계집애였을 적에, 그 건방진 표정을 지으며 나한테 얼마나 자주 이렇게 말했는지 — '미스터 나이틀리, 저는 이러이러한 걸 할 거예요. 아빠가 해도 된댔어요."], ["Knightley, I am going to do so-and-so; papa says I may, or I have Miss Taylor’s leave’—something which, you knew, I did not approve.", "아니면 미스 테일러가 허락했어요' — 내가 못마땅해할 줄 뻔히 알면서 말이지."], ["In such cases my interference was giving you two bad feelings instead of one.”", "그런 경우에 내 참견은 자네에게 안 좋은 기분을 하나에서 둘로 늘려 줄 뿐이었어.\""]], [["“What an amiable creature I was!—No wonder you should hold my speeches in such affectionate remembrance.”", "\"제가 참 사랑스러운 아이였군요! 당신이 제 말들을 그토록 애틋하게 기억하시는 것도 무리가 아니네요.\""]], [["“‘Mr. Knightley.’—You always called me, ‘Mr.", "\"'미스터 나이틀리.'"], ["Knightley;’ and, from habit, it has not so very formal a sound.—And yet it is formal.", "자네는 늘 나를 '미스터 나이틀리'라고 불렀지. 습관이 되어 그리 딱딱하게 들리지는 않지만 — 그래도 딱딱한 건 딱딱해."], ["I want you to call me something else, but I do not know what.”", "나는 자네가 나를 다른 무언가로 불러 주면 좋겠는데, 그게 뭔지는 모르겠어.\""]], [["“I remember once calling you ‘George,’ in one of my amiable fits, about ten years ago.", "\"한번은 제가 그 사랑스러운 발작 가운데 한 번을 일으켜 당신을 '조지'라고 부른 적이 있어요."], ["I did it because I thought it would offend you; but, as you made no objection, I never did it again.”", "한 십 년 전에요. 당신이 기분 상할 줄 알고 그렇게 불렀던 건데, 아무 반대도 안 하시니까 다시는 안 불렀죠.\""]], [["“And cannot you call me ‘George’ now?”", "\"그럼 지금은 나를 '조지'라고 못 부르겠나?\""]], [["“Impossible!—I never can call you any thing but ‘Mr.", "\"불가능해요! 저는 당신을 절대로 '미스터 나이틀리'"], ["Knightley.’ I will not promise even to equal the elegant terseness of Mrs.", "말고는 못 불러요. 엘턴 부인의 그 우아한 간결함을 흉내 내어 당신을 '미스터 케이'라고 부르겠다는 약속조차 못 하겠어요."], ["Elton, by calling you Mr.", "하지만 약속할게요.\""], ["K.—But I will promise,” she added presently, laughing and blushing—“I will promise to call you once by your Christian name.", "그녀가 곧 웃으며 얼굴을 붉히고 덧붙였다."], ["I do not say when, but perhaps you may guess where;—in the building in which N.", "\"딱 한 번은 당신을 세례명으로 부르겠다고 약속할게요. 언제라고는 말 안 할래요."], ["takes M. for better, for worse.”", "하지만 어디서일지는 아마 짐작하시겠죠 — 아무개가 아무개를 좋을 때나 궂을 때나 함께하겠노라 맞아들이는 그 건물 안에서요.\""]], [["Emma grieved that she could not be more openly just to one important service which his better sense would have rendered her, to the advice which would have saved her from the worst of all her womanly follies—her wilful intimacy with Harriet Smith; but it was too tender a subject.—She could not enter on it.—Harriet was very seldom mentioned between them.", "엠마는 그의 더 나은 분별이 자기에게 베풀어 주었을 한 가지 중요한 도움에 대해 더 솔직하게 정당한 인정을 해 줄 수 없는 것이 못내 안타까웠다 — 여자로서 저지른 온갖 어리석음 가운데 가장 못난 짓, 곧 해리엇 스미스와의 고집스러운 친밀함으로부터 자기를 구해 주었을 그 충고에 대해. 그러나 그것은 너무도 민감한 주제였다."], ["This, on his side, might merely proceed from her not being thought of; but Emma was rather inclined to attribute it to delicacy, and a suspicion, from some appearances, that their friendship were declining.", "그녀는 그 이야기를 꺼낼 수 없었다. 두 사람 사이에 해리엇이 입에 오르는 일은 아주 드물었다."], ["She was aware herself, that, parting under any other circumstances, they certainly should have corresponded more, and that her intelligence would not have rested, as it now almost wholly did, on Isabella’s letters.", "그쪽으로서는, 단지 그녀를 떠올리지 않아서일 수도 있었다. 그러나 엠마는 차라리 그것을 그의 섬세함 탓으로, 그리고 몇몇 정황으로 미루어 두 사람의 우정이 시들어 가고 있다는 의심 탓으로 돌리고 싶었다."], ["He might observe that it was so.", "그녀 자신도 알고 있었다 — 다른 어떤 상황에서 헤어졌더라면 두 사람은 분명 더 자주 편지를 주고받았으리라는 것을, 그리고 지금처럼 거의 전적으로 이저벨라의 편지에만 소식을 의지하지는 않았으리라는 것을."], ["The pain of being obliged to practise concealment towards him, was very little inferior to the pain of having made Harriet unhappy.", "그도 그 점을 눈치챘을지 몰랐다. 그를 향해 숨김을 해야만 하는 고통은, 해리엇을 불행하게 만들었다는 고통에 조금도 못지않았다."]], [["Isabella sent quite as good an account of her visitor as could be expected; on her first arrival she had thought her out of spirits, which appeared perfectly natural, as there was a dentist to be consulted; but, since that business had been over, she did not appear to find Harriet different from what she had known her before.—Isabella, to be sure, was no very quick observer; yet if Harriet had not been equal to playing with the children, it would not have escaped her.", "이저벨라는 그 손님에 대해 기대할 수 있는 만큼 좋은 소식을 전해 왔다. 처음 도착했을 때는 기운이 없어 보인다고 여겼는데, 치과 의사에게 진찰을 받아야 했으니 지극히 당연한 일로 보였고, 그 일이 끝난 뒤로는 해리엇이 예전에 알던 모습과 다르다고는 보지 않는 것 같았다."], ["Emma’s comforts and hopes were most agreeably carried on, by Harriet’s being to stay longer; her fortnight was likely to be a month at least.", "물론 이저벨라가 그리 예리한 관찰자는 아니었으나, 해리엇이 아이들과 함께 놀 수 없을 만큼 기운이 없었다면 그것마저 놓치지는 않았으리라. 엠마의 위안과 희망은 해리엇이 더 오래 머물게 되면서 더없이 흐뭇하게 이어졌다."], ["Mr. and Mrs.", "그녀의 보름은 적어도 한 달은 될 듯했다."], ["John Knightley were to come down in August, and she was invited to remain till they could bring her back.", "미스터 존 나이틀리 부부가 팔월에 내려올 예정이었고, 해리엇은 그들이 데리고 돌아올 수 있을 때까지 머물러 달라는 청을 받았다."]], [["“John does not even mention your friend,”", "\"존은 자네 친구 얘기는 꺼내지도 않더군.\""], ["said Mr. Knightley.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말했다."], ["“Here is his answer, if you like to see it.”", "\"여기 그의 답장이 있으니, 보고 싶으면 보게.\""]], [["It was the answer to the communication of his intended marriage.", "그것은 그가 결혼할 작정이라고 알린 데 대한 답장이었다."], ["Emma accepted it with a very eager hand, with an impatience all alive to know what he would say about it, and not at all checked by hearing that her friend was unmentioned.", "엠마는 그가 그 일에 대해 무어라 했을지 알고 싶은 조바심으로 가득 차, 자기 친구가 언급되지 않았다는 말에도 조금도 움츠러들지 않고, 몹시도 다급한 손길로 그것을 받아 들었다."]], [["“John enters like a brother into my happiness,”", "\"존은 형제답게 내 행복을 함께 기뻐해 주고 있어.\""], ["continued Mr.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말을 이었다."], ["Knightley, “but he is no complimenter; and though I well know him to have, likewise, a most brotherly affection for you, he is so far from making flourishes, that any other young woman might think him rather cool in her praise.", "\"하지만 그는 빈말을 하는 사람이 아니야. 그가 자네에게도 더없이 형제 같은 애정을 품고 있다는 걸 내가 잘 알지만, 그는 미사여구를 늘어놓는 일과는 워낙 거리가 멀어서, 다른 젊은 여자라면 자기 칭찬에 그가 꽤 냉랭하다고 여길지도 모르지."], ["But I am not afraid of your seeing what he writes.”", "그래도 나는 자네가 그가 쓴 걸 보는 게 두렵지 않아.\""]], [["“He writes like a sensible man,”", "\"분별 있는 사람답게 썼네요.\""], ["replied Emma, when she had read the letter.", "편지를 다 읽고 엠마가 대꾸했다."], ["“I honour his sincerity.", "\"그분의 진실함이 존경스러워요."], ["It is very plain that he considers the good fortune of the engagement as all on my side, but that he is not without hope of my growing, in time, as worthy of your affection, as you think me already.", "이 약혼의 행운이 죄다 제 쪽에 있다고 여기시는 게 아주 분명하지만, 그래도 제가 머지않아 당신이 이미 그렇다고 여기시는 만큼 당신의 애정에 걸맞은 사람으로 자라 가리라는 희망을 버리지는 않으시는군요."], ["Had he said any thing to bear a different construction, I should not have believed him.”", "다르게 해석될 만한 말을 하셨다면, 저는 믿지 않았을 거예요.\""]], [["“My Emma, he means no such thing.", "\"엠마, 그는 그런 뜻이 아니야."], ["He only means—”", "그저 이런 뜻이지 —\""]], [["“He and I should differ very little in our estimation of the two,”", "\"저와 그분이 우리 둘을 견주어 매기는 평가에는 별 차이가 없을 거예요.\" 그녀가 자못 진지한 미소를 띠고 말을 가로챘다."], ["interrupted she, with a sort of serious smile—“much less, perhaps, than he is aware of, if we could enter without ceremony or reserve on the subject.”", "\"어쩌면 그분이 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 차이가 적을걸요. 우리가 격식이나 거리낌 없이 그 이야기를 나눌 수만 있다면요.\""]], [["“Emma, my dear Emma—”", "\"엠마, 사랑하는 엠마 —\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"아!\""], ["she cried with more thorough gaiety, “if you fancy your brother does not do me justice, only wait till my dear father is in the secret, and hear his opinion.", "그녀가 한결 더 환한 명랑함으로 외쳤다. \"당신 형님이 저를 공정하게 봐 주지 않는다 싶거든, 우리 아버지가 이 비밀을 아실 때까지 기다렸다가 아버지 의견을 들어 보세요."], ["Depend upon it, he will be much farther from doing you justice.", "분명히 말하는데, 아버지는 당신을 공정하게 봐 주는 것과는 훨씬 더 거리가 멀 거예요."], ["He will think all the happiness, all the advantage, on your side of the question; all the merit on mine.", "모든 행복, 모든 이득이 당신 쪽에 있고, 모든 미덕은 제 쪽에 있다고 여기실걸요. 아버지 입에서 곧장 '가엾은 엠마'"], ["I wish I may not sink into ‘poor Emma’ with him at once.—His tender compassion towards oppressed worth can go no farther.”", "신세로 떨어지지나 않으면 다행이고요. 짓눌린 가치를 향한 아버지의 애틋한 측은지심은 그보다 더 나아갈 데가 없거든요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["he cried, “I wish your father might be half as easily convinced as John will be, of our having every right that equal worth can give, to be happy together.", "그가 외쳤다. \"자네 아버지께서도, 우리가 대등한 가치가 줄 수 있는 온갖 권리를 다 갖추어 함께 행복할 자격이 있다는 걸, 존만큼이나 쉽게 납득해 주시면 좋으련만."], ["I am amused by one part of John’s letter—did you notice it?—where he says, that my information did not take him wholly by surprize, that he was rather in expectation of hearing something of the kind.”", "존 편지의 한 대목이 재미있던데 — 눈치챘나? — 내 소식이 자기를 아주 놀라게 하지는 않았다고, 오히려 그런 종류의 무언가를 듣게 되리라 짐작하고 있었다고 한 대목 말이야.\""]], [["“If I understand your brother, he only means so far as your having some thoughts of marrying.", "\"제가 형님을 제대로 이해했다면, 그분 말씀은 그저 당신이 결혼할 생각을 어느 정도 품고 있었다는 데까지일 거예요."], ["He had no idea of me.", "제 생각은 전혀 못 하셨을걸요."], ["He seems perfectly unprepared for that.”", "그 점에 대해선 전혀 마음의 준비가 안 되신 것 같던데요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes—but I am amused that he should have seen so far into my feelings.", "\"맞아, 맞아. 하지만 그가 내 마음을 그토록 깊이 들여다봤다는 게 재미있어."], ["What has he been judging by?—I am not conscious of any difference in my spirits or conversation that could prepare him at this time for my marrying any more than at another.—But it was so, I suppose.", "대체 뭘 보고 판단한 걸까? 이번이라고 다른 때보다 내가 결혼하리라 짐작할 만한 기운이나 말투의 차이가 있었다고는 의식하지 못하겠는데."], ["I dare say there was a difference when I was staying with them the other day.", "하지만 그랬던 모양이지. 요전에 그들 집에 머무는 동안 무언가 다른 점이 있었을 거야."], ["I believe I did not play with the children quite so much as usual.", "아무래도 평소만큼 아이들하고 놀아 주지 못한 것 같아."], ["I remember one evening the poor boys saying, ‘Uncle seems always tired now.’”", "어느 날 저녁엔 가엾은 사내아이들이 이러더군. '삼촌은 요새 늘 피곤한가 봐요.'\""]], [["The time was coming when the news must spread farther, and other persons’ reception of it tried. As soon as Mrs.", "이 소식이 더 멀리 퍼지고, 다른 사람들이 그것을 어떻게 받아들이는지 시험해 볼 때가 다가오고 있었다."], ["Weston was sufficiently recovered to admit Mr. Woodhouse’s visits, Emma having it in view that her gentle reasonings should be employed in the cause, resolved first to announce it at home, and then at Randalls.—But how to break it to her father at last!—She had bound herself to do it, in such an hour of Mr.", "웨스턴 부인이 미스터 우드하우스의 방문을 받아들일 만큼 충분히 회복되자, 엠마는 그녀의 온화한 설득이 이 일에 한몫해 주기를 바라는 마음에서, 먼저 집에서 알린 다음 랜덜스에서 알리기로 마음먹었다."], ["Knightley’s absence, or when it came to the point her heart would have failed her, and she must have put it off; but Mr.", "하지만 끝내 아버지에게 이 일을 어떻게 털어놓는단 말인가! 그녀는 미스터 나이틀리가 자리를 비운 어느 시각에 반드시 해내기로 자신을 묶어 두었다."], ["Knightley was to come at such a time, and follow up the beginning she was to make.—She was forced to speak, and to speak cheerfully too.", "그러지 않으면 막상 닥쳐서는 가슴이 무너져 미루고 말 터였으니까. 미스터 나이틀리는 정해진 시각에 와서 그녀가 떼어 놓은 첫머리를 이어 가기로 했다."], ["She must not make it a more decided subject of misery to him, by a melancholy tone herself.", "그녀는 말을 꺼내야만 했고, 그것도 명랑하게 꺼내야만 했다."], ["She must not appear to think it a misfortune.—With all the spirits she could command, she prepared him first for something strange, and then, in a few words, said, that if his consent and approbation could be obtained—which, she trusted, would be attended with no difficulty, since it was a plan to promote the happiness of all—she and Mr.", "자기가 침울한 어조를 보임으로써 이 일을 아버지에게 한층 더 확정된 비탄거리로 만들어서는 안 되었다. 그것을 불행으로 여기는 기색을 보여서는 안 되었다."], ["Knightley meant to marry; by which means Hartfield would receive the constant addition of that person’s company whom she knew he loved, next to his daughters and Mrs. Weston, best in the world.", "그녀는 끌어모을 수 있는 온 기운을 다해, 먼저 아버지가 무언가 이상한 일에 대비하도록 한 다음, 몇 마디로 말했다 — 만약 아버지의 동의와 승낙을 얻을 수 있다면, 그것은 모두의 행복을 도모하려는 계획이니 별 어려움 없이 따라오리라 믿는다고, 그리하여 자기와 미스터 나이틀리가 결혼할 작정이며, 그렇게 되면 하트필드는 아버지가 두 딸과 웨스턴 부인 다음으로 세상에서 가장 사랑하는 줄 자기가 아는 바로 그 사람과 늘 함께하게 되리라고."]], [["Poor man!—it was at first a considerable shock to him, and he tried earnestly to dissuade her from it.", "가엾은 양반! 처음에 그것은 그에게 적잖은 충격이었고, 그는 진지하게 딸을 만류하려 애썼다."], ["She was reminded, more than once, of having always said she would never marry, and assured that it would be a great deal better for her to remain single; and told of poor Isabella, and poor Miss Taylor.—But it would not do.", "그녀는 한 번 아니라 여러 번, 자기는 결코 결혼하지 않겠노라 늘 말해 왔다는 사실을 일깨움 받았고, 독신으로 지내는 편이 훨씬 나으리라는 다짐을 들었으며, 가엾은 이저벨라와 가엾은 미스 테일러 이야기까지 들었다. 그러나 소용없었다."], ["Emma hung about him affectionately, and smiled, and said it must be so; and that he must not class her with Isabella and Mrs.", "엠마는 아버지에게 다정하게 매달려 웃으며 말했다 — 이렇게 될 수밖에 없다고, 그리고 자기를 이저벨라나 웨스턴 부인과 한데 묶지 말아 달라고."], ["Weston, whose marriages taking them from Hartfield, had, indeed, made a melancholy change: but she was not going from Hartfield; she should be always there; she was introducing no change in their numbers or their comforts but for the better; and she was very sure that he would be a great deal the happier for having Mr.", "그들의 결혼은 하트필드에서 그들을 데려갔으니 정말이지 침울한 변화였지만, 자기는 하트필드를 떠나지 않을 것이며, 늘 여기 있을 것이며, 식구 수에도 안락함에도 더 좋아지는 쪽으로 말고는 아무 변화도 들이지 않을 것이라고. 그리고 일단 이 생각에 익숙해지시기만 하면, 미스터 나이틀리가 늘 곁에 있어서 아버지가 훨씬 더 행복해지시리라 확신한다고."], ["Knightley always at hand, when he were once got used to the idea.—Did he not love Mr.", "아버지는 미스터 나이틀리를 무척 사랑하지 않으시던가? 그러신다는 걸 부인하지 못하시리라 그녀는 확신했다."], ["Knightley very much?—He would not deny that he did, she was sure.—Whom did he ever want to consult on business but Mr.", "일이 생기면 미스터 나이틀리 말고 누구와 의논하고 싶어 하셨던가? 누가 그토록 도움이 되고, 누가 그토록 기꺼이 편지를 대신 써 주고, 누가 그토록 흔쾌히 거들어 주었던가?"], ["Knightley?—Who was so useful to him, who so ready to write his letters, who so glad to assist him?—Who so cheerful, so attentive, so attached to him?—Would not he like to have him always on the spot?—Yes.", "누가 그토록 쾌활하고, 그토록 자상하고, 그토록 아버지를 따랐던가? 그를 늘 곁에 두고 싶지 않으시던가?"], ["That was all very true.", "그래, 그건 다 옳은 말이었다. 미스터 나이틀리는 아무리 자주 와도 좋았다."], ["Mr.", "날마다 보아도 좋았다."], ["Knightley could not be there too often; he should be glad to see him every day;—but they did see him every day as it was.—Why could not they go on as they had done?", "하지만 지금도 날마다 보지 않느냐. 그러니 지금까지 해 온 대로 그냥 지내면 안 되겠느냐."]], [["Mr. Woodhouse could not be soon reconciled; but the worst was overcome, the idea was given; time and continual repetition must do the rest.—To Emma’s entreaties and assurances succeeded Mr.", "미스터 우드하우스는 쉬이 마음을 풀지 못했다."], ["Knightley’s, whose fond praise of her gave the subject even a kind of welcome; and he was soon used to be talked to by each, on every fair occasion.—They had all the assistance which Isabella could give, by letters of the strongest approbation; and Mrs.", "그러나 최악의 고비는 넘긴 셈이었고, 생각의 씨는 뿌려졌으니, 나머지는 시간과 끊임없는 되풀이가 해 줄 일이었다. 엠마의 간청과 다짐에 이어 미스터 나이틀리의 그것이 뒤따랐고, 엠마를 향한 그의 애정 어린 칭찬은 이 주제에 일종의 반가움마저 입혀 주었다."], ["Weston was ready, on the first meeting, to consider the subject in the most serviceable light—first, as a settled, and, secondly, as a good one—well aware of the nearly equal importance of the two recommendations to Mr.", "그리하여 아버지는 적당한 기회가 있을 때마다 두 사람에게서 번갈아 이 이야기를 듣는 일에 곧 익숙해졌다. 두 사람은 이저벨라가 더없이 강력한 찬성의 편지로 보태 줄 수 있는 온갖 지원을 받았고, 웨스턴 부인은 첫 만남에서부터 이 일을 가장 쓸모 있는 빛 아래서 바라볼 채비가 되어 있었다 — 첫째로 이미 정해진 일로서, 둘째로 좋은 일로서."], ["Woodhouse’s mind.—It was agreed upon, as what was to be; and every body by whom he was used to be guided assuring him that it would be for his happiness; and having some feelings himself which almost admitted it, he began to think that some time or other—in another year or two, perhaps—it might not be so very bad if the marriage did take place.", "미스터 우드하우스의 마음에는 그 두 가지 권유가 거의 같은 무게라는 것을 그녀는 잘 알고 있었다. 그렇게 될 일로 합의가 모아졌고, 늘 그가 따르던 사람들이 하나같이 그것이 그의 행복을 위한 일이라 장담해 주었으며, 그 자신도 거의 그것을 받아들이는 듯한 마음이 얼마간 든 끝에, 그는 차차 — 어쩌면 한두 해 뒤쯤이라면 — 그 결혼이 정말로 이루어진다 해도 그리 나쁘지만은 않을지 모른다고 생각하기 시작했다."]], [["Mrs. Weston was acting no part, feigning no feelings in all that she said to him in favour of the event.—She had been extremely surprized, never more so, than when Emma first opened the affair to her; but she saw in it only increase of happiness to all, and had no scruple in urging him to the utmost.—She had such a regard for Mr.", "웨스턴 부인은 이 일을 두고 그에게 한 모든 말에서 어떤 역을 꾸민 것도, 감정을 가장한 것도 아니었다."], ["Knightley, as to think he deserved even her dearest Emma; and it was in every respect so proper, suitable, and unexceptionable a connexion, and in one respect, one point of the highest importance, so peculiarly eligible, so singularly fortunate, that now it seemed as if Emma could not safely have attached herself to any other creature, and that she had herself been the stupidest of beings in not having thought of it, and wished it long ago.—How very few of those men in a rank of life to address Emma would have renounced their own home for Hartfield!", "엠마가 처음 이 일을 그녀에게 털어놓았을 때 그녀는 더없이 놀랐다 — 그토록 놀란 적이 다시없을 만큼. 그러나 그녀는 그 안에서 오직 모두의 행복이 더해지는 것만을 보았고, 그를 한껏 다그치는 데 아무 거리낌도 없었다."], ["And who but Mr. Knightley could know and bear with Mr.", "그녀는 미스터 나이틀리를 어찌나 높이 사는지 그가 자기의 더없이 소중한 엠마조차 받을 자격이 있다고 여길 정도였다."], ["Woodhouse, so as to make such an arrangement desirable!—The difficulty of disposing of poor Mr. Woodhouse had been always felt in her husband’s plans and her own, for a marriage between Frank and Emma.", "게다가 그것은 모든 면에서 그토록 온당하고, 어울리고, 흠잡을 데 없는 인연이었으며, 한 가지 면에서, 더없이 중요한 한 가지 점에서 유독 적합하고 유달리 다행스러운 일이라, 이제는 엠마가 다른 어떤 사람에게 마음을 주었더라면 도저히 안전하지 못했으리라는 듯, 그리고 진작 그것을 생각하고 바라지 못한 자기야말로 둘도 없이 어리석은 사람이었던 듯 여겨질 지경이었다."], ["How to settle the claims of Enscombe and Hartfield had been a continual impediment—less acknowledged by Mr.", "엠마에게 청혼할 만한 계급의 남자들 가운데, 하트필드를 위해 제 집을 버릴 사람이 과연 몇이나 되겠는가! 게다가 미스터 나이틀리 말고 누가 미스터 우드하우스를 이해하고 견뎌 내어 그런 살림을 바람직하게 만들 수 있겠는가!"], ["Weston than by herself—but even he had never been able to finish the subject better than by saying—“Those matters will take care of themselves; the young people will find a way.”", "가엾은 미스터 우드하우스를 어떻게 처리하느냐 하는 어려움은, 프랭크와 엠마를 맺어 주려던 남편의 계획에서도 그녀 자신의 계획에서도 늘 느껴지던 바였다. 엔스컴과 하트필드의 권리를 어떻게 조정하느냐가 한결같은 걸림돌이었다 — 그녀보다는 미스터 웨스턴이 덜 인정한 바였지만."], ["But here there was nothing to be shifted off in a wild speculation on the future.", "그러나 그조차도 이 문제를 매번 이렇게 마무리하는 것 이상은 해내지 못했다 — \"그런 일이야 절로 해결되겠지. 젊은이들이 길을 찾아낼 거야.\""], ["It was all right, all open, all equal.", "그러나 지금 이 일에는 미래에 대한 막연한 억측으로 떠넘길 거리가 하나도 없었다."], ["No sacrifice on any side worth the name.", "모든 것이 옳고, 모든 것이 환히 트였으며, 모든 것이 대등했다."], ["It was a union of the highest promise of felicity in itself, and without one real, rational difficulty to oppose or delay it.", "어느 쪽에도 희생이라 할 만한 희생이 없었다. 그것은 그 자체로 더없이 행복이 약속된 결합이었고, 그것을 가로막거나 미룰 실질적이고 합당한 어려움이 단 하나도 없었다."]], [["Mrs. Weston, with her baby on her knee, indulging in such reflections as these, was one of the happiest women in the world.", "웨스턴 부인은 무릎에 갓난아기를 앉히고서 이런 생각에 잠겨, 세상에서 가장 행복한 여자들 가운데 하나였다."], ["If any thing could increase her delight, it was perceiving that the baby would soon have outgrown its first set of caps.", "그 기쁨에 무언가 더 보탤 것이 있었다면, 그것은 아기가 첫 모자 한 벌을 머지않아 머리가 자라 못 쓰게 되리라는 것을 알아챈 일이었다."]], [["The news was universally a surprize wherever it spread; and Mr. Weston had his five minutes share of it; but five minutes were enough to familiarise the idea to his quickness of mind.—He saw the advantages of the match, and rejoiced in them with all the constancy of his wife; but the wonder of it was very soon nothing; and by the end of an hour he was not far from believing that he had always foreseen it.", "이 소식은 퍼지는 곳마다 한결같이 놀라움이었다. 미스터 웨스턴도 오 분 동안은 제 몫의 놀라움을 누렸다. 그러나 오 분이면 그 빠른 머리에 이 생각을 익히기에 충분했다. 그는 이 혼사의 이점들을 알아보았고, 아내 못지않은 한결같음으로 그것을 기뻐했다. 그러나 그 경이로움은 이내 아무것도 아닌 것이 되었으며, 한 시간이 채 못 되어 그는 자기가 늘 이 일을 예견해 왔노라 믿기에 그리 멀지 않았다."]], [["“It is to be a secret, I conclude,”", "\"이건 비밀이렷다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he. “These matters are always a secret, till it is found out that every body knows them.", "\"이런 일은 으레 비밀인 법이지, 모두가 다 알고 있다는 게 드러나기 전까지는 말이야."], ["Only let me be told when I may speak out.—I wonder whether Jane has any suspicion.”", "언제부터 말해도 되는지나 일러 주구려. 제인이 짐작이라도 하고 있을지 궁금하군.\""]], [["He went to Highbury the next morning, and satisfied himself on that point.", "그는 이튿날 아침 하이베리로 가서 그 점을 확인했다. 그는 그녀에게 그 소식을 알렸다."], ["He told her the news.", "그녀가 딸이나 다름없지 않은가, 자기의 맏딸이 아닌가?"], ["Was not she like a daughter, his eldest daughter?—he must tell her; and Miss Bates being present, it passed, of course, to Mrs.", "그녀에게 말해야만 했다. 그리고 미스 베이츠가 자리에 있었으니, 그것은 당연히 곧이어 콜 부인과 페리 부인과 엘턴 부인에게로 흘러갔다."], ["Cole, Mrs. Perry, and Mrs.", "그것은 당사자들이 각오한 그대로였다."], ["Elton, immediately afterwards. It was no more than the principals were prepared for; they had calculated from the time of its being known at Randalls, how soon it would be over Highbury; and were thinking of themselves, as the evening wonder in many a family circle, with great sagacity.", "그들은 그 소식이 랜덜스에 알려진 시각으로부터, 얼마나 빨리 하이베리 전역으로 번질지 헤아려 두었고, 자신들이 여러 집안의 저녁 자리에서 화젯거리가 되리라는 것을 자못 영리하게 떠올리고 있었다."]], [["In general, it was a very well approved match.", "대체로 이 혼사는 무척 좋은 평을 받았다. 어떤 이들은 그가, 또 어떤 이들은 그녀가 가장 운이 좋다고 여겼다."], ["Some might think him, and others might think her, the most in luck.", "한 무리는 그들이 다 함께 돈웰로 옮겨 가고 하트필드는 존 나이틀리 부부에게 넘기는 게 좋겠다고 권할 만했고, 또 한 무리는 양쪽 하인들 사이에 불화가 생기리라 점칠 만했다. 그래도 전체적으로 보면 단 한 군데, 곧 목사관을 빼고는 이렇다 할 반대가 일지 않았다."], ["One set might recommend their all removing to Donwell, and leaving Hartfield for the John Knightleys; and another might predict disagreements among their servants; but yet, upon the whole, there was no serious objection raised, except in one habitation, the Vicarage.—There, the surprize was not softened by any satisfaction.", "거기서는 그 놀라움이 어떤 흡족함으로도 누그러지지 않았다. 미스터 엘턴은 아내에 비하면 이 일에 별 관심이 없었다."], ["Mr. Elton cared little about it, compared with his wife; he only hoped “the young lady’s pride would now be contented;”", "그는 그저 \"이제 그 아가씨의 자존심이 흡족해졌으면 좋겠다\"고 바랐고, \"그녀가 잡을 수만 있다면 나이틀리를 잡으려고 늘 마음먹고 있었던 모양\"이라고 짐작했으며, 하트필드에 산다는 대목에 이르러서는 대담하게도 이렇게 내뱉을 수 있었다 — \"나라면 질색인데, 차라리 그 사람이 낫지!\""], ["and supposed “she had always meant to catch Knightley if she could;”", "그러나 엘턴 부인은 정말이지 몹시도 심란해했다. \"가엾은 나이틀리!"], ["and, on the point of living at Hartfield, could daringly exclaim, “Rather he than I!”—But Mrs.", "딱한 양반!— 그에게 참 안된 일이지.\" 그녀는 더없이 마음을 졸였다."], ["Elton was very much discomposed indeed.—“Poor Knightley!", "그는 비록 아주 별난 사람이긴 해도 천 가지 좋은 자질을 갖춘 사람이었으니까. 어쩌면 그렇게 넘어갔단 말인가?"], ["poor fellow!—sad business for him.”—She was extremely concerned; for, though very eccentric, he had a thousand good qualities.—How could he be so taken in?—Did not think him at all in love—not in the least.—Poor Knightley!—There would be an end of all pleasant intercourse with him.—How happy he had been to come and dine with them whenever they asked him!", "그가 사랑에 빠졌다고는 도무지 여겨지지 않았다 — 눈곱만큼도. 가엾은 나이틀리!"], ["But that would be all over now.—Poor fellow!—No more exploring parties to Donwell made for her.", "이제 그와 나누던 그 즐거운 교제도 다 끝장이로구나. 그가 우리 집에 청할 때마다 와서 식사하기를 얼마나 좋아했던가!"], ["Oh!", "하지만 그것도 이제 다 끝이지. 딱한 양반!"], ["no; there would be a Mrs.", "그녀를 위해 마련될 돈웰 탐방도 이제 없으리라. 오!"], ["Knightley to throw cold water on every thing.—Extremely disagreeable!", "안 되지. 매사에 찬물을 끼얹을 나이틀리 부인이 떡하니 버티고 있을 테니."], ["But she was not at all sorry that she had abused the housekeeper the other day.—Shocking plan, living together.", "더없이 못마땅한 일! 그래도 그녀는 요전에 가정부를 험담해 둔 것이 조금도 후회되지 않았다."], ["It would never do.", "함께 산다니 끔찍한 작정이로다."], ["She knew a family near Maple Grove who had tried it, and been obliged to separate before the end of the first quarter.", "그게 잘될 리가 없지. 그녀는 메이플 그로브 근처에서 그것을 시도했다가 첫 분기가 끝나기도 전에 갈라설 수밖에 없었던 한 집안을 알고 있었다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter XVIII", "# 제3권 18장"]], [["Time passed on.", "세월이 흘렀다."], ["A few more to-morrows, and the party from London would be arriving.", "며칠만 더 지나면 런던의 일행이 도착할 참이었다. 마음을 졸이게 하는 변화였다."], ["It was an alarming change; and Emma was thinking of it one morning, as what must bring a great deal to agitate and grieve her, when Mr. Knightley came in, and distressing thoughts were put by.", "그것이 자신을 한껏 동요시키고 슬프게 하리라 여기며 어느 날 아침 엠마가 그 생각에 잠겨 있을 때, 미스터 나이틀리가 들어왔고, 괴로운 생각은 한쪽으로 밀려났다."], ["After the first chat of pleasure he was silent; and then, in a graver tone, began with,", "처음의 즐거운 잡담이 끝나자 그는 말이 없었다. 그러더니 한결 진지한 어조로 입을 열었다."]], [["“I have something to tell you, Emma; some news.”", "\"할 말이 있어, 엠마. 소식이 하나 있네.\""]], [["“Good or bad?”", "\"좋은 거예요, 나쁜 거예요?\""], ["said she, quickly, looking up in his face.", "그녀가 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 얼른 물었다."]], [["“I do not know which it ought to be called.”", "\"어느 쪽이라 해야 할지 나도 모르겠군.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["good I am sure.—I see it in your countenance.", "좋은 거예요, 분명히. 얼굴에 다 나타나는걸요."], ["You are trying not to smile.”", "웃음을 참고 계시잖아요.\""]], [["“I am afraid,”", "\"걱정이군.\""], ["said he, composing his features, “I am very much afraid, my dear Emma, that you will not smile when you hear it.”", "그가 얼굴을 가다듬으며 말했다. \"정말이지 걱정이야, 엠마, 이 이야기를 들으면 자네가 웃지 못할 것 같아서.\""]], [["“Indeed!", "\"그럴 리가요!"], ["but why so?—I can hardly imagine that any thing which pleases or amuses you, should not please and amuse me too.”", "그런데 왜요? 당신을 기쁘게 하거나 즐겁게 하는 일이 저까지 기쁘게 하고 즐겁게 하지 않을 거라고는 도무지 상상이 안 되는걸요.\""]], [["“There is one subject,”", "\"한 가지 주제가 있지.\""], ["he replied, “I hope but one, on which we do not think alike.”", "그가 대답했다. \"한 가지뿐이길 바라네만, 우리 둘이 생각이 같지 않은 주제 말이야.\""], ["He paused a moment, again smiling, with his eyes fixed on her face.", "그는 다시 미소를 띤 채 그녀의 얼굴에 시선을 고정하고 잠시 말을 멈추었다. \"짐작 가는 거 없나?"], ["“Does nothing occur to you?—Do not you recollect?—Harriet Smith.”", "기억 안 나? 해리엇 스미스 말이야.\""]], [["Her cheeks flushed at the name, and she felt afraid of something, though she knew not what.", "그 이름에 그녀의 두 뺨이 붉어졌고, 무엇인지는 몰라도 무언가가 두려워졌다."]], [["“Have you heard from her yourself this morning?”", "\"오늘 아침 그녀에게서 직접 무슨 소식을 들은 건가?\""], ["cried he.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“You have, I believe, and know the whole.”", "\"들은 것 같은데. 다 알고 있는 것 같군.\""]], [["“No, I have not; I know nothing; pray tell me.”", "\"아니요, 못 들었어요. 아무것도 몰라요. 어서 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“You are prepared for the worst, I see—and very bad it is.", "\"최악의 경우를 각오하고 있군, 보아하니. 그래, 아주 안 좋은 일이야."], ["Harriet Smith marries Robert Martin.”", "해리엇 스미스가 로버트 마틴과 결혼한다네.\""]], [["Emma gave a start, which did not seem like being prepared—and her eyes, in eager gaze, said, “No, this is impossible!”", "엠마는 흠칫 놀랐는데, 도무지 각오하고 있던 사람 같지는 않았다."], ["but her lips were closed.", "그리고 그녀의 두 눈은 간절히 응시하며 \"안 돼요, 그럴 리 없어요!\"라고 말하고 있었지만, 입술은 다물려 있었다."]], [["“It is so, indeed,” continued Mr.", "\"정말이라네.\""], ["Knightley; “I have it from Robert Martin himself.", "미스터 나이틀리가 말을 이었다. \"로버트 마틴 본인에게서 들었어."], ["He left me not half an hour ago.”", "채 반 시간도 안 됐네, 나와 헤어진 게.\""]], [["She was still looking at him with the most speaking amazement.", "그녀는 여전히 더없이 말 많은 놀라움을 담고 그를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“You like it, my Emma, as little as I feared.—I wish our opinions were the same.", "\"내가 걱정한 그대로, 자네는 이 일이 영 마뜩잖은 게로군, 엠마. 우리 생각이 같다면 좋으련만."], ["But in time they will.", "하지만 머잖아 같아질 거야."], ["Time, you may be sure, will make one or the other of us think differently; and, in the meanwhile, we need not talk much on the subject.”", "시간이 흐르면, 분명 우리 둘 중 누구 하나는 생각이 달라질 테니. 그동안은 이 이야기를 길게 할 필요 없겠지.\""]], [["“You mistake me, you quite mistake me,”", "\"오해하신 거예요, 완전히 오해하신 거예요.\""], ["she replied, exerting herself.", "그녀가 기운을 내며 대답했다."], ["“It is not that such a circumstance would now make me unhappy, but I cannot believe it.", "\"그런 일이 지금 저를 불행하게 만들어서가 아니라, 도무지 믿기지가 않아서 그래요."], ["It seems an impossibility!—You cannot mean to say, that Harriet Smith has accepted Robert Martin.", "있을 수 없는 일 같은걸요! 해리엇 스미스가 로버트 마틴을 받아들였다는 말씀은 아니실 거예요."], ["You cannot mean that he has even proposed to her again—yet.", "설마 그가 그녀에게 다시 청혼했다는 말씀도 — 아직은 아니죠."], ["You only mean, that he intends it.”", "그가 그럴 작정이라는 말씀이시겠죠.\""]], [["“I mean that he has done it,” answered Mr.", "\"그가 청혼을 했다는 말이야.\""], ["Knightley, with smiling but determined decision, “and been accepted.”", "미스터 나이틀리가 미소를 띠었으나 단호한 결단으로 대답했다. \"그리고 승낙을 받았네.\""]], [["“Good God!” she cried.—“Well!”—Then having recourse to her workbasket, in excuse for leaning down her face, and concealing all the exquisite feelings of delight and entertainment which she knew she must be expressing, she added, “Well, now tell me every thing; make this intelligible to me. How, where, when?—Let me know it all. I never was more surprized—but it does not make me unhappy, I assure you.—How—how has it been possible?”", "\"맙소사!\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"이런!\" 그러고는 얼굴을 숙이는 핑계로 반짇고리에 손을 뻗어, 분명 자신이 드러내고 있을 그 더없는 기쁨과 즐거움의 감정을 죄다 감추며 덧붙였다. \"이런, 자, 이제 다 말씀해 주세요. 알아듣게 설명해 주세요. 어떻게, 어디서, 언제요? 죄다 알려 주세요. 이렇게 놀란 적이 없어요 — 그래도 이게 저를 불행하게 만들진 않아요, 정말이에요. 어떻게, 어떻게 이런 일이 가능했죠?\""]], [["“It is a very simple story.", "\"아주 간단한 이야기야."], ["He went to town on business three days ago, and I got him to take charge of some papers which I was wanting to send to John.—He delivered these papers to John, at his chambers, and was asked by him to join their party the same evening to Astley’s.", "그가 사흘 전에 볼일이 있어 런던에 갔는데, 내가 존에게 보내려던 서류 몇 장을 그 편에 맡겼지. 그가 존의 사무실에 그 서류를 전했더니, 존이 그날 저녁 애슬리 극장에 가는 자기들 일행에 끼라고 청한 거야."], ["They were going to take the two eldest boys to Astley’s.", "위로 두 사내아이를 애슬리에 데려갈 참이었거든."], ["The party was to be our brother and sister, Henry, John—and Miss Smith.", "일행은 우리 아우 부부와 헨리, 존 — 그리고 미스 스미스였네. 내 친구 로버트가 거절할 수 있었겠나."], ["My friend Robert could not resist.", "그들이 가는 길에 그를 데리러 들렀고, 다들 더없이 즐거워했지."], ["They called for him in their way; were all extremely amused; and my brother asked him to dine with them the next day—which he did—and in the course of that visit (as I understand) he found an opportunity of speaking to Harriet; and certainly did not speak in vain.—She made him, by her acceptance, as happy even as he is deserving.", "그리고 아우가 다음 날 함께 저녁을 들자고 청했고 — 그가 그렇게 했지 — 그 자리에서 (내가 듣기로는) 해리엇에게 말할 기회를 얻었고, 헛되이 말하지 않은 게 분명해. 그녀가 승낙함으로써, 그가 받을 자격이 있는 만큼이나 그를 행복하게 만들어 주었거든."], ["He came down by yesterday’s coach, and was with me this morning immediately after breakfast, to report his proceedings, first on my affairs, and then on his own.", "그는 어제 마차로 내려와서, 오늘 아침 식사 직후에 곧장 나를 찾아와 그간의 일을 보고했네. 먼저 내 용건을, 그다음에 제 일을."], ["This is all that I can relate of the how, where, and when.", "어떻게, 어디서, 언제에 관해 내가 들려줄 수 있는 건 이게 다일세. 자네 친구 해리엇은 만나면 훨씬 긴 이야기를 들려줄 거야."], ["Your friend Harriet will make a much longer history when you see her.—She will give you all the minute particulars, which only woman’s language can make interesting.—In our communications we deal only in the great.—However, I must say, that Robert Martin’s heart seemed for him, and to me, very overflowing; and that he did mention, without its being much to the purpose, that on quitting their box at Astley’s, my brother took charge of Mrs.", "오직 여자의 말솜씨라야 흥미롭게 만들 수 있는 자잘한 세부까지 죄다 말해 주겠지. 우리끼리의 대화에서야 큰 줄거리만 다루니까."], ["John Knightley and little John, and he followed with Miss Smith and Henry; and that at one time they were in such a crowd, as to make Miss Smith rather uneasy.”", "그래도 한마디 하자면, 로버트 마틴의 마음은 그로서도, 또 내가 보기에도 한껏 넘쳐 보이더군. 그리고 별로 요점은 아니었지만, 애슬리에서 그 박스석을 나설 때 아우가 미시즈 존 나이틀리와 어린 존을 맡고, 자기는 미스 스미스와 헨리를 데리고 뒤따랐다는 것, 그리고 한때 어찌나 사람이 붐볐던지 미스 스미스가 좀 불안해했다는 것까지 말하더군.\""]], [["He stopped.—Emma dared not attempt any immediate reply.", "그는 말을 멈췄다. 엠마는 당장은 무슨 대답도 시도할 엄두가 나지 않았다."], ["To speak, she was sure would be to betray a most unreasonable degree of happiness.", "입을 열면 분명 도무지 사리에 닿지 않을 만큼 큰 행복을 들켜 버릴 터였다. 잠시 기다려야 했다."], ["She must wait a moment, or he would think her mad.", "그러지 않으면 그가 자기를 미쳤다고 여길 테니."], ["Her silence disturbed him; and after observing her a little while, he added,", "그녀의 침묵이 그를 불안하게 했고, 그는 잠시 그녀를 살피더니 덧붙였다."]], [["“Emma, my love, you said that this circumstance would not now make you unhappy; but I am afraid it gives you more pain than you expected. His situation is an evil—but you must consider it as what satisfies your friend; and I will answer for your thinking better and better of him as you know him more. His good sense and good principles would delight you.—As far as the man is concerned, you could not wish your friend in better hands. His rank in society I would alter if I could, which is saying a great deal I assure you, Emma.—You laugh at me about William Larkins; but I could quite as ill spare Robert Martin.”", "\"엠마, 내 사랑, 이 일이 지금 자네를 불행하게 만들지는 않을 거라 했지. 하지만 자네가 예상한 것보다 더 큰 아픔을 주는 것 같아 걱정이야. 그의 처지가 좋지 않은 건 사실이지. 하지만 자네 친구를 만족시키는 일이라 여겨 주어야 해. 그리고 자네가 그를 알아 갈수록 점점 더 좋게 여기게 되리라는 건 내가 장담하지. 그의 분별과 올바른 신념은 자네를 흐뭇하게 할 거야. 사람 됨됨이로 말하자면, 자네 친구가 그보다 더 든든한 손에 맡겨지기를 바랄 순 없을 걸세. 그의 사회적 신분이야 내가 바꿀 수 있다면 바꾸고 싶지만 — 이건 정말 대단한 말이야, 엠마. 자네는 윌리엄 라킨스를 두고 나를 놀리지만, 로버트 마틴도 그만큼이나 아쉬운 사람이야.\""]], [["He wanted her to look up and smile; and having now brought herself not to smile too broadly—she did—cheerfully answering,", "그는 그녀가 고개를 들고 미소 짓기를 바랐다. 이제 너무 활짝 웃지는 않을 만큼 마음을 추스른 그녀는 — 미소를 지으며 — 명랑하게 대답했다."]], [["“You need not be at any pains to reconcile me to the match.", "\"이 혼사를 두고 저를 달래려고 애쓰실 필요 없어요."], ["I think Harriet is doing extremely well.", "해리엇이 더없이 잘하고 있다고 생각하는걸요."], ["Her connexions may be worse than his.", "그녀의 집안 쪽이 그의 집안보다 못할 수도 있죠."], ["In respectability of character, there can be no doubt that they are.", "인품의 떳떳함으로 따지면, 그게 못하다는 건 의심할 여지가 없고요."], ["I have been silent from surprize merely, excessive surprize.", "제가 잠자코 있었던 건 그저 놀라서였어요, 너무 놀라서."], ["You cannot imagine how suddenly it has come on me!", "이게 얼마나 갑작스럽게 닥쳤는지 당신은 상상도 못 하실 거예요!"], ["how peculiarly unprepared I was!—for I had reason to believe her very lately more determined against him, much more, than she was before.”", "제가 얼마나 유달리 마음의 준비가 안 돼 있었는지를요! 바로 얼마 전까지만 해도 그녀가 그에게 전보다 훨씬 더, 아주아주 단단히 마음을 닫았다고 믿을 만한 이유가 있었거든요.\""]], [["“You ought to know your friend best,” replied Mr.", "\"자네 친구는 자네가 제일 잘 알겠지.\""], ["Knightley; “but I should say she was a good-tempered, soft-hearted girl, not likely to be very, very determined against any young man who told her he loved her.”", "미스터 나이틀리가 대답했다. \"하지만 내가 보기에 그녀는 성품이 곱고 마음이 여린 아가씨라, 자기를 사랑한다고 말해 주는 젊은이에게 아주아주 단단히 마음을 닫고 있을 사람 같지는 않던데.\""]], [["Emma could not help laughing as she answered, “Upon my word, I believe you know her quite as well as I do.—But, Mr. Knightley, are you perfectly sure that she has absolutely and downright accepted him. I could suppose she might in time—but can she already?—Did not you misunderstand him?—You were both talking of other things; of business, shows of cattle, or new drills—and might not you, in the confusion of so many subjects, mistake him?—It was not Harriet’s hand that he was certain of—it was the dimensions of some famous ox.”", "엠마는 웃음을 참지 못하고 대답했다. \"정말이지, 당신도 그녀를 저만큼이나 잘 아시는 것 같네요. 하지만 미스터 나이틀리, 그녀가 정말로 명백하고 분명하게 그를 받아들였다고 완전히 확신하세요? 머잖아 그럴 수도 있다고는 짐작했지만 — 벌써요? 그를 오해하신 건 아니고요? 두 분이 다른 이야기들도 하고 계셨잖아요. 용건이며 가축 품평회며 새 파종기 같은 거요 — 그렇게 많은 주제가 뒤섞인 통에 혹시 그를 잘못 알아들으신 건 아닐까요? 그가 확신한 게 해리엇의 손이 아니라 — 어떤 이름난 황소의 치수였을지도 모르잖아요.\""]], [["The contrast between the countenance and air of Mr. Knightley and Robert Martin was, at this moment, so strong to Emma’s feelings, and so strong was the recollection of all that had so recently passed on Harriet’s side, so fresh the sound of those words, spoken with such emphasis, “No, I hope I know better than to think of Robert Martin,”", "이 순간 엠마의 마음속에서 미스터 나이틀리와 로버트 마틴의 얼굴과 분위기의 대조가 어찌나 강렬했던지, 또 바로 얼마 전 해리엇 쪽에서 일어났던 모든 일의 기억이 어찌나 강렬했던지, 그토록 힘주어 말했던 그 말 — \"아니요, 저는 로버트 마틴 따위는 생각하지 않을 만큼은 분별이 있어요\""], ["that she was really expecting the intelligence to prove, in some measure, premature.", "— 의 울림이 어찌나 생생했던지, 그녀는 정말로 이 소식이 어느 정도 성급한 것으로 판명되기를 기대하고 있었다."], ["It could not be otherwise.", "달리 될 리가 없었다."]], [["“Do you dare say this?”", "\"감히 그런 말을 하는 건가?\""], ["cried Mr. Knightley.", "미스터 나이틀리가 외쳤다."], ["“Do you dare to suppose me so great a blockhead, as not to know what a man is talking of?—What do you deserve?”", "\"감히 나를, 남이 무슨 이야기를 하는지도 못 알아들을 만큼 멍청이라고 생각하는 거야? 무슨 벌을 받아야 마땅하지?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I always deserve the best treatment, because I never put up with any other; and, therefore, you must give me a plain, direct answer.", "저야 늘 최상의 대접을 받아 마땅하죠. 다른 건 결코 참지 않으니까요."], ["Are you quite sure that you understand the terms on which Mr.", "그러니 분명하고 직접적인 대답을 해 주셔야 해요."], ["Martin and Harriet now are?”", "미스터 마틴과 해리엇이 지금 어떤 사이가 되었는지, 그 조건을 제대로 알고 계신다고 정말로 확신하세요?\""]], [["“I am quite sure,”", "\"확실히 알고 있지.\""], ["he replied, speaking very distinctly, “that he told me she had accepted him; and that there was no obscurity, nothing doubtful, in the words he used; and I think I can give you a proof that it must be so.", "그가 또렷하게 말했다. \"그녀가 자기를 받아들였다고 그가 내게 말했고, 그가 쓴 말에는 모호한 구석이라곤, 의심스러운 데라곤 하나도 없었어."], ["He asked my opinion as to what he was now to do. He knew of no one but Mrs.", "그리고 분명히 그럴 수밖에 없다는 증거를 하나 댈 수 있을 것 같군."], ["Goddard to whom he could apply for information of her relations or friends.", "그가 이제 어떻게 해야 할지 내 의견을 물었거든. 그녀의 친척이나 친구들에 관해 알아볼 사람으로 고더드 부인 말고는 떠오르는 이가 없다더군."], ["Could I mention any thing more fit to be done, than to go to Mrs. Goddard?", "고더드 부인을 찾아가는 것 말고 더 마땅한 방도를 내가 댈 수 있었겠나?"], ["I assured him that I could not.", "나는 그럴 수 없다고 단언했지."], ["Then, he said, he would endeavour to see her in the course of this day.”", "그러자 그는 오늘 안으로 그녀를 만나 보겠다고 하더군.\""]], [["“I am perfectly satisfied,”", "\"완전히 만족했어요.\""], ["replied Emma, with the brightest smiles, “and most sincerely wish them happy.”", "엠마가 더없이 환한 미소로 대답했다. \"그리고 진심으로 두 사람이 행복하기를 빌어요.\""]], [["“You are materially changed since we talked on this subject before.”", "\"이 문제로 전에 이야기 나눴을 때와는 자네가 사뭇 달라졌군.\""]], [["“I hope so—for at that time I was a fool.”", "\"그러길 바라요 — 그때 저는 바보였으니까요.\""]], [["“And I am changed also; for I am now very willing to grant you all Harriet’s good qualities.", "\"나도 달라졌네. 이제는 해리엇의 온갖 좋은 점들을 자네에게 기꺼이 인정해 줄 수 있게 됐으니."], ["I have taken some pains for your sake, and for Robert Martin’s sake, (whom I have always had reason to believe as much in love with her as ever,) to get acquainted with her.", "자네를 위해서, 또 로버트 마틴을 위해서 (나는 늘 그가 변함없이 그녀를 사랑한다고 믿을 만한 이유가 있었거든) 그녀와 친해지려고 얼마간 애를 썼어. 그녀와 이야기를 꽤 많이 나누었지."], ["I have often talked to her a good deal.", "자네도 내가 그러는 걸 봤을 거야. 실은 가끔, 자네가 내가 가엾은 마틴의 편을 들고 있다고 절반쯤 의심하는 것 같다는 생각이 들기도 했어."], ["You must have seen that I did.", "결코 그런 건 아니었지만."], ["Sometimes, indeed, I have thought you were half suspecting me of pleading poor Martin’s cause, which was never the case; but, from all my observations, I am convinced of her being an artless, amiable girl, with very good notions, very seriously good principles, and placing her happiness in the affections and utility of domestic life.—Much of this, I have no doubt, she may thank you for.”", "어쨌든 내가 살펴본 모든 것으로 보아, 그녀는 꾸밈없고 사랑스러운 아가씨로, 아주 올바른 생각과 아주 진지하고 올바른 신념을 지녔으며, 가정생활의 애정과 보람에 제 행복을 두는 사람이라고 확신하게 됐어. 이 가운데 많은 부분은 분명 그녀가 자네 덕분이라 할 수 있을 거야.\""]], [["“Me!”", "\"제 덕분이라뇨!\""], ["cried Emma, shaking her head.—“Ah!", "엠마가 고개를 저으며 외쳤다. \"아!"], ["poor Harriet!”", "가엾은 해리엇!\""]], [["She checked herself, however, and submitted quietly to a little more praise than she deserved.", "그래도 그녀는 자제하며, 받을 자격보다 조금 더한 칭찬을 잠자코 받아들였다."]], [["Their conversation was soon afterwards closed by the entrance of her father.", "그들의 대화는 곧 아버지가 들어오면서 끝났다."], ["She was not sorry.", "그녀는 아쉽지 않았다."], ["She wanted to be alone.", "혼자 있고 싶었다."], ["Her mind was in a state of flutter and wonder, which made it impossible for her to be collected.", "마음이 들뜨고 어리둥절한 상태라 도무지 차분할 수가 없었다."], ["She was in dancing, singing, exclaiming spirits; and till she had moved about, and talked to herself, and laughed and reflected, she could be fit for nothing rational.", "춤추고 노래하고 탄성을 지르고 싶은 기분이었다. 이리저리 거닐며, 혼잣말을 하고, 웃고, 곰곰이 되짚어 보기 전에는 무엇 하나 이성적인 일을 할 수가 없었다."]], [["Her father’s business was to announce James’s being gone out to put the horses to, preparatory to their now daily drive to Randalls; and she had, therefore, an immediate excuse for disappearing.", "아버지의 용건은, 이제 날마다 다니는 랜덜스 나들이 채비로 제임스가 말을 매러 나갔다는 것을 알리는 일이었다. 그래서 그녀는 곧바로 빠져나갈 핑계가 생겼다."]], [["The joy, the gratitude, the exquisite delight of her sensations may be imagined.", "그녀가 느낀 그 기쁨, 그 고마움, 그 더없는 환희는 가히 짐작할 만하다."], ["The sole grievance and alloy thus removed in the prospect of Harriet’s welfare, she was really in danger of becoming too happy for security.—What had she to wish for?", "해리엇의 앞날에 대한 전망에서 유일한 근심거리이자 불순물이 이렇게 사라지고 나니, 그녀는 정말로 안심하기에는 지나치게 행복해질 위험에 빠질 지경이었다. 더 바랄 게 무엇이 있겠는가?"], ["Nothing, but to grow more worthy of him, whose intentions and judgment had been ever so superior to her own.", "아무것도 없었다 — 그의 뜻과 판단이 늘 자신의 것보다 그토록 빼어났던 그 사람에게 더 어울리는 사람이 되는 것 말고는."], ["Nothing, but that the lessons of her past folly might teach her humility and circumspection in future.", "아무것도 없었다 — 지난날 어리석음의 교훈이 앞으로 그녀에게 겸손과 신중함을 가르쳐 주는 것 말고는."]], [["Serious she was, very serious in her thankfulness, and in her resolutions; and yet there was no preventing a laugh, sometimes in the very midst of them.", "그녀는 진지했다, 그 감사와 그 결심에 있어 무척 진지했다. 그러면서도 더러는 그 한복판에서조차 웃음이 터지는 것을 막을 길이 없었다."], ["She must laugh at such a close!", "이런 결말이라니 웃지 않을 수가 있나!"], ["Such an end of the doleful disappointment of five weeks back!", "다섯 주 전 그 처량한 실연의 끝이 이렇게 나다니!"], ["Such a heart—such a Harriet!", "이런 마음이라니 — 이런 해리엇이라니!"]], [["Now there would be pleasure in her returning—Every thing would be a pleasure.", "이제 그녀가 돌아오는 일이 즐거움이 되리라. 모든 것이 즐거움이 되리라."], ["It would be a great pleasure to know Robert Martin.", "로버트 마틴을 알아 가는 것도 큰 즐거움이 되리라."]], [["High in the rank of her most serious and heartfelt felicities, was the reflection that all necessity of concealment from Mr. Knightley would soon be over.", "그녀의 가장 진지하고 진심 어린 행복들 가운데서도 높은 자리를 차지한 것은, 미스터 나이틀리에게 무언가를 숨겨야 할 필요가 머잖아 모두 끝나리라는 생각이었다."], ["The disguise, equivocation, mystery, so hateful to her to practise, might soon be over.", "그토록 행하기 싫었던 그 위장, 얼버무림, 비밀이 머잖아 끝날 수 있으리라."], ["She could now look forward to giving him that full and perfect confidence which her disposition was most ready to welcome as a duty.", "이제 그녀는, 자신의 천성이 의무로서 기꺼이 받아들이려는 그 온전하고 완전한 신뢰를 그에게 바칠 일을 고대할 수 있었다."]], [["In the gayest and happiest spirits she set forward with her father; not always listening, but always agreeing to what he said; and, whether in speech or silence, conniving at the comfortable persuasion of his being obliged to go to Randalls every day, or poor Mrs.", "더없이 들뜨고 행복한 기분으로 그녀는 아버지와 함께 길을 나섰다. 그의 말을 늘 귀담아듣지는 않았으나 늘 맞장구는 쳐 주었다."], ["Weston would be disappointed.", "그리고 말로든 침묵으로든, 날마다 랜덜스에 가지 않으면 가엾은 웨스턴 부인이 실망하리라는 그의 편안한 확신을 모르는 척 거들어 주었다."]], [["They arrived.—Mrs.", "그들이 도착했다."], ["Weston was alone in the drawing-room:—but hardly had they been told of the baby, and Mr.", "웨스턴 부인은 응접실에 혼자 있었다."], ["Woodhouse received the thanks for coming, which he asked for, when a glimpse was caught through the blind, of two figures passing near the window.", "그런데 아기 이야기를 막 전해 듣고, 미스터 우드하우스가 청한 대로 와 주어 고맙다는 인사를 받기가 무섭게, 블라인드 너머로 창가를 지나가는 두 사람의 모습이 언뜻 눈에 들어왔다."]], [["“It is Frank and Miss Fairfax,”", "\"프랭크와 미스 페어팩스예요.\""], ["said Mrs. Weston.", "웨스턴 부인이 말했다."], ["“I was just going to tell you of our agreeable surprize in seeing him arrive this morning.", "\"마침 그 얘기를 해 드리려던 참이었는데, 오늘 아침 그 애가 도착하는 걸 보고 우리가 얼마나 기쁘게 놀랐는지요."], ["He stays till to-morrow, and Miss Fairfax has been persuaded to spend the day with us.—They are coming in, I hope.”", "내일까지 머물고요, 미스 페어팩스도 오늘 하루를 우리와 함께 보내자고 설득해 두었답니다. 들어오고 있군요, 그러길 바라요.\""]], [["In half a minute they were in the room.", "삼십 초쯤 지나 두 사람이 방으로 들어왔다. 엠마는 그를 보고 무척 반가웠다."], ["Emma was extremely glad to see him—but there was a degree of confusion—a number of embarrassing recollections on each side.", "하지만 어딘가 어색함이 있었다 — 양쪽 모두 거북한 기억이 한둘이 아니었다. 두 사람은 선뜻 미소 지으며 만났으나, 처음에는 무슨 말을 하기 어려울 만큼 서로 의식하고 있었다."], ["They met readily and smiling, but with a consciousness which at first allowed little to be said; and having all sat down again, there was for some time such a blank in the circle, that Emma began to doubt whether the wish now indulged, which she had long felt, of seeing Frank Churchill once more, and of seeing him with Jane, would yield its proportion of pleasure. When Mr.", "다들 다시 자리에 앉고 나자 한동안 좌중에 어찌나 침묵이 흐르던지, 엠마는 프랭크 처칠을 다시 한번, 그것도 제인과 함께 보고 싶다던 오래 품어 온 바람, 지금에야 채워진 그 바람이 과연 그만큼의 기쁨을 안겨 줄지 의심이 들기 시작했다."], ["Weston joined the party, however, and when the baby was fetched, there was no longer a want of subject or animation—or of courage and opportunity for Frank Churchill to draw near her and say,", "그러나 미스터 웨스턴이 일행에 합류하고 아기를 데려오자, 더는 화젯거리도 활기도 모자라지 않게 되었다. 프랭크 처칠이 그녀에게 다가와 말할 용기와 기회도 모자라지 않았다."]], [["“I have to thank you, Miss Woodhouse, for a very kind forgiving message in one of Mrs.", "\"미스 우드하우스, 감사 인사를 드려야겠습니다. 웨스턴 부인의 편지 가운데 하나에서 보내 주신, 그토록 너그럽게 용서하시는 전언에 대해서요."], ["Weston’s letters. I hope time has not made you less willing to pardon.", "시간이 흘렀다고 용서하실 마음이 줄어들지는 않으셨기를 바랍니다."], ["I hope you do not retract what you then said.”", "그때 하신 말씀을 거두지 않으시기를 바라요.\""]], [["“No, indeed,”", "\"그럼요, 정말이에요.\""], ["cried Emma, most happy to begin, “not in the least.", "엠마가 말문을 열게 된 것이 더없이 기뻐 외쳤다."], ["I am particularly glad to see and shake hands with you—and to give you joy in person.”", "\"조금도 그렇지 않아요. 당신을 만나 악수하게 되어 — 그리고 직접 축하를 전하게 되어 특별히 기뻐요.\""]], [["He thanked her with all his heart, and continued some time to speak with serious feeling of his gratitude and happiness.", "그는 진심을 다해 그녀에게 고마워했고, 한동안 진지한 감정으로 자신의 감사와 행복을 이야기했다."]], [["“Is not she looking well?”", "\"그녀가 좋아 보이지 않습니까?\""], ["said he, turning his eyes towards Jane.", "그가 제인 쪽으로 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"전보다 한결 좋아 보이죠?"], ["“Better than she ever used to do?—You see how my father and Mrs. Weston doat upon her.”", "아버지와 웨스턴 부인이 그녀를 얼마나 끔찍이 아끼시는지 보세요.\""]], [["But his spirits were soon rising again, and with laughing eyes, after mentioning the expected return of the Campbells, he named the name of Dixon.—Emma blushed, and forbade its being pronounced in her hearing.", "그러나 그의 기분은 곧 다시 살아났고, 캠벨 부부가 돌아올 예정이라는 이야기를 꺼낸 뒤, 웃음 띤 눈으로 딕슨이라는 이름을 입에 올렸다. 엠마는 얼굴을 붉히며 자기 앞에서 그 이름을 입에 담지 못하게 했다."]], [["“I can never think of it,”", "\"그 일은 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없어요.\""], ["she cried, “without extreme shame.”", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"이루 말할 수 없는 부끄러움 없이는요.\""]], [["“The shame,” he answered, “is all mine, or ought to be.", "\"부끄러움이라면 죄다 제 몫이죠, 아니 그래야 마땅하고요.\""], ["But is it possible that you had no suspicion?—I mean of late.", "그가 대답했다. \"한데 정말로 조금도 의심하지 못하셨던 겁니까?"], ["Early, I know, you had none.”", "후반에 말입니다. 처음엔 전혀 못 하셨던 거 압니다.\""]], [["“I never had the smallest, I assure you.”", "\"눈곱만큼도 의심하지 못했어요, 정말이에요.\""]], [["“That appears quite wonderful.", "\"그것참 신기하군요."], ["I was once very near—and I wish I had—it would have been better.", "한번은 거의 말할 뻔했는데 — 그때 말해 버렸더라면 — 그편이 나았을 거예요."], ["But though I was always doing wrong things, they were very bad wrong things, and such as did me no service.—It would have been a much better transgression had I broken the bond of secrecy and told you every thing.”", "하지만 저는 늘 그릇된 짓만 했어도, 그게 죄다 아주 못된 그릇된 짓이라, 저한테 아무 득도 안 됐죠. 차라리 비밀의 약속을 깨고 당신께 모든 걸 털어놓았더라면 훨씬 나은 잘못이 됐을 텐데요.\""]], [["“It is not now worth a regret,”", "\"이제 와 후회할 가치도 없어요.\""], ["said Emma.", "엠마가 말했다."]], [["“I have some hope,”", "\"바라건대.\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["resumed he, “of my uncle’s being persuaded to pay a visit at Randalls; he wants to be introduced to her.", "\"외삼촌이 랜덜스에 한번 와 주시도록 설득되면 좋겠어요. 그분이 그녀를 소개받고 싶어 하시거든요."], ["When the Campbells are returned, we shall meet them in London, and continue there, I trust, till we may carry her northward.—But now, I am at such a distance from her—is not it hard, Miss Woodhouse?—Till this morning, we have not once met since the day of reconciliation.", "캠벨 부부가 돌아오면 런던에서 그분들을 만날 거고, 그녀를 북쪽으로 데려갈 수 있을 때까지 거기 머무를 작정입니다. 한데 지금은 그녀와 이렇게 멀리 떨어져 있으니 — 괴롭지 않겠습니까, 미스 우드하우스?"], ["Do not you pity me?”", "오늘 아침까지, 화해한 날 이후로 우리는 한 번도 못 만났답니다. 제가 가엾지 않으세요?\""]], [["Emma spoke her pity so very kindly, that with a sudden accession of gay thought, he cried,", "엠마가 어찌나 다정하게 가엾다는 말을 건넸던지, 그는 갑자기 명랑한 생각이 솟구쳐 외쳤다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["by the bye,”", "그러고 보니.\""], ["then sinking his voice, and looking demure for the moment—“I hope Mr.", "그러더니 목소리를 낮추고 잠시 짐짓 시치미를 떼며 — \"미스터 나이틀리는 잘 지내시죠?\" 그가 말을 멈췄다."], ["Knightley is well?”", "그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 웃었다. \"당신이 제 편지를 보신 거 압니다."], ["He paused.—She coloured and laughed.—“I know you saw my letter, and think you may remember my wish in your favour.", "거기서 제가 당신을 위해 빌었던 바람도 기억하실 테고요. 이번엔 제가 축하를 돌려드릴게요."], ["Let me return your congratulations.—I assure you that I have heard the news with the warmest interest and satisfaction.—He is a man whom I cannot presume to praise.”", "정말이지 그 소식을 더없이 따뜻한 관심과 만족으로 들었답니다. 그분은 제가 감히 칭찬할 수도 없는 분이죠.\""]], [["Emma was delighted, and only wanted him to go on in the same style; but his mind was the next moment in his own concerns and with his own Jane, and his next words were,", "엠마는 기뻤고, 그가 그 어조로 계속 말해 주기만을 바랐다. 그러나 다음 순간 그의 마음은 제 일과 제 제인에게로 옮아갔고, 그가 이어 한 말은 이러했다."]], [["“Did you ever see such a skin?—such smoothness!", "\"이런 살결을 본 적 있으세요? 이런 매끄러움이라니!"], ["such delicacy!—and yet without being actually fair.—One cannot call her fair.", "이런 고움이라니! 그러면서도 마냥 희기만 한 건 아니죠."], ["It is a most uncommon complexion, with her dark eye-lashes and hair—a most distinguishing complexion!", "그녀를 흰 살결이라고는 할 수 없어요. 짙은 속눈썹과 머리칼과 어우러진 더없이 보기 드문 살빛이라 — 더없이 돋보이는 살빛이라니까요!"], ["So peculiarly the lady in it.—Just colour enough for beauty.”", "그 안에 그토록 기품이 어려 있고요. 아름다움에 꼭 알맞을 만큼의 혈색이죠.\""]], [["“I have always admired her complexion,” replied Emma, archly; “but do not I remember the time when you found fault with her for being so pale?—When we first began to talk of her.—Have you quite forgotten?”", "\"저야 늘 그녀의 살빛을 칭찬했어요.\" 엠마가 짓궂게 대답했다. \"그런데 당신이 그녀가 너무 창백하다고 흠을 잡던 때가 있지 않았던가요? 우리가 처음 그녀 이야기를 꺼냈을 적에요. 까맣게 잊으셨어요?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["no—what an impudent dog I was!—How could I dare—”", "아니, 제가 얼마나 뻔뻔한 작자였던지! 어찌 감히 그런 —\""]], [["But he laughed so heartily at the recollection, that Emma could not help saying,", "그러나 그는 그 기억에 어찌나 유쾌하게 웃었던지, 엠마는 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“I do suspect that in the midst of your perplexities at that time, you had very great amusement in tricking us all.—I am sure you had.—I am sure it was a consolation to you.”", "\"제 짐작엔 말이죠, 당신은 그때 그 곤혹스러움의 한복판에서도 우리 모두를 감쪽같이 속이는 데 큰 재미를 보셨을 것 같아요. 분명 그러셨을 거예요. 분명 그게 당신께 위안이 됐을 거고요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["no, no, no—how can you suspect me of such a thing?", "아니, 아니, 아니에요 — 어떻게 저를 그런 사람으로 의심하실 수 있어요?"], ["I was the most miserable wretch!”", "저는 세상에서 가장 가련한 비참한 놈이었는데요!\""]], [["“Not quite so miserable as to be insensible to mirth.", "\"즐거움을 못 느낄 만큼 비참하지는 않으셨겠죠. 우리 모두를 깜빡 속이고 있다는 걸 느끼는 게 분명 당신껜 큰 재미의 원천이었을 거예요."], ["I am sure it was a source of high entertainment to you, to feel that you were taking us all in.—Perhaps I am the readier to suspect, because, to tell you the truth, I think it might have been some amusement to myself in the same situation.", "어쩌면 제가 더 쉽게 의심하는지도 몰라요. 솔직히 말하면, 같은 처지였다면 저 자신한테도 얼마간 재미있는 일이었을 것 같거든요."], ["I think there is a little likeness between us.”", "우리 둘 사이엔 좀 닮은 데가 있는 것 같아요.\""]], [["He bowed.", "그가 고개를 숙여 보였다."]], [["“If not in our dispositions,”", "\"성정으론 아닐지라도,\" 그녀가 이내 참된 감회가 어린 표정으로 덧붙였다."], ["she presently added, with a look of true sensibility, “there is a likeness in our destiny; the destiny which bids fair to connect us with two characters so much superior to our own.”", "\"운명으로는 닮은 데가 있죠. 우리 둘 다 우리보다 훨씬 뛰어난 사람과 맺어질 듯한 그 운명 말이에요.\""]], [["“True, true,”", "\"맞아요, 맞아요.\""], ["he answered, warmly.", "그가 열렬히 대답했다."], ["“No, not true on your side.", "\"아니, 당신 쪽으로는 맞지 않아요."], ["You can have no superior, but most true on mine.—She is a complete angel.", "당신보다 뛰어난 사람이 있을 수 없으니까요. 하지만 제 쪽으로는 더없이 맞는 말이에요."], ["Look at her.", "그녀는 완벽한 천사거든요."], ["Is not she an angel in every gesture?", "보세요. 몸짓 하나하나가 천사 같지 않습니까?"], ["Observe the turn of her throat.", "저 목선이 돌아가는 모양을 보세요. 아버지를 올려다볼 때 저 눈을 보세요."], ["Observe her eyes, as she is looking up at my father.—You will be glad to hear (inclining his head, and whispering seriously) that my uncle means to give her all my aunt’s jewels.", "들으면 기뻐하실 텐데\" (고개를 기울이고 진지하게 속삭이며) \"외삼촌이 그녀에게 외숙모의 보석을 죄다 물려주실 작정이랍니다."], ["They are to be new set.", "새로 세공을 할 거예요."], ["I am resolved to have some in an ornament for the head.", "저는 그중 몇 개로 머리 장식을 만들기로 마음먹었어요."], ["Will not it be beautiful in her dark hair?”", "그녀의 짙은 머리칼에 얹으면 아름답지 않을까요?\""]], [["“Very beautiful, indeed,”", "\"정말 아름다울 거예요.\""], ["replied Emma; and she spoke so kindly, that he gratefully burst out,", "엠마가 대답했다. 어찌나 다정하게 말했던지 그가 고마운 마음에 불쑥 외쳤다."]], [["“How delighted I am to see you again!", "\"당신을 다시 뵙게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지요!"], ["and to see you in such excellent looks!—I would not have missed this meeting for the world.", "게다가 이렇게 좋은 모습으로요! 이 만남을 놓쳤다면 세상을 다 준대도 아까웠을 거예요."], ["I should certainly have called at Hartfield, had you failed to come.”", "당신이 안 오셨더라면 분명 제가 하트필드로 찾아갔을 겁니다.\""]], [["The others had been talking of the child, Mrs. Weston giving an account of a little alarm she had been under, the evening before, from the infant’s appearing not quite well.", "다른 이들은 아기 이야기를 하고 있었는데, 웨스턴 부인이 어젯밤 잠깐 겪은 작은 놀람을 들려주는 중이었다."], ["She believed she had been foolish, but it had alarmed her, and she had been within half a minute of sending for Mr.", "아기가 영 좋지 않아 보였던 것이다. 자기가 어리석었던 것 같다고는 했지만, 그래도 가슴이 철렁했고, 하마터면 삼십 초만 더 있었으면 미스터 페리를 부르러 사람을 보낼 뻔했다고 했다."], ["Perry. Perhaps she ought to be ashamed, but Mr.", "어쩌면 부끄러워해야 할 일이지만, 미스터 웨스턴도 자기만큼이나 안절부절못했다고 했다."], ["Weston had been almost as uneasy as herself.—In ten minutes, however, the child had been perfectly well again.", "그래도 십 분쯤 지나자 아기는 다시 멀쩡해졌다고 했다. 이것이 그녀의 이야기였다."], ["This was her history; and particularly interesting it was to Mr. Woodhouse, who commended her very much for thinking of sending for Perry, and only regretted that she had not done it.", "그리고 그것은 미스터 우드하우스에게 유독 흥미로운 이야기였으니, 그는 페리를 부르러 보낼 생각을 한 그녀를 무척 칭찬하면서, 다만 정말로 그러지 않은 것만은 아쉬워했다."], ["“She should always send for Perry, if the child appeared in the slightest degree disordered, were it only for a moment.", "\"아기가 조금이라도, 단 한순간이라도 이상해 보이면 언제든 페리를 부르러 보내야 해요."], ["She could not be too soon alarmed, nor send for Perry too often.", "아무리 빨리 놀라도 지나치지 않고, 아무리 자주 페리를 불러도 지나치지 않으니까요."], ["It was a pity, perhaps, that he had not come last night; for, though the child seemed well now, very well considering, it would probably have been better if Perry had seen it.”", "어쩌면 어젯밤에 그가 안 온 게 안된 일이에요. 아기가 지금은 멀쩡해 보이긴 해도, 그만하면 아주 멀쩡하긴 해도, 페리가 한번 봤더라면 십중팔구 더 나았을 테니 말이오.\""]], [["Frank Churchill caught the name.", "프랭크 처칠이 그 이름을 알아챘다."]], [["“Perry!”", "\"페리라고요!\""], ["said he to Emma, and trying, as he spoke, to catch Miss Fairfax’s eye.", "그가 엠마에게 말하면서, 말하는 동안 미스 페어팩스의 눈길을 붙잡으려 했다. \"제 친구 미스터 페리 말이군요!"], ["“My friend Mr. Perry!", "미스터 페리에 관해 다들 무슨 이야기를 하시는 거죠?"], ["What are they saying about Mr.", "그가 오늘 아침 여기 왔던가요?"], ["Perry?—Has he been here this morning?—And how does he travel now?—Has he set up his carriage?”", "그리고 그는 요즘 어떻게 다니죠? 마차라도 한 대 장만했나요?\""]], [["Emma soon recollected, and understood him; and while she joined in the laugh, it was evident from Jane’s countenance that she too was really hearing him, though trying to seem deaf.", "엠마는 곧 기억해 내고 그의 뜻을 알아챘다. 그리고 그녀가 함께 웃는 동안, 제인이 안 들리는 척 애쓰면서도 실은 정말로 듣고 있다는 것이 그 표정에서 역력히 드러났다."]], [["“Such an extraordinary dream of mine!”", "\"그 참 별난 제 꿈이었지요!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“I can never think of it without laughing.—She hears us, she hears us, Miss Woodhouse.", "\"그 생각만 하면 웃음이 안 나올 수가 없어요. 그녀가 듣고 있어요, 듣고 있다니까요, 미스 우드하우스."], ["I see it in her cheek, her smile, her vain attempt to frown.", "저 뺨에서, 저 미소에서, 찌푸려 보려는 저 헛된 안간힘에서 다 보입니다."], ["Look at her.", "그녀를 보세요."], ["Do not you see that, at this instant, the very passage of her own letter, which sent me the report, is passing under her eye—that the whole blunder is spread before her—that she can attend to nothing else, though pretending to listen to the others?”", "바로 지금 이 순간, 제게 그 소식을 전해 준 그녀 자신의 편지 구절이 그녀 눈앞을 스쳐 지나가고 있는 게 — 그 온갖 실수가 그녀 앞에 죄다 펼쳐져 있는 게 — 다른 이들 말을 듣는 척하면서도 그 외엔 아무것에도 마음을 못 쓰고 있는 게 안 보이세요?\""]], [["Jane was forced to smile completely, for a moment; and the smile partly remained as she turned towards him, and said in a conscious, low, yet steady voice,", "제인은 한순간 완연한 미소를 짓지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그를 향해 돌아서며 무언가를 의식하는 듯한, 낮지만 흔들림 없는 목소리로 말할 때까지 그 미소가 얼마간 남아 있었다."]], [["“How you can bear such recollections, is astonishing to me!—They will sometimes obtrude—but how you can court them!”", "\"그런 기억들을 어떻게 견디시는지, 저로선 놀라울 따름이에요! 그것들이 가끔 비집고 들어오긴 하죠 — 하지만 어떻게 스스로 그것들을 불러들이실 수가 있죠!\""]], [["He had a great deal to say in return, and very entertainingly; but Emma’s feelings were chiefly with Jane, in the argument; and on leaving Randalls, and falling naturally into a comparison of the two men, she felt, that pleased as she had been to see Frank Churchill, and really regarding him as she did with friendship, she had never been more sensible of Mr.", "그는 대꾸로 할 말이 많았고, 무척 재미나게 받아넘겼다."], ["Knightley’s high superiority of character.", "그러나 그 입씨름에서 엠마의 마음은 주로 제인 쪽에 가 있었다."], ["The happiness of this most happy day, received its completion, in the animated contemplation of his worth which this comparison produced.", "그리고 랜덜스를 나서며 자연스레 두 남자를 견주어 보게 되자, 그녀는 프랭크 처칠을 만나 그토록 반가웠고 또 실제로 우정 어린 마음으로 그를 여기고 있으면서도, 미스터 나이틀리의 인품이 얼마나 높이 빼어난지를 그 어느 때보다 절실히 느꼈다. 이 더없이 행복한 날의 행복은, 이 견줌이 자아낸 그의 가치에 대한 생기 넘치는 음미 속에서 비로소 완성되었다."]], [["# Volume III — Chapter XIX", "# 제3권 19장"]], [["If Emma had still, at intervals, an anxious feeling for Harriet, a momentary doubt of its being possible for her to be really cured of her attachment to Mr. Knightley, and really able to accept another man from unbiased inclination, it was not long that she had to suffer from the recurrence of any such uncertainty.", "엠마가 여전히 이따금 해리엇 때문에 마음을 졸이고, 그녀가 정말로 미스터 나이틀리에 대한 연정에서 벗어날 수 있는지, 그리고 정말로 한 점 치우침 없는 마음으로 다른 남자를 받아들일 수 있는지 잠시 의심하기도 했지만, 그런 막연한 불안이 되살아나 그녀를 괴롭히는 것도 오래가지 않았다."], ["A very few days brought the party from London, and she had no sooner an opportunity of being one hour alone with Harriet, than she became perfectly satisfied—unaccountable as it was!—that Robert Martin had thoroughly supplanted Mr.", "며칠 지나지 않아 런던의 일행이 도착했고, 해리엇과 단둘이 한 시간을 보낼 기회를 얻기가 무섭게 그녀는 완전히 마음을 놓았다 — 영문은 알 수 없었으나!"], ["Knightley, and was now forming all her views of happiness.", "— 로버트 마틴이 미스터 나이틀리를 깨끗이 밀어내고, 이제 해리엇의 행복에 대한 모든 그림을 채우고 있다는 것을."]], [["Harriet was a little distressed—did look a little foolish at first: but having once owned that she had been presumptuous and silly, and self-deceived, before, her pain and confusion seemed to die away with the words, and leave her without a care for the past, and with the fullest exultation in the present and future; for, as to her friend’s approbation, Emma had instantly removed every fear of that nature, by meeting her with the most unqualified congratulations.—Harriet was most happy to give every particular of the evening at Astley’s, and the dinner the next day; she could dwell on it all with the utmost delight. But what did such particulars explain?—The fact was, as Emma could now acknowledge, that Harriet had always liked Robert Martin; and that his continuing to love her had been irresistible.—Beyond this, it must ever be unintelligible to Emma.", "해리엇은 조금 거북해했고 — 처음에는 어쩐지 멋쩍은 기색이었다. 그러나 한번 지난날 자기가 주제넘고 어리석었으며 스스로를 속여 왔노라고 인정하고 나자, 그 고통과 혼란은 말과 함께 스러져 버린 듯, 과거에는 아무런 미련도 남지 않고 현재와 미래에 대한 더없이 벅찬 기쁨만이 가득했다. 친구의 인정으로 말하자면, 엠마가 더없이 무조건적인 축하로 그녀를 맞아 그런 종류의 두려움은 단번에 말끔히 걷어 주었으니까. — 해리엇은 애슬리 극장에서 보낸 그 저녁이며 이튿날의 만찬을 시시콜콜 들려주는 것이 더없이 행복했고, 그 모든 일을 한없는 기쁨에 잠겨 곱씹을 수 있었다. 그러나 그런 시시콜콜한 이야기들이 무엇을 설명해 주었던가? — 사실인즉, 엠마도 이제는 인정할 수밖에 없었으니, 해리엇은 언제나 로버트 마틴을 좋아해 왔던 것이며, 그가 변함없이 그녀를 사랑한 것은 거역할 수 없는 힘이었던 것이다. — 그 너머의 일은 엠마에게 영영 알 수 없는 것으로 남을 수밖에 없었다."]], [["The event, however, was most joyful; and every day was giving her fresh reason for thinking so.—Harriet’s parentage became known.", "그러나 이 일은 더없이 기쁜 것이었고, 날마다 그렇게 여길 새로운 이유가 생겨났다. — 해리엇의 혈통이 밝혀졌다."], ["She proved to be the daughter of a tradesman, rich enough to afford her the comfortable maintenance which had ever been hers, and decent enough to have always wished for concealment.—Such was the blood of gentility which Emma had formerly been so ready to vouch for!—It was likely to be as untainted, perhaps, as the blood of many a gentleman: but what a connexion had she been preparing for Mr.", "그녀는 어느 상인의 딸로 드러났는데, 그동안 그녀가 누려 온 안락한 부양을 감당할 만큼 넉넉하고, 또 그 일을 늘 숨기고 싶어 할 만큼은 점잖은 사람이었다. — 엠마가 한때 그토록 선뜻 보증하려 들었던 양반의 핏줄이란 게 고작 이러했던 것이다!"], ["Knightley—or for the Churchills—or even for Mr.", "— 어쩌면 여느 신사의 핏줄 못지않게 깨끗한 피였을지도 모른다."], ["Elton!—The stain of illegitimacy, unbleached by nobility or wealth, would have been a stain indeed.", "그러나 그녀가 미스터 나이틀리에게 — 또는 처칠 집안에 — 아니 심지어 미스터 엘턴에게까지 짝지어 주려 했던 것이 대체 어떤 인연이었단 말인가! — 고귀한 가문으로도 재산으로도 표백되지 않은 사생아의 오점은, 그야말로 오점이었을 터였다."]], [["No objection was raised on the father’s side; the young man was treated liberally; it was all as it should be: and as Emma became acquainted with Robert Martin, who was now introduced at Hartfield, she fully acknowledged in him all the appearance of sense and worth which could bid fairest for her little friend. She had no doubt of Harriet’s happiness with any good-tempered man; but with him, and in the home he offered, there would be the hope of more, of security, stability, and improvement. She would be placed in the midst of those who loved her, and who had better sense than herself; retired enough for safety, and occupied enough for cheerfulness. She would be never led into temptation, nor left for it to find her out. She would be respectable and happy; and Emma admitted her to be the luckiest creature in the world, to have created so steady and persevering an affection in such a man;—or, if not quite the luckiest, to yield only to herself.", "아버지 쪽에서는 아무런 반대도 없었다. 그 청년은 후하게 대접받았다. 모든 것이 마땅히 그래야 할 대로였다. 이제 하트필드에 소개된 로버트 마틴과 알고 지내게 되면서, 엠마는 그에게서 작은 친구에게 더없이 좋은 짝이 되어 줄 만한 분별과 인품의 면모를 남김없이 인정했다. 마음씨 고운 남자라면 누구와 살더라도 해리엇이 행복하리라는 것을 의심하지 않았다. 그러나 그와 더불어, 그가 마련해 준 가정에서라면 그 이상을 — 안정과 든든함과 향상을 바랄 수 있을 것이었다. 해리엇은 자기를 사랑하고 자기보다 분별 있는 이들 한가운데 자리 잡게 될 것이며, 안전할 만큼 한갓진 곳에서 명랑할 만큼 분주히 지내게 될 것이었다. 결코 유혹에 이끌릴 일도, 유혹이 그녀를 찾아오도록 방치될 일도 없을 것이었다. 그녀는 떳떳하고 행복하게 살아갈 것이었다. 그리하여 엠마는 그런 남자에게서 이토록 한결같고 끈질긴 애정을 불러일으켰으니 해리엇이야말로 세상에서 가장 운 좋은 사람이라고 인정했다. — 아니, 꼭 가장 운 좋은 사람은 아니라 해도, 자기 자신에게만은 양보해 둘 정도라고."]], [["Harriet, necessarily drawn away by her engagements with the Martins, was less and less at Hartfield; which was not to be regretted.—The intimacy between her and Emma must sink; their friendship must change into a calmer sort of goodwill; and, fortunately, what ought to be, and must be, seemed already beginning, and in the most gradual, natural manner.", "해리엇은 마틴 집안과 얽힌 약속들에 자연히 끌려, 하트필드에 점점 뜸하게 드나들게 되었으니, 그것은 아쉬워할 일이 아니었다. — 그녀와 엠마 사이의 친밀함은 가라앉아야 마땅했고, 그 우정은 한결 잔잔한 종류의 호의로 바뀌어야 했다. 그리고 다행히도, 마땅히 그러해야 하고 또 그럴 수밖에 없는 일이 이미 시작되고 있는 듯했으며, 그것도 더없이 점진적이고 자연스러운 모양새로였다."]], [["Before the end of September, Emma attended Harriet to church, and saw her hand bestowed on Robert Martin with so complete a satisfaction, as no remembrances, even connected with Mr. Elton as he stood before them, could impair.—Perhaps, indeed, at that time she scarcely saw Mr.", "구월이 끝나기 전에 엠마는 해리엇을 데리고 교회에 가, 그녀의 손이 로버트 마틴에게 건네지는 것을 더없는 만족 속에 지켜보았으니, 그 앞에 미스터 엘턴이 서 있어 떠오르는 어떤 기억으로도 그 만족은 흠집 하나 나지 않았다."], ["Elton, but as the clergyman whose blessing at the altar might next fall on herself.—Robert Martin and Harriet Smith, the latest couple engaged of the three, were the first to be married.", "— 실로 그 순간 엠마의 눈에는 미스터 엘턴이, 어쩌면 다음번엔 제단 앞에서 자기에게 축복을 내려 줄 성직자로밖에는 거의 보이지 않았을 것이다. — 셋 가운데 가장 늦게 약혼한 로버트 마틴과 해리엇 스미스가 가장 먼저 결혼한 한 쌍이 되었다."]], [["Jane Fairfax had already quitted Highbury, and was restored to the comforts of her beloved home with the Campbells.—The Mr.", "제인 페어팩스는 이미 하이베리를 떠나, 사랑하는 보금자리에서 캠벨 일가와 함께 누리던 안락함으로 돌아가 있었다."], ["Churchills were also in town; and they were only waiting for November.", "— 두 미스터 처칠 또한 런던에 머물고 있었고, 그들은 그저 십일월을 기다리고 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["The intermediate month was the one fixed on, as far as they dared, by Emma and Mr.", "엠마와 미스터 나이틀리는, 감히 정할 수 있는 한 가장 이른 때로 그 사이의 한 달을 택해 두었다."], ["Knightley.—They had determined that their marriage ought to be concluded while John and Isabella were still at Hartfield, to allow them the fortnight’s absence in a tour to the seaside, which was the plan.—John and Isabella, and every other friend, were agreed in approving it.", "— 두 사람은 존과 이저벨라가 아직 하트필드에 머무는 동안 혼례를 치르기로 마음먹었는데, 그래야 바닷가로 보름간 여행을 다녀올 수 있을 터였고, 그것이 계획이었다. — 존과 이저벨라를 비롯해 다른 모든 벗들도 그 계획을 한마음으로 찬성했다."], ["But Mr. Woodhouse—how was Mr.", "그러나 미스터 우드하우스는 — 미스터 우드하우스를 어떻게 승낙하게 한단 말인가?"], ["Woodhouse to be induced to consent?—he, who had never yet alluded to their marriage but as a distant event.", "— 두 사람의 혼인을 여태껏 그저 먼 훗날의 일로만 입에 올리던 그를."]], [["When first sounded on the subject, he was so miserable, that they were almost hopeless.—A second allusion, indeed, gave less pain.—He began to think it was to be, and that he could not prevent it—a very promising step of the mind on its way to resignation.", "처음 이 일을 넌지시 꺼냈을 때 그는 어찌나 비참해하던지, 두 사람은 거의 단념할 지경이었다. — 두 번째로 운을 떼었을 때는 정말이지 덜 괴로워했다."], ["Still, however, he was not happy. Nay, he appeared so much otherwise, that his daughter’s courage failed.", "— 그는 이 일이 일어나고야 말 것이며, 자기로서는 막을 수 없으리라고 생각하기 시작했으니 — 체념으로 가는 마음에 더없이 가망 있는 한 걸음이었다."], ["She could not bear to see him suffering, to know him fancying himself neglected; and though her understanding almost acquiesced in the assurance of both the Mr.", "그러나 여전히 그는 마음이 편치 않았다. 아니, 어찌나 편치 않은 기색이던지 딸의 용기가 꺾일 지경이었다."], ["Knightleys, that when once the event were over, his distress would be soon over too, she hesitated—she could not proceed.", "아버지가 괴로워하는 것을, 자기가 버림받았다고 여기는 것을 차마 볼 수가 없었다. 그리고 일단 일이 끝나고 나면 그 괴로움도 곧 끝나리라는 두 미스터 나이틀리의 장담에 머리로는 거의 수긍하면서도, 엠마는 망설였고 — 더는 밀고 나가지 못했다."]], [["In this state of suspense they were befriended, not by any sudden illumination of Mr. Woodhouse’s mind, or any wonderful change of his nervous system, but by the operation of the same system in another way.—Mrs.", "이렇게 마음을 졸이던 와중에 두 사람을 도운 것은, 미스터 우드하우스의 마음에 갑작스러운 깨우침이 비친 것도, 그의 신경 체계에 무슨 놀라운 변화가 생긴 것도 아니요, 바로 그 같은 신경 체계가 다른 방식으로 작동한 덕분이었다."], ["Weston’s poultry-house was robbed one night of all her turkeys—evidently by the ingenuity of man.", "— 어느 날 밤 웨스턴 부인의 가금사가 털려 칠면조가 몽땅 없어졌으니 — 영락없이 사람의 솜씨였다."], ["Other poultry-yards in the neighbourhood also suffered.—Pilfering was housebreaking to Mr.", "이웃의 다른 가금사들도 피해를 입었다. — 미스터 우드하우스의 겁먹은 머릿속에서 좀도둑질은 곧 가택 침입이었다."], ["Woodhouse’s fears.—He was very uneasy; and but for the sense of his son-in-law’s protection, would have been under wretched alarm every night of his life.", "— 그는 몹시 불안해했고, 사위가 지켜 주고 있다는 든든함이 없었더라면 평생 밤마다 처참한 공포에 시달렸을 것이다."], ["The strength, resolution, and presence of mind of the Mr. Knightleys, commanded his fullest dependence.", "두 미스터 나이틀리의 기운과 결단, 그리고 침착함은 그에게 더없는 의지가 되었다."], ["While either of them protected him and his, Hartfield was safe.—But Mr.", "그들 중 누구라도 자기와 식구들을 지켜 주는 한 하트필드는 안전했다."], ["John Knightley must be in London again by the end of the first week in November.", "— 그러나 미스터 존 나이틀리는 십일월 첫째 주가 끝날 무렵엔 다시 런던으로 가야 했다."]], [["The result of this distress was, that, with a much more voluntary, cheerful consent than his daughter had ever presumed to hope for at the moment, she was able to fix her wedding-day—and Mr. Elton was called on, within a month from the marriage of Mr. and Mrs. Robert Martin, to join the hands of Mr. Knightley and Miss Woodhouse.", "이 근심이 가져온 결과는, 딸이 그 순간 감히 바랐던 것보다 훨씬 더 자발적이고 흔쾌한 승낙이 떨어져, 그녀가 혼례 날짜를 정할 수 있게 되었다는 것이었다 — 그리하여 미스터 엘턴은 로버트 마틴 부부의 결혼으로부터 한 달이 채 못 되어, 미스터 나이틀리와 미스 우드하우스의 손을 맞잡게 해 달라는 부름을 받았다."]], [["The wedding was very much like other weddings, where the parties have no taste for finery or parade; and Mrs. Elton, from the particulars detailed by her husband, thought it all extremely shabby, and very inferior to her own.—“Very little white satin, very few lace veils; a most pitiful business!—Selina would stare when she heard of it.”—But, in spite of these deficiencies, the wishes, the hopes, the confidence, the predictions of the small band of true friends who witnessed the ceremony, were fully answered in the perfect happiness of the union.", "그 혼례는 당사자들이 화려함이나 떠들썩함에 도통 취미가 없는 여느 혼례와 매한가지였다. 그래서 엘턴 부인은 남편이 시시콜콜 늘어놓은 이야기를 듣고는, 그 모든 것이 지독히 초라하며 자기 혼례보다 한참 떨어진다고 여겼다. — \"흰 공단도 쥐꼬리만큼, 레이스 면사포도 몇 개 없고, 그야말로 가엾기 짝이 없는 일이지! — 셀리나가 들으면 눈이 휘둥그레질 거야.\" 그러나 이런 부족함에도 불구하고, 그 예식을 지켜본 한 줌의 참된 벗들이 품은 소망과 희망과 믿음과 예언은, 그 결합의 더없는 행복으로 남김없이 응답을 받았다."]], [["FINIS", "끝"]]];
